अंशु|అంశు|ಅಂಶು|aṃśu|aMshu|_vei_120@1|_vei1_pn000
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120pn">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn001">1:001</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn002">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aṃśu.</b>--I. Name of a protégé of the Aśvins in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201">1</a></sup> 2. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धानंजय्य">dhānaṃjayya</a>,</b> pupil of <b>Amāvāsya <a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्डिल्यायन">śāṇḍilyāyana</a>,</b> according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201">↑</a> viii. 5, 26. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 160; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 89; Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 129, suggests that he may be identical with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="खेल">khela</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373.</li></ol></div>

अंहसस्-पति|अंहसस्पति|अंहसस्|अंहस|पति|అంహసస్-పతి|అంహసస్పతి|అంహసస్|అంహస|పతి|ಅಂಹಸಸ್-ಪತಿ|ಅಂಹಸಸ್ಪತಿ|ಅಂಹಸಸ್|ಅಂಹಸ|ಪತಿ|aṃhasas-pati|aṃhasaspati|aṃhasas|aṃhasa|pati|aMhasas-pati|aMhasaspati|aMhasas|aMhasa|_vei_120@2|_vei1_pn000
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120pn">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn001">1:001</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn002">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aṃhasas-pati.</b>--This is the name of the intercalary month in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā (vii. 30; xxii. 31). See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></p></div>

अक्र|అక్ర|ಅಕ್ರ|akra|_vei_120@3|_vei1_pn000
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@4">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120pn">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn001">1:001</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn002">↷</a><hr><p><b>Akra.</b>--In several passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203">1</a></sup> this word means, according to Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203">2</a></sup> ‘horse.’ Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203">3</a></sup> suggests that ‘riding horse’ is the precise sense. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्व">aśva</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203">↑</a> i. 143, 7; 189, 7; iii. 1, 12; iv. 6, 3; x. 77, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 168, 169.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 118. <i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 414.</li></ol></div>

अक्ष|अक्ष|అక్ష_౧|అక్ష|ಅಕ್ಷ_೧|ಅಕ್ಷ|akṣa_1|akṣa|akSha_1|akSha|axa_1|axa|_vei_120@4|_vei1_pn000
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@5">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120pn">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn001">1:001</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn002">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Akṣa,</b> ‘axle,’ is a part of a chariot often referred to in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1204"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1204">1</a></sup> and later. It was apparently<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1204"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1204">2</a></sup> fastened to the body of the chariot (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="कोश">kośa</a></b>) by straps (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्षा">akṣā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="नह्">nah</a>,</i> lit. ‘tied to the axle,’ though this word is also<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1204"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1204">3</a></sup> rendered ‘horse’). The heating of the axle and the danger of its breaking were known.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1204"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1204">4</a></sup> The part of the axle round which the nave of the wheel revolved was called <a class="vei_120a" href="आणि">āṇi</a>, ‘pin.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1204"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1204">↑</a> i. 30, 14; 166, 9; iii. 53, 17; vi. 24, 3; x. 89; 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1204"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1204">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 246.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1204"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1204">↑</a> Rv. x. 53, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1204"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1204">↑</a> Rv. i. 164, 13.</li></ol></div>

अक्ष|अक्ष|అక్ష_౨|అక్ష|ಅಕ್ಷ_೨|ಅಕ್ಷ|akṣa_2|akṣa|akSha_2|akSha|axa_2|axa|_vei_120@5|_vei1_pn002
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@4">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@6">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn001">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn002">1:002</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn005">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Akṣa.</b>--This word occurs frequently, from the Rigveda onwards, both in the singular and plural, meaning ‘die’ and ‘dice.’ Dicing, along with horse-racing, was one of the main amusements of the Vedic Indian; but, despite the frequent mention of the game in the literature, there is considerable difficulty in obtaining any clear picture of the mode in which it was played.</p><p>(1) <i>The Material.</i>--The dice appear normally to have been made of Vibhīdaka nuts. Such dice are alluded to in both the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1205">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1205">2</a></sup> hence being called ‘brown’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="बभ्रु">babhru</a></i>), and ‘born on a windy spot.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1205">3</a></sup> In the ritual game of dice at the Agnyādheya and the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a> ceremonies the material of the dice is not specified, but it is possible that occasionally gold imitations of Vibhīdaka nuts were used.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1205">4</a></sup> There is no clear trace in the Vedic literature of the later use of cowries as dice.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1205">5</a></sup></p><p>(2) <i>The Number.</i>--In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1205">6</a></sup> the dicer is described as ‘leader of a great horde’ (<i>senānīr mahato gaṇasya</i>), and in another passage<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1205">7</a></sup> the number is given as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रि">tri</a>-pañcāśaḥ,</i> an expression which has been variously interpreted. Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1205">8</a></sup> Weber,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1205">9</a></sup> and Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1205">10</a></sup> render it as fifteen, which is grammatically hardly possible. Roth<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1205">11</a></sup> and Grassmann<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1205">12</a></sup> render it as ‘consisting of fifty-three.’ Lüders<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1205">13</a></sup> takes it as ‘consisting of one hundred and fifty,’ but he points out that this may be merely a vague expression for a large number. For a small number Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1205">14</a></sup> cites a reference in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1205">15</a></sup> to one who fears ‘him who holds four’ (<i>caturaś cid dadamānāt</i>), but the sense of that passage is dependent on the view taken of the method of playing the game.</p><p>(3) <i>The Method of Play.</i>--In several passages of the later  Saṃhitās and Brāhmaṇas lists are given of expressions connected with dicing. The names are <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत">kṛta</a>, Tretā, <a class="vei_120a" href="द्वापर">dvāpara</a>, Āskanda, and Abhibhū in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1205">16</a></sup> In the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1205">17</a></sup> among the victims at the Puruṣamedha, the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कितव">kitava</a></i> is offered to the Akṣarāja, the <i>ādinava-<a class="vei_120a" href="दर्श">darśa</a></i> to the <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत">kṛta</a>, the <i>kalpin</i> to the Tretā, the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अधि">adhi</a>-kalpin</i> to the <a class="vei_120a" href="द्वापर">dvāpara</a>, the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सभा-स्थाणु">sabhā-sthāṇu</a></i> to the Āskanda. The lists in the parallel version of the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> are <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कितव">kitava</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सभाविन्">sabhāvin</a>, ādinavadarśa, bahiḥ-<a class="vei_120a" href="सद्">sad</a>,</i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सभा-स्थाणु">sabhā-sthāṇu</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1205">18</a></sup> and Aksarāja, <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत">kṛta</a>, Tretā, <a class="vei_120a" href="द्वापर">dvāpara</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="कलि">kali</a>. From the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1205">19</a></sup> it appears that another name of <a class="vei_120a" href="कलि">kali</a> was Abhibhū, and the parallel lists in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> and Vājasaneyi Saṃhitās suggest that Abhibhū and Akṣarāja are identical, though both appear in the late <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> list. The names of some of these throws go back even to the Rigveda and the Atharvaveda. <a class="vei_120a" href="कलि">kali</a> occurs in the latter,<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1205">20</a></sup> and Lüders<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1205">21</a></sup> shows that in a considerable number of passages in the former <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत">kṛta</a> means a ‘throw’ (not ‘a stake’<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1205">22</a></sup> or ‘what is won’<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1205">23</a></sup> and this sense is clearly found in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1205">24</a></sup> Moreover, that there were more throws (<i>ayāḥ</i>) than one is proved by a passage in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1205">25</a></sup> when the gods are compared to throws as giving or destroying wealth.</p><p>The nature of the throws is obscure. The St. Petersburg Dictionary conjectures that the names given above were applied either to dice marked 4, 3, 2, or 1, or to the sides of the dice so marked, and the latter interpretation is supported by some late commentators.<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_1205">26</a></sup> But there is no evidence for the former interpretation, and, as regards the latter, the shape of the Vibhīdaka nuts,<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_1205">27</a></sup> used as dice, forbids any side being properly on the top. Light is thrown on the expressions by the descrip tion of a ritual game<sup id="ref_120_28_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_1205">28</a></sup> at the Agnyādheya and at the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a> ceremonies. The details are not certain,<sup id="ref_120_29_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29_vei_1205">29</a></sup> but it is clear that the game consisted in securing even numbers of dice, usually a number divisible by four, the <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत">kṛta</a>, the other three throws then being the Tretā, when three remained over after division by four; the <a class="vei_120a" href="द्वापर">dvāpara</a>, when two was the remainder; and the <a class="vei_120a" href="कलि">kali</a>, when one remained. If five were the dividing number, then the throw which showed no remainder was <a class="vei_120a" href="कलि">kali</a>, the <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत">kṛta</a> was that when four was left, and so on. The dice had no numerals marked on them, the only question being what was the total number of the dice themselves.</p><p>There is no reason to doubt that the game as played in the Rigveda was based on the same principle, though the details must remain doubtful. The number of dice used was certainly large,<sup id="ref_120_30_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_30_vei_1205">30</a></sup> and the reference to throwing fours,<sup id="ref_120_31_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_31_vei_1205">31</a></sup> and losing by one, points to the use of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत">kṛta</a> as the winning throw. The Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_32_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_32_vei_1205">32</a></sup> on the other hand, possibly knew of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कलि">kali</a> as the winning throw. In one respect the ordinary game must have differed from the ritual game. In the latter the players merely pick out the number of dice required--no doubt to avoid ominous errors, such as must have happened if a real game had been played. In the secular game the dice were thrown,<sup id="ref_120_33_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_33_vei_1205">33</a></sup> perhaps on the principle suggested by Lüders:<sup id="ref_120_34_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_34_vei_1205">34</a></sup> the one throwing a certain number on the place of playing, and the other then throwing a number to make up with those already thrown a multiple of four or five. This theory, at any rate, accounts for the later stress laid on the power of computation in a player, as in the Nala.</p><p>No board appears to have been used, but a depression on which  the dice were thrown (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अधि-देवन">adhi-devana</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="देवन">devana</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_35_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_35_vei_1205">35</a></sup> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="इरिण">iriṇa</a></i><sup id="ref_120_36_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_36_vei_1205">36</a></sup> ), was made in the ground. No dice box was used, but reference is made to a case for keeping dice in (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वपन">vapana</a></i><sup id="ref_120_37_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_37_vei_1205">37</a></sup> The throw was called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्रह">graha</a></i><sup id="ref_120_38_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_38_vei_1205">38</a></sup> or earlier <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राभ">grābha</a>.</i><sup id="ref_120_39_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_39_vei_1205">39</a></sup> The stake is called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="विज्">vij</a>.</i><sup id="ref_120_40_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_40_vei_1205">40</a></sup> Serious losses could be made at dicing: in the Rigveda a dicer laments the loss of all his property, including his wife.<sup id="ref_120_41_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_41_vei_1205">41</a></sup> Lüders<sup id="ref_120_42_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_42_vei_1205">42</a></sup> finds a different form of the game referred to in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_43_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_43_vei_1205">43</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1205">↑</a> vii. 86, 6; x. 34, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1205">↑</a> Av. Paipp. xx. 4, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1205">↑</a> Rv. x. 34, 5; Av. vii. 114, 7; Rv. x. 34, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1205">↑</a> Sāyaṇa on <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 6, 12; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 4, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1205">↑</a> Sāyaṇa, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> and on Rv. i. 41, 9; Mahīdhara on Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x. 28.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1205">↑</a> x. 34, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1205">↑</a> x. 34, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1205">↑</a> See his translation.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1205">↑</a> <i>Über das Rājāsūya,</i> 72.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1205">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 284.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1205">↑</a> Following Sāyaṇa on Rv. x. 34, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1205">↑</a> In his translation.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1205">↑</a> <i>Das Würfelspiel im alten Indien,</i> 25.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1205">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 283.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1205">↑</a> i. 41, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1205">↑</a> iv. 3, 3, 1. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1205">↑</a> xxx. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1205">↑</a> iii. 4, 1, 16. These must be persons conversant with dicing, but the exact sense of the names is unknown.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1205">↑</a> v. 4, 4, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1205">↑</a> vii. 114, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1205">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 43 <i>et seq,</i></li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1205">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1205">↑</a> Grassmann's Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1205">↑</a> vii. 52. See Rv. x. 42, 9 (<i>kṛtaṃ vicinoti</i>); 43, 5; x. 102, 2; v. 60, 1; ix. 97, 58; i. 132, 1; x. 34, 6; i. 100, 9; viii. 19, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1205">↑</a> x. 116, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_1205">↑</a> Ānandagiri on Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1, 4; Nīlakaṇṭha on Mahābhārata, iv. 50, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_1205">↑</a> Lüders, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 18.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_1205">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 8; 9; Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, v, 19, 4; 20, 1, with Rudradatta's note, for the Agnyādheya. Āpastamba, xviii. 18, 16 <i>st seq.,</i> describes the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a> game, and <i>cf.</i> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 4, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 10, 5; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 4, 6; Kātyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 7, 5 <i>et seq.</i> For <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत">kṛta</a> as four, see Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 3, 2, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 11, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_29_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29_vei_1205">↑</a> See Caland, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 62, 123 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_30_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_30_vei_1205">↑</a> Rv. x. 34, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_31_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_31_vei_1205">↑</a> Rv. i. 41, 9. In x. 34, 2, the loss is ascribed to <i>akṣasya ekaparasya,</i> which confirms the explanation of <a class="vei_120a" href="द्वापर">dvāpara</a> given in Pāṇiṇi, ii. 1, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_32_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_32_vei_1205">↑</a> vii. 114, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_33_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_33_vei_1205">↑</a> Rv. x. 34, 1. 8. 9; Av. iv. 38, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_34_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_34_vei_1205">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i> 56.</li><li id="fx_120_35_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_35_vei_1205">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अधिदेवन">adhidevana</a></i> in Av. v. 31, 6; vi. 70, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā; i. 6, 11; iv. 4, 6, etc.; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="देवन">devana</a></i> in Rv. x. 43, 5. The falling of the dice on the ground is referred to in Av. vii. 114, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_36_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_36_vei_1205">↑</a> Rv. x. 34, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_37_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_37_vei_1205">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 1, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_38_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_38_vei_1205">↑</a> Av. iv. 38, 1 <i>et seq.; cf.</i> vii. 114, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_39_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_39_vei_1205">↑</a> Rv. viii. 81, 1; ix. 106, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_40_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_40_vei_1205">↑</a> Rv. i. 92, 10; ii. 12, 5; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="लक्ष">lakṣa</a></i> in ii. 12, 4, and often <i><a class="vei_120a" href="धन">dhana</a>.</i> So Lüders, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 10, n. 5; 62, n. 1. Roth and Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 286, render ‘he makes the dice secretly disappear’ (i. 92, 10).</li><li id="fx_120_41_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_41_vei_1205">↑</a> Rv. x. 34, 2. For cheating at play <i>cf.</i> Rv. v, 85, 8; vii. 86, 6; 104 14; Av. vi. 118.</li><li id="fx_120_42_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_42_vei_1205">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 61.</li><li id="fx_120_43_vei_1205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_43_vei_1205">↑</a> iv. 1, 4; 6. According to Nīlakaṇṭha on Harivaṃśa, ii. 61, 39, the stake was divided into ten parts, and the <a class="vei_120a" href="कलि">kali</a> then took one, the <a class="vei_120a" href="द्वापर">dvāpara</a> three, the Tretā six, and the <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत">kṛta</a> all ten. This explanation seems harsh.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Roth, Gurupūjākaumudī, 1-4; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 283-287; Lüders, <i>Das Würfelspiel im alten Indien;</i> Caland, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 62, 123 <i>et seq.;</i> Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 823 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

अक्ष|अक्ष|అక్ష_౩|అక్ష|ಅಕ್ಷ_೩|ಅಕ್ಷ|akṣa_3|akṣa|akSha_3|akSha|axa_3|axa|_vei_120@6|_vei1_pn005
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@5">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@7">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn002">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn005">1:005</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn006">↷</a><hr><p>3. <b>Akṣa.</b>--In the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vii. 3, 1) this word seems to denote the nut of the Vibhīdaka (<i>Terminalia bellerica</i>).</p></div>

अक्षत|अक्षित|అక్షత|అక్షిత|ಅಕ್ಷತ|ಅಕ್ಷಿತ|akṣata|akṣita|akShata|akShita|axata|axita|_vei_120@7|_vei1_pn005
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@6">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@8">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn002">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn005">1:005</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn006">↷</a><hr><p><b>Akṣata</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्षित">akṣita</a>.</b>--In one passage of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1207">1</a></sup> dealing with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जायान्य">jāyānya</a>,</b> mention is made of a remedy for sores designated both <a class="vei_120a" href="अक्षित">akṣita</a> and Sukṣata, or, according to the reading of the Kauṣika <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अक्षत">akṣata</a> and Sukṣata, while Sāyaṇa has <a class="vei_120a" href="अक्षित">akṣita</a> and Sukṣita. Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1207">2</a></sup> renders ‘not caused by cutting’ and ‘caused by cutting.’ Formerly<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1207">3</a></sup> he suggested ‘tumour’ or ‘boil.’ Whitney<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1207">4</a></sup> thinks that two varieties of <a class="vei_120a" href="जायान्य">jāyānya</a> are meant. Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1207">5</a></sup> reads with Sāyana <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्षित">akṣita</a>,</i> which he renders by ‘not firmly established’ in the invalid. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1207">6</a></sup> finds in it a disease <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत">kṣata</a>. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1207">↑</a> vii. 76, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1207">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 17, 562.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1207">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, cxvii. <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1207">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda 442.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1207">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 500.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1207">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 377.</li></ol></div>

अक्षा-वपन|अक्षावपन|अक्षा|वपन|అక్షా-వపన|అక్షావపన|అక్షా|వపన|ಅಕ್ಷಾ-ವಪನ|ಅಕ್ಷಾವಪನ|ಅಕ್ಷಾ|ವಪನ|akṣā-vapana|akṣāvapana|akṣā|vapana|akShaa-vapana|akShaavapana|akShaa|axaa-vapana|axaavapana|axaa|_vei_120@8|_vei1_pn006
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@7">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@9">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn005">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn006">1:006</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn007">↷</a><hr><p><b>Akṣā-vapana.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a>.</b></p></div>

अक्षु|అక్షు|ಅಕ್ಷು|akṣu|akShu|axu|_vei_120@9|_vei1_pn006
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@8">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@10">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn005">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn006">1:006</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn007">↷</a><hr><p><b>Akṣu.</b>--The word occurs in two passages of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1209">1</a></sup> and one of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1209">2</a></sup> Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1209">3</a></sup> renders it by ‘net,’ while Bo7htlingk<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1209">4</a></sup> suggests ‘axle of a car.’ Geldner<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1209">5</a></sup> sees in it a stake or pole used with a fishermen's net (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="जाल">jāla</a></b>),<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1209">6</a></sup> the pole of a wagon,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1209">7</a></sup> and the pole of a house, whether vertical or horizontal, he leaves uncertain (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1209">8</a></sup> Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1209">9</a></sup> takes it as a covering of wickerwork stretched across a beam and sloping down to both sides--like a thatched roof, and this best explains the epithet ‘thousand-eyed’ (<i>i.e.,</i> with countless holes) ascribed to it. In the other Atharvaveda passage<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1209">10</a></sup> he accepts the sense ‘net,’ and doubts if the word in the Rigveda is not an adjective (<i>a-kṣu</i>) as it is taken by Sāyaṇa. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गृह">gṛha</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1209">↑</a> viii. 8, 18 (<i>akṣujālābhyām</i>); ix. 3, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1209">↑</a> i. 180, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1209">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1209">↑</a> Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1209">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 136.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1209">↑</a> Av. viii. 8, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1209">↑</a> Av. i. 180, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1209">↑</a> ix. 3, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1209">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 598.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1209">↑</a> Av. viii. 8, 18.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 153, 265; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 506, 526; Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 179.</li></ol></div>

अगस्ति|అగస్తి|ಅಗಸ್ತಿ|agasti|_vei_120@10|_vei1_pn006
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@9">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@11">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn005">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn006">1:006</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn007">↷</a><hr><p><b>Agasti.</b>--This form of <b>Agastya's</b> name occurs once in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12010"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12010">1</a></sup> where he appears as a favourite of <a class="vei_120a" href="मित्र">mitra</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12010"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12010">↑</a> iv. 9, 3. <i>Cf.</i> <b>Sieg,</b> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 127, n. 5.</li></ol></div>

अगस्त्य|అగస్త్య|ಅಗಸ್ತ್ಯ|agastya|_vei_120@11|_vei1_pn006
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@10">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@12">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn005">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn006">1:006</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn007">↷</a><hr><p><b>Agastya.</b>--This is the name of a sage, of mythical character, who plays a great part in the later literature. He was a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मान">māna</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12011">1</a></sup> and therefore is called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मान्य">mānya</a></b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12011">2</a></sup> and son of <a class="vei_120a" href="मान">māna</a>, and only once is there a reference<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12011">3</a></sup> to the legend prevalent in latter times that he was a son of <a class="vei_120a" href="मित्र">mitra</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a>.</p><p>His greatest feat was the reconciliation of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> and the Maruts after <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> had been annoyed at his proposing to give the Maruts an offering to the exclusion of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>. This feat is the subject of three hymns of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12011">4</a></sup> and is often referred  to in the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_12011">5</a></sup> though the exact details and significance of the legend are variously treated by Oldenberg,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_12011">6</a></sup> Sieg,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_12011">7</a></sup> Hertel,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_12011">8</a></sup> and von Schroeder.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_12011">9</a></sup></p><p>He also appears in a strange dialogue with <a class="vei_120a" href="लोपामुद्रा">lopāmudrā</a> in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_12011">10</a></sup> which appears to show him as an ascetic who finally yields to temptation. Von <b>Schroeder</b><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_12011">11</a></sup> regards it as a ritual drama of vegetation magic.</p><p>In another passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_12011">12</a></sup> he appears as helping in the Aśvins’ gift of a leg to <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्पला">viśpalā</a>. Sāyaṇa holds that be was the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="खेल">khela</a>,</b> and Sieg<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_12011">13</a></sup> accepts this view, while Pischel<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_12011">14</a></sup> thinks that <a class="vei_120a" href="खेल">khela</a> is a deity, Vivasvant.</p><p>Geldner<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_12011">15</a></sup> shows from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_12011">16</a></sup> that <a class="vei_120a" href="अगस्त्य">agastya</a>, as brother of <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>--both being miraculous sons of <a class="vei_120a" href="मित्र">mitra</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a> -introduces <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a></b> to the <b>Tṛtsus.</b> There are two other references to <a class="vei_120a" href="अगस्त्य">agastya</a> in the Rigveda, the one<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_12011">17</a></sup> including him in a long list of persons, the other alluding to his sister's sons (<i>nadbhyaḥ</i>),<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_12011">18</a></sup> apparently <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बन्धु">bandhu</a>,</b> etc. In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_12011">19</a></sup> he appears as connected with witchcraft, and in a long list of sages.<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_12011">20</a></sup> In the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_12011">21</a></sup> cows, with a peculiar mark on their ears (<i>viṣṭya-karṇyaḥ</i>), are associated with him.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12011">↑</a> Rv. vii. 33, 10 (<a class="vei_120a" href="अगस्त्य">agastya</a>), 13 (<a class="vei_120a" href="मान">māna</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12011">↑</a> Rv. i. 165, 15 = 166, 15 = 167, 11 = 168, 10; 165, 14; 177, 5; 184, 4 (<a class="vei_120a" href="मान्य">mānya</a>); i. 189, 8; 117, 11 (<i>Mānasya <a class="vei_120a" href="सूनु">sūnu</a></i>).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12011">↑</a> Rv. vii. 33, 13. <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 138 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12011">↑</a> Rv. i. 165; 170; 171.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_12011">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 5, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 11, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, x. 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxi. 14, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxvi. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_12011">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 39, 60 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_12011">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 108-119.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_12011">↑</a> <i>Vienna Oriental Journal,</i> 18, 152-154.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_12011">↑</a> <i>Mysterium und Mimus im Rigveda,</i> 91 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_12011">↑</a> i. 179. <i>Cf.</i> Sieg, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 120-126; Oldenberg, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 66-68.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_12011">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 156-172.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_12011">↑</a> i. 117, 11; <i>cf.</i> i. 116, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_12011">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 128.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_12011">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 171-173.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_12011">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 138, 143.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_12011">↑</a> Rv. vii. 33, 10. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_12011">↑</a> vii. 5, 26. Sieg, 129, suggests that this refers to the <a class="vei_120a" href="खेल">khela</a> legend.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_12011">↑</a> x. 60, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_12011">↑</a> ii. 32, 3; iv. 37, 1. Perhaps for this reason the Rigveda Anukramaṇī ascribes to him (Rv. i. 191) a magic hymn.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_12011">↑</a> xviii. 3, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_12011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_12011">↑</a> iv. 2, 9.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 117; Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 106-129; Macdonell, <i>Bṛhaddevatā,</i> 2, 136 <i>et seq.;</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 221; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 110.</li></ol></div>

अगर|అగర|ಅಗರ|agara|_vei_120@12|_vei1_pn007
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@11">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@13">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn006">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn007">1:007</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn008">↷</a><hr><p><b>Agara.</b>--This rare word is found as ‘house’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12012"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12012">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12012"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12012">↑</a> ii. 15. <i>Cf. āgara,</i> ‘chamber’ (?), n Av. iv. 36, 3; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 407. Agāra occurs also in Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 7, 21.</li></ol></div>

अग्नि-दग्ध|अग्निदग्ध|अग्नि|दग्ध|అగ్ని-దగ్ధ|అగ్నిదగ్ధ|అగ్ని|దగ్ధ|ಅಗ್ನಿ-ದಗ್ಧ|ಅಗ್ನಿದಗ್ಧ|ಅಗ್ನಿ|ದಗ್ಧ|agni-dagdha|agnidagdha|agni|dagdha|_vei_120@13|_vei1_pn008
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@12">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@14">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn007">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn008">1:008</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn009">↷</a><hr><p><b>Agni-dagdha.</b>--This epithet (‘burnt with fire’)<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12013">1</a></sup> applies to the dead who were burned on the funeral pyre. This is one of the two normal methods of disposing of the dead, the other being burial (<i>an-agnidagdhāḥ,</i> ‘not burnt with fire’).<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12013">2</a></sup> The Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12013">3</a></sup> adds two further modes of disposal to those-viz., casting out (<i>paroptāḥ</i>), and the exposure of the dead (<i>uddhitāḥ</i>). The exact sense of these expressions is doubtful. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12013">4</a></sup> considers that the former is a parallel to the Iranian practice of casting out the dead to be devoured by beasts, and that the latter refers to the old who are exposed when helpless.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_12013">5</a></sup> Whitney<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_12013">6</a></sup> refers the latter expression to the exposure of the dead body on a raised platform of some sort.</p><p>Burial was clearly not rare in the Rigvedic period: a whole hymn<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_12013">7</a></sup> describes the ritual attending it. The dead man was buried apparently in full attire, with his bow in his hand, and probably at one time his wife was immolated to accompany him, in accordance with a practice common among savage tribes. But in the Vedic period both customs appear in a modified form: the son takes the bow from the hand of the dead man, and the widow is led away from her dead husband by his brother or other nearest kinsman. A stone is set between the dead and the living to separate them. In the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_12013">8</a></sup> but not in the Rigveda, a coffin (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वृक्ष">vṛkṣa</a></i>) is alluded to. In both Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_12013">9</a></sup> occur other allusions to the ‘house of earth’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="भूमि">bhūmi</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="गृह">gṛha</a></i>). To remove the apparent discrepancy between burning and burial, by assuming that the references to burial are to the burial of the burned bones, as does Oldenberg,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_12013">10</a></sup> is unnecessary and improbable, as burning and burial subsisted side by side in Greece for many years.</p><p>Burning was, however, equally usual, and it grew steadily in frequency, for in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_12013">11</a></sup> the adornment  of the body of the dead with curd (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="आमिक्षा">āmikṣā</a></b>), clothes, and ornaments, in order to win the next world, is referred to as something erroneous and wrong, and in the funeral Mantras of the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_12013">12</a></sup> only burning seems to be contemplated; the verses which refer to burial here really alluding to the burial of the ashes in the burying-ground (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="श्मशान">śmaśāna</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_12013">13</a></sup> The body was wrapped in fat,<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_12013">14</a></sup> as we learn from the funeral hymn in the Rigveda, a goat being apparently burned with it,<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_12013">15</a></sup> to act as a guide on the way to the next world. According to the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_12013">16</a></sup> a draft-ox was burned presumably for the dead to ride with in the next world. It was expected that the dead would revive with his whole body and all his limbs (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सर्व">sarva</a>-tanūḥ sāṅgaḥ</i>),<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_12013">17</a></sup> although it is also said<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_12013">18</a></sup> that the eye goes to the sun, the breath to the wind, and so forth.</p><p>Before burial or burning, the corpse was washed,<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_12013">19</a></sup> a clog (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कूदी">kūdī</a></i>) being tied to the foot to prevent the deceased returning to earth.<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_12013">20</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12013">↑</a> Rv. x. 15, 14; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 1, 7; <i>dagdhāḥ,</i> Av. xviii. 2, 34.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12013">↑</a> Rv., <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> = <i>nikhātāḥ,</i> Av. xviii. 2, 34.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12013">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12013">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 402.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_12013">↑</a> Rv. viii. 51, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_12013">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 841.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_12013">↑</a> x. 18. The interpretation of v. 8 is a famous crux, see <a class="vei_120a" href="पत्नी">patnī</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_12013">↑</a> xviii. 2, 25; 3, 70.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_12013">↑</a> Rv. vii. 89, 1; Av. v. 30, 14; xviii. 2, 52.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_12013">↑</a> <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 571.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_12013">↑</a> viii. 8. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_12013">↑</a> xxxv. <i>Cf.</i> also <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, 80 <i>et seq.,</i> which treats the Atharvaveda hymns, xviii. 1-3, as intended for burning only.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_12013">↑</a> Av. v. 31, 8; x. 1, 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 2, 8, 5; 4, 11, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_12013">↑</a> Rv. x. 16, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_12013">↑</a> Rv. x. 16, 4. But <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अज">aja</a></i> may mean ‘the unborn part,’ as Weber prefers to take it, Proceedings of the Berlin Academy, 1895, 847.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_12013">↑</a> xii. 2, 48.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_12013">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 6, 1, 1; xi. 1, 8, 6; xii. 8, 3, 31. <i>Cf.</i> Av. xi. 3, 32. This fact probably explains the use of <i>śeṣaḥ</i> in Rv. x. 16, 5. The dead enjoy sexual pleasures in the next world; see Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 307, n. 462.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_12013">↑</a> Rv. x. 16, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_12013">↑</a> Av. v. 19, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_12013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_12013">↑</a> Av. v. 19, 12; see Roth, <i>Festgruss an Bo7htlingk,</i> 98; Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 12, 416.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 401407; Roth, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 8, 468 <i>et seq.; Siebenzig Lieder,</i> 150 <i>et seq.;</i> Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 570 <i>et seq.;</i> Caland, <i>Die altindischen Todten- und Bestattungsgebräuche;</i> von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 40-42; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythology,</i> 3, 413423; <i>Rituallitteratur,</i> 87 <i>et seq.;</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> 165, 166; Proceedings of the Berlin Academy, 1895, 815 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

अग्नि-भू काश्यप|अग्निभू काश्यप|अग्नि|भू काश्यप|అగ్ని-భూ కాశ్యప|అగ్నిభూ కాశ్యప|అగ్ని|భూ కాశ్యప|ಅಗ್ನಿ-ಭೂ ಕಾಶ್ಯಪ|ಅಗ್ನಿಭೂ ಕಾಶ್ಯಪ|ಅಗ್ನಿ|ಭೂ ಕಾಶ್ಯಪ|agni-bhū kāśyapa|agnibhū kāśyapa|agni|bhū kāśyapa|agni-bhuu kaashyapa|agnibhuu kaashyapa|bhuu kaashyapa|agni-bhoo kaashyapa|agnibhoo kaashyapa|bhoo kaashyapa|_vei_120@14|_vei1_pn009
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@13">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@15">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn008">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn009">1:009</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn010">↷</a><hr><p><b>Agni-<a class="vei_120a" href="भू">bhū</a> Kāśyapa</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12014"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12014">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12014"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12014">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 374.</li></ol></div>

अग्नि-शाल|अग्निशाल|अग्नि|शाल|అగ్ని-శాల|అగ్నిశాల|అగ్ని|శాల|ಅಗ್ನಿ-ಶಾಲ|ಅಗ್ನಿಶಾಲ|ಅಗ್ನಿ|ಶಾಲ|agni-śāla|agniśāla|agni|śāla|agni-shaala|agnishaala|shaala|_vei_120@15|_vei1_pn009
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@14">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@16">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn008">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn009">1:009</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn010">↷</a><hr><p><b>Agni-śāla.</b>--This term, which designates part of the sacrificial  apparatus,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12015"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12015">1</a></sup> is applied in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12015"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12015">2</a></sup> to a part of an ordinary house, presumably the central hall where the fireplace was.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12015"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12015">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12015"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12015">↑</a> ix. 3, 7.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns. of the Atharvaveda,</i> 598; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 154.</li></ol></div>

अघा|అఘా|ಅಘಾ|aghā|aghaa|_vei_120@16|_vei1_pn010
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@15">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@17">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn009">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn010">1:010</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn011">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aghā.</b>--In the wedding hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12016"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12016">1</a></sup> it is said that cows are slain in the Aghās, and the wedding takes place at the <b>Arjunīs</b> (dual). The Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12016"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12016">2</a></sup> has the ordinary <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मघास्">maghās</a></b> instead. It is impossible to resist the conclusion that the reading of the Rigveda was deliberately altered because of the connection of the slaughter of kine with sin (<i>agha</i>)--possibly, too, with a further desire to emphasize the contrast with <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अघ्न्या">aghnyā</a>,</i> a name for ‘cow.’ Moreover, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12016"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12016">3</a></sup> occurs the formula ‘Svāhā to the <a class="vei_120a" href="मघास्">maghās</a>, Svāhā to the Anaghās.’ See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12016"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12016">↑</a> x. 85, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12016"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12016">↑</a> xiv. 1, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12016"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12016">↑</a> iii. 1, 4, 8.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 364; Proceedings of the Berlin Academy, 1894, 804; Jacobi, <i>Festgruss an Roth,</i> 69; Winternitz, <i>Das altindische Hochzeitsrituell,</i> 32; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 742; Thibaut, <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 24, 95.</li></ol></div>

अघाश्व|అఘాశ్వ|ಅಘಾಶ್ವ|aghāśva|aghaashva|_vei_120@17|_vei1_pn010
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@16">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@18">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn009">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn010">1:010</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn011">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aghāśva.</b>--The name of a serpent in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12017"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12017">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12017"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12017">↑</a> x. 4, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 95.</li></ol></div>

अ-घ्न्या|अघ्न्या|घ्न्या|అ-ఘ్న్యా|అఘ్న్యా|ఘ్న్యా|ಅ-ಘ್ನ್ಯಾ|ಅಘ್ನ್ಯಾ|ಘ್ನ್ಯಾ|a-ghnyā|aghnyā|ghnyā|a-ghnyaa|aghnyaa|ghnyaa|_vei_120@18|_vei1_pn010
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@17">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@19">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn009">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn010">1:010</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn011">↷</a><hr><p><b>A-ghnyā.</b>--See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मांस">māṃsa</a>.</b></p></div>

अंक|अङ्क|అంక|అఙ్క|ಅಂಕ|ಅಙ್ಕ|aṅka|aMka|anka|_vei_120@19|_vei1_pn010
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@18">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@20">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn009">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn010">1:010</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn011">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aṅka.</b>--The <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12019">1</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12019">2</a></sup> refer to two Aṅkas and two Nyaṅkas as parts of a chariot. The meaning of these terms is quite obscure. The commentators refer them to the sides or wheels. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12019">3</a></sup> compares the Greek <sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12019">4</a></sup> and thinks that the Aṅkau were the upper border of the body of the chariot (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कोश">kośa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वन्धुर">vandhura</a></i>), and the Nyaṅkau the lower rims for greater security. Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_12019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_12019">5</a></sup> confesses that the exact sense is impossible to make out, but considers that the terms at once refer to parts of the chariot and to divinities, while Bo7htlingk<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_12019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_12019">6</a></sup> takes the term as referring to divinities alone. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12019">↑</a> i. 7, 7, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12019">↑</a> ii. 7, 8, 1. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12019">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 251, 252.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12019">↑</a> <i>Iliad,</i> v. 728. <i>Cf.</i> Smith's <i>Dictionary of Antiquities,</i> 1, 578.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_12019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_12019">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 29, 364, on Pāraskara <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 14, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_12019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_12019">↑</a> Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

अंग|अङ्ग|అంగ|అఙ్గ|ಅಂಗ|ಅಙ್ಗ|aṅga|aMga|anga|_vei_120@20|_vei1_pn011
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@19">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@21">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn010">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn011">1:011</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn012">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aṅga.</b>--The name occurs only once in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12020">1</a></sup> in connection with the <b>Gandhāris, Mūjavants,</b> and <b>Magadhas,</b> as distinct peoples. They appear also in the Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12020">2</a></sup> in the compound name <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अंग">aṅga</a>-magadhāḥ.</i> As in later times they were settled on the Sone and Ganges,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12020">3</a></sup> their earlier seat was presumably there also. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वंग">vaṅga</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12020">↑</a> v. 22, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12020">↑</a> ii. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12020">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 35; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 446, 449; Pargiter, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 852, inclines to regard them as a non-Aryan people that came over-sea to Eastern India. There is nothing in the Vedic literature to throw light on this hypothesis.</li></ol></div>

अंग वैरोचन|अङ्ग वैरोचन|అంగ వైరోచన|అఙ్గ వైరోచన|ಅಂಗ ವೈರೋಚನ|ಅಙ್ಗ ವೈರೋಚನ|aṅga vairocana|aMga vairochana|anga vairochana|_vei_120@21|_vei1_pn011
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@20">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@22">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn010">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn011">1:011</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn012">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aṅga Vairocana.</b>--He is included in the list of anointed kings in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12021"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12021">1</a></sup> His <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> was <b>Udamaya,</b> an <a class="vei_120a" href="आत्रेय">ātreya</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12021"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12021">↑</a> viii. 22. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 214.</li></ol></div>

अंगारावक्षयण|अङ्गारावक्षयण|అంగారావక్షయణ|అఙ్గారావక్షయణ|ಅಂಗಾರಾವಕ್ಷಯಣ|ಅಙ್ಗಾರಾವಕ್ಷಯಣ|aṅgārāvakṣayaṇa|aMgaaraavakShayaNa|angaaraavakShayaNa|aMgaaraavaxayaNa|_vei_120@22|_vei1_pn011
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@21">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@23">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn010">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn011">1:011</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn012">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aṅgārāvakṣayaṇa.</b>--A word of doubtful meaning found in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12022"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12022">1</a></sup> It is rendered ‘tongs’ by Max Müller and Bo7htlingk in their translations. The St. Petersburg Dictionary explains it as ‘a vessel in which coals are extinguished,’ and Monier-Williams as ‘an instrument for extinguishing coals.’ The smaller St. Petersburg Dictionary renders the word ‘coal-shovel or tongs.’ <i>Cf.</i> <b>Ulmukāvakṣayaṇa.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12022"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12022">↑</a> iii. 9, 18.</li></ol></div>

अंगिरस्|अंगिर|अङ्गिरस्|अङ्गिर|అంగిరస్|అంగిర|అఙ్గిరస్|అఙ్గిర|ಅಂಗಿರಸ್|ಅಂಗಿರ|ಅಙ್ಗಿರಸ್|ಅಙ್ಗಿರ|aṅgiras|aṅgira|aMgiras|aMgira|angiras|angira|_vei_120@23|_vei1_pn011
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@22">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@24">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn010">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn011">1:011</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn012">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aṅgiras.</b>--The Aṅgirases appear in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12023">1</a></sup> as semimythical beings, and no really historical character can be assigned even to those passages<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12023">2</a></sup> which recognize a father of the race, <a class="vei_120a" href="अंगिरस्">aṅgiras</a>. Later, however, there were definite families of Aṅgirases, to whose ritual practices (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अयन">ayana</a>, dvirātra</i>) references are made.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12023">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12023">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> ii. 156-169.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12023">↑</a> Rv. i. 45, 3; 139, 9; iii. 31, 7, etc.; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 2, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12023">↑</a> Av. xviii. 4, 8, but this may be mythical; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xx. 11, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 1, 4, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> 142, 143.</li></ol></div>

अंगुष्ठ|अङ्गुष्ठ|అంగుష్ఠ|అఙ్గుష్ఠ|ಅಂಗುಷ್ಠ|ಅಙ್ಗುಷ್ಠ|aṅguṣṭha|aMguShTha|anguShTha|_vei_120@24|_vei1_pn011
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@23">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@25">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn010">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn011">1:011</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn012">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aṅguṣṭha.</b>--As a measure of size this word appears in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iv. 12; vi. 17). </p></div>

अ-च्युत्|अच्युत्|च्युत्|అ-చ్యుత్|అచ్యుత్|చ్యుత్|ಅ-ಚ್ಯುತ್|ಅಚ್ಯುತ್|ಚ್ಯುತ್|a-cyut|acyut|cyut|a-chyut|achyut|chyut|_vei_120@25|_vei1_pn012
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@24">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@26">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn011">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn012">1:012</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn013">↷</a><hr><p><b>A-cyut.</b>--He acted as <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतिहर्तृ">pratihartṛ</a> at the Sattra celebrated by the Vibhindukīyas and described in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12025"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12025">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12025"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12025">↑</a> iii. 233. See <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 38.</li></ol></div>

अज|अजा|అజ|అజా|ಅಜ|ಅಜಾ|aja|ajā|ajaa|_vei_120@26|_vei1_pn012
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@25">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@27">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn011">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn012">1:012</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn013">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aja, Ajā.</b>--This is the ordinary name for goat in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12026"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12026">1</a></sup> and the later literature. The goat is also called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बस्त">basta</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="छाग">chāga</a>, Chagala.</b> Goats and sheep (<i>ajāvayaḥ</i>) are very frequently mentioned together.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12026"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12026">2</a></sup> The female goat is spoken of as producing two or three kids,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12026"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12026">3</a></sup> and goat's milk is well known.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12026"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12026">4</a></sup> The goat as representative of Pūṣan plays an important part in the ritual of burial.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_12026"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_12026">5</a></sup> The occupation of a goatherd (<i>ajapāla</i>) was a recognized one, being distinguished from that of a cowherd and of a shepherd.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_12026"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_12026">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12026"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12026">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अज">aja</a></i> in Rv. x. 16, 4; i. 162, 2. 4; Av. ix. 5, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxi. 9, etc.; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अजा">ajā</a></i> in Rv. viii. 70, 15; Av. vi. 71, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii., 56, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12026"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12026">↑</a> Rv. x. 90, 10; Av. viii. 7, 25; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iii. 43, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12026"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12026">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 5, 10, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12026"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12026">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 1, 6, 1; v. 1, 7, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 364, n. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_12026"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_12026">↑</a> Rv. x. 16, 4, etc. <i>Cf.</i> p. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_12026"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_12026">↑</a> Vājasaneyī Saṃhitā, xxx. 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 9, 1.</li></ol></div>

अज|అజ|ಅಜ|aja|_vei_120@27|_vei1_pn012
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@26">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@28">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn011">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn012">1:012</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn013">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aja.</b>--The Ajas are named in one verse of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12027">1</a></sup> as having been defeated by the <b>Tṛtsus</b> under <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>.</b> They are there mentioned with the <b>Yakṣus</b> and <b>Śigrus,</b> and Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12027">2</a></sup> conjectures that they formed part of a confederacy under <a class="vei_120a" href="भेद">bheda</a> against <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>. The name has been regarded as a sign of totemism,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12027">3</a></sup> but this is very uncertain, and it is impossible to say if they were or were not Āryans.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12027">↑</a> vii. 18. 19.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12027">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 127, <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 173.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12027">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> 153; Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1907, 929; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, 200, 21; Risley, <i>Peoples of India,</i> 83 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

अजकव|అజకవ|ಅಜಕವ|ajakava|_vei_120@28|_vei1_pn012
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@27">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@29">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn011">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn012">1:012</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn013">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ajakava.</b>--This name of a poisonous scorpion occurs once in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12028">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12028">↑</a> vii. 50, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 99.</li></ol></div>

अज-गर|अजगर|अज|गर|అజ-గర|అజగర|అజ|గర|ಅಜ-ಗರ|ಅಜಗರ|ಅಜ|ಗರ|aja-gara|ajagara|aja|gara|_vei_120@29|_vei1_pn012
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@28">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@30">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn011">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn012">1:012</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn013">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aja-gara</b> (‘goat-swallower’) occurs in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12029"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12029">1</a></sup> and in the list of animals at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12029"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12029">2</a></sup> or horse sacrifice, as the  name of the boa-constrictor. Elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12029"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12029">3</a></sup> it is called <a class="vei_120a" href="वाहस">vāhasa</a>. It denotes a person at the snake feast in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12029"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12029">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12029"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12029">↑</a> xi. 2, 25; xx. 129, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12029"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12029">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā 5, 14, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii, 14, 19; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 38.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12029"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12029">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 13, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 34.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12029"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12029">↑</a> xxv. 15, in the form of Ajagāva, with which <i>cf.</i> <b>Ajakāva.</b></li></ol></div>

अज-मीढ|अजमीढ|अज|मीढ|అజ-మీఢ|అజమీఢ|అజ|మీఢ|ಅಜ-ಮೀಢ|ಅಜಮೀಢ|ಅಜ|ಮೀಢ|aja-mīḍha|ajamīḍha|aja|mīḍha|aja-miiDha|ajamiiDha|miiDha|aja-meeDha|ajameeDha|meeDha|_vei_120@30|_vei1_pn013
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@29">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@31">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn012">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn013">1:013</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn014">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aja-mīḍha.</b>--The Ājamīḍhas, or descendants of <a class="vei_120a" href="अजमीढ">ajamīḍha</a>, are referred to in a hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12030"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12030">1</a></sup> Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12030"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12030">2</a></sup> and Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12030"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12030">3</a></sup> deduce from the use of this patronymic that <a class="vei_120a" href="अजमीढ">ajamīḍha</a> was the seer of that hymn.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12030"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12030">↑</a> iv. 44, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12030"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12030">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 123, 135.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12030"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12030">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenlāndischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 215.</li></ol></div>

अज-शृंगी|अज-शृंगि|अजशृंगी|अजशृंगि|अज|शृंगी|शृंगि|अज-शृङ्गी|अज-शृङ्गि|अजशृङ्गी|अजशृङ्गि|शृङ्गी|शृङ्गि|అజ-శృంగీ|అజ-శృంగి|అజశృంగీ|అజశృంగి|అజ|శృంగీ|శృంగి|అజ-శృఙ్గీ|అజ-శృఙ్గి|అజశృఙ్గీ|అజశృఙ్గి|శృఙ్గీ|శృఙ్గి|ಅಜ-ಶೃಂಗೀ|ಅಜ-ಶೃಂಗಿ|ಅಜಶೃಂಗೀ|ಅಜಶೃಂಗಿ|ಅಜ|ಶೃಂಗೀ|ಶೃಂಗಿ|ಅಜ-ಶೃಙ್ಗೀ|ಅಜ-ಶೃಙ್ಗಿ|ಅಜಶೃಙ್ಗೀ|ಅಜಶೃಙ್ಗಿ|ಶೃಙ್ಗೀ|ಶೃಙ್ಗಿ|aja-śṛṅgī|aja-śṛṅgi|ajaśṛṅgī|ajaśṛṅgi|aja|śṛṅgī|śṛṅgi|aja-shRMgii|aja-shRMgi|ajashRMgii|ajashRMgi|shRMgii|shRMgi|aja-shRMgee|ajashRMgee|shRMgee|aja-shRngii|aja-shRngi|ajashRngii|ajashRngi|shRngii|shRngi|aja-shRngee|ajashRngee|shRngee|aja-shRiMgii|aja-shRiMgi|ajashRiMgii|ajashRiMgi|shRiMgii|shRiMgi|aja-shRuMgii|aja-shRuMgi|ajashRuMgii|ajashRuMgi|shRuMgii|shRuMgi|aja-shRRiMgii|aja-shRRiMgi|ajashRRiMgii|ajashRRiMgi|shRRiMgii|shRRiMgi|aja-shRingii|aja-shRingi|ajashRingii|ajashRingi|shRingii|shRingi|aja-shRungii|aja-shRungi|ajashRungii|ajashRungi|shRungii|shRungi|aja-shRRingii|aja-shRRingi|ajashRRingii|ajashRRingi|shRRingii|shRRingi|_vei_120@31|_vei1_pn013
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@30">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@32">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn012">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn013">1:013</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn014">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aja-śṛṅgī.</b>--This plant (‘goat's horn’), equated by the commentator with <a class="vei_120a" href="विषाणिन्">viṣāṇin</a> (the <i>Odina pinnata</i>), is celebrated as a demon-destroyer in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12031"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12031">1</a></sup> Its other name is <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अराटकी">arāṭakī</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12031"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12031">2</a></sup> Weber<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12031"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12031">3</a></sup> suggests that it is the <i>Prosopis spicigera</i> or <i>Mimosa suma.</i></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12031"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12031">↑</a> iv. 37.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12031"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12031">↑</a> iv. 37, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12031"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12031">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 144. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 408, 409; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 68; Caland, <i>Altindisches Zauberritual,</i> 89.</li></ol></div>

अजात-शत्रु|अजातशत्रु|अजात|शत्रु|అజాత-శత్రు|అజాతశత్రు|అజాత|శత్రు|ಅಜಾತ-ಶತ್ರು|ಅಜಾತಶತ್ರು|ಅಜಾತ|ಶತ್ರು|ajāta-śatru|ajātaśatru|ajāta|śatru|ajaata-shatru|ajaatashatru|ajaata|shatru|_vei_120@32|_vei1_pn013
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@31">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@33">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn012">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn013">1:013</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn014">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ajāta-śatru.</b>--He is mentioned as a King of Kāśī (<a class="vei_120a" href="काश्य">kāśya</a>) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12032"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12032">1</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12032"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12032">2</a></sup> Upaniṣads, where he instructs the proud <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="बालाकि">bālāki</a> as to the real nature of the self. He is not to be identified with the Ajātasattu of the Buddhist texts.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12032"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12032">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12032"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12032">↑</a> ii. 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12032"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12032">↑</a> iv. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12032"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12032">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 213; Hoernle, <i>Osteology,</i> 106; Keith, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 62, 138.</li></ol></div>

अज्ञात-यक्ष्म|अज्ञातयक्ष्म|अज्ञात|यक्ष्म|అజ్ఞాత-యక్ష్మ|అజ్ఞాతయక్ష్మ|అజ్ఞాత|యక్ష్మ|ಅಜ್ಞಾತ-ಯಕ್ಷ್ಮ|ಅಜ್ಞಾತಯಕ್ಷ್ಮ|ಅಜ್ಞಾತ|ಯಕ್ಷ್ಮ|ajñāta-yakṣma|ajñātayakṣma|ajñāta|yakṣma|aj~naata-yakShma|aj~naatayakShma|aj~naata|yakShma|aj~naata-yaxma|aj~naatayaxma|yaxma|_vei_120@33|_vei1_pn013
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@32">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@34">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn012">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn013">1:013</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn014">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ajñāta-yakṣma,</b> the ‘unknown sickness,’ is mentioned in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12033">1</a></sup> Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12033">2</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12033">3</a></sup> It is referred to in connection with <a class="vei_120a" href="राजयक्ष्म">rājayakṣma</a>. Grohmann<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12033">4</a></sup> thinks that the two are different forms of disease, hypertrophy and atrophy, the purpose of the spell in the Rigveda being thus the removal of all disease. From the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_12033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_12033">5</a></sup> he deduces its identity with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बलास">balāsa</a>.</b> Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_12033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_12033">6</a></sup> however, points out that this  conclusion is unjustified, leaving the disease unidentified, which seems to accord with its name.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12033">↑</a> x. 161, 1 = Av. iii. 11, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12033">↑</a> vi. 127, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12033">↑</a> xiii. 16.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12033">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 400.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_12033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_12033">↑</a> vi. 127. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_12033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_12033">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 377, 378.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield. <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 342; Atharvaveda, 60; Jolly, <i>Medicin</i> (in Bühler's Encyclopædia), 89.</li></ol></div>

अजिन|అజిన|ಅಜಿನ|ajina|_vei_120@34|_vei1_pn014
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@33">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@35">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn013">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn014">1:014</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn015">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ajina.</b>--This word denotes generally the skin of an animal -<i>e.g.,</i> a gazelle,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12034">1</a></sup> as well as that of a goat (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अज">aja</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12034">2</a></sup> The use of skins as clothing is shown by the adjective ‘clothed in skins’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अजिन">ajina</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वासिन्">vāsin</a></i>) in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12034">3</a></sup> and the furrier's trade is mentioned in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12034">4</a></sup> The Maruts also wear deer-skins,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_12034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_12034">5</a></sup> and the wild ascetics (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="मुनि">muni</a></i>) of a late Rigveda hymn<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_12034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_12034">6</a></sup> seem to be clad in skins (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="मल">mala</a></b>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12034">↑</a> Av. v. 21, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12034">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 2, 1, 21. 24.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12034">↑</a> iii. 9, 1, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12034">↑</a> xxx. 15 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अजिन">ajina</a>-saṃdha</i>); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 13, 1 (<i>ajinasaṃdhāya</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_12034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_12034">↑</a> Rv. i. 166, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_12034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_12034">↑</a> x. 136, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 262.</li></ol></div>

अजिर|అజిర|ಅಜಿರ|ajira|_vei_120@35|_vei1_pn014
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@34">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@36">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn013">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn014">1:014</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn015">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ajira.</b>--He was <a class="vei_120a" href="सुब्रह्मण्य">subrahmaṇya</a> priest at the snake festival of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12035"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12035">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12035"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12035">↑</a> xxv. 15. See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 35.</li></ol></div>

अजीगर्त सौयवस|అజీగర్త సౌయవస|ಅಜೀಗರ್ತ ಸೌಯವಸ|ajīgarta sauyavasa|ajiigarta sauyavasa|ajeegarta sauyavasa|_vei_120@36|_vei1_pn014
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@35">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@37">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn013">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn014">1:014</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn015">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ajīgarta Sauyavasa.</b>--This is the name given to the father of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a></b> in the famous legend of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12036"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12036">1</a></sup> perhaps invented for the occasion, as Weber<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12036"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12036">2</a></sup> suggests.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12036"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12036">↑</a> vii. 15; 17. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 19.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12036"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12036">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 460; Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

अ-ज्येयता|अज्येयता|ज्येयता|అ-జ్యేయతా|అజ్యేయతా|జ్యేయతా|ಅ-ಜ್ಯೇಯತಾ|ಅಜ್ಯೇಯತಾ|ಜ್ಯೇಯತಾ|a-jyeyatā|ajyeyatā|jyeyatā|a-jyeyataa|ajyeyataa|jyeyataa|_vei_120@37|_vei1_pn014
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@36">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@38">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn013">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn014">1:014</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn015">↷</a><hr><p><b>A-jyeyatā.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.</b></p></div>

अणीचिन् मौन|అణీచిన్ మౌన|ಅಣೀಚಿನ್ ಮೌನ|aṇīcin mauna|aNiichin mauna|aNeechin mauna|_vei_120@38|_vei1_pn014
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@37">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@39">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn013">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn014">1:014</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn015">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aṇīcin Mauna.</b>--He is mentioned as an authority on ritual, and contemporary with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जाबाल">jābāla</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चित्र">citra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गौश्रायणि">gauśrāyaṇi</a></b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौश्र">gauśra</a>,</b> in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a></b> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12038">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12038">↑</a> xxiii. 5.</li></ol></div>

अणु|అణు|ಅಣು|aṇu|aNu|_vei_120@39|_vei1_pn014
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@38">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@40">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn013">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn014">1:014</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn015">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aṇu.</b>--This is the designation in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12039">1</a></sup> and the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12039">2</a></sup> of a cultivated grain, <b>apparently</b> the <i>Panicum miliaceum.</i></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12039">↑</a> xviii. 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12039">↑</a> vi. 3, 15 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>), where see Dviveda's note.</li></ol></div>

अतिथि|అతిథి|ಅತಿಥಿ|atithi|_vei_120@40|_vei1_pn014
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@39">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@41">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn013">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn014">1:014</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn015">↷</a><hr><p><b>Atithi</b> (‘guest’).--A hymn of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12040">1</a></sup> celebrates in detail the merits of hospitality. The guest should be fed before  the host eats, water should be offered to him, and so forth. The <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12040">2</a></sup> also lays stress on hospitality, using the expression ‘one whose deity is his guest’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अतिथि">atithi</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="देव">deva</a></i>). In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12040">3</a></sup> it is said that only the good are deemed worthy of receiving hospitality. The guest-offering forms a regular part of the ritual,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12040">4</a></sup> and cows were regularly slain in honour of guests.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_12040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_12040">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12040">↑</a> ix. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12040">↑</a> i. 11, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12040">↑</a> i. 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12040">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 3, 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_12040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_12040">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 17, 426; Hillebrandt, <i>Rituallitteratur,</i> 79.</li></ol></div>

अतिथि-ग्व|अतिथिग्व|अतिथि|ग्व|అతిథి-గ్వ|అతిథిగ్వ|అతిథి|గ్వ|ಅತಿಥಿ-ಗ್ವ|ಅತಿಥಿಗ್ವ|ಅತಿಥಿ|ಗ್ವ|atithi-gva|atithigva|atithi|gva|_vei_120@41|_vei1_pn015
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@40">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@42">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn014">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn015">1:015</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn016">↷</a><hr><p><b>Atithi-gva.</b>--This name occurs frequently in the Rigveda, apparently applying, in nearly all cases, to the same king, otherwise called <b>Divodāsa.</b> The identity of the two persons has been denied by Bergaigne,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12041">1</a></sup> but is certainly proved by a number of passages, when the two names occur together,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12041">2</a></sup> in connection with the defeat of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शम्बर">śambara</a>.</b> In other passages<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12041">3</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="अतिथिग्व">atithigva</a> is said to have assisted <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> in slaying <a class="vei_120a" href="पर्णय">parṇaya</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="करञ्ज">karañja</a>. Sometimes he is only vaguely referred to, while once<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_12041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_12041">5</a></sup> he is mentioned as an enemy of <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a>. Again<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_12041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_12041">6</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="अतिथिग्व">atithigva</a> is coupled with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आयु">āyu</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुत्स">kutsa</a></b> as defeated by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तूर्वयाण">tūrvayāṇa</a>.</b></p><p>A different <a class="vei_120a" href="अतिथिग्व">atithigva</a> appears to be referred to in a Dānastuti<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_12041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_12041">7</a></sup> (‘Praise of Gifts’), where his son, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्रोत">indrota</a>,</b> is mentioned.</p><p>Roth<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_12041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_12041">8</a></sup> distinguishes three Atithigvas--the <a class="vei_120a" href="अतिथिग्व">atithigva</a> Divodāsa, the enemy of <a class="vei_120a" href="पर्णय">parṇaya</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="करञ्ज">karañja</a>, and the enemy of <a class="vei_120a" href="तूर्वयाण">tūrvayāṇa</a>. But the various passages can be reconciled, especially if it is admitted that <a class="vei_120a" href="अतिथिग्व">atithigva</a> Divodāsa was already an ancient hero in the earliest hymns, and was becoming almost mythical.</p><p><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12041">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12041">↑</a> <i>Religion Védique,</i> 2, 342 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12041">↑</a> Rv. i. 51, 6; 112, 14; 130, 7; iv. 26, 3; vi. 47, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12041">↑</a> Rv. i. 53, 8; x. 48, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_12041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_12041">↑</a> Rv. vii. 19, 8. There is no ground for assuming the reference here to be to a later <a class="vei_120a" href="अतिथिग्व">atithigva</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_12041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_12041">↑</a> Rv. i. 53, 10; ii. 14, 7; vi. 18, 13; viii. 53, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_12041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_12041">↑</a> Rv. viii. 68, 16. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_12041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_12041">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 123; Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 17, 426, who renders the name ‘presenting cows to guests.’</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12041">↑</a> Rv. vi. 26, 3.</li></ol></div>

अति-धन्वन् शौनक|अतिधन्वन् शौनक|अति|धन्वन् शौनक|అతి-ధన్వన్ శౌనక|అతిధన్వన్ శౌనక|అతి|ధన్వన్ శౌనక|ಅತಿ-ಧನ್ವನ್ ಶೌನಕ|ಅತಿಧನ್ವನ್ ಶೌನಕ|ಅತಿ|ಧನ್ವನ್ ಶೌನಕ|ati-dhanvan śaunaka|atidhanvan śaunaka|ati|dhanvan śaunaka|ati-dhanvan shaunaka|atidhanvan shaunaka|dhanvan shaunaka|_vei_120@42|_vei1_pn015
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@41">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@43">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn014">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn015">1:015</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn016">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ati-<a class="vei_120a" href="धन्वन्">dhanvan</a> Śaunaka.</b>--He is mentioned as a teacher in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12042"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12042">1</a></sup> and the Vaṃsa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12042"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12042">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12042"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12042">↑</a> i. 9, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12042"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12042">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 384.</li></ol></div>

अ-तृणाद|अतृणाद|तृणाद|అ-తృణాద|అతృణాద|తృణాద|ಅ-ತೃಣಾದ|ಅತೃಣಾದ|ತೃಣಾದ|a-tṛṇāda|atṛṇāda|tṛṇāda|a-tRNaada|atRNaada|tRNaada|a-tRiNaada|atRiNaada|tRiNaada|a-tRuNaada|atRuNaada|tRuNaada|a-tRRiNaada|atRRiNaada|tRRiNaada|_vei_120@43|_vei1_pn016
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@42">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@44">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn015">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn016">1:016</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn017">↷</a><hr><p><b>A-tṛṇāda.</b>--This term (‘not eating grass’) was applied, according to the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, to a newborn calf.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12043"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12043">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12043"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12043">↑</a> i. 5, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 268.</li></ol></div>

अत्क|अत्क|అత్క_౧|అత్క|ಅತ್ಕ_೧|ಅತ್ಕ|atka_1|atka|_vei_120@44|_vei1_pn016
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@43">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@45">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn015">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn016">1:016</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn017">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Atka.</b>--This word occurs frequently in the Rigveda, but its sense is doubtful. Roth, Grassmann, Ludwig, Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12044"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12044">1</a></sup> and others render it as ‘garment’ in several passages,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12044"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12044">2</a></sup> when the expressions ‘put on’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="व्या">vyā</a></i> or <i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रति">prati</a> muñc</i>) or ‘put off’ (<i>muñc</i>) are used of it, and when it is said to be ‘woven’ (<i>vyuta</i>)<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12044"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12044">3</a></sup> or ‘wellfitting’ (<i>surabhi</i>).<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12044"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12044">4</a></sup> On the other hand, Pischel<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_12044"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_12044">5</a></sup> denies that this sense occurs, and otherwise explains the passages. He takes the term to mean ‘axe’ in four places.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_12044"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_12044">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12044"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12044">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 262.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12044"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12044">↑</a> i. 95, 7; ii. 35, 14; iv. 18, 5; v. 55, 6; 74, 5; vi. 29, 3; viii. 41, 7; ix. 101, 14; 107, 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>, ii. 1193.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12044"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12044">↑</a> Rv. i. 122, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12044"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12044">↑</a> Rv. vi. 29, 3; x. 123, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_12044"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_12044">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 193-204.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_12044"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_12044">↑</a> Rv. v. 55, 6; vi. 33, 3; x. 49, 3; 99, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 94, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

अत्क|अत्क|అత్క_౨|అత్క|ಅತ್ಕ_೨|ಅತ್ಕ|atka_2|atka|_vei_120@45|_vei1_pn016
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@44">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@46">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn015">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn016">1:016</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn017">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Atka.</b>--In two passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12045"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12045">1</a></sup> this word is regarded as a proper name by Roth, Grassmann, and Ludwig. But Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12045"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12045">2</a></sup> explains it in these passages as the ‘armour of a warrior as a whole,’ and Pischel<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12045"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12045">3</a></sup> thinks that in both cases an ‘axe’ is meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12045"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12045">↑</a> x. 49, 3; 99, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12045"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12045">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 262, 297.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12045"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12045">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 195.</li></ol></div>

अत्य्-अंहस् आरुणि|अत्यंहस् आरुणि|अत्य्|अंहस् आरुणि|అత్య్-అంహస్ ఆరుణి|అత్యంహస్ ఆరుణి|అత్య్|అంహస్ ఆరుణి|ಅತ್ಯ್-ಅಂಹಸ್ ಆರುಣಿ|ಅತ್ಯಂಹಸ್ ಆರುಣಿ|ಅತ್ಯ್|ಅಂಹಸ್ ಆರುಣಿ|aty-aṃhas āruṇi|atyaṃhas āruṇi|aty|aṃhas āruṇi|aty-aMhas aaruNi|atyaMhas aaruNi|aMhas aaruNi|_vei_120@46|_vei1_pn016
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@45">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@47">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn015">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn016">1:016</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn017">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aty-aṃhas Āruṇi.</b>--According to the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 10, 9, 3-5), this teacher sent a pupil to question <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्लक्ष">plakṣa</a> Dayyāṃpati</b> as to the Sāvitra (a form of <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>). For this impertinence his pupil was severely rebuked.</p></div>

अत्य्-अराति जानं-तपि|अत्य्-अराति जानंतपि|अत्य्-अराति जानं|तपि|अत्य्-अराति जानन्तपि|అత్య్-అరాతి జానం-తపి|అత్య్-అరాతి జానంతపి|అత్య్-అరాతి జానం|తపి|అత్య్-అరాతి జానన్తపి|ಅತ್ಯ್-ಅರಾತಿ ಜಾನಂ-ತಪಿ|ಅತ್ಯ್-ಅರಾತಿ ಜಾನಂತಪಿ|ಅತ್ಯ್-ಅರಾತಿ ಜಾನಂ|ತಪಿ|ಅತ್ಯ್-ಅರಾತಿ ಜಾನನ್ತಪಿ|aty-arāti jānaṃ-tapi|aty-arāti jānaṃtapi|aty-arāti jānaṃ|tapi|aty-araati jaanaM-tapi|aty-araati jaanaMtapi|aty-araati jaanaM|aty-araati jaanantapi|_vei_120@47|_vei1_pn016
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@46">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@48">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn015">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn016">1:016</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn017">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aty-arāti <a class="vei_120a" href="जानं">jānaṃ</a>-tapi,</b> though not a prince, was taught the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a> by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वासिष्ठ">vāsiṣṭha</a> Satyahavya,</b> and thereupon conquered the earth. When <a class="vei_120a" href="वासिष्ठ">vāsiṣṭha</a> reminded him of his indebtedness, and claimed a great reward, the warrior replied irascibly that he intended to conquer the <b>Uttara Kurus,</b> and that <a class="vei_120a" href="वासिष्ठ">vāsiṣṭha</a> would then become King of the Earth, Atyarāti himself being his general (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सेना">senā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a></i>). <a class="vei_120a" href="वासिष्ठ">vāsiṣṭha</a> replied that as no mortal man could conquer the Uttara Kurus he was cheated of his reward.  He consequently procured Atyarāti's defeat and death at the hands of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अमित्रतपन शुष्मिण शैब्य">amitratapana śuṣmiṇa śaibya</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12047">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12047">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 23. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 214.</li></ol></div>

अत्रि|అత్రి|ಅತ್ರಿ|atri|_vei_120@48|_vei1_pn017
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@47">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@49">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn016">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn017">1:017</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn018">↷</a><hr><p><b>Atri.</b>--Neither <a class="vei_120a" href="अत्रि">atri</a> himself nor the Atris can claim any historical reality,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12048">1</a></sup> beyond the fact that Maṇḍala V. of the Rigveda is attributed, no doubt correctly, to the family of the Atris.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12048">2</a></sup> The Atris as a family probably stood in close relations with the <b>Priyamedhas</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12048">3</a></sup> and <b>Kaṇvas,</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12048">4</a></sup> perhaps also with the <b>Gotamas</b><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_12048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_12048">5</a></sup> and <b>Kākṣīvatas.</b><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_12048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_12048">6</a></sup> The mention of both the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परुष्णी">paruṣṇī</a></b> and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a></b> in one hymn<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_12048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_12048">7</a></sup> of the fifth Maṇḍala seems to justify the presumption that the family was spread over a wide extent of territory.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12048">↑</a> For <a class="vei_120a" href="अत्रि">atri</a> in the Rigveda, see Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> 145. <i>Cf.</i> also Av. ii. 32, 3; iv. 29, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, iv. 36, etc.; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 2, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12048">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. v. 39, 5; 67, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxiv. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii, 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12048">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 45, 3; 139, 9; viii. 5, 25; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 22.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12048">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 118, 7; v. 41, 4; x. 150, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_12048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_12048">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 183, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_12048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_12048">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. x. 143, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_12048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_12048">↑</a> Rv. v. 52, 9, 17. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 128, 142; Bergaigne, <i>Religion Védique,</i> 2, 469; Oldenber, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 212215; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 310.</li></ol></div>

अथरी|अथरि|అథరీ|అథరి|ಅಥರೀ|ಅಥರಿ|atharī|athari|atharii|atharee|_vei_120@49|_vei1_pn017
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@48">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@50">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn016">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn017">1:017</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn018">↷</a><hr><p><b>Atharī.</b>--This word occurs only in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12049"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12049">1</a></sup> and the sense is doubtful. Roth,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12049"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12049">2</a></sup> followed by most interpreters, renders it ‘point of a lance,’ but Pischel<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12049"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12049">3</a></sup> thinks that it means ‘an elephant.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12049"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12049">↑</a> iv. 6, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12049"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12049">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12049"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12049">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 99.</li></ol></div>

अथर्वन्|అథర్వన్|ಅಥರ್ವನ್|atharvan|_vei_120@50|_vei1_pn017
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@49">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@51">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn016">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn017">1:017</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn018">↷</a><hr><p><b>Atharvan.</b>--The name in the singular denotes the head of a semi-divine family of mythical priests,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12050">1</a></sup> of whom nothing historical can be said. In the plural the family as a whole is meant. In a few places an actual family seems to be referred to. Thus, for instance, they are mentioned as recipients of gifts in the Dānastuti<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12050">2</a></sup> (‘Praise of Gifts’), of <b>Aśvattha's</b>  generosity; their use of milk mingled with honey in the ritual is referred to;<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12050">3</a></sup> and a cow that miscarries (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अव">ava</a>-tokā</i>) from accident is dedicated to the Atharvans, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12050">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12050">↑</a> See Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> 141. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 6, 3, <a class="vei_120a" href="अथर्वन्">atharvan</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दैव">daiva</a> is characteristically the pupil of <a class="vei_120a" href="मृत्यु">mṛtyu</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12050">↑</a> Rv. vi. 47, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12050">↑</a> Rv. ix. 11, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12050">↑</a> iii. 4, 11, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 15.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> xxxv. <i>et seq.,</i> who (p. xxxviii) takes <i>avatokā</i> as a woman, and the Atharvans as the hymns; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 174 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

अथर्वाणः|అథర్వాణః|ಅಥರ್ವಾಣಃ|atharvāṇaḥ|atharvaaNaH|_vei_120@51|_vei1_pn018
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@50">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@52">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn017">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn018">1:018</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn019">↷</a><hr><p><b>Atharvāṇaḥ.</b>--This expression<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12051"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12051">1</a></sup> is used with <b>Aṅgirasaḥ,</b> to denote the Atharvaveda. The compound <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अथर्वांगिरसः">atharvāṅgirasaḥ</a></b> is employed in the same sense.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12051"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12051">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 12, 9, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvi. 10, 10; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 3, 5 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

अथर्वांगिरसः|अथर्वाङ्गिरसः|అథర్వాంగిరసః|అథర్వాఙ్గిరసః|ಅಥರ್ವಾಂಗಿರಸಃ|ಅಥರ್ವಾಙ್ಗಿರಸಃ|atharvāṅgirasaḥ|atharvaaMgirasaH|atharvaangirasaH|_vei_120@52|_vei1_pn018
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@51">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@53">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn017">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn018">1:018</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn019">↷</a><hr><p><b>Atharvāṅgirasaḥ.</b>--This is the collective name of the Atharvaveda in several passages<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12052">1</a></sup> of the later Brāhmaṇas. It occurs once in the Atharvaveda itself,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12052">2</a></sup> while the term Atharvaveda is not found before the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> period.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12052">3</a></sup> The compound seems, according to Bloomfield,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12052">4</a></sup> to denote the two elements which make up the Atharvaveda. The former part refers to the auspicious practices of the <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a> (<i>bheṣajāni</i>);<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_12052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_12052">5</a></sup> the latter to its hostile witchcraft, the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="यातु">yātu</a></i><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_12052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_12052">6</a></sup> or <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अभि">abhi</a>-cāra.</i><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_12052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_12052">7</a></sup> This theory is supported by the names of the two mythic personages <b><a class="vei_120a" href="घोर आंगिरस">ghora āṅgirasa</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भिषज् आथर्वण">bhiṣaj ātharvaṇa</a>,</b> as well as by the connection of <a class="vei_120a" href="अथर्वाणः">atharvāṇaḥ</a> and <b>Ātharvaṇāni</b> with healing (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="भेषज">bheṣaja</a></i>) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_12052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_12052">8</a></sup> Moreover, the term <i>bhesajā</i> (‘remedies’) designates in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_12052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_12052">9</a></sup> that <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a> itself, while in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_12052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_12052">10</a></sup> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="यातु">yātu</a></i> (‘sorcery’) conveys the same meaning. The evidence, however, being by no means convincing, it remains probable that there existed no clear differentiation between the two sages as responsible for the Atharvaveda as a whole. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12052">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 12, 8, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 9; 10; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 6, 7; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 4, 10; iv. 1, 2; 5, 11; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 4, 1. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12052">↑</a> x. 7, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12052">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 2, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12052">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 11, 387 <i>et seq.; Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> xviii. <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_12052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_12052">↑</a> Av. xi. 6, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_12052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_12052">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 5, 2, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_12052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_12052">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, 3, 19.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_12052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_12052">↑</a> xii. 9, 10; xvi. 10, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_12052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_12052">↑</a> x. 6, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_12052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_12052">↑</a> x. 5, 2, 20.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 177.</li></ol></div>

अ-दृष्ट|अदृष्ट|दृष्ट|అ-దృష్ట|అదృష్ట|దృష్ట|ಅ-ದೃಷ್ಟ|ಅದೃಷ್ಟ|ದೃಷ್ಟ|a-dṛṣṭa|adṛṣṭa|dṛṣṭa|a-dRShTa|adRShTa|dRShTa|a-dRiShTa|adRiShTa|dRiShTa|a-dRuShTa|adRuShTa|dRuShTa|a-dRRiShTa|adRRiShTa|dRRiShTa|_vei_120@53|_vei1_pn019
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@52">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@54">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn018">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn019">1:019</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn020">↷</a><hr><p><b>A-dṛṣṭa.</b>--‘The unseen’ is a term used in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12053"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12053">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12053"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12053">2</a></sup> to designate a species of vermin. The sun is also described as ‘the slayer of the unseen’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अदृष्ट">adṛṣṭa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="हन्">han</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12053"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12053">3</a></sup> and as a counterpart a ‘seen’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दृष्ट">dṛṣṭa</a></i>) is mentioned.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12053"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12053">4</a></sup> In one passage<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_12053"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_12053">5</a></sup> the epithets ‘seen’ and ‘unseen’ are applied to the worm (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृमि">kṛmi</a></b>), their use being no doubt due to the widespread theory of diseases being due to worms, whether discernable by examination or not.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_12053"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_12053">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12053"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12053">↑</a> Rv. i. 191, 4 = Av. vi. 52, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12053"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12053">↑</a> vi. 52, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12053"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12053">↑</a> Rv. i. 191, 9 = Av. vi. 52, 1; Av. v. 23, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12053"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12053">↑</a> Av. ii. 31, 2; viii. 8, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_12053"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_12053">↑</a> Av. v. 23, 6. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_12053"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_12053">↑</a> Kuhn, <i>Zeitschrift für vergleichende Sprachforschung,</i> 13, 135 <i>et seq.;</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 313315; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 98.</li></ol></div>

अद्म-सद्|अद्मसद्|अद्म|सद्|అద్మ-సద్|అద్మసద్|అద్మ|సద్|ಅದ್ಮ-ಸದ್|ಅದ್ಮಸದ್|ಅದ್ಮ|ಸದ್|adma-sad|admasad|adma|sad|_vei_120@54|_vei1_pn019
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@53">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@55">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn018">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn019">1:019</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn020">↷</a><hr><p><b>Adma-sad.</b>--This expression (<i>lit.</i> ‘sitting at the meal’), found several times in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12054"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12054">1</a></sup> is usually rendered ‘guest at the feast,’ but Geldner<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12054"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12054">2</a></sup> adduces reasons to show that it means ‘a fly,’ so called because of its settling on food.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12054"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12054">↑</a> i. 124, 4; vi. 30, 3; vii. 83, 7; viii. 44, 29; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अद्म">adma</a>-sadvan,</i> vi. 4, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12054"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12054">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 179, 180; but <i>of</i> Oldenberg, <i>Vedaforschung,</i> 90.</li></ol></div>

अद्रि|అద్రి|ಅದ್ರಿ|adri|_vei_120@55|_vei1_pn019
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@54">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@56">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn018">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn019">1:019</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn020">↷</a><hr><p><b>Adri.</b>--Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12055"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12055">1</a></sup> deduces from the use of this word (‘rock,’ ‘stone’) in a passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12055"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12055">2</a></sup> that sling-stones were used in Vedic fighting. But the passage is mythical, referring to Indra's aid, and cannot be used with any certainty as evidence for human war. More probably it merely denotes Indra's bolt. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अशनि">aśani</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12055"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12055">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 301. <i>Cf.</i> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12055"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12055">↑</a> i. 51, 3.</li></ol></div>

अधि-देवन|अधिदेवन|अधि|देवन|అధి-దేవన|అధిదేవన|అధి|దేవన|ಅಧಿ-ದೇವನ|ಅಧಿದೇವನ|ಅಧಿ|ದೇವನ|adhi-devana|adhidevana|adhi|devana|_vei_120@56|_vei1_pn019
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@55">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@57">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn018">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn019">1:019</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn020">↷</a><hr><p><b>Adhi-devana.</b>--The place where the dice were thrown is thus designated in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12056">1</a></sup> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12056">2</a></sup> according to Lüders.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12056">3</a></sup> Roth,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12056">4</a></sup> followed by Whitney, takes it to mean ‘gambling-board.’ See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12056">↑</a> v. 31, 6; vi. 70, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12056">↑</a> v. 4, 4, 20. 22, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12056">↑</a> <i>Das Würfelspiel im alten Indien,</i> 11-13.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12056">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

अधि-राज|अधिराज|अधि|राज|అధి-రాజ|అధిరాజ|అధి|రాజ|ಅಧಿ-ರಾಜ|ಅಧಿರಾಜ|ಅಧಿ|ರಾಜ|adhi-rāja|adhirāja|adhi|rāja|adhi-raaja|adhiraaja|raaja|_vei_120@57|_vei1_pn019
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@56">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@58">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn018">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn019">1:019</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn020">↷</a><hr><p><b>Adhi-rāja.</b>--The word occurs fairly often throughout the early literature,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12057">1</a></sup> denoting ‘overlord’ among kings or princes.  In no passage is it clear that a real over-king is meant, as the word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a></i> may mean king or merely prince, a person of royal blood. On the whole it seems most probable that the word connotes no more than ‘king’ as opposed to ‘prince.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12057">↑</a> Rv. x. 128, 9 Av. vi. 98, 1; ix. 10, 24; Taiturīya Saṃhitā, ii. 4, 14, 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhita, iv. 12, 3; Kāthaka Saṃhitā, viii. 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 2, 9 (<i>adhirājan</i>); Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 2, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, viii. 2.</li></ol></div>

अधि-षवण|अधिषवण|अधि|षवण|అధి-షవణ|అధిషవణ|అధి|షవణ|ಅಧಿ-ಷವಣ|ಅಧಿಷವಣ|ಅಧಿ|ಷವಣ|adhi-ṣavaṇa|adhiṣavaṇa|adhi|ṣavaṇa|adhi-ShavaNa|adhiShavaNa|ShavaNa|_vei_120@58|_vei1_pn020
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@57">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@59">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn019">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn020">1:020</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn021">↷</a><hr><p><b>Adhi-ṣavaṇa.</b>--The two Adhiṣavaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12058">1</a></sup> are usually understood, as by Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12058">2</a></sup> and Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12058">3</a></sup> to designate the two boards between which the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> was pressed. Hillebrandt,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12058">4</a></sup> however, shows from the ritual that the boards were not placed one over the other, but were placed one behind the other, the two serving as a foundation upon which the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> was pressed by a stone. This theory seems to account best for the etymological sense of the name ‘over-press,’ as well as for the use of the word as an adjective (‘used for pressing <i>upon</i>’). But according to the procedure as witnessed by Haug<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_12058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_12058">5</a></sup> in the Deccan, the shoots of the plant are first placed on the skin, one of the boards being then laid over them and pounded with a stone. The shoots are then taken out and placed upon the board, the second board being then laid over them.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12058">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 21; Av. v. 20, 10; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 9, 4, 1; 5, 3, 22 (<i>adhiṣavaṇe phalake</i>); <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 32 (<i>adhiṣavaṇaṃ carma,</i> ‘the skin upon which the pressing takes place’; <i>adhiṣavaṇe phalake,</i> ‘the boards on which the pressing takes place,’ etc.).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12058">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12058">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 277.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12058">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 148 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_12058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_12058">↑</a> See Haug, <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, 2, p. 488, n. 10.</li></ol></div>

अधी¤-वास|अधी¤वास|अधी¤|वास|అధీ¤-వాస|అధీ¤వాస|అధీ¤|వాస|ಅಧೀ¤-ವಾಸ|ಅಧೀ¤ವಾಸ|ಅಧೀ¤|ವಾಸ|adhī¤-vāsa|adhī¤vāsa|adhī¤|vāsa|adhii¤-vaasa|adhii¤vaasa|adhii¤|vaasa|adhee¤-vaasa|adhee¤vaasa|adhee¤|_vei_120@59|_vei1_pn020
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@58">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@60">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn019">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn020">1:020</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn021">↷</a><hr><p><b>Adhī¤-vāsa.</b>--This word<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12059"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12059">1</a></sup> denotes the ‘upper garment’ of the Vedic Indian. Its exact nature is not described, but as the king in the ritual set forth in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12059"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12059">2</a></sup> puts on first an undergarment, then a garment, and finally an upper garment, it presumably denotes some sort of cloak or mantle.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12059"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12059">↑</a> Rv. i. 140, 9; 162, 16; x. 5, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 5, 22 (<i>pratimuñc,</i> ‘put on’); 4, 4, 3 (<i>ā-<a class="vei_120a" href="स्तृ">stṛ</a>,</i> ‘spread out’), etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12059"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12059">↑</a> v. 3, 5, 19 <i>et seq.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 268.</li></ol></div>

अध्याण्डा|అధ్యాణ్డా|ಅಧ್ಯಾಣ್ಡಾ|adhyāṇḍā|adhyaaNDaa|_vei_120@60|_vei1_pn020
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@59">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@61">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn019">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn020">1:020</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn021">↷</a><hr><p><b>Adhyāṇḍā.</b>--This is a plant mentioned with many others in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 8, 1, 16). </p></div>

अध्रि-गु|अध्रिगु|अध्रि|అధ్రి-గు|అధ్రిగు|అధ్రి|ಅಧ್ರಿ-ಗು|ಅಧ್ರಿಗು|ಅಧ್ರಿ|adhri-gu|adhrigu|adhri|_vei_120@61|_vei1_pn021
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@60">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@62">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn020">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn021">1:021</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn022">↷</a><hr><p><b>Adhri-gu.</b>--This is the name of a man twice referred to in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12061">1</a></sup> as a protégé of the Aśvins and of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> respectively.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12061">↑</a> i. 112, 20; viii. 12, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 90.</li></ol></div>

अध्वर्यु|అధ్వర్యు|ಅಧ್ವರ್ಯು|adhvaryu|_vei_120@62|_vei1_pn021
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@61">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@63">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn020">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn021">1:021</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn022">↷</a><hr><p><b>Adhvaryu.</b>--In one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12062"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12062">1</a></sup> Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12062"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12062">2</a></sup> thinks that the five Adhvaryus who are mentioned do not designate actual priests, but refer to the five planets which move about in the heavens like the <a class="vei_120a" href="अध्वर्यु">adhvaryu</a> priests on the sacrificial ground. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्रह">graha</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12062"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12062">↑</a> iii. 7, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12062"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12062">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 423.</li></ol></div>

अन्-अग्नि-दग्ध|अन्-अग्निदग्ध|अन्-अग्नि|दग्ध|అన్-అగ్ని-దగ్ధ|అన్-అగ్నిదగ్ధ|అన్-అగ్ని|దగ్ధ|ಅನ್-ಅಗ್ನಿ-ದಗ್ಧ|ಅನ್-ಅಗ್ನಿದಗ್ಧ|ಅನ್-ಅಗ್ನಿ|ದಗ್ಧ|an-agni-dagdha|an-agnidagdha|an-agni|dagdha|_vei_120@63|_vei1_pn021
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@62">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@64">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn020">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn021">1:021</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn022">↷</a><hr><p><b>An-<a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>-dagdha,</b> ‘not burnt with fire.’ See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि-दग्ध">agni-dagdha</a>,</b> ‘burnt with fire.’</p></div>

अनड्-वाह्|अनड्वाह्|अनड्|वाह्|అనడ్-వాహ్|అనడ్వాహ్|అనడ్|వాహ్|ಅನಡ್-ವಾಹ್|ಅನಡ್ವಾಹ್|ಅನಡ್|ವಾಹ್|anaḍ-vāh|anaḍvāh|anaḍ|vāh|anaD-vaah|anaDvaah|anaD|vaah|_vei_120@64|_vei1_pn021
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@63">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@65">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn020">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn021">1:021</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn022">↷</a><hr><p><b>Anaḍ-vāh</b> (lit. ‘cart-drawer’).--This is the common<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12064">1</a></sup> name of oxen as employed for drawing carts (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अनस्">anas</a></b>). Such oxen were normally castrated,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12064">2</a></sup> though not always. Female draft cattle were also used (<i>anaḍuhī</i>), but rarely.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12064">3</a></sup> See also <b>Gō.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12064">↑</a> Rv. x. 59, 10; 85, 10; iii. 53, 18; Av. iii. 11, 5; iv. 11, 1, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 14; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 4, 17, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12064">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 1, 5 (<i>anaḍvān sāṇḍaḥ</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12064">↑</a> Av. iv. 11; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v, 3, 4, 11. 13.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 151, n.; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 226.</li></ol></div>

अनस्|अन|అనస్|అన|ಅನಸ್|ಅನ|anas|ana|_vei_120@65|_vei1_pn021
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@64">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@66">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn020">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn021">1:021</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn022">↷</a><hr><p><b>Anas.</b>--This is the term<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12065">1</a></sup> used to designate the draft wagon, as opposed to the chariot (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a></i>) for war or sport, with which it is sometimes expressly contrasted,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12065">2</a></sup> though <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> is once said to be ‘seated in a wagon’ (<i>anar-<a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a></i>)<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12065">3</a></sup> instead of on a chariot. Though Uṣas, Goddess of Dawn, sometimes rides on a chariot (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a></i>), the wagon is her characteristic vehicle.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12065">4</a></sup> Of its construction we know little. The bridal wagon on which Sūryā, the daughter of the Sun, was borne in the marriage hymn in the Rigveda had a covering (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="छदिस्">chadis</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_12065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_12065">5</a></sup> The axle-box (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="ख">kha</a></b>) is also  mentioned.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_12065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_12065">6</a></sup> In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_12065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_12065">7</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="विपथ">vipatha</a> appears to denote a rough vehicle used for bad tracks. The wagon was usually drawn by oxen (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अनड्वाह्">anaḍvāh</a></b>), as in wedding processions.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_12065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_12065">8</a></sup> The wagon of Dawn is described as drawn by ruddy cows or bulls.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_12065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_12065">9</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12065">↑</a> Rv. iv. 30, 10; x. 85, 10; 86, 18, etc.; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 2, 5, etc.; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vii. 15, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12065">↑</a> Rv. iii. 33, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12065">↑</a> Rv. i. 121, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12065">↑</a> Rv. ii. 15, 6; iv. 30, 11; viii. 91, 7; x. 73, 6; 138, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_12065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_12065">↑</a> Rv. x. 85, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_12065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_12065">↑</a> Rv. viii. 91, 7; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_12065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_12065">↑</a> xv. 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_12065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_12065">↑</a> Rv. x. 85, 11</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_12065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_12065">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> 47.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 246; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 4.</li></ol></div>

अनास्|अना|అనాస్|అనా|ಅನಾಸ್|ಅನಾ|anās|anā|anaas|anaa|_vei_120@66|_vei1_pn022
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@65">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@67">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn021">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn022">1:022</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn023">↷</a><hr><p><b>Anās.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दस्यु">dasyu</a>.</b></p></div>

अनितभा|అనితభా|ಅನಿತಭಾ|anitabhā|anitabhaa|_vei_120@67|_vei1_pn022
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@66">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@68">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn021">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn022">1:022</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn023">↷</a><hr><p><b>Anitabhā</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12067"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12067">1</a></sup> is taken by Max Müller<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12067"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12067">2</a></sup> to denote some river.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12067"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12067">↑</a> v. 53. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12067"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12067">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 323; but <i>of.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रसा">rasā</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

अनु|आनव|అను|ఆనవ|ಅನು|ಆನವ|anu|ānava|aanava|_vei_120@68|_vei1_pn022
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@67">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@69">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn021">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn022">1:022</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn023">↷</a><hr><p><b>Anu, Ānava.</b>--Grassmann and Roth<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12068">1</a></sup> see in these words designations of people foreign to the Aryans. But it is clear<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12068">2</a></sup> that they denote a special people, the Anus, who are mentioned with the <b>Yadus, Turvaśas, Druhyus,</b> and <b>Pūrus,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12068">3</a></sup> with the Druhyus,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12068">4</a></sup> and with the Turvaśas, Yadus, and Druhyus.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_12068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_12068">5</a></sup> It is also a fair conclusion from their mention in a passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_12068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_12068">6</a></sup> that they dwelt on the <a class="vei_120a" href="परुष्णी">paruṣṇī</a>. The inference that the Bhṛgus were connected with this tribe is much more doubtful,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_12068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_12068">7</a></sup> for it rests solely on the fact that in one place<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_12068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_12068">8</a></sup> the Bhṛgus and Druhyus are mentioned together, and not Anus and Druhyus. <a class="vei_120a" href="आनव">ānava</a> is used as an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_12068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_12068">9</a></sup> but also in the sense of ‘Anu prince,’<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_12068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_12068">10</a></sup> in one case in conjunction with <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_12068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_12068">11</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12068">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12068">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 125.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12068">↑</a> Rv. i. 108, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12068">↑</a> Rv. vii. 18, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_12068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_12068">↑</a> viii. 10, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_12068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_12068">↑</a> Rv. viii. 74. 15, compared with vii. 18, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_12068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_12068">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 221, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_12068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_12068">↑</a> Rv. vii. 18, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_12068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_12068">↑</a> Rv. viii. 74, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Rv. v. 31, 4, where the Anus prepare the chariot of the Aśvins.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_12068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_12068">↑</a> Rv. vii. 18, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_12068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_12068">↑</a> viii. 4, 1. Apparently as prince also in vi. 62, 9. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुंग">kuruṅga</a>.</b></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 205; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 153; Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 154.</li></ol></div>

अनु-क्षत्तृ|अनुक्षत्तृ|अनु|क्षत्तृ|అను-క్షత్తృ|అనుక్షత్తృ|అను|క్షత్తృ|ಅನು-ಕ್ಷತ್ತೃ|ಅನುಕ್ಷತ್ತೃ|ಅನು|ಕ್ಷತ್ತೃ|anu-kṣattṛ|anukṣattṛ|anu|kṣattṛ|anu-kShattR|anukShattR|kShattR|anu-kShattRi|anukShattRi|kShattRi|anu-kShattRu|anukShattRu|kShattRu|anu-kShattRRi|anukShattRRi|kShattRRi|anu-xattR|anuxattR|xattR|_vei_120@69|_vei1_pn022
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@68">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@70">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn021">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn022">1:022</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn023">↷</a><hr><p><b>Anu-kṣattṛ.</b>--This word occurs in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12069"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12069">1</a></sup> and means, according to Mahīdhara,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12069"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12069">2</a></sup> ‘an attendant on the door-keeper,’ and, according to Sāyaṇa, ‘an attendant on the charioteer’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सारथि">sārathi</a></i>). See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्तृ">kṣattṛ</a>.</b></p><p>3) On <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12069"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12069">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 11; 73; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 9, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12069"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12069">↑</a> On Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

अनु-चर|अनुचर|अनु|चर|అను-చర|అనుచర|అను|చర|ಅನು-ಚರ|ಅನುಚರ|ಅನು|ಚರ|anu-cara|anucara|anu|cara|anu-chara|anuchara|chara|_vei_120@70|_vei1_pn023
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@69">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@71">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn022">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn023">1:023</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn024">↷</a><hr><p><b>Anu-cara.</b>--This is a general expression<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12070"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12070">1</a></sup> for an ‘attendant’ (the feminine being Anucarī),<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12070"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12070">2</a></sup> but it is not often used.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12070"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12070">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 7, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12070"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12070">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 4, 27 (apparently slave girls are meant, 400 being enumerated).</li></ol></div>

अनु-मति|अनुमति|अनु|मति|అను-మతి|అనుమతి|అను|మతి|ಅನು-ಮತಿ|ಅನುಮತಿ|ಅನು|ಮತಿ|anu-mati|anumati|anu|mati|_vei_120@71|_vei1_pn023
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@70">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@72">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn022">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn023">1:023</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn024">↷</a><hr><p><b>Anu-mati.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></p></div>

अनु-राधा|अनुराधा|अनु|राधा|అను-రాధా|అనురాధా|అను|రాధా|ಅನು-ರಾಧಾ|ಅನುರಾಧಾ|ಅನು|ರಾಧಾ|anu-rādhā|anurādhā|anu|rādhā|anu-raadhaa|anuraadhaa|raadhaa|_vei_120@72|_vei1_pn023
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@71">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@73">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn022">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn023">1:023</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn024">↷</a><hr><p><b>Anu-rādhā.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p></div>

अनु-वक्तृ सत्य सात्य-कीर्त|अनु-वक्तृ सत्य सात्यकीर्त|अनु-वक्तृ सत्य सात्य|कीर्त|అను-వక్తృ సత్య సాత్య-కీర్త|అను-వక్తృ సత్య సాత్యకీర్త|అను-వక్తృ సత్య సాత్య|కీర్త|ಅನು-ವಕ್ತೃ ಸತ್ಯ ಸಾತ್ಯ-ಕೀರ್ತ|ಅನು-ವಕ್ತೃ ಸತ್ಯ ಸಾತ್ಯಕೀರ್ತ|ಅನು-ವಕ್ತೃ ಸತ್ಯ ಸಾತ್ಯ|ಕೀರ್ತ|anu-vaktṛ satya sātya-kīrta|anu-vaktṛ satya sātyakīrta|anu-vaktṛ satya sātya|kīrta|anu-vaktR satya saatya-kiirta|anu-vaktR satya saatyakiirta|anu-vaktR satya saatya|kiirta|anu-vaktR satya saatya-keerta|anu-vaktR satya saatyakeerta|keerta|anu-vaktRi satya saatya-kiirta|anu-vaktRi satya saatyakiirta|anu-vaktRi satya saatya|anu-vaktRu satya saatya-kiirta|anu-vaktRu satya saatyakiirta|anu-vaktRu satya saatya|anu-vaktRRi satya saatya-kiirta|anu-vaktRRi satya saatyakiirta|anu-vaktRRi satya saatya|_vei_120@73|_vei1_pn023
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@72">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@74">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn022">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn023">1:023</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn024">↷</a><hr><p><b>Anu-vaktṛ <a class="vei_120a" href="सत्य">satya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सात्य">sātya</a>-kīrta</b> is mentioned as a teacher in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (i. 5, 4).</p></div>

अनुव्या-ख्यान|अनुव्याख्यान|अनुव्या|ख्यान|అనువ్యా-ఖ్యాన|అనువ్యాఖ్యాన|అనువ్యా|ఖ్యాన|ಅನುವ್ಯಾ-ಖ್ಯಾನ|ಅನುವ್ಯಾಖ್ಯಾನ|ಅನುವ್ಯಾ|ಖ್ಯಾನ|anuvyā-khyāna|anuvyākhyāna|anuvyā|khyāna|anuvyaa-khyaana|anuvyaakhyaana|anuvyaa|khyaana|_vei_120@74|_vei1_pn023
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@73">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@75">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn022">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn023">1:023</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn024">↷</a><hr><p><b>Anuvyā-khyāna</b> is a species of writing referred to in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12074"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12074">1</a></sup> Śaṅkara interprets it as ‘explanation of the Mantras.’ As the term, in the plural, follows <b>Sūtras,</b> this interpretation is reasonable. Sieg,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12074"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12074">2</a></sup> however, equates the word with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अन्वाख्यान">anvākhyāna</a>,</b> ‘supplementary narrative.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12074"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12074">↑</a> ii. 4, 10; iv. 1, 2; 5, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12074"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12074">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 34.</li></ol></div>

अनु-शासन|अनुशासन|अनु|शासन|అను-శాసన|అనుశాసన|అను|శాసన|ಅನು-ಶಾಸನ|ಅನುಶಾಸನ|ಅನು|ಶಾಸನ|anu-śāsana|anuśāsana|anu|śāsana|anu-shaasana|anushaasana|shaasana|_vei_120@75|_vei1_pn023
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@74">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@76">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn022">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn023">1:023</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn024">↷</a><hr><p><b>Anu-śāsana</b> in the plural denotes in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xi. 5, 6, 8) some form of literature; according to Sāyaṇa, the Vedāṅgas.</p></div>

अनु-शिख|अनुशिख|अनु|शिख|అను-శిఖ|అనుశిఖ|అను|శిఖ|ಅನು-ಶಿಖ|ಅನುಶಿಖ|ಅನು|ಶಿಖ|anu-śikha|anuśikha|anu|śikha|anu-shikha|anushikha|shikha|_vei_120@76|_vei1_pn023
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@75">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@77">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn022">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn023">1:023</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn024">↷</a><hr><p><b>Anu-śikha</b> is the name of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पोतृ">potṛ</a> at the snake festival in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12076"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12076">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12076"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12076">↑</a> xxv. 15. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 35.</li></ol></div>

अन्ते-वासिन्|अन्तेवासिन्|अन्ते|वासिन्|అన్తే-వాసిన్|అన్తేవాసిన్|అన్తే|వాసిన్|ಅನ್ತೇ-ವಾಸಿನ್|ಅನ್ತೇವಾಸಿನ್|ಅನ್ತೇ|ವಾಸಿನ್|ante-vāsin|antevāsin|ante|vāsin|ante-vaasin|antevaasin|vaasin|_vei_120@77|_vei1_pn023
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@76">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@78">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn022">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn023">1:023</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn024">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ante-vāsin,</b> ‘dwelling near,’ is the epithet of the <b>Brahmacārin</b> who lives in the house of his teacher. The expression does not occur before the late <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> period.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12077"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12077">1</a></sup> Secrecy is often enjoined on others than Antevāsins.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12077"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12077">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12077"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12077">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1, 5, 17; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 3, 7; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 11, 5; iv. 10, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 3, 3; 11, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12077"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12077">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 2, 6; Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, viii, 11.</li></ol></div>

अन्ध्र|అన్ధ్ర|ಅನ್ಧ್ರ|andhra|_vei_120@78|_vei1_pn023
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@77">«</a>pada9<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@79">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn022">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn023">1:023</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn024">↷</a><hr><p><b>Andhra</b> is the name of a people, and is mentioned with the <b>Puṇḍras, Śabaras, Pulindas,</b> and <b>Mūtibas,</b> as being the outcasts resulting from the refusal of the fifty eldest sons of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a></b> to accept his adoption of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12078"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12078">1</a></sup> It may fairly be deduced  from this statement that these people were recognized as nonAryan, as the Andhras certainly seem to have been.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12078"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12078">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12078"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12078">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 26, where instead of <i>udāntyaḥ</i> (‘beyond the borders’) the reading is <i>udañcaḥ</i> (‘northern’), but the former is obviously correct. The Sāṅkhāyana omits the Pulindas, and has Mūcīpas.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12078"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12078">↑</a> Vincent Smith, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 56, 657 <i>et seq.,</i> who places them originally in Eastern India between the Kṛṣṇā and Godāvarī rivers; <i>cf.</i> Rapson, <i>Catalogue of Indian Coins,</i> xv., xvi.; Bhandarkar, <i>Bombay Gazetteer,</i> 1. ii. 138.</li></ol></div>

अन्यतः-प्लक्षा|अन्यतःप्लक्षा|अन्यतः|प्लक्षा|అన్యతః-ప్లక్షా|అన్యతఃప్లక్షా|అన్యతః|ప్లక్షా|ಅನ್ಯತಃ-ಪ್ಲಕ್ಷಾ|ಅನ್ಯತಃಪ್ಲಕ್ಷಾ|ಅನ್ಯತಃ|ಪ್ಲಕ್ಷಾ|anyataḥ-plakṣā|anyataḥplakṣā|anyataḥ|plakṣā|anyataH-plakShaa|anyataHplakShaa|anyataH|plakShaa|anyataH-plaxaa|anyataHplaxaa|plaxaa|_vei_120@79|_vei1_pn024
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@78">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@80">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn023">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn024">1:024</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn025">↷</a><hr><p><b>Anyataḥ-plakṣā</b> (‘with wave-leafed fig-trees on one side only’) is the name of a lake in <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a> according to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12079"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12079">1</a></sup> where it occurs in the story of <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरूरवस्">purūravas</a> and Urvaśī. Pischel<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12079"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12079">2</a></sup> places it somewhere in Sirmor.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12079"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12079">↑</a> xi. 5, 1, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12079"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12079">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 217.</li></ol></div>

अन्य-वाप|अन्यवाप|अन्य|वाप|అన్య-వాప|అన్యవాప|అన్య|వాప|ಅನ್ಯ-ವಾಪ|ಅನ್ಯವಾಪ|ಅನ್ಯ|ವಾಪ|anya-vāpa|anyavāpa|anya|vāpa|anya-vaapa|anyavaapa|vaapa|_vei_120@80|_vei1_pn024
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@79">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@81">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn023">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn024">1:024</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn025">↷</a><hr><p><b>Anya-vāpa</b> (‘sowing for others’).--The cuckoo is so called<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12080"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12080">1</a></sup> from its habit of depositing its eggs in the nests of other birds</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12080"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12080">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 37; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 18.</li></ol></div>

अन्वा-ख्यान|अन्वाख्यान|अन्वा|ख्यान|అన్వా-ఖ్యాన|అన్వాఖ్యాన|అన్వా|ఖ్యాన|ಅನ್ವಾ-ಖ್ಯಾನ|ಅನ್ವಾಖ್ಯಾನ|ಅನ್ವಾ|ಖ್ಯಾನ|anvā-khyāna|anvākhyāna|anvā|khyāna|anvaa-khyaana|anvaakhyaana|anvaa|khyaana|_vei_120@81|_vei1_pn024
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@80">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@82">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn023">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn024">1:024</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn025">↷</a><hr><p><b>Anvā-khyāna.</b>--From the literal translation (‘after-story’) the meaning of ‘supplementary narrative’ seems to follow. In two<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12081">1</a></sup> of its three occurrences in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> this sense is hardly felt, the expression being used to indicate a subsequent portion of the book itself. But in the third<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12081">2</a></sup> passage it is distinguished from the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a></b> (‘story’) proper, and there must mean ‘supplementary narrative.’ <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अनुव्याख्यान">anuvyākhyāna</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12081">↑</a> vi. 5, 2, 22; 6, 4, 7 (the reference is to vi. 6, 4, 8).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12081">↑</a> xi. 1, 6, 9.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 34.</li></ol></div>

अप-चित्|अपचित्|अप|चित्|అప-చిత్|అపచిత్|అప|చిత్|ಅಪ-ಚಿತ್|ಅಪಚಿತ್|ಅಪ|ಚಿತ್|apa-cit|apacit|apa|cit|apa-chit|apachit|chit|_vei_120@82|_vei1_pn024
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@81">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@83">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn023">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn024">1:024</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn025">↷</a><hr><p><b>Apa-cit.</b>--This word occurs several times in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12082"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12082">1</a></sup> It is held by Roth,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12082"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12082">2</a></sup> Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12082"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12082">3</a></sup> and others to denote an insect whose sting produced swellings, etc. (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्लौ">glau</a></i>). But Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12082"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12082">4</a></sup> shows that the disease, scrofulous swellings, is what is really meant, as is shown by the rendering (<i>gaṇḍa-mālā,</i> ‘inflammation of the glands of the neck’) of Keśava and Sāyaṇa, and by the parallelism of the later disease, <i>apacī,</i> the derivation being from <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अप">apa</a></i> and <i>ci,</i> ‘to pick off.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12082"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12082">↑</a> vi. 25, 1; 83, 1; vii. 75, 1; 77, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12082"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12082">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12082"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12082">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 97. So also Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, {??} 342, 500.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12082"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12082">↑</a> <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 11, 320 <i>et seq.; Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 503, 504. <i>Cf.</i> Jolly, <i>Medicin,</i> 89; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 343.</li></ol></div>

अप-श्रय|अपश्रय|अप|श्रय|అప-శ్రయ|అపశ్రయ|అప|శ్రయ|ಅಪ-ಶ್ರಯ|ಅಪಶ್ರಯ|ಅಪ|ಶ್ರಯ|apa-śraya|apaśraya|apa|śraya|apa-shraya|apashraya|shraya|_vei_120@83|_vei1_pn025
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@82">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@84">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn024">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn025">1:025</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn026">↷</a><hr><p><b>Apa-śraya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपश्रय">upaśraya</a>.</b></p></div>

अप-स्कम्भ|अपस्कम्भ|अप|स्कम्भ|अप-स्कंभ|अपस्कंभ|स्कंभ|అప-స్కమ్భ|అపస్కమ్భ|అప|స్కమ్భ|అప-స్కంభ|అపస్కంభ|స్కంభ|ಅಪ-ಸ್ಕಮ್ಭ|ಅಪಸ್ಕಮ್ಭ|ಅಪ|ಸ್ಕಮ್ಭ|ಅಪ-ಸ್ಕಂಭ|ಅಪಸ್ಕಂಭ|ಸ್ಕಂಭ|apa-skambha|apaskambha|apa|skambha|_vei_120@84|_vei1_pn025
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@83">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@85">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn024">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn025">1:025</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn026">↷</a><hr><p><b>Apa-skambha.</b>--The word occurs only in one passage of the Atharvaveda, where the tip of it is mentioned as poisoned. Roth<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12084"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12084">1</a></sup> suggests that the fastening of the arrow-point to the shaft is meant. Whitney<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12084"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12084">2</a></sup> inclines to this version, but suggests corruption of text. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12084"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12084">3</a></sup> follows Roth. Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12084"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12084">4</a></sup> renders the word by ‘barb.’ Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_12084"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_12084">5</a></sup> thinks it means ‘tearing (arrow),’ a sense deduced from the etymology.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12084"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12084">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> The passage is Av. iv. 6, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12084"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12084">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 153.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12084"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12084">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 300.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12084"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12084">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 512.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_12084"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_12084">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 375.</li></ol></div>

अपाच्यस्|अपाच्य|అపాచ్యస్|అపాచ్య|ಅಪಾಚ್ಯಸ್|ಅಪಾಚ್ಯ|apācyas|apācya|apaachyas|apaachya|_vei_120@85|_vei1_pn025
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@84">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@86">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn024">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn025">1:025</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn026">↷</a><hr><p><b>Apācyas.</b>--The kings of the ‘Westerners’ are referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 14) in connection with those of the <b>Nīcyas.</b></p></div>

अपान|అపాన|ಅಪಾನ|apāna|apaana|_vei_120@86|_vei1_pn025
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@85">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@87">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn024">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn025">1:025</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn026">↷</a><hr><p><b>Apāna.</b>--The word appears repeatedly in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12086">1</a></sup> and later as one of the vital breaths (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a></b>), usually with <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a>, and often with one or more of the other three. Its original sense<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12086">2</a></sup> appears to have been ‘inspiration.’ Its connection with the lower part of the body (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="नाभि">nābhi</a>,</i> ‘navel’), which is found already in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12086">3</a></sup> is not unnatural.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12086">↑</a> Av. ii. 28, 3; v. 30, 12, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiii. 19; 24, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12086">↑</a> See Caland, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> lv. 261; 56, 556-558, correcting Ewing, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 22, 249 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12086">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 4, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Deussen, <i>Philosophy of the Upanishads,</i> 263 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

अपा-मार्ग|अपामार्ग|अपा|मार्ग|అపా-మార్గ|అపామార్గ|అపా|మార్గ|ಅಪಾ-ಮಾರ್ಗ|ಅಪಾಮಾರ್ಗ|ಅಪಾ|ಮಾರ್ಗ|apā-mārga|apāmārga|apā|mārga|apaa-maarga|apaamaarga|apaa|maarga|_vei_120@87|_vei1_pn025
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@86">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@88">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn024">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn025">1:025</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn026">↷</a><hr><p><b>Apā-mārga.</b>--A plant (<i>Achyranthes aspera</i>) used frequently<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12087">1</a></sup> in witchcraft practices, and for medical purposes, especially against <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षेत्रिय">kṣetriya</a>.</b> It is described in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12087">2</a></sup> as ‘revertive’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पुनः-सर">punaḥ-sara</a></i>), either, as Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12087">3</a></sup> and Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12087">4</a></sup> think, because of its having reverted leaves (a view also accepted by Whitney<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_12087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_12087">5</a></sup> ),  or because, as Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_12087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_12087">6</a></sup> holds, it wards off a spell by causing it to recoil on its user.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12087">↑</a> Atharvaveda, iv. 17, 6; 18, 7; 19, 4; vii. 65, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxv. 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 1, 8; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 2, 4, 14; xiii. 8, 4, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12087">↑</a> iv. 17, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12087">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12087">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 67.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_12087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_12087">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 180.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_12087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_12087">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 394. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 160, 161; Weber <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 94.</li></ol></div>

अप-लम्ब|अपलम्ब|अप|लम्ब|अप-लंब|अपलंब|लंब|అప-లమ్బ|అపలమ్బ|అప|లమ్బ|అప-లంబ|అపలంబ|లంబ|ಅಪ-ಲಮ್ಬ|ಅಪಲಮ್ಬ|ಅಪ|ಲಮ್ಬ|ಅಪ-ಲಂಬ|ಅಪಲಂಬ|ಲಂಬ|apa-lamba|apalamba|apa|lamba|_vei_120@88|_vei1_pn026
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@87">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@89">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn025">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn026">1:026</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn027">↷</a><hr><p><b>Apa-lamba.</b>--By this word<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12088">1</a></sup> is denoted a brake or drag, let down (from <i>lamb,</i> ‘hang down’) to check the speed of a wagon.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12088">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 4, 13. <i>Cf.</i> Caland and Henry, <i>L'Agniṣṭoma,</i> 50; Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 79.</li></ol></div>

अपाष्ठ|అపాష్ఠ|ಅಪಾಷ್ಠ|apāṣṭha|apaaShTha|_vei_120@89|_vei1_pn026
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@88">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@90">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn025">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn026">1:026</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn027">↷</a><hr><p><b>Apāṣṭha.</b>--This word occurs twice in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12089"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12089">1</a></sup> denoting the barb of an arrow.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12089"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12089">↑</a> iv. 6, 5; v. 18, 7 (<i>śatāpāṣṭha,</i> ‘huṅdred-barbed’). <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 300; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 375.</li></ol></div>

अपि-शर्वर|अपिशर्वर|अपि|शर्वर|అపి-శర్వర|అపిశర్వర|అపి|శర్వర|ಅಪಿ-ಶರ್ವರ|ಅಪಿಶರ್ವರ|ಅಪಿ|ಶರ್ವರ|api-śarvara|apiśarvara|api|śarvara|api-sharvara|apisharvara|sharvara|_vei_120@90|_vei1_pn026
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@89">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@91">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn025">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn026">1:026</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn027">↷</a><hr><p><b>Api-śarvara.</b> See <b>Anan.</b></p></div>

अपी|अपि|అపీ|అపి|ಅಪೀ|ಅಪಿ|apī|api|apii|apee|_vei_120@91|_vei1_pn026
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@90">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@92">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn025">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn026">1:026</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn027">↷</a><hr><p><b>Apī.</b>--Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12091"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12091">1</a></sup> finds an <a class="vei_120a" href="अपी">apī</a> whose sons are described as not performing sacrifice (<i>a-<a class="vei_120a" href="यज्ञ">yajña</a>-sāc</i>) and as breakers of the law of <a class="vei_120a" href="मित्र">mitra</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12091"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12091">2</a></sup> Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12091"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12091">3</a></sup> and Grassmann take the expression used (<i>apyaḥ putrāḥ</i>) as referring to the sons of the waters.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12091"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12091">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 158, 159.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12091"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12091">↑</a> vi. 67, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12091"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12091">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v., apya.</i></li></ol></div>

अपूप|అపూప|ಅಪೂಪ|apūpa|apuupa|apoopa|_vei_120@92|_vei1_pn026
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@91">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@93">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn025">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn026">1:026</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn027">↷</a><hr><p><b>Apūpa</b> is the general word from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12092">1</a></sup> onwards for a cake, which might be mixed with ghee (<i>ghṛtavant</i>),<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12092">2</a></sup> or be made of rice (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रीहि">vrīhi</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12092">3</a></sup> or of barley (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="यव">yava</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12092">4</a></sup> In the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_12092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_12092">5</a></sup> there is a difference of interpretation. Max Müller renders it as ‘hive,’ Bo7htlingk as ‘honeycomb,’ Little<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_12092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_12092">6</a></sup> as ‘cake.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12092">↑</a> iii. 52, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12092">↑</a> Rv. x. 45, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12092">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 2, 3, 12, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12092">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 2, 5, 19.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_12092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_12092">↑</a> iii. 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_12092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_12092">↑</a> <i>Grammatical Index, s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

अप्नवान|అప్నవాన|ಅಪ್ನವಾನ|apnavāna|apnavaana|_vei_120@93|_vei1_pn026
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@92">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@94">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn025">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn026">1:026</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn027">↷</a><hr><p><b>Apnavāna</b> appears only twice in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12093"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12093">1</a></sup> as an ancient sage, coupled with the Bhṛgus, to whose family Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12093"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12093">2</a></sup> conjectures him to have belonged. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12093"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12093">↑</a> iv. 7, 1; viii. 91, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12093"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12093">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 128.</li></ol></div>

अ-प्रति-रथ|अ-प्रतिरथ|अ-प्रति|रथ|అ-ప్రతి-రథ|అ-ప్రతిరథ|అ-ప్రతి|రథ|ಅ-ಪ್ರತಿ-ರಥ|ಅ-ಪ್ರತಿರಥ|ಅ-ಪ್ರತಿ|ರಥ|a-prati-ratha|a-pratiratha|a-prati|ratha|_vei_120@94|_vei1_pn027
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@93">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@95">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn026">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn027">1:027</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn028">↷</a><hr><p><b>A-<a class="vei_120a" href="प्रति">prati</a>-ratha</b> (‘he who has no match in fight’) is the name of an obviously invented <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>, to whom is ascribed by the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12094"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12094">1</a></sup> and Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12094"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12094">2</a></sup> the composition of a Rigveda hymn<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12094"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12094">3</a></sup> celebrating <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> as the invincible warrior.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12094"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12094">↑</a> viii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12094"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12094">↑</a> ix. 2, 3, 1. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12094"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12094">↑</a> x. 103.</li></ol></div>

अप्वा|అప్వా|ಅಪ್ವಾ|apvā|apvaa|_vei_120@95|_vei1_pn027
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@94">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@96">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn026">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn027">1:027</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn028">↷</a><hr><p><b>Apvā.</b>--A disease affecting the stomach,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12095">1</a></sup> possibly dysentery, as suggested by Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12095">2</a></sup> on the ground that the disease is invoked to confound the enemy.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_12095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_12095">3</a></sup> Weber<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_12095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_12095">4</a></sup> considers that it is diarrhœa induced by fear, as often in the Epic.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_12095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_12095">5</a></sup> This view is supported by Bloomfield,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_12095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_12095">6</a></sup> and was apparently that of <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_12095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_12095">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12095">↑</a> Av. ix. 8, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12095">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 389.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_12095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_12095">↑</a> Rv. x. 103, 12 = Av. iii. 2, 5 = <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>, ii. 1211 = Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvii. 44.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_12095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_12095">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 482; 17, 184.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_12095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_12095">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 184.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_12095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_12095">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 327.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_12095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_12095">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ix. 33.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 86, 87.</li></ol></div>

अप्सस्|अप्स|అప్సస్|అప్స|ಅಪ್ಸಸ್|ಅಪ್ಸ|apsas|apsa|_vei_120@96|_vei1_pn027
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@95">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@97">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn026">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn027">1:027</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn028">↷</a><hr><p><b>Apsas.</b>--This word usually refers to the body, meaning ‘front part.’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12096">1</a></sup> In one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_12096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_12096">2</a></sup> however, the adjective ‘with a long front part’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दीर्घाप्सस्">dīrghāpsas</a></i>) is applied to the chariot (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a></b>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12096">↑</a> See Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 308-313; 2, 245, 246.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_12096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_12096">↑</a> i. 122, 15. <i>Cf.</i> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

अभि-क्रोशक|अभिक्रोशक|अभि|क्रोशक|అభి-క్రోశక|అభిక్రోశక|అభి|క్రోశక|ಅಭಿ-ಕ್ರೋಶಕ|ಅಭಿಕ್ರೋಶಕ|ಅಭಿ|ಕ್ರೋಶಕ|abhi-krośaka|abhikrośaka|abhi|krośaka|abhi-kroshaka|abhikroshaka|kroshaka|_vei_120@97|_vei1_pn027
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@96">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@98">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn026">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn027">1:027</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn028">↷</a><hr><p><b>Abhi-krośaka</b> designates one of the victims in the Puruṣamedha, meaning, perhaps, ‘herald.’ The commentator Mahīdhara<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_12097"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_12097">1</a></sup> renders it as ‘reviler’ (<i>nindaka</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_12097"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_12097">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 20. <i>Cf.</i> Anukrośaka, <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 15, 1.</li></ol></div>

अभि-जित्|अभिजित्|अभि|जित्|అభి-జిత్|అభిజిత్|అభి|జిత్|ಅಭಿ-ಜಿತ್|ಅಭಿಜಿತ್|ಅಭಿ|ಜಿತ್|abhi-jit|abhijit|abhi|jit|_vei_120@98|_vei1_pn027
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@97">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@99">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn026">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn027">1:027</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn028">↷</a><hr><p><b>Abhi-jit.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p></div>

अभि-पित्व|अभिपित्व|अभि|पित्व|అభి-పిత్వ|అభిపిత్వ|అభి|పిత్వ|ಅಭಿ-ಪಿತ್ವ|ಅಭಿಪಿತ್ವ|ಅಭಿ|ಪಿತ್ವ|abhi-pitva|abhipitva|abhi|pitva|_vei_120@99|_vei1_pn027
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@98">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@100">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn026">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn027">1:027</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn028">↷</a><hr><p><b>Abhi-pitva.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अहन्">ahan</a>.</b></p></div>

अभि-प्रतारिन् काक्ष-सेनि|अभि-प्रतारिन् काक्षसेनि|अभि-प्रतारिन् काक्ष|सेनि|అభి-ప్రతారిన్ కాక్ష-సేని|అభి-ప్రతారిన్ కాక్షసేని|అభి-ప్రతారిన్ కాక్ష|సేని|ಅಭಿ-ಪ್ರತಾರಿನ್ ಕಾಕ್ಷ-ಸೇನಿ|ಅಭಿ-ಪ್ರತಾರಿನ್ ಕಾಕ್ಷಸೇನಿ|ಅಭಿ-ಪ್ರತಾರಿನ್ ಕಾಕ್ಷ|ಸೇನಿ|abhi-pratārin kākṣa-seni|abhi-pratārin kākṣaseni|abhi-pratārin kākṣa|seni|abhi-prataarin kaakSha-seni|abhi-prataarin kaakShaseni|abhi-prataarin kaakSha|abhi-prataarin kaaxa-seni|abhi-prataarin kaaxaseni|abhi-prataarin kaaxa|_vei_120@100|_vei1_pn027
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@99">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@101">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn026">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn027">1:027</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn028">↷</a><hr><p><b>Abhi-pratārin <a class="vei_120a" href="काक्ष">kākṣa</a>-seni</b> is mentioned in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120100"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120100">1</a></sup> the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120100"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120100">2</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120100"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120100">3</a></sup> as engaged in discussions on philosophy. The Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120100"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120100">4</a></sup> further reports that his sons divided the property amongst themselves while he was yet alive. He was a <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a> and a prince. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120100"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120100">↑</a> i. 59, 1; iii. 1, 21; 2, 2. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120100"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120100">↑</a> iv. 3, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120100"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120100">↑</a> x. 5, 7; xiv. 1, 12. 15.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120100"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120100">↑</a> iii. 156 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 26, 61).</li></ol></div>

अभि-प्रश्निन्|अभिप्रश्निन्|अभि|प्रश्निन्|అభి-ప్రశ్నిన్|అభిప్రశ్నిన్|అభి|ప్రశ్నిన్|ಅಭಿ-ಪ್ರಶ್ನಿನ್|ಅಭಿಪ್ರಶ್ನಿನ್|ಅಭಿ|ಪ್ರಶ್ನಿನ್|abhi-praśnin|abhipraśnin|abhi|praśnin|abhi-prashnin|abhiprashnin|prashnin|_vei_120@101|_vei1_pn028
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@100">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@102">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn027">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn028">1:028</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn029">↷</a><hr><p><b>Abhi-praśnin.</b>--This term occurs after <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रश्निन्">praśnin</a>,</b> and followed by <b>Praśnavivāka</b> in the list of victims for the Puruṣamedha given in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120101">1</a></sup> and the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120101">2</a></sup> The commentators, Sāyaṇa and Mahīdhara, see in it merely a reference to an inquisitive man. But there can be little doubt that the term must have had a legal reference of some sort-perhaps indicating the defendant as opposed to plaintiff and judge.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120101">↑</a> iii. 4, 6, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120101">↑</a> xxx. 10.</li></ol></div>

अभि-श्री|अभि-श्रि|अभिश्री|अभिश्रि|अभि|श्री|श्रि|అభి-శ్రీ|అభి-శ్రి|అభిశ్రీ|అభిశ్రి|అభి|శ్రీ|శ్రి|ಅಭಿ-ಶ್ರೀ|ಅಭಿ-ಶ್ರಿ|ಅಭಿಶ್ರೀ|ಅಭಿಶ್ರಿ|ಅಭಿ|ಶ್ರೀ|ಶ್ರಿ|abhi-śrī|abhi-śri|abhiśrī|abhiśri|abhi|śrī|śri|abhi-shrii|abhi-shri|abhishrii|abhishri|shrii|shri|abhi-shree|abhishree|shree|_vei_120@102|_vei1_pn028
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@101">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@103">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn027">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn028">1:028</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn029">↷</a><hr><p><b>Abhi-śrī</b> (‘admixture’).--This word<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120102"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120102">1</a></sup> designates the milk used to mingle with the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> juice before it was offered.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120102"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120102">↑</a> Rv. ix. 79, 5; 86, 27. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 227; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> i. 211.</li></ol></div>

अभि-षवणी|अभि-षवणि|अभिषवणी|अभिषवणि|अभि|षवणी|षवणि|అభి-షవణీ|అభి-షవణి|అభిషవణీ|అభిషవణి|అభి|షవణీ|షవణి|ಅಭಿ-ಷವಣೀ|ಅಭಿ-ಷವಣಿ|ಅಭಿಷವಣೀ|ಅಭಿಷವಣಿ|ಅಭಿ|ಷವಣೀ|ಷವಣಿ|abhi-ṣavaṇī|abhi-ṣavaṇi|abhiṣavaṇī|abhiṣavaṇi|abhi|ṣavaṇī|ṣavaṇi|abhi-ShavaNii|abhi-ShavaNi|abhiShavaNii|abhiShavaNi|ShavaNii|ShavaNi|abhi-ShavaNee|abhiShavaNee|ShavaNee|_vei_120@103|_vei1_pn028
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@102">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@104">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn027">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn028">1:028</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn029">↷</a><hr><p><b>Abhi-ṣavaṇī.</b>--Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120103">1</a></sup> renders the expression in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120103">2</a></sup> as a pressing instrument, but it appears to be merely an adjective, (waters) ‘used in pressing.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120103">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120103">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 277.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120103">↑</a> ix. 6, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120103">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Whitney's Translation of Av. ix. 6, 16.</li></ol></div>

अभि-षेक|अभिषेक|अभि|षेक|అభి-షేక|అభిషేక|అభి|షేక|ಅಭಿ-ಷೇಕ|ಅಭಿಷೇಕ|ಅಭಿ|ಷೇಕ|abhi-ṣeka|abhiṣeka|abhi|ṣeka|abhi-Sheka|abhiSheka|Sheka|_vei_120@104|_vei1_pn028
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@103">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@105">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn027">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn028">1:028</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn029">↷</a><hr><p><b>Abhi-ṣeka</b> (‘besprinkling’).--The Vedic king was consecrated after his election with an elaborate ritual, which is fully described in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120104">1</a></sup> Pañcaviṃśa,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120104">2</a></sup> Śatapatha,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120104">3</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120104">4</a></sup> and for which the Mantras are given in the Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120104">5</a></sup> The consecration took place by sprinkling with water (<i>abhiṣecanīyā āpaḥ</i>).<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120104">6</a></sup> Only kings could be consecrated, the people not being worthy of it (<i>anabhiṣecanīyāḥ</i>).<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120104">7</a></sup> The sprinkler (<i>abhiṣektṛ</i>) is mentioned in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120104">8</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="अभिषेक">abhiṣeka</a> is an essential part of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>,</b> or sacrifice of royal inauguration, being the second of its component members.</p><p>9 <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Rituallitteratur,</i> 143-147; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, xxvi; Weber, <i>Über den <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>.</i> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120104">↑</a> i. 7, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120104">↑</a> xviii. 8 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120104">↑</a> v. 3, 3 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120104">↑</a> viii. 5 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120104">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 6; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 6; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x. 1-4.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120104">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 5, 10-15,</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120104">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 2, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120104">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 8, 1.</li></ol></div>

अभीशु|అభీశు|ಅಭೀಶು|abhīśu|abhiishu|abheeshu|_vei_120@105|_vei1_pn029
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@104">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@106">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn028">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn029">1:029</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn030">↷</a><hr><p><b>Abhīśu</b> is a common Vedic word<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120105"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120105">1</a></sup> denoting the ‘reins’ or ‘bridle’ of the chariot horses. The use of the plural is due to the fact that two or four horses, possibly five (<i>daśābhīśu</i> ‘tenbridled’),<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120105"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120105">2</a></sup> were yoked to the car.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120105"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120105">↑</a> Rv. i. 38, 12; v. 44, 4; vi. 75, 6; viii. 33, 11; Av. vi. 137, 2; viii. 8, 22; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxiv. 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 3, 14 (where it = <i>raśmayaḥ,</i> ‘reins’), etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120105"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120105">↑</a> Rv. x. 94, 7.</li></ol></div>

अभ्य्-अग्नि ऐतशायन|अभ्यग्नि ऐतशायन|अभ्य्|अग्नि ऐतशायन|అభ్య్-అగ్ని ఐతశాయన|అభ్యగ్ని ఐతశాయన|అభ్య్|అగ్ని ఐతశాయన|ಅಭ್ಯ್-ಅಗ್ನಿ ಐತಶಾಯನ|ಅಭ್ಯಗ್ನಿ ಐತಶಾಯನ|ಅಭ್ಯ್|ಅಗ್ನಿ ಐತಶಾಯನ|abhy-agni aitaśāyana|abhyagni aitaśāyana|abhy|agni aitaśāyana|abhy-agni aitashaayana|abhyagni aitashaayana|agni aitashaayana|_vei_120@106|_vei1_pn029
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@105">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@107">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn028">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn029">1:029</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn030">↷</a><hr><p><b>Abhy-<a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> Aitaśāyana.</b>--This man was, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120106">1</a></sup> unfortunate enough to quarrel with his father, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतश">aitaśa</a>.</b> The result was that he and his progeny were called the worst of the <b>Aurvas.</b> In the version of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120106">2</a></sup> the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतशायन">aitaśāyana</a> Ājāneyas</b> take the place of the Abhyagnis and the <b>Bhṛgus</b> of the <b>Aurvas,</b> the latter being probably a branch of the former family.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120106">↑</a> vi. 33.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120106">↑</a> xxx. 5. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 173.</li></ol></div>

अभ्या-वर्तिन् चायमान|अभ्यावर्तिन् चायमान|अभ्या|वर्तिन् चायमान|అభ్యా-వర్తిన్ చాయమాన|అభ్యావర్తిన్ చాయమాన|అభ్యా|వర్తిన్ చాయమాన|ಅಭ್ಯಾ-ವರ್ತಿನ್ ಚಾಯಮಾನ|ಅಭ್ಯಾವರ್ತಿನ್ ಚಾಯಮಾನ|ಅಭ್ಯಾ|ವರ್ತಿನ್ ಚಾಯಮಾನ|abhyā-vartin cāyamāna|abhyāvartin cāyamāna|abhyā|vartin cāyamāna|abhyaa-vartin chaayamaana|abhyaavartin chaayamaana|abhyaa|vartin chaayamaana|_vei_120@107|_vei1_pn029
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@106">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@108">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn028">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn029">1:029</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn030">↷</a><hr><p><b>Abhyā-vartin Cāyamāna</b> appears in a Dānastuti (‘Praise of Gifts’) in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120107"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120107">1</a></sup> and as conqueror of the Vṛcīvants under the leader <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वरशिख">varaśikha</a>.</b> It is probable though not absolutely certain, that he is identical with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सृञ्जय">sṛñjaya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दैववात">daivavāta</a>,</b> mentioned in the same hymn<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120107"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120107">2</a></sup> as having the <b>Turvaśas</b> and <b>Vṛcīvants</b> defeated for him by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>. In this case he would be prince (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सम्राज्">samrāj</a></i>) of the Sṛñjayas. <a class="vei_120a" href="दैववात">daivavāta</a> is mentioned elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120107"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120107">3</a></sup> as a worshipper of <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>.</p><p>Abhyāvartin is also referred to as a <a class="vei_120a" href="पार्थव">pārthava</a>. Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120107"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120107">4</a></sup> and Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120107"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120107">5</a></sup> maintained that he is thus a Parthian, the latter using the evidence of the two places mentioned in the description<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120107"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120107">6</a></sup> of Daivavāta's victories, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हरियूपीया">hariyūpīyā</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यव्यावती">yavyāvatī</a>,</b> as proofs for the western position of Abhyāvartin's people in Arachosia, in Iran. But Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120107"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120107">7</a></sup> is probably right in holding that the name <a class="vei_120a" href="पार्थव">pārthava</a> merely means ‘a descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="पृथु">pṛthu</a>,’ and that its similarity to the Iranian Parthians is only on a par with the numerous other points of identity between the Indian and Iranian cultures. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120107"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120107">↑</a> vi. 27, 8. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120107"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120107">↑</a> vi. 27, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120107"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120107">↑</a> iv. 15, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120107"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120107">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 198 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120107"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120107">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 105; 3, 268, n. 1. <i>Cf.</i> Grierson, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 604 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120107"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120107">↑</a> Rv. vi. 27, 5. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120107"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120107">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 133 <i>et seq.,</i> 433; Bergaigne, <i>Religion Védique,</i> 2, 362.</li></ol></div>

अ-भ्रातरः|अभ्रातरः|भ्रातरः|అ-భ్రాతరః|అభ్రాతరః|భ్రాతరః|ಅ-ಭ್ರಾತರಃ|ಅಭ್ರಾತರಃ|ಭ್ರಾತರಃ|a-bhrātaraḥ|abhrātaraḥ|bhrātaraḥ|a-bhraataraH|abhraataraH|bhraataraH|_vei_120@108|_vei1_pn030
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@107">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@109">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn029">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn030">1:030</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn031">↷</a><hr><p><b>A-bhrātaraḥ</b> (‘brotherless’).--The lot of girls without brothers is referred to in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120108">1</a></sup> as unsatisfactory--apparently they became prostitutes. The <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120108">2</a></sup> expressly forbids marriage with a brotherless maiden, probably because of the risk of her being made a <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पुत्रिका">putrikā</a></i> (‘adoptive daughter’) by her father--that is, any son of hers being counted as belonging to her father's, instead of to her husband's, family. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अयोगू">ayogū</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120108">↑</a> i. 124, 7; iv. 5, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Av. i. 17, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120108">↑</a> iii. 5 (<i>abhrātrī</i>).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 259; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 328.</li></ol></div>

अभ्रि|అభ్రి|ಅಭ್ರಿ|abhri|_vei_120@109|_vei1_pn030
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@108">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@110">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn029">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn030">1:030</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn031">↷</a><hr><p><b>Abhri</b> (‘spade’) is a Vedic word<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120109"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120109">1</a></sup> of frequent occurrence. Various possible materials and forms are enumerated in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120109"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120109">2</a></sup> It may be made of bamboo or of the wood of <a class="vei_120a" href="विकंकत">vikaṅkata</a> or <a class="vei_120a" href="उदुम्बर">udumbara</a>. It may be a span or a cubit in size. It is hollow, and either one or both edges are sharp. From this it would appear that the handle was made of some wood, but the head of some metal.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120109"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120109">↑</a> Av. iv. 7, 5. 6 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अभ्रि">abhri</a>-khāte</i> is ‘dug out with a spade,’ not ‘prepared ground’ with Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i>); x. 4, 14; <i>hiraṇyayībhir abhribhiḥ,</i> ‘with golden spades’); Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, v. 22; xi. 10; xxxvii. 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvi. 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 3, 2, 15, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120109"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120109">↑</a> vi. 3, 1, 30 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

अमत्र|అమత్ర|ಅಮತ್ರ|amatra|_vei_120@110|_vei1_pn030
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@109">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@111">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn029">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn030">1:030</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn031">↷</a><hr><p><b>Amatra</b> was a vessel into which the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>, after being pressed, was poured,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120110"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120110">1</a></sup> and out of which the libation to the god was made.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120110"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120110">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120110"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120110">↑</a> Rv. ii. 14, 1; v. 51, 4; vi. 42, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120110"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120110">↑</a> Rv. x. 29, 7.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 278; Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a> Noten,</i> 1, 61.</li></ol></div>

अमला|అమలా|ಅಮಲಾ|amalā|amalaa|_vei_120@111|_vei1_pn030
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@110">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@112">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn029">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn030">1:030</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn031">↷</a><hr><p><b>Amalā.</b>--This plant<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120111"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120111">1</a></sup> is probably the <i>Emblica officinalis,</i> or Myrobalan tree; also called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आमलक">āmalaka</a>,</b> or <b>Āmalakā.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120111"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120111">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120111"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120111">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 38, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120111"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120111">↑</a> The reading in Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vii. 3, 1, is <i>vāmalake,</i> which may give <i>a¤°</i> or <i>ā°.</i></li></ol></div>

अमा-जुर्|अमाजुर्|अमा|जुर्|అమా-జుర్|అమాజుర్|అమా|జుర్|ಅಮಾ-ಜುರ್|ಅಮಾಜುರ್|ಅಮಾ|ಜುರ್|amā-jur|amājur|amā|jur|amaa-jur|amaajur|amaa|_vei_120@112|_vei1_pn030
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@111">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@113">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn029">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn030">1:030</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn031">↷</a><hr><p><b>Amā-jur</b> is an epithet<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120112">1</a></sup> denoting maidens ‘who grow old at home’ without finding husbands, or, as they are elsewhere called, ‘who sit with their father’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पितृ">pitṛ</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="षद्">ṣad</a></i>). A well-known example of such was <a class="vei_120a" href="घोषा">ghoṣā</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120112">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120112">↑</a> Rv. ii. 17, 7; viii. 21, 15; x. 37, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120112">↑</a> Rv. i. 117, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 305.</li></ol></div>

अमा-वास्य शाण्दिल्यायन|अमावास्य शाण्दिल्यायन|अमा|वास्य शाण्दिल्यायन|అమా-వాస్య శాణ్దిల్యాయన|అమావాస్య శాణ్దిల్యాయన|అమా|వాస్య శాణ్దిల్యాయన|ಅಮಾ-ವಾಸ್ಯ ಶಾಣ್ದಿಲ್ಯಾಯನ|ಅಮಾವಾಸ್ಯ ಶಾಣ್ದಿಲ್ಯಾಯನ|ಅಮಾ|ವಾಸ್ಯ ಶಾಣ್ದಿಲ್ಯಾಯನ|amā-vāsya śāṇdilyāyana|amāvāsya śāṇdilyāyana|amā|vāsya śāṇdilyāyana|amaa-vaasya shaaNdilyaayana|amaavaasya shaaNdilyaayana|amaa|vaasya shaaNdilyaayana|_vei_120@113|_vei1_pn031
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@112">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@114">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn030">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn031">1:031</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn032">↷</a><hr><p><b>Amā-vāsya Śāṇdilyāyana</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120113">1</a></sup> as the teacher of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अंशु">aṃśu</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धानंजय्य">dhānaṃjayya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120113">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> iv. 373.</li></ol></div>

अमित्र-तपन शुष्मिण शैब्य|अमित्रतपन शुष्मिण शैब्य|अमित्र|तपन शुष्मिण शैब्य|అమిత్ర-తపన శుష్మిణ శైబ్య|అమిత్రతపన శుష్మిణ శైబ్య|అమిత్ర|తపన శుష్మిణ శైబ్య|ಅಮಿತ್ರ-ತಪನ ಶುಷ್ಮಿಣ ಶೈಬ್ಯ|ಅಮಿತ್ರತಪನ ಶುಷ್ಮಿಣ ಶೈಬ್ಯ|ಅಮಿತ್ರ|ತಪನ ಶುಷ್ಮಿಣ ಶೈಬ್ಯ|amitra-tapana śuṣmiṇa śaibya|amitratapana śuṣmiṇa śaibya|amitra|tapana śuṣmiṇa śaibya|amitra-tapana shuShmiNa shaibya|amitratapana shuShmiNa shaibya|tapana shuShmiNa shaibya|_vei_120@114|_vei1_pn031
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@113">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@115">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn030">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn031">1:031</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn032">↷</a><hr><p><b>Amitra-tapana Śuṣmiṇa Śaibya</b> is the name of him who killed <b>Atyarāti <a class="vei_120a" href="जानंतपि">jānaṃtapi</a>,</b> according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 23).</p></div>

अ-मूला|अमूला|मूला|అ-మూలా|అమూలా|మూలా|ಅ-ಮೂಲಾ|ಅಮೂಲಾ|ಮೂಲಾ|a-mūlā|amūlā|mūlā|a-muulaa|amuulaa|muulaa|a-moolaa|amoolaa|moolaa|_vei_120@115|_vei1_pn031
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@114">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@116">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn030">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn031">1:031</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn032">↷</a><hr><p><b>A-mūlā</b> (‘without root’) is the name in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120115"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120115">1</a></sup> of a plant (<i>Methonica superba</i>), which was used for poisoning arrows. Bloomfield,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120115"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120115">2</a></sup> however, renders it as ‘movable property.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120115"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120115">↑</a> v. 314. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 286; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 279, accepts ‘rootless (plant).’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120115"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120115">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 457.</li></ol></div>

अम्बरीष|अंबरीष|అమ్బరీష|అంబరీష|ಅಮ್ಬರೀಷ|ಅಂಬರೀಷ|ambarīṣa|ambariiSha|ambareeSha|_vei_120@116|_vei1_pn031
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@115">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@117">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn030">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn031">1:031</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn032">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ambarīṣa</b> is mentioned as a <a class="vei_120a" href="वार्षागिर">vārṣāgira</a> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120116"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120116">1</a></sup> along with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋज्राश्व">ṛjrāśva</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सहदेव">sahadeva</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सुराधस्">surādhas</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भयमान">bhayamāna</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120116"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120116">↑</a> i. 100, 17. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 140.</li></ol></div>

अम्बष्ठ|अंबष्ठ|అమ్బష్ఠ|అంబష్ఠ|ಅಮ್ಬಷ್ಠ|ಅಂಬಷ್ಠ|ambaṣṭha|ambaShTha|_vei_120@117|_vei1_pn031
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@116">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@118">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn030">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn031">1:031</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn032">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ambaṣṭha.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आम्बष्ठ्य">āmbaṣṭhya</a>.</b></p></div>

अय|అయ|ಅಯ|aya|_vei_120@118|_vei1_pn031
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@117">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@119">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn030">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn031">1:031</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn032">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a>.</b></p></div>

अयस्|अय|అయస్|అయ|ಅಯಸ್|ಅಯ|ayas|aya|_vei_120@119|_vei1_pn031
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@118">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@120">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn030">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn031">1:031</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn032">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ayas.</b>--The exact metal denoted by this word when used by itself, as always in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120119">1</a></sup> is uncertain. As favouring the sense of ‘bronze’ rather than that of ‘iron’ may perhaps be cited with Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120119">2</a></sup> the fact that <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> is called <i>ayo-<a class="vei_120a" href="दंष्ट्र">daṃṣṭra</a>,</i> ‘with teeth of <a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्">ayas</a>,’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120119">3</a></sup> with reference to the colour of his flames, and that the car-seat of <a class="vei_120a" href="मित्र">mitra</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a> is called <i>ayaḥ-<a class="vei_120a" href="स्थूण">sthūṇa</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120119">4</a></sup> ‘with pillars of Ayas’ at the setting of the sun.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120119">5</a></sup> Moreover, in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120119">6</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्">ayas</a> is enumerated in a list of six metals: gold (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्य">hiraṇya</a></i>), <a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्">ayas</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="श्याम">śyāma</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="लोह">loha</a>, lead (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सीस">sīsa</a></i>), tin (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रपु">trapu</a></i>). Here <i><a class="vei_120a" href="श्याम">śyāma</a></i> (‘swarthy’) and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="लोह">loha</a></i> (‘red’) must mean ‘iron’ and ‘copper’ respectively; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्">ayas</a></i> would therefore seem to mean ‘bronze.’ In many passages in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120119">7</a></sup> and  other books, the <a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्">ayas</a> is divided into two species--the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="श्याम">śyāma</a></i> (‘iron’) and the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="लोहित">lohita</a></i> (‘copper’ or ‘bronze’). In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120119">8</a></sup> a distinction is drawn between <a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्">ayas</a> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="लोहायस">lohāyasa</a>,</i> which may either be a distinction between iron and copper as understood by Eggeling,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120119">9</a></sup> or between copper and bronze as held by Schrader.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120119">10</a></sup> In one passage of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120119">11</a></sup> the sense of iron seems certain. Possibly, too, the arrow of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120119">12</a></sup> which had a tip of <a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्">ayas</a> (<i>yasyā ayo mukham</i>), was pointed with iron. Copper, however, is conceivable, and bronze quite likely.</p><p>Iron is called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="श्याम">śyāma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्">ayas</a></i> or <i><a class="vei_120a" href="श्याम">śyāma</a></i> alone.<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120119">13</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कार्ष्णायस">kārṣṇāyasa</a>.</b> Copper is <b><a class="vei_120a" href="लोहायस">lohāyasa</a></b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="लोहितायस">lohitāyasa</a>.</b></p><p>The smelting (<i>dhmā</i> ‘to blow’) of the metal is frequently referred to. The Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120119">14</a></sup> states that if ‘well smelted’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="बहु">bahu</a>-dhmātam</i>) it is like gold, referring evidently to bronze. A heater of <a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्">ayas</a> is mentioned in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_120119">15</a></sup> and bowls of <a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्">ayas</a> are also spoken of.<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_120119">16</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120119">↑</a> Rv. i. 57, 3; 163, 9; iv. 2, 17; vi. 3, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120119">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 52.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120119">↑</a> Rv. i. 88, 5; x. 87, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120119">↑</a> Rv. v. 62, 8 (<i>cf.</i> 7).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120119">↑</a> But this is not convincing, as in the same verse it is said to be ‘of golden appearance at the flush of dawn.’</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120119">↑</a> xviii. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120119">↑</a> xi. 3, 1. 7; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120119">↑</a> v. 4, 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120119">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 90</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120119">↑</a> <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 189.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120119">↑</a> v. 28, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120119">↑</a> vi. 75, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120119">↑</a> Av. ix. 5, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120119">↑</a> vi. 1, 3, 5. <i>Cf.</i> vi. 1, 1, 13; v. 1, 2, 14; xii. 7, 1, 7; 2, 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_120119">↑</a> xxx. 14; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 10, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_120119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_120119">↑</a> Av. viii. 10, 22; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 13.</li></ol></div>

अय-स्थूण|अयस्थूण|अय|स्थूण|అయ-స్థూణ|అయస్థూణ|అయ|స్థూణ|ಅಯ-ಸ್ಥೂಣ|ಅಯಸ್ಥೂಣ|ಅಯ|ಸ್ಥೂಣ|aya-sthūṇa|ayasthūṇa|aya|sthūṇa|aya-sthuuNa|ayasthuuNa|sthuuNa|aya-sthooNa|ayasthooNa|sthooNa|_vei_120@120|_vei1_pn032
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@119">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@121">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn031">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn032">1:032</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn033">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aya-sthūṇa.</b>--He was Gṛhapati (‘householder,’ the sacrificer at sacrificial sessions) of those whose <a class="vei_120a" href="अध्वर्यु">adhvaryu</a> was <a class="vei_120a" href="शौल्वायन">śaulvāyana</a>, and taught the latter the proper mode of using certain spoons.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120120">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120120">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 4, 2, 17 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

अयास्य आंगिरस|अयास्य आङ्गिरस|అయాస్య ఆంగిరస|అయాస్య ఆఙ్గిరస|ಅಯಾಸ್ಯ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಅಯಾಸ್ಯ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|ayāsya āṅgirasa|ayaasya aaMgirasa|ayaasya aangirasa|_vei_120@121|_vei1_pn032
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@120">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@122">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn031">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn032">1:032</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn033">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ayāsya Āṅgirasa.</b>--This sage appears to be mentioned in two passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120121"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120121">1</a></sup> and the Anukramaṇī ascribes to him several hymns of the Rigveda (ix. 44-46; x. 67; 68). In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> tradition he was Udgātṛ at the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a> or Royal Inauguration Sacrifice, at which <a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a> was to have been slain, and his Udgītha (<a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a> chant) is referred to elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120121"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120121">3</a></sup> He is also referred to several times as a ritual</p><p>2) <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 16.  authority.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120121"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120121">4</a></sup> In the Vaṃśas, or Genealogies of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120121"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120121">5</a></sup> he is named as the pupil of Ābhūti <a class="vei_120a" href="त्वाष्ट्र">tvāṣṭra</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120121"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120121">↑</a> x. 67, 1; 108, 8. Perhaps x. 92. 15 also refers to him, but not i. 62, 7; x. 138, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120121"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120121">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 2. 6; 8, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 2, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120121"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120121">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 3, 22; xvi. 12, 4; xi. 8, 10; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 3, 8. 19. 24; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxx. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120121"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120121">↑</a> ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3 (in both recensions).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 136; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 159; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 255, n.; Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 204.</li></ol></div>

अयोगू|అయోగూ|ಅಯೋಗೂ|ayogū|ayoguu|ayogoo|_vei_120@122|_vei1_pn033
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@121">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@123">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn032">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn033">1:033</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn034">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ayogū</b> is a word of quite doubtful meaning, found in the list of victims in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120122"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120122">1</a></sup> It may, like the late <i>āyogava,</i> denote a member of a mixed caste (theoretically a descendant of a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> by a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> wife).<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120122"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120122">2</a></sup> Weber<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120122"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120122">3</a></sup> rendered it as ‘unchaste woman.’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120122"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120122">4</a></sup> Zimmer thinks it denotes a brotherless maiden who is exposed to the dangers of prostitution (<i>cf. āyogava</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120122"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120122">↑</a> xxx. 5; Taithrīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120122"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120122">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120122"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120122">↑</a> <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 76, n. b. In the <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 18, 277, he thought it referred to dicing (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्">ayas</a></i>). Risley, <i>Peoples of India,</i> 250, regards the Āyogavas as a functional caste of carpenters (<i>cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>, x. 48).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120122"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120122">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 328.</li></ol></div>

अर|అర|ಅರ|ara|_vei_120@123|_vei1_pn033
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@122">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@124">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn032">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn033">1:033</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn034">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ara.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a>.</b></p></div>

अरटु|అరటు|ಅರಟು|araṭu|araTu|_vei_120@124|_vei1_pn033
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@123">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@125">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn032">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn033">1:033</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn034">↷</a><hr><p><b>Araṭu.</b>--A plant<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120124">1</a></sup> (<i>Colosanthes Indica</i>) from the wood of which the axle of a chariot was sometimes made.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120124">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120124">↑</a> Av. xx. 131, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120124">↑</a> Rv. viii. 46, 27.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 62, 247.</li></ol></div>

अरण्य|అరణ్య|ಅರಣ್ಯ|araṇya|araNya|_vei_120@125|_vei1_pn033
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@124">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@126">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn032">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn033">1:033</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn034">↷</a><hr><p><b>Araṇya.</b>--This term denotes the uncultivated land--not necessarily forest land--beyond the village. It is contrasted with home (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अमा">amā</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120125">1</a></sup> and with the plough land (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कृषि">kṛṣi</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120125">2</a></sup> being spoken of as apart (<i>tiras</i>) from men.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120125">3</a></sup> It is also contrasted with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120125">4</a></sup> and it is the place where thieves live.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120125">5</a></sup> The character of the forest is described in a hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120125">6</a></sup> to the forest spirit (Araṇyānī). The dead are carried there for burial,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120125">7</a></sup> and hermits live there.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120125">8</a></sup> Forest fires were common.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120125">9</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120125">↑</a> Rv. vi. 24, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120125">↑</a> Av. ii. 4, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120125">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 6, 2, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120125">↑</a> Av. xii. 1, 56; Rv. i. 163, 11; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iii. 45; xx. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120125">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 2, 3, 5; xiii. 2, 4, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120125">↑</a> x. 146.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120125">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120125">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, viii. 5, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120125">↑</a> Rv. i. 65, 4; 94, 10. 11; ii. 14, 2; x. 92, 1; 142, 4; Av. vii. 50, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 48, 142.</li></ol></div>

अरत्नि|అరత్ని|ಅರತ್ನಿ|aratni|_vei_120@126|_vei1_pn034
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@125">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@127">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn033">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn034">1:034</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn035">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aratni.</b>--This word, which primarily means ‘elbow,’ occurs frequently from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120126"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120126">1</a></sup> onwards as denoting a measure of length (‘ell’ or ‘cubit’), the distance from the elbow to the tip of the hand. The exact length nowhere appears from the early texts.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120126"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120126">↑</a> Rv. viii. 80, 8; Av. xix. 57, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 5; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 3, 1, 33, etc. For Rv. viii. 80, 8, see also <a class="vei_120a" href="आजि">āji</a>.</li></ol></div>

अ-राजानः|अराजानः|राजानः|అ-రాజానః|అరాజానః|రాజానః|ಅ-ರಾಜಾನಃ|ಅರಾಜಾನಃ|ರಾಜಾನಃ|a-rājānaḥ|arājānaḥ|rājānaḥ|a-raajaanaH|araajaanaH|raajaanaH|_vei_120@127|_vei1_pn034
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@126">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@128">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn033">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn034">1:034</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn035">↷</a><hr><p><b>A-rājānaḥ,</b> ‘not princes,’ is a term used to describe persons in two passages of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120127"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120127">1</a></sup> and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120127"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120127">2</a></sup> Weber<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120127"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120127">3</a></sup> also finds them mentioned in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120127"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120127">4</a></sup> and thinks that they--<b>Sūtas</b> (‘charioteers’) and <b>Grāmaṇīs</b> (‘troop-leaders’) there referred to--were called thus because, while not themselves princes, they assisted in the consecration of princes.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120127"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120127">↑</a> iii. 4, 1, 7. 8; xiii. 4, 2, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120127"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120127">↑</a> viii. 23.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120127"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120127">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 199.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120127"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120127">↑</a> iii. 5, 7, where he emends <i><a class="vei_120a" href="राजानः">rājānaḥ</a></i> to <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अ-राजानः">a-rājānaḥ</a></i> (see Whitney's note on the passage). <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveḍa,</i> 333; Weber, <i>Über den <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>,</i> 22 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

अराटकी|अराटकि|అరాటకీ|అరాటకి|ಅರಾಟಕೀ|ಅರಾಟಕಿ|arāṭakī|arāṭaki|araaTakii|araaTaki|araaTakee|_vei_120@128|_vei1_pn034
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@127">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@129">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn033">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn034">1:034</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn035">↷</a><hr><p><b>Arāṭakī</b> is a plant mentioned once in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120128"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120128">1</a></sup> and apparently identical with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अजशृंगी">ajaśṛṅgī</a>.</b> <i>Cf.</i> also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अरटु">araṭu</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120128"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120128">↑</a> iv. 37, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 68; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 408.</li></ol></div>

अराड दात्रेय शौनक|అరాడ దాత్రేయ శౌనక|ಅರಾಡ ದಾತ್ರೇಯ ಶೌನಕ|arāḍa dātreya śaunaka|araaDa daatreya shaunaka|_vei_120@129|_vei1_pn034
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@128">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@130">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn033">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn034">1:034</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn035">↷</a><hr><p><b>Arāḍa <a class="vei_120a" href="दात्रेय">dātreya</a> Śaunaka.</b>--He is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120129"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120129">1</a></sup> as the pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृति ऐन्द्रोत">dṛti aindrota</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शौनक">śaunaka</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120129"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120129">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 384.</li></ol></div>

अरित्र|అరిత్ర|ಅರಿತ್ರ|aritra|_vei_120@130|_vei1_pn034
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@129">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@131">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn033">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn034">1:034</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn035">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aritra</b> denotes the ‘oar’ by which boats were propelled. The Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120130">1</a></sup> and the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120130">2</a></sup> speak of a vessel with a hundred oars, and a boat (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="नौ">nau</a></i>) is said to be ‘propelled by oars’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अरित्र">aritra</a>-paraṇa</i>).<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120130">3</a></sup> In two passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120130">4</a></sup> the term, according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, denotes a part of a chariot. The rower of a boat is called <i>aritṛ.</i><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120130">5</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नौ">nau</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120130">↑</a> i. 116, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120130">↑</a> xxi. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120130">↑</a> Rv. x. 101, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 2, 5, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120130">↑</a> i. 46, 8; <i>daśāritra,</i> ii. 18, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120130">↑</a> Rv. ii. 42, 1; ix. 95, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 256.</li></ol></div>

अरिं-दम सन-श्रुत|अरिं-दम सनश्रुत|अरिं-दम सन|श्रुत|అరిం-దమ సన-శ్రుత|అరిం-దమ సనశ్రుత|అరిం-దమ సన|శ్రుత|ಅರಿಂ-ದಮ ಸನ-ಶ್ರುತ|ಅರಿಂ-ದಮ ಸನಶ್ರುತ|ಅರಿಂ-ದಮ ಸನ|ಶ್ರುತ|ariṃ-dama sana-śruta|ariṃ-dama sanaśruta|ariṃ-dama sana|śruta|ariM-dama sana-shruta|ariM-dama sanashruta|ariM-dama sana|shruta|_vei_120@131|_vei1_pn035
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@130">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@132">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn034">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn035">1:035</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn036">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ariṃ-<a class="vei_120a" href="दम">dama</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सन">sana</a>-śruta</b> is mentioned as a <a class="vei_120a" href="महाराज">mahārāja</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 34.</p></div>

अरिम्-एजय|अरिमेजय|अरिम्|एजय|अरिं|అరిమ్-ఏజయ|అరిమేజయ|అరిమ్|ఏజయ|అరిం|ಅರಿಮ್-ಏಜಯ|ಅರಿಮೇಜಯ|ಅರಿಮ್|ಏಜಯ|ಅರಿಂ|arim-ejaya|arimejaya|arim|ejaya|_vei_120@132|_vei1_pn035
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@131">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@133">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn034">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn035">1:035</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn036">↷</a><hr><p><b>Arim-ejaya</b> is mentioned as one who served as <a class="vei_120a" href="अध्वर्यु">adhvaryu</a> at the snake feast celebrated in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120132"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120132">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120132"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120132">↑</a> xxv. 15. See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 35.</li></ol></div>

अरुण आट|అరుణ ఆట|ಅರುಣ ಆಟ|aruṇa āṭa|aruNa aaTa|_vei_120@133|_vei1_pn035
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@132">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@134">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn034">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn035">1:035</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn036">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aruṇa Āṭa</b> was Achāvāka at the snake feast in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xxv. 15).</p></div>

अरुण औपवेशि गौतम|అరుణ ఔపవేశి గౌతమ|ಅರುಣ ಔಪವೇಶಿ ಗೌತಮ|aruṇa aupaveśi gautama|aruNa aupaveshi gautama|_vei_120@134|_vei1_pn035
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@133">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@135">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn034">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn035">1:035</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn036">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aruṇa <a class="vei_120a" href="औपवेशि">aupaveśi</a> Gautama</b> is the full style<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120134"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120134">1</a></sup> of a teacher, who is repeatedly referred to in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120134"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120134">2</a></sup> and Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120134"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120134">3</a></sup> and whose son was the famous <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उद्दालक आरुणि">uddālaka āruṇi</a>.</b> He was a pupil of Upaveśa,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120134"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120134">4</a></sup> and a contemporary of the prince <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वपति">aśvapati</a>,</b> by whom he was instructed. <i>Cf.</i> <b>Āruṇa.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120134"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120134">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 9, 2; 4, 5, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 4, 10; iii. 6, 4. 6; 7, 4; 8, 6; 10, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxvi. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120134"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120134">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 5, 11; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 2, 2, 20; xi. 4, 1, 4; 5, 3, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120134"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120134">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 5, 3 (in both recensions).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120134"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120134">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 6, 1, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 146, n.^4</li></ol></div>

अरुन्धती|अरुन्धती|अरुन्धति|అరున్ధతీ_౧|అరున్ధతీ|అరున్ధతి|ಅರುನ್ಧತೀ_೧|ಅರುನ್ಧತೀ|ಅರುನ್ಧತಿ|arundhatī_1|arundhatī|arundhati|arundhatii_1|arundhatii|arundhatee_1|arundhatee|_vei_120@135|_vei1_pn035
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@134">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@136">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn034">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn035">1:035</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn036">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Arundhatī</b> is the name of a plant celebrated in several passages of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120135"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120135">1</a></sup> as possessing healing properties in case of wounds, as a febrifuge, and as inducing cows to give milk. The plant was a climber which attached itself to trees like the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्लक्ष">plakṣa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वत्थ">aśvattha</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="न्यग्रोध">nyagrodha</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्ण">parṇa</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120135"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120135">2</a></sup> It was of golden colour (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्य">hiraṇya</a>-varṇā</i>), and had a hairy stem (<i>lomaśavakṣaṇā</i>).<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120135"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120135">3</a></sup> It was also called <a class="vei_120a" href="सिलाची">silācī</a>, and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="लाक्षा">lākṣā</a></b> appears to have been a product of it.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120135"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120135">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120135"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120135">↑</a> iv. 12, 1; v. 5, 5. 9; vi. 59, 1. 2; viii. 7, 6; xix. 38, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120135"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120135">↑</a> Av. v. 5, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120135"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120135">↑</a> Av. v. 5, 7; Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 174; Bloomfield, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 574.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120135"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120135">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Whitney's note on Av. iv. 12; Bloomfield, <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 61.</li></ol></div>

अरुन्धती|अरुन्धती|अरुन्धति|అరున్ధతీ_౨|అరున్ధతీ|అరున్ధతి|ಅರುನ್ಧತೀ_೨|ಅರುನ್ಧತೀ|ಅರುನ್ಧತಿ|arundhatī_2|arundhatī|arundhati|arundhatii_2|arundhatii|arundhatee_2|arundhatee|_vei_120@136|_vei1_pn035
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@135">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@137">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn034">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn035">1:035</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn036">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Arundhatī,</b> as the name of a star, is often referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> literature, but only once in a late Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120136"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120136">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120136"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120136">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 9, 2.</li></ol></div>

अर्क|అర్క|ಅರ್ಕ|arka|_vei_120@137|_vei1_pn036
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@136">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@138">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn035">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn036">1:036</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn037">↷</a><hr><p><b>Arka,</b> as the name of the tree <i>Colotropis gigantea,</i> is perhaps found in one obscure passage of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120137"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120137">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120137"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120137">↑</a> vi. 72, 1, where see Whitney's note. <i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

अर्गल|అర్గల|ಅರ್ಗಲ|argala|_vei_120@138|_vei1_pn036
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@137">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@139">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn035">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn036">1:036</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn037">↷</a><hr><p><b>Argala.</b>--The word which is usual later to denote the wooden pin of a door is found in the Śāṇkhāyana Āraṇyaka (ii. 16) in the compound <i>argaleṣīke</i> to denote the pin and bar of the door of a cow-pen. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इषीका">iṣīkā</a>.</b></p></div>

अर्गल-काहोडि|अर्गलकाहोडि|अर्गल|काहोडि|అర్గల-కాహోడి|అర్గలకాహోడి|అర్గల|కాహోడి|ಅರ್ಗಲ-ಕಾಹೋಡಿ|ಅರ್ಗಲಕಾಹೋಡಿ|ಅರ್ಗಲ|ಕಾಹೋಡಿ|argala-kāhoḍi|argalakāhoḍi|argala|kāhoḍi|argala-kaahoDi|argalakaahoDi|kaahoDi|_vei_120@139|_vei1_pn036
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@138">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@140">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn035">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn036">1:036</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn037">↷</a><hr><p><b>Argala-Kāhoḍi</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Samhitā (xxv. 7) as a teacher. The name may, however, be <a class="vei_120a" href="अर्यल">aryala</a>, as suggested in the St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> and as read by v. Schroeder in his edition. The Kapiṣṭhala (xxxix. 5) has Ayala. See below.</p></div>

अर्चन्त्|అర్చన్త్|ಅರ್ಚನ್ತ್|arcant|archant|_vei_120@140|_vei1_pn036
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@139">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@141">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn035">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn036">1:036</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn037">↷</a><hr><p><b>Arcant</b> is possibly, as Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120140"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120140">1</a></sup> thinks, the name of the author of a hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120140"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120140">2</a></sup> ; but the word may be merely the ordinary participle ‘praising.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120140"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120140">↑</a> Translation of the Ṛigveda, 3, 133.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120140"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120140">↑</a> x. 149, 5.</li></ol></div>

अर्चनानस्|अर्चनान|అర్చనానస్|అర్చనాన|ಅರ್ಚನಾನಸ್|ಅರ್ಚನಾನ|arcanānas|arcanāna|archanaanas|archanaana|_vei_120@141|_vei1_pn036
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@140">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@142">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn035">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn036">1:036</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn037">↷</a><hr><p><b>Arcanānas.</b>--In one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120141"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120141">1</a></sup> the gods MitraVaruṇa are besought to protect <a class="vei_120a" href="अर्चनानस्">arcanānas</a>. He is also invoked with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्यावाश्व">śyāvāśva</a></b> and several other ancestors enumerated in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120141"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120141">2</a></sup> He appears as father of <a class="vei_120a" href="श्यावाश्व">śyāvāśva</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120141"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120141">3</a></sup> The later tradition makes him play a part in the legend of his son's wedding, which Sieg<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120141"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120141">4</a></sup> endeavours to show is known to the Rigveda.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120141"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120141">↑</a> v. 64, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120141"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120141">↑</a> xviii. 3, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120141"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120141">↑</a> viii. 5, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120141"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120141">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 50 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 127; Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 354.</li></ol></div>

अर्चा|అర్చా|ಅರ್ಚಾ|arcā|archaa|_vei_120@142|_vei1_pn036
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@141">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@143">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn035">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn036">1:036</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn037">↷</a><hr><p><b>Arcā.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.</b></p></div>

अर्जुनी|अर्जुनि|అర్జునీ|అర్జుని|ಅರ್ಜುನೀ|ಅರ್ಜುನಿ|arjunī|arjuni|arjunii|arjunee|_vei_120@143|_vei1_pn036
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@142">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@144">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn035">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn036">1:036</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn037">↷</a><hr><p><b>Arjunī</b> is, in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120143"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120143">1</a></sup> the name of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a></b> (‘lunar mansion’), elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120143"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120143">2</a></sup> called <a class="vei_120a" href="फल्गुनी">phalgunī</a>. It occurs in the marriage hymn, with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अघा">aghā</a></b> for <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मघा">maghā</a>,</b> and, like that word, is apparently a deliberate modification. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120143"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120143">↑</a> x. 85, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120143"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120143">↑</a> Av. xiv. 1, 13. <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 11, 2.</li></ol></div>

अर्बुद|అర్బుద|ಅರ್ಬುದ|arbuda|_vei_120@144|_vei1_pn037
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@143">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@145">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn036">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn037">1:037</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn038">↷</a><hr><p><b>Arbuda</b> is mentioned as Grāvastut priest at the snake festival described in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120144">1</a></sup> He is obviously the same mythical figure as <a class="vei_120a" href="अर्बुद">arbuda</a> Kādraveya, a seer spoken of in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120144">2</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120144">3</a></sup> as a maker of Mantras.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120144">↑</a> xxv. 15.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120144">↑</a> vi. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120144">↑</a> xxix. 1. <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 3, 9.</li></ol></div>

अर्य|అర్య|ಅರ್ಯ|arya|_vei_120@145|_vei1_pn037
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@144">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@146">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn036">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn037">1:037</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn038">↷</a><hr><p><b>Arya.</b>--This word is not common in the older literature, in places where the quantity of the first vowel is fixed as short, except in a mere adjectival sense. Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120145">1</a></sup> indeed, contends that no other sense is anywhere needed; but Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120145">2</a></sup> and Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120145">3</a></sup> agree in thinking that in several passages of the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120145">4</a></sup> the word has the same sense as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a>,</b> and this appears probable. Whether it is necessary to ascribe this sense to the word in the compound <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अर्य">arya</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पत्नी">patnī</a></i><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120145">5</a></sup> applied to the waters set free by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, is more doubtful. The commentator, Mahīdhara,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120145">6</a></sup> suggests that the word means a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>,</b> not an <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a></b> generally. This view is supported by the explanation in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120145">7</a></sup> of one of the passages of the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120145">8</a></sup> But though the use of <a class="vei_120a" href="अर्य">arya</a> to denote a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> became common later, it is not clear that it was original.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120145">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 96.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120145">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120145">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 214, 215.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120145">↑</a> xiv. 30; xx. 17; xxiii. 21; xxvi. 2. <i>Cf.</i> also <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxviii. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 4, 19, 3. In Atharvaveda, xix. 32, 8, an analogous form occurs, as contrasted with <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a>,</i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>;</i> but even there Whitney renders it as ‘Āryan.’ <i>Cf.</i> xix. 62, 1; Rv. viii. 94, 3; Pischel, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 40, 125.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120145">↑</a> Rv. vii. 6, 5; x. 43, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120145">↑</a> On Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 30.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120145">↑</a> xiii. 2, 9, 8. Contrast <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 4, 4 <i>et seq.,</i> where <a class="vei_120a" href="अर्य">arya</a> appears to be taken in the wider sense.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120145">↑</a> xxiii. 30. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 212; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> x. 6; Oldenberg, <i>ṚgvedaNoten,</i> 1, 126, 363.</li></ol></div>

अर्यम्णः पन्था|అర్యమ్ణః పన్థా|ಅರ್ಯಮ್ಣಃ ಪನ್ಥಾ|aryamṇaḥ panthā|aryamNaH panthaa|_vei_120@146|_vei1_pn037
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@145">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@147">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn036">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn037">1:037</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn038">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aryamṇaḥ Panthā,</b> ‘Aryaman's Way,’ an expression which occurs in the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120146"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120146">1</a></sup> denotes, according to Weber,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120146"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120146">2</a></sup> ‘the milky way,’ but, according to Hillebrandt,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120146"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120146">3</a></sup> ‘the ecliptic.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120146"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120146">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 6, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 12, 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120146"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120146">↑</a> <i>Über den <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>,</i> 48, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120146"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120146">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 79, 80.</li></ol></div>

अर्यल|అర్యల|ಅರ್ಯಲ|aryala|_vei_120@147|_vei1_pn038
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@146">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@148">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn037">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn038">1:038</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn039">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aryala.</b>--Those at whose snake feast the Gṛhapati was <a class="vei_120a" href="अर्यल">aryala</a>, and the <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a> was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आरुणि">āruṇi</a></b> are mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xxiii. 1, 5). See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अर्गल">argala</a>.</b></p></div>

अर्वन्त्|అర్వన్త్|ಅರ್ವನ್ತ್|arvant|_vei_120@148|_vei1_pn038
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@147">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@149">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn037">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn038">1:038</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn039">↷</a><hr><p><b>Arvant.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्व">aśva</a>.</b></p></div>

अर्शस्|अर्श|అర్శస్|అర్శ|ಅರ್ಶಸ್|ಅರ್ಶ|arśas|arśa|arshas|arsha|_vei_120@149|_vei1_pn038
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@148">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@150">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn037">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn038">1:038</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn039">↷</a><hr><p><b>Arśas</b> is the name of a disease mentioned in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120149"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120149">1</a></sup> with consumption and other ailments. It appears to designate ‘hæmorrhoids,’ as in the later medical literature.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120149"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120149">↑</a> xii. 98.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 398; Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

अलज|అలజ|ಅಲಜ|alaja|_vei_120@150|_vei1_pn038
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@149">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@151">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn037">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn038">1:038</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn039">↷</a><hr><p><b>Alaja</b> designates some kind of bird--one of the victims in the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120150"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120150">1</a></sup> or Horse Sacrifice.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120150"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120150">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 4, 11, 1; 5, 20, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxi. 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 34.</li></ol></div>

अलजि|అలజి|ಅಲಜಿ|alaji|_vei_120@151|_vei1_pn038
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@150">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@152">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn037">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn038">1:038</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn039">↷</a><hr><p><b>Alaji</b> is the name of a disease in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120151"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120151">1</a></sup> The later <i>alajī</i> denotes an eye-disease--a discharge at the junction of the cornea and the sclerotica.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120151"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120151">↑</a> ix. 8, 20. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 390; St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

अलम्म पारिजानत|అలమ్మ పారిజానత|ಅಲಮ್ಮ ಪಾರಿಜಾನತ|alamma pārijānata|alamma paarijaanata|_vei_120@152|_vei1_pn038
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@151">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@153">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn037">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn038">1:038</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn039">↷</a><hr><p><b>Alamma Pārijānata</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 4, 11; 10, 8) as a sage.</p></div>

अलसाला|అలసాలా|ಅಲಸಾಲಾ|alasālā|alasaalaa|_vei_120@153|_vei1_pn038
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@152">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@154">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn037">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn038">1:038</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn039">↷</a><hr><p><b>Alasālā.</b>--This word occurring only in one passage of the Atharvaveda (vi. 16, 4) is said to denote a grain-creeper.</p></div>

अलाण्डु|అలాణ్డు|ಅಲಾಣ್ಡು|alāṇḍu|alaaNDu|_vei_120@154|_vei1_pn038
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@153">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@155">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn037">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn038">1:038</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn039">↷</a><hr><p><b>Alāṇḍu.</b>--This is the reading in the text of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120154"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120154">1</a></sup> of the name of a species of worm. Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120154"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120154">2</a></sup> shows reason to adopt the reading Algaṇḍu as the correct form of the word.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120154"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120154">↑</a> ii. 31, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120154"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120154">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 315.</li></ol></div>

अलाबु|అలాబు|ಅಲಾಬು|alābu|alaabu|_vei_120@155|_vei1_pn038
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@154">«</a>pada9<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@156">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn037">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn038">1:038</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn039">↷</a><hr><p><b>Alābu.</b>--The bottle-gourd (<i>Lagenaria vulgaris</i>). Vessels made of it are referred to in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120155"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120155">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120155"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120155">↑</a> viii. 10, 29. 30; xx. 132, 1. 2. <i>Cf. alāpu</i> in Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 13.</li></ol></div>

अलाय्य|అలాయ్య|ಅಲಾಯ್ಯ|alāyya|alaayya|_vei_120@156|_vei1_pn038
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@155">«</a>pada10<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@157">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn037">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn038">1:038</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn039">↷</a><hr><p><b>Alāyya</b> is a word occurring in an obscure verse of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120156"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120156">1</a></sup> and appearing to be a proper name. Hillebrandt,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120156"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120156">2</a></sup> however, amends the text so as to remove the name. The St. Petersburg Dictionary suggests that it refers to <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>. Pischel<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120156"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120156">3</a></sup> holds that  it is the name of a person whose axe was stolen, and for whom the hymn was written as a spell for the recovery of the axe.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120156"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120156">↑</a> ix. 67, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120156"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120156">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 418.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120156"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120156">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 701.</li></ol></div>

अलिक्लव|అలిక్లవ|ಅಲಿಕ್ಲವ|aliklava|_vei_120@157|_vei1_pn039
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@156">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@158">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn038">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn039">1:039</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn040">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aliklava</b> is a kind of carrion bird mentioned in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120157"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120157">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120157"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120157">↑</a> xi. 2, 2; 9, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 88.</li></ol></div>

अलिन|అలిన|ಅಲಿನ|alina|_vei_120@158|_vei1_pn039
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@157">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@159">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn038">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn039">1:039</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn040">↷</a><hr><p><b>Alina</b> is the name of a people mentioned once only in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120158">1</a></sup> Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120158">2</a></sup> thought that the Alinas were allies--possibly a subdivision--of the <b>Tṛtsus.</b> Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120158">3</a></sup> more probably thinks that they were defeated by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>,</b> together with the <b>Pakthas, <a class="vei_120a" href="भलानस्">bhalānas</a>, Śivas,</b> and <b>Viṣāṇins,</b> with whom they are mentioned, at the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परुष्णी">paruṣṇī</a>;</b> and Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120158">4</a></sup> suggests that they lived to the north-east of Kafiristan.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120158">↑</a> vii. 18, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120158">↑</a> <i>Zur Litteratur und Geschichte des Weda,</i> 95; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 126.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120158">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 207.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120158">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 431. The land is mentioned by Hiouen Thsang.</li></ol></div>

अलीकयु वाचस्-पत्य|अलीकयु वाचस्पत्य|अलीकयु वाचस्|अलीकयु वाच|पत्य|అలీకయు వాచస్-పత్య|అలీకయు వాచస్పత్య|అలీకయు వాచస్|అలీకయు వాచ|పత్య|ಅಲೀಕಯು ವಾಚಸ್-ಪತ್ಯ|ಅಲೀಕಯು ವಾಚಸ್ಪತ್ಯ|ಅಲೀಕಯು ವಾಚಸ್|ಅಲೀಕಯು ವಾಚ|ಪತ್ಯ|alīkayu vācas-patya|alīkayu vācaspatya|alīkayu vācas|alīkayu vāca|patya|aliikayu vaachas-patya|aliikayu vaachaspatya|aliikayu vaachas|aliikayu vaacha|aleekayu vaachas-patya|aleekayu vaachaspatya|aleekayu vaachas|aleekayu vaacha|_vei_120@159|_vei1_pn039
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@158">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@160">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn038">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn039">1:039</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn040">↷</a><hr><p><b>Alīkayu Vācas-patya</b> is twice mentioned as an authority in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xxvi. 5; xxviii. 4).</p></div>

अल्प-शयु|अल्पशयु|अल्प|शयु|అల్ప-శయు|అల్పశయు|అల్ప|శయు|ಅಲ್ಪ-ಶಯು|ಅಲ್ಪಶಯು|ಅಲ್ಪ|ಶಯು|alpa-śayu|alpaśayu|alpa|śayu|alpa-shayu|alpashayu|shayu|_vei_120@160|_vei1_pn039
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@159">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@161">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn038">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn039">1:039</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn040">↷</a><hr><p><b>Alpa-śayu</b> is a kind of insect mentioned in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120160"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120160">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120160"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120160">↑</a> iv. 36, 9.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 408; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 210.</li></ol></div>

अवका|అవకా|ಅವಕಾ|avakā|avakaa|_vei_120@161|_vei1_pn039
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@160">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@162">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn038">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn039">1:039</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn040">↷</a><hr><p><b>Avakā</b> is an aquatic plant (<i>Blyxa octandra</i>) frequently referred to in the Atharvaveda as well as in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120161"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120161">2</a></sup> and Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120161"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120161">3</a></sup> The Gandharvas are said to eat it.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120161"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120161">4</a></sup> Its later name is <i>śaivala,</i> and it is identical with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शीपाल">śīpāla</a>.</b></p><p>1) viii. 7, 9; 37, 8-10.</p><p>5) With which it is glossed in Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 8; iv. 4. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Proceedings of the American Oriental Society,</i> October, 1890, xli.-xliii.; <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 11, 349; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 71.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="2"><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120161"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120161">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 6, 1, 1; v. 4, 2, 1; 4, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvii. 4; xxv. 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 10, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120161"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120161">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 5, 1, 11; viii. 3, 2, 5; ix. 1, 2, 20. 22; xiii. 8, 3, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120161"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120161">↑</a> Av. iv. 37, 8.</li></ol></div>

अवत|అవత|ಅವತ|avata|_vei_120@162|_vei1_pn039
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@161">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@163">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn038">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn039">1:039</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn040">↷</a><hr><p><b>Avata,</b> a word occurring several times in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120162">1</a></sup> denotes a well, artificially made (<i>khan</i> ‘to dig’) in contrast with a spring  (<i>utsa</i>), though the latter expression is also applied to an artificial well. Such wells were covered by the makers,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120162">2</a></sup> and are described as unfailing (<i>a-<a class="vei_120a" href="क्षित">kṣita</a></i>) and full of water.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120162">3</a></sup> The water was raised by a wheel (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="चक्र">cakra</a></i>) of stone, to which was fastened a strap (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वरत्रा">varatrā</a></i>), with a pail (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कोश">kośa</a></i>)<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120162">4</a></sup> attached to it. When raised it was poured (<i>siñc</i>) into buckets (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="आहाव">āhāva</a></i>) of wood.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120162">5</a></sup> Sometimes those wells appear to have been used for irrigation purposes, the water being led off into broad channels (<i>sārmī suṣirā</i>).<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120162">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120162">↑</a> i. 55, 8; 85, 10. 11; 116, 9, 22; 130, 2; iv. 17, 16; 50, 3; viii. 49, 6; 62, 6; 72, 10, 12; x. 25, 4; 101, 5. 7. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 26.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120162">↑</a> Rv. i. 55, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120162">↑</a> Rv. x. 101, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120162">↑</a> <i>Aṃsatra-kośam,</i> Rv. x. 101, 7, is best thus rendered. For the stone wheel (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्म">aśma</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="चक्र">cakra</a></i>) which was above (<i>uccācakra</i>), see Rv. x. 101, 7; viii. 72, 10. For the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वरत्रा">varatrā</a>,</i> see Rv. x. 101, 6. Perhaps <i>kū-<a class="vei_120a" href="चक्र">cakra</a></i> in Rv. x. 102, 11, is another name for the wheel, but <i>cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120162">↑</a> Rv. x. 101, 6. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120162">↑</a> Rv. viii. 69, 12. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 156, 157; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 14.</li></ol></div>

अवत्सार|అవత్సార|ಅವತ್ಸಾರ|avatsāra|avatsaara|_vei_120@163|_vei1_pn040
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@162">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@164">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn039">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn040">1:040</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn041">↷</a><hr><p><b>Avatsāra</b> is mentioned as a seer in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120163">1</a></sup> as a priest in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120163">2</a></sup> and as <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रास्रवण">prāsravaṇa</a> (or <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राश्रवण">prāśravaṇa</a>), son of <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रस्रवण">prasravaṇa</a>, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120163">3</a></sup> A hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120163">4</a></sup> is incorrectly ascribed to him in the Anukramaṇī.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120163">↑</a> v. 44. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120163">↑</a> ii. 24.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120163">↑</a> xiii. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120163">↑</a> ix. 58. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 138; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 188; 2, 315; Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 62, <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

अ-वध्यता|अवध्यता|वध्यता|అ-వధ్యతా|అవధ్యతా|వధ్యతా|ಅ-ವಧ್ಯತಾ|ಅವಧ್ಯತಾ|ವಧ್ಯತಾ|a-vadhyatā|avadhyatā|vadhyatā|a-vadhyataa|avadhyataa|vadhyataa|_vei_120@164|_vei1_pn040
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@163">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@165">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn039">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn040">1:040</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn041">↷</a><hr><p><b>A-vadhyatā.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.</b></p></div>

अवस|అవస|ಅವಸ|avasa|_vei_120@165|_vei1_pn040
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@164">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@166">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn039">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn040">1:040</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn041">↷</a><hr><p><b>Avasa</b> in the adjective <i>an-<a class="vei_120a" href="अवस">avasa</a></i> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120165"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120165">1</a></sup> may mean ‘drag.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120165"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120165">↑</a> vi. 66, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 372.</li></ol></div>

अव-स्कव|अवस्कव|अव|स्कव|అవ-స్కవ|అవస్కవ|అవ|స్కవ|ಅವ-ಸ್ಕವ|ಅವಸ್ಕವ|ಅವ|ಸ್ಕವ|ava-skava|avaskava|ava|skava|_vei_120@166|_vei1_pn040
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@165">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@167">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn039">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn040">1:040</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn041">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ava-skava.</b>--A kind of worm, mentioned along with others in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120166"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120166">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120166"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120166">↑</a> ii. 31, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, <i>ad loc.;</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 201; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 90; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 316.</li></ol></div>

अवात|అవాత|ಅವಾತ|avāta|avaata|_vei_120@167|_vei1_pn040
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@166">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@168">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn039">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn040">1:040</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn041">↷</a><hr><p><b>Avāta.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वात">vāta</a>.</b></p></div>

अवि|అవి|ಅವಿ|avi|_vei_120@168|_vei1_pn040
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@167">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@169">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn039">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn040">1:040</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn041">↷</a><hr><p><b>Avi.</b>--‘Sheep’ are repeatedly mentioned in the Rigveda, and later, often in conjunction with goats (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अज">aja</a></i>). The wolf (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वृक">vṛka</a></i>) was their great enemy,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120168">1</a></sup> and they were tended by shepherds.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120168">2</a></sup>  Sheep as well as kine were captured from the enemy.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120168">3</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> sieve was made of sheep's wool, and is repeatedly referred to (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अवि">avi</a>, meṣī, avya, avyaya</i>).<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120168">4</a></sup> Considerable herds must have existed, as <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋज्राश्व">ṛjrāśva</a> is said<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120168">5</a></sup> to have slain one hundred rams, and in a Dānastuti (‘Praise of Gifts’)<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120168">6</a></sup> a hundred sheep are mentioned as a gift. The (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="मेष">meṣa</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120168">7</a></sup> <i>vṛṣṇi</i><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120168">8</a></sup> ) ram was sometimes castrated (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पेत्व">petva</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120168">9</a></sup> The main use of sheep was their wool; hence the expression ‘woolly’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऊर्णावती">ūrṇāvatī</a></i>)<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120168">10</a></sup> is employed to designate a sheep. In the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120168">11</a></sup> the ram is described as ‘woolly,’ and as ‘the skin of beasts, quadruped and biped,’ with reference to the use of its wool as clothing for men and shelter for animals. Pūṣan is said<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120168">12</a></sup> to weave raiment from the wool of sheep. Normally the sheep stayed out at pasture; in an obscure passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120168">13</a></sup> reference appears to be made to rams in stall. <a class="vei_120a" href="गन्धार">gandhāra</a><sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120168">14</a></sup> ewes were famous for their wool. Pischel<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_120168">15</a></sup> considers that the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परुष्णी">paruṣṇī</a></b><sup id="ref_120_16_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_120168">16</a></sup> was named from its richness in sheep, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="परुस्">parus</a></i><sup id="ref_120_17_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_120168">17</a></sup> denoting the ‘flocks’ of wool.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120168">↑</a> Av. v. 8, 4; Rv. viii. 34, 3; 66, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120168">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अवि">avi</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पाल">pāla</a>,</i> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 11; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 5, 2; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अवि">avi</a>-pa,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 9, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120168">↑</a> Rv. viii. 86, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120168">↑</a> Rv. ix. 109, 16; 36, 4, etc. See Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 203.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120168">↑</a> Rv. i. 116, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120168">↑</a> Rv. viii. 67, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120168">↑</a> Rv. i. 43, 6; 116, 16, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120168">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 7, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120168">↑</a> Rv. vii. 18, 17; Av. iv. 4, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 22, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxix. 58; 59.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120168">↑</a> Rv. viii. 67, 3. <i>Cf.</i> x. 75, 8; so <i><a class="vei_120a" href="उरा">urā</a>,</i> x. 95, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120168">↑</a> xiii. 50.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120168">↑</a> Rv. x. 26, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120168">↑</a> x. 106, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120168">↑</a> Rv. i. 126, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_120168">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 210.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_120168">↑</a> Rv. iv. 22, 2; v. 52, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_120168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_120168">↑</a> Rv. ix. 15, 6; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्वन्">parvan</a>,</i> iv. 22, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 229, 230; Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 348.</li></ol></div>

अशनि|అశని|ಅಶನಿ|aśani|ashani|_vei_120@169|_vei1_pn041
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@168">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@170">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn040">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn041">1:041</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn042">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aśani.</b>--Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120169"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120169">1</a></sup> cites this word from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120169"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120169">2</a></sup> as denoting a sling stone, and compares a similar use of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अद्रि">adri</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120169"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120169">3</a></sup> In either case, however, the weapons are mythical, being used in descriptions of Indra's deeds. Schrader<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120169"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120169">4</a></sup> also cites <i>aśan</i> in this sense, but no Vedic passage requires this sense.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120169"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120169">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 301.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120169"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120169">↑</a> vi. 6, 5. <i>Cf.</i> 1, 121, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120169"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120169">↑</a> i. 51, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120169"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120169">↑</a> <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 221.</li></ol></div>

अश्म-गन्धा|अश्मगन्धा|अश्म|गन्धा|అశ్మ-గన్ధా|అశ్మగన్ధా|అశ్మ|గన్ధా|ಅಶ್ಮ-ಗನ್ಧಾ|ಅಶ್ಮಗನ್ಧಾ|ಅಶ್ಮ|ಗನ್ಧಾ|aśma-gandhā|aśmagandhā|aśma|gandhā|ashma-gandhaa|ashmagandhaa|ashma|gandhaa|_vei_120@170|_vei1_pn041
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@169">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@171">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn040">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn041">1:041</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn042">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aśma-gandhā</b> (‘rock-smell’) is a plant mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120170"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120170">1</a></sup> probably identical with the late <i>aśvagandhā,</i> ‘horse-smell’ (<i>Physalis flexuosa</i>). </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120170"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120170">↑</a> xiii. 8, 1, 16, with Eggeling's note in the <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 427.</li></ol></div>

अश्व|అశ్వ|ಅಶ್ವ|aśva|ashva|_vei_120@171|_vei1_pn042
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@170">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@172">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn041">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn042">1:042</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn043">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aśva</b> is the commonest word for ‘horse’ in the Vedic literature. The horse is also called ‘the runner’ (<i>atya</i>), ‘the swift’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अर्वन्त्">arvant</a></i>), ‘the strong,’ for pulling (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वाजिन्">vājin</a></i>), ‘the runner’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सप्ति">sapti</a></i>), and ‘the speeding’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="हय">haya</a></i>). The mare is termed <i>aśvā, atyā, arvatī, vaḍavā,</i> etc. Horses of various colour were known, dun (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="हरित">harita</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="हरि">hari</a></i>), ruddy (<i>aruṇa, aruṣa, <a class="vei_120a" href="पिशंग">piśaṅga</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="रोहित">rohita</a></i>), dark brown (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="श्याव">śyāva</a></i>), white (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="श्वेत">śveta</a></i>), etc. A white horse with black ears is mentioned in the Atharvaveda as of special value.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120171">1</a></sup> Horses were highly prized,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120171">2</a></sup> and were not rare, as Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120171">3</a></sup> thought, for as many as four hundred mares are mentioned in one Dānastuti (‘Praise of Gifts’).<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120171">4</a></sup> They were on occasion ornamented with pearls and gold.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120171">5</a></sup></p><p>Mares were preferred for drawing chariots because of their swiftness and sureness.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120171">6</a></sup> They were also used for drawing carts, but were not ordinarily so employed.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120171">7</a></sup> No mention is made of riding in battle, but for other purposes it was not unknown.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120171">8</a></sup></p><p>Horses were often kept in stalls,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120171">9</a></sup> and fed there.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120171">10</a></sup> But they were also allowed to go out to grass,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120171">11</a></sup> and were then hobbled.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120171">12</a></sup> They were watered to cool them after racing.<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120171">13</a></sup> Their attendants  are frequently referred to (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्व">aśva</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पाल">pāla</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120171">14</a></sup> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्व">aśva</a>-pa,</i><sup id="ref_120_15_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_120171">15</a></sup> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्व-पति">aśva-pati</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_120171">16</a></sup> Stallions were frequently castrated (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वध्रि">vadhri</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_120171">17</a></sup></p><p>Besides reins (<i>raśmayaḥ</i>), reference is made to halters (<i>aśvābhidhānī</i>),<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_120171">18</a></sup> and whips (<i>aśvājani</i>).<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_120171">19</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a>.</b></p><p>Horses from the Indus were of special value,<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_120171">20</a></sup> as also horses from the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120171">↑</a> Av. v. 17, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120171">↑</a> Rv. i. 83, 1; iv. 32, 17; v. 4, 11; viii. 78, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120171">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 35, 686.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120171">↑</a> Rv. viii. 55, 3. <i>Cf.</i> v. 33, 8; vi. 47, 22-24; 63, 10; viii. 6, 47; 46, 22, and Hopkins, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 15, 157.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120171">↑</a> Rv. x. 68, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120171">↑</a> Pischel, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 35, 712714; <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 10, 305. <i>Cf. ratho vadhūmān,</i> Rv. i. 126, 3; vii. 18, 22, <i>vājinīvān,</i> vii. 69, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120171">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 4, 35.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120171">↑</a> The Aśvins ride, Rv. v. 61, 1-3. An <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्व">aśva</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="साद">sāda</a></i> is referred to in Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 7, 1; and riding is meant in Rv. i. 162, 17; 163, 9. Av. xi. 10, 24. is doubtful. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 262; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 221. Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 230, denies the use; but see p. 295, where it is admitted for ordinary purposes.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120171">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> the epithet of richness, ‘filling the stalls with horses’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्व">aśva</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पस्त्य">pastya</a></i>), in Rv. ix. 86, 41, and see Av. vi. 77, 1; xix. 55, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120171">↑</a> Av. <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120171">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 232, denies this, but it is the natural sense of the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xv. 41.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120171">↑</a> The expression for this is <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पड्बीश">paḍbīśa</a>,</i> Rv. i. 162, 14. 16; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 2, 13; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 1, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, ix. 7. <i>Cf.</i> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 234-236.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120171">↑</a> Rv. ii. 13, 5; 34, 3; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 11, 6; Pischel, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 189, 190.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120171">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 4, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_120171">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 9, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_120171">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 24; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_120171">↑</a> Rv. viii. 46, 30.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_120171">↑</a> Av. iv. 36, 10; v. 14, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 35; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 3, 1, 26; xiii. 1, 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_120171">↑</a> Rv. v. 62, 7; vi. 75, 13; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxix. 50.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_120171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_120171">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 2, 13; Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, ix. 7; <i>vājinīvatī</i> as epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धु">sindhu</a>, Rv. x. 75, 8, of <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>, i. 3, 10; ii. 41, 18; vi. 61, 3. 4; vii. 96, 3; Pischel, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> i. 10. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 230-232.</li></ol></div>

अश्व-तर|अश्वतर|अश्व|तर|अश्व-तरी|अश्व-तरि|अश्वतरी|अश्वतरि|तरी|तरि|అశ్వ-తర|అశ్వతర|అశ్వ|తర|అశ్వ-తరీ|అశ్వ-తరి|అశ్వతరీ|అశ్వతరి|తరీ|తరి|ಅಶ್ವ-ತರ|ಅಶ್ವತರ|ಅಶ್ವ|ತರ|ಅಶ್ವ-ತರೀ|ಅಶ್ವ-ತರಿ|ಅಶ್ವತರೀ|ಅಶ್ವತರಿ|ತರೀ|ತರಿ|aśva-tara|aśvatara|aśva|tara|aśva-tarī|aśva-tari|aśvatarī|aśvatari|tarī|tari|ashva-tara|ashvatara|ashva|ashva-tarii|ashva-tari|ashvatarii|ashvatari|tarii|ashva-taree|ashvataree|taree|_vei_120@172|_vei1_pn043
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@171">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@173">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn042">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn043">1:043</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn044">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aśva-<a class="vei_120a" href="तर">tara</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्व">aśva</a>-tarī</b> are respectively the masculine and feminine name of ‘mule.’ These animals are mentioned frequently from the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120172"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120172">1</a></sup> onwards. They were known not to be fruitful,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120172"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120172">2</a></sup> and were probably considered inferior to horses,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120172"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120172">3</a></sup> but a mule-car was quite common.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120172"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120172">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120172"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120172">↑</a> iv. 4, 8; viii. 8, 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 47; iv. 9; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 4, 1, 10, etc.; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120172"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120172">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 1, 1, 2. 3. <i>Cf.</i> Adbhuta <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> in <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 40.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120172"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120172">↑</a> As were asses, <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 1, 2, 2; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 4, 4, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120172"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120172">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 9; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 2, 1 (in both passages <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वतरी">aśvatarī</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a></i>). <i>Cf.</i> Oertel, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 175.</li></ol></div>

अश्व-त्थ|अश्व-त्थ|अश्वत्थ|अश्व|त्थ|అశ్వ-త్థ_౧|అశ్వ-త్థ|అశ్వత్థ|అశ్వ|త్థ|ಅಶ್ವ-ತ್ಥ_೧|ಅಶ್ವ-ತ್ಥ|ಅಶ್ವತ್ಥ|ಅಶ್ವ|ತ್ಥ|aśva-ttha_1|aśva-ttha|aśvattha|aśva|ttha|ashva-ttha_1|ashva-ttha|ashvattha|ashva|_vei_120@173|_vei1_pn043
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@172">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@174">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn042">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn043">1:043</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn044">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Aśva-ttha</b> (‘horse-stand’) is one of India's greatest trees, the <i>Ficus religiosa,</i> later called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पिप्पल">pippala</a></i><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120173">1</a></sup> (now Peepal). Vessels made of the wood of the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वत्थ">aśvattha</a> are mentioned in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120173">2</a></sup> and the tree itself is constantly referred to later.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120173">3</a></sup> Its hard wood formed the upper of the two pieces of wood used for kindling fire, the lower being of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शमी">śamī</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120173">4</a></sup> It planted its roots in shoots of other trees, especially the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="खदिर">khadira</a>,</b> and destroyed them;<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120173">5</a></sup> hence it is called ‘the destroyer’ (<i>vaibādha</i>). Its berries  are referred to as sweet, and as eaten by birds.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120173">6</a></sup> The gods are said to sit under it in the third heaven.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120173">7</a></sup> It and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="न्यग्रोध">nyagrodha</a></b> are styled the ‘crested ones’ (<i>śikhaṇḍin</i>).<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120173">8</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120173">↑</a> The word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पिप्पल">pippala</a></i> as a neuter occurs once in the Rv. (i. 164, 20) designating the berry of the Peepal-tree.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120173">↑</a> i. 135, 8; x. 97, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120173">↑</a> Atharvaveda, iii. 6, 1; iv. 37, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120173">↑</a> Av. vi. 11, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 1, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120173">↑</a> Av. iii. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120173">↑</a> Rv. i. 164, 20. 22.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120173">↑</a> Av. v. 4, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, viii. 5, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120173">↑</a> Av. iv. 37, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 57, 58.</li></ol></div>

अश्व-त्थ|अश्व-त्थ|अश्वत्थ|अश्व|त्थ|అశ్వ-త్థ_౨|అశ్వ-త్థ|అశ్వత్థ|అశ్వ|త్థ|ಅಶ್ವ-ತ್ಥ_೨|ಅಶ್ವ-ತ್ಥ|ಅಶ್ವತ್ಥ|ಅಶ್ವ|ತ್ಥ|aśva-ttha_2|aśva-ttha|aśvattha|aśva|ttha|ashva-ttha_2|ashva-ttha|ashvattha|ashva|_vei_120@174|_vei1_pn044
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@173">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@175">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn043">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn044">1:044</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn045">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Aśva-ttha.</b>--This prince is celebrated in a Dānastuti<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120174">1</a></sup> as having given gifts to <a class="vei_120a" href="पायु">pāyu</a>. He is identified with <b>Divodāsa</b> by Griffith,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120174">2</a></sup> but it is impossible to be certain of this identification.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120174">↑</a> Rv. vi. 47, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120174">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 1, 611. The name is spelt Aśvatha in the text, but that is merely the usual shortened spelling of the group <i>tth.</i></li></ol></div>

अश्व-दावन्|अश्वदावन्|अश्व|दावन्|అశ్వ-దావన్|అశ్వదావన్|అశ్వ|దావన్|ಅಶ್ವ-ದಾವನ್|ಅಶ್ವದಾವನ್|ಅಶ್ವ|ದಾವನ್|aśva-dāvan|aśvadāvan|aśva|dāvan|ashva-daavan|ashvadaavan|ashva|daavan|_vei_120@175|_vei1_pn044
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@174">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@176">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn043">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn044">1:044</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn045">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aśva-dāvan.</b>--Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120175"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120175">1</a></sup> finds a prince of this name in a passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120175"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120175">2</a></sup> where a gift of fifty horses is mentioned, but the word appears to be an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> (‘bestower of horses’).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120175"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120175">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 274.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120175"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120175">↑</a> v. 18, 3.</li></ol></div>

अश्व-पति|अश्वपति|अश्व|पति|అశ్వ-పతి|అశ్వపతి|అశ్వ|పతి|ಅಶ್ವ-ಪತಿ|ಅಶ್ವಪತಿ|ಅಶ್ವ|ಪತಿ|aśva-pati|aśvapati|aśva|pati|ashva-pati|ashvapati|ashva|_vei_120@176|_vei1_pn044
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@175">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@177">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn043">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn044">1:044</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn045">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aśva-pati</b> (‘lord of horses’) is a name of a prince of the <b>Kekayas,</b> who instructed <b>Prācīnaśāla</b> and other Brahmins.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120176"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120176">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120176"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120176">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 11, 4; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 6, 1, 2.</li></ol></div>

अश्व-मेध|अश्वमेध|अश्व|मेध|అశ్వ-మేధ|అశ్వమేధ|అశ్వ|మేధ|ಅಶ್ವ-ಮೇಧ|ಅಶ್ವಮೇಧ|ಅಶ್ವ|ಮೇಧ|aśva-medha|aśvamedha|aśva|medha|ashva-medha|ashvamedha|ashva|_vei_120@177|_vei1_pn044
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@176">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@178">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn043">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn044">1:044</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn045">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aśva-medha</b> (‘offering a horse-sacrifice’) is a prince mentioned in a hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120177"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120177">1</a></sup> which contains a Dānastuti (‘Praise of Gifts’) of <b>Tryaruṇa,</b> and to which three verses have been added in praise of <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>. See also <b>Āśvamedha.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120177"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120177">↑</a> v. 27, 4-6. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 275.</li></ol></div>

अश्व-युजौ|अश्वयुजौ|अश्व|युजौ|అశ్వ-యుజౌ|అశ్వయుజౌ|అశ్వ|యుజౌ|ಅಶ್ವ-ಯುಜೌ|ಅಶ್ವಯುಜೌ|ಅಶ್ವ|ಯುಜೌ|aśva-yujau|aśvayujau|aśva|yujau|ashva-yujau|ashvayujau|ashva|_vei_120@178|_vei1_pn044
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@177">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@179">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn043">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn044">1:044</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn045">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aśva-yujau.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p></div>

अश्वल|అశ్వల|ಅಶ್ವಲ|aśvala|ashvala|_vei_120@179|_vei1_pn044
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@178">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@180">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn043">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn044">1:044</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn045">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aśvala,</b> the <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a> priest of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a>,</b> King of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>,</b> appears as an authority in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iii. 1, 2. 10).</p></div>

अश्व-वार|अश्ववार|अश्व|वार|अश्व-वाल|अश्ववाल|वाल|అశ్వ-వార|అశ్వవార|అశ్వ|వార|అశ్వ-వాల|అశ్వవాల|వాల|ಅಶ್ವ-ವಾರ|ಅಶ್ವವಾರ|ಅಶ್ವ|ವಾರ|ಅಶ್ವ-ವಾಲ|ಅಶ್ವವಾಲ|ವಾಲ|aśva-vāra|aśvavāra|aśva|vāra|aśva-vāla|aśvavāla|vāla|ashva-vaara|ashvavaara|ashva|vaara|ashva-vaala|ashvavaala|vaala|_vei_120@180|_vei1_pn044
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@179">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@181">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn043">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn044">1:044</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn045">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aśva-<a class="vei_120a" href="वार">vāra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्व">aśva</a>-vāla</b> (‘hair of a horse's tail’).--The former form occurs in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120180">1</a></sup> the latter in the  <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120180">2</a></sup> and Kapiṣṭhala Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120180">3</a></sup> and Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120180">4</a></sup> denoting a species of reed (<i>Saccharum spontaneum</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120180">↑</a> iii. 7, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120180">↑</a> xxiv. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120180">↑</a> xxxviii. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120180">↑</a> iii. 4, 1, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Von Schroeder's edition of the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i., p. xv.</li></ol></div>

अश्व-सूक्ति|अश्वसूक्ति|अश्व|सूक्ति|అశ్వ-సూక్తి|అశ్వసూక్తి|అశ్వ|సూక్తి|ಅಶ್ವ-ಸೂಕ್ತಿ|ಅಶ್ವಸೂಕ್ತಿ|ಅಶ್ವ|ಸೂಕ್ತಿ|aśva-sūkti|aśvasūkti|aśva|sūkti|ashva-suukti|ashvasuukti|ashva|suukti|ashva-sookti|ashvasookti|sookti|_vei_120@181|_vei1_pn045
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@180">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@182">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn044">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn045">1:045</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn046">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aśva-sūkti</b> is the name of a seer to whom the Rigveda Anukramaṇī ascribes two hymns of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120181"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120181">1</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120181"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120181">2</a></sup> knows a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> of <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वसूक्ति">aśvasūkti</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120181"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120181">↑</a> viii. 14; 15.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120181"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120181">↑</a> xix. 4, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 230, n. 4.</li></ol></div>

अश्विनी|अश्विनि|అశ్వినీ|అశ్విని|ಅಶ್ವಿನೀ|ಅಶ್ವಿನಿ|aśvinī|aśvini|ashvinii|ashvini|ashvinee|_vei_120@182|_vei1_pn045
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@181">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@183">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn044">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn045">1:045</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn046">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aśvinī.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p></div>

अषाढ कैशिन्|అషాఢ కైశిన్|ಅಷಾಢ ಕೈಶಿನ್|aṣāḍha kaiśin|aShaaDha kaishin|_vei_120@183|_vei1_pn045
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@182">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@184">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn044">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn045">1:045</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn046">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aṣāḍha Kaiśin</b> is the name of a man referred to in a corrupt and obscure passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120183">1</a></sup> as connected with the defeat of the <b>Pañcālas</b> by the <b>Kuntis.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120183">↑</a> xxvi. 9; Kapiṣṭhala, xli. 7. See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 471.</li></ol></div>

अषाढ उत्तर पाराशर्य|అషాఢ ఉత్తర పారాశర్య|ಅಷಾಢ ಉತ್ತರ ಪಾರಾಶರ್ಯ|aṣāḍha uttara pārāśarya|aShaaDha uttara paaraasharya|_vei_120@184|_vei1_pn045
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@183">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@185">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn044">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn045">1:045</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn046">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aṣāḍha Uttara Pārāśarya</b> is mentioned as a teacher in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> or Genealogy in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 41, 1).</p></div>

अषाढा|అషాఢా|ಅಷಾಢಾ|aṣāḍhā|aShaaDhaa|_vei_120@185|_vei1_pn045
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@184">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@186">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn044">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn045">1:045</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn046">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aṣāḍhā.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p></div>

अषाढि सौश्रोमतेय|అషాఢి సౌశ్రోమతేయ|ಅಷಾಢಿ ಸೌಶ್ರೋಮತೇಯ|aṣāḍhi sauśromateya|aShaaDhi saushromateya|_vei_120@186|_vei1_pn045
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@185">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@187">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn044">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn045">1:045</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn046">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aṣāḍhi Sauśromateya</b> was a man who is stated in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vi. 2, 1, 37) to have died because the heads for the sacrifice in connection with the laying of the bricks of the fire-altar had been obtained in an improper manner.</p></div>

अष्टक|అష్టక|ಅಷ್ಟಕ|aṣṭaka|aShTaka|_vei_120@187|_vei1_pn045
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@186">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@188">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn044">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn045">1:045</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn046">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aṣṭaka</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120187">1</a></sup> as one of the sons of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120187">↑</a> vii. 17. Also in the Sāṅkhāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 26.</li></ol></div>

अष्टका|అష్టకా|ಅಷ್ಟಕಾ|aṣṭakā|aShTakaa|_vei_120@188|_vei1_pn045
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@187">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@189">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn044">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn045">1:045</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn046">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aṣṭakā.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></p></div>

अष्ट-कर्णी|अष्ट-कर्णि|अष्टकर्णी|अष्टकर्णि|अष्ट|कर्णी|कर्णि|అష్ట-కర్ణీ|అష్ట-కర్ణి|అష్టకర్ణీ|అష్టకర్ణి|అష్ట|కర్ణీ|కర్ణి|ಅಷ್ಟ-ಕರ್ಣೀ|ಅಷ್ಟ-ಕರ್ಣಿ|ಅಷ್ಟಕರ್ಣೀ|ಅಷ್ಟಕರ್ಣಿ|ಅಷ್ಟ|ಕರ್ಣೀ|ಕರ್ಣಿ|aṣṭa-karṇī|aṣṭa-karṇi|aṣṭakarṇī|aṣṭakarṇi|aṣṭa|karṇī|karṇi|aShTa-karNii|aShTa-karNi|aShTakarNii|aShTakarNi|aShTa|karNii|karNi|aShTa-karNee|aShTakarNee|karNee|_vei_120@189|_vei1_pn045
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@188">«</a>pada9<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@190">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn044">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn045">1:045</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn046">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aṣṭa-karṇī</b> is an expression which occurs in one passage of the Ṛigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120189">1</a></sup> and which Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120189">2</a></sup> was at first inclined to interpret  as a proper name. There can, however, be no doubt that it means a cow, not a man, as suggested by Grassmann. The exact reason why a cow should be so described is uncertain. Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120189">3</a></sup> was later inclined to see in it the sense ‘having pierced ears,’ similar epithets being at a later period known to Pāṇini<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120189">4</a></sup> (<i>bhinna-<a class="vei_120a" href="कर्ण">karṇa</a>, chinna-<a class="vei_120a" href="कर्ण">karṇa</a></i>). Grassmann's more obvious rendering, ‘having the sign for (the number) 8 marked on the ear,’ is supported by the similar epithets, ‘having the mark of a lute on the ear’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कर्करि">karkari</a>-karṇyaḥ</i>), ‘having the mark of a sickle on the ear’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दात्र">dātra</a>-karṇyaḥ</i>), ‘having the mark of a stake on the ear’ (<i>sthūṇākarṇyaḥ</i>), ‘having the ears bored’ (<i>chidra-karṇyaḥ</i>), and <i>viṣtyakarṇyaḥ,</i> given in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120189">5</a></sup> The simple meaning, ‘with marked ears,’ is, however, supported by the same passage of the Maitrāyaṇī, where the verb <i>akṣ</i> occurs in the sense ‘to mark.’ In the Atharvaveda the mark used is the Mithuna, no doubt as a magical device to secure fertility.</p><p>The marking of ears was apparently a regular practice. It is twice referred to in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120189">6</a></sup> The mark is termed <i><a class="vei_120a" href="लक्ष्मन्">lakṣman</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120189">7</a></sup> and was made with a copper-knife<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120189">8</a></sup> (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="लोहित">lohita</a></i>). The Maitrayaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120189">9</a></sup> forbids the use of an arrow-shaft (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="तेजन">tejana</a></i>), or of iron, but permits that of a stem of sugar-cane (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="इक्षु">ikṣu</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्ड">kāṇḍa</a></i>) or copper.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120189">↑</a> x. 62, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120189">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120189">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120189">↑</a> vi. 3, 115.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120189">↑</a> iv. 2, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120189">↑</a> vi. 141, 1. 2; xii. 4, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120189">↑</a> Av. vi. 141, 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120189">↑</a> Av., <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120189">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 234, 348; Delbrück, <i>Gurupūjākaumudī,</i> 48, 49; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 466; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 387.</li></ol></div>

अष्टा-दंष्ट्र वैरूप|अष्टादंष्ट्र वैरूप|अष्टा|दंष्ट्र वैरूप|అష్టా-దంష్ట్ర వైరూప|అష్టాదంష్ట్ర వైరూప|అష్టా|దంష్ట్ర వైరూప|ಅಷ್ಟಾ-ದಂಷ್ಟ್ರ ವೈರೂಪ|ಅಷ್ಟಾದಂಷ್ಟ್ರ ವೈರೂಪ|ಅಷ್ಟಾ|ದಂಷ್ಟ್ರ ವೈರೂಪ|aṣṭā-daṃṣṭra vairūpa|aṣṭādaṃṣṭra vairūpa|aṣṭā|daṃṣṭra vairūpa|aShTaa-daMShTra vairuupa|aShTaadaMShTra vairuupa|aShTaa|daMShTra vairuupa|aShTaa-daMShTra vairoopa|aShTaadaMShTra vairoopa|daMShTra vairoopa|_vei_120@190|_vei1_pn046
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@189">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@191">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn045">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn046">1:046</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn047">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aṣṭā-<a class="vei_120a" href="दंष्ट्र">daṃṣṭra</a> Vairūpa.</b>--To him the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 9, 21) ascribes two Sāmans.</p></div>

अष्ट्रा|అష్ట్రా|ಅಷ್ಟ್ರಾ|aṣṭrā|aShTraa|_vei_120@191|_vei1_pn046
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@190">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@192">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn045">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn046">1:046</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn047">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aṣṭrā</b> is the plougher's ‘goad,’ the badge of agriculture. It is mentioned several times in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120191"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120191">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120191"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120191">↑</a> iv. 57, 4; vi. 53, 9; 58, 2; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अष्ट्रा">aṣṭrā</a>-vin</i> occurs in x. 102, 8. See also <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, 80. <i>Cf.</i> Roscher, <i>Archiv für Religionswissenschaft,</i> 1, 63; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 364, n. 8.</li></ol></div>

असमाति राथ-प्रौष्ठ|असमाति राथप्रौष्ठ|असमाति राथ|प्रौष्ठ|అసమాతి రాథ-ప్రౌష్ఠ|అసమాతి రాథప్రౌష్ఠ|అసమాతి రాథ|ప్రౌష్ఠ|ಅಸಮಾತಿ ರಾಥ-ಪ್ರೌಷ್ಠ|ಅಸಮಾತಿ ರಾಥಪ್ರೌಷ್ಠ|ಅಸಮಾತಿ ರಾಥ|ಪ್ರೌಷ್ಠ|asamāti rātha-prauṣṭha|asamāti rāthaprauṣṭha|asamāti rātha|prauṣṭha|asamaati raatha-prauShTha|asamaati raathaprauShTha|asamaati raatha|prauShTha|_vei_120@192|_vei1_pn046
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@191">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@193">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn045">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn046">1:046</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn047">↷</a><hr><p><b>Asamāti Rātha-prauṣṭha.</b>--The story of the quarrel between Asamāti, the <a class="vei_120a" href="इक्ष्वाकु">ikṣvāku</a> prince of the <a class="vei_120a" href="रथप्रोष्ठ">rathaproṣṭha</a> family, and  his priests, the Gaupāyanas, is found only in the later Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120192"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120192">1</a></sup> It appears to be based on a misreading of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120192"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120192">2</a></sup> where <i>asamāti</i> is merely an adjective. The later story is that the king was induced to abandon his family priests by two Asuras, <a class="vei_120a" href="किरात">kirāta</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="आकुलि">ākuli</a>, who by their magic compassed the death of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुबन्धु">subandhu</a>, one of the brother priests, and that the others revived him by the use of the hymns (Rigveda, x. 57-60).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120192"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120192">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 167 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 41 <i>et seq.</i>); Sāṭyāyanaka, cited in Sāyaṇa on Rv. x. 57, 1; 60, 7; Bṛhaddevatā, vii. 83 <i>et seq.,</i> with Macdonell's notes; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 12, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120192"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120192">↑</a> x. 60, 2. 5; Av. vi. 79, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 499; Max Müller, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1866, 426-465; Bo7htlingk's Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 48, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

असि|అసి|ಅಸಿ|asi|_vei_120@193|_vei1_pn047
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@192">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@194">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn046">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn047">1:047</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn048">↷</a><hr><p><b>Asi</b> usually denotes the sacrificial knife,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120193">1</a></sup> but occasionally appears to mean a knife used in war.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120193">2</a></sup> Mention is made of a sheath (<i>vavri</i>)<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120193">3</a></sup> to which a belt (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वाल">vāla</a></i>)<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120193">4</a></sup> was attached. The word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="असि">asi</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="धारा">dhārā</a></i><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120193">5</a></sup> also denotes ‘sheath.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120193">↑</a> Rv. i. 162, 20; x. 79, 6; 18; Av. ix. 3, 9; x. 1, 20, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120193">↑</a> Av. xi. 9, 1. The use of the ‘sword’ increases in the Epic period. See Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 284.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120193">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120193">↑</a> <i>Ibid.;</i> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120193">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 139.</li></ol></div>

असिक्नी|असिक्नि|అసిక్నీ|అసిక్ని|ಅಸಿಕ್ನೀ|ಅಸಿಕ್ನಿ|asiknī|asikni|asiknii|asiknee|_vei_120@194|_vei1_pn047
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@193">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@195">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn046">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn047">1:047</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn048">↷</a><hr><p><b>Asiknī</b> (‘black’) is the name in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120194"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120194">1</a></sup> of the river known later as <a class="vei_120a" href="चन्द्र">candra</a>-bhāgā, and to the Greeks as Akesines, now the Chenab in the Punjab.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120194"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120194">↑</a> viii. 20, 25; x. 75, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ix. 26. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 12.</li></ol></div>

असित|असित|అసిత_౧|అసిత|ಅಸಿತ_೧|ಅಸಿತ|asita_1|asita|_vei_120@195|_vei1_pn047
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@194">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@196">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn046">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn047">1:047</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn048">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Asita</b> is a name of the ‘black snake,’ referred to in the later Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120195">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120195">↑</a> Av. iii. 27, 1; v. 13, 5. 6; vi. 56, 2, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 10, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 18; perhaps Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 37.</li></ol></div>

असित|असित|అసిత_౨|అసిత|ಅಸಿತ_೨|ಅಸಿತ|asita_2|asita|_vei_120@196|_vei1_pn047
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@195">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@197">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn046">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn047">1:047</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn048">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Asita.</b>--(<i>a</i>) A mythical sage of this name figures as a magician in the Atharvaveda in conjunction with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गय">gaya</a></b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120196">1</a></sup> or with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जमदग्नि">jamadagni</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120196">2</a></sup> In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120196">3</a></sup> he appears as  <a class="vei_120a" href="असित">asita</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धान्व">dhānva</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120196">4</a></sup> and as <a class="vei_120a" href="दैवल">daivala</a> or <a class="vei_120a" href="देवल">devala</a> in the Pañcaviṃsa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120196">5</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120196">6</a></sup></p><p>(<i>b</i>) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="असित">asita</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वार्ष-गण">vārṣa-gaṇa</a></b> is a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हरित कश्यप">harita kaśyapa</a></b> according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> or Genealogy in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_120196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_120196">1b</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120196">↑</a> Av. i. 14, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120196">↑</a> Av. vi. 137, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120196">↑</a> xiii. 4, 3, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120196">↑</a> Dhānvana in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 2, 19.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120196">↑</a> xiv. 11, 18. 19, <i>Cf.</i> xv. 5, 27.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120196">↑</a> xxii. 11. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 132.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_120196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_120196">↑</a> vi. 5, 3 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> = vi. 4, 33, Mādhyandina).</li></ol></div>

असित-मृग|असितमृग|असित|मृग|అసిత-మృగ|అసితమృగ|అసిత|మృగ|ಅಸಿತ-ಮೃಗ|ಅಸಿತಮೃಗ|ಅಸಿತ|ಮೃಗ|asita-mṛga|asitamṛga|asita|mṛga|asita-mRga|asitamRga|mRga|asita-mRiga|asitamRiga|mRiga|asita-mRuga|asitamRuga|mRuga|asita-mRRiga|asitamRRiga|mRRiga|_vei_120@197|_vei1_pn048
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@196">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@198">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn047">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn048">1:048</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn050">↷</a><hr><p><b>Asita-mṛga</b> is the designation in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120197">1</a></sup> of a family of the <b>Kaśyapas</b> who were excluded from a sacrifice by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनमेजय">janamejaya</a>,</b> but who took away the conduct of the offering from the <b>Bhūtavīras,</b> whom the king employed. In the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120197">2</a></sup> and the Ṣaḍviṃsa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120197">3</a></sup> the Asitamṛgas are called ‘sons of the Kaśyapas,’ and one is mentioned as <b>Kusurubindu</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120197">4</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="औद्दालकि">auddālaki</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120197">↑</a> vii. 27. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 43, 345.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120197">↑</a> i. 75.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120197">↑</a> i. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120197">↑</a> Variously read as Asurbinda, Kusurbinda, Kusurubinda.</li></ol></div>

असुर-विद्या|असुरविद्या|असुर|विद्या|అసుర-విద్యా|అసురవిద్యా|అసుర|విద్యా|ಅಸುರ-ವಿದ್ಯಾ|ಅಸುರವಿದ್ಯಾ|ಅಸುರ|ವಿದ್ಯಾ|asura-vidyā|asuravidyā|asura|vidyā|asura-vidyaa|asuravidyaa|vidyaa|_vei_120@198|_vei1_pn048
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@197">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@199">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn047">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn048">1:048</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn050">↷</a><hr><p><b>Asura-vidyā,</b> ‘the science of the Asuras,’ the term used in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> and Āśvalāyana<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120198"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120198">1</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> Sūtras as the equivalent of the term <i>māyā</i> employed in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120198"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120198">2</a></sup> clearly means ‘magic,’ as it is rendered by Professor Eggeling.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120198"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120198">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120198"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120198">↑</a> x. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120198"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120198">↑</a> xiii. 4, 3, 11. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 61, 2. 21.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120198"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120198">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 368.</li></ol></div>

अस्तृ|అస్తృ|ಅಸ್ತೃ|astṛ|astR|astRi|astRu|astRRi|_vei_120@199|_vei1_pn048
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@198">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@200">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn047">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn048">1:048</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn050">↷</a><hr><p><b>Astṛ,</b> ‘shooter,’ is a term frequently used in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120199"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120199">1</a></sup> and Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120199"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120199">2</a></sup> for the archer who fought from the chariot.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120199"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120199">↑</a> i. 8, 4; 64, 10; ii. 42, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120199"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120199">↑</a> vi. 93, 1. 2; xi. 2, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 296.</li></ol></div>

अहन्|అహన్|ಅಹನ್|ahan|_vei_120@200|_vei1_pn048
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@199">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@201">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn047">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn048">1:048</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn050">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ahan,</b> ‘day.’ Like other peoples, the Indians used night as a general expression of time as well as day, but by no means predominantly.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120200">1</a></sup> Night is also termed the dark (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण">kṛṣṇa</a></i>), as  opposed to the light (<i>arjuna</i>), day.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120200">2</a></sup> Aho-<a class="vei_120a" href="रात्र">rātra</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120200">3</a></sup> is a regular term for ‘day and night’ combined.</p><p>The day itself is variously divided. In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120200">4</a></sup> a division into ‘the rising sun’ (<i>udyan sūryaḥ</i>), ‘the coming together of the cows’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सं-गव">saṃ-gava</a></i>), ‘midday’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यं-दिन">madhyaṃ-dina</a></i>), ‘afternoon’ (<i>aparāhṇa</i>), and ‘sunset’ (<i>astaṃ-yan</i>) is found. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120200">5</a></sup> the same series appears with ‘early’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रातर्">prātar</a></i>) and ‘evening’ (<i>sāyāhna</i>) substituted for the first and last members, while a shorter list gives <i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रातर्">prātar</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="संगव">saṃgava</a>, sāyam.</i> In the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120200">6</a></sup> there is the series ‘dawn’ <i>(uṣas), <a class="vei_120a" href="संगव">saṃgava</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यंदिन">madhyaṃdina</a>,</i> and <i>aparāhṇa.</i></p><p>The morning is also, according to Zimmer, called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अपि-शर्वर">api-śarvara</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120200">7</a></sup> as the time when the dark is just past. It is named <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्वसर">svasara</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120200">8</a></sup> as the time when the cows are feeding, before the first milking at the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="संगव">saṃgava</a>,</i> or when the birds are awakening.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120200">9</a></sup> It is also called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्र-पित्व">pra-pitva</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120200">10</a></sup> according to Zimmer.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120200">11</a></sup> But Geldner<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120200">12</a></sup> points out that that term refers to the late midday, which also is called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अपि-शर्वर">api-śarvara</a>,</i> as bordering on the coming night, being the time when day is hastening to its close, as in a race. From another point of view, evening is called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अभि-पित्व">abhi-pitva</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120200">13</a></sup> the time when all come to rest. Or again, morning and evening are denoted as the dawning of the sun (<i>uditā sūryasya</i>), or its setting (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="नि-म्रुच्">ni-mruc</a></i>). The midday is regularly <i>madhyam ahnām,</i><sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120200">14</a></sup> <i>madhye,</i><sup id="ref_120_15_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_120200">15</a></sup> or <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यंदिन">madhyaṃdina</a>. <a class="vei_120a" href="संगव">saṃgava</a></i><sup id="ref_120_16_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_120200">16</a></sup> is the forenoon, between the early morning (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रातर्">prātar</a></i>) and midday (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यंदिन">madhyaṃdina</a></i>).</p><p>The divisions of time less than the day are seldom precisely given. In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_120200">17</a></sup> however, a day and  night make up 30 <i>muhūrtas;</i> 1 <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मुहूर्त">muhūrta</a></i> = 15 <i><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षिप्र">kṣipra</a>;</i> 1 <i><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षिप्र">kṣipra</a></i> = 15 <i>etarhi;</i> 1 <i>etarhi</i> = 15 <i>idāni;</i> 1 <i>idāni</i> = 15 breathings; 1 breathing = 1 spiration; 1 spiration = 1 twinkling (<i>nimeṣa</i>), etc. In the Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_120200">18</a></sup> the series is <i>dhvaṃsayo, nimeṣāḥ, kāṣṭhāḥ, kalāḥ, kṣanā, muhūrtā, ahorātrāḥ.</i> A thirtyfold division of day as well as of night is seen in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_120200">19</a></sup> by Zimmer, who compares the Babylonian sixtyfold division of the day and night. But the expression used-thirty Yojanas--is too vague and obscure--Bergaigne<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_120200">20</a></sup> refers it to the firmament--to build any theory upon with safety.</p><p>The longer divisions of time are regularly ‘half month’ (<i>ardha-<a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a></i>), ‘month’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a></i>), ‘season’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋतु">ṛtu</a></i>), and ‘year’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="संवत्सर">saṃvatsara</a></i>), which often<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_120200">21</a></sup> occur in this sequence after <i>ahorātrāṇi</i> (‘days and nights’).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120200">↑</a> Rv. iv. 16, 19; viii. 26, 3; i. 70, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Av. x. 7, 42.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120200">↑</a> Rv. vi. 9. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120200">↑</a> Rv. x. 190, 2; Av. xiii. 3, 8, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 41, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120200">↑</a> ix. 6, 45.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120200">↑</a> i. 5, 3, 1; 4, 9, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120200">↑</a> iv. 2, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120200">↑</a> Rv. iii. 9, 7; <i>cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>ṚgvedaNoten,</i> 1, 230.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120200">↑</a> Rv. ii. 34, 8; ix. 94, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120200">↑</a> Rv. ii. 19, 2; 34, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120200">↑</a> Rv. vii. 41, 4; viii. 1, 29. Sieg. <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 127 <i>et seq.,</i> interprets <i>paritakmyāyām</i> (Rv. i. 116, 15) similarly.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120200">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben.</i> 362.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120200">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 155-179.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120200">↑</a> Rv. i. 126, 3; iv. 34, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120200">↑</a> Rv. vii. 41, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_120200">↑</a> Rv. viii. 27, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_120200">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. v. 76, 3 (<i>saṃgave, <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रातर्">prātar</a> ahno, madhyaṃdine</i>); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 1, 3; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 12, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 18, 14; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 112, 113. Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 362, places it too early--before the cows are driven out.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_120200">↑</a> xii. 3, 2, 5. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 10, 1, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_120200">↑</a> vii. 20. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiv. 78 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 92-95.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_120200">↑</a> Rv. i. 123, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_120200">↑</a> <i>Religion Védique.</i> 3, 283 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. <a class="vei_120a" href="क्रतु">kratu</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_120200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_120200">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 1, 15; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 12, 7; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxii. 28; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, vii. 20; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>. iii. 8, 9, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 361-363.</li></ol></div>

अहल्या मैत्रेयी|अहल्या मैत्रेयि|అహల్యా మైత్రేయీ|అహల్యా మైత్రేయి|ಅಹಲ್ಯಾ ಮೈತ್ರೇಯೀ|ಅಹಲ್ಯಾ ಮೈತ್ರೇಯಿ|ahalyā maitreyī|ahalyā maitreyi|ahalyaa maitreyii|ahalyaa maitreyi|ahalyaa maitreyee|_vei_120@201|_vei1_pn050
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@200">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@202">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn048">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn050">1:050</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn051">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ahalyā Maitreyī</b> is practically a mythical name, the existence of the lady whose story is alluded to in several Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120201"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120201">1</a></sup> being derived from the epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, ‘lover of Ahalyā’ (<i>ahalyāyai <a class="vei_120a" href="जार">jāra</a></i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120201"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120201">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 4, 18; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 79; Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1.</li></ol></div>

अहि|అహి|ಅಹಿ|ahi|_vei_120@202|_vei1_pn050
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@201">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@203">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn048">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn050">1:050</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn051">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ahi.</b>--This word occurs frequently from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120202">1</a></sup> onwards to denote ‘snake.’ Reference is several times<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120202">2</a></sup> made to its casting its slough. Mention is also made of the serpent's peculiar movement,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120202">3</a></sup> which earns for it the designation<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120202">4</a></sup> of ‘the toothed rope’ (<i>datvatī rajjuḥ</i>). The poisonous character  of its bite is spoken of,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120202">5</a></sup> as well as the torpidity of the reptile in winter, when it creeps into the earth.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120202">6</a></sup> The cast skin is used as an amulet against highwaymen.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120202">7</a></sup> Mention is made of a mythical horse, <a class="vei_120a" href="पैद्व">paidva</a>, which the Aśvins gave to <a class="vei_120a" href="पेदु">pedu</a> as a protection against snakes,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120202">8</a></sup> and which is invoked as a destroyer of serpents.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120202">9</a></sup> The ichneumon (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="नकुल">nakula</a></i>) is regarded as their deadly enemy, and as immune against their poison through the use of a healing plant,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120202">10</a></sup> while men kill them with sticks<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120202">11</a></sup> or strike off their heads.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120202">12</a></sup></p><p>Many species of snakes are mentioned: see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अघाश्व">aghāśva</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अजगर">ajagara</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="असित">asita</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कंकपर्वन्">kaṅkaparvan</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="करिक्रत">karikrata</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कल्माषग्रीव">kalmāṣagrīva</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कसर्णील">kasarṇīla</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कुम्भीनस">kumbhīnasa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="तिरश्चराजि">tiraścarāji</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="तैमात">taimāta</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="दर्वि">darvi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="दशोनसि">daśonasi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पुष्करसाद">puṣkarasāda</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पृदाकु">pṛdāku</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="लोहिताहि">lohitāhi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शर्कोट">śarkoṭa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="श्वित्र">śvitra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सर्प">sarpa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120202">↑</a> vii. 104, 7, etc. The word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सर्प">sarpa</a>,</i> which is usual in the Atharvaveda, occurs only once (x. 16, 6) in the Rv.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120202">↑</a> Rv. ix. 86, 44; Av. i. 27; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 2, 6, 13; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 4, 10; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 9. ii. 139; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 6; 17.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120202">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, v. 1, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120202">↑</a> Av. iv. 3, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120202">↑</a> Rv. vii. 104. 7; Av. x. 4, 4 <i>et seq.;</i> 6, 56.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120202">↑</a> Av. xii. 1, 46.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120202">↑</a> Av. i. 27.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120202">↑</a> Rv. i. 117-119</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120202">↑</a> Av. x. 4, 6. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120202">↑</a> Av. vi. 139, 5; viii. 7, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120202">↑</a> Av. x. 4, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120202">↑</a> Av. vi. 67, 2. See Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 94, 95.</li></ol></div>

अहीना आश्वत्थ्य|అహీనా ఆశ్వత్థ్య|ಅಹೀನಾ ಆಶ್ವತ್ಥ್ಯ|ahīnā āśvatthya|ahiinaa aashvatthya|aheenaa aashvatthya|_vei_120@203|_vei1_pn051
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@202">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@204">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn050">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn051">1:051</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn052">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ahīnā Āśvatthya</b> was a sage (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="मुनि">muni</a></i>) who achieved immortality by knowledge of a certain rite (<i>sāvitram</i>).<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120203">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120203">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 10, 9, 10. The form of the first name is remarkable. The second may be compared with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वत्थ">aśvattha</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

आकुलि|ఆకులి|ಆಕುಲಿ|ākuli|aakuli|_vei_120@204|_vei1_pn051
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@203">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@205">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn050">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn051">1:051</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn052">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ākuli.</b>--This mythical priest plays, together with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="किरात">kirāta</a>,</b> a part in the later tale of <b>Asamāti</b> and the <b>Gaupāyanas.</b></p></div>

आक्ताक्ष्य|ఆక్తాక్ష్య|ಆಕ್ತಾಕ್ಷ್ಯ|āktākṣya|aaktaakShya|aaktaaxya|_vei_120@205|_vei1_pn051
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@204">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@206">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn050">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn051">1:051</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn052">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āktākṣya</b> is mentioned as a teacher who had peculiar views on the fire ritual (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>-citi</i>), which are rejected in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120205">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120205">↑</a> vi. 1, 2, 24. <i>Cf.</i> Lévi, <i>La Doctrine du Sacrifice,</i> 140.</li></ol></div>

आ-क्रमण|आक्रमण|क्रमण|ఆ-క్రమణ|ఆక్రమణ|క్రమణ|ಆ-ಕ್ರಮಣ|ಆಕ್ರಮಣ|ಕ್ರಮಣ|ā-kramaṇa|ākramaṇa|kramaṇa|aa-kramaNa|aakramaNa|kramaNa|_vei_120@206|_vei1_pn051
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@205">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@207">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn050">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn051">1:051</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn052">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ā-kramaṇa.</b>--In the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> Brāhmaṇa^1 (i. 3) this word is used with the specific sense of ‘steps to climb trees.’ </p></div>

आखु|ఆఖు|ಆಖು|ākhu|aakhu|_vei_120@207|_vei1_pn052
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@206">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@208">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn051">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn052">1:052</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn053">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ākhu.</b>--The exact sense of this word is uncertain. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120207">1</a></sup> renders it ‘mole,’ while Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120207">2</a></sup> prefers ‘mouse’ or ‘rat.’ It is frequently mentioned in the later Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120207">3</a></sup> and is known to the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120207">4</a></sup> where, however, the word is regarded by Pischel<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120207">5</a></sup> as having acquired the secondary sense of ‘thief.’ This is denied by Hillebrandt.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120207">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120207">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 84, 85, followed by Bloomfield. <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 142.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120207">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> followed by Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 317, 318.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120207">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 14, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14. 7; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iii. 57; xxiv. 26; 28; Av. vi. 50, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120207">↑</a> ix. 67, 30.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120207">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 246; <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 701.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120207">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift,</i> 48, 418; <i>Vedainterpretation,</i> 7.</li></ol></div>

आ-ख्यान|आख्यान|ख्यान|ఆ-ఖ్యాన|ఆఖ్యాన|ఖ్యాన|ಆ-ಖ್ಯಾನ|ಆಖ್ಯಾನ|ಖ್ಯಾನ|ā-khyāna|ākhyāna|khyāna|aa-khyaana|aakhyaana|khyaana|_vei_120@208|_vei1_pn052
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@207">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@209">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn051">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn052">1:052</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn053">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ā-khyāna.</b>--In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120208"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120208">1</a></sup> we hear of the Śaunaḥśepa <a class="vei_120a" href="आख्यान">ākhyāna</a>, ‘the story of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a>,</b>’ which is told by the <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a> priest at the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a> (‘royal inauguration’). The series of stories<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120208"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120208">2</a></sup> used at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) during the year while the sacrificial horse is allowed to wander at its will is called the ‘cyclic’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="परि">pari</a>-plavam</i>). The <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120208"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120208">3</a></sup> mentions also <a class="vei_120a" href="आख्यान">ākhyāna</a>-vids (‘men versed in tales’), who tell the Sauparṇa legend, elswhere<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120208"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120208">4</a></sup> known as a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्याख्यान">vyākhyāna</a>.</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120208"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120208">5</a></sup> frequently uses the term, sometimes in a pregnant sense as denoting the doctrine of the Aitihāsikas or traditional interpreters of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120208"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120208">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120208"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120208">↑</a> vii. 18, 10. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 27.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120208"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120208">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>. xiii. 4, 3, 2. 15.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120208"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120208">↑</a> iii. 25, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120208"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120208">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 6. 2. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120208"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120208">↑</a> v. 21; vii. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120208"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120208">↑</a> xi. 19; 25.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Sieg. <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>.</i> 16 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

आ-ख्यायिका|आख्यायिका|ख्यायिका|ఆ-ఖ్యాయికా|ఆఖ్యాయికా|ఖ్యాయికా|ಆ-ಖ್ಯಾಯಿಕಾ|ಆಖ್ಯಾಯಿಕಾ|ಖ್ಯಾಯಿಕಾ|ā-khyāyikā|ākhyāyikā|khyāyikā|aa-khyaayikaa|aakhyaayikaa|khyaayikaa|_vei_120@209|_vei1_pn052
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@208">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@210">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn051">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn052">1:052</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn053">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ā-khyāyikā.</b>--This word occurs apparently but once in the Vedic literature, in the late <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120209">1</a></sup> where its significance is doubtful.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120209">↑</a> i. 6. 3. <i>Cf.</i> Sieg. <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 20, n.</li></ol></div>

आगस्त्य|ఆగస్త్య|ಆಗಸ್ತ್ಯ|āgastya|aagastya|_vei_120@210|_vei1_pn052
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@209">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@211">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn051">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn052">1:052</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn053">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āgastya</b> appears as a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> (iii. 1, 1) and <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> (vii. 2) Āraṇyakas. </p></div>

आग्नि-वेशि शत्रि|आग्निवेशि शत्रि|आग्नि|वेशि शत्रि|ఆగ్ని-వేశి శత్రి|ఆగ్నివేశి శత్రి|ఆగ్ని|వేశి శత్రి|ಆಗ್ನಿ-ವೇಶಿ ಶತ್ರಿ|ಆಗ್ನಿವೇಶಿ ಶತ್ರಿ|ಆಗ್ನಿ|ವೇಶಿ ಶತ್ರಿ|āgni-veśi śatri|āgniveśi śatri|āgni|veśi śatri|aagni-veshi shatri|aagniveshi shatri|aagni|veshi shatri|_vei_120@211|_vei1_pn053
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@210">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@212">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn052">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn053">1:053</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn055">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āgni-<a class="vei_120a" href="वेशि">veśi</a> Śatri.</b>--A prince of this name appears to be referred to in a Dānastuti (‘Praise of Gifts’) in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120211"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120211">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120211"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120211">↑</a> v. 34, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 155.</li></ol></div>

आग्नि-वेश्य|आग्निवेश्य|आग्नि|वेश्य|ఆగ్ని-వేశ్య|ఆగ్నివేశ్య|ఆగ్ని|వేశ్య|ಆಗ್ನಿ-ವೇಶ್ಯ|ಆಗ್ನಿವೇಶ್ಯ|ಆಗ್ನಿ|ವೇಶ್ಯ|āgni-veśya|āgniveśya|āgni|veśya|aagni-veshya|aagniveshya|aagni|veshya|_vei_120@212|_vei1_pn053
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@211">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@213">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn052">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn053">1:053</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn055">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āgni-veśya.</b>--Several teachers of this name are mentioned in the Vaṃśas or Genealogies of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>. In the Mādhyandina recension<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120212"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120212">1</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="आग्निवेश्य">āgniveśya</a> is a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सैतव">saitava</a>.</b> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension he is a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्डिल्य">śāṇḍilya</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आनभिम्लात">ānabhimlāta</a></b> in one <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120212"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120212">2</a></sup> and of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गार्ग्य">gārgya</a></b> in the second <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120212"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120212">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120212"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120212">↑</a> ii. 5, 21; iv. 5, 27.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120212"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120212">↑</a> ii. 6, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120212"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120212">↑</a> iv. 6, 2.</li></ol></div>

आ-घाटि|आघाटि|घाटि|ఆ-ఘాటి|ఆఘాటి|ఘాటి|ಆ-ಘಾಟಿ|ಆಘಾಟಿ|ಘಾಟಿ|ā-ghāṭi|āghāṭi|ghāṭi|aa-ghaaTi|aaghaaTi|ghaaTi|_vei_120@213|_vei1_pn053
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@212">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@214">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn052">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn053">1:053</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn055">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ā-ghāṭi</b> is a musical instrument, the ‘cymbal,’ used to accompany dancing. It is known to the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120213"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120213">1</a></sup> and Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120213"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120213">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120213"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120213">↑</a> x. 146, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120213"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120213">↑</a> iv. 37, 4 (<i>āgkāṭa</i>). <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 289.</li></ol></div>

आंगिरस|आङ्गिरस|ఆంగిరస|ఆఙ్గిరస|ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|āṅgirasa|aaMgirasa|aangirasa|_vei_120@214|_vei1_pn053
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@213">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@215">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn052">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn053">1:053</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn055">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āṅgirasa</b> is a title denoting a claim to be of the family of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अंगिरस्">aṅgiras</a>,</b> borne by many sages and teachers, like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण">kṛṣṇa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="आजीगर्ति">ājīgarti</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="च्यवन">cyavana</a>, Ayāsya, <a class="vei_120a" href="संवर्त">saṃvarta</a>, Sudhanvan,</b> etc.</p></div>

आज-केशिन्|आजकेशिन्|आज|केशिन्|ఆజ-కేశిన్|ఆజకేశిన్|ఆజ|కేశిన్|ಆಜ-ಕೇಶಿನ್|ಆಜಕೇಶಿನ್|ಆಜ|ಕೇಶಿನ್|āja-keśin|ājakeśin|āja|keśin|aaja-keshin|aajakeshin|aaja|keshin|_vei_120@215|_vei1_pn053
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@214">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@216">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn052">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn053">1:053</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn055">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āja-keśin</b> is the name of a family in which, according to the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 9, 3), <b>Baka</b> used violence against <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>.</p></div>

आजनि|ఆజని|ಆಜನಿ|ājani|aajani|_vei_120@216|_vei1_pn053
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@215">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@217">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn052">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn053">1:053</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn055">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ājani.</b>--This word is used to denote a ‘goad’ in the Atharvaveda (iii. 25, 5).</p></div>

आजात-शत्रव|आजातशत्रव|आजात|शत्रव|ఆజాత-శత్రవ|ఆజాతశత్రవ|ఆజాత|శత్రవ|ಆಜಾತ-ಶತ್ರವ|ಆಜಾತಶತ್ರವ|ಆಜಾತ|ಶತ್ರವ|ājāta-śatrava|ājātaśatrava|ājāta|śatrava|aajaata-shatrava|aajaatashatrava|aajaata|shatrava|_vei_120@217|_vei1_pn053
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@216">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@218">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn052">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn053">1:053</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn055">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ājāta-śatrava.</b> See <b>Bhadrasena.</b></p></div>

आजि|ఆజి|ಆಜಿ|āji|aaji|_vei_120@218|_vei1_pn053
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@217">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@219">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn052">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn053">1:053</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn055">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āji</b> is constantly used in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120218">1</a></sup> and the later literature to express the sense of ‘a race,’ and only seldom denotes ‘a battle.’ <b>Horse</b>-racing was one of the favourite amusements of the <b>Vedic</b> Indian,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120218">2</a></sup> the other being dicing  (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a></b>). The racecourse, called <a class="vei_120a" href="काष्ठा">kāṣṭhā</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120218">3</a></sup> or <a class="vei_120a" href="आजि">āji</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120218">4</a></sup> itself, appears from the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120218">5</a></sup> to have been a quasi-circular one to a mark (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कार्ष्मन्">kārṣman</a></i><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120218">6</a></sup> ) and back again. In the Rigveda the course is described as broad (<i>urvī</i>) and the distance as measured out (<i>apāvṛktā aratnayaḥ</i>).<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120218">7</a></sup> Prizes (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="धन">dhana</a></i>) were offered (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="धा">dhā</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120218">8</a></sup> and eagerly competed for. Other words for victory and the prize are <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कार">kāra</a></i><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120218">9</a></sup> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="भर">bhara</a>;</i><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120218">10</a></sup> and to ‘run a race’ is described by the expressions <i>ājim aj, i, dhāv, sṛ.</i><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120218">11</a></sup> The person who instituted a race is referred to as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="आजि">āji</a>-sṛt,</i><sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120218">12</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> is called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="आजि">āji</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्">kṛt</a></i><sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120218">13</a></sup> (‘race-maker’), and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="आजि">āji</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a></i><sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120218">14</a></sup> (‘lord of the race’).</p><p>The swift steeds (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वाजिन्">vājin</a>, atya</i>) used for the races were often washed and adorned.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_120218">15</a></sup> According to Pischel<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_120218">16</a></sup> the name of one swift mare is preserved--viz., <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्पला">viśpalā</a>,<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_120218">17</a></sup> whose broken leg was replaced by the Aśvins in a race; but the interpretation is very doubtful. Geldner<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_120218">18</a></sup> has also found a comic picture of a horse-chariot race in the <a class="vei_120a" href="मुद्गल">mudgala</a> hymn in the Rigveda, but Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_120218">19</a></sup> has shown that that interpretation is unsound. Pischel<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_120218">20</a></sup> also seeks to show that races were run in honour of gods, but the evidence for the theory is inadequate.<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_120218">21</a></sup>  A formal race, however, is a feature of the ritual of the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a> or royal consecration.<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_120218">22</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120218">↑</a> v. 37, 7; vi. 24, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120218">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 291; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 120; 2. 1 <i>seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120218">↑</a> Rv. viii. 80, 8; Av. ii. 14, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120218">↑</a> Rv. iv. 24, 8; Av. xiii. 2, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120218">↑</a> ii. 14, 6; xiii. 2, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120218">↑</a> Rv. ix. 36, 1; 74, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120218">↑</a> The sense is doubtful in Rv. viii. 80, 8. Zimmer suggests that it may mean ‘the course is straight,’ without twistings, for which idea <i>cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 160, quoting the comparison of the courser's race with a bowstring (Rv. iii. 53, 24). It is also rendered ‘the barriers are removed.’</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120218">↑</a> Rv. i. 81, 3; 116, 15; vi. 45, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> viii. 80, 8; ix. 53, 2; 109, 10. According to Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 120, n. 2, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="धन">dhana</a></i> is from <i>dhan,</i> ‘start.’ <i>Cf.</i> Pischel, <i>ibid.,</i> 171. <i>Cf. dhanasā,</i> Rv. i. 112, 7. 10; ii. 10, 6; viii. 3, 15, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120218">↑</a> Rv. v. 29, 8; ix. 14, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120218">↑</a> Rv. v. 29, 8; ix. 16, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120218">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 25; iv. 27; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 3, 4; v. 1, 1, 3; 4, 1; vi. 1, 2, 12; vii. 1, 2, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120218">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1, 5, 10. 28; xi. 1, 2, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120218">↑</a> Rv. viii. 53, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120218">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 14.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_120218">↑</a> Rv. ii. 34, 3; ix. 109, 10; x. 68, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_120218">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 171-173. <i>Cf.</i> Sieg. <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 127 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_120218">↑</a> Rv. i. 116, 15. Pischel finds here a race in honour of Vivasvant, but his equation of <a class="vei_120a" href="खेल">khela</a> and Vivasvant is denied even by Sieg, who accepts his theory of <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्पला">viśpalā</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_120218">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_120218">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 541 <i>et seq.</i> Von Schroeder, <i>Mysterium und Mimus im Rigveda,</i> 346 <i>et seq.,</i> follows Geldner. <i>Cf.</i> Winternitz. <i>Vienna Oriental Journal,</i> 23, 137.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_120218">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 172.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_120218">↑</a> Sieg, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 128.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_120218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_120218">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x. 19 <i>et seq.;</i> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 2; 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1. 43.</li></ol></div>

आजीगर्ति|ఆజీగర్తి|ಆಜೀಗರ್ತಿ|ājīgarti|aajiigarti|aajeegarti|_vei_120@219|_vei1_pn055
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@218">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@220">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn053">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn055">1:055</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn056">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ājīgarti.</b>--See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a>,</b> who bears this patronymic in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120219"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120219">1</a></sup> He is called an <a class="vei_120a" href="आंगिरस">āṅgirasa</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120219"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120219">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120219"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120219">↑</a> vii. 17. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 11, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120219"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120219">↑</a> xix. 11.</li></ol></div>

आज्य|ఆజ్య|ಆಜ್ಯ|ājya|aajya|_vei_120@220|_vei1_pn055
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@219">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@221">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn053">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn055">1:055</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn056">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ājya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="घृत">ghṛta</a>.</b></p></div>

आञ्जन|ఆఞ్జన|ಆಞ್ಜನ|āñjana|aa~njana|_vei_120@221|_vei1_pn055
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@220">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@222">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn053">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn055">1:055</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn056">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āñjana.</b>--A salve frequently referred to in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120221"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120221">1</a></sup> which came from Mount <b>Trikakubh</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120221"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120221">2</a></sup> in the Himālaya, and was used to anoint the eyes.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120221"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120221">3</a></sup> The region of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a></b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120221"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120221">4</a></sup> is also given as a possible place of origin, and the ointment is declared as potent to remove jaundice, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यक्ष्म">yakṣma</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="जायान्य">jāyānya</a>,</b> and other diseases.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120221"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120221">5</a></sup> A female ointment-maker is mentioned in the list of victims of the <b>Puruṣamedha</b> (‘human sacrifice’).<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120221"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120221">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120221"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120221">↑</a> iv. 9; vi. 102, 3; ix. 6, 11; xix. 44.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120221"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120221">↑</a> Av. iv. 9, 9. 10; xix. 44, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120221"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120221">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Av. iv. 9, 1 (<i>akṣyam</i>); <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3. Hence the legend in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 1, 5; <i>cf.</i> i. 2, 1, 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, 6, 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 3, 15; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iv. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120221"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120221">↑</a> Av. iv. 9, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120221"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120221">↑</a> Av. xix. 44, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120221"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120221">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 14 (<i>āñjanī-<a class="vei_120a" href="कारि">kāri</a></i>); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 10, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 5, 69; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda</i> 381 <i>et seq.; American Journal of Philology,</i> 17, 405, 406; Whitney Translation of the Atharvaveda, 159.</li></ol></div>

आटिकी|आटिकि|ఆటికీ|ఆటికి|ಆಟಿಕೀ|ಆಟಿಕಿ|āṭikī|āṭiki|aaTikii|aaTiki|aaTikee|_vei_120@222|_vei1_pn055
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@221">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@223">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn053">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn055">1:055</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn056">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āṭikī</b> is the name of the wife of <b>Uṣasti</b> in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (i. 10, 1).</p></div>

अट्णार|అట్ణార|ಅಟ್ಣಾರ|aṭṇāra|aTNaara|_vei_120@223|_vei1_pn055
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@222">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@224">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn053">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn055">1:055</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn056">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aṭṇāra.</b>--Patronymic of <b>Para.</b></p></div>

आडम्बर|आडंबर|ఆడమ్బర|ఆడంబర|ಆಡಮ್ಬರ|ಆಡಂಬರ|āḍambara|aaDambara|_vei_120@224|_vei1_pn055
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@223">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@225">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn053">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn055">1:055</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn056">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āḍambara</b> was a kind of ‘drum.’ A ‘drummer’ (<i>āḍambarāghāta</i>) is mentioned in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुष">puruṣa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="मेध">medha</a> (‘human sacrifice’) in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120224"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120224">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120224"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120224">↑</a> xxx. 19. <i>Cf.</i> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 4, 8, 1.</li></ol></div>

आणि|ఆణి|ಆಣಿ|āṇi|aaNi|_vei_120@225|_vei1_pn056
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@224">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@226">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn055">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn056">1:056</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn057">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āṇi.</b>--This word, which is found in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120225"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120225">1</a></sup> but rarely later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120225"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120225">2</a></sup> appears to be best taken with Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120225"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120225">3</a></sup> and Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120225"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120225">4</a></sup> as denoting the part of the axle of the chariot which is inserted into the nave of the wheel. Sāyaṇa renders it as lynch-pin, and this sense is accepted by Leumann,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120225"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120225">5</a></sup> being apparently also found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120225"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120225">6</a></sup> In one place in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120225"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120225">7</a></sup> the word appears by synecdoche to denote the whole chariot, but the passage is, according to Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120225"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120225">8</a></sup> completely obscure.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120225"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120225">↑</a> i. 35, 6; v. 43, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120225"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120225">↑</a> In a <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 7. See Keith's edition, pp. 266, 267, and Vānī.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120225"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120225">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120225"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120225">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 247.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120225"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120225">↑</a> <i>Etymologisches Wo7rterbuch,</i> 30.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120225"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120225">↑</a> vi. 32.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120225"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120225">↑</a> i. 63, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 96.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120225"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120225">↑</a> Geldner, <i>ibid.,</i> 1, 141, n. 3.</li></ol></div>

आण्डीक|ఆణ్డీక|ಆಣ್ಡೀಕ|āṇḍīka|aaNDiika|aaNDeeka|_vei_120@226|_vei1_pn056
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@225">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@227">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn055">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn056">1:056</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn057">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āṇḍīka</b> (‘bearing eggs’) is a term found in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120226"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120226">1</a></sup> denoting an edible plant, apparently with fruit or leaves of egg shape (<i>āṇḍa</i>), akin to the lotus.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120226"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120226">↑</a> iv. 34, 5; v. 17, 16. In the first passage the Paippalāda version has <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पौण्डरीक">pauṇḍarīka</a></i> in its place; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 207. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 70; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 138.</li></ol></div>

आता|ఆతా|ಆತಾ|ātā|aataa|_vei_120@227|_vei1_pn056
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@226">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@228">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn055">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn056">1:056</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn057">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ātā.</b>--The framework of the door of a house appears to be denoted by the plural of this word in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120227">1</a></sup> (though in all passages there it is used only by synecdoche of the doors of the sky), and in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120227">2</a></sup> Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120227">3</a></sup> compares the Latin <i>antae,</i> to which the word etymologically corresponds.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120227">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120227">↑</a> i. 56, 5; 113, 14; iii. 43, 6; ix. 5, 5 (<i>ātaiḥ</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120227">↑</a> xxix. 5 <i>(ātaiḥ). Cf. ātābhiḥ</i> in <a class="vei_120a" href="दुर्ग">durga</a> on <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iv. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120227">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 154.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120227">↑</a> Brugmann, <i>Grundriss,</i> 1, 209; 2, 214.</li></ol></div>

आति|ఆతి|ಆತಿ|āti|aati|_vei_120@228|_vei1_pn056
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@227">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@229">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn055">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn056">1:056</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn057">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āti,</b> an aquatic bird. The Apsarases in the legend of <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरूरवस्">purūravas</a> and Urvaśī appear to him like Ātis, probably swans.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120228">1</a></sup> The birds appear also in the list of animals in the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’),<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120228">2</a></sup> where Mahīdhara<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120228">3</a></sup> renders them as the  later Āḍī (<i>Turdus ginginianus</i>), and Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120228">4</a></sup> quotes a view, according to which the <a class="vei_120a" href="आति">āti</a> was the <a class="vei_120a" href="चाष">cāṣa</a>, or blue jay (<i>Coracias indica</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120228">↑</a> Rv. x. 95, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 1, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120228">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 13, 1; Maītrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 18; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 34.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120228">↑</a> On Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120228">↑</a> On <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>. Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben;</i> 89.</li></ol></div>

आतिथि-ग्व|आतिथिग्व|आतिथि|ग्व|ఆతిథి-గ్వ|ఆతిథిగ్వ|ఆతిథి|గ్వ|ಆತಿಥಿ-ಗ್ವ|ಆತಿಥಿಗ್ವ|ಆತಿಥಿ|ಗ್ವ|ātithi-gva|ātithigva|ātithi|gva|aatithi-gva|aatithigva|aatithi|_vei_120@229|_vei1_pn057
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@228">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@230">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn056">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn057">1:057</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn058">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ātithi-gva.</b>--Patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्रोत">indrota</a>.</b></p></div>

आत्रेय|ఆత్రేయ|ಆತ್ರೇಯ|ātreya|aatreya|_vei_120@230|_vei1_pn057
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@229">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@231">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn056">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn057">1:057</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn058">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ātreya</b> is the patronymic of a pupil of <a class="vei_120a" href="माण्टि">māṇṭi</a> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120230"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120230">1</a></sup> An <a class="vei_120a" href="आत्रेय">ātreya</a> appears as a <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अंग">aṅga</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120230"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120230">2</a></sup> An <a class="vei_120a" href="आत्रेय">ātreya</a> was regularly the priest in certain rites,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120230"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120230">3</a></sup> and an <a class="vei_120a" href="आत्रेयी">ātreyī</a> occurs in an obscure passage in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120230"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120230">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120230"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120230">↑</a> ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3 (in both versions).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120230"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120230">↑</a> viii. 22.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120230"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120230">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> vii. 7; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 3, 4, 21; Kātyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 2, 21 (<i>sadasaḥ purastāt</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120230"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120230">↑</a> i. 4, 5, 13. <i>Cf.</i> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

आत्रेयी-पुत्र|आत्रेयीपुत्र|आत्रेयी|आत्रेयि|पुत्र|ఆత్రేయీ-పుత్ర|ఆత్రేయీపుత్ర|ఆత్రేయీ|ఆత్రేయి|పుత్ర|ಆತ್ರೇಯೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಆತ್ರೇಯೀಪುತ್ರ|ಆತ್ರೇಯೀ|ಆತ್ರೇಯಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|ātreyī-putra|ātreyīputra|ātreyī|ātreyi|putra|aatreyii-putra|aatreyiiputra|aatreyii|aatreyi|aatreyee-putra|aatreyeeputra|aatreyee|_vei_120@231|_vei1_pn057
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@230">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@232">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn056">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn057">1:057</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn058">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ātreyī-putra</b> is mentioned as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौतमीपुत्र">gautamīputra</a></b> in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a>, or Genealogy, in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 5, 2, in both versions).</p></div>

आथर्वण|ఆథర్వణ|ಆಥರ್ವಣ|ātharvaṇa|aatharvaNa|_vei_120@232|_vei1_pn057
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@231">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@233">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn056">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn057">1:057</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn058">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ātharvaṇa,</b> a patronymic formed from the name of the mythic <a class="vei_120a" href="अथर्वन्">atharvan</a>, is found normally in the plural neuter as a designation of the hymns of the Atharvans. This use appears in the late nineteenth book of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120232">1</a></sup> and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120232">2</a></sup> In the singular the expression <a class="vei_120a" href="आथर्वण">ātharvaṇa</a> (<a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>), though not occurring till the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120232">3</a></sup> is earlier than the term ‘Atharvaveda,’ which is first found in the Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120232">4</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120232">5</a></sup> Ātharvaṇikas, or ‘followers of the Atharvaveda,’ appear.</p><p>Specific but mainly mythical Ātharvaṇas are <b>Kabandha, <a class="vei_120a" href="बृहद्दिव">bṛhaddiva</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="भिषज्">bhiṣaj</a>, Dadhyaṅc,</b> and <b>Vicārin.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120232">↑</a> xix. 23, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120232">↑</a> xii. 9, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120232">↑</a> vii. 1, 2, 4; 2, 1; 7, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120232">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 2, 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120232">↑</a> ii. 12.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> xxv.; <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 8 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

आ-दर्श|आदर्श|दर्श|ఆ-దర్శ|ఆదర్శ|దర్శ|ಆ-ದರ್ಶ|ಆದರ್ಶ|ದರ್ಶ|ā-darśa|ādarśa|darśa|aa-darsha|aadarsha|darsha|_vei_120@233|_vei1_pn058
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@232">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@234">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn057">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn058">1:058</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn059">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ā-darśa,</b> ‘mirror,’ is a term found only in the Upaniṣads<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120233"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120233">1</a></sup> and Āraṇyakas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120233"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120233">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120233"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120233">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 1, 9; iii. 9, 15; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, viii. 7. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 2; 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120233"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120233">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 2, 4; Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, viii. 7.</li></ol></div>

आदार|ఆదార|ಆದಾರ|ādāra|aadaara|_vei_120@234|_vei1_pn058
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@233">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@235">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn057">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn058">1:058</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn059">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ādāra</b> was a kind of plant which was prescribed as a substitute for <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120234"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120234">1</a></sup> It is identified in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120234"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120234">2</a></sup> with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पूतीक">pūtīka</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120234"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120234">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 5, 10, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120234"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120234">↑</a> xiv. 1, 2, 12. <i>Cf.</i> Kāthaka Saṃhitā, xxiv. 3; Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxv. 12, 19.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 276.</li></ol></div>

आनन्द-ज चान्धनायन|आनन्दज चान्धनायन|आनन्द|ज चान्धनायन|ఆనన్ద-జ చాన్ధనాయన|ఆనన్దజ చాన్ధనాయన|ఆనన్ద|జ చాన్ధనాయన|ಆನನ್ದ-ಜ ಚಾನ್ಧನಾಯನ|ಆನನ್ದಜ ಚಾನ್ಧನಾಯನ|ಆನನ್ದ|ಜ ಚಾನ್ಧನಾಯನ|ānanda-ja cāndhanāyana|ānandaja cāndhanāyana|ānanda|ja cāndhanāyana|aananda-ja chaandhanaayana|aanandaja chaandhanaayana|aananda|ja chaandhanaayana|_vei_120@235|_vei1_pn058
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@234">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@236">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn057">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn058">1:058</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn059">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ānanda-ja Cāndhanāyana</b> is mentioned as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाम्ब">śāmba</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120235"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120235">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120235"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120235">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372.</li></ol></div>

आनभि-म्लात|आनभिम्लात|आनभि|म्लात|ఆనభి-మ్లాత|ఆనభిమ్లాత|ఆనభి|మ్లాత|ಆನಭಿ-ಮ್ಲಾತ|ಆನಭಿಮ್ಲಾತ|ಆನಭಿ|ಮ್ಲಾತ|ānabhi-mlāta|ānabhimlāta|ānabhi|mlāta|aanabhi-mlaata|aanabhimlaata|aanabhi|mlaata|_vei_120@236|_vei1_pn058
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@235">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@237">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn057">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn058">1:058</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn059">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ānabhi-mlāta</b> is mentioned in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a>, or Genealogy, in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120236"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120236">1</a></sup> as a pupil of <a class="vei_120a" href="आनभिम्लात">ānabhimlāta</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120236"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120236">↑</a> ii. 6, 2 (not in the Mādhyandina version).</li></ol></div>

आनव|ఆనవ|ಆನವ|ānava|aanava|_vei_120@237|_vei1_pn058
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@236">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@238">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn057">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn058">1:058</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn059">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ānava.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अनु">anu</a>.</b></p></div>

आनूक|ఆనూక|ಆನೂಕ|ānūka|aanuuka|aanooka|_vei_120@238|_vei1_pn058
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@237">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@239">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn057">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn058">1:058</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn059">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ānūka.</b>--Geldner<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120238"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120238">1</a></sup> thinks that in its solitary occurrence in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120238"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120238">2</a></sup> this word means an ornament. Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120238"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120238">3</a></sup> takes it adverbially, and so do Ludwig and Oldenberg.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120238"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120238">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 94.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120238"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120238">↑</a> v. 33, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120238"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120238">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary. <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

आपया|ఆపయా|ಆಪಯಾ|āpayā|aapayaa|_vei_120@239|_vei1_pn058
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@238">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@240">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn057">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn058">1:058</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn059">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āpayā</b> is the name of a river mentioned once only in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120239">1</a></sup> when it occurs between the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृषद्वती">dṛṣadvatī</a></b> and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>.</b> Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120239">2</a></sup> was inclined to identify it with the Āpagā as a name for the Ganges, but Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120239">3</a></sup> correctly places it near the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>, either as the small tributary which flows past Thānesar or the modern Indramatī farther west, while Pischel<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120239">4</a></sup> assigns it to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>,</b> of which the <a class="vei_120a" href="आपया">āpayā</a> is mentioned as a famous river in the Mahābhārata.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120239">5</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120239">↑</a> iii. 23, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120239">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 200. But <i>cf. ibid.,</i> 4, 304.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120239">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 18.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120239">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 218.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120239">↑</a> Mahābhārata, iii. 83, 68.</li></ol></div>

आबयु|ఆబయు|ಆಬಯು|ābayu|aabayu|_vei_120@240|_vei1_pn059
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@239">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@241">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn058">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn059">1:059</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn060">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ābayu</b> is apparently the name of a plant in the Atharvaveda;<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120240">1</a></sup> the mustard plant<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120240">2</a></sup> may have been meant, but the sense is quite uncertain.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120240">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120240">↑</a> vi. 16, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120240">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 465, following the indication of the use of the hymn in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120240">↑</a> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 292; Zimmer. <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 72.</li></ol></div>

आभि-प्रतारिण|आभिप्रतारिण|आभि|प्रतारिण|ఆభి-ప్రతారిణ|ఆభిప్రతారిణ|ఆభి|ప్రతారిణ|ಆಭಿ-ಪ್ರತಾರಿಣ|ಆಭಿಪ್ರತಾರಿಣ|ಆಭಿ|ಪ್ರತಾರಿಣ|ābhi-pratāriṇa|ābhipratāriṇa|ābhi|pratāriṇa|aabhi-prataariNa|aabhiprataariNa|aabhi|prataariNa|_vei_120@241|_vei1_pn059
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@240">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@242">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn058">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn059">1:059</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn060">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ābhi-pratāriṇa.</b>--Patronymic of <b>Vṛddhadyumna.</b></p></div>

आ भूति त्वाष्ट्र|ఆ భూతి త్వాష్ట్ర|ಆ ಭೂತಿ ತ್ವಾಷ್ಟ್ರ|ā bhūti tvāṣṭra|aa bhuuti tvaaShTra|aa bhooti tvaaShTra|_vei_120@242|_vei1_pn059
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@241">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@243">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn058">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn059">1:059</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn060">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ā <a class="vei_120a" href="भूति">bhūti</a> Tvāṣṭra</b> is mentioned in two Vaṃśas, or Genealogies, of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120242"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120242">1</a></sup> as a pupil of <b>Viśvarūpa <a class="vei_120a" href="त्वाष्ट्र">tvāṣṭra</a>,</b> both teachers being no doubt equally mythical.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120242"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120242">↑</a> ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3 (in both recensions).</li></ol></div>

आमलक|ఆమలక|ಆಮಲಕ|āmalaka|aamalaka|_vei_120@243|_vei1_pn059
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@242">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@244">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn058">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn059">1:059</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn060">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āmalaka</b> (neuter), a common word later, is found in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vii. 3, 1), denoting the Myrobalan fruit. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अमला">amalā</a>.</b></p></div>

आमिक्षा|ఆమిక్షా|ಆಮಿಕ್ಷಾ|āmikṣā|aamikShaa|aamixaa|_vei_120@244|_vei1_pn059
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@243">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@245">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn058">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn059">1:059</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn060">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āmikṣā</b> designates a mess of clotted curds. It is not known to the Rigveda, but occurs in all the later Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120244">1</a></sup> Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120244">2</a></sup> etc., and is associated with the <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120244">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120244">↑</a> Av. x. 9, 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 5, 4; iii. 3, 9, 2; vi. 2, 5, 3; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 9; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 21; 23, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120244">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 1, 7. 9; iii. 3, 3, 2, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 8, 8; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 438 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 19, 99, 101); Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, viii. 8, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120244">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>. Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 2, 40.</li></ol></div>

आम्ब|आंब|ఆమ్బ|ఆంబ|ಆಮ್ಬ|ಆಂಬ|āmba|aamba|_vei_120@245|_vei1_pn059
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@244">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@246">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn058">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn059">1:059</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn060">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āmba</b> denotes in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120245"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120245">1</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120245"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120245">2</a></sup> Saṃhitās a grain, called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नाम्ब">nāmba</a></b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120245"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120245">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120245"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120245">↑</a> i. 8, 10, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120245"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120245">↑</a> xv. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120245"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120245">↑</a> v. 3, 3, 8.</li></ol></div>

आम्बष्ठ्य|आंबष्ठ्य|ఆమ్బష్ఠ్య|ఆంబష్ఠ్య|ಆಮ್ಬಷ್ಠ್ಯ|ಆಂಬಷ್ಠ್ಯ|āmbaṣṭhya|aambaShThya|_vei_120@246|_vei1_pn059
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@245">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@247">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn058">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn059">1:059</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn060">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āmbaṣṭhya</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 21) as a king, whose priest for the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>, or royal inauguration, was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नारद">nārada</a>.</b> Presumably the name is local, meaning ‘King of  the Ambaṣṭhas,’ as interpreted in the St. Petersburg Dictionary. Later the term <a class="vei_120a" href="अम्बष्ठ">ambaṣṭha</a> denotes ‘a man of mingled <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> parentage by father and mother’ respectively.</p></div>

आ-यतन|आयतन|यतन|ఆ-యతన|ఆయతన|యతన|ಆ-ಯತನ|ಆಯತನ|ಯತನ|ā-yatana|āyatana|yatana|aa-yatana|aayatana|_vei_120@247|_vei1_pn060
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@246">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@248">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn059">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn060">1:060</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn061">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ā-yatana.</b>--The general sense of ‘abode’ or ‘home’ appears to be limited in one passage of the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vii. 24, 2) to the sense of ‘holy place,’ ‘sanctuary,’ which is found in the epic.</p></div>

आयवस|ఆయవస|ಆಯವಸ|āyavasa|aayavasa|_vei_120@248|_vei1_pn060
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@247">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@249">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn059">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn060">1:060</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn061">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āyavasa</b> is mentioned apparently as a king in a corrupt and unintelligible verse of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120248">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120248">↑</a> i. 122, 15. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 206; Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

आयु|ఆయు|ಆಯು|āyu|aayu|_vei_120@249|_vei1_pn060
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@248">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@250">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn059">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn060">1:060</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn061">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āyu</b> appears in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120249"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120249">1</a></sup> with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुत्स">kutsa</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अतिथिग्व">atithigva</a></b> as having been defeated, with Indra's aid, by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तूर्वयाण">tūrvayāṇa</a>,</b> who is believed by Pischel<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120249"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120249">2</a></sup> to have been King of the <b>Pakthas.</b> Possibly he is elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120249"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120249">3</a></sup> referred to as victorious, by Indra's aid, over <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वेश">veśa</a>.</b> Elsewhere he is quite mythical.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120249"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120249">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120249"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120249">↑</a> i. 53, 10; ii. 14, 7; vi. 18, 3; viii. 53, 2; Bergaigne, <i>Religion Védique,</i> 1, 60.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120249"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120249">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 71-75.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120249"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120249">↑</a> Rv. x. 49, 5; but the word is possibly not a proper name.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120249"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120249">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> 100, 135, 140.</li></ol></div>

आयुत|ఆయుత|ಆಯುತ|āyuta|aayuta|_vei_120@250|_vei1_pn060
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@249">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@251">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn059">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn060">1:060</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn061">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āyuta.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="घृत">ghṛta</a>.</b></p></div>

आ-युध|आयुध|युध|ఆ-యుధ|ఆయుధ|యుధ|ಆ-ಯುಧ|ಆಯುಧ|ಯುಧ|ā-yudha|āyudha|yudha|aa-yudha|aayudha|_vei_120@251|_vei1_pn060
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@250">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@252">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn059">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn060">1:060</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn061">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ā-yudha,</b> ‘weapon,’ in its widest sense covers the whole of a Kṣatriya's warlike equipment, which in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120251">1</a></sup> is summed up as horse-chariot (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्व">aśva</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a></i>), bow and arrows (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="इषु">iṣu</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="धन्व">dhanva</a></i>), and corselet (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कवच">kavaca</a></i>). As the bow and arrow (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="इषु">iṣu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="धन्वन्">dhanvan</a></i>) were essential as the main weapons of the Vedic fighter, they are probably meant when <a class="vei_120a" href="आयुध">āyudha</a> is used specifically of weapons, as often from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120251">2</a></sup> onwards. The battle hymn in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120251">3</a></sup> confirms this view, as it presents to us the warrior armed with bow and arrow on his chariot, and clad in armour (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्मन्">varman</a></b>), with a guard (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="हस्तघ्न">hastaghna</a></b>) on the left arm to avoid the friction of the bow-string. The corselet was not a single solid piece of metal, but consisted of many pieces fitted together (<i>syūta</i>);<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120251">4</a></sup> it may  have been made either of metal plates or, as is more likely, of some stiff material plated with metal. In addition the warrior wore a helmet (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="शिप्रा">śiprā</a></b>). There is no trace of the use of a shield, nor is there any clear record of the employment of greaves or other guard for the feet.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120251">5</a></sup> Skill in the use of weapons is referred to in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120251">6</a></sup></p><p>It is doubtful whether sling stones (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अद्रि">adri</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अशनि">aśani</a></b>) were in ordinary use. The hook (<i>aṅkuśa</i>)<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120251">7</a></sup> also is merely a divine weapon, and the axe (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्वधिति">svadhiti</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120251">8</a></sup> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वाशी">vāśī</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="परशु">paraśu</a></i>) does not occur in mortal combats. For the use of the spear see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋष्टि">ṛṣṭi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="रम्भिणी">rambhiṇī</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शक्ति">śakti</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शरु">śaru</a>;</b> of the sword, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="असि">asi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कृति">kṛti</a>.</b> Neither weapon can be considered ordinary in warfare, nor was the club (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="वज्र">vajra</a></b>) used. For the modes of warfare see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="संग्राम">saṃgrāma</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120251">↑</a> vii. 19, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120251">↑</a> i. 39, 2; 61. 13; 92, 1; ii. 30, 9. etc.; Av. vi. 133, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120251">↑</a> vi. 75.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120251">↑</a> Rv. i. 31, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120251">↑</a> Grassmann saw greaves in <i>vaṭūriṇā <a class="vei_120a" href="पदा">padā</a></i> in Rv. i. 133, 2, but this is most improbable.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120251">↑</a> i. 92, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 183; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 295.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120251">↑</a> Rv. viii. 17, 10; x. 44, 9; 134, 6; Av. vi. 83, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts.</i> 5, 87.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120251">↑</a> Rv. v. 32, 10; ix. 67, 30; x. 43, 9.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 298301; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts.</i> 5, 469-472; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 281 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

आयोगव.--मरुत्त आवि-क्षित|आयोगव.--मरुत्त आविक्षित|आयोगव.--मरुत्त आवि|क्षित|ఆయోగవ.--మరుత్త ఆవి-క్షిత|ఆయోగవ.--మరుత్త ఆవిక్షిత|ఆయోగవ.--మరుత్త ఆవి|క్షిత|ಆಯೋಗವ.--ಮರುತ್ತ ಆವಿ-ಕ್ಷಿತ|ಆಯೋಗವ.--ಮರುತ್ತ ಆವಿಕ್ಷಿತ|ಆಯೋಗವ.--ಮರುತ್ತ ಆವಿ|ಕ್ಷಿತ|āyogava.--marutta āvi-kṣita|āyogava.--marutta āvikṣita|āyogava.--marutta āvi|kṣita|aayogava.--marutta aavi-kShita|aayogava.--marutta aavikShita|aayogava.--marutta aavi|kShita|aayogava.--marutta aavi-xita|aayogava.--marutta aavixita|xita|_vei_120@252|_vei1_pn061
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@251">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@253">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn060">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn061">1:061</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn062">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āyogava.--<a class="vei_120a" href="मरुत्त आवि">marutta āvi</a>-kṣita,</b> the Āyogava king, is mentioned as a sacrificer in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120252">1</a></sup> where also a <a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a> (‘stanza’) celebrating his sacrifice is cited. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अयोगू">ayogū</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120252">↑</a> xiii. 5. 4, 6. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 9, 14-16.</li></ol></div>

आरंगर|आरङ्गर|ఆరంగర|ఆరఙ్గర|ಆರಂಗರ|ಆರಙ್ಗರ|āraṅgara|aaraMgara|aarangara|_vei_120@253|_vei1_pn061
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@252">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@254">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn060">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn061">1:061</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn062">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āraṅgara</b> is one of the names of the bee found in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120253"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120253">1</a></sup> Other names are <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरह्">sarah</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भृंगा">bhṛṅgā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120253"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120253">↑</a> x. 106, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 97.</li></ol></div>

आरा|ఆరా|ಆರಾ|ārā|aaraa|_vei_120@254|_vei1_pn061
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@253">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@255">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn060">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn061">1:061</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn062">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ārā,</b> a word later<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120254"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120254">1</a></sup> known as an ‘awl’ or ‘gimlet,’ occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120254"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120254">2</a></sup> only to designate a weapon of Pūṣan, with whose pastoral character its later use for piercing leather is consistent. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वाशी">vāśī</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120254"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120254">↑</a> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 365. n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120254"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120254">↑</a> vi. 53. 8.</li></ol></div>

आराढि|ఆరాఢి|ಆರಾಢಿ|ārāḍhi|aaraaDhi|_vei_120@255|_vei1_pn061
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@254">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@256">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn060">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn061">1:061</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn062">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ārāḍhi.</b>--Patronymic of <b>Saujāta.</b> <i>Cf.</i> <b>Arāḍa.</b> </p></div>

आरुण औप-वेशि|आरुण औपवेशि|आरुण औप|वेशि|ఆరుణ ఔప-వేశి|ఆరుణ ఔపవేశి|ఆరుణ ఔప|వేశి|ಆರುಣ ಔಪ-ವೇಶಿ|ಆರುಣ ಔಪವೇಶಿ|ಆರುಣ ಔಪ|ವೇಶಿ|āruṇa aupa-veśi|āruṇa aupaveśi|āruṇa aupa|veśi|aaruNa aupa-veshi|aaruNa aupaveshi|aaruNa aupa|veshi|_vei_120@256|_vei1_pn062
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@255">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@257">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn061">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn062">1:062</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn063">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āruṇa <a class="vei_120a" href="औप">aupa</a>-veśi.</b>--So the manuscripts let us read the former word in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, but this is doubtless an error for <b>Aruṇa.</b></p></div>

आरुणि|ఆరుణి|ಆರುಣಿ|āruṇi|aaruNi|_vei_120@257|_vei1_pn062
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@256">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@258">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn061">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn062">1:062</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn063">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āruṇi</b> is the patronymic normally referring to <b>Uddālaka,</b> son of <b>Aruṇa <a class="vei_120a" href="औपवेशि">aupaveśi</a>.</b> Uddālaka is probably also meant by <a class="vei_120a" href="आरुणि">āruṇi</a> Yaśasvin, who occurs as a teacher of the Subrahmaṇyā (a kind of recitation) in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120257"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120257">1</a></sup> Āruṇis are referred to both in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120257"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120257">2</a></sup> and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120257"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120257">3</a></sup> as well as in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120257"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120257">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120257"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120257">↑</a> ii. 80.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120257"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120257">↑</a> ii. 5, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 204.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120257"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120257">↑</a> xiii. 12.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120257"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120257">↑</a> ii. 4. 1.</li></ol></div>

आरुणेय|ఆరుణేయ|ಆರುಣೇಯ|āruṇeya|aaruNeya|_vei_120@258|_vei1_pn062
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@257">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@259">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn061">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn062">1:062</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn063">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āruṇeya.</b>--An epithet of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्वेतकेतु">śvetaketu</a>,</b> indicating his descent from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उद्दालक आरुणि">uddālaka āruṇi</a></b> and <b>Aruṇa <a class="vei_120a" href="औपवेशि">aupaveśi</a>.</b> It is apparently confined to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120258"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120258">1</a></sup> and Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120258"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120258">2</a></sup> in which <a class="vei_120a" href="श्वेतकेतु">śvetaketu</a> plays a great part.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120258"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120258">↑</a> x. 3, 4, 1; xi. 2, 7, 12; 5, 4, 18; 6, 2, 1; xii. 2, 1, 9; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120258"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120258">↑</a> v. 3, 1; vi. 1, 1.</li></ol></div>

आर्क्ष|ఆర్క్ష|ಆರ್ಕ್ಷ|ārkṣa|aarkSha|aarxa|_vei_120@259|_vei1_pn062
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@258">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@260">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn061">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn062">1:062</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn063">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ārkṣa.</b>--Patronymic of <b>Śrutarvan</b> and of <b>Āśvamedha.</b></p></div>

आर्जीक|ఆర్జీక|ಆರ್ಜೀಕ|ārjīka|aarjiika|aarjeeka|_vei_120@260|_vei1_pn062
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@259">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@261">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn061">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn062">1:062</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn063">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ārjīka</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120260">1</a></sup> and <b>Ārjīkīya</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120260">2</a></sup> (masc.), <b>Ārjīkīyā</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120260">3</a></sup> (fem.).--The two masculine forms probably denote the people or land, while the feminine word designates the river of the land. Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120260">4</a></sup> locates the country in or near Kaśmir, as Arrian<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120260">5</a></sup> mentions Arsaces, brother of Abhisares, who presumably took his name from his people, and Abhisāra bordered on Kaśmir. Pischel<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120260">6</a></sup> accepts <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्जीक">ārjīka</a> as designating a country, which he, however, thinks cannot be identified. But neither Roth<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120260">7</a></sup> nor Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120260">8</a></sup> recognizes the word as a proper name. On the other hand, all authorities agree in regarding Ārjīkīyā as the name of a  river. <b>Roth</b><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120260">9</a></sup> does so in one passage<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120260">10</a></sup> only, elsewhere seeing references to <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> vessels; but it seems necessary to treat the word alike in all passages containing it. Zimmer does not locate the river, and Pischel denies the possibility of its identification. Hillebrandt thinks it may have been the Upper Indus, or the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वितस्ता">vitastā</a></b> (the Jhelum), or some other stream. Grassmann follows <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120260">11</a></sup> in identifying it with the the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विपाश्">vipāś</a></b> (Beäs), but this is rendered improbable by the position of the name in the hymn in praise of rivers (<i>nadīstuti</i>).<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120260">12</a></sup> Brunnhofer<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120260">13</a></sup> identifies it with the Arghesan, a tributary of the Arghanab.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120260">↑</a> Singular: Rv. viii. 7, 29; ix. 113, 2. Plural: ix. 65, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120260">↑</a> Rv. viii. 64, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120260">↑</a> Rv. x. 75, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120260">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 126-137.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120260">↑</a> Anabasis, v. 29, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120260">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 209, 217.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120260">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120260">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben.</i> 12-14.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120260">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>., s.v. <a class="vei_120a" href="सुषोमा">suṣomā</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120260">↑</a> Rv. x. 75, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120260">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ix. 26.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120260">↑</a> Rv. x. 75.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120260">↑</a> <i>Iran und Turan,</i> 52.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Max Mūller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 398; 399.</li></ol></div>

आर्जुनेय|ఆర్జునేయ|ಆರ್ಜುನೇಯ|ārjuneya|aarjuneya|_vei_120@261|_vei1_pn063
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@260">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@262">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn062">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn063">1:063</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn064">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ārjuneya.</b>--In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120261"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120261">1</a></sup> this name occurs as the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौत्स">kautsa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120261"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120261">↑</a> i. 112, 23; iv. 26, 1; vii. 19, 2; viii. 1, 11.</li></ol></div>

आर्तभागी-पुत्र|आर्तभागीपुत्र|आर्तभागी|आर्तभागि|पुत्र|ఆర్తభాగీ-పుత్ర|ఆర్తభాగీపుత్ర|ఆర్తభాగీ|ఆర్తభాగి|పుత్ర|ಆರ್ತಭಾಗೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಆರ್ತಭಾಗೀಪುತ್ರ|ಆರ್ತಭಾಗೀ|ಆರ್ತಭಾಗಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|ārtabhāgī-putra|ārtabhāgīputra|ārtabhāgī|ārtabhāgi|putra|aartabhaagii-putra|aartabhaagiiputra|aartabhaagii|aartabhaagi|aartabhaagee-putra|aartabhaageeputra|aartabhaagee|_vei_120@262|_vei1_pn063
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@261">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@263">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn062">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn063">1:063</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn064">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ārtabhāgī-putra</b> is mentioned as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शौंगी-पुत्र">śauṅgī-putra</a></b> in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> or Genealogy in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120262"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120262">1</a></sup> Ārtabhāga is a patronymic of Jaratkārava in the same <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120262"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120262">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120262"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120262">↑</a> vi. 5, 2 (also in the Mādhyandina version).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120262"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120262">↑</a> iii. 2, 1, 13.</li></ol></div>

आर्तव|ఆర్తవ|ಆರ್ತವ|ārtava|aartava|_vei_120@263|_vei1_pn063
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@262">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@264">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn062">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn063">1:063</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn064">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ārtava.</b>--This expression denotes a portion of the year consisting of more seasons than one. But it does not bear the exact sense of ‘half-year,’ as suggested by Zimmer.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120263">1</a></sup> This is shown by the fact that it occurs regularly in the plural, not in the dual. In the Atharvaveda it occurs between seasons and years (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="हायन">hāyana</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120263">2</a></sup> but also in the combinations, ‘seasons, Ārtavas, months, years,;<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120263">3</a></sup> ‘half-months, months, Ārtavas, seasons’;<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120263">4</a></sup>  ‘seasons, Ārtavas, months, half-months, days and nights, day’;<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120263">5</a></sup> and in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā ‘months, seasons, Ārtavas, the year,’<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120263">6</a></sup> or simply with the seasons.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120263">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120263">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben.</i> 374.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120263">↑</a> iii. 10, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120263">↑</a> iii. 10, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120263">↑</a> xi. 7, 20. <i>Cf.</i> xv. 6, 6; 17, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120263">↑</a> xvi. 8, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120263">↑</a> xxii. 28.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120263">↑</a> Av. v. 28, 2. 13; x. 6, 18; 7, 5; xi. 3, 17; 6, 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 2, 6, 1, 3. <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i, 3. cited in this sense in the St. Petersburg Dictionary, is not so to be understood, as the word there is merely adjectival.</li></ol></div>

आर्त्नी|आर्त्नि|ఆర్త్నీ|ఆర్త్ని|ಆರ್ತ್ನೀ|ಆರ್ತ್ನಿ|ārtnī|ārtni|aartnii|aartni|aartnee|_vei_120@264|_vei1_pn064
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@263">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@265">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn063">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn064">1:064</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn066">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ārtnī</b> denotes the end of the bow to which the bow-string (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ज्या">jyā</a></i>) was attached.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120264"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120264">1</a></sup> The string was not normally kept fastened to both ends of the bow, but when an arrow was to be shot it was strung taut.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120264"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120264">2</a></sup> On the other hand, the legend of the death of Viṣṇu, told in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120264"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120264">3</a></sup> and Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120264"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120264">4</a></sup> expressly contemplates his leaning on his strung bow, which cleaves his head by the sudden springing apart of the two ends when the bow-string is gnawed through.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120264"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120264">↑</a> Rv. vi. 75, 4; Av. i. 1, 3; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 11; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120264"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120264">↑</a> Rv. x. 166, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Av. vi. 42, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120264"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120264">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 5. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120264"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120264">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 5. 6; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 1, 1, 7 <i>et seq.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 297, 298; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 270.</li></ol></div>

आर्य|ఆర్య|ಆರ್ಯ|ārya|aarya|_vei_120@265|_vei1_pn064
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@264">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@266">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn063">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn064">1:064</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn066">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ārya</b> is the normal designation in the Vedic literature from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120265">1</a></sup> onwards of an Āryan, a member of the three upper classes, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>,</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>,</b> as the formal division is given in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120265">2</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a> stands in opposition to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120265">3</a></sup> but also to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>.</b> Sometimes<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120265">4</a></sup> the expression is restricted to the <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> caste,  the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> receiving special designations; but this use is not common, and it is often uncertain also whether <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अर्य">arya</a></b> is not meant. The phrase Śūdrāryau<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120265">5</a></sup> is especially ambiguous, but appears to have denoted originally the <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> and the Āryan, for in the Mahāvrata ceremony the fight between a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> and an <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a> is represented in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> as one between a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> and a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>, though the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> treats it as a fight between a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> and a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>.</p><p>The word <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a> (fem. Āryā or Ārī) also occurs frequently used as an adjective to describe the Āryan classes (<i>viśaḥ</i>),<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120265">6</a></sup> or name (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="नामन्">nāman</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120265">7</a></sup> or caste (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120265">8</a></sup> or dwellings (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="धामन्">dhāman</a></i>);<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120265">9</a></sup> or again reference is made to the Āryan supremacy (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रत">vrata</a></i>)<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120265">10</a></sup> being extended over the land. Āryan foes (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वृत्र">vṛtra</a></i>)<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120265">11</a></sup> are referred to beside <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a> foes, and there are many<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120265">12</a></sup> references to war of Āryan versus Āryan, as well as to war of Āryan against <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>. From this it can be fairly deduced that even by the time of the Rigveda the Āryan communities had advanced far beyond the stage of simple conquest of the aborigines. In the later Saṃhitās and Brāhmaṇas the wars alluded to seem mainly Āryan wars, no doubt in consequence of the fusion of <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a> into one community.</p><p>Weber<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120265">13</a></sup> considers that the five peoples known to the Rigveda were the Āryans and the four peoples of the quarters (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दिश्">diś</a></i>) of the earth, but this is doubtful. Āryan speech (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वाच्">vāc</a></i>)<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120265">14</a></sup> is specially referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyakas. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120265">↑</a> Rv. i. 51, 8; 130, 8; 156, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120265">↑</a> iv. 1, 6 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120265">↑</a> Rv. i. 51, 8. 9; 103, 3; vi. 20, 10; 25, 2, 3, etc. (opposed to <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>); Av. iv. 20, 4. 8; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 6, 6; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiv. 30, etc. (opposed to Sūdra).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120265">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 205, 215, finds this use--understanding <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a> as meant--in Atharvaveda, xix. 32, 8, and 62, 1, as well as in passages where Śūdrāryau is found. Lanman, in Whitney's Translation of the Atharvaveda, 948, 1003, quotes his view with approval; but Whitney's version leaves no doubt that he read and understood the text as <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a>, the Āryan, not the <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>. For Whitney's view, Av. iv. 20, 4. 8 may be cited; and so Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> takes the passages. In <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 10, 8, Śūdrāryau must mean Sūdra and <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>; but the Padapāṭha takes it as <a class="vei_120a" href="अर्य">arya</a>, and so does Zimmer.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120265">↑</a> See <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 9, 3, with Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiii. 3, 7. 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxiv. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 6, 7; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 2, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvii. 6, 2; Anupada <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, vii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120265">↑</a> Rv. i. 77, 3; 96, 31; x. 11, 4; 43, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120265">↑</a> Rv. x; 49, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120265">↑</a> Rv. iii. 34, 9. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120265">↑</a> Rv. ix. 63, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120265">↑</a> Rv. x. 65, 11. So <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> are styled Āryan, as supporters of the Āryan people (Rv. vi. 60, 6).</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120265">↑</a> Rv. vi. 33, 3; vii. 83, 1; x. 69, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120265">↑</a> Rv. i. 102, 5; iii. 32, 14; vi. 22, 10; 25, 2. 3; viii. 2, 4. 27; x. 38, 3; 83, 1; 102, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120265">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 288. <i>Cf.</i> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. <a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्टि">kṛṣṭi</a>,</i> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्च">pañca</a> Janāsaḥ.</b></li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120265">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 2, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, viii. 9. <i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 196, 255; and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वाच्">vāc</a>.</b></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 207 <i>et seq.;</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 214 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

आर्य|ఆర్య|ಆರ್ಯ|ārya|aarya|_vei_120@266|_vei1_pn066
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@265">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@267">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn064">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn066">1:066</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn067">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ārya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="माल्य">mālya</a>.</b></p></div>

अर्ष्टि-षेण|अर्ष्टिषेण|अर्ष्टि|षेण|అర్ష్టి-షేణ|అర్ష్టిషేణ|అర్ష్టి|షేణ|ಅರ್ಷ್ಟಿ-ಷೇಣ|ಅರ್ಷ್ಟಿಷೇಣ|ಅರ್ಷ್ಟಿ|ಷೇಣ|arṣṭi-ṣeṇa|arṣṭiṣeṇa|arṣṭi|ṣeṇa|arShTi-SheNa|arShTiSheNa|arShTi|SheNa|_vei_120@267|_vei1_pn066
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@266">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@268">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn064">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn066">1:066</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn067">↷</a><hr><p><b>Arṣṭi-ṣeṇa.</b>--Patronymic of <b>Devāpi.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120267"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120267">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120267"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120267">↑</a> Rv. x. 98, 5. 6. 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 11; Bṛhaddevatā, vii. 155.</li></ol></div>

आल|ఆల|ಆಲ|āla|aala|_vei_120@268|_vei1_pn066
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@267">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@269">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn064">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn066">1:066</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn067">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āla</b> appears to mean ‘weed’ in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120268">1</a></sup> and to form part of three other words,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120268">2</a></sup> denoting, according to Sāyaṇa, grass-creepers (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सस्य">sasya</a>-vallī</i>)--viz., <a class="vei_120a" href="अलसाला">alasālā</a>, Silañjālā,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120268">3</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="नीलागलसाल">nīlāgalasāla</a>. Whitney,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120268">4</a></sup> however, does not think that the words can be given any determinate sense.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120268">↑</a> Av. vi. 16, 3. But Whitney takes the word as a verb, comparing v. 22, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120268">↑</a> Av. vi. 16, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120268">↑</a> Sāyaṇa reads Śalāñjālā, and the manuscripts of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (vi. 16), have Śilāñjālā. But <i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सिलाची">silācī</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120268">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 292, 293. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 466.</li></ol></div>

आलम्बायनी-पुत्र|आलम्बायनीपुत्र|आलम्बायनी|आलम्बायनि|पुत्र|आलंबायनी-पुत्र|आलंबायनीपुत्र|आलंबायनी|आलंबायनि|ఆలమ్బాయనీ-పుత్ర|ఆలమ్బాయనీపుత్ర|ఆలమ్బాయనీ|ఆలమ్బాయని|పుత్ర|ఆలంబాయనీ-పుత్ర|ఆలంబాయనీపుత్ర|ఆలంబాయనీ|ఆలంబాయని|ಆಲಮ್ಬಾಯನೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಆಲಮ್ಬಾಯನೀಪುತ್ರ|ಆಲಮ್ಬಾಯನೀ|ಆಲಮ್ಬಾಯನಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|ಆಲಂಬಾಯನೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಆಲಂಬಾಯನೀಪುತ್ರ|ಆಲಂಬಾಯನೀ|ಆಲಂಬಾಯನಿ|ālambāyanī-putra|ālambāyanīputra|ālambāyanī|ālambāyani|putra|aalambaayanii-putra|aalambaayaniiputra|aalambaayanii|aalambaayani|aalambaayanee-putra|aalambaayaneeputra|aalambaayanee|_vei_120@269|_vei1_pn066
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@268">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@270">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn064">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn066">1:066</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn067">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ālambāyanī-putra</b> is mentioned in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> or Genealogy of the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 5, 2) as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आलम्बी-पुत्र">ālambī-putra</a>.</b> In the Mādhyandina recension (vi. 4, 32) the relation is reversed, for there he is teacher of <a class="vei_120a" href="आलम्बी-पुत्र">ālambī-putra</a> and pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जायन्ती-पुत्र">jāyantī-putra</a>.</b></p></div>

आलम्बी-पुत्र|आलम्बीपुत्र|आलम्बी|आलम्बि|पुत्र|आलंबी-पुत्र|आलंबीपुत्र|आलंबी|आलंबि|ఆలమ్బీ-పుత్ర|ఆలమ్బీపుత్ర|ఆలమ్బీ|ఆలమ్బి|పుత్ర|ఆలంబీ-పుత్ర|ఆలంబీపుత్ర|ఆలంబీ|ఆలంబి|ಆಲಮ್ಬೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಆಲಮ್ಬೀಪುತ್ರ|ಆಲಮ್ಬೀ|ಆಲಮ್ಬಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|ಆಲಂಬೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಆಲಂಬೀಪುತ್ರ|ಆಲಂಬೀ|ಆಲಂಬಿ|ālambī-putra|ālambīputra|ālambī|ālambi|putra|aalambii-putra|aalambiiputra|aalambii|aalambi|aalambee-putra|aalambeeputra|aalambee|_vei_120@270|_vei1_pn066
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@269">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@271">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn064">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn066">1:066</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn067">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ālambī-putra</b> is a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जायन्ती-पुत्र">jāyantī-putra</a></b> according to a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 5, 2), but of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आलम्बायनी-पुत्र">ālambāyanī-putra</a></b> according to the Mādhyandina (vi. 4, 32).</p></div>

आलिगी|आलिगि|ఆలిగీ|ఆలిగి|ಆಲಿಗೀ|ಆಲಿಗಿ|āligī|āligi|aaligii|aaligi|aaligee|_vei_120@271|_vei1_pn066
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@270">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@272">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn064">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn066">1:066</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn067">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āligī</b> is the name of a kind of serpent in the Atharvaveda (v. 13, 7). <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विलिगी">viligī</a>.</b></p></div>

आ-वसथ|आवसथ|वसथ|ఆ-వసథ|ఆవసథ|వసథ|ಆ-ವಸಥ|ಆವಸಥ|ವಸಥ|ā-vasatha|āvasatha|vasatha|aa-vasatha|aavasatha|_vei_120@272|_vei1_pn066
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@271">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@273">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn064">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn066">1:066</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn067">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ā-vasatha</b> (‘dwelling’).--The precise sense<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120272"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120272">1</a></sup> of this term appears to be a place for the reception of guests, especially Brāhmaṇas and others on the occasion of feasts and sacrifices (somewhat like the modern meaning of <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="शाला">śālā</a> as a resthouse for pilgrims), a use derived from the more general sense of ‘abode.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120272"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120272">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120272"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120272">↑</a> Av. ix. 6, 5 (a hymn in praise of entertaining Brāhmaṇas); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 10, 6; iii. 7, 4, 6; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 4, 4, 6 (where Eggeling renders the word as if meaning ‘house’ merely); Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1, 1, etc. Details are given in the Sūtras--<i>e.g.,</i> Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, v. 9, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 9, 25, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120272"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120272">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 12. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 120, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

आविक|ఆవిక|ಆವಿಕ|āvika|aavika|_vei_120@273|_vei1_pn067
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@272">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@274">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn066">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn067">1:067</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn068">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āvika</b> (‘coming from the sheep,’ <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अवि">avi</a></i>) is a term for ‘wool,’ which occurs first in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (ii. 3, 6). <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अवि">avi</a>.</b></p></div>

अवि-क्षित|अविक्षित|अवि|क्षित|అవి-క్షిత|అవిక్షిత|అవి|క్షిత|ಅವಿ-ಕ್ಷಿತ|ಅವಿಕ್ಷಿತ|ಅವಿ|ಕ್ಷಿತ|avi-kṣita|avikṣita|avi|kṣita|avi-kShita|avikShita|kShita|avi-xita|avixita|xita|_vei_120@274|_vei1_pn067
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@273">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@275">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn066">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn067">1:067</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn068">↷</a><hr><p><b>Avi-kṣita.</b>--Patronymic of <b>Marutta,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120274">1</a></sup> the <b>Āyogava.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120274">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 21; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 4, 6.</li></ol></div>

आ-शरीक|आशरीक|शरीक|ఆ-శరీక|ఆశరీక|శరీక|ಆ-ಶರೀಕ|ಆಶರೀಕ|ಶರೀಕ|ā-śarīka|āśarīka|śarīka|aa-shariika|aashariika|shariika|aa-shareeka|aashareeka|shareeka|_vei_120@275|_vei1_pn067
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@274">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@276">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn066">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn067">1:067</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn068">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ā-śarīka</b> appears to denote a disease in a hymn of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120275"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120275">1</a></sup> celebrating the powers of the <b>Jaṅgida</b> plant. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120275"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120275">2</a></sup> thinks that it merely denotes the pain in the limbs accompanying fever. Whitney<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120275"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120275">3</a></sup> suggests taking the word as merely an epithet.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120275"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120275">↑</a> xix. 34, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120275"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120275">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 65, 391.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120275"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120275">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 953. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 673.</li></ol></div>

आ-शिर्|आशिर्|शिर्|ఆ-శిర్|ఆశిర్|శిర్|ಆ-ಶಿರ್|ಆಶಿರ್|ಶಿರ್|ā-śir|āśir|śir|aa-shir|aashir|shir|_vei_120@276|_vei1_pn067
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@275">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@277">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn066">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn067">1:067</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn068">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ā-śir</b> (‘admixture’) denotes the mixing, and more especially the milk mixed with the juice, of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> before it was offered to the gods. In this sense it is not rare from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120276"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120276">1</a></sup> onwards. Not milk alone was employed for this purpose. The epithet ‘having three admixtures’ applied to <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120276"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120276">2</a></sup> is explained by the other epithets, ‘mixed with milk’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="गवाशिर्">gavāśir</a></i>), ‘mixed with curds’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दध्य्-आशिर्">dadhy-āśir</a></i>), and ‘mixed with grain’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="यवाशिर्">yavāśir</a></i>)--all referring to <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120276"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120276">↑</a> i. 134, 6; iii. 53, 14; viii. 2, 10. 11, etc.; Av. ii. 29, 1, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 8; 32.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120276"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120276">↑</a> Rv. v. 27, 5; <i>cf.</i> viii. 2, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 209 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

आशी-विष|आशीविष|आशी|आशि|विष|ఆశీ-విష|ఆశీవిష|ఆశీ|ఆశి|విష|ಆಶೀ-ವಿಷ|ಆಶೀವಿಷ|ಆಶೀ|ಆಶಿ|ವಿಷ|āśī-viṣa|āśīviṣa|āśī|āśi|viṣa|aashii-viSha|aashiiviSha|aashii|aashi|viSha|aashee-viSha|aasheeviSha|aashee|_vei_120@277|_vei1_pn067
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@276">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@278">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn066">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn067">1:067</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn068">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āśī-viṣa,</b> occurring only in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120277"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120277">1</a></sup> is understood by Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120277"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120277">2</a></sup> as designating a particular kind of snake, and perhaps means ‘having poison (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="विष">viṣa</a></i>) in its fangs’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="आशी">āśī</a></i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120277"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120277">↑</a> vi. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120277"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120277">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> It is a common word in the Epic and later.</li></ol></div>

आशु|ఆశు|ಆಶು|āśu|aashu|_vei_120@278|_vei1_pn067
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@277">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@279">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn066">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn067">1:067</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn068">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āśu,</b> the ‘swift,’ is frequently used without <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्व">aśva</a></b> to denote the chariot steed from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120278">1</a></sup> onwards. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120278">↑</a> ii. 16, 3; 31, 2; 38, 3, etc.; Av. ii. 14, 6; iv. 27, 1; xiii. 2, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 10; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 3, 3, etc.</li></ol></div>

आशुं-ग|आशुंग|आशुं|आशुङ्ग|ఆశుం-గ|ఆశుంగ|ఆశుం|ఆశుఙ్గ|ಆಶುಂ-ಗ|ಆಶುಂಗ|ಆಶುಂ|ಆಶುಙ್ಗ|āśuṃ-ga|āśuṃga|āśuṃ|aashuM-ga|aashuMga|aashuM|aashunga|_vei_120@279|_vei1_pn068
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@278">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@280">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn067">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn068">1:068</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn069">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āśuṃ-ga</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120279">1</a></sup> seems to denote some sort of animal. It is qualified by the word ‘young’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शिशुक">śiśuka</a></i>), and Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120279">2</a></sup> suggests that it may mean a bird (‘swift-flying’), or that the expression denotes ‘a foal going to its dam’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="आशु">āśu</a>-ga</i>). Sāyaṇa, however, reads the accompanying word as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शुशुक">śuśuka</a>,</i> which he assumes to denote an animal. Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120279">3</a></sup> renders the two words ‘a swift (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="आशुंग">āśuṃga</a></i>) foal (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शिशुक">śiśuka</a></i>),’ thus agreeing with one of Roth's suggestions in sense, though not in the explanation of Aśuṃga.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120279">↑</a> vi. 14, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120279">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120279">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 464.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 291.</li></ol></div>

आ-श्रम|आश्रम|श्रम|ఆ-శ్రమ|ఆశ్రమ|శ్రమ|ಆ-ಶ್ರಮ|ಆಶ್ರಮ|ಶ್ರಮ|ā-śrama|āśrama|śrama|aa-shrama|aashrama|shrama|_vei_120@280|_vei1_pn068
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@279">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@281">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn067">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn068">1:068</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn069">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ā-śrama</b> (‘resting-place’) does not occur in any <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> which can be regarded as pre-Buddhistic. Its earliest use as denoting the stages of a Hindu's life is found in the Śvetāśvatara <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120280">1</a></sup> In one passage<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120280">2</a></sup> of the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> reference is made only to the <b>Brahmacārin</b> and householder, to whom, as a reward for study, the procreation of children, the practice of <a class="vei_120a" href="योग">yoga</a>, abstention from injury to living creatures, and sacrifices, freedom from transmigration are promised. In another place<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120280">3</a></sup> three states are contemplated, but not as consecutive. The Brahmacārin may either become a householder or become an anchorite, or remain in his teacher's house all his life. Similarly, reference<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120280">4</a></sup> is made to the death of the anchorite in the forest, or the sacrifice in the village. In contrast with all three<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120280">5</a></sup> is the man who stands fast in <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> (Brahmasaṃstha). In the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120280">6</a></sup> the knower of the Ātman is contrasted with those who (1) study, or (2) sacrifice and give alms, or (3) are anchorites, and in another place<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120280">7</a></sup> with those who sacrifice and make benefactions, and those who practice asceticism. This position of superiority to, and distinction from, the Āśramas became later a fourth<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120280">8</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="आश्रम">āśrama</a>,  the <b>Gṛhastha,</b> or householder, who was in the second stage, being required to pass not only into the stage of <b>Vānaprastha,</b> but also that of the Sannyāsin (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="भिक्षु">bhikṣu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="परिव्राजक">parivrājaka</a></b>). The first stage, that of the <b>Brahmacārin,</b> was still obligatory, but was no longer allowed to remain a permanent one, as was originally possible.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Deussen, <i>Philosophy of the Upanishads,</i> 60, 367 <i>et seq.</i></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120280">↑</a> <i>Atyāśramin,</i> Śvetāśvatara <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 21; Maitrāyaṇī <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120280">↑</a> viii. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120280">↑</a> ii. 23, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120280">↑</a> v. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120280">↑</a> ii. 23, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120280">↑</a> iv. 2, 22. <i>Cf.</i> iii. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120280">↑</a> iii. 8, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120280">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="जाबाल">jābāla</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Muṇḍaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 1, 7.</li></ol></div>

आ-श्रेषा|आश्रेषा|श्रेषा|आ-श्लेषा|आश्लेषा|श्लेषा|ఆ-శ్రేషా|ఆశ్రేషా|శ్రేషా|ఆ-శ్లేషా|ఆశ్లేషా|శ్లేషా|ಆ-ಶ್ರೇಷಾ|ಆಶ್ರೇಷಾ|ಶ್ರೇಷಾ|ಆ-ಶ್ಲೇಷಾ|ಆಶ್ಲೇಷಾ|ಶ್ಲೇಷಾ|ā-śreṣā|āśreṣā|śreṣā|ā-śleṣā|āśleṣā|śleṣā|aa-shreShaa|aashreShaa|shreShaa|aa-shleShaa|aashleShaa|shleShaa|_vei_120@281|_vei1_pn069
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@280">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@282">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn068">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn069">1:069</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn070">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ā-<a class="vei_120a" href="श्रेषा">śreṣā</a>, Ā-śleṣā.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p></div>

आश्व-घ्न|आश्वघ्न|आश्व|घ्न|ఆశ్వ-ఘ్న|ఆశ్వఘ్న|ఆశ్వ|ఘ్న|ಆಶ್ವ-ಘ್ನ|ಆಶ್ವಘ್ನ|ಆಶ್ವ|ಘ್ನ|āśva-ghna|āśvaghna|āśva|ghna|aashva-ghna|aashvaghna|aashva|_vei_120@282|_vei1_pn069
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@281">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@283">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn068">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn069">1:069</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn070">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āśva-ghna.</b>--This name occurs in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120282"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120282">1</a></sup> in a very obscure hymn, where it appears to denote a prince who had made offerings to <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, and who may, as Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120282"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120282">2</a></sup> thinks, have been called <b>Vitaraṇa.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120282"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120282">↑</a> x. 61, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120282"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120282">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 165.</li></ol></div>

अश्वतर आश्वि|आश्वतराश्वि|అశ్వతర ఆశ్వి|ఆశ్వతరాశ్వి|ಅಶ್ವತರ ಆಶ್ವಿ|ಆಶ್ವತರಾಶ್ವಿ|aśvatara āśvi|āśvatarāśvi|ashvatara aashvi|aashvataraashvi|_vei_120@283|_vei1_pn069
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@282">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@284">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn068">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn069">1:069</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn070">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aśvatara Āśvi,</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आश्वतराश्वि">āśvatarāśvi</a>.</b>--These two expressions are used<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120283"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120283">1</a></sup> as patronymics of <b>Buḍila,</b> denoting, according to Sāyaṇa,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120283"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120283">2</a></sup> that he was son of <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्व">aśva</a>, and descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वतर">aśvatara</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120283"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120283">↑</a> The first is found in <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 30; the second in Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 6, 1, 9; x. 6, 1, 1; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 15, 8; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 11, 1; 16, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120283"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120283">↑</a> On <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

अश्व-त्थ्य|अश्वत्थ्य|अश्व|त्थ्य|అశ్వ-త్థ్య|అశ్వత్థ్య|అశ్వ|త్థ్య|ಅಶ್ವ-ತ್ಥ್ಯ|ಅಶ್ವತ್ಥ್ಯ|ಅಶ್ವ|ತ್ಥ್ಯ|aśva-tthya|aśvatthya|aśva|tthya|ashva-tthya|ashvatthya|ashva|_vei_120@284|_vei1_pn069
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@283">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@285">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn068">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn069">1:069</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn070">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aśva-tthya.</b>--Patronymic of Ahīnā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120284"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120284">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120284"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120284">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 10, 9, 10. Possibly to be read Āśvathya.</li></ol></div>

अश्व-मेध|अश्वमेध|अश्व|मेध|అశ్వ-మేధ|అశ్వమేధ|అశ్వ|మేధ|ಅಶ್ವ-ಮೇಧ|ಅಶ್ವಮೇಧ|ಅಶ್ವ|ಮೇಧ|aśva-medha|aśvamedha|aśva|medha|ashva-medha|ashvamedha|ashva|_vei_120@285|_vei1_pn069
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@284">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@286">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn068">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn069">1:069</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn070">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aśva-medha</b> is the patronymic of an unnamed prince, who occurs in a Dānastuti (‘Praise of Gifts’) in the Rigveda (viii. 68, 15. 16).</p></div>

आश्व-वाल|आश्ववाल|आश्व|वाल|ఆశ్వ-వాల|ఆశ్వవాల|ఆశ్వ|వాల|ಆಶ್ವ-ವಾಲ|ಆಶ್ವವಾಲ|ಆಶ್ವ|ವಾಲ|āśva-vāla|āśvavāla|āśva|vāla|aashva-vaala|aashvavaala|aashva|vaala|_vei_120@286|_vei1_pn069
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@285">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@287">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn068">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn069">1:069</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn070">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āśva-vāla.</b>--From this adjective (‘made of horse-tail grass’), applied to a strew (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रस्तर">prastara</a></i>) in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 4, 1, 17; 6, 3, 10), follows the existence of the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्ववाल">aśvavāla</a> grass (<i>Saccharum spontaneum</i>).</p></div>

आश्व-सूक्ति|आश्वसूक्ति|आश्व|सूक्ति|ఆశ్వ-సూక్తి|ఆశ్వసూక్తి|ఆశ్వ|సూక్తి|ಆಶ್ವ-ಸೂಕ್ತಿ|ಆಶ್ವಸೂಕ್ತಿ|ಆಶ್ವ|ಸೂಕ್ತಿ|āśva-sūkti|āśvasūkti|āśva|sūkti|aashva-suukti|aashvasuukti|aashva|suukti|aashva-sookti|aashvasookti|sookti|_vei_120@287|_vei1_pn069
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@286">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@288">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn068">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn069">1:069</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn070">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āśva-sūkti</b> is mentioned in <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xix. 4, 2, <i>et seq.</i>) as an author of Sāmans (<a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> chants) along with <b>Gausūkti.</b> </p></div>

आश्विन|आश्वीन|ఆశ్విన|ఆశ్వీన|ಆಶ್ವಿನ|ಆಶ್ವೀನ|āśvina|āśvīna|aashvina|aashviina|aashveena|_vei_120@288|_vei1_pn070
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@287">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@289">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn069">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn070">1:070</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn071">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āśvina,</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आश्वीन">āśvīna</a>,</b> designates, in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120288">1</a></sup> and two Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120288">2</a></sup> the length of journey made in a day by a horseman (<i>aśvin</i>). The exact distance is not defined. In the Atharva it appears to exceed five leagues, being mentioned immediately after a distance of three or five <b>Yojanas;</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> the heavenly world is placed at a distance of a thousand <b>Āśvinas.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120288">↑</a> vi. 131, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120288">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> in <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 34.</li></ol></div>

आषाधि सौश्रोमतेय|ఆషాధి సౌశ్రోమతేయ|ಆಷಾಧಿ ಸೌಶ್ರೋಮತೇಯ|āṣādhi sauśromateya|aaShaadhi saushromateya|_vei_120@289|_vei1_pn070
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@288">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@290">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn069">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn070">1:070</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn071">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āṣādhi Sauśromateya.</b>--According to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120289">1</a></sup> he was ruined because the heads were put on at the Agniciti in a certain manner, and not correctly.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120289">↑</a> vi. 2, 1, 37. Eggeling has Aṣāḍhi, but as it is a patronymic of Aṣāḍha, the form Āṣāḍhi seems correct.</li></ol></div>

आष्ट्रा|ఆష్ట్రా|ಆಷ್ಟ್ರಾ|āṣṭrā|aaShTraa|_vei_120@290|_vei1_pn070
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@289">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@291">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn069">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn070">1:070</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn071">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āṣṭrā</b> seems in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā (xxxvii. 1) to denote the ploughman's goad.</p></div>

आष्ट्री|आष्ट्रि|ఆష్ట్రీ|ఆష్ట్రి|ಆಷ್ಟ್ರೀ|ಆಷ್ಟ್ರಿ|āṣṭrī|āṣṭri|aaShTrii|aaShTri|aaShTree|_vei_120@291|_vei1_pn070
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@290">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@292">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn069">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn070">1:070</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn071">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āṣṭrī.</b>--In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120291"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120291">1</a></sup> the word seems to denote a fireplace. The evil bird is entreated not to settle there on the hearth.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120291"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120291">↑</a> x. 165, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 347.</li></ol></div>

आसंग प्लायोगि|आसङ्ग प्लायोगि|ఆసంగ ప్లాయోగి|ఆసఙ్గ ప్లాయోగి|ಆಸಂಗ ಪ್ಲಾಯೋಗಿ|ಆಸಙ್ಗ ಪ್ಲಾಯೋಗಿ|āsaṅga plāyogi|aasaMga plaayogi|aasanga plaayogi|_vei_120@292|_vei1_pn070
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@291">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@293">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn069">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn070">1:070</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn071">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āsaṅga Plāyogi</b> is a king who appears in a Dānastuti (‘Praise of Gifts’) in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120292"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120292">1</a></sup> as a generous patron. Owing, however, to the addition of a curious phallic verse to the hymn, and its early misunderstanding,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120292"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120292">2</a></sup> a legend was invented that he lost his manhood and became a woman, but by the intercession of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मेध्यातिथि">medhyātithi</a></b> was transformed into a man, much to the delight of his wife, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शश्वती">śaśvatī</a>,</b> whose existence is based on a misunderstanding of the phrase ‘every woman’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शश्वती">śaśvatī</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="नारी">nārī</a></i>) in the added verse.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120292"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120292">3</a></sup> Another misunderstanding  of the Dānastuti<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120292"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120292">4</a></sup> gives him a son <a class="vei_120a" href="स्वनद्-रथ">svanad-ratha</a>, really a mere epithet, and makes him a descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120292"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120292">↑</a> viii. 1, 32. 33.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120292"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120292">↑</a> viii. 1, 34. See Hopkins, <i>Religions of India,</i> 150, n. 1; Bṛhaddevatā, ii. 83; vi. 41, with Macdonell's notes. Dyādvi. eda gives the tale at length in Vedic words taken from the Nighaṇṭu, a curious <i>jeu d'esprit.</i> See the extract from the Nītimañjarī given by Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 40, 41.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120292"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120292">↑</a> viii. 1, 34.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120292"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120292">↑</a> viii. 1, 31. 32. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 159; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 89; Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 2, 106, 107; Oldenberg. <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 354.</li></ol></div>

आ-सन्दी|आ-सन्दि|आसन्दी|आसन्दि|सन्दी|सन्दि|ఆ-సన్దీ|ఆ-సన్ది|ఆసన్దీ|ఆసన్ది|సన్దీ|సన్ది|ಆ-ಸನ್ದೀ|ಆ-ಸನ್ದಿ|ಆಸನ್ದೀ|ಆಸನ್ದಿ|ಸನ್ದೀ|ಸನ್ದಿ|ā-sandī|ā-sandi|āsandī|āsandi|sandī|sandi|aa-sandii|aa-sandi|aasandii|aasandi|sandii|aa-sandee|aasandee|sandee|_vei_120@293|_vei1_pn071
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@292">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@294">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn070">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn071">1:071</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn072">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ā-sandī.</b>--This is a generic term for a seat of some sort, occurring frequently in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120293">1</a></sup> and Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120293">2</a></sup> but not in the Rigveda. In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120293">3</a></sup> the settle brought for the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात्य">vrātya</a></b> is described at length. It had two feet, lengths wise and cross-pieces, forward and cross-cords, showing that it was made of wood and also cording. It was also covered with a cushion (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="आस्तरण">āstaraṇa</a></b>) and a pillow (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपबर्हण">upabarhaṇa</a></b>), had a seat (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="आसाद">āsāda</a></b>) and a support (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपश्रय">upaśraya</a></b>). Similar seats are described in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120293">4</a></sup> and the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120293">5</a></sup> The seat for the king at the royal consecration is described in very similar terms in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120293">6</a></sup> where the height of the feet is placed at a span, and the lengthwise and cross-pieces are each to be a cubit, while the interwoven part (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="विवयन">vivayana</a></i>) is to be of <a class="vei_120a" href="मुञ्ज">muñja</a> grass, and the seat of <a class="vei_120a" href="उदुम्बर">udumbara</a> wood. In another passage of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120293">7</a></sup> Lanman seems to take the seat meant as a ‘long reclining chair.’ There also a cushion (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपधान">upadhāna</a></b>) and coverlet (<b>Upavāsana</b>) are mentioned. The Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> repeatedly describes the <a class="vei_120a" href="आसन्दी">āsandī</a> in terms showing that it was an elaborate seat. In one place<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120293">8</a></sup> it is said to be made of <a class="vei_120a" href="खदिर">khadira</a> wood, perforated (<i>vi-tṛṇṇā</i>), and joined with straps (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ध्र">vardhra</a>-yutā</i>) like that of the Bhāratas. At the <a class="vei_120a" href="सौत्रामणी">sautrāmaṇī</a> rite<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120293">9</a></sup> (an <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> sacrifice) the seat is of <a class="vei_120a" href="उदुम्बर">udumbara</a> wood, is knee-high, and of unlimited width and depth, and is covered with plaited reed-work. The  imperial seat<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120293">10</a></sup> is to be shoulder-high, of <a class="vei_120a" href="उदुम्बर">udumbara</a> wood, and wound all over with cords of Balvaja grass (<i>Eleusina indica</i>). Elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120293">11</a></sup> the seat is a span high, a cubit in width and depth, of <a class="vei_120a" href="उदुम्बर">udumbara</a> wood, and covered with reed-grass cords, and daubed with clay.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120293">↑</a> Av. xiv. 2, 65; xv. 3, 2 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 8, 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, viii. 56; xix. 16; 86, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120293">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 5; 6; 12; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 4, 26; v. 2, 1, 22; 4, 4, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120293">↑</a> xv. 3, 2 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120293">↑</a> i. 5. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 397; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Aranyaka,</i> 19.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120293">↑</a> ii. 24.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120293">↑</a> viii. 5; 6; 12.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120293">↑</a> xiv. 2, 65. See his note in Whitney's Translation of the Atharvaveda, 765.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120293">↑</a> v. 4, 4, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120293">↑</a> xii. 8, 3, 4 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120293">↑</a> xiv. 1, 3, 8 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120293">↑</a> vi. 7, 1, 12 <i>et seq.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 155.</li></ol></div>

आसन्दी-वन्त्|आसन्दीवन्त्|आसन्दी|आसन्दि|वन्त्|ఆసన్దీ-వన్త్|ఆసన్దీవన్త్|ఆసన్దీ|ఆసన్ది|వన్త్|ಆಸನ್ದೀ-ವನ್ತ್|ಆಸನ್ದೀವನ್ತ್|ಆಸನ್ದೀ|ಆಸನ್ದಿ|ವನ್ತ್|āsandī-vant|āsandīvant|āsandī|āsandi|vant|aasandii-vant|aasandiivant|aasandii|aasandi|aasandee-vant|aasandeevant|aasandee|_vei_120@294|_vei1_pn072
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@293">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@295">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn071">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn072">1:072</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn073">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āsandī-vant,</b> ‘possessing the throne,’ is the title of the royal city of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनमेजय">janamejaya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पारिक्षित">pārikṣita</a>,</b> in which the horse, for his famous <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, was bound. The authorities both cite a <a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a> for the fact, but they differ as to the priest who celebrated the rite. In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120294">1</a></sup> he is stated to have been <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्रोत दैवाप शौनक">indrota <a class="vei_120a" href="दैवाप">daivāpa</a> śaunaka</a>, but in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120294">2</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर कावषेय">tura kāvaṣeya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120294">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120294">↑</a> viii. 21. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 9, 1. Pāṇini, viii. 2, 12, knows the name.</li></ol></div>

आ-साद|आसाद|साद|ఆ-సాద|ఆసాద|సాద|ಆ-ಸಾದ|ಆಸಾದ|ಸಾದ|ā-sāda|āsāda|sāda|aa-saada|aasaada|saada|_vei_120@295|_vei1_pn072
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@294">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@296">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn071">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn072">1:072</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn073">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ā-sāda,</b> ‘seat,’ occurs in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120295"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120295">1</a></sup> as a description of part of the settle (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="आसन्दी">āsandī</a></b>) of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात्य">vrātya</a>.</b> It seems best to regard it with Whitney<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120295"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120295">2</a></sup> as the seat proper; Aufrecht,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120295"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120295">3</a></sup> Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120295"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120295">4</a></sup> and Roth<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120295"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120295">5</a></sup> render it as the ‘cushion for the seat,’ but that is sufficiently described by the word <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आस्तरण">āstaraṇa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120295"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120295">↑</a> xv. 3, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120295"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120295">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 771.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120295"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120295">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 131.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120295"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120295">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 155.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120295"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120295">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

आसुरायण|ఆసురాయణ|ಆಸುರಾಯಣ|āsurāyaṇa|aasuraayaNa|_vei_120@296|_vei1_pn072
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@295">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@297">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn071">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn072">1:072</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn073">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āsurāyaṇa</b> is mentioned as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रैवणि">traivaṇi</a></b> in the first two Vaṃśas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120296">1</a></sup> (lists of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> in both recensions, but as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आसुरि">āsuri</a></b> in the third <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120296">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120296">↑</a> ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120296">↑</a> vi. 5, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 434, n.</li></ol></div>

आसुरि|ఆసురి|ಆಸುರಿ|āsuri|aasuri|_vei_120@297|_vei1_pn072
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@296">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@298">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn071">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn072">1:072</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn073">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āsuri</b> occurs in the first two Vaṃśas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120297"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120297">1</a></sup> (lists of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भारद्वाज">bhāradvāja</a></b> and teacher of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="औपजन्धनि">aupajandhani</a>,</b> but in the third<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120297"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120297">2</a></sup> as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a></b> and teacher of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आसुरायण">āsurāyaṇa</a>.</b> He appears as a  ritual authority in the first four books of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120297"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120297">3</a></sup> and as an authority on dogmatic, specially noted for his insistence on truth, in the last book.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120297"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120297">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120297"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120297">↑</a> ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120297"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120297">↑</a> vi. 5, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120297"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120297">↑</a> i. 6, 3, 26; ii. 1, 4, 27; 3, 1, 9; 4, 1, 2; 6, 1, 25. 33; 3, 17; iv. 5, 8, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120297"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120297">↑</a> xiv. 1, 1, 33, and notes 1, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 430 <i>et seq.,</i> whose suggestion of the identity of this teacher and the founder of the Sāṃkhya system is not, however, acceptable. See Garbe, <i>Sāṃkhya Philosophie,</i> 29, 30.</li></ol></div>

आसुरि-वासिन्|आसुरिवासिन्|आसुरि|वासिन्|ఆసురి-వాసిన్|ఆసురివాసిన్|ఆసురి|వాసిన్|ಆಸುರಿ-ವಾಸಿನ್|ಆಸುರಿವಾಸಿನ್|ಆಸುರಿ|ವಾಸಿನ್|āsuri-vāsin|āsurivāsin|āsuri|vāsin|aasuri-vaasin|aasurivaasin|aasuri|vaasin|_vei_120@298|_vei1_pn073
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@297">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@299">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn072">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn073">1:073</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn074">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āsuri-vāsin</b> is a name of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राश्नी-पुत्र">prāśnī-putra</a></b> in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 5, 2, in both recensions).</p></div>

आ-सेचन|आसेचन|सेचन|ఆ-సేచన|ఆసేచన|సేచన|ಆ-ಸೇಚನ|ಆಸೇಚನ|ಸೇಚನ|ā-secana|āsecana|secana|aa-sechana|aasechana|sechana|_vei_120@299|_vei1_pn073
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@298">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@300">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn072">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn073">1:073</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn074">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ā-secana</b> designates a vessel to hold liquids, such as meat juice (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="यूषन्">yūṣan</a></i>)<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120299"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120299">1</a></sup> or ghee.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120299"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120299">2</a></sup> Of its shape and make we know nothing.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120299"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120299">↑</a> Rv. i. 162, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120299"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120299">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 9, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 271.</li></ol></div>

आ-स्तरण|आस्तरण|स्तरण|ఆ-స్తరణ|ఆస్తరణ|స్తరణ|ಆ-ಸ್ತರಣ|ಆಸ್ತರಣ|ಸ್ತರಣ|ā-staraṇa|āstaraṇa|staraṇa|aa-staraNa|aastaraNa|staraNa|_vei_120@300|_vei1_pn073
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@299">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@301">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn072">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn073">1:073</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn074">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ā-staraṇa</b> denotes the coverlet of the settle (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="आसन्दी">āsandī</a></b>) of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात्य">vrātya</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120300"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120300">1</a></sup> A tiger's skin serves as the coverlet of the king's seat in the royal consecration (<a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>).<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120300"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120300">2</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120300"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120300">3</a></sup> the word used is <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपस्तरण">upastaraṇa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120300"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120300">↑</a> Av. xv. 3, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120300"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120300">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120300"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120300">↑</a> i. 5. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 155.</li></ol></div>

आस्त्र-बुध्न|आस्त्रबुध्न|आस्त्र|बुध्न|ఆస్త్ర-బుధ్న|ఆస్త్రబుధ్న|ఆస్త్ర|బుధ్న|ಆಸ್ತ್ರ-ಬುಧ್ನ|ಆಸ್ತ್ರಬುಧ್ನ|ಆಸ್ತ್ರ|ಬುಧ್ನ|āstra-budhna|āstrabudhna|āstra|budhna|aastra-budhna|aastrabudhna|aastra|_vei_120@301|_vei1_pn073
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@300">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@302">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn072">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn073">1:073</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn074">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āstra-budhna</b> is the name of a man whom <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> is said in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120301"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120301">1</a></sup> to have assisted. It is not clear whether <b>Venya,</b> who is mentioned in the same line, was his friend<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120301"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120301">2</a></sup> or his enemy,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120301"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120301">3</a></sup> whom <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> saved or defeated for him.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120301"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120301">↑</a> x. 171, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120301"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120301">↑</a> So Grassmann and Griffith in their translations.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120301"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120301">↑</a> So Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 167.</li></ol></div>

आ-स्थातृ|आस्थातृ|स्थातृ|ఆ-స్థాతృ|ఆస్థాతృ|స్థాతృ|ಆ-ಸ್ಥಾತೃ|ಆಸ್ಥಾತೃ|ಸ್ಥಾತೃ|ā-sthātṛ|āsthātṛ|sthātṛ|aa-sthaatR|aasthaatR|sthaatR|aa-sthaatRi|aasthaatRi|sthaatRi|aa-sthaatRu|aasthaatRu|sthaatRu|aa-sthaatRRi|aasthaatRRi|sthaatRRi|_vei_120@302|_vei1_pn073
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@301">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@303">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn072">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn073">1:073</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn074">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ā-sthātṛ.</b>--The warrior in the chariot is once thus designated (as ‘standing on the car’) in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120302"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120302">1</a></sup> Normally he is named <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथिन्">rathin</a></b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथेष्ठा">ratheṣṭhā</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120302"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120302">↑</a> vi. 47, 26. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 296.</li></ol></div>

आ-स्राव|आस्राव|स्राव|ఆ-స్రావ|ఆస్రావ|స్రావ|ಆ-ಸ್ರಾವ|ಆಸ್ರಾವ|ಸ್ರಾವ|ā-srāva|āsrāva|srāva|aa-sraava|aasraava|sraava|_vei_120@303|_vei1_pn074
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@302">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@304">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn073">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn074">1:074</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn075">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ā-srāva</b> (‘discharge’) denotes a disease, thrice referred to in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120303">1</a></sup> the precise nature of which is uncertain. The Scholiast<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120303">2</a></sup> in one place interprets it as painful urination (<i>mūtrātisāra</i>), while Lanman<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120303">3</a></sup> suggests diabetes. Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120303">4</a></sup> takes it to be diarrhœa, and Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120303">5</a></sup> argues that as the remedy is called ‘wound healer’ (<i>arus-srāṇa</i>), the sense is ‘the flux from unhealed wounds.’ Whitney<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120303">6</a></sup> renders it ‘flux,’ and questions Bloomfield's rendering. Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120303">7</a></sup> translates it vaguely by ‘sickness’ and ‘cold.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120303">↑</a> i. 2, 4; ii. 3, 2; vi. 44, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120303">↑</a> On Av. i. 2, 4. <i>Cf.</i> on ii. 3, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120303">↑</a> In Whitney's Translation of the Atharvaveda, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120303">↑</a> <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 7, 467; <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, cxiii; <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 233, 234.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120303">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 392.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120303">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 3, 41.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120303">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 507, 509.</li></ol></div>

आहनस्या|ఆహనస్యా|ಆಹನಸ್ಯಾ|āhanasyā|aahanasyaa|_vei_120@304|_vei1_pn074
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@303">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@305">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn073">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn074">1:074</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn075">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āhanasyā</b> (‘unchaste’).--This word in the plural (‘lascivious verses’) denotes a section (xx. 136) of the Kuntāpa hymns of the Atharvaveda, which are of an obscene character.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120304"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120304">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120304"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120304">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 36; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxx. 7. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 99.</li></ol></div>

आ-हाव|आहाव|हाव|ఆ-హావ|ఆహావ|హావ|ಆ-ಹಾವ|ಆಹಾವ|ಹಾವ|ā-hāva|āhāva|hāva|aa-haava|aahaava|haava|_vei_120@305|_vei1_pn074
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@304">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@306">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn073">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn074">1:074</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn075">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ā-hāva</b> denotes a pail or bucket, especially in conjunction with a well (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अवत">avata</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120305">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120305">↑</a> Rv. i. 34, 8; vi. 7, 2; x. 101, 5; 112, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 26.</li></ol></div>

आह्नेय|ఆహ్నేయ|ಆಹ್ನೇಯ|āhneya|aahneya|_vei_120@306|_vei1_pn074
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@305">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@307">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn073">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn074">1:074</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn075">↷</a><hr><p><b>Āhneya.</b>--Patronymic of <b>Sauca</b> (<a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 12).</p></div>

इक्षु|ఇక్షు|ಇಕ್ಷು|ikṣu|ikShu|ixu|_vei_120@307|_vei1_pn074
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@306">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@308">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn073">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn074">1:074</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn075">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ikṣu,</b> the generic name for the sugar-cane, is first found in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120307"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120307">1</a></sup> and the later Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120307"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120307">2</a></sup> Whether it grew wild, or was cultivated, does not appear from the references. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120307"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120307">↑</a> i. 34, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120307"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120307">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 7, 9; iv. 2, 9 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="इक्षु">ikṣu</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्ड">kāṇḍa</a></i>). In Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxv. 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhiṭā, vii. 3, 16, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, in. 8, ‘eyelash’ is meant. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 72; Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

इक्ष्वाकु|ఇక్ష్వాకు|ಇಕ್ಷ್ವಾಕು|ikṣvāku|ikShvaaku|ixvaaku|_vei_120@308|_vei1_pn075
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@307">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@309">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn074">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn075">1:075</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn076">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ikṣvāku.</b>--In the Rigveda this name occurs but once,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120308">1</a></sup> and in a doubtful context. It is clear, however, that it denotes a prince; later interpretations make <b>Asamāti,</b> whose name is read into the hymn, an <a class="vei_120a" href="इक्ष्वाकु">ikṣvāku</a> prince.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120308">2</a></sup> In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120308">3</a></sup> also the name is found in only one passage, where it is uncertain whether a descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="इक्ष्वाकु">ikṣvāku</a>, or <a class="vei_120a" href="इक्ष्वाकु">ikṣvāku</a> himself, is referred to; in either case he seems to be regarded as an ancient hero. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120308">4</a></sup> mention is made of <b>Tryaruṇa <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रैधात्व">traidhātva</a></b> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐक्ष्वाक">aikṣvāka</a>, who is identical with the <b>Tryaruṇa <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रैवृष्ण">traivṛṣṇa</a></b> of the Bṛhaddevatā,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120308">5</a></sup> and with <b>Tryaruṇa <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a></b> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120308">6</a></sup> The connection of <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a> with the Ikṣvākus is confirmed by the fact that <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a></b> was an <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐक्ष्वाक">aikṣvāka</a>, according to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120308">7</a></sup> Thus the <a class="vei_120a" href="इक्ष्वाकु">ikṣvāku</a> line was originally a line of princes of the Pūrus. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120308">8</a></sup> places them on the upper Indus, but they may well have been somewhat further east.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120308">9</a></sup> Later <a class="vei_120a" href="इक्ष्वाकु">ikṣvāku</a> is connected with Ayodhyā</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120308">↑</a> x. 60, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120308">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 167; Sāṭyāyanaka in Max Müller, Rigveda, 4, c-cvii, 167; <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 42; Bṛhaddevatā, vii. 85 <i>et seq.,</i> with Macdonell's notes.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120308">↑</a> xiv. 39, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 680; Whitney Translation of the Atharvaveda, 961.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120308">↑</a> xiii. 3, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120308">↑</a> v. 14 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120308">↑</a> v. 27, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 68-75; Macdonell, <i>Bṛhaddevatā,</i> 2, 170; Oldenberg, <i>Vedic Hymns,</i> 366 <i>et seq.;</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 133, 138; 4, 324.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120308">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120308">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 104, 130.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120308">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 218; Geldner, <i>ibid.,</i> 3, 152.</li></ol></div>

इट|इट|ఇట_౧|ఇట|ಇಟ_೧|ಇಟ|iṭa_1|iṭa|iTa_1|iTa|_vei_120@309|_vei1_pn075
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@308">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@310">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn074">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn075">1:075</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn076">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Iṭa.</b>--This word occurs twice in the Atharvaveda. in the first passage<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120309">1</a></sup> it seems to denote a bulrush of the sort that dies in a year; in the second<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120309">2</a></sup> it refers to the reed work of the house.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120309">↑</a> vi. 14, 3. <i>Cf.</i> iv. 19, 1; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 464.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120309">↑</a> ix. 3, 18. <i>Cf.</i> Pischel, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenlāndischen Gesellschaft,</i> 35, 718.</li></ol></div>

इट|इट|ఇట_౨|ఇట|ಇಟ_೨|ಇಟ|iṭa_2|iṭa|iTa_2|iTa|_vei_120@310|_vei1_pn075
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@309">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@311">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn074">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn075">1:075</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn076">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Iṭa</b> appears as a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> and a protégé of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> in one hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120310">1</a></sup> Roth,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120310">2</a></sup> however, thinks that the word is really part of a verb (<i>iṭ</i>) meaning ‘to err, wander’ (cp. <i>aṭ</i>), and  that the name is a mere misunderstanding. It is already so regarded in the Anukramaṇī, but not apparently in the Bṛhaddevatā.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120310">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120310">↑</a> x. 171, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120310">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120310">↑</a> viii. 73. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 133.</li></ol></div>

इटन्त् काव्य|ఇటన్త్ కావ్య|ಇಟನ್ತ್ ಕಾವ್ಯ|iṭant kāvya|iTant kaavya|_vei_120@311|_vei1_pn076
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@310">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@312">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn075">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn076">1:076</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn078">↷</a><hr><p><b>Iṭant Kāvya</b> is the name of a sage, contemporary with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="केशिन् दार्भ्य">keśin dārbhya</a>,</b> in the Kauṣitaki <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120311">1</a></sup> He is also mentioned as Iḍhant in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120311">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120311">↑</a> vii. 4. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 293; 2, 308.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120311">↑</a> xiv. 9, 16.</li></ol></div>

इतिहास|ఇతిహాస|ಇತಿಹಾಸ|itihāsa|itihaasa|_vei_120@312|_vei1_pn076
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@311">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@313">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn075">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn076">1:076</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn078">↷</a><hr><p><b>Itihāsa,</b> as a kind of literature, is repeatedly mentioned along with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a></b> in the later texts of the Vedic period. The earliest reference to both occurs in the late fifteenth book of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120312">1</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a> then appears in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120312">2</a></sup> the Jaiminīya,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120312">3</a></sup> Bṛhadāraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120312">4</a></sup> and Chāndogya Upaniṣads.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120312">5</a></sup> In the latter it is expressly declared with <a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a> to make up the fifth <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>, while the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120312">6</a></sup> makes the <a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a> a <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a> a <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>. The <a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a> appear also in the Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120312">7</a></sup> while the Śatapatha<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120312">8</a></sup> identifies the <a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a> as well as the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a> with the <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>. In one passage <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अन्वाख्यान">anvākhyāna</a></b> and <a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a> are distinguished<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120312">9</a></sup> as different classes of works, but the exact point of distinction is obscure; probably the former was supplementary. The <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120312">10</a></sup> mentions Itihāsas and Purāṇas in the plural.</p><p>There is nothing to show in the older literature what distinction there was, if any, between <a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a>; and the late literature,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120312">11</a></sup> which has been elaborately examined by Sieg,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120312">12</a></sup> yields no consistent result. Geldner<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120312">13</a></sup> has conjectured that there existed a single work, the <a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a>, a collection  of the old legends of all sorts, heroic, cosmogonic, genealogical; but though a work called <a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a>, and another called <a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a>, were probably known to Patañjali,<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120312">14</a></sup> the inaccuracy of Geldner's view is proved by the fact that <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a> shows no sign of having known any such work. To him the <a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a> may be a part of the <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> literature itself,<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_120312">15</a></sup> Aitihāsikas being merely people who interpret the Rigveda by seeing in it legends where others see myths.<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_120312">16</a></sup> The fact, however, that the use of the compound form is rare, and that <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a> regularly has <a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_120312">17</a></sup> not <a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a>, is against the theory of there ever having been one work.</p><p>The relation of <a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a> to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आख्यान">ākhyāna</a></b> is also uncertain. Sieg<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_120312">18</a></sup> considers that the words <a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a> referred to the great body of mythology, legendary history, and cosmogonic legend available to the Vedic poets, and roughly classed as a fifth <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>, though not definitely and finally fixed. Thus, Anvākhyānas, <b>Anuvyākhyānas,</b> and <b>Vyākhyānas</b> could arise, and separate Ākhyānas could still exist outside the cycle, while an <a class="vei_120a" href="आख्यान">ākhyāna</a> could also be a part of the <a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a>. He also suggests that the word <a class="vei_120a" href="आख्यान">ākhyāna</a> has special reference to the form of the narrative. Oldenberg,<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_120312">19</a></sup> following Windisch,<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_120312">20</a></sup> and followed by Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_120312">21</a></sup> Sieg, and others, has found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="आख्यान">ākhyāna</a> form a mixture of prose and verse, alternating as the narrative was concerned with the mere accessory parts of the tale, or with the chief points, at which the poetic form was naturally produced to correspond with the stress of the emotion. This theory has been severely criticized by Hertel<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_120312">22</a></sup> and von Schroeder.<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_120312">23</a></sup> These scholars, in accordance with older suggestions of Max Müller<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_120312">24</a></sup> and Lévi,<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_120312">25</a></sup> see in the  so-called <a class="vei_120a" href="आख्यान">ākhyāna</a> hymns of the Rigveda, in which Oldenberg finds actual specimens of the supposed literary genus, though the prose has been lost, actual remains of ritual dramas. Elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_120312">26</a></sup> it has been suggested that the hymns in question are merely literary dialogues.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120312">↑</a> xv. 6, 4 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120312">↑</a> xiii. 4, 3, 12. 13, and as compounded in xi. 5, 6, 8; 7, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120312">↑</a> i. 53.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120312">↑</a> ii. 4, 10; iv. 1, 2; v. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120312">↑</a> iii. 4, 1. 2; vii. 1, 2. 4; 2, 1; 7, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120312">↑</a> xvi. 2, 21. 27.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120312">↑</a> i. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120312">↑</a> xiii. 4, 3, 12. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120312">↑</a> xi. 1, 6, 9. <i>Cf.</i> p. 24.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120312">↑</a> ii. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120312">↑</a> See Sāyaṇa's Introduction to the Rigveda, p. 12 (ed. Max Müller), and his commentary on Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 6, 8; Śaṃkara on Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 4, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120312">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 31 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120312">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 290. <i>Cf.</i> Sieg, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 33.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120312">↑</a> Vārttika on Pāṇini, iv. 2, 60, and Mahābhāṣya (ed. Kielhorn), 2, 284.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_120312">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iv. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_120312">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> ii. 16; xii. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_120312">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> ii. 10; 24; iv. 6; x. 26; xii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_120312">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 31 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_120312">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 37, 54 <i>et seq.;</i> 39, 52 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> also <i>Go7ttingische Gelehrte Anzeigen,</i> 1908, 67 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_120312">↑</a> <i>Verhandlungen der dreiunddreissigsten Versammlung deutcher Philologen und Sckulmänner in Gera</i> (1879), 15 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_120312">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 284; 2, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_120312">↑</a> <i>Vienna Oriental Journal,</i> 18, 59 <i>et seq.;</i> 23, 273 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> Winternitz, <i>ibid.,</i> 23, 102 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_120312">↑</a> <i>Mystcrium und Mimus im Rigveda,</i> 3 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_120312">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 183.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_120312">↑</a> <i>Le Théâtre indien,</i> 303, 307.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_120312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_120312">↑</a> Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1909, 200 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

इद्-|इद्|इदा-|इदा|इदा|इदु-वत्सर|इदुवत्सर|इदु|वत्सर|ఇద్-|ఇద్|ఇద్|ఇదా-|ఇదా|ఇదా|ఇదు-వత్సర|ఇదువత్సర|ఇదు|వత్సర|ಇದ್-|ಇದ್|ಇದ್|ಇದಾ-|ಇದಾ|ಇದಾ|ಇದು-ವತ್ಸರ|ಇದುವತ್ಸರ|ಇದು|ವತ್ಸರ|id-|id|id|idā-|idā|idu-vatsara|iduvatsara|idu|vatsara|id|idaa-|idaa|idu|vatsara|_vei_120@313|_vei1_pn078
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@312">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@314">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn076">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn078">1:078</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn079">↷</a><hr><p><b>Id-, <a class="vei_120a" href="इदा-">idā-</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="इदु">idu</a>- Vatsara.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="संवत्सर">saṃvatsara</a>.</b></p></div>

इन्द्र-गोप|इन्द्रगोप|इन्द्र|गोप|ఇన్ద్ర-గోప|ఇన్ద్రగోప|ఇన్ద్ర|గోప|ಇನ್ದ್ರ-ಗೋಪ|ಇನ್ದ್ರಗೋಪ|ಇನ್ದ್ರ|ಗೋಪ|indra-gopa|indragopa|indra|gopa|_vei_120@314|_vei1_pn078
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@313">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@315">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn076">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn078">1:078</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn079">↷</a><hr><p><b>Indra-gopa</b> (‘protected by Indra’), masc., is a designation of the cochineal insect in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (ii. 3, 6).</p></div>

इन्द्र-द्युम्न भाल्लवेय वैयाघ्र-पद्य|इन्द्र-द्युम्न भाल्लवेय वैयाघ्रपद्य|इन्द्र-द्युम्न भाल्लवेय वैयाघ्र|पद्य|ఇన్ద్ర-ద్యుమ్న భాల్లవేయ వైయాఘ్ర-పద్య|ఇన్ద్ర-ద్యుమ్న భాల్లవేయ వైయాఘ్రపద్య|ఇన్ద్ర-ద్యుమ్న భాల్లవేయ వైయాఘ్ర|పద్య|ಇನ್ದ್ರ-ದ್ಯುಮ್ನ ಭಾಲ್ಲವೇಯ ವೈಯಾಘ್ರ-ಪದ್ಯ|ಇನ್ದ್ರ-ದ್ಯುಮ್ನ ಭಾಲ್ಲವೇಯ ವೈಯಾಘ್ರಪದ್ಯ|ಇನ್ದ್ರ-ದ್ಯುಮ್ನ ಭಾಲ್ಲವೇಯ ವೈಯಾಘ್ರ|ಪದ್ಯ|indra-dyumna bhāllaveya vaiyāghra-padya|indra-dyumna bhāllaveya vaiyāghrapadya|indra-dyumna bhāllaveya vaiyāghra|padya|indra-dyumna bhaallaveya vaiyaaghra-padya|indra-dyumna bhaallaveya vaiyaaghrapadya|indra-dyumna bhaallaveya vaiyaaghra|_vei_120@315|_vei1_pn078
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@314">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@316">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn076">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn078">1:078</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn079">↷</a><hr><p><b>Indra-<a class="vei_120a" href="द्युम्न">dyumna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="भाल्लवेय">bhāllaveya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वैयाघ्र">vaiyāghra</a>-padya</b> is mentioned as a teacher who with others was unable to agree as to the nature of <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> Vaiśvānara, and who was instructed by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वपति">aśvapati</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कैकेय">kaikeya</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120315">1</a></sup> As <a class="vei_120a" href="भाल्लवेय">bhāllaveya</a> he is cited several times in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120315">2</a></sup> on ritual points.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120315">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 6, 1, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 11, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120315">↑</a> i. 6, 1, 19; xiii. 5, 3, 4. <i>Cf.</i> ii. 1, 4. 6.</li></ol></div>

इन्द्र-भू काश्यप|इन्द्रभू काश्यप|इन्द्र|भू काश्यप|ఇన్ద్ర-భూ కాశ్యప|ఇన్ద్రభూ కాశ్యప|ఇన్ద్ర|భూ కాశ్యప|ಇನ್ದ್ರ-ಭೂ ಕಾಶ್ಯಪ|ಇನ್ದ್ರಭೂ ಕಾಶ್ಯಪ|ಇನ್ದ್ರ|ಭೂ ಕಾಶ್ಯಪ|indra-bhū kāśyapa|indrabhū kāśyapa|indra|bhū kāśyapa|indra-bhuu kaashyapa|indrabhuu kaashyapa|bhuu kaashyapa|indra-bhoo kaashyapa|indrabhoo kaashyapa|bhoo kaashyapa|_vei_120@316|_vei1_pn078
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@315">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@317">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn076">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn078">1:078</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn079">↷</a><hr><p><b>Indra-<a class="vei_120a" href="भू">bhū</a> Kāśyapa</b> is mentioned as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मित्रभू काश्यप">mitrabhū kāśyapa</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120316">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120316">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 374.</li></ol></div>

इन्द्रोत|इन्द्रोत|ఇన్ద్రోత_౧|ఇన్ద్రోత|ಇನ್ದ್ರೋತ_೧|ಇನ್ದ್ರೋತ|indrota_1|indrota|_vei_120@317|_vei1_pn078
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@316">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@318">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn076">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn078">1:078</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn079">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Indrota</b> is twice mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120317">1</a></sup> in a Dānastuti (‘Praise of Liberality’) as a giver of gifts. In the second passage he has the epithet <a class="vei_120a" href="आतिथिग्व">ātithigva</a>, which shows conclusively that he was a son of <a class="vei_120a" href="अतिथिग्व">atithigva</a>, as Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120317">2</a></sup> holds, and not of <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋक्ष">ṛkṣa</a>, as Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120317">3</a></sup> states.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120317">↑</a> viii. 68, 10 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120317">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 163.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120317">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

इन्द्रोत दैवाप शौनक|इन्द्रोत दैवाप शौनक|ఇన్ద్రోత దైవాప శౌనక_౨|ఇన్ద్రోత దైవాప శౌనక|ಇನ್ದ್ರೋತ ದೈವಾಪ ಶೌನಕ_೨|ಇನ್ದ್ರೋತ ದೈವಾಪ ಶೌನಕ|indrota daivāpa śaunaka_2|indrota daivāpa śaunaka|indrota daivaapa shaunaka_2|indrota daivaapa shaunaka|_vei_120@318|_vei1_pn078
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@317">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@319">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn076">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn078">1:078</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn079">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Indrota <a class="vei_120a" href="दैवाप">daivāpa</a> Śaunaka</b> is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120318">1</a></sup> as the priest who officiated at the horse  sacrifice of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनमेजय">janamejaya</a>,</b> although this honour is attributed in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120318">2</a></sup> to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर कावषेय">tura kāvaṣeya</a>.</b> He also appears in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120318">3</a></sup> as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्रुत">śruta</a>,</b> and is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120318">4</a></sup> He cannot be connected in any way with <b>Devāpi,</b> who occurs in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120318">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120318">↑</a> xiii. 5, 3, 5; 4, 1; Sāṅkhāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 7, 7; 8, 27.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120318">↑</a> viii. 21.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120318">↑</a> iii. 40, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120318">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 384, 385.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120318">↑</a> x. 98. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenlāndischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 240.</li></ol></div>

इभ|इभ|ఇభ_౧|ఇభ|ಇಭ_೧|ಇಭ|ibha_1|ibha|_vei_120@319|_vei1_pn079
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@318">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@320">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn078">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn079">1:079</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn080">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Ibha</b> is a word of somewhat doubtful sense and interpretation. It is found only in the Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120319">1</a></sup> and especially in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120319">2</a></sup> According to Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120319">3</a></sup> and Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120319">4</a></sup> the sense is ‘retainer,’ and Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120319">5</a></sup> thinks that it includes not only dependants and servants, but also the royal family and the youthful cadets of the chief families. In the opinion of Pischel and Geldner<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120319">6</a></sup> it denotes ‘elephant.’ This view is supported by the authority of the commentators Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120319">7</a></sup> and Mahīdhara;<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120319">8</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120319">9</a></sup> too, gives ‘elephant’ as one of the senses of the word. Megasthenes<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120319">10</a></sup> and Nearchos<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120319">11</a></sup> tell us that elephants were a royal prerogative, and the derivative word <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इभ्य">ibhya</a></b> may thus be naturally explained as denoting merely ‘rich’ (lit., ‘possessor of elephants’).<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120319">12</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120319">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 2, 14, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiii. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120319">↑</a> i. 84, 17; iv. 4, 1; ix. 57, 3, and perhaps vi. 20, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120319">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120319">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 246, 247.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120319">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 167.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120319">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, xv, xvi.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120319">↑</a> On <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120319">↑</a> On Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120319">↑</a> vi. 12. It gives also the sense ‘retainer,’ and in Aśoka's Edicts, No. 5, Bühler, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 37, 279, finds a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> denoted by its Pāli form.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120319">↑</a> <i>Apud</i> Strabo, 704.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120319">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 705.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120319">↑</a> But it may equally well be explained as ‘rich’ from the other sense of the primary word: ‘possessor of (many) retainers.’</li></ol></div>

इभ|इभ|ఇభ_౨|ఇభ|ಇಭ_೨|ಇಭ|ibha_2|ibha|_vei_120@320|_vei1_pn079
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@319">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@321">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn078">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn079">1:079</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn080">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Ibha</b> in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120320"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120320">1</a></sup> appears certainly to be intended as an abbreviation of the proper name <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्मदिभ">smadibha</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120320"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120320">↑</a> vi. 20, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Pischel and Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, xvi; Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 380. Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 167, treats it as ‘retinue,’ as does Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 246, 247.</li></ol></div>

इभ्य|ఇభ్య|ಇಭ್ಯ|ibhya|_vei_120@321|_vei1_pn080
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@320">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@322">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn079">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn080">1:080</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn081">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ibhya</b> occurs once in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120321">1</a></sup> in the plural, when a king is said to devour his Ibhyas as the fire the forest; and twice in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120321">2</a></sup> in one passage as the first member of a compound, and in the other as either a proper name or an adjective. Roth,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120321">3</a></sup> Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120321">4</a></sup> and Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120321">5</a></sup> interpret the word as ‘retainers’ in the Rigveda, but in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> Roth thinks it means ‘rich.’ Pischel and Geldner<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120321">6</a></sup> accept the sense in all passages. Bo7htlingk in his translation of the Chāndogya treats the word as simply a proper name, ‘Ibhya's village’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="इभ्य">ibhya</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a></i>) and ‘Ibhya.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120321">↑</a> i. 65, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120321">↑</a> i. 10, 1. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120321">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120321">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda 3, 247.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120321">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 168.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120321">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, xvi. <i>Cf.</i> Sāyaṇa on Rigveda, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>., dhaninaḥ,</i> and Śaṃkara on Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>., īśvaro hastyāroho vā;</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 476. <i>Cf.</i> also Little, <i>Grammatical Index,</i> 35.</li></ol></div>

इरिण|ఇరిణ|ಇರಿಣ|iriṇa|iriNa|_vei_120@322|_vei1_pn080
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@321">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@323">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn079">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn080">1:080</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn081">↷</a><hr><p><b>Iriṇa</b> (neut.) occurs not rarely in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120322">1</a></sup> and Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120322">2</a></sup> in the sense of ‘a cleft in the ground,’ usually natural (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्व">sva</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कृत">kṛta</a></i>). The same meaning is also, as Pischel<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120322">3</a></sup> shows, to be ascribed to it in three passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120322">4</a></sup> in one<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120322">5</a></sup> of which the hole is referred to as ‘made by water’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अपा">apā</a> kṛtam</i>). In another passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120322">6</a></sup> the word refers to the place on which the dice are thrown. Hence Pischel<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120322">7</a></sup> concludes that the dicing-board must have been so called because it contained holes into which the dice had to be thrown if possible. Lüders,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120322">8</a></sup> however, points out that this assumption is not necessary; the dice (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a></b>) were merely thrown on a space dug out, which could be called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इरिण">iriṇa</a>,</b> as being a hole in the ground, though not a natural one. This view is supported by the commentary of Sāyaṇa,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120322">9</a></sup> as well as by <a class="vei_120a" href="दुर्ग">durga</a> in his note on the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120322">10</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120322">↑</a> Av. iv. 15, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 1, 3; iii. 4, 8, 5; v. 2, 4, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, ix. 16.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120322">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 2, 3, 2; vii. 2, 1, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120322">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 222-225.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120322">↑</a> i. 186, 9; viii. 4, 3; 87, 1. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120322">↑</a> viii. 4, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120322">↑</a> x. 34, 1. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120322">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 225.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120322">↑</a> <i>Das Würfelspiel im alben Indian,</i> 14.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120322">↑</a> On Rv., <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>. (ā-sphūra).</i></li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120322">↑</a> ix. 8 (<i>āsphuraka-sthāna</i>).</li></ol></div>

इष श्यावाश्वि|ఇష శ్యావాశ్వి|ಇಷ ಶ್ಯಾವಾಶ್ವಿ|iṣa śyāvāśvi|iSha shyaavaashvi|_vei_120@323|_vei1_pn081
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@322">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@324">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn080">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn081">1:081</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn082">↷</a><hr><p><b>Iṣa Śyāvāśvi</b> is mentioned in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (‘list of teachers’) of the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iv. 16, 1) as a pupil of <a class="vei_120a" href="अगस्त्य">agastya</a>.</p></div>

इषीका|ఇషీకా|ಇಷೀಕಾ|iṣīkā|iShiikaa|iSheekaa|_vei_120@324|_vei1_pn081
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@323">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@325">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn080">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn081">1:081</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn082">↷</a><hr><p><b>Iṣīkā,</b> ‘a stalk of reed grass,’ occurs frequently from the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120324">1</a></sup> onwards, often as an emblem of fragility. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120324">2</a></sup> it seems to denote the pin fixed in the bar of a pen to keep cattle in (<i>argaleṣīke,</i> ‘bolt and pin’). A basket (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शूर्प">śūrpa</a></i>) of <a class="vei_120a" href="इषीका">iṣīkā</a> is referred to in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120324">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120324">↑</a> Av. vii. 56, 4; xii. 2, 54; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 3, 4, 16, etc.; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 9; ii. 134; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 24, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 6, 17, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ix. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120324">↑</a> ii. 16 (<i>v. l. ºike</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120324">↑</a> i. 1, 4, 19.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 71; Oertel, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 19, 122, n. 3.</li></ol></div>

इषु|ఇషు|ಇಷು|iṣu|iShu|_vei_120@325|_vei1_pn081
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@324">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@326">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn080">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn081">1:081</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn082">↷</a><hr><p><b>Iṣu</b> is the usual name for ‘arrow’ from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120325">1</a></sup> onwards. Other names are <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शर्य">śarya</a>, Śārī,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बाण">bāṇa</a>.</b> In the hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120325">2</a></sup> which gives a catalogue of armour, two kinds of arrows are distinctly referred to: the one is poisoned (<i>ālāktā</i>), and has a head of horn (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="रुरु">ruru</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="शीर्ष्णी">śīrṣṇī</a></i>); the other is copper-, bronze-, or iron-headed (<i>ayo-mukham</i>). Poisoned (<i>digdhā</i>) arrows are also referred to in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120325">3</a></sup> The arrows were feathered.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120325">4</a></sup> The parts of an arrow are enumerated in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120325">5</a></sup> as the shaft (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शल्य">śalya</a></i>), the feather-socket (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्ण-धि">parṇa-dhi</a></i>), the point (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शृंग">śṛṅga</a></i>), the neck of the point in which the shaft is fixed (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कुल्मल">kulmala</a></i>), and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अपस्कम्भ">apaskambha</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अपाष्ठ">apāṣṭha</a>,</b> which are of more doubtful significance. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120325">6</a></sup> the parts of an arrow are given as the point (<i>anīka</i>), the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शल्य">śalya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="तेजन">tejana</a>,</i> and the feathers (<i>parṇāni</i>), where <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शल्य">śalya</a></i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="तेजन">tejana</a></i> must apparently mean the upper and lower parts of the shaft, since it is reasonable to suppose that the arrow is described as a whole consecutively. So in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120325">7</a></sup> the arrow of <a class="vei_120a" href="काम">kāma</a> is described as having feathers, a shaft  (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शल्य">śalya</a></i>), and a firm fastening (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कुल्मल">kulmala</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120325">8</a></sup> The arrow was shot from the ear, and so is described in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120325">9</a></sup> as ‘having the ear for its place of birth.’</p><p>As a measure of length, the <a class="vei_120a" href="इषु">iṣu</a> was five spans, say three feet.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120325">10</a></sup> A regular profession of arrow-making existed (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="इषु">iṣu</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्">kṛt</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120325">11</a></sup> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="इषु">iṣu</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कार">kāra</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120325">12</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120325">↑</a> ii. 24, 8; viii. 7, 4, etc.; Av. i. 13, 4, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 3, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ix. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120325">↑</a> vi. 75, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120325">↑</a> iv. 6, 7; v. 18, 8. 15; 31, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120325">↑</a> Rv. x. 18, 14; vi. 75, 11; Av. v. 25, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120325">↑</a> iv. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120325">↑</a> i. 25.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120325">↑</a> Av. iii. 25, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120325">↑</a> See also Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 8, 1. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxv. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120325">↑</a> Rv. vi. 75, 3; ii. 24, 8 (<i>karṇayoni</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120325">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 5, 2, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120325">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 46, <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 184, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120325">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> xxx. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 3, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 300; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 29, 286; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 275 <i>et seq.;</i> 25, 337.</li></ol></div>

इषु त्रि-काण्डा|इषु त्रिकाण्डा|इषु त्रि|काण्डा|ఇషు త్రి-కాణ్డా|ఇషు త్రికాణ్డా|ఇషు త్రి|కాణ్డా|ಇಷು ತ್ರಿ-ಕಾಣ್ಡಾ|ಇಷು ತ್ರಿಕಾಣ್ಡಾ|ಇಷು ತ್ರಿ|ಕಾಣ್ಡಾ|iṣu tri-kāṇḍā|iṣu trikāṇḍā|iṣu tri|kāṇḍā|iShu tri-kaaNDaa|iShu trikaaNDaa|iShu tri|kaaNDaa|_vei_120@326|_vei1_pn082
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@325">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@327">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn081">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn082">1:082</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn083">↷</a><hr><p><b>Iṣu <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रि">tri</a>-kāṇḍā</b> is the name given in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120326"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120326">1</a></sup> to some constellation, perhaps Orion's girdle. It is mentioned with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मृग">mṛga</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मृगव्याध">mṛgavyādha</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रोहिणी">rohiṇī</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120326"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120326">↑</a> iii. 33. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 205, n.</li></ol></div>

इषु-धि|इषुधि|इषु|धि|ఇషు-ధి|ఇషుధి|ఇషు|ధి|ಇಷು-ಧಿ|ಇಷುಧಿ|ಇಷು|ಧಿ|iṣu-dhi|iṣudhi|iṣu|dhi|iShu-dhi|iShudhi|iShu|_vei_120@327|_vei1_pn082
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@326">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@328">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn081">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn082">1:082</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn083">↷</a><hr><p><b>Iṣu-dhi</b> (‘arrow-holder’) is the name of the quiver which was carried by every bowman. The word is common from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120327">1</a></sup> onwards. No trace is to be found in Vedic literature of the later practice of carrying two quivers.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120327">2</a></sup> According to Pischel,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120327">3</a></sup> the curious expression <i><a class="vei_120a" href="इषु">iṣu</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्">kṛt</a></i> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120327">4</a></sup> means quiver.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120327">↑</a> i. 33, 3; vi. 75, 5; x. 95, 3; Av. ii. 33, 2; iv. 10, 6, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ix. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120327">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 274. Each quiver held from ten to twenty arrows.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120327">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 17. But see Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 182.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120327">↑</a> i. 184, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 300.</li></ol></div>

ईषा|ఈషా|ಈಷಾ|īṣā|iiShaa|eeShaa|_vei_120@328|_vei1_pn082
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@327">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@329">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn081">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn082">1:082</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn083">↷</a><hr><p><b>Īṣā</b> denotes the ‘pole of a chariot.’ Normally the chariot had one pole (<i>ekeṣaḥ</i>),<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120328">1</a></sup> but sometimes two poles are referred to.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120328">2</a></sup> The word is often<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120328">3</a></sup> compounded with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="युग">yuga</a>,</b> ‘yoke,’ into which it was fastened (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ख">kha</a></b>), and tied with ropes.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120328">4</a></sup> Exactly how it was attached to the chariot we do not know.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120328">5</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120328">↑</a> Rv. x. 135, 3; iii. 53, 17; viii. 5, 29; Av. viii. 8, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120328">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Av. ii. 8, 4; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 2, 12 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ईषा">īṣā</a>-yugāni,</i> but in iii. 9, 4, 3 dual); Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, vii. 9, 14, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120328">↑</a> Av., <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120328">↑</a> Rv. x. 60, 8. <i>Cf.</i> iii. 6, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120328">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वाणी">vāṇī</a></i> in Rv. i. 119, 5 is taken by Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 249, to refer to the front part of the chariot, but it seems simply to be ‘voice.’</li></ol></div>

उक्षन्|ఉక్షన్|ಉಕ್ಷನ್|ukṣan|ukShan|uxan|_vei_120@329|_vei1_pn083
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@328">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@330">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn082">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn083">1:083</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn084">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ukṣan.</b> See <b>Go.</b></p></div>

उक्षण्यायन|ఉక్షణ్యాయన|ಉಕ್ಷಣ್ಯಾಯನ|ukṣaṇyāyana|ukShaNyaayana|uxaNyaayana|_vei_120@330|_vei1_pn083
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@329">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@331">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn082">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn083">1:083</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn084">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ukṣaṇyāyana</b> is mentioned in a Dānastuti (‘Praise of Gifts’) in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120330">1</a></sup> along with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हरयाण">harayāṇa</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुषामन्">suṣāman</a>.</b> Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120330">2</a></sup> thinks that all three are identical. Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120330">3</a></sup> finds a reference to <a class="vei_120a" href="उक्षन्">ukṣan</a> himself in the verb <i>ukṣaṇyati</i><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120330">4</a></sup> and in the adjective <i>ukṣaṇyu.</i><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120330">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120330">↑</a> viii. 25, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120330">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 162, 270.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120330">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120330">↑</a> Rv. viii. 26, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120330">↑</a> Rv. viii. 23, 16.</li></ol></div>

उक्ष्णो-रन्ध्र काव्य|उक्ष्णोरन्ध्र काव्य|उक्ष्णो|रन्ध्र काव्य|ఉక్ష్ణో-రన్ధ్ర కావ్య|ఉక్ష్ణోరన్ధ్ర కావ్య|ఉక్ష్ణో|రన్ధ్ర కావ్య|ಉಕ್ಷ್ಣೋ-ರನ್ಧ್ರ ಕಾವ್ಯ|ಉಕ್ಷ್ಣೋರನ್ಧ್ರ ಕಾವ್ಯ|ಉಕ್ಷ್ಣೋ|ರನ್ಧ್ರ ಕಾವ್ಯ|ukṣṇo-randhra kāvya|ukṣṇorandhra kāvya|ukṣṇo|randhra kāvya|ukShNo-randhra kaavya|ukShNorandhra kaavya|ukShNo|randhra kaavya|uxNo-randhra kaavya|uxNorandhra kaavya|uxNo|_vei_120@331|_vei1_pn083
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@330">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@332">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn082">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn083">1:083</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn084">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ukṣṇo-<a class="vei_120a" href="रन्ध्र">randhra</a> Kāvya</b> is mentioned as a seer in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 9, 19).<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120331"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120331">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120331"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120331">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 397.</li></ol></div>

उखा|ఉఖా|ಉಖಾ|ukhā|ukhaa|_vei_120@332|_vei1_pn083
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@331">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@333">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn082">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn083">1:083</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn084">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ukhā</b> is the regular word or a ‘cooking pot,’ usually mentioned in connexion with sacrifice, from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120332"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120332">1</a></sup> onwards. It was made of clay (<i>mṛṇ-mayī</i>).<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120332"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120332">2</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्थाली">sthālī</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120332"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120332">↑</a> i. 162, 13. 15; iii. 53, 22; Av. xii. 3, 23; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 1, 6, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120332"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120332">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xi. 59; Taitdrīya Saṃhitā, iv. 1, 5, 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 253, 271.</li></ol></div>

उग्र|ఉగ్ర|ಉಗ್ರ|ugra|_vei_120@333|_vei1_pn083
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@332">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@334">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn082">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn083">1:083</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn084">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ugra</b> in one passage of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120333"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120333">1</a></sup> seems to have a technical force, denoting ‘man in authority,’ or according to Max Müller's rendering, ‘policeman.’ Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120333"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120333">2</a></sup> compares a passage in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120333"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120333">3</a></sup> where, however, the word has simply the general sense of ‘mighty man.’ Bo7htlingk,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120333"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120333">4</a></sup> in his rendering of the <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, treats the word as merely adjectival.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120333"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120333">↑</a> iv. 3, 37. 38.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120333"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120333">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120333"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120333">↑</a> vii. 38, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120333"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120333">↑</a> P. 66 (with <i>pratyenasaḥ</i>).</li></ol></div>

उग्र-देव|उग्रदेव|उग्र|देव|ఉగ్ర-దేవ|ఉగ్రదేవ|ఉగ్ర|దేవ|ಉಗ್ರ-ದೇವ|ಉಗ್ರದೇವ|ಉಗ್ರ|ದೇವ|ugra-deva|ugradeva|ugra|deva|_vei_120@334|_vei1_pn083
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@333">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@335">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn082">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn083">1:083</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn084">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ugra-deva</b> is mentioned with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a></b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120334"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120334">1</a></sup> apparently as a powerful protector. The name occurs also in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120334"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120334">2</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120334"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120334">3</a></sup> where he is styled <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजनि">rājani</a></b> and called a leper (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="किलास">kilāsa</a></i>). </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120334"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120334">↑</a> i. 36, 18 (Ugrādeva).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120334"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120334">↑</a> xiv. 3, 17; xxiii. 16, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120334"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120334">↑</a> v. 4, 12.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 147; Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> who suggests that in the Rv. passage the word should be taken adjectivally.</li></ol></div>

उग्र-सेन|उग्रसेन|उग्र|सेन|ఉగ్ర-సేన|ఉగ్రసేన|ఉగ్ర|సేన|ಉಗ್ರ-ಸೇನ|ಉಗ್ರಸೇನ|ಉಗ್ರ|ಸೇನ|ugra-sena|ugrasena|ugra|sena|_vei_120@335|_vei1_pn084
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@334">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@336">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn083">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn084">1:084</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn085">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ugra-sena</b> is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 5, 4, 3), and in a <a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a> there cited as being, with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भीमसेन">bhīmasena</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्रुतसेन">śrutasena</a>,</b> a Pārikṣitīya and a brother of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनमेजय">janamejaya</a>.</b> The brothers were cleansed by the horse sacrifice from sin.</p></div>

उच्चैः-श्रवस् कौपयेय|उच्चैःश्रवस् कौपयेय|उच्चैः|श्रवस् कौपयेय|ఉచ్చైః-శ్రవస్ కౌపయేయ|ఉచ్చైఃశ్రవస్ కౌపయేయ|ఉచ్చైః|శ్రవస్ కౌపయేయ|ಉಚ್ಚೈಃ-ಶ್ರವಸ್ ಕೌಪಯೇಯ|ಉಚ್ಚೈಃಶ್ರವಸ್ ಕೌಪಯೇಯ|ಉಚ್ಚೈಃ|ಶ್ರವಸ್ ಕೌಪಯೇಯ|uccaiḥ-śravas kaupayeya|uccaiḥśravas kaupayeya|uccaiḥ|śravas kaupayeya|uchchaiH-shravas kaupayeya|uchchaiHshravas kaupayeya|uchchaiH|shravas kaupayeya|_vei_120@336|_vei1_pn084
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@335">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@337">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn083">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn084">1:084</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn085">↷</a><hr><p><b>Uccaiḥ-<a class="vei_120a" href="श्रवस्">śravas</a> Kaupayeya</b> appears in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 29, 1-3) as a king of the <b>Kurus</b> and as maternal uncle of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="केशिन्">keśin</a>.</b> His connexion with the Kurus is borne out by the fact that <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपमश्रवस्">upamaśravas</a></b> was son of <b>Kuruśravaṇa,</b> the names being strikingly similar.</p></div>

उच्-छीर्षक|उच्छीर्षक|उच्|छीर्षक|ఉచ్-ఛీర్షక|ఉచ్ఛీర్షక|ఉచ్|ఛీర్షక|ಉಚ್-ಛೀರ್ಷಕ|ಉಚ್ಛೀರ್ಷಕ|ಉಚ್|ಛೀರ್ಷಕ|uc-chīrṣaka|ucchīrṣaka|uc|chīrṣaka|uch-ChiirShaka|uchChiirShaka|uch|ChiirShaka|uch-CheerShaka|uchCheerShaka|CheerShaka|_vei_120@337|_vei1_pn084
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@336">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@338">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn083">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn084">1:084</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn085">↷</a><hr><p><b>Uc-chīrṣaka.</b>--This word, occurring in the description of the couch (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्यंक">paryaṅka</a></i>) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120337"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120337">1</a></sup> (i. 5), apparently denotes a cushion for the head. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आसन्दी">āsandī</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120337"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120337">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 403; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 155.</li></ol></div>

उत्तर कुरु|ఉత్తర కురు|ಉತ್ತರ ಕುರು|uttara kuru|_vei_120@338|_vei1_pn084
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@337">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@339">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn083">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn084">1:084</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn085">↷</a><hr><p><b>Uttara Kuru.</b>--The Uttara Kurus, who play a mythical part in the Epic and later literature, are still a historical people in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120338"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120338">1</a></sup> where they are located beyond the Himālaya (<i>pareṇa Himavantam</i>). In another passage,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120338"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120338">2</a></sup> however, the country of the Uttara Kurus is stated by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वासिष्ठ">vāsiṣṭha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सात्यहव्य">sātyahavya</a></b> to be a land of the gods (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="देव">deva</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="क्षेत्र">kṣetra</a></i>), but <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जानंतपि">jānaṃtapi</a> Atyarāti</b> was anxious to conquer it, so that it is still not wholly mythical. It is reasonable to accept Zimmer's view that the northern Kurus were settled in Kaśmīr, especially as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a></b> is the region where tribes advancing from Kaśmīr might naturally be found. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उदीच्यस्">udīcyas</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120338"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120338">↑</a> viii. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120338"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120338">↑</a> viii. 23.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 165; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 101, 102; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 75, n.</li></ol></div>

उत्तर मद्र|ఉత్తర మద్ర|ಉತ್ತರ ಮದ್ರ|uttara madra|_vei_120@339|_vei1_pn084
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@338">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@340">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn083">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn084">1:084</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn085">↷</a><hr><p><b>Uttara Madra</b> is the name of a tribe mentioned with the <b>Uttara Kurus</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120339"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120339">1</a></sup> as living beyond the Himālaya. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120339"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120339">2</a></sup> points out that in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120339"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120339">3</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काम्बोज">kāmboja</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="औपमन्यव">aupamanyava</a></b> is a pupil of <b>Madragāra,</b> and thence  infers that Kambojas and Madras were not far distant in space. This conclusion is perfectly reasonable in view of the probable position of the Kāmbojas.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120339"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120339">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120339"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120339">↑</a> viii. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120339"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120339">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 102.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120339"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120339">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 371.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120339"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120339">↑</a> See map in Pargiter, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, p. 332. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 165.</li></ol></div>

उत्-तान आंगिरस|उत्तान आंगिरस|उत्|तान आंगिरस|उत्-तान आङ्गिरस|उत्तान आङ्गिरस|तान आङ्गिरस|ఉత్-తాన ఆంగిరస|ఉత్తాన ఆంగిరస|ఉత్|తాన ఆంగిరస|ఉత్-తాన ఆఙ్గిరస|ఉత్తాన ఆఙ్గిరస|తాన ఆఙ్గిరస|ಉತ್-ತಾನ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಉತ್ತಾನ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಉತ್|ತಾನ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಉತ್-ತಾನ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|ಉತ್ತಾನ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|ತಾನ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|ut-tāna āṅgirasa|uttāna āṅgirasa|ut|tāna āṅgirasa|ut-taana aaMgirasa|uttaana aaMgirasa|taana aaMgirasa|ut-taana aangirasa|uttaana aangirasa|taana aangirasa|_vei_120@340|_vei1_pn085
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@339">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@341">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn084">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn085">1:085</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn086">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ut-<a class="vei_120a" href="तान">tāna</a> Āṅgirasa</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120340"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120340">1</a></sup> as a quasi-mythical person who received all good things, and yet was not harmed, as he was really a form of the earth, according to Sāyaṇa's<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120340"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120340">2</a></sup> explanation. His name occurs also in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120340"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120340">3</a></sup> the Pancaviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120340"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120340">4</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120340"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120340">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120340"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120340">↑</a> ii. 3, 2, 5. <i>Cf.</i> ii. 2, 5, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120340"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120340">↑</a> On <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 3, 2, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120340"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120340">↑</a> ix. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120340"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120340">↑</a> i. 8, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120340"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120340">↑</a> iii. 10, 2. 3.</li></ol></div>

उदग्-अयन|उदगयन|उदग्|अयन|ఉదగ్-అయన|ఉదగయన|ఉదగ్|అయన|ಉದಗ್-ಅಯನ|ಉದಗಯನ|ಉದಗ್|ಅಯನ|udag-ayana|udagayana|udag|ayana|_vei_120@341|_vei1_pn085
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@340">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@342">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn084">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn085">1:085</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn086">↷</a><hr><p><b>Udag-ayana.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सूर्य">sūrya</a>.</b></p></div>

उद्-अंक शौल्बायन|उदंक शौल्बायन|उद्|अंक शौल्बायन|उद्-अङ्क शौल्बायन|उदङ्क शौल्बायन|अङ्क शौल्बायन|ఉద్-అంక శౌల్బాయన|ఉదంక శౌల్బాయన|ఉద్|అంక శౌల్బాయన|ఉద్-అఙ్క శౌల్బాయన|ఉదఙ్క శౌల్బాయన|అఙ్క శౌల్బాయన|ಉದ್-ಅಂಕ ಶೌಲ್ಬಾಯನ|ಉದಂಕ ಶೌಲ್ಬಾಯನ|ಉದ್|ಅಂಕ ಶೌಲ್ಬಾಯನ|ಉದ್-ಅಙ್ಕ ಶೌಲ್ಬಾಯನ|ಉದಙ್ಕ ಶೌಲ್ಬಾಯನ|ಅಙ್ಕ ಶೌಲ್ಬಾಯನ|ud-aṅka śaulbāyana|udaṅka śaulbāyana|ud|aṅka śaulbāyana|ud-aMka shaulbaayana|udaMka shaulbaayana|aMka shaulbaayana|ud-anka shaulbaayana|udanka shaulbaayana|anka shaulbaayana|_vei_120@342|_vei1_pn085
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@341">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@343">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn084">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn085">1:085</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn086">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ud-<a class="vei_120a" href="अंक">aṅka</a> Śaulbāyana.</b>--His views on <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>, which he identified with the vital airs (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a></i>), are mentioned in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iv. 1, 3). He would thus have been a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a></b> of <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>. He is also mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (vii. 5, 4, 2) as holding that the Daśarātra ceremony was the prosperity or best part of the Sattra (‘sacrificial session’).</p></div>

उद्-अञ्चन|उदञ्चन|उद्|अञ्चन|ఉద్-అఞ్చన|ఉదఞ్చన|ఉద్|అఞ్చన|ಉದ್-ಅಞ್ಚನ|ಉದಞ್ಚನ|ಉದ್|ಅಞ್ಚನ|ud-añcana|udañcana|ud|añcana|ud-a~nchana|uda~nchana|a~nchana|_vei_120@343|_vei1_pn085
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@342">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@344">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn084">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn085">1:085</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn086">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ud-añcana.</b>--This word, occurring only metaphorically in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120343"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120343">1</a></sup> means a ‘pail’ or ‘bucket’ in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120343"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120343">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120343"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120343">↑</a> v. 44, 13 (<i>dhiyām udañcanaḥ,</i> ‘a very well of prayers’).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120343"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120343">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 32; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 3, 5, 21.</li></ol></div>

उदमय आत्रेय|ఉదమయ ఆత్రేయ|ಉದಮಯ ಆತ್ರೇಯ|udamaya ātreya|udamaya aatreya|_vei_120@344|_vei1_pn085
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@343">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@345">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn084">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn085">1:085</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn086">↷</a><hr><p><b>Udamaya Ātreya</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 22) as <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, or domestic priest, of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अंग वैरोचन">aṅga vairocana</a>.</b></p></div>

उदर शाण्डिल्य|ఉదర శాణ్డిల్య|ಉದರ ಶಾಣ್ಡಿಲ್ಯ|udara śāṇḍilya|udara shaaNDilya|_vei_120@345|_vei1_pn085
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@344">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@346">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn084">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn085">1:085</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn086">↷</a><hr><p><b>Udara Śāṇḍilya</b> is mentioned as a teacher in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120345">1</a></sup> and as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अतिधन्वन् शौनक">atidhanvan śaunaka</a></b> in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120345">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120345">↑</a> i. 9, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120345">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 384.</li></ol></div>

उदल|ఉదల|ಉದಲ|udala|_vei_120@346|_vei1_pn085
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@345">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@347">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn084">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn085">1:085</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn086">↷</a><hr><p><b>Udala,</b> a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्वामित्र">vaiśvāmitra</a>, is mentioned in the Pañcaviṃsa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xiv. 11, 33) as the seer of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a>. </p></div>

उद्-आज|उदाज|उद्|आज|ఉద్-ఆజ|ఉదాజ|ఉద్|ఆజ|ಉದ್-ಆಜ|ಉದಾಜ|ಉದ್|ಆಜ|ud-āja|udāja|ud|āja|ud-aaja|udaaja|aaja|_vei_120@347|_vei1_pn086
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@346">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@348">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn085">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn086">1:086</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn087">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ud-āja</b> is the word used in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120347"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120347">1</a></sup> to denote the share of the booty of war taken by the king after a victory (<i>saṃgrāmaṃ jitvā</i>). This interpretation, which is that of Delbrück,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120347"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120347">2</a></sup> is clearly correct, as against the older version, ‘march out,’ given by von Schroeder<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120347"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120347">3</a></sup> and accepted by Bo7htlingk.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120347"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120347">4</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="उदाज">udāja</a> thus corresponds precisely with the Homeric  This sense also suits Nirāja, the variant of both the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120347"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120347">5</a></sup> and the Kapiṣṭhala<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120347"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120347">6</a></sup> Saṃhitās.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120347"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120347">↑</a> i. 10, 16; iv. 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120347"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120347">↑</a> <i>Festgruss an Bo7htlingk,</i> 25.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120347"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120347">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, 1, xv.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120347"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120347">↑</a> Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120347"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120347">↑</a> xxviii. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120347"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120347">↑</a> xliv. 3.</li></ol></div>

उदान|ఉదాన|ಉದಾನ|udāna|udaana|_vei_120@348|_vei1_pn086
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@347">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@349">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn085">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn086">1:086</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn087">↷</a><hr><p><b>Udāna</b> is usually the fifth of the vital breaths (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a></b>) when five are enumerated.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120348"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120348">1</a></sup> Sometimes<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120348"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120348">2</a></sup> it appears as the second, coming after <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a>, and followed by <a class="vei_120a" href="व्यान">vyāna</a> or <a class="vei_120a" href="समान">samāna</a>. Again,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120348"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120348">3</a></sup> it is found simply opposed to <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a>, or it simply follows <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="अपान">apāna</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120348"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120348">4</a></sup> In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120348"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120348">5</a></sup> it is treated as the breath that consumes food, a notion traceable in the later Upaniṣads,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120348"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120348">6</a></sup> while it is also regarded as the wind that rises up by the throat,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120348"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120348">7</a></sup> and leads the spirit forth at death.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120348"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120348">8</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120348"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120348">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 12, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, v. 4; 10; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 2, 2, 5; xi. 8, 3, 6 (in the order <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="व्यान">vyāna</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अपान">apāna</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="उदान">udāna</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="समान">samāna</a>); Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 5, 3; iii. 4, 1 (<a class="vei_120a" href="समान">samāna</a> is omitted); iii. 9, 26; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 13, 5; v. 23, 1. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 3, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120348"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120348">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, i. 20; vii. 27; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 4, 2, 10, etc. (with <a class="vei_120a" href="व्यान">vyāna</a>); <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 2 (with <a class="vei_120a" href="समान">samāna</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120348"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120348">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, vi. 20; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 2, 2; ix. 2, 4, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120348"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120348">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, viii. 8; xi. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120348"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120348">↑</a> xi. 2, 4, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120348"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120348">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120348"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120348">↑</a> Amṛtabindu <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, 34.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120348"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120348">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रश्न">praśna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 7. <i>Cf.</i> Deussen, <i>Philosophy of the Upaniṣads,</i> 280.</li></ol></div>

उद्-ईच्यस्|उद्-ईच्य|उदीच्यस्|उदीच्य|उद्|ईच्यस्|ईच्य|ఉద్-ఈచ్యస్|ఉద్-ఈచ్య|ఉదీచ్యస్|ఉదీచ్య|ఉద్|ఈచ్యస్|ఈచ్య|ಉದ್-ಈಚ್ಯಸ್|ಉದ್-ಈಚ್ಯ|ಉದೀಚ್ಯಸ್|ಉದೀಚ್ಯ|ಉದ್|ಈಚ್ಯಸ್|ಈಚ್ಯ|ud-īcyas|ud-īcya|udīcyas|udīcya|ud|īcyas|īcya|ud-iichyas|ud-iichya|udiichyas|udiichya|iichyas|iichya|ud-eechyas|ud-eechya|udeechyas|udeechya|eechyas|eechya|_vei_120@349|_vei1_pn086
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@348">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@350">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn085">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn086">1:086</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn087">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ud-īcyas.</b>--The Brāhmaṇas of the northern parts are referred to in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120349">1</a></sup> as engaging, with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्वैदायन">svaidāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शौनक">śaunaka</a></b> as their spokesman, in a dispute with the Kurupañcāla <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उद्दालक आरुणि">uddālaka āruṇi</a>,</b> and as vanquishing him. Their relation to the Kurupañcālas appears also from the fact that in  the same <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120349">2</a></sup> reference is made to the speech of the north being similar to that of the Kurupañcālas. The speech of the Northerners was also celebrated for purity; hence Brāhmaṇas used to go to the north for purposes of study, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120349">3</a></sup> while in the Buddhist texts the school of Takṣaśilā (in <a class="vei_120a" href="गन्धार">gandhāra</a>) is famous as a resort of students.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120349">4</a></sup> Possibly, too, Sanskrit was specially developed in Kaśmīr, as suggested by Franke.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120349">5</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120349">↑</a> xi. 4, 1, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120349">↑</a> iii. 2, 3, 15. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 191; Lévi, <i>La Doctrine du Sacrifice,</i> 35.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120349">↑</a> vii. 6. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 153; 2, 309.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120349">↑</a> Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India,</i> 8, 28, 203.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120349">↑</a> <i>Cf. Pāli und Sanskrit</i> (1902), 88, 89.</li></ol></div>

उदुम्बर|उदुंबर|ఉదుమ్బర|ఉదుంబర|ಉದುಮ್ಬರ|ಉದುಂಬರ|udumbara|_vei_120@350|_vei1_pn087
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@349">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@351">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn086">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn087">1:087</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn089">↷</a><hr><p><b>Udumbara.</b>--This name of the <i>Ficus glomerata</i> does not occur in the Rigveda, but is often found from the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120350">1</a></sup> onwards. For ritual purposes of all kinds its wood was constantly used. The sacrificial post (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="यूप">yūpa</a></i>)<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120350">2</a></sup> and the sacrificial ladle<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120350">3</a></sup> were made of it, and amulets of <a class="vei_120a" href="उदुम्बर">udumbara</a> are mentioned.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120350">4</a></sup> Its wood, like that of other kinds of figtree-<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वत्थ">aśvattha</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="न्यग्रोध">nyagrodha</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्लक्ष">plakṣa</a></b>--was considered suitable for employment at the sacrifice.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120350">5</a></sup> The sweetness of its fruit is referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120350">6</a></sup> where it is put on the same level as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मधु">madhu</a>.</b> It is there also spoken of as ripening three times a year.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120350">7</a></sup> A forest of <a class="vei_120a" href="उदुम्बर">udumbara</a> trees is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120350">8</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120350">↑</a> xix. 31, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 1, 6, etc.; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 1, 33; vii. 4, 1, 38, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120350">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 1, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120350">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> v. 4, 7, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120350">↑</a> Atharvaveda, xix. 31, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120350">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 8, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120350">↑</a> vii. 15.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120350">↑</a> v. 24.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120350">↑</a> xvi. 6, 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 59.</li></ol></div>

उद्दालक आरुणि|ఉద్దాలక ఆరుణి|ಉದ್ದಾಲಕ ಆರುಣಿ|uddālaka āruṇi|uddaalaka aaruNi|_vei_120@351|_vei1_pn087
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@350">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@352">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn086">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn087">1:087</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn089">↷</a><hr><p><b>Uddālaka Āruṇi.</b>--Uddālaka, son of Aruṇa, is one of the most prominent teachers of the Vedic period. He was a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> of the Kurupañcālas, according to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120351">1</a></sup> This statement is confirmed by the fact that he was teacher of <b>Proti <a class="vei_120a" href="कौसुरुबिन्दि">kausurubindi</a></b> of Kauśāmbī,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120351">2</a></sup> and that his son  <b>Svetaketu</b> is found disputing among the Pañcālas.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120351">3</a></sup> He was a pupil of Aruṇa, his father,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120351">4</a></sup> as well as of <a class="vei_120a" href="पतञ्चल काप्य">patañcala kāpya</a>,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120351">5</a></sup> of <a class="vei_120a" href="मद्र">madra</a>, while he was the teacher of the famous <a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120351">6</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="वाजसनेय">vājasaneya</a> and of <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a>,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120351">7</a></sup> although the former is represented elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120351">8</a></sup> as having silenced him. He overcame in argument <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राचीनयोग्य">prācīnayogya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शौचेय">śauceya</a>,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120351">9</a></sup> and apparently also <a class="vei_120a" href="भद्रसेन आजातशत्रव">bhadrasena ājātaśatrava</a>,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120351">10</a></sup> though the text here seems to read the name as Āraṇi. He was a <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a>,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120351">11</a></sup> and is often alluded to as such. As an authority on questions of ritual and philosophy, he is repeatedly referred to by his patronymic name <a class="vei_120a" href="आरुणि">āruṇi</a> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120351">12</a></sup> the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120351">13</a></sup> the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120351">14</a></sup> and occasionally in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a>,<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_120351">15</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a>,<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_120351">16</a></sup> and the Ṣaḍviṃśa<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_120351">17</a></sup> Brāhmaṇas, as well as the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_120351">18</a></sup> In the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā he is not mentioned, according to Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_120351">19</a></sup> but only his father Aruṇa; his name does not occur, according to Weber,<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_120351">20</a></sup> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, but in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_120351">21</a></sup> he is, as <a class="vei_120a" href="आरुणि">āruṇi</a>, known as a contemporary of <b>Divodāsa <a class="vei_120a" href="भैमसेनि">bhaimaseni</a>,</b> and in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_22_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_120351">22</a></sup> he is mentioned as serving <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वासिष्ठ">vāsiṣṭha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="चैकितानेय">caikitāneya</a>.</b> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> tradition he seldom appears. There is an allusion in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_120351">23</a></sup> to Kusurubinda <a class="vei_120a" href="औद्दालकि">auddālaki</a>, and according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_120351">24</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="नचिकेतस्">naciketas</a> was a son of <a class="vei_120a" href="वाजश्रवस">vājaśravasa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a>, who  is made out to be Uddālaka by Sāyaṇa.<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_120351">25</a></sup> But the episode of <a class="vei_120a" href="नचिकेतस्">naciketas</a>, being somewhat unreal, cannot be regarded as of historical value in proving relationship. Aruṇa is known to the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā. A real son of Uddālaka was the famous <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्वेतकेतु">śvetaketu</a>,</b> who is expressly reported by Āpastamba<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_120351">26</a></sup> to have been in his time an Avara or later authority, a statement of importance for the date of <a class="vei_120a" href="आरुणि">āruṇi</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120351">↑</a> xi. 4, 1, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120351">↑</a> xii. 2, 2, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120351">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 1; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120351">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 33 (in both recensions).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120351">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> iii. 7, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120351">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> vi. 3, 15; 4, 33.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120351">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xv.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120351">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 7, 31.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120351">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 3, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120351">↑</a> v. 5, 5, 14. Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 141, has Āraṇi. There is a chronological difficulty in taking <a class="vei_120a" href="आरुणि">āruṇi</a> as meant, since <a class="vei_120a" href="आजातशत्रव">ājātaśatrava</a> must presumably have been a descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="अजातशत्रु">ajātaśatru</a>, a contemporary of <a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a> (see <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1), who again was a patron of <a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a>, a pupil of <a class="vei_120a" href="आरुणि">āruṇi</a>. But this difficulty is not fatal.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120351">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 3, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120351">↑</a> i. 1, 2, 11; ii. 2, 1, 34; iii. 3, 4, 19; iv. 4, 8, 9; xi. 2, 6, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120351">↑</a> iii. 5, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120351">↑</a> iii. 11, 4; v. 11, 2; 17, 1; vi. 8, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_120351">↑</a> viii. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_120351">↑</a> xxvi. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_120351">↑</a> i. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_120351">↑</a> i. 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_120351">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 146.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_120351">↑</a> <i>Indian Literature,</i> 69. But <i>cf.</i> xxiii. 1, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_120351">↑</a> vii. 8. <i>Cf.</i> viii. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_120351">↑</a> i. 42, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_120351">↑</a> vii. 2, 2, 1 (a late passage).</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_120351">↑</a> iii. 11, 8, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_120351">↑</a> On <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>. Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_120351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_120351">↑</a> See Bühler, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 2, xxxviii; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 39.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 170, n.; 2, 201, 202; Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 396, n.; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, xl., xli.</li></ol></div>

उद्दालकायन|ఉద్దాలకాయన|ಉದ್ದಾಲಕಾಯನ|uddālakāyana|uddaalakaayana|_vei_120@352|_vei1_pn089
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@351">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@353">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn087">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn089">1:089</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn090">↷</a><hr><p><b>Uddālakāyana</b> is mentioned as a pupil of <a class="vei_120a" href="जाबालायन">jābālāyana</a> in the second <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) contained in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iv. 6, 2).</p></div>

उद्र|ఉద్ర|ಉದ್ರ|udra|_vei_120@353|_vei1_pn089
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@352">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@354">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn087">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn089">1:089</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn090">↷</a><hr><p><b>Udra</b> is the name of an animal occurring only in the list of sacrificial victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> given in the Saṃhitās of the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120353"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120353">1</a></sup> According to Mahīdhara<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120353"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120353">2</a></sup> it was a crab; but as the commentary on the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120353"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120353">3</a></sup> calls it a watercat, there can be no doubt that it was an otter.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120353"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120353">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 20, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 18; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 37.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120353"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120353">↑</a> On Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120353"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120353">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 95, 96; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 247. <i>Udrin</i> occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 5.</li></ol></div>

उद्धि|ఉద్ధి|ಉದ್ಧಿ|uddhi|_vei_120@354|_vei1_pn089
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@353">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@355">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn087">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn089">1:089</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn090">↷</a><hr><p><b>Uddhi</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120354">1</a></sup> denotes some part of a chariot, probably the seat,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120354">2</a></sup> but, according to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120354">3</a></sup> the frame resting on the axle.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120354">↑</a> Av. viii. 8, 22; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 2, 2, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 3, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120354">↑</a> So Whitney's Translation of the Atharvaveda, 506; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 149.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120354">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

उप-केतु|उपकेतु|उप|केतु|ఉప-కేతు|ఉపకేతు|ఉప|కేతు|ಉಪ-ಕೇತು|ಉಪಕೇತು|ಉಪ|ಕೇತು|upa-ketu|upaketu|upa|ketu|_vei_120@355|_vei1_pn089
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@354">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@356">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn087">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn089">1:089</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn090">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upa-ketu</b> is the name of a man mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā (xiii. 1).</p></div>

उप-कोसल कामलायन|उपकोसल कामलायन|उप|कोसल कामलायन|ఉప-కోసల కామలాయన|ఉపకోసల కామలాయన|ఉప|కోసల కామలాయన|ಉಪ-ಕೋಸಲ ಕಾಮಲಾಯನ|ಉಪಕೋಸಲ ಕಾಮಲಾಯನ|ಉಪ|ಕೋಸಲ ಕಾಮಲಾಯನ|upa-kosala kāmalāyana|upakosala kāmalāyana|upa|kosala kāmalāyana|upa-kosala kaamalaayana|upakosala kaamalaayana|kosala kaamalaayana|_vei_120@356|_vei1_pn089
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@355">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@357">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn087">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn089">1:089</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn090">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upa-<a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a> Kāmalāyana</b> is mentioned as a teacher and a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सत्यकाम">satyakāma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="जाबाल">jābāla</a></b> in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iv. 10, 1; 14, 1). </p></div>

उप-क्वस|उपक्वस|उप|क्वस|ఉప-క్వస|ఉపక్వస|ఉప|క్వస|ಉಪ-ಕ್ವಸ|ಉಪಕ್ವಸ|ಉಪ|ಕ್ವಸ|upa-kvasa|upakvasa|upa|kvasa|_vei_120@357|_vei1_pn090
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@356">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@358">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn089">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn090">1:090</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn091">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upa-kvasa</b> is the name in the Atharvaveda (vi. 50, 2) of a noxious insect injurious to seed. Sāyaṇa, however, reads the word as a plural adjective (<i>a-pakvasaḥ = a-dagdāḥ</i>), but the Paippalāda recension supports the form <i>upakvasaḥ.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 237; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 486; Whitney's Translation of the Atharvaveda, 318.</p></div>

उप-गु सौश्रवस|उपगु सौश्रवस|उप|गु सौश्रवस|ఉప-గు సౌశ్రవస|ఉపగు సౌశ్రవస|ఉప|గు సౌశ్రవస|ಉಪ-ಗು ಸೌಶ್ರವಸ|ಉಪಗು ಸೌಶ್ರವಸ|ಉಪ|ಗು ಸೌಶ್ರವಸ|upa-gu sauśravasa|upagu sauśravasa|upa|gu sauśravasa|upa-gu saushravasa|upagu saushravasa|gu saushravasa|_vei_120@358|_vei1_pn090
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@357">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@359">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn089">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn090">1:090</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn091">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upa-gu Sauśravasa</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xiv. 6, 8) as <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुत्स औरव">kutsa aurava</a>,</b> by whom he was murdered because of his paying homage to <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 268; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 57.</p></div>

उप-चित्|उपचित्|उप|चित्|ఉప-చిత్|ఉపచిత్|ఉప|చిత్|ಉಪ-ಚಿತ್|ಉಪಚಿತ್|ಉಪ|ಚಿತ್|upa-cit|upacit|upa|cit|upa-chit|upachit|chit|_vei_120@359|_vei1_pn090
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@358">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@360">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn089">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn090">1:090</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn091">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upa-cit</b> occurs in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120359"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120359">1</a></sup> as the name of a disease, which Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120359"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120359">2</a></sup> renders as ‘swelling,’ and which Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120359"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120359">3</a></sup> identifies with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अपचित्">apacit</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120359"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120359">↑</a> xii. 97.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120359"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120359">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120359"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120359">↑</a> <i>Proceedings of the American Oriental Society,</i> October, 1887, xviii.</li></ol></div>

उप-जिह्विका|उपजिह्विका|उप|जिह्विका|उप-जीका|उपजीका|जीका|उप-दीका|उपदीका|दीका|ఉప-జిహ్వికా|ఉపజిహ్వికా|ఉప|జిహ్వికా|ఉప-జీకా|ఉపజీకా|జీకా|ఉప-దీకా|ఉపదీకా|దీకా|ಉಪ-ಜಿಹ್ವಿಕಾ|ಉಪಜಿಹ್ವಿಕಾ|ಉಪ|ಜಿಹ್ವಿಕಾ|ಉಪ-ಜೀಕಾ|ಉಪಜೀಕಾ|ಜೀಕಾ|ಉಪ-ದೀಕಾ|ಉಪದೀಕಾ|ದೀಕಾ|upa-jihvikā|upajihvikā|upa|jihvikā|upa-jīkā|upajīkā|jīkā|upa-dīkā|upadīkā|dīkā|upa-jihvikaa|upajihvikaa|jihvikaa|upa-jiikaa|upajiikaa|jiikaa|upa-diikaa|upadiikaa|diikaa|upa-jeekaa|upajeekaa|jeekaa|upa-deekaa|upadeekaa|deekaa|_vei_120@360|_vei1_pn090
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@359">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@361">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn089">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn090">1:090</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn091">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upa-<a class="vei_120a" href="जिह्विका">jihvikā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="उप-जीका">upa-jīkā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="उप">upa</a>-dīkā</b> are all forms of one word denoting a species of ant.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120360"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120360">1</a></sup> To these ants is attributed in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120360"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120360">2</a></sup> the power of penetrating to water which possesses curative properties. They were accordingly used in all sorts of spells against poisoning. The belief in their healing qualities was no doubt due to the well-known properties of the earth of ant-heaps which contains their water.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120360"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120360">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="उपजिह्विका">upajihvikā</a></i> is the form in Rv. viii. 102, 21; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="उपजीका">upajīkā</a></i> in Av. ii. 3, 4; vi. 100, 2; but <i>upacīkā</i> in both passages in the Paippalāda recension; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="उपदीका">upadīkā</a></i> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 3, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, v. 1, 4; 10, 9; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 1, 1, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120360"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120360">↑</a> vi. 100, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 7, 482 <i>et seq.; Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 511; Whitney's Translation of the Atharvaveda, 41, 354; Bergaigne and Henry, <i>Manuel Védique,</i> 153.</li></ol></div>

उप-धान|उपधान|उप|धान|ఉప-ధాన|ఉపధాన|ఉప|ధాన|ಉಪ-ಧಾನ|ಉಪಧಾನ|ಉಪ|ಧಾನ|upa-dhāna|upadhāna|upa|dhāna|upa-dhaana|upadhaana|dhaana|_vei_120@361|_vei1_pn090
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@360">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@362">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn089">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn090">1:090</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn091">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upa-dhāna</b> denotes the ‘cushion’ of a seat (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="आसन्दी">āsandī</a></b>) in the Atharvaveda (xiv. 2, 65). It corresponds to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपबर्हण">upabarhaṇa</a></b> of other texts. </p></div>

उप-धि|उपधि|उप|धि|ఉప-ధి|ఉపధి|ఉప|ధి|ಉಪ-ಧಿ|ಉಪಧಿ|ಉಪ|ಧಿ|upa-dhi|upadhi|upa|dhi|_vei_120@362|_vei1_pn091
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@361">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@363">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn090">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn091">1:091</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn092">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upa-dhi</b> occurs once each in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120362">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120362">2</a></sup> in conjunction with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रधि">pradhi</a>,</b> denoting part of the wheel of a chariot. It is impossible to decide exactly what part is meant. Roth,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120362">3</a></sup> Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120362">4</a></sup> and Bloomfield,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120362">5</a></sup> agree in thinking that the word denotes the spokes collectively. Whitney,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120362">6</a></sup> considering this improbable, prefers to see in it the designation of a solid wheel, <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रधि">pradhi</a> being presumably the rim and <a class="vei_120a" href="उपधि">upadhi</a> the rest. Other possibilities<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120362">7</a></sup> are that the <a class="vei_120a" href="उपधि">upadhi</a> is a rim beneath the felly, or the felly itself as compared with the tire (ordinarily <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पवि">pavi</a></b>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120362">↑</a> ii. 39, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120362">↑</a> vi. 70, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120362">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120362">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 248 (ignoring the Atharvaveda passage).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120362">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 493.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120362">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 334.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120362">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

उप-निषद्|उपनिषद्|उप|निषद्|ఉప-నిషద్|ఉపనిషద్|ఉప|నిషద్|ಉಪ-ನಿಷದ್|ಉಪನಿಷದ್|ಉಪ|ನಿಷದ್|upa-niṣad|upaniṣad|upa|niṣad|upa-niShad|upaniShad|niShad|_vei_120@363|_vei1_pn091
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@362">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@364">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn090">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn091">1:091</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn092">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upa-niṣad</b> in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120363">1</a></sup> normally denotes the ‘secret sense’ of some word or text, sometimes the ‘secret rule’ of the mendicant. But in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120363">2</a></sup> it is already used in the plural as the designation of a class of writings, no doubt actually existing and similar to the Upaniṣads in the nature of their subject-matter and its treatment. Similarly the sections of the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> end with the words <i>ity <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.</i> The <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120363">3</a></sup> commences its third part with the title ‘The <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> of the Saṃhitā,’ and the title occurs also in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120363">4</a></sup> The exact primary sense of the expression is doubtful. The natural derivation, adopted by Max Müller<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120363">5</a></sup> and usual ever since, makes the word mean firstly a session of pupils, hence secret doctrine, and secondly the title of a work on secret doctrine. Oldenberg,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120363">6</a></sup> however, traces the use of the word to the earlier  sense of ‘worship’ (<i>cf. upāsana</i>). Deussen<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120363">7</a></sup> considers the original sense to have been ‘secret word,’ next ‘secret text,’ and then ‘secret import,’ but this order of meaning is improbable. Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120363">8</a></sup> suggests that <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> denotes a subsidiary treatise, but this sense does not account naturally for the common use as ‘secret meaning,’ which is far more frequent than any other.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120363">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 3, 5, 12; 4, 5, 1; 5, 1, 1; xii. 2, 2, 23, etc.; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 1, 10; 13, 4; viii. 8, 4. 5; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 1, 20; iv. 2, 1; v. 5, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 1, 6; 2, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120363">↑</a> ii. 4, 10; iv. 1, 2; 5, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120363">↑</a> iii. 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120363">↑</a> vii. 2. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120363">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 1, xxxiii <i>et seq. Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 204.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120363">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 50, 457; 54, 70; <i>Die Literatur des alten Indien,</i> 72.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120363">↑</a> <i>Philosophy of the Upaniṣads,</i> 16 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120363">↑</a> <i>Religions of India,</i> 218.</li></ol></div>

उप-पति|उपपति|उप|पति|ఉప-పతి|ఉపపతి|ఉప|పతి|ಉಪ-ಪತಿ|ಉಪಪತಿ|ಉಪ|ಪತಿ|upa-pati|upapati|upa|pati|_vei_120@364|_vei1_pn092
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@363">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@365">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn091">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn092">1:092</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn093">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upa-pati,</b> ‘the gallant,’ is mentioned with the <a class="vei_120a" href="जार">jāra</a>, ‘lover,’ in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120364"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120364">1</a></sup> as a victim in the human sacrifice (<i>puruṣamedha</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120364"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120364">↑</a> xxx. 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 4, 1.</li></ol></div>

उप-बर्हण|उपबर्हण|उप|बर्हण|ఉప-బర్హణ|ఉపబర్హణ|ఉప|బర్హణ|ಉಪ-ಬರ್ಹಣ|ಉಪಬರ್ಹಣ|ಉಪ|ಬರ್ಹಣ|upa-barhaṇa|upabarhaṇa|upa|barhaṇa|upa-barhaNa|upabarhaNa|barhaNa|_vei_120@365|_vei1_pn092
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@364">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@366">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn091">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn092">1:092</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn093">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upa-barhaṇa</b> denotes a ‘pillow’ or ‘cushion,’ especially of a seat (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="आसन्दी">āsandī</a></i>), occurring in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120365"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120365">1</a></sup> the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120365"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120365">2</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120365"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120365">3</a></sup> The feminine Upabarhaṇī is found in the Rigveda with the same sense, but used metaphorically of the earth.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120365"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120365">↑</a> x. 85, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120365"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120365">↑</a> ix. 5, 28; xii. 2, 19. 20; xv. 3, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120365"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120365">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 12; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 8, 4, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 6, 10; 6, 8, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxviii. 4, etc.</li></ol></div>

उप-मन्थनी|उप-मन्थनि|उपमन्थनी|उपमन्थनि|उप|मन्थनी|मन्थनि|ఉప-మన్థనీ|ఉప-మన్థని|ఉపమన్థనీ|ఉపమన్థని|ఉప|మన్థనీ|మన్థని|ಉಪ-ಮನ್ಥನೀ|ಉಪ-ಮನ್ಥನಿ|ಉಪಮನ್ಥನೀ|ಉಪಮನ್ಥನಿ|ಉಪ|ಮನ್ಥನೀ|ಮನ್ಥನಿ|upa-manthanī|upa-manthani|upamanthanī|upamanthani|upa|manthanī|manthani|upa-manthanii|upamanthanii|manthanii|upa-manthanee|upamanthanee|manthanee|_vei_120@366|_vei1_pn092
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@365">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@367">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn091">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn092">1:092</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn093">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upa-manthanī</b> is used in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120366"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120366">1</a></sup> to denote ‘churning sticks.’ In the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120366"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120366">2</a></sup> the ‘churner’ (<i>upamanthitṛ</i>) is included in the list of victims at the human sacrifice (<i>puruṣamedha</i>), and the verb <i><a class="vei_120a" href="उप">upa</a>-manth</i> is often used of churning or mixing fluids.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120366"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120366">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120366"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120366">↑</a> vi. 3, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120366"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120366">↑</a> xxx. 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 8, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120366"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120366">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 8, 4. 5; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 1, 6; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 2, 4.</li></ol></div>

उप-मन्यु|उपमन्यु|उप|मन्यु|ఉప-మన్యు|ఉపమన్యు|ఉప|మన్యు|ಉಪ-ಮನ್ಯು|ಉಪಮನ್ಯು|ಉಪ|ಮನ್ಯು|upa-manyu|upamanyu|upa|manyu|_vei_120@367|_vei1_pn092
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@366">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@368">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn091">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn092">1:092</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn093">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upa-manyu</b> is, according to Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120367"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120367">1</a></sup> the name of a person in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120367"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120367">2</a></sup> but is more probably explained by Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120367"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120367">3</a></sup> as a mere epithet. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120367"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120367">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 113.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120367"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120367">↑</a> i. 102. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120367"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120367">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

उपम-श्रवस्|उपम-श्रव|उपमश्रवस्|उपमश्रव|उपम|श्रवस्|श्रव|ఉపమ-శ్రవస్|ఉపమ-శ్రవ|ఉపమశ్రవస్|ఉపమశ్రవ|ఉపమ|శ్రవస్|శ్రవ|ಉಪಮ-ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಉಪಮ-ಶ್ರವ|ಉಪಮಶ್ರವಸ್|ಉಪಮಶ್ರವ|ಉಪಮ|ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಶ್ರವ|upama-śravas|upama-śrava|upamaśravas|upamaśrava|upama|śravas|śrava|upama-shravas|upama-shrava|upamashravas|upamashrava|shravas|shrava|_vei_120@368|_vei1_pn093
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@367">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@369">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn092">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn093">1:093</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn094">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upama-śravas</b> is mentioned in a hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120368"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120368">1</a></sup> as a son of <b>Kuruśravaṇa,</b> and grandson of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मेधातिथि">medhātithi</a>.</b> The exact force of the reference to him in however, uncertain. According to the Bṛhaddevatā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120368"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120368">2</a></sup> followed by Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120368"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120368">3</a></sup> and by Lanman,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120368"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120368">4</a></sup> the poet in the hymn consoles <a class="vei_120a" href="उपमश्रवस्">upamaśravas</a> for the death of his grandfather, <a class="vei_120a" href="मेधातिथि">medhātithi</a>. Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120368"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120368">5</a></sup> on the other hand, thinks that the poet, who was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कवष">kavaṣa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐलूष">ailūṣa</a>,</b> was ill-treated by his patron's son, <a class="vei_120a" href="उपमश्रवस्">upamaśravas</a>, and cast into a ditch or well, where he uttered his complaint and appeal for mercy. But of this there is no adequate evidence, and the tradition of the Bṛhaddevatā seems sound.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120368"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120368">↑</a> x. 33, 6. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120368"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120368">↑</a> vii. 35. 36, with Macdonell's notes.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120368"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120368">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 165.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120368"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120368">↑</a> <i>Sanskrit reader,</i> 386, 389.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120368"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120368">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 150, n.</li></ol></div>

उप-मित्|उपमित्|उप|मित्|ఉప-మిత్|ఉపమిత్|ఉప|మిత్|ಉಪ-ಮಿತ್|ಉಪಮಿತ್|ಉಪ|ಮಿತ್|upa-mit|upamit|upa|mit|_vei_120@369|_vei1_pn093
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@368">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@370">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn092">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn093">1:093</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn094">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upa-mit</b> occurs twice in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120369">1</a></sup> and once in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120369">2</a></sup> as the designation of some part of a house. The passages in the Rigveda leave little doubt that the word means an upright pillar. As it is, in the Atharvaveda, coupled with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परिमित्">parimit</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतिमित्">pratimit</a>,</b> the conclusion is natural that the latter word denotes the beams supporting the <a class="vei_120a" href="उपमित्">upamit</a>, presumably by leaning against it at an angle, while <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परिमित्">parimit</a></b> denotes the beams connecting the Upamits horizontally. These interpretations, however, can only be conjectural. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गृह">gṛha</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120369">↑</a> i. 59, 1; iv. 5, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120369">↑</a> ix. 3, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 153; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 596; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 525.</li></ol></div>

उपर|ఉపర|ಉಪರ|upara|_vei_120@370|_vei1_pn093
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@369">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@371">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn092">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn093">1:093</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn094">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upara,</b> which, according to Pischel,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120370">1</a></sup> means ‘stone’ in general, is the technical name of the stone on which the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> plant was laid in order to be pounded for the extraction of the juice by other stones (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अद्रि">adri</a>, grāvan</i>). The word is rare, occurring only thrice in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120370">2</a></sup> and once in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120370">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120370">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 109. This is the sense of the form <i><a class="vei_120a" href="उपल">upala</a></i> (Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxv. 8, etc.).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120370">↑</a> i. 79, 3; x. 94, 5; 175, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120370">↑</a> vi. 49, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 154; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 317; Von Schroeder, <i>Mysterium und Mimus,</i> 414.</li></ol></div>

उपल-प्रक्षिणी|उपल-प्रक्षिणि|उपलप्रक्षिणी|उपलप्रक्षिणि|उपल|प्रक्षिणी|प्रक्षिणि|ఉపల-ప్రక్షిణీ|ఉపల-ప్రక్షిణి|ఉపలప్రక్షిణీ|ఉపలప్రక్షిణి|ఉపల|ప్రక్షిణీ|ప్రక్షిణి|ಉಪಲ-ಪ್ರಕ್ಷಿಣೀ|ಉಪಲ-ಪ್ರಕ್ಷಿಣಿ|ಉಪಲಪ್ರಕ್ಷಿಣೀ|ಉಪಲಪ್ರಕ್ಷಿಣಿ|ಉಪಲ|ಪ್ರಕ್ಷಿಣೀ|ಪ್ರಕ್ಷಿಣಿ|upala-prakṣiṇī|upala-prakṣiṇi|upalaprakṣiṇī|upalaprakṣiṇi|upala|prakṣiṇī|prakṣiṇi|upala-prakShiNii|upala-prakShiNi|upalaprakShiNii|upalaprakShiNi|prakShiNii|prakShiNi|upala-prakShiNee|upalaprakShiNee|prakShiNee|upala-praxiNii|upala-praxiNi|upalapraxiNii|upalapraxiNi|praxiNii|praxiNi|_vei_120@371|_vei1_pn094
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@370">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@372">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn093">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn094">1:094</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn095">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upala-prakṣiṇī</b> occurs once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120371"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120371">1</a></sup> where it designates the occupation of a woman, as opposed to that of her son, who is a poet (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कारु">kāru</a></i>), and to that of his father, who is a physician (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="भिषज्">bhiṣaj</a></i>). <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120371"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120371">2</a></sup> renders the word by ‘maker of groats’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सक्तु">saktu</a>-kārikā</i>), and Roth,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120371"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120371">3</a></sup> Grassmann,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120371"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120371">4</a></sup> Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120371"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120371">5</a></sup> as well as others, connect it with the operation of grinding corn. Pischel,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120371"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120371">6</a></sup> however, who points out that corn was not ground between two stones, but beaten on a stone with a pestle (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दृषद्">dṛṣad</a></i>), considers that <a class="vei_120a" href="उपल-प्रक्षिणी">upala-prakṣiṇī</a> denotes a woman that assisted at the crushing of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपर">upara</a></b>). Von Schroeder,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120371"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120371">7</a></sup> who more correctly points out that there is no objection to regarding <i><a class="vei_120a" href="उपल">upala</a></i> as the mortar in which the corn was placed and then beaten with the pestle, renders the word literally as ‘one who fills the (lower) stone (with corn).’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120371"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120371">↑</a> ix. 112, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120371"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120371">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120371"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120371">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120371"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120371">↑</a> <i>Ibid., s.v.,</i> ‘fitting the upper (to the lower) millstone.’</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120371"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120371">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 269. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedaschrestomathie, s.v.,</i> who, taking <i>pṛc</i> in the sense of ‘fill,’ explains the compound as ‘filling the upper millstone,’ an interpretation which as it stands is unintelligible.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120371"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120371">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 308-310.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120371"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120371">↑</a> <i>Mysterium und Mimus,</i> 412 <i>et seq.</i> Von Schroeder does not accept the view that the mother of the singer is alluded to; but it seems impossible to draw any other conclusion from the language of the passage, and his own explanation of the word as referring to a corn-mother is very improbable; <i>cf.</i> Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1909, 204.</li></ol></div>

उपला|ఉపలా|ಉಪಲಾ|upalā|upalaa|_vei_120@372|_vei1_pn094
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@371">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@373">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn093">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn094">1:094</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn095">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upalā</b> in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120372"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120372">1</a></sup> may denote the upper and smaller ‘stone,’ which was used as a pestle with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृषद्">dṛṣad</a></b> as the mortar, whereas <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपर">upara</a></b> in the Saṃhitās denotes rather the mortar, and <a class="vei_120a" href="दृषद्">dṛṣad</a> the pestle. But see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृषद्">dṛṣad</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120372"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120372">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 1, 22; ii. 1, 14, 17; ii. 2, 2, 1, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Von Schroeder, <i>Mysterium und Mimus,</i> 413, n. 3.</li></ol></div>

उप-वाक|उपवाक|उप|वाक|ఉప-వాక|ఉపవాక|ఉప|వాక|ಉಪ-ವಾಕ|ಉಪವಾಕ|ಉಪ|ವಾಕ|upa-vāka|upavāka|upa|vāka|upa-vaaka|upavaaka|vaaka|_vei_120@373|_vei1_pn094
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@372">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@374">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn093">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn094">1:094</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn095">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upa-vāka</b> occurs in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120373"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120373">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120373"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120373">2</a></sup> as a description of a species of grain, the <i>Wrightia antidysenterica,</i> known later as <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="यव">yava</a>. The commentator Mahīdhara<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120373"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120373">3</a></sup> simply glosses it with the more general term <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यव">yava</a>.</b>  According to the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, it formed the essential element of gruel (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="करम्भ">karambha</a></i>), and <a class="vei_120a" href="उपवाक">upavāka</a> groats (<i>saktavaḥ</i>) are mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120373"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120373">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120373"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120373">↑</a> xix. 22; 90; xxi. 30 (as ‘healing’).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120373"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120373">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 7, 1, 3; 2, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120373"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120373">↑</a> On Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 22.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120373"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120373">↑</a> xii. 9, 1, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 240, 270.</li></ol></div>

उप-वेशि|उपवेशि|उप|वेशि|ఉప-వేశి|ఉపవేశి|ఉప|వేశి|ಉಪ-ವೇಶಿ|ಉಪವೇಶಿ|ಉಪ|ವೇಶಿ|upa-veśi|upaveśi|upa|veśi|upa-veshi|upaveshi|veshi|_vei_120@374|_vei1_pn095
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@373">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@375">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn094">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn095">1:095</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn096">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upa-veśi</b> is mentioned as a pupil of <b>Kuśri</b> in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 5, 3, in both recensions). See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="औपवेशि">aupaveśi</a>.</b></p></div>

उप-श्री|उप-श्रि|उपश्री|उपश्रि|उप|श्री|श्रि|उप-श्रय|उपश्रय|श्रय|ఉప-శ్రీ|ఉప-శ్రి|ఉపశ్రీ|ఉపశ్రి|ఉప|శ్రీ|శ్రి|ఉప-శ్రయ|ఉపశ్రయ|శ్రయ|ಉಪ-ಶ್ರೀ|ಉಪ-ಶ್ರಿ|ಉಪಶ್ರೀ|ಉಪಶ್ರಿ|ಉಪ|ಶ್ರೀ|ಶ್ರಿ|ಉಪ-ಶ್ರಯ|ಉಪಶ್ರಯ|ಶ್ರಯ|upa-śrī|upa-śri|upaśrī|upaśri|upa|śrī|śri|upa-śraya|upaśraya|śraya|upa-shrii|upa-shri|upashrii|upashri|shrii|shri|upa-shraya|upashraya|shraya|upa-shree|upashree|shree|_vei_120@375|_vei1_pn095
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@374">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@376">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn094">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn095">1:095</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn096">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upa-<a class="vei_120a" href="श्री">śrī</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="उप">upa</a>-śraya,</b> are two readings of the same term. The former is found in one recension of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120375">1</a></sup> while the latter is probably the reading of the other recension of the <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120375">2</a></sup> and certainly the reading in one passage of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120375">3</a></sup> though the text has <i>apaśrayaḥ,</i> which is accepted as possible by Roth.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120375">4</a></sup> In both cases the term clearly means something connected with a couch (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="आसन्दी">āsandī</a></b> in the Atharvaveda, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्यंक">paryaṅka</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>). Aufrecht,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120375">5</a></sup> Roth,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120375">6</a></sup> and Max Müller<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120375">7</a></sup> render it as ‘coverlet’ or ‘cushion,’ but Whitney<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120375">8</a></sup> seems evidently right in holding that it must mean a ‘support’ or something similar.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120375">↑</a> i. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120375">↑</a> See Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 20, n. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120375">↑</a> xv. 3, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney's note in his Translation.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120375">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> and still followed by Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120375">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 131.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120375">↑</a> <i>S.v. <a class="vei_120a" href="अपश्रय">apaśraya</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120375">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books af the East,</i> 1, 278.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120375">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 777.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 402; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 155.</li></ol></div>

उप-स्तरण|उपस्तरण|उप|स्तरण|ఉప-స్తరణ|ఉపస్తరణ|ఉప|స్తరణ|ಉಪ-ಸ್ತರಣ|ಉಪಸ್ತರಣ|ಉಪ|ಸ್ತರಣ|upa-staraṇa|upastaraṇa|upa|staraṇa|upa-staraNa|upastaraNa|staraNa|_vei_120@376|_vei1_pn095
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@375">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@377">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn094">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn095">1:095</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn096">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upa-staraṇa</b> denotes in the description of the couch (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्यंक">paryaṅka</a></b>) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120376">1</a></sup> a ‘coverlet,’ and has this sense, used metaphorically, in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120376">2</a></sup> also. In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120376">3</a></sup> it seems to have the same meaning. Whitney,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120376">4</a></sup> however, renders it ‘couch,’ though he translates<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120376">5</a></sup> the parallel word <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आस्तरण">āstaraṇa</a></b> in another passage<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120376">6</a></sup> by ‘cushion.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120376">↑</a> i. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120376">↑</a> ix. 69, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120376">↑</a> v. 19, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120376">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 254.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120376">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 776.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120376">↑</a> xv. 3, 7.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 403; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 155.</li></ol></div>

उप-स्ति|उपस्ति|उप|स्ति|ఉప-స్తి|ఉపస్తి|ఉప|స్తి|ಉಪ-ಸ್ತಿ|ಉಪಸ್ತಿ|ಉಪ|ಸ್ತಿ|upa-sti|upasti|upa|sti|_vei_120@377|_vei1_pn096
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@376">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@378">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn095">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn096">1:096</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn097">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upa-sti</b> denotes both in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120377">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120377">2</a></sup> a ‘dependent,’ just as later in the Epic<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120377">3</a></sup> the subordination of the <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> to the two superior castes is expressed by the verb <i><a class="vei_120a" href="उप">upa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="स्था">sthā</a>,</i> ‘stand under,’ ‘support.’ The word also appears, with the same sense, in the form of <a class="vei_120a" href="स्ति">sti</a>, but only in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120377">4</a></sup> The exact nature of the dependence connoted by the term is quite uncertain. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120377">5</a></sup> conjectures that the ‘dependents’ were the members of defeated Aryan tribes who became clients of the king, as among the Greeks, Romans, and Germans, the term possibly including persons who had lost their freedom through dicing.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120377">6</a></sup> The evidence of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120377">7</a></sup> shows that among the Upastis were included the chariot-makers (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ-कार">ratha-kāra</a></i>), the smiths (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="तक्षन्">takṣan</a></i>), and the charioteers (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सूत">sūta</a></i>), and troop-leaders (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a>-ṇī</i>), while the Rigveda passages negative the possibility of the ‘subjects’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्ति">sti</a></i>) being the whole people. It is therefore fair to assume that they were the clients proper of the king, not servile, but attached in a special relation to him as opposed to the ordinary population. They may well have included among them not only the classes suggested by Zimmer, but also higher elements, such as refugees from other clans, as well as ambitious men who sought advancement in the royal service. Indeed, the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत">sūta</a> and the Grāmaṇī were, as such, officers of the king's household--kingmakers, not themselves kings, as they are described in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120377">8</a></sup> The use of the word in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120377">9</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120377">10</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a>,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120377">11</a></sup> is purely metaphorical, as well as in the one passage of the Rigveda in which it occurs. In the Paippalāda recension of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120377">12</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a> are referred to as Upastis, perhaps in the general sense of ‘subject.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120377">↑</a> x. 97, 23 (= Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xii. 101; Av. vi. 15, 1).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120377">↑</a> iii. 5, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120377">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 92.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120377">↑</a> vii. 19, 11; x. 148, 4; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्ति">sti</a>-pa,</i> vii. 66, 3; x. 69, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120377">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 184, 185.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120377">↑</a> Rv. x. 34.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120377">↑</a> Av. iii. 5, 6. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120377">↑</a> iii. 5, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120377">↑</a> vii. 2, 5, 4. <i>Cf.</i> vi. 5, 8, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120377">↑</a> iii. 3, 5, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120377">↑</a> xxxi. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120377">↑</a> iii. 5, 7.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 246; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 92; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 196 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

उप-स्तुत|उपस्तुत|उप|स्तुत|ఉప-స్తుత|ఉపస్తుత|ఉప|స్తుత|ಉಪ-ಸ್ತುತ|ಉಪಸ್ತುತ|ಉಪ|ಸ್ತುತ|upa-stuta|upastuta|upa|stuta|_vei_120@378|_vei1_pn097
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@377">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@379">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn096">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn097">1:097</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn098">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upa-stuta</b> is mentioned several times in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120378"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120378">1</a></sup> always as a sage of old, and usually in connexion with <a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a>, who was aided or favoured by <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>, the Aśvins, and other gods. The Upastutas, sons of <a class="vei_120a" href="वृष्टिहव्य">vṛṣṭihavya</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120378"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120378">2</a></sup> are mentioned as singers<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120378"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120378">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120378"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120378">↑</a> i. 36, 10. 17; 112, 15; viii. 5, 25; x. 115, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120378"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120378">↑</a> x. 115, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120378"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120378">↑</a> viii. 103, 8; x. 115, 9.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 108; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 152, 153.</li></ol></div>

उप-ह्वर|उपह्वर|उप|ह्वर|ఉప-హ్వర|ఉపహ్వర|ఉప|హ్వర|ಉಪ-ಹ್ವರ|ಉಪಹ್ವರ|ಉಪ|ಹ್ವರ|upa-hvara|upahvara|upa|hvara|_vei_120@379|_vei1_pn097
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@378">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@380">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn096">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn097">1:097</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn098">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upa-hvara</b> denotes, in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120379"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120379">1</a></sup> according to Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120379"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120379">2</a></sup> the body of a chariot (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="उप">upa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="स्थ">stha</a></i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120379"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120379">↑</a> i. 87, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120379"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120379">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 40.</li></ol></div>

उपानस|ఉపానస|ಉಪಾನಸ|upānasa|upaanasa|_vei_120@380|_vei1_pn097
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@379">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@381">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn096">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn097">1:097</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn098">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upānasa</b> is in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120380"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120380">1</a></sup> opposed to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a>,</b> and must mean something like ‘the body of the wagon,’ though Sāyaṇa suggests that it signifies either a ‘granary’ or a ‘wagon full of grain.’ In the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120380"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120380">2</a></sup> where the word occurs only once, its sense is doubtful. Pischel<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120380"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120380">3</a></sup> explains the form which occurs there not as an adjective, but as an infinitive.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120380"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120380">↑</a> ii. 14, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120380"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120380">↑</a> x. 105, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120380"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120380">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 197.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 301; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 56.</li></ol></div>

उपा-नह्|उपानह्|उपा|नह्|ఉపా-నహ్|ఉపానహ్|ఉపా|నహ్|ಉಪಾ-ನಹ್|ಉಪಾನಹ್|ಉಪಾ|ನಹ್|upā-nah|upānah|upā|nah|upaa-nah|upaanah|upaa|_vei_120@381|_vei1_pn097
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@380">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@382">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn096">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn097">1:097</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn098">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upā-nah</b> is the regular word for a ‘sandal’ or ‘shoe’ from the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120381"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120381">1</a></sup> onwards. Boarskin is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120381"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120381">2</a></sup> as a material of which shoes were made. The combination ‘staff and sandals’ (<i>daṇḍopānaha</i>) occurs as early as the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120381"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120381">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120381"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120381">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 4, 4, 4; 6, 6, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120381"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120381">↑</a> v. 4, 3, 19.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120381"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120381">↑</a> iii. 3.</li></ol></div>

उपावि जान-श्रुतेय|उपावि जानश्रुतेय|उपावि जान|श्रुतेय|ఉపావి జాన-శ్రుతేయ|ఉపావి జానశ్రుతేయ|ఉపావి జాన|శ్రుతేయ|ಉಪಾವಿ ಜಾನ-ಶ್ರುತೇಯ|ಉಪಾವಿ ಜಾನಶ್ರುತೇಯ|ಉಪಾವಿ ಜಾನ|ಶ್ರುತೇಯ|upāvi jāna-śruteya|upāvi jānaśruteya|upāvi jāna|śruteya|upaavi jaana-shruteya|upaavi jaanashruteya|upaavi jaana|shruteya|_vei_120@382|_vei1_pn097
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@381">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@383">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn096">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn097">1:097</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn098">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upāvi <a class="vei_120a" href="जान">jāna</a>-śruteya</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 25, 15) as an authority on the Upasads (a kind of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> ceremony).</p></div>

उपोदिति गौपालेय|ఉపోదితి గౌపాలేయ|ಉಪೋದಿತಿ ಗೌಪಾಲೇಯ|upoditi gaupāleya|upoditi gaupaaleya|_vei_120@383|_vei1_pn097
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@382">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@384">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn096">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn097">1:097</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn098">↷</a><hr><p><b>Upoditi Gaupāleya</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xii. 13, 11) as a seer of Sāmans.</p></div>

उभया-दन्त्|उभयादन्त्|उभया|दन्त्|ఉభయా-దన్త్|ఉభయాదన్త్|ఉభయా|దన్త్|ಉಭಯಾ-ದನ್ತ್|ಉಭಯಾದನ್ತ್|ಉಭಯಾ|ದನ್ತ್|ubhayā-dant|ubhayādant|ubhayā|dant|ubhayaa-dant|ubhayaadant|ubhayaa|_vei_120@384|_vei1_pn097
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@383">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@385">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn096">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn097">1:097</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn098">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ubhayā-dant,</b> ‘having incisors in both jaws,’ is an expression employed to distinguish, among domestic animals, the horse,  the ass, etc., from the goat, the sheep, and cattle. The distinction occurs in a late hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120384">1</a></sup> and is several times alluded to in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120384">2</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120384">3</a></sup> In one passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120384">4</a></sup> man is classed with the horse as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="उभया-दन्त्">ubhayā-dant</a>.</i> The opposite is <i>anyato-<a class="vei_120a" href="दन्त्">dant</a>,</i> ‘having incisors in one jaw only,’ a term regularly applied to cattle,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120384">5</a></sup> the eight incisors of which are, in fact, limited to the lower jaw. The ass is styled <i><a class="vei_120a" href="उभया-दन्त्">ubhayā-dant</a></i> in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120384">6</a></sup> In one passage of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120384">7</a></sup> however, the epithet is applied to a ram; but the sense here is that a marvel occurs, just as in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120384">8</a></sup> a ram destroys a lioness. Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120384">9</a></sup> suggests in the Atharvaveda passage another reading which would mean ‘horse.’ A parallel division of animals is that of the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120384">10</a></sup> and Vājasaneyi Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120384">11</a></sup> into ‘whole-hoofed’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="एक">eka</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="शफ">śapha</a></i>) and ‘small’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षुद्र">kṣudra</a></i>).</p><p>Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120384">12</a></sup> seeks to show from the Greek <sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120384">13</a></sup> and the Latin <i>ambidens</i><sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120384">14</a></sup> that the Indo-European was familiar with the division of the five sacrificial animals into the two classes of man and horse on the one hand, and cattle, sheep, and goats on the other. But this supposition is not necessary.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120384">↑</a> x. 90, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120384">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 6, 3; v. 1, 2, 6; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 8, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120384">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 3, 30 (<i>ubhayato-<a class="vei_120a" href="दन्त्">dant</a></i>).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120384">↑</a> ii. 2, 6, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120384">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 1, 5; v. 1, 2, 6; 5, 1, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120384">↑</a> v. 31, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120384">↑</a> v. 19, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120384">↑</a> viii. 18, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120384">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 434.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120384">↑</a> iv. 3, 10, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120384">↑</a> xiv. 30.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120384">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 74-76.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120384">↑</a> Aristotle, <i>Hist. An.,</i> ii. 1, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120384">↑</a> Festus <i>apud</i> Paulum Diaconum</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 58.</li></ol></div>

उरा|ఉరా|ಉರಾ|urā|uraa|_vei_120@385|_vei1_pn098
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@384">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@386">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn097">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn098">1:098</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn099">↷</a><hr><p><b>Urā</b> as a name for ‘sheep’ is confined to the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120385"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120385">1</a></sup> It is curious that in one of its two occurrences the wolf should be referred to as terrifying sheep, and that the epithet of the wolf, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="उरा">urā</a>-mathi,</i> ‘killing sheep,’ should occur once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120385"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120385">2</a></sup> both references being in one book of the Saṃhitā, a fact which suggests a dialectical origin of the word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="उरा">urā</a>.</i> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अवि">avi</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120385"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120385">↑</a> viii. 34, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120385"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120385">↑</a> viii. 66, 8. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 21.</li></ol></div>

उरु-कक्ष|उरुकक्ष|उरु|कक्ष|ఉరు-కక్ష|ఉరుకక్ష|ఉరు|కక్ష|ಉರು-ಕಕ್ಷ|ಉರುಕಕ್ಷ|ಉರು|ಕಕ್ಷ|uru-kakṣa|urukakṣa|uru|kakṣa|uru-kakSha|urukakSha|kakSha|uru-kaxa|urukaxa|kaxa|_vei_120@386|_vei1_pn099
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@385">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@387">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn098">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn099">1:099</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn101">↷</a><hr><p><b>Uru-kakṣa</b> occurs in only one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120386"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120386">1</a></sup> where the sense of the word is much disputed. The reading of the text is <i>uruḥ kakṣo na gāṅgyaḥ,</i> which may refer to a man, <a class="vei_120a" href="उरुकक्ष">urukakṣa</a>, ‘dwelling on the Ganges,’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120386"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120386">2</a></sup> or to a man, son of <a class="vei_120a" href="गंगा">gaṅgā</a>, or to a wood so called,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120386"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120386">3</a></sup> or may simply denote the ‘broad thicket on the Ganges.’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120386"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120386">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120386"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120386">↑</a> vi. 45, 31.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120386"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120386">↑</a> Grassmann; St. Petersburg Dictionary. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> 291.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120386"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120386">↑</a> Ludwig's translation (<i>der wald <a class="vei_120a" href="उरुकक्ष">urukakṣa</a>,</i> or <i>das weite dickicht</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120386"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120386">↑</a> Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 28, n. 5; Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 396.</li></ol></div>

उरु-क्षय|उरुक्षय|उरु|क्षय|ఉరు-క్షయ|ఉరుక్షయ|ఉరు|క్షయ|ಉರು-ಕ್ಷಯ|ಉರುಕ್ಷಯ|ಉರು|ಕ್ಷಯ|uru-kṣaya|urukṣaya|uru|kṣaya|uru-kShaya|urukShaya|kShaya|uru-xaya|uruxaya|xaya|_vei_120@387|_vei1_pn099
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@386">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@388">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn098">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn099">1:099</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn101">↷</a><hr><p><b>Uru-kṣaya.</b>--A family of Urukṣayas, singers and worshippers of <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>, is referred to in one hymn of the Rigveda (x. 118, 8. 9).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 167.</p></div>

उरुञ्जिरा|ఉరుఞ్జిరా|ಉರುಞ್ಜಿರಾ|uruñjirā|uru~njiraa|_vei_120@388|_vei1_pn099
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@387">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@389">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn098">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn099">1:099</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn101">↷</a><hr><p><b>Uruñjirā</b> is given in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> (ix. 26) as one of the names of the river <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विपाश्">vipāś</a></b> (now Beäs).</p></div>

उर्वरा|ఉర్వరా|ಉರ್ವರಾ|urvarā|urvaraa|_vei_120@389|_vei1_pn099
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@388">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@390">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn098">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn099">1:099</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn101">↷</a><hr><p><b>Urvarā</b> is with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षेत्र">kṣetra</a></b> the regular expression, from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120389">1</a></sup> onwards, denoting a piece of ‘ploughland’ (). Fertile (<i>apnasvatī</i>) fields<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120389">2</a></sup> are spoken of as well as waste fields (<i>ārtanā</i>).<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120389">3</a></sup> Intensive cultivation by means of irrigation is clearly referred to both in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120389">4</a></sup> and in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120389">5</a></sup> while allusion is also made to the use of manure.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120389">6</a></sup> The fields (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षेत्र">kṣetra</a></i>) were carefully measured according to the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120389">7</a></sup> This fact points clearly to individual ownership in land for the plough, a conclusion supported by the reference of Apālā, in a hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120389">8</a></sup> to her father's field (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="उर्वरा">urvarā</a></i>), which is put on the same level as his head of hair as a personal possession. Consistent with this are the epithets ‘winning fields’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="उर्वरा">urvarā</a>-sā, <a class="vei_120a" href="उर्वरा">urvarā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="जित्">jit</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षेत्र">kṣetra</a>-sā</i>),<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120389">9</a></sup> while ‘lord of fields’ used of a god<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120389">10</a></sup> is presumably a transfer of a human epithet (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="उर्वरा">urvarā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a></i>). Moreover, fields are spoken of in the same  connexion as children,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120389">11</a></sup> and the conquest of fields (<i>kṣetrāṇi <a class="vei_120a" href="सं">saṃ</a>-ji</i>) is often referred to in the Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120389">12</a></sup> Very probably, as suggested by Pischel,<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120389">13</a></sup> the ploughland was bounded by grass land (perhaps denoted by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="खिल">khila</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="खिल्य">khilya</a></b>) which in all likelihood would be joint property on the analogy of property elsewhere. There is no trace in Vedic literature of communal property in the sense of ownership by a community of any sort,<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120389">14</a></sup> nor is there mention of communal cultivation. Individual property in land seems also presumed later on. In the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_15_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_120389">15</a></sup> the things given as examples of wealth include fields and houses (<i>āyatanāni</i>). The Greek evidence<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_120389">16</a></sup> also points to individual ownership. The precise nature of the ownership is of course not determined by the expression ‘individual ownership.’ The legal relationship of the head of a family and its members is nowhere explained, and can only be conjectured (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पितृ">pitṛ</a></b>). Very often a family may have lived together with undivided shares in the land. The rules about the inheritance of landed property do not occur before the Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_120389">17</a></sup> In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_18_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_120389">18</a></sup> the giving of land as a fee to priests is mentioned, but with reproof: land was no doubt even then a very special kind of property, not lightly to be given away or parted with.<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_120389">19</a></sup> </p><p>On the relation of the owners of land to the king and others see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a>;</b> on its cultivation see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृषि">kṛṣi</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120389">↑</a> i. 127, 6; iv. 41, 6; v. 33, 4; vi. 25, 4; x. 30, 3; 142, 3, etc.; Av. x. 6, 33; 10, 8; xiv. 2, 14, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120389">↑</a> Rv. i. 127, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120389">↑</a> <i>Ibid.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120389">↑</a> vii. 49, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120389">↑</a> i. 6, 4; xix. 2, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120389">↑</a> Av. iii. 14, 3, 4; xix. 31, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120389">↑</a> i. 110, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120389">↑</a> viii. 91, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120389">↑</a> Rv. iv. 38, 1, and vi. 20, 1; ii. 21, 1; iv. 38, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120389">↑</a> viii. 21, 3. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षेत्र">kṣetra</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120389">↑</a> Rv. iv. 41, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120389">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 2, 8, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, v. 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 12, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120389">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 204-207.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120389">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Baden Powell, <i>Indian Village Community</i> (1899); Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 236; Mrs. Rhys Davids, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1901, 860.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_120389">↑</a> vii. 24, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_120389">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Diodorus, ii. 40; Arrian, <i>Indica,</i> 11; Strabo, p. 703; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 87 <i>et seq. Cf. ibid.,</i> 20, 22, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_120389">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xviii. 5 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 2, 3; Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 6, 14. Of course, the rules probably go back to the earlier period, but how far it is impossible to say. With the settlement of the country, however, inheritance of land and its partition must have become inevitable.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_120389">↑</a> xiii. 6, 2, 18; 7, 1, 13. 15.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_120389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_120389">↑</a> It is significant that in the famous episode (<a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 1, 9, 4) of Manu's division of his property, from which <a class="vei_120a" href="नाभानेदिष्ठ">nābhānediṣṭha</a> was excluded, this exclusion is made good by the son's obtaining cattle (<i>paśavaḥ</i>). It is clear that cattle, not land, was the real foundation of wealth, just as in Ireland, Italy (<i>cf. pecunia</i>), Greece, etc. Cattle could be, and were, used individually, but land was not open to a man's free disposal; no doubt, at any rate, the consent of the family or the community might be required, but we are reduced to reliance on analogy in view of the silence of the texts. <i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 289; Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 94-96; Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India,</i> 48 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

उर्वारू|ఉర్వారూ|ಉರ್ವಾರೂ|urvārū|urvaaruu|urvaaroo|_vei_120@390|_vei1_pn101
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@389">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@391">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn099">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn101">1:101</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn102">↷</a><hr><p><b>Urvārū,</b> f., <b>Urvāruka,</b> n., ‘cucumber.’ The former<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120390"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120390">1</a></sup> of these words denotes the plant, the latter<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120390"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120390">2</a></sup> the fruit, but both are very rare. The passages all seem to refer to the fact that the stem of the plant becomes loosened when the fruit is ripe.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120390"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120390">3</a></sup> The fruit is also called Urvāru in a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120390"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120390">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120390"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120390">↑</a> Av. vi. 14, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120390"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120390">↑</a> Rv. vii. 59, 12 = Av. xiv. 1, 17 = Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 10, 4 = <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 6, 2 = Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iii. 60.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120390"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120390">↑</a> Sāyaṇa on Av. vi. 14, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120390"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120390">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 2. 19.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 242.</li></ol></div>

उल|ఉల|ಉಲ|ula|_vei_120@391|_vei1_pn101
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@390">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@392">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn099">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn101">1:101</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn102">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ula</b> is the name of some unknown wild animal, perhaps, as Whitney<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120391"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120391">1</a></sup> suggests, the ‘jackal.’ It is mentioned in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120391"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120391">2</a></sup> and later Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120391"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120391">3</a></sup> but not definitely identified by the commentators.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120391"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120391">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 669.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120391"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120391">↑</a> xii. 1, 49.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120391"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120391">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 12, 1 (as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऊल">ūla</a></i>); Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 13, 12; 14, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 31. <i>Cf. ulala</i> in <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 82.</li></ol></div>

उल वार्ष्णि-वृद्ध|उल वार्ष्णिवृद्ध|उल वार्ष्णि|वृद्ध|ఉల వార్ష్ణి-వృద్ధ|ఉల వార్ష్ణివృద్ధ|ఉల వార్ష్ణి|వృద్ధ|ಉಲ ವಾರ್ಷ್ಣಿ-ವೃದ್ಧ|ಉಲ ವಾರ್ಷ್ಣಿವೃದ್ಧ|ಉಲ ವಾರ್ಷ್ಣಿ|ವೃದ್ಧ|ula vārṣṇi-vṛddha|ula vārṣṇivṛddha|ula vārṣṇi|vṛddha|ula vaarShNi-vRddha|ula vaarShNivRddha|ula vaarShNi|vRddha|ula vaarShNi-vRiddha|ula vaarShNivRiddha|vRiddha|ula vaarShNi-vRuddha|ula vaarShNivRuddha|vRuddha|ula vaarShNi-vRRiddha|ula vaarShNivRRiddha|vRRiddha|_vei_120@392|_vei1_pn101
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@391">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@393">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn099">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn101">1:101</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn102">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ula <a class="vei_120a" href="वार्ष्णि">vārṣṇi</a>-vṛddha</b> is mentioned as a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vii. 4).</p></div>

उलप|ఉలప|ಉಲಪ|ulapa|_vei_120@393|_vei1_pn101
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@392">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@394">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn099">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn101">1:101</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn102">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ulapa</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120393"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120393">1</a></sup> is the name of a species of grass referred to in the Rigveda and the later Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120393"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120393">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120393"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120393">↑</a> x. 142, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120393"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120393">↑</a> Av. vii. 66, 1. Adjectives derived from the word are <i>ulapya</i> (Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 45, etc.) and <i>upolapa</i> (Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 7, 2).</li></ol></div>

उलुक्य जान-श्रुतेय|उलुक्य जानश्रुतेय|उलुक्य जान|श्रुतेय|ఉలుక్య జాన-శ్రుతేయ|ఉలుక్య జానశ్రుతేయ|ఉలుక్య జాన|శ్రుతేయ|ಉಲುಕ್ಯ ಜಾನ-ಶ್ರುತೇಯ|ಉಲುಕ್ಯ ಜಾನಶ್ರುತೇಯ|ಉಲುಕ್ಯ ಜಾನ|ಶ್ರುತೇಯ|ulukya jāna-śruteya|ulukya jānaśruteya|ulukya jāna|śruteya|ulukya jaana-shruteya|ulukya jaanashruteya|ulukya jaana|shruteya|_vei_120@394|_vei1_pn101
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@393">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@395">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn099">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn101">1:101</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn102">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ulukya <a class="vei_120a" href="जान">jāna</a>-śruteya</b> is mentioned as a teacher in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 6, 3). </p></div>

उलूक|ఉలూక|ಉಲೂಕ|ulūka|uluuka|ulooka|_vei_120@395|_vei1_pn102
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@394">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@396">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn101">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn102">1:102</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn103">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ulūka</b> is the ordinary word for ‘owl’ from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120395">1</a></sup> onwards. The bird was noted for its cry,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120395">2</a></sup> and was deemed the harbinger of ill-fortune (<i>nairṛta</i>).<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120395">3</a></sup> Owls were offered at the horse sacrifice to the forest trees,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120395">4</a></sup> no doubt because they roosted there.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120395">↑</a> x. 165, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120395">↑</a> Rv. <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120395">↑</a> Av. vi. 19, 2: <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 18, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 38.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120395">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Samhitā, xxiv. 23; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14. 4.</li></ol></div>

उलूखल|ఉలూఖల|ಉಲೂಖಲ|ulūkhala|uluukhala|ulookhala|_vei_120@396|_vei1_pn102
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@395">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@397">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn101">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn102">1:102</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn103">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ulūkhala</b> is the regular expression for ‘mortar’ from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120396"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120396">1</a></sup> onwards, occurring frequently also in the compound<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120396"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120396">2</a></sup> Ulūkala-<a class="vei_120a" href="मुसल">musala</a>, ‘mortar and pestle.’ The exact construction of the vessel is quite unkown till we reach the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> period.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120396"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120396">↑</a> i. 28, 6; Av. x. 9, 26; xi. 3, 3; xii. 3, 13: <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 2, 8, 7; vii. 2, 1, 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 4, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120396"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120396">↑</a> Av. ix. 6, 15; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 1, 22</li></ol></div>

उल्का|ఉల్కా|ಉಲ್ಕಾ|ulkā|ulkaa|_vei_120@397|_vei1_pn102
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@396">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@398">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn101">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn102">1:102</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn103">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ulkā</b> regularly denotes a meteor from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120397"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120397">1</a></sup> onwards. In the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120397"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120397">2</a></sup> it also signifies a ‘firebrand.’ The much rarer form Ulkuṣī<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120397"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120397">3</a></sup> has both senses.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120397"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120397">↑</a> iv. 4, 2; x. 68, 4; Av. xix. 9, 8, Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120397"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120397">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 4, 19.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120397"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120397">↑</a> As ‘meteor,’ Av. v. 17, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 2, 7, 21; as ‘firebrand,’ <i>ibid.,</i> iii. 9, 2, 9.</li></ol></div>

उल्मुक|ఉల్ముక|ಉಲ್ಮುಕ|ulmuka|_vei_120@398|_vei1_pn102
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@397">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@399">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn101">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn102">1:102</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn103">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ulmuka</b> is the common word in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120398">1</a></sup> for ‘firebrand,’ from which a coal (<i>aṅgāra</i>)<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120398">2</a></sup> could be taken.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120398">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 11; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 2, 1; ii. 1, 4, 28, etc.; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 76 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 239)</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120398">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 4, 3, 3; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 61, 1 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 23, 342).</li></ol></div>

उल्मुकवक्षयण|ఉల్ముకవక్షయణ|ಉಲ್ಮುಕವಕ್ಷಯಣ|ulmukavakṣayaṇa|ulmukavakShayaNa|ulmukavaxayaNa|_vei_120@399|_vei1_pn102
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@398">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@400">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn101">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn102">1:102</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn103">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ulmukavakṣayaṇa</b> is an expression that occurs several times in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120399">1</a></sup> signifying a ‘means of extinguishing (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अव">ava</a>-kṣayaṇa</i>) a firebrand,’ or possibly more precisely ‘tongs.’ Compare <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अंगारावक्षयण">aṅgārāvakṣayaṇa</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120399">↑</a> iv. 6. 8, 7; v. 2, 4, 15; xi. 6, 3, 3; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 76. <i>Cf.</i> Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, s.v.</li></ol></div>

उशनस् काव्य|ఉశనస్ కావ్య|ಉಶನಸ್ ಕಾವ್ಯ|uśanas kāvya|ushanas kaavya|_vei_120@400|_vei1_pn103
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@399">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@401">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn102">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn103">1:103</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn104">↷</a><hr><p><b>Uśanas Kāvya</b> is an ancient seer, already a half-mythical figure in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120400">1</a></sup> where he is often mentioned, especially as associated with <a class="vei_120a" href="कुत्स">kutsa</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>. Later on<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120400">2</a></sup> he becomes the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> of the Asuras in their contests with the gods. A variant of his name is Kavi Uśanas.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120400">3</a></sup> He appears in the Brāhmaṇas as a teacher also.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120400">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120400">↑</a> i. 51, 10; 83. 5; 121, 12; iv. 16, 2; vi. 20, 11; viii. 23, 17; ix. 87, 3; 97, 7; x. 40, 7; probably also i. 130, 9; v. 31, 8; 34. 2; viii. 7, 26; x. 22, 6. Also in Av. iv. 29, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120400">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 8, 5: <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 5, 20; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiv. 27, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120400">↑</a> Rv. iv. 26, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120400">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 12, 5; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>. ii. 7. 2, 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 167 <i>et seq.;</i> Bergaingne, <i>Religiou Vidique,</i> 2, 339. <i>et seq.;</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 147.</li></ol></div>

उशना|ఉశనా|ಉಶನಾ|uśanā|ushanaa|_vei_120@401|_vei1_pn103
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@400">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@402">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn102">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn103">1:103</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn104">↷</a><hr><p><b>Uśanā</b> occurs in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 4, 3, 13; iv. 2, 5, 15) as the name of a plant from which <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> was prepared.</p></div>

उशीनर|ఉశీనర|ಉಶೀನರ|uśīnara|ushiinara|usheenara|_vei_120@402|_vei1_pn103
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@401">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@403">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn102">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn103">1:103</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn104">↷</a><hr><p><b>Uśīnara</b>--In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Brāhmaṇā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120402">1</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas are mentioned as dwelling together in the ‘Middle Country’ with the Vaśas and the Uśīnaras. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120402">2</a></sup> also the Uśīnaras are associated with the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas and Vaśas, but in the Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120402">3</a></sup> the Uśīnaras and Vaśas are regarded as northerners. In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120402">4</a></sup> the people is alluded to in one passage by reference to their queen, Uśīnarāṇī. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120402">5</a></sup> thinks that the Uśīnaras earlier lived farther to the north-west, but for this there is no clear evidence. His theory is based merely on the fact that the Anukramaṇī (Index) of the Rigveda ascribes one hymn<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120402">6</a></sup> to <a class="vei_120a" href="शिबि">śibi</a> Auśīnara, and that the Śibis were known to Alexander's followers as ,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120402">7</a></sup> living between the Indus and the Akesines (Chenab). But this is in no way conclusive, as the Śibis, at any rate in Epic times,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120402">8</a></sup> occupied the land to the north of <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>, and there is no reason whatever to show that in the Vedic period the Uśīnaras were farther west than the ‘Middle Country.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120402">↑</a> viii. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120402">↑</a> iv. 1. See Keith <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āranyaka,</i> 36.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120402">↑</a> ii 9.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120402">↑</a> x. 59, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120402">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 130.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120402">↑</a> Rv. x. 179.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120402">↑</a> Diodorus, xvii. 19.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120402">↑</a> See Pargiter's map. <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908. p. 322.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 213, 419; Hultzsch <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 34, 179.</li></ol></div>

उष|ఉష|ಉಷ|uṣa|uSha|_vei_120@403|_vei1_pn104
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@402">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@404">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn103">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn104">1:104</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn105">↷</a><hr><p><b>Uṣa,</b> ‘salt ground,’ occurs as a variant of <a class="vei_120a" href="ऊष">ūṣa</a> in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā (i. 6, 3).</p></div>

उषस्त चाक्रायण|ఉషస్త చాక్రాయణ|ಉಷಸ್ತ ಚಾಕ್ರಾಯಣ|uṣasta cākrāyaṇa|uShasta chaakraayaNa|_vei_120@404|_vei1_pn104
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@403">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@405">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn103">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn104">1:104</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn105">↷</a><hr><p><b>Uṣasta Cākrāyaṇa</b> is mentioned as a teacher in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka (iii. 5, 1) and Chāndogya (i. 10, 1; 11, 1) Upaniṣads, the name in the latter work appearing as Uṣasti.</p></div>

उष्टि|उष्ट्र|ఉష్టి|ఉష్ట్ర|ಉಷ್ಟಿ|ಉಷ್ಟ್ರ|uṣṭi|uṣṭra|uShTi|uShTra|_vei_120@405|_vei1_pn104
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@404">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@406">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn103">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn104">1:104</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn105">↷</a><hr><p><b>Uṣṭi, Uṣṭra.</b>--Both of these words, of which the former is quite rare,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120405">1</a></sup> must have the same sense. Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120405">2</a></sup> and Aufrecht<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120405">3</a></sup> hold that in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120405">4</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120405">5</a></sup> the sense is ‘humped bull’ or ‘buffalo,’ but the former thinks that in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120405">6</a></sup> the sense is doubtful, and ‘camel’ may be meant. Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120405">7</a></sup> is decidedly of opinion that the sense in every case is ‘camel.’ The animal was used as a beast of burden yoked in fours.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120405">8</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120405">↑</a> Perhaps in Rv. x. 106, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 6, 21, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120405">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120405">↑</a> Cited in Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 468. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 224.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120405">↑</a> i. 138, 2; viii. 5, 37; 6, 48; 46, 22. 31; Av. xx. 127, 2; 132, 13; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiii. 50.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120405">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, 1, 2, 3, 9, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120405">↑</a> xxiv. 28. 39.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120405">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 83.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120405">↑</a> Av. xx. 127, 2; Rv. viii. 6, 48.</li></ol></div>

उष्णीष|ఉష్ణీష|ಉಷ್ಣೀಷ|uṣṇīṣa|uShNiiSha|uShNeeSha|_vei_120@406|_vei1_pn104
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@405">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@407">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn103">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn104">1:104</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn105">↷</a><hr><p><b>Uṣṇīṣa</b> denotes the ‘turban’ worn by Vedic Indians, men and women<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120406">1</a></sup> alike. The Vrātya's turban is expressly referred to in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120406">2</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120406">3</a></sup> A turban was also worn at the <a class="vei_120a" href="वाजपेय">vājapeya</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120406">4</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120406">5</a></sup> ceremonies by the king as a token of his position.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120406">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 1; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 2, 3; iv. 5, 2, 7 (used at the sacrifice to wrap the embryo in); xiv. 2, 1, 8 (Indrāṇī's <a class="vei_120a" href="उष्णीष">uṣṇīṣa</a>), etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xiii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120406">↑</a> xv. 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120406">↑</a> xvii. 1, 14. <i>Cf.</i> xvi. 6, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120406">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 5, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120406">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 4, 3.</li></ol></div>

उष्यल|ఉష్యల|ಉಷ್ಯಲ|uṣyala|uShyala|_vei_120@407|_vei1_pn104
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@406">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@408">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn103">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn104">1:104</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn105">↷</a><hr><p><b>Uṣyala</b> occurs once in the description of the couch or the bridal car in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120407"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120407">1</a></sup> where it seems to mean the four ‘frame-pieces.’ The form is doubtful: <i>uṣpala</i> is possible.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120407"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120407">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120407"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120407">↑</a> xiv. 1, 60.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120407"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120407">↑</a> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 385.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 155; Whitney, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 752.</li></ol></div>

उस्र|ఉస్ర|ಉಸ್ರ|usra|_vei_120@408|_vei1_pn105
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@407">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@409">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn104">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn105">1:105</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn106">↷</a><hr><p><b>Usra,</b> m.; <b>Usrā,</b> f.; <b>Usrika,</b> m.; <b>Usriya,</b> m.; <b>Usriyā,</b> f. All these words denote a ‘bull’ or a ‘cow,’ occurring frequently in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120408">1</a></sup> and sometimes later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120408">2</a></sup> but usually with some reference to the morning light. In some passages the sense is doubtful. See <b>Go.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120408">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="उस्र">usra</a>,</i> Rv. vi. 12, 4; <i>usrā,</i> i. 3, 8; viii. 75, 8; 96, 8; ix. 58, 2, etc.; <i>usrika,</i> i. 190, 5; <i>usriya,</i> v. 58, 6 (with <i>vṛṣabhaḥ</i>); ix. 74. 3; <i>usriyā,</i> i. 153, 4; 180, 3; ii. 40, 2, etc. In ix. 70, 6, <i>usriya</i> is applied to a calf; and in ix. 68, 1; 93, 2, <i>usriyā</i> means ‘milk.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120408">↑</a> <i>Usrau dhūrṣāhau,</i> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iv. 33; <i>usrā,</i> Av. xii. 3, 37; <i>usriya,</i> Av. i. 12, 1; <i>usriyā,</i> Av. ix. 4, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxv. 2. 3. In Av. v. 20, 1; 28, 3, it means ‘cowhide,’ or perhaps in v. 28, 3, ‘milk.’</li></ol></div>

ऊर्जयन्त् औपमन्यव|ఊర్జయన్త్ ఔపమన్యవ|ಊರ್ಜಯನ್ತ್ ಔಪಮನ್ಯವ|ūrjayant aupamanyava|uurjayant aupamanyava|oorjayant aupamanyava|_vei_120@409|_vei1_pn105
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@408">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@410">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn104">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn105">1:105</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn106">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ūrjayant Aupamanyava</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120409"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120409">1</a></sup> as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भानुमन्त् औपमन्यव">bhānumant aupamanyava</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120409"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120409">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372.</li></ol></div>

ऊर्जयन्ती|ऊर्जयन्ति|ఊర్జయన్తీ|ఊర్జయన్తి|ಊರ್ಜಯನ್ತೀ|ಊರ್ಜಯನ್ತಿ|ūrjayantī|ūrjayanti|uurjayantii|uurjayanti|oorjayantee|oorjayanti|_vei_120@410|_vei1_pn105
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@409">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@411">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn104">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn105">1:105</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn106">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ūrjayantī</b> is regarded by Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120410"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120410">1</a></sup> in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120410"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120410">2</a></sup> as the name of a fort, the stronghold of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नार्मर">nārmara</a>.</b> The verse is, however, quite unintelligible.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120410"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120410">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120410"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120410">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 152.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120410"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120410">↑</a> ii. 13, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120410"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120410">↑</a> Sāyaṇa makes Urjayantī a Piśācī; Grassmann, the sun; Roth, <i>s.v. ūrjay,</i> takes it adjectivally. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 199.</li></ol></div>

ऊर्जव्य|ఊర్జవ్య|ಊರ್ಜವ್ಯ|ūrjavya|uurjavya|oorjavya|_vei_120@411|_vei1_pn105
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@410">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@412">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn104">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn105">1:105</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn106">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ūrjavya,</b> a word occurring only once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120411">1</a></sup> is taken by Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120411">2</a></sup> to be the name of a sacrificer. Roth,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120411">3</a></sup> however, regards the word as an adjective meaning ‘rich in strength,’ and this is the more probable interpretation.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120411">↑</a> v. 41, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120411">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 155.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120411">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

ऊर्ण-नाभी|ऊर्ण-नाभि|ऊर्णनाभी|ऊर्णनाभि|ऊर्ण|नाभी|नाभि|ఊర్ణ-నాభీ|ఊర్ణ-నాభి|ఊర్ణనాభీ|ఊర్ణనాభి|ఊర్ణ|నాభీ|నాభి|ಊರ್ಣ-ನಾಭೀ|ಊರ್ಣ-ನಾಭಿ|ಊರ್ಣನಾಭೀ|ಊರ್ಣನಾಭಿ|ಊರ್ಣ|ನಾಭೀ|ನಾಭಿ|ūrṇa-nābhī|ūrṇa-nābhi|ūrṇanābhī|ūrṇanābhi|ūrṇa|nābhī|nābhi|uurNa-naabhii|uurNa-naabhi|uurNanaabhii|uurNanaabhi|uurNa|naabhii|naabhi|oorNa-naabhee|oorNa-naabhi|oorNanaabhee|oorNanaabhi|oorNa|naabhee|_vei_120@412|_vei1_pn105
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@411">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@413">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn104">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn105">1:105</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn106">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ūrṇa-nābhī,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120412"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120412">1</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऊर्ण">ūrṇa</a>-vābhi, <a class="vei_120a" href="ऊर्णा">ūrṇā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वन्त्">vant</a></b> are all names of the spider in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas in allusion to the insect's spinning threads of wool, as it were. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120412"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120412">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऊर्ण-नाभि">ūrṇa-nābhi</a></i> (‘having wool in its navel’), <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 2, 5; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 1, 23; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऊर्ण">ūrṇa</a>-vābhī</i> (‘wool-spinner’), <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, viii. 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 5, 1, 23; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऊर्णा">ūrṇā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वन्त्">vant</a></i> (‘possessing wool’), <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xix. 3 (in a <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a>).</li></ol></div>

ऊर्णा|ఊర్ణా|ಊರ್ಣಾ|ūrṇā|uurNaa|oorNaa|_vei_120@413|_vei1_pn106
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@412">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@414">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn105">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn106">1:106</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn107">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ūrṇā,</b> ‘wool,’ is very frequently mentioned from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120413">1</a></sup> onwards. The <a class="vei_120a" href="परुष्णी">paruṣṇī</a> country was famous for its wool,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120413">2</a></sup> like <a class="vei_120a" href="गन्धार">gandhāra</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120413">3</a></sup> for its sheep. The term for the separate tufts was <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्वन्">parvan</a></i><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120413">4</a></sup> or <i><a class="vei_120a" href="परुस्">parus</a>.</i><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120413">5</a></sup> ‘Soft as wool’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऊर्ण">ūrṇa</a>-mradas</i>)<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120413">6</a></sup> is not a rare epithet. The sheep is called ‘woolly’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऊर्णावती">ūrṇāvatī</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120413">7</a></sup> ‘Woollen thread’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऊर्णा">ūrṇā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a></i>) is repeatedly referred to in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120413">8</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120413">9</a></sup> The word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऊर्णा">ūrṇā</a></i> was not restricted to the sense of sheep's wool,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120413">10</a></sup> but might denote goat's hair also.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120413">11</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120413">↑</a> iv. 22, 2; v. 52, 9; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 5, 1, 13: 7, 2, 10, etc.; <i>ūrṇāyu,</i> ‘woolly,’ Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiii. 50; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 11, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120413">↑</a> Rv., <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 210. But <i>cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 315.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120413">↑</a> Rv. i. 126, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120413">↑</a> Rv. iv. 22, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120413">↑</a> Rv. ix. 15, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120413">↑</a> Rv. v. 5, 4; x. 18, 10; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ii. 2: iv. 10; xxi. 33, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120413">↑</a> Rv. viii. 56, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120413">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 11, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Samhitā, xxxviii. 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 80, etc. <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="ऊर्णा">ūrṇā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="स्तुका">stukā</a>,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 28; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxv. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120413">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 7, 2, 11, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120413">↑</a> <i>Cf. anaiḍakīr ūrṇāḥ</i> (‘wool not coming from the <i>eḍaha,</i>’ a species of sheep) in Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 5 2, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120413">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 83, n.</li></ol></div>

ऊर्णा-वती|ऊर्णा-वति|ऊर्णावती|ऊर्णावति|ऊर्णा|वती|वति|ఊర్ణా-వతీ|ఊర్ణా-వతి|ఊర్ణావతీ|ఊర్ణావతి|ఊర్ణా|వతీ|వతి|ಊರ್ಣಾ-ವತೀ|ಊರ್ಣಾ-ವತಿ|ಊರ್ಣಾವತೀ|ಊರ್ಣಾವತಿ|ಊರ್ಣಾ|ವತೀ|ವತಿ|ūrṇā-vatī|ūrṇā-vati|ūrṇāvatī|ūrṇāvati|ūrṇā|vatī|vati|uurNaa-vatii|uurNaa-vati|uurNaavatii|uurNaavati|uurNaa|vatii|oorNaa-vatee|oorNaa-vati|oorNaavatee|oorNaavati|oorNaa|vatee|_vei_120@414|_vei1_pn106
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@413">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@415">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn105">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn106">1:106</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn107">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ūrṇā-vatī.</b>--In the hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120414"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120414">1</a></sup> which celebrates the rivers Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120414"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120414">2</a></sup> finds a reference to an affluent of the Indus called <a class="vei_120a" href="ऊर्णावती">ūrṇāvatī</a>. This interpretation, however, seems certainly wrong. Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120414"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120414">3</a></sup> renders the word merely as ‘woolly,’ and Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120414"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120414">4</a></sup> rejects Ludwig's explanation on the ground that it throws the structure of the hymn into confusion. Pischel<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120414"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120414">5</a></sup> makes the word an epithet of the Indus, ‘rich in sheep.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120414"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120414">↑</a> x. 75. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120414"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120414">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3. 200.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120414"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120414">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120414"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120414">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben.</i> 429.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120414"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120414">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 210.</li></ol></div>

ऊर्दर|ఊర్దర|ಊರ್ದರ|ūrdara|uurdara|oordara|_vei_120@415|_vei1_pn106
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@414">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@416">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn105">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn106">1:106</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn107">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ūrdara.</b>--This word occurs once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120415">1</a></sup> when reference is made to filling <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> with <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> as one fills an  <a class="vei_120a" href="ऊर्दर">ūrdara</a> with grain (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="यव">yava</a></b>). Sāyaṇa renders it granāry, but Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120415">2</a></sup> and Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120415">3</a></sup> seem more correct in simply making it a measure for holding grain, or ‘garner.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120415">↑</a> ii. 14, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120415">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120415">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 238.</li></ol></div>

ऊल|ఊల|ಊಲ|ūla|uula|oola|_vei_120@416|_vei1_pn107
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@415">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@417">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn106">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn107">1:107</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn108">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ūla</b> is a variant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उल">ula</a>.</b></p></div>

ऊष|ఊష|ಊಷ|ūṣa|uuSha|ooSha|_vei_120@417|_vei1_pn107
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@416">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@418">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn106">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn107">1:107</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn108">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ūṣa</b> in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120417"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120417">1</a></sup> and Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120417"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120417">2</a></sup> denotes salt ground suited for cattle. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उष">uṣa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120417"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120417">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 2, 3, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120417"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120417">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 27; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 2, 1, 16, etc.</li></ol></div>

ऋक्ष|ऋक्ष|ఋక్ష_౧|ఋక్ష|ಋಕ್ಷ_೧|ಋಕ್ಷ|ṛkṣa_1|ṛkṣa|RkSha_1|RkSha|RikSha_1|RikSha|RukSha_1|RukSha|RRikSha_1|RRikSha|Rxa_1|Rxa|_vei_120@418|_vei1_pn107
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@417">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@419">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn106">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn107">1:107</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn108">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Ṛkṣa,</b> ‘bear,’ is found only once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120418"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120418">1</a></sup> and seldom later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120418"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120418">2</a></sup> the animal having evidently been scarce in the regions occupied by the Vedic Indians. Not more frequent<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120418"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120418">3</a></sup> is the use of the word in the plural to denote the ‘seven bears,’ later called the ‘seven <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषिस्">ṛṣis</a>,’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120418"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120418">4</a></sup> the constellation of the ‘Great Bear’ (, <i>ursa</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120418"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120418">↑</a> v. 56, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120418"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120418">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 17; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 36; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 184. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 81.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120418"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120418">↑</a> Rv. i. 24, 10; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>. ii. 1, 2, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 11, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 422.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120418"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120418">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 144 (D).</li></ol></div>

ऋक्ष|ऋक्ष|ఋక్ష_౨|ఋక్ష|ಋಕ್ಷ_೨|ಋಕ್ಷ|ṛkṣa_2|ṛkṣa|RkSha_2|RkSha|RikSha_2|RikSha|RukSha_2|RukSha|RRikSha_2|RRikSha|Rxa_2|Rxa|_vei_120@419|_vei1_pn107
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@418">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@420">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn106">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn107">1:107</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn108">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Ṛkṣa</b> is the name of a patron mentioned in one verse of a Dānastuti (‘Praise of Gifts’) in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120419"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120419">1</a></sup> his son being referred to in the next verse as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आर्क्ष">ārkṣa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120419"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120419">↑</a> viii. 68, 15. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 163.</li></ol></div>

ऋक्षाका|ఋక్షాకా|ಋಕ್ಷಾಕಾ|ṛkṣākā|RkShaakaa|RikShaakaa|RukShaakaa|RRikShaakaa|Rxaakaa|_vei_120@420|_vei1_pn107
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@419">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@421">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn106">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn107">1:107</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn108">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ṛkṣākā</b> is a word occurring once in an obscure passage of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120420"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120420">1</a></sup> The sense is quite unknown. Weber<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120420"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120420">2</a></sup> thinks it refers to the ‘milky way,’ but his view rests on no evidence. Whitney<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120420"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120420">3</a></sup> despairs of the passage. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120420"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120420">↑</a> xviii. 2, 31.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120420"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120420">↑</a> <i>Festgruss an Roth,</i> 138, n. 2; Berlin Catalogue, 2, 59, 11.; <i>Proccedings of the Berlin Academy,</i> 1895, 856.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120420"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120420">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 840.</li></ol></div>

ऋक्षीका|ఋక్షీకా|ಋಕ್ಷೀಕಾ|ṛkṣīkā|RkShiikaa|RkSheekaa|RikShiikaa|RukShiikaa|RRikShiikaa|Rxiikaa|_vei_120@421|_vei1_pn108
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@420">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@422">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn107">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn108">1:108</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn109">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ṛkṣīkā,</b> a word found in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120421"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120421">1</a></sup> the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120421"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120421">2</a></sup> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120421"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120421">3</a></sup> appears to denote a demon. Harisvāmin, however, in his commentary on the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, connects the word with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋक्ष">ṛkṣa</a>,</b> as meaning ‘bear.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120421"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120421">↑</a> xii. 1, 49.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120421"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120421">↑</a> xxx. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120421"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120421">↑</a> xiii. 2, 4, 2. 4. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 307.</li></ol></div>

ऋग्-वेद|ऋग्वेद|ऋग्|वेद|ఋగ్-వేద|ఋగ్వేద|ఋగ్|వేద|ಋಗ್-ವೇದ|ಋಗ್ವೇದ|ಋಗ್|ವೇದ|ṛg-veda|ṛgveda|ṛg|veda|Rg-veda|Rgveda|Rg|Rig-veda|Rigveda|Rig|Rug-veda|Rugveda|Rug|RRig-veda|RRigveda|RRig|_vei_120@422|_vei1_pn108
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@421">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@423">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn107">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn108">1:108</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn109">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ṛg-veda,</b> the formal name of the collection of Ṛcs, first appears in the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120422"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120422">1</a></sup> and thereafter frequently in the Āraṇyakas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120422"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120422">2</a></sup> and Upaniṣads.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120422"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120422">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120422"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120422">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 32, and implied in <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 12, 9, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 5, 4, 6; 8, 3; xii. 3, 4, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120422"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120422">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 2, 3. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, viii. 3. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120422"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120422">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 5, 12; ii. 4, 10; iv. 1, 6; 5, 11; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 3, 7; iii. 1, 2. 3; 15, 7; vii. 1, 2. 4; 2, 1; 7, 1.</li></ol></div>

ऋजिश्वन्|ఋజిశ్వన్|ಋಜಿಶ್ವನ್|ṛjiśvan|Rjishvan|Rijishvan|Rujishvan|RRijishvan|_vei_120@423|_vei1_pn108
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@422">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@424">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn107">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn108">1:108</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn109">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ṛjiśvan</b> is mentioned several times in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120423">1</a></sup> but always in a vague manner, as if very ancient. He assists <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> in fights against demoniac figures like <a class="vei_120a" href="पिप्रु">pipru</a> and the dusky brood (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण">kṛṣṇa</a>-garbhāḥ</i>). According to Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120423">2</a></sup> he was called Auśija's son,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120423">3</a></sup> but this is doubtful. He is twice<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120423">4</a></sup> clearly called <a class="vei_120a" href="वैदथिन">vaidathina</a>, or descendant of Vidathin.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120423">↑</a> i. 51, 5; 53, 8; 101, 1; vi. 20, 7; viii. 49, 10; x. 99, 11; 138, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120423">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 143, 149.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120423">↑</a> Rv. x. 99, 11. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="औशिज">auśija</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120423">↑</a> Rv. iv. 16, 13; v. 29, 11. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 161 (C).</li></ol></div>

ऋजूनस्|ऋजून|ఋజూనస్|ఋజూన|ಋಜೂನಸ್|ಋಜೂನ|ṛjūnas|ṛjūna|Rjuunas|Rjuuna|Rjoonas|Rjoona|Rijuunas|Rijuuna|Rujuunas|Rujuuna|RRijuunas|RRijuuna|_vei_120@424|_vei1_pn108
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@423">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@425">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn107">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn108">1:108</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn109">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ṛjūnas</b> is mentioned once only in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120424"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120424">1</a></sup> along with six other <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> sacrificers.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120424"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120424">↑</a> viii. 52, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 163.</li></ol></div>

ऋज्राश्व|ఋజ్రాశ్వ|ಋಜ್ರಾಶ್ವ|ṛjrāśva|Rjraashva|Rijraashva|Rujraashva|RRijraashva|_vei_120@425|_vei1_pn108
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@424">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@426">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn107">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn108">1:108</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn109">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ṛjrāśva</b> appears in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120425"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120425">1</a></sup> as one of the <b>Vārṣāgiras,</b> along with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अम्बरीष">ambarīṣa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सुराधस्">surādhas</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सहदेव">sahadeva</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भयमान">bhayamāna</a>,</b> and as apparently victorious in a race. Elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120425"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120425">2</a></sup> in the  Rigveda he is celebrated as having been blinded by his father for slaying one hundred rams for a she-wolf, and as having been restored to sight by the Aśvins, a legend of quite obscure meaning.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 52.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120425"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120425">↑</a> i. 100, 16. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120425"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120425">↑</a> i. 116, 17; 117, 16. 17.</li></ol></div>

ऋण|ఋణ|ಋಣ|ṛṇa|RNa|RiNa|RuNa|RRiNa|_vei_120@426|_vei1_pn109
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@425">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@427">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn108">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn109">1:109</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn110">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ṛṇa,</b> ‘debt,’ is repeatedly mentioned from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120426">1</a></sup> onwards, having apparently been a normal condition among the Vedic Indians. Reference is often made<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120426">2</a></sup> to debts contracted at dicing. To pay off a debt was called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋणं">ṛṇaṃ</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सं">saṃ</a>-nī.</i><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120426">3</a></sup> Allusion is made to debt contracted without intention of payment.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120426">4</a></sup></p><p>The result of non-payment of a debt might be very serious: the dicer might fall into slavery.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120426">5</a></sup> Debtors, like other malefactors, such as thieves, were frequently bound by their creditors to posts (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="द्रु-पद">dru-pada</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120426">6</a></sup> presumably as a means of putting pressure on them or their friends to pay up the debt.</p><p>The amount of interest payable is impossible to make out. In one passage of the Rigveda and Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120426">7</a></sup> an eighth (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शफ">śapha</a></i>) and a sixteenth (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कला">kalā</a></i>) are mentioned as paid, but it is quite uncertain whether interest or an instalment of the principal is meant. Presumably the interest would be paid in kind.</p><p>How far a debt was a heritable interest or obligation does not appear. The <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120426">8</a></sup> regards three hymns of the  Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120426">9</a></sup> as applicable to the occasion of the payment of a debt after the creditor's decease. For the payment of a debt. by a relation of the debtor the evidence is still less clear.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120426">10</a></sup></p><p>Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120426">11</a></sup> thinks that payments of debt were made in the presence of witnesses who could be appealed to in case of dispute. This conclusion is, however, very uncertain, resting solely on a vague verse in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120426">12</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120426">↑</a> ii. 27, 4, etc., usually in a metaphorical sense.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120426">↑</a> Rv. x. 34, 10; Av. vi. 119, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120426">↑</a> Rv. viii. 47, 17 = Av. vi. 46, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120426">↑</a> Av. vi. 119, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120426">↑</a> Rv. x. 34. <i>Cf.</i> Lüders, <i>Das Wūrfelspiel im alten Indien,</i> 61.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120426">↑</a> Rv. x. 34, 4, seems to refer rather to the binding and taking away as a slave, though Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 228, explains it as the binding of a debtor for non-payment, interpreting the obscure verse i. 169, 7, in the same way. But Av. vi. 115, 2, 3, may refer to debt, and if this is the meaning the allusion to binding to a post as a punishment is clear. See, however, Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 528, n. 1; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 364, who interprets the hymn as referring only to sin. Rv. i. 24, 13. 15; Av. vi. 63, 3 = 84, 4; 121, 1 <i>et seq.,</i> are general; while Rv. vii. 86, 5; Av. xix. 47, 9; 50, 1, refer to the binding of thieves in the stocks. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तस्कर">taskara</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120426">↑</a> Rv. viii. 47, 17 = Av. vi. 46, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120426">↑</a> xlvi. 36-40. See Caland, <i>Altindisches Zauberritual,</i> 154; Bloomfield, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 528.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120426">↑</a> vi. 117-119. The name for unpaid debt is in Av. vi. 117, 1, <i>apamityam apratītam.</i> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 3, 8, 1, <i>kusīdam apratīttam;</i> in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 14, 17, and <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 3, 1, 8, <i>kusīdam apratītam;</i> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 3. 20, <i>apradattam.</i></li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120426">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. iv. 3, 13 (a brother's sin or debt); Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 99, 100.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120426">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 181. This suggestion is ignored by Bloomfield, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 375, and Whitney, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 304.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120426">↑</a> vi. 32, 3 = viii. 8, 21. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xii. 14, and see <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्ञातृ">jñātṛ</a>.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 181, 182; 259.</li></ol></div>

ऋणं-चय|ऋणंचय|ऋणं|चय|ऋणञ्चय|ఋణం-చయ|ఋణంచయ|ఋణం|చయ|ఋణఞ్చయ|ಋಣಂ-ಚಯ|ಋಣಂಚಯ|ಋಣಂ|ಚಯ|ಋಣಞ್ಚಯ|ṛṇaṃ-caya|ṛṇaṃcaya|ṛṇaṃ|caya|RNaM-chaya|RNaMchaya|RNaM|chaya|RNanchaya|RiNaM-chaya|RiNaMchaya|RiNaM|RuNaM-chaya|RuNaMchaya|RuNaM|RRiNaM-chaya|RRiNaMchaya|RRiNaM|RiNanchaya|RuNanchaya|RRiNanchaya|_vei_120@427|_vei1_pn110
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@426">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@428">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn109">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn110">1:110</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn112">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ṛṇaṃ-caya,</b> a prince of the <b>Ruśamas,</b> is celebrated in a Dānastuti (‘Praise of Gifts’) of the Rigveda (v. 30, 12. 14) for his generosity to a poet named <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बभ्रु">babhru</a>.</b></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 129; Bṛhadevatā, ed. Macdonell, 2, 169, 174.</p></div>

ऋतु|ఋతు|ಋತು|ṛtu|Rtu|Ritu|Rutu|RRitu|_vei_120@428|_vei1_pn110
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@427">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@429">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn109">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn110">1:110</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn112">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ṛtu,</b> ‘season,’ is a term repeatedly mentioned from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120428">1</a></sup> onwards, Three seasons of the year are often alluded to,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120428">2</a></sup> but the names are not usually specified. In one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120428">3</a></sup> spring (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वसन्त">vasanta</a></i>), summer (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्रीष्म">grīṣma</a></i>), and autumn (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शरद्">śarad</a></i>) are given. The Rigveda knows also the rainy season (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रा-वृष्">prā-vṛṣ</a></i>) and the winter (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="हिमा">himā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="हेमन्त">hemanta</a></i>). A more usual<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120428">4</a></sup> division (not found in the Rigveda) is into five seasons,  <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वसन्त">vasanta</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="ग्रीष्म">grīṣma</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वर्षा">varṣā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शरद्">śarad</a>, hemama-siśira,</i> but occasionally the five are otherwise divided, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्षा">varṣā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="शरद्">śarad</a></i> being made one season.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120428">5</a></sup> Sometimes six<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120428">6</a></sup> seasons are reckoned, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="हेमन्त">hemanta</a></i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शिशिर">śiśira</a></i> being divided, so that the six seasons can be made parallel to the twelve months of the year. A still more artificial arrangement<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120428">7</a></sup> makes the seasons seven, possibly by reckoning the intercalary month as a season, as Weber and Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120428">8</a></sup> hold, or more probably because of the predilection for the number seven, as Roth<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120428">9</a></sup> suggests. Occasionally the word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋतु">ṛtu</a></i> is applied to the months.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120428">10</a></sup> The last season, according to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120428">11</a></sup> is <i><a class="vei_120a" href="हेमन्त">hemanta</a>.</i></p><p>The growth of the division of the seasons from three to five is rightly explained by Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120428">12</a></sup> as indicating the advance of the Vedic Indians towards the east. It is not Rigvedic, but dominates the later Saṃhitās. Traces of an earlier division of the year into winter and summer do not appear clearly in the Rigveda, where the appropriate words <i><a class="vei_120a" href="हिमा">himā</a></i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="समा">samā</a></i> are merely general appellations of the year, and where <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शरद्">śarad</a></i><sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120428">13</a></sup> is commoner than either as a designation of the year, because it denotes the harvest, a time of overwhelming importance to a young agricultural people. The division of the year in one passage of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120428">14</a></sup> into two periods of six months is merely formal, and in no way an indication of old tradition. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120428">↑</a> i. 49, 3; 84, 18, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120428">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 164, 2 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रि">tri</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="नाभि">nābhi</a></i>), 48 (<i>trīṇi nabhyāni</i>); also perhaps the Ṛbhus as the genii of the three seasons and the three dawns. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 133; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 33 <i>et seq.:</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 1, 1, 28, and the <i>cāturmāsyāni,</i> or four-monthly sacrifices performed at the beginning of the seasons in the ritual (Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 329 <i>et seq.</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120428">↑</a> x. 90, 6. Hillebrandt, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 35, finds in Rv. v. 14, 4: ix. 91, 6, reference to three seasons in the triad <i>gāvaḥ</i> (? spring), <i>āpaḥ</i> (rains), <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्वर्">svar</a> (= <a class="vei_120a" href="घर्म">gharma</a>),</i> and in the ritual literature (Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 4, 2) in the threefold division into <i>ṛta, <a class="vei_120a" href="घर्म">gharma</a>,</i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ओषधि">oṣadhi</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120428">↑</a> Av. viii. 2, 22; 9, 15; xiii. 1, 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 2, 3; iv. 3, 3, 1. 2; v. 1, 10, 3; 3, 1, 2; 4. 12, 2; 6. 10, 1; 7, 2, 4; vii. 1, 18, 1. 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 7, 3; iii. 4, 8; 13, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 14; ix. 16; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x. 10-14; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 5, 11; vi. 2, 2, 3. etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 10, 4, 1; 11, 10, 4, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i, 164, 13. See also Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 352.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120428">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 6, 1, 10. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120428">↑</a> Av. vi. 55, 2; xii. 1, 36; Taittīrīya Saṃhitā, v. 1, 5, 2; 7, 3; 2. 6, 1, etc.; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 7, 3; iii. 11, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, viii. 6; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxi. 23-28; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 2, 21; ii. 4, 2, 24; xii. 8, 2, 34; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 19, etc. <i>Cf.</i> also Rv. i. 23, 15, as interpreted by Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. indu.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120428">↑</a> Av. vi. 61, 2; viii. 9, 18; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 5, 1, 15; ix. 1, 2, 31; 2, 3, 45; 3, 1, 19; 5, 2, 8; perhaps Av. iv. 11, 9, and <i>of.</i> Rv. i. 164. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120428">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 44; <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 374.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120428">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋतु">ṛtu</a>. Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Religions of India,</i> 18, 33.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120428">↑</a> Av. xv. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 4, 11, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiii. 25; xiv. 6. 15. 26. 27; xv. 57, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120428">↑</a> i. 5. 3. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120428">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 373.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120428">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 15, 159, 160: Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 232; Bühler, <i>Zcitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 41, 28.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120428">↑</a> viii. 9, 17. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, 372.</li></ol></div>

ऋतु-पर्ण|ऋतुपर्ण|ऋतु|पर्ण|ఋతు-పర్ణ|ఋతుపర్ణ|ఋతు|పర్ణ|ಋತು-ಪರ್ಣ|ಋತುಪರ್ಣ|ಋತು|ಪರ್ಣ|ṛtu-parṇa|ṛtuparṇa|ṛtu|parṇa|Rtu-parNa|RtuparNa|Rtu|parNa|Ritu-parNa|RituparNa|Ritu|Rutu-parNa|RutuparNa|Rutu|RRitu-parNa|RRituparNa|RRitu|_vei_120@429|_vei1_pn112
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@428">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@430">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn110">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn112">1:112</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn115">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ṛtu-parṇa</b> appears in a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>-like passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120429"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120429">1</a></sup> as son of <a class="vei_120a" href="भंगाश्विन">bhaṅgāśvina</a> and king of <a class="vei_120a" href="शफाल">śaphāla</a>. In the Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120429"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120429">2</a></sup> are mentioned <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋतुपर्ण">ṛtuparṇa</a>-Kayovadhī Bhaṅgyaśvinau.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120429"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120429">↑</a> xx. 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120429"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120429">↑</a> xxi. 20, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Caland, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 57, 745.</li></ol></div>

ऋत्व्-इज्|ऋत्विज्|ऋत्व्|इज्|ఋత్వ్-ఇజ్|ఋత్విజ్|ఋత్వ్|ఇజ్|ಋತ್ವ್-ಇಜ್|ಋತ್ವಿಜ್|ಋತ್ವ್|ಇಜ್|ṛtv-ij|ṛtvij|ṛtv|ij|Rtv-ij|Rtvij|Rtv|Ritv-ij|Ritvij|Ritv|Rutv-ij|Rutvij|Rutv|RRitv-ij|RRitvij|RRitv|_vei_120@430|_vei1_pn112
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@429">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@431">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn110">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn112">1:112</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn115">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ṛtv-ij</b> is the regular term for ‘sacrificial priest,’ covering all the different kinds of priests employed at the sacrifice. It appears certain that all the priests were Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120430">1</a></sup> The number of priests officiating at a sacrifice with different functions was almost certainly seven. The oldest list, occurring in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120430">2</a></sup> enumerates their names as <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पोतृ">potṛ</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="नेष्टृ">neṣṭṛ</a>, Agnīdh, <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रशास्तृ">praśāstṛ</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अध्वर्यु">adhvaryu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>, besides the institutor of the sacrifice. The number of seven probably explains the phrase ‘seven Hotṛs’ occurring so frequently in the Rigveda, and is most likely connected with that of the mythical ‘seven <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषिस्">ṛṣis</a>.’ It may be compared with the eight of Iran.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120430">3</a></sup> The chief of the seven priests was the <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a>, who was the singer of the hymns, and in the early times their composer also. The <a class="vei_120a" href="अध्वर्यु">adhvaryu</a> performed the practical work of the sacrifice, and accompanied his performance with muttered formulas of prayer and deprecation of evil. His chief assistance was derived from the Agnīdh, the two performing the smaller sacrifices without other help in practical matters. The <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रशास्तृ">praśāstṛ</a>, Upavaktṛ, or Maitrāvaruṇa, as he was variously called, appeared only in the greater sacrifices as giving instructions to the <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a>, and as entrusted with certain litanies. The <a class="vei_120a" href="पोतृ">potṛ</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="नेष्टृ">neṣṭṛ</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> belonged to the ritual of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> sacrifice, the latter being later styled <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मणाच्छंसिन्">brāhmaṇācchaṃsin</a> to distinguish him from the priest who in the later  ritual acted as supervisor. Other priests referred to in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120430">4</a></sup> are the singers of Sāmans or chants, the Udgātṛ and his assistant the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रस्तोतृ">prastotṛ</a>, while the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतिहर्तृ">pratihartṛ</a>, another assistant, though not mentioned, may quite well have been known. Their functions undoubtedly represent a later stage of the ritual, the development of the elaborate series of sacrificial calls on the one hand, and on the other the use of long hymns addressed to the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> plant. Other priests, such as the Achāvāka,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120430">5</a></sup> the Grāvastut, the Unnetṛ, and the <a class="vei_120a" href="सुब्रह्मण्य">subrahmaṇya</a>, were known later in the developed ritual of the Brāhmaṇas, making in all sixteen priests, who were technically and artificially classed in four groups<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120430">6</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a>, Maitrāvaruṇa, Achāvāka, and Grāvastut; Udgātṛ, <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रस्तोतृ">prastotṛ</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतिहर्तृ">pratihartṛ</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="सुब्रह्मण्य">subrahmaṇya</a>; <a class="vei_120a" href="अध्वर्यु">adhvaryu</a>, Pratisthātṛ, <a class="vei_120a" href="नेष्टृ">neṣṭṛ</a>, and Unnetṛ; <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मणाच्छंसिन्">brāhmaṇācchaṃsin</a>, Agnīdhra, and Petṛ.</p><p>Apart from all these priests was the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>,</b> who was the spiritual adviser of the king in all his religious duties. Geldner<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120430">7</a></sup> holds that, as a rule, when the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> actually took part in one of the great sacrifices he played the part of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>, in the sense of the priest who superintended the whole conduct of the ritual. He sees evidence for this view in a considerable number of passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120430">8</a></sup> and the later literature,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120430">9</a></sup> where <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> were combined or identified. Oldenberg,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120430">10</a></sup> however, more correctly points out  that in the earlier period this was not the case: the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> was then normally the <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a>, the singer of the most important of the songs; it was only later that the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>, who in the capacity of overseer of the rite is not known to the Rigveda, acquired the function of general supervision hitherto exercised by the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, who was <i>ex officio</i> skilled in the use of magic and in guarding the king by spells which could also be applied to guarding the sacrifice from evil demons. With this agrees the fact that <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>, pre-eminently<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120430">11</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> of men, is also a <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a>, and that the two divine Hotṛs of the Āprī hymns are called<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120430">12</a></sup> the divine Purohitas. On the other hand, the rule is explicitly recognized in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120430">13</a></sup> that a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> should have a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> as a <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>; and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120430">14</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> family have a special claim to the office of <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, perhaps an indication that it was they who first as Purohitas exchanged the function of Hotṛs for that of Brahmans in the sacrificial ritual.</p><p>The sacrifices were performed for an individual in the great majority of cases. The Sattra,<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_120430">15</a></sup> or prolonged sacrificial session, was, however, performed for the common benefit of the priests taking part in it, though its advantageous results could only be secured if all the members actually engaged were consecrated (<i>dīkṣita</i>). Sacrifices for a people as such were unknown. The sacrifice for the king was, it is true, intended to bring about the prosperity of his people also; but it is characteristic that the prayer<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_120430">16</a></sup> for welfare includes by name only the priest and the king, referring to the people indirectly in connexion with the prosperity of their cattle and agriculture. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120430">↑</a> This is assumed throughout the Vedic texts, and is accompanied by the rule that no <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> can eat of the sacrificial offering (<i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 26): no doubt because only the Brāhmaṇas were sufficiently holy to receive the divine essence of the sacrifice into which, by partaking of it, the deity has entered in part.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120430">↑</a> ii. 1, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg. <i>Rdigion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 383.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120430">↑</a> Darmesteter, <i>Le Zend-Avesta,</i> 1, lxx <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120430">↑</a> Rv. viii. 81. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120430">↑</a> <i>Cf.,</i> for the Achāvāka, <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxviii. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 14, 8, etc.; Bergaigne, <i>Recherches sur l'histoire de la liturgie védique,</i> 47; Oldenberg. <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 397, n. 2. The other three occur in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> and other Brāhmaṇas. See St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120430">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 1, 4-6; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> Sūtras, xiii. 14, 1, etc. In the Rigveda <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> the order of the four sets is <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>, Udgātṛ, and <a class="vei_120a" href="अध्वर्यु">adhvaryu</a>. Sometimes a seventeenth priest is mentioned, but he was not usually approved, though the Kauṣītakins maintained him as the <a class="vei_120a" href="सदस्य">sadasya</a>. See Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 4, 1, 19; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 43, 348, n.; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 37; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 375.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120430">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 143 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120430">↑</a> Rv. i. 44, 10; 94, 6; viii. 27, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120430">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="बृहस्पति">bṛhaspati</a> is <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> of the gods, Rv. ii. 24, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 1, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 17, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 1, 2; but <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> in Rv. x. 141, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 13; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 4, 21. <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> is <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, Rv. x. 150, 5, of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पैजवन">paijavana</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 11, 4; but <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> of the Sunaḥśepa sacrifice, <i>ibid,</i> xv. 21.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120430">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 380 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120430">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> as <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> occurs in Rv. i. 1, 1; iii. 3, 2; 11, 1; v. 11, 2. His Purohitaship is described in terms characteristic of the Hotṛ's funcitons in Rv. viii. 27, 1; x. 1, 6. Devāpi is <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a>. Rv. x. 98.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120430">↑</a> Rv. x. 66, 13; in x. 70, 7, <i>purohitāv ṛtvijā.</i></li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120430">↑</a> vii. 26.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120430">↑</a> iii. 5, 2, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_120430">↑</a> Oldenberg, 371.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_120430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_120430">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxii. 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 18; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 12, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 14, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 141 <i>et seq.;</i> 376 <i>et seq.;</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Rituallitteratur,</i> 97; Oldenberg, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 370397; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 224.</li></ol></div>

ऋश्य|ఋశ్య|ಋಶ್ಯ|ṛśya|Rshya|Rishya|Rushya|RRishya|_vei_120@431|_vei1_pn115
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@430">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@432">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn112">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn115">1:115</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn117">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ṛśya</b>--This is the correct<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120431"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120431">1</a></sup> spelling of a word that occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120431"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120431">2</a></sup> and the later literature<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120431"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120431">3</a></sup> meaning ‘stag,’ the feminine being <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रोहित्">rohit</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120431"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120431">4</a></sup> Apparently deer were caught in pits (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋश्य">ṛśya</a>-da</i>).<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120431"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120431">5</a></sup> The procreative power of the stag (<i>ārśya vṛṣṇya</i>) was celebrated.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120431"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120431">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120431"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120431">↑</a> It appears in Av. iv. 4. 7, as Ṛśa; as <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋष्य">ṛṣya</a> in Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 9. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120431"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120431">↑</a> viii. 4, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120431"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120431">↑</a> Av. iv. 4. 5. 7; v. 14, 3; i. 18. 4 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋश्य">ṛśya</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पद्">pad</a></i>); Vājasaneyi Samhitā, xxiv. 27.37; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 33; citation in <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 25, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120431"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120431">↑</a> Av. iv. 4, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120431"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120431">↑</a> Rv. x. 39, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120431"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120431">↑</a> Av. iv. 4, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 18; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 82; Whitney. Translation of the Atharvaveda, 150, 151.</li></ol></div>

ऋषभ|ऋषभ|ఋషభ_౧|ఋషభ|ಋಷಭ_೧|ಋಷಭ|ṛṣabha_1|ṛṣabha|RShabha_1|RShabha|RiShabha_1|RiShabha|RuShabha_1|RuShabha|RRiShabha_1|RRiShabha|_vei_120@432|_vei1_pn115
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@431">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@433">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn112">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn115">1:115</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn117">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Ṛṣabha</b> is the common name of the ‘bull’ from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120432">1</a></sup> onwards.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120432">2</a></sup> See also <b>Go.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120432">↑</a> vi. 16, 47; 28, 8; x. 91, 14, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120432">↑</a> Av. iii. 6, 4; 23, 4, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 3, 2, etc.; vajasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxi. 22, etc.; Pañcavimśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 18, etc.</li></ol></div>

ऋषभ|ऋषभ|ఋషభ_౨|ఋషభ|ಋಷಭ_೨|ಋಷಭ|ṛṣabha_2|ṛṣabha|RShabha_2|RShabha|RiShabha_2|RiShabha|RuShabha_2|RuShabha|RRiShabha_2|RRiShabha|_vei_120@433|_vei1_pn115
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@432">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@434">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn112">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn115">1:115</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn117">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Ṛṣabha,</b> king of the Śviknas, appears in the Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120433"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120433">1</a></sup> with the patronymic <a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञतुर">yājñatura</a>, as one of those who performed an <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> or horse sacrifice. He is also mentioned there<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120433"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120433">2</a></sup> as having probably been the source of a saying of <b>Gaurīviti Śāktya's.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120433"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120433">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 15. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 9. 8-10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120433"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120433">↑</a> xii. 8, 3. 7.</li></ol></div>

ऋषभ|ऋषभ|ఋషభ_౩|ఋషభ|ಋಷಭ_೩|ಋಷಭ|ṛṣabha_3|ṛṣabha|RShabha_3|RShabha|RiShabha_3|RiShabha|RuShabha_3|RuShabha|RRiShabha_3|RRiShabha|_vei_120@434|_vei1_pn115
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@433">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@435">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn112">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn115">1:115</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn117">↷</a><hr><p>3. <b>Ṛṣabha</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vii. 17) as a son of <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>.</p></div>

ऋषि|ఋషి|ಋಷಿ|ṛṣi|RShi|RiShi|RuShi|RRiShi|_vei_120@435|_vei1_pn115
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@434">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@436">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn112">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn115">1:115</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn117">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ṛṣi,</b> ‘seer,’ is primarily a composer of hymns to the gods. In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120435">1</a></sup> reference is often made to previous singers and to contemporary poets. Old poems were inherited and refurbished by members of the composer's family,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120435">2</a></sup> but the great aim of the singers was to produce new and approved hymns.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120435">3</a></sup> It is not till the time of the Brāhmaṇas that the  composition of hymns appears to have fallen into disuse,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120435">4</a></sup> though poetry was still produced, for example, in the form of <b>Gāthās,</b> which the priests were required to compose themselves<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120435">5</a></sup> and sing to the accompaniment of the lute at the sacrifice. The <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> was the most exalted of Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120435">6</a></sup> and his skill, which is often compared with that of a carpenter,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120435">7</a></sup> was regarded as heaven-sent.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120435">8</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, whether as <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a> or as <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋत्विज्">ṛtvij</a></b>), was a singer.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120435">9</a></sup> No doubt the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषिस्">ṛṣis</a> were normally<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120435">10</a></sup> attached to the houses of the great, the petty kings of Vedic times, or the nobles of the royal household. Nor need it be doubted that occasionally<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120435">11</a></sup> the princes themselves essayed poetry: a <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्यर्षि">rājanyarṣi</a>, the prototype of the later Rājarṣi or ‘royal seer,’ who appears in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120435">12</a></sup> though he must be mythical as Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120435">13</a></sup> points out, indicates that kings cultivated poetry<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120435">14</a></sup> just as later they engaged in philosophic disputations.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_120435">15</a></sup> Normally, however, the poetical function is Brahminical, Viśāmitra and others not being kings, but merely Brāhmaṇas, in the Rigveda.</p><p>In the later literature the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषिस्">ṛṣis</a> are the poets of the hymns preserved in the Saṃhitās. a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> being regularly<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_120435">16</a></sup> cited when a Vedic Saṃhitā is quoted. Moreover, the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषिस्">ṛṣis</a> become the representatives of a sacred past, and are regarded as holy sages,  whose deeds are narrated as if they were the deeds of gods or Asuras.<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_120435">17</a></sup> They are typified by a particular group of seven,<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_120435">18</a></sup> mentioned four times in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_120435">19</a></sup> several times in the later Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_120435">20</a></sup> and enumerated in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_21_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_120435">21</a></sup> as <a class="vei_120a" href="गोतम">gotama</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="जमदग्नि">jamadagni</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कश्यप">kaśyapa</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="अत्रि">atri</a>. In the Rigveda itself <a class="vei_120a" href="कुत्स">kutsa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_120435">22</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="अत्रि">atri</a>,<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_120435">23</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="रेभ">rebha</a>,<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_120435">24</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="अगस्त्य">agastya</a>,<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_120435">25</a></sup> the Kuśikas,<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_120435">26</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>,<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_120435">27</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="व्यश्व">vyaśva</a>,<sup id="ref_120_28_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_120435">28</a></sup> and others appear as <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषिस्">ṛṣis</a>; and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_29_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29_vei_120435">29</a></sup> contains a long list, including <a class="vei_120a" href="अंगिरस्">aṅgiras</a>, Agastī, <a class="vei_120a" href="जमदग्नि">jamadagni</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अत्रि">atri</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कश्यप">kaśyapa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a>, Gaviṣṭhira, <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कुत्स">kutsa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मेधातिथि">medhātithi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रिशोक">triśoka</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="उशना">uśanā</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काव्य">kāvya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="गोतम">gotama</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="मुद्गल">mudgala</a>.</p><p>Competition among the bards appears to have been known. This is one of the sides of the riddle poetry (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मोद्य">brahmodya</a></b>) that forms a distinctive feature<sup id="ref_120_30_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_30_vei_120435">30</a></sup> of the Vedic ritual of the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or horse sacrifice. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> period such competitions were quite frequent. The most famous was that of <a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a>, which was held at the court of <a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a> of <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>, as detailed in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_31_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_31_vei_120435">31</a></sup> and which was a source of annoyance to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अजातशत्रु">ajātaśatru</a></b> of <b>Kāśī.</b><sup id="ref_120_32_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_32_vei_120435">32</a></sup> According to an analogous practice, a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, like <b>Uddālaka Aruṇi,</b> would go about disputing with all he came across, and compete with them for a prize of money.<sup id="ref_120_33_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_33_vei_120435">33</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120435">↑</a> i. 1, 2; 45, 3; viii. 43, 13, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120435">↑</a> i. 89, 3; 96, 2; iii. 39, 2; viii. 6. 11. 43; 76, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120435">↑</a> i. 109, 2; ii. 18, 3; iii. 62, 7; vi. 50, 6; vii. 14, 4; 93, 1; viii. 23, 14, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120435">↑</a> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 151.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120435">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 2, 8; 3, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120435">↑</a> Rv. ix. 96, 6, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 4, 4, 6, where preeminence is assigned to a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> descended from a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120435">↑</a> Rv. i. 130, 6; v. 2, 11; 29, 15; 73, 10; x. 39, 14. So a poet is a <a class="vei_120a" href="कारु">kāru</a>(if from <i>kṛ,</i> ‘make,’ but usually derived from <i>kṛ,</i> ‘commemorate’), and makes (<i>kṛ,</i> Rv. ii. 39. 8; viii. 62, 4) as well as creates (<i>jan,</i> Rv. vii. 15, 4; viii. 88, 4) hymns.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120435">↑</a> Rv. i. 37, 4; vii. 36. 1. 9; viii. 32, 27; 57. 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120435">↑</a> Rv. i. 151, 7; Geldner, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 153; Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 380.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120435">↑</a> Geldner, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 154, cites the Dānastutis as characteristic of princes in the tradition of the Bṛhaddevatā, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120435">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 154.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120435">↑</a> xii. 12, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120435">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift dev Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 45, 235. n. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120435">↑</a> Later on it was deemed quite normal and natural. See the story of <a class="vei_120a" href="रथवीति दार्भ्य">rathavīti dārbhya</a>, or <a class="vei_120a" href="दाल्भ्य">dālbhya</a>, himself a royal seer, and <a class="vei_120a" href="तरन्त">taranta</a> and Purumīḷha, seers and also kings, in Bṛhaddevata, v. 50 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_120435">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Garbe, <i>Philosophy of Ancient India,</i> 73 <i>et seq.;</i> Deussen, <i>Philosophy of the Upaniṣads,</i> 16 <i>et seq.;</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 50.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_120435">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 25; viii. 26; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 4, 4; ii. 2, 3, 6; 5, 1, 4; vi, 1, 1, 1, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vii. 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_120435">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 17; ii. 19; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 2, 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_120435">↑</a> <i>Cf. Indische Studien,</i> 8, 167.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_120435">↑</a> iv. 42, 8; x. 109, 4; 130, 7; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 144.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_120435">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiv. 24; Av xi. 1, 1. 24; xii. 1, 39, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_120435">↑</a> ii. 2, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_120435">↑</a> i. 106, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_120435">↑</a> i. 117, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_120435">↑</a> i. 117, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_120435">↑</a> i. 179, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_120435">↑</a> iii. 53. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_120435">↑</a> vii. 33. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_120435">↑</a> viii. 23, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_29_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29_vei_120435">↑</a> iv. 29. <i>Cf.</i> xviii. 3, 15. 16.</li><li id="fx_120_30_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_30_vei_120435">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 345, 346; Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 172; <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 216 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_31_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_31_vei_120435">↑</a> iii. 1, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_32_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_32_vei_120435">↑</a> <i>Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,</i> ii. 1, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_33_vei_120435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_33_vei_120435">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 4, 1, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3. 8 <i>et seq.;</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 185, 344.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 340347; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 3, 120 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

ऋषिस्|ऋषि|ఋషిస్|ఋషి|ಋಷಿಸ್|ಋಷಿ|ṛṣis|ṛṣi|RShis|RShi|RiShis|RiShi|RuShis|RuShi|RRiShis|RRiShi|_vei_120@436|_vei1_pn117
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@435">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@437">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn115">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn117">1:117</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn118">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ṛṣis.</b>--The term ‘Seven Ṛṣis’ denotes the ‘Great Bear’ (see 1. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋक्ष">ṛkṣa</a></b>) in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120436"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120436">1</a></sup> and occasionally  later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120436"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120436">2</a></sup> This is probably a secondary use, instead of the seven Ṛkṣas, brought about by the frequent mention of the seven <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषिस्">ṛṣis</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120436"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120436">↑</a> x. 82, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120436"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120436">↑</a> Av vi. 40, 1 (where, however, Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 310. merely renders it as ‘seven seers,’ and appears not to take it in a technical sense); Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 2, 4; xiii. 8, 1, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, x. 26, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 422; Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 144.</li></ol></div>

ऋष्टि|ఋష్టి|ಋಷ್ಟಿ|ṛṣṭi|RShTi|RiShTi|RuShTi|RRiShTi|_vei_120@437|_vei1_pn118
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@436">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@438">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn117">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn118">1:118</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn119">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ṛṣṭi</b> is a term frequently employed in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120437">1</a></sup> to designate a weapon held in the hands of the Maruts, and doubtless meant to indicate lightning. That it denotes a spear in mortal warfare, as Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120437">2</a></sup> thinks, is not shown by a single passage.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120437">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120437">↑</a> Rv. i. 37, 1; 64, 4. 8; 166, 4; v. 52, 6; 54, 11; 57, 6; viii. 20, 11. <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> has a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋष्टि">ṛṣṭi</a> in Rv. i. 169, 3 (<i>cf.</i> Av. iv. 37. 8). <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 79.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120437">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 301.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120437">↑</a> Rv. i. 167. 3; vii. 55. 2; viii. 28, 5; x. 87, 7. 24 are all mythological or contain similes.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Preshistoric Antiquities,</i> 221.</li></ol></div>

ऋष्टि-षेण|ऋष्टिषेण|ऋष्टि|षेण|ఋష్టి-షేణ|ఋష్టిషేణ|ఋష్టి|షేణ|ಋಷ್ಟಿ-ಷೇಣ|ಋಷ್ಟಿಷೇಣ|ಋಷ್ಟಿ|ಷೇಣ|ṛṣṭi-ṣeṇa|ṛṣṭiṣeṇa|ṛṣṭi|ṣeṇa|RShTi-SheNa|RShTiSheNa|RShTi|SheNa|RiShTi-SheNa|RiShTiSheNa|RiShTi|RuShTi-SheNa|RuShTiSheNa|RuShTi|RRiShTi-SheNa|RRiShTiSheNa|RRiShTi|_vei_120@438|_vei1_pn118
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@437">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@439">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn117">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn118">1:118</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn119">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ṛṣṭi-ṣeṇa</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120438"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120438">1</a></sup> as an explanation of the patronymic <b>Ārṣṭiṣeṇa,</b> but nothing else is known of him.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120438"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120438">↑</a> ii. 11. <i>Cf.</i> Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 130, 136.</li></ol></div>

ऋष्य-शृंग|ऋष्यशृंग|ऋष्य|शृंग|ऋष्य-शृङ्ग|ऋष्यशृङ्ग|शृङ्ग|ఋష్య-శృంగ|ఋష్యశృంగ|ఋష్య|శృంగ|ఋష్య-శృఙ్గ|ఋష్యశృఙ్గ|శృఙ్గ|ಋಷ್ಯ-ಶೃಂಗ|ಋಷ್ಯಶೃಂಗ|ಋಷ್ಯ|ಶೃಂಗ|ಋಷ್ಯ-ಶೃಙ್ಗ|ಋಷ್ಯಶೃಙ್ಗ|ಶೃಙ್ಗ|ṛṣya-śṛṅga|ṛṣyaśṛṅga|ṛṣya|śṛṅga|RShya-shRMga|RShyashRMga|RShya|shRMga|RShya-shRnga|RShyashRnga|shRnga|RiShya-shRiMga|RiShyashRiMga|RiShya|shRiMga|RuShya-shRuMga|RuShyashRuMga|RuShya|shRuMga|RRiShya-shRRiMga|RRiShyashRRiMga|RRiShya|shRRiMga|RiShya-shRinga|RiShyashRinga|shRinga|RuShya-shRunga|RuShyashRunga|shRunga|RRiShya-shRRinga|RRiShyashRRinga|shRRinga|_vei_120@439|_vei1_pn118
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@438">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@440">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn117">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn118">1:118</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn119">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ṛṣya-śṛṅga</b> appears as a teacher, pupil of Kāśyapa, and as bearing the patronymic Kāśyapa in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> Brahmaṇa<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120439"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120439">1</a></sup> and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120439"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120439">2</a></sup> The more correct spelling of the name is <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋश्य">ṛśya</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="शृंग">śṛṅga</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120439"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120439">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120439"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120439">↑</a> iii. 40, 1 (in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a>, or list of teachers).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120439"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120439">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 374, 385.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120439"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120439">↑</a> The later legend connected with the name may contain old elements (see Lūders, <i>Die Sage von Ṛshyaśṛṅga,</i> 1897; Von Schroeder, <i>Mysterium und Mimus,</i> 292-301), but it is not known to any Vedic text.</li></ol></div>

एक-द्यू|एकद्यू|एक|द्यू|ఏక-ద్యూ|ఏకద్యూ|ఏక|ద్యూ|ಏಕ-ದ್ಯೂ|ಏಕದ್ಯೂ|ಏಕ|ದ್ಯೂ|eka-dyū|ekadyū|eka|dyū|eka-dyuu|ekadyuu|dyuu|eka-dyoo|ekadyoo|dyoo|_vei_120@440|_vei1_pn118
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@439">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@441">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn117">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn118">1:118</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn119">↷</a><hr><p><b>Eka-dyū</b> is mentioned as a poet in one hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120440"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120440">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120440"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120440">↑</a> viii. 80, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 112.</li></ol></div>

एक-यवन् गां-दम|एक-यवन् गांदम|एक-यवन् गां|दम|एक-यवन् गान्दम|ఏక-యవన్ గాం-దమ|ఏక-యవన్ గాందమ|ఏక-యవన్ గాం|దమ|ఏక-యవన్ గాన్దమ|ಏಕ-ಯವನ್ ಗಾಂ-ದಮ|ಏಕ-ಯವನ್ ಗಾಂದಮ|ಏಕ-ಯವನ್ ಗಾಂ|ದಮ|ಏಕ-ಯವನ್ ಗಾನ್ದಮ|eka-yavan gāṃ-dama|eka-yavan gāṃdama|eka-yavan gāṃ|dama|eka-yavan gaaM-dama|eka-yavan gaaMdama|eka-yavan gaaM|eka-yavan gaandama|_vei_120@441|_vei1_pn118
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@440">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@442">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn117">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn118">1:118</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn119">↷</a><hr><p><b>Eka-yavan <a class="vei_120a" href="गां">gāṃ</a>-dama</b> is a man mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120441">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120441">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120441">↑</a> xxi. 14, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120441">↑</a> ii. 7, 11 (Kāṃdama). <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> i. 32; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 69.</li></ol></div>

एक-राज्|एकराज्|एक|राज्|ఏక-రాజ్|ఏకరాజ్|ఏక|రాజ్|ಏಕ-ರಾಜ್|ಏಕರಾಜ್|ಏಕ|ರಾಜ್|eka-rāj|ekarāj|eka|rāj|eka-raaj|ekaraaj|raaj|_vei_120@442|_vei1_pn119
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@441">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@443">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn118">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn119">1:119</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn120">↷</a><hr><p><b>Eka-rāj,</b> ‘sole ruler,’ ‘monarch,’ seems to mean no more than ‘king.’ In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120442">1</a></sup> the term is used metaphorically only. But it is found with the literal sense in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120442">2</a></sup> as well as in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120442">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120442">↑</a> viii. 37. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120442">↑</a> viii. 15.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120442">↑</a> iii. 4, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>,</i> 141</li></ol></div>

एकायन|ఏకాయన|ಏಕಾಯನ|ekāyana|ekaayana|_vei_120@443|_vei1_pn119
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@442">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@444">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn118">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn119">1:119</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn120">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ekāyana</b> denotes some object of study in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120443"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120443">1</a></sup> The St. Petersburg Dictionary renders it ‘doctrine (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अयन">ayana</a></i>) of unity’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="एक">eka</a></i>), ‘monotheism,’ while Max Müller prefers ‘ethics,’ and Monier-Williams in his Dicitionary ‘worldly wisdom.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120443"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120443">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120443"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120443">↑</a> vii. 1, 2. 4; 2, 1; 7, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120443"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120443">↑</a> Max Müller and Monier-Williams thus follow Śaṅkara's interpretation as <i>nīti-śāstra,</i> ‘moral teaching.’ <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 267, 484; Little, <i>Grammatical Index,</i> 43.</li></ol></div>

एकाष्टका|ఏకాష్టకా|ಏಕಾಷ್ಟಕಾ|ekāṣṭakā|ekaaShTakaa|_vei_120@444|_vei1_pn119
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@443">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@445">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn118">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn119">1:119</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn120">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ekāṣṭakā.</b>--That <a class="vei_120a" href="अष्टका">aṣṭakā</a> is the eighth day after the full moon appears clearly from the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120444"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120444">1</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="एकाष्टका">ekāṣṭakā</a>, or ‘sole <a class="vei_120a" href="अष्टका">aṣṭakā</a>,’ must denote not merely any <a class="vei_120a" href="अष्टका">aṣṭakā</a>, but some particular one. Sāyaṇa, in his commentary on the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120444"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120444">2</a></sup> in which a whole hymn celebrates the <a class="vei_120a" href="एकाष्टका">ekāṣṭakā</a>, fixes the date meant by the term as the eighth day in the dark half of the month of Māgha (January--February). The <a class="vei_120a" href="एकाष्टका">ekāṣṭakā</a>, is declared in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120444"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120444">3</a></sup> to be the time for the consecration (<i>dīkṣā</i>) of those who are going to perform a year long sacrifice. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120444"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120444">↑</a> xv. 16, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 2, 2, 23; 4, 2, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120444"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120444">↑</a> iii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120444"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120444">↑</a> vii. 4, 8 1. <i>Cf.</i> iii. 3, 8, 4; iv. 3, 11, 1; v. 7, 2, 2; Pañcaviṃśa Brāl maṇa, v. 9, 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 305; Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 341, 342.</li></ol></div>

एजत्क|ఏజత్క|ಏಜತ್ಕ|ejatka|_vei_120@445|_vei1_pn119
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@444">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@446">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn118">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn119">1:119</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn120">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ejatka</b> is the name of an insect in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120445"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120445">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120445"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120445">↑</a> v. 23, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindiches Leben,</i> 98; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 262.</li></ol></div>

एडक|ఏడక|ಏಡಕ|eḍaka|eDaka|_vei_120@446|_vei1_pn119
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@445">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@447">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn118">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn119">1:119</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn120">↷</a><hr><p><b>Eḍaka</b> appears to denote a ‘vicious ram’ in the Śatapatha<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120446"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120446">1</a></sup> and Jaiminīya<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120446"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120446">2</a></sup> Brāhmaṇas. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120446"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120446">↑</a> xii. 4, 1, 4; <i>cf.</i> ii. 5, 2, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120446"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120446">↑</a> i. 51, 4 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 23, 332). <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacrea Books of the East,</i> 44, 178.</li></ol></div>

एणी|एणि|ఏణీ|ఏణి|ಏಣೀ|ಏಣಿ|eṇī|eṇi|eNii|eNi|eNee|_vei_120@447|_vei1_pn120
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@446">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@448">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn119">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn120">1:120</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn121">↷</a><hr><p><b>Eṇī</b> denotes the ‘female antelope’ in the later Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120447"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120447">1</a></sup> perhaps as the feminine of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="एत">eta</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120447"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120447">↑</a> Av. v. 14, 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 15, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 17; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 36; <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 82.</li></ol></div>

एत|ఏత|ಏತ|eta|_vei_120@448|_vei1_pn120
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@447">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@449">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn119">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn120">1:120</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn121">↷</a><hr><p><b>Eta</b> in the plural (<i>etāḥ</i>) denotes the steeds of the Maruts, being a swift species of deer, which are mentioned several times in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120448">1</a></sup> and the skins of which are also said to be worn by the Maruts on their shoulders.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120448">2</a></sup> The epithet <i>pṛthubudhna,</i> once applied to them in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120448">3</a></sup> and variously interpreted as ‘broad-hoofed,’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120448">4</a></sup> ‘broad-chested,’<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120448">5</a></sup> and ‘broad in the hinder part,’<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120448">6</a></sup> seems to indicate that they were not gazelles.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120448">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120448">↑</a> i. 165. 2; 169, 6, 7; v. 54, 5; x. 77. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120448">↑</a> Rv. i. 166, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120448">↑</a> i. 169. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120448">↑</a> By Grassman and Zimmer.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120448">↑</a> Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 1, 235.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120448">↑</a> Monier-Williams, Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120448">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 83.</li></ol></div>

एतश|एतश|ఏతశ_౧|ఏతశ|ಏತಶ_೧|ಏತಶ|etaśa_1|etaśa|etasha_1|etasha|_vei_120@449|_vei1_pn120
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@448">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@450">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn119">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn120">1:120</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn121">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Etaśa</b> is in several passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120449">1</a></sup> according to Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120449">2</a></sup> the name of a protégé whom <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> helped against the sun-god <a class="vei_120a" href="सूर्य">sūrya</a>. But in all these passages <a class="vei_120a" href="एतश">etaśa</a> seems merely to designate the horse of the sun.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120449">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120449">↑</a> i. 62, 15; iv. 30. 6; v. 29. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120449">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120449">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> pp. 149. 150.</li></ol></div>

एतश|एतश|ఏతశ_౨|ఏతశ|ಏತಶ_೨|ಏತಶ|etaśa_2|etaśa|etasha_2|etasha|_vei_120@450|_vei1_pn120
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@449">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@451">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn119">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn120">1:120</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn121">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Etaśa</b> is in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120450"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120450">1</a></sup> the name of a sage who is said to have cursed his children because they interrupted him in the midst of a rite; hence the Aitaśāyanas (descendants of <a class="vei_120a" href="एतश">etaśa</a>) are declared to be the worst of the Bhṛgus. The same story appears in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Brāhmaṇā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120450"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120450">2</a></sup> where, however, the sage's name is <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतश">aitaśa</a>, and the Aitaśāyanas are described as the worst of the Aurvas.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120450"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120450">↑</a> xxx. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120450"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120450">↑</a> vi. 33. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt. <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 173.</li></ol></div>

एदिधिषुः-पति|एदिधिषुःपति|एदिधिषुः|पति|ఏదిధిషుః-పతి|ఏదిధిషుఃపతి|ఏదిధిషుః|పతి|ಏದಿಧಿಷುಃ-ಪತಿ|ಏದಿಧಿಷುಃಪತಿ|ಏದಿಧಿಷುಃ|ಪತಿ|edidhiṣuḥ-pati|edidhiṣuḥpati|edidhiṣuḥ|pati|edidhiShuH-pati|edidhiShuHpati|edidhiShuH|_vei_120@451|_vei1_pn120
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@450">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@452">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn119">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn120">1:120</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn121">↷</a><hr><p><b>Edidhiṣuḥ-pati</b> is a term occurring only in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120451"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120451">1</a></sup> where the commentator Mahīdhara interprets it as  meaning the ‘husband of a younger sister married before the elder sister.’ Though this sense is probably correct, the form is doubtless, as Delbrück<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120451"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120451">2</a></sup> points out, corrupt. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दिधिषूपति">didhiṣūpati</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120451"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120451">↑</a> xxx. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120451"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120451">↑</a> <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandschaftsnamen,</i> 569. n. 1.</li></ol></div>

एरण्ड|ఏరణ్డ|ಏರಣ್ಡ|eraṇḍa|eraNDa|_vei_120@452|_vei1_pn121
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@451">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@453">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn120">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn121">1:121</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn122">↷</a><hr><p><b>Eraṇḍa,</b> the castor-oil plant (<i>Ricinus communis</i>), is first mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka (xii. 8).</p></div>

एवावद|ఏవావద|ಏವಾವದ|evāvada|evaavada|_vei_120@453|_vei1_pn121
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@452">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@454">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn120">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn121">1:121</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn122">↷</a><hr><p><b>Evāvada</b> is regarded by Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120453"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120453">1</a></sup> in a very obscure passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120453"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120453">2</a></sup> as the name of a singer beside <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्र">kṣatra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मनस">manasa</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यजत">yajata</a>.</b> The commentator Sāyaṇa also interprets the word as a proper name. Roth,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120453"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120453">3</a></sup> however, considers it to be an adjective meaning ‘truthful.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120453"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120453">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 138.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120453"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120453">↑</a> v. 44, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120453"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120453">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

ऐकादशाक्ष मानु-तन्तव्य|ऐकादशाक्ष मानुतन्तव्य|ऐकादशाक्ष मानु|तन्तव्य|ఐకాదశాక్ష మాను-తన్తవ్య|ఐకాదశాక్ష మానుతన్తవ్య|ఐకాదశాక్ష మాను|తన్తవ్య|ಐಕಾದಶಾಕ್ಷ ಮಾನು-ತನ್ತವ್ಯ|ಐಕಾದಶಾಕ್ಷ ಮಾನುತನ್ತವ್ಯ|ಐಕಾದಶಾಕ್ಷ ಮಾನು|ತನ್ತವ್ಯ|aikādaśākṣa mānu-tantavya|aikādaśākṣa mānutantavya|aikādaśākṣa mānu|tantavya|aikaadashaakSha maanu-tantavya|aikaadashaakSha maanutantavya|aikaadashaakSha maanu|aikaadashaaxa maanu-tantavya|aikaadashaaxa maanutantavya|aikaadashaaxa maanu|_vei_120@454|_vei1_pn121
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@453">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@455">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn120">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn121">1:121</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn122">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aikādaśākṣa <a class="vei_120a" href="मानु">mānu</a>-tantavya</b> appears in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120454"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120454">1</a></sup> as a king who observed the rule of sacrificing when the sun had risen (<i>udita-homin</i>), and as a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नगरिन् जान-श्रुतेय">nagarin <a class="vei_120a" href="जान">jāna</a>-śruteya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120454"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120454">↑</a> v. 30. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 223.</li></ol></div>

ऐक्ष्वाक|ఐక్ష్వాక|ಐಕ್ಷ್ವಾಕ|aikṣvāka|aikShvaaka|aixvaaka|_vei_120@455|_vei1_pn121
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@454">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@456">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn120">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn121">1:121</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn122">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aikṣvāka,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="इक्ष्वाकु">ikṣvāku</a>,’ is the patronymic borne by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a></b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120455"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120455">1</a></sup> Another <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐक्ष्वाक">aikṣvāka</a> is <a class="vei_120a" href="वार्ष्णि">vārṣṇi</a>, a teacher mentioned in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120455"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120455">2</a></sup> A king <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हरिश्चन्द्र वैधस">hariścandra vaidhasa</a></b> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐक्ष्वाक">aikṣvāka</a> is known to the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120455"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120455">3</a></sup> and <b>Tryaruṇa</b> is an <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐक्ष्वाक">aikṣvāka</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120455"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120455">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120455"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120455">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120455"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120455">↑</a> i. 5, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120455"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120455">↑</a> vii. 13, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120455"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120455">↑</a> xiii. 3, 12.</li></ol></div>

ऐतरेय|ఐతరేయ|ಐತರೇಯ|aitareya|_vei_120@456|_vei1_pn121
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@455">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@457">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn120">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn121">1:121</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn122">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aitareya,</b> perhaps a patronymic from Itara, though the commentator Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120456">1</a></sup> regards the word as a metronymic from  Itarā, is an epithet of <b>Mahidāsa</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120456">2</a></sup> and the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120456">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120456">↑</a> Cited by Aufrecht, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,</i> 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120456">↑</a> ii. 1, 8; 3. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120456">↑</a> iii. 16, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 389. The form Aitareyin occurs in the Anupada <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 1; Āśvalāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 3, etc.; and a <a class="vei_120a" href="महैतरेय">mahaitareya</a> in Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 4, 4, etc.</li></ol></div>

ऐतश|ऐतशायन|ఐతశ|ఐతశాయన|ಐತಶ|ಐತಶಾಯನ|aitaśa|aitaśāyana|aitasha|aitashaayana|_vei_120@457|_vei1_pn122
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@456">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@458">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn121">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn122">1:122</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn123">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aitaśa, Aitaśāyana.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="एतश">etaśa</a>, Etaśāyana.</b> The Aitaśapralāpa, or ‘Discourse of <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतश">aitaśa</a>,’ is a part of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120457">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120457">↑</a> xx. 129-132. <i>Cf.</i> Bṛhaddevatā, viii. 101, with Macdonell's note.</li></ol></div>

ऐति-हासिक|ऐतिहासिक|ऐति|हासिक|ఐతి-హాసిక|ఐతిహాసిక|ఐతి|హాసిక|ಐತಿ-ಹಾಸಿಕ|ಐತಿಹಾಸಿಕ|ಐತಿ|ಹಾಸಿಕ|aiti-hāsika|aitihāsika|aiti|hāsika|aiti-haasika|aitihaasika|haasika|_vei_120@458|_vei1_pn122
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@457">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@459">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn121">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn122">1:122</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn123">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aiti-hāsika.</b>--This term was applied to the people who explained the Vedic hymns by treating them as legendary history (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a></b>), as Sieg<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120458">1</a></sup> shows by the passages of the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120458">2</a></sup> where their views are opposed to those of the <b>Nairuktas,</b> who relied rather on etymology. Sieg<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120458">3</a></sup> also seems right in finding them in the Naidānas of the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>:<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120458">4</a></sup> it is possible that their textbook was called the <a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120458">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 13 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120458">↑</a> ii. 16; xii. 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120458">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 29.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120458">↑</a> vi. 9; vii. 6.</li></ol></div>

ऐभावत|ఐభావత|ಐಭಾವತ|aibhāvata|aibhaavata|_vei_120@459|_vei1_pn122
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@458">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@460">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn121">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn122">1:122</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn123">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aibhāvata,</b> ‘descendant of Ibhāvant,’ is the patronymic of <b>Pratīdarśa.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120459"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120459">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120459"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120459">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 8, 2, 3.</li></ol></div>

ऐरावत|ఐరావత|ಐರಾವತ|airāvata|airaavata|_vei_120@460|_vei1_pn122
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@459">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@461">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn121">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn122">1:122</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn123">↷</a><hr><p><b>Airāvata,</b> ‘son of Irāvant,’ is the patronymic of <a class="vei_120a" href="धृतराष्ट्र">dhṛtarāṣṭra</a>, as a snake demon,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120460"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120460">1</a></sup> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120460"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120460">2</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120460"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120460">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120460"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120460">↑</a> In the later literature <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐरावत">airāvata</a> is the elephant of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>: perhaps connected with this Vedic snake demon. as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नाग">nāga</a></i> means both ‘serpent’ and ‘elephant.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120460"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120460">↑</a> viii. 10, 29.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120460"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120460">↑</a> xxv. 15, 3.</li></ol></div>

ऐलूष|ఐలూష|ಐಲೂಷ|ailūṣa|ailuuSha|ailooSha|_vei_120@461|_vei1_pn122
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@460">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@462">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn121">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn122">1:122</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn123">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ailūṣa,</b> ‘descendant of Ilūṣa,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कवष">kavaṣa</a>.</b></p></div>

ऐष-कृत|ऐषकृत|ऐष|कृत|ఐష-కృత|ఐషకృత|ఐష|కృత|ಐಷ-ಕೃತ|ಐಷಕೃತ|ಐಷ|ಕೃತ|aiṣa-kṛta|aiṣakṛta|aiṣa|kṛta|aiSha-kRta|aiShakRta|aiSha|kRta|aiSha-kRita|aiShakRita|kRita|aiSha-kRuta|aiShakRuta|kRuta|aiSha-kRRita|aiShakRRita|kRRita|_vei_120@462|_vei1_pn122
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@461">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@463">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn121">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn122">1:122</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn123">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aiṣa-kṛta.</b> See <b>Śitibāhu.</b> </p></div>

ऐषा-वीर|ऐषावीर|ऐषा|वीर|ఐషా-వీర|ఐషావీర|ఐషా|వీర|ಐಷಾ-ವೀರ|ಐಷಾವೀರ|ಐಷಾ|ವೀರ|aiṣā-vīra|aiṣāvīra|aiṣā|vīra|aiShaa-viira|aiShaaviira|aiShaa|viira|aiShaa-veera|aiShaaveera|veera|_vei_120@463|_vei1_pn123
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@462">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@464">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn122">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn123">1:123</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn124">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aiṣā-vīra.</b>--The Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> once<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120463">1</a></sup> refers to the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐषा">aiṣā</a>-vīras as officiating at a sacrifice, with the implication that they were bad sacrificers. Sāyaṇa regards the word as a proper name (‘descendants of Eṣavīra’), denoting the members of a despised family. But Roth may be right in explaining the word both in the passage mentioned above and elsewhere as meaning ‘weak’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120463">2</a></sup> or ‘insignificant man.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120463">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120463">↑</a> xi. 2, 7, 32.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120463">↑</a> In the St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120463">↑</a> In Bo7htlingk's Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> (‘one who wishes to be a man, but is not’). <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 5, 1, 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, where, however, Lindner's edition reads <i>saiṣā <a class="vei_120a" href="वीर">vīra</a> iva. Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 228; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 45.</li></ol></div>

ऐषुमत|ఐషుమత|ಐಷುಮತ|aiṣumata|aiShumata|_vei_120@464|_vei1_pn123
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@463">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@465">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn122">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn123">1:123</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn124">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aiṣumata,</b> ‘descendant of Iṣumant,’ is the patronymic of <b>Trāta</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120464"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120464">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120464"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120464">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372.</li></ol></div>

ओगण|ఓగణ|ಓಗಣ|ogaṇa|ogaNa|_vei_120@465|_vei1_pn123
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@464">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@466">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn122">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn123">1:123</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn124">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ogaṇa</b> is a word occurring only once, as a plural, in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120465"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120465">1</a></sup> where it appears to indicate persons hostile to the seer of the hymn, and apparently opposed to the Āryan religion. Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120465"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120465">2</a></sup> regards the terms as the proper name of a people, but Pischel<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120465"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120465">3</a></sup> thinks that it is merely an adjective meaning ‘weak’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ओगण">ogaṇa</a> = <a class="vei_120a" href="अव">ava</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="गण">gaṇa</a></i>), as in Pāli.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120465"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120465">↑</a> x. 89, 15</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120465"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120465">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 5, 209.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120465"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120465">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 191, 192.</li></ol></div>

ओतु|ఓతు|ಓತು|otu|_vei_120@466|_vei1_pn123
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@465">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@467">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn122">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn123">1:123</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn124">↷</a><hr><p><b>Otu</b> in Vedic literature<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120466"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120466">1</a></sup> denotes the ‘woof’ in weaving and corresponds to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तन्तु">tantu</a>,</b> ‘the warp,’ the roots <i>vā,</i><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120466"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120466">2</a></sup> ‘to weave,’ and <i>tan,</i><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120466"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120466">3</a></sup> to stretch,’ from which these two terms are derived, being used in parallel senses. In the process of weaving a shuttle (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="तसर">tasara</a></b>) was used. The ‘weaver’ is termed <i>vāya,</i><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120466"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120466">4</a></sup> and the ‘loom’ <i>veman.</i><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120466"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120466">5</a></sup> A wooden peg (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="मयूख">mayūkha</a></b>) was used to  stretch the web on, while lead was employed as a weight to extend it.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120466"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120466">6</a></sup></p><p>The work of weaving was probably the special care of women.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120466"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120466">7</a></sup> A metaphor in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120466"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120466">8</a></sup> personifies Night and Day as two sisters weaving the web of the year, the nights serving as warp, the days as woof.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120466"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120466">↑</a> Rv. vi. 9, 2. 3; Av. xiv. 2, 51; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 1, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120466"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120466">↑</a> Rv. vi. 9, 2. etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120466"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120466">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 80; Rv. x. 130, 2; Av. x. 7, 43, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120466"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120466">↑</a> Rv. x. 26, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120466"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120466">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 83.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120466"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120466">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 80.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120466"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120466">↑</a> Av. x. 7, 42; xiv. 2, 51. <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 92. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120466"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120466">↑</a> x. 7, 42; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 5, 5, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 254, 255; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 465.</li></ol></div>

ओदन|ఓదన|ಓದನ|odana|_vei_120@467|_vei1_pn124
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@466">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@468">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn123">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn124">1:124</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn125">↷</a><hr><p><b>Odana</b> is a common expression<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120467">1</a></sup> denoting a mess, usually of grain cooked with milk (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षीर">kṣīra</a>-pākam odanam</i>).<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120467">2</a></sup> Special varieties are mentioned, such as the ‘milk-mess’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षीरौदन">kṣīraudana</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120467">3</a></sup> the ‘curd-mess’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दध्य्">dadhy</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="ओदन">odana</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120467">4</a></sup> the ‘bean-mess’ (<i>mudgaudana</i>),<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120467">5</a></sup> the ‘sesame-mess’ (<i>tilaudana</i>),<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120467">6</a></sup> the ‘water-mess’ (<i>udaudana</i>),<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120467">7</a></sup> the ‘meat-mess’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="मांसौदन">māṃsaudana</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120467">8</a></sup> the ‘ghee-mess’ (<i>ghṛtaudana</i>),<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120467">9</a></sup> etc.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120467">↑</a> Rv. viii. 69, 14, etc.; Av. iv. 14, 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120467">↑</a> Rv. viii. 77, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120467">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 5, 3, 4; xi. 5, 7, 5; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120467">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120467">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xii. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120467">↑</a> <i>Ibid.;</i> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120467">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> vi. 4, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120467">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> vi. 4, 16; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 7, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xii. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120467">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xii. 8.</li></ol></div>

ओपश|ఓపశ|ಓಪಶ|opaśa|opasha|_vei_120@468|_vei1_pn124
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@467">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@469">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn123">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn124">1:124</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn125">↷</a><hr><p><b>Opaśa</b> is a word of somewhat doubtful sense, occurring in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120468">1</a></sup> the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120468">2</a></sup> and occasionally later.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120468">3</a></sup> It probably means a ‘plait’ as used in dressing the hair, especially of women,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120468">4</a></sup> but apparently, in earlier times,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120468">5</a></sup> of men also. The goddess <a class="vei_120a" href="सिनीवाली">sinīvālī</a> is called <i>svaupaśā,</i><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120468">6</a></sup> an epithet of doubtful sense, from which Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120468">7</a></sup> conjectures that the wearing of  false plaits of hair was not unknown in Vedic times. What was the difference between the braic referred to in the epithets <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पृथु">pṛthu</a> ṣṭuka,</i><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120468">8</a></sup> ‘having broad braids,’ and <i>viṣita-ṣṭuka,</i><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120468">9</a></sup> ‘having loosened braids,’ and the <a class="vei_120a" href="ओपश">opaśa</a> cannot be made out from the evidence available. Geldner<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120468">10</a></sup> thinks that the original sense was ‘horn,’ and that when the word applies to <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120468">11</a></sup> it means ‘diadem.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120468">↑</a> x. 85. 8, <i>Cf.</i> i. 173, 6; viii. 14, 5; ix. 71. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120468">↑</a> vi. 138, 1. 2; ix. 3, 8, where it is applied metaphorically in describing the roof of a house.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120468">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120468">↑</a> Av. vi. 138. 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120468">↑</a> Rv. i. 173, 6; viii. 14, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120468">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 1, 5, 3; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 7, 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xi. 56. The reading is uncertain. Bloomfield (see below) assumes <i>sv-opaśā</i> to be the correct form (‘having a fair <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ओपश">opaśa</a></i>’).</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120468">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 264.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120468">↑</a> Rv. x. 86, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120468">↑</a> Rv. i. 167, 5 (of Rodasī).</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120468">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 131, quoting <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 3, where <i><a class="vei_120a" href="द्व्य्">dvy</a>-epaśāḥ</i> is used of cattle; but the sense may be figurative.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120468">↑</a> Rv. viii. 14, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 538, 539; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 348.</li></ol></div>

ओषधि|ఓషధి|ಓಷಧಿ|oṣadhi|oShadhi|_vei_120@469|_vei1_pn125
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@468">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@470">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn124">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn125">1:125</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn126">↷</a><hr><p><b>Oṣadhi.</b>--Roughly speaking, the vegetable world is divided in Vedic literature<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120469">1</a></sup> between <a class="vei_120a" href="ओषधि">oṣadhi</a> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वीरुध्">vīrudh</a></b> ‘plants’ and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वन">vana</a></b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वृक्ष">vṛkṣa</a></b> ‘trees.’ <a class="vei_120a" href="ओषधि">oṣadhi</a> is employed in opposition to <a class="vei_120a" href="वीरुध्">vīrudh</a> to denote plants as possessing a healing power or some other quality useful to men, while <a class="vei_120a" href="वीरुध्">vīrudh</a> is rather a generic term for minor vegetable growths, but sometimes,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120469">2</a></sup> when occurring beside <a class="vei_120a" href="ओषधि">oṣadhi</a>, signifies those plants which do not possess medicinal properties.</p><p>A list of the minor parts of which a plant is made up is given in the later Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120469">3</a></sup> It comprises the root (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="मूल">mūla</a></i>), the panicle (<i>tūla</i>), the stem (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="काण्ड">kāṇḍa</a></i>), the twig (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वल्श">valśa</a></i>), the flower (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पुष्प">puṣpa</a></i>), and the fruit (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="फल">phala</a></i>), while trees<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120469">4</a></sup> have, in addition, a corona (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्कन्ध">skandha</a></i>), branches (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शाखा">śākhā</a></i>), and leaves (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्ण">parṇa</a></i>). The Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120469">5</a></sup> gives an elaborate, though not very intelligible, division of plants into those which expand (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्र">pra</a>-stṛṇatīḥ</i>), are bushy (<i>stambinīḥ</i>), have only one sheath (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="एक">eka</a>-śuṅgāḥ</i>), are creepers (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्र">pra</a>-tanvatīḥ</i>), have many stalks (<i>aṃśumatīḥ</i>), are  jointed (<i>kāṇḍinīḥ</i>), or have spreading branches (<i>vi-śākhāḥ</i>). In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120469">6</a></sup> plants are termed ‘fruitful’ (<i>phalinīḥ</i>), ‘blossoming’ (<i>puṣpavatīḥ</i>), and ‘having flowers’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्र">pra</a>-sūvarīḥ</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120469">↑</a> Rv. x. 97 and <i>passim. Oṣadhivanaspati</i> is a frequent compound, from the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vi. 1, 1, 12) onwards. The medicinal properties of plants account for the epithet ‘of manifold powers’ (<i>nānā-vīryā</i>) applied to them in Av. xii. 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120469">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 3, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120469">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> vii. 3, 19, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxii. 28.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120469">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 3, 20, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 32, 5; Av. x. 7, 38.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120469">↑</a> viii. 7, 4, with Whitney's notes. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 579; Henry, <i>Les livres VIII. et IX. de l' Atharvavéda,</i> 58 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120469">↑</a> x. 97. 3. 15. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben.</i> 57.</li></ol></div>

औक्ष-गन्धि|औक्षगन्धि|औक्ष|गन्धि|ఔక్ష-గన్ధి|ఔక్షగన్ధి|ఔక్ష|గన్ధి|ಔಕ್ಷ-ಗನ್ಧಿ|ಔಕ್ಷಗನ್ಧಿ|ಔಕ್ಷ|ಗನ್ಧಿ|aukṣa-gandhi|aukṣagandhi|aukṣa|gandhi|aukSha-gandhi|aukShagandhi|aukSha|auxa-gandhi|auxagandhi|auxa|_vei_120@470|_vei1_pn126
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@469">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@471">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn125">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn126">1:126</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn127">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aukṣa-gandhi</b> (‘having the smell of bull's grease’) appears in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120470"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120470">1</a></sup> as the name of an Apsaras, beside other names, of which <b>Guggulū</b> and <b>Naladī</b> clearly indicate plants. This name, therefore, presumably also denotes some sort of fragrant plant. <a class="vei_120a" href="औक्ष">aukṣa</a> in the same Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120470"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120470">2</a></sup> means ‘bull's grease’ (from <i>akṣan,</i> ‘bull’).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120470"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120470">↑</a> iv. 37. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120470"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120470">↑</a> ii. 36. 7.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 69; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 324; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 211, 212, and on <a class="vei_120a" href="औक्ष">aukṣa</a>, <i>ibid.,</i> 82, 83.</li></ol></div>

औग्र-सैन्य|औग्रसैन्य|औग्र|सैन्य|ఔగ్ర-సైన్య|ఔగ్రసైన్య|ఔగ్ర|సైన్య|ಔಗ್ರ-ಸೈನ್ಯ|ಔಗ್ರಸೈನ್ಯ|ಔಗ್ರ|ಸೈನ್ಯ|augra-sainya|augrasainya|augra|sainya|_vei_120@471|_vei1_pn126
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@470">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@472">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn125">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn126">1:126</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn127">↷</a><hr><p><b>Augra-sainya,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="उग्रसेन">ugrasena</a>,’ is the patronymic of King <b>Yuddhāmśrauṣṭi</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 21).</p></div>

औदन्य|ఔదన్య|ಔದನ್ಯ|audanya|_vei_120@472|_vei1_pn126
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@471">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@473">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn125">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn126">1:126</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn127">↷</a><hr><p><b>Audanya,</b> ‘descendant of Udanya or <a class="vei_120a" href="ओदन">odana</a>,’ is the patronymic in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120472"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120472">1</a></sup> of Muṇḍibha, who is credited with inventing an expiation for the crime of slaying a Brahmin. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120472"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120472">2</a></sup> the name appears in the form of Audanyava.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120472"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120472">↑</a> xiii. 3, 5, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120472"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120472">↑</a> iii. 9, 15, 3. <i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ओदन">odana</a>; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 341, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

औदमय|ఔదమయ|ಔದಮಯ|audamaya|_vei_120@473|_vei1_pn126
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@472">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@474">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn125">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn126">1:126</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn127">↷</a><hr><p><b>Audamaya</b> is Weber's<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120473"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120473">1</a></sup> reading of the name of the <a class="vei_120a" href="आत्रेय">ātreya</a>, who was <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अंग वैरोचन">aṅga vairocana</a>,</b> according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120473"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120473">2</a></sup> Aufrecht, however, in his edition more probably takes the correct form of the name to be <b>Udamaya.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120473"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120473">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 228.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120473"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120473">↑</a> viii. 22. <i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> Udamaya.</li></ol></div>

औद-वाहि|औदवाहि|औद|वाहि|ఔద-వాహి|ఔదవాహి|ఔద|వాహి|ಔದ-ವಾಹಿ|ಔದವಾಹಿ|ಔದ|ವಾಹಿ|auda-vāhi|audavāhi|auda|vāhi|auda-vaahi|audavaahi|vaahi|_vei_120@474|_vei1_pn127
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@473">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@475">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn126">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn127">1:127</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn128">↷</a><hr><p><b>Auda-vāhi,</b> ‘descendant of Udavāha,’ appears in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120474">1</a></sup> as a teacher of <a class="vei_120a" href="भारद्वाज">bhāradvāja</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120474">↑</a> ii. 5, 20; iv. 5, 26 (in the Mādhyaṃdina recension).</li></ol></div>

औद्-उम्बरायण|औदुम्बरायण|औद्|उम्बरायण|औद्-उंबरायण|औदुंबरायण|उंबरायण|ఔద్-ఉమ్బరాయణ|ఔదుమ్బరాయణ|ఔద్|ఉమ్బరాయణ|ఔద్-ఉంబరాయణ|ఔదుంబరాయణ|ఉంబరాయణ|ಔದ್-ಉಮ್ಬರಾಯಣ|ಔದುಮ್ಬರಾಯಣ|ಔದ್|ಉಮ್ಬರಾಯಣ|ಔದ್-ಉಂಬರಾಯಣ|ಔದುಂಬರಾಯಣ|ಉಂಬರಾಯಣ|aud-umbarāyaṇa|audumbarāyaṇa|aud|umbarāyaṇa|aud-umbaraayaNa|audumbaraayaNa|umbaraayaNa|_vei_120@475|_vei1_pn127
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@474">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@476">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn126">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn127">1:127</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn128">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aud-umbarāyaṇa,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="उदुम्बर">udumbara</a>,’ is the patronymic of a grammarian in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> (i. 1).</p></div>

औद्-दालकि|औद्दालकि|औद्|दालकि|ఔద్-దాలకి|ఔద్దాలకి|ఔద్|దాలకి|ಔದ್-ದಾಲಕಿ|ಔದ್ದಾಲಕಿ|ಔದ್|ದಾಲಕಿ|aud-dālaki|auddālaki|aud|dālaki|aud-daalaki|auddaalaki|daalaki|_vei_120@476|_vei1_pn127
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@475">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@477">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn126">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn127">1:127</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn128">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aud-dālaki,</b> ‘descendant of Uddālaka,’ is the patronymic of the teacher variously called <b>Asurbinda</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120476"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120476">1</a></sup> or <b>Kusurubinda,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120476"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120476">2</a></sup> and of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्वेतकेतु">śvetaketu</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120476"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120476">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120476"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120476">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 75 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 23, 327).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120476"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120476">↑</a> Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxii. 15, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120476"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120476">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 3, 13; iv. 2, 5, 15. He is perhaps also meant in Katha <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 11.</li></ol></div>

औद्-भारि|औद्भारि|औद्|भारि|ఔద్-భారి|ఔద్భారి|ఔద్|భారి|ಔದ್-ಭಾರಿ|ಔದ್ಭಾರಿ|ಔದ್|ಭಾರಿ|aud-bhāri|audbhāri|aud|bhāri|aud-bhaari|audbhaari|bhaari|_vei_120@477|_vei1_pn127
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@476">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@478">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn126">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn127">1:127</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn128">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aud-bhāri,</b> ‘descendant of Udbhāra,’ is the patronymic in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xi. 8, 4. 6) of <b>Khaṇḍika,</b> teacher of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="केशिन्">keśin</a>.</b></p></div>

औप-जन्धनि|औपजन्धनि|औप|जन्धनि|ఔప-జన్ధని|ఔపజన్ధని|ఔప|జన్ధని|ಔಪ-ಜನ್ಧನಿ|ಔಪಜನ್ಧನಿ|ಔಪ|ಜನ್ಧನಿ|aupa-jandhani|aupajandhani|aupa|jandhani|_vei_120@478|_vei1_pn127
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@477">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@479">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn126">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn127">1:127</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn128">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aupa-jandhani,</b> ‘descendant of Upajandhana,’ is the patronymic of a teacher mentioned in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120478"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120478">1</a></sup> as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आसुरि">āsuri</a>,</b> and also<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120478"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120478">2</a></sup> as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सायकायन">sāyakāyana</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120478"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120478">↑</a> ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3 (in Vaṃśas).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120478"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120478">↑</a> iv. 5, 27 (in the Mādhyaṃdina recension)</li></ol></div>

औप-तस्विनि|औपतस्विनि|औप|तस्विनि|ఔప-తస్విని|ఔపతస్విని|ఔప|తస్విని|ಔಪ-ತಸ್ವಿನಿ|ಔಪತಸ್ವಿನಿ|ಔಪ|ತಸ್ವಿನಿ|aupa-tasvini|aupatasvini|aupa|tasvini|_vei_120@479|_vei1_pn127
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@478">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@480">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn126">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn127">1:127</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn128">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aupa-tasvini,</b> ‘descendant of Upatasvina,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राम">rāma</a></b> in the Śatapatha <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a></b> (iv. 6, 1. 7).</p></div>

औप-मन्यव|औपमन्यव|औप|मन्यव|ఔప-మన్యవ|ఔపమన్యవ|ఔప|మన్యవ|ಔಪ-ಮನ್ಯವ|ಔಪಮನ್ಯವ|ಔಪ|ಮನ್ಯವ|aupa-manyava|aupamanyava|aupa|manyava|_vei_120@480|_vei1_pn127
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@479">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@481">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn126">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn127">1:127</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn128">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aupa-manyava,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="उपमन्यु">upamanyu</a>,’ is the patronymic of various persons: see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काम्बोज">kāmboja</a>, Prācīnaśāla, <a class="vei_120a" href="महाशाल">mahāśāla</a>.</b> The best known bearer of the name is the grammarian who disagreed with the onomatopetic theory of the derivation of names, and who is mentioned by <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120480"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120480">1</a></sup> An Aupamanyavīputra occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120480"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120480">2</a></sup> as a teacher. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120480"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120480">↑</a> i. 1; ii. 2. 6. 11, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120480"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120480">↑</a> xxii. 1 <i>et seq</i></li></ol></div>

औपर|ఔపర|ಔಪರ|aupara|_vei_120@481|_vei1_pn128
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@480">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@482">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn127">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn128">1:128</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn129">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aupara,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="उपर">upara</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दण्ड">daṇḍa</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (vi. 2, 9, 4).</p></div>

औप-वेशि|औपवेशि|औप|वेशि|ఔప-వేశి|ఔపవేశి|ఔప|వేశి|ಔಪ-ವೇಶಿ|ಔಪವೇಶಿ|ಔಪ|ವೇಶಿ|aupa-veśi|aupaveśi|aupa|veśi|aupa-veshi|aupaveshi|veshi|_vei_120@482|_vei1_pn128
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@481">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@483">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn127">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn128">1:128</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn129">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aupa-veśi,</b> ‘descendant of Upaveśa,’ is the patronymic borne by <b>Aruṇa,</b> father of Uddālaka.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120482"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120482">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120482"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120482">↑</a> See <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxvi. 10, and <b>Aruṇa.</b></li></ol></div>

औपस्वती-पुत्र|औपस्वतीपुत्र|औपस्वती|औपस्वति|पुत्र|ఔపస్వతీ-పుత్ర|ఔపస్వతీపుత్ర|ఔపస్వతీ|ఔపస్వతి|పుత్ర|ಔಪಸ್ವತೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಔಪಸ್ವತೀಪುತ್ರ|ಔಪಸ್ವತೀ|ಔಪಸ್ವತಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|aupasvatī-putra|aupasvatīputra|aupasvatī|aupasvati|putra|aupasvatii-putra|aupasvatiiputra|aupasvatii|aupasvatee-putra|aupasvateeputra|aupasvatee|_vei_120@483|_vei1_pn128
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@482">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@484">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn127">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn128">1:128</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn129">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aupasvatī-putra,</b> ‘son of a female descendant of Upasvant’(?), is mentioned as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पाराशरीपुत्र">pārāśarīputra</a></b> in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120483"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120483">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120483"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120483">↑</a> vi. 5, 1 (only in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension).</li></ol></div>

औपावि|ఔపావి|ಔಪಾವಿ|aupāvi|aupaavi|_vei_120@484|_vei1_pn128
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@483">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@485">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn127">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn128">1:128</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn129">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aupāvi</b> (‘descendant of Upāva’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जान-श्रुतेय">jāna-śruteya</a></b> (‘descendant of Janaśruti’), appears in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120484"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120484">1</a></sup> and the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120484"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120484">2</a></sup> as a sacrificer who used to offer the <a class="vei_120a" href="वाजपेय">vājapeya</a> sacrifice and ascend to the other world.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120484"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120484">↑</a> v. 1, 1, 5. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120484"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120484">↑</a> i. 4, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 222, 223.</li></ol></div>

औपोदिति|ఔపోదితి|ಔಪೋದಿತಿ|aupoditi|_vei_120@485|_vei1_pn128
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@484">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@486">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn127">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn128">1:128</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn129">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aupoditi,</b> ‘descendant of Upodita,’ is the patronymic applied in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120485">1</a></sup> to <b>Tumiñja,</b> and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120485">2</a></sup> to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौपालायन">gaupālāyana</a>,</b> son of <b>Vyāghrapad,</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="स्थपति">sthapati</a> (‘general’) of the Kurus. In the form of Aupoditeya, a metronymic from Upoditā, the name is found in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120485">3</a></sup> where the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> text calls him Tumiñja Aupoditeya <a class="vei_120a" href="वैयाघ्रपद्य">vaiyāghrapadya</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120485">↑</a> i. 7, 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120485">↑</a> xx. 25.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120485">↑</a> i. 9, 3. 16.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East.</i> 12, 271, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

और्ण-वाभ|और्णवाभ|और्ण|वाभ|ఔర్ణ-వాభ|ఔర్ణవాభ|ఔర్ణ|వాభ|ಔರ್ಣ-ವಾಭ|ಔರ್ಣವಾಭ|ಔರ್ಣ|ವಾಭ|aurṇa-vābha|aurṇavābha|aurṇa|vābha|aurNa-vaabha|aurNavaabha|aurNa|vaabha|_vei_120@486|_vei1_pn128
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@485">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@487">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn127">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn128">1:128</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn129">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aurṇa-vābha,</b> ‘descendant of Ūrṇavābhi.’ (1) This is the name of a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौण्डिन्य">kauṇḍinya</a></b> mentioned in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120486"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120486">1</a></sup> (2) A teacher of this name is frequently referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>. His explanations in two passages<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120486"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120486">2</a></sup> agree with those of the Nairuktas  or etymological school of interpreters of the Rigveda. In other passages<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120486"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120486">3</a></sup> he appears rather to belong to the school of the Aitihāsikas, who relied on traditional legends. He was thus probably, as Sieg<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120486"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120486">4</a></sup> suggests, an eclectic.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120486"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120486">↑</a> iv. 5, 26 (Mādhyaṃdina recension).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120486"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120486">↑</a> vii. 15; xii. 19.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120486"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120486">↑</a> vi. 13; xii. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120486"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120486">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 13, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

और्व|ఔర్వ|ಔರ್ವ|aurva|_vei_120@487|_vei1_pn129
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@486">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@488">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn128">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn129">1:129</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn130">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aurva,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="उरु">uru</a> or Urva,’ appears in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120487"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120487">1</a></sup> in close connexion with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भृगु">bhṛgu</a>,</b> being probably a <a class="vei_120a" href="भृगु">bhṛgu</a> himself. As in one passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120487"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120487">2</a></sup> the descendants of <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतश">aitaśa</a> are called the worst of the Aurvas, while the parallel version of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120487"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120487">3</a></sup> calls them the worst of the Bhṛgus, the Aurvas must have been a branch of the larger family of the Bhṛgus. <a class="vei_120a" href="और्व">aurva</a> himself is said in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120487"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120487">4</a></sup> to have received offspring from <a class="vei_120a" href="अत्रि">atri</a>. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120487"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120487">5</a></sup> two Aurvas are referred to as authorities. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुत्स">kutsa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120487"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120487">↑</a> viii. 102, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120487"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120487">↑</a> vi. 33.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120487"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120487">↑</a> xxx. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120487"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120487">↑</a> vii. 1, 8, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120487"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120487">↑</a> xxi. 10, 6. Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 54, reads <i>ūrvau.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 173, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

औलान|ఔలాన|ಔಲಾನ|aulāna|aulaana|_vei_120@488|_vei1_pn129
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@487">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@489">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn128">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn129">1:129</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn130">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aulāna</b> is a word occurring in a single passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120488"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120488">1</a></sup> where it may possibly be a patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शंतनु">śaṃtanu</a></b> as a ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="उल">ula</a>.’ Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120488"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120488">2</a></sup> however, conjectures that the reading should be ‘Kaulāna.’ Sieg<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120488"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120488">3</a></sup> regards <a class="vei_120a" href="औलान">aulāna</a> as a later descendant<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120488"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120488">4</a></sup> of <a class="vei_120a" href="शंतनु">śaṃtanu</a>, who utilized the story of Devāpi's rain-making as an introduction to his rain hymn.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120488"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120488">↑</a> x. 98, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120488"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120488">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 166.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120488"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120488">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 141.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120488"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120488">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Sāyaṇa on Rv. x, 98, 11; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कुल">kula</a>-jātaḥ Śāṃtaṇavaḥ,</i> ‘a descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="शंतनु">śaṃtanu</a>, born in the family of the Kurus</li></ol></div>

औलुण्ड्य|ఔలుణ్డ్య|ಔಲುಣ್ಡ್ಯ|auluṇḍya|auluNDya|_vei_120@489|_vei1_pn129
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@488">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@490">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn128">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn129">1:129</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn130">↷</a><hr><p><b>Auluṇḍya,</b> ‘descendant of Uluṇḍa,’ is the patronymic of <b>Supratīta</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120489"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120489">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120489"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120489">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372.</li></ol></div>

औशिज|ఔశిజ|ಔಶಿಜ|auśija|aushija|_vei_120@490|_vei1_pn130
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@489">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@491">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn129">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn130">1:130</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn131">↷</a><hr><p><b>Auśija,</b> ‘descendant of Uśij,’ is a patronymic clearly applied to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a></b> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120490">1</a></sup> It is also applied to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋजिश्वन्">ṛjiśvan</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120490">2</a></sup> but Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120490">3</a></sup> thinks that the correct reading of the passage in question is <i>auśijasyarjiśvā,</i> ‘Ṛjiśvan, son of <a class="vei_120a" href="औशिज">auśija</a>.’ In one verse<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120490">4</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="औशिज">auśija</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a> are both mentioned, but in such a way that two different persons must apparently be meant. In other passages where the patronymic occurs alone, it is doubtful who is meant, or whether a proper name at all is intended.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120490">5</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="औशिज">auśija</a> appears also in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120490">6</a></sup> and elsewhere.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120490">↑</a> i. 18, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120490">↑</a> x. 99, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120490">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 143, 149.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120490">↑</a> Rv. i. 112, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120490">↑</a> Rv. i. 119, 9; 122, 4; iv. 21, 6, 7; v. 41, 5; vi. 4, 6. <i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120490">↑</a> xiv. 11, 16. See Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> xv. 56, n., and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a>,</b> n. 15.</li></ol></div>

औष्ट्राक्षि|ఔష్ట్రాక్షి|ಔಷ್ಟ್ರಾಕ್ಷಿ|auṣṭrākṣi|auShTraakShi|auShTraaxi|_vei_120@491|_vei1_pn130
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@490">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@492">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn129">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn130">1:130</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn131">↷</a><hr><p><b>Auṣṭrākṣi,</b> ‘descendant of Uṣṭrākṣa,’ occurs as the patronymic of <b>Sāti</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120491"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120491">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120491"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120491">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 75.</li></ol></div>

कंस|కంస|ಕಂಸ|kaṃsa|kaMsa|_vei_120@492|_vei1_pn130
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@491">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@493">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn129">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn130">1:130</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn131">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaṃsa,</b> a word denoting a ‘pot or vessel of metal,’ occurs in the Atharvaveda and elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120492">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120492">↑</a> Av. x. 10, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 10; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 3, 1, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vii. 23; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xii. 8.</li></ol></div>

ककर|కకర|ಕಕರ|kakara|_vei_120@493|_vei1_pn130
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@492">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@494">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn129">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn130">1:130</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn131">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kakara</b> occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120493"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120493">1</a></sup> as the name of a victim at the horse sacrifice (<a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>). It probably denotes some ‘sort of bird,’ as rendered by the commentator Mahīdhara.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120493"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120493">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120493"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120493">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xx. 24.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120493"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120493">↑</a> On Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>. Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 94.</li></ol></div>

ककुठ|కకుఠ|ಕಕುಠ|kakuṭha|kakuTha|_vei_120@494|_vei1_pn130
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@493">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@495">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn129">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn130">1:130</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn131">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kakuṭha,</b> a word occurring in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120494"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120494">1</a></sup> presumably denotes some kind of animal. According to Bo7htlingk,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120494"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120494">2</a></sup> it is identical with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कक्कट">kakkaṭa</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120494"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120494">↑</a> iii. 14, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120494"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120494">↑</a> Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

ककुह|కకుహ|ಕಕುಹ|kakuha|_vei_120@495|_vei1_pn131
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@494">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@496">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn130">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn131">1:131</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn132">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kakuha,</b> a word occurring several times in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120495">1</a></sup> is understood by Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120495">2</a></sup> to designate part of a chariot, perhaps the seat. Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120495">3</a></sup> again, regards it in one passage<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120495">4</a></sup> as the proper name of a Yādava prince who took spoil from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तिरिन्दिर">tirindira</a>,</b> the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्शु">parśu</a>,</b> but this view is hardly probable.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120495">5</a></sup> It is, on the whole, most likely that the word always means ‘chief,’ ‘pre-eminent,’ being applied as an epithet to horses, chariots, princes, etc.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120495">6</a></sup> This is the only sense given by Grassmann,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120495">7</a></sup> and later adopted by Roth.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120495">8</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120495">↑</a> i. 46, 3; 181 5; 184, 3; ii. 34, 11; iii. 54, 14; v. 73, 7; 75, 4; viii. 6, 48.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120495">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120495">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 2, 182; 3, 160, 161; 5, 142.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120495">↑</a> viii. 6, 48.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120495">↑</a> Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 36, 37.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120495">↑</a> So certainly in Rv. viii. 45, 14; ix. 67, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 3, 3, 1. 2, and often in the older form <i>kakubha.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120495">↑</a> In his Lexicon, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120495">↑</a> In Bo7htlingk's Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

कक्कट|కక్కట|ಕಕ್ಕಟ|kakkaṭa|kakkaTa|_vei_120@496|_vei1_pn131
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@495">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@497">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn130">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn131">1:131</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn132">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kakkaṭa</b> denotes the ‘crab’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120496">1</a></sup> being a Prākritized form of Karkaṭa, which is common in the later literature.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120496">2</a></sup> Roth,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120496">3</a></sup> however, takes the word to mean a bird, and compares <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ककर">kakara</a>.</b> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ककुठ">kakuṭha</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120496">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 15, 1 (where Weber has <i>katkaṭa</i>); Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 32.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120496">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 95.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120496">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

कक्ष|కక్ష|ಕಕ್ಷ|kakṣa|kakSha|kaxa|_vei_120@497|_vei1_pn131
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@496">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@498">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn130">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn131">1:131</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn132">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kakṣa</b> is the name of two men mentioned as teachers in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>. One is <a class="vei_120a" href="कक्ष">kakṣa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वारक्य">vārakya</a>, pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रोष्ठपद">proṣṭhapada</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वारक्य">vārakya</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120497">1</a></sup> and the other <a class="vei_120a" href="कक्ष">kakṣa</a> Vārāki<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120497">2</a></sup> or Vārakyā,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120497">3</a></sup> pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दक्ष कात्यायनि आत्रेय">dakṣa <a class="vei_120a" href="कात्यायनि">kātyāyani</a> ātreya</a>.</b> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उरुकक्ष">urukakṣa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120497">↑</a> iii. 41, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120497">↑</a> iii. 41, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120497">↑</a> iv. 17, 1.</li></ol></div>

कक्षीवन्त्|కక్షీవన్త్|ಕಕ್ಷೀವನ್ತ್|kakṣīvant|kakShiivant|kakSheevant|kaxiivant|_vei_120@498|_vei1_pn131
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@497">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@499">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn130">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn131">1:131</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn132">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kakṣīvant</b> is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> mentioned frequently in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120498">1</a></sup> and occasionally elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120498">2</a></sup> He appears to have been a descendant of a female slave named Uśij.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120498">3</a></sup> He must have been a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पज्र">pajra</a></b> by family, as he bears the epithet <a class="vei_120a" href="पज्रिय">pajriya</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120498">4</a></sup>  and his descendants are called Pajras.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120498">5</a></sup> In a hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120498">6</a></sup> he celebrates the prince <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्वनय भाव्य">svanaya bhāvya</a>,</b> who dwelt on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धु">sindhu</a></b> (Indus), as having bestowed magnificent gifts on him; and the list of Nārāśaṃsas (‘Praises of Heroes’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120498">7</a></sup> mentions one by <a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="औशिज">auśija</a> in honour of Svanaya <a class="vei_120a" href="भावयव्य">bhāvayavya</a>. In his old age he obtained as a wife the maiden <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वृचया">vṛcayā</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120498">8</a></sup> He appears to have lived to be a hundred,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120498">9</a></sup> the typical length of life in the Vedas. He seems always to be thought of as belonging to the past, and in a hymn of the fourth book of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120498">10</a></sup> he is mentioned with the semi-mythical <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुत्स">kutsa</a></b> and <b>Kavi Uśanas.</b> Later, also, he is a teacher of bygone days.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120498">11</a></sup></p><p>In Vedic literature he is not connected with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दीर्घतमस्">dīrghatamas</a></b> beyond being once mentioned along with him in a hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120498">12</a></sup> But in the Bṛhaddevatā<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120498">13</a></sup> he appears as a son of <a class="vei_120a" href="दीर्घतमस्">dīrghatamas</a> by a slave woman, Uśij.</p><p>Weber<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120498">14</a></sup> considers that <a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a> was originally a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>, not a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, quoting in favour of this view the fact that he is mentioned beside kings like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर आट्णार">para āṭṇāra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वीतहव्य">vītahavya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रायस">śrāyasa</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a> Paurukutsya.</b><sup id="ref_120_15_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_120498">15</a></sup> But that these are all kings is an unnecessary assumption: these persons are mentioned in the passages in question undoubtedly only as famous men of old, to whom are ascribed mythical sacrificial performances, and who thus gained numerous sons.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120498">↑</a> i. 18, 1; 51, 13; 112, 11; 116, 7; 117, 6; 126, 3; iv. 26, 1; viii. 9, 10; ix. 74, 8; x. 25, 10; 61, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120498">↑</a> Av. iv. 29, 5, and passage noted below.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120498">↑</a> Rv. i. 18, 1; perhaps also i, 112, 11, but <a class="vei_120a" href="औशिज">auśija</a> may there be a separate name (see <a class="vei_120a" href="औशिज">auśija</a>). <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 11, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120498">↑</a> Rv. i. 116, 7; 117, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120498">↑</a> Rv. i. 126, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120498">↑</a> i. 126.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120498">↑</a> xvi. 4, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120498">↑</a> Rv. i. 51, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120498">↑</a> Rv. ix. 74, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120498">↑</a> iv. 26, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120498">↑</a> Av. iv. 29, 5; xviii. 3, 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 21, 6. 7; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120498">↑</a> viii. 9, 10</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120498">↑</a> iv. 11 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120498">↑</a> <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 22-25.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_120498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_120498">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 6, 5, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxii. 3; Pancaviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 16, 3, <i>Cf.</i> xiv. 11, 16.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 221, 236, n. 1; Ludwig Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 102; Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Kommentar,</i> 23, 24.</li></ol></div>

कंक|कङ्क|కంక|కఙ్క|ಕಂಕ|ಕಙ್ಕ|kaṅka|kaMka|kanka|_vei_120@499|_vei1_pn132
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@498">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@500">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn131">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn132">1:132</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn133">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaṅka</b> is the name of a bird, usually taken to mean ‘heron,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120499">1</a></sup> but, at any rate in some passages, rather denoting some bird of prey.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120499">2</a></sup> It first appears in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120499">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120499">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 92.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120499">↑</a> Roth. St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. Cf.</i> Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, xii. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120499">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 4, 11, 1 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कंक">kaṅka</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>,</i> an altar ‘piled in the form of a heron’); Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 31; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>, ii. 9, 3, 6, 1</li></ol></div>

कंकत|कङ्कत|కంకత|కఙ్కత|ಕಂಕತ|ಕಙ್ಕತ|kaṅkata|kaMkata|kankata|_vei_120@500|_vei1_pn133
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@499">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@501">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn132">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn133">1:133</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn134">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaṅkata</b> is the name of an animal mentioned once in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120500"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120500">1</a></sup> According to Sāyaṇa it is a destructive beast; perhaps, as Grassmann renders it, a ‘scorpion.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120500"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120500">↑</a> i. 191, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 98.</li></ol></div>

कंकतीय|कङ्कतीय|కంకతీయ|కఙ్కతీయ|ಕಂಕತೀಯ|ಕಙ್ಕತೀಯ|kaṅkatīya|kaMkatiiya|kaMkateeya|kankatiiya|kankateeya|_vei_120@501|_vei1_pn133
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@500">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@502">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn132">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn133">1:133</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn134">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaṅkatīya</b> is the name of a family said in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120501">1</a></sup> to have learned from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्डिल्य">śāṇḍilya</a></b> the piling up of the sacrificial firse (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>-cayana.</i>) In the Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120501">2</a></sup> a Kaṅkati <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, no doubt the textbook of the school, is referred to. It may have been identical with the Chāgaleya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, cited in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120501">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120501">↑</a> ix. 4, 4, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120501">↑</a> xiv. 20, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120501">↑</a> xxv. 5. <i>Cf.</i> Caland, <i>Über das vituclle <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> des <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>,</i> 40.</li></ol></div>

कंक-पर्वन्|कंकपर्वन्|कंक|पर्वन्|कङ्क-पर्वन्|कङ्कपर्वन्|कङ्क|కంక-పర్వన్|కంకపర్వన్|కంక|పర్వన్|కఙ్క-పర్వన్|కఙ్కపర్వన్|కఙ్క|ಕಂಕ-ಪರ್ವನ್|ಕಂಕಪರ್ವನ್|ಕಂಕ|ಪರ್ವನ್|ಕಙ್ಕ-ಪರ್ವನ್|ಕಙ್ಕಪರ್ವನ್|ಕಙ್ಕ|kaṅka-parvan|kaṅkaparvan|kaṅka|parvan|kaMka-parvan|kaMkaparvan|kaMka|kanka-parvan|kankaparvan|kanka|_vei_120@502|_vei1_pn133
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@501">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@503">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn132">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn133">1:133</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn134">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaṅka-parvan</b> (‘heron-jointed’?), a term occurring once in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120502"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120502">1</a></sup> is applied to a snake, meaning perhaps ‘scorpion.’ As the Paippalāda recension has a different reading (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अंग">aṅga</a>-parvaṇaḥ</i>), the passage may be corrupt.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120502"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120502">↑</a> vii. 56, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 94; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 426; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 553; Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

कट|కట|ಕಟ|kaṭa|kaTa|_vei_120@503|_vei1_pn133
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@502">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@504">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn132">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn133">1:133</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn134">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaṭa</b> denotes a ‘mat,’ which was ‘made of reeds’ (<i>vaitasa</i>). The maker of mats from reeds (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="बिदल-कारी">bidala-kārī</a></i>) is mentioned in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120503">2</a></sup> and the process of splitting reeds for the purpose is referred to in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120503">3</a></sup></p><p>^1) <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā. v. 3, 12, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 3, 1, 3.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="2"><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120503">↑</a> xxx. 8, with Mahīdhara's Commentary. <i><a class="vei_120a" href="बिदल">bidala</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कार">kāra</a></i> is read in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 5, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120503">↑</a> vi. 138, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 255.</li></ol></div>

कण्टकी-कारी|कण्टकी-कारि|कण्टकीकारी|कण्टकीकारि|कण्टकी|कण्टकि|कारी|कारि|కణ్టకీ-కారీ|కణ్టకీ-కారి|కణ్టకీకారీ|కణ్టకీకారి|కణ్టకీ|కణ్టకి|కారీ|కారి|ಕಣ್ಟಕೀ-ಕಾರೀ|ಕಣ್ಟಕೀ-ಕಾರಿ|ಕಣ್ಟಕೀಕಾರೀ|ಕಣ್ಟಕೀಕಾರಿ|ಕಣ್ಟಕೀ|ಕಣ್ಟಕಿ|ಕಾರೀ|ಕಾರಿ|kaṇṭakī-kārī|kaṇṭakī-kāri|kaṇṭakīkārī|kaṇṭakīkāri|kaṇṭakī|kaṇṭaki|kārī|kāri|kaNTakii-kaarii|kaNTakii-kaari|kaNTakiikaarii|kaNTakiikaari|kaNTakii|kaNTaki|kaarii|kaari|kaNTakee-kaaree|kaNTakee-kaari|kaNTakeekaaree|kaNTakeekaari|kaNTakee|kaaree|_vei_120@504|_vei1_pn133
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@503">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@505">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn132">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn133">1:133</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn134">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaṇṭakī-kārī,</b> ‘worker in thorns,’ is one of the victims at the human sacrifice (Puruṣamedha) in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120504"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120504">1</a></sup> No doubt the thorns were cut up and used to plait mats (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="कट">kaṭa</a></b>) or to stuff cushions. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120504"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120504">↑</a> xxx. 8. The <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 5, 1, has <i>kaṇṭaka-<a class="vei_120a" href="कार">kāra</a>. Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 255.</li></ol></div>

कण्व|కణ్వ|ಕಣ್ವ|kaṇva|kaNva|_vei_120@505|_vei1_pn134
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@504">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@506">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn133">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn134">1:134</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn135">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaṇva</b> is the name of an ancient <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> repeatedly referred to in the Rigveda and later.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120505"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120505">1</a></sup> His sons and descendants,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120505"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120505">2</a></sup> the Kaṇvas, are also often mentioned, especially in the eighth book of the Rigveda, the authorship of that book, as well as of part of the first, being attributed to this family. A descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a> is also denoted by the name in the singular, either alone<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120505"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120505">3</a></sup> or accompanied by a patronymic, as <a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="नार्षद">nārṣada</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120505"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120505">4</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रायस">śrāyasa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120505"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120505">5</a></sup> besides in the plural the Kaṇvas Sauśravasas.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120505"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120505">6</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a> family appears to have been connected with the <a class="vei_120a" href="अत्रि">atri</a> family,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120505"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120505">7</a></sup> but not to have been of great importance.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120505"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120505">8</a></sup> In one passage of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120505"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120505">9</a></sup> they seem to be definitely regarded with hostility.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120505"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120505">↑</a> Rv. i. 36, 8. 10. 11. 17. 19; 39, 7. 9; 47, 5; 112, 5; 117, 18; 118, 7; 139, 9; v. 41, 4; viii. 5, 23. 25; 7, 18; 8, 20; 49, 10; 50, 10; x. 71, 11; 115, 5; 150, 5; Av. iv. 37, 1; vii. 15, 1; xviii. 3, 15; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvii. 74; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 2, 2; ix. 2, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxviii. 8, Kaṇvavat occurs in Rv. viii. 6, 11; 52, 8; Av. ii. 32, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a>-mant in Rv. viii. 2, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120505"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120505">↑</a> As <i>Kaṇvāḥ</i> (in the plural), Rv. i. 14, 2. 5; 37, 1. 14; 44, 8; 46, 9; 47. 2. 4-10; 49, 4; viii. 2, 16; 3, 16; 4, 2. 3; 5, 4; 6, 3. 18. 21. 31. 34. 47; 7, 32; 8, 3; 9, 14; 32, 1; 33, 3; 34, 4; as <i>Kaṇvasya sūnavaḥ,</i> Rv. i, 45, 5; as <i>putrāḥ,</i> viii. 8, 4. 8; as <i>Kāṇvāyanāḥ,</i> viii. 55, 4. <i><a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a></i> is found in viii. 1, 8; 2, 40; 4, 20; 7, 19; 9, 3, 9; 10, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120505"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120505">↑</a> <i>Eg.,</i> Rv. i. 48, 4; viii. 34, 1, and probably elsewhere.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120505"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120505">↑</a> Rv. i. 117, 8; Av. iv. 19, 2; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 150.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120505"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120505">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 4, 7, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxi. 8; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 3, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120505"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120505">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xiii. 12. There is also <a class="vei_120a" href="वत्स">vatsa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> in <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 11, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120505"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120505">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 214.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120505"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120505">↑</a> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 285. <i>Cf.</i> 1, 207, 438.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120505"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120505">↑</a> Av. ii. 25. <i>Cf.</i> Vārttika on Pāṇini, iii. 1, 14; Bergaigne, <i>Religion Védique,</i> 2, 465; Hillebrandt, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 207; Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 110.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 216 <i>et seq.;</i> Ludwig, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 3, 105.</li></ol></div>

कथा|కథా|ಕಥಾ|kathā|kathaa|_vei_120@506|_vei1_pn134
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@505">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@507">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn133">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn134">1:134</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn135">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kathā.</b>--The later use of this word<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120506">1</a></sup> in the sense of a ‘philosophical discussion’ appears in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120506">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120506">↑</a> Colebrooke, <i>Miscellaneous Essays,</i> 1, 293.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120506">↑</a> i. 8, 1: <i>hantodgīthe kathāṃ vadāma,</i> ‘let us begin a discussion regarding the Udgītha.’</li></ol></div>

कद्रू|కద్రూ|ಕದ್ರೂ|kadrū|kadruu|kadroo|_vei_120@507|_vei1_pn134
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@506">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@508">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn133">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn134">1:134</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn135">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kadrū,</b> a word occurring only once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120507"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120507">1</a></sup> is interpreted by Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120507"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120507">2</a></sup> as the name of a priest, but it more probably means a <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> vessel.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120507"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120507">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120507"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120507">↑</a> viii. 45, 26.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120507"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120507">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 162.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120507"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120507">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

कनक्नक|కనక్నక|ಕನಕ್ನಕ|kanaknaka|_vei_120@508|_vei1_pn135
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@507">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@509">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn134">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn135">1:135</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn136">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kanaknaka,</b> a word occurring once in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120508"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120508">1</a></sup> either denotes a poison or is an adjective qualifying <i><a class="vei_120a" href="काण्डा-विष">kāṇḍā-viṣa</a>,</i> a species of poison.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120508"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120508">↑</a> x. 4, 22. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 604; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 578.</li></ol></div>

कना|कन्या|కనా|కన్యా|ಕನಾ|ಕನ್ಯಾ|kanā|kanyā|kanaa|kanyaa|_vei_120@509|_vei1_pn135
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@508">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@510">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn134">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn135">1:135</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn136">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kanā, Kanyā.</b>--Both these words, of which the former is very rare,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120509"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120509">1</a></sup> while the latter is the normal term from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120509"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120509">2</a></sup> onwards, denote ‘maiden’ or ‘young woman.’ It is doubtful whether Kanīnakā (accented on the final syllable) has this meaning,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120509"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120509">3</a></sup> or only denotes the pupil of the eye,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120509"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120509">4</a></sup> which is the sense of <i>kanīnakā</i> or <i>kanīnikā</i> (both accented on the antepenultimate) in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्त्री">strī</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120509"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120509">↑</a> Rv. x. 61, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120509"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120509">↑</a> i. 123, 10; 161, 5; iii. 23, 10, etc.; Av. i. 14, 2; xi. 5, 18; xii, 1, 25, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120509"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120509">↑</a> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> cites in this sense Rv. iv. 32, 23; x. 40, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iv. 15; but neither of the Rv. passages is at all clear.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120509"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120509">↑</a> See Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 401; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyāka,</i> 207. Other rare forms are <i>kanyanā,</i> Rv. viii. 35, 5; <i>kauyalā,</i> Av. v. 5, 3; xiv. 2, 52.</li></ol></div>

कपना|కపనా|ಕಪನಾ|kapanā|kapanaa|_vei_120@510|_vei1_pn135
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@509">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@511">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn134">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn135">1:135</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn136">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kapanā,</b> from its solitary occurrence in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120510">1</a></sup> appears to mean a ‘worm’ that destroys the leaves of trees, and is so interpreted in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120510">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120510">↑</a> v. 54, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120510">↑</a> vi. 4. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 97; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 330.</li></ol></div>

कपर्द|కపర్ద|ಕಪರ್ದ|kaparda|_vei_120@511|_vei1_pn135
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@510">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@512">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn134">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn135">1:135</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn136">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaparda,</b> ‘braid,’ <b>Kapardin,</b> ‘wearing braids.’ These words refer to the Vedic custom of wearing the hair in braids or plaits. Thus a maiden is said to have her hair in four plaits (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="चतुष्">catuṣ</a>-kapardā</i>),<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120511">1</a></sup> and the goddess <a class="vei_120a" href="सिनीवाली">sinīvālī</a> is described as ‘wearing fair braids’ (<i>su-kapardā</i>).<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120511">2</a></sup> Men also wore their hair in this style, for both <a class="vei_120a" href="रुद्र">rudra</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120511">3</a></sup> and Pūṣan<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120511">4</a></sup> are said to have done so, while the <b>Vasiṣṭhas</b><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120511">5</a></sup> were distinguished by wearing their hair in a plait on the right (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दक्षिणतस्-कपर्द">dakṣiṇatas-kaparda</a></i>). The opposite was to wear one's hair ‘plain’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पुलस्ति">pulasti</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120511">6</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ओपश">opaśa</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120511">↑</a> Rv. x. 114, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120511">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xi. 56.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120511">↑</a> Rv. i. 114, 1. 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 10. 29. 43. 48. 59.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120511">↑</a> Rv. vi. 55, 2; ix. 67. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120511">↑</a> Rv. vii. 33, 1. <i>Cf.</i> 83, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120511">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 43. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 264, 265; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> v. 462; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 424.</li></ol></div>

कपि|कपि|కపి_౧|కపి|ಕಪಿ_೧|ಕಪಿ|kapi_1|kapi|_vei_120@512|_vei1_pn136
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@511">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@513">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn135">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn136">1:136</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn137">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Kapi,</b> ‘monkey,’ occurs only once in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120512"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120512">1</a></sup> with reference to <a class="vei_120a" href="वृषा">vṛṣā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कपि">kapi</a>, the ‘Man-ape,’ in the dialogue of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> and Indrāṇī in the presence of Vṛṣākapi. There the ape is termed the ‘tawny’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="हरित">harita</a></i>). In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120512"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120512">2</a></sup> the monkey is mentioned several times as hairy, and an enemy of dogs. That the ape was tamed appears from its position in the Vṛṣākapi hymn, and from the mention, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120512"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120512">3</a></sup> of a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मयु">mayu</a></b> as belonging to the forest. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मयु">mayu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मर्कट">markaṭa</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुष हस्तिन्">puruṣa hastin</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120512"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120512">↑</a> x. 86. 5. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 174; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 22 <i>et seq.;</i> von Schroeder, <i>Mysterium und Mimus,</i> 304 <i>et seq.;</i> Schirmeisen, <i>Die Arischen Go7ttergestalten,</i> 218 <i>et seq.;</i> Tilak, <i>Orion,</i> 170-197.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120512"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120512">↑</a> iii. 9, 4; iv. 32, 11; vi. 49, 1. <i>Cf.</i> also Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 6, 7 (<i>kapy-<a class="vei_120a" href="आस">āsa</a>,</i> ‘seat of an ape’).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120512"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120512">↑</a> iv. 2, 10, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 85, 86.</li></ol></div>

कपि|कपि|కపి_౨|కపి|ಕಪಿ_೨|ಕಪಿ|kapi_2|kapi|_vei_120@513|_vei1_pn136
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@512">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@514">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn135">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn136">1:136</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn137">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Kapi</b> is, according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, another name for <a class="vei_120a" href="लुश">luśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="खार्गलि">khārgali</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā (xxx. 2), but the name appears rather to be <b>Luśākapi.</b></p></div>

कपिञ्जल|కపిఞ్జల|ಕಪಿಞ್ಜಲ|kapiñjala|kapi~njala|_vei_120@514|_vei1_pn136
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@513">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@515">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn135">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn136">1:136</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn137">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kapiñjala,</b> the name of the ‘francoline partridge’ or ‘hazelcock,’ is found in all the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120514">1</a></sup> and occasionally later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120514">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120514">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 1, 1; v. 5, 16, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 10; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 20. 38.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120514">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 3, 3; v. 5, 4, 4; xiii. 5, 1, 13; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 154, 2 (<i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 181).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 91.</li></ol></div>

कपिल|కపిల|ಕಪಿಲ|kapila|_vei_120@515|_vei1_pn136
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@514">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@516">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn135">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn136">1:136</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn137">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kapila</b> appears in the Śvetāśvatara <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120515">1</a></sup> as a teacher, according to Weber<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120515">2</a></sup> and Garbe,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120515">3</a></sup> who think that the expression <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कपिल">kapila</a> ṛṣiḥ</i> there refers to the founder of the Sāṅkhya philosophy. But this is doubtful.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120515">4</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120515">↑</a> v. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120515">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 24 <i>et seq.;</i> 5, 412; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 236.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120515">↑</a> <i>Sāṅkhya Philosophie,</i> 27 <i>et seq.;</i> Translation of the Śāṅkhyatattvakaumudī, 531.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120515">↑</a> Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 2, xli, and Deussen, in his translation (<i>Sechzig Upanishads,</i> 304), do not take the word as a teacher's name. The latter renders <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कपिल">kapila</a> ṛṣiḥ,</i> by ‘the red sage,’ as referring to Hiraṇyagarbha.</li></ol></div>

कपि-वन भौवायन|कपिवन भौवायन|कपि|वन भौवायन|కపి-వన భౌవాయన|కపివన భౌవాయన|కపి|వన భౌవాయన|ಕಪಿ-ವನ ಭೌವಾಯನ|ಕಪಿವನ ಭೌವಾಯನ|ಕಪಿ|ವನ ಭೌವಾಯನ|kapi-vana bhauvāyana|kapivana bhauvāyana|kapi|vana bhauvāyana|kapi-vana bhauvaayana|kapivana bhauvaayana|vana bhauvaayana|_vei_120@516|_vei1_pn137
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@515">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@517">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn136">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn137">1:137</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn138">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kapi-<a class="vei_120a" href="वन">vana</a> Bhauvāyana</b> is mentioned as a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120516"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120516">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120516"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120516">2</a></sup> A rite called Kapivana's Dvyaha (‘ceremony lasting two days’) is also referred to in the Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120516"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120516">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120516"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120516">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 4, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxii. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120516"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120516">↑</a> xx. 13, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120516"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120516">↑</a> xxv. 2, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Āśvalāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 24, 3, 473; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 55, n. 2; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 157.</li></ol></div>

कपोत|కపోత|ಕಪೋತ|kapota|_vei_120@517|_vei1_pn137
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@516">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@518">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn136">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn137">1:137</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn138">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kapota</b> is the name of a bird, probably the ‘pigeon’ (its sense in the later language), occurring from the Rigveda onwards.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120517"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120517">1</a></sup> It is associated in some passages<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120517"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120517">2</a></sup> with the owl (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="उलूक">ulūka</a></b>) as a messenger of Nirṛti (‘dissolution,’ ‘misfortune’). This aspect of the pigeon as a bird of evil omen is probably based on an ancient belief which is also found beyond the confines of India.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120517"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120517">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120517"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120517">↑</a> Rv. i. 30, 4; Av. xx. 135, 12; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 23. 38.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120517"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120517">↑</a> Rv. x. 165, 1-5; Av. vi. 29, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120517"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120517">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prchistoric Antiquities,</i> 253.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 89; St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

कबन्ध आथर्वण|కబన్ధ ఆథర్వణ|ಕಬನ್ಧ ಆಥರ್ವಣ|kabandha ātharvaṇa|kabandha aatharvaNa|_vei_120@518|_vei1_pn137
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@517">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@519">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn136">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn137">1:137</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn138">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kabandha Ātharvaṇa</b> is mentioned in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120518"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120518">1</a></sup> along with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुधन्वन् आंगिरस">sudhanvan āṅgirasa</a>,</b> as a teacher, but is semi-mythical. His son was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विचारिन् काबन्धि">vicārin kābandhi</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120518"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120518">↑</a> vi. 7, 1. <i>Cf.</i> also Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 9. 18; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 176, n. 4; Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 149.</li></ol></div>

कम-द्यू|कमद्यू|कम|द्यू|కమ-ద్యూ|కమద్యూ|కమ|ద్యూ|ಕಮ-ದ್ಯೂ|ಕಮದ್ಯೂ|ಕಮ|ದ್ಯೂ|kama-dyū|kamadyū|kama|dyū|kama-dyuu|kamadyuu|dyuu|kama-dyoo|kamadyoo|dyoo|_vei_120@519|_vei1_pn137
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@518">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@520">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn136">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn137">1:137</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn138">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kama-dyū</b> appears once in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120519">1</a></sup> as the wife of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विमद">vimada</a>.</b> She is probably identical with the ‘maiden’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="योषा">yoṣā</a></i>) of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुमित्र">purumitra</a>,</b> no doubt his daughter. She is elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120519">2</a></sup> referred to in connexion with <a class="vei_120a" href="विमद">vimada</a>, who appears to have taken her for his bride against the will of her father.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120519">↑</a> x. 65, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120519">↑</a> i. 117, 20; x. 39, 7.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 310.</li></ol></div>

कम्बल|कंबल|కమ్బల|కంబల|ಕಮ್ಬಲ|ಕಂಬಲ|kambala|_vei_120@520|_vei1_pn137
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@519">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@521">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn136">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn137">1:137</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn138">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kambala</b> denotes in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120520"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120520">1</a></sup> a ‘woollen coverlet’ or ‘blanket.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120520"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120520">↑</a> xiv. 2, 66. 67. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 2.</li></ol></div>

कम्बोज.--यास्क|कम्बोज.-यास्क|कम्बोज.-|यास्क|कंबोज.--यास्क|कंबोज.-यास्क|कंबोज.-|కమ్బోజ.--యాస్క|కమ్బోజ.-యాస్క|కమ్బోజ.-|యాస్క|కంబోజ.--యాస్క|కంబోజ.-యాస్క|కంబోజ.-|ಕಮ್ಬೋಜ.--ಯಾಸ್ಕ|ಕಮ್ಬೋಜ.-ಯಾಸ್ಕ|ಕಮ್ಬೋಜ.-|ಯಾಸ್ಕ|ಕಂಬೋಜ.--ಯಾಸ್ಕ|ಕಂಬೋಜ.-ಯಾಸ್ಕ|ಕಂಬೋಜ.-|kamboja.--yāska|kamboja.-yāska|kamboja.-|yāska|kamboja.--yaaska|kamboja.-yaaska|yaaska|_vei_120@521|_vei1_pn138
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@520">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@522">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn137">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn138">1:138</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn139">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kamboja.--Yāska,</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120521">1</a></sup> refers to the speech of the Kambojas as differing from that of the other Āryas. The Kambojas were later settled to the north-west of the Indus, and are known as Kambujiya in the old Persian inscriptions. A teacher, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काम्बोज">kāmboja</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="औपमन्यव">aupamanyava</a>,</b> pupil of <b>Madragāra,</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120521">2</a></sup> This points to a possible connexion of the Madras, or more probably the <b>Uttara Madras,</b> with the Kambojas, who probably had Iranian as well as Indian affinities.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120521">↑</a> ii. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120521">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 102; Weber, <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 2, 493; 3, 384; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 7; <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 45; Max Müller, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 7, 373. On the relation of Indian and Iranian, see also Jacobi, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1909, 721 <i>et seq.;</i> 1910, 457 <i>et seq.;</i> Oldenberg, <i>ibid.,</i> 1095 <i>et seq.;</i> Keith, <i>ibid.,</i> 1100 <i>et seq.;</i> Kennedy, <i>ibid.,</i> 1107 <i>et seq.;</i> and see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्शु">parśu</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

करञ्ज|కరఞ్జ|ಕರಞ್ಜ|karañja|kara~nja|_vei_120@522|_vei1_pn138
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@521">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@523">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn137">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn138">1:138</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn139">↷</a><hr><p><b>Karañja,</b> a word which in the Sūtras and later denotes the tree <i>Pongamia glabra,</i> occurs only twice in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120522">1</a></sup> as the name of a foe of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, but whether a demon or a man<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120522">2</a></sup> is intended remains uncertain.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120522">↑</a> i. 53, 8; x. 48, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120522">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 63; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 149; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 292.</li></ol></div>

करम्भ|करंभ|కరమ్భ|కరంభ|ಕರಮ್ಭ|ಕರಂಭ|karambha|_vei_120@523|_vei1_pn138
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@522">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@524">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn137">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn138">1:138</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn139">↷</a><hr><p><b>Karambha</b> is the name, from the Rigveda onwards,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120523">1</a></sup> of a kind of porridge made of grain (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="यव">yava</a></b>), which was unhusked, parched slightly, and kneaded.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120523">2</a></sup> It was the especial sacrificial portion of Pūṣan, no doubt in his capacity of an agricultural deity. Karmabha was also made of barley (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपवाक">upavāka</a></b>)<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120523">3</a></sup> or of sesame (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="तिर्य">tirya</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120523">4</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120523">↑</a> Rv. i. 187, 16; iii. 52, 7; vi. 56, 1; 57, 2; viii. 102, 2; Av. iv. 7, 2. 3; vi. 16, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 1, 10, 2; vi. 5, 11, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120523">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 5, 2, 14; iv. 2, 4, 18. <i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 317; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 395, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120523">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 22.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120523">↑</a> Av. iv. 7, 3, but see Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 377; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 155.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 270.</li></ol></div>

करिक्रत|కరిక్రత|ಕರಿಕ್ರತ|karikrata|_vei_120@524|_vei1_pn139
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@523">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@525">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn138">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn139">1:139</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn140">↷</a><hr><p><b>Karikrata</b> denotes, according to Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120524"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120524">1</a></sup> a snake in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120524"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120524">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120524"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120524">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 95</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120524"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120524">↑</a> x. 4, 13. The Paippalāda version has Kanikrada.</li></ol></div>

करीर|కరీర|ಕರೀರ|karīra|kariira|kareera|_vei_120@525|_vei1_pn139
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@524">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@526">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn138">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn139">1:139</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn140">↷</a><hr><p><b>Karīra,</b> the name of a leafless shrub, <i>Capparis aphylla,</i> or its fruit, first appears in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120525"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120525">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120525"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120525">↑</a> ii. 4, 9, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 11; xxxvi. 7; Śatapatha Brahmaṇa, ii. 5, 2, 11.</li></ol></div>

करीष|కరీష|ಕರೀಷ|karīṣa|kariiSha|kareeSha|_vei_120@526|_vei1_pn139
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@525">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@527">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn138">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn139">1:139</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn140">↷</a><hr><p><b>Karīṣa</b> denotes ‘dry cow dung’ in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120526"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120526">1</a></sup> The Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120526"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120526">2</a></sup> shows that the value of the natural manure of animals in the fields was appreciated.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120526"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120526">↑</a> ii. 1, 1, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120526"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120526">↑</a> Av. iii. 14, 3. 4; xix. 31, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 236.</li></ol></div>

कर्कन्धु|कर्कन्धु|కర్కన్ధు_౧|కర్కన్ధు|ಕರ್ಕನ್ಧು_೧|ಕರ್ಕನ್ಧು|karkandhu_1|karkandhu|_vei_120@527|_vei1_pn139
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@526">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@528">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn138">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn139">1:139</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn140">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Karkandhu</b> is the ordinary word for ‘jujube,’ the tree (<i>Zizyphus jujuba</i>) and the fruit, from the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās onwards.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120527"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120527">1</a></sup> The berry is red (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="रोहित">rohita</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120527"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120527">2</a></sup> Compare <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुवल">kuvala</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बदर">badara</a>,</b> which denote the fruit.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120527"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120527">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 10; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 11, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 23. 91; xxi. 32; xxiv. 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 4, 10; xii. 7, 2, 9; 9, 1, 5, etc.; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 156. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120527"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120527">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 242.</li></ol></div>

कर्कन्धु|कर्कन्धु|కర్కన్ధు_౨|కర్కన్ధు|ಕರ್ಕನ್ಧು_೨|ಕರ್ಕನ್ಧು|karkandhu_2|karkandhu|_vei_120@528|_vei1_pn139
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@527">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@529">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn138">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn139">1:139</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn140">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Karkandhu</b> is only the name borne by a protégé of the Aśvins in the Rigveda (i. 112, 6). Its identity with the word for jujube indicates that the latter, though not otherwise mentioned there, was known at the time of the Rigveda.</p></div>

कर्करि|కర్కరి|ಕರ್ಕರಿ|karkari|_vei_120@529|_vei1_pn139
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@528">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@530">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn138">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn139">1:139</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn140">↷</a><hr><p><b>Karkari,</b> a musical instrument, probably the ‘lute,’ occurs from the Rigveda onwards.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120529">1</a></sup> The Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120529">2</a></sup> mentions cattle branded on the ears with a mark resembling a lute (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कर्करि">karkari</a>-karṇyaḥ</i>). </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120529">↑</a> Rv. ii. 43, 3; Av. iv. 37. 4. <i>Cf.</i> xx. 132. 3. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120529">↑</a> iv. 2, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>Gurupūjākaumudī,</i> 48, 49.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 289.</li></ol></div>

कर्की|कर्कि|కర్కీ|కర్కి|ಕರ್ಕೀ|ಕರ್ಕಿ|karkī|karki|karkii|karkee|_vei_120@530|_vei1_pn140
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@529">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@531">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn139">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn140">1:140</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn141">↷</a><hr><p><b>Karkī</b> may denote in one passage of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120530">1</a></sup> a white cow,’ according to the suggestion of Roth.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120530">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120530">↑</a> iv. 38, 6. 7. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 414.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120530">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary and Bo7htlingk's Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

कर्ण-शोभन|कर्णशोभन|कर्ण|शोभन|కర్ణ-శోభన|కర్ణశోభన|కర్ణ|శోభన|ಕರ್ಣ-ಶೋಭನ|ಕರ್ಣಶೋಭನ|ಕರ್ಣ|ಶೋಭನ|karṇa-śobhana|karṇaśobhana|karṇa|śobhana|karNa-shobhana|karNashobhana|karNa|shobhana|_vei_120@531|_vei1_pn140
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@530">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@532">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn139">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn140">1:140</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn141">↷</a><hr><p><b>Karṇa-śobhana</b> denotes an ‘ornament for the ear’ in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120531"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120531">1</a></sup> apparently for the use of men. Some deity is called ‘gold-eared’ in another passage of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120531"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120531">2</a></sup> Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120531"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120531">3</a></sup> considers the use of ear-rings later than that of necklets and wristlets.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120531"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120531">↑</a> viii. 78, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120531"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120531">↑</a> i. 122, 14. See also i. 64, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120531"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120531">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 35.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 262.</li></ol></div>

कर्ण-श्रवस् आंगिरस|कर्णश्रवस् आंगिरस|कर्ण|श्रवस् आंगिरस|कर्ण-श्रवस् आङ्गिरस|कर्णश्रवस् आङ्गिरस|श्रवस् आङ्गिरस|కర్ణ-శ్రవస్ ఆంగిరస|కర్ణశ్రవస్ ఆంగిరస|కర్ణ|శ్రవస్ ఆంగిరస|కర్ణ-శ్రవస్ ఆఙ్గిరస|కర్ణశ్రవస్ ఆఙ్గిరస|శ్రవస్ ఆఙ్గిరస|ಕರ್ಣ-ಶ್ರವಸ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಕರ್ಣಶ್ರವಸ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಕರ್ಣ|ಶ್ರವಸ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಕರ್ಣ-ಶ್ರವಸ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|ಕರ್ಣಶ್ರವಸ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|ಶ್ರವಸ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|karṇa-śravas āṅgirasa|karṇaśravas āṅgirasa|karṇa|śravas āṅgirasa|karNa-shravas aaMgirasa|karNashravas aaMgirasa|karNa|shravas aaMgirasa|karNa-shravas aangirasa|karNashravas aangirasa|shravas aangirasa|_vei_120@532|_vei1_pn140
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@531">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@533">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn139">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn140">1:140</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn141">↷</a><hr><p><b>Karṇa-<a class="vei_120a" href="श्रवस्">śravas</a> Āṅgirasa</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 11, 14) as a seer of Sāmans or chants, the same tale being told of him as of <b>Dāvasu.</b></p></div>

कर्मार|కర్మార|ಕರ್ಮಾರ|karmāra|karmaara|_vei_120@533|_vei1_pn140
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@532">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@534">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn139">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn140">1:140</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn141">↷</a><hr><p><b>Karmāra,</b> the ‘smith,’ is several times mentioned with approval in the Vedic Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120533">1</a></sup> In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120533">2</a></sup> smiths appear with fishermen (<i>dhīvānaḥ</i>) and chariot-builders (<i>rathakārāḥ</i>), all being classified as clever workers (<i>manīṣiṇaḥ</i>): possibly a quasi-caste of smiths was already developing from the guild organization that probably existed.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120533">3</a></sup></p><p>Little is known of the smith's methods of work and of his tools. No doubt he smelted (<i>dhmā</i>) the ore in the fire; hence he is called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ध्मातृ">dhmātṛ</a>,</i> the ‘smelter.’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120533">4</a></sup> Mention is also made of  his bellows of birds' feathers.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120533">5</a></sup> He made metal vessels (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="घर्म">gharma</a> ayasmaya</i>)<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120533">6</a></sup> to be put on the fire: even the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> cup could occasionally be made of hammered metal (<i>ayo-hata</i>).<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120533">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120533">↑</a> Rv. x. 72, 2; Av. iii. 5, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 13; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9, 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 27; xxx. 7. <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="कर्मार">karmāra</a>,</i> Rv. ix. 112, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120533">↑</a> iii. 5, 6. The exact sense of the passage is doubtful. Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 252; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 144; and Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 92, take the reference to be to ‘skilled chariot-makers’ (<i>dhīvāno <a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a>-kārāḥ</i>) and ‘clever smiths,’ but this is perhaps less likely. The commentator interprets <i>dhīvānaḥ</i> as ‘fishermen’ (in the later language <i>dhīvara</i> means both a ‘clever man’ and a ‘fisherman’).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120533">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Fick, <i>Die sociale Gliederung,</i> 182.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120533">↑</a> Rv. v. 9. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120533">↑</a> Rv. ix. 112, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120533">↑</a> Rv. v. 30, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120533">↑</a> Rv. ix. 1, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 252, 253; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 196 <i>et seq.; Über den <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>,</i> 19 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

कर्वर|కర్వర|ಕರ್ವರ|karvara|_vei_120@534|_vei1_pn141
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@533">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@535">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn140">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn141">1:141</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn142">↷</a><hr><p><b>Karvara,</b> a word found in one passage of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120534"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120534">1</a></sup> seems to mean some kind of fish<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120534"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120534">2</a></sup> caught by a fisherman (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पौञ्जिष्ठ">pauñjiṣṭha</a></i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120534"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120534">↑</a> x. 4, 19.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120534"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120534">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 96; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 578.</li></ol></div>

कर्षू|కర్షూ|ಕರ್ಷೂ|karṣū|karShuu|karShoo|_vei_120@535|_vei1_pn141
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@534">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@536">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn140">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn141">1:141</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn142">↷</a><hr><p><b>Karṣū,</b> a rare word found in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120535"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120535">1</a></sup> denotes a ‘furrow’ or ‘trench.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120535"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120535">↑</a> i. 8, 1, 3; xiii. 8, 3, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 283.</li></ol></div>

कलविंक|कलविङ्क|కలవింక|కలవిఙ్క|ಕಲವಿಂಕ|ಕಲವಿಙ್ಕ|kalaviṅka|kalaviMka|kalavinka|_vei_120@536|_vei1_pn141
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@535">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@537">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn140">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn141">1:141</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn142">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kalaviṅka,</b> a name of the ‘sparrow,’ is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120536"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120536">1</a></sup> and occasionally later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120536"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120536">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120536"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120536">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 1, 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 10; Vājasaneyi Samhitā, xxiv. 20. 31.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120536"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120536">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 3, 4; v. 5, 4, 5; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 154, 3 (<i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 181). <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 91.</li></ol></div>

कलश|కలశ|ಕಲಶ|kalaśa|kalasha|_vei_120@537|_vei1_pn141
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@536">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@538">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn140">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn141">1:141</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn142">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kalaśa</b> is, from the Rigveda onwards,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120537"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120537">1</a></sup> a common word for ‘pot’ or ‘jar,’ probably either formed of a gourd or made of clay (unburnt or baked), as we know that both kinds of pot were in use.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120537"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120537">2</a></sup> The wooden <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> tub (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="द्रोण">droṇa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कलश">kalaśa</a></i>) is frequently referred to in the ritual. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कोश">kośa</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120537"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120537">↑</a> Rv. i. 117, 12; iii. 32, 15; iv. 27, 5; 32, 19, etc.; Av. iii. 12, 7; ix. 1, 6; 4, 15; xviii. 4, 13, etc. In Rv. x. 32, 9, the word, according to the Śt. Petersburg Dictionary, is used as a proper name, but the passage is very doubtful.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120537"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120537">↑</a> Av. iv. 17, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 1, 8, 1; iv. 1, 5, 4; v. 1, 7, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, i. 22; xi. 59. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 253; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 257; Oertel, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 185, n. 3; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 183 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

कला|కలా|ಕಲಾ|kalā|kalaa|_vei_120@538|_vei1_pn142
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@537">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@539">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn141">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn142">1:142</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn143">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kalā</b> denotes a fractional part, normally ‘one-sixteenth,’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120538"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120538">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120538"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120538">2</a></sup> It is often mentioned in connexion with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शफ">śapha</a>,</b> ‘one-eighth.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120538"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120538">↑</a> viii. 47, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120538"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120538">↑</a> Av. vi. 96, 3; xix. 57, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 10, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 7, 7; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 3, 1; xii. 8, 3, 13, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, xi. 12. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, 278; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 259.</li></ol></div>

कलि|कलि|కలి_౧|కలి|ಕಲಿ_೧|ಕಲಿ|kali_1|kali|_vei_120@539|_vei1_pn142
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@538">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@540">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn141">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn142">1:142</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn143">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Kali.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a>.</b></p></div>

कलि|कलि|కలి_౨|కలి|ಕಲಿ_೨|ಕಲಿ|kali_2|kali|_vei_120@540|_vei1_pn142
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@539">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@541">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn141">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn142">1:142</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn143">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Kali</b> occurs in the Rigveda, twice in the singular<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120540"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120540">1</a></sup> as the name of a protégé of the Aśvins, and once in the plural.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120540"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120540">2</a></sup> The persons meant in the latter passage seem to be different from the former one. The Kalis are once mentioned in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120540"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120540">3</a></sup> beside the Gandharvas.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120540"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120540">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120540"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120540">↑</a> i. 112, 15; x. 39, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120540"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120540">↑</a> viii. 66, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120540"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120540">↑</a> x. 10, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120540"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120540">↑</a> These Kalis may be connected with dicing, as in the Atharvaveda the Apsarases, the wives of the Gandharvas, are fond of dice, and bestow luck at play. See Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 135.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 89; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 163.</li></ol></div>

कल्प|కల్ప|ಕಲ್ಪ|kalpa|_vei_120@541|_vei1_pn142
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@540">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@542">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn141">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn142">1:142</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn143">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kalpa</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka (ii. 10) seems to denote <a class="vei_120a" href="कल्प">kalpa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.</p></div>

कल्माष-ग्रीव|कल्माषग्रीव|कल्माष|ग्रीव|కల్మాష-గ్రీవ|కల్మాషగ్రీవ|కల్మాష|గ్రీవ|ಕಲ್ಮಾಷ-ಗ್ರೀವ|ಕಲ್ಮಾಷಗ್ರೀವ|ಕಲ್ಮಾಷ|ಗ್ರೀವ|kalmāṣa-grīva|kalmāṣagrīva|kalmāṣa|grīva|kalmaaSha-griiva|kalmaaShagriiva|kalmaaSha|griiva|kalmaaSha-greeva|kalmaaShagreeva|greeva|_vei_120@542|_vei1_pn142
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@541">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@543">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn141">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn142">1:142</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn143">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kalmāṣa-grīva</b> (‘speckled-neck’) is the name of a snake in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120542">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120542">↑</a> iii. 27, 5 (where the Paippalāda recension has <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कुल्माष">kulmāṣa</a>-</i>); xii. 3, 59. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 10, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 94, 95.</li></ol></div>

कल्याण|కల్యాణ|ಕಲ್ಯಾಣ|kalyāṇa|kalyaaNa|_vei_120@543|_vei1_pn142
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@542">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@544">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn141">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn142">1:142</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn143">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kalyāṇa</b> is the name, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120543"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120543">1</a></sup> of an <a class="vei_120a" href="आंगिरस">āṅgirasa</a> who saw the Aurṇāyava <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a>. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120543"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120543">↑</a> xii. 11, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 68, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

कवच|కవచ|ಕವಚ|kavaca|kavacha|_vei_120@544|_vei1_pn143
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@543">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@545">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn142">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn143">1:143</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn144">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kavaca</b> denotes a ‘corselet’ or ‘breastplate’ in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120544">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120544">2</a></sup> There is nothing to show whether it was made of metal, but that it was so is quite possible (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्मन्">varman</a></b>). The Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120544">3</a></sup> refers to a ‘corselet-strap’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कवच">kavaca</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पाश">pāśa</a></i>), which may point to a linen corselet such as those known to Herodotus.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120544">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120544">↑</a> Av. xi. 10, 22 (<i>kavacin</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120544">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 2, 2, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 19, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 25 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कवच">kavaca</a></i>); Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 1, 6, 3; 4, 1, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 48; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 45 (<i>kavacin</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120544">↑</a> xi. 10, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120544">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 129, and Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 659, seem to recognize coats of mail only.</li></ol></div>

कवष|కవష|ಕವಷ|kavaṣa|kavaSha|_vei_120@545|_vei1_pn143
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@544">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@546">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn142">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn143">1:143</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn144">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kavaṣa</b> is mentioned in a hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120545"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120545">1</a></sup> as one of those whom, together with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="द्रुह्यु">druhyu</a></b> king, <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> overthrew for the <b>Tṛtsus.</b> The Anukramaṇī (Index) also attributes to him the authorship of several hymns of the Rigveda, including two (x. 32. 33) that deal with a prince <b>Kuruśravaṇa</b> and his descendant <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपमश्रवस्">upamaśravas</a>.</b> There seems no reason to doubt this attribution, which is accepted by both Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120545"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120545">2</a></sup> and Geldner.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120545"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120545">3</a></sup> The former holds that <a class="vei_120a" href="कवष">kavaṣa</a> was the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> of the joint tribes named <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैकर्ण">vaikarṇa</a>,</b> in whom he sees the KuruKrivi (<a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a>) peoples, and that <a class="vei_120a" href="कवष">kavaṣa</a> in that capacity is mentioned in the Rigveda as representative of those peoples. He also suggests that the language of Rigveda x. 33, 4 is best explained by the reduced position in which the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Krivis found themselves on their defeat by the Tṛtsus. Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120545"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120545">4</a></sup> on the other hand, thinks that <a class="vei_120a" href="कवष">kavaṣa</a> was the priest of the five peoples. Geldner<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120545"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120545">5</a></sup> holds that <a class="vei_120a" href="कवष">kavaṣa</a> was the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> of Kuruśravaṇa, by whose son, <a class="vei_120a" href="उपमश्रवस्">upamaśravas</a>, he was ill-treated, and that he composed Rigveda x. 33 to deprecate the anger of his royal master. Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120545"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120545">6</a></sup> thinks that he was a king.</p><p>In the Brāhmaṇas of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120545"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120545">7</a></sup> mention is made of  <a class="vei_120a" href="कवष">kavaṣa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐलूष">ailūṣa</a>, who was a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> born of a female slave, and was reproached on this ground by the other <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषिस्">ṛṣis</a>. He is possibly identical with the <a class="vei_120a" href="कवष">kavaṣa</a> of the Rigveda.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120545"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120545">↑</a> vii. 18, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120545"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120545">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 127.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120545"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120545">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 150.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120545"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120545">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 139.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120545"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120545">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120545"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120545">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 261, 263.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120545"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120545">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 1, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 459; Lanman, <i>Sanskrit Reader,</i> 386, 387; Pargiter, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1910, 50.</li></ol></div>

कश|కశ|ಕಶ|kaśa|kasha|_vei_120@546|_vei1_pn144
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@545">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@547">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn143">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn144">1:144</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn145">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaśa</b> is the name of an unknown animal mentioned as a victim at the horse sacrifice in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120546"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120546">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120546"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120546">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 17, 1; 18, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 26; 38. <i>Cf.</i> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 7.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 84.</li></ol></div>

कशीका|కశీకా|ಕಶೀಕಾ|kaśīkā|kashiikaa|kasheekaa|_vei_120@547|_vei1_pn144
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@546">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@548">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn143">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn144">1:144</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn145">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaśīkā</b> is name of an animal mentioned once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120547">1</a></sup> and interpreted as ‘weasel’ by the commentator Sāyaṇa. Fick<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120547">2</a></sup> suggests that the meaning is ‘pole-cat.’ Geldner<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120547">3</a></sup> takes it as ‘female ichneumon.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120547">↑</a> i. 126, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120547">↑</a> Bezzenberger, <i>Beiträge,</i> 3, 165; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 247. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 84; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 57.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120547">↑</a> <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 44.</li></ol></div>

कशिपु|కశిపు|ಕಶಿಪು|kaśipu|kashipu|_vei_120@548|_vei1_pn144
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@547">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@549">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn143">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn144">1:144</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn145">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaśipu</b> denotes a ‘mat’ or ‘cushion’ made, according to the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120548">1</a></sup> by women from reeds (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="नड">naḍa</a></i>), which they crushed for the purpose by means of stones. On the other hand, the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120548">2</a></sup> refers to a mat as made of gold.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120548">↑</a> vi. 138, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120548">↑</a> xiii. 4, 3, 1.</li></ol></div>

कशु|కశు|ಕಶು|kaśu|kashu|_vei_120@549|_vei1_pn144
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@548">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@550">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn143">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn144">1:144</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn145">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaśu</b> is the name of a prince mentioned in the Rigveda with the patronymic <a class="vei_120a" href="चैद्य">caidya</a>, or descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चेदि">cedi</a>,</b> as a generous patron of the singer, who praises the liberality of the Cedis. Neither this king nor the Cedis appear again in Vedic literature.</p><p>^1) viii. 5, 37. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 129.</p></div>

कशो-जू|कशोजू|कशो|కశో-జూ|కశోజూ|కశో|ಕಶೋ-ಜೂ|ಕಶೋಜೂ|ಕಶೋ|kaśo-jū|kaśojū|kaśo|kasho-juu|kashojuu|kasho|kasho-joo|kashojoo|_vei_120@550|_vei1_pn144
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@549">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@551">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn143">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn144">1:144</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn145">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaśo-jū</b> occurs once in the Rigveda (i. 112, 14) either as a proper name or as an epithet of <b>Divodāsa.</b> The sense of the word is quite uncertain.</p></div>

कश्यप|కశ్యప|ಕಶ್ಯಪ|kaśyapa|kashyapa|_vei_120@551|_vei1_pn144
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@550">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@552">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn143">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn144">1:144</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn145">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaśyapa,</b> a word denoting ‘tortoise,’ occurs in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120551"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120551">1</a></sup> and often later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120551"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120551">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120551"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120551">↑</a> iv. 20. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120551"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120551">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 18; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 37; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 5, 1, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 86; Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 17, 403.</li></ol></div>

कश्यप|కశ్యప|ಕಶ್ಯಪ|kaśyapa|kashyapa|_vei_120@552|_vei1_pn145
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@551">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@553">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn144">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn145">1:145</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn146">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaśyapa</b> is the name of a sage who is mentioned only once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120552">1</a></sup> but is a common figure in the later Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120552">2</a></sup> He is always of a mythical character, as belonging to the distant past. According to the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120552">3</a></sup> he anointed King <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वकर्मन् भौवन">viśvakarman bhauvana</a>,</b> and in the Upaniṣads<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120552">4</a></sup> he is mentioned as a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>. The Kaśyapas appear in connexion with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनमेजय">janamejaya</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120552">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120552">↑</a> ix. 114, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120552">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>, i. 1, 2, 4, 10; 4, 2, 3, 2 (but in these passages the St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> accepts the sense of a divine being, identical with Prajāpati); Av. i. 14, 4; ii. 33, 7; iv. 20, 7; 29, 3; 37, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 9, Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iii. 62.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120552">↑</a> viii. 21; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 7, 1, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120552">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 2, 6; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 3, 1 (in a quotation).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120552">↑</a> vii. 27. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 235, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

कश्यप नैध्रुवि|కశ్యప నైధ్రువి|ಕಶ್ಯಪ ನೈಧ್ರುವಿ|kaśyapa naidhruvi|kashyapa naidhruvi|_vei_120@553|_vei1_pn145
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@552">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@554">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn144">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn145">1:145</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn146">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaśyapa Naidhruvi</b> is mentioned as a teacher in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120553"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120553">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120553"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120553">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 33 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 5, 3, <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

कष्कष|కష్కష|ಕಷ್ಕಷ|kaṣkaṣa|kaShkaSha|_vei_120@554|_vei1_pn145
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@553">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@555">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn144">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn145">1:145</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn146">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaṣkaṣa</b> designates a kind of worm in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120554"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120554">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120554"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120554">↑</a> v. 23, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 98.</li></ol></div>

कसर्णील|కసర్ణీల|ಕಸರ್ಣೀಲ|kasarṇīla|kasarNiila|kasarNeela|_vei_120@555|_vei1_pn145
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@554">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@556">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn144">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn145">1:145</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn146">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kasarṇīla</b> is the name of a kind of snake in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120555"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120555">1</a></sup> It occurs also in the form Kasarṇīra, personified as the seer Kasarṇīra Kādraveya in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Sāṃhita.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120555"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120555">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120555"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120555">↑</a> x. 4, 5, where the Paippalāda recension has <i>kvaṣarṣṇīla.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120555"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120555">↑</a> i. 5, 4, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 98; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 607.</li></ol></div>

क-स्तम्भी|क-स्तम्भि|कस्तम्भी|कस्तम्भि|स्तम्भी|स्तम्भि|क-स्तंभी|क-स्तंभि|कस्तंभी|कस्तंभि|स्तंभी|स्तंभि|క-స్తమ్భీ|క-స్తమ్భి|కస్తమ్భీ|కస్తమ్భి|స్తమ్భీ|స్తమ్భి|క-స్తంభీ|క-స్తంభి|కస్తంభీ|కస్తంభి|స్తంభీ|స్తంభి|ಕ-ಸ್ತಮ್ಭೀ|ಕ-ಸ್ತಮ್ಭಿ|ಕಸ್ತಮ್ಭೀ|ಕಸ್ತಮ್ಭಿ|ಸ್ತಮ್ಭೀ|ಸ್ತಮ್ಭಿ|ಕ-ಸ್ತಂಭೀ|ಕ-ಸ್ತಂಭಿ|ಕಸ್ತಂಭೀ|ಕಸ್ತಂಭಿ|ಸ್ತಂಭೀ|ಸ್ತಂಭಿ|ka-stambhī|ka-stambhi|kastambhī|kastambhi|stambhī|stambhi|ka-stambhii|kastambhii|stambhii|stambhi|ka-stambhee|kastambhee|stambhee|ka-stambhii|_vei_120@556|_vei1_pn145
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@555">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@557">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn144">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn145">1:145</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn146">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ka-stambhī</b> denotes in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120556"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120556">1</a></sup> a piece of wood used as a prop for the end of a wagon-pole to rest on.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120556"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120556">↑</a> i. 1, 2, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Caland and Henry, <i>L'Agniṣṭoma,</i> 49; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 14, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

कहोड कौषीतकि|కహోడ కౌషీతకి|ಕಹೋಡ ಕೌಷೀತಕಿ|kahoḍa kauṣītaki|kahoDa kauShiitaki|kahoDa kauSheetaki|_vei_120@557|_vei1_pn145
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@556">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@558">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn144">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn145">1:145</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn146">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kahoḍa Kauṣītaki</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120557"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120557">1</a></sup> or <b>Kauṣītakeya</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120557"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120557">2</a></sup> is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_120557"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_120557">1b</a></sup> the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2b_vei_120557"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2b_vei_120557">2b</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_1c_vei_120557"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1c_vei_120557">1c</a></sup> as a teacher, contemporary with <a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a>. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काहोडि">kāhoḍi</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120557"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120557">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 3, 1; Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, xv.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120557"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120557">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 5, 1.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_120557"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_120557">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 3, 1; Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, xv.</li><li id="fx_120_2b_vei_120557"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2b_vei_120557">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 5, 1.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1c_vei_120557"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1c_vei_120557">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 3, 1; Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, xv.</li></ol></div>

काकम्बीर|काकंबीर|కాకమ్బీర|కాకంబీర|ಕಾಕಮ್ಬೀರ|ಕಾಕಂಬೀರ|kākambīra|kaakambiira|kaakambeera|_vei_120@558|_vei1_pn146
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@557">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@559">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn145">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn146">1:146</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kākambīra</b> is the name in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120558">1</a></sup> of a useful tree of some kind.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120558">↑</a> vi. 48, 17. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 62.</li></ol></div>

काक्ष-सेनि|काक्षसेनि|काक्ष|सेनि|కాక్ష-సేని|కాక్షసేని|కాక్ష|సేని|ಕಾಕ್ಷ-ಸೇನಿ|ಕಾಕ್ಷಸೇನಿ|ಕಾಕ್ಷ|ಸೇನಿ|kākṣa-seni|kākṣaseni|kākṣa|seni|kaakSha-seni|kaakShaseni|kaakSha|kaaxa-seni|kaaxaseni|kaaxa|_vei_120@559|_vei1_pn146
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@558">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@560">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn145">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn146">1:146</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kākṣa-seni</b> is the patronymic (‘son of <b>Kakṣasena’</b>) of <b>Abhipratārin</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xiv. 1, 12).</p></div>

काक्षीवत|కాక్షీవత|ಕಾಕ್ಷೀವತ|kākṣīvata|kaakShiivata|kaakSheevata|kaaxiivata|_vei_120@560|_vei1_pn146
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@559">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@561">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn145">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn146">1:146</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kākṣīvata.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नोधस्">nodhas</a>.</b></p></div>

काठक|కాఠక|ಕಾಠಕ|kāṭhaka|kaaThaka|_vei_120@561|_vei1_pn146
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@560">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@562">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn145">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn146">1:146</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāṭhaka,</b> the name of the recension of the Black <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> belonging to the school of the Kaṭhas, is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120561"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120561">1</a></sup> of <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a> and in the Anupada <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120561"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120561">2</a></sup> The Saṃhitā which bears the name has been in part edited by L. v. Schroeder.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120561"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120561">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120561"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120561">↑</a> x. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120561"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120561">↑</a> iii. 11; vii. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120561"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120561">↑</a> Two volumes have so far appeared, the first containing i-xviii, the second xix-xxx. <i>Cf. Indische Studien,</i> 1, 44; 3, 451; von Schroeder, <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, 1900, 1909; <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 49, 145171; <i>Die Tübinger Kaṭha-Handschriften,</i> Vienna, 1898; <i>Zwei Handschriften der K.K. Hofbiblliothek in Wien <a class="vei_120a" href="मित्">mit</a> Fragmenten des <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a>,</i> Vienna, 1896.</li></ol></div>

काण्ठे-विद्धि|काण्ठेविद्धि|काण्ठे|विद्धि|కాణ్ఠే-విద్ధి|కాణ్ఠేవిద్ధి|కాణ్ఠే|విద్ధి|ಕಾಣ್ಠೇ-ವಿದ್ಧಿ|ಕಾಣ್ಠೇವಿದ್ಧಿ|ಕಾಣ್ಠೇ|ವಿದ್ಧಿ|kāṇṭhe-viddhi|kāṇṭheviddhi|kāṇṭhe|viddhi|kaaNThe-viddhi|kaaNTheviddhi|kaaNThe|_vei_120@562|_vei1_pn146
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@561">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@563">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn145">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn146">1:146</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāṇṭhe-viddhi</b> (‘descendant of Kaṇṭheviddha’) is mentioned as a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120562"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120562">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120562"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120562">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 382.</li></ol></div>

काण्ड-वीणा|काण्डवीणा|काण्ड|वीणा|కాణ్డ-వీణా|కాణ్డవీణా|కాణ్డ|వీణా|ಕಾಣ್ಡ-ವೀಣಾ|ಕಾಣ್ಡವೀಣಾ|ಕಾಣ್ಡ|ವೀಣಾ|kāṇḍa-vīṇā|kāṇḍavīṇā|kāṇḍa|vīṇā|kaaNDa-viiNaa|kaaNDaviiNaa|kaaNDa|viiNaa|kaaNDa-veeNaa|kaaNDaveeNaa|veeNaa|_vei_120@563|_vei1_pn146
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@562">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@564">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn145">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn146">1:146</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāṇḍa-vīṇā,</b> the name of a musical instrument, a kind of lute made out of joints of reed, which is mentioned as used at the Mahāvrata ceremony in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120563"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120563">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120563"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120563">↑</a> xxxiv. 5 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 477). <i>Cf.</i> Lāṭyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 2, 6; Kātyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiii. 3, 16; Sāṅkhāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvii, 3, 12.</li></ol></div>

काण्ड्विय|కాణ్డ్వియ|ಕಾಣ್ಡ್ವಿಯ|kāṇḍviya|kaaNDviya|_vei_120@564|_vei1_pn146
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@563">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@565">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn145">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn146">1:146</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāṇḍviya</b> is mentioned as an Udgātṛ in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 10, 2).</p></div>

काण्व|కాణ్వ|ಕಾಣ್ವ|kāṇva|kaaNva|_vei_120@565|_vei1_pn146
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@564">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@566">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn145">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn146">1:146</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāṇva.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a>:</b> among others, <b>Devātithi, <a class="vei_120a" href="मेधातिथि">medhātithi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वत्स">vatsa</a>,</b> were prominent members of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a> family. </p></div>

काण्वी-पुत्र|काण्वीपुत्र|काण्वी|काण्वि|पुत्र|కాణ్వీ-పుత్ర|కాణ్వీపుత్ర|కాణ్వీ|కాణ్వి|పుత్ర|ಕಾಣ್ವೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಕಾಣ್ವೀಪುತ್ರ|ಕಾಣ್ವೀ|ಕಾಣ್ವಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|kāṇvī-putra|kāṇvīputra|kāṇvī|kāṇvi|putra|kaaNvii-putra|kaaNviiputra|kaaNvii|kaaNvi|kaaNvee-putra|kaaNveeputra|kaaNvee|_vei_120@566|_vei1_pn147
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@565">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@567">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn146">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">1:147</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn148">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāṇvī-putra</b> is mentioned as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कापीपुत्र">kāpīputra</a></b> in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120566">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120566">↑</a> vi. 5, 1 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension).</li></ol></div>

काण्वायन|కాణ్వాయన|ಕಾಣ್ವಾಯನ|kāṇvāyana|kaaNvaayana|_vei_120@567|_vei1_pn147
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@566">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@568">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn146">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">1:147</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn148">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāṇvāyana</b> (‘descendant of Kaṇva’) and <b>Kāṇvyāyana</b> (‘descendant of Kāṇvya’) are patronymics occurring in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120567">1</a></sup> and the Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120567">2</a></sup> respectively.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120567">↑</a> viii. 55, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120567">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 38; Sāyaṇa on Rv. i. 51, 1; viii. 2, 40.</li></ol></div>

कात्यायनि|కాత్యాయని|ಕಾತ್ಯಾಯನಿ|kātyāyani|kaatyaayani|_vei_120@568|_vei1_pn147
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@567">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@569">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn146">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">1:147</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn148">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kātyāyani.</b> See <b>Dakṣa.</b></p></div>

कात्यायनी|कात्यायनि|కాత్యాయనీ|కాత్యాయని|ಕಾತ್ಯಾಯನೀ|ಕಾತ್ಯಾಯನಿ|kātyāyanī|kātyāyani|kaatyaayanii|kaatyaayani|kaatyaayanee|_vei_120@569|_vei1_pn147
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@568">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@570">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn146">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">1:147</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn148">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kātyāyanī</b> is the name of one of the two wives of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a></b> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120569"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120569">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120569"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120569">↑</a> ii. 4, 1; iv. 5, 1. 2. A Kātya appears in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 15 <i>et seq.</i> See Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 138.</li></ol></div>

कात्यायनी-पुत्र|कात्यायनीपुत्र|कात्यायनी|कात्यायनि|पुत्र|కాత్యాయనీ-పుత్ర|కాత్యాయనీపుత్ర|కాత్యాయనీ|కాత్యాయని|పుత్ర|ಕಾತ್ಯಾಯನೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಕಾತ್ಯಾಯನೀಪುತ್ರ|ಕಾತ್ಯಾಯನೀ|ಕಾತ್ಯಾಯನಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|kātyāyanī-putra|kātyāyanīputra|kātyāyanī|kātyāyani|putra|kaatyaayanii-putra|kaatyaayaniiputra|kaatyaayanii|kaatyaayani|kaatyaayanee-putra|kaatyaayaneeputra|kaatyaayanee|_vei_120@570|_vei1_pn147
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@569">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@571">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn146">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">1:147</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn148">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kātyāyanī-putra,</b> ‘son of <a class="vei_120a" href="कात्यायनी">kātyāyanī</a>,’ is mentioned in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120570"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120570">1</a></sup> as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोतमीपुत्र">gotamīputra</a></b> and of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिकीपुत्र">kauśikīputra</a>.</b> A <a class="vei_120a" href="जातूकर्ण्य">jātūkarṇya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कात्यायनीपुत्र">kātyāyanīputra</a> is named as a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120570"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120570">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120570"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120570">↑</a> vi. 5, 1, <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120570"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120570">↑</a> viii. 10. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 138.</li></ol></div>

कानान्ध|కానాన్ధ|ಕಾನಾನ್ಧ|kānāndha|kaanaandha|_vei_120@571|_vei1_pn147
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@570">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@572">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn146">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">1:147</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn148">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kānāndha</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> xxi. 10) as son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वध्र्यश्व">vadhryaśva</a>.</b></p></div>

कानीत|కానీత|ಕಾನೀತ|kānīta|kaaniita|kaaneeta|_vei_120@572|_vei1_pn147
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@571">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@573">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn146">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">1:147</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn148">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kānīta</b> is the patronymic (‘son of Kanīta’) in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120572"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120572">1</a></sup> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पृथुश्रवस्">pṛthuśravas</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120572"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120572">↑</a> viii. 46, 21, 24. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 11, 23.</li></ol></div>

कानीन|కానీన|ಕಾನೀನ|kānīna|kaaniina|kaaneena|_vei_120@573|_vei1_pn147
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@572">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@574">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn146">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">1:147</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn148">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kānīna</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120573"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120573">1</a></sup> apparently denotes the ‘son of a maiden.’ See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120573"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120573">↑</a> v. 5, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 334.</li></ol></div>

काण्डा-विष|काण्डाविष|काण्डा|विष|కాణ్డా-విష|కాణ్డావిష|కాణ్డా|విష|ಕಾಣ್ಡಾ-ವಿಷ|ಕಾಣ್ಡಾವಿಷ|ಕಾಣ್ಡಾ|ವಿಷ|kāṇḍā-viṣa|kāṇḍāviṣa|kāṇḍā|viṣa|kaaNDaa-viSha|kaaNDaaviSha|kaaNDaa|viSha|_vei_120@574|_vei1_pn148
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@573">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@575">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn148">1:148</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn149">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāṇḍā-viṣa</b> in the Atharvaveda (x. 4, 22) denotes some kind of poison. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कनक्नक">kanaknaka</a>.</b></p></div>

कापटव सु-नीथ|कापटव सुनीथ|कापटव सु|नीथ|కాపటవ సు-నీథ|కాపటవ సునీథ|కాపటవ సు|నీథ|ಕಾಪಟವ ಸು-ನೀಥ|ಕಾಪಟವ ಸುನೀಥ|ಕಾಪಟವ ಸು|ನೀಥ|kāpaṭava su-nītha|kāpaṭava sunītha|kāpaṭava su|nītha|kaapaTava su-niitha|kaapaTava suniitha|kaapaTava su|niitha|kaapaTava su-neetha|kaapaTava suneetha|neetha|_vei_120@575|_vei1_pn148
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@574">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@576">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn148">1:148</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn149">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāpaṭava Su-nītha</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120575"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120575">1</a></sup> as a pupil of <b>Sutemanas <a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्डिल्यायन">śāṇḍilyāyana</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120575"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120575">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 383.</li></ol></div>

कापिलेय|కాపిలేయ|ಕಾಪಿಲೇಯ|kāpileya|kaapileya|_vei_120@576|_vei1_pn148
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@575">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@577">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn148">1:148</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn149">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāpileya.</b>--The Kāpileyas and the <b>Bābhravas</b> are mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120576"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120576">1</a></sup> as descendants of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="देवरात">devarāta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्वामित्र">vaiśvāmitra</a>,</b> the adoptive name of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120576"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120576">↑</a> vii. 17. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 216, n., 433.</li></ol></div>

कापी-पुत्र|कापीपुत्र|कापी|कापि|पुत्र|కాపీ-పుత్ర|కాపీపుత్ర|కాపీ|కాపి|పుత్ర|ಕಾಪೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಕಾಪೀಪುತ್ರ|ಕಾಪೀ|ಕಾಪಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|kāpī-putra|kāpīputra|kāpī|kāpi|putra|kaapii-putra|kaapiiputra|kaapii|kaapi|kaapee-putra|kaapeeputra|kaapee|_vei_120@577|_vei1_pn148
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@576">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@578">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn148">1:148</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn149">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāpī-putra</b> (‘son of Kāpī’) is mentioned in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 5, 1) as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आत्रेयीपुत्र">ātreyīputra</a>.</b></p></div>

कापेय|కాపేయ|ಕಾಪೇಯ|kāpeya|kaapeya|_vei_120@578|_vei1_pn148
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@577">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@579">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn148">1:148</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn149">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāpeya</b> (‘descendant of Kapi’). The Kāpeyas are mentioned as priests of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चित्ररथ">citraratha</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120578"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120578">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120578"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120578">2</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शौनक">śaunaka</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120578"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120578">↑</a> xiii. 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120578"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120578">↑</a> xx. 12, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 52, 53; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 157.</li></ol></div>

काप्य|కాప్య|ಕಾಪ್ಯ|kāpya|kaapya|_vei_120@579|_vei1_pn148
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@578">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@580">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn148">1:148</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn149">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāpya</b> (‘descendant of Kapi’) is the patronymic of <a class="vei_120a" href="सनक">sanaka</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="नवक">navaka</a>, two obviously fictitious persons who served at the Sattra (‘sacrificial session’) of the Vibhindukīyas in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120579">1</a></sup> It is also the patronymic of Patañcala in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upanisad.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120579">2</a></sup> See also <b>Kaiśerya.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120579">↑</a> iii. 233.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120579">↑</a> iii. 3, 1; 7, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 126, 137.</li></ol></div>

काबन्धि|కాబన్ధి|ಕಾಬನ್ಧಿ|kābandhi|kaabandhi|_vei_120@580|_vei1_pn148
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@579">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@581">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn148">1:148</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn149">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kābandhi</b> (‘descendant of Kabandha’) is the patronymic of <b>Vicārin</b> in the Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 2, 9. 18).</p></div>

काम-प्रि|कामप्रि|काम|प्रि|కామ-ప్రి|కామప్రి|కామ|ప్రి|ಕಾಮ-ಪ್ರಿ|ಕಾಮಪ್ರಿ|ಕಾಮ|ಪ್ರಿ|kāma-pri|kāmapri|kāma|pri|kaama-pri|kaamapri|kaama|_vei_120@581|_vei1_pn148
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@580">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@582">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn147">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn148">1:148</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn149">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāma-pri</b> (descendant of Kāmapra’) is the patronymic of <b>Marutta</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 21). In the St. Petersburg Dictionary it is suggested that the reading in this passage should be <i>kāmapre,</i> ‘fulfilling desires,’ as an epithet of the sacrifice (<i>yajñe</i>). </p></div>

कामलायन|కామలాయన|ಕಾಮಲಾಯನ|kāmalāyana|kaamalaayana|_vei_120@582|_vei1_pn149
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@581">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@583">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn148">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn149">1:149</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn150">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāmalāyana</b> (‘descendant of Kamala’) is the patronymic of <b>Upakosala</b> in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iv. 10, 1).</p></div>

काम्पील|कांपील|కామ్పీల|కాంపీల|ಕಾಮ್ಪೀಲ|ಕಾಂಪೀಲ|kāmpīla|kaampiila|kaampeela|_vei_120@583|_vei1_pn149
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@582">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@584">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn148">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn149">1:149</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn150">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāmpīla.</b>--In one passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120583">1</a></sup> the epithet <a class="vei_120a" href="काम्पील">kāmpīla</a>-vāsinī is applied to a woman, perhaps the king's <a class="vei_120a" href="महिषी">mahiṣī</a> or chief wife, whose duty it was to sleep beside the slaughtered animal at the horse sacrifice (<a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>). The exact interpretation of the passage is very uncertain, but both Weber<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120583">2</a></sup> and Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120583">3</a></sup> agree in regarding <a class="vei_120a" href="काम्पील">kāmpīla</a> as the name of the town known as Kāmpīlya in the later literature, and the capital of <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a> in <a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120583">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 4, 19, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 12, 20; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, iv. 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 9, 6; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 2, 8, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120583">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 184; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 114, 115.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120583">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 36, 37. So also Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 204; von Schroeder, Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, 1, xxi; <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 164; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 321, 322.</li></ol></div>

काम्बोज|कांबोज|కామ్బోజ|కాంబోజ|ಕಾಮ್ಬೋಜ|ಕಾಂಬೋಜ|kāmboja|kaamboja|_vei_120@584|_vei1_pn149
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@583">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@585">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn148">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn149">1:149</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn150">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāmboja</b> (‘native of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कम्बोज">kamboja</a></b>’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="औपमन्यव">aupamanyava</a></b> (‘descendant of Upamanyu’) is mentioned as a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120584"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120584">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120584"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120584">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372; <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 45: Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 102.</li></ol></div>

कारपचव|కారపచవ|ಕಾರಪಚವ|kārapacava|kaarapachava|_vei_120@585|_vei1_pn149
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@584">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@586">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn148">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn149">1:149</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn150">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kārapacava</b> is the name in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120585"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120585">1</a></sup> of a place on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120585"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120585">↑</a> xxv. 10, 23. <i>Cf.</i> Āśvalāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiii. 29, 25; Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxiv. 6, 10; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 34.</li></ol></div>

कारस्कर|కారస్కర|ಕಾರಸ್ಕರ|kāraskara|kaaraskara|_vei_120@586|_vei1_pn149
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@585">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@587">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn148">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn149">1:149</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn150">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāraskara</b> is the name of a people mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120586"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120586">1</a></sup> and the Āpastamba<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120586"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120586">2</a></sup> and Hiraṇyakeśi<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120586"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120586">3</a></sup> Sūtras. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120586"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120586">↑</a> xx. 13. (14). <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 2, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120586"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120586">↑</a> xxii. 6, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120586"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120586">↑</a> xvii. 6, <i>Cf.</i> Bühler, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 14, 148; Caland, <i>Zcitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 56, 553.</li></ol></div>

कारि|కారి|ಕಾರಿ|kāri|kaari|_vei_120@587|_vei1_pn150
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@586">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@588">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn149">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn150">1:150</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn151">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāri</b> is the name of one of the victims of the human sacrifice (Puruṣamedha) in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120587"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120587">1</a></sup> and is there dedicated to ‘laughter.’ The commentator Mahīdhara<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120587"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120587">2</a></sup> interprets the word as ‘worker’ (<i>karaṇa-śīla</i>), but the St. Petersburg Dictionary suggests that it means a ‘jubilant’ person (as derived from the root <i>kṛ,</i> ‘to praise’).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120587"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120587">↑</a> xxx. 6. 20; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120587"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120587">↑</a> On Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

कारीरदि|కారీరది|ಕಾರೀರದಿ|kārīradi|kaariiradi|kaareeradi|_vei_120@588|_vei1_pn150
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@587">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@589">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn149">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn150">1:150</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn151">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kārīradi</b> is the name of persons mentioned in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (ii. 4, 4) as holding a special view of the Udgītha (<a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a> Chant).</p></div>

कारु|కారు|ಕಾರು|kāru|kaaru|_vei_120@589|_vei1_pn150
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@588">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@590">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn149">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn150">1:150</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn151">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāru,</b> ‘poet,’ is a word almost confined to the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120589">1</a></sup> There is evidence that the poet was regarded as a professional man, just as much as the physician (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="भिषज्">bhiṣaj</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120589">2</a></sup> The poets, no doubt, mainly lived at the courts of princes amid their retainers,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120589">3</a></sup> though they would probably also sing the praises of rich merchants. There was probably no essential connexion between the priest and the poet. Though the priest was often a poet, yet poetry can hardly have been restricted to the priestly caste. Indeed, at the horse sacrifice (<a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>) the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120589">4</a></sup> expressly requires that one of the singers of panegyrics should be a <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a>, while the other was a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, both singing verses of their own composition. The Anukramaṇī (Index) in several cases<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120589">5</a></sup> attributes hymns of the Rigveda to princes; and even though this may often be merely the same sort of procedure<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120589">6</a></sup> as has made Śūdraka the author of the Mṛcchakaṭikā, or Harṣa of the Ratnāvalī, and has given us royal teachers of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> doctrine,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120589">7</a></sup> still the Indian tradition evidently saw nothing odd in the idea of nonBrāhmaṇas as poets. Most of the non-sacred poetry has,  however, disappeared, for the epic is a product, as it stands, of a later period. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120589">↑</a> i. 148, 2; 165, 12; 177, 5; 178, 3; ii. 43, 1; iii. 33, 8; 39, 7; v. 33, 7; vii. 27; 68, 9; 72, 4, etc.: Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 8, 7; Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120589">↑</a> ix. 112. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120589">↑</a> vii. 73. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120589">↑</a> xiii. 1, 5, 1; 4, 3, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120589">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> x. 92 is attributed to <a class="vei_120a" href="शार्यात">śāryāta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120589">↑</a> See Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 202.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120589">↑</a> Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 20, n. 4, sees in Av. xx. a recension of <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> character. He also finds Kṣatriyas in <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a>,</b> but hardly with reason. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

कारोतर|కారోతర|ಕಾರೋತರ|kārotara|kaarotara|_vei_120@590|_vei1_pn151
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@589">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@591">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn150">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn151">1:151</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn152">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kārotara</b> appears to denote in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120590"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120590">1</a></sup> and occasionally later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120590"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120590">2</a></sup> a ‘filter’ or ‘sieve’ for purifying the liquor called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुरा">surā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120590"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120590">↑</a> i. 116, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120590"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120590">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 16, 82; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 9, 1, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 280.</li></ol></div>

कारोती|कारोति|కారోతీ|కారోతి|ಕಾರೋತೀ|ಕಾರೋತಿ|kārotī|kāroti|kaarotii|kaaroti|kaarotee|_vei_120@591|_vei1_pn151
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@590">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@592">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn150">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn151">1:151</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn152">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kārotī</b> is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (ix. 5, 2, 15) as a place, or perhaps a river, where <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर कावषेय">tura kāvaṣeya</a></b> made a fire-altar--that is, as a seat of the fire-cult <i>par excellence.</i></p></div>

कार्शकेयी-पुत्र|कार्शकेयीपुत्र|कार्शकेयी|कार्शकेयि|पुत्र|కార్శకేయీ-పుత్ర|కార్శకేయీపుత్ర|కార్శకేయీ|కార్శకేయి|పుత్ర|ಕಾರ್ಶಕೇಯೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಕಾರ್ಶಕೇಯೀಪುತ್ರ|ಕಾರ್ಶಕೇಯೀ|ಕಾರ್ಶಕೇಯಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|kārśakeyī-putra|kārśakeyīputra|kārśakeyī|kārśakeyi|putra|kaarshakeyii-putra|kaarshakeyiiputra|kaarshakeyii|kaarshakeyi|kaarshakeyee-putra|kaarshakeyeeputra|kaarshakeyee|_vei_120@592|_vei1_pn151
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@591">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@593">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn150">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn151">1:151</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn152">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kārśakeyī-putra</b> (‘son of Kārśakeyī’) is the name of a man mentioned in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> (vi. 5, 2) recension he is a pupil of <b>Prācīnayogīputra;</b> in the Mādhyaṃdina (vi. 4, 33) recension his teacher's name is <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राश्नीपुत्र">prāśnīputra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="आसुरिवासिन्">āsurivāsin</a>.</b></p></div>

कार्ष्णायस|కార్ష్ణాయస|ಕಾರ್ಷ್ಣಾಯಸ|kārṣṇāyasa|kaarShNaayasa|_vei_120@593|_vei1_pn151
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@592">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@594">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn150">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn151">1:151</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn152">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kārṣṇāyasa</b> (‘black metal’), a word found in the Upaniṣads,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120593"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120593">1</a></sup> must clearly mean ‘iron.’ See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्">ayas</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120593"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120593">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 17, 7; vi. 1, 5; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 17, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 52.</li></ol></div>

कार्ष्मन्|కార్ష్మన్|ಕಾರ್ಷ್ಮನ್|kārṣman|kaarShman|_vei_120@594|_vei1_pn151
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@593">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@595">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn150">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn151">1:151</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn152">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kārṣman,</b> a word meaning literally ‘furrow,’ and found only in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120594">1</a></sup> is the designation of the goal in the chariot race. The competitor probably turned round it and came back to the starting-place.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120594">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120594">↑</a> i. 116, 17; ix. 36, 1; 74, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120594">↑</a> Av. ii. 14, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 291, 292.</li></ol></div>

कार्ष्मर्य|కార్ష్మర్య|ಕಾರ್ಷ್ಮರ್ಯ|kārṣmarya|kaarShmarya|_vei_120@595|_vei1_pn151
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@594">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@596">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn150">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn151">1:151</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn152">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kārṣmarya</b> is the name of a tree (<i>Gmelina arborea</i>) which is often alluded to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120595"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120595">1</a></sup> the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120595"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120595">2</a></sup> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120595"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120595">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120595"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120595">↑</a> v. 2, 7, 3. 4; vi. 2, 1, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120595"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120595">↑</a> iii. 2, 6; 7, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120595"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120595">↑</a> iii. 4, 1, 6; 8, 2, 17; iv. 3, 3, 6; vii. 4, 1, 37. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 62.</li></ol></div>

काल|కాల|ಕಾಲ|kāla|kaala|_vei_120@596|_vei1_pn152
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@595">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@597">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn151">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn152">1:152</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn153">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāla,</b> the generic expression for ‘time,’ first occurs in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120596"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120596">1</a></sup> where, however, it is used only once, in the late tenth book. It is known to the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120596"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120596">2</a></sup> where <a class="vei_120a" href="काल">kāla</a> has already developed the sense of time as fate. The word is frequent in the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120596"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120596">3</a></sup> superseding the earlier use of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋतु">ṛtu</a>.</b> The more general division of time is into ‘past’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="भूत">bhūta</a></i>), ‘present’ (<i>bhavat</i>), and ‘future’ (<i>bhaviṣyat</i>).<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120596"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120596">4</a></sup> For other divisons see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अहन्">ahan</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="संवत्सर">saṃvatsara</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120596"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120596">↑</a> x. 42, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120596"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120596">↑</a> xix. 53. 54.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120596"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120596">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 3, 3; ii. 4, 2, 4; iii. 8, 3, 36; vii. 2, 2, 21, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120596"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120596">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, vii. 20.</li></ol></div>

कालका|కాలకా|ಕಾಲಕಾ|kālakā|kaalakaa|_vei_120@597|_vei1_pn152
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@596">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@598">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn151">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn152">1:152</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn153">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kālakā</b> is the name of one of the victims at the horse sacrifice (<a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120597"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120597">1</a></sup> variously identified with a bird<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120597"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120597">2</a></sup> or a chameleon.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120597"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120597">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120597"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120597">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 15, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 16; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 35.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120597"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120597">↑</a> Mahīdhara on Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120597"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120597">↑</a> Sāyaṇa on <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>. Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 99.</li></ol></div>

काल-काञ्ज|कालकाञ्ज|काल|काञ्ज|కాల-కాఞ్జ|కాలకాఞ్జ|కాల|కాఞ్జ|ಕಾಲ-ಕಾಞ್ಜ|ಕಾಲಕಾಞ್ಜ|ಕಾಲ|ಕಾಞ್ಜ|kāla-kāñja|kālakāñja|kāla|kāñja|kaala-kaa~nja|kaalakaa~nja|kaala|kaa~nja|_vei_120@598|_vei1_pn152
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@597">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@599">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn151">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn152">1:152</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn153">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāla-kāñja.</b>--In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120598"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120598">1</a></sup> mention is made of the Kālakāñjas as being in the sky. Both Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120598"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120598">2</a></sup> and Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120598"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120598">3</a></sup> hold that some constellation is meant. But as the defeat of the Kālakāñjas is one of Indra's exploits,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120598"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120598">4</a></sup> it is doubtful whether any stress can be laid on that interpretation of the passage in the Atharvaveda. Whitney<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120598"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120598">5</a></sup> suggests that the three stars of Orion are meant, Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120598"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120598">6</a></sup> that the galaxy or the stars in general are intended. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120598"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120598">↑</a> vi. 80, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120598"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120598">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120598"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120598">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 353.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120598"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120598">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, viii. 1, <i>Cf.</i> also Maitrāyanī Saṃhitā, i. 6, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 2, 4-6; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120598"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120598">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 341.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120598"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120598">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 500; <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 163-169.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 410, 414 <i>et seq.;</i> 3, 465; Oertel, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 19, 121.</li></ol></div>

कावषेय|కావషేయ|ಕಾವಷೇಯ|kāvaṣeya|kaavaSheya|_vei_120@599|_vei1_pn153
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@598">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@600">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn152">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn153">1:153</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn155">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāvaṣeya</b> (‘descendant of Kavaṣa’) is the constant patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर">tura</a>.</b> The Kāvaṣeyas are also mentioned as teachers of philosophical points in the Rigveda Āraṇyakas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120599">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120599">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 2, 6; Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, viii. 11.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 391, n.; 2, 418; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 257.</li></ol></div>

काव्य|కావ్య|ಕಾವ್ಯ|kāvya|kaavya|_vei_120@600|_vei1_pn153
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@599">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@601">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn152">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn153">1:153</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn155">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāvya</b> (‘descendant of Kavi’) is the constant<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120600"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120600">1</a></sup> patronymic of <b>Uśanas.</b> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> it is also applied to <b>Iḍhat</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120600"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120600">2</a></sup> and <b>Ukṣṇorandhra.</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120600"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120600">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120600"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120600">↑</a> Rv. i. 51, 11; 83, 5; 121, 12; vi. 20. 11; viii. 23, 17; Av. iv. 29, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 8, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120600"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120600">↑</a> xiv. 9, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120600"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120600">↑</a> xiii. 9, 19.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 48, 49.</li></ol></div>

काश|కాశ|ಕಾಶ|kāśa|kaasha|_vei_120@601|_vei1_pn153
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@600">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@602">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn152">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn153">1:153</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn155">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāśa.</b>--Roth<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120601"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120601">1</a></sup> finds this word, which denotes a species of grass (<i>Saccharum spontaneum</i>) used for mats, etc., in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120601"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120601">2</a></sup> but the reading is uncertain. The word has this sense in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120601"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120601">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120601"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120601">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120601"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120601">↑</a> x. 100, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120601"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120601">↑</a> vi. 9, 1.</li></ol></div>

काशि|काश्य|కాశి|కాశ్య|ಕಾಶಿ|ಕಾಶ್ಯ|kāśi|kāśya|kaashi|kaashya|_vei_120@602|_vei1_pn153
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@601">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@603">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn152">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn153">1:153</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn155">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāśi, Kāśya.</b>--The name <a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a> denotes (in the plural<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120602">1</a></sup>) the people of <a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a> (Benares), and <a class="vei_120a" href="काश्य">kāśya</a>, the king of <a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a>. The Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120602">2</a></sup> tells of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धृतराष्ट्र">dhṛtarāṣṭra</a>,</b> king of <a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a>, who was defeated by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शतानीक सात्राजित">śatānīka sātrājita</a>,</b> with the result that the Kāśis, down to the time of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, gave up the kindling of the sacred fire. <a class="vei_120a" href="सात्राजित">sātrājita</a> was a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a>.</b> We hear also of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अजातशत्रु">ajātaśatru</a></b> as a king of <a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a>;<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120602">3</a></sup> and no doubt <b>Bhadrasena Ajātaśatrava,</b> a contemporary of <b>Uddālaka,</b> was also a king of <a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a>.</p><p>The Kāśis and <b>Videhas</b> were closely connected, as was natural in view of their geographical position. The compound name <a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a> occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>;<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120602">4</a></sup> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120602">5</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गार्गी">gārgī</a></b> describes <a class="vei_120a" href="अजातशत्रु">ajātaśatru</a> as either a <a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a> or a <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a> king. The <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120602">6</a></sup>  mentions one <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> as acting for the kings of <a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>; and the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120602">7</a></sup> mentions <a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a> in close proximity. Weber,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120602">8</a></sup> indeed, throws out the suggestion that the Kāśis and the Videhas together constitute the <b>Uśīnaras,</b> whose name is very rare in Vedic literature.</p><p>As <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a> were in close connexion, <a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a> are found combined in the compound name KāśiKauśalyas of the Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120602">9</a></sup></p><p>Though <a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a> is a late word, it is quite possible that the town is older, as the river <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वरणावती">varaṇāvatī</a></b> referred to in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120602">10</a></sup> may be connected with the later Vārāṇasī (Benares).</p><p>It is significant that while the Kāśis, Kosalas, and Videhas were united, any relations which the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcala peoples may have had with them were hostile. It is a fair conclusion that between these two great groups of peoples there did exist some political conflict as well as probably a difference of culture in some degree. The Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120602">11</a></sup> in the story of the advance of Āryan civilization over <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>, preserves a clear tradition of this time, and a piece of evidence that in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a> country lay the real centre of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> culture (see also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a></b>). That the <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a>-Videhas were originally settlers of older date than the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas is reasonably obvious from their geographical position, but the true <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> culture appears to have been brought to them from the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcala country. It is very probable that the East was less Āryan than the West, and that it was less completely reduced under Brahmin spiritual supremacy, as the movement of Buddhism was Eastern, and the Buddhist texts<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120602">12</a></sup> reveal a position in which the Kṣatriyas rank above Brāhmaṇas. With this agrees the fact that the later Vedic texts<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120602">13</a></sup> display  towards the people of <a class="vei_120a" href="मगध">magadha</a> a marked antipathy, which may be reasonably explained by that people's lack of orthodoxy, and which may perhaps be traced as far back as the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120602">14</a></sup> It is, of course, possible that the <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a>-Videhas and Kāśis actually were merely offshoots of the tribes later known as the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas, and that they by reason of distance and less complete subjugation of the aborigines lost their Brahminical culture. This hypothesis, however, appears less likely, though it might be supported by a literal interpretation of the legend of the Aryan migration in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_120602">15</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120602">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 4, 19. 21. The plural occurs also in the Paippalāda recension of the Atharvaveda, v. 22, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120602">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 19.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120602">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 1, 1; iii. 8, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120602">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120602">↑</a> iii. 8, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120602">↑</a> xvi. 29, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120602">↑</a> xxi. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120602">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 212, 213.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120602">↑</a> i. 2, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120602">↑</a> iv. 7. 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 20; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 376.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120602">↑</a> i. 4, 1, 10 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 170 <i>et seq.;</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, xlii <i>et seq.,</i> 104, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120602">↑</a> See Fick, <i>Die seciale Gliederung,</i> chap. iv.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120602">↑</a> Kātyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxii. 4, 22; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 6, 28. See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 99; Fick, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 140, n. 1; and <i>of.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मगध">magadha</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120602">↑</a> xxx. 5. 22. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मगध">magadha</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_120602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_120602">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 104, n. 1. <i>Cf.</i> Grierson, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 837, 1143; Keith, <i>ibid.,</i> 831, 1138; Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 402 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

कास्यप|కాస్యప|ಕಾಸ್ಯಪ|kāsyapa|kaasyapa|_vei_120@603|_vei1_pn155
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@602">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@604">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn153">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn155">1:155</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn156">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāsyapa</b> (‘descendant of Kaśyapa’) is a common patronymic,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120603"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120603">1</a></sup> and is applied specially to <b>Ṛśyaśṛṅga, Devataras <a class="vei_120a" href="श्यावसायन">śyāvasāyana</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शूष वाह्नेय">śūṣa vāhneya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120603"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120603">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 5, 1, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 18; x. 1, 8, etc.</li></ol></div>

काश्यपी-बालाक्या-माठरी-पुत्र|काश्यपी-बालाक्या-माठरीपुत्र|काश्यपी-बालाक्या-माठरी|काश्यपी-बालाक्या-माठरि|पुत्र|కాశ్యపీ-బాలాక్యా-మాఠరీ-పుత్ర|కాశ్యపీ-బాలాక్యా-మాఠరీపుత్ర|కాశ్యపీ-బాలాక్యా-మాఠరీ|కాశ్యపీ-బాలాక్యా-మాఠరి|పుత్ర|ಕಾಶ್ಯಪೀ-ಬಾಲಾಕ್ಯಾ-ಮಾಠರೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಕಾಶ್ಯಪೀ-ಬಾಲಾಕ್ಯಾ-ಮಾಠರೀಪುತ್ರ|ಕಾಶ್ಯಪೀ-ಬಾಲಾಕ್ಯಾ-ಮಾಠರೀ|ಕಾಶ್ಯಪೀ-ಬಾಲಾಕ್ಯಾ-ಮಾಠರಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|kāśyapī-bālākyā-māṭharī-putra|kāśyapī-bālākyā-māṭharīputra|kāśyapī-bālākyā-māṭharī|kāśyapī-bālākyā-māṭhari|putra|kaashyapii-baalaakyaa-maaTharii-putra|kaashyapii-baalaakyaa-maaThariiputra|kaashyapii-baalaakyaa-maaTharii|kaashyapii-baalaakyaa-maaThari|kaashyapee-baalaakyaa-maaTharee-putra|kaashyapee-baalaakyaa-maaThareeputra|kaashyapee-baalaakyaa-maaTharee|kaashyapee-baalaakyaa-maaThari|_vei_120@604|_vei1_pn155
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@603">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@605">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn153">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn155">1:155</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn156">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāśyapī-<a class="vei_120a" href="बालाक्या">bālākyā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="माठरी">māṭharī</a>-putra</b> (‘son of Kāśyapī, <a class="vei_120a" href="बालाक्या">bālākyā</a>, and Māṭharī’). This curious name is given in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120604"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120604">1</a></sup> to a teacher, pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौत्सीपुत्र">kautsīputra</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120604"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120604">↑</a> vi. 4, 31 (Mādhyaṃdina recension).</li></ol></div>

काषायण|కాషాయణ|ಕಾಷಾಯಣ|kāṣāyaṇa|kaaShaayaNa|_vei_120@605|_vei1_pn155
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@604">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@606">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn153">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn155">1:155</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn156">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāṣāyaṇa</b> is mentioned in the second <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> as a teacher, pupil of <a class="vei_120a" href="सायकायन">sāyakāyana</a> according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> (iv. 6, 2), of <a class="vei_120a" href="सौकरायण">saukarāyaṇa</a> according to the Mādhyaṃdina recension (iv. 5, 27).</p></div>

काष्ठा|కాష్ఠా|ಕಾಷ್ಠಾ|kāṣṭhā|kaaShThaa|_vei_120@606|_vei1_pn155
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@605">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@607">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn153">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn155">1:155</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn156">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāṣṭhā</b> seems to have the sense in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120606"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120606">1</a></sup> of ‘course’ for a chariot race. It also means in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120606"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120606">2</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120606"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120606">3</a></sup> ‘goal,’ either like the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कार्ष्मन्">kārṣman</a></b> the turning place, or the final goal (<i>paramā <a class="vei_120a" href="काष्ठा">kāṣṭhā</a></i>). </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120606"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120606">↑</a> i. 37, 10; 65, 3; iv. 58, 7; vi. 46, 1; vii. 93, 3; viii. 80, 8; ix. 21, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120606"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120606">↑</a> x. 102, 9, is perhaps so to be taken.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120606"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120606">↑</a> Av. ii. 14, 6: <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 9, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ix. 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 7; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 7, 2. etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 291, 292; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 77.</li></ol></div>

कास्|का|कास|कासा|कासिका|కాస్|కా|కాస|కాసా|కాసికా|ಕಾಸ್|ಕಾ|ಕಾಸ|ಕಾಸಾ|ಕಾಸಿಕಾ|kās|kā|kāsa|kāsā|kāsikā|kaas|kaa|kaasa|kaasaa|kaasikaa|_vei_120@607|_vei1_pn156
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@606">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@608">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn155">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn156">1:156</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn157">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kās, <a class="vei_120a" href="कास">kāsa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कासा">kāsā</a>, Kāsikā.</b>--All these four forms<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120607">1</a></sup> of the same word denote ‘cough,’ which is mentioned in the Atharvaveda as accompanying a headache,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120607">2</a></sup> as a symptom in fever (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="तक्मन्">takman</a></b>),<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120607">3</a></sup> and as an independent disease.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120607">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120607">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कास्">kās</a>: Av. i. 12, 3; v. 22, 10; <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कास">kāsa</a>:</b> Av. v. 22, 11 (probably); <a class="vei_120a" href="कासा">kāsā</a>: Av. vi. 105, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="कासिका">kāsikā</a>: Av, v. 22, 12; xi. 2, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120607">↑</a> Av. i. 12, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120607">↑</a> v. 22, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120607">↑</a> vi. 105.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 385; Grohmann, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 394; Jolly, <i>Medicin,</i> 89.</li></ol></div>

काहोडि|కాహోడి|ಕಾಹೋಡಿ|kāhoḍi|kaahoDi|_vei_120@608|_vei1_pn156
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@607">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@609">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn155">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn156">1:156</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn157">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāhoḍi</b> (‘descendant of Kahoḍa’) is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अर्गल">argala</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā (xxv. 5).</p></div>

किंशुक|కింశుక|ಕಿಂಶುಕ|kiṃśuka|kiMshuka|_vei_120@609|_vei1_pn156
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@608">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@610">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn155">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn156">1:156</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn157">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kiṃśuka</b> is the name of a tree (<i>Butea frondosa</i>) mentioned in the wedding hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120609"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120609">1</a></sup> the bridal car being described as adorned with its blossoms (<i>su-<a class="vei_120a" href="किंशुक">kiṃśuka</a></i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120609"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120609">↑</a> x. 85, 20. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 62. Sāyaṇa thinks the meaning is that the car is made of the wood of the tree.</li></ol></div>

किकि-दीवि|किकिदीवि|किकि|दीवि|కికి-దీవి|కికిదీవి|కికి|దీవి|ಕಿಕಿ-ದೀವಿ|ಕಿಕಿದೀವಿ|ಕಿಕಿ|ದೀವಿ|kiki-dīvi|kikidīvi|kiki|dīvi|kiki-diivi|kikidiivi|diivi|kiki-deevi|kikideevi|deevi|_vei_120@610|_vei1_pn156
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@609">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@611">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn155">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn156">1:156</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn157">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kiki-dīvi</b> denotes some kind of bird, perhaps the blue jay.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120610"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120610">1</a></sup> According to the commentator, it means ‘partridge’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="तित्तिरि">tittiri</a></i>) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120610"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120610">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120610"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120610">↑</a> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> See Rv. x. 97, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120610"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120610">↑</a> v. 6, 22, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 92; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 251.</li></ol></div>

कितव|కితవ|ಕಿತವ|kitava|_vei_120@611|_vei1_pn156
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@610">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@612">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn155">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn156">1:156</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn157">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kitava,</b> ‘the gambler,’ is frequently referred to in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120611">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120611">2</a></sup> A father is represented as chastising his son for gambling.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120611">3</a></sup> The gambler seems at times to have fallen, along with his family, into servitude, presumably by selling himself to pay his debts.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120611">4</a></sup> Technical names<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120611">5</a></sup> for different sorts of gamblers given in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās are Ādinava-<a class="vei_120a" href="दर्श">darśa</a>,  Kalpin, <a class="vei_120a" href="अधि">adhi</a>-kalpin, and <a class="vei_120a" href="सभा-स्थाणु">sabhā-sthāṇu</a>. None of these can be safely<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120611">6</a></sup> explained, though the last has usually<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120611">7</a></sup> been taken as a satirical name derived from the gambler's devotion to the dicing place (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a></b>), ‘pillar of the dicing hall.’ The first literally means ‘seeing ill-luck,’<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120611">8</a></sup> and may refer to the quickness of the dicer to note an error on the part of his antagonist, or to his eagerness to see the defeat of his rival.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120611">↑</a> ii. 29, 5; v. 85, 8; x. 34, 3. 7. 10. 11. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120611">↑</a> Av. vii. 50, 1; 109, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 8. 18. 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 19, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120611">↑</a> Rv. ii. 29, 5. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पितृ">pitṛ</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120611">↑</a> Rv. x. 34. <i>Cf.</i> perhaps the <i>bhaktadāsa,</i> ‘slave for hire,’ of the <a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Sāstra, viii. 415; Fick, <i>Die sociale Gliederung,</i> 197.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120611">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 3, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120611">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 18, 282; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 284.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120611">↑</a> So Mahīdhara on Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 18; Sāyaṇa on <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 16, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120611">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Weber, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

किं-पुरुष|किंपुरुष|किं|पुरुष|కిం-పురుష|కింపురుష|కిం|పురుష|ಕಿಂ-ಪುರುಷ|ಕಿಂಪುರುಷ|ಕಿಂ|ಪುರುಷ|kiṃ-puruṣa|kiṃpuruṣa|kiṃ|puruṣa|kiM-puruSha|kiMpuruSha|kiM|puruSha|_vei_120@612|_vei1_pn157
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@611">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@613">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn156">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn157">1:157</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn158">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kiṃ-puruṣa,</b> lit. ‘what sort of man,’ appears in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120612">1</a></sup> to designate the ‘ape,’ which is a mimic man. Possibly the same sense should be seen in the passage of the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120612">2</a></sup> where it occurs, and where Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120612">3</a></sup> assumes it to refer to a contemptible man. Max Müller<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120612">4</a></sup> renders it ‘savage.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120612">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 8; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 3, 9; vii. 5, 2, 32.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120612">↑</a> xxx. 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 12, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120612">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120612">↑</a> <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 420.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 246; <i>Omina und Portenta,</i> 356; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 51, n. 3.</li></ol></div>

कियाम्बु|कियांबु|కియామ్బు|కియాంబు|ಕಿಯಾಮ್ಬು|ಕಿಯಾಂಬು|kiyāmbu|kiyaambu|_vei_120@613|_vei1_pn157
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@612">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@614">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn156">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn157">1:157</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn158">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kiyāmbu</b> is the name of one of the water-plants which are to grow, according to a funeral hymn in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120613">1</a></sup> on the place where the body of the dead was burned. The word seems to mean ‘having some water,’ possibly by popular etymology.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120613">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120613">↑</a> x. 16, 13 = Av. xviii. 3, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120613">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Sāyaṇa on Rv., <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> and on <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, vi. 4, 1, 2, where Kyāmbu is the form.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 62; Bloomfield, <i>Proceedings of the American Oriental Society,</i> October, 1890, xl.</li></ol></div>

किरात|किरात|కిరాత_౧|కిరాత|ಕಿರಾತ_೧|ಕಿರಾತ|kirāta_1|kirāta|kiraata_1|kiraata|_vei_120@614|_vei1_pn157
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@613">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@615">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn156">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn157">1:157</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn158">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Kirāta</b> is a name applied to a people living in the caves of the mountains, as appears clearly from the dedication of the <a class="vei_120a" href="किरात">kirāta</a> to the caves (<i>guhā</i>) in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120614"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120614">1</a></sup> and from the reference in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120614"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120614">2</a></sup> to a <a class="vei_120a" href="किरात">kirāta</a> girl (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कैरातिका">kairātikā</a></i>), who digs a remedy on the ridges of the mountains.  Later<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120614"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120614">3</a></sup> the people called Kirātas were located in Eastern Nepal, but the name seems to have been applied to any hill folk, no doubt aborigines, though the <a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Śāstra<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120614"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120614">4</a></sup> regards them as degraded Kṣatriyas.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120614"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120614">↑</a> xxx. 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 12, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120614"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120614">↑</a> x. 4, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120614"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120614">↑</a> Lassen, <i>Indische Alterthumskunde,</i> 1^2, 530, 534.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120614"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120614">↑</a> x. 44.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 32; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 207; V. Smith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Socicty,</i> 1909, 258, n. 1; Lévi, <i>Le Népal,</i> 2, 77.</li></ol></div>

किरात|किरात|కిరాత_౨|కిరాత|ಕಿರಾತ_೨|ಕಿರಾತ|kirāta_2|kirāta|kiraata_2|kiraata|_vei_120@615|_vei1_pn158
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@614">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@616">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn157">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn158">1:158</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn159">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Kirāta.</b>--In the story of <b>Asamāti</b> there appear, as the two priests who are opposed to the <b>Gaupāyanas,</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="किरात">kirāta</a> and <b>Akuli</b> according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120615">1</a></sup> or <a class="vei_120a" href="किलात">kilāta</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="आकुलि">ākuli</a> according to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120615">2</a></sup> No doubt the name is chosen, not as that of a historic person, but as a suitable designation of a hostile priest; for it is probably identical with the name of the mountaineers described in the preceding article.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120615">↑</a> xiii. 12, 5 (where the text reads <i><a class="vei_120a" href="किरात">kirāta</a>-kulyau</i>). Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> takes the word, with Sāyaṇa, as an adjective, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="किरात">kirāta</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कुल">kula</a>,</i> ‘of the family of <a class="vei_120a" href="किरात">kirāta</a>.’ The reading in the Bṛhaddevatā (vii. 86) is <i>kirātākulī.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120615">↑</a> i. 1, 4, 14 (where the text reads <i>kilātākulī</i>). <i>Cf.</i> also Sāṭyāyanaka <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> <i>apud</i> Sāyaṇa on Rv. x. 57, 1; 60, 1; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 167; <i>Journal of the American Oriental Socicty,</i> 18, 41 <i>et seq.;</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 48, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

किलात|కిలాత|ಕಿಲಾತ|kilāta|kilaata|_vei_120@616|_vei1_pn158
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@615">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@617">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn157">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn158">1:158</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn159">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kilāta</b> is the form of the name 2. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="किरात">kirāta</a></b> that appears in the Śatapatha, Śāṭyāyanaka, and Jaiminīya Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120616"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120616">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120616"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120616">↑</a> See note^2 under the preceding article.</li></ol></div>

किलास|కిలాస|ಕಿಲಾಸ|kilāsa|kilaasa|_vei_120@617|_vei1_pn158
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@616">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@618">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn157">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn158">1:158</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn159">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kilāsa</b> is the name of a disease, ‘white leprosy,’ in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120617"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120617">1</a></sup> and the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, etc.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120617"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120617">2</a></sup> It resulted in the appearance of grey (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पलित">palita</a></i>) and white (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शुक्ल">śukla</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="श्वेत">śveta</a></i>) spots all over the skin. Haug gave the same sense to <i>alasa</i> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120617"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120617">3</a></sup> but this is doubtful. The fem. Kilāsī is taken by Max Müller to mean a ‘spotted deer’ in one passage of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120617"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120617">4</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120617"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120617">↑</a> i. 23, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120617"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120617">↑</a> xxx. 21; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 3, 17; xxiii. 11, 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, v. 4, 12. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 391; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 266; Jolly, <i>Medicin,</i> 98; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 68.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120617"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120617">↑</a> vi. 33, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120617"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120617">↑</a> v. 53, 1.</li></ol></div>

कीकट|కీకట|ಕೀಕಟ|kīkaṭa|kiikaTa|keekaTa|_vei_120@618|_vei1_pn159
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@617">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@619">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn158">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn159">1:159</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn160">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kīkaṭa.</b>--The name of this people occurs only in one passage of the Rigveda, where they appear as hostile to the singer and as under the leadership of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रमगन्द">pramaganda</a>.</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120618"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120618">2</a></sup> declares that <a class="vei_120a" href="कीकट">kīkaṭa</a> was the name of a non-Āryan country, and later<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120618"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120618">3</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="कीकट">kīkaṭa</a> is given as a synonym of <a class="vei_120a" href="मगध">magadha</a>. Hence Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120618"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120618">4</a></sup> concludes that the Kīkaṭas were a non-Āryan people living in the country later known as <a class="vei_120a" href="मगध">magadha</a>. Weber<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120618"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120618">5</a></sup> holds that this people were located in <a class="vei_120a" href="मगध">magadha</a>, but were Āryan, though at variance with other Āryan tribes, perhaps because of heretical tendencies, for <a class="vei_120a" href="मगध">magadha</a> was later a seat of Buddhism. But the identification is uncertain, and is doubted by Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120618"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120618">6</a></sup> and Hillebrandt.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120618"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120618">7</a></sup></p><p>^1 iii. 53, 14.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="2"><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120618"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120618">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 32.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120618"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120618">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120618"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120618">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 31, 118. <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Kommentar,</i> 58.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120618"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120618">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 186; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 79, n. *.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120618"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120618">↑</a> <i>Buddha,</i> 402, 403; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 253.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120618"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120618">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 14-18.</li></ol></div>

कीट|కీట|ಕೀಟ|kīṭa|kiiTa|keeTa|_vei_120@619|_vei1_pn159
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@618">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@620">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn158">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn159">1:159</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn160">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kīṭa</b> is the name of a species of worm mentioned in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120619"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120619">1</a></sup> and frequently in the Upaniṣads.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120619"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120619">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120619"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120619">↑</a> ix. 4, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120619"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120619">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 19; 2, 14; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 9. 3; 10, 2; vii. 2, 1; 7, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 2, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 98.</li></ol></div>

कीनाश|కీనాశ|ಕೀನಾಶ|kīnāśa|kiinaasha|keenaasha|_vei_120@620|_vei1_pn159
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@619">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@621">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn158">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn159">1:159</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn160">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kīnāśa,</b> a name of the ploughman or cultivator of the soil, is found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120620"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120620">1</a></sup> and the later Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120620"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120620">2</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृषि">kṛṣi</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120620"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120620">↑</a> iv. 57, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120620"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120620">↑</a> Av. iv. 11, 10; vi. 30, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 8, 7.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 237; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 45; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 86, n.</li></ol></div>

कीरि|కీరి|ಕೀರಿ|kīri|kiiri|keeri|_vei_120@621|_vei1_pn159
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@620">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@622">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn158">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn159">1:159</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn160">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kīri</b> is in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120621"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120621">1</a></sup> a regular designation of the ‘poet.’ <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120621"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120621">↑</a> i. 31, 13; ii. 12, 6; v. 52, 12 (<i>kīriṇaḥ;</i> Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 317). But see Geldner. <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 46; Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 223.</li></ol></div>

कीर्शा|కీర్శా|ಕೀರ್ಶಾ|kīrśā|kiirshaa|keershaa|_vei_120@622|_vei1_pn159
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@621">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@623">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn158">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn159">1:159</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn160">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kīrśā,</b> the name of some kind of animal, or perhaps bird, is mentioned in the list of victims for the horse sacrifice (<a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120622"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120622">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120622"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120622">↑</a> v. 5, 20, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 99; St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

कीलाल|కీలాల|ಕೀಲಾಲ|kīlāla|kiilaala|keelaala|_vei_120@623|_vei1_pn160
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@622">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@624">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn159">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn160">1:160</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn161">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kīlāla,</b> a word denoting a ‘sweet drink,’ is found in all the later Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120623"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120623">1</a></sup> but not in the Rigveda. As the <a class="vei_120a" href="सुरा-कार">surā-kāra</a>, ‘maker of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुरा">surā</a>,’ is dedicated in the list of victims in the human sacrifice<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120623"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120623">2</a></sup> (Puruṣamedha) to <a class="vei_120a" href="कीलाल">kīlāla</a>, it must have been a drink of somewhat the same nature as the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुरा">surā</a></b> itself, possibly, as Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120623"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120623">3</a></sup> suggests, a kind of rum.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120623"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120623">↑</a> Av. iv. 11, 10; 26, 6; 27, 5; vi. 69, 1; x. 6, 25; xii. 1, 59; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 12, 13; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 7, 12; iii. 11, 3. 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ii. 34; iii. 43; xx. 65; xxx. 11, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120623"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120623">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 9, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120623"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120623">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 281.</li></ol></div>

कीश्मील|కీశ్మీల|ಕೀಶ್ಮೀಲ|kīśmīla|kiishmiila|keeshmeela|_vei_120@624|_vei1_pn160
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@623">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@625">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn159">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn160">1:160</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn161">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kīśmīla</b> denotes, according to Bo7htlingk,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120624">1</a></sup> a certain disease in the Paippalāda recension of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120624">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120624">↑</a> Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120624">↑</a> xix. 8, 4.</li></ol></div>

कीस्त|కీస్త|ಕೀಸ್ತ|kīsta|kiista|keesta|_vei_120@625|_vei1_pn160
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@624">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@626">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn159">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn160">1:160</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn161">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kīsta</b> in two passages of the <b>Rigveda</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120625"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120625">1</a></sup> means ‘poet,’ like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कीरि">kīri</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120625"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120625">↑</a> i. 127, 7; vi. 67, 10. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 15.</li></ol></div>

कुक्कुट|కుక్కుట|ಕುಕ್ಕುಟ|kukkuṭa|kukkuTa|_vei_120@626|_vei1_pn160
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@625">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@627">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn159">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn160">1:160</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn161">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kukkuṭa,</b> ‘cock,’ occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120626"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120626">1</a></sup> only.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120626"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120626">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120626"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120626">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, i. 16. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 91.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120626"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120626">↑</a> It is common in the later language.</li></ol></div>

कुटरु|కుటరు|ಕುಟರು|kuṭaru|kuTaru|_vei_120@627|_vei1_pn160
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@626">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@628">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn159">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn160">1:160</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn161">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kuṭaru</b> is, according to the commentator Mahīdhara,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120627">1</a></sup> synonymous with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुक्कुट">kukkuṭa</a>,</b> ‘cock.’ The word is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās only.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120627">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120627">↑</a> On Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120627">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 17, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 1, 6; iii. 14, 4. 20; iv. 1, 6; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 23, 39.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 93.</li></ol></div>

कुण्ड-पायिन्|कुण्डपायिन्|कुण्ड|पायिन्|కుణ్డ-పాయిన్|కుణ్డపాయిన్|కుణ్డ|పాయిన్|ಕುಣ್ಡ-ಪಾಯಿನ್|ಕುಣ್ಡಪಾಯಿನ್|ಕುಣ್ಡ|ಪಾಯಿನ್|kuṇḍa-pāyin|kuṇḍapāyin|kuṇḍa|pāyin|kuNDa-paayin|kuNDapaayin|kuNDa|paayin|_vei_120@628|_vei1_pn160
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@627">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@629">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn159">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn160">1:160</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn161">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kuṇḍa-pāyin</b> (‘drinking from a jug’) is the name of a teacher mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120628">1</a></sup> and in the Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120628">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120628">↑</a> xxv. 4, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120628">↑</a> Āśvalāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 4, 6; Kātyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxiv. 4, 21.</li></ol></div>

कुण्ड-पाय्य|कुण्डपाय्य|कुण्ड|पाय्य|కుణ్డ-పాయ్య|కుణ్డపాయ్య|కుణ్డ|పాయ్య|ಕುಣ್ಡ-ಪಾಯ್ಯ|ಕುಣ್ಡಪಾಯ್ಯ|ಕುಣ್ಡ|ಪಾಯ್ಯ|kuṇḍa-pāyya|kuṇḍapāyya|kuṇḍa|pāyya|kuNDa-paayya|kuNDapaayya|kuNDa|paayya|_vei_120@629|_vei1_pn161
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@628">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@630">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn160">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn161">1:161</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn162">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kuṇḍa-pāyya</b> (‘descendant of Kuṇḍapāyin’) is a patronymic connected with a man named <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शृंगवृष्">śṛṅgavṛṣ</a></b> in one passage of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120629">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120629">↑</a> viii. 17, 13. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 161; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 90.</li></ol></div>

कुण्डृणाची|कुण्डृणाचि|కుణ్డృణాచీ|కుణ్డృణాచి|ಕುಣ್ಡೃಣಾಚೀ|ಕುಣ್ಡೃಣಾಚಿ|kuṇḍṛṇācī|kuṇḍṛṇāci|kuNDRNaachii|kuNDRNaachi|kuNDRNaachee|kuNDRiNaachii|kuNDRiNaachi|kuNDRuNaachii|kuNDRuNaachi|kuNDRRiNaachii|kuNDRRiNaachi|_vei_120@630|_vei1_pn161
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@629">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@631">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn160">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn161">1:161</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn162">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kuṇḍṛṇācī</b> is the name of an animal of unknown character occurring in the lists of victims at the horse sacrifice (<a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120630"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120630">1</a></sup> The word also occurs in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120630"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120630">2</a></sup> in which a bird would seem to be intended, though Sāyaṇa interprets it as meaning ‘with crooked flight’ (<i>kuṭila-gatyā</i>). In his commentary on the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120630"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120630">3</a></sup> he takes the word to denote the houselizard (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="गृह">gṛha</a>-godhikā</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120630"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120630">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 16, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 18; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 37.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120630"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120630">↑</a> i. 29, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120630"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120630">↑</a> v. 5, 16, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 89.</li></ol></div>

कुत्स|కుత్స|ಕುತ್ಸ|kutsa|_vei_120@631|_vei1_pn161
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@630">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@632">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn160">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn161">1:161</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn162">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kutsa</b> is the name of a hero frequently mentioned in the Rigveda, which, however, gives practically no information about him, for he was no doubt already a figure of the mythic past. He is several times<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120631">1</a></sup> called <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्जुनेय">ārjuneya</a>, ‘descendant of Arjuna,’ and is usually<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120631">2</a></sup> associated with <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> in the exploit of defeating the demon Śuṣṇa and winning the sun. He is said<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120631">3</a></sup> to have defeated <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्मदिभ">smadibha</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="तुग्र">tugra</a>,</b> and the <b>Vetasus,</b> but, on the other hand, he is several times<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120631">4</a></sup> mentioned with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अतिथिग्व">atithigva</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आयु">āyu</a></b> as being vanquished by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, his defeat in one passage<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120631">5</a></sup> being attributed to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तूर्वयाण">tūrvayāṇa</a>.</b> Elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120631">6</a></sup> he appears with <a class="vei_120a" href="अतिथिग्व">atithigva</a> as a friend of Indra's. In the later literature he is seldom<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120631">7</a></sup> mentioned except in connexion with the myth of his binding <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, which is found in the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120631">8</a></sup> and which is based on an obscure verse in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120631">9</a></sup> </p><p>The Kutsas, or descendants of <a class="vei_120a" href="कुत्स">kutsa</a>, are mentioned in one hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120631">10</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120631">↑</a> Rv. iv. 26, 1; vii. 19, 2; viii. 1, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120631">↑</a> Rv. i. 63, 3; 121, 19; 174, 5; 175, 4; iv. 30, 4; v. 29, 4; vi. 20, 5; vii. 19, 2; x. 99, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120631">↑</a> Rv. x. 49, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120631">↑</a> Rv. i. 53, 10; ii. 14, 7; viii. 53, 2. <i>Cf.</i> iv. 26, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120631">↑</a> Rv. i. 53, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120631">↑</a> Rv. i. 51, 6; vi. 26, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120631">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> Av. iv. 29, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 11, 26.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120631">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 2, 22; Śāṭyāyanaka in Sāyaṇa on Rv. x. 38, 5; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 228; Oertel. <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 31.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120631">↑</a> x. 38, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120631">↑</a> vii. 25, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 113, 148; Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 210, 211; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 284 <i>et seq.,</i> who suggests that perhaps two Kutsas--one a friend of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, and the other a foe --may be distinguished; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 171; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 57, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

कुत्स औरव|కుత్స ఔరవ|ಕುತ್ಸ ಔರವ|kutsa aurava|_vei_120@632|_vei1_pn162
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@631">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@633">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn161">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn162">1:162</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn163">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kutsa Aurava</b> (‘son of Uru’) is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120632"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120632">1</a></sup> as having murdered his domestic priest (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a></i>), <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपगु सौश्रवस">upagu sauśravasa</a>,</b> because the father of the latter insisted on paying homage to <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>. This fact may be compared with the hostility to <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुत्स">kutsa</a></b> according to certain passages of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120632"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120632">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120632"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120632">↑</a> xiv. 6, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120632"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120632">↑</a> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुत्स">kutsa</a>.</b> <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 284; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 57; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 32.</li></ol></div>

कुन्ति|కున్తి|ಕುನ್ತಿ|kunti|_vei_120@633|_vei1_pn162
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@632">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@634">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn161">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn162">1:162</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn163">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kunti.</b>--The Kuntis are referred to in an obscure and corrupt passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120633"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120633">1</a></sup> as having defeated the <b>Pañcālas.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120633"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120633">↑</a> xxvi. 9. See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 471, and <i>cf.</i> perhaps Maitrāyaṇī aṃhitā, iv. 2, 6.</li></ol></div>

कुबेर वारक्य|కుబేర వారక్య|ಕುಬೇರ ವಾರಕ್ಯ|kubera vārakya|kubera vaarakya|_vei_120@634|_vei1_pn162
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@633">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@635">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn161">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn162">1:162</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn163">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kubera Vārakya</b> is mentioned in a list of teachers in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 41, 1) as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जयन्त">jayanta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वारक्य">vārakya</a>.</b></p></div>

कुभा|కుభా|ಕುಭಾ|kubhā|kubhaa|_vei_120@635|_vei1_pn162
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@634">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@636">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn161">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn162">1:162</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn163">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kubhā</b> is the name of a river mentioned twice in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120635"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120635">1</a></sup> and no doubt identical with the modern Kabul river, the Greek .</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120635"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120635">↑</a> v. 53, 9; x. 75. 6. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 14; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 200.</li></ol></div>

कुभ्र|కుభ్ర|ಕುಭ್ರ|kubhra|_vei_120@636|_vei1_pn162
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@635">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@637">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn161">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn162">1:162</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn163">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kubhra</b> is the name of some animal in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā (ii. 5, 3). </p></div>

कु-मुद|कुमुद|मुद|కు-ముద|కుముద|ముద|ಕು-ಮುದ|ಕುಮುದ|ಮುದ|ku-muda|kumuda|muda|_vei_120@637|_vei1_pn163
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@636">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@638">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn162">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn163">1:163</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn164">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ku-muda</b> is the name of a plant mentioned with other water plants in one passage of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120637">1</a></sup> It is no doubt the white water-lily (<i>Nymphœa esculenta</i>), being the name of that plant in post-Vedic Sanskrit also.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120637">↑</a> iv. 34. 5. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 70.</li></ol></div>

कुम्ब|कुंब|కుమ్బ|కుంబ|ಕುಮ್ಬ|ಕುಂಬ|kumba|_vei_120@638|_vei1_pn163
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@637">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@639">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn162">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn163">1:163</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn164">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kumba</b> is mentioned with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ओपश">opaśa</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरीर">kurīra</a></b> as an ornament of women's hair in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120638"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120638">1</a></sup> Geldner<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120638"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120638">2</a></sup> thinks that, like those two words, it originally meant ‘horn,’ but this is very doubtful. Indian tradition<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120638"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120638">3</a></sup> simply regards the term as denoting a female adornment connected with the dressing of the hair.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120638"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120638">↑</a> vi. 138, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120638"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120638">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 131.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120638"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120638">↑</a> Sāyaṇa on Av. vi. 138, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 265; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 538, 539; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 348; Caland, <i>Über das vituclle <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> des <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>,</i> 59.</li></ol></div>

कुम्ब्या|कुम्व्या|कुंब्या|కుమ్బ్యా|కుమ్వ్యా|కుంబ్యా|ಕುಮ್ಬ್ಯಾ|ಕುಮ್ವ್ಯಾ|ಕುಂಬ್ಯಾ|kumbyā|kumvyā|kumbyaa|kumvyaa|_vei_120@639|_vei1_pn163
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@638">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@640">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn162">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn163">1:163</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn164">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kumbyā</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुम्व्या">kumvyā</a></b> is a word mentioned after Ṛc, <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुस्">yajus</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120639"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120639">1</a></sup> to denote a form of speech. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120639"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120639">2</a></sup> it appears as one of the forms of measured speech together with Ṛc and <a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a>. The precise meaning of the term is unknown. Weber<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120639"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120639">3</a></sup> suggests the sense ‘refrain.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120639"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120639">↑</a> xi. 5, 7, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120639"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120639">↑</a> ii. 3, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120639"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120639">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 111, n.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 221; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44. 101.</li></ol></div>

कुम्भ|कुंभ|కుమ్భ|కుంభ|ಕುಮ್ಭ|ಕುಂಭ|kumbha|_vei_120@640|_vei1_pn163
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@639">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@641">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn162">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn163">1:163</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn164">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kumbha</b> is a word of frequent occurrence in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120640"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120640">1</a></sup> as well as later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120640"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120640">2</a></sup> and denotes a ‘pot.’ Usually no doubt made of clay, it was easily broken.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120640"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120640">3</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उखा">ukhā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120640"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120640">↑</a> i. 116, 7; 117, 6; vii. 33, 13, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120640"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120640">↑</a> Av. i. 6, 4; iii. 12, 7, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 87, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120640"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120640">↑</a> Rv. x. 89, 7.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 367.</li></ol></div>

कुम्भी-नस|कुम्भीनस|कुम्भी|कुम्भि|नस|कुंभी-नस|कुंभीनस|कुंभी|कुंभि|కుమ్భీ-నస|కుమ్భీనస|కుమ్భీ|కుమ్భి|నస|కుంభీ-నస|కుంభీనస|కుంభీ|కుంభి|ಕುಮ್ಭೀ-ನಸ|ಕುಮ್ಭೀನಸ|ಕುಮ್ಭೀ|ಕುಮ್ಭಿ|ನಸ|ಕುಂಭೀ-ನಸ|ಕುಂಭೀನಸ|ಕುಂಭೀ|ಕುಂಭಿ|kumbhī-nasa|kumbhīnasa|kumbhī|kumbhi|nasa|kumbhii-nasa|kumbhiinasa|kumbhii|kumbhee-nasa|kumbheenasa|kumbhee|kumbhii|_vei_120@641|_vei1_pn163
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@640">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@642">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn162">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn163">1:163</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn164">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kumbhī-nasa</b> (‘pot-nosed’) is the name of an animal mentioned in the list of victims at the horse sacrifice (<a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120641"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120641">1</a></sup> Possibly some sort of snake is meant, as in the later literature. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120641"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120641">↑</a> v. 5, 14, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 95; St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s. v.</i></li></ol></div>

कुय-वाच्|कुयवाच्|कुय|वाच्|కుయ-వాచ్|కుయవాచ్|కుయ|వాచ్|ಕುಯ-ವಾಚ್|ಕುಯವಾಚ್|ಕುಯ|ವಾಚ್|kuya-vāc|kuyavāc|kuya|vāc|kuya-vaach|kuyavaach|vaach|_vei_120@642|_vei1_pn164
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@641">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@643">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn163">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn164">1:164</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn165">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kuya-vāc</b> (‘evil-speaking’) appears in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120642"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120642">1</a></sup> to denote a demon slain by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, probably as a personification of the barbarian opponents of the Āryans. The expression <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मृध्र-वाच्">mṛdhra-vāc</a></i> (‘speaking insultingly’) is similarly used of barbarians in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120642"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120642">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120642"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120642">↑</a> i. 174, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120642"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120642">↑</a> v. 29, 10; 32, 8. See <a class="vei_120a" href="दस्यु">dasyu</a>.</li></ol></div>

कुरीर|కురీర|ಕುರೀರ|kurīra|kuriira|kureera|_vei_120@643|_vei1_pn164
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@642">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@644">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn163">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn164">1:164</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn165">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kurīra,</b> like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ओपश">opaśa</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुम्ब">kumba</a>,</b> denotes some sort of female head ornament in the description of the bride's adornment in the wedding hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120643">1</a></sup> and in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120643">2</a></sup> According to the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120643">3</a></sup> the goddess <a class="vei_120a" href="सिनीवाली">sinīvālī</a> is described by the epithets <i>su-kapardā, <a class="vei_120a" href="सु-कुरीर">su-kurīra</a>, sv-opaśā,</i> as wearing a beautiful head-dress.</p><p>According to Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120643">4</a></sup> the word originally meant ‘horn’; but this is uncertain, as this sense is not required in any passage in which the term occurs.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120643">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120643">↑</a> x. 85. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120643">↑</a> vi. 138, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120643">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 1, 5, 3: Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 7. 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xi. 56.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120643">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 131, 132.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120643">↑</a> Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 21 (= vaitāna <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xi. 22), cited by Geldner, is quite vague.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 265; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharanaveda,</i> 539; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 348; Caland, <i>Über das vituelle <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> des <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>,</i> 59.</li></ol></div>

कुरीरिन्|కురీరిన్|ಕುರೀರಿನ್|kurīrin|kuriirin|kureerin|_vei_120@644|_vei1_pn164
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@643">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@645">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn163">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn164">1:164</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn165">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kurīrin</b> (‘having a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरीर">kurīra</a></b>’) is a word occurring in an ambiguous passage of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120644">1</a></sup> in which it may be taken either as a noun meaning a ‘crested animal,’ perhaps as Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120644">2</a></sup> suggests the ‘peacock,’ or as an epithet of the word <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अज">aja</a>,</b> ‘goat,’ in which case it might mean ‘horned.’ But even in the latter alternative a metaphorical application of the word seems sufficient, just as in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120644">3</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ओपश">opaśa</a></b> is used of the horns of cattle, and thus renders unnecessary the adoption of Geldner's<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120644">4</a></sup> view that the original meaning of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरीर">kurīra</a></b> is ‘horn.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120644">↑</a> v. 31, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120644">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 91.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120644">↑</a> xiii. 4, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120644">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 130.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 457, 539; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 285; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 279.</li></ol></div>

कुरु|కురు|ಕುರು|kuru|_vei_120@645|_vei1_pn165
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@644">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@646">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn164">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn165">1:165</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn169">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kuru.</b>--The Kurus appear as by far the most important people in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> literature. There is clear evidence that it was in the country of the Kurus, or the allied KuruPañcālas, that the great Brāhmaṇas were composed.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120645">1</a></sup> The Kurus are comparatively seldom mentioned alone, their name being usually coupled with that of the Pañcālas on account of the intimate connexion of the two peoples. The <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas are often expressly referred to as a united nation.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120645">2</a></sup> In the land of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas speech is said to have its particular home;<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120645">3</a></sup> the mode of sacrifice among the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas is proclaimed to be the best;<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120645">4</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a> kings perform the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a> or royal sacrifice;<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120645">5</a></sup> their princes march forth on raids in the dewy season, and return in the hot season.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120645">6</a></sup> Later on the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a> Brahmins are famous in the Upaniṣads.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120645">7</a></sup> Weber<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120645">8</a></sup> and Grierson<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120645">9</a></sup> have sought to find traces in Vedic literature of a breach between the two tribes, the latter scholar seeing therein a confirmation of the theory that the Kurus belonged to the later stream of immigrants into India, who were specially Brahminical, as opposed to the Pañcālas, who were anti-Brahminical. In support of this view, Weber refers to the story in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120645">10</a></sup> of a dispute between <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वक दाल्भ्य">vaka dālbhya</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धृतराष्ट्र वैचित्रवीर्य">dhṛtarāṣṭra vaicitravīrya</a>,</b> the former being held to be by origin a <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a>, while the latter is held  to be a <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>. But there is no trace of a quarrel between Kurus and Pañcālas in the passage in question, which merely preserves the record of a dispute on a ritual matter between a priest and a prince: the same passage refers to the Naimiṣīya sacrifice among the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas, and emphasizes the close connexion of the two peoples.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120645">11</a></sup> Secondly, Weber conjectures in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120645">12</a></sup> that <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुभद्रिका">subhadrikā</a></b> of <b>Kāmpila</b> was the chief queen of the king of a tribe living in the neighbourhood of the clan, for whose king the horse sacrifice described in the Saṃhitā was performed. But the interpretation of this passage by Weber is open to grave doubt;<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120645">13</a></sup> and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension of the Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120645">14</a></sup> a passage used at the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a> shows that the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas had actually one king. Moreover, there is the evidence of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_15_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_120645">15</a></sup> that the old name of the Pañcālas was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रिवि">krivi</a>.</b> This word looks very like a variant of <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>, and Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_120645">16</a></sup> plausibly conjectures that the Kurus and Krivis formed the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैकर्ण">vaikarṇa</a></b><sup id="ref_120_17_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_120645">17</a></sup> of the Rigveda, especially as both peoples are found about the <a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धु">sindhu</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="असिक्नी">asiknī</a>.<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_120645">18</a></sup></p><p>The Kurus alone are chiefly mentioned in connexion with the locality which they occupied, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>,</b> We are told, however, of a domestic priest (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a></b>) in the service of both the Kurus and the <b>Sṛñjayas,</b><sup id="ref_120_19_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_120645">19</a></sup> who must therefore at one time have been closely connected.<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_120645">20</a></sup> In the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> reference is made to the Kurus being saved by a mare (<i>aśvā</i>),<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_120645">21</a></sup> and to some disaster which befel them owing to a hailstorm.<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_120645">22</a></sup> In the Sūtras, again, a ceremony (<a class="vei_120a" href="वाजपेय">vājapeya</a>) of the Kurus is mentioned.<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_120645">23</a></sup> There also a curse, which was pronounced on them and led to their being driven from <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>, is alluded to.<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_120645">24</a></sup>  This possibly adumbrates the misfortunes of the Kauravas in the epic tradition.</p><p>In the Rigveda the Kurus do not appear under that name as a people. But mention is made of a prince, <b>Kuruśravaṇa</b> (‘Glory of the Kurus’),<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_120645">25</a></sup> and of a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पाकस्थामन् कौरयाण">pākasthāman kaurayāṇa</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_26_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_120645">26</a></sup> In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_120645">27</a></sup> there occurs as a king of the Kurus <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परिक्षित्">parikṣit</a>,</b> whose son, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनमेजय">janamejaya</a>,</b> is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_28_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_120645">28</a></sup> as one of the great performers of the horse sacrifice.</p><p>It is a probable conjecture of Oldenberg's<sup id="ref_120_29_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29_vei_120645">29</a></sup> that the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a> people, as known later, included some of the tribes referred to by other names in the Rigveda. Kuruśravaṇa, shown by his name to be connected with the Kurus, is in the Rigveda called <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रासदस्यव">trāsadasyava</a>, ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a>,</b>’ who is well known as a king of the <b>Pūrus.</b> Moreover, it is likely that the <a class="vei_120a" href="तृत्सु">tṛtsu</a><b>Bharatas,</b> who appear in the Rigveda as enemies of the Pūtus, later coalesced with them to form the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a> people.<sup id="ref_120_30_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_30_vei_120645">30</a></sup> Since the Bharatas appear so prominently in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> texts as a great people of the past, while the later literature ignores them in its list of nations, it is difficult to avoid the conclusion that they became merged in some other tribe. Moreover, there is evidence that the Bharatas occupied the territory in which the Kurus were later found. Two of them are spoken of in a hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_31_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_31_vei_120645">31</a></sup> as having kindled fire on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृषद्वती">dṛṣadvatī</a>,</b> the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आपया">āpayā</a>,</b> and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a></b>--that is to say, in the sacred places of the later <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>. Similarly, the goddess Bhāratī (‘belonging to the Bharatas’) is constantly mentioned in the Āprī (‘propitiatory’) hymns together with <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>.<sup id="ref_120_32_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_32_vei_120645">32</a></sup> Again, according to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, one <a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a> king was victorious over the Kāśis,<sup id="ref_120_33_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_33_vei_120645">33</a></sup> and another made offerings to <a class="vei_120a" href="गंगा">gaṅgā</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a>,<sup id="ref_120_34_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_34_vei_120645">34</a></sup> while raids of the Bharatas against the <b>Satvants</b> are mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_35_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_35_vei_120645">35</a></sup> Nor is it  without importance that the Bharatas appear as a variant for the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a> Pañcālas in a passage of the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_36_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_36_vei_120645">36</a></sup> and that in the list of the great performers of the horse sacrifice the names of one <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a> and two <a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a> princes are given without any mention of the people over which they ruled, while in other cases that information is specifically given.<sup id="ref_120_37_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_37_vei_120645">37</a></sup></p><p>The territory of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas is declared in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> to be the middle country (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_38_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_38_vei_120645">38</a></sup> A group of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a> people still remained further north--the <b>Uttara Kurus</b> beyond the Himālaya. It appears from a passage of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> that the speech of the Northerners-that is, presumably, the Northern Kurus--and of the KuruPañcālas was similar, and regarded as specially pure.<sup id="ref_120_39_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_39_vei_120645">39</a></sup> There seems little doubt that the Brahminical culture was developed in the country of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas, and that it spread thence east, south, and west. Traces of this are seen in the <a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात्य">vrātya</a> Stomas (sacrifices for the admission of non-Brahminical Āryans) of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_40_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_40_vei_120645">40</a></sup> and in the fact that in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka it is unusual for a Brahmin to dwell in the territory of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मगध">magadha</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_41_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_41_vei_120645">41</a></sup> The repeated mention of KuruPañcāla Brahmins is another indication of their missionary activity.<sup id="ref_120_42_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_42_vei_120645">42</a></sup></p><p>The geographical position of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas renders it probable that they were later immigrants into India than the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a></b> or the <b>Kāśis,</b><sup id="ref_120_43_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_43_vei_120645">43</a></sup> who must have been pushed  into their more eastward territories by a new wave of Āryan settlers from the west. But there is no evidence in Vedic literature to show in what relation of time the immigration of the latter peoples stood to that of their neighbours on the west. It has, however, been conjectured,<sup id="ref_120_44_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_44_vei_120645">44</a></sup> mainly on the ground of later linguistic phenomena, which have no cogency for the Vedic period, that the Kurus were later immigrants, who, coming by a new route, thrust themselves between the original Āryan tribes which were already in occupation of the country from east to west. <i>Cf.</i> also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्वन्">kṛtvan</a>.</b> For other <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a> princes see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौरव्य">kauravya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120645">↑</a> For the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 49, 50, with Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 67, 68; for the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, Weber, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 45; for the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> and Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyakas, Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 387; for the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, Weber, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 132, <i>Transactions of the Bcrlin Academy,</i> 1895, 859. The Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> refers repeatedly to the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas, whose name also occurs in the late and confused Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>. For the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, see i. 8, 4, 1. 2, and for the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120645">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 7, 6; 8, 7; iv. 7, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1; Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, 1. 2, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, x. 6; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xi. 3, 3 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120645">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 3, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120645">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> i. 7, 2, 8; <i>cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वाजपेय">vājapeya</a></i> in Sāṅkhāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 3, 15; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 11, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120645">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 2, 3. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120645">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, 1. 8, 4. 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120645">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 78; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 30, 6; iv. 6, 2; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 1, 1; 9, 20, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120645">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 470; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 114.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120645">↑</a> <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 602-607; 837-844.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120645">↑</a> x. 6. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, xli.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120645">↑</a> See Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 831-836; 11381142.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120645">↑</a> xxiii. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120645">↑</a> Eggeling. <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 322.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120645">↑</a> xi. 3, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 114, note *.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_120645">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_120645">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 103.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_120645">↑</a> vii. 18, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_120645">↑</a> Keith, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 835.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_120645">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 4, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_120645">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 123.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_120645">↑</a> iv. 17, 9: for <i>aśvā</i> Bo7htlingk in his edition reads <i>akṣṇā,</i> followed by Little, <i>Grammatical Index,</i> 1.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_120645">↑</a> i. 10, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_120645">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 3, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_120645">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> xv. 16, 11. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literathre,</i> 136.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_120645">↑</a> Rv. x. 33, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_120645">↑</a> Rv. viii. 3, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_120645">↑</a> xx. 127, 7 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="खिल">khila</a>, v. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_120645">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_29_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29_vei_120645">↑</a> <i>Buddha,</i> 403, 404.</li><li id="fx_120_30_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_30_vei_120645">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 406-409.</li><li id="fx_120_31_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_31_vei_120645">↑</a> iii. 23.</li><li id="fx_120_32_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_32_vei_120645">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Scheftelowitz, <i>Die Apokṛyphen des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 145.</li><li id="fx_120_33_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_33_vei_120645">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_34_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_34_vei_120645">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 21.</li><li id="fx_120_35_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_35_vei_120645">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 25 (<i>cf.</i> Haug's edition, 2, 128, n. 3); Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 407, note*.</li><li id="fx_120_36_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_36_vei_120645">↑</a> xi. 3, 3. See note 14; Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 408, 409.</li><li id="fx_120_37_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_37_vei_120645">↑</a> Oldenberg, 409, note*.</li><li id="fx_120_38_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_38_vei_120645">↑</a> viii. 14. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, 392, 393.</li><li id="fx_120_39_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_39_vei_120645">↑</a> iii. 2, 3, 15. This is the sense which it appears to bear, as the KuruPañcālas can bardly be reckoned as being northerly (Oldenberg, 395), and the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 6 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 2, 309) in independent evidence for the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरो">puro</a> speech of the north. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeeling Sacred <i>Books of the East,</i> 12. xiii. Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 45; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 191.</li><li id="fx_120_40_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_40_vei_120645">↑</a> xvii. 1, 1. See also Av. xv. with Whitney's and Lanman notes; Weber, <i>Ind the Studien,</i> 1, 33 <i>et seq.; Indian Literature,</i> 67, 78, 80.</li><li id="fx_120_41_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_41_vei_120645">↑</a> vii. 13. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 400, note*; Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 112, n. 126.</li><li id="fx_120_42_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_42_vei_120645">↑</a> See <i>e.g.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 4, 1, 2, and note 6.</li><li id="fx_120_43_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_43_vei_120645">↑</a> This is recognized, <i>e.g.,</i> by Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 9, 391, 398, 399; Lanman, <i>Sanskrit Keader,</i> 297, etc. The narrative of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 1, 10 <i>et seq.</i> (Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 170), rather implies that the KosalaVidehas are offshoots of the KuruPañcālas, but Oldenberg and Macdonell (<i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 214 interpret this as referring to the spread of Vedic tradition and culture, not of nationality.</li><li id="fx_120_44_vei_120645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_44_vei_120645">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Grierson, <i>Languages of India,</i> 52 <i>et seq.; Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 837 <i>et seq.</i> On the other hand, it is probably an error to assume that the Bharatas were originally situated far west of <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>, and that the main action of the Rigveda was confined to the Panjab. When <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> celebrates the crossing of the <a class="vei_120a" href="विपाश्">vipāś</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="शुतुद्री">śutudrī</a> (Rv. iii. 33), he probably came from the east, as Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 218, points out, and not from the west. Adopting the ordinary view, Hopkins, <i>India, Old and New,</i> 52, finds it necessary to suggest that <a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a> is only another name in the Rv. for the <a class="vei_120a" href="परुष्णी">paruṣṇī</a>. But the necessity for this suggestion, which is not in itself plausible, disappears when it is realized that the Bharatas held a territory roughly corresponding to <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>, and bounded on the east by the <a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a>. On the other hand, Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 142, 143. places the Kurus near the Ārjīkīyā in Kaśmīr, which puts them too far north. So also Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 103, and Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, xlii. It seems probable that the Kurus were at a very early period widely scattered to the north of the Himālaya, in <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>, and about the <a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धु">sindhu</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="असिक्नी">asiknī</a>.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 400 <i>et seq.;</i> Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 152-157; von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literature und Cultur,</i> 164 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 187 <i>et seq.; Indian Literature,</i> 114, 135, 136; Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India,</i> 27; Pargiter, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 333 <i>et seq.;</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 205, n.</li></ol></div>

कुरु-क्षेत्र|कुरुक्षेत्र|कुरु|क्षेत्र|కురు-క్షేత్ర|కురుక్షేత్ర|కురు|క్షేత్ర|ಕುರು-ಕ್ಷೇತ್ರ|ಕುರುಕ್ಷೇತ್ರ|ಕುರು|ಕ್ಷೇತ್ರ|kuru-kṣetra|kurukṣetra|kuru|kṣetra|kuru-kShetra|kurukShetra|kShetra|kuru-xetra|kuruxetra|xetra|_vei_120@646|_vei1_pn169
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@645">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@647">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn165">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn169">1:169</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn170">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kuru-kṣetra</b> (‘land of the Kurus’) is always regarded in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> texts<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120646"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120646">1</a></sup> as a particularly sacred country. Within its boundaries flowed the rivers <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृषद्वती">dṛṣadvatī</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>,</b> as well as the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आपया">āpayā</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120646"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120646">2</a></sup> Here, too, was situated <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शर्यणावन्त्">śaryaṇāvant</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120646"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120646">3</a></sup> which  appears to have been a lake, like that known to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> by the name of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अन्यतः-प्लक्षा">anyataḥ-plakṣā</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120646"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120646">4</a></sup> According to Pischel, there was also in <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a> a stream called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पस्त्या">pastyā</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120646"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120646">5</a></sup> which he sees in certain passages of the Rigveda. The boundaries of <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a> are given in a passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120646"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120646">6</a></sup> as being <a class="vei_120a" href="खाण्डव">khāṇḍava</a> on the south, the <a class="vei_120a" href="तूर्घ्न">tūrghna</a> on the north, and the <a class="vei_120a" href="परीणह्">parīṇah</a> on the west. Roughly speaking, it corresponded to the modern Sirhind.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120646"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120646">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 10; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 5, 13; xi. 5, 1, 4; xiv. 1, 1, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 30; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 4; iv. 5, 9; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 126 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 11, cxlvi); <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 16, 11, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120646"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120646">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. iii. 23; Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 218.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120646"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120646">↑</a> See Pischel, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> and <i>cf.</i> <b>Ārjīkīyā.</b></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120646"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120646">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 1, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120646"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120646">↑</a> Pischel, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 219.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120646"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120646">↑</a> v. 1, 1. These places cannot be further identified. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मर">mara</a>.</b></p><p><i>Cf.</i> von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 164, 165; Max Mūller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 398, 399; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 78, 79; Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 174. Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, xli., seems to place <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>, as lying between the <a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="गंगा">gaṅgā</a>, too far east.</li></ol></div>

कुरुंग|कुरुङ्ग|కురుంగ|కురుఙ్గ|ಕುರುಂಗ|ಕುರುಙ್ಗ|kuruṅga|kuruMga|kurunga|_vei_120@647|_vei1_pn170
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@646">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@648">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn169">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn170">1:170</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn171">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kuruṅga</b> is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120647">1</a></sup> as a prince and a patron. Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120647">2</a></sup> suggests that he was a king of the <b>Anus,</b> but for this theory there seems no good ground. As the <b>Turvaśas</b> are mentioned in the same verse, he may possibly have been one of their kings. The name suggests a connexion with the <b>Kurus,</b> and it may be noted that in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120647">3</a></sup> the Turvaśas are connected with the <b>Pañcālas (Krivis).</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120647">↑</a> viii. 4, 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 22.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120647">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 160.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120647">↑</a> xi. 5, 4, 16. See Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 404.</li></ol></div>

कुरु|కురు|ಕುರು|kuru|_vei_120@648|_vei1_pn170
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@647">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@649">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn169">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn170">1:170</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn171">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kuru</b>-<a class="vei_120a" href="श्रवण">śravaṇa</a> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रासदस्यव">trāsadasyava</a></b> is alluded to as dead in a hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120648"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120648">1</a></sup> which refers also to his son <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपमश्रवस्">upamaśravas</a>,</b> and his father <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मित्रातिथि">mitrātithi</a>.</b> In another hymn<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120648"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120648">2</a></sup> he is mentioned as still alive. His name connects him on the one hand with the <b>Kurus,</b> and on the other with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a></b> and the <b>Pūrus.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120648"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120648">↑</a> x. 33, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Bṛhaddevatā, vii. 35, 36.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120648"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120648">↑</a> x. 32, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 165: Geldner, <i>Vediscke Studien,</i> 2, 150, 184; Lanman, <i>Sanskrit Reader,</i> 386.</li></ol></div>

कुरूरु|కురూరు|ಕುರೂರು|kurūru|kuruuru|kurooru|_vei_120@649|_vei1_pn170
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@648">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@650">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn169">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn170">1:170</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn171">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kurūru,</b> apparently the name of a species of worm, is mentioned twice in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120649"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120649">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120649"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120649">↑</a> ii. 31, 2; ix. 2, 22. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 98.</li></ol></div>

कुर्कुर|కుర్కుర|ಕುರ್ಕುರ|kurkura|_vei_120@650|_vei1_pn171
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@649">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@651">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn170">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn171">1:171</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn172">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kurkura</b> is an onomatopoetic name for the dog in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120650"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120650">1</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्वन्">śvan</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120650"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120650">↑</a> vii. 95, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 233.</li></ol></div>

कुल|कुल-पा|कुलपा|కుల|కుల-పా|కులపా|ಕುಲ|ಕುಲ-ಪಾ|ಕುಲಪಾ|kula|kula-pā|kulapā|kula-paa|kulapaa|_vei_120@651|_vei1_pn171
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@650">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@652">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn170">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn171">1:171</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn172">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kula, <a class="vei_120a" href="कुल">kula</a>-pā.</b>--As an uncompounded word, <a class="vei_120a" href="कुल">kula</a> does not occur before the period of the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120651"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120651">1</a></sup> It denotes the ‘home’ or ‘house of the family,’ and by metonymy the family itself, as connected with the home. The <a class="vei_120a" href="कुल-पा">kula-pā</a> (lit. ‘house protector’), or chief of the family, is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120651"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120651">2</a></sup> as inferior to and attendant on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्राजपति">vrājapati</a></b> in war, the latter being perhaps the leader of the village contingent of the clan. In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120651"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120651">3</a></sup> a girl is ironically called <a class="vei_120a" href="कुलपा">kulapā</a>, because she is left without a husband in the world, and has only <a class="vei_120a" href="यम">yama</a> (the god of death) for a spouse.</p><p>The use of the term <a class="vei_120a" href="कुल">kula</a> points clearly to a system of individual families, each no doubt consisting of several members under the headship of the father or eldest brother, whose <a class="vei_120a" href="कुल">kula</a> the dwelling is. As distinct from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a>,</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="कुल">kula</a> seems to mean the family in the narrower sense of the members who still live in one house, the undivided family. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गृह">gṛha</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120651"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120651">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 2, 22; ii. 1, 4, 4; 4, 1, 14; xi. 5, 3, 11; 8, 1, 3; xiii. 4, 2, 17; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 5, 32; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii., 13, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120651"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120651">↑</a> x. 179, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120651"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120651">↑</a> i. 14, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 15; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Athcrvaveda,</i> 252, correcting Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 314.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 162.</li></ol></div>

कुलाल|కులాల|ಕುಲಾಲ|kulāla|kulaala|_vei_120@652|_vei1_pn171
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@651">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@653">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn170">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn171">1:171</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn172">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kulāla,</b> the word denoting a ‘potter,’ occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शतरुद्रिय">śatarudriya</a>, or litany to <a class="vei_120a" href="रुद्र">rudra</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120652"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120652">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120652"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120652">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 27. <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="कुलाल">kulāla</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कृत">kṛta</a>,</i> ‘made by a potter,’ Maitrāyaṇi Saṃhitā, i. 8. 3, and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौलाल">kaulāla</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

कुलिश|కులిశ|ಕುಲಿಶ|kuliśa|kulisha|_vei_120@653|_vei1_pn171
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@652">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@654">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn170">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn171">1:171</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn172">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kuliśa,</b> ‘axe,’ is mentioned in the Rigveda as used for the making of chariots,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120653">1</a></sup> and also in warfare,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120653">2</a></sup> while the Atharvaveda refers to its employment in cutting down trees.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120653">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120653">↑</a> iii. 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120653">↑</a> i. 32. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120653">↑</a> ii. 12, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 252.</li></ol></div>

कुलीकय|కులీకయ|ಕುಲೀಕಯ|kulīkaya|kuliikaya|kuleekaya|_vei_120@654|_vei1_pn172
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@653">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@655">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn171">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn172">1:172</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn173">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kulīkaya</b> is the form in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120654"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120654">1</a></sup> of the name of an animal, apparently a kind of fish, as explained by Mahīdhara in his commentary, which is called Kulīpaya in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120654"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120654">2</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरीकय">purīkaya</a> in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120654"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120654">3</a></sup> variants probably due to the faulty tradition of an unfamiliar. name.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120654"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120654">↑</a> v. 5, 13, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120654"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120654">↑</a> xxiv. 21. 35.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120654"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120654">↑</a> xi. 2, 25. The commentator reads <a class="vei_120a" href="पुलीकय">pulīkaya</a>, as in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 2. See Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 624.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 96.</li></ol></div>

कुलीका|కులీకా|ಕುಲೀಕಾ|kulīkā|kuliikaa|kuleekaa|_vei_120@655|_vei1_pn172
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@654">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@656">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn171">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn172">1:172</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn173">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kulīkā</b> is the name of a bird mentioned in the list of victims at the horse sacrifice in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120655"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120655">1</a></sup> The Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120655"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120655">2</a></sup> has <a class="vei_120a" href="पुलीका">pulīkā</a> instead.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120655"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120655">↑</a> xxiv. 24.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120655"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120655">↑</a> iii. 14, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 94.</li></ol></div>

कुलुंग|कुलुङ्ग|కులుంగ|కులుఙ్గ|ಕುಲುಂಗ|ಕುಲುಙ್ಗ|kuluṅga|kuluMga|kulunga|_vei_120@656|_vei1_pn172
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@655">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@657">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn171">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn172">1:172</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn173">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kuluṅga</b> is the name of an animal, perhaps a gazelle, mentioned in the list of victims at the horse sacrifice in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120656">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120656">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 11, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 9, 13 (with the variant Kulaṅga); Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 27. 32.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 83.</li></ol></div>

कुल्मल|కుల్మల|ಕುಲ್ಮಲ|kulmala|_vei_120@657|_vei1_pn172
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@656">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@658">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn171">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn172">1:172</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn173">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kulmala</b> seems, in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120657"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120657">1</a></sup> the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120657"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120657">2</a></sup> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120657"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120657">3</a></sup> to denote the neck of an arrow in which the shaft is fixed.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120657"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120657">↑</a> iv. 6, 5; v. 18, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120657"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120657">↑</a> iii. 8, 1. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120657"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120657">↑</a> iii. 4, 4, 14.</li></ol></div>

कुमल-बर्हिस्|कुमल-बर्हि|कुमलबर्हिस्|कुमलबर्हि|कुमल|बर्हिस्|बर्हि|కుమల-బర్హిస్|కుమల-బర్హి|కుమలబర్హిస్|కుమలబర్హి|కుమల|బర్హిస్|బర్హి|ಕುಮಲ-ಬರ್ಹಿಸ್|ಕುಮಲ-ಬರ್ಹಿ|ಕುಮಲಬರ್ಹಿಸ್|ಕುಮಲಬರ್ಹಿ|ಕುಮಲ|ಬರ್ಹಿಸ್|ಬರ್ಹಿ|kumala-barhis|kumala-barhi|kumalabarhis|kumalabarhi|kumala|barhis|barhi|barhis|barhi|_vei_120@658|_vei1_pn172
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@657">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@659">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn171">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn172">1:172</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn173">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kumala-barhis</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xv. 3, 21) as the seer of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> or Chant.</p></div>

कुमार हारित|కుమార హారిత|ಕುಮಾರ ಹಾರಿತ|kumāra hārita|kumaara haarita|_vei_120@659|_vei1_pn172
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@658">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@660">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn171">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn172">1:172</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn173">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kumāra Hārita</b> is mentioned in the first <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120659">1</a></sup> as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गालव">gālava</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120659">↑</a> ii. 5, 22 (Mādhyaṃdina = ii. 6, 3, <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

कुल्माष|కుల్మాష|ಕುಲ್ಮಾಷ|kulmāṣa|kulmaaSha|_vei_120@660|_vei1_pn172
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@659">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@661">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn171">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn172">1:172</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn173">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kulmāṣa,</b> a word mentioned by the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120660">1</a></sup> in the plural, is interpreted by the commentator as ‘bad beans’ (<i>kutsitā māṣāḥ</i>), a version adopted by Bo7htlingk in his  Dictionary.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120660">2</a></sup> Little<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120660">3</a></sup> renders it ‘sour gruel’ in accordance with the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120660">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120660">↑</a> i. 10, 2. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120660">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Bhāgavata <a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a>, v. 9. 12. where it is glossed ‘worm-eaten beans.’</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120660">↑</a> <i>Grammatical Index,</i> 52.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120660">↑</a> i. 4.</li></ol></div>

कुल्या|కుల్యా|ಕುಲ್ಯಾ|kulyā|kulyaa|_vei_120@661|_vei1_pn173
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@660">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@662">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn172">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn173">1:173</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn174">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kulyā</b> in two passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120661"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120661">1</a></sup> according to Muir,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120661"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120661">2</a></sup> possibly refers to artificial watercourses flowing into a reservoir (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ह्रद">hrada</a></i>). See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अवत">avata</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120661"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120661">↑</a> iii. 45, 3; x. 43, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120661"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120661">↑</a> <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 465, 466.</li></ol></div>

कुवय|కువయ|ಕುವಯ|kuvaya|_vei_120@662|_vei1_pn173
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@661">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@663">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn172">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn173">1:173</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn174">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kuvaya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्वयि">kvayi</a>.</b></p></div>

कुवल|కువల|ಕುವಲ|kuvala|_vei_120@663|_vei1_pn173
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@662">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@664">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn172">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn173">1:173</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn174">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kuvala</b> is a name of the jujube fruit (<i>Zizyphus jujuba</i>) occurring frequently in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120663">1</a></sup> and Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120663">2</a></sup> in connexion with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कर्कन्धु">karkandhu</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बदर">badara</a>.</b> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कोल">kola</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120663">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 11, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 22, 89; xxi. 29; Kāthaka Saṃhitā, xii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120663">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 4, 10; xii. 7, 1, 2; 2, 9; 9, 1, 5, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 242.</li></ol></div>

कुश|కుశ|ಕುಶ|kuśa|kusha|_vei_120@664|_vei1_pn173
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@663">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@665">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn172">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn173">1:173</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn174">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kuśa,</b> a word later denoting the ‘sacred grass’ (<i>Poa cynosuroides</i>), is taken by the St. Petersburg Dictionary to mean simply ‘grass’ in the passages of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> in which it occurs.</p><p>^1) ii. 5, 2, 15; iii. 1, 2, 16; v. 3, 2, 7, etc. Kuśā and Kuśī occur in Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 7; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 6, 2, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 10, 1. 2, 7, apparently denoting pins of wood or metal, used as a mark in a special mode of recitation.</p></div>

कु-सर|कुसर|सर|కు-సర|కుసర|సర|ಕು-ಸರ|ಕುಸರ|ಸರ|ku-sara|kusara|sara|_vei_120@665|_vei1_pn173
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@664">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@666">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn172">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn173">1:173</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn174">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ku-sara</b> is mentioned with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शर">śara</a></b> and other grasses in one hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120665">1</a></sup> as affording lurking places for serpents.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120665">↑</a> i. 191, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 72.</li></ol></div>

कुशिक|కుశిక|ಕುಶಿಕ|kuśika|kushika|_vei_120@666|_vei1_pn173
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@665">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@667">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn172">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn173">1:173</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn174">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kuśika</b> is the probably mythical forefather<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120666">1</a></sup> of the Kuśikas, and especially the father of the most important member of that family, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120666">2</a></sup> The Kuśikas are repeatedly referred  to in the third Maṇḍala of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120666">3</a></sup> and figure in the legend of <a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120666">4</a></sup> They were clearly a family of priests who attached themselves to the service of the princes of the <b>Bharatas.</b> They were especially devoted to the worship of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>; hence he is styled <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> even in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120666">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120666">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 25.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120666">↑</a> Rv. iii. 33, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120666">↑</a> iii. 26, 1; 29, 15; 30, 20; 33, 5; 42, 9; 50, 4; 53, 9. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120666">↑</a> vii. 18; Sāṅkhāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 27.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120666">↑</a> i. 10, 11, with Sāyaṇa's note. <i>Cf.</i> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 7; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 4, 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 12, 4; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> pp. 62, 63. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> i, 38; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 342 <i>et seq.;</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 101, 121; Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 155; Oldenberg. <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 209.</li></ol></div>

कुश्रि वाज-श्रवस|कुश्रि वाजश्रवस|कुश्रि वाज|श्रवस|కుశ్రి వాజ-శ్రవస|కుశ్రి వాజశ్రవస|కుశ్రి వాజ|శ్రవస|ಕುಶ್ರಿ ವಾಜ-ಶ್ರವಸ|ಕುಶ್ರಿ ವಾಜಶ್ರವಸ|ಕುಶ್ರಿ ವಾಜ|ಶ್ರವಸ|kuśri vāja-śravasa|kuśri vājaśravasa|kuśri vāja|śravasa|kushri vaaja-shravasa|kushri vaajashravasa|kushri vaaja|shravasa|_vei_120@667|_vei1_pn174
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@666">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@668">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn173">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn174">1:174</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn175">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kuśri <a class="vei_120a" href="वाज">vāja</a>-śravasa</b> appears as a teacher concerned with the lore of the sacred fire in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120667"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120667">1</a></sup> and in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120667"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120667">2</a></sup> he is mentioned as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वाजश्रवस्">vājaśravas</a>.</b> It is not clear whether he is identical with the Kuśri of the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120667"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120667">3</a></sup> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension, and of the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> in the tenth book of the Śatapatha,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120667"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120667">4</a></sup> who is mentioned as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यज्ञवचस् राजस्तम्बायन">yajñavacas rājastambāyana</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120667"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120667">↑</a> x. 5, 5. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120667"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120667">↑</a> vi. 4, 33 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 5, 3, <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120667"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120667">↑</a> vi. 5, 4 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> only).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120667"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120667">↑</a> x. 6, 5, 9. In the Vaṃśas the name is accented Kúśri, but in x. 5, 5, 1, as Kuśrī; no stress can, however, be laid on this. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 70; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, xxxiii.</li></ol></div>

कु-षण्ड|कुषण्ड|षण्ड|కు-షణ్డ|కుషణ్డ|షణ్డ|ಕು-ಷಣ್ಡ|ಕುಷಣ್ಡ|ಷಣ್ಡ|ku-ṣaṇḍa|kuṣaṇḍa|ṣaṇḍa|ku-ShaNDa|kuShaNDa|ShaNDa|_vei_120@668|_vei1_pn174
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@667">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@669">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn173">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn174">1:174</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn175">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ku-ṣaṇḍa</b> is mentioned with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="षण्ड">ṣaṇḍa</a></b> as a priest at the snake festival described in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120668"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120668">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120668"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120668">↑</a> xxv. 15, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 34; Lāṭyāyana Śranta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 20, 10.</li></ol></div>

कुषीतक|कुषीतक|కుషీతక_౧|కుషీతక|ಕುಷೀತಕ_೧|ಕುಷೀತಕ|kuṣītaka_1|kuṣītaka|kuShiitaka_1|kuShiitaka|kuSheetaka_1|kuSheetaka|_vei_120@669|_vei1_pn174
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@668">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@670">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn173">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn174">1:174</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn175">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Kuṣītaka</b> denotes, according to the commentary on the one passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120669">1</a></sup> in which it is found, the sea crow (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="समुद्र">samudra</a>-kāka</i>)</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120669">↑</a> v. 5, 13. 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 72.</li></ol></div>

कुषीतक साम-श्रवस|कुषीतक साम-श्रवस|कुषीतक सामश्रवस|कुषीतक साम|श्रवस|కుషీతక సామ-శ్రవస_౨|కుషీతక సామ-శ్రవస|కుషీతక సామశ్రవస|కుషీతక సామ|శ్రవస|ಕುಷೀತಕ ಸಾಮ-ಶ್ರವಸ_೨|ಕುಷೀತಕ ಸಾಮ-ಶ್ರವಸ|ಕುಷೀತಕ ಸಾಮಶ್ರವಸ|ಕುಷೀತಕ ಸಾಮ|ಶ್ರವಸ|kuṣītaka sāma-śravasa_2|kuṣītaka sāma-śravasa|kuṣītaka sāmaśravasa|kuṣītaka sāma|śravasa|kuShiitaka saama-shravasa_2|kuShiitaka saama-shravasa|kuShiitaka saamashravasa|kuShiitaka saama|shravasa|kuSheetaka saama-shravasa_2|kuSheetaka saama-shravasa|kuSheetaka saamashravasa|kuSheetaka saama|_vei_120@670|_vei1_pn174
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@669">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@671">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn173">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn174">1:174</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn175">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Kuṣītaka <a class="vei_120a" href="साम">sāma</a>-śravasa</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120670"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120670">1</a></sup> as the Gṛhapati, or householder at a sacrificial session, of the Kauṣītakis. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120670"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120670">↑</a> xvii. 4. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 34.</li></ol></div>

कुषुम्भक|कुषुंभक|కుషుమ్భక|కుషుంభక|ಕುಷುಮ್ಭಕ|ಕುಷುಂಭಕ|kuṣumbhaka|kuShumbhaka|_vei_120@671|_vei1_pn175
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@670">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@672">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn174">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn175">1:175</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn176">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kuṣumbhaka</b> seems in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120671"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120671">1</a></sup> to mean a poisonous insect, Kuṣumbha in th Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120671"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120671">2</a></sup> clearly having the sense of a poison bag. Sāyaṇa renders it as ‘ichneumon’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="नकुल">nakula</a></i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120671"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120671">↑</a> i. 191, 16. The sense of ‘poisonbag’ is possible in i. 191, 15, and is accepted there by Bo7htlingk in his Dictionary.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120671"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120671">↑</a> ii. 32, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 99; Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 1, 257.</li></ol></div>

कुष्ठ|कुष्ठ|కుష్ఠ_౧|కుష్ఠ|ಕುಷ್ಠ_೧|ಕುಷ್ಠ|kuṣṭha_1|kuṣṭha|kuShTha_1|kuShTha|_vei_120@672|_vei1_pn175
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@671">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@673">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn174">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn175">1:175</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn176">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Kuṣṭha</b> is the name of a plant (<i>Costus speciosus</i> or <i>arabicus</i>)<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120672">1</a></sup> which is prominent in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120672">2</a></sup> It grew especially on the mountains, along with the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>, on the high peaks of the Himālaya (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="हिमवन्त्">himavant</a></i>) where the eagles nest, and was thence brought east to men.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120672">3</a></sup> Like <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>, it is said to have grown in the third heaven under the famous <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वत्थ">aśvattha</a></b> tree, where the gods were wont to assemble, and thence it was brought in a golden ship.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120672">4</a></sup> As a remedy, it held the highest place among herbs, being called by the auspicious names <a class="vei_120a" href="नघ-मार">nagha-māra</a> and Naghā-riṣa, and styled the offspring of Jīvala and Jīvalā, the ‘lively’ ones.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120672">5</a></sup> It cured headache (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शीर्षामय">śīrṣāmaya</a></i>), diseases of the eyes, bodily affliction,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120672">6</a></sup> but especially fever--hence called ‘feverdestroyer’ (<i>takma-nāśana</i>)--and consumption (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="यक्ष्म">yakṣma</a></b>). From its general properties it was also named ‘all-healing’ (<i>viśvabheṣaja</i>).<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120672">7</a></sup> Its aromatic qualities were apparently known, as it is classed with ‘salve’ (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="आञ्जन">āñjana</a></b>) and ‘nard’ (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="नलद">nalada</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120672">8</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120672">↑</a> Or <i>Saussurea auriculata,</i> Hillebreandt. <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 65.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120672">↑</a> v. 4; vi. 102; xix. 139.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120672">↑</a> v. 4, 1. 2. 8; xix. 39, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120672">↑</a> v. 4, 3-6; vi. 75, 1. 2; xix. 39, 6-8.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120672">↑</a> v. 4, 1; xix. 39, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120672">↑</a> v. 4, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120672">↑</a> xix. 39, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120672">↑</a> vi. 102, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Grohmann, <i>Indische, Studien,</i> 9, 420 <i>et seq.;</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 63, 64; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 415, 680; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 227, 228.</li></ol></div>

कुष्ठ|कुष्ठ|కుష్ఠ_౨|కుష్ఠ|ಕುಷ್ಠ_೨|ಕುಷ್ಠ|kuṣṭha_2|kuṣṭha|kuShTha_2|kuShTha|_vei_120@673|_vei1_pn175
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@672">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@674">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn174">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn175">1:175</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn176">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Kuṣṭha.</b>--In one passage of the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120673">1</a></sup> there is a series of fractions, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कला">kalā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कुष्ठ">kuṣṭha</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शफ">śapha</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पद्">pad</a>,</b> which appear to denote one-sixteenth, one-twelfth, one-eighth, and one-fourth respectively. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120673">↑</a> iii. 7, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Bōhtlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

कुसीदिन्|కుసీదిన్|ಕುಸೀದಿನ್|kusīdin|kusiidin|kuseedin|_vei_120@674|_vei1_pn176
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@673">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@675">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn175">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn176">1:176</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn177">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kusīdin</b> is a designation of the ‘usurer’ found in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120674">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120674">2</a></sup> and often in the Sūtras. Jolly,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120674">3</a></sup> referring doubtless to the expression <i>kusīda apratītta,</i><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120674">4</a></sup> ‘a loan not yet repaid,’ occurring in connexion with <i>an-<a class="vei_120a" href="ऋण">ṛṇa</a>,</i> ‘free from debt,’ appears to be right in taking Kusīda to have the sense of ‘loan’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā. The rate of interest on loans is not specified before the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> period.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120674">5</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋण">ṛṇa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120674">↑</a> xiii. 4, 3, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120674">↑</a> vi. 32.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120674">↑</a> <i>Recht und Sitle,</i> 98, 99.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120674">↑</a> iii. 3, 8, 1. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120674">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 29 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 259.</li></ol></div>

कुसुरुबिन्द औद्दलकि|కుసురుబిన్ద ఔద్దలకి|ಕುಸುರುಬಿನ್ದ ಔದ್ದಲಕಿ|kusurubinda auddalaki|_vei_120@675|_vei1_pn176
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@674">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@676">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn175">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn176">1:176</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn177">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kusurubinda Auddalaki</b> appears as an authority on ritual matters in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120675">1</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120675">2</a></sup> the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120675">3</a></sup> and the Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120675">4</a></sup> He may have been the brother of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्वेतकेतु">śvetaketu</a>,</b> as suggested by Weber.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120675">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120675">↑</a> xxii. 15, 1. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120675">↑</a> vii. 2, 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120675">↑</a> i. 75 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 23, 327), where the reading seems to be Asurbinda.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120675">↑</a> i. 16. See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 39. The name is there read as Kusurubindu; in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 22, 14 it is Kusurabindu.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120675">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 61, n.</li></ol></div>

कुहू|కుహూ|ಕುಹೂ|kuhū|kuhuu|kuhoo|_vei_120@676|_vei1_pn176
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@675">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@677">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn175">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn176">1:176</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn177">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kuhū.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></p></div>

कूचक्र|కూచక్ర|ಕೂಚಕ್ರ|kūcakra|kuuchakra|koochakra|_vei_120@677|_vei1_pn176
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@676">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@678">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn175">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn176">1:176</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn177">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kūcakra</b> is a word occurring only once in an obscure verse of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120677">1</a></sup> where Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120677">2</a></sup> suggests that it has the sense of the wheel by which water is raised from a well. Much more probable is the interpretation of Roth,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120677">3</a></sup> who understands it to mean the female breast.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120677">↑</a> x. 102, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120677">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 157. <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120677">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

कूट|కూట|ಕೂಟ|kūṭa|kuuTa|kooTa|_vei_120@678|_vei1_pn176
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@677">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@679">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn175">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn176">1:176</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn177">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kūṭa,</b> a word found in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120678">1</a></sup> the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120678">2</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120678">3</a></sup> is of doubtful signification. On the whole,  the most probable sense is hammer,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120678">4</a></sup> which suits every passage adequately. The St. Petersburg Dictionary renders it ‘horn,’ which is the sense accepted by Whitney<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120678">5</a></sup> for the Atharvaveda passage where it occurs. Geldner<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120678">6</a></sup> thinks that it means ‘trap.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120678">↑</a> x. 102, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120678">↑</a> viii. 8, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120678">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 24; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8, 1, 15; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 49, 9; 50, 2 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 19, 114).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120678">↑</a> So Bloomfield, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 546; <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 585.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120678">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 505.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120678">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 138; 2, 7. <i>Cf.</i> von Bradke, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 46, 458; Kuhn's <i>Zeitschrift,</i> 34, 156; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 222.</li></ol></div>

कूदी|कूदि|కూదీ|కూది|ಕೂದೀ|ಕೂದಿ|kūdī|kūdi|kuudii|kuudi|koodee|koodi|_vei_120@679|_vei1_pn177
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@678">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@680">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn176">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn177">1:177</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn178">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kūdī,</b> written also Kūṭī in the manuscripts, occurs in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120679"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120679">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120679"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120679">2</a></sup> denoting a twig--identified by the scholiast with <b>Badarī,</b> the jujube--which was tied to the bodies of the dead to efface their traces, presumably in order to render the return of the spirit to the old home difficult.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120679"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120679">↑</a> v. 19, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120679"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120679">↑</a> Bloomfield's edition, xliv. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 11, 355; 12, 416; Roth, <i>Festgrnss an Bo7htlingk,</i> 98; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 254; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 165.</li></ol></div>

कूप|కూప|ಕೂಪ|kūpa|kuupa|koopa|_vei_120@680|_vei1_pn177
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@679">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@681">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn176">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn177">1:177</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn178">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kūpa</b> occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120680"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120680">1</a></sup> and later literature<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120680"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120680">2</a></sup> denoting an artificial hollow in the earth, or <a class="vei_120a" href="पित्">pit</a>. In some cases they must have been deep, as <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रित">trita</a> in the myth is said to have fallen into one from which he could not escape unaided.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120680"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120680">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120680"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120680">↑</a> i. 105, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120680"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120680">↑</a> Av. v. 31, 8; Satapatha Brahmaṇa, iii. 5, 4, 1; iv. 4, 5, 3; vi. 3, 3. 26, etc.; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 184. etc. The adjective <i>kūpya,</i> ‘being in a hole,’ occurs frequently in the later Saṃhitās.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120680"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120680">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 67.</li></ol></div>

कूबर|కూబర|ಕೂಬರ|kūbara|kuubara|koobara|_vei_120@681|_vei1_pn177
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@680">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@682">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn176">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn177">1:177</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn178">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kūbara</b> in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā (ii. 1, 11) and <b>Kūbarī</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iv. 6, 9, 11. 12) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xxvii. 6) denote the pole of a cart.</p></div>

कूर्च|కూర్చ|ಕೂರ್ಚ|kūrca|kuurcha|koorcha|_vei_120@682|_vei1_pn177
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@681">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@683">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn176">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn177">1:177</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn178">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kūrca</b> is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120682">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120682">2</a></sup> denoting a bundle of grass used as a seat. In one passage of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120682">3</a></sup> a golden <a class="vei_120a" href="कूर्च">kūrca</a> is referred to. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120682">↑</a> vii. 5, 8, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120682">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 3, 4. 7; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 11, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, v, 1, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120682">↑</a> xiii. 4, 3, 1.</li></ol></div>

कूर्म|కూర్మ|ಕೂರ್ಮ|kūrma|kuurma|koorma|_vei_120@683|_vei1_pn178
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@682">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@684">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn177">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn178">1:178</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn179">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kūrma,</b> the ‘tortoise,’ is mentioned frequently in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120683">1</a></sup> and Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120683">2</a></sup> but nothing is said of its characteristics. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कश्यप">kaśyapa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120683">↑</a> Av. ix. 4, 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 3, 3; v. 2, 8, 4. 5; 7, 13, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 15, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 34, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120683">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 2, 3; vi. 1, 1, 12, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 95; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 153.</li></ol></div>

कूशाम्ब स्वायव लातव्य|कूशांब स्वायव लातव्य|కూశామ్బ స్వాయవ లాతవ్య|కూశాంబ స్వాయవ లాతవ్య|ಕೂಶಾಮ್ಬ ಸ್ವಾಯವ ಲಾತವ್ಯ|ಕೂಶಾಂಬ ಸ್ವಾಯವ ಲಾತವ್ಯ|kūśāmba svāyava lātavya|kuushaamba svaayava laatavya|kooshaamba svaayava laatavya|_vei_120@684|_vei1_pn178
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@683">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@685">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn177">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn178">1:178</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn179">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kūśāmba <a class="vei_120a" href="स्वायव">svāyava</a> Lātavya</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120684"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120684">1</a></sup> as a priest. His name apparently means<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120684"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120684">2</a></sup> Kūśāmba,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120684"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120684">3</a></sup> of the <a class="vei_120a" href="लातव्य">lātavya</a> clan, son of Svāyu.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120684"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120684">↑</a> viii. 6, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120684"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120684">↑</a> The form is peculiar, as Kuśāmba would be expected.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120684"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120684">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 55, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

कृकलास|కృకలాస|ಕೃಕಲಾಸ|kṛkalāsa|kRkalaasa|kRikalaasa|kRukalaasa|kRRikalaasa|_vei_120@685|_vei1_pn178
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@684">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@686">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn177">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn178">1:178</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn179">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṛkalāsa</b> denotes the ‘chameleon’ mentioned in the list of sacrificial victims at the horse sacrifice in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120685"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120685">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120685"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120685">2</a></sup> The female chameleon, Kṛkalāsī, is also referred to in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120685"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120685">3</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोधा">godhā</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शयण्डक">śayaṇḍaka</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120685"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120685">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 19, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 21; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 40.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120685"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120685">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 5, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120685"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120685">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 221 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 29), Sāṭyāyanaka in Sāyaṇa on Rv. viii. 91.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 95.</li></ol></div>

कृक-वाकु|कृकवाकु|कृक|वाकु|కృక-వాకు|కృకవాకు|కృక|వాకు|ಕೃಕ-ವಾಕು|ಕೃಕವಾಕು|ಕೃಕ|ವಾಕು|kṛka-vāku|kṛkavāku|kṛka|vāku|kRka-vaaku|kRkavaaku|kRka|vaaku|kRika-vaaku|kRikavaaku|kRika|kRuka-vaaku|kRukavaaku|kRuka|kRRika-vaaku|kRRikavaaku|kRRika|_vei_120@686|_vei1_pn178
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@685">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@687">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn177">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn178">1:178</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn179">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṛka-vāku,</b> the ‘cock,’ being named in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120686"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120686">1</a></sup> with sheep, goats, and other domesticated animals, was presumably tamed.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120686"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120686">2</a></sup> In the list of victims at the horse sacrifice in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120686"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120686">3</a></sup> it appears as dedicated to Savitṛ: <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120686"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120686">4</a></sup> explains this by the fact that it declares the time of day (<i>kālānuvāda</i>). The commentator Mahīdhara<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120686"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120686">5</a></sup> explains the name by <i>tāmra-cūḍa,</i> ‘red-crested.’ It is of course onomatopoetic (‘calling kṛka’).<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120686"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120686">6</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुक्कुट">kukkuṭa</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120686"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120686">↑</a> v. 31, 2. <i>Cf.</i> x. 136, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120686"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120686">↑</a> <i>Cf.,</i> however, Sāyaṇa on <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 18, 1, who says that it is a ‘forest’ <a class="vei_120a" href="कुक्कुट">kukkuṭa</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120686"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120686">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 15; Vājasaneyi; Saṃhitā, xxiv. 35.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120686"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120686">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, xii. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120686"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120686">↑</a> On Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120686"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120686">↑</a> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 251; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18. 285.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 91.</li></ol></div>

कृत|కృత|ಕೃತ|kṛta|kRta|kRita|kRuta|kRRita|_vei_120@687|_vei1_pn179
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@686">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@688">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn178">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn179">1:179</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn180">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṛta.</b> See 2. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a></b> and 2. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="युग">yuga</a>.</b></p></div>

कृति|కృతి|ಕೃತಿ|kṛti|kRti|kRiti|kRuti|kRRiti|_vei_120@688|_vei1_pn179
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@687">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@689">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn178">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn179">1:179</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn180">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṛti.</b>--From one passage in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120688"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120688">1</a></sup> where the Maruts are described as having Kṛtis, Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120688"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120688">2</a></sup> concludes that the word means a dagger used in war. But there is no evidence that <a class="vei_120a" href="कृति">kṛti</a> was ever a human weapon. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="असि">asi</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120688"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120688">↑</a> i. 168, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120688"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120688">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 301. <i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 221.</li></ol></div>

कृत्तिकास्|कृत्तिका|కృత్తికాస్|కృత్తికా|ಕೃತ್ತಿಕಾಸ್|ಕೃತ್ತಿಕಾ|kṛttikās|kṛttikā|kRttikaas|kRttikaa|kRittikaas|kRittikaa|kRuttikaas|kRuttikaa|kRRittikaas|kRRittikaa|_vei_120@689|_vei1_pn179
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@688">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@690">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn178">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn179">1:179</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn180">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṛttikās.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p></div>

कृत्वन्|కృత్వన్|ಕೃತ್ವನ್|kṛtvan|kRtvan|kRitvan|kRutvan|kRRitvan|_vei_120@690|_vei1_pn179
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@689">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@691">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn178">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn179">1:179</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn180">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṛtvan.</b>--In one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120690"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120690">1</a></sup> the word <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्वन्">kṛtvan</a> in the plural is mentioned with the <b>Ārjīkas</b> and the five peoples. Pischel<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120690"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120690">2</a></sup> thinks that it means a people, and Sāyaṇa expressly says that the Kṛtvans designate a country.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120690"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120690">3</a></sup> The name in that case would point to some connexion with the <b>Kurus</b> or <b>Krivis.</b> Hillebrandt,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120690"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120690">4</a></sup> however, thinks that the word is an adjective which qualifies Ārjīkas and designates this people as magicians, being applied to them by an opponent. In favour of this view, he quotes Hiouen Thsang's statement<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120690"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120690">5</a></sup> that the neighbouring kings held the base Kaśmīrians in such scorn as to refuse all alliance with them, and to give them the name of Ki-li-to, or Kṛtyas. He suggests that the Ārjīkas settled in Kasmīr in ancient times already had the same evil reputation as their successors in later days.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120690"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120690">↑</a> ix. 65, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120690"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120690">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 209.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120690"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120690">↑</a> <i>Kṛtvāna <a class="vei_120a" href="इति">iti</a> deśābhidhānam.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120690"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120690">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 136, 137</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120690"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120690">↑</a> Cunningham, <i>Aneient Gevgraphy of India,</i> 93.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

कृप|కృప|ಕೃಪ|kṛpa|kRpa|kRipa|kRupa|kRRipa|_vei_120@691|_vei1_pn179
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@690">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@692">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn178">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn179">1:179</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn180">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṛpa</b> is mentioned in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120691"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120691">1</a></sup> along with <a class="vei_120a" href="रुशम">ruśama</a> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्यावक">śyāvaka</a>,</b> as a protégé of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120691"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120691">↑</a> viii. 3, 12; 4, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 163.</li></ol></div>

कृमि|కృమి|ಕೃಮಿ|kṛmi|kRmi|kRimi|kRumi|kRRimi|_vei_120@692|_vei1_pn179
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@691">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@693">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn178">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn179">1:179</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn180">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṛmi,</b> ‘worm.’ In the later Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120692"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120692">1</a></sup> and especially in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120692"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120692">2</a></sup> worms play a considerable part. They are  regarded as poisonous, and are spoken of as found in the mountains, in forests, in waters, in plants, and in the human body. In accordance with widespread primitive ideas, they are considered to be the causes of disease in men and animals. The Atharvaveda contains three hymns<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120692"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120692">2</a></sup> as charms directed against them. The first of these hymns is of a general character, the second is meant to destroy worms in cattle, and the third is intended to cure children of worms. When found in men, worms are said to have their place in the head and ribs,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120692"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120692">3</a></sup> and to creep into the eyes, nose, and teeth.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120692"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120692">4</a></sup> They are described as dark brown, but white in the fore part of the body, with black ears, and as having three heads.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120692"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120692">5</a></sup> They are given many specific names: <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अलाण्डु">alāṇḍu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="एजत्क">ejatka</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कष्कष">kaṣkaṣa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कीट">kīṭa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरूरु">kurūru</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="नीलंगु">nīlaṅgu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="येवाष">yevāṣa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वघा">vaghā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वृक्षसर्पी">vṛkṣasarpī</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शलुन">śaluna</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शवर्त">śavarta</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शिपवित्नुक">śipavitnuka</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="स्तेग">stega</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120692"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120692">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 11, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 11; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 30; <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, iv. 36; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 1, 2; and <i>of.</i> Rv. i. 191.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120692"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120692">↑</a> ii. 31. 32; v. 23.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120692"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120692">↑</a> ii. 31, 32; v. 23.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120692"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120692">↑</a> Av. ii. 31, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120692"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120692">↑</a> Av. v. 23, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120692"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120692">↑</a> Av. v. 23, 4 <i>et seq.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 98, 393; Kuhn, <i>Zeitschrift für vergleichende Sprachforschung,</i> 13, 49 <i>et seq.;</i> 113 <i>et seq.;</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 313 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 199; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 73.</li></ol></div>

कृमुक|కృముక|ಕೃಮುಕ|kṛmuka|kRmuka|kRimuka|kRumuka|kRRimuka|_vei_120@693|_vei1_pn180
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@692">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@694">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn179">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn180">1:180</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn181">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṛmuka</b> is the name in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120693"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120693">1</a></sup> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120693"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120693">2</a></sup> of a species of wood used for fuel.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120693"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120693">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120693"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120693">↑</a> xix. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120693"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120693">↑</a> vi. 6, 2, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120693"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120693">↑</a> <i>Ibid.</i> (<i>Krāmuha</i> as applied to <i><a class="vei_120a" href="समिध्">samidh</a></i>).</li></ol></div>

कृश|కృశ|ಕೃಶ|kṛśa|kRsha|kRisha|kRusha|kRRisha|_vei_120@694|_vei1_pn180
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@693">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@695">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn179">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn180">1:180</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn181">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṛśa</b> is mentioned with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="संवर्त">saṃvarta</a></b> as a pious sacrificer to <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> in one of the <a class="vei_120a" href="वालखिल्य">vālakhilya</a> hymns of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120694"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120694">1</a></sup> and in another<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120694"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120694">2</a></sup> as a speaker of truth, while a third is traditionally<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120694"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120694">3</a></sup> ascribed to his authorship. He seems also to be mentioned with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शयु">śayu</a></b> as a protégé of the Aśvins in another hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120694"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120694">4</a></sup> but here the word may merely denote the ‘feeble man.’<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120694"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120694">5</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120694"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120694">↑</a> viii. 54, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120694"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120694">↑</a> viii. 59, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120694"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120694">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 293, n.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120694"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120694">↑</a> x. 40, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120694"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120694">↑</a> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 132, 164.</li></ol></div>

कृशन|కృశన|ಕೃಶನ|kṛśana|kRshana|kRishana|kRushana|kRRishana|_vei_120@695|_vei1_pn181
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@694">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@696">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn180">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn181">1:181</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn183">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṛśana,</b> ‘pearl.’ In the Rigveda pearls are mentioned as adorning the car of Savitṛ<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120695">1</a></sup> as well as being used for the adornment of a horse.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120695">2</a></sup> Hence the horse is spoken of as the ‘pearled one’ (<i>kṛśanāvant</i>).<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120695">3</a></sup> The Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120695">4</a></sup> also refers to pearls, and mentions that ‘pearl shell’ (<i>śañkhaḥ kṛśanaḥ</i>) won from the sea was used as an amulet.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120695">5</a></sup> The Nighaṇṭu<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120695">6</a></sup> renders the word as ‘gold.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120695">↑</a> i. 35, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120695">↑</a> x. 68, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120695">↑</a> i. 126, 4. <i>Cf. kṛśanin,</i> vii. 18, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120695">↑</a> x. 1, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120695">↑</a> iv. 10, 1. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120695">↑</a> i. 2. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="साम">sāma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, 1, 6, 22.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i>, 53, 54; Lanman in Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 161.</li></ol></div>

कृशानु|కృశాను|ಕೃಶಾನು|kṛśānu|kRshaanu|kRishaanu|kRushaanu|kRRishaanu|_vei_120@696|_vei1_pn181
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@695">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@697">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn180">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn181">1:181</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn183">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṛśānu</b> appears in the Rigveda as a mythological personage.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120696"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120696">1</a></sup> In one verse,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120696"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120696">2</a></sup> however, Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120696"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120696">3</a></sup> sees in this word the name of a bowman, but there seems no reason to dissociate this passage from the rest.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120696"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120696">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> pp. 74, 112, 137; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 448.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120696"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120696">↑</a> i. 112, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120696"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120696">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> 4.</li></ol></div>

कृषि|కృషి|ಕೃಷಿ|kṛṣi|kRShi|kRiShi|kRuShi|kRRiShi|_vei_120@697|_vei1_pn181
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@696">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@698">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn180">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn181">1:181</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn183">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṛṣi,</b> ‘ploughing.’ The cultivation of the soil was no doubt known to the Indians before they separated from the Iranians, as is indicated by the identity of the expressions <i>yavaṃ kṛṣ</i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सस्य">sasya</a></i> in the Rigveda with <i>yao karesh</i> and <i>hahya</i> in the Avesta, referring to the ploughing in of the seed and to the grain which resulted.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120697">1</a></sup> But it is not without significance that the expressions for ploughing occur mainly in the first<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120697">2</a></sup> and tenth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120697">3</a></sup> books of the Rigveda, and only rarely in the so-called ‘family’ books (ii.-vii.).<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120697">4</a></sup> In the Atharvaveda <b>Pṛthī <a class="vei_120a" href="वैन्य">vainya</a></b> is credited with the origination of ploughing,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120697">5</a></sup> and even in the Rigveda the Aśvins are spoken of as concerned with the sowing of grain by means of the plough.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120697">6</a></sup> In the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas ploughing is repeatedly referred to.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120697">7</a></sup> </p><p>Even in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120697">8</a></sup> there is clear proof of the importance attached to agriculture. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120697">9</a></sup> the <b>Vrātyas,</b> Hindus without the pale of Brahminism, are described as not cultivating the soil.</p><p>The plough land was called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उर्वरा">urvarā</a></b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षेत्र">kṣetra</a>;</b> manure (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="शकन्">śakan</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="करीष">karīṣa</a></b>) was used, and irrigation was practised (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="खनित्र">khanitra</a></b>). The plough (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="लांगल">lāṅgala</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सीर">sīra</a></b>) was drawn by oxen, teams of six, eight, or even twelve being employed.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120697">10</a></sup> The operations of agriculture are neatly summed up in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120697">11</a></sup> as ‘ploughing, sowing, reaping, and threshing’ (<i>kṛṣantaḥ, vapantaḥ, lunantaḥ, mṛṇantaḥ</i>). The ripe grain was cut with a sickle (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="दात्र">dātra</a>, Sṛṇi</b>), bound into bundles<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120697">12</a></sup> (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्ष">parṣa</a></b>), and beaten out on the floor of the granary (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="खल">khala</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120697">13</a></sup> The grain was then separated from the straw and refuse either by a sieve (<b>Titau</b>) or a winnowing fan (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="शूर्प">śūrpa</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120697">14</a></sup> The winnower was called <b>Dhānyākṛt,</b><sup id="ref_120_15_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_120697">15</a></sup> and the grain was measured in a vessel called <b>Urdara.</b><sup id="ref_120_16_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_120697">16</a></sup></p><p>The Rigveda leaves us in doubt as to the kind of grain grown, for <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यव">yava</a></b> is a word of doubtful signification, and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धाना">dhānā</a></b> is also vague. In the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_120697">17</a></sup> things are different. Rice (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रीहि">vrīhi</a></b>) appears, <a class="vei_120a" href="यव">yava</a> means barley, with a species styled <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपवाक">upavāka</a>.</b> Beans (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="मुद्ग">mudga</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="माष">māṣa</a></b>), sesamum (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="तिल">tila</a></b>), and other grains (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अणु">aṇu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="खल्व">khalva</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="गोधूम">godhūma</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="नीवार">nīvāra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रियंगु">priyaṅgu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मसूर">masūra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="श्यामाक">śyāmāka</a></b>) are mentioned, while cucumbers (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="उर्वारू">urvārū</a>, Uryārūka</b>) were known. It is uncertain whether fruit trees (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="वृक्ष">vṛkṣa</a></b>) were cultivated, or merely grew wild;<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_120697">18</a></sup> but frequent mention is made of the jujube (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="कर्कन्धु">karkandhu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कुवल">kuvala</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="बदर">badara</a>.</b>).</p><p>The seasons for agriculture are briefly summed up in a passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā:<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_120697">19</a></sup> barley ripened in summer, being no doubt sown, as in modern India, in winter; rice  ripened in autumn, being sown in the beginning of the rains; beans and sesamum, planted at the time of the summer rains, ripened in the winter and the cool season. There were two harvests (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="सस्य">sasya</a></b>) a year according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā;<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_120697">20</a></sup> the winter crop was ripe by the month of <a class="vei_120a" href="चैत्र">caitra</a> (March-April) according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_120697">21</a></sup></p><p>The farmer had plenty of trouble of his own: moles destroyed the seed, birds and various kinds of reptiles (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपक्वस">upakvasa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="जभ्य">jabhya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="तर्द">tarda</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पतंग">pataṅga</a></b>) injured the young shoots, excessive rain or drought might damage the crops. The Atharvaveda contains spells to prevent these evils.<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_120697">22</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120697">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 235; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 85.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120697">↑</a> Forms of the root <i>kṛṣ,</i> ‘to plough,’ occur in Rv. i. 23, 15; 176, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120697">↑</a> Rv. x. 34, 13; 117, 7. In x. 146, 6, <i>akṛṣīvala,</i> ‘not agricultural,’ occurs. <i>Cf.</i> x. 101, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120697">↑</a> <i>Kṛṣ</i> is also found in viii. 20, 19; 22, 6; in the family books only in iv. 57, 4, and as <i>vi-kṛṣ</i> in iv. 57, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120697">↑</a> viii. 10, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120697">↑</a> i. 117, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120697">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कृषि">kṛṣi</a></i> is found, <i>e.g.,</i> in Av. ii. 4, 5; viii. 2, 19; 10, 24; x. 6, 12; xii. 2, 27, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 1, 11; 1, etc.; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 2, 2; iii. 6, 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iv. 10; ix. 27; xiv. 19. 21, etc.; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 2, 2, 7; viii. 6, 2, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 2, 5, etc. In Av. vi. 116, 1, <i>kārṣīvaṇa</i> denotes a ‘plougher.’ See also <a class="vei_120a" href="कार्ष्मन्">kārṣman</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120697">↑</a> x. 34, 13; 117, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>India, Old and New,</i> 208.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120697">↑</a> xvii. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120697">↑</a> Av. vi. 91, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 2. <i>Cf.</i> Rv. viii. 6, 48; x. 101, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120697">↑</a> i. 6, 1, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120697">↑</a> viii. 78, 10; x. 101, 3; 131, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120697">↑</a> Rv. x. 48, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120697">↑</a> Rv. x. 71, 2; Av. xii. 3, 19. The technical terms are <i>tuṣair vi-vic,</i> Av. xi. 1, 12; <i>palāvān <a class="vei_120a" href="अप">apa</a>-vic,</i> xii. 3, 19.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_120697">↑</a> Rv. x. 94, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_120697">↑</a> Rv. ii. 14, 11. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्थिवि">sthivi</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_120697">↑</a> See Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 12, for a list.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_120697">↑</a> The pulling of ripe fruit is referred to in Rv. iii. 45, 4. <i>Cf. pakvā <a class="vei_120a" href="शाखा">śākhā</a>,</i> Rv. i. 8, 8; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वृक्ष">vṛkṣa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पक्व">pakva</a>,</i> Rv. iv. 20, 5; Av. xx. 127, 4. But that does not prove arboriculture.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_120697">↑</a> vii. 2, 10, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_120697">↑</a> v. 1, 7, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_120697">↑</a> xix. 3. <i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 81, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_120697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_120697">↑</a> See Av. vi. 50. 142; vii. 11.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 235243.</li></ol></div>

कृष्टि|కృష్టి|ಕೃಷ್ಟಿ|kṛṣṭi|kRShTi|kRiShTi|kRuShTi|kRRiShTi|_vei_120@698|_vei1_pn183
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@697">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@699">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn181">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn183">1:183</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn184">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṛṣṭi</b> denotes ‘people’ in general from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120698"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120698">1</a></sup> onwards. Its common and regular use in this sense appears to show that the Āryans, when they invaded India, were already agriculturists, though the employment of the words referring to ploughing mentioned under <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृषि">kṛṣi</a></b> indicates that not all of the people devoted themselves equally to that occupation. <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> are <i>par excellence</i> the lords of men (<a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्टि">kṛṣṭi</a>).<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120698"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120698">2</a></sup> Sometimes the word is further defined by the addition of an adjective meaning ‘belonging to mankind,’ ‘of men’ (<i>mānuṣīḥ,</i><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120698"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120698">3</a></sup> <i>mānavīḥ</i>).<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120698"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120698">4</a></sup></p><p>Special mention is frequently<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120698"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120698">5</a></sup> made of the ‘five peoples’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्च">pañca</a> kṛṣṭayaḥ</i>). The exact sense of this expression is doubtful. See <b>Panca Janāsaḥ.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120698"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120698">↑</a> i. 52, 11; 100, 10; 160, 5; 189, 3; iii. 49, 1; iv. 21, 2, etc.; Av. xii. 1, 3. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120698"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120698">↑</a> i. 177, 1; iv. 17, 5; vii. 26, 5; viii. 13, 9 (<a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>); i. 59, 5; vi. 18, 2; vii. 5, 5 (<a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120698"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120698">↑</a> Rv. i. 59, 5; vi. 18, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120698"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120698">↑</a> Av. iii. 24, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120698"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120698">↑</a> Rv. ii. 2, 10; iii. 53, 16; iv. 38, 10; x. 60, 4; 119, 6; 178, 3; Av. iii. 24, 2; xii. 1, 42.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 141.</li></ol></div>

कृष्ण|कृष्ण|కృష్ణ_౧|కృష్ణ|ಕೃಷ್ಣ_೧|ಕೃಷ್ಣ|kṛṣṇa_1|kṛṣṇa|kRShNa_1|kRShNa|kRiShNa_1|kRiShNa|kRuShNa_1|kRuShNa|kRRiShNa_1|kRRiShNa|_vei_120@699|_vei1_pn183
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@698">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@700">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn181">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn183">1:183</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn184">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Kṛṣṇa</b> (‘black’) denotes a dark-coloured animal or bird. In some passages,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120699"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120699">1</a></sup> as the context shows, an antelope is certainly  meant. In a few others<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120699"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120699">2</a></sup> a bird of prey seems indicated. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्णाजिन">kṛṣṇājina</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120699"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120699">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 2, 6, 5; vi. 1, 3, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 4, 1; iii. 2, 1, 28. So <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण">kṛṣṇa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="विषाणा">viṣāṇā</a>,</i> ‘horn of the black antelope,’ <i>ibid.,</i> iii. 2, 1, 18, 28; 2, 20; iv. 4, 5, 2; v. 4, 2, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 3, 7. See also the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> passages, Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 17; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 36 (<i>cf.</i> ii. 1).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120699"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120699">↑</a> Rv. x. 94, 5; Av. xi. 2, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xii. 27.</li></ol></div>

कृष्ण|कृष्ण|కృష్ణ_౨|కృష్ణ|ಕೃಷ್ಣ_೨|ಕೃಷ್ಣ|kṛṣṇa_2|kṛṣṇa|kRShNa_2|kRShNa|kRiShNa_2|kRiShNa|kRuShNa_2|kRuShNa|kRRiShNa_2|kRRiShNa|_vei_120@700|_vei1_pn184
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@699">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@701">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn183">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn184">1:184</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn185">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Kṛṣṇa</b> appears as the name of a seer in one hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120700"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120700">1</a></sup> Tradition assigns to him or to <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वक">viśvaka</a>, son of <a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण">kṛṣṇa</a> (<i>Kārṣṇi</i>), the authorship of the following hymn.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120700"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120700">2</a></sup> The word Kṛṣṇiya may be a patronymic<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120700"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120700">3</a></sup> formed from the same name in two other hymns of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120700"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120700">4</a></sup> where the Aśvins are said to have restored <b>Viṣṇāpū</b> to <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वक">viśvaka</a> Kṛṣṇya. In that case <a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण">kṛṣṇa</a> would seem to be the grandfather of Viṣṇāpū. This <a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण">kṛṣṇa</a> may be identical with <a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण">kṛṣṇa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="आंगिरस">āṅgirasa</a> mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120700"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120700">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120700"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120700">↑</a> viii. 85, 3. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120700"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120700">↑</a> viii. 86.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120700"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120700">↑</a> i. 116, 23; 117, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120700"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120700">↑</a> As a patronymic, it would be an isolated formation (<i>cf.</i> however <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पज्रिय">pajriya</a></i>), instead of <i>Kārṣṇya. Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> 228<i>a</i> and 200.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120700"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120700">↑</a> xxx. 9.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 108; Macdonell, <i>Vedia Mythology,</i> p. 52.</li></ol></div>

कृष्ण देवकी-पुत्र|कृष्ण देवकी-पुत्र|कृष्ण देवकीपुत्र|कृष्ण देवकी|कृष्ण देवकि|पुत्र|కృష్ణ దేవకీ-పుత్ర_౩|కృష్ణ దేవకీ-పుత్ర|కృష్ణ దేవకీపుత్ర|కృష్ణ దేవకీ|కృష్ణ దేవకి|పుత్ర|ಕೃಷ್ಣ ದೇವಕೀ-ಪುತ್ರ_೩|ಕೃಷ್ಣ ದೇವಕೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಕೃಷ್ಣ ದೇವಕೀಪುತ್ರ|ಕೃಷ್ಣ ದೇವಕೀ|ಕೃಷ್ಣ ದೇವಕಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|kṛṣṇa devakī-putra_3|kṛṣṇa devakī-putra|kṛṣṇa devakīputra|kṛṣṇa devakī|kṛṣṇa devaki|putra|kRShNa devakii-putra_3|kRShNa devakii-putra|kRShNa devakiiputra|kRShNa devakii|kRShNa devaki|kRShNa devakee-putra_3|kRShNa devakee-putra|kRShNa devakeeputra|kRShNa devakee|kRiShNa devakii-putra_3|kRiShNa devakii-putra|kRiShNa devakiiputra|kRiShNa devakii|kRiShNa devaki|kRuShNa devakii-putra_3|kRuShNa devakii-putra|kRuShNa devakiiputra|kRuShNa devakii|kRuShNa devaki|kRRiShNa devakii-putra_3|kRRiShNa devakii-putra|kRRiShNa devakiiputra|kRRiShNa devakii|kRRiShNa devaki|_vei_120@701|_vei1_pn184
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@700">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@702">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn183">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn184">1:184</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn185">↷</a><hr><p>3. <b>Kṛṣṇa <a class="vei_120a" href="देवकी">devakī</a>-putra</b> is mentioned in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120701"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120701">1</a></sup> as a pupil of the mythical <b><a class="vei_120a" href="घोर आंगिरस">ghora āṅgirasa</a>.</b> Tradition,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120701"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120701">2</a></sup> and several modern writers like Grierson, Garbe, and von Schroeder, recognize in him the hero <a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण">kṛṣṇa</a>, who later is deified. In their view he is a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> teacher of morals, as opposed to Brahminism.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120701"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120701">3</a></sup> This is extremely doubtful. It appears better either to regard the coincidence of name as accidental, or to suppose that the reference is a piece of Euhemerism. To identify this <a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण">kṛṣṇa</a> with the preceding, as does the St. Petersburg Dictionary, seems to be quite groundless.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120701"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120701">↑</a> iii. 17, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120701"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120701">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 169.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120701"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120701">↑</a> Von Schroeder, <i>Vienna Oriental Journal,</i> 19, 414, 415; Grierson, <i>Encyclopadia of Religions,</i> article ‘Bhakti’; Garbe, <i>Bhagavadgītā.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 71; 148; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1905, 386.</li></ol></div>

कृष्ण हारीत|कृष्ण हारीत|కృష్ణ హారీత_౪|కృష్ణ హారీత|ಕೃಷ್ಣ ಹಾರೀತ_೪|ಕೃಷ್ಣ ಹಾರೀತ|kṛṣṇa hārīta_4|kṛṣṇa hārīta|kRShNa haariita_4|kRShNa haariita|kRShNa haareeta_4|kRShNa haareeta|kRiShNa haariita_4|kRiShNa haariita|kRuShNa haariita_4|kRuShNa haariita|kRRiShNa haariita_4|kRRiShNa haariita|_vei_120@702|_vei1_pn184
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@701">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@703">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn183">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn184">1:184</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn185">↷</a><hr><p>4. <b>Kṛṣṇa Hārīta</b> is mentioned as a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120702"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120702">1</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120702"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120702">2</a></sup> has Kṛtsna in the parallel passage. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120702"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120702">↑</a> iii. 2. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120702"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120702">↑</a> viii. 10.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 391, n.; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 50.</li></ol></div>

कृष्ण-दत्त लौहितय|कृष्णदत्त लौहितय|कृष्ण|दत्त लौहितय|కృష్ణ-దత్త లౌహితయ|కృష్ణదత్త లౌహితయ|కృష్ణ|దత్త లౌహితయ|ಕೃಷ್ಣ-ದತ್ತ ಲೌಹಿತಯ|ಕೃಷ್ಣದತ್ತ ಲೌಹಿತಯ|ಕೃಷ್ಣ|ದತ್ತ ಲೌಹಿತಯ|kṛṣṇa-datta lauhitaya|kṛṣṇadatta lauhitaya|kṛṣṇa|datta lauhitaya|kRShNa-datta lauhitaya|kRShNadatta lauhitaya|kRShNa|kRiShNa-datta lauhitaya|kRiShNadatta lauhitaya|kRiShNa|kRuShNa-datta lauhitaya|kRuShNadatta lauhitaya|kRuShNa|kRRiShNa-datta lauhitaya|kRRiShNadatta lauhitaya|kRRiShNa|_vei_120@703|_vei1_pn185
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@702">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@704">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn184">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn185">1:185</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn186">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṛṣṇa-<a class="vei_120a" href="दत्त">datta</a> Lauhitaya</b> (‘descendant of Lohita’) is mentioned in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 42, 1) as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्यामसुजयन्त लौहित्य">śyāmasujayanta lauhitya</a>.</b></p></div>

कृष्ण-धृति सात्यकि|कृष्णधृति सात्यकि|कृष्ण|धृति सात्यकि|కృష్ణ-ధృతి సాత్యకి|కృష్ణధృతి సాత్యకి|కృష్ణ|ధృతి సాత్యకి|ಕೃಷ್ಣ-ಧೃತಿ ಸಾತ್ಯಕಿ|ಕೃಷ್ಣಧೃತಿ ಸಾತ್ಯಕಿ|ಕೃಷ್ಣ|ಧೃತಿ ಸಾತ್ಯಕಿ|kṛṣṇa-dhṛti sātyaki|kṛṣṇadhṛti sātyaki|kṛṣṇa|dhṛti sātyaki|kRShNa-dhRti saatyaki|kRShNadhRti saatyaki|kRShNa|dhRti saatyaki|kRiShNa-dhRiti saatyaki|kRiShNadhRiti saatyaki|kRiShNa|dhRiti saatyaki|kRuShNa-dhRuti saatyaki|kRuShNadhRuti saatyaki|kRuShNa|dhRuti saatyaki|kRRiShNa-dhRRiti saatyaki|kRRiShNadhRRiti saatyaki|kRRiShNa|dhRRiti saatyaki|_vei_120@704|_vei1_pn185
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@703">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@705">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn184">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn185">1:185</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn186">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṛṣṇa-dhṛti Sātyaki</b> (‘descendant of Satyaka’) is mentioned in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 42, 1) as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सत्यश्रवस्">satyaśravas</a>.</b></p></div>

कृष्ण-रात लौहित्य|कृष्णरात लौहित्य|कृष्ण|रात लौहित्य|కృష్ణ-రాత లౌహిత్య|కృష్ణరాత లౌహిత్య|కృష్ణ|రాత లౌహిత్య|ಕೃಷ್ಣ-ರಾತ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|ಕೃಷ್ಣರಾತ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|ಕೃಷ್ಣ|ರಾತ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|kṛṣṇa-rāta lauhitya|kṛṣṇarāta lauhitya|kṛṣṇa|rāta lauhitya|kRShNa-raata lauhitya|kRShNaraata lauhitya|kRShNa|raata lauhitya|kRiShNa-raata lauhitya|kRiShNaraata lauhitya|kRiShNa|kRuShNa-raata lauhitya|kRuShNaraata lauhitya|kRuShNa|kRRiShNa-raata lauhitya|kRRiShNaraata lauhitya|kRRiShNa|_vei_120@705|_vei1_pn185
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@704">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@706">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn184">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn185">1:185</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn186">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṛṣṇa-<a class="vei_120a" href="रात">rāta</a> Lauhitya</b> (‘descendant of Lohita’) is mentioned in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 42, 1) as a pupil of <b>Śyāmaiajayanta <a class="vei_120a" href="लौहित्य">lauhitya</a>.</b></p></div>

कृष्णल|కృష్ణల|ಕೃಷ್ಣಲ|kṛṣṇala|kRShNala|kRiShNala|kRuShNala|kRRiShNala|_vei_120@706|_vei1_pn185
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@705">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@707">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn184">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn185">1:185</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn186">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṛṣṇala</b> denotes the berry of the <i>Abrus precatorius,</i> used as a weight according to the later authorities, one <a class="vei_120a" href="माष">māṣa</a> (‘bean’) being equated to four Kṛṣṇalas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120706"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120706">1</a></sup> In the sense of a weight it occurs in the Taittirīva<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120706"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120706">2</a></sup> and other Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120706"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120706">3</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120706"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120706">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120706"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120706">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>, viii. 134.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120706"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120706">↑</a> ii. 3, 2, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120706"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120706">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 4 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्य">hiraṇya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्णल">kṛṣṇala</a></i>).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120706"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120706">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 6, 7; Anupada <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ix. 6. In the later language it is also called <i>raktikā</i> or <i>guñjā</i> (being a smooth red berry with a black spot at one end).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber's edition of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्योतिष">jyotiṣa</a>, 82 <i>et seq.; Indische Strcifen,</i> 1, 102, 103.</li></ol></div>

कृष्णाजिन|కృష్ణాజిన|ಕೃಷ್ಣಾಜಿನ|kṛṣṇājina|kRShNaajina|kRiShNaajina|kRuShNaajina|kRRiShNaajina|_vei_120@707|_vei1_pn185
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@706">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@708">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn184">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn185">1:185</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn186">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṛṣṇājina</b> is the skin (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अजिन">ajina</a></i>) of the black antelope (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण">kṛṣṇa</a>.</b>) It is repeatedly referred to in the later Saṃhitās and Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120707"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120707">1</a></sup> with regard to its ritual use.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120707"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120707">↑</a> Av. ix. 6, 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 4, 9, 2; v. 4, 4, 4: Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 1, 22; 4, 1; 9, 2, 35, etc.</li></ol></div>

कृष्णायस|కృష్ణాయస|ಕೃಷ್ಣಾಯಸ|kṛṣṇāyasa|kRShNaayasa|kRiShNaayasa|kRuShNaayasa|kRRiShNaayasa|_vei_120@708|_vei1_pn185
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@707">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@709">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn184">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn185">1:185</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn186">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṛṣṇāyasa</b> (‘black metal’), ‘iron,’ is referred to in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 1, 6). See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्">ayas</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कार्ष्णायस">kārṣṇāyasa</a>.</b></p></div>

कृसर|కృసర|ಕೃಸರ|kṛsara|kRsara|kRisara|kRusara|kRRisara|_vei_120@709|_vei1_pn185
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@708">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@710">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn184">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn185">1:185</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn186">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṛsara,</b> a term denoting a mess of rice and sesamum, often mentioned in the Sūtras, occurs in the Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120709"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120709">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120709"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120709">↑</a> v. 2. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Omina und Portenta,</i> 315 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

केकय|కేకయ|ಕೇಕಯ|kekaya|_vei_120@710|_vei1_pn185
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@709">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@711">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn184">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn185">1:185</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn186">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kekaya</b> is the name of a tribe which in later days, and probably also in Vedic times, was settled in the north-west,  oetween the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धु">sindhu</a></b> (Indus) and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वितस्ता">vitastā</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120710"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120710">1</a></sup> In the Vedic texts the Kekayas are mentioned indirectly only in the name of their prince <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वपति">aśvapati</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कैकेय">kaikeya</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120710"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120710">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120710"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120710">↑</a> Pargiter, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 317, 332.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120710"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120710">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 6, 1, 2. <i>et seq.;</i> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 11, 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Litenature,</i> 120; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 126.</li></ol></div>

केतु|केतु|కేతు_౧|కేతు|ಕೇತು_೧|ಕೇತು|ketu_1|ketu|_vei_120@711|_vei1_pn186
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@710">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@712">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn185">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn186">1:186</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn187">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Ketu</b> is a term which Weber<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120711"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120711">1</a></sup> understands in the sense of ‘meteor’ or ‘comet’ in the late Adbhuta <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120711"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120711">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 41. The <i>aruṇāḥ ketavaḥ</i> (Av. xi. 10, 1. 2. 7), referred to in this sense in the St. Petersburg Dictionary, are not so treated by Bo7htlingk in his Dictionary.</li></ol></div>

केतु वाज्य|केतु वाज्य|కేతు వాజ్య_౨|కేతు వాజ్య|ಕೇತು ವಾಜ್ಯ_೨|ಕೇತು ವಾಜ್ಯ|ketu vājya_2|ketu vājya|ketu vaajya_2|ketu vaajya|_vei_120@712|_vei1_pn186
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@711">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@713">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn185">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn186">1:186</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn187">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Ketu Vājya</b> (‘descendant of Vāja’) is mentioned as a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120712"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120712">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120712"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120712">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372.</li></ol></div>

केवर्त|कैवर्त|కేవర్త|కైవర్త|ಕೇವರ್ತ|ಕೈವರ್ತ|kevarta|kaivarta|_vei_120@713|_vei1_pn186
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@712">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@714">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn185">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn186">1:186</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn187">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kevarta, Kaivarta</b> are two variant forms denoting ‘fisherman’ in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120713">1</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120713">2</a></sup> lists of victims at the Puruṣamedha, or human sacrifice.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120713">↑</a> xxx. 16, with Mahīdhara's note.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120713">↑</a> iii. 4, 12, 1, with Sāyaṇa's note.</li></ol></div>

केश|కేశ|ಕೇಶ|keśa|kesha|_vei_120@714|_vei1_pn186
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@713">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@715">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn185">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn186">1:186</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn187">↷</a><hr><p><b>Keśa,</b> ‘hair of the head,’ is repeatedly mentioned in the later Saṃhitās and Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120714"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120714">1</a></sup> The hair was a matter of great care to the Vedic Indian, and several hymns of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120714"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120714">2</a></sup> are directed to securing its plentiful growth. Cutting or shaving (<i>vap</i>) the hair is often referred to.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120714"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120714">3</a></sup> For a man to wear long hair was considered effeminate.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120714"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120714">4</a></sup> As to modes of dressing the hair see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ओपश">opaśa</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कपर्द">kaparda</a>;</b> as to the beard see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्मश्रु">śmaśru</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120714"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120714">↑</a> Av. v. 19, 3; vi. 136, 3, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 22; xx. 5; xxv. 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 5, 2, 48, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120714"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120714">↑</a> vi. 136. 137. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 68; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 536, 537.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120714"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120714">↑</a> Av. viii. 2, 17; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 3, 1, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 425 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120714"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120714">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1, 2, 14. But <i>cf.</i> Vincent Smith, <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 34, 203.</li></ol></div>

केशिन्|केशिन्|కేశిన్_౧|కేశిన్|ಕೇಶಿನ್_೧|ಕೇಶಿನ್|keśin_1|keśin|keshin_1|keshin|_vei_120@715|_vei1_pn186
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@714">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@716">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn185">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn186">1:186</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn187">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Keśin</b> is the name of a people occurring in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120715"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120715">1</a></sup> where their king is mentioned as learning from <b>Khaṇḍika</b> the atonement for a bad omen at the sacrifice. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120715"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120715">↑</a> xi. 8, 4, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Pānini, vi. 4, 165; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 131, 134.</li></ol></div>

केशिन् दार्भ्य|केशिन् दार्भ्य|కేశిన్ దార్భ్య_౨|కేశిన్ దార్భ్య|ಕೇಶಿನ್ ದಾರ್ಭ್ಯ_೨|ಕೇಶಿನ್ ದಾರ್ಭ್ಯ|keśin dārbhya_2|keśin dārbhya|keshin daarbhya_2|keshin daarbhya|_vei_120@716|_vei1_pn187
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@715">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@717">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn186">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn187">1:187</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn188">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Keśin Dārbhya</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120716">1</a></sup> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दाल्भ्य">dālbhya</a></b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120716">2</a></sup> (‘descendant of Darbha’) is a somewhat enigmatic figure. According to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120716">3</a></sup> and the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> Brāhmana<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120716">4</a></sup> he was a king, sister's son of <b>Uccaiḥśravas,</b> according to the latter authority. His people were the <b>Pañcālas,</b> of whom the Keśins must therefore have been a branch, and who are said to have been threefold (<i>tryanīka</i>).<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120716">5</a></sup> A story is told of his having a ritual dispute with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="षण्डिक">ṣaṇḍika</a></b> in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120716">6</a></sup> this appears in another form in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120716">3</a></sup> He was a contemporary of a fellow sage, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="केशिन् सात्यकामि">keśin sātyakāmi</a>,</b> according to the Maitrāyaṇī<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120716">7</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120716">8</a></sup> Saṃhitās. The <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120716">9</a></sup> attributes to him a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> or chant, and the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120716">10</a></sup> tells how he was taught by a golden bird.</p><p>In view of the fact that the early literature always refers to <a class="vei_120a" href="दार्भ्य">dārbhya</a> as a sage, it seems doubtful whether the commentator is right in thinking that the Śatapatha refers to a king and a people, when a sage alone may well be meant, while the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> is of no great authority. The latter work may have assumed that the reference in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120716">11</a></sup> to the <a class="vei_120a" href="केशिन्">keśin</a> people signifies kingship, but this is hardly necessary.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120716">↑</a> This is the form of the name in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, and the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>; also later in the Bṛhaddevatā.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120716">↑</a> This is the form in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>. It also appears later in the Rigveda Anukramaṇī.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120716">↑</a> xi. 8, 4, 1 <i>et seq.,</i> as explained by Sāyaṇa.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120716">↑</a> iii. 29, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120716">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxx. 2 (Weber <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 471); Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xx. 25.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120716">↑</a> i. 4, 12 (von Schroeder gives no variant reading; but <i>ṣ</i> and <i>kh</i> are constantly confused in manuscripts).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120716">↑</a> xi. 8, 4, 1 <i>et seq.,</i> as explained by Sāyaṇa.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120716">↑</a> i. 6, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120716">↑</a> ii. 6, 2, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120716">↑</a> xiii. 10, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120716">↑</a> vii. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120716">↑</a> xxx. 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 193, 209; 2, 308; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 58, 59; Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 62, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

केशिन् सात्य-कामि|केशिन् सात्य-कामि|केशिन् सात्यकामि|केशिन् सात्य|कामि|కేశిన్ సాత్య-కామి_౩|కేశిన్ సాత్య-కామి|కేశిన్ సాత్యకామి|కేశిన్ సాత్య|కామి|ಕೇಶಿನ್ ಸಾತ್ಯ-ಕಾಮಿ_೩|ಕೇಶಿನ್ ಸಾತ್ಯ-ಕಾಮಿ|ಕೇಶಿನ್ ಸಾತ್ಯಕಾಮಿ|ಕೇಶಿನ್ ಸಾತ್ಯ|ಕಾಮಿ|keśin sātya-kāmi_3|keśin sātya-kāmi|keśin sātyakāmi|keśin sātya|kāmi|keshin saatya-kaami_3|keshin saatya-kaami|keshin saatyakaami|keshin saatya|kaami|_vei_120@717|_vei1_pn187
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@716">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@718">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn186">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn187">1:187</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn188">↷</a><hr><p>3. <b>Keśin <a class="vei_120a" href="सात्य">sātya</a>-kāmi</b> (‘descendant of Satyakāma’) is mentioned as a teacher and contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="केशिन् दार्भ्य">keśin dārbhya</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> (ii. 6, 2, 3) and Maitrāyaṇī (i. 6, 5) Saṃhitās. </p></div>

केसर-प्राबन्धा|केसरप्राबन्धा|केसर|प्राबन्धा|కేసర-ప్రాబన్ధా|కేసరప్రాబన్ధా|కేసర|ప్రాబన్ధా|ಕೇಸರ-ಪ್ರಾಬನ್ಧಾ|ಕೇಸರಪ್ರಾಬನ್ಧಾ|ಕೇಸರ|ಪ್ರಾಬನ್ಧಾ|kesara-prābandhā|kesaraprābandhā|kesara|prābandhā|kesara-praabandhaa|kesarapraabandhaa|praabandhaa|_vei_120@718|_vei1_pn188
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@717">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@719">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn187">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn188">1:188</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn189">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kesara-prābandhā.</b>--In the list of the crimes of the <b>Vaitahavyas</b> narrated in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120718">1</a></sup> is the cooking of the last she-goat (<i>caramājām</i>) of <a class="vei_120a" href="केसरप्राबन्धा">kesaraprābandhā</a>, who may presumably be deemed to have been a woman, ‘having braided hair.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120718">2</a></sup> Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120718">3</a></sup> followed by Whitney,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120718">4</a></sup> appears to amend the passage (<i>carama-jām</i>) as meaning ‘the last-born calf’ of <a class="vei_120a" href="केसरप्राबन्धा">kesaraprābandhā</a>, a cow. But this interpretation does not suit the name so well.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120718">↑</a> v. 18, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120718">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 432, 433.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120718">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 2, 447.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120718">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 252.</li></ol></div>

कैकेय|కైకేయ|ಕೈಕೇಯ|kaikeya|_vei_120@719|_vei1_pn188
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@718">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@720">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn187">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn188">1:188</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn189">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaikeya,</b> ‘king of the Kekayas,’ is an epithet of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वपति">aśvapati</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120719"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120719">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120719"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120719">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 6, 1, 2; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 11, 4.</li></ol></div>

कैरात|కైరాత|ಕೈರಾತ|kairāta|kairaata|_vei_120@720|_vei1_pn188
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@719">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@721">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn187">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn188">1:188</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn189">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kairāta</b> is the name of a snake in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120720"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120720">1</a></sup>--possibly, but not probably, the modern Karait.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120720"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120720">↑</a> v. 13, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 243</li></ol></div>

कैरातिका|కైరాతికా|ಕೈರಾತಿಕಾ|kairātikā|kairaatikaa|_vei_120@721|_vei1_pn188
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@720">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@722">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn187">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn188">1:188</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn189">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kairātikā,</b> a ‘maiden of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="किरात">kirāta</a></b> people,’ is mentioned in the Atharvaveda (x. 4, 14) as digging up roots for medicinal use.</p></div>

कैरिशि|కైరిశి|ಕೈರಿಶಿ|kairiśi|kairishi|_vei_120@722|_vei1_pn188
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@721">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@723">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn187">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn188">1:188</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn189">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kairiśi,</b> ‘descendant of Kiriśa,’ is the patronymic of <b>Sutvan</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 28).</p></div>

कैवर्त|కైవర్త|ಕೈವರ್ತ|kaivarta|_vei_120@723|_vei1_pn188
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@722">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@724">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn187">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn188">1:188</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn189">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaivarta.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="केवर्त">kevarta</a>.</b></p></div>

कैशिनी|कैशिनि|కైశినీ|కైశిని|ಕೈಶಿನೀ|ಕೈಶಿನಿ|kaiśinī|kaiśini|kaishinii|kaishini|kaishinee|_vei_120@724|_vei1_pn188
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@723">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@725">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn187">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn188">1:188</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn189">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaiśinī.</b>--The <i>Kaiśinyaḥ prajāḥ,</i> ‘offspring or people<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120724"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120724">1</a></sup> of <a class="vei_120a" href="केशिन्">keśin</a>,’ are mentioned in an obscure passage of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120724"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120724">2</a></sup> either as still existing at the date of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120724"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120724">3</a></sup> or as extinct.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120724"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120724">↑</a> So Sāyaṇa.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120724"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120724">↑</a> xi. 8, 4, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120724"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120724">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 134.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 208.</li></ol></div>

कैशोर्य|కైశోర్య|ಕೈಶೋರ್ಯ|kaiśorya|kaishorya|_vei_120@725|_vei1_pn188
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@724">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@726">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn187">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn188">1:188</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn189">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaiśorya,</b> ‘descendant of Kaiśori,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काप्य">kāpya</a></b> in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120725">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120725">↑</a> ii. 5, 22; iv. 5, 28 (Mādhyaṃdina = ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

कोक|कोक|కోక_౧|కోక|ಕೋಕ_೧|ಕೋಕ|koka_1|koka|_vei_120@726|_vei1_pn189
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@725">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@727">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn188">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn189">1:189</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn190">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Koka,</b> a word occurring in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120726"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120726">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120726"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120726">2</a></sup> seems to denote the ‘cuckoo.’ In all the three passages in which it is found, Sāyaṇa explains it as the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चक्रवाक">cakravāka</a>.</b> Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120726"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120726">3</a></sup> renders it in the Atharvaveda passages as a certain destructive parasitic animal. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अन्यवाप">anyavāpa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120726"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120726">↑</a> vii. 104, 22 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कोक">koka</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="यातु">yātu</a>,</i> a ghost in the shape of a cuckoo).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120726"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120726">↑</a> v. 23, 4; viii. 6, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120726"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120726">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 454; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 262; Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 49; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 92.</li></ol></div>

कोक|कोक|కోక_౨|కోక|ಕೋಕ_೨|ಕೋಕ|koka_2|koka|_vei_120@727|_vei1_pn189
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@726">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@728">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn188">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn189">1:189</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn190">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Koka</b> is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 5, 4, 17) as a son of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a> king, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सात्रासाह">sātrāsāha</a>.</b></p></div>

कोकिल|కోకిల|ಕೋಕಿಲ|kokila|_vei_120@728|_vei1_pn189
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@727">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@729">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn188">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn189">1:189</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn190">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kokila,</b> a very frequent word in the Epic and later, denoting the cuckoo, is only inferred for the Vedic period from its being the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="राजपुत्र">rājaputra</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Anukramaṇī.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120728"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120728">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120728"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120728">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 460.</li></ol></div>

कोणेय|कौणेय|కోణేయ|కౌణేయ|ಕೋಣೇಯ|ಕೌಣೇಯ|koṇeya|kauṇeya|koNeya|kauNeya|_vei_120@729|_vei1_pn189
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@728">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@730">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn188">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn189">1:189</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn190">↷</a><hr><p><b>Koṇeya, Kauṇeya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रजन">rajana</a>.</b></p></div>

कोल|కోల|ಕೋಲ|kola|_vei_120@730|_vei1_pn189
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@729">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@731">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn188">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn189">1:189</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn190">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kola,</b> another form of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुवल">kuvala</a>,</b> the <i>Zizyphus jujuba,</i> is mentioned in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vii. 3, 1).</p></div>

कोश|कोश|కోశ_౧|కోశ|ಕೋಶ_೧|ಕೋಶ|kośa_1|kośa|kosha_1|kosha|_vei_120@731|_vei1_pn189
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@730">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@732">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn188">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn189">1:189</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn190">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Kośa</b> is the name in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120731"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120731">1</a></sup> for the ‘bucket’ used in drawing water by means of a rope from a well (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अवत">avata</a></b>). In the ritual<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120731"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120731">2</a></sup> it denotes a large vessel to hold <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>, as opposed to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कलश">kalaśa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120731"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120731">↑</a> i. 130, 2; iii. 32, 15; iv. 17, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 156.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120731"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120731">↑</a> Rv. ix. 75. 3; Av. xviii. 4. 30, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 183 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

कोस|कोस|కోస_౨|కోస|ಕೋಸ_೨|ಕೋಸ|kosa_2|kosa|_vei_120@732|_vei1_pn189
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@731">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@733">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn188">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn189">1:189</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn190">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Kosa</b> denotes the body of a chariot.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120732"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120732">1</a></sup> Presumably it was fastened to the axles, but it was probably not very secure, as the body of Pūṣan's chariot is said not to fall.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120732"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120732">2</a></sup> The ropes<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120732"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120732">3</a></sup> used to fasten the <a class="vei_120a" href="कोश">kośa</a> are perhaps referred to in the word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्षा">akṣā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="नह्">nah</a>.</i><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120732"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120732">4</a></sup> By synecdoche this word also denotes the whole chariot.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120732"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120732">5</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वन्धुर">vandhura</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120732"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120732">↑</a> Rv. i. 87, 2; x. 85, 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120732"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120732">↑</a> Rv. vi. 54, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120732"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120732">↑</a> <i>Gāvaḥ,</i> Rv. viii. 48, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120732"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120732">↑</a> See under 1. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120732"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120732">↑</a> Rv. viii. 20, 8; 22, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 246.</li></ol></div>

कोश|कोश|కోశ_౩|కోశ|ಕೋಶ_೩|ಕೋಶ|kośa_3|kośa|kosha_3|kosha|_vei_120@733|_vei1_pn190
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@732">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@734">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn189">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn190">1:190</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn191">↷</a><hr><p>3. <b>Kośa.</b>--The exact sense of this word in <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कोश">kośa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कारी">kārī</a>,</b> the designation of a female victim at the Puruṣamedha, or human sacrifice,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120733"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120733">1</a></sup> is uncertain. It may be ‘sheath.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120733"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120733">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 14; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 10, 1.</li></ol></div>

कोष|కోష|ಕೋಷ|koṣa|koSha|_vei_120@734|_vei1_pn190
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@733">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@735">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn189">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn190">1:190</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn191">↷</a><hr><p><b>Koṣa.</b>--The Koṣas appear as a priestly family in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, where one of them, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुश्रवस्">suśravas</a>,</b> is mentioned by name.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120734"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120734">2</a></sup></p><p>^1 x. 5, 5, 8.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="2"><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120734"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120734">↑</a> x. 5, 5, 1.</li></ol></div>

कोसल|కోసల|ಕೋಸಲ|kosala|_vei_120@735|_vei1_pn190
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@734">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@736">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn189">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn190">1:190</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn191">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kosala</b> is the name of a people not occurring in the earliest Vedic literature. In the story of the spread of Āryan culture told in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120735">1</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a>-Videhas, as the offspring of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विदेघ">videgha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="माथव">māthava</a>,</b> appear as falling later than the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas under the influence of Brahminism. The same passage gives the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सदानीरा">sadānīrā</a></b> as the boundary of the two peoples --<a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>. Elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120735">2</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौसल्य">kausalya</a>, or <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a> king, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर आट्णार">para āṭṇāra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="हैरण्यनाभ">hairaṇyanābha</a>,</b> is described as having performed the great <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or horse sacrifice. Connexion with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a></b> appears also from a passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120735">3</a></sup> Weber<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120735">4</a></sup> points out that Āśvalāyana, who was very probably a descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वल">aśvala</a>,</b> the <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a> priest of <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>, is called a <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रश्न">praśna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120735">5</a></sup> The later distinction of North and South <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a> is unknown to both Vedic and Buddhist literature.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120735">6</a></sup></p><p><a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a> lay to the north-east of the Ganges, and corresponded roughly to the modern Oudh.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120735">↑</a> i. 4, 1, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120735">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 4, 4. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्य-नाभ">hiraṇya-nābha</a>, a <a class="vei_120a" href="राजपुत्र">rājaputra</a>, in <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रश्न">praśna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 2, and <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 9, 13, as a <a class="vei_120a" href="कौसल्य">kausalya</a>, while <i>ibid.,</i> 11, Para is styled <a class="vei_120a" href="वैदेह">vaideha</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120735">↑</a> xvi. 29, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120735">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 182, 441.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120735">↑</a> vi. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120735">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 393, n.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 167; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, xlii; Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 39, 132 <i>et seq.;</i> Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 213-215; Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India,</i> 25.</li></ol></div>

कौकूस्त|కౌకూస్త|ಕೌಕೂಸ್ತ|kaukūsta|kaukuusta|kaukoosta|_vei_120@736|_vei1_pn190
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@735">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@737">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn189">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn190">1:190</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn191">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaukūsta</b> is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120736"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120736">1</a></sup> as a giver of a Daksiṇā, or gift to the priests officiating at a sacrifice. The <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension reads the name <b>Kaükthasta.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120736"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120736">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120736"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120736">↑</a> iv. 6, 1, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120736"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120736">↑</a> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 426, n. 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 134.</li></ol></div>

कौणेय|కౌణేయ|ಕೌಣೇಯ|kauṇeya|kauNeya|_vei_120@737|_vei1_pn191
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@736">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@738">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn190">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn191">1:191</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn192">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kauṇeya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रजन">rajana</a>.</b></p></div>

कौण्ठ-रव्य|कौण्ठरव्य|कौण्ठ|रव्य|కౌణ్ఠ-రవ్య|కౌణ్ఠరవ్య|కౌణ్ఠ|రవ్య|ಕೌಣ್ಠ-ರವ್ಯ|ಕೌಣ್ಠರವ್ಯ|ಕೌಣ್ಠ|ರವ್ಯ|kauṇṭha-ravya|kauṇṭharavya|kauṇṭha|ravya|kauNTha-ravya|kauNTharavya|kauNTha|_vei_120@738|_vei1_pn191
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@737">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@739">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn190">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn191">1:191</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn192">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kauṇṭha-ravya</b> is mentioned as a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120738"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120738">1</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyakas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120738"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120738">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120738"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120738">↑</a> iii. 2, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120738"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120738">↑</a> vii. 14; viii. 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 249.</li></ol></div>

कौण्डिनी|कौण्डिनि|కౌణ్డినీ|కౌణ్డిని|ಕೌಣ್ಡಿನೀ|ಕೌಣ್ಡಿನಿ|kauṇḍinī|kauṇḍini|kauNDinii|kauNDini|kauNDinee|_vei_120@739|_vei1_pn191
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@738">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@740">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn190">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn191">1:191</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn192">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kauṇḍinī.</b> See <b>Pārāśarīkauṇḍinīputra.</b></p></div>

कौण्डिन्य|కౌణ్డిన్య|ಕೌಣ್ಡಿನ್ಯ|kauṇḍinya|kauNDinya|_vei_120@740|_vei1_pn191
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@739">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@741">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn190">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn191">1:191</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn192">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kauṇḍinya</b> is mentioned as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्डिल्य">śāṇḍilya</a></b> in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120740"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120740">1</a></sup> See also <b>Vidarbhīkauṇḍinya,</b> and the following.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120740"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120740">↑</a> ii. 5, 20; iv. 5, 26 (Mādhyaṃdina = ii. 6, 1; iv. 6, 1, <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

कौण्डिन्यायन|కౌణ్డిన్యాయన|ಕೌಣ್ಡಿನ್ಯಾಯನ|kauṇḍinyāyana|kauNDinyaayana|_vei_120@741|_vei1_pn191
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@740">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@742">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn190">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn191">1:191</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn192">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kauṇḍinyāyana</b> is mentioned in the first <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Mādhyaṃdina recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120741"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120741">1</a></sup> as a pupil of <a class="vei_120a" href="कौण्डिन्य">kauṇḍinya</a>, pupil of <a class="vei_120a" href="कौण्डिन्य">kauṇḍinya</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="आग्निवेश्य">āgniveśya</a>; in the second<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120741"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120741">2</a></sup> as a pupil of the two Kauṇḍinyas, pupil of <a class="vei_120a" href="कौण्डिन्य">kauṇḍinya</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="आग्निवेश्य">āgniveśya</a>. Neither <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> is of much value.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120741"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120741">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120741"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120741">↑</a> ii. 5, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120741"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120741">↑</a> iv. 5, 26.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120741"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120741">↑</a> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, xxxiv.</li></ol></div>

कौतस्त|కౌతస్త|ಕೌತಸ್ತ|kautasta|_vei_120@742|_vei1_pn191
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@741">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@743">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn190">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn191">1:191</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn192">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kautasta,</b> a word occurring once in the dual, is apparently a patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अरिमेजय">arimejaya</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनमेजय">janamejaya</a>,</b> two <a class="vei_120a" href="अध्वर्यु">adhvaryu</a> priests at the snake sacrifice described in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120742"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120742">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120742"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120742">↑</a> xxv. 15, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 35.</li></ol></div>

कौत्स|కౌత్స|ಕೌತ್ಸ|kautsa|_vei_120@743|_vei1_pn191
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@742">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@744">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn190">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn191">1:191</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn192">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kautsa</b> (‘descendant of Kutsa’) is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120743">1</a></sup> as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="माहित्थि">māhitthi</a>.</b> A <a class="vei_120a" href="कौत्स">kautsa</a> is also attacked in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120743">2</a></sup> as denying the value of the Vedas, and there is a strong ritual tradition of hostility to the Kautsas.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120743">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120743">↑</a> x. 6, 5, 9; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 5, 4 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension only).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120743">↑</a> i. 15.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120743">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 20, 12; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 285. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 77, 140.</li></ol></div>

कौत्सी-पुत्र|कौत्सीपुत्र|कौत्सी|कौत्सि|पुत्र|కౌత్సీ-పుత్ర|కౌత్సీపుత్ర|కౌత్సీ|కౌత్సి|పుత్ర|ಕೌತ್ಸೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಕೌತ್ಸೀಪುತ್ರ|ಕೌತ್ಸೀ|ಕೌತ್ಸಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|kautsī-putra|kautsīputra|kautsī|kautsi|putra|kautsii-putra|kautsiiputra|kautsii|kautsee-putra|kautseeputra|kautsee|_vei_120@744|_vei1_pn192
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@743">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@745">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn191">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn192">1:192</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn193">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kautsī-putra</b> (‘son of a female descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुत्स">kutsa</a></b>’) is mentioned as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बौधीपुत्र">baudhīputra</a></b> in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> in the Mādhyaṃdina recension (vi. 4, 31).</p></div>

कौपयेय|కౌపయేయ|ಕೌಪಯೇಯ|kaupayeya|_vei_120@745|_vei1_pn192
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@744">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@746">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn191">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn192">1:192</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn193">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaupayeya</b> is the patronymic of <b>Uccaiḥśravas.</b></p></div>

कौम्भ्य|कौंभ्य|కౌమ్భ్య|కౌంభ్య|ಕೌಮ್ಭ್ಯ|ಕೌಂಭ್ಯ|kaumbhya|_vei_120@746|_vei1_pn192
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@745">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@747">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn191">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn192">1:192</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn193">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaumbhya</b> (‘descendant of Kumbhya’) is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बभ्रु">babhru</a></b></p></div>

कौरम|కౌరమ|ಕೌರಮ|kaurama|_vei_120@747|_vei1_pn192
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@746">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@748">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn191">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn192">1:192</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn193">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaurama.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौरव">kaurava</a>.</b></p></div>

कौरयाण|కౌరయాణ|ಕೌರಯಾಣ|kaurayāṇa|kaurayaaNa|_vei_120@748|_vei1_pn192
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@747">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@749">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn191">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn192">1:192</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn193">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaurayāṇa</b> is apparently a patronymic of <b>Pākasthāman</b> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120748"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120748">1</a></sup> Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120748"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120748">2</a></sup> suggests that Kaurāyaṇa may be meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120748"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120748">↑</a> viii. 3, 21. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 25.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120748"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120748">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 90, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

कौरव|కౌరవ|ಕೌರವ|kaurava|_vei_120@749|_vei1_pn192
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@748">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@750">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn191">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn192">1:192</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn193">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaurava</b> is the reading of the text of the Khilas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120749"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120749">1</a></sup> and of some manuscripts of the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120749"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120749">2</a></sup> for the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौरम">kaurama</a> of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120749"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120749">3</a></sup> who appears in a Dānastuti (‘Praise of Gifts’) as a generous donor among the <b>Ruśamas.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120749"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120749">↑</a> v. 8, 1 (Scheftelowitz, <i>Die Apokryphen des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 155).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120749"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120749">↑</a> xii. 14, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120749"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120749">↑</a> xx. 127, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 689.</li></ol></div>

कौरव्य|కౌరవ్య|ಕೌರವ್ಯ|kauravya|_vei_120@750|_vei1_pn192
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@749">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@751">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn191">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn192">1:192</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn193">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kauravya</b> (‘belonging to the <b>Kurus’</b>). A man of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a> people, is described in the Atharvaveda as enjoying prosperity with his wife under the rule of King <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परिक्षित्">parikṣit</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120750"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120750">1</a></sup> Mention is also made of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौरव्य">kauravya</a> king <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बल्हिक प्रातिपीय">balhika prātipīya</a></b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120750"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120750">2</a></sup> and in the later legend <b>Ārṣṭiṣeṇa</b> and <b>Devāpi</b> are alleged to have been Kauravyas.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120750"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120750">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120750"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120750">↑</a> xx. 127, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="खिल">khila</a>, v. 10, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 17, 2. <i>Cf.</i> a <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> in the Vaitāna <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxxiv. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120750"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120750">↑</a> xii. 9, 3, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120750"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120750">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 10.</li></ol></div>

कौरव्यायणी-पुत्र|कौरव्यायणीपुत्र|कौरव्यायणी|कौरव्यायणि|पुत्र|కౌరవ్యాయణీ-పుత్ర|కౌరవ్యాయణీపుత్ర|కౌరవ్యాయణీ|కౌరవ్యాయణి|పుత్ర|ಕೌರವ್ಯಾಯಣೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಕೌರವ್ಯಾಯಣೀಪುತ್ರ|ಕೌರವ್ಯಾಯಣೀ|ಕೌರವ್ಯಾಯಣಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|kauravyāyaṇī-putra|kauravyāyaṇīputra|kauravyāyaṇī|kauravyāyaṇi|putra|kauravyaayaNii-putra|kauravyaayaNiiputra|kauravyaayaNii|kauravyaayaNi|kauravyaayaNee-putra|kauravyaayaNeeputra|kauravyaayaNee|_vei_120@751|_vei1_pn192
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@750">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@752">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn191">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn192">1:192</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn193">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kauravyāyaṇī-putra</b> (‘son of a female descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a></b>’) is mentioned as a teacher in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (v. 1, 1). </p></div>

कौरु-पञ्चाल|कौरुपञ्चाल|कौरु|पञ्चाल|కౌరు-పఞ్చాల|కౌరుపఞ్చాల|కౌరు|పఞ్చాల|ಕೌರು-ಪಞ್ಚಾಲ|ಕೌರುಪಞ್ಚಾಲ|ಕೌರು|ಪಞ್ಚಾಲ|kauru-pañcāla|kaurupañcāla|kauru|pañcāla|kauru-pa~nchaala|kaurupa~nchaala|pa~nchaala|_vei_120@752|_vei1_pn193
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@751">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@753">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn192">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn193">1:193</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn194">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kauru-pañcāla,</b> ‘belonging to the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas,’ is an epithet of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आरुणि">āruṇi</a></b> in the Śatapatha Brāhmana (xi. 4, 1, 2), and a practice of those tribes is designated by this word in the same work (i. 7, 2, 8).</p></div>

कौलकावती|कौलकावति|కౌలకావతీ|కౌలకావతి|ಕೌಲಕಾವತೀ|ಕೌಲಕಾವತಿ|kaulakāvatī|kaulakāvati|kaulakaavatii|kaulakaavati|kaulakaavatee|_vei_120@753|_vei1_pn193
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@752">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@754">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn192">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn193">1:193</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn194">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaulakāvatī</b> are two persons mentioned in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā (ii. 1, 3) as having given advice, in the capacity of priests, to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथप्रोत दार्भ्य">rathaprota dārbhya</a>.</b></p></div>

कौलाल|కౌలాల|ಕೌಲಾಲ|kaulāla|kaulaala|_vei_120@754|_vei1_pn193
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@753">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@755">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn192">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn193">1:193</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn194">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaulāla</b> is a word denoting a hereditary potter (‘son of a <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कुलाल">kulāla</a></i> or potter’) according to the commentator Mahīdhara on the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120754">1</a></sup> The other Samhitās<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120754">2</a></sup> have <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुलाल">kulāla</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120754">↑</a> xxx. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120754">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 13, and <i>cf.</i> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 27.</li></ol></div>

कौलितर|కౌలితర|ಕೌಲಿತರ|kaulitara|_vei_120@755|_vei1_pn193
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@754">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@756">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn192">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn193">1:193</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn194">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaulitara</b> is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120755"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120755">1</a></sup> as a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>.</b> Apparently the name is an epithet of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शम्बर">śambara</a>,</b> meaning ‘son of Kulitara’: this points to <a class="vei_120a" href="शम्बर">śambara</a> having been a terrestrial foe, and not a mere demon.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120755"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120755">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120755"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120755">↑</a> iv. 30. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120755"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120755">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 273; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> pp. 64, 161.</li></ol></div>

कौलीक|కౌలీక|ಕೌಲೀಕ|kaulīka|kauliika|kauleeka|_vei_120@756|_vei1_pn193
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@755">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@757">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn192">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn193">1:193</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn194">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kaulīka,</b> like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुलीका">kulīkā</a>,</b> is the name of some sort of bird in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or horse sacrifice, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120756"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120756">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120756"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120756">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 24; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 5.</li></ol></div>

कौशाम्बेय|कौशांबेय|కౌశామ్బేయ|కౌశాంబేయ|ಕೌಶಾಮ್ಬೇಯ|ಕೌಶಾಂಬೇಯ|kauśāmbeya|kaushaambeya|_vei_120@757|_vei1_pn193
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@756">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@758">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn192">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn193">1:193</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn194">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kauśāmbeya</b> is the patronymic (‘descendant of Kuśāmba’) of a teacher <b>Proti</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120757">1</a></sup> according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary: a view supported by the fact that <b>Kūśāmba</b> actually occurs as the name of a man in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120757">2</a></sup> It is, however, possible that the word means a ‘native of the town Kauśāmbī’ as understood by Harisvāmin in his commentary on the Śatapatha Brāhmana.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120757">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120757">↑</a> xii. 2, 2, 13; Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120757">↑</a> viii. 6, 8. The name is also found later (in the Epic) in the form of Kuśāmba.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120757">↑</a> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 153, n. 5. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 193; Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India,</i> 3, 36; Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 397.</li></ol></div>

कौशिक|కౌశిక|ಕೌಶಿಕ|kauśika|kaushika|_vei_120@758|_vei1_pn194
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@757">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@759">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn193">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn194">1:194</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn195">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kauśika</b> is an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> as ‘connected with the Kuśikas,’ and also of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a></b> as ‘son of <a class="vei_120a" href="कुशिक">kuśika</a>.’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120758"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120758">1</a></sup> A teacher named <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> is mentioned as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौण्डिन्य">kauṇḍinya</a></b> in the first two Vaṃśias (lists of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120758"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120758">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120758"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120758">↑</a> In a late <a class="vei_120a" href="खिल">khila</a>, Scheftelowitz, <i>Die Apokryphen des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 104.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120758"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120758">↑</a> ii. 6, 1; iv. 6, 1 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension).</li></ol></div>

कौशिकायनि|కౌశికాయని|ಕೌಶಿಕಾಯನಿ|kauśikāyani|kaushikaayani|_vei_120@759|_vei1_pn194
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@758">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@760">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn193">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn194">1:194</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn195">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kauśikāyani</b> (‘descendant of Kauśika’) is mentioned as a teacher and a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="घृतकौशिक">ghṛtakauśika</a></b> in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120759"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120759">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120759"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120759">↑</a> ii. 5, 21; iv. 5, 27 (Mādhyaṃdina = ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3, <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

कौशिकी-पुत्र|कौशिकीपुत्र|कौशिकी|कौशिकि|पुत्र|కౌశికీ-పుత్ర|కౌశికీపుత్ర|కౌశికీ|కౌశికి|పుత్ర|ಕೌಶಿಕೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಕೌಶಿಕೀಪುತ್ರ|ಕೌಶಿಕೀ|ಕೌಶಿಕಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|kauśikī-putra|kauśikīputra|kauśikī|kauśiki|putra|kaushikii-putra|kaushikiiputra|kaushikii|kaushiki|kaushikee-putra|kaushikeeputra|kaushikee|_vei_120@760|_vei1_pn194
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@759">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@761">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn193">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn194">1:194</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn195">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kauśikī-putra</b> (‘son of a female descendant of Kuśika’) is mentioned in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 5, 1) as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आलम्बीपुत्र">ālambīputra</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैयाघ्रपदीपुत्र">vaiyāghrapadīputra</a>.</b></p></div>

कौश्रेय|కౌశ్రేయ|ಕೌಶ್ರೇಯ|kauśreya|kaushreya|_vei_120@761|_vei1_pn194
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@760">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@762">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn193">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn194">1:194</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn195">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kauśreya</b> (‘descendant of Kuśri’) is the patronymic of <b>Somadakṣa</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā (xx. 8; xxi. 9).</p></div>

कौषारव|కౌషారవ|ಕೌಷಾರವ|kauṣārava|kauShaarava|_vei_120@762|_vei1_pn194
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@761">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@763">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn193">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn194">1:194</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn195">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kauṣārava</b> (‘descendant of Kuṣāru’) is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मैत्रेय">maitreya</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 28).</p></div>

कौषीतकि|కౌషీతకి|ಕೌಷೀತಕಿ|kauṣītaki|kauShiitaki|kauSheetaki|_vei_120@763|_vei1_pn194
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@762">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@764">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn193">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn194">1:194</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn195">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kauṣītaki</b> (‘descendant of Kuṣītaka’) is the patronymic of a teacher, or series of teachers, to whom the doctrines set forth in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120763">1</a></sup> and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120763">2</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> Sūtras,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120763">3</a></sup> are referred. He is rarely mentioned elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120763">4</a></sup> The doctrine of <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> is called the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतक">kauṣītaka</a>.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120763">5</a></sup> The pupils of <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> are known as the Kauṣītakis in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120763">6</a></sup> and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120763">7</a></sup>  they with <a class="vei_120a" href="कुषीतक">kuṣītaka</a> are stated to have been cursed by <b>Luśākapi.</b> Elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120763">8</a></sup> they are called Kauṣītakins. If the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120763">9</a></sup> can be trusted, there were among them at least two leading teachers, <b>Kahoḍa</b> and <b>Sarvajit,</b> the former of whom is mentioned elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120763">10</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120763">↑</a> ii. 9; vii. 4, 10; viii. 8; xi. 5. 7; xiv. 3. 4; xv. 2; xvi. 9; xviii. 5; xxii. 1. 2; xxiii. 1. 4; xxiv. 8. 9; xxv. 8. 10. 14. 15, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120763">↑</a> ii. 17: xv. 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 1. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120763">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 15, 11; vii. 21, 6; ix. 20, 33; xi. 11, 3. 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120763">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 3, 1; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 5, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120763">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1; xix. 3; Sāṅkhāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 2, 13; xi. 14, 26; Anupada <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 7: vii. 11 viii. 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120763">↑</a> vi. 12.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120763">↑</a> xvii. 4, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120763">↑</a> Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 1. 10; Āśvalāvana Grhya <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 23.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120763">↑</a> <i>Cf</i> Kelth, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 14, 24, 71</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120763">↑</a> Sarapatna <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 3, 1; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 4, 1; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 259; 2, 289 <i>et seq.; Indian Literature,</i> 44 <i>et seq.;</i> Lindner, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,</i> ix.</li></ol></div>

कौष्य|కౌష్య|ಕೌಷ್ಯ|kauṣya|kauShya|_vei_120@764|_vei1_pn195
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@763">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@765">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn194">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn195">1:195</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn196">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kauṣya,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="कोष">koṣa</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुश्रवस्">suśravas</a>.</b></p></div>

कौसल्य|కౌసల్య|ಕೌಸಲ್ಯ|kausalya|_vei_120@765|_vei1_pn195
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@764">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@766">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn194">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn195">1:195</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn196">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kausalya,</b> ‘prince of <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a>,’ is the designation of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर आट्णार">para āṭṇāra</a></b> in the Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120765"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120765">1</a></sup> and of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्यनाभ">hiraṇyanābha</a></b> in the Sāṅkhāvana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120765"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120765">2</a></sup> Āśvalāyana is styled <a class="vei_120a" href="कौसल्य">kausalya</a>, as ‘belonging to the <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a> country,’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रश्न">praśna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120765"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120765">3</a></sup> and the Kaśī-Kausalyāḥ, or the ‘Kāśis and people of <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a>,’ are mentioned in the Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120765"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120765">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120765"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120765">↑</a> xiii 5, 4, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120765"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120765">↑</a> xvi. 9, 13. <i>Cf.</i> xvi. 29, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120765"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120765">↑</a> i. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120765"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120765">↑</a> i. 2, 9 (spelt <i>Kauśalyāḥ</i>).</li></ol></div>

कौसित|కౌసిత|ಕೌಸಿತ|kausita|_vei_120@766|_vei1_pn195
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@765">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@767">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn194">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn195">1:195</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn196">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kausita</b> appears in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā (ii. 1, 11) in connexion with the demon Kusitāyin as the name of a lake. The <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā (x. 5) has Kausida instead.</p></div>

कौसुरुबिन्दि|కౌసురుబిన్ది|ಕೌಸುರುಬಿನ್ದಿ|kausurubindi|_vei_120@767|_vei1_pn195
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@766">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@768">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn194">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn195">1:195</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn196">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kausurubindi,</b> ‘descendant of <b>Kusurubinda,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b>Proti <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशाम्बेय">kauśāmbeya</a></b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xii. 2, 2, 13). In the Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 4, 24) the form is Kausuravindu.</p></div>

कौहड|కౌహడ|ಕೌಹಡ|kauhaḍa|kauhaDa|_vei_120@768|_vei1_pn195
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@767">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@769">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn194">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn195">1:195</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn196">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kauhaḍa,</b> ‘descendant of Kohaḍa,’ is the patronymic of a teacher, <b>Mitravinda,</b> mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120768"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120768">1</a></sup> as well as of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्रवणदत्त">śravaṇadatta</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120768"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120768">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372, 382 <i>et seq.</i> A school of Kauhaḍīyas is known in the Gobhila <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 4, 34.</li></ol></div>

क्रतु-जित् जानकि|क्रतुजित् जानकि|क्रतु|जित् जानकि|క్రతు-జిత్ జానకి|క్రతుజిత్ జానకి|క్రతు|జిత్ జానకి|ಕ್ರತು-ಜಿತ್ ಜಾನಕಿ|ಕ್ರತುಜಿತ್ ಜಾನಕಿ|ಕ್ರತು|ಜಿತ್ ಜಾನಕಿ|kratu-jit jānaki|kratujit jānaki|kratu|jit jānaki|kratu-jit jaanaki|kratujit jaanaki|jit jaanaki|_vei_120@769|_vei1_pn195
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@768">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@770">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn194">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn195">1:195</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn196">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kratu-<a class="vei_120a" href="जित्">jit</a> Jānaki</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a></b>’) is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120769"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120769">1</a></sup> as the priest of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रजन">rajana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौणेय">kauṇeya</a>.</b> See also <b>Kratuvid.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120769"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120769">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 8, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 1. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 474.</li></ol></div>

क्रतु-विद् जानकि|क्रतुविद् जानकि|क्रतु|विद् जानकि|క్రతు-విద్ జానకి|క్రతువిద్ జానకి|క్రతు|విద్ జానకి|ಕ್ರತು-ವಿದ್ ಜಾನಕಿ|ಕ್ರತುವಿದ್ ಜಾನಕಿ|ಕ್ರತು|ವಿದ್ ಜಾನಕಿ|kratu-vid jānaki|kratuvid jānaki|kratu|vid jānaki|kratu-vid jaanaki|kratuvid jaanaki|vid jaanaki|_vei_120@770|_vei1_pn196
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@769">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@771">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn195">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn196">1:196</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn197">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kratu-<a class="vei_120a" href="विद्">vid</a> Jānaki</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a></b>’) is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vii. 34) as having learned a certain doctrine regarding <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> from <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>.</p></div>

क्रय|క్రయ|ಕ್ರಯ|kraya|_vei_120@771|_vei1_pn196
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@770">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@772">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn195">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn196">1:196</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn197">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kraya,</b> ‘sale,’ is a word which does not actually occur in the Rigveda, though the verb <i>krī,</i> from which this noun is derived, is found there.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120771">1</a></sup> Both noun and verb are common in the later Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120771">2</a></sup> Sale appears to have regularly consisted in barter in the Rigveda:<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120771">3</a></sup> ten cows are regarded as a possible price for an (image of) <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> to be used as a fetish, while elsewhere not a hundred, nor a thousand, nor a myriad are considered as an adequate price (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शुल्क">śulka</a></i>) for the purchase of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120771">4</a></sup> The Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120771">5</a></sup> mentions, as possible objects of commerce, garments (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दूर्श">dūrśa</a></i>), coverlets (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पवस्त">pavasta</a></i>), and goatskins (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अजिन">ajina</a></i>). The haggling of the market was already familiar in the days of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120771">6</a></sup> and a characteristic hymn of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120771">7</a></sup> is directed to procuring success in trade. The ‘price’ was called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वस्न">vasna</a>,</b> and the ‘merchant’ <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वणिज्">vaṇij</a>.</b> his greed being well known.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120771">8</a></sup></p><p>There is little evidence of a standard of value in currency having been adopted. When no specific mention is made of the standard, the unit was probably the cow.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120771">9</a></sup> In a considerable number of passages of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120771">10</a></sup> and elsewhere,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120771">11</a></sup> however, the expression <i>hiraṇyaṃ <a class="vei_120a" href="शत">śata</a>-mānam</i> suggests that there must have been some standard other than  cows, though it might in all these passages be rendered as ‘gold worth a hundred cows.’ But the use of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्णल">kṛṣṇala</a></b> as a measure of weight<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120771">12</a></sup> suggests that the meaning is ‘gold weighing a hundred Kṛṣṇalas,’ and this seems the more probable explanation. This unit seems not to be known in the Rigveda, where the meaning of the term <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मना">manā</a>,</b> which occurs once, is mysterious, and where necklets (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="निष्क">niṣka</a></b>) seem to have been one of the more portable forms of wealth, like jewellery in modern India, and may perhaps have served as a means of exchange.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120771">↑</a> iv. 24, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120771">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रय">kraya</a>:</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 1, 2, 1; vi. 1, 3, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, viii. 55; xix. 13; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 2, 10, etc.; <i>krī:</i> Av. iii. 15, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 10, 3; vii. 1, 6, 2, etc.; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अप">apa</a>-krī:</i> Av. viii. 7, 11; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="परि">pari</a>-krī:</i> Av. iv. 7, 6, etc.; <i>vi-krī:</i> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iii. 49, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120771">↑</a> iv. 24, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120771">↑</a> Rv. viii. 1, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120771">↑</a> iv. 7, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120771">↑</a> iv. 24, 9. See Oldenberg, <i>ṚgvedaNoten,</i> 1, 419, 420, correcting Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 91, and Geldner's <i>Kommentar</i> on iv. 24.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120771">↑</a> iii. 15. See Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 352; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 111, 112.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120771">↑</a> Rv. i. 33, 3, and See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पणि">paṇi</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120771">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Harisvāmin on Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 2, 1, where he renders <i>sahasrārha</i> as equal to ‘worth a thousand cows,’ in which Eggeling follows him; Saṃkṣiptasāra on Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxii. 10, 33.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120771">↑</a> xii. 7, 2, 13; 9, 1, 4; xiii. 1, 1, 4; 2, 3, 2; 4, 1, 13; xiv. 3, 1, 32. <i>Cf.</i> v. 5, 5, 16; xiii. 4, 1, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120771">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xviii. 3, 2, where a long series of compounds of numerals with <i>-<a class="vei_120a" href="मान">māna</a></i> occurs; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, viii. 5; xiv. 8; xxii. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120771">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 6, 7; Anupada <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ix. 6: Weber, <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 99-103.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 255260. Barter had for the most part passed away by the time of the Jātakas, an illustration of the modern character of the society they represent. See Mrs. Rhys Davids, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1901, 874 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

क्रवण|క్రవణ|ಕ್ರವಣ|kravaṇa|kravaNa|_vei_120@772|_vei1_pn197
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@771">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@773">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn196">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn197">1:197</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn198">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kravaṇa,</b> a word occurring only once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120772"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120772">1</a></sup> is understood by Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120772"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120772">2</a></sup> as the name of the <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a> priest or the sacrificer. Roth considered it an adjective without at first<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120772"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120772">3</a></sup> assigning a sense, but afterwards<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120772"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120772">4</a></sup> as meaning ‘timid.’ Sāyaṇa interprets it as ‘worshipping.’ Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120772"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120772">5</a></sup> considers the meaning uncertain, suggesting as possible ‘the slayer of the victim.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120772"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120772">↑</a> v. 44. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120772"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120772">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 138.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120772"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120772">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120772"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120772">↑</a> Bo7htlingk's Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120772"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120772">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 342.</li></ol></div>

क्रव्य|క్రవ్య|ಕ್ರವ್ಯ|kravya|_vei_120@773|_vei1_pn197
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@772">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@774">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn196">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn197">1:197</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn198">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kravya,</b> ‘raw flesh,’ is never mentioned in Vedic literature as eaten by men. Demons alone are spoken of as consuming it,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120773"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120773">1</a></sup> apart from <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> being called <i>kravyād,</i> ‘eating raw flesh,’ as consumer of the bodies of the dead.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120773"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120773">2</a></sup> The man who in the Rigveda is compelled by starvation to eat dog's flesh, nevertheless cooks it.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120773"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120773">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120773"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120773">↑</a> Rv. vii. 104, 2; x. 87, 2. 19; 162, 2; Av. iii. 28, 2; iv. 36, 3: v. 29 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120773"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120773">↑</a> Rv. x. 16, 9. 10. See Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> pp. 97, 165.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120773"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120773">↑</a> iv. 18, 13.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 270, 271.</li></ol></div>

क्रातु-जातेय|क्रातुजातेय|क्रातु|जातेय|క్రాతు-జాతేయ|క్రాతుజాతేయ|క్రాతు|జాతేయ|ಕ್ರಾತು-ಜಾತೇಯ|ಕ್ರಾತುಜಾತೇಯ|ಕ್ರಾತು|ಜಾತೇಯ|krātu-jāteya|krātujāteya|krātu|jāteya|kraatu-jaateya|kraatujaateya|kraatu|jaateya|_vei_120@774|_vei1_pn197
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@773">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@775">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn196">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn197">1:197</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn198">↷</a><hr><p><b>Krātu-jāteya</b> is a patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राम क्रातुजातेय">rāma krātujāteya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वैयाघ्रपद्य">vaiyāghrapadya</a></b> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 40, 2; iv. 16, 1).</p></div>

क्रिमि|క్రిమి|ಕ್ರಿಮಿ|krimi|_vei_120@775|_vei1_pn197
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@774">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@776">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn196">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn197">1:197</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn198">↷</a><hr><p><b>Krimi.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृमि">kṛmi</a>.</b> </p></div>

क्रिवि|క్రివి|ಕ್ರಿವಿ|krivi|_vei_120@776|_vei1_pn198
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@775">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@777">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn197">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn198">1:198</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn199">↷</a><hr><p><b>Krivi</b> is asserted in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120776">1</a></sup> to have been the older name of the <b>Pañcālas.</b> This statement is supported by the name of the king there mentioned, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रैव्य.पाञ्चाल">kraivya.pāñcāla</a>,</b> The Krivis appear in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120776">2</a></sup> as settled on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धु">sindhu</a></b> and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="असिक्नी">asiknī</a>.</b> It is a plausible conjecture of Zimmer's<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120776">3</a></sup> that with the <b>Kurus</b> they made up the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैकर्ण">vaikarṇa</a></b> people.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120776">4</a></sup> The importance of the Pañcālas, and the insignificance of the Krivis, may be explained in part by the fact that the later <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a> alliance included the <b>Bharatas.</b> It is also probable, as Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120776">5</a></sup> suggests, from the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120776">6</a></sup> that the <b>Turvaśas</b> were included in the Pañcālas, and as the latter name indicates, probably other tribes also. Or, if Hopkins' view<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120776">7</a></sup> is accepted that <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> was king of the Yadus, the latter may in part have been allied with the Krivis to form the Pañcālas.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120776">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120776">↑</a> viii. 20, 24; 22, 12. ElsewhereKrivi is doubtful in sense. In several passages (i. 30, 1; viii. 87, 1; ix. 9, 6, and perhaps i. 166, 6, where <i>krivir-datī</i> is an epithet of lightning) Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> i. 166, 341, understands the word to mean ‘horse.’ Elsewhere (ii. 17, 6; 22, 2; viii. 51, 8) he takes it to be a proper name, while in v. 44, 4, he is doubtful. In the passages last cited this view may very well be correct.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120776">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 103.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120776">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कवष">kavaṣa</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120776">↑</a> <i>Buddha,</i> 404.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120776">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120776">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 258 <i>et seq.</i> This view is hardly convincing, while the disappearance of the Turvaśas is easily to be accounted for by their being merged, along with the Krivis, in the Pañcālas. The name of <a class="vei_120a" href="क्रिवि">krivi</a> is lost in the Epic as completely as that of <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> (Pargiter, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1910, 48, notes 4, 5).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 155, 157; Grierson, <i>Journal of the Royal Astatic Society,</i> 1908, 602-607; Keith, <i>ibid.,</i> 831 <i>et seq.;</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 152, 153; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, xli.; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 407.</li></ol></div>

क्रीत वैत-होत्र|क्रीत वैतहोत्र|क्रीत वैत|होत्र|క్రీత వైత-హోత్ర|క్రీత వైతహోత్ర|క్రీత వైత|హోత్ర|ಕ್ರೀತ ವೈತ-ಹೋತ್ರ|ಕ್ರೀತ ವೈತಹೋತ್ರ|ಕ್ರೀತ ವೈತ|ಹೋತ್ರ|krīta vaita-hotra|krīta vaitahotra|krīta vaita|hotra|kriita vaita-hotra|kriita vaitahotra|kriita vaita|kreeta vaita-hotra|kreeta vaitahotra|kreeta vaita|_vei_120@777|_vei1_pn198
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@776">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@778">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn197">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn198">1:198</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn199">↷</a><hr><p><b>Krīta Vaita-hotra</b> (‘descendant of Vītahotra’) is mentioned in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā (iv. 2, 6) in connexion with the <b>Kurus.</b></p></div>

क्रुञ्च्|क्रुञ्च्|క్రుఞ్చ్_౧|క్రుఞ్చ్|ಕ್ರುಞ್ಚ್_೧|ಕ್ರುಞ್ಚ್|kruñc_1|kruñc|kru~nch_1|kru~nch|_vei_120@778|_vei1_pn198
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@777">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@779">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn197">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn198">1:198</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn199">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Kruñc,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120778"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120778">1</a></sup> <b>Kruñca,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120778"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120778">2</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रौञ्च">krauñca</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120778"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120778">3</a></sup> are variant forms denoting the ‘curlew’ or ‘snipe.’ To it is attributed in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a><sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_120778"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_120778">1b</a></sup>  the faculty, later assigned to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हंस">haṃsa</a>,</b> of extracting milk from water when the two fluids are mixed.<sup id="ref_120_4b_vei_120778"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4b_vei_120778">4b</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120778"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120778">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 11, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxviii. 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 73 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 2, 1-3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120778"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120778">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 22. 31 (in xxv. 6 the sense is quite uncertain); Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120778"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120778">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 12, 1.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_120778"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_120778">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 11, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxviii. 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 73 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 2, 1-3.</li><li id="fx_120_4b_vei_120778"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4b_vei_120778">↑</a> Lanman, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 19, 151-158; Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 150.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 91, 92.</li></ol></div>

क्रुन्च् आंगिरस|क्रुन्च् आंगिरस|क्रुन्च् आङ्गिरस_२|क्रुन्च् आङ्गिरस|క్రున్చ్ ఆంగిరస_౨|క్రున్చ్ ఆంగిరస|క్రున్చ్ ఆఙ్గిరస_౨|క్రున్చ్ ఆఙ్గిరస|ಕ್ರುನ್ಚ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ_೨|ಕ್ರುನ್ಚ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಕ್ರುನ್ಚ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ_೨|ಕ್ರುನ್ಚ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|krunc āṅgirasa_2|krunc āṅgirasa|krunch aaMgirasa_2|krunch aaMgirasa|krunch aangirasa_2|krunch aangirasa|_vei_120@779|_vei1_pn199
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@778">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@780">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn198">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn199">1:199</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn200">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Krunc Āṅgirasa</b> is in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120779"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120779">1</a></sup> the name of the seer of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> or Chant called the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्रौञ्च">krauñca</a>. It is doubtless invented to explain the name of the Chant on the ordinary principle that Sāmans are called after their authors, though this rule has many exceptions.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120779"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120779">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120779"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120779">↑</a> xiii. 9, 11; 11, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120779"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120779">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 68. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 160.</li></ol></div>

क्रुमु|క్రుము|ಕ್ರುಮು|krumu|_vei_120@780|_vei1_pn199
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@779">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@781">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn198">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn199">1:199</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn200">↷</a><hr><p><b>Krumu</b> is the name of a stream mentioned twice in the Rigveda--once in the fifth book<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120780">1</a></sup> and once in the last, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="नदी">nadī</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="स्तुति">stuti</a>, or ‘praise of rivers.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120780">2</a></sup> There can be little doubt that this river is identical with the modern Kurum, a western tributary of the Indus.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120780">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120780">↑</a> v. 53, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120780">↑</a> x. 75, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120780">↑</a> Roth, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, Erläuterungen,</i> 43; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 14: Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 200.</li></ol></div>

क्रुमुक|క్రుముక|ಕ್ರುಮುಕ|krumuka|_vei_120@781|_vei1_pn199
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@780">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@782">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn198">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn199">1:199</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn200">↷</a><hr><p><b>Krumuka</b> as the name of ‘wood’ appears to be a variant form of <a class="vei_120a" href="कृमुक">kṛmuka</a>.</p><p>^1) <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 1, 9, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 7, 3.</p></div>

क्रैव्य.पाञ्चाल|క్రైవ్య.పాఞ్చాల|ಕ್ರೈವ್ಯ.ಪಾಞ್ಚಾಲ|kraivya.pāñcāla|kraivya.paa~nchaala|_vei_120@782|_vei1_pn199
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@781">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@783">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn198">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn199">1:199</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn200">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kraivya. Pāñcāla,</b> the king of the <b>Krivis,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120782"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120782">1</a></sup> is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120782"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120782">2</a></sup> as having performed the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or horse sacrifice, on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परिवक्रा">parivakrā</a>.</b> Eggeling,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120782"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120782">3</a></sup> however, takes the word as a proper name, ‘Kraivya, the <a class="vei_120a" href="पाञ्चाल">pāñcāla</a> king.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120782"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120782">↑</a> So the St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 125, n.; Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 409, n.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120782"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120782">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120782"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120782">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 397 (but <i>cf.</i> p. 398, top).</li></ol></div>

क्रोश|క్రోశ|ಕ್ರೋಶ|krośa|krosha|_vei_120@783|_vei1_pn199
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@782">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@784">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn198">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn199">1:199</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn200">↷</a><hr><p><b>Krośa,</b> as a measure of distance (lit. ‘a shout,’ as expressing the range of the voice), is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120783"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120783">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120783"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120783">↑</a> xvi. 13, 12. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 8, 432 <i>et seq.</i> In the later literature it is equivalent to about two miles. The word still survives in the vernacular form of Kos as the most popular measure of distance in India.</li></ol></div>

क्रोष्टृ|క్రోష్టృ|ಕ್ರೋಷ್ಟೃ|kroṣṭṛ|kroShTR|kroShTRi|kroShTRu|kroShTRRi|_vei_120@784|_vei1_pn200
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@783">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@785">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn199">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn200">1:200</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn201">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kroṣṭṛ</b> (lit. ‘howler’), the ‘jackal,’ is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120784">1</a></sup> as by nature cowardly compared with the wild boar (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="वराह">varāha</a></b>). In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120784">2</a></sup> it is spoken of as devouring corpses. The word also occurs in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120784">3</a></sup> where the commentator glosses it with <a class="vei_120a" href="सृगाल">sṛgāla</a>, another name of the jackal. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="लोपाश">lopāśa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120784">↑</a> x. 28, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120784">↑</a> xi. 2, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120784">↑</a> xxiv. 32.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 84.</li></ol></div>

क्रौञ्च|क्रौञ्च|క్రౌఞ్చ_౧|క్రౌఞ్చ|ಕ್ರೌಞ್ಚ_೧|ಕ್ರೌಞ್ಚ|krauñca_1|krauñca|krau~ncha_1|krau~ncha|_vei_120@785|_vei1_pn200
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@784">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@786">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn199">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn200">1:200</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn201">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Krauñca.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रुञ्च्">kruñc</a>.</b></p></div>

क्रौञ्च|क्रौञ्च|క్రౌఞ్చ_౨|క్రౌఞ్చ|ಕ್ರೌಞ್ಚ_೨|ಕ್ರೌಞ್ಚ|krauñca_2|krauñca|krau~ncha_2|krau~ncha|_vei_120@786|_vei1_pn200
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@785">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@787">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn199">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn200">1:200</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn201">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Krauñca,</b> as the name of a mountain, occurs only in the latest Vedic literature.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120786"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120786">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120786"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120786">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 31, 2. See Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 93; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 78.</li></ol></div>

क्रौञ्चिकी-पुत्र|क्रौञ्चिकीपुत्र|क्रौञ्चिकी|क्रौञ्चिकि|पुत्र|క్రౌఞ్చికీ-పుత్ర|క్రౌఞ్చికీపుత్ర|క్రౌఞ్చికీ|క్రౌఞ్చికి|పుత్ర|ಕ್ರೌಞ್ಚಿಕೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಕ್ರೌಞ್ಚಿಕೀಪುತ್ರ|ಕ್ರೌಞ್ಚಿಕೀ|ಕ್ರೌಞ್ಚಿಕಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|krauñcikī-putra|krauñcikīputra|krauñcikī|krauñciki|putra|krau~nchikii-putra|krau~nchikiiputra|krau~nchikii|krau~nchiki|krau~nchikee-putra|krau~nchikeeputra|krau~nchikee|_vei_120@787|_vei1_pn200
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@786">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@788">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn199">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn200">1:200</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn201">↷</a><hr><p><b>Krauñcikī-putra,</b> ‘son of a female descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="क्रौञ्च">krauñca</a>,’ is mentioned as a pupil of <b>Vaiṭṭabhatīputra</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120787"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120787">1</a></sup> in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120787"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120787">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120787"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120787">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वैदभृती-पुत्र">vaidabhṛtī-putra</a> in the Mādhyaṃdina recension, vi. 4, 32.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120787"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120787">↑</a> vi. 5, 2 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

क्रौष्टुकि|క్రౌష్టుకి|ಕ್ರೌಷ್ಟುಕಿ|krauṣṭuki|krauShTuki|_vei_120@788|_vei1_pn200
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@787">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@789">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn199">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn200">1:200</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn201">↷</a><hr><p><b>Krauṣṭuki,</b> ‘descendant of Kroṣṭuka,’ is mentioned as a grammarian in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120788"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120788">1</a></sup> the Bṛhaddevatā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120788"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120788">2</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="छन्दस्">chandas</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120788"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120788">3</a></sup> but as an astrologer in a Pariśiṣṭa of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120788"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120788">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120788"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120788">↑</a> viii. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120788"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120788">↑</a> iv. 137. <i>Cf. Indische Studien,</i> 1, 105.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120788"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120788">↑</a> 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120788"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120788">↑</a> Weber, Berlin Catalogue of Sanskrit MSS., 1, 94. See Bolling and von Negelein. <i>The Pariśiṣṭas of the Atharvaveda,</i> 2, 438 <i>et seq.,</i> where in Pariśiṣṭa lxviii (Svapnādhyāyaḥ), i. 2; ii. 8, the name appears as Kroṣṭuki.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ज्योतिष">jyotiṣa</a>,</i> 12; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 61.</li></ol></div>

क्वयी|क्वयि|క్వయీ|క్వయి|ಕ್ವಯೀ|ಕ್ವಯಿ|kvayī|kvayi|kvayii|kvayee|_vei_120@789|_vei1_pn200
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@788">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@790">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn199">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn200">1:200</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn201">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kvayī</b> is the name of some species of bird in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120789"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120789">1</a></sup> occurring in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or horse sacrifice. The form in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120789"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120789">2</a></sup> is <a class="vei_120a" href="कुवय">kuvaya</a>. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120789"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120789">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 17, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 29.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120789"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120789">↑</a> iii. 14, 18.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 99</li></ol></div>

क्वल|క్వల|ಕ್ವಲ|kvala|_vei_120@790|_vei1_pn201
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@789">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@791">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn200">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn201">1:201</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn202">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kvala</b> is a substance, perhaps<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120790">1</a></sup> identical with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुवल">kuvala</a>,</b> the fruit of the jujube, used to coagulate milk according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120790">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120790">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120790">↑</a> ii. 5, 3, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 227.</li></ol></div>

क्षत|క్షత|ಕ್ಷತ|kṣata|kShata|xata|_vei_120@791|_vei1_pn201
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@790">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@792">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn200">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn201">1:201</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn202">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṣata</b> is regarded by Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120791">1</a></sup> as denoting a special disease (a sort of <i>Phthisis pulmonalis</i>) in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120791">2</a></sup> but the word is probably only an adjective.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120791">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120791">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 377.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120791">↑</a> vii. 76, 4 (where the reading is doubtful, the text having <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्षित">akṣita</a>.</i> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्षत">akṣata</a></b>).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120791">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 509; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 442.</li></ol></div>

क्षत्तृ|క్షత్తృ|ಕ್ಷತ್ತೃ|kṣattṛ|kShattR|kShattRi|kShattRu|kShattRRi|xattR|_vei_120@792|_vei1_pn201
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@791">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@793">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn200">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn201">1:201</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn202">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṣattṛ</b> is a word of frequent occurrence in the later Saṃhitās and Brāhmaṇas, denoting a member of the royal entourage, but the sense is somewhat uncertain. In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120792">1</a></sup> it is used of a god as the ‘distributor’ of good things to his worshippers; the same sense seems to be found in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120792">2</a></sup> and elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120792">3</a></sup> In one passage of the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120792">4</a></sup> the interpretation ‘doorkeeper’ is given by the commentator Mahīdhara, a sense which seems possible in other passages,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120792">5</a></sup> while Sāyaṇa ascribes to it in one passage of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120792">6</a></sup> the more dignified meaning of <i>antaḥpurādhyakṣa,</i> ‘a chamberlain.’ In other passages,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120792">7</a></sup> again, the sense of ‘charioteer’ is not unlikely. Later the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्तृ">kṣattṛ</a> was regarded as a man of mixed caste.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120792">8</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120792">↑</a> vi. 13, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120792">↑</a> iii. 24, 7; v. 17, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120792">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 4, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 9, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120792">↑</a> xxx. 13. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 3, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120792">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 4, 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 13; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xix. 1, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120792">↑</a> v. 3, 1, 7. <i>Cf.</i> on xiii. 4, 2, 5 (<i>āyavyayādhyakṣa</i>), and Harisvāmin on xiii. 5, 4, 6 (<i>kośādhyakṣa</i>). The scholiast on the Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 3, 9. has <i>mantrī dūto vā;</i> on xx. 1, 16, <i>pratīhāro dūto vā.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 61, etc., renders it ‘chamberlain.’</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120792">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 26; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 7, 1, with the scholiast's note, and <i>ibid., <a class="vei_120a" href="अनुक्षत्तृ">anukṣattṛ</a>,</i> rendered as <i>sārather <a class="vei_120a" href="अनुचर">anucara</a>,</i> ‘the attendant of the charioteer’; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 1, 20, with the scholiast's note.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120792">↑</a> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 481.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 2, 36; 17, 290; St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

क्षत्र|क्षत्र|క్షత్ర_౧|క్షత్ర|ಕ್ಷತ್ರ_೧|ಕ್ಷತ್ರ|kṣatra_1|kṣatra|kShatra_1|kShatra|xatra_1|xatra|_vei_120@793|_vei1_pn202
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@792">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@794">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn201">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn202">1:202</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn208">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Kṣatra,</b> in the general sense of ‘dominion,’ ‘rule,’ ‘power,’ as exercised by gods and men, occurs frequently from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120793"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120793">1</a></sup> onwards. The word is also found in the concrete sense of ‘rulers’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120793"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120793">2</a></sup> and later;<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120793"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120793">3</a></sup> but in no case does it in the Rigveda certainly<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120793"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120793">4</a></sup> mean what it regularly denotes in the later Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120793"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120793">5</a></sup> the ruling class as opposed to the priests (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a></b>), the subject people (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a></b>), and the servile class (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a></b>). See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>.</b> A <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्र">kṣatra</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a> is several times mentioned<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120793"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120793">6</a></sup> as an equivalent of ‘king.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120793"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120793">↑</a> i. 24, 11; 136, 1. 3; iv. 17. 1; v. 62, 6, etc.; Av. iii. 5, 2; v. 18, 4, etc. So <i><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्र">kṣatra</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="श्री">śrī</a>,</i> Rv. i. 25, 5; vi. 26. 8; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्र">kṣatra</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="भृत्">bhṛt</a>,</i> ‘bringers of lordship.’ <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 4, 7, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 6, 12; 7, 6, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxvii. 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120793"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120793">↑</a> Singular: i. 157, 2; viii. 35, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120793"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120793">↑</a> Plural: Av. iv. 22, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x. 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 6, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120793"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120793">↑</a> See Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120793"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120793">↑</a> Av. ii. 15, 4: ix. 7, 9; xii. 5, 8; xv. 10, 5, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 1, 2; ii. 2, 11, 2, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, v. 27; xiv. 24; xviii. 38, etc. See other citations under <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120793"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120793">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 14, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x. 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 8, 5; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 2, 2.</li></ol></div>

क्षत्र|क्षत्र|క్షత్ర_౨|క్షత్ర|ಕ್ಷತ್ರ_೨|ಕ್ಷತ್ರ|kṣatra_2|kṣatra|kShatra_2|kShatra|xatra_2|xatra|_vei_120@794|_vei1_pn202
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@793">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@795">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn201">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn202">1:202</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn208">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Kṣatra</b> seems to be the name of a man mentioned with others, including <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मनस">manasa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="यजत">yajata</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अवत्सार">avatsāra</a>,</b> in one quite obscure passage of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120794"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120794">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120794"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120794">↑</a> v. 44, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig. Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 138.</li></ol></div>

क्षत्र-विद्या|क्षत्रविद्या|क्षत्र|विद्या|క్షత్ర-విద్యా|క్షత్రవిద్యా|క్షత్ర|విద్యా|ಕ್ಷತ್ರ-ವಿದ್ಯಾ|ಕ್ಷತ್ರವಿದ್ಯಾ|ಕ್ಷತ್ರ|ವಿದ್ಯಾ|kṣatra-vidyā|kṣatravidyā|kṣatra|vidyā|kShatra-vidyaa|kShatravidyaa|kShatra|vidyaa|xatra-vidyaa|xatravidyaa|xatra|_vei_120@795|_vei1_pn202
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@794">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@796">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn201">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn202">1:202</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn208">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṣatra-vidyā,</b> ‘the science of the ruling class,’ is mentioned in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120795">1</a></sup> Śaṅkara glosses the term with <i>dhanur-<a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> ‘the science of the bow,’ which is the most probable sense.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120795">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120795">↑</a> vii. 1, 2. 4; 2, 1; 7, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120795">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 104.</li></ol></div>

क्षत्रिय|క్షత్రియ|ಕ್ಷತ್ರಿಯ|kṣatriya|kShatriya|xatriya|_vei_120@796|_vei1_pn202
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@795">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@797">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn201">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn202">1:202</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn208">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṣatriya.</b>--As the origin of caste, the relation of the castes, intermarriage, and cognate matters may most conveniently be discussed under <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>,</b> this article will be confined to determining, as far as possible, the real character of the class called Kṣatriyas, or collectively <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्र">kṣatra</a>.</b></p><p>The evidence of the Jātakas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120796">1</a></sup> points to the word Khattiya  denoting the members of the old Āryan nobility who had led the tribes to conquest, <b>as well</b> as those families of the aborigines who had managed to maintain their princely status in spite of the conquest. In the epic<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120796">2</a></sup> also the term <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> seems to include these persons, but it has probably a wider signification than Khattiya, and would cover all the royal military vassals and feudal chiefs, expressing, in fact, pretty much the same as the <i>barones</i> of early English history. Neither in the Jātakas<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120796">3</a></sup> nor in the epic<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120796">4</a></sup> is the term co-extensive with all warriors; the army contains many besides the Kṣatriyas, who are the leaders or officers, rather than the rank and file.</p><p>In the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120796">5</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120796">6</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> stands as a definite member of the social body, distinct from the priest, the subject people, and the slaves, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>.</b> It is significant that <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a> is a variant to <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>, and an earlier one. Hence it is reasonable to suppose that the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a> are both of similar origin, being princely or connected with royalty. Moreover, the early use of <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120796">7</a></sup> is exclusively connected with royal authority or divine authority.</p><p>It is impossible to say exactly what persons would be included in the term <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>. That it covered the royal house and the various branches of the royal family may be regarded as certain. It, no doubt, also included the nobles and their families: this would explain the occasional opposition of <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>, as in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120796">8</a></sup> where a <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a> asks a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> for a place for sacrifice (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="देव">deva</a>-yajana</i>). Thus, when strictly applied, <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> would have a wider denotation than <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a>. As a rule, however, the two expressions are identical, and both are used as evidence in what follows. That <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> ever included the mere fighting  man has not been proved: in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120796">9</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120796">10</a></sup> others than Kṣatriyas regularly fought; but possibly if the nobles had retinues as the kings had, <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> would embrace those retainers who had military functions. The term did not apply to all members of the royal entourage; for example, the <b>Grāmaṇī</b> was usually a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>.</p><p>The connexion of the Kṣatriyas with the Brahmins was very close. The prosperity of the two is repeatedly asserted<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120796">11</a></sup> to be indissolubly associated, especially in the relation of king (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a></b>) and domestic priest (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a></b>). Sometimes there was feud between <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> and Brahmin.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120796">12</a></sup> His management of the sacrifice then gave the Brahmin power to ruin the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> by embroiling him with the people<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120796">13</a></sup> or with other Kṣatriyas.<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120796">14</a></sup></p><p>Towards the common people, on the other hand, the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> stood in a relation of well-nigh unquestioned superiority.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_120796">15</a></sup> There are, however, references to occasional feuds between  the people and the nobles,<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_120796">16</a></sup> in which no doubt the inferior numbers of the latter were compensated by their superior arms and prowess. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_17_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_120796">17</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> is described as tributary to another (<i>anyasya <a class="vei_120a" href="बलि">bali</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्">kṛt</a></i>), to be devoured by another (<i>anyasyādya</i>), and to be oppressed at will (<i>yathākāma-jyeya</i>). Probably these epithets apply most strictly to the relation of the king and his people, but the passage shows that the people were greatly at the mercy of the nobles. No doubt the king granted to them the right, which may have been hereditary, to be supported by the common people, whose feudal superiors they thus became. In return for these privileges the Kṣatriyas had probably duties of protection to perform, as well as some judicial functions, to judge from an obscure passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_120796">18</a></sup></p><p>The main duty of the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> in the small states<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_120796">19</a></sup> of the Vedic period was readiness for war. The bow is thus his special attribute,<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_120796">20</a></sup> just as the goad is that of the agriculturist; for the bow is the main weapon of the <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>. Whether the Kṣatriyas paid much attention to mental occupations is uncertain. In the latest stratum of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> literature there are references to learned princes like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a></b> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>,</b> who is said to have become a Brahmin (<i>brahmā</i>), apparently in the sense that he had the full knowledge which a Brahmin possessed.<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_120796">21</a></sup> Other learned Kṣatriyas of this period were  <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रवाहण जैवलि">pravāhaṇa jaivali</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_22_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_120796">22</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वपति">aśvapati</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कैकेय">kaikeya</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_23_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_120796">23</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="अजातशत्रु">ajātaśatru</a>.<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_120796">24</a></sup> Garbe,<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_120796">25</a></sup> Grierson,<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_120796">26</a></sup> and others believe they are justified in holding the view that the Ksatriyas developed a special philosophy of their own as opposed to Brahminism, which appears later as Bhakti, or Faith. On the other hand, there is clear evidence<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_120796">27</a></sup> that the opinion of Ksatriyas on such topics were held in little respect, and it must be remembered that to attribute wisdom to a king was a delicate and effective piece of flattery. There are earlier references to royal sages (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्यर्षि">rājanyarṣi</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_28_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_120796">28</a></sup> but it is very doubtful if much stress can be laid on them, and none can be laid on the later tradition of Sāyaṇa.<sup id="ref_120_29_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29_vei_120796">29</a></sup> Again, the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_30_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_30_vei_120796">30</a></sup> gives a tradition relating how <b>Devāpi,</b> a king's son, became the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> of his younger brother <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शंतनु">śaṃtanu</a>;</b> but it is very doubtful if the story can really be traced with Sieg<sup id="ref_120_31_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_31_vei_120796">31</a></sup> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_32_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_32_vei_120796">32</a></sup> itself. In any case, the stories refer only to a few selected Kṣatriyas of high rank, while there is no evidence that the average <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> was concerned with intellectual pursuits. Nor is there any reference to Kṣatriyas engaging in agriculture or in trade or commerce. It may be assumed that the duties of administration and war were adequate to absorb his attention. On the other hand, we do hear of a <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a>  as a lute player and singer at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> or horse sacrifice.<sup id="ref_120_33_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_33_vei_120796">33</a></sup></p><p>Of the training and education of a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> we have no record; presumably, as in fact if not in theory later on, he was mainly instructed in the art of war, the science of the bow, and the rudimentary administrative functions which would devolve on him. At this early state of the development of the nobility which appears to be represented in the Rigveda, it was probably not unusual or impossible for a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> to become a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>; at least, this assumption best explains the phrase<sup id="ref_120_34_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_34_vei_120796">34</a></sup> ‘claiming falsely a Kṣatriya's rank’ (<i>kṣatriyaṃ mithuyā dhārayantam</i>).</p><p>The king and the Kṣatriyas must have stood in a particularly close relation. The former being the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> <i>par excellence,</i> it is to him rather than to the ordinary <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> that we must refer passages like that in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_35_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_35_vei_120796">35</a></sup> where it is said that the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>, with the consent of the clansmen, gives a settlement to a man: clearly a parallel to the rule found among many peoples that the chief, but only with the consent of the people, can make a grant of unoccupied land. In the same <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_36_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_36_vei_120796">36</a></sup> it is said that a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> consecrates a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>, a clear reference, as the commentator explains, to the practice of the old king consecrating the prince (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कुमार">kumāra</a></i>) who is to succeed him; and again,<sup id="ref_120_37_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_37_vei_120796">37</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> are regarded as alone complete in contrast with other people, the parallel with the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> here suggesting that the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> <i>par excellence</i> is meant. On the other hand, the king is sometimes contrasted with the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a>.<sup id="ref_120_38_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_38_vei_120796">38</a></sup></p><p>The <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> literature contains elaborate rules<sup id="ref_120_39_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_39_vei_120796">39</a></sup> for the educa tion and occupations of Kṣatriyas, but their contents cannot always be traced in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> literature, and their value is questionable.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120796">↑</a> See Fick, <i>Die sociale Gliederung im nordo7stlichen Indien zu Buddhas Zoit,</i> 59 <i>et seq.;</i> Rhys Davids, <i>Dialogues of the Buddha,</i> 1, 95 <i>et seq.; Buddhist India,</i> 52 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120796">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 73 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120796">↑</a> Fick, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 52, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120796">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 184 <i>et seq.,</i> 190.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120796">↑</a> Av. vi. 76, 3. 4; xii. 5, 5. 44. 46, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 5, etc. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120796">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 24, etc.; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 2, 15; iv. 1, 4, 5. 6, etc. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120796">↑</a> iv. 12, 3; 42, 1; v. 69, 1; vii. 64, 2; viii. 25, 8; 56, 1; x. 109, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iv. 19; x. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 7, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120796">↑</a> vii. 20. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxiv. 18, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xx. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120796">↑</a> In the following passages there is reference to the people (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a></i>) fighting: i. 69, 3; 126, 5 (<i>cf.,</i> however, Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 121); iv. 24, 4; vi. 26, 1; vii. 79, 2; viii. 18, 18; 96, 15; probably also vii. 33, 6, where the <i>Tṛtsūnāṃ viśaḥ</i> means ‘the subjects of the <a class="vei_120a" href="तृत्सु">tṛtsu</a> princes,’ as Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 136, thinks. In vi. 41, 5, on the other hand, the people and wars (<i>pṛtanāsu</i>) are contrasted, the normal rule of the common folk being peace.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120796">↑</a> In Av. ix. 7, 9, the people are clearly designated as <i>balam,</i> or ‘force,’ a regular term later for an armed force. The later law books (<i>e.g.,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a>, vii. 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>, ii. 22) allow even Brahmins to maintain themselves by the occupation of Kṣatriyas in case of need. For the Epic, <i>cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 94, 95; 184 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120796">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 1, 10, 3; Maitrayaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 3; iii. 1, 9; 2, 3; iv. 3, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxix. 10; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, v. 27; vii. 21; xviii. 14; xix. 5; xxxviii. 14, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 11, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 22; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 1, 7; iii. 5, 2, 11; 6, 1, 17; vi. 6, 3, 14. The superiority of the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a> to all other castes is asserted in <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 10, 1, etc. The superiority of the Brahmin to the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> is sometimes asserted--<i>e.g.,</i> in the Atharvaveda hymns, v. 18. 19; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxi. 21; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 1, 9, 1; 3, 7, 8. So the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a> sacrifice of the king is inferior to the highest sacrifice (the <a class="vei_120a" href="वाजपेय">vājapeya</a>) of the priest (<i>ibid.,</i> v. 1, 1, 12), and though the Brahmin goes after the king, he is yet stronger than he (v. 4, 2, 7, and v. 4, 4, 15). <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 76.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120796">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxviii. 5; Av. v. 18. 19.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120796">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 11, 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 6, 5; ii. 1, 9; iii. 3, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxix. 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120796">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 3, 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_120796">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvi. 4; xxi. 10; xxii. 9; xxix. 9. 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 33; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 2, 7, 15. 16, etc.; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 4, 9. 10; 6, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_120796">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> note 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 4, 6, 7; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 6, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_120796">↑</a> vii. 29. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_120796">↑</a> xxvii. 4 (<i>tasmād rājanyenādhyakṣeṇa vaiśyaṃ ghnanti,</i> ‘so with a <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a> as a supervisor[?] they smite a Vaiśya’). It is not clear whether <i><a class="vei_120a" href="हन्">han</a></i> here means ‘kill’ or ‘beat.’</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_120796">↑</a> See Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 30, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_120796">↑</a> Av. xviii. 2, 60; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xviii. 9; xxxvii. 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 5, 30; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, vi. 1, 3. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 19, the list is longer--chariot, breastplate (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="कवच">kavaca</a></b>), bow and arrow (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="इषु">iṣu</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="धन्वन्">dhanvan</a></i>)--and in the prayer for the prosperity of the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> (called, as usual in the older texts, <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a>), at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, the Rājanva is to be an archer and a good chariot-fighter; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 18, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 12, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, v. 14; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxii. 2. So <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> is the god of the Kṣatriyas, Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 1; iv. 5, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_120796">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 6, 2, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1. See Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Litcrature,</i> 421 <i>et seq.;</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 426 <i>et seq.</i> Similarly at the Dīkṣā a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> becomes temporarily a Brahmin, <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 23.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 1, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_120796">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 1; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 8, 1; v. 3, 1; Muir, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 433-435; 515; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 117; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 1, lxxv.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_120796">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 6, 1, 2 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_120796">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 1, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_120796">↑</a> <i>Beiträge zur indischen Kulturgeschichte,</i> 1 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> Deussen, <i>Philosophy of the Upanishads,</i> 17 <i>et seq.;</i> Winterniz, <i>Geschichte der indischen Litteratur,</i> 1, 199.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_120796">↑</a> Article ‘Bhakti’ in <i>Encyclopœdia of Religion and Ethics; Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 843.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_120796">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 1, 4, 10.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg. <i>Buddha,</i> 73, n. 1; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āranyaka,</i> 50, 257; <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 868, 883, 1140-1142. Professor Eggeling concurs in the view that the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> share in the religious movement was not substantially real.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_120796">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> in Pañcavimśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 12, 6; but see on this Oldenberg. <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenlandischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 235, n., and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_29_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29_vei_120796">↑</a> Cited in Muir, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 12, 265 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_30_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_30_vei_120796">↑</a> ii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_31_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_31_vei_120796">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 91 <i>et seq.</i> See <b>Devāpi.</b></li><li id="fx_120_32_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_32_vei_120796">↑</a> x. 98. The case of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a></b> may also be cited; but his royal rank, which is attested by the mention of him as a <a class="vei_120a" href="राजपुत्र">rājaputra</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 17, is at most merely a matter of descent, and is of very doubtful authenticity. See under <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_33_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_33_vei_120796">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 3, 5. This mention is proof of the existence of a class of <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> bards (as opposed to priestly reciters), from whose productions the Epic naturally grew up.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 258.</li><li id="fx_120_34_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_34_vei_120796">↑</a> vii. 104, 13. <i>Cf.</i> for a similarly false claim to be a Brahmin, x. 71, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_35_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_35_vei_120796">↑</a> vii. 1, 1, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_36_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_36_vei_120796">↑</a> xii. 8, 3, 19; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 254, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_37_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_37_vei_120796">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>ibid.,</i> 41, 259.</li><li id="fx_120_38_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_38_vei_120796">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 2, 17, and see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_39_vei_120796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_39_vei_120796">↑</a> See references in Bühler, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 14, 395, 396.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 212 <i>et seq.;</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 231 <i>et seq.;</i> von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 151 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 4 <i>et seq.</i> (where practically every passage on the subject is cited or referred to); Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 98 <i>et seq.</i> (for the Epic parallels).</li></ol></div>

क्ष-पावन्|क्षपावन्|क्ष|पावन्|క్ష-పావన్|క్షపావన్|క్ష|పావన్|ಕ್ಷ-ಪಾವನ್|ಕ್ಷಪಾವನ್|ಕ್ಷ|ಪಾವನ್|kṣa-pāvan|kṣapāvan|kṣa|pāvan|kSha-paavan|kShapaavan|kSha|paavan|xa-paavan|xapaavan|xa|_vei_120@797|_vei1_pn208
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@796">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@798">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn202">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn208">1:208</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn209">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṣa-pāvan,</b> ‘protector of the earth,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120797">1</a></sup> is an epithet of a king,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120797">2</a></sup> or denotes a king<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120797">3</a></sup> in the Rigveda. The word is significant, as showing the function of the king as the protector of the tribal territory.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120797">↑</a> The word occurs only in the nominative singular as <i>kṣapāvān,</i> which would be the regular form from a stem <i>kṣapāvant;</i> but it is probably an irregularity for <i>kṣapāvā. Cf.</i> Oldenberg. <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 72.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120797">↑</a> iii. 55, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120797">↑</a> i. 70, 5; vii. 10, 5; viii. 71, 2; x. 29, 1. <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षिति">kṣiti</a>-pa,</i> ‘guardian of earth,’ ‘king,’ in later Sanskrit.</li></ol></div>

क्षिति|క్షితి|ಕ್ಷಿತಿ|kṣiti|kShiti|xiti|_vei_120@798|_vei1_pn208
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@797">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@799">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn202">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn208">1:208</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn209">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṣiti</b> is in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120798">1</a></sup> a regular word for ‘dwelling,’ and in particular the <i>kṣitir <a class="vei_120a" href="ध्रुवा">dhruvā</a>,</i> ‘the secure dwelling,’ is mentioned<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120798">2</a></sup> in a context that shows it, to be equivalent to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वृजन">vṛjana</a></b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a></b> regarded as a stronghold. From this sense is developed that of the peoples occupying the settlements,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120798">3</a></sup> and in particular the five peoples<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120798">4</a></sup> (for whom see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्च">pañca</a> Janāsaḥ</b>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120798">↑</a> i. 65, 3; iii. 13, 4; v. 37. 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120798">↑</a> i. 73, 4 (<i>cf.</i> 2); vii. 88, 7. See Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 142.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120798">↑</a> Rv. iii. 38, 1; iv. 24, 4; 38, 5; v. 1, 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120798">↑</a> i. 7, 9; 176, 3; v. 35, 2; vi. 46, 7; vii. 75, 4; 79, 1.</li></ol></div>

क्षिप्त|క్షిప్త|ಕ್ಷಿಪ್ತ|kṣipta|kShipta|xipta|_vei_120@799|_vei1_pn208
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@798">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@800">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn202">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn208">1:208</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn209">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṣipta,</b> ‘a wound’ (caused by shooting), or ‘bruise’ (caused by throwing), is mentioned in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120799"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120799">1</a></sup> together with a remedy for it, the <b>Pippalī.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120799"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120799">↑</a> vi. 109, 1. 3. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 389.</li></ol></div>

क्षिप्र-श्येन|क्षिप्रश्येन|क्षिप्र|श्येन|క్షిప్ర-శ్యేన|క్షిప్రశ్యేన|క్షిప్ర|శ్యేన|ಕ್ಷಿಪ್ರ-ಶ್ಯೇನ|ಕ್ಷಿಪ್ರಶ್ಯೇನ|ಕ್ಷಿಪ್ರ|ಶ್ಯೇನ|kṣipra-śyena|kṣipraśyena|kṣipra|śyena|kShipra-shyena|kShiprashyena|kShipra|shyena|xipra-shyena|xiprashyena|xipra|_vei_120@800|_vei1_pn208
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@799">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@801">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn202">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn208">1:208</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn209">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṣipra-śyena,</b> ‘swift falcon,’ is the name of a bird in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā (iii. 14, 11) and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> x. 5, 2, 10).</p></div>

क्षीर|క్షీర|ಕ್ಷೀರ|kṣīra|kShiira|kSheera|xiira|_vei_120@801|_vei1_pn208
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@800">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@802">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn202">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn208">1:208</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn209">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṣīra,</b> ‘milk,’ also called <b>Go</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पयस्">payas</a>,</b> played a large part in the economy of the Vedic Indians.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120801"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120801">1</a></sup> It was taken warm (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पक्व">pakva</a></i>  as it came from the cow,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120801"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120801">2</a></sup> or was used with grain to make a ‘mess cooked with milk’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षीर">kṣīra</a>-pākam odanam</i>).<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120801"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120801">3</a></sup> It was also used for mixing with <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अभिश्री">abhiśrī</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="आशिर्">āśir</a></b>). From it butter (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="घृत">ghṛta</a></b>) was made. Milk was also curdled, the <b>Pūtīkā</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्वल">kvala</a></b> plants, among others, being used for the purpose.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120801"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120801">4</a></sup> The curdled milk (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="दधि">dadhi</a></b>) was undoubtedly used for food; and a kind of cheese is perhaps referred to in one passage of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120801"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120801">5</a></sup> Goat's milk (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अज">aja</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="क्षीर">kṣīra</a></i>) is also mentioned.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120801"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120801">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120801"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120801">↑</a> The word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षीर">kṣīra</a></i> does not occur in Rv. ii. vii. It is found in i. 109, 3; 164, 7; viii. 2, 9; ix. 67, 32; x. 87, 16 (= Av. viii. 3, 15). See Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 64, 73 <i>et seq.</i> See also Av. ii. 26, 4; v. 19, 5; x. 9, 12, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 8, 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120801"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120801">↑</a> i. 62, 9; 180, 3; iii. 30, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120801"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120801">↑</a> Rv. viii. 77, 10; Av. xiii. 2, 20. <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षीर">kṣīra</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="श्री">śrī</a>,</i> ‘milk-mixed,’ <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 4, 0, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, viii. 57, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120801"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120801">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 3, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120801"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120801">↑</a> vi. 48, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120801"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120801">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 1, 2, 13; see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अज">aja</a>.</b></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 63, 226, 226, 268.</li></ol></div>

क्षीरौदन|క్షీరౌదన|ಕ್ಷೀರೌದನ|kṣīraudana|kShiiraudana|kSheeraudana|xiiraudana|_vei_120@802|_vei1_pn209
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@801">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@803">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn208">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn209">1:209</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn210">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṣīraudana,</b> ‘rice cooked with milk,’ is mentioned frequently in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (ii. 5, 3, 4; xi. 5, 7, 5, etc.).</p></div>

क्षुद्र-सूक्तस्|क्षुद्र-सूक्त|क्षुद्रसूक्तस्|क्षुद्रसूक्त|क्षुद्र|सूक्तस्|सूक्त|క్షుద్ర-సూక్తస్|క్షుద్ర-సూక్త|క్షుద్రసూక్తస్|క్షుద్రసూక్త|క్షుద్ర|సూక్తస్|సూక్త|ಕ್ಷುದ್ರ-ಸೂಕ್ತಸ್|ಕ್ಷುದ್ರ-ಸೂಕ್ತ|ಕ್ಷುದ್ರಸೂಕ್ತಸ್|ಕ್ಷುದ್ರಸೂಕ್ತ|ಕ್ಷುದ್ರ|ಸೂಕ್ತಸ್|ಸೂಕ್ತ|kṣudra-sūktas|kṣudra-sūkta|kṣudrasūktas|kṣudrasūkta|kṣudra|sūktas|sūkta|kShudra-suuktas|kShudra-suukta|kShudrasuuktas|kShudrasuukta|kShudra|suuktas|suukta|kShudra-sooktas|kShudra-sookta|kShudrasooktas|kShudrasookta|sooktas|sookta|xudra-suuktas|xudra-suukta|xudrasuuktas|xudrasuukta|xudra|_vei_120@803|_vei1_pn209
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@802">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@804">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn208">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn209">1:209</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn210">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṣudra-sūktas,</b> ‘makers of short hymns,’ is the name given in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120803"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120803">1</a></sup> to the authors of certain hymns of the Rigveda. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="महासूक्त">mahāsūkta</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120803"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120803">↑</a> ii. 2, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 212, and Macdonell, <i>Bṛhaddevatā.</i> notes on iii. 116.</li></ol></div>

क्षुम्प|क्षुंप|క్షుమ్ప|క్షుంప|ಕ್ಷುಮ್ಪ|ಕ್ಷುಂಪ|kṣumpa|kShumpa|xumpa|_vei_120@804|_vei1_pn209
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@803">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@805">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn208">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn209">1:209</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn210">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṣumpa</b> is a word occurring only in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120804"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120804">1</a></sup> where it seems to mean a ‘bush.’ The <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120804"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120804">2</a></sup> identifies it with the Ahichatraka, a ‘mushroom.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120804"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120804">↑</a> i. 84, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120804"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120804">↑</a> v. 16. <i>Cf.</i> Benfey, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>, Glossar,</i> 53.</li></ol></div>

क्षुर|క్షుర|ಕ್ಷುರ|kṣura|kShura|xura|_vei_120@805|_vei1_pn209
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@804">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@806">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn208">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn209">1:209</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn210">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṣura</b> occurs three times in the Rigveda. The word appears to have the general sense of ‘blade’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120805">1</a></sup> in one passage,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120805">2</a></sup> possibly also in another,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120805">3</a></sup> where it is said that the hare swallowed a  <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षुर">kṣura</a>, and where the sense ‘blade’ is adequate. In the third passage<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120805">4</a></sup> there seems to be a reference to the sharpening of a razor on a grindstone<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120805">5</a></sup> (<i>bhurijos,</i> the dual denoting precisely, as Pischel<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120805">6</a></sup> points out, the two sides of the apparatus, between which the stone revolved like the modern grindstone). But Muir,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120805">7</a></sup> following another view of Roth,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120805">8</a></sup> adopts the sense ‘the edge of scissors,’ which, however, hardly suits the other passage, one in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120805">9</a></sup> where a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षुर">kṣura</a> is described as moving about on the <i>bhurijos,</i><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120805">10</a></sup> as the tongue on the lip. The meaning ‘razor’ is perfectly clear in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120805">11</a></sup> where shaving by means of it is mentioned; in many other passages<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120805">12</a></sup> either sense is adequate. A <i>kṣuro bhṛjvān</i> occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>:<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120805">13</a></sup> it seems to denote, as Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120805">14</a></sup> suggests, a razor with a strop (in the shape of a small grinding apparatus). <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षुर">kṣura</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="धारा">dhārā</a><sup id="ref_120_15_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_120805">15</a></sup> denotes ‘the edge of a razor,’ like <i>kṣurasya <a class="vei_120a" href="धारा">dhārā</a>.</i><sup id="ref_120_16_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_120805">16</a></sup> In the Upaniṣads<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_120805">17</a></sup> a razor-case (<a class="vei_120a" href="क्षुर">kṣura</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="धान">dhāna</a>) is mentioned. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्मश्रु">śmaśru</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120805">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 61, 69. <i>Cf.</i> 13, 292 (as ‘knife’ in the Epic).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120805">↑</a> i. 166, 10 (<i>paviṣu kṣurāḥ</i> on the fellies of the Maruts' car; possibly ‘razors’ may be meant, as Max Müller says in his note on this passage, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 235, n. 4).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120805">↑</a> x. 28, 9, where Sāyaṇa renders it as meaning ‘having claws.’ The later tradition ascribes the swallowing to a goat.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120805">↑</a> viii. 4, 16 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सं">saṃ</a> naḥ śiśīhi bhurijor iva kṣuram,</i> ‘sharpen us like a razor on a grindstone or stropping apparatus’).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120805">↑</a> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120805">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 243.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120805">↑</a> <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 466.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120805">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. <a class="vei_120a" href="भुरिज्">bhurij</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120805">↑</a> xx. 127, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120805">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 197, translates <i>bhurijos</i> by ‘on a strop.’</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120805">↑</a> vi. 68, 1. 3; viii. 2, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120805">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 4, 5; iii. 1, 2, 7; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षुर">kṣura</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पवि">pavi</a>,</i> Av. xii. 5, 20. 55; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 5, 7; 5, 5, 6; v. 6, 6, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 6, 2, 9. etc.; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 10, 14; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvi. 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120805">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 12, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 8, 7; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xv. 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 5, 2, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120805">↑</a> <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 17, 418.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_120805">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 13, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_120805">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 3, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_120805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_120805">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 20.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 266; Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 239-243; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 38 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

क्षेत्र|క్షేత్ర|ಕ್ಷೇತ್ರ|kṣetra|kShetra|xetra|_vei_120@806|_vei1_pn210
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@805">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@807">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn209">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn210">1:210</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn211">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṣetra,</b> ‘field.’ The use of this word in the Rigveda points clearly to the existence of separate fields<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120806">1</a></sup> carefully measured off,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120806">2</a></sup> though in some passages the meaning is less definite,  indicating cultivated land generally.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120806">3</a></sup> In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120806">4</a></sup> and later the sense of a separate field is clearly marked, though the more general use is also found.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120806">5</a></sup> The deity Kṣetrasya <a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120806">6</a></sup> ‘Lord of the Field,’ should probably be understood as the god presiding over each field, just as Vāstoṣ <a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a> presides over each dwelling.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120806">7</a></sup> It is a fair conclusion from the evidence that the system of separate holdings already existed in early Vedic times.^8 See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उर्वरा">urvarā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="खिल्य">khilya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120806">↑</a> x. 33, 6. <i>Cf.</i> iii. 31, 15; v. 62, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120806">↑</a> i. 110, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120806">↑</a> i. 100, 18; ix. 85, 4; 91, 6; <i>Kṣetrajeṣa,</i> i. 33, 15, ‘acquisition of land’; <i>kṣetrā-sā,</i> iv. 38, 1, ‘gaining land’; <i>kṣetraṃ-jaya,</i> ‘conquering cultivated land,’ Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 11. The wider sense of ‘place’ also occurs, v. 2, 3; 45, 9; vi. 47, 20, etc., and often later.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120806">↑</a> iv. 18, 5; v. 31, 4; x. 1, 18; xi. 1, 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 1, 2; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vii. 24, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120806">↑</a> Av. ii. 29, 3; xiv. 2, 7; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 1, 15. 16, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120806">↑</a> Rv. iv. 37, 1, 2; vii. 35, 10; x. 66, 13; Av. ii. 8, 5; <i>kṣetrasya <a class="vei_120a" href="पत्नी">patnī</a>,</i> ‘Mistress of the Field,’ 12, 1; <i>kṣetrāṇāṃ patiḥ,</i> ‘Lord of Fields,’ Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120806">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 138.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 236; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 1, 1, 8, where the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>, with the consent of the people, gives a settlement to a man: that is, presumably assigns to him a definite <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षेत्र">kṣetra</a> for his own, probably measured out as recorded in Rv. i. 110, 5.</li></ol></div>

क्षेत्रिय|క్షేత్రియ|ಕ್ಷೇತ್ರಿಯ|kṣetriya|kShetriya|xetriya|_vei_120@807|_vei1_pn211
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@806">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@808">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn210">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn211">1:211</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn212">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṣetriya</b> is a disease which is mentioned several times in the Atharvaveda, and against which three hymns are specially directed.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120807">1</a></sup> It is also mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120807">2</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120807">3</a></sup> The commentators on the Atharvaveda agree in taking it to be a hereditary disease. The word may mean ‘organic,’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120807">4</a></sup> or possibly ‘produced in the field,’ as a theory of its origin. What disease is really intended is quite uncertain. Weber<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120807">5</a></sup> considered that the aim of the <a class="vei_120a" href="अथर्वन्">atharvan</a> hymns was to drive away injury threatening the fields, but this is improbable. Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120807">6</a></sup> suggests ‘scrofula’ or ‘syphilis.’ The remedies mentioned throw no light on the symptoms. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120807">↑</a> ii. 8. 10; iii. 7. <i>Cf.</i> ii. 14, 5; iv. 18, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120807">↑</a> xv. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120807">↑</a> ii. 5, 6, 1-3, where the form is Kṣetrī, explained as a demon causing illness, merely an incorrect version of Av. iii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120807">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120807">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 145; 13, 150 <i>et seq.:</i> 17, 208; <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 292.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120807">↑</a> <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 60.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 286 <i>et seq.;</i> Whitney, Transiation of the Atharvaveda, 48, 49; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 391, 392; Speijer, <i>De ceremonia apud Indos quœ jātakarma vocatur,</i> 76-83; Pāṇini, v. 2, 92, with the Kāśikā <a class="vei_120a" href="वृत्ति">vṛtti</a>.</li></ol></div>

क्षेम-धृत्वन् पौण्डरीक|क्षेमधृत्वन् पौण्डरीक|क्षेम|धृत्वन् पौण्डरीक|క్షేమ-ధృత్వన్ పౌణ్డరీక|క్షేమధృత్వన్ పౌణ్డరీక|క్షేమ|ధృత్వన్ పౌణ్డరీక|ಕ್ಷೇಮ-ಧೃತ್ವನ್ ಪೌಣ್ಡರೀಕ|ಕ್ಷೇಮಧೃತ್ವನ್ ಪೌಣ್ಡರೀಕ|ಕ್ಷೇಮ|ಧೃತ್ವನ್ ಪೌಣ್ಡರೀಕ|kṣema-dhṛtvan pauṇḍarīka|kṣemadhṛtvan pauṇḍarīka|kṣema|dhṛtvan pauṇḍarīka|kShema-dhRtvan pauNDariika|kShemadhRtvan pauNDariika|kShema|dhRtvan pauNDariika|kShema-dhRtvan pauNDareeka|kShemadhRtvan pauNDareeka|dhRtvan pauNDareeka|kShema-dhRitvan pauNDariika|kShemadhRitvan pauNDariika|dhRitvan pauNDariika|kShema-dhRutvan pauNDariika|kShemadhRutvan pauNDariika|dhRutvan pauNDariika|kShema-dhRRitvan pauNDariika|kShemadhRRitvan pauNDariika|dhRRitvan pauNDariika|xema-dhRtvan pauNDariika|xemadhRtvan pauNDariika|xema|_vei_120@808|_vei1_pn212
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@807">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@809">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn211">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn212">1:212</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn213">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṣema-dhṛtvan Pauṇḍarīka</b> (‘descendant of Puṇḍarīka’) is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120808"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120808">1</a></sup> as having sacrificed on the bank of the river <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदामन्">sudāman</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120808"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120808">↑</a> xxii. 18, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 32. Later, the name is <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षेम">kṣema</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="धन्वन्">dhanvan</a>, Harivaṃśa, 824, etc.</li></ol></div>

क्षैमि|క్షైమి|ಕ್ಷೈಮಿ|kṣaimi|kShaimi|xaimi|_vei_120@809|_vei1_pn212
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@808">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@810">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn211">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn212">1:212</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn213">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṣaimi,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षेम">kṣema</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b>Sudakṣiṇa</b> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 6, 3; 7, 1, etc.; 8, 6).</p></div>

क्षोणी|क्षोणि|క్షోణీ|క్షోణి|ಕ್ಷೋಣೀ|ಕ್ಷೋಣಿ|kṣoṇī|kṣoṇi|kShoNii|kShoNi|kShoNee|xoNii|xoNi|_vei_120@810|_vei1_pn212
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@809">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@811">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn211">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn212">1:212</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn213">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṣoṇī.</b>--This word, when used in the plural, denotes, according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary and Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120810"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120810">1</a></sup> in several passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120810"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120810">2</a></sup> the free retainers of the king. Geldner<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120810"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120810">3</a></sup> at one time thought it referred to the wives of the king, pointing to polygamy; but later<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120810"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120810">4</a></sup> he concluded that it means certain divine wives.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120810"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120810">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 247.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120810"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120810">↑</a> i. 57, 4; 173, 7; viii. 3, 10; 13, 17; x. 95. 19. In ii. 34, 13; x. 22, 9, the sense is doubtful.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120810"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120810">↑</a> Bezzenberger, <i>Beiträge,</i> 11, 327.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120810"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120810">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 279, 283.</li></ol></div>

क्षौम|క్షౌమ|ಕ್ಷೌಮ|kṣauma|kShauma|xauma|_vei_120@811|_vei1_pn212
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@810">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@812">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn211">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn212">1:212</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn213">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṣauma,</b> ‘a linen garment,’ is mentioned in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā (iii. 6, 7, etc.) and in the Sūtras.</p></div>

क्ष्विंका|क्ष्विङ्का|క్ష్వింకా|క్ష్విఙ్కా|ಕ್ಷ್ವಿಂಕಾ|ಕ್ಷ್ವಿಙ್ಕಾ|kṣviṅkā|kShviMkaa|kShvinkaa|xviMkaa|_vei_120@812|_vei1_pn212
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@811">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@813">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn211">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn212">1:212</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn213">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṣviṅkā</b> is mentioned once in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120812">1</a></sup> as a bird of <b>prey.</b> The word also occurs in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or horse sacrifice, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120812">2</a></sup> where the commentary absurdly explains it as ‘a red-mouthed female ape’ (<i>rakta-mukhī vānarī</i>). </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120812">↑</a> x. 87, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120812">↑</a> v. 5, 15, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 93.</li></ol></div>

ख|ఖ|ಖ|kha|_vei_120@813|_vei1_pn213
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@812">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@814">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn212">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn213">1:213</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn214">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kha</b> denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120813"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120813">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120813"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120813">2</a></sup> the hole in the nave of the wheel in which the axle is inserted. There was a difference, it seems, in the size of the hole in the wheel of a cart (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अनस्">anas</a></b>) and of a chariot (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120813"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120813">3</a></sup> See also 1. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="युग">yuga</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120813"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120813">↑</a> Rv. viii. 77, 3; 91, 7; x. 156, 3, where only the <a class="vei_120a" href="ख">kha</a> is referred to.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> the adjective <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सु-ख">su-kha</a>,</i> ‘having a good axle-hole,’ ‘running easily’; later, ‘agreeable.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120813"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120813">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 12, 1 (Mādhyaṃdina; v. 10, 1 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120813"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120813">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, 1, 3, 6; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 333.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 247.</li></ol></div>

खंग|खङ्ग|ఖంగ|ఖఙ్గ|ಖಂಗ|ಖಙ್ಗ|khaṅga|khaMga|khanga|_vei_120@814|_vei1_pn213
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@813">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@815">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn212">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn213">1:213</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn214">↷</a><hr><p><b>Khaṅga.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="खड्ग">khaḍga</a>.</b></p></div>

खड्ग|ఖడ్గ|ಖಡ್ಗ|khaḍga|khaDga|_vei_120@815|_vei1_pn213
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@814">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@816">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn212">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn213">1:213</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn214">↷</a><hr><p><b>Khaḍga</b> is the reading in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120815"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120815">1</a></sup> of the name of an animal which, in the text of the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120815"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120815">2</a></sup> variously appears as <a class="vei_120a" href="खंग">khaṅga</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="खड्ग">khaḍga</a>. The rhinoceros seems clearly to be meant.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120815"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120815">3</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120815"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120815">4</a></sup> a rhinoceros hide is mentioned as the covering of a chariot.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120815"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120815">↑</a> iii. 14, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120815"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120815">↑</a> xxiv. 40.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120815"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120815">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 86.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120815"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120815">↑</a> xiv. 33, 26 (<i>khāḍga-<a class="vei_120a" href="कवच">kavaca</a> aśvaratha</i>)</li></ol></div>

खण्डिक औद्भारि|ఖణ్డిక ఔద్భారి|ಖಣ್ಡಿಕ ಔದ್ಭಾರಿ|khaṇḍika audbhāri|khaNDika audbhaari|_vei_120@816|_vei1_pn213
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@815">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@817">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn212">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn213">1:213</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn214">↷</a><hr><p><b>Khaṇḍika Audbhāri</b> (‘descendant of Udbhāra’) is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120816"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120816">1</a></sup> as a teacher of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="केशिन्">keśin</a>,</b> and in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120816"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120816">2</a></sup> as having been defeated by <a class="vei_120a" href="केशिन्">keśin</a> as a sacrificer. A Khāṇḍika appears in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120816"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120816">3</a></sup> as an enemy of <a class="vei_120a" href="केशिन्">keśin</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120816"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120816">↑</a> xi. 8, 4, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120816"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120816">↑</a> i. 4, 12, where <a class="vei_120a" href="षण्डिक">ṣaṇḍika</a> is the reading of the MSS.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120816"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120816">↑</a> Caland, <i>Über das rituelle <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> des <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>,</i> 20.</li></ol></div>

खदिर|ఖదిర|ಖದಿರ|khadira|_vei_120@817|_vei1_pn213
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@816">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@818">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn212">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn213">1:213</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn214">↷</a><hr><p><b>Khadira</b> is mentioned frequently from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120817">1</a></sup> onwards<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120817">2</a></sup> as a tree with hard wood<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120817">3</a></sup> --the <i>Acacia catechu.</i> The <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वत्थ">aśvattha</a></b> is referred to as engrafting itself upon it in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120817">4</a></sup>  and from it the climbing plant <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अरुन्धती">arundhatī</a></b> is said to have sprung.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120817">5</a></sup> The <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्रुव">sruva</a></i> or sacrificial ladle is spoken of as having been made from it,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120817">6</a></sup> no doubt because of its hardness. It is in the same passage also said to have sprung from the sap (<i>rasa</i>) of the Gāyatrī. There is no clear reference to Catechu having been prepared from its core,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120817">7</a></sup> as it was later. The core (<i>sāra</i>) was used for making amulets.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120817">8</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120817">↑</a> iii. 53, 19.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120817">↑</a> Av. iii. 6, 1; v. 5, 5; viii. 8, 3; x. 6, 6; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 9, 3, etc. So <i>khādira,</i> ‘made of <a class="vei_120a" href="खदिर">khadira</a> wood,’ <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 5, 7, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 3, 20; iii. 6, 2, 12 etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120817">↑</a> Av. x. 6, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120817">↑</a> iii. 6, 1. <i>Cf.</i> viii. 8, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120817">↑</a> Av. v. 5. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120817">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 5, 7, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120817">↑</a> It is called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="बहु">bahu</a>-sāra,</i> ‘of great strength,’ in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 4, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120817">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xii. 8.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 58, 58.</li></ol></div>

ख-द्योत|खद्योत|ख|द्योत|ఖ-ద్యోత|ఖద్యోత|ఖ|ద్యోత|ಖ-ದ್ಯೋತ|ಖದ್ಯೋತ|ಖ|ದ್ಯೋತ|kha-dyota|khadyota|kha|dyota|_vei_120@818|_vei1_pn214
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@817">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@819">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn213">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn214">1:214</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn215">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kha-dyota</b> (‘sky-illuminator’), ‘the firefly,’ is mentioned in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 7, 3. 5).</p></div>

खनित्र|ఖనిత్ర|ಖನಿತ್ರ|khanitra|_vei_120@819|_vei1_pn214
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@818">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@820">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn213">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn214">1:214</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn215">↷</a><hr><p><b>Khanitra,</b> a ‘shovel’ or ‘spade’ for digging, is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120819"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120819">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120819"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120819">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120819"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120819">↑</a> i. 179, 6 (possibly metaphorical: see Oldenberg. <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 172; the passage is obscure).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120819"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120819">↑</a> Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 2, 4, etc.</li></ol></div>

खनित्रिम|ఖనిత్రిమ|ಖನಿತ್ರಿಮ|khanitrima|_vei_120@820|_vei1_pn214
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@819">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@821">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn213">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn214">1:214</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn215">↷</a><hr><p><b>Khanitrima,</b> ‘produced by digging,’ as an epithet of <i>āpaḥ,</i> ‘waters,’ clearly refers to artificial water channels used for irrigation, as practised in the times of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120820">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120820">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120820">↑</a> vii. 49, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120820">↑</a> i. 6, 4; xix. 2, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 236; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 466.</li></ol></div>

खर|ఖర|ಖರ|khara|_vei_120@821|_vei1_pn214
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@820">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@822">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn213">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn214">1:214</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn215">↷</a><hr><p><b>Khara,</b> ‘ass,’ is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120821">1</a></sup> where a team of asses is alluded to. Probably the passages in the Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120821">2</a></sup> where the word is used to denote an earth mound on which the sacrificial vessels were placed, presuppose the sense of ‘ass,’ the mound being shaped in this form.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120821">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120821">↑</a> iii. 2, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120821">↑</a> v. 1, 2, 15; xiv. 1, 2, 17; 2, 2, 30.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120821">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

खर्गला|ఖర్గలా|ಖರ್ಗಲಾ|khargalā|khargalaa|_vei_120@822|_vei1_pn215
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@821">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@823">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn214">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn215">1:215</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn216">↷</a><hr><p><b>Khargalā</b> is an ‘owl’ or other nocturnal bird mentioned in one passage of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120822"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120822">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120822"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120822">↑</a> vii. 104, 17. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, 107; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 93.</li></ol></div>

खर्जूर|ఖర్జూర|ಖರ್ಜೂರ|kharjūra|kharjuura|kharjoora|_vei_120@823|_vei1_pn215
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@822">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@824">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn214">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn215">1:215</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn216">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kharjūra</b> is the name of a tree (<i>Phœnix silvestris</i>) which is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120823"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120823">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120823"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120823">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 4, 9, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 10; xxxvi. 7.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 63.</li></ol></div>

खल|ఖల|ಖಲ|khala|_vei_120@824|_vei1_pn215
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@823">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@825">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn214">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn215">1:215</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn216">↷</a><hr><p><b>Khala,</b> the ‘threshing-floor,’ is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120824">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120824">2</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृषि">kṛṣi</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120824">↑</a> x. 48, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120824">↑</a> xi. 3, 9; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="खल">khala</a>-ja,</i> ‘produced on a threshing-floor,’ viii. 6, 15; <i>khalya,</i> ‘being on a threshing-floor,’ Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9, 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 238.</li></ol></div>

खल-कुल|खलकुल|खल|कुल|ఖల-కుల|ఖలకుల|ఖల|కుల|ಖಲ-ಕುಲ|ಖಲಕುಲ|ಖಲ|ಕುಲ|khala-kula|khalakula|khala|kula|_vei_120@825|_vei1_pn215
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@824">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@826">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn214">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn215">1:215</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn216">↷</a><hr><p><b>Khala-kula</b> is a word occurring in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120825">1</a></sup> where Sāyaṇa glosses it by Kulattha, a kind of pulse (<i>Dolichos uniflorus</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120825">↑</a> vi. 3. 22 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 3, 13 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>). <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien</i> 1, 355.</li></ol></div>

खल्व|ఖల్వ|ಖಲ್ವ|khalva|_vei_120@826|_vei1_pn215
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@825">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@827">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn214">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn215">1:215</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn216">↷</a><hr><p><b>Khalva</b> is some sort of grain or leguminous plant, perhaps, as Weber<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120826"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120826">1</a></sup> thinks, the <i>Phaselus radiatus.</i> It is mentioned with other grains of all sorts in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120826"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120826">2</a></sup> and as being crushed with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृषद्">dṛṣad</a></b> in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120826"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120826">3</a></sup> It occurs also in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120826"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120826">4</a></sup> where Śaṅkara glosses it with <i>niṣpāva.</i></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120826"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120826">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 355.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120826"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120826">↑</a> xviii. 12, where Mahīdhara glosses it by <i>caṇaka,</i> ‘chick-pea.’</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120826"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120826">↑</a> ii. 31. 1; v. 23, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120826"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120826">↑</a> vi. 3. 22 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 3, 13 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 241.</li></ol></div>

खाण्डव|ఖాణ్డవ|ಖಾಣ್ಡವ|khāṇḍava|khaaNDava|_vei_120@827|_vei1_pn215
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@826">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@828">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn214">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn215">1:215</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn216">↷</a><hr><p><b>Khāṇḍava</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120827"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120827">1</a></sup> as one of the boundaries of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>.</b> There seems no reason to doubt its identity with the famous <a class="vei_120a" href="खाण्डव">khāṇḍava</a> forest of the Mahābhārata. The name occurs also in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120827"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120827">2</a></sup> and the Śāṭyāyanaka.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120827"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120827">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120827"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120827">↑</a> v. 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120827"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120827">↑</a> xxv. 3, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120827"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120827">↑</a> Max Müller, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> ^2 iv. ci.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 78.</li></ol></div>

खादि|ఖాది|ಖಾದಿ|khādi|khaadi|_vei_120@828|_vei1_pn216
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@827">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@829">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn215">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn216">1:216</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn217">↷</a><hr><p><b>Khādi</b> occurs frequently in the Rigveda denoting either anklets<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120828">1</a></sup> or armlets,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120828">2</a></sup> or sometimes rings on the hands.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120828">3</a></sup> Max Müller<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120828">4</a></sup> considers that the word means quoits, the later <a class="vei_120a" href="चक्र">cakra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120828">5</a></sup> The rings were sometimes of gold.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120828">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120828">↑</a> v. 54, 11, and perhaps 53, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120828">↑</a> This is what Khādis on the shoulders must mean, i. 166, 9; vii. 56, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120828">↑</a> i. 168, 3; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="खादि">khādi</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="हस्त">hasta</a>,</i> ‘with rings on the hands,’ 5, 58, 2. So Roth takes Khādin in vi. 16, 40; St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> Khādin occurs also in ii. 34, 2; x. 38, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120828">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 120, 230.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120828">↑</a> <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="वृष-खादि">vṛṣa-khādi</a>,</i> Rv. i. 64, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120828">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्य">hiraṇya</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="खादि">khādi</a>,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Śranta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 5, 12; viii. 23, 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 262; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 149.</li></ol></div>

खारी|खारि|ఖారీ|ఖారి|ಖಾರೀ|ಖಾರಿ|khārī|khāri|khaarii|khaari|khaaree|_vei_120@829|_vei1_pn216
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@828">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@830">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn215">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn216">1:216</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn217">↷</a><hr><p><b>Khārī</b> designates a measure of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> in one passage of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120829"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120829">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120829"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120829">↑</a> iv. 32, 17. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 280.</li></ol></div>

खार्गलि|ఖార్గలి|ಖಾರ್ಗಲಿ|khārgali|khaargali|_vei_120@830|_vei1_pn216
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@829">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@831">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn215">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn216">1:216</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn217">↷</a><hr><p><b>Khārgali,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="खर्गला">khargalā</a> or <a class="vei_120a" href="खृगल">khṛgala</a>,’ is the metronymic or patronymic of Luśākapi.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120830"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120830">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120830"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120830">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxx. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvii. 4, 3.</li></ol></div>

खिल|खिल|ఖిల_౧|ఖిల|ಖಿಲ_೧|ಖಿಲ|khila_1|khila|_vei_120@831|_vei1_pn216
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@830">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@832">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn215">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn216">1:216</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn217">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Khila,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120831">1</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="खिल्य">khilya</a></b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120831">2</a></sup> appear to have the same meaning. According to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120831">3</a></sup> these terms denote the waste land lying between cultivated fields; but he admits that this sense does not suit the passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120831">4</a></sup> in which it is said that the god places the worshipper on an unbroken <a class="vei_120a" href="खिल्य">khilya</a> (<i>abhinne khilye</i>), and he accordingly conjectures the reading <i>akhilyabhinne,</i> ‘land unbroken by barren strips.’ Pischel<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120831">5</a></sup> thinks that the meaning intended is broad lands, which were used for the pasturing of the cattle of the community, and were not broken up by cultivated fields. Oldenberg,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120831">6</a></sup> however, points out that the sense is rather the land which lay between cultivated fields, but which need not be deemed to have been unfertile, as Roth thought. This agrees with the fact that in Vedic times separate fields were already known: see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षेत्र">kṣetra</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120831">↑</a> Av. vii. 115, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 3, 4, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120831">↑</a> Rv. vi. 28, 2; x. 142, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120831">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> So Whitney on Av., <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120831">↑</a> vi. 28, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120831">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 205.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120831">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 385, 386.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 236; Ludwig. Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 499; Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiutic Society,</i> 1910, 228.</li></ol></div>

खिल|खिल|ఖిల_౨|ఖిల|ಖಿಲ_೨|ಖಿಲ|khila_2|khila|_vei_120@832|_vei1_pn217
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@831">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@833">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn216">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn217">1:217</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn218">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Khila</b> as a designation of certain hymns appended to the received text of the Rigveda occurs only in the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> period.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120832">1</a></sup> The term is a metaphorical application of the preceding word, ‘a space not filled up,’ ‘a supplement.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120832">↑</a> See Scheftelowitz, <i>Die Apokryphen des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 16 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

खृगल|ఖృగల|ಖೃಗಲ|khṛgala|khRgala|khRigala|khRugala|khRRigala|_vei_120@833|_vei1_pn217
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@832">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@834">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn216">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn217">1:217</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn218">↷</a><hr><p><b>Khṛgala,</b> or, as the Paippalāda recension of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120833"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120833">1</a></sup> has it, Khugila, is an obscure expression found in two passages only--once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120833"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120833">2</a></sup> and once in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_120833"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_120833">1b</a></sup> In the former the meaning ‘crutch’ seems required; in the latter Sāyaṇa glosses it by ‘armour’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="तनु">tanu</a>-trāṇa</i>), but the sense is quite uncertain.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120833"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120833">↑</a> iii. 9. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120833"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120833">↑</a> ii. 39, 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 340; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 98.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_120833"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_120833">↑</a> iii. 9. 3.</li></ol></div>

खेल|ఖేల|ಖೇಲ|khela|_vei_120@834|_vei1_pn217
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@833">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@835">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn216">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn217">1:217</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn218">↷</a><hr><p><b>Khela</b> occurs in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120834"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120834">1</a></sup> where Pischel<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120834"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120834">2</a></sup> considers that a god, Vivasvant, is meant, and that races were run in his honour, explaining thus the phrase <i>ājā khelasya,</i> as ‘in the race of <a class="vei_120a" href="खेल">khela</a>.’ Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120834"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120834">3</a></sup> thinks that a man is meant, and Sieg,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120834"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120834">4</a></sup> following Sāyaṇa, sees in him a king whose <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> was <a class="vei_120a" href="अगस्त्य">agastya</a>. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अंशु">aṃśu</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120834"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120834">↑</a> i. 116, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120834"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120834">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 171-173.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120834"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120834">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120834"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120834">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 127, 128.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 4. 28.</li></ol></div>

गंगा|गङ्गा|గంగా|గఙ్గా|ಗಂಗಾ|ಗಙ್ಗಾ|gaṅgā|gaMgaa|gangaa|_vei_120@835|_vei1_pn217
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@834">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@836">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn216">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn217">1:217</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn218">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gaṅgā,</b> the modern Ganges, is mentioned directly in the Rigveda only once, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="नदी">nadī</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="स्तुति">stuti</a> or ‘Praise of Rivers.’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120835">1</a></sup> But it is also referred to in the derivative form <i>gāṅgyaḥ</i> as an epithet of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उरुकक्ष">urukakṣa</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120835">3</a></sup> The name of this river does not occur</p><p><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120835">2</a></sup>  in the other Saṃhitā, but appears in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120835">4</a></sup> where victories of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दौःषन्ति">dauḥṣanti</a></b> on both <a class="vei_120a" href="गंगा">gaṅgā</a> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a></b> are referred to, and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120835">5</a></sup> especial honour is assigned to those who dwell between the <a class="vei_120a" href="गंगा">gaṅgā</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a>, this being. To doubt, the region in which that text originated. The identification of the <a class="vei_120a" href="गंगा">gaṅgā</a> with the Apayā<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120835">6</a></sup> made by Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120835">7</a></sup> must be rejected: see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आपया">āpayā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120835">↑</a> x. 75, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120835">↑</a> The reference to the <a class="vei_120a" href="गंगा">gaṅgā</a> remains, even if with Oldenberg (<i>ṚgvedaNoten,</i> 1, 396) we assume that a thicket i meant, and not a proper name (<i>cf.</i> Wackernagel, <i>Altindische Grammatik,</i> 2, 288). See also Weber, Proceedings of the Berlin Academy, 1898, 563, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120835">↑</a> vi. 45, 31.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120835">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 11. The victory on the <a class="vei_120a" href="गंगा">gaṅgā</a> represents the farthest extent of <a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a> or <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a> rule. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 23, and a verse in the Vaitāna <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxxiv. 9, where the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> is also referred to.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120835">↑</a> ii. 20.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120835">↑</a> Rv. iii. 23, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120835">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 200.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 4. 5.</li></ol></div>

गज|గజ|ಗಜ|gaja|_vei_120@836|_vei1_pn218
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@835">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@837">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn217">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn218">1:218</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn219">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gaja,</b> the common name of the elephant in Epic<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120836"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120836">1</a></sup> and later Sanskrit, is only found in the late Adbhuta <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120836"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120836">2</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हस्तिन्">hastin</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120836"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120836">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 265, 269.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120836"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120836">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 39.</li></ol></div>

गणक|గణక|ಗಣಕ|gaṇaka|gaNaka|_vei_120@837|_vei1_pn218
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@836">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@838">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn217">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn218">1:218</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn219">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gaṇaka,</b> ‘an astrologer,’ occurs in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha, or human sacrifice, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120837"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120837">1</a></sup> See also <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्रदर्श">nakṣatradarśa</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120837"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120837">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 20; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 15, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Weber. <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 78.</li></ol></div>

गन्धर्वायण बालेय|గన్ధర్వాయణ బాలేయ|ಗನ್ಧರ್ವಾಯಣ ಬಾಲೇಯ|gandharvāyaṇa bāleya|gandharvaayaNa baaleya|_vei_120@838|_vei1_pn218
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@837">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@839">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn217">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn218">1:218</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn219">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gandharvāyaṇa Bāleya</b> (‘descendant of Bali’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आग्निवेश्य">āgniveśya</a></b> is mentioned as a <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (xx. 25).</p></div>

गन्धार|గన్ధార|ಗನ್ಧಾರ|gandhāra|gandhaara|_vei_120@839|_vei1_pn218
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@838">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@840">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn217">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn218">1:218</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn219">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gandhāra</b> is a later form of the name of the people called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गन्धारि">gandhāri</a></b> in the Rigveda and Atharvaveda. In the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120839"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120839">1</a></sup> the Gandhāras are referred to as being distant from the writer. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गान्धार">gāndhāra</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120839"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120839">↑</a> vi. 14, 1. 2. See Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 399, n.; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 219, n. On the other hand, Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 15. 106, thought the passage meant that the Gandhāras were near the writer.</li></ol></div>

गन्धारि|గన్ధారి|ಗನ್ಧಾರಿ|gandhāri|gandhaari|_vei_120@840|_vei1_pn219
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@839">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@841">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn218">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn219">1:219</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn220">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gandhāri</b> is the name of a people in the north-west of India. In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120840"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120840">1</a></sup> the good wool of the sheep of the Gandhāris is referred to. The Gandhāris are also mentioned with the <b>Mūjavants, Angas,</b> and <b>Magadhas</b> in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120840"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120840">2</a></sup> Gandhāris<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120840"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120840">3</a></sup> or Gāndhāris<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120840"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120840">4</a></sup> are also spoken of in the <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120840"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120840">5</a></sup> Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120840"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120840">6</a></sup> considers that they were settled in Vedic times on the south bank of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुभा">kubhā</a></b> up to its mouth in the Indus, and for some distance down the east side of the Indus itself. They later formed a portion of the Persian empire, and detachments of Gandarians accompanied Xerxes in his expedition against Greece.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120840"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120840">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120840"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120840">↑</a> i. 126, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120840"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120840">↑</a> v. 22, 14. The latter two tribes are apparently the Eastern limit of the poet's knowledge, the two former the Northern.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120840"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120840">↑</a> Hiraṇyakeśi <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvii. 6; Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxii. 6, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120840"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120840">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxi. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120840"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120840">↑</a> See Caland, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 56, 553.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120840"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120840">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 30, 31.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120840"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120840">↑</a> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 23.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 206.</li></ol></div>

गभस्ति|గభస్తి|ಗಭಸ್ತಿ|gabhasti|_vei_120@841|_vei1_pn219
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@840">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@842">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn218">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn219">1:219</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn220">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gabhasti</b> denotes, according to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120841"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120841">1</a></sup> the pole of a chariot in the epithet <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्यूम-गभस्ति">syūma-gabhasti</a>,</i> ‘having reins as a pole,’ used of the car of the gods in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120841"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120841">2</a></sup> and independently in the plural in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120841"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120841">3</a></sup> The meaning is, however, doubtful.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120841"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120841">4</a></sup> Roth<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120841"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120841">5</a></sup> himself suggests that <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्यूम-गभस्ति">syūma-gabhasti</a></i> may refer to a sort of double reins.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120841"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120841">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120841"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120841">↑</a> i. 122, 5; vii. 71, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120841"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120841">↑</a> ii. 7, 13, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120841"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120841">↑</a> See Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 55.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120841"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120841">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>., s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

गय|गय|గయ_౧|గయ|ಗಯ_೧|ಗಯ|gaya_1|gaya|_vei_120@842|_vei1_pn219
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@841">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@843">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn218">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn219">1:219</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn220">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Gaya,</b> ‘house,’ is a common word in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120842"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120842">1</a></sup> and sometimes occurs later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120842"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120842">2</a></sup> As its sense includes the inmates as well as their belongings, it is equivalent to ‘household.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120842"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120842">↑</a> i. 74, 2; v. 10, 3; 44, 7; vi. 2, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120842"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120842">↑</a> Av. vi. 3, 3; 84, 1; <b>Vājasaneyi</b> Saṃhitā, xxvii. 3.</li></ol></div>

गय प्लात|गय प्लात|గయ ప్లాత_౨|గయ ప్లాత|ಗಯ ಪ್ಲಾತ_೨|ಗಯ ಪ್ಲಾತ|gaya plāta_2|gaya plāta|gaya plaata_2|gaya plaata|_vei_120@843|_vei1_pn219
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@842">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@844">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn218">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn219">1:219</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn220">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Gaya Plāta</b> (‘son of Plati’) is referred to in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120843"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120843">1</a></sup> two hymns of which he clearly claims to have composed, and which are attributed to him in the Sarvānukramaṇī and the  <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120843"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120843">2</a></sup> In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120843"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120843">3</a></sup> he appears with <a class="vei_120a" href="असित">asita</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="कश्यप">kaśyapa</a> as a half-mythical magician.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120843"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120843">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120843"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120843">↑</a> x. 63, 17; 64, 17 (as son of <a class="vei_120a" href="प्लति">plati</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120843"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120843">↑</a> v. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120843"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120843">↑</a> i. 14, 4. <i>Cf.</i> also the <a class="vei_120a" href="खिल">khila</a> after Rv. v. 51, 15; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 214.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120843"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120843">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 17, 403.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 133; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 460.</li></ol></div>

गर|गर|గర_౧|గర|ಗರ_೧|ಗರ|gara_1|gara|_vei_120@844|_vei1_pn220
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@843">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@845">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn219">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn220">1:220</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn221">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Gara,</b> ‘poison,’ is referred to in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120844">1</a></sup> in the compound <i><a class="vei_120a" href="गर">gara</a>-gīrṇa,</i> ‘poisoned.’ In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120844">2</a></sup> it means simply a ‘fluid.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120844">↑</a> v. 18, 13. <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="गर">gara</a></i> alone, <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xix. 4, 2 (see <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 33); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 9, 10; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="गर">gara</a>-gir,</i> ‘poisoned,’ <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvii. 1, 9; xix. 4, 2. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120844">↑</a> xi. 5, 8, 6.</li></ol></div>

गर|गर|గర_౨|గర|ಗರ_೨|ಗರ|gara_2|gara|_vei_120@845|_vei1_pn220
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@844">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@846">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn219">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn220">1:220</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn221">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Gara</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120845"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120845">1</a></sup> as the author of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> or Chant, and as a friend of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120845"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120845">↑</a> ix. 2, 16. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 52.</li></ol></div>

गर्ग|గర్గ|ಗರ್ಗ|garga|_vei_120@846|_vei1_pn220
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@845">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@847">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn219">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn220">1:220</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn221">↷</a><hr><p><b>Garga</b> is the name of a sage who is not mentioned in any of the Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120846"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120846">1</a></sup> but his descendants, the <i>Gargāḥ Prāvareyāḥ,</i> are referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120846"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120846">2</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="गर्ग">garga</a> himself does not occur till the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> period.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120846"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120846">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120846"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120846">↑</a> The authorship of Rv. vi. 47 is attributed to <a class="vei_120a" href="गर्ग">garga</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="भारद्वाज">bhāradvāja</a> in the Anukramaṇī.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120846"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120846">↑</a> xiii. 12. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 374.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120846"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120846">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गर्ग">garga</a>-trirātra, <a class="vei_120a" href="गर्ग">garga</a>-tryaha, a three-night or three-day feast of <a class="vei_120a" href="गर्ग">garga</a>. See Āśvalāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 22. 2; Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxiii. 2, 8.</li></ol></div>

गर्गर|గర్గర|ಗರ್ಗರ|gargara|_vei_120@847|_vei1_pn220
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@846">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@848">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn219">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn220">1:220</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn221">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gargara,</b> apparently the designation of a musical instrument, is mentioned once in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120847"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120847">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120847"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120847">↑</a> viii. 69, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 144, n. 1; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 289.</li></ol></div>

गर्त|గర్త|ಗರ್ತ|garta|_vei_120@848|_vei1_pn220
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@847">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@849">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn219">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn220">1:220</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn221">↷</a><hr><p><b>Garta</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120848"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120848">1</a></sup> primarily denotes the seat of the chariot on which the archer sat. It seems to have been of considerable  size, being described as <i>bṛhant,</i><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120848"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120848">2</a></sup> ‘large.’ The word then comes to denote the chariot<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120848"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120848">3</a></sup> itself, either really or metaphorically.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120848"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120848">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120848"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120848">↑</a> vi. 20, 9. Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 246, 247. Zimmer wrongly takes this passage to refer to standing in the car. See Hopkins. <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 238, 239; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 48, and <i>cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="गर्त">garta</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="सद्">sad</a>,</i> ‘sitting on the car seat,’ in Rv. ii. 33. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120848"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120848">↑</a> v. 62, 8; 68, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120848"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120848">↑</a> So probably in v. 62, 5; <i>gartā-ruh,</i> ‘mounting the chariot,’ in Rv. i. 124, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 5, may refer merely to mounting the seat. The passage is obscure; see Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Kommentar,</i> 22.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120848"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120848">↑</a> vii. 64, 4 (of the hymn).</li></ol></div>

गर्दभ|గర్దభ|ಗರ್ದಭ|gardabha|_vei_120@849|_vei1_pn221
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@848">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@850">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn220">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn221">1:221</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn222">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gardabha,</b> ‘the ass,’ is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120849">1</a></sup> as inferior to the horse. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā he again appears as inferior to the horse,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120849">2</a></sup> but at the same time as the best bearer of burdens (<i>bhāra-bhāritama</i>) among animals.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120849">3</a></sup> The same authority styles the ass <i><a class="vei_120a" href="द्वि-रेतस्">dvi-retas</a>,</i> ‘having double seed,’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120849">4</a></sup> in allusion to his breeding with the mare as well as the she-ass. The smallness of the young of the ass, and his capacity for eating, are both referred to.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120849">5</a></sup> The disagreeable cry of the animal is mentioned in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120849">6</a></sup> and in allusion to this the term ‘ass’ is applied opprobriously to a singer in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120849">7</a></sup> A hundred asses are spoken of as a gift to a singer in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वालखिल्य">vālakhilya</a> hymn.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120849">8</a></sup> The mule (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वतर">aśvatara</a></i>) is the offspring of an ass and a mare, the latter, like the ass, being called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="द्विरेतस्">dviretas</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120849">9</a></sup> ‘receiving double seed,’ for similar reasons. The male ass is often also termed <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रासभ">rāsabha</a>.</b> The female ass, <a class="vei_120a" href="गर्दभी">gardabhī</a>, is mentioned in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120849">10</a></sup> and the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120849">11</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120849">↑</a> iii. 53, 23. A car drawn by asses is referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 9; see also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="खर">khara</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120849">↑</a> v. 1, 2, 1. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120849">↑</a> v. 1, 5, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120849">↑</a> v. 1, 5, 5; vii. 1, 1, 2; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 57, 4 (Oertel, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 177-180): of the <a class="vei_120a" href="रासभ">rāsabha</a>, Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 3, 1, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120849">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 1, 5, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120849">↑</a> viii. 6, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120849">↑</a> i. 29, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120849">↑</a> viii. 56, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120849">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 1, 1, 2. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 1, 6; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 57, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120849">↑</a> x. 1, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120849">↑</a> i. 4, 8. For other references to the ass as <a class="vei_120a" href="गर्दभ">gardabha</a>, see Av. v. 31, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 34; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 5, 1, 9; xii. 7, 1, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 232, 233.</li></ol></div>

गर्दभी-मुख|गर्दभीमुख|गर्दभी|गर्दभि|मुख|గర్దభీ-ముఖ|గర్దభీముఖ|గర్దభీ|గర్దభి|ముఖ|ಗರ್ದಭೀ-ಮುಖ|ಗರ್ದಭೀಮುಖ|ಗರ್ದಭೀ|ಗರ್ದಭಿ|ಮುಖ|gardabhī-mukha|gardabhīmukha|gardabhī|gardabhi|mukha|gardabhii-mukha|gardabhiimukha|gardabhii|gardabhee-mukha|gardabheemukha|gardabhee|_vei_120@850|_vei1_pn221
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@849">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@851">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn220">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn221">1:221</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn222">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gardabhī-mukha</b> is mentioned as a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120850"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120850">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120850"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120850">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 384.</li></ol></div>

गर्दभी-विपीत|गर्दभीविपीत|गर्दभी|गर्दभि|विपीत|गर्दभी-विभीत|गर्दभीविभीत|विभीत|గర్దభీ-విపీత|గర్దభీవిపీత|గర్దభీ|గర్దభి|విపీత|గర్దభీ-విభీత|గర్దభీవిభీత|విభీత|ಗರ್ದಭೀ-ವಿಪೀತ|ಗರ್ದಭೀವಿಪೀತ|ಗರ್ದಭೀ|ಗರ್ದಭಿ|ವಿಪೀತ|ಗರ್ದಭೀ-ವಿಭೀತ|ಗರ್ದಭೀವಿಭೀತ|ವಿಭೀತ|gardabhī-vipīta|gardabhīvipīta|gardabhī|gardabhi|vipīta|gardabhī-vibhīta|gardabhīvibhīta|vibhīta|gardabhii-vipiita|gardabhiivipiita|gardabhii|vipiita|gardabhii-vibhiita|gardabhiivibhiita|vibhiita|gardabhee-vipeeta|gardabheevipeeta|gardabhee|vipeeta|gardabhee-vibheeta|gardabheevibheeta|vibheeta|_vei_120@851|_vei1_pn222
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@850">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@852">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn221">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn222">1:222</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn223">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gardabhī-vipīta,</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गर्दभी-विभीत">gardabhī-vibhīta</a>,</b> is the name of a teacher who was a <a class="vei_120a" href="भारद्वाज">bhāradvāja</a> and a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a>,</b> mentioned in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.</p><p>1) iv. 1, 11 (Mādhyaṃdina = iv. 1, 5 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</p></div>

गर्मुत्|గర్ముత్|ಗರ್ಮುತ್|garmut|_vei_120@852|_vei1_pn222
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@851">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@853">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn221">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn222">1:222</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn223">↷</a><hr><p><b>Garmut</b> is the name of a kind of wild bean mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120852"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120852">1</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120852"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120852">2</a></sup> has the form Ganmut, which is probably a false reading. The adjective <i>gārmuta,</i> ‘made from the <a class="vei_120a" href="गर्मुत्">garmut</a> bean,’ is found in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120852"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120852">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120852"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120852">↑</a> ii. 4, 4, 1. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120852"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120852">↑</a> x. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120852"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120852">↑</a> ii. 2, 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 71.</li></ol></div>

गलुन्त|గలున్త|ಗಲುನ್ತ|galunta|_vei_120@853|_vei1_pn222
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@852">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@854">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn221">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn222">1:222</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn223">↷</a><hr><p><b>Galunta</b> is a word occurring only once in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120853"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120853">1</a></sup> apparently in the sense of ‘swelling,’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120853"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120853">2</a></sup> but Whitney<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120853"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120853">3</a></sup> translates it by ‘neck.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120853"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120853">↑</a> vi. 83, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120853"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120853">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>Proceedings of the American Oriental Society,</i> October, 1887, xvi; <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 505.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120853"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120853">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 343.</li></ol></div>

गलूनस आर्क्षाकायण|గలూనస ఆర్క్షాకాయణ|ಗಲೂನಸ ಆರ್ಕ್ಷಾಕಾಯಣ|galūnasa ārkṣākāyaṇa|galuunasa aarkShaakaayaNa|galoonasa aarkShaakaayaNa|galuunasa aarxaakaayaNa|_vei_120@854|_vei1_pn222
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@853">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@855">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn221">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn222">1:222</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn223">↷</a><hr><p><b>Galūnasa Ārkṣākāyaṇa</b> (‘descendant of Ṛkṣāka’) is mentioned as a teacher in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 38, 4).</p></div>

गवय|గవయ|ಗವಯ|gavaya|_vei_120@855|_vei1_pn222
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@854">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@856">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn221">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn222">1:222</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn223">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gavaya,</b> the name of a species of ox (<i>Bos gavacus</i>) occurs frequently from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120855">1</a></sup> onwards.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120855">2</a></sup> It is mentioned with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौर">gaura</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="महिष">mahiṣa</a></b> in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120855">3</a></sup> where also a wild <a class="vei_120a" href="गवय">gavaya</a> is spoken of.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120855">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120855">↑</a> iv. 21, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120855">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvi. 17; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 28; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 8; iii. 34; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 3, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 3, 14, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120855">↑</a> xxiv. 28.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120855">↑</a> xiii. 49; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Samhitā, iv. 2, 10, 3; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 7, 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvi. 17.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 83, 84.</li></ol></div>

गवाशिर्|గవాశిర్|ಗವಾಶಿರ್|gavāśir|gavaashir|_vei_120@856|_vei1_pn222
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@855">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@857">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn221">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn222">1:222</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn223">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gavāśir,</b> ‘mixed with milk,’ is a frequent epithet of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a></b> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120856"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120856">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120856"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120856">↑</a> i. 137, 1; 187, 9; ii. 41, 3; iii. 32, 2; 42, 1. 7; viii. 52, 10; 101, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 279.</li></ol></div>

गव्-इष्टि|गविष्टि|गव्|इष्टि|గవ్-ఇష్టి|గవిష్టి|గవ్|ఇష్టి|ಗವ್-ಇಷ್ಟಿ|ಗವಿಷ್ಟಿ|ಗವ್|ಇಷ್ಟಿ|gav-iṣṭi|gaviṣṭi|gav|iṣṭi|gav-iShTi|gaviShTi|iShTi|_vei_120@857|_vei1_pn223
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@856">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@858">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn222">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn223">1:223</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn224">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gav-iṣṭi</b> (lit. ‘desire of cows’) in several passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120857"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120857">1</a></sup> denotes ‘conflict’ or ‘battle,’ evidently with reference to cattle raids. <a class="vei_120a" href="गव्या">gavyā</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120857"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120857">2</a></sup> is similarly used.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120857"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120857">↑</a> i. 91, 23; iii. 47, 4; v. 63, 5; vi. 31, 3; 47, 20; 59, 7; viii. 24, 2; ix. 76, 2. So Av. iv. 24, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120857"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120857">↑</a> Rv. vii. 18, 7.</li></ol></div>

गवि-ष्ठिर आत्रेय|गविष्ठिर आत्रेय|गवि|ष्ठिर आत्रेय|గవి-ష్ఠిర ఆత్రేయ|గవిష్ఠిర ఆత్రేయ|గవి|ష్ఠిర ఆత్రేయ|ಗವಿ-ಷ್ಠಿರ ಆತ್ರೇಯ|ಗವಿಷ್ಠಿರ ಆತ್ರೇಯ|ಗವಿ|ಷ್ಠಿರ ಆತ್ರೇಯ|gavi-ṣṭhira ātreya|gaviṣṭhira ātreya|gavi|ṣṭhira ātreya|gavi-ShThira aatreya|gaviShThira aatreya|ShThira aatreya|_vei_120@858|_vei1_pn223
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@857">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@859">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn222">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn223">1:223</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn224">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gavi-<a class="vei_120a" href="ष्ठिर">ṣṭhira</a> Ātreya</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अत्रि">atri</a></b>’) is mentioned as a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>,</b> or seer, in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120858">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120858">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120858">↑</a> v. 1, 12; x. 150, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120858">↑</a> iv. 29, 5. See also Āśvalāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 14. 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 126.</li></ol></div>

गवीधुका|గవీధుకా|ಗವೀಧುಕಾ|gavīdhukā|gaviidhukaa|gaveedhukaa|_vei_120@859|_vei1_pn223
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@858">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@860">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn222">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn223">1:223</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn224">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gavīdhukā,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120859"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120859">1</a></sup> <b>Gavedhukā,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120859"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120859">2</a></sup> is the name of a species of grass (<i>Coix barbata</i>). It is also referred to in the adjectival forms <i>gāvīdhuka</i><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120859"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120859">3</a></sup> and <i>gāvedhuka.</i><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120859"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120859">4</a></sup> It was boiled with rice<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120859"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120859">5</a></sup> (<i>gavīdhukāyavāgā</i>) or barley<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120859"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120859">6</a></sup> (<i>gavedhukā-saktavaḥ</i>) in preparing gruel.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120859"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120859">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 4, 3, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120859"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120859">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 2, 4, 13; 3, 1, 10; xiv. 1, 2, 19.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120859"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120859">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 7, 1; 9, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 3, 6; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 5; iv. 3, 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xv. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120859"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120859">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 2, 4, 11. 13; 3, 1, 10; 3, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120859"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120859">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 4, 3, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120859"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120859">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 1, 1, 8.</li></ol></div>

गव्य|గవ్య|ಗವ್ಯ|gavya|_vei_120@860|_vei1_pn223
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@859">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@861">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn222">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn223">1:223</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn224">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gavya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गव्यूति">gavyūti</a>.</b></p></div>

गव्या|గవ్యా|ಗವ್ಯಾ|gavyā|gavyaa|_vei_120@861|_vei1_pn223
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@860">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@862">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn222">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn223">1:223</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn224">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gavyā.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गविष्टि">gaviṣṭi</a>.</b></p></div>

गव्यूति|గవ్యూతి|ಗವ್ಯೂತಿ|gavyūti|gavyuuti|gavyooti|_vei_120@862|_vei1_pn223
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@861">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@863">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn222">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn223">1:223</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn224">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gavyūti</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120862">1</a></sup> means, according to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120862">2</a></sup> grass land for the pasturing of cattle, in which sense <a class="vei_120a" href="गव्य">gavya</a> is also found.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120862">3</a></sup> Thence it derives the sense of a measure of distance found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120862">4</a></sup> Geldner, on the other hand, takes the original meaning to be ‘road,’ real<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120862">6</a></sup> or metaphorical,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120862">7</a></sup> thence a measure of distance,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120862">8</a></sup> and finally ‘land.’<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120862">9</a></sup></p><p>5) <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 290, 291. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120862">↑</a> i. 25, 16; iii. 62, 16; v. 66, 3; vii. 77, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120862">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120862">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 28; St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 3b.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120862">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvi. 13, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120862">↑</a> Rv. i. 25, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120862">↑</a> Rv. vi. 47, 20; x. 14, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120862">↑</a> Rv. viii. 60, 20, and n. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120862">↑</a> Rv. iii. 62, 16; vii. 62, 5; 65, 4; viii. 5, 6.</li></ol></div>

गांग्य|गाङ्ग्य|గాంగ్య|గాఙ్గ్య|ಗಾಂಗ್ಯ|ಗಾಙ್ಗ್ಯ|gāṅgya|gaaMgya|gaangya|_vei_120@863|_vei1_pn224
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@862">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@864">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn223">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn224">1:224</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn225">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gāṅgya,</b> ‘being on the Ganges,’ is the epithet of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उरुकक्ष">urukakṣa</a></b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120863"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120863">1</a></sup> or of a thicket<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120863"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120863">2</a></sup> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120863"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120863">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120863"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120863">↑</a> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. Cf.</i> Wackernagel, <i>Altindische Grammatik,</i> 2, 288; Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 28.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120863"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120863">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 398.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120863"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120863">↑</a> vi. 45, 31.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 2, 291, n.</li></ol></div>

गांग्यायनि|गाङ्ग्यायनि|గాంగ్యాయని|గాఙ్గ్యాయని|ಗಾಂಗ್ಯಾಯನಿ|ಗಾಙ್ಗ್ಯಾಯನಿ|gāṅgyāyani|gaaMgyaayani|gaangyaayani|_vei_120@864|_vei1_pn224
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@863">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@865">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn223">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn224">1:224</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn225">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gāṅgyāyani,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="गांग्य">gāṅgya</a>,’ occurs as the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चित्र">citra</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120864"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120864">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120864"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120864">↑</a> i. 1. There is a <i>v.l.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="गार्ग्यायणि">gārgyāyaṇi</a>. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 395.</li></ol></div>

गातु|గాతు|ಗಾತು|gātu|gaatu|_vei_120@865|_vei1_pn224
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@864">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@866">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn223">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn224">1:224</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn225">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gātu.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a>.</b></p></div>

गाथा|గాథా|ಗಾಥಾ|gāthā|gaathaa|_vei_120@866|_vei1_pn224
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@865">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@867">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn223">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn224">1:224</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn225">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gāthā</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120866">1</a></sup> usually means only ‘song,’ ‘verse,’ like <a class="vei_120a" href="गातु">gātu</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120866">2</a></sup> In one passage,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120866">3</a></sup> however, it already has a more special sense, as it is classed with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नाराशंसी">nārāśaṃsī</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रैभी">raibhī</a>,</b> a collocation repeatedly found later.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120866">4</a></sup> The commentators identify the three terms with certain verses of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120866">5</a></sup> but Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120866">6</a></sup> has shown that this identification is incorrect for the Rigveda. Gāthās are often mentioned elsewhere,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120866">7</a></sup> and are referred to as metrical in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120866">8</a></sup> where the <b>Ṛc, <a class="vei_120a" href="कुम्ब्या">kumbyā</a>,</b> and <a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a> are classed as forms of verse. The <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120866">9</a></sup> distinguishes between Ṛc and <a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a> as divine and human respectively. According to the usage of the  Brāhmaṇas and the liturgical literature, as stated by the St. Petersburg Dictionary, the Gāthās are, though religious in content, distinguished from Ṛc, <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुस्">yajus</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> as nonVedic--that is, are not Mantras. This view is consistent with the fact that the phrase <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यज्ञ-गाथा">yajña-gāthā</a>,</b> meaning a verse summarizing a sacrificial usage, is not rare. The Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120866">10</a></sup> preserves several Gāthās, which generally accord with this description as epitomizing the sacrifices of famous kings, and the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120866">11</a></sup> states that a <a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a> is sung at a wedding. Sometimes<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120866">12</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a> is qualified as <a class="vei_120a" href="नाराशंसी">nārāśaṃsī</a>, where it must be a eulogy of a generous donor.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120866">↑</a> viii. 32, 1; 71, 14; 98, 9; ix. 99, 4; <i>gātha,</i> i. 167, 6; ix. 11, 4; <i>gātha-<a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>,</i> ‘lord of song,’ i. 43, 4; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a>-nī,</i> ‘leading a song,’ i. 190, 1; viii. 92, 2; <i>ṛjugātha,</i> ‘singing correctly,’ v. 44, 5; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="गाथिन्">gāthin</a>,</i> ‘singer,’ i. 7, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 65.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120866">↑</a> i. 151, 2; ii. 20, 5; iii. 4, 4; iv. 4, 6; v. 87, 8; x. 20, 4; 122, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120866">↑</a> x. 85, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120866">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 11, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, v. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 32; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxx. 5; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 6, 8, where <a class="vei_120a" href="रैभी">raibhī</a> does not occur; Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120866">↑</a> Viz., <a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a> = Av. xx. 127, 12 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="नाराशंसी">nārāśaṃsī</a> = Av. xx. 127, 1-3; <a class="vei_120a" href="रैभी">raibhī</a> = Av. xx. 127, 4-6; while <i>ibid.,</i> 7-10, are known as Pārikṣityaḥ (<i>scil., ṛcaḥ</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120866">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 238. Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 689 <i>et seq.,</i> seems to accept the identification even for the Rigveda.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120866">↑</a> Av. x. 10, 20; xv. 6, 4 (distinct from <a class="vei_120a" href="नाराशंसी">nārāśaṃsī</a>); Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 4, 16; xi. 5, 7, 10; xiii. 1, 5, 6; 4, 2, 8; 5, 4, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 10 (distinct from <a class="vei_120a" href="नाराशंसी">nārāśaṃsī</a>); Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 17, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120866">↑</a> ii. 3, 6, with Keith's note; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 7, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120866">↑</a> vii. 18. The story of <a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a> is described as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शत">śata</a>-gātham,</i> ‘told in a hundred Gāthās.’</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120866">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, etc., and see xiii. 4, 2, 8, where the Gāthās are plainly Dānastutis, or ‘praises of gifts,’ just as the <a class="vei_120a" href="नाराशंसी">nārāśaṃsī</a> verses are declared to be in the Bṛhaddevatā, iii. 154.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120866">↑</a> iii. 7, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120866">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 2, 6. So Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 98, takes Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 6, 8, where Sāyaṇa hesitates between identifying the two and distinguishing them. It seems reasonable to regard <a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a> as the wider term which covers, but is not coextensive with, <a class="vei_120a" href="नाराशंसी">nārāśaṃsī</a>.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Sāyaṇa's example of a <a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a> in his commentary on <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 3, 6: <i>prātaḥ <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रातर्">prātar</a> anṛtaṃ te vadanti,</i> ‘they every morning tell an untruth,’ which is clearly not a <a class="vei_120a" href="नाराशंसी">nārāśaṃsī</a>.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 689 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Episches im Vedischen Ritual,</i> 4 <i>et seq.;</i> Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 493.</li></ol></div>

गाथिन्|గాథిన్|ಗಾಥಿನ್|gāthin|gaathin|_vei_120@867|_vei1_pn225
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@866">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@868">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn224">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn225">1:225</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn226">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gāthin</b> is mentioned as the son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुशिक">kuśika</a></b> and father of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a></b> in the Sarvānukramaṇī. It is difficult to say whether this tradition is correct; it derives some support from the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vii. 18), where reference is made to the divine lore (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दैव">daiva</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a></i>) of the Gāthins, which is said to be shared by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a></b> as a result of his adoption by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गाथिन">gāthina</a>.</b></p></div>

गाथिन|గాథిన|ಗಾಥಿನ|gāthina|gaathina|_vei_120@868|_vei1_pn225
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@867">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@869">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn224">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn225">1:225</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn226">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gāthina.</b>--The sons of <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> are described in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120868">1</a></sup> as Gāthinas, or descendants of <a class="vei_120a" href="गाथिन्">gāthin</a>, who, according to tradition, was their grandfather;<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120868">2</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a></b> himself is styled <a class="vei_120a" href="गाथिन">gāthina</a> in the Sarvānukramaṇī. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120868">↑</a> vii. 18. <i>Cf.</i> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, vii. 18; Weber, <i>Episches im Vedischen Ritual,</i> 16, n. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120868">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 348 <i>et seq.;</i> Pargiter, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1910, 32 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

गां-दम|गांदम|गां|दम|गान्दम|గాం-దమ|గాందమ|గాం|దమ|గాన్దమ|ಗಾಂ-ದಮ|ಗಾಂದಮ|ಗಾಂ|ದಮ|ಗಾನ್ದಮ|gāṃ-dama|gāṃdama|gāṃ|dama|gaaM-dama|gaaMdama|gaaM|gaandama|_vei_120@869|_vei1_pn226
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@868">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@870">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn225">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn226">1:226</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn227">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gāṃ-dama</b> is the form in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120869"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120869">1</a></sup> of the name of <b>Ekayāvan,</b> which in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120869"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120869">2</a></sup> is read as Kāṃdama.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120869"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120869">↑</a> xxi. 14, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120869"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120869">↑</a> ii. 7, 11, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transac tions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 69.</li></ol></div>

गान्धार|గాన్ధార|ಗಾನ್ಧಾರ|gāndhāra|gaandhaara|_vei_120@870|_vei1_pn226
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@869">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@871">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn225">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn226">1:226</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn227">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gāndhāra,</b> ‘a king of Gandhāra’ named <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नग्नजित्">nagnajit</a>,</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120870"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120870">1</a></sup> In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120870"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120870">2</a></sup> he or some descendant figures as <b>Svarjit <a class="vei_120a" href="नाग्नजित">nāgnajita</a></b> or <a class="vei_120a" href="नग्नजित्">nagnajit</a>, and as giving an opinion on the ritual, which is rejected with the observation that the author was merely a princely person (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य-बन्धु">rājanya-bandhu</a></i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120870"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120870">↑</a> vii. 34. in the list of teachers who handed down the knowledge of the substitute for <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120870"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120870">↑</a> viii. 1, 4, 10.</li></ol></div>

गार्गी वाचक्नवी|गार्गी वाचक्नवि|గార్గీ వాచక్నవీ|గార్గీ వాచక్నవి|ಗಾರ್ಗೀ ವಾಚಕ್ನವೀ|ಗಾರ್ಗೀ ವಾಚಕ್ನವಿ|gārgī vācaknavī|gārgī vācaknavi|gaargii vaachaknavii|gaargii vaachaknavi|gaargee vaachaknavee|gaargee vaachaknavi|_vei_120@871|_vei1_pn226
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@870">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@872">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn225">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn226">1:226</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn227">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gārgī Vācaknavī</b> is referred to in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120871"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120871">1</a></sup> as a female contemporary and rival of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120871"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120871">↑</a> iii. 6, 1; 8, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 118.</li></ol></div>

गार्गी-पुत्र|गार्गीपुत्र|गार्गी|गार्गि|पुत्र|గార్గీ-పుత్ర|గార్గీపుత్ర|గార్గీ|గార్గి|పుత్ర|ಗಾರ್ಗೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಗಾರ್ಗೀಪುತ್ರ|ಗಾರ್ಗೀ|ಗಾರ್ಗಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|gārgī-putra|gārgīputra|gārgī|gārgi|putra|gaargii-putra|gaargiiputra|gaargii|gaargi|gaargee-putra|gaargeeputra|gaargee|_vei_120@872|_vei1_pn226
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@871">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@873">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn225">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn226">1:226</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn227">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gārgī-putra,</b> ‘son of Gargī,’ occurs as the name of three teachers in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Mādhyaṃdina recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 4, 30). The earliest of these three was the pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बाडेयीपुत्र">bāḍeyīputra</a></b> and the teacher of the second <a class="vei_120a" href="गार्गीपुत्र">gārgīputra</a>. The latter was the teacher of <b>Pārāśarīkauṇḍinīputra,</b> the teacher of the third <a class="vei_120a" href="गार्गीपुत्र">gārgīputra</a>.</p></div>

गार्ग्य|గార్గ్య|ಗಾರ್ಗ್ಯ|gārgya|gaargya|_vei_120@873|_vei1_pn226
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@872">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@874">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn225">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn226">1:226</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn227">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gārgya,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="गर्ग">garga</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बालाकि">bālāki</a></b> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120873"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120873">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120873"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120873">2</a></sup> Upaniṣads. Two Gārgyas are mentioned in the second <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>:<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120873"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120873">3</a></sup> one of them is the pupil of <a class="vei_120a" href="गार्ग्य">gārgya</a>, who again is the pupil of <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a>. Others occur in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120873"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120873">4</a></sup> and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120873"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120873">5</a></sup> as well as later in the ritual Sūtras. Thus the family was evidently long connected with the development of liturgy and grammar. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120873"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120873">↑</a> ii. 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120873"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120873">↑</a> iv. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120873"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120873">↑</a> iv. 6, 2 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>)</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120873"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120873">↑</a> i. 7, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120873"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120873">↑</a> i. 3. 12; iii. 13.</li></ol></div>

गार्ग्यायण|గార్గ్యాయణ|ಗಾರ್ಗ್ಯಾಯಣ|gārgyāyaṇa|gaargyaayaNa|_vei_120@874|_vei1_pn227
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@873">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@875">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn226">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn227">1:227</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn228">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gārgyāyaṇa,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="गार्ग्य">gārgya</a>,’ is mentioned as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उद्दालकायन">uddālakāyana</a></b> in the second <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iv. 6, 2 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</p></div>

गार्ग्यायणि|గార్గ్యాయణి|ಗಾರ್ಗ್ಯಾಯಣಿ|gārgyāyaṇi|gaargyaayaNi|_vei_120@875|_vei1_pn227
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@874">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@876">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn226">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn227">1:227</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn228">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gārgyāyaṇi,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="गार्ग्य">gārgya</a>,’ is a variant reading for <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गांग्यायनि">gāṅgyāyani</a></b> as the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चित्र">citra</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (i. 1).</p></div>

गालव|గాలవ|ಗಾಲವ|gālava|gaalava|_vei_120@876|_vei1_pn227
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@875">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@877">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn226">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn227">1:227</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn228">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gālava</b> is mentioned as a pupil of <b>Vidarbhīkauṇḍinya</b> in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120876">1</a></sup> It is possibly the same man that is referred to regarding a point of ritual in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120876">2</a></sup> A grammarian of this name is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120876">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120876">↑</a> ii. 5, 22; iv. 5, 28 (Mādhyaṃdina = ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120876">↑</a> v. 3, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120876">↑</a> iv. 3. <i>Cf.</i> Pāṇini, vi. 3, 61; vii. 1, 74; 3, 99; viii. 4, 67.</li></ol></div>

गिरि|గిరి|ಗಿರಿ|giri|_vei_120@877|_vei1_pn227
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@876">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@878">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn226">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn227">1:227</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn228">↷</a><hr><p><b>Giri,</b> ‘mountain’ or ‘height,’ is a word that occurs repeatedly in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120877">1</a></sup> Thus reference is made to the trees on the hills, hence called ‘tree-haired’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वृक्ष">vṛkṣa</a>-keśāḥ</i>),<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120877">2</a></sup> and to the streams proceeding from the hills to the sea (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="समुद्र">samudra</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120877">3</a></sup> The term is frequently coupled with the adjectival <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्वत">parvata</a>.</i><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120877">4</a></sup> The Rigveda mentions the waters from the hills,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120877">5</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120877">6</a></sup> refers to the snowy mountains. Actual names of mountains, as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मूजवन्त्">mūjavant</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रिककुद्">trikakud</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="हिमवन्त्">himavant</a>,</b> are very rare. References to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रौञ्च">krauñca</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="महामेरु">mahāmeru</a>,</b> and <b>Maināga,</b> are confined to the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, while <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नावप्रभ्रंशन">nāvaprabhraṃśana</a></b> can no longer be considered a proper name.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120877">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120877">↑</a> i. 56, 3; 61, 14; 63, 1; iv. 20, 6; vi. 24, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120877">↑</a> Rv. v. 41, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120877">↑</a> Rv. vii. 95, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120877">↑</a> Rv. i. 56, 4; viii. 64, 5; Av. iv. 7, 8; vi. 12, 3; 17, 3; ix. 1, 18, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120877">↑</a> Rv. vi. 66, 11, on which passage, see Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 411; viii. 32, 4; x. 68, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120877">↑</a> xii. 1, 11. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हिमवन्त्">himavant</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120877">↑</a> Av. xix. 37, 8, with Whitney's note in his Translation; Macdonell, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1909, 1107.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 47.</li></ol></div>

गिरि-क्षित् औच्चा-मन्यव|गिरि-क्षित् औच्चामन्यव|गिरि-क्षित् औच्चा|मन्यव|గిరి-క్షిత్ ఔచ్చా-మన్యవ|గిరి-క్షిత్ ఔచ్చామన్యవ|గిరి-క్షిత్ ఔచ్చా|మన్యవ|ಗಿರಿ-ಕ್ಷಿತ್ ಔಚ್ಚಾ-ಮನ್ಯವ|ಗಿರಿ-ಕ್ಷಿತ್ ಔಚ್ಚಾಮನ್ಯವ|ಗಿರಿ-ಕ್ಷಿತ್ ಔಚ್ಚಾ|ಮನ್ಯವ|giri-kṣit auccā-manyava|giri-kṣit auccāmanyava|giri-kṣit auccā|manyava|giri-kShit auchchaa-manyava|giri-kShit auchchaamanyava|giri-kShit auchchaa|giri-xit auchchaa-manyava|giri-xit auchchaamanyava|giri-xit auchchaa|_vei_120@878|_vei1_pn227
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@877">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@879">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn226">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn227">1:227</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn228">↷</a><hr><p><b>Giri-<a class="vei_120a" href="क्षित्">kṣit</a> Auccā-manyava,</b> ‘descendant of Uccāmanyu,’ is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (x. 5, 7) as a contemporary of <b>Abhipratārin Kākṣaseṇi</b> </p></div>

गिरि-ज बाभ्रव्य|गिरिज बाभ्रव्य|गिरि|ज बाभ्रव्य|గిరి-జ బాభ్రవ్య|గిరిజ బాభ్రవ్య|గిరి|జ బాభ్రవ్య|ಗಿರಿ-ಜ ಬಾಭ್ರವ್ಯ|ಗಿರಿಜ ಬಾಭ್ರವ್ಯ|ಗಿರಿ|ಜ ಬಾಭ್ರವ್ಯ|giri-ja bābhravya|girija bābhravya|giri|ja bābhravya|giri-ja baabhravya|girija baabhravya|ja baabhravya|_vei_120@879|_vei1_pn228
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@878">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@880">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn227">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn228">1:228</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn229">↷</a><hr><p><b>Giri-ja Bābhravya,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="बभ्रु">babhru</a>,’ is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vii. 1) as having been taught by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a></b> the method of dividing the sacrificial animal (<i>paśor vibhakti</i>).</p></div>

गुग्गुलु|గుగ్గులు|ಗುಗ್ಗುಲು|guggulu|_vei_120@880|_vei1_pn228
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@879">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@881">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn227">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn228">1:228</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn229">↷</a><hr><p><b>Guggulu,</b> ‘bdellium,’ is referred to in one passage of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120880">1</a></sup> as produced by the <a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धु">sindhu</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120880">2</a></sup> and by the sea. The latter source presumably alludes, as Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120880">3</a></sup> assumes, to seaborne trade, bdellium being the gum of a tree, not a product of the sea. It is, however, possible that in this passage some other substance may be meant. The word in this form also occurs elsewhere in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120880">4</a></sup> and later;<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120880">5</a></sup> it is often<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120880">6</a></sup> mentioned in the older form of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गुल्गुलु">gulgulu</a>,</b> between which and <a class="vei_120a" href="गुग्गुलु">guggulu</a> the manuscripts constantly vary</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120880">↑</a> xix. 38, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120880">↑</a> Or ‘from streams’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सैन्धव">saindhava</a></i>), as Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. <a class="vei_120a" href="गुग्गुलु">guggulu</a>,</i> suggests.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120880">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 28.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120880">↑</a> ii. 36, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120880">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 28.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120880">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 8, 6; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 8, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxiv. 13; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 5, 2, 16.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 675; Lassen, <i>Indische Alterthumskunde,</i> 12, 339; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 957, 958.</li></ol></div>

गुंगु|गुङ्गु|గుంగు|గుఙ్గు|ಗುಂಗು|ಗುಙ್ಗು|guṅgu|guMgu|gungu|_vei_120@881|_vei1_pn228
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@880">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@882">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn227">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn228">1:228</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn229">↷</a><hr><p><b>Guṅgu.</b>--The descendants of <a class="vei_120a" href="गुंगु">guṅgu</a> are referred to as Guṅgus in a hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120881"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120881">1</a></sup> apparently as friends of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अतिथिग्व">atithigva</a>.</b> Possibly a people may be meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120881"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120881">↑</a> x. 48, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig. Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 165.</li></ol></div>

गुप्त|గుప్త|ಗುಪ್ತ|gupta|_vei_120@882|_vei1_pn228
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@881">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@883">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn227">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn228">1:228</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn229">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gupta</b> is the name in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 42) of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैपश्चित">vaipaścita</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दार्ढजयन्ति">dārḍhajayanti</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गुप्त लौहित्य">gupta lauhitya</a>.</b> All the three other names being patronymics show that he was descended from the families of Vipaścit, <a class="vei_120a" href="दृढजयन्त">dṛḍhajayanta</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="लोहित">lohita</a>.</p></div>

गुल्गुलु|గుల్గులు|ಗುಲ್ಗುಲು|gulgulu|_vei_120@883|_vei1_pn228
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@882">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@884">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn227">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn228">1:228</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn229">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gulgulu.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गुग्गुलु">guggulu</a>.</b></p></div>

गृत्स-मद|गृत्समद|गृत्स|मद|గృత్స-మద|గృత్సమద|గృత్స|మద|ಗೃತ್ಸ-ಮದ|ಗೃತ್ಸಮದ|ಗೃತ್ಸ|ಮದ|gṛtsa-mada|gṛtsamada|gṛtsa|mada|gRtsa-mada|gRtsamada|gRtsa|gRitsa-mada|gRitsamada|gRitsa|gRutsa-mada|gRutsamada|gRutsa|gRRitsa-mada|gRRitsamada|gRRitsa|_vei_120@884|_vei1_pn228
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@883">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@885">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn227">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn228">1:228</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn229">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gṛtsa-mada</b> is the name of a seer to whom the Sarvānukramaṇī attributes the authorship of the second Maṇḍala of the Rigveda. This tradition is supported by the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Brāh maṇa<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120884"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120884">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Araṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120884"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120884">2</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120884"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120884">3</a></sup> speaks of him as a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भार्गव">bhārgava</a>,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="भृगु">bhṛgu</a>,’ with a variant <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बाभ्रव">bābhrava</a>,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="बभ्रु">babhru</a>,’ but the later tradition keeps to the former patronymic.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120884"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120884">4</a></sup> The Gṛtsamadas are often mentioned in the second Maṇḍala of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120884"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120884">5</a></sup> and are also called Śunahotras,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120884"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120884">6</a></sup> but never Gārtsamadas or Śaunahotras, and <a class="vei_120a" href="गृत्समद">gṛtsamada</a> himself never occurs there.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120884"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120884">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120884"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120884">↑</a> v. 2, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120884"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120884">↑</a> ii. 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120884"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120884">↑</a> xxii. 4. <i>Cf.</i> Gārtsamadī, xxviii. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120884"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120884">↑</a> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 226 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120884"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120884">↑</a> ii. 4, 9; 19, 8; 39, 8; 41, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120884"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120884">↑</a> ii. 18, 6; 41, 14. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120884"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120884">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 200, 201.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 118; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 287.</li></ol></div>

गृध्र|గృధ్ర|ಗೃಧ್ರ|gṛdhra|gRdhra|gRidhra|gRudhra|gRRidhra|_vei_120@885|_vei1_pn229
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@884">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@886">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn228">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn229">1:229</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn231">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gṛdhra,</b> ‘the vulture,’ is often mentioned from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120885"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120885">1</a></sup> downwards.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120885"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120885">2</a></sup> The swiftness of its flight<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120885"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120885">3</a></sup> and its fondness for devouring carrion are especially noticed.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120885"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120885">4</a></sup> More generally the word is used to designate any bird of prey, the eagle (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्येन">śyena</a></b>) being classed as the chief of the Gṛdhras.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120885"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120885">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120885"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120885">↑</a> i. 118, 4; ii. 39, 1; vii. 104, 22; x. 123, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120885"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120885">↑</a> Av. vii. 95, 1; xi. 2, 2; 9, 9; 10. 8. 24; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 4, 7, 1; v. 5, 20, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 9, 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, iv. 29; Adbhuta <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> in <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 40; etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120885"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120885">↑</a> Rv. ii. 39, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120885"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120885">↑</a> Av. xi. 10, 8. 24; Maitrāyaṃ Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120885"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120885">↑</a> Rv. ix. 96, 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 88; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 225.</li></ol></div>

गृष्टि|గృష్టి|ಗೃಷ್ಟಿ|gṛṣṭi|gRShTi|gRiShTi|gRuShTi|gRRiShTi|_vei_120@886|_vei1_pn229
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@885">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@887">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn228">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn229">1:229</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn231">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gṛṣṭi,</b> ‘a young cow,’ which has only calved once, occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120886"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120886">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120886"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120886">2</a></sup> as well as in the later <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> literature.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120886"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120886">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120886"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120886">↑</a> iv. 18, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120886"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120886">↑</a> ii. 13, 3; viii. 9, 24; xix. 24, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120886"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120886">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, 19. 24, etc.</li></ol></div>

गृह|గృహ|ಗೃಹ|gṛha|gRha|gRiha|gRuha|gRRiha|_vei_120@887|_vei1_pn229
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@886">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@888">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn228">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn229">1:229</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn231">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gṛha</b> is used in the singular,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120887">1</a></sup> or oftener in the plural,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120887">2</a></sup> to denote the ‘house’ of the Vedic Indian. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दम">dama</a></b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दम्">dam</a></b> has  the same sense, while <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पस्त्या">pastyā</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हर्म्य">harmya</a></b> denote more especially the home with its surroundings, the family settlement. The house held not only the family, which might be of considerable size, but also the cattle<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120887">3</a></sup> and the sheep<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120887">4</a></sup> at night. It was composed of several rooms, as the use of the plural indicates, and it could be securely shut up.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120887">5</a></sup> The door (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="द्वार्">dvār</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="द्वार">dvāra</a></b>) is often referred to, and from it the house is called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दुरोण">duroṇa</a>.</b> In every house the fire was kept burning.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120887">6</a></sup></p><p>Very little is known of the structure of the house. Presumably stone was not used,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120887">7</a></sup> and houses were, as in Megasthenes' time,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120887">8</a></sup> built of wood. The hymns of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120887">9</a></sup> give some information about the construction of a house, but the details are extremely obscure, for most of the expressions used do not recur in any context in which their sense is clear. According to Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120887">10</a></sup> four pillars (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपमित्">upamit</a></b>) were set up on a good site, and against them beams were leant at an angle as props (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतिमित्">pratimit</a></b>). The upright pillars were connected by cross beams (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="परिमित्">parimit</a></b>) resting upon them. The roof was formed of ribs of bamboo cane (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120887">11</a></sup> a ridge called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विषूवन्त्">viṣūvant</a>,</b> and a net (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्षु">akṣu</a></b>), which may mean a thatched covering<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120887">12</a></sup> over the bamboo ribs. The walls were filled up with grass in bundles (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पलद">palada</a></i>), and the whole structure was held together with ties of  various sorts (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="नहन">nahana</a>, prāṇāha, <a class="vei_120a" href="संदंश">saṃdaṃśa</a>, pariṣvañjalya</i>).<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120887">13</a></sup> In connexion with the house, mention is made of four terms which, though primarily sacrificial in meaning, seem to designate parts of the building: <a class="vei_120a" href="हविर्धान">havirdhāna</a>, ‘oblation-holder’; <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्निशाल">agniśāla</a>,<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120887">14</a></sup> ‘fireplace’; Patnīnāṃ <a class="vei_120a" href="सदन">sadana</a>, ‘wives’ room’; and <a class="vei_120a" href="सदस्">sadas</a>, ‘sitting room.’ Slings or hanging vessels (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="शिक्य">śikya</a></b>) are also mentioned.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_120887">15</a></sup> Reedwork (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="इट">iṭa</a></i>) is spoken of, no doubt as part of the finishing of the walls of the house.<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_120887">16</a></sup> The sides are called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पक्ष">pakṣa</a>.</b> The door with its framework was named <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आता">ātā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120887">↑</a> Rv. iii. 53, 6; iv. 49, 6; viii. 10. 1, etc.; Av. vii. 83, 1; x. 6, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 21.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120887">↑</a> Rv. ii. 42, 3; v. 76, 4; x. 18, 12; 85, 26; 142, 4; 165, 2; Av. i. 27, 4; iii. 10, 11; vi. 137, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 31; viii. 26; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ii. 32; iv. 33; xviii. 44; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 2, 22; 6, 1, 19, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120887">↑</a> Rv. vii. 56, 16; Av. i. 3, 4; ix. 3, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120887">↑</a> Rv. x. 106, 5; Av. iii. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120887">↑</a> Rv. vii. 85, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120887">↑</a> Rv. i. 69, 2. <i>Cf.</i> the Gārhapatya <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>, Av. v. 31, 5; vi. 120, 1; 121, 2; viii. 102; ix. 6, 30; xii. 2, 34; xviii. 4, 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā. iii. 39; xix. 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 6. 12; Kauṣīt taki <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 6, 1, 28; vii. 1, 1, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120887">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 153. Muir's view, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 461, that clay was used can only apply to the minor finishing of the walls of a house.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120887">↑</a> Arrian, <i>Indica,</i> x. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120887">↑</a> iii. 12; ix. 3. See Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 343 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 234 <i>et seq.;</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 525 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120887">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 153. No certainty can attach to the rendering of the words.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120887">↑</a> It seems likely that, as the ribs were of bamboo and were probably fixed in the ridge, the roof was wagon headed, like the huts of the Todas at the present day (see illustrations in Rivers, <i>The Todas,</i> pp. 25, 27, 28, 51), and the rock-cut Chaityas, or Assembly Halls, of the Buddhists in Western India, in some of the earliest of which the wooden ribs of the arched roof are still preserved. See Fergusson, <i>History of Indian Architecture,</i> 22, 135, <i>cf.</i> 126.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120887">↑</a> Av. ix. 3, 8, where Bloomfield, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 598, thinks of a wickerwork roof; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 136, of a pole with countless holes.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120887">↑</a> Av. ix. 3, 4. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120887">↑</a> Zimmer conjecturally identifies the <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्निशाल">agniśāla</a> with the central room, the <a class="vei_120a" href="हविर्धान">havirdhāna</a> with a place for keeping the grain, etc. (<i>e.g.,</i> Av. iii. 3, 4), the Patnīnāṃ <a class="vei_120a" href="सदन">sadana</a> with the women's apartments, and the <a class="vei_120a" href="सदस्">sadas</a> with the subsidiary buildings.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_120887">↑</a> Av. ix. 3, 6. See Whitney, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 526; Bloomfield, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 597.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_120887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_120887">↑</a> Av. ix. 3, 17.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 148-156.</li></ol></div>

गृह-प|गृहप|गृह|గృహ-ప|గృహప|గృహ|ಗೃಹ-ಪ|ಗೃಹಪ|ಗೃಹ|gṛha-pa|gṛhapa|gṛha|gRha-pa|gRhapa|gRha|gRiha-pa|gRihapa|gRiha|gRuha-pa|gRuhapa|gRuha|gRRiha-pa|gRRihapa|gRRiha|_vei_120@888|_vei1_pn231
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@887">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@889">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn229">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn231">1:231</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn234">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gṛha-pa</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120888"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120888">1</a></sup> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गृह">gṛha</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a></b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120888"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120888">2</a></sup> is the regular name, from the Rigveda onwards, of the householder as master of the house. Similarly the mistress is called <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह">gṛha</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पत्नी">patnī</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120888"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120888">3</a></sup> For the powers and position of the Gṛhapati see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पितृ">pitṛ</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120888"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120888">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120888"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120888">↑</a> Rv. vi. 53, 2; Av. xiv. 1, 51; xix. 31, 13; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 6, 8, 5; viii. 6, 1, 11, and repeatedly as an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>, Rv. i. 12, 6; 36, 5; 60, 4; vi. 48, 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ii. 27; iii. 39; ix. 39; xxiv. 24, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120888"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120888">↑</a> Rv. x. 85, 26; Av. iii. 24, 6. <i>Cf</i> Gārhapatya, Rv. i. 15, 12; vi. 15, 19 x. 85, 27. 36.</li></ol></div>

गृह्य|గృహ్య|ಗೃಹ್ಯ|gṛhya|gRhya|gRihya|gRuhya|gRRihya|_vei_120@889|_vei1_pn231
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@888">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@890">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn229">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn231">1:231</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn234">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gṛhya</b> denotes the members of the house or family in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.</p><p>1) ii. 5, 2, 14; 3, 16; 6, 2, 4; iii. 4, 1, 6; xii. 4, 1, 4. <i>Cf. gṛhāḥ,</i> i. 7, 4; 12.</p></div>

गैरि-क्षित|गैरिक्षित|गैरि|क्षित|గైరి-క్షిత|గైరిక్షిత|గైరి|క్షిత|ಗೈರಿ-ಕ್ಷಿತ|ಗೈರಿಕ್ಷಿತ|ಗೈರಿ|ಕ್ಷಿತ|gairi-kṣita|gairikṣita|gairi|kṣita|gairi-kShita|gairikShita|kShita|gairi-xita|gairixita|xita|_vei_120@890|_vei1_pn231
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@889">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@891">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn229">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn231">1:231</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn234">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gairi-kṣita,</b> ‘descendant of <b>Girikṣit,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a></b> in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120890"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120890">1</a></sup> and of the <b>Yaskas</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120890"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120890">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120890"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120890">↑</a> v. 33, 8; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 155, 174.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120890"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120890">↑</a> xiii. 12; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 474, 475.</li></ol></div>

गो|गो|గో_౧|గో|ಗೋ_೧|ಗೋ|go_1|go|_vei_120@891|_vei1_pn231
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@890">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@892">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn229">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn231">1:231</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn234">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Go</b> (<i>a</i>), ‘ox’ or ‘cow.’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120891">1</a></sup> These were among the chief sources of wealth to the Vedic Indian, and are repeatedly  referred to from the Rigveda onwards.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120891">2</a></sup> The milk (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षीर">kṣīra</a></b>) was either drunk fresh or made into butter (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="घृत">ghṛta</a></b>) or curds (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="दधि">dadhi</a></b>), or was mixed with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a></b> or used for cooking with grain (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षीरौदन">kṣīraudana</a></b>). The cows were milked thrice a day, early (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रातर्">prātar</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="दोह">doha</a></i>), in the forenoon (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="संगव">saṃgava</a></b>), and in the evening (<i>sāyaṃ-<a class="vei_120a" href="दोह">doha</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120891">3</a></sup> Thrice a day they were driven out to graze, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120891">4</a></sup> (<i>prātaḥ, saṃgave, sāyam</i>). The first milking was productive, the last two scanty.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120891">3</a></sup> According to the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120891">5</a></sup> among the Bharatas the herds in the evening are in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोष्ठ">goṣṭha</a>,</b> at midday in the <b>Saṃgavīnī.</b> This passage Sāyaṇa expands by saying that the herds go home to the <a class="vei_120a" href="शाला">śālā</a>, or house for animals, at night so far as they consist of animals giving milk, while the others stayed out in the <a class="vei_120a" href="गोष्ठ">goṣṭha</a>, or open pasturage; but both were together in the cattle-shed during the heat of the day. The time before the <a class="vei_120a" href="संगव">saṃgava</a>, when the cows were grazing freely on the pastureland, was called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्वसर">svasara</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120891">6</a></sup> When the cows were out feeding they were separated from the calves, which were, however, allowed to join them at the <a class="vei_120a" href="संगव">saṃgava</a>,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120891">7</a></sup> and sometimes in the evening.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120891">8</a></sup></p><p>While grazing the cattle were under the care of a herdsman (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोपा">gopā</a>, Gopāla</b>) armed with a goad,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120891">9</a></sup> but they were liable to all sorts of dangers, such as being lost, falling into pits, breaking limbs,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120891">10</a></sup>  and being stolen. The marking of the ears of cattle was repeatedly adopted, no doubt, to indicate ownership.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120891">11</a></sup></p><p>Large herds of cattle were well-known, as is shown by the Dānastutis, or ‘praises of gifts,’ in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120891">12</a></sup> even when allowances are made for the exaggeration of priestly gratitude. The importance attached to the possession of cattle is shown<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120891">13</a></sup> by the numerous passages in which the gods are asked to prosper them, and by the repeated prayers<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120891">14</a></sup> for wealth in kine. Hence, too, forays for cattle (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="गविष्टि">gaviṣṭi</a></b>) were well known; the <a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a> host is called the ‘horde desiring cows’ (<i>gavyan grāmaḥ</i>) in the Rigveda;<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_120891">15</a></sup> and a verbal root <i>gup,</i><sup id="ref_120_16_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_120891">16</a></sup> ‘to protect,’ was evolved as early as the Rigveda from the denominative <i>go-pāya,</i> ‘to guard cows.’ The Vedic poets<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_120891">17</a></sup> do not hesitate to compare their songs with the lowing of cows, or to liken the choir of the singing Apsarases to cows.<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_120891">18</a></sup></p><p>The cattle of the Vedic period were of many colours: red (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="रोहित">rohita</a></i>), light (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शुक्र">śukra</a></i>), dappled (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पृश्नि">pṛśni</a></i>), even black (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण">kṛṣṇa</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_120891">19</a></sup> Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_120891">20</a></sup> sees a reference to cows with blazes on the face in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_120891">21</a></sup> but this is uncertain.</p><p>Oxen were regularly used for ploughing or for drawing wagons (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अनड्वाह्">anaḍvāh</a></i>), in which case they were, it seems, usually castrated.<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_120891">22</a></sup> Cows were not properly used for drawing carts, hough they at times did so.<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_120891">23</a></sup> The flesh of both cows and bulls was sometimes eaten (<b>Māmsa</b>). Cattle were certainly the  objects of individual ownership, and they formed one of the standards of exchange and valuation (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रय">kraya</a></b>).</p><p>(<i>b</i>) The term <b>Go</b> is often applied to express the products of the cow. It frequently means the milk,<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_120891">1b</a></sup> but rarely the flesh<sup id="ref_120_2b_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2b_vei_120891">2b</a></sup> of the animal. In many passages it designates leather used as the material of various objects, as a bowstring,<sup id="ref_120_3b_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3b_vei_120891">3b</a></sup> or a sling,<sup id="ref_120_4b_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4b_vei_120891">4b</a></sup> or thongs to fasten part of the chariot,<sup id="ref_120_5b_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5b_vei_120891">5b</a></sup> or reins,<sup id="ref_120_6b_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6b_vei_120891">6b</a></sup> or the lash of a whip.<sup id="ref_120_7b_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7b_vei_120891">7b</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चर्मन्">carman</a>,</b> with which Go is sometimes synonymous.<sup id="ref_120_8b_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8b_vei_120891">8b</a></sup></p><p>(<i>c</i>) <b>Gāvaḥ</b> means the stars of heaven in two passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1c_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1c_vei_120891">1c</a></sup> according to Roth.<sup id="ref_120_2c_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2c_vei_120891">2c</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120891">↑</a> i. 83, 1; 135, 8; ii. 23, 18, etc.; <i>gāva ukṣaṇaḥ,</i> i. 168, 2; Av. iii. 11, 8; <b>Vājasaṇeyi</b> Saṃhitā, xxi. 20; <i>gāvo dhenavaḥ,</i> Rv. i. 173. 1; vi. 45. 28; x. 95, 6; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxi. 19. etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120891">↑</a> The five sacrificial animals are man, goat, sheep, ox, horse, Sāṅkhāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ix. 23, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 3, 13; iii. 1, 2, 13; iv. 5, 5, 10; xiv. 1, 1, 32.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120891">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120891">↑</a> i. 4, 9, 2. The exact sense of this notice is obscure. Strictly speaking the cows were driven out from the cattleshed in the morning, spent the heat of the day in the <a class="vei_120a" href="संगविनी">saṃgavinī</a>, were then driven out during the evening to graze, and finally came or were driven home, as is often mentioned: Rv. i. 66, 5; 149, 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xv. 41.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120891">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120891">↑</a> iii. 18, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120891">↑</a> Rv. ii. 2, 2; 34, 8; v. 62, 2; viii. 88, 1; ix. 94, 2. The going of the cows to their pasture in the morning is often referred to--<i>e.g.,</i> Rv. i. 25, 16; x. 97, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120891">↑</a> Rv. ii. 2, 2; viii. 88, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 1, 3; Śankara on Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 9, 4; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 12, 4; Nārāyaṇa on Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 12, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120891">↑</a> Gobhila <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 8, 7; Rv. ii. 2, 2. See Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 111-114.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120891">↑</a> <i>Pavīravān,</i> Rv. x. 60, 3, is probably so meant. The usual name was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अष्ट्रा">aṣṭrā</a>,</b> the significant mark of a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>.</b> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. vii. 33, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120891">↑</a> Rv. i. 120, 8; vi. 54, 5-7. Also Pūṣan was the special deity expected to guard cattle, and hence is called <i>anaṣṭapaśu,</i> ‘losing no kine.’ See Rv. x. 17, 2. and Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 36.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120891">↑</a> Rv. vi. 28, 3; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 9, and <i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अष्टकर्णी">aṣṭakarṇī</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्वधिति">svadhiti</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120891">↑</a> Rv. viii. 5, 37, etc. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvii. 14, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 21. 23; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 4, 8 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120891">↑</a> Rv. i. 43, 2; 162, 22; v. 4, 11; ix. 9, 9, etc.; Av. i. 31, 4; ii. 26, 4; v. 29, 2; vi. 68, 3; viii. 7, 11; x. 1, 17. 29; xi. 2, 9. 21, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 2, 3, 1; v. 5, 5, 1; vi. 5, 10, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iii. 59.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120891">↑</a> Rv. i. 83, 1; iv. 32, 17; v. 4, 11; viii. 89, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_120891">↑</a> iii. 33, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_120891">↑</a> Rv. vii. 103, 9; Av. x. 9, 7, 8; xix. 27, 9. 10. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> p. 358, n. 13</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_120891">↑</a> Rv. vii. 32, 22; viii. 95, 1; 106, 1; ix. 12, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_120891">↑</a> Rv. x. 95, 6. It is, however, uncertain whether the names of Apsarases are meant in this passage. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 5, 517.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_120891">↑</a> Rv. i. 62, 9. Various other colours are mentioned in the lists of animals at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or human sacrifice, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>, but apparently as exceptional.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_120891">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 226.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_120891">↑</a> i. 87, 1. It is also rendered as ‘the heavens with stars.’</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_120891">↑</a> Av. iii. 9, 2; vi. 138, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8. 9, 1; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 151, n. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="महानिरष्ट">mahāniraṣṭa</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_120891">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 2, 4, 13.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_120891">↑</a> Rv. i. 33, 10; 151, 8; 181, 8; ii. 30, 7; iv. 27, 5; ix. 46, 4; 71, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2b_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2b_vei_120891">↑</a> Rv. x. 16, 7 (in the funeral ritual).</li><li id="fx_120_3b_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3b_vei_120891">↑</a> Rv. vi. 75, 11; x. 27, 22; Av. i. 2, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4b_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4b_vei_120891">↑</a> Rv. i. 121, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_5b_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5b_vei_120891">↑</a> Rv. vi. 47, 26; viii. 59. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_6b_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6b_vei_120891">↑</a> Rv. vi. 46, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_7b_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7b_vei_120891">↑</a> Rv. vi. 53. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_8b_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8b_vei_120891">↑</a> Rv. x. 94. 9.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 228</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1c_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1c_vei_120891">↑</a> i. 154, 6; vii. 36, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2c_vei_120891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2c_vei_120891">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 5.</li></ol></div>

गो आंगिरस|गो आंगिरस|गो आङ्गिरस_२|गो आङ्गिरस|గో ఆంగిరస_౨|గో ఆంగిరస|గో ఆఙ్గిరస_౨|గో ఆఙ్గిరస|ಗೋ ಆಂಗಿರಸ_೨|ಗೋ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಗೋ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ_೨|ಗೋ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|go āṅgirasa_2|go āṅgirasa|go aaMgirasa_2|go aaMgirasa|go aangirasa_2|go aangirasa|_vei_120@892|_vei1_pn234
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@891">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@893">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn231">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn234">1:234</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn235">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Go Āṅgirasa</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अंगिरस्">aṅgiras</a></b>’) is the reputed author of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> or Chant in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120892"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120892">1</a></sup> There is little doubt that he is mythical.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120892"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120892">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120892"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120892">↑</a> xvi. 7, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, vi. 11, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120892"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120892">↑</a> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 160; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 68.</li></ol></div>

गो-घात|गोघात|घात|గో-ఘాత|గోఘాత|ఘాత|ಗೋ-ಘಾತ|ಗೋಘಾತ|ಘಾತ|go-ghāta|goghāta|ghāta|go-ghaata|goghaata|ghaata|_vei_120@893|_vei1_pn234
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@892">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@894">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn231">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn234">1:234</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn235">↷</a><hr><p><b>Go-ghāta,</b> ‘a cow-killer,’ is enumerated in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha, or human sacrifice, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120893"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120893">1</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मांस">māṃsa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120893"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120893">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 16, 1.</li></ol></div>

गोतम|గోతమ|ಗೋತಮ|gotama|_vei_120@894|_vei1_pn234
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@893">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@895">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn231">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn234">1:234</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn235">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gotama</b> is mentioned several times in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120894">1</a></sup> but never in such a way as to denote personal authorship of any hymn.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120894">2</a></sup> It seems clear that he was closely connected with the  Aṅgirases, for the Gotamas frequently refer to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अंगिरस्">aṅgiras</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120894">3</a></sup> That he bore the patronymic <a class="vei_120a" href="राहूगण">rāhūgaṇa</a> is rendered probable by one hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120894">4</a></sup> and is assumed in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120894">5</a></sup> where he appears as the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, or domestic priest, of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="माथव">māthava</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेघ">videgha</a>,</b> and as a bearer of Vedic civilization. He is also mentioned in the same <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120894">6</a></sup> as a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a></b> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a>,</b> and as the author of a <a class="vei_120a" href="स्तोम">stoma</a>.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120894">7</a></sup> He occurs, moreover, in two passages of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120894">8</a></sup></p><p>The Gotamas are mentioned in several passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120894">9</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वामदेव">vāmadeva</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नोधस्">nodhas</a></b> being specified as sons of <a class="vei_120a" href="गोतम">gotama</a>. They include the <b>Vājaśravases.</b> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120894">↑</a> Rv. i. 62, 13; 78, 2; 84, 5; 85, 11; iv. 4, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120894">↑</a> Oldenberg. <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 215.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120894">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 62, 1; 71, 2; 74, 5; 75, 2; 78, 3; iv. 2, 5; 16, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120894">↑</a> Rv. i. 78, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 236, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120894">↑</a> i. 4, 1, 10 <i>et seq.;</i> xi. 4, 3, 20. The former passage is wrongly cited by Sāyaṇa on Rv. i. 81, 3. See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 2, 9, n.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120894">↑</a> xi. 4, 3, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120894">↑</a> xiii. 5, 1, 1; Āśvalāvana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ix. 5, 6; 10, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120894">↑</a> iv. 29, 6; xviii. 3, 16. See also Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> in <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 38; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 2, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120894">↑</a> i. 60, 5; 61, 16; 63, 9; 77, 5; 78, 1; 88, 4; 92, 7; iv. 32, 9. 12; viii. 88, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 10.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 110, 123; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 170, 180; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 151, 152.</li></ol></div>

गोतमी-पुत्र|गोतमीपुत्र|गोतमी|गोतमि|पुत्र|గోతమీ-పుత్ర|గోతమీపుత్ర|గోతమీ|గోతమి|పుత్ర|ಗೋತಮೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಗೋತಮೀಪುತ್ರ|ಗೋತಮೀ|ಗೋತಮಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|gotamī-putra|gotamīputra|gotamī|gotami|putra|gotamii-putra|gotamiiputra|gotamii|gotamee-putra|gotameeputra|gotamee|_vei_120@895|_vei1_pn235
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@894">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@896">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn234">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn235">1:235</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn236">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gotamī-putra,</b> ‘son of <a class="vei_120a" href="गोतमी">gotamī</a>,’ is mentioned as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भारद्वाजी-पुत्र">bhāradvājī-putra</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 5, 1). See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौतमी-पुत्र">gautamī-putra</a>.</b></p></div>

गोत्र|గోత్ర|ಗೋತ್ರ|gotra|_vei_120@896|_vei1_pn235
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@895">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@897">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn234">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn235">1:235</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn236">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gotra</b> occurs several times in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120896">1</a></sup> in the account of the mythic exploits of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>. Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120896">2</a></sup> interprets the word as ‘cowstall,’ while Geldner<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120896">3</a></sup> thinks ‘herd’ is meant. The latter sense seems to explain best the employment which the term shows in the later literature as denoting the ‘family’ or ‘clan,’ and which is found in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120896">4</a></sup></p><p>In the <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120896">5</a></sup> stress is laid on the prohibition of  marriage within a <a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a>, or with a Sapiṇḍa of the mother of the bridegroom--that is to say, roughly, with agnates and cognates. Senart<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120896">6</a></sup> has emphasized this fact as a basis of caste, on the ground that marriage within a <i>curia, phratria,</i> or caste (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a></b>) was Indo-European, as was marriage outside the circle of agnates and cognates. But there is no evidence at all<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120896">7</a></sup> to prove that this practice was Indo-European, while in India the Śatapatha<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120896">8</a></sup> expressly recognizes marriage within the third or fourth degree on either side. According to Sāyaṇa, the Kāṇvas accepted marriage in the third degree, the Saurāṣṭras only in the fourth, while the scholiast on the Vajrasūcī<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120896">9</a></sup> adḍs to the Kāṇvas the Andhras and the Dākṣiṇātyas, and remarks that the Vājasaneyins forbade marriage with the daughter of the mother's brother. All apparently allowed marriage with the daughter of a paternal uncle, which later was quite excluded. Change of <a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a> was quite possible, as in the case of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गृत्समद">gṛtsamada</a>,</b> who, once an Aṅgirasa, became a <a class="vei_120a" href="भार्गव">bhārgava</a>.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120896">10</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120896">↑</a> i. 51, 3; ii. 17, 1; 23, 18; iii. 39, 4; 43, 7; viii. 74, 5; x. 48, 2; 103, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120896">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120896">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 275, 276, where he divides the passages according as real or mythical herds are meant.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120896">↑</a> iv. 4, 1. So <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>; i. 4, 16, etc.; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 4, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 15.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120896">↑</a> Gobhila <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 4, 4; Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 5, 11, 15. 16, in Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 387. For <i>sapiṇḍa,</i> see <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiv. 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 17-19.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120896">↑</a> <i>Les Castes dans l'Inde,</i> 210 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> De la Vallée Poussin, <i>Le Védisme,</i> 15.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120896">↑</a> Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1909, 471, 472.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120896">↑</a> i. 8, 3, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120896">↑</a> See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 73-76.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120896">↑</a> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 157; <i>Festgruss an Roth,</i> 108.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 61 <i>et seq.;</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 323.</li></ol></div>

गो-दान|गोदान|दान|గో-దాన|గోదాన|దాన|ಗೋ-ದಾನ|ಗೋದಾನ|ದಾನ|go-dāna|godāna|dāna|go-daana|godaana|daana|_vei_120@897|_vei1_pn236
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@896">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@898">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn235">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn236">1:236</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn237">↷</a><hr><p><b>Go-dāna</b> appears to mean ‘the whiskers’ in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120897"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120897">1</a></sup> where the person, being consecrated, first shaves off the right and then the left ‘whisker.’ Later on the Godānavidhi, or ceremony of shaving the head, is a regular part of the initiation of a youth on the attainment of manhood and on marriage;<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120897"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120897">2</a></sup> but though the ceremony is recognized in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120897"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120897">3</a></sup> the name<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120897"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120897">4</a></sup> does not occur there. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120897"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120897">↑</a> iii. 1, 2, 5, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120897"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120897">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 28, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120897"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120897">↑</a> vi. 68. See <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, liii. 1720. But Av. ii. 13 is not to be classed here as in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a>; it refers to the giving of a new garment to a child, and the removal of its first wrap. See Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 56, 57, correcting Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 173, and Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 322, 323. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 306, 574, 665.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120897"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120897">↑</a> The meaning ‘whisker’ is a secondary one, derived, doubtless, from the ‘gift of a cow’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="गो-दान">go-dāna</a></i>), accompanying the ceremony of shaving the whiskers or hair.</li></ol></div>

गोधा|గోధా|ಗೋಧಾ|godhā|godhaa|_vei_120@898|_vei1_pn237
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@897">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@899">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn236">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn237">1:237</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn238">↷</a><hr><p><b>Godhā.</b>--(<i>a</i>) The sense of ‘bowstring’ seems certain in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120898">1</a></sup> and possible in another.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120898">2</a></sup> Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120898">3</a></sup> also adopts this meaning in the only passage of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120898">4</a></sup> where the word occurs.</p><p>(<i>b</i>) In one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_120898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_120898">1b</a></sup> the sense of ‘musical instrument’ is recognized by Roth and by Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_2b_vei_120898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2b_vei_120898">2b</a></sup> for this word.</p><p>(<i>c</i>) Elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_1c_vei_120898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1c_vei_120898">1c</a></sup> an animal seems to be meant, perhaps the ‘crocodile,’ as Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2c_vei_120898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2c_vei_120898">2c</a></sup> and Weber<sup id="ref_120_3c_vei_120898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3c_vei_120898">3c</a></sup> think; perhaps a large ‘lizard,’ as Roth and Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_4c_vei_120898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4c_vei_120898">4c</a></sup> assume. An animal is probably also meant in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_5c_vei_120898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5c_vei_120898">5c</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120898">↑</a> x. 28, 10. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120898">↑</a> viii. 69, 9. See Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 53.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120898">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120898">↑</a> iv. 3, 6.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_120898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_120898">↑</a> viii. 69, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2b_vei_120898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2b_vei_120898">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 144, n. 1.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1c_vei_120898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1c_vei_120898">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 15, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 35; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 2, 14; <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 5; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 221; Śāṭyāyanaka in Sāyaṇa on Rv. viii. 91; <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 29.</li><li id="fx_120_2c_vei_120898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2c_vei_120898">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 499.</li><li id="fx_120_3c_vei_120898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3c_vei_120898">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 15, 16. Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 368, renders the word vaguely by ‘dragon.’</li><li id="fx_120_4c_vei_120898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4c_vei_120898">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 95.</li><li id="fx_120_5c_vei_120898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5c_vei_120898">↑</a> iv. 3, 6, where Whitney offers no rendering at all.</li></ol></div>

गो-धूम|गोधूम|धूम|గో-ధూమ|గోధూమ|ధూమ|ಗೋ-ಧೂಮ|ಗೋಧೂಮ|ಧೂಮ|go-dhūma|godhūma|dhūma|go-dhuuma|godhuuma|dhuuma|go-dhooma|godhooma|dhooma|_vei_120@899|_vei1_pn237
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@898">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@900">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn236">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn237">1:237</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn238">↷</a><hr><p><b>Go-dhūma,</b> ‘wheat,’ is frequently referred to in the plural in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120899"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120899">1</a></sup> and Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120899"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120899">2</a></sup> and is expressly distinguished from ‘rice’ (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रीहि">vrīhi</a></b>) or ‘barley’ (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="यव">yava</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120899"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120899">3</a></sup> ‘Groats’ (<i>saktavaḥ</i>) made of this grain are also mentioned.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120899"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120899">4</a></sup> The word occurs in the singular in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120899"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120899">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120899"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120899">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 2, 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 12; xix. 22. 89; xxi. 29, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120899"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120899">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 7, 1, 2; 2, 9; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 3, 22 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 3, 13 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>), etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120899"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120899">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 7, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120899"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120899">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 9, 1, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120899"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120899">↑</a> v. 2, 1, 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 241.</li></ol></div>

गो-पति|गोपति|पति|గో-పతి|గోపతి|పతి|ಗೋ-ಪತಿ|ಗೋಪತಿ|ಪತಿ|go-pati|gopati|pati|_vei_120@900|_vei1_pn237
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@899">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@901">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn236">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn237">1:237</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn238">↷</a><hr><p><b>Go-pati,</b> ‘lord of cows,’ is freely used in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120900"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120900">1</a></sup> to denote any lord or master, a natural usage considering that cattle formed the main species of wealth. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120900"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120900">↑</a> i. 101, 4; iv. 24, 1; vi. 45, 21; vii. 18, 4, etc. Av. iii. 14, 6, etc.</li></ol></div>

गोप-वन|गोपवन|गोप|वन|గోప-వన|గోపవన|గోప|వన|ಗೋಪ-ವನ|ಗೋಪವನ|ಗೋಪ|ವನ|gopa-vana|gopavana|gopa|vana|_vei_120@901|_vei1_pn238
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@900">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@902">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn237">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn238">1:238</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn239">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gopa-vana</b> is the name of a poet of the race of <a class="vei_120a" href="अत्रि">atri</a> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120901"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120901">1</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौपवन">gaupavana</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120901"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120901">↑</a> viii. 74, 11. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 215; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 107.</li></ol></div>

गो-पा|गोपा|గో-పా|గోపా|ಗೋ-ಪಾ|ಗೋಪಾ|go-pā|gopā|go-paa|gopaa|_vei_120@902|_vei1_pn238
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@901">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@903">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn237">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn238">1:238</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn239">↷</a><hr><p><b>Go-pā</b> and <b>Go-<a class="vei_120a" href="पाल">pāla</a>,</b> ‘protector of cows,’ occur in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120902"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120902">1</a></sup> and later, but the former is usually metaphorical in sense, applying to any protector, while the latter has the literal force of ‘cow-herd.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120902"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120902">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="गोपा">gopā</a>:</i> Rv. i. 164, 21; ii. 23, 6; iii. 10, 2; v. 12, 4, etc.; <i>Gopāla:</i> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 11; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 5, 4. <i><a class="vei_120a" href="गोपा">gopā</a>,</i> in the sense of ‘guardian,’ occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxiv. 18, in a compound. <i>Gopītha,</i> in the sense of ‘protection,’ occurs in Rv. v. 65, 6; x. 35, 14, etc. <i>Goptṛ,</i> ‘protector,’ first occurs in Av. x. 10, 5, and is thereafter common.</li></ol></div>

गो-बल|गोबल|बल|గో-బల|గోబల|బల|ಗೋ-ಬಲ|ಗೋಬಲ|ಬಲ|go-bala|gobala|bala|_vei_120@903|_vei1_pn238
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@902">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@904">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn237">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn238">1:238</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn239">↷</a><hr><p><b>Go-bala</b> (‘ox-strength’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वार्ष्ण">vārṣṇa</a></b> (‘descendant of Vṛṣṇi’) is mentioned as a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (iii. 11, 9, 3) and the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 6, 1).</p></div>

गो-मती|गो-मति|गोमती|गोमति|मती|मति|గో-మతీ|గో-మతి|గోమతీ|గోమతి|మతీ|మతి|ಗೋ-ಮತೀ|ಗೋ-ಮತಿ|ಗೋಮತೀ|ಗೋಮತಿ|ಮತೀ|ಮತಿ|go-matī|go-mati|gomatī|gomati|matī|mati|go-matii|gomatii|matii|go-matee|gomatee|matee|_vei_120@904|_vei1_pn238
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@903">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@905">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn237">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn238">1:238</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn239">↷</a><hr><p><b>Go-matī,</b> ‘possessing cows,’ is mentioned as a river in the <a class="vei_120a" href="नदी">nadī</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="स्तुति">stuti</a>, or ‘Praise of Rivers,’ in the tenth Maṇḍala of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120904"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120904">1</a></sup> In that hymn a river flowing into the Indus must be meant, and its identification<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120904"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120904">2</a></sup> with the Gomal, a western tributary of the Indus, cannot be doubted. In one other passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120904"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120904">3</a></sup> the accentuation of <a class="vei_120a" href="गोमति">gomati</a> shows that a river is meant. It is possible that in a third passage<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120904"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120904">4</a></sup> the reading should be changed to <i>gomatír</i> from <i>gómatīr.</i> Geldner<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120904"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120904">5</a></sup> suggests that in the two last passages the Gumti, or rather its four upper arms (hence the use of the plural) is meant: this accords well with the later use of the name and with the general probability of the river here intended being in <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>,</b> as the centre of Vedic civilization.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120904"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120904">6</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120904"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120904">↑</a> x. 75, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120904"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120904">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 14; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 200.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120904"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120904">↑</a> viii. 24. 30.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120904"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120904">↑</a> v. 61, 19. See Oldenberg, <i>ṚgvedaNoten.</i> 1, 355, 356.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120904"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120904">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 152, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120904"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120904">↑</a> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 218; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 19, 19 <i>et seq.;</i> Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 174; Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 1141.</li></ol></div>

गो-मायु|गोमायु|मायु|గో-మాయు|గోమాయు|మాయు|ಗೋ-ಮಾಯು|ಗೋಮಾಯು|ಮಾಯು|go-māyu|gomāyu|māyu|go-maayu|gomaayu|maayu|_vei_120@905|_vei1_pn239
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@904">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@906">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn238">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn239">1:239</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn240">↷</a><hr><p><b>Go-māyu,</b> ‘lowing like a cow,’ does not occur as the name of the ‘jackal’ till the late Adbhuta <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120905">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120905">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 40.</li></ol></div>

गो-मृग|गोमृग|मृग|గో-మృగ|గోమృగ|మృగ|ಗೋ-ಮೃಗ|ಗೋಮೃಗ|ಮೃಗ|go-mṛga|gomṛga|mṛga|go-mRga|gomRga|mRga|go-mRiga|gomRiga|mRiga|go-mRuga|gomRuga|mRuga|go-mRRiga|gomRRiga|mRRiga|_vei_120@906|_vei1_pn239
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@905">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@907">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn238">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn239">1:239</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn240">↷</a><hr><p><b>Go-mṛga,</b> a species of ox, now called Gayal (<i>Bos gavaeus</i>), is mentioned in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or horse sacrifice, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120906"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120906">1</a></sup> and Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120906"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120906">2</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120906"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120906">3</a></sup> it is declared to be neither a wild nor a tame animal; this presumably means that it was semidomesticated, or perhaps that it was both tamed and found wild. With the name of this animal may be compared the <a class="vei_120a" href="मृग">mṛga</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="महिष">mahiṣa</a>, which is clearly mentioned as wild in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120906"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120906">4</a></sup> See also <b>Gayava.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120906"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120906">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 11; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 1. 30.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120906"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120906">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 3, 4, 3; 5, 2, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8, 20, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120906"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120906">↑</a> ii. 1, 10, 2</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120906"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120906">↑</a> ix. 92, 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 83, 84; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 338, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

गोलत्तिका|గోలత్తికా|ಗೋಲತ್ತಿಕಾ|golattikā|golattikaa|_vei_120@907|_vei1_pn239
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@906">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@908">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn238">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn239">1:239</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn240">↷</a><hr><p><b>Golattikā</b> is the name of some unknown animal in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or horse sacrifice, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120907"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120907">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120907"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120907">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 16, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 18; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 37. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 99.</li></ol></div>

गो-विकर्तन|गोविकर्तन|विकर्तन|గో-వికర్తన|గోవికర్తన|వికర్తన|ಗೋ-ವಿಕರ್ತನ|ಗೋವಿಕರ್ತನ|ವಿಕರ್ತನ|go-vikartana|govikartana|vikartana|_vei_120@908|_vei1_pn239
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@907">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@909">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn238">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn239">1:239</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn240">↷</a><hr><p><b>Go-vikartana</b> (‘cow-butcher’) designates the ‘huntsman’ in the Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (v. 3, 1, 10).<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120908"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120908">1</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोघात">goghāta</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120908"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120908">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 16, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 18; Weber, <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 82.</li></ol></div>

गो-व्यच|गोव्यच|व्यच|గో-వ్యచ|గోవ్యచ|వ్యచ|ಗೋ-ವ್ಯಚ|ಗೋವ್ಯಚ|ವ್ಯಚ|go-vyaca|govyaca|vyaca|go-vyacha|govyacha|vyacha|_vei_120@909|_vei1_pn239
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@908">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@910">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn238">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn239">1:239</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn240">↷</a><hr><p><b>Go-vyaca.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्यच">vyaca</a>.</b></p></div>

गोशर्य|గోశర్య|ಗೋಶರ್ಯ|gośarya|gosharya|_vei_120@910|_vei1_pn239
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@909">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@911">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn238">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn239">1:239</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn240">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gośarya</b> is the name of a protégé of the Aśvins in the Rigveda (viii. 8, 20; 49, 1; 50, 10).</p></div>

गो-श्रु जाबाल|गोश्रु जाबाल|श्रु जाबाल|గో-శ్రు జాబాల|గోశ్రు జాబాల|శ్రు జాబాల|ಗೋ-ಶ್ರು ಜಾಬಾಲ|ಗೋಶ್ರು ಜಾಬಾಲ|ಶ್ರು ಜಾಬಾಲ|go-śru jābāla|gośru jābāla|śru jābāla|go-shru jaabaala|goshru jaabaala|shru jaabaala|_vei_120@911|_vei1_pn239
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@910">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@912">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn238">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn239">1:239</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn240">↷</a><hr><p><b>Go-śru Jābāla</b> is mentioned as a sage in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 7, 7).</p></div>

गो-श्रुति वैयाघ्र-पद्य|गो-श्रुति वैयाघ्रपद्य|गो-श्रुति वैयाघ्र|पद्य|గో-శ్రుతి వైయాఘ్ర-పద్య|గో-శ్రుతి వైయాఘ్రపద్య|గో-శ్రుతి వైయాఘ్ర|పద్య|ಗೋ-ಶ್ರುತಿ ವೈಯಾಘ್ರ-ಪದ್ಯ|ಗೋ-ಶ್ರುತಿ ವೈಯಾಘ್ರಪದ್ಯ|ಗೋ-ಶ್ರುತಿ ವೈಯಾಘ್ರ|ಪದ್ಯ|go-śruti vaiyāghra-padya|go-śruti vaiyāghrapadya|go-śruti vaiyāghra|padya|go-shruti vaiyaaghra-padya|go-shruti vaiyaaghrapadya|go-shruti vaiyaaghra|_vei_120@912|_vei1_pn239
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@911">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@913">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn238">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn239">1:239</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn240">↷</a><hr><p><b>Go-<a class="vei_120a" href="श्रुति">śruti</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वैयाघ्र">vaiyāghra</a>-padya</b> (‘descendant of Vyāghrapad’) is mentioned as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सत्यकाम">satyakāma</a></b> in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (v. 2, 3). In the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka (ix. 7) the name appears as Gośruta. </p></div>

गो-षादी|गो-षादि|गोषादी|गोषादि|षादी|षादि|గో-షాదీ|గో-షాది|గోషాదీ|గోషాది|షాదీ|షాది|ಗೋ-ಷಾದೀ|ಗೋ-ಷಾದಿ|ಗೋಷಾದೀ|ಗೋಷಾದಿ|ಷಾದೀ|ಷಾದಿ|go-ṣādī|go-ṣādi|goṣādī|goṣādi|ṣādī|ṣādi|go-Shaadii|go-Shaadi|goShaadii|goShaadi|Shaadii|Shaadi|go-Shaadee|goShaadee|Shaadee|_vei_120@913|_vei1_pn240
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@912">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@914">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn239">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn240">1:240</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn241">↷</a><hr><p><b>Go-ṣādī</b> (‘sitting on a cow’) is the name of a bird in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or horse sacrifice, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120913"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120913">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120913"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120913">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 24. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 94.</li></ol></div>

गो-षूक्तिन्|गोषूक्तिन्|षूक्तिन्|గో-షూక్తిన్|గోషూక్తిన్|షూక్తిన్|ಗೋ-ಷೂಕ್ತಿನ್|ಗೋಷೂಕ್ತಿನ್|ಷೂಕ್ತಿನ್|go-ṣūktin|goṣūktin|ṣūktin|go-Shuuktin|goShuuktin|Shuuktin|go-Shooktin|goShooktin|Shooktin|_vei_120@914|_vei1_pn240
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@913">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@915">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn239">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn240">1:240</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn241">↷</a><hr><p><b>Go-ṣūktin</b> is mentioned in the Sarvānukramaṇī as the author of Rigveda viii. 14 and 15, and a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> or Chant of his seems to be referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xix. 4, 9), under the title of Gauṣūkta. But see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौषूक्ति">gauṣūkti</a>.</b></p></div>

गो-ष्ठ|गोष्ठ|ष्ठ|గో-ష్ఠ|గోష్ఠ|ష్ఠ|ಗೋ-ಷ್ಠ|ಗೋಷ್ಠ|ಷ್ಠ|go-ṣṭha|goṣṭha|ṣṭha|go-ShTha|goShTha|ShTha|_vei_120@915|_vei1_pn240
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@914">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@916">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn239">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn240">1:240</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn241">↷</a><hr><p><b>Go-ṣṭha,</b> ‘standing-place for cows,’ denotes not so much a ‘cowstall’ as the ‘grazing ground of cows,’ as Geldner<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120915"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120915">1</a></sup> shows from a passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120915"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120915">2</a></sup> and from a note of Mahīdhara on the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120915"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120915">3</a></sup> This sense suits adequately all the passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120915"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120915">4</a></sup> where it occurs, and it greatly improves the interpretation of a hymn of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120915"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120915">5</a></sup> besides being acceptable elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120915"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120915">6</a></sup> See also <b>Go.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120915"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120915">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 112, 113.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120915"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120915">↑</a> iii. 18, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120915"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120915">↑</a> iii. 21.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120915"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120915">↑</a> i. 191, 4; vi. 28, 1; viii. 43, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120915"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120915">↑</a> iii. 14, 1. 5. 6, where Whitney's rendering ‘stall’ is very unsatisfactory, and Bloomfield's ‘stable’ is no better.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120915"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120915">↑</a> Av. ii. 26, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iii. 21; v. 17; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 8, 3, 2, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 7; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 11.</li></ol></div>

गौतम|గౌతమ|ಗೌತಮ|gautama|_vei_120@916|_vei1_pn240
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@915">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@917">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn239">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn240">1:240</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn241">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gautama,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="गोतम">gotama</a>,’ is a common patronymic, being applied to <b>Aruṇa,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120916">1</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उद्दालक आरुणि">uddālaka āruṇi</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120916">2</a></sup> <b>Kuśri,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120916">3</a></sup> <b>Sāti,</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120916">4</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हारिद्रुमत">hāridrumata</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120916">5</a></sup></p><p>Several Gautamas are mentioned in the Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> as pupils of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आग्निवेश्य">āgniveśya</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120916">6</a></sup> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सैतव">saitava</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राचीनयोग्य">prācīnayogya</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120916">7</a></sup> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सैतव">saitava</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120916">8</a></sup> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भारद्">bhārad</a> vāja,</b><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120916">9</a></sup> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120916">10</a></sup> and of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वात्स्य">vātsya</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120916">11</a></sup> A <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a> is also referred to elsewhere.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120916">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 6, 1, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120916">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> xi. 4, 1, 3; 5, 1, 2; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 7; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 3, 6 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 1; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 42, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120916">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 5, 5, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120916">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> in <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120916">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, 1. 4, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120916">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 6, 1 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120916">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> ii. 6, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120916">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> iv. 6, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120916">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> ii. 6, 2 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> = ii. 5, 22; iv. 5, 27 Mādhyaṃdina).</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120916">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> = ii. 5, 22; iv. 5, 28 Mādhyaṃdina).</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120916">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> = ii. 5, 20. 22; iv. 5, 28 Mādhyaṃdina). The Mādhyaṃdina, ii. 5, 20; iv. 5, 26, knows a <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a>, pupil of <b>Vaijavāpāyana</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैष्ठपुरेय">vaiṣṭhapureya</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

गौतमी-पुत्र|गौतमीपुत्र|गौतमी|गौतमि|पुत्र|గౌతమీ-పుత్ర|గౌతమీపుత్ర|గౌతమీ|గౌతమి|పుత్ర|ಗೌತಮೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಗೌತಮೀಪುತ್ರ|ಗೌತಮೀ|ಗೌತಮಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|gautamī-putra|gautamīputra|gautamī|gautami|putra|gautamii-putra|gautamiiputra|gautamii|gautamee-putra|gautameeputra|gautamee|_vei_120@917|_vei1_pn241
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@916">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@918">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn240">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn241">1:241</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn242">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gautamī-putra</b> (‘son of a female descendant of Gotama’) is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 5, 2) as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भारद्वाजीपुत्र">bhāradvājīputra</a>.</b> In the Mādhyaṃdina (vi. 4, 31) a <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतमीपुत्र">gautamīputra</a> is a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आत्रेयीपुत्र">ātreyīputra</a>,</b> pupil of a <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतमीपुत्र">gautamīputra</a>, pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वात्सीपुत्र">vātsīputra</a>.</b> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोतमीपुत्र">gotamīputra</a>.</b></p></div>

गौप-वन|गौपवन|गौप|वन|గౌప-వన|గౌపవన|గౌప|వన|ಗೌಪ-ವನ|ಗೌಪವನ|ಗೌಪ|ವನ|gaupa-vana|gaupavana|gaupa|vana|_vei_120@918|_vei1_pn241
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@917">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@919">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn240">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn241">1:241</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn242">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gaupa-vana</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोपवन">gopavana</a></b>’) is mentioned as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पौतिमाष्य">pautimāṣya</a></b> in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (ii. 6, 1; iv. 6, 1).</p></div>

गौपायन|గౌపాయన|ಗೌಪಾಯನ|gaupāyana|gaupaayana|_vei_120@919|_vei1_pn241
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@918">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@920">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn240">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn241">1:241</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn242">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gaupāyana</b> (‘descendant of Gopa’). The Gaupāyanas appear in the legend of <b>Asamāti, <a class="vei_120a" href="किरात">kirāta</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आकुलि">ākuli</a>,</b> which is first met with in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120919">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120919">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 12, 5; Jaiminīva <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 167 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 41); Sāṭyāyanaka in Sāyaṇa on Rv. x. 57 (Max Müller's edition, 4^2, c <i>et seq.</i>); Bṛhaddevatā, vii. 83 <i>et seq.,</i> with Macdonell's notes.</li></ol></div>

गौपालायन|గౌపాలాయన|ಗೌಪಾಲಾಯನ|gaupālāyana|gaupaalaayana|_vei_120@920|_vei1_pn241
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@919">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@921">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn240">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn241">1:241</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn242">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gaupālāyana</b> (‘descendant of Gopāla’) is the patronymic of <b>Śucivṛkṣa</b> in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120920"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120920">1</a></sup> It is also the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="औपोदिति">aupoditi</a>,</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="स्थपति">sthapati</a> of the <b>Kurus,</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120920"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120920">2</a></sup> and, as Gaupāleya, of Upoditi or <a class="vei_120a" href="औपोदिति">aupoditi</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120920"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120920">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120920"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120920">↑</a> iii. 10, 4 (p. 135, line 9). <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 48, 9, where Aufrecht reads <i>Gaupalāyana.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120920"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120920">↑</a> xx. 25.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120920"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120920">↑</a> xii. 13, 11, where the edition has <i>Upoditi.</i></li></ol></div>

गौर|గౌర|ಗೌರ|gaura|_vei_120@921|_vei1_pn241
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@920">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@922">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn240">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn241">1:241</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn242">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gaura,</b> a species of ox (<i>Bos gaurus</i>), is frequently mentioned with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गवय">gavaya</a></b> from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120921">1</a></sup> onwards.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120921">2</a></sup> As the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120921">3</a></sup> expressly mentions wild (<i>āraṇya</i>) Gauras, they  must usually have been tame. The female, Gaurī, is also often referred to.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120921">4</a></sup> The compound term <a class="vei_120a" href="गौर">gaura</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="मृग">mṛga</a> (‘the <a class="vei_120a" href="गौर">gaura</a> wild beast’) is sometimes met with.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120921">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120921">↑</a> i. 16, 5; iv. 21, 8; 58, 2; v. 78, 2; vii. 69, 6; 98, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120921">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 10; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 28; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 34, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120921">↑</a> xiii. 48.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120921">↑</a> Rv. i. 84, 10; iv. 12, 6; ix. 12, 3; and in the obscure verse i. 164, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120921">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 32; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 8.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 83, 224.</li></ol></div>

गौरि-वीति शाक्त्य|गौरिवीति शाक्त्य|गौरि|वीति शाक्त्य|గౌరి-వీతి శాక్త్య|గౌరివీతి శాక్త్య|గౌరి|వీతి శాక్త్య|ಗೌರಿ-ವೀತಿ ಶಾಕ್ತ್ಯ|ಗೌರಿವೀತಿ ಶಾಕ್ತ್ಯ|ಗೌರಿ|ವೀತಿ ಶಾಕ್ತ್ಯ|gauri-vīti śāktya|gaurivīti śāktya|gauri|vīti śāktya|gauri-viiti shaaktya|gauriviiti shaaktya|viiti shaaktya|gauri-veeti shaaktya|gauriveeti shaaktya|veeti shaaktya|_vei_120@922|_vei1_pn242
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@921">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@923">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn241">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn242">1:242</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn243">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gauri-vīti Śāktya</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शक्ति">śakti</a></b>’) or <b>Gaurīviti,</b> as the name is also spelt,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120922">1</a></sup> is the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>, or Seer, of a hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120922">2</a></sup> and is frequently mentioned in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120922">3</a></sup> According to the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120922">4</a></sup> he was <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रस्तोतृ">prastotṛ</a> at the Sattra, or sacrificial session, celebrated by the <b>Vibhindukīyas</b> and mentioned in that <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120922">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 8, 3, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5; xii. 13; xxv. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120922">↑</a> v. 29, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120922">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 19; viii. 2 and see n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120922">↑</a> ii. 233 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 38).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 126; Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 215.</li></ol></div>

गौश्र|గౌశ్ర|ಗೌಶ್ರ|gauśra|gaushra|_vei_120@923|_vei1_pn242
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@922">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@924">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn241">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn242">1:242</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn243">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gauśra</b> (‘descendant of Guśri’) is the name of a teacher mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xvi. 9; xxiii. 5). See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौश्ल">gauśla</a>.</b></p></div>

गौश्रायणि|గౌశ్రాయణి|ಗೌಶ್ರಾಯಣಿ|gauśrāyaṇi|gaushraayaNi|_vei_120@924|_vei1_pn242
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@923">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@925">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn241">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn242">1:242</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn243">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gauśrāyaṇi</b> (‘descendant of Gauśra’) is the patronymic of a teacher, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चित्र">citra</a>,</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xxiii. 5).</p></div>

गौश्ल|గౌశ్ల|ಗೌಶ್ಲ|gauśla|gaushla|_vei_120@925|_vei1_pn242
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@924">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@926">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn241">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn242">1:242</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn243">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gauśla,</b> a variant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौश्र">gauśra</a>,</b> is the name of a teacher represented as in disagreement with <b>Buḍila <a class="vei_120a" href="आश्वतर आश्वि">āśvatara āśvi</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120925"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120925">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120925"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120925">↑</a> vi. 30. <i>Cf.</i> Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 9 (Gośla).</li></ol></div>

गौ-षूक्ति|गौषूक्ति|गौ|षूक्ति|గౌ-షూక్తి|గౌషూక్తి|గౌ|షూక్తి|ಗೌ-ಷೂಕ್ತಿ|ಗೌಷೂಕ್ತಿ|ಗೌ|ಷೂಕ್ತಿ|gau-ṣūkti|gauṣūkti|gau|ṣūkti|gau-Shuukti|gauShuukti|Shuukti|gau-Shookti|gauShookti|Shookti|_vei_120@926|_vei1_pn242
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@925">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@927">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn241">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn242">1:242</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn243">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gau-ṣūkti</b> is the name of a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इष श्यावाश्वि">iṣa śyāvāśvi</a></b> according to the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120926"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120926">1</a></sup> in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers). It is also the name, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120926"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120926">2</a></sup>  of a teacher who appears to have been needlessly invented to explain the Gauṣūkta <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> (chant), which is really the <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोषूक्तिन्">goṣūktin</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120926"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120926">↑</a> iv. 16, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120926"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120926">↑</a> xix. 4, 9. <i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 30.</li></ol></div>

ग्रह|గ్రహ|ಗ್ರಹ|graha|_vei_120@927|_vei1_pn243
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@926">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@928">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn242">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn243">1:243</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn244">↷</a><hr><p><b>Graha</b> (‘seizing’) is a term applied to the sun in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120927">1</a></sup> most probably not in the later sense of ‘planet,’ but to denote a power exercising magical influence.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120927">2</a></sup> The sense of ‘planet’ seems first to occur in the later literature, as in the Maitrāyaṇī <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120927">3</a></sup> The question whether the planets were known to the Vedic Indians is involved in obscurity. Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120927">4</a></sup> recognizes them in the Ādityas, whose number is, he believes, seven; sun, moon, and the five planets. But this view, though it cannot be said to be impossible or even unlikely, is not susceptible of proof, and has been rejected by Hillebrandt,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120927">5</a></sup> Pischel,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120927">6</a></sup> von Schroeder,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120927">7</a></sup> Macdonell,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120927">8</a></sup> and Bloomfield,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120927">9</a></sup> among others. Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120927">10</a></sup> sees the planets in the five Adhvaryus mentioned in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120927">11</a></sup> but this is a mere conjecture. The five bulls (<i>ukṣāṇaḥ</i>) in another passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120927">12</a></sup> have received a similar interpretation with equal uncertainty,<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120927">13</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="दुर्ग">durga</a>, in his commentary on the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120927">14</a></sup> even explains the term <i>bhūmija,</i> ‘earth-born,’ which is only mentioned by <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>, as meaning the planet Mars.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_120927">15</a></sup> Thibaut,<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_120927">16</a></sup> who is generally sceptical as to the mention of planets in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>, thinks that <a class="vei_120a" href="बृहस्पति">bṛhaspati</a> there refers to Jupiter; but this is extremely improbable, though in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_120927">17</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="बृहस्पति">bṛhaspati</a> is made the regent of <a class="vei_120a" href="तिष्य">tiṣya</a>. A reference to the  planets is much more probable in the seven suns (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सप्त सूर्याः">sapta sūryāḥ</a></i>) of the late <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_120927">18</a></sup> On the other hand, Ludwig's efforts to find the five planets with the sun, the moon, and the twenty-seven Nakṣatras (lunar mansions) in the Rigveda, as corresponding to the number thirty-four used in connexion with light<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_120927">19</a></sup> (<i>jyotis</i>) and the ribs of the sacrificial horse,<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_120927">20</a></sup> is farfetched. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुक्र">śukra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्थिन्">manthin</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वेन">vena</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120927">↑</a> iv. 6, 5, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120927">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 432, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120927">↑</a> vi. 16. See Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 98, n.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120927">↑</a> <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 185 <i>et seq.; Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 50, 56 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120927">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 102 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120927">↑</a> <i>Go7ttingische Gelehrte Anxeigen,</i> 1895. 447.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120927">↑</a> <i>Vienna Oriental Journal,</i> 9, 109.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120927">↑</a> <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 44.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120927">↑</a> <i>Religion of the <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 133 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120927">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 423.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120927">↑</a> iii. 7, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120927">↑</a> i. 105, 10. <i>Cf.</i> also i. 105, 16, with Oldenberg's note.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120927">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 24, 36.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120927">↑</a> i. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_120927">↑</a> See Weber, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ज्योतिष">jyotiṣa</a>,</i> 10, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_120927">↑</a> <i>Astronomie, Astrologie, und Mathematik,</i> 6.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_120927">↑</a> iv. 4 10, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> pp. 102-104.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_120927">↑</a> i. 7. See on them Weber, <i>Omina und Portenta,</i> 339; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 2, 238; 9, 363; 10, 240, 271; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ज्योतिष">jyotiṣa</a>,</i> 10; <i>Rāmāyaṇa,</i> 28, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_120927">↑</a> x. 55, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_120927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_120927">↑</a> i. 162, 18.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 183 <i>et seq.;</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 354 <i>et seq.;</i> Max Müller, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> iv. 2, xxx. <i>et seq.;</i> Whitney, <i>Oriental and Linguistic Essays,</i> 2, 412, n. <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society</i> 16, lxxxviii.</li></ol></div>

ग्राभ|గ్రాభ|ಗ್ರಾಭ|grābha|graabha|_vei_120@928|_vei1_pn244
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@927">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@929">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn243">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn244">1:244</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn248">↷</a><hr><p><b>Grābha</b> (lit. ‘grasping’) designates the ‘throw’ of dice in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120928"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120928">1</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्लह">glaha</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120928"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120928">↑</a> viii. 81, 1; ix. 106, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Lüders, <i>Das Wūrfelspiel im alten Indien,</i> 49, 50.</li></ol></div>

ग्राम|గ్రామ|ಗ್ರಾಮ|grāma|graama|_vei_120@929|_vei1_pn244
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@928">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@930">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn243">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn244">1:244</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn248">↷</a><hr><p><b>Grāma.</b>--The primitive sense of this word, which occurs frequently from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120929">1</a></sup> onwards, appears to have been ‘village.’ The Vedic Indians must have dwelt in villages which were scattered over the country, some close together,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120929">2</a></sup> some far apart, and were connected by roads.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120929">3</a></sup> The village is regularly contrasted with the forest (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अरण्य">araṇya</a></i>), and its animals and plants with those that lived or grew wild in the woods.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120929">4</a></sup> The villages contained cattle, horses, and other domestic animals, as well as men.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120929">5</a></sup> Grain was also stored in them.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120929">6</a></sup> In the evening the cattle regularly returned thither from the forest.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120929">7</a></sup> The villages were probably open, though perhaps a fort (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुर्">pur</a></b>) might on  occasion be built inside.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120929">8</a></sup> Presumably they consisted of detached houses with enclosures, but no details are to be found in Vedic literature. Large villages (<i>mahāgrāmāḥ</i>) were known.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120929">9</a></sup></p><p>The relation of the villagers is difficult to ascertain with precision. In several passages<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120929">10</a></sup> the word occurs with what appears to be the derivative sense of ‘body of men.’ This sense presumably started from the use of the word to denote the ‘village folk,’ as when <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शर्यात">śaryāta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a></b> is said in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120929">11</a></sup> to have wandered about with his ‘village’ (<i>grāmeṇa</i>); but, as Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120929">12</a></sup> observes, this restricted sense nowhere appears clearly in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120929">13</a></sup> where indeed the ‘folk’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a></i>)<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120929">14</a></sup> of the Bharatas is in one passage<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_120929">15</a></sup> called the ‘horde seeking cows’ (<i>gavyan grāmaḥ</i>). Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_120929">16</a></sup> tends to regard the <a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a> as a clan, and as standing midway between the family and the tribe (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a></b>). The <a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a> may, however, perhaps be regarded more correctly<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_120929">17</a></sup> as an aggregate of several families, not necessarily forming a clan, but only part of a clan (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a></b>), as is often the case at the present day.<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_120929">18</a></sup></p><p>Vedic literature tells us very little about the social economy of the village. There is nothing to show that the community as such held land. What little evidence there is indicates that individual tenure of land was known (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उर्वरा">urvarā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षेत्र">kṣetra</a></b>), but this, in effect though not in law, presumably meant tenure by a family rather than by an individual person. The expression  ‘desirous of a village’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="काम">kāma</a></i>), which occurs frequently in the later Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_120929">19</a></sup> points, however, to the practice of the king's granting to his favourites his royal prerogatives over villages so far as fiscal matters were concerned. Later<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_120929">20</a></sup> the idea developed that the king was owner of all the land, and parallel with that idea the view that the holders of such grants were landlords. But of either idea there is no vestige in Vedic literature beyond the word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="काम">kāma</a>,</i> which much more probably refers to the grant of regalia than to the grant of land, as Teutonic parallels show.<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_120929">21</a></sup> Such grants probably tended to depress the position of the actual cultivators, and to turn them into tenants, but they can hardly have had this effect to any appreciable extent in early times.</p><p>The village does not appear to have been a unit for legal purposes in early days,<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_120929">22</a></sup> and it can hardly be said to have been a political unit. The village no doubt, as later, included in its members various menials, besides the cultivating owners, and also the Brāhmaṇas and Kṣatriyas who might hold interest in it by royal grant or usage without actually cultivating land, such as chariot-makers (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ-कार">ratha-kāra</a></b>), carpenters (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="तक्षन्">takṣan</a></b>), smiths (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="कर्मार">karmāra</a></b>), and others, but they did not presumably, in any sense, form part of the brotherhood.<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_120929">23</a></sup> All alike were politically subject to the king, and bound to render him food or service or other tribute, unless he had transferred his rights to  others of the royal family or household, as was no doubt often the case, either in whole or part. The king's share in a village is referred to as early as the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_120929">24</a></sup></p><p>At the head of the village was the <a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a>-ṇī, or ‘leader of the village,’ who is referred to in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_120929">25</a></sup> and often in the later Saṃhitās and in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_120929">26</a></sup> The exact meaning of the title is not certain. By Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_120929">27</a></sup> the Grāmaṇī is regarded as having had military functions only, and he is certainly often connected with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सेनानी">senānī</a>,</b> or ‘leader of an army.’ But there is no reason so to restrict the sense: presumably the Grāmaṇī was the head of the village both for civil purposes and for military operations. He is ranked in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_28_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_120929">28</a></sup> as inferior to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सूत">sūta</a>,</b> or ‘charioteer,’ with whom, however, he is associated<sup id="ref_120_29_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29_vei_120929">29</a></sup> as one of the <b>Ratnins,</b> the ‘jewels’ of the royal establishment. The post was especially valuable to a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>, who, if he attained it, was at the summit of prosperity (<i>gataśrī</i>).<sup id="ref_120_30_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_30_vei_120929">30</a></sup> The Grāmaṇī's connexion with the royal person seems to point to his having been a nominee of the king rather than a popularly elected officer. But the post may have been sometimes hereditary, and sometimes nominated or elective: there is no decisive evidence available. The use of the singular presents difficulties: possibly the Grāmaṇī of the village or city where the royal residence was situated was specially honoured and influential.<sup id="ref_120_31_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_31_vei_120929">31</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120929">↑</a> i. 44, 10; 114, 1; ii. 12, 7 (perhaps to be taken as in n. 10); x. 146, 1; 149, 4, etc.; Av. iv. 36, 7, 8; v. 17, 4; vi. 40, 2, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iii. 45; xx. 17, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120929">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 2, 4, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 44.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120929">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, viii. 6, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120929">↑</a> Animals: Rv. x. 90, 8; Av. ii. 34, 4; iii. 10, 6; 31, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 2, 2, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 7; xiii. 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ix. 32; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvi. 1, 9; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8. 4, 16, etc. Plants: <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 2, 5, 5; vii. 3, 4, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120929">↑</a> Av. iv. 22, 2; viii. 7, 11, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120929">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 3, 13 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> = 22, Mādhyaṃdina).</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120929">↑</a> Rv. x. 149, 4; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120929">↑</a> As nowadays. See Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 144, citing Hügel, Kashmir, 2, 45.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120929">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 13. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120929">↑</a> Rv. i. 100, 10; iii. 33, 11; x. 27, 1; 127, 5; Av. iv. 7, 5; v. 20, 3 (where, however, ‘villages’ is quite probable); Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 5, 2; vi. 7, 4, 9; xii. 4, 1, 3. <i>Cf.</i> n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120929">↑</a> iv. 1, 5, 2. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120929">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 161.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120929">↑</a> See passages cited in n. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120929">↑</a> Rv. iii. 53, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_120929">↑</a> Rv. iii. 33, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_120929">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 159, 160, where, however, his language is not very clear. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Religions of India,</i> 27, who points out that Zimmer is inaccurate in identifying the tribe with <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>. It is the clan, a division below that of the tribe (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a></b>).</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_120929">↑</a> A village might contain a whole clan, but probably it contained at most a section of a clan. By family is meant a Hindu joint family; but the extent to which such families existed, and the number of persons included, cannot even be conjectured from the avail able evidence. <i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prchistoric Antiquities,</i> 393; Leist, <i>Altarisches Jos Gentium,</i> 34.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_120929">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Baden Powell, <i>Village Communities in India,</i> 85 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_120929">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 1, 2; 3, 2; 3, 9, 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 9; 2, 3; iv. 2, 7, etc.; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 32, thinks that <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a></i> here means ‘herd’ (of cattle).</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_120929">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Baden Powell, <i>Indian Village Community,</i> 207 <i>et seq.</i> Whether or not the idea is already found in <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>, ix. 34, is disputed and uncertain. See <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a>. The germ of it lies in a different sphere--the right of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>,</b> with the consent of the clan, to apportion land (Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 1, 1, 8).</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_120929">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Pollock and Maitland, <i>History of English Law,</i> 2, 237 <i>et seq.;</i> Baden Powell, <i>Village Communities in India,</i> 83; Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India,</i> 48. It may be mentioned that we have no Vedic evidence as to the non-sale of land by the members of a family, except the indications mentioned under <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उर्वरा">urvarā</a>.</b> The later evidence is overwhelming for <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a>,</i> meaning ‘village.’</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 2, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 14; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, 94.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_120929">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Foy, <i>Die ko7nigliche Gewalt,</i> 20, n.; Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 93; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 78, 128.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_120929">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Baden Powell, <i>Indian Village Community,</i> 17, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_120929">↑</a> iv. 22, 2. <i>Cf.</i> n. 20.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_120929">↑</a> x. 62, 11; 107, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_120929">↑</a> Av. iii. 5, 7; xix. 31, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 4, 4; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 6, 5 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a>-ṇīthya,</i> ‘the rank of Grāmaṇī’: <i>cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 4, 5, 2); <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, viii. 4; x. 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xv. 15; xxx. 20; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 4, 8; 7, 3, 4; ii. 7, 18, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 1, 7; v. 4, 4, 8; viii. 6, 2, 1 (<i>grāmaṇīthya</i>); Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 3, 37. 38, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_120929">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 171.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_120929">↑</a> v. 4, 4, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_29_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29_vei_120929">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 1, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_30_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_30_vei_120929">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 4, 4; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 6, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 20, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_31_vei_120929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_31_vei_120929">↑</a> Presumably, there must have been many Grāmaṇīs in a kingdom, but the texts seem to contemplate only one as in the royal entourage. <i>Cf.</i> also Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 60, n.; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 96; Rhys Davids, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 48, thinks that he was elected by the village council or a hereditary officer, because the appointment is only claimed for the king in late authorities like <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>, vii. 115. But there is not even so much authority for election or heredity, and we really cannot say how far the power of the early princes extended: it probably varied very much. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चित्ररथ">citraratha</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

ग्राम्य-वादिन्|ग्राम्यवादिन्|ग्राम्य|वादिन्|గ్రామ్య-వాదిన్|గ్రామ్యవాదిన్|గ్రామ్య|వాదిన్|ಗ್ರಾಮ್ಯ-ವಾದಿನ್|ಗ್ರಾಮ್ಯವಾದಿನ್|ಗ್ರಾಮ್ಯ|ವಾದಿನ್|grāmya-vādin|grāmyavādin|grāmya|vādin|graamya-vaadin|graamyavaadin|graamya|vaadin|_vei_120@930|_vei1_pn248
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@929">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@931">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn244">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn248">1:248</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn249">↷</a><hr><p><b>Grāmya-vādin</b> apparently means a ‘village judge’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120930"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120930">1</a></sup> His <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a>,</b> ‘court,’ is mentioned in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120930"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120930">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 1, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 4; Maitrāyanī Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 1.</li></ol></div>

ग्राह|గ్రాహ|ಗ್ರಾಹ|grāha|graaha|_vei_120@931|_vei1_pn248
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@930">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@932">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn244">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn248">1:248</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn249">↷</a><hr><p><b>Grāha,</b> ‘the seizer,’ is the name of a disease in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120931"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120931">1</a></sup> In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120931"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120931">2</a></sup> it perhaps means ‘paralysis’ of the thigh.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120931"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120931">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120931"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120931">↑</a> iii. 5, 3, 25; 6, 1, 25.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120931"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120931">↑</a> xi. 9, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120931"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120931">↑</a> If the reading of the commentary <i>ūru-grāhaiḥ</i> be adopted; but Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 653, retaining the reading of the text <i>urugrāhaiḥ,</i> renders the compound as an adjective, ‘wide-gripping.’ <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 635.</li></ol></div>

ग्राहि|గ్రాహి|ಗ್ರಾಹಿ|grāhi|graahi|_vei_120@932|_vei1_pn248
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@931">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@933">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn244">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn248">1:248</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn249">↷</a><hr><p><b>Grāhi,</b> ‘the seizer,’ appears in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120932"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120932">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120932"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120932">2</a></sup> as a female demon of disease. Her son is sleep (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्वप्न">svapna</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120932"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120932">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120932"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120932">↑</a> x. 161, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120932"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120932">↑</a> ii. 9, 1; 10, 6. 8; vi. 112, 1; 113, 1; viii. 2, 12; 3, 18; xvi. 7, 1; 8, 1; xix. 45, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120932"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120932">↑</a> xvi. 5, 1; or perhaps ‘dream’ is meant.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 154.</li></ol></div>

ग्रीष्म|గ్రీష్మ|ಗ್ರೀಷ್ಮ|grīṣma|griiShma|greeShma|_vei_120@933|_vei1_pn248
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@932">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@934">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn244">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn248">1:248</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn249">↷</a><hr><p><b>Grīṣma.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋतु">ṛtu</a>.</b></p></div>

ग्रैव्य|గ్రైవ్య|ಗ್ರೈವ್ಯ|graivya|_vei_120@934|_vei1_pn248
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@933">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@935">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn244">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn248">1:248</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn249">↷</a><hr><p><b>Graivya,</b> in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120934"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120934">1</a></sup> appears to denote ‘tumours on the neck’ (<i>grīvāḥ</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120934"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120934">↑</a> vi. 25, 2; vii. 76, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Proceedings of the American Oriental Society,</i> October, 1887, xix.; <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 472.</li></ol></div>

ग्लह|గ్లహ|ಗ್ಲಹ|glaha|_vei_120@935|_vei1_pn248
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@934">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@936">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn244">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn248">1:248</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn249">↷</a><hr><p><b>Glaha</b> denotes the ‘throw’ at dice, like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राभ">grābha</a>,</b> of which it is a later form, occurring in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120935"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120935">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120935"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120935">↑</a> iv. 28, 1 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> Lüders, <i>Das Würfelspiel im alten Indien,</i> 49.</li></ol></div>

ग्लाव मैत्रेय|గ్లావ మైత్రేయ|ಗ್ಲಾವ ಮೈತ್ರೇಯ|glāva maitreya|glaava maitreya|_vei_120@936|_vei1_pn248
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@935">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@937">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn244">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn248">1:248</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn249">↷</a><hr><p><b>Glāva Maitreya</b> (‘descendant of Maitrī’) is mentioned in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120936"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120936">1</a></sup> where he is said to be the same as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वक दाल्भ्य">vaka dālbhya</a>.</b> He appears as Pratistotṛ at the snake festival of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120936"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120936">2</a></sup> and is referred to in the Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120936"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120936">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120936"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120936">↑</a> i. 12, 1. 3. <i>Cf.</i> Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 31.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120936"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120936">↑</a> xxv. 15, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120936"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120936">↑</a> i. 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 35, 38.</li></ol></div>

ग्लौ|గ్లౌ|ಗ್ಲೌ|glau|_vei_120@937|_vei1_pn249
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@936">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@938">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn248">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn249">1:249</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn250">↷</a><hr><p><b>Glau</b> occurs in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120937"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120937">1</a></sup> and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120937"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120937">2</a></sup> as the name of some symptom of a disease, probably, as Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120937"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120937">3</a></sup> thinks, ‘boils.’ In the one passage of the Vājasaneyi Saṃhita,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120937"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120937">4</a></sup> where it is found the sense is obscure, some part of the sacrificial victim being perhaps meant.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120937"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120937">5</a></sup></p><p><i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गलुन्त">galunta</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120937"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120937">↑</a> vi. 83, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120937"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120937">↑</a> i. 25.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120937"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120937">↑</a> <i>Proceedings of the American Oriental Society,</i> October, 1887, xv.; <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 17, 503; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 343.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120937"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120937">↑</a> xxv. 8; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 15, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120937"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120937">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 500, takes <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्लौ">glau</a></i> as ‘owl.’</li></ol></div>

घर्म|ఘర్మ|ಘರ್ಮ|gharma|_vei_120@938|_vei1_pn249
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@937">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@939">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn248">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn249">1:249</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn250">↷</a><hr><p><b>Gharma</b> denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120938"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120938">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120938"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120938">2</a></sup> the pot used for heating milk, especially for the offering to the Aśvins. It hence often<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120938"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120938">3</a></sup> denotes the hot milk itself, or some other hot drink.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120938"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120938">↑</a> iii. 53, 14; v. 30, 15; 43, 7; 76, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120938"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120938">↑</a> Av. vii. 73, 6; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, viii. 61; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 18. 22, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120938"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120938">↑</a> Rv. i. 119, 2; 180, 4; vii. 70, 2; viii. 9, 4, etc.; Av. iv. 1, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxviii. 6, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 32; xi. 42; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 271; St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

घास|ఘాస|ಘಾಸ|ghāsa|ghaasa|_vei_120@939|_vei1_pn249
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@938">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@940">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn248">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn249">1:249</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn250">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ghāsa</b> means ‘fodder’ in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120939">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120939">2</a></sup> In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120939">3</a></sup> Ghāsi is used of the fodder of the horse victim at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120939">↑</a> Av. iv. 38, 7; viii. 7, 8; xi. 5, 18, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120939">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xi. 75; xxi. 43; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 5, 9, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 3, 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120939">↑</a> i. 162, 14.</li></ol></div>

घृणीवन्त्|ఘృణీవన్త్|ಘೃಣೀವನ್ತ್|ghṛṇīvant|ghRNiivant|ghRNeevant|ghRiNiivant|ghRuNiivant|ghRRiNiivant|_vei_120@940|_vei1_pn249
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@939">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@941">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn248">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn249">1:249</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn250">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ghṛṇīvant</b> is the name of some animal in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or horse sacrifice, in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120940"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120940">1</a></sup> In the parallel passage of the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120940"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120940">2</a></sup> Ghṛṇāvant is the reading. Elsewhere the word is adjectival.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120940"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120940">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120940"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120940">↑</a> xxiv. 39.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120940"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120940">↑</a> iii. 14, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120940"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120940">↑</a> Rv. x. 176, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 99.</li></ol></div>

घृत|ఘృత|ಘೃತ|ghṛta|ghRta|ghRita|ghRuta|ghRRita|_vei_120@941|_vei1_pn250
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@940">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@942">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn249">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn250">1:250</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn251">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ghṛta,</b> the modern Ghee or ‘clarified butter,’ is repeatedly mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120941"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120941">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120941"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120941">2</a></sup> both as in ordinary use and as a customary form of sacrifice. According to a citation in Sāyaṇa's commentary on the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120941"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120941">3</a></sup> the distinction between <a class="vei_120a" href="घृत">ghṛta</a> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सर्पिस्">sarpis</a></b> consisted in the latter being butter fully melted, while the former was butter melted and hardened (<i>ghanī-<a class="vei_120a" href="भूत">bhūta</a></i>), but this distinction cannot be pressed. Because the butter was thrown into the fire, <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> is styled ‘butter-faced’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="घृत">ghṛta</a>-pratīka</i>),<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120941"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120941">4</a></sup> ‘butter-backed’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="घृत">ghṛta</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पृष्ठ">pṛṣṭha</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120941"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120941">5</a></sup> and ‘propitiated with butter’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="घृत">ghṛta</a>-prasatta</i>),<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120941"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120941">6</a></sup> and ‘found of butter’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="घृत">ghṛta</a>-prī</i>).<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120941"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120941">7</a></sup> Water was used to purify the butter: the waters were therefore called ‘butter-cleansing’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="घृत">ghṛta</a>-pū</i>).<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120941"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120941">8</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Brāhmaṇa^9 it is said that <a class="vei_120a" href="आज्य">ājya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="घृत">ghṛta</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="आयुत">āyuta</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="नवनीत">navanīta</a> pertain to gods, men, Pitṛs, and embryos respectively.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120941"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120941">↑</a> i. 134, 6; ii. 10, 4; iv. 10, 6; 58, 5. 7. 9; v. 12, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120941"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120941">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ii. 22, etc.; Av. iii. 13, 5, etc.; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 1, 7 (with <a class="vei_120a" href="दधि">dadhi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मस्तु">mastu</a>, Amikṣā); ix. 2, 1, 1 (<a class="vei_120a" href="दधि">dadhi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मधु">madhu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="घृत">ghṛta</a>), etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120941"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120941">↑</a> i. 3 (p. 240, edition Aufrecht).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120941"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120941">↑</a> Rv. i. 143, 7; iii. 1, 18; v. 11, 1; x. 21, 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120941"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120941">↑</a> Rv. i. 164, 1; v. 4, 3; 37, 1; vii. 2, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120941"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120941">↑</a> Rv. v. 15. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120941"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120941">↑</a> Av. xii. 1, 20; xviii. 4, 41.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120941"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120941">↑</a> i. 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 227.</li></ol></div>

घृत-कौशिक|घृतकौशिक|घृत|कौशिक|ఘృత-కౌశిక|ఘృతకౌశిక|ఘృత|కౌశిక|ಘೃತ-ಕೌಶಿಕ|ಘೃತಕೌಶಿಕ|ಘೃತ|ಕೌಶಿಕ|ghṛta-kauśika|ghṛtakauśika|ghṛta|kauśika|ghRta-kaushika|ghRtakaushika|ghRta|kaushika|ghRita-kaushika|ghRitakaushika|ghRita|ghRuta-kaushika|ghRutakaushika|ghRuta|ghRRita-kaushika|ghRRitakaushika|ghRRita|_vei_120@942|_vei1_pn250
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@941">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@943">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn249">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn250">1:250</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn251">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ghṛta-kauśika</b> is mentioned in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) of the Mādhyaṃdina recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120942">1</a></sup> as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पाराशर्यायण">pārāśaryāyaṇa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120942">↑</a> ii. 5. 21; iv. 5, 27. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 384.</li></ol></div>

घोर आंगिरस|घोर आङ्गिरस|ఘోర ఆంగిరస|ఘోర ఆఙ్గిరస|ಘೋರ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಘೋರ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|ghora āṅgirasa|ghora aaMgirasa|ghora aangirasa|_vei_120@943|_vei1_pn250
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@942">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@944">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn249">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn250">1:250</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn251">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ghora Āṅgirasa</b> is the name of a mythical teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120943"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120943">1</a></sup> and the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120943"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120943">2</a></sup> where he is teacher of the strange <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण देवकीपुत्र">kṛṣṇa devakīputra</a>.</b> That the name is certainly a mere figment is shown by the fact that this ‘dread descendant of the Aṅgirases’ has a counterpart in <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भिषज् आथर्वण">bhiṣaj ātharvaṇa</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120943"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120943">3</a></sup> ‘the healing descendant of the Atharvans,’ while in the Rigveda Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120943"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120943">4</a></sup> the <i>Atharvāṇo vedaḥ</i> is connected  with <i>bheṣajam</i> and the <i>Āṅgiraso vedaḥ</i> with <i>ghoram.</i> He is accordingly a personification of the dark side of the practice of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120943"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120943">5</a></sup> He is also mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> section of the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120943"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120943">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120943"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120943">↑</a> xxx. 6. <i>Cf.</i> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> Sütra, xii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120943"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120943">↑</a> iii. 17, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120943"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120943">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 459.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120943"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120943">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 2; <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society.</i> 17, 181.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120943"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120943">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> xx, xxi, xxxviii; <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 8, 23; Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 189, 190; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 160, n. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120943"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120943">↑</a> i. 1.</li></ol></div>

घोष|ఘోష|ಘೋಷ|ghoṣa|ghoSha|_vei_120@944|_vei1_pn251
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@943">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@945">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn250">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn251">1:251</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn252">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ghoṣa.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="घोषा">ghoṣā</a>.</b></p></div>

घोषवन्त्|ఘోషవన్త్|ಘೋಷವನ್ತ್|ghoṣavant|ghoShavant|_vei_120@945|_vei1_pn251
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@944">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@946">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn250">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn251">1:251</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn252">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ghoṣavant.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्वर">svara</a>.</b></p></div>

घोषा|ఘోషా|ಘೋಷಾ|ghoṣā|ghoShaa|_vei_120@946|_vei1_pn251
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@945">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@947">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn250">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn251">1:251</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn252">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ghoṣā</b> is mentioned as a protégée of the Aśvins in two passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120946">1</a></sup> probably as the recipient of a husband, who is perhaps referred to in another passage<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120946">2</a></sup> as Arjuna, though this is not likely. Sāyaṇa finds a reference there to a skin disease, which is considered in the later tradition of the Bṛhaddevatā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120946">3</a></sup> to have been the cause of her remaining unwed, but this view is not tenable. According to Sāyaṇa, her son, Suhastya, is alluded to in an obscure verse of the Rigveda;<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120946">4</a></sup> Oldenberg,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120946">5</a></sup> however, here sees a reference to <a class="vei_120a" href="घोषा">ghoṣā</a> herself, while Pischel<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120946">6</a></sup> thinks that the form (<i>ghoṣe</i>) is not a noun at all, but verbal.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120946">↑</a> i. 117, 7; x. 40, 5. <i>Cf.</i> x. 39, 3. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120946">↑</a> i. 122, 5. See Oldenberg, <i>ṚgvedaNoten,</i> 1, 123.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120946">↑</a> vii. 41-48, with Macdonell's notes.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120946">↑</a> i. 120, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120946">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 119. Suhastya is apparently invented from x. 41, 3, probably assisted by the fact that <a class="vei_120a" href="वध्रिमती">vadhrimatī</a> was given a son, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्यहस्त">hiraṇyahasta</a>,</b> by the Aśvins (Rv. i. 117, 24).</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120946">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 4; 2, 92.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 143; <i>Über Methode bei Interpretation des Rigveda,</i> 43; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 247; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 52.</li></ol></div>

चक|చక|ಚಕ|caka|chaka|_vei_120@947|_vei1_pn251
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@946">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@948">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn250">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn251">1:251</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn252">↷</a><hr><p><b>Caka</b> is mentioned with <a class="vei_120a" href="पिशंग">piśaṅga</a> as one of the two Unnetṛ priests at the snake festival in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120947"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120947">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120947"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120947">↑</a> xxv. 15, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 35. who reads <i>Cakka;</i> 10, 142. n. 3, 144</li></ol></div>

चक्र|చక్ర|ಚಕ್ರ|cakra|chakra|_vei_120@948|_vei1_pn252
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@947">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@949">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn251">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn252">1:252</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn253">↷</a><hr><p><b>Cakra,</b> the ‘wheel’ of a chariot or wagon, is repeatedly mentioned from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120948">1</a></sup> onwards, often in a metaphorical sense. The wheel was fixed on the axle (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a></b>) when the chariot was required for use; this required considerable strength, as is shown by a reference in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120948">2</a></sup> The wheel consisted normally of spokes (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अर">ara</a></b>), and a nave (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="नाभि">nābhi</a></b>),<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120948">3</a></sup> in the opening (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="ख">kha</a></b>) of which the end of the axle (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="आणि">āṇi</a></b>) was inserted. An indication of the importance attached to the strength of the wheel is the celebration of the car of the god Pūṣan as having a wheel that suffers no damage.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120948">4</a></sup> The usual number of wheels was two,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120948">5</a></sup> but in seven passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120948">6</a></sup> a chariot is called ‘three-wheeled,’ in a few others ‘sevenwheeled,’<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120948">7</a></sup> while in one of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120948">8</a></sup> it is styled ‘eightwheeled.’ Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120948">9</a></sup> argues that these epithets do not refer to real chariots, pointing out that in all the passages where <i><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रि">tri</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="चक्र">cakra</a>,</i> ‘three-wheeled,’ occurs there is a mythical reference. On the other hand, Weber<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120948">10</a></sup> thinks that there might have been chariots with three wheels, one being in the centre between the two occupants. This is not very conclusive; at any rate, the seven-wheeled and the eight-wheeled chariots can hardly be regarded as indicating the existence of real vehicles with that number of wheels.</p><p>In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120948">11</a></sup> the potter's wheel (<i>kaulālacakra</i>) is referred to.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120948">↑</a> i. 130, 9; 155, 6; 164, 2. 11. 14; 174, 5; iv. 1, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120948">↑</a> Av. xi. 7, 4; xix. 53, 1. 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120948">↑</a> Rv. viii. 41, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120948">↑</a> Rv. vi. 54, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120948">↑</a> Rv. viii. 5, 29; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 16, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120948">↑</a> i. 118, 2; 157, 3; 183, 1; viii. 58, 3; x. 41, 1; 85, 14 (all of the Aśvins' chariot); iv. 36, 1 (of a chariot made by the Ṛbhus, who are three in number).</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120948">↑</a> Rv. i. 164, 3, 12; ii. 40, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120948">↑</a> xi. 4, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120948">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> viii., ix.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120948">↑</a> <i>Proceedings of the Berlin Acadcmy,</i> 1898, 564, quoting Virchow, <i>Zeistchrift für Ethnologie,</i> 5, 200.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120948">↑</a> xi. 8, 1, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 247.</li></ol></div>

चक्र-वाक|चक्रवाक|चक्र|वाक|చక్ర-వాక|చక్రవాక|చక్ర|వాక|ಚಕ್ರ-ವಾಕ|ಚಕ್ರವಾಕ|ಚಕ್ರ|ವಾಕ|cakra-vāka|cakravāka|cakra|vāka|chakra-vaaka|chakravaaka|chakra|vaaka|_vei_120@949|_vei1_pn252
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@948">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@950">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn251">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn252">1:252</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn253">↷</a><hr><p><b>Cakra-vāka</b> is the name, apparently derived from the nature of its cry, of a species of gander (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अनस्">anas</a> casarca</i>), the modern Chakwā, as it is called in Hindī, or Brahmany duck in English.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120949">1</a></sup> It is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120949">2</a></sup> and in the list of victims at the  <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or horse sacrifice, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120949">3</a></sup> while in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120949">4</a></sup> it already appears as the type of conjugal fidelity, its characteristic in the classical literature.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120949">↑</a> Criffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 1, 309, n. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120949">↑</a> ii. 39, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120949">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 3. 13; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 22. 32; xxv. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120949">↑</a> xiv. 2, 64.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 89.</li></ol></div>

चक्षुस्|चक्षु|చక్షుస్|చక్షు|ಚಕ್ಷುಸ್|ಚಕ್ಷು|cakṣus|cakṣu|chakShus|chakShu|chaxus|chaxu|_vei_120@950|_vei1_pn253
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@949">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@951">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn252">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn253">1:253</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn254">↷</a><hr><p><b>Cakṣus,</b> ‘eye.’ The ‘evil eye’ (<i>ghoraṃ <a class="vei_120a" href="चक्षुस्">cakṣus</a></i>) was well known in the Atharvaveda, which contains spells to counteract its influence.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120950"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120950">1</a></sup> As remedies against it are mentioned salve from Mount <b>Trikakubh</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120950"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120950">2</a></sup> and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जंगिड">jaṅgiḍa</a></b> plant.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120950"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120950">3</a></sup> In the wedding ceremony the wife is entreated not to have the evil eye (<i>aghoracakṣus</i>).<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120950"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120950">4</a></sup> The structure of the eye, and its division into white (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शुक्ल">śukla</a></i>), dark (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण">kṛṣṇa</a></i>), and the pupil (<i>kanīn ikā</i>) are repeatedly referred to in the later Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120950"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120950">5</a></sup> The desease <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अलजि">alaji</a></b> appears to have been an affection of the eyes.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120950"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120950">↑</a> ii. 7; xix. 45, are so employed in the ritual.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120950"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120950">↑</a> Av. iv. 9, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120950"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120950">↑</a> Av. xix. 35, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120950"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120950">↑</a> Pāraskara <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 16.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120950"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120950">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 8, 2, 26; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 254. 324; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 26, 1; 34, 1; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 2, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 1, 5, etc. So the man (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरु">puru</a> 1</b>) in the eye is repeatedly mentioned: Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 7, 5; iv. 15, 1; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 3, 5; iv. 2, 2; v. 5, 2. 4, etc.; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 27, 2. The Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 2, 3, adds the water (<i>āpaḥ</i>) in the eye, the upper and the lower lids (<i>vartanī</i>), and seven red lines (<i>lohinyo rājayaḥ</i>).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 149.</li></ol></div>

चण्डाल|చణ్డాల|ಚಣ್ಡಾಲ|caṇḍāla|chaNDaala|_vei_120@951|_vei1_pn253
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@950">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@952">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn252">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn253">1:253</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn254">↷</a><hr><p><b>Caṇḍāla,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120951"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120951">1</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चाण्डाल">cāṇḍāla</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120951"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120951">2</a></sup> are the variant forms of the name of a despised caste, which in origin was probably a tribal body,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120951"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120951">3</a></sup> but which in the Brahminical theory was the offspring of <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> athers and Brahmin mothers.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120951"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120951">4</a></sup> The references to the caste in he <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās and in the Upaniṣads show clearly hat it was a degraded one, but they yield no particulars. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120951"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120951">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 10, 7; 14, 4; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 12; vi. 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120951"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120951">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 21; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 17, 1; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120951"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120951">↑</a> Fick, <i>Die sociale Gliederung.</i> 204 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120951"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120951">↑</a> Apparently accepted for the Vedic period by Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 217.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Litcratur und Cultur,</i> 433.</li></ol></div>

चतुष्-पद्|चतुष्पद्|चतुष्|पद्|చతుష్-పద్|చతుష్పద్|చతుష్|పద్|ಚತುಷ್-ಪದ್|ಚತುಷ್ಪದ್|ಚತುಷ್|ಪದ್|catuṣ-pad|catuṣpad|catuṣ|pad|chatuSh-pad|chatuShpad|chatuSh|_vei_120@952|_vei1_pn254
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@951">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@953">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn253">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn254">1:254</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn255">↷</a><hr><p><b>Catuṣ-pad,</b> ‘quadruped,’ is a regular name for animals from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120952"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120952">1</a></sup> onwards, being frequently contrasted with <b>Dvipad,</b> ‘biped.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120952"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120952">2</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="चतुष्">catuṣ</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पाद">pāda</a>, as an adjective applying to <i>paśavaḥ,</i> ‘animals,’ is also found.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120952"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120952">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120952"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120952">↑</a> Rv. i. 49, 3; 94, 5; 119, 1; iii. 62, 14, etc.; Av. iv. 11, 5; x. 8, 21; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, viii. 30; ix. 31; xiv. 8. 25, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 2; viii. 20, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120952"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120952">↑</a> Rv. x. 117, 8; Av. vi. 107, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120952"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120952">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 18; vi. 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 7, 3, 2; vi. 8, 2, 17, etc.</li></ol></div>

चन्द्र|चन्द्र|చన్ద్ర_౧|చన్ద్ర|ಚನ್ದ್ರ_೧|ಚನ್ದ್ರ|candra_1|candra|chandra_1|chandra|_vei_120@953|_vei1_pn254
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@952">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@954">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn253">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn254">1:254</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn255">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Candra,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120953"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120953">1</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चन्द्र">candra</a>-mas,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120953"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120953">2</a></sup> are the names of the ‘moon,’ the latter occurring from the Rigveda onwards, but the former being first used in this sense by the Atharvaveda. Very little is said about the moon in Vedic literature, except as identified with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120953"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120953">3</a></sup> both alike being described as waxing and waning. Reference is, however, made to the regular changes of the moon, and to its alternation with the sun,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120953"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120953">5</a></sup> to which it, as <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>, is declared in the Rigveda to be married.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120953"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120953">6</a></sup> Mention is also made of its disappearance at the time of new moon,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120953"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120953">7</a></sup> and of its birth from the light of the sun.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120953"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120953">8</a></sup> In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120953"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120953">9</a></sup> reference is made to demons eclipsing the moon (<i>grahāś cāndramāsāḥ</i>).</p><p>For the phases of the moon, and the month as a measure of time, see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b> For the moon and its mansions, see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p><p>^4) Rv. x. 55, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Av. x. 8, 32.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120953"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120953">↑</a> Av. ii. 15, 2; 22, 1; iii. 31, 6, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxii. 28; xxxix. 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 2, 2, 16, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120953"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120953">↑</a> Rv. i. 105, 1; viii. 82, 8; x. 64, 3; 85, 19; Av. xi. 6, 7; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, i. 28; xxiii. 10. 59, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120953"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120953">↑</a> See Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> pp. 112, 113. The identification is clearly found in the later parts of the Rigveda.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120953"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120953">↑</a> Rv. x. 68, 10. <i>Cf.</i> i. 62, 8; 72, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120953"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120953">↑</a> x. 85, 18. 19.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120953"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120953">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 4, 18; iv. 6, 7, 12; xi. 1, 6, 19; xiv. 4, 2, 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 28, 8; perhaps Rv. x. 138, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120953"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120953">↑</a> Rv. ix. 71, 9; 76, 4; 86, 32; <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>, ii. 9, 2, 12, 1; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 463 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूर्य">sūrya</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120953"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120953">↑</a> xix. 9, 10. Av. vi. 128 is also regarded by the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, c. 3, as referring to an eclipse of the moon. See Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 533.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 349, 350, 352.</li></ol></div>

चन्द्र|चन्द्र|చన్ద్ర_౨|చన్ద్ర|ಚನ್ದ್ರ_೨|ಚನ್ದ್ರ|candra_2|candra|chandra_2|chandra|_vei_120@954|_vei1_pn254
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@953">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@955">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn253">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn254">1:254</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn255">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Candra</b> appears to denote ‘gold’ in a certain number of passages from the Rigveda onwards.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120954"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120954">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120954"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120954">↑</a> Rv. ii. 2, 4; iii. 31, 5; Av. xii. 2, 53; Jaittirīya Saṃhitā, i. 2, 7, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 6; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iv. 26; xix. 93; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 3, 4, etc. <i>Cf.</i> the adjective <i>candrin</i> in Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xx. 37; xxxi. 31.</li></ol></div>

चप्य|చప్య|ಚಪ್ಯ|capya|chapya|_vei_120@955|_vei1_pn255
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@954">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@956">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn254">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn255">1:255</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn256">↷</a><hr><p><b>Capya</b> is found in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120955"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120955">1</a></sup> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120955"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120955">2</a></sup> as the name of a sacrificial vessel.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120955"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120955">↑</a> xix. 88; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 2, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxviii. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120955"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120955">↑</a> xii. 7, 2, 13; 9, 1, 3.</li></ol></div>

चमस|చమస|ಚಮಸ|camasa|chamasa|_vei_120@956|_vei1_pn255
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@955">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@957">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn254">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn255">1:255</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn256">↷</a><hr><p><b>Camasa</b> denotes a ‘drinking vessel,’ usually as employed for holding <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a></b> at the sacrifice. It is frequently mentioned from the Rigveda onwards.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120956"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120956">1</a></sup> It was made of wood (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वृक्ष">vṛkṣa</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120956"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120956">2</a></sup> and is hence called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="द्रु">dru</a>.</i><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120956"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120956">3</a></sup> According to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120956"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120956">4</a></sup> it was made of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उदुम्बर">udumbara</a></b> wood.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120956"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120956">↑</a> Rv. i. 20, 6; 110, 3; viii. 82, 7; x. 16, 8; 68, 8; 96, 9, etc.; Av. vii. 73, 3; xviii. 3, 54; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 13, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, xi. 2; xii. 38.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120956"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120956">↑</a> Rv. x. 68, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120956"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120956">↑</a> Rv. i. 161, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120956"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120956">↑</a> vii. 2, 11, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 280; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 167, 168.</li></ol></div>

चमू|చమూ|ಚಮೂ|camū|chamuu|chamoo|_vei_120@957|_vei1_pn255
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@956">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@958">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn254">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn255">1:255</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn256">↷</a><hr><p><b>Camū</b> is a term of somewhat doubtful sense occurring repeatedly in the Rigveda, and connected with the preparation of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120957"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120957">1</a></sup> considers that in the dual it denotes the two boards between which, in his opinion, the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> was crushed (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अधिषवण">adhiṣavaṇa</a></b>). Roth,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120957"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120957">2</a></sup> however, appears to be right in taking the normal sense to designate a vessel into which the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> was poured from the press, and Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120957"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120957">3</a></sup> shows clearly that when it occurs in the plural<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120957"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120957">4</a></sup> it always has this sense, corresponding to the <a class="vei_120a" href="ग्रह">graha</a>-pātras of the later ritual, and that sometimes it is so used in the singular<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120957"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120957">5</a></sup> or dual<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120957"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120957">6</a></sup> also. In some cases,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120957"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120957">7</a></sup> however, he recognizes its use as denoting the mortar in which the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> was pressed: he may be right here, as this mode of preparation was probably Indo-Iranian.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120957"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120957">8</a></sup></p><p>In a derivative sense <a class="vei_120a" href="चमू">camū</a> appears in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120957"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120957">9</a></sup> to denote a trough, either of solid stone or consisting of  bricks, used by the Eastern people to protect the body of the dead from contact with the earth, like modern stone-lined graves or vaults.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120957"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120957">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 277, 278.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120957"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120957">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. Cf.</i> Grassmann, <i>Rigveda,</i> 1, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120957"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120957">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 164-175.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120957"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120957">↑</a> Rv. iii. 48, 5; viii. 2, 8; 82, 7. 8; ix. 20, 6; 62, 16; 63, 2; 92, 2; 93, 3; 97, 21. 37. 46; 99, 6. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120957"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120957">↑</a> Rv. ix. 107, 18; x. 91, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120957"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120957">↑</a> Rv. ix. 69, 5; 71, 1; 72, 5; 86, 47; 96, 20. 21; 97 2. 48; 103, 4; 107, 10; 108. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120957"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120957">↑</a> Singular: Rv. v. 51, 4; viii. 4, 4; 76, 10; ix. 46, 3; x. 24, 1. Dual: i. 28, 9; iv. 18, 3; vi. 57, 2; ix. 36, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120957"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120957">↑</a> Hillebrandt, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 158-164.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120957"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120957">↑</a> xiii. 8, 2, 1; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 430, n. 1. In <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiv. 22, 19, the sense is doubtful.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> pp. 105 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

चरक|చరక|ಚರಕ|caraka|charaka|_vei_120@958|_vei1_pn256
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@957">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@959">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn255">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn256">1:256</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn257">↷</a><hr><p><b>Caraka</b> primarily denotes a ‘wandering student,’ a sense actually found in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120958"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120958">1</a></sup> More especially it denotes the members of a school of the Black <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>, the practices of which are several times referred to with disapproval in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120958"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120958">2</a></sup> In the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120958"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120958">3</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="चरक">caraka</a> teacher (<i>Carakācārya</i>) is enumerated among the sacrificial victims at the Puruṣamedha, or human sacrifice. His dedication there to ill-doing is a clear hint of a ritual feud.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120958"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120958">↑</a> iii. 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120958"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120958">↑</a> iii. 8, 2, 24 (where the reference is to <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā vi. 3, 9, 6; 10, 2, or some parallel passage); iv. 1, 2, 19; 2, 3, 15; 4, 1, 10; vi. 2, 2, 1. 10; viii. 1, 3, 7; 7, 1, 14. 24.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120958"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120958">↑</a> xxx. 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 16, 1. Its occurrence in the latter text renders improbable von Schroeder's view, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 188, that <a class="vei_120a" href="चरक">caraka</a> included all the Black <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> schools.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 2, 287, n. 2; 3, 256, 257, 454; <i>Indian Literature.</i> 87; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 212.</li></ol></div>

चरक-ब्राह्मण|चरकब्राह्मण|चरक|ब्राह्मण|చరక-బ్రాహ్మణ|చరకబ్రాహ్మణ|చరక|బ్రాహ్మణ|ಚರಕ-ಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ|ಚರಕಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ|ಚರಕ|ಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ|caraka-brāhmaṇa|carakabrāhmaṇa|caraka|brāhmaṇa|charaka-braahmaNa|charakabraahmaNa|charaka|braahmaNa|_vei_120@959|_vei1_pn256
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@958">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@960">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn255">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn256">1:256</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn257">↷</a><hr><p><b>Caraka-brāhmaṇa</b> is the name of a work from which Sāyaṇa quotes in his commentary on the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120959"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120959">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120959"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120959">↑</a> viii. 66, 10; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 41.</li></ol></div>

चराचर|చరాచర|ಚರಾಚರ|carācara|charaachara|_vei_120@960|_vei1_pn256
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@959">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@961">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn255">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn256">1:256</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn257">↷</a><hr><p><b>Carācara</b> (‘running about’), a term found classed with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरीसृप">sarīsṛpa</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120960"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120960">1</a></sup> must apparently denote some kind of animal.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120960"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120960">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 13, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 3; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 12, 10; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxii. 29.</li></ol></div>

चरु|చరు|ಚರು|caru|charu|_vei_120@961|_vei1_pn256
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@960">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@962">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn255">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn256">1:256</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn257">↷</a><hr><p><b>Caru</b> designates a ‘kettle’ or ‘pot’ from the Rigveda onwards.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120961"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120961">1</a></sup> It had a lid (<i>apidhāna</i>) and hooks (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अंक">aṅka</a></i>) by which it could be hung over a fire.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120961"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120961">2</a></sup> It was made of iron or bronze<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120961"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120961">3</a></sup>  (<i>ayasmaya</i>). The word is also secondarily used<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120961"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120961">4</a></sup> to denote the contents of the pot, the mess of grain which was cooked in it.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120961"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120961">↑</a> Rv. i. 7, 6; vii. 104, 2; ix. 52, 3; x. 86, 18; 167, 4; Av. iv. 7, 4; ix. 5, 6; xi. 1, 16; 3, 18; xviii. 4, 16 <i>et seq.,</i> etc. It is called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्च">pañca</a>-bild,</i> ‘with five openings,’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 1, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, v. 6; xxxii. 6; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 4, 4. 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120961"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120961">↑</a> Rv. i. 162, 13; Av. xviii. 4, 53.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120961"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120961">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 3, 4, 5</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 271.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120961"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120961">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 10, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 4, 7; ii. 5, 3, 4; iii. 2, 3, 1, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Ināische Studien,</i> 9, 216.</li></ol></div>

चर्मन्|చర్మన్|ಚರ್ಮನ್|carman|charman|_vei_120@962|_vei1_pn257
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@961">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@963">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn256">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn257">1:257</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn258">↷</a><hr><p><b>Carman,</b> denoting ‘hide’ in general, is a common expression from the Rigveda onwards.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120962">1</a></sup> The oxhide was turned to many uses, such as the manufacture of bowstrings, slings, and reins (see <b>Go</b>). It was especially often employed to place above the boards<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120962">2</a></sup> on which the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> was pressed with the stones.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120962">3</a></sup> It was possibly also used for making skin bags.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120962">4</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="चर्मण्य">carmaṇya</a> denotes leather-work generally in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120962">5</a></sup></p><p>The art of tanning hides (<i>mlā</i>) was known as early as the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120962">6</a></sup> where also the word for ‘tanner’ (<i>carmamna</i>) occurs.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120962">7</a></sup> Details of the process are lacking, but the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120962">8</a></sup> refers to stretching out a hide with pegs (<i>śañkubhiḥ</i>), and the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120962">9</a></sup> mentions the wetting of the hide.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120962">↑</a> Rv. i. 85, 5; 110, 8; 161, 7; iii. 60, 2; iv. 13, 4, etc.; Av. v. 8, 13; x. 9, 2; xi. 1, 9, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 1, 7, 1; vi. 1, 9, 2, etc. The stem <i>carma,</i> neuter (loc., <i>carme</i>), is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 2, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120962">↑</a> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 148-150; 181-183.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120962">↑</a> Rv. x. 94, 9; 116, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120962">↑</a> Rv. x. 106, 10, is so taken by Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben.</i> 228, who compares <i>Odyssey,</i> x. 19.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120962">↑</a> v. 32. <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="परिचर्मण्य">paricarmaṇya</a>,</i> Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, ii. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120962">↑</a> viii. 55, 3 (a late hymn).</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120962">↑</a> viii. 5, 38; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 13, 1. For the form, <i>cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> p. 38, n. 1; p. 249, n. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120962">↑</a> ii. 1, 1, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120962">↑</a> i. 85, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 228, 253.</li></ol></div>

चर्षणि|చర్షణి|ಚರ್ಷಣಿ|carṣaṇi|charShaNi|_vei_120@963|_vei1_pn257
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@962">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@964">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn256">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn257">1:257</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn258">↷</a><hr><p><b>Carṣaṇi,</b> used in the plural, denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120963"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120963">1</a></sup> ‘men’ in general or ‘people,’ conceived either as active beings<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120963"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120963">2</a></sup> or as cultivators<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120963"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120963">3</a></sup> in opposition to nomads. The expression ‘king of men’ (<i>rājā carṣaṇīnām</i>) is frequently found.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120963"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120963">4</a></sup> The ‘people’ are  also mentioned in connexion with war.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120963"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120963">5</a></sup> In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120963"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120963">6</a></sup> ‘animals’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पशु">paśu</a></i>) and ‘men’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="चर्षणि">carṣaṇi</a></i>) are spoken of together.</p><p>For the five <i>carṣaṇayaḥ,</i><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120963"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120963">7</a></sup> see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्च">pañca</a> Janāsaḥ.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120963"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120963">↑</a> Rv. i. 86, 5; 184, 4; iii. 43, 2; iv. 7, 4; v. 23, 1; vi. 2, 2; x. 180, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120963"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120963">↑</a> If derived from <i>car,</i> ‘move,’ which is probable.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120963"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120963">↑</a> If derived from <i>kṛṣ,</i> ‘plough or ‘till.’</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120963"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120963">↑</a> Rv. iii. 10, 1; v. 39, 4; vi. 30, 5; viii. 70, 1; x. 139, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120963"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120963">↑</a> Rv. i. 55, 1; 109, 6; iv. 31, 4; 37, 8; vi. 31, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120963"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120963">↑</a> xiii. 1, 38.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120963"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120963">↑</a> Rv. v. 86, 2; vii. 15, 2; ix. 101, 9. For the derivation, see Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> 185, and especially 122, 2<i>a</i> (from <i>car,</i> ‘move’); Monier Williams, Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> (from <i>kṛṣ,</i> ‘plough’).</li></ol></div>

चषाल|చషాల|ಚಷಾಲ|caṣāla|chaShaala|_vei_120@964|_vei1_pn258
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@963">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@965">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn257">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn258">1:258</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn259">↷</a><hr><p><b>Caṣāla,</b> the mortar-shaped top-piece of the sacrificial post (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="यूप">yūpa</a></b>), is mentioned from the Rigveda onwards.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120964"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120964">1</a></sup> In one passage of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120964"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120964">2</a></sup> it is directed to be made of wheaten dough (<i>gaudhūma</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120964"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120964">↑</a> Rv. i. 162, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 3, 4, 2, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxvi. 4, etc.; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 11, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120964"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120964">↑</a> v. 2, 1, 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 168, n. 1; 41, 31, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

चाक्र|చాక్ర|ಚಾಕ್ರ|cākra|chaakra|_vei_120@965|_vei1_pn258
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@964">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@966">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn257">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn258">1:258</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn259">↷</a><hr><p><b>Cākra</b> is the name of a man, variously styled <a class="vei_120a" href="रेवोत्तरस्">revottaras</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="स्थपति">sthapati</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पाटव">pāṭava</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="चाक्र">cākra</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120965"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120965">1</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="रेवोत्तरस्">revottaras</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पाटव">pāṭava</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="चाक्र">cākra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="स्थपति">sthapati</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120965"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120965">2</a></sup> who is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> only. He is there said to have been expelled by the <b>Sṛñjayas,</b> but to have restored to them their prince <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दुष्टरीतु">duṣṭarītu</a></b> despite the opposition of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौरव्य">kauravya</a> king <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बल्हिक प्रातिपीय">balhika prātipīya</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120965"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120965">2</a></sup> He must have been a sage rather than a warrior, as the first passage of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_120965"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_120965">1b</a></sup> shows him in the capacity of a teacher only. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्थपति">sthapati</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120965"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120965">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>. xii. 8, 1, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120965"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120965">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> xii. 9, 3, 1 <i>et seq.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 205207; 10, 85. n. 1; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 123; Eggeling. <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 269 <i>et seq.,</i> whose version is followed above.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120965"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120965">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> xii. 9, 3, 1 <i>et seq.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 205207; 10, 85, n. 1; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 123; Eggeling. <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 269 <i>et seq.,</i> whose version is followed above.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_120965"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_120965">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>. xii. 8, 1, 17.</li></ol></div>

चाक्रायण|చాక్రాయణ|ಚಾಕ್ರಾಯಣ|cākrāyaṇa|chaakraayaNa|_vei_120@966|_vei1_pn258
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@965">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@967">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn257">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn258">1:258</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn259">↷</a><hr><p><b>Cākrāyaṇa,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="चक्र">cakra</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b>Uṣasta</b> or <b>Uṣasti.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120966"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120966">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120966"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120966">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 5, 1; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 10, 1; 11, 1.</li></ol></div>

चाण्डाल|చాణ్డాల|ಚಾಣ್ಡಾಲ|cāṇḍāla|chaaNDaala|_vei_120@967|_vei1_pn258
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@966">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@968">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn257">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn258">1:258</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn259">↷</a><hr><p><b>Cāṇḍāla.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चण्डाल">caṇḍāla</a>.</b></p></div>

चाक्षुष|చాక్షుష|ಚಾಕ್ಷುಷ|cākṣuṣa|chaakShuSha|chaaxuSha|_vei_120@968|_vei1_pn258
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@967">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@969">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn257">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn258">1:258</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn259">↷</a><hr><p><b>Cākṣuṣa,</b> a word occurring once only in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120968"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120968">1</a></sup> is, according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, a patronymic (of Suyāman, a personification). Whitney<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120968"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120968">2</a></sup> treats it as probably a simple adjective (‘of sight’). </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120968"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120968">↑</a> xvi. 7, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120968"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120968">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 800.</li></ol></div>

चातुर्-मास्य|चातुर्मास्य|चातुर्|मास्य|చాతుర్-మాస్య|చాతుర్మాస్య|చాతుర్|మాస్య|ಚಾತುರ್-ಮಾಸ್ಯ|ಚಾತುರ್ಮಾಸ್ಯ|ಚಾತುರ್|ಮಾಸ್ಯ|cātur-māsya|cāturmāsya|cātur|māsya|chaatur-maasya|chaaturmaasya|chaatur|maasya|_vei_120@969|_vei1_pn259
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@968">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@970">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn258">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn259">1:259</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn260">↷</a><hr><p><b>Cātur-māsya,</b> ‘four-monthly,’ denotes the festival of the Vedic ritual held at the beginning of the three seasons of four months each, into which the Vedic year was artificially divided.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120969">1</a></sup> It is clear that the sacrifices commenced with the beginning of each season,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120969">2</a></sup> and it is certain that the first of them, the Vaiśvadeva, coincided with the Phālgunī full moon,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120969">3</a></sup> the second, the <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a>-praghāsas, with the Āṣāḍhī full moon,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120969">4</a></sup> and the third, the Sāka-<a class="vei_120a" href="मेध">medha</a>, with the Kārttikī full moon.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120969">5</a></sup> There were, however, two alternative datings: the festivals could also be held in the Caitrī, the Śrāvaṇī, and Āgrahāyaṇī (Mārgaśīrṣī) full moons,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120969">6</a></sup> or in the Vaiśākhī, Bhādrapadī, and Pauṣī full moons.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120969">7</a></sup> Neither of the later datings is found in a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> text, but each may well have been known early, since the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120969">8</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120969">9</a></sup> both recognize the full moon in the month <a class="vei_120a" href="चैत्र">caitra</a> as an alternative to the full moon in the month Phālguna, for the beginning of the year.</p><p>Jacobi considers that the commencement of the year with the full moon in the asterism <a class="vei_120a" href="फल्गुनी">phalgunī</a>, which is supported by other evidence,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120969">10</a></sup> indicates that the year at one time began with the winter solstice with the moon in <a class="vei_120a" href="फल्गुनी">phalgunī</a>, corresponding to the summer solstice when the sun was in <a class="vei_120a" href="फल्गुनी">phalgunī</a>. These astronomical conditions, he believes, existed in the time of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120969">11</a></sup> and prevailed in the fourth millennium B.C. The alternative  dates would then indicate periods when the winter solstice coincided with the Caitrī or the Vaiśākhī full moon. But Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_120969">12</a></sup> and Thibaut<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_120969">13</a></sup> seem clearly right in holding that the coincidence of Phālgunī with the beginning of spring,<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_120969">14</a></sup> which is certain, is fatal to this view, and that there is no difficulty in regarding this date as consistent with the date of the winter solstice in the new moon of Māgha, which is given by the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_120969">15</a></sup> and which forms the basis of the calculations of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्योतिष">jyotiṣa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_120969">16</a></sup> The full moon in Phālguna would be placed about one month and a half after the winter solstice, or, say, in the first week of February, which date, according to Thibaut, may reasonably be deemed to mark the beginning of a new season in India about 800 B.C. At the same time it must be remembered that the date was necessarily artificial, inasmuch as the year was divided into three seasons, each of four months, and the Indian year does not in fact consist of three equal seasons. The variations of the other datings would then not be unnatural if any school wished to defer its spring festival, the Vaiśvadeva, to the time when spring had really manifested itself. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="संवत्सर">saṃvatsara</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120969">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 5, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 9, 5; ii. 2, 2, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 3, 36; ii. 5, 2, 48; 6, 4, 1; v. 2, 3, 10; xiii. 2, 5, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120969">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 3, 36 (<i>cf.</i> xiv. 1, 1, 28); <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120969">↑</a> In the month Phālguna, or February-March.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120969">↑</a> In the month Āṣāḍhā, or JuneJuly.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120969">↑</a> That is, in the month Kārttika, when the moon is in the asterism <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्तिका">kṛttikā</a>: Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 3, 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120969">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 13, 1; 14, 1. 2; 15, 1. These are the fullmoon days in the months <a class="vei_120a" href="चैत्र">caitra</a> (March-April), Śrāvaṇa (July-August), and Mārgaśīṛṣa (November-December) respectively.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120969">↑</a> Deva's Paddhati on Kātyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, pp. 430, 450, 497. These are the full-moon days in the months Vaiśākha (April-May), Bhādrapada (August-September), and Pauṣa (December-January) respectively.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120969">↑</a> vii. 4, 8, 1. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120969">↑</a> v. 9, 8. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120969">↑</a> <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 23, 156 <i>et seq.; Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 49, 223 <i>et seq.;</i> 50, 72-81.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120969">↑</a> vii. 103, 9; x. 85, 13. <i>Cf. Festgruss an Rudolf von Roth,</i> 68 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_120969">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 630 <i>et seq.;</i> 49, 475, 476; 50, 453-457.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_120969">↑</a> <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 24, 86 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_120969">↑</a> See <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 2, 6, 8; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 1, 2-4. So the Phālgunī full moon is called ‘the mouth of the seasons (<i>ṛtūnāṃ mukham</i>)--<i>e.g.,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxi. 15, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, viii. 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 6, 9; and the first season is always spring: Śatapaṭha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 3, 8-14; ii. 1, 3, 1; vii. 2, 4, 26; xi. 2, 7, 32; xii. 8, 2, 34; xiii. 5, 4, 28; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 2, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xiii. 1. 7, etc. See Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 352.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_120969">↑</a> xix. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_120969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_120969">↑</a> Thibaut, <i>Astronomie, Astrologie, und Mathematik,</i> 17, 18.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 329 <i>et seq.;</i> Whitney, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, lxxxvi., lxxxvii.; Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1909, 1101-1104.</li></ol></div>

चान्धनायन|చాన్ధనాయన|ಚಾನ್ಧನಾಯನ|cāndhanāyana|chaandhanaayana|_vei_120@970|_vei1_pn260
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@969">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@971">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn259">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn260">1:260</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn261">↷</a><hr><p><b>Cāndhanāyana</b> is the patronymic of <b>Ānandaja</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120970"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120970">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120970"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120970">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372, 383.</li></ol></div>

चायमान|చాయమాన|ಚಾಯಮಾನ|cāyamāna|chaayamaana|_vei_120@971|_vei1_pn260
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@970">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@972">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn259">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn260">1:260</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn261">↷</a><hr><p><b>Cāyamāna</b> is the patronymic in the Rigveda (vi. 27, 5. 8) of <b>Abhyāvartin.</b> </p></div>

चाष|చాష|ಚಾಷ|cāṣa|chaaSha|_vei_120@972|_vei1_pn261
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@971">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@973">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn260">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn261">1:261</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn262">↷</a><hr><p><b>Cāṣa,</b> the ‘blue woodpecker’ (<i>Coracias indica</i>), is mentioned in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120972"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120972">1</a></sup> as well as in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or horse sacrifice, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120972"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120972">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120972"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120972">↑</a> x. 97, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120972"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120972">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 4; 15, 9; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 23; xxv. 7.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 92.</li></ol></div>

चिच्चिक|చిచ్చిక|ಚಿಚ್ಚಿಕ|ciccika|chichchika|_vei_120@973|_vei1_pn261
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@972">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@974">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn260">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn261">1:261</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn262">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ciccika</b> is a bird mentioned with the equally unknown <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वृषारव">vṛṣārava</a></b> in one hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120973"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120973">1</a></sup> It may perhaps be compared with the Ciṭaka mentioned by Dārila in his commentary on the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120973"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120973">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120973"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120973">↑</a> x. 146, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120973"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120973">↑</a> xxvi. 20; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 266.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 90; Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 2, 589.</li></ol></div>

चित्र|చిత్ర|ಚಿತ್ರ|citra|chitra|_vei_120@974|_vei1_pn261
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@973">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@975">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn260">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn261">1:261</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn262">↷</a><hr><p><b>Citra</b> is the name of several persons. (<i>a</i>) The Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120974"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120974">1</a></sup> contains a Dānastuti (‘Praise of Gifts’) of a prince <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्र">citra</a>. The later legend<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120974"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120974">2</a></sup> attributes this panegyric to <a class="vei_120a" href="सोभरि">sobhari</a>, and describes <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्र">citra</a> as king of the rats.</p><p>(<i>b</i>) <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्र">citra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गांग्यायनि">gāṅgyāyani</a> or Gārgyāyaṃ is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_120974"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_120974">1b</a></sup> as a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आरुणि">āruṇi</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्वेतकेतु">śvetaketu</a>.</b></p><p>(<i>c</i>) <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्र">citra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गौश्रायणि">gauśrāyaṇi</a> is mentioned as a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1c_vei_120974"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1c_vei_120974">1c</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120974"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120974">↑</a> viii. 21, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120974"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120974">↑</a> Bṛhaddevatā, vii. 58 <i>et. seq.,</i> with Macdonell's notes.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_120974"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_120974">↑</a> i. 1. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 395; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 16, n. 1.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1c_vei_120974"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1c_vei_120974">↑</a> xxiii. 5. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

चित्र-रथ|चित्ररथ|चित्र|रथ|చిత్ర-రథ|చిత్రరథ|చిత్ర|రథ|ಚಿತ್ರ-ರಥ|ಚಿತ್ರರಥ|ಚಿತ್ರ|ರಥ|citra-ratha|citraratha|citra|ratha|chitra-ratha|chitraratha|chitra|_vei_120@975|_vei1_pn261
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@974">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@976">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn260">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn261">1:261</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn262">↷</a><hr><p><b>Citra-ratha</b> (‘having a brilliant car’) is the name of two persons.</p><p>(<i>a</i>) It designates an Āryan prince, who, with Arṇa, was defeated by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> for the <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a>-Yadus on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरयु">sarayu</a></b> (perhaps the modern Sarju in Oudh), according to the Rigveda (iv. 30, 18). The locality would accord with the close connexion of <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रिवि">krivi</a></b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a>.</b> </p><p>(<i>b</i>) <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्ररथ">citraratha</a> is also the name of a king for whom the <b>Kāpeyas</b> performed a special kind of sacrifice (<i>dvirātra</i>), with the result, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120975"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120975">1</a></sup> that in the <a class="vei_120a" href="चैत्ररथि">caitrarathi</a> family only one member was a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्र">kṣatra</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>, the rest dependents. Apparently this must mean that the Caitrarathis were distinguished from other families of princes by the fact that the chief of the clan received a markedly higher position than in most cases, in which probably the heads of the family were rather an oligarchy than a monarch and his dependents. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120975"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120975">↑</a> xx. 12, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins. <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 52, 53; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 32; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 68, n.</li></ol></div>

चित्रा|చిత్రా|ಚಿತ್ರಾ|citrā|chitraa|_vei_120@976|_vei1_pn262
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@975">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@977">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn261">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn262">1:262</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn263">↷</a><hr><p><b>Citrā.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p></div>

चिल्वटि|చిల్వటి|ಚಿಲ್ವಟಿ|cilvaṭi|chilvaTi|_vei_120@977|_vei1_pn262
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@976">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@978">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn261">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn262">1:262</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn263">↷</a><hr><p><b>Cilvaṭi</b> is the name of an unknown animal in the Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 2, 7).</p></div>

चीपुद्रु|చీపుద్రు|ಚೀಪುದ್ರು|cīpudru|chiipudru|cheepudru|_vei_120@978|_vei1_pn262
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@977">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@979">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn261">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn262">1:262</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn263">↷</a><hr><p><b>Cīpudru</b> designates some substance mentioned in a hymn of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120978"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120978">1</a></sup> as of use in healing. The commentator Sāyaṇa reads Cīpadru, and explains the word as a kind of tree. This interpretation is supported by the fact that the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120978"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120978">2</a></sup> refers to the employment of splinters of <a class="vei_120a" href="पलाश">palāśa</a> wood in the ritual application of this hymn.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120978"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120978">3</a></sup> Whitney<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120978"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120978">4</a></sup> suggests that the form of the word should be Cīpuḍu.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120978"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120978">↑</a> vi. 127, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120978"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120978">↑</a> xxvi. 34.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120978"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120978">↑</a> Av. vi. 127.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120978"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120978">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 376.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 350-352; <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 62; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 386. The text of Roth and Whitney reads by error <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शीपुद्रु">śīpudru</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

चुमुरि|చుమురి|ಚುಮುರಿ|cumuri|chumuri|_vei_120@979|_vei1_pn262
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@978">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@980">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn261">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn262">1:262</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn263">↷</a><hr><p><b>Cumuri</b> is the name of an enemy of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दभीति">dabhīti</a>,</b> for whom he, along with his friend <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धुनि">dhuni</a>,</b> is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120979"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120979">1</a></sup> as having been defeated by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>. Elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120979"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120979">2</a></sup> the two are spoken of, along with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शम्बर">śambara</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पिप्रु">pipru</a>,</b> and Śuṣṇa, as having been crushed by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, who destroyed their castles. It is impossible to say whether real men or demons are meant, but in favour of  a man being denoted by <a class="vei_120a" href="चुमुरि">cumuri</a> is the form of the name, which seems not to be Āryan.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120979"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120979">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120979"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120979">↑</a> vi. 20, 13; x. 113, 9. In vi. 26, 6, <a class="vei_120a" href="चुमुरि">cumuri</a> alone is mentioned, and Dāsas or Dasyus generally are stated to have been subdued for <a class="vei_120a" href="दभीति">dabhīti</a> in iv. 30, 21; ii. 13, 9. See also ii. 15, 9; vii. 19, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120979"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120979">↑</a> Rv. vi. 18, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120979"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120979">↑</a> Wackernagel, <i>Altindische Grammatik,</i> 1, xxii.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 275; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology</i> p. 162.</li></ol></div>

चूड भागवित्ति|చూడ భాగవిత్తి|ಚೂಡ ಭಾಗವಿತ್ತಿ|cūḍa bhāgavitti|chuuDa bhaagavitti|chooDa bhaagavitti|_vei_120@980|_vei1_pn263
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@979">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@981">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn262">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn263">1:263</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn264">↷</a><hr><p><b>Cūḍa Bhāgavitti</b> (‘descendant of Bhagavitta’) is mentioned in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaṇiṣad<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120980">1</a></sup> as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मधुक पैंग्य">madhuka paiṅgya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120980">↑</a> vi. 3, 9 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>=vi. 3, 17. 18 Mādhyaṃdina). The text of the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> has, as usual, Cūla.</li></ol></div>

चूर्ण|చూర్ణ|ಚೂರ್ಣ|cūrṇa|chuurNa|choorNa|_vei_120@981|_vei1_pn263
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@980">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@982">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn262">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn263">1:263</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn264">↷</a><hr><p><b>Cūrṇa</b> appears to denote an aromatic powder in the phrase <i><a class="vei_120a" href="चूर्ण">cūrṇa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="हस्त">hasta</a>,</i> used of the Apsarases in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (i. 4).</p></div>

चेदि|చేది|ಚೇದಿ|cedi|chedi|_vei_120@982|_vei1_pn263
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@981">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@983">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn262">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn263">1:263</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn264">↷</a><hr><p><b>Cedi</b> is the name of a people who, with their king <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कशु">kaśu</a>,</b> the <a class="vei_120a" href="चैद्य">caidya</a>, are mentioned only in a Dānastuti (‘Praise of Gifts’), occurring at the end of one hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120982"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120982">1</a></sup> where their generosity is celebrated as unsurpassed. They occur later in the Epic with the <b>Matsyas,</b> and lived in Bandela Khaṇḍa (Bundelkhand).<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120982"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120982">2</a></sup> In Vedic times they were probably situated in much the same locality.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120982"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120982">↑</a> viii. 5, 37-39.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120982"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120982">↑</a> Lassen, <i>Indische Alterthumskunde,</i> 12, 688, n. 3; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 129; Pargiter, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 332; Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 402.</li></ol></div>

चेलक शाण्डिल्यायन|చేలక శాణ్డిల్యాయన|ಚೇಲಕ ಶಾಣ್ಡಿಲ್ಯಾಯನ|celaka śāṇḍilyāyana|chelaka shaaNDilyaayana|_vei_120@983|_vei1_pn263
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@982">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@984">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn262">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn263">1:263</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn264">↷</a><hr><p><b>Celaka Śāṇḍilyāyana</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्डिल्य">śāṇḍilya</a></b>’) is mentioned as a teacher in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (x. 4, 5, 3).</p></div>

चैकितानेय|చైకితానేయ|ಚೈಕಿತಾನೇಯ|caikitāneya|chaikitaaneya|_vei_120@984|_vei1_pn263
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@983">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@985">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn262">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn263">1:263</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn264">↷</a><hr><p><b>Caikitāneya</b> (‘descendant of Cekitāna’) is mentioned as a teacher in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120984"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120984">1</a></sup> The Caikitāneyas are also referred to there<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120984"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120984">2</a></sup> in connexion with the <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> which they worshipped. <b>Brahmadatta</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="चैकितानेय">caikitāneya</a> is brought into connexion with the <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120984"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120984">3</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वासिष्ठ">vāsiṣṭha</a></b> <a class="vei_120a" href="चैकितानेय">caikitāneya</a> is known to the Ṣaḍviṃśa<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120984"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120984">4</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120984"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120984">5</a></sup> The word is a patronymic, formed from  Caikitāna, according to Śaṅkara,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120984"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120984">6</a></sup> but more probably from Cekitāna,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120984"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120984">7</a></sup> a name found in the Epic.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120984"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120984">↑</a> i. 37, 7; ii. 5, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120984"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120984">↑</a> i. 42, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120984"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120984">↑</a> i. 3, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120984"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120984">↑</a> iv. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120984"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120984">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373, 384.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120984"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120984">↑</a> On {|Bṛhadāraṇyaka|} <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120984"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120984">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

चैकितायन|చైకితాయన|ಚೈಕಿತಾಯನ|caikitāyana|chaikitaayana|_vei_120@985|_vei1_pn264
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@984">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@986">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn263">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn264">1:264</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn265">↷</a><hr><p><b>Caikitāyana,</b> ‘descendant of Cikitāyana<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120985"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120985">1</a></sup> or Cekita,’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120985"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120985">2</a></sup> is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दाल्भ्य">dālbhya</a></b> in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120985"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120985">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120985"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120985">↑</a> Śaṅkara on Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 8, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120985"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120985">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120985"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120985">↑</a> i. 8, 1.</li></ol></div>

चैत्र|చైత్ర|ಚೈತ್ರ|caitra|chaitra|_vei_120@986|_vei1_pn264
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@985">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@987">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn263">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn264">1:264</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn265">↷</a><hr><p><b>Caitra</b> is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यज्ञसेन">yajñasena</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā (xxi. 4).</p></div>

चैत्र-रथि|चैत्ररथि|चैत्र|रथि|చైత్ర-రథి|చైత్రరథి|చైత్ర|రథి|ಚೈತ್ರ-ರಥಿ|ಚೈತ್ರರಥಿ|ಚೈತ್ರ|ರಥಿ|caitra-rathi|caitrarathi|caitra|rathi|chaitra-rathi|chaitrarathi|chaitra|_vei_120@987|_vei1_pn264
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@986">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@988">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn263">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn264">1:264</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn265">↷</a><hr><p><b>Caitra-rathi.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चित्ररथ">citraratha</a></b> and <b>Satyādhivāka.</b></p></div>

चैत्रियायण|చైత్రియాయణ|ಚೈತ್ರಿಯಾಯಣ|caitriyāyaṇa|chaitriyaayaNa|_vei_120@988|_vei1_pn264
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@987">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@989">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn263">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn264">1:264</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn265">↷</a><hr><p><b>Caitriyāyaṇa</b> is the patronymic or metronymic of the teacher <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यज्ञसेन">yajñasena</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (v. 3, 8, 1).</p></div>

चैद्य|చైద్య|ಚೈದ್ಯ|caidya|chaidya|_vei_120@989|_vei1_pn264
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@988">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@990">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn263">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn264">1:264</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn265">↷</a><hr><p><b>Caidya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चेदि">cedi</a>.</b></p></div>

चैलकि|చైలకి|ಚೈಲಕಿ|cailaki|chailaki|_vei_120@990|_vei1_pn264
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@989">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@991">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn263">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn264">1:264</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn265">↷</a><hr><p><b>Cailaki,</b> ‘descendant of <b>Celaka,</b>’ is the patronymic of Jīvala in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (ii. 3, 1, 34).</p></div>

चोर|చోర|ಚೋರ|cora|chora|_vei_120@991|_vei1_pn264
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@990">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@992">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn263">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn264">1:264</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn265">↷</a><hr><p><b>Cora,</b> ‘thief,’ is only found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, a late work, in its last book (x. 65). The Vedic terms are <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तस्कर">taskara</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तायु">tāyu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="स्तेन">stena</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परिपन्थिन्">paripanthin</a>.</b></p></div>

च्यवतान मारुताश्व|చ్యవతాన మారుతాశ్వ|ಚ್ಯವತಾನ ಮಾರುತಾಶ್ವ|cyavatāna mārutāśva|chyavataana maarutaashva|_vei_120@992|_vei1_pn264
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@991">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@993">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn263">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn264">1:264</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn265">↷</a><hr><p><b>Cyavatāna Mārutāśva</b> (‘descendant of Marutāśva’) is apparently the name of a prince in a Dānastuti (‘Praise of Gifts’), in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120992"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120992">1</a></sup> Two distinct persons may, however, be meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120992"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120992">↑</a> v. 33, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3. 155.</li></ol></div>

च्यवन|చ్యవన|ಚ್ಯವನ|cyavana|chyavana|_vei_120@993|_vei1_pn264
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@992">«</a>pada9<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@994">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn263">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn264">1:264</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn265">↷</a><hr><p><b>Cyavana,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120993">1</a></sup> <b>Cyavāna,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120993">2</a></sup> are variant forms of the name of an ancient <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>, or seer. The Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120993">3</a></sup> represents him as an old decrepit man, to whom the Aśvins restored youth and strength, making him acceptable to his wife, and a husband of maidens.  The legend is given in another form in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120993">4</a></sup> where <a class="vei_120a" href="च्यवन">cyavana</a> is described as wedding <a class="vei_120a" href="सुकन्या">sukanyā</a>, the daughter of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शर्यात">śaryāta</a>.</b> He is there called a <a class="vei_120a" href="भृगु">bhṛgu</a> or <a class="vei_120a" href="आंगिरस">āṅgirasa</a>, and is represented as having been rejuvenated by immersion in a pond--the first occurrence of a motive, later very common in Oriental literature. Another legend about Cyavāna is apparently alluded to in an obscure hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120993">5</a></sup> where he seems to be opposed to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पक्थ">paktha</a></b> prince <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तूर्वयाण">tūrvayāṇa</a>,</b> an <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> worshipper, while Cyavāna seems to have been specially connected with the Aśvins. This explanation of the hymn, suggested by Pischel,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120993">6</a></sup> is corroborated by the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120993">7</a></sup> which relates that <b>Vidanvant,</b> another son of <a class="vei_120a" href="भृगु">bhṛgu</a>, supported <a class="vei_120a" href="च्यवन">cyavana</a> against <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, who was angry with him for sacrificing to the Aśvins; it is also noteworthy that the Aśvins appear in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120993">8</a></sup> as obtaining a share in the sacrifice on the suggestion of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुकन्या">sukanyā</a>. But a reconciliation of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="च्यवन">cyavana</a> must have taken place, because the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120993">9</a></sup> relates the inauguration of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शार्यात">śāryāta</a></b> by <a class="vei_120a" href="च्यवन">cyavana</a> with the great <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> consecration (<i>aindreṇa mahābhiṣekeṇa</i>). In the Pañcaviṃśa Brāhmaṇā<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120993">10</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="च्यवन">cyavana</a> is mentioned as a seer of Sāmans or Chants.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120993">↑</a> This form is found even in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> (iv. 19), regularly in all the Vedic texts other than the Rigveda, and in the Epic.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120993">↑</a> The Rv. has this form throughout.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120993">↑</a> i. 116, 10; 117, 13; 118, 6; v. 74, 5; vii. 68, 6; 71, 5; x. 39, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120993">↑</a> iv. 1, 5, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120993">↑</a> x. 61, 1-3.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120993">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 71-77; accepted by Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 2, 465.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120993">↑</a> iii. 121-128; <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 11, cxlvi; 26, 43 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120993">↑</a> iv. 1, 5, 13 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120993">↑</a> viii. 21, 4; Pischel, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 75.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120993">↑</a> xiii. 5, 12; xix. 3, 6; xiv. 6, 10; xi. 8, 11.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 243, 250-254; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 156; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> pp. 51, 52; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 26, 43 <i>et seq.; Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 56, 57.</li></ol></div>

छग|ఛగ|ಛಗ|chaga|Chaga|_vei_120@994|_vei1_pn265
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@993">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@995">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn264">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn265">1:265</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn266">↷</a><hr><p><b>Chaga</b> is the name of the ‘goat’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (v. 6, 22, 1). <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अज">aja</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="छाग">chāga</a>.</b></p></div>

छदिस्|छदि|ఛదిస్|ఛది|ಛದಿಸ್|ಛದಿ|chadis|chadi|Chadis|Chadi|_vei_120@995|_vei1_pn265
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@994">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@996">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn264">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn265">1:265</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn266">↷</a><hr><p><b>Chadis</b> is used once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120995"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120995">1</a></sup> and not rarely later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120995"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120995">2</a></sup> to denote the covering of a wagon or the thatch of a house, or  something analogous to these. Weber<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120995"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120995">3</a></sup> thinks that in one passage of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120995"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120995">4</a></sup> the word designates a constellation, and Whitney,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120995"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120995">5</a></sup> who does not decide whether that interpretation is necessary, suggests that the constellation  Aquarii may be meant, sicne the next verse mentions <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विचृतौ">vicṛtau</a>,</b> which is the constellation  and  Scorpionis, and is not far from Aquarius. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="छर्दिस्">chardis</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120995"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120995">↑</a> x. 85, 10 (of Sūryā's bridal car).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120995"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120995">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 9, 4; 10, 5. 7; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, v. 28; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 29; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 5, 3, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120995"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120995">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 208.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120995"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120995">↑</a> iii. 7, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120995"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120995">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 95.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 336.</li></ol></div>

छन्दस्|छन्दस्|छन्द|ఛన్దస్_౧|ఛన్దస్|ఛన్ద|ಛನ್ದಸ್_೧|ಛನ್ದಸ್|ಛನ್ದ|chandas_1|chandas|chanda|Chandas_1|Chandas|Chanda|_vei_120@996|_vei1_pn266
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@995">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@997">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn265">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn266">1:266</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn267">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Chandas</b> in the Rigveda usually denotes a ‘song of praise’ or ‘hymn.’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120996">1</a></sup> The original sense of the word, as derived from the verb <i>chand,</i> ‘to please,’ was probably ‘attractive spell,’ ‘magic hymn,’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120996">2</a></sup> which prevailed on the gods. In a very late hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120996">3</a></sup> as well as in one of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120996">4</a></sup> the word is mentioned in the plural (<i>chandāṃsi</i>), beside Ṛc (<i>ṛcaḥ</i>), <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> (<i>sāmāni</i>), and <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुस्">yajus</a>, and seems to retain its original meaning, not improbably with reference to the magical subject-matter of the Atharvaveda. From denoting a (metrical) hymn it comes to mean ‘metre’ in a very late verse of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_120996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_120996">5</a></sup> in which the ‘Gāyatrī, the Triṣṭubh, and all (<i>sarvā</i>) the metres (<i>chandāṃsi</i>) are mentioned. In the later Saṃhitās three<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_120996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_120996">6</a></sup> or seven<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_120996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_120996">7</a></sup> metres are enumerated, and in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_120996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_120996">8</a></sup> eight. By the time of the Rigveda Prātiśākhya<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_120996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_120996">9</a></sup> the metres were subjected to a detailed examination, though much earlier references are found to the number of syllables in the several metres.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_120996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_120996">10</a></sup> Later the word definitely denotes a Vedic text generally, as in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_120996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_120996">11</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120996">↑</a> Rv. x. 85, 8 (an obscure verse); 114, 5; Av. iv. 34, 1; v. 26, 5; vi. 124, 1; xi. 7, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120996">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Roth in St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120996">↑</a> Rv. x. 90, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120996">↑</a> Av. xi. 7, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_120996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_120996">↑</a> x. 14, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_120996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_120996">↑</a> Av. xviii. 1, 17; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, i. 27, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_120996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_120996">↑</a> Av. viii. 9, 17. 19, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_120996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_120996">↑</a> viii. 3, 3, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_120996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_120996">↑</a> xvi. 1 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, xcv. <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_120996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_120996">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xiv. 4; Jaittirīya Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 2, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_120996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_120996">↑</a> xi. 5, 7, 3. So Gobhila <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 3, 4. 15, etc.</li></ol></div>

छन्दस्|छन्दस्|छन्द|ఛన్దస్_౨|ఛన్దస్|ఛన్ద|ಛನ್ದಸ್_೨|ಛನ್ದಸ್|ಛನ್ದ|chandas_2|chandas|chanda|Chandas_2|Chandas|Chanda|_vei_120@997|_vei1_pn267
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@996">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@998">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn266">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn267">1:267</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn268">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Chandas</b> occurs in one passage of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120997">1</a></sup> in the adjectival compound <i><a class="vei_120a" href="बृहच्-छन्दस्">bṛhac-chandas</a>,</i> which is used of a house, and must mean ‘having a large roof.’ Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120997">2</a></sup> accepts the reading as correct, but Whitney<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120997">3</a></sup> considers emendation to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="छदिस्">chadis</a></b> necessary.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120997">↑</a> iii. 12, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120997">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 345; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 150.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120997">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 105.</li></ol></div>

छन्दो-ग|छन्दोग|छन्दो|ఛన్దో-గ|ఛన్దోగ|ఛన్దో|ಛನ್ದೋ-ಗ|ಛನ್ದೋಗ|ಛನ್ದೋ|chando-ga|chandoga|chando|Chando-ga|Chandoga|Chando|_vei_120@998|_vei1_pn267
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@997">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@999">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn266">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn267">1:267</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn268">↷</a><hr><p><b>Chando-ga,</b> ‘metre-singing,’ is the term applied to reciters of the Sāmans, no doubt because these chants were sung according to their order in the Chandaārcika of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>. It is only found in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120998"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120998">1</a></sup> and often in the Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120998"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120998">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120998"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120998">↑</a> x. 5, 2, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120998"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120998">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 2; xxii. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 8, 33; xiii. 1, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Go7ttingische Gelehrte Anzeigen,</i> 1908, 720.</li></ol></div>

छर्दिस्|छर्दि|ఛర్దిస్|ఛర్ది|ಛರ್ದಿಸ್|ಛರ್ದಿ|chardis|chardi|Chardis|Chardi|_vei_120@999|_vei1_pn267
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@998">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1000">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn266">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn267">1:267</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn268">↷</a><hr><p><b>Chardis</b> occurs often in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_120999"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_120999">1</a></sup> and occasionally later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_120999"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_120999">2</a></sup> denoting a secure dwelling-place. The word appears to be incorrectly written, because the metre shows that the first syllable is always short. Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_120999"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_120999">3</a></sup> accordingly suggested that <b><a class="vei_120a" href="छदिस्">chadis</a></b> should be read instead. But <a class="vei_120a" href="छदिस्">chadis</a> means ‘roof,’ while <a class="vei_120a" href="छर्दिस्">chardis</a> never has that sense. Bartholomae<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_120999"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_120999">4</a></sup> is therefore probably right in suggesting some other form, such as Chaḍis.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_120999"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_120999">↑</a> i. 48, 15; 114, 5; vi. 15, 3; 46, 9. 12, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_120999"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_120999">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 9, 2; 3, 6, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiii. 19; xiv. 12.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_120999"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_120999">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Oldenberg, <i>Prolegomena,</i> 477.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_120999"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_120999">↑</a> <i>Studien,</i> 1, 47; 2, 58. <i>Cf.</i> Wackernagel, <i>Altindische Grammatik,</i> 1, xii., n. 2; Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 55, 312.</li></ol></div>

छाग|ఛాగ|ಛಾಗ|chāga|Chaaga|_vei_120@1000|_vei1_pn267
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@999">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1001">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn266">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn267">1:267</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn268">↷</a><hr><p><b>Chāga,</b> ‘goat,’ is found in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201000"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201000">1</a></sup> and not rarely later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201000"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201000">2</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अज">aja</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="छग">chaga</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201000"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201000">↑</a> i. 162, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201000"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201000">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 89; xxi. 40. 41; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 3, 4; v. 1, 3, 14; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 11, 2.</li></ol></div>

जगत्|జగత్|ಜಗತ್|jagat|_vei_120@1001|_vei1_pn268
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1000">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1002">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn267">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn268">1:268</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn269">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jagat,</b> ‘moving,’ is applied sometimes in the Atharvaveda, and later to the domestic animals in particular, as opposed to wild animals (<i>śvapad</i>).<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201001"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201001">1</a></sup> Occasionally the cow is mentioned separately, when the word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="जगत्">jagat</a></i> covers the rest of the domesticated animals.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201001"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201001">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201001"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201001">↑</a> Av. viii. 5, 11, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201001"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201001">↑</a> Av. i. 31, 4; x. 1, 29; xix. 47, 10; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iii. 59. In the Rv. the sense of animal in general is usually sufficient; but <i>cf. jagatī</i> in i. 157, 5; vi. 72, 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 150, n.</li></ol></div>

जंगिड|जङ्गिड|జంగిడ|జఙ్గిడ|ಜಂಗಿಡ|ಜಙ್ಗಿಡ|jaṅgiḍa|jaMgiDa|jangiDa|_vei_120@1002|_vei1_pn268
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1001">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1003">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn267">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn268">1:268</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn269">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jaṅgiḍa</b> is the name of a healing plant mentioned in the hymns of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201002">1</a></sup> It was used as an amulet against the diseases, or symptoms of disease, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तक्मन्">takman</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="बलास">balāsa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="आशरीक">āśarīka</a>, Viśarīka, <a class="vei_120a" href="पृष्ट्यामय">pṛṣṭyāmaya</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201002">2</a></sup> fevers and rheumatic pains, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विष्कन्ध">viṣkandha</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="संस्कन्ध">saṃskandha</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201002">3</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जम्भ">jambha</a>,</b> and so on. But it is also regarded as a specific against all diseases, and as the best of healing powers.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201002">4</a></sup> It is said to be produced from the juices (<i>rasa</i>) of ploughing (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कृषि">kṛṣi</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201002">5</a></sup> but this need only mean that it grew in cultivated land, not that it was itself cultivated. What plant the name designates is quite uncertain, for it disappears in the later literature. Caland<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201002">6</a></sup> takes it in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> to be the <i>Terminalia arjuneya.</i></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201002">↑</a> ii. 4; xix. 34. 35.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201002">↑</a> Av. xix. 34, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201002">↑</a> Av. ii. 4, 1; xix. 34, 1. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201002">↑</a> Av. xix. 34, 9. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201002">↑</a> Av. ii. 4, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201002">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Zauberritual,</i> 15, rendering <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 15.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 433; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 42; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 141; Grohmann, <i>ibid.,</i> 9, 417; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 65, 66, 390.</li></ol></div>

जतू|జతూ|ಜತೂ|jatū|jatuu|jatoo|_vei_120@1003|_vei1_pn268
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1002">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1004">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn267">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn268">1:268</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn269">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jatū,</b> the ‘bat,’ occurs in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201003"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201003">1</a></sup> and is mentioned as one of the victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or horse sacrifice, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201003"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201003">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201003"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201003">↑</a> ix. 2, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201003"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201003">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 6; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 25. 26.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 86.</li></ol></div>

जन|జన|ಜನ|jana|_vei_120@1004|_vei1_pn269
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1003">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1005">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn268">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn269">1:269</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn271">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jana,</b> besides meaning ‘man’ as an individual, with a tendency to the collective sense, commonly denotes a ‘people’ or ‘tribe’ in the Rigveda and later. Thus, the ‘five tribes’ (<b>Panca <a class="vei_120a" href="जनाः">janāḥ</a></b> or <b>Janāsaḥ</b>) are frequently referred to, and in one hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201004">1</a></sup> the ‘people of Yadu’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="याद्व">yādva</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a></i>) and the <b>Yadus</b> (<i>yādvāḥ</i>) are synonymous. Again, the king (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a></i>) is described as ‘protector (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="गोपा">gopā</a></i>) of the people (<i>janasya</i>),’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201004">2</a></sup> and there are other references to king and <a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201004">3</a></sup> The people of the <b>Bharatas</b> (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="भारत">bhārata</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a></i>) is also mentioned;<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201004">4</a></sup> there is no ground to assume with Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201004">5</a></sup> that <a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a> in this case means a clan or horde (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a></b>), as distinguished from a people.</p><p>It is difficult to say exactly how a people was divided. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201004">6</a></sup> argues from a passage in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201004">7</a></sup> that a people was divided into cantons (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a></b>), cantons into joint families or clans, or village communities (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वृजन">vṛjana</a></b>), and these again into single families. He thinks that the four divisions are reflected in the passage in question. by <a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="जन्मन्">janman</a>, and Putrāḥ, or sons, and argues that each village community was originally founded on relationship. But it is very doubtful whether this precise division of the people can be pressed. The division of the <a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a> into several <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> may be regarded as probable, for it is supported by the evidence of another passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201004">8</a></sup> which mentions the <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> as a unit of the fighting men, and thus shows that, as in Homeric times and in ancient Germany, relationship was deemed a good principle of military arrangement. But the subdivision of the <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> into several Grāmas is very doubtful. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201004">9</a></sup> admits that neither <a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201004">10</a></sup>  nor <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वृजन">vṛjana</a></b><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201004">11</a></sup> has the special sense of a subdivision of the <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> when used for war, for both words only denote generally an armed host. He finds other designations of the village host in <b>Vrā</b><sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201004">12</a></sup> and in <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्राज">vrāja</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201004">13</a></sup> but it is sufficient to say that the former passage is of extremely doubtful import,<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201004">14</a></sup> and that the latter has no reference to war at all. It is therefore impossible to state in what exact relation the <a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a> in Vedic times stood to the <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> or to the family (<a class="vei_120a" href="कुल">kula</a> or <a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a>). The confusion is increased by the vagueness of the sense of both <a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>. If the latter be regarded as a local division, then no doubt the <a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a> must have been a part of a district; but if a <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> was a unit of relationship, then a <a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a> may have contained families of different Viśes, or may have sometimes coincided with a <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>, or have contained only a part of a <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>. But in any case the original state of affairs must have been greatly modified by the rise of the system of caste, and the substitution of a hierarchical for a political point of view. The elements of the people were represented by the family--either as an individual family inhabiting one home (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुल">kula</a></b>), and consisting often, no doubt, of a joint family of brothers, or as a patriarchal family of sons who still lived with their father--and by the clan, the later <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a>,</b> which included all those who claimed a common ancestor. The <a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a> may be regarded as roughly corresponding to the Latin <i>gens</i> and the Greek , and possibly the <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> may be the equivalent of the <i>curia</i> and, and the <a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a> of the <i>tribus</i> and  or .<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201004">15</a></sup> These three divisions may also be seen in the <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>, Zantu, and Daqyu of the Iranian world, where the use of <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> suggests that in the Indian <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> a relationship based on blood rather than locality is meant--and perhaps even in the <i>vicus, pagus,</i> and <i>civitas</i> of the old German polity described in the Germania<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1201004">16</a></sup> of Tacitus. The family in some form appears as the third element of the <a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a> in a passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1201004">17</a></sup> where the house (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="गृह">gṛha</a></i>) is  contrasted with the <a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>. Possibly, too, another passage<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1201004">18</a></sup> contrasts the <i>adhvara,</i> or family sacrifice, with that of the <a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a> or <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>, rather than, as Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1201004">19</a></sup> thinks, the village with the two larger units. But it is significant of the particularism of the Vedic Indians that while the king maintained a fire which might be regarded as the sacred fire of the tribe, there is no sure trace<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1201004">20</a></sup> of any intermediate cult between that of the king and that of the individual householder. The real elements in the state are the <a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a>, just as ultimately the <i>gens</i> and <i>tribus,</i> the  and  are alone important. It may be that <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> sometimes represents in the older texts what later was known as the <a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a>. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>.</b></p><p>This appears clearly when the constitution of society in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> period is considered. The tribe or people still exists, and is presupposed, but the division into <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> disappears. The real division is now the separate castes (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a></b>), but the numerous sections into which each of them is divided appear to be based in part on the ancient <a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201004">↑</a> viii. 6, 46. 48.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201004">↑</a> Rv. iii. 43, 5. So <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> is called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="गोपति">gopati</a> janasya,</i> ‘protector of the people,’ Rv. ix. 35, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201004">↑</a> Rv. v. 58, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201004">↑</a> Rv. iii. 53, 12. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a>.</b></p><p><i>Cf.</i> also x. 174, 5 = Av. i. 29, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201004">↑</a> <i>Religions of India,</i> 26, 27. It is true that the Bharatas are called a <i>gavyan grāmaḥ,</i> ‘a horde eager for booty,’ in Rv. iii. 33, 11; but <a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a> has there merely a general application. Seen. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201004">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 159, 160.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201004">↑</a> ii. 26, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201004">↑</a> x. 84, 4. <i>Viśaḥ</i> may have the same sense in several other passages--iv. 24, 4; v. 61, 1; vi. 26, 1; vii. 79, 2; viii. 12, 29--but it need not necessarily bear this sense. But in x. 91, 2, there is a clear contrast between <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201004">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 161. He also relies on Rv. v. 53, 11, where the Maruts are divided into <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शर्ध">śardha</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात">vrāta</a>,</i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="गण">gaṇa</a>;</i> but these words are vague.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201004">↑</a> Rv. iii. 33. 11. See n. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201004">↑</a> Rv. vii. 32, 27; x. 42, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201004">↑</a> Rv. i. 126, 5 (<i>viśyā iva vrāḥ</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201004">↑</a> Rv. x. 179, 2=Av. vii. 72, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201004">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 121, 319.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201004">↑</a> <i>Cf. Iliad,</i> 2, 362.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1201004">↑</a> Chap. vii. Zimmer gives other equations, for which <i>cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 393 <i>et seq.</i> The exact parallelism cannot in any case be pressed.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1201004">↑</a> x. 91, 2, where <i>janaṃ <a class="vei_120a" href="जनम्">janam</a></i> and <i>viśaṃ viśam</i> occur, and where a contrast must be meant.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1201004">↑</a> Rv. vii. 82, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1201004">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 435.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1201004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1201004">↑</a> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 126.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 158; von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 32, 33; Jolly, <i>Zeitschrift der Dcutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 50, 512 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

जन शार्करक्ष्य|जन शार्करक्ष्य|జన శార్కరక్ష్య_౨|జన శార్కరక్ష్య|ಜನ ಶಾರ್ಕರಕ್ಷ್ಯ_೨|ಜನ ಶಾರ್ಕರಕ್ಷ್ಯ|jana śārkarakṣya_2|jana śārkarakṣya|jana shaarkarakShya_2|jana shaarkarakShya|jana shaarkaraxya_2|jana shaarkaraxya|_vei_120@1005|_vei1_pn271
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1004">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1006">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn269">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn271">1:271</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn273">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Jana Śārkarakṣya</b> (‘descendant of <b>Śarkarakṣa</b>’) is mentioned as a teacher in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (x. 6, 1, 1 <i>et seq.</i>) and the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (v. 11, 1; 15, 1). He was a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वपति">aśvapati</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कैकेय">kaikeya</a>,</b> and of <b>Aruṇa <a class="vei_120a" href="औपवेशि">aupaveśi</a></b> and his son <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उद्दालक आरुणि">uddālaka āruṇi</a>.</b></p></div>

जनक|జనక|ಜನಕ|janaka|_vei_120@1006|_vei1_pn271
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1005">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1007">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn269">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn271">1:271</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn273">↷</a><hr><p><b>Janaka,</b> king of <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>, plays a considerable part in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201006">1</a></sup> and the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201006">2</a></sup> as well as in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201006">3</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201006">4</a></sup> He was a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वाज">vāja</a> saneya,</b><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201006">5</a></sup> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्वेतकेतु">śvetaketu</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="आरुणेय">āruṇeya</a>,</b> and of other sages.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201006">6</a></sup> He had become famous for his generosity and his interest in the discussion of the nature of <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>, as ultimate basis of reality, in the life-time of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अजातशत्रु">ajātaśatru</a></b> of <a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a>.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201006">7</a></sup> It is significant that he maintained a close intercourse with the Brahmins of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas, such as <a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="श्वेतकेतु">śvetaketu</a>; for this indicates that the home of the philosophy of the Upaniṣads was in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a> country rather than in the east. There is a statement in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201006">8</a></sup> that he became a Brahmin (<i>brahmā</i>). This does not, however, signify a change of caste, but merely that in knowledge he became a Brahmin (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a></b>). <a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a> is occasionally mentioned in later texts: in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201006">9</a></sup> he has already become quite mythical; in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201006">10</a></sup> a <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सप्त">sapta</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="रात्र">rātra</a></i> or seven nights' rite is ascribed to him.</p><p>It is natural to attempt to date <a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a> by his being a contemporary of <a class="vei_120a" href="अजातशत्रु">ajātaśatru</a>, and by identifying the latter with the Ajātasattu of the Pāli texts:<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201006">11</a></sup> this would make the end of the sixth century B.C. the approximate date of <a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a>.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201006">12</a></sup> But it is very doubtful whether this identification can be supported: <a class="vei_120a" href="अजातशत्रु">ajātaśatru</a> was king of <a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a>, whereas Ajātasattu was king of <a class="vei_120a" href="मगध">magadha</a>, and his only connexion with <a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a> was through his marriage with the daughter of Pasenadi of <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a>.<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201006">13</a></sup> Moreover, the acceptance of this chronology would be difficult to reconcile with the history of the development of thought; for it would make the rise of Buddhism contemporaneous with the Upaniṣads, whereas it is reasonably certain that the older Upaniṣads preceded Buddhism.<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201006">14</a></sup> Nor do the Vedic texts know anything of Bimbisāra or Pasenadi, or any of the other princes famed in Buddhist records. </p><p>The identification<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201006">15</a></sup> of <a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a> of <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a> and the father of <a class="vei_120a" href="सीता">sītā</a> is less open to objection, but it cannot be proved, and is somewhat doubtful. In the Sūtras <a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a> appears as an ancient king who knew of a time when wifely honour was less respected than later.<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1201006">16</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201006">↑</a> xi. 3, 1, 2; 4, 3, 20; 6, 2, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201006">↑</a> iii. 1, 1; iv. 1, 1; 2, 1; 4, 7; v. 14, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201006">↑</a> i. 19, 2 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 23, 329); ii. 76 (<i>ibid.,</i> 15, 238).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201006">↑</a> iv. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201006">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 3, 1, 2; 4, 3, 20; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201006">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 6, 2, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201006">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201006">↑</a> xi. 6, 2, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201006">↑</a> iii. 10, 9, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201006">↑</a> xvi. 26, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201006">↑</a> Vincent Smith, <i>Early History of India,</i> 26 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201006">↑</a> Hoernle, <i>Osteology,</i> 106.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201006">↑</a> Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India,</i> 3 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201006">↑</a> See <i>e.g.,</i> von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur and Cultur,</i> 243; Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 224; Deussen, <i>Philosophy of the Upaniṣads,</i> 23 <i>et seq.;</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 25, 29.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201006">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 135; Von Schroeder, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 189; Macdonell, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 214.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1201006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1201006">↑</a> Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 48.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 426 <i>et seq.;</i> Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 421 <i>et seq.;</i> Von Schroeder, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 187-189; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 175, 231; Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 31, n., who properly emphasizes the difficulty of attaching much importance to the names of the leaders of the thought of the Upaniṣads.</li></ol></div>

जनता|జనతా|ಜನತಾ|janatā|janataa|_vei_120@1007|_vei1_pn273
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1006">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1008">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn271">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn273">1:273</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn274">↷</a><hr><p><b>Janatā,</b> a word frequently found in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201007"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201007">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201007"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201007">2</a></sup> denotes the people as a community (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a></b>) or as a religious unit.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201007"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201007">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 1, 4; 6, 4; 3, 4, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, ix. 17; Av. v. 18, 12, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201007"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201007">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 6, 1; ii. 3, 1, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7. 9; iii. 31; v. 9, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 153, n.</li></ol></div>

जन-पद|जनपद|जन|पद|జన-పద|జనపద|జన|పద|ಜನ-ಪದ|ಜನಪದ|ಜನ|ಪದ|jana-pada|janapada|jana|pada|_vei_120@1008|_vei1_pn273
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1007">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1009">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn271">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn273">1:273</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn274">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jana-pada</b> in the Brāhmaṇas denotes both the ‘people,’ as opposed to the king,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201008">1</a></sup> and the ‘land’ or ‘relam.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201008">2</a></sup> The ‘subjects’ are also denoted by the adjectival <i><a class="vei_120a" href="जानपद">jānapada</a>.</i><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201008">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201008">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 14 (plural); Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 2, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201008">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 3, 9, 9; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 1, 20; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 11, 5; viii. 1, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201008">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 5, 1, 20.</li></ol></div>

जनम्-एजय|जनम्-एजय|जनमेजय|जनम्|एजय|जनं|జనమ్-ఏజయ_౧|జనమ్-ఏజయ|జనమేజయ|జనమ్|ఏజయ|జనం|ಜನಮ್-ಏಜಯ_೧|ಜನಮ್-ಏಜಯ|ಜನಮೇಜಯ|ಜನಮ್|ಏಜಯ|ಜನಂ|janam-ejaya_1|janam-ejaya|janamejaya|janam|ejaya|_vei_120@1009|_vei1_pn273
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1008">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1010">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn271">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn273">1:273</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn274">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Janam-ejaya</b> (‘man-impelling’) is the name of a king, a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पारिक्षित">pārikṣita</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201009"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201009">1</a></sup> famous towards the end of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> period. He is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201009"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201009">2</a></sup> as owning horses which when wearied were refreshed with sweet drinks, and as a performer of the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or horse sacrifice.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201009"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201009">3</a></sup> His capital, according to a <a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a> quoted in the Śatapatha<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201009"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201009">4</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201009"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201009">5</a></sup> was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आसन्दीवन्त्">āsandīvant</a>.</b> His brothers <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उग्रसेन">ugrasena</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="भीमसेन">bhīmasena</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्रुतसेन">śrutasena</a></b> are mentioned as having  by the horse sacrifice purified themselves from sin. The priest who performed the sacrifice for him was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्रोत">indrota</a> Daivāpi <a class="vei_120a" href="शौनक">śaunaka</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201009"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201009">6</a></sup> On the other hand the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201009"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201009">7</a></sup> which also mentions his <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, names <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर कावषेय">tura kāvaṣeya</a></b> as his priest. It also contains an obscure tale stating that at one sacrifice of his he did not employ the <b>Kaśyapas,</b> but the <b>Bhūtavīras,</b> being, however, induced by the <b>Asitamṛgas</b> to have recourse to the Kaśyapas again.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201009"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201009">8</a></sup> He was a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a></b> prince; see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परिक्षित्">parikṣit</a>.</b> The Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201009"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201009">9</a></sup> tells an absurd tale about him, evidently as of an ancient hero.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201009"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201009">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 4, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 34; viii. 11. 21; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 8, 27, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201009"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201009">↑</a> xi. 5, 5, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201009"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201009">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 1-3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201009"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201009">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201009"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201009">↑</a> viii. 21.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201009"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201009">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 4, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, <i>loe. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201009"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201009">↑</a> viii. 21. <i>Cf.</i> iv. 27; vii. 34.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201009"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201009">↑</a> vii. 27. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 204; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 438, n. 229; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 43, 345, n.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201009"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201009">↑</a> i. 2, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 123125; 134-136; Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 37, 65 <i>et seq.;</i> 42, 239; Pargiter, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1910, 28 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

जनम्-एजय|जनम्-एजय|जनमेजय|जनम्|एजय|जनं|జనమ్-ఏజయ_౨|జనమ్-ఏజయ|జనమేజయ|జనమ్|ఏజయ|జనం|ಜನಮ್-ಏಜಯ_೨|ಜನಮ್-ಏಜಯ|ಜನಮೇಜಯ|ಜನಮ್|ಏಜಯ|ಜನಂ|janam-ejaya_2|janam-ejaya|janamejaya|janam|ejaya|_vei_120@1010|_vei1_pn274
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1009">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1011">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn273">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn274">1:274</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn275">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Janam-ejaya</b> is in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201010"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201010">1</a></sup> the name of a priest who officiated at the snake sacrifice.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201010"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201010">↑</a> xxv. 15, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 35.</li></ol></div>

जन-श्रुत|जनश्रुत|जन|श्रुत|జన-శ్రుత|జనశ్రుత|జన|శ్రుత|ಜನ-ಶ್ರುತ|ಜನಶ್ರುತ|ಜನ|ಶ್ರುತ|jana-śruta|janaśruta|jana|śruta|jana-shruta|janashruta|shruta|_vei_120@1011|_vei1_pn274
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1010">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1012">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn273">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn274">1:274</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn275">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jana-śruta</b> (‘famed among men’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काण्ड्विय">kāṇḍviya</a></b> is the name of a pupil of <b>Hṛtsvāśaya,</b> mentioned in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 40, 2), and of <a class="vei_120a" href="वारक्य">vārakya</a>, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जयन्त">jayanta</a>,</b> referred to in the same <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 41, 1; iv. 17, 1). <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जानश्रुति">jānaśruti</a>.</b></p></div>

जनि|जनी|జని|జనీ|ಜನಿ|ಜನೀ|jani|janī|janii|janee|_vei_120@1012|_vei1_pn274
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1011">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1013">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn273">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn274">1:274</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn275">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jani, Janī.</b>--These words appear to denote ‘wife,’ usually applying to her in relation to her husband (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a></b>). The more general sense of ‘woman’ is doubtful; for when Uṣas is called a fair <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनी">janī</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201012"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201012">1</a></sup> ‘wife’ may be meant, and the other passage<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201012"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201012">2</a></sup> cited for this sense by Delbrück,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201012"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201012">3</a></sup> which refers to the begetting of childern, seems to demand the sense of ‘wives.’ Since the words usually appear in the plural,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201012"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201012">4</a></sup> it is possible they may  refer not to ‘wives’ proper, but to Hetairai. This is, however, rendered unlikely because the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201012"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201012">5</a></sup> uses the phrase <i>patyur janitvam,</i> denoting ‘wifehood to a husband,’ as well as the expression <i>janayo na patnīḥ,</i><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201012"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201012">6</a></sup> ‘like wives (who are) mistresses,’<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201012"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201012">7</a></sup> besides containing passages in which the word has reference to marriage.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201012"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201012">8</a></sup> The singular occurs in the dialogue of <a class="vei_120a" href="यम">yama</a> and Yamī.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201012"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201012">9</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201012"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201012">↑</a> Rv. iv. 52, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201012"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201012">↑</a> v. 61, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201012"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201012">↑</a> <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandschaftsnamen,</i> 413.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201012"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201012">↑</a> i. 85, 1; iv. 5, 5; 19, 5; vii. 18, 2; 26, 3; ix. 86, 32; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xii. 35; xx. 40. 43, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Rv. x. 43, 1. In x. 110, 5, the phrase is <i>patibhyo na janayaḥ,</i> where both plurals may be generic</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201012"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201012">↑</a> x. 18, 8. <i>Cf. janitvana</i> in viii. 2, 42.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201012"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201012">↑</a> i. 62, 10; 186, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201012"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201012">↑</a> The distinction of sense was probably this: <i><a class="vei_120a" href="जनि">jani</a></i> meant ‘wife,’ as bearing children (from <i>jan,</i> ‘beget’), while <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पत्नी">patnī</a></i> was ‘wife,’ as being mistress of the house (feminine of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>,</i> ‘lord,’ ‘husband’).</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201012"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201012">↑</a> v. 61, 3. So in x. 40, 10, the word seems certainly to refer to marriage.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201012"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201012">↑</a> x. 10, 3.</li></ol></div>

जनितृ|జనితృ|ಜನಿತೃ|janitṛ|janitR|janitRi|janitRu|janitRRi|_vei_120@1013|_vei1_pn275
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1012">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1014">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn274">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn275">1:275</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn276">↷</a><hr><p><b>Janitṛ</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201013">1</a></sup> and <b>Janitrī</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201013">2</a></sup> are frequent words, in the Rigveda and later, for ‘father’ and ‘mother’ regarded as the ‘begetter’ and the ‘bearer’ respectively of the child. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पितृ">pitṛ</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मातृ">mātṛ</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201013">↑</a> Rv. i. 129, 11; 164, 33; iii. 1, 10; 54, 9, etc.; Av. iv. 1, 7; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 87, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201013">↑</a> Rv. iii. 48, 2; 54, 14; Av. vi. 110, 13; ix. 5, 30, etc.</li></ol></div>

जन्तु|జన్తు|ಜನ್ತು|jantu|_vei_120@1014|_vei1_pn275
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1013">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1015">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn274">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn275">1:275</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn276">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jantu,</b> besides the general sense of ‘man,’ has also in a few passages<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201014"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201014">1</a></sup> the more restricted sense of ‘follower’ or ‘subject.’ The ‘followers of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्वैत्रेय">śvaitreya</a></b>’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201014"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201014">2</a></sup> may be compared with the ‘subjects (<i>viśaḥ</i>) of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तृणस्कन्द">tṛṇaskanda</a>.</b>’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201014"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201014">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201014"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201014">↑</a> Rv. 1. 94, 5; x. 140, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201014"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201014">↑</a> Rv. v. 19, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201014"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201014">↑</a> Rv. i. 172, 3.</li></ol></div>

जन्मन्|జన్మన్|ಜನ್ಮನ್|janman|_vei_120@1015|_vei1_pn275
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1014">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1016">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn274">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn275">1:275</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn276">↷</a><hr><p><b>Janman</b> appears to have the sense of ‘relations’ in two passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201015"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201015">1</a></sup> being used collectively in the second of them.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201015"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201015">↑</a> iii. 15, 2; ii. 26, 3 (where <i>janena, viśā, janmanā, putraiḥ,</i> is the series).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 160, and see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

जन्य|జన్య|ಜನ್ಯ|janya|_vei_120@1016|_vei1_pn275
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1015">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1017">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn274">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn275">1:275</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn276">↷</a><hr><p><b>Janya</b> has in the Rigveda (iv. 38, 6) and the Atharvaveda (xi. 8, 1) the special sense of bridesman.’</p></div>

जबाला|జబాలా|ಜಬಾಲಾ|jabālā|jabaalaa|_vei_120@1017|_vei1_pn275
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1016">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1018">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn274">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn275">1:275</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn276">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jabālā</b> is the name of the mother of an illegitimate son, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सत्यकाम">satyakāma</a>,</b> in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iv. 4, 1. 2. 4). </p></div>

जभ्य|జభ్య|ಜಭ್ಯ|jabhya|_vei_120@1018|_vei1_pn276
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1017">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1019">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn275">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn276">1:276</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn277">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jabhya,</b> ‘snapper,’ denotes in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201018"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201018">1</a></sup> an insect destructive to grain.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201018"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201018">↑</a> vi. 50, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 237.</li></ol></div>

जमद्-अग्नि|जमदग्नि|जमद्|अग्नि|జమద్-అగ్ని|జమదగ్ని|జమద్|అగ్ని|ಜಮದ್-ಅಗ್ನಿ|ಜಮದಗ್ನಿ|ಜಮದ್|ಅಗ್ನಿ|jamad-agni|jamadagni|jamad|agni|_vei_120@1019|_vei1_pn276
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1018">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1020">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn275">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn276">1:276</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn277">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jamad-agni</b> is one of the somewhat mythical sages of the Rigveda, where he is frequently mentioned. In some passages<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201019">1</a></sup> his name occurs in such a way as to indicate that he is the author of the hymn; once<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201019">2</a></sup> he is thus associated with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>.</b> In other passages<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201019">3</a></sup> he is merely referred to, and the Jamadagnis are mentioned once.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201019">4</a></sup> In the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201019">5</a></sup> as well as the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201019">6</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201019">7</a></sup> he is quite a frequent figure. Here he appears as a friend of <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201019">8</a></sup> and a rival of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201019">9</a></sup> He owed his prosperity to his <i>catūrātra,</i> or ‘four-night’ ritual, with which his family were also very successful.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201019">10</a></sup> In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201019">11</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="जमदग्नि">jamadagni</a> is connected with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अत्रि">atri</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a>,</b> as well as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="असित">asita</a></b> and <b>Vitahavya.</b> He was <a class="vei_120a" href="अध्वर्यु">adhvaryu</a> priest at the proposed sacrifice of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201019">12</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201019">↑</a> Rv. iii. 62, 18; viii. 101, 8; ix. 62, 24; 65, 25.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201019">↑</a> Rv. x. 167, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201019">↑</a> Rv. vii. 96, 3; ix. 97, 51.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201019">↑</a> Rv. iii. 53, 15. 16.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201019">↑</a> ii. 32, 3 (<i>cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, iv. 36; <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 1); iv. 29, 3; v. 28, 7; vi. 137, 1; xviii. 3, 15. 16.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201019">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 12, 4; iii. 1, 7, 3; 3, 5, 2; v. 2, 10, 5; 4, 11, 3; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 7, 19; iv. 2, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvi. 19; xx. 9; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iii. 62; xiii. 56.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201019">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 4, 14; xiii. 5, 15; xxi. 10, 5-7; xxii. 7, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 16; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 2, 2, 14; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 9, 7; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 2, 4; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 11; iv. 3, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201019">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 1, 7, 3; v. 4, 11, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201019">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201019">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxi. 10, 5-7.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201019">↑</a> ii. 32, 3; vi. 137, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201019">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 16.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connscticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 53, 54; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 319; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 95.</li></ol></div>

जम्भ|जंभ|జమ్భ|జంభ|ಜಮ್ಭ|ಜಂಭ|jambha|_vei_120@1020|_vei1_pn276
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1019">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1021">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn275">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn276">1:276</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn277">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jambha</b> occurs twice in the Atharvaveda as the name of a disease or a demon of disease. In one passage<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201020">1</a></sup> it is said to be cured by the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जंगिड">jaṅgiḍa</a></b> plant; in the other<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201020">2</a></sup> it is described as <i>samhanuḥ,</i> ‘bringing the jaws together.’ Weber<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201020">3</a></sup> argued from  the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201020">4</a></sup> that it was a child's ailment, especially ‘teething.’ Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201020">5</a></sup> considers it to mean ‘convulsions,’ while Caland<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201020">6</a></sup> thinks it denotes ‘tetanus.’ Whitney<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201020">7</a></sup> decides for ‘lockjaw’ or ‘convulsions.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201020">↑</a> ii. 4, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201020">↑</a> viii. 1, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201020">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 142.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201020">↑</a> xxxii. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201020">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 283.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201020">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgen ländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 53, 224; <i>Altindisches Zauberritual,</i> 103.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201020">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 42.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 392.</li></ol></div>

जम्भक|जंभक|జమ్భక|జంభక|ಜಮ್ಭಕ|ಜಂಭಕ|jambhaka|_vei_120@1021|_vei1_pn277
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1020">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1022">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn276">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn277">1:277</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn278">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jambhaka,</b> as the name of a demon, presumably identical with the demon causing <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जम्भ">jambha</a>,</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वाज">vāja</a> saneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201021"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201021">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201021"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201021">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201021"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201021">↑</a> xxx. 16.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201021"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201021">↑</a> xii. 25. <i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āranyaka,</i> 67, n. 7.</li></ol></div>

जयक लौहित्य|జయక లౌహిత్య|ಜಯಕ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|jayaka lauhitya|_vei_120@1022|_vei1_pn277
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1021">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1023">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn276">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn277">1:277</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn278">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jayaka Lauhitya</b> (‘descendant of Lohita’) is mentioned in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 42, 1) as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यशस्विन् जयन्त लौहित्य">yaśasvin <a class="vei_120a" href="जयन्त">jayanta</a> lauhitya</a>.</b></p></div>

जयन्त|జయన్త|ಜಯನ್ತ|jayanta|_vei_120@1023|_vei1_pn277
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1022">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1024">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn276">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn277">1:277</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn278">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jayanta</b> is the name of several teachers in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>:</p><p>(<i>a</i>) <a class="vei_120a" href="जयन्त">jayanta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पाराशर्य">pārāśarya</a> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पराशर">parāśara</a></b>’) is mentioned as a pupil of <b>Vipaścit</b> in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers).<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201023">1</a></sup></p><p>(<i>b</i>) <a class="vei_120a" href="जयन्त">jayanta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वारक्य">vārakya</a> (‘descendant of Varaka’) appears in the same <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a><sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1201023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1201023">1b</a></sup> as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुबेर वारक्य">kubera vārakya</a>.</b> His grandtather is also mentioned there as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कंस">kaṃsa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वारक्य">vārakya</a>.</b></p><p>(<i>c</i>) A <a class="vei_120a" href="जयन्त">jayanta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वारक्य">vārakya</a>, pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुयज्ञ शाण्डिल्य">suyajña śāṇḍilya</a>,</b> perhaps identical with the preceding, is found in another <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2b_vei_1201023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2b_vei_1201023">2b</a></sup></p><p>(<i>d</i>) <a class="vei_120a" href="जयन्त">jayanta</a> is a name of <b>Yaśasvin <a class="vei_120a" href="लौहित्य">lauhitya</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_3b_vei_1201023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3b_vei_1201023">3b</a></sup></p><p>See also <b>Dakṣa <a class="vei_120a" href="जयन्त">jayanta</a></b> <a class="vei_120a" href="लौहित्य">lauhitya</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201023">↑</a> iii. 41, 1.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1201023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1201023">↑</a> iii. 41, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2b_vei_1201023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2b_vei_1201023">↑</a> iv. 17, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3b_vei_1201023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3b_vei_1201023">↑</a> iii. 42, 1. Not only is the formation of the name a late one (<i>cf.</i> Whitney, <i>Sanskrit Grammar,</i> 1209d, and Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar</i> 191<i>a</i>), but the <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> in which it occurs is also a late one.</li></ol></div>

जरा-बोध|जराबोध|जरा|बोध|జరా-బోధ|జరాబోధ|జరా|బోధ|ಜರಾ-ಬೋಧ|ಜರಾಬೋಧ|ಜರಾ|ಬೋಧ|jarā-bodha|jarābodha|jarā|bodha|jaraa-bodha|jaraabodha|jaraa|_vei_120@1024|_vei1_pn277
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1023">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1025">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn276">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn277">1:277</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn278">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jarā-bodha,</b> a word occurring only once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201024"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201024">1</a></sup> is of doubtful meaning. It is held by Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201024"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201024">2</a></sup> to be the name of  a seer. Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201024"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201024">3</a></sup> regards it as a mere adjective meaning ‘attending to the invocation,’ which is perhaps the most probable interpretation. Oldenburg,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201024"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201024">4</a></sup> however, thinks that the word is a proper name, the literal sense being ‘alert in old age.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201024"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201024">↑</a> i. 27, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201024"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201024">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 103.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201024"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201024">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, x. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201024"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201024">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 23. He compares the <i>ṛṣī <a class="vei_120a" href="बोध">bodha</a>-pratibodhau</i> of Av. v. 30, 10.</li></ol></div>

जरायु|జరాయు|ಜರಾಯು|jarāyu|jaraayu|_vei_120@1025|_vei1_pn278
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1024">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1026">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn277">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn278">1:278</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn279">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jarāyu</b> is found once in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201025"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201025">1</a></sup> in the sense of a ‘serpent's skin.’ Usually<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201025"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201025">2</a></sup> it denotes the outer covering (chorion) of the embryo, as opposed to the <i>ulva,</i> the inner covering (amnion).</p><p>Living things are occasionally classified according to their mode of origin. In the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201025"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201025">3</a></sup> they are divided into <i>(a) āṇḍa-ja,</i> ‘egg-born’; <i>(b) <a class="vei_120a" href="जीव-ज">jīva-ja</a>,</i> ‘born alive,’ or born from the womb; <i>(c) udbhij-ja,</i> ‘propagated by sprouts.’ In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201025"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201025">4</a></sup> the division is fourfold: <i>(a) āṇḍa-ja; (b) <a class="vei_120a" href="जारु">jāru</a>-ja,</i> that is, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="जरायु">jarāyu</a>-ja</i> (found in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201025"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201025">5</a></sup> and needlessly read here by Bo7htlingk);<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201025"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201025">6</a></sup> <i>(c) udbhij-ja;</i> and <i>(d) svcda-ja,</i> ‘sweat-born,’ explained as ‘insects.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201025"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201025">↑</a> i. 27, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201025"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201025">↑</a> Rv. v. 78, 8; Av. i. 11, 4; vi. 49, 1; ix. 4, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 5, 6, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x. 8; xix. 76; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 1, 11, etc; Chāndogya Upanisad, iii. 10. 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201025"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201025">↑</a> vi. 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201025"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201025">↑</a> ii. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201025"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201025">↑</a> i. 12, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201025"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201025">↑</a> See <i><a class="vei_120a" href="जारु">jāru</a></i> in Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 430, 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Deussen, <i>Philosophy of the Upanishads,</i> 196, 292; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 235.</li></ol></div>

जरितृ|जरितृ|జరితృ_౧|జరితృ|ಜರಿತೃ_೧|ಜರಿತೃ|jaritṛ_1|jaritṛ|jaritR_1|jaritR|jaritRi_1|jaritRi|jaritRu_1|jaritRu|jaritRRi_1|jaritRRi|_vei_120@1026|_vei1_pn278
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1025">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1027">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn277">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn278">1:278</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn279">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Jaritṛ</b> is the regular term in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201026"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201026">1</a></sup> and occasionally later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201026"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201026">2</a></sup> for a singer of hymns of praise or worshipper.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201026"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201026">↑</a> i. 2, 2; 165, 14; ii. 33, 11; iii. 60, 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201026"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201026">↑</a> Av. v. 11, 8; xx. 135, 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

जरितृ|जरितृ|జరితృ_౨|జరితృ|ಜರಿತೃ_೨|ಜರಿತೃ|jaritṛ_2|jaritṛ|jaritR_2|jaritR|jaritRi_2|jaritRi|jaritRu_2|jaritRu|jaritRRi_2|jaritRRi|_vei_120@1027|_vei1_pn278
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1026">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1028">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn277">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn278">1:278</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn279">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Jaritṛ.</b> According to Sieg,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201027">1</a></sup> mention is made in one hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201027">2</a></sup> of <a class="vei_120a" href="जरितृ">jaritṛ</a>, one of the Śārṅgas. That hymn he seeks to bring into connexion with the epic<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201027">3</a></sup> tradition  of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> Mandapāla, who wedded Jaritā, a female <a class="vei_120a" href="शार्ंग">śārṅga</a> bird--apparently a hen sparrow (<i>caṭakā</i>)--and had four sons. These being abandoned by him and exposed to the danger of being consumed by a forest fire, prayed to <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> with the hymn Rigveda x. 142. This interpretation is very doubtful, though Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201027">4</a></sup> appears to have adopted it.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201027">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 44 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201027">↑</a> x. 142.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201027">↑</a> Mahābhārata, i. 222, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201027">↑</a> On Rv. x. 142, 7. 8.</li></ol></div>

जरूथ|జరూథ|ಜರೂಥ|jarūtha|jaruutha|jarootha|_vei_120@1028|_vei1_pn279
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1027">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1029">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn278">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn279">1:279</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn280">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jarūtha,</b> mentioned in three passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201028">1</a></sup> appears to denote a <b>demon</b> defeated by <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201028">2</a></sup> Ludwig, however, followed by Griffith,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201028">3</a></sup> sees in him a foe slain in a battle in which <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>,</b> the traditional author of the seventh Maṇḍala of the Rigveda, was <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, or domestic priest.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201028">↑</a> vii. 1, 7; 9, 6; x. 80. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201028">↑</a> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201028">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 2, 11, n.</li></ol></div>

जर्तिल|జర్తిల|ಜರ್ತಿಲ|jartila|_vei_120@1029|_vei1_pn279
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1028">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1030">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn278">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn279">1:279</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn280">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jartila,</b> ‘wild sesamum,’ is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (v. 4, 3, 2) as an unsuitable sacrificial offering. In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (ix. 1, 1, 3) sesamum seeds are regarded as combining the qualities of cultivation (viz., edibility) with those of wild growth (because they are produced on unploughed land).</p></div>

जर्वर|జర్వర|ಜರ್ವರ|jarvara|_vei_120@1030|_vei1_pn279
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1029">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1031">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn278">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn279">1:279</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn280">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jarvara</b> was Gṛhapati or ‘householder’ at the snake festival described in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201030"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201030">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201030"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201030">↑</a> xxv. 15, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 35.</li></ol></div>

जल जातूकर्ण्य|జల జాతూకర్ణ్య|ಜಲ ಜಾತೂಕರ್ಣ್ಯ|jala jātūkarṇya|jala jaatuukarNya|jala jaatookarNya|_vei_120@1031|_vei1_pn279
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1030">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1032">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn278">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn279">1:279</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn280">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jala Jātūkarṇya</b> (‘descendant of Jātūkarṇa’), is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (xvi. 29, 6) as having obtained the position of <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, or domestic priest, of the three peoples or kings of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a>.</b></p></div>

जलाष-भेषज|जलाषभेषज|जलाष|भेषज|జలాష-భేషజ|జలాషభేషజ|జలాష|భేషజ|ಜಲಾಷ-ಭೇಷಜ|ಜಲಾಷಭೇಷಜ|ಜಲಾಷ|ಭೇಷಜ|jalāṣa-bheṣaja|jalāṣabheṣaja|jalāṣa|bheṣaja|jalaaSha-bheShaja|jalaaShabheShaja|jalaaSha|bheShaja|_vei_120@1032|_vei1_pn279
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1031">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1033">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn278">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn279">1:279</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn280">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jalāṣa-bheṣaja,</b> ‘whose remedy is <a class="vei_120a" href="जलाष">jalāṣa</a>,’ is an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="रुद्र">rudra</a> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201032"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201032">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201032"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201032">2</a></sup> The word  <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जालाष">jālāṣa</a></b> occurs in a hymn of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201032"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201032">3</a></sup> where it denotes a remedy, perhaps, for a tumour or boil.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201032"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201032">4</a></sup> The commentator on this passage and the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201032"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201032">5</a></sup> regard <a class="vei_120a" href="जालाष">jālāṣa</a> as meaning ‘urine,’ which seems a probable interpretation.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201032"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201032">6</a></sup> But Geldner<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201032"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201032">7</a></sup> thinks that rain-water, conceived as urine, is meant; and the Naighaṇṭuka<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201032"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201032">8</a></sup> identifies <i><a class="vei_120a" href="जलाष">jalāṣa</a></i> and <i>udaka</i> ‘water.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201032"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201032">↑</a> i. 43, 4; viii. 29, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201032"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201032">↑</a> ii. 27, 6. It also occurs in the Nīlarudra <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (a very late work), 3, and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="जलाष">jalāṣa</a></i> as an adjective is found in Rv. ii. 33, 7; viii. 33, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201032"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201032">↑</a> vi. 57.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201032"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201032">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 11, 321 <i>et seq.;</i> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 489.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201032"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201032">↑</a> xxxi. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201032"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201032">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 12, 425 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201032"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201032">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 139, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201032"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201032">↑</a> i. 12.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 323, 324; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> pp. 76, 77; Hopkins, <i>Proceedings of the American Oriental Society,</i> 1894, cl.</li></ol></div>

जष|జష|ಜಷ|jaṣa|jaSha|_vei_120@1033|_vei1_pn280
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1032">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1034">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn279">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn280">1:280</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn281">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jaṣa</b> is the name of some aquatic animal or fish in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201033">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201033">2</a></sup> The commentary on the latter text explains it by <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मकर">makara</a>,</i> probably meaning ‘dolphin.’ The word also occurs in the Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201033">3</a></sup></p><p><i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="झष">jhaṣa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201033">↑</a> xi. 2, 25. There are various readings: <i><a class="vei_120a" href="झष">jhaṣa</a>, jakha, jagha.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201033">↑</a> v. 5, 13, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201033">↑</a> ii. 2, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 96; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 624</li></ol></div>

जहका|జహకా|ಜಹಕಾ|jahakā|jahakaa|_vei_120@1034|_vei1_pn280
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1033">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1035">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn279">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn280">1:280</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn281">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jahakā,</b> the ‘polecat,’ is mentioned as a victim at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or horse sacrifice, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201034">1</a></sup> Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201034">2</a></sup> thinks it means a jackal living in holes (<i>vila-vāsī kroṣṭā</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201034">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 18, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 17; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 36. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 86.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201034">↑</a> On <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

जह्नु|జహ్ను|ಜಹ್ನು|jahnu|_vei_120@1035|_vei1_pn280
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1034">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1036">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn279">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn280">1:280</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn281">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jahnu</b> occurs only in the plural in the legend of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a>,</b> who is said to have obtained, as <a class="vei_120a" href="देवरात">devarāta</a>, both the lordship of the Jahnus and the divine lore of the Gāthins.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201035"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201035">1</a></sup> A <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जाह्नव">jāhnava</a>,</b> or descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="जह्नु">jahnu</a>, was, according to the Pañcaviṃśa  <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201035"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201035">2</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>,</b> who is said, by means of a certain <i>catū-<a class="vei_120a" href="रात्र">rātra</a></i> or ‘four-night’ ritual, to have secured the kingdom for the Jahnus in their conflict with the <b>Vṛcīvants.</b> He is here described as a king. Again, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201035"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201035">3</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> is addressed as a <i><a class="vei_120a" href="राज-पुत्र">rāja-putra</a>,</i> ‘prince,’ and <i>Bharatarṣabha,</i> ‘bull of the Bharatas.’ It is therefore clear that the Brāhmaṇas, though not the Saṃhitās, saw in him at once a priest and a prince by origin, though there is no trace whatever of their seeing in him a prince who won Brahmanhood as in the version of the later texts.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201035"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201035">4</a></sup></p><p>A Jahnāvī is mentioned twice in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201035"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201035">5</a></sup> being either the wife of <a class="vei_120a" href="जह्नु">jahnu</a>, or, as Sāyaṇa thinks, the race of <a class="vei_120a" href="जह्नु">jahnu</a>. The family must clearly once have been a great one, later merged in the Bharatas.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201035"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201035">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 18 (<i>Jahnūnām cādhipatye daive vede ca Gāthinām</i>); Asvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 14; Sāṅkhāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 27 (p. 195, 1. 21, ed. Hillebrandt, where the reading is different and the sense altered: <i>Jahnūnāṃ cādhitasthire daive vede ca Gāthinaḥ.</i> The two <i>ca</i>'s cannot be justified, and the text must be incorrect).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201035"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201035">↑</a> xxi. 12. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 54, who correctly explains the passage which Sāyaṇa misunderstands.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201035"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201035">↑</a> vii. 17, 6, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201035"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201035">↑</a> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1, 337 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201035"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201035">↑</a> i. 116, 19; iii. 58, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 153.</li></ol></div>

जात शाकायन्य|జాత శాకాయన్య|ಜಾತ ಶಾಕಾಯನ್ಯ|jāta śākāyanya|jaata shaakaayanya|_vei_120@1036|_vei1_pn281
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1035">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1037">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn280">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn281">1:281</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn282">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jāta Śākāyanya</b> (‘descendant of Śāka’) is mentioned as a ritual authority and contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शंख">śaṅkha</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā (xxii. 7).</p></div>

जात-रूप|जातरूप|जात|रूप|జాత-రూప|జాతరూప|జాత|రూప|ಜಾತ-ರೂಪ|ಜಾತರೂಪ|ಜಾತ|ರೂಪ|jāta-rūpa|jātarūpa|jāta|rūpa|jaata-ruupa|jaataruupa|jaata|ruupa|jaata-roopa|jaataroopa|roopa|_vei_120@1037|_vei1_pn281
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1036">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1038">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn280">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn281">1:281</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn282">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jāta-rūpa,</b> ‘possessing native beauty,’ is the name of ‘gold’ in the later Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201037"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201037">1</a></sup> and the Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201037"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201037">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201037"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201037">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 13 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="जातरूप">jātarūpa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="मय">maya</a>,</i> ‘composed of gold’); Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 25; Naighaṇṭuka, i. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201037"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201037">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="रजत">rajata</a>-jātarūpe,</i> ‘silver and gold,’ Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 6, 24. <i>Cf.</i> viii. 1, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 16; xiii. 3, etc.; Sāṅkhāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 19, 9.</li></ol></div>

जाति|జాతి|ಜಾತಿ|jāti|jaati|_vei_120@1038|_vei1_pn281
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1037">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1039">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn280">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn281">1:281</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn282">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jāti,</b> which in the Pāli<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201038">1</a></sup> texts is the word denoting ‘caste,’ does not occur at all in the early Vedic literature; when it is found, as in the Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201038">2</a></sup> it has only the sense of ‘family’ (for which <i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुल">kula</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a></b>). For the influence of the family system on the growth of caste, see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>.</b> To assume that it was the basis of caste, as does  Senart,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201038">3</a></sup> is difficult in face of the late appearance of words for family and of stress on family.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201038">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201038">↑</a> Fick, <i>Die sociale Gliederung,</i> 22, n. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201038">↑</a> xv. 4, 14. So <i>jātīya,</i> xx. 2, 11, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201038">↑</a> <i>Les Castes dans l'Inde</i> (1896).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201038">↑</a> Fick, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 3; Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 51, 267 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

जातू-कर्ण्य|जातूकर्ण्य|जातू|कर्ण्य|జాతూ-కర్ణ్య|జాతూకర్ణ్య|జాతూ|కర్ణ్య|ಜಾತೂ-ಕರ್ಣ್ಯ|ಜಾತೂಕರ್ಣ್ಯ|ಜಾತೂ|ಕರ್ಣ್ಯ|jātū-karṇya|jātūkarṇya|jātū|karṇya|jaatuu-karNya|jaatuukarNya|jaatuu|karNya|jaatoo-karNya|jaatookarNya|jaatoo|_vei_120@1039|_vei1_pn282
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1038">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1040">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn281">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn282">1:282</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn283">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jātū-karṇya,</b> ‘descendant of <b>Jātūkarṇa,</b>’ is the patronymic of several persons.</p><p>(<i>a</i>) A pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आसुरायण">āsurāyaṇa</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a></b> bears this name in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201039">1</a></sup> In the Mādhyaṃdina<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201039">2</a></sup> he is a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भारद्वाज">bhāradvāja</a>.</b></p><p>(<i>b</i>) A <a class="vei_120a" href="कात्यायनी-पुत्र">kātyāyanī-putra</a>, ‘son of <a class="vei_120a" href="कात्यायनी">kātyāyanī</a>,’ bears this name in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201039">3</a></sup></p><p>(<i>c</i>) A <a class="vei_120a" href="जातूकर्ण्य">jātūkarṇya</a> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201039">4</a></sup> as a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अलीकयु वाचस्पत्य">alīkayu vācaspatya</a></b> and other sages.</p><p>(<i>d</i>) <a class="vei_120a" href="जातूकर्ण्य">jātūkarṇya</a> is in the Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201039">5</a></sup> frequently a patronymic of teachers whose identity cannot be determined. The same person or different persons may here be meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201039">↑</a> ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201039">↑</a> ii. 5, 21; iv. 5, 27.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201039">↑</a> viii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201039">↑</a> xxvi. 5 (Jātukarṇya in Lindner's index, 159, is a misprint).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201039">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, v. 3, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 2, 17; iii. 16, 14; 20, 19; xvi. 29, 6 (<b>Jala</b>); Kātyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 1, 27; xx. 3, 17; xxv. 7, 34, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 138140.</li></ol></div>

जातू-ष्ठिर|जातूष्ठिर|जातू|ष्ठिर|జాతూ-ష్ఠిర|జాతూష్ఠిర|జాతూ|ష్ఠిర|ಜಾತೂ-ಷ್ಠಿರ|ಜಾತೂಷ್ಠಿರ|ಜಾತೂ|ಷ್ಠಿರ|jātū-ṣṭhira|jātūṣṭhira|jātū|ṣṭhira|jaatuu-ShThira|jaatuuShThira|jaatuu|ShThira|jaatoo-ShThira|jaatooShThira|jaatoo|_vei_120@1040|_vei1_pn282
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1039">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1041">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn281">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn282">1:282</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn283">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jātū-ṣṭhira</b> occurs in one verse of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201040">1</a></sup> where Sāyaṇa and Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201040">2</a></sup> interpret the word as a proper name. Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201040">3</a></sup> renders it as an adjective meaning ‘naturally powerful.’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201040">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201040">↑</a> ii. 13, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201040">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 152.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201040">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> (with a wrong reference, ii. 23, 11).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201040">↑</a> <i>Urkräftig.</i> Grassmann, <i>Wo7rterbuch,</i> similarly explains the word as ‘powerful by nature or birth’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="जातू">jātū</a></i>).</li></ol></div>

जान|జాన|ಜಾನ|jāna|jaana|_vei_120@1041|_vei1_pn282
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1040">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1042">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn281">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn282">1:282</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn283">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jāna,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b>Vṛśa</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201041">1</a></sup> and apparently in the Śāṭyāyanaka.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201041">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201041">↑</a> xiii. 3, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201041">↑</a> In Sāyaṇa on Rv. v. 5. <i>Cf.</i> Bṛhaddevatā, v. 14 <i>et seq.,</i> with Macdonell's notes; Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 64 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

जानक|జానక|ಜಾನಕ|jānaka|jaanaka|_vei_120@1042|_vei1_pn283
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1041">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1043">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn282">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn283">1:283</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn284">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jānaka,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b>Kratuvid</b> in some MSS. of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201042"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201042">1</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201042"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201042">2</a></sup> the name appears instead as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रतुजित् जानकि">kratujit jānaki</a>.</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="जानक">jānaka</a> is also, according to some manuscripts of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201042"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201042">3</a></sup> the patronymic of <b>Āyasthūṇa,</b> but is here no doubt a misreading of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जानकि">jānaki</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201042"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201042">↑</a> vii. 34.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201042"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201042">↑</a> ii. 3, 8, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201042"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201042">↑</a> vi. 3, 10 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

जानकि|జానకి|ಜಾನಕಿ|jānaki|jaanaki|_vei_120@1043|_vei1_pn283
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1042">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1044">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn282">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn283">1:283</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn284">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jānaki,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b>Kratujit</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201043"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201043">1</a></sup> of <b>Kratuvid</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201043"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201043">2</a></sup> and of <b>Āyasthūṇa</b> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201043"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201043">3</a></sup> where he is mentioned as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चूड भागवित्ति">cūḍa bhāgavitti</a>,</b> and as teacher of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सत्यकाम">satyakāma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="जाबाल">jābāla</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201043"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201043">↑</a> ii. 3, 8, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201043"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201043">↑</a> vii. 34.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201043"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201043">↑</a> vi. 3, 10 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> = vi. 3, 18. 19, Mādhyaṃdina).</li></ol></div>

जानं-तपि|जानंतपि|जानं|तपि|जानन्तपि|జానం-తపి|జానంతపి|జానం|తపి|జానన్తపి|ಜಾನಂ-ತಪಿ|ಜಾನಂತಪಿ|ಜಾನಂ|ತಪಿ|ಜಾನನ್ತಪಿ|jānaṃ-tapi|jānaṃtapi|jānaṃ|tapi|jaanaM-tapi|jaanaMtapi|jaanaM|jaanantapi|_vei_120@1044|_vei1_pn283
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1043">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1045">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn282">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn283">1:283</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn284">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jānaṃ-tapi,</b> ‘descendant of Janaṃtapa,’ is the patronymic of <b>Atyarāti</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 23).</p></div>

जान-पद|जानपद|जान|पद|జాన-పద|జానపద|జాన|పద|ಜಾನ-ಪದ|ಜಾನಪದ|ಜಾನ|ಪದ|jāna-pada|jānapada|jāna|pada|jaana-pada|jaanapada|jaana|_vei_120@1045|_vei1_pn283
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1044">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1046">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn282">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn283">1:283</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn284">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jāna-pada.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनपद">janapada</a>.</b></p></div>

जान-श्रुति|जानश्रुति|जान|श्रुति|జాన-శ్రుతి|జానశ్రుతి|జాన|శ్రుతి|ಜಾನ-ಶ್ರುತಿ|ಜಾನಶ್ರುತಿ|ಜಾನ|ಶ್ರುತಿ|jāna-śruti|jānaśruti|jāna|śruti|jaana-shruti|jaanashruti|jaana|shruti|_vei_120@1046|_vei1_pn283
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1045">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1047">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn282">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn283">1:283</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn284">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jāna-śruti,</b> ‘descendant of Jānaśruta,’ is the patronymic of <b>Pautrāyaṇa</b> in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iv. 1, 1; 2, 1).</p></div>

जान-श्रुतेय|जानश्रुतेय|जान|श्रुतेय|జాన-శ్రుతేయ|జానశ్రుతేయ|జాన|శ్రుతేయ|ಜಾನ-ಶ್ರುತೇಯ|ಜಾನಶ್ರುತೇಯ|ಜಾನ|ಶ್ರುತೇಯ|jāna-śruteya|jānaśruteya|jāna|śruteya|jaana-shruteya|jaanashruteya|jaana|shruteya|_vei_120@1047|_vei1_pn283
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1046">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1048">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn282">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn283">1:283</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn284">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jāna-śruteya,</b> ‘descendant of Jānaśruti’ or of ‘Janaśrutā.’ is the patronymic or metronymic of several persons--<b>Upāvi</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201047">1</a></sup> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="औपावि">aupāvi</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201047">2</a></sup> <b>Ulukya,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201047">3</a></sup> <b>Nagarin,</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201047">4</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सायक">sāyaka</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201047">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201047">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 25, 115.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201047">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1, 1, 5. 7; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 4, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201047">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201047">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> iii. 40, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201047">↑</a> <i>Ibid.</i></li></ol></div>

जाबाल|జాబాల|ಜಾಬಾಲ|jābāla|jaabaala|_vei_120@1048|_vei1_pn283
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1047">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1049">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn282">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn283">1:283</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn284">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jābāla,</b> ‘descendant of <b>Jabāla,</b>’ is the metronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="महाशाल">mahāśāla</a></b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201048">1</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सत्यकाम">satyakāma</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201048">2</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="जाबाल">jābāla</a> is also mentioned as a teacher in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201048">3</a></sup> which refers to the  Jābālas<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201048">4</a></sup> as well. The <a class="vei_120a" href="जाबाल">jābāla</a> Gṛhapatis are spoken of in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201048">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201048">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 3, 3, 1; 6, 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201048">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> xiii. 5, 3, 1; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1, 14; vi. 3, 19; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 4, 1, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201048">↑</a> iii. 9, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201048">↑</a> iii. 7, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201048">↑</a> xxiii. 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 395.</li></ol></div>

जाबालायन|జాబాలాయన|ಜಾಬಾಲಾಯನ|jābālāyana|jaabaalaayana|_vei_120@1049|_vei1_pn284
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1048">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1050">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn283">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn284">1:284</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn285">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jābālāyana,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="जाबाल">jābāla</a>,’ is the patronymic of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="माध्यंदिनायन">mādhyaṃdināyana</a>,</b> who is mentioned in the second <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iv. 6, 2).</p></div>

जामदग्निय|జామదగ్నియ|ಜಾಮದಗ್ನಿಯ|jāmadagniya|jaamadagniya|_vei_120@1050|_vei1_pn284
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1049">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1051">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn283">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn284">1:284</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn285">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jāmadagniya</b> is the patronymic of two ‘descendants of Jamadagni’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201050">1</a></sup> It appears from the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201050">2</a></sup> that the <b>Aurvas</b> are meant, and that Jamadagni's descendants were ever prosperous.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201050">↑</a> vii. 1, 9, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201050">↑</a> xxi. 10, 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 12, 251, n.; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 54.</li></ol></div>

जामातृ|జామాతృ|ಜಾಮಾತೃ|jāmātṛ|jaamaatR|jaamaatRi|jaamaatRu|jaamaatRRi|_vei_120@1051|_vei1_pn284
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1050">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1052">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn283">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn284">1:284</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn285">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jāmātṛ</b> is a rare word denoting ‘son-in-law’ in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201051"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201051">1</a></sup> where also occurs the word <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विजामातृ">vijāmātṛ</a>,</b> denoting an ‘unsatisfactory son-in-law,’ as one who does not pay a sufficient price, or one who, having other defects, must purchase a bride. Friendly relations between son-in-law and father-in-law are referred to in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201051"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201051">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201051"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201051">↑</a> viii. 2, 20. Vāyu is called the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="जामातृ">jāmātṛ</a></i> of <a class="vei_120a" href="त्वष्टृ">tvaṣṭṛ</a> in viii. 26, 21. 22.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 517; Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 78, 79.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201051"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201051">↑</a> x. 28, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 255.</li></ol></div>

जामि|జామి|ಜಾಮಿ|jāmi|jaami|_vei_120@1052|_vei1_pn284
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1051">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1053">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn283">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn284">1:284</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn285">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jāmi,</b> a word which appears originally to have meant ‘related in blood,’ is not rarely used as an epithet of ‘sister’ (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्वसृ">svasṛ</a></b>), and sometimes even denotes ‘sister’ itself, the emphasis being on the blood-relationship.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201052">1</a></sup> So it appears in a passage of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201052">2</a></sup> where ‘brotherless sisters’  (<i>abhrātara iva jāmayaḥ</i>) are referred to. The word is similarly used in the dispute occurring in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201052">3</a></sup> as to the precedence of <a class="vei_120a" href="राका">rākā</a>, or of the wives of the gods, in a certain rite. One party is there described as holding that the sister should be preferred (<i>jāmyai <a class="vei_120a" href="वै">vai</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पूर्व">pūrva</a>-peyam</i>)--apparently at a ceremonial family meal--to the wife, presumably as being of one blood with the husband, while the wife is not (being <i>anyodaryā,</i> ‘of another womb’).<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201052">4</a></sup> In the neuter<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201052">5</a></sup> the word means ‘relationship,’ like <i><a class="vei_120a" href="जामि">jāmi</a>-tva,</i> which also occurs in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201052">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201052">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>Dic indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 463, 464. As ‘relation,’ it occurs, <i>e.g.,</i> Rv. i. 31, 10; 75, 3. 4; 100, 11; 124, 6, etc.; as ‘sister,’ Rv. i. 65, 7; x. 10, 10, etc.; with <i>svasā,</i> i. 123, 5; 185, 5; iii. 1, 11; ix. 65, 1; 89, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201052">↑</a> i. 17, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201052">↑</a> iii. 37.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201052">↑</a> Delbrück, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201052">↑</a> Rv. iii. 54, 9; x. 10, 4; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="जामि">jāmi</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्">kṛt</a>,</i> ‘making relationship,’ Av. iv. 19, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Altareya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201052">↑</a> i. 105, 9; 166, 13; x. 55, 4; 64, 13.</li></ol></div>

जामि-शंस|जामिशंस|जामि|शंस|జామి-శంస|జామిశంస|జామి|శంస|ಜಾಮಿ-ಶಂಸ|ಜಾಮಿಶಂಸ|ಜಾಮಿ|ಶಂಸ|jāmi-śaṃsa|jāmiśaṃsa|jāmi|śaṃsa|jaami-shaMsa|jaamishaMsa|jaami|shaMsa|_vei_120@1053|_vei1_pn285
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1052">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1054">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn284">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn285">1:285</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn286">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jāmi-śaṃsa,</b> the ‘imprecation by a sister’ or ‘relation,’ is mentioned in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201053"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201053">1</a></sup> showing that family disputes were not rare. This is also indicated by the word <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भ्रातृव्य">bhrātṛvya</a>,</b> which, while properly meaning ‘father's brother's son,’ regularly denotes simply ‘enemy.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201053"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201053">↑</a> ii. 10, 1 (= <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 5, 6, 3), and personified in ix. 4, 15.</p><p><i>Cf. jāmyāḥ śapathaḥ,</i> Av. ii. 7, 2; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 362.</li></ol></div>

जाम्बिल|जांबिल|జామ్బిల|జాంబిల|ಜಾಮ್ಬಿಲ|ಜಾಂಬಿಲ|jāmbila|jaambila|_vei_120@1054|_vei1_pn285
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1053">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1055">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn284">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn285">1:285</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn286">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jāmbila,</b> ‘hollow of the knee,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201054"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201054">1</a></sup> occurs once in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201054"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201054">2</a></sup> The word also occurs in the form of Jāmbīla in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201054"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201054">3</a></sup> and the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201054"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201054">4</a></sup> Mahīdhara, in his commentary on the latter text, interprets the word as ‘knee-pan,’ which he says is so named because of its resemblance to the citron, <i>jāmbīra.</i></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201054"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201054">↑</a> Perhaps for <i>jānu-bila. Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> p. 11, n. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201054"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201054">↑</a> iii. 15, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201054"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201054">↑</a> v. 13, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201054"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201054">↑</a> xxv. 3.</li></ol></div>

जायन्ती-पुत्र|जायन्तीपुत्र|जायन्ती|जायन्ति|पुत्र|జాయన్తీ-పుత్ర|జాయన్తీపుత్ర|జాయన్తీ|జాయన్తి|పుత్ర|ಜಾಯನ್ತೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಜಾಯನ್ತೀಪುತ್ರ|ಜಾಯನ್ತೀ|ಜಾಯನ್ತಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|jāyantī-putra|jāyantīputra|jāyantī|jāyanti|putra|jaayantii-putra|jaayantiiputra|jaayantii|jaayanti|jaayantee-putra|jaayanteeputra|jaayantee|_vei_120@1055|_vei1_pn285
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1054">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1056">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn284">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn285">1:285</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn286">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jāyantī-putra,</b> ‘son of <a class="vei_120a" href="जायन्ती">jāyantī</a>,’ is mentioned in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201055"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201055">1</a></sup> as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="माण्डूकायनीपुत्र">māṇḍūkāyanīputra</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201055"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201055">↑</a> vi. 5, 2 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> = vi. 4, 32 Mādhyaṃdina).</li></ol></div>

जाया|జాయా|ಜಾಯಾ|jāyā|jaayaa|_vei_120@1056|_vei1_pn285
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1055">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1057">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn284">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn285">1:285</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn286">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jāyā</b> regularly denotes ‘wife,’ and, as opposed to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पत्नी">patnī</a>,</b> wife as an object of marital affection, the source of the continuance  of the race.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201056">1</a></sup> So it is used of the wife of the gambler, and of the wife of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> in the Rigveda;<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201056">2</a></sup> it is also frequently combined with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>,</b> ‘husband,’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201056">3</a></sup> both there and in the later literature.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201056">4</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="पत्नी">patnī</a>, on the other hand, is used to denote the wife as partner in the sacrifice;<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201056">5</a></sup> when no share in it is assigned to her, she is called <a class="vei_120a" href="जाया">jāyā</a>.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201056">6</a></sup> The distinction is, of course, merely relative; hence one text<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201056">7</a></sup> calls Manu's wife <a class="vei_120a" href="जाया">jāyā</a>, another<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201056">8</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="पत्नी">patnī</a>. Later on <a class="vei_120a" href="जाया">jāyā</a> is superseded by <a class="vei_120a" href="दार">dāra</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201056">↑</a> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 411, 412. <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 105, 2; 124, 7; iii. 53, 4; iv. 3, 2; 18, 3; ix. 82, 4; x. 10, 7; 17, 1; 71, 4, etc.; Av. iii. 30, 2; vi. 60, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201056">↑</a> x. 34, 2. 3. 13, and x. 109.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201056">↑</a> Rv. iv. 3, 2; x. 149, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201056">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 23, 1. <i>Cf.</i> vii. 13, 10; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 6, 7, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 6, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201056">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 9, 2, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201056">↑</a> i. 1, 4, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201056">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> i. 1, 4, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201056">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 8, 1.</li></ol></div>

जायान्य|జాయాన్య|ಜಾಯಾನ್ಯ|jāyānya|jaayaanya|_vei_120@1057|_vei1_pn286
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1056">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1058">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn285">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn286">1:286</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn287">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jāyānya,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201057">1</a></sup> <b>Jāyenya,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201057">2</a></sup> are variant forms of the name of a disease mentioned in the Atharvaveda and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā. In one passage of the former text<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201057">3</a></sup> it is mentioned with jaundice (<i>harimā</i>) and pains in the limbs (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अंग">aṅga</a>-bhedo visalpakaḥ</i>). Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201057">4</a></sup> thinks these are its symptoms, and identifies it with a kind of <a class="vei_120a" href="यक्ष्म">yakṣma</a>, or disease of the lungs. Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201057">5</a></sup> prefers to identify it with syphilis, in accordance with certain indications in the ritual of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201057">6</a></sup> Roth conjectures ‘gout,’ but Whitney<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201057">7</a></sup> leaves the nature of the disease doubtful.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201057">↑</a> Av. vii. 76, 3-5; xix. 44, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201057">↑</a> ii. 3, 5, 2; 5, 6, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201057">↑</a> xix. 44, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201057">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 377, following Wise, <i>Hindu System of Medicint,</i> 321, describing <a class="vei_120a" href="अक्षत">akṣata</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201057">↑</a> <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 11, 320 <i>et seq.; Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 559-561.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201057">↑</a> xxxii. 11. <i>Cf.</i> also the commentary on Av. vii. 76, and <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201057">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 442.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Henry, <i>Lelivre vii de l' Atharvavéda,</i> 98.</li></ol></div>

जार|జార|ಜಾರ|jāra|jaara|_vei_120@1058|_vei1_pn286
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1057">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1059">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn285">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn286">1:286</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn287">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jāra,</b> ‘lover,’ has no sinister sense in the early texts<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201058">1</a></sup> generally, where the word applies to any lover. But it seems probable that the <a class="vei_120a" href="जार">jāra</a> at the Puruṣamedha, or human sacrifice,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201058">2</a></sup>  must be regarded as an illegitimate lover; this sense also appears in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201058">3</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> is styled the lover of <b>Ahalyā,</b> wife of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a></b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201058">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201058">↑</a> Rv. i. 66, 8; 117, 18; 134, 3; 152, 4; ix. 32, 5, etc. The word is often used mythologically, as, <i>e.g., <a class="vei_120a" href="जार">jāra</a> uṣasām,</i> ‘lover of the dawns,’ vii. 9, 1. <i>Cf</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 308.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201058">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 4. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201058">↑</a> vi. 4, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201058">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 65.</li></ol></div>

जारत्-कारव|जारत्कारव|जारत्|कारव|జారత్-కారవ|జారత్కారవ|జారత్|కారవ|ಜಾರತ್-ಕಾರವ|ಜಾರತ್ಕಾರವ|ಜಾರತ್|ಕಾರವ|jārat-kārava|jāratkārava|jārat|kārava|jaarat-kaarava|jaaratkaarava|jaarat|kaarava|_vei_120@1059|_vei1_pn287
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1058">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1060">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn286">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn287">1:287</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn288">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jārat-kārava</b> (‘descendant of Jaratkāru’) <b>Ārtabhāga</b> (‘descendant of Ṛtabhāga’) is the name of a teacher mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka (vii. 20) and the Bṛhadāranyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iii. 2, 1, in both recensions).</p></div>

जारु|జారు|ಜಾರು|jāru|jaaru|_vei_120@1060|_vei1_pn287
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1059">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1061">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn286">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn287">1:287</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn288">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jāru.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जरायु">jarāyu</a>.</b></p></div>

जाल|జాల|ಜಾಲ|jāla|jaala|_vei_120@1061|_vei1_pn287
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1060">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1062">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn286">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn287">1:287</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn288">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jāla</b> occurs in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201061">1</a></sup> and the Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201061">2</a></sup> in the sense of ‘net.’ Jālaka is used in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201061">3</a></sup> of a reticulated membrane resembling a woven covering.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201061">↑</a> viii. 8. 5. 3 (as used against foes); x. 1, 30.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201061">↑</a> Kātyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, vii. 4, 7. etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201061">↑</a> iv. 2, 3.</li></ol></div>

जालाष|జాలాష|ಜಾಲಾಷ|jālāṣa|jaalaaSha|_vei_120@1062|_vei1_pn287
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1061">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1063">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn286">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn287">1:287</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn288">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jālāṣa.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जलाष">jalāṣa</a>,</b> which is read by Sāyaṇa in the Atharvaveda (vi. 57, 2) for <a class="vei_120a" href="जालाष">jālāṣa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201062"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201062">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201062"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201062">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 11, 320.</li></ol></div>

जाष्कमद|జాష్కమద|ಜಾಷ್ಕಮದ|jāṣkamada|jaaShkamada|_vei_120@1063|_vei1_pn287
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1062">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1064">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn286">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn287">1:287</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn288">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jāṣkamada</b> is the name of an unknown animal in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201063">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201063">↑</a> xi. 9, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 88.</li></ol></div>

जास्-पति|जास्पति|जास्|पति|జాస్-పతి|జాస్పతి|జాస్|పతి|ಜಾಸ್-ಪತಿ|ಜಾಸ್ಪತಿ|ಜಾಸ್|ಪತಿ|jās-pati|jāspati|jās|pati|jaas-pati|jaaspati|jaas|_vei_120@1064|_vei1_pn287
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1063">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1065">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn286">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn287">1:287</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn288">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jās-pati</b> occurs once in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201064">1</a></sup> in the sense of the ‘head of the family.’ The abstract formed from this word, <a class="vei_120a" href="जास्">jās</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पत्य">patya</a>, apparently denoting ‘lordship of children,’ is also found there.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201064">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201064">↑</a> i. 135. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201064">↑</a> Rv. v. 28, 3; x. 85, 23.</li></ol></div>

जाहुष|జాహుష|ಜಾಹುಷ|jāhuṣa|jaahuSha|_vei_120@1065|_vei1_pn287
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1064">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1066">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn286">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn287">1:287</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn288">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jāhuṣa</b> is the name in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201065">1</a></sup> of a protégé of the Aśvins. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201065">↑</a> i. 116, 10; vii. 71, 5 <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 159.</li></ol></div>

जाह्नव|జాహ్నవ|ಜಾಹ್ನವ|jāhnava|jaahnava|_vei_120@1066|_vei1_pn288
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1065">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1067">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn287">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn288">1:288</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn289">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jāhnava,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जह्नु">jahnu</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201066"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201066">1</a></sup> This fact is of some importance as disproving Aufrecht's theory<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201066"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201066">2</a></sup> that the Jahnus were the clan of <b>Ajīgarta</b> the father of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201066"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201066">↑</a> xxi. 12. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 32; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 54.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201066"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201066">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, 424.</li></ol></div>

जित्वन् शैलिनि|జిత్వన్ శైలిని|ಜಿತ್ವನ್ ಶೈಲಿನಿ|jitvan śailini|jitvan shailini|_vei_120@1067|_vei1_pn288
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1066">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1068">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn287">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn288">1:288</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn289">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jitvan Śailini</b> is the name of a teacher in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201067"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201067">1</a></sup> a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a>.</b> He held that speech (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वाच्">vāc</a></i>) was <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201067"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201067">↑</a> iv. 1, 2 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> = iv. 1, 5 Mādhyaṃdina, which has <a class="vei_120a" href="शैलिन">śailina</a> as the patronymic).</li></ol></div>

जिह्वावन्त् बाध्योग|జిహ్వావన్త్ బాధ్యోగ|ಜಿಹ್ವಾವನ್ತ್ ಬಾಧ್ಯೋಗ|jihvāvant bādhyoga|jihvaavant baadhyoga|_vei_120@1068|_vei1_pn288
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1067">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1069">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn287">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn288">1:288</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn289">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jihvāvant Bādhyoga</b> is the name, in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201068">1</a></sup> of a teacher, pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="असित">asita</a> Vārṣāgaṇa.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201068">↑</a> vi. 5, 3 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> = vi. 4, 33 Mādhyaṃdina).</li></ol></div>

जीव-गृभ्|जीवगृभ्|जीव|गृभ्|జీవ-గృభ్|జీవగృభ్|జీవ|గృభ్|ಜೀವ-ಗೃಭ್|ಜೀವಗೃಭ್|ಜೀವ|ಗೃಭ್|jīva-gṛbh|jīvagṛbh|jīva|gṛbh|jiiva-gRbh|jiivagRbh|jiiva|gRbh|jeeva-gRbh|jeevagRbh|jeeva|jiiva-gRibh|jiivagRibh|gRibh|jiiva-gRubh|jiivagRubh|gRubh|jiiva-gRRibh|jiivagRRibh|gRRibh|_vei_120@1069|_vei1_pn288
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1068">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1070">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn287">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn288">1:288</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn289">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jīva-gṛbh,</b> ‘seizing alive,’ is, according to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201069"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201069">1</a></sup> the term for a police official in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201069"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201069">2</a></sup> But although this sense is rendered possible by the mention of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यमशी">madhyamaśī</a>,</b> perhaps ‘arbitrator,’ in the same passage,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201069"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201069">3</a></sup> it is neither necessary nor probable.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201069"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201069">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201069"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201069">↑</a> St Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.; Siebenzig Lieder,</i> 174.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201069"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201069">↑</a> x. 97, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201069"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201069">↑</a> Rv. x. 97, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201069"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201069">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 180.</li></ol></div>

जीव-ज|जीवज|जीव|జీవ-జ|జీవజ|జీవ|ಜೀವ-ಜ|ಜೀವಜ|ಜೀವ|jīva-ja|jīvaja|jīva|jiiva-ja|jiivaja|jiiva|jeeva-ja|jeevaja|jeeva|_vei_120@1070|_vei1_pn288
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1069">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1071">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn287">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn288">1:288</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn289">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jīva-ja.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जरायु">jarāyu</a>.</b></p></div>

जीवन्त्|జీవన్త్|ಜೀವನ್ತ್|jīvant|jiivant|jeevant|_vei_120@1071|_vei1_pn288
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1070">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1072">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn287">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn288">1:288</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn289">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jīvant</b> appears to denote a certain plant in one passage of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201071"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201071">1</a></sup> where the edition of Roth and Whitney has the unjustified emendation <b>Jīvala.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201071"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201071">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201071"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201071">↑</a> xix. 39, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 960.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201071"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201071">↑</a> With this conjectural form, <i>cf.</i> Jīvalā, an epithet of a plant in Av. vi. 59, 3; viii. 2, 6; 7, 6; xix. 39. 3.</li></ol></div>

जीवल चल्लकि|జీవల చల్లకి|ಜೀವಲ ಚಲ್ಲಕಿ|jīvala callaki|jiivala challaki|jeevala challaki|_vei_120@1072|_vei1_pn289
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1071">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1073">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn288">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn289">1:289</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn290">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jīvala Callaki,</b> ‘descendant of Celaka,’ is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201072"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201072">1</a></sup> as reproving <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तक्षन्">takṣan</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201072"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201072">↑</a> ii. 3, 1, 31-35. <i>Cf.</i> Lévi, <i>La Doctrine du Sacrifice,</i> 140.</li></ol></div>

जुहू|జుహూ|ಜುಹೂ|juhū|juhuu|juhoo|_vei_120@1073|_vei1_pn289
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1072">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1074">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn288">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn289">1:289</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn290">↷</a><hr><p><b>Juhū</b> is the regular name in the Rigveda and later for the tongue-shaped ladle in which butter was offered to the gods.</p><p>1) Rv. viii. 44, 5; x. 21, 3; Av. xviii. 4, 5. 6, etc.</p></div>

जूर्णि|జూర్ణి|ಜೂರ್ಣಿ|jūrṇi|juurNi|joorNi|_vei_120@1074|_vei1_pn289
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1073">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1075">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn288">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn289">1:289</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn290">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jūrṇi,</b> ‘firebrand,’ is regarded by Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201074"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201074">1</a></sup> as one of the weapons of the Vedic Indians. But since it is only mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201074"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201074">2</a></sup> as a weapon used by demons, its employment in normal war cannot be safely assumed.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201074"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201074">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 301.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201074"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201074">↑</a> i. 129, 8. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 4.</li></ol></div>

जूर्णी|जूर्णि|జూర్ణీ|జూర్ణి|ಜೂರ್ಣೀ|ಜೂರ್ಣಿ|jūrṇī|jūrṇi|juurNii|juurNi|joorNee|joorNi|_vei_120@1075|_vei1_pn289
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1074">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1076">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn288">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn289">1:289</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn290">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jūrṇī</b> is one of the names given to serpents in a hymn of the Atharvaveda (ii. 24, 5), perhaps from their habit of casting their slough. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अहि">ahi</a>.</b></p></div>

जेतृ|జేతృ|ಜೇತೃ|jetṛ|jetR|jetRi|jetRu|jetRRi|_vei_120@1076|_vei1_pn289
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1075">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1077">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn288">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn289">1:289</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn290">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jetṛ.</b> See <b>Sṛṇi.</b></p></div>

जैत्रायण सहो-जित्|जैत्रायण सहोजित्|जैत्रायण सहो|जित्|జైత్రాయణ సహో-జిత్|జైత్రాయణ సహోజిత్|జైత్రాయణ సహో|జిత్|ಜೈತ್ರಾಯಣ ಸಹೋ-ಜಿತ್|ಜೈತ್ರಾಯಣ ಸಹೋಜಿತ್|ಜೈತ್ರಾಯಣ ಸಹೋ|ಜಿತ್|jaitrāyaṇa saho-jit|jaitrāyaṇa sahojit|jaitrāyaṇa saho|jit|jaitraayaNa saho-jit|jaitraayaNa sahojit|jaitraayaNa saho|_vei_120@1077|_vei1_pn289
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1076">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1078">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn288">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn289">1:289</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn290">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jaitrāyaṇa <a class="vei_120a" href="सहो">saho</a>-jit</b> is apparently in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201077"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201077">1</a></sup> the name of a prince who celebrated the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>, or ‘royal consecration.’ Von Schroeder<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201077"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201077">2</a></sup> quotes in support of Jaitrāyaṇa as a proper name the derivative Jaitrāyaṇi, ‘descendant of Jaitra,’ formed according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="गण">gaṇa</a> <i>karṇādi,</i> which is referred to by Pāṇini;<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201077"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201077">3</a></sup> but it should be noted that in the parallel passage of the Kapiṣṭhala Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201077"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201077">4</a></sup> the reading is different, and no proper personal name appears, the subject being <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, the god. This reading seems much more probable, for the verse should be general, and suit every king performing the rite. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201077"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201077">↑</a> xviii. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201077"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201077">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenlāndischen Gesellschaft,</i> 49, 168.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201077"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201077">↑</a> iv. 2, 80.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201077"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201077">↑</a> xxviii. 5, cited by von Schroeder <i><a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a>,</i> 1, p. 269.</li></ol></div>

जैमिनि|జైమిని|ಜೈಮಿನಿ|jaimini|_vei_120@1078|_vei1_pn290
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1077">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1079">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn289">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn290">1:290</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn291">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jaimini</b> does not appear till the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> period.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201078"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201078">1</a></sup> But a Jaiminīya Saṃhitā of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a> is extant, and has been edited and discussed by Caland;<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201078"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201078">2</a></sup> and a Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, of which a special section is the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201078"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201078">3</a></sup> is known and has formed the subject of several articles by Oertel.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201078"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201078">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201078"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201078">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 10; vi. 6, etc. He appears also as a pupil of Vyāsa, Sāmavidhāna <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>ad fin.;</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 377. <i>Cf.</i> his <i>Indian Literature,</i> 56.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201078"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201078">↑</a> As part ii. of Hillebrandt's <i>Indische Forschungen, Breslau,</i> 1907. See Oldenberg, <i>Go7ttingische Gelehrte Anzeigen,</i> 1908, 712 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201078"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201078">↑</a> Edited by Oertel, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, 79-260.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201078"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201078">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 15 <i>et seq.;</i> 19, 97; 23, 325, 26, 176, 306; 28, 81; <i>Actes du onzieme Congres International des Orientalistes,</i> 1, 225; <i>Transactions of the Connectieut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 155 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

जैवन्तायन|జైవన్తాయన|ಜೈವನ್ತಾಯನ|jaivantāyana|jaivantaayana|_vei_120@1079|_vei1_pn290
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1078">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1080">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn289">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn290">1:290</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn291">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jaivantāyana,</b> ‘descendant of Jīvanta,’ is mentioned in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201079"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201079">1</a></sup> as a teacher, with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शौनक">śaunaka</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रैभ्य">raibhya</a>,</b> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रौहिणायन">rauhiṇāyana</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201079"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201079">↑</a> iv. 5, 26 (Mādhyaṃdina). The name is recognised by Pāṇini, iv. 1, 103.</li></ol></div>

जैवल|जैवलि|జైవల|జైవలి|ಜೈವಲ|ಜೈವಲಿ|jaivala|jaivali|_vei_120@1080|_vei1_pn290
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1079">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1081">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn289">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn290">1:290</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn291">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jaivala</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जैवलि">jaivali</a>,</b> ‘descendant of Jīvala,’ is the patronymic of <b>Pravāhaṇa</b> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201080"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201080">1</a></sup> and Chāndogya Upaniṣads.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201080"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201080">2</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="जैवलि">jaivali</a>, the king, in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201080"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201080">3</a></sup> is the same person.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201080"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201080">↑</a> vi. 2, 1 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> = vi. 1, 1 Mādhyaṃdina), where the form is <a class="vei_120a" href="जैवल">jaivala</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201080"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201080">↑</a> i. 8, 1. 2. 8; v. 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201080"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201080">↑</a> i. 38, 4.</li></ol></div>

ज्ञातृ|జ్ఞాతృ|ಜ್ಞಾತೃ|jñātṛ|j~naatR|j~naatRi|j~naatRu|j~naatRRi|_vei_120@1081|_vei1_pn290
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1080">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1082">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn289">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn290">1:290</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn291">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jñātṛ</b> occurs in two passages of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201081">1</a></sup> and one of the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201081">2</a></sup> with a somewhat obscure sense. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201081">3</a></sup> conjectures not unnaturally that the word is a technical term taken from law, meaning ‘witness.’ The reference is, perhaps, to a custom of carrying on transactions of business before witnesses as practised in other primitive societies.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201081">4</a></sup> Roth<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201081">5</a></sup> suggests that the word has the sense of  ‘surety.’ But Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201081">6</a></sup> and Whitney<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201081">7</a></sup> ignore these interpretations.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201081">↑</a> vi. 32, 3; viii. 8, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201081">↑</a> xii. 14. <i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 66, n. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201081">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 181.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201081">↑</a> In <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>, viii. 57, the word is a various reading for <i>sākṣin,</i> ‘witness.’</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 140.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201081">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201081">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 475.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201081">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 306.</li></ol></div>

ज्ञाति|జ్ఞాతి|ಜ್ಞಾತಿ|jñāti|j~naati|_vei_120@1082|_vei1_pn291
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1081">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1083">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn290">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn291">1:291</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn292">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jñāti</b> (masc.), a word which originally seems to have meant ‘acquaintance,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201082"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201082">1</a></sup> denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201082"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201082">2</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201082"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201082">3</a></sup> a ‘relation,’ apparently one who was connected by blood on the father's side, though the passages do not necessarily require the limitation. But this sense follows naturally enough from the patriarchal basis of Vedic society.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201082"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201082">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201082"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201082">↑</a> Being in all probability derived from <i>jñā,</i> ‘know,’ not from <i>jan,</i> ‘beget,’ as would at first sight seem more likely on account of the sense. <i>Cf.</i> the St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201082"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201082">↑</a> vii. 55, 5, seems to refer to the members of the joint family sleeping in the paternal house; x. 66, 14; 85, 28 (the kinsmen of the bride are meant); 117, 9 (perhaps ‘brother and sister’ are meant by <i>jñātī</i> here, but ‘kinsfolk’ will do; <i>of.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 432).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201082"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201082">↑</a> Av. xii. 5, 44 (where Whitney in his Translation renders the word by ‘acquaintances,’ which seems too vague and feeble); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 5, 2; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 4, 3 (<i>jñātibhyāṃ vā sakhibhyāṃ, vā,</i> ‘where ‘relations’ are contrasted with ‘friends’ or ‘companions’); ii. 2, 2, 20; 5, 2, 20; xi. 3, 3, 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201082"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201082">↑</a> For the transition from the etymological meaning, <i>cf.</i> , which in Homer designate ‘brother’ and ‘sister’; St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

ज्या|జ్యా|ಜ್ಯಾ|jyā|jyaa|_vei_120@1083|_vei1_pn291
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1082">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1084">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn290">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn291">1:291</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn292">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jyā</b> is the regular word for ‘bowstring’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201083"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201083">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201083"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201083">2</a></sup> The making of bowstrings was a special craft, as is shown by the occurrence of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्या">jyā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कार">kāra</a>, or ‘maker of bowstrings,’ among the victims at the Puruṣamedha, or human sacrifice, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201083"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201083">3</a></sup> The bowstring consisted of a thong of ox-hide.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201083"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201083">4</a></sup> It was not usually kept taut,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201083"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201083">5</a></sup> but was specially tightened when the bow was to be used.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201083"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201083">6</a></sup> The sound of the bowstring (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ज्या">jyā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="घोष">ghoṣa</a></i>) is referred to in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201083"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201083">7</a></sup></p><p><i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आर्त्नी">ārtnī</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201083"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201083">↑</a> iv. 27, 3; vi. 75, 3; x. 51, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201083"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201083">↑</a> Av. i. 1, 3; v. 13, 6; vi. 42, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 9; xxix. 51, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201083"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201083">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201083"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201083">↑</a> Rv. vi. 75, 3; Av. i. 1, 3. In the Epic the bowstring is made of hemp (<i>maurvī</i>); Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 271.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201083"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201083">↑</a> Av. vi. 42, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201083"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201083">↑</a> Rv. x. 166, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201083"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201083">↑</a> v. 21, 9.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 298 299.</li></ol></div>

ज्याका|జ్యాకా|ಜ್ಯಾಕಾ|jyākā|jyaakaa|_vei_120@1084|_vei1_pn292
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1083">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1085">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn291">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn292">1:292</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn293">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jyākā</b> means ‘bowstring,’ with a contemptuous sense, in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201084"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201084">1</a></sup> and in the simple sense in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201084"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201084">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201084"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201084">↑</a> x. 133, 1, where <i>anyakeṣāṃ jyākāḥ</i> is unmistakably contemptuous. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> p. 137.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201084"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201084">↑</a> i. 2, 2.</li></ol></div>

ज्या-पाश|ज्यापाश|ज्या|पाश|జ్యా-పాశ|జ్యాపాశ|జ్యా|పాశ|ಜ್ಯಾ-ಪಾಶ|ಜ್ಯಾಪಾಶ|ಜ್ಯಾ|ಪಾಶ|jyā-pāśa|jyāpāśa|jyā|pāśa|jyaa-paasha|jyaapaasha|jyaa|paasha|_vei_120@1085|_vei1_pn292
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1084">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1086">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn291">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn292">1:292</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn293">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jyā-pāśa</b> means ‘bowstring’ in the Atharvaveda (xi. 10, 22).</p></div>

ज्या-ह्रोड|ज्याह्रोड|ज्या|ह्रोड|జ్యా-హ్రోడ|జ్యాహ్రోడ|జ్యా|హ్రోడ|ಜ್ಯಾ-ಹ್ರೋಡ|ಜ್ಯಾಹ್ರೋಡ|ಜ್ಯಾ|ಹ್ರೋಡ|jyā-hroḍa|jyāhroḍa|jyā|hroḍa|jyaa-hroDa|jyaahroDa|jyaa|hroDa|_vei_120@1086|_vei1_pn292
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1085">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1087">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn291">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn292">1:292</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn293">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jyā-hroḍa</b> occurs in the description of the arms of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात्य">vrātya</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201086">1</a></sup> and is also mentioned in the Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201086">2</a></sup> The sense is somewhat obscure, for one <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> describes it as a ‘bow not meant for use’ (<i>ayogyaṃ <a class="vei_120a" href="धनुस्">dhanus</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201086">3</a></sup> while the other speaks of it as a ‘bow without an arrow’ (<i>dhanuṣka aniṣu</i>).<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201086">4</a></sup> Some sort of a bow, therefore, seems to be meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201086">↑</a> xvii. 1, 14 (spelt <i>-hṇoḍa</i> in text, <i>-hnoḍa</i> in comm.).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201086">↑</a> Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxii. 4, 11 (spelt <i>-<a class="vei_120a" href="ह्रोड">hroḍa</a></i>); Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 6, 8 (spelt <i>-hnoḍa:</i> the editor notes that the Drāhyāyaṇa <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> has the same reading).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201086">↑</a> Kātyāyana, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201086">↑</a> Lāṭyāyana, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 38; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Acad my of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 32; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 33, 52.</li></ol></div>

ज्येष्ठ|జ్యేష్ఠ|ಜ್ಯೇಷ್ಠ|jyeṣṭha|jyeShTha|_vei_120@1087|_vei1_pn292
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1086">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1088">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn291">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn292">1:292</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn293">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jyeṣṭha,</b> ordinarily meaning ‘greatest,’ has further the specific sense of ‘eldest’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201087">1</a></sup> brother in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201087">2</a></sup> It also means the eldest among sons, which is another side of the same sense.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201087">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201087">↑</a> With changed accent, <i>jyeṣṭhá. Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> p. 83, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201087">↑</a> iv. 33, 5; x. 11, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201087">↑</a> Av. xii. 2, 35; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 17; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 3, 8, <i>and cf.</i> Jyaiṣthineya.</li></ol></div>

ज्येष्ठ-घ्नी|ज्येष्ठ-घ्नि|ज्येष्ठघ्नी|ज्येष्ठघ्नि|ज्येष्ठ|घ्नी|घ्नि|జ్యేష్ఠ-ఘ్నీ|జ్యేష్ఠ-ఘ్ని|జ్యేష్ఠఘ్నీ|జ్యేష్ఠఘ్ని|జ్యేష్ఠ|ఘ్నీ|ఘ్ని|ಜ್ಯೇಷ್ಠ-ಘ್ನೀ|ಜ್ಯೇಷ್ಠ-ಘ್ನಿ|ಜ್ಯೇಷ್ಠಘ್ನೀ|ಜ್ಯೇಷ್ಠಘ್ನಿ|ಜ್ಯೇಷ್ಠ|ಘ್ನೀ|ಘ್ನಿ|jyeṣṭha-ghnī|jyeṣṭha-ghni|jyeṣṭhaghnī|jyeṣṭhaghni|jyeṣṭha|ghnī|ghni|jyeShTha-ghnii|jyeShTha-ghni|jyeShThaghnii|jyeShThaghni|jyeShTha|ghnii|jyeShTha-ghnee|jyeShThaghnee|ghnee|_vei_120@1088|_vei1_pn292
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1087">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1089">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn291">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn292">1:292</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn293">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jyeṣṭha-ghnī,</b> ‘slaying the eldest,’ is the name of a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>,</b> or lunar mansion, usually called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ज्येष्ठा">jyeṣṭhā</a>,</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201088">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201088">2</a></sup> It is Antares or <i>Cor Scipionis.</i></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201088">↑</a> vi. 110, 2. <i>Cf.</i> vi. 112, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201088">↑</a> i. 5, 2, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 361.</li></ol></div>

ज्येष्ठा|జ్యేష్ఠా|ಜ್ಯೇಷ್ಠಾ|jyeṣṭhā|jyeShThaa|_vei_120@1089|_vei1_pn292
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1088">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1090">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn291">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn292">1:292</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn293">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jyeṣṭhā.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b> </p></div>

ज्यैष्ठिनेय|జ్యైష్ఠినేయ|ಜ್ಯೈಷ್ಠಿನೇಯ|jyaiṣṭhineya|jyaiShThineya|_vei_120@1090|_vei1_pn293
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1089">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1091">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn292">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn293">1:293</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn294">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jyaiṣṭhineya</b> denotes, in combination with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ज्येष्ठ">jyeṣṭha</a>,</b> the eldest,’ a ‘son of the father's first wife’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ज्येष्ठा">jyeṣṭhā</a></i>), in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201090"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201090">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201090"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201090">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 8, 1 (opposed to <i>haniṣṭha</i> and <i>hāniṣṭhineya</i>); <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 2; xx. 5, 2.</li></ol></div>

ज्योतिष|జ్యోతిష|ಜ್ಯೋತಿಷ|jyotiṣa|jyotiSha|_vei_120@1091|_vei1_pn293
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1090">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1092">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn292">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn293">1:293</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn294">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jyotiṣa,</b> ‘astronomy.’ It is important to note that no reference to any work on astronomy occurs in the Saṃhitās or Brāhmaṇas. The text which claims to represent the astronomical science of the <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a> has been edited by Weber,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201091"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201091">1</a></sup> and has frequently been discussed since.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201091"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201091">2</a></sup> Its date is unknown, but is undoubtedly late, as is shown alike by the contents and form of the work.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201091"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201091">↑</a> <i>Ueber den Vedakalender namens Jyotiṣam</i> (1862).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201091"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201091">↑</a> See references in Thibaut, <i>Astrenomie, Astrologie und Mathematik,</i> 20, 29.</li></ol></div>

ज्वालायन|జ్వాలాయన|ಜ್ವಾಲಾಯನ|jvālāyana|jvaalaayana|_vei_120@1092|_vei1_pn293
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1091">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1093">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn292">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn293">1:293</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn294">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jvālāyana,</b> ‘descendant of Jvāla,’ is the name of a man, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौषूक्ति">gauṣūkti</a>,</b> mentioned in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iv. 16, 1), in a list of teachers.</p></div>

झष|ఝష|ಝಷ|jhaṣa|jhaSha|_vei_120@1093|_vei1_pn293
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1092">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1094">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn292">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn293">1:293</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn294">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jhaṣa</b> is mentioned in the story of <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a> told in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201093"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201093">1</a></sup> where it means a ‘great fish’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="महा-मत्स्य">mahā-matsya</a></i>) according to the commentator. Eggeling<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201093"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201093">2</a></sup> suggests that a horned fish is meant, because in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201093"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201093">3</a></sup> the Iḍā, or personified libation, is represented as a cow, and this may have brought in the idea of a horned fish in the later form of an old legend. But <i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जष">jaṣa</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201093"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201093">↑</a> i. 8, 1, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201093"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201093">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 217, n. 3; 26, xxxi.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201093"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201093">↑</a> i. 7, 1; ii. 6, 7.</li></ol></div>

तकवान|తకవాన|ತಕವಾನ|takavāna|takavaana|_vei_120@1094|_vei1_pn294
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1093">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1095">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn293">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn294">1:294</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn296">↷</a><hr><p><b>Takavāna</b> appears to be a patronymic from Taku,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201094"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201094">1</a></sup> and to be the name of a seer in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201094"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201094">2</a></sup> presumably a descendant of Taku <a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a>, for his name occurs in a group of hymns composed by the Kākṣīvatas.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201094"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201094">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201094"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201094">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="भृगवाण">bhṛgavāṇa</a>, from <a class="vei_120a" href="भृगु">bhṛgu</a>; Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. Taku,</i> as an adjective(?), occurs in Rigveda, ix. 97, 52.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201094"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201094">↑</a> i. 120, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201094"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201094">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenlāndischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 221.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 92; Ludwig, <i>Über Methode bei Interpretation des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 47.</li></ol></div>

तक्मन्|తక్మన్|ತಕ್ಮನ್|takman|_vei_120@1095|_vei1_pn294
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1094">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1096">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn293">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn294">1:294</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn296">↷</a><hr><p><b>Takman</b> is a disease repeatedly mentioned in the Atharvaveda, but later not known under this name. It is the subject of five hymns<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201095">1</a></sup> of the Atharvaveda, and is often mentioned elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201095">2</a></sup> Weber<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201095">3</a></sup> first identified it with ‘fever,’ and Grohmann<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201095">4</a></sup> showed that all the symptoms pointed to that ailment.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201095">5</a></sup> Reference is made to the alternate hot and shivering fits of the patient,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201095">6</a></sup> to the yellow colour of the jaundice which accompanies the fever,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201095">7</a></sup> and to its peculiar periodicity. The words used to describe its varieties are <i>anye-dyuḥ,</i><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201095">8</a></sup> <i>ubhaya-dyuḥ,</i><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201095">9</a></sup> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="तृतीयक">tṛtīyaka</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201095">10</a></sup> <i>vi-tṛtīya,</i><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201095">11</a></sup> and <i>sadaṃ-di,</i><sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201095">12</a></sup> the exact sense of most of which terms is somewhat uncertain. It is agreed<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201095">13</a></sup> that the first epithet designates the fever known as <i>quotidianus,</i> which recurs each day at the same hour, though the word is curious (lit.  ‘on the other--<i>i.e.,</i> next, day’). The <i>ubhaya-dyuḥ</i> (‘on both days’) variety appears to mean a disease recurring for two successive days, the third being free; this corresponds to the <i>rhythmus quartanus complicatus.</i><sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201095">14</a></sup> But Sāyaṇa considers that it means a fever recurring on the third day, the ‘tertian.’ The <i><a class="vei_120a" href="तृतीयक">tṛtīyaka</a>,</i> however, must be the ‘tertian’ fever,<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201095">15</a></sup> though Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1201095">16</a></sup> suggests that it may mean a fever which is fatal at the third paroxysm. Grohmann<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1201095">17</a></sup> regards the <i>vi-<a class="vei_120a" href="तृतीयक">tṛtīyaka</a></i> as equivalent to the <i>tertiana duplicata,</i> a common form in southern countries, in which the fever occurs daily, but with a correspondence in point of time or severity of attack on alternate days. Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1201095">18</a></sup> suggests that it is identical with the <i>ubhaya-dyuḥ</i> variety. The <i>sadaṃ-di</i><sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1201095">19</a></sup> type appears to be the kind later known as <i>saṃtata-jvara</i> (‘continuous fever’), in which there are attacks of several days’ duration, with an interval followed by a fresh period of attack. Fever occurred at different seasons, in the autumn (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शारद">śārada</a></i>), in the hot weather (<i>graiṣma</i>), in the rains (<i>vārṣika</i>),<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1201095">20</a></sup> but was especially prevalent in the first, as is indicated by the epithet <i><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्व">viśva</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="शारद">śārada</a>,</i> ‘occurring every autumn.’<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1201095">21</a></sup></p><p>The disease is said to arise when <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> enters the waters.<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1201095">22</a></sup> From this Weber<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1201095">23</a></sup> deduced that it was considered to be the result of a chill supervening on heat, or the influence of heat on marshy land. Grohmann<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1201095">24</a></sup> preferred to see in this connexion of the origin of the disease with Agni's entering the waters<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1201095">25</a></sup> an allusion to the fact that fever arises in the rainy season, the time when <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>, as lightning, descends to earth with the rain. Zimmer.<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_1201095">26</a></sup> who accepts this view, further refers to the prevalence  of fever in the Terai, and interprets <i>vanya,</i> an epithet of fever found in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_1201095">27</a></sup> as meaning ‘sprung from the forest,’ pointing out that fever is mentioned as prevalent among the <b>Mūjavants</b> and <b>Mahāvṛṣas,</b> two mountain tribes of the western Himālaya.<sup id="ref_120_28_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_1201095">28</a></sup> There is no trace of fever having been observed to be caused by the bite of the <i>anopheles</i> mosquito, which breeds in stagnant water: this theory has without reason been held to be known to classical Indian medicine.<sup id="ref_120_29_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29_vei_1201095">29</a></sup></p><p>Among the symptoms of <a class="vei_120a" href="तक्मन्">takman</a>, or among complications accompanying it, are mentioned ‘itch’ (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="पामन्">pāman</a></b>), ‘headache’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शीर्ष">śīrṣa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="शोक">śoka</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_30_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_30_vei_1201095">30</a></sup> ‘cough’ (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="कासिका">kāsikā</a></b>), and ‘consumption,’ or perhaps some form of itch (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="बलास">balāsa</a></b>).</p><p>It is perhaps significant that the <a class="vei_120a" href="तक्मन्">takman</a> does not appear until the Atharvaveda. It is quite possible that the Vedic Āryans, when first settled in India, did not know the disease, which would take some generations to become endemic and recognized as dangerous. What remedies they used against it is quite uncertain, for the Atharvaveda mentions only spells and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुष्ठ">kuṣṭha</a>,</b> which can hardly have been an effective remedy, though still used in later times. Fever must, even in the <a class="vei_120a" href="अथर्वन्">atharvan</a> period, have claimed many victims, or it would not be mentioned so prominently.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201095">↑</a> i. 25; v. 22; vi. 20; vii. 116; xix. 39 (<i>cf.</i> v. 4).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201095">↑</a> Av. iv. 9, 8; v. 4. 1. 9; 30, 16; ix. 8, 6; xi. 2, 22. 26, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201095">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 119; Roth, <i>Zur Litteratur und Geschishte des Weda,</i> 39, had, from the use of <a class="vei_120a" href="कुष्ठ">kuṣṭha</a> as a remedy, regarded it as denoting ‘leprosy,’ and was followed by Pictet, Kuhn's <i>Zeitschrift</i> 5, 337. Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 4, 280, thought ‘consumption’ was meant.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201095">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 381 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201095">↑</a> See also Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 451 <i>et seq.;</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 379-385, and compare the <i>jvara</i> (a non-Vedic word) of the classical medicine, Wise, <i>Hindu System of Medicine,</i> 219 <i>et seq.;</i> Jolly, <i>Medicin,</i> 70-72. Dārila and Keśava, the commentators on the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, everywhere equate <i><a class="vei_120a" href="तक्मन्">takman</a></i> and <i>jvara.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201095">↑</a> Av. i. 25, 2-4; v. 22, 2. 7. 10; vi. 20, 3; vii. 116, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201095">↑</a> Av. i. 25, 2; v. 22, 2; vi. 20, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201095">↑</a> Av. i. 25, 4; vii. 116, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201095">↑</a> <i>Ibid.</i></li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201095">↑</a> Av. i. 25, 4; v. 22, 13; xix. 39, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201095">↑</a> Av. v. 22, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201095">↑</a> Av. v. 22, 13; xix. 39, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201095">↑</a> Grohmann, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 387; Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 382; Bloomfield, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 274.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201095">↑</a> Grohmann, 388; Zimmer, 382; Bloomfield, 274. It may conceivably be the form styled Cāturthaka Viparyaya (Wise, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 232), in which the paroxysm occurs every fourth day, and lasts for two days.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201095">↑</a> Sāyaṇa on Av. i. 25, 4; Bloomfield, 451. It is the <i>jvara <a class="vei_120a" href="तृतीयक">tṛtīyaka</a></i> of Suśruta (2, 404, 7).</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1201095">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 383, quoting Hügel, Kashmir, 1, 133.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1201095">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 388.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1201095">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 451.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1201095">↑</a> Of doubtful derivation: either ‘always cutting’ (<i>of</i> Sāyaṇa on Av. xix. 39, 10), or ‘always fastening upon’ (Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i>), or ‘belonging to every day’ = <i>sadaṃdina</i> (Zimmer, 383, n.; Bloomfield, 452).</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1201095">↑</a> Av. v. 22, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1201095">↑</a> Av. ix. 8, 6; xix. 34, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1201095">↑</a> Av. 1, 25. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1201095">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 119.</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1201095">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 9, 493.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1201095">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_1201095">↑</a> <i>Of. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 384.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_1201095">↑</a> Av. vi. 20, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_1201095">↑</a> Av. v. 22, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_29_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29_vei_1201095">↑</a> Jolly, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1906, 222.</li><li id="fx_120_30_vei_1201095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_30_vei_1201095">↑</a> Av. xix. 39, 10.</p><p>For the present position of the disease in India, <i>cf.</i> the Report of the Simla Conference of 1909.</li></ol></div>

तक्वन्|తక్వన్|ತಕ್ವನ್|takvan|_vei_120@1096|_vei1_pn296
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1095">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1097">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn294">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn296">1:296</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn297">↷</a><hr><p><b>Takvan,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201096">1</a></sup> <b>Takvarī,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201096">2</a></sup> seem in the Rigveda to denote a ‘swiftflying bird.’ Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201096">3</a></sup> explains <a class="vei_120a" href="तक्वन्">takvan</a> as a swift steed.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201096">↑</a> Rv. i. 66, 2. <i>Cf.</i> i. 134, 5, and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्सारिन्">tsārin</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201096">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> i. 151, 5; x. 91, 2. But in both places the word may be adjectival.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201096">↑</a> On Rv. i. 66, 2.</li></ol></div>

तक्षक वैशालेय|తక్షక వైశాలేయ|ತಕ್ಷಕ ವೈಶಾಲೇಯ|takṣaka vaiśāleya|takShaka vaishaaleya|taxaka vaishaaleya|_vei_120@1097|_vei1_pn296
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1096">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1098">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn294">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn296">1:296</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn297">↷</a><hr><p><b>Takṣaka Vaiśāleya</b> (‘descendant of Viśālā’) is a mythical figure, mentioned as the son of <a class="vei_120a" href="विराज्">virāj</a> in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201097"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201097">1</a></sup> and as <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मणाच्छंसिन्">brāhmaṇācchaṃsin</a> priest at the snake sacrifice in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201097"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201097">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201097"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201097">↑</a> vii. 10, 29.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201097"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201097">↑</a> xxv. 15, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 35.</li></ol></div>

तक्षन्|तक्षन्|తక్షన్_౧|తక్షన్|ತಕ್ಷನ್_೧|ತಕ್ಷನ್|takṣan_1|takṣan|takShan_1|takShan|taxan_1|taxan|_vei_120@1098|_vei1_pn297
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1097">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1099">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn296">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn297">1:297</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn298">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Takṣan,</b> ‘carpenter,’ is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201098"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201098">1</a></sup> and often later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201098"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201098">2</a></sup> He was employed to do all sorts of work in wood, such as the making of chariots (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a></b>) and wagons (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अनस्">anas</a></b>). Carved work of a finer type seems also to have fallen to his lot.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201098"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201098">3</a></sup> The axe (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कुलिश">kuliśa</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201098"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201098">4</a></sup> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="परशु">paraśu</a></i>)<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201098"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201098">5</a></sup> is mentioned as one of his tools, and perhaps the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भुरिज्">bhurij</a>,</b> a word which is, however, uncertain in sense. In one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201098"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201098">6</a></sup> reference seems to be made to the pains of the carpenter in bending over his work. That the carpenters were a low caste, or formed a separate class of the people, is certainly not true of Vedic times.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201098"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201098">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201098"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201098">↑</a> ix. 112, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201098"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201098">↑</a> Av. x. 6, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 10; xviii. 13; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9, 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 27; xxx. 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 2, 1; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 3, 12; iii. 6, 4, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201098"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201098">↑</a> Rv. x. 86, 5; Av. xix. 49, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 161, 9; iii. 60. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201098"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201098">↑</a> Rv. iii. 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201098"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201098">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201098"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201098">↑</a> Rv. i. 105, 18. <i>Cf.</i> Roth, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, Erläuterungen,</i> 67; Oldenberg. <i>ṚgvedaNoten,</i> 1, 100.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201098"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201098">↑</a> Fick, <i>Die sociale Gliederung,</i> 210, n. 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 245, 253.</li></ol></div>

तक्षन्|तक्षन्|తక్షన్_౨|తక్షన్|ತಕ್ಷನ್_೨|ತಕ್ಷನ್|takṣan_2|takṣan|takShan_2|takShan|taxan_2|taxan|_vei_120@1099|_vei1_pn297
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1098">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1100">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn296">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn297">1:297</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn298">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Takṣan</b> is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201099"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201099">1</a></sup> as a teacher whose view of a certain formula was not accepted by <b>Jīvala <a class="vei_120a" href="चैलकि">cailaki</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201099"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201099">↑</a> ii. 3, 1, 31-35. <i>Cf.</i> Lévi, <i>La Doctrine du Sacrifice,</i> 140.</li></ol></div>

तक्षन्|तक्षन्|తక్షన్_౩|తక్షన్|ತಕ್ಷನ್_೩|ತಕ್ಷನ್|takṣan_3|takṣan|takShan_3|takShan|taxan_3|taxan|_vei_120@1100|_vei1_pn297
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1099">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1101">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn296">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn297">1:297</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn298">↷</a><hr><p>3. <b>Takṣan.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बृबु">bṛbu</a>.</b></p></div>

तण्डुल|తణ్డుల|ತಣ್ಡುಲ|taṇḍula|taNDula|_vei_120@1101|_vei1_pn297
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1100">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1102">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn296">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn297">1:297</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn298">↷</a><hr><p><b>Taṇḍula,</b> ‘grain,’ especially ‘rice grain,’ is mentioned very often in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201101">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201101">2</a></sup> but not in the Rigveda. This accords with the fact that rice cultivation seems hardly known in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201101">3</a></sup> Husked (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कर्ण">karṇa</a></i>) and unhusked (<i>akarṇa</i>) rice is referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201101">4</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201101">↑</a> x. 9, 26; xi. 1, 18; xii. 3, 18. 29. 30.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201101">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, x. 1, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 4, 3; ii. 5, 3, 4; v. 2, 3, 2; vi. 6, 1, 8, etc.; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="श्यामाक">śyāmāka</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="तण्डुल">taṇḍula</a>,</i> ‘millet grain,’ <i>ibid.,</i> x. 6, 3, 2; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 14, 3; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अपामार्ग">apāmārga</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="तण्डुल">taṇḍula</a>,</i> ‘grain of the <i>Achyranthes aspera,</i>’ v. 2, 4, 15, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201101">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 239. See <a class="vei_120a" href="व्रीहि">vrīhi</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201101">↑</a> i. 8, 9, 3. See Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 190.</li></ol></div>

तत|తత|ತತ|tata|_vei_120@1102|_vei1_pn298
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1101">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1103">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn297">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn298">1:298</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn299">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tata,</b> ‘dada,’ is the pet name for ‘father’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201102"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201102">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201102"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201102">2</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तात">tāta</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पितृ">pitṛ</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201102"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201102">↑</a> viii. 91, 6; ix. 112, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201102"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201102">↑</a> Av. v. 24, 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 2, 5, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 9, 7; in the vocative as a form of address, Av. viii. 4, 77; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 14; vii. 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 3, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 449.</li></ol></div>

ततामह|తతామహ|ತತಾಮಹ|tatāmaha|tataamaha|_vei_120@1103|_vei1_pn298
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1102">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1104">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn297">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn298">1:298</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn299">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tatāmaha,</b> ‘grandfather,’ is found in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201103">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201103">↑</a> v. 24, 17; viii. 4, 76. It seems to mean literally ‘great father,’ and to be an analogical formation following <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पितामह">pitāmaha</a>;</i> Delbrück, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 473, 474.</li></ol></div>

तनय|తనయ|ತನಯ|tanaya|_vei_120@1104|_vei1_pn298
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1103">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1105">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn297">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn298">1:298</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn299">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tanaya,</b> n., denotes ‘offspring,’ ‘descendants’ in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201104">1</a></sup> where also it is often used adjectivally with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तोक">toka</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201104">2</a></sup> There seems no ground for the view<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201104">3</a></sup> that <i><a class="vei_120a" href="तोक">toka</a></i> means ‘sons,’ ‘children,’ and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="तनय">tanaya</a></i> ‘grandchildren.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201104">↑</a> i. 96, 4; 183, 3; 184, 5; ii. 23, 19; vii. 1, 21, etc.; <i>tokaṃ ca tanayaṃ ca,</i> i. 92, 13; ix. 74, 5. <i>Cf.</i> vi. 25, 4; 31, 1; 66, 8; and i. 31, 12, as explained by Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 193.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201104">↑</a> Rv. i. 64, 14; 114, 6; 147, 1; 189, 2; ii. 30, 5, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201104">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, x. 7; xii. 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. Tan, tana,</i> and <i>tanas,</i> have the same sense as <a class="vei_120a" href="तनय">tanaya</a>. See Rv. vi. 46, 12; 49, 13; vii. 104, 10; viii. 68, 12, etc. (<i>tan</i>); viii. 25, 2 (<i>tana</i>); v. 70, 4 (<i>tanas</i>).</li></ol></div>

तन्ति|తన్తి|ತನ್ತಿ|tanti|_vei_120@1105|_vei1_pn298
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1104">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1106">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn297">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn298">1:298</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn299">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tanti</b> occurs in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201105"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201105">1</a></sup> where Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201105"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201105">2</a></sup> renders the plural of the word by ‘files’ of calves. But it seems rather to have the sense which it has in the later literature, of ‘cords,’ here used to fasten the calves.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201105"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201105">↑</a> vi. 24, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201105"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201105">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

तन्तु|తన్తు|ತನ್ತು|tantu|_vei_120@1106|_vei1_pn298
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1105">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1107">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn297">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn298">1:298</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn299">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tantu</b> appears properly to mean ‘thread,’ and in particular the ‘warp’ of a piece of weaving, as opposed to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ओतु">otu</a>,</b> the ‘woof.’ Both senses are found in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201106">1</a></sup> In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201106">2</a></sup> the ‘warp’ is called <i>anuchāda,</i> the ‘woof’ <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्यास">paryāsa</a>,</i> the <i>tantavaḥ</i> being the ‘threads.’ In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201106">3</a></sup>  on the other hand, the ‘warp’ is <i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राचीन-तान">prācīna-tāna</a>,</i> the ‘woof’ <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ओतु">otu</a>.</i> The threads or cords of the throne (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्य्">pary</a> ṅka</b>) are referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201106">4</a></sup></p><p>In the Rigveda the word is used only metaphorically, and this is its most frequent use even in the Brāhmanas.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201106">5</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वाण">vāṇa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201106">↑</a> xiv. 2, 51 (opposed to <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ओतु">otu</a></i>); xv. 3, 6 (the <i>prāñcaḥ</i> and <i>tiryañcaḥ</i> threads or cords of the throne of the <a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात्य">vrātya</a> [<b><a class="vei_120a" href="आसन्दी">āsandī</a></b>]).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201106">↑</a> iii. 1, 2, 18; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 8, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201106">↑</a> vi. 1, 1, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201106">↑</a> i. 5; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 20, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201106">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> It is applied to the filaments of plants in Rv. x. 134, 5; to the spider's web in Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upanisad. ii. 1, 23.</li></ol></div>

तन्त्र|తన్త్ర|ತನ್ತ್ರ|tantra|_vei_120@1107|_vei1_pn299
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1106">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1108">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn298">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn299">1:299</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn300">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tantra</b> means, like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तन्तु">tantu</a>,</b> the ‘warp’ of a piece of weaving, or more generally the ‘web’ itself. It is found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201107"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201107">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201107"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201107">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201107"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201107">↑</a> x. 71, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201107"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201107">↑</a> Av. x. 7, 42; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 5, 5, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 5; Śatapa ha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 2, 2, 22.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 254.</li></ol></div>

तपस्|तप|तपस्य|తపస్|తప|తపస్య|ತಪಸ್|ತಪ|ತಪಸ್ಯ|tapas|tapa|tapasya|_vei_120@1108|_vei1_pn299
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1107">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1109">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn298">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn299">1:299</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn300">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tapas, Tapasya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></p></div>

तपो-नित्य|तपोनित्य|तपो|नित्य|తపో-నిత్య|తపోనిత్య|తపో|నిత్య|ತಪೋ-ನಿತ್ಯ|ತಪೋನಿತ್ಯ|ತಪೋ|ನಿತ್ಯ|tapo-nitya|taponitya|tapo|nitya|_vei_120@1109|_vei1_pn299
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1108">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1110">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn298">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn299">1:299</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn300">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tapo-nitya</b> (‘constant in penance’) <a class="vei_120a" href="पौरु-शिष्टि">pauru-śiṣṭi</a> (‘descendant of Puruśiṣṭa’) is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (i. 9, 1) who believed in the value of penance (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="तपस्">tapas</a></i>).</p></div>

तयादर|తయాదర|ತಯಾದರ|tayādara|tayaadara|_vei_120@1110|_vei1_pn299
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1109">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1111">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn298">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn299">1:299</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn300">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tayādara</b> is the name of an animal only mentioned in the adjectival form <i><a class="vei_120a" href="तायादर">tāyādara</a></i> along with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परस्वन्त्">parasvant</a>,</b> ‘wild ass’(?) in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201110"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201110">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201110"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201110">↑</a> vi. 72, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 335.</li></ol></div>

तरक्षु|తరక్షు|ತರಕ್ಷು|tarakṣu|tarakShu|taraxu|_vei_120@1111|_vei1_pn299
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1110">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1112">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn298">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn299">1:299</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn300">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tarakṣu,</b> the ‘hyæna,’ is mentioned in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or horse sacrifice, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201111"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201111">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201111"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201111">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 19, 1, where Sāyaṇa explains the animal to be ‘a kind of tiger with an ass's appearance’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="व्याघ्र">vyāghra</a>-viśeṣo gardabhākāraḥ</i>); Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 21; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 40.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 81.</li></ol></div>

तरन्त|తరన్త|ತರನ್ತ|taranta|_vei_120@1112|_vei1_pn300
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1111">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1113">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn299">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn300">1:300</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn301">↷</a><hr><p><b>Taranta</b> appears, along with <b>Purumīḍha,</b> as a patron of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्यावाश्व">śyāvāśva</a></b> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201112">1</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201112">2</a></sup> and other Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201112">3</a></sup> he, together with Purumīḍha, is described as receiving gifts from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ध्वस्र">dhvasra</a></b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201112">4</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुषन्ति">puruṣanti</a>;</b> but since the receipt of gifts was forbidden to Kṣatriyas, they for the nonce became <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषिस्">ṛṣis</a>, and composed a passage in honour of the donors.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201112">4</a></sup> He, like Purumīḍha, was a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैददश्वि">vaidadaśvi</a>, or son of Vidadaśva.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201112">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201112">↑</a> v. 61, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201112">↑</a> xiii. 7, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201112">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 139; Śāṭyāyanaka <i>apud</i> Sāyaṇa on Rv. ix. 38, 3 = <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>, ii. 410.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201112">↑</a> Rv. ix. 58, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201112">↑</a> Rv. ix. 58. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201112">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. v. 61, 10; notes 2 and 3. This is merely a misunderstanding of the Rv. <i>Cf.</i> Oertel, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 39; Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 50 <i>et seq.;</i> 62, 63; Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 232, n. 1; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 353, 354, where he points out that the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> tradition, and that of the Bṛhaddevatā (v. 50-81, with Macdonell's notes), are not to be accepted as real explanations of the Rigveda.</li></ol></div>

तरु|తరు|ತರು|taru|_vei_120@1113|_vei1_pn300
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1112">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1114">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn299">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn300">1:300</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn301">↷</a><hr><p><b>Taru,</b> the usual term for ‘tree’ in classical Sanskrit, never occurs in Vedic literature, except perhaps in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201113">1</a></sup> where Sāyaṇa finds it, and where it can be so translated. But the form (<i>tarubhiḥ</i>) is probably to be interpreted otherwise.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201113">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201113">↑</a> v. 44, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201113">↑</a> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> cites <i>tarobhiḥ</i> in Rv. ii. 39, 3, as a parallel, and so Oldenberg, <i>ṚgvedaNoten,</i> 1, 341.</li></ol></div>

तरुक्ष|తరుక్ష|ತರುಕ್ಷ|tarukṣa|tarukSha|taruxa|_vei_120@1114|_vei1_pn300
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1113">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1115">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn299">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn300">1:300</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn301">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tarukṣa</b> is the name of a man in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201114"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201114">1</a></sup> who is mentioned along with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बल्बूथ">balbūtha</a>,</b> the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>,</b> in a <a class="vei_120a" href="दान">dāna</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="स्तुति">stuti</a>, or ‘Praise of Gifts.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201114"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201114">↑</a> viii. 46, 32. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 391; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 117.</li></ol></div>

तर्कु|తర్కు|ತರ್ಕು|tarku|_vei_120@1115|_vei1_pn300
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1114">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1116">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn299">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn300">1:300</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn301">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tarku,</b> ‘spindle,’ is known only in Vedic literature from the mention of it in Yāska's <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> (ii. 1) as an example of the transposition of letters, the word being derived, according to him, from the root <i>kart,</i> ‘to spin.’ </p></div>

तर्द|తర్ద|ತರ್ದ|tarda|_vei_120@1116|_vei1_pn301
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1115">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1117">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn300">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn301">1:301</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn302">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tarda,</b> ‘borer,’ occurs in a hymn of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201116"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201116">1</a></sup> enumerating insects that injure grain. Whitney<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201116"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201116">2</a></sup> suggests that a kind of mouse or rat may be meant. Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201116"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201116">3</a></sup> thought a bird was denoted.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201116"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201116">↑</a> vi. 50, 1. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201116"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201116">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 318.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201116"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201116">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 485.</li></ol></div>

तर्द्मन्|తర్ద్మన్|ತರ್ದ್ಮನ್|tardman|_vei_120@1117|_vei1_pn301
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1116">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1118">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn300">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn301">1:301</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn302">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tardman</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201117"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201117">1</a></sup> applies to the hole in the yoke (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="युग">yuga</a></b>). In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201117"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201117">2</a></sup> it designates a hole in a skin.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201117"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201117">↑</a> xiv. 1, 40.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201117"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201117">↑</a> iii. 2, 1, 2; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 26, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

तर्य|తర్య|ತರ್ಯ|tarya|_vei_120@1118|_vei1_pn301
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1117">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1119">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn300">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn301">1:301</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn302">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tarya</b> is, according to Sāyaṇa, the name of a man in one passage of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201118"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201118">1</a></sup> But the verse is hopelessly obscure.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201118"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201118">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201118"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201118">↑</a> v, 44, 12. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 158, 159.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201118"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201118">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 342.</li></ol></div>

तलाश|తలాశ|ತಲಾಶ|talāśa|talaasha|_vei_120@1119|_vei1_pn301
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1118">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1120">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn300">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn301">1:301</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn302">↷</a><hr><p><b>Talāśa</b> is the name of a tree in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201119">1</a></sup> Whitney<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201119">2</a></sup> suggests that it may be the same as <i>tālīśa (Flacourtia cataphracta).</i></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201119">↑</a> vi. 15, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201119">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 291.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 62.</li></ol></div>

तल्प|తల్ప|ತಲ್ಪ|talpa|_vei_120@1120|_vei1_pn301
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1119">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1121">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn300">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn301">1:301</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn302">↷</a><hr><p><b>Talpa</b> is the regular term for ‘bed’ or ‘couch’ from the Rigveda and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201120">1</a></sup> onwards.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201120">2</a></sup> One made of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उदुम्बर">udumbara</a></b> wood is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201120">3</a></sup> The violation of the bed of a Guru, or teacher, is already mentioned in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201120">4</a></sup> while the adjective <i>talpya,</i> ‘born in the nuptial couch,’ denotes ‘legitimate’ in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201120">5</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201120">↑</a> Rv. vii. 55, 8; Av. v. 17, 12; xiv. 2, 31. 41.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201120">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 6, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 2, 5, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxiii. 4, 2; xxv. 1, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201120">↑</a> i. 2, 6, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201120">↑</a> v. 10, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201120">↑</a> xiii. 1, 6, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 154.</li></ol></div>

तलव|తలవ|ತಲವ|talava|_vei_120@1121|_vei1_pn302
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1120">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1122">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn301">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn302">1:302</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn303">↷</a><hr><p><b>Talava</b> in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha, or human sacrifice, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201121"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201121">1</a></sup> denotes a ‘musician’ of some kind.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201121"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201121">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 20; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 15, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 83, n. 15.</li></ol></div>

तष्टृ|తష్టృ|ತಷ್ಟೃ|taṣṭṛ|taShTR|taShTRi|taShTRu|taShTRRi|_vei_120@1122|_vei1_pn302
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1121">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1123">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn301">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn302">1:302</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn303">↷</a><hr><p><b>Taṣṭṛ</b> is found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201122"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201122">1</a></sup> in the sense of ‘carpenter,’ like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तक्षन्">takṣan</a>,</b> which is from the same root <i>takṣ,</i> ‘to fashion.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201122"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201122">↑</a> i. 61, 4; 105, 18; 130, 4; iii. 38, 1; vii. 32, 20; x. 93, 12; 119, 5. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 21.</li></ol></div>

तसर|తసర|ತಸರ|tasara|_vei_120@1123|_vei1_pn302
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1122">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1124">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn301">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn302">1:302</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn303">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tasara</b> denotes the weaver's ‘shuttle’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201123"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201123">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201123"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201123">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201123"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201123">↑</a> x. 130, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201123"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201123">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 83; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 11, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxviii. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 4, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 254.</li></ol></div>

तस्कर|తస్కర|ತಸ್ಕರ|taskara|_vei_120@1124|_vei1_pn302
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1123">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1125">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn301">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn302">1:302</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn303">↷</a><hr><p><b>Taskara</b> occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201124">1</a></sup> and frequently later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201124">2</a></sup> denoting ‘thief’ or ‘robber.’ It appears to be practically synonymous with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्तेन">stena</a>,</b> in connexion with which it is often mentioned.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201124">3</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="स्तेन">stena</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तस्कर">taskara</a> are contrasted in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201124">4</a></sup> with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मलिम्लु">malimlu</a>,</b> who is a burglar or house-breaker, while they are highwaymen, or, as the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201124">5</a></sup> puts it, ‘men who haunt the woods and risk their lives’ (<i>tanū-tyajā <a class="vei_120a" href="वनर्">vanar</a>-gū</i>). In another passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201124">6</a></sup> however, the dog is told to bark at the <a class="vei_120a" href="तस्कर">taskara</a> or the <a class="vei_120a" href="स्तेन">stena</a>, which clearly points to an attempt at house-breaking. The thief goes about at night,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201124">7</a></sup> and knows the paths<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201124">8</a></sup> on which he attacks his victim. In one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201124">9</a></sup> the use of cords is mentioned, but whether to bind the thieves when captured, or to bind the  victim, is not clear.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201124">10</a></sup> The Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201124">11</a></sup> refers to the <a class="vei_120a" href="स्तेन">stena</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तस्कर">taskara</a> as cattle and horse thieves.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201124">12</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201124">↑</a> i. 191, 5; vi. 27, 3; vii. 55, 3; viii. 29, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201124">↑</a> Av. iv. 3, 2; xix. 47, 7; 50, 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xi. 77-78; xii. 62; xvi. 21, etc; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201124">↑</a> Rv. vii. 55. 3; Av. xix. 47, 7; 50, 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xi. 79; xvi. 21, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201124">↑</a> xi. 79 (the <a class="vei_120a" href="मलिम्लु">malimlu</a> is <i>janeṣu,</i> ‘among men’; the others <i>vane,</i> ‘in the forest’). <i>Cf.</i> for the <a class="vei_120a" href="मलिम्लु">malimlu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 3, 2, 6; Atharvaveda, xix. 49, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201124">↑</a> x. 4, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201124">↑</a> vii. 55, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201124">↑</a> Rv. i. 191, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201124">↑</a> Rv. viii. 29, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201124">↑</a> x. 4, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201124">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 178, n.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201124">↑</a> xix. 50, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Rv. x. 97, 10 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्तेन">stena</a></i>).</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201124">↑</a> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 984.</li></ol></div>

तायु|తాయు|ತಾಯು|tāyu|taayu|_vei_120@1125|_vei1_pn303
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1124">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1126">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn302">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn303">1:303</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn304">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tāyu</b> was another name for thief, perhaps of a less distinguished and more domestic character than the highwayman, for though he is referred to as a cattle-thief,<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201125">13</a></sup> he is also alluded to as a stealer of clothes (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वस्त्र">vastra</a>-mathi</i>)<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201125">14</a></sup> and as a debtor.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201125">15</a></sup> In one passage the Tāyus are said to disappear at the coming of dawn (which is elsewhere called <i>yāvayad-dveṣas,</i> ‘driving away hostile beings,’ and <i>ṛta-pā,</i> ‘guardian of order’), like the stars of heaven (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1201125">16</a></sup></p><p>In the <a class="vei_120a" href="शतरुद्रिय">śatarudriya</a> litany of the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1201125">17</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="रुद्र">rudra</a> is called lord of assailers (<i>ā-vyādhin</i>), thieves (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्तेन">stena</a></i>), robbers (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="तस्कर">taskara</a></i>), pickpockets (<i>stāyu</i>), stealers (<i>muṣṇant</i>), and cutters (<i>vi-kṛnta</i>); and designations of sharpers (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="गृत्स">gṛtsa</a></i>) and bands (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="गण">gaṇa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात">vrāta</a></i>), apparently of robbers, are mentioned.<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1201125">18</a></sup> It is therefore not surprising that the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1201125">19</a></sup> should contain many prayers for safety at home or on the way, or that the Atharvaveda should devote several hymns to night<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1201125">20</a></sup> chiefly for protection against the evil doings of thieves and robbers.</p><p>Pischel<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1201125">21</a></sup> suggests that in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1201125">22</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a></b> is represented as a burglar, but the admits that, since <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> was attacking the house of his father <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a>, he was only seeking to obtain what he may have regarded as his own. But the interpretation of the hymn is not certain.<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1201125">23</a></sup></p><p>Sāyaṇa's explanation of one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1201125">24</a></sup> as  referring to professional cattle-trackers, like the Khojis of the Panjab, seems quite probable.<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1201125">25</a></sup></p><p>The punishment of thieves appears primarily to have been left to the action of the robbed. The practice of binding them in stocks<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_1201125">26</a></sup> seems clearly referred to. But later, at any rate-and in all probability earlier also, as in othe countries--a more severe penalty could be exacted, and death inflicted by the king.<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_1201125">27</a></sup> There is no hint in Vedic literature of the mode of conviction; a fire ordeal is not known to the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_28_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_1201125">28</a></sup> and the ordeal known to the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_29_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29_vei_1201125">29</a></sup> is not said to be used in the case of theft. No doubt the stolen property was recovered by the person robbed if he could obtain it. Nothing is known as to what happened if the property had passed from the actual thief into the possession of another person.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="13"><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201125">↑</a> Rv. i. 65, 1; vii. 86, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201125">↑</a> Rv. iv. 38, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201125">↑</a> Rv. vi. 12, 5. No doubt this theft is the result of despair at being in debt, which might lead to loss of liberty (<a class="vei_120a" href="ऋण">ṛṇa</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1201125">↑</a> Rv. i. 50, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 47.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1201125">↑</a> xvi. 20, 21. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 4, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 13; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1201125">↑</a> xvi. 25.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1201125">↑</a> i. 129, 9; ii. 23, 16; vi. 24, 10; 41, 5; 51, 15; x. 63, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1201125">↑</a> Av. xix. 47-50.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1201125">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 55, 56. Contrast 1, 106.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1201125">↑</a> Rv. vii. 55.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1201125">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Aufrecht, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 337 <i>et seq.;</i> Lanman, <i>Sanskrit Reader,</i> 370; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 308; Bṛhaddevatā, vii. 11 <i>et seq.,</i> with Macdonell's notes.</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1201125">↑</a> vi. 54, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1201125">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 182, 183, citing Elliot, <i>Memoirs,</i> 1, 276; Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 123.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_1201125">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 24, 13, 15; vii. 86, 5; Av. vi. 63, 3 = 84, 4; 115, 2. 3; 121; xix. 47, 9; 50, 1, all of which passages are cited by Zimmer, 181, 182, to prove this practice. But it must be noted that Rv. vii. 86, 5, alone is not at all conclusive evidence, though Av. xix. 47, 9; 50, 1 (<i>drupade āhan</i>), probably mean the same thing. Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 976, 983, renders the passages as referring to casting a thief into a snare, and Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 106, makes Rv. vii. 86, 5, refer to the cattle-thief (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पशु">paśu</a>-tṛp</i>) taking away the rope from the calf he means to steal. For Rv. x. 4, 6, see above, n. 10. The German and Slavonic parallels cited by Zimmer, 182, n., support his view. See also for a similar punishment in case of debt, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋण">ṛṇa</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_1201125">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 4345; Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 9, 25, 4. 5; Jolly, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 124.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_1201125">↑</a> Av. ii. 12 was so interpreted by Schlagintweit, <i>Die Gottesurtheile der Inder,</i> 9 <i>et seq.</i> (1866); Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 164 <i>et seq.;</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 445; Zimmer, 183 <i>et seq.;</i> but see Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 11, 330 <i>et seq.; Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 294-296; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 54; Grill, <i>Hundert Lieder,</i>^2 47, 85; Jolly, <i>op <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 146.</li><li id="fx_120_29_vei_1201125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29_vei_1201125">↑</a> vi. 16; Jolly, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

तस्तुव|तस्रुव|తస్తువ|తస్రువ|ತಸ್ತುವ|ತಸ್ರುವ|tastuva|tasruva|_vei_120@1126|_vei1_pn304
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1125">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1127">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn303">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn304">1:304</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn305">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tastuva,</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तस्रुव">tasruva</a>,</b> as the Paippalāda recension has it, is the name of a remedy against snake poison, and is mentioned along with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ताबुव">tābuva</a></b> in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201126"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201126">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201126"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201126">↑</a> v. 13, 10, 11. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 428; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 244.</li></ol></div>

ताजद्-भंग|ताजद्भंग|ताजद्|भंग|ताजद्-भङ्ग|ताजद्भङ्ग|भङ्ग|తాజద్-భంగ|తాజద్భంగ|తాజద్|భంగ|తాజద్-భఙ్గ|తాజద్భఙ్గ|భఙ్గ|ತಾಜದ್-ಭಂಗ|ತಾಜದ್ಭಂಗ|ತಾಜದ್|ಭಂಗ|ತಾಜದ್-ಭಙ್ಗ|ತಾಜದ್ಭಙ್ಗ|ಭಙ್ಗ|tājad-bhaṅga|tājadbhaṅga|tājad|bhaṅga|taajad-bhaMga|taajadbhaMga|taajad|bhaMga|taajad-bhanga|taajadbhanga|bhanga|_vei_120@1127|_vei1_pn305
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1126">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1128">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn304">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn305">1:305</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn306">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tājad-bhaṅga</b> (‘easily broken’) is apparently the name of a tree or plant in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201127"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201127">1</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201127"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201127">2</a></sup> treats it as a compound word, and its commentator makes it out to be the castor-oil plant (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="एरण्ड">eraṇḍa</a></i>). Whitney,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201127"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201127">3</a></sup> however, treats the expression as two separate words, and thinks that the passage means ‘may they be broken suddenly (<i>tājat</i>) like hemp (<i>bhañga</i>).’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201127"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201127">↑</a> viii. 8, 3 (a battle hymn).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201127"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201127">↑</a> xvi 14. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield's edition, xliv; <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 583, 584; Caland, <i>Altindisches Zauberritual,</i> 35; Lanman in Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 502; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 72.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201127"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201127">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 504.</li></ol></div>

ताण्ड|తాణ్డ|ತಾಣ್ಡ|tāṇḍa|taaNDa|_vei_120@1128|_vei1_pn305
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1127">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1129">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn304">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn305">1:305</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn306">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tāṇḍa</b> seems to be the name of a sage to whose school belonged the <a class="vei_120a" href="ताण्ड">tāṇḍa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> mentioned in the Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201128"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201128">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201128"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201128">↑</a> vii. 10, 17. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 49.</li></ol></div>

ताण्ड-विन्द|ताण्डविन्द|ताण्ड|विन्द|ताण्ड-विन्दव|ताण्डविन्दव|विन्दव|తాణ్డ-విన్ద|తాణ్డవిన్ద|తాణ్డ|విన్ద|తాణ్డ-విన్దవ|తాణ్డవిన్దవ|విన్దవ|ತಾಣ್ಡ-ವಿನ್ದ|ತಾಣ್ಡವಿನ್ದ|ತಾಣ್ಡ|ವಿನ್ದ|ತಾಣ್ಡ-ವಿನ್ದವ|ತಾಣ್ಡವಿನ್ದವ|ವಿನ್ದವ|tāṇḍa-vinda|tāṇḍavinda|tāṇḍa|vinda|tāṇḍa-vindava|tāṇḍavindava|vindava|taaNDa-vinda|taaNDavinda|taaNDa|taaNDa-vindava|taaNDavindava|_vei_120@1129|_vei1_pn305
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1128">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1130">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn304">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn305">1:305</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn306">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tāṇḍa-vinda,</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ताण्ड-विन्दव">tāṇḍa-vindava</a>,</b> is the name of a teacher mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201129"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201129">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201129"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201129">↑</a> viii. 10. The manuscripts differ as to the form of the name.</li></ol></div>

ताण्डि|తాణ్డి|ತಾಣ್ಡಿ|tāṇḍi|taaNDi|_vei_120@1130|_vei1_pn305
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1129">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1131">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn304">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn305">1:305</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn306">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tāṇḍi</b> occurs as the name of a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बादरायण">bādarāyaṇa</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) at the end of the Sāmavidhāna <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201130">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201130">↑</a> See Konow's Translation, 80, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

ताण्ड्य|తాణ్డ్య|ತಾಣ್ಡ್ಯ|tāṇḍya|taaNDya|_vei_120@1131|_vei1_pn305
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1130">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1132">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn304">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn305">1:305</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn306">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tāṇḍya</b> is the name of a teacher in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201131"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201131">1</a></sup> quoted on a point bearing on the Agniciti, or piling of the sacred fire. He is also mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201131"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201131">2</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="ताण्ड्य">tāṇḍya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="महाब्राह्मण">mahābrāhmaṇa</a> or <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201131"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201131">3</a></sup> of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a> represents the school of the Tāṇḍins. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201131"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201131">↑</a> vi. 1, 2, 25. <i>Cf.</i> Lévi, <i>La Doctrine du Sacrifice,</i> 140.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201131"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201131">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373, 384.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201131"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201131">↑</a> Edited in the <i>Bibliotheca Indica</i> Series, 1869-74. See Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 66 <i>et seq.,</i> 74, 133; Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 203, 210; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 23 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

तात|తాత|ತಾತ|tāta|taata|_vei_120@1132|_vei1_pn306
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1131">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1133">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn305">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn306">1:306</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn307">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tāta,</b> apparently ‘dada's boy,’ an affectionate term of address by a father (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तत">tata</a></b>) to a son, is found in the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201132"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201132">1</a></sup> occurring in the vocative only. But in the sense of ‘father,’ through confusion with <a class="vei_120a" href="तत">tata</a>, it occurs also as early as the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201132"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201132">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201132"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201132">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 14, 4; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 6; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 4, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201132"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201132">↑</a> i. 3, 3, where <a class="vei_120a" href="तत">tata</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="तात">tāta</a> are given as variant forms of the address of the child to the father. Little, <i>Grammatical Index,</i> 75, takes <a class="vei_120a" href="तात">tāta</a> to mean ‘father’ primarily, but this seems unlikely.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 449, 454.</li></ol></div>

तादुरी|तादुरि|తాదురీ|తాదురి|ತಾದುರೀ|ತಾದುರಿ|tādurī|tāduri|taadurii|taaduri|taaduree|_vei_120@1133|_vei1_pn306
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1132">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1134">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn305">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn306">1:306</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn307">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tādurī</b> is mentioned in a verse of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201133"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201133">1</a></sup> together with the female frog (<b>Maṇḍūkī</b>). Some similar animal must be meant,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201133"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201133">2</a></sup> but Roth,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201133"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201133">3</a></sup> with the commentator <a class="vei_120a" href="दुर्ग">durga</a> on the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201133"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201133">4</a></sup> regards the word as an adjective describing the frog.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201133"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201133">↑</a> iv. 15, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201133"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201133">↑</a> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 175.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201133"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201133">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> where he suggests <i>tāḍurī,</i> from the root <i>taḍ,</i> ‘beat,’ with the sense of ‘splashing.’</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201133"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201133">↑</a> ix. 7.</li></ol></div>

तान्व|तान्व|తాన్వ_౧|తాన్వ|ತಾನ್ವ_೧|ತಾನ್ವ|tānva_1|tānva|taanva_1|taanva|_vei_120@1134|_vei1_pn306
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1133">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1135">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn305">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn306">1:306</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn307">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Tānva</b> in an obscure passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201134"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201134">1</a></sup> seems to mean a ‘legitimate son,’ who is said not to leave the heritage (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="रिक्थ">riktha</a></i>) of his father to his sister (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="जामि">jāmi</a></i>). The exact meaning is probably unascertainable,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201134"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201134">2</a></sup> but the passage may convey a statement of what was no doubt the fact, that the daughter had no share in the paternal inheritance; her brother had to provide for her during her life if she remained unmarried, but she had no independent portion.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201134"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201134">3</a></sup> (See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दाय">dāya</a>.</b>)</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201134"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201134">↑</a> iii. 31, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201134"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201134">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 1, 348; Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a> Noten,</i> 1, 240; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 34.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201134"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201134">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 87, for the modern law of the Panjab.</li></ol></div>

तान्व|तान्व|తాన్వ_౨|తాన్వ|ತಾನ್ವ_೨|ತಾನ್ವ|tānva_2|tānva|taanva_2|taanva|_vei_120@1135|_vei1_pn306
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1134">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1136">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn305">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn306">1:306</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn307">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Tānva</b> appears to be a patronymic, ‘descendant of Tanva,’ in a verse of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201135"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201135">1</a></sup> Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201135"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201135">2</a></sup> thinks that it is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दुःशीम">duḥśīma</a>,</b> who is mentioned in the preceding verse, but this is uncertain. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201135"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201135">↑</a> x. 93, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201135"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201135">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 166.</li></ol></div>

तापस|तापस|తాపస_౧|తాపస|ತಾಪಸ_೧|ತಾಪಸ|tāpasa_1|tāpasa|taapasa_1|taapasa|_vei_120@1136|_vei1_pn307
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1135">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1137">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn306">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn307">1:307</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn308">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Tāpasa,</b> ‘ascetic,’ is not found in Vedic literature till the Upaniṣads.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201136"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201136">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201136"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201136">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 3, 22. <i>Cf.</i> Fick, <i>Die sociale Gliederung,</i> 40.</li></ol></div>

तापस|तापस|తాపస_౨|తాపస|ತಾಪಸ_೨|ತಾಪಸ|tāpasa_2|tāpasa|taapasa_2|taapasa|_vei_120@1137|_vei1_pn307
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1136">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1138">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn306">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn307">1:307</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn308">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Tāpasa</b> is a name of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दत्त">datta</a></b> who was <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a> priest at the snake festival described in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xxv. 15).</p></div>

ताबुव|తాబువ|ತಾಬುವ|tābuva|taabuva|_vei_120@1138|_vei1_pn307
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1137">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1139">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn306">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn307">1:307</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn308">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tābuva</b> is the name in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201138"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201138">1</a></sup> of a remedy against snake poison. The Paippalāda recension has Tāvuca instead. Weber<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201138"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201138">2</a></sup> thinks that the original form was Tāthuva, from the root <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्था">sthā</a>,</i> ‘stand,’ and that it meant ‘stopping’; but this is hardly probable.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201138"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201138">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201138"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201138">↑</a> v. 13, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201138"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201138">↑</a> Proceedings of the Berlin Academy, 1896, 681.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201138"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201138">↑</a> Barth. <i>Revne del'Histoire des Religions,</i> 39, 26. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 244; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 428.</li></ol></div>

तायादर|తాయాదర|ತಾಯಾದರ|tāyādara|taayaadara|_vei_120@1139|_vei1_pn307
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1138">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1140">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn306">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn307">1:307</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn308">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tāyādara,</b> ‘belonging to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तयादर">tayādara</a></b>’ (Av. vi. 72, 2).</p></div>

तायु|తాయు|ತಾಯು|tāyu|taayu|_vei_120@1140|_vei1_pn307
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1139">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1141">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn306">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn307">1:307</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn308">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tāyu,</b> ‘thief,’ is mentioned several times in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201140"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201140">1</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तस्कर">taskara</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201140"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201140">↑</a> i. 50, 2; 65, 1; iv. 38, 5; v. 15, 5; 52, 12; vi. 12, 5; vii. 86, 5 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पशु">paśu</a>-tṛṭ,</i> ‘cattle-thief,’ see Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 106).</li></ol></div>

तारका|తారకా|ತಾರಕಾ|tārakā|taarakaa|_vei_120@1141|_vei1_pn307
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1140">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1142">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn306">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn307">1:307</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn308">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tārakā</b> is found several times in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201141"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201141">1</a></sup> denoting a star. The masculine form Tāraka occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201141"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201141">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201141"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201141">↑</a> ii. 8, 1; iii. 7, 4; vi. 121, 3; xix. 49, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201141"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201141">↑</a> i. 5, 2, 5.</li></ol></div>

तारुक्ष्य|తారుక్ష్య|ತಾರುಕ್ಷ್ಯ|tārukṣya|taarukShya|taaruxya|_vei_120@1142|_vei1_pn307
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1141">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1143">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn306">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn307">1:307</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn308">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tārukṣya</b> is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201142"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201142">1</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyakas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201142"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201142">2</a></sup> In the former passage <a class="vei_120a" href="तार्क्ष्य">tārkṣya</a> is a variant reading, and in the latter <a class="vei_120a" href="तार्क्ष्य">tārkṣya</a> is read, but this is probably only due to confusion with <a class="vei_120a" href="तार्क्ष्य">tārkṣya</a>, the reputed author of a Rigvedic hymn.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201142"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201142">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201142"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201142">↑</a> iii. 1, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201142"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201142">↑</a> vii. 19.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201142"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201142">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 5, 2, with Keith's note; Sāṅkhāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> xi. 14, 28; xii. 11, 12, Āśvalāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ix. 1.</li></ol></div>

तार्क्ष्य|తార్క్ష్య|ತಾರ್ಕ್ಷ್ಯ|tārkṣya|taarkShya|taarxya|_vei_120@1143|_vei1_pn308
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1142">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1144">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn307">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn308">1:308</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn309">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tārkṣya</b> is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201143"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201143">1</a></sup> as a divine steed, apparently the sun conceived as a horse.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201143"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201143">2</a></sup> But Foy,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201143"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201143">3</a></sup> judging by the name, apparently a patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तृक्षि">tṛkṣi</a>,</b> who is known from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201143"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201143">4</a></sup> onwards as a descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a>,</b> thinks that a real steed, the property of <a class="vei_120a" href="तृक्षि">tṛkṣi</a>, is meant; but this is not very probable.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201143"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201143">5</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तारुक्ष्य">tārukṣya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201143"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201143">↑</a> i. 89, 6; x. 178.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201143"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201143">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 149.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201143"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201143">↑</a> Kuhn's <i>Zeitschrift,</i> 11, 366, 367.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201143"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201143">↑</a> viii. 22, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201143"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201143">↑</a> In <a class="vei_120a" href="खिल">khila</a>, ii. 4, 1, <a class="vei_120a" href="तार्क्ष्य">tārkṣya</a> is represented as a bird (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वायस">vāyasa</a></i>), also a symbol of the sun. In the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xv. 18, he is mentioned with Ariṣṭanemi, originally an epithet of his (Rv. i. 89, 6; x. 178, 1), as a person, and in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 3, 13, he appears as Vaipaśyata (<a class="vei_120a" href="वैपश्चित">vaipaścita</a> in Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 7), king of the birds (<i>cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 369).</li></ol></div>

तार्प्य|తార్ప్య|ತಾರ್ಪ್ಯ|tārpya|taarpya|_vei_120@1144|_vei1_pn308
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1143">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1145">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn307">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn308">1:308</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn309">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tārpya</b> denotes, in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201144">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201144">2</a></sup> a garment made of some material, the nature of which is uncertain. The commentators on the Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201144">3</a></sup> suggest that a linen garment, or one thrice soaked in ghee, or one made of the <i>tṛpā</i> or of the <i>triparṇa</i> plant, is meant: it is doubtful whether the sense was known even to the author of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> himself. Goldstücker's<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201144">4</a></sup> rendering of the word is ‘silken garment,’ which Eggeling<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201144">5</a></sup> is inclined to accept.^6</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201144">↑</a> xviii. 4, 31.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201144">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 4, 11, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 7, 1; 7, 6, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxi. 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 5, 20; Kātyāyana Śranta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 5, 7 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 12, 19.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201144">↑</a> v. 3, 5, 20. <i>Cf.</i> Kātyāyana, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Sāyaṇa on Av., <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 879.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201144">↑</a> Dictionary, <i>s.v. abhiṣecanīya.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201144">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 85, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

तार्ष्टाघ|తార్ష్టాఘ|ತಾರ್ಷ್ಟಾಘ|tārṣṭāgha|taarShTaagha|_vei_120@1145|_vei1_pn308
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1144">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1146">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn307">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn308">1:308</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn309">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tārṣṭāgha,</b> a species of tree, is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201145">1</a></sup> while the adjective formed from it, <i>tārṣṭāghī,</i> ‘derived from the <a class="vei_120a" href="तार्ष्टाघ">tārṣṭāgha</a> tree,’ is found in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201145">2</a></sup> Weber<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201145">3</a></sup> thinks that the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सर्षप">sarṣapa</a>,</i> or mustard plant, is meant. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201145">↑</a> xxv. 23.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201145">↑</a> v. 29, 15. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney's note in his Translation of the Atharvaveda.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201145">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 280.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 62.</li></ol></div>

titaü|_vei_120@1146|_vei1_pn309
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1145">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1147">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn308">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn309">1:309</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn310">↷</a><hr><p><b>Titaü</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201146"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201146">1</a></sup> is found once in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201146"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201146">2</a></sup> denoting a ‘sieve,’ or perhaps ‘winnowing fan,’ which was used for purifying corn (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सक्तु">saktu</a></i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201146"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201146">↑</a> On the peculiar form of this word, <i>of.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> 20, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201146"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201146">↑</a> x. 71, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 238.</li></ol></div>

तित्तिर|तित्तिरि|తిత్తిర|తిత్తిరి|ತಿತ್ತಿರ|ತಿತ್ತಿರಿ|tittira|tittiri|_vei_120@1147|_vei1_pn309
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1146">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1148">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn308">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn309">1:309</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn310">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tittira, Tittiri,</b> is the name of the partridge in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201147"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201147">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201147"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201147">2</a></sup> being presumably an onomatopoetic formation. The bird is described as having variegated plumage (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="बहु">bahu</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="रूप">rūpa</a></i>). It is usually associated with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कपिञ्जल">kapiñjala</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कलविंक">kalaviṅka</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201147"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201147">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 1, 2; v. 5, 16, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 4, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 10; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 30, 36. The form <a class="vei_120a" href="तित्तिर">tittira</a> occurs in the Maitrāyaṇī Samhitā, iii. 14, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201147"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201147">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 3, 5; v. 5, 4, 6; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 154, 6 (Oertel, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 181).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 91; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 251.</li></ol></div>

तिथि|తిథి|ತಿಥಿ|tithi|_vei_120@1148|_vei1_pn309
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1147">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1149">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn308">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn309">1:309</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn310">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tithi,</b> as the name of a lunar day, the thirtieth part of a lunar month of rather over twenty-seven days, is only found in the later Sūtras,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201148"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201148">1</a></sup> being completely unknown to the Brāhmaṇas, in which the only day is the natural one.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201148"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201148">2</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201148"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201148">↑</a> Gobhila <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 1, 13; ii. 8, 12, 20; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 25; v. 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201148"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201148">↑</a> Thibaut, <i>Astronomie, Astrologie und Mathematik,</i> 7, 8.</li></ol></div>

तिमिर्घ दौरे-श्रुत|तिमिर्घ दौरेश्रुत|तिमिर्घ दौरे|श्रुत|తిమిర్ఘ దౌరే-శ్రుత|తిమిర్ఘ దౌరేశ్రుత|తిమిర్ఘ దౌరే|శ్రుత|ತಿಮಿರ್ಘ ದೌರೇ-ಶ್ರುತ|ತಿಮಿರ್ಘ ದೌರೇಶ್ರುತ|ತಿಮಿರ್ಘ ದೌರೇ|ಶ್ರುತ|timirgha daure-śruta|timirgha daureśruta|timirgha daure|śruta|timirgha daure-shruta|timirgha daureshruta|shruta|_vei_120@1149|_vei1_pn309
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1148">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1150">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn308">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn309">1:309</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn310">↷</a><hr><p><b>Timirgha <a class="vei_120a" href="दौरे">daure</a>-śruta</b> (‘descendant of Dūreśruta’) is mentioned as Agnīdh (‘fire-kindling’) priest at the snake sacrifice described in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201149"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201149">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201149"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201149">↑</a> xxv. 15. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 35.</li></ol></div>

तिरश्च|తిరశ్చ|ತಿರಶ್ಚ|tiraśca|tirashcha|_vei_120@1150|_vei1_pn309
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1149">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1151">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn308">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn309">1:309</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn310">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tiraśca</b> is read in some manuscripts of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201150"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201150">1</a></sup> in the description of the Vrātya's throne (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="आसन्दी">āsandī</a></b>), meaning ‘the cross-pieces.’ But the reading should be <i>tiraścye,</i> which is adjectival, and is used in the same sense. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201150"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201150">↑</a> xv. 3, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 776; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Araṇyaka,</i> 19, n. 3.</li></ol></div>

तिरश्च-राजि|तिरश्चराजि|तिरश्च|राजि|తిరశ్చ-రాజి|తిరశ్చరాజి|తిరశ్చ|రాజి|ತಿರಶ್ಚ-ರಾಜಿ|ತಿರಶ್ಚರಾಜಿ|ತಿರಶ್ಚ|ರಾಜಿ|tiraśca-rāji|tiraścarāji|tiraśca|rāji|tirashcha-raaji|tirashcharaaji|tirashcha|raaji|_vei_120@1151|_vei1_pn310
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1150">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1152">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn309">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn310">1:310</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn311">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tiraśca-rāji,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201151"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201151">1</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तिरश्चि">tiraści</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="राजि">rāji</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201151"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201151">2</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तिरश्चीन">tiraścīna</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="राजि">rāji</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201151"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201151">3</a></sup> are variant forms of a name for ‘snake’ (lit., ‘striped across’), found in the later Saṃhitās.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201151"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201151">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 10, 2; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 94, 95, reports this form from the Av., but the text and Roth (St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i>) read <i><a class="vei_120a" href="तिरश्चि">tiraści</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="राजि">rāji</a></i> there.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201151"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201151">↑</a> Av. iii. 27, 2; vi. 56, 2; vii. 56, 1; x. 4, 13; xii. 3, 56.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201151"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201151">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 13, 21; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xii. 27.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 488, 553; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 68, n. 2; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 295-297.</li></ol></div>

तिरश्ची|तिरश्चि|తిరశ్చీ|తిరశ్చి|ತಿರಶ್ಚೀ|ತಿರಶ್ಚಿ|tiraścī|tiraści|tirashchii|tirashchi|tirashchee|_vei_120@1152|_vei1_pn310
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1151">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1153">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn309">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn310">1:310</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn311">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tiraścī</b> is, according to the Anukramaṇī, the author of a Rigvedic hymn<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201152"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201152">1</a></sup> in which he appeals to <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> to hear his call. The <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201152"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201152">2</a></sup> adopts this view of the name, and mentions a <a class="vei_120a" href="तिरश्ची">tiraścī</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="आंगिरस">āṅgirasa</a>. But Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201152"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201152">3</a></sup> thinks that the word is not a proper name at all.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201152"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201152">↑</a> viii. 95, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201152"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201152">↑</a> xii. 6, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201152"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201152">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 115. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 5, 187; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 90; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> p. 273.</li></ol></div>

तिरश्चीन-वंश|तिरश्चीनवंश|तिरश्चीन|वंश|తిరశ్చీన-వంశ|తిరశ్చీనవంశ|తిరశ్చీన|వంశ|ತಿರಶ್ಚೀನ-ವಂಶ|ತಿರಶ್ಚೀನವಂಶ|ತಿರಶ್ಚೀನ|ವಂಶ|tiraścīna-vaṃśa|tiraścīnavaṃśa|tiraścīna|vaṃśa|tirashchiina-vaMsha|tirashchiinavaMsha|tirashchiina|vaMsha|tirashcheena-vaMsha|tirashcheenavaMsha|tirashcheena|_vei_120@1153|_vei1_pn310
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1152">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1154">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn309">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn310">1:310</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn311">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tiraścīna-vaṃśa,</b> ‘cross-beam,’ is used to denote a ‘beehive’ in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201153"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201153">1</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201153"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201153">↑</a> iii. 1, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Little, <i>Grammatical Index,</i> 75.</li></ol></div>

तिरिन्दिर|తిరిన్దిర|ತಿರಿನ್ದಿರ|tirindira|_vei_120@1154|_vei1_pn310
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1153">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1155">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn309">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn310">1:310</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn311">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tirindira</b> is mentioned in a Dānastuti, or ‘Praise of Gifts,’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201154"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201154">1</a></sup> as having, along with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्शु">parśu</a>,</b> bestowed gifts on the singer. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201154"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201154">2</a></sup> this statement is represented by a tale that the <a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वत्स">vatsa</a> obtained a gift from <a class="vei_120a" href="तिरिन्दिर">tirindira</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पारशव्य">pāraśavya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="तिरिन्दिर">tirindira</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="पर्शु">parśu</a> being in this version thus treated as one and the same man. Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201154"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201154">3</a></sup> sees in the Rigvedic passage a proof that the Yadus had gained a victory over <a class="vei_120a" href="तिरिन्दिर">tirindira</a>, and gave a part of the booty to the singers; but there is no proof whatever of the correctness of this interpretation, which Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201154"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201154">4</a></sup> shows to be most unlikely. <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a> princes must be meant by <a class="vei_120a" href="तिरिन्दिर">tirindira</a> and  <a class="vei_120a" href="पर्शु">parśu</a>, though Weber<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201154"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201154">5</a></sup> thinks that the singers were Yadus, not the princes. The latter he holds to have been Iranian (<i>cf.</i>, and see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्शु">parśu</a></b>), and he thinks that in this there is evidence of continual close relations between India and Iran. This is perfectly possible, but the evidence for it is rather slight.</p><p>6 For the recent controversy as to Iranian names found at Boghaz-kio7i, <i>cf.</i> Jacobi, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1909, 721 <i>et seq.;</i> Oldenberg, <i>ibid.,</i> 1095-1100; Keith, <i>ibid.,</i> 1100-1106; Sayce, <i>ibid.,</i> 1106, 1107; Kennedy, <i>ibid.,</i> 1107-1119. Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 94 <i>et seq.,</i> argues in favour of an early connexion of Iranians and Indians in Arachosia, where he places part of the action of the Rigveda. Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 16, 277, holds that traces of Iranian connexion are signs of late date; Arnold, <i>ibid.,</i> 18, 205 <i>et seq.,</i> opposes this view.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201154"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201154">↑</a> viii. 6, 46-48.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201154"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201154">↑</a> xvi. 11. 20.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201154"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201154">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 160, 161; 5, 142.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201154"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201154">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 136, 137.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201154"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201154">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 356, n.; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 3, 4; <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 37, 38.</li></ol></div>

तिरीट|తిరీట|ತಿರೀಟ|tirīṭa|tiriiTa|tireeTa|_vei_120@1155|_vei1_pn311
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1154">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1156">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn310">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn311">1:311</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn312">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tirīṭa</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201155"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201155">1</a></sup> is found in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201155"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201155">2</a></sup> in the adjectival derivative <i>tirīṭin</i> used of a demon, and presumably meaning ‘adorned with a tiara.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201155"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201155">↑</a> Meaning, according to later native lexicographers, ‘head-dress’ or ‘diadem.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201155"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201155">↑</a> viii. 6, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 265; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 495.</li></ol></div>

तिर्य|తిర్య|ತಿರ್ಯ|tirya|_vei_120@1156|_vei1_pn311
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1155">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1157">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn310">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn311">1:311</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn312">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tirya</b> occurs in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201156"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201156">1</a></sup> as an epithet of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="करम्भ">karambha</a>,</b> ‘gruel.’ It is probably equivalent to <i>tilya,</i> ‘made of sesamum,’ as rendered by Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201156"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201156">2</a></sup> and Whitney,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201156"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201156">3</a></sup> but <i>tiriya</i> is read by Roth<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201156"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201156">4</a></sup> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="राज">rāja</a>-nighaṇṭu as a kind of rice.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201156"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201156">↑</a> iv. 7, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201156"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201156">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201156"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201156">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 155.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201156"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201156">↑</a> See Whitney, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> with Lanman's additional note. Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 377, construes the adjective with <i>viṣam,</i> and renders ‘the poison which comes in a horizontal direction’ (<i>cf. tiryañc</i>). Grill, <i>Hundert Lieder,</i>^2 121, amends to <i>atiriya,</i> ‘overflowing.’</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 270; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 201.</li></ol></div>

तिर्यञ्च् आंगिरस|तिर्यञ्च् आङ्गिरस|తిర్యఞ్చ్ ఆంగిరస|తిర్యఞ్చ్ ఆఙ్గిరస|ತಿರ್ಯಞ್ಚ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ತಿರ್ಯಞ್ಚ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|tiryañc āṅgirasa|tirya~nch aaMgirasa|tirya~nch aangirasa|_vei_120@1157|_vei1_pn311
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1156">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1158">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn310">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn311">1:311</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn312">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tiryañc Āṅgirasa</b> is mentioned as a seer of Sāmans, or Chants, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201157"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201157">1</a></sup> The name is doubtless feigned. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201157"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201157">↑</a> xii. 6, 12. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 160.</li></ol></div>

तिल|తిల|ತಿಲ|tila|_vei_120@1158|_vei1_pn312
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1157">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1159">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn311">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn312">1:312</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn313">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tila</b> denotes in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201158">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201158">2</a></sup> the sesamum plant, and particularly its grains, from which a rich oil (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="तैल">taila</a></b>) was extracted. It is often<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201158">3</a></sup> mentioned in connexion with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="माष">māṣa</a>,</b> ‘kidney bean.’ The <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201158">4</a></sup> attributes the bean and the sesamum to the winter (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="हेमन्त">hemanta</a></i>) and the cool (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शिशिर">śiśira</a></i>) seasons. The stalk of the sesamum plant (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="तिल">tila</a>-piñjī,</i><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201158">5</a></sup> <i>til-piñja</i><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201158">6</a></sup>) was used for fuel, and the seed was boiled in the form of porridge (<i>tilaudana</i><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201158">7</a></sup>) for food.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201158">↑</a> ii. 8, 3; vi. 140, 2; xviii. 3, 69; 4, 32.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201158">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 2, 10, 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 12; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 1, 1, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201158">↑</a> Av. vi. 140, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 3, 22; Chāndogva <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 10, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201158">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201158">↑</a> Av. ii. 8, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201158">↑</a> Av. xii. 2, 54.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201158">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xii. 8.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 240.</li></ol></div>

तिल्वक|తిల్వక|ತಿಲ್ವಕ|tilvaka|_vei_120@1159|_vei1_pn312
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1158">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1160">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn311">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn312">1:312</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn313">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tilvaka</b> is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201159"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201159">1</a></sup> as a tree (<i>Symplocos racemosa</i>), near which it is inauspicious to construct a grave. The adjectival derivative <i>tailvaka,</i> ‘made of the wood of the <a class="vei_120a" href="तिल्वक">tilvaka</a>,’ is found in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201159"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201159">2</a></sup> and is employed to describe the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="यूप">yūpa</a>,</i> or sacrificial post, in the Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201159"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201159">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201159"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201159">↑</a> xiii. 8, 1, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201159"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201159">↑</a> iii. 1, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201159"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201159">↑</a> iii. 8.</li></ol></div>

तिष्य|తిష్య|ತಿಷ್ಯ|tiṣya|tiShya|_vei_120@1160|_vei1_pn312
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1159">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1161">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn311">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn312">1:312</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn313">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tiṣya</b> occurs twice in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201160"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201160">1</a></sup> apparently as the name of a star,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201160"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201160">2</a></sup> though Sāyaṇa takes it to mean the sun. It is doubtless identical with the Avestan Tistrya. Later it is the name of a lunar mansion: see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201160"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201160">↑</a> v. 54, 13; x. 64, 8 (with <a class="vei_120a" href="कृशानु">kṛśānu</a> as in archer).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201160"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201160">↑</a> Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 290; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 355; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 331; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 77, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

तिसृ-धन्व|तिसृधन्व|तिसृ|धन्व|తిసృ-ధన్వ|తిసృధన్వ|తిసృ|ధన్వ|ತಿಸೃ-ಧನ್ವ|ತಿಸೃಧನ್ವ|ತಿಸೃ|ಧನ್ವ|tisṛ-dhanva|tisṛdhanva|tisṛ|dhanva|tisR-dhanva|tisRdhanva|tisR|tisRi-dhanva|tisRidhanva|tisRi|tisRu-dhanva|tisRudhanva|tisRu|tisRRi-dhanva|tisRRidhanva|tisRRi|_vei_120@1161|_vei1_pn312
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1160">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1162">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn311">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn312">1:312</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn313">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tisṛ-dhanva,</b> ‘a bow with three (arrows),’ is mentioned as a gift to the priest at the sacrifice in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201161"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201161">1</a></sup> and in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201161"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201161">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201161"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201161">↑</a> i. 8, 19, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201161"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201161">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 3, 4; ii. 7, 9, 2; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 1, 5, 10; xiv. 1, 1, 7.</li></ol></div>

तुग्र|తుగ్ర|ತುಗ್ರ|tugra|_vei_120@1162|_vei1_pn313
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1161">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1163">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn312">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn313">1:313</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn314">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tugra</b> appears in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201162">1</a></sup> as the name of the father of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भुज्यु">bhujyu</a>,</b> a protégé of the Aśvins, who is accordingly called <a class="vei_120a" href="तुग्र्य">tugrya</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201162">2</a></sup> or <a class="vei_120a" href="तौग्र्य">taugrya</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201162">3</a></sup> A different <a class="vei_120a" href="तुग्र">tugra</a> seems to be referred to in other passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201162">4</a></sup> as an enemy of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201162">↑</a> i. 116, 3; 117, 14; vi. 62, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201162">↑</a> Rv. viii. 3, 23; 74, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201162">↑</a> Rv. i. 117, 15; 118, 6; 182, 5. 6; viii. 5, 22; x. 39, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201162">↑</a> vi. 20, 8; 26, 4; x. 49, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 55, 328, 329; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 157.</li></ol></div>

तुग्र्य|తుగ్ర్య|ತುಗ್ರ್ಯ|tugrya|_vei_120@1163|_vei1_pn313
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1162">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1164">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn312">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn313">1:313</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn314">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tugrya</b> occurs in the Rigveda as a patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भुज्यु">bhujyu</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201163">1</a></sup> but also in a passage<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201163">2</a></sup> in which no reference to <a class="vei_120a" href="भुज्यु">bhujyu</a> appears to be meant, and in which it may mean ‘a man of the house of <a class="vei_120a" href="तुग्र">tugra</a>.’ A similar sense seems to occur in the locative plural feminine in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201163">3</a></sup> where (supplying <i>vikṣu</i>) the meaning must be ‘among the Tugrians.’ This explanation may also apply to the epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201163">4</a></sup> or <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201163">5</a></sup> <i>tugryā-vṛdh,</i> ‘rejoicing among the Tugrians.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201163">↑</a> Who is also called <i>Tugrasya <a class="vei_120a" href="सूनु">sūnu</a>,</i> Rv. vi. 62, 6. <i>Cf.</i> the use of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पज्रिय">pajriya</a></i> and perhaps <i>Kṛṣṇiya</i> in the patronymic sense without <a class="vei_120a" href="वृद्धि">vṛddhi</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201163">↑</a> viii. 32, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201163">↑</a> i. 33, 15. <i>Cf.</i> Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 1, 47.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201163">↑</a> viii. 45, 29; 99, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201163">↑</a> viii. 1, 15, where, however, Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> suggests an alteration to make it refer to <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 128.</li></ol></div>

तुच्|తుచ్|ತುಚ್|tuc|tuch|_vei_120@1164|_vei1_pn313
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1163">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1165">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn312">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn313">1:313</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn314">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tuc</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201164"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201164">1</a></sup> occasionally occurs denoting ‘children.’ Tuj occurs rather more often in the same sense.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201164"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201164">2</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तनय">tanaya</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तोक">toka</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201164"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201164">↑</a> viii. 18, 18; 27, 14; vi. 48, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201164"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201164">↑</a> iii. 45, 4; iv. 1, 3; v. 41, 9; viii. 4, 15.</li></ol></div>

तुजि|తుజి|ತುಜಿ|tuji|_vei_120@1165|_vei1_pn313
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1164">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1166">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn312">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn313">1:313</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn314">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tuji</b> is the name in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201165"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201165">1</a></sup> of a protégé of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, who in another hymn<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201165"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201165">2</a></sup> appears to be called <a class="vei_120a" href="तूतुजि">tūtuji</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201165"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201165">↑</a> vi. 26, 4; x. 49, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201165"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201165">↑</a> vi. 20, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 156; Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 55, 328.</li></ol></div>

तुमिञ्ज औपोदिति|తుమిఞ్జ ఔపోదితి|ತುಮಿಞ್ಜ ಔಪೋದಿತಿ|tumiñja aupoditi|tumi~nja aupoditi|_vei_120@1166|_vei1_pn313
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1165">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1167">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn312">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn313">1:313</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn314">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tumiñja Aupoditi</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (i. 7, 2, 1) as a <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a> priest at a Sattra, or ‘sacrificial session,’ and as having been engaged in a discussion with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुश्रवस्">suśravas</a>.</b> </p></div>

तुर कावषेय|తుర కావషేయ|ತುರ ಕಾವಷೇಯ|tura kāvaṣeya|tura kaavaSheya|_vei_120@1167|_vei1_pn314
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1166">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1168">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn313">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn314">1:314</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn315">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tura Kāvaṣeya</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) at the end of the tenth book of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201167"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201167">1</a></sup> as the source of the doctrine set forth in that book, and as separated, in the succession of teachers, from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्डिल्य">śāṇḍilya</a></b> by <b>Yajñavacas</b> and <b>Kuśri.</b> In the same <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201167"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201167">2</a></sup> he is quoted by <a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्डिल्य">śāṇḍilya</a> as having erected a fire-altar on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कारोती">kārotī</a>.</b> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201167"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201167">3</a></sup> he appears as a <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, or ‘domestic priest,’ of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनमेजय">janamejaya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पारिक्षित">pārikṣita</a>,</b> whom he consecrated king. In the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201167"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201167">4</a></sup> and a <a class="vei_120a" href="खिल">khila</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201167"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201167">5</a></sup> he appears as an ancient sage. Oldenberg,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201167"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201167">6</a></sup> no doubt rightly, assigns him to the end of the Vedic period. He is probably<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201167"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201167">7</a></sup> identical with <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर">tura</a>, the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="देव-मुनि">deva-muni</a>,</i> ‘saint of the gods,’ who is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201167"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201167">8</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201167"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201167">↑</a> x. 6, 5, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201167"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201167">↑</a> ix. 5, 2, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201167"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201167">↑</a> iv. 27; vii. 34; viii. 21.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201167"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201167">↑</a> vi. 5, 4 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>, not in Mādhyaṃdina).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201167"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201167">↑</a> i. 9, 6; Scheftelowitz, <i>Die Apokryphen des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 65, 190.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201167"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201167">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 239.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201167"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201167">↑</a> So the St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201167"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201167">↑</a> xxv. 14, 5. See Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 68.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 203, n.; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 120, 131; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 43, xviii.</li></ol></div>

तुर-श्रवस्|तुर-श्रव|तुरश्रवस्|तुरश्रव|तुर|श्रवस्|श्रव|తుర-శ్రవస్|తుర-శ్రవ|తురశ్రవస్|తురశ్రవ|తుర|శ్రవస్|శ్రవ|ತುರ-ಶ್ರವಸ್|ತುರ-ಶ್ರವ|ತುರಶ್ರವಸ್|ತುರಶ್ರವ|ತುರ|ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಶ್ರವ|tura-śravas|tura-śrava|turaśravas|turaśrava|tura|śravas|śrava|tura-shravas|tura-shrava|turashravas|turashrava|shravas|shrava|_vei_120@1168|_vei1_pn314
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1167">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1169">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn313">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn314">1:314</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn315">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tura-śravas</b> is the name of a seer mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201168">1</a></sup> as having pleased <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> by two Sāmans (Chants) of his composition. <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> in return appears to have given him the oblation of the <b>Pārāvatas</b> on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201168">↑</a> ix. 4, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 53; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 316.</li></ol></div>

तुर्य-वाह्|तुर्यवाह्|तुर्य|वाह्|తుర్య-వాహ్|తుర్యవాహ్|తుర్య|వాహ్|ತುರ್ಯ-ವಾಹ್|ತುರ್ಯವಾಹ್|ತುರ್ಯ|ವಾಹ್|turya-vāh|turyavāh|turya|vāh|turya-vaah|turyavaah|vaah|_vei_120@1169|_vei1_pn314
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1168">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1170">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn313">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn314">1:314</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn315">↷</a><hr><p><b>Turya-vāh,</b> masc.; <b>Turyauhī,</b> fem., ‘a four-year-old ox or cow,’ is mentioned in the later Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201169"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201169">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201169"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201169">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 3, 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 11, 11; 13, 17; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiv. 10; xviii. 26, etc.</li></ol></div>

तुर्व|తుర్వ|ತುರ್ವ|turva|_vei_120@1170|_vei1_pn314
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1169">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1171">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn313">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn314">1:314</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn315">↷</a><hr><p><b>Turva</b> occurs only once in the Rigveda (x. 62, 10), doubtless as a name of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a></b> people or king. </p></div>

तुर्वश|తుర్వశ|ತುರ್ವಶ|turvaśa|turvasha|_vei_120@1171|_vei1_pn315
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1170">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1172">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn314">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn315">1:315</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn317">↷</a><hr><p><b>Turvaśa</b> occurs frequently in the Rigveda as the name of a man or of a people, usually in connexion with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a>.</b> The two words usually occur in the singular without any connecting particle, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a></i> or <i><a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a>.</i><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201171">2</a></sup> In a plural form the name <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> occurs once with the Yadus,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201171">3</a></sup> and once alone<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201171">4</a></sup> in a hymn in which the singular has already been used. In one passage<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201171">5</a></sup> the dual <i>Turvaśā-Yadū</i> actually occurs, and in another<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201171">6</a></sup> <i>Yadus Turvaś ca,</i> ‘Yadu and <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्व">turva</a>.’ In other passages<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201171">7</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> appears alone, while in one<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201171">8</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="याद्व">yādva</a> occur.</p><p>From these facts Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201171">9</a></sup> deduces the erroneousness of the ordinary view,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201171">10</a></sup> according to which <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> is the name of a tribe, the singular denoting the king, and regards <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> as the name of the <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a> king. But the evidence for this is not conclusive. Without laying any stress on the argument based on the theory<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201171">11</a></sup> that the ‘five peoples’ of the Rigveda are the <b>Anus, Druhyus,</b> Turvaśas, Yadus, and <b>Pūrus,</b> it is perfectly reasonable to hold that the Turvaśas and Yadus were two distinct though closely allied tribes. Such they evidently were to the seers of the hymns which mention in the dual the <i>Turvaśā-Yadū</i> and speak of <i>Yadus Turvaś ca.</i> This explanation also suits best the use of the plural of <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> in two Rigvedic hymns.</p><p>In the Rigveda the chief exploit of <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> was his participation in the war against <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>,</b> by whom he was defeated.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201171">12</a></sup> Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201171">13</a></sup> suggests that he may have been named <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> because of his fleet (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर">tura</a></i>) escape from the battle. His escape</p><p>1 i. 36, 18; 54, 6; 174, 9; vi. 20, 12; 45, 1; viii. 4, 7; 7, 18; 9, 14; 45, 27; x. 49, 8. In vii. 18, 6, <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> is joined with <a class="vei_120a" href="यक्षु">yakṣu</a>, apparently a contemptuous variant of <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a> (Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 261). <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तृत्सु">tṛtsu</a>.</b>  may have been assisted by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, for in some passages<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201171">14</a></sup> Indra's aid to <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> (and) <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a> is referred to; it is also significant that the <a class="vei_120a" href="अनु">anu</a>, and apparently the <a class="vei_120a" href="द्रुह्यु">druhyu</a>, kings are mentioned as having been drowned in the defeat, but not the <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a> kings, and that <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> appears in the eighth book of the Rigveda as a worshipper of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> with the <a class="vei_120a" href="अनु">anu</a> prince, the successor, presumably, of the one who was drowned.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201171">15</a></sup> Griffith,<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1201171">16</a></sup> however, proposes to refer these passages to a defeat by <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a> of Arṇa and <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्ररथ">citraratha</a> on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरयु">sarayu</a>;</b><sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1201171">17</a></sup> but the evidence for this is quite inadequate.</p><p>Two passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1201171">18</a></sup> seem to refer to an attack by <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a> on <b>Divodāsa,</b> the father of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>. It is reasonable to suppose that this was an attack of the two peoples on Divodāsa, for there is some improbability of the references being to the <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a>, who was concerned in the attack on <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>, the son.</p><p>Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1201171">19</a></sup> considers that the Turvaśas were also called <b>Vṛcīvants.</b> This view is based on a hymn<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1201171">20</a></sup> in which reference is made to the defeat of the Vṛcīvants on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यव्यावती">yavyāvatī</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हरियूपीया">hariyūpīyā</a></b> in aid of Daivarāta, and of <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> in aid of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सृञ्जय">sṛñjaya</a>,</b> the latter being elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1201171">21</a></sup> clearly the son of <a class="vei_120a" href="देवरात">devarāta</a>. But as this evidence for the identification of the Turvaśas with the Vṛcīvants is not clear, it seems sufficient<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1201171">22</a></sup> to assume that they were allies.</p><p>Later, in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1201171">23</a></sup> the Turvaśas appear as allies of the Pañcālas, <a class="vei_120a" href="तौर्वश">taurvaśa</a> horses, thirty-three in number, and armed men, to the number of 6,000, being mentioned.<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1201171">24</a></sup> But otherwise the name disappears: this lends  probability to Oldenberge's conjecture<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1201171">25</a></sup> that the Turvaśas became merged in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a></b> people. Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_1201171">26</a></sup> considers that in the Śatapatha passage the horses were merely named from the family of <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a>; but this view is less likely, since it ignores the difficulty involved in the reference to the men.</p><p>It is impossible to be certain regarding the home of the Turvaśas at the time of their conflict with <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>. They apparently crossed the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परुष्णी">paruṣṇī</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_27_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_1201171">27</a></sup> but from which side is disputed. The view of Pischel<sup id="ref_120_28_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_1201171">28</a></sup> and Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_29_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29_vei_1201171">29</a></sup> that they advanced from the west towards the east, where the <b>Bharatas</b> were (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a></b>), is the more probable.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="2"><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201171">↑</a> v. 31, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201171">↑</a> i. 108, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201171">↑</a> viii. 4, 18; singular with <a class="vei_120a" href="आनव">ānava</a> in viii. 4, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201171">↑</a> iv. 30, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201171">↑</a> x. 62, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 166; Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 220, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201171">↑</a> i. 4, 77; vi. 27, 7. <i>Cf.</i> viii. 4, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201171">↑</a> vii. 19, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201171">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 258 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201171">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 122, 124; Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 404; Ludwig, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 153; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 64; <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 153 <i>et seq.,</i> etc.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201171">↑</a> Zimmer, 122, 124; Macdonell, 153, 154.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201171">↑</a> vii. 18, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201171">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 264.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201171">↑</a> Rv. i. 174, 9; iv. 30, 17; v. 31, 8; viii. 4, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201171">↑</a> Hopkins, 265.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1201171">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 1, 433, n.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1201171">↑</a> The hymn is a late one, and the connexion of verse 18, where Arṇa and <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्ररथ">citraratha</a> are mentioned, is obscure. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins. 259.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1201171">↑</a> vi. 45, 1; ix. 61, 2 (where Divodāsa is mentioned); vii. 19, 8 (where he appears as <a class="vei_120a" href="अतिथिग्व">atithigva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1201171">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 124.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1201171">↑</a> vi. 27, 5-7.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1201171">↑</a> iv. 15, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1201171">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 404, n. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 105.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1201171">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1201171">↑</a> The sense is obscure. The St. Petersburg Dictionary takes it apparently as 6,033 horses (of armed warriors); Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 400, prefers to understand it as 33 horses and 6,000 men; Oldenberg, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> takes it as 6,033 warriors. Harisvāmin's Commentary, cited by Eggeling, is obscure.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1201171">↑</a> <i>Buddha,</i> 404.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_1201171">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 258, n. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 220.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_1201171">↑</a> Rv. vii. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_1201171">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 218, <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 126.</li><li id="fx_120_29_vei_1201171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29_vei_1201171">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 152. If in Rv. viii. 20, 24, 1 <i>rvaśa</i> is read with Ludwig for <i>tūrvath</i> hey are connected with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धु">sindhu</a>.</b></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 167; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Text,</i> 5, 286; Bergaigne, <i>Religion Védique,</i> 2, 354 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

तुर्वीति|తుర్వీతి|ತುರ್ವೀತಿ|turvīti|turviiti|turveeti|_vei_120@1172|_vei1_pn317
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1171">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1173">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn315">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn317">1:317</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn318">↷</a><hr><p><b>Turvīti</b> is mentioned several times in the Rigveda, both in association with <a class="vei_120a" href="वय्य">vayya</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201172"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201172">1</a></sup> and alone.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201172"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201172">2</a></sup> In three passages<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201172"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201172">3</a></sup> reference is made to <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> aiding him over a flood. Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201172"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201172">4</a></sup> has conjectured that he was king of the Turvaśas and Yadus. But there is no sufficient evidence for this view, though presumably he was of the <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> tribe.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201172"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201172">↑</a> i. 54, 6; ii. 13, 12; iv. 19, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201172"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201172">↑</a> Rv. i. 36, 18; 61, 11; 112, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201172"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201172">↑</a> i. 61, 11; ii. 13, 12; iv. 19, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201172"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201172">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 147; 4, 254. <i>Cf.</i> <b>Turvas1a,</b> and Bergaigne, <i>Religion Védique,</i> 2, 358; Oldenberg, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 42, 36.</li></ol></div>

तुला|తులా|ತುಲಾ|tulā|tulaa|_vei_120@1173|_vei1_pn317
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1172">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1174">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn315">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn317">1:317</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn318">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tulā,</b> ‘scales,’ is mentioned in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201173">1</a></sup> The Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201173">2</a></sup> also speaks of the balance in connexion with the weighing of a man's good and evil deeds in the next and in this world. This differs very considerably from the later balance<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201173">3</a></sup> ordeal, in which a man was weighed twice, and was pronounced guilty or innocent according as, on the second occasion, he was more or less heavy than on the  first. It is not possible to read the later practice into the earlier.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201173">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201173">↑</a> xxx. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201173">↑</a> xi. 2, 7, 33.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201173">↑</a> Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 145.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201173"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201173">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 21; 2, 363, quotes Schlagintweit as giving this passage as an example of the balance ordeal. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 45, n. 4.</li></ol></div>

तुष|తుష|ತುಷ|tuṣa|tuSha|_vei_120@1174|_vei1_pn318
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1173">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1175">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn317">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn318">1:318</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn319">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tuṣa,</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201174">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201174">2</a></sup> regularly denotes the ‘husk’ of grain, often used for a fire.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201174">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201174">↑</a> ix. 6, 16; xi. 1, 12, 29; 3, 5; xii. 3, 19.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201174">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 5, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201174">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="तुष">tuṣa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पक्व">pakva</a>,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 2, 4, 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 2, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 2, 1, 7.</li></ol></div>

तूणव|తూణవ|ತೂಣವ|tūṇava|tuuNava|tooNava|_vei_120@1175|_vei1_pn318
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1174">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1176">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn317">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn318">1:318</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn319">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tūṇava</b> denotes in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201175"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201175">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201175"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201175">2</a></sup> a musical instrument of wood, probably the ‘flute.’ A ‘fluteblower’ is enumerated among the victims of the Puruṣamedha, or ‘human sacrifice.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201175"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201175">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201175"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201175">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Samhitā, vi. 1, 4, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 6, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxiii. 4; xxxiv. 5 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 477).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201175"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201175">↑</a> Pañcavimśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 5, 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 13, 1; 15, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, xiii. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201175"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201175">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 19, 20; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 289.</li></ol></div>

तूतुजि|తూతుజి|ತೂತುಜಿ|tūtuji|tuutuji|tootuji|_vei_120@1176|_vei1_pn318
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1175">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1177">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn317">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn318">1:318</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn319">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tūtuji.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुजि">tuji</a>.</b></p></div>

तूपर|తూపర|ತೂಪರ|tūpara|tuupara|toopara|_vei_120@1177|_vei1_pn318
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1176">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1178">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn317">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn318">1:318</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn319">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tūpara,</b> ‘hornless,’ is a frequent description of animals intended for the sacrifice, especially of the goat, in the Atharvaveda and later.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201177"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201177">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201177"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201177">↑</a> Av. xi. 9, 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 1, 4, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 1, 15; xxix. 59, etc.; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1, 3, 7, etc.</li></ol></div>

तूर्घ्न|తూర్ఘ్న|ತೂರ್ಘ್ನ|tūrghna|tuurghna|toorghna|_vei_120@1178|_vei1_pn318
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1177">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1179">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn317">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn318">1:318</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn319">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tūrghna</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyak (v. 1) as the northern part of <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201178"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201178">1</a></sup> Its exact position, however, cannot be ascertained.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201178"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201178">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 78.</li></ol></div>

तूर्णाश|తూర్ణాశ|ತೂರ್ಣಾಶ|tūrṇāśa|tuurNaasha|toorNaasha|_vei_120@1179|_vei1_pn318
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1178">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1180">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn317">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn318">1:318</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn319">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tūrṇāśa,</b> in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201179"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201179">1</a></sup> seems to denote a ‘mountain torrent.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201179"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201179">↑</a> viii. 32, 4. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 16.</li></ol></div>

तूर्वयाण|తూర్వయాణ|ತೂರ್ವಯಾಣ|tūrvayāṇa|tuurvayaaNa|toorvayaaNa|_vei_120@1180|_vei1_pn319
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1179">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1181">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn318">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn319">1:319</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn320">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tūrvayāṇa</b> is the name of a prince mentioned in the Rigveda. He appears by name in two passages,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201180">1</a></sup> and is clearly alluded to in a third,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201180">2</a></sup> as an enemy of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अतिथिग्व">atithigva</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="आयु">āyu</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुत्स">kutsa</a>.</b> With this accords the fact that the <b>Pakthas</b> were opposed in the battle of the ten kings to the Tṛtsus,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201180">3</a></sup> and that <a class="vei_120a" href="तूर्वयाण">tūrvayāṇa</a>  is shown by another passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201180">4</a></sup> to have been a prince of the Pakthas. He is there represented as having been a protégé of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, who aided him against <b>Cyavāna</b> and his guardians, the Maruts. It is not probable that he is identical with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुश्रवस्">suśravas</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201180">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201180">↑</a> i. 53, 10; vi. 18, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201180">↑</a> ii. 14, 7 (as a comparison of the two preceding passages shows); perhaps also viii. 53, 2. In i. 174, 3, <a class="vei_120a" href="तूर्वयाण">tūrvayāṇa</a> seems also to be a proper name, though Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> here regards the word as an adjective.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201180">↑</a> vii. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201180">↑</a> x. 61, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 71-77.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201180">↑</a> Mentioned in i. 53, 9. 10. <i>Cf.</i> Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 1, 75, n.</li></ol></div>

तूष|తూష|ತೂಷ|tūṣa|tuuSha|tooSha|_vei_120@1181|_vei1_pn319
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1180">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1182">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn318">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn319">1:319</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn320">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tūṣa</b> is found in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201181"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201181">1</a></sup> denoting the ‘fringe’ or ‘trimming’ of a garment.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201181"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201181">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 1, 1; ii. 4, 9, 1; vi. 1, 1, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxiii. 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 1, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvii. 1, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 262.</li></ol></div>

तृक्षि|తృక్షి|ತೃಕ್ಷಿ|tṛkṣi|tRkShi|tRikShi|tRukShi|tRRikShi|tRxi|_vei_120@1182|_vei1_pn319
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1181">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1183">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn318">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn319">1:319</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn320">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tṛkṣi</b> is, in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201182"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201182">1</a></sup> the name of a prince who was a <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रासदस्यव">trāsadasyava</a>, ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a>.</b>’ He also appears with the <a class="vei_120a" href="द्रुह्यु">druhyu</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पूरु">pūru</a> peoples in another hymn.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201182"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201182">2</a></sup> It has been conjectured, but it is not probable, that the steed <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तार्क्ष्य">tārkṣya</a></b> (as ‘belonging to Tṛkṣi’) was his.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201182"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201182">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201182"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201182">↑</a> viii. 22, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201182"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201182">↑</a> vi. 46, 8. It is not certain that the two persons are identical (<i>cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologic,</i> 1, 113, notes 3, 4), but it is probable.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201182"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201182">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 149.</li></ol></div>

तृण|తృణ|ತೃಣ|tṛṇa|tRNa|tRiNa|tRuNa|tRRiNa|_vei_120@1183|_vei1_pn319
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1182">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1184">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn318">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn319">1:319</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn320">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tṛṇa,</b> ‘grass,’ is often mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201183">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201183">2</a></sup> It was used as straw to roof in a house or hut.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201183">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201183">↑</a> i. 161, 1; 162, 8, 11; x. 102, 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201183">↑</a> Av. ii. 30, 1; vi. 54, 1, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 22; viii. 24, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201183">↑</a> Av. iii. 12, 5; ix. 3, 4, 7.</li></ol></div>

तृण-जलायुक|तृणजलायुक|तृण|जलायुक|తృణ-జలాయుక|తృణజలాయుక|తృణ|జలాయుక|ತೃಣ-ಜಲಾಯುಕ|ತೃಣಜಲಾಯುಕ|ತೃಣ|ಜಲಾಯುಕ|tṛṇa-jalāyuka|tṛṇajalāyuka|tṛṇa|jalāyuka|tRNa-jalaayuka|tRNajalaayuka|tRNa|jalaayuka|tRiNa-jalaayuka|tRiNajalaayuka|tRiNa|tRuNa-jalaayuka|tRuNajalaayuka|tRuNa|tRRiNa-jalaayuka|tRRiNajalaayuka|tRRiNa|_vei_120@1184|_vei1_pn320
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1183">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1185">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn319">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn320">1:320</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn323">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tṛṇa-jalāyuka,</b> ‘caterpillar,’ is mentioned in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iv. 2, 4).</p></div>

तृण-स्कन्द|तृणस्कन्द|तृण|स्कन्द|తృణ-స్కన్ద|తృణస్కన్ద|తృణ|స్కన్ద|ತೃಣ-ಸ್ಕನ್ದ|ತೃಣಸ್ಕನ್ದ|ತೃಣ|ಸ್ಕನ್ದ|tṛṇa-skanda|tṛṇaskanda|tṛṇa|skanda|tRNa-skanda|tRNaskanda|tRNa|tRiNa-skanda|tRiNaskanda|tRiNa|tRuNa-skanda|tRuNaskanda|tRuNa|tRRiNa-skanda|tRRiNaskanda|tRRiNa|_vei_120@1185|_vei1_pn320
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1184">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1186">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn319">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn320">1:320</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn323">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tṛṇa-skanda</b> occurs once in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201185"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201185">1</a></sup> as the name of a prince, his subjects (<i>viśaḥ</i>) being referred to.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201185"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201185">2</a></sup> The word may originally have meant ‘grasshopper.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201185"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201185">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201185"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201185">↑</a> i. 172, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201185"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201185">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 159, takes <i>viśaḥ</i> as ‘cantons,’ but see <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201185"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201185">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

तृतीयक|తృతీయక|ತೃತೀಯಕ|tṛtīyaka|tRtiiyaka|tRteeyaka|tRitiiyaka|tRutiiyaka|tRRitiiyaka|_vei_120@1186|_vei1_pn320
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1185">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1187">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn319">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn320">1:320</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn323">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tṛtīyaka,</b> ‘the tertian (fever),’ is mentioned in the Atharvaveda (i. 25, 4; v. 22, 13; xix. 39, 10). See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तक्मन्">takman</a>.</b></p></div>

तृत्सु|తృత్సు|ತೃತ್ಸು|tṛtsu|tRtsu|tRitsu|tRutsu|tRRitsu|_vei_120@1187|_vei1_pn320
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1186">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1188">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn319">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn320">1:320</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn323">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tṛtsu</b> occurs in the Rigveda, once in the singular<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201187">1</a></sup> and several times in the plural,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201187">2</a></sup> as a proper name. The Tṛtsus were clearly helpers of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a></b> in the great battle against the ten kings, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शिम्यु">śimyu</a>,</b> the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a>,</b> the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="द्रुह्यु">druhyu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कवष">kavaṣa</a>,</b> the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पूरु">pūru</a>,</b> the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अनु">anu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="भेद">bheda</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शम्बर">śambara</a>,</b> the two <b>Vaikarṇas,</b> and perhaps the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a>,</b> who led with them as allies<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201187">3</a></sup> the <b>Matsyas, Pakthas, <a class="vei_120a" href="भलानस्">bhalānas</a>, Alinas, Viṣāṇins, Śivas, Ajas, Śigrus,</b> and perhaps Yakṣus.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201187">4</a></sup> The defeat of the ten kings is celebrated in one  hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201187">5</a></sup> and is evidently alluded to in two others.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201187">6</a></sup> The great battle took place on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परुष्णी">paruṣṇī</a>,</b> but there was also a fight on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a></b> with <a class="vei_120a" href="भेद">bheda</a>, the Ajas, Śigrus, and Yakṣus. As the <a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a> and the Parusṇī represent opposite ends of the territory of the Tṛtsus (for we cannot with Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201187">7</a></sup> safely identify the streams), it is difficult to see exactly how the ten kings could be confederated, but it should be noted that the references to the ten kings occur in the two later hymns,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201187">6</a></sup> and not in the hymn<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201187">5</a></sup> describing the battle itself; besides, absolute numerical accuracy cannot be insisted upon.</p><p>It is difficult exactly to determine the character of the Tṛtsus; especially in their relation to the Bharatas, who under Viśvāmitra's guidance are represented as prospering and as advancing to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विपाश्">vipāś</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुतुद्री">śutudrī</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201187">8</a></sup> Roth ingeniously brought this into connexion with the defeat of his enemies by <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>, which is celebrated in the seventh book of the Rigveda--a book attributed to the <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> family--and thought that there was a reference in one verse<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201187">9</a></sup> to the defeat of the Bharatas by <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>. But it seems certain that the verse is mistranslated, and that the Bharatas are really represented as victors with <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201187">10</a></sup> Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201187">11</a></sup> accordingly identifies the Tṛtsus and the Bharatas. Oldenberg,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201187">12</a></sup> after accepting this view at first,<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201187">13</a></sup> later expressed the opinion that the Tṛtsus were the priests of the <a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a> people, and therefore identical with the Vasiṣṭhas. This view is supported by the fact that in one passage<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201187">14</a></sup> the Tṛtsus are clearly described as wearing their hair in the peculiar manner affected by the Vasiṣṭhas,<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201187">15</a></sup> and would in that passage thus seem to represent the Vasiṣṭhas.  But Geldner<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1201187">16</a></sup> has suggested with great probability that <a class="vei_120a" href="तृत्सु">tṛtsu</a>, who is once mentioned in the singular,<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1201187">17</a></sup> means the <a class="vei_120a" href="तृत्सु">tṛtsu</a> king--that is, <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>.<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1201187">18</a></sup> This explanation alone justifies the description<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1201187">19</a></sup> of the Bharatas as <i>Tṛtsūnāṃ viśaḥ,</i><sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1201187">20</a></sup> ‘subjects of the Tṛtsus,’ meaning the <a class="vei_120a" href="तृत्सु">tṛtsu</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a> or family, for the people could not be said to be subjects of a body of priests. The Vasiṣṭhas might be called Tṛtsus because of their close connexion with the royal house of that people. The reverse process is also quite possible, but is rendered improbable by the fact that the <b>Pratṛdaḥ</b> are referred to as receiving <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>.<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1201187">21</a></sup> This name of the <a class="vei_120a" href="तृत्सु">tṛtsu</a> dynasty is probably older than its connexion with <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> in the time of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>, a conclusion supported by the name of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतर्दन">pratardana</a>,</b> who is mentioned later as a descendant of Divodāsa,<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1201187">22</a></sup> an ancestor of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>. The <a class="vei_120a" href="तृत्सु">tṛtsu</a> dynasty could therefore hardly have been referred to as Vasiṣṭhas. For the further history of the dynasty and its relation with <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>, see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>.</b></p><p>If the Tṛtsus and their subjects, the Bharatas, were in the Rigvedic period at war with the tribes on either side of the territory between the <a class="vei_120a" href="परुष्णी">paruṣṇī</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a>, it is clear<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1201187">23</a></sup> that later on they coalesced with the Pūrus and probably others of those tribes to form the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a></b> people. Already in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1201187">24</a></sup> the Tṛtsus are allied with the <b>Sṛñjayas,</b> and in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1201187">25</a></sup> one <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> serves both Kurus and Sṛñjayas. </p><p>Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_1201187">26</a></sup> considers that the Tṛtsus cannot be identified with the Bharatas, but that <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a> and the Bharatas represent an invading body, which, however, became allied with the Tṛtsus and the <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> priests. He also thinks that the Rigveda reveals a time when <b>Divodāsa,</b> the grandfather or ancestor of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>, was living in Arachosia, on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>,</b> and warring against the <b>Paṇis,</b> whom he identifies with the Parnians. But this conjecture<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_1201187">27</a></sup> cannot be regarded as probable. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a><sup id="ref_120_28_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_1201187">28</a></sup> it is not necessary to see any other river than the later <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>, in the middle country, which flowed within the boundaries of the Tṛtsus: it is also significant that there are references<sup id="ref_120_29_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29_vei_1201187">29</a></sup> to contests between <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a></b> and <a class="vei_120a" href="अतिथिग्व">atithigva</a> or Divodāsa. Thus there is no reason to doubt that Divodāsa and the Bharatas were in the middle country, and not in Iran.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201187">↑</a> vii. 18, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201187">↑</a> vii. 18, 7. 15. 19; 33, 5. 6; 83, 4. 6. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201187">↑</a> They were regarded as enemies of the kings by Roth, <i>Zur Litteratur und Geschichte des Weda,</i> 95, and by Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 126, The latter, however, altered his view (see pp. 430, 431, which Hopkins, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 260, has overlooked), and there is no doubt that the later opinion is correct. <i>Cf.</i> also Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 173; Hopkins, 260, 261. Of these tribes the Pakthas, Alinas, <a class="vei_120a" href="भलानस्">bhalānas</a>, Viṣāṇins, and Śivas, were probably settled in the north-west, to the west of the Indus, and around the Kabul River. The Anus, Pūrus, Turvaśas, Yadus, and Druhyus, were probably tribes of the Panjab; the Ajas, Śigrus, and Yakṣus, tribes of the east, under <a class="vei_120a" href="भेद">bheda</a>; <a class="vei_120a" href="शम्बर">śambara</a> may also have been a native of the east; <a class="vei_120a" href="शिम्यु">śimyu</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="कवष">kavaṣa</a> are doubtful; and the Vaikarṇau probably belonged to the north-west.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201187">↑</a> This is uncertain; the text of the Rigveda, vii. 18, 6, has <a class="vei_120a" href="यक्षु">yakṣu</a>, and the same word recurs in verse 19. On the other hand, the word <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a> would naturally be expected in verse 6, as <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> is mentioned. Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 122, says that <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a> occurs in vii. 18, but on p. 126 he cites <a class="vei_120a" href="यक्षु">yakṣu</a> in both places, evidently by oversight. Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 261, n., considers that <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a>, the <a class="vei_120a" href="यक्षु">yakṣu</a>, is a sarcastic expression, instead of <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a>, the <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a>, making <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a>, whom he regards as king of the Yadus, ridiculous as a member of an insignificant people, and alluding to him also as a sacrificial victim (as it were, <i>yaṣṭavya,</i> ‘to be offered’; <i>of <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोडाश्">puroḍāś</a>,</i> ‘cake of sacrifice,’ in verse 6, as a pun on <i>purogās,</i> ‘leader’). Whether <a class="vei_120a" href="यक्षु">yakṣu</a> is used contemptuously for <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a> or not, it seems hard not to believe that the Yadus are referred to.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201187">↑</a> vii. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201187">↑</a> vii. 33 and 83.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201187">↑</a> <i>India, Old and New,</i> 52. No such conjecture was made by him in the <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 259 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201187">↑</a> vii. 33 and 83.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201187">↑</a> vii. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201187">↑</a> Rv. iii. 33; 53, 9-12.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201187">↑</a> vii. 33, 6. See Roth, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 90, 121: Muir. <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 320; Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 154, 155; von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur and Cultur,</i> 35, 36; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 110, 111; Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society.</i> 16, 41.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201187">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 406: Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 34.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201187">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 175.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201187">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 207. <i>Cf.</i> Bergaigne, <i>Religion Védique,</i> 2, 362.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201187">↑</a> <i>Buddha,</i> 405, 406.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201187">↑</a> Rv. vii. 33, 1 (<i>śvityañco dakṣiṇataskaparainaḥ</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201187">↑</a> Rv. vii. 83. (<i>śvityañco dakṣiṇataskaparainaḥ</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1201187">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 136; <i>ṚgvedaGlossar,</i> 74.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1201187">↑</a> Rv. vii. 18, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1201187">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. vii. 18, 24. The parallelism of verses 13 and 24 is quite beyond question. Moreover, the praise of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a> and of the Bharatas is found coupled in Rv. iii. 53, 9. 12. 24, and in Rv. vi. 16, 4, 5, Divodāsa is coupled with the Bharatas in such a way as to suggest irresistibly that Divodāsa was a <a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1201187">↑</a> Rv. vii. 33, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1201187">↑</a> That this is the sense of <i>viśaḥ</i> is almost certain. See Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien, loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 159, and Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 111, render it ‘cantons,’ but see Vīś.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1201187">↑</a> Rv. vii. 33, 14. Geldner (<i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 138, 139) ingeniously suggests that <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>, being miraculously born, needed a <a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a>, and so became a <a class="vei_120a" href="तृत्सु">tṛtsu</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1201187">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतर्दन">pratardana</a> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxvi. 5, as <a class="vei_120a" href="दैवोदासि">daivodāsi</a>, ‘descendant of Divodāsa.’</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1201187">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 406 <i>et seq.,</i> and see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1201187">↑</a> See Rv. vi. 47, where Divodāsa and <a class="vei_120a" href="सार्ञ्जय">sārñjaya</a> are both praised. In vi. 27, 5, the Turvaśas are opposed to the Sṛñjayas, and in vii. 18, 6; 19, 8, the Tṛtsus are opposed to the Turvaśas.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1201187">↑</a> ii. 4, 4, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_1201187">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 98 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_1201187">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> also Grierson, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 837 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_1201187">↑</a> Rv. vi. 61, 3. Brunnhofer, <i>Iran und Turan,</i> 127, identifies this river with the Oxus, but Hillebrandt identifies it with the Haraqaiti.</li><li id="fx_120_29_vei_1201187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29_vei_1201187">↑</a> Rv. ix. 61, 2. <i>Cf.</i> vi. 45, 1; Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 124.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 424.</li></ol></div>

तृष्ट|తృష్ట|ತೃಷ್ಟ|tṛṣṭa|tRShTa|tRiShTa|tRuShTa|tRRiShTa|_vei_120@1188|_vei1_pn323
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1187">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1189">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn320">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn323">1:323</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn324">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tṛṣṭa</b> is mentioned in the Maitrāyaṇī<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201188">1</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201188">2</a></sup> as being along with <b>Varutri</b> the priest of the Asuras.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201188">↑</a> iv. 8, 1. The reading is uncertain; it may be <i>Tṛṣṭhā-varutrī.</i> See von Schroeder's edition, p. 106, n.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201188">↑</a> xxx. 1, where again the reading is uncertain. The Kapiṣṭhala Saṃhitā, xlvi. 4, has <i>Tvaṣṭā-varutrī</i> (von Schroeder's edition of the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a>, 2, 181, n.).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 190, 191; Lévi, <i>La Doctrine du Sacrifice,</i> 119.</li></ol></div>

तृष्टामा|తృష్టామా|ತೃಷ್ಟಾಮಾ|tṛṣṭāmā|tRShTaamaa|tRiShTaamaa|tRuShTaamaa|tRRiShTaamaa|_vei_120@1189|_vei1_pn323
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1188">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1190">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn320">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn323">1:323</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn324">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tṛṣṭāmā</b> is mentioned as a stream in the <a class="vei_120a" href="नदी">nadī</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="स्तुति">stuti</a>, or ‘praise of rivers,’ in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201189">1</a></sup> There seems to be no means of identifying it.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201189">↑</a> x. 75, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 14.</li></ol></div>

तेजन|తేజన|ತೇಜನ|tejana|_vei_120@1190|_vei1_pn323
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1189">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1191">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn320">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn323">1:323</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn324">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tejana</b> denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201190"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201190">1</a></sup> a rod or staff of reed used for measuring a field. In the Atharvaveda the sense of ‘bamboo’ is found twice,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201190"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201190">2</a></sup> the bamboo being specified in the  second passage as ‘of the spring’ (<i>vāsantika</i>); more particularly it denotes the shaft of an arrow,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201190"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201190">3</a></sup> a sense often found in later Vedic texts.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201190"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201190">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201190"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201190">↑</a> i. 110, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201190"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201190">↑</a> i. 2, 4; xx. 136, 3 (= <a class="vei_120a" href="खिल">khila</a>, v. 22, 3). <i>Cf. taijana</i> as an adjective in <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxi. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201190"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201190">↑</a> Av. vi. 49, 1 (Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 317); <i><a class="vei_120a" href="इषु">iṣu</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="एक">eka</a>-tejanā,</i> ‘an arrow with one shaft,’ vi. 57, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201190"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201190">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 25; iii. 26; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxv. 1 (with <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शृंग">śṛṅga</a></i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शल्य">śalya</a></i> as the three parts of an arrow; in Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 8, 1, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कुल्मल">kulmala</a></i> takes the place of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="तेजन">tejana</a>; cf. ibid.,</i> 2). The <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 3, 3, 1, has <i>anīka, <a class="vei_120a" href="शल्य">śalya</a>,</i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="तेजन">tejana</a>. Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इषु">iṣu</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

तेजनी|तेजनि|తేజనీ|తేజని|ತೇಜನೀ|ತೇಜನಿ|tejanī|tejani|tejanii|tejanee|_vei_120@1191|_vei1_pn324
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1190">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1192">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn323">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn324">1:324</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn325">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tejanī</b> denotes in the later Saṃhitās and Brāhmaṇas a bundle of reeds,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201191"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201191">1</a></sup> and in some cases such a bundle twisted into a rope,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201191"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201191">2</a></sup> for the two ends of the <a class="vei_120a" href="तेजनी">tejanī</a> are mentioned.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201191"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201191">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 8, 3, 12; perhaps <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxiii. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201191"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201191">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxii. 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 11, as rendered by Sāyaṇa.</li></ol></div>

तेजस्|तेज|తేజస్|తేజ|ತೇಜಸ್|ತೇಜ|tejas|teja|_vei_120@1192|_vei1_pn324
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1191">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1193">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn323">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn324">1:324</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn325">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tejas</b> is regarded by Schrader<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201192"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201192">1</a></sup> as having in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201192"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201192">2</a></sup> the specific sense of ‘axe.’ But in all the passages the sense of the ‘bolt’ of the god is adequate.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201192"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201192">↑</a> <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 221.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201192"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201192">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> vi. 3, 5; 8, 5; 15, 19.</li></ol></div>

तैत्तिरीय|తైత్తిరీయ|ತೈತ್ತಿರೀಯ|taittirīya|taittiriiya|taittireeya|_vei_120@1193|_vei1_pn324
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1192">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1194">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn323">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn324">1:324</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn325">↷</a><hr><p><b>Taittirīya</b> is the name of one of the divisions of the Black <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>, which is, however, not found thus described until the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> period.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201193">1</a></sup> The school is represented by a Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201193">2</a></sup> a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201193">3</a></sup> and an Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201193">4</a></sup> besides an <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201193">5</a></sup> which forms a part of the Āraṇyaka.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201193">↑</a> Anupada <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 6; vii. 7. 10, etc. See Weber <i>Indian Literature,</i> 87 <i>et seq.;</i> Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 175 <i>et seq.;</i> von Schroeder, Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, 1, x <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201193">↑</a> Edited by Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> xi, xii, and in the <i>Bibliotheca Indica,</i> 1854-1899.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201193">↑</a> Edited in the <i>Bibliotheca Indica,</i> 1855-1870, and in the Ānandāśrama Series, 1898.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201193">↑</a> Edited in the <i>Bibliotheca Indica,</i> 18641872, and in the Ānandāśrama Series, 1898.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201193">↑</a> Edited by Roer, 1850, and in the Ānandāśrama Series, 1889.</li></ol></div>

तैमात|తైమాత|ತೈಮಾತ|taimāta|taimaata|_vei_120@1194|_vei1_pn324
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1193">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1195">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn323">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn324">1:324</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn325">↷</a><hr><p><b>Taimāta</b> is twice mentioned as a species of snake in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201194"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201194">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201194"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201194">↑</a> v. 13, 6; 18, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 243; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 425; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 95.</li></ol></div>

तैल|తైల|ತೈಲ|taila|_vei_120@1195|_vei1_pn325
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1194">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1196">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn324">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn325">1:325</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn326">↷</a><hr><p><b>Taila,</b> ‘sesamum oil,’ is mentioned in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201195">1</a></sup> where<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201195">2</a></sup> reference is made to keeping such oil in jars. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201195">3</a></sup> reference is made to anointing with sesamum oil.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201195">↑</a> i. 7, 2 (all the manuscripts have <i><a class="vei_120a" href="तौल">taula</a>,</i> which must be wrong; the Paippalāda MS has <i>tūla:</i> see Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 7).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201195">↑</a> xx. 136, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201195">↑</a> xi. 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben.</i> 240, 241.</li></ol></div>

तोक|తోక|ತೋಕ|toka|_vei_120@1196|_vei1_pn325
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1195">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1197">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn324">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn325">1:325</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn326">↷</a><hr><p><b>Toka</b> denotes ‘children’ or ‘descendants’ generally in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201196">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201196">2</a></sup> The word is often joined with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तनय">tanaya</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201196">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201196">↑</a> i. 43, 2; ii. 2, 11; 9, 2; vii. 62, 6; viii. 5, 20; 67, 11, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201196">↑</a> Av. i. 13, 2; 28, 3; v. 19, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvi. 7 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 466); Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 5, 2, 39, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201196">↑</a> Rv. i. 31, 12; 64, 14; 114, 6; 147, 1; ii. 33, 14; v. 53, 13; vi. 1, 12, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7.</li></ol></div>

तोक्मन्|తోక్మన్|ತೋಕ್ಮನ್|tokman|_vei_120@1197|_vei1_pn325
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1196">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1198">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn324">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn325">1:325</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn326">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tokman,</b> neut., denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201197">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201197">2</a></sup> the green shoots of any kind of grain. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201197">3</a></sup> reference is made to the shoots of rice (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रीहि">vrīhi</a></i>), large rice (<i>mahāvrīhi</i>), panic seed (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रियंगु">priyaṅgu</a></i>), and barley (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="यव">yava</a></i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201197">↑</a> x. 62, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201197">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 13, 81; xxi. 30. 42; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 11; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 11, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201197">↑</a> viii. 16. <i>Cf.</i> for its use at the <a class="vei_120a" href="सौत्रामणी">sautrāmaṇī</a>, Hillebrandt, <i>Rituallitteratur,</i> 160.</li></ol></div>

तोत्त्र|తోత్త్ర|ತೋತ್ತ್ರ|tottra|_vei_120@1198|_vei1_pn325
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1197">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1199">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn324">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn325">1:325</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn326">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tottra,</b> a ‘goad’ for driving cattle, is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xii. 4, 1, 10).</p></div>

तोद|తోద|ತೋದ|toda|_vei_120@1199|_vei1_pn325
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1198">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1200">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn324">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn325">1:325</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn326">↷</a><hr><p><b>Toda</b> appears once to denote a ‘goad’ in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201199"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201199">1</a></sup> but more often<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201199"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201199">2</a></sup> it is an agent noun meaning ‘impeller.’ Geldner<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201199"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201199">3</a></sup> considers that in one passage<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201199"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201199">4</a></sup> the sense is ‘wielder of the rod of punishment’ (later <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दण्ड">daṇḍa</a>-dhara</i>)--that is, ‘prince.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201199"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201199">↑</a> iv. 16, 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> in <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, 107.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201199"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201199">↑</a> Rv. vi. 6, 6; 12, 1, 3, are probably so to be understood.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201199"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201199">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 74.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201199"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201199">↑</a> Rv. i. 150, 1.</li></ol></div>

तौग्र्य|తౌగ్ర్య|ತೌಗ್ರ್ಯ|taugrya|_vei_120@1200|_vei1_pn326
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1199">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1201">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn325">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn326">1:326</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn327">↷</a><hr><p><b>Taugrya,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुग्र">tugra</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भुज्यु">bhujyu</a></b> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201200">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201200">↑</a> i. 117, 16; 118, 6; 182, 5. 6; viii. 5, 22; x. 39, 4.</li></ol></div>

तौदी|तौदि|తౌదీ|తౌది|ತೌದೀ|ತೌದಿ|taudī|taudi|taudii|taudee|_vei_120@1201|_vei1_pn326
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1200">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1202">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn325">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn326">1:326</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn327">↷</a><hr><p><b>Taudī</b> in one passage of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201201"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201201">1</a></sup> appears to denote a plant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201201"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201201">↑</a> x. 4, 24. <i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 578, leaves the word untranslated. Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 608, suggests that it is a fanciful name, ‘the piercer,’ since it is associated with <i>ghṛtācī,</i> ‘dripping with ghee,’ which is clearly such a word.</li></ol></div>

तौर्वश|తౌర్వశ|ತೌರ್ವಶ|taurvaśa|taurvasha|_vei_120@1202|_vei1_pn326
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1201">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1203">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn325">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn326">1:326</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn327">↷</a><hr><p><b>Taurvaśa.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a>.</b></p></div>

तौल|తౌల|ತೌಲ|taula|_vei_120@1203|_vei1_pn326
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1202">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1204">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn325">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn326">1:326</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn327">↷</a><hr><p><b>Taula,</b> the reading of the text of the Atharvaveda (i. 7, 2), and a form which is otherwise unknown and cannot be satisfactorily explained, must doubtless be meant for <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तैल">taila</a>.</b></p></div>

तौविलिका|తౌవిలికా|ತೌವಿಲಿಕಾ|tauvilikā|tauvilikaa|_vei_120@1204|_vei1_pn326
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1203">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1205">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn325">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn326">1:326</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn327">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tauvilikā,</b> occurring once in a hymn of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201204"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201204">1</a></sup> is a word of quite uncertain sense. Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201204"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201204">2</a></sup> thinks it means some kind of beast; Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201204"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201204">3</a></sup> and Whitney<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201204"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201204">4</a></sup> regard it as a sort of plant; Sāyaṇa explains it as a disease-causing demon, while Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201204"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201204">5</a></sup> leaves the sense doubtful.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201204"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201204">↑</a> vi. 16, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201204"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201204">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201204"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201204">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 72.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201204"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201204">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 292.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201204"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201204">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 30, 466.</li></ol></div>

त्रपु|త్రపు|ತ್ರಪು|trapu|_vei_120@1205|_vei1_pn326
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1204">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1206">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn325">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn326">1:326</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn327">↷</a><hr><p><b>Trapu</b> denotes ‘tin’ in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201205">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201205">2</a></sup> Its quality of being easily smelted, which Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201205">3</a></sup> thinks is indicated by the name (as derived from the root <i>trap,</i> ‘be ashamed’), is clearly alluded to in the Atharvaveda passage. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201205">↑</a> xi. 3, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201205">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xviii. 10; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 11, 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 13 (all in enumerations of metals); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 12, 6, 5; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 17, 3; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 17, 7. In <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 7, 5, 1, the form is <i>trapus.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201205">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 53.</li></ol></div>

त्रस-दस्यु|त्रसदस्यु|त्रस|दस्यु|త్రస-దస్యు|త్రసదస్యు|త్రస|దస్యు|ತ್ರಸ-ದಸ್ಯು|ತ್ರಸದಸ್ಯು|ತ್ರಸ|ದಸ್ಯು|trasa-dasyu|trasadasyu|trasa|dasyu|_vei_120@1206|_vei1_pn327
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1205">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1207">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn326">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn327">1:327</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn328">↷</a><hr><p><b>Trasa-dasyu,</b> son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201206">1</a></sup> is mentioned in the Rigveda as king of the Pūrus.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201206">2</a></sup> He was born to <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a> by his wife, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्सानी">purukutsānī</a>,</b> at a time of great distress;<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201206">3</a></sup> this, according to Sāyaṇa, refers to Purukutsa's captivity: possibly his death is really meant. <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a> was also a descendant of Girikṣit,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201206">4</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a> was a descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="दुर्गह">durgaha</a>. The genealogy, therefore, appears to be: <a class="vei_120a" href="दुर्गह">durgaha</a>, Girikṣit, <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a>. <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a> was the ancestor of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तृक्षि">tṛkṣi</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201206">5</a></sup> and, according to Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201206">6</a></sup> had a son <a class="vei_120a" href="हिरणिन्">hiraṇin</a>. Trasadasyu's chronological position is determined by the fact that his father, <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a>, was a contemporary of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>, either as an opponent<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201206">7</a></sup> or as a friend.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201206">8</a></sup> That <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a> was an enemy of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a> is more probable, because the latter's predecessor, Divodāsa, was apparently<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201206">9</a></sup> at enmity with the Pūrus, and in the battle of the ten kings Pūrus were ranged against <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a> and the Tṛtsus. <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a> himself seems to have been an energetic king. His people, the Pūrus, were settled on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201206">10</a></sup> which was, no doubt, the stream in the middle country, that locality according well with the later union of the Pūrus with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a></b> people, who inhabited that country. This union is exemplified in the person of <b>Kuruśravaṇa,</b> who is called <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रासदस्यव">trāsadasyava</a>, ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a>,’ in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201206">11</a></sup> whose father was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मित्रातिथि">mitrātithi</a>,</b> and whose son was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपमश्रवस्">upamaśravas</a>.</b> The relation of <a class="vei_120a" href="मित्रातिथि">mitrātithi</a> to <a class="vei_120a" href="तृक्षि">tṛkṣi</a> does not appear.</p><p>Another descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a> was <b>Tryaruṇa <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रैवृष्ण">traivṛṣṇa</a>,</b> who is simply called <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a> in a hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201206">12</a></sup>  He was not only a ‘descendant of Trivṛṣan,’ but, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201206">13</a></sup> he was also <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रैधात्व">traidhātva</a>, ‘descendant of Tridhātu.’ The order of these two predecessors of Tryaruṇa cannot be determined in any way from Vedic literature. According to the later tradition,<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201206">14</a></sup> a prince named Tridhanvan preceded Tryaruṇa in the succession. Vedic tradition further fails to show in what precise relation <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a> stood to Trivṛṣan or Tryaruṇa.</p><p>Trasdasyu <a class="vei_120a" href="पौरुकुत्स">paurukutsa</a> appears in several Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201206">15</a></sup> as a famous sacrificer of ancient times, together with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर आट्णार">para āṭṇāra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वीतहव्य">vītahavya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रायस">śrāyasa</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="औशिज">auśija</a>,</b> who in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1201206">16</a></sup> are called ‘ancient great kings’ (<i>pūrve mahārājāḥ</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201206">↑</a> Rv. v. 33, 8; vii. 19, 3; viii. 19, 36; iv. 42, 8 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201206">↑</a> Rv. iv. 38, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> vii. 19, 3. He is merely alluded to in i. 63, 7; 112, 4; viii. 8, 21; 36, 7; 37, 7; 49, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201206">↑</a> Rv. iv. 42, 8 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201206">↑</a> Rv. v. 33, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201206">↑</a> Rv. viii. 22, 7. He was a <a class="vei_120a" href="पूरु">pūru</a> king. See vi. 46, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201206">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 155, with reference to Rv. v. 33, 7 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201206">↑</a> So Ludwig, 3, 174, who alters <i>Sudāsam</i> to <i>Sudāse,</i> in support of this view, in Rv. i. 63, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 204, 205, 219, <i>ṚgvedaNoten,</i> 1, 63; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 153; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 112, n. 1. Foy, Kuhn's <i>Zeitschrift,</i> 34, 242, denies that the word in this passage is a proper name at all.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201206">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201206">↑</a> Rv. i. 130, 7; Ludwig, 3, 114; but see Hillebrandt, 1, 113, 114.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201206">↑</a> Rv. vii. 95, 96; Ludwig, 3, 175; Hillebrandt, 1, 115.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201206">↑</a> x. 33, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Lanman, <i>Sanskrit Reader,</i> 386 <i>et seq.;</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 150, 184.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201206">↑</a> v. 27.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201206">↑</a> xiii. 3, 12. The Tāṇḍaka, cited by Sāyaṇa (Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 67), has <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a>, like the Rv.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201206">↑</a> Harivaṃśa, 714 <i>et seq.,</i> where the name (716) is also misread as Tridharman. <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रैधात्व">traidhātva</a> cannot reasonably be taken as representing a patronymic from Tridhanvan, as Sieg, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 7476, seems to do. Trivṛṣan has entirely disappeared from the Epic tradition; there is thus no way of assigning a relative priority to either Trivṛṣan or Tridhanvan.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201206">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxii. 3 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 473); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 6, 5, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1201206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1201206">↑</a> ii. 6, 11.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 217 <i>et seq.;</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 111-116; 2, 165, n. 4; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 25; Lanman, <i>Sanskrit Reader,</i> 386.</li></ol></div>

त्रात ऐषुमत|త్రాత ఐషుమత|ತ್ರಾತ ಐಷುಮತ|trāta aiṣumata|traata aiShumata|_vei_120@1207|_vei1_pn328
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1206">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1208">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn327">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn328">1:328</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn329">↷</a><hr><p><b>Trāta Aiṣumata</b> (‘descendant of Iṣumant’) is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201207">1</a></sup> as a pupil of <b>Nigaḍa <a class="vei_120a" href="पार्णवल्कि">pārṇavalki</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201207">↑</a> i. 3. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372.</li></ol></div>

त्रायमाणा|త్రాయమాణా|ತ್ರಾಯಮಾಣಾ|trāyamāṇā|traayamaaNaa|_vei_120@1208|_vei1_pn328
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1207">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1209">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn327">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn328">1:328</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn329">↷</a><hr><p><b>Trāyamāṇā</b> denotes in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201208"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201208">1</a></sup> a plant of an unknown species. The word is possibly only an epithet, retaining its participial sense of ‘preserving,’ though this interpretation is not favoured by the accent.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201208"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201208">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201208"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201208">↑</a> viii. 2, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201208"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201208">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रायमाणा">trāyamāṇā</a>. Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 477.</li></ol></div>

त्रासदस्यव|త్రాసదస్యవ|ತ್ರಾಸದಸ್ಯವ|trāsadasyava|traasadasyava|_vei_120@1209|_vei1_pn328
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1208">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1210">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn327">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn328">1:328</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn329">↷</a><hr><p><b>Trāsadasyava,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic in the Rigveda of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तृक्षि">tṛkṣi</a></b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201209">1</a></sup> and of <b>Kuruśravaṇa.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201209">2</a></sup> The  word is also applied to <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> as ‘protector of, or worshipped by, Trasadasyu’ and his line.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201209">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201209">↑</a> viii. 22, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201209">↑</a> x. 33, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201209">↑</a> viii. 19, 32; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 96.</li></ol></div>

त्रि-ककुद्|त्रिककुद्|त्रि|ककुद्|త్రి-కకుద్|త్రికకుద్|త్రి|కకుద్|ತ್ರಿ-ಕಕುದ್|ತ್ರಿಕಕುದ್|ತ್ರಿ|ಕಕುದ್|tri-kakud|trikakud|tri|kakud|_vei_120@1210|_vei1_pn329
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1209">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1211">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn328">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn329">1:329</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn330">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tri-kakud</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201210"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201210">1</a></sup> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रि">tri</a>-kakubh,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201210"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201210">2</a></sup> ‘having three peaks,’ occurs in the Atharvaveda and later as the name of a mountain in the Himālaya, the modern Trikota. From it came the salve (<a class="vei_120a" href="आञ्जन">āñjana</a>),<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201210"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201210">3</a></sup> which tradition made out to be derived from Vṛtra's eye.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201210"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201210">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201210"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201210">↑</a> Av. iv. 9, 8; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 3, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201210"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201210">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 6, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxiii. 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xv. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxii. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201210"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201210">↑</a> Hence called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रैककुद">traikakuda</a>,</i> Av. iv. 9, 9, 10; xix. 44, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201210"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201210">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Maitrāyaṇī and <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitās, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 198; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 5, 29, 30; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 239, n. 4; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 381.</li></ol></div>

त्रि-कद्रुक|त्रिकद्रुक|त्रि|कद्रुक|త్రి-కద్రుక|త్రికద్రుక|త్రి|కద్రుక|ತ್ರಿ-ಕದ್ರುಕ|ತ್ರಿಕದ್ರುಕ|ತ್ರಿ|ಕದ್ರುಕ|tri-kadruka|trikadruka|tri|kadruka|_vei_120@1211|_vei1_pn329
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1210">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1212">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn328">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn329">1:329</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn330">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tri-kadruka,</b> a term used in the plural only, appears to denote three vessels of some kind for holding <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>.</p><p>^1 i. 32, 3; ii. 11, 17; 15, 1; 22, 1; x. 14, 16.</p></div>

त्रि-खर्व|त्रिखर्व|त्रि|खर्व|త్రి-ఖర్వ|త్రిఖర్వ|త్రి|ఖర్వ|ತ್ರಿ-ಖರ್ವ|ತ್ರಿಖರ್ವ|ತ್ರಿ|ಖರ್ವ|tri-kharva|trikharva|tri|kharva|_vei_120@1212|_vei1_pn329
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1211">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1213">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn328">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn329">1:329</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn330">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tri-kharva</b> is the name of a school of priests mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (ii. 8, 3) as practising a special rite with success.</p></div>

त्रित|త్రిత|ತ್ರಿತ|trita|_vei_120@1213|_vei1_pn329
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1212">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1214">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn328">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn329">1:329</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn330">↷</a><hr><p><b>Trita</b> is clearly a god<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201213"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201213">1</a></sup> in Vedic literature, but <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a> in one passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201213"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201213">2</a></sup> already explains the name as that of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> or seer.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201213"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201213">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> pp. 67-69.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201213"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201213">↑</a> iv. 6.</li></ol></div>

त्रि-पुर|त्रिपुर|त्रि|पुर|త్రి-పుర|త్రిపుర|త్రి|పుర|ತ್ರಿ-ಪುರ|ತ್ರಿಪುರ|ತ್ರಿ|ಪುರ|tri-pura|tripura|tri|pura|_vei_120@1214|_vei1_pn329
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1213">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1215">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn328">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn329">1:329</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn330">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tri-pura,</b> ‘a threefold stronghold,’ is alluded to in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201214"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201214">1</a></sup> as a secure protection. But as the passages are mythical no stress can be laid on them as evidence for the existence of forts with three concentric walls. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201214"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201214">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 3, 3, 25; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> in <i>Indische Studien,</i> 2, 310. See also <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxiv. 10, etc., and Lévi, <i>La Doctrine du Sacrifice,</i> 46, n 1.</li></ol></div>

त्रि-प्लक्ष|त्रिप्लक्ष|त्रि|प्लक्ष|త్రి-ప్లక్ష|త్రిప్లక్ష|త్రి|ప్లక్ష|ತ್ರಿ-ಪ್ಲಕ್ಷ|ತ್ರಿಪ್ಲಕ್ಷ|ತ್ರಿ|ಪ್ಲಕ್ಷ|tri-plakṣa|triplakṣa|tri|plakṣa|tri-plakSha|triplakSha|plakSha|tri-plaxa|triplaxa|plaxa|_vei_120@1215|_vei1_pn330
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1214">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1216">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn329">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn330">1:330</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn331">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tri-plakṣa,</b> masc. plur., ‘the three fig-trees,’ is the name of the place where the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृषद्वती">dṛṣadvatī</a></b> disappeared, near the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a>,</b> according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201215"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201215">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201215"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201215">↑</a> xxv. 13, 4. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiii. 29, 33; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 19, 9; Kātyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxiv. 6, 39.</li></ol></div>

त्रिय्-अवि|त्रियवि|त्रिय्|अवि|త్రియ్-అవి|త్రియవి|త్రియ్|అవి|ತ್ರಿಯ್-ಅವಿ|ತ್ರಿಯವಿ|ತ್ರಿಯ್|ಅವಿ|triy-avi|triyavi|triy|avi|_vei_120@1216|_vei1_pn330
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1215">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1217">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn329">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn330">1:330</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn331">↷</a><hr><p><b>Triy-avi.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्र्यवि">tryavi</a>.</b></p></div>

त्रि-युग|त्रियुग|त्रि|युग|త్రి-యుగ|త్రియుగ|త్రి|యుగ|ತ್ರಿ-ಯುಗ|ತ್ರಿಯುಗ|ತ್ರಿ|ಯುಗ|tri-yuga|triyuga|tri|yuga|_vei_120@1217|_vei1_pn330
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1216">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1218">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn329">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn330">1:330</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn331">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tri-yuga,</b> neut., is an expression occurring in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201217"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201217">1</a></sup> where it is said that the plants (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ओषधि">oṣadhi</a></i>) were born ‘three ages’ before the gods (<i>devebhyas triyugaṃ purā</i>). The commentator on the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201217"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201217">2</a></sup> thinks that the ages here meant are the Yugas of the later Indian chronology, the sense of the passage being that the plants were born in the first <a class="vei_120a" href="युग">yuga</a>. The author of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201217"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201217">3</a></sup> understands three seasons--spring, the rains, and autumn--to be meant in the verse, taking the two words <i>triyugaṃ purā</i> separately as ‘formerly, in the three seasons.’ The vague sense ‘three ages’ is quite adequate: the use of ‘three’ in such cases is a favourite feature in folklore. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="युग">yuga</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201217"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201217">↑</a> x. 97, 1 = <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 6, 1, and Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā; xii. 75.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201217"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201217">↑</a> ix. 28.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201217"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201217">↑</a> vii. 2, 4, 26.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 340.</li></ol></div>

त्रि-वत्स|त्रिवत्स|त्रि|वत्स|త్రి-వత్స|త్రివత్స|త్రి|వత్స|ತ್ರಿ-ವತ್ಸ|ತ್ರಿವತ್ಸ|ತ್ರಿ|ವತ್ಸ|tri-vatsa|trivatsa|tri|vatsa|_vei_120@1218|_vei1_pn330
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1217">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1219">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn329">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn330">1:330</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn331">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tri-vatsa,</b> ‘three years old,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201218">1</a></sup> is an expression applied to cattle in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201218">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201218">↑</a> As regards the form and meaning of this compound, <i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्र्यवि">tryavi</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201218">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiv. 10; xviii. 26; xxviii. 27; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvi. 13; xviii. 9; xxi. 14, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 3, 9 <i>et seq.,</i> where one explanation of the word is <i><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रि">tri</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ष">varṣa</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

त्रि-वृत्|त्रिवृत्|त्रि|वृत्|త్రి-వృత్|త్రివృత్|త్రి|వృత్|ತ್ರಿ-ವೃತ್|ತ್ರಿವೃತ್|ತ್ರಿ|ವೃತ್|tri-vṛt|trivṛt|tri|vṛt|tri-vRt|trivRt|vRt|tri-vRit|trivRit|vRit|tri-vRut|trivRut|vRut|tri-vRRit|trivRRit|vRRit|_vei_120@1219|_vei1_pn330
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1218">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1220">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn329">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn330">1:330</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn331">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tri-vṛt,</b> ‘threefold,’ is the designation of an amulet in the Atharvaveda (v. 28, 2. 4).</p></div>

त्रि-वेद कृष्ण-रात लौहित्य|त्रि-वेद कृष्णरात लौहित्य|त्रि-वेद कृष्ण|रात लौहित्य|త్రి-వేద కృష్ణ-రాత లౌహిత్య|త్రి-వేద కృష్ణరాత లౌహిత్య|త్రి-వేద కృష్ణ|రాత లౌహిత్య|ತ್ರಿ-ವೇದ ಕೃಷ್ಣ-ರಾತ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|ತ್ರಿ-ವೇದ ಕೃಷ್ಣರಾತ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|ತ್ರಿ-ವೇದ ಕೃಷ್ಣ|ರಾತ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|tri-veda kṛṣṇa-rāta lauhitya|tri-veda kṛṣṇarāta lauhitya|tri-veda kṛṣṇa|rāta lauhitya|tri-veda kRShNa-raata lauhitya|tri-veda kRShNaraata lauhitya|tri-veda kRShNa|raata lauhitya|tri-veda kRiShNa-raata lauhitya|tri-veda kRiShNaraata lauhitya|tri-veda kRiShNa|tri-veda kRuShNa-raata lauhitya|tri-veda kRuShNaraata lauhitya|tri-veda kRuShNa|tri-veda kRRiShNa-raata lauhitya|tri-veda kRRiShNaraata lauhitya|tri-veda kRRiShNa|_vei_120@1220|_vei1_pn330
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1219">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1221">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn329">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn330">1:330</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn331">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tri-<a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण">kṛṣṇa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="रात">rāta</a> Lauhitya</b> (‘descendant of Lohita’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्यामजयन्त लौहित्य">śyāmajayanta lauhitya</a>,</b> according to a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 42, 1). </p></div>

त्रि-शंकु|त्रिशंकु|त्रि|शंकु|त्रि-शङ्कु|त्रिशङ्कु|शङ्कु|త్రి-శంకు|త్రిశంకు|త్రి|శంకు|త్రి-శఙ్కు|త్రిశఙ్కు|శఙ్కు|ತ್ರಿ-ಶಂಕು|ತ್ರಿಶಂಕು|ತ್ರಿ|ಶಂಕು|ತ್ರಿ-ಶಙ್ಕು|ತ್ರಿಶಙ್ಕು|ಶಙ್ಕು|tri-śaṅku|triśaṅku|tri|śaṅku|tri-shaMku|trishaMku|shaMku|tri-shanku|trishanku|shanku|_vei_120@1221|_vei1_pn331
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1220">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1222">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn330">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn331">1:331</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn332">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tri-śaṅku</b> is in Vedic literature the name of a sage mentioned as a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201221"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201221">1</a></sup> There is no trace of the later legend by which he becomes the victim of <b>Vasiṣṭha's</b> curse and the object of <b>Viśvāmitra's</b> solicitude, being eventually fixed in the sky as a constellation.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201221"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201221">2</a></sup> The confusion of the chronology in the tales of <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रिशंकु">triśaṅku</a> is a good example of the worthlessness of the supposed epic tradition.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201221"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201221">↑</a> i. 10, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201221"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201221">↑</a> See Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 362, 375 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

त्रि-शोक|त्रिशोक|त्रि|शोक|త్రి-శోక|త్రిశోక|త్రి|శోక|ತ್ರಿ-ಶೋಕ|ತ್ರಿಶೋಕ|ತ್ರಿ|ಶೋಕ|tri-śoka|triśoka|tri|śoka|tri-shoka|trishoka|shoka|_vei_120@1222|_vei1_pn331
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1221">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1223">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn330">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn331">1:331</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn332">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tri-śoka</b> is the name of an ancient mythical seer who is mentioned both in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201222"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201222">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201222"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201222">2</a></sup> A <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a>, or chant, named after him is referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201222"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201222">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201222"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201222">↑</a> i. 112, 13; viii. 45, 30. In x. 29, 2, the word seems merely to be an adjective, meaning ‘with triple splendour.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201222"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201222">↑</a> iv. 29, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201222"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201222">↑</a> viii. 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 107, 162; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 33.</li></ol></div>

त्रै-ककुद|त्रैककुद|त्रै|ककुद|త్రై-కకుద|త్రైకకుద|త్రై|కకుద|ತ್ರೈ-ಕಕುದ|ತ್ರೈಕಕುದ|ತ್ರೈ|ಕಕುದ|trai-kakuda|traikakuda|trai|kakuda|_vei_120@1223|_vei1_pn331
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1222">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1224">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn330">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn331">1:331</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn332">↷</a><hr><p><b>Trai-kakuda.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रिककुद्">trikakud</a>.</b></p></div>

त्रैतन|త్రైతన|ತ್ರೈತನ|traitana|_vei_120@1224|_vei1_pn331
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1223">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1225">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn330">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn331">1:331</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn332">↷</a><hr><p><b>Traitana</b> appears in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201224"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201224">1</a></sup> as a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>,</b> an enemy of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दीर्घतमस्">dīrghatamas</a>,</b> who seems to have engaged him in single combat and defeated him. The St. Petersburg Dictionary suggests that he is rather a supernatural being allied to <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रित">trita</a> (<i>cf.</i> the Avestan Thrita and Thraetaona).<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201224"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201224">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201224"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201224">↑</a> i. 158, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201224"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201224">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 68. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 151; Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 144.</li></ol></div>

त्रै-धात्व|त्रैधात्व|त्रै|धात्व|త్రై-ధాత్వ|త్రైధాత్వ|త్రై|ధాత్వ|ತ್ರೈ-ಧಾತ್ವ|ತ್ರೈಧಾತ್ವ|ತ್ರೈ|ಧಾತ್ವ|trai-dhātva|traidhātva|trai|dhātva|trai-dhaatva|traidhaatva|dhaatva|_vei_120@1225|_vei1_pn331
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1224">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1226">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn330">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn331">1:331</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn332">↷</a><hr><p><b>Trai-dhātva</b> (‘descendant of Tridhātu,’) is the patronymic of <b>Tryaruṇa</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 3, 12).</p></div>

त्रै-पद|त्रैपद|त्रै|पद|త్రై-పద|త్రైపద|త్రై|పద|ತ್ರೈ-ಪದ|ತ್ರೈಪದ|ತ್ರೈ|ಪದ|trai-pada|traipada|trai|pada|_vei_120@1226|_vei1_pn331
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1225">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1227">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn330">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn331">1:331</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn332">↷</a><hr><p><b>Trai-pada,</b> neut., occurs as a measure of distance, ‘threequarters’ of a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="योजन">yojana</a>,</b> in the Pañcāviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, where half a <a class="vei_120a" href="योजन">yojana</a> is termed <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गव्यूति">gavyūti</a></b> and a quarter <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रोश">krośa</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201226"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201226">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201226"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201226">↑</a> xvi. 13. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiv. 41, 12.</li></ol></div>

त्रैवणि|త్రైవణి|ತ್ರೈವಣಿ|traivaṇi|traivaNi|_vei_120@1227|_vei1_pn332
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1226">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1228">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn331">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn332">1:332</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn333">↷</a><hr><p><b>Traivaṇi</b> is mentioned as a pupil of <b>Aupacandhani</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="औपजन्धनि">aupajandhani</a></b> in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201227">1</a></sup> In the Mādhyaṃdina recension<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201227">2</a></sup> his name occurs twice in the second <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a>, in both cases as a pupil of <a class="vei_120a" href="औपजन्धनि">aupajandhani</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201227">↑</a> ii. 6, 3 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> = ii. 5, 21 Mādhyaṃdina); iv. 6, 3 (= iv. 5, 27).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201227">↑</a> iv. 5, 27.</li></ol></div>

त्रै-वृष्ण|त्रैवृष्ण|त्रै|वृष्ण|త్రై-వృష్ణ|త్రైవృష్ణ|త్రై|వృష్ణ|ತ್ರೈ-ವೃಷ್ಣ|ತ್ರೈವೃಷ್ಣ|ತ್ರೈ|ವೃಷ್ಣ|trai-vṛṣṇa|traivṛṣṇa|trai|vṛṣṇa|trai-vRShNa|traivRShNa|vRShNa|trai-vRiShNa|traivRiShNa|vRiShNa|trai-vRuShNa|traivRuShNa|vRuShNa|trai-vRRiShNa|traivRRiShNa|vRRiShNa|_vei_120@1228|_vei1_pn332
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1227">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1229">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn331">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn332">1:332</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn333">↷</a><hr><p><b>Trai-vṛṣṇa,</b> ‘descendant of Trivṛṣan,’ is the patronymic of <b>Trayaruṇa</b> in the Rigveda (v. 27, 1).</p></div>

त्र्य्-अरुण त्रै-वृष्ण त्रसदस्यु|त्र्य्-अरुण त्रैवृष्ण त्रसदस्यु|त्र्य्-अरुण त्रै|वृष्ण त्रसदस्यु|త్ర్య్-అరుణ త్రై-వృష్ణ త్రసదస్యు|త్ర్య్-అరుణ త్రైవృష్ణ త్రసదస్యు|త్ర్య్-అరుణ త్రై|వృష్ణ త్రసదస్యు|ತ್ರ್ಯ್-ಅರುಣ ತ್ರೈ-ವೃಷ್ಣ ತ್ರಸದಸ್ಯು|ತ್ರ್ಯ್-ಅರುಣ ತ್ರೈವೃಷ್ಣ ತ್ರಸದಸ್ಯು|ತ್ರ್ಯ್-ಅರುಣ ತ್ರೈ|ವೃಷ್ಣ ತ್ರಸದಸ್ಯು|try-aruṇa trai-vṛṣṇa trasadasyu|try-aruṇa traivṛṣṇa trasadasyu|try-aruṇa trai|vṛṣṇa trasadasyu|try-aruNa trai-vRShNa trasadasyu|try-aruNa traivRShNa trasadasyu|try-aruNa trai|vRShNa trasadasyu|try-aruNa trai-vRiShNa trasadasyu|try-aruNa traivRiShNa trasadasyu|vRiShNa trasadasyu|try-aruNa trai-vRuShNa trasadasyu|try-aruNa traivRuShNa trasadasyu|vRuShNa trasadasyu|try-aruNa trai-vRRiShNa trasadasyu|try-aruNa traivRRiShNa trasadasyu|vRRiShNa trasadasyu|_vei_120@1229|_vei1_pn332
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1228">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1230">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn331">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn332">1:332</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn333">↷</a><hr><p><b>Try-aruṇa <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रै-वृष्ण">trai-vṛṣṇa</a> Trasadasyu</b> is the name of a prince whose generosity to a singer is celebrated in a hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201229"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201229">1</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201229"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201229">2</a></sup> he appears as <b>Tryaruṇa <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रैधात्व">traidhātva</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐक्ष्वाक">aikṣvāka</a>,</b> and is the hero of the following story. He was out in his chariot with his <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, or domestic priest, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वृश जान">vṛśa jāna</a>,</b> and by excessive speed in driving killed a Brahmin boy. This sin was atoned for by the Purohita's using his Vārśa <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> (chant). The <a class="vei_120a" href="शाट्यायन">śāṭyāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, cited by Sāyaṇa,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201229"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201229">3</a></sup> elaborates the tale. As Vṛśa had held the reins, king and priest accused each other of the murder. The Ikṣvākus being consulted threw the responsibility for the crime on Vṛśa, who thereupon revived the boy by the Vārśa <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a>. In consequence of this unfairness of theirs--being Kṣatriyas they were partial to a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>--Agni's glow ceased to burn in their houses. In response to their appeal to restore it, Vṛśa came to them, saw the Piśācī (demoness), who, in the form of Trasadasyu's wife, had stolen the glow, and succeeded in restoring it to <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>. This version with some variations occurs also in the Bṛhaddevatā,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201229"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201229">4</a></sup> which connects the story with a hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201229"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201229">5</a></sup> Sieg's attempt<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201229"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201229">6</a></sup> to show that the hymn really refers to this tale is not at all successful.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201229"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201229">7</a></sup> </p><p>It is clear that <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a> must here mean ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a>,’ and not King <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a> himself. The difference of the patronymics, <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रैवृष्ण">traivṛṣṇa</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रैधात्व">traidhātva</a>, by which he is referred to can best be explained by assuming that there were two kings, Trivṛṣan and Tridhātu (or possibly Tridhanvan), from whom Tryaruṇa was descended.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201229"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201229">8</a></sup> The connexion with the Ikṣvākus is important (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इक्ष्वाकु">ikṣvāku</a></b>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201229"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201229">↑</a> v. 27, 1-3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201229"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201229">↑</a> xiii. 3, 12. <i>Cf.</i> the Tāṇḍaka recension, cited in Sāyaṇa, on Rv. v. 2, where <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a> is given as the king's name.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201229"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201229">↑</a> On Rv., <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i> See also the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> version in Oertel, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201229"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201229">↑</a> v. 14 <i>et seq.,</i> with Macdonell's notes.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201229"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201229">↑</a> v. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201229"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201229">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 64-76. <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Festtgruss an Roth,</i> 192.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201229"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201229">↑</a> See Oldenberg, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 46, 366 <i>et seq.; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 312; Hillebrandt, <i>Go7ttingische Gelehrte Anzeigen,</i> 1903, 240 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201229"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201229">↑</a> See Sieg, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 74-76, and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

त्र्य्-अवि|त्र्यवि|त्र्य्|अवि|త్ర్య్-అవి|త్ర్యవి|త్ర్య్|అవి|ತ್ರ್ಯ್-ಅವಿ|ತ್ರ್ಯವಿ|ತ್ರ್ಯ್|ಅವಿ|try-avi|tryavi|try|avi|_vei_120@1230|_vei1_pn333
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1229">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1231">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn332">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn333">1:333</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn334">↷</a><hr><p><b>Try-avi</b> designates a calf eighteen months old<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201230"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201230">1</a></sup> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201230"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201230">2</a></sup> and later Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201230"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201230">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201230"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201230">↑</a> The etymological meaning is apparently ‘having three sheep (periods)’ -that is, ‘having periods of thrice six months,’ just as the adjective <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चावि">pañcāvi</a></i> means ‘having five periods of six months,’ or ‘thirty months old.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201230"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201230">↑</a> iii. 55, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201230"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201230">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 2; xviii. 12, etc. (in the form <i><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रियवि">triyavi</a></i>); Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiv. 10; xviii. 26, etc.</li></ol></div>

त्र्य्-आशिर्|त्र्याशिर्|त्र्य्|आशिर्|త్ర్య్-ఆశిర్|త్ర్యాశిర్|త్ర్య్|ఆశిర్|ತ್ರ್ಯ್-ಆಶಿರ್|ತ್ರ್ಯಾಶಿರ್|ತ್ರ್ಯ್|ಆಶಿರ್|try-āśir|tryāśir|try|āśir|try-aashir|tryaashir|aashir|_vei_120@1231|_vei1_pn333
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1230">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1232">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn332">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn333">1:333</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn334">↷</a><hr><p><b>Try-āśir,</b> ‘with three admixtures,’ is an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201231"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201231">1</a></sup> According to Sāyaṇa this means mixed with curds (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="दधि">dadhi</a></b>), meal (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="सक्तु">saktu</a></b>), and milk (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="पयस्">payas</a></b>). More accurately it would seem<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201231"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201231">2</a></sup> to denote the milk (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="गवाशिर्">gavāśir</a></i>), the barley (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="यवाशिर्">yavāśir</a></i>), and the curds (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दध्याशिर्">dadhyāśir</a></i>), which were used to mix with the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201231"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201231">↑</a> v. 27, 5. <i>Cf.</i> perhaps viii. 2, 7 (<i>traya Indrasya somāḥ sutāsaḥ,</i> ‘three kinds of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> pressed for Indra’).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201231"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201231">↑</a> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologic,</i> 1, 209; Oldenberg, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 46, 422.</li></ol></div>

त्वच्|త్వచ్|ತ್ವಚ್|tvac|tvach|_vei_120@1232|_vei1_pn333
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1231">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1233">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn332">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn333">1:333</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn334">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tvac,</b> ‘skin,’ ‘hide,’ (<i>a</i>) denotes specially in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201232">1</a></sup> the hide used in the process of extracting the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a></b> juice from the plant. The <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> was pounded with stones (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अद्रि">adri</a></i>) upon the skin laid on the pressing boards (<i>adhiṣavaṇe phalake</i>),<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201232">2</a></sup> which, however, are not mentioned in the Rigveda. Or if a pestle and mortar were used, the skin was still placed underneath them to catch the drops of juice, not above, as Pischel<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201232">3</a></sup> thought. </p><p>(<i>b</i>) <a class="vei_120a" href="त्वच्">tvac</a> also denotes the rind of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> plant that remains after the juice has been extracted.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201232">4</a></sup></p><p>(<i>c</i>) Metaphorically the term <i>kṛṣṇā <a class="vei_120a" href="त्वच्">tvac</a>,</i> ‘the black skins,’ is applied to the aboriginal enemies of the invading Āryans.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201232">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201232">↑</a> i. 79, 3; iii. 21, 5; ix. 65, 25; 66, 29; 70, 7; 79, 4; 101, 11, 16, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201232">↑</a> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologic,</i> 1, 181-183, and <b>Adhisavaṇa.</b></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201232">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 110.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201232">↑</a> Rv. ix. 86, 44; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 7, 13, 1; Hillebrandt, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 52.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201232">↑</a> Rv. i. 130, 8, and probably ix. 41, 1, for which, however, <i>cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 51, n. 2, and see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

त्वष्टृ|త్వష్టృ|ತ್ವಷ್ಟೃ|tvaṣṭṛ|tvaShTR|tvaShTRi|tvaShTRu|tvaShTRRi|_vei_120@1233|_vei1_pn334
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1232">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1234">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn333">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn334">1:334</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn335">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tvaṣṭṛ</b> is employed once in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201233"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201233">1</a></sup> to denote a ‘carpenter,’ with a deliberate play on the name of the god <a class="vei_120a" href="त्वष्टृ">tvaṣṭṛ</a>. He is there mentioned as using an axe (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्वधिति">svadhiti</a></i>) to fashion (from wood) ‘a well-made form’ (<i>rūpaṃ sukṛtam</i>). See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तष्टृ">taṣṭṛ</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201233"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201233">↑</a> xii. 3, 33. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 688; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 651.</li></ol></div>

त्वाष्ट्र|త్వాష్ట్ర|ತ್ವಾಷ್ಟ್ರ|tvāṣṭra|tvaaShTra|_vei_120@1234|_vei1_pn334
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1233">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1235">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn333">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn334">1:334</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn335">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tvāṣṭra,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="त्वष्टृ">tvaṣṭṛ</a>,’ is the patronymic, in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201234"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201234">1</a></sup> of the mythical teacher <b>Ābhūti.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201234"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201234">↑</a> ii. 6, 3 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> = ii. 5, 22 Mādhyaṃdina); iv. 6, 3 (= iv. 5, 28).</li></ol></div>

त्सरु|త్సరు|ತ್ಸರು|tsaru|_vei_120@1235|_vei1_pn334
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1234">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1236">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn333">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn334">1:334</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn335">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tsaru,</b>--(<i>a</i>) This word seems to denote some sort of crawling animal in one passage of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201235"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201235">1</a></sup></p><p>(<i>b</i>) In the later literature the word means a ‘handle,’ as of a beaker (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="चमस">camasa</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1201235"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1201235">1b</a></sup> In this sense also it seems to occur in the description of the plough (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="लांगल">lāṅgala</a></b>) in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2b_vei_1201235"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2b_vei_1201235">2b</a></sup> and the later Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_3b_vei_1201235"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3b_vei_1201235">3b</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201235"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201235">↑</a> vii. 50, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 99.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1201235"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1201235">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 4. <i>Cf.</i> Lāṭyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 12, 12, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2b_vei_1201235"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2b_vei_1201235">↑</a> iii. 17, 3, where the ordinary text has <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>-satsaru</i> (so the <a class="vei_120a" href="पद">pada</a> text), and the Paippalāda recension has <i>somapitsalam.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3b_vei_1201235"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3b_vei_1201235">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 5, 6, has <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सुमति-त्सरु">sumati-tsaru</a>;</i> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 7, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvi. 12; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xii. 71; <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 34, have <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सोमपित्सरु">somapitsaru</a>,</i> which <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> renders as ‘provided with a handle for the drinker of Soma’ (<i>i.e., <a class="vei_120a" href="सोमपि-त्सरु">somapi-tsaru</a></i>). Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 255, suggests <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>-sa-<a class="vei_120a" href="त्सरु">tsaru</a>,</i> ‘with (<i>sa-</i>) strap (<i>uman,</i> a conjectural word) and handle’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="त्सरु">tsaru</a></i>). Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 116, prefers to read throughout <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सुमति-त्सरु">sumati-tsaru</a>,</i> ‘with well-smoothed handle,’ from the root seen in <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मती">matī</a>-kṛ,</i> etc. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 236; Bühler, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 14, 13.</li></ol></div>

त्सारिन्|త్సారిన్|ತ್ಸಾರಿನ್|tsārin|tsaarin|_vei_120@1236|_vei1_pn335
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1235">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1237">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn334">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn335">1:335</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn336">↷</a><hr><p><b>Tsārin</b> denotes, in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201236"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201236">1</a></sup> a ‘hunter’ engaged in the chase of the <i>takva</i> (an unknown beast), according to Ludwig and Max Müller.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201236"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201236">2</a></sup> But this explanation is quite conjectural.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201236"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201236">↑</a> i. 134, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201236"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201236">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 448.</li></ol></div>

दंश|దంశ|ದಂಶ|daṃśa|daMsha|_vei_120@1237|_vei1_pn335
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1236">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1238">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn334">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn335">1:335</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn336">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daṃśa</b> (lit., ‘biter’), ‘gad-fly,’ is mentioned in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 9, 3; 10, 2).</p></div>

दंष्ट्र|దంష్ట్ర|ದಂಷ್ಟ್ರ|daṃṣṭra|daMShTra|_vei_120@1238|_vei1_pn335
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1237">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1239">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn334">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn335">1:335</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn336">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daṃṣṭra,</b> denoting a prominent tooth, ‘tusk,’ or ‘fang’ of an animal, occurs often from the Rigveda onwards.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201238"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201238">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201238"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201238">↑</a> Rv. ii. 13, 4; x. 87, 3; Av. iv. 36, 2; x. 5, 43; xvi. 7, 3, etc.</li></ol></div>

दक्ष कात्यायनि आत्रेय|దక్ష కాత్యాయని ఆత్రేయ|ದಕ್ಷ ಕಾತ್ಯಾಯನಿ ಆತ್ರೇಯ|dakṣa kātyāyani ātreya|dakSha kaatyaayani aatreya|daxa kaatyaayani aatreya|_vei_120@1239|_vei1_pn335
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1238">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1240">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn334">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn335">1:335</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn336">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dakṣa <a class="vei_120a" href="कात्यायनि">kātyāyani</a> Ātreya</b> (‘descendant of Atri’) is mentioned in the Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) of the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 41, 1; iv. 17, 1) as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शंख बाभ्रव्य">śaṅkha bābhravya</a>.</b></p></div>

दक्ष जयन्त लौहित्य|దక్ష జయన్త లౌహిత్య|ದಕ್ಷ ಜಯನ್ತ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|dakṣa jayanta lauhitya|dakSha jayanta lauhitya|daxa jayanta lauhitya|_vei_120@1240|_vei1_pn335
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1239">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1241">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn334">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn335">1:335</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn336">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dakṣa <a class="vei_120a" href="जयन्त">jayanta</a> Lauhitya</b> (‘descendant of Lohita’) is mentioned in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 42, 1) as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्णरात लौहित्य">kṛṣṇarāta lauhitya</a>.</b></p></div>

दक्ष पार्वति|దక్ష పార్వతి|ದಕ್ಷ ಪಾರ್ವತಿ|dakṣa pārvati|dakSha paarvati|daxa paarvati|_vei_120@1241|_vei1_pn335
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1240">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1242">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn334">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn335">1:335</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn336">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dakṣa Pārvati</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्वत">parvata</a></b>’) is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201241"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201241">1</a></sup> as having performed a certain rite which his descendants, the Dākṣāyaṇas, still maintained, thus enjoying royal dignity down to the time of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> itself. He appears in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201241"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201241">2</a></sup> also.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201241"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201241">↑</a> ii. 4, 4, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201241"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201241">↑</a> iv. 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 223; 4, 358; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 374 <i>et seq.;</i> Lévi, <i>La Doctrine du Sacrifice,</i> 138.</li></ol></div>

दक्षिणतस्-कपर्द|दक्षिणतस्कपर्द|दक्षिणतस्|दक्षिणत|कपर्द|దక్షిణతస్-కపర్ద|దక్షిణతస్కపర్ద|దక్షిణతస్|దక్షిణత|కపర్ద|ದಕ್ಷಿಣತಸ್-ಕಪರ್ದ|ದಕ್ಷಿಣತಸ್ಕಪರ್ದ|ದಕ್ಷಿಣತಸ್|ದಕ್ಷಿಣತ|ಕಪರ್ದ|dakṣiṇatas-kaparda|dakṣiṇataskaparda|dakṣiṇatas|dakṣiṇata|kaparda|dakShiNatas-kaparda|dakShiNataskaparda|dakShiNatas|dakShiNata|daxiNatas-kaparda|daxiNataskaparda|daxiNatas|daxiNata|_vei_120@1242|_vei1_pn335
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1241">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1243">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn334">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn335">1:335</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn336">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dakṣiṇatas-kaparda</b> is an epithet of the <b>Vasiṣṭhas</b> in the Rigveda (vii. 33, 1) referring to their mode of ‘wearing the hair in a braid on the right side.’ See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कपर्द">kaparda</a>.</b> </p></div>

दक्षिणा|దక్షిణా|ದಕ್ಷಿಣಾ|dakṣiṇā|dakShiNaa|daxiNaa|_vei_120@1243|_vei1_pn336
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1242">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1244">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn335">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn336">1:336</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn337">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dakṣiṇā</b> appears repeatedly in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201243"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201243">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201243"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201243">2</a></sup> as the designation of the gift presented to priests at the sacrifice, apparently because a cow--a prolific (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दक्षिणा">dakṣiṇā</a></i>) one--was the usual ‘fee’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201243"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201243">3</a></sup> on such an occasion.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201243"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201243">4</a></sup> The later <a class="vei_120a" href="दान">dāna</a>-stutis, or ‘Praises of Gifts,’ in the Rigveda immensely exaggerate these donations, and the exaggeration grows in the Brāhmaṇas. It is important to notice that these enumerations of gifts in the main include nothing but articles of personal property, such as kine, horses, buffaloes, or camels (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="उष्ट्र">uṣṭra</a></i>), ornaments, and so forth, but not land.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201243"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201243">5</a></sup> Reference is, however, made in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201243"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201243">6</a></sup> to land as a <a class="vei_120a" href="दक्षिणा">dakṣiṇā</a>, but with disapproval, probably because the land came to be regarded as inalienable without the consent of the clansmen.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201243"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201243">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201243"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201243">↑</a> A whole hymn, Rv. x. 107, is devoted to its praise. <i>Cf.</i> i. 168, 7; vi. 27, 8; viii. 24, 29; 39, 5; x. 62, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201243"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201243">↑</a> Av. iv. 11, 4; v. 7, 11; xi. 7, 9; 8, 22; xiii. 1, 52; xviii. 4, 8, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 7, 3, 1; 8, 1, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iv. 19, 23; xix. 30; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 3, 3 <i>et seq.;</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 9, 3, 1 <i>et seq.</i> The verses (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="नाराशंसी">nārāśaṃsī</a>,</i> either as a single expression or as two separate terms) used to win these Dakṣiṇās were notoriously false. See <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xiv. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 2, 6. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, i. 7; xi. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201243"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201243">↑</a> The transition of meaning is similar in the use of the English word ‘fee’; ‘cattle,’ ‘money,’ ‘payment for service’ (see Murray's English Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> ‘fee’). <i>Cf.</i> also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गो-दान">go-dāna</a>,</b> n. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201243"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201243">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> the rule that when nothing is specified a cow is the <a class="vei_120a" href="दक्षिणा">dakṣiṇā</a>, Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 2, 13; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201243"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201243">↑</a> So, <i>e.g.,</i> Rv. i. 126, 1-4; v. 30, 12-15; viii. 1, 32. 33; 3, 21 <i>et seq.;</i> 4, 19-21; 5, 37-39; 6, 46-48; 55; 56; vii. 18, 21-24, and the full list in Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 273-277. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 49 <i>et seq.</i> Clothes (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वासस्">vāsas</a></i>) and gold are mentioned as a <a class="vei_120a" href="दक्षिणा">dakṣiṇā</a> in Av. ix. 5, 14. The four Dakṣiṇās, according to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 3, 4, 7, are gold, a cow, clothes, and a horse. This is practically exhaustive if the trappings of the horse and ornaments are included.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201243"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201243">↑</a> xiii. 7, 1, 13, with which compare xiii. 6, 2, 18, where the Brahmin's land is excluded; and see xiii. 7, 1, 15, where the gift of land is disapproved.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201243"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201243">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 1, 1, 4. <i>Cf.</i> above, pp. 100, n. 19, 246; below, pp. 351, 352.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 169171; Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 104, 105; Weber, <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 96-98; Bloomfield, <i>Religion of the <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 69-74; <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 76 <i>et seq.,</i> 100, 121.</li></ol></div>

दक्षिणा-पथ|दक्षिणापथ|दक्षिणा|पथ|దక్షిణా-పథ|దక్షిణాపథ|దక్షిణా|పథ|ದಕ್ಷಿಣಾ-ಪಥ|ದಕ್ಷಿಣಾಪಥ|ದಕ್ಷಿಣಾ|ಪಥ|dakṣiṇā-patha|dakṣiṇāpatha|dakṣiṇā|patha|dakShiNaa-patha|dakShiNaapatha|dakShiNaa|daxiNaa-patha|daxiNaapatha|daxiNaa|_vei_120@1244|_vei1_pn336
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1243">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1245">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn335">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn336">1:336</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn337">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dakṣiṇā-patha</b> (lit., ‘the road to the south’), ‘the south country,’ is found, probably as a designation of the Deccan, as early as the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201244">1</a></sup> coupled with  Surāṣṭra. A similar expression is <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दक्षिणा">dakṣiṇā</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पदा">padā</a>,</i> ‘with southward foot,’ in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201244">2</a></sup> referring to the place where the exile (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="परा-वृज्">parā-vṛj</a></i>) goes on being expelled. This no doubt simply means ‘the south’ beyond the limits of the recognized Āryan world, which even as late as the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201244">3</a></sup> appears as bounded by the Vindhyas on the south.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201244">↑</a> i. 1, 2, 13. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 394, n., and <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, v. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201244">↑</a> x. 61, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201244">↑</a> ii. 13.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 185; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 408; Rhys Davids, Buddhist India, 30; Keith <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 28, n. 1; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 200.</li></ol></div>

दक्षिणा-प्रष्टि|दक्षिणाप्रष्टि|दक्षिणा|प्रष्टि|దక్షిణా-ప్రష్టి|దక్షిణాప్రష్టి|దక్షిణా|ప్రష్టి|ದಕ್ಷಿಣಾ-ಪ್ರಷ್ಟಿ|ದಕ್ಷಿಣಾಪ್ರಷ್ಟಿ|ದಕ್ಷಿಣಾ|ಪ್ರಷ್ಟಿ|dakṣiṇā-praṣṭi|dakṣiṇāpraṣṭi|dakṣiṇā|praṣṭi|dakShiNaa-praShTi|dakShiNaapraShTi|dakShiNaa|praShTi|daxiNaa-praShTi|daxiNaapraShTi|daxiNaa|_vei_120@1245|_vei1_pn337
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1244">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1246">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn336">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn337">1:337</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn338">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dakṣiṇā-praṣṭi</b> denotes ‘the side horse on the right.’ It appears from two passages of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201245"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201245">1</a></sup> that there were at times four horses yoked to the chariot, the right and the left yoke horse (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दक्षिणा-युग्य">dakṣiṇā-yugya</a>, savyā-<a class="vei_120a" href="युग्य">yugya</a></i>) in the middle, flanked by one on each side, the two latter of course not being fastened to the yoke at all, but presumably by traces alongside of the yoke horses. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201245"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201245">↑</a> v. 1, 4, 9; ix. 4, 2, 11 (this passage appears to speak of three horses only, but <i>of.</i> v. 4, 3, 17). <i>Cf.</i> also <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvi. 13, 12.</li></ol></div>

दक्षिणायन|దక్షిణాయన|ದಕ್ಷಿಣಾಯನ|dakṣiṇāyana|dakShiNaayana|daxiNaayana|_vei_120@1246|_vei1_pn337
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1245">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1247">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn336">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn337">1:337</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn338">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dakṣiṇāyana.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सूर्य">sūrya</a>.</b></p></div>

दक्षिणा-युग्य|दक्षिणायुग्य|दक्षिणा|युग्य|దక్షిణా-యుగ్య|దక్షిణాయుగ్య|దక్షిణా|యుగ్య|ದಕ್ಷಿಣಾ-ಯುಗ್ಯ|ದಕ್ಷಿಣಾಯುಗ್ಯ|ದಕ್ಷಿಣಾ|ಯುಗ್ಯ|dakṣiṇā-yugya|dakṣiṇāyugya|dakṣiṇā|yugya|dakShiNaa-yugya|dakShiNaayugya|dakShiNaa|daxiNaa-yugya|daxiNaayugya|daxiNaa|_vei_120@1247|_vei1_pn337
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1246">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1248">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn336">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn337">1:337</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn338">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dakṣiṇā-yugya,</b> ‘the yoke horse on the right,’ is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (v. 1, 4, 6; 4, 3, 8; ix. 4, 2, 11). See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a>.</b></p></div>

दण्ड|దణ్డ|ದಣ್ಡ|daṇḍa|daNDa|_vei_120@1248|_vei1_pn337
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1247">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1249">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn336">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn337">1:337</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn338">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daṇḍa,</b> ‘staff.’ (<i>a</i>) This word is often mentioned in the ordinary sense; for example, when used for driving cattle<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201248">1</a></sup> (<i>go-ajanāsaḥ</i>), or as a weapon.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201248">2</a></sup> A staff was given to a man on consecration for driving away demons, according to the Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201248">3</a></sup> The staff also played a part in the initiation (<i>upanayana</i>) of a youth on attaining manhood.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201248">4</a></sup> In a modified sense the word is used to denote the handle of a ladle or similar implement.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201248">5</a></sup> </p><p>(<i>b</i>) The ‘staff’ as the symbol of temporal power, implying punishment, is applied by the king (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="राज">rāja</a>-preṣito daṇḍaḥ</i>).<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1201248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1201248">1b</a></sup> The king, in modern phraseology, was the source of criminal law; and he clearly retained this branch of law in his own hands even in later times.<sup id="ref_120_2b_vei_1201248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2b_vei_1201248">2b</a></sup> The punishment of the non-guilty (<i>a-daṇḍya</i>) is given as one of the characteristics of the nonBrahminical <b>Vrātyas</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3b_vei_1201248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3b_vei_1201248">3b</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201248">↑</a> Rv. vii. 33, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201248">↑</a> Av. v. 5, 4. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 35; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 4, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201248">↑</a> iii. 2, 1, 32.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201248">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 19; 22; Sāṅkhāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 1, 6, 11, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201248">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 5; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 4, 1, 36. Of a musical instrument, Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, viii. 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvii. 3, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1201248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1201248">↑</a> Pāraskara <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 15. <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 4, 7, where the king, being himself ‘exempt from punishment’ (<i>a-daṇḍya</i>), inflicts judicial punishment (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दण्ड">daṇḍa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वध">vadha</a></i>).</li><li id="fx_120_2b_vei_1201248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2b_vei_1201248">↑</a> Foy, <i>Die ko7nigliche Gewalt,</i> 21 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3b_vei_1201248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3b_vei_1201248">↑</a> xvii. 1, 9; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 33.</li></ol></div>

दण्ड औपर|దణ్డ ఔపర|ದಣ್ಡ ಔಪರ|daṇḍa aupara|daNDa aupara|_vei_120@1249|_vei1_pn338
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1248">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1250">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn337">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn338">1:338</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn339">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daṇḍa Aupara</b> (‘descendant of Upara’) is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (vi. 2, 9, 4) and the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā (iii. 8, 7) as having performed a certain rite.</p></div>

दण्डन|దణ్డన|ದಣ್ಡನ|daṇḍana|daNDana|_vei_120@1250|_vei1_pn338
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1249">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1251">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn337">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn338">1:338</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn339">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daṇḍana</b> occurs in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201250"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201250">1</a></sup> among other names of ‘reed’ or ‘cane.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201250"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201250">↑</a> xii. 2, 54. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 682.</li></ol></div>

दत्त तापस|దత్త తాపస|ದತ್ತ ತಾಪಸ|datta tāpasa|datta taapasa|_vei_120@1251|_vei1_pn338
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1250">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1252">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn337">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn338">1:338</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn339">↷</a><hr><p><b>Datta Tāpasa</b> was <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a> priest at the snake festival described in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201251">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201251">↑</a> xxv. 15, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 35.</li></ol></div>

दधि|దధి|ದಧಿ|dadhi|_vei_120@1252|_vei1_pn338
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1251">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1253">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn337">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn338">1:338</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn339">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dadhi,</b> ‘sour milk,’ is repeatedly mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201252">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201252">2</a></sup> The Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201252">3</a></sup> mentions in order <b>Ghrta</b> (‘clarified butter’), <a class="vei_120a" href="दधि">dadhi</a>, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मस्तु">mastu</a>,</b> which Eggeling<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201252">4</a></sup> renders ‘whey,’ and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आमिक्षा">āmikṣā</a>,</b> ‘curds.’ <a class="vei_120a" href="दधि">dadhi</a> often has the meaning of ‘curds’ also. It was used for mixing with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201252">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201252">↑</a> viii. 2, 9; ix. 87, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201252">↑</a> Av. iii. 12, 7; iv. 34, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 3, 4, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xviii. 5, 12, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201252">↑</a> i. 8, 1, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 348.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201252">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 218.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201252">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दध्याशिर्">dadhyāśir</a>,</i> ‘mixed with sour milk,’ is an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> in Rv. i. 5, 5; 137, 2; v. 51, 7; vii. 32, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 219 <i>et seq.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 227.</li></ol></div>

दध्यञ्च् आथर्वण|దధ్యఞ్చ్ ఆథర్వణ|ದಧ್ಯಞ್ಚ್ ಆಥರ್ವಣ|dadhyañc ātharvaṇa|dadhya~nch aatharvaNa|_vei_120@1253|_vei1_pn338
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1252">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1254">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn337">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn338">1:338</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn339">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dadhyañc Ātharvaṇa</b> is a purely mythical sage. In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201253"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201253">1</a></sup> he is clearly a divinity of some kind, but in the later  Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201253"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201253">2</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201253"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201253">3</a></sup> he is metamorphosed into a teacher. In the Pañcaviṃśa Brāhmana<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201253"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201253">4</a></sup> he is by oversight called an <a class="vei_120a" href="आंगिरस">āṅgirasa</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201253"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201253">↑</a> i. 80, 16; 84, 13, 14; 116, 12; 117, 22; 119, 9, etc. See Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> pp. 141, 142; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 176.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201253"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201253">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 1, 4, 4; 6, 6, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xix. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201253"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201253">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 5, 18; vi. 4, 2, 3; xiv. 1, 1, 18. 20. 25; 4, 13; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 5, 22; iv. 5, 28, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201253"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201253">↑</a> xii. 8, 6. So also Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 21.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> xxxv; <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 23, 116, 118, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

दध्य्-आशिर्|दध्याशिर्|दध्य्|आशिर्|దధ్య్-ఆశిర్|దధ్యాశిర్|దధ్య్|ఆశిర్|ದಧ್ಯ್-ಆಶಿರ್|ದಧ್ಯಾಶಿರ್|ದಧ್ಯ್|ಆಶಿರ್|dadhy-āśir|dadhyāśir|dadhy|āśir|dadhy-aashir|dadhyaashir|aashir|_vei_120@1254|_vei1_pn339
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1253">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1255">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn338">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn339">1:339</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn340">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dadhy-āśir.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दधि">dadhi</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>.</b></p></div>

दन्त्|दन्त|దన్త్|దన్త|ದನ್ತ್|ದನ್ತ|dant|danta|_vei_120@1255|_vei1_pn339
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1254">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1256">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn338">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn339">1:339</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn340">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dant, Danta,</b> ‘tooth,’ is frequently mentioned from the Rigveda onwards.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201255">1</a></sup> Cleansing (<i>dhāv</i>) the teeth was an ordinary act, especially in preparation for a sacrifice, and accompanied bathing, shaving of the hair and beard (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="केश">keśa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="श्मश्रु">śmaśru</a></i>), and the cutting of the nails.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201255">2</a></sup> A hymn of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201255">3</a></sup> celebrates the appearance of the first two teeth of a child, though its exact interpretation is doubtful.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201255">4</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201255">5</a></sup> there is a reference to a child's first teeth falling out. The word seems in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201255">6</a></sup> once to denote an elephant's tusk. Whether dentistry was practised is doubtful. The occurrence in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201255">7</a></sup> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्य-दन्त्">hiraṇya-dant</a>,</b> ‘gold-toothed,’ as the name of a man, is perhaps significant, especially as it is certain that the stopping of teeth with gold was known at Rome as early as the legislation of the Twelve Tables.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201255">8</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201255">↑</a> Rv. vii. 55, 2; x. 68, 6; Av. v. 23, 3; 29, 4; vi. 56, 3, etc. The more usual form is <a class="vei_120a" href="दन्त">danta</a>, Rv. iv. 6, 8; vi. 75, 11; Av. iv. 3, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201255">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 6, 2 (not exactly paralleled in <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 1, 2 <i>et seq.</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201255">↑</a> vi. 140.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201255">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 321; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 224; Grill, <i>Hundert Lieder,</i>^2 176; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 540, 541; <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 71; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 386.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201255">↑</a> vii. 14; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201255">↑</a> iv. 6, 8; Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 99; Oldenberg, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 46, 341, 342.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201255">↑</a> ii. 1, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201255">↑</a> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 206. See Wordsworth, <i>Fragments and Specimens of Early Latin,</i> 537.</li></ol></div>

दभीति|దభీతి|ದಭೀತಿ|dabhīti|dabhiiti|dabheeti|_vei_120@1256|_vei1_pn339
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1255">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1257">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn338">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn339">1:339</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn340">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dabhīti</b> appears several times in the Rigveda as a hero or sage. <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> overcomes on his behalf <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चुमुरि">cumuri</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धुनि">dhuni</a>;</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201256"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201256">1</a></sup>  he pressed <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> for <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201256"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201256">2</a></sup> who rewarded him.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201256"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201256">3</a></sup> For him 30,000 <b>Dāsas</b> were sent to sleep,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201256"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201256">4</a></sup> and for him the <b>Dasyus</b> were bound without cords.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201256"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201256">5</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="दभीति">dabhīti</a> also appears, with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वीति">turvīti</a>,</b> as a protégé of the Aśvins.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201256"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201256">6</a></sup> There seems no reason to deny that he was a real person.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201256"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201256">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201256"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201256">↑</a> x. 113, 9; ii. 15, 9; vii. 19, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201256"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201256">↑</a> vi. 20, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201256"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201256">↑</a> vi. 26, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201256"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201256">↑</a> iv. 30, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201256"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201256">↑</a> ii. 13, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201256"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201256">↑</a> i. 112, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201256"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201256">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 155, 157, 158.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 162.</li></ol></div>

दम|దమ|ದಮ|dama|_vei_120@1257|_vei1_pn340
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1256">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1258">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn339">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn340">1:340</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn341">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dama,</b> ‘house,’ is a word that occurs several times in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201257"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201257">1</a></sup> It denotes, according to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201257"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201257">2</a></sup> the place in which a man wields uncontrolled power (from the root <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दम्">dam</a>,</i> ‘control’).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201257"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201257">↑</a> i. 1, 8; 61, 9; 75, 5; 143, 4; ii. 1, 2, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, viii. 24.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201257"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201257">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> But this seems very doubtful in view of the apparent connexion of  and , ‘build,’ in Greek.</li></ol></div>

दम्-पति|दम्पति|दम्|पति|दंपति|दं|దమ్-పతి|దమ్పతి|దమ్|పతి|దంపతి|దం|ದಮ್-ಪತಿ|ದಮ್ಪತಿ|ದಮ್|ಪತಿ|ದಂಪತಿ|ದಂ|dam-pati|dampati|dam|pati|_vei_120@1258|_vei1_pn340
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1257">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1259">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn339">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn340">1:340</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn341">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dam-pati</b> denotes ‘the master of the house’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201258"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201258">1</a></sup> in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201258"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201258">2</a></sup> but is more often used in the dual to designate ‘the master and the mistress,’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201258"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201258">3</a></sup> an expression that may legitimately be deemed to show the high status of women at the time of the Rigveda. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्त्री">strī</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201258"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201258">↑</a> For the form, <i>cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> p. 37, n. 9. Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2. 307 <i>et seq.,</i> argues in favour of the spelling <i>daṃpati</i> (adopted by Geldner in his <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>, Glossar</i>). <i>Cf. patir dan.</i> Rv. i. 149, 2; Oldenberg, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 46, 176, 177.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201258"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201258">↑</a> i. 127, 8; ii. 39, 2; v. 22, 4; viii. 69. 16; 84, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201258"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201258">↑</a> Rv. v. 3, 2; viii. 31, 5; x. 10, 5; 68, 2; 85, 32; 95, 12, etc.; Av. vi. 123, 3; xii. 3, 14; xiv. 2, 9, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 418, 420.</li></ol></div>

दर्भ|దర్భ|ದರ್ಭ|darbha|_vei_120@1259|_vei1_pn340
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1258">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1260">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn339">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn340">1:340</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn341">↷</a><hr><p><b>Darbha</b> is the name of a grass in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201259"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201259">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201259"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201259">2</a></sup> In the Atharvaveda it is used for the calming of anger (<i>manyuśamana</i>),<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201259"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201259">3</a></sup> and as an amulet for protection against the scattering of one's hair or the striking of one's breast.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201259"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201259">4</a></sup> It is also said to be ‘rich in roots’ (<i>bhūri-<a class="vei_120a" href="मूल">mūla</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201259"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201259">5</a></sup> to possess a thousand leaves (<i>sahasra-<a class="vei_120a" href="पर्ण">parṇa</a></i>) and a hundred stalks (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शत">śata</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्ड">kāṇḍa</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201259"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201259">6</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201259"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201259">↑</a> i. 191, 3 (with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शर">śara</a></b> and <b>Kuśara,</b> varieties of grass).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201259"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201259">↑</a> Av. vi. 43, 2; viii. 7, 20; x. 4, 13; xi. 6, 15; xix. 28, 1. etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 5, 1, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201259"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201259">↑</a> Av. vi. 43.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201259"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201259">↑</a> xix. 32, 2. <i>Cf.</i> xix. 30.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201259"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201259">↑</a> Av. vi. 43, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201259"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201259">↑</a> Av. xix. 32, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 70.</li></ol></div>

दर्वि|दर्वी|దర్వి|దర్వీ|ದರ್ವಿ|ದರ್ವೀ|darvi|darvī|darvii|darvee|_vei_120@1260|_vei1_pn341
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1259">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1261">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn340">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn341">1:341</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn342">↷</a><hr><p><b>Darvi,</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दर्वी">darvī</a>,</b> properly denotes a ‘ladle,’ in which sense it is found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201260">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201260">2</a></sup> But the word also means a serpent's ‘hood’ in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201260">3</a></sup> though Zimmer regards it as the name of a serpent.</p><p>^4 <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 95, where he takes <b><a class="vei_120a" href="करिक्रत">karikrata</a></b> also as the name of a snake.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201260">↑</a> v. 6, 9; x. 105, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201260">↑</a> Av. iii. 10, 7; iv. 14, 7; ix. 6, 17, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201260">↑</a> x. 1, 13. See Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 577. Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 153.</li></ol></div>

दर्विदा|దర్విదా|ದರ್ವಿದಾ|darvidā|darvidaa|_vei_120@1261|_vei1_pn341
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1260">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1262">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn340">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn341">1:341</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn342">↷</a><hr><p><b>Darvidā,</b> the ‘woodpecker,’ is mentioned as a victim at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> or horse sacrifice, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201261"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201261">1</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दार्वाघात">dārvāghāta</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201261"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201261">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 13, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 15, Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 3. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 93. The St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> suggests ‘woodpiercer’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दारु">dāru</a>-vidha</i>) as the literal sense. On the etymology, <i>cf.</i> F. W. Thomas's article. ‘The D.Suffix,’ p. 121, in <i>Transactions of the Cambridge Philological Society,</i> 5, part ii.</li></ol></div>

दर्श|దర్శ|ದರ್ಶ|darśa|darsha|_vei_120@1262|_vei1_pn341
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1261">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1263">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn340">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn341">1:341</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn342">↷</a><hr><p><b>Darśa</b> (‘appearance’) denotes the new moon day,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201262"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201262">1</a></sup> usually in opposition to the day of full moon (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पूर्ण-मास">pūrṇa-māsa</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201262"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201262">2</a></sup> Most frequently the word occurs in the compound<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201262"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201262">3</a></sup> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दर्श">darśa</a>-pūrṇamāsau,</i> ‘new and full moon,’ the days of special ritual importance.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201262"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201262">4</a></sup> The order of the first two words here is worthy of note, for it distinctly suggests, though it does not conclusively prove, that the month was reckoned from new moon to new moon, not from full moon to full moon. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201262"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201262">↑</a> Av. vii. 81, 3, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 1, 14; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 2, 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201262"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201262">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 4, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201262"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201262">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> i. 6, 7, 1; 9, 3; ii. 5, 6, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 2, 2, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 5, 11, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201262"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201262">↑</a> Hillebrandt, <i>Das altindische Neuund Vollmondsopfer,</i> Jena, 1880; <i>Rituallitteratur,</i> 111-114; Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 439.</li></ol></div>

दश-ग्व|दशग्व|दश|ग्व|దశ-గ్వ|దశగ్వ|దశ|గ్వ|ದಶ-ಗ್ವ|ದಶಗ್ವ|ದಶ|ಗ್ವ|daśa-gva|daśagva|daśa|gva|dasha-gva|dashagva|dasha|_vei_120@1263|_vei1_pn341
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1262">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1264">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn340">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn341">1:341</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn342">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daśa-gva</b> appears in one hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201263">1</a></sup> as the name of a person who was assisted by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>. The other references in that work,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201263">2</a></sup> however, clearly show the mythical character of the Daśagvas, and of any individual among them. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201263">↑</a> viii. 12, 2</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201263">↑</a> They are mentioned with the Navagvas in i. 62, 4; iii. 39, 5; iv. 51, 4; v. 29, 12; x. 62, 6, and alone in ii. 34, 12. See Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 144 (C).</li></ol></div>

दशतयी|दशतयि|దశతయీ|దశతయి|ದಶತಯೀ|ದಶತಯಿ|daśatayī|daśatayi|dashatayii|dashatayi|dashatayee|_vei_120@1264|_vei1_pn342
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1263">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1265">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn341">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn342">1:342</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn344">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daśatayī</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201264"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201264">1</a></sup> frequently denotes the text of the Rigveda as divided into ten Maṇḍalas.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201264"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201264">↑</a> vii. 8. 20; xi. 16; xii. 40.</li></ol></div>

दश-द्यु|दशद्यु|दश|द्यु|దశ-ద్యు|దశద్యు|దశ|ద్యు|ದಶ-ದ್ಯು|ದಶದ್ಯು|ದಶ|ದ್ಯು|daśa-dyu|daśadyu|daśa|dyu|dasha-dyu|dashadyu|dasha|_vei_120@1265|_vei1_pn342
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1264">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1266">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn341">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn342">1:342</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn344">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daśa-dyu</b> appears twice in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201265">1</a></sup> as the name of a hero, but nothing can be made out regarding him or his relation to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वेतसु">vetasu</a>,</b> who is mentioned in one passage along with him.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201265">↑</a> i. 33, 14; vi. 26, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 55, 328.</li></ol></div>

दशन्|దశన్|ದಶನ್|daśan|dashan|_vei_120@1266|_vei1_pn342
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1265">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1267">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn341">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn342">1:342</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn344">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daśan,</b> ‘ten,’ forms the basis of the numerical system of the Vedic Indians, as it does of the Āryan people generally. But it is characteristic of India<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201266">1</a></sup> that there should be found at a very early period long series of names for very high numerals, whereas the Āryan knowledge did not go beyond 1,000. In the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201266">2</a></sup> the list is 1; 10; 100; 1,000; 10,000 (<i>ayuta</i>); 100,000 (<i>niyuta</i>); 1,000,000 (<i>prayuta</i>); 10,000,000 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अर्बुद">arbuda</a></i>); 100,000,000 (<i>nyarbuda</i>); 1,000,000,000 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="समुद्र">samudra</a></i>); 10,000,000,000 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्य">madhya</a></i>); 100,000,000,000 (<i>anta</i>); 1,000,000,000,000 (<i>parārdha</i>). In the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201266">3</a></sup> the list is the same, but <i>niyuta</i> and <i>prayuta</i> exchange places, and after <i>nyarbuda</i> a new figure (<i>badva</i>) intervenes, thus increasing <i><a class="vei_120a" href="समुद्र">samudra</a></i> to 10,000,000,000, and so on. The <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā has in two places<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201266">4</a></sup> exactly the same list as the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā. The Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201266">5</a></sup> has the list <i>ayuta, prayuta,</i> then <i>ayuta</i> again, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अर्बुद">arbuda</a>, nyarbuda, <a class="vei_120a" href="समुद्र">samudra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मध्य">madhya</a>, anta, parārdha.</i> The <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201266">6</a></sup> has the Vājasaneyi list up to <i>nyarbuda</i> inclusive, then follow <i>nikharvaka, badva, <a class="vei_120a" href="अक्षित">akṣita</a>,</i> and apparently <i>go</i> = 1,000,000,000,000. The Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201266">7</a></sup> list  replaces <i>nikharvaka</i> by <i>nikharva, badva</i> by <i>padma,</i> and ends with <i>akṣitir vyomāntaḥ.</i> The <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201266">8</a></sup> continues the series after <i>nyarbuda</i> with <i>nikharvāda, <a class="vei_120a" href="समुद्र">samudra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सलिल">salila</a>, antya, ananta</i> (= 10 billions).</p><p>But beyond <i>ayuta</i><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201266">9</a></sup> none of these numbers has any vitality. <i>Badva,</i> indeed, occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201266">10</a></sup> but it cannot there have any precise numerical sense;<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201266">11</a></sup> and later on the names of these high numerals are very much confused.</p><p>An arithmetical progression of some interest is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201266">12</a></sup> where occurs a list of sacrificial gifts in which each successive figure doubles the amount of the preceding one. It begins with <i><a class="vei_120a" href="द्वादश">dvādaśa</a>-mānaṃ hiraṇyam,</i> ‘gold to the value of 12’ (the unit being uncertain, but probably the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्णल">kṛṣṇala</a></b>),<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201266">13</a></sup> followed by ‘to the value of 24, 48, 96, 192, 384, 768, 1,536, 3072,’ then <i>dve aṣṭāviṃśati-<a class="vei_120a" href="शत">śata</a>-māne,</i> which must mean 2×128×24 (the last unit being not a single <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मान">māna</a>,</i> but a number of 24 <i>mānas</i>) = 6,144, then 12,288, 24,576, 49,152, 98,304, 196,608, 393, 216. With these large numbers may be compared the minute theoretical subdivision of time found in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201266">14</a></sup> where a day is divided into 15 <i>muhūrtas</i>--1 <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मुहूर्त">muhūrta</a></i> = 15 <i>kṣipras,</i> 1 <i><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षिप्र">kṣipra</a></i> = 15 <i>etarhis,</i> 1 <i>etarhi</i> = 15 <i>idānis,</i> 1 <i>idāni</i> = 15 <i>prāṇas.</i> The <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201266">15</a></sup> has a decimal division of the day into 15 <i>muhūrtas</i>-1 <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मुहूर्त">muhūrta</a></i> = 10 <i>nimeṣas,</i> 1 <i>nimeṣa</i> = 10 <i>dhvaṃsis.</i></p><p>Few fractions are mentioned in Vedic literature. <i>Ardha, <a class="vei_120a" href="पाद">pāda</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शफ">śapha</a>,</i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कला">kalā</a></i> denote (1/2), (1/4), (1/8), (1/16) respectively, but only  the first two are common. <i>Tṛtīya</i> denotes the third part.<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1201266">16</a></sup> In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1201266">17</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> and Viṣṇu are said to have divided 1,000 by 3, though how they did so is uncertain. <i><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रि">tri</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पाद्">pād</a></i> denotes ‘three-fourths.’<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1201266">18</a></sup></p><p>There is no clear evidence that the Indians of the Vedic period had any knowledge of numerical figures, though it is perfectly possible.<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1201266">19</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201266">↑</a> Thibaut, <i>Astronomie, Astrologie und Mathematik,</i> 70.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201266">↑</a> xvii. 2 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> xxii. 34; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 1, 2, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201266">↑</a> xxxix. 6. In xvii. 10 the number <i>badva</i> disappears, and the list corresponds with that of the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, except for the fact that <i>niyuta</i> and <i>prayuta</i> change places.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201266">↑</a> iv. 4, 11, 4; vii. 2, 20, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201266">↑</a> ii. 8, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201266">↑</a> xvii. 14, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201266">↑</a> i. 10, 28, 29. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, v. 3, 2; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 30, n. 2; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka</i> 293, 294.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201266">↑</a> xv. 11, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201266">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. iii. 6, 15; viii. 1, 5; 2, 41, 21, 18; 34, 15; 46, 22; Av. viii. 2, 21; 8, 7; x. 8, 24; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xix. 13, 6; xxi. 18, 3, etc. Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 348, considers that it has not any definite sense in the Rigveda; this cannot be either proved or disproved. The Rv. has the phrase <i>śatā sahasrāṇi</i> several times (iv. 32, 18; viii. 32, 18, etc.) = 100,000; and <i>ayuta</i> may easily have been already specialized, though it may also have retained a vague sense.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201266">↑</a> vii. 21. 23.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201266">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 96.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201266">↑</a> xviii. 3. <i>Cf.</i> Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 10, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxii. 9, 1-6.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201266">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxii. 9, 1; Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 102, 103.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201266">↑</a> xii. 3, 2, 1 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> also <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 10, 1, 1, where a series of names of the divisions of the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मुहूर्त">muhūrta</a></i> is given, apparently as alternatives, not as successive stages (<i>idānīm, tadānīm, etarhi, kṣipram, ajiram, āśuḥ (? <a class="vei_120a" href="आशु">āśu</a>), nimeṣaḥ, phaṇaḥ, dravan, atidravan, tvaran, tvaramāṇaḥ, āśuḥ, āśīyān, javaḥ</i>). See Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 92-94.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201266">↑</a> xiv. 75 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Araṇyaka, vii. 20.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1201266">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 1, 4; v. 2, 6, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 6, 1; 7, 1, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8, 4, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1201266">↑</a> vi. 69, 8 = Av. vii. 44, 1 = <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 2, 11, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 15; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 1, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1201266">↑</a> Rv. x. 90, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1201266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1201266">↑</a> If <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अष्ट-कर्णी">aṣṭa-karṇī</a></i> means in Rv. x. 62, 7, having the figure 8 marked on the ears' of cattle, then the mention of numerical signs would be certain. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 234, 235, 348. But this is doubtful. See Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> p. 309, n. 10.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> p. 308; Weber, <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 90-103; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 349; Kaegi, <i>Rigveda,</i> n. 65; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, 275 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

दश-पुरुषं-राज्य|दश-पुरुषंराज्य|दश-पुरुषं|राज्य|దశ-పురుషం-రాజ్య|దశ-పురుషంరాజ్య|దశ-పురుషం|రాజ్య|ದಶ-ಪುರುಷಂ-ರಾಜ್ಯ|ದಶ-ಪುರುಷಂರಾಜ್ಯ|ದಶ-ಪುರುಷಂ|ರಾಜ್ಯ|daśa-puruṣaṃ-rājya|daśa-puruṣaṃrājya|daśa-puruṣaṃ|rājya|dasha-puruShaM-raajya|dasha-puruShaMraajya|dasha-puruShaM|raajya|_vei_120@1267|_vei1_pn344
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1266">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1268">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn342">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn344">1:344</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn345">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daśa-puruṣaṃ-rājya,</b> occurring in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201267"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201267">1</a></sup> doubtless<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201267"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201267">2</a></sup> means ‘sovereignty inherited through ten ancestors,’ a striking case of hereditary rule. Weber<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201267"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201267">3</a></sup> once rendered the word as the ‘kingdom<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201267"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201267">4</a></sup> of Daśapuru,’ comparing the Daśapura of Kālidāsa's Meghadūta<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201267"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201267">5</a></sup> and the Daśārṇa of the ‘middle country.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201267"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201267">↑</a> xii. 9, 3, 1. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201267"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201267">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ix. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 14, 18. So <i><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रि">tri</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुष">puruṣa</a>,</i> ‘three generations,’ <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 7. The St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> and Eggeling <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 269, have the correct rendering.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201267"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201267">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 209. But see 10, 75, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201267"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201267">↑</a> This would be <i><a class="vei_120a" href="साम्राज्य">sāmrājya</a>,</i> which is always spelt with <i>m,</i> not <i>ṃ; cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> 75, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201267"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201267">↑</a> i. 48.</li></ol></div>

दश-मास्य|दशमास्य|दश|मास्य|దశ-మాస్య|దశమాస్య|దశ|మాస్య|ದಶ-ಮಾಸ್ಯ|ದಶಮಾಸ್ಯ|ದಶ|ಮಾಸ್ಯ|daśa-māsya|daśamāsya|daśa|māsya|dasha-maasya|dashamaasya|dasha|maasya|_vei_120@1268|_vei1_pn344
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1267">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1269">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn342">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn344">1:344</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn345">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daśa-māsya,</b> ‘ten months old,’ describes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201268">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201268">2</a></sup> the embryo immediately before birth. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201268">↑</a> v. 78, 7-8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201268">↑</a> Av. i. 11, 6; iii. 23, 2. There are several references in Vedic literature to birth in the tenth month, as Rv. x. 184, 3, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 366; Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 313, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

दशमी|दशमि|దశమీ|దశమి|ದಶಮೀ|ದಶಮಿ|daśamī|daśami|dashamii|dashami|dashamee|_vei_120@1269|_vei1_pn344
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1268">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1270">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn342">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn344">1:344</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn345">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daśamī</b> denotes in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201269">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201269">2</a></sup> the period of life between 90 and 100 years  which the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201269">3</a></sup> calls the <i>daśama <a class="vei_120a" href="युग">yuga</a>,</i> ‘the tenth stage of life.’ Longevity seems not to have been rare among the Vedic Indians, for the desire to live a ‘hundred autumns’ (<i>śaradaḥ śatam</i>) is constantly expressed.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201269">4</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दीर्घतमस्">dīrghatamas</a></b> is said to have lived 100 years,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201269">5</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="महिदास ऐतरेय">mahidāsa aitareya</a></b> is credited with 116.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201269">6</a></sup> Onesikritos<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201269">7</a></sup> reported that they sometimes lived 130 years, a statement with which corresponds the wish expressed in the Jātaka<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201269">8</a></sup> for a life of 120 years. Probably the number was always rather imaginary than real, but the comparative brevity of modern life in India<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201269">9</a></sup> may be accounted for by the cumulative effect of fever, which is hardly known to the Rigveda. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तक्मन्">takman</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201269">↑</a> iii. 4, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201269">↑</a> xxii. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201269">↑</a> i. 158, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201269">↑</a> Rv. i. 89, 9; x. 18, 10, and <i>passim.</i> See Lanman, <i>Sanskrit Reader,</i> 384; Bloomfield, <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 62, 63.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201269">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201269">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 16, 7; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 2, 11; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 17.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201269">↑</a> In Strabo, p. 701.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201269">↑</a> Ed. Fausbo7ll, ii. 16.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201269">↑</a> <i>Indian Empire,</i> 1, 513 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

दश-वृक्ष|दशवृक्ष|दश|वृक्ष|దశ-వృక్ష|దశవృక్ష|దశ|వృక్ష|ದಶ-ವೃಕ್ಷ|ದಶವೃಕ್ಷ|ದಶ|ವೃಕ್ಷ|daśa-vṛkṣa|daśavṛkṣa|daśa|vṛkṣa|dasha-vRkSha|dashavRkSha|dasha|vRkSha|dasha-vRikSha|dashavRikSha|vRikSha|dasha-vRukSha|dashavRukSha|vRukSha|dasha-vRRikSha|dashavRRikSha|vRRikSha|dasha-vRxa|dashavRxa|vRxa|_vei_120@1270|_vei1_pn345
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1269">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1271">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn344">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn345">1:345</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn346">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daśa-vṛkṣa</b> is the name of a tree, according to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201270"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201270">1</a></sup> in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201270"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201270">2</a></sup> But Whitney<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201270"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201270">3</a></sup> treats the word as a mere adjective meaning ‘of ten trees.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201270"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201270">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201270"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201270">↑</a> ii. 9, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201270"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201270">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 50.</li></ol></div>

दश-व्रज|दशव्रज|दश|व्रज|దశ-వ్రజ|దశవ్రజ|దశ|వ్రజ|ದಶ-ವ್ರಜ|ದಶವ್ರಜ|ದಶ|ವ್ರಜ|daśa-vraja|daśavraja|daśa|vraja|dasha-vraja|dashavraja|dasha|_vei_120@1271|_vei1_pn345
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1270">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1272">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn344">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn345">1:345</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn346">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daśa-vraja</b> is the name of a protégé of the Aśvins in the Rigveda (viii. 8, 20; 49, 1; 50, 9).</p></div>

दश-शिप्र|दशशिप्र|दश|शिप्र|దశ-శిప్ర|దశశిప్ర|దశ|శిప్ర|ದಶ-ಶಿಪ್ರ|ದಶಶಿಪ್ರ|ದಶ|ಶಿಪ್ರ|daśa-śipra|daśaśipra|daśa|śipra|dasha-shipra|dashashipra|dasha|shipra|_vei_120@1272|_vei1_pn345
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1271">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1273">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn344">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn345">1:345</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn346">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daśa-śipra</b> is the name of a sacrificer mentioned in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201272"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201272">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201272"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201272">↑</a> viii. 52, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 163.</li></ol></div>

दशा|దశా|ದಶಾ|daśā|dashaa|_vei_120@1273|_vei1_pn345
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1272">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1274">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn344">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn345">1:345</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn346">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daśā</b> denotes the ‘fringe’ or ‘border’ of a garment (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वासः">vāsaḥ</a></i>) in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201273"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201273">1</a></sup> The word also occurs in the compound <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दशा">daśā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पवित्र">pavitra</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201273"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201273">2</a></sup> which means ‘a filtering cloth with a fringe.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201273"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201273">↑</a> iii. 3, 2, 9, and often in the Sūtras.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201273"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201273">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 32; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 2, 2, 11. <i>Cf.</i> iv. 1, 1. 28.</li></ol></div>

दशोणि|దశోణి|ದಶೋಣಿ|daśoṇi|dashoNi|_vei_120@1274|_vei1_pn346
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1273">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1275">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn345">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn346">1:346</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn347">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daśoṇi</b> appears in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201274">1</a></sup> apparently as a favourite of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> and as opposed to the <b>Paṇis,</b> who fell in hundreds for his benefit. The view of Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201274">2</a></sup> that he is here the priest of the Paṇis is very improbable. Elsewhere his name is simply mentioned.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201274">3</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दशोण्य">daśoṇya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201274">↑</a> vi. 20, 4, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201274">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 156; 5, 107.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201274">↑</a> x. 96, 12, where, however, the word may be merely an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 92, n. 1; Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 55, 328.</li></ol></div>

दशोण्य|దశోణ్య|ದಶೋಣ್ಯ|daśoṇya|dashoNya|_vei_120@1275|_vei1_pn346
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1274">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1276">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn345">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn346">1:346</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn347">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daśoṇya</b> is the name of a sacrificer mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201275"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201275">1</a></sup> along with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दशशिप्र">daśaśipra</a></b> and others. Whether he is identical with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दशोणि">daśoṇi</a></b> cannot be decided.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201275"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201275">↑</a> viii. 52, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 163.</li></ol></div>

दशोनसि|దశోనసి|ದಶೋನಸಿ|daśonasi|dashonasi|_vei_120@1276|_vei1_pn346
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1275">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1277">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn345">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn346">1:346</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn347">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daśonasi</b> is the name of a kind of snake in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201276"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201276">1</a></sup> The reading of the Paippalāda recension is Naśonaśī.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201276"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201276">↑</a> x. 4, 17. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 95; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 577.</li></ol></div>

दस्यवे वृक|దస్యవే వృక|ದಸ್ಯವೇ ವೃಕ|dasyave vṛka|dasyave vRka|dasyave vRika|dasyave vRuka|dasyave vRRika|_vei_120@1277|_vei1_pn346
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1276">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1278">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn345">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn346">1:346</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn347">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dasyave vṛka,</b> ‘wolf to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दस्यु">dasyu</a>,</b>’ is the name of a man mentioned four times in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201277"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201277">1</a></sup> In one hymn<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201277"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201277">2</a></sup> he is called a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>, but in two others<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201277"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201277">3</a></sup> he is clearly a prince victorious over the Dasyus, and a generous patron of the singer. It is hardly necessary to assume different persons,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201277"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201277">4</a></sup> for the term <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> is not altogether inconsistent with royalty. He was son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पूतक्रतु">pūtakratu</a></b><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201277"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201277">5</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पूतक्रता">pūtakratā</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201277"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201277">6</a></sup> his wife.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201277"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201277">↑</a> viii. 51, 2; 55, 1; 56, 1. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201277"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201277">↑</a> viii. 51.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201277"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201277">↑</a> viii. 55. 56.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201277"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201277">↑</a> Especially as the name occurs only in the small collection of <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> hymns forming the <a class="vei_120a" href="वालखिल्य">vālakhilya</a> group in the eighth Maṇḍala.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201277"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201277">↑</a> viii. 56, 2. <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पौतक्रत">pautakrata</a>. Cf.</i> viii. 68, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201277"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201277">↑</a> viii. 56, 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 139, 164; 5, 552.</li></ol></div>

दस्यवे सह|దస్యవే సహ|ದಸ್ಯವೇ ಸಹ|dasyave saha|_vei_120@1278|_vei1_pn346
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1277">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1279">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn345">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn346">1:346</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn347">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dasyave saha</b> is, according to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201278">1</a></sup> the name of a man or a clan in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201278">2</a></sup> But he admits that the words may  be an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>. This is the interpretation given to them by Oldenberg.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201278">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201278">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201278">↑</a> i. 36, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201278">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 46, 33.</li></ol></div>

दस्यु|దస్యు|ದಸ್ಯು|dasyu|_vei_120@1279|_vei1_pn347
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1278">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1280">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn346">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn347">1:347</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn349">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dasyu,</b> a word of somewhat doubtful origin, is in many passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201279">1</a></sup> clearly applied to superhuman enemies. On the other hand, there are several passages in which human foes, probably the aborigines, are thus designated. This may be regarded as certain in those passages where the <a class="vei_120a" href="दस्यु">dasyu</a> is opposed to the Āryan, who defeats him with the aid of the gods.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201279">2</a></sup> The great difference between the Dasyus and the Āryans was their religion: the former are styled ‘not sacrificing,’ ‘devoid of rites,’ ‘addicted to strange vows,’ ‘godhating,’ and so forth.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201279">3</a></sup> As compared with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>,</b> they are less distinctively a people: no clans (<i>viśaḥ</i>) of the Dasyus are mentioned, and while Indra's <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दस्यु">dasyu</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="हत्य">hatya</a>,</i> ‘slaughter of the Dasyus,’ is often spoken of, there is no corresponding use of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="हत्य">hatya</a>.</i> That the Dasyus were real people is, however, shown by the epithet <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अनास्">anās</a></i> applied to them in one passage of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201279">5</a></sup> The sense of this word is not absolutely certain: the <a class="vei_120a" href="पद">pada</a> text and Sāyaṇa both take it to mean ‘without face’ (<i>an-ās</i>),<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201279">6</a></sup> but the other rendering, ‘noseles’ (<i>a-nās</i>), is quite possible,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201279">7</a></sup></p><p>4 Rv. i. 51, 5, 6; 103, 4; x. 95, 7; 99, 7; 105, 11. <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="दस्यु">dasyu</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="हन्">han</a>.</i> ‘Dasyuslaying,’ i. 100, 12; vi. 45, 24; viii. 76, 11; 77, 3; x. 47, 4 (all of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>); vi. 16, 15; viii. 39, 8 (of <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>), etc.  and would accord well with the flat-nosed aborigines of the Dravidian<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201279">8</a></sup> type, whose language still persists among the Brahuis, who are found in the north-west. This interpretation would receive some support from Vṛtra's being called ‘broken-nosed’ if this were a correct explanation of the obscure word <i>rujānās.</i><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201279">9</a></sup></p><p>The other epithet of the Dasyus is <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मृध्र-वाच्">mṛdhra-vāc</a>,</i> which occurs with <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अनास्">anās</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201279">10</a></sup> and which has been rendered<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201279">11</a></sup> ‘of stammering, or unintelligible speech.’ This version is by no means certain, and since the epithet is elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201279">12</a></sup> applied to Āryans, its correct meaning is more probably ‘of hostile speech.’</p><p><a class="vei_120a" href="दस्यु">dasyu</a> corresponds with the Iranian <i>daṅhu, daqyu,</i> which denotes a ‘province.’ Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201279">13</a></sup> thinks that the original meaning was ‘enemy,’ whence the Iranians developed the sense of ‘hostile country,’ ‘conquered country,’ ‘province,’ while the Indians, retaining the signification of ‘enemy,’ extended it to include demon foes. Roth<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201279">14</a></sup> considers that the meaning of human enemy is a transfer from the strife of gods  and demons. Lassen<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201279">15</a></sup> attempted to onnect the contrast <i>daqyu: <a class="vei_120a" href="दस्यु">dasyu</a></i> with that of <i>daeva: <a class="vei_120a" href="देव">deva</a>,</i> and to see in it a result of the religious differences which, according to Haug's theory, had separated the Iranians and the Indians. The word may have originally meant ‘ravaged land’<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1201279">16</a></sup> as a result of invasion; hence ‘enemies’ country,' then ‘hostile people,’ who as human foes were more usually called by the cognate name of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>.</b></p><p>Individual Dasyus are <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चुमुरि">cumuri</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शम्बर">śambara</a>, Śuṣṇa,</b> etc.</p><p>In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1201279">17</a></sup> the word has, as later,<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1201279">18</a></sup> the sense of uncivilized peoples generally.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201279">↑</a> i. 34, 7; 100, 18; ii. 13, 9, etc. See Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> pp. 157, 158.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201279">↑</a> Rv. i. 51, 8; 103, 3; 117, 21; ii. 11, 18, 19; iii. 34, 9; vi. 18, 3; vii. 5, 6; x. 49, 3. Probably also in v. 70, 3; x. 83, 6, people are meant.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201279">↑</a> The <a class="vei_120a" href="दस्यु">dasyu</a> is called <i>a-karman,</i> ‘riteless,’ x. 22, 8; <i>a-devayu,</i> ‘indifferent to the gods,’ viii. 70, 11; <i>a-<a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>,</i> ‘without devotion,’ iv. 16, 9; <i>a-yajvan,</i> ‘not sacrificing,’ viii. 70, 11; <i>a-yajyu, id.,</i> vii. 6, 3; <i>a-<a class="vei_120a" href="व्रत">vrata</a>,</i> ‘lawless,’ i. 51, 8; 175, 3; vi. 14, 3; ix. 41, 2; <i>anyavrata,</i> ‘following strange ordinances,’ viii. 70, 11; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="देव">deva</a>-pīyu,</i> ‘reviling the gods,’ Av. xii. 1, 37. It is impossible in all cases to be certain that people are meant.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201279">↑</a> Rv. v. 29, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201279">↑</a> This sense allows of two interpretations: ‘misfeatured,’ which seems that of Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> and Grassmann, <i>Wo7rterbuch;</i> or ‘speechless’ (that is, unable to speak the language of the Āryans), which is that of Bollensen, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 41, 496.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201279">↑</a> This view is supported by Megasthenes' report as to natives who were: see Strabo, p. 711; Pliny, <i>Nat. Hist.,</i> vii. 2, 18, cited by Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 430. See also Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 2, 109; 5, 95; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 277; Knauer, Kuhn's <i>Zeitschrift,</i> 29, 52; Wackernagel, <i>Altindische Grammatik,</i> 2, 293 (accent).</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201279">↑</a> The suggestion in the <i>Indian Empire,</i> 1, 390, that the modern Brahui type is the true Dravidian, while the modern Dravidian is the result of fusion with Muṇḍā-speaking tribes, would render this theory improbable. But it seems more probable that the Brahuis in speech preserve the tradition of Dravidian settlements in North India.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201279">↑</a> See Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 17, 415 (who takes <i>rujānāḥ</i> of Rv. i. 32, 8, as = <i>rujāna-nāḥ</i>); Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 31, 32 (who suggests as possible the analysis of the word as <i>rujā-anāḥ</i>). But <i>cf.</i> Lanman, <i>Sanskrit Reader,</i> 361, who suggests the emendation <i>rujānaḥ</i> as nominative singular of the simple participle ‘broken’; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> p. 59, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201279">↑</a> Rv. v. 29, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201279">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 2^2, 393 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201279">↑</a> The expression is used of the Āryan Pūrus in vii. 18, 13; of the Paṇis in vii. 6, 3; and of hostile persons in i. 174, 2; v. 32, 8; x. 23, 5. Roth, <i>Erläuterungen zum <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,</i> 97, thinks the sense is ‘of insulting speech,’ and Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 114, 115, strongly supports this view. But Hillebrandt, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 89, 90, 114, prefers to see in it ‘speaking an enemy's speech,’ and thinks that the Pūrus were dialectically different from the Bharatas--a view which can be supported from the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 1, 23, 24, where the Asuras say <i>he'lavo</i> (= <i>he'rayo,</i> ‘ho, enemies,’ in Sanskrit). See Muir, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2^2, 114; Davidson, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 37, 23 (the Mahābhāṣya version); Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 31, n. 3. The word could thus apply to the Dasyus also, as the strange speech of the enemy could be either Āryan or aboriginal.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201279">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 110 <i>et seq.</i> So Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 158.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201279">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201279">↑</a> <i>Indische Alterthumskunde,</i> 1^2, 633 <i>et seq.</i> This theory is now generally discredited. <i>Cf.</i> Justi, <i>Go7ttingische Gelehrte Anzeigen,</i> 1866, 1446 <i>et seq.;</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 142; Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 162 <i>et seq.;</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 156.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1201279">↑</a> Both this word and <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a> appear to be derived from the root <i>das,</i> which, according to Whitney, <i>Roots,</i> means ‘lay waste’; but, according to Roth, ‘suffer want,’ ‘waste away.’</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1201279">↑</a> vii. 18, where the descendants of <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> are called <i>dasyūnāṃ bhūyiṣṭhāḥ; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,</i> xv. 26, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1201279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1201279">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>, v. 131; x. 32. 45; Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 118.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 3, 276 <i>et seq.;</i> Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 101 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

दाक्षायण|దాక్షాయణ|ದಾಕ್ಷಾಯಣ|dākṣāyaṇa|daakShaayaNa|daaxaayaNa|_vei_120@1280|_vei1_pn349
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1279">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1281">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn347">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn349">1:349</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn350">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dākṣāyaṇa,</b> ‘descendant of Dakṣa.’ The Dākṣāyaṇas are mentioned in the Atharvaveda and the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201280">1</a></sup> as having given gold to <b>Śatānīka.</b> In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201280">2</a></sup> the word is actually used to denote ‘gold.’ The Dākṣāyaṇas appear there<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201280">3</a></sup> as a race of princes who, because of performing a certain rite, prospered down to the time of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> itself.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201280">↑</a> Av. i. 35, 1, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxiv. 51, 52; Kaṭha, cited by von Schroeder, <i>Tūbinger Kaṭha-Handschriften,</i> 36; <a class="vei_120a" href="खिल">khila</a>, iv. 7, 7, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201280">↑</a> vi. 7, 4, 2: <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दाक्षायण">dākṣāyaṇa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="हस्त">hasta</a>,</i> ‘goldenhande.’ Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 283, n. 2, seems unnecessarily doubtful as to this.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201280">↑</a> ii. 4, 4, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Artareya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 40.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 224; 4, 358; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 195; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 35; Lévi, <i>La Doctrine du Sacrifice,</i> 138.</li></ol></div>

दात्यौह|దాత్యౌహ|ದಾತ್ಯೌಹ|dātyauha|daatyauha|_vei_120@1281|_vei1_pn349
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1280">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1282">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn347">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn349">1:349</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn350">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dātyauha,</b> a ‘gallinule,’ is mentioned in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or horse sacrifice, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201281"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201281">1</a></sup> The  word is clearly a variant of <i>dātyūha,</i> which occurs in the epics and law books.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201281"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201281">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 17, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 6; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 25. 39. Pāṇini, vii. 3. 1, derives the word from <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दित्य">ditya</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वह्">vah</a>. Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 91.</li></ol></div>

दात्र|దాత్ర|ದಾತ್ರ|dātra|daatra|_vei_120@1282|_vei1_pn350
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1281">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1283">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn349">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn350">1:350</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn351">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dātra</b> (‘cutter’), denoting a ‘sickle,’ is mentioned in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201282"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201282">1</a></sup> Cows ‘with sickle-shaped marks on their ears’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दात्र">dātra</a>-karṇyaḥ</i>) are referred to in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201282"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201282">2</a></sup> Otherwise the expression is only found later, occurring in the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> and epic literature.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201282"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201282">3</a></sup> See also <b>Sṛṇi.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201282"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201282">↑</a> viii. 78, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201282"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201282">↑</a> iv. 2, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201282"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201282">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 86.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 238.</li></ol></div>

दात्रेय|దాత్రేయ|ದಾತ್ರೇಯ|dātreya|daatreya|_vei_120@1283|_vei1_pn350
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1282">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1284">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn349">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn350">1:350</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn351">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dātreya</b> is the patronymic of <b>Arāḍa <a class="vei_120a" href="शौनक">śaunaka</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201283"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201283">1</a></sup> Possibly <a class="vei_120a" href="दार्तेय">dārteya</a>, ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="दृति">dṛti</a>,’ should be read,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201283"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201283">2</a></sup> but the word may have the same derivation as the latter form with metathesis.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201283"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201283">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201283"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201283">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

दाधीच|దాధీచ|ದಾಧೀಚ|dādhīca|daadhiicha|daadheecha|_vei_120@1284|_vei1_pn350
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1283">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1285">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn349">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn350">1:350</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn351">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dādhīca,</b> ‘descendant of <b>Dadhyanc,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="च्यवन">cyavana</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xiv. 6).</p></div>

दान|दान|దాన_౧|దాన|ದಾನ_೧|ದಾನ|dāna_1|dāna|daana_1|daana|_vei_120@1285|_vei1_pn350
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1284">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1286">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn349">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn350">1:350</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn351">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Dāna,</b> ‘giving,’ ‘gift,’ is a word of frequent occurrence in the Rigveda, especially in the <a class="vei_120a" href="दान">dāna</a>-stutis<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201285"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201285">1</a></sup> (‘Praises of Gifts’) of generous patrons (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दक्षिणा">dakṣiṇā</a></b>). One of the characteristics of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a></b> is his right to receive gifts, which it is obligatory on the other castes to present.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201285"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201285">2</a></sup> The gift of a daughter (<i>kanyāyā dānam</i>) was a form of marriage<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201285"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201285">3</a></sup> (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विवाह">vivāha</a></b>), because in it the girl was ‘given’ away by her father or brother.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201285"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201285">↑</a> The term seems first to occur in the Bṛhaddevatā, vi. 45, 92, and in similar works.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201285"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201285">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 7, 1; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 47-61.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201285"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201285">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 4.</li></ol></div>

दान|दान|దాన_౨|దాన|ದಾನ_೨|ದಾನ|dāna_2|dāna|daana_2|daana|_vei_120@1286|_vei1_pn350
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1285">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1287">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn349">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn350">1:350</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn351">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Dāna</b> (‘distribution’)<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201286"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201286">1</a></sup> seems in several passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201286"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201286">2</a></sup> to be a designation of the sacrificial feast to which  the god is invited (<i>cf.</i> ). In one passage<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201286"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201286">3</a></sup> Sāyaṇa thinks that it denotes the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मद">mada</a>-jalāni,</i> ‘drops of water falling from the temples of a rutting elephant,’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201286"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201286">4</a></sup> but this is doubtful. In another passage<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201286"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201286">5</a></sup> Roth thinks that ‘pasture land’ is meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201286"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201286">↑</a> From <i>dā,</i> ‘divide.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201286"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201286">↑</a> i. 55, 7; 48, 4; 180, 5; viii. 46, 26; 60, 8; 99, 4, etc. <i>Cf.,</i> however, Pischel <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 100.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201286"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201286">↑</a> Rv. viii. 33, 8; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 5, 157.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201286"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201286">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दान">dāna</a> in this sense, so common in the post-Vedic language, is probably derived from <i>dā,</i> ‘divide,’ meaning originally ‘secretion.’</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201286"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201286">↑</a> ii. 13, 7.</li></ol></div>

दान|दान|దాన_౩|దాన|ದಾನ_೩|ದಾನ|dāna_3|dāna|daana_3|daana|_vei_120@1287|_vei1_pn351
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1286">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1288">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn350">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn351">1:351</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn353">↷</a><hr><p>3. <b>Dāna</b> is in three passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201287"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201287">1</a></sup> held by Roth to designate a chariot horse.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201287"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201287">↑</a> v. 27, 5; vii. 18, 23; viii. 46, 24. But in all these cases ‘gifts’ seems an adequate version, ‘horses’ being understood.</li></ol></div>

दामन्|దామన్|ದಾಮನ್|dāman|daaman|_vei_120@1288|_vei1_pn351
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1287">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1289">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn350">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn351">1:351</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn353">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dāman,</b> a ‘rope’ or ‘girdle,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201288">1</a></sup> is often mentioned in the Rigveda and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201288">2</a></sup> Reference is made to the rope of the sacrificial horse,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201288">3</a></sup> as well as to the practice of tying calves with ropes.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201288">4</a></sup> The word occurs in the sense of a ‘band’ of horse hair in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201288">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201288">↑</a> Originally ‘bond,’ from <i>dā,</i> ‘bind.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201288">↑</a> Rv. i. 56, 3, etc.; Av. vi. 63, 1; 103, 2; vii. 103, 1. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 4, 13, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201288">↑</a> Rv. i. 162, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201288">↑</a> Rv. ii. 28, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201288">↑</a> v. 3, 1, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 62, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

दाय|దాయ|ದಾಯ|dāya|daaya|_vei_120@1289|_vei1_pn351
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1288">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1290">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn350">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn351">1:351</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn353">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dāya</b> occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201289">1</a></sup> only in the sense of ‘reward’ of exertion (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="श्रम">śrama</a></i>), but later it means ‘inheritance’--that is, a father's property which is to be divided among his sons either during his lifetime or after his death. The passages all negative the idea that the property of the family was legally family property: it is clear that it was the property of the head of the house, usually the father, and that the other members of the family only had moral claims upon it which the father could ignore, though he might be coerced by his sons if they were physically stronger.</p><p>Thus <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a></b> is said in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201289">2</a></sup> to have divided his property among his sons. He omitted <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नाभानेदिष्ठ">nābhānediṣṭha</a>,</b>  whom he afterwards taught how to appease the Aṅgirases, and to procure cows. This is a significant indication that the property he divided was movable property, rather than land (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="उर्वरा">urvarā</a></b>). In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201289">3</a></sup> the division is said to have been made during Manu's lifetime by his sons, who left only their aged father to <a class="vei_120a" href="नाभानेदिष्ठ">nābhānediṣṭha</a>. According to the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201289">4</a></sup> again, four sons divided the inheritance while their old father, Abhipratārin, was still alive. It is, of course, possible to regard <a class="vei_120a" href="दाय">dāya</a> as denoting the heritable property of the family, but the developed <i>patria potestas</i> of the father, which was early very marked, as shown by the legend of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a>,</b> is inconsistent with the view that the sons were legally owners with their father, unless and until. they actually insisted on a division of the property.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201289">5</a></sup> Probably-there is no evidence of any decisive character--land was not divided at first, but no doubt its disposal began to follow the analogy of cattle and other movable property as soon as the available supply of arable land became limited.</p><p>As for the method of division, it is clear from the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201289">6</a></sup> that the elder son was usually preferred; perhaps  this was always the case after death. During the father's lifetime another might be preferred, as appears from a passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201289">7</a></sup> Women were excluded from partition or inheritance, according to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201289">8</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201289">9</a></sup> They were, no doubt, supported by their brothers; but if they had none they might be reduced to prostitution.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201289">10</a></sup> Detailed rules of inheritance appear in the Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201289">11</a></sup></p><p>The heir is called Dayada,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201289">12</a></sup> ‘receiver (<i>ā-da</i>) of inheritance.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201289">↑</a> x. 114, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201289">↑</a> iii. 1, 9, 4 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 191-194; Lévi, <i>La Doctrine du Sacrifice,</i> 67, 68.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201289">↑</a> v. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201289">↑</a> iii. 156 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 26, 61, 62).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201289">↑</a> The same question has been raised as to the origin of English or Teutonic property in land generally. Against any idea even of family ownership in a strict sense of the word, see Fustel de Coulanges, <i>Recherches sur quelques Problèmes d'Histoire,</i> 322 <i>et seq.;</i> Ashley, in Fustel de Coulanges, <i>Origin of Property in Land,</i> xvi-xxi; Pollock and Maitland, <i>History of English Law,</i> 2, 237 <i>et seq.</i> The older view, which accepted family and communal ownership, represented in different forms by Maine (<i>Village Communities in the East and West</i>), Stubbs, Green, and others, is defended in a new form by Vinogradoff, <i>Villanage in England.</i> See also Keith, <i>Journal of the African Society,</i> 6, 201 <i>et seq.</i> Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 93-96, does not accept the communal ownership of land, but, <i>ibid.,</i> 80, is inclined to believe in the joint ownership of a family. He admits that this is inconsistent with the strict rule of <i>patria potestas,</i> which still exists in Bengal; Baden Powell, <i>Village Communities in India,</i> 133 <i>et seq.,</i> doubts the existence in early India of such a <i>patria potestas.</i> But the facts seem clearly to show that there was such a power, and that the father owned the property. His sons, as they grew up, came to claim the property, and he might have to divide it; hence the idea naturally developed that every child on birth had a legal share in the property. No doubt also from the first the right to part with land was one which grown-up sons and the rest of the community could object to, once the village had acquired a fixed existence. This would account adequately for the later system. <i>Cf.</i> also pp. 100, n. 19; 336, n. 7, and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201289">↑</a> ii. 5, 2, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201289">↑</a> xvi. 4, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201289">↑</a> iv. 4, 2, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201289">↑</a> iii. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201289">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <b>Ztrī.</b></li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201289">↑</a> Inheritance is also alluded to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 27, 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 2, 22; iii. 2, 1, 18. For the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> rules, see Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 80 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201289">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 4, 3, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 4; metaphorically, Av. v. 18, 6, 14.</li></ol></div>

दार|దార|ದಾರ|dāra|daara|_vei_120@1290|_vei1_pn353
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1289">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1291">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn351">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn353">1:353</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn354">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dāra,</b> ‘wife,’ is found in the Sūtras (usually as a plural masculine), and once (as a singular) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201290">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201290">↑</a> vi. 4, 12 (where <i>dvāreṇa</i> is a <i>varia lectio;</i> see St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i>). <i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 415, 416, who igneres the Bṛhadāraṇyaka passage.</li></ol></div>

दारु|దారు|ದಾರು|dāru|daaru|_vei_120@1291|_vei1_pn353
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1290">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1292">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn351">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn353">1:353</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn354">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dāru,</b> ‘wood,’ is frequently mentioned in the Rigveda and later,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201291"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201291">1</a></sup> denoting amongst other things the pole of a chariot,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201291"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201291">2</a></sup> logs as fuel,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201291"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201291">3</a></sup> the wooden parts of a car,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201291"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201291">4</a></sup> possibly wooden stocks,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201291"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201291">5</a></sup> and so forth.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201291"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201291">↑</a> Rv. vi. 3, 4; x. 145, 4, etc.; Av. x. 4, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 8, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201291"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201291">↑</a> Rv. x. 102, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201291"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201291">↑</a> Rv. viii. 102, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201291"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201291">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 6, 2, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201291"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201291">↑</a> Av. vi. 121, 2. But this is doubtful. <i>Cf.</i> <b>Tāya</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="द्रुपद">drupada</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

दार्ढ-जयन्ति|दार्ढजयन्ति|दार्ढ|जयन्ति|దార్ఢ-జయన్తి|దార్ఢజయన్తి|దార్ఢ|జయన్తి|ದಾರ್ಢ-ಜಯನ್ತಿ|ದಾರ್ಢಜಯನ್ತಿ|ದಾರ್ಢ|ಜಯನ್ತಿ|dārḍha-jayanti|dārḍhajayanti|dārḍha|jayanti|daarDha-jayanti|daarDhajayanti|daarDha|_vei_120@1292|_vei1_pn353
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1291">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1293">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn351">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn353">1:353</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn354">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dārḍha-jayanti,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृढजयन्त">dṛḍhajayanta</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैपश्चित">vaipaścita</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गुप्त लौहित्य">gupta lauhitya</a></b> and of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैपश्चित">vaipaścita</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दृढजयन्त">dṛḍhajayanta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="लौहित्य">lauhitya</a></b> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 42, 1).</p></div>

दार्तेय|దార్తేయ|ದಾರ್ತೇಯ|dārteya|daarteya|_vei_120@1293|_vei1_pn353
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1292">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1294">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn351">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn353">1:353</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn354">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dārteya,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृति">dṛti</a>.</b>’ The Dārteyas are mentioned as authorities on sacrificial matters in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201293">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201293">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201293">↑</a> xxxi. 2 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 473).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201293"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201293">↑</a> xxv. 3, 6.</li></ol></div>

दार्भ्य|దార్భ్య|ದಾರ್ಭ್ಯ|dārbhya|daarbhya|_vei_120@1294|_vei1_pn354
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1293">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1295">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn353">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn354">1:354</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn355">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dārbhya,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="दर्भ">darbha</a>,’ is mentioned in a verse of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201294">1</a></sup> Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201294">2</a></sup> identifies him with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्यावाश्व">śyāvāśva</a>,</b> but the Bṛhaddevatā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201294">3</a></sup> with <b>Rathavīti.</b> The same patronymic is frequently<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201294">4</a></sup> connected with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="केशिन्">keśin</a>,</b> and is also applied to <b>Rathaprota.</b><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201294">5</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दाल्भ्य">dālbhya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201294">↑</a> v. 61, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201294">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201294">↑</a> v. 50. 77.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201294">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 2, 3; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 4, 12; 6, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 4. <i>Cf.</i> Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 62, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201294">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 3.</li></ol></div>

दार्व्-आघात|दार्वाघात|दार्व्|आघात|దార్వ్-ఆఘాత|దార్వాఘాత|దార్వ్|ఆఘాత|ದಾರ್ವ್-ಆಘಾತ|ದಾರ್ವಾಘಾತ|ದಾರ್ವ್|ಆಘಾತ|dārv-āghāta|dārvāghāta|dārv|āghāta|daarv-aaghaata|daarvaaghaata|daarv|aaghaata|_vei_120@1295|_vei1_pn354
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1294">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1296">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn353">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn354">1:354</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn355">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dārv-āghāta,</b> the ‘woodpecker,’ is included in the list of sacrificial victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or ‘horse sacrifice,’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201295"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201295">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201295"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201295">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 15, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 16; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 35. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 92.</li></ol></div>

दार्व्-आहार|दार्वाहार|दार्व्|आहार|దార్వ్-ఆహార|దార్వాహార|దార్వ్|ఆహార|ದಾರ್ವ್-ಆಹಾರ|ದಾರ್ವಾಹಾರ|ದಾರ್ವ್|ಆಹಾರ|dārv-āhāra|dārvāhāra|dārv|āhāra|daarv-aahaara|daarvaahaara|daarv|aahaara|_vei_120@1296|_vei1_pn354
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1295">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1297">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn353">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn354">1:354</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn355">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dārv-āhāra,</b> a ‘gatherer of wood,’ is included in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha, or ‘human sacrifice,’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201296">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201296">↑</a> Vājasaneyī Saṃhitā, xxx. 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 8, 1.</li></ol></div>

दाल्भि|దాల్భి|ದಾಲ್ಭಿ|dālbhi|daalbhi|_vei_120@1297|_vei1_pn354
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1296">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1298">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn353">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn354">1:354</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn355">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dālbhi,</b> ‘descendant of Dalbha,’ is the patronymic of <b>Vaka</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā (x. 6).</p></div>

दाल्भ्य|దాల్భ్య|ದಾಲ್ಭ್ಯ|dālbhya|daalbhya|_vei_120@1298|_vei1_pn354
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1297">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1299">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn353">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn354">1:354</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn355">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dālbhya,</b> ‘descendant of Dalbha,’ is a variant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दार्भ्य">dārbhya</a>.</b> It is the patronymic of (<i>a</i>) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="केशिन्">keśin</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>;<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201298"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201298">1</a></sup> (<i>b</i>) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चैकितायन">caikitāyana</a></b> in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201298"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201298">2</a></sup> and the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>;<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201298"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201298">3</a></sup> (<i>c</i>) <b>Vaka</b> in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201298"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201298">4</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201298"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201298">5</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201298"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201298">↑</a> xiii. 10, 8. <i>Cf.</i> the <a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a>, reported by Ṣaḍguruśiṣya (Sarvānukramaṇī, ed. Macdonell, 118). The St. Petersburg Dictionary quotes the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 4, for <a class="vei_120a" href="दाल्भ्य">dālbhya</a> (but also for <a class="vei_120a" href="दार्भ्य">dārbhya</a>, which is the reading of Lindner's edition).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201298"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201298">↑</a> i. 8, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201298"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201298">↑</a> i. 38, 1; 56, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201298"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201298">↑</a> i. 2, 13; 12, 1, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201298"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201298">↑</a> xxx. 2, where <a class="vei_120a" href="दाल्भ्य">dālbhya</a> is read, not <a class="vei_120a" href="दाल्भि">dālbhi</a>, as stated in the St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> The Kapiṣṭhala Saṃhitā, xlvi. 5, has <i>Darbhasya.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="दाल्भि">dālbhi</a> is found, however, in <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, x. 6.</li></ol></div>

दाव|దావ|ದಾವ|dāva|daava|_vei_120@1299|_vei1_pn355
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1298">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1300">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn354">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn355">1:355</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn356">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dāva,</b> ‘forest fire,’ is mentioned in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201299"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201299">1</a></sup> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201299"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201299">2</a></sup> In the latter work such fires are referred to as occurring in spring. According to Sieg,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201299"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201299">3</a></sup> a hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201299"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201299">4</a></sup> describes a forest fire. Watchers were employed to guard against surprise from such conflagrations (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दाव">dāva</a>-pa</i>).<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201299"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201299">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201299"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201299">↑</a> vii. 45, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201299"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201299">↑</a> xi. 2, 7, 32.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201299"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201299">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 44 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201299"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201299">↑</a> x. 142. Sieg's interpretation of this hymn is not at all probable.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201299"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201299">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 11, 1.</li></ol></div>

दाव-सु आंगिरस|दावसु आंगिरस|दाव|सु आंगिरस|दाव-सु आङ्गिरस|दावसु आङ्गिरस|सु आङ्गिरस|దావ-సు ఆంగిరస|దావసు ఆంగిరస|దావ|సు ఆంగిరస|దావ-సు ఆఙ్గిరస|దావసు ఆఙ్గిరస|సు ఆఙ్గిరస|ದಾವ-ಸು ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ದಾವಸು ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ದಾವ|ಸು ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ದಾವ-ಸು ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|ದಾವಸು ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|ಸು ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|dāva-su āṅgirasa|dāvasu āṅgirasa|dāva|su āṅgirasa|daava-su aaMgirasa|daavasu aaMgirasa|daava|su aaMgirasa|daava-su aangirasa|daavasu aangirasa|su aangirasa|_vei_120@1300|_vei1_pn355
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1299">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1301">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn354">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn355">1:355</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn356">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dāva-su Āṅgirasa,</b> a seer of Sāmans, or chants, is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201300"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201300">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201300"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201300">↑</a> xxv. 5, 12, 14. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 160.</li></ol></div>

दाश|దాశ|ದಾಶ|dāśa|daasha|_vei_120@1301|_vei1_pn355
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1300">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1302">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn354">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn355">1:355</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn356">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dāśa,</b> ‘fisherman,’ is mentioned in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha, or ‘human sacrifice,’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201301"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201301">1</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धैवर">dhaivara</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201301"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201301">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 12, 1. Weber, <i>Indische Streifen</i> 1, 81, renders the word by <i>Fischerknecht,</i> perhaps regarding it as equivalent to <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>,</i> ‘servant.’ <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>, x. 34; St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="दाश">dāśa</a>, 2. 3.</li></ol></div>

दाशतय|దాశతయ|ದಾಶತಯ|dāśataya|daashataya|_vei_120@1302|_vei1_pn355
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1301">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1303">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn354">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn355">1:355</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn356">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dāśataya,</b> ‘belonging to the (Rigveda text) divided into ten (books),’ is an epithet of Adhyāya, ‘section,’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201302"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201302">1</a></sup> The feminine form of the word is also found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201302"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201302">2</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201302"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201302">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201302"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201302">↑</a> ii. 11 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 45)</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201302"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201302">↑</a> viii. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201302"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201302">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a> Prātiśākhya, xvi. 54; xvii. 30; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 2, 16, 22, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxvi. 13; xxvii. 4, etc.</li></ol></div>

दाश-राज्ञ|दाशराज्ञ|दाश|राज्ञ|దాశ-రాజ్ఞ|దాశరాజ్ఞ|దాశ|రాజ్ఞ|ದಾಶ-ರಾಜ್ಞ|ದಾಶರಾಜ್ಞ|ದಾಶ|ರಾಜ್ಞ|dāśa-rājña|dāśarājña|dāśa|rājña|daasha-raaj~na|daasharaaj~na|daasha|raaj~na|_vei_120@1303|_vei1_pn355
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1302">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1304">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn354">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn355">1:355</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn356">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dāśa-rājña</b> is the name in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201303">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201303">2</a></sup> of <b>Sudās'</b> famous ‘battle with the ten kings.’ It is somewhat difficult to make out exactly who the kings were (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a></b>), but the number is probably a round one, and cannot be pressed. The actual battle hymn<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201303">3</a></sup> does not contain  the word, and the passages in which it is found may reasonably be considered late.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201303">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201303">↑</a> vii. 33, 2, 5; 83, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201303">↑</a> x. 128, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201303">↑</a> 8, 18</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201303">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> for the late date of vii. 33, Bergaigne, <i>L'histoire de la Saṃhita,</i> 38, 72; Oldenberg, <i>Prolegomena,</i> 198, 200, 265, n. 1; Arnold, <i>Vedic Metre,</i> 309; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 130, opposes this view, but not convincingly.</li></ol></div>

दाशर्म|దాశర్మ|ದಾಶರ್ಮ|dāśarma|daasharma|_vei_120@1304|_vei1_pn356
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1303">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1305">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn355">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn356">1:356</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn358">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dāśarma</b> appears in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201304"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201304">1</a></sup> as a teacher and a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आरुणि">āruṇi</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201304"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201304">↑</a> vii. 6. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 472.</li></ol></div>

दास|దాస|ದಾಸ|dāsa|daasa|_vei_120@1305|_vei1_pn356
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1304">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1306">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn355">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn356">1:356</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn358">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dāsa,</b> like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दस्यु">dasyu</a>,</b> sometimes denotes enemies of a demoniac character in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201305">1</a></sup> but in many passages<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201305">2</a></sup> the word refers to human foes of the Āryans. The Dāsas are described as having forts (<i>puraḥ</i>),<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201305">3</a></sup> and their clans (<i>viśaḥ</i>) are mentioned.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201305">4</a></sup> It is possible that the forts, which are called ‘autumnal’ (<i>śaradīḥ</i>),<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201305">5</a></sup> may be mythical, but it is not essential, for the epithet may allude to their being resorted to in the autumn season. The <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a> colour (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a></b>)<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201305">6</a></sup> is probably an allusion to the black skin of the aborigines, which is also directly mentioned.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201305">7</a></sup> The aborigines (as Dasyus) are called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अनास्">anās</a>,</i> ‘noseless’(?),<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201305">8</a></sup> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मृध्र-वाच्">mṛdhra-vāc</a>,</i> ‘of hostile speech,’<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201305">9</a></sup> and are probably meant by the phallus-worshippers (<i>śiśna-devāḥ,</i> ‘whose deity is a phallus’) of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201305">10</a></sup> It is significant that constant  reference is made to the differences in religion between Ārva and <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a> or <a class="vei_120a" href="दस्यु">dasyu</a>.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201305">11</a></sup></p><p>Since the Dāsas were in many cases reduced to slavery, the word <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a> has the sense of ‘slave’ in several passages of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201305">12</a></sup> Dāsī, the feminine, always has this sense from the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201305">13</a></sup> onwards. Aboriginal women were, no doubt, the usual slaves, for on their husbands being slain in battle they would naturally have been taken as servants. They would sometimes also become concubines; thus <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कवष">kavaṣa</a></b> was taunted with being the son of a female slave (<i>dāsyāḥ putraḥ</i>) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201305">14</a></sup></p><p>Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201305">15</a></sup> considers that in some passages<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1201305">16</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a> is applied, in the sense of ‘enemy,’ to Āryan foes, but this is uncertain. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1201305">17</a></sup> and Meyer<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1201305">18</a></sup> think that <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a><sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1201305">19</a></sup> originally meant ‘enemy’ in general, later developing in Iran into the name of the Dahae<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1201305">20</a></sup> of the Caspian steppes, and in India into a designation of the aborigines. On the other hand, Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1201305">21</a></sup> argues that, as the Dāsas and the Paṇis are mentioned together,<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1201305">22</a></sup> they must be deemed to be closely related tribes, identifying  the Paṇis with the Parnians and the Dāsas of the Rigveda with the Dahae. This view, of course, necessitates a transfer of the scenes of the Rigveda, where Dāsas are prominent, and especially those in which Divodāsa--‘the heavenly Dāsa’--plays an important part,<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1201305">23</a></sup> to the far west. Hillebrandt justifies this by regarding the scene of the sixth book of the Rigveda as quite different from that of the seventh and third, in which <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>, the Bharatas, <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> appear. The <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> of the sixth book he locates in Arachosia, that of the seventh in the ‘Middle Country.’ It is, however, extremely doubtful whether this theory can be upheld. That Divodāsa should have been a <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>, and yet have fought against other Dāsas, is not in itself likely, especially when his son <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a> appears as a protagonist of Āryan civilization. It also seems unreasonable to seek in Arachosia for the river <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वति">sarasvati</a>,</b> which it is natural to locate in the ‘Middle Country.’</p><p>The wealth of the Dāsas was no doubt considerable,<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1201305">24</a></sup> but in civilization there is no reason to suppose that they were ever equal to the invaders.<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1201305">25</a></sup> Leading Dāsas were <b>Ilībiśa, <a class="vei_120a" href="चुमुरि">cumuri</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धुनि">dhuni</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पिप्रु">pipru</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वर्चिन्">varcin</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शम्बर">śambara</a>.</b> For names of aboriginal tribes, see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="किरात">kirāta</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कीकट">kīkaṭa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="चण्डाल">caṇḍāla</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पर्णक">parṇaka</a>, Śiṃyu.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201305">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 157.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201305">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. v. 34, 6; vi. 22, 10; 33, 3; 50, 6; vii. 83, 1; x. 38, 3; 69, 6; 3, 1; Av. v. 11, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201305">↑</a> ii. 20, 8 (called <i>āyasīḥ,</i> ‘made of iron’); i. 103, 3; iii. 12, 6; iv. 32, 10. They are called <i>śāradīḥ,</i> ‘autumnal,’ in 1. 131, 4; 174, 2; vi. 20, 10. <i>Cf.</i> also <i>dehyaḥ,</i> ‘ramparts,’ in vi. 47, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201305">↑</a> ii. 11, 4; iv. 28, 4; vi. 25, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201305">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 60.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201305">↑</a> ii. 12, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 25, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 101, 1; 130, 8; ii. 20, 7; iv. 16, 13; vi. 47, 21; vii. 5, 3. The <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a> colour is mentioned in iii. 34, 9, and the <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a> is contrasted with the <a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a> (of the singers) in i. 104, 2. The ‘white-hued (<i>śvitnya</i>) friends’ who, in i. 100, 18, aid in the conquest of the <a class="vei_120a" href="दस्यु">dasyu</a> and Siṃyu are doubtless Āryans. In the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 30, the day and night (<i>ahorātre</i>) are paralleled with the Śūdrāryau--that is, probably with the Āryan and <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> (the compound is not to be taken as giving the words in the correct order; <i>cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> 268). See also Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 140; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 10, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201305">↑</a> <i>kṛṣṇā <a class="vei_120a" href="त्वच्">tvac</a>,</i> ‘black skin,’ i. 130, 8; ix. 41, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201305">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दस्यु">dasyu</a>,</b> notes 6, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201305">↑</a> v. 29, 10. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दस्यु">dasyu</a>;</b> Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 138.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201305">↑</a> vii. 21, 5; x. 99, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> p. 155.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201305">↑</a> Rv. i. 33, 4. 5; iv. 16, 9; v. 7, 10; 42, 9; vi. 14, 3; viii. 70, 10; x. 22, 7. 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201305">↑</a> vii. 86, 7; viii. 56, 3; x. 62, 10. Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> 2, suggests that in viii. 46, 32, the word <i>dāsān,</i> ‘slaves,’ should be read in place of <i>dāse,</i> qualifying <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बल्बूथ">balbūtha</a>.</b> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 117, quotes the passage to indicate the admixture of Āryan and <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a> blood. See also Av. iv. 9, 8; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vii. 24, 2. It is uncertain whether <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>-pravarga,</i> as an epithet of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="रयि">rayi</a>,</i> ‘wealth,’ in Rv. i. 92, 8, means ‘consisting of troops of slaves.’ Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 82, so takes the expression in i. 158, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201305">↑</a> Av. v. 22, 6; xii. 3, 13; 4, 9; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 13, 2; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 10 (Mādhyaṃdina = 2, 7 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>). Zimmer, 107, sees this sense in <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वधू">vadhū</a></i> in Rv. viii. 19, 36. See also <b>Vadhūmant.</b></li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201305">↑</a> ii. 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201305">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 209.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1201305">↑</a> See i. 158, 5; ii. 13, 8; iv. 30, 14. 15; vi. 20, 10; vii. 99, 5; x. 49, 6, 7. None of these passages need certainly be so taken.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1201305">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 110 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1201305">↑</a> <i>Geschichte des Altertums,</i> 1, 515.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1201305">↑</a> If derived from <i>das</i> in the sense of ‘lay waste’ (Whitney, <i>Roots</i>), the original meaning would have been ‘devastator,’ ‘ravager.’</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1201305">↑</a> The Dahae may have been closely allied in race and language with the Iranians, but this is not very clearly proved. <i>Cf.</i> E. Kuhn in Kuhn's <i>Zeitschrift,</i> 28, 214; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 95. The possibility or probability of mixture with Mongolian blood is always present. So Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 112, calls the Daoi or Daai of Herodotus, i. 126, a Turanian tribe.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1201305">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 94.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1201305">↑</a> Rv. v. 34, 6. 7; vii. 6, 3 (<a class="vei_120a" href="दस्यु">dasyu</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="पणि">paṇi</a> together); Av. v. 11, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1201305">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 96 <i>et seq.,</i> He argues that <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a> occurs only four times in Maṇḍala vii., but eight times in vi., and that similarly <a class="vei_120a" href="शम्बर">śambara</a>, the <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>, is mentioned six times in vi., but only twice in vii. But Divodāsa much more probably means, as Oldenberg interprets the name, ‘the servant of heaven.’ See his <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 155, n. 1; Bergaigne, <i>Religion Védique,</i> 2, 209; below, p. 363, n. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1201305">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 176, 4; iv. 30, 13; viii. 40, 6; x. 69. 5; Av. vii. 90, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1201305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1201305">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. ii. 12, 11; iv. 30. 14; vi. 26, 5, whence it appears that the Dāsas were often dwellers in mountains, a natural refuge for beaten tribes.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 269-275, 368; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 207-213; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 101-118; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 35 (who derives <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a></i> from <i>dā,</i> ‘bind’), 254; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 2, 359 <i>et seq.;</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 96.</li></ol></div>

दास-वेश|दासवेश|दास|वेश|దాస-వేశ|దాసవేశ|దాస|వేశ|ದಾಸ-ವೇಶ|ದಾಸವೇಶ|ದಾಸ|ವೇಶ|dāsa-veśa|dāsaveśa|dāsa|veśa|daasa-vesha|daasavesha|daasa|vesha|_vei_120@1306|_vei1_pn358
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1305">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1307">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn356">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn358">1:358</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn359">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dāsa-veśa,</b> occurring only once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201306"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201306">1</a></sup> probably designates a <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a> named <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वेश">veśa</a>.</b> Sāyaṇa's interpretation of the word as ‘destruction of foes’ can hardly be correct. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201306"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201306">↑</a> ii. 13, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 209.</li></ol></div>

दास्य|దాస్య|ದಾಸ್ಯ|dāsya|daasya|_vei_120@1307|_vei1_pn359
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1306">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1308">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn358">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn359">1:359</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn360">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dāsya</b> occurs once in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iv. 2. 30 Mādhyaṃdina = 23 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>) in the sense of ‘slavery.’</p></div>

दित्य-वाह्|दित्यवाह्|दित्य|वाह्|దిత్య-వాహ్|దిత్యవాహ్|దిత్య|వాహ్|ದಿತ್ಯ-ವಾಹ್|ದಿತ್ಯವಾಹ್|ದಿತ್ಯ|ವಾಹ್|ditya-vāh|dityavāh|ditya|vāh|ditya-vaah|dityavaah|vaah|_vei_120@1308|_vei1_pn359
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1307">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1309">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn358">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn359">1:359</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn360">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ditya-vāh,</b> m.; <b>Dityauhī,</b> f., ‘a two-year-old bull or cow.’ is mentioned in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmanas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201308">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201308">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 3, 1; v. 6, 15, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiv. 10; xviii. 26; xxviii. 25; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxi. 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

दिद्यु|दिद्युत्|దిద్యు|దిద్యుత్|ದಿದ್ಯು|ದಿದ್ಯುತ್|didyu|didyut|_vei_120@1309|_vei1_pn359
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1308">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1310">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn358">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn359">1:359</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn360">↷</a><hr><p><b>Didyu, Didyut,</b> both<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201309">1</a></sup> denote in the Rigveda ‘missile,’ ‘arrow,’ whether divine or human.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201309">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दिद्यु">didyu</a>: i. 71, 5, iv. 41, 4; vii. 56, 9; 85, 2, etc.; Av. i. 2, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ii. 20; x. 17, etc. <a class="vei_120a" href="दिद्युत्">didyut</a>: Rv. i. 66, 7; v. 86, 3; vii. 25, 1, etc.; certainly divine in ii. 13, 7.</li></ol></div>

दिधिषु|దిధిషు|ದಿಧಿಷು|didhiṣu|didhiShu|_vei_120@1310|_vei1_pn359
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1309">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1311">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn358">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn359">1:359</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn360">↷</a><hr><p><b>Didhiṣu</b> in the Rigveda denotes a ‘wooer.’ It is applied<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201310">1</a></sup> to the relative, probably brother-in-law,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201310">2</a></sup> who takes the place of the husband at the funeral rite, and who, as in the Hebraic levirate, is to beget a child by the brother's wife if there is no son.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201310">3</a></sup> Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201310">4</a></sup> and Lanman<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201310">5</a></sup> consider that the word originally meant only ‘wooer,’ and applied to the king who, after the chief queen had lain beside the dead victim in the Puruṣamedha or ‘human sacrifice,’ claimed her again; but this view is hardly plausible.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201310">6</a></sup> The term is also applied to the god Pūṣan<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201310">7</a></sup> as the wooer of his mother, apparently Sūryā.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201310">8</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201310">↑</a> x. 18, 18 = Av. xviii. 3, 2 (where <i>didhiṣus</i> is merely a bad reading) = <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, vi. 1, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201310">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 2, 18, where are mentioned the brother-in-law (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="देवृ">devṛ</a></i>), a representative of the husband (there is nothing to show whether he is identical with the preceding or not), a pupil, or an aged servant (<i>jarad-<a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a></i>).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201310">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. x. 40, 2; Kaegi, <i>Der Rigveda,</i> n. 51.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201310">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 40, 708 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201310">↑</a> <i>Sanskrit Reader,</i> 385.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201310">↑</a> See Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 848, 849; Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1907, 946.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201310">↑</a> vi. 55, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201310">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 21; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 35.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Kommentar,</i> 154.</li></ol></div>

दिधिषू-पति|दिधिषूपति|दिधिषू|पति|దిధిషూ-పతి|దిధిషూపతి|దిధిషూ|పతి|ದಿಧಿಷೂ-ಪತಿ|ದಿಧಿಷೂಪತಿ|ದಿಧಿಷೂ|ಪತಿ|didhiṣū-pati|didhiṣūpati|didhiṣū|pati|didhiShuu-pati|didhiShuupati|didhiShuu|didhiShoo-pati|didhiShoopati|didhiShoo|_vei_120@1311|_vei1_pn359
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1310">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1312">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn358">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn359">1:359</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn360">↷</a><hr><p><b>Didhiṣū-pati</b> occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201311">1</a></sup> and Kapiṣṭhala Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201311">2</a></sup> as well as in the Āpastamba,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201311">3</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201311">4</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>  <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Sūtras,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201311">5</a></sup> in lists of people who have committed sin (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="एनस्">enas</a></i>). The traditional rendering<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201311">6</a></sup> is ‘husband of a woman married a second time’; <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201311">7</a></sup> seems to apply the term to the brother-in-law who is ‘married’ to his sister-in-law after his brother's death for the purpose of begetting a child, if he displays conjugal affection to her (<i>anurajyate kāmataḥ</i>).<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201311">8</a></sup> This sense would be possible, since <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दिधिषु">didhiṣu</a></b> denotes a ‘wooer,’ and a widow could be regarded as a ‘wooer’ when able to control her own choice of a spouse. But another tradition<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201311">9</a></sup> holds that <a class="vei_120a" href="दिधिषू">didhiṣū</a> means the elder sister whose younger sister has married before her. This view is supported by a passage in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201311">10</a></sup> and by the use of the word <i>agredidhiṣū-<a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201311">11</a></sup> which must mean the ‘husband of a younger sister married before the elder.’ In this case also <a class="vei_120a" href="दिधिषू">didhiṣū</a> would mean ‘wooer,’ the elder sister being so called because, if her parents do not arrange a marriage for her, she is, according to Viṣṇu,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201311">12</a></sup> to make her own choice of a husband (<i>kuryāt svayaṃvaram</i>). See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="एदिधिषुःपति">edidhiṣuḥpati</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दैधिषव्य">daidhiṣavya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201311">↑</a> xxxi. 7, quoted in Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 579.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201311">↑</a> xlvii. 7, quoted <i>ibid.,</i> 579, 580.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201311">↑</a> ii. 5, 12, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201311">↑</a> xv. 16.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201311">↑</a> i. 18; xx. 7 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201311">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. <a class="vei_120a" href="दिधिषु">didhiṣu</a>,</i> 3.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201311">↑</a> iii. 173.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201311">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Leist, <i>Altarisches Jus Gentium,</i> 106.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201311">↑</a> Laugākṣi, quoted by Kullūka on <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>, iii. 160; Commentary on Āpastamba, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201311">↑</a> xx. 7 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201311">↑</a> <i>Cf. agre-<a class="vei_120a" href="दिधिषु">didhiṣu</a>,</i> ‘one who woos (a younger sister) before (her elder sister is married),’ in Āpastamba, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a>, xv. 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>, i. 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.; agre-dadhus,</i> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 1, 9; <i>agre-dadhiṣu,</i> Kapiṣṭhala, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.; agra-<a class="vei_120a" href="दिधिषु">didhiṣu</a>,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 8, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201311">↑</a> Viṣṇu <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxiv. 40.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 579-586.</li></ol></div>

दिव्|దివ్|ದಿವ್|div|_vei_120@1312|_vei1_pn360
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1311">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1313">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn359">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn360">1:360</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn363">↷</a><hr><p><b>Div,</b> ‘sky.’ The world as a whole is regarded as divided into the three domains of ‘earth,’ ‘air’ or ‘atmosphere,’ and ‘heaven’ or ‘sky’ (<i>div</i>),<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201312">1</a></sup> or alternatively into ‘heaven and earth’ (<i>dyāvā-<a class="vei_120a" href="पृथिवी">pṛthivī</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201312">2</a></sup> which two are then considered as comprising the universe, the atmosphere being included in the sky. Lightning, wind, and rain belong to the atmosphere, solar and  similar phenomena to the sky. In some passages<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201312">3</a></sup> the vault (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="नाक">nāka</a></i>) of the sky is added after the usual triad, and before the celestial light (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्वर्">svar</a>, jyotis</i>).</p><p>The threefold division of the universe is reflected in a threefold division of the three elements--earth, air, and sky. Thus a highest (<i>uttama,</i><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201312">4</a></sup> <i>uttara,</i><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201312">5</a></sup> <i>pārya</i><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201312">6</a></sup> ), a middle, and a lowest heaven are specified.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201312">4</a></sup> In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201312">7</a></sup> the three heavens are distinguished as ‘rich in water’ (<i>udanvatī</i>), as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पीलुमती">pīlumatī</a></i> (of uncertain meaning), and as the <i>pradyaus,</i> where the Fathers sit. Heaven is frequently called <i>vyoman</i> as well as <i>rocana</i><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201312">8</a></sup> (properly the ‘luminous space’ of heaven), and the dividing firmament which separates the visible upper world from the highest heaven is called, besides <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नाक">nāka</a>,</i> ‘vault,’ <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सानु">sānu</a>,</i> ‘summit,’ <i>viṣṭap,</i> ‘surface,’ and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पृष्ठ">pṛṣṭha</a>,</i> ‘ridge,’ and even ‘ridge of the vault,’<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201312">9</a></sup> or ‘summit of the vault.’<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201312">10</a></sup></p><p>Similarly three atmospheres (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="रजस्">rajas</a></i>), or oftener two, are alluded to,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201312">11</a></sup> but the division here is merely artificial. In one passage<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201312">12</a></sup> six <i>rajāṃsi,</i> ‘regions,’ are referred to, the heavens and the earths no doubt being meant. The usual name for the atmosphere is <i>antarikṣa.</i></p><p>The three earths are equally artificial, the origin of the triad being probably the use of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पृथिवी">pṛthivī</a></i> in the plural<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201312">13</a></sup> to denote the three divisions of the universe (just as <i>pitarau,</i> ‘two fathers,’ denotes ‘father and mother’).<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201312">14</a></sup> The earth is called <i>kṣam, kṣā, gmā,</i> or designated by the epithets <i>mahī,</i> ‘the great,’ <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पृथिवी">pṛthivī</a></i> or <i>urvī,</i> ‘the broad,’ <i>uttānā,</i> ‘the extended,’ and is regularly contrasted as <i>idam,</i> ‘this world here,’ with the upper sphere.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201312">15</a></sup>  The shape of the earth is compared with a wheel in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1201312">16</a></sup> and is expressly called ‘circular’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="परि">pari</a>-maṇḍala</i>) in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1201312">17</a></sup> When earth is conjoined with heaven, the two are conceived as great bowls (<i>camvā</i>) turned towards each other.<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1201312">18</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1201312">19</a></sup> the two are regarded as halves of an egg. The distance of heaven from the earth is given by the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1201312">20</a></sup> as a thousand days' journey for the sun-bird, by the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1201312">21</a></sup> as a thousand days' journey for a horse, while the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1201312">22</a></sup> whimsically estimates the distance as equivalent to a thousand cows standing one on the top of the other.</p><p>According to Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1201312">23</a></sup> the Vedic poets conceived the atmosphere to be above the earth in its upper division only, but below it in its lower stratum. The evidence,<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1201312">24</a></sup> however, for the latter assumption is quite insufficient.<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1201312">25</a></sup> The theory of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_26_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_1201312">26</a></sup> is that the sun merely reverses its bright side at night, turning its light on the stars and the moon while it retraverses its course to the east; and it has been shown<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1201312">25</a></sup> that this is probably the doctrine of the Rigveda also.<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_1201312">27</a></sup> literature. The Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सूर्य">sūrya</a></b> and <b>Candramās.</b> For the Vedic knowledge of the planets, see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्रह">graha</a>.</b></p><p>There is no geographical division of the earth in Vedic literature. The Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_28_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_1201312">28</a></sup> states that the centre of the earth is a span north of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्लक्ष">plakṣa</a> Prāsravaṇā,</b> and that the centre of the sky is the constellation of the seven <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषिस्">ṛṣis</a>, the Great Bear. For the quarters, see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दिश्">diś</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201312">↑</a> Rv. ii. 40; viii. 6, 15; 10, 6; 90, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201312">↑</a> Rv. i. 143, 2; 159, 1; 160, 1; iv. 14, 2, etc.; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 8, 3, 9; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vii. 4, 2; viii. 1, 3. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 1, 2, and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, vii. 3, it is said that when heavy and constant rain falls people say. ‘Heaven and earth have united.’</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201312">↑</a> Av. iv. 14, 3 = Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvii. 67.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201312">↑</a> Rv. v. 60, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201312">↑</a> Rv. iv. 26, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201312">↑</a> Rv. vi. 40, 5. In Rv. v. 4, 3, it is called <i>tṛtīya.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201312">↑</a> Rv. v. 60, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201312">↑</a> xviii. 2, 48.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201312">↑</a> <i>Trīṇi</i> or <i>trī rocanā,</i> Rv. i. 102, 8; 149, 4; v. 69, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201312">↑</a> Rv. i. 125, 5. <i>Cf.</i> iii. 2, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201312">↑</a> Rv. viii. 103, 2. <i>Cf.</i> also ix. 86, 27.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201312">↑</a> Rv. iv. 53, 5; v. 69, 1. <i>Cf.</i> also the references to the ‘highest’ atmosphere, <i>uttama,</i> ix. 22, 5; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="परम">parama</a>,</i> iii. 30, 2; <i>tṛtīya,</i> ix. 74, 6; x. 45, 3; 123, 8. The ‘lower’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="उपर">upara</a></i>) or ‘terrestrial’ (<i>pārthiva</i>) is contrasted with the ‘heavenly’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दिव्य">divya</a></i>) space. See i. 62, 5; iv. 53, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201312">↑</a> Rv. i. 164, 6. <i>Cf.</i> vii. 87, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201312">↑</a> Rv. i. 188, 9. 10; vii. 104, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201312">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>Altindische Syntax,</i> p. 98; Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Grammar,</i> 183<i>c</i> (p. 158).</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201312">↑</a> Rv. i. 22, 17; 154, 1. 3; and regularly in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1201312">↑</a> x. 89, 4. On the other hand, the earth is regarded as <i>catur-bhṛṣṭi,</i> ‘four-cornered,’ in Rv. x. 58, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1201312">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1201312">↑</a> Rv. iii. 55, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1201312">↑</a> iii. 1, 2; Śāṅkhāvana Āraṇyaka, vii. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1201312">↑</a> x. 8, 18 = xiii. 2, 38; 3, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1201312">↑</a> ii. 17. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आश्विन">āśvina</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1201312">↑</a> xvi. 8, 6; in xxi. 1, 9, with the alternatives of 1,000 days of the journey of a horse, or of the sun, or 1,000 leagues.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1201312">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 357, 358.</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1201312">↑</a> Rv. v. 81, 4; vi. 9, 1; vii. 80, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1201312">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_1201312">↑</a> iii. 44, 4. Speyer's interpretation of this passage, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1906, 723-727, is anticipated and supplemented by Macdonell, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1201312">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_1201312">↑</a> i. 115, 5; x. 37, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_1201312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_1201312">↑</a> iv. 26, 12. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 10, 16; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 31, n. 2. <i>Cf.</i> Bergaigne, <i>Religion Vedique,</i> 1, 1-3; Wallis, <i>Cosmology of the Rigveda,</i> 111-117; Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 357-359; Macdonell, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> pp. 8-11; Thibaut. <i>Astronomie, Astrologie und Mathematik,</i> 5, 6; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 358-364.</li></ol></div>

दिवोदास अतिथिग्व|దివోదాస అతిథిగ్వ|ದಿವೋದಾಸ ಅತಿಥಿಗ್ವ|divodāsa atithigva|divodaasa atithigva|_vei_120@1313|_vei1_pn363
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1312">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1314">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn360">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn363">1:363</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn364">↷</a><hr><p><b>Divodāsa Atithigva</b> is one of the leading princes of the early Vedic age. He was a son of <a class="vei_120a" href="वध्र्यश्व">vadhryaśva</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201313">1</a></sup> and father, or more probably grandfather, of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>,</b> the famous king of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तृत्सु">tṛtsu</a></b> family, among the <b>Bharatas.</b> Probably <a class="vei_120a" href="पिजवन">pijavana</a> was the son and <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a> the grandson. Divodāsa was naturally a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201313">2</a></sup> and, like <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>, was an opponent of the <b>Turvaśas</b> and <b>Yadus.</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201313">3</a></sup> His great enemy was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शम्बर">śambara</a>,</b> the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>,</b> who was apparently chief of a mountain people,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201313">4</a></sup> and whom he repeatedly defeated.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201313">5</a></sup> He was also, it seems, like his father <a class="vei_120a" href="वध्र्यश्व">vadhryaśva</a>,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201313">6</a></sup> an energetic supporter of the fire ritual, for <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> is once called by his name in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201313">7</a></sup> On the other hand, he was defeated, with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आयु">āyu</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुत्स">kutsa</a>,</b> by Indra's aid. In several passages he seems closely connected with the singer family, the <b>Bharadvājas.</b><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201313">8</a></sup></p><p>From one passage,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201313">9</a></sup> where Divodāsa is said to have fought against the <b>Paṇis,</b> the <b>Pārāvatas,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बृसय">bṛsaya</a>,</b> Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201313">10</a></sup> has inferred that he was engaged in conflicts with the tribes of Arachosia, and interpreting the name as the ‘heavenly Dāsa’<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201313">11</a></sup> conjectures that he was himself a <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>. This conclusion is not probable, for the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a></b> on which the battle in question took place, and which can hardly be the Haraqaiti of Arachosia, would naturally designate the later <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>, while the Pārāvatas are mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201313">12</a></sup> as in the east, about the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a>.</b> Bergaigne's  opinion<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201313">13</a></sup> that Divodāsa and <a class="vei_120a" href="अतिथिग्व">atithigva</a> were different people cannot be supported in view of the complete parallelism in the acts of the two persons.<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201313">14</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतर्दन">pratardana</a>.</b></p><p>The people of Divodāsa are referred to in a hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201313">15</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201313">↑</a> Rv. vi. 61, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201313">↑</a> Rv. vi. 16, 4, 5, 19. For <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a> as his descendant, see vii. 18, 25, with verse 23, where <a class="vei_120a" href="पैजवन">paijavana</a> is an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201313">↑</a> As <a class="vei_120a" href="अतिथिग्व">atithigva</a>, Rv. vii. 19, 8; as Divodāsa, ix. 61, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201313">↑</a> Rv. i. 130, 7; ii. 12, 11; vi. 26, 5; vii. 18, 20. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 161.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201313">↑</a> See Rv. i. 112, 14; 116, 18; 119, 4; 130, 7-10; ii. 19, 6; iv. 26, 3; 30, 20; vi. 26, 3. 5; 43, 1; 47, 21. 22; ix. 61, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201313">↑</a> Rv. x. 69, 1 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 176; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologic,</i> 1, 96, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201313">↑</a> Daivodāsa, ‘worshipped by Divodāsa’; viii. 103, 2. <i>Cf.</i> vi. 16, 5. 19; 31, 1. For the defeat by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, <i>cf.</i> Rv. i. 53, 10; ii. 14, 7; vi. 18, 13; viii. 64, 2; Bergaigne, <i>Religion Védique,</i> 2, 337, 344.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201313">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 112, 13, 14; 116, 18; vi. 16, 5; 31, 4; 47, 22 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xv. 3, 7; Hillebrandt, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 104.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201313">↑</a> vi. 61, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201313">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 97 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201313">↑</a> This is very improbable. See Bergaigne, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 209; Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 155; <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 49, 175; 51, 272.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201313">↑</a> ix. 4, 11. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पारावत">pārāvata</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201313">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 342 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201313">↑</a> Compare, <i>e.g.,</i> vii. 19, 8, with ix. 61, 2 (opposed to <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a>); i. 51, 6; vi. 26, 3, with ii. 19, 6; vi. 31, 4 (defeat of <a class="vei_120a" href="शम्बर">śambara</a>); and see Hillebrandt, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 3, 268; Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 210 <i>et seq.;</i> Macdonell, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> p. 161.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201313">↑</a> i. 130, 10 (one of the series attributed to <a class="vei_120a" href="परुच्छेप">parucchepa</a>).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 126; Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 406; Ludwig, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 3, 114, 176; Grierson, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 604, 837; Keith, <i>ibid.,</i> 831 <i>et seq.;</i> 1138 <i>et seq.</i> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> distinguishes two Divodāsas, one being the ancestor or father of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>, the other the enemy of <a class="vei_120a" href="शम्बर">śambara</a>. Divodāsa is not mentioned in Maṇḍalas, iii, v. viii, x.</li></ol></div>

दिवो-दास भैम-सेनि|दिवो-दास भैमसेनि|दिवो-दास भैम|सेनि|దివో-దాస భైమ-సేని|దివో-దాస భైమసేని|దివో-దాస భైమ|సేని|ದಿವೋ-ದಾಸ ಭೈಮ-ಸೇನಿ|ದಿವೋ-ದಾಸ ಭೈಮಸೇನಿ|ದಿವೋ-ದಾಸ ಭೈಮ|ಸೇನಿ|divo-dāsa bhaima-seni|divo-dāsa bhaimaseni|divo-dāsa bhaima|seni|divo-daasa bhaima-seni|divo-daasa bhaimaseni|divo-daasa bhaima|_vei_120@1314|_vei1_pn364
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1313">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1315">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn363">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn364">1:364</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn365">↷</a><hr><p><b>Divo-<a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="भैम">bhaima</a>-seni</b> (‘descendant of Bhīmasena’) is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201314"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201314">1</a></sup> as a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आरुणि">āruṇi</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201314"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201314">↑</a> vii. 1, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 472.</li></ol></div>

दिव्य|దివ్య|ದಿವ್ಯ|divya|_vei_120@1315|_vei1_pn364
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1314">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1316">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn363">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn364">1:364</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn365">↷</a><hr><p><b>Divya,</b> ‘ordeal,’ is a term not found until the later literature, but several references to the practice of ordeals have been seen in Vedic literature. The fire ordeal seen in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201315">1</a></sup> by Schlagintweit,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201315">2</a></sup> Weber,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201315">3</a></sup> Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201315">4</a></sup> Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201315">5</a></sup> and others, has been disproved by Grill,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201315">6</a></sup> Bloomfield,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201315">7</a></sup> and Whitney.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201315">8</a></sup> But such an ordeal appears in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201315">9</a></sup> and an ordeal with a glowing axe<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201315">10</a></sup> occurs in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> as applied in an accusation of theft. Geldner<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201315">11</a></sup> suggests that this usage is referred to even in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201315">12</a></sup> but this is most improbable.<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201315">13</a></sup> Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201315">14</a></sup> and Griffith<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201315">15</a></sup> discover in another  passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1201315">16</a></sup> references to <b>Dīrghatamas'</b> having been subjected to the fire and water ordeals, but this view cannot be supported. According to Weber,<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1201315">17</a></sup> the ‘balance’ ordeal is referred to in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1201315">18</a></sup> but see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुला">tulā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201315">↑</a> ii. 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201315">↑</a> <i>Die Gottesurtheile der Indier,</i> 13 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201315">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 168.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201315">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 445.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201315">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 184.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201315">↑</a> <i>Hundert Lieder,</i>^2 45, 87.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201315">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, ccxxi; <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 11, 334, 335; <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 294.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201315">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda. 54.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201315">↑</a> xiv. 6, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201315">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 16.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201315">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 150.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201315">↑</a> iii. 53, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201315">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 254.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201315">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 4, 44.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201315">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 1, 210.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1201315">↑</a> i. 158, 4 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1201315">↑</a> <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 21; 2, 363.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1201315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1201315">↑</a> xi. 2, 7, 33.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 145; <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 44, 347, 348; Stenzler, <i>ibid.,</i> 9, 669 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

दिव्य श्वन्|దివ్య శ్వన్|ದಿವ್ಯ ಶ್ವನ್|divya śvan|divya shvan|_vei_120@1316|_vei1_pn365
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1315">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1317">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn364">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn365">1:365</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn366">↷</a><hr><p><b>Divya Śvan,</b> the ‘divine dog,’ in one passage of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201316">1</a></sup> appears to denote Canis major or Sirius. But Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201316">2</a></sup> thinks that the two divine dogs referred to in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201316">3</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201316">4</a></sup> are the sun and moon, and that the sun is meant in the Atharvaveda.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201316">↑</a> vi. 80, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201316">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 163; <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 500, 501.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201316">↑</a> i. 6, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201316">↑</a> i. 1, 2, 4-6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 353; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 341.</li></ol></div>

दिश्|దిశ్|ದಿಶ್|diś|dish|_vei_120@1317|_vei1_pn365
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1316">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1318">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn364">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn365">1:365</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn366">↷</a><hr><p><b>Diś,</b> ‘direction,’ is a word very frequently used in the Rigveda and later<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201317">1</a></sup> to denote a quarter of the sky. As a general rule, four quarters are mentioned--east, south, west, north.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201317">2</a></sup> But the number of the ‘directions’ is sometimes increased up to ten by the addition to these four of various others. The five points include the zenith (<i>ūrdhvā</i>);<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201317">3</a></sup> the six, the zenith and the nadir (<i>ūrdhvā</i> and <i>avācī</i>);<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201317">4</a></sup> the seven, the zenith, the ground on which one stands (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ध्रुवा">dhruvā</a></i>), and the air (<i>antarikṣa</i>) between these two (<i>vyadhvā</i>);<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201317">5</a></sup> the eight include the intermediate quarters (S.E., S.W., N.E., N.W.);<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201317">6</a></sup> the nine add to these the zenith;<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201317">7</a></sup> the ten, zenith and nadir.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201317">8</a></sup> The number  five is sometimes made up by the ground beneath the observer's feet (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ध्रुवा">dhruvā</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201317">9</a></sup> and the number six by that point (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ध्रुवा">dhruvā</a></i>) and the zenith (<i>ūrdhvā</i>);<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201317">10</a></sup> the ‘lofty’ (<i>bṛhatī</i>)<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201317">11</a></sup> sometimes taking the place of the ‘vertical’ (<i>ūrdhvā</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201317">↑</a> Rv. i. 124, 3; 183, 5; iii. 30, 12; Av. iii. 31, 4; xi. 2, 12, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201317">↑</a> Rv. vii. 72, 5; x. 36, 14; 42, 11; Av. xv. 2, 1 <i>et seq.,</i> etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201317">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 1, 15; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 8, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201317">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 12, 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxii. 24; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 2, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201317">↑</a> Rv. ix. 114, 3; Av. iv. 40, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 4, 1, 20; ix. 5, 2, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201317">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 1, 15; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 1, 40, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201317">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 28, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201317">↑</a> Rv. i. 164, 14; viii. 101, 13; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 2, 2, 34; viii. 4, 2, 13, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201317">↑</a> Av. viii. 9, 15; xiii. 3, 6; xv. 14, 1-5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ix. 32; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 4, 3, 10, and <i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ध्रुवा">dhruvā</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201317">↑</a> Av. iii. 27, 1; iv. 14, 8; xii. 3, 55; xv. 4, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> xviii. 3, 34; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 14, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Rv. x. 14, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201317">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiv. 13, <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 8.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 359; Weber, <i>Proceedings of the Berlin Academy,</i> 1895, 846; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 293. 294; 18, 153; St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

दीर्घ-तमस्|दीर्घ-तम|दीर्घतमस्|दीर्घतम|दीर्घ|तमस्|तम|దీర్ఘ-తమస్|దీర్ఘ-తమ|దీర్ఘతమస్|దీర్ఘతమ|దీర్ఘ|తమస్|తమ|ದೀರ್ಘ-ತಮಸ್|ದೀರ್ಘ-ತಮ|ದೀರ್ಘತಮಸ್|ದೀರ್ಘತಮ|ದೀರ್ಘ|ತಮಸ್|ತಮ|dīrgha-tamas|dīrgha-tama|dīrghatamas|dīrghatama|dīrgha|tamas|tama|diirgha-tamas|diirgha-tama|diirghatamas|diirghatama|diirgha|deergha-tamas|deergha-tama|deerghatamas|deerghatama|deergha|_vei_120@1318|_vei1_pn366
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1317">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1319">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn365">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn366">1:366</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn367">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dīrgha-tamas</b> (‘long darkness’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मामतेय">māmateya</a></b> (‘son of Mamatā’) <b>Aucathya</b> (‘son of Ucatha’) is mentioned as a singer in one hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201318">1</a></sup> and is referred to in several passages<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201318">2</a></sup> by his metronymic, <a class="vei_120a" href="मामतेय">māmateya</a>, alone. He is said, both in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1201318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1201318">1b</a></sup> and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_3b_vei_1201318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3b_vei_1201318">3b</a></sup> to have attained the tenth decade of life. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4b_vei_1201318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4b_vei_1201318">4b</a></sup> he appears as the priest of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a>.</b> The Bṛhaddevatā<sup id="ref_120_5b_vei_1201318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5b_vei_1201318">5b</a></sup> contains a preposterous legend made up of fragments of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_6b_vei_1201318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6b_vei_1201318">6b</a></sup> according to which <a class="vei_120a" href="दीर्घतमस्">dīrghatamas</a> was born blind, but recovered his sight; in old age he was thrown into a river by his servants, one of whom, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रैतन">traitana</a>,</b> attacked him, but killed himself instead. Carried down by the stream, he was cast up in the <a class="vei_120a" href="अंग">aṅga</a> country, where he married Uśij, a slave girl, and begot <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a>.</b> The two legends here combined are not even consistent, for the second ignores Dīrghatamas' recovery of sight. To attach any historical importance to them, as does Pargiter,<sup id="ref_120_7b_vei_1201318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7b_vei_1201318">7b</a></sup> would seem to be unwise. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201318">↑</a> i. 158, 1. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201318">↑</a> i. 147, 3; 152, 6; iv. 4, 13. In viii. 9, 10, <a class="vei_120a" href="दीर्घतमस्">dīrghatamas</a> is mentioned with <a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a>, but not as a relative.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1201318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1201318">↑</a> i. 158, 1. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3b_vei_1201318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3b_vei_1201318">↑</a> ii. 17; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaha,</i> 14.</li><li id="fx_120_4b_vei_1201318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4b_vei_1201318">↑</a> viii. 23.</li><li id="fx_120_5b_vei_1201318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5b_vei_1201318">↑</a> iv. 11-15; 21-25, with Macdonell's notes.</li><li id="fx_120_6b_vei_1201318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6b_vei_1201318">↑</a> From i. 140-164, which hymns are traditionally attributed to <a class="vei_120a" href="दीर्घतमस्">dīrghatamas</a>. But see Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischn Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 221.</li><li id="fx_120_7b_vei_1201318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7b_vei_1201318">↑</a> <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society.</i> 1910, 44.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rig <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>, 3, 164, 165; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 226, 232, 247, 268, 279.</li></ol></div>

दीर्घ-नीथ|दीर्घनीथ|दीर्घ|नीथ|దీర్ఘ-నీథ|దీర్ఘనీథ|దీర్ఘ|నీథ|ದೀರ್ಘ-ನೀಥ|ದೀರ್ಘನೀಥ|ದೀರ್ಘ|ನೀಥ|dīrgha-nītha|dīrghanītha|dīrgha|nītha|diirgha-niitha|diirghaniitha|diirgha|niitha|deergha-neetha|deerghaneetha|deergha|neetha|_vei_120@1319|_vei1_pn367
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1318">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1320">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn366">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn367">1:367</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn368">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dīrgha-nītha</b> appears to be the proper name of a sacrificer in one hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201319">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201319">↑</a> viii. 50, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> Ludwig understands the word as an adjective, meaning ‘of long duration.’</li></ol></div>

दीर्घ-श्रवस्|दीर्घ-श्रव|दीर्घश्रवस्|दीर्घश्रव|दीर्घ|श्रवस्|श्रव|దీర్ఘ-శ్రవస్|దీర్ఘ-శ్రవ|దీర్ఘశ్రవస్|దీర్ఘశ్రవ|దీర్ఘ|శ్రవస్|శ్రవ|ದೀರ್ಘ-ಶ್ರವಸ್|ದೀರ್ಘ-ಶ್ರವ|ದೀರ್ಘಶ್ರವಸ್|ದೀರ್ಘಶ್ರವ|ದೀರ್ಘ|ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಶ್ರವ|dīrgha-śravas|dīrgha-śrava|dīrghaśravas|dīrghaśrava|dīrgha|śravas|śrava|diirgha-shravas|diirgha-shrava|diirghashravas|diirghashrava|diirgha|shravas|shrava|deergha-shravas|deergha-shrava|deerghashravas|deerghashrava|deergha|_vei_120@1320|_vei1_pn367
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1319">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1321">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn366">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn367">1:367</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn368">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dīrgha-śravas</b> (‘far-famed’) is the name of a royal seer who, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201320"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201320">1</a></sup> having been banished from his kingdom, and suffering from actual hunger, ‘saw’ a certain <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> (chant), and thus obtained food. In one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201320"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201320">2</a></sup> an <a class="vei_120a" href="औशिज">auśija</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201320"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201320">3</a></sup> a merchant (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वणिज्">vaṇij</a></i>), is mentioned as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दीर्घ-श्रवस्">dīrgha-śravas</a>,</i> which may be a proper name, as Sāyaṇa holds, or an adjective, as it is understood by Roth.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201320"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201320">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201320"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201320">↑</a> xv. 3, 25.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201320"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201320">↑</a> i. 112, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201320"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201320">↑</a> A metronymic, ‘descendant of Uśij,’ according to Sāyaṇa; but an adjective, meaning ‘desirous,’ according to Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201320"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201320">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 114.</li></ol></div>

दीर्घाप्सस्|दीर्घाप्स|దీర్ఘాప్సస్|దీర్ఘాప్స|ದೀರ್ಘಾಪ್ಸಸ್|ದೀರ್ಘಾಪ್ಸ|dīrghāpsas|dīrghāpsa|diirghaapsas|diirghaapsa|deerghaapsas|deerghaapsa|_vei_120@1321|_vei1_pn367
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1320">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1322">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn366">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn367">1:367</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn368">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dīrghāpsas,</b> used in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201321">1</a></sup> as an epithet of a chariot, means, according to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201321">2</a></sup> ‘having a long front part.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201321">↑</a> i. 122, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201321">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. Cf.</i> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 312.</li></ol></div>

दीर्घायुत्व|దీర్ఘాయుత్వ|ದೀರ್ಘಾಯುತ್ವ|dīrghāyutva|diirghaayutva|deerghaayutva|_vei_120@1322|_vei1_pn367
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1321">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1323">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn366">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn367">1:367</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn368">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dīrghāyutva,</b> ‘longevity,’ is a constant object of the prayers of the Vedic Indians,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201322">1</a></sup> and length of life is never deprecated in the Saṃhitās and Brāhmaṇas, while the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201322">2</a></sup> is full of spells intended to prolong existence (<i>āyuṣyāṇi</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201322">↑</a> Rv. x. 62, 2; Av. i. 22, 2, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 9, 1, 13, etc. So the adjective <i>dīrghāyus,</i> Rv. iv. 15, 9. 10; x. 85, 39; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xii. 100, etc. The Brāhmaṇas regularly express the reward for ritual actions by the phrase <i>sarvam āyur eti,</i> ‘he lives all his days.’ The ideal of life is 100 years. See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 193; <i>Festgruss an Roth,</i> 137; Lanman, <i>Sanskrit Reader,</i> 384.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201322">↑</a> ii. 13; 28; 29; vii. 32, and many other hymns. See Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 49 <i>et seq.; Atharvaveda,</i> 63-65.</li></ol></div>

दीर्घारण्य|దీర్ఘారణ్య|ದೀರ್ಘಾರಣ್ಯ|dīrghāraṇya|diirghaaraNya|deerghaaraNya|_vei_120@1323|_vei1_pn367
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1322">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1324">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn366">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn367">1:367</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn368">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dīrghāraṇya,</b> ‘wide tract of forest,’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201323"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201323">1</a></sup> and Śatapatha<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201323"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201323">2</a></sup> Brāhmanas refers to the extensive jungles which  must clearly then have covered Northern India. In one of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> passages<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201323"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201323">3</a></sup> it is said that in the east the villages are close together and frequent, while in the west there are forests.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201323"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201323">↑</a> iii. 44; vi. 23.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201323"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201323">↑</a> xiii. 3, 7, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201323"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201323">↑</a> iii. 44.</li></ol></div>

दीव्|దీవ్|ದೀವ್|dīv|diiv|deev|_vei_120@1324|_vei1_pn368
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1323">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1325">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn367">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn368">1:368</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn369">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dīv</b> (fem.) in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201324">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201324">2</a></sup> denotes the ‘game of dice.’ See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201324">↑</a> x. 27, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201324">↑</a> vii. 50, 9; 109, 5.</li></ol></div>

दुघा|దుఘా|ದುಘಾ|dughā|dughaa|_vei_120@1325|_vei1_pn368
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1324">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1326">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn367">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn368">1:368</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn369">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dughā,</b> ‘yielding milk,’ denotes ‘cow’ in a few passages in the Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201325">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201325">↑</a> Rv. viii. 50, 3; x. 67, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxviii. 16, 39, etc.</li></ol></div>

दुन्दुभि|దున్దుభి|ದುನ್ದುಭಿ|dundubhi|_vei_120@1326|_vei1_pn368
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1325">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1327">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn367">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn368">1:368</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn369">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dundubhi,</b> apparently an onomatopoetic word, means ‘drum,’ as used in both war and peace. It is often mentioned from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201326"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201326">1</a></sup> onwards.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201326"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201326">2</a></sup> A special sort of drum was the ‘earth drum,’ made by digging a hole in the ground and covering it with a hide. This was employed in the Mahāvrata, a rite performed at the winter solstice, for the purpose of driving away influences hostile to the return of the sun.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201326"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201326">3</a></sup> A ‘drumbeater’ is included in the list of sacrificial victims at the Puruṣamedha or ‘human sacrifice.’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201326"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201326">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201326"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201326">↑</a> i. 28, 5; vi. 47, 29. 31.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201326"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201326">↑</a> Av. v. 20, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> 21, 7; 31, 7; vi. 38, 4; xii. 1, 41; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 6, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1, 5, 6; <i>dundubhya,</i> ‘connected with the drum,’ Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 35.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201326"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201326">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxiv. 5 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 477); <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvii. 14, 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, v. 1, 5, with Keith's notes; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 148, n. 2; Friedlaender, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 29, 45.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201326"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201326">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 13, 1 (not in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā). <i>Cf.</i> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 4, 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 289; and for the epic drum in battle, Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 318.</li></ol></div>

दुर्|దుర్|ದುರ್|dur|_vei_120@1327|_vei1_pn368
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1326">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1328">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn367">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn368">1:368</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn369">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dur</b> is used several times in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201327">1</a></sup> to denote ‘door,’ both literally and metaphorically. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201327">↑</a> i. 68, 10; 113, 4; 121, 4; 188, 5; ii. 2, 7, etc.</li></ol></div>

दुरोण|దురోణ|ದುರೋಣ|duroṇa|duroNa|_vei_120@1328|_vei1_pn369
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1327">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1329">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn368">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn369">1:369</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn370">↷</a><hr><p><b>Duroṇa</b> is used in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201328">1</a></sup> and sometimes later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201328">2</a></sup> to denote ‘home,’ both literally and metaphorically. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गृह">gṛha</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201328">↑</a> iii. 1, 18; 25, 5; iv. 13, 1; v. 76, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201328">↑</a> Av. vii. 17, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxiii. 71, etc.</li></ol></div>

दुर्-ग|दुर्ग|दुर्|దుర్-గ|దుర్గ|దుర్|ದುರ್-ಗ|ದುರ್ಗ|ದುರ್|dur-ga|durga|dur|_vei_120@1329|_vei1_pn369
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1328">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1330">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn368">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn369">1:369</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn370">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dur-ga,</b> ‘hard to approach,’ occurs in the Rigveda as a neuter substantive only, sometimes in the sense of ‘fort,’ ‘stronghold.’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201329"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201329">1</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुर्">pur</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201329"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201329">↑</a> v. 34, 7; vii. 25, 2.</li></ol></div>

दुर्-गह|दुर्गह|दुर्|गह|దుర్-గహ|దుర్గహ|దుర్|గహ|ದುರ್-ಗಹ|ದುರ್ಗಹ|ದುರ್|ಗಹ|dur-gaha|durgaha|dur|gaha|_vei_120@1330|_vei1_pn369
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1329">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1331">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn368">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn369">1:369</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn370">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dur-gaha</b> is mentioned in a hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201330">1</a></sup> where his grandsons are lauded for their generosity, though Sāyaṇa renders the word adjectivally.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201330">2</a></sup> In another passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201330">3</a></sup> however, Sāyaṇa sees in the epithet <a class="vei_120a" href="दौर्गह">daurgaha</a> a description of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a></b> as Durgaha's son, who was either captured by the enemy or slain, and whose wife, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्सानी">purukutsānī</a>,</b> then obtained a son, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a>,</b> to restore the line; he also quotes a story, not found in the Bṛhaddevatā,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201330">4</a></sup> to support this interpretation. On the other hand, the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201330">5</a></sup> seems to take <a class="vei_120a" href="दौर्गह">daurgaha</a> as meaning a horse. Sieg<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201330">6</a></sup> thinks that the same sense should be adopted in the Rigveda passage, which he interprets as referring to the sacrifice of a horse, <a class="vei_120a" href="दौर्गह">daurgaha</a>, by King <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a> to gain a son; he also sees in Dadhikrāvan, with Pischel<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201330">7</a></sup> and Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201330">8</a></sup> a real horse, the charger of <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a>. The Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa's interpretation of <a class="vei_120a" href="दौर्गह">daurgaha</a> is, however, doubtful, and cannot be regarded as receiving support from the case of Dadhikrāvan, who was probably a divinity, and not a real horse at all.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201330">9</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201330">↑</a> viii. 65, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201330">↑</a> ‘Plunged in distress’ (<i>duḥkhaṃ gāhamāna</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201330">↑</a> iv. 42, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201330">↑</a> As Roth. St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> says it is.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201330">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 5. According to the Naighaṇṭuka (i. 14), <a class="vei_120a" href="दौर्गह">daurgaha</a> is a synonym of ‘horse.’</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201330">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 96-102.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201330">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 124.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201330">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 4, 79. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 71.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201330">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> pp. 148, 149.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 3, 163, 174; Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 301, 302.</li></ol></div>

दुर्-णामन्|दुर्णामन्|दुर्|णामन्|దుర్-ణామన్|దుర్ణామన్|దుర్|ణామన్|ದುರ್-ಣಾಮನ್|ದುರ್ಣಾಮನ್|ದುರ್|ಣಾಮನ್|dur-ṇāman|durṇāman|dur|ṇāman|dur-Naaman|durNaaman|Naaman|_vei_120@1331|_vei1_pn370
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1330">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1332">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn369">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn370">1:370</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn371">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dur-ṇāman,</b> ‘of evil name,’ is the designation in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201331"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201331">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201331"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201331">2</a></sup> of a demon causing disease, or the disease itself. The <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201331"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201331">3</a></sup> explains the words as meaning a ‘worm,’ an interpretation which accords with the widespread belief in disease-causing worms.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201331"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201331">4</a></sup> Later Durnāman denotes ‘hæmorrhoids.’<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201331"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201331">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201331"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201331">↑</a> x. 162, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201331"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201331">↑</a> ii. 25, 2; viii. 6, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> xvi. 6, 7; xix. 36, 1 <i>et seq.</i> So also the feminine Durṇāmnī, iv. 17, 5; xix. 36, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201331"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201331">↑</a> vi. 12.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201331"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201331">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 61; <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 314 <i>et seq.,</i> 351.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201331"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201331">↑</a> Suśruta, 1, 177, 10, etc.</li></ol></div>

दुर्-मुख|दुर्मुख|दुर्|मुख|దుర్-ముఖ|దుర్ముఖ|దుర్|ముఖ|ದುರ್-ಮುಖ|ದುರ್ಮುಖ|ದುರ್|ಮುಖ|dur-mukha|durmukha|dur|mukha|_vei_120@1332|_vei1_pn370
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1331">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1333">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn369">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn370">1:370</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn371">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dur-mukha,</b> ‘ugly-faced,’ is the name, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201332"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201332">1</a></sup> of a <a class="vei_120a" href="पाञ्चाल">pāñcāla</a>, that is, <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a> king, who conquered the world, and whose priest was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बृहदुक्थ">bṛhaduktha</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201332"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201332">↑</a> viii. 23. The reading may be <i>a-rājā,</i> ‘not a king,’ but this is not necessary.</li></ol></div>

दुर्य|దుర్య|ದುರ್ಯ|durya|_vei_120@1333|_vei1_pn370
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1332">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1334">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn369">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn370">1:370</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn371">↷</a><hr><p><b>Durya,</b> ‘belonging to the door or house,’ appears in several passages of the Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201333"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201333">1</a></sup> as a plural substantive denoting the ‘door-posts,’ or more generally ‘dwelling.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201333"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201333">↑</a> Masculine plural, Rv. i. 91, 19; x. 40, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 3, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, i. 11; feminine plural, Rv. iv. 1, 9. 18; 2, 12; vii. 1, 11.</li></ol></div>

दुर्योण|దుర్యోణ|ದುರ್ಯೋಣ|duryoṇa|duryoNa|_vei_120@1334|_vei1_pn370
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1333">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1335">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn369">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn370">1:370</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn371">↷</a><hr><p><b>Duryoṇa</b> occurs a few times in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201334"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201334">1</a></sup> in the sense of ‘house.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201334"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201334">↑</a> i. 174, 7; v. 29, 10; 32, 8.</li></ol></div>

दुर्-वराह|दुर्वराह|दुर्|वराह|దుర్-వరాహ|దుర్వరాహ|దుర్|వరాహ|ದುರ್-ವರಾಹ|ದುರ್ವರಾಹ|ದುರ್|ವರಾಹ|dur-varāha|durvarāha|dur|varāha|dur-varaaha|durvaraaha|varaaha|_vei_120@1335|_vei1_pn370
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1334">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1336">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn369">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn370">1:370</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn371">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dur-varāha</b> probably denotes a ‘wild boar.’ It is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201335"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201335">1</a></sup> and the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201335"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201335">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201335"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201335">↑</a> xii. 4, 1, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201335"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201335">↑</a> i. 51, 4 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 23, 332).</li></ol></div>

दुला|దులా|ದುಲಾ|dulā|dulaa|_vei_120@1336|_vei1_pn370
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1335">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1337">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn369">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn370">1:370</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn371">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dulā.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> (<a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्तिकास्">kṛttikās</a>).</b></p></div>

दुश्-चर्मन्|दुश्चर्मन्|दुश्|चर्मन्|దుశ్-చర్మన్|దుశ్చర్మన్|దుశ్|చర్మన్|ದುಶ್-ಚರ್ಮನ್|ದುಶ್ಚರ್ಮನ್|ದುಶ್|ಚರ್ಮನ್|duś-carman|duścarman|duś|carman|dush-charman|dushcharman|dush|charman|_vei_120@1337|_vei1_pn370
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1336">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1338">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn369">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn370">1:370</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn371">↷</a><hr><p><b>Duś-carman,</b> ‘afflicted with a skin disease,’ occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201337"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201337">1</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201337"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201337">2</a></sup> The disease meant is probably leprosy, the usual name of which is <b><a class="vei_120a" href="किलास">kilāsa</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201337"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201337">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201337"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201337">↑</a> ii. 1, 4, 3; 5, 1, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201337"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201337">↑</a> i. 7, 8, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201337"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201337">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 3, 17; xxiii. 16, 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka v. 4, 12.</li></ol></div>

दुः-शासु|दुःशासु|दुः|शासु|దుః-శాసు|దుఃశాసు|దుః|శాసు|ದುಃ-ಶಾಸು|ದುಃಶಾಸು|ದುಃ|ಶಾಸು|duḥ-śāsu|duḥśāsu|duḥ|śāsu|duH-shaasu|duHshaasu|duH|shaasu|_vei_120@1338|_vei1_pn371
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1337">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1339">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn370">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn371">1:371</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn372">↷</a><hr><p><b>Duḥ-śāsu</b> is possibly a proper name in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201338"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201338">1</a></sup> and would then denote an enemy of <b>Kuruśravaṇa.</b> Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201338"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201338">2</a></sup> thinks that he was a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्शु">parśu</a></b> or Persian, but this is most improbable, and the word may simply be an adjective meaning ‘malignant.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201338"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201338">↑</a> x. 33, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201338"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201338">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 165.</li></ol></div>

दुः-शीम|दुःशीम|दुः|शीम|దుః-శీమ|దుఃశీమ|దుః|శీమ|ದುಃ-ಶೀಮ|ದುಃಶೀಮ|ದುಃ|ಶೀಮ|duḥ-śīma|duḥśīma|duḥ|śīma|duH-shiima|duHshiima|duH|shiima|duH-sheema|duHsheema|sheema|_vei_120@1339|_vei1_pn371
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1338">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1340">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn370">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn371">1:371</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn372">↷</a><hr><p><b>Duḥ-śīma</b> is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201339"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201339">1</a></sup> as a generous donor, his patronymic perhaps being <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तान्व">tānva</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201339"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201339">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201339"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201339">↑</a> x. 93, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201339"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201339">↑</a> x. 93, 15.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 166.</li></ol></div>

दुष्-टरीतु|दुष्टरीतु|दुष्|टरीतु|దుష్-టరీతు|దుష్టరీతు|దుష్|టరీతు|ದುಷ್-ಟರೀತು|ದುಷ್ಟರೀತು|ದುಷ್|ಟರೀತು|duṣ-ṭarītu|duṣṭarītu|duṣ|ṭarītu|duSh-Tariitu|duShTariitu|duSh|Tariitu|duSh-Tareetu|duShTareetu|Tareetu|_vei_120@1340|_vei1_pn371
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1339">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1341">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn370">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn371">1:371</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn372">↷</a><hr><p><b>Duṣ-ṭarītu,</b> ‘hard to defeat,’ is the name of a king of the <b>Sṛñjayas,</b> who was deposed from a principality that had existed for ten generations, but was re-instated by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चाक्र">cākra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="स्थपति">sthapati</a></b> in spite of the resistance of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बल्हिक प्रातिपीय">balhika prātipīya</a>,</b> according to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201340"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201340">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201340"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201340">↑</a> xii. 9, 3, 1 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 205-207.</li></ol></div>

दुः-षन्त|दुःषन्त|दुः|षन्त|దుః-షన్త|దుఃషన్త|దుః|షన్త|ದುಃ-ಷನ್ತ|ದುಃಷನ್ತ|ದುಃ|ಷನ್ತ|duḥ-ṣanta|duḥṣanta|duḥ|ṣanta|duH-Shanta|duHShanta|duH|Shanta|_vei_120@1341|_vei1_pn371
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1340">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1342">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn370">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn371">1:371</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn372">↷</a><hr><p><b>Duḥ-ṣanta.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दौःषन्ति">dauḥṣanti</a>.</b></p></div>

दुहितृ|దుహితృ|ದುಹಿತೃ|duhitṛ|duhitR|duhitRi|duhitRu|duhitRRi|_vei_120@1342|_vei1_pn371
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1341">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1343">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn370">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn371">1:371</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn372">↷</a><hr><p><b>Duhitṛ</b> is the regular designation of ‘daughter’ from the Rigveda onwards.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201342">1</a></sup> The word appears to be derived from <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दुह्">duh</a>,</i> ‘milk,’ in the sense of one who nourishes a child, rather than as the ‘milker’ of the primitive family or the suckling.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201342">2</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्त्री">strī</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पितृ">pitṛ</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="भ्रातृ">bhrātṛ</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201342">↑</a> Rv. viii. 101, 15; x. 17, 1; 40, 5; 61, 5, 7; Av. ii. 14, 2; vi. 100, 3; vii. 12, 1; x. 1, 25; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 4, 1; 8, 1, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201342">↑</a> Delbrück, <i>Dic indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 454.</li></ol></div>

दूत|దూత|ದೂತ|dūta|duuta|doota|_vei_120@1343|_vei1_pn371
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1342">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1344">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn370">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn371">1:371</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn372">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dūta,</b> ‘messenger’ or envoy,' is found several times in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201343"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201343">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201343"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201343">2</a></sup> used metaphorically. The <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सूत">sūta</a></b> seems to have performed the duties later assigned to the <a class="vei_120a" href="दूत">dūta</a>. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201343"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201343">↑</a> iii. 3, 2; vi. 8, 4; vii. 3, 3; x. 14, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201343"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201343">↑</a> Av. viii. 8, 10, etc.; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 5, 1, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 1, etc. The feminine form Dūtī is found in Rv. x. 108, 2, 3, in the story of Saramā's mission to the Paṇis. Dūtya, ‘mission,’ occurs in Rv. 1, 12, 4; 161, 1; iv. 7, 8; 8, 4, etc.</li></ol></div>

दूर्वा|దూర్వా|ದೂರ್ವಾ|dūrvā|duurvaa|doorvaa|_vei_120@1344|_vei1_pn372
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1343">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1345">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn371">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn372">1:372</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn373">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dūrvā,</b> a species of grass (<i>Panicum dactylon</i>), is mentioned frequently from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201344"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201344">1</a></sup> onwards.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201344"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201344">2</a></sup> It grew in damp ground.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201344"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201344">3</a></sup> A simile occurring in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201344"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201344">4</a></sup> seems to indicate that the ears lay horizontal with the stem. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पाकदूर्वा">pākadūrvā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201344"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201344">↑</a> x. 16, 13; 134, 5; 142, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201344"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201344">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 9, 2; v. 2, 8, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiii. 20; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 5. 8; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 5, 10, 5; vii. 4, 2, 10, 12, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201344"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201344">↑</a> Rv. x. 16, 13; 142, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201344"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201344">↑</a> x. 134, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 70.</li></ol></div>

दूर्श|దూర్శ|ದೂರ್ಶ|dūrśa|duursha|doorsha|_vei_120@1345|_vei1_pn372
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1344">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1346">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn371">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn372">1:372</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn373">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dūrśa,</b> denoting some kind of garment, is mentioned twice in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201345">1</a></sup> Weber<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201345">2</a></sup> thinks that it was worn by the aborigines.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201345">↑</a> iv. 7, 6; viii. 6, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201345">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 29.</li></ol></div>

दूषीका|దూషీకా|ದೂಷೀಕಾ|dūṣīkā|duuShiikaa|dooSheekaa|_vei_120@1346|_vei1_pn372
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1345">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1347">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn371">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn372">1:372</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn373">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dūṣīkā,</b> ‘rheum of the eyes,’ is mentioned as a disease in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201346"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201346">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201346"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201346">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201346"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201346">↑</a> xvi. 6, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201346"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201346">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxiv. 12; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxv. 9; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 3, 10.</li></ol></div>

दृढ-च्युत् आगस्ति|दृढच्युत् आगस्ति|दृढ|च्युत् आगस्ति|దృఢ-చ్యుత్ ఆగస్తి|దృఢచ్యుత్ ఆగస్తి|దృఢ|చ్యుత్ ఆగస్తి|ದೃಢ-ಚ್ಯುತ್ ಆಗಸ್ತಿ|ದೃಢಚ್ಯುತ್ ಆಗಸ್ತಿ|ದೃಢ|ಚ್ಯುತ್ ಆಗಸ್ತಿ|dṛḍha-cyut āgasti|dṛḍhacyut āgasti|dṛḍha|cyut āgasti|dRDha-chyut aagasti|dRDhachyut aagasti|dRDha|chyut aagasti|dRiDha-chyut aagasti|dRiDhachyut aagasti|dRiDha|dRuDha-chyut aagasti|dRuDhachyut aagasti|dRuDha|dRRiDha-chyut aagasti|dRRiDhachyut aagasti|dRRiDha|_vei_120@1347|_vei1_pn372
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1346">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1348">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn371">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn372">1:372</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn373">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dṛḍha-<a class="vei_120a" href="च्युत्">cyut</a> Āgasti</b> (‘descendant of Agastya’) is mentioned in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201347"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201347">1</a></sup> as having been Udgātṛ priest at the Sattra (‘sacrificial session’) of the Vibhindukīyas.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201347"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201347">↑</a> iii. 233 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 38). He is given in the Anukramaṇī (where the form of the patronymic is <a class="vei_120a" href="आगस्त्य">āgastya</a>) as the author of Rv. ix. 25. <i>Cf. Indische Studien,</i> 3, 219.</li></ol></div>

दृढ-जयन्त|दृढजयन्त|दृढ|जयन्त|దృఢ-జయన్త|దృఢజయన్త|దృఢ|జయన్త|ದೃಢ-ಜಯನ್ತ|ದೃಢಜಯನ್ತ|ದೃಢ|ಜಯನ್ತ|dṛḍha-jayanta|dṛḍhajayanta|dṛḍha|jayanta|dRDha-jayanta|dRDhajayanta|dRDha|dRiDha-jayanta|dRiDhajayanta|dRiDha|dRuDha-jayanta|dRuDhajayanta|dRuDha|dRRiDha-jayanta|dRRiDhajayanta|dRRiDha|_vei_120@1348|_vei1_pn372
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1347">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1349">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn371">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn372">1:372</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn373">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dṛḍha-jayanta.</b> See <b>Vipaścit</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैपश्चित">vaipaścita</a>.</b></p></div>

दृति|दृति|దృతి_౧|దృతి|ದೃತಿ_೧|ದೃತಿ|dṛti_1|dṛti|dRti_1|dRti|dRiti_1|dRiti|dRuti_1|dRuti|dRRiti_1|dRRiti|_vei_120@1349|_vei1_pn372
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1348">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1350">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn371">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn372">1:372</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn373">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Dṛti,</b> a ‘leather bag to hold fluids,’ is frequently mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201349">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201349">2</a></sup> In one passage<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201349">3</a></sup> it is called <i>dhmāta,</i> ‘inflated,’ the man afflicted with dropsy being compared with such a bag. Milk (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षीर">kṣīra</a></b>) and intoxicating liquor (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुरा">surā</a></b>) are mentioned as kept in bags.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201349">4</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201349">↑</a> i. 191, 10; iv. 51, 1, 3; v. 83, 7; vi. 48, 18; 103, 2; viii. 5, 19; 9, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201349">↑</a> Av. vii. 18, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 19, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxvi. 18, 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 3, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 10, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201349">↑</a> Rv. vii. 89, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 20, 30.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201349">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 11, 26; xvi. 13, 13.</li></ol></div>

दृति ऐन्द्रोत|दृति ऐन्द्रोत|దృతి ఐన్ద్రోత_౨|దృతి ఐన్ద్రోత|ದೃತಿ ಐನ್ದ್ರೋತ_೨|ದೃತಿ ಐನ್ದ್ರೋತ|dṛti aindrota_2|dṛti aindrota|dRti aindrota_2|dRti aindrota|dRiti aindrota_2|dRiti aindrota|dRuti aindrota_2|dRuti aindrota|dRRiti aindrota_2|dRRiti aindrota|_vei_120@1350|_vei1_pn373
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1349">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1351">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn372">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn373">1:373</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn374">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Dṛti Aindrota</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्रोत">indrota</a></b>’) is mentioned in the Pañcaviṃśa Brāhmaṇā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201350">1</a></sup> as a contemporary of <b>Abhipratārin <a class="vei_120a" href="काक्षसेनि">kākṣaseni</a></b> and as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्रोत">indrota</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दैवाप">daivāpa</a></b> in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Jaīminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201350">2</a></sup> Possibly the same <a class="vei_120a" href="दृति">dṛti</a> is meant in the compound <a class="vei_120a" href="दृति">dṛti</a>-Vātavantau, which is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201350">3</a></sup> The former is here said to have continued, after the Mahāvrata was over, the sacrificial session in which both had been engaged, with the result that his descendants prospered more than the Vātavatas.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201350">↑</a> xiv. 1, 12. 15.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201350">↑</a> iii. 40, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201350">↑</a> xxv. 3, 6. So a Sattra of a year's duration is later called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दृति">dṛti</a>-vātavator <a class="vei_120a" href="अयन">ayana</a>,</i> Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxiv. 4, 16; 6, 25; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiii. 23, 1; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 10, 7.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 52, 53.</li></ol></div>

दृप्त-बालाकि गार्ग्य|दृप्तबालाकि गार्ग्य|दृप्त|बालाकि गार्ग्य|దృప్త-బాలాకి గార్గ్య|దృప్తబాలాకి గార్గ్య|దృప్త|బాలాకి గార్గ్య|ದೃಪ್ತ-ಬಾಲಾಕಿ ಗಾರ್ಗ್ಯ|ದೃಪ್ತಬಾಲಾಕಿ ಗಾರ್ಗ್ಯ|ದೃಪ್ತ|ಬಾಲಾಕಿ ಗಾರ್ಗ್ಯ|dṛpta-bālāki gārgya|dṛptabālāki gārgya|dṛpta|bālāki gārgya|dRpta-baalaaki gaargya|dRptabaalaaki gaargya|dRpta|baalaaki gaargya|dRipta-baalaaki gaargya|dRiptabaalaaki gaargya|dRipta|dRupta-baalaaki gaargya|dRuptabaalaaki gaargya|dRupta|dRRipta-baalaaki gaargya|dRRiptabaalaaki gaargya|dRRipta|_vei_120@1351|_vei1_pn373
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1350">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1352">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn372">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn373">1:373</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn374">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dṛpta-<a class="vei_120a" href="बालाकि">bālāki</a> Gārgya</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गर्ग">garga</a></b>’) is the name of a teacher who is mentioned in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (ii. 1, 1) as a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अजातशत्रु">ajātaśatru</a></b> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a>.</b></p></div>

दृभीक|దృభీక|ದೃಭೀಕ|dṛbhīka|dRbhiika|dRbheeka|dRibhiika|dRubhiika|dRRibhiika|_vei_120@1352|_vei1_pn373
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1351">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1353">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn372">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn373">1:373</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn374">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dṛbhīka</b> is the name of a man<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201352"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201352">1</a></sup> or a demon,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201352"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201352">2</a></sup> who, according to the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201352"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201352">3</a></sup> was slain by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201352"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201352">↑</a> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 152, 207, who compares the Derbikes; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 162.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201352"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201352">↑</a> Grassmann, <i>Wo7rterbuch, s.v.;</i> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 85.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201352"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201352">↑</a> ii. 14, 3.</li></ol></div>

दृशान भार्गव|దృశాన భార్గవ|ದೃಶಾನ ಭಾರ್ಗವ|dṛśāna bhārgava|dRshaana bhaargava|dRishaana bhaargava|dRushaana bhaargava|dRRishaana bhaargava|_vei_120@1353|_vei1_pn373
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1352">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1354">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn372">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn373">1:373</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn374">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dṛśāna Bhārgava</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भृगु">bhṛgu</a></b>’) is mentioned as a seer in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201353"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201353">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201353"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201353">↑</a> xvi. 8. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 459.</li></ol></div>

दृषद्|దృషద్|ದೃಷದ್|dṛṣad|dRShad|dRiShad|dRuShad|dRRiShad|_vei_120@1354|_vei1_pn373
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1353">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1355">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn372">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn373">1:373</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn374">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dṛṣad</b> appears in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201354">1</a></sup> and Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201354">2</a></sup> to denote not a millstone,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201354">3</a></sup> but merely a stone used to pound grain, which was placed on another stone as a support. When used later<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201354">4</a></sup>  in connexion with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपला">upalā</a>,</b> the lower and the upper millstone, or mortar and pestle may be meant; but this is not certain. Eggeling<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201354">5</a></sup> renders them as the large and small millstones. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपर">upara</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपला">upalā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201354">↑</a> vii. 104, 22; viii. 72, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201354">↑</a> ii. 31, 1; v. 23, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201354">↑</a> Roth. St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 269.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201354">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 8, 3; 9, 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 1, 22; ii. 6, 1, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201354">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 11 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दृषद्">dṛṣad</a>-upale,</i> which are here distinguished from ‘mortar and pestle,’ <i><a class="vei_120a" href="उलूखल">ulūkhala</a>-musale</i>).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 108, 109.</li></ol></div>

दृषद्वती|दृषद्वति|దృషద్వతీ|దృషద్వతి|ದೃಷದ್ವತೀ|ದೃಷದ್ವತಿ|dṛṣadvatī|dṛṣadvati|dRShadvatii|dRShadvati|dRShadvatee|dRiShadvatii|dRiShadvati|dRuShadvatii|dRuShadvati|dRRiShadvatii|dRRiShadvati|_vei_120@1355|_vei1_pn374
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1354">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1356">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn373">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn374">1:374</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn375">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dṛṣadvatī,</b> ‘stony,’ is the name of a river which flows into the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a></b> after running for a time parallel to it. It is mentioned in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201355"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201355">1</a></sup> along with the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आपया">āpayā</a>,</b> as the scene of action of the <a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a> princes. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201355"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201355">2</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201355"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201355">3</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="दृषद्वती">dṛṣadvatī</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> are the scene of special sacrifices. In <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201355"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201355">4</a></sup> these two rivers form the western boundary of the Middle Country.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201355"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201355">↑</a> iii. 23, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201355"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201355">↑</a> xxv. 10, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201355"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201355">↑</a> Kātyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxiv. 6, 6, 38; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 19, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201355"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201355">↑</a> ii. 17.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 18; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 34; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 67, 102; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 87.</li></ol></div>

दृष्ट|దృష్ట|ದೃಷ್ಟ|dṛṣṭa|dRShTa|dRiShTa|dRuShTa|dRRiShTa|_vei_120@1356|_vei1_pn374
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1355">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1357">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn373">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn374">1:374</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn375">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dṛṣṭa.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अदृष्ट">adṛṣṭa</a>.</b></p></div>

देवक मान्यमान|దేవక మాన్యమాన|ದೇವಕ ಮಾನ್ಯಮಾನ|devaka mānyamāna|devaka maanyamaana|_vei_120@1357|_vei1_pn374
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1356">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1358">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn373">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn374">1:374</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn375">↷</a><hr><p><b>Devaka Mānyamāna</b> (‘descendant of Manyamāna’) appears in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201357"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201357">1</a></sup> as an opponent of the Tṛtsus, and as connected with <a class="vei_120a" href="शम्बर">śambara</a>. Possibly, however, as Grassmann suggests, the words should be understood as denoting <a class="vei_120a" href="शम्बर">śambara</a>, ‘who deemed himself a god,’ <i>devaka</i> being used contemptuously.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201357"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201357">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201357"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201357">↑</a> vii. 18, 20 (<i>devakaṃ cin mānyamānam</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201357"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201357">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. ii. 11. 2 (<i>amartyaṃ cid dāsam manyamānam</i>).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 173.</li></ol></div>

देवकी-पुत्र|देवकीपुत्र|देवकी|देवकि|पुत्र|దేవకీ-పుత్ర|దేవకీపుత్ర|దేవకీ|దేవకి|పుత్ర|ದೇವಕೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ದೇವಕೀಪುತ್ರ|ದೇವಕೀ|ದೇವಕಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|devakī-putra|devakīputra|devakī|devaki|putra|devakii-putra|devakiiputra|devakii|devakee-putra|devakeeputra|devakee|_vei_120@1358|_vei1_pn374
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1357">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1359">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn373">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn374">1:374</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn375">↷</a><hr><p><b>Devakī-putra,</b> ‘son of <a class="vei_120a" href="देवकी">devakī</a>,’ is the metronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण">kṛṣṇa</a></b> in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201358"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201358">1</a></sup> According to the Epic,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201358"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201358">2</a></sup> a Devaka was father of <a class="vei_120a" href="देवकी">devakī</a>, Kṛṣṇa's mother; the St. Petersburg Dictionary suggests that he was the ‘king of the Gandharvas’, also referred to in the Epic.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201358"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201358">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201358"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201358">↑</a> iii. 17, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201358"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201358">↑</a> Mahābhārata, i. 4480; v. 80, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201358"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201358">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> i. 2704.</li></ol></div>

देव-जन-विद्या|देव-जनविद्या|देव-जन|विद्या|దేవ-జన-విద్యా|దేవ-జనవిద్యా|దేవ-జన|విద్యా|ದೇವ-ಜನ-ವಿದ್ಯಾ|ದೇವ-ಜನವಿದ್ಯಾ|ದೇವ-ಜನ|ವಿದ್ಯಾ|deva-jana-vidyā|deva-janavidyā|deva-jana|vidyā|deva-jana-vidyaa|deva-janavidyaa|vidyaa|_vei_120@1359|_vei1_pn375
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1358">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1360">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn374">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn375">1:375</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn376">↷</a><hr><p><b>Deva-<a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a>-vidyā,</b> ‘knowledge of divine beings,’ is one of the sciences enumerated in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201359"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201359">1</a></sup> and the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201359"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201359">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201359"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201359">↑</a> xiii. 4, 3, 10. <i>Cf.</i> x. 5, 2, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201359"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201359">↑</a> vii. 1, 2. 4; 2, 1; 7, 1.</li></ol></div>

देव-तरस् श्यावसायन काश्यप|देवतरस् श्यावसायन काश्यप|देव|तरस् श्यावसायन काश्यप|దేవ-తరస్ శ్యావసాయన కాశ్యప|దేవతరస్ శ్యావసాయన కాశ్యప|దేవ|తరస్ శ్యావసాయన కాశ్యప|ದೇವ-ತರಸ್ ಶ್ಯಾವಸಾಯನ ಕಾಶ್ಯಪ|ದೇವತರಸ್ ಶ್ಯಾವಸಾಯನ ಕಾಶ್ಯಪ|ದೇವ|ತರಸ್ ಶ್ಯಾವಸಾಯನ ಕಾಶ್ಯಪ|deva-taras śyāvasāyana kāśyapa|devataras śyāvasāyana kāśyapa|deva|taras śyāvasāyana kāśyapa|deva-taras shyaavasaayana kaashyapa|devataras shyaavasaayana kaashyapa|taras shyaavasaayana kaashyapa|_vei_120@1360|_vei1_pn375
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1359">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1361">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn374">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn375">1:375</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn376">↷</a><hr><p><b>Deva-taras <a class="vei_120a" href="श्यावसायन">śyāvasāyana</a> Kāśyapa</b> (‘descendant of Kaśyapa’) is mentioned in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201360"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201360">1</a></sup> as a pupil of <b>Rsyasṛṅga.</b> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201360"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201360">2</a></sup> as Śāvasāyana, he is a pupil of his father <a class="vei_120a" href="शवस्">śavas</a>, who again was a pupil of Kāśyapa.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201360"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201360">↑</a> iii. 40, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201360"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201360">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373.</li></ol></div>

देवत्या|దేవత్యా|ದೇವತ್ಯಾ|devatyā|devatyaa|_vei_120@1361|_vei1_pn375
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1360">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1362">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn374">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn375">1:375</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn376">↷</a><hr><p><b>Devatyā</b> occurs in the text of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201361"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201361">1</a></sup> where it must, if the reading is correct, denote some animal.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201361"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201361">2</a></sup> But the reading should no doubt be <i><a class="vei_120a" href="रोहिणी">rohiṇī</a>-devatyās,</i> ‘having the red one as deity.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201361"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201361">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201361"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201361">↑</a> i. 22, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201361"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201361">↑</a> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201361"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201361">↑</a> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 23.</li></ol></div>

देवन|దేవన|ದೇವನ|devana|_vei_120@1362|_vei1_pn375
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1361">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1363">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn374">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn375">1:375</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn376">↷</a><hr><p><b>Devana</b> is mentioned once in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201362">1</a></sup> in connexion with dicing. The word must designate the place on which the dice are thrown (elsewhere called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अधिदेवन">adhidevana</a></b>), and it is so explained by <a class="vei_120a" href="दुर्ग">durga</a> in his commentary on the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201362">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201362">↑</a> x. 43, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201362">↑</a> v. 22.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Lüders, <i>Das Würfelspiel im alten Indien,</i> 14.</li></ol></div>

देव-नक्षत्र|देवनक्षत्र|देव|नक्षत्र|దేవ-నక్షత్ర|దేవనక్షత్ర|దేవ|నక్షత్ర|ದೇವ-ನಕ್ಷತ್ರ|ದೇವನಕ್ಷತ್ರ|ದೇವ|ನಕ್ಷತ್ರ|deva-nakṣatra|devanakṣatra|deva|nakṣatra|deva-nakShatra|devanakShatra|nakShatra|deva-naxatra|devanaxatra|naxatra|_vei_120@1363|_vei1_pn375
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1362">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1364">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn374">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn375">1:375</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn376">↷</a><hr><p><b>Deva-nakṣatra,</b> ‘asterism of the gods,’ is the name applied in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201363">1</a></sup> to the first fourteen lunar mansions, which are said to be south, while the others are called <a class="vei_120a" href="यमनक्षत्र">yamanakṣatra</a>, ‘asterisms of <a class="vei_120a" href="यम">yama</a>,’ and are said to be north. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201363">↑</a> i. 5, 2, 6, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 309, 310.</li></ol></div>

देव-भाग श्रौतर्ष|देवभाग श्रौतर्ष|देव|भाग श्रौतर्ष|దేవ-భాగ శ్రౌతర్ష|దేవభాగ శ్రౌతర్ష|దేవ|భాగ శ్రౌతర్ష|ದೇವ-ಭಾಗ ಶ್ರೌತರ್ಷ|ದೇವಭಾಗ ಶ್ರೌತರ್ಷ|ದೇವ|ಭಾಗ ಶ್ರೌತರ್ಷ|deva-bhāga śrautarṣa|devabhāga śrautarṣa|deva|bhāga śrautarṣa|deva-bhaaga shrautarSha|devabhaaga shrautarSha|bhaaga shrautarSha|_vei_120@1364|_vei1_pn375
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1363">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1365">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn374">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn375">1:375</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn376">↷</a><hr><p><b>Deva-<a class="vei_120a" href="भाग">bhāga</a> Śrautarṣa</b> is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201364"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201364">1</a></sup> as the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, or ‘domestic priest,’ of both the  <b>Sṛñjayas</b> and the <b>Kurus.</b> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201364"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201364">2</a></sup> he is said to have taught <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गिरिज बाभ्रव्य">girija bābhravya</a></b> the science of the dissection of the sacrificial animal (<i>pasor vibhakti</i>). In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201364"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201364">3</a></sup> he is an authority on the Sāvitra <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201364"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201364">↑</a> ii. 4, 4, 5. This passage is misquoted by Sāyaṇa on Rv. i. 81, 3. See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 2, 9. n.; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 152.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201364"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201364">↑</a> vii. x.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201364"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201364">↑</a> iii. 10, 9, 11.</li></ol></div>

देव-मलिम्लुच्|देवमलिम्लुच्|देव|मलिम्लुच्|దేవ-మలిమ్లుచ్|దేవమలిమ్లుచ్|దేవ|మలిమ్లుచ్|ದೇವ-ಮಲಿಮ್ಲುಚ್|ದೇವಮಲಿಮ್ಲುಚ್|ದೇವ|ಮಲಿಮ್ಲುಚ್|deva-malimluc|devamalimluc|deva|malimluc|deva-malimluch|devamalimluch|malimluch|_vei_120@1365|_vei1_pn376
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1364">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1366">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn375">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn376">1:376</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn377">↷</a><hr><p><b>Deva-malimluc,</b> ‘robber of the gods,’ is the epithet of Rahasya,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201365"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201365">1</a></sup> who is said in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201365"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201365">2</a></sup> to have slain the pious <b>Vaikhānasas</b> at <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मुनिमरण">munimaraṇa</a></b> (‘saint's death’). He was apparently an <a class="vei_120a" href="असुर">asura</a>, but may have been a real person.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201365"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201365">↑</a> Or Rahasyu.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201365"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201365">↑</a> xiv. 4, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 51, 52.</li></ol></div>

देव-मुनि|देवमुनि|देव|मुनि|దేవ-ముని|దేవముని|దేవ|ముని|ದೇವ-ಮುನಿ|ದೇವಮುನಿ|ದೇವ|ಮುನಿ|deva-muni|devamuni|deva|muni|_vei_120@1366|_vei1_pn376
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1365">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1367">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn375">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn376">1:376</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn377">↷</a><hr><p><b>Deva-muni,</b> ‘divine saint,’ is the epithet of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर">tura</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xxv. 14, 5). The name is given in the Anukramaṇī to the author of a hymn of the Rigveda (x. 146).</p></div>

देव-राजन्|देवराजन्|देव|राजन्|దేవ-రాజన్|దేవరాజన్|దేవ|రాజన్|ದೇವ-ರಾಜನ್|ದೇವರಾಜನ್|ದೇವ|ರಾಜನ್|deva-rājan|devarājan|deva|rājan|deva-raajan|devaraajan|raajan|_vei_120@1367|_vei1_pn376
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1366">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1368">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn375">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn376">1:376</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn377">↷</a><hr><p><b>Deva-rājan</b> apparently denotes a king of Brahminical descent in the phrase ‘Sāmans of Devarājans’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xviii. 10, 5). <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्यर्षि">rājanyarṣi</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>.</b></p></div>

देव-रात|देवरात|देव|रात|దేవ-రాత|దేవరాత|దేవ|రాత|ದೇವ-ರಾತ|ದೇವರಾತ|ದೇವ|ರಾತ|deva-rāta|devarāta|deva|rāta|deva-raata|devaraata|raata|_vei_120@1368|_vei1_pn376
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1367">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1369">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn375">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn376">1:376</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn377">↷</a><hr><p><b>Deva-rāta</b> (‘god-given’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्वामित्र">vaiśvāmitra</a></b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a></b>’) is the name given to <a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a> after his adoption by <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201368"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201368">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201368"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201368">↑</a> vii. 17. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 27.</li></ol></div>

देवल|దేవల|ದೇವಲ|devala|_vei_120@1369|_vei1_pn376
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1368">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1370">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn375">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn376">1:376</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn377">↷</a><hr><p><b>Devala</b> is mentioned as a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā (xxii. 11). See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दैवल">daivala</a>.</b></p></div>

देववन्त्|దేవవన్త్|ದೇವವನ್ತ್|devavant|_vei_120@1370|_vei1_pn376
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1369">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1371">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn375">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn376">1:376</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn377">↷</a><hr><p><b>Devavant</b> is mentioned in a Dānastuti (‘Praise of Gifts’) in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201370">1</a></sup> as the ancestor of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>,</b> apparently his grandfather; or if <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पिजवन">pijavana</a></b> be accepted as Sudās' father, and <b>Divodāsa</b> as his grandfather, then his great-great-grandfather, and father of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वध्र्यश्व">vadhryaśva</a>.</b> The succession in the latter case would then be <a class="vei_120a" href="देववन्त्">devavant</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वध्र्यश्व">vadhryaśva</a>, Divodāsa, <a class="vei_120a" href="पिजवन">pijavana</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201370">↑</a> vii. 18, 22. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 171; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 138.</li></ol></div>

देव-वात|देववात|देव|वात|దేవ-వాత|దేవవాత|దేవ|వాత|ದೇವ-ವಾತ|ದೇವವಾತ|ದೇವ|ವಾತ|deva-vāta|devavāta|deva|vāta|deva-vaata|devavaata|vaata|_vei_120@1371|_vei1_pn377
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1370">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1372">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn376">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn377">1:377</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn378">↷</a><hr><p><b>Deva-vāta</b> (‘desired of the gods’) is the name of a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a></b> prince in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201371"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201371">1</a></sup> where he is mentioned as sacrificing on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृषद्वती">dṛṣadvatī</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आपया">āpayā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201371"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201371">↑</a> iii. 23, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 409; Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 218.</li></ol></div>

देव-विद्या|देवविद्या|देव|विद्या|దేవ-విద్యా|దేవవిద్యా|దేవ|విద్యా|ದೇವ-ವಿದ್ಯಾ|ದೇವವಿದ್ಯಾ|ದೇವ|ವಿದ್ಯಾ|deva-vidyā|devavidyā|deva|vidyā|deva-vidyaa|devavidyaa|vidyaa|_vei_120@1372|_vei1_pn377
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1371">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1373">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn376">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn377">1:377</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn378">↷</a><hr><p><b>Deva-vidyā,</b> ‘knowledge of the gods,’ is one of the sciences enumerated in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vii. 1, 2, 4; 2, 1; 7, 1).</p></div>

देव-श्रवस्|देव-श्रव|देवश्रवस्|देवश्रव|देव|श्रवस्|श्रव|దేవ-శ్రవస్|దేవ-శ్రవ|దేవశ్రవస్|దేవశ్రవ|దేవ|శ్రవస్|శ్రవ|ದೇವ-ಶ್ರವಸ್|ದೇವ-ಶ್ರವ|ದೇವಶ್ರವಸ್|ದೇವಶ್ರವ|ದೇವ|ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಶ್ರವ|deva-śravas|deva-śrava|devaśravas|devaśrava|deva|śravas|śrava|deva-shravas|deva-shrava|devashravas|devashrava|shravas|shrava|_vei_120@1373|_vei1_pn377
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1372">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1374">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn376">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn377">1:377</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn378">↷</a><hr><p><b>Deva-śravas</b> is the name of a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a></b> prince who with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="देववात">devavāta</a></b> appears as a sacrificer on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृषद्वती">dṛṣadvatī</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आपया">āpayā</a></b> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201373"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201373">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201373"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201373">↑</a> iii. 23, 2. 3. In the Anukramaṇī he is called a son of <a class="vei_120a" href="यम">yama</a>, and has a hymn, x. 17, ascribed to him.</li></ol></div>

देवातिथि काण्व|దేవాతిథి కాణ్వ|ದೇವಾತಿಥಿ ಕಾಣ್ವ|devātithi kāṇva|devaatithi kaaNva|_vei_120@1374|_vei1_pn377
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1373">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1375">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn376">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn377">1:377</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn378">↷</a><hr><p><b>Devātithi Kāṇva</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a></b>’) is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201374"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201374">1</a></sup> as the seer of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> (chant) by which he turned pumpkins into cows for himself and his son when they were starving in the desert, whither they had been driven by rivals. He is also the reputed author of a hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201374"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201374">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201374"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201374">↑</a> ix. 2, 19.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201374"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201374">↑</a> viii. 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 61.</li></ol></div>

देवापि आर्ष्टिषेण|దేవాపి ఆర్ష్టిషేణ|ದೇವಾಪಿ ಆರ್ಷ್ಟಿಷೇಣ|devāpi ārṣṭiṣeṇa|devaapi aarShTiSheNa|_vei_120@1375|_vei1_pn377
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1374">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1376">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn376">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn377">1:377</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn378">↷</a><hr><p><b>Devāpi Ārṣṭiṣeṇa</b> (‘descendant of Ṛṣṭiṣeṇa’) is mentioned in a hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201375">1</a></sup> and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201375">2</a></sup> According to the latter source there were two brothers, <b>Devāpi</b> and <b>Śantanu,</b> princes of the Kurus. The elder was Devāpi, but Śantanu got himself anointed king, whereupon no rain fell for twelve years. The drought being attributed by the Brahmins to his having superseded his elder brother, Śantanu offered the kingdom to Devāpi. The latter, however, refused, but acting as <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, or domestic priest, for his brother, obtained rain. The Bṛhaddevatā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201375">3</a></sup> tells much the same tale, but adds that the reason for Devāpi's exclusion from the throne was the fact that he suffered from a skin disease. The Epic and later legends further  develop the story, presenting two somewhat discrepant accounts. According to the one version,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201375">4</a></sup> the ground of Devāpi's being passed over was leprosy, while in the other his devoting himself to asceticism in his youth was the cause of his brother's taking his place. The Epic,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201375">5</a></sup> moreover, treats him as a son of Pratīpa, and names as his brothers Bāhlīka<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201375">6</a></sup> and Āṛṣṭiṣeṇa,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201375">7</a></sup> who is a new figure developed from the patronymic of Devāpi Possibly Sieg<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201375">8</a></sup> is right in holding that two stories, those of Devāpi, Pratīpa's son, and of Devāpi, Ṛṣṭiṣeṇa's son, have been confused; but in any case it is impossible to extract history from them.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201375">9</a></sup></p><p>The Rigvedic hymn certainly appears to represent Devāpi as sacrificing for Śantanu, who seems to be called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="औलान">aulāna</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201375">10</a></sup> But there is no trace in it of the brotherhood of the two men, nor is there anything to show that Devāpi was not a Brahmin, but a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>. Sieg,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201375">11</a></sup> who interprets the hymn by the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, thinks that he was a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>, but on this occasion was enabled by the favour of <a class="vei_120a" href="बृहस्पति">bṛhaspati</a> to officiate as priest, and that the hymn shows clear recognition of the unusual character of his action; but this view seems very improbable.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201375">↑</a> x. 98.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201375">↑</a> ii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201375">↑</a> vii. 148 <i>et seq.,</i> with Macdonell's notes.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201375">↑</a> Mahābhārata, v. 5054 <i>et seq.</i> (= 149, 15 <i>et seq.</i>), where Śāntanu is (as also in the <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>, cclxxvii. 34, the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्म">brahma</a>, xiii. 114, 118, and the Viṣṇu Purāṇas) the form of the name; <a class="vei_120a" href="मत्स्य">matsya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a>, l, 39 <i>et seq.,</i> in which, as well as in the Bhāgavata, ix. 22, 12, 13, and the Vāyu <a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a>, xcix. 234, 237, the form is Śantanu.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201375">↑</a> Mahābhārata, i. 3751 (= 94, 62); ix. 2285 (= 40, 1); Vāyu <a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a>, ii. 37, 230, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201375">↑</a> Mahābhārata, cited in n. 4; Harivaṃśa, 1819.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201375">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> cited in n. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201375">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 136.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201375">↑</a> As does Pargiter, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1910, 52, 53.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201375">↑</a> Rv. x. 98, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201375">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 129-142.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 272 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 203; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 192 <i>et seq.;</i> Macdonell, <i>Bṛhaddevatā,</i> 1, xxix; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 131, 132.</li></ol></div>

देवृ|దేవృ|ದೇವೃ|devṛ|devR|devRi|devRu|devRRi|_vei_120@1376|_vei1_pn378
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1375">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1377">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn377">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn378">1:378</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn379">↷</a><hr><p><b>Devṛ</b> is a rare word denoting the wife's ‘brother-in-law’ (that is, the husband's brother). He is included with the sisters of the husband among those over whom the wife of the husband--his elder brother--rules;<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201376">1</a></sup> at the same time the wife is to be devoted to him,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201376">2</a></sup> and friendly to him.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201376">3</a></sup> After the death  of the husband the <a class="vei_120a" href="देवृ">devṛ</a> could perform the duty of begetting a son for him.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201376">4</a></sup> No word occurs for the wife's brother corresponding to <a class="vei_120a" href="देवृ">devṛ</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201376">↑</a> Rv. x. 85, 46. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201376">↑</a> Rv. x. 85, 44.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201376">↑</a> Av. xiv. 2, 18. <i>Cf.</i> xiv. 1, 39.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201376">↑</a> Rv. x. 40, 2. <i>Cf.</i> x. 18, 8; Kaegi, <i>Der Rigveda,</i> n. 51; Lanman, <i>Sanskrit Reader,</i> 385; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 948. <i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 516.</li></ol></div>

देश|దేశ|ದೇಶ|deśa|desha|_vei_120@1377|_vei1_pn379
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1376">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1378">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn378">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn379">1:379</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn380">↷</a><hr><p><b>Deśa,</b> ‘land,’ is a word that does not come into use till the time of the Upaniṣads and Sūtras,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201377">1</a></sup> excepting one occurrence in the latest period of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201377">2</a></sup> literature, and one in a much-discussed passage of the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201377">3</a></sup> where the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a></b> is mentioned as having five tributaries. This passage militates against the view that <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> was a name of the Indus, because the use of <a class="vei_120a" href="देश">deśa</a> here seems to indicate<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201377">4</a></sup> that the seer of the verse placed the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a> or ‘Middle Country,’ to which all the geographical data of the Yajurvedas point.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201377">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201377">↑</a> Where its use becomes common; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1, 16; 2, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 14, 6; Kātyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 4, 17, etc. So the adjective <i><a class="vei_120a" href="देशीय">deśīya</a>,</i> ‘belonging to a land,’ Kātyāyana, xxii. 4, 22; Lāṭyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 6, 28.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201377">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 10 (a late passage).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201377">↑</a> xxxiv. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201377">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 10, who thinks that the word crept into the text, where the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> originally meant the Indus, with the five tributaries of the Panjab.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201377">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 174.</li></ol></div>

देही|देहि|దేహీ|దేహి|ದೇಹೀ|ದೇಹಿ|dehī|dehi|dehii|dehee|_vei_120@1378|_vei1_pn379
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1377">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1379">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn378">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn379">1:379</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn380">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dehī</b> in two passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201378"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201378">1</a></sup> refers to defences thrown up against an enemy, apparently earthworks or dikes. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुर्">pur</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201378"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201378">↑</a> vi. 47, 2; vii. 6, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 344; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 143.</li></ol></div>

दैधिषव्य|దైధిషవ్య|ದೈಧಿಷವ್ಯ|daidhiṣavya|daidhiShavya|_vei_120@1379|_vei1_pn379
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1378">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1380">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn378">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn379">1:379</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn380">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daidhiṣavya</b> is mentioned in a <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> of the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201379"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201379">1</a></sup> Apparently the word (as derived from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दिधिषू">didhiṣū</a></b>) denotes the son of a younger sister married before the elder sister,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201379"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201379">2</a></sup> rather than the son of a woman twice married, the explanation of the St. Petersburg Dictionary. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201379"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201379">↑</a> iii. 2, 4, 4; Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 1, 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, 3, 5; 137, 37.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201379"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201379">↑</a> <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 17, 431, n.</li></ol></div>

दैयांपाति|దైయాంపాతి|ದೈಯಾಂಪಾತಿ|daiyāṃpāti|daiyaaMpaati|_vei_120@1380|_vei1_pn380
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1379">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1381">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn379">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn380">1:380</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn381">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daiyāṃpāti,</b> ‘descendant of Dayāṃpāta,’ is the name of a teacher of the east, who was instructed by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्डिल्यायन">śāṇḍilyāyana</a>,</b> according to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (ix. 5, 1, 14), in the lore of the construction of the fire-altar. The same patronymic is given, in the form of Dayyāṃpāti, to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्लक्ष">plakṣa</a>,</b> the contemporary of <b>Atyaṃhas</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 10, 9, 3-5).</p></div>

दैव|दैव|దైవ_౧|దైవ|ದೈವ_೧|ದೈವ|daiva_1|daiva|_vei_120@1381|_vei1_pn380
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1380">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1382">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn379">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn380">1:380</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn381">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Daiva</b> (masc.) appears in the list of sciences in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201381"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201381">1</a></sup> where Śaṅkara explains it as <i>utpātajñāna,</i> apparently the ‘knowledge of portents.’ The St. Petersburg Dictionary suggests that the word is here used adjectivally, and this view is followed by Little<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201381"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201381">2</a></sup> and by Bo7htlingk in his translation.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201381"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201381">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201381"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201381">↑</a> vii. 1, 2, 4; 2, 1; 7, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201381"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201381">↑</a> <i>Grammatical Index,</i> 83.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201381"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201381">↑</a> Though he does not render it (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दैव">daiva</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निधि">nidhi</a></i>).</li></ol></div>

दैव|दैव|దైవ_౨|దైవ|ದೈವ_೨|ದೈವ|daiva_2|daiva|_vei_120@1382|_vei1_pn380
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1381">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1383">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn379">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn380">1:380</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn381">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Daiva</b> is the patronymic of the mythical <a class="vei_120a" href="अथर्वन्">atharvan</a> in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201382"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201382">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201382"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201382">↑</a> ii. 5, 22; iv. 5, 28 (Mādhyaṃdina).</li></ol></div>

दैवल|దైవల|ದೈವಲ|daivala|_vei_120@1383|_vei1_pn380
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1382">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1384">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn379">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn380">1:380</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn381">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daivala,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="देवल">devala</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="असित">asita</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xiv. 11, 18).</p></div>

दैव-वात|दैववात|दैव|वात|దైవ-వాత|దైవవాత|దైవ|వాత|ದೈವ-ವಾತ|ದೈವವಾತ|ದೈವ|ವಾತ|daiva-vāta|daivavāta|daiva|vāta|daiva-vaata|daivavaata|vaata|_vei_120@1384|_vei1_pn380
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1383">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1385">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn379">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn380">1:380</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn381">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daiva-vāta,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="देववात">devavāta</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सृञ्जय">sṛñjaya</a>,</b> probably the <a class="vei_120a" href="सृञ्जय">sṛñjaya</a> king, in the Rigveda. He is mentioned<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201384">1</a></sup> as a devotee of the fire cult, and as victorious over the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a></b> king and the <b>Vṛcīvants.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201384">2</a></sup> According to Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201384">3</a></sup> his name was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अभ्यावर्तिन् चायमान">abhyāvartin cāyamāna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पार्थव">pārthava</a></b> (‘descendant of Pṛthu’), but Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201384">4</a></sup> recognizes this as doubtful, though he none the less places the Sṛñjayas to the west of the Indus with <b>Divodāsa.</b> What is more important is to note that the name suggests connexion with the <a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="देववात">devavāta</a>,</b> and as <b>Kurus</b> and Sṛñjayas were closely connected<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201384">5</a></sup> this is not immaterial. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201384">↑</a> Rv. iv. 15, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201384">↑</a> Rv. vi. 27, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201384">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 133, 134.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201384">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 105, 106.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201384">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 4, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 402, 405; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 153.</li></ol></div>

दैवाप|దైవాప|ದೈವಾಪ|daivāpa|daivaapa|_vei_120@1385|_vei1_pn381
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1384">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1386">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn380">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn381">1:381</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn382">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daivāpa,</b> ‘descendant of <b>Devāpi,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्रोत">indrota</a></b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201385"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201385">1</a></sup> and the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201385"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201385">2</a></sup> No connexion can be traced with the Devāpi of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201385"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201385">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201385"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201385">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201385"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201385">↑</a> iii. 40, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201385"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201385">↑</a> x. 98. See Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenlāndischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 240.</li></ol></div>

दैवावृध|దైవావృధ|ದೈವಾವೃಧ|daivāvṛdha|daivaavRdha|daivaavRidha|daivaavRudha|daivaavRRidha|_vei_120@1386|_vei1_pn381
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1385">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1387">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn380">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn381">1:381</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn382">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daivāvṛdha,</b> ‘descendant of Devāvṛdha,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बभ्रु">babhru</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vii. 34).</p></div>

दैवो-दासि|दैवोदासि|दैवो|दासि|దైవో-దాసి|దైవోదాసి|దైవో|దాసి|ದೈವೋ-ದಾಸಿ|ದೈವೋದಾಸಿ|ದೈವೋ|ದಾಸಿ|daivo-dāsi|daivodāsi|daivo|dāsi|daivo-daasi|daivodaasi|daasi|_vei_120@1387|_vei1_pn381
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1386">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1388">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn380">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn381">1:381</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn382">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daivo-dāsi,</b> ‘descendant of <b>Divodāsa,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतर्दन">pratardana</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201387"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201387">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201387"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201387">2</a></sup> It is impossible to ascertain whether the famous Divodāsa is meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201387"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201387">↑</a> xxvi. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201387"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201387">↑</a> iii. 1. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 214.</li></ol></div>

दोषा|దోషా|ದೋಷಾ|doṣā|doShaa|_vei_120@1388|_vei1_pn381
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1387">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1389">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn380">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn381">1:381</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn382">↷</a><hr><p><b>Doṣā,</b> ‘evening,’ is frequently referred to from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201388"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201388">1</a></sup> onwards,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201388"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201388">2</a></sup> usually as contrasted with <i>uṣas,</i> ‘dawn.’ In the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201388"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201388">3</a></sup> the word is contrasted with <i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रातर्">prātar</a>,</i> ‘early.’ See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अहन्">ahan</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201388"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201388">↑</a> i. 34, 3; 179, 1; ii. 8, 3; iv. 2, 8; v. 5, 6; 32, 11; vi. 5, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201388"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201388">↑</a> Av. vi. 1, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iv. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201388"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201388">↑</a> vi. 13, 1.</li></ol></div>

दोह|దోహ|ದೋಹ|doha|_vei_120@1389|_vei1_pn381
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1388">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1390">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn380">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn381">1:381</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn382">↷</a><hr><p><b>Doha,</b> ‘milking,’ is a common word in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201389">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201389">2</a></sup> Reference is made in the Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201389">3</a></sup> to the <i>sāyaṃ-<a class="vei_120a" href="दोह">doha</a>,</i> ‘evening milking,’ and the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रातर्">prātar</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="दोह">doha</a>,</i> ‘morning milking.’ Dohana has the same sense.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201389">4</a></sup> See also <b>Go.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201389">↑</a> iv. 11, 4. 9. 12; v. 17, 17; viii. 9, 15 (where five milkings are referred to metaphorically). In Rv. x. 42, 2, the literal sense is found.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201389">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, viii. 62; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 10, 2; ii. 2, 9, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201389">↑</a> Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 2, 38, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201389">↑</a> Rv. viii. 12, 32; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 2, 3, 30; Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 2, 37, etc.</li></ol></div>

दौरे-श्रवस्|दौरे-श्रव|दौरेश्रवस्|दौरेश्रव|दौरे|श्रवस्|श्रव|దౌరే-శ్రవస్|దౌరే-శ్రవ|దౌరేశ్రవస్|దౌరేశ్రవ|దౌరే|శ్రవస్|శ్రవ|ದೌರೇ-ಶ್ರವಸ್|ದೌರೇ-ಶ್ರವ|ದೌರೇಶ್ರವಸ್|ದೌರೇಶ್ರವ|ದೌರೇ|ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಶ್ರವ|daure-śravas|daure-śrava|daureśravas|daureśrava|daure|śravas|śrava|daure-shravas|daure-shrava|daureshravas|daureshrava|shravas|shrava|_vei_120@1390|_vei1_pn381
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1389">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1391">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn380">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn381">1:381</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn382">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daure-śravas,</b> ‘descendant of Dūreśravas,’ is the patronymic of the priest <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पृथुश्रवस्">pṛthuśravas</a>,</b> who officiated at the snake sacrifice described in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xxv. 15, 3). </p></div>

दौरे-श्रुत|दौरेश्रुत|दौरे|श्रुत|దౌరే-శ్రుత|దౌరేశ్రుత|దౌరే|శ్రుత|ದೌರೇ-ಶ್ರುತ|ದೌರೇಶ್ರುತ|ದೌರೇ|ಶ್ರುತ|daure-śruta|daureśruta|daure|śruta|daure-shruta|daureshruta|shruta|_vei_120@1391|_vei1_pn382
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1390">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1392">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn381">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn382">1:382</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn383">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daure-śruta,</b> ‘descendant of Dūreśruta,’ is the patronymic of the priest <b>Timirgha,</b> who officiated at the snake sacrifice described in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xxv. 15, 3).</p></div>

दौर्-गह|दौर्गह|दौर्|गह|దౌర్-గహ|దౌర్గహ|దౌర్|గహ|ದೌರ್-ಗಹ|ದೌರ್ಗಹ|ದೌರ್|ಗಹ|daur-gaha|daurgaha|daur|gaha|_vei_120@1392|_vei1_pn382
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1391">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1393">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn381">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn382">1:382</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn383">↷</a><hr><p><b>Daur-gaha.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दुर्गह">durgaha</a>.</b></p></div>

दौः-षन्ति|दौःषन्ति|दौः|षन्ति|దౌః-షన్తి|దౌఃషన్తి|దౌః|షన్తి|ದೌಃ-ಷನ್ತಿ|ದೌಃಷನ್ತಿ|ದೌಃ|ಷನ್ತಿ|dauḥ-ṣanti|dauḥṣanti|dauḥ|ṣanti|dauH-Shanti|dauHShanti|dauH|Shanti|_vei_120@1393|_vei1_pn382
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1392">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1394">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn381">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn382">1:382</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn383">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dauḥ-ṣanti</b> (‘descendant of Duḥṣanta’) is the patronymic of <a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> (viii. 23) and Śatapatha (xiii. 5, 4, 11) Brāhmaṇas.</p></div>

द्युतान मारुत|ద్యుతాన మారుత|ದ್ಯುತಾನ ಮಾರುತ|dyutāna māruta|dyutaana maaruta|_vei_120@1394|_vei1_pn382
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1393">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1395">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn381">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn382">1:382</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn383">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dyutāna Māruta</b> (‘descendant of the Maruts’) is the name of a divine being invoked in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201394"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201394">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201394"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201394">2</a></sup> and also mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201394"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201394">3</a></sup> In the Śatapatha<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201394"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201394">4</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> the name is explained to mean Vāyu, while in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201394"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201394">5</a></sup> he seems to be regarded as the author of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> (chant). He is treated as a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> by the Anukramaṇī, which credits him with the authorship of a hymn of the Rigveda (viii. 96).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201394"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201394">↑</a> v. 27.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201394"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201394">↑</a> v. 5, 9, 4. <i>Cf.</i> vi. 2, 10, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201394"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201394">↑</a> xv. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201394"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201394">↑</a> iii. 6, 1, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201394"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201394">↑</a> xvii. 1, 7. <i>Cf.</i> vi. 4, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 311; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 220.</li></ol></div>

द्युम्न|ద్యుమ్న|ದ್ಯುಮ್ನ|dyumna|_vei_120@1395|_vei1_pn382
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1394">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1396">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn381">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn382">1:382</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn383">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dyumna,</b> according to Pischel,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201395">1</a></sup> denotes ‘raft’ in one passage of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201395">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201395">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 35, 720 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201395">↑</a> viii. 19, 14.</li></ol></div>

द्यूत|ద్యూత|ದ್ಯೂತ|dyūta|dyuuta|dyoota|_vei_120@1396|_vei1_pn382
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1395">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1397">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn381">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn382">1:382</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn383">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dyūta,</b> ‘dicing,’ is mentioned in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201396"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201396">1</a></sup> and the Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201396"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201396">2</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201396"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201396">↑</a> xii. 3, 46.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201396"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201396">↑</a> Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 6, 2; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 10, 23, etc.</li></ol></div>

द्योतन|ద్యోతన|ದ್ಯೋತನ|dyotana|_vei_120@1397|_vei1_pn382
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1396">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1398">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn381">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn382">1:382</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn383">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dyotana</b> is, according to Sāyaṇa, the name of a prince in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201397"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201397">1</a></sup> This is probably correct, though the word may also<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201397"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201397">2</a></sup> be interpreted as denoting ‘glorification’ but it is not clear what relation existed between <a class="vei_120a" href="द्योतन">dyotana</a> and the other persons mentioned in the same passage, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वेतसु">vetasu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="दशोणि">daśoṇi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="तूतुजि">tūtuji</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुग्र">tugra</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201397"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201397">↑</a> vi. 20, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201397"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201397">↑</a> As by Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 1, 380.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 55, 328.</li></ol></div>

द्रप्स|ద్రప్స|ದ್ರಪ್ಸ|drapsa|_vei_120@1398|_vei1_pn383
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1397">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1399">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn382">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn383">1:383</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn384">↷</a><hr><p><b>Drapsa</b> is a common word from the Rigveda onwards<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201398">1</a></sup> for a ‘drop’: according to Sāyaṇa,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201398">2</a></sup> a ‘thick drop’ as opposed to <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्तोक">stoka</a>,</i> a ‘small drop.’ Hence there frequently occurs the expression <i>dadḥi-<a class="vei_120a" href="द्रप्स">drapsa</a>,</i>’ ‘drop of curds.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201398">3</a></sup> In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201398">4</a></sup> the word normally denotes the thick drops of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> or the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> itself.</p><p>In two passages<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201398">5</a></sup> Roth<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201398">6</a></sup> sees the sense of ‘banner,’ which is adopted by Oldenberg.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201398">7</a></sup> Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201398">8</a></sup> on the other hand, considers that ‘dust’ is meant, but this interpretation is not very probable. Max Müller<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201398">9</a></sup> renders the word ‘rain-drop’ in one of the passages.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201398">↑</a> Rv. i. 94, 11 (perhaps a ‘drop’ of fire); v. 63, 4 (‘rain-drop’); vii. 33, 11 (= <i><a class="vei_120a" href="रेतस्">retas</a></i>); Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 1, 2, 6; <i>drapsin</i> (‘thick-flowing’), xi. 4, 1, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201398">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, 1, p. 70, 7. <i>Cf.</i> the epithet <i><a class="vei_120a" href="उरु">uru</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="द्रप्स">drapsa</a>,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 3, 10, 2; Sāyaṇa, on Av. xviii. 4, 18, takes <i><a class="vei_120a" href="द्रप्स">drapsa</a></i> to mean ‘drops of curd’; so Agnisvāmin on Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 2, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201398">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 2, 3, 40.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201398">↑</a> ix. 78, 4; 85, 10; 89, 2; 97, 56; 108, 8; x. 11, 4; 17, 11. 12. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 3, 9. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201398">↑</a> iv. 13, 2, and <i>drapsin</i> in i. 64, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201398">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. <a class="vei_120a" href="सत्वन्">satvan</a>;</i> Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v. <a class="vei_120a" href="द्रप्स">drapsa</a>, drapsin.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201398">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 46, 357: <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 64, 65.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201398">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 57, 58; <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 88.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201398">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 104. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 80, with reference to Rv. i. 64, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> pp. 105, 113.</li></ol></div>

द्रापि|ద్రాపి|ದ್ರಾಪಿ|drāpi|draapi|_vei_120@1399|_vei1_pn383
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1398">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1400">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn382">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn383">1:383</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn384">↷</a><hr><p><b>Drāpi</b> occurs several times in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201399">1</a></sup> in the sense of ‘mantle’ or ‘cloak.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201399">2</a></sup> Sāyaṇa, however, renders the word by ‘coat of mail’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201399">3</a></sup> (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कवच">kavaca</a></i>). This seems needless, but none of the passages are very decisive one way or the other.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201399">↑</a> i. 25, 13; 116, 10; iv. 33, 2; ix. 86, 14; 100, 9; Av. iii. 13, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201399">↑</a> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 472; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 333.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201399">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 536; Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 201, 202.</li></ol></div>

द्रु|ద్రు|ದ್ರು|dru|_vei_120@1400|_vei1_pn383
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1399">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1401">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn382">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn383">1:383</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn384">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dru</b> denotes a vessel made of wood,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201400">1</a></sup> and in particular the vessel used at the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> sacrifice,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201400">2</a></sup> perhaps, as Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201400">3</a></sup> suggests, to catch the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> juice when running through the sieve. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201400">4</a></sup> the word simply means ‘wood.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201400">↑</a> Rv. i. 161, 1; v. 86, 3; viii. 66, 11; in x. 101, 10, the mortar seems meant. In v. 86, 3, Bo7htlingk takes it to denote a ‘wooden handle.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201400">↑</a> ix. 1, 2; 65, 6; 98, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201400">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 191, 192.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201400">↑</a> i. 3, 9, 1. So often in compounds, <i>e.g.,</i> Rv. ii. 7, 6; vi. 12, 4, etc.</li></ol></div>

द्रु-घण|द्रुघण|द्रु|घण|ద్రు-ఘణ|ద్రుఘణ|ద్రు|ఘణ|ದ್ರು-ಘಣ|ದ್ರುಘಣ|ದ್ರು|ಘಣ|dru-ghaṇa|drughaṇa|dru|ghaṇa|dru-ghaNa|drughaNa|ghaNa|_vei_120@1401|_vei1_pn384
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1400">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1402">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn383">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn384">1:384</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn385">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dru-ghaṇa</b> is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="मुद्गल">mudgala</a> hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201401"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201401">1</a></sup> and in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201401"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201401">2</a></sup> The sense is uncertain. <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201401"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201401">3</a></sup> renders it as a ‘<i><a class="vei_120a" href="घन">ghana</a></i> made of wood,’ probably, as Roth<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201401"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201401">4</a></sup> takes it, meaning a ‘club of wood.’ Geldner<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201401"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201401">5</a></sup> thinks that it was a wooden bull used by <a class="vei_120a" href="मुद्गल">mudgala</a> as a substitute for a second bull when he wanted to join in a race. But this interpretation of the legend is very improbable.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201401"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201401">6</a></sup> Whitney<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201401"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201401">7</a></sup> translates the word as ‘tree-smiter’ in the Atharvaveda, quoting Sāyaṇa, who explains it as a ‘cutting instrument,’ so called because trees are struck with it.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201401"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201401">↑</a> x. 101, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201401"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201401">↑</a> vii. 28, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201401"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201401">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ix. 23.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201401"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201401">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201401"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201401">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 3, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201401"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201401">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> von Bradke, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 46, 462; Bloomfield, <i>ibid.,</i> 48, 456; Franke, <i>Vienna Oriental Journal,</i> 8, 342.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201401"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201401">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 407.</li></ol></div>

द्रु-पद|द्रुपद|द्रु|पद|ద్రు-పద|ద్రుపద|ద్రు|పద|ದ್ರು-ಪದ|ದ್ರುಪದ|ದ್ರು|ಪದ|dru-pada|drupada|dru|pada|_vei_120@1402|_vei1_pn384
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1401">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1403">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn383">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn384">1:384</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn385">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dru-pada,</b> a ‘wooden pillar’ or ‘post,’ is several times referred to in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201402">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201402">2</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a> was bound to three posts for sacrifice.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201402">3</a></sup> Thieves, there is some evidence to show, were tied to posts as a penalty for stealing.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201402">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201402">↑</a> i. 24, 13; iv. 32, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201402">↑</a> Av. vi. 63, 3; 115, 2; xix. 47, 9; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xx. 20.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201402">↑</a> Rv. i. 24, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201402">↑</a> Av. xix. 47, 9; 50, 1. <i>Cf.</i> vi. 63, 3 = 84, 4. See Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 181, 182, and <a class="vei_120a" href="तस्कर">taskara</a>. n. 26.</li></ol></div>

द्रुम|ద్రుమ|ದ್ರುಮ|druma|_vei_120@1403|_vei1_pn384
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1402">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1404">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn383">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn384">1:384</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn385">↷</a><hr><p><b>Druma,</b> ‘tree,’ is not found until the later period in the Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (v. 11) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> (iv. 19; v. 26; ix. 23).</p></div>

द्रुवय|ద్రువయ|ದ್ರುವಯ|druvaya|_vei_120@1404|_vei1_pn384
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1403">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1405">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn383">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn384">1:384</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn385">↷</a><hr><p><b>Druvaya,</b> ‘wooden,’ is used in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201404"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201404">1</a></sup> as an epithet of the drum.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201404"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201404">↑</a> v. 20, 2. <i>Cf.</i> xi. 1, 12, where it is an epithet of <i>upaśvasa,</i> ‘blower,’ possibly ‘bellows’; but the manuscripts in part read <i>dhruvaye,</i> and the Paippalāda has <i>druye.</i></li></ol></div>

द्रु-हन्|द्रुहन्|द्रु|हन्|ద్రు-హన్|ద్రుహన్|ద్రు|హన్|ದ್ರು-ಹನ್|ದ್ರುಹನ್|ದ್ರು|ಹನ್|dru-han|druhan|dru|han|_vei_120@1405|_vei1_pn384
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1404">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1406">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn383">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn384">1:384</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn385">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dru-han,</b> ‘wood-cutter,’ seems to be meant by the word <i>druhantara</i> in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201405">1</a></sup> where it is usually taken as <i>druhaṃtara,</i> ‘fiend-overpowering.’ But as an epithet of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="परशु">paraśu</a>,</i> ‘axe,’ the other sense (‘mighty wood-cutter’)<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201405">2</a></sup> is more probable.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201405">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201405">↑</a> i. 127, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201405">↑</a> But if this explanation is correct, the word should have the accentuation of the comparative. See Oldenberg, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 46, 132.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201405">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 130.</li></ol></div>

द्रुह्यु|ద్రుహ్యు|ದ್ರುಹ್ಯು|druhyu|_vei_120@1406|_vei1_pn385
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1405">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1407">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn384">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn385">1:385</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn386">↷</a><hr><p><b>Druhyu</b> is the name of a people mentioned several times in the Rigveda. In one passage<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201406">1</a></sup> it occurs, in the plural, with the <b>Yadus, Turvaśas, Anus,</b> and <b>Pūrus,</b> suggesting that these are the famous five peoples of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201406">2</a></sup> Again, the <a class="vei_120a" href="द्रुह्यु">druhyu</a> king shared in the defeat of his allies by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>,</b> and appears to have perished in the waters.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201406">3</a></sup> In a second passage <a class="vei_120a" href="द्रुह्यु">druhyu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अनु">anu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a> are all mentioned in the singular,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201406">4</a></sup> while in another <a class="vei_120a" href="पूरु">pūru</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="द्रुह्यु">druhyu</a> occur.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201406">5</a></sup> From the tribal grouping it is probable that the Druhyus were a north-western people,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201406">6</a></sup> and the later tradition of the Epic connects <a class="vei_120a" href="गान्धार">gāndhāra</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="द्रुह्यु">druhyu</a>.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201406">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201406">↑</a> i. 108, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201406">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 122, 125; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 258 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201406">↑</a> vii. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201406">↑</a> viii. 10, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201406">↑</a> vi. 46, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201406">↑</a> Roth, <i>Zur Litteratur und Geschichte des Weda,</i> 131-133.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201406"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201406">↑</a> Pargiter, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1910, 49.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 205; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 140.</li></ol></div>

द्रोण|ద్రోణ|ದ್ರೋಣ|droṇa|droNa|_vei_120@1407|_vei1_pn385
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1406">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1408">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn384">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn385">1:385</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn386">↷</a><hr><p><b>Droṇa</b> denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201407"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201407">1</a></sup> a ‘wooden trough,’ and more specifically it designates in the plural vessels used for holding <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201407"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201407">2</a></sup> The great wooden reservoir for <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> is called a <a class="vei_120a" href="द्रोण">droṇa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कलश">kalaśa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201407"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201407">3</a></sup> The altar was sometimes made in the form of a <a class="vei_120a" href="द्रोण">droṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201407"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201407">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201407"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201407">↑</a> vi. 2, 8; 37, 2; 44, 20; ix. 93, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 26.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201407"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201407">↑</a> ix. 3, 1; 15, 7; 28, 4; 30, 4; 67, 14, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 280.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201407"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201407">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 2, 1, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 21; xix. 27; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 17, 32. Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 3, 17, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201407"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201407">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxi. 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 7, 2, 8.</li></ol></div>

द्रोणाहाव|ద్రోణాహావ|ದ್ರೋಣಾಹಾವ|droṇāhāva|droNaahaava|_vei_120@1408|_vei1_pn385
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1407">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1409">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn384">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn385">1:385</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn386">↷</a><hr><p><b>Droṇāhāva</b> is used as an epithet of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अवत">avata</a>,</b> ‘well,’ in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201408">1</a></sup> apparently in the sense of ‘having wooden buckets’ with reference to the drawing up of water.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201408">↑</a> x. 101, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 157.</li></ol></div>

द्वादश|ద్వాదశ|ದ್ವಾದಶ|dvādaśa|dvaadasha|_vei_120@1409|_vei1_pn385
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1408">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1410">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn384">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn385">1:385</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn386">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dvādaśa,</b> ‘consisting of twelve,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201409"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201409">1</a></sup> is used of the year in the Rigveda (vii. 103, 9). See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201409"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201409">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 645 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

द्वापर|ద్వాపర|ದ್ವಾಪರ|dvāpara|dvaapara|_vei_120@1410|_vei1_pn385
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1409">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1411">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn384">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn385">1:385</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn386">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dvāpara.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="युग">yuga</a>.</b> </p></div>

द्वार्|ద్వార్|ದ್ವಾರ್|dvār|dvaar|_vei_120@1411|_vei1_pn386
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1410">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1412">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn385">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn386">1:386</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn387">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dvār</b> is frequently used from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201411">1</a></sup> onwards<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201411">2</a></sup> to denote the ‘door’ of a house. The later form, <a class="vei_120a" href="द्वार">dvāra</a>, has the same sense.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201411">3</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गृह">gṛha</a>.</b> The ‘door-fastener’ is called Dvārapidhāna in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201411">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201411">↑</a> i. 13, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201411">↑</a> Av. viii. 3, 22; xiv. 1, 63; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 10; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 1, 1, 2; xiv. 3, 1, 13, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201411">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 1, 19; iv. 3, 5, 9; 6, 7, 9; xi. 4, 4, 2, etc. Av. x. 8, 43, has <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नव">nava</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="द्वार">dvāra</a>,</i> ‘having nine openings,’ of the body.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201411">↑</a> xi. 1, 1, 1. <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="द्वार">dvāra</a>-bāhū,</i> ‘doorposts,’ in Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 3, 1; ii. 3, 9.</li></ol></div>

द्वार-प|द्वारप|द्वार|ద్వార-ప|ద్వారప|ద్వార|ದ್ವಾರ-ಪ|ದ್ವಾರಪ|ದ್ವಾರ|dvāra-pa|dvārapa|dvāra|dvaara-pa|dvaarapa|dvaara|_vei_120@1412|_vei1_pn386
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1411">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1413">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn385">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn386">1:386</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn387">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dvāra-pa,</b> ‘door-keeper,’ is only found in a metaphorical sense in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 30), where Viṣṇu is called the ‘doorkeeper’ of the gods, and in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iii. 13, 6).</p></div>

द्वि-गत् भार्गव|द्विगत् भार्गव|द्वि|गत् भार्गव|ద్వి-గత్ భార్గవ|ద్విగత్ భార్గవ|ద్వి|గత్ భార్గవ|ದ್ವಿ-ಗತ್ ಭಾರ್ಗವ|ದ್ವಿಗತ್ ಭಾರ್ಗವ|ದ್ವಿ|ಗತ್ ಭಾರ್ಗವ|dvi-gat bhārgava|dvigat bhārgava|dvi|gat bhārgava|dvi-gat bhaargava|dvigat bhaargava|gat bhaargava|_vei_120@1413|_vei1_pn386
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1412">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1414">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn385">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn386">1:386</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn387">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dvi-gat Bhārgava</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भृगु">bhṛgu</a></b>’) is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xiv. 9) as the seer of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> or Chant, by means of which he twice went to the heavenly world.</p></div>

द्वि-ज|द्विज|द्वि|ద్వి-జ|ద్విజ|ద్వి|ದ್ವಿ-ಜ|ದ್ವಿಜ|ದ್ವಿ|dvi-ja|dvija|dvi|_vei_120@1414|_vei1_pn386
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1413">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1415">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn385">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn386">1:386</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn387">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dvi-ja,</b> ‘twice-born,’ as an epithet of the Āryans generally, or of the Brahmins in particular, is not found in Vedic literature except in a quite obscure verse of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201414"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201414">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201414"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201414">↑</a> xix. 71, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 1008; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 204. Neither <i>dvijanman</i> nor <i><a class="vei_120a" href="द्वि">dvi</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="जाति">jāti</a></i> occurs early, and the idea is not in this form an early one.</li></ol></div>

द्वि-पाद्|द्विपाद्|द्वि|पाद्|ద్వి-పాద్|ద్విపాద్|ద్వి|పాద్|ದ್ವಿ-ಪಾದ್|ದ್ವಿಪಾದ್|ದ್ವಿ|ಪಾದ್|dvi-pād|dvipād|dvi|pād|dvi-paad|dvipaad|paad|_vei_120@1415|_vei1_pn386
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1414">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1416">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn385">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn386">1:386</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn387">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dvi-pād,</b> ‘two-footed,’ ‘biped,’ denotes man, as opposed to quadrupeds, from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201415">1</a></sup> onwards.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201415">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201415">↑</a> i. 49, 3; iii. 62, 14; viii. 27, 12; x. 97, 20; 117, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201415">↑</a> Av. ii. 34, 1; x. 1, 24; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, viii. 30; ix. 31; xiii. 17; xiv. 8, etc.</li></ol></div>

द्वि-बन्धु|द्विबन्धु|द्वि|बन्धु|ద్వి-బన్ధు|ద్విబన్ధు|ద్వి|బన్ధు|ದ್ವಿ-ಬನ್ಧು|ದ್ವಿಬನ್ಧು|ದ್ವಿ|ಬನ್ಧು|dvi-bandhu|dvibandhu|dvi|bandhu|_vei_120@1416|_vei1_pn386
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1415">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1417">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn385">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn386">1:386</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn387">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dvi-bandhu</b> is in an obscure hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201416"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201416">1</a></sup> according to Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201416"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201416">2</a></sup> and Grassmann,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201416"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201416">3</a></sup> the name of a man, while Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201416"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201416">4</a></sup> renders it as a simple adjective meaning ‘of double kinship.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201416"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201416">↑</a> x. 61, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201416"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201416">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201416"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201416">↑</a> <i>Wo7rterbuch, s.v.,</i> and Translation of the Rigveda, 2, 475.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201416"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201416">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 2, 643, and 5, 526.</li></ol></div>

द्वि-राज|द्विराज|द्वि|राज|ద్వి-రాజ|ద్విరాజ|ద్వి|రాజ|ದ್ವಿ-ರಾಜ|ದ್ವಿರಾಜ|ದ್ವಿ|ರಾಜ|dvi-rāja|dvirāja|dvi|rāja|dvi-raaja|dviraaja|raaja|_vei_120@1417|_vei1_pn387
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1416">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1418">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn386">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn387">1:387</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn388">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dvi-rāja</b> (neut,), ‘conflict between two kings,’ or ‘battle,’ is mentioned in the Atharvaveda (v. 20, 9). <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दाशराज्ञ">dāśarājña</a>.</b></p></div>

द्वि-रेतस्|द्वि-रेत|द्विरेतस्|द्विरेत|द्वि|रेतस्|रेत|ద్వి-రేతస్|ద్వి-రేత|ద్విరేతస్|ద్విరేత|ద్వి|రేతస్|రేత|ದ್ವಿ-ರೇತಸ್|ದ್ವಿ-ರೇತ|ದ್ವಿರೇತಸ್|ದ್ವಿರೇತ|ದ್ವಿ|ರೇತಸ್|ರೇತ|dvi-retas|dvi-reta|dviretas|dvireta|dvi|retas|reta|retas|reta|_vei_120@1418|_vei1_pn387
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1417">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1419">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn386">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn387">1:387</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn388">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dvi-retas,</b> ‘having double seed,’ is an epithet of both the ass<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201418"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201418">1</a></sup> and the mare.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201418"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201418">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201418"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201418">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 9; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 3, 1, 23. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गर्दभ">gardabha</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201418"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201418">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 1, 4.</li></ol></div>

द्वीप|ద్వీప|ದ್ವೀಪ|dvīpa|dviipa|dveepa|_vei_120@1419|_vei1_pn387
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1418">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1420">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn386">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn387">1:387</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn388">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dvīpa,</b> ‘island,’ is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201419"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201419">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201419"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201419">2</a></sup> But there is no reason to imagine that the islands referred to were other than sandbanks in the great rivers, Indus or Ganges.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201419"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201419">3</a></sup> Vedic literature knows nothing of the system of geography according to which the earth consists of four, seven, or thirteen Dvīpas grouped round Mount <a class="vei_120a" href="मेरु">meru</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201419"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201419">↑</a> i. 169, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201419"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201419">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xiii. 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 2, 1, 3; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> Sütra, i. 6, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201419"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201419">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 256.</li></ol></div>

द्वीपिन्|ద్వీపిన్|ದ್ವೀಪಿನ್|dvīpin|dviipin|dveepin|_vei_120@1420|_vei1_pn387
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1419">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1421">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn386">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn387">1:387</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn388">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dvīpin,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201420"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201420">1</a></sup> ‘panther’ or ‘leopard,’ is mentioned in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201420"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201420">2</a></sup> and the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201420"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201420">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201420"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201420">↑</a> Lit., ‘insulated’--<i>i.e.,</i> ‘spotted.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201420"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201420">↑</a> iv. 8, 7; vi. 38, 2; xix. 49, 4, in every case associated with the tiger.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201420"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201420">↑</a> ii. 1. 9.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 80.</li></ol></div>

द्वैत-वन|द्वैतवन|द्वैत|वन|ద్వైత-వన|ద్వైతవన|ద్వైత|వన|ದ್ವೈತ-ವನ|ದ್ವೈತವನ|ದ್ವೈತ|ವನ|dvaita-vana|dvaitavana|dvaita|vana|_vei_120@1421|_vei1_pn387
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1420">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1422">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn386">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn387">1:387</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn388">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dvaita-vana,</b> ‘descendant of Dvitavana,’ is the patronymic of <b>Dhvasan,</b> the king of the <b>Matsyas,</b> whose <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or ‘horse sacrifice,’ is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 5, 4, 9).</p></div>

द्व्य्-ओपश|द्व्योपश|द्व्य्|ओपश|ద్వ్య్-ఓపశ|ద్వ్యోపశ|ద్వ్య్|ఓపశ|ದ್ವ್ಯ್-ಓಪಶ|ದ್ವ್ಯೋಪಶ|ದ್ವ್ಯ್|ಓಪಶ|dvy-opaśa|dvyopaśa|dvy|opaśa|dvy-opasha|dvyopasha|opasha|_vei_120@1422|_vei1_pn387
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1421">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1423">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn386">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn387">1:387</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn388">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dvy-opaśa.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ओपश">opaśa</a>.</b> </p></div>

धन|ధన|ಧನ|dhana|_vei_120@1423|_vei1_pn388
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1422">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1424">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn387">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn388">1:388</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn389">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhana,</b> ‘prize,’ is often found in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201423">1</a></sup> probably the prize in racing rather than the ‘booty’ in battle. It also denotes<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201423">2</a></sup> the ‘stake’ at dicing. In some passages it possibly means the ‘contest’ itself.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201423">3</a></sup> More generally it denotes ‘wealth’ or ‘gift.’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201423">4</a></sup> But it sometimes expresses ‘booty,’<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201423">5</a></sup> probably from the notion of ‘wealth’ rather than of ‘prize.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201423">↑</a> Rv. i. 81, 3; vi. 45, 2; viii. 80, 8; ix. 53, 2; 109, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 120; Pischel, <i>ibid.,</i> 1, 171.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201423">↑</a> Rv. x. 34, 10; Av. iv. 38, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201423">↑</a> Rv. i. 31, 6; v. 35, 7; vii. 38, 8; viii. 5, 26; 8, 21; 49, 9; 50, 9; x. 48, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201423">↑</a> Rv. i. 42, 6; x. 18, 2; 84, 7; Av. i. 15, 3; ii. 7, 4; iii. 15, 2; v. 19, 9; vi. 81, 1; vii. 81, 4; viii. 5, 16, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201423">↑</a> Rv. i. 74, 3; 157, 2, etc.</li></ol></div>

धन-धानी|धन-धानि|धनधानी|धनधानि|धन|धानी|धानि|ధన-ధానీ|ధన-ధాని|ధనధానీ|ధనధాని|ధన|ధానీ|ధాని|ಧನ-ಧಾನೀ|ಧನ-ಧಾನಿ|ಧನಧಾನೀ|ಧನಧಾನಿ|ಧನ|ಧಾನೀ|ಧಾನಿ|dhana-dhānī|dhana-dhāni|dhanadhānī|dhanadhāni|dhana|dhānī|dhāni|dhana-dhaanii|dhana-dhaani|dhanadhaanii|dhanadhaani|dhaanii|dhaani|dhana-dhaanee|dhanadhaanee|dhaanee|_vei_120@1424|_vei1_pn388
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1423">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1425">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn387">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn388">1:388</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn389">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhana-dhānī,</b> a ‘treasure house,’ is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka (x. 67).</p></div>

धनिष्ठा|ధనిష్ఠా|ಧನಿಷ್ಠಾ|dhaniṣṭhā|dhaniShThaa|_vei_120@1425|_vei1_pn388
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1424">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1426">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn387">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn388">1:388</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn389">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhaniṣṭhā</b> (‘very rich’), used in the plural, is the later name<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201425"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201425">1</a></sup> of the lunar mansion <b>(<a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>) <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रविष्ठा">śraviṣṭhā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201425"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201425">↑</a> Sāntikalpa, 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 26.</li></ol></div>

धनु|ధను|ಧನು|dhanu|_vei_120@1426|_vei1_pn388
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1425">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1427">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn387">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn388">1:388</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn389">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhanu</b> (fem.), ‘sandbank,’ occurs several times in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201426">1</a></sup> but only metaphorically of the clouds in the atmosphere. Dhanū is found in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201426">2</a></sup> where it seems to denote a sandbag used to prevent bleeding.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201426">3</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धन्वन्">dhanvan</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201426">↑</a> i. 33, 4; 144, 5; viii. 3, 19; x. 4, 3; 27, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201426">↑</a> i. 17, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201426">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 411; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 18; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 259, 260.</li></ol></div>

धनुस्|धनु|ధనుస్|ధను|ಧನುಸ್|ಧನು|dhanus|dhanu|_vei_120@1427|_vei1_pn388
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1426">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1428">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn387">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn388">1:388</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn389">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhanus,</b> the ‘bow,’ frequently mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201427">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201427">2</a></sup> was the chief weapon of the Vedic Indian.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201427">3</a></sup> The last act of the funeral rite included the removal of the bow from  the right hand of the dead man.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201427">4</a></sup> The weapon was composed of a stout staff bent into a curved shape (<i>vakra</i>),<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201427">5</a></sup> and of a bowstring (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="ज्या">jyā</a></b>) made of a strip of cowhide<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201427">6</a></sup> which joined the ends. The tips of the bow, when the string was fastened, were called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आर्त्नी">ārtnī</a>.</b> Relaxed when not in actual use, the bow was specially strung up when needed for shooting.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201427">7</a></sup> The stages of the process are given in detail in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā:<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201427">8</a></sup> the stringing (<i>ā-tan</i>) of the bow, the placing (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रति-धा">prati-dhā</a></i>) of the arrow, the bending (<i>ā-yam</i>) of the bow, and the shooting (<i>as</i>). The arrow was discharged from the ear,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201427">9</a></sup> and is hence called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कर्ण">karṇa</a>-yoni,</i><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201427">10</a></sup> ‘having the ear as its point of origin.’<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201427">10</a></sup> The making of bows was a regular profession (<i>dhanuṣ-<a class="vei_120a" href="कार">kāra</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201427">11</a></sup> <i>dhanuṣ-<a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्">kṛt</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201427">12</a></sup> For the arrow see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इषु">iṣu</a>,</b> and for the handguard <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हस्तघ्न">hastaghna</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201427">↑</a> viii. 72, 4; 77, 11; ix. 99, 1; x. 18, 9; 125, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201427">↑</a> Av. iv. 4, 6; 6, 6; v. 18, 8; vii. 50. 9; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 5, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 14; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 4, 6; v. 3, 1, 11, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201427">↑</a> Rv. vi. 75, 2. Practically no other weapon plays any substantial part in Vedic warfare.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201427">↑</a> Rv. x. 18, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201427">↑</a> Av. iv. 6, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201427">↑</a> Rv. vi. 75, 11; Av. i. 2, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201427">↑</a> Rv. x. 166, 3; Av. vi. 42, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201427">↑</a> xvi. 22.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201427">↑</a> Rv. vi. 75, 2 <i>et seq.</i> So also in the Epic, Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 271. The Homeric method is to draw to the breast--<i>e.g., Iliad,</i> iv. 123.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201427">↑</a> Rv. ii. 24, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201427">↑</a> Rv. ii. 24, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201427">↑</a> xxx. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201427">↑</a> xvi. 46.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 298, 299; Hopkins, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 13, 270 <i>et seq.</i> The Epic bow is about 5(1/2) feet, and the arrow 3 feet in length.</li></ol></div>

धन्वन्|धन्वन्|ధన్వన్_౧|ధన్వన్|ಧನ್ವನ್_೧|ಧನ್ವನ್|dhanvan_1|dhanvan|_vei_120@1428|_vei1_pn389
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1427">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1429">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn388">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn389">1:389</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn390">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Dhanvan,</b> ‘bow,’ is found frequently in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201428">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201428">2</a></sup> It also occurs in the compounds <i><a class="vei_120a" href="इषु">iṣu</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="धन्व">dhanva</a>,</i> ‘bow and arrow,’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201428">3</a></sup> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="आज्य">ājya</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="धन्व">dhanva</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201428">4</a></sup> ‘having clarified butter for its bow,’<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201428">5</a></sup> <i>adhijya-<a class="vei_120a" href="धन्व">dhanva</a>,</i> ‘bow with string fixed,’ etc. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धनुस्">dhanus</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201428">↑</a> ii. 24, 8; 33, 10; vi. 59, 7; 75, 2; viii. 20, 2; ix. 69, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ix. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201428">↑</a> Av. i. 3, 9; iv. 4, 7; xi. 9, 1, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201428">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 19; <i>iṣudhanvin,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201428">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 25.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201428">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 1, 1, 6.</li></ol></div>

धन्वन्|धन्वन्|ధన్వన్_౨|ధన్వన్|ಧನ್ವನ್_೨|ಧನ್ವನ್|dhanvan_2|dhanvan|_vei_120@1429|_vei1_pn389
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1428">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1430">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn388">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn389">1:389</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn390">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Dhanvan,</b> ‘desert,’ is repeatedly mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201429"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201429">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201429"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201429">2</a></sup> Death from thirst in the desert was not rare,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201429"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201429">3</a></sup> and  the value of a spring in the desert was fully appreciated.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201429"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201429">4</a></sup> The great desert east of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धु">sindhu</a></b> (Indus) and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुतुद्री">śutudrī</a></b> (Sutlej) is possibly referred to in one hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201429"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201429">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201429"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201429">↑</a> ii. 38, 7; iii. 45, 1; iv. 17, 2; 19, 7; 33, 7; v. 53, 6; 83, 10, etc. In i. 116, 4, the strand of the ocean (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="समुद्र">samudra</a></b>) is mentioned.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201429"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201429">↑</a> Av. v. 13, 1; vi. 100, 1; vii. 41, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201429"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201429">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 19.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201429"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201429">↑</a> Rv. x. 4, 1. <i>Cf.</i> vi. 34, 4, etc.; Av. i. 6, 4; xix. 2, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201429"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201429">↑</a> x. 86, 20.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 47, 48.</li></ol></div>

धमनि|ధమని|ಧಮನಿ|dhamani|_vei_120@1430|_vei1_pn390
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1429">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1431">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn389">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn390">1:390</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn397">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhamani,</b> ‘reed,’ appears to denote ‘pipe’ in a passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201430">1</a></sup> and in a citation appearing in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201430">2</a></sup> In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201430">3</a></sup> it denotes, perhaps, ‘artery’ or ‘vein,’ or more generally ‘intestinal channel,’ being coupled in some passages<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201430">4</a></sup> with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरा">hirā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201430">↑</a> ii. 11, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201430">↑</a> vi. 24.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201430">↑</a> i. 17, 23; ii. 33, 6; vi. 90, 2; vii. 35, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 19, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201430">↑</a> i. 17, 3; vii. 35, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 259, 546.</li></ol></div>

धरुण|ధరుణ|ಧರುಣ|dharuṇa|dharuNa|_vei_120@1431|_vei1_pn390
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1430">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1432">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn389">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn390">1:390</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn397">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dharuṇa</b> in one passage of the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā (viii. 51) denotes a ‘sucking calf.’</p></div>

धर्म|ధర్మ|ಧರ್ಮ|dharma|_vei_120@1432|_vei1_pn390
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1431">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1433">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn389">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn390">1:390</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn397">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dharma,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201432">1</a></sup> <b>Dharman,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201432">2</a></sup> are the regular words, the latter in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1201432">1b</a></sup> and both later,<sup id="ref_120_2b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2b_vei_1201432">2b</a></sup> for ‘law’ or ‘custom.’ But there is very little evidence in the early literature as to the administration of justice or the code of law followed. On the other hand, the <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_3b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3b_vei_1201432">3b</a></sup> contain full particulars.</p><p>(1) <i>Criminal Law.</i>--The crimes recognized in Vedic literature vary greatly in importance, while there is no distinction adopted in principle between real crimes and what now are regarded as fanciful bodily defects or infringements of merely  conventional practices.<sup id="ref_120_4b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4b_vei_1201432">4b</a></sup> The crimes enumerated include the slaying of an embryo (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="भ्रूण">bhrūṇa</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_5b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5b_vei_1201432">5b</a></sup> the slaying of a man (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वीर">vīra</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_6b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6b_vei_1201432">6b</a></sup> and  the slaying of a Brahmin,<sup id="ref_120_7b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7b_vei_1201432">7b</a></sup> a much more serious crime. Treachery is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_8b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8b_vei_1201432">8b</a></sup> as being punishable by death, as it was punished later.<sup id="ref_120_9b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9b_vei_1201432">9b</a></sup> But there is no trace of an organized criminal justice vested either in the king or in the people. There still seems to have prevailed the system of wergeld (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैर">vaira</a></b>), which indicates that criminal justice remained in the hands of those who were wronged. In the Sūtras,<sup id="ref_120_10b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10b_vei_1201432">10b</a></sup> on the other hand, the king's peace is recognized as infringed by crimes, a penalty being paid to him, or, according to the Brahminical textbooks, to the Brahmins. It may there fore reasonably be conjectured that the royal power of jurisdiction steadily increased; the references in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> to the king as wielding punishment (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="दण्ड">daṇḍa</a></b>) confirm this supposition. Whether, as the analogy of other systems suggests, the king was assisted in his judicial duties, as he undoubtedly was later, by assessors, presumably of the Brahmin caste, cannot be made out clearly.<sup id="ref_120_11b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11b_vei_1201432">11b</a></sup></p><p>The procedure adopted in deciding cases is quite uncertain. In the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_12b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12b_vei_1201432">12b</a></sup> the ordeal of the red-hot axe is mentioned as applied in an accusation of theft. It must apparently be understood to have been inflicted by the direction of the king. But no other judicial ordeal is known to Vedic literature (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दिव्य">divya</a></b>). The punishment of theft was in some cases at least death, probably when the thief was taken redhanded;<sup id="ref_120_13b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13b_vei_1201432">13b</a></sup> in other cases binding to posts was the penalty,<sup id="ref_120_14b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14b_vei_1201432">14b</a></sup> presumably accompanied by the return of the stolen goods. In the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_15b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15b_vei_1201432">15b</a></sup> the list of sins given as apparently equal in wickedness is stealing gold, drinking spirits, defiling a Guru's bed, and the murder of a Brahmin.</p><p>(2) <i>Civil Law.</i>--There is little recorded as to civil law in Vedic literature. The relations of the family and the question of family property are dealt with under <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उर्वरा">urvarā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षेत्र">kṣetra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>;</b> succession and partition of property are treated under <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दाय">dāya</a>.</b> As regards the transfer of chattels--for land as transferable <i>inter vivos</i> is hardly yet recognized, save exceptionally as a sacrificial fee (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="दक्षिणा">dakṣiṇā</a></b>), and then disapproved<sup id="ref_120_16b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16b_vei_1201432">16b</a></sup> --the recognized modes are gift (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="दान">dāna</a></b>) and barter or sale (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रय">kraya</a></b>), which includes exchange. Original acquisition of land was no doubt brought  about by occupation and apportionment among the tribesmen,<sup id="ref_120_17b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17b_vei_1201432">17b</a></sup> while chattels were acquired by taking possession of them, provided that they were found on one's own land or on unoccupied land, and did not belong to any other person originally. The Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_18b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18b_vei_1201432">18b</a></sup> contain rules for the disposal of lost property, which tend to give it to the king, with the deduction of a percentage for the finder, unless the latter is a Brahmin, who keeps it all. As for contract, save in regard to moneylending (for which see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋण">ṛṇa</a></b>), practically nothing is to be gathered from Vedic literature, doubtless because of the primitive conditions prevailing in that early period. Much of the labour, which would in a more developed society have been done by workers for hire, would be performed by slaves (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a></b>), while the technical workers of the village--of whom long lists are given in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_19b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19b_vei_1201432">19b</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_20b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20b_vei_1201432">20b</a></sup> --may have been recompensed not by any sum based on each piece of work done, but by fixed allowances, much as the village servants are in modern times.<sup id="ref_120_21b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21b_vei_1201432">21b</a></sup> But this must remain a matter of conjecture, and it is uncertain what exact status the carpenter or smith held in the village. Similarly it is impossible to trace in the early literature any legal theory or practice as to torts, but rules as to penalties for insults appear in the Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_22b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22b_vei_1201432">22b</a></sup></p><p>Very little is recorded as to procedure. The list<sup id="ref_120_23b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23b_vei_1201432">23b</a></sup> of victims at the Puruṣamedha, or ‘horse sacrifice,’ includes a <i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रश्निन्">praśnin</a>,</i> an <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अभि-प्रश्निन्">abhi-praśnin</a>,</i> and a <i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रश्न">praśna</a>-vivāka,</i> in whom it is not unreasonable to see the plaintiff, the defendant, and the arbitrator or judge: the terms may refer to what is probably an early  form of judicial procedure, a voluntary arbitration. The same idea may be conveyed by the word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यम-शी">madhyama-śī</a>,</i> ‘lying in the midst,’ which occurs in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_24b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24b_vei_1201432">24b</a></sup> and which Roth,<sup id="ref_120_25b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25b_vei_1201432">25b</a></sup> followed by Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_26b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26b_vei_1201432">26b</a></sup> understood to mean an arbitrator or judge, the expression being derived from the judge acting with other judicial persons,<sup id="ref_120_27b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27b_vei_1201432">27b</a></sup> and being surrounded by the assembly of the people. But this interpretation is uncertain; Whitney<sup id="ref_120_28b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28b_vei_1201432">28b</a></sup> thinks that the word merely alludes to a chief round whom his men encamp. The king is later the chief civil judge, and may presumably have been so earlier, no doubt in conjunction with the elders of the tribe, but for this we are reduced to conjecture.<sup id="ref_120_29b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29b_vei_1201432">29b</a></sup></p><p>The use of witnesses as evidence is uncertain (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ज्ञातृ">jñātṛ</a></b>), and the ordeal is not recorded as deciding any civil matter except the dispute between <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वत्स">vatsa</a></b> and his rival as to the true Brahminical descent of the former, which was settled by his walking unharmed through the flame of a fire.<sup id="ref_120_30b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_30b_vei_1201432">30b</a></sup> But it is probable on analogy that the ordeal may have been used for the purpose of deciding disputes. Whether the oath was so used cannot be certainly shown. It appears, however, that a Brahmin was preferred in legal matters to a non-Brahmin.<sup id="ref_120_31b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_31b_vei_1201432">31b</a></sup></p><p>There are very few references to police officials: no doubt the king employed some of his dependents to execute sentences and arrest offenders (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उग्र">ugra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="जीवगृभ्">jīvagṛbh</a></b>).</p><p>(3) <i>Morality.</i>--It is convenient to notice under this head  several points bearing on the moral condition of the people: (<i>a</i>) the exposure of children; (<i>b</i>) the exposure of the aged; (<i>c</i>) prostitution; (<i>d</i>) adultery; (<i>e</i>) incest.</p><p>(<i>a</i>) The exposure of girl infants is asserted by Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_32b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_32b_vei_1201432">32b</a></sup> on the strength of a passage in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_33b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_33b_vei_1201432">33b</a></sup> but it seems clear that the passage has been misunderstood,<sup id="ref_120_34b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_34b_vei_1201432">34b</a></sup> and that it refers merely to laying the child aside, not exposing it, while a boy was lifted up. It is, however, true that the birth of a girl was not at all popular, not an unnatural sentiment in an early society, and paralleled among other Āryan peoples.<sup id="ref_120_35b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_35b_vei_1201432">35b</a></sup></p><p>(<i>b</i>) The exposure of the aged is also inferred by Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_36b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_36b_vei_1201432">36b</a></sup> from a passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_37b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_37b_vei_1201432">37b</a></sup> and from the mention of persons exposed (<i>ud-hitāḥ</i>) in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_38b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_38b_vei_1201432">38b</a></sup> The latter passage may well refer merely to the bodies being exposed after death to the elements (as is done by the Parsis). The former passage merely refers to the individual case of some person who may have been cast out, and proves absolutely nothing as to a habitual or recognized custom, nor can such a custom be inferred from, <i>e.g.,</i> the legend of <b>Cyavāna.</b></p><p>(<i>c</i>) That prostitution existed in Rigvedic times is certain, but its extent is disputed. Brotherless girls were frequently reduced to becoming prostitutes;<sup id="ref_120_39b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_39b_vei_1201432">39b</a></sup> the putting away of an illegitimate child is referred to in the Rigveda;<sup id="ref_120_40b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_40b_vei_1201432">40b</a></sup> besides the  terms <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पुंश्चली">puṃścalī</a></i><sup id="ref_120_41b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_41b_vei_1201432">41b</a></sup> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="महानग्नी">mahānagnī</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_42b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_42b_vei_1201432">42b</a></sup> which undoubtedly mean ‘harlot,’ there are other clear references to prostitution;<sup id="ref_120_43b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_43b_vei_1201432">43b</a></sup> and expressions like <i>kumārī-<a class="vei_120a" href="पुत्र">putra</a>,</i> ‘son of a maiden,’<sup id="ref_120_44b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_44b_vei_1201432">44b</a></sup> and the ‘son of an unmarried girl’ (<i>agrū</i>), spoken of in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_45b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_45b_vei_1201432">45b</a></sup> as exposed and attacked by animals, point in the same direction. The Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_46b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_46b_vei_1201432">46b</a></sup> seems to recognize prostitution as a profession. Pischel<sup id="ref_120_47b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_47b_vei_1201432">47b</a></sup> sees many references to Hetairai in the Rigveda, which as Geldner<sup id="ref_120_48b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_48b_vei_1201432">48b</a></sup> insists reflects in its imagery the court life of Indian princes. But the correctness of the view of these two scholars on this point is not by any means certain.<sup id="ref_120_49b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_49b_vei_1201432">49b</a></sup></p><p>(<i>d</i>) Adultery was generally regarded among Āryan peoples as a serious offence against the husband of the woman affected. We accordingly find in the legal literature of India traces of the rule that an adulterer can be slain with impunity if taken in the act.<sup id="ref_120_50b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_50b_vei_1201432">50b</a></sup> Weber,<sup id="ref_120_51b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_51b_vei_1201432">51b</a></sup> however, has adduced some material indicating an indifference to these matters in Vedic times, and Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_52b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_52b_vei_1201432">52b</a></sup> has adopted the same view. But, as Delbrück<sup id="ref_120_53b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_53b_vei_1201432">53b</a></sup> has clearly shown, the evidence is not convincing; the cited prescriptions<sup id="ref_120_54b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_54b_vei_1201432">54b</a></sup> forbidding connexion with another man's wife during a certain rite do not imply that such connexion would otherwise be allowed: the ritual of the <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a>-praghāsas,<sup id="ref_120_55b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_55b_vei_1201432">55b</a></sup> when a wife names her lover or lovers, seems originally to have been a solemn means of banishing the evil brought on a family by a wife's fall; <b>Yājñavalkya's</b> famous saying<sup id="ref_120_56b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_56b_vei_1201432">56b</a></sup> that no one  cares whether a wife is ‘unchaste’ (<i>paraḥ-puṃsā</i>) or not is a mere mistranslation,<sup id="ref_120_57b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_57b_vei_1201432">57b</a></sup> the expression <i>paraḥ-puṃsā</i> really meaning ‘removed from the male persons.’ And the uncertainty asserted in some passages<sup id="ref_120_58b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_58b_vei_1201432">58b</a></sup> as to origin from a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> is not a sign of doubtful descent, but is due to the fact that Ṛṣihood was a difficult matter to ascertain. None the less woman's position was lowered by the prevalence of polygamy, and such stories as that of Ahalyā and <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a><sup id="ref_120_59b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_59b_vei_1201432">59b</a></sup> are not compatible with a very high standard of morality. A similar conclusion is pointed to by references in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a><sup id="ref_120_60b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_60b_vei_1201432">60b</a></sup> to relations between the <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a> man and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्रा">śūdrā</a> woman, and by a spell given in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_61b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_61b_vei_1201432">61b</a></sup> to expiate relations with the wife of a <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रोत्रिय">śrotriya</a> (Brahmin theologian).</p><p>(<i>e</i>) Incest was recognized in the marriage of brother and sister as appears from the legend of <a class="vei_120a" href="यम">yama</a> and Yamī in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_62b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_62b_vei_1201432">62b</a></sup> which clearly shows that such a marriage was not approved by the feeling of the Vedic age. There is also another hymn<sup id="ref_120_63b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_63b_vei_1201432">63b</a></sup> in which reference to such intercourse appears to be made. Mention is further made in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_64b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_64b_vei_1201432">64b</a></sup> to the wedlock of Prajāpati and his daughter, which is, however, interpreted mythologically in the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_65b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_65b_vei_1201432">65b</a></sup> an interpretation which may be correct. That incest, however, actually did take place is clear from the Atharvaveda;<sup id="ref_120_66b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_66b_vei_1201432">66b</a></sup> but even though the mythological interpretation of the passage were not justified, no conclusion could be drawn from the hymn as to the normal occurrence of such relations.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201432">↑</a> i. 22, 18; 164, 43, 50; iii. 3, 1; 17, 1; 60, 6; v. 26, 6; 63, 7; 72, 2, etc.; Av. xiv. 1, 51; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x. 29, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 90.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201432">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> is found in Av. xi. 7, 17; xii. 5, 7; xviii. 3, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 5, 2, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xv. 6; xx. 9; xxx. 6, etc.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1201432">↑</a> i. 22, 18; 164, 43, 50; iii. 3, 1; 17, 1; 60, 6; v. 26, 6; 63, 7; 72, 2, etc.; Av. xiv. 1, 51; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x. 29, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 90.</li><li id="fx_120_2b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> is found in Av. xi. 7, 17; xii. 5, 7; xviii. 3, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 5, 2, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xv. 6; xx. 9; xxx. 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3b_vei_1201432">↑</a> See Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte;</i> Foy, <i>Die ko7nigliche Gewalt nach den altindischen Rechtsbüchern;</i> Bühler, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 2 and 14.</li><li id="fx_120_4b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4b_vei_1201432">↑</a> Compare the list in Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 1, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxi. 7; Kapiṣṭhala Saṃhitā, xlvii. 7; and <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 8, 11 (see Delbrück. <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 579 <i>et seq.</i>), where bodily defects (bad nails and discoloured teeth), marrying a younger daughter when her elder sister was unmarried, are coupled with murder, though not equated with it. See also Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 11, 5, where Aśvapati's list of sinners includes a drinker of intoxicating liquor, a thief, and one who does not maintain a sacrificial fire.</li><li id="fx_120_5b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 5, 10, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxvii. 9; xxxi. 7; Kapiṣṭhala Saṃhitā, xli. 7; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 1, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 8, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 7, 8; 8, 3; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1, 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 27; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 1. <i>Cf.</i> Av. vi. 112, 3; 113, 2; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 481; 10, 66; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 522; <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 17, 430.</li><li id="fx_120_6b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a>, xxxi. 7; Kapiṣṭhala, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Maitrāyaṇī, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 5, and <i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैर">vaira</a>.</b> For cases of justifiable homicide, see, <i>e.g.,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 15-18. <i>Cf.</i> also the story of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वृश जान">vṛśa jāna</a></b> in <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 3, 12, where the death of a boy by careless driving is mentioned, and the king is reproached for it by his <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>. They dispute as to the guilt, and, according to one version (see Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 66, 67), the <b>Ikṣvākus</b> decide that the action was sinful, and required expiation.</li><li id="fx_120_7b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 1, 2; v. 3, 12, 1; vi. 5, 10, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxi. 7 (where the Kapiṣṭhala has <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्म-ज्य">brahma-jya</a>,</i> ‘oppressor of a Brahmin’); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 8, 12. The <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, x. 38, declares that the slaying of a Brahmin alone is truly murder, and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 3, 1, 1 <i>et seq.,</i> states that the sin of murdering a Brahmin can be expiated only by the performance of an <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or ‘horse sacrifice’--the <i>ne plus ultra</i> of human generosity to Brahmins. See also <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 27. The later tradition also interprets <i><a class="vei_120a" href="भ्रूण">bhrūṇa</a></i> as Brahmin (see Saṅkara, cited in Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 410, n.; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 30, n. 5; Konow, <i>Sāmavidhāna <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,</i> 46, n. 1, and <i>cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xx. 23).</li><li id="fx_120_8b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8b_vei_1201432">↑</a> xiv. 6, 8, the story of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुत्स">kutsa</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_9b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9b_vei_1201432">↑</a> Jolly, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 127.</li><li id="fx_120_10b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10b_vei_1201432">↑</a> See references in Bühler, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 14, 345.</li><li id="fx_120_11b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11b_vei_1201432">↑</a> Compare the story of the death of the child killed by Tryaruṇa, and the decision of the Ikṣväkus referred to in n. 6 above, and the notice in <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxvii. 4, that a <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a> is <i>adhyakṣa,</i> when a Sūdra is punished (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="हन्">han</a></i>).</li><li id="fx_120_12b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12b_vei_1201432">↑</a> vi. 16. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 72, 73.</li><li id="fx_120_13b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 43; Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 9, 25, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_14b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14b_vei_1201432">↑</a> See Av. xix. 47, 9; 50, 1, and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तस्कर">taskara</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_15b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15b_vei_1201432">↑</a> v. 10, 9. Another list is given in <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, x. 65, which includes slaying a Brahmin, defiling a Guru's bed, stealing a cow, drinking <a class="vei_120a" href="सुरा">surā</a>, and killing an embryo, along with irregularities in offering a Śrāddha, ‘water offering to the dead.’ <i>Cf.</i> also <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 27, for a list of seven. Many more appear in the Sāmavidhāna <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, but that work cannot claim to be a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> proper.</li><li id="fx_120_16b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16b_vei_1201432">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 7, 1, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_17b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Cæsar, <i>Bellum Gallicum,</i> iv. 1; vi. 22; Tacitus, <i>Germania,</i> 26, for Germany; Mommsen, <i>Ro7misches Staatsrecht,</i> 3, 1, 21, for the Roman <i>hortus;</i> and the Greek , Lang, <i>Homer and the Epic,</i> 236-241; Ridgeway, <i>Journal of Hellenic Studies,</i> 6, 319 <i>et seq.;</i> Grote, <i>History of Greece,</i> 2, 36, 37. See also Pollock and Maitland, <i>History of English Law,</i> 2, 337 <i>et seq.;</i> Baden Powell, <i>Village Communities in India,</i> 6 <i>et seq.;</i> 131.</li><li id="fx_120_18b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 36 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_19b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19b_vei_1201432">↑</a> xxx.</li><li id="fx_120_20b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20b_vei_1201432">↑</a> iii. 4. See Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 426 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 75 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_21b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Maine, <i>Village Communities,</i> 127, 175; Baden Powell, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 124 <i>et seq.;</i> Grote, <i>History of Greece,</i> 2, 36, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_22b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Jolly, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 126-128.</li><li id="fx_120_23b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23b_vei_1201432">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 6, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_24b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24b_vei_1201432">↑</a> x. 97, 12 = Av. iv. 9, 4 = Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xii. 86. <i>Madhyamaśīvan,</i> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 408, is of quite doubtful sense.</li><li id="fx_120_25b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <i>Siebenzig Lieder,</i> 174. This fact renders doubtful Lanman's view (Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 159) that the St. Petersburg Dictionary, in giving <i>intercessor</i> as the interpretation, did not mean ‘mediator,’ but ‘adversary.’</li><li id="fx_120_26b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 180.</li><li id="fx_120_27b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27b_vei_1201432">↑</a> As, apparently, in early Germany. See Cæsar, <i>Bellum Gallicum,</i> vi. 23; Tacitus, <i>Germania,</i> 11, 12; Coulanges, <i>Recherches sur quelques problèmes d'histoire,</i> 361 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_28b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28b_vei_1201432">↑</a> See n. 25.</li><li id="fx_120_29b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> the later <a class="vei_120a" href="परिषद्">pariṣad</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxviii. 48, 49; <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 1, 7-16; <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xi. 5-7, 20; Jolly, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 132 <i>et seq.</i> The parallels from other Āryan peoples suggest the use of assessors, as in the Anglo-Saxon courts of the shire and hundred. <i>Cf.</i> Sohm, <i>Altdeutsches Reichs-und Gerichtsverfassung,</i> 6 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_30b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_30b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 6, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_31b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_31b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 11, 9, which seems to refer either to giving evidence for or passing judgment on a case when both a Brahmin and a nonBrahmin are engaged.</li><li id="fx_120_32b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_32b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 319, 320. <i>Cf.</i> also Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 54, 260; Kaegi, <i>Der Rigveda,</i> n. 49; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 389, 390; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda. 6, 142; Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 48.</li><li id="fx_120_33b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_33b_vei_1201432">↑</a> xxvii. 9. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 5, 10, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 17, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> iii. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_34b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_34b_vei_1201432">↑</a> Bo7htlingk, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 44, 494496. The traditional rendering of the passages is not that it refers to exposure, but to getting rid of a daughter on her marriage.</li><li id="fx_120_35b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_35b_vei_1201432">↑</a> Av. viii. 6, 25; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 15; Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 409; Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 320; Schrader, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 390.</li><li id="fx_120_36b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_36b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 327, 328. Strabo, pp. 513, 517, 520, reports the practice as prevailing in Iran, Bactria, and the Massagetae; it prevailed among the Norsemen, Weinhold, <i>Altuordisches Leben,</i> 473, and conceivably among the early Romans (<i>depontani senes,</i> Cicero, <i>Pro Roscio,</i> 100; but this and other cases may be really instances of the ritual casting into water of the wornout vegetation spirit for the purpose of reviving it). See Kaegi, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> n. 50; Schrader, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 379, n.</li><li id="fx_120_37b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_37b_vei_1201432">↑</a> viii. 51, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_38b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_38b_vei_1201432">↑</a> xviii. 2, 34. See <b>Anagnidagdha.</b></li><li id="fx_120_39b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_39b_vei_1201432">↑</a> Rv. i. 124, 7; iv. 5, 5; Av. i. 17, 1; and <i>of.</i> <b>Ayogü.</b></li><li id="fx_120_40b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_40b_vei_1201432">↑</a> ii. 29, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 26.</li><li id="fx_120_41b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_41b_vei_1201432">↑</a> Av. xv. 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_42b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_42b_vei_1201432">↑</a> Av. xiv. 1, 36; xx. 136, 5 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 27. <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="नग्ना">nagnā</a></i> in Av. v. 7, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_43b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_43b_vei_1201432">↑</a> Rv. i. 167, 4 (Wilson, Translation of the Rigveda, 2, xvii), can hardly be so interpreted; see Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 332, n. Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 277, interprets it as a reference to polyandry, but this is still more doubtful; but see Rv. viii. 17, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_44b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_44b_vei_1201432">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_45b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_45b_vei_1201432">↑</a> iv. 19, 9; 30, 16. 19; ii. 13, 12; 15, 17; Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 334, 335.</li><li id="fx_120_46b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_46b_vei_1201432">↑</a> Apparently this is meant by the epithets <i>atiṣkadvarī</i> (<i>apaskadvarī</i> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 11, 1), <i>atītvarī, vijarjarā,</i> in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 15.</li><li id="fx_120_47b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_47b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, xxv; 196, 275, 299, 309, etc.; 2, 120.</li><li id="fx_120_48b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_48b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 2, 154.</li><li id="fx_120_49b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_49b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Winternitz, <i>Geschichte der indischen Litteratur,</i> 1, 60; Jolly, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 48.</li><li id="fx_120_50b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_50b_vei_1201432">↑</a> Leist, <i>Altarisches Jus Gentium,</i> 276 <i>et seq.,</i> 309.</li><li id="fx_120_51b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_51b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 83 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_52b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_52b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 5, 573.</li><li id="fx_120_53b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_53b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 545 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_54b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_54b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 6, 8, 3; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_55b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_55b_vei_1201432">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 10, 11; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 5, 2, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_56b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_56b_vei_1201432">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 1, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_57b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_57b_vei_1201432">↑</a> So Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Delbrück, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 548.</li><li id="fx_120_58b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_58b_vei_1201432">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 4, 11; Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, cited in Ludwig, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_59b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_59b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 65.</li><li id="fx_120_60b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_60b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 4, 19, 2. 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 30. 31.</li><li id="fx_120_61b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_61b_vei_1201432">↑</a> vi. 4, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_62b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_62b_vei_1201432">↑</a> x. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_63b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_63b_vei_1201432">↑</a> x. 162, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_64b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_64b_vei_1201432">↑</a> x. 61, 5-7.</li><li id="fx_120_65b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_65b_vei_1201432">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 33; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 4, 1; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 4, 46, 47; Max Müller, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 529, 530.</li><li id="fx_120_66b_vei_1201432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_66b_vei_1201432">↑</a> viii. 6, 7.</li></ol></div>

धव|धव|ధవ_౧|ధవ|ಧವ_೧|ಧವ|dhava_1|dhava|_vei_120@1433|_vei1_pn397
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1432">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1434">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn390">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn397">1:397</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn398">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Dhava</b> is the name of a tree (<i>Grislea tormentosa</i>) mentioned, together with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्लक्ष">plakṣa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वत्थ">aśvattha</a>,</b> and <b>Khādira,</b> in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201433"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201433">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201433"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201433">↑</a> v. 5, 5; xx. 137, 11. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 62.</li></ol></div>

धव|धव|ధవ_౨|ధవ|ಧವ_೨|ಧವ|dhava_2|dhava|_vei_120@1434|_vei1_pn398
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1433">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1435">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn397">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn398">1:398</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn399">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Dhava,</b> ‘man,’ is not found before the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201434">1</a></sup> The word clearly owes its existence merely to <i><a class="vei_120a" href="विधवा">vidhavā</a>,</i> ‘widow,’ wrongly interpreted as <i>vi-dhavā,</i> ‘without a husband.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201434">↑</a> iii. 15. <i>Cf.</i> Naighaṇṭuka, ii. 3.</li></ol></div>

धवित्र|ధవిత్ర|ಧವಿತ್ರ|dhavitra|_vei_120@1435|_vei1_pn398
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1434">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1436">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn397">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn398">1:398</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn399">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhavitra,</b> occurring in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201435">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201435">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘fan’ of hide or leather for blowing the sacrificial fire.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201435">↑</a> xiv. 1, 3, 30; 3, 1, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201435">↑</a> v. 4, 33.</li></ol></div>

धानं-जय्य|धानंजय्य|धानं|जय्य|धानञ्जय्य|ధానం-జయ్య|ధానంజయ్య|ధానం|జయ్య|ధానఞ్జయ్య|ಧಾನಂ-ಜಯ್ಯ|ಧಾನಂಜಯ್ಯ|ಧಾನಂ|ಜಯ್ಯ|ಧಾನಞ್ಜಯ್ಯ|dhānaṃ-jayya|dhānaṃjayya|dhānaṃ|jayya|dhaanaM-jayya|dhaanaMjayya|dhaanaM|dhaananjayya|_vei_120@1436|_vei1_pn398
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1435">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1437">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn397">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn398">1:398</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn399">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhānaṃ-jayya,</b> ‘descendant of <b>Dhanaṃ-jaya,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अंशु">aṃśu</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201436"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201436">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201436"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201436">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373. Under this patronymic he is frequently named by Lāṭyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 1, 25; ii. 1, 2; 9, 10, etc. (misread Dhānaṃjapya in many manuscripts). <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 76, 77, 82.</li></ol></div>

धाना|ధానా|ಧಾನಾ|dhānā|dhaanaa|_vei_120@1437|_vei1_pn398
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1436">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1438">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn397">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn398">1:398</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn399">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhānā,</b> always used in the plural, and frequently referred to in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201437">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201437">2</a></sup> means ‘grains of corn.’ They were sometimes parched (<i>bhṛjj</i>),<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201437">3</a></sup> and were regularly mixed with <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201437">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201437">↑</a> i. 16, 2; iii. 35, 3; 52, 5; vi. 29, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201437">↑</a> Av. xviii. 3, 69; 4, 32. 34; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 21, 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 11, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201437">↑</a> Rv. iv. 24, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201437">↑</a> Rv. iii. 43, 4; 52, 1; viii. 91, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 1, 10, 2; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 4, 3, 9.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prchistoric Antiquities,</i> 283.</li></ol></div>

धान्य|ధాన్య|ಧಾನ್ಯ|dhānya|dhaanya|_vei_120@1438|_vei1_pn398
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1437">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1439">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn397">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn398">1:398</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn399">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhānya</b> (neut.), a derivative from the preceding word,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201438"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201438">1</a></sup> denotes ‘grain’ in general. It is found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201438"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201438">2</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201438"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201438">3</a></sup> According to the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201438"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201438">4</a></sup> there are ten cultivated (<i>grāmyāṇi</i>) kinds of grain: rice and barley (<i>vrīhiyavāḥ</i>), sesamum and beans (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="तिल">tila</a>-māṣāḥ</i>), Panicum Miliaceum and Italicum (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अणु">aṇu</a>-priyaṅgavaḥ</i>), maize (<i>godhūmāḥ</i>), lentils (<i>masūrāḥ</i>), Khalvāḥ and Dolichos uniflorus (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="खल">khala</a>-kulāḥ</i>). The  horse is called ‘corn-eating’ (<i>dhānyāda</i>) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201438"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201438">5</a></sup> and Śatapatha<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201438"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201438">6</a></sup> Brāhmaṇas, and men are mentioned as ‘purifying corn’ (<i>dhānyā-<a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्">kṛt</a></i>) in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201438"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201438">8</a></sup></p><p>7 Lit., ‘preparing corn.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201438"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201438">↑</a> Primarily as an adjective, ‘consisting of grain.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201438"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201438">↑</a> vi. 13, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201438"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201438">↑</a> Av. iii. 24, 2. 4; v. 29, 7; vi. 50, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 8; Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201438"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201438">↑</a> vi. 3, 22 (Mādhyaṃdina = 13 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201438"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201438">↑</a> viii. 21.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201438"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201438">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201438"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201438">↑</a> x. 94, 13.</li></ol></div>

धान्व|ధాన్వ|ಧಾನ್ವ|dhānva|dhaanva|_vei_120@1439|_vei1_pn399
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1438">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1440">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn398">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn399">1:399</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn400">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhānva</b> is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="असित">asita</a></b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201439"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201439">1</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201439"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201439">2</a></sup> the form of the name is Dhānvana.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201439"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201439">↑</a> xiii. 4, 3, 11; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201439"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201439">↑</a> xvi. 2, 20.</li></ol></div>

धामन्|ధామన్|ಧಾಮನ್|dhāman|dhaaman|_vei_120@1440|_vei1_pn399
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1439">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1441">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn398">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn399">1:399</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn400">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhāman</b> denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201440"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201440">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201440"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201440">2</a></sup> ‘dwelling’ and ‘house,’ or sometimes<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201440"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201440">3</a></sup> its inmates. The word is also<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201440"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201440">4</a></sup> found in the sense of ‘ordinance,’ ‘law,’ expressing much the same as <b>Dharman,</b> especially in conjunction<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201440"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201440">5</a></sup> with Rta, ‘eternal order.’ Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201440"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201440">6</a></sup> sees in one passage<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201440"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201440">7</a></sup> the sense of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201440"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201440">↑</a> i. 144, 1; ii. 3, 2; iii. 55, 10; vii. 61, 4; 87, 2; x. 13, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201440"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201440">↑</a> Av. iv. 25, 7; vii. 68, 1; xii. 1, 52; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iv. 34; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 7, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201440"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201440">↑</a> Rv. viii. 101, 6; ix. 63, 14; x. 82, 3; Av. ii. 14, 6. Many of the examples given in the St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v., C,</i> are doubtful.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201440"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201440">↑</a> Rv. iv. 55, 2; vi. 21, 3; vii. 63, 3; viii. 41, 10; x. 48, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201440"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201440">↑</a> Rv. i. 123, 9; iv. 7, 7; vii. 36, 5; x. 124, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201440"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201440">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 446.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201440"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201440">↑</a> Rv. ix. 66, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 92, 93.</li></ol></div>

धारा|ధారా|ಧಾರಾ|dhārā|dhaaraa|_vei_120@1441|_vei1_pn399
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1440">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1442">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn398">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn399">1:399</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn400">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhārā</b> denotes the ‘edge’ of a weapon,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201441">1</a></sup> as of an axe (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्वधिति">svadhiti</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201441">2</a></sup> or of a razor (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षुर">kṣura</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201441">3</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="असि">asi</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201441">↑</a> Rv. vi. 3, 5; 47, 10. <i>Cf.</i> viii. 73, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, iv. 38, 1, for metaphorical applications.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201441">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, 44.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201441">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 3, 2.</li></ol></div>

धिषणा|ధిషణా|ಧಿಷಣಾ|dhiṣaṇā|dhiShaNaa|_vei_120@1442|_vei1_pn399
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1441">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1443">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn398">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn399">1:399</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn400">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhiṣaṇā,</b> according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, denotes<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201442">1</a></sup> an implement used in preparing the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>, ‘bowl’ or ‘vat,’ and by metonymy also the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> draught itself.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201442">2</a></sup> The dual, by  a metaphor,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201442">3</a></sup> also expresses the ‘two worlds,’ heaven and earth.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201442">4</a></sup> Hillebrandt,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201442">5</a></sup> however, thinks that the word properly means earth,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201442">6</a></sup> in the dual heaven and earth,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201442">7</a></sup> in the plural the triad, earth, atmosphere, and heaven,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201442">8</a></sup> while in some passages<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201442">9</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="धिषणा">dhiṣaṇā</a> denotes the Vedi, the excavated ground used as an altar. This is not, however, certain, while it seems clear that the Vājasaneyi<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201442">10</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201442">11</a></sup> Saṃhitās understand the Dhiṣaṇās (dual) to be the planks over which the pressing of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> took place (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अधिषवण">adhiṣavaṇa</a>-phalake</i>).<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201442">12</a></sup> Pischel<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201442">13</a></sup> sees in <a class="vei_120a" href="धिषणा">dhiṣaṇā</a> a goddess of wealth akin to Aditi and the earth.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201442">↑</a> Rv. i. 96, 1; 102, 1; 109, 3, 4; iii. 49, 1; iv. 34, 1; 36, 8; viii. 61, 9; ix. 59, 2; x. 17, 12; 30, 6; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, i. 19; vi. 26, 35, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201442">↑</a> Rv. i. 102, 7; iii. 32, 14; 49, 4; vi. 19, 2; vii. 90, 3; viii. 15, 7; x. 96, 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201442">↑</a> Like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चमू">camū</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201442">↑</a> Rv. i. 160, 1; vi. 8, 3; 50, 3; 70, 3; x. 44, 8; in the plural, ‘the three worlds,’ Rv. v. 69, 2. In other passages, Rv. i. 22, 10; iii. 56, 6; v. 41, 8; vi. 11, 3; x. 35, 7, the sense of ‘a genius of prosperity’ was assigned to <a class="vei_120a" href="धिषणा">dhiṣaṇā</a> by Roth.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201442">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 175-181.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201442">↑</a> Rv. i. 22, 10; 96, 1; 102, 1; iii. 31, 13; 56, 6; vi. 19, 2; vii. 90, 3; viii. 15, 7; x. 30, 6; 35, 7; 96, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201442">↑</a> See n. 3; also Rv. viii. 61, 2; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="निविद्">nivid</a></i> in Sāṅkhāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 19, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201442">↑</a> Rv. iv. 36, 8; v. 69, 2; ix. 59, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201442">↑</a> Rv. i. 109, 3, 4; iii. 2, 1; 49, 4 (or perhaps ‘earth’); iv. 34, 1; v. 41, 8; vi. 11, 3; x. 17, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201442">↑</a> vii. 26.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201442">↑</a> iii. 1, 10, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201442">↑</a> Mahīdhara on Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, vii. 26; Sāyaṇa on <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201442">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 82-87. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 124; Oldenberg, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 46, 120-122.</li></ol></div>

धी|धि|ధీ|ధి|ಧೀ|ಧಿ|dhī|dhi|dhii|dhee|_vei_120@1443|_vei1_pn400
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1442">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1444">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn399">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn400">1:400</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn401">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhī,</b> ‘thought,’ is used several times in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201443"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201443">1</a></sup> to denote the ‘prayer’ or ‘hymn of praise’ of the singer. One poet speaks of himself as ‘weaving’ such a prayer,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201443"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201443">2</a></sup> while another refers to his ‘ancient ancestral hymn,’ which he refurbishes presumably for use.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201443"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201443">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201443"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201443">↑</a> i. 3, 5; 135, 5; 151, 6; 185, 8; ii. 3, 8 (where it is connected with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a></b>); 40, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201443"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201443">↑</a> Rv. ii. 28, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201443"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201443">↑</a> Rv. iii. 39, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 338.</li></ol></div>

धीति|ధీతి|ಧೀತಿ|dhīti|dhiiti|dheeti|_vei_120@1444|_vei1_pn400
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1443">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1445">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn399">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn400">1:400</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn401">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhīti</b> has in several passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201444"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201444">1</a></sup> practically the same sense as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धी">dhī</a>,</b> ‘prayer,’ or ‘hymn of praise.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201444"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201444">↑</a> i. 110, 1; iii. 12, 7; 52, 6; v. 25, 3; 53, 11; vi. 15, 9, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 24.</li></ol></div>

धीर शात-पर्णेय|धीर शातपर्णेय|धीर शात|पर्णेय|ధీర శాత-పర్ణేయ|ధీర శాతపర్ణేయ|ధీర శాత|పర్ణేయ|ಧೀರ ಶಾತ-ಪರ್ಣೇಯ|ಧೀರ ಶಾತಪರ್ಣೇಯ|ಧೀರ ಶಾತ|ಪರ್ಣೇಯ|dhīra śāta-parṇeya|dhīra śātaparṇeya|dhīra śāta|parṇeya|dhiira shaata-parNeya|dhiira shaataparNeya|dhiira shaata|parNeya|dheera shaata-parNeya|dheera shaataparNeya|dheera shaata|_vei_120@1445|_vei1_pn400
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1444">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1446">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn399">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn400">1:400</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn401">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhīra <a class="vei_120a" href="शात">śāta</a>-parṇeya</b> (‘descendant of Śataparṇa’) is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (x. 3, 3, 1) as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="महाशाल">mahāśāla</a>.</b> </p></div>

धीवन्|ధీవన్|ಧೀವನ್|dhīvan|dhiivan|dheevan|_vei_120@1446|_vei1_pn401
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1445">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1447">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn400">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn401">1:401</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn402">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhīvan</b> occurs in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201446"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201446">1</a></sup> where it may either be taken with Roth,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201446"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201446">2</a></sup> Bloomfield,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201446"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201446">3</a></sup> and Whitney<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201446"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201446">4</a></sup> as an epithet of ‘chariot-builders’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a>-kārāḥ</i>), meaning ‘clever,’ or be construed with the scholiast as denoting ‘fishermen’ (<i>dhīvara</i>). The Paippalāda recension has <i>takṣāṇaḥ,</i> ‘carpenters.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201446"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201446">↑</a> iii. 5, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201446"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201446">↑</a> St. Petersbuig Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201446"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201446">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 114.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201446"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201446">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 114.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indīsche Studien,</i> 17, 194 <i>et seq.;</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 252.</li></ol></div>

धुंक्षा|धुङ्क्षा|ధుంక్షా|ధుఙ్క్షా|ಧುಂಕ್ಷಾ|ಧುಙ್ಕ್ಷಾ|dhuṅkṣā|dhuMkShaa|dhunkShaa|dhuMxaa|_vei_120@1447|_vei1_pn401
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1446">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1448">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn400">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn401">1:401</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn402">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhuṅkṣā</b> is the name of some sort of bird in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or ‘horse sacrifice,’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201447"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201447">1</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धूंक्ष्णा">dhūṅkṣṇā</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ध्वांक्ष">dhvāṅkṣa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201447"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201447">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 12; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 31. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 93.</li></ol></div>

धुनि|ధుని|ಧುನಿ|dhuni|_vei_120@1448|_vei1_pn401
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1447">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1449">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn400">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn401">1:401</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn402">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhuni</b> is the name of a foe of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, normally mentioned along with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चुमुरि">cumuri</a></b> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201448">1</a></sup> He and <a class="vei_120a" href="चुमुरि">cumuri</a> seem to have been opposed to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दभीति">dabhīti</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201448">2</a></sup> His name is probably that of an aboriginal chief.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201448">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201448">↑</a> ii. 15, 9; vi. 18, 8; 20, 13; vii. 19, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201448">↑</a> Rv. x. 113. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201448">↑</a> Wackernagel, <i>Altindische Grammatik,</i> 1, xxii; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 162. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 157, 158.</li></ol></div>

धुर्|ధుర్|ಧುರ್|dhur|_vei_120@1449|_vei1_pn401
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1448">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1450">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn400">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn401">1:401</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn402">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhur</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201449">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201449">2</a></sup> denotes, according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, that part of the yoke which is placed on the shoulders of the animals drawing the chariot or cart, whence they are called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="धूर्">dhūr</a>-ṣāh,</i> ‘yoke-bearing,’ in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201449">3</a></sup> In one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201449">4</a></sup> the sense is uncertain: Roth<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201449">5</a></sup> takes it to mean the pin at either end of the axle (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a></b>) which goes through the nave of the wheel, and would thus be equivalent to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आणि">āṇi</a>,</b> and Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201449">6</a></sup>  seems to adopt the same view. Monier Williams<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201449">7</a></sup> seems to think that ‘load’ is meant, but this is not probable. It is possible that <a class="vei_120a" href="धुर्">dhur</a> has the sense of ‘pole,’<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201449">8</a></sup> and then more generally still the pole and the axle together regarded as the drawing part of the chariot: this might explain the use in the doubtful passage of the Rigveda.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201449">↑</a> i. 84, 16; 100, 16; 134, 3; 164, 19; ii. 18, 7; iii. 35, 2; v. 55. 6; vii. 34, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201449">↑</a> Av. v. 17, 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> vi. 18; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 2 10; 4, 4, 13, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka i. 5, 2 (the <a class="vei_120a" href="धुर्">dhur</a> is the end), etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201449">↑</a> iv. 33. <i>Cf.</i> <b>Uara.</b></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201449">↑</a> v. 43. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201449">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201449">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 339; Griffith. <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 1, 508. The <i>akṣadhurau</i> are mentioned in the Āpastamba Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xi. 6, 5; Kātyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 3, 22. <i>Cf.</i> Caland and Henry. <i>L'Agniṣṭoma,</i> 31.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201449">↑</a> Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201449">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 246. Later, the word means the ‘end of the pole,’ a sense already found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka (n. 2). This modification of meaning seems to be due to the fact that the yoke is at the end of the pole. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धूर्षद्">dhūrṣad</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

धूंक्ष्णा|धूङ्क्ष्णा|ధూంక్ష్ణా|ధూఙ్క్ష్ణా|ಧೂಂಕ್ಷ್ಣಾ|ಧೂಙ್ಕ್ಷ್ಣಾ|dhūṅkṣṇā|dhuuMkShNaa|dhooMkShNaa|dhuunkShNaa|dhoonkShNaa|dhuuMxNaa|_vei_120@1450|_vei1_pn402
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1449">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1451">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn401">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn402">1:402</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn403">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhūṅkṣṇā</b> is the form in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201450"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201450">1</a></sup> of the name of the bird elsewhere found as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धुंक्षा">dhuṅkṣā</a>.</b> It is glossed as ‘white crow’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="श्वेत">śveta</a>-kākī</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201450"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201450">↑</a> v. 5, 19, 1. Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 93, gives the form as Dhūṅkṣṇa. perhaps in error. <i>Cf.</i> also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ध्वांक्ष">dhvāṅkṣa</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

धूम-केतु|धूमकेतु|धूम|केतु|ధూమ-కేతు|ధూమకేతు|ధూమ|కేతు|ಧೂಮ-ಕೇತು|ಧೂಮಕೇತು|ಧೂಮ|ಕೇತು|dhūma-ketu|dhūmaketu|dhūma|ketu|dhuuma-ketu|dhuumaketu|dhuuma|dhooma-ketu|dhoomaketu|dhooma|_vei_120@1451|_vei1_pn402
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1450">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1452">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn401">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn402">1:402</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn403">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhūma-ketu,</b> ‘smoke-bannered,’ is an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="मृत्यु">mṛtyu</a>, ‘death,’ in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201451"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201451">1</a></sup> Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201451"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201451">2</a></sup> thinks that a comet is meant, but Whitney<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201451"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201451">3</a></sup> considers this extremely improbable. Lanman<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201451"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201451">4</a></sup> plausibly suggests that the smoke of the funeral pile is referred to.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201451"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201451">↑</a> xix. 9, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201451"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201451">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 358.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201451"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201451">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 914.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201451"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201451">↑</a> <i>Ibid.</i></li></ol></div>

धूम्र|ధూమ్ర|ಧೂಮ್ರ|dhūmra|dhuumra|dhoomra|_vei_120@1452|_vei1_pn402
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1451">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1453">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn401">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn402">1:402</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn403">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhūmra</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (i. 8, 21, 1) denotes ‘camel’ according to Bo7htlingk's Dictionary.</p></div>

धूर्-षद्|धूर्षद्|धूर्|षद्|ధూర్-షద్|ధూర్షద్|ధూర్|షద్|ಧೂರ್-ಷದ್|ಧೂರ್ಷದ್|ಧೂರ್|ಷದ್|dhūr-ṣad|dhūrṣad|dhūr|ṣad|dhuur-Shad|dhuurShad|dhuur|Shad|dhoor-Shad|dhoorShad|dhoor|_vei_120@1453|_vei1_pn402
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1452">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1454">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn401">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn402">1:402</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn403">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhūr-ṣad</b> means, according to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201453"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201453">1</a></sup> ‘standing under the yoke’ and so ‘burden-bearing,’ and thus metaphorically ‘promoting,’ in the passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201453"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201453">2</a></sup> where it occurs. More probable, however, is the view<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201453"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201453">3</a></sup> that it means ‘sitting on the pole,’ that is, ‘charioteer,’ with reference to the fact that  to get near his horses the charioteer might well go forward and sit on the pole or even on the yoke.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201453"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201453">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201453"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201453">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201453"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201453">↑</a> i. 143, 7; ii. 2, 1; 34, 4 (but <i>cf.</i> Pischel. <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 301). In x. 132, 7, Roth renders ‘resting on the yoke.’</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201453"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201453">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 237 <i>et seq.;</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 195.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201453"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201453">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Mahābhārata, viii. 617: <i>dhuryān dhuryagatān sūtān.</i></li></ol></div>

धृत-राष्ट्र|धृत-राष्ट्र|धृतराष्ट्र|धृत|राष्ट्र|ధృత-రాష్ట్ర_౧|ధృత-రాష్ట్ర|ధృతరాష్ట్ర|ధృత|రాష్ట్ర|ಧೃತ-ರಾಷ್ಟ್ರ_೧|ಧೃತ-ರಾಷ್ಟ್ರ|ಧೃತರಾಷ್ಟ್ರ|ಧೃತ|ರಾಷ್ಟ್ರ|dhṛta-rāṣṭra_1|dhṛta-rāṣṭra|dhṛtarāṣṭra|dhṛta|rāṣṭra|dhRta-raaShTra_1|dhRta-raaShTra|dhRtaraaShTra|dhRta|raaShTra|dhRita-raaShTra_1|dhRita-raaShTra|dhRitaraaShTra|dhRita|dhRuta-raaShTra_1|dhRuta-raaShTra|dhRutaraaShTra|dhRuta|dhRRita-raaShTra_1|dhRRita-raaShTra|dhRRitaraaShTra|dhRRita|_vei_120@1454|_vei1_pn403
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1453">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1455">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn402">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn403">1:403</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn404">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Dhṛta-rāṣṭra</b> (‘having his kingdom firmly established’) is the name of a snake demon with the patronymic <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐरावत">airāvata</a>,</b> ‘descendant of <b>Irāvant,</b>’ in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201454"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201454">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201454"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201454">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201454"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201454">↑</a> viii. 10, 29.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201454"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201454">↑</a> xxv. 15, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 26, 15; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17. 257.</li></ol></div>

धृतराष्ट्र वैचित्र-वीर्य|धृतराष्ट्र वैचित्र-वीर्य|धृतराष्ट्र वैचित्रवीर्य|धृतराष्ट्र वैचित्र|वीर्य|ధృతరాష్ట్ర వైచిత్ర-వీర్య_౨|ధృతరాష్ట్ర వైచిత్ర-వీర్య|ధృతరాష్ట్ర వైచిత్రవీర్య|ధృతరాష్ట్ర వైచిత్ర|వీర్య|ಧೃತರಾಷ್ಟ್ರ ವೈಚಿತ್ರ-ವೀರ್ಯ_೨|ಧೃತರಾಷ್ಟ್ರ ವೈಚಿತ್ರ-ವೀರ್ಯ|ಧೃತರಾಷ್ಟ್ರ ವೈಚಿತ್ರವೀರ್ಯ|ಧೃತರಾಷ್ಟ್ರ ವೈಚಿತ್ರ|ವೀರ್ಯ|dhṛtarāṣṭra vaicitra-vīrya_2|dhṛtarāṣṭra vaicitra-vīrya|dhṛtarāṣṭra vaicitravīrya|dhṛtarāṣṭra vaicitra|vīrya|dhRtaraaShTra vaichitra-viirya_2|dhRtaraaShTra vaichitra-viirya|dhRtaraaShTra vaichitraviirya|dhRtaraaShTra vaichitra|viirya|dhRtaraaShTra vaichitra-veerya_2|dhRtaraaShTra vaichitra-veerya|dhRtaraaShTra vaichitraveerya|veerya|dhRitaraaShTra vaichitra-viirya_2|dhRitaraaShTra vaichitra-viirya|dhRitaraaShTra vaichitraviirya|dhRitaraaShTra vaichitra|dhRutaraaShTra vaichitra-viirya_2|dhRutaraaShTra vaichitra-viirya|dhRutaraaShTra vaichitraviirya|dhRutaraaShTra vaichitra|dhRRitaraaShTra vaichitra-viirya_2|dhRRitaraaShTra vaichitra-viirya|dhRRitaraaShTra vaichitraviirya|dhRRitaraaShTra vaichitra|_vei_120@1455|_vei1_pn403
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1454">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1456">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn402">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn403">1:403</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn404">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Dhṛtarāṣṭra Vaicitra-vīrya</b> (‘descendant of Vicitravīrya’) is mentioned in a passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201455"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201455">1</a></sup> which is, unhappily, far from intelligible. But there is no ground for supposing that he was a <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a> king; he seems rather to have lived at some distance from the KuruPañcālas. There is no good reason to deny his identity<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201455"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201455">2</a></sup> with the <a class="vei_120a" href="धृतराष्ट्र">dhṛtarāṣṭra</a> of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201455"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201455">3</a></sup> king of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a>,</b> who was defeated, when he attempted to offer a horse sacrifice, by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सात्राजित">sātrājita</a> Śatānīka.</b> The fact that the latter was a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a></b> also points to Dhṛtarāṣṭra's not having been a <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a> at all. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā he appears as having a dispute with <b>Vaka <a class="vei_120a" href="दाल्भि">dālbhi</a>;</b> but even assuming that the latter was a <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a>, there is nothing to hint that the former was a <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a> or that this dispute is a sign of an early hostility of <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201455"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201455">4</a></sup> It is true that in the Epic <b>Śantanu</b> and Vicitravīrya and <a class="vei_120a" href="धृतराष्ट्र">dhṛtarāṣṭra</a> himself are all connected, but this connexion seems to be due, as so often in the Epic, to a confused derangement of great figures of the past.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201455"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201455">↑</a> x. 6. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 469 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201455"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201455">↑</a> As does Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 90, 114, 125; <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 7, 8. Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> treats them as identical.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201455"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201455">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201455"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201455">↑</a> Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 831 <i>et seq.</i> This argument is independent of the identification of the two Dhṛtarāṣṭras, but is confirmed by it.</li></ol></div>

धृष्टि|ధృష్టి|ಧೃಷ್ಟಿ|dhṛṣṭi|dhRShTi|dhRiShTi|dhRuShTi|dhRRiShTi|_vei_120@1456|_vei1_pn403
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1455">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1457">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn402">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn403">1:403</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn404">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhṛṣṭi,</b> found in the dual in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201456">1</a></sup> the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201456">2</a></sup> and the Sūtras,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201456">3</a></sup> seems to denote ‘firetongs.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201456">↑</a> v. 9, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201456">↑</a> xiv. 3, 1, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201456">↑</a> Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxvi. 2, 10, etc.</li></ol></div>

धेना|ధేనా|ಧೇನಾ|dhenā|dhenaa|_vei_120@1457|_vei1_pn404
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1456">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1458">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn403">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn404">1:404</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn405">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhenā</b> denotes a ‘milch cow,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201457">1</a></sup> or in the plural, ‘draughts of milk.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201457">2</a></sup> In two passages<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201457">3</a></sup> Roth<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201457">4</a></sup> takes the word to mean ‘mare,’ and in another the ‘team’ of Vāyu's chariot. Benfey,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201457">6</a></sup> on the other hand, renders it ‘lips’ in one passage,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201457">7</a></sup> with Sāyaṇa and with Durga's commentary on the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201457">8</a></sup> Geldner<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201457">9</a></sup> assigns to the word the senses of ‘lips,’<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201457">10</a></sup> ‘speech,’<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201457">11</a></sup> ‘cow,’<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201457">12</a></sup> ‘beloved,’<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201457">13</a></sup> and ‘streams.’<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201457">14</a></sup></p><p>5) Rv. i. 2, 3.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201457">↑</a> Rv. iii. 34, 3 (Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 61); v. 62, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 114.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201457">↑</a> Rv. iii. 1, 9; iv. 58, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201457">↑</a> i. 101, 10; v. 30, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201457">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201457">↑</a> <i>Orient und Occident,</i> 3, 130.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201457">↑</a> Rv. i. 101. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201457">↑</a> vi. 17. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 249.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201457">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 35-43; 166; <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 95.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201457">↑</a> Rv. i. 101. 10; iii. 1, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201457">↑</a> Rv. iv. 58, 6; i. 55, 4; 141, 1; viii. 32, 22; x. 104, 3. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201457">↑</a> Rv. v. 62, 2, and Vāyu's cow of plenty, i. 2, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201457">↑</a> Rv. v. 30, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201457">↑</a> Rv. vii. 21, 3; iii. 34, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 441, 442.</li></ol></div>

धेनु|ధేను|ಧೇನು|dhenu|_vei_120@1458|_vei1_pn404
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1457">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1459">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn403">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn404">1:404</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn405">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhenu</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201458">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201458">2</a></sup> means ‘milch cow,’ which is often mentioned with special reference to the production of milk,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201458">3</a></sup> and is contrasted with the ‘bull’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वृषभ">vṛṣabha</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201458">4</a></sup> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पुमांस्">pumāṃs</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201458">5</a></sup> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अनड्वाह्">anaḍvāh</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201458">6</a></sup> In the plural<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201458">7</a></sup> the word denotes ‘draughts of milk.’ The derivative, <i>dhenukā,</i> means merely ‘female.’<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201458">8</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201458">↑</a> i. 32, 9 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सह">saha</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वत्सा">vatsā</a>,</i> ‘with her calf’); 134, 4; ii. 2, 2; 34, 8; vi. 135, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201458">↑</a> Av. v. 17, 18; vii. 104. 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 2, 3; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 4, 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 27; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 2, 1, 21, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201458">↑</a> Rv. vii. 33. 22; viii. 14, 3; Av. iv. 34, 8 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="काम">kāma</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="दुघा">dughā</a>,</i> ‘milking desires,’ the later ‘cow of plenty’ of the Epic); Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 8, 2, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201458">↑</a> Rv. x. 5, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201458">↑</a> Av. xi. 1, 34.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201458">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 27; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 2, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201458">↑</a> Rv. iv. 22, 6; viii. 2, 6; 4, 8; ix. 61, 21; 72, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201458">↑</a> Av. iii. 23, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>. xxv. 10, 23; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 6, etc.</li></ol></div>

धेनु-ष्टरी|धेनु-ष्टरि|धेनुष्टरी|धेनुष्टरि|धेनु|ष्टरी|ष्टरि|ధేను-ష్టరీ|ధేను-ష్టరి|ధేనుష్టరీ|ధేనుష్టరి|ధేను|ష్టరీ|ష్టరి|ಧೇನು-ಷ್ಟರೀ|ಧೇನು-ಷ್ಟರಿ|ಧೇನುಷ್ಟರೀ|ಧೇನುಷ್ಟರಿ|ಧೇನು|ಷ್ಟರೀ|ಷ್ಟರಿ|dhenu-ṣṭarī|dhenu-ṣṭari|dhenuṣṭarī|dhenuṣṭari|dhenu|ṣṭarī|ṣṭari|dhenu-ShTarii|dhenu-ShTari|dhenuShTarii|dhenuShTari|ShTarii|ShTari|dhenu-ShTaree|dhenuShTaree|ShTaree|_vei_120@1459|_vei1_pn404
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1458">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1460">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn403">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn404">1:404</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn405">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhenu-ṣṭarī</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> (xiii. 6) and Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā (ii. 5, 4) denotes a cow which has ceased to give milk.</p></div>

धैवर|ధైవర|ಧೈವರ|dhaivara|_vei_120@1460|_vei1_pn404
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1459">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1461">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn403">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn404">1:404</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn405">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhaivara</b> means a ‘fisherman,’ as a member of a caste,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201460"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201460">1</a></sup> in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha, or ‘human sacrifice,’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201460"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201460">2</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b>Dhīvara.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201460"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201460">↑</a> This seems to be shown by the patronymic form, ‘descendant of a <i>dhīvara.</i>’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201460"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201460">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 16; Tait tirīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 15, 1.</li></ol></div>

ध्मातृ|ధ్మాతృ|ಧ್ಮಾತೃ|dhmātṛ|dhmaatR|dhmaatRi|dhmaatRu|dhmaatRRi|_vei_120@1461|_vei1_pn405
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1460">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1462">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn404">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn405">1:405</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn406">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhmātṛ</b> (lit. ‘blower’) occurs twice in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201461">1</a></sup> in the two forms, <i>dhmātā,</i> nom. ‘smelter,’ and <i>dhmātarī,</i> which, according to the Padapāṭha, stands for <i>dhmātari,</i> a locative probably meaning ‘in the smelting furnace.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201461">2</a></sup> Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201461">3</a></sup> Bartholomae,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201461">4</a></sup> and Oldenburg<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201461">5</a></sup> regard the latter form as a locative infinitive, ‘in the smelting.’ Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201461">6</a></sup> and Neisser<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201461">7</a></sup> think <i>dhmātarī</i> is a nom. sing. masc. used in the same sense as <i>dhmātā.</i> Smelting is also clearly referred to,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201461">8</a></sup> and the smelter is described as using the wings of birds (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्ण">parṇa</a> śakunānām</i>) to fan the flame.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201461">9</a></sup> That the art was widely applied is shown by the fact that reference is made to arrows with points of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्">ayas</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201461">10</a></sup> to kettles which were fashioned of the same metal and could be placed upon a fire,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201461">11</a></sup> and to <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> cups of beaten <a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्">ayas</a>.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201461">12</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201461">↑</a> v. 9. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201461">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1893, 446.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201461">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 146, n, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201461">↑</a> <i>Indogermanische Forschungen,</i> 1, 496. n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201461">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 46, 388.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201461">↑</a> <i>Infinitiv im <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 9; Translation of the Rigveda, 4, 334.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201461">↑</a> Bezzenberger's <i>Beitrāge,</i> 20, 40.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201461">↑</a> Rv. iv. 2, 17. In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 1, 3. 5, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्">ayas</a></b> is smelted from the ore (<i>aśman</i>), and gold from the <a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्">ayas</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201461">↑</a> Rv. ix. 112, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201461">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <b>Īṣu.</b></li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201461">↑</a> Rv. v. 30, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201461">↑</a> Rv. ix. 1, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 252; Schrader, <i>Prchistoric Antiquities,</i> 159.</li></ol></div>

ध्राजि|ధ్రాజి|ಧ್ರಾಜಿ|dhrāji|dhraaji|_vei_120@1462|_vei1_pn405
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1461">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1463">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn404">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn405">1:405</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn406">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhrāji</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201462"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201462">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201462"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201462">2</a></sup> denotes the ‘sweep’ of the wind, referring no doubt to the violent gales which often blow in India devastating the forests, and which figure in the descriptions of the Maruts, or storm gods.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201462"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201462">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201462"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201462">↑</a> i. 164. 44; x. 97, 13; 136, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201462"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201462">↑</a> Av. iii. 1, 5; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 2, 17; iv. 9, 5 <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, i 11, 19, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201462"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201462">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> xxxii. xxiii <i>et seq.;</i> Macdonell. <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 79.</li></ol></div>

ध्रुव|ధ్రువ|ಧ್ರುವ|dhruva|_vei_120@1463|_vei1_pn405
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1462">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1464">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn404">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn405">1:405</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn406">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhruva</b> in the Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201463">1</a></sup> denotes the pole star, being mentioned in connexion with the marriage ritual, in which the star is pointed out to the bride as an emblem of constancy. In the  Maitrāyaṇī <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201463">2</a></sup> a late work, the movement of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ध्रुव">dhruva</a> (<i>dhruvasya pracalanam</i>) is mentioned, but this can hardly be interpreted as referring to an actual observed motion of the nominal pole star,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201463">3</a></sup> but rather to an extraordinary event, such as a destruction of the world, as Cowell understood the expression.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201463">4</a></sup> Jacobi<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201463">5</a></sup> sees in the motion of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ध्रुव">dhruva</a> the possibility of fixing a date, on the ground that the only star which could have been deemed a pole star, as ‘immovable,’ was one (<i>a</i> Draconis) of the third millenium B.C. But this attempt to extract chronology from the name of the star is of very doubtful validity.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201463">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201463">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 7, 22; Sāṅkhāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 17, 2 <i>et seq.;</i> Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 3, 6, etc. It is to be noted that the marriage Mantras, of which we have a great many, do not include any reference to the <a class="vei_120a" href="ध्रुव">dhruva</a>; but it is not possible to say definitely whether the practice is really an old one or not</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201463">↑</a> See Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 15, 289; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 2, 396.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201463">↑</a> As understood by Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 98, n. 103; Bühler, <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 23, 245, n. 21; Jacobi, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 49, 228, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201463">↑</a> In his edition of the <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, p. 244.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201463">↑</a> <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 23, 157; <i>Zeitschrift, loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 50, 69 <i>et seq.; Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1909, 721 <i>et seq.;</i> 1910, 461 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201463">↑</a> Whitney, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, xc; Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1909, 1102; 1910, 465 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

ध्रुवा|ధ్రువా|ಧ್ರುವಾ|dhruvā|dhruvaa|_vei_120@1464|_vei1_pn406
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1463">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1465">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn405">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn406">1:406</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn407">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhruvā,</b> ‘fixed,’ as an epithet of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दिश्">diś</a>,</b> ‘cardinal point,’ denotes the ground under one's feet.</p><p>1) Av. iii. 27, 5; xii. 3, 59; xv. 6, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 14 (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a></b>); Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 9, 25, etc.</p></div>

ध्वज|ధ్వజ|ಧ್ವಜ|dhvaja|_vei_120@1465|_vei1_pn406
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1464">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1466">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn405">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn406">1:406</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn407">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhvaja</b> occurs twice in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201465"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201465">1</a></sup> in the sense of ‘banner’ used in battle. It is characteristic of Vedic fighting that in both passages reference is made to arrows being discharged and falling on the banners.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201465"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201465">↑</a> vii. 85, 2; x. 103, 11. In Epic warfare banners are of vast importance -<i>e.g.,</i> Rāmāyaṇa, ii. 67, 26; they were attached to a pole on the chariot, Mahabhārata, vii. 3332, etc. The army was called <i>dhvajinī,</i> ‘bannered host,’ <i>ibid.,</i> i. 2875, etc.</li></ol></div>

ध्वन्य|ధ్వన్య|ಧ್ವನ್ಯ|dhvanya|_vei_120@1466|_vei1_pn406
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1465">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1467">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn405">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn406">1:406</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn407">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhvanya</b> is apparently the name of a patron, son of <b>Lakṣmaṇa,</b> in a hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201466"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201466">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201466"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201466">↑</a> v. 33, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 155.</li></ol></div>

ध्वसन् द्वैत-वन|ध्वसन् द्वैतवन|ध्वसन् द्वैत|वन|ధ్వసన్ ద్వైత-వన|ధ్వసన్ ద్వైతవన|ధ్వసన్ ద్వైత|వన|ಧ್ವಸನ್ ದ್ವೈತ-ವನ|ಧ್ವಸನ್ ದ್ವೈತವನ|ಧ್ವಸನ್ ದ್ವೈತ|ವನ|dhvasan dvaita-vana|dhvasan dvaitavana|dhvasan dvaita|vana|_vei_120@1467|_vei1_pn407
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1466">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1468">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn406">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn407">1:407</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn408">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhvasan <a class="vei_120a" href="द्वैत">dvaita</a>-vana</b> (‘descendant of Dvitavana’) is the name in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201467">1</a></sup> of the king of the <b>Matsyas</b> who celebrated an <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or ‘horse sacrifice,’ near the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201467">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 211; <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 6.</li></ol></div>

ध्वसन्ति|ధ్వసన్తి|ಧ್ವಸನ್ತಿ|dhvasanti|_vei_120@1468|_vei1_pn407
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1467">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1469">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn406">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn407">1:407</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn408">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhvasanti</b> is in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201468">1</a></sup> mentioned together with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुषन्ति">puruṣanti</a></b> as having been aided by the Aśvins. There can be no doubt that this is the longer form of the name <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ध्वस्र">dhvasra</a>,</b> which is found with <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुषन्ति">puruṣanti</a> both in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201468">2</a></sup> and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201468">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201468">↑</a> i. 112, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201468">↑</a> ix. 58. 3 = Sv. ii. 409.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201468">↑</a> xiii. 7, 12 (where the dual of <a class="vei_120a" href="ध्वस्र">dhvasra</a> appears as a feminine <i>Dhvasre</i>).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Sieg. <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 62, 63; Benfey, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>,</i> 105, 126, who is inclined to think that <a class="vei_120a" href="ध्वसन्ति">dhvasanti</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुषन्ति">puruṣanti</a> are names of women.</li></ol></div>

ध्वस्र|ధ్వస్ర|ಧ್ವಸ್ರ|dhvasra|_vei_120@1469|_vei1_pn407
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1468">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1470">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn406">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn407">1:407</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn408">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhvasra</b> is named with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुषन्ति">puruṣanti</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201469">1</a></sup> as giving gifts to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तरन्त">taranta</a></b> and <b>Purumīḍha.</b> These two, being kings, could not<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201469">2</a></sup> properly accept gifts which Brāhmaṇas alone could accept, but by becoming authors of a verse of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201469">3</a></sup> they qualified themselves to accept them. The verse mentions the names in the dual as <i>Dhvasrayoḥ Puruṣantyoḥ,</i> ‘from the two, <a class="vei_120a" href="ध्वस्र">dhvasra</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुषन्ति">puruṣanti</a>.’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201469">4</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201469">5</a></sup> the names occur in the dual as <i>Dhvasre Puruṣantī,</i> a reading which is confirmed by the <a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201469">6</a></sup> The former is necessarily a feminine form, though Sāyaṇa, in his comment on the passage, explains it as really an irregular masculine. According to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201469">7</a></sup> the feminine is a corruption based on the dual form in the verse of the Rigveda mentioned above; but the names may be those of women,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201469">8</a></sup> as Benfey<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201469">9</a></sup>  inclines to believe. Weber<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201469">10</a></sup> suggests that the two were demons, but this is, as Sieg<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201469">11</a></sup> shows, quite unnecessary. <a class="vei_120a" href="ध्वस्र">dhvasra</a> is no doubt identical with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ध्वसन्ति">dhvasanti</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201469">↑</a> xiii. 7, 12. <i>Cf.</i> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 139; Sāṭyāyanaka, <i>apud</i> Sāyaṇa, on Rv. ix. 58, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201469">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Śāstra, x. 75, 77.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201469">↑</a> ix. 58, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201469">↑</a> Both words are in the dual, as if they were members of a Dvandva compound. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> 261.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201469">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201469">↑</a> ix. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201469">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. <a class="vei_120a" href="ध्वस्र">dhvasra</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201469">↑</a> The first would in that case be Dhvasrā.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201469">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>,</i> 105. 126, under <a class="vei_120a" href="ध्वसन्ति">dhvasanti</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुषन्ति">puruṣanti</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201469">↑</a> <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 27, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201469">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 62, 63.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 139; Oertel, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 39; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 360, points out that the sense of the Rīgveda passage is quite uncertain, and that the two, <a class="vei_120a" href="तरन्त">taranta</a> and Purumīḷha, as they appear in Rv. v. 61, are rather donors than receivers (see, however, verse 9, <i>Purumīṭhāya viprāva</i>). See also Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenlāndischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 232; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noteu,</i> 1, 354.</li></ol></div>

ध्वांक्ष|ध्वाङ्क्ष|ధ్వాంక్ష|ధ్వాఙ్క్ష|ಧ್ವಾಂಕ್ಷ|ಧ್ವಾಙ್ಕ್ಷ|dhvāṅkṣa|dhvaaMkSha|dhvaankSha|dhvaaMxa|_vei_120@1470|_vei1_pn408
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1469">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1471">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn407">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn408">1:408</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn409">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhvāṅkṣa,</b> ‘crow,’ is mentioned twice in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201470"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201470">1</a></sup> and in the Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201470"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201470">2</a></sup> Possibly the same bird is meant by the words <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धुंक्षा">dhuṅkṣā</a></b> and <b>Dhūṅksnā.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201470"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201470">↑</a> xi. 9, 9; xii. 4, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201470"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201470">↑</a> Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> xxv. 6, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 88.</li></ol></div>

ध्वान्त|ధ్వాన్త|ಧ್ವಾನ್ತ|dhvānta|dhvaanta|_vei_120@1471|_vei1_pn408
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1470">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1472">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn407">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn408">1:408</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn409">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dhvānta</b> is the name of some wind in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201471"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201471">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201471"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201471">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201471"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201471">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 7, 7, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxix. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201471"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201471">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 16, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, iv. 24, 1; 25, 1.</li></ol></div>

नकुल|నకుల|ನಕುಲ|nakula|_vei_120@1472|_vei1_pn408
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1471">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1473">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn407">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn408">1:408</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn409">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nakula,</b> ‘ichneumon,’ is mentioned in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201472"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201472">1</a></sup> as being able to cut a snake in two and then join it up again. Its knowledge<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201472"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201472">2</a></sup> of a remedy against snake poison is also mentioned. The animal figures in the list of sacrificial victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or ‘horse sacrifice,’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201472"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201472">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201472"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201472">↑</a> vi. 139, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201472"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201472">↑</a> Av. viii. 7, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201472"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201472">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 12, 1; 21, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 26. 2; Maītrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 7 In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 3, 18, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a> Prātiśākhya, xvii. 9, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नकुल">nakula</a></i> denotes a colour--no doubt that of the ichneumon.</li></ol></div>

नक्त|నక్త|ನಕ್ತ|nakta|_vei_120@1473|_vei1_pn409
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1472">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1474">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn408">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn409">1:409</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn415">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nakta,</b> ‘night,’ is found frequently in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201473"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201473">1</a></sup> and sometimes later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201473"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201473">2</a></sup> usually in the adverbial form <i>naktaṃ,</i> ‘by night’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201473"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201473">↑</a> i. 13, 7; 73, 7; 96, 5; vii. 2, 6; x. 70, 6; adverbially, i. 24, 10; 90, 7; v. 76, 3; vii. 15, 15; 104, 17; viii. 96, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201473"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201473">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, viii. 4, 2, adverbially, Av. vi. 128, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 4, 2; xiii. 1, 5, 5, etc.</li></ol></div>

नक्षत्र|నక్షత్ర|ನಕ್ಷತ್ರ|nakṣatra|nakShatra|naxatra|_vei_120@1474|_vei1_pn409
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1473">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1475">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn408">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn409">1:409</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn415">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nakṣatra</b> is a word of obscure origin and derivation. The Indian interpreters already show a great divergence of opinion as to its primary meaning. The Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201474">1</a></sup> resolves it into <i>na-<a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्र">kṣatra</a></i> (‘no power’), explaining it by a legend. The <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201474">2</a></sup> refers it to the root <i>nakṣ,</i> ‘obtain,’ following the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201474">3</a></sup> Aufrecht<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201474">4</a></sup> and Weber<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201474">5</a></sup> derived it from <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्त">nakta</a>-tra,</i> ‘guardian of night,’ and more recently<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201474">6</a></sup> the derivation from <i>nak-<a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्र">kṣatra</a>,</i> ‘having rule over night,’ seems to be gaining acceptance. The generic meaning of the word therefore seems to be ‘star.’</p><p><i>The Nakṣatras as Stars in the Rigveda and Later.</i>--The sense of ‘star’ appears to be adequate for all or nearly all the passages in which <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> occurs in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201474">7</a></sup> The same sense occurs in the later Saṃhitās also: the sun and the Nakṣatras are mentioned together,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201474">8</a></sup> or the sun, the moon, and the Nakṣatras,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201474">9</a></sup> or the moon and the Nakṣatras,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201474">10</a></sup> or the Nakṣatras alone;<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201474">11</a></sup>  but there is no necessity to attribute to the word the sense of ‘lunar mansion’ in these passages.</p><p>On the other hand, the names of at least three of the Nakṣatras in the later sense occur in the Rigveda. <a class="vei_120a" href="तिष्य">tiṣya</a>,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201474">12</a></sup> however, does not seem to be mentioned as a lunar mansion. With <b>Aghās</b> (plur.) and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अर्जुनी">arjunī</a></b> (dual)<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201474">13</a></sup> the case is different: it seems probable that they are the later lunar mansions called <a class="vei_120a" href="मघास्">maghās</a> (plur.) and <a class="vei_120a" href="फल्गुनी">phalgunī</a> (dual). The names appear to have been deliberately changed in the Rigveda, and it must be remembered that the hymn in which they occur, the wedding hymn of Sūryā, has no claim to great age.<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201474">14</a></sup> Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201474">15</a></sup> and Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1201474">16</a></sup> have seen other references to the Nakṣatras as 27 in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1201474">17</a></sup> but these seem most improbable. Nor do the adjectives <i><a class="vei_120a" href="रेवती">revatī</a></i> (‘rich’) and <i>punarvasā</i> (‘bringing wealth again’) in another hymn<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1201474">18</a></sup> appear to refer to the Nakṣatras.</p><p><i>The Nakṣatras as Lunar Mansions.</i>--In several passages of the later Saṃhitās the connexion of the moon and the Nakṣatras is conceived of as a marriage union. Thus in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a><sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1201474">19</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1201474">20</a></sup> it is expressly stated that <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> was wedded to the mansions, but dwelt only with <a class="vei_120a" href="रोहिणी">rohiṇī</a>; the others being angry, he had ultimately to undertake to live with them all equally. Weber<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1201474">21</a></sup> hence deduced that the Nakṣatras were regarded as of equal extent, but this is to press the texts unduly, except in the sense of approximate equality. The number of the mansions is not stated as 27 in the story told in the two Saṃhitās: the Taittīriya has 33, and the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> no  number; but 27 appears as their number in the list which is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1201474">22</a></sup> and elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1201474">23</a></sup> The number 28 is much less well attested: in one passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1201474">24</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="अभिजित्">abhijit</a> is practically marked as a new comer, though in a later book,<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1201474">25</a></sup> in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_1201474">26</a></sup> and in the Atharvaveda list,<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_1201474">27</a></sup> it has found acceptance. It is perfectly possible that 28 is the earlier number, and that <a class="vei_120a" href="अभिजित्">abhijit</a> dropped out because it was faint, or too far north, or because 27 was a more mystic (3×3×3) number: it is significant that the Chinese Sieou and the Arabic Manāzil are 28 in number.<sup id="ref_120_28_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_1201474">28</a></sup> Weber,<sup id="ref_120_29_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29_vei_1201474">29</a></sup> however, believes that 27 is the older number in India.</p><p>The meaning of the number is easily explained when it is remembered that a periodic month occupies something between 27 and 28 days, more nearly the former number. Such a month is in fact recognized in the Lāṭyāyana<sup id="ref_120_30_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_30_vei_1201474">30</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a> Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_31_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_31_vei_1201474">31</a></sup> as consisting of 27 days, 12 months making a year of 324 days, a <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> year, or with an intercalary month, a year of 351 days. The <a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_32_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_32_vei_1201474">32</a></sup> makes an attempt to introduce the <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> reckoning into the civil or solar (<i>sāvana</i>) year of 360 days, for it holds that the sun spends 13(1/3) days in each <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> (13(1/3)×27 = 360). But the month of 27 or 28 days plays no part in the chronological calculations of the <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_33_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_33_vei_1201474">33</a></sup></p><p><i>The Names of the Nakṣatras.</i>--In addition to the two mentioned in the Rigveda, the earlier Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_34_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_34_vei_1201474">34</a></sup> gives the  names of <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्येष्ठघ्नी">jyeṣṭhaghnī</a><sup id="ref_120_35_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_35_vei_1201474">35</a></sup> (the later <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्येष्ठा">jyeṣṭhā</a>) and <a class="vei_120a" href="विचृतौ">vicṛtau</a>,<sup id="ref_120_36_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_36_vei_1201474">36</a></sup> which are mentioned as in close connexion, and of Revatīs (plural) and <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्तिकास्">kṛttikās</a>.<sup id="ref_120_37_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_37_vei_1201474">37</a></sup> With reference to possible times for the ceremony of the Agnyādhāna, or ‘laying of the sacred fires,’ the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_38_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_38_vei_1201474">38</a></sup> the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_39_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_39_vei_1201474">39</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_40_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_40_vei_1201474">40</a></sup> mention the Nakṣatras called <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्तिकास्">kṛttikās</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="रोहिणी">rohiṇī</a>, Phalgunyas, <a class="vei_120a" href="हस्त">hasta</a>; the latter <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> adds <a class="vei_120a" href="पुनर्वसू">punarvasū</a>, and in an additional remark<sup id="ref_120_41_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_41_vei_1201474">41</a></sup> excludes Pūrve <a class="vei_120a" href="फल्गुनी">phalgunī</a> in favour of Uttare <a class="vei_120a" href="फल्गुनी">phalgunī</a>. The Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_42_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_42_vei_1201474">42</a></sup> adds <a class="vei_120a" href="मृगशीर्ष">mṛgaśīrṣa</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्रा">citrā</a> as possibilities. On the other hand, <a class="vei_120a" href="पुनर्वसू">punarvasū</a> is recommended by all authorities<sup id="ref_120_43_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_43_vei_1201474">43</a></sup> as suitable for the Punarādheya, ‘relaying of the sacred fires,’ which takes place if the first fire has failed to effect the aim of its existence, the prosperity of the sacrificer.<sup id="ref_120_44_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_44_vei_1201474">44</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_45_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_45_vei_1201474">45</a></sup> however, allows Anurādhās also.</p><p>In the ceremony of the Agnicayana, or ‘piling of the firealtar,’ the bricks are assumed to be equal in number to the Nakṣatras. The bricks number 756, and they are equated to 27 Nakṣatras multiplied by 27 secondary Nakṣatras, reckoned as 720 (instead of 729), with the addition of 36 days, the length of an intercalary month. Nothing can be usefully derived from this piece of priestly nonsense.<sup id="ref_120_46_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_46_vei_1201474">46</a></sup> But in connexion with this ceremony the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_47_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_47_vei_1201474">47</a></sup> enumerate the 27  Nakṣatras, and these lists<sup id="ref_120_48_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_48_vei_1201474">48</a></sup> may be given <i>in extenso</i> as follows:<table><tr><td><i><a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā.</i> </td><td ><i>Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhita.</i> </td><td ><i><a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.</i></td></tr><tr><td>1. <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्तिकास्">kṛttikās</a> (fem. plur.)</td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्तिकास्">kṛttikās</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्तिकास्">kṛttikās</a></td></tr><tr><td>2. <a class="vei_120a" href="रोहिणी">rohiṇī</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="रोहिणी">rohiṇī</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="रोहिणी">rohiṇī</a></td></tr><tr><td>3. <a class="vei_120a" href="मृगशीर्ष">mṛgaśīrṣa</a> (neut.)</td><td >Invagā</td><td >Invakā</td></tr><tr><td>4. <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्द्रा">ārdrā</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="बाहु">bāhu</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="बाहु">bāhu</a></td></tr><tr><td>5. <a class="vei_120a" href="पुनर्वसू">punarvasū</a> (dual)</td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="पुनर्वसु">punarvasu</a> (sing.)</td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="पुनर्वसु">punarvasu</a></td></tr><tr><td>6. <a class="vei_120a" href="तिष्य">tiṣya</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="तिष्य">tiṣya</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="तिष्य">tiṣya</a></td></tr><tr><td>7. <a class="vei_120a" href="आश्रेषास्">āśreṣās</a> (fem. plur.)</td><td >Āśleṣās(plur.; <a class="vei_120a" href="पद">pada</a> Aśleṣā) </td><td >Āśleṣās (or Aśleṣās)</td></tr><tr><td>8. <a class="vei_120a" href="मघास्">maghās</a> (fem. plur.)</td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="मघास्">maghās</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="मघास्">maghās</a></td></tr><tr><td>9. <a class="vei_120a" href="फल्गुनी">phalgunī</a> (fem. dual)</td><td >Phalgunīs (plur.)</td><td >Phalgunis</td></tr><tr><td>10. <a class="vei_120a" href="फल्गुनी">phalgunī</a> (fem. dual)</td><td >Phalgunīs (plur.)</td><td >Uttarāḥa Phalgunīs</td></tr><tr><td>11. <a class="vei_120a" href="हस्त">hasta</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="हस्त">hasta</a></td><td >Hastau (dual)</td></tr><tr><td>12. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्रा">citrā</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="चित्रा">citrā</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="चित्रा">citrā</a></td></tr><tr><td>13. <a class="vei_120a" href="स्वाती">svātī</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="निष्ट्य">niṣṭya</a> (neut.)</td><td >Niṣṭyā</td></tr><tr><td>14. <a class="vei_120a" href="विशाखे">viśākhe</a> (fem. dual)</td><td >Viśākha (neut. sing.)</td><td >Viśākhā (fem. sing.)</td></tr><tr><td>15. <a class="vei_120a" href="अनूराधास्">anūrādhās</a> (plur.)</td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="अनूराधा">anūrādhā</a> (<a class="vei_120a" href="पद">pada</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="अनु">anu</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="अनूराधास्">anūrādhās</a> (masc. <a class="vei_120a" href="राधा">rādhā</a>) </td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="अनूराधास्">anūrādhās</a> (masc. plur.)</td></tr><tr><td>16. <a class="vei_120a" href="रोहिणी">rohiṇī</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="ज्येष्ठा">jyeṣṭhā</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="ज्येष्ठा">jyeṣṭhā</a></td></tr><tr><td>17. <a class="vei_120a" href="विचृतौ">vicṛtau</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="मूल">mūla</a> (neut.)</td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="मूल">mūla</a></td></tr><tr><td>18. Aṣāḍhās (fem. plur.)</td><td >Aṣāḍhās</td><td >Aṣāḍhās</td></tr><tr><td>19. Aṣāḍhās (fem. plur.)</td><td >Aṣāḍhās</td><td >Uttarā Aṣāḍhās</td></tr><tr><td>20. </td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="अभिजित्">abhijit</a></td></tr><tr><td>21. <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रोणा">śroṇā</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="श्रोणा">śroṇā</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वत्थ">aśvattha</a></td></tr><tr><td>22. <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रविष्ठास्">śraviṣṭhās</a> (plur.)</td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="श्रविष्ठास्">śraviṣṭhās</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="श्रविष्ठास्">śraviṣṭhās</a></td></tr><tr><td>23. <a class="vei_120a" href="शतभिषज्">śatabhiṣaj</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="शतभिषज्">śatabhiṣaj</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="शतभिषज्">śatabhiṣaj</a></td></tr><tr><td>24. Proṣṭhapadās (masc. plur.) </td><td >Proṣṭhapadās</td><td >Proṣṭhapadās</td></tr><tr><td>25. Proṣṭhapadās(masc. plur.) </td><td >Proṣṭhapadās</td><td >Uttare Proṣṭhapadās</td></tr><tr><td>26. <a class="vei_120a" href="रेवती">revatī</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="रेवती">revatī</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="रेवती">revatī</a></td></tr><tr><td>27. <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वयुजौ">aśvayujau</a> (dual)</td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वयुजौ">aśvayujau</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वयुजौ">aśvayujau</a></td></tr><tr><td>28. <a class="vei_120a" href="अपभरणीस्">apabharaṇīs</a> (fem. plur.) </td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="भरणीस्">bharaṇīs</a></td><td ><a class="vei_120a" href="अपभरणीस्">apabharaṇīs</a></td></tr></table><p>The <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Brahmaṇa<sup id="ref_120_49_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_49_vei_1201474">49</a></sup> has a list of the Nakṣatras which agrees generally with the list of the Saṃhitās. It runs as follows: <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्तिकास्">kṛttikās</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="रोहिणी">rohiṇī</a>, Invakās, Bāhū (dual), <a class="vei_120a" href="तिष्य">tiṣya</a>, Āśleṣās, <a class="vei_120a" href="मघास्">maghās</a>, Pūrve <a class="vei_120a" href="फल्गुनी">phalgunī</a>, Uttare <a class="vei_120a" href="फल्गुनी">phalgunī</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="हस्त">hasta</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्रा">citrā</a>, Niṣṭyā, <a class="vei_120a" href="विशाखे">viśākhe</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अनूराधास्">anūrādhās</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="रोहिणी">rohiṇī</a>, Mūlabarhaṇī, Pūrvā Aṣāḍhās, Uttarā Aṣāḍhās, <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रोणा">śroṇā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रविष्ठास्">śraviṣṭhās</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शतभिषज्">śatabhiṣaj</a>, Pūrve Proṣṭhapadās, Uttare Proṣṭhapadās, <a class="vei_120a" href="रेवती">revatī</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वयुजौ">aśvayujau</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अपभरणीस्">apabharaṇīs</a>. In a later book,<sup id="ref_120_50_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_50_vei_1201474">50</a></sup> however, the list grows to 28,  and the full moon is inserted after number 14, and the new moon after number 28, as an attempt to bring the Nākṣatra (lunar) month into accordance with the Sāvana (solar) month of 30 days. The names in this second list are as in the Saṃhitās with the following exceptions. The seven stars of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्तिकास्">kṛttikās</a> are named as Ambā, <a class="vei_120a" href="दुला">dulā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="नितत्नी">nitatnī</a>, Abhrayantī, Meghayantī, Varṣayantī, Cupuṇīkā, names found also in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a><sup id="ref_120_51_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_51_vei_1201474">51</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_52_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_52_vei_1201474">52</a></sup> Beside <a class="vei_120a" href="मृगशीर्ष">mṛgaśīrṣa</a>, Invakās are also mentioned.<sup id="ref_120_53_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_53_vei_1201474">53</a></sup> Then come <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्द्रा">ārdrā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पुनर्वसू">punarvasū</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="तिष्य">tiṣya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="आश्रेषास्">āśreṣās</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मघास्">maghās</a> (beside which Anaghās, Agadās, and Arundhatīs are also mentioned), Phalgunyas (but elsewhere in the dual, Phalgunyau),<sup id="ref_120_54_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_54_vei_1201474">54</a></sup> Phalgunyas, <a class="vei_120a" href="हस्त">hasta</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्रा">citrā</a>, Niṣṭyā, <a class="vei_120a" href="विशाखे">viśākhe</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अनूराधास्">anūrādhās</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्येष्ठा">jyeṣṭhā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मूल">mūla</a>, Aṣāḍhās, Aṣāḍhās, <a class="vei_120a" href="अभिजित्">abhijit</a>, Śronā, <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रविष्ठास्">śraviṣṭhās</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शतभिषज्">śatabhiṣaj</a>, Proṣṭhapadās, Proṣṭhapadās, <a class="vei_120a" href="रेवती">revatī</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वयुजौ">aśvayujau</a>, Bharaṇyas, but also <a class="vei_120a" href="अपभरणीस्">apabharaṇīs</a>.<sup id="ref_120_55_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_55_vei_1201474">55</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="अभिजित्">abhijit</a>, which occurs also in an earlier part of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_56_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_56_vei_1201474">56</a></sup> is perhaps interpolated. But Weber's<sup id="ref_120_57_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_57_vei_1201474">57</a></sup> argument that <a class="vei_120a" href="अभिजित्">abhijit</a> is out of place in this list because <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> is here mentioned as the 28th <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>, loses some force from the fact (of course unknown to him) that the list in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_58_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_58_vei_1201474">58</a></sup> contains 28 Nakṣatras, including <a class="vei_120a" href="अभिजित्">abhijit</a>, and adds <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> at the end as another.</p><p>In another passage<sup id="ref_120_59_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_59_vei_1201474">59</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> divides the Nakṣatras into two sets, the <a class="vei_120a" href="देव">deva</a> Nakṣatras and the <a class="vei_120a" href="यम">yama</a> Nakṣatras, being 1-14 and 15-27 (with the omission of <a class="vei_120a" href="अभिजित्">abhijit</a>) respectively. This division corresponds with one in the third book of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_60_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_60_vei_1201474">60</a></sup> where the days of the light half of the month and those of the dark half are equated with the Nakṣatras. The <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> treats the former series as south, the latter as north; but this has no relation to facts, and can only be regarded as a ritual absurdity.</p><p>The late nineteenth book of the Atharvaveda contains a list<sup id="ref_120_61_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_61_vei_1201474">61</a></sup> of the Nakṣatras, including <a class="vei_120a" href="अभिजित्">abhijit</a>. The names here  given are: <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्तिकास्">kṛttikās</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="रोहिणी">rohiṇī</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मृगशिरस्">mṛgaśiras</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्द्रा">ārdrā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पुनर्वसू">punarvasū</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पुष्य">puṣya</a>, Āśleṣās, <a class="vei_120a" href="मघास्">maghās</a>, Pūrvā Phalgunyau (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सिच्">sic</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_62_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_62_vei_1201474">62</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="हस्त">hasta</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्रा">citrā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="स्वाति">svāti</a> (masc.),<sup id="ref_120_63_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_63_vei_1201474">63</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="विशाखे">viśākhe</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अनुराधा">anurādhā</a>,<sup id="ref_120_64_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_64_vei_1201474">64</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्येष्ठा">jyeṣṭhā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मूल">mūla</a>, Pūrvā Aṣāḍhās,<sup id="ref_120_65_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_65_vei_1201474">65</a></sup> Uttarā Aṣāḍhās, <a class="vei_120a" href="अभिजित्">abhijit</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रवण">śravaṇa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रविष्ठास्">śraviṣṭhās</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शतभिषज्">śatabhiṣaj</a>, Dvayā Proṣṭhapadā, <a class="vei_120a" href="रेवती">revatī</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वयुजौ">aśvayujau</a>, Bharaṇyas.</p><p><i>The Position of the Nakṣatras.</i>--There is nothing definite in Vedic literature regarding the position of most of the Nakṣatras, but the later astronomy precisely locates all of them, and its statements agree on the whole satisfactorily with what is said in the earlier texts, though Weber<sup id="ref_120_66_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_66_vei_1201474">66</a></sup> was inclined to doubt this. The determinations adopted below are due to Whitney<sup id="ref_120_67_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_67_vei_1201474">67</a></sup> in his notes on the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूर्य">sūrya</a> Siddhānta.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201474">↑</a> ii. 1, 2, 18. 19. <i>Cf.</i> a citation in <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201474">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> and <i>of.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201474">↑</a> i. 5, 2, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201474">↑</a> Kuhn's <i>Zeitschrift,</i> 8, 71, 72. So Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 288, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201474">↑</a> <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 268.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201474">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> p. 74. line 8.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201474">↑</a> See i. 50, 2; vii. 86, 1; x. 68, 11; 111, 7; used of the sun itself, vi. 67, 6 (as masculine); vii. 81, 2; x. 88, 13. The sun is allied with them, iii. 54, 19. <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="शवस्">śavas</a>,</i> ‘equalling the multitude of the stars,’ is used as an epithet in x. 21, 10. Even in x. 85, 2, where <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>, on the lap of the Nakṣatras, is mentioned, ‘stars’ would do; but, as this hymn refers to two of the later Nakṣatras, ‘lunar mansions’ may well be meant.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201474">↑</a> Av. vi. 10, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 43; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 1, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, iv. 10, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201474">↑</a> Av. vi. 128, 3; xv. 6, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 13, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxii. 29, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201474">↑</a> Av. v. 24, 10; vi. 86, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 5, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxv. 15; xxxvii. 12; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 21; xxxix. 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201474">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 2, 2, 2; ii 6, 2, 6, etc; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 21 etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, v. 5, and very often elsewhere.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201474">↑</a> Rv. v. 59, 13; x. 64, 8; Weber, 2, 290.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201474">↑</a> x. 85, 13; Weber, 364-367, and see references under <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अघा">aghā</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अर्जुनी">arjunī</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201474">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Arnold, <i>Vedic Metre,</i> 322.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201474">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 184 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1201474">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 354. <i>Cf.</i> Tilak, <i>Orion,</i> 158.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1201474">↑</a> i. 162, 18 (the 34 ribs of the horse = moon, sun, 5 planets, 27 Nakṣatras); x. 55, 3 (34 lights).</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1201474">↑</a> x. 19, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1201474">↑</a> xi. 3 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 467).</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1201474">↑</a> ii. 3, 5, 1-3. <i>Cf.</i> also iii. 4, 7. 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xviii. 14; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 40; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 4, 1, 9; Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 12. The dwelling of the moon in a <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> is mentioned, Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 5, 4, 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 21; a <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> in <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, 135; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 11, 6; v. 12, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1201474">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 277. <i>Cf.</i> the later system of the Siddhāntas, Whitney, <i>Oriental and Linguistic Studies,</i> 2, 372, and see Tilak, <i>Orion,</i> 33 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1201474">↑</a> iv. 4, 10, 1-3.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1201474">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxix. 13, but Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 13, 20, has 28; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 1, 1-5, in lists of Nakṣatras. See also Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ix, 7; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 5, 4, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxiii, 23; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1; Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, ii. 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 1, 2, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्योतिष">jyotiṣa</a>, 18. 20 (verse 34 has 28, but it is interpolated); <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiv. 78, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1201474">↑</a> i. 5, 2, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, 1, 360, n.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1201474">↑</a> iii. 1, 2, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_1201474">↑</a> ii. 13, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_1201474">↑</a> xix. 7, 1; 8, 1 = Nakṣatrakalpa, 10. 26. So in <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 26.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_1201474">↑</a> Whitney, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 409-411; <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 8, 390.</li><li id="fx_120_29_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29_vei_1201474">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 280; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 446; 10, 223, 224, 226, 227.</li><li id="fx_120_30_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_30_vei_1201474">↑</a> iv. 8, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_31_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_31_vei_1201474">↑</a> v. 11. 12. See Weber, 2, 281288.</li><li id="fx_120_32_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_32_vei_1201474">↑</a> Thibaut, <i>Astronomic, Astrologie und Mathematik,</i> 7.</li><li id="fx_120_33_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_33_vei_1201474">↑</a> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_34_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_34_vei_1201474">↑</a> <i>l. e.,</i> books i. xvi.</li><li id="fx_120_35_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_35_vei_1201474">↑</a> vi. 110, 2. This constellation, ‘the slayer of the oldest,’ was apparently of evil omen. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 2, 8. Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 361, equates it with Antares or Cor Scorpionis, with or without , Scorpionis (Jyaīṣṭhaghnī is a misreading in the edition of Whitney and Roth).</li><li id="fx_120_36_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_36_vei_1201474">↑</a> vi. 110, 2. It is also mentioned in ii. 8, 1; iii. 7, 4; vi. 121, 3. It is identified by the commentators with <a class="vei_120a" href="मूल">mūla</a>, ‘the root,’ the two stars,  and Scorpionis, which form the sting of the Scorpion's tail; Whitney, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 48.</li><li id="fx_120_37_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_37_vei_1201474">↑</a> ix. 7, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_38_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_38_vei_1201474">↑</a> viii. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_39_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_39_vei_1201474">↑</a> i. 6, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_40_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_40_vei_1201474">↑</a> i. 1, 2, 1-6.</li><li id="fx_120_41_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_41_vei_1201474">↑</a> i. 1, 2, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_42_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_42_vei_1201474">↑</a> ii. 1, 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_43_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_43_vei_1201474">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 5, 1, 4; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 7, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, viii. 15; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 2, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_44_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_44_vei_1201474">↑</a> Hillebrandt, <i>Rituallitteratur,</i> 109.</li><li id="fx_120_45_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_45_vei_1201474">↑</a> viii. 15; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā. i. 7, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_46_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_46_vei_1201474">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 5, 4, 5. See Weber, 2, 298, with whom Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 42, 383, n. 1, concurs. For a wild speculation, see Shamasastry, <i>Gavam <a class="vei_120a" href="अयन">ayana</a>,</i> 122 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_47_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_47_vei_1201474">↑</a> Taittiriya Samhitā, iv. 4, 10. 1-3; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 13, 20; Kātnaka Saṃhitā, xxxix. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_48_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_48_vei_1201474">↑</a> The forms and genders are given as accepted by Weber, 2, 300. The latter depend on references to the names of the Nakṣatras in other passages in some cases--<i>e.g., Anūrādheṣu,</i> in <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a>, viii. 15, shows that the name is a masculine in that Saṃhitā.</li><li id="fx_120_49_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_49_vei_1201474">↑</a> i. 5, 1</li><li id="fx_120_50_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_50_vei_1201474">↑</a> iii. 1, 4, 1 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> iii. 1, 1-2.</li><li id="fx_120_51_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_51_vei_1201474">↑</a> iv. 4, 5, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_52_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_52_vei_1201474">↑</a> xl. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_53_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_53_vei_1201474">↑</a> iii. 1, 4, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_54_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_54_vei_1201474">↑</a> iii. 1, 4, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_55_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_55_vei_1201474">↑</a> iii. 1, 5, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_56_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_56_vei_1201474">↑</a> i. 5, 2, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_57_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_57_vei_1201474">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 305, 306.</li><li id="fx_120_58_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_58_vei_1201474">↑</a> ii. 13, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_59_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_59_vei_1201474">↑</a> i. 5, 2, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Tilak, <i>Orion,</i> 41 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_60_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_60_vei_1201474">↑</a> iii. 1, 2. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 12, with Vināyaka's note.</li><li id="fx_120_61_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_61_vei_1201474">↑</a> xix. 7, 1 <i>et seq.</i> The number is given as 28 in xix. 7, 1 (as emended) and 8, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Lanman's introductory note to the former hymn in Whitney's Translation, 906, 907.</li><li id="fx_120_62_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_62_vei_1201474">↑</a> The reading Pūrvā Phalgunyau must be wrong; perhaps Dvaye (<i>cf.</i> verse 5) or Pūrve should be read. See Lanman in Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 908. The Uttare Phalgunyau are omitted.</li><li id="fx_120_63_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_63_vei_1201474">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="स्वाती">svātī</a> should, no doubt, be read; but for the <a class="vei_120a" href="स्वाति">svāti</a> (<i>sie</i>) of all the manuscripts (Saṃhitā and <a class="vei_120a" href="पद">pada</a>), <i>cf.</i> the <i>navasrakti</i> of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 3, 6, with Keith's note.</li><li id="fx_120_64_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_64_vei_1201474">↑</a> See Lanman in Whitney, 908.</li><li id="fx_120_65_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_65_vei_1201474">↑</a> Lanman, <i>ibid.,</i> 909. reads Pūrvā <a class="vei_120a" href="अषाढा">aṣāḍhā</a> and Uttarā <a class="vei_120a" href="अषाढा">aṣāḍhā</a>; Whitney reads Pūrvā and Uttarā Aṣāḍhās. The manuscripts have Pūrvā and Uttare, which cannot stand.</li><li id="fx_120_66_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_66_vei_1201474">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 367 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_67_vei_1201474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_67_vei_1201474">↑</a> <i>Oriental and Linguis'tic Essays,</i> 2, 350 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

कृत्तिकास्|कृत्तिकास्|कृत्तिका|కృత్తికాస్_౧|కృత్తికాస్|కృత్తికా|ಕೃತ್ತಿಕಾಸ್_೧|ಕೃತ್ತಿಕಾಸ್|ಕೃತ್ತಿಕಾ|kṛttikās_1|kṛttikās|kṛttikā|kRttikaas_1|kRttikaas|kRttikaa|kRittikaas_1|kRittikaas|kRittikaa|kRuttikaas_1|kRuttikaas|kRuttikaa|kRRittikaas_1|kRRittikaas|kRRittikaa|_vei_120@1475|_vei1_pn415
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1474">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1476">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn409">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn415">1:415</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn416">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Kṛttikās</b> are unquestionably  Tauri, etc., the Pleiades. The names of the seven stars forming this constellation, and given above from <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> texts,<sup id="ref_120_68_vei_1201475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_68_vei_1201475">68</a></sup> include three--<i>abhrayantī,</i> ‘forming clouds’; <i>meghayantī,</i> ‘making cloudy’; <i>varṣayantī,</i> ‘causing rain’--which clearly refer to the rainy Pleiades. The word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्तिका">kṛttikā</a></i> possibly means ‘web,’ from the root <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्">kṛt</a>,</i> ‘spin.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="68"><li id="fx_120_68_vei_1201475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_68_vei_1201475">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 4, 5, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xl. 4; Taittīrīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 4, 1.</li></ol></div>

रोहिणी|रोहिणी|रोहिणि|రోహిణీ_౨|రోహిణీ|రోహిణి|ರೋಹಿಣೀ_೨|ರೋಹಿಣೀ|ರೋಹಿಣಿ|rohiṇī_2|rohiṇī|rohiṇi|rohiNii_2|rohiNii|rohiNi|rohiNee_2|rohiNee|_vei_120@1476|_vei1_pn415
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1475">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1477">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn409">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn415">1:415</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn416">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Rohiṇī,</b> ‘ruddy,’ is the name of the conspicuously reddish star, <i>a</i> Tauri or Aldebaran, and denotes the group of the Hyades,  Tauri. Its identification seems absolutely assured by the legend of Prajāpati in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_69_vei_1201476"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_69_vei_1201476">69</a></sup> He is there represented as pursuing his daughter with incestuous intention, and as having been shot with an arrow (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="इषु त्रिकाण्डा">iṣu trikāṇḍā</a>,</b> ‘the belt of Orion’) by the ‘huntsman’ (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="मृगव्याध">mṛgavyādha</a>,</b> ‘Sirius’). Prajāpati is clearly Orion (<a class="vei_120a" href="मृगशिरस्">mṛgaśiras</a> being the name of the little group of stars in Orion's head).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="69"><li id="fx_120_69_vei_1201476"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_69_vei_1201476">↑</a> iii. 33. <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 2, 8; Tilak, <i>Orion,</i> 98 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

मृगशीर्ष|मृगशीर्ष|మృగశీర్ష_౩|మృగశీర్ష|ಮೃಗಶೀರ್ಷ_೩|ಮೃಗಶೀರ್ಷ|mṛgaśīrṣa_3|mṛgaśīrṣa|mRgashiirSha_3|mRgashiirSha|mRgasheerSha_3|mRgasheerSha|mRigashiirSha_3|mRigashiirSha|mRugashiirSha_3|mRugashiirSha|mRRigashiirSha_3|mRRigashiirSha|_vei_120@1477|_vei1_pn415
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1476">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1478">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn409">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn415">1:415</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn416">↷</a><hr><p>3. <b>Mṛgaśīrṣa</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मृगशिरस्">mṛgaśiras</a>,</b> also called Invakā or Invagā,  seems to be the faint stars , Orionis. They are called Andhakā, ‘blind,’ in the Śāntikalpa of the Atharvaveda, probably because of their dimness.<sup id="ref_120_70_vei_1201477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_70_vei_1201477">70</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="70"><li id="fx_120_70_vei_1201477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_70_vei_1201477">↑</a> Whitney, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 401. <i>Cf.</i> Tilak, 102 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

आर्द्रा|आर्द्रा|ఆర్ద్రా_౪|ఆర్ద్రా|ಆರ್ದ್ರಾ_೪|ಆರ್ದ್ರಾ|ārdrā_4|ārdrā|aardraa_4|aardraa|_vei_120@1478|_vei1_pn416
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1477">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1479">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn415">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn416">1:416</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn417">↷</a><hr><p>4. <b>Ārdrā,</b> ‘moist,’ is the name of the brilliant star,  Orionis. But the names by which it is styled, in the plural as Ārdrās in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_71_vei_1201478"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_71_vei_1201478">71</a></sup> and the Nakṣatrakalpa,<sup id="ref_120_72_vei_1201478"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_72_vei_1201478">72</a></sup> and in the dual as Bāhū, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_73_vei_1201478"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_73_vei_1201478">73</a></sup> point to a constellation of two or more stars, and it may be noted<sup id="ref_120_74_vei_1201478"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_74_vei_1201478">74</a></sup> that the corresponding Chinese Sieou includes the seven brilliant stars composing the shoulders, the belt, and the knees of Orion.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="71"><li id="fx_120_71_vei_1201478"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_71_vei_1201478">↑</a> i. 26.</li><li id="fx_120_72_vei_1201478"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_72_vei_1201478">↑</a> 10.</li><li id="fx_120_73_vei_1201478"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_73_vei_1201478">↑</a> i. 5, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_74_vei_1201478"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_74_vei_1201478">↑</a> Whitney, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 352, 401, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

पुनर्वसू|पुनर्वसू|పునర్వసూ_౫|పునర్వసూ|ಪುನರ್ವಸೂ_೫|ಪುನರ್ವಸೂ|punarvasū_5|punarvasū|punarvasuu_5|punarvasuu|punarvasoo_5|punarvasoo|_vei_120@1479|_vei1_pn416
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1478">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1480">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn415">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn416">1:416</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn417">↷</a><hr><p>5. <b>Punarvasū,</b> ‘the two that give wealth again,’ denotes the two stars,  and  Geminorum, on the heads of Castor and Pollux. The name is no doubt connected with the beneficent character of the Aśvins, who correspond to the Dioscuri.<sup id="ref_120_75_vei_1201479"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_75_vei_1201479">75</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="75"><li id="fx_120_75_vei_1201479"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_75_vei_1201479">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 212; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 53.</li></ol></div>

तिष्य|तिष्य|తిష్య_౬|తిష్య|ತಿಷ್ಯ_೬|ತಿಷ್ಯ|tiṣya_6|tiṣya|tiShya_6|tiShya|_vei_120@1480|_vei1_pn416
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1479">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1481">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn415">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn416">1:416</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn417">↷</a><hr><p>6. <b>Tiṣya</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुष्य">puṣya</a></b> includes the somewhat faint group in the body of the Crab, , and  Cancri. The singular is rather curious, as primarily one star would seem to have been meant. and none of the group is at all prominent.<sup id="ref_120_76_vei_1201480"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_76_vei_1201480">76</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="76"><li id="fx_120_76_vei_1201480"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_76_vei_1201480">↑</a> Whitney, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 403, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

आश्रेषास्|आश्रेषास्|आश्रेषा|ఆశ్రేషాస్_౭|ఆశ్రేషాస్|ఆశ్రేషా|ಆಶ್ರೇಷಾಸ್_೭|ಆಶ್ರೇಷಾಸ್|ಆಶ್ರೇಷಾ|āśreṣās_7|āśreṣās|āśreṣā|aashreShaas_7|aashreShaas|aashreShaa|_vei_120@1481|_vei1_pn416
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1480">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1482">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn415">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn416">1:416</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn417">↷</a><hr><p>7. <b>Āśreṣās</b> or <b>Āśleṣās,</b> which in some texts<sup id="ref_120_77_vei_1201481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_77_vei_1201481">77</a></sup> is certainly to be read Aśreṣās or Aśleṣas, denotes , and perhaps also , Hydræ. The word means ‘embracer,’ a name which admirably fits the constellation.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="77"><li id="fx_120_77_vei_1201481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_77_vei_1201481">↑</a> Aśreṣās, <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 26; Śāntikalpa; Nakṣatrakalpa; Aśleṣās, Śāntikalpa, 2; Nakṣatrakalpa, 4. 48.</li></ol></div>

मघास्|मघास्|मघा|మఘాస్_౮|మఘాస్|మఘా|ಮಘಾಸ್_೮|ಮಘಾಸ್|ಮಘಾ|maghās_8|maghās|maghā|maghaas_8|maghaas|maghaa|_vei_120@1482|_vei1_pn416
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1481">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1483">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn415">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn416">1:416</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn417">↷</a><hr><p>8. <b>Maghās,</b> the ‘bounties,’ are the Sickle, or , Leonis. The variants <b>Anaghā,</b> the ‘sinless one,’ etc., clearly refer to the auspicious influence of the constellation.</p><p>9, 10. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="फल्गुनी">phalgunī</a>, Phalgunyau, Phalgū,</b><sup id="ref_120_78_vei_1201482"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_78_vei_1201482">78</a></sup> <b>Phalgunīs, Phalgunyas,</b> is really a-double constellation, divided into Pūrve, ‘former,’ and Uttare, ‘latter.’ The former is  and  Leonis, the latter  and 93 Leonis. According to Weber, the word denotes, like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अर्जुनी">arjunī</a>,</b> the variant of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_79_vei_1201482"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_79_vei_1201482">79</a></sup> a ‘brightcoloured’ constellation. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="78"><li id="fx_120_78_vei_1201482"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_78_vei_1201482">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_79_vei_1201482"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_79_vei_1201482">↑</a> x. 85, 13.</li></ol></div>

हस्त|हस्त|హస్త_౧౧|హస్త|ಹಸ್ತ_೧೧|ಹಸ್ತ|hasta_11|hasta|_vei_120@1483|_vei1_pn417
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1482">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1484">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn416">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn417">1:417</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn418">↷</a><hr><p>11. <b>Hasta,</b> ‘hand,’ is made up of the five conspicuous stars () in Corvus, a number which the word itself suggests. According to Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_80_vei_1201483"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_80_vei_1201483">80</a></sup> the ‘five bulls’ of the Rigveda are this constellation.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="80"><li id="fx_120_80_vei_1201483"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_80_vei_1201483">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 177; Rv. i. 105, 10. <i>Cf.</i> below, p. 427, n. 156.</li></ol></div>

चित्रा|चित्रा|చిత్రా_౧౨|చిత్రా|ಚಿತ್ರಾ_೧೨|ಚಿತ್ರಾ|citrā_12|citrā|chitraa_12|chitraa|_vei_120@1484|_vei1_pn417
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1483">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1485">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn416">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn417">1:417</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn418">↷</a><hr><p>12. <b>Citrā,</b> ‘bright,’ is the beautiful star,  Virginis. It is mentioned in a legend of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_81_vei_1201484"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_81_vei_1201484">81</a></sup> and in that of the ‘two divine dogs’ (<i>divyau śvānau</i>) in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_82_vei_1201484"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_82_vei_1201484">82</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="81"><li id="fx_120_81_vei_1201484"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_81_vei_1201484">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 2, 4-6.</li><li id="fx_120_82_vei_1201484"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_82_vei_1201484">↑</a> ii. 1, 2, 13-17.</li></ol></div>

स्वाती|स्वाती|स्वाति|స్వాతీ_౧౩|స్వాతీ|స్వాతి|ಸ್ವಾತೀ_೧೩|ಸ್ವಾತೀ|ಸ್ವಾತಿ|svātī_13|svātī|svāti|svaatii_13|svaatii|svaati|svaatee_13|svaatee|_vei_120@1485|_vei1_pn417
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1484">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1486">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn416">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn417">1:417</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn418">↷</a><hr><p>13. <b>Svātī</b> or <b>Niṣṭyā</b> is later clearly the brilliant star Arcturus or  Bootis, its place in the north being assured by the notice in the Śāntikalpa,<sup id="ref_120_83_vei_1201485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_83_vei_1201485">83</a></sup> where it is said to be ‘ever traversing the northern way’ (<i>nityam uttara-mārgagam</i>). The <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_84_vei_1201485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_84_vei_1201485">84</a></sup> however, constructs an asterismal Prajāpati, giving him <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्रा">citrā</a> (<i>a</i> Virginis) for head, <a class="vei_120a" href="हस्त">hasta</a> (Corvus) for hand, the <a class="vei_120a" href="विशाखे">viśākhe</a> (<i>a</i> and  Libræ) for thighs, and the Anurādhās (, and  Scorpionis) for standing place, with Niṣṭyā for heart. But Arcturus, being 30º out, spoils this figure, while, on the other hand, the Arabic and Chinese systems have respectively, instead of Arcturus, , and  Virginis and  Virginis, which would well fit into the Prajāpati figure. But in spite of the force of this argument of Weber's,<sup id="ref_120_85_vei_1201485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_85_vei_1201485">85</a></sup> Whitney<sup id="ref_120_86_vei_1201485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_86_vei_1201485">86</a></sup> is not certain that Niṣṭyā here must mean a star in Virgo, pointing out that the name Niṣṭyā, ‘outcast,’ suggests the separation of this <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> from the others in question.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="83"><li id="fx_120_83_vei_1201485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_83_vei_1201485">↑</a> 3.</li><li id="fx_120_84_vei_1201485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_84_vei_1201485">↑</a> i. 5, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Tilak, <i>Orion,</i> 204.</li><li id="fx_120_85_vei_1201485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_85_vei_1201485">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 307, 308.</li><li id="fx_120_86_vei_1201485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_86_vei_1201485">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 409.</li></ol></div>

विशाखे|विशाखे|విశాఖే_౧౪|విశాఖే|ವಿಶಾಖೇ_೧೪|ವಿಶಾಖೇ|viśākhe_14|viśākhe|vishaakhe_14|vishaakhe|_vei_120@1486|_vei1_pn417
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1485">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1487">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn416">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn417">1:417</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn418">↷</a><hr><p>14. <b>Viśākhe</b> is the couple of stars  and  Libræ. This mansion is later called <a class="vei_120a" href="राधा">rādhā</a> according to the Amarakośa, and it is curious that in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_87_vei_1201486"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_87_vei_1201486">87</a></sup> the expression <i>rādho <a class="vei_120a" href="विशाखे">viśākhe</a>,</i> ‘the <a class="vei_120a" href="विशाखे">viśākhe</a> are prosperity,’ should occur. But probably <a class="vei_120a" href="राधा">rādhā</a> is merely an invention due to the name of the next <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अनुराधा">anurādhā</a>, wrongly conceived as meaning ‘that which is after or follows <a class="vei_120a" href="राधा">rādhā</a>.’<sup id="ref_120_88_vei_1201486"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_88_vei_1201486">88</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="87"><li id="fx_120_87_vei_1201486"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_87_vei_1201486">↑</a> xix. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_88_vei_1201486"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_88_vei_1201486">↑</a> Lanman in Whitney's Translation of the Atharvaveda, 908. <i>Cf.</i> Thibaut, <i>Journal of the Asiatic Society of Bengal,</i> 63, 156.</li></ol></div>

अनूराधास्|अनूराधास्|अनूराधा|అనూరాధాస్_౧౫|అనూరాధాస్|అనూరాధా|ಅನೂರಾಧಾಸ್_೧೫|ಅನೂರಾಧಾಸ್|ಅನೂರಾಧಾ|anūrādhās_15|anūrādhās|anūrādhā|anuuraadhaas_15|anuuraadhaas|anuuraadhaa|anooraadhaas_15|anooraadhaas|anooraadhaa|_vei_120@1487|_vei1_pn417
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1486">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1488">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn416">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn417">1:417</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn418">↷</a><hr><p>15. <b>Anūrādhās</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अनुराधा">anurādhā</a>,</b> ‘propitious,’ is , and  (perhaps also ) Scorpionis. </p></div>

रोहिणी|रोहिणी|रोहिणि|రోహిణీ_౧౬|రోహిణీ|రోహిణి|ರೋಹಿಣೀ_೧೬|ರೋಹಿಣೀ|ರೋಹಿಣಿ|rohiṇī_16|rohiṇī|rohiṇi|rohiNii_16|rohiNii|rohiNi|rohiNee_16|rohiNee|_vei_120@1488|_vei1_pn418
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1487">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1489">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn417">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn418">1:418</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn419">↷</a><hr><p>16. <b>Rohiṇī,</b> ‘ruddy’; <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ज्येष्ठघ्नी">jyeṣṭhaghnī</a>,</b> ‘slaying the eldest’; or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ज्येष्ठा">jyeṣṭhā</a>,</b> ‘eldest,’ is the name of the constellation , and  Scorpionis, of which the central star, , is the brilliant reddish Antares (or Cor Scorpionis).</p></div>

विचृतौ|विचृतौ|విచృతౌ_౧౭|విచృతౌ|ವಿಚೃತೌ_೧೭|ವಿಚೃತೌ|vicṛtau_17|vicṛtau|vichRtau_17|vichRtau|vichRitau_17|vichRitau|vichRutau_17|vichRutau|vichRRitau_17|vichRRitau|_vei_120@1489|_vei1_pn418
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1488">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1490">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn417">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn418">1:418</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn419">↷</a><hr><p>17. <b>Vicṛtau,</b> ‘the two releasers’; <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मूल">mūla</a>,</b> ‘root’; or <b>Mūlabarhaṇī,</b> ‘uprooting,’ denote primarily  and  at the extremity of the tail of the Scorpion, but including also the nine or eleven stars from  to .</p><p>18, 19. <b>Aṣāḍhās</b> (‘unconquered’), distinguished as <b>Pūrvās,</b> ‘former,’ and <b>Uttarās,</b> ‘latter,’ are really two constellations, of which the former is composed of , and  Sagittarii, or of and  only, and the latter of , and  Sagittarii, or of two, and , only. It is probable that originally only four stars forming a square were meant as included in the whole constellation<sup id="ref_120_89_vei_1201489"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_89_vei_1201489">89</a></sup> --viz.,  and , with  and .</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="89"><li id="fx_120_89_vei_1201489"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_89_vei_1201489">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Thibaut, <i>Journal of the Asiatic Society of Bengal,</i> 63, 156.</li></ol></div>

अभिजित्०|अभिजित्|అభిజిత్_౨౦|అభిజిత్|ಅಭಿಜಿತ್_೨೦|ಅಭಿಜಿತ್|abhijit_20|abhijit|_vei_120@1490|_vei1_pn418
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1489">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1491">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn417">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn418">1:418</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn419">↷</a><hr><p>20. <b>Abhijit</b> is the brilliant star  Lyræ with its two companions  and . Its location in 60º north latitude is completely discordant with the position of the corresponding Arabian and Chinese asterisms. This fact is considered by Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_90_vei_1201490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_90_vei_1201490">90</a></sup> to support the view that it was a later addition to the system; its occurrence, however, as early as the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_91_vei_1201490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_91_vei_1201490">91</a></sup> which he does not note, somewhat invalidates<sup id="ref_120_92_vei_1201490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_92_vei_1201490">92</a></sup> that view. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_93_vei_1201490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_93_vei_1201490">93</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="अभिजित्">abhijit</a> is said to be ‘over Aṣāḍhās, under <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रोणा">śroṇā</a>,’ which Weber<sup id="ref_120_94_vei_1201490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_94_vei_1201490">94</a></sup> held to refer to its position in space, inferring thence that its Vedic position corresponded to that of the Arab Manāzil and the Chinese Sieou--viz., , Capricorni. But Whitney<sup id="ref_120_95_vei_1201490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_95_vei_1201490">95</a></sup> argues effectively that the words ‘over’ and ‘under’ really refer to the place of <a class="vei_120a" href="अभिजित्">abhijit</a> in the list, ‘after’ Aṣāḍhās and ‘before’ <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रोणा">śroṇā</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="90"><li id="fx_120_90_vei_1201490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_90_vei_1201490">↑</a> <i>Nachrichten der ko7nigl. Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften zu Go7ttingen,</i> 1909, 551, 552.</li><li id="fx_120_91_vei_1201490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_91_vei_1201490">↑</a> ii. 13, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_92_vei_1201490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_92_vei_1201490">↑</a> It is at the same time to be noted that <a class="vei_120a" href="अभिजित्">abhijit</a> is wanting both in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā lists.</li><li id="fx_120_93_vei_1201490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_93_vei_1201490">↑</a> i. 5, 2, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_94_vei_1201490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_94_vei_1201490">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 320, 321; 2, 307; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 224 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_95_vei_1201490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_95_vei_1201490">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 8, 393.</li></ol></div>

श्रोणा|श्रोणा|శ్రోణా_౨౧|శ్రోణా|ಶ್ರೋಣಾ_೨೧|ಶ್ರೋಣಾ|śroṇā_21|śroṇā|shroNaa_21|shroNaa|_vei_120@1491|_vei1_pn418
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1490">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1492">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn417">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn418">1:418</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn419">↷</a><hr><p>21. <b>Śroṇā,</b> ‘lame,’ or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्रवण">śravaṇa</a>,</b> ‘ear,’ denotes the bright star Aquilæ with  below and  above it. Weber<sup id="ref_120_96_vei_1201491"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_96_vei_1201491">96</a></sup> very need lessly thinks that the name <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रवण">śravaṇa</a> suggested two ears and the head between. It is quite out of correspondence with the Manāzil and the Sieou, and is clearly an Indian invention.<sup id="ref_120_97_vei_1201491"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_97_vei_1201491">97</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="96"><li id="fx_120_96_vei_1201491"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_96_vei_1201491">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 382; but see Whitney, 404.</li><li id="fx_120_97_vei_1201491"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_97_vei_1201491">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

श्रविष्ठास्|श्रविष्ठास्|श्रविष्ठा|శ్రవిష్ఠాస్_౨౨|శ్రవిష్ఠాస్|శ్రవిష్ఠా|ಶ್ರವಿಷ್ಠಾಸ್_೨೨|ಶ್ರವಿಷ್ಠಾಸ್|ಶ್ರವಿಷ್ಠಾ|śraviṣṭhās_22|śraviṣṭhās|śraviṣṭhā|shraviShThaas_22|shraviShThaas|shraviShThaa|_vei_120@1492|_vei1_pn419
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1491">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1493">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn418">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn419">1:419</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn431">↷</a><hr><p>22. <b>Śraviṣṭhās,</b> ‘most famous,’ or later <b>Dhaniṣṭhās,</b><sup id="ref_120_98_vei_1201492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_98_vei_1201492">98</a></sup> ‘most wealthy,’ is the diamond-shaped group, , and , in the Dolphin, perhaps also  in the same constellation. Like the preceding <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>, it is out of harmony with the Manāzil and Sieou.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="98"><li id="fx_120_98_vei_1201492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_98_vei_1201492">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 26; Śāntikalpa, 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="धनिष्ठा">dhaniṣṭhā</a>, <i>ibid.,</i> 5.</li></ol></div>

शतभिषज्|शतभिषज्|శతభిషజ్_౨౩|శతభిషజ్|ಶತಭಿಷಜ್_೨೩|ಶತಭಿಷಜ್|śatabhiṣaj_23|śatabhiṣaj|shatabhiShaj_23|shatabhiShaj|_vei_120@1493|_vei1_pn419
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1492">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1494">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn418">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn419">1:419</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn431">↷</a><hr><p>23. <b>Śatabhiṣaj</b> or <b>Śatabhiṣa,</b><sup id="ref_120_99_vei_1201493"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_99_vei_1201493">99</a></sup> ‘having a hundred physicians,’ seems to be  Aquarii with the others around it vaguely conceived as numbering a hundred.</p><p>24, 25. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रोष्ठ">proṣṭha</a>-padās</b> (fem. plur.), ‘feet of a stool,’ or later <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भद्र">bhadra</a>-padās,</b><sup id="ref_120_100_vei_1201493"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_100_vei_1201493">100</a></sup> ‘auspicious feet,’ a double asterism forming a square, the former (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पूर्व">pūrva</a></i>) consisting of  and  Pegasi, the latter (<i>uttara</i>) of  Pegasi and  Andromedæ.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="99"><li id="fx_120_99_vei_1201493"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_99_vei_1201493">↑</a> So probably in Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 13, 20, where see von Schroeder's critical note. The Śāntikalpa, 5, and Nakṣatrakalpa, 2, have Śatabhiṣā, and the latter, 1, has Śatabhiṣa (masculine).</li><li id="fx_120_100_vei_1201493"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_100_vei_1201493">↑</a> Śāntikalpa, 5, etc.</li></ol></div>

रेवती|रेवती|रेवति|రేవతీ_౨౬|రేవతీ|రేవతి|ರೇವತೀ_೨೬|ರೇವತೀ|ರೇವತಿ|revatī_26|revatī|revati|revatii_26|revatii|revatee_26|revatee|_vei_120@1494|_vei1_pn419
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1493">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1495">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn418">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn419">1:419</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn431">↷</a><hr><p>26. <b>Revatī,</b> ‘wealthy,’ denotes a large number of stars (later 32), of which  Piscium, close upon the ecliptic where it was crossed by the equator of about 570 A.D., is given as the southernmost.</p></div>

अश्व-युजौ|अश्व-युजौ|अश्वयुजौ|अश्व|युजौ|అశ్వ-యుజౌ_౨౭|అశ్వ-యుజౌ|అశ్వయుజౌ|అశ్వ|యుజౌ|ಅಶ್ವ-ಯುಜೌ_೨೭|ಅಶ್ವ-ಯುಜೌ|ಅಶ್ವಯುಜೌ|ಅಶ್ವ|ಯುಜೌ|aśva-yujau_27|aśva-yujau|aśvayujau|aśva|yujau|ashva-yujau_27|ashva-yujau|ashvayujau|ashva|_vei_120@1495|_vei1_pn419
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1494">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1496">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn418">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn419">1:419</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn431">↷</a><hr><p>27. <b>Aśva-yujau,</b> ‘the two horse-harnessers,’ denotes the stars and  Arietis. Aśvinyau<sup id="ref_120_101_vei_1201495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_101_vei_1201495">101</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्विनी">aśvinī</a><sup id="ref_120_102_vei_1201495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_102_vei_1201495">102</a></sup> are later names.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="101"><li id="fx_120_101_vei_1201495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_101_vei_1201495">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 26: Nakṣatrakalpa, 9. 30.</li><li id="fx_120_102_vei_1201495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_102_vei_1201495">↑</a> Nakṣatrakalpa, 4. 45; Śāntikalpa, 5. 11.</li></ol></div>

अपभरणीस्|अपभरणी|अपभरणि|भरणीस्|भरणीस्|भरणी|भरणि|అపభరణీస్|అపభరణీ|అపభరణి|భరణీస్_౨౮|భరణీస్|భరణీ|భరణి|ಅಪಭರಣೀಸ್|ಅಪಭರಣೀ|ಅಪಭರಣಿ|ಭರಣೀಸ್_೨೮|ಭರಣೀಸ್|ಭರಣೀ|ಭರಣಿ|apabharaṇīs|apabharaṇī|apabharaṇi|bharaṇīs_28|bharaṇīs|bharaṇī|bharaṇi|apabharaNiis|apabharaNii|apabharaNi|bharaNiis_28|bharaNiis|bharaNii|bharaNi|apabharaNees|apabharaNee|bharaNees_28|bharaNees|bharaNee|_vei_120@1496|_vei1_pn419
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1495">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1497">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn418">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn419">1:419</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn431">↷</a><hr><p>28. <b>Apabharaṇīs, Bharaṇīs,</b> or <b>Bharaṇyas,</b> ‘the bearers,’ is the name of the small triangle in the northern part of the Ram known as Musca or 35, 39, and 41 Arietis.</p><p><i>The Nakṣatras and the Months.</i>--In the Brāhmaṇas the <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> names are regularly used to denote dates. This is done in two ways. The name, if not already a feminine, may be turned into a feminine and compounded with <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पूर्ण-मास">pūrṇa-māsa</a>,</i> ‘the full moon,’ as in <i>Tiṣyā-<a class="vei_120a" href="पूर्णमास">pūrṇamāsa</a>,</i> ‘the full moon in the <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तिष्य">tiṣya</a>.’<sup id="ref_120_103_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_103_vei_1201496">103</a></sup> Much more often, however, it is turned into a derivative adjective, used with <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पौर्णमासी">paurṇamāsī</a>,</i> ‘the full  moon (night),’ or with <i>amāvāsyā,</i> ‘the new moon (night),’ as in <i>Phālgunī <a class="vei_120a" href="पौर्णमासी">paurṇamāsī</a>,</i> ‘the full-moon night in the <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> Phalgunī’;<sup id="ref_120_104_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_104_vei_1201496">104</a></sup> or, as is usual in the Sūtras, the <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> adjective alone is used to denote the full-moon night. The month itself is called by a name derived<sup id="ref_120_105_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_105_vei_1201496">105</a></sup> from that of a <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>, but only Phālguna,<sup id="ref_120_106_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_106_vei_1201496">106</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="चैत्र">caitra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_107_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_107_vei_1201496">107</a></sup> Vaiśākha,<sup id="ref_120_108_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_108_vei_1201496">108</a></sup> Taiṣya,<sup id="ref_120_109_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_109_vei_1201496">109</a></sup> Māgha<sup id="ref_120_110_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_110_vei_1201496">110</a></sup> occur in the Brāhmaṇas, the complete list later being Phālguna, <a class="vei_120a" href="चैत्र">caitra</a>, Vaiśākha, Jyaiṣṭha, Āṣāḍha, Śrāvaṇa, Prauṣṭhapada, Āśvayuja, Kārttika, Mārgaśīrṣa, Taiṣya, Māgha. Strictly speaking, these should be lunar months, but the use of a lunar year was clearly very restricted: we have seen that as early as the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> there was a tendency to equate lunar months with the twelve months of thirty days which made up the solar year (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a></b>).</p><p><i>The Nakṣatras and Chronology.</i>--(1) An endeavour has been made to ascertain from the names of the months the period at which the systematic employment of those names was introduced. Sir William Jones<sup id="ref_120_111_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_111_vei_1201496">111</a></sup> refers to this possibility, and Bentley, by the gratuitous assumption that Śrāvaṇa always marked the summer solstice, concluded that the names of the months did not date before B.C. 1181. Weber<sup id="ref_120_112_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_112_vei_1201496">112</a></sup> considered that there was a possibility of fixing a date by this means, but Whitney<sup id="ref_120_113_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_113_vei_1201496">113</a></sup> has convincingly shown that it is an impossible feat, and Thibaut<sup id="ref_120_114_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_114_vei_1201496">114</a></sup> concurs in this view. Twelve became fixed as the number of the months because of the desire, evident in the Brāhmaṇas, somehow or other to harmonize lunar with solar time; but the selection of twelve Nakṣatras out of twenty-seven as connected with the night of full moon can have no chronological significance, because full moon at  no period occurred in those twelve only, but has at all periods occurred in every one of the twenty-seven at regularly recurrent intervals.</p><p>(2) All the lists of the Nakṣatras begin with Kṛttīkās. It is only fair to suppose that there was some special reason for this fact. Now the later list of the Nakṣatras begins with <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्विनी">aśvinī</a>, and it was unquestionably rearranged because at the time of its adoption the vernal equinox coincided with the star  Piscium on the border of <a class="vei_120a" href="रेवती">revatī</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्विनी">aśvinī</a>,<sup id="ref_120_115_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_115_vei_1201496">115</a></sup> say in the course of the sixth century A.D. Weber<sup id="ref_120_116_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_116_vei_1201496">116</a></sup> has therefore accepted the view that the <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्तिकास्">kṛttikās</a> were chosen for a similar reason, and the date at which that <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> coincided with the vernal equinox has been estimated at some period in the third millennium B.C.<sup id="ref_120_117_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_117_vei_1201496">117</a></sup> A very grave objection to this view is its assumption that the sun, and not the moon, was then regarded as connected with the Nakṣatras; and both Thibaut<sup id="ref_120_118_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_118_vei_1201496">118</a></sup> and Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_119_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_119_vei_1201496">119</a></sup> have pronounced decidedly against the idea of connecting the equinox with the <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्तिकास्">kṛttikās</a>. Jacobi<sup id="ref_120_120_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_120_vei_1201496">120</a></sup> has contended that in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_121_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_121_vei_1201496">121</a></sup> the commencement of the rains and the summer solstice mark the beginning of the new year and the end of the old, and that further the new year began with the summer solstice in <a class="vei_120a" href="फल्गुनी">phalgunī</a>.<sup id="ref_120_121_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_121_vei_1201496">121</a></sup> He has also referred to the distinction of the two sets of <a class="vei_120a" href="देव">deva</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="यम">yama</a> Nakṣatras in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_122_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_122_vei_1201496">122</a></sup> as supporting his view of the connexion of the sun and the Nakṣatras. But this view is far from satisfactory: the Rigveda passages cannot yield the sense required except by translating the word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="द्वादश">dvādaśa</a></i><sup id="ref_120_123_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_123_vei_1201496">123</a></sup> as ‘the twelfth (month)’ instead of ‘consisting of twelve parts,’ that is, ‘year,’ the accepted  interpretation; and the division of the Nakṣatras is not at all satisfactorily explained by a supposed connexion with the sun. It may further be mentioned that even if the <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> of <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्तिकास्">kṛttikās</a> be deemed to have been chosen because of its coincidence with the vernal equinox, both Whitney<sup id="ref_120_124_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_124_vei_1201496">124</a></sup> and Thibaut<sup id="ref_120_125_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_125_vei_1201496">125</a></sup> are prepared to regard it as no more than a careless variant of the date given by the <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्योतिष">jyotiṣa</a>, which puts the winter solstice in Māgha.</p><p>(3) The winter solstice in Māgha is assured by a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> text, for the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_126_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_126_vei_1201496">126</a></sup> expressly places it in the new moon of Māgha (<i>māghasyāmāvāsyāyām</i>). It is not very important whether we take this with the commentators<sup id="ref_120_127_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_127_vei_1201496">127</a></sup> as the new moon in the middle of a month commencing with the day after full moon in Taiṣa, or, which is much more likely, as the new moon beginning the month and preceding full moon in Māgha. The datum gives a certain possibility of fixing an epoch in the following way. If the end of <a class="vei_120a" href="रेवती">revatī</a> marked the vernal equinox at one period, then the precession of the equinoxes would enable us to calculate at what point of time the vernal equinox was in a position corresponding to the winter solstice in Māgha, when the solstitial colure cut the ecliptic at the beginning of <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रविष्ठास्">śraviṣṭhās</a>. This would be, on the strict theory, in the third quarter of <a class="vei_120a" href="भरणी">bharaṇī</a>, 6(3/4) asterisms removed from <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रविष्ठास्">śraviṣṭhās</a>, and the difference between that and the beginning of <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्विनी">aśvinī</a>=  1(3/4) asterisms = 23(1/3)º (27 asterisms being = 360º). Taking the starting-point at 499 A.D., the assured period of <a class="vei_120a" href="वराह">varāha</a> Mihira, Jones<sup id="ref_120_128_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_128_vei_1201496">128</a></sup> arrived at the date B. C. 1181 for the vernal equinox corresponding to the winter solstice in Māgha--that is, on the basis of 1º = 72 years as the precession. Pratt<sup id="ref_120_129_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_129_vei_1201496">129</a></sup> arrived at precisely the same date, taking the same rate of precession and adopting as his basis the ascertained position in the Siddhāntas of the junction star<sup id="ref_120_130_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_130_vei_1201496">130</a></sup> of <a class="vei_120a" href="मघा">maghā</a>,  Leonis or Regulus. Davis<sup id="ref_120_131_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_131_vei_1201496">131</a></sup> and Colebrooke<sup id="ref_120_132_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_132_vei_1201496">132</a></sup> arrived at a different date, B.C. 1391, by taking as the basis of their calculation the junction star of <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्रा">citrā</a>, which happens to be of uncertain position, varying as much as 3º in the different textbooks. But though the twelfth century has received a certain currency as the epoch of the observation in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्योतिष">jyotiṣa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_133_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_133_vei_1201496">133</a></sup> it is of very doubtful value. As Whitney points out, it is impossible to say that the earlier asterisms coincided in position with the later asterisms of 13(1/3)º extent each. They were not chosen as equal divisions, but as groups of stars which stood in conjunction with the moon; and the result of subsequently making them strictly equal divisions was to throw the principal stars of the later groups altogether out of their asterisms.<sup id="ref_120_134_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_134_vei_1201496">134</a></sup> Nor can we say that the star  Piscium early formed the eastern boundary of <a class="vei_120a" href="रेवती">revatī</a>; it may possibly not even have been in that asterism at all, for it is far remote from the Chinese and Arabic asterisms corresponding to <a class="vei_120a" href="रेवती">revatī</a>. Added to all this, and to the uncertainty of the starting-point-582 A.D., 560 A.D., or 491 A.D. being variants<sup id="ref_120_135_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_135_vei_1201496">135</a></sup> --is the fact that the place of the equinox is not a matter accurately determinable by mere observation, and that the Hindu astronomers of the Vedic period cannot be deemed to have been very accurate observers, since they made no precise determination of the  number of days of the year, which even in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्योतिष">jyotiṣa</a> they do not determine more precisely than as 366 days, and even the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूर्य">sūrya</a> Siddhānta<sup id="ref_120_136_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_136_vei_1201496">136</a></sup> does not know the precession of the equinoxes. It is therefore only fair to allow a thousand years for possible errors,<sup id="ref_120_137_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_137_vei_1201496">137</a></sup> and the only probable conclusion to be drawn from the datum of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> is that it was recording an observation which must have been made some centuries B.C., in itself a result quite in harmony with the probable date of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> literature,<sup id="ref_120_138_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_138_vei_1201496">138</a></sup> say B.C. 800-600.</p><p>(4) Another chronological argument has been derived from the fact that there is a considerable amount of evidence for Phālguna having been regarded as the beginning of the year, since the full moon in <a class="vei_120a" href="फल्गुनी">phalgunī</a> is often described as the ‘mouth (<i>mukham</i>) of the year.’<sup id="ref_120_139_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_139_vei_1201496">139</a></sup> Jacobi<sup id="ref_120_140_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_140_vei_1201496">140</a></sup> considers that this  was due to the fact that the year was reckoned from the winter solstice, which would coincide with the month of Phālguna about B.C. 4000. Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_141_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_141_vei_1201496">141</a></sup> and Thibaut,<sup id="ref_120_142_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_142_vei_1201496">142</a></sup> on the other hand, maintain that the choice of Phālguna as the ‘mouth’ of the year was due to its being the first month of spring. This view is favoured by the fact that there is distinct evidence<sup id="ref_120_143_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_143_vei_1201496">143</a></sup> of the correspondence of Phālguna and the beginning of spring: as we have seen above in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, the new moon in Māgha is placed at the winter solstice,<sup id="ref_120_144_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_144_vei_1201496">144</a></sup> which puts the full moon of <a class="vei_120a" href="फल्गुनी">phalgunī</a> at a month and a half after the winter solstice, or in the first week of February, a date not in itself improbable for about B.C. 800, and corresponding with the February 7 of the <i>veris initium</i> in the Roman Calendar. This fact accords with the only natural division of the year into three periods of four months, as the rainy season lasts from June 7-10 to October 7-10, and it is certain that the second set of four months dates from the beginning of the rains (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चातुर्मास्य">cāturmāsya</a></b>). Tilak,<sup id="ref_120_145_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_145_vei_1201496">145</a></sup> on the other hand, holds that the winter solstice coincided with Māghī full moon at the time of the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (B.C. 2350), and had coincided with Phālgunī and Caitrī in early periods--viz., B.C. 4000-2500, and B.C. 60004000.</p><p>(5) The passages of the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_146_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_146_vei_1201496">146</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_147_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_147_vei_1201496">147</a></sup> which treat the full moon in Phālguna as the beginning of the year, give as an alternative the full moon in <a class="vei_120a" href="चैत्र">caitra</a>. Probably the latter month was chosen so as to secure that the initial day should fall well within the season of spring,<sup id="ref_120_148_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_148_vei_1201496">148</a></sup> and was not, as Jacobi believes, a relic of a period  when the winter solstice corresponded with <a class="vei_120a" href="चैत्र">caitra</a>. Another alternative is the <a class="vei_120a" href="एकाष्टका">ekāṣṭakā</a>, interpreted by the commentators as the eighth day after the full moon in <a class="vei_120a" href="मघास्">maghās</a>, a time which might, as being the last quarter of the waning half of the old year, well be considered as representing the end of the year. A fourth alternative is the fourth day before full moon; the full moon meant must be that of <a class="vei_120a" href="चैत्र">caitra</a>, as Ālekhana quoted by Āpastamba held, not of Māgha, as Āśmarathya, Laugākṣi and the Mīmāṃsists believed, and as Tilak believes.<sup id="ref_120_149_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_149_vei_1201496">149</a></sup></p><p>(6) Others, again, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> ritual, began the year with the month Mārgaśīrṣa, as is shown by its other name Āgrahāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_150_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_150_vei_1201496">150</a></sup> (‘belonging to the commencement of the year’). Jacobi and Tilak<sup id="ref_120_151_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_151_vei_1201496">151</a></sup> think that this one denoted the autumn equinox in <a class="vei_120a" href="मृगशिरस्">mṛgaśiras</a>, corresponding to the winter solstice in <a class="vei_120a" href="फल्गुनी">phalgunī</a>. But, as Thibaut<sup id="ref_120_152_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_152_vei_1201496">152</a></sup> shows clearly, it was selected as the beginning of a year that was taken to commence with autumn, just as some took the spring to commence with <a class="vei_120a" href="चैत्र">caitra</a> instead of Phālguna.<sup id="ref_120_153_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_153_vei_1201496">153</a></sup></p><p>(7) Jacobi has also argued, with the support of Bühler,<sup id="ref_120_154_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_154_vei_1201496">154</a></sup> from the terms given for the beginning of Vedic study in the <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> Sūtras, on the principle that study commenced with the rains (as in the Buddhist <i>vassā</i>) which mark the summer solstice. He concludes that if Bhādrapada appears as the date of commencing study in some texts, it was fixed thus because at one time Proṣṭhapadās (the early name of Bhadrapadās) coincided with the summer solstice, this having been the case when the winter solstice was in Phālguna. But Whitney<sup id="ref_120_155_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_155_vei_1201496">155</a></sup> has pointed out that this argument is utterly illegitimate; we cannot say that there was any necessary connexion between the rains and learning--a month like Śrāvaṇa might be preferred  because of its connexion with the word <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रवण">śravaṇa</a>, ‘ear’--and in view of the precession of the equinoxes, we must assume that Bhādrapada was kept because of its traditional coincidence with the beginning of the rains after it had ceased actually so to coincide.<sup id="ref_120_156_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_156_vei_1201496">156</a></sup></p><p><i>The Origin of the Nakṣatras.</i>--As we have seen, there is no evidence showing the process by which the Nakṣatras may  have originated in India. They are mentioned only as stars in the earlier parts of the Rigveda, then the names of three of them are found in the latest parts of that Saṃhitā, and finally in the later Atharvaveda and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās the full list appears. It may also be noted that the Vedic Indians show (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्रह">graha</a></b>) a remarkably small knowledge of the other astronomical phenomena; the discovery of a series of 27 lunar mansions by them would therefore be rather surprising. On the other hand, the nature of such an operation is not very complicated; it consists merely in selecting a star or a star group with which the moon is in conjunction. It is thus impossible <i>a priori</i> to deny that the Vedic Indians could have invented for themselves a lunar Zodiac.<sup id="ref_120_157_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_157_vei_1201496">157</a></sup></p><p>But the question is complicated by the fact that there exist two similar sets of 28 stars or star groups in Arabia and in China, the Manāzil and the Sieou. The use of the Manāzil in Arabia is consistent and effective; the calendar is regulated by them, and the position of the asterisms corresponds best with the positions required for a lunar Zodiac. The Indians might therefore have borrowed the system from Arabia, but that is a mere possibility, because the evidence for the existence of the Manāzil is long posterior to that for the existence of the Nakṣatras, while again the Mazzaroth or Mazzaloth of the Old Testament<sup id="ref_120_158_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_158_vei_1201496">158</a></sup> may really be the lunar mansions.<sup id="ref_120_159_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_159_vei_1201496">159</a></sup> That the Arabian system is borrowed from India, as Burgess<sup id="ref_120_160_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_160_vei_1201496">160</a></sup> held, is, on the other hand, not at all probable.</p><p>Biot, the eminent Chinese scholar, in a series of papers published by him between 1839 and 1861,<sup id="ref_120_161_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_161_vei_1201496">161</a></sup> attempted to prove  the derivation of the <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> from the Chinese Sieou. The latter he did not regard as being in origin lunar mansions at all. He thought that they were equatorial stars used, as in modern astronomy, as a standard to which planets or other stars observed in the neighbourhood can be eferred; they were, as regards twenty-four of them, selecte about B.C. 2357 on account of their proximity to the equator, and of their having the same right ascension as certain circumpolar stars which had attracted the attention of Chinese observers. Four more were added in B.C. 1100 in order to mark the equinoxes and solstices of the period. He held that the list of stars commenced with Mao (= <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्तिकास्">kṛttikās</a>), which was at the vernal equinox in B.C. 2357. Weber,<sup id="ref_120_162_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_162_vei_1201496">162</a></sup> in an elaborate essay of 1860, disputed this theory, and endeavoured to show that the Chinese literary evidence for the Sieou was late, dating not even from before the third century B.C. The last point does not appear<sup id="ref_120_163_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_163_vei_1201496">163</a></sup> to be correct, but his objections against the basis of Biot's theory were reinforced by Whitney,<sup id="ref_120_164_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_164_vei_1201496">164</a></sup> who insisted that Biot's supposition of the Sieou's not having been ultimately derived from a system of lunar mansions, was untenable. This is admitted by the latest defender of the hypothesis of borrowing from China, Léopold de Saussure,<sup id="ref_120_165_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_165_vei_1201496">165</a></sup> , but his arguments in favour of a Chinese origin for the Indian lunar mansions have been refuted by Oldenberg,<sup id="ref_120_166_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_166_vei_1201496">166</a></sup> who has also pointed out<sup id="ref_120_167_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_167_vei_1201496">167</a></sup> that the series does not begin with Mao (= <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्तिकास्">kṛttikās</a>).</p><p>There remains only the possibility that a common source for all the three sets--<a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>, Manāzil, and Sieou--may be found in Babylonia. Hommel<sup id="ref_120_168_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_168_vei_1201496">168</a></sup> has endeavoured to show that recent research has established in Babylonia the existence of a lunar zodiac of twenty-four members headed by the  Pleiades (= <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्तिकास्">kṛttikās</a>); but Thibaut's researches<sup id="ref_120_169_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_169_vei_1201496">169</a></sup> are not favourable to this claim. On the other hand, Weber,<sup id="ref_120_170_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_170_vei_1201496">170</a></sup> Whitney,<sup id="ref_120_171_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_171_vei_1201496">171</a></sup> Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_172_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_172_vei_1201496">172</a></sup> and Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_173_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_173_vei_1201496">173</a></sup> all incline to the view that in Babylonia is to be found the origin of the system, and this must for the present be regarded as the most probable view, for there are other traces of Babylonian influence in Vedic literature, such as the legend of the flood, perhaps the Ādityas,<sup id="ref_120_174_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_174_vei_1201496">174</a></sup> and possibly the word <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मना">manā</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="103"><li id="fx_120_103_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_103_vei_1201496">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 10, 1. <i>Cf.</i> vii. 4, 8, 1. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 9, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_104_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_104_vei_1201496">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 3. 11 <i>et seq.;</i> vi. 2, 2, 18; xiii. 4, 1, 4; Kausītaki <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3; iv. 4; v. 1. See also Caland, <i>Über das rituelle <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> des <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>,</i> 36, 37, and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_105_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_105_vei_1201496">↑</a> Primarily an adjective, with <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a></i> to be supplied--<i>e.g.,</i> Phālguna, ‘(the month) connected with the <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="फल्गुनी">phalgunī</a>.’</li><li id="fx_120_106_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_106_vei_1201496">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 9, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_107_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_107_vei_1201496">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xix. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_108_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_108_vei_1201496">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 1, 1, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_109_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_109_vei_1201496">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xix. 2. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_110_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_110_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Ibid.;</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 8, 1, 4. For the later list, see Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 327, 328.</li><li id="fx_120_111_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_111_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Asiatic Researches,</i> 2, 296.</li><li id="fx_120_112_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_112_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 347, 348; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 455; 10, 230, 231.</li><li id="fx_120_113_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_113_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 6, 413; 8, 85 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_114_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_114_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Astronomie, Astrologie und Mathematik,</i> 16.</li><li id="fx_120_115_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_115_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Colebrooke, <i>Essays,</i> 2, 264; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 234.</li><li id="fx_120_116_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_116_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 362-364; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 234; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 2, n. 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_117_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_117_vei_1201496">↑</a> See Weber. <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Bühler, <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 23, 245, n. 20; Tilak, <i>Orion,</i> 40 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_118_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_118_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 24, 96.</li><li id="fx_120_119_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_119_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenlāndischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 631; 49, 473; 50, 451, 452; <i>Nachrichten der ko7nigl. Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften zu Go7ttingen,</i> 1909, 564; Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1909, 1103.</li><li id="fx_120_120_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_120_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Festgruss an Roth,</i> 68 <i>et seq.</i> = <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 23, 154 <i>et seq.; Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 49, 218 <i>et seq.;</i> 50, 83; <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1910, 463.</li><li id="fx_120_121_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_121_vei_1201496">↑</a> vii. 103 (the ‘frog’ hymn); x. 85 (the ‘marriage’ hymn).</li><li id="fx_120_121_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_121_vei_1201496">↑</a> vii. 103 (the ‘frog’ hymn); x. 85 (the ‘marriage’ hymn).</li><li id="fx_120_122_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_122_vei_1201496">↑</a> i. 5, 2, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_123_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_123_vei_1201496">↑</a> Rv. vii. 103, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_124_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_124_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Oriental and Linguistic Essays,</i> 2, 383.</li><li id="fx_120_125_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_125_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 24, 97. <i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1910, 464, n. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_126_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_126_vei_1201496">↑</a> xix. 3. This was first noticed by Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 345 <i>et seq.,</i> who pointed out its relation to the datum of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्योतिष">jyotiṣa</a>. The same date as that of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्योतिष">jyotiṣa</a> is found in a passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> cited by Shamasastry, <i>Gavām <a class="vei_120a" href="अयन">ayana</a>,</i> 137 (<i>māghe māse dhaniṣṭhābhir uttareṇaiti bhānumān, ardhāśleṣasya śrāvaṇasya dakṣiṇenopanivartate,</i> ‘in the month of Māgha the sun goes north with the asterism Dhaniṣṭhās, in the month of Śrāvaṇa he returns south in the middle of the asterism Āśleṣa’; the sense is clear, though the text is corrupt). The passage is apparently not in Caland's manuscripts, or he would have mentioned it in his paper, <i>Über das vituelle <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> des <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>,</i> 36, 37. Its date and value are therefore not quite certain.</li><li id="fx_120_127_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_127_vei_1201496">↑</a> Vināyaka on <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Ānartīya on <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiii. 19, 1; Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 345. The assumption of the scholiasts seems to be due to the fact that to their minds a month must end with a new moon (<i>amānta</i>) or with full moon (<i>pūrṇimānta</i>). But there is no reason to say that in Vedic times the month may not have commenced with the new moon; the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> passage would thus be quite satisfactorily explained.</li><li id="fx_120_128_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_128_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Asiatic Researches,</i> 2, 393.</li><li id="fx_120_129_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_129_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Journal of the Asiatic Society of Bengal,</i> 31, 49.</li><li id="fx_120_130_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_130_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, <i>Oriental and Linguistic Essays,</i> 2, 373.</li><li id="fx_120_131_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_131_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Asiatic Researches,</i> 2, 268; 5, 288.</li><li id="fx_120_132_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_132_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Essays,</i> 1, 109, 110. See Sir T. Colebrooke, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1, 335 <i>et seq.;</i> Whitney, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 381, 382.</li><li id="fx_120_133_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_133_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> Lassen, <i>Indische Alterthumskunde,</i> 1^2, 606, 607, 976, and <i>cf.</i> Thibaut, <i>Astronomie, Astrologic und Mathematik,</i> 17, 18; Tilak, <i>Orion,</i> 38, 39.</li><li id="fx_120_134_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_134_vei_1201496">↑</a> Whitney, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 375.</li><li id="fx_120_135_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_135_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 377, 379; Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 363, 364, where he prefers A.D. 582.</li><li id="fx_120_136_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_136_vei_1201496">↑</a> See Whitney's note on <a class="vei_120a" href="सूर्य">sūrya</a> Siddhānta, iii. 12; <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 369, n. 1; 374, n. 1. <i>Cf.</i> Tilak, <i>Orion,</i> 18.</li><li id="fx_120_137_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_137_vei_1201496">↑</a> Whitney, 384, followed by Thibaut, <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 24, 98; <i>Astronomie, Astrologie und Mathematik,</i> 18. See also Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 236; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 2, n. 2; Whitney, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1, 313 <i>et seq.;</i> in Colebrooke's <i>Essays,</i> 1^2, 120 <i>et seq.;</i> Max Müller, in his edition of the Rigveda, iv^2, xxx <i>et seq.,</i> was also inclined to regard the date as very uncertain; only in his popular works (<i>Chips,</i> 1, 113, etc.) did he accept 1181 B.C., or rather 1186 B.C, as recalculated by Main from Pratt's calculation. Shamasastry's defence, <i>Gavām <a class="vei_120a" href="अयन">ayana</a>,</i> 122 <i>et seq.,</i> of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्योतिष">jyotiṣa</a> shows a misunderstanding the criticisms made. See Keith, <i>ournal of the Royal A siatic Society,</i> 1910, 66, n. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_138_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_138_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 12, 202; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaha,</i> 20 <i>et seq.</i> It has been put earlier: see Thibaut, <i>Astronomie,</i> etc., 18; Būhler, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 55, 544, and <i>cf.</i> Būhler, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 2, xl <i>et seq.; Indian Antiquary,</i> 23, 247; von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 45 <i>et seq.</i> See also Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 3; Hillebrandt, <i>Rituallitteratur,</i> 31, who are inclined to accept an early date, fourth or fifth century B.C., for the Āpastamba Sūtras, from which a still earlier date for the Brāhmaṇas must be conceded. But Eggeling is more probably correct when he assigns the Āpastamba Sūtras to the third century, B.C. See <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, xl, and it seems unwise unduly to press back the date of Vedic literature. It is noteworthy that in the Epic the solstice is still in Māgha (Mahābhārata, xiii. 168, 6. 28). Reference is, however, made (<i>ibid.,</i> i. 71, 34) to the Nakṣatras commencing with <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रवण">śravaṇa</a>, and the first month is Mārgaśīrṣa (see Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 24, 21 <i>et seq.</i>). <i>Cf.</i> also Tilak, <i>Orion,</i> 37, 216.</li><li id="fx_120_139_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_139_vei_1201496">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 4, 8, 1. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 9, 9. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 4; v. 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 2, 8; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 2, 2, 18; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, v. 3. 16. According to the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> Brāhmaṇas, the beginning falls at the middle of the joint asterisṃ.</li><li id="fx_120_140_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_140_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 23, 156 <i>et seq.; Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 49, 223 <i>et seq.;</i> 50, 72-81. See Tilak, <i>Orion,</i> 53 <i>et seq.;</i> 198 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_141_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_141_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenlāndischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 630 <i>et seq.;</i> 49. 475, 476; 50, 453-457. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, lxxxvii.</li><li id="fx_120_142_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_142_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 24, 86 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_143_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_143_vei_1201496">↑</a> See Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 329 <i>et seq.,</i> and <i>cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 3, 36; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1; a <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रुति">śruti</a> passage in the commentary on Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 2, 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 2, 4, 23. and especially Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4. 1, 2. 4. So the Phālguna full moon is called the ‘month of the seasons’ (<i>ṛtūnām mukham</i>) in <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, viii. 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 6, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_144_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_144_vei_1201496">↑</a> xix. 2. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_145_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_145_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Orion,</i> 53 <i>et seq.;</i> 198 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_146_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_146_vei_1201496">↑</a> vii. 4, 8, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_147_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_147_vei_1201496">↑</a> v. 9. See Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 341344; Thibaut, <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 24, 85 <i>et seq.,</i> for a full discussion of the points raised by Tilak, <i>Orion,</i> 43 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_148_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_148_vei_1201496">↑</a> Thibaut, <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 24, 93. On the other side, Tilak, 198 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_149_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_149_vei_1201496">↑</a> Thibaut, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 94; Tilak, 51 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> also Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiii. 1. 8-10; Weber, 2, 343, n. 2, 344.</li><li id="fx_120_150_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_150_vei_1201496">↑</a> Thibaut, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 94, 95. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, 2, 332-334.</li><li id="fx_120_151_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_151_vei_1201496">↑</a> Tilak's view is given in <i>Orion,</i> 62 <i>et seq.</i> It is based mainly on Amara's (i. 2, 23) <i>āgra-hāyaṇā</i> as a synonym of <a class="vei_120a" href="मृगशिरस्">mṛgaśiras</a>, and on certain myths (chaps. v.-vii.); he equates (221 <i>et seq.</i>) Āgrayaṇa and Orion (!).</li><li id="fx_120_152_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_152_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 94, 95.</li><li id="fx_120_153_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_153_vei_1201496">↑</a> A corresponding Kārttika year is not early, Thibaut, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 96. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 334.</li><li id="fx_120_154_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_154_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 23, 242 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_155_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_155_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, lxxxiv <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_156_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_156_vei_1201496">↑</a> Mention should here be made of the following points: (1) Jacobi's argument from the word <a class="vei_120a" href="ध्रुव">dhruva</a>, the name of the star pointed out to the bride in the marriage ritual. The word does not occur in the literature anterior to the <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> Sūtras, and it must remain an undecided question whether the practice was or was not old. Jacobi urges that <a class="vei_120a" href="ध्रुव">dhruva</a> means ‘fixed,’ and that it must originally have referred to a real fixed pole star, and he thinks that such a star could only be found in the third millennium B.C. Whitney and Oldenberg definitely reject this view on the ground that too much must not be made out of a piece of folk-lore, and that the marriage ritual requirements would be satisfied by any star of some magnitude which was approximately polar. This conclusion seems convincing. <i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1909, 1102; 1910, 465; <i>contra,</i> Jacobi, <i>ibid.,</i> 1909, 726 <i>et seq.;</i> 1910, 464. (2) The Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 2, 3, asserts that the <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्तिकास्">kṛttikās</a> do not move from the eastern quarter, which the others do; and stress has been laid (by Jacobi, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1910, 463, 464) upon this assertion as giving a date of the third millennium B.C. for the Śatapatha observation. But this notice is quite inadequate to support any such result, and its lack of trustworthiness as a chronological guide is increased by the fact that the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xviii 5, has a similar notice, coupled with another notice, which, according to Barth, would only be true somewhere in or after the sixth century A.D., the equatorial point being placed between <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्रा">citrā</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="स्वाती">svātī</a>, which in the early period were both very much north of the equator (see Caland, <i>Über das rituelle <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> des <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>,</i> 37-39). The same passage of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 2, 2, in the Mādhyaṃdina recension, states that the number of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्तिकास्">kṛttikās</a> is greater than that of the stars in any of the other Nakṣatras, which consist of one, two, three, or four stars, or which, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension (see Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 282, n. 2), have four stars. It is not possible to put much faith in this assertion, for <a class="vei_120a" href="हस्त">hasta</a> later has five stars, and its name (with reference to the fingers) suggests five (<i>cf.</i> Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 368, 381), and that number is possibly referred to in the Rigveda (i. 105, 10). See Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 177. (3) Attempts have been made to regard the names of the Nakṣatras as significant of their position in the list. Thus Bentley, <i>Historical View,</i> 2, thought Viśākhā was so called because the equinoctial colure divided the equator about 1426 B.C.; this is refuted by Tilak, <i>Orion,</i> 57 <i>et seq.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्येष्ठघ्नी">jyeṣṭhaghnī</a> has been interpreted as ‘slaying the eldest’--<i>i.e.,</i> as marking the new year by putting an end to the old year. Tilak, 90, suggests that <a class="vei_120a" href="मूल">mūla</a> was so called because its acronycal rising marked the beginning of the year when the vernal equinox was near <a class="vei_120a" href="मृगशिरस्">mṛgaśiras</a>. More probable is Whitney's view, <i>Sārya Siddhānta,</i> 194, that it was the most southern, and so, as it were, the basis of the asterisms.</li><li id="fx_120_157_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_157_vei_1201496">↑</a> Max Müller, <i>Rigveda,</i> 4^2, xliv <i>et seq.,</i> maintains the Indian origin of the system. Thrbaut, <i>Astronomie, Astrologie und Mathematik,</i> 14, 15, admits it to be possible, as does Whitney, <i>Oriental and Linguistic Essays,</i> 2, 418.</li><li id="fx_120_158_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_158_vei_1201496">↑</a> 2 Kings xxiii. 5; Job xxxviii. 32.</li><li id="fx_120_159_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_159_vei_1201496">↑</a> Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 1, 317, 318; Whitney, <i>op, <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 359.</li><li id="fx_120_160_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_160_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 8, 309-334. This was Weber's view also, according to Whitney, 413 <i>et seq.;</i> but Weber himself disclaimed it (see <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 425, 426; 10, 246, 247). On the other hand, Sédillot, <i>Matériaux pour servir à l'histoire comparée des 'Sciences Mathématiques par les Grecs et les Orientaux</i> (Paris, 1845-1849), favoured influence from Arabia on India.</li><li id="fx_120_161_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_161_vei_1201496">↑</a> Summed up in his two works, <i>Recherches sur l'ancienne astronomie Chinoise,</i> and <i>Études sur l'astronomie Indieune et l'astronomie Chinoise.</i></li><li id="fx_120_162_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_162_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Naxatra,</i> 1, 284 <i>et seq.</i> (1860).</li><li id="fx_120_163_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_163_vei_1201496">↑</a> See Chavannes, cited by Oldenberg, <i>Nachrichten der ko7nigl. Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften zu Gōttingen,</i> 1909, 566, 567.</li><li id="fx_120_164_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_164_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 8, 1 <i>et seq.; Oriental and Linguistic Essays,</i> 2, 385 <i>et seq.</i> For his controversy with Weber, see Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 424 <i>et seq.;</i> 10, 213 <i>et seq.;</i> Whitney, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 8, 384 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_165_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_165_vei_1201496">↑</a> T'oung Pao, 1909, 121 <i>et seq.;</i> 255 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_166_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_166_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Nachrichten,</i> 1909, 544-572.</li><li id="fx_120_167_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_167_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 548, n. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_168_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_168_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenlāndischen Gesellschaft,</i> 45, 592 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_169_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_169_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Journal of the Asiatic Society of Bengal,</i> 63. 144-163. <i>Cf. Astronomie,</i> etc., 15; Oldenberg, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 572.</li><li id="fx_120_170_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_170_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Naxatra,</i> 1, 316 <i>et seq.; Indische Studien,</i> 10, 246, and elsewhere. Weber, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>,</i> 2, 362, 400, laid great stress on the fact that the <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्योतिष">jyotiṣa</a>, 8, referred to the difference of the longest and shortest day as being six <i>muhūrtas,</i> which makes the longest day fourteen hours twenty-four minutes: and he compared the Babylonian day of fourteen hours twenty-five minutes, and a Chinese day of fourteen hours twentyfour minutes. But Whitney, <i>Oriental and Linguistic Essays,</i> 2, 417, 418, shows that no stress can be laid on this argument, since the correspondence is only approximate, and the latitudes of the Babylonian and Chinese observations are approximately the same.</li><li id="fx_120_171_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_171_vei_1201496">↑</a> See <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 418-420.</li><li id="fx_120_172_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_172_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 356, 357, where he is quite confident of the Semitic origin of the Nakṣatras.</li><li id="fx_120_173_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_173_vei_1201496">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 572.</li><li id="fx_120_174_vei_1201496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_174_vei_1201496">↑</a> For the flood, see Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 101, 357, who is opposed to Weber's view (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 160; <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 11) that the story preserves an old Āryan tradition, and a reminiscence of the home of the Indians beyond the Himālaya (<i>cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 190; 2^2, 323, n. 96; Lassen, <i>Indische Alterthumskunde,</i> 1^2, 638, and <i>cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 276, n. 3). For the Ādityas, see Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 185 <i>et seq.; Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 50, 43 <i>et seq.</i> His view is not accepted by Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 44; Bloomfield, <i>Religion of the <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 133. Still more doubtful is Zimmer's view (<i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 363, 364) of the division of day and night into thirty parts, which he sees in Rv. i. 123, 8, and which he thinks is based on the Babylonian division of the same period of time into sixtieths. <i>Cf.</i> also V. Smith, <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 34, 230, who argues, but inconclusively, that the use of iron was introduced from Babylonia.</p><p>The facts about the Nakṣatras are (with the exception of the data from the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā and the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>) collected in Weber's second essay, <i>Die vedischen Nachrichten von den Naxatra,</i> 1861. The first essay, 1860, deals with the problem of origins. See also his discussions in <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 424 <i>et seq.;</i> 10, 213 <i>et seq.</i> Whitney's work lies partly in his scientific determination (in many places correcting Colebrooke's discoveries) of the later Naxatras in his edition and version of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूर्य">sūrya</a> Siddhānta (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 6), and partly in his discussions of the question of origin (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 8), <i>Oriental and Linguistic Essays,</i> 2, 341-421 (with a stellar chart), and of the question of date as against Jacobi and Tilak's <i>Orion</i> (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, lxxxii <i>et seq.</i>). The views of Max Müller are found in his <i>Rigveda,</i> 4^2, xxxiv <i>et seq.</i> The modern discussion of the dates inferable from the <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> was inaugurated by Jacobi (1893) in the <i>Festgruss an Roth,</i> 68-74 (translated in the <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 23). See also his articles in the <i>Nachrichten der kōnigl. Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften zu Gōttingen,</i> 1894, 110 <i>et seq.; Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 49, 218 <i>et seq.;</i> 50, 70 <i>et seq.; Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1909, 721727. Independently Tilak, in his <i>Orion,</i> developed similar views; but most of his special points are disposed of by Whitney in his review cited above. Oldenberg has discussed and refuted Jacobi's arguments in the <i>Zeitschrift,</i> 48, 629 <i>et seq.;</i> 49, 470 <i>et seq.;</i> 50, 450 <i>et seq.; Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1909, 1090 <i>et seq.</i> Thibaut has also rejected Jacobi's views in an article in the <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 24, 85 <i>et seq.</i> See also his <i>Astronomie, Astrologie und Mathematik,</i> 17-19. The recent literature on the origin of the Nakṣatras consists of articles by Thibaut, <i>Journa of the Asiatic Society of Bengal,</i> 63, 144 <i>et seq.;</i> Saussure, <i>Toung Pao,</i> 1909, 121 <i>et seq.;</i> 255 <i>et seq.;</i> Oldenberg, <i>Nachrichten der ko7nigl. Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften zu Go7ttingen,</i> 1909, 544 <i>et seq.</i> The Nakṣatras in the Epic are dealt with by Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 24, 29-36. Ludwig's views are given in his Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 183 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

नक्षत्र-दर्श|नक्षत्रदर्श|नक्षत्र|दर्श|నక్షత్ర-దర్శ|నక్షత్రదర్శ|నక్షత్ర|దర్శ|ನಕ್ಷತ್ರ-ದರ್ಶ|ನಕ್ಷತ್ರದರ್ಶ|ನಕ್ಷತ್ರ|ದರ್ಶ|nakṣatra-darśa|nakṣatradarśa|nakṣatra|darśa|nakShatra-darsha|nakShatradarsha|nakShatra|darsha|naxatra-darsha|naxatradarsha|naxatra|_vei_120@1497|_vei1_pn431
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1496">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1498">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn419">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn431">1:431</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn432">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nakṣatra-darśa</b> (‘gazer at the lunar mansions’), an ‘astrologer,’ is mentioned in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha, or ‘human sacrifice,’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201497">1</a></sup> A notice in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201497">2</a></sup> indicates that that work regarded the practice of choosing a particular <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> under which to set up the sacrificial fires as an idle one, because it decides in favour of choosing the sun as one's <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201497">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 4, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201497">↑</a> ii. 1, 2, 19, and <i>cf.</i> the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> text in Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> xii, 288, n. 3.</li></ol></div>

नक्षत्र-विद्या|नक्षत्रविद्या|नक्षत्र|विद्या|నక్షత్ర-విద్యా|నక్షత్రవిద్యా|నక్షత్ర|విద్యా|ನಕ್ಷತ್ರ-ವಿದ್ಯಾ|ನಕ್ಷತ್ರವಿದ್ಯಾ|ನಕ್ಷತ್ರ|ವಿದ್ಯಾ|nakṣatra-vidyā|nakṣatravidyā|nakṣatra|vidyā|nakShatra-vidyaa|nakShatravidyaa|nakShatra|vidyaa|naxatra-vidyaa|naxatravidyaa|naxatra|_vei_120@1498|_vei1_pn431
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1497">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1499">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn419">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn431">1:431</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn432">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nakṣatra-vidyā,</b> the ‘science of the lunar mansions,’ ‘astronomy,’ is mentioned with other sciences in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vii. 1, 2. 4; 2, 1; 7, 1).</p></div>

नख|నఖ|ನಖ|nakha|_vei_120@1499|_vei1_pn431
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1498">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1500">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn419">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn431">1:431</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn432">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nakha</b> denotes either the ‘nail’ of a man,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201499">1</a></sup> or the ‘claw’ of a wild beast, such as a tiger.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201499">2</a></sup> The trimming (<i>nikṛntana</i>)<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201499">3</a></sup> of the nails was a regular part of the toilet of the Vedic Indian, especially on occasions of special sanctity, when it accompanied the cleansing of the teeth.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201499">4</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201499">↑</a> Rv. i. 162, 9; x. 163, 5; Av. ii. 33, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201499">↑</a> Rv. iv. 3, 3. <i>Cf.</i> x. 28, 10, of the eagle's talon.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201499">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201499">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 1, 7; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 6, 2, etc.; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 3, 4.</li></ol></div>

न-ग|नग|న-గ|నగ|ನ-ಗ|ನಗ|na-ga|naga|_vei_120@1500|_vei1_pn432
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1499">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1501">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn431">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn432">1:432</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn433">↷</a><hr><p><b>Na-ga</b> (‘not moving’), ‘mountain,’ is a word occurring only in a late book of the Atharvaveda (xix. 8, 1), and then in the Sūtras.</p></div>

नगर|నగర|ನಗರ|nagara|_vei_120@1501|_vei1_pn432
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1500">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1502">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn431">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn432">1:432</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn433">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nagara</b> is in early Vedic literature found only in the derivative adjective, used as a proper name, <b>Nagarin,</b> but it appears in the sense of ‘town’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka (i. 11, 18; 31, 4), and frequently in the later language.</p></div>

नगरिन् जान-श्रुतेय|नगरिन् जानश्रुतेय|नगरिन् जान|श्रुतेय|నగరిన్ జాన-శ్రుతేయ|నగరిన్ జానశ్రుతేయ|నగరిన్ జాన|శ్రుతేయ|ನಗರಿನ್ ಜಾನ-ಶ್ರುತೇಯ|ನಗರಿನ್ ಜಾನಶ್ರುತೇಯ|ನಗರಿನ್ ಜಾನ|ಶ್ರುತೇಯ|nagarin jāna-śruteya|nagarin jānaśruteya|nagarin jāna|śruteya|nagarin jaana-shruteya|nagarin jaanashruteya|nagarin jaana|shruteya|_vei_120@1502|_vei1_pn432
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1501">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1503">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn431">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn432">1:432</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn433">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nagarin <a class="vei_120a" href="जान">jāna</a>-śruteya</b> (‘descendant of Janaśruti’) is mentioned as a priest in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (v. 30), and as <a class="vei_120a" href="नगरिन् जानश्रुतेय">nagarin jānaśruteya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्ड्विय">kāṇḍviya</a> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 40, 2).</p></div>

नग्न-जित्|नग्नजित्|नग्न|जित्|నగ్న-జిత్|నగ్నజిత్|నగ్న|జిత్|ನಗ್ನ-ಜಿತ್|ನಗ್ನಜಿತ್|ನಗ್ನ|ಜಿತ್|nagna-jit|nagnajit|nagna|jit|_vei_120@1503|_vei1_pn432
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1502">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1504">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn431">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn432">1:432</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn433">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nagna-jit,</b> King of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गन्धार">gandhāra</a>,</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201503">1</a></sup> as having been consecrated by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्वत">parvata</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नारद">nārada</a>.</b> The same king is mentioned with his son <b>Svarjit</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201503">2</a></sup> where a remark attributed to one of them on a ritual topic is treated with contempt.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201503">↑</a> vii. 34.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201503">↑</a> viii. 1, 4, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 132, 134; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 515.</li></ol></div>

नग्ना|నగ్నా|ನಗ್ನಾ|nagnā|nagnaa|_vei_120@1504|_vei1_pn432
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1503">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1505">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn431">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn432">1:432</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn433">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nagnā.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a>.</b></p></div>

नघ-मार|नघमार|नघ|मार|నఘ-మార|నఘమార|నఘ|మార|ನಘ-ಮಾರ|ನಘಮಾರ|ನಘ|ಮಾರ|nagha-māra|naghamāra|nagha|māra|nagha-maara|naghamaara|maara|_vei_120@1505|_vei1_pn432
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1504">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1506">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn431">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn432">1:432</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn433">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nagha-māra</b> and <b>Naghā-riṣa.</b> See 1. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुष्ठ">kuṣṭha</a>.</b></p></div>

न-चिकेतस्|न-चिकेत|नचिकेतस्|नचिकेत|चिकेतस्|चिकेत|న-చికేతస్|న-చికేత|నచికేతస్|నచికేత|చికేతస్|చికేత|ನ-ಚಿಕೇತಸ್|ನ-ಚಿಕೇತ|ನಚಿಕೇತಸ್|ನಚಿಕೇತ|ಚಿಕೇತಸ್|ಚಿಕೇತ|na-ciketas|na-ciketa|naciketas|naciketa|ciketas|ciketa|na-chiketas|na-chiketa|nachiketas|nachiketa|chiketas|chiketa|_vei_120@1506|_vei1_pn432
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1505">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1507">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn431">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn432">1:432</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn433">↷</a><hr><p><b>Na-ciketas</b> occurs in the well-known legend of the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201506">1</a></sup> (where he is a <a class="vei_120a" href="गोतम">gotama</a>, the son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वाजश्रवस">vājaśravasa</a></b>), and in the Kaṭha <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201506">2</a></sup> His historical reality is extremely doubtful: in the <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> he is called son of <a class="vei_120a" href="आरुणि">āruṇi</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="औद्दालकि">auddālaki</a> or <a class="vei_120a" href="वाजश्रवस">vājaśravasa</a>, an impossible attribution, and one due only to a desire to give <a class="vei_120a" href="नचिकेतस्">naciketas</a> a connexion with the famous <a class="vei_120a" href="आरुणि">āruṇi</a>. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201506">↑</a> iii. 11, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201506">↑</a> i. 1, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 154, n, 1; Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 157; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 168.</li></ol></div>

नड|नड|నడ_౧|నడ|ನಡ_೧|ನಡ|naḍa_1|naḍa|naDa_1|naDa|_vei_120@1507|_vei1_pn433
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1506">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1508">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn432">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn433">1:433</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn434">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Naḍa,</b> ‘reed,’ is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201507"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201507">1</a></sup> as growing in lakes, and in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201507"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201507">2</a></sup> is described as <i>vārṣika,</i> ‘produced in the rains.’ Reeds were used, after being split, for making mats, a work carried out by women.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201507"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201507">3</a></sup> They are frequently mentioned elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201507"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201507">4</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नद">nada</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201507"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201507">↑</a> viii. 1, 33.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201507"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201507">↑</a> iv. 19, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201507"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201507">↑</a> Av. vi. 138, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201507"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201507">↑</a> Av. vi. 137, 2; xii. 2, 1, 19. 50. 54; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxv. 7; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 4, 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, vi. 7, 10.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 71.</li></ol></div>

नड नैषध|नड नैषध|నడ నైషధ_౨|నడ నైషధ|ನಡ ನೈಷಧ_೨|ನಡ ನೈಷಧ|naḍa naiṣadha_2|naḍa naiṣadha|naDa naiShadha_2|naDa naiShadha|_vei_120@1508|_vei1_pn433
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1507">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1509">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn432">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn433">1:433</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn434">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Naḍa Naiṣadha</b> is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201508"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201508">1</a></sup> where he seems to be<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201508"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201508">2</a></sup> a human king who is compared with <a class="vei_120a" href="यम">yama</a>, the god of death, because of his conquests. Being there identified with the southern sacrificial fire, he was presumably a king of the south, just as <a class="vei_120a" href="यम">yama</a> is connected with the south.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201508"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201508">↑</a> ii. 2, 2, 1. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201508"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201508">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 225-227, followed by Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 338, notes 4 and 5. The printed text has <a class="vei_120a" href="नैषिध">naiṣidha</a> by error.</li></ol></div>

नड्वला|నడ్వలా|ನಡ್ವಲಾ|naḍvalā|naDvalaa|_vei_120@1509|_vei1_pn433
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1508">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1510">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn432">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn433">1:433</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn434">↷</a><hr><p><b>Naḍvalā,</b> a ‘reed bed,’ is mentioned in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā (xxx. 16) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 4, 12, 1).</p></div>

नद|నద|ನದ|nada|_vei_120@1510|_vei1_pn433
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1509">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1511">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn432">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn433">1:433</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn434">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nada</b> is found in several passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201510">1</a></sup> but its sense is still obscure. It is identified by Pischel<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201510">2</a></sup> with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नड">naḍa</a>,</b> being explained by him in one passage<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201510">3</a></sup> as a reed boat, which is split, and over which the waters go; in another<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201510">4</a></sup> as a reed whip, of which the sharp points (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कर्ण">karṇa</a></i>) are used to urge horses on; and in others<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201510">5</a></sup> again as figuratively designating the penis. Roth<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201510">6</a></sup> takes the sense to be ‘bull’ (either literally or meta phorically) in all passages. Once at least<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201510">7</a></sup> the ‘neigher’ (from the root <i>nad,</i> ‘sound’) seems to be meant with reference to Indra's horse. In the phrase <i>nadasya karṇaiḥ</i>^8 the sense is, perhaps, ‘through the ears of the (side) horse’ (that is, by their being ready to hear the word of command) of their chariot, the Maruts ‘hasten on with their swift steeds’ (<i>turayanta āśubhiḥ</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201510">↑</a> i. 32, 8; 179, 4; ii. 34, 3; viii. 69, 2; x. 11, 2; 105, 4. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201510">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 35, 717 <i>et seq.; Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 183 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201510">↑</a> i. 32, 8. Here Caland and Henry, <i>L'Agniṣṭoma,</i> 312, n., would read <i>naḷam.</i> See also Wackernagel, <i>Altindische Grammatik,</i> 1, 173.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201510">↑</a> ii. 34, 3, followed by Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 301 (who, however, does not construe <i>āśubhiḥ,</i> ‘sharp,’ with <i>karṇaiḥ</i> as Pischel does in <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 190). He sees ‘reed’ also in x. 11, 2, but ‘horse’ in x. 105, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201510">↑</a> i. 179, 4; viii. 69, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201510">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> The sense of ‘bull’ seems imperative in viii. 89, 2; it is admissible in i. 179, 4, where ‘bull’ may denote a man, and in x. 11, 2, and possibly in i. 32, 8, but ‘reed’ there seems far more likely.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201510">↑</a> x. 105, 4. and in x. 11, 2. The latter passage suggests that ‘river’ may, after all, be the sense there.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 32, 178, 215.</li></ol></div>

नदी|नदि|నదీ|నది|ನದೀ|ನದಿ|nadī|nadi|nadii|nadee|_vei_120@1511|_vei1_pn434
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1510">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1512">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn433">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn434">1:434</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn435">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nadī,</b> ‘stream,’ is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201511">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201511">2</a></sup> Reference is made to shallows (<i>gādha</i>)<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201511">3</a></sup> in the river's bed, to the opposite bank (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पार">pāra</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201511">4</a></sup> and to the bathing of horses in streams.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201511">5</a></sup> Rivers are also mentioned in close connexion with mountains.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201511">6</a></sup> The title <a class="vei_120a" href="नदी">nadī</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>, ‘lord of rivers,’<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201511">7</a></sup> is once used to express ‘ocean’ or ‘sea-water.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201511">↑</a> i. 158, 5; ii. 35, 3; iii. 33, 4; v. 46, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201511">↑</a> Av. iii. 13, 1; xiv. 1, 43.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201511">↑</a> Rv. vii. 60, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201511">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 1, 6, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201511">↑</a> Rv. viii. 2, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201511">↑</a> Rv. v. 55, 7; x. 64, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201511">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 4, 10.</li></ol></div>

नना|ననా|ನನಾ|nanā|nanaa|_vei_120@1512|_vei1_pn434
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1511">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1513">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn433">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn434">1:434</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn435">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nanā</b> is a familiar name for mother, parallel with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तत">tata</a>,</b> for father, with which it is found in a verse of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201512"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201512">1</a></sup> describing the occupations of the parents of the poet.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201512"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201512">↑</a> ix. 112, 3. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 6, and see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपल-प्रक्षिणी">upala-prakṣiṇī</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

ननान्दृ|ననాన్దృ|ನನಾನ್ದೃ|nanāndṛ|nanaandR|nanaandRi|nanaandRu|nanaandRRi|_vei_120@1513|_vei1_pn434
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1512">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1514">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn433">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn434">1:434</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn435">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nanāndṛ</b> is a word occurring only once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201513"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201513">1</a></sup> where it denotes, according to Sāyaṇa, the ‘husband's sister,’ over whom the wife is to rule. This interpretation is confirmed by the fact that the same position is ascribed to the husband's sister--no doubt while unmarried and living in her brother's care--by the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201513"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201513">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201513"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201513">↑</a> x. 85, 46.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201513"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201513">↑</a> iii. 22.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 516. The native lexicographers recognize the word, though its occurrence in the later literature is very rare (it has been noted in the Uttararāmacarita). See the St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

नपात्|నపాత్|ನಪಾತ್|napāt|napaat|_vei_120@1514|_vei1_pn435
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1513">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1515">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn434">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn435">1:435</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn436">↷</a><hr><p><b>Napāt</b> in Vedic literature apparently has both the wider sense of ‘descendant,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201514">1</a></sup> and the narrower one of ‘grandson’ in the Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201514">2</a></sup> In the Brāhmaṇas the word seems hardly to have the sense of ‘descendant’ at all, while it denotes not only ‘grandson,’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201514">3</a></sup> but also ‘great-grandson’ in the sequence ‘sons, grandsons, great-grandsons’ (<i>putrān, pautrān, naptṝn</i>).<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201514">4</a></sup> ‘Grandson’ is also expressed by <a class="vei_120a" href="पौत्र">pautra</a> (‘son's son’) in the Atharvaveda and later,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201514">5</a></sup> while the sense of ‘great-grandson’ is accurately conveyed as early as the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201514">6</a></sup> by <a class="vei_120a" href="प्र-णपात्">pra-ṇapāt</a>, used beside <a class="vei_120a" href="नपात्">napāt</a>, ‘grandson.’ Naptī, the feminine, is practically limited to the Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201514">7</a></sup> and denotes ‘daughter.’ The use in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a> throws no light on the original use of the word.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201514">8</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201514">↑</a> It is equivalent to ‘son’ in a number of mythological epithets such as <i>apāṃ <a class="vei_120a" href="नपात्">napāt</a>,</i> ‘son of waters.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201514">↑</a> Rv. x. 10, 1, clearly ‘son’; vi. 20, 11, may be ‘grandson.’ Most passages, vi. 50, 15; vii. 18, 22; viii. 65, 12; 102, 7; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxi. 61; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxii. 2, require ‘descendant.’</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201514">↑</a> As in <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 48; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पुत्र">putra</a>-naptāraḥ.</i> ‘sons and grandsons.’ <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, viii. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201514">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 10, 3; Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 11, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201514">↑</a> Av. ix. 5, 30; xi. 7, 16; Aitarey <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 10, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 8, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201514">↑</a> Rv. viii. 17, 13, with <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नपात्">napāt</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201514">↑</a> Rv. iii. 31, 1 (<a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 4); viii. 2, 42. <i>Cf.</i> i. 50, 9; ix. 9, 1; 14, 5; 69, 3; Av. i. 28, 4; ii. 14, 1; vii. 82, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201514">↑</a> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 403-405; Lanmann, <i>Festgruss an Bo7htlingk,</i> 77.</li></ol></div>

नप्त्री|नप्त्रि|నప్త్రీ|నప్త్రి|ನಪ್ತ್ರೀ|ನಪ್ತ್ರಿ|naptrī|naptri|naptrii|naptree|_vei_120@1515|_vei1_pn435
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1514">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1516">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn434">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn435">1:435</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn436">↷</a><hr><p><b>Naptrī,</b> as feminine of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नपात्">napāt</a>,</b> is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>, Āraṇya (v. 13).</p></div>

नभ(स्) नभस्य|నభ(స్) నభస్య|ನಭ(ಸ್) ನಭಸ್ಯ|nabha(s) nabhasya|_vei_120@1516|_vei1_pn435
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1515">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1517">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn434">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn435">1:435</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn436">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nabha(s) Nabhasya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></p></div>

नभाक|నభాక|ನಭಾಕ|nabhāka|nabhaaka|_vei_120@1517|_vei1_pn435
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1516">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1518">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn434">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn435">1:435</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn436">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nabhāka</b> is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> who is referred to in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201517"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201517">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201517"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201517">2</a></sup> The Anukramaṇī (Index) attributes to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नाभाक">nābhāka</a></b> the composition of several hymns of the Rigveda (viii. 39-42). </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201517"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201517">↑</a> viii. 40, 4. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201517"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201517">↑</a> vi. 24.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 107.</li></ol></div>

नभ्य|నభ్య|ನಭ್ಯ|nabhya|_vei_120@1518|_vei1_pn436
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1517">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1519">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn435">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn436">1:436</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn437">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nabhya,</b> the ‘nave’ of the wheel, is mentioned in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201518"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201518">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201518"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201518">2</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नाभि">nābhi</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201518"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201518">↑</a> vi. 70, 3; xii. 1, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201518"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201518">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 15; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 5, 3, 20; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 4; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 3, 23, etc.</li></ol></div>

नमी साप्य|నమీ సాప్య|ನಮೀ ಸಾಪ್ಯ|namī sāpya|namii saapya|namee saapya|_vei_120@1519|_vei1_pn436
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1518">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1520">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn435">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn436">1:436</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn437">↷</a><hr><p><b>Namī Sāpya</b> is the name of a man in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201519">1</a></sup> Weber<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201519">2</a></sup> thinks that he is mentioned as a priest, but the passages suit a king better, and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201519">3</a></sup> he appears as <a class="vei_120a" href="नमी साप्य">namī sāpya</a>, <i>Vaidcho rājā,</i> ‘King of <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>.’ In one passage<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201519">4</a></sup> he is represented as engaged in the contest against Namuci.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201519">↑</a> vi. 20, 6; x. 48, 9. Simply Namī in i. 53, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201519">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 231, 232.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201519">↑</a> xxv. 10, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201519">↑</a> Rv. i. 53, 7.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 149; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 161; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 49. <a class="vei_120a" href="साप्य">sāpya</a> may be read <a class="vei_120a" href="साय्य">sāyya</a>, but Sāyaṇa recognizes the <i>p;</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutscheu Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 55, 328.</li></ol></div>

नर|नृ|నర|నృ|ನರ|ನೃ|nara|nṛ|nR|nRi|nRu|nRRi|_vei_120@1520|_vei1_pn436
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1519">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1521">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn435">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn436">1:436</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn437">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nara, Nṛ.</b>--The general name for ‘man’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201520"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201520">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201520"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201520">2</a></sup> is Nṛ, while <a class="vei_120a" href="नर">nara</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201520"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201520">3</a></sup> is found occasionally in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201520"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201520">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201520"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201520">↑</a> i. 25, 5; 167, 20; 178, 3; ii. 34, 6; iii. 16, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201520"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201520">↑</a> Av. ii. 9, 2; ix. 1, 3; xiv. 2, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 34; vi. 27. 32, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201520"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201520">↑</a> This form of the word, common in the post-Vedic language, is secondary, having originated from cases like <i>naram,</i> understood as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नर">nara</a>-m;</i> but its origin goes back to the Indo-Iranian period. See Brugmann, <i>Grundriss,</i> 2, 106. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> 318, <i>a</i> 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201520"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201520">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 1, 12, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 3, 1, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

नराची|नराचि|నరాచీ|నరాచి|ನರಾಚೀ|ನರಾಚಿ|narācī|narāci|naraachii|naraachi|naraachee|_vei_120@1521|_vei1_pn436
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1520">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1522">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn435">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn436">1:436</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn437">↷</a><hr><p><b>Narācī</b> occurs once in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201521">1</a></sup> perhaps meaning a poisonous plant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201521">↑</a> v. 31, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 286.</li></ol></div>

नर्य|నర్య|ನರ್ಯ|narya|_vei_120@1522|_vei1_pn436
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1521">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1523">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn435">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn436">1:436</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn437">↷</a><hr><p><b>Narya</b> (‘manly’) is in two passages of the Rigveda (i. 54, 6; 112, 9) understood by the commentator Sāyaṇa as the proper name of a man. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नार्य">nārya</a>.</b> </p></div>

नलद|నలద|ನಲದ|nalada|_vei_120@1523|_vei1_pn437
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1522">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1524">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn436">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn437">1:437</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn438">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nalada,</b> ‘nard’ (<i>Nardastachys Jatamansi</i>) is a plant mentioned in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201523">1</a></sup> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201523">2</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201523">3</a></sup> Āraṇyakas (where it is mentioned as used for a garland), as well as in the Sūtras. In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201523">4</a></sup> the feminine form of the word, Naladī, occurs as the name of an Apsaras, or celestial nymph.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201523">↑</a> vi. 102, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201523">↑</a> iii. 2, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201523">↑</a> xi. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201523">↑</a> iv. 37, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 68, 69; Grohmann, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 420; Caland, <i>Altindisches Zauberritual,</i> 177, n. 4.</li></ol></div>

नवक|నవక|ನವಕ|navaka|_vei_120@1524|_vei1_pn437
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1523">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1525">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn436">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn437">1:437</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn438">↷</a><hr><p><b>Navaka</b> is mentioned as having wished for a wife at the Sattra of the <b>Vibhindukīyas</b> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201524"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201524">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201524"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201524">↑</a> ii. 233 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 38).</li></ol></div>

नव-ग्व|नवग्व|नव|ग्व|నవ-గ్వ|నవగ్వ|నవ|గ్వ|ನವ-ಗ್ವ|ನವಗ್ವ|ನವ|ಗ್ವ|nava-gva|navagva|nava|gva|_vei_120@1525|_vei1_pn437
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1524">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1526">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn436">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn437">1:437</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn438">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nava-gva</b> occurs in several passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201525"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201525">1</a></sup> as a man, an <a class="vei_120a" href="अंगिरस्">aṅgiras</a> in the highest degree (<i>Aṅgirastama</i>),<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201525"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201525">2</a></sup> apparently being the type of the Navagvas,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201525"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201525">3</a></sup> who appear as a mystic race of olden times, coupled with, and conceived probably as related to, the Aṅgirases. They are often associated with the <b>Daśagvas.</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201525"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201525">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201525"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201525">↑</a> iv. 51, 4; ix. 108, 4; x. 62, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201525"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201525">↑</a> x. 62, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201525"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201525">↑</a> Rv. i. 62, 4; iii. 39, 5; v. 29, 12; 45, 7. 11; vi. 22, 2; x. 14, 6; 61, 10; 108, 8; Av. xiv. 1, 56; xviii. 3, 20, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201525"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201525">↑</a> Rv. i. 62, 4; iv. 51, 4; v. 29, 12; x. 62, 6, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig. Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 165; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> pp. 144 (B), 170.</li></ol></div>

नव-नीत|नवनीत|नव|नीत|నవ-నీత|నవనీత|నవ|నీత|ನವ-ನೀತ|ನವನೀತ|ನವ|ನೀತ|nava-nīta|navanīta|nava|nīta|nava-niita|navaniita|niita|nava-neeta|navaneeta|neeta|_vei_120@1526|_vei1_pn437
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1525">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1527">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn436">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn437">1:437</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn438">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nava-nīta,</b> ‘fresh butter,’ is mentioned frequently in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201526"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201526">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201526"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201526">2</a></sup> According to the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201526"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201526">3</a></sup> this is the kind of butter which is fitted for anointing an embryo (<i>garbha</i>), while the gods receive <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आज्य">ājya</a>,</b> men fragrant ghee (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="घृत">ghṛta</a></b>), and the fathers <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आयुत">āyuta</a>.</b> Elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201526"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201526">4</a></sup> it is contrasted with <a class="vei_120a" href="घृत">ghṛta</a> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सर्पिस्">sarpis</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201526"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201526">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā. ii. 3, 10, 1; vi. 1, 1, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 7; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201526"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201526">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 3, 7. 8; v. 3, 2, 6; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 5, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201526"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201526">↑</a> i. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201526"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201526">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 10, 1, etc</li></ol></div>

नव-वास्त्व|नववास्त्व|नव|वास्त्व|నవ-వాస్త్వ|నవవాస్త్వ|నవ|వాస్త్వ|ನವ-ವಾಸ್ತ್ವ|ನವವಾಸ್ತ್ವ|ನವ|ವಾಸ್ತ್ವ|nava-vāstva|navavāstva|nava|vāstva|nava-vaastva|navavaastva|vaastva|_vei_120@1527|_vei1_pn438
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1526">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1528">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn437">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn438">1:438</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn439">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nava-vāstva</b> appears in three passages of the Rigveda. In one<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201527"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201527">1</a></sup> he seems to be a protégé of <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>; in another<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201527"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201527">2</a></sup> as perhaps a son of <b>Uśanas</b> and favourite of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, but in the last he seems to be defeated, or even slain, by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201527"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201527">3</a></sup> But he may be a mythic figure altogether. <i>Cf.</i> also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बृहद्रथ">bṛhadratha</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201527"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201527">↑</a> i. 36, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201527"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201527">↑</a> vi. 20, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201527"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201527">↑</a> x. 49, 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bergaigne, <i>Religion Védique,</i> 2, 223; Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 1, 581; Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 128, 129; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 147; <i>Uber die neuesten Arbeiten auf dem Gebiete der Ṛgvedaforschung,</i> 160; Perry, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 11, 202; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 158.</li></ol></div>

नह्|నహ్|ನಹ್|nah|_vei_120@1528|_vei1_pn438
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1527">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1529">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn437">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn438">1:438</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn439">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nah</b> has been taken by Roth<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201528"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201528">1</a></sup> and Grassmann<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201528"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201528">2</a></sup> to be the stem, meaning ‘bond,’ of the dative form <i>nadbhyas,</i> which occurs once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201528"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201528">3</a></sup> and which Sieg<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201528"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201528">4</a></sup> thinks means ‘sister's sons.’ But the sense of this dative is probably rather ‘to the grandsons.’<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201528"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201528">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201528"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201528">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201528"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201528">↑</a> <i>Wo7rterbuch, s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201528"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201528">↑</a> x. 60, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201528"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201528">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 129.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201528"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201528">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> p. 56, 3<i>b.</i></li></ol></div>

नहुस्|नहु|నహుస్|నహు|ನಹುಸ್|ನಹು|nahus|nahu|_vei_120@1529|_vei1_pn438
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1528">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1530">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn437">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn438">1:438</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn439">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nahus</b> occurs several times in the Rigveda, but the exact sense is not certain. Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201529">1</a></sup> sees in the <a class="vei_120a" href="नहुस्">nahus</a> a tribe on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धु">sindhu</a></b> (Indus)<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201529">2</a></sup> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201529">3</a></sup> rich in horses,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201529">4</a></sup> allied with the <b>Bharatas</b> and <b>Śimyus,</b><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201529">5</a></sup> connected with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a></b> and the <b>Vārṣāgiras,</b><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201529">6</a></sup> and having as kings <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मशर्शार">maśarśāra</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आयवस">āyavasa</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201529">7</a></sup> Roth,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201529">8</a></sup> on the other hand, sees in <a class="vei_120a" href="नहुस्">nahus</a> the general sense of ‘neighbour’ as opposed to a member of one's own people (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a></b>); this interpretation is supported by the occurrence of the phrase <i>nahuṣo nahuṣṭara,</i><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201529">9</a></sup> ‘closer than a neighbour.’ Nahuṣa has the same sense as <a class="vei_120a" href="नहुस्">nahus</a> in two passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201529">10</a></sup> but in  one it seems to be intended for the proper name of a man.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201529">11</a></sup> Possibly <a class="vei_120a" href="नहुस्">nahus</a> was originally a man like <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201529">12</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201529">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 206.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201529">↑</a> Rv. i. 31, 11; vi. 22, 10; 46, 7; x. 80, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201529">↑</a> Rv. vii. 95, 2. <i>Cf.</i> ix. 88, 2; 91, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201529">↑</a> Rv. viii. 6, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201529">↑</a> Rv. i. 100, 18; vii. 18, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201529">↑</a> Rv. i. 100, 16. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201529">↑</a> Rv. i. 122, 15. <i>Cf.</i> also <i>nahuṣo viśaḥ,</i> Rv. vii. 6, 5; x. 49, 8; 99, 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201529">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201529">↑</a> Rv. x. 49, 8. <i>Cf.</i> also viii. 8, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201529">↑</a> i. 31, 11; v. 12, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201529">↑</a> Rv. viii. 46, 27.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201529">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 46, 28; Bergaigne, <i>Religion Védique,</i> 2, 324. But <a class="vei_120a" href="नहुस्">nahus</a>, if it was originally the name of a mythic forefather, cannot have been that of a forefather recognized by all the tribes, for there is no passage in which it applies to all men. Geldner, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>, Glossar,</i> 92, regards <a class="vei_120a" href="नहुस्">nahus</a> as a tribe, Nahuṣa as a king. Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 128, leaves the question open. <i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 165, n. 7; 179 <i>et seq.;</i> 307 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

नाक|नाक|నాక_౧|నాక|ನಾಕ_೧|ನಾಕ|nāka_1|nāka|naaka_1|naaka|_vei_120@1530|_vei1_pn439
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1529">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1531">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn438">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn439">1:439</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn440">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Nāka</b> denotes the ‘firmament’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201530">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201530">2</a></sup> It is often used with the epithet ‘highest’ (<i>uttama</i>)<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201530">3</a></sup> or ‘third’ (<i>tṛtīya</i>)<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201530">4</a></sup> referring to the threefold division of heaven, parallel to the threefold division of earth, atmosphere, and sky (<b>Div</b>). The <a class="vei_120a" href="नाक">nāka</a> is said to be on the third ridge (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पृष्ठ">pṛṣṭha</a></i>), above the luminous space (<i>rocana</i>) of the sky.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201530">5</a></sup> Elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201530">6</a></sup> the series earth, atmosphere, sky, and the firmament (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="नाक">nāka</a></i>), heaven (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्वर्">svar</a></i>), the celestial light (<i>jyotis</i>), occurs. The word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नाक">nāka</a></i> is explained in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201530">7</a></sup> as derived from <i>na,</i> ‘not,’ and <i>aka,</i> ‘pain,’ because those who go there are free from sorrow.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201530">↑</a> i. 60, 10; 125, 5; iii. 2, 12; iv. 13, 5; vii. 86, 1; 99, 2; viii. 103, 2; ix. 73, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201530">↑</a> Av. vii. 18, 1; xviii. 2, 47; xiii. 1, 7; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xv. 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xviii. 7, 10; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 5, 3, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201530">↑</a> Av. iv. 14, 6; xi. 1, 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ix. 10; xii. 63.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201530">↑</a> Av. vi. 122, 4; ix. 5, 1. 4; xviii. 4, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201530">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xv. 50.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201530">↑</a> Av. iv. 14, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvii. 67. In Rv. x. 121, 5, the earth and sky (<i>dyauḥ</i>), and heaven (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्वर्">svar</a></i>), and the firmament (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="नाक">nāka</a></i>), are all mentioned.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201530">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 1, 18; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 4, 1, 24; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 14; and <i>cf.</i> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 10, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 9; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 50, 56, 57.</li></ol></div>

नाक|नाक|నాక_౨|నాక|ನಾಕ_೨|ನಾಕ|nāka_2|nāka|naaka_2|naaka|_vei_120@1531|_vei1_pn439
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1530">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1532">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn438">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn439">1:439</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn440">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Nāka</b> is the name of a teacher in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201531"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201531">1</a></sup> Presumably he is identical with <a class="vei_120a" href="नाक">nāka</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मौद्गल्य">maudgalya</a> (‘descendant of Mudgala’), who is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201531"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201531">2</a></sup> the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201531"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201531">3</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201531"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201531">4</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201531"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201531">↑</a> iii. 13, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201531"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201531">↑</a> xii. 5, 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201531"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201531">↑</a> vi. 4, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201531"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201531">↑</a> i. 9, 1.</li></ol></div>

नाक्र|నాక్ర|ನಾಕ್ರ|nākra|naakra|_vei_120@1532|_vei1_pn440
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1531">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1533">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn439">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn440">1:440</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn441">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nākra</b> is the name of an aquatic animal included among the victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or ‘horse sacrifice,’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201532"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201532">1</a></sup> Perhaps the animal meant is the crocodile, which later is called Nakra.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201532"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201532">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201532"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201532">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 13, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 35.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201532"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201532">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 96, following one version given by Mahīdhara on Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 21, n. 4.</li></ol></div>

नाग|నాగ|ನಾಗ|nāga|naaga|_vei_120@1533|_vei1_pn440
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1532">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1534">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn439">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn440">1:440</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn441">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nāga</b> appears once in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201533">1</a></sup> in the form <i><a class="vei_120a" href="महानाग">mahānāga</a>,</i> where ‘great snake’ or ‘great elephant’ may be meant. In the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201533">2</a></sup> and in a citation found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201533">3</a></sup> the sense of ‘elephant’ is clearly intended. In the Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201533">4</a></sup> the mythic <a class="vei_120a" href="नाग">nāga</a> already occurs.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201533">↑</a> xi. 2, 7, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201533">↑</a> i. 3, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201533">↑</a> viii. 22.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201533">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 4, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Winternitz, <i>Sarpabali,</i> 43; Mac. donell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 153.</li></ol></div>

नाग्न-जित|नाग्नजित|नाग्न|जित|నాగ్న-జిత|నాగ్నజిత|నాగ్న|జిత|ನಾಗ್ನ-ಜಿತ|ನಾಗ್ನಜಿತ|ನಾಗ್ನ|ಜಿತ|nāgna-jita|nāgnajita|nāgna|jita|naagna-jita|naagnajita|naagna|_vei_120@1534|_vei1_pn440
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1533">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1535">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn439">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn440">1:440</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn441">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nāgna-jita,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नग्नजित्">nagnajit</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b>Svarjit</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 1, 4, 10).</p></div>

नाचिकेत|నాచికేత|ನಾಚಿಕೇತ|nāciketa|naachiketa|_vei_120@1535|_vei1_pn440
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1534">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1536">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn439">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn440">1:440</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn441">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nāciketa,</b> ‘connected with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नचिकेतस्">naciketas</a>,</b>’ is the title of a narrative (<i>upākhyāna</i>) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201535"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201535">1</a></sup> The word is also applied as an epithet to a special fire in that <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201535"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201535">2</a></sup> and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201535"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201535">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201535"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201535">↑</a> iii. 16.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201535"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201535">↑</a> i. 18; ii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201535"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201535">↑</a> i. 22, 11; 26, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 386. The native lexicographers give Nāchiketa and Nāciketu as synonyms of fire generally.</li></ol></div>

नाड-पित्|नाडपित्|नाड|पित्|నాడ-పిత్|నాడపిత్|నాడ|పిత్|ನಾಡ-ಪಿತ್|ನಾಡಪಿತ್|ನಾಡ|ಪಿತ್|nāḍa-pit|nāḍapit|nāḍa|pit|naaDa-pit|naaDapit|naaDa|_vei_120@1536|_vei1_pn440
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1535">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1537">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn439">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn440">1:440</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn441">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nāḍa-pit</b> occurs in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201536"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201536">1</a></sup> as the birthplace of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a>.</b> The word may, however, be read as <a class="vei_120a" href="नाड">nāḍa</a>-pitī, the name of Bharata's mother,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201536"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201536">2</a></sup> but this is less probable. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201536"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201536">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201536"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201536">↑</a> Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 6, n. 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Leumann, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 81.</li></ol></div>

नाडी|नाडी|नाडि|నాడీ_౧|నాడీ|నాడి|ನಾಡೀ_೧|ನಾಡೀ|ನಾಡಿ|nāḍī_1|nāḍī|nāḍi|naaDii_1|naaDii|naaDi|naaDee_1|naaDee|_vei_120@1537|_vei1_pn441
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1536">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1538">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn440">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn441">1:441</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn442">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Nāḍī</b> denotes a ‘vein’ or ‘artery’ in the human body in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201537"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201537">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201537"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201537">2</a></sup> a natural extension of the literal sense of ‘reed.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201537"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201537">↑</a> vi. 138, 4; x. 7, 15. 16.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201537"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201537">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 10; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 4, 5, 2; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 1, 21; iv. 2, 3, etc.; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, viii. 6, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 19.</li></ol></div>

नाडी|नाडी|नाडि|నాడీ_౨|నాడీ|నాడి|ನಾಡೀ_೨|ನಾಡೀ|ನಾಡಿ|nāḍī_2|nāḍī|nāḍi|naaDii_2|naaDii|naaDi|naaDee_2|naaDee|_vei_120@1538|_vei1_pn441
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1537">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1539">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn440">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn441">1:441</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn442">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Nāḍī</b> means a musical instrument, a ‘reed flute,’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201538"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201538">1</a></sup> as well as the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201538"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201538">2</a></sup> where in one passage it is mentioned along with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तूणव">tūṇava</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201538"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201538">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201538"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201538">↑</a> x. 135, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201538"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201538">↑</a> xxiii. 4; xxxiv. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201538"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201538">↑</a> xxxiv. 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 329.</li></ol></div>

नाडी|नाडी|नाडि|నాడీ_౩|నాడీ|నాడి|ನಾಡೀ_೩|ನಾಡೀ|ನಾಡಿ|nāḍī_3|nāḍī|nāḍi|naaDii_3|naaDii|naaDi|naaDee_3|naaDee|_vei_120@1539|_vei1_pn441
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1538">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1540">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn440">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn441">1:441</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn442">↷</a><hr><p>3. <b>Nāḍī</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201539"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201539">1</a></sup> seems to mean the box of the chariot wheel.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201539"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201539">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 8, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 12.</li></ol></div>

नाडीका|నాడీకా|ನಾಡೀಕಾ|nāḍīkā|naaDiikaa|naaDeekaa|_vei_120@1540|_vei1_pn441
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1539">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1541">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn440">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn441">1:441</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn442">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nāḍīkā</b> occurs once in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201540"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201540">1</a></sup> where the sense seems clearly to be ‘wind-pipe,’ with a reference also to the ‘shaft of an arrow’ made of reed.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201540"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201540">↑</a> v. 18, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 229; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 251; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 432.</li></ol></div>

नाथ|నాథ|ನಾಥ|nātha|naatha|_vei_120@1541|_vei1_pn441
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1540">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1542">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn440">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn441">1:441</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn442">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nātha</b> in Vedic literature<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201541"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201541">1</a></sup> appears only as a neuter meaning ‘protection,’ and is of rare occurrence.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201541"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201541">2</a></sup> Generally, too, very little appears in Vedic literature of practices such as those which produced Anglo-Saxon society or the Roman <i>patronatus.</i></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201541"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201541">↑</a> Av. iv. 20, 9; ix. 2, 17; xviii. 1, 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 4, 1. Also in Av. xiii. 2, 37, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नाथ">nātha</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="काम">kāma</a>,</i> ‘seeking help’; xi, 1, 15, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नाथ">nātha</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="विद्">vid</a>,</i> ‘finding help’; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 11, 23, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नाथ">nātha</a>-vindu,</i> ‘procuring protection,’ as the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a>, or chant.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201541"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201541">↑</a> In the post-Vedic literature, on the other hand, the word is a masculine, meaning ‘protector,’ and is very common.</li></ol></div>

नापित|నాపిత|ನಾಪಿತ|nāpita|naapita|_vei_120@1542|_vei1_pn441
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1541">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1543">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn440">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn441">1:441</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn442">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nāpita,</b> ‘barber,’ is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201542">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201542">2</a></sup> But the older word is <a class="vei_120a" href="वप्तृ">vaptṛ</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201542">3</a></sup> a derivative of  <i>vap,</i> ‘shave,’ with forms of which verb shaving is referred to as early as the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201542">4</a></sup> The dead were shaved before burial.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201542">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201542">↑</a> iii. 1, 2, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201542">↑</a> Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, vii. 2, 8. 13; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 17, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201542">↑</a> Rv. x. 142, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201542">↑</a> x. 142, 4. <i>Cf.</i> i. 65, 4; Av. vi. 68; v 2, 17, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201542">↑</a> Av. v. 19, 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 266; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 265.</li></ol></div>

नाभाक|నాభాక|ನಾಭಾಕ|nābhāka|naabhaaka|_vei_120@1543|_vei1_pn442
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1542">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1544">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn441">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn442">1:442</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn443">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nābhāka,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="नभाक">nabhāka</a>,’ is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>, or seer, in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201543"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201543">1</a></sup> The Anukramaṇī (Index) ascribes three or four hymns of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201543"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201543">2</a></sup> to him. According to Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201543"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201543">3</a></sup> the man was an <a class="vei_120a" href="आंगिरस">āṅgirasa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201543"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201543">4</a></sup> not a <a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201543"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201543">↑</a> viii. 41, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, x. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201543"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201543">↑</a> viii. 39-41, and doubtfully 42.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201543"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201543">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 107.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201543"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201543">↑</a> viii. 40, 12.</li></ol></div>

नाभा-नेदिष्ठ|नाभानेदिष्ठ|नाभा|नेदिष्ठ|నాభా-నేదిష్ఠ|నాభానేదిష్ఠ|నాభా|నేదిష్ఠ|ನಾಭಾ-ನೇದಿಷ್ಠ|ನಾಭಾನೇದಿಷ್ಠ|ನಾಭಾ|ನೇದಿಷ್ಠ|nābhā-nediṣṭha|nābhānediṣṭha|nābhā|nediṣṭha|naabhaa-nediShTha|naabhaanediShTha|naabhaa|nediShTha|_vei_120@1544|_vei1_pn442
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1543">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1545">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn441">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn442">1:442</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn443">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nābhā-nediṣṭha</b> (‘nearest in descent’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a></b> (‘descendant of Manu’) is famous in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201544">1</a></sup> for the way in which he was treated when his father <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a></b> divided his property among his sons, or they divided it: <a class="vei_120a" href="नाभानेदिष्ठ">nābhānediṣṭha</a> was left out, but was solaced by obtaining, through his father's advice, cows from the Aṅgirases, a feat which is regarded in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201544">2</a></sup> as on a level with the exploits of other seers who celebrated their patrons in hymns, and as giving rise to the hymn, Rigveda x. 62. Nābhānediṣṭha's hymn is repeatedly mentioned in the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201544">3</a></sup> but beyond its authorship nothing is recorded of him. In the Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201544">4</a></sup> itself he seems to be spoken of as a poet in one passage, which is, however, of quite uncertain meaning.</p><p><a class="vei_120a" href="नाभानेदिष्ठ">nābhānediṣṭha</a> is etymologically connected in all probability with Nabānazdiṣta in the Avesta, which refers to the Fravaṣi of the paoiryō-ṭkaēsha and the Fravaṣi of the Nabānazdiṣta. Lassen<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201544">5</a></sup> saw in the legend a reminiscence of an Indo-Iranian split; but Roth<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201544">6</a></sup> showed conclusively that this was impossible, and that <a class="vei_120a" href="नाभानेदिष्ठ">nābhānediṣṭha</a> meant simply ‘nearest in birth,’ and  Weber<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201544">7</a></sup> admits that the connexion of the words is not one of borrowing on either side, but that in the Avesta it has kept its original sense of ‘nearest relation,’ while in the Rigveda it has become a proper name.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201544">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 1. 9. 4-6; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 14. <i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 191 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201544">↑</a> xvi. 11. 28-30.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201544">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxviii. 4, merely refers to him as connected with the Aṅgirases. See also <i>ibid.,</i> xxx. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 30. 31; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xx. 9, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201544">↑</a> x. 61, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201544">↑</a> <i>Indische Alterthumskunde,</i> 1, 520, and <i>Addenda,</i> p. lxxvii.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201544">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 6, 243 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201544">↑</a> <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 40-50. <i>Cf.</i> also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दाय">dāya</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

नाभि|नाभि|నాభి_౧|నాభి|ನಾಭಿ_೧|ನಾಭಿ|nābhi_1|nābhi|naabhi_1|naabhi|_vei_120@1545|_vei1_pn443
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1544">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1546">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn442">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn443">1:443</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn444">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Nābhi</b> develops from the literal sense of ‘navel’ the figurative meaning of ‘relationship,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201545"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201545">1</a></sup> or, concretely, ‘relation.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201545"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201545">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201545"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201545">↑</a> i. 105, 9; 164, 33; ii. 3, 9; 40, 4, etc.; Av. xii. 1, 40; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x. 8; xi. 12; xx. 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201545"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201545">↑</a> Rv. i. 163, 12; vi. 47, 28; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiii. 42-44. 50, etc.</li></ol></div>

नाभि|नाभि|నాభి_౨|నాభి|ನಾಭಿ_೨|ನಾಭಿ|nābhi_2|nābhi|naabhi_2|naabhi|_vei_120@1546|_vei1_pn443
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1545">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1547">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn442">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn443">1:443</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn444">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Nābhi,</b> ‘nave’ of a chariot wheel, is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201546"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201546">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201546"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201546">2</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a>,</b> and <i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नभ्य">nabhya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201546"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201546">↑</a> v. 43, 8; vi. 39, 4; viii. 41, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201546"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201546">↑</a> Av. iii. 30, 6; x. 8, 34; xi. 7, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 4; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 5, 11; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vii. 15, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 2, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 8.</li></ol></div>

नाम-धेय|नामधेय|नाम|धेय|నామ-ధేయ|నామధేయ|నామ|ధేయ|ನಾಮ-ಧೇಯ|ನಾಮಧೇಯ|ನಾಮ|ಧೇಯ|nāma-dheya|nāmadheya|nāma|dheya|naama-dheya|naamadheya|naama|_vei_120@1547|_vei1_pn443
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1546">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1548">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn442">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn443">1:443</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn444">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nāma-dheya,</b> ‘name,’ is found in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201547">1</a></sup> and often in the later language.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201547">2</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नामन्">nāman</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201547">↑</a> x. 71, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201547">↑</a> Av. vii. 109, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 4, 9, 3; iii. 3, 4, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 1, 6, 1; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 3, 11; vi. 4, 25; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 2, etc.</li></ol></div>

नामन्|నామన్|ನಾಮನ್|nāman|naaman|_vei_120@1548|_vei1_pn443
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1547">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1549">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn442">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn443">1:443</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn444">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nāman,</b> ‘name,’ is a common word from the Rigveda onwards. The <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201548">1</a></sup> give elaborate rules for the formation of the names of children, but more important is the distinction between the secret (<i>guhya</i>) and the ordinary name, though the rules as to the secret name are not at all consistent. The secret name is already recognized in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201548">2</a></sup> and is referred to in the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201548">3</a></sup> one secret name, that of Arjuna for <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, being given in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201548">4</a></sup> It is to be noted that the rule as to giving the  designation of a <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> (lunar asterism) as the secret name or otherwise is not illustrated by a single recorded name of a teacher in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201548">5</a></sup></p><p>The Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201548">6</a></sup> several times mentions the adoption of a second name with a view to securing success, and also refers to the adoption of another name for purposes of distinction.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201548">7</a></sup></p><p>In actual practice two names are usually found in the Brāhmaṇas, the second being a patronymic or a metronymic, as in <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="औशिज">auśija</a></b><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201548">8</a></sup> (if the story of the slave woman Uśij as his mother is correct), or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बृहदुक्थ">bṛhaduktha</a> Vāmneya,</b><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201548">9</a></sup> ‘son of Vāmnī,’ though the relationship may, of course, be not direct parentage, but more remote descent.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201548">10</a></sup> Three names are less common--for example, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कूशाम्ब स्वायव लातव्य">kūśāmba <a class="vei_120a" href="स्वायव">svāyava</a> lātavya</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201548">11</a></sup> ‘son of Svāyu, of the <a class="vei_120a" href="लातव्य">lātavya</a> (son of Latu) family,’ or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="देवतरस् श्यावसायन काश्यप">devataras <a class="vei_120a" href="श्यावसायन">śyāvasāyana</a> kāśyapa</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201548">12</a></sup> where the patronymic and the <a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a> name are both found. In other cases the names probably have a local reference--<i>e.g.,</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौशाम्बेय">kauśāmbeya</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गांग्य">gāṅgya</a>.</b> Frequently the patronymic only is given, as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भार्गव">bhārgava</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मौद्गल्य">maudgalya</a>,</b> etc., or two patronymics are used. The simple name is often used for the patronymic--<i>e.g.,</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201548">13</a></sup> In a few cases the name of the wife is formed from the husband's name,<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201548">14</a></sup> as <b>Uśīnarāṇī, <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्सानी">purukutsānī</a>, Mudgalānī.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201548">↑</a> Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 316 <i>et seq.;</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Rituallitteratur,</i> 46, 47; Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 152.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201548">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> x. 55, 2; 71, 1, as explained in <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 3, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201548">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 1, 3, 9 (the name is given to a child at birth); Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 25.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201548">↑</a> ii. 1, 2, 11; v. 4, 3, 7; Weber, 2, 317, n. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201548">↑</a> Weber, 2, 318, 319. See <b>Aṣāḍha, <a class="vei_120a" href="रौहिण">rauhiṇa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="रौहिणायन">rauhiṇāyana</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201548">↑</a> iii. 6, 2, 24; v. 3, 3, 14; ix. 4, 3, 3, which directs that the name should be derived from a feast performed by the person in question. See also <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxvi. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201548">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 4, 4. <i>Cf.</i> vi. 1, 3, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201548">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 11, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201548">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> xiv. 9, 38.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201548">↑</a> Pargiter, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1910, 14; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 55, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201548">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 6, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201548">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 40, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201548">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 1, 2, 13; Hopkins, <i>Religions of India,</i> 201, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201548">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> p. 135.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 316-320; Hopkins, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

नाम्ब|नांब|నామ్బ|నాంబ|ನಾಮ್ಬ|ನಾಂಬ|nāmba|naamba|_vei_120@1549|_vei1_pn444
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1548">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1550">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn443">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn444">1:444</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn445">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nāmba</b> is the name of a kind of grain mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201549"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201549">1</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="आम्ब">āmba</a> is the form of the word in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201549"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201549">2</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201549"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201549">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201549"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201549">↑</a> v. 3, 3, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201549"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201549">↑</a> i. 8, 10, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201549"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201549">↑</a> xv. 15.</li></ol></div>

नाय|నాయ|ನಾಯ|nāya|naaya|_vei_120@1550|_vei1_pn445
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1549">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1551">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn444">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn445">1:445</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn446">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nāya</b> in two passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201550"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201550">1</a></sup> is, according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, probably a proper name. Sāyaṇa takes the word to mean ‘leader,’ while Pischel<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201550"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201550">2</a></sup> considers it a gerund with passive sense.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201550"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201550">↑</a> vi. 24, 10; 46, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201550"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201550">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 41. For other explanations, see Oldenberg, <i>ṚgvedaNoten,</i> 1, 123, 370.</li></ol></div>

नारद|నారద|ನಾರದ|nārada|naarada|_vei_120@1551|_vei1_pn445
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1550">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1552">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn444">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn445">1:445</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn446">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nārada</b> is the name of a mythical seer mentioned several times in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201551"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201551">1</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> he appears in conjunction with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्वत">parvata</a></b> as priest of <b>Hariścandra,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201551"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201551">2</a></sup> as teaching <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सोमक साहदेव्य">somaka sāhadevya</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201551"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201551">3</a></sup> and as anointing <b>Āmbāṣṭhya</b> and <b>Yudhāṃśrauṣṭi.</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201551"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201551">4</a></sup> In the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201551"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201551">5</a></sup> he is mentioned as a teacher, and in the Sāmavidhāna <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201551"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201551">6</a></sup> as a pupil of <a class="vei_120a" href="बृहस्पति">bṛhaspati</a>. In the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201551"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201551">7</a></sup> he is coupled with <a class="vei_120a" href="सनत्कुमार">sanatkumāra</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201551"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201551">↑</a> v. 19, 9; xii. 4, 16. 24. 41.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201551"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201551">↑</a> vii. 13. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201551"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201551">↑</a> vii. 34.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201551"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201551">↑</a> viii. 21.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201551"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201551">↑</a> i. 5, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201551"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201551">↑</a> iii. 9 (the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a>, or ‘list of teachers,’ at the end).</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201551"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201551">↑</a> vii. 1, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 204, n.</li></ol></div>

नाराशंसी|नाराशंसि|నారాశంసీ|నారాశంసి|ನಾರಾಶಂಸೀ|ನಾರಾಶಂಸಿ|nārāśaṃsī|nārāśaṃsi|naaraashaMsii|naaraashaMsi|naaraashaMsee|_vei_120@1552|_vei1_pn445
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1551">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1553">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn444">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn445">1:445</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn446">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nārāśaṃsī</b> (scil. Ṛc), ‘(verse) celebrating men,’ is mentioned as early as the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201552">1</a></sup> and is distinguished from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a></b> in a number of passages in the later literature.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201552">2</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201552">3</a></sup> while distinguishing the two, asserts that both are false (<i>anṛtam</i>). It is hardly probable that the two were absolutely distinct, for the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201552">4</a></sup> has the phrase ‘a <a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a> celebrating men’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="नाराशंसी">nārāśaṃsī</a></i>). What such verses were may be seen from the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201552">5</a></sup> which enumerates the <i>Nārāśaṃsāni</i> at the Puruṣamedha, or ‘human  sacrifice.’ They may legitimately be reckoned as a source of the epic.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201552">6</a></sup></p><p>The term <a class="vei_120a" href="नाराशंसी">nārāśaṃsī</a> is restricted in some passages<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201552">7</a></sup> to a particular group of three verses of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201552">8</a></sup> but Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201552">9</a></sup> must be right in holding that the restricted sense is not to be read into the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201552">10</a></sup> Not even in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201552">11</a></sup> is the technical sense certain, and the Bṛhaddevatā<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201552">12</a></sup> gives the word a general application.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201552">↑</a> x. 85, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201552">↑</a> Av. xv. 6, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 11, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 32; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxx. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 10, etc.; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 78. The passage, Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 6, 8, is uncertain. See Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 98, n. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201552">↑</a> xiv. 5; Weber, <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 98.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201552">↑</a> i. 3, 2, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201552">↑</a> xvi. 11, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 10 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201552">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 264, n. Bloomfield, <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 100 (<i>cf. Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 688, 689), lays stress rather on their character as mere eulogies of donors, and that, no doubt, was one of their sides; but the other elements may have been more prominent in reality than the priestly tradition shows.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201552">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 32; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxx. 5. Possibly, in the other passages mentioned in note 2, the reference may be to the Atharvaveda verses, but this is not at all likely.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201552">↑</a> xx. 127, 1-3 = <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 14, 1-3. <i>Cf.</i> Scheftelowitz, <i>Die Apokryphen des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 155.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201552">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 238.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201552">↑</a> x. 85, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201552">↑</a> vii. 5, 11, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201552">↑</a> iii. 154.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Episches im Vedischen Ritual,</i> 4 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

नारी|नारि|నారీ|నారి|ನಾರೀ|ನಾರಿ|nārī|nāri|naarii|naari|naaree|_vei_120@1553|_vei1_pn446
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1552">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1554">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn445">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn446">1:446</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn447">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nārī,</b> ‘woman,’ occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201553"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201553">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201553"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201553">2</a></sup> The word seems in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201553"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201553">3</a></sup> to have a distinct reference to a woman as a wife, because it occurs in several passages with distinct reference to matrimonial relations,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201553"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201553">3</a></sup> and in the later Vedic literature, where it is not common, it sometimes<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201553"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201553">4</a></sup> has that sense. Delbrück,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201553"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201553">5</a></sup> however, thinks that it does not indicate marital relations, but merely the woman as the sexual complement of the man.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201553"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201553">↑</a> vii. 20, 5; 55, 8; viii. 77, 8; x. 18, 7; 86, 10. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201553"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201553">↑</a> Av. xiv. 2, 13; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 36; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 34.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201553"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201553">↑</a> i. 73, 3 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>-juṣṭā,</i> ‘dear to her husband’); vii. 20. 5; x. 18, 7 (<i>avidhavāḥ supatnīḥ,</i> ‘not widowed, with noble husbands’), etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201553"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201553">↑</a> i. 73, 3 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>-juṣṭā,</i> ‘dear to her husband’); vii. 20. 5; x. 18, 7 (<i>avidhavāḥ supatnīḥ,</i> ‘not widowed, with noble husbands’), etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201553"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201553">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ix. 28.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201553"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201553">↑</a> <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 417, 439.</li></ol></div>

नार्मर|నార్మర|ನಾರ್ಮರ|nārmara|naarmara|_vei_120@1554|_vei1_pn446
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1553">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1555">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn445">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn446">1:446</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn447">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nārmara</b> occurs once in a corrupt verse of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201554"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201554">1</a></sup> Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201554"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201554">2</a></sup> regards the word as the proper name of the prince of a fort, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऊर्जयन्ती">ūrjayantī</a>,</b> but Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201554"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201554">3</a></sup> as that of a demon. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201554"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201554">↑</a> ii. 13, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201554"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201554">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 152.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201554"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201554">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

नार्मिणी|नार्मिणि|నార్మిణీ|నార్మిణి|ನಾರ್ಮಿಣೀ|ನಾರ್ಮಿಣಿ|nārmiṇī|nārmiṇi|naarmiNii|naarmiNi|naarmiNee|_vei_120@1555|_vei1_pn447
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1554">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1556">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn446">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn447">1:447</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn448">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nārmiṇī</b> is found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201555"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201555">1</a></sup> as an epithet of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुर्">pur</a>,</b> ‘fort’: it must apparently either be a proper name of the fort,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201555"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201555">2</a></sup> or mean ‘belonging to Narmin or Narmiṇa,’ some prince.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201555"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201555">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201555"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201555">↑</a> i. 149, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201555"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201555">↑</a> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 204.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201555"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201555">↑</a> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> Possibly the expression may consist of two words--<i>na,</i> ‘not’ or ‘like,’ and <i>armiṇī,</i> whatever that may mean. See Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 148; <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 46, 177.</li></ol></div>

नार्य|నార్య|ನಾರ್ಯ|nārya|naarya|_vei_120@1556|_vei1_pn447
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1555">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1557">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn446">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn447">1:447</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn448">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nārya,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नर्य">narya</a>,’</b> is the name of a generous donor in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201556"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201556">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201556"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201556">↑</a> viii. 24, 29. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 161, 162.</li></ol></div>

नार्षद|నార్షద|ನಾರ್ಷದ|nārṣada|naarShada|_vei_120@1557|_vei1_pn447
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1556">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1558">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn446">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn447">1:447</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn448">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nārṣada,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="नृषद्">nṛṣad</a>,’ is the patronymic of <a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a> (<i>i.e.,</i> a descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a>) in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201557"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201557">1</a></sup> and in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201557"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201557">2</a></sup> where probably the same man is referred to in another passage<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201557"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201557">3</a></sup> as a protégé of the Aśvins, and perhaps as the husband of <b>Ruśatī.</b> But in a third passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201557"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201557">4</a></sup> the name seems applied to a demon, though this is not certain.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201557"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201557">↑</a> iv. 19, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201557"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201557">↑</a> x. 31, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201557"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201557">↑</a> i. 117, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201557"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201557">↑</a> x. 61, 13.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 108, 150; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 397.</li></ol></div>

नाव-प्रभ्रंशन|नावप्रभ्रंशन|नाव|प्रभ्रंशन|నావ-ప్రభ్రంశన|నావప్రభ్రంశన|నావ|ప్రభ్రంశన|ನಾವ-ಪ್ರಭ್ರಂಶನ|ನಾವಪ್ರಭ್ರಂಶನ|ನಾವ|ಪ್ರಭ್ರಂಶನ|nāva-prabhraṃśana|nāvaprabhraṃśana|nāva|prabhraṃśana|naava-prabhraMshana|naavaprabhraMshana|naava|prabhraMshana|_vei_120@1558|_vei1_pn447
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1557">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1559">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn446">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn447">1:447</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn448">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nāva-prabhraṃśana,</b> the ‘sliding down of the ship,’ is read in Whitney and Roth's text of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201558">1</a></sup> and has been connected by Weber<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201558">2</a></sup> and others<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201558">3</a></sup> with <a class="vei_120a" href="मनोर् अवसर्पण">manor avasarpaṇa</a>, the name in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201558">4</a></sup> of the northern mountain on which Manu's ship settled on the subsidence of the deluge. But both Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201558">5</a></sup> and Whitney<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201558">6</a></sup> point out that this interpretation is highly improbable, and this view is accepted by  Macdonell.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201558">7</a></sup> The expression is analyzed as <i>na <a class="vei_120a" href="अव">ava</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="प्रभ्रंशन">prabhraṃśana</a></i> by the <a class="vei_120a" href="पद">pada</a> text and the commentator alike, and is never found elsewhere with reference to the descent of a boat or ship.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201558">8</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201558">↑</a> xix. 39, 8, where the reading <i>nāvaprabhráṃśana</i> is a conjectural emendātion, the manuscripts of the Saṃhitā text all having two accents, <i>nāvaprabhráṃśana</i> (one of them reading <i>nāvaḥ-</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201558">↑</a> <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201558">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 198; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 218, n.; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 30.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201558">↑</a> i. 8, 1, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201558">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 679.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201558">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 961.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201558">↑</a> <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1907, 1107, where his acceptance of Weber's interpretation in his <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 144, is withdrawn.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201558">↑</a> The word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नौ">nau</a>,</i> ship, never occurs as the first member of a compound in the form of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नाव">nāva</a>,</i> while <i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्र">pra</a>-bhraṃś,</i> ‘fall down,’ is never used of the gliding down of a boat, and would be inappropriately applied in that sense.</li></ol></div>

नावा|నావా|ನಾವಾ|nāvā|naavaa|_vei_120@1559|_vei1_pn448
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1558">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1560">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn447">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn448">1:448</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn449">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nāvā,</b> ‘ship,’ occurs once in the Rigveda (i. 97, 8). See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नौ">nau</a>.</b></p></div>

नावाज|నావాజ|ನಾವಾಜ|nāvāja|naavaaja|_vei_120@1560|_vei1_pn448
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1559">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1561">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn447">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn448">1:448</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn449">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nāvāja</b> (‘ship-propeller’), a ‘boatman,’ is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (ii. 3, 3, 5).</p></div>

नाव्या|నావ్యా|ನಾವ್ಯಾ|nāvyā|naavyaa|_vei_120@1561|_vei1_pn448
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1560">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1562">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn447">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn448">1:448</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn449">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nāvyā</b> is found several times in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201561"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201561">1</a></sup> and the later literature<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201561"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201561">2</a></sup> in the sense of a ‘navigable stream.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201561"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201561">↑</a> i. 33, 11; 80, 8; 121, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201561"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201561">↑</a> Av. viii. 5, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxiii. 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 5, 4, 14, etc.</li></ol></div>

नाहुष|నాహుష|ನಾಹುಷ|nāhuṣa|naahuSha|_vei_120@1562|_vei1_pn448
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1561">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1563">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn447">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn448">1:448</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn449">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nāhuṣa,</b> like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नहुस्">nahus</a>,</b> means, according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, in some passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201562"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201562">1</a></sup> ‘neighbouring’ as an adjective, and once<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201562"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201562">2</a></sup> as a substantive ‘neighbour.’ If, on the other hand, <a class="vei_120a" href="नहुस्">nahus</a> is taken as a proper name, then <a class="vei_120a" href="नाहुष">nāhuṣa</a> no doubt denotes ‘belonging to the <a class="vei_120a" href="नहुस्">nahus</a> people,’ and, as a substantive, ‘King of the <a class="vei_120a" href="नहुस्">nahus</a>.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201562"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201562">↑</a> i. 100, 16; v. 73, 3; vi. 22, 10; viii. 6, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201562"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201562">↑</a> viii. 95, 2.</li></ol></div>

निकोथक भाय-जात्य|निकोथक भायजात्य|निकोथक भाय|जात्य|నికోథక భాయ-జాత్య|నికోథక భాయజాత్య|నికోథక భాయ|జాత్య|ನಿಕೋಥಕ ಭಾಯ-ಜಾತ್ಯ|ನಿಕೋಥಕ ಭಾಯಜಾತ್ಯ|ನಿಕೋಥಕ ಭಾಯ|ಜಾತ್ಯ|nikothaka bhāya-jātya|nikothaka bhāyajātya|nikothaka bhāya|jātya|nikothaka bhaaya-jaatya|nikothaka bhaayajaatya|nikothaka bhaaya|jaatya|_vei_120@1563|_vei1_pn448
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1562">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1564">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn447">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn448">1:448</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn449">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nikothaka <a class="vei_120a" href="भाय">bhāya</a>-jātya</b> (‘descendant of Bhayajāta’) is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201563"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201563">1</a></sup> as a pupil of <b>Pratithi.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201563"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201563">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373. <i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 444.</li></ol></div>

निगद पार्ण-वल्कि|निगद पार्णवल्कि|निगद पार्ण|वल्कि|నిగద పార్ణ-వల్కి|నిగద పార్ణవల్కి|నిగద పార్ణ|వల్కి|ನಿಗದ ಪಾರ್ಣ-ವಲ್ಕಿ|ನಿಗದ ಪಾರ್ಣವಲ್ಕಿ|ನಿಗದ ಪಾರ್ಣ|ವಲ್ಕಿ|nigada pārṇa-valki|nigada pārṇavalki|nigada pārṇa|valki|nigada paarNa-valki|nigada paarNavalki|nigada paarNa|_vei_120@1564|_vei1_pn448
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1563">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1565">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn447">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn448">1:448</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn449">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nigada <a class="vei_120a" href="पार्ण">pārṇa</a>-valki</b> (‘descendant of Parṇavalka’) is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201564"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201564">1</a></sup> as a pupil of Giriśarman. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201564"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201564">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372. <i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 443.</li></ol></div>

नि-गुत्|निगुत्|गुत्|ని-గుత్|నిగుత్|గుత్|ನಿ-ಗುತ್|ನಿಗುತ್|ಗುತ್|ni-gut|nigut|gut|_vei_120@1565|_vei1_pn449
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1564">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1566">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn448">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn449">1:449</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn450">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ni-gut</b> occurs in two passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201565">1</a></sup> where Sāyaṇa takes it to mean ‘enemy,’ a possible interpretation. Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201565">2</a></sup> suggests that non-Āryan foes are meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201565">↑</a> ix. 97, 53, 54; x. 128, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201565">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 164.</li></ol></div>

निगुस्थ|నిగుస్థ|ನಿಗುಸ್ಥ|nigustha|_vei_120@1566|_vei1_pn449
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1565">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1567">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn448">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn449">1:449</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn450">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nigustha</b> is a term of unknown meaning applied in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (xvi. 29, 6) to the peoples of <a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a>.</p></div>

नि-तत्नी|नि-तत्नी|नि-तत्नि|नितत्नी|नितत्नि|तत्नी|तत्नि|ని-తత్నీ_౧|ని-తత్నీ|ని-తత్ని|నితత్నీ|నితత్ని|తత్నీ|తత్ని|ನಿ-ತತ್ನೀ_೧|ನಿ-ತತ್ನೀ|ನಿ-ತತ್ನಿ|ನಿತತ್ನೀ|ನಿತತ್ನಿ|ತತ್ನೀ|ತತ್ನಿ|ni-tatnī_1|ni-tatnī|ni-tatni|nitatnī|nitatni|tatnī|tatni|ni-tatnii_1|ni-tatnii|nitatnii|tatnii|ni-tatnee_1|ni-tatnee|nitatnee|tatnee|_vei_120@1567|_vei1_pn449
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1566">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1568">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn448">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn449">1:449</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn450">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Ni-tatnī,</b> ‘striking downwards,’ occurs in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201567">1</a></sup> as the name of an unknown plant which was used as a means of restoring the hair.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201567">↑</a> vi. 136; probably 137 also refers to this plant. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxxi. 28, with Caland's note in his translation; Bloomfield, <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 61; <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 536, 537; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 383.</li></ol></div>

नि-तत्नी|नि-तत्नी|नि-तत्नि|नितत्नी|नितत्नि|तत्नी|तत्नि|ని-తత్నీ_౨|ని-తత్నీ|ని-తత్ని|నితత్నీ|నితత్ని|తత్నీ|తత్ని|ನಿ-ತತ್ನೀ_೨|ನಿ-ತತ್ನೀ|ನಿ-ತತ್ನಿ|ನಿತತ್ನೀ|ನಿತತ್ನಿ|ತತ್ನೀ|ತತ್ನಿ|ni-tatnī_2|ni-tatnī|ni-tatni|nitatnī|nitatni|tatnī|tatni|ni-tatnii_2|ni-tatnii|nitatnii|tatnii|ni-tatnee_2|ni-tatnee|nitatnee|tatnee|_vei_120@1568|_vei1_pn449
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1567">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1569">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn448">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn449">1:449</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn450">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Ni-tatnī</b> is the name of one of the seven <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्तिकास्">kṛttikās</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 1, 4, 1). See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p></div>

नि-तान मारुत|नितान मारुत|तान मारुत|ని-తాన మారుత|నితాన మారుత|తాన మారుత|ನಿ-ತಾನ ಮಾರುತ|ನಿತಾನ ಮಾರುತ|ತಾನ ಮಾರುತ|ni-tāna māruta|nitāna māruta|tāna māruta|ni-taana maaruta|nitaana maaruta|taana maaruta|_vei_120@1569|_vei1_pn449
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1568">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1570">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn448">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn449">1:449</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn450">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ni-<a class="vei_120a" href="तान">tāna</a> Māruta</b> is the name of a man in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a></b> Saṃhitā (xxv. 10).</p></div>

नि-दाघ|निदाघ|दाघ|ని-దాఘ|నిదాఘ|దాఘ|ನಿ-ದಾಘ|ನಿದಾಘ|ದಾಘ|ni-dāgha|nidāgha|dāgha|ni-daagha|nidaagha|daagha|_vei_120@1570|_vei1_pn449
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1569">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1571">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn448">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn449">1:449</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn450">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ni-dāgha</b> (‘burning down’), ‘summer,’ is the name of one of the seasons in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 8, 1, 4). See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नैदाघ">naidāgha</a>.</b></p></div>

नि-दान|निदान|दान|ని-దాన|నిదాన|దాన|ನಿ-ದಾನ|ನಿದಾನ|ದಾನ|ni-dāna|nidāna|dāna|ni-daana|nidaana|daana|_vei_120@1571|_vei1_pn449
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1570">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1572">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn448">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn449">1:449</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn450">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ni-dāna</b> is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, which is referred to in the Bṛhaddevatā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201571"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201571">1</a></sup> apparently as containing a quotation from the <a class="vei_120a" href="भाल्लवि">bhāllavi</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>. The quotation cannot be verified in the existing text of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201571"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201571">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201571"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201571">↑</a> v. 23, with Macdonell's note.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201571"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201571">↑</a> Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 65.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 44; Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 111, etc.</li></ol></div>

नि-धा|निधा|धा|ని-ధా|నిధా|ధా|ನಿ-ಧಾ|ನಿಧಾ|ಧಾ|ni-dhā|nidhā|dhā|ni-dhaa|nidhaa|dhaa|_vei_120@1572|_vei1_pn450
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1571">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1573">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn449">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn450">1:450</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn451">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ni-dhā,</b> ‘net,’ is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201572"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201572">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201572"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201572">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201572"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201572">↑</a> ix. 83, 4; x. 73, 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iv. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201572"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201572">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 19.</li></ol></div>

नि-धि|निधि|धि|ని-ధి|నిధి|ధి|ನಿ-ಧಿ|ನಿಧಿ|ಧಿ|ni-dhi|nidhi|dhi|_vei_120@1573|_vei1_pn450
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1572">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1574">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn449">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn450">1:450</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn451">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ni-dhi</b> means primarily ‘(place of) deposit,’ ‘store,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201573"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201573">1</a></sup> and then ‘treasure’ generally.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201573"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201573">2</a></sup> In the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201573"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201573">3</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="निधि">nidhi</a> denotes some sort of science.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201573"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201573">↑</a> Rv. i. 183, 4; v. 43, 8; vii. 67, 7; 69, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201573"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201573">↑</a> Rv. ii. 24, 6; viii. 29, 6; x. 68, 6; Av. x. 7, 23, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201573"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201573">↑</a> vii. 1, 2. 4; 2, 1; 7, 1. The St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> and Bo7hṭlingk in his edition, take <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दैवो">daivo</a> nidhiḥ</i> as one expression. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दैव">daiva</a>.</b> Sāyaṇa takes each as a separate entity, and renders <a class="vei_120a" href="निधि">nidhi</a> as <i>mahākālādinidhiśāstram,</i> presumably meaning some sort of chronology.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> for <a class="vei_120a" href="निधि">nidhi</a> as ‘treasure,’ Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 103, 104.</li></ol></div>

नि-नाह्य|निनाह्य|नाह्य|ని-నాహ్య|నినాహ్య|నాహ్య|ನಿ-ನಾಹ್ಯ|ನಿನಾಹ್ಯ|ನಾಹ್ಯ|ni-nāhya|nināhya|nāhya|ni-naahya|ninaahya|naahya|_vei_120@1574|_vei1_pn450
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1573">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1575">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn449">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn450">1:450</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn451">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ni-nāhya</b> denotes in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 9, 2, 8) a ‘water jar,’ so called, according to the commentator, because it was buried in the ground.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201574"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201574">1</a></sup> Eggeling<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201574"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201574">2</a></sup> explains it as ‘a vessel or cistern dug into the ground for keeping water cool.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201574"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201574">↑</a> As meaning etymologically ‘to be fastened or fixed down.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201574"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201574">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 223, n. 4.</li></ol></div>

निन्दिताश्व|నిన్దితాశ్వ|ನಿನ್ದಿತಾಶ್ವ|ninditāśva|ninditaashva|_vei_120@1575|_vei1_pn450
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1574">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1576">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn449">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn450">1:450</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn451">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ninditāśva</b> (‘possessing contemptible steeds’) is the name of a patron in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201575"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201575">1</a></sup> The name may suggest connexion with Iran, but such a reference is not at all necessary.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201575"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201575">2</a></sup> Sāyaṇa ingeniously turns the name--probably a nickname-into a compliment by rendering it ‘one who puts to shame the horses of his rivals.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201575"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201575">↑</a> viii. 1, 30.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201575"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201575">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 90. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwing, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 159.</li></ol></div>

नि-पाद|निपाद|पाद|ని-పాద|నిపాద|పాద|ನಿ-ಪಾದ|ನಿಪಾದ|ಪಾದ|ni-pāda|nipāda|pāda|ni-paada|nipaada|paada|_vei_120@1576|_vei1_pn450
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1575">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1577">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn449">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn450">1:450</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn451">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ni-pāda</b> in the Rigveda (v. 83, 7) denotes ‘low ground,’. ‘valley,’ as opposed to ‘hill’ (<i>udvat</i>). <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="निवत्">nivat</a>.</b></p></div>

नि-म्रुच्|निम्रुच्|म्रुच्|ని-మ్రుచ్|నిమ్రుచ్|మ్రుచ్|ನಿ-ಮ್ರುಚ್|ನಿಮ್ರುಚ್|ಮ್ರುಚ್|ni-mruc|nimruc|mruc|ni-mruch|nimruch|mruch|_vei_120@1577|_vei1_pn450
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1576">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1578">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn449">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn450">1:450</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn451">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ni-mruc,</b> ‘sunset,’ is repeatedly mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201577"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201577">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201577"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201577">2</a></sup> as a division of time. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201577"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201577">↑</a> i. 151, 5; 161, 10; viii. 27, 19; x. 151, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201577"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201577">↑</a> Av. xiii. 3, 21; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 5, 10, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 5, 2, etc.</li></ol></div>

निर्-अष्ट|निरष्ट|निर्|अष्ट|నిర్-అష్ట|నిరష్ట|నిర్|అష్ట|ನಿರ್-ಅಷ್ಟ|ನಿರಷ್ಟ|ನಿರ್|ಅಷ್ಟ|nir-aṣṭa|niraṣṭa|nir|aṣṭa|nir-aShTa|niraShTa|aShTa|_vei_120@1578|_vei1_pn451
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1577">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1579">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn450">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn451">1:451</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn452">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nir-aṣṭa,</b> ‘castrated,’ is found in some of the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201578"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201578">1</a></sup> as applied to oxen, and in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201578"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201578">2</a></sup> to horses.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201578"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201578">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 9, 1; 17, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 4. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201578"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201578">↑</a> xiii. 4, 2, 5.</li></ol></div>

निर्-आल|निराल|निर्|आल|నిర్-ఆల|నిరాల|నిర్|ఆల|ನಿರ್-ಆಲ|ನಿರಾಲ|ನಿರ್|ಆಲ|nir-āla|nirāla|nir|āla|nir-aala|niraala|aala|_vei_120@1579|_vei1_pn451
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1578">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1580">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn450">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn451">1:451</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn452">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nir-āla</b> occurs once in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201579">1</a></sup> where Sāyaṇa regards it as the name of a disease. Bloomfield,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201579">2</a></sup> with the Padapāṭha, explains it as two words, understanding <i><a class="vei_120a" href="निर्">nir</a></i> as an elliptical imperative, ‘(go) out,’ with the vocative <i><a class="vei_120a" href="आल">āla</a>,</i> a kind of weed. Whitney<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201579">3</a></sup> at first took <i><a class="vei_120a" href="आल">āla</a></i> to be a verbal form, but finally came to the conclusion that the expression is one word, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="निराल">nirāla</a>,</i> of unknown sense.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201579">↑</a> vi. 16, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201579">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvavéda,</i> 466.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201579">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 292.</li></ol></div>

निरुक्त|నిరుక్త|ನಿರುಕ್ತ|nirukta|_vei_120@1580|_vei1_pn451
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1579">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1581">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn450">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn451">1:451</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn452">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nirukta,</b> ‘explanation’ of a word or passage, is found in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (viii. 3, 3), but does not appear as the name of a work before the later Upaniṣads. It is, however, probable that Yāska's <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> is not later than the rise of Buddhism. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="निर्वचन">nirvacana</a>.</b></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 13, 17; 3, 260 <i>et seq.; Indian Literature,</i> 25, 26, 41, 42, etc.; Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 269, 270; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 24, 25; Roth, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,</i> xv. <i>et seq.</i></p></div>

निर्-यास|निर्यास|निर्|यास|నిర్-యాస|నిర్యాస|నిర్|యాస|ನಿರ್-ಯಾಸ|ನಿರ್ಯಾಸ|ನಿರ್|ಯಾಸ|nir-yāsa|niryāsa|nir|yāsa|nir-yaasa|niryaasa|yaasa|_vei_120@1581|_vei1_pn451
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1580">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1582">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn450">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn451">1:451</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn452">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nir-yāsa</b> denotes the ‘exudation’ of trees. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (ii. 1, 5, 4) it is tabooed as food because of its red colour.</p></div>

निर्-वचन|निर्वचन|निर्|वचन|నిర్-వచన|నిర్వచన|నిర్|వచన|ನಿರ್-ವಚನ|ನಿರ್ವಚನ|ನಿರ್|ವಚನ|nir-vacana|nirvacana|nir|vacana|nir-vachana|nirvachana|vachana|_vei_120@1582|_vei1_pn451
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1581">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1583">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn450">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn451">1:451</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn452">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nir-vacana</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201582"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201582">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201582"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201582">2</a></sup> means ‘explanation,’ especially etymological. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201582"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201582">↑</a> i. 6, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201582"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201582">↑</a> ii. 1. <i>Cf. a-nirvacanam,</i> ‘not serving as an explanation,’ <i>ibid.,</i> vii. 24.</li></ol></div>

नि-वत्|निवत्|वत्|ని-వత్|నివత్|వత్|ನಿ-ವತ್|ನಿವತ್|ವತ್|ni-vat|nivat|vat|_vei_120@1583|_vei1_pn451
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1582">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1584">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn450">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn451">1:451</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn452">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ni-vat</b> denotes ‘valley’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201583">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201583">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201583">↑</a> i. 161, 11; iii. 2, 10; vii. 50, 4; x. 127, 2; 142, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201583">↑</a> Av. vi. 22, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 2, 4; 4, etc.</li></ol></div>

निवान्य-वत्सा|निवान्यवत्सा|निवान्य|वत्सा|నివాన్య-వత్సా|నివాన్యవత్సా|నివాన్య|వత్సా|ನಿವಾನ್ಯ-ವತ್ಸಾ|ನಿವಾನ್ಯವತ್ಸಾ|ನಿವಾನ್ಯ|ವತ್ಸಾ|nivānya-vatsā|nivānyavatsā|nivānya|vatsā|nivaanya-vatsaa|nivaanyavatsaa|nivaanya|vatsaa|_vei_120@1584|_vei1_pn452
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1583">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1585">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn451">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn452">1:452</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn453">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nivānya-vatsā</b> and <b>Nivānyā</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201584"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201584">1</a></sup> denotes a ‘cow with a calf to which she has to be won over,’ that is, with a calf substituted for one of her own which has died. Nivānyā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201584"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201584">2</a></sup> is a contracted form of the compound term. Similar expressions are <i>abhivānya-<a class="vei_120a" href="वत्सा">vatsā</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201584"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201584">3</a></sup> <i>abhivānyā,</i><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201584"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201584">4</a></sup> <i>vānyā,</i><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201584"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201584">5</a></sup> and <i>apivānya-<a class="vei_120a" href="वत्सा">vatsā</a>.</i><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201584"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201584">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201584"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201584">↑</a> xii. 5, 1, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201584"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201584">↑</a> ii. 6, 1, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201584"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201584">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201584"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201584">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 8, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201584"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201584">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> ii. 6, 16, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201584"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201584">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, lxxxii. 22.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Lanman in Whitney's Translation of the Atharvaveda, 880.</li></ol></div>

नि-विद्|निविद्|विद्|ని-విద్|నివిద్|విద్|ನಿ-ವಿದ್|ನಿವಿದ್|ವಿದ್|ni-vid|nivid|vid|_vei_120@1585|_vei1_pn452
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1584">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1586">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn451">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn452">1:452</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn453">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ni-vid</b> denotes a brief invocation of the deity that is invited in a liturgy in honour of the god. The Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201585"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201585">1</a></sup> repeatedly mention Nivids as inserted in the Śastras (recitations), and the <b>Khilas</b> of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201585"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201585">2</a></sup> preserve among them a set of Nivids. But it is doubtful<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201585"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201585">3</a></sup> whether the habit of using such brief formulas--the <a class="vei_120a" href="निविद्">nivid</a> is usually not more than a <a class="vei_120a" href="पद">pada</a> or quarter-verse in length--is known to the Rigveda, though it has been seen even there,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201585"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201585">4</a></sup> and the word <a class="vei_120a" href="निविद्">nivid</a> is several times found in that Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201585"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201585">5</a></sup> but hardly in the technical sense of the Brāhmaṇas. In the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201585"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201585">6</a></sup> the technical sense is common.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201585"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201585">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 33. 34; iii. 10. 11; vi. 33. 35; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 9, 3, 28; xiii. 5, 1. 9, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 5, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201585"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201585">↑</a> See Scheftelowitz, <i>Die Apokryphen des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 137-143.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201585"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201585">↑</a> The antiquity of the Nivids was asserted by Haug, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,</i> 1, 26 <i>et seq.,</i> and often since, <i>e.g.,</i> by Tilak, <i>Orion,</i> 206; Scheftelowitz, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 3. It is shown to be most improbable by Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 265, 355, and Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 242 <i>et seq.; Go7ttingische Gelehrte Anzeigen,</i> 1907, 232, 233.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201585"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201585">↑</a> i. 86, 4; Bezzenberger's <i>Beiträge,</i> 9, 192. So Oldenberg, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 46, 119, 122, takes Rv. i. 96, 2, to refer to the Nivids in the technical sense, but not to the Nivids as preserved.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201585"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201585">↑</a> i. 89, 3; 96, 2; 175, 6; ii. 36, 6; iv. 18, 7; vi. 67, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201585"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201585">↑</a> Av. v. 26, 4; xi. 7, 19; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix, 25, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Rituallitteratur,</i> 102; Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 387, n. 2; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 241.</li></ol></div>

निविद्-धान|निविद्धान|निविद्|धान|నివిద్-ధాన|నివిద్ధాన|నివిద్|ధాన|ನಿವಿದ್-ಧಾನ|ನಿವಿದ್ಧಾನ|ನಿವಿದ್|ಧಾನ|nivid-dhāna|nividdhāna|nivid|dhāna|nivid-dhaana|nividdhaana|dhaana|_vei_120@1586|_vei1_pn452
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1585">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1587">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn451">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn452">1:452</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn453">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nivid-dhāna,</b> ‘containing a <a class="vei_120a" href="निविद्">nivid</a>,’ is found several times as an epithet of a hymn or verse in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201586"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201586">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201586"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201586">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 17; Kauṣīaki <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxi. 6; xxiv. 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 1, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 2, 2; 5, 3.</li></ol></div>

नि-वेशन|निवेशन|वेशन|ని-వేశన|నివేశన|వేశన|ನಿ-ವೇಶನ|ನಿವೇಶನ|ವೇಶನ|ni-veśana|niveśana|veśana|ni-veshana|niveshana|veshana|_vei_120@1587|_vei1_pn453
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1586">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1588">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn452">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn453">1:453</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn454">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ni-veśana,</b> ‘dwelling,’ occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201587"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201587">1</a></sup> and the Sūtras. In the latter<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201587"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201587">2</a></sup> the word is sometimes contrasted with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गृह">gṛha</a></b> as the resting-place of animals.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201587"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201587">↑</a> iv. 19, 9; vii. 19, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201587"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201587">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 6, etc.</li></ol></div>

नि-षंगथि|निषंगथि|षंगथि|नि-षङ्गथि|निषङ्गथि|षङ्गथि|ని-షంగథి|నిషంగథి|షంగథి|ని-షఙ్గథి|నిషఙ్గథి|షఙ్గథి|ನಿ-ಷಂಗಥಿ|ನಿಷಂಗಥಿ|ಷಂಗಥಿ|ನಿ-ಷಙ್ಗಥಿ|ನಿಷಙ್ಗಥಿ|ಷಙ್ಗಥಿ|ni-ṣaṅgathi|niṣaṅgathi|ṣaṅgathi|ni-ShaMgathi|niShaMgathi|ShaMgathi|ni-Shangathi|niShangathi|Shangathi|_vei_120@1588|_vei1_pn453
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1587">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1589">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn452">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn453">1:453</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn454">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ni-ṣaṅgathi</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201588"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201588">1</a></sup> or <b>Niṣaṅga-<a class="vei_120a" href="धि">dhi</a></b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201588"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201588">2</a></sup> is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās. If the second form of the word is correct, the sense may be ‘sheath of a sword’ (<i>niṣaṅga</i>), as it is taken by the St. Petersburg Dictionary, but probably the first form is the correct one, the word then having the same meaning as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="निषंगिन्">niṣaṅgin</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201588"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201588">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 11; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201588"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201588">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 10.</li></ol></div>

नि-षंगिन्|निषंगिन्|षंगिन्|नि-षङ्गिन्|निषङ्गिन्|षङ्गिन्|ని-షంగిన్|నిషంగిన్|షంగిన్|ని-షఙ్గిన్|నిషఙ్గిన్|షఙ్గిన్|ನಿ-ಷಂಗಿನ್|ನಿಷಂಗಿನ್|ಷಂಗಿನ್|ನಿ-ಷಙ್ಗಿನ್|ನಿಷಙ್ಗಿನ್|ಷಙ್ಗಿನ್|ni-ṣaṅgin|niṣaṅgin|ṣaṅgin|ni-ShaMgin|niShaMgin|ShaMgin|ni-Shangin|niShangin|Shangin|_vei_120@1589|_vei1_pn453
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1588">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1590">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn452">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn453">1:453</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn454">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ni-ṣaṅgin</b> is found three times in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201589">1</a></sup> where, owing to its following in one passage<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201589">2</a></sup> the words <i>sudhanvāna iṣumanto,</i> ‘having good bows and arrows,’ the sense of <i>niṣaṅginaḥ</i> as ‘having quivers’ seems quite certain. In the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201589">3</a></sup> it is taken by the commentator Mahīdhara to mean ‘having a sword,’ a sense which is quite possible both there and in the other passages<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201589">4</a></sup> where it occurs. But the word far more probably means ‘having a quiver,’ for the bow was the Vedic weapon, not the sword (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="असि">asi</a></b>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201589">↑</a> iii. 30, 15; v. 57, 2; x. 103, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201589">↑</a> v. 57, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201589">↑</a> xvi. 20. <i>Cf.</i> Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xx, 2, 11, with the commentator.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201589">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>, ii. 1199; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 12; xxxvii. 11; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 3, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 2, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 274.</li></ol></div>

नि-षाद|निषाद|षाद|ని-షాద|నిషాద|షాద|ನಿ-ಷಾದ|ನಿಷಾದ|ಷಾದ|ni-ṣāda|niṣāda|ṣāda|ni-Shaada|niShaada|Shaada|_vei_120@1590|_vei1_pn453
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1589">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1591">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn452">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn453">1:453</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn454">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ni-ṣāda</b> is found in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201590"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201590">1</a></sup> The word seems to denote not so much a particular tribe, but to be the general term for the non-Āryan tribes who were not under Āryan control, as the Śūdras were, for <a class="vei_120a" href="औपमन्यव">aupamanyava</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201590"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201590">2</a></sup> took the five peoples (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्च">pañca</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="जनाः">janāḥ</a></i>) to be the four castes (<i>catvāro  varṇāḥ</i>) and the Niṣādas, and the commentator Mahīdhara explains the word where it occurs in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201590"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201590">3</a></sup> as meaning a Bhilla, or Bhīl. A village of Niṣādas is mentioned in the Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201590"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201590">4</a></sup> and a <a class="vei_120a" href="निषाद">niṣāda</a> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्थपति">sthapati</a>,</b> a leader of some kind, is referred to in the Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201590"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201590">5</a></sup> and in a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> cited by the scholiast on that passage. Weber<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201590"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201590">6</a></sup> thinks that the Niṣādas were the settled aborigines (form <i>ni,</i> ‘down,’ and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सद्">sad</a>,</i> ‘settle’), a view supported by the fact that the ritual of the Viśvajit sacrifice<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201590"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201590">7</a></sup> requires a temporary residence with Niṣādas; for the Niṣādas who would permit an Āryan to reside temporarily amongst them must have been partially amenable to Āryan influence. But the name might easily be applied to the whole body of aborigines outside the Āryan organization. Von Schroeder<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201590"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201590">8</a></sup> thinks that the Niṣādas were most probably identical with the Nysæans, who, according to the Greek account, sent an embassy to Alexander when he was in the territory of the Aśvakas, but this identification is doubtful.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201590"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201590">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 4, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 13; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9, 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 27; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvi. 6, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201590"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201590">↑</a> In <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201590"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201590">↑</a> xvi. 27. <i>Cf.</i> xxx. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201590"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201590">↑</a> viii. 2, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201590"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201590">↑</a> i. 1, 12; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201590"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201590">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 340. <i>Cf.</i> 10, 13, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201590"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201590">↑</a> See <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 15; Lāṭyāyana, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201590"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201590">↑</a> <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 366. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 39, 119. In the later system (<a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>, x. 8) the <a class="vei_120a" href="निषाद">niṣāda</a> is the offspring of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> and of a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> woman, while the Bṛhatsaṃhitā of Varāhamihira (xiv. 10) recognizes a kingdom (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="राष्ट्र">rāṣṭra</a></i>) of Niṣādas in the south-east of <a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a>. In the Pāli texts (Fick, <i>Die sociale Gliederung,</i> 12, 160, 206 <i>et seq.</i>) they are wild hunters and fishers. <i>Cf.</i> also Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 301, 303, 366, n. 164, 403, 481.</li></ol></div>

निष्क|నిష్క|ನಿಷ್ಕ|niṣka|niShka|_vei_120@1591|_vei1_pn454
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1590">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1592">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn453">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn454">1:454</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn455">↷</a><hr><p><b>Niṣka</b> is frequently found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201591"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201591">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201591"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201591">2</a></sup> denoting a gold ornament worn on the neck, as is shown by the two epithets <i><a class="vei_120a" href="निष्क">niṣka</a>-kaṇṭha</i><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201591"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201591">3</a></sup> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="निष्क">niṣka</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="ग्रीव">grīva</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201591"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201591">4</a></sup> ‘having a gold ornament on the neck.’ A <a class="vei_120a" href="निष्क">niṣka</a> of silver is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201591"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201591">5</a></sup> As early as the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201591"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201591">6</a></sup> traces are seen of the  use of Niṣkas as a sort of currency, for a singer celebrates the receipt of a hundred Niṣkas and a hundred steeds: he could hardly require the Niṣkas merely for purposes of personal adornment. Later the use of Niṣkas as currency is quite clear.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201591"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201591">7</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्णल">kṛṣṇala</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201591"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201591">↑</a> ii. 33, 10; viii. 47, 15. etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201591"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201591">↑</a> Av. v. 14, 3; vii. 99, 1; xx. 131. 8; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 2, 1. 2; v. 13, 2; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 36, 7. 8; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 1, 7. 11, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201591"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201591">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 22.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201591"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201591">↑</a> Rv. v. 19, 3; Av. v. 17, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201591"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201591">↑</a> xvii. 1, 14, as worn by the <a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात्य">vrātya</a> <i>Cf.</i> Av. xv. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201591"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201591">↑</a> i. 126, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201591"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201591">↑</a> Av. xx. 127, 3; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ix. 9, 20, etc. In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 4, 1, 1. 8; Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 6; the sense of ‘coin’ is seen by Eggeling. <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 50, 51, and by Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 185.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 51, 259, 263; Geldner, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 268, n. 2; <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 40, 127.</li></ol></div>

निष्किरीय|నిష్కిరీయ|ನಿಷ್ಕಿರೀಯ|niṣkirīya|niShkiriiya|niShkireeya|_vei_120@1592|_vei1_pn455
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1591">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1593">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn454">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn455">1:455</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn456">↷</a><hr><p><b>Niṣkirīya</b> is the name of a school of priests who are mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201592"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201592">1</a></sup> as holding a Sattra, or ‘sacrificial session.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201592"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201592">↑</a> xii. 5, 14. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 58.</li></ol></div>

निष्ट्य|నిష్ట్య|ನಿಷ್ಟ್ಯ|niṣṭya|niShTya|_vei_120@1593|_vei1_pn455
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1592">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1594">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn454">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn455">1:455</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn456">↷</a><hr><p><b>Niṣṭya</b> means in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201593"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201593">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201593"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201593">2</a></sup> an outsider or stranger. Hence the constellation usually known as <a class="vei_120a" href="स्वाति">svāti</a> (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a></b>) is named Niṣṭyā in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201593"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201593">3</a></sup> because it occupies a position markedly away from the ecliptic.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201593"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201593">↑</a> vi. 75, 19; viii. 1, 13; x. 133, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201593"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201593">↑</a> Av. iii. 3, 6; Vājasaneyi, v. 23; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 4, 17, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201593"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201593">↑</a> i. 5, 2, 2. 3; iii. 1, 1, 13.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 215.</li></ol></div>

निहाका|నిహాకా|ನಿಹಾಕಾ|nihākā|nihaakaa|_vei_120@1594|_vei1_pn455
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1593">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1595">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn454">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn455">1:455</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn456">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nihākā</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201594">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201594">2</a></sup> appears to denote some phenomenon of a storm, perhaps the ‘whirlwind.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201594">↑</a> x. 97, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201594">↑</a> vii. 5, 11, 1 (following <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नीहार">nīhāra</a></i>).</li></ol></div>

नीक्षण|నీక్షణ|ನೀಕ್ಷಣ|nīkṣaṇa|niikShaNa|neekShaNa|niixaNa|_vei_120@1595|_vei1_pn455
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1594">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1596">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn454">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn455">1:455</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn456">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nīkṣaṇa.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नेक्षण">nekṣaṇa</a>.</b></p></div>

नीच्य|నీచ్య|ನೀಚ್ಯ|nīcya|niichya|neechya|_vei_120@1596|_vei1_pn455
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1595">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1597">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn454">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn455">1:455</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn456">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nīcya</b> (‘living below’) is a designation of certain nations of the west. The Nīcyas are mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 14) as distinguished from the people of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a>,</b> and no doubt mean the inhabitants of the Indus and Panjab regions. </p></div>

नीथ|నీథ|ನೀಥ|nītha|niitha|neetha|_vei_120@1597|_vei1_pn456
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1596">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1598">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn455">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn456">1:456</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn457">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nītha</b> (‘leading’), neut., means musical ‘mode’ and then ‘hymn of praise.’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201597"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201597">1</a></sup> The feminine form Nīthā occurs once in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201597"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201597">2</a></sup> meaning ‘artifice.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201597"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201597">↑</a> Rv. iv. 3, 16; vii. 26, 2; x. 92, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 38. <i>Cf. nīthāvid</i> of singers (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="जरितृ">jaritṛ</a></i>) in Rv. iii. 12, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201597"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201597">↑</a> i. 104, 5. <i>Cf.</i> the epithet <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शत">śata</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="नीथ">nītha</a>,</i> i. 100, 12; 179, 3; x. 69, 7; <i>sahasranītha,</i> iii. 60, 7; ix. 85, 4; 96, 18 (used of the poet's art, <i>padavīḥ kavīnām</i>).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 241.</li></ol></div>

नीनाह|నీనాహ|ನೀನಾಹ|nīnāha|niinaaha|neenaaha|_vei_120@1598|_vei1_pn456
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1597">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1599">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn455">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn456">1:456</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn457">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nīnāha,</b> a word occurring once in the Atharvaveda (xix. 57, 4), seems to denote a ‘girdle’ or something similar, as derived from the verb <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नह्">nah</a>,</i> ‘fasten.’</p></div>

नीपातिथि|నీపాతిథి|ನೀಪಾತಿಥಿ|nīpātithi|niipaatithi|neepaatithi|_vei_120@1599|_vei1_pn456
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1598">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1600">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn455">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn456">1:456</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn457">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nīpātithi</b> occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201599">1</a></sup> as the name of a man to whom a hymn<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201599">2</a></sup> is ascribed by the Anukramaṇī (Index). A <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a>, or Chant, of his is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201599">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201599">↑</a> viii. 49, 9, where he appears as engaged in battle; viii. 51, 1, where he seems to be a sacrificer. Either a king or a seer may be meant.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201599">↑</a> viii. 34. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 140; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 90.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201599">↑</a> xiv. 10, 4.</li></ol></div>

नीलंगु|नीलङ्गु|నీలంగు|నీలఙ్గు|ನೀಲಂಗು|ನೀಲಙ್ಗು|nīlaṅgu|niilaMgu|neelaMgu|niilangu|neelangu|_vei_120@1600|_vei1_pn456
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1599">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1601">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn455">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn456">1:456</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn457">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nīlaṅgu</b> is the name of a species of ‘worm’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201600"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201600">1</a></sup> in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or ‘horse sacrifice.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201600"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201600">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 11, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 11 (<i>v.l. nīlāṅgu</i>); Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 30. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 98.</li></ol></div>

नील-शीर्ष्णी|नील-शीर्ष्णि|नीलशीर्ष्णी|नीलशीर्ष्णि|नील|शीर्ष्णी|शीर्ष्णि|నీల-శీర్ష్ణీ|నీల-శీర్ష్ణి|నీలశీర్ష్ణీ|నీలశీర్ష్ణి|నీల|శీర్ష్ణీ|శీర్ష్ణి|ನೀಲ-ಶೀರ್ಷ್ಣೀ|ನೀಲ-ಶೀರ್ಷ್ಣಿ|ನೀಲಶೀರ್ಷ್ಣೀ|ನೀಲಶೀರ್ಷ್ಣಿ|ನೀಲ|ಶೀರ್ಷ್ಣೀ|ಶೀರ್ಷ್ಣಿ|nīla-śīrṣṇī|nīla-śīrṣṇi|nīlaśīrṣṇī|nīlaśīrṣṇi|nīla|śīrṣṇī|śīrṣṇi|niila-shiirShNii|niila-shiirShNi|niilashiirShNii|niilashiirShNi|niila|shiirShNii|shiirShNi|neela-sheerShNee|neela-sheerShNi|neelasheerShNee|neelasheerShNi|neela|sheerShNee|sheerShNi|_vei_120@1601|_vei1_pn456
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1600">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1602">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn455">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn456">1:456</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn457">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nīla-śīrṣṇī,</b> ‘black-headed,’ is the name of an unknown animal in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or ‘horse sacrifice,’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201601"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201601">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201601"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201601">↑</a> v. 5, 15, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 99.</li></ol></div>

नीलागलसाल|నీలాగలసాల|ನೀಲಾಗಲಸಾಲ|nīlāgalasāla|niilaagalasaala|neelaagalasaala|_vei_120@1602|_vei1_pn456
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1601">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1603">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn455">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn456">1:456</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn457">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nīlāgalasāla,</b> or, as the Paippalāda version reads, <b>Nīlākalasālā,</b> is the name, according to the commentator, of a graincreeper in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201602">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201602">↑</a> vi. 16, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 292, 293; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 466.</li></ol></div>

नीवार|నీవార|ನೀವಾರ|nīvāra|niivaara|neevaara|_vei_120@1603|_vei1_pn457
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1602">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1604">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn456">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn457">1:457</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn458">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nīvāra,</b> ‘wild rice,’ is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201603"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201603">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201603"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201603">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201603"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201603">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 4; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 10; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201603"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201603">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1, 4, 14; 3, 3, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 6, 7, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 240.</li></ol></div>

नीवि|నీవి|ನೀವಿ|nīvi|niivi|neevi|_vei_120@1604|_vei1_pn457
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1603">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1605">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn456">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn457">1:457</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn458">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nīvi</b> is the name of the ‘undergarment,’ probably a simple apron of cloth, worn by both men and women, but especially by the latter. It is mentioned in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201604"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201604">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201604"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201604">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201604"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201604">↑</a> viii. 2, 16; xiv. 2, 50. <i>Cf. nīvibhārya,</i> ‘to be borne in the apron,’ viii. 6, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201604"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201604">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 1, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iv. 10; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 3, 6; iii. 2, 1, 15, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 262; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 331.</li></ol></div>

नीहार|నీహార|ನೀಹಾರ|nīhāra|niihaara|neehaara|_vei_120@1605|_vei1_pn457
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1604">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1606">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn456">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn457">1:457</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn458">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nīhāra,</b> ‘mist,’ occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201605"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201605">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201605"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201605">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201605"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201605">↑</a> x. 82, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201605"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201605">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 11, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxviii. 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxii. 26; xxv. 9; Av. vi. 113, 2; xviii. 3, 60; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 10, 7; vi. 4, 1; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 19, 2, etc.</li></ol></div>

नृ|నృ|ನೃ|nṛ|nR|nRi|nRu|nRRi|_vei_120@1606|_vei1_pn457
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1605">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1607">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn456">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn457">1:457</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn458">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nṛ.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नर">nara</a>.</b></p></div>

नृति|నృతి|ನೃತಿ|nṛti|nRti|nRiti|nRuti|nRRiti|_vei_120@1607|_vei1_pn457
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1606">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1608">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn456">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn457">1:457</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn458">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nṛti</b> in one passage of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201607">1</a></sup> seems to mean a bag of skin. But though the Paippalāda recension has the same text, it is clear that we must read <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृति">dṛti</a></b> with Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201607">2</a></sup> and Whitney.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201607">3</a></sup> Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201607">4</a></sup> renders the word ‘dancer,’ which makes no sense in the context.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201607">↑</a> vi. 18, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201607">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201607">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 294. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 235; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 468.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201607">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 514.</li></ol></div>

नृतू|నృతూ|ನೃತೂ|nṛtū|nRtuu|nRtoo|nRituu|nRutuu|nRRituu|_vei_120@1608|_vei1_pn457
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1607">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1609">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn456">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn457">1:457</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn458">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nṛtū</b> occurs once in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201608"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201608">1</a></sup> denoting a female ‘dancer.’ In another passage<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201608"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201608">2</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="नृति">nṛti</a> is found coupled with <i>hāsa,</i> ‘laughter,’ in the description of the funeral ritual; but though it is clear that a joyful celebration is meant (like the Irish ‘wake’ or the  old-fashioned feasting in Scotland after a funeral), it is difficult to be certain that actual dancing is here meant. Dancing is, however, often referred to in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201608"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201608">3</a></sup> and later. Nṛttagīta, ‘dance and song,’ are mentioned in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201608"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201608">4</a></sup> as found in the sixth world. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शैलूष">śailūṣa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201608"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201608">↑</a> i. 92, 4 (where Uṣas, Goddess of Dawn, is compared to a dancer).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201608"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201608">↑</a> x. 18, 3. <i>Cf.</i> 29, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201608"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201608">↑</a> i. 10, 1; 92, 4, etc. See Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 196 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201608"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201608">↑</a> i. 42 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 235).</li></ol></div>

नृ-पति|नृपति|नृ|पति|నృ-పతి|నృపతి|నృ|పతి|ನೃ-ಪತಿ|ನೃಪತಿ|ನೃ|ಪತಿ|nṛ-pati|nṛpati|nṛ|pati|nR-pati|nRpati|nR|nRi-pati|nRipati|nRi|nRu-pati|nRupati|nRu|nRRi-pati|nRRipati|nRRi|_vei_120@1609|_vei1_pn458
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1608">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1610">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn457">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn458">1:458</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn459">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nṛ-pati,</b> ‘lord of men,’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201609"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201609">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201609"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201609">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘king’ or a man of the ruling class (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a></b>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201609"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201609">↑</a> ii. 1, 1. 7; iv. 20, 1; vii. 69, 1; x. 44, 2. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201609"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201609">↑</a> Av. v. 18, 1. 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, vi. 3, 3; x. 77, etc.</li></ol></div>

नृ-मेध|नृमेध|नृ|मेध|నృ-మేధ|నృమేధ|నృ|మేధ|ನೃ-ಮೇಧ|ನೃಮೇಧ|ನೃ|ಮೇಧ|nṛ-medha|nṛmedha|nṛ|medha|nR-medha|nRmedha|nR|nRi-medha|nRimedha|nRi|nRu-medha|nRumedha|nRu|nRRi-medha|nRRimedha|nRRi|_vei_120@1610|_vei1_pn458
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1609">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1611">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn457">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn458">1:458</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn459">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nṛ-medha,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201610"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201610">1</a></sup> <b>Nṛ-medhas,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201610"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201610">2</a></sup> is the name of a protégé of <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201610"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201610">3</a></sup> where he also appears with <b>Sumedhas</b> in a hymn that Griffith<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201610"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201610">4</a></sup> with justice declares to be unintelligible. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1201610"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1201610">1b</a></sup> he is an unsuccessful rival of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परुच्छेप">parucchepa</a>,</b> and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2b_vei_1201610"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2b_vei_1201610">2b</a></sup> he is an <a class="vei_120a" href="आंगिरस">āṅgirasa</a> and a seer of Sāmans (Chants).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201610"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201610">↑</a> Rv. x. 80, 3; 132, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 8, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201610"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201610">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 8, 21 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201610"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201610">↑</a> x. 80, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201610"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201610">↑</a> x. 132; Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 2, 578, n.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 160; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 61.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1201610"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1201610">↑</a> Rv. x. 80, 3; 132, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 8, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2b_vei_1201610"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2b_vei_1201610">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 8, 21 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

नृ-षद्|नृषद्|नृ|षद्|నృ-షద్|నృషద్|నృ|షద్|ನೃ-ಷದ್|ನೃಷದ್|ನೃ|ಷದ್|nṛ-ṣad|nṛṣad|nṛ|ṣad|nR-Shad|nRShad|nR|Shad|nRi-Shad|nRiShad|nRi|nRu-Shad|nRuShad|nRu|nRRi-Shad|nRRiShad|nRRi|_vei_120@1611|_vei1_pn458
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1610">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1612">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn457">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn458">1:458</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn459">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nṛ-ṣad,</b> ‘sitting among men,’ is the name of the father of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a></b> in the Rigveda (x. 31, 11). <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नार्षद">nārṣada</a>.</b></p></div>

नेक्षण|నేక్షణ|ನೇಕ್ಷಣ|nekṣaṇa|nekShaNa|nexaNa|_vei_120@1612|_vei1_pn458
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1611">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1613">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn457">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn458">1:458</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn459">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nekṣaṇa</b> occurs once in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201612">1</a></sup> where a ‘spit’ seems to be meant. In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201612">2</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="नीक्षण">nīkṣaṇa</a> occurs, and must have the same sense; Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201612">3</a></sup> inclines to think that this word refers to the ‘inspection’ of food, to see if it is ready (as from <i>ni-īkṣ,</i> ‘look into’). </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201612">↑</a> ix. 6, 17. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 11; lxxxvii. 12; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 540.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201612">↑</a> i. 162, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201612">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 155.</li></ol></div>

नेमि|నేమి|ನೇಮಿ|nemi|_vei_120@1613|_vei1_pn459
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1612">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1614">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn458">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn459">1:459</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn460">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nemi</b> denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201613">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201613">2</a></sup> the ‘felly’ of a chariot wheel. It was required to be of good wood (<i>su-drū</i>),<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201613">3</a></sup> and was bent into shape.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201613">4</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201613">↑</a> i. 32, 15; 141, 9; ii. 5, 3; v. 13, 6; vii. 32, 20; viii. 46, 23; 75, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201613">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 2, 15; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 5, 15, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201613">↑</a> Rv. vii. 32, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201613">↑</a> Rv. viii. 75, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 248.</li></ol></div>

नेष्टृ|నేష్టృ|ನೇಷ್ಟೃ|neṣṭṛ|neShTR|neShTRi|neShTRu|neShTRRi|_vei_120@1614|_vei1_pn459
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1613">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1615">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn458">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn459">1:459</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn460">↷</a><hr><p><b>Neṣṭṛ,</b> the name of one of the chief priests at the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> sacrifice, occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201614"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201614">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201614"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201614">2</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋत्विज्">ṛtvij</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201614"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201614">↑</a> i. 15, 3; ii. 5, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201614"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201614">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 18, 1; vi. 5, 8, 5. 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 3, 10, etc.; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8, 2, 1, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 15, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 250, 261, 527.</li></ol></div>

नैचा-शाख|नैचाशाख|नैचा|शाख|నైచా-శాఖ|నైచాశాఖ|నైచా|శాఖ|ನೈಚಾ-ಶಾಖ|ನೈಚಾಶಾಖ|ನೈಚಾ|ಶಾಖ|naicā-śākha|naicāśākha|naicā|śākha|naichaa-shaakha|naichaashaakha|naichaa|shaakha|_vei_120@1615|_vei1_pn459
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1614">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1616">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn458">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn459">1:459</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn460">↷</a><hr><p><b>Naicā-śākha</b> is found in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201615">1</a></sup> where Sāyaṇa renders it as ‘of low origin,’ but elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201615">2</a></sup> he explains it as the name of a place. The former sense is accepted by Grassmann and Ludwig in their versions, and by Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201615">3</a></sup> but Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201615">4</a></sup> points out that the reference is rather to the ‘low-branched’ <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> plant. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कीकट">kīkaṭa</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रमगन्द">pramaganda</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201615">↑</a> vii. 53, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201615">↑</a> See St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201615">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 31.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201615">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 14-18; 2, 241245, where he opposes Bo7htlingk's view that it is a proper name.</li></ol></div>

नैचु-दार|नैचुदार|नैचु|दार|నైచు-దార|నైచుదార|నైచు|దార|ನೈಚು-ದಾರ|ನೈಚುದಾರ|ನೈಚು|ದಾರ|naicu-dāra|naicudāra|naicu|dāra|naichu-daara|naichudaara|naichu|daara|_vei_120@1616|_vei1_pn459
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1615">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1617">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn458">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn459">1:459</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn460">↷</a><hr><p><b>Naicu-dāra</b> occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201616"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201616">1</a></sup> meaning ‘composed of the wood of the Nicudāra.’ What tree is meant by the latter name is unknown.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201616"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201616">↑</a> xxi. 4, 13. <i>Cf.</i> Anupada <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, vi. 4.</li></ol></div>

नैतन्धव|నైతన్ధవ|ನೈತನ್ಧವ|naitandhava|_vei_120@1617|_vei1_pn459
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1616">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1618">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn458">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn459">1:459</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn460">↷</a><hr><p><b>Naitandhava</b> is mentioned as a place on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201617"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201617">1</a></sup> and the Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201617"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201617">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201617"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201617">↑</a> xxv. 13, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201617"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201617">↑</a> Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 19, 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiii. 29, 31; Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxiv. 6, 23.</li></ol></div>

नै-दाघ|नैदाघ|नै|दाघ|నై-దాఘ|నైదాఘ|నై|దాఘ|ನೈ-ದಾಘ|ನೈದಾಘ|ನೈ|ದಾಘ|nai-dāgha|naidāgha|nai|dāgha|nai-daagha|naidaagha|daagha|_vei_120@1618|_vei1_pn459
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1617">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1619">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn458">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn459">1:459</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn460">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nai-dāgha</b> is the name of the ‘summer’ season in the later Saṃhitās and Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201618"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201618">1</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋतु">ṛtu</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="निदाघ">nidāgha</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201618"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201618">↑</a> Av. ix. 5, 31; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 4, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 1, 16, etc.; <i>naidāghīya,</i> ‘belonging to the summer,’ <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxiii. 16, 8, etc.</li></ol></div>

नै-दान|नैदान|नै|दान|నై-దాన|నైదాన|నై|దాన|ನೈ-ದಾನ|ನೈದಾನ|ನೈ|ದಾನ|nai-dāna|naidāna|nai|dāna|nai-daana|naidaana|daana|_vei_120@1619|_vei1_pn460
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1618">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1620">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn459">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn460">1:460</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn461">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nai-dāna</b> is a term applied in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201619"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201619">1</a></sup> to a class of Vedic interpreters. Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201619"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201619">2</a></sup> takes the Naidānas to be ‘etymologists,’ but Sieg<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201619"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201619">3</a></sup> thinks they are the same as the <b>Aitihāsikas</b> or ‘legendarists.’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201619"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201619">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201619"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201619">↑</a> vi. 9; vii. 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201619"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201619">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> But <i>of.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, <i>Erlāuterungen,</i> 220, 221; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 2^2, 176.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201619"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201619">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 29.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201619"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201619">↑</a> The word seems to mean ‘one concerned with the original form’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a></i>).</li></ol></div>

नै-ध्रुवि|नैध्रुवि|नै|ध्रुवि|నై-ధ్రువి|నైధ్రువి|నై|ధ్రువి|ನೈ-ಧ್ರುವಿ|ನೈಧ್ರುವಿ|ನೈ|ಧ್ರುವಿ|nai-dhruvi|naidhruvi|nai|dhruvi|_vei_120@1620|_vei1_pn460
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1619">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1621">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn459">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn460">1:460</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn461">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nai-dhruvi,</b> ‘descendant of Nidhruva,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कश्यप">kaśyapa</a></b> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201620"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201620">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201620"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201620">↑</a> vi. 4, 33 (Mādhyandina = vi. 5, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

नैमिशि|నైమిశి|ನೈಮಿಶಿ|naimiśi|naimishi|_vei_120@1621|_vei1_pn460
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1620">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1622">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn459">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn460">1:460</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn461">↷</a><hr><p><b>Naimiśi</b> is the epithet of <b>Śitibāhu <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐषकृत">aiṣakṛta</a></b> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201621"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201621">1</a></sup> It is probably to be taken as an indication that Śitibāhu came from the Naimiśa forest.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201621"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201621">↑</a> i. 363 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 26, 192).</li></ol></div>

नैमिशीय|నైమిశీయ|ನೈಮಿಶೀಯ|naimiśīya|naimishiiya|naimisheeya|_vei_120@1622|_vei1_pn460
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1621">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1623">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn459">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn460">1:460</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn461">↷</a><hr><p><b>Naimiśīya,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201622"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201622">1</a></sup> <b>Naimiṣīya</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201622"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201622">2</a></sup> denotes the dwellers in the Naimiśa forest. They are mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201622"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201622">2</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201622"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201622">2</a></sup> being clearly of special sanctity. Hence in the Epic the Mahābhārata is said to have been recited to the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषिस्">ṛṣis</a> dwelling in the Naimiṣa forest.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201622"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201622">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201622"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201622">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 6, 4; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 363 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 26, 192).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201622"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201622">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxvi. 5; xxviii. 4; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 2, 13; Naimiṣya, <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, x. 6 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 469). The cerebrals seems to be universal later.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201622"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201622">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxvi. 5; xxviii. 4; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 2, 13; Naimiṣya, <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, x. 6 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 469). The cerebrals seems to be universal later.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201622"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201622">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxvi. 5; xxviii. 4; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 2, 13; Naimiṣya, <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, x. 6 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 469). The cerebrals seems to be universal later.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201622"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201622">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 34, 45, 54, 68, 70, 185.</li></ol></div>

नैर्-उक्त|नैरुक्त|नैर्|उक्त|నైర్-ఉక్త|నైరుక్త|నైర్|ఉక్త|ನೈರ್-ಉಕ್ತ|ನೈರುಕ್ತ|ನೈರ್|ಉಕ್ತ|nair-ukta|nairukta|nair|ukta|_vei_120@1623|_vei1_pn460
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1622">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1624">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn459">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn460">1:460</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn461">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nair-ukta</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201623"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201623">1</a></sup> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201623"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201623">2</a></sup> denotes a man who knows the true etymology of words, and explains their meaning accordingly. Yāska's <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> is the classic work of this school, and forms a commentary on an earlier <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, the so-called Naighaṇṭuka, a glossary consisting of five collections of Vedic words. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201623"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201623">↑</a> ‘One concerned with etymological explanation’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="निर्">nir</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="उक्त">ukta</a></i>).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201623"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201623">↑</a> i. 12; vi. 11; xi. 19. 29. 31; xii. 10; xiii. 9.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 26, 85; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 2, 39, n.; Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffc des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 10-13; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 2^2, 165 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

नै-षाद|नैषाद|नै|षाद|నై-షాద|నైషాద|నై|షాద|ನೈ-ಷಾದ|ನೈಷಾದ|ನೈ|ಷಾದ|nai-ṣāda|naiṣāda|nai|ṣāda|nai-Shaada|naiShaada|Shaada|_vei_120@1624|_vei1_pn461
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1623">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1625">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn460">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn461">1:461</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn462">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nai-ṣāda,</b> a ‘man of <a class="vei_120a" href="निषाद">niṣāda</a>,’ is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xxv. 15) and the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā (xxx. 8).</p></div>

नैषिध|నైషిధ|ನೈಷಿಧ|naiṣidha|naiShidha|_vei_120@1625|_vei1_pn461
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1624">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1626">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn460">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn461">1:461</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn462">↷</a><hr><p><b>Naiṣidha</b> is the reading in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शत">śata</a> atha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (ii. 3, 2, 1. 2) of the epithet of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नड">naḍa</a>,</b> a king of the south. The later form of the name is Naiṣadha; the St. Petersburg Dictionarv suggests that its original form was Naiḥṣidha.</p></div>

नोधस्|नोध|నోధస్|నోధ|ನೋಧಸ್|ನೋಧ|nodhas|nodha|_vei_120@1626|_vei1_pn461
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1625">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1627">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn460">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn461">1:461</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn462">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nodhas</b> is the name of a poet who is mentioned in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201626"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201626">1</a></sup> and to whom certain of its hymns are ascribed.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201626"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201626">2</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201626"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201626">3</a></sup> he is called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काक्षीवत">kākṣīvata</a>,</b> a ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a>.</b>’ Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201626"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201626">4</a></sup> regards him as contemporary with the defeat of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a>.</b> He was a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोतम">gotama</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201626"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201626">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201626"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201626">↑</a> i. 61, 14; 62, 13; 64, 1, and 124, 4, according to <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iv. 16.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201626"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201626">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 18; Rv. i. 58-64 are ascribed to him in the Anukramaṇī (Index).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201626"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201626">↑</a> vii. 10, 10; xxi. 9, 12. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 27; viii. 12. 17; Av. xv. 2, 4; 4, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201626"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201626">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 110.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201626"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201626">↑</a> Rv. i. 62, 13; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 125. For the controversy as to the meaning of Rv. i. 124, 4, see Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 137. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 33.</li></ol></div>

नौ|నౌ|ನೌ|nau|_vei_120@1627|_vei1_pn461
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1626">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1628">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn460">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn461">1:461</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn462">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nau</b> is the regular word in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201627">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201627">2</a></sup> for a ‘boat’ or ‘ship.’ In the great majority of cases the ship was merely a boat for crossing rivers, though no doubt a large boat was needed for crossing many of the broad rivers of the Panjab as well as the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गंगा">gaṅgā</a>.</b> Often no doubt the <a class="vei_120a" href="नौ">nau</a> was a mere dug-out canoe (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दारु">dāru</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201627">3</a></sup> It is certainly against the theory<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201627">4</a></sup> of the existence in Vedic times of an extensive sea trade that there is no mention of any of the parts of a ship, such as masts and sails, except the oar (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अरित्र">aritra</a></b>), Yet there are some allusions indicating a trade more extensive than that implied by boats used for crossing rivers. The Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201627">5</a></sup> compares the ruin of a kingdom where Brahmins are oppressed to the  sinking of a ship which is leaking (<i>bhinnā</i>); though the language here employed can be made to fit the theory that the ship was only a canoe, it cannot naturally be so interpreted. Moreover, there is mention made in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201627">6</a></sup> of men who go to the ocean (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="समुद्र">samudra</a></b>) eager for gain (<i>saniṣyavaḥ</i>). It is not altogether satisfactory to restrict such references with Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201627">7</a></sup> to the broad stream of the Indus after the union of that river with the tributaries of the Panjab. In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201627">8</a></sup> too it is said that the Aśvins rescued <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भुज्यु">bhujyu</a></b> in the ocean with a ship of a hundred oars (<i>śatāritra</i>). It is not easy to refuse to recognize here the existence of larger vessels with many oars used for sea voyages. The <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201627">9</a></sup> at any rate, clearly refers to maritime navigation. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="समुद्र">samudra</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201627">↑</a> i. 131, 2; ii. 39, 4; viii. 42, 3; 83, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201627">↑</a> Av. ii. 36, 5; v. 19. 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 3, 10, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x. 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 13; vi. 6. 21; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 1, 4; iv. 2, 5, 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201627">↑</a> Rv. x. 155, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201627">↑</a> Wilson, <i>Rigveda,</i> 1, xli.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201627">↑</a> v. 19, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 19, 139. So perhaps the passage, Rv. i. 32, 8, <i>nadaṃ na bhinnam,</i> refers to a ship. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नड">naḍa</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201627">↑</a> Rv. i. 56, 2; iv. 55, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201627">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 22, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201627">↑</a> i. 116, 3 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201627">↑</a> i. 2, 4; ii. 2, 2. But it is not of very early date.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 255-257.</li></ol></div>

न्यग्-रोध|न्यग्रोध|न्यग्|रोध|న్యగ్-రోధ|న్యగ్రోధ|న్యగ్|రోధ|ನ್ಯಗ್-ರೋಧ|ನ್ಯಗ್ರೋಧ|ನ್ಯಗ್|ರೋಧ|nyag-rodha|nyagrodha|nyag|rodha|_vei_120@1628|_vei1_pn462
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1627">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1629">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn461">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn462">1:462</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn463">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nyag-rodha,</b> ‘growing downwards,’ is the name of the <i>Ficus indica,</i> a tree remarkable for sending down from its branches fibres which take root and form new stems. Though the tree is not mentioned by name in the Rigveda, it appears to have been known, as Pischel<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201628">1</a></sup> has shown from a hymn<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201628">2</a></sup> in which its characteristics may be recognized. It is frequently mentioned in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201628">3</a></sup> and the later literature.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201628">4</a></sup> The sacrificial bowls (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="चमस">camasa</a></b>) were made of its wood.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201628">5</a></sup> It was doubtless of the greatest importance, as in modern times, to the Vedic village. The sister tree, the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वत्थ">aśvattha</a></b> (<i>Ficus religiosa</i>), already occurs in the Rigveda.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201628">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 113, 114.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201628">↑</a> i. 24, 7, where <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्तूप">stūpa</a></i> seems to denote the crest or crown of the tree above the main trunk.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201628">↑</a> iv. 37, 4; v. 5, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201628">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 30. 31; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 5, 13 xiii. 2, 7, 3; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 12, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201628">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Samhitā, vii. 4, 12, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 13.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 58.</li></ol></div>

न्य्-अंक|न्यंक|न्य्|अंक|न्य्-अङ्क|न्यङ्क|अङ्क|న్య్-అంక|న్యంక|న్య్|అంక|న్య్-అఙ్క|న్యఙ్క|అఙ్క|ನ್ಯ್-ಅಂಕ|ನ್ಯಂಕ|ನ್ಯ್|ಅಂಕ|ನ್ಯ್-ಅಙ್ಕ|ನ್ಯಙ್ಕ|ಅಙ್ಕ|ny-aṅka|nyaṅka|ny|aṅka|ny-aMka|nyaMka|aMka|ny-anka|nyanka|anka|_vei_120@1629|_vei1_pn462
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1628">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1630">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn461">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn462">1:462</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn463">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ny-aṅka</b> in the dual denotes in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201629">1</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201629">2</a></sup> some part of the chariot, parallel with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अंक">aṅka</a>.</b> The <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201629">3</a></sup> has Nyaṅkū, the dual of <a class="vei_120a" href="न्यंकु">nyaṅku</a>. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201629">↑</a> i. 7, 7, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201629">↑</a> i. 3, 5, 4; ii. 7, 8, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201629">↑</a> i. 7, 5; Lāṭyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 8, 9.</li></ol></div>

न्यंकु|न्यङ्कु|న్యంకు|న్యఙ్కు|ನ್ಯಂಕು|ನ್ಯಙ್ಕು|nyaṅku|nyaMku|nyanku|_vei_120@1630|_vei1_pn463
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1629">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1631">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn462">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn463">1:463</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn464">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nyaṅku</b> is the name of an animal in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or ‘horse sacrifice,’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201630"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201630">1</a></sup> It seems clear that a kind a ‘gazelle’ is meant, but the commentary on the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1201630"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1201630">1b</a></sup> suggests ‘bear’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋक्ष">ṛkṣa</a></i>) as a variant rendering.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201630"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201630">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 17, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 9; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 27. 32. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 83. The metre <i><a class="vei_120a" href="न्यंकु">nyaṅku</a>-sāriṇī,</i> ‘with a gazelle's gait,’ is already mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a> Prātiśākhya, xvi. 31; <a class="vei_120a" href="छन्दस्">chandas</a>, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 2.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1201630"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1201630">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 17, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 9; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 27. 32. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 83. The metre <i><a class="vei_120a" href="न्यंकु">nyaṅku</a>-sāriṇī,</i> ‘with a gazelle's gait,’ is already mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a> Prātiśākhya, xvi. 31; <a class="vei_120a" href="छन्दस्">chandas</a>, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 2.</li></ol></div>

न्य्-अस्तिका|न्यस्तिका|न्य्|अस्तिका|న్య్-అస్తికా|న్యస్తికా|న్య్|అస్తికా|ನ್ಯ್-ಅಸ್ತಿಕಾ|ನ್ಯಸ್ತಿಕಾ|ನ್ಯ್|ಅಸ್ತಿಕಾ|ny-astikā|nyastikā|ny|astikā|ny-astikaa|nyastikaa|astikaa|_vei_120@1631|_vei1_pn463
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1630">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1632">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn462">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn463">1:463</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn464">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ny-astikā</b> seems in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201631">1</a></sup> to denote a plant of some kind, identified by the scholiast with the Śaṅkhapuṣpikā (<i>Andropogon aciculatus</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201631">↑</a> vi. 139, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 385; Bloomfield, <i>ymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 539, 540.</li></ol></div>

न्य्-ओचनी|न्य्-ओचनि|न्योचनी|न्योचनि|न्य्|ओचनी|ओचनि|న్య్-ఓచనీ|న్య్-ఓచని|న్యోచనీ|న్యోచని|న్య్|ఓచనీ|ఓచని|ನ್ಯ್-ಓಚನೀ|ನ್ಯ್-ಓಚನಿ|ನ್ಯೋಚನೀ|ನ್ಯೋಚನಿ|ನ್ಯ್|ಓಚನೀ|ಓಚನಿ|ny-ocanī|ny-ocani|nyocanī|nyocani|ny|ocanī|ocani|ny-ochanii|ny-ochani|nyochanii|nyochani|ochanii|ochani|ny-ochanee|nyochanee|ochanee|_vei_120@1632|_vei1_pn463
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1631">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1633">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn462">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn463">1:463</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn464">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ny-ocanī</b> is found in the marriage hymn of the Rigveda (x. 85, 6), where some kind of ornament worn by women seems to be meant. The commentator Sāyaṇa interprets it as ‘female slave.’</p></div>

पक्ति|పక్తి|ಪಕ್ತಿ|pakti|_vei_120@1633|_vei1_pn463
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1632">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1634">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn462">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn463">1:463</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn464">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pakti</b> denotes a cooked object, probably a kind of ‘cake,’ in the Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201633"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201633">1</a></sup> One who cooks food is called <b>Paktṛ.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201633"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201633">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201633"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201633">↑</a> Rv. iv. 24, 5. 7; 25, 6. 7; vi. 29, 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxi. 59, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201633"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201633">↑</a> Av. x. 9, 7. 11. 25; xi. 1, 17; xii. 3, 17; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 4, 17; x. 4, 2, 19.</li></ol></div>

पक्थ|పక్థ|ಪಕ್ಥ|paktha|_vei_120@1634|_vei1_pn463
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1633">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1635">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn462">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn463">1:463</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn464">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paktha</b> is the name of a people in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201634"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201634">1</a></sup> where they appear as one of the tribes that opposed<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201634"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201634">2</a></sup> the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तृत्सु">tṛtsu</a>-Bharatas</b> in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दाशराज्ञ">dāśarājña</a>.</b> or ‘battle of the ten kings.’ Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201634"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201634">3</a></sup> compares  them with the tribe of  and their country , mentioned as in the north-west of India by Herodotus,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201634"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201634">4</a></sup> and with the modern Pakhthūn in Eastern Afghanistan, holding that they were a northern tribe; this is probable, since the Bharatas seem to have occupied the <a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a>, or ‘Middle Land.’ In three passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201634"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201634">5</a></sup> a <a class="vei_120a" href="पक्थ">paktha</a> is referred to as a protégé of the Aśvins. The second connects him with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a>,</b> whose tribe, the <b>Pūrus,</b> were aided by the Pakthas in their unsuccessful onslaught on <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>.</b> In the third passage he seems specified as <b>Tūrvāyaṇa,</b> and appears as an opponent of <b>Cyavāna.</b><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201634"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201634">6</a></sup> Probably, therefore, <a class="vei_120a" href="पक्थ">paktha</a> in all cases denotes the king of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पक्थ">paktha</a> people.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201634"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201634">↑</a> vii. 18. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201634"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201634">↑</a> Roth, <i>Zur Litteratur und Geschichte des Weda,</i> 95, thought that the Pakthas were allies of the Trtsus, but this view is certainly incorrect. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 260.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201634"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201634">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 430, 431.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201634"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201634">↑</a> vii. 65 (); iii. 102 and iv. 44 ().</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201634"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201634">↑</a> viii. 22, 10; 49, 10; x. 61, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201634"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201634">↑</a> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 71-77.</li></ol></div>

पक्व|పక్వ|ಪಕ್ವ|pakva|_vei_120@1635|_vei1_pn464
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1634">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1636">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn463">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn464">1:464</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn465">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pakva,</b> ‘cooked,’ is used substantively as meaning ‘cooked food’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201635"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201635">1</a></sup> or ‘cooked milk.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201635"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201635">2</a></sup> The word is also used of ‘baked’ bricks.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201635"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201635">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201635"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201635">↑</a> Rv. vi. 63, 9; Av. vi. 119, 2; xii. 3, 55; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 1, 26; ii. 6, 1, 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201635"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201635">↑</a> Rv. i. 62, 9; 180, 3; ii. 40, 2; iii. 30, 14; vi. 44, 24, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201635"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201635">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 1, 2, 22; vii. 2, 1, 7.</li></ol></div>

पक्ष|పక్ష|ಪಕ್ಷ|pakṣa|pakSha|paxa|_vei_120@1636|_vei1_pn464
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1635">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1637">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn463">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn464">1:464</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn465">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pakṣa</b> is, in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201636"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201636">1</a></sup> applied to some part of a house, either the ‘side posts,’ according to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201636"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201636">2</a></sup> Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201636"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201636">3</a></sup> and Grill,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201636"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201636">4</a></sup> or ‘sides,’ as understood by Whitney<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201636"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201636">5</a></sup> and Bloomfield.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201636"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201636">6</a></sup> The description of the roof (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="छदिस्">chadis</a></b>) in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201636"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201636">7</a></sup> as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="चतुष्">catuṣ</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पक्ष">pakṣa</a>,</i> ‘four-sided,’ tells in favour of the second explanation. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201636"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201636">8</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="पक्ष">pakṣa</a> is used of the sides of a chariot. For <a class="vei_120a" href="पक्ष">pakṣa</a> as the ‘half’ of a month, see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201636"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201636">↑</a> ix. 3, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201636"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201636">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201636"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201636">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 153.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201636"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201636">↑</a> <i>Hundert Lieder^2,</i> 188.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201636"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201636">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 526.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201636"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201636">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 597.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201636"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201636">↑</a> iii. 7, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201636"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201636">↑</a> i. 5, 12, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 210.</li></ol></div>

पक्षस्|पक्ष|పక్షస్|పక్ష|ಪಕ್ಷಸ್|ಪಕ್ಷ|pakṣas|pakṣa|pakShas|pakSha|paxas|paxa|_vei_120@1637|_vei1_pn465
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1636">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1638">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn464">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn465">1:465</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn466">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pakṣas</b> is found in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201637">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201637">2</a></sup> meaning the ‘sides’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201637">3</a></sup> of a chariot. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201637">4</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201637">5</a></sup> it is used of the sides of a hut or chamber (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाला">śālā</a></b>). In the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201637">6</a></sup> it means the ‘wing’ of a door. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201637">7</a></sup> the ‘half’ of an army is so named, and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201637">8</a></sup> it means the ‘half’ of a month, or ‘fortnight.’ <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पक्ष">pakṣa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201637">↑</a> viii. 8, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201637">↑</a> vii. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201637">↑</a> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 506; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 117.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201637">↑</a> xxx. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201637">↑</a> i. 2, 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201637">↑</a> xxix. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201637">↑</a> ii. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201637">↑</a> xxiii. 6, 6.</li></ol></div>

पक्षिन्|పక్షిన్|ಪಕ್ಷಿನ್|pakṣin|pakShin|paxin|_vei_120@1638|_vei1_pn465
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1637">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1639">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn464">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn465">1:465</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn466">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pakṣin</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201638"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201638">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201638"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201638">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘winged’ creature, more particularly a ‘bird.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201638"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201638">↑</a> i. 48. 5; 182, 5; x. 127, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201638"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201638">↑</a> Av. iv. 34, 4; xi. 5, 21; xii. 1, 51; xiii. 2, 33; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxiv. 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 23; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 5, 18, etc.</li></ol></div>

पंक्ति|पङ्क्ति|పంక్తి|పఙ్క్తి|ಪಂಕ್ತಿ|ಪಙ್ಕ್ತಿ|paṅkti|paMkti|pankti|_vei_120@1639|_vei1_pn465
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1638">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1640">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn464">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn465">1:465</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn466">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paṅkti,</b> originally a ‘set of five,’ denotes as early as the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201639"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201639">1</a></sup> a ‘series’ generally. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201639"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201639">2</a></sup> the word is used of the series of a man's ancestors whom he purifies by certain conduct.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201639"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201639">↑</a> x. 117, 8, as taken by the St. Petersburg Dictionary.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201639"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201639">↑</a> x. 38, 39.</li></ol></div>

पचत|పచత|ಪಚತ|pacata|pachata|_vei_120@1640|_vei1_pn465
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1639">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1641">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn464">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn465">1:465</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn466">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pacata,</b> like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पक्ति">pakti</a>,</b> denotes ‘cooked food’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201640"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201640">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201640"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201640">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201640"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201640">↑</a> i. 61, 7; x. 116, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201640"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201640">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxi. 60; xxiii. 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 21, etc.</li></ol></div>

पचन|పచన|ಪಚನ|pacana|pachana|_vei_120@1641|_vei1_pn465
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1640">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1642">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn464">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn465">1:465</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn466">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pacana</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201641"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201641">1</a></sup> and the Śatapatha Brāhamaṇa<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201641"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201641">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘vessel for cooking’ food. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201641"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201641">↑</a> i. 162, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201641"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201641">↑</a> vi. 5, 43, 3. 4; xiv. 1, 2, 21.</li></ol></div>

पज्र|పజ్ర|ಪಜ್ರ|pajra|_vei_120@1642|_vei1_pn466
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1641">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1643">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn465">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn466">1:466</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn468">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pajra</b> is the name of the family from which <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a></b> sprang (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="पज्रिय">pajriya</a></b>). It is mentioned several times in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201642"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201642">1</a></sup> According to Pischel,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201642"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201642">2</a></sup> the epithet <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पृक्ष-याम">pṛkṣa-yāma</a></i><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201642"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201642">3</a></sup> applied to them means ‘carrying out brilliant sacrificial performances,’ which won for them <b>Śrutaratha's</b> generosity. In two passages<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201642"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201642">4</a></sup> Roth<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201642"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201642">5</a></sup> sees a <a class="vei_120a" href="पज्र">pajra</a> called Sāmaṅ. This is uncertain, but in any case a <a class="vei_120a" href="पज्र">pajra</a> seems clearly alluded to. Elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201642"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201642">6</a></sup> it is very doubtful whether the word is a proper name at all. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="शाट्यायन">śāṭyāyana</a><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201642"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201642">7</a></sup> the Pajras are declared to be Aṅgirases.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201642"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201642">↑</a> i. 117, 10; 122, 7. 8; 126, 4. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201642"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201642">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 97, 98.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201642"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201642">↑</a> Rv. i. 127, 8, where Roth (St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i>) thinks this compound is probably a proper name.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201642"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201642">↑</a> Rv. viii. 4, 17; 6, 47.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201642"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201642">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. <a class="vei_120a" href="पज्र">pajra</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201642"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201642">↑</a> Rv. i. 190, 5, where the reference is clearly hostile and contemptuous.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201642"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201642">↑</a> Cited by Sāyaṇa on Rv. i. 51, 4.</li></ol></div>

पज्रा|పజ్రా|ಪಜ್ರಾ|pajrā|pajraa|_vei_120@1643|_vei1_pn466
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1642">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1644">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn465">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn466">1:466</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn468">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pajrā</b> occurs once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201643">1</a></sup> where Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201643">2</a></sup> sees in the word the name of the wife of the sacrificer, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पज्र">pajra</a>,</b> while Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201643">3</a></sup> takes it to be an epithet (‘stout’) of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> plant. The sense is thus uncertain.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201643">↑</a> ix. 82, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201643">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 110.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201643">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. <a class="vei_120a" href="पज्र">pajra</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

पज्रिय|పజ్రియ|ಪಜ್ರಿಯ|pajriya|_vei_120@1644|_vei1_pn466
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1643">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1645">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn465">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn466">1:466</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn468">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pajriya</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="पज्र">pajra</a>,’ is the patronymic<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201644">1</a></sup> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a></b> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201644">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201644">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुग्र्य">tugrya</a>,</b> n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201644">↑</a> i. 116, 7; 117, 6; 120, 5.</li></ol></div>

पञ्च-जनाः|पञ्चजनाः|पञ्च|जनाः|పఞ్చ-జనాః|పఞ్చజనాః|పఞ్చ|జనాః|ಪಞ್ಚ-ಜನಾಃ|ಪಞ್ಚಜನಾಃ|ಪಞ್ಚ|ಜನಾಃ|pañca-janāḥ|pañcajanāḥ|pañca|janāḥ|pa~ncha-janaaH|pa~nchajanaaH|pa~ncha|janaaH|_vei_120@1645|_vei1_pn466
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1644">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1646">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn465">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn466">1:466</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn468">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pañca-janāḥ,</b> the ‘five peoples,’ are mentioned under various names in Vedic literature.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201645">1</a></sup> Who are meant by the five is very uncertain. The <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201645">2</a></sup> explains the five to be gods, men, Gandharvas and Apsarases, snakes, and the Fathers.  <a class="vei_120a" href="औपमन्यव">aupamanyava</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201645">3</a></sup> held that the four castes (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a></b>) and the <b>Niṣādas</b> made up the five, and Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201645">4</a></sup> is of the same opinion. <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201645">5</a></sup> thinks that the five are the Gandharvas, fathers, gods, Asuras, and Rakṣases. No one of these explanations can be regarded as probable. Roth<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201645">6</a></sup> and Geldner<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201645">7</a></sup> think that all the peoples of the earth are méant: just as there are four quarters (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="दिश्">diś</a></b>), there are peoples at the four quarters (N. E. S. W.), with the Āryan folk in the middle. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201645">8</a></sup> opposes this view on the ground that the inclusion of all peoples in one expression is not in harmony with the distinction so often made between Āryan and <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>; that neither <i>janāsaḥ,</i> ‘men,’<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201645">9</a></sup> nor <i>mānuṣāḥ,</i> ‘people,’<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201645">10</a></sup> could be used of non-Āryans; that the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> is referred to as being among the five tribes;<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201645">11</a></sup> that the five tribes are mentioned as on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201645">12</a></sup> and that <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> is <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पाञ्चजन्य">pāñcajanya</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201645">13</a></sup> ‘belonging to the five peoples.’ He concludes that Āryans alone are meant, and in particular the five tribes of the <b>Anus, Druhyus, Yadus, Turvaśas,</b> and <b>Pūrus,</b> who are all mentioned together in one or perhaps two hymns of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201645">14</a></sup> and four of whom occur in another hymn.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201645">15</a></sup> But he admits that the expression might easily be used more generally later. Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1201645">16</a></sup> has combated Zimmer's view, but his own opinion rests mainly on his theory that there was no people named <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a>, but only a king of the Yadus called <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a>, and that theory is not very probable. </p><p>In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1201645">17</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1201645">18</a></sup> the five peoples are opposed to the Bharatas, and in the former work<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1201645">19</a></sup> seven peoples are alluded to.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201645">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 31; iv. 27; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 1, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, v. 6; xxxii. 6; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 2, 9 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्च-जनाः">pañca-janāḥ</a></i> as a compound). See also <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्च">pañca</a> mānuṣāḥ,</i> Rv. viii. 9, 2; <i>mānavāḥ,</i> Av. iii. 21, 5; 24, 3; xii. 1, 15; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="जनाः">janāḥ</a>,</i> Rv. iii. 37, 9; 59, 8; vi. 14, 4; viii. 32, 22; ix. 65, 23; 92, 3; x. 45, 6; <i>kṛṣṭayaḥ,</i> ii. 2, 10; iii. 53, 16; iv. 38, 10; x. 60, 4; 119, 6; Av. iii. 24, 3; <i>kṣitayaḥ,</i> Rv. i. 7, 9; 176, 3; v. 35, 2; vi. 46, 7; vii. 75, 4; 79, 1; <i>carṣaṇyaḥ,</i> Rv. v. 86, 2; vii. 15, 2; ix. 101, 9. See Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 204. Every book of the Rigveda has a mention of the five peoples: one such in ii. and iv.; two in i., v., vi., vii., viii.; three in iii. and ix.; four in x.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201645">↑</a> iii. 31.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201645">↑</a> In <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201645">↑</a> On Rv. i. 7, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201645">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201645">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. <a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्टि">kṛṣṭi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, Erläuterungen,</i> 28. For his view, Av. iii. 24, 3, can be cited: <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्च">pañca</a> pradiśo mānavīḥ <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्च">pañca</a> kṛṣṭayaḥ,</i> ‘the five directions, the five races of men.’</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201645">↑</a> <i>Siebenzig Lieder,</i> 18. See, however, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>, Glossar,</i> 103, where he recognizes the use of the phrase to denote five tribes, as well as all mankind.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201645">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 119-123. His view is accepted by Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 153; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 179, is doubtful.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201645">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> its use in Rv. ii. 12, <i>sa janāsa Indraḥ,</i> where the address must be to Āryan men.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201645">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. viii. 9, 2, and i. 52, 9, with viii. 70, 11; x. 28, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201645">↑</a> Rv. ix. 65, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201645">↑</a> Rv. vi. 61, 12 <i>(<a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्च">pañca</a> jātā). Cf.</i> x. 53, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201645">↑</a> v. 32, 11. <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> is ‘of the five tribes,’ Rv. ix. 66, 20. <a class="vei_120a" href="अत्रि">atri</a> also is so described, Rv. i. 117, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201645">↑</a> Rv. i. 108, 8. In vii. 18, cited by Zimmer, 122, the five tribes do not occur <i>eo nomine,</i> for <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यक्षु">yakṣu</a></b> replaces <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a>. But it is probable that <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a> is meant by <a class="vei_120a" href="यक्षु">yakṣu</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201645">↑</a> Rv. viii. 10, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1201645">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 260.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1201645">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1201645">↑</a> viii. 23.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1201645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1201645">↑</a> It is a conjecture of Weber's, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 202, that the five peoples are identical with the <b>Pañcālas,</b> and the seven mentioned in Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 4, 23, with the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas.</li></ol></div>

पञ्च-दशी|पञ्च-दशि|पञ्चदशी|पञ्चदशि|पञ्च|दशी|दशि|పఞ్చ-దశీ|పఞ్చ-దశి|పఞ్చదశీ|పఞ్చదశి|పఞ్చ|దశీ|దశి|ಪಞ್ಚ-ದಶೀ|ಪಞ್ಚ-ದಶಿ|ಪಞ್ಚದಶೀ|ಪಞ್ಚದಶಿ|ಪಞ್ಚ|ದಶೀ|ದಶಿ|pañca-daśī|pañca-daśi|pañcadaśī|pañcadaśi|pañca|daśī|daśi|pa~ncha-dashii|pa~ncha-dashi|pa~nchadashii|pa~nchadashi|pa~ncha|dashii|dashi|pa~ncha-dashee|pa~nchadashee|dashee|_vei_120@1646|_vei1_pn468
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1645">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1647">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn466">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn468">1:468</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn469">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pañca-daśī,</b> ‘the fifteenth day of the month,’ is already mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 5, 10, 5).</p></div>

पञ्च-नद|पञ्चनद|पञ्च|नद|పఞ్చ-నద|పఞ్చనద|పఞ్చ|నద|ಪಞ್ಚ-ನದ|ಪಞ್ಚನದ|ಪಞ್ಚ|ನದ|pañca-nada|pañcanada|pañca|nada|pa~ncha-nada|pa~nchanada|pa~ncha|_vei_120@1647|_vei1_pn468
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1646">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1648">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn466">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn468">1:468</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn469">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pañca-nada,</b> ‘having five streams,’ is not found until the epic period as the name of the Panjāb, which has no designation in the earlier literature. The importance of the Panjāb as the home<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201647">1</a></sup> of the Rigveda has been greatly diminished by recent research, Hopkins,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201647">2</a></sup> Pischel,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201647">3</a></sup> and Geldner<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201647">4</a></sup> having on different grounds shown reason for believing that the Rigveda, at least in great part, was composed farther east, in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a>,</b> which admittedly was the home of the later Vedic culture. Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201647">5</a></sup> considers that the Rigveda belongs in part to the Panjāb, or rather to Arachosia, and in part to the Middle Country. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="तृत्सु">tṛtsu</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201647">↑</a> See, <i>e.g.,</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 32 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201647">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 19, 19-28. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 145, 441.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201647">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 218.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201647">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 3, 152.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201647">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 98 <i>et seq.</i> But see <b>Divodāsa.</b> <i>Cf.</i> also Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 189.</li></ol></div>

पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण|పఞ్చవింశ బ్రాహ్మణ|ಪಞ್ಚವಿಂಶ ಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ|pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa|pa~nchaviMsha braahmaNa|_vei_120@1648|_vei1_pn468
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1647">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1649">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn466">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn468">1:468</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn469">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pañcaviṃśa Brāhmaṇa.</b> See <b>Tāṇdya.</b></p></div>

पञ्चाल|పఞ్చాల|ಪಞ್ಚಾಲ|pañcāla|pa~nchaala|_vei_120@1649|_vei1_pn468
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1648">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1650">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn466">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn468">1:468</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn469">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pañcāla</b> is the later name<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201649"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201649">1</a></sup> of the people called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रिवि">krivi</a></b> in the Rigveda. The Pañcālas are rarely referred to except in connexion with the <b>Kurus,</b> and the kings of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas are mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201649"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201649">2</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201649"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201649">3</a></sup> the Pañcālas appear as the people of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="केशिन्">keśin</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दाल्भ्य">dālbhya</a>.</b>  In the Upaniṣads and later<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201649"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201649">4</a></sup> the Brahmins of the Pañcālas figure as taking part in philosophical and philological discussions. The Saṃhitopaniṣad <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201649"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201649">5</a></sup> makes mention of the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राच्य">prācya</a>-Pañcālas.</p><p>The Pañcālas, no doubt, included other tribes besides the Krivis. The name seems to refer to five tribes, and it has been suggested<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201649"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201649">6</a></sup> that the Pañcālas represent the five tribes of the Rigveda, but the suggestion is not very probable. There is no trace in Vedic literature of the Epic division of the Pañcālas into northern (<i>uttara</i>) and southern (<i>dakṣiṇa</i>). The Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201649"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201649">7</a></sup> mentions their town <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परिचक्रा">paricakrā</a>;</b> other towns to which allusion seems to be made were <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काम्पील">kāmpīla</a></b> and kauśāmbī.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201649"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201649">8</a></sup> Of their kings and chiefs, as distinguished from kings of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas, we hear of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रैव्य">kraivya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="दुर्मुख">durmukha</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रवाहण जैवलि">pravāhaṇa jaivali</a>,</b> and <b>Śona.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201649"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201649">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 4, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201649"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201649">↑</a> viii. 14</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201649"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201649">↑</a> xxx. 2 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 471).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201649"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201649">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 1 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 2, 1 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>); Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 3, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a> Prātiśākhya, ii. 12, 44; <a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 6; Sāṅkhāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 13, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201649"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201649">↑</a> 2. <i>Cf. Indische Studien,</i> 4, 375, n.; 8, 92, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201649"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201649">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 202; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 108, n. 1. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 191 <i>et seq.</i>; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 10, 90, 114, 115, 125, 135, 136.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201649"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201649">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201649"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201649">↑</a> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौशाम्बेय">kauśāmbeya</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

पञ्चाल-चण्ड|पञ्चालचण्ड|पञ्चाल|चण्ड|పఞ్చాల-చణ్డ|పఞ్చాలచణ్డ|పఞ్చాల|చణ్డ|ಪಞ್ಚಾಲ-ಚಣ್ಡ|ಪಞ್ಚಾಲಚಣ್ಡ|ಪಞ್ಚಾಲ|ಚಣ್ಡ|pañcāla-caṇḍa|pañcālacaṇḍa|pañcāla|caṇḍa|pa~nchaala-chaNDa|pa~nchaalachaNDa|pa~nchaala|chaNDa|_vei_120@1650|_vei1_pn469
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1649">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1651">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn468">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn469">1:469</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn470">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pañcāla-caṇḍa</b> is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201650"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201650">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201650"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201650">2</a></sup> Āraṇyakas.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201650"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201650">↑</a> iii. 1, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201650"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201650">↑</a> vii. 18.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 391; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 50, 315, 326.</li></ol></div>

पञ्चावि|పఞ్చావి|ಪಞ್ಚಾವಿ|pañcāvi|pa~nchaavi|_vei_120@1651|_vei1_pn469
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1650">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1652">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn468">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn469">1:469</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn470">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pañcāvi</b> occurs several times in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201651"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201651">1</a></sup> in the sense of ‘aged five lamb-periods’ (of six months), that is, ‘thirty months old.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201651"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201651">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201651"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201651">↑</a> xviii. 26; xxi. 14; xxiv. 12; xxviii. 26.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201651"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201651">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्र्यवि">tryavi</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

पञ्चौदन|పఞ్చౌదన|ಪಞ್ಚೌದನ|pañcaudana|pa~nchaudana|_vei_120@1652|_vei1_pn469
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1651">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1653">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn468">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn469">1:469</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn470">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pañcaudana</b> is an adjective in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201652"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201652">1</a></sup> meaning ‘prepared with five rice-messes.’ The cooking of five rice dishes is referred to in the same Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201652"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201652">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201652"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201652">↑</a> iv. 14, 7; ix. 5, 8 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201652"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201652">↑</a> ix. 5, 37.</li></ol></div>

पटल|పటల|ಪಟಲ|paṭala|paTala|_vei_120@1653|_vei1_pn470
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1652">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1654">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn469">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn470">1:470</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn471">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paṭala</b> as early as the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201653">1</a></sup> denotes ‘section’ of a work, a sense occurring in the Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201653">2</a></sup> and later.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201653">↑</a> i. 21. 22.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201653">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xi. 9. 20; xiii. 21, 2; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 6. 7.</li></ol></div>

पठर्वन्|పఠర్వన్|ಪಠರ್ವನ್|paṭharvan|paTharvan|_vei_120@1654|_vei1_pn470
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1653">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1655">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn469">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn470">1:470</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn471">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paṭharvan</b> appears to be the name of a man in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201654"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201654">1</a></sup> According to Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201654"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201654">2</a></sup> however, the word is Paṭharu, and is the name of a fort which was saved by a rain-storm from being set on fire.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201654"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201654">↑</a> i. 112, 17, with Sāyaṇa's note.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201654"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201654">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 204.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 1, 147, 148.</li></ol></div>

पड्गृभि|పడ్గృభి|ಪಡ್ಗೃಭಿ|paḍgṛbhi|paDgRbhi|paDgRibhi|paDgRubhi|paDgRRibhi|_vei_120@1655|_vei1_pn470
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1654">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1656">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn469">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn470">1:470</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn471">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paḍgṛbhi,</b> ‘seizing by the foot,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201655"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201655">1</a></sup> is the name in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201655"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201655">2</a></sup> of either a man<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201655"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201655">3</a></sup> or a demon. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पड्बीश">paḍbīśa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201655"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201655">↑</a> But it may mean ‘seizing with a cord.’ <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> p. 34 (top); Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 236.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201655"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201655">↑</a> x. 49, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201655"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201655">↑</a> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 165.</li></ol></div>

पड्-बीश|पड्बीश|पड्|बीश|పడ్-బీశ|పడ్బీశ|పడ్|బీశ|ಪಡ್-ಬೀಶ|ಪಡ್ಬೀಶ|ಪಡ್|ಬೀಶ|paḍ-bīśa|paḍbīśa|paḍ|bīśa|paD-biisha|paDbiisha|paD|biisha|paD-beesha|paDbeesha|beesha|_vei_120@1656|_vei1_pn470
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1655">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1657">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn469">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn470">1:470</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn471">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paḍ-bīśa,</b> the ‘foot-fetter’ of a horse in five passages, two in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201656">1</a></sup> and one each in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201656">2</a></sup> the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201656">3</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201656">4</a></sup> Elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201656">5</a></sup> its uses are metaphorical. According to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201656">6</a></sup> the literal sense is ‘foot-fastening’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पड्">paḍ</a></i> being = <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पद्">pad</a>,</i> ‘foot,’ and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="बीश">bīśa</a>,</i> written <i>vīśa</i> in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, being connected with the Latin <i>vincire,</i> ‘bind’). Pischel<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201656">7</a></sup> objects that the sense of ‘foot-fastening’ involves the absurdity, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> passages, of a fine horse from the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धु">sindhu</a></b> (Indus) being spoken of as tearing up the peg to which it is fastened. He suggests instead the meaning of ‘hobble,’ which must be right.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201656">8</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201656">↑</a> i. 162, 14. 16 = <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 6, 9, 1. 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxv. 38. 39.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201656">↑</a> vi. 2, 13 (Mādhyaṃdina).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201656">↑</a> v. 1, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201656">↑</a> ix. 7; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 57, n. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201656">↑</a> Rv. x. 97, 16; Av. viii. 1, 4; xii. 5, 15; xvi. 8, 27; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 10, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201656">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201656">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 233-236.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201656">↑</a> He explains the first part of the word as derived from <i>paś,</i> ‘tie.’ <i>Cf.</i> Macdoneli, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> p. 34 (top). But the form <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पड्">paḍ</a></i> may be due to a false analogy, and the sense of ‘footfastener’ may express ‘hobble’ quite satisfactorily; it is not confined to a rope tied to a peg in the ground.</li></ol></div>

पण|పణ|ಪಣ|paṇa|paNa|_vei_120@1657|_vei1_pn471
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1656">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1658">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn470">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn471">1:471</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn473">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paṇa,</b> with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतिपण">pratipaṇa</a>,</b> is found in a hymn of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201657"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201657">1</a></sup> denoting the process of bargaining and selling. The root <i>paṇ,</i> from which the word is derived, is employed in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201657"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201657">2</a></sup> while Paṇana in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201657"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201657">3</a></sup> denotes ‘trafficking.’ <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वणिज्">vaṇij</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201657"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201657">↑</a> iii. 15, 4. 6 (in the Paippalāda recension; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 112).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201657"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201657">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, viii. 55; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 3, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 27. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 10, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201657"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201657">↑</a> iii. 3, 2, 19. The root does not occur in the Rigveda, but its etymology is vouched for by the Greek , <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 84, n. 3.</li></ol></div>

पणि|పణి|ಪಣಿ|paṇi|paNi|_vei_120@1658|_vei1_pn471
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1657">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1659">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn470">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn471">1:471</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn473">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paṇi</b> in the Rigveda appears to denote a person who is rich, but who does not give offerings to the gods, or bestow <b>Dakṣiṇās</b> on the priests, and who is therefore an object of intense dislike to the composers of the Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201658">1</a></sup> Hence the gods are asked to attack the Paṇis, who are also referred to as being defeated with slaughter.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201658">2</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="पणि">paṇi</a> is opposed to the pious sacrificer as a niggard,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201658">3</a></sup> and is spoken of as a wolf,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201658">4</a></sup> the symbol of enmity. In some passages<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201658">5</a></sup> the Paṇis definitely appear as mythological figures, demons who withhold the cows or waters of heaven, and to whom Saramā goes on a mission from <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201658">6</a></sup> Among the Paṇis <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बृबु">bṛbu</a></b> was apparently important. In one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201658">7</a></sup> they are described as <b>Bekanāṭas,</b> or ‘usurers’ (?). In another<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201658">8</a></sup> they are called <b>Dasyus,</b> and styled <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मृध्र-वाच्">mṛdhra-vāc</a>,</i> probably ‘of hostile speech,’ and <i>grathin,</i> a word of uncertain meaning. Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201658">9</a></sup> thinks that the latter epithet refers to the continuous flow of a speech which  is not understood, and that <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मृध्र-वाच्">mṛdhra-vāc</a></i> means ‘śpeaking an enemy's speech,’ though not necessarily with reference to non-Āryans.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201658">10</a></sup> In two passages<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201658">11</a></sup> the Paṇis appear as <b>Dāsas,</b> and in one<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201658">12</a></sup> a <a class="vei_120a" href="पणि">paṇi</a> is mentioned in connexion with wergeld (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैर">vaira</a></b>), being apparently regarded as equal to a man merely in the price put on his life, but in other respects as inferior.</p><p>It is difficult to be certain exactly who a <a class="vei_120a" href="पणि">paṇi</a> was. Roth<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201658">13</a></sup> thinks that the word is derived from <i>paṇ,</i> ‘barter,’ and that the <a class="vei_120a" href="पणि">paṇi</a> is properly the man who will give nothing without return, hence the niggard, who neither worships the gods nor rewards their priests. This view is accepted by Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201658">14</a></sup> and by Ludwig.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201658">15</a></sup> The latter scholar thinks the apparent references to fights with Paṇis are to be explained by their having been aboriginal traders who went in caravans--as in Arabia and Northern Africa--prepared to fight, if need be, to protect their goods against attacks which the Āryans would naturally deem quite justified. He supports this explanation by the references to the Paṇis as Dasyus and Dāsas. It is, however, hardly necessary to do more than regard the Paṇis generally as non-worshippers of the gods favoured by the singers; the term is wide enough to cover either the aborigines or hostile Āryan tribes, as well as demons. Hillebrandt,<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1201658">16</a></sup> however, thinks that a real tribe is meant, the Parnians of Strabo, and that they were associated with the Dahae (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a></b>). Moreover, he finds them associated in one passage<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1201658">17</a></sup> with the <b>Pārāvatas,</b> whom  he identifies with the  of Ptolemy,<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1201658">18</a></sup> and with <b>Bṛṣaya,</b> whom he connects with  of Arrian;<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1201658">19</a></sup> he also considers that the frequent<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1201658">20</a></sup> mention of the Paṇis as opponents of <b>Divodāsa</b> shows that the latter was on the Arachosian Haraqaiti (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a></b>) fighting against the Parnians and Dahae, as well as other Iranian tribes. But the identification of <a class="vei_120a" href="पणि">paṇi</a> and the Parnians is needless, especially as the root <i>paṇ,</i> which is found also in the Greek , shows a satisfactory derivation, while the transfer of Divodāsa to the Haraqaiti is improbable. See also <b>Divodāsa</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बेकनाट">bekanāṭa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201658">↑</a> Rv. i. 33, 3; 83, 2; 151, 9; 180, 7; iv. 28, 7; v. 34, 5-7; 61, 8; vi. 13, 3; 53, 3; viii. 64, 2; 97, 2; x. 60, 6; Av. v. 11, 7; xx. 128, 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxv. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201658">↑</a> Rv. 1, 83, 4; 184, 2; iii. 58, 2; v. 34, 7; 61, 8; vi. 13, 3; 20, 4; 33, 2; viii. 64, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201658">↑</a> Rv. i. 124, 10; iv. 51, 3; viii. 45, 14 (where the sense is doubtful). <i>Cf.</i> i. 93, 4; v. 61, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201658">↑</a> Rv. vi. 51, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201658">↑</a> Rv. i. 32, 11; ii. 24, 6; iv. 58, 4; vi. 44, 22; vii. 9, 2; x. 67, 6; 92, 3; Av. iv. 23, 5; xix. 46, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 8, 2, 3. It is impossible definitely to decide in which passages the mythical sense is meant, <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 157.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201658">↑</a> Rv. x. 108.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201658">↑</a> Rv. viii. 66, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201658">↑</a> Rv. vii. 6, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201658">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 89.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201658">↑</a> See Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 1, 23; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 2^2, 114; Davidson, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 37, 23; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 31, n. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201658">↑</a> Rv. v. 34, 5-7; Av. v. 11, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201658">↑</a> Rv. v. 61, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Roth, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 41, 673; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 361, who thinks that the <a class="vei_120a" href="पणि">paṇi</a> is compared unfavourably with a generous woman, but this is unlikely; Hillebrandt, 1, 92, n. 3; Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 58, 59 Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 364.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201658">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 17; vi. 26.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201658">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 257. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Geldner, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>, Glossar,</i> 103.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201658">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 213215. <i>Cf.</i> Bergaigne, <i>Religion Védique,</i> 2, 319.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1201658">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 83 <i>et seq.;</i> 3, 268; <i>Go7ttingische Gelehrte Anzeigen,</i> 1894, 648.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1201658">↑</a> Rv. vi. 61, 1-3.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1201658">↑</a> vi. 20, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1201658">↑</a> iii. 8, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1201658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1201658">↑</a> The Paṇis occur twelve times in Maṇḍala vi.; once each in ii. and viii.; twice in v. and ix.; thrice in iv. and vii.; six times in viii.; nine times in i.; and four times in x., besides the references in the Saramā hymn, x. 108.</li></ol></div>

पण्डित|పణ్డిత|ಪಣ್ಡಿತ|paṇḍita|paNDita|_vei_120@1659|_vei1_pn473
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1658">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1660">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn471">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn473">1:473</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn474">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paṇḍita,</b> a ‘learned man,’ is not found until the <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> period.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201659">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201659">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 4, 1; vi. 4, 16. 17; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 14, 2; Muṇḍaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 2, 8, etc.</li></ol></div>

पतंग|पतंग|पतङ्ग_१|पतङ्ग|పతంగ_౧|పతంగ|పతఙ్గ_౧|పతఙ్గ|ಪತಂಗ_೧|ಪತಂಗ|ಪತಙ್ಗ_೧|ಪತಙ್ಗ|pataṅga_1|pataṅga|pataMga_1|pataMga|patanga_1|patanga|_vei_120@1660|_vei1_pn473
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1659">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1661">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn471">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn473">1:473</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn474">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Pataṅga,</b> ‘flying,’ denotes a ‘winged insect’ in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201660">1</a></sup> and the Upaniṣads.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201660">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201660">↑</a> vi. 50, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201660">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 19 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 2, 14 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>); 2, 14 (= 1, 14); Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 9, 3; 10, 2; vii. 2, 1; 7, 1; 8, 1; 10, 1; Adbhuta <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 5 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 40).</li></ol></div>

पतंग प्राजापत्य|पतंग प्राजापत्य|पतङ्ग प्राजापत्य_२|पतङ्ग प्राजापत्य|పతంగ ప్రాజాపత్య_౨|పతంగ ప్రాజాపత్య|పతఙ్గ ప్రాజాపత్య_౨|పతఙ్గ ప్రాజాపత్య|ಪತಂಗ ಪ್ರಾಜಾಪತ್ಯ_೨|ಪತಂಗ ಪ್ರಾಜಾಪತ್ಯ|ಪತಙ್ಗ ಪ್ರಾಜಾಪತ್ಯ_೨|ಪತಙ್ಗ ಪ್ರಾಜಾಪತ್ಯ|pataṅga prājāpatya_2|pataṅga prājāpatya|pataMga praajaapatya_2|pataMga praajaapatya|patanga praajaapatya_2|patanga praajaapatya|_vei_120@1661|_vei1_pn473
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1660">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1662">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn471">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn473">1:473</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn474">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Pataṅga Prājāpatya</b> (‘descendant of Prajāpati’) is credited by the Anukramaṇī (Index) with the authorship of a hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201661"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201661">1</a></sup> in which <a class="vei_120a" href="पतंग">pataṅga</a> means the ‘sun-bird.’ He is also mentioned in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201661"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201661">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201661"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201661">↑</a> x. 177, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201661"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201661">↑</a> iii. 30, 1. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xi. 14, 28.</li></ol></div>

पतञ्चल काप्य|పతఞ్చల కాప్య|ಪತಞ್ಚಲ ಕಾಪ್ಯ|patañcala kāpya|pata~nchala kaapya|_vei_120@1662|_vei1_pn473
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1661">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1663">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn471">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn473">1:473</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn474">↷</a><hr><p><b>Patañcala Kāpya</b> is the name of a sage mentioned twice in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201662"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201662">1</a></sup> According to Weber,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201662"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201662">2</a></sup> his  name is reminiscent of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कपिल">kapila</a></b> and Patañjali of the ŚāṅkhyaYoga system, but this suggestion may be regarded as quite improbable.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201662"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201662">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201662"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201662">↑</a> iii. 3, 1; 7, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201662"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201662">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 434, 435; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 126, 137, 223, 236, 237.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201662"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201662">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Garbe, <i>Sāṅkhya Philosophie,</i> 25, 26.</li></ol></div>

पतत्रिन्|పతత్రిన్|ಪತತ್ರಿನ್|patatrin|_vei_120@1663|_vei1_pn474
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1662">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1664">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn473">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn474">1:474</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn489">↷</a><hr><p><b>Patatrin</b> denotes a ‘flying creature’ generally in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201663">1</a></sup> or more particularly a ‘bird’ in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201663">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201663">↑</a> iii. 3, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201663">↑</a> viii. 7, 24; x. 10, 14; xiv. 2, 44.</li></ol></div>

पताका|పతాకా|ಪತಾಕಾ|patākā|pataakaa|_vei_120@1664|_vei1_pn474
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1663">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1665">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn473">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn474">1:474</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn489">↷</a><hr><p><b>Patākā,</b> ‘banner,’ is not found until the Adbhuta <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201664"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201664">1</a></sup> Its Vedic equivalent is <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ध्वज">dhvaja</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201664"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201664">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 39, 41 (here erroneously mentioned as <i>patāka,</i> masculine).</li></ol></div>

पति|पत्नी|पत्नि|పతి|పత్నీ|పత్ని|ಪತಿ|ಪತ್ನೀ|ಪತ್ನಿ|pati|patnī|patni|patnii|patnee|_vei_120@1665|_vei1_pn474
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1664">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1666">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn473">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn474">1:474</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn489">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pati, Patnī.</b>--Under these words denoting primarily, as the evidence collected in the St. Petersburg Dictionary shows, ‘lord’ and ‘lady,’ and so ‘husband’ and ‘wife,’ it is convenient to consider the marital relations of the Vedic community.</p><p><i>Child Marriage.</i>--Marriage in the early Vedic texts appears essentially as a union of two persons of full development. This is shown by the numerous references<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201665">1</a></sup> to unmarried girls who grow old in the house of their fathers (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अमा-जुर्">amā-jur</a></i>), and who adorn themselves in desire of marriage, as well as to the paraphernalia of spells and potions used in the Atharvavedic tradition<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201665">2</a></sup> to compel the love of man or woman respectively, while even the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201665">3</a></sup> itself seems to present us with a spell by which a  lover seeks to send all the household to sleep when he visits his beloved. Child wives first occur regularly in the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> period, though it is still uncertain to what extent the rule of marriage before puberty there obtained.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201665">4</a></sup> The marriage ritual also quite clearly presumes that the marriage is a real and not a nominal one: an essential feature is the taking of the bride to her husband's home, and the ensuing cohabitation.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201665">5</a></sup></p><p><i>Limitations on Marriage.</i>--It is difficult to say with certainty within what limits marriage was allowed. The dialogue of <a class="vei_120a" href="यम">yama</a> and Yamī in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201665">6</a></sup> seems clearly to point to a prohibition of the marriage of brother and sister. It can hardly be said, as Weber<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201665">7</a></sup> thinks, to point to a practice that was once in use and later became antiquated. In the Gobhila <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201665">8</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201665">9</a></sup> are found prohibitions against marriage in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a></b> (‘family’) or within six degrees on the mother's or father's side, but in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201665">10</a></sup> marriage is allowed in the third or fourth generation, the former being allowed, according to Harisvamin,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201665">11</a></sup> by the Kāṇvas, and the second by the Saurāṣṭras, while the Dākṣiṇātyas allowed marriage with the daughter of the mother's brother or the son of the father's sister, but presumably not with the daughter of the mother's sister or the son of the father's brother. The prohibition of marriage within the <a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a> cannot then have existed,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201665">12</a></sup> though naturally marriages  outside the <a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a> were frequent.<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201665">13</a></sup> Similarity of caste was also not an essential to marriage, as hypergamy was permitted even by the <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Sūtras,<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201665">14</a></sup> so that a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> could marry wives of any lower caste, a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> wives of the two lowest castes as well as of his own caste, a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्रा">śūdrā</a> as well as a Vaiśyā, although the <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्रा">śūdrā</a> marriages were later disapproved <i>in toto.</i> Instances of such intermarriage are common in the Epic, and are viewed as normal in the Bṛhaddevatā.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201665">15</a></sup></p><p>It was considered proper that the younger brothers and sisters should not anticipate their elders by marrying before them. The later Saṃhitās and Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1201665">16</a></sup> present a series of names expressive of such anticipation, censuring as sinful those who bear them. These terms are the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="परि">pari</a>-vividāna,</i><sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1201665">17</a></sup> or perhaps <i>agre-dadhus,</i><sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1201665">18</a></sup> the man who, though a younger brother, marries before his elder brother, the latter being then called the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="परिवित्त">parivitta</a>;</i><sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1201665">19</a></sup> the <i>agre-<a class="vei_120a" href="दिधिषु">didhiṣu</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1201665">20</a></sup> the man who weds a younger daughter while her elder sister is still unmarried; and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दिधिषू-पति">didhiṣū-pati</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1201665">21</a></sup> who is the husband of the latter. The passages do not explicitly say that the exact order of birth must always be followed, but the mention of the terms shows that the order was often broken.</p><p><i>Widow Remarriage.</i>--The remarriage of a widow was  apparently permitted. This seems originally to have taken the form of the marriage of the widow to the brother or other nearest kinsman of the dead man in order to produce children. At any rate, the ceremony is apparently alluded to in a funeral hymn of the Rigveda;<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1201665">22</a></sup> for the alternative explanation, which sees in the verse a reference to the ritual of the Puruṣamendha (‘human sacrifice’), although accepted by Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1201665">23</a></sup> and Delbrück,<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1201665">24</a></sup> is not at all probable, while the ordinary view is supported by the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> evidence.<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1201665">25</a></sup> Moreover, another passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_1201665">26</a></sup> clearly refers to the marriage of the widow and the husband's brother (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="देवृ">devṛ</a></i>), which constitutes what the Indians later knew as Niyoga.<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_1201665">27</a></sup> This custom was probably not followed except in cases where no son was already born. This custom was hardly remarriage in the strict sense, since the brother might--so far as appears--be already married himself. In the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_28_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_1201665">28</a></sup> a verse refers to a charm which would secure the reunion, in the next world, of a wife and her second husband. Though, as Delbrück<sup id="ref_120_29_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29_vei_1201665">29</a></sup> thinks, this very possibly refers to a case in which the first husband was still alive,<sup id="ref_120_30_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_30_vei_1201665">30</a></sup> but was impotent or had lost caste (<i>patita</i>),<sup id="ref_120_31_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_31_vei_1201665">31</a></sup> still it is certain that the later <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_32_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_32_vei_1201665">32</a></sup> began to recognize ordinary remarriage in case of the death of the first husband.  Pischel<sup id="ref_120_33_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_33_vei_1201665">33</a></sup> finds some evidence in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_34_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_34_vei_1201665">34</a></sup> to the effect that a woman could remarry if her husband disappeared and could not be found or heard of.</p><p><i>Polygamy.</i>--A Vedic Indian could have more than one wife. This is proved clearly by many passages in the Rigveda;<sup id="ref_120_35_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_35_vei_1201665">35</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>, according to the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_36_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_36_vei_1201665">36</a></sup> had ten wives; and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_37_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_37_vei_1201665">37</a></sup> explains polygamy by a characteristic legend. Moreover, the king regularly has four wives attributed to him, the <a class="vei_120a" href="महिषी">mahiṣī</a>,<sup id="ref_120_38_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_38_vei_1201665">38</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="परिवृक्ती">parivṛktī</a>,<sup id="ref_120_39_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_39_vei_1201665">39</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="वावाता">vāvātā</a>,<sup id="ref_120_40_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_40_vei_1201665">40</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पालागली">pālāgalī</a>.<sup id="ref_120_41_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_41_vei_1201665">41</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="महिषी">mahiṣī</a> appears to be the chief wife, being the first one married according to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_42_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_42_vei_1201665">42</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="परिवृक्ती">parivṛktī</a>, ‘the neglected,’ is explained by Weber<sup id="ref_120_43_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_43_vei_1201665">43</a></sup> and Pischel<sup id="ref_120_44_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_44_vei_1201665">44</a></sup> as one that has had no son. The <a class="vei_120a" href="वावाता">vāvātā</a> is ‘the favourite,’ while the <a class="vei_120a" href="पालागली">pālāgalī</a> is, according to Weber, the daughter of the last of the court officials. The names are curious, and not very intelligible, but the evidence points to the wife first wedded alone being a wife in the fullest sense. This view is supported by the fact emphasized by  Delbrück,<sup id="ref_120_45_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_45_vei_1201665">45</a></sup> that in the sacrifice the <a class="vei_120a" href="पत्नी">patnī</a> is usually mentioned in the singular, apparent exceptions being due to some mythological reason.<sup id="ref_120_46_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_46_vei_1201665">46</a></sup> Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_47_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_47_vei_1201665">47</a></sup> is of opinion that polygamy is dying out in the Rigvedic period, monogamy being developed from pologamy; Weber,<sup id="ref_120_48_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_48_vei_1201665">48</a></sup> however, thinks that polygamy is secondary, a view that is supported by more recent anthropology.<sup id="ref_120_49_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_49_vei_1201665">49</a></sup></p><p><i>Polyandry.</i>--On the other hand, polyandry is not Vedic.<sup id="ref_120_50_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_50_vei_1201665">50</a></sup> There is no passage containing any clear reference to such a custom. The most that can be said is that in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_51_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_51_vei_1201665">51</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_52_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_52_vei_1201665">52</a></sup> verses are occasionally found in which husbands are mentioned in relation to a single wife. It is difficult to be certain of the correct explanation of each separate instance of this mode of expression; but even if Weber's<sup id="ref_120_53_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_53_vei_1201665">53</a></sup> view, that the plural is here used <i>majestatis causā,</i> is not accepted, Delbrück's<sup id="ref_120_54_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_54_vei_1201665">54</a></sup> explanation by mythology is probably right. In other passages<sup id="ref_120_55_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_55_vei_1201665">55</a></sup> the plural is simply generic.</p><p><i>Marital Relations.</i>--Despite polygamy, however, there is ample evidence that the marriage tie was not, as Weber<sup id="ref_120_56_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_56_vei_1201665">56</a></sup> has suggested, lightly regarded as far as the fidelity of the wife was concerned. There is, however, little trace of the husband's being expected to be faithful as a matter of morality. Several  passages,<sup id="ref_120_57_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_57_vei_1201665">57</a></sup> indeed, forbid, with reference to ritual abstinence, intercourse with the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्त्री">strī</a></i> of another. This may imply that adultery on the husband's part was otherwise regarded as venial. But as the word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्त्री">strī</a></i> includes all the ‘womenfolk,’ daughters and slaves, as well as wife, the conclusion can hardly be drawn that intercourse with another man's ‘wife’ was normally regarded with indifference.<sup id="ref_120_58_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_58_vei_1201665">58</a></sup> The curious ritual of the Varuṇapraghāsās,<sup id="ref_120_59_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_59_vei_1201665">59</a></sup> in which the wife of the sacrificer is questioned as to her lovers, is shown by Delbrück<sup id="ref_120_60_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_60_vei_1201665">60</a></sup> to be a part of a rite meant to expiate unchastity on the part of a wife, not as a normal question for a sacrificer to put to his own wife. Again, Yājñavalkya's doctrine in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_61_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_61_vei_1201665">61</a></sup> which seems to assert that no one cares if a wife is unchaste (<i>paraḥ-puṃsā</i>) or not, really means that no one cares if the wife is away from the men who are sacrificing, as the wives of the gods are apart from them during the particular rite in question. Monogamy is also evidently approved,<sup id="ref_120_62_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_62_vei_1201665">62</a></sup> so that some higher idea of morality was in course of formation. On the other hand, no Vedic text gives us the rule well known to other Indo-Germanic<sup id="ref_120_63_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_63_vei_1201665">63</a></sup> peoples that the adulterer taken in the act can be killed with impunity, though the later legal literature has traces of this rule.<sup id="ref_120_64_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_64_vei_1201665">64</a></sup> There is also abundant evidence that the standard of ordinary sexual morality was not high.</p><p><i>Hetairai.</i>--In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_65_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_65_vei_1201665">65</a></sup> there are many references to illegitimate love and to the abandonment of the offspring of  such unions,<sup id="ref_120_66_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_66_vei_1201665">66</a></sup> especially in the case of a protégé of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, often mentioned as the <i>parāvṛkta</i> or <i><a class="vei_120a" href="परावृज्">parāvṛj</a>.</i><sup id="ref_120_67_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_67_vei_1201665">67</a></sup> The ‘son of a maiden’ (<i>kumārī-<a class="vei_120a" href="पुत्र">putra</a></i>) is already spoken of in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_68_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_68_vei_1201665">68</a></sup> Such a person appears with a metronymic in the <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> period:<sup id="ref_120_69_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_69_vei_1201665">69</a></sup> this custom may be the origin of metronymics such as those which make up a great part of the lists of teachers (Vaṃśas) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_70_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_70_vei_1201665">70</a></sup> The Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_71_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_71_vei_1201665">71</a></sup> refers to illicit unions of <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a>, both male and female, besides giving in its list of victims at the Puruṣamedha, or ‘human sacrifice,’ several whose designations apparently mean ‘courtesan’ (<i>atītvarī</i>)<sup id="ref_120_72_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_72_vei_1201665">72</a></sup> and ‘procuress of abortion’ (<i>atiṣkadvarī</i>),<sup id="ref_120_73_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_73_vei_1201665">73</a></sup> while the ‘dyeing woman’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="रजयित्री">rajayitrī</a></i>) is dedicated to sensuality.<sup id="ref_120_74_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_74_vei_1201665">74</a></sup> Pischel and Geldner also see many references to Hetairai in other passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_75_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_75_vei_1201665">75</a></sup> especially where mention is made of Uṣas, the goddess of Dawn, who in their view is the characteristic Hetaira. At any rate, there is little doubt that the ‘dancer’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="नृतू">nṛtū</a></i>) referred to in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_76_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_76_vei_1201665">76</a></sup> was a Hetaira. When women are referred to as going to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="समन">samana</a>,</b> or ‘place of meeting,’ Hetairai are probably also meant.<sup id="ref_120_77_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_77_vei_1201665">77</a></sup> Grave cases of immorality are alluded to in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_78_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_78_vei_1201665">78</a></sup> The love of father and daughter, as shown in the myth of Prajāpati,<sup id="ref_120_79_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_79_vei_1201665">79</a></sup> is evidently censured, but the actual existence of this form of incest is recognized in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_80_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_80_vei_1201665">80</a></sup> Girls who had lost their  natural protectors--father or brother<sup id="ref_120_81_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_81_vei_1201665">81</a></sup> --were apt to be reduced to live by immorality.</p><p><i>Forms of Marriage.</i>--The state of society revealed in the Vedic age seems to point to considerable freedom on the part of both man and woman in selecting a wife or a husband. At any rate, it is not clear that either the father or the mother controlled the marriage of son or daughter of mature age,<sup id="ref_120_82_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_82_vei_1201665">82</a></sup> though no doubt the parents or parent often arranged a suitable match.<sup id="ref_120_83_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_83_vei_1201665">83</a></sup> The marriage was frequently arranged through an intermediary, the ‘wooer’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वर">vara</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_84_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_84_vei_1201665">84</a></sup> presumably after those concerned had in effect come to an agreement. The sale of a daughter was not unknown,<sup id="ref_120_85_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_85_vei_1201665">85</a></sup> but a certain amount of discredit would seem to have attached to it,<sup id="ref_120_86_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_86_vei_1201665">86</a></sup> and sons-in-law in such cases were sometimes stingy. On the other hand, dowries were not infrequently given, especially no doubt when damsels Suffered from bodily defects.<sup id="ref_120_87_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_87_vei_1201665">87</a></sup> Occasionally marriages by  capture may have taken place, but only as knightly feats, as when <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विमद">vimada</a></b> carried off <b>Purumitra's</b> daughter against her father's wish, but very possibly with her own consent.<sup id="ref_120_88_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_88_vei_1201665">88</a></sup> The later law-books and the Epic describe in much detail various forms of marriage, but they all seem reducible to three types: (<i>a</i>) that which is based on mutual consent, the <i>prājāpatya</i> (‘connected with Prajāpati’); (<i>b</i>) that in which a price is paid for the bride, the <i>āsura</i> (‘Asura-like’), <i>ārṣa</i> (‘connected with the Ṛṣis’), <i>brāhma</i> (‘relating to Brahman’), or <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दैव">daiva</a></i> (‘divine’); (<i>c</i>) those which consist in stealing the bride, the <i>kṣātra</i> (‘warrior-like’) or the <i>rākṣasa</i> (‘demon-like’) mode, of all of which traces are found in Vedic literature.<sup id="ref_120_89_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_89_vei_1201665">89</a></sup> For instance, the gift of a maiden for services rendered or other object is exemplified in the story of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="च्यवन">cyavana</a></b> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_90_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_90_vei_1201665">90</a></sup> and in that of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्यावाश्व">śyāvāśva</a></b> in the Bṛhaddevatā.<sup id="ref_120_91_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_91_vei_1201665">91</a></sup></p><p><i>Wedding Ceremony.</i>--In normal marriages the bridal was celebrated by an elaborate ceremony which bears in essentials and details the strongest resemblance to the form observed by other Indo-Germanic as well as non-Indo-Germanic peoples,<sup id="ref_120_92_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_92_vei_1201665">92</a></sup> and which was destined to secure the stability and fruitfulness of the union. The ceremony commenced at the bride's house,<sup id="ref_120_93_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_93_vei_1201665">93</a></sup> to which the bridegroom with his friends and relations repaired, and in which he met the friends and relations of the bride.<sup id="ref_120_94_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_94_vei_1201665">94</a></sup> A cow or cows were slain for the entertainment of the guests.<sup id="ref_120_95_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_95_vei_1201665">95</a></sup> The bridegroom having caused the bride to mount a stone, formally grasped her hand, and led her round the household  fire.<sup id="ref_120_96_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_96_vei_1201665">96</a></sup> This act constituted the marriage, the husband hence being called ‘he who takes by the hand’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="हस्त">hasta</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राभ">grābha</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_97_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_97_vei_1201665">97</a></sup> The festivities being over,<sup id="ref_120_98_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_98_vei_1201665">98</a></sup> the bridegroom took the bride to his home on a car in a marriage procession,<sup id="ref_120_99_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_99_vei_1201665">99</a></sup> all to the accompaniment of suitable stanzas. Then followed cohabitation.<sup id="ref_120_100_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_100_vei_1201665">100</a></sup></p><p><i>Wife's Property and Status.</i>--We have very little information as to the legal relations of wife and husband after marriage. It may be assumed that the husband appropriated the wife's dowry, if any, as well as her earnings, if any: even in the Epic<sup id="ref_120_101_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_101_vei_1201665">101</a></sup> the rise of the recognition of women's property as their own (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्त्री">strī</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="धन">dhana</a></i>) is only slow. That the husband was absolute master of a wife as of a slave is not probable, though he doubtless exercised the same power of correction as was expressly allowed in the eighteenth century by English law. The poetical ideal<sup id="ref_120_102_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_102_vei_1201665">102</a></sup> of the family was decidedly high, and we have no reason to doubt that it was often actually fulfilled. Moreover, the wife on her marriage was at once given an honoured position in the house; she is emphatically mistress in her husband's home, exercising authority over her fatherin-law, her husband's brothers, and her unmarried sisters.<sup id="ref_120_103_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_103_vei_1201665">103</a></sup> No doubt the case contemplated is one in which the eldest son  of a family has become its head owing to the decrepitude<sup id="ref_120_104_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_104_vei_1201665">104</a></sup> of the parents, his wife then taking the place of the mistress of the joint family while the brothers and sisters are still unmarried. It is not inconsistent with the great stress elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_105_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_105_vei_1201665">105</a></sup> laid on the respect due to a father-in-law, who then is probably regarded as still in full possession of his faculties, and controls the house while his son continues to live with him. The respect would no doubt equally apply if the son had set up a separate family of his own.<sup id="ref_120_106_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_106_vei_1201665">106</a></sup></p><p>Moreover, the wife was a regular participator in the offerings of the husband. In this connexion the term <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पत्नी">patnī</a></b> regularly applies to her in the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_107_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_107_vei_1201665">107</a></sup> where <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जाया">jāyā</a></b> designates her in her conjugal capacity, not in that of sharer in the sacrifice. In this respect her position gradually deteriorated: thus the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_108_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_108_vei_1201665">108</a></sup> describes a certain ceremony in which the wife (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="जाया">jāyā</a></i>) alone offered the oblation in former times, while later a priest might do so instead. The same <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> shows other traces of a lowering in the position of women, probably due to the growing sense of the importance of ceremonial priority.<sup id="ref_120_109_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_109_vei_1201665">109</a></sup> So in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_110_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_110_vei_1201665">110</a></sup> women generally are classed with dice and drink as three chief evils, and woman is declared to be ‘untruth,’<sup id="ref_120_111_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_111_vei_1201665">111</a></sup> and connected with Nirṛti, ‘calamity.’<sup id="ref_120_112_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_112_vei_1201665">112</a></sup> A woman too, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_113_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_113_vei_1201665">113</a></sup> is inferior even to a bad man, and a sarcastic reference is made in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_114_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_114_vei_1201665">114</a></sup> to her power of  getting things from her husband by cajolery at night. On the other hand must be set the encomia on woman: a woman is half her husband,<sup id="ref_120_115_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_115_vei_1201665">115</a></sup> and completes him;<sup id="ref_120_116_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_116_vei_1201665">116</a></sup> and in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_117_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_117_vei_1201665">117</a></sup> attacks on women mingle with the general assumption of their good qualities. None the less, the Brāhmaṇas clearly indicate a gradual decline in their position, which is evident from the rule that requires the wife to eat after her husband.<sup id="ref_120_118_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_118_vei_1201665">118</a></sup> Scolds were also known: the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_119_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_119_vei_1201665">119</a></sup> praises the wife ‘who does not answer back’ (<i>aprativādinī</i>). Women bore no part in political life: men go to the assembly, not women, the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_120_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_120_vei_1201665">120</a></sup> expressly says. On the other hand, with the advance of education, women shared in the intellectual interests of the day, as is exemplified by Yājñavalkya's<sup id="ref_120_121_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_121_vei_1201665">121</a></sup> two wives, of whom one was interested in his philosophical discussions, the other not. Other women are also referred to in the Upaniṣads as teachers, but whether they were married is not certain.<sup id="ref_120_122_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_122_vei_1201665">122</a></sup></p><p>But the main object of a woman's marriage was the production of children, this being repeatedly asserted in the Rigveda and later.<sup id="ref_120_123_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_123_vei_1201665">123</a></sup> The desire for offspring, as was natural in a society which mainly counted relationship through the father, took the form of a wish for a son to perform the necessary funeral rites for the father, and to continue his line. It was no doubt possible to adopt a son, but in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_124_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_124_vei_1201665">124</a></sup> this custom is plainly viewed as unsatisfactory. The practice  is recognized,<sup id="ref_120_125_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_125_vei_1201665">125</a></sup> as we have seen above of Niyoga, in the appointment of a brother to beget children with the wife of a dead man, or perhaps of a man who is childless. ‘Sonlessness’ (<i>avīratā</i>) is placed on the same level as lack of property (<i>amati</i>), and <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> is besought to protect from it.<sup id="ref_120_126_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_126_vei_1201665">126</a></sup> The birth of a daughter was certainly not specially welcome: the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_127_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_127_vei_1201665">127</a></sup> in one hymn distinctly invokes the birth of a son, and deprecates that of a daughter, while the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_128_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_128_vei_1201665">128</a></sup> contains an old verse which says that a daughter is a misery (<i>kṛpaṇam</i>), while a son is a light in the highest heaven (<i>jyotir ha putraḥ parame vyoman</i>). But there is no proof that the Vedic Indians practised the exposure of female children. This conclusion, deduced from certain passages in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_129_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_129_vei_1201665">129</a></sup> by Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_130_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_130_vei_1201665">130</a></sup> and Delbrück,<sup id="ref_120_131_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_131_vei_1201665">131</a></sup> has been disproved by Bo7htlingk.</p><p><i>Child Life.</i>--No doubt the care of a child was left to the mother, but we learn little from the earlier literature<sup id="ref_120_132_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_132_vei_1201665">132</a></sup> of the life of the young. The length of the period of pregnancy is frequently placed at ten (doubtless lunar) months.<sup id="ref_120_133_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_133_vei_1201665">133</a></sup> On birth the child was first fed with milk or ghee, and then given the breast.<sup id="ref_120_134_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_134_vei_1201665">134</a></sup> On the eighth day after birth  the infant was washed.<sup id="ref_120_135_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_135_vei_1201665">135</a></sup> The cutting of the teeth was also a solemn occasion,<sup id="ref_120_136_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_136_vei_1201665">136</a></sup> and is the subject of a hymn in the Atharvaveda. Reference is also made to children's learning to speak, which the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_137_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_137_vei_1201665">137</a></sup> ascribes to the end of the first year of life. The <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_138_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_138_vei_1201665">138</a></sup> asserts that the words <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तत">tata</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तात">tāta</a>,</b> onomatopoetic words like ‘dada,’<sup id="ref_120_139_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_139_vei_1201665">139</a></sup> are the first words of a child's speech, giving therein perhaps an unfair prominence to the father. The Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_140_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_140_vei_1201665">140</a></sup> further contains at least one hymn for the ceremony of the first shaving of the young man's beard. The giving of a name was also an occasion of importance, a second one being often added.<sup id="ref_120_141_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_141_vei_1201665">141</a></sup></p><p><i>Satī.</i>--On the death of her husband, in some cases the widow burned herself or was burned by his relations.<sup id="ref_120_142_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_142_vei_1201665">142</a></sup> This is clearly implied in the reference to this ancient custom in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_143_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_143_vei_1201665">143</a></sup> On the other hand, the Rigveda does not contemplate the custom anywhere, but on the contrary considers the widow as married apparently to the brother of the dead man.<sup id="ref_120_144_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_144_vei_1201665">144</a></sup> The custom of Suttee would therefore appear buring the Vedic age to have been in abeyance, at least as a general rule. At all times the practice seems to have been mainly usual among families of the warrior class, to judge from the other Indo-Germanic parallels.<sup id="ref_120_145_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_145_vei_1201665">145</a></sup> In other classes  the survival of wives was more necessary, and the remarriage of widows, whether prohibited or allowed in the texts, is proof that there were widows who could be remarried.<sup id="ref_120_146_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_146_vei_1201665">146</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 117, 7; ii. 17, 7; x. 39, 3; 40, 5. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="घोषा">ghoṣā</a></b> is the chief example of this condition. The Atharvaveda (i. 14) also refers to such a case (see Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 253). The ornaments of maidens, especially at seasons of festival, are referred to in Rv. i. 123, 11; vii. 2, 5; Av. ii. 36, 1; xiv. 2, 59 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Av. iii. 18 (= Rv. x. 145); vi. 89; 102; 130; 131; vii. 36; 37; 38. Similarly there are many references to the love of the youth for the maiden, and his seeking her--<i>e.g.,</i> Rv. i. 115, 2; Av. ii. 30; iii. 25; vi. 8; 9; 82; to their mutual affection--<i>e.g.,</i> Rv. i. 167, 3; ix. 32, 5; 56, 3; x. 34, 5; and to jealousy and love philtres for the purpose of recalling wandering affections -<i>e.g.,</i> Av. vi. 18; 42; 43; 94; 139; vii. 45. The gifts of the lover are referred to in Rv. i. 117, 18. Some of these passages may, of course, refer to Hetairai, but not all.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201665">↑</a> vii. 55, 5. 8. <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 134, 3; Aufrecht, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 337 <i>et seq.</i> A different view of the passage is taken by Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 57 <i>et seq.</i> The Atharvaveda (iv. 5) shows that the view of Aufrecht was that early adopted in India.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 59; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 340 <i>et seq.;</i> 23, 356; Risley, <i>People of India,</i> 179 <i>et seq.</i> There is a possible reference to a child-wife in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 10, 1. For the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> evidence, see Bhandarkar, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenlāndischen Gesellschaft,</i> 47, 143-156; Jolly, <i>ibid.,</i> 46, 413-426; 47, 610-615.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201665">↑</a> Rv. x. 85, especially verse 29 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201665">↑</a> x. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Proceedings of the Berlin Academy,</i> 1895, 822. <i>Cf.</i> also <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 427; 10, 76, n.; Pischel, <i>Hermes,</i> 18, 465-468; Max Müller, <i>Science of Language,</i> 2, 507; Herodotus, iii. 19. Crawley's <i>Mystic Rose</i> gives strong reasons against the early prevalence of such marriages.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201665">↑</a> iii. 4, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201665">↑</a> Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 5, 15, 16, etc. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Śāstra, iii. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Śāstra, i. 52, 53.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201665">↑</a> i. 8, 3, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201665">↑</a> On Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 75, 76; Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 387; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 392; Geiger, <i>Ostiranische Kultur,</i> 246; <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 43, 308-312; Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 62, 63; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 345 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 51, 279.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201665">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 16, 2-5; <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 24; 25; Pāraskara <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 4, etc.; Risley, <i>People of India,</i> 156 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201665">↑</a> See Hopkins, cited in note 12; Bṛhaddevatā, v. 79; and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1201665">↑</a> See Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 578 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1201665">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 1, 9, and <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> and Kapiṣṭhala Saṃhitās, cited by Delbrück, 579, 580; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 9. In Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 5, 12, 22, the expression is <i>paryāhita.</i></li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1201665">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 1, 9, according to Delbrūck, 581. But, as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="परि">pari</a>-vividāna</i> follows, it seems very doubtful; the reading is probably wrong, especially in view of the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> and Kapiṣṭhala parallels, which have <i>agre-didhiṣau</i> and <i>agre-dadhiṣau.</i></li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1201665">↑</a> See passages cited in note 17; also Av. vi. 112, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 8, 11. Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ix. 12, 11, and <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 5, 12, 22, add <i>parivinna</i> to <i><a class="vei_120a" href="परिवित्त">parivitta</a>,</i> but probably the two words should be identical in sense.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1201665">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā (see note 17) has <i>agre-<a class="vei_120a" href="दिधिषु">didhiṣu</a>;</i> Kapiṣṭhala, <i>agre-dadhiṣu;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 8, 11, <i>agra-<a class="vei_120a" href="दिधिषु">didhiṣu</a>.</i> The <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Sūtras adopt <i>agre-<a class="vei_120a" href="दिधिषु">didhiṣu</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1201665">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā has <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दिधिषू-पति">didhiṣū-pati</a>;</i> Kapiṣṭhala, <i>dadhiṣū-<a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>;</i> and so the <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Sūtras. Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 9, has the corrupt <i><a class="vei_120a" href="एदिधिषुः-पति">edidhiṣuḥ-pati</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1201665">↑</a> x. 18, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 40, 708.</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 553. <i>Cf.</i> also Lanman, <i>Sanskrit Reader,</i> 385; for the other view, see Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 848; Roth, <i>Siebenzig Lieder,</i> 151, n.; Zimmer. <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 329.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1201665">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 2, 18. <i>Cf.</i> Lanman in Whitney, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 849.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_1201665">↑</a> x. 40, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 15, with Roth's note; Geldner, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>, Kommentar,</i> 160; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> v, 343, n.; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 355, n., 367; Jolly, <i>Ṛecht und Sitte,</i> 71; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 459; von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 429. The custom died out in later times, is seems.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_1201665">↑</a> ix. 5, 27. 28.</li><li id="fx_120_29_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 553-555. <i>Cf.</i> Jolly, <i>Rechtund Sitte,</i> 59; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 371, n.</li><li id="fx_120_30_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_30_vei_1201665">↑</a> This is certainly the case in Av. v. 17, 8, which, however, merely exalts the sanctity of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, and does not necessarily imply remarriage at all.</li><li id="fx_120_31_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_31_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 2, 3, 27.</li><li id="fx_120_32_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_32_vei_1201665">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvii. 19. 20. 72-74; <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 1, 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Śāstra, ix. 175. <i>Cf.</i> also Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 281; 5, 306.</li><li id="fx_120_33_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_33_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 27.</li><li id="fx_120_34_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_34_vei_1201665">↑</a> vi. 49, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Mahābhārata, iii. 70, 26.</li><li id="fx_120_35_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_35_vei_1201665">↑</a> Rv. i. 62, 11; 71, 1; 104, 3; 105, 8; 112, 19; 186, 7; vi. 53, 4; vii. 18, 2; 26, 3; x. 43, 1; 101, 11. <i>Cf.</i> Av. iii. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 5, 1, 4, etc. See Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 455 <i>et seq.;</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 387; Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 64; von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 430, 431; Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 539, 540; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 353; Bloomfield, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 561.</li><li id="fx_120_36_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_36_vei_1201665">↑</a> i. 5, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_37_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_37_vei_1201665">↑</a> ix. 1, 4, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_38_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_38_vei_1201665">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 9, 4, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 1, 4; vi. 5, 3, 1; vii. 5, 1, 1; xiii. 2, 6, 4; 4, 1, 8; 5, 2, 2. 5. 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xix. 1, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Rv. v. 2, 2; 37, 3; Av. ii. 36, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 9, 1; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 220.</li><li id="fx_120_39_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_39_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="परि-वृक्ता">pari-vṛktā</a></i> occurs in Rv. x. 102, 11; Av. vii. 113, 2; xx. 128, 10. 11; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 2, 6, 6; 4, 1, 8; 5, 2, 7; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="परिवृक्ती">parivṛktī</a></i> in <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 9, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7. 3, 4; iii. 9, 4, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, x. 10; xv. 4; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 1, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_40_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_40_vei_1201665">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 3, 3; iii. 9, 4, 4; Av. xx. 128, 10. 11; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 2, 6, 5; 4, 1, 8; 5, 2, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 308, n.; Bloomfield, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 553, 554.</li><li id="fx_120_41_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_41_vei_1201665">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 3, 3 <i>et seq.;</i> iii. 9, 4, 5; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 1, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 4, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_42_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_42_vei_1201665">↑</a> vi. 5, 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_43_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_43_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_44_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_44_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 199. <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>ibid.,</i> 2, 38.</li><li id="fx_120_45_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_45_vei_1201665">↑</a> {%{|Indogermanische|} Verwandtschaftsnamen,%} 539. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 325. <a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a> had, however, two apparently equal wives (Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 1, and <i>cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 10, 3).</li><li id="fx_120_46_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_46_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 6, 4; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_47_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_47_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 323.</li><li id="fx_120_48_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_48_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 222. Weber's theory that <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सपत्न">sapatna</a></i> cannot be derived from <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सपत्नी">sapatnī</a></i> is, however, quite untenable.</li><li id="fx_120_49_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_49_vei_1201665">↑</a> See, <i>e.g.,</i> Westermaarck, <i>Origin and Development of Marriage;</i> Crawley, <i>Mystic Rose.</i></li><li id="fx_120_50_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_50_vei_1201665">↑</a> Mayr, <i>Indisches Erbrecht,</i> Wien, 1873, contends in favour of its existence. But see Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 191, 207; 10, 83, 84; Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 48; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 354 <i>et seq.;</i> von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 431, n. 2; <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 44, 340342; Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 541-545.</li><li id="fx_120_51_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_51_vei_1201665">↑</a> x. 85, 37. 38.</li><li id="fx_120_52_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_52_vei_1201665">↑</a> Av. xiv. 1, 44. 52. 61; 2, 14. 27.</li><li id="fx_120_53_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_53_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 191. So Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 326, who, however, suggests that the plural is generic.</li><li id="fx_120_54_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_54_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 543.</li><li id="fx_120_55_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_55_vei_1201665">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 2, 14. <i>Cf.</i> the plural <i>śvaśurāḥ,</i> ‘fathers-in-law,’ in <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 12. The Niyoga has, of course, nothing to do with polyandry.</li><li id="fx_120_56_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_56_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 83. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 5, 573, and <i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_57_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_57_vei_1201665">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 6, 8, 3; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_58_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_58_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> above, p. 396.</li><li id="fx_120_59_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_59_vei_1201665">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 10, 11; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 5, 2, 20; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 5, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_60_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_60_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 550.</li><li id="fx_120_61_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_61_vei_1201665">↑</a> i. 3, 1, 21. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 76, n. 2; Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v. paraḥpuṃsā</i> (<i>cf.</i> above, p. 397). Delbrück, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 551, shows also that neither the Dīkṣā (‘consecration’) nor the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रवर">pravara</a> (‘invitation’ to <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>, as described by the names of the mythical ancestors of the invoker) gives any countenance to the theory of doubt as to the parentage of the Vedic Indians.</li><li id="fx_120_62_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_62_vei_1201665">↑</a> Rv. i. 124, 7; iv. 3, 2; x. 71, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_63_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_63_vei_1201665">↑</a> Leist, <i>Altarisches Jus Gentium,</i> 276 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 388, 389; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 366, 367.</li><li id="fx_120_64_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_64_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> above, p. 396.</li><li id="fx_120_65_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_65_vei_1201665">↑</a> Rv. i. 134, 3; iii. 53, 8; viii. 17, 7. <i><a class="vei_120a" href="महानग्नी">mahānagnī</a>,</i> Av. xiv. 1, 36; xx. 136, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 27, denotes a courtesan. <i>Cf.</i> Av. v. 7, 8. So also <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पुंश्चली">puṃścalī</a>,</i> Av. xv. 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 22; <i>puṃścalū,</i> Taittirīyā <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 15, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_66_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_66_vei_1201665">↑</a> Rv. ii. 29, 1 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="रह">raha</a>-sūḥ,</i> ‘one who bears in secret.’ <i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 26; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 333, 334).</li><li id="fx_120_67_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_67_vei_1201665">↑</a> Rv. ii. 13, 12; 15, 7; iv. 19, 9; 30, 16; Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 335. The child, when exposed, was in danger of being consumed by ants <i>(vamrī). Cf.</i> below, p. 493.</li><li id="fx_120_68_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_68_vei_1201665">↑</a> xxx. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_69_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_69_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जाबाल">jābāla</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सत्यकाम">satyakāma</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_70_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_70_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Pāṇini, iv. 1, 116. But the custom may be due simply to polygamy (Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> p. 244, n. 2).</li><li id="fx_120_71_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_71_vei_1201665">↑</a> xxiii. 30. 31: <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 4, 19, 2. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_72_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_72_vei_1201665">↑</a> xxx. 15.</li><li id="fx_120_73_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_73_vei_1201665">↑</a> xxx. 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 11, 1, has <i>apaskadvarī.</i></li><li id="fx_120_74_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_74_vei_1201665">↑</a> xxx. 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 7, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_75_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_75_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf. Vedische Studien,</i> 1, xxv, 196, 275, 299, 309; 2, 120, 154, 179, etc.; Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 48.</li><li id="fx_120_76_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_76_vei_1201665">↑</a> i. 92, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_77_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_77_vei_1201665">↑</a> Rv. iv. 58, 8; vi. 75, 4; x. 168, 2. Perhaps also <i>vrā</i> in i. 124, 8; 126, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_78_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_78_vei_1201665">↑</a> x. 162, 5 (brother and sister: <i>cf.</i> above, p. 397).</li><li id="fx_120_79_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_79_vei_1201665">↑</a> Rv. x. 61, 5-7; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 2, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 33; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 4, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_80_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_80_vei_1201665">↑</a> viii. 6, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_81_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_81_vei_1201665">↑</a> Rv. i. 124, 7. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुत्रिका">putrikā</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_82_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_82_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 574. Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 309, asserts that the consent of parent or brother was needed, but no clear evidence of this can be adduced. The later custom is not conclusive, since it is bound up with the usage of child marriage, which deprived both son and daughter of anyfree choice. <i>Cf. ibid.,</i> 315; Kaegi, <i>Der Rigveda,</i> 15.</li><li id="fx_120_83_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_83_vei_1201665">↑</a> This is so natural as not to need express evidence. <i>Cf., e.g.,</i> the marriage proposals of <a class="vei_120a" href="श्यावाश्व">śyāvāśva</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="आत्रेय">ātreya</a>, as detailed in the Bṛhaddevatā, v. 49 <i>et seq.;</i> Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 51 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_84_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_84_vei_1201665">↑</a> Rv. x. 78, 4; 85, 15. 23. Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 310, exalts this into a universal practice, and compares the use of <i>aryaman,</i> ‘friend,’ as ‘bride-wooer.’ In Śyāvāśva's <b>case,</b> his father acted for him.</li><li id="fx_120_85_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_85_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 10, 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 4, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 2, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xx. vi. 5. See also <a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Śāstra, iii. 53; viii. 204; ix. 98; Megasthenes in McCrindle's translation, p. 70; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 407; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 345 <i>et seq.;</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 381; Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 78 <i>et seq.;</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 86, n.; Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 52.</li><li id="fx_120_86_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_86_vei_1201665">↑</a> Rv. i. 109, 2, refers to the gods <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> as more generous than a <i><a class="vei_120a" href="विजामातृ">vijāmātṛ</a>,</i> ‘son-in-law,’ or a <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्याल">syāla</a>,</i> ‘brother-in-law.’ The force of <i>vi</i> in the former word must be unfavourable, and the sense, as indicated by Pischel, is no doubt, that a son-in-law who was not in other respects altogether suitable might have to buy his bride at a heavy cost. The <i><a class="vei_120a" href="विजामातृ">vijāmātṛ</a></i> is, in fact, the <i>aśrīro jāmātā,</i> the ‘ignoble son-in-law,’ of Rv. viii. 2, 20. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 9; Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 255.</li><li id="fx_120_87_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_87_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. vi. 28, 5; x. 27, 12; Av. v. 17, 12. Possibly in Rv. i. 109, 2, there is a reference to a generous brother giving his sister a dowry in order to get her a husband. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 345; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 459; Kaegi, <i>Der Rigveda,</i> n. 352; Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 310, n. It is doubtful whether <i>anudeyī</i> in Rv. x. 85, 6, means ‘dowry’ or not. See Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 741.</li><li id="fx_120_88_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_88_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 112, 19; 116, 1; 117, 20; x. 39, 7; 65, 12. Sāyaṇa's view that <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कमद्यू">kamadyū</a></b> was daughter of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुमित्र">purumitra</a></b> seems certain, though Zimmer, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> is doubtful.</li><li id="fx_120_89_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_89_vei_1201665">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 361, 362; Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 50 <i>et seq.;</i> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 29; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 383.</li><li id="fx_120_90_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_90_vei_1201665">↑</a> iii. 122.</li><li id="fx_120_91_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_91_vei_1201665">↑</a> v. 49 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_92_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_92_vei_1201665">↑</a> The older ritual is described with considerable detail in Rv. x. 85 and Av. xiv. 1 and 2. The later ritual, as elaborately traced in the <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> Sūtras, is set out by Weber and Haas, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 177-411. See also Leist, <i>Altarisches Jus Gentium,</i> 144 <i>et seq.;</i> von Schroeder, <i>Die Hochzeitsgebräuche der Esten,</i> Berlin, 1888; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 384 <i>et seq.;</i> Hopkins, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 13, 355 <i>et seq.;</i> Winternitz, <i>Das altindische Hochzeitsrituell,</i> 1892; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 739 <i>et seq.;</i> Lanman, <i>Sanskrit Reader,</i> 389 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_93_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_93_vei_1201665">↑</a> x. 17, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_94_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_94_vei_1201665">↑</a> Rv. iv. 58, 9; Av. vi. 60; xiv. 2, 59.</li><li id="fx_120_95_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_95_vei_1201665">↑</a> Rv. x. 85, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_96_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_96_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. x. 85, 36. 38; Av. xiv. 1, 47. 48. Before the bride mounted the stone, the groom repeated, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> Sūtras (Āśvalāyana, i. 7. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a>, i. 13, 4; Pāraskara, i. 6, 3, etc.), the words, ‘I am he, thou art she; I the <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a>, thou the Ṛc; I the heaven, thou the earth; here will we unite ourselves and produce offspring,’ for which see Av. xiv. 2, 71; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxv. 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 27; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 19 (Mādhyaṃdina).</li><li id="fx_120_97_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_97_vei_1201665">↑</a> x. 18, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Av. xiv. 1, 51.</li><li id="fx_120_98_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_98_vei_1201665">↑</a> Av. xiv. 2, 59 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_99_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_99_vei_1201665">↑</a> Rv. x. 85, 7. 8. 10. 24, 25. 26. 27. 42 <i>et seq.;</i> Av. xiv. 1, 60.</li><li id="fx_120_100_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_100_vei_1201665">↑</a> See for the purification of the bride's garment, Rv. x. 85. 28-30. 35.</li><li id="fx_120_101_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_101_vei_1201665">↑</a> ‘They own neither themselves nor an inheritance’ (<i>nātmanaś caneśate na dāyasyed</i>), says the Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 4, 2, 13. <i>Cf.</i> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 6, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 5, 8, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 4. <i>Cf.</i> for the Epic, Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 368. For compulsory obedience of the wife, <i>cf.</i> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 7. In the same Upanīṣad <a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a>, on retiring from the ordinary life, divides his goods between his two wives.</li><li id="fx_120_102_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_102_vei_1201665">↑</a> Rv. viii. 31, 5-9; x. 34, 11; 85, 18. 19. 42 <i>et seq.;</i> Av. iii. 30; xiv. 2, 32.</li><li id="fx_120_103_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_103_vei_1201665">↑</a> Rv. x. 85, 46. <i>Cf.</i> as regards the bridegroom's sisters, <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 37. In Av. xiv. 2, 26, the daughter-in-law is to be ‘wealful’ (<i>śambhūḥ</i>) to her father-in-law, and ‘pleasant’ (<i>syonā</i>) to her mother-in-law, which is correct on either theory of her position as a daughter or a mistress.</li><li id="fx_120_104_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_104_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 70, 5, where an old fatḥer's goods are divided by his sons, and Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 327. <i>Cf.</i> also the possible case of a father who recovers after giving over all his goods to his son, <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 15.</li><li id="fx_120_105_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_105_vei_1201665">↑</a> Av. viii. 6, 24; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 4, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 12 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 260); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 6, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 22; Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 514, 515.</li><li id="fx_120_106_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_106_vei_1201665">↑</a> No doubt it might also apply even if the father-in-law were decrepit; but it is hardly likely that, in these circumstances, the strong sense of respect evident in Av. viii. 6, 24, which implies fear, would have developed.</li><li id="fx_120_107_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_107_vei_1201665">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 9, 2, 14; Pāṇini, iv. 1, 33; Delbrück, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 510, 512.</li><li id="fx_120_108_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_108_vei_1201665">↑</a> i. 1, 4, 13. For the older practice, <i>cf.</i> Rv. i. 122, 2; iii. 53, 4-6; viii. 31, 5 <i>et seq.;</i> x. 86, 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_109_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_109_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> i. 3, 1, 9. 12. 13. <i>Cf.</i> Lévi, <i>La doctrine du sacrifice,</i> 157, 158.</li><li id="fx_120_110_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_110_vei_1201665">↑</a> iii. 6, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_111_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_111_vei_1201665">↑</a> i. 10, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_112_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_112_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Ibid.</i></li><li id="fx_120_113_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_113_vei_1201665">↑</a> vi. 5, 8, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 1, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_114_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_114_vei_1201665">↑</a> xxxi. 1. <i>Cf.</i> Aitareva Brāhmana, iii. 22.</li><li id="fx_120_115_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_115_vei_1201665">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 2, 1, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_116_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_116_vei_1201665">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 4, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_117_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_117_vei_1201665">↑</a> In viii. 33, 17, <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> is credited with a poor opinion of woman's intelligence, and <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरूरवस्">purūravas</a> in x. 95, 15, frankly calls them hyenas. They are defended in v. 61, 6-8, but only against mean men (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="पणि">paṇi</a></b>). <i>Cf.</i> Kaegi, <i>Der Rigveda,</i> n. 351.</li><li id="fx_120_118_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_118_vei_1201665">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 9, 2, 12; x. 5, 2, 9. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="वासिष्ठ">vāsiṣṭha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 1, 2, 2; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 330, n.; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 365, n.</li><li id="fx_120_119_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_119_vei_1201665">↑</a> iii. 24, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 3, 22; Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 19, 14, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_120_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_120_vei_1201665">↑</a> iv. 7, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Av. vii. 38, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_121_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_121_vei_1201665">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 4, 1; iv. 5, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_122_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_122_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> the epithet <i>gandharva-gṛhitā,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 29; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 9; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 3, 1; 7, 1; and see Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>. iii. 4, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_123_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_123_vei_1201665">↑</a> Rv. i. 91, 20; 92, 13; iii. 1, 23; x. 85, 25. 41. 42. 45; Av. iii. 23, 2; v. 25, 11; vi. 11, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_124_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_124_vei_1201665">↑</a> vii. 4, 7. 8. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_125_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_125_vei_1201665">↑</a> x. 18, 8; 40, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_126_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_126_vei_1201665">↑</a> Rv. iii. 16, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_127_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_127_vei_1201665">↑</a> vi. 11, 3. <i>Cf.</i> viii. 6, 25.</li><li id="fx_120_128_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_128_vei_1201665">↑</a> vii. 15. <i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 409.</li><li id="fx_120_129_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_129_vei_1201665">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 5, 10, 3; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 6, 4; 7, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxvii. 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 17, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_130_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_130_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 319. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 314, n., who cites <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 8, 8, as evidence of the exposure of two boys, but the sense is doubtful.</li><li id="fx_120_131_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_131_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 575. See also Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 54, 210; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 6, 142; Kaegi, <i>Der Rigveda,</i> n. 49; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 389, 390. Bo7htlingk's view is given in <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 44, 494-496, and <i>cf.</i> Pischel, <i>Vedisehe Studien,</i> 2, 48, who compares iv. 18, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_132_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_132_vei_1201665">↑</a> The later literature is full of details of the ceremonies before and after birth (see Delbrück, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 573 <i>et seq.</i>). Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 314, n., gives the Vedic embryology; twins were disliked, <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_133_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_133_vei_1201665">↑</a> Rv. v. 78, 9; x. 184, 3; Av. i. 11, 6; iii. 23, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 13, 9; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 5. 2, 4; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 9. 1; Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 314, n. There are in the Av. many spells concerned with birth (i. 11, etc.), and miscarriages are mentioned (<i>avatokā, avasū,</i> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 15; Av. viii. 6, 9, etc.).</li><li id="fx_120_134_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_134_vei_1201665">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 3, 4 (Mādhyaṃdina = i. 5, 2 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>). <i>Cf.</i> also vi. 4, 24 <i>et seq.;</i> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 5, 1, 6. After being weaned the child is <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अति">ati</a>-stana</i> (Kauṣitaki <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 2).</li><li id="fx_120_135_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_135_vei_1201665">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 7, 2 (on <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>, ii. 525 = Rv. ix. 96, 17). The first ten days were the dangerous period (<a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 14; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxii. 14, 3).</li><li id="fx_120_136_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_136_vei_1201665">↑</a> Av. vi. 140.</li><li id="fx_120_137_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_137_vei_1201665">↑</a> vi. 1, 6, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 4, 2, 38; xi. 1, 6, 3 5.</li><li id="fx_120_138_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_138_vei_1201665">↑</a> i. 3, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_139_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_139_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 449, 596.</li><li id="fx_120_140_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_140_vei_1201665">↑</a> vi. 68, <i>Cf.</i> ii. 13, according to <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, 53. 54, and <i>cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 4, 1, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_141_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_141_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 3, 3, with Keith's note; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 1, 3, 9, and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नामन्">nāman</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_142_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_142_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 391; von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 41; Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 67-69; Weber, <i>Proceedings of the Berlin Academy,</i> 1896, 254 <i>et seq.;</i> Roth, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 8, 468; Wilson, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 16, 202; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 329; Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Kommentar,</i> 154.</li><li id="fx_120_143_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_143_vei_1201665">↑</a> Av. xviii. 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_144_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_144_vei_1201665">↑</a> x. 18, 7. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_145_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_145_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Herodotos, v. 5 (of the Thracians); iv. 71 (of the Scythians); Procopius, <i>De Bello Gothico,</i> ii. 14 (of the Heruli). So in Germany Brynhild and Nanna are instances (<i>cf.</i> Weinhold, <i>Altnordisches Leben,</i> 476 <i>et seq.</i>). The universality of the custom must not be exaggerated, as Zimmer, 331, is inclined to do. To burn all the wives of a king would, in primitive ages, have been a wasteful action; even the chief wife would often have had to be spared on one ground or another. The Rigveda already reveals a state of society in which the actual burning of the wife was avoided by a semblance of it in the funeral ritual of (<i>cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 126). The reward of a good wife was to go to the world of her husband (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="लोक">loka</a></i>) after death (<i>cf.</i> Av. xiv. 1, 64; xviii. 3, 1; Rv. x. 85, 43). A Vedic citation in the scholiast on Pāṇini, iii. 2, 8, Vārttika, 2, says that a Brahmin woman who drinks <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुरा">surā</a>,</b> an intoxicating liquor, does not go to the world of her husband after death.</li><li id="fx_120_146_vei_1201665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_146_vei_1201665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> perhaps the <i>gartāruh</i> of Rv. i. 124, 7, as explained by <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 5; Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Kommentar,</i> 22.</li></ol></div>

पत्ति|పత్తి|ಪತ್ತಿ|patti|_vei_120@1666|_vei1_pn489
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1665">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1667">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn474">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn489">1:489</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn490">↷</a><hr><p><b>Patti</b> is used in the Atharvaveda (vii. 62, 1) to designate the ‘foot soldier’ in war as opposed to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथिन्">rathin</a>,</b> ‘charioteer,’ the latter defeating (<i>ji</i>) the former. One of the epithets of <a class="vei_120a" href="रुद्र">rudra</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शतरुद्रिय">śatarudriya</a> liturgy of the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā (xvi. 19) is ‘lord of footmen’ (<i>pattīnāṃ <a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a></i>).</p></div>

पत्नी|पत्नि|పత్నీ|పత్ని|ಪತ್ನೀ|ಪತ್ನಿ|patnī|patni|patnii|patnee|_vei_120@1667|_vei1_pn489
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1666">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1668">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn474">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn489">1:489</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn490">↷</a><hr><p><b>Patnī.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>.</b> A part of the house is the <i>Patnīnāṃ <a class="vei_120a" href="सदन">sadana</a>,</i> mentioned in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201667"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201667">1</a></sup> presumably the women's quarters. The phrase is borrowed from the <i>Patnīśāla,</i> ‘hut for the wife,’ of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201667"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201667">2</a></sup> ritual.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201667"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201667">↑</a> ix. 3, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201667"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201667">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 18; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 6, 9, 8; x. 2, 3, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 22 (<i>-<a class="vei_120a" href="शाला">śālā</a></i>); <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xix. 6, etc.</li></ol></div>

पथिन् सौभर|పథిన్ సౌభర|ಪಥಿನ್ ಸೌಭರ|pathin saubhara|_vei_120@1668|_vei1_pn489
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1667">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1669">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn474">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn489">1:489</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn490">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pathin Saubhara</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सोभरि">sobhari</a></b>’) is mentioned in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201668"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201668">1</a></sup> as the pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अयास्य आंगिरस">ayāsya āṅgirasa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201668"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201668">↑</a> ii. 5, 22 (Mādhyaṃdina = ii. 6, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>); iv. 5, 28 (Mādhyaṃdina = iv. 6, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

पथि-कृत्|पथिकृत्|पथि|कृत्|పథి-కృత్|పథికృత్|పథి|కృత్|ಪಥಿ-ಕೃತ್|ಪಥಿಕೃತ್|ಪಥಿ|ಕೃತ್|pathi-kṛt|pathikṛt|pathi|kṛt|pathi-kRt|pathikRt|kRt|pathi-kRit|pathikRit|kRit|pathi-kRut|pathikRut|kRut|pathi-kRRit|pathikRRit|kRRit|_vei_120@1669|_vei1_pn489
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1668">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1670">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn474">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn489">1:489</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn490">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pathi-kṛt,</b> ‘path-maker,’ is not a rare epithet in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201669">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201669">2</a></sup> showing clearly the importance naturally attached in primitive times to the finding of roads. The frequency  with which the epithet is applied to <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201669">3</a></sup> suggests that there is here an allusion to fire burning the primæval forest and rendering advance possible. The god Pūṣan is <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पथि-कृत्">pathi-kṛt</a>,</i> as guarding the flocks.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201669">4</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषिस्">ṛṣis</a>,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201669">5</a></sup> or seers, as ‘path-makers,’ may be compared with the Roman Pontifices.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201669">↑</a> ii. 23, 6; vi. 21, 12; ix. 106, 5; x. 14, 15; 111, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201669">↑</a> Av. xviii. 2, 53; 3, 25, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201669">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 1, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 1, 5, 5; xii. 4, 4, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201669">↑</a> Sāṅkhāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 4, 9; xvi. 1, 17. The <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 1, 18, explains <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पथि-कृत्">pathi-kṛt</a></i> merely as <i>adhipati,</i> ‘lord’; but the sense must be more pregnant than that.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201669">↑</a> Rv. x. 14, 15, where the expression refers to their finding the way to the heavenly world; but it is probably a transfer of an epithet of terrestrial application.</li></ol></div>

पद्|పద్|ಪದ್|pad|_vei_120@1670|_vei1_pn490
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1669">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1671">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn489">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn490">1:490</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn491">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pad</b> in the Atharvaveda (xix. 6, 2) and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xi. 3, 2, 3) denotes a ‘quarter.’ This sense is derived from the primary meaning of ‘foot,’ which as applied to quadrupeds would represent ‘one-fourth.’ <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पाद">pāda</a>.</b></p></div>

पद|పద|ಪದ|pada|_vei_120@1671|_vei1_pn490
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1670">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1672">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn489">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn490">1:490</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn491">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pada</b> in the sense of ‘quarter of a stanza’ is found as early as the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201671"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201671">1</a></sup> and often later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201671"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201671">2</a></sup> In the Brāhmaṇas it also denotes a ‘word’ as opposed to a ‘letter’ (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201671"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201671">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201671"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201671">↑</a> i. 164, 24. 45.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201671"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201671">↑</a> Av. ix. 10, 19; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 25; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6. 10. 17, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxii. 1. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201671"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201671">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxvi. 5, where the sequence is half-stanza (<i>ardharca</i>), quarter-stanza (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पाद">pāda</a></i>), word (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पद">pada</a></i>), and letter <i>(<a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>). Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 2, 6, 13; xi. 5, 6, 9, etc.</li></ol></div>

पदि|పది|ಪದಿ|padi|_vei_120@1672|_vei1_pn490
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1671">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1673">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn489">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn490">1:490</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn491">↷</a><hr><p><b>Padi</b> is found once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201672">1</a></sup> where, according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, the word probably designates some kind of animal. <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201672">2</a></sup> explains it as equivalent to <i>gantu,</i> ‘a moving creature,’ but <a class="vei_120a" href="दुर्ग">durga</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201672">3</a></sup> as meaning ‘bird.’ The passage may refer to catching the <a class="vei_120a" href="पदि">padi</a> in a net (<i>? <a class="vei_120a" href="मुक्षीजा">mukṣījā</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201672">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201672">↑</a> i. 125, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201672">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201672">↑</a> In his commentary on <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201672">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 129; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 244.</li></ol></div>

पयस्|पय|పయస్|పయ|ಪಯಸ್|ಪಯ|payas|paya|_vei_120@1673|_vei1_pn490
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1672">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1674">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn489">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn490">1:490</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn491">↷</a><hr><p><b>Payas</b> denotes the ‘milk’ of the cow in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201673">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201673">2</a></sup> More generally it has also the sense of ‘sap’ or ‘fluid’  found in plants,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201673">3</a></sup> and giving them life and strength. In other passages it denotes the ‘water’ of heaven.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201673">4</a></sup> A vow to live for a time on milk alone occurs in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201673">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201673">↑</a> i. 164, 28; ii. 14, 10; iv. 3, 9; v. 85, 2; x. 30, 13; 63, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201673">↑</a> Av. iv. 11, 4; xii. 1, 10; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iv. 3. <i>Cf.</i> <b>Go</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षीर">kṣīra</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201673">↑</a> Av. iii. 5, 1; x. 1, 12; xiii. 1, 9; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvii. 1; xviii. 36, etc. So of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>, Rv. ix. 97, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201673">↑</a> Rv. i. 64, 5; 166, 3; iii. 33, 1. 4; iv. 57, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201673">↑</a> <i>Payo-<a class="vei_120a" href="व्रत">vrata</a>,</i> ‘one who undergoes a vow (to subsist) on (nothing but) milk,’ ix. 5, 1, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 9. The Dīkṣita subsists on it alone.</li></ol></div>

पयस्या|పయస్యా|ಪಯಸ್ಯಾ|payasyā|payasyaa|_vei_120@1674|_vei1_pn491
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1673">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1675">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn490">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn491">1:491</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn492">↷</a><hr><p><b>Payasyā</b> in the later Saṃhitās and Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201674">1</a></sup> denotes curds, said to consist of a mixture of sour milk and hot or cold fresh milk.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201674">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201674">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 13, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 11, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 22. 24; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 4, 10. 21; 5, 1, 12; 2, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201674">↑</a> See Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 381, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

पर आट्णार|పర ఆట్ణార|ಪರ ಆಟ್ಣಾರ|para āṭṇāra|para aaTNaara|_vei_120@1675|_vei1_pn491
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1674">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1676">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn490">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn491">1:491</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn492">↷</a><hr><p><b>Para Āṭṇāra</b> (‘descendant of Aṭṇāra’) appears in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201675">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201675">2</a></sup> as one of the ancient great kings who won sons by performing a particular sacrifice. In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201675">3</a></sup> he is styled <a class="vei_120a" href="हैरण्यनाभ">hairaṇyanābha</a>, ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्यनाभ">hiraṇyanābha</a>,’ and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201675">4</a></sup> he is called Para Āhlāra <a class="vei_120a" href="वैदेह">vaideha</a>, a fact testifying to the close connexion of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>.</b> A <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यज्ञ-गाथा">yajña-gāthā</a>,</b> or ‘sacrificial verse,’ there<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201675">5</a></sup> cited mentions <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्यनाभ">hiraṇyanābha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौसल्य">kausalya</a></b> in connexion with Para.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201675">↑</a> Taittīrīya Saṃhitā, v. 6, 5, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxii. 3 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 473).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201675">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 16, 3; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201675">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201675">↑</a> xvi. 9, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201675">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 13. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 7; <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 7; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 165, n. 4.</li></ol></div>

परम-ज्या|परमज्या|परम|ज्या|పరమ-జ్యా|పరమజ్యా|పరమ|జ్యా|ಪರಮ-ಜ್ಯಾ|ಪರಮಜ್ಯಾ|ಪರಮ|ಜ್ಯಾ|parama-jyā|paramajyā|parama|jyā|parama-jyaa|paramajyaa|jyaa|_vei_120@1676|_vei1_pn491
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1675">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1677">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn490">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn491">1:491</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn492">↷</a><hr><p><b>Parama-jyā,</b> ‘of supreme power,’ is understood by Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201676"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201676">1</a></sup> in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201676"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201676">2</a></sup> as the proper name of a great man among the <b>Yadus.</b> But it is doubtful whether the word is more than an epithet.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201676"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201676">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201676"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201676">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 159.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201676"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201676">↑</a> viii. 1, 30.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201676"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201676">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 39.</li></ol></div>

परशु|పరశు|ಪರಶು|paraśu|parashu|_vei_120@1677|_vei1_pn492
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1676">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1678">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn491">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn492">1:492</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn493">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paraśu</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201677">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201677">2</a></sup> denotes the axe of the woodcutter. Of its form we know nothing. A red-hot axe was used in a form of ordeal (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="दिव्य">divya</a></b>) applied in accusations of theft.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201677">3</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्शु">parśu</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201677">↑</a> i. 127, 3; vii. 104, 21; x. 28, 8; 53, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201677">↑</a> Av. iii. 19, 4; vii. 28, 1; xi. 9, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 10; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 6, 4, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 35; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 11, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201677">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 16, 1.</li></ol></div>

परश्वन्|పరశ్వన్|ಪರಶ್ವನ್|paraśvan|parashvan|_vei_120@1678|_vei1_pn492
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1677">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1679">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn491">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn492">1:492</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn493">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paraśvan.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परस्वन्त्">parasvant</a>.</b></p></div>

परस्वन्त्|పరస్వన్త్|ಪರಸ್ವನ್ತ್|parasvant|_vei_120@1679|_vei1_pn492
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1678">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1680">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn491">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn492">1:492</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn493">↷</a><hr><p><b>Parasvant</b> denotes a large wild animal which Roth<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201679"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201679">1</a></sup> conjectures to be the wild ass. It is mentioned in the Vṛṣākapi hymn<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201679"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201679">2</a></sup> of the Rigveda, twice in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201679"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201679">3</a></sup> and in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201679"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201679">4</a></sup> in all of which passages the sense of ‘wild ass’ is satisfactory. More doubtful is the meaning of the word <i>paraśvā(n)</i> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201679"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201679">5</a></sup> where the commentary explains it as ‘serpent.’ It is, of course, quite possible that the word has nothing to do with <i><a class="vei_120a" href="परस्वन्त्">parasvant</a>.</i> Bühler<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201679"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201679">6</a></sup> suggests connexion with the Pāli <i>palāsāda,</i> ‘rhinoceros.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201679"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201679">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201679"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201679">↑</a> x. 86, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201679"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201679">↑</a> vi. 72, 2; xx. 131, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201679"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201679">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 10; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 21, 1, where the commentator takes it to be the wild buffalo.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201679"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201679">↑</a> i. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201679"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201679">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 63; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 17, n. 1; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 377, n. 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 2, 633; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 86, 87; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 335; Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 105.</li></ol></div>

परा-वृज्|परावृज्|परा|वृज्|పరా-వృజ్|పరావృజ్|పరా|వృజ్|ಪರಾ-ವೃಜ್|ಪರಾವೃಜ್|ಪರಾ|ವೃಜ್|parā-vṛj|parāvṛj|parā|vṛj|paraa-vRj|paraavRj|paraa|vRj|paraa-vRij|paraavRij|vRij|paraa-vRuj|paraavRuj|vRuj|paraa-vRRij|paraavRRij|vRRij|_vei_120@1680|_vei1_pn492
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1679">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1681">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn491">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn492">1:492</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn493">↷</a><hr><p><b>Parā-vṛj</b> is a term found in four passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201680"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201680">1</a></sup> in all of which it refers to a person in a forlorn condition, while one<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201680"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201680">2</a></sup> of them also speaks of him as going south. Sāyaṇa's<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201680"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201680">3</a></sup> view that the word is a proper name is most unlikely, while Grassmann's<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201680"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201680">4</a></sup> explanation of it as ‘cripple’ is still less probable.  Roth's<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201680"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201680">5</a></sup> interpretation of it as ‘exile’ seems clearly right in the passage which refers to the <a class="vei_120a" href="परावृज्">parāvṛj</a> as going south. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201680"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201680">6</a></sup> accepts Roth's view for this passage, but in the others sees a reference to the child of a maiden exposed by her and in danger of being eaten by insects (<i>vamrī</i>). This view is supported by the fact that <i>parāvṛkta</i> seems to have the same sense,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201680"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201680">7</a></sup> and is accepted by Oldenberg.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201680"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201680">8</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201680"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201680">↑</a> i. 112, 8; ii. 13, 12; 15, 7; x. 61, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201680"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201680">↑</a> x. 61, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201680"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201680">↑</a> On i. 112, 8, etc., <i>of.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> 152.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201680"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201680">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 1, 23, and <i>cf. Wo7rterbuch, s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201680"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201680">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201680"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201680">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 185, 334, 335.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201680"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201680">↑</a> Rv. iv. 30, 19. <i>Cf.</i> iv. 30, 16; 19, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201680"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201680">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 200. <i>Cf.</i> above, p. 481, n. 67.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 248; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 402.</li></ol></div>

पराशर|పరాశర|ಪರಾಶರ|parāśara|paraashara|_vei_120@1681|_vei1_pn493
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1680">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1682">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn492">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn493">1:493</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn494">↷</a><hr><p><b>Parāśara</b> is mentioned with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शतयातु">śatayātu</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a></b> in the Rigvedic hymn celebrating <b>Sudās'</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201681"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201681">1</a></sup> victory over the ten kings. According to the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201681"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201681">2</a></sup> he was a son of <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>, but the Epic version makes him a son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शक्ति">śakti</a></b> and grandson of <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>. Geldner<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201681"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201681">3</a></sup> thinks that he is mentioned in the Rigveda along with <a class="vei_120a" href="शतयातु">śatayātu</a>, perhaps his uncle, and his grandfather <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>, as the three sages who approached <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> and won his favour for <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>. He is erroneously credited with the authorship of certain hymns of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201681"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201681">4</a></sup> by the Anukramaṇī (Index).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201681"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201681">↑</a> vii. 18, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201681"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201681">↑</a> vi. 30.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201681"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201681">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 132.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201681"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201681">↑</a> i. 65-73.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 110, 111; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 324. The Parāśaras as a school appear in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Anukramaṇī (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 460).</li></ol></div>

परि-क्षित्|परिक्षित्|परि|क्षित्|పరి-క్షిత్|పరిక్షిత్|పరి|క్షిత్|ಪರಿ-ಕ್ಷಿತ್|ಪರಿಕ್ಷಿತ್|ಪರಿ|ಕ್ಷಿತ್|pari-kṣit|parikṣit|pari|kṣit|pari-kShit|parikShit|kShit|pari-xit|parixit|xit|_vei_120@1682|_vei1_pn493
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1681">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1683">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn492">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn493">1:493</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn494">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-kṣit</b> appears in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201682">1</a></sup> as a king in whose realm, that of the Kurus, prosperity and peace abound. The verses in which he is celebrated are later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201682">2</a></sup> called <i>Pārikṣityaḥ,</i> and the Brāhmaṇas explain that <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> is <i><a class="vei_120a" href="परि-क्षित्">pari-kṣit</a></i> because he dwells among men. Hence Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201682">3</a></sup> and Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201682">4</a></sup> regard <a class="vei_120a" href="परिक्षित्">parikṣit</a> in the Atharvaveda not as a human king at all. This may be correct, but it is not certain. Both Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201682">5</a></sup> and  Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201682">6</a></sup> recognize <a class="vei_120a" href="परिक्षित्">parikṣit</a> as a real king, a view supported by the fact that in the later Vedic literature King <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनमेजय">janamejaya</a></b> bears the patronymic <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पारिक्षित">pārikṣita</a>.</b> If this be so, <a class="vei_120a" href="परिक्षित्">parikṣit</a> belonged to the later period, since the <a class="vei_120a" href="अथर्वन्">atharvan</a> passage in which his name occurs is certainly late, and none of the other Saṃhitās know <a class="vei_120a" href="परिक्षित्">parikṣit</a> at all. The Epic<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201682">7</a></sup> makes him grandfather of Pratiśravas and great-grandfather of Pratīpa, and Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201682">5</a></sup> probably with justice, compares the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रातिसुत्वन">prātisutvana</a> and Pratīpa found in another late <a class="vei_120a" href="अथर्वन्">atharvan</a> passage.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201682">8</a></sup> But <b>Devāpi</b> and <b>Śantanu</b> cannot be brought into connexion with Pratīpa.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201682">9</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201682">↑</a> xx. 127, 7-10. See also Scheftelowitz, <i>Die Apokryphen des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 156, 157, and the verses in the Vaitāna <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxxiv. 9. The Vedic spelling is <a class="vei_120a" href="परिक्षित्">parikṣit</a>, not Parīkṣit.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201682">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 32, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxx. 5; Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201682">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201682">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 690, 691. but see <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 101, n. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201682">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 131.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201682">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenlāndischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 237; <i>Buddha,</i> 396.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201682">↑</a> See Zimmer, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201682">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 131.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201682">↑</a> xx. 129.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201682">↑</a> Devāpi is really a Brahmin, son of <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋष्टिषेण">ṛṣṭiṣeṇa</a>, and not connected directly with Śantanu. <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 10, identifies them as brothers and Kurus; but the former part of the identification is, no doubt, wrong.</li></ol></div>

परि-घ|परिघ|परि|घ|పరి-ఘ|పరిఘ|పరి|ఘ|ಪರಿ-ಘ|ಪರಿಘ|ಪರಿ|ಘ|pari-gha|parigha|pari|gha|_vei_120@1683|_vei1_pn494
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1682">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1684">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn493">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn494">1:494</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn495">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-gha</b> denotes an iron bolt or bar in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (ii. 24, 6. 10. 15) as often later.</p></div>

परि-चक्रा|परिचक्रा|परि|चक्रा|పరి-చక్రా|పరిచక్రా|పరి|చక్రా|ಪರಿ-ಚಕ್ರಾ|ಪರಿಚಕ್ರಾ|ಪರಿ|ಚಕ್ರಾ|pari-cakrā|paricakrā|pari|cakrā|pari-chakraa|parichakraa|chakraa|_vei_120@1684|_vei1_pn494
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1683">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1685">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn493">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn494">1:494</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn495">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-cakrā</b> is according to one reading the name of a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a></b> town mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201684"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201684">1</a></sup> and identified by Weber<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201684"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201684">2</a></sup> with the later Ekacakrā, which was near <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काम्पील">kāmpīla</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201684"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201684">3</a></sup> There is a various reading <a class="vei_120a" href="परिवक्रा">parivakrā</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201684"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201684">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201684"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201684">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201684"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201684">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 192.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201684"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201684">↑</a> Mahābhārata, i. 6094.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201684"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201684">↑</a> Accepted by the scholiast and Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 397.</li></ol></div>

परि-चर|परिचर|परि|चर|పరి-చర|పరిచర|పరి|చర|ಪರಿ-ಚರ|ಪರಿಚರ|ಪರಿ|ಚರ|pari-cara|paricara|pari|cara|pari-chara|parichara|chara|_vei_120@1685|_vei1_pn494
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1684">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1686">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn493">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn494">1:494</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn495">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-cara</b> is found in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201685"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201685">1</a></sup> in the sense of ‘attendant.’ In the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201685"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201685">2</a></sup> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="परि">pari</a>-caraṇa</i> has the same sense metaphorically, the other two Vedas (<a class="vei_120a" href="साम">sāma</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्">yajur</a>) being said to be subsidiary to the Rigveda.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201685"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201685">↑</a> iv. 3, 5, 9. <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="परि">pari</a>-caritṛ,</i> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vii. 8, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201685"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201685">↑</a> vi. 11; Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 457.</li></ol></div>

परि-चर्मण्य|परिचर्मण्य|परि|चर्मण्य|పరి-చర్మణ్య|పరిచర్మణ్య|పరి|చర్మణ్య|ಪರಿ-ಚರ್ಮಣ್ಯ|ಪರಿಚರ್ಮಣ್ಯ|ಪರಿ|ಚರ್ಮಣ್ಯ|pari-carmaṇya|paricarmaṇya|pari|carmaṇya|pari-charmaNya|paricharmaNya|charmaNya|_vei_120@1686|_vei1_pn494
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1685">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1687">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn493">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn494">1:494</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn495">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-carmaṇya</b> denotes a thong of leather in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vi. 12) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka (ii. 1).</p></div>

परि-तक्म्या|परितक्म्या|परि|तक्म्या|పరి-తక్మ్యా|పరితక్మ్యా|పరి|తక్మ్యా|ಪರಿ-ತಕ್ಮ್ಯಾ|ಪರಿತಕ್ಮ್ಯಾ|ಪರಿ|ತಕ್ಮ್ಯಾ|pari-takmyā|paritakmyā|pari|takmyā|pari-takmyaa|paritakmyaa|takmyaa|_vei_120@1687|_vei1_pn494
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1686">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1688">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn493">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn494">1:494</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn495">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-takmyā</b> in a number of passages of the <b>Rigveda</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201687"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201687">1</a></sup> denotes ‘night’ according to the St. Petersburg <b>Dictionary.</b>  Sieg<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201687"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201687">2</a></sup> thinks that in one place<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201687"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201687">3</a></sup> at least the word signifies the decisive point of the race, something like the sense of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रपित्व">prapitva</a>.</b> But this is very doubtful.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201687"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201687">↑</a> i. 116, 15; iv. 41, 6; 43, 3; v. 30, 13; 31, 11; vi. 24, 9; vii. 69, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201687"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201687">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 128. <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 36; <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 106.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201687"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201687">↑</a> i. 116, 15.</li></ol></div>

परि-दा|परिदा|परि|పరి-దా|పరిదా|పరి|ಪರಿ-ದಾ|ಪರಿದಾ|ಪರಿ|pari-dā|paridā|pari|pari-daa|paridaa|_vei_120@1688|_vei1_pn495
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1687">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1689">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn494">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn495">1:495</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn496">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-dā</b> in a few passages of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201688"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201688">1</a></sup> has the sense of ‘giving oneself up to the mercy or protection of another.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201688"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201688">↑</a> ii. 4, 1, 11; ix. 2, 1, 17; 4, 2, 17; 4, 5; 5, 1, 53.</li></ol></div>

परि-धान|परिधान|परि|धान|పరి-ధాన|పరిధాన|పరి|ధాన|ಪರಿ-ಧಾನ|ಪರಿಧಾನ|ಪರಿ|ಧಾನ|pari-dhāna|paridhāna|pari|dhāna|pari-dhaana|paridhaana|dhaana|_vei_120@1689|_vei1_pn495
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1688">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1690">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn494">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn495">1:495</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn496">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-dhāna</b> denotes ‘garment,’ probably ‘under garment,’ in the Atharvaveda (viii. 2, 16) and the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 1, 10). A garment of saffron is mentioned in the Śānkhāyana Āraṇyaka (xi. 4).</p></div>

परि-पद्|परिपद्|परि|पद्|పరి-పద్|పరిపద్|పరి|పద్|ಪರಿ-ಪದ್|ಪರಿಪದ್|ಪರಿ|ಪದ್|pari-pad|paripad|pari|pad|_vei_120@1690|_vei1_pn495
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1689">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1691">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn494">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn495">1:495</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn496">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-pad</b> seems in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201690"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201690">1</a></sup> to denote a pitfall used to capture lions.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201690"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201690">↑</a> x. 28, 10; metaphorically, viii. 24, 24.</li></ol></div>

परि-पन्थिन्|परिपन्थिन्|परि|पन्थिन्|పరి-పన్థిన్|పరిపన్థిన్|పరి|పన్థిన్|ಪರಿ-ಪನ್ಥಿನ್|ಪರಿಪನ್ಥಿನ್|ಪರಿ|ಪನ್ಥಿನ್|pari-panthin|paripanthin|pari|panthin|_vei_120@1691|_vei1_pn495
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1690">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1692">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn494">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn495">1:495</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn496">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-panthin,</b> ‘besetting the path,’ denotes ‘robber’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201691"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201691">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201691"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201691">2</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तस्कर">taskara</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="तायु">tāyu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="स्तेन">stena</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201691"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201691">↑</a> i. 42, 3; 103, 6; x. 85, 32.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201691"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201691">↑</a> Av. i. 27, 1; iii. 15, 1; xii. 1, 32; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iv. 34, etc.</li></ol></div>

परि-पवन|परिपवन|परि|पवन|పరి-పవన|పరిపవన|పరి|పవన|ಪರಿ-ಪವನ|ಪರಿಪವನ|ಪರಿ|ಪವನ|pari-pavana|paripavana|pari|pavana|_vei_120@1692|_vei1_pn495
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1691">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1693">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn494">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn495">1:495</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn496">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-pavana</b> signifies in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> (iv. 9. 10) an instrument for winnowing grain.</p></div>

परि-मित्|परिमित्|परि|मित्|పరి-మిత్|పరిమిత్|పరి|మిత్|ಪರಿ-ಮಿತ್|ಪರಿಮಿತ್|ಪರಿ|ಮಿತ್|pari-mit|parimit|pari|mit|_vei_120@1693|_vei1_pn495
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1692">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1694">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn494">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn495">1:495</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn496">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-mit</b> occurs once in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201693"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201693">1</a></sup> in the description of a house, meaning perhaps the ‘crossbeams’ connecting the vertical posts.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201693"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201693">2</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गृह">gṛha</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201693"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201693">↑</a> ix. 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201693"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201693">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 596; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 158; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 525.</li></ol></div>

परि-मोष|परिमोष|परि|मोष|పరి-మోష|పరిమోష|పరి|మోష|ಪರಿ-ಮೋಷ|ಪರಿಮೋಷ|ಪರಿ|ಮೋಷ|pari-moṣa|parimoṣa|pari|moṣa|pari-moSha|parimoSha|moSha|_vei_120@1694|_vei1_pn495
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1693">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1695">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn494">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn495">1:495</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn496">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-moṣa</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201694"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201694">1</a></sup> signifies ‘theft,’ and <i>parimoṣin</i> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201694"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201694">2</a></sup> ‘thief.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201694"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201694">↑</a> ii. 5, 5, 1; vi. 1, 11, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201694"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201694">↑</a> xi. 6, 3, 11; xiii. 2, 4, 2, etc.</li></ol></div>

परि-रथ्य|परिरथ्य|परि|रथ्य|పరి-రథ్య|పరిరథ్య|పరి|రథ్య|ಪರಿ-ರಥ್ಯ|ಪರಿರಥ್ಯ|ಪರಿ|ರಥ್ಯ|pari-rathya|parirathya|pari|rathya|_vei_120@1695|_vei1_pn496
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1694">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1696">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn495">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn496">1:496</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn497">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-rathya</b> occurs once in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201695">1</a></sup> meaning either ‘road’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201695">2</a></sup> or a part of the chariot, perhaps as Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201695">3</a></sup> and Whitney<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201695">4</a></sup> render it, the ‘rim.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201695">↑</a> viii. 8, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201695">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 587, following Nīlakaṇṭha's explanation of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="परिरथ्य">parirathya</a>,</i> Mahābhārata, viii. 1487.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201695">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 528.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201695">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 506.</li></ol></div>

परि-वक्रा|परिवक्रा|परि|वक्रा|పరి-వక్రా|పరివక్రా|పరి|వక్రా|ಪರಿ-ವಕ್ರಾ|ಪರಿವಕ್ರಾ|ಪರಿ|ವಕ್ರಾ|pari-vakrā|parivakrā|pari|vakrā|pari-vakraa|parivakraa|vakraa|_vei_120@1696|_vei1_pn496
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1695">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1697">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn495">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn496">1:496</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn497">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-vakrā</b> is the reading accepted in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 5, 4, 7) by the scholiast instead of the variant <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परिचक्रा">paricakrā</a>,</b> which is supported by the Epic Ekacakrā.</p></div>

परि-वत्सर|परिवत्सर|परि|वत्सर|పరి-వత్సర|పరివత్సర|పరి|వత్సర|ಪರಿ-ವತ್ಸರ|ಪರಿವತ್ಸರ|ಪರಿ|ವತ್ಸರ|pari-vatsara|parivatsara|pari|vatsara|_vei_120@1697|_vei1_pn496
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1696">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1698">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn495">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn496">1:496</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn497">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-vatsara</b> denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201697">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201697">2</a></sup> a ‘full year.’ It is often mentioned with other names of year (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="संवत्सर">saṃvatsara</a></b>), and in the later five year cycle counts as the second year.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201697">↑</a> x. 62, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201697">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> i. 5, 5, 6; Mahābhārata, i. 3202, etc. So <i>parivatsarīṇa,</i> as an adjective, ‘relating to a full year,’ Rv. vii. 103, 8; Av. iii. 10, 3.</li></ol></div>

परि-वाप|परिवाप|परि|वाप|పరి-వాప|పరివాప|పరి|వాప|ಪರಿ-ವಾಪ|ಪರಿವಾಪ|ಪರಿ|ವಾಪ|pari-vāpa|parivāpa|pari|vāpa|pari-vaapa|parivaapa|vaapa|_vei_120@1698|_vei1_pn496
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1697">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1699">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn495">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn496">1:496</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn497">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-vāpa</b> in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201698"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201698">1</a></sup> and Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201698"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201698">2</a></sup> denotes ‘fried grains of rice.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201698"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201698">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 1, 10, 1; vi. 5, 11, 4; vii. 2, 10, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxiv. 11; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 21. 22.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201698"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201698">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 24; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 11, 2, etc.</li></ol></div>

परि-वित्त|परिवित्त|परि|वित्त|పరి-విత్త|పరివిత్త|పరి|విత్త|ಪರಿ-ವಿತ್ತ|ಪರಿವಿತ್ತ|ಪರಿ|ವಿತ್ತ|pari-vitta|parivitta|pari|vitta|_vei_120@1699|_vei1_pn496
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1698">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1700">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn495">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn496">1:496</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn497">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-vitta</b> denotes an ‘elder brother who is not married when his younger brother is.’ The term occurs in the list of sinful persons in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201699"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201699">1</a></sup> as well as in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201699"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201699">2</a></sup> where Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201699"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201699">3</a></sup> needlessly proposes to read <i>parivettā,</i> the ‘younger brother who marries before his eldest brother.’ The name for the younger brother in the older texts is Parivividāna.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201699"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201699">4</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201699"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201699">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxi. 7; Kapiṣṭhala Saṃhitā, xlvii. 7; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 1, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 8, 11; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201699"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201699">↑</a> vi. 112, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201699"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201699">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 470.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201699"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201699">↑</a> So in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a>, Kapiṣṭhala, Maitrāyaṇī, and Vājasaneyi Saṃhitās, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 580 <i>et seq.;</i> Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Phīlology,</i> 17, 430 <i>et seq.; Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 522 <i>et seq.;</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 315; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 362.</li></ol></div>

परि-वृक्ता|परिवृक्ता|परि|वृक्ता|परि-वृक्ती|परि-वृक्ति|परिवृक्ती|परिवृक्ति|वृक्ती|वृक्ति|परि-वृत्ती|परि-वृत्ति|परिवृत्ती|परिवृत्ति|वृत्ती|वृत्ति|పరి-వృక్తా|పరివృక్తా|పరి|వృక్తా|పరి-వృక్తీ|పరి-వృక్తి|పరివృక్తీ|పరివృక్తి|వృక్తీ|వృక్తి|పరి-వృత్తీ|పరి-వృత్తి|పరివృత్తీ|పరివృత్తి|వృత్తీ|వృత్తి|ಪರಿ-ವೃಕ್ತಾ|ಪರಿವೃಕ್ತಾ|ಪರಿ|ವೃಕ್ತಾ|ಪರಿ-ವೃಕ್ತೀ|ಪರಿ-ವೃಕ್ತಿ|ಪರಿವೃಕ್ತೀ|ಪರಿವೃಕ್ತಿ|ವೃಕ್ತೀ|ವೃಕ್ತಿ|ಪರಿ-ವೃತ್ತೀ|ಪರಿ-ವೃತ್ತಿ|ಪರಿವೃತ್ತೀ|ಪರಿವೃತ್ತಿ|ವೃತ್ತೀ|ವೃತ್ತಿ|pari-vṛktā|parivṛktā|pari|vṛktā|pari-vṛktī|pari-vṛkti|parivṛktī|parivṛkti|vṛktī|vṛkti|pari-vṛttī|pari-vṛtti|parivṛttī|parivṛtti|vṛttī|vṛtti|pari-vRktaa|parivRktaa|vRktaa|pari-vRktii|pari-vRkti|parivRktii|parivRkti|vRktii|vRkti|pari-vRttii|pari-vRtti|parivRttii|parivRtti|vRttii|vRtti|pari-vRktee|parivRktee|vRktee|pari-vRttee|parivRttee|vRttee|pari-vRiktaa|parivRiktaa|vRiktaa|pari-vRiktii|pari-vRikti|parivRiktii|parivRikti|vRiktii|vRikti|pari-vRittii|pari-vRitti|parivRittii|parivRitti|vRittii|vRitti|pari-vRuktaa|parivRuktaa|vRuktaa|pari-vRuktii|pari-vRukti|parivRuktii|parivRukti|vRuktii|vRukti|pari-vRuttii|pari-vRutti|parivRuttii|parivRutti|vRuttii|vRutti|pari-vRRiktaa|parivRRiktaa|vRRiktaa|pari-vRRiktii|pari-vRRikti|parivRRiktii|parivRRikti|vRRiktii|vRRikti|pari-vRRittii|pari-vRRitti|parivRRittii|parivRRitti|vRRittii|vRRitti|_vei_120@1700|_vei1_pn497
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1699">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1701">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn496">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn497">1:497</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn498">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-<a class="vei_120a" href="वृक्ता">vṛktā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="परि-वृक्ती">pari-vṛktī</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="परि">pari</a>-vṛttī,</b> are variant forms of the name of the rejected one among the royal wives. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>.</b></p></div>

परि-वेष्टृ|परिवेष्टृ|परि|वेष्टृ|పరి-వేష్టృ|పరివేష్టృ|పరి|వేష్టృ|ಪರಿ-ವೇಷ್ಟೃ|ಪರಿವೇಷ್ಟೃ|ಪರಿ|ವೇಷ್ಟೃ|pari-veṣṭṛ|pariveṣṭṛ|pari|veṣṭṛ|pari-veShTR|pariveShTR|veShTR|pari-veShTRi|pariveShTRi|veShTRi|pari-veShTRu|pariveShTRu|veShTRu|pari-veShTRRi|pariveShTRRi|veShTRRi|_vei_120@1701|_vei1_pn497
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1700">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1702">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn496">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn497">1:497</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn498">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-veṣṭṛ</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201701"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201701">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201701"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201701">2</a></sup> denotes an ‘attendant,’ more especially one who serves up food, a ‘waiter.’ The feminine form Pariveṣṭrī signifies a ‘female attendant’ or ‘handmaid.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201701"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201701">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201701"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201701">↑</a> ix. 6, 51.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201701"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201701">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 3, 1, 3; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 2, 16; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, vi. 13; xxx. 12. 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 8, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 21; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 4, 6; iii. 8, 2, 3; vi. 2, 13, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201701"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201701">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 2, 7, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 1; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 21, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

परि-व्राजक|परिव्राजक|परि|व्राजक|పరి-వ్రాజక|పరివ్రాజక|పరి|వ్రాజక|ಪರಿ-ವ್ರಾಜಕ|ಪರಿವ್ರಾಜಕ|ಪರಿ|ವ್ರಾಜಕ|pari-vrājaka|parivrājaka|pari|vrājaka|pari-vraajaka|parivraajaka|vraajaka|_vei_120@1702|_vei1_pn497
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1701">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1703">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn496">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn497">1:497</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn498">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-vrājaka</b> (lit., ‘wandering about’) denotes in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> (i. 14; ii. 8) a ‘mendicant monk.’</p></div>

परि-षद्|परिषद्|परि|षद्|పరి-షద్|పరిషద్|పరి|షద్|ಪರಿ-ಷದ್|ಪರಿಷದ್|ಪರಿ|ಷದ್|pari-ṣad|pariṣad|pari|ṣad|pari-Shad|pariShad|Shad|_vei_120@1703|_vei1_pn497
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1702">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1704">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn496">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn497">1:497</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn498">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-ṣad</b> (lit., ‘sitting around’) denotes in the Upaniṣads<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201703"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201703">1</a></sup> an ‘assemblage’ of advisers in questions of philosophy, and the Gobhila <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201703"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201703">2</a></sup> refers to a teacher with his <a class="vei_120a" href="परिषद्">pariṣad</a> or ‘council.’ In the later literature the word denotes a body of advisers on religious topics, but also the assessors of a judge, or the council of ministers of a prince.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201703"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201703">3</a></sup> But in none of these senses is the word found in the early literature, though the institutions indicated by it must have existed at least in embryo.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201703"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201703">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 1 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 2, 1 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>); <i>daivī <a class="vei_120a" href="परिषद्">pariṣad</a>,</i> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 11, 13. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201703"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201703">↑</a> iii. 2, 40.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201703"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201703">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 136, 137; Foy, <i>Die kōnigliche Gewalt,</i> 16-19; 3337; 66; Bühler, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 55, 56; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 124.</li></ol></div>

परि-ष्कन्द|परिष्कन्द|परि|ष्कन्द|పరి-ష్కన్ద|పరిష్కన్ద|పరి|ష్కన్ద|ಪರಿ-ಷ್ಕನ್ದ|ಪರಿಷ್ಕನ್ದ|ಪರಿ|ಷ್ಕನ್ದ|pari-ṣkanda|pariṣkanda|pari|ṣkanda|pari-Shkanda|pariShkanda|Shkanda|_vei_120@1704|_vei1_pn497
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1703">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1705">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn496">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn497">1:497</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn498">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-ṣkanda</b> (lit., ‘leaping around’) occurs in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात्य">vrātya</a></b> hymn of the Atharvaveda (xv. 2, 1 <i>et seq.</i>) denoting, in the dual, the two footmen running beside a chariot.</p></div>

परि-ष्यन्द|परिष्यन्द|परि|ष्यन्द|పరి-ష్యన్ద|పరిష్యన్ద|పరి|ష్యన్ద|ಪರಿ-ಷ್ಯನ್ದ|ಪರಿಷ್ಯನ್ದ|ಪರಿ|ಷ್ಯನ್ದ|pari-ṣyanda|pariṣyanda|pari|ṣyanda|pari-Shyanda|pariShyanda|Shyanda|_vei_120@1705|_vei1_pn497
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1704">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1706">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn496">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn497">1:497</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn498">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-ṣyanda</b> (with water ‘flowing around’) in two passages of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (ix. 1, 19; xiv. 3, 1, 14) denotes a sandbank or island in a river. </p></div>

परि-सारक|परिसारक|परि|सारक|పరి-సారక|పరిసారక|పరి|సారక|ಪರಿ-ಸಾರಕ|ಪರಿಸಾರಕ|ಪರಿ|ಸಾರಕ|pari-sāraka|parisāraka|pari|sāraka|pari-saaraka|parisaaraka|saaraka|_vei_120@1706|_vei1_pn498
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1705">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1707">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn497">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn498">1:498</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn499">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-sāraka</b> is the name of a place, an island formed by the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a></b> ‘flowing around’ it, according to a story in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (ii. 19).</p></div>

परि-स्रुत्|परिस्रुत्|परि|स्रुत्|పరి-స్రుత్|పరిస్రుత్|పరి|స్రుత్|ಪರಿ-ಸ್ರುತ್|ಪರಿಸ್ರುತ್|ಪರಿ|ಸ್ರುತ್|pari-srut|parisrut|pari|srut|_vei_120@1707|_vei1_pn498
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1706">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1708">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn497">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn498">1:498</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn499">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pari-srut</b> is the name of a drink which is mentioned first in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201707"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201707">1</a></sup> and which was distinct from both <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुरा">surā</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201707"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201707">2</a></sup> but was intoxicating. According to Mahīdhara,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201707"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201707">3</a></sup> the liquor was made from flowers (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुष्प">puṣpa</a></b>). Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201707"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201707">4</a></sup> thinks that it was the family drink, and this is supported by the fact that in the Atharvaveda it twice occurs as a household beverage.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201707"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201707">5</a></sup> Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201707"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201707">6</a></sup> is of opinion that it was very much the same as <a class="vei_120a" href="सुरा">surā</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201707"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201707">↑</a> iii. 12, 7; xx. 127, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 68.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201707"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201707">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1, 2, 14. <i>Cf.</i> v. 5, 4, 10; xi. 5, 5, 13; xii. 7, 1, 7; 8, 2, 15; 9, 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201707"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201707">↑</a> On Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ii. 34.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201707"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201707">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 281, 282.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201707"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201707">↑</a> See also Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 15; xx. 59; xxi. 29; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 11, 2. Its nature is more elaborately explained in the Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiv. 1, 14; xv. 10, 11; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 349, 350.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201707"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201707">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 24, 8.</li></ol></div>

परी-णह्|परी-णह्|परीणह्|परी|परि|णह्|పరీ-ణహ్_౧|పరీ-ణహ్|పరీణహ్|పరీ|పరి|ణహ్|ಪರೀ-ಣಹ್_೧|ಪರೀ-ಣಹ್|ಪರೀಣಹ್|ಪರೀ|ಪರಿ|ಣಹ್|parī-ṇah_1|parī-ṇah|parīṇah|parī|pari|ṇah|parii-Nah_1|parii-Nah|pariiNah|parii|Nah|paree-Nah_1|paree-Nah|pareeNah|paree|_vei_120@1708|_vei1_pn498
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1707">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1709">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn497">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn498">1:498</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn499">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Parī-ṇah</b> appears to denote a ‘box’ or something similar in the Atharvaveda (xix. 48, 1).</p></div>

परी-णह्|परी-णह्|परीणह्|परी|परि|णह्|పరీ-ణహ్_౨|పరీ-ణహ్|పరీణహ్|పరీ|పరి|ణహ్|ಪರೀ-ಣಹ್_೨|ಪರೀ-ಣಹ್|ಪರೀಣಹ್|ಪರೀ|ಪರಿ|ಣಹ್|parī-ṇah_2|parī-ṇah|parīṇah|parī|pari|ṇah|parii-Nah_2|parii-Nah|pariiNah|parii|Nah|paree-Nah_2|paree-Nah|pareeNah|paree|_vei_120@1709|_vei1_pn498
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1708">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1710">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn497">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn498">1:498</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn499">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Parī-ṇah</b> is the name of a place in <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a></b> mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201709"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201709">1</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201709"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201709">2</a></sup> and the Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201709"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201709">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201709"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201709">↑</a> xxv. 13, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201709"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201709">↑</a> v. 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201709"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201709">↑</a> Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 19, 1; Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxiv. 6, 34; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiii. 29, 32.</li></ol></div>

परी-शास|परीशास|परी|परि|शास|పరీ-శాస|పరీశాస|పరీ|పరి|శాస|ಪರೀ-ಶಾಸ|ಪರೀಶಾಸ|ಪರೀ|ಪರಿ|ಶಾಸ|parī-śāsa|parīśāsa|parī|pari|śāsa|parii-shaasa|pariishaasa|parii|shaasa|paree-shaasa|pareeshaasa|paree|_vei_120@1710|_vei1_pn498
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1709">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1711">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn497">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn498">1:498</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn499">↷</a><hr><p><b>Parī-śāsa</b> is the name of an instrument of the nature of tongs, used to lift the sacrificial kettle off the fire.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201710"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201710">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201710"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201710">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 1, 3, 1; 2, 1, 16; 2, 54; 3, 1, 20, etc.</li></ol></div>

परुच्-छेप|परुच्छेप|परुच्|छेप|పరుచ్-ఛేప|పరుచ్ఛేప|పరుచ్|ఛేప|ಪರುಚ್-ಛೇಪ|ಪರುಚ್ಛೇಪ|ಪರುಚ್|ಛೇಪ|paruc-chepa|parucchepa|paruc|chepa|paruch-Chepa|paruchChepa|paruch|Chepa|_vei_120@1711|_vei1_pn498
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1710">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1712">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn497">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn498">1:498</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn499">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paruc-chepa</b> is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> to whom the Anukramaṇī (Index) attributes a series of hymns<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201711"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201711">1</a></sup> in the Rigveda, and whose  authorship is asserted in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201711"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201711">2</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201711"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201711">3</a></sup> Brāhmaṇas, as well as in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201711"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201711">4</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201711"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201711">5</a></sup> he appears as a rival of <b>Nṛmedhas.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201711"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201711">↑</a> Rv. i. 127-139.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201711"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201711">↑</a> v. 12. 13 (where hymns Rv. i. 128, 129, 130, 133, 135, 137, 139, are attributed to him).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201711"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201711">↑</a> xxiii. 4. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201711"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201711">↑</a> x. 42.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201711"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201711">↑</a> ii. 5, 8, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 116. Parucchepa's claim to authorship is very doubtful.</li></ol></div>

परुष|పరుష|ಪರುಷ|paruṣa|paruSha|_vei_120@1712|_vei1_pn499
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1711">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1713">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn498">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn499">1:499</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn500">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paruṣa</b> seems to mean ‘reed’ in the Atharvaveda (viii. 8, 4) and ‘arrow’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (xiv. 22, 20).</p></div>

परुष्णी|परुष्णि|పరుష్ణీ|పరుష్ణి|ಪರುಷ್ಣೀ|ಪರುಷ್ಣಿ|paruṣṇī|paruṣṇi|paruShNii|paruShNi|paruShNee|_vei_120@1713|_vei1_pn499
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1712">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1714">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn498">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn499">1:499</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn500">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paruṣṇī</b> is the name of a river which is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="नदी">nadī</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="स्तुति">stuti</a> (‘Praise of Rivers’),<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201713">1</a></sup> and in the song of Sudās’ victory over the ten kings,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201713">2</a></sup> which seems to have been made decisive by the rise of the river drowning the fugitives.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201713">3</a></sup> In these passages and one of the eighth book of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201713">4</a></sup> where it is called a ‘great stream’ (<i>mahenadi</i>), the name is certainly that of the river later called Ravi (Irāvatī), as recognized by <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201713">5</a></sup> Pischel<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201713">6</a></sup> sees a reference to it in two other passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201713">7</a></sup> where ‘wool’ (<i>ūṛṇā</i>) is connected with the word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="परुष्णी">paruṣṇī</a>,</i> and the allusion to the river is accepted by Max Müller<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201713">8</a></sup> and Oldenberg,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201713">9</a></sup> though they are not fully agreed as to the exact sense of the passages in question. Pischel suggests that the name is derived from the ‘flocks’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="परुस्">parus</a></i>) of wool, not from the bends of the river, as understood by the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201713">5</a></sup> or from its reeds, as Roth<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201713">10</a></sup> suggests. </p><p>The mention of the <a class="vei_120a" href="परुष्णी">paruṣṇī</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a> in the hymn celebrating the victory of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a> has given rise to the conjectures of Hopkins,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201713">11</a></sup> that the <a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a> in that hymn is merely another name for the <a class="vei_120a" href="परुष्णी">paruṣṇī</a>, and of Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201713">12</a></sup> that the <a class="vei_120a" href="परुष्णी">paruṣṇī</a> there is merely a tributary of the <a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a> (Jumna). But neither interpretation is either essential or even probable. The hymn is a condensed one, and may well be taken as celebrating two great victories of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>. There is a doubtful reference to the <a class="vei_120a" href="परुष्णी">paruṣṇī</a> in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201713">13</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201713">↑</a> x. 75, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201713">↑</a> vii. 18, 8. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201713">↑</a> It is impossible to decide precisely what part the river played in the battle. It is usually held that the enemies of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a> tried to divert the stream, but failed, and were drowned in its current. So Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 11; Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 154; Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Kommentar,</i> 103, holds that <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a> was caught between two opposing armies, and had to escape over the <a class="vei_120a" href="परुष्णी">paruṣṇī</a>, that his enemies tried to divert it to render him more accessible to their attack, but failed, and were overwhelmed in the river. Hopkins, <i>India, Old and New,</i> 52 <i>et seq.,</i> may be right in rejecting <i>in toto</i> the theory of the attempted diversion of the waters, though in the <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 261 <i>et seq.,</i> he accepted the traditional view.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201713">↑</a> viii. 74, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201713">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ix. 26.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201713">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 208-210.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201713">↑</a> iv. 22, 2; v. 52, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201713">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 315, 323.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201713">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 348.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201713">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ix. 26.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201713">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 4<i>a.</i></li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201713">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 52.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201713">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>, Glossar,</i> 106.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201713">↑</a> vi. 12, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 462; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 289.</li></ol></div>

परुस्|परु|పరుస్|పరు|ಪರುಸ್|ಪರು|parus|paru|_vei_120@1714|_vei1_pn500
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1713">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1715">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn499">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn500">1:500</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn501">↷</a><hr><p><b>Parus</b> means first a ‘limb’ or ‘member’ of the body,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201714"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201714">1</a></sup> and is then applied metaphorically to the divisions of the sacrifice<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201714"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201714">2</a></sup> or of the year<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201714"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201714">3</a></sup> (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्वन्">parvan</a></b>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201714"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201714">↑</a> Rv. i. 162, 18; x. 97, 12; 100, 5; Av. i. 12, 3; iv. 12, 2. 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201714"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201714">↑</a> Rv. x. 53, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 9, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201714"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201714">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 6, 1.</li></ol></div>

पर्ण|पर्ण|పర్ణ_౧|పర్ణ|ಪರ್ಣ_೧|ಪರ್ಣ|parṇa_1|parṇa|parNa_1|parNa|_vei_120@1715|_vei1_pn500
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1714">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1716">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn499">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn500">1:500</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn501">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Parṇa</b> denotes the ‘wing’ of a bird in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201715"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201715">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201715"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201715">2</a></sup> It also means the ‘feather’ of an arrow in a late passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201715"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201715">3</a></sup> and more often later;<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201715"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201715">4</a></sup> and the ‘leaf’ of a tree from the Rigveda onwards.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201715"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201715">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201715"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201715">↑</a> i. 116, 15; 182, 7; 183, 1; iv. 27, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201715"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201715">↑</a> Av. x. 1, 29; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 3, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201715"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201715">↑</a> x. 18, 14. <i>Cf.</i> Lanman, <i>Sanskrit Reader,</i> 386.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201715"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201715">↑</a> Av. v. 25, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxv. 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 25; iii. 26, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201715"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201715">↑</a> Rv. x. 68, 10; Av. viii. 7, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 1, 7; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 46, etc.</li></ol></div>

पर्ण|पर्ण|పర్ణ_౨|పర్ణ|ಪರ್ಣ_೨|ಪರ್ಣ|parṇa_2|parṇa|parNa_2|parNa|_vei_120@1716|_vei1_pn500
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1715">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1717">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn499">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn500">1:500</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn501">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Parṇa</b> denotes the tree <i>Butea frondosa,</i> later usually called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पलाश">palāśa</a>.</b> It occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201716">1</a></sup> in connexion with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वत्थ">aśvattha</a>,</b> and with that tree as well as the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="न्यग्रोध">nyagrodha</a></b> in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201716">2</a></sup> which mentions both amulets<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201716">3</a></sup> and the cover of sacrificial dishes<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201716">4</a></sup> as made from its wood. Its use for the  making of sacrificial implements like the ladle (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="जुहू">juhū</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201716">5</a></sup> or sacrificial posts,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201716">6</a></sup> or the small ladle called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्रुव">sruva</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201716">7</a></sup> is mentioned. The <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201716">8</a></sup> ascribes its origin to the loss of a feather by the Gāyatrī when winning the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>. The tree is also often mentioned elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201716">9</a></sup> Reference too is sometimes made to its bark (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्ण">parṇa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वल्क">valka</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201716">10</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201716">↑</a> x. 97, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201716">↑</a> v. 5, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201716">↑</a> iii. 5, 4. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201716">↑</a> xviii. 4, 53.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201716">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 59, 7, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201716">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxi. 4, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201716">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 2. <i>Cf.</i> viii. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 3, 11; 7, 1, 9; 8, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201716">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> and <i>cf.</i> Kuhn, <i>Die Herabkunft des Feuers und des Go7ttertranks,</i> 148, 192; Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, 20, 24; <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 331, 332; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 91.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201716">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 4, 10; vi. 5, 1, 1; xi. 1, 4, 2; 7, 28; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 5, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201716">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 3, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 7, 4, 2, 18, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altinadisches Leben,</i> 59; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 194, 195.</li></ol></div>

पर्णक|పర్ణక|ಪರ್ಣಕ|parṇaka|parNaka|_vei_120@1717|_vei1_pn501
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1716">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1718">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn500">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn501">1:501</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn502">↷</a><hr><p><b>Parṇaka</b> is the name of a man included in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201717"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201717">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201717"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201717">2</a></sup> According to Mahīdhara,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201717"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201717">3</a></sup> a Bhilla is meant--<i>i.e.,</i> presumably a wild hillman, for he glosses <b><a class="vei_120a" href="निषाद">niṣāda</a></b> in the same way.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201717"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201717">4</a></sup> Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201717"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201717">5</a></sup> explains the word as meaning ‘one who catches fish by putting over the water a <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्ण">parṇa</a></i> with poison,’ but this is apparently a mere etymological guess. Weber's<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201717"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201717">6</a></sup> rendering of the term as referring to a savage ‘wearing feathers’ is ingenious, but uncertain.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201717"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201717">↑</a> xxx. 16.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201717"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201717">↑</a> iii. 4, 12, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201717"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201717">↑</a> On Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201717"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201717">↑</a> On Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 27.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201717"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201717">↑</a> On <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201717"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201717">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländiscnen Gesellschaft,</i> 18, 281.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 119.</li></ol></div>

पर्ण-धि|पर्णधि|पर्ण|धि|పర్ణ-ధి|పర్ణధి|పర్ణ|ధి|ಪರ್ಣ-ಧಿ|ಪರ್ಣಧಿ|ಪರ್ಣ|ಧಿ|parṇa-dhi|parṇadhi|parṇa|dhi|parNa-dhi|parNadhi|parNa|_vei_120@1718|_vei1_pn501
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1717">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1719">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn500">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn501">1:501</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn502">↷</a><hr><p><b>Parṇa-dhi</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201718">1</a></sup> denotes the part of the shaft in which the feather of the arrow is fastened.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201718">↑</a> iv. 6, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 300; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 375; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 154.</li></ol></div>

पर्णय|పర్ణయ|ಪರ್ಣಯ|parṇaya|parNaya|_vei_120@1719|_vei1_pn501
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1718">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1720">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn500">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn501">1:501</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn502">↷</a><hr><p><b>Parṇaya</b> is the name in two passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201719"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201719">1</a></sup> either of a hero, as Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201719"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201719">2</a></sup> thinks, or of a demon<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201719"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201719">3</a></sup> overcome by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201719"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201719">↑</a> i. 53, 8; x. 48, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201719"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201719">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 149.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201719"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201719">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

पर्य्-अंक|पर्यंक|पर्य्|अंक|पर्य्-अङ्क|पर्यङ्क|अङ्क|పర్య్-అంక|పర్యంక|పర్య్|అంక|పర్య్-అఙ్క|పర్యఙ్క|అఙ్క|ಪರ್ಯ್-ಅಂಕ|ಪರ್ಯಂಕ|ಪರ್ಯ್|ಅಂಕ|ಪರ್ಯ್-ಅಙ್ಕ|ಪರ್ಯಙ್ಕ|ಅಙ್ಕ|pary-aṅka|paryaṅka|pary|aṅka|pary-aMka|paryaMka|aMka|pary-anka|paryanka|anka|_vei_120@1720|_vei1_pn502
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1719">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1721">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn501">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn502">1:502</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn503">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pary-aṅka</b> is the name of the seat of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201720"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201720">1</a></sup> It seems to correspond to what is elsewhere called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आसन्दि">āsandi</a>;</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201720"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201720">2</a></sup> as used in the <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, it can, however, hardly mean a long seat for reclining on, but rather a throne.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201720"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201720">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201720"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201720">↑</a> i. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201720"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201720">↑</a> Av. xv. 3, 3. <i>Cf.</i> xiv. 2, 65; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 5, 6, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201720"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201720">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 397, 401; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 155; Lanmar in Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 765, 776.</li></ol></div>

पर्य्-आस|पर्यास|पर्य्|आस|పర్య్-ఆస|పర్యాస|పర్య్|ఆస|ಪರ್ಯ್-ಆಸ|ಪರ್ಯಾಸ|ಪರ್ಯ್|ಆಸ|pary-āsa|paryāsa|pary|āsa|pary-aasa|paryaasa|aasa|_vei_120@1721|_vei1_pn502
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1720">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1722">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn501">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn502">1:502</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn503">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pary-āsa</b> is used in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 1, 2, 18) to denote the woof of cloth, the warp being called <i>anuchāda.</i></p></div>

पर्वत|पर्वत|పర్వత_౧|పర్వత|ಪರ್ವತ_೧|ಪರ್ವತ|parvata_1|parvata|_vei_120@1722|_vei1_pn502
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1721">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1723">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn501">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn502">1:502</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn503">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Parvata</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201722">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201722">2</a></sup> is conjoined with <i><a class="vei_120a" href="गिरि">giri</a></i> in the sense of ‘hill’ or ‘mountain.’ From the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201722">3</a></sup> onwards<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201722">4</a></sup> it is common in this sense as connected with the waters of rivers which flow in the hills.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201722">5</a></sup> The legend of the mountains having wings is already found in the Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201722">6</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201722">7</a></sup> are mentioned the southern (<i>dakṣiṇa</i>) and the northern (<i>uttara</i>) mountains, evidently in allusion to the Himālaya and the Vindhya ranges. The plants (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ओषधि">oṣadhi</a></i>) and aromatic products (<i>añjana</i>) of the mountains are referred to in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201722">8</a></sup> and their mineral treasures in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201722">9</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201722">↑</a> i. 37, 7; v. 56, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201722">↑</a> Av. iv. 6, 8; vi. 12, 3; 17, 3; ix. 1, 18; xii. 1, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201722">↑</a> i. 39, 5; 52, 2; 155; 1; 191, 9; ii. 12, 2, 3; 17, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201722">↑</a> Av. i. 14, 1; iii. 21, 10; iv. 9, 8; viii. 7, 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 5, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvii. 1; xviii. 13, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201722">↑</a> Rv. vii. 34, 23; 35, 8; viii. 18, 16; 31, 10; x. 35, 2; 36, 1, etc.; Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> i. 80; 2, 66.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201722">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvi. 9; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 10, 13; and Rv. iv. 54, 5, as explained by Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 174.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201722">↑</a> ii. 13; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 407; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 28, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201722">↑</a> xix. 44, 6; 45. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201722">↑</a> x. 69, 6.</li></ol></div>

पर्वत|पर्वत|పర్వత_౨|పర్వత|ಪರ್ವತ_೨|ಪರ್ವತ|parvata_2|parvata|_vei_120@1723|_vei1_pn502
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1722">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1724">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn501">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn502">1:502</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn503">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Parvata</b> in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201723"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201723">1</a></sup> denotes, according to Ludwing,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201723"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201723">2</a></sup> a sacrificer whose generosity is praised. But it is probable that the god <a class="vei_120a" href="पर्वत">parvata</a>, the spirit of the mountain, is meant.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201723"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201723">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201723"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201723">↑</a> vii. 87, 8</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201723"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201723">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 159.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201723"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201723">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

पर्वत|पर्वत|పర్వత_౩|పర్వత|ಪರ್ವತ_೩|ಪರ್ವತ|parvata_3|parvata|_vei_120@1724|_vei1_pn503
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1723">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1725">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn502">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn503">1:503</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn504">↷</a><hr><p>3. <b>Parvata</b> is mentioned several times in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201724"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201724">1</a></sup> along with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नारद">nārada</a>.</b> The Anukramaṇī (Index) attributes to him the authorship of several hymns of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201724"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201724">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201724"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201724">↑</a> vii. 13, 34; viii. 21; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 17. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201724"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201724">↑</a> viii. 12, ix, 104; 105.</li></ol></div>

पर्वन्|పర్వన్|ಪರ್ವನ್|parvan|_vei_120@1725|_vei1_pn503
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1724">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1726">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn502">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn503">1:503</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn504">↷</a><hr><p><b>Parvan</b> denotes the knots of the reed or the joints of a plant,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201725">1</a></sup> and more generally a part or limb of the body.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201725">2</a></sup> It also designates a period of time, probably with reference to the breaks in the month at new and full moon.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201725">3</a></sup> In one passage<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201725">4</a></sup> Geldner<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201725">5</a></sup> thinks the word indicates a song section of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201725">↑</a> Av. xii. 3, 31; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i, 1, 2, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 3, 1, 31, and <i>cf.</i> Rv. x. 68, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201725">↑</a> Rv. i. 61, 12; iv. 19, 9; viii. 48, 5; x. 89, 8; Av. i. 11, 1; 12, 2; ii. 9, 1; vi. 14, 1; xi. 8, 12; xii. 5, 71; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 31; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 3, 35 <i>et seq.;</i> iii. 4, 4, 2; vi. 1, 2, 31; x. 4, 5, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201725">↑</a> Rv. i. 94, 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiii. 43; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 3, 35; vi. 2, 2, 34, etc. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b> In the Sūtras the days of the four-monthly festivals (<i>cāturmāsaya</i>) are so called: Kātyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, v. 2, 13; xxii. 7, 1. 16. 17; xxiv. 4, 30; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiv. 5, 6; 10, 4. 18; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ix. 2, 3; and more often the periods of the change of moon: Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>. xxiv. 6, 4. 25. 30; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 2, 1; 3, 1; Lāṭyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 8, 46, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201725">↑</a> vii. 103, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201725">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>, Glossar,</i> 107.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 364, who quotes Tacitus, <i>Germania,</i> 11.</li></ol></div>

पर्शान|పర్శాన|ಪರ್ಶಾನ|parśāna|parshaana|_vei_120@1726|_vei1_pn503
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1725">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1727">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn502">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn503">1:503</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn504">↷</a><hr><p><b>Parśāna,</b> ‘hollow,’ is mentioned several times in the Rigveda (vii. 104, 5; viii. 7, 34; 45, 41).</p></div>

पर्शु|पर्शु|పర్శు_౧|పర్శు|ಪರ್ಶು_೧|ಪರ್ಶು|parśu_1|parśu|parshu_1|parshu|_vei_120@1727|_vei1_pn503
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1726">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1728">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn502">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn503">1:503</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn504">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Parśu</b> denotes ‘rib’ in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201727"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201727">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201727"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201727">2</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शरीर">śarīra</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201727"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201727">↑</a> ix. 7, 6; x. 9, 20; xi. 3, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201727"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201727">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 25, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxi. 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 6, 2, 10; x. 6, 4, 1; xii. 3, 1, 6; Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, etc.</li></ol></div>

पर्शु|पर्शु|పర్శు_౨|పర్శు|ಪರ್ಶು_೨|ಪರ್ಶು|parśu_2|parśu|parshu_2|parshu|_vei_120@1728|_vei1_pn503
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1727">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1729">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn502">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn503">1:503</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn504">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Parśu</b> seems in some passages<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201728"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201728">1</a></sup> to denote a ‘sickle,’ being apparently a variant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परशु">paraśu</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201728"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201728">↑</a> Av. xii. 3, 31 (<a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 24. 25; viii. 11; lxi. 38. 39); perhaps vii. 28, 1 = <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 2, 4, 1. See Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 407, 408; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 26 (where <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्शु">parśu</a></i> is metrically needed), etc. <i>Cf.</i> Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

पर्शु|पर्शु|పర్శు_౩|పర్శు|ಪರ್ಶು_೩|ಪರ್ಶು|parśu_3|parśu|parshu_3|parshu|_vei_120@1729|_vei1_pn504
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1728">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1730">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn503">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn504">1:504</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn505">↷</a><hr><p>3. <b>Parśu</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201729"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201729">1</a></sup> is explained in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201729"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201729">2</a></sup> as meaning the sides of a cistern (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कूप">kūpa</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201729"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201729">3</a></sup> But the sense of ‘ribs’ is quite adequate there.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201729"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201729">↑</a> iv. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201729"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201729">↑</a> i. 105, 8; x. 33, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201729"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201729">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 100; Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 107.</li></ol></div>

पर्शु|पर्शु|పర్శు_౪|పర్శు|ಪರ್ಶು_೪|ಪರ್ಶು|parśu_4|parśu|parshu_4|parshu|_vei_120@1730|_vei1_pn504
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1729">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1731">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn503">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn504">1:504</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn505">↷</a><hr><p>4. <b>Parśu</b> occurs in one passage in a Dānastuti (‘praise of gifts’) in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201730">1</a></sup> as the name of a man. It is not certain that he is identical with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तिरिन्दिर">tirindira</a>,</b> but the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201730">2</a></sup> mentions <a class="vei_120a" href="तिरिन्दिर">tirindira</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पारशव्य">pāraśavya</a> as the patron of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वत्स">vatsa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</b> In another passage occurring in the Vṛṣākapi hymn,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201730">3</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="पर्शु">parśu</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मानवी">mānavī</a> occurs, apparently as a woman, daughter of <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>, but who is meant it is quite impossible to say. Excepting these two, there are no other occurrences in which the word has with any probability the value of a proper name in the Rigveda.</p><p>Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201730">4</a></sup> however, sees in several other places an allusion to the Parśus. Thus in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201730">5</a></sup> he finds a reference to the defeat of <b>Kuruśravaṇa</b> by the Parśus; in another<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201730">6</a></sup> he finds a reference to the Pṛthus and Parśus--<i>i.e.,</i> the Parthians and the Persians. He also sees the Parthians in <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पार्थव">pārthava</a>,</b> a name found in one hymn.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201730">7</a></sup> The same view is taken by Weber,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201730">8</a></sup> who holds that historical connexions with the  Persians are referred to. But Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201730">9</a></sup> points out that this conclusion is not justified; the Parśus were known to Pāṇini<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201730">10</a></sup> as a warrior tribe; the Pāraśavas were a tribe in south-west <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a>;</b> and the <i>Periplus</i><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201730">11</a></sup> knows a tribe of Parthoi in north India. At most the only conclusion to be drawn is that the Indians and Iranians were early connected, as was of course the case. Actual historical contact cannot be asserted with any degree of probability.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201730">↑</a> viii. 6, 46.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201730">↑</a> xvi. 11, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201730">↑</a> x. 86, 23. Apparently Vārttika 2, on Pāṇini, iv. 1, 177, where <a class="vei_120a" href="पर्शु">parśu</a> is explained as a feminine, Princess of the Parśus, refers to this passage. On the sense, <i>cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 42; <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 107; and <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 2, 2, where the expression occurs, but where the sense is very dubious.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201730">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 196 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201730">↑</a> x. 33, 2. The sense here is, no doubt, ‘ribs.’ See Geldner, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 184, n. 3; Bergaigne, <i>Religion Védique,</i> 2, 362, n.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201730">↑</a> vii. 83, 1, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पृथु">pṛthu</a>-parśavaḥ,</i> which really means either ‘with large ribs’-<i>i.e.,</i> ‘strong,’ as Roth, with Sāyaṇa, inclines to take it--or ‘with broad axes,’ according to Zimmer.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201730">↑</a> vi. 27, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201730">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 379; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 4; <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 36 <i>et seq.</i> He confines his view to the equation of <a class="vei_120a" href="पर्शु">parśu</a> in Rigveda, viii. 6, 46, and the Persians. Hillebrandt, who is inclined to see relations with Iran in early times (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पणि">paṇi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पारावत">pārāvata</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सृञ्जय">sṛñjaya</a></b>), does not in this connexion quote <a class="vei_120a" href="पर्शु">parśu</a> at all, and, though he mentions <a class="vei_120a" href="पार्थव">pārthava</a>, does not regard it as probably referring to a Parthian (<i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 105). Brunnhofer, in his various works (<i>Iran und Turan,</i> 1889; <i>vom Pontus bis zum Indus,</i> 1890, etc.), finds constant references in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a> to events in Iran, but his theories must be regarded as definitely unscientific. See also Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 264, n.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201730">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 134 <i>et seq.;</i> 433. <i>Ibid.,</i> 434, 435, he refutes conclusively Ludwig's extraordinary view that <a class="vei_120a" href="पृथु">pṛthu</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="पर्शु">parśu</a> are dialectical forms of the same word.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201730">↑</a> v. 3, 117.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201730">↑</a> c. 38.</li></ol></div>

पर्ष|పర్ష|ಪರ್ಷ|parṣa|parSha|_vei_120@1731|_vei1_pn505
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1730">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1732">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn504">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn505">1:505</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn506">↷</a><hr><p><b>Parṣa</b> occurs in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201731"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201731">1</a></sup> denoting in the plural ‘sheaves’ strewn over the threshing floor. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="खल">khala</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201731"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201731">↑</a> x. 48, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 10. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 238. Perhaps <i>parṣin,</i> in the compound <i><a class="vei_120a" href="इषु">iṣu</a>-parṣin</i> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 2, 5, means ‘having a bundle (of arrows).’</li></ol></div>

पलद|పలద|ಪಲದ|palada|_vei_120@1732|_vei1_pn505
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1731">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1733">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn504">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn505">1:505</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn506">↷</a><hr><p><b>Palada</b> occurs twice in one hymn of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201732"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201732">1</a></sup> in the description of a house. It seems to mean bundles of straw or reeds used to thatch the house and render the sides wind and weather proof.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201732"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201732">↑</a> ix. 3, 5, 17. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer. <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 153; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 194, 195.</li></ol></div>

पलस्ति|పలస్తి|ಪಲಸ್ತಿ|palasti|_vei_120@1733|_vei1_pn505
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1732">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1734">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn504">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn505">1:505</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn506">↷</a><hr><p><b>Palasti.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पलित">palita</a>.</b></p></div>

पलाल|పలాల|ಪಲಾಲ|palāla|palaala|_vei_120@1734|_vei1_pn505
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1733">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1735">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn504">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn505">1:505</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn506">↷</a><hr><p><b>Palāla</b> is found with <a class="vei_120a" href="अनु">anu</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पलाल">palāla</a> in the Atharvaveda (viii. 6, 2) as the name of a demon. The meaning of the word is ‘straw,’ in which sense it occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (lxxx. 27), while the feminine form, Palālī, is found in the Atharvaveda itself (ii. 8, 3) as the straw of barley (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="यव">yava</a></b>).</p></div>

पलाव|పలావ|ಪಲಾವ|palāva|palaava|_vei_120@1735|_vei1_pn505
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1734">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1736">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn504">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn505">1:505</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn506">↷</a><hr><p><b>Palāva</b> is found in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201735">1</a></sup> and the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201735">2</a></sup> in the sense of ‘chaff.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201735">↑</a> xii. 3, 19, where some manuscripts read <i>palāvā.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201735">↑</a> i. 54, 1</li></ol></div>

पलाश|పలాశ|ಪಲಾಶ|palāśa|palaasha|_vei_120@1736|_vei1_pn506
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1735">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1737">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn505">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn506">1:506</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn507">↷</a><hr><p><b>Palāśa,</b> like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्ण">parṇa</a>,</b> denotes ‘leaf’ in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201736"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201736">1</a></sup> It also<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201736"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201736">2</a></sup> designates the tree <i>Butea frondosa,</i> of which <a class="vei_120a" href="पर्ण">parṇa</a> is the early name.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201736"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201736">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 4, 5; v. 2, 1, 17 etc.; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 14, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201736"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201736">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 3, 19; ii. 6, 2, 8, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 59.</li></ol></div>

पलित|పలిత|ಪಲಿತ|palita|_vei_120@1737|_vei1_pn506
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1736">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1738">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn505">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn506">1:506</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn507">↷</a><hr><p><b>Palita,</b> ‘grey-haired,’ occurs frequently from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201737"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201737">1</a></sup> onwards.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201737"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201737">2</a></sup> It is the distinctive sign of old age. Those who, like certain descendants of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जमदग्नि">jamadagni</a>,</b> do not grow old,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201737"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201737">3</a></sup> are said not to become grey-haired, while <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a></b> is described as having in his old age become thin and grey-haired.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201737"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201737">4</a></sup> The Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201737"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201737">5</a></sup> in one passage observes that grey hairs appear first on the head, and elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201737"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201737">6</a></sup> alludes to the hair on the arms having become grey.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201737"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201737">↑</a> i. 144, 4; 164, 1; iii. 55, 9; x. 4, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201737"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201737">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 15, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201737"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201737">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 1, 9, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxi. 10, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 54, and Rv. iii. 53, 16, where <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पलस्ति">palasti</a></i> seems to mean <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पलित">palita</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201737"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201737">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 49.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201737"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201737">↑</a> xi. 4, 1, 6. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201737"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201737">↑</a> iii. 8, 2, 25.</li></ol></div>

पल्पूलन|పల్పూలన|ಪಲ್ಪೂಲನ|palpūlana|palpuulana|palpoolana|_vei_120@1738|_vei1_pn506
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1737">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1739">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn505">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn506">1:506</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn507">↷</a><hr><p><b>Palpūlana</b> is found in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201738"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201738">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201738"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201738">2</a></sup> apparently meaning, properly, ‘lye,’ or water impregnated with some biting substance for washing clothes. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="अथर्वन्">atharvan</a> passage urine seems to be meant.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201738"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201738">3</a></sup> The verb <i>palpūlaya,</i> ‘to wash with alkaline water,’ occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201738"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201738">2</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>;<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201738"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201738">4</a></sup> and the Sūtras refer to hides (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="चर्मन्">carman</a></i>)<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201738"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201738">5</a></sup> and garments<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201738"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201738">6</a></sup> so washed. <i>Cf.</i> also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वासःपल्पूली">vāsaḥpalpūlī</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201738"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201738">↑</a> xii. 4, 9. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xi. 16.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201738"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201738">↑</a> ii. 5, 5, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201738"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201738">↑</a> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 695. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 74, 175.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201738"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201738">↑</a> ii. 5, 5, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201738"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201738">↑</a> i. 3, 5, 2. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201738"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201738">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, 67.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201738"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201738">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 8, 12. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>. i. 6, 13, 15; Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

पल्लि-गुप्त लौहित्य|पल्लिगुप्त लौहित्य|पल्लि|गुप्त लौहित्य|పల్లి-గుప్త లౌహిత్య|పల్లిగుప్త లౌహిత్య|పల్లి|గుప్త లౌహిత్య|ಪಲ್ಲಿ-ಗುಪ್ತ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|ಪಲ್ಲಿಗುಪ್ತ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|ಪಲ್ಲಿ|ಗುಪ್ತ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|palli-gupta lauhitya|palligupta lauhitya|palli|gupta lauhitya|_vei_120@1739|_vei1_pn506
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1738">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1740">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn505">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn506">1:506</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn507">↷</a><hr><p><b>Palli-<a class="vei_120a" href="गुप्त">gupta</a> Lauhitya</b> (‘descendant of Lohita’) is mentioned in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (‘list of teachers’) in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 42, 1) as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्यामजयन्त लौहित्य">śyāmajayanta lauhitya</a>.</b>  The name is obviously a late one, for <a class="vei_120a" href="पल्लि">palli</a> is not found in the early literature, and the name of the <a class="vei_120a" href="लौहित्य">lauhitya</a> family is otherwise known in post-Vedic works only.</p></div>

पवन|పవన|ಪವನ|pavana|_vei_120@1740|_vei1_pn507
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1739">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1741">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn506">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn507">1:507</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn508">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pavana</b> (‘purifier’) in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201740"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201740">1</a></sup> denotes an instrument for purifying grain from husks, etc.; either a ‘sieve’ or a ‘winnowing basket’ may be meant. In the Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201740"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201740">2</a></sup> it is mentioned as used for cleaning the bones of the dead after cremation.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201740"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201740">↑</a> iv. 34, 2; xviii. 3, 11. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201740"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201740">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 5, 7.</li></ol></div>

पवमान|పవమాన|ಪವಮಾನ|pavamāna|pavamaana|_vei_120@1741|_vei1_pn507
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1740">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1742">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn506">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn507">1:507</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn508">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pavamāna</b> is a term very frequently applied in the Rigveda to the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> ‘purifying itself’ by running through the sieve. Later it appears in a few passages<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201741"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201741">1</a></sup> in the sense of ‘wind’ (as a purifier).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201741"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201741">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 20, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, vi. 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7.</li></ol></div>

पवस्त|పవస్త|ಪವಸ್ತ|pavasta|_vei_120@1742|_vei1_pn507
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1741">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1743">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn506">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn507">1:507</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn508">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pavasta</b> in one passage of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201742"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201742">1</a></sup> apparently denotes ‘covers.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201742"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201742">↑</a> iv. 5, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda. 156.</li></ol></div>

पवि|పవి|ಪವಿ|pavi|_vei_120@1743|_vei1_pn507
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1742">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1744">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn506">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn507">1:507</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn508">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pavi</b> denotes the ‘tire’ of the wheel of a chariot in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201743">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201743">2</a></sup> Reference is made<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201743">3</a></sup> to the necessity for fastening it on firmly, and the epithet <i>su-<a class="vei_120a" href="पवि">pavi</a>,</i> ‘having a good tire,’ is found in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201743">4</a></sup> with <i>su-<a class="vei_120a" href="नाभि">nābhi</a>,</i> ‘having a good nave,’ and <i>su-<a class="vei_120a" href="चक्र">cakra</a>,</i> ‘having a good wheel.’ The tires were, of course, of metal,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201743">5</a></sup> and being sharp,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201743">6</a></sup> could serve on occasion as weapons.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201743">7</a></sup> The St. Petersburg Dictionary in one passage of  the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201743">8</a></sup> takes <a class="vei_120a" href="पवि">pavi</a> to mean a metal rim on the stone for pounding <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>, but this seems improbable, because no such metal attachment is elsewhere alluded to. Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201743">9</a></sup> seems clearly right in accepting the sense of ‘sharp edge’ in this passage, especially as the stones in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201743">10</a></sup> are, in allusion to their rolling action, styled ‘rims without horses and without chariots’ (<i>anaśvāsaḥ pavayo 'rathāḥ</i>).</p><p>The <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201743">11</a></sup> ascribes to <a class="vei_120a" href="पवि">pavi</a> the sense of arrow (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शल्य">śalya</a></i>), but this is very uncertain. The St. Petersburg Dictionary cites for this use two passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201743">12</a></sup> but in one the secondary sense of sharp-edged weapon with reference to the bolt of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> is quite likely, and in the other, where the expression <i>vāṇasya <a class="vei_120a" href="पवि">pavi</a></i> occurs, the sharp-edged pounding-stone of the ‘reed,’<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201743">13</a></sup> meaning the stalk of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> plant, may be meant. Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201743">14</a></sup> thinks a reference to the shape of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> plant is intended. Pavī-<a class="vei_120a" href="नस">nasa</a>, the name of a demon mentioned in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201743">15</a></sup> seems to throw no light on this point, for while the St. Petersburg Dictionary takes it to mean ‘whose nose is like a spearhead,’ it is translated as ‘rim-nosed’ (presumably in allusion to the curved shape of the nose) by Whitney.<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1201743">16</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201743">↑</a> i. 34, 2; 88, 2; 139, 3; 166, 10, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201743">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>, ii. 7, 1, 15, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201743">↑</a> Rv. vi. 54, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201743">↑</a> Av. iv. 12, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201743">↑</a> Of gold in the case of the Aśvins and the Maruts, Rv. i. 64, 11; 180, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201743">↑</a> Rv. i. 166, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201743">↑</a> Rv. v. 52, 9. <i>Cf.</i> vi. 8, 5, and x 180, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201743">↑</a> vi. 30. <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 9, 4, 5. Mahīdhara, on the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, takes <i>pavinā</i> as <i>vajrasadṛśena,</i> ‘like a thunderbolt,’ and Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 239, 240, renders <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पवि">pavi</a></i> by ‘bolt.’</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201743">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 44.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201743">↑</a> v. 31. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201743">↑</a> xii. 30.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201743">↑</a> ix. 50, 1; x. 180, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201743">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. iv. 24, 9, where the expression <i>vāṇaṃ duhanti,</i> ‘they milk out the reed,’ occurs.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201743">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 43, 44.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201743">↑</a> viii. 6, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1201743"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1201743">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 497.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 248; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 12, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

पवित्र|పవిత్ర|ಪವಿತ್ರ|pavitra|_vei_120@1744|_vei1_pn508
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1743">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1745">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn507">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn508">1:508</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn509">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pavitra</b> denotes in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201744"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201744">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201744"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201744">2</a></sup> the sieve used for purifying the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>, the only mode of purifying it certainly<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201744"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201744">3</a></sup> known to the Rigveda. It seems clearly<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201744"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201744">4</a></sup> to have been made of  sheep's wool, whether woven or plaited is not certain, for the expressions used are too vague to be decisive, though Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201744"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201744">5</a></sup> thinks <i>hvarāṃsi</i><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201744"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201744">6</a></sup> points to plaiting.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201744"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201744">↑</a> i. 28, 9; iii. 36, 7; viii. 33, 1; 101, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201744"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201744">↑</a> Av. vi. 124, 3; ix. 6, 16; xii. 1, 30; 3, 3. 14. 25, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201744"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201744">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 239, 240.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201744"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201744">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> the names of it: <i>aṇva,</i> Rv. ix. 16, 2; <i>aṇvāni meṣyaḥ,</i> 86, 47; 107, 11; <i>avayaḥ,</i> ii. 36, 1; ix. 86, 11; 91, 2; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="त्वच्">tvac</a></i> with <i>avya</i> or <i>avyaya,</i> ix. 69, 3; 70, 7; <i>meṣyaḥ,</i> ix. 8, 5; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="रूप">rūpa</a> avyaya,</i> ix. 16, 6; <i>roman,</i> alone or with <i>avyaya; <a class="vei_120a" href="वार">vāra</a>,</i> alone or with <i>avyaya,</i> etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201744"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201744">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 278, n.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201744"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201744">↑</a> ix. 3, 2; 63, 4.</li></ol></div>

पवीर|పవీర|ಪವೀರ|pavīra|paviira|paveera|_vei_120@1745|_vei1_pn509
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1744">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1746">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn508">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn509">1:509</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn511">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pavīra,</b> according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201745"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201745">1</a></sup> denotes a ‘lance.’ The epithet derived from this word, <i>pavīravant</i> or <i>pavīrava,</i> which is found in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201745"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201745">2</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201745"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201745">3</a></sup> is used of the plough, apparently in the sense of ‘having a metal share.’ The same epithet occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201745"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201745">4</a></sup> applied to a man, in the sense of ‘having a goad’ or ‘having a spear.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201745"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201745">↑</a> xii. 30, In Rv. i. 174, 4, <i>pavīrava</i> seems to mean ‘thunderbolt.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201745"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201745">↑</a> iii. 17, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201745"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201745">↑</a> <i>Pavīravant,</i> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xii. 71; <i>pavīrava,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, 10, 2, 5, 6; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 7, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvi. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201745"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201745">↑</a> x. 60, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 116.</li></ol></div>

पवीरु|పవీరు|ಪವೀರು|pavīru|paviiru|paveeru|_vei_120@1746|_vei1_pn509
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1745">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1747">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn508">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn509">1:509</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn511">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pavīru</b> appears in a hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201746"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201746">1</a></sup> as a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रुशम">ruśama</a>,</b> being a prince or at least a wealthy noble.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201746"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201746">↑</a> viii. 51, 9 = Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxiii. 82.</li></ol></div>

पशु|పశు|ಪಶು|paśu|pashu|_vei_120@1747|_vei1_pn509
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1746">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1748">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn508">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn509">1:509</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn511">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paśu</b> means ‘animal’ generally, including man. There is frequent mention<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201747">1</a></sup> of the five sacrificial animals--the horse, the cow, the sheep, the goat, and man. Seven such domestic animals are spoken of in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201747">2</a></sup> and later;<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201747">3</a></sup> probably, as Whitney<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201747">4</a></sup> observes, merely as a sacred mystic number, not, as the commentator<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201747">5</a></sup> explains, the usual five with the ass and the camel added. Animals are also referred to as <i>ubhayadant</i>  and <i>anyatodant.</i> They are further<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201747">6</a></sup> classified as those which take hold with the hand (<i>hastādānāḥ</i>), man (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुष">puruṣa</a></i>), elephant (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="हस्तिन्">hastin</a></i>), and ape (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="मर्कट">markaṭa</a></i>), and those which grasp by the mouth (<i>mukhādānāḥ</i>). Another division is that of biped (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="द्विपाद्">dvipād</a></i>) and quadruped (<i>catuṣpād</i>).<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201747">7</a></sup> Man is a biped;<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201747">8</a></sup> he is the first (<i>prathama</i>) of the beasts;<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201747">9</a></sup> he alone of animals lives a hundred years (<i>śatāyus</i>),<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201747">10</a></sup> and he is king of the animals.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201747">11</a></sup> He possesses speech (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वाच्">vāc</a></i>) in conjunction with the other animals.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201747">12</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201747">13</a></sup> an elaborate distinction is drawn between vegetables, animals, and man in point of intellect.</p><p>Of animals apart from man a threefold division is offered in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201747">14</a></sup> --into those of the air (<i>vāyavya</i>), those of the jungle (<i>āraṇya</i>), and those of the village (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम्य">grāmya</a></i>), or tame animals. The division into <i>āraṇya</i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम्य">grāmya</a></i> animals is quite common.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201747">15</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1201747">16</a></sup> is found a division into <i><a class="vei_120a" href="एक">eka</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="शफ">śapha</a>,</i> ‘whole-hoofed’; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षुद्र">kṣudra</a>,</i> ‘small’; and <i>āraṇya,</i> ‘wild,’ the two former classes denoting the tame animals.<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1201747">16</a></sup> The horse and the ass are <i><a class="vei_120a" href="एक">eka</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="शफ">śapha</a>;</i><sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1201747">17</a></sup> the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षुद्र">kṣudra</a></i> are the sheep, the goat, and the ox: this distinction being parallel to that of <i>ubhayadant</i> and <i>anyatodant.</i><sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1201747">18</a></sup> Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1201747">19</a></sup> sees in a passage of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1201747">20</a></sup> a division of wild animals (<i>āraṇya</i>) into five classes: (1) those of the jungle described as the ‘dread beasts  which are in the wood’ (<i>mṛgā bhīmā vane hitāḥ</i>); (2) winged creatures, represented by the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हंस">haṃsa</a>,</b> ‘gander,’ <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुपर्ण">suparṇa</a>,</b> ‘eagle,’ <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शकुन">śakuna</a>,</b> ‘bird’; (3) amphibia--<b><a class="vei_120a" href="शिंशुमार">śiṃśumāra</a>,</b> ‘alligator,’ and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अजगर">ajagara</a>,</b> ‘crocodile’ (?); (4) ‘fish,’ <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरीकय">purīkaya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="जष">jaṣa</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मत्स्य">matsya</a>;</b> (5) insects and worms (described as <i>rajasāḥ</i>). But this division is more ingenious than probable, and it is ignored by both Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1201747">21</a></sup> and Whitney.<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1201747">22</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201747">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 10, 1-4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvi. 17; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 7, 17; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiii. 47-51, <i>Cf.</i> Av. xi. 2, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 10, 1-3; v. 5, 1, 1. 2; vi. 5, 10, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiv. 28-31, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201747">↑</a> Av. iii. 10, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201747">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 8, 4, 16; ix. 3, 1, 20; xii. 8, 3, 13 (where they are called <i>jāgatāḥ,</i> perhaps as numbering 12); <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 2, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201747">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 103.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201747">↑</a> On Av. iii. 10, 6. The St. Petersburg Dictionary suggests ‘mule’ and ‘ass’ as the two making up seven (<i>cf.</i> Mahābhārata, vi. 165 <i>et seq.</i>). Zimmer's view (<i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 76) is that ‘goat,’ ‘sheep,’ ‘ox,’ ‘horse,’ ‘dog,’ ‘ass,’ and ‘camel’ or ‘mule,’ are meant.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201747">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 4, 5, 7; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 7 (where <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुष">puruṣa</a></i> must be read for <i><a class="vei_120a" href="परुष">paruṣa</a></i>).</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201747">↑</a> Rv. iii. 62, 14; Av. iii. 34, 1, etc. Zimmer, 73, n., suggests that the division is Indo-European, as the Inguvenic tables make a distinction between <i>dupursus</i> and <i>peturpursus.</i></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201747">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 10, 1, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvii. 47, 48.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201747">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 2, 1, 18; vii. 5, 2, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201747">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 2, 6, 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 2, 5, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201747">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xx. 10; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 5, 5, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 18, 274.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201747">↑</a> Rv. viii. 100, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201747">↑</a> ii. 3, 2, with Keith's note.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201747">↑</a> x. 90, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201747">↑</a> Av. iii. 31, 3. <i>Cf.</i> ii. 34, 1, with Whitney's note, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 78; xi. 2, 24; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 2, 3; 9, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xiii. 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 2, 29. 32; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 1, 8; 2, 8. <i>Cf.</i> xi. 8, 3, 2, where there is reference to animals being tied up at night in their stalls.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1201747">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv, 3, 10, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiv. 30.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1201747">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 10, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiv. 30.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1201747">↑</a> Zimmer, 74.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1201747">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Av. v. 31, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 6, 3, with <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 1, 5: v. 1, 1, 3; 2, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1201747">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 77, 78.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1201747">↑</a> xi, 2, 24. 25, compared with xii. 1, 40, 51.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1201747">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 631.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1201747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1201747">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 633, 634.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 72. 77.</li></ol></div>

पशु-प|पशुप|पशु|పశు-ప|పశుప|పశు|ಪಶು-ಪ|ಪಶುಪ|ಪಶು|paśu-pa|paśupa|paśu|pashu-pa|pashupa|pashu|_vei_120@1748|_vei1_pn511
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1747">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1749">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn509">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn511">1:511</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn512">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paśu-pa</b> denotes ‘herdsman’ in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201748"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201748">1</a></sup> Metaphorically the term is applied to Pūṣan.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201748"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201748">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201748"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201748">↑</a> i. 114, 9; 144, 6; iv. 6, 4; x. 142, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201748"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201748">↑</a> vi. 58, 2. <i>Cf.</i> of Pūṣan and <a class="vei_120a" href="रेवती">revatī</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 2, 12.</li></ol></div>

पष्ठ-वाह्|पष्ठ-वाह्|पष्ठवाह्|पष्ठ|वाह्|పష్ఠ-వాహ్_౧|పష్ఠ-వాహ్|పష్ఠవాహ్|పష్ఠ|వాహ్|ಪಷ್ಠ-ವಾಹ್_೧|ಪಷ್ಠ-ವಾಹ್|ಪಷ್ಠವಾಹ್|ಪಷ್ಠ|ವಾಹ್|paṣṭha-vāh_1|paṣṭha-vāh|paṣṭhavāh|paṣṭha|vāh|paShTha-vaah_1|paShTha-vaah|paShThavaah|paShTha|vaah|_vei_120@1749|_vei1_pn511
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1748">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1750">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn509">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn511">1:511</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn512">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Paṣṭha-vāh</b> occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201749"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201749">1</a></sup> meaning an ox, four years old, according to the commentators. This qualification is, however, very doubtful, for Paṣṭhauhī, ‘cow,’ a word occurring quite frequently,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201749"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201749">2</a></sup> is in one passage<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201749"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201749">3</a></sup> accompanied by the adjective <i>prathama-garbhāḥ,</i> ‘having a first calf,’ which disproves the theory of the age adopted by the commentators.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201749"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201749">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 5, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiv. 9; xviii. 27; xxi. 17; xxiv. 13. 28. 29. etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201749"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201749">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā vii. 1, 6, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 2; xii. 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 27; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 3, 3; 8. 3, 2; ii. 7, 2, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201749"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201749">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 6, 1, 11.</li></ol></div>

पष्ठ-वाह्|पष्ठ-वाह्|पष्ठवाह्|पष्ठ|वाह्|పష్ఠ-వాహ్_౨|పష్ఠ-వాహ్|పష్ఠవాహ్|పష్ఠ|వాహ్|ಪಷ್ಠ-ವಾಹ್_೨|ಪಷ್ಠ-ವಾಹ್|ಪಷ್ಠವಾಹ್|ಪಷ್ಠ|ವಾಹ್|paṣṭha-vāh_2|paṣṭha-vāh|paṣṭhavāh|paṣṭha|vāh|paShTha-vaah_2|paShTha-vaah|paShThavaah|paShTha|vaah|_vei_120@1750|_vei1_pn511
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1749">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1751">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn509">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn511">1:511</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn512">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Paṣṭha-vāh</b> is mentioned as a seer of Sāmans in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201750"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201750">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201750"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201750">↑</a> xii. 5, 11. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 160.</li></ol></div>

पस्त्य-सद्|पस्त्यसद्|पस्त्य|सद्|పస్త్య-సద్|పస్త్యసద్|పస్త్య|సద్|ಪಸ್ತ್ಯ-ಸದ್|ಪಸ್ತ್ಯಸದ್|ಪಸ್ತ್ಯ|ಸದ್|pastya-sad|pastyasad|pastya|sad|_vei_120@1751|_vei1_pn511
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1750">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1752">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn509">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn511">1:511</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn512">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pastya-sad</b> (‘sitting in the house’) occurs in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201751"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201751">1</a></sup> where the sense seems to be ‘inmate,’ ‘companion.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201751"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201751">↑</a> vi. 51, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 211.</li></ol></div>

पस्त्या|పస్త్యా|ಪಸ್ತ್ಯಾ|pastyā|pastyaa|_vei_120@1752|_vei1_pn512
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1751">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1753">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn511">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn512">1:512</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn513">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pastyā</b> (fem. pl.) is a word occurring in several passages of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201752">1</a></sup> Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201752">2</a></sup> ascribes to it the meaning of ‘house’ or ‘dwelling,’ in the wide sense of the term, as well as that of the ‘family’ living in the house; and this view is accepted by Zimmer.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201752">3</a></sup> On the other hand, Pischel<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201752">4</a></sup> finds in two of the passages<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201752">5</a></sup> usually referred to <a class="vei_120a" href="पस्त्या">pastyā</a> the neuter <a class="vei_120a" href="पस्त्य">pastya</a>, which appears in <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पस्त्य-सद्">pastya-sad</a></b> and in <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पस्त्या-वन्त्">pastyā-vant</a></b> (where the length of the second syllable is not primitive¤), and which is certainly found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201752">6</a></sup> in the metaphorical sense of ‘dwelling,’ ascribed to it in the Naighaṇṭuka.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201752">7</a></sup> In the other passages<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201752">8</a></sup> he thinks the word means ‘rivers’ or ‘waters’; in particular, where <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> in the middle of the Pastyās<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201752">9</a></sup> is spoken of, the sees a reference to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>,</b> with its several rivers,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201752">10</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आपया">āpayā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="दृषद्वती">dṛṣadvatī</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a></b> (<i>cf.</i> 2. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पस्त्यावन्त्">pastyāvant</a></b>). In some passages<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201752">11</a></sup> he sees in <a class="vei_120a" href="पस्त्या">pastyā</a> the proper name of a stream, just as <a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धु">sindhu</a> primarily means ‘river,’ then the ‘Indus.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201752">↑</a> Rv. i. 25, 10; 40, 7; 164, 30; iv, 1, 11; vi, 49, 9; vii. 97, 5; ix, 65, 23; x. 46, 6. See also iv. 55, 3; viii. 27, 5, where <a class="vei_120a" href="पस्त्या">pastyā</a> appears as a goddess.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201752">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201752">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 149. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Über den <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>,</i> 43, n. 4; 63.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201752">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 211-22. So Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 107.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201752">↑</a> vi. 49. 9; vii. 97, 5, where the sense is ‘householder’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="गृह">gṛha</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="स्थ">stha</a></i> or <i>gṛhin,</i> as Sāyaṇa renders it).</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201752">↑</a> x. 96, 10. 11. In x. 96, 10, Roth takes <i>pastyoḥ</i> to denote the two parts of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> press, but Pischel, 2, 211, accepts Sāyaṇa's rendering ‘heaven and earth.’ In the compounds <i>tripastya</i> of <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>, Rv. viii. 39, 8; <i>vājapastya</i> of Pūṣan, vi. 58, 2, of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>, ix. 98, 12; and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वीर">vīra</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पस्त्य">pastya</a>,</i> v. 50, 4, the primitive word may very well be <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पस्त्य">pastya</a>,</i> not <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पस्त्या">pastyā</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201752">↑</a> iii. 4, misquoted by Sāyaṇa on Rv. i. 151, 2, as applying to <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पस्त्या">pastyā</a>,</i> whereas it really refers to <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पस्त्य">pastya</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201752">↑</a> Rv. i. 25, 10 = <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 16, 1 = Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 6, 2; ii. 6, 12; 7, 16; iv. 4, 6 = Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x. 27; Rv. i. 40, 7; 164, 30 (used of Agni's home); iv. 1, 11; ix. 65, 23; x. 46, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 12, 1 = Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 8 = Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201752">↑</a> Rv. ix. 65, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201752">↑</a> Rv. iii. 23, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201752">↑</a> Rv. iv. 55, 3; viii. 27, 5; and in <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पस्त्यावन्त्">pastyāvant</a></i> in ix. 97, 18.</li></ol></div>

पस्त्या-वन्त्|पस्त्या-वन्त्|पस्त्यावन्त्|पस्त्या|वन्त्|పస్త్యా-వన్త్_౧|పస్త్యా-వన్త్|పస్త్యావన్త్|పస్త్యా|వన్త్|ಪಸ್ತ್ಯಾ-ವನ್ತ್_೧|ಪಸ್ತ್ಯಾ-ವನ್ತ್|ಪಸ್ತ್ಯಾವನ್ತ್|ಪಸ್ತ್ಯಾ|ವನ್ತ್|pastyā-vant_1|pastyā-vant|pastyāvant|pastyā|vant|pastyaa-vant_1|pastyaa-vant|pastyaavant|pastyaa|_vei_120@1753|_vei1_pn512
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1752">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1754">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn511">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn512">1:512</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn513">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Pastyā-vant,</b> explained in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पद">pada</a> text as <a class="vei_120a" href="पस्त्य">pastya</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वन्त्">vant</a>, occurs in several passages of the Rigveda. In two of them<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201753"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201753">1</a></sup>  a rich householder seems meant, and in the two others reference to a ‘house’ is clear.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201753"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201753">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201753"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201753">↑</a> i. 151, 2; ix. 97, 18; but the latter passage is explained by Pischel as referring to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पस्त्या">pastyā</a>,</b> the river, and by Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> as ‘kept in the stall.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201753"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201753">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="बर्हिस्">barhis</a>,</i> Rv. ii. 11, 16, ‘of the house’; <i>kṣayān pastyāvataḥ,</i> iv. 54, 5, ‘abodes having (fixed) habitations.’</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 212.</li></ol></div>

पस्त्या-वन्त्|पस्त्या-वन्त्|पस्त्यावन्त्|पस्त्या|वन्त्|పస్త్యా-వన్త్_౨|పస్త్యా-వన్త్|పస్త్యావన్త్|పస్త్యా|వన్త్|ಪಸ್ತ್ಯಾ-ವನ್ತ್_೨|ಪಸ್ತ್ಯಾ-ವನ್ತ್|ಪಸ್ತ್ಯಾವನ್ತ್|ಪಸ್ತ್ಯಾ|ವನ್ತ್|pastyā-vant_2|pastyā-vant|pastyāvant|pastyā|vant|pastyaa-vant_2|pastyaa-vant|pastyaavant|pastyaa|_vei_120@1754|_vei1_pn513
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1753">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1755">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn512">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn513">1:513</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn514">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Pastyā-vant</b> occurs in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201754">1</a></sup> in the locative parallel with <b>Suṣoma, <a class="vei_120a" href="शर्यणावन्त्">śaryaṇāvant</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आर्जीक">ārjīka</a>.</b> It must apparently denote a place, as Pischel<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201754">2</a></sup> argues, probably corresponding to the locality ‘in the middle of the streams’ (<i>madhye pastyānām</i>), elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201754">3</a></sup> referred to as the home of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>. Pischel<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201754">4</a></sup> suggests that Patiāla is meant, though he does not lay any stress on the similarity of name. In the north of Patiāla there are hills where the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> might have grown. Roth<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201754">5</a></sup> thought that something connected with the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> press was meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201754">↑</a> viii. 7, 29.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201754">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 209.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201754">↑</a> Rv. ix. 65, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201754">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 219.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201754">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 2. Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 260, 398, 399, takes <a class="vei_120a" href="पस्त्यावन्त्">pastyāvant</a> as a place-name, but thinks that <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पस्त्या">pastyā</a></i> denotes ‘hamlet,’ or, as an epithet of Aditi, refers to her as a ‘housewife’ (Rv. iv. 55, 3; viii. 27, 5).</li></ol></div>

पांसु|పాంసు|ಪಾಂಸು|pāṃsu|paaMsu|_vei_120@1755|_vei1_pn513
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1754">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1756">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn512">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn513">1:513</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn514">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāṃsu</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201755"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201755">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201755"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201755">2</a></sup> denotes ‘dust’ or ‘sand,’ usually in the plural. Among the portents enumerated in the Adbhuta <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201755"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201755">3</a></sup> is a rain of dust or sand (<i>pāṃsuvarṣa</i>), a phenomenon not rare in India.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201755"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201755">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201755"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201755">↑</a> vii. 109, 2; xii. 1, 26.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201755"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201755">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 10, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, xii. 19, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201755"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201755">↑</a> vi. 8 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 40). <i>Cf.</i> Varāhamihira, Bṛhatsaṃhitā, xxii. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201755"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201755">↑</a> The adjective <i>pāṃsura</i> is found in Rv. i. 22, 17, with a variant, <i>pāṃsula,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>, i. 3, 1, 3, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 5, 1, 9.</li></ol></div>

पाक-दूर्वा|पाकदूर्वा|पाक|दूर्वा|పాక-దూర్వా|పాకదూర్వా|పాక|దూర్వా|ಪಾಕ-ದೂರ್ವಾ|ಪಾಕದೂರ್ವಾ|ಪಾಕ|ದೂರ್ವಾ|pāka-dūrvā|pākadūrvā|pāka|dūrvā|paaka-duurvaa|paakaduurvaa|paaka|duurvaa|paaka-doorvaa|paakadoorvaa|doorvaa|_vei_120@1756|_vei1_pn513
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1755">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1757">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn512">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn513">1:513</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn514">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāka-dūrvā</b> is, in a verse of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201756"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201756">1</a></sup> included with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कियाम्बु">kiyāmbu</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्यल्कशा">vyalkaśā</a></b> among the plants used for growing on the spot where the corpse of the dead man has been consumed with fire.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201756"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201756">2</a></sup> The verse is repeated in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201756"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201756">3</a></sup>  with the variant Kyāmbu. In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201756"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201756">4</a></sup> the word is read Śāṇḍadūrvā. <a class="vei_120a" href="पाकदूर्वा">pākadūrvā</a> is probably, as Sāyaṇa-understands it, <i>paripakva-<a class="vei_120a" href="दूर्वा">dūrvā</a>,</i> ‘ripe or edible millet.’ Śāṇḍadūrvā is explained by the commentator<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201756"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201756">5</a></sup> in various ways, as millet ‘having egg-shaped roots’ (<i>i.e., sāṇḍa,</i> not <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्ड">śāṇḍa</a></i>), or as ‘having long joints,’ with the additional remark that it was called <i>bṛhaddūrvā,</i> ‘large millet.’ In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, on the other hand, the commentary explains <a class="vei_120a" href="पाकदूर्वा">pākadūrvā</a> as small millet.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201756"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201756">↑</a> x. 16, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201756"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201756">↑</a> See Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 11, 342-350; <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, xxxix.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201756"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201756">↑</a> vi. 4, 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201756"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201756">↑</a> xviii. 3, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201756"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201756">↑</a> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 850. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 70.</li></ol></div>

पाक-स्थामन् कौरयाण|पाकस्थामन् कौरयाण|पाक|स्थामन् कौरयाण|పాక-స్థామన్ కౌరయాణ|పాకస్థామన్ కౌరయాణ|పాక|స్థామన్ కౌరయాణ|ಪಾಕ-ಸ್ಥಾಮನ್ ಕೌರಯಾಣ|ಪಾಕಸ್ಥಾಮನ್ ಕೌರಯಾಣ|ಪಾಕ|ಸ್ಥಾಮನ್ ಕೌರಯಾಣ|pāka-sthāman kaurayāṇa|pākasthāman kaurayāṇa|pāka|sthāman kaurayāṇa|paaka-sthaaman kaurayaaNa|paakasthaaman kaurayaaNa|paaka|sthaaman kaurayaaNa|_vei_120@1757|_vei1_pn514
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1756">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1758">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn513">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn514">1:514</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn515">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāka-sthāman Kaurayāṇa</b> is celebrated as a generous donor  in a hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201757">1</a></sup> Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201757">2</a></sup> suggests, without much reason, that he may have been a king of the <b>Anus.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201757">↑</a> viii. 3, 21. 24.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201757">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 160.</li></ol></div>

पाकरु|పాకరు|ಪಾಕರು|pākaru|paakaru|_vei_120@1758|_vei1_pn514
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1757">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1759">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn513">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn514">1:514</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn515">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pākaru</b> is mentioned as a disease, together with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विषूचिका">viṣūcikā</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अर्शस्">arśas</a>,</b> ‘hæmorrhoids,’ in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201758"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201758">1</a></sup> Its nature is unknown; the etymology<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201758"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201758">2</a></sup> points to the sense of ‘developed sores,’ ‘ulcers.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201758"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201758">↑</a> xii. 97 <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 393.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201758"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201758">↑</a> From <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पाक">pāka</a>,</i> ‘maturity,’ and <i>aru = arus,</i> ‘sore.’</li></ol></div>

पांक्त्र|पाङ्क्त्र|పాంక్త్ర|పాఙ్క్త్ర|ಪಾಂಕ್ತ್ರ|ಪಾಙ್ಕ್ತ್ರ|pāṅktra|paaMktra|paanktra|_vei_120@1759|_vei1_pn514
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1758">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1760">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn513">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn514">1:514</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn515">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāṅktra</b> is the name of an animal mentioned in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, or ‘horse-sacrifice,’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201759"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201759">1</a></sup> The ‘field-rat’ seems to be meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201759"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201759">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 7; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 26. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 85.</li></ol></div>

पाञ्च-जन्य|पाञ्चजन्य|पाञ्च|जन्य|పాఞ్చ-జన్య|పాఞ్చజన్య|పాఞ్చ|జన్య|ಪಾಞ್ಚ-ಜನ್ಯ|ಪಾಞ್ಚಜನ್ಯ|ಪಾಞ್ಚ|ಜನ್ಯ|pāñca-janya|pāñcajanya|pāñca|janya|paa~ncha-janya|paa~nchajanya|paa~ncha|_vei_120@1760|_vei1_pn514
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1759">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1761">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn513">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn514">1:514</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn515">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāñca-janya,</b> ‘relating to the five peoples.’ See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चजनाः">pañcajanāḥ</a>.</b></p></div>

पाञ्चाल|పాఞ్చాల|ಪಾಞ್ಚಾಲ|pāñcāla|paa~nchaala|_vei_120@1761|_vei1_pn514
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1760">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1762">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn513">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn514">1:514</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn515">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāñcāla</b> means a ‘king of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a> people,’ and is applied to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दुर्मुख">durmukha</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201761"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201761">1</a></sup> and to <b>Śoṇa</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201761"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201761">2</a></sup> The term is also found in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201761"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201761">3</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201761"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201761">↑</a> viii. 23.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201761"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201761">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201761"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201761">↑</a> iii. 29, 1. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Anukramaṇī in <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3 460.</li></ol></div>

पाञ्चि|పాఞ్చి|ಪಾಞ್ಚಿ|pāñci|paa~nchi|_vei_120@1762|_vei1_pn515
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1761">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1763">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn514">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn515">1:515</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn516">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāñci,</b> ‘descendant of Pañcan,’ is the name of a teacher mentioned with disapproval in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201762"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201762">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201762"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201762">↑</a> i. 2, 5, 9; ii. 1, 4, 27. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 434.</li></ol></div>

पाटव|పాటవ|ಪಾಟವ|pāṭava|paaTava|_vei_120@1763|_vei1_pn515
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1762">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1764">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn514">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn515">1:515</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn516">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāṭava,</b> ‘descendant of Paṭu,’ is a patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चाक्र">cākra</a></b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xii. 8, 1, 17; 9, 3, 1).</p></div>

पाटा|పాటా|ಪಾಟಾ|pāṭā|paaTaa|_vei_120@1764|_vei1_pn515
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1763">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1765">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn514">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn515">1:515</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn516">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāṭā</b> is mentioned in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201764"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201764">1</a></sup> and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201764"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201764">2</a></sup> It is assumed by the commentator to be identical with the later Pāṭhā, the plant <i>Clypea hernandifolia,</i> which was much used medicinally, and is still so used at the present day according to Roth.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201764"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201764">3</a></sup> Very possibly the word should be read as Pāṭhā.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201764"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201764">↑</a> ii. 27, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201764"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201764">↑</a> xxxvii. 1; xxxviii. 18. <i>Cf.</i> Ṛgvidhāna, iv. 12, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201764"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201764">↑</a> Quoted in Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 68. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 190; 17, 266; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 305, n. 1; <i>Proceedings of the American Oriental Society,</i> May, 1885, xlii-xliv.</li></ol></div>

पाणि-घ्न|पाणिघ्न|पाणि|घ्न|పాణి-ఘ్న|పాణిఘ్న|పాణి|ఘ్న|ಪಾಣಿ-ಘ್ನ|ಪಾಣಿಘ್ನ|ಪಾಣಿ|ಘ್ನ|pāṇi-ghna|pāṇighna|pāṇi|ghna|paaNi-ghna|paaNighna|paaNi|_vei_120@1765|_vei1_pn515
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1764">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1766">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn514">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn515">1:515</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn516">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāṇi-ghna,</b> ‘hand-clapper,’ is enumerated among the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201765"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201765">1</a></sup> Presumably a man who drives away birds from the fields by making a noise is intended.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201765"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201765">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 20; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 15, 1.</li></ol></div>

पाण्ड्व|పాణ్డ్వ|ಪಾಣ್ಡ್ವ|pāṇḍva|paaNDva|_vei_120@1766|_vei1_pn515
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1765">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1767">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn514">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn515">1:515</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn516">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāṇḍva</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (v. 3, 5, 21) denotes an uncoloured woollen garment.</p></div>

पातल्य|పాతల్య|ಪಾತಲ್ಯ|pātalya|paatalya|_vei_120@1767|_vei1_pn515
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1766">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1768">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn514">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn515">1:515</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn516">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pātalya</b> is found in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201767"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201767">1</a></sup> meaning some part of the chariot. What it was is quite uncertain. Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201767"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201767">2</a></sup> suggests that, as in the Epic, it was possibly a piece of wood on the axle to hold the pole of the car. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201767"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201767">↑</a> iii. 53, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201767"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201767">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Socicty,</i> 13, 242, 243; 20, 224. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 251; Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 108.</li></ol></div>

पात्र|పాత్ర|ಪಾತ್ರ|pātra|paatra|_vei_120@1768|_vei1_pn516
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1767">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1769">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn515">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn516">1:516</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn517">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pātra,</b> primarily a ‘drinking vessel’ (from <i>pā,</i> ‘to drink’) denotes a vessel generally both in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201768"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201768">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201768"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201768">2</a></sup> It was made either of wood<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201768"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201768">3</a></sup> or clay.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201768"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201768">4</a></sup> In some passages<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201768"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201768">5</a></sup> the word is, according to Roth, used to indicate a measure. The feminine Pātrī occasionally occurs<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201768"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201768">6</a></sup> in the sense of ‘vessel.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201768"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201768">↑</a> i. 82, 4; 110, 5; 162, 13 (to hold the broth from the flesh of the horse); 175, 1; ii. 37, 4; vi. 27, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201768"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201768">↑</a> Av. iv. 17, 4; vi. 142, 1; ix. 6, 17; xii. 3, 25. 36; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 1, 6, 2; vi. 3, 4, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 62; xix. 86. etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201768"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201768">↑</a> Rv. i. 175, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201768"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201768">↑</a> Av. iv. 17, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201768"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201768">↑</a> Av. x. 10, 9; xii. 3, 30; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 1, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> Śūtra, xvi. 1, 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201768"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201768">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 17; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 2, 8; ii. 5, 3, 6; 6, 2, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, v. 8, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 271.</li></ol></div>

पाथ्य|పాథ్య|ಪಾಥ್ಯ|pāthya|paathya|_vei_120@1769|_vei1_pn516
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1768">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1770">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn515">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn516">1:516</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn517">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāthya,</b> a word occurring only once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201769"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201769">1</a></sup> is either an epithet meaning ‘being in heaven’ (<i>pāthas</i>), or a patronymic, as Sāyaṇa interprets it, of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वृषन्">vṛṣan</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201769"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201769">↑</a> vi. 16, 15. <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 4, 2, 4; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 153.</li></ol></div>

पाद|पाद|పాద_౧|పాద|ಪಾದ_೧|ಪಾದ|pāda_1|pāda|paada_1|paada|_vei_120@1770|_vei1_pn516
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1769">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1771">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn515">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn516">1:516</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn517">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Pāda</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201770"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201770">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201770"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201770">2</a></sup> denotes the ‘foot’ of an animal, a bird, and other creatures.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201770"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201770">↑</a> xiv. 1, 60.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201770"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201770">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 5. 12; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 8, 3, 6, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 5.</li></ol></div>

पाद|पाद|పాద_౨|పాద|ಪಾದ_೨|ಪಾದ|pāda_2|pāda|paada_2|paada|_vei_120@1771|_vei1_pn516
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1770">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1772">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn515">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn516">1:516</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn517">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Pāda,</b> as a measure of length, denotes ‘foot’ in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201771">1</a></sup> The term is occasionally<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201771">2</a></sup> used to express a measure of weight. As a fraction it means a ‘quarter,’ a sense derived from that of ‘foot’ of a quadruped (just as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शफ">śapha</a>,</i> the divided hoof, comes to mean an ‘eighth’).</p><p>^3) Rv. x. 90, 3. 4.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201771">↑</a> vi. 5, 3, 2; vii. 2, 1, 7; viii. 7, 2, 17; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, vi. 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201771">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 7; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 1, 2.</li></ol></div>

पाद|पाद|పాద_౩|పాద|ಪಾದ_೩|ಪಾದ|pāda_3|pāda|paada_3|paada|_vei_120@1772|_vei1_pn516
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1771">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1773">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn515">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn516">1:516</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn517">↷</a><hr><p>3. <b>Pāda</b> is the regular expression for a ‘quarter verse’ in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201772"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201772">1</a></sup> This sense is merely a limitation of ‘quarter’ = the ‘foot’ of a quadruped </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201772"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201772">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 4; Kauṣīaki <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxvi. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vii. 9; xi. 6; Lāṭyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 2, 1; x. 6, 9, etc.</li></ol></div>

पान|పాన|ಪಾನ|pāna|paana|_vei_120@1773|_vei1_pn517
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1772">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1774">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn516">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn517">1:517</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn518">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāna,</b> ‘drink,’ occurs in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201773"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201773">1</a></sup> and the Upaniṣads.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201773"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201773">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201773"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201773">↑</a> xiii. 4, 2, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201773"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201773">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1, 43; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, viii. 2, 7, etc.</li></ol></div>

पान्त|పాన్త|ಪಾನ್ತ|pānta|paanta|_vei_120@1774|_vei1_pn517
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1773">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1775">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn516">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn517">1:517</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn518">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pānta</b> occurs several times in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201774"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201774">1</a></sup> apparently<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201774"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201774">2</a></sup> meaning ‘drink,’ ‘beverage’ (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पान">pāna</a></b>). Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201774"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201774">3</a></sup> however, thinks that in one passage^4 <a class="vei_120a" href="पान्त">pānta</a> is the name of a prince.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201774"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201774">↑</a> i. 122, 1; 155, 1; viii. 92, 1; ix. 65, 28 (a very doubtful passage); x. 88, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201774"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201774">↑</a> So <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vii. 25; Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 122, 123.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201774"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201774">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 139; <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 108.</li></ol></div>

पान्-नेजन|पान्नेजन|पान्|नेजन|పాన్-నేజన|పాన్నేజన|పాన్|నేజన|ಪಾನ್-ನೇಜನ|ಪಾನ್ನೇಜನ|ಪಾನ್|ನೇಜನ|pān-nejana|pānnejana|pān|nejana|paan-nejana|paannejana|paan|_vei_120@1775|_vei1_pn517
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1774">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1776">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn516">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn517">1:517</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn518">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pān-nejana</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201775"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201775">1</a></sup> denotes a ‘vessel for washing the feet.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201775"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201775">↑</a> iii. 8, 2, 1; 9, 3, 27; xiii. 5, 2, 1.</li></ol></div>

पाप-यक्ष्म|पापयक्ष्म|पाप|यक्ष्म|పాప-యక్ష్మ|పాపయక్ష్మ|పాప|యక్ష్మ|ಪಾಪ-ಯಕ್ಷ್ಮ|ಪಾಪಯಕ್ಷ್ಮ|ಪಾಪ|ಯಕ್ಷ್ಮ|pāpa-yakṣma|pāpayakṣma|pāpa|yakṣma|paapa-yakShma|paapayakShma|paapa|yakShma|paapa-yaxma|paapayaxma|yaxma|_vei_120@1776|_vei1_pn517
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1775">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1777">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn516">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn517">1:517</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn518">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāpa-yakṣma.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यक्ष्म">yakṣma</a>.</b></p></div>

पाप-सम|पापसम|पाप|सम|పాప-సమ|పాపసమ|పాప|సమ|ಪಾಪ-ಸಮ|ಪಾಪಸಮ|ಪಾಪ|ಸಮ|pāpa-sama|pāpasama|pāpa|sama|paapa-sama|paapasama|paapa|_vei_120@1777|_vei1_pn517
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1776">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1778">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn516">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn517">1:517</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn518">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāpa-sama,</b> a ‘bad season,’ is in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201777"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201777">1</a></sup> opposed to Puṇya-<a class="vei_120a" href="सम">sama</a>, a ‘good season.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201777"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201777">↑</a> iii. 3, 8, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 342.</li></ol></div>

पामन्|పామన్|ಪಾಮನ್|pāman|paaman|_vei_120@1778|_vei1_pn517
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1777">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1779">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn516">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn517">1:517</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn518">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāman</b> occurs in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201778"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201778">1</a></sup> as the name of a skin disease. The derivative adjective, Pāmana, ‘suffering from skin disease,’ is found in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201778"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201778">2</a></sup> Since it is mentioned as an accompaniment of fever, probably a cutaneous eruption or scab consequent on fever is meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201778"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201778">↑</a> v. 22, 12. <i>Cf.</i> for the reading, Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 261. See also Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201778"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201778">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 3, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxiii. 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 1, 31.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Grohmann, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 401 <i>et seq.;</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 388; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 421, n.; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 450, 451; <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 63.</li></ol></div>

पायु|पायु|పాయు_౧|పాయు|ಪಾಯು_೧|ಪಾಯು|pāyu_1|pāyu|paayu_1|paayu|_vei_120@1779|_vei1_pn517
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1778">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1780">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn516">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn517">1:517</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn518">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Pāyu,</b> meaning ‘guard,’ ‘protector,’ occurs several times in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201779"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201779">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201779"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201779">↑</a> i. 147, 3; ii. 1, 7; iv. 2, 6; 4, 3. 12; vi. 15, 8; viii. 18, 2; 60, 19; x, 100, 9.</li></ol></div>

पायु|पायु|పాయు_౨|పాయు|ಪಾಯು_೨|ಪಾಯು|pāyu_2|pāyu|paayu_2|paayu|_vei_120@1780|_vei1_pn518
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1779">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1781">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn517">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn518">1:518</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn519">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Pāyu</b> is found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201780">1</a></sup> as the name of a poet, a <a class="vei_120a" href="भारद्वाज">bhāradvāja</a>. In the Bṛhaddevatā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201780">2</a></sup> he is credited with assisting <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अभ्यावर्तिन् चायमान">abhyāvartin cāyamāna</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रस्तोक">prastoka</a> Sārṅjaya</b> by consecrating their weapons with a hymn.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201780">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201780">↑</a> vi. 47, 24. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 128.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201780">↑</a> v. 124 <i>et seq.,</i> with Macdonell's notes.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201780">↑</a> vi. 75 (the ‘battle’ hymn).</li></ol></div>

पार|పార|ಪಾರ|pāra|paara|_vei_120@1781|_vei1_pn518
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1780">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1782">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn517">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn518">1:518</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn519">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāra,</b> in accordance with its derivation (<i>pṛ,</i> ‘bring across’), denotes the ‘farther bank’ of a river or stream, in which sense<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201781"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201781">1</a></sup> it occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201781"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201781">2</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201781"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201781">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201781"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201781">↑</a> It also often has the generalized sense of ‘extreme limit’ or ‘end,’ as in i. 92, 6 (<i>tamasas,</i> ‘of darkness’); v. 54, 10 (<i>adhavanaḥ,</i> ‘of a road’).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201781"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201781">↑</a> i. 121, 13 (<i>nāvyānām,</i> ‘of streams’); viii. 96, 11 (<i>nadīnām</i>); i. 167, 2 (<i>samudrasya</i>); x. 155, 3 (<i>sindhoḥ</i>), etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201781"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201781">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 1, 2. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxiii. 5; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 6, 2, 4 (<i>salilasya</i>); <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 21 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पार">pāra</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="काम">kāma</a>,</i> ‘desiring the farther bank’), etc.</li></ol></div>

पारशव्य|పారశవ్య|ಪಾರಶವ್ಯ|pāraśavya|paarashavya|_vei_120@1782|_vei1_pn518
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1781">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1783">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn517">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn518">1:518</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn519">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāraśavya,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="परशु">paraśu</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तिरिन्दिर">tirindira</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> Śūtra (xvi. 11, 20). <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्शु">parśu</a>.</b></p></div>

पारावत|पारावत|పారావత_౧|పారావత|ಪಾರಾವತ_೧|ಪಾರಾವತ|pārāvata_1|pārāvata|paaraavata_1|paaraavata|_vei_120@1783|_vei1_pn518
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1782">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1784">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn517">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn518">1:518</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn519">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Pārāvata</b> occurs in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’), in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201783"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201783">1</a></sup> meaning ‘turtle-dove.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201783"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201783">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 6; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 25.</li></ol></div>

पारावत|पारावत|పారావత_౨|పారావత|ಪಾರಾವತ_೨|ಪಾರಾವತ|pārāvata_2|pārāvata|paaraavata_2|paaraavata|_vei_120@1784|_vei1_pn518
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1783">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1785">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn517">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn518">1:518</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn519">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Pārāvata</b> occurs in several passages of the Rigveda. Roth<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201784">1</a></sup> thinks that in most places<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201784">2</a></sup> it means ‘coming from a distance,’ but in two passages<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201784">3</a></sup> he regards it as the proper name of a people on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a></b> (Jumna). It is certain that in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> the Pārāvatas are a people on that river (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुरश्रवस्">turaśravas</a></b>). Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201784">4</a></sup> sees in all the passages<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201784">5</a></sup> the name of a people, comparing the  of  Ptolemy,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201784">6</a></sup> who apparently were settled on the northern border of Gedrosia, or the , who were found in <sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201784">7</a></sup> He suggests that they were originally ‘mountaineers’ (<i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्वत">parvata</a></b>). Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201784">8</a></sup> holds a similar view, and Geldner<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201784">9</a></sup> recognizes a people as meant. The mention of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a></b> in connexion with the Pārāvatas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201784">2</a></sup> in the Rigveda accords generally with their position on the <a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201784">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201784">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201784">↑</a> Rv. v. 52, 11; viii. 100, 6; Av. xx. 135, 14; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पारावत">pārāvata</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="घ्नी">ghnī</a></i> of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>, Rv. vi. 61, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201784">↑</a> Rv. viii. 34, 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 4, 11. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 53.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201784">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 97 <i>et seq.;</i> 3, 310, following Brunnhofer, <i>Iran und Turan,</i> 99.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201784">↑</a> See notes 2 and 3.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201784">↑</a> vi. 20, 3. It is suggested by Hillebrandt that the  of Herodotus, iii. 91, may be the same.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201784">↑</a> Ptolemy, vi. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201784">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 162. 197.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201784">↑</a> <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 109.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 91; Max Mūller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 316.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201784">↑</a> Rv. v. 52, 11; viii. 100, 6; Av. xx. 135, 14; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पारावत">pārāvata</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="घ्नी">ghnī</a></i> of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>, Rv. vi. 61, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201784">↑</a> Rv. viii. 34, 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 4, 11. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 53.</li></ol></div>

पाराशरी-कौण्डिनी-पुत्र|पाराशरी-कौण्डिनीपुत्र|पाराशरी-कौण्डिनी|पाराशरी-कौण्डिनि|पुत्र|పారాశరీ-కౌణ్డినీ-పుత్ర|పారాశరీ-కౌణ్డినీపుత్ర|పారాశరీ-కౌణ్డినీ|పారాశరీ-కౌణ్డిని|పుత్ర|ಪಾರಾಶರೀ-ಕೌಣ್ಡಿನೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಪಾರಾಶರೀ-ಕೌಣ್ಡಿನೀಪುತ್ರ|ಪಾರಾಶರೀ-ಕೌಣ್ಡಿನೀ|ಪಾರಾಶರೀ-ಕೌಣ್ಡಿನಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|pārāśarī-kauṇḍinī-putra|pārāśarī-kauṇḍinīputra|pārāśarī-kauṇḍinī|pārāśarī-kauṇḍini|putra|paaraasharii-kauNDinii-putra|paaraasharii-kauNDiniiputra|paaraasharii-kauNDinii|paaraasharii-kauNDini|paaraasharee-kauNDinee-putra|paaraasharee-kauNDineeputra|paaraasharee-kauNDinee|paaraasharee-kauNDini|_vei_120@1785|_vei1_pn519
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1784">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1786">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn518">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn519">1:519</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn520">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pārāśarī-<a class="vei_120a" href="कौण्डिनी">kauṇḍinī</a>-putra</b> is mentioned in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Bṛhādāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 4, 30), in the Mādhyaṃdina recension, as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गार्गीपुत्र">gārgīputra</a>.</b></p></div>

पाराशरी-पुत्र|पाराशरीपुत्र|पाराशरी|पाराशरि|पुत्र|పారాశరీ-పుత్ర|పారాశరీపుత్ర|పారాశరీ|పారాశరి|పుత్ర|ಪಾರಾಶರೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಪಾರಾಶರೀಪುತ್ರ|ಪಾರಾಶರೀ|ಪಾರಾಶರಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|pārāśarī-putra|pārāśarīputra|pārāśarī|pārāśari|putra|paaraasharii-putra|paaraashariiputra|paaraasharii|paaraashari|paaraasharee-putra|paaraashareeputra|paaraasharee|_vei_120@1786|_vei1_pn519
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1785">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1787">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn518">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn519">1:519</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn520">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pārāśarī-putra,</b> ‘son of a female descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पराशर">parāśara</a>,</b>’ is mentioned in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कात्यायनीपुत्र">kātyāyanīputra</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201786"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201786">1</a></sup> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="औपस्वतीपुत्र">aupasvatīputra</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1201786"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1201786">1b</a></sup> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वात्सीपुत्र">vātsīputra</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2b_vei_1201786"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2b_vei_1201786">2b</a></sup> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वार्कारुणीपुत्र">vārkāruṇīputra</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_3b_vei_1201786"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3b_vei_1201786">3b</a></sup> and of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गार्गीपुत्र">gārgīputra</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_4b_vei_1201786"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4b_vei_1201786">4b</a></sup> Different men are no doubt meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201786"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201786">↑</a> vi. 5, 1, <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1201786"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1201786">↑</a> vi. 5, 1, <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_2b_vei_1201786"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2b_vei_1201786">↑</a> vi. 5, 2, <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_3b_vei_1201786"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3b_vei_1201786">↑</a> vi. 4, 31, Mādhyaṃdina.</li><li id="fx_120_4b_vei_1201786"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4b_vei_1201786">↑</a> vi. 4, 30, Mādhyaṃdina.</li></ol></div>

पाराशर्य|పారాశర్య|ಪಾರಾಶರ್ಯ|pārāśarya|paaraasharya|_vei_120@1787|_vei1_pn519
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1786">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1788">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn518">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn519">1:519</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn520">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pārāśarya,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पराशर">parāśara</a>,</b>’ is mentioned in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जातूकर्ण्य">jātūkarṇya</a></b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201787"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201787">1</a></sup> or of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भारद्वाज">bhāradvāja</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201787"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201787">2</a></sup> A <a class="vei_120a" href="पाराशर्य">pārāśarya</a> is also mentioned as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बैजवापायन">baijavāpāyana</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201787"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201787">3</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्यास पाराशर्य">vyāsa pārāśarya</a></b> is the pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विष्वक्सेन">viṣvaksena</a></b> according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> at the end of the Sāmavidhāna <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201787"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201787">4</a></sup> See also <b>Aṣāḍha, <a class="vei_120a" href="जयन्त">jayanta</a>, Vipaścit, Sudatta.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201787"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201787">↑</a> ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3, <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>; ii. 5, 21; iv. 5, 27, Mādhyaṃdina.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201787"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201787">↑</a> ii. 5, 20; iv. 5, 26, Mādhyaṃdina; ii. 6, 2; iv. 6, 2, 3. <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201787"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201787">↑</a> ii. 6, 2, <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 9, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201787"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201787">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 41. 1.</li></ol></div>

पाराशर्यायण|పారాశర్యాయణ|ಪಾರಾಶರ್ಯಾಯಣ|pārāśaryāyaṇa|paaraasharyaayaNa|_vei_120@1788|_vei1_pn520
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1787">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1789">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn519">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn520">1:520</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn521">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pārāśaryāyaṇa</b> is mentioned in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201788"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201788">1</a></sup> as a pupil of <a class="vei_120a" href="पाराशर्य">pārāśarya</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201788"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201788">↑</a> ii. 5, 21; iv. 5, 27 (Mādhyaṃdina = ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3, <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

पारिकुट|పారికుట|ಪಾರಿಕುಟ|pārikuṭa|paarikuTa|_vei_120@1789|_vei1_pn520
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1788">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1790">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn519">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn520">1:520</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn521">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pārikuṭa</b> is an obscure word--probably corrupt--occurring in a verse cited in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 22, 7), and apparently meaning ‘attendant.’</p></div>

पारिक्षित|పారిక్షిత|ಪಾರಿಕ್ಷಿತ|pārikṣita|paarikShita|paarixita|_vei_120@1790|_vei1_pn520
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1789">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1791">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn519">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn520">1:520</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn521">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pārikṣita,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परिक्षित्">parikṣit</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनमेजय">janamejaya</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201790">1</a></sup> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201790">2</a></sup> The Pārikṣitīya appear in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201790">3</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201790">4</a></sup> as performers of the horse sacrifice. In a <a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a> there cited they are called Pārikṣitas. Apparently they were the brothers of <a class="vei_120a" href="जनमेजय">janamejaya</a>, named <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उग्रसेन">ugrasena</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="भीमसेन">bhīmasena</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्रुतसेन">śrutasena</a>.</b> In the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201790">5</a></sup> the question whither they have gone is made the subject of a philosophical discussion. It is clear that the family had passed away before the time of the <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, and it is also clear that there had been some serious scandal mingled with their greatness which they had, in the opinion of the Brahmins, atoned for by their horse sacrifice with its boundless gifts to the priests. Weber<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201790">6</a></sup> sees in this the germ of the Epic stories which are recorded in the Mahābhārata.</p><p>The verses relating to <a class="vei_120a" href="परिक्षित्">parikṣit</a> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201790">7</a></sup> are called Pārikṣityaḥ in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201790">8</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201790">↑</a> vii. 27 and 34; viii. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201790">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 6; ii. 6, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201790">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201790">↑</a> xvi. 9. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201790">↑</a> iii. 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201790">↑</a> <i>Indian Literature,</i> 125, 126; 135, 136. The later legend of the Pārikṣitas and Vāmadeva's mares is dealt with by Weber in <i>Vedische Beiträge</i> (1894).</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201790">↑</a> xx. 127, 7-10; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 17; Scheftelowitz, <i>Die Apokryphen des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 156, 157.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201790">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 32, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxx. 5; Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 12; Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 136, n. 144.</li></ol></div>

पारि-प्लव|पारिप्लव|पारि|प्लव|పారి-ప్లవ|పారిప్లవ|పారి|ప్లవ|ಪಾರಿ-ಪ್ಲವ|ಪಾರಿಪ್ಲವ|ಪಾರಿ|ಪ್ಲವ|pāri-plava|pāriplava|pāri|plava|paari-plava|paariplava|paari|_vei_120@1791|_vei1_pn520
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1790">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1792">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn519">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn520">1:520</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn521">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāri-plava,</b> ‘cyclic,’ is a term applied to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आख्यान">ākhyāna</a>,</b> or ‘tale,’ which is to be recited at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse  sacrifice’), and to be repeated at intervals throughout the year. It is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201791">1</a></sup> and in the Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201791">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201791">↑</a> xiii. 4, 3, 2. 15.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201791">↑</a> Āśvalāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 1, 26; 2, 36; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ix. 9, 11.</li></ol></div>

पारी-णह्य|पारीणह्य|पारी|पारि|णह्य|పారీ-ణహ్య|పారీణహ్య|పారీ|పారి|ణహ్య|ಪಾರೀ-ಣಹ್ಯ|ಪಾರೀಣಹ್ಯ|ಪಾರೀ|ಪಾರಿ|ಣಹ್ಯ|pārī-ṇahya|pārīṇahya|pārī|pāri|ṇahya|paarii-Nahya|paariiNahya|paarii|paari|Nahya|paaree-Nahya|paareeNahya|paaree|_vei_120@1792|_vei1_pn521
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1791">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1793">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn520">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn521">1:521</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn522">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pārī-ṇahya</b> denotes household utensils' in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201792">1</a></sup> where it is said that the wife (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पत्नी">patnī</a></i>), as mistress of the house, has charge of all these.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201792">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201792">↑</a> vi. 2, 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201792">↑</a> The word occurs <b>later</b> in the variant form of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पारि">pāri</a>-ṇāhya</i> in <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>, ix. 11.</li></ol></div>

पारूष्ण|పారూష్ణ|ಪಾರೂಷ್ಣ|pārūṣṇa|paaruuShNa|paarooShNa|_vei_120@1793|_vei1_pn521
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1792">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1794">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn520">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn521">1:521</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn522">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pārūṣṇa,</b> occurring in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201793"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201793">1</a></sup> appears to mean some kind of bird.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201793"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201793">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 24.</li></ol></div>

पारोवर्य-विद्|पारोवर्यविद्|पारोवर्य|विद्|పారోవర్య-విద్|పారోవర్యవిద్|పారోవర్య|విద్|ಪಾರೋವರ್ಯ-ವಿದ್|ಪಾರೋವರ್ಯವಿದ್|ಪಾರೋವರ್ಯ|ವಿದ್|pārovarya-vid|pārovaryavid|pārovarya|vid|paarovarya-vid|paarovaryavid|paarovarya|_vei_120@1794|_vei1_pn521
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1793">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1795">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn520">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn521">1:521</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn522">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pārovarya-vid</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> (xiii. 12) denotes ‘knowers of tradition.’</p></div>

पार्ण-वल्कि|पार्णवल्कि|पार्ण|वल्कि|పార్ణ-వల్కి|పార్ణవల్కి|పార్ణ|వల్కి|ಪಾರ್ಣ-ವಲ್ಕಿ|ಪಾರ್ಣವಲ್ಕಿ|ಪಾರ್ಣ|ವಲ್ಕಿ|pārṇa-valki|pārṇavalki|pārṇa|valki|paarNa-valki|paarNavalki|paarNa|_vei_120@1795|_vei1_pn521
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1794">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1796">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn520">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn521">1:521</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn522">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pārṇa-valki,</b> ‘descendant of Parṇavalka,’ is the patronymic of <b>Nigada</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201795">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201795">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372; Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 443.</li></ol></div>

पार्थव|పార్థవ|ಪಾರ್ಥವ|pārthava|paarthava|_vei_120@1796|_vei1_pn521
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1795">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1797">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn520">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn521">1:521</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn522">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pārthava,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पृथु">pṛthu</a>,</b>’ occurs once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201796">1</a></sup> where the Pārthavas are mentioned as generous donors. The passage is somewhat obscure, as there is a reference<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201796">2</a></sup> to a defeat of the <b>Turvaśas</b> and the <b>Vṛcīvants</b> by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सृञ्जय">sṛñjaya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दैववात">daivavāta</a>,</b> followed in the next verse by the praise of the bounty to the singer of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अभ्यावर्तिन् चायमान">abhyāvartin cāyamāna</a>,</b> who was clearly a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पार्थव">pārthava</a>,</b> and who, in the earlier part of the hymn, has been referred to as victorious over <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वरशिख">varaśikha</a>.</b> It is uncertain whether, as Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201796">3</a></sup> suggests, the two princes, Abhyāvartin  <a class="vei_120a" href="चायमान">cāyamāna</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="सृञ्जय">sṛñjaya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दैववात">daivavāta</a>, are identical or not.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201796">4</a></sup> That <a class="vei_120a" href="पार्थव">pārthava</a> has any direct connexion with the Parthians, as held by Brunnhofer, is most improbable.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201796">5</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्शु">parśu</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201796">↑</a> vi. 27, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201796">↑</a> vi. 27, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201796">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 133, 134.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201796">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 105.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201796">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Ludwing, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 196 <i>et seq.</i> Herodotos, iii. 93, mentions the .</li></ol></div>

पार्थ-श्रवस|पार्थश्रवस|पार्थ|श्रवस|పార్థ-శ్రవస|పార్థశ్రవస|పార్థ|శ్రవస|ಪಾರ್ಥ-ಶ್ರವಸ|ಪಾರ್ಥಶ್ರವಸ|ಪಾರ್ಥ|ಶ್ರವಸ|pārtha-śravasa|pārthaśravasa|pārtha|śravasa|paartha-shravasa|paarthashravasa|paartha|shravasa|_vei_120@1797|_vei1_pn522
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1796">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1798">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn521">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn522">1:522</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn523">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pārtha-śravasa,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="पृथु-श्रवस्">pṛthu-śravas</a>,’ is found as the name of a demon in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201797">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201797">↑</a> iv. 26, 15. This form of the name is supported by its occurrence in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ix. 10; xvii. 27. But Pārthu-is also possible.</li></ol></div>

पार्थ्य|పార్థ్య|ಪಾರ್ಥ್ಯ|pārthya|paarthya|_vei_120@1798|_vei1_pn522
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1797">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1799">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn521">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn522">1:522</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn523">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pārthya,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="पृथि">pṛthi</a>,’ is the patronymic of some donor in a hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201798">1</a></sup> The form of the name in the Aśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201798">2</a></sup> is <a class="vei_120a" href="पार्थ">pārtha</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201798">↑</a> x. 93. 15.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201798">↑</a> xii. 10. <i>Cf.</i> the Anukramaṇī on Rv. x. 93.</li></ol></div>

पार्वति|పార్వతి|ಪಾರ್ವತಿ|pārvati|paarvati|_vei_120@1799|_vei1_pn522
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1798">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1800">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn521">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn522">1:522</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn523">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pārvati,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="पर्वत">parvata</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b>Dakṣa</b> in the Śatapatha (ii. 4, 4, 6) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> (iv. 4) Brāhmaṇas.</p></div>

पार्षद|పార్షద|ಪಾರ್ಷದ|pārṣada|paarShada|_vei_120@1800|_vei1_pn522
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1799">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1801">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn521">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn522">1:522</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn523">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pārṣada,</b> which first appears in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201800"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201800">1</a></sup> denotes a textbook recognized by a school of grammarians.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201800"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201800">↑</a> i. 17. <i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 128 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Studien,</i> 3, 269; 4, 217.</li></ol></div>

पार्षद्-वाण|पार्षद्वाण|पार्षद्|वाण|పార్షద్-వాణ|పార్షద్వాణ|పార్షద్|వాణ|ಪಾರ್ಷದ್-ವಾಣ|ಪಾರ್ಷದ್ವಾಣ|ಪಾರ್ಷದ್|ವಾಣ|pārṣad-vāṇa|pārṣadvāṇa|pārṣad|vāṇa|paarShad-vaaNa|paarShadvaaNa|paarShad|vaaNa|_vei_120@1801|_vei1_pn522
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1800">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1802">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn521">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn522">1:522</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn523">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pārṣad-vāṇa,</b> ‘descendant of Pṛṣadvāṇa,’ is mentioned as a wonder-worker in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201801"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201801">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201801"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201801">↑</a> viii. 51, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 139.</li></ol></div>

पार्ष्ण शैलन|పార్ష్ణ శైలన|ಪಾರ್ಷ್ಣ ಶೈಲನ|pārṣṇa śailana|paarShNa shailana|_vei_120@1802|_vei1_pn522
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1801">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1803">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn521">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn522">1:522</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn523">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pārṣṇa Śailana</b> is mentioned as a teacher in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (ii. 4, 8).</p></div>

पालागल|పాలాగల|ಪಾಲಾಗಲ|pālāgala|paalaagala|_vei_120@1803|_vei1_pn522
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1802">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1804">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn521">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn522">1:522</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn523">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pālāgala</b> occurs in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201803"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201803">1</a></sup> apparently in the sense of ‘messenger,’ or ‘bearer of false news.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201803"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201803">↑</a> v. 3, 1, 11. Eggeling; <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 64, renders it ‘courier.’</li></ol></div>

पालागली|पालागलि|పాలాగలీ|పాలాగలి|ಪಾಲಾಗಲೀ|ಪಾಲಾಗಲಿ|pālāgalī|pālāgali|paalaagalii|paalaagali|paalaagalee|_vei_120@1804|_vei1_pn523
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1803">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1805">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn522">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn523">1:523</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn524">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pālāgalī</b> is the name of the fourth and least respected wife of the king.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201804"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201804">1</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201804"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201804">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 1, 8; 5, 2, 8, etc.</li></ol></div>

पावमानी|पावमानि|పావమానీ|పావమాని|ಪಾವಮಾನೀ|ಪಾವಮಾನಿ|pāvamānī|pāvamāni|paavamaanii|paavamaani|paavamaanee|_vei_120@1805|_vei1_pn523
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1804">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1806">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn522">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn523">1:523</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn524">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāvamānī</b> means the verses (<i>ṛcas</i>) in the ninth Maṇḍala of the Rigveda ‘relating to <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> Pavamāna’ (‘purifying itself’). The name is found in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201805">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201805">2</a></sup> possibly even in one hymn of the Rigveda itself.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201805">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201805">↑</a> xix. 71, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201805">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 20; ii. 37; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xv. 1; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 8, 1, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, xi. 2; xii. 31; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 2, 2, etc.; Maitrāyaṇī <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201805">↑</a> ix. 67, 31. 32; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 99. n. 3.</li></ol></div>

पाश|పాశ|ಪಾಶ|pāśa|paasha|_vei_120@1806|_vei1_pn523
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1805">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1807">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn522">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn523">1:523</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn524">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāśa</b> denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201806">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201806">2</a></sup> a ‘rope’ used for fastening or tying up. Rope and knot (<i>granthi</i>) are mentioned together in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201806">3</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="पाश">pāśa</a> is in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201806">4</a></sup> used of the rope by which Manu's ship was fastened to the mountain. It is often employed metaphorically of the ‘fetter’ of <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201806">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201806">↑</a> i. 24, 13. 15; ii. 27, 16; 29, 5. etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201806">↑</a> Av. ii. 12, 2; ix. 3, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, vi. 8. 45, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201806">↑</a> ix. 3, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201806">↑</a> i. 8, 1, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201806">↑</a> Rv. vi. 74, 4; vii. 88, 7; x. 85, 24; Av. iv. 16, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 5, 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

पाशिन्|పాశిన్|ಪಾಶಿನ್|pāśin|paashin|_vei_120@1807|_vei1_pn523
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1806">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1808">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn522">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn523">1:523</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn524">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāśin,</b> ‘having a noose,’ denotes a ‘hunter’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201807">1</a></sup> and in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201807">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201807">↑</a> iii. 45, 1; ix. 73, 4. So of Nirṛti in <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201807">↑</a> xvii. 1, 8.</li></ol></div>

पाश-द्युम्न वायत|पाशद्युम्न वायत|पाश|द्युम्न वायत|పాశ-ద్యుమ్న వాయత|పాశద్యుమ్న వాయత|పాశ|ద్యుమ్న వాయత|ಪಾಶ-ದ್ಯುಮ್ನ ವಾಯತ|ಪಾಶದ್ಯುಮ್ನ ವಾಯತ|ಪಾಶ|ದ್ಯುಮ್ನ ವಾಯತ|pāśa-dyumna vāyata|pāśadyumna vāyata|pāśa|dyumna vāyata|paasha-dyumna vaayata|paashadyumna vaayata|paasha|dyumna vaayata|_vei_120@1808|_vei1_pn523
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1807">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1809">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn522">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn523">1:523</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn524">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāśa-<a class="vei_120a" href="द्युम्न">dyumna</a> Vāyata</b> is the name of a king to whom the <b>Vasiṣṭhas</b> claim to have been preferred by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> in one hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201808"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201808">1</a></sup> Apparently he was, as Sāyaṇa says, son of Vayat, who may be compared with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्यत्">vyat</a> of another</b> passage of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201808"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201808">2</a></sup> Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201808"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201808">3</a></sup> sees in him a priest of the <b>Pṛthus</b> and <b>Parśus,</b> but this is most improbable.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201808"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201808">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201808"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201808">↑</a> vii. 33. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201808"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201808">↑</a> i. 122, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201808"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201808">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 173.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201808"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201808">↑</a> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 130, 139.</li></ol></div>

पाष्य|పాష్య|ಪಾಷ್ಯ|pāṣya|paaShya|_vei_120@1809|_vei1_pn523
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1808">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1810">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn522">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn523">1:523</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn524">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pāṣya</b> occurs in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201809"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201809">1</a></sup> with reference to the defeat of <a class="vei_120a" href="वृत्र">vṛtra</a>, and apparently denotes ‘stone bulwarks.’  In another passage<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201809"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201809">2</a></sup> the word may mean the stones used for pressing <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201809"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201809">↑</a> i. 56, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201809"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201809">↑</a> ix. 102, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Macdone, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1893, 457, 458.</li></ol></div>

पिक|పిక|ಪಿಕ|pika|_vei_120@1810|_vei1_pn524
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1809">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1811">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn523">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn524">1:524</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn525">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pika,</b> the Indian ‘cuckoo,’ is mentioned in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201810"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201810">1</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अन्यवाप">anyavāpa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कोक">koka</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201810"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201810">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 15, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 20; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 39. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 92.</li></ol></div>

पिंगा|पिङ्गा|పింగా|పిఙ్గా|ಪಿಂಗಾ|ಪಿಙ್ಗಾ|piṅgā|piMgaa|pingaa|_vei_120@1811|_vei1_pn524
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1810">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1812">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn523">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn524">1:524</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn525">↷</a><hr><p><b>Piṅgā</b> is found in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201811"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201811">1</a></sup> where it is explained by the St. Petersburg Dictionary with Sāyaṇa as ‘bowstring,’ but where Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201811"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201811">2</a></sup> thinks that a musical instrument of some kind is meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201811"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201811">↑</a> viii. 69, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201811"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201811">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 144, n.</li></ol></div>

पिजवन|పిజవన|ಪಿಜವನ|pijavana|_vei_120@1812|_vei1_pn524
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1811">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1813">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn523">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn524">1:524</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn525">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pijavana</b> is the name of the father of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a></b> according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201812">1</a></sup> Probably this statement is based on a mere conjecture from the epithet <a class="vei_120a" href="पैजवन">paijavana</a> used of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a> in a verse of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201812">2</a></sup> but may very well be correct.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201812">↑</a> ii. 24.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201812">↑</a> vii. 18, 19. So <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 21.</li></ol></div>

पिञ्जूल|పిఞ్జూల|ಪಿಞ್ಜೂಲ|piñjūla|pi~njuula|pi~njoola|_vei_120@1813|_vei1_pn524
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1812">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1814">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn523">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn524">1:524</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn525">↷</a><hr><p><b>Piñjūla</b> denotes a ‘bundle’ of grass or stalks, especially of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दर्भ">darbha</a>.</b> The word is only found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> style.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201813"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201813">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201813"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201813">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxiii. 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xviii. 8. It appears in the form of <i>piñjula</i> in Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 8, 7; Pāraskara <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 15; of <a class="vei_120a" href="पुञ्जील">puñjīla</a> in <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 1, 7; 2, 4, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 6, 4; ii. 7, 9, 5.</li></ol></div>

पिठीनस्|पिठीन|పిఠీనస్|పిఠీన|ಪಿಠೀನಸ್|ಪಿಠೀನ|piṭhīnas|piṭhīna|piThiinas|piThiina|piTheenas|piTheena|_vei_120@1814|_vei1_pn524
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1813">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1815">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn523">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn524">1:524</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn525">↷</a><hr><p><b>Piṭhīnas</b> is the name of a man, a friend of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201814"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201814">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201814"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201814">↑</a> vi. 26, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 156.</li></ol></div>

पिण्ड|పిణ్డ|ಪಿಣ್ಡ|piṇḍa|piNDa|_vei_120@1815|_vei1_pn524
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1814">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1816">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn523">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn524">1:524</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn525">↷</a><hr><p><b>Piṇḍa,</b> denoting specifically a ball of flour offered to the Manes, especially on the evening of new moon, occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201815"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201815">1</a></sup> and repeatedly in the Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201815"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201815">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201815"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201815">↑</a> iii. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201815"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201815">↑</a> Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 10, 4, etc.</li></ol></div>

पिता-पुत्र|पितापुत्र|पिता|पुत्र|పితా-పుత్ర|పితాపుత్ర|పితా|పుత్ర|ಪಿತಾ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಪಿತಾಪುತ್ರ|ಪಿತಾ|ಪುತ್ರ|pitā-putra|pitāputra|pitā|putra|pitaa-putra|pitaaputra|pitaa|_vei_120@1816|_vei1_pn525
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1815">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1817">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn524">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn525">1:525</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn526">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pitā-putra,</b> ‘father and son,’ is a compound of rare occurrence.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201816"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201816">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201816"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201816">↑</a> Av. vi. 112, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 2, 4, 4.</li></ol></div>

पिता-पुत्रीय|पितापुत्रीय|पिता|पुत्रीय|పితా-పుత్రీయ|పితాపుత్రీయ|పితా|పుత్రీయ|ಪಿತಾ-ಪುತ್ರೀಯ|ಪಿತಾಪುತ್ರೀಯ|ಪಿತಾ|ಪುತ್ರೀಯ|pitā-putrīya|pitāputrīya|pitā|putrīya|pitaa-putriiya|pitaaputriiya|pitaa|putriiya|pitaa-putreeya|pitaaputreeya|putreeya|_vei_120@1817|_vei1_pn525
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1816">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1818">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn524">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn525">1:525</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn526">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pitā-putrīya</b> (‘relating to father and son’), used with Sampradāna (‘handing over’) means the ceremony by which a father, when about to die, bequeathes his bodily and mental powers to his son. It is described in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201817">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201817">↑</a> ii. 15. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 408.</li></ol></div>

पिता-मह|पितामह|पिता|मह|పితా-మహ|పితామహ|పితా|మహ|ಪಿತಾ-ಮಹ|ಪಿತಾಮಹ|ಪಿತಾ|ಮಹ|pitā-maha|pitāmaha|pitā|maha|pitaa-maha|pitaamaha|pitaa|_vei_120@1818|_vei1_pn525
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1817">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1819">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn524">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn525">1:525</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn526">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pitā-maha,</b> beside <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ततामह">tatāmaha</a>,</b> denotes from the Atharvaveda onwards<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201818">1</a></sup> the ‘paternal grandfather,’ apparmtly as a ‘father in a higher sense.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201818">2</a></sup> The great-grandfather is <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रपितामह">prapitāmaha</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रततामह">pratatāmaha</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201818">3</a></sup> It is significant that there are no corrésponding Vedic words for maternal grandparents, and that the words used in the latter language, such as Mātāmaha, are imitations of the terms for paternal relations.</p><p>In one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201818">4</a></sup> Delbrück<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201818">5</a></sup> suggests that <i>mahe pitre</i> means ‘grandfather,’ a sense which would well suit the <i>napātam,</i> ‘grandson,’ following, but the sense of the whole passage is uncertain.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201818">6</a></sup></p><p>We learn very little from the texts of the position of grandparents. No doubt they were entitled to marks of respect similar to those shown to parents,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201818">7</a></sup> as the epic expressly testifies. A grandfather might easily be the head of the family, or be living with his eldest son, after he ceased to be able to control the family.</p><p>The grandmother (Pitāmahī) is not mentioned in the extant Vedic literature. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201818">↑</a> Av. v. 5, 1; ix. 5, 30; xi. 1, 19; xviii. 4, 35; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 5, 1; vii. 2, 7, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 36; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 5, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201818">↑</a> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 474.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201818">↑</a> Av. xviii. 4, 75.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201818">↑</a> vi. 20. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201818">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 473.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201818">↑</a> See Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 128, n. 1. Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> also doubts the view which is accepted by Delbrück, and denies that in Rv. i. 71, 5, the sense of ‘grandfather’ can be found.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201818">↑</a> Delbrück, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 480, citing Mahābhārata, ii. 1634.</li></ol></div>

पितु|పితు|ಪಿತು|pitu|_vei_120@1819|_vei1_pn526
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1818">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1820">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn525">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn526">1:526</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn529">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pitu</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201819"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201819">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201819"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201819">2</a></sup> has the general sense of ‘nutriment,’ whether food or drink.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201819"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201819">↑</a> i. 61, 7; 132, 6; 187, 1; vi. 20, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201819"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201819">↑</a> Av. iv. 6, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 7, 2, 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ii. 20; xii. 65; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 13.</li></ol></div>

पितृ|పితృ|ಪಿತೃ|pitṛ|pitR|pitRi|pitRu|pitRRi|_vei_120@1820|_vei1_pn526
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1819">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1821">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn525">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn526">1:526</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn529">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pitṛ,</b> common from the Rigveda onwards, denotes ‘father,’ not so much as the ‘begetter’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="जनितृ">janitṛ</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201820">1</a></sup> but rather as the protector of the child, this being probably also the etymological sense of the word.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201820">2</a></sup> The father in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201820">3</a></sup> stands for all that is good and kind. Hence <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> is compared with a father,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201820">4</a></sup> while <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> is even dearer than a father.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201820">5</a></sup> The father carries his son in his arms,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201820">6</a></sup> and places him on his lap,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201820">7</a></sup> while the child pulls his garment to attract attention.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201820">8</a></sup> In later years the son depends on his father for help in trouble,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201820">9</a></sup> and greets him with joy.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201820">10</a></sup></p><p>It is difficult to ascertain precisely how far the son was subject to parental control, and how long such control continued. Reference is made in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201820">11</a></sup> to a father's chastising his son for gambling, and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋज्राश्व">ṛjrāśva</a></b> is said to have been blinded by his father.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201820">12</a></sup> From the latter statement Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201820">13</a></sup> infers the existence of a developed <i>patria potestas,</i> but to lay stress on this isolated and semi-mythical incident would be unwise. It is, however, quite likely that the <i>patria potestas</i> was originally strong, for we have other support for the thesis in the Roman <i>patria potestas.</i> If there is no proof that a father  legally controlled his son's wedding,<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201820">14</a></sup> and not much that he controlled his daughter's,<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201820">15</a></sup> the fact is in itself not improbable.</p><p>There is again no evidence to show whether a son, when grown up, normally continued to stay with his father, his wife becoming a member of the father's household, or whether he set up a house of his own: probably the custom varied. Nor do we know whether the son was granted a special plot of land on marriage or otherwise, or whether he only came into such property after his father's death. But any excessive estimate of the father's powers over a son who was no longer a minor and naturally under his control, must be qualified by the fact that in his old age the sons might divide their father's property,<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1201820">16</a></sup> or he might divide it amongst them,<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1201820">17</a></sup> and that when the father-in-law became aged he fell under the control of his son's wife.<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1201820">18</a></sup> There are also obscure traces that in old age a father might be exposed, though there is no reason to suppose that this was usual in Vedic India.<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1201820">19</a></sup></p><p>Normally the son was bound to give his father full obedience.<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1201820">20</a></sup> The later Sūtras show in detail the acts of courtesy which he owed his father, and they allow him to eat the remnants of his father's food.<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1201820">21</a></sup> On the other hand, the father was expected to be kind. The story of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Brāh maṇa<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1201820">22</a></sup> emphasizes the horror with which the father's heartless treatment of his son was viewed. The Upaniṣads<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1201820">23</a></sup> insist on the spiritual succession from father to son. The kissing of a son<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1201820">24</a></sup> was a frequent and usual token of affection, even in mature years.</p><p>On the failure of natural children, adoption was possible.<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1201820">25</a></sup> It was even resorted to when natural children existed, but when it was desired to secure the presence in the family of a person of specially high qualifications, as in Viśvāmitra's adoption of <a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_1201820">26</a></sup> It is not clear that adoption from one caste into another was possible, for there is no good evidence that <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> was, as Weber<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_1201820">27</a></sup> holds, a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> who adopted a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>. Adoption was also not always in high favour: it may be accidental or not that a hymn of the <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> book of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_28_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_1201820">28</a></sup> condemns the usage. It was also possible for the father who had a daughter, but no sons, to appoint her to bear a son for him. At any rate the practice appears to be referred to in an obscure verse of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_29_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29_vei_1201820">29</a></sup> as interpreted by <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>.<sup id="ref_120_30_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_30_vei_1201820">30</a></sup> Moreover, it is possible that the difficulty of a brotherless maiden finding a husband<sup id="ref_120_31_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_31_vei_1201820">31</a></sup> may have been due in part to the possibility of her father desiring to make her a <a class="vei_120a" href="पुत्रिका">putrikā</a>, the later technical name for a daughter whose son is to belong to her father's family.</p><p>There can be no doubt that in a family the father took precedence of the mother.<sup id="ref_120_32_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_32_vei_1201820">32</a></sup> Delbrück<sup id="ref_120_33_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_33_vei_1201820">33</a></sup> explains away the apparent cases to the contrary.<sup id="ref_120_34_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_34_vei_1201820">34</a></sup> There is no trace of the  family as a land-owning corporation.<sup id="ref_120_35_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_35_vei_1201820">35</a></sup> The dual form Pitarau regularly means ‘father and mother,’ ‘parents.’<sup id="ref_120_36_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_36_vei_1201820">36</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201820">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पिता">pitā</a> janitā,</i> is used of gods in the Rigveda--<i>e.g.,</i> iv. 17, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201820">↑</a> As derived from <i>pā,</i> ‘protect.’ But, as Bo7htlingk and Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. Mātar,</i> footnote, suggest, <i>pa</i> and <i>mā</i> were probably the much older original onomatopoetic names for ‘father’ and ‘mother,’ which in a later reflective age influenced the formation of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पितृ">pitṛ</a></i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मातृ">mātṛ</a></i> (which themselves go back to the Indo-European period).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201820">↑</a> See, <i>e.g.,</i> iv. 17, 17; viii. 86, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201820">↑</a> Rv. x. 7, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201820">↑</a> Rv. vii. 32, 19; viii. 1, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201820">↑</a> Rv. i. 38, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201820">↑</a> Rv. v. 43, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201820">↑</a> Rv. iii. 53, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201820">↑</a> In Rv. x. 48, 1, the <i>jantavaḥ</i> possibly are the sons.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201820">↑</a> Rv. vii. 103, 3. <i>Cf.</i> 1, 24, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201820">↑</a> Rv. ii. 29, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201820">↑</a> Rv. i. 116, 16; 117, 17. There is also the case of the sale of Sunaḥśepa, <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 12-18; and <i>cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 3, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201820">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 316.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201820">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 576. <i>Ibid.,</i> 582, he quotes Mahābhārata, xii. 6108 <i>et seq.,</i> which refers in one line to the control of the marriage of the son by the father, and in the next to a case of free marriage. The fact is, no doubt, that the son could marry freely, unless his father had arranged matters for him when he was too young to object.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201820">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 309, assumes this as certain, but it is far from proved. See, however, Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 12, 2, which is in favour of Zimmer's view. <i>Cf.</i> Kaegi, <i>Dev Rigveda,</i> 15, and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1201820">↑</a> Rv. i. 70, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 14; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 156 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 26, 61, 62).</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1201820">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 1, 9, 4-6. <i>Cf.</i> the handing over from father to son in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 15. If the father recovered, he lived subject to his son.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1201820">↑</a> Rv. x. 85, 46.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1201820">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. viii. 51, 2; Av. xviii. 2, 34. The first passage need not refer to exposure, and the second merely refers to the exposure of a dead body; but Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 326-328, thinks that they prove exposure. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1201820">↑</a> Rv. i. 68, 5</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1201820">↑</a> Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 1, 4, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1201820">↑</a> vii. 12 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 17 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1201820">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 15; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 5, 25 (Mādhyaṃdina = i. 5, 17, <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1201820">↑</a> See Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 28, 120-134; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 26, n. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1201820">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 318; Mayr, <i>Indisches Erbrecht,</i> 73; Jolly, <i>Die Adoption in Indien</i> (Würzburg, 1910), 7 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_1201820">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 17 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 17. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 157.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_1201820">↑</a> <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 33, 34.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_1201820">↑</a> vii. 4, 7. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_29_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29_vei_1201820">↑</a> iii. 31, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_30_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_30_vei_1201820">↑</a> iii. 5 <i>ad fin. Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 343; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 34; Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 239-241.</li><li id="fx_120_31_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_31_vei_1201820">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भ्रातृ">bhrātṛ</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_32_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_32_vei_1201820">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 5, 1, 18; a citation in <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 9; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> vii. 15, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_33_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_33_vei_1201820">↑</a> {%Die {|indogermanischen|} Verwandtschaftsnamen,%} 577.</li><li id="fx_120_34_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_34_vei_1201820">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 7, 5. Some passages in the Sūtras present difficulties, but they are of no importance for Vedic times proper.</li><li id="fx_120_35_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_35_vei_1201820">↑</a> Baden Powell, whose various works (<i>Indian Village Community,</i> 1896; <i>Village Communities in India,</i> 1899, etc.) have done most to combat the view of the village community in India as a land-holding institution, is prepared to recognize the family as a land-owning unit, considering that the <i>patria potestas</i> is a later growth, and not Indian (see, <i>e.g., Village Communities in India,</i> 128 <i>et seq.</i>). Hopkins, <i>India, Old and New,</i> 218 <i>et seq.,</i> adopts a theory which allows of individual and joint family ownership side by side, the latter being apparently the earlier but the decadent stage. He expressly considers (p. 222) that the son had an indefeasible right to prevent the father from alienating the hereditary land, which could only be parted with by the consent of the village if it were a case of joint ownership (<i>cf.</i> the verse cited by Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 94). But it must be remembered that, as is very clearly shown in the case of English law by Pollock and Maitland (<i>History of English Law,</i> 2, 337-352), the recognition of the rights of sons may well be, not a sign of original joint or family ownership, but a development from the existence of intestate succession, and as in England, so in India, there is no trace of a corporate joint family in the early books. And, as Jolly (<i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 76, 80) shows, there are clear traces, both in old and modern times, of a despotic control of the family by the father even after his sons grew up, provided only that he was physically able to control them. The same state of affairs seems proved for early English law, as it is beyond question for Roman law (see Smith's <i>Dictionary of Antiquities,</i> 2, 351 <i>et seq.</i>). In Greece also, which is sometimes contrasted with Rome, there is the clearest trace of both a real <i>patria potestas,</i> and of the absolute ownership of the land by the father as against the son, especially in the archaic laws of Gortyn (see Gardner and Jevons, <i>Greek Antiquities,</i> 404, 405, 563, 566).</li><li id="fx_120_36_vei_1201820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_36_vei_1201820">↑</a> Rv. i. 20, 4; 160, 3; ii. 17, 7; vii. 67, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxiii 10; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 11, etc.</li></ol></div>

पितृ-याण|पितृयाण|पितृ|याण|పితృ-యాణ|పితృయాణ|పితృ|యాణ|ಪಿತೃ-ಯಾಣ|ಪಿತೃಯಾಣ|ಪಿತೃ|ಯಾಣ|pitṛ-yāṇa|pitṛyāṇa|pitṛ|yāṇa|pitR-yaaNa|pitRyaaNa|pitR|yaaNa|pitRi-yaaNa|pitRiyaaNa|pitRi|pitRu-yaaNa|pitRuyaaNa|pitRu|pitRRi-yaaNa|pitRRiyaaNa|pitRRi|_vei_120@1821|_vei1_pn529
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1820">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1822">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn526">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn529">1:529</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn530">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pitṛ-yāṇa,</b> the ‘way of the fathers,’ mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201821">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201821">2</a></sup> is opposed to the <a class="vei_120a" href="देव">deva</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="यान">yāna</a>, or ‘way of the gods.’ Tilak<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201821">3</a></sup> considers that the Devayāna corresponds with the Uttarāyaṇa, ‘northern journey’ of the sun, and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पितृयाण">pitṛyāṇa</a> with the <a class="vei_120a" href="दक्षिणायन">dakṣiṇāyana</a>, its ‘southern journey.’ He concludes from a passage of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201821">4</a></sup> where three of the seasons--spring, summer, and the rains--are ascribed to the gods, but the others to the Pitṛs, or Fathers, that the Devayāna began with the vernal equinox, and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पितृयाण">pitṛyāṇa</a> with the autumnal equinox. With this he connects the curious  distinction of <a class="vei_120a" href="देव">deva</a>- and <a class="vei_120a" href="यम">yama</a>-Nakṣatras in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201821">5</a></sup> These conclusions are, however, very improbable. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सूर्य">sūrya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201821">↑</a> x. 2, 7. <i>Cf.</i> the allusion to it in x. 18, 1, as other than the Devavāna, which appears in x. 98, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201821">↑</a> Av. viii. 10, 19; xii. 2, 10, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 45; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 3, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201821">↑</a> <i>Orion,</i> 22 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201821">↑</a> ii. 1, 3, 1-3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201821">↑</a> i. 5, 2, 6.</li></ol></div>

पितृ-हन्|पितृहन्|पितृ|हन्|పితృ-హన్|పితృహన్|పితృ|హన్|ಪಿತೃ-ಹನ್|ಪಿತೃಹನ್|ಪಿತೃ|ಹನ್|pitṛ-han|pitṛhan|pitṛ|han|pitR-han|pitRhan|pitR|pitRi-han|pitRihan|pitRi|pitRu-han|pitRuhan|pitRu|pitRRi-han|pitRRihan|pitRRi|_vei_120@1822|_vei1_pn530
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1821">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1823">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn529">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn530">1:530</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn531">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pitṛ-han,</b> ‘parricide,’ is found in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201822"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201822">1</a></sup> Paippalāda recension.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201822"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201822">↑</a> ix. 4, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

पित्र्य|పిత్ర్య|ಪಿತ್ರ್ಯ|pitrya|_vei_120@1823|_vei1_pn530
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1822">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1824">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn529">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn530">1:530</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn531">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pitrya</b> occurs in the list of sciences given in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201823"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201823">1</a></sup> Apparently it is to be taken as the science relating to the cult of the Manes, as explained by Śaṅkara in his commentary. As it is in that list followed by <b>Rāśi,</b> the St. Petersburg Dictionary is inclined to take <a class="vei_120a" href="पित्र्य">pitrya</a> Rāśi as one expression, but in what exact sense does not appear.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201823"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201823">↑</a> vii. 1, 2, 4; 2, 1; 7, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 267; Little, <i>Grammatical Index,</i> 98.</li></ol></div>

पित्व|పిత్వ|ಪಿತ್ವ|pitva|_vei_120@1824|_vei1_pn530
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1823">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1825">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn529">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn530">1:530</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn531">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pitva</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201824">1</a></sup> or <b>Pidva</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201824">2</a></sup> is the name of an animal included in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās. According to the commentator on the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1201824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1201824">1b</a></sup> it means ‘lion.’ But it may be identical with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पेत्व">petva</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201824">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 17, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201824">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 13; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 32. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 79; Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 29, 290.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1201824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1201824">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 17, 1.</li></ol></div>

पिनाक|పినాక|ಪಿನಾಕ|pināka|pinaaka|_vei_120@1825|_vei1_pn530
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1824">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1826">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn529">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn530">1:530</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn531">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pināka,</b> ‘club,’ is found in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201825">1</a></sup> Later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201825">2</a></sup> it is used to denote the club of <a class="vei_120a" href="रुद्र">rudra</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="शिव">śiva</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201825">↑</a> i. 27, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201825">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 6, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iii. 61; xvi. 51. etc.</li></ol></div>

पिन्वन|పిన్వన|ಪಿನ್ವನ|pinvana|_vei_120@1826|_vei1_pn530
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1825">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1827">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn529">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn530">1:530</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn531">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pinvana</b> occurs in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xiv. 1, 2, 17; 2, 1, 11; 3, 1, 22) as the name of a vessel used in the ritual.</p></div>

पिपील|పిపీల|ಪಿಪೀಲ|pipīla|pipiila|pipeela|_vei_120@1827|_vei1_pn530
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1826">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1828">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn529">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn530">1:530</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn531">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pipīla,</b> ‘ant,’ is mentioned in the Rigveda (x. 16, 6) as eating the flesh of the dead. </p></div>

पिपीलिका|పిపీలికా|ಪಿಪೀಲಿಕಾ|pipīlikā|pipiilikaa|pipeelikaa|_vei_120@1828|_vei1_pn531
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1827">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1829">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn530">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn531">1:531</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn532">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pipīlikā</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201828">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201828">2</a></sup> denotes an ‘ant,’ the form of the word referring doubtless not so much to the small species of ant, as it is taken in the later lexicons,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201828">3</a></sup> but rather to the insect's tiny size, which would naturally be expressed by a diminutive formation of the name. The form Pipīlaka<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201828">4</a></sup> is found in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201828">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201828">↑</a> vii. 56, 7. <i>Cf.</i> xx. 134. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201828">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 6, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 6, 10; xv. 17, 8; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 4, 9. 29 (Mādhyaṃdina = i. 4, 4, 16 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>); <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vii. 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 3, 8; ii. 1, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201828">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201828">↑</a> <i>Cf. kanīnaka</i> beside <i>kanīnikā,</i> ‘pupil of the eye.’</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201828">↑</a> vii. 2, 1; 7, 1; 8, 1; 10, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 97; Edgerton, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 31, 128.</li></ol></div>

पिप्पका|పిప్పకా|ಪಿಪ್ಪಕಾ|pippakā|pippakaa|_vei_120@1829|_vei1_pn531
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1828">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1830">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn530">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn531">1:531</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn532">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pippakā</b> is mentioned in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> <b>Saṃhitās.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201829"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201829">1</a></sup> Some bird seems to be meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201829"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201829">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 19, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 21; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 40. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 93.</li></ol></div>

पिप्पल|పిప్పల|ಪಿಪ್ಪಲ|pippala|_vei_120@1830|_vei1_pn531
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1829">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1831">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn530">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn531">1:531</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn532">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pippala,</b> n., is found in two passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201830"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201830">1</a></sup> meaning ‘berry,’ used with a mystic signification, and in neither case with any certain reference to the berry of the fig-tree.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201830"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201830">2</a></sup> In the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201830"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201830">3</a></sup> the general sense of ‘berry’ is not necessary, and the special sense of ‘berry’ of the Peepal is quite possible: the latter meaning is perhaps intended in the Satapatha Brāhmana.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201830"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201830">4</a></sup> In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201830"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201830">5</a></sup> the feminine form of the word, Pippalī, appears denoting berries used as a remedy for wounds, like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अरुन्धती">arundhatī</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201830"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201830">6</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201830"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201830">↑</a> i. 164, 20 = Muṇḍaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 1, 1; Śvetāśvatara <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 6, 22; v. 54, 12 (the ‘berry’ of heaven, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नाक">nāka</a></i>).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201830"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201830">↑</a> The word <a class="vei_120a" href="पिप्पल">pippala</a> appears in the later literature as a masculine, denoting the <i>Ficus veligiosa</i> (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वत्थ">aśvattha</a></b> in Vedic literature).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201830"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201830">↑</a> iv. 1, 41.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201830"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201830">↑</a> iii. 7, 1, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201830"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201830">↑</a> vi. 109, 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201830"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201830">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 61; <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 516; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 359, 360; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 389; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 331.</li></ol></div>

पिप्पलाद|పిప్పలాద|ಪಿಪ್ಪಲಾದ|pippalāda|pippalaada|_vei_120@1831|_vei1_pn532
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1830">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1832">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn531">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn532">1:532</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn533">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pippalāda</b> (‘eater of berries’) is the name of a teacher mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रश्न">praśna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201831">1</a></sup> In the plural the name denotes a school of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201831">2</a></sup> Their (Paippalāda) recension of the text of the Saṃhitā has been reproduced in facsimile by Garbe and Bloomfield,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201831">3</a></sup> and in part published.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201831">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201831">↑</a> i. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201831">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 277; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 153, 159, 160, 164.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201831">↑</a> Baltimore, 1901.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201831">↑</a> The variants of the Paippalāda are given in part in Whitney's Translation of the Atharvaveda, and the text of books i. and ii. has been edited by Barret in the <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 26, 197-295; 30, 187 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> also Lanman in Whitney's Translation, lxxix <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

पिप्रु|పిప్రు|ಪಿಪ್ರು|pipru|_vei_120@1832|_vei1_pn532
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1831">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1833">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn531">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn532">1:532</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn533">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pipru</b> is the name of a foe of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> in the Rigveda. He was repeatedly defeated by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> for <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋजिश्वन्">ṛjiśvan</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201832">1</a></sup> Mentioned as possessing forts,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201832">2</a></sup> he is called a <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201832">3</a></sup> as well as an <a class="vei_120a" href="असुर">asura</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201832">4</a></sup> He is described as having a black brood,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201832">5</a></sup> and as being allied with blacks.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201832">6</a></sup> It is uncertain whether he was a demon, according to Roth's<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201832">7</a></sup> view, which is favoured by the use of the word <a class="vei_120a" href="असुर">asura</a>, or a human foe, as Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201832">8</a></sup> Oldenberg,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201832">9</a></sup> and Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201832">10</a></sup> believe. The name may mean ‘resister,’ from the root <i>pṛ.</i></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201832">↑</a> i. 101, 1, 2; iv. 16, 13; v. 29, 11; vi. 20, 7; viii. 49, 10; x. 99, 11; 138, 3. In i. 103, 8; ii. 14, 5; vi. 18, 8, the reference is general to a defeat of <a class="vei_120a" href="पिप्रु">pipru</a> by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201832">↑</a> Rv. i. 51, 5; vi. 20, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201832">↑</a> Rv. viii. 32, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201832">↑</a> Rv. x. 138, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201832">↑</a> Rv. i. 101, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201832">↑</a> iv. 16, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201832">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201832">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 149.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201832">↑</a> <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 155.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201832">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 273.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 161 (C).</li></ol></div>

पिश|పిశ|ಪಿಶ|piśa|pisha|_vei_120@1833|_vei1_pn532
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1832">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1834">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn531">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn532">1:532</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn533">↷</a><hr><p><b>Piśa</b> is found in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201833"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201833">1</a></sup> where <b>Sāyaṇa</b> takes it to mean a deer (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="रुरु">ruru</a></i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201833"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201833">↑</a> i. 64, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Av. xix. 49, 4; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 83; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 118; Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 110.</li></ol></div>

पिशंग|पिशङ्ग|పిశంగ|పిశఙ్గ|ಪಿಶಂಗ|ಪಿಶಙ್ಗ|piśaṅga|pishaMga|pishanga|_vei_120@1834|_vei1_pn532
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1833">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1835">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn531">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn532">1:532</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn533">↷</a><hr><p><b>Piśaṅga</b> is the name of one of the two Unnetṛ priests officiating at the snake festival mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201834"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201834">1</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चक">caka</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201834"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201834">↑</a> xxv. 15, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 35.</li></ol></div>

पिशाच|పిశాచ|ಪಿಶಾಚ|piśāca|pishaacha|_vei_120@1835|_vei1_pn533
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1834">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1836">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn532">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn533">1:533</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn534">↷</a><hr><p><b>Piśāca</b> is the name of a class of demon mentioned in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201835">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201835">2</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201835">3</a></sup> they are associated with Rakṣases and Asuras, while opposed to gods, men, and fathers. In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201835">4</a></sup> they are described as <i>kravyād,</i> ‘eaters of raw flesh,’ which may be the etymological sense of the word <a class="vei_120a" href="पिशाच">piśāca</a> itself.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201835">5</a></sup> It is possible that the Piśācas were, as suggested by Grierson,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201835">6</a></sup> really human foes, like the north-western tribes, who even in later times were reputed eaters of raw flesh (not necessarily as cannibals, but rather as eaters of human flesh in ritual). This is, however, not at all likely, the Piśācas having in all probability only meant ‘ghouls’ originally: when they appear as human tribes, they were presumably thus designated in scorn. A science called Piśācaveda<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201835">7</a></sup> or <a class="vei_120a" href="पिशाच">piśāca</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="विद्या">vidyā</a><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201835">8</a></sup> is known in the later Vedic period.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201835">↑</a> ii. 18, 4; iv. 20, 6. 9; 36, 4; 37, 10; v. 29, 4. 5. 14; vi. 32, 2; viii. 2, 12; xii. 1, 50. The word occurs once in the Rv. (i. 133, 5) in the form of Piśāci.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201835">↑</a> See St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201835">↑</a> ii. 4, 1, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201835">↑</a> v. 25, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201835">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 264, n.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201835">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Grierson, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1905, 285-288. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 164 (B).</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201835">↑</a> Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>. i. 1, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201835">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 7, 6.</li></ol></div>

पिशित|పిశిత|ಪಿಶಿತ|piśita|pishita|_vei_120@1836|_vei1_pn533
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1835">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1837">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn532">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn533">1:533</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn534">↷</a><hr><p><b>Piśita</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201836"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201836">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201836"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201836">2</a></sup> denotes raw flesh (<i>of.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पिशाच">piśāca</a></b>). In one passage of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201836"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201836">3</a></sup> the sense seems to be ‘small piece,’ ‘bit,’ but the St. Petersburg Dictionary suggests that <a class="vei_120a" href="पिशित">piśita</a> here stands for <i>piṣita,</i> equivalent to <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पिष्ट">piṣṭa</a></i> (what is ‘pounded,’ then ‘particle’).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201836"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201836">↑</a> v. 19, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201836"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201836">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 8; xxxv. 18; xxxix. 14. etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201836"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201836">↑</a> vi. 127, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 531; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 376.</li></ol></div>

पिशील|పిశీల|ಪಿಶೀಲ|piśīla|pishiila|pisheela|_vei_120@1837|_vei1_pn533
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1836">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1838">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn532">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn533">1:533</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn534">↷</a><hr><p><b>Piśīla</b> is found in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (ii. 5, 3, 6) as the name of a wooden vessel or dish. In the Lāṭyāyana Śrautā <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (iv. 2, 4. 5) a <a class="vei_120a" href="पिशील">piśīla</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वीणा">vīṇā</a> is mentioned, which seems to have been a kind of guitar, with strings stretched over a body of wood. </p></div>

पिशुन|పిశున|ಪಿಶುನ|piśuna|pishuna|_vei_120@1838|_vei1_pn534
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1837">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1839">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn533">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn534">1:534</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn535">↷</a><hr><p><b>Piśuna,</b> ‘traitor,’ is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201838">1</a></sup> and occasionally later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201838">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201838">↑</a> vii. 104, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201838">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 13; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vii. 6, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 7, 1.</li></ol></div>

पिष्ट|పిష్ట|ಪಿಷ್ಟ|piṣṭa|piShTa|_vei_120@1839|_vei1_pn534
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1838">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1840">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn533">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn534">1:534</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn535">↷</a><hr><p><b>Piṣṭa</b> (‘pounded’), n., ‘meal,’ ‘flour,’ is mentioned in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201839"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201839">1</a></sup> In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201839"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201839">2</a></sup> reference is made to pounded beans (<i>māṣāḥ</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201839"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201839">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 9; Śatapatha Brāhṃaṇa, i. 1, 4, 3; 2, 1, 2; vi. 5, 1, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201839"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201839">↑</a> xii. 2, 53.</li></ol></div>

पीठ|పీఠ|ಪೀಠ|pīṭha|piiTha|peeTha|_vei_120@1840|_vei1_pn534
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1839">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1841">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn533">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn534">1:534</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn535">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pīṭha,</b> ‘stool,’ does not occur as an uncompounded word before the Sūtras, but the compound <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पीठ">pīṭha</a>-sarpin</i> (‘moving about in a little cart’) is found as the designation of a ‘cripple’ in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā (xxx. 21) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 4, 17, 1) in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’).</p></div>

पीतु-दारु|पीतुदारु|पीतु|दारु|పీతు-దారు|పీతుదారు|పీతు|దారు|ಪೀತು-ದಾರು|ಪೀತುದಾರು|ಪೀತು|ದಾರು|pītu-dāru|pītudāru|pītu|dāru|piitu-daaru|piitudaaru|piitu|daaru|peetu-daaru|peetudaaru|peetu|_vei_120@1841|_vei1_pn534
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1840">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1842">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn533">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn534">1:534</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn535">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pītu-dāru</b> is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201841"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201841">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201841"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201841">2</a></sup> denoting the Deodar (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="देव">deva</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="दारु">dāru</a></i>) tree, or, according to others, the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="खदिर">khadira</a></b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उदुम्बर">udumbara</a></b> tree.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201841"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201841">3</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b>Pūtadru.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201841"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201841">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxv. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201841"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201841">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 5, 2, 15; xiii. 4, 4, 5, 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxiv. 13, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201841"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201841">↑</a> Mahīdhara on Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, v. 14; Sāyaṇa on <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 28.</li></ol></div>

पीयूष|పీయూష|ಪೀಯೂಷ|pīyūṣa|piiyuuSha|peeyooSha|_vei_120@1842|_vei1_pn534
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1841">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1843">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn533">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn534">1:534</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn535">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pīyūṣa</b> is found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201842"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201842">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201842"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201842">2</a></sup> in the sense of the first milk of the cow after calving, ‘biestings.’ Usually the term is applied metaphorically to the sap of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> plant.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201842"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201842">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201842"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201842">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> ii. 35, 5, where it is applied figuratively to the mothers of <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201842"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201842">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xix. 15. <i>Cf.</i> Av. viii. 9, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201842"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201842">↑</a> Rv. ii. 13, 1; iii. 48, 2; vi. 47, 4; x. 94, 8, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 110.</li></ol></div>

पीला|పీలా|ಪೀಲಾ|pīlā|piilaa|peelaa|_vei_120@1843|_vei1_pn534
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1842">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1844">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn533">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn534">1:534</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn535">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pīlā</b> occurs once in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201843"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201843">1</a></sup> as the name of an  Apsaras, being no doubt originally a name of some fragrant  plant, like <b>Naladī</b> and <b>Guggulū,</b> two other names of Apsarases given in the same verse.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201843"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201843">↑</a> iv. 37, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 69; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 211.</li></ol></div>

पीलु|పీలు|ಪೀಲು|pīlu|piilu|peelu|_vei_120@1844|_vei1_pn535
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1843">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1845">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn534">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn535">1:535</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn536">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pīlu</b> is the name in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201844">1</a></sup> of a tree (<i>Careya arborea</i> or <i>Salvadora persica</i>) on the fruit of which doves fed.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201844">↑</a> xx. 135, 12. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 62; Weber, <i>Transactions of the Berlin Academy,</i> 1895, 861.</li></ol></div>

पीलुमती|पीलुमति|పీలుమతీ|పీలుమతి|ಪೀಲುಮತೀ|ಪೀಲುಮತಿ|pīlumatī|pīlumati|piilumatii|piilumati|peelumatee|peelumati|_vei_120@1845|_vei1_pn535
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1844">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1846">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn534">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn535">1:535</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn536">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pīlumatī</b> is in the Atharvaveda (xviii. 2, 48) the name of the intermediate heaven lying between the <i>udanuvatī,</i> ‘watery,’ and the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्र">pra</a>-dyauḥ,</i> ‘farthest heaven.’ It presumably means ‘rich in <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पीलु">pīlu</a>.</b>’ <i>Cf.</i> <b>Div.</b></p></div>

पुंश्-चली|पुंश्-चलि|पुंश्चली|पुंश्चलि|पुंश्|चली|चलि|పుంశ్-చలీ|పుంశ్-చలి|పుంశ్చలీ|పుంశ్చలి|పుంశ్|చలీ|చలి|ಪುಂಶ್-ಚಲೀ|ಪುಂಶ್-ಚಲಿ|ಪುಂಶ್ಚಲೀ|ಪುಂಶ್ಚಲಿ|ಪುಂಶ್|ಚಲೀ|ಚಲಿ|puṃś-calī|puṃś-cali|puṃścalī|puṃścali|puṃś|calī|cali|puMsh-chalii|puMsh-chali|puMshchalii|puMshchali|puMsh|chalii|chali|puMsh-chalee|puMshchalee|chalee|_vei_120@1846|_vei1_pn535
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1845">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1847">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn534">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn535">1:535</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn536">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puṃś-calī</b> (‘running after men’) is found in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201846"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201846">1</a></sup> the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201846"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201846">2</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201846"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201846">3</a></sup> to denote a ‘wanton woman.’ In the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201846"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201846">4</a></sup> also occurs the form Puṃścalū. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201846"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201846">↑</a> xxx. 22.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201846"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201846">↑</a> xv. 2, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201846"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201846">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 1, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxvii. 1; Lāṭyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 3, 9, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201846"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201846">↑</a> xxx. 5. 20; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 1, 1; 15, 1; Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiii. 3, 6.</li></ol></div>

पुं-सवन|पुंसवन|पुं|सवन|పుం-సవన|పుంసవన|పుం|సవన|ಪುಂ-ಸವನ|ಪುಂಸವನ|ಪುಂ|ಸವನ|puṃ-savana|puṃsavana|puṃ|savana|puM-savana|puMsavana|puM|_vei_120@1847|_vei1_pn535
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1846">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1848">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn534">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn535">1:535</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn536">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puṃ-savana</b> (‘male-production’ ceremony) is found in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201847"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201847">1</a></sup> in a hymn which is obviously intended to accompany a rite aiming at securing the birth of a male child and which is so applied in the ritual.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201847"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201847">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201847"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201847">↑</a> vi. 11, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201847"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201847">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxxv. 8. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 460; whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 288. The later <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> ritual knows a special rite called <a class="vei_120a" href="पुंसवन">puṃsavana</a>. See Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 13; Sāṅkhāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 20; Gobhila <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 6, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Rituallitteratur,</i> 41.</li></ol></div>

पुक्लक|పుక్లక|ಪುಕ್ಲಕ|puklaka|_vei_120@1848|_vei1_pn535
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1847">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1849">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn534">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn535">1:535</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn536">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puklaka.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पौल्कस">paulkasa</a>.</b></p></div>

पुञ्जि-ष्ठ|पुञ्जिष्ठ|पुञ्जि|ष्ठ|పుఞ్జి-ష్ఠ|పుఞ్జిష్ఠ|పుఞ్జి|ష్ఠ|ಪುಞ್ಜಿ-ಷ್ಠ|ಪುಞ್ಜಿಷ್ಠ|ಪುಞ್ಜಿ|ಷ್ಠ|puñji-ṣṭha|puñjiṣṭha|puñji|ṣṭha|pu~nji-ShTha|pu~njiShTha|pu~nji|ShTha|_vei_120@1849|_vei1_pn535
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1848">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1850">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn534">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn535">1:535</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn536">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puñji-ṣṭha</b> is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201849">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201849">2</a></sup> apparently meaning ‘fisherman,’ though Mahīdhara<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201849">3</a></sup> explains it as ‘bird-catcher.’ <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पौञ्जिष्ठ">pauñjiṣṭha</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201849">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 27; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 4, 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201849">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 7; Pāṇini, viii. 3, 97.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201849">↑</a> On Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 27.</li></ol></div>

पुञ्जील|పుఞ్జీల|ಪುಞ್ಜೀಲ|puñjīla|pu~njiila|pu~njeela|_vei_120@1850|_vei1_pn536
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1849">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1851">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn535">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn536">1:536</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn537">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puñjīla</b> is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201850"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201850">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201850"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201850">2</a></sup> in the sense of a ‘bundle’ of grass, being a variant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पिञ्जूल">piñjūla</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201850"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201850">↑</a> vi. 1, 1, 7; 2, 4, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201850"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201850">↑</a> i. 7, 6, 4; ii. 7, 9, 5.</li></ol></div>

पुण्डरीक|పుణ్డరీక|ಪುಣ್ಡರೀಕ|puṇḍarīka|puNDariika|puNDareeka|_vei_120@1851|_vei1_pn536
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1850">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1852">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn535">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn536">1:536</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn537">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puṇḍarīka</b> denotes the blossom of the lotus in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201851"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201851">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201851"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201851">2</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201851"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201851">3</a></sup> states that the lotus flower is born of the light of the Nakṣatras, and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201851"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201851">4</a></sup> compares the human heart to the lotus.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201851"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201851">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201851"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201851">↑</a> x. 142, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201851"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201851">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 2, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 5, 6; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 3, 10; vi. 3, 14; Chāndogya Upaṇiṣad, i. 6, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 2, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201851"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201851">↑</a> xviii. 9, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201851"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201851">↑</a> x. 8, 43; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, viii. 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201851"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201851">↑</a> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 18, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 2, 1, <i>puṇḍari-srajā</i> denotes a ‘wreath of lotus leaves.’</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 71.</li></ol></div>

पुण्ड्र|పుణ్డ్ర|ಪುಣ್ಡ್ರ|puṇḍra|puNDra|_vei_120@1852|_vei1_pn536
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1851">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1853">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn535">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn536">1:536</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn537">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puṇḍra</b> is the name of a people regarded as outcasts in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201852"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201852">1</a></sup> Their name occurs in the Sūtras also.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201852"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201852">2</a></sup> In the Epic their country corresponds with Bengal and Bihar.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201852"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201852">↑</a> vii. 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 26.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201852"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201852">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 2, 14, <i>Cf.</i> Caland, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 56, 553; Būhler, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 14, 148; Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 394, n. For the later geographical position of the Puṇḍras, <i>cf.</i> the map in Pargiter, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 333.</li></ol></div>

पुत्र|పుత్ర|ಪುತ್ರ|putra|_vei_120@1853|_vei1_pn536
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1852">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1854">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn535">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn536">1:536</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn537">↷</a><hr><p><b>Putra</b> is, with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सूनु">sūnu</a>,</b> the usual name for ‘son’ from the Rigveda onwards.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201853"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201853">1</a></sup> The original sense of the word was apparently ‘small,’ or something analogous.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201853"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201853">2</a></sup> The form Putraka<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201853"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201853">3</a></sup> is often used with the distinct intention of an affectionate address to a younger man, not merely a son proper. Reference is frequently made to the desire for a son.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201853"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201853">4</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201853"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201853">↑</a> Rv. ii. 29, 5; v. 47, 6; vi. 9, 2, etc.; Av. iii. 30, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201853"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201853">↑</a> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 454.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201853"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201853">↑</a> Rv. viii. 69, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 14; vi. 33 (here the narrative uses <a class="vei_120a" href="पुत्र">putra</a>; the reported words have Putraka); Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 6, 1, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 3, 21, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201853"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201853">↑</a> Rv. x. 183, 1; Av. vi. 81, 3; xi. 1, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 5, 6, 1; vii. 1, 8, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 9, 1.</li></ol></div>

पुत्र-सेन|पुत्रसेन|पुत्र|सेन|పుత్ర-సేన|పుత్రసేన|పుత్ర|సేన|ಪುತ್ರ-ಸೇನ|ಪುತ್ರಸೇನ|ಪುತ್ರ|ಸೇನ|putra-sena|putrasena|putra|sena|_vei_120@1854|_vei1_pn537
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1853">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1855">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn536">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn537">1:537</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn538">↷</a><hr><p><b>Putra-sena</b> is the name of a man in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā (iv. 6, 6).</p></div>

पुत्रिका|పుత్రికా|ಪುತ್ರಿಕಾ|putrikā|putrikaa|_vei_120@1855|_vei1_pn537
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1854">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1856">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn536">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn537">1:537</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn538">↷</a><hr><p><b>Putrikā</b> in the later literature<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201855">1</a></sup> has the technical sense of the daughter of a man without sons, whom he gives in marriage on the express terms that her son shall perform the funeral rites for him, and be counted as his. The thing as well as the name is recognized by <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201855">2</a></sup> and traced to the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201855">3</a></sup> But the passages in the Rigveda are of very uncertain meaning,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201855">4</a></sup> and in all probability do not refer to this custom at all.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201855">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Śāstra, ix. 127 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxviii. 20; <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvii. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201855">↑</a> iii. 5 <i>ad fin.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201855">↑</a> i. 124, 7. <i>Cf.</i> iii. 31, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201855">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 34; <i>Rigveda, Kommentar,</i> 48, 49; Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 239 <i>et seq.;</i> Roth, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,' Erlāuterungen,</i> 27; Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 72, 73; Bṛhaddevatā, iv. 110. 111, with Macdonell's note; Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1910, 924, 925; Jolly, <i>Die Adoption in Indien,</i> 32.</li></ol></div>

पुनर्-दत्त|पुनर्दत्त|पुनर्|दत्त|పునర్-దత్త|పునర్దత్త|పునర్|దత్త|ಪುನರ್-ದತ್ತ|ಪುನರ್ದತ್ತ|ಪುನರ್|ದತ್ತ|punar-datta|punardatta|punar|datta|_vei_120@1856|_vei1_pn537
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1855">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1857">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn536">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn537">1:537</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn538">↷</a><hr><p><b>Punar-datta</b> (‘given again’) is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka (viii. 8).</p></div>

पुनर्-भू|पुनर्भू|पुनर्|भू|పునర్-భూ|పునర్భూ|పునర్|భూ|ಪುನರ್-ಭೂ|ಪುನರ್ಭೂ|ಪುನರ್|ಭೂ|punar-bhū|punarbhū|punar|bhū|punar-bhuu|punarbhuu|bhuu|punar-bhoo|punarbhoo|bhoo|_vei_120@1857|_vei1_pn537
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1856">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1858">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn536">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn537">1:537</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn538">↷</a><hr><p><b>Punar-bhū</b> is found in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201857"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201857">1</a></sup> meaning a wife who marries again, a rite being mentioned by which she can ensure reunion with her second (not her first) husband in the next world.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201857"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201857">↑</a> ix. 5, 28. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 537.</li></ol></div>

पुनर्-वसु|पुनर्वसु|पुनर्|वसु|పునర్-వసు|పునర్వసు|పునర్|వసు|ಪುನರ್-ವಸು|ಪುನರ್ವಸು|ಪುನರ್|ವಸು|punar-vasu|punarvasu|punar|vasu|_vei_120@1858|_vei1_pn537
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1857">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1859">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn536">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn537">1:537</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn538">↷</a><hr><p><b>Punar-vasu</b> (‘bringing goods again’), used in the dual, denotes the fifth in the series of the Vedic <b>Nakṣatras,</b> or ‘Lunar Mansions.’ Roth<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201858">1</a></sup> takes the word to have this sense in its only occurrence in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201858">2</a></sup> but this must be regarded as decidedly doubtful. The term is, however, found in the ordinary lists of the Nakṣatras in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201858">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201858">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201858">↑</a> x. 19, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201858">↑</a> Av. xix. 7, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 5, 1, 4; iv. 4, 10, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 2, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, viii. 15; xxxix. 13; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 2, 10, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 289, 290; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 355.</li></ol></div>

पुनः-सर|पुनःसर|पुनः|सर|పునః-సర|పునఃసర|పునః|సర|ಪುನಃ-ಸರ|ಪುನಃಸರ|ಪುನಃ|ಸರ|punaḥ-sara|punaḥsara|punaḥ|sara|punaH-sara|punaHsara|punaH|_vei_120@1859|_vei1_pn538
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1858">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1860">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn537">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn538">1:538</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn540">↷</a><hr><p><b>Punaḥ-sara,</b> ‘recurrent,’ is the epithet of the barking dog in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201859"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201859">1</a></sup> which is told to bark at the thief. It refers, not doubt, to the dog's practice of running to and fro when it barks. It is also applied to a plant, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अपामार्ग">apāmārga</a></b> (<i>Achyranthes aspera</i>), in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201859"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201859">2</a></sup> with the sense of ‘having revertent leaves.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201859"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201859">↑</a> vii. 55, 3; Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 56, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201859"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201859">↑</a> iv. 17, 2; vi. 129, 3; x. 1, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 179. Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 394, prefers the sense of ‘attacking,’ which is the meaning of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रति-सर">prati-sara</a>,</i> Av. viii. 5, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 2, 4, 20.</li></ol></div>

पुमांस्|पुमां|పుమాంస్|పుమాం|ಪುಮಾಂಸ್|ಪುಮಾಂ|pumāṃs|pumāṃ|pumaaMs|pumaaM|_vei_120@1860|_vei1_pn538
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1859">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1861">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn537">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn538">1:538</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn540">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pumāṃs</b> denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201860"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201860">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201860"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201860">2</a></sup> man as the ‘male.’ It has no special reference to marriage like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>,</b> or to heroism like <b>Nṛ</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नर">nara</a>.</b> In grammar it denotes the masculine gender.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201860"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201860">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201860"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201860">↑</a> i. 124, 7; 162, 22; iii. 29, 13; iv. 3, 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201860"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201860">↑</a> Av. iii. 6, 1; 23, 3; iv. 4, 4; vi. 11, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, viii. 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201860"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201860">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 8; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 1, 1, 8; 5, 1, 3; <i>Cf.</i> iv. 5, 2, 10, and <i>puṃṣā nakṣatreṇa,</i> ‘a <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> with a masculine name,’ in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 3, 1.</li></ol></div>

पुर्|పుర్|ಪುರ್|pur|_vei_120@1861|_vei1_pn538
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1860">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1862">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn537">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn538">1:538</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn540">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pur</b> is a word of frequent occurrence in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201861">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201861">2</a></sup> meaning ‘rampart,’ ‘fort,’ or ‘stronghold.’ Such fortifications must have been occasionally of considerable size, as one is called ‘broad’ (<i>pṛthvī</i>) and ‘wide’ (<i>urvī</i>).<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201861">3</a></sup> Elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201861">4</a></sup> a fort ‘made of stone’ (<i>aśmamayī</i>) is mentioned. Sometimes strongholds ‘of iron’ (<i>āyasī</i>) are referred to,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201861">5</a></sup> but these are probably only metaphorical. A fort ‘full of kine’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="गोमती">gomatī</a></i>) is mentioned,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201861">6</a></sup> showing that strongholds were used to hold cattle. ‘Autumnal’ (<i>śāradī</i>) forts are named, apparently as belonging to the <b>Dāsas:</b> this may refer to the forts in that season being  occupied against Āryan attacks or against inundations caused by overflowing rivers. Forts ‘with a hundred walls’ (<i>śatabhuji</i>) are spoken of.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201861">7</a></sup></p><p>It would probably be a mistake to regard these forts as premanently occupied fortified places like the fortresses of the mediæval barony. They were probably merely places of refuge against attack, ramparts of hardened earth with palisades and a ditch (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="देही">dehī</a></b>). Pischel and Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201861">8</a></sup> however, think that there were towns with wooden walls and ditches ( and) like the Indian town of Pāṭaliputra known to Megasthenes<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201861">9</a></sup> and the Pāli texts.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201861">10</a></sup> This is possible, but hardly susceptible of proof, and it is not without significance that the word <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नगर">nagara</a></b> is of late occurrence. On the whole it is hardly likely that in early Vedic times city life was much developed. In the Epic, according to Hopkins,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201861">11</a></sup> there are found the <a class="vei_120a" href="नगर">nagara</a>, ‘city’; <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a>,</b> ‘village’; and <a class="vei_120a" href="घोष">ghoṣa</a>, ‘ranch.’ Vedic literature hardly seems to go beyond the village, no doubt with modifications in its later period.</p><p>The siege of forts is mentioned in the Saṃhitās and Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201861">12</a></sup> According to the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201861">13</a></sup> fire was used. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201861">↑</a> i. 53, 7, 58, 8; 131, 4; 166, 8; iii. 15, 4; iv. 27, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201861">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 7, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 23; ii. 11; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 4, 3; vi. 3, 3, 25; xi. 1, 1, 2, 3; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, viii. 5, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201861">↑</a> i. 189, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201861">↑</a> Rv. iv. 30, 20. Perhaps sun-dried bricks are alluded to by <i>āmā</i> (lit. ‘raw,’ ‘unbaked’) in Rv. ii. 35, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201861">↑</a> Rv. i. 58, 8; ii. 20, 8; iv. 27, 1. vii. 3, 7; 15, 4; 95, 1; x. 101, 8. See Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 2^2, 378 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201861">↑</a> Av. viii. 6, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201861">↑</a> Rv. i. 166, 8; vii. 15, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201861">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, xxii, xxiii, where <i><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षिति">kṣiti</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ध्रुवा">dhruvā</a>,</i> i. 73, 4, is compared.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201861">↑</a> Strabo, p. 702; Arrian, <i>Indica,</i> 10.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201861">↑</a> Mahāparinibbānasutta, p. 12.  <i>Cf.</i> Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India,</i> 262.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201861">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 77; 174 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201861">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 3, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 23; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 4, 3-5; Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 2, 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201861">↑</a> vii. 5, 3. Possibly, in some cases, the palisade was no more than a hedge of thorn or a row of stakes (<i>cf.</i> Rv. x. 101, 8), as suggested by Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 143, 145; and <i>cf.</i> Rv. viii. 53, 5, as corrected by Roth, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 109.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 142148, who compares the fact that neither the Germans (Tacitus, <i>Germania,</i> 16) nor the Slavs (Procopius, <i>De bello Gotico,</i> iii. 14) lived in towns, but, like the ancient Indians, were scattered in villages, each consisting of the houses and steadings of the several families living in the village. The evidence seems pretty convincing. It is true that the Greeks, when we first find them, evidently knew castles and fortresses of the mediæval type; but the Greeks were clearly an invading race, superimposed on an older and in civilization more advanced people (see, <i>e.g.,</i> Burrows, <i>Discoveries in Crete</i>). But the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुर्">pur</a> may, as Zimmer allows, have sometimes been built within the limits of the village. Whether, as he urges (144), the <i>śāradī <a class="vei_120a" href="पुर्">pur</a></i> was a protection against the floods of antumn is uncertain. <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 131, 4; 174, 2; vi. 20, 10. In particular, it is not legitimate to connect the mention of those forts with the fact that the Pūrus lived on either side of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धु">sindhu</a> (Indus), and to assume that Purukutsa's attack on the aborigines was directed against the forts in which they normally protected themselves on the rising of the river. No argument for the large size of cities can be drawn from the mention in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 1, of <i>ekādaśa-<a class="vei_120a" href="द्वार">dvāra</a></i> as an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="पुर">pura</a> (<i>cf.</i> Śvetāśvatara Upaniśad, iii. 18; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नव">nava</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="द्वार">dvāra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पुर">pura</a>,</i> ‘the citadel of nine doors’), because it is used metaphorically of the body, and the number of doors depends on the nature of the body (Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 185). The evidence of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 1, 1, 2, 3, seems rather to point to only one gate in a city.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 412; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 451; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 229; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 203, and <b>Mahāpur.</b></li></ol></div>

पुरं-धि|पुरंधि|पुरं|धि|पुरन्धि|పురం-ధి|పురంధి|పురం|ధి|పురన్ధి|ಪುರಂ-ಧಿ|ಪುರಂಧಿ|ಪುರಂ|ಧಿ|ಪುರನ್ಧಿ|puraṃ-dhi|puraṃdhi|puraṃ|dhi|puraM-dhi|puraMdhi|puraM|purandhi|_vei_120@1862|_vei1_pn540
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1861">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1863">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn538">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn540">1:540</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn541">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puraṃ-dhi</b> occurs in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201862">1</a></sup> possibly as the name of a woman, a protégée of the Aśvins, who gave her a son, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्यहस्त">hiraṇyahasta</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201862">↑</a> i. 116, 13. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 398.</li></ol></div>

पुरय|పురయ|ಪುರಯ|puraya|_vei_120@1863|_vei1_pn540
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1862">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1864">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn538">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn540">1:540</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn541">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puraya</b> is the name of a patron celebrated in a Dānastuti (‘Praise of Gifts’) in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201863"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201863">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201863"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201863">↑</a> vi. 63, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwing, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 158.</li></ol></div>

पुराण|पुराण|పురాణ_౧|పురాణ|ಪುರಾಣ_೧|ಪುರಾಣ|purāṇa_1|purāṇa|puraaNa_1|puraaNa|_vei_120@1864|_vei1_pn540
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1863">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1865">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn538">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn540">1:540</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn541">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Purāṇa,</b> denoting a tale ‘of olden times,’ is often found<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201864"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201864">1</a></sup> in the combination <a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a>, which is probably a Dvandva compound meaning ‘<b><a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a></b> and <a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a>.’ It sometimes<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201864"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201864">2</a></sup> occurs as a separate word, but beside <a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a>, no doubt with the same sense as in the Dvandva. Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201864"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201864">3</a></sup> defines a <a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a> as a tale which deals with the primitive condition of the universe and the creation of the world, but there is no ground for supposing that this view is correct, or for clearly distinguishing <a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a>. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201864"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201864">↑</a> Satāpatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 6, 8; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 4, 1. 2; vii. 1, 2. 4; 2, 1; 7, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201864"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201864">↑</a> Av. xv. 6, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 3, 13; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii 4, 10; iv. 1, 2; 5, 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 9; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 53; <a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>: <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 2, 27; Purāṇavidyā: Āśvalāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201864"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201864">↑</a> Introduction to <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, cited by St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

पुराण|पुराण|పురాణ_౨|పురాణ|ಪುರಾಣ_೨|ಪುರಾಣ|purāṇa_2|purāṇa|puraaNa_2|puraaNa|_vei_120@1865|_vei1_pn540
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1864">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1866">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn538">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn540">1:540</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn541">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Purāṇa</b> is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā (xxxix. 7). </p></div>

पुरीकय|పురీకయ|ಪುರೀಕಯ|purīkaya|puriikaya|pureekaya|_vei_120@1866|_vei1_pn541
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1865">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1867">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn540">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn541">1:541</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn542">↷</a><hr><p><b>Purīkaya</b> is the name of a water animal in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201866">1</a></sup> being clearly a variant of the name that appears as <a class="vei_120a" href="पुलीकय">pulīkaya</a> in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201866">2</a></sup> and as Kulīpaya in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201866">3</a></sup> and as <a class="vei_120a" href="कुलीकय">kulīkaya</a> in the Taittirī, <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201866">4</a></sup> What animal is meant is quite unknown.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201866">↑</a> xi. 2, 25.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201866">↑</a> iii. 14, 2. <a class="vei_120a" href="पुलीका">pulīkā</a>, <i>ibid.,</i> 5, is a variant of <a class="vei_120a" href="कुलीका">kulīkā</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201866">↑</a> xxiv. 21. 35.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201866">↑</a> v. 5, 13, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 96; Bloomfield, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 557; <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 621.</li></ol></div>

पुरीषिणी|पुरीषिणि|పురీషిణీ|పురీషిణి|ಪುರೀಷಿಣೀ|ಪುರೀಷಿಣಿ|purīṣiṇī|purīṣiṇi|puriiShiNii|puriiShiNi|pureeShiNee|pureeShiNi|_vei_120@1867|_vei1_pn541
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1866">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1868">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn540">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn541">1:541</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn542">↷</a><hr><p><b>Purīṣiṇī</b> is found in a hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201867"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201867">1</a></sup> apparently either as the name of a river,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201867"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201867">2</a></sup> or much more probably as an epithet of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरयु">sarayu</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201867"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201867">3</a></sup> meaning, perhaps, ‘abounding in water,’ ‘swollen,’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201867"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201867">4</a></sup> or ‘carrying rubble.’<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201867"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201867">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201867"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201867">↑</a> v. 53, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201867"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201867">↑</a> An alternative suggested by Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201867"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201867">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 17; Geldner, <i>Riveda, Glossar,</i> 111.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201867"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201867">↑</a> Geldner, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201867"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201867">↑</a> Roth, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

पुरु-कुत्स|पुरुकुत्स|पुरु|कुत्स|పురు-కుత్స|పురుకుత్స|పురు|కుత్స|ಪುರು-ಕುತ್ಸ|ಪುರುಕುತ್ಸ|ಪುರು|ಕುತ್ಸ|puru-kutsa|purukutsa|puru|kutsa|_vei_120@1868|_vei1_pn541
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1867">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1869">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn540">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn541">1:541</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn542">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puru-kutsa</b> is the name of a king who is mentioned several times in the Rigveda. In one passage<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201868">1</a></sup> he is mentioned as a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>,</b> but whether as a foe, according to Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201868">2</a></sup> or merely as a contemporary, according to Hillebrandt,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201868">3</a></sup> is uncertain. In two other passages<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201868">4</a></sup> he is mentioned as victorious by divine favour, and in another<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201868">5</a></sup> he appears as a king of the <b>Pūrus</b> and a conqueror of the <b>Dāsas.</b> His son was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201868">6</a></sup> who is accordingly called <b>Paurukutsya</b><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201868">7</a></sup> or <b>Paurukutsi.</b><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201868">8</a></sup> Different conclusions have been drawn from one hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201868">9</a></sup> in which the birth of Purukutsa's son,  <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a>, is mentioned. The usual interpretation is that <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a> was killed in battle or captured, whereupon his wife secured a son to restore the fortunes of the Pūrus. But Sieg<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201868">10</a></sup> offers a completely different interpretation. According to him the word <b>daurgahe,</b> which occurs in the hymn, and which in the ordinary view is rendered ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="दुर्गह">durgaha</a>,’ an ancestor of <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a>, is the name of a horse, the hymn recording the success of an <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) undertaken by <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a> for his wife, as by kings in later times, to secure a son. This interpretation is supported by the version of <i>daurgahe</i> given in the Śatapatha,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201868">11</a></sup> but is by no means certain. Moreover, if <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a> was a contemporary of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>, the defeat of the Pūrus by <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a> in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दाशराज्ञ">dāśarājña</a></b><sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201868">12</a></sup> might well have been the cause of the troubles from which <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्सानी">purukutsānī</a>,</b> by the birth of <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a>, rescued the family. In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201868">13</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a> is called an <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐक्ष्वाक">aikṣvāka</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201868">↑</a> i. 63, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201868">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 174, emending <i>sudāse</i> in the text to <i>sudāsam,</i> plausibly, but not, of course, convincingly.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201868">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 115. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 204. 205, 219.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201868">↑</a> i. 112, 7, 14; 174, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201868">↑</a> vi. 20, 10. <i>Cf.</i> i. 63, 7, where <a class="vei_120a" href="पूरु">pūru</a> also is mentioned. Ludwig suggests reading in vi. 20, 10, <i>saudāsīḥ</i> for <i>dāsīḥ,</i> referring to the forts of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>; but this must be regarded as illegitimate. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift,</i> 55, 330.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201868">↑</a> Rv. iv. 42, 8. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201868">↑</a> Rv. v. 33, 8; viii. 19, 36.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201868">↑</a> Rv. vii. 19, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201868">↑</a> Rv. iv. 42, 8. 9, with Sāyaṇa's note; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 266, 267.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201868">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 96-102.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201868">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201868">↑</a> vii. 18. <i>Cf.</i> also the reference to a <a class="vei_120a" href="पूरु">pūru</a> defeat in vii. 8, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201868">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 5. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इक्ष्वाकु">ikṣvāku</a>, Tryaruṇa,</b> and Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 403.</li></ol></div>

पुरुकुत्सानी|पुरुकुत्सानि|పురుకుత్సానీ|పురుకుత్సాని|ಪುರುಕುತ್ಸಾನೀ|ಪುರುಕುತ್ಸಾನಿ|purukutsānī|purukutsāni|purukutsaanii|purukutsaani|purukutsaanee|_vei_120@1869|_vei1_pn542
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1868">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1870">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn541">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn542">1:542</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn543">↷</a><hr><p><b>Purukutsānī,</b> ‘wife of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a>,</b>’ is mentioned as the mother of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a></b> in one hymn of the Rigveda (iv. 42, 9).</p></div>

पुरु-णीथ शात-वनेय|पुरु-णीथ शातवनेय|पुरु-णीथ शात|वनेय|పురు-ణీథ శాత-వనేయ|పురు-ణీథ శాతవనేయ|పురు-ణీథ శాత|వనేయ|ಪುರು-ಣೀಥ ಶಾತ-ವನೇಯ|ಪುರು-ಣೀಥ ಶಾತವನೇಯ|ಪುರು-ಣೀಥ ಶಾತ|ವನೇಯ|puru-ṇītha śāta-vaneya|puru-ṇītha śātavaneya|puru-ṇītha śāta|vaneya|puru-Niitha shaata-vaneya|puru-Niitha shaatavaneya|puru-Niitha shaata|puru-Neetha shaata-vaneya|puru-Neetha shaatavaneya|puru-Neetha shaata|_vei_120@1870|_vei1_pn542
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1869">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1871">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn541">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn542">1:542</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn543">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puru-ṇītha <a class="vei_120a" href="शात">śāta</a>-vaneya</b> (‘descendant of Śatavani’) is the name of a sacrificer, or perhaps a priest, a <a class="vei_120a" href="भारद्वाज">bhāradvāja</a>, in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201870"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201870">1</a></sup> It is doubtful whether he is also mentioned as a singer in another passage of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201870"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201870">2</a></sup> In both places Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201870"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201870">3</a></sup> sees in <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरु">puru</a>-ṇītha merely a word meaning ‘choral song.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201870"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201870">↑</a> i. 59, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201870"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201870">↑</a> vii. 9. 6. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 160; Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 60.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201870"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201870">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

पुरु-दम|पुरुदम|पुरु|दम|పురు-దమ|పురుదమ|పురు|దమ|ಪುರು-ದಮ|ಪುರುದಮ|ಪುರು|ದಮ|puru-dama|purudama|puru|dama|_vei_120@1871|_vei1_pn542
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1870">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1872">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn541">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn542">1:542</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn543">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puru-dama</b> occurs in the plural in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201871"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201871">1</a></sup> where, according to Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201871"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201871">2</a></sup> it is probably the proper name of the singers, but by Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201871"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201871">3</a></sup> and Whitney<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201871"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201871">4</a></sup> is understood as merely an adjective meaning ‘possessed of many houses’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201871"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201871">↑</a> vii. 73, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201871"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201871">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, xxv.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201871"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201871">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201871"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201871">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 437.</li></ol></div>

पुरु-पन्था|पुरुपन्था|पुरु|पन्था|పురు-పన్థా|పురుపన్థా|పురు|పన్థా|ಪುರು-ಪನ್ಥಾ|ಪುರುಪನ್ಥಾ|ಪುರು|ಪನ್ಥಾ|puru-panthā|purupanthā|puru|panthā|puru-panthaa|purupanthaa|panthaa|_vei_120@1872|_vei1_pn543
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1871">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1873">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn542">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn543">1:543</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn544">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puru-panthā</b> is mentioned as a generous donor to a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a></b> in one hymn of the Rigveda (vi. 63, 10).</p></div>

पुरु-माय्य|पुरुमाय्य|पुरु|माय्य|పురు-మాయ్య|పురుమాయ్య|పురు|మాయ్య|ಪುರು-ಮಾಯ್ಯ|ಪುರುಮಾಯ್ಯ|ಪುರು|ಮಾಯ್ಯ|puru-māyya|purumāyya|puru|māyya|puru-maayya|purumaayya|maayya|_vei_120@1873|_vei1_pn543
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1872">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1874">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn542">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn543">1:543</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn544">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puru-māyya</b> occurs in one hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201873"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201873">1</a></sup> as a protégé of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>. It is quite possible that he was the father of, or at least connected with, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अतिथिग्व">atithigva</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋक्ष">ṛkṣa</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>,</b> who are celebrated in the hymn.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201873"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201873">↑</a> viii. 68, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 163.</li></ol></div>

पुरु-मित्र|पुरुमित्र|पुरु|मित्र|పురు-మిత్ర|పురుమిత్ర|పురు|మిత్ర|ಪುರು-ಮಿತ್ರ|ಪುರುಮಿತ್ರ|ಪುರು|ಮಿತ್ರ|puru-mitra|purumitra|puru|mitra|_vei_120@1874|_vei1_pn543
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1873">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1875">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn542">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn543">1:543</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn544">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puru-mitra</b> is mentioned twice in the Rigveda (i. 117, 20; x. 39, 7) as the father of a maiden who wedded <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विमद">vimada</a>,</b> apparently against her father's will.</p></div>

पुरु|పురు|ಪುರು|puru-mīḷha|purumīḷha|puru|mīḷha|puru-miiḷha|purumiiḷha|miiḷha|puru-meeḷha|purumeeḷha|meeḷha|_vei_120@1875|_vei1_pn543
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1874">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1876">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn542">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn543">1:543</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn544">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puru-mīḷha</b> is mentioned twice in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201875">1</a></sup> as an ancient sage, in which capacity he appears in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201875">2</a></sup> also. Perhaps the same<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201875">3</a></sup> Purumīḷha is intended in an obscure hymn in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201875">4</a></sup> where, according to the legends reported in the Bṛhaddevatā<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201875">5</a></sup> and by Ṣaḍguruśiṣya in his commentary on the Sarvānukramaṇī,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201875">6</a></sup> and by Sāyaṇa in his commentary on the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201875">4</a></sup> he as well as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तरन्त">taranta</a></b> was a son of Vidadaśva, and a patron of the singer <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्यावाश्व">śyāvāśva</a>.</b> The correctness of the legend has been shown to be most improbable by Oldenberg,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201875">7</a></sup> who points out<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201875">8</a></sup> that the legend misinterprets the Rigveda by making Purumīḷha a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैददश्वि">vaidadaśvi</a>, for he is there only compared in generosity to one.</p><p>In another legend found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201875">9</a></sup> and based on a hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201875">10</a></sup> Purumīḷha and <a class="vei_120a" href="तरन्त">taranta</a> appear as persons who received gifts from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ध्वस्र">dhvasra</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुषन्ति">puruṣanti</a>,</b> and as sons of Vidadaśva. The legend, which also occurs in the Śāṭyāyanaka,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201875">11</a></sup> is apparently best explained by  Sieg,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201875">12</a></sup> who says that as the two were kings they could not under the rules of caste accept gifts, unless for the nonce they became singers. The legend has no claim at all, as Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201875">13</a></sup> shows, to validity. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201875">↑</a> i. 151, 2; 183. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201875">↑</a> iv. 29, 4; xviii. 3, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201875">↑</a> See, however, Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 27, n. 3; Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 62, n. 3</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201875">↑</a> v. 61, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201875">↑</a> v. 49 <i>et seq.,</i> with Macdonell's notes.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201875">↑</a> Edition Macdonell, pp. 118 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201875">↑</a> v. 61, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201875">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 353, 354. <i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 359.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201875">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 354, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201875">↑</a> xiii. 7, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201875">↑</a> ix. 58, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201875">↑</a> Cited by Sāyaṇa on Rv., <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201875">↑</a> <i>Op, <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 63.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201875"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201875">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 232, n. 1. He points out, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 354, that the legend is not accepted by the Anukramaṇī (Index), since its list of authors gives <a class="vei_120a" href="अवत्सार">avatsāra</a> as the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>, not the two <a class="vei_120a" href="तरन्त">taranta</a> and Purumīḷha.</li></ol></div>

पुरुष|पूरुष|పురుష|పూరుష|ಪುರುಷ|ಪೂರುಷ|puruṣa|pūruṣa|puruSha|puuruSha|pooruSha|_vei_120@1876|_vei1_pn544
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1875">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1877">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn543">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn544">1:544</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn002">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puruṣa,</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पूरुष">pūruṣa</a>,</b> is the generic term for ‘man’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201876">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201876">2</a></sup> Man is composed of five parts according to the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201876">3</a></sup> or of six according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201876">4</a></sup> or of sixteen,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201876">5</a></sup> or of twenty,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201876">6</a></sup> or of twenty-one,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201876">7</a></sup> or of twenty-four,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201876">8</a></sup> or of twenty-five,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201876">9</a></sup> all more or less fanciful enumerations. Man is the first of animals,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201876">10</a></sup> but also essentially an animal (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पशु">paśu</a></b>). The height of a man is given in the Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201876">11</a></sup> as four Aratnis (‘cubits’) , each of two Padas (‘feet’), each of twelve Aṅgulis (‘finger's breadths’); and the term <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुष">puruṣa</a> itself is found earlier<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201876">12</a></sup> as a measure of length.</p><p><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुष">puruṣa</a> is also applied to denote the length of a man's life, a ‘generation’;<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201876">13</a></sup> the ‘pupil’ in the eye;<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201876">14</a></sup> and in the grammatical literature the ‘person’ of the verb.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201876">15</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201876">↑</a> vii. 104, 15; x. 97. 4. 5. 8; 165, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201876">↑</a> Av. iii. 21, 1; v. 21, 4; viii. 2, 25; 7, 2; xii. 3, 51; 4, 25; xiii. 4, 42, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 1, 5; 2, 2, 8; v. 2, 5, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201876">↑</a> xii. 3, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 5, 26; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 14; vi. 29.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201876">↑</a> ii. 39.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201876">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 4, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201876">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxiii. 14, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201876">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 1, 8, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 1, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 2, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201876">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 2, 1, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201876">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 12, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 2, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201876">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 2, 1, 18; vii. 5, 2, 17. He is the master of animals, <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xx. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201876">↑</a> xvi. 8, 21. 25.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201876">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 5, 14; xiii. 8, 1, 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 2, 5, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201876">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 5, 5; v. 4, 10, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 3, 6; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="द्वि">dvi</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुष">puruṣa</a></i> (‘two generations’), <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201876">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 5, 2. 7. 8; xii. 9, 1, 12; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 3, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201876">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vii. 1, 2.</li></ol></div>

पुरुष मृग|పురుష మృగ|ಪುರುಷ ಮೃಗ|puruṣa mṛga|puruSha mRga|puruSha mRiga|puruSha mRuga|puruSha mRRiga|_vei_120@1877|_vei2_pn002
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1876">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1878">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn544">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn002">2:002</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn003">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puruṣa Mṛga</b> the ‘man wild beast,’ occurs in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201877">1</a></sup> Zimmer's<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201877">2</a></sup> view that the ape is meant seems probable. According to him also, the word <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुष">puruṣa</a> alone, in two passages of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201877">3</a></sup> refers to the ape and its cry (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="मायु">māyu</a></i>); but this sense is not necessary, and it is not adopted by Bloomfield,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201877">4</a></sup> though Whitney<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201877">5</a></sup> does not think the rendering ‘cry of a man’ satisfactory, the term <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मायु">māyu</a></i> not being properly applicable to the noise made by human beings.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201877">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 15, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 16; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 35.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201877">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 85.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201877">↑</a> vi. 38, 4; xix. 39, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201877">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda.</i> 117.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201877"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201877">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 309.</li></ol></div>

पुरुष हस्तिन्|పురుష హస్తిన్|ಪುರುಷ ಹಸ್ತಿನ್|puruṣa hastin|puruSha hastin|_vei_120@1878|_vei2_pn002
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1877">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1879">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn544">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn002">2:002</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn003">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puruṣa Hastin</b> (‘the man with a hand’) is found in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201878"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201878">1</a></sup> It must be the ‘ape.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201878"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201878">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 29; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 8.</li></ol></div>

पुरुषन्ति|పురుషన్తి|ಪುರುಷನ್ತಿ|puruṣanti|puruShanti|_vei_120@1879|_vei2_pn002
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1878">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1880">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei1_pn544">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn002">2:002</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn003">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puruṣanti</b> is a name that occurs twice in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201879"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201879">1</a></sup> in the first passage denoting a protégé of the Aśvins, in the second a patron who gave presents to one of the Vedic singers. In both cases the name is joined with that of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ध्वसन्ति">dhvasanti</a></b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ध्वस्र">dhvasra</a>.</b> The presumption from the manner in which these three names are mentioned is that they designate men, but the grammatical form of the words might equally well be feminine. Females must be meant, if the evidence of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201879"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201879">2</a></sup> is to be taken as decisive, for the form of the first of the two names there occurring, <i>Dhvasre Puruṣantī,</i> ‘Dhvasrā and <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुषन्ति">puruṣanti</a>,’ is exclusively feminine, though <b>here</b> as well as elsewhere Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201879"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201879">3</a></sup> interprets the names as masculines. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तरन्त">taranta</a></b> and <b>Purumīḷha.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201879"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201879">↑</a> i. 112, 23; ix. 58, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201879"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201879">↑</a> xiii. 7, 12. Roth thinks the feminine form <i>Dhvasre</i> here is a corruption, based on the dual form occurring in the Rigveda, <i>Dhvasrayoḥ,</i> which might be feminine as well as masculine.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201879"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201879">↑</a> Also on the Śāṭyāyanaka, cited on Rv. ix. 58, 3, and on Rv. i. 112, 23.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 27, n. 1; Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 62, 63; Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenlāndischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 232, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

पुरु-हन्मन्|पुरुहन्मन्|पुरु|हन्मन्|పురు-హన్మన్|పురుహన్మన్|పురు|హన్మన్|ಪುರು-ಹನ್ಮನ್|ಪುರುಹನ್ಮನ್|ಪುರು|ಹನ್ಮನ್|puru-hanman|puruhanman|puru|hanman|_vei_120@1880|_vei2_pn003
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1879">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1881">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn002">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn003">2:003</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn004">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puru-hanman</b> is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> in a hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201880">1</a></sup> an <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आंगिरस">āṅgirasa</a>,</b> according to the Rigvedic Anukramaṇī (Index), but according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201880">2</a></sup> a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैखानस">vaikhānasa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201880">↑</a> viii. 70,</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201880">↑</a> xiv. 9, 29. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 107.</li></ol></div>

पुरू-रवस्|पुरू-रव|पुरूरवस्|पुरूरव|पुरू|रवस्|रव|పురూ-రవస్|పురూ-రవ|పురూరవస్|పురూరవ|పురూ|రవస్|రవ|ಪುರೂ-ರವಸ್|ಪುರೂ-ರವ|ಪುರೂರವಸ್|ಪುರೂರವ|ಪುರೂ|ರವಸ್|ರವ|purū-ravas|purū-rava|purūravas|purūrava|purū|ravas|rava|puruu-ravas|puruu-rava|puruuravas|puruurava|puruu|puroo-ravas|puroo-rava|purooravas|puroorava|puroo|_vei_120@1881|_vei2_pn003
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1880">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1882">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn002">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn003">2:003</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn004">↷</a><hr><p><b>Purū-ravas</b> is the name of a hero in a hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201881"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201881">1</a></sup> containing a curious dialogue between him and a nymph, Urvaśī, an Apsaras. He is also mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201881"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201881">2</a></sup> where several verses of the Rigvedic dialogue find a setting in a continuous story. In the later literature he is recognized as a king.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201881"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201881">3</a></sup> His name is perhaps intended in one other passage of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201881"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201881">4</a></sup> It is impossible to say whether he is a mythical figure pure and simple, or really an ancient king. His epithet, Aiḷa,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201881"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201881">5</a></sup> ‘descendant of Iḍā’ (a sacrificial goddess), is certainly in favour of the former alternative.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201881"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201881">↑</a> x. 95.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201881"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201881">↑</a> xi. 5, 1, 1. <i>Cf.</i> iii. 4, 1, 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, viii. 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, x. 46.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201881"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201881">↑</a> See Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 283 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201881"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201881">↑</a> i. 31, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201881"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201881">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 1, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 196; Max Müller, <i>Chips,</i> 42, 109 <i>et seq.;</i> Kuhn, <i>Die Herabkunft des Feuers,</i> 85 <i>et seq.;</i> Roth, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, Erläuterungen,</i> 153; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> 124, 135; Oldenberg, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 46, 28, 323.</li></ol></div>

पुरूरु|పురూరు|ಪುರೂರು|purūru|puruuru|purooru|_vei_120@1882|_vei2_pn003
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1881">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1883">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn002">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn003">2:003</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn004">↷</a><hr><p><b>Purūru</b> is the name of a poet, an <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आत्रेय">ātreya</a>,</b> in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201882"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201882">1</a></sup> according to Ludwig.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201882"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201882">2</a></sup> But the only form of the word found, <i>purūruṇā,</i> seems merely an adverb meaning ‘far and wide.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201882"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201882">↑</a> v. 70, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201882"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201882">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 126. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 215, n. 1; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 360.</li></ol></div>

पुरू-वसु|पुरूवसु|पुरू|वसु|పురూ-వసు|పురూవసు|పురూ|వసు|ಪುರೂ-ವಸು|ಪುರೂವಸು|ಪುರೂ|ವಸು|purū-vasu|purūvasu|purū|vasu|puruu-vasu|puruuvasu|puruu|puroo-vasu|puroovasu|puroo|_vei_120@1883|_vei2_pn003
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1882">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1884">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn002">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn003">2:003</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn004">↷</a><hr><p><b>Purū-vasu</b> (‘abounding in wealth’) is the name of a poet, an <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आत्रेय">ātreya</a>,</b> according to Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201883"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201883">1</a></sup> in one passage of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201883"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201883">2</a></sup> But this is very doubtful. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201883"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201883">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 126.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201883"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201883">↑</a> v. 36, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 215, n. 1; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 333.</li></ol></div>

पुरो-डाश्|पुरोडाश्|पुरो|डाश्|పురో-డాశ్|పురోడాశ్|పురో|డాశ్|ಪುರೋ-ಡಾಶ್|ಪುರೋಡಾಶ್|ಪುರೋ|ಡಾಶ್|puro-ḍāś|puroḍāś|puro|ḍāś|puro-Daash|puroDaash|Daash|_vei_120@1884|_vei2_pn004
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1883">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1885">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn003">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn004">2:004</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn005">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puro-ḍāś</b> is the name of the sacrificial cake in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201884"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201884">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201884"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201884">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201884"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201884">↑</a> iii. 28, 2; 41, 3; 52, 2; iv. 24, 5; vi. 23, 7; viii. 31, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201884"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201884">↑</a> Av. ix. 6, 12; x. 9, 25; xii. 4, 35; xviii. 4, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 2, 8; vii. 1, 9, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 85; xxviii. 23, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 270.</li></ol></div>

पुरो-धा|पुरोधा|पुरो|धा|పురో-ధా|పురోధా|పురో|ధా|ಪುರೋ-ಧಾ|ಪುರೋಧಾ|ಪುರೋ|ಧಾ|puro-dhā|purodhā|puro|dhā|puro-dhaa|purodhaa|dhaa|_vei_120@1885|_vei2_pn004
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1884">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1886">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn003">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn004">2:004</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn005">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puro-dhā</b> denotes the office of <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, ‘domestic priest.’ Its mention as early as the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201885"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201885">1</a></sup> and often later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201885"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201885">2</a></sup> shows that the post was a fully recognized and usual one.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201885"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201885">↑</a> v. 24, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201885"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201885">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 2, 9; vii. 4, 1, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 1, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 3, 12; 9, 27; xv. 4, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 31; viii. 24, 27; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 4, 5.</li></ol></div>

पुरो-'नुवाक्या|पुरो'नुवाक्या|पुरो|'नुवाक्या|పురో-'నువాక్యా|పురో'నువాక్యా|పురో|'నువాక్యా|ಪುರೋ-'ನುವಾಕ್ಯಾ|ಪುರೋ'ನುವಾಕ್ಯಾ|ಪುರೋ|'ನುವಾಕ್ಯಾ|puro-'nuvākyā|puro'nuvākyā|puro|'nuvākyā|puro-'nuvaakyaa|puro'nuvaakyaa|'nuvaakyaa|_vei_120@1886|_vei2_pn004
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1885">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1887">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn003">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn004">2:004</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn005">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puro-'nuvākyā</b> (‘introductory verse to be recited’) is the technical term for the address to a god inviting him to partake of the offering; it was followed by the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="याज्या">yājyā</a>,</b> which accompanied the actual oblation.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201886"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201886">1</a></sup> Such addresses are not unknown, but are rare, according to Oldenberg,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201886"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201886">2</a></sup> in the Rigveda; subsequently they are regular, the word itself occurring in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201886"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201886">3</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201886"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201886">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201886"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201886">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 387, 388.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201886"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201886">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 243 <i>et seq.,</i> against Bergaigne, <i>Recherches surl'llistoire de la liturgie védique,</i> 13 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201886"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201886">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 10, 4; ii. 2, 9, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xx. 12, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201886"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201886">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 17; ii. 13, 26; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 1, 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 5, 2, 21, etc.</li></ol></div>

पुरो-रुच्|पुरोरुच्|पुरो|रुच्|పురో-రుచ్|పురోరుచ్|పురో|రుచ్|ಪುರೋ-ರುಚ್|ಪುರೋರುಚ್|ಪುರೋ|ರುಚ್|puro-ruc|puroruc|puro|ruc|puro-ruch|puroruch|ruch|_vei_120@1887|_vei2_pn004
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1886">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1888">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn003">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn004">2:004</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn005">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puro-ruc</b> is the technical description of certain <b><a class="vei_120a" href="निविद्">nivid</a></b> verses which were recited at the morning libation in the <a class="vei_120a" href="आज्य">ājya</a> and Praüga ceremonies before the hymn (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सूक्त">sūkta</a></i>) of the litany or its parts. It occurs in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201887">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201887">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 5, 10, 13; vii. 2, 7, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 39; iii. 9; iv. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 1, 4, 5; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 3, 15; 2, 1, 8; v. 4, 4, 20, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Rituallitteratur,</i> 102.</li></ol></div>

पुरो-वात|पुरोवात|पुरो|वात|పురో-వాత|పురోవాత|పురో|వాత|ಪುರೋ-ವಾತ|ಪುರೋವಾತ|ಪುರೋ|ವಾತ|puro-vāta|purovāta|puro|vāta|puro-vaata|purovaata|vaata|_vei_120@1888|_vei2_pn005
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1887">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1889">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn004">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn005">2:005</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn008">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puro-vāta,</b> the ‘east wind,’ is mentioned in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201888"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201888">1</a></sup> Geldner<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201888"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201888">2</a></sup> thinks it merely means the wind preceding the rains.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201888"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201888">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 11, 3; ii. 4, 7, 1; iv. 3, 3, 1; 4, 6, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 1, 5; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 2, 18; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 3, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201888"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201888">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 120, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

पुरो-हित|पुरोहित|पुरो|हित|పురో-హిత|పురోహిత|పురో|హిత|ಪುರೋ-ಹಿತ|ಪುರೋಹಿತ|ಪುರೋ|ಹಿತ|puro-hita|purohita|puro|hita|_vei_120@1889|_vei2_pn005
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1888">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1890">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn004">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn005">2:005</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn008">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puro-hita</b> (‘placed in front,’ ‘appointed’) is the name of a priest in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201889">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201889">2</a></sup> The office of <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> is called Purohiti<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201889">3</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोधा">purodhā</a>.</b> It is clear that the primary function of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> was that of ‘domestic priest’ of a king, or perhaps a great noble; his quite exceptional position is shown by the fact that only one <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> seems ever to be mentioned in Vedic literature.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201889">4</a></sup> Examples of Purohitas in the Rigveda are <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a></b><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201889">5</a></sup> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a></b><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201889">6</a></sup> in the service of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a></b> king; <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>,</b> of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तृत्सु">tṛtsu</a></b> family; the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> of <b>Kuruśravaṇa;</b><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201889">7</a></sup> and <b>Devāpi,</b> the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> of <b>Śantanu.</b><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201889">8</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> was in all religious matters the <i>alter ego</i> of the king. In the ritual<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201889">9</a></sup> it is laid down that a king must have a <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, else the gods will not accept his offerings. He ensures the king's safety and victory in battle by his prayers;<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201889">10</a></sup> he procures  the fall of rain for the crops;<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201889">11</a></sup> he is the flaming fire that guards the kingdom.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201889">12</a></sup> Divodāsa in trouble is rescued by <a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a>;<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201889">13</a></sup> and King <b>Tryaruṇa <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रैधात्व">traidhātva</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐक्ष्वाक">aikṣvāka</a></b> reproaches his <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वृश जान">vṛśa jāna</a>,</b> when his car runs over a Brahmin boy and kills him.<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201889">14</a></sup> The close relation of king and <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> is illustrated by the case of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुत्स औरव">kutsa aurava</a>,</b> who slew his <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपगु सौश्रवस">upagu sauśravasa</a>,</b> for disloyalty in serving <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, to whom <a class="vei_120a" href="कुत्स">kutsa</a> was hostile.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201889">15</a></sup> Other disputes between kings and priests who officiated for them are those of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनमेजय">janamejaya</a></b> and the <b>Kaśyapas,</b> and of <b>Viśvantara</b> and the <b>Śyāparṇas;</b><sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1201889">16</a></sup> and between <b>Asamāti</b> and the <b>Gaupāyanas.</b><sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1201889">17</a></sup> In some cases one <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> served more than one king; for example, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="देवभाग श्रौतर्ष">devabhāga śrautarṣa</a></b> was the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> of the <b>Kurus</b> and the <b>Sṛṅjayas</b> at the same time,<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1201889">18</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जल जातूकर्ण्य">jala jātūkarṇya</a></b> was the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> of the kings of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1201889">19</a></sup></p><p>There is no certain proof that the office of <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> was hereditary in a family, though it probably was so.<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1201889">20</a></sup> At any rate, it seems clear from the relations of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> with King <b>Kuruśravaṇa,</b> and with his son <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपमश्रवस्">upamaśravas</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1201889">21</a></sup> that a king would keep on the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> of his father.</p><p>Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1201889">22</a></sup> thinks that the king might act as his own <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, as shown by the case of King Viśvantara, who sacrificed without the help of the Śyāparṇas,<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1201889">23</a></sup> and that a <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> need not be a priest, as shown by the case of Devāpi and Śantanu.<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1201889">24</a></sup> But neither opinion seems to be justified. It is not said that  Viśvantara sacrificed without priests, while Devāpi is not regarded as a king until the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1201889">25</a></sup> and there is no reason to suppose that Yāska's view expressed in that work is correct.</p><p>According to Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_1201889">26</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> from the beginning acted as the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> priest in the sacrificial ritual, being there the general superintendent of the sacrifice. In favour of this view, he cites the fact that <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> is mentioned both as <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a><sup id="ref_120_27_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_1201889">27</a></sup> and as <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>:<sup id="ref_120_28_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_1201889">28</a></sup> at the sacrifice of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a></b> he served as <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>,<sup id="ref_120_29_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29_vei_1201889">29</a></sup> but he was the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>;<sup id="ref_120_30_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_30_vei_1201889">30</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="बृहस्पति">bṛhaspati</a> is called the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a><sup id="ref_120_31_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_31_vei_1201889">31</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a><sup id="ref_120_32_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_32_vei_1201889">32</a></sup> of the gods; and the Vasiṣṭhas who are Purohitas are also the Brahmans at the sacrifice.<sup id="ref_120_33_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_33_vei_1201889">33</a></sup> It is thus clear that the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> was often the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>; and it was natural that this should be the case when once the Brahman's place became, as it did in the later ritual, the most important position at the sacrifice.<sup id="ref_120_34_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_34_vei_1201889">34</a></sup> But the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> can hardly be said to have held this place in the earlier ritual; Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_35_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_35_vei_1201889">35</a></sup> seems to be right in holding that the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> was originally the <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a> priest, the singer <i>par excellence,</i> when he took any part at all in the ritual of the great sacrifices with the <b>Ṛtvijs.</b> So Devāpi seems clearly to have  been a <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a>;<sup id="ref_120_36_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_36_vei_1201889">36</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> is at once <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a><sup id="ref_120_37_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_37_vei_1201889">37</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a>;<sup id="ref_120_38_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_38_vei_1201889">38</a></sup> and the ‘two divine Hotṛs’ referred to in the Āprī litanies are also called the ‘two Purohitas.’<sup id="ref_120_39_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_39_vei_1201889">39</a></sup> Later, no doubt, when the priestly activity ceased to centre in the song, the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, with his skill in magic, became the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>, who also required magic to undo the errors of the sacrifice.<sup id="ref_120_40_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_40_vei_1201889">40</a></sup></p><p>There is little doubt that in the original growth of the priesthood the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> played a considerable part. In historical times he represented the real power of the kingship, and may safely be deemed to have exercised great influence in all public affairs, such as the administration of justice and the king's conduct of business. But it is not at all probable that the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> represents, as Roth<sup id="ref_120_41_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_41_vei_1201889">41</a></sup> and Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_42_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_42_vei_1201889">42</a></sup> thought, the source which gave rise to caste. The priestly class is already in existence in the Rigveda (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a></b>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201889">↑</a> i. 1, 1; 44, 10. 12; ii. 24, 9; iii. 2, 8; 3, 2; v. 11, 2; vi. 70, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201889">↑</a> Av. viii. 5, 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ix. 23; xi. 81; xxxi. 20; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 24, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 12; vii. 15.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201889">↑</a> Rv. vii. 60, 12; 83, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201889">↑</a> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 144, thinks that several Purohitas were possible, quoting Sāyaṇa, on Rv. x. 57, 1, who gives the tale of the Gaupāyanas and King Asamāti from the Śāṭyāyanaka, and comparing the case of <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> as Purohitas, probably contemporaneously, of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>. But that the two were contemporaneous is most unlikely, especially if we adopt the very probable view of Hopkins (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 260 <i>et seq.</i>) that <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> was with the ten kings (Rv. vii. 18) when they unsuccessfully attacked <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>. The other narrative has, as Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 375, n. 3, observes, a markedly fictitious character; while every other passage that mentions a <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> speaks of him in the singular, and as there was only one <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> priest at the sacrifice, so the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> acted as <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201889">↑</a> iii. 33. 53. <i>Cf.</i> vii. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201889">↑</a> Rv. vii. 18. 83.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201889">↑</a> Rv. x. 33. See Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 150, 184.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201889">↑</a> Rv. x. 98.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201889">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 24.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201889">↑</a> See Av. iii. 19; Rv. vii. 18, 13, from which Geldner, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 135, n. 3, concludes that the priest prayed in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a>,</b> ‘house of assembly,’ while the king fought on the field of battle. See Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 12. 19. 20. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पूरु">pūru</a>,</b> n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201889">↑</a> Rv. x. 98.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201889">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 24. 25.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201889">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xv. 3, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201889">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> xiii. 3, 12. See Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 64, <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201889">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> xiv. 6, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1201889">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 27. 35.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1201889">↑</a> See Śāṭyāyanaka, cited by Sāyaṇa, on Rv. x. 57, 1; and <i>cf.</i> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 167 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 41).</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1201889">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 4, 5. According to Sāyaṇa, on Rv. i. 81, 3. is was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राहूगण">rāhūgaṇa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गोतम">gotama</a></b> who was <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>; but this is hardly more than a mere blunder. See Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 152; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 2, 9, n.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1201889">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 29, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1201889">↑</a> See Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 375, who compares the permanent character of the relation of the king and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> with that of husband and wife, as shown in the ritual laid down in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 27.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1201889">↑</a> See Rv. x. 33, and n. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1201889">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 195, 196.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1201889">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 27; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 436-440.</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1201889">↑</a> Rv. x. 98.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1201889">↑</a> ii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_1201889">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 144; 3, 155. <i>Cf.</i> Pischel, <i>Go7ttingisehe Gelehrte Anzeigen,</i> 1894, 420; Hillebrandt, <i>Rituallitteratur,</i> 13. Rv. i. 94, 6, does not prove that the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> was a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋत्विज्">ṛtvij</a>: it merely shows that he could be one when he wished.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_1201889">↑</a> Rv. x. 150, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_1201889">↑</a> Rv. vii. 33, 11. But this need mean no more than Brahmin.</li><li id="fx_120_29_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29_vei_1201889">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 16, 1; Sāṅkhāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 21, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_30_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_30_vei_1201889">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 11 14.</li><li id="fx_120_31_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_31_vei_1201889">↑</a> Rv. ii. 24, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 17, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 1, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 1, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiv. 23, 1</li><li id="fx_120_32_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_32_vei_1201889">↑</a> Rv. x. 141, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 13; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 4, 21; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 6, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_33_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_33_vei_1201889">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 5, 2, 1. This point is not in the parallel versions, <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 17 (but <i>cf.</i> xxvii. 4: <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्म">brahma</a>-purohitaṃ kṣatram,</i> unless this means ‘the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्र">kṣatra</a> is inferior to the Brahma’); <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xv. 5, 24, and <i>cf.</i> Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 2, 13. The <a class="vei_120a" href="अथर्वन्">atharvan</a> literature (Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> lx, lxi) requires a follower of that <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a> to act as <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>, and the spells of the <a class="vei_120a" href="अथर्वन्">atharvan</a> are, in fact, closely allied to the spells of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> as represented in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 24-28. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 193, 195.</li><li id="fx_120_34_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_34_vei_1201889">↑</a> See Bloomfield, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> lviii, lxii, lxv, lxviii <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_35_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_35_vei_1201889">↑</a> <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 380, 381.</li><li id="fx_120_36_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_36_vei_1201889">↑</a> Rv. x. 98; and <i>cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 6, 8; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 12, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_37_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_37_vei_1201889">↑</a> Rv. i. 1, 1; iii. 3, 2; 11, 1; v. 11, 2. In viii. 27, 1; x. 1, 6, he is called <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, and credited with the characteristic activities of the <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a> priest.</li><li id="fx_120_38_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_38_vei_1201889">↑</a> Rv. i. 1, 1; iii. 3, 2; 11, 1; v. 11, 2. etc.</li><li id="fx_120_39_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_39_vei_1201889">↑</a> Rv. x. 66, 13; 70, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_40_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_40_vei_1201889">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 26.</li><li id="fx_120_41_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_41_vei_1201889">↑</a> <i>Zur Litteratur und Geschichte des Weda,</i> 117 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_42_vei_1201889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_42_vei_1201889">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 195.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 168, 169; 195 <i>et seq.;</i> Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 485; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 31-35; 138; Haug, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्म">brahma</a> und die Brahmanen,</i> 9 <i>et seq.;</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 144; Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 374-383; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> lxx <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

पुलस्ति|పులస్తి|ಪುಲಸ್ತಿ|pulasti|_vei_120@1890|_vei2_pn008
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1889">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1891">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn005">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn008">2:008</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn009">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pulasti</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201890"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201890">1</a></sup> or <b>Pulastin</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201890"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201890">2</a></sup> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās denotes ‘wearing the hair plain,’ as opposed to <i>kapardin,</i> ‘wearing the hair in braids.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201890"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201890">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 9, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 43.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201890"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201890">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhītā, xvii. 15. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 265.</li></ol></div>

पुलिन्द|పులిన్ద|ಪುಲಿನ್ದ|pulinda|_vei_120@1891|_vei2_pn008
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1890">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1892">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn005">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn008">2:008</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn009">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pulinda</b> is the name of an outcast tribe mentioned with the <b>Andhras</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201891">1</a></sup> but not in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201891">2</a></sup> in connexion with the story of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a>.</b> The Pulindas again appear associated with the Andhras in the time of Aśoka.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201891">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201891">↑</a> vii. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201891">↑</a> xv. 26.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201891">↑</a> Vincent Smith, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 56, 652.</li></ol></div>

पुलीकय|పులీకయ|ಪುಲೀಕಯ|pulīkaya|puliikaya|puleekaya|_vei_120@1892|_vei2_pn009
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1891">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1893">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn008">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn009">2:009</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn010">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pulīkaya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरीकय">purīkaya</a>.</b></p></div>

पुलीका|పులీకా|ಪುಲೀಕಾ|pulīkā|puliikaa|puleekaa|_vei_120@1893|_vei2_pn009
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1892">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1894">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn008">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn009">2:009</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn010">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pulīkā</b> seems to designate some kind of bird in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā (iii. 14, 5). The name appears in the form of <a class="vei_120a" href="कुलीका">kulīkā</a> in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā (xxiv. 24).</p></div>

पुलुष प्राचीन-योग्य|पुलुष प्राचीनयोग्य|पुलुष प्राचीन|योग्य|పులుష ప్రాచీన-యోగ్య|పులుష ప్రాచీనయోగ్య|పులుష ప్రాచీన|యోగ్య|ಪುಲುಷ ಪ್ರಾಚೀನ-ಯೋಗ್ಯ|ಪುಲುಷ ಪ್ರಾಚೀನಯೋಗ್ಯ|ಪುಲುಷ ಪ್ರಾಚೀನ|ಯೋಗ್ಯ|puluṣa prācīna-yogya|puluṣa prācīnayogya|puluṣa prācīna|yogya|puluSha praachiina-yogya|puluSha praachiinayogya|puluSha praachiina|puluSha praacheena-yogya|puluSha praacheenayogya|puluSha praacheena|_vei_120@1894|_vei2_pn009
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1893">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1895">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn008">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn009">2:009</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn010">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puluṣa <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राचीन">prācīna</a>-yogya</b> (‘descendant of Prācīnayoga’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृति">dṛti</a> Aindroti <a class="vei_120a" href="शौनक">śaunaka</a>,</b> in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 40, 2). He taught <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पौलुषि">pauluṣi</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सत्ययज्ञ">satyayajña</a>.</b></p></div>

पुष्कर|పుష్కర|ಪುಷ್ಕರ|puṣkara|puShkara|_vei_120@1895|_vei2_pn009
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1894">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1896">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn008">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn009">2:009</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn010">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puṣkara</b> is the name in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201895"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201895">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201895"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201895">2</a></sup> of the blue lotus flower. The Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201895"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201895">3</a></sup> mentions its sweet perfume. The lotus grew in lakes, which were thence called <i>puṣkariṇī,</i> ‘lotus-bearing.’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201895"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201895">4</a></sup> That the flower was early used for personal adornment is shown by an epithet of the Aśvins, ‘lotuscrowned’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पुष्कर">puṣkara</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="स्रज्">sraj</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201895"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201895">5</a></sup></p><p>Presumably because of its likeness in shape to the flower of the lotus, the bowl of the ladle is called <a class="vei_120a" href="पुष्कर">puṣkara</a>, perhaps already in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201895"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201895">6</a></sup> and certainly in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201895"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201895">7</a></sup> Moreover, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201895"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201895">8</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="पुष्कर">puṣkara</a> means ‘water,’ a sense actually found in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201895"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201895">9</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201895"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201895">↑</a> vi. 16, 13; vii. 33, 11, may be so taken, though Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 3, and Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 112, prefer to see in these passages a reference to the bowl of the sacrificial ladle.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201895"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201895">↑</a> Av. xi. 3, 8; xii. 1, 24; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 1, 4, 1; 2, 6, 5; 6, 4, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xi. 29; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 1, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 5, 1, 16; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 1, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201895"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201895">↑</a> Av. xii. 1, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201895"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201895">↑</a> Rv. v. 78, 7; x. 107, 10; Av. iv. 34, 5; v. 16, 17; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 3, 11, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201895"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201895">↑</a> Rv. x. 184, 2; Av. iii. 22, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 5, 16, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201895"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201895">↑</a> Rv. viii 72, 11, where the sense is doubtful, and the bowl of the ladle is not particularly appropriate. See also note 1.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201895"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201895">↑</a> vii. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201895"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201895">↑</a> v. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201895"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201895">↑</a> vi. 4, 2, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 71.</li></ol></div>

पुष्कर-साद|पुष्करसाद|पुष्कर|साद|పుష్కర-సాద|పుష్కరసాద|పుష్కర|సాద|ಪುಷ್ಕರ-ಸಾದ|ಪುಷ್ಕರಸಾದ|ಪುಷ್ಕರ|ಸಾದ|puṣkara-sāda|puṣkarasāda|puṣkara|sāda|puShkara-saada|puShkarasaada|puShkara|saada|_vei_120@1896|_vei2_pn009
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1895">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1897">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn008">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn009">2:009</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn010">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puṣkara-sāda,</b> ‘sitting on the lotus,’ is the name of an animal in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’)  in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201896">1</a></sup> It can hardly be a ‘snake,’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201896">2</a></sup> but rather either, as Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201896">3</a></sup> thinks, a ‘bird,’ or perhaps, according to the commentator on the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1201896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1201896">1b</a></sup> a ‘bee.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201896">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 14, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 12; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201896">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 95, so takes it.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201896">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1201896"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1201896">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 14, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 12; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 5.</li></ol></div>

पुष्टि-गु|पुष्टिगु|पुष्टि|పుష్టి-గు|పుష్టిగు|పుష్టి|ಪುಷ್ಟಿ-ಗು|ಪುಷ್ಟಿಗು|ಪುಷ್ಟಿ|puṣṭi-gu|puṣṭigu|puṣṭi|puShTi-gu|puShTigu|puShTi|_vei_120@1897|_vei2_pn010
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1896">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1898">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn009">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn010">2:010</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn011">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puṣṭi-gu</b> is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> mentioned in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वालखिल्य">vālakhilya</a> hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201897"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201897">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201897"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201897">↑</a> viii. 51, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 140, 141.</li></ol></div>

पुष्प|పుష్ప|ಪುಷ್ಪ|puṣpa|puShpa|_vei_120@1898|_vei2_pn010
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1897">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1899">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn009">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn010">2:010</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn011">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puṣpa</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201898">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201898">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘flower’ generally.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201898">↑</a> viii. 7, 12. <i>Cf.</i> x. 8, 34.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201898">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxii. 28; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 4, 1; xv. 3, 23; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 4, 4, 2; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 1, 2; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

पुष्य|పుష్య|ಪುಷ್ಯ|puṣya|puShya|_vei_120@1899|_vei2_pn010
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1898">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1900">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn009">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn010">2:010</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn011">↷</a><hr><p><b>Puṣya</b> is the name in the Atharvaveda (xix. 7, 2 for the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a></b> called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तिष्य">tiṣya</a></b> elsewhere.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 371. On <a class="vei_120a" href="तिष्य">tiṣya</a>, see also <i>Journal of the Royal Asatic Society,</i> 1911, 514-518; 794-800.</p></div>

पूत-क्रता|पूतक्रता|पूत|क्रता|పూత-క్రతా|పూతక్రతా|పూత|క్రతా|ಪೂತ-ಕ್ರತಾ|ಪೂತಕ್ರತಾ|ಪೂತ|ಕ್ರತಾ|pūta-kratā|pūtakratā|pūta|kratā|puuta-krataa|puutakrataa|puuta|krataa|poota-krataa|pootakrataa|poota|_vei_120@1900|_vei2_pn010
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1899">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1901">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn009">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn010">2:010</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn011">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pūta-kratā</b> is the name of a woman in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वालखिल्य">vālakhilya</a> hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201900"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201900">1</a></sup> perhaps the wife of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पूतक्रतु">pūtakratu</a>,</b> but this is doubtful, since the more regular form would be Pūtakratāyī,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201900"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201900">2</a></sup> which Scheftelowitz<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201900"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201900">3</a></sup> reads in the hymn.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201900"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201900">↑</a> viii. 64, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201900"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201900">↑</a> Pāṇini, iv. 1, 36.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201900"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201900">↑</a> <i>Die Apokryphen des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 41, 42.</li></ol></div>

पूत-क्रतु|पूतक्रतु|पूत|क्रतु|పూత-క్రతు|పూతక్రతు|పూత|క్రతు|ಪೂತ-ಕ್ರತು|ಪೂತಕ್ರತು|ಪೂತ|ಕ್ರತು|pūta-kratu|pūtakratu|pūta|kratu|puuta-kratu|puutakratu|puuta|poota-kratu|pootakratu|poota|_vei_120@1901|_vei2_pn010
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1900">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1902">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn009">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn010">2:010</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn011">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pūta-kratu</b> (‘of clear insight’) is the name of a patron in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201901"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201901">1</a></sup> apparently the son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201901"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201901">↑</a> viii. 68, 17. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 163. Scheftelowitz, <i>Die Apokryphen des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 41, reads <a class="vei_120a" href="पूतक्रतु">pūtakratu</a> for <a class="vei_120a" href="पौतक्रत">pautakrata</a> in Rv. viii. 56, 2, but this is improbable. See Oldenberg, <i>Go7ttingische Gelekrte Anzeigen,</i> 1907, 237, 238; Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 39, n. 4.</li></ol></div>

पूति-रज्जु|पूतिरज्जु|पूति|रज्जु|పూతి-రజ్జు|పూతిరజ్జు|పూతి|రజ్జు|ಪೂತಿ-ರಜ್ಜು|ಪೂತಿರಜ್ಜು|ಪೂತಿ|ರಜ್ಜು|pūti-rajju|pūtirajju|pūti|rajju|puuti-rajju|puutirajju|puuti|pooti-rajju|pootirajju|pooti|_vei_120@1902|_vei2_pn011
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1901">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1903">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn010">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn011">2:011</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn013">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pūti-rajju</b> is the name of a certain plant of unknown kind in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201902"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201902">1</a></sup> according to Roth.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201902"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201902">2</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201902"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201902">3</a></sup> treats it as a ‘putrid rope,’ but Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201902"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201902">4</a></sup> suggests that a snake is meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201902"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201902">↑</a> viii. 8, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201902"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201902">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201902"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201902">↑</a> xvi. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201902"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201902">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 527. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney's Translation of the Rigveda, 503; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 583.</li></ol></div>

पूतीक|పూతీక|ಪೂತೀಕ|pūtīka|puutiika|pooteeka|_vei_120@1903|_vei2_pn011
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1902">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1904">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn010">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn011">2:011</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn013">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pūtīka</b> is the name of a plant often mentioned<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201903"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201903">1</a></sup> as a substitute for the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> plant. It is also given in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201903"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201903">2</a></sup> as a means of making milk curdle, being an alternative to the bark of the <i>Butea frondosa (<a class="vei_120a" href="पर्ण">parṇa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वल्क">valka</a>).</i> It is usually identified with the <i>Guilandina Bonduc,</i> but Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201903"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201903">3</a></sup> makes it out to be the <i>Basella Cordifolia.</i></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201903"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201903">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxiv. 3 (<i>pūtika,</i>  as quoted in the St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i>); Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 1, 2, 12. <i>Cf.</i> iv. 5, 10, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 4, 1; ix. 5, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201903"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201903">↑</a> ii. 5, 3, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201903"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201903">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 24, n. 3. <i>Cf.</i> Roth, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 35, 689; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 63, 276.</li></ol></div>

पूतु-द्रु|पूतुद्रु|पूतु|द्रु|పూతు-ద్రు|పూతుద్రు|పూతు|ద్రు|ಪೂತು-ದ್ರು|ಪೂತುದ್ರು|ಪೂತು|ದ್ರು|pūtu-dru|pūtudru|pūtu|dru|puutu-dru|puutudru|puutu|pootu-dru|pootudru|pootu|_vei_120@1904|_vei2_pn011
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1903">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1905">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn010">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn011">2:011</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn013">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pūtu-dru</b> is another name for the Deodar (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="देव">deva</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="दारु">dāru</a></i>) in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201904"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201904">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201904"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201904">2</a></sup> The longer form, <a class="vei_120a" href="पूतु">pūtu</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="दारु">dāru</a>, is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201904"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201904">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201904"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201904">↑</a> viii. 2, 28.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201904"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201904">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 8, 4 (in 6 the fruit is meant); Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 8, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201904"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201904">↑</a> viii. 15; lviii. 15. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 59.</li></ol></div>

पूरु|పూరు|ಪೂರು|pūru|puuru|pooru|_vei_120@1905|_vei2_pn011
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1904">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1906">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn010">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn011">2:011</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn013">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pūru</b> is the name of a people and their king in the Rigveda. They are mentioned with the <b>Anus, Druhyus, Turvaśas,</b> and <b>Yadus</b> in one passage.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201905">1</a></sup> They also occur as enemies of the <b>Tṛtsus</b> in the hymn of <b>Sudās’</b> victory.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201905">2</a></sup> In another  hymn<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201905">3</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> of the <b>Bharatas</b> is celebrated as victorious over the Pūrus, probably a reference to the same decisive overthrow. On the other hand, victories of the Pūrus over the aborigines seem to be referred to in several passages.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201905">4</a></sup></p><p>The great kings of the Pūrus were <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a></b> and his son <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a>,</b> whose name bears testimony to his prowess against aboriginal foes, while a later prince was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तृक्षि">tṛkṣi</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रासदस्यव">trāsadasyava</a>.</b></p><p>In the Rigveda the Pūrus are expressly<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201905">5</a></sup> mentioned as on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>.</b> Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201905">6</a></sup> thinks that the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धु">sindhu</a></b> (Indus) is meant in this passage. But Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201905">7</a></sup> and Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201905">8</a></sup> with much greater probability think that the eastern <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> in <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a> is meant. This view accords well with the sudden disappearance of the name of the Pūrus from Vedic tradition, a disappearance accounted for by Oldenberg's<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201905">9</a></sup> conjecture that the Pūrus became part of the great <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a> people, just as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रिवि">krivi</a></b> disappear from the tradition on their being merged in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a></b> nation. <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रासदस्यव">trāsadasyava</a>, the patronymic of <b>Kuruśravaṇa</b> in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201905">10</a></sup> shows that the royal families of the Kurus and the Pūrus were allied by intermarriage.</p><p>Hillebrandt,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201905">11</a></sup> admitting that the Pūrus in later times lived in the eastern country round the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>, thinks that in earlier days they were to be found to the west of the Indus with <b>Divodāsa.</b> This theory must fall with the theory that Divodāsa was in the far west. It might, however, be held to be supported by the fact that Alexander found a --that is, a Paurava prince on the Hydaspes,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201905">12</a></sup> a sort of half-way locality between the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> and the West. But it is quite simple to suppose either that the Hydaspes was the earlier home of the Pūrus, where some remained after the others had  wandered east, or that the later Paurava represents a successful onslaught upon the west from the east.</p><p>In several other passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201905">13</a></sup> the Pūrus as a people seem to be meant. The <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1201905">14</a></sup> recognizes the general sense of ‘man,’ but in no passage is this really necessary or even probable. So utterly, however, is the tradition lost that the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1201905">15</a></sup> explains <a class="vei_120a" href="पूरु">pūru</a> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1201905">16</a></sup> as an <a class="vei_120a" href="असुर">asura</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="रक्षस्">rakṣas</a>; it is only in the Epic that <a class="vei_120a" href="पूरु">pūru</a> revives as the name of a son of <a class="vei_120a" href="ययाति">yayāti</a> and Śarmiṣṭhā.<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1201905">17</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201905">↑</a> i. 108. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201905">↑</a> vii. 18, 13. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a>.</b> Apparently, as Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 263, n., and Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 135, think, in this verse the words <i>jeṣma Pūruṃ vidathe mṛdhravācam</i> refer to the <a class="vei_120a" href="पूरु">pūru</a> king and to the priest <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>,</b> who prayed for the defeat of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>, though in vain. Hopkins seems to take the words <i>vidathe mṛdhravācam</i> generally as ‘the false speaker in the assembly’; but, according to Geldner, the meaning intended is that, while the king fought, the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> prayed in the <a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a>, or meeting house of the people.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201905">↑</a> Rv. vii. 8, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201905">↑</a> i. 59, 6; 131, 4; 174, 2; iv. 21, 10; 38, 1; vi. 20, 10; vii. 5, 3; 19, 3. <i>Cf.</i> note 13.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201905">↑</a> vii. 96, 2. Perhaps they are also meant as living on the <a class="vei_120a" href="शर्यणावन्त्">śaryaṇāvant</a> in Rv. viii. 64, 10. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201905">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 124.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201905">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 175.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201905">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 50, 115; 3, 374.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201905">↑</a> <i>Buddha,</i> 404. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, 3, 205.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201905">↑</a> x. 33, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201905">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 114 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201905">↑</a> Arrian, <i>Indica,</i> viii. 4; ix. 1; xix. 3, etc. See Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 132, 133.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201905">↑</a> In Rv. i. 36, 1, <i>Pūrūṇām</i> might be read for <i>purūṇām,</i> with improvement in the sense. In i. 63, 7, there is a reference to the <a class="vei_120a" href="पूरु">pūru</a> king, <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>, but in what relation is uncertain (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a></b>). In i. 130, 7. the <a class="vei_120a" href="पूरु">pūru</a> king and <a class="vei_120a" href="दिवोदास अतिथिग्व">divodāsa atithigva</a> are both mentioned, apparently as victorious over aboriginal foes. See also i. 129, 5; iv. 39, 2; v. 17, 1; vi. 46, 8; x. 4, 1; 48, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1201905">↑</a> vii. 23; Naighaṇṭuka, ii. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1201905">↑</a> vi. 8, 1, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1201905">↑</a> vii. 8, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1201905"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1201905">↑</a> Pargiter, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1910, 26, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 110 <i>et seq.;</i> Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 398.</li></ol></div>

पूरुष|పూరుష|ಪೂರುಷ|pūruṣa|puuruSha|pooruSha|_vei_120@1906|_vei2_pn013
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1905">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1907">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn011">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn013">2:013</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn014">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pūruṣa</b> has in several passages<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201906"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201906">1</a></sup> the sense of ‘menial’ or ‘dependent,’ like the English ‘man.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201906"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201906">↑</a> Rv. vi. 39, 5 (<i>cf.,</i> however, Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 43); x. 97, 4; Av. iv. 9, 7; x. 1, 17; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 3, 1, 22, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 383.</li></ol></div>

पूर्ण-मास|पूर्णमास|पूर्ण|मास|పూర్ణ-మాస|పూర్ణమాస|పూర్ణ|మాస|ಪೂರ್ಣ-ಮಾಸ|ಪೂರ್ಣಮಾಸ|ಪೂರ್ಣ|ಮಾಸ|pūrṇa-māsa|pūrṇamāsa|pūrṇa|māsa|puurNa-maasa|puurNamaasa|puurNa|maasa|poorNa-maasa|poorNamaasa|poorNa|_vei_120@1907|_vei2_pn013
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1906">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1908">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn011">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn013">2:013</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn014">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pūrṇa-māsa</b> denotes the full moon and the festival of that day, occurring frequently in the later Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201907"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201907">1</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201907"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201907">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 7, 2; ii. 2, 10, 2; 5, 4, 1; iii. 4, 4, 1; vii. 4, 8, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 1, 14; iii. 5, 7, 13; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 2, 4, 8, etc.</li></ol></div>

पूर्त|పూర్త|ಪೂರ್ತ|pūrta|puurta|poorta|_vei_120@1908|_vei2_pn013
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1907">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1909">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn011">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn013">2:013</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn014">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pūrta,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201908"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201908">1</a></sup> or <b>Pūrti,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201908"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201908">2</a></sup> occurs in the Rigveda and later denoting the reward to the priest for his services. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दक्षिणा">dakṣiṇā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201908"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201908">↑</a> Rv. vi. 16, 18; viii. 46, 21; Av. vi. 123, 5; ix. 5, 13; 6, 31; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 64; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 21, 24, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201908"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201908">↑</a> Rv. vi. 13, 6; x. 107, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 2, 3, 2; ii. 4, 7, 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

पूर्-पति|पूर्पति|पूर्|पति|పూర్-పతి|పూర్పతి|పూర్|పతి|ಪೂರ್-ಪತಿ|ಪೂರ್ಪತಿ|ಪೂರ್|ಪತಿ|pūr-pati|pūrpati|pūr|pati|puur-pati|puurpati|puur|poor-pati|poorpati|poor|_vei_120@1909|_vei2_pn013
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1908">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1910">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn011">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn013">2:013</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn014">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pūr-pati,</b> ‘lord of the fort,’ occurring only once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201909"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201909">1</a></sup> is of somewhat doubtful interpretation. The term may denote a regular office,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201909"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201909">2</a></sup> similar to that of the <b>Grāmaṇī:</b> the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुर्">pur</a></b> would then be a permanently occupied settlement. The expression may, however, merely mean the chief over a fort when it was actually occupied against hostile attack. The rarity of the word seems to favour the latter sense.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201909"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201909">↑</a> i. 173. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201909"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201909">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Sāyaṇa's note, on Rv. i. 173, 10; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 456. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 204.</li></ol></div>

पूर्व-पक्ष|पूर्वपक्ष|पूर्व|पक्ष|పూర్వ-పక్ష|పూర్వపక్ష|పూర్వ|పక్ష|ಪೂರ್ವ-ಪಕ್ಷ|ಪೂರ್ವಪಕ್ಷ|ಪೂರ್ವ|ಪಕ್ಷ|pūrva-pakṣa|pūrvapakṣa|pūrva|pakṣa|puurva-pakSha|puurvapakSha|puurva|pakSha|poorva-pakSha|poorvapakSha|poorva|puurva-paxa|puurvapaxa|paxa|_vei_120@1910|_vei2_pn014
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1909">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1911">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn013">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn014">2:014</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn015">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pūrva-pakṣa</b> denotes the first half of the month. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></p></div>

पूर्व-वयस|पूर्ववयस|पूर्व|वयस|పూర్వ-వయస|పూర్వవయస|పూర్వ|వయస|ಪೂರ್ವ-ವಯಸ|ಪೂರ್ವವಯಸ|ಪೂರ್ವ|ವಯಸ|pūrva-vayasa|pūrvavayasa|pūrva|vayasa|puurva-vayasa|puurvavayasa|puurva|poorva-vayasa|poorvavayasa|poorva|_vei_120@1911|_vei2_pn014
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1910">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1912">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn013">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn014">2:014</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn015">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pūrva-vayasa,</b> the ‘first period of life,’ is a term used in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201911"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201911">1</a></sup> to denote ‘youth.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201911"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201911">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xix. 4, 3; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 2, 3, 4; 9, 1, 8; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पूर्व">pūrva</a>-vayasin,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8, 13, 3. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, v. 3, 3, where <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वत्स">vatsa</a></i> and <i>tṛtīya,</i> ‘the third (stage),’ are used to cover ‘youth’ and ‘old age,’ as opposed to manhood, when the knowledge of the doctrines of the Āraṇyaka is to be imparted.</li></ol></div>

पूर्व-वह्|पूर्ववह्|पूर्व|वह्|పూర్వ-వహ్|పూర్వవహ్|పూర్వ|వహ్|ಪೂರ್ವ-ವಹ್|ಪೂರ್ವವಹ್|ಪೂರ್ವ|ವಹ್|pūrva-vah|pūrvavah|pūrva|vah|puurva-vah|puurvavah|puurva|poorva-vah|poorvavah|poorva|_vei_120@1912|_vei2_pn014
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1911">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1913">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn013">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn014">2:014</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn015">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pūrva-vah</b> is a term applied to the horse (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्व">aśva</a></b>) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201912"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201912">1</a></sup> and elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201912"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201912">2</a></sup> It may either refer to a horse fastened in front as a ‘leader,’ or merely mean ‘drawing (a chariot) for the first time,’ as understood by the commentator on the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201912"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201912">↑</a> i. 1, 5, 6</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201912"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201912">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 4, 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xiii. 3. <i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

पूर्वाह्ण|పూర్వాహ్ణ|ಪೂರ್ವಾಹ್ಣ|pūrvāhṇa|puurvaahNa|poorvaahNa|_vei_120@1913|_vei2_pn014
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1912">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1914">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn013">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn014">2:014</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn015">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pūrvāhṇa,</b> ‘the earlier (part of the) day,’ ‘forenoon,’ is a common designation of time from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201913"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201913">1</a></sup> onwards.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201913"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201913">2</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अहन्">ahan</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201913"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201913">↑</a> x. 34, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201913"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201913">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 20; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 3, 12; iii. 4. 4, 2; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 11, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, viii. 9, etc.</li></ol></div>

पूल्य|पूल्प|పూల్య|పూల్ప|ಪೂಲ್ಯ|ಪೂಲ್ಪ|pūlya|pūlpa|puulya|puulpa|poolya|poolpa|_vei_120@1914|_vei2_pn014
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1913">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1915">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn013">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn014">2:014</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn015">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pūlya,</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पूल्प">pūlpa</a>,</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201914"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201914">1</a></sup> seems to mean ‘shrivelled grain’ (<i>cf.</i> <b>Lājā</b>). </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201914"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201914">↑</a> xiv. 2, 63. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 765.</li></ol></div>

पृक्ष|పృక్ష|ಪೃಕ್ಷ|pṛkṣa|pRkSha|pRikSha|pRukSha|pRRikSha|pRxa|_vei_120@1915|_vei2_pn015
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1914">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1916">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn014">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn015">2:015</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn016">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pṛkṣa</b> (literally, perhaps ‘swift’) is the proper name of a man in an obscure verse of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201915"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201915">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201915"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201915">↑</a> ii. 13, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 97.</li></ol></div>

पृक्ष-याम|पृक्षयाम|पृक्ष|याम|పృక్ష-యామ|పృక్షయామ|పృక్ష|యామ|ಪೃಕ್ಷ-ಯಾಮ|ಪೃಕ್ಷಯಾಮ|ಪೃಕ್ಷ|ಯಾಮ|pṛkṣa-yāma|pṛkṣayāma|pṛkṣa|yāma|pRkSha-yaama|pRkShayaama|pRkSha|yaama|pRikSha-yaama|pRikShayaama|pRikSha|pRukSha-yaama|pRukShayaama|pRukSha|pRRikSha-yaama|pRRikShayaama|pRRikSha|pRxa-yaama|pRxayaama|pRxa|_vei_120@1916|_vei2_pn015
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1915">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1917">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn014">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn015">2:015</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn016">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pṛkṣa-yāma</b> occurs once in the plural in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201916">1</a></sup> Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201916">2</a></sup> suggests the sense of ‘faring with swift steeds,’ and thinks a proper name is meant. Pischel<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201916">3</a></sup> holds that the word is an epithet of the <b>Pajras,</b> and that it means ‘performing splendid sacrifices.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201916">↑</a> i. 122, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201916">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201916">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 97, 98.</li></ol></div>

पृड|పృడ|ಪೃಡ|pṛḍa|pRDa|pRiDa|pRuDa|pRRiDa|_vei_120@1917|_vei2_pn015
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1916">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1918">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn014">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn015">2:015</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn016">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pṛḍa.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मृड">mṛḍa</a>.</b></p></div>

पृत्|పృత్|ಪೃತ್|pṛt|pRt|pRit|pRut|pRRit|_vei_120@1918|_vei2_pn015
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1917">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1919">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn014">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn015">2:015</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn016">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pṛt</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201918"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201918">1</a></sup> and Pṛtanā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201918"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201918">2</a></sup> denote, in the Rigveda and later, ‘contest,’ whether in arms or in the chariot race. Pṛtanā has also the concrete sense of ‘army’ in some passages;<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201918"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201918">3</a></sup> in the Epic system<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201918"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201918">4</a></sup> it denotes a definite body of men, elephants, chariots, and horses. Pṛtanājya<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201918"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201918">5</a></sup> has only the sense of ‘combat.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201918"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201918">↑</a> Only in the locative, Rv. ii. 27, 15; 26, 1; iii. 49, 3; vi. 20, 1, etc.; <i>pṛtsuṣn,</i> i. 129, 4 (with double case-ending).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201918"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201918">↑</a> Rv. i. 85, 8; 91, 21; 119, 10; 152. 7; ii. 40, 5; iii. 24, 1; vi. 41, 5; x. 29, 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xi. 76; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xv. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 1, 6; 2, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201918"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201918">↑</a> Rv. vii. 20, 3; viii. 36, 1; 37, 2; Av. vi. 97, 1; viii. 5, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ix. 24; perhaps also as neuter in <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 7, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201918"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201918">↑</a> Mahābhārata, i. 291.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201918"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201918">↑</a> Rv. iii. 8, 10; 37, 7; vii. 99, 4; viii. 12, 25; ix. 102, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 4, 1.</li></ol></div>

पृथ|పృథ|ಪೃಥ|pṛtha|pRtha|pRitha|pRutha|pRRitha|_vei_120@1919|_vei2_pn015
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1918">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1920">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn014">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn015">2:015</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn016">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pṛtha,</b> the ‘palm’ of the hand in the sense of its breath, is used as a measure of length in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Brāhmaṇā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201919">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201919">↑</a> i. 6, 4, 2, 3; <i>cf.</i> Kātyāyana Śrauṭa <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, vi. 1, 28; Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 2, 7; viii. 5, 10.</li></ol></div>

पृथवान|పృథవాన|ಪೃಥವಾನ|pṛthavāna|pRthavaana|pRithavaana|pRuthavaana|pRRithavaana|_vei_120@1920|_vei2_pn015
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1919">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1921">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn014">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn015">2:015</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn016">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pṛthavāna</b> is in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201920"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201920">1</a></sup> the name of a man, perhaps also called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दुःशीम">duḥśīma</a>,</b> but this is uncertain. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पृथि">pṛthi</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201920"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201920">↑</a> x. 93, 14. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 433.</li></ol></div>

पृथि|పృథి|ಪೃಥಿ|pṛthi|pRthi|pRithi|pRuthi|pRRithi|_vei_120@1921|_vei2_pn016
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1920">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1922">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn015">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn016">2:016</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn017">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pṛthi,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201921">1</a></sup> <b>Pṛthī,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201921">2</a></sup> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पृथु">pṛthu</a></b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201921">3</a></sup> is the name of a semi-mythical personage who is mentioned in the Rigveda and later as a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>, and more specially as the inventor of agriculture<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201921">4</a></sup> and the lord of both worlds, of men and of animals.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201921">5</a></sup> He bears in several passages<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201921">6</a></sup> the epithet <a class="vei_120a" href="वैन्य">vainya</a>, ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="वेन">vena</a>,’ and must probably be regarded as a culture hero rather than as a real man. According to other accounts,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201921">7</a></sup> he was the first of consecrated kings. <i>Cf.</i> <b>Pārthiva.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201921">↑</a> Rv. i. 112, 15, as a seer; as <a class="vei_120a" href="वैन्य">vainya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 7, 4, and perhaps ii. 7, 5, 1 (<i>Pṛthaye</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201921">↑</a> As <a class="vei_120a" href="वैन्य">vainya</a>, Rv. viii. 9, 10; Av. viii. 10, 24; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 19; as <a class="vei_120a" href="पृथि">pṛthi</a> or Pṛthī, <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 5, 1; as <a class="vei_120a" href="वैन्य">vainya</a>, Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 5, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 4 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 463). Venya mentioned with Pṛthī in Rv. x. 148, 5, may be meant for his patronymic (= <a class="vei_120a" href="वैन्य">vainya</a>): <i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुग्र्य">tugrya</a>,</b> n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201921">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 186 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 19, 125); Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 10, 9; 34, 6; 45, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201921">↑</a> Av., <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201921">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>. Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 5, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201921">↑</a> See notes 1-3.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201921">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 7, 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 166; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 221, 222; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 50, n. 2; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 134. Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 81, gives the name as Pṛthin <a class="vei_120a" href="वैन्य">vainya</a>; but the oblique cases, when found, are all in favour of <a class="vei_120a" href="पृथि">pṛthi</a> or Pṛthī as the stem.</li></ol></div>

पृथिवी|पृथिवि|పృథివీ|పృథివి|ಪೃಥಿವೀ|ಪೃಥಿವಿ|pṛthivī|pṛthivi|pRthivii|pRthivi|pRthivee|pRithivii|pRithivi|pRuthivii|pRuthivi|pRRithivii|pRRithivi|_vei_120@1922|_vei2_pn016
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1921">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1923">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn015">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn016">2:016</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn017">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pṛthivī</b> denotes the ‘earth’ as the ‘broad’ one in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201922">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201922">2</a></sup> being often personified as a deity<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201922">3</a></sup> both alone and with <b>Div,</b> ‘heaven,’ as Dyāvā-<a class="vei_120a" href="पृथिवी">pṛthivī</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201922">4</a></sup> Mention is often made of three earths,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201922">5</a></sup> of which the world on which we live is the highest.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201922">6</a></sup> The earth is girdled by the ocean, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201922">7</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201922">8</a></sup> places one of the three earths in each of the worlds into which the universe is divided  (see <b>Div</b>). In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201922">9</a></sup> the earth is called the ‘firstborn of being,’ and its riches (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वित्त">vitta</a></i>) are referred to;<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201922">10</a></sup> hence in a late passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1201922">11</a></sup> the earth is styled <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वसु">vasu</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="मती">matī</a>,</i> ‘full of wealth.’ The word also occurs in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1201922">12</a></sup> though rarely, in the form of Pṛthvī.<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1201922">13</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201922">↑</a> Rv. vii. 7, 2, 5; 99, 3; v. 85, 1, 5; viii. 89, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201922">↑</a> Av. xii. 1, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xi. 53, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201922">↑</a> Rv. iv. 3, 5; 51, 11; v. 49, 5; 84, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> vi. 50, 13, 14; vii. 34, 23, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xii. 103, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201922">↑</a> Rv. iv. 56, 1; vii. 53, 1, etc. See Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> pp. 20, 21, 123, 126.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201922">↑</a> Rv. i. 34, 8; iv. 53, 5; vii. 104, 11; Av. iv. 20, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, v. 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201922">↑</a> Av. vi. 21, 1; xix. 27, 3; 32, 4; 53, 5; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 5, 1, 31; v. 1, 5, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201922">↑</a> viii. 20. This idea is not found in the Saṃhitās, Macdonell, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> p. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201922">↑</a> ix. 31; xi. 36; xii. 30; Naighaṇṭuka, v. 3, 5, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Bruce, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 19, 321 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201922">↑</a> xiv. 1, 2, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201922">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 6, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1201922">↑</a> xiii. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1201922">↑</a> vi. 12, 5; x. 187, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 34.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1201922"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1201922">↑</a> The regular adjectival feminine form of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पृथु">pṛthu</a>,</i> ‘broad.’</li></ol></div>

पृथु|పృథు|ಪೃಥು|pṛthu|pRthu|pRithu|pRuthu|pRRithu|_vei_120@1923|_vei2_pn017
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1922">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1924">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn016">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn017">2:017</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn018">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pṛthu.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पृथि">pṛthi</a>.</b> Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201923"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201923">1</a></sup> also finds a mention of the Pṛthus as a tribe, allied with the <b>Parśus,</b> in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201923"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201923">2</a></sup> as opponents of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तृत्सु">tṛtsu</a> Bharatas.</b> But this interpretation is certainly incorrect.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201923"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201923">3</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्शु">parśu</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201923"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201923">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 196 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201923"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201923">↑</a> vii. 83, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201923"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201923">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 134 <i>et seq.;</i> 433, 434; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 184, n. 3; Bergaigne, <i>Religion Védique,</i> 2, 362, n.</li></ol></div>

पृथु-श्रवस्|पृथु-श्रवस्|पृथु-श्रव|पृथुश्रवस्|पृथुश्रव|पृथु|श्रवस्|श्रव|పృథు-శ్రవస్_౧|పృథు-శ్రవస్|పృథు-శ్రవ|పృథుశ్రవస్|పృథుశ్రవ|పృథు|శ్రవస్|శ్రవ|ಪೃಥು-ಶ್ರವಸ್_೧|ಪೃಥು-ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಪೃಥು-ಶ್ರವ|ಪೃಥುಶ್ರವಸ್|ಪೃಥುಶ್ರವ|ಪೃಥು|ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಶ್ರವ|pṛthu-śravas_1|pṛthu-śravas|pṛthu-śrava|pṛthuśravas|pṛthuśrava|pṛthu|śravas|śrava|pRthu-shravas_1|pRthu-shravas|pRthu-shrava|pRthushravas|pRthushrava|pRthu|shravas|shrava|pRithu-shravas_1|pRithu-shravas|pRithu-shrava|pRithushravas|pRithushrava|pRithu|pRuthu-shravas_1|pRuthu-shravas|pRuthu-shrava|pRuthushravas|pRuthushrava|pRuthu|pRRithu-shravas_1|pRRithu-shravas|pRRithu-shrava|pRRithushravas|pRRithushrava|pRRithu|_vei_120@1924|_vei2_pn017
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1923">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1925">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn016">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn017">2:017</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn018">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Pṛthu-śravas</b> (‘far-famed’) is mentioned in connexion with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वश">vaśa</a></b> in two hymns<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201924"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201924">1</a></sup> of the Rigveda. In the second passage the generosity of <a class="vei_120a" href="पृथुश्रवस्">pṛthuśravas</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कानीत">kānīta</a> to <a class="vei_120a" href="वश अश्व्य">vaśa aśvya</a> is celebrated, and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201924"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201924">2</a></sup> refers to the episode.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201924"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201924">↑</a> i. 116, 21; viii. 46, 21. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 162.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201924"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201924">↑</a> xvi. 11, 13.</li></ol></div>

पृथु-श्रवस् दौरे-श्रवस|पृथु-श्रवस् दौरे-श्रवस|पृथु-श्रवस् दौरेश्रवस|पृथु-श्रवस् दौरे|श्रवस|పృథు-శ్రవస్ దౌరే-శ్రవస_౨|పృథు-శ్రవస్ దౌరే-శ్రవస|పృథు-శ్రవస్ దౌరేశ్రవస|పృథు-శ్రవస్ దౌరే|శ్రవస|ಪೃಥು-ಶ್ರವಸ್ ದೌರೇ-ಶ್ರವಸ_೨|ಪೃಥು-ಶ್ರವಸ್ ದೌರೇ-ಶ್ರವಸ|ಪೃಥು-ಶ್ರವಸ್ ದೌರೇಶ್ರವಸ|ಪೃಥು-ಶ್ರವಸ್ ದೌರೇ|ಶ್ರವಸ|pṛthu-śravas daure-śravasa_2|pṛthu-śravas daure-śravasa|pṛthu-śravas daureśravasa|pṛthu-śravas daure|śravasa|pRthu-shravas daure-shravasa_2|pRthu-shravas daure-shravasa|pRthu-shravas daureshravasa|pRthu-shravas daure|shravasa|pRithu-shravas daure-shravasa_2|pRithu-shravas daure-shravasa|pRithu-shravas daureshravasa|pRithu-shravas daure|pRuthu-shravas daure-shravasa_2|pRuthu-shravas daure-shravasa|pRuthu-shravas daureshravasa|pRuthu-shravas daure|pRRithu-shravas daure-shravasa_2|pRRithu-shravas daure-shravasa|pRRithu-shravas daureshravasa|pRRithu-shravas daure|_vei_120@1925|_vei2_pn017
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1924">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1926">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn016">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn017">2:017</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn018">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Pṛthu-<a class="vei_120a" href="श्रवस्">śravas</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दौरे">daure</a>-śravasa</b> (‘descendant of Dūreśravas’) is the name of the Udgātṛ priest at the snake festival mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201925"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201925">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201925"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201925">↑</a> xxv. 15, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 35.</li></ol></div>

पृदाकु|పృదాకు|ಪೃದಾಕು|pṛdāku|pRdaaku|pRidaaku|pRudaaku|pRRidaaku|_vei_120@1926|_vei2_pn017
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1925">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1927">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn016">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn017">2:017</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn018">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pṛdāku,</b> the name of a ‘snake’ in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201926"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201926">1</a></sup> is mentioned in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’), in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201926"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201926">2</a></sup> and occasionally else where.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201926"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201926">3</a></sup> Its skin was specially valuable, according to the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201926"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201926">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201926"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201926">↑</a> i. 27, 1; iii. 27, 3; vi. 38, 1; vii. 56, 1; x. 4, 11 <i>et seq.;</i> xii. 3, 57.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201926"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201926">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 10, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 14; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 33.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201926"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201926">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, vi. 12; Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, xii. 27.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201926"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201926">↑</a> i. 27. 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 94.</li></ol></div>

पृदाकु-सानु|पृदाकुसानु|पृदाकु|सानु|పృదాకు-సాను|పృదాకుసాను|పృదాకు|సాను|ಪೃದಾಕು-ಸಾನು|ಪೃದಾಕುಸಾನು|ಪೃದಾಕು|ಸಾನು|pṛdāku-sānu|pṛdākusānu|pṛdāku|sānu|pRdaaku-saanu|pRdaakusaanu|pRdaaku|saanu|pRidaaku-saanu|pRidaakusaanu|pRidaaku|pRudaaku-saanu|pRudaakusaanu|pRudaaku|pRRidaaku-saanu|pRRidaakusaanu|pRRidaaku|_vei_120@1927|_vei2_pn018
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1926">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1928">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn017">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn018">2:018</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn019">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pṛdāku-sānu,</b> ‘having the surface of a snake,’ is taken by Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201927">1</a></sup> and Griffith<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201927">2</a></sup> as the name of the institutor of a sacrifice in one hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201927">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201927">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 161.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201927">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 2, 141.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201927">↑</a> viii. 17, 15.</li></ol></div>

पृशन|పృశన|ಪೃಶನ|pṛśana|pRshana|pRishana|pRushana|pRRishana|_vei_120@1928|_vei2_pn018
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1927">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1929">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn017">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn018">2:018</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn019">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pṛśana</b> in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201928"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201928">1</a></sup> is considered by Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201928"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201928">2</a></sup> to denote a place where a battle was fought.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201928"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201928">↑</a> ix. 97, 54.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201928"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201928">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 16.</li></ol></div>

पृश्नि-गु|पृश्नि-गु|पृश्निगु|पृश्नि|పృశ్ని-గు_౧|పృశ్ని-గు|పృశ్నిగు|పృశ్ని|ಪೃಶ್ನಿ-ಗು_೧|ಪೃಶ್ನಿ-ಗು|ಪೃಶ್ನಿಗು|ಪೃಶ್ನಿ|pṛśni-gu_1|pṛśni-gu|pṛśnigu|pṛśni|pRshni-gu_1|pRshni-gu|pRshnigu|pRshni|pRishni-gu_1|pRishni-gu|pRishnigu|pRishni|pRushni-gu_1|pRushni-gu|pRushnigu|pRushni|pRRishni-gu_1|pRRishni-gu|pRRishnigu|pRRishni|_vei_120@1929|_vei2_pn018
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1928">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1930">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn017">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn018">2:018</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn019">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Pṛśni-gu</b> is the name of a man who is mentioned with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुचन्ति">śucanti</a></b> as a protégé of the Aśvins in one hymn of the Rigveda (i. 112, 7). Possibly the word is only an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a>.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 114.</p></div>

पृश्नि-गु|पृश्नि-गु|पृश्निगु|पृश्नि|పృశ్ని-గు_౨|పృశ్ని-గు|పృశ్నిగు|పృశ్ని|ಪೃಶ್ನಿ-ಗು_೨|ಪೃಶ್ನಿ-ಗು|ಪೃಶ್ನಿಗು|ಪೃಶ್ನಿ|pṛśni-gu_2|pṛśni-gu|pṛśnigu|pṛśni|pRshni-gu_2|pRshni-gu|pRshnigu|pRshni|pRishni-gu_2|pRishni-gu|pRishnigu|pRishni|pRushni-gu_2|pRushni-gu|pRushnigu|pRushni|pRRishni-gu_2|pRRishni-gu|pRRishnigu|pRRishni|_vei_120@1930|_vei2_pn018
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1929">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1931">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn017">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn018">2:018</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn019">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Pṛśni-gu,</b> pl., is taken in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201930"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201930">1</a></sup> by Geldner<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201930"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201930">2</a></sup> as denoting the name of a people. But this is not probable.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201930"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201930">↑</a> vii. 18, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201930"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201930">↑</a> <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 114.</li></ol></div>

पृश्नि-पर्णी|पृश्नि-पर्णि|पृश्निपर्णी|पृश्निपर्णि|पृश्नि|पर्णी|पर्णि|పృశ్ని-పర్ణీ|పృశ్ని-పర్ణి|పృశ్నిపర్ణీ|పృశ్నిపర్ణి|పృశ్ని|పర్ణీ|పర్ణి|ಪೃಶ್ನಿ-ಪರ್ಣೀ|ಪೃಶ್ನಿ-ಪರ್ಣಿ|ಪೃಶ್ನಿಪರ್ಣೀ|ಪೃಶ್ನಿಪರ್ಣಿ|ಪೃಶ್ನಿ|ಪರ್ಣೀ|ಪರ್ಣಿ|pṛśni-parṇī|pṛśni-parṇi|pṛśniparṇī|pṛśniparṇi|pṛśni|parṇī|parṇi|pRshni-parNii|pRshni-parNi|pRshniparNii|pRshniparNi|pRshni|parNii|parNi|pRshni-parNee|pRshniparNee|parNee|pRishni-parNii|pRishni-parNi|pRishniparNii|pRishniparNi|pRishni|pRushni-parNii|pRushni-parNi|pRushniparNii|pRushniparNi|pRushni|pRRishni-parNii|pRRishni-parNi|pRRishniparNii|pRRishniparNi|pRRishni|_vei_120@1931|_vei2_pn018
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1930">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1932">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn017">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn018">2:018</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn019">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pṛśni-parṇī</b> (‘having a speckled leaf’) is the name of a plant mentioned in a hymn of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201931"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201931">1</a></sup> as a protection against evil beings procuring abortion, called Kaṇvas (presumably a sign of hostility to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a></b> family).<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201931"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201931">2</a></sup> It also appears in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201931"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201931">3</a></sup> being identified with <i>Hermionitis cordifolia</i> by the St. Petersburg Dictionary, but  Roth<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201931"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201931">4</a></sup> in a subsequent contribution suggests that it is the same as a plant later called <i>lakṣmaṇā,</i> and regarded as curing barrenness. The scholiast on the Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201931"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201931">5</a></sup> thinks that the <i>Glycine debilis</i> is meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201931"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201931">↑</a> ii. 25, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201931"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201931">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Lanman in Whitney's Translation of the Atharvaveda, 65; Bergaigne, <i>Religion Védique,</i> 2, 465; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 207.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201931"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201931">↑</a> xiii. 8, 1, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201931"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201931">↑</a> Cited by Whitney, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201931"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201931">↑</a> xxv. 7, 17.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 187; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 69; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 302.</li></ol></div>

पृषत|పృషత|ಪೃಷತ|pṛṣata|pRShata|pRiShata|pRuShata|pRRiShata|_vei_120@1932|_vei2_pn019
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1931">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1933">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn018">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn019">2:019</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn020">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pṛṣata</b> is the name of an animal mentioned in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201932"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201932">1</a></sup> The dappled antelope or gazelle seems to be meant.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201932"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201932">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201932"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201932">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 17, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 9. 21; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 27. 40.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201932"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201932">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 83.</li></ol></div>

पृषती|पृषति|పృషతీ|పృషతి|ಪೃಷತೀ|ಪೃಷತಿ|pṛṣatī|pṛṣati|pRShatii|pRShati|pRShatee|pRiShatii|pRiShati|pRuShatii|pRuShati|pRRiShatii|pRRiShati|_vei_120@1933|_vei2_pn019
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1932">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1934">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn018">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn019">2:019</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn020">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pṛṣatī</b> in some passages<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201933">1</a></sup> clearly means a ‘speckled’ cow. The term is, however, generally<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201933">2</a></sup> applied to the team of the Maruts, when its sense is doubtful. The commentators usually explain it as ‘speckled antelope.’ But Mahīdhara,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201933">3</a></sup> followed by Roth,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201933">4</a></sup> prefers to see in it a ‘dappled mare’: it is true that the Maruts are often called<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201933">5</a></sup> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पृषद्">pṛṣad</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="अश्व">aśva</a>,</i> which is more naturally interpreted as ‘having dappled steeds,’ than as ‘having Pṛṣatīs as steeds.’<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201933">6</a></sup> In the later literature, which Grassmann<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201933">7</a></sup> prefers to follow, the word means the female of the dappled gazelle.  Aufrecht<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201933">8</a></sup> concurs in the view of Roth, but Max Müller<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201933">9</a></sup> is inclined to accept the traditional interpretation, while Muir<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201933">10</a></sup> leaves the matter open.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201933">↑</a> Rv. viii. 64, 10. 11, where ‘deer’ is nonsense, and ‘mares’ is improbable. The regular donation is ‘cows’; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 2, 9 (see Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 125); Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 2 (though this is not certain); <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 14, 23, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201933">↑</a> Rv. i. 37, 2; 39, 6; 64, 8; 85, 4, 5; ii. 34, 3; 36, 2; iii. 26, 4; v. 55, 6; 58, 6; 60, 2; i. 162, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201933">↑</a> On Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ii. 16.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201933">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> He had earlier (<i>ibid.,</i> 1, 1091) been inclined to follow the usual interpretation given by Sāyaṇa on Rv. i. 37, 2, etc., which Benfey, <i>Orient und Occident,</i> 2, 250, accepted.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201933">↑</a> Rv. i. 87, 4; 89, 7; 186, 8; ii. 34, 4; iii. 26, 6; v. 42, 15; vii. 40, 3</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201933">↑</a> So Sāyaṇa on Rv. i. 87, 4. This view is far-fetched, but is supported, in so far as the interpretation of <a class="vei_120a" href="पृषती">pṛṣatī</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्व">aśva</a> is concerned, by such passages as v. 55, 6, where the Maruts are said to yoke the Pṛṣatīs as <i>aśvān</i> to their chariots; but the sense may be ‘horses (and) dappled (mares).’ See, however, Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 226.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201933">↑</a> <i>Wo7rterbuch, s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201933">↑</a> See Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 152.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201933">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 70; 184.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201933">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 5, 151, 152.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 83.</li></ol></div>

पृषद्-आज्य|पृषदाज्य|पृषद्|आज्य|పృషద్-ఆజ్య|పృషదాజ్య|పృషద్|ఆజ్య|ಪೃಷದ್-ಆಜ್ಯ|ಪೃಷದಾಜ್ಯ|ಪೃಷದ್|ಆಜ್ಯ|pṛṣad-ājya|pṛṣadājya|pṛṣad|ājya|pRShad-aajya|pRShadaajya|pRShad|aajya|pRiShad-aajya|pRiShadaajya|pRiShad|pRuShad-aajya|pRuShadaajya|pRuShad|pRRiShad-aajya|pRRiShadaajya|pRRiShad|_vei_120@1934|_vei2_pn020
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1933">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1935">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn019">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn020">2:020</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn021">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pṛṣad-ājya</b> denotes ‘sprinkled butter’--that is, butter (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="आज्य">ājya</a></b>) mixed with sour milk, in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201934"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201934">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201934"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201934">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201934"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201934">↑</a> x. 90, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201934"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201934">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 2, 6, 2; vi. 3, 9, 6; 11, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 5, 2, 41; 4, 2; iii. 8, 4, 8, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 404, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

पृषध्र|పృషధ్ర|ಪೃಷಧ್ರ|pṛṣadhra|pRShadhra|pRiShadhra|pRuShadhra|pRRiShadhra|_vei_120@1935|_vei2_pn020
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1934">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1936">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn019">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn020">2:020</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn021">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pṛṣadhra</b> occurs in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वालखिल्य">vālakhilya</a> hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201935"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201935">1</a></sup> as the name of a man. He is also mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201935"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201935">2</a></sup> as a patron of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रस्कण्व">praskaṇva</a>,</b> and called <a class="vei_120a" href="पृषध्र">pṛṣadhra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मेध्य">medhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मातरिश्वन्">mātariśvan</a> (or Mātariśva); but for once there is a discrepancy between the statement of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> and the text of the Rigveda, for the hymns<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201935"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201935">3</a></sup> there attributed to <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रस्कण्व">praskaṇva</a> as in praise of <a class="vei_120a" href="पृषध्र">pṛṣadhra</a> have nothing in them connected with <a class="vei_120a" href="पृषध्र">pṛṣadhra</a>, while the Anukramaṇī (Index) ascribes to <a class="vei_120a" href="पृषध्र">pṛṣadhra</a> himself the authorship of one of them.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201935"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201935">4</a></sup> On the other hand, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मेध्य">medhya</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मातरिश्वन्">mātariśvan</a></b> appear as separate persons in the <b>Rigveda</b><sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1201935"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1201935">1b</a></sup> along with <a class="vei_120a" href="पृषध्र">pṛṣadhra</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201935"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201935">↑</a> viii. 52, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201935"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201935">↑</a> xvi. 11, 25-27.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201935"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201935">↑</a> viii. 55. 56.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201935"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201935">↑</a> viii. 56.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 39.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1201935"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1201935">↑</a> viii. 52, 2.</li></ol></div>

पृषातक|పృషాతక|ಪೃಷಾತಕ|pṛṣātaka|pRShaataka|pRiShaataka|pRuShaataka|pRRiShaataka|_vei_120@1936|_vei2_pn020
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1935">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1937">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn019">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn020">2:020</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn021">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pṛṣātaka</b> is the name of a mixture like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पृषदाज्य">pṛṣadājya</a>,</b> and consisting, according to the late Gṛhyasaṃgraha,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201936"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201936">1</a></sup> of curds (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="दधि">dadhi</a></b>), honey (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="मधु">madhu</a></b>), and <b>Ajya.</b> It is mentioned in a late passage of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201936"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201936">2</a></sup> and in the Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201936"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201936">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201936"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201936">↑</a> ii. 59.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201936"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201936">↑</a> xx. 134, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201936"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201936">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 3, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 35, 580.</li></ol></div>

पृष्ट्या|పృష్ట్యా|ಪೃಷ್ಟ್ಯಾ|pṛṣṭyā|pRShTyaa|pRiShTyaa|pRuShTyaa|pRRiShTyaa|_vei_120@1937|_vei2_pn020
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1936">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1938">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn019">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn020">2:020</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn021">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pṛṣṭyā</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201937"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201937">1</a></sup> denotes in the Atharvaveda (vi. 102, 2) the side horse (mare). </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201937"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201937">↑</a> So Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> Pṛṣṭhyā is read in the St. Petersburg Dictionary. See, however, Grill, <i>Hundert Lieder,</i>^2 169; Bloomfield; <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 513.</li></ol></div>

पृष्ट्य्-आमय|पृष्ट्यामय|पृष्ट्य्|आमय|పృష్ట్య్-ఆమయ|పృష్ట్యామయ|పృష్ట్య్|ఆమయ|ಪೃಷ್ಟ್ಯ್-ಆಮಯ|ಪೃಷ್ಟ್ಯಾಮಯ|ಪೃಷ್ಟ್ಯ್|ಆಮಯ|pṛṣṭy-āmaya|pṛṣṭyāmaya|pṛṣṭy|āmaya|pRShTy-aamaya|pRShTyaamaya|pRShTy|aamaya|pRiShTy-aamaya|pRiShTyaamaya|pRiShTy|pRuShTy-aamaya|pRuShTyaamaya|pRuShTy|pRRiShTy-aamaya|pRRiShTyaamaya|pRRiShTy|_vei_120@1938|_vei2_pn021
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1937">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1939">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn020">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn021">2:021</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn022">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pṛṣṭy-āmaya</b> denotes in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201938"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201938">1</a></sup> a pain in the sides or ribs.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201938"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201938">2</a></sup> It appears to be mentioned there merely as an accompaniment of fever (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="तक्मन्">takman</a></b>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201938"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201938">↑</a> xix. 34, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 65, 391.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201938"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201938">↑</a> The derivative adjective <i>Pṛṇṭyāmayin,</i> ‘suffering from a pain in the side,’ occurs in Rv. i. 105, 18.</li></ol></div>

पेत्व|పేత్వ|ಪೇತ್ವ|petva|_vei_120@1939|_vei2_pn021
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1938">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1940">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn020">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn021">2:021</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn022">↷</a><hr><p><b>Petva</b> is found twice in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201939">1</a></sup> In the first passage reference is made to its <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वाज">vāja</a>,</i> which Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201939">2</a></sup> argues can only mean ‘strength,’ ‘swiftness,’ though naturally the sense of ‘male power’ would seem more appropriate in a spell intended to remove lack of virility. In the second passage the <a class="vei_120a" href="पेत्व">petva</a> is mentioned as overcoming the horse (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उभयादन्त्">ubhayādant</a></b>), a miracle which has a parallel in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201939">3</a></sup> where the <a class="vei_120a" href="पेत्व">petva</a> overcomes the female<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201939">4</a></sup> lion. The animal also occurs in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201939">5</a></sup> and occasionally elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201939">6</a></sup> It appears to be the ‘ram’ or the ‘wether,’ the latter<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201939">7</a></sup> being the sense given to it by the commentator on the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā. But there is no conclusive evidence in favour of this meaning, while on the whole the passage of the Atharvaveda, in which <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वाज">vāja</a></i> is found, accords best with the sense of ‘ram.’ Hopkins,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201939">8</a></sup> however, renders the word as ‘goat,’ though for what reason is not clear. Whether it is connected in any way with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पित्व">pitva</a></b> or <b>Pidva</b> is quite uncertain. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201939">↑</a> iv. 4, 8; v. 19, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201939">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 229, 230.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201939">↑</a> vii. 18, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201939">↑</a> <i>Siṃhyam</i> in the text. Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 264, takes it as masculine, and as a play on <i>śiṃyum,</i> the name of one of the kings or peoples defeated in the battle of the ten kings. But, admitting the play, <i>siṃhī</i> as fem. seems to be still more pointed than <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सिंह">siṃha</a>,</i> contrasting with the masculine <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पेत्व">petva</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201939">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 22; 1. Though not in the parallel passage of the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, it appears to be found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a>, according to Weber's note in his edition of the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201939">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 8, 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxix. 58. 59; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 5, 3. etc.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201939">↑</a> <i>Galita-retasko meṣaḥ.</i></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201939">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.; India, Old and New,</i> 58. He thinks the born of the goat pierced the lion. Curiously enough, Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 253. renders the word a ‘goat’ in Av. v. 19, 2, but (p. 151) as ‘ram’ in iv. 4, 8; and Bloomfield <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 434, speaks Both of a ‘ram’ and a ‘goat’ in connexion with v. 19, 2.</li></ol></div>

पेदु|పేదు|ಪೇದು|pedu|_vei_120@1940|_vei2_pn022
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1939">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1941">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn021">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn022">2:022</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn023">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pedu</b> is the name in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201940"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201940">1</a></sup> of a protégé of the Aśvins, who gave him, in order, as it seems, to replace a bad steed, a mythical horse, hence called <a class="vei_120a" href="पैद्व">paidva</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201940"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201940">2</a></sup> which probably represents the horse of the sun.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201940"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201940">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201940"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201940">↑</a> Rv. i. 117, 9; 118, 9; 119, 10; vii. 71, 5; x. 39, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201940"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201940">↑</a> Rv. ix. 88, 4; Av. x. 4, 5 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201940"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201940">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> pp. 52, 149.</li></ol></div>

पेरुक|పేరుక|ಪೇರುಕ|peruka|_vei_120@1941|_vei2_pn022
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1940">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1942">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn021">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn022">2:022</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn023">↷</a><hr><p><b>Peruka</b> occurs in an obscure verse of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201941"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201941">1</a></sup> as the name of a patron of the poet.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201941"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201941">↑</a> vi. 63, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 158.</li></ol></div>

पेसस्|पेस|పేసస్|పేస|ಪೇಸಸ್|ಪೇಸ|pesas|pesa|_vei_120@1942|_vei2_pn022
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1941">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1943">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn021">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn022">2:022</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn023">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pesas</b> denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201942">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201942">2</a></sup> an embroidered garment such as a female dancer would wear.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201942">3</a></sup> The fondness of the Indians for such raiment is noted by Megasthenes<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201942">4</a></sup> and by Arrian,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201942">5</a></sup> who refer to their . So in one passage<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201942">6</a></sup> a garment (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वस्त्र">vastra</a></i>) is called <i>peśana,</i> with which Roth<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201942">7</a></sup> happily compares the Roman <i>vestis coloribus intexta.</i> The making of such garments was a regular occupation of women, as is indicated by the Peśas-<a class="vei_120a" href="कारी">kārī</a>, the ‘female embroiderer,’ figuring in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201942">8</a></sup> though the, commentator on the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> interprets the word as ‘wife of a maker of gold.’<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201942">9</a></sup> Pischel,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1201942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1201942">10</a></sup> however, thinks that Peśas never means anything but colour or form.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201942">↑</a> ii. 3, 6; iv. 36, 7; vii. 34, 11; 42, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201942">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 82. 89; xx. 40; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 19, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201942">↑</a> Rv. i. 92, 4. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201942">↑</a> See S¤trabo, p. 509, where he refers to a .</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201942">↑</a> <i>Indica,</i> 5, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201942">↑</a> Rv. x. 1, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201942">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201942">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 5, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201942">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> perhaps <i>suvarṇaṃ hiraṇyaṃ peśalam</i> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 4, 5, where <i>peśala</i> probably refers to cunningly-worked gold. But this does not suit the compound <i>peśas-<a class="vei_120a" href="कारी">kārī</a>,</i> which must denote a ‘maker of <i>peśas,</i>’ and <i>peśas</i> has not the sense of wrought gold in any passage. <i>Cf.</i> also Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 4, 5; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 261.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1201942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1201942">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 113-125.</li></ol></div>

पेसितृ|పేసితృ|ಪೇಸಿತೃ|pesitṛ|pesitR|pesitRi|pesitRu|pesitRRi|_vei_120@1943|_vei2_pn022
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1942">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1944">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn021">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn022">2:022</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn023">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pesitṛ</b> is the name of one of the victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201943"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201943">1</a></sup> The sense is quite  uncertain. The word is rendered by the St. Petersburg Dictionary and by Weber<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201943"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201943">2</a></sup> as ‘one who cuts in pieces,’ a ‘carver,’ but Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201943"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201943">3</a></sup> thinks that it means one who causes an enmity which has been lulled to rest to break out again.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201943"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201943">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Bṛāhmaṇa, iii. 4, 8, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201943"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201943">↑</a> <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 75, n. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201943"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201943">↑</a> On <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

पैंग-राज|पैंगराज|पैंग|राज|पैङ्ग-राज|पैङ्गराज|पैङ्ग|పైంగ-రాజ|పైంగరాజ|పైంగ|రాజ|పైఙ్గ-రాజ|పైఙ్గరాజ|పైఙ్గ|ಪೈಂಗ-ರಾಜ|ಪೈಂಗರಾಜ|ಪೈಂಗ|ರಾಜ|ಪೈಙ್ಗ-ರಾಜ|ಪೈಙ್ಗರಾಜ|ಪೈಙ್ಗ|paiṅga-rāja|paiṅgarāja|paiṅga|rāja|paiMga-raaja|paiMgaraaja|paiMga|raaja|painga-raaja|paingaraaja|painga|_vei_120@1944|_vei2_pn023
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1943">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1945">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn022">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn023">2:023</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn024">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paiṅga-rāja</b> is the name of one of the victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201944"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201944">1</a></sup> That a bird is meant is certain, but what particular kind is quite unknown.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201944"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201944">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 13, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 16; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 34. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 99.</li></ol></div>

पैंगी-पुत्र|पैंगीपुत्र|पैंगी|पैंगि|पुत्र|पैङ्गी-पुत्र|पैङ्गीपुत्र|पैङ्गी|पैङ्गि|పైంగీ-పుత్ర|పైంగీపుత్ర|పైంగీ|పైంగి|పుత్ర|పైఙ్గీ-పుత్ర|పైఙ్గీపుత్ర|పైఙ్గీ|పైఙ్గి|ಪೈಂಗೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಪೈಂಗೀಪುತ್ರ|ಪೈಂಗೀ|ಪೈಂಗಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|ಪೈಙ್ಗೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಪೈಙ್ಗೀಪುತ್ರ|ಪೈಙ್ಗೀ|ಪೈಙ್ಗಿ|paiṅgī-putra|paiṅgīputra|paiṅgī|paiṅgi|putra|paiMgii-putra|paiMgiiputra|paiMgii|paiMgi|paiMgee-putra|paiMgeeputra|paiMgee|paingii-putra|paingiiputra|paingii|paingi|paingee-putra|paingeeputra|paingee|_vei_120@1945|_vei2_pn023
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1944">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1946">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn022">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn023">2:023</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn024">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paiṅgī-putra</b> (‘son of a female descendant of Piṅga’) is the name of a teacher, pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शौनकीपुत्र">śaunakīputra</a>,</b> in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 4, 30 Mādhyaṃdina).</p></div>

पैंग्य|पैङ्ग्य|పైంగ్య|పైఙ్గ్య|ಪೈಂಗ್ಯ|ಪೈಙ್ಗ್ಯ|paiṅgya|paiMgya|paingya|_vei_120@1946|_vei2_pn023
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1945">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1947">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn022">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn023">2:023</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn024">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paiṅgya,</b> ‘descendant of Piṅga,’ is the name of a teacher who is repeatedly mentioned as an authority in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201946">1</a></sup> where<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201946">2</a></sup> also his doctrine is called the <a class="vei_120a" href="पैंग्य">paiṅgya</a>. This teacher is further referred to in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201946">3</a></sup> which also speaks of <b>Madhuka</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="पैंग्य">paiṅgya</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201946">4</a></sup> It is, of course, impossible to say whether there was only one <a class="vei_120a" href="पैंग्य">paiṅgya</a> or several Paiṅgyas. The followers of <a class="vei_120a" href="पैंग्य">paiṅgya</a> are called Paiṅgins in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201946">5</a></sup> and Anupada<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1201946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1201946">6</a></sup> Sūtras. His text-book is called <a class="vei_120a" href="पैंग">paiṅga</a> in the Anupada <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1201946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1201946">7</a></sup> while the Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1201946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1201946">8</a></sup> mentions a Paiṅgāyani <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>. It is clear that <a class="vei_120a" href="पैंग्य">paiṅgya</a> was a teacher of a Rigveda school allied to the Kauṣītakis. <a class="vei_120a" href="पैंगि">paiṅgi</a> is a patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a></b> in the Anukramanī of the <a class="vei_120a" href="आत्रेयी">ātreyī</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शाखा">śākhā</a>.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1201946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1201946">9</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201946">↑</a> viii. 9; xvi. 9; xxvi. 3, 4, 14; xxviii. 7. 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201946">↑</a> iii. 1; xix. 9; xxiv. 4. <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="पैंगी">paiṅgī</a> saṃpad,</i> xxv. 7. <a class="vei_120a" href="पैंग्य">paiṅgya</a> is found also in the Sāṅkhāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 2, 11; xi. 11, 5; 14, 9; xv. 3, 1; xvii. 7, 1, 3; 10, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201946">↑</a> xii. 2, 2, 4; 4, 8. (Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 3, 17.)</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201946">↑</a> xi. 7, 2, 8; 16.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201946">↑</a> iv. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1201946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1201946">↑</a> i. 8; ii. 2, 4, 10; vi. 7; xi. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1201946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1201946">↑</a> ii. 4; iii. 12; iv. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1201946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1201946">↑</a> v. 15, 8; 29, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1201946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1201946">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 71, n.; 3, 396.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 44, 45, 404 <i>et seq.;</i> 2, 295; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 41, 46, 47, 56, 81, 90, 130, etc.</li></ol></div>

पैजवन|పైజవన|ಪೈಜವನ|paijavana|_vei_120@1947|_vei2_pn024
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1946">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1948">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn023">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn024">2:024</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn025">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paijavana,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पिजवन">pijavana</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201947"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201947">1</a></sup> It seems most probable that <a class="vei_120a" href="पिजवन">pijavana</a> intervened in the line of succession between <b>Divodāsa</b> and <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>, because the two kings have, according to tradition, quite different Purohitas, the former being served by the <b>Bharadvājas</b> as his priests, the latter by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>;</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201947"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201947">2</a></sup> this is more natural if they were divided by a period of time than if they had been, as is usually supposed, father and son. Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201947"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201947">3</a></sup> however, identifies Divodāsa and <a class="vei_120a" href="पिजवन">pijavana</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201947"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201947">↑</a> Rv. vii. 18, 22. 25; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 24. 25; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 34; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 11, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201947"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201947">↑</a> See Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie</i> 1, 104 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201947"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201947">↑</a> <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 115.</li></ol></div>

पैद्व|పైద్వ|ಪೈದ್ವ|paidva|_vei_120@1948|_vei2_pn024
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1947">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1949">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn023">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn024">2:024</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn025">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paidva.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पेदु">pedu</a>.</b></p></div>

पोतृ|పోతృ|ಪೋತೃ|potṛ|potR|potRi|potRu|potRRi|_vei_120@1949|_vei2_pn024
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1948">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1950">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn023">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn024">2:024</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn025">↷</a><hr><p><b>Potṛ</b> is the name of one of the priests (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋत्विज्">ṛtvij</a></b>) of the sacrificial ritual. Already known to the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201949">1</a></sup> he is frequently mentioned later in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201949">2</a></sup> But as Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201949">3</a></sup> observes, the <a class="vei_120a" href="पोतृ">potṛ</a> is not in the later literature a priest of any importance, but is practically a mere name. Judging by the derivation of the name from the root <i>pū,</i> ‘purify,’ it would seem that he was properly engaged in the purification of the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पवमान">pavamāna</a>,</i> ‘Soma purifying itself,’ and was perhaps employed to sing hymns to this <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>. Potra<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201949">4</a></sup> denotes both the office and the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> vessel of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पोतृ">potṛ</a>.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201949">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201949">↑</a> i. 94, 6; ii. 5, 2; iv. 9, 3; vii. 16, 5; ix. 67, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201949">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 10 <i>et seq.;</i> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 3, 4, 22; v. 4, 5, 22; xii. 1, 1, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201949">↑</a> <i>Religien des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 383, 391, 395.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201949">↑</a> Rv. ii. 1, 2, and probably i. 76, 4, though the St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> gives this as an example of the second use.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201949">↑</a> Rv. i. 15, 2; ii. 36, 2; 37, 2. 4.</li></ol></div>

पौंश्चलेय|పౌంశ్చలేయ|ಪೌಂಶ್ಚಲೇಯ|pauṃścaleya|pauMshchaleya|_vei_120@1950|_vei2_pn024
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1949">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1951">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn023">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn024">2:024</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn025">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pauṃścaleya</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 8, 4, 2) denotes the son of a courtesan (Puṃśealī).</p></div>

पौंसायन|పౌంసాయన|ಪೌಂಸಾಯನ|pauṃsāyana|pauMsaayana|_vei_120@1951|_vei2_pn024
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1950">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1952">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn023">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn024">2:024</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn025">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pauṃsāyana</b> is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दुष्टरीतु">duṣṭarītu</a></b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xii. 9, 3, 1). </p></div>

पौञ्जि-ष्ठ|पौञ्जिष्ठ|पौञ्जि|ष्ठ|పౌఞ్జి-ష్ఠ|పౌఞ్జిష్ఠ|పౌఞ్జి|ష్ఠ|ಪೌಞ್ಜಿ-ಷ್ಠ|ಪೌಞ್ಜಿಷ್ಠ|ಪೌಞ್ಜಿ|ಷ್ಠ|pauñji-ṣṭha|pauñjiṣṭha|pauñji|ṣṭha|pau~nji-ShTha|pau~njiShTha|pau~nji|ShTha|_vei_120@1952|_vei2_pn025
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1951">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1953">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn024">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn025">2:025</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn026">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pauñji-ṣṭha</b> is the form in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201952"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201952">1</a></sup> the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201952"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201952">2</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201952"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201952">3</a></sup> of the word <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुञ्जिष्ठ">puñjiṣṭha</a>,</b> denoting ‘fisherman.’ It is probably a caste name, ‘son of a <a class="vei_120a" href="पुञ्जिष्ठ">puñjiṣṭha</a>,’ as the designation of a functional caste.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201952"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201952">↑</a> x. 4, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201952"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201952">↑</a> xxx. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201952"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201952">↑</a> iii. 4, 5, 1, where Sāyaṇa glosses it with the word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कैवर्त">kaivarta</a>,</i> also probably the name of a functional caste.</li></ol></div>

पौण्डरीक|పౌణ్డరీక|ಪೌಣ್ಡರೀಕ|pauṇḍarīka|pauNDariika|pauNDareeka|_vei_120@1953|_vei2_pn025
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1952">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1954">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn024">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn025">2:025</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn026">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pauṇḍarīka</b> is the patronymic of <b>Kṣemadhṛtvan</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xxii. 18, 7).</p></div>

पौत-क्रत|पौतक्रत|पौत|क्रत|పౌత-క్రత|పౌతక్రత|పౌత|క్రత|ಪೌತ-ಕ್ರತ|ಪೌತಕ್ರತ|ಪೌತ|ಕ್ರತ|pauta-krata|pautakrata|pauta|krata|_vei_120@1954|_vei2_pn025
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1953">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1955">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn024">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn025">2:025</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn026">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pauta-krata,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पूतक्रता">pūtakratā</a>,</b>’ is the metronymic of a man, apparently <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दस्यवे वृक">dasyave vṛka</a>,</b> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201954"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201954">1</a></sup> Scheftelowitz<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201954"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201954">2</a></sup> proposes to read <a class="vei_120a" href="पूतक्रतु">pūtakratu</a> with the Kashmir MS. of the Rigveda, arguing that in the same hymn Pūtakratāyī, the wife of <a class="vei_120a" href="पूतक्रतु">pūtakratu</a>, is referred to, and that therefore <a class="vei_120a" href="पूतक्रतु">pūtakratu</a> is appropriate, Pūtakratāyī<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201954"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201954">3</a></sup> being the feminine, like Manāyī,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201954"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201954">4</a></sup> for <a class="vei_120a" href="मनावी">manāvī</a>. But the ordinary reading in the sense of descendant is perfectly legitimate, as Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201954"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201954">5</a></sup> has pointed out.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201954"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201954">↑</a> viii. 56, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201954"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201954">↑</a> <i>Die Apokryphen des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 41, 42.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201954"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201954">↑</a> See Pāṇini, iv. 1, 36.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201954"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201954">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 8, 6; Pāṇini, iv. 1, 38. Perhaps also Vasāvī, Rv. x. 73, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201954"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201954">↑</a> <i>Go7ttingische Gelehrte Anzeigen,</i> 1907, 237.</li></ol></div>

पौतिमाषी-पुत्र|पौतिमाषीपुत्र|पौतिमाषी|पौतिमाषि|पुत्र|పౌతిమాషీ-పుత్ర|పౌతిమాషీపుత్ర|పౌతిమాషీ|పౌతిమాషి|పుత్ర|ಪೌತಿಮಾಷೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಪೌತಿಮಾಷೀಪುತ್ರ|ಪೌತಿಮಾಷೀ|ಪೌತಿಮಾಷಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|pautimāṣī-putra|pautimāṣīputra|pautimāṣī|pautimāṣi|putra|pautimaaShii-putra|pautimaaShiiputra|pautimaaShii|pautimaaShi|pautimaaShee-putra|pautimaaSheeputra|pautimaaShee|_vei_120@1955|_vei2_pn025
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1954">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1956">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn024">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn025">2:025</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn026">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pautimāṣī-putra,</b> ‘son of a female descendant of Pūtimāṣa,’ is the metronymic of a teacher in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 5, 1).</p></div>

पौति-माष्य|पौतिमाष्य|पौति|माष्य|పౌతి-మాష్య|పౌతిమాష్య|పౌతి|మాష్య|ಪೌತಿ-ಮಾಷ್ಯ|ಪೌತಿಮಾಷ್ಯ|ಪೌತಿ|ಮಾಷ್ಯ|pauti-māṣya|pautimāṣya|pauti|māṣya|pauti-maaShya|pautimaaShya|maaShya|_vei_120@1956|_vei2_pn025
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1955">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1957">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn024">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn025">2:025</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn026">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pauti-māṣya,</b> ‘descendant of Pūtimāṣa,’ is the patronymic of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौपवन">gaupavana</a>,</b> in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) of the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (ii. 6, 1; iv. 6, 1).</p></div>

पौतिमाष्यायण|పౌతిమాష్యాయణ|ಪೌತಿಮಾಷ್ಯಾಯಣ|pautimāṣyāyaṇa|pautimaaShyaayaNa|_vei_120@1957|_vei2_pn025
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1956">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1958">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn024">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn025">2:025</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn026">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pautimāṣyāyaṇa,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पौतिमाष्य">pautimāṣya</a></b>’ is the patronymic of a teacher, who, with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौण्डिन्यायन">kauṇḍinyāyana</a>,</b> taught <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रैभ्य">raibhya</a>,</b> in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) of the Mādhyaṃdina recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (ii. 5, 20; iv. 5, 26). </p></div>

पौत्र|పౌత్ర|ಪೌತ್ರ|pautra|_vei_120@1958|_vei2_pn026
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1957">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1959">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn025">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn026">2:026</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn027">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pautra</b> (‘descended from a son’) is the regular term for a ‘grandson’ from the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201958"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201958">1</a></sup> onwards.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201958"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201958">2</a></sup> When it is used beside <b>Naptṛ,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201958"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201958">3</a></sup> the latter word must denote ‘great-grandson.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201958"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201958">↑</a> ix. 5, 30; xi. 7, 16; xviii. 43, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201958"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201958">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 8, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201958"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201958">↑</a> Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 3, 18; Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 11, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 10, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Delbrūck, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 478.</li></ol></div>

पौर|పౌర|ಪೌರ|paura|_vei_120@1959|_vei2_pn026
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1958">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1960">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn025">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn026">2:026</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn027">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paura,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="पूरु">pūru</a>,’ is the name of a man, presumably a <a class="vei_120a" href="पूरु">pūru</a> prince helped by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, in a hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201959"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201959">1</a></sup> The Greek  the name of Alexander's rival, is probably the representative of this word. Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201959"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201959">2</a></sup> sees the same name in another passage also.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201959"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201959">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201959"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201959">↑</a> viii. 3, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201959"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201959">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 362; as also Grassmann, <i>Wo7rterbuch, s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201959"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201959">↑</a> v. 74, 4.</li></ol></div>

पौरु-कुत्स|पौरुकुत्स|पौरु|कुत्स|పౌరు-కుత్స|పౌరుకుత్స|పౌరు|కుత్స|ಪೌರು-ಕುತ್ಸ|ಪೌರುಕುತ್ಸ|ಪೌರು|ಕುತ್ಸ|pauru-kutsa|paurukutsa|pauru|kutsa|_vei_120@1960|_vei2_pn026
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1959">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1961">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn025">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn026">2:026</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn027">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pauru-kutsa,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201960"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201960">1</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पौरु">pauru</a>-kutsi,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201960"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201960">2</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पौरु">pauru</a>-kutsya,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201960"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201960">3</a></sup> are variant forms of the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a>,</b> the descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201960"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201960">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxii. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 16, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201960"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201960">↑</a> Rv. vii. 19, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201960"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201960">↑</a> Rv. v. 33, 8; viii. 19, 36; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 6, 5, 3.</li></ol></div>

पौरु-शिष्टि|पौरुशिष्टि|पौरु|शिष्टि|పౌరు-శిష్టి|పౌరుశిష్టి|పౌరు|శిష్టి|ಪೌರು-ಶಿಷ್ಟಿ|ಪೌರುಶಿಷ್ಟಿ|ಪೌರು|ಶಿಷ್ಟಿ|pauru-śiṣṭi|pauruśiṣṭi|pauru|śiṣṭi|pauru-shiShTi|paurushiShTi|shiShTi|_vei_120@1961|_vei2_pn026
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1960">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1962">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn025">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn026">2:026</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn027">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pauru-śiṣṭi,</b> ‘descendant of Puruśiṣṭa,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तपोनित्य">taponitya</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (i. 9, 1 = <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, vii. 8, 1).</p></div>

पौर्ण-मासी|पौर्ण-मासि|पौर्णमासी|पौर्णमासि|पौर्ण|मासी|मासि|పౌర్ణ-మాసీ|పౌర్ణ-మాసి|పౌర్ణమాసీ|పౌర్ణమాసి|పౌర్ణ|మాసీ|మాసి|ಪೌರ್ಣ-ಮಾಸೀ|ಪೌರ್ಣ-ಮಾಸಿ|ಪೌರ್ಣಮಾಸೀ|ಪೌರ್ಣಮಾಸಿ|ಪೌರ್ಣ|ಮಾಸೀ|ಮಾಸಿ|paurṇa-māsī|paurṇa-māsi|paurṇamāsī|paurṇamāsi|paurṇa|māsī|māsi|paurNa-maasii|paurNa-maasi|paurNamaasii|paurNamaasi|paurNa|maasii|maasi|paurNa-maasee|paurNamaasee|maasee|_vei_120@1962|_vei2_pn026
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1961">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1963">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn025">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn026">2:026</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn027">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paurṇa-māsī,</b> denoting the ‘night of the full moon,’ is celebrated in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201962">1</a></sup> as sacred, while it is repeatedly mentioned later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201962">2</a></sup> Gobhila<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201962">3</a></sup> defines it as the greatest separation (<i>vikarṣa</i>) of the sun and the moon. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201962">↑</a> vii. 80.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201962">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 9, 1; ii. 2, 2, 1; iii. 4, 9, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 11; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 2, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201962">↑</a> i. 5, 7. Three sorts of full moon are distinguished by Gobhila--that which occurs when the full moon rises at the meeting of day and night (<i>sandhyā</i>), when it rises shortly after sunset, or when it stands high in the sky. The two former alternatives are apparently those described in the passage (<a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 11 = <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1) as <i>pūrvā</i> and <i>uttarā.</i> See Weber, <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्योतिष">jyotiṣa</a>, 51; Oldenberg, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 30, 26, n.</li></ol></div>

पौलुषि|పౌలుషి|ಪೌಲುಷಿ|pauluṣi|pauluShi|_vei_120@1963|_vei2_pn027
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1962">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1964">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn026">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn027">2:027</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn028">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pauluṣi,</b> ‘descendant of Puluṣa,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सत्ययज्ञ">satyayajña</a></b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (x. 6, 1, 1) and the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (v. 11, 1). In the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 39, 1) the form is Pauluṣita, which is perhaps merely an error.</p></div>

पौल्कस|పౌల్కస|ಪೌಲ್ಕಸ|paulkasa|_vei_120@1964|_vei2_pn027
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1963">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1965">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn026">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn027">2:027</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn028">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paulkasa</b> is the name of one of the victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201964"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201964">1</a></sup> The name also occurs in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201964"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201964">2</a></sup> as that of a despised race of men, together with the <a class="vei_120a" href="चाण्डाल">cāṇḍāla</a>. The Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201964"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201964">3</a></sup> has the variant <a class="vei_120a" href="पुक्लक">puklaka</a> or Pulkaka, clearly the same as Pulkasa, of which <a class="vei_120a" href="पौल्कस">paulkasa</a> is a derivative form, showing that a caste is meant (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौलाल">kaulāla</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पौञ्जिष्ठ">pauñjiṣṭha</a></b>). In the accepted theory<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201964"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201964">4</a></sup> the Pulkasa is the son of a <a class="vei_120a" href="निषाद">niṣāda</a> or <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> by a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> woman, but this is merely speculative; the <a class="vei_120a" href="पौल्कस">paulkasa</a> may either have been a functional caste, or, as Fick<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201964"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201964">5</a></sup> believes, an aboriginal clan living by catching wild beasts, and only occasionally reduced to menial tasks.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201964"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201964">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 14, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201964"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201964">↑</a> iv. 3, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201964"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201964">↑</a> i. 6, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201964"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201964">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v., Pukkaśa.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 217, takes <a class="vei_120a" href="पौल्कस">paulkasa</a> as a mixed caste.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201964"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201964">↑</a> <i>Die sociale Gliederung,</i> 206. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 416, n. 6.</li></ol></div>

पौष्कर-सादि|पौष्करसादि|पौष्कर|सादि|పౌష్కర-సాది|పౌష్కరసాది|పౌష్కర|సాది|ಪೌಷ್ಕರ-ಸಾದಿ|ಪೌಷ್ಕರಸಾದಿ|ಪೌಷ್ಕರ|ಸಾದಿ|pauṣkara-sādi|pauṣkarasādi|pauṣkara|sādi|pauShkara-saadi|pauShkarasaadi|pauShkara|saadi|_vei_120@1965|_vei2_pn027
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1964">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1966">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn026">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn027">2:027</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn028">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pauṣkara-sādi</b> (‘descendant of Puṣkarasādi’) is the name of a teacher mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201965"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201965">1</a></sup> as well as the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Prātiśākhya.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201965"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201965">2</a></sup> A Puṣkarasādi is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201965"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201965">3</a></sup> of Āpastamba and elsewhere.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201965"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201965">↑</a> vii. 17. <i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 371.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201965"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201965">↑</a> i. 5; ii. 1, 2, 5; Pāṇini, viii. 4, 48; Vārttika, 3; Kielhorn, <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 16, 103; Pischel, <i>ibid.,</i> 34, 26.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201965"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201965">↑</a> i. 6, 19, 7; 10, 28, 1.</li></ol></div>

पौष्पिण्ड्य|పౌష్పిణ్డ్య|ಪೌಷ್ಪಿಣ್ಡ್ಯ|pauṣpiṇḍya|pauShpiNDya|_vei_120@1966|_vei2_pn027
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1965">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1967">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn026">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn027">2:027</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn028">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pauṣpiṇḍya</b> is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जैमिनि">jaimini</a>,</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) at the end of the Sāmavidhāna <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201966"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201966">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201966"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201966">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 377.</li></ol></div>

प्युक्ष्ण|ప్యుక్ష్ణ|ಪ್ಯುಕ್ಷ್ಣ|pyukṣṇa|pyukShNa|pyuxNa|_vei_120@1967|_vei2_pn027
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1966">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1968">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn026">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn027">2:027</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn028">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pyukṣṇa</b> is found in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (v. 3, 1, 11) denoting the ‘covering’ for a bow (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="धनुस्">dhanus</a></b>), presumably made of skin. </p></div>

प्रौउग|ప్రౌఉగ|ಪ್ರೌಉಗ|praūga|prauuga|praooga|_vei_120@1968|_vei2_pn028
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1967">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1969">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn027">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn028">2:028</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn029">↷</a><hr><p><b>Praūga</b> is apparently equivalent to <i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्र">pra</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="युग">yuga</a>,</i> denoting the fore part of the pole of the cart, the part in front of the yoke. It is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201968"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201968">1</a></sup> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201968"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201968">2</a></sup> where it is said to be the part of the pole behind the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कस्तम्भी">kastambhī</a>,</b> or prop on which the pole rests.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201968"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201968">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 4, 11, 1, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxi. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201968"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201968">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 2, 9; iii. 5, 3, 4, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 248; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 14, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

प्र-कंकत|प्रकंकत|प्र|कंकत|प्र-कङ्कत|प्रकङ्कत|कङ्कत|ప్ర-కంకత|ప్రకంకత|ప్ర|కంకత|ప్ర-కఙ్కత|ప్రకఙ్కత|కఙ్కత|ಪ್ರ-ಕಂಕತ|ಪ್ರಕಂಕತ|ಪ್ರ|ಕಂಕತ|ಪ್ರ-ಕಙ್ಕತ|ಪ್ರಕಙ್ಕತ|ಕಙ್ಕತ|pra-kaṅkata|prakaṅkata|pra|kaṅkata|pra-kaMkata|prakaMkata|kaMkata|pra-kankata|prakankata|kankata|_vei_120@1969|_vei2_pn028
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1968">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1970">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn027">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn028">2:028</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn029">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-kaṅkata</b> is the name of some noxious insect in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201969">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201969">↑</a> i. 191, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 98.</li></ol></div>

प्र-करितृ|प्रकरितृ|प्र|करितृ|ప్ర-కరితృ|ప్రకరితృ|ప్ర|కరితృ|ಪ್ರ-ಕರಿತೃ|ಪ್ರಕರಿತೃ|ಪ್ರ|ಕರಿತೃ|pra-karitṛ|prakaritṛ|pra|karitṛ|pra-karitR|prakaritR|karitR|pra-karitRi|prakaritRi|karitRi|pra-karitRu|prakaritRu|karitRu|pra-karitRRi|prakaritRRi|karitRRi|_vei_120@1970|_vei2_pn028
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1969">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1971">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn027">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn028">2:028</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn029">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-karitṛ</b> is the name of one of the victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201970"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201970">1</a></sup> The exact sense is uncertain; the commentator Sāyaṇa on the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> explains it to mean the ‘divider of dear ones by producing enmity,’ but the sense of ‘sprinkler’--that is, ‘seasoner’--is more likely.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201970"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201970">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 8, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 315, n. 1; Weber, <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 79, n. 6.</li></ol></div>

प्र-कश|प्रकश|प्र|कश|ప్ర-కశ|ప్రకశ|ప్ర|కశ|ಪ್ರ-ಕಶ|ಪ್ರಕಶ|ಪ್ರ|ಕಶ|pra-kaśa|prakaśa|pra|kaśa|pra-kasha|prakasha|kasha|_vei_120@1971|_vei2_pn028
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1970">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1972">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn027">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn028">2:028</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn029">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-kaśa</b> in the Atharvaveda (ix. 1, 21) seems to mean either the ‘thong’ or the ‘lash’ of a whip.</p></div>

प्र-क्रम|प्रक्रम|प्र|क्रम|ప్ర-క్రమ|ప్రక్రమ|ప్ర|క్రమ|ಪ್ರ-ಕ್ರಮ|ಪ್ರಕ್ರಮ|ಪ್ರ|ಕ್ರಮ|pra-krama|prakrama|pra|krama|_vei_120@1972|_vei2_pn028
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1971">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1973">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn027">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn028">2:028</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn029">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-krama,</b> ‘stride,’ is mentioned as a measure of distance in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (x. 2, 3, 1 <i>et seq.</i>), but its exact length in unknown.</p></div>

प्रक्ष|ప్రక్ష|ಪ್ರಕ್ಷ|prakṣa|prakSha|praxa|_vei_120@1973|_vei2_pn028
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1972">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1974">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn027">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn028">2:028</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn029">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prakṣa</b> is the form in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201973"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201973">1</a></sup> of the usual name, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्लक्ष">plakṣa</a>,</b> of a tree, being merely a phonetic alteration for the sake of the etymology. According to Aufrecht,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201973"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201973">2</a></sup> the same word is found in two passages of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201973"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201973">3</a></sup> the same reading occurring in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201973"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201973">4</a></sup> Oldenberg,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201973"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201973">5</a></sup> however, questions the correctness of the reading <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रक्ष">prakṣa</a>, both in the latter passage and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201973"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201973">↑</a> vi. 3, 10, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201973"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201973">↑</a> <i>Rigveda,</i> 2, xlvi. n.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201973"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201973">↑</a> i. 444; ii. 465.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201973"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201973">↑</a> v. 2, 2, with Keith's notes.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201973"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201973">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 344.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 59.</li></ol></div>

प्रगाथ|ప్రగాథ|ಪ್ರಗಾಥ|pragātha|pragaatha|_vei_120@1974|_vei2_pn029
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1973">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1975">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn028">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn029">2:029</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn030">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pragātha</b> is the name given in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka (ii. 2, 2) to the poets of the eighth Maṇḍala of the Rigveda, so called because they composed <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रगाथ">pragātha</a> strophes (that is, verses consisting of a Bṛhatī or Kakubh followed by a Satobṛhatī).</p></div>

प्र-घात|प्रघात|प्र|घात|ప్ర-ఘాత|ప్రఘాత|ప్ర|ఘాత|ಪ್ರ-ಘಾತ|ಪ್ರಘಾತ|ಪ್ರ|ಘಾತ|pra-ghāta|praghāta|pra|ghāta|pra-ghaata|praghaata|ghaata|_vei_120@1975|_vei2_pn029
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1974">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1976">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn028">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn029">2:029</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn030">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-ghāta</b> is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201975"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201975">1</a></sup> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201975"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201975">2</a></sup> in the sense of the closely woven ends of a cloth from which depend the loose threads of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नीवि">nīvi</a>,</b> or unwoven fringe.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201975"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201975">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 1, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxiii. 1. The word does not occur in Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 6, 2, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201975"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201975">↑</a> iii. 1, 2, 18.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 10, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

प्र-चलाका|प्रचलाका|प्र|चलाका|ప్ర-చలాకా|ప్రచలాకా|ప్ర|చలాకా|ಪ್ರ-ಚಲಾಕಾ|ಪ್ರಚಲಾಕಾ|ಪ್ರ|ಚಲಾಕಾ|pra-calākā|pracalākā|pra|calākā|pra-chalaakaa|prachalaakaa|chalaakaa|_vei_120@1976|_vei2_pn029
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1975">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1977">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn028">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn029">2:029</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn030">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-calākā</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (vii. 5, 11, 1) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā (<a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, v. 2) seems to mean a ‘cloudburst.’</p></div>

प्रजावन्त् प्राजापत्य|ప్రజావన్త్ ప్రాజాపత్య|ಪ್ರಜಾವನ್ತ್ ಪ್ರಾಜಾಪತ್ಯ|prajāvant prājāpatya|prajaavant praajaapatya|_vei_120@1977|_vei2_pn029
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1976">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1978">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn028">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn029">2:029</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn030">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prajāvant Prājāpatya,</b> ‘descendant of Prajāpati,’ is, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 21), the author of a hymn of the Rigveda (x. 183).</p></div>

प्र-णपात्|प्रणपात्|प्र|णपात्|ప్ర-ణపాత్|ప్రణపాత్|ప్ర|ణపాత్|ಪ್ರ-ಣಪಾತ್|ಪ್ರಣಪಾತ್|ಪ್ರ|ಣಪಾತ್|pra-ṇapāt|praṇapāt|pra|ṇapāt|pra-Napaat|praNapaat|Napaat|_vei_120@1978|_vei2_pn029
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1977">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1979">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn028">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn029">2:029</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn030">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-ṇapāt</b> in the Rigveda (viii. 17, 13) denotes ‘greatgrandson.’</p></div>

प्र-णेजन|प्रणेजन|प्र|णेजन|ప్ర-ణేజన|ప్రణేజన|ప్ర|ణేజన|ಪ್ರ-ಣೇಜನ|ಪ್ರಣೇಜನ|ಪ್ರ|ಣೇಜನ|pra-ṇejana|praṇejana|pra|ṇejana|pra-Nejana|praNejana|Nejana|_vei_120@1979|_vei2_pn029
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1978">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1980">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn028">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn029">2:029</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn030">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-ṇejana</b> is the word used in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 2, 2, 18) to denote the ‘water used for washing.’</p></div>

प्र-ततामह|प्रततामह|प्र|ततामह|ప్ర-తతామహ|ప్రతతామహ|ప్ర|తతామహ|ಪ್ರ-ತತಾಮಹ|ಪ್ರತತಾಮಹ|ಪ್ರ|ತತಾಮಹ|pra-tatāmaha|pratatāmaha|pra|tatāmaha|pra-tataamaha|pratataamaha|tataamaha|_vei_120@1980|_vei2_pn029
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1979">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1981">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn028">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn029">2:029</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn030">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-tatāmaha,</b> ‘great-grandfather,’ is found in the Atharvaveda (xviii. 4, 75).</p></div>

प्र-तर्दन|प्रतर्दन|प्र|तर्दन|ప్ర-తర్దన|ప్రతర్దన|ప్ర|తర్దన|ಪ್ರ-ತರ್ದನ|ಪ್ರತರ್ದನ|ಪ್ರ|ತರ್ದನ|pra-tardana|pratardana|pra|tardana|_vei_120@1981|_vei2_pn029
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1980">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1982">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn028">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn029">2:029</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn030">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-tardana</b> is the name in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201981"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201981">1</a></sup> of a king who had a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a></b> for his <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201981"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201981">2</a></sup> he appears as arriving at the sacrifice of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषिस्">ṛṣis</a> in the Naimiṣa forest, and asking them how errors in the sacrifice could be remedied; and as finding <b>Alīkayu Vācaspata,</b> the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> priest at the sacrifice, unable to say what was to be  done. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201981"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201981">3</a></sup> it is said that <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतर्दन">pratardana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दैवोदासि">daivodāsi</a> went to Indra's world through his death in battle. The patronymic connects him with <b>Divodāsa,</b> the ancestor or father of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>,</b> and the mention of <a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a> (probably ‘a Bharadvāja’ is meant) as his priest supports the patronymic, for Divodāsa is a special favourite of the singers of the <a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a> family. The name, moreover, is reminiscent of the Tṛtsus (the root <i>tard</i> appears in both) and of the <i>Pratṛdaḥ</i> (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतृद्">pratṛd</a></b>). But he is not in Vedic literature a king of <a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201981"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201981">4</a></sup> Geldner<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201981"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201981">5</a></sup> regards him as Divodāsa's son, but this is not likely. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रातर्दनि">prātardani</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201981"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201981">↑</a> xxi. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201981"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201981">↑</a> xxvi. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201981"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201981">↑</a> iii. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201981"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201981">↑</a> As in the Epic; Pargiter, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1910, 38</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201981"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201981">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 138.</li></ol></div>

प्र-तिथि देव-तरथ|प्र-तिथि देवतरथ|प्र-तिथि देव|तरथ|ప్ర-తిథి దేవ-తరథ|ప్ర-తిథి దేవతరథ|ప్ర-తిథి దేవ|తరథ|ಪ್ರ-ತಿಥಿ ದೇವ-ತರಥ|ಪ್ರ-ತಿಥಿ ದೇವತರಥ|ಪ್ರ-ತಿಥಿ ದೇವ|ತರಥ|pra-tithi deva-taratha|pra-tithi devataratha|pra-tithi deva|taratha|_vei_120@1982|_vei2_pn030
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1981">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1983">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn029">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn030">2:030</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn031">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-<a class="vei_120a" href="तिथि">tithi</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="देव">deva</a>-taratha</b> is the name of a teacher, pupil of <b>Devataras Śāvasāyana</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201982"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201982">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201982"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201982">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373, 385; Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 444.</li></ol></div>

प्रति-दीवन्|प्रतिदीवन्|प्रति|दीवन्|ప్రతి-దీవన్|ప్రతిదీవన్|ప్రతి|దీవన్|ಪ್ರತಿ-ದೀವನ್|ಪ್ರತಿದೀವನ್|ಪ್ರತಿ|ದೀವನ್|prati-dīvan|pratidīvan|prati|dīvan|prati-diivan|pratidiivan|diivan|prati-deevan|pratideevan|deevan|_vei_120@1983|_vei2_pn030
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1982">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1984">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn029">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn030">2:030</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn031">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prati-dīvan</b> denotes in the Rigveda (x. 38, 6) and the Atharvaveda (vii. 109, 4) ‘opponent in the game of dice.’</p></div>

प्रति-दुह्|प्रतिदुह्|प्रति|दुह्|ప్రతి-దుహ్|ప్రతిదుహ్|ప్రతి|దుహ్|ಪ್ರತಿ-ದುಹ್|ಪ್ರತಿದುಹ್|ಪ್ರತಿ|ದುಹ್|prati-duh|pratiduh|prati|duh|_vei_120@1984|_vei2_pn030
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1983">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1985">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn029">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn030">2:030</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn031">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prati-duh</b> has the specific sense of ‘fresh milk,’ warm from the cow, in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201984"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201984">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201984"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201984">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201984"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201984">↑</a> Av. ix. 4, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 3, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201984"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201984">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 5, 5; xviii. 4, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 3, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 6, 2, etc.</li></ol></div>

प्रति-धा|प्रतिधा|प्रति|धा|ప్రతి-ధా|ప్రతిధా|ప్రతి|ధా|ಪ್ರತಿ-ಧಾ|ಪ್ರತಿಧಾ|ಪ್ರತಿ|ಧಾ|prati-dhā|pratidhā|prati|dhā|prati-dhaa|pratidhaa|dhaa|_vei_120@1985|_vei2_pn030
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1984">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1986">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn029">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn030">2:030</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn031">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prati-dhā</b> apparently means ‘draught’ or ‘pull’ in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201985"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201985">1</a></sup> where <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> is said to have drunk thirty streams (<i>sarāṃsi</i>) with one <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतिधा">pratidhā</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201985"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201985">↑</a> viii. 77, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 11.</li></ol></div>

प्रति-धि|प्रतिधि|प्रति|धि|ప్రతి-ధి|ప్రతిధి|ప్రతి|ధి|ಪ್ರತಿ-ಧಿ|ಪ್ರತಿಧಿ|ಪ್ರತಿ|ಧಿ|prati-dhi|pratidhi|prati|dhi|_vei_120@1986|_vei2_pn030
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1985">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1987">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn029">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn030">2:030</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn031">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prati-dhi</b> is mentioned in the Sūryā hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201986"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201986">1</a></sup> as part of the chariot on which the bride is taken home. It is  impossible to determine with certainty exactly what is meant; Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201986"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201986">2</a></sup> understands it to mean a cross-piece of wood fastened to the pole.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201986"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201986">↑</a> x. 85, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201986"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201986">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

प्रति-पण|प्रतिपण|प्रति|पण|ప్రతి-పణ|ప్రతిపణ|ప్రతి|పణ|ಪ್ರತಿ-ಪಣ|ಪ್ರತಿಪಣ|ಪ್ರತಿ|ಪಣ|prati-paṇa|pratipaṇa|prati|paṇa|prati-paNa|pratipaNa|paNa|_vei_120@1987|_vei2_pn031
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1986">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1988">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn030">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn031">2:031</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn032">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prati-paṇa</b> is found in the Atharvaveda (iii. 15, 4) denoting ‘barter’ or ‘exchange.’ <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पण">paṇa</a>.</b></p></div>

प्रति-प्रश्न|प्रतिप्रश्न|प्रति|प्रश्न|ప్రతి-ప్రశ్న|ప్రతిప్రశ్న|ప్రతి|ప్రశ్న|ಪ್ರತಿ-ಪ್ರಶ್ನ|ಪ್ರತಿಪ್ರಶ್ನ|ಪ್ರತಿ|ಪ್ರಶ್ನ|prati-praśna|pratipraśna|prati|praśna|prati-prashna|pratiprashna|prashna|_vei_120@1988|_vei2_pn031
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1987">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1989">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn030">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn031">2:031</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn032">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prati-praśna</b> occurs in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201988"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201988">1</a></sup> applied to Prajāpati as the decider of doubts; it may have been a technical term for an ‘arbitrator’ (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यमशी">madhyamaśī</a></b> and <b>harma</b>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201988"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201988">↑</a> i. 4, 5, 11; iv. 1, 3, 14; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 131, and 26, 267, renders <i>pratipraśnam</i> by ‘(went to Prajāpati) for his decision,’ which leaves it ambiguous how he took the passages.</li></ol></div>

प्रति-प्र-स्थातृ|प्रति-प्रस्थातृ|प्रति-प्र|स्थातृ|ప్రతి-ప్ర-స్థాతృ|ప్రతి-ప్రస్థాతృ|ప్రతి-ప్ర|స్థాతృ|ಪ್ರತಿ-ಪ್ರ-ಸ್ಥಾತೃ|ಪ್ರತಿ-ಪ್ರಸ್ಥಾತೃ|ಪ್ರತಿ-ಪ್ರ|ಸ್ಥಾತೃ|prati-pra-sthātṛ|prati-prasthātṛ|prati-pra|sthātṛ|prati-pra-sthaatR|prati-prasthaatR|sthaatR|prati-pra-sthaatRi|prati-prasthaatRi|sthaatRi|prati-pra-sthaatRu|prati-prasthaatRu|sthaatRu|prati-pra-sthaatRRi|prati-prasthaatRRi|sthaatRRi|_vei_120@1989|_vei2_pn031
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1988">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1990">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn030">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn031">2:031</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn032">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prati-<a class="vei_120a" href="प्र">pra</a>-sthātṛ</b> is the name of a priest (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋत्विज्">ṛtvij</a></b>), one of the assistants of the <a class="vei_120a" href="अध्वर्यु">adhvaryu</a>, in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201989"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201989">1</a></sup> He is not mentioned in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201989"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201989">2</a></sup> but mention is once made in that Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201989"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201989">3</a></sup> of the two Adhvaryus. These may have meant, as later, the <a class="vei_120a" href="अध्वर्यु">adhvaryu</a> and the Pratiprasthātṛ. Oldenberg,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201989"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201989">4</a></sup> however, thinks that the <a class="vei_120a" href="अध्वर्यु">adhvaryu</a> and the Agnīdh are intended, a conjecture for which there is some authority.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1201989"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1201989">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201989"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201989">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 5, 3, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 29; vii. 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 5, 2, 2; 3, 13, 22, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201989"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201989">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 384, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201989"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201989">↑</a> ii. 16, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201989"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201989">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 390, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1201989"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1201989">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. x. 41, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> in <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 6, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Rituallitteratur,</i> 97.</li></ol></div>

प्रति-प्राश्|प्रतिप्राश्|प्रति|प्राश्|ప్రతి-ప్రాశ్|ప్రతిప్రాశ్|ప్రతి|ప్రాశ్|ಪ್ರತಿ-ಪ್ರಾಶ್|ಪ್ರತಿಪ್ರಾಶ್|ಪ್ರತಿ|ಪ್ರಾಶ್|prati-prāś|pratiprāś|prati|prāś|prati-praash|pratipraash|praash|_vei_120@1990|_vei2_pn031
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1989">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1991">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn030">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn031">2:031</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn032">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prati-prāś.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राश्">prāś</a>.</b></p></div>

प्रतिबोधी-पुत्र|प्रतिबोधीपुत्र|प्रतिबोधी|प्रतिबोधि|पुत्र|ప్రతిబోధీ-పుత్ర|ప్రతిబోధీపుత్ర|ప్రతిబోధీ|ప్రతిబోధి|పుత్ర|ಪ್ರತಿಬೋಧೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಪ್ರತಿಬೋಧೀಪುತ್ರ|ಪ್ರತಿಬೋಧೀ|ಪ್ರತಿಬೋಧಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|pratibodhī-putra|pratibodhīputra|pratibodhī|pratibodhi|putra|pratibodhii-putra|pratibodhiiputra|pratibodhii|pratibodhee-putra|pratibodheeputra|pratibodhee|_vei_120@1991|_vei2_pn031
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1990">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1992">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn030">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn031">2:031</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn032">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pratibodhī-putra</b> is a wrong reading for <b>Prātībodhī-<a class="vei_120a" href="पुत्र">putra</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201991"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201991">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201991"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201991">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 391; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 244, 310,</li></ol></div>

प्रति-मित्|प्रतिमित्|प्रति|मित्|ప్రతి-మిత్|ప్రతిమిత్|ప్రతి|మిత్|ಪ್ರತಿ-ಮಿತ್|ಪ್ರತಿಮಿತ್|ಪ್ರತಿ|ಮಿತ್|prati-mit|pratimit|prati|mit|_vei_120@1992|_vei2_pn031
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1991">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1993">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn030">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn031">2:031</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn032">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prati-mit</b> is found in the description of a house in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201992"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201992">1</a></sup> The sense must be ‘support’ of some sort, probably beams leaning up at an angle against the <b>Upamits.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201992"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201992">↑</a> ix. 3, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 153; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 596.</li></ol></div>

प्रति-वेश|प्रतिवेश|प्रति|वेश|ప్రతి-వేశ|ప్రతివేశ|ప్రతి|వేశ|ಪ್ರತಿ-ವೇಶ|ಪ್ರತಿವೇಶ|ಪ್ರತಿ|ವೇಶ|prati-veśa|prativeśa|prati|veśa|prati-vesha|prativesha|vesha|_vei_120@1993|_vei2_pn032
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1992">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1994">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn031">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn032">2:032</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn033">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prati-veśa,</b> ‘neighbour,’ occurs, often metaphorically, from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201993">1</a></sup> onwards.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201993">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201993">↑</a> x. 66, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201993">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 97; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xi. 75; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvi. 9; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 5, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 4, 3.</li></ol></div>

प्रति-वेश्य|प्रतिवेश्य|प्रति|वेश्य|ప్రతి-వేశ్య|ప్రతివేశ్య|ప్రతి|వేశ్య|ಪ್ರತಿ-ವೇಶ್ಯ|ಪ್ರತಿವೇಶ್ಯ|ಪ್ರತಿ|ವೇಶ್ಯ|prati-veśya|prativeśya|prati|veśya|prati-veshya|prativeshya|veshya|_vei_120@1994|_vei2_pn032
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1993">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1995">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn031">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn032">2:032</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn033">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prati-veśya</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) at the end of the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka (xv. 1) as the pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बृहद्दिव">bṛhaddiva</a>.</b> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रातिवेश्य">prātiveśya</a>.</b></p></div>

प्रति-श्रुत्का|प्रतिश्रुत्का|प्रति|श्रुत्का|ప్రతి-శ్రుత్కా|ప్రతిశ్రుత్కా|ప్రతి|శ్రుత్కా|ಪ್ರತಿ-ಶ್ರುತ್ಕಾ|ಪ್ರತಿಶ್ರುತ್ಕಾ|ಪ್ರತಿ|ಶ್ರುತ್ಕಾ|prati-śrutkā|pratiśrutkā|prati|śrutkā|prati-shrutkaa|pratishrutkaa|shrutkaa|_vei_120@1995|_vei2_pn032
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1994">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1996">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn031">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn032">2:032</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn033">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prati-śrutkā,</b> ‘echo,’ shows that this phenomenon had already received a name as early as the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201995"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201995">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iv. 13).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201995"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201995">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 14, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, vii. 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 32; xxx. 19.</li></ol></div>

प्रति-ष्ठा|प्रतिष्ठा|प्रति|ष्ठा|ప్రతి-ష్ఠా|ప్రతిష్ఠా|ప్రతి|ష్ఠా|ಪ್ರತಿ-ಷ್ಠಾ|ಪ್ರತಿಷ್ಠಾ|ಪ್ರತಿ|ಷ್ಠಾ|prati-ṣṭhā|pratiṣṭhā|prati|ṣṭhā|prati-ShThaa|pratiShThaa|ShThaa|_vei_120@1996|_vei2_pn032
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1995">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1997">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn031">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn032">2:032</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn033">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prati-ṣṭhā</b> is found in one passage of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201996">1</a></sup> where Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201996">2</a></sup> thinks the word is used as a technical term of law; possibly a ‘sanctuary’ may be meant, but it is more than doubtful whether the sense of ‘home’ or ‘abode,’ as given by Roth,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201996">3</a></sup> is not quite adequate. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ज्ञातृ">jñātṛ</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201996">↑</a> vi. 32, 3 = viii. 8, 21 = <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xii. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201996">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 181.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201996">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> 3. So a <i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतिष्ठा">pratiṣṭhā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="काम">kāma</a>,</i> ‘one desirous of a fixed abode,’ <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 3, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxiii. 18, 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

प्रति-सर|प्रतिसर|प्रति|सर|ప్రతి-సర|ప్రతిసర|ప్రతి|సర|ಪ್ರತಿ-ಸರ|ಪ್ರತಿಸರ|ಪ್ರತಿ|ಸರ|prati-sara|pratisara|prati|sara|_vei_120@1997|_vei2_pn032
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1996">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1998">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn031">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn032">2:032</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn033">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prati-sara</b> is used in several passages of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201997">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201997">2</a></sup> to denote an amulet, according to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201997">3</a></sup> because it was a band, and so returned on itself (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रति">prati</a>-sṛ,</i> ‘go back’). The sense is doubtful; perhaps ‘attacking’ may really be the root idea.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201997">4</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुनःसर">punaḥsara</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201997">↑</a> ii. 11, 2; iv. 40, 1; viii. 5, 1. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201997">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 2, 4, 20; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xii. 30, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201997">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> followed by Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 53, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201997">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, cxxxiii; <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 576.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 263; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 345; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 164.</li></ol></div>

प्रति-हर्तृ|प्रतिहर्तृ|प्रति|हर्तृ|ప్రతి-హర్తృ|ప్రతిహర్తృ|ప్రతి|హర్తృ|ಪ್ರತಿ-ಹರ್ತೃ|ಪ್ರತಿಹರ್ತೃ|ಪ್ರತಿ|ಹರ್ತೃ|prati-hartṛ|pratihartṛ|prati|hartṛ|prati-hartR|pratihartR|hartR|prati-hartRi|pratihartRi|hartRi|prati-hartRu|pratihartRu|hartRu|prati-hartRRi|pratihartRRi|hartRRi|_vei_120@1998|_vei2_pn033
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1997">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1999">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn032">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn033">2:033</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn034">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prati-hartṛ</b> is the name of the assistant of the Udgātṛ in the list of the sixteen priests (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋत्विज्">ṛtvij</a></b>). It is found in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201998"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201998">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201998"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201998">2</a></sup> but not in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201998"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201998">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201998"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201998">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 3, 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201998"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201998">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 2, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 3, 4, 22; xii. 1, 1, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 15, 3; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 10, 11; 11, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201998"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201998">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 227.</li></ol></div>

प्रती-दर्श श्वैक्न|प्रतीदर्श श्वैक्न|प्रती|प्रति|दर्श श्वैक्न|ప్రతీ-దర్శ శ్వైక్న|ప్రతీదర్శ శ్వైక్న|ప్రతీ|ప్రతి|దర్శ శ్వైక్న|ಪ್ರತೀ-ದರ್ಶ ಶ್ವೈಕ್ನ|ಪ್ರತೀದರ್ಶ ಶ್ವೈಕ್ನ|ಪ್ರತೀ|ಪ್ರತಿ|ದರ್ಶ ಶ್ವೈಕ್ನ|pratī-darśa śvaikna|pratīdarśa śvaikna|pratī|prati|darśa śvaikna|pratii-darsha shvaikna|pratiidarsha shvaikna|pratii|darsha shvaikna|pratee-darsha shvaikna|prateedarsha shvaikna|pratee|_vei_120@1999|_vei2_pn033
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1998">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2000">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn032">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn033">2:033</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn034">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pratī-<a class="vei_120a" href="दर्श">darśa</a> Śvaikna</b> is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1201999"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1201999">1</a></sup> as sacrificing with the <a class="vei_120a" href="दाक्षायण">dākṣāyaṇa</a> offering, and as teaching <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुप्लन् सार्ञ्जय">suplan sārñjaya</a>,</b> who thence became <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सहदेव">sahadeva</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सार्ञ्जय">sārñjaya</a>.</b> In a second passage<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1201999"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1201999">2</a></sup> he is called Pratīdarśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐभावत">aibhāvata</a>, and again brought into connexion with <a class="vei_120a" href="सुप्लन् सार्ञ्जय">suplan sārñjaya</a>. According to Eggeling,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1201999"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1201999">3</a></sup> he is to be deemed a king of the Śviknas; apparently, too, he was a descendant of Ibhāvant. A Pratīdarśa is also mentioned in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1201999"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1201999">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1201999"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1201999">↑</a> ii. 4, 4, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1201999"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1201999">↑</a> xii. 8, 2, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1201999"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1201999">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 239, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1201999"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1201999">↑</a> iv. 8, 7.</li></ol></div>

प्रतीप प्रातिसत्वन|ప్రతీప ప్రాతిసత్వన|ಪ್ರತೀಪ ಪ್ರಾತಿಸತ್ವನ|pratīpa prātisatvana|pratiipa praatisatvana|prateepa praatisatvana|_vei_120@2000|_vei2_pn033
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@1999">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2001">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn032">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn033">2:033</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn034">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pratīpa Prātisatvana,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202000"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202000">1</a></sup> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रातिसुत्वन">prātisutvana</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202000"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202000">2</a></sup> is the name of a man mentioned in a hymn of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202000"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202000">2</a></sup> Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202000"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202000">3</a></sup> with great ingenuity, compares the fact that <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परिक्षित्">parikṣit</a></b> is mentioned as a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a></b> king in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202000"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202000">4</a></sup> and that, according to the Epic genealogies, his grandson was Pratiśravas, with which name <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रातिसुत्वन">prātisutvana</a>, as very possibly a Prākritized version of Prātiśrutvana may be compared, and his great-grandson was Pratīpa. The identification cannot, however, be regarded as at all certain, and while the Epic may have derived its genealogy from the Atharvaveda, it may have preserved an independent tradition. Bo7htlingk<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202000"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202000">5</a></sup> renders <i>prātisatvanam</i> as ‘in the direction opposed to the Satvans’, and this may be right. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202000"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202000">↑</a> So <a class="vei_120a" href="खिल">khila</a>, v. 15, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 33, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202000"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202000">↑</a> So Av. xx. 129, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Scheftelowitz, <i>Die Apokryphen des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 161; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 18, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202000"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202000">↑</a> So Av. xx. 129, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Scheftelowitz, <i>Die Apokryphen des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 161; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 18, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202000"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202000">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 131.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202000"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202000">↑</a> xx. 127.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202000"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202000">↑</a> Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

प्रती-बोध|प्रतीबोध|प्रती|प्रति|बोध|ప్రతీ-బోధ|ప్రతీబోధ|ప్రతీ|ప్రతి|బోధ|ಪ್ರತೀ-ಬೋಧ|ಪ್ರತೀಬೋಧ|ಪ್ರತೀ|ಪ್ರತಿ|ಬೋಧ|pratī-bodha|pratībodha|pratī|prati|bodha|pratii-bodha|pratiibodha|pratii|pratee-bodha|prateebodha|pratee|_vei_120@2001|_vei2_pn034
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2000">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2002">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn033">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn034">2:034</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn035">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pratī-bodha</b> is mentioned with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बोध">bodha</a></b> in two passages of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202001"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202001">1</a></sup> apparently as the name of a very mythic <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>, ‘Intelligence.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202001"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202001">↑</a> v. 30, 10; viii. 1, 13. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 15, 1.</li></ol></div>

प्रतृद्|ప్రతృద్|ಪ್ರತೃದ್|pratṛd|pratRd|pratRid|pratRud|pratRRid|_vei_120@2002|_vei2_pn034
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2001">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2003">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn033">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn034">2:034</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn035">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pratṛd</b> occurs once in the plural in a hymn of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202002">1</a></sup> where it is clearly a variant of the word <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तृत्सु">tṛtsu</a>.</b> Moreover, the name of King <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतर्दन">pratardana</a>,</b> a descendant of the <a class="vei_120a" href="तृत्सु">tṛtsu</a> king, <b>Divodāsa,</b> confirms the identification of <a class="vei_120a" href="तृत्सु">tṛtsu</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतृद्">pratṛd</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202002">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202002">↑</a> vii. 33, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202002">↑</a> See Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 159; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 138.</li></ol></div>

प्र-तोद|प्रतोद|प्र|तोद|ప్ర-తోద|ప్రతోద|ప్ర|తోద|ಪ್ರ-ತೋದ|ಪ್ರತೋದ|ಪ್ರ|ತೋದ|pra-toda|pratoda|pra|toda|_vei_120@2003|_vei2_pn034
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2002">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2004">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn033">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn034">2:034</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn035">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-toda</b> denotes in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202003"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202003">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202003"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202003">2</a></sup> the ‘goad’ of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात्य">vrātya</a>,</b> the non-Brahminical Āryan or aborigine. Later the word is regularly used for ‘goad’ in general.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202003"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202003">↑</a> xv. 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202003"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202003">↑</a> xvii. 1, 14. See <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xii. 8; Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxii. 4, 10; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 6, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiv. 72, 3. The rendering ‘lance’ seems to have no authority. But see Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 67.</li></ol></div>

प्रत्यक्ष-दर्शन|प्रत्यक्षदर्शन|प्रत्यक्ष|दर्शन|ప్రత్యక్ష-దర్శన|ప్రత్యక్షదర్శన|ప్రత్యక్ష|దర్శన|ಪ್ರತ್ಯಕ್ಷ-ದರ್ಶನ|ಪ್ರತ್ಯಕ್ಷದರ್ಶನ|ಪ್ರತ್ಯಕ್ಷ|ದರ್ಶನ|pratyakṣa-darśana|pratyakṣadarśana|pratyakṣa|darśana|pratyakSha-darshana|pratyakShadarshana|pratyakSha|darshana|pratyaxa-darshana|pratyaxadarshana|pratyaxa|_vei_120@2004|_vei2_pn034
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2003">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2005">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn033">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn034">2:034</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn035">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pratyakṣa-darśana,</b> n., means ‘seeing with one's own eyes,’ as opposed to seeing in a vision (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्वप्न">svapna</a></i>). A section on such visions appears in the Rigveda Āraṇyakas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202004">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202004">↑</a> Aitareva Āranyaka, iii. 2, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, viii. 7.</li></ol></div>

प्रत्य्-एनस्|प्रत्य्-एन|प्रत्येनस्|प्रत्येन|प्रत्य्|एनस्|एन|ప్రత్య్-ఏనస్|ప్రత్య్-ఏన|ప్రత్యేనస్|ప్రత్యేన|ప్రత్య్|ఏనస్|ఏన|ಪ್ರತ್ಯ್-ಏನಸ್|ಪ್ರತ್ಯ್-ಏನ|ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇನಸ್|ಪ್ರತ್ಯೇನ|ಪ್ರತ್ಯ್|ಏನಸ್|ಏನ|praty-enas|praty-ena|pratyenas|pratyena|praty|enas|ena|enas|ena|_vei_120@2005|_vei2_pn034
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2004">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2006">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn033">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn034">2:034</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn035">↷</a><hr><p><b>Praty-enas</b> is found with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उग्र">ugra</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सूत">sūta</a>-grāmaṇī</b> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202005"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202005">1</a></sup> clearly denoting an officer of police. The sense must be that of the humbler ‘servants’ of the king<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202005"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202005">2</a></sup> rather than ‘magistrates,’ as Max Müller, in his translation, takes it. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202005"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202005">3</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202005"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202005">4</a></sup> the word means, according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, the next heir, who is responsible for the debts of a dead man. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202005"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202005">↑</a> iv. 3, 43. 44 (Mādhyaṃdina = iv. 3, 37. 38 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202005"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202005">↑</a> Bo7htlingk's Translation, p. 66, where he takes <i><a class="vei_120a" href="उग्र">ugra</a></i> as an adjective.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202005"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202005">↑</a> viii. 4 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 463).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202005"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202005">↑</a> iv. 16, 16. 17.</li></ol></div>

प्र-दर|प्रदर|प्र|दर|ప్ర-దర|ప్రదర|ప్ర|దర|ಪ್ರ-ದರ|ಪ್ರದರ|ಪ್ರ|ದರ|pra-dara|pradara|pra|dara|_vei_120@2006|_vei2_pn035
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2005">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2007">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn034">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn035">2:035</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn036">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-dara</b> in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202006">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘cleft’ in the ground.</p><p>^1 <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 8, 5; v. 2, 4, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxv. 7.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="2"><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202006">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 35, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 10, 7; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 2, 3, 8; xiii. 8, 3, 10, etc.</li></ol></div>

प्र-दिव्|प्रदिव्|प्र|दिव्|ప్ర-దివ్|ప్రదివ్|ప్ర|దివ్|ಪ್ರ-ದಿವ್|ಪ್ರದಿವ್|ಪ್ರ|ದಿವ್|pra-div|pradiv|pra|div|_vei_120@2007|_vei2_pn035
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2006">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2008">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn034">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn035">2:035</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn036">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-div</b> in the Atharvaveda (xviii. 2, 48) is the third and highest heaven, in which the Fathers dwell. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xx. 1) it is the fifth of a series of seven heavens.</p></div>

प्र-दिश्|प्रदिश्|प्र|दिश्|ప్ర-దిశ్|ప్రదిశ్|ప్ర|దిశ్|ಪ್ರ-ದಿಶ್|ಪ್ರದಿಶ್|ಪ್ರ|ದಿಶ್|pra-diś|pradiś|pra|diś|pra-dish|pradish|dish|_vei_120@2008|_vei2_pn035
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2007">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2009">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn034">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn035">2:035</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn036">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-diś,</b> like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दिश्">diś</a>,</b> normally designates only a ‘quarter’ of the sky, or ‘point’ of the compass. Four,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202008">1</a></sup> five,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202008">2</a></sup> six,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202008">3</a></sup> and seven<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202008">4</a></sup> such points are enumerated, or more generally ‘all’ are mentioned.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202008">5</a></sup> In some passages,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202008">6</a></sup> on the other hand, the word has the definite sense of an ‘intermediate quarter,’ which is more precisely denoted by <i>avāntara-<a class="vei_120a" href="दिश्">diś</a>.</i></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202008">↑</a> Rv. i. 164, 42; vii. 35, 8; x. 19, 8; Av. i. 11, 2; ii. 10, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202008">↑</a> Rv. ix. 86, 29; Av. i. 30, 4; iii. 4, 2; 20, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202008">↑</a> Av. iv. 11, 1; 20, 2; x. 7, 35.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202008">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 32.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202008">↑</a> Rv. vi. 75, 2; x. 121, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202008">↑</a> Av. v. 28, 2; ix. 2, 21; xix. 20, 2, etc.</li></ol></div>

प्र-धन|प्रधन|प्र|धन|ప్ర-ధన|ప్రధన|ప్ర|ధన|ಪ್ರ-ಧನ|ಪ್ರಧನ|ಪ್ರ|ಧನ|pra-dhana|pradhana|pra|dhana|_vei_120@2009|_vei2_pn035
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2008">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2010">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn034">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn035">2:035</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn036">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-dhana</b> denotes ‘contest,’ whether the real conflict of war or the competition of the chariot race, in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202009"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202009">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202009"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202009">↑</a> i. 116, 2; 154, 3; 169, 2; x. 102, 5, etc.</li></ol></div>

प्र-धि|प्रधि|प्र|धि|ప్ర-ధి|ప్రధి|ప్ర|ధి|ಪ್ರ-ಧಿ|ಪ್ರಧಿ|ಪ್ರ|ಧಿ|pra-dhi|pradhi|pra|dhi|_vei_120@2010|_vei2_pn035
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2009">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2011">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn034">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn035">2:035</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn036">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-dhi</b> is the name of some part of the wheel of a chariot, probably the ‘felly.’ In one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202010"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202010">1</a></sup> and in one of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202010"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202010">2</a></sup> the ‘nave’ (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="नभ्य">nabhya</a></b>) and the ‘felly’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रधि">pradhi</a></i>) are mentioned along with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपधि">upadhi</a>,</b> which must then be either a collective name for the spokes or an inner rim within the felly and binding the spokes. In the riddle hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202010"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202010">3</a></sup> twelve Pradhis are mentioned with three naves, one wheel, and three hundred and sixty spokes; what exactly is here meant by this particular term it would be useless to conjecture, though it is clear that the passage as a whole symbolizes  the year with three seasons, twelve months, and three hundred and sixty days. Elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202010"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202010">4</a></sup> the nave and the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रधि">pradhi</a> alone are mentioned, or the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रधि">pradhi</a> occurs by itself.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202010"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202010">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202010"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202010">↑</a> ii. 39, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202010"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202010">↑</a> vi. 70, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202010"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202010">↑</a> i. 164, 48.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202010"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202010">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 4, 11, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 15; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 5, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202010"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202010">↑</a> Rv. iv. 30, 15; x. 102, 7, etc. In Av. xviii. 2, 14, <i>Pradhāv <a class="vei_120a" href="अधि">adhi</a></i> is merely an incorrect variant of the <i>pradhāvati</i> of Rv. x. 154, 1. The same corruption is seen by Lanman (in Whitney's Translation of the Atharvaveda, xcii) in Av. vi. 70, 3 (n. 2).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Whitney, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 334; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 248.</li></ol></div>

प्र-ध्वंसन|प्रध्वंसन|प्र|ध्वंसन|ప్ర-ధ్వంసన|ప్రధ్వంసన|ప్ర|ధ్వంసన|ಪ್ರ-ಧ್ವಂಸನ|ಪ್ರಧ್ವಂಸನ|ಪ್ರ|ಧ್ವಂಸನ|pra-dhvaṃsana|pradhvaṃsana|pra|dhvaṃsana|pra-dhvaMsana|pradhvaMsana|dhvaMsana|_vei_120@2011|_vei2_pn036
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2010">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2012">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn035">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn036">2:036</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn037">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-dhvaṃsana.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राध्वंसन">prādhvaṃsana</a></b></p></div>

प्र-पण|प्रपण|प्र|पण|ప్ర-పణ|ప్రపణ|ప్ర|పణ|ಪ್ರ-ಪಣ|ಪ್ರಪಣ|ಪ್ರ|ಪಣ|pra-paṇa|prapaṇa|pra|paṇa|pra-paNa|prapaNa|paNa|_vei_120@2012|_vei2_pn036
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2011">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2013">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn035">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn036">2:036</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn037">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-paṇa</b> in the Atharvaveda (xii. 15, 4. 5) denotes ‘barter’ or ‘exchange,’ balanced by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतिपण">pratipaṇa</a>.</b></p></div>

प्र-पथ|प्रपथ|प्र|पथ|ప్ర-పథ|ప్రపథ|ప్ర|పథ|ಪ್ರ-ಪಥ|ಪ್ರಪಥ|ಪ್ರ|ಪಥ|pra-patha|prapatha|pra|patha|_vei_120@2013|_vei2_pn036
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2012">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2014">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn035">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn036">2:036</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn037">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-patha</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202013">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202013">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘long journey.’ Wilson<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202013">3</a></sup> has seen in one passage<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202013">4</a></sup> the sense of ‘resting-place,’ where travellers can obtain food (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="खादि">khādi</a></i>). Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202013">5</a></sup> shows that this is impossible, and the reading (<i>prapatheṣu</i>) in the passage in question is not improbably<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202013">6</a></sup> an error for <i>prapadeṣu.</i> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202013">7</a></sup> the word means a ‘broad road.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202013">↑</a> x. 17, 4. 6; 63, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202013">↑</a> vii. 15.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202013">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 2, 151.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202013">↑</a> Rv. i. 166, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202013">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 231.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202013">↑</a> Roth. St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.; Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 108; Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 166. Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> does not follow Roth.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202013"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202013">↑</a> xxxvii. 14 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 466).</li></ol></div>

प्र-पथिन्|प्रपथिन्|प्र|पथिन्|ప్ర-పథిన్|ప్రపథిన్|ప్ర|పథిన్|ಪ್ರ-ಪಥಿನ್|ಪ್ರಪಥಿನ್|ಪ್ರ|ಪಥಿನ್|pra-pathin|prapathin|pra|pathin|_vei_120@2014|_vei2_pn036
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2013">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2015">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn035">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn036">2:036</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn037">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-pathin</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202014"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202014">1</a></sup> is the name of a patron, perhaps a Yādava, in one hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202014"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202014">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202014"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202014">↑</a> <i>Prapathī</i> might be the stem, the word occurring as a proper name in the nominative singular only (<i>cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammer,</i> 377, 3); but this is not probable, the stem <i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रपथिन्">prapathin</a></i> being otherwise found as an adjective</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202014"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202014">↑</a> viii. 1, 30. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 159; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 90.</li></ol></div>

प्र-पा|प्रपा|प्र|ప్ర-పా|ప్రపా|ప్ర|ಪ್ರ-ಪಾ|ಪ್ರಪಾ|ಪ್ರ|pra-pā|prapā|pra|pra-paa|prapaa|_vei_120@2015|_vei2_pn037
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2014">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2016">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn036">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn037">2:037</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn038">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-pā</b> seems to denote a ‘spring’ in the desert in the only Rigveda passage where it occurs.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202015"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202015">1</a></sup> In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202015"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202015">2</a></sup> it has merely the sense of ‘drinking,’ or a ‘drink.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202015"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202015">↑</a> x. 4, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202015"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202015">↑</a> iii. 30, 6. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 10, 1, 2.</li></ol></div>

प्र-पितामह|प्रपितामह|प्र|पितामह|ప్ర-పితామహ|ప్రపితామహ|ప్ర|పితామహ|ಪ್ರ-ಪಿತಾಮಹ|ಪ್ರಪಿತಾಮಹ|ಪ್ರ|ಪಿತಾಮಹ|pra-pitāmaha|prapitāmaha|pra|pitāmaha|pra-pitaamaha|prapitaamaha|pitaamaha|_vei_120@2016|_vei2_pn037
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2015">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2017">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn036">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn037">2:037</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn038">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-pitāmaha,</b> ‘great-grandfather,’ is found in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202016"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202016">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202016"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202016">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202016"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202016">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 5, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 36; Av. xviii. 4, 35.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202016"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202016">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 2, 16; xii. 8, 1, 7.</li></ol></div>

प्र-पित्व|प्रपित्व|प्र|पित्व|ప్ర-పిత్వ|ప్రపిత్వ|ప్ర|పిత్వ|ಪ್ರ-ಪಿತ್ವ|ಪ್ರಪಿತ್ವ|ಪ್ರ|ಪಿತ್ವ|pra-pitva|prapitva|pra|pitva|_vei_120@2017|_vei2_pn037
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2016">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2018">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn036">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn037">2:037</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn038">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-pitva</b> is found in several passages of the Rigveda as a designation of time. In one passage<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202017"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202017">1</a></sup> the sense is made clear by the context: ‘at the rising of the sun’ (<i>sūra udite</i>), ‘at midday’ (<i>madhyaṃdine divaḥ</i>), and ‘at the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रपित्व">prapitva</a>, bordering on the night’ (<i>apiśarvare</i>). In another passage<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202017"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202017">2</a></sup> the sense of ‘late in the day’ also seems adequate, while the phrase<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202017"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202017">3</a></sup> <i>abhipitve ahnaḥ,</i> ‘at the close of day,’ also denotes the evening. According to Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202017"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202017">4</a></sup> the sense of the word is the ‘decisive moment’ in a race or a battle, and so the ‘end of the day.’<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202017"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202017">5</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अहन्">ahan</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202017"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202017">↑</a> viii. 1, 29.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202017"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202017">↑</a> vii. 41, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202017"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202017">↑</a> iv. 16, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202017"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202017">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 174 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202017"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202017">↑</a> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> took it to mean ‘daybreak’; so also Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 362. Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> on the other hand, gives the meaning as ‘decline of day,’ ‘evening.’ See also Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, 24 <i>et seq.;</i> Oldenberg, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 46, 183 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

प्र-प्रोथ|प्रप्रोथ|प्र|प्रोथ|ప్ర-ప్రోథ|ప్రప్రోథ|ప్ర|ప్రోథ|ಪ್ರ-ಪ್ರೋಥ|ಪ್ರಪ್ರೋಥ|ಪ್ರ|ಪ್ರೋಥ|pra-protha|praprotha|pra|protha|_vei_120@2018|_vei2_pn037
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2017">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2019">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn036">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn037">2:037</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn038">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-protha</b> is the name in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 4, 1) of a plant used as a substitute for <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>.</p></div>

प्र-फर्वी|प्र-फर्वि|प्रफर्वी|प्रफर्वि|प्र|फर्वी|फर्वि|ప్ర-ఫర్వీ|ప్ర-ఫర్వి|ప్రఫర్వీ|ప్రఫర్వి|ప్ర|ఫర్వీ|ఫర్వి|ಪ್ರ-ಫರ್ವೀ|ಪ್ರ-ಫರ್ವಿ|ಪ್ರಫರ್ವೀ|ಪ್ರಫರ್ವಿ|ಪ್ರ|ಫರ್ವೀ|ಫರ್ವಿ|pra-pharvī|pra-pharvi|prapharvī|prapharvi|pra|pharvī|pharvi|pra-pharvii|prapharvii|pharvii|pra-pharvee|prapharvee|pharvee|_vei_120@2019|_vei2_pn037
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2018">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2020">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn036">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn037">2:037</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn038">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-pharvī</b> denotes a ‘wanton woman’ in the Rigveda (x. 85, 22), the Atharvaveda (v. 22, 7), and the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202019">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202019">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 5, 6; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 7, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvi. 12; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xii. 71.</li></ol></div>

प्र-बुध्|प्रबुध्|प्र|बुध्|ప్ర-బుధ్|ప్రబుధ్|ప్ర|బుధ్|ಪ್ರ-ಬುಧ್|ಪ್ರಬುಧ್|ಪ್ರ|ಬುಧ್|pra-budh|prabudh|pra|budh|_vei_120@2020|_vei2_pn037
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2019">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2021">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn036">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn037">2:037</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn038">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-budh,</b> occurring in one passage of the Rigveda (viii. 27, 19), is used in the locative parallel with <i>nimruci,</i> ‘at the setting (of the sun),’ and clearly means ‘at the rising (of the sun).’ </p></div>

प्र-मगन्द|प्रमगन्द|प्र|मगन्द|ప్ర-మగన్ద|ప్రమగన్ద|ప్ర|మగన్ద|ಪ್ರ-ಮಗನ್ದ|ಪ್ರಮಗನ್ದ|ಪ್ರ|ಮಗನ್ದ|pra-maganda|pramaganda|pra|maganda|_vei_120@2021|_vei2_pn038
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2020">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2022">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn037">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn038">2:038</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn039">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-maganda</b> is the name of a prince in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202021"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202021">1</a></sup> where he is mentioned as the king of the <b>Kīkaṭas,</b> and where he seems to be designated by the epithet <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नैचाशाख">naicāśākha</a>,</i> ‘belonging to a low branch or race.’ On the other hand, <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202021"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202021">2</a></sup> takes <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रमगन्द">pramaganda</a> to mean the ‘son of a usurer,’ an explanation that is hardly probable. Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202021"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202021">3</a></sup> thinks that <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नैचाशाख">naicāśākha</a></i> refers not to <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रमगन्द">pramaganda</a>, but to the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> plant, the plant being called <i>nīcāśākha,</i> ‘having shoots turned downwards,’ and that the passage refers to a raid against the Kīkaṭas, who were not observers of the milk cult or the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> cult, with the intention of winning their lands where the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> grew and where there were cows. Bo7htlingk,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202021"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202021">4</a></sup> however, questions this view, which is not very probable. A place name is possibly meant by <a class="vei_120a" href="नैचाशाख">naicāśākha</a>.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202021"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202021">5</a></sup> The name <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रमगन्द">pramaganda</a> seems un-Āryan.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202021"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202021">↑</a> iii. 53, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202021"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202021">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 32.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202021"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202021">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 14-16. 241-245.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202021"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202021">↑</a> <i>Proceedings of the Saxon Academy,</i> December 12, 1891.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202021"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202021">↑</a> Sāyaṇa, in his introduction to his commentary on the Rigveda, p. 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 31; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 6, 153; Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Kommentar,</i> 58.</li></ol></div>

प्र-मन्दनी|प्र-मन्दनि|प्रमन्दनी|प्रमन्दनि|प्र|मन्दनी|मन्दनि|ప్ర-మన్దనీ|ప్ర-మన్దని|ప్రమన్దనీ|ప్రమన్దని|ప్ర|మన్దనీ|మన్దని|ಪ್ರ-ಮನ್ದನೀ|ಪ್ರ-ಮನ್ದನಿ|ಪ್ರಮನ್ದನೀ|ಪ್ರಮನ್ದನಿ|ಪ್ರ|ಮನ್ದನೀ|ಮನ್ದನಿ|pra-mandanī|pra-mandani|pramandanī|pramandani|pra|mandanī|mandani|pra-mandanii|pramandanii|mandanii|pra-mandanee|pramandanee|mandanee|_vei_120@2022|_vei2_pn038
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2021">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2023">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn037">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn038">2:038</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn039">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-mandanī</b> is the name of an Apsaras in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202022"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202022">1</a></sup> Probably the word primarily denoted a certain sweet-scented plant, which seems to be the sense of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्र">pra</a>-manda</i> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202022"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202022">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202022"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202022">↑</a> iv. 37, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202022"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202022">↑</a> viii. 17; xxv. 11; xxxii. 29; <i>niṣpramanda,</i> xxxvi. 15. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 69; Caland, <i>Altindisches Zauberritual,</i> 15, n. 11.</li></ol></div>

प्र-मर|प्रमर|प्र|मर|ప్ర-మర|ప్రమర|ప్ర|మర|ಪ್ರ-ಮರ|ಪ್ರಮರ|ಪ್ರ|ಮರ|pra-mara|pramara|pra|mara|_vei_120@2023|_vei2_pn038
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2022">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2024">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn037">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn038">2:038</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn039">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-mara</b> in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202023">1</a></sup> is taken by Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202023">2</a></sup> to be a proper name.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202023">↑</a> x. 27, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202023">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 165.</li></ol></div>

प्र-मोत|प्रमोत|प्र|मोत|ప్ర-మోత|ప్రమోత|ప్ర|మోత|ಪ್ರ-ಮೋತ|ಪ್ರಮೋತ|ಪ್ರ|ಮೋತ|pra-mota|pramota|pra|mota|_vei_120@2024|_vei2_pn038
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2023">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2025">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn037">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn038">2:038</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn039">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-mota</b> is the name of some sort of disease in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202024"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202024">1</a></sup> according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary. Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202024"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202024">2</a></sup> however, thinks that the word must be an adjective meaning ‘dumb.’ This view is accepted, though with doubt, by Whitney<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202024"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202024">3</a></sup> and by Bloomfield.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202024"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202024">4</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202024"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202024">↑</a> ix. 8, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202024"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202024">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 378, n.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202024"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202024">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 550.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202024"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202024">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 601.</li></ol></div>

प्र-योग|प्रयोग|प्र|योग|ప్ర-యోగ|ప్రయోగ|ప్ర|యోగ|ಪ್ರ-ಯೋಗ|ಪ್ರಯೋಗ|ಪ್ರ|ಯೋಗ|pra-yoga|prayoga|pra|yoga|_vei_120@2025|_vei2_pn039
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2024">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2026">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn038">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn039">2:039</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn040">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-yoga</b> is the name of a seer in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202025"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202025">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202025"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202025">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 1, 10, 1; <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā,</b> xix. 10 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 478).</li></ol></div>

प्र-योग्य|प्रयोग्य|प्र|योग्य|ప్ర-యోగ్య|ప్రయోగ్య|ప్ర|యోగ్య|ಪ್ರ-ಯೋಗ್ಯ|ಪ್ರಯೋಗ್ಯ|ಪ್ರ|ಯೋಗ್ಯ|pra-yogya|prayogya|pra|yogya|_vei_120@2026|_vei2_pn039
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2025">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2027">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn038">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn039">2:039</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn040">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-yogya</b> denotes in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (viii. 12, 3) an animal yoked to a carriage, ‘draught animal.’</p></div>

प्र-लाप|प्रलाप|प्र|लाप|ప్ర-లాప|ప్రలాప|ప్ర|లాప|ಪ್ರ-ಲಾಪ|ಪ್ರಲಾಪ|ಪ್ರ|ಲಾಪ|pra-lāpa|pralāpa|pra|lāpa|pra-laapa|pralaapa|laapa|_vei_120@2027|_vei2_pn039
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2026">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2028">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn038">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn039">2:039</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn040">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-lāpa,</b> ‘prattle,’ is found with other words of similar import in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202027">1</a></sup> and in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202027">2</a></sup> of the Rigveda. The phrase <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतश">aitaśa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="प्रलाप">pralāpa</a>,</b> ‘Discourse of <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतश">aitaśa</a>,’ occurs as a designation of certain passages of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202027">3</a></sup> The name has no justification in the text itself.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202027">↑</a> xi. 8, 25.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202027">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 33; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxx. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 17, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202027">↑</a> See Bloomfield, <i>Atharvaveda,</i> pp. 98, 101, n. 12; Scheftelowitz, <i>Die Apokryphen des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 159 <i>et seq.;</i> Macdonell, <i>Bṛhaddevatā,</i> 2, 323.</li></ol></div>

प्र-वचन|प्रवचन|प्र|वचन|ప్ర-వచన|ప్రవచన|ప్ర|వచన|ಪ್ರ-ವಚನ|ಪ್ರವಚನ|ಪ್ರ|ವಚನ|pra-vacana|pravacana|pra|vacana|pra-vachana|pravachana|vachana|_vei_120@2028|_vei2_pn039
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2027">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2029">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn038">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn039">2:039</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn040">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-vacana</b> means ‘oral instruction,’ ‘teaching,’ in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202028">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202028">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202028">↑</a> xi. 5, 7, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202028">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 1, 3, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 23; Muṇḍaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 2, 3, etc.</li></ol></div>

प्र-वत्|प्रवत्|प्र|वत्|ప్ర-వత్|ప్రవత్|ప్ర|వత్|ಪ್ರ-ವತ್|ಪ್ರವತ್|ಪ್ರ|ವತ್|pra-vat|pravat|pra|vat|_vei_120@2029|_vei2_pn039
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2028">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2030">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn038">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn039">2:039</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn040">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-vat,</b> ‘height,’ is contrasted with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="निवत्">nivat</a>,</b> ‘valley,’ in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202029"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202029">1</a></sup> where it occurs several times.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202029"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202029">2</a></sup> The word is also found later.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202029"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202029">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202029"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202029">↑</a> vii. 50, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202029"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202029">↑</a> Rv. ii. 13, 2; iv. 17, 7; 22, 4; vi. 17, 12; vii. 32, 27; x. 14, 1; 57, 12; 75, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202029"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202029">↑</a> Av. i. 13, 2; 26, 3; vi. 28, 3; x. 10, 2; xii. 1, 2; xviii. 4, 7.</li></ol></div>

प्र-वर|प्र-वर|प्रवर|प्र|वर|ప్ర-వర_౧|ప్ర-వర|ప్రవర|ప్ర|వర|ಪ್ರ-ವರ_೧|ಪ್ರ-ವರ|ಪ್ರವರ|ಪ್ರ|ವರ|pra-vara_1|pra-vara|pravara|pra|vara|_vei_120@2030|_vei2_pn039
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2029">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2031">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn038">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn039">2:039</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn040">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Pra-vara</b> denotes properly the ‘summons’ addressed to <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> at the beginning of the sacrifice to perform his functions. But as <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> was then invoked by the names of the ancestors of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202030"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202030">1</a></sup> the term <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रवर">pravara</a> denotes the series of ancestors invoked.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202030"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202030">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202030"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202030">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 25. See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 78.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202030"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202030">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 1, 9; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 1, 1. 20; iii. 7, 4, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 31, etc.</li></ol></div>

प्र-वर|प्र-वर|प्रवर|प्र|वर|ప్ర-వర_౨|ప్ర-వర|ప్రవర|ప్ర|వర|ಪ್ರ-ವರ_೨|ಪ್ರ-ವರ|ಪ್ರವರ|ಪ್ರ|ವರ|pra-vara_2|pra-vara|pravara|pra|vara|_vei_120@2031|_vei2_pn040
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2030">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2032">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn039">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn040">2:040</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn041">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Pra-vara,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202031"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202031">1</a></sup> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्र-वार">pra-vāra</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202031"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202031">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘covering’ or ‘woollen cloth’ in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202031"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202031">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 10. in the Mādhyaṃdina recension.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202031"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202031">↑</a> Sāyaṇa on Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> and <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension, vi. 2, 7.</li></ol></div>

प्र-वर्त|प्रवर्त|प्र|वर्त|ప్ర-వర్త|ప్రవర్త|ప్ర|వర్త|ಪ್ರ-ವರ್ತ|ಪ್ರವರ್ತ|ಪ್ರ|ವರ್ತ|pra-varta|pravarta|pra|varta|_vei_120@2032|_vei2_pn040
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2031">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2033">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn039">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn040">2:040</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn041">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-varta,</b> occurring in the description of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात्य">vrātya</a></b> in the Atharvaveda (xv. 2, 1, <i>et seq.</i>), is explained by the St. Petersburg Dictionary as a ‘round ornament.’ According to the commentator on the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (2, 453 <i>Bibl. Ind.</i>), it means an ‘ear-ring.’</p></div>

प्र-वल्हिका|प्रवल्हिका|प्र|वल्हिका|ప్ర-వల్హికా|ప్రవల్హికా|ప్ర|వల్హికా|ಪ್ರ-ವಲ್ಹಿಕಾ|ಪ್ರವಲ್ಹಿಕಾ|ಪ್ರ|ವಲ್ಹಿಕಾ|pra-valhikā|pravalhikā|pra|valhikā|pra-valhikaa|pravalhikaa|valhikaa|_vei_120@2033|_vei2_pn040
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2032">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2034">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn039">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn040">2:040</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn041">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-valhikā,</b> a ‘riddle,’ is the name given in the Brāhmaṇas of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202033">1</a></sup> to certain verses of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202033">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202033">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 33; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxx. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202033">↑</a> xx. 133; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="खिल">khila</a>, v. 16.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 98-100.</li></ol></div>

प्र-वात|प्रवात|प्र|वात|ప్ర-వాత|ప్రవాత|ప్ర|వాత|ಪ್ರ-ವಾತ|ಪ್ರವಾತ|ಪ್ರ|ವಾತ|pra-vāta|pravāta|pra|vāta|pra-vaata|pravaata|vaata|_vei_120@2034|_vei2_pn040
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2033">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2035">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn039">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn040">2:040</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn041">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-vāta,</b> ‘a windy spot,’ is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202034">1</a></sup> as the place where the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विभीतक">vibhītaka</a></b> nuts, used as dice (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a></b>) grow. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202034">2</a></sup> reference is made to the exposure of decaying matter in such a place.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202034">↑</a> x. 34, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ix. 8. Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 119, thinks the reference here is to nuts being blown down in a storm of wind.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202034">↑</a> vi. 4, 7, 2.</li></ol></div>

प्र-वार|प्रवार|प्र|वार|ప్ర-వార|ప్రవార|ప్ర|వార|ಪ್ರ-ವಾರ|ಪ್ರವಾರ|ಪ್ರ|ವಾರ|pra-vāra|pravāra|pra|vāra|pra-vaara|pravaara|vaara|_vei_120@2035|_vei2_pn040
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2034">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2036">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn039">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn040">2:040</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn041">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-vāra.</b> See 2. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रवर">pravara</a>.</b></p></div>

प्र-वा¤स|प्रवा¤स|प्र|वा¤स|ప్ర-వా¤స|ప్రవా¤స|ప్ర|వా¤స|ಪ್ರ-ವಾ¤ಸ|ಪ್ರವಾ¤ಸ|ಪ್ರ|ವಾ¤ಸ|pra-vā¤sa|pravā¤sa|pra|vā¤sa|pra-vaa¤sa|pravaa¤sa|vaa¤sa|_vei_120@2036|_vei2_pn040
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2035">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2037">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn039">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn040">2:040</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn041">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-vā¤sa,</b> ‘dwelling abroad,’ is mentioned in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202036"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202036">1</a></sup> Ceremonies applicable to one who has returned from foreign residence are given in the Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202036"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202036">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202036"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202036">↑</a> viii. 29, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202036"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202036">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 17, etc.</li></ol></div>

प्र-वाहण जैवलि|प्रवाहण जैवलि|प्र|वाहण जैवलि|जैवल|ప్ర-వాహణ జైవలి|ప్రవాహణ జైవలి|ప్ర|వాహణ జైవలి|జైవల|ಪ್ರ-ವಾಹಣ ಜೈವಲಿ|ಪ್ರವಾಹಣ ಜೈವಲಿ|ಪ್ರ|ವಾಹಣ ಜೈವಲಿ|ಜೈವಲ|pra-vāhaṇa jaivali|pravāhaṇa jaivali|pra|vāhaṇa jaivali|jaivala|pra-vaahaNa jaivali|pravaahaNa jaivali|vaahaNa jaivali|_vei_120@2037|_vei2_pn040
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2036">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2038">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn039">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn040">2:040</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn041">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-vāhaṇa Jaivali</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जैवल">jaivala</a></b> (‘descendant of <b>Jīvala</b>’) is the name of a prince, contemporary with <b>Uddālaka,</b> who  appears in the Upaniṣads<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202037"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202037">1</a></sup> as engaged in philosophical discussions. He is probably identical with the <a class="vei_120a" href="जैवलि">jaivali</a> of the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202037"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202037">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202037"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202037">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 1. 7 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 2, 1, 4 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>); Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 8, 1; v. 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202037"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202037">↑</a> i. 38, 4.</li></ol></div>

प्र-शस्|प्र-श|प्रशस्|प्रश|प्र|शस्|श|ప్ర-శస్|ప్ర-శ|ప్రశస్|ప్రశ|ప్ర|శస్|శ|ಪ್ರ-ಶಸ್|ಪ್ರ-ಶ|ಪ್ರಶಸ್|ಪ್ರಶ|ಪ್ರ|ಶಸ್|ಶ|pra-śas|pra-śa|praśas|praśa|pra|śas|śa|pra-shas|pra-sha|prashas|prasha|shas|sha|_vei_120@2038|_vei2_pn041
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2037">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2039">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn040">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn041">2:041</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn042">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-śas</b> in a <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202038">1</a></sup> denotes, according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, an ‘axe,’ or some similar instrument for cutting.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202038">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202038">↑</a> ii. 6, 5. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="दुर्ग">durga</a> on <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202038">↑</a> From <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शस्">śas</a>,</i> ‘to cut.’</li></ol></div>

प्र-शास्तृ|प्रशास्तृ|प्र|शास्तृ|ప్ర-శాస్తృ|ప్రశాస్తృ|ప్ర|శాస్తృ|ಪ್ರ-ಶಾಸ್ತೃ|ಪ್ರಶಾಸ್ತೃ|ಪ್ರ|ಶಾಸ್ತೃ|pra-śāstṛ|praśāstṛ|pra|śāstṛ|pra-shaastR|prashaastR|shaastR|pra-shaastRi|prashaastRi|shaastRi|pra-shaastRu|prashaastRu|shaastRu|pra-shaastRRi|prashaastRRi|shaastRRi|_vei_120@2039|_vei2_pn041
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2038">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2040">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn040">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn041">2:041</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn042">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-śāstṛ</b> is the name of one of the priests (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋत्विज्">ṛtvij</a></b>) at the Vedic sacrifice. In the lesser sacrifices he plays no part at all, but he appears in the animal (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पशु">paśu</a></i>) and <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> sacrifices, in the former as the only, in the latter as the main, assistant of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a></b> priest in the singing of the litanies. He is mentioned by name in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202039">1</a></sup> and often later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202039">2</a></sup> He is also in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202039">3</a></sup> called <b>Upavaktṛ,</b> this name, like <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रशास्तृ">praśāstṛ</a>, being derived from the fact that one of his chief functions was to issue directions (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रैष">praiṣa</a></i>) to the other priests. Another name for him was Maitrāvaruṇa, because his litanies were mainly addressed to <a class="vei_120a" href="मित्र">mitra</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a>, a connexion already visible in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202039">4</a></sup> The ‘two divine Hotṛs’ of the Āprī litanies denote, according to Oldenberg,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202039">5</a></sup> the heavenly counterparts of the <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रशास्तृ">praśāstṛ</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202039">↑</a> i. 94, 6; ii. 5, 4; <i>praśāstra,</i> ‘the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> bowl of the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रशास्तृ">praśāstṛ</a>,’ 36, 6; <i>praśāstra,</i> ‘the office of the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रशास्तृ">praśāstṛ</a>,’ ii. 1, 2 = x, 91, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202039">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x. 21; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 34; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 6, 6, 6; xi. 5, 5, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202039">↑</a> iv. 9, 5; vi. 71, 5; ix. 95, 5. According to Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 226, the Upavaktṛ is the earliest equivalent of the Ashāvāka.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202039">↑</a> ii. 36, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202039">↑</a> <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 391. Ludwig, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 3, 227, identifies the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रशास्तृ">praśāstṛ</a> with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रस्तोतृ">prastotṛ</a>,</b> but this is most improbable.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 383, 390, 391; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 141 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

प्रश्न|ప్రశ్న|ಪ್ರಶ್ನ|praśna|prashna|_vei_120@2040|_vei2_pn041
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2039">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2041">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn040">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn041">2:041</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn042">↷</a><hr><p><b>Praśna</b> denotes generally ‘enquiry’ or ‘disputed question,’ the phrase <i>praśnam eti</i> having the sense ‘he asks a person for  the decision of a disputed point’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202040">1</a></sup> and elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202040">2</a></sup> Thus <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रश्न">praśna</a> comes to have the definite meaning of ‘decision in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202040">3</a></sup> In the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202040">4</a></sup> are included the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रश्निन्">praśnin</a>, the <a class="vei_120a" href="अभि-प्रश्निन्">abhi-praśnin</a>, and the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रश्न">praśna</a>-vivāka; it is quite likely that here the three parties to a civil case are meant--the plaintiff, the defendant, and the arbitrator or judge (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यमशी">madhyamaśī</a></b>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202040">↑</a> ii. 5, 8, 5; 11, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202040">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 6, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 28.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202040">↑</a> v. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202040">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 6, 1.</li></ol></div>

प्रष्टि|ప్రష్టి|ಪ್ರಷ್ಟಿ|praṣṭi|praShTi|_vei_120@2041|_vei2_pn042
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2040">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2042">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn041">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn042">2:042</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn043">↷</a><hr><p><b>Praṣṭi,</b> like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पृष्ट्या">pṛṣṭyā</a>,</b> denotes a ‘side horse,’ which, however, possibly<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202041">1</a></sup> did not necessarily mean a horse running beside the yoke-horses, but may also have meant a third horse yoked in front as a leader. This seems to be indicated by the reference in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202041">2</a></sup> to the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रष्टि">praṣṭi</a>--here applied to the Maruts’ team--leading (<i>vahati</i>) the team (<i>rohitaḥ</i>). In an obscure passage of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202041">3</a></sup> there is a reference to the Praṣṭis in connexion with a <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्च">pañca</a>-vāhī,</i> ‘drawn by five,’ but it is impossible to gain any clear idea of what is meant. The <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रष्टि">praṣṭi</a> is not rarely referred to elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202041">4</a></sup> In one passage<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202041">5</a></sup> the <i>dhuryau</i> and the <i>praṣṭyau</i> are mentioned together; this probably means the two horses yoked to the pole, with two others fastened in some way one on each side. The adjectives <i>praṣṭimant,</i><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202041">6</a></sup> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रष्टि">praṣṭi</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वाहन">vāhana</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202041">7</a></sup> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रष्टि">praṣṭi</a>-vāhin,</i><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202041">8</a></sup> are all used of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a>,</b> ‘chariot,’ meaning ‘drawn by a side horse (or horses)’ in addition to the yoke-horses. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202041">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202041">↑</a> i. 39, 6; viii. 27, 8. In i. 100, 17, <i>praṣṭibhiḥ</i> seems to refer to the assistants or comrades of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋज्राश्व">ṛjrāśva</a></b> (<i>cf.</i> Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 12, 14); but Ludwig thinks that the word refers to the steeds by which a victory was won.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202041">↑</a> x. 8, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 597.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202041">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8, 21, 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 3, 3, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202041">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 12, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202041">↑</a> Rv. vi. 27, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202041">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 2, 4, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202041">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 6, 4; 7, 1, 5; 9, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvi. 13, 12 (where <i>praṣṭhi-vāhin</i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रष्टि">praṣṭi</a>-vāhin</i> are confused).</p><p>Geldner's conjecture, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 119, that <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रष्टि">praṣṭi</a> denotes a horse yoked in the middle, is withdrawn by himself, <i>Kommentar,</i> 97.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 250; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 102.</li></ol></div>

प्र-सिति|प्रसिति|प्र|सिति|ప్ర-సితి|ప్రసితి|ప్ర|సితి|ಪ್ರ-ಸಿತಿ|ಪ್ರಸಿತಿ|ಪ್ರ|ಸಿತಿ|pra-siti|prasiti|pra|siti|_vei_120@2042|_vei2_pn043
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2041">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2043">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn042">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn043">2:043</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn044">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-siti</b> in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā (ii. 19) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 7, 13, 4) denotes a divine ‘missile,’ but does not seem to be used of human combatants.</p></div>

प्र-सू|प्रसू|प्र|ప్ర-సూ|ప్రసూ|ప్ర|ಪ್ರ-ಸೂ|ಪ್ರಸೂ|ಪ್ರ|pra-sū|prasū|pra|pra-suu|prasuu|pra-soo|prasoo|_vei_120@2043|_vei2_pn043
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2042">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2044">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn042">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn043">2:043</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn044">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-sū</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202043"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202043">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202043"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202043">2</a></sup> denotes the young shoots of grass or herbs used at the sacrifice.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202043"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202043">↑</a> i. 95, 10; iii. 5, 8; vii. 9, 3; 35, 7; viii. 6, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202043"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202043">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvi. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 3, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 5, 1, 18.</li></ol></div>

प्र-सृत|प्रसृत|प्र|सृत|ప్ర-సృత|ప్రసృత|ప్ర|సృత|ಪ್ರ-ಸೃತ|ಪ್ರಸೃತ|ಪ್ರ|ಸೃತ|pra-sṛta|prasṛta|pra|sṛta|pra-sRta|prasRta|sRta|pra-sRita|prasRita|sRita|pra-sRuta|prasRuta|sRuta|pra-sRRita|prasRRita|sRRita|_vei_120@2044|_vei2_pn043
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2043">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2045">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn042">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn043">2:043</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn044">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-sṛta</b> is found in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202044"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202044">1</a></sup> as a measure of capacity, meaning a ‘handful.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202044"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202044">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202044"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202044">↑</a> iv. 5, 10, 7; xiii. 4, 1, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 1, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202044"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202044">↑</a> Primarily, the word designates the hollowed hand ‘stretched out’ to receive what is offered.</li></ol></div>

प्र-स्कण्व|प्रस्कण्व|प्र|स्कण्व|ప్ర-స్కణ్వ|ప్రస్కణ్వ|ప్ర|స్కణ్వ|ಪ್ರ-ಸ್ಕಣ್ವ|ಪ್ರಸ್ಕಣ್ವ|ಪ್ರ|ಸ್ಕಣ್ವ|pra-skaṇva|praskaṇva|pra|skaṇva|pra-skaNva|praskaNva|skaNva|_vei_120@2045|_vei2_pn043
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2044">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2046">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn042">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn043">2:043</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn044">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-skaṇva</b> is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> who is credited by the Anukramaṇī (Index) with the authorship of certain hymns of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202045"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202045">1</a></sup> where<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202045"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202045">2</a></sup> he is mentioned several times. The statement in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202045"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202045">3</a></sup> that he obtained bounty from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पृषध्र">pṛṣadhra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मेध्य">medhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मातरिश्वन्">mātariśvan</a></b> is apparently a blunder.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202045"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202045">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202045"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202045">↑</a> i. 44-50; viii. 49; ix. 95.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202045"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202045">↑</a> i. 44, 6; 45, 3; viii. 3, 9; 51, 2; 54, 8. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202045"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202045">↑</a> xvi. 11, 26.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202045"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202045">↑</a> Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 39.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 104 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

प्र-स्तर|प्रस्तर|प्र|स्तर|ప్ర-స్తర|ప్రస్తర|ప్ర|స్తర|ಪ್ರ-ಸ್ತರ|ಪ್ರಸ್ತರ|ಪ್ರ|ಸ್ತರ|pra-stara|prastara|pra|stara|_vei_120@2046|_vei2_pn043
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2045">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2047">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn042">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn043">2:043</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn044">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-stara</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202046"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202046">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202046"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202046">2</a></sup> denotes the grass strewn as a sacrificial seat.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202046"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202046">↑</a> x. 14, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202046"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202046">↑</a> Av. xvi. 2, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 7, 7, 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ii. 18; xviii. 63; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 26; ii. 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 3, 5, etc.</li></ol></div>

प्र-स्तोक|प्रस्तोक|प्र|स्तोक|ప్ర-స్తోక|ప్రస్తోక|ప్ర|స్తోక|ಪ್ರ-ಸ್ತೋಕ|ಪ್ರಸ್ತೋಕ|ಪ್ರ|ಸ್ತೋಕ|pra-stoka|prastoka|pra|stoka|_vei_120@2047|_vei2_pn043
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2046">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2048">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn042">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn043">2:043</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn044">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-stoka</b> is the name of a generous donor in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202047">1</a></sup> where Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202047">2</a></sup> identifies him with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दिवोदास अतिथिग्व">divodāsa atithigva</a></b> and  <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वत्थ">aśvattha</a></b> or Aśvatha. According to the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202047">3</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a></b> obtained gifts from <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रस्तोक">prastoka</a> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सार्ञ्जय">sārñjaya</a>,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="सृञ्जय">sṛñjaya</a>.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202047">↑</a> vi. 47, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202047">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 158.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202047">↑</a> xvi. 11, 11.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 30, 31; Macdonell, <i>Bṛhaddevatā,</i> 2, 198 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

प्र-स्तोतृ|प्रस्तोतृ|प्र|स्तोतृ|ప్ర-స్తోతృ|ప్రస్తోతృ|ప్ర|స్తోతృ|ಪ್ರ-ಸ್ತೋತೃ|ಪ್ರಸ್ತೋತೃ|ಪ್ರ|ಸ್ತೋತೃ|pra-stotṛ|prastotṛ|pra|stotṛ|pra-stotR|prastotR|stotR|pra-stotRi|prastotRi|stotRi|pra-stotRu|prastotRu|stotRu|pra-stotRRi|prastotRRi|stotRRi|_vei_120@2048|_vei2_pn044
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2047">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2049">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn043">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn044">2:044</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn045">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-stotṛ</b> is the name of an assistant of the Udgātṛ priest who sings the Prastāva,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202048">1</a></sup> or prelude of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> chant. His not being mentioned by name in the Rigveda is merely an accident, for he is clearly referred to in one passage,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202048">2</a></sup> and in the later literature<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202048">3</a></sup> he is a frequent figure. Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202048">4</a></sup> erroneously thinks that <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रशास्तृ">praśāstṛ</a></b> is the earlier name of the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रस्तोतृ">prastotṛ</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202048">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 10, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 23; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 7, 4, 6; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 10, 9; ii. 2, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202048">↑</a> viii. 81, 5 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्र">pra</a> stoṣat</i>). See Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 393, n. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202048">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 3, 2, 1; vi. 6, 3, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 2, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 34; vii. 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 2, 5, 3; v. 4, 5, 22; xii. 1, 1, 6, etc.; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 10, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202048">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 227.</li></ol></div>

प्रस्रवण|ప్రస్రవణ|ಪ್ರಸ್ರವಣ|prasravaṇa|prasravaNa|_vei_120@2049|_vei2_pn044
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2048">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2050">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn043">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn044">2:044</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn045">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prasravaṇa.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्लक्ष">plakṣa</a>.</b></p></div>

प्र-हा|प्रहा|प्र|ప్ర-హా|ప్రహా|ప్ర|ಪ್ರ-ಹಾ|ಪ್ರಹಾ|ಪ್ರ|pra-hā|prahā|pra|pra-haa|prahaa|_vei_120@2050|_vei2_pn044
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2049">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2051">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn043">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn044">2:044</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn045">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pra-hā</b> in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202050">1</a></sup> the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202050">2</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202050">3</a></sup> denotes a ‘winning throw’ at dice, or, generally, any ‘gain’ or ‘advantage.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202050">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202050">↑</a> x. 42, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202050">↑</a> iv. 38, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202050">↑</a> xvi. 14, 2; xx. 11, 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 241, and <i>prahāvant,</i> Rv. iv. 20, 8, meaning ‘acquiring gain,’ according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202050">↑</a> xvi. 14, 2; xx. 11, 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 241, and <i>prahāvant,</i> Rv. iv. 20, 8, meaning ‘acquiring gain,’ according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

प्रा-कार|प्राकार|प्रा|कार|ప్రా-కార|ప్రాకార|ప్రా|కార|ಪ್ರಾ-ಕಾರ|ಪ್ರಾಕಾರ|ಪ್ರಾ|ಕಾರ|prā-kāra|prākāra|prā|kāra|praa-kaara|praakaara|praa|kaara|_vei_120@2051|_vei2_pn044
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2050">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2052">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn043">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn044">2:044</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn045">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prā-kāra</b> in the Śaṅkhāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (xvi. 18, 14) denotes a walled mound supporting a raised platform (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रासाद">prāsāda</a></i>) for spectators.</p></div>

प्रा-काश|प्राकाश|प्रा|काश|ప్రా-కాశ|ప్రాకాశ|ప్రా|కాశ|ಪ್ರಾ-ಕಾಶ|ಪ್ರಾಕಾಶ|ಪ್ರಾ|ಕಾಶ|prā-kāśa|prākāśa|prā|kāśa|praa-kaasha|praakaasha|praa|kaasha|_vei_120@2052|_vei2_pn044
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2051">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2053">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn043">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn044">2:044</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn045">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prā-kāśa</b> is found several times in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202052">1</a></sup> denoting an ornament of metal or a metal mirror. According to Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202052">2</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रावेप">prāvepa</a> has the same sense in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202052">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202052">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 2, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xviii. 9, 10; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 5, 22. etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202052">↑</a> <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 120.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202052">↑</a> iv. 4, 8.</li></ol></div>

प्रा-गहि|प्रागहि|प्रा|गहि|ప్రా-గహి|ప్రాగహి|ప్రా|గహి|ಪ್ರಾ-ಗಹಿ|ಪ್ರಾಗಹಿ|ಪ್ರಾ|ಗಹಿ|prā-gahi|prāgahi|prā|gahi|praa-gahi|praagahi|praa|_vei_120@2053|_vei2_pn045
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2052">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2054">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn044">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn045">2:045</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn046">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prā-gahi</b> is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xxvi. 4) according to Lindner's edition. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रावहि">prāvahi</a>.</b></p></div>

प्राचीन-तान|प्राचीनतान|प्राचीन|तान|ప్రాచీన-తాన|ప్రాచీనతాన|ప్రాచీన|తాన|ಪ್ರಾಚೀನ-ತಾನ|ಪ್ರಾಚೀನತಾನ|ಪ್ರಾಚೀನ|ತಾನ|prācīna-tāna|prācīnatāna|prācīna|tāna|praachiina-taana|praachiinataana|praachiina|taana|praacheena-taana|praacheenataana|praacheena|_vei_120@2054|_vei2_pn045
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2053">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2055">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn044">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn045">2:045</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn046">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prācīna-tāna</b> denotes the ‘warp’ of a piece of cloth in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (vi. 1, 1, 4). <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राचीनातान">prācīnātāna</a>.</b></p></div>

प्राचीन-योगी-पुत्र|प्राचीन-योगीपुत्र|प्राचीन-योगी|प्राचीन-योगि|पुत्र|ప్రాచీన-యోగీ-పుత్ర|ప్రాచీన-యోగీపుత్ర|ప్రాచీన-యోగీ|ప్రాచీన-యోగి|పుత్ర|ಪ್ರಾಚೀನ-ಯೋಗೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಪ್ರಾಚೀನ-ಯೋಗೀಪುತ್ರ|ಪ್ರಾಚೀನ-ಯೋಗೀ|ಪ್ರಾಚೀನ-ಯೋಗಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|prācīna-yogī-putra|prācīna-yogīputra|prācīna-yogī|prācīna-yogi|putra|praachiina-yogii-putra|praachiina-yogiiputra|praachiina-yogii|praachiina-yogi|praacheena-yogee-putra|praacheena-yogeeputra|praacheena-yogee|praacheena-yogi|_vei_120@2055|_vei2_pn045
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2054">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2056">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn044">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn045">2:045</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn046">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prācīna-yogī-putra,</b> ‘son of a female descendant of Prācīnayoga,’ is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सांजीवीपुत्र">sāṃjīvīputra</a></b> in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Mādhyaṃdina recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 4, 32).</p></div>

प्राचीन-योग्य|प्राचीनयोग्य|प्राचीन|योग्य|ప్రాచీన-యోగ్య|ప్రాచీనయోగ్య|ప్రాచీన|యోగ్య|ಪ್ರಾಚೀನ-ಯೋಗ್ಯ|ಪ್ರಾಚೀನಯೋಗ್ಯ|ಪ್ರಾಚೀನ|ಯೋಗ್ಯ|prācīna-yogya|prācīnayogya|prācīna|yogya|praachiina-yogya|praachiinayogya|praachiina|praacheena-yogya|praacheenayogya|praacheena|_vei_120@2056|_vei2_pn045
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2055">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2057">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn044">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn045">2:045</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn046">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prācīna-yogya,</b> ‘descendant of Prācīnayoga,’ is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पाराशर्य">pārāśarya</a>,</b> in the first <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202056">1</a></sup> A <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राचीनयोग्य">prācīnayogya</a> is mentioned also in the Chāndogya<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202056">2</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202056">3</a></sup> Upaniṣads, and the same patronymic is found in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202056">4</a></sup> and in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (see <b>Puluṣa, <a class="vei_120a" href="सत्ययज्ञ">satyayajña</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202056">5</a></sup> <b>Somaśuṣma</b>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202056">↑</a> ii. 6, 2 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>) .</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202056">↑</a> v. 13, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202056">↑</a> i. 6, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202056">↑</a> Of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सत्ययज्ञ">satyayajña</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पौलुषि">pauluṣi</a></b>) x. 6, 1, 5; (of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शौचेय">śauceya</a></b>) xi. 5, 3, 1. 8 (<i>cf.</i> Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 11). <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 61; 2, 213; 3, 274.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202056">↑</a> Called Prācīnayoga in i. 39, 1, but this is probably merely a blunder of the manuscript.</li></ol></div>

प्राचीन-वंश|प्राचीनवंश|प्राचीन|वंश|ప్రాచీన-వంశ|ప్రాచీనవంశ|ప్రాచీన|వంశ|ಪ್ರಾಚೀನ-ವಂಶ|ಪ್ರಾಚೀನವಂಶ|ಪ್ರಾಚೀನ|ವಂಶ|prācīna-vaṃśa|prācīnavaṃśa|prācīna|vaṃśa|praachiina-vaMsha|praachiinavaMsha|praachiina|vaMsha|praacheena-vaMsha|praacheenavaMsha|praacheena|_vei_120@2057|_vei2_pn045
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2056">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2058">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn044">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn045">2:045</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn046">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prācīna-vaṃśa</b> as an adjective denotes ‘having the supporting beam of the roof facing the east’ in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202057">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202057">2</a></sup> The reference is to the central beam running from the middle of the western end of a hall to the middle of the eastern end. This beam was possibly higher than those at the side.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202057">↑</a> iii. 1, 1, 6. 7; 6, 1, 23; iv. 6, 8, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202057">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxii. 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 1, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 3, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

प्राचीन-शाल औपमन्यव|प्राचीनशाल औपमन्यव|प्राचीन|शाल औपमन्यव|ప్రాచీన-శాల ఔపమన్యవ|ప్రాచీనశాల ఔపమన్యవ|ప్రాచీన|శాల ఔపమన్యవ|ಪ್ರಾಚೀನ-ಶಾಲ ಔಪಮನ್ಯವ|ಪ್ರಾಚೀನಶಾಲ ಔಪಮನ್ಯವ|ಪ್ರಾಚೀನ|ಶಾಲ ಔಪಮನ್ಯವ|prācīna-śāla aupamanyava|prācīnaśāla aupamanyava|prācīna|śāla aupamanyava|praachiina-shaala aupamanyava|praachiinashaala aupamanyava|praachiina|shaala aupamanyava|praacheena-shaala aupamanyava|praacheenashaala aupamanyava|praacheena|_vei_120@2058|_vei2_pn045
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2057">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2059">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn044">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn045">2:045</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn046">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prācīna-<a class="vei_120a" href="शाल">śāla</a> Aupamanyava</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपमन्यु">upamanyu</a></b>’) is the name of a householder and theologian in the Chāndogya  <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202058">1</a></sup> A Prācīnaśāli appears as an Udgātṛ priest in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202058">2</a></sup> and the Prācīnaśālas are mentioned in the same <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202058">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202058">↑</a> v. 11, 1. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="महाशाल">mahāśāla</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202058">↑</a> iii. 7, 2; 10, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202058"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202058">↑</a> iii. 10, 1.</li></ol></div>

प्राचीनातान|ప్రాచీనాతాన|ಪ್ರಾಚೀನಾತಾನ|prācīnātāna|praachiinaataana|praacheenaataana|_vei_120@2059|_vei2_pn046
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2058">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2060">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn045">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn046">2:046</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn047">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prācīnātāna,</b> denoting the ‘warp’ of a piece of cloth, is found in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202059"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202059">1</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राचीनतान">prācīnatāna</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202059"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202059">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 12, 3; 17, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 5 (<i>cf.</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 20, n. 2).</li></ol></div>

प्राचीनावीत|ప్రాచీనావీత|ಪ್ರಾಚೀನಾವೀತ|prācīnāvīta|praachiinaaviita|praacheenaaveeta|_vei_120@2060|_vei2_pn046
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2059">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2061">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn045">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn046">2:046</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn047">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prācīnāvīta</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202060"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202060">1</a></sup> denotes the wearing of the sacred thread of the Āryan over the right shoulder and under the left arm, Prācīnāvītin<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202060"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202060">2</a></sup> being the name for the man so wearing the thread. Tilak,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202060"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202060">3</a></sup> however, thinks that these terms do not imply the wearing of a thread, but of a garment.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202060"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202060">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 11, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202060"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202060">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 6, 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 2, 2. 9; 6, 1, 8; xii. 5, 1, 6; <i>prācīnopavīta</i> has the same sense in Av. ix. 1, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202060"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202060">↑</a> <i>Orion,</i> 146, citing <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 1.</li></ol></div>

प्राच्य|ప్రాచ్య|ಪ್ರಾಚ್ಯ|prācya|praachya|_vei_120@2061|_vei2_pn046
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2060">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2062">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn045">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn046">2:046</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn047">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prācya</b> denotes in the plural ‘dwellers in the east.’ They are mentioned in the list of peoples in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202061">1</a></sup> It is very probable that the <b>Kāśis, Kosalas, Videhas,</b> and perhaps <b>Magadhas,</b> are meant, as Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202061">2</a></sup> supposes. In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202061">3</a></sup> the Easterns are said to call <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> by the name of <a class="vei_120a" href="शर्व">śarva</a>, and their mode of making tombs is there<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202061">4</a></sup> referred to with disapproval. The Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202061">5</a></sup> explains the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विपथ">vipatha</a>,</b> ‘rough car,’ of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202061">6</a></sup> as a car of the Easterns (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राच्य">prācya</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a></i>). In the Saṃhitopaniṣad <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202061">7</a></sup> reference is made to the PrācyaPāñcālas. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202061">↑</a> viii. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202061">↑</a> <i>Buddha,</i> 393, n.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202061">↑</a> i. 7, 3, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202061">↑</a> xiii. 8, 1, 5; 2, 1. <i>Cf.</i> also ix. 5, 1, 64. These passages render improbable the earlier view of Weber (<i>Indian Literature,</i> 132, 133) that this <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> is a product of the Eastern peoples, and support his later view that the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, like the other great Brāhmaṇas, belongs to the <a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a> (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>,</b> n. 1).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202061">↑</a> viii. 6, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202061">↑</a> xvii. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202061">↑</a> 2; Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 34, n. 25.</li></ol></div>

प्रजापत्य|ప్రజాపత్య|ಪ್ರಜಾಪತ್ಯ|prajāpatya|prajaapatya|_vei_120@2062|_vei2_pn047
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2061">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2063">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn046">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn047">2:047</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn049">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prajāpatya,</b> ‘descendant of Prajāpati,’ is only a patronymic of mythical persons like <a class="vei_120a" href="आरुणि">āruṇi</a> Suparṇeya (‘descendant of Suparṇā’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka (x. 79), or of <b>Prajāvant</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 21).</p></div>

प्राण|ప్రాణ|ಪ್ರಾಣ|prāṇa|praaNa|_vei_120@2063|_vei2_pn047
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2062">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2064">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn046">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn047">2:047</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn049">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prāṇa,</b> properly denoting ‘breath,’ is a term of wide and vague significance in Vedic literature. It is frequently mentioned from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202063">1</a></sup> onwards; in the Āraṇyakas and Upaniṣads it is one of the commonest symbols of the unity of the universe.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202063">2</a></sup> In the narrow sense <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a> denotes one of the vital airs, of which five are usually<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202063">3</a></sup> enumerated--<a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a>, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अपान">apāna</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="व्यान">vyāna</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="उदान">udāna</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="समान">samāna</a>;</b> but often only two, <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="अपान">apāna</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202063">4</a></sup> or <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="व्यान">vyāna</a>,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202063">5</a></sup> or <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="उदान">udāna</a>;<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202063">6</a></sup> or three, <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अपान">apāna</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="व्यान">vyāna</a>,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202063">7</a></sup> or <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="उदान">udāna</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="व्यान">vyāna</a>,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202063">8</a></sup> or <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="उदान">udāna</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="समान">samāna</a>;<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202063">9</a></sup> or four, <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अपान">apāna</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="व्यान">vyāna</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="समान">samāna</a>,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202063">10</a></sup> or <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अपान">apāna</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="उदान">udāna</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="व्यान">vyāna</a>.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202063">11</a></sup> The exact sense of each of these breaths when all are mentioned cannot be determined.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202063">12</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202063">↑</a> i. 66, 1; x. 59, 6; 90, 13, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202063">↑</a> Deussen, <i>Philosophy of the Upanishads,</i> 89 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202063">↑</a> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उदान">udāna</a>,</b> n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202063">↑</a> Av. ii. 28, 3; v. 4, 7 (Paipp.); vii. 53, 4 (in vii. 53, 3, <a class="vei_120a" href="अपान">apāna</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a>); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 1, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202063">↑</a> Av. v, 4, 7; vi. 41, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202063">↑</a> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उदान">udāna</a>,</b> n. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202063">↑</a> Av. xiii. 2, 46; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 6, 9; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxii. 23; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 29; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, viii. 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202063">↑</a> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उदान">udāna</a>,</b> n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202063">↑</a> <i>Ibid.</i></li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202063">↑</a> Av. x. 2, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202063">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> iii. 4, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202063"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202063">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Deussen, <i>Philosophy of the Upanishads,</i> 273 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

प्राण|ప్రాణ|ಪ್ರಾಣ|prāṇa|praaNa|_vei_120@2064|_vei2_pn047
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2063">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2065">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn046">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn047">2:047</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn049">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prāṇa</b> is also used in a wider sense to denote the organs of sense,<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202064">13</a></sup> or as Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1202064">14</a></sup> puts it, the ‘orifices of the head,’ etc. These are given as six in one passage of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1202064">15</a></sup> presumably the eyes, ears, and nostrils. More frequently there are stated to be seven in the head, the mouth being then included.<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1202064">16</a></sup> Sometimes again they are mentioned  as nine,<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1202064">17</a></sup> or as seven in the head and two below.<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1202064">18</a></sup> Ten are counted in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1202064">19</a></sup> and the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1202064">20</a></sup> while even eleven are mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1202064">21</a></sup> and twelve in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1202064">22</a></sup> where the two breasts are added. Exactly what organs are taken to make up the numbers beyond seven is not certain.<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1202064">23</a></sup> The tenth is the navel (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="नाभि">nābhi</a></i>) in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā;<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1202064">24</a></sup> when eleven are named the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्म">brahma</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="रन्ध्र">randhra</a><sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1202064">25</a></sup> (suture in the crown) may be included; in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_1202064">26</a></sup> as interpreted by the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_1202064">27</a></sup> the seventh and eighth are the organs of taste and speech respectively. But usually these make one only, and the eighth and ninth are either in the breast<sup id="ref_120_28_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_1202064">28</a></sup> or below (the organs of evacuation).<sup id="ref_120_29_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29_vei_1202064">29</a></sup></p><p>The word <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a> has sometimes merely the general sense of breath, even when opposed to <a class="vei_120a" href="अपान">apāna</a>.<sup id="ref_120_30_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_30_vei_1202064">30</a></sup> But its proper sense is beyond question ‘breathing forth,’ ‘expiration,’ and not as the St. Petersburg Dictionary explains it, ‘the breath inspired,’ a version due to the desire to interpret <a class="vei_120a" href="अपान">apāna</a> as ‘expiration,’ a meaning suggested by the preposition <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अप">apa</a>,</i> ‘away.’ This being clearly shown both by the native scholiasts<sup id="ref_120_31_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_31_vei_1202064">31</a></sup> and by other evidence,<sup id="ref_120_32_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_32_vei_1202064">32</a></sup> Bo7htlingk<sup id="ref_120_33_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_33_vei_1202064">33</a></sup> later accepted the new view. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="13"><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202064">↑</a> Colebrooke, <i>Miscellaneous Essays,</i> 1, 339, 355; St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 1.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1202064">↑</a> On <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 3, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1202064">↑</a> xiv. 1. 3, 32; 4, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1202064">↑</a> Av. ii. 12, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 17; iii. 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 3, 21; vi. 4, 2, 5; xiii. 1, 7, 2; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 5, 9, 10; 6, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1202064">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 5, 10, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 7, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 2, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxii. 12, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 4, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 2; Av. v. 28, 1; x. 8, 43 (<i>navadvāram</i>), etc.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1202064">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 5, 9, 10; 6, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1202064">↑</a> xi. 6, 3, 17, where the eleventh is given as the Ātman.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1202064">↑</a> ii. 77 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 240).</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1202064">↑</a> v. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1202064">↑</a> xxxiii. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1202064">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Deussen, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 269; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaha,</i> 185, 187.</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1202064">↑</a> iv. 6, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, ix. 16.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1202064">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_1202064">↑</a> x. 8, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_1202064">↑</a> ii. 2, 3, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_1202064">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxiii. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_29_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29_vei_1202064">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 5, 9, 10; 6, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_30_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_30_vei_1202064">↑</a> Av. v. 4, 7 (Paippalāda) . See Blloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 552.</li><li id="fx_120_31_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_31_vei_1202064">↑</a> Rudradatta on Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 8, 8; xiv. 11, 1; Sāyaṇa on Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 3, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 5, 6, 4; Śaṅkara on Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 3, 2; Ānartīya on <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, vi. 8, 1, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_32_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_32_vei_1202064">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 2, 2, 15, as compared with Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 8, 29; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 2, 2; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 60, 5; ii. 1, 16. 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, v. 1, 4. See Caland, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 55, 261-265; 56, 556-558; and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अपान">apāna</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_33_vei_1202064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_33_vei_1202064">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift,</i> 55, 518.</li></ol></div>

प्राण-भृत्|प्राणभृत्|प्राण|भृत्|ప్రాణ-భృత్|ప్రాణభృత్|ప్రాణ|భృత్|ಪ್ರಾಣ-ಭೃತ್|ಪ್ರಾಣಭೃತ್|ಪ್ರಾಣ|ಭೃತ್|prāṇa-bhṛt|prāṇabhṛt|prāṇa|bhṛt|praaNa-bhRt|praaNabhRt|praaNa|bhRt|praaNa-bhRit|praaNabhRit|bhRit|praaNa-bhRut|praaNabhRut|bhRut|praaNa-bhRRit|praaNabhRRit|bhRRit|_vei_120@2065|_vei2_pn049
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2064">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2066">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn047">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn049">2:049</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prāṇa-bhṛt</b> denotes a ‘living being’ or ‘man’ in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202065">1</a></sup> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202065">2</a></sup> Prānin has the same sense.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202065">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202065">↑</a> i. 5, 22; iii. 1, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202065">↑</a> xi. 2, 6, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202065">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 13; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 4, 2, 2; x. 4, 2, 2; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 11, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>. iii. 3, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 36.</li></ol></div>

प्रातर्|ప్రాతర్|ಪ್ರಾತರ್|prātar|praatar|_vei_120@2066|_vei2_pn049
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2065">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2067">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn047">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn049">2:049</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prātar</b> as a denotation of time signifies the ‘early morning’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202066"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202066">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202066"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202066">2</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अहन्">ahan</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202066"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202066">↑</a> i. 125, 1; ii. 18, 1; iii. 41, 2; 52, 1; iv. 35, 7; v. 76, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202066"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202066">↑</a> Av. iv. 11, 12; vi. 128, 2; vii. 101, 1; xi. 2, 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxii. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 31; iii. 22. 44; iv. 20; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 1, 12; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 11, 7, etc.</li></ol></div>

प्रातर्-अनुवाक|प्रातरनुवाक|प्रातर्|अनुवाक|ప్రాతర్-అనువాక|ప్రాతరనువాక|ప్రాతర్|అనువాక|ಪ್ರಾತರ್-ಅನುವಾಕ|ಪ್ರಾತರನುವಾಕ|ಪ್ರಾತರ್|ಅನುವಾಕ|prātar-anuvāka|prātaranuvāka|prātar|anuvāka|praatar-anuvaaka|praataranuvaaka|praatar|anuvaaka|_vei_120@2067|_vei2_pn049
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2066">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2068">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn047">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn049">2:049</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prātar-anuvāka</b> occurs in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202067"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202067">1</a></sup> as the name of the litany which begins the morning <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> libation.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202067"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202067">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 9, 7; ii. 2, 3, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 15. 17. 18; iv. 10; v. 33; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 9, 3, 7; iv. 3, 4, 21; xi. 5, 5, 9; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 24, 3; iv. 16, 2, etc.</li></ol></div>

प्रातर्-अह्न कौहल|प्रातरह्न कौहल|प्रातर्|अह्न कौहल|ప్రాతర్-అహ్న కౌహల|ప్రాతరహ్న కౌహల|ప్రాతర్|అహ్న కౌహల|ಪ್ರಾತರ್-ಅಹ್ನ ಕೌಹಲ|ಪ್ರಾತರಹ್ನ ಕೌಹಲ|ಪ್ರಾತರ್|ಅಹ್ನ ಕೌಹಲ|prātar-ahna kauhala|prātarahna kauhala|prātar|ahna kauhala|praatar-ahna kauhala|praatarahna kauhala|praatar|_vei_120@2068|_vei2_pn049
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2067">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2069">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn047">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn049">2:049</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prātar-ahna Kauhala</b> is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="केतु वाज्य">ketu vājya</a>,</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202068">1</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौहड">kauhaḍa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202068"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202068">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372; Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 443.</li></ol></div>

प्रा-तर्दनि|प्रातर्दनि|प्रा|तर्दनि|ప్రా-తర్దని|ప్రాతర్దని|ప్రా|తర్దని|ಪ್ರಾ-ತರ್ದನಿ|ಪ್ರಾತರ್ದನಿ|ಪ್ರಾ|ತರ್ದನಿ|prā-tardani|prātardani|prā|tardani|praa-tardani|praatardani|praa|_vei_120@2069|_vei2_pn049
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2068">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2070">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn047">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn049">2:049</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prā-tardani,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतर्दन">pratardana</a>,</b>’ is the name of a prince in one passage of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202069"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202069">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202069"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202069">↑</a> vi. 27, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 157, 159.</li></ol></div>

प्राति-पीय|प्रातिपीय|प्राति|पीय|ప్రాతి-పీయ|ప్రాతిపీయ|ప్రాతి|పీయ|ಪ್ರಾತಿ-ಪೀಯ|ಪ್ರಾತಿಪೀಯ|ಪ್ರಾತಿ|ಪೀಯ|prāti-pīya|prātipīya|prāti|pīya|praati-piiya|praatipiiya|praati|piiya|praati-peeya|praatipeeya|peeya|_vei_120@2070|_vei2_pn049
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2069">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2071">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn047">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn049">2:049</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prāti-pīya</b> is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बल्हिक">balhika</a></b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xii. 9, 3, 3).</p></div>

प्राति-वेश्य|प्रातिवेश्य|प्राति|वेश्य|ప్రాతి-వేశ్య|ప్రాతివేశ్య|ప్రాతి|వేశ్య|ಪ್ರಾತಿ-ವೇಶ್ಯ|ಪ್ರಾತಿವೇಶ್ಯ|ಪ್ರಾತಿ|ವೇಶ್ಯ|prāti-veśya|prātiveśya|prāti|veśya|praati-veshya|praativeshya|praati|veshya|_vei_120@2071|_vei2_pn049
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2070">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2072">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn047">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn049">2:049</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prāti-veśya</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka (xv. 1) as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतिवेश्य">prativeśya</a>.</b></p></div>

प्राति-सुत्वन|प्रातिसुत्वन|प्राति|सुत्वन|ప్రాతి-సుత్వన|ప్రాతిసుత్వన|ప్రాతి|సుత్వన|ಪ್ರಾತಿ-ಸುತ್ವನ|ಪ್ರಾತಿಸುತ್ವನ|ಪ್ರಾತಿ|ಸುತ್ವನ|prāti-sutvana|prātisutvana|prāti|sutvana|praati-sutvana|praatisutvana|praati|_vei_120@2072|_vei2_pn049
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2071">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2073">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn047">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn049">2:049</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prāti-sutvana.</b> See <b>Pratīpa.</b></p></div>

प्रति-बोधी-पुत्र|प्रति-बोधीपुत्र|प्रति-बोधी|प्रति-बोधि|पुत्र|ప్రతి-బోధీ-పుత్ర|ప్రతి-బోధీపుత్ర|ప్రతి-బోధీ|ప్రతి-బోధి|పుత్ర|ಪ್ರತಿ-ಬೋಧೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಪ್ರತಿ-ಬೋಧೀಪುತ್ರ|ಪ್ರತಿ-ಬೋಧೀ|ಪ್ರತಿ-ಬೋಧಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|prati-bodhī-putra|prati-bodhīputra|prati-bodhī|prati-bodhi|putra|prati-bodhii-putra|prati-bodhiiputra|prati-bodhii|prati-bodhee-putra|prati-bodheeputra|prati-bodhee|_vei_120@2073|_vei2_pn049
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2072">«</a>pada9<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2074">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn047">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn049">2:049</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prati-bodhī-putra,</b> ‘son of a female descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रती">pratī</a> bodha,’ is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> (iii. 1, 5) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> (vii. 13) Āraṇyakas.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 244, 310.</p></div>

प्रा-तृद|प्रातृद|प्रा|तृद|ప్రా-తృద|ప్రాతృద|ప్రా|తృద|ಪ್ರಾ-ತೃದ|ಪ್ರಾತೃದ|ಪ್ರಾ|ತೃದ|prā-tṛda|prātṛda|prā|tṛda|praa-tRda|praatRda|praa|tRda|praa-tRida|praatRida|tRida|praa-tRuda|praatRuda|tRuda|praa-tRRida|praatRRida|tRRida|_vei_120@2074|_vei2_pn050
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2073">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2075">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn049">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">2:050</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn051">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prā-tṛda,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतृद्">pratṛd</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of a teacher called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भाल्ल">bhālla</a></b> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 31, 4), and of another teacher in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (v. 13, 2).</p></div>

प्रा-देश|प्रादेश|प्रा|देश|ప్రా-దేశ|ప్రాదేశ|ప్రా|దేశ|ಪ್ರಾ-ದೇಶ|ಪ್ರಾದೇಶ|ಪ್ರಾ|ದೇಶ|prā-deśa|prādeśa|prā|deśa|praa-desha|praadesha|praa|desha|_vei_120@2075|_vei2_pn050
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2074">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2076">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn049">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">2:050</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn051">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prā-deśa</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202075"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202075">1</a></sup> frequently occurs in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202075"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202075">2</a></sup> as a measure of length, a ‘span.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202075"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202075">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202075"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202075">↑</a> ‘Formed with the <i>pradeśa</i>’ (probably ‘indicator,’ as a name of the forefinger; <i>cf. pradeśinī,</i> ‘forefinger,’ Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 10, 1; ii. 9, 14).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202075"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202075">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 5; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 5, 4, 5; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 18, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202075"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202075">↑</a> That is, between thumb and forefinger.</li></ol></div>

प्रा-ध्वंसन|प्राध्वंसन|प्रा|ध्वंसन|ప్రా-ధ్వంసన|ప్రాధ్వంసన|ప్రా|ధ్వంసన|ಪ್ರಾ-ಧ್ವಂಸನ|ಪ್ರಾಧ್ವಂಸನ|ಪ್ರಾ|ಧ್ವಂಸನ|prā-dhvaṃsana|prādhvaṃsana|prā|dhvaṃsana|praa-dhvaMsana|praadhvaMsana|praa|dhvaMsana|_vei_120@2076|_vei2_pn050
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2075">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2077">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn049">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">2:050</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn051">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prā-dhvaṃsana,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रध्वंसन">pradhvaṃsana</a>,’ is the patronymic, in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202076"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202076">1</a></sup> of the mythical <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मृत्यु">mṛtyu</a>,</b> who is there said to be the pupil of <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रध्वंसन">pradhvaṃsana</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202076"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202076">↑</a> ii. 5, 22; iv. 5, 28. Mādhyaṃdina.</li></ol></div>

प्रायश्-चित्त|प्रायश्चित्त|प्रायश्|चित्त|ప్రాయశ్-చిత్త|ప్రాయశ్చిత్త|ప్రాయశ్|చిత్త|ಪ್ರಾಯಶ್-ಚಿತ್ತ|ಪ್ರಾಯಶ್ಚಿತ್ತ|ಪ್ರಾಯಶ್|ಚಿತ್ತ|prāyaś-citta|prāyaścitta|prāyaś|citta|praayash-chitta|praayashchitta|praayash|chitta|_vei_120@2077|_vei2_pn050
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2076">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2078">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn049">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">2:050</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn051">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prāyaś-citta</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202077"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202077">1</a></sup> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रायश्">prāyaś</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="चित्ति">citti</a></b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202077"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202077">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘penance’ or ‘expiation,’ both words occurring frequently in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas. The penances are prescribed for every conceivable sort of ritual, social or moral; a complete list of them is included in the Sāmavidhāna <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202077"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202077">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202077"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202077">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 4, 1, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 9; vi. 12, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202077"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202077">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 4, 1; iii. 1, 3, 2; v. 1, 9, 3; 3, 12, 1; Av. xiv. 1, 30; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxix. 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 11, 46; v. 27; vii. 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 4, 9; iv. 5, 7, 1; xi. 5, 3, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202077"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202077">↑</a> See Konow's Translation, p. 43 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

प्रा-वरेय|प्रावरेय|प्रा|वरेय|ప్రా-వరేయ|ప్రావరేయ|ప్రా|వరేయ|ಪ್ರಾ-ವರೇಯ|ಪ್ರಾವರೇಯ|ಪ್ರಾ|ವರೇಯ|prā-vareya|prāvareya|prā|vareya|praa-vareya|praavareya|praa|_vei_120@2078|_vei2_pn050
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2077">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2079">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn049">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">2:050</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn051">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prā-vareya,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रवर">pravara</a>,’ is the patronymic of the <b>Gargas</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202078"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202078">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202078"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202078">↑</a> xiii. 12 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 474).</li></ol></div>

प्रा-वहि|प्रावहि|प्रा|वहि|ప్రా-వహి|ప్రావహి|ప్రా|వహి|ಪ್ರಾ-ವಹಿ|ಪ್ರಾವಹಿ|ಪ್ರಾ|ವಹಿ|prā-vahi|prāvahi|prā|vahi|praa-vahi|praavahi|praa|_vei_120@2079|_vei2_pn050
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2078">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2080">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn049">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">2:050</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn051">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prā-vahi</b> is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xxvi. 4), where, however, Lindner's edition has <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रागहि">prāgahi</a>.</b> </p></div>

प्रा-वाहणि|प्रावाहणि|प्रा|वाहणि|ప్రా-వాహణి|ప్రావాహణి|ప్రా|వాహణి|ಪ್ರಾ-ವಾಹಣಿ|ಪ್ರಾವಾಹಣಿ|ಪ್ರಾ|ವಾಹಣಿ|prā-vāhaṇi|prāvāhaṇi|prā|vāhaṇi|praa-vaahaNi|praavaahaNi|praa|vaahaNi|_vei_120@2080|_vei2_pn051
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2079">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2081">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn051">2:051</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn052">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prā-vāhaṇi,</b> ‘descendant of <b>Pravāhaṇa,</b>’ is the patronymic of a man called <b>Babara</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (vii. 1, 10, 2).</p></div>

प्रा-वृष्|प्रावृष्|प्रा|वृष्|ప్రా-వృష్|ప్రావృష్|ప్రా|వృష్|ಪ್ರಾ-ವೃಷ್|ಪ್ರಾವೃಷ್|ಪ್ರಾ|ವೃಷ್|prā-vṛṣ|prāvṛṣ|prā|vṛṣ|praa-vRSh|praavRSh|praa|vRSh|praa-vRiSh|praavRiSh|vRiSh|praa-vRuSh|praavRuSh|vRuSh|praa-vRRiSh|praavRRiSh|vRRiSh|_vei_120@2081|_vei2_pn051
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2080">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2082">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn051">2:051</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn052">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prā-vṛṣ</b> is the name of the ‘rainy season’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202081">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202081">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202081">↑</a> vii. 103, 3. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202081">↑</a> Av. xii. 1, 46; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvi. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 4, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 2, 3; vii. 2, 4, 26, etc.</li></ol></div>

प्रा-वेप|प्रावेप|प्रा|वेप|ప్రా-వేప|ప్రావేప|ప్రా|వేప|ಪ್ರಾ-ವೇಪ|ಪ್ರಾವೇಪ|ಪ್ರಾ|ವೇಪ|prā-vepa|prāvepa|prā|vepa|praa-vepa|praavepa|praa|_vei_120@2082|_vei2_pn051
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2081">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2083">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn051">2:051</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn052">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prā-vepa.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राकाश">prākāśa</a>.</b></p></div>

प्राश्|ప్రాశ్|ಪ್ರಾಶ್|prāś|praash|_vei_120@2083|_vei2_pn051
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2082">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2084">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn051">2:051</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn052">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prāś</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202083"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202083">1</a></sup> denotes a ‘debater’ or a ‘debate,’ while <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतिप्राश्">pratiprāś</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202083"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202083">2</a></sup> denotes an ‘opponent in debate.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202083"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202083">↑</a> ii. 27, 1, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202083"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202083">↑</a> ii. 27, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 7, 479 <i>et seq.; Atharvaveda,</i> 73; <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 305, 306, who completely disproves the theory that <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राश्">prāś</a> meant ‘means of life,’ ‘victuals’ (<i>cf.</i> Bo7htlingk, <i>s.v. <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतिप्राश्">pratiprāś</a></i>).</li></ol></div>

प्राश्नी-पुत्र|प्राश्नीपुत्र|प्राश्नी|प्राश्नि|पुत्र|ప్రాశ్నీ-పుత్ర|ప్రాశ్నీపుత్ర|ప్రాశ్నీ|ప్రాశ్ని|పుత్ర|ಪ್ರಾಶ್ನೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಪ್ರಾಶ್ನೀಪುತ್ರ|ಪ್ರಾಶ್ನೀ|ಪ್ರಾಶ್ನಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|prāśnī-putra|prāśnīputra|prāśnī|prāśni|putra|praashnii-putra|praashniiputra|praashnii|praashni|praashnee-putra|praashneeputra|praashnee|_vei_120@2084|_vei2_pn051
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2083">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2085">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn051">2:051</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn052">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prāśnī-putra</b> (‘son of Prāśnī’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आसुरि-वासिन्">āsuri-vāsin</a></b> is mentioned in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202084"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202084">1</a></sup> as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आसुरायण">āsurāyaṇa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202084"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202084">↑</a> vi. 4, 33 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 5, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

प्रा-श्रवण|प्राश्रवण|प्रा|श्रवण|ప్రా-శ్రవణ|ప్రాశ్రవణ|ప్రా|శ్రవణ|ಪ್ರಾ-ಶ್ರವಣ|ಪ್ರಾಶ್ರವಣ|ಪ್ರಾ|ಶ್ರವಣ|prā-śravaṇa|prāśravaṇa|prā|śravaṇa|praa-shravaNa|praashravaNa|praa|shravaNa|_vei_120@2085|_vei2_pn051
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2084">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2086">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn051">2:051</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn052">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prā-śravaṇa.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रास्रवण">prāsravaṇa</a>.</b></p></div>

प्रा-सच|प्रासच|प्रा|सच|ప్రా-సచ|ప్రాసచ|ప్రా|సచ|ಪ್ರಾ-ಸಚ|ಪ್ರಾಸಚ|ಪ್ರಾ|ಸಚ|prā-saca|prāsaca|prā|saca|praa-sacha|praasacha|praa|sacha|_vei_120@2086|_vei2_pn051
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2085">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2087">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn051">2:051</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn052">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prā-saca,</b> m., in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202086">1</a></sup> denotes a ‘cloudburst,’ while in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202086">2</a></sup> the adjective <i>prāsacyaḥ (āpaḥ)</i> means ‘(waters) produced by torrential rain.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202086">↑</a> vii. 5, 11, 1; according to the commentator, ‘congealing.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202086">↑</a> iii. 12, 7, 4; according to the commentator, ‘congealed’ (water).</li></ol></div>

प्रा-साद|प्रासाद|प्रा|साद|ప్రా-సాద|ప్రాసాద|ప్రా|సాద|ಪ್ರಾ-ಸಾದ|ಪ್ರಾಸಾದ|ಪ್ರಾ|ಸಾದ|prā-sāda|prāsāda|prā|sāda|praa-saada|praasaada|praa|saada|_vei_120@2087|_vei2_pn051
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2086">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2088">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn050">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn051">2:051</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn052">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prā-sāda</b> in the sense of ‘palace’ does not occur until the late Adbhuta <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202087">1</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राकार">prākāra</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202087">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 40.</li></ol></div>

प्रा-स्रवण|प्रास्रवण|प्रा|स्रवण|ప్రా-స్రవణ|ప్రాస్రవణ|ప్రా|స్రవణ|ಪ್ರಾ-ಸ್ರವಣ|ಪ್ರಾಸ್ರವಣ|ಪ್ರಾ|ಸ್ರವಣ|prā-sravaṇa|prāsravaṇa|prā|sravaṇa|praa-sravaNa|praasravaNa|praa|sravaNa|_vei_120@2088|_vei2_pn052
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2087">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2089">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn051">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn052">2:052</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn053">↷</a><hr><p><b>Prā-sravaṇa</b> occurs as part of the local name <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्लक्ष प्रास्रवण">plakṣa prāsravaṇa</a>.</b> It also appears as a patronymic ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रस्रवण">prasravaṇa</a>,’ applied to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अवत्सार">avatsāra</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202088">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202088">↑</a> xiii. 3. There is also a various reading, <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राश्रवण">prāśravaṇa</a>.</li></ol></div>

प्रियंगु|प्रियङ्गु|ప్రియంగు|ప్రియఙ్గు|ಪ್ರಿಯಂಗು|ಪ್ರಿಯಙ್ಗು|priyaṅgu|priyaMgu|priyangu|_vei_120@2089|_vei2_pn052
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2088">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2090">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn051">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn052">2:052</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn053">↷</a><hr><p><b>Priyaṅgu</b> denotes ‘panic seed’ (<i>Panicum italicum</i>) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202089"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202089">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202089"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202089">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202089"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202089">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 11, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, x. 11; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202089"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202089">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8, 14, 6 <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 16; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 3, 22 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 3, 13 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>), with Śaṅkara's note.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 241.</li></ol></div>

प्रिय-मेध|प्रियमेध|प्रिय|मेध|ప్రియ-మేధ|ప్రియమేధ|ప్రియ|మేధ|ಪ್ರಿಯ-ಮೇಧ|ಪ್ರಿಯಮೇಧ|ಪ್ರಿಯ|ಮೇಧ|priya-medha|priyamedha|priya|medha|_vei_120@2090|_vei2_pn052
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2089">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2091">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn051">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn052">2:052</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn053">↷</a><hr><p><b>Priya-medha</b> is the name of a seer in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202090"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202090">1</a></sup> where his family, the Priyamedhas, are also repeatedly alluded to.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202090"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202090">2</a></sup> It is not probable that any hymns are really Priyamedha's own composition.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202090"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202090">3</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रैयमेध">praiyamedha</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202090"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202090">↑</a> i. 139, 9; viii. 5, 25; <i>Priyamedhavat,</i> i. 45, 3; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रियमेध">priyamedha</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="स्तुत">stuta</a>,</i> viii. 6, 45.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202090"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202090">↑</a> i. 45, 4; viii. 2, 37; 3, 16; 4, 20; 8, 18; 69, 8; 87, 3; x. 73, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202090"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202090">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 217.</li></ol></div>

प्रिय-रथ|प्रियरथ|प्रिय|रथ|ప్రియ-రథ|ప్రియరథ|ప్రియ|రథ|ಪ್ರಿಯ-ರಥ|ಪ್ರಿಯರಥ|ಪ್ರಿಯ|ರಥ|priya-ratha|priyaratha|priya|ratha|_vei_120@2091|_vei2_pn052
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2090">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2092">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn051">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn052">2:052</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn053">↷</a><hr><p><b>Priya-ratha</b> is the name of a patron of the <b>Pajras</b> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202091"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202091">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202091"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202091">↑</a> i. 122, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 150.</li></ol></div>

प्रिय-व्रत सोमापि|प्रियव्रत सोमापि|प्रिय|व्रत सोमापि|ప్రియ-వ్రత సోమాపి|ప్రియవ్రత సోమాపి|ప్రియ|వ్రత సోమాపి|ಪ್ರಿಯ-ವ್ರತ ಸೋಮಾಪಿ|ಪ್ರಿಯವ್ರತ ಸೋಮಾಪಿ|ಪ್ರಿಯ|ವ್ರತ ಸೋಮಾಪಿ|priya-vrata somāpi|priyavrata somāpi|priya|vrata somāpi|priya-vrata somaapi|priyavrata somaapi|vrata somaapi|_vei_120@2092|_vei2_pn052
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2091">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2093">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn051">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn052">2:052</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn053">↷</a><hr><p><b>Priya-<a class="vei_120a" href="व्रत">vrata</a> Somāpi</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202092">1</a></sup> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सौमापि">saumāpi</a></b> is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1202092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1202092">1b</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_2b_vei_1202092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2b_vei_1202092">2b</a></sup> in which he is said to be the son of <b>Somapa.</b> The name Priyavrata is also found in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3b_vei_1202092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3b_vei_1202092">3b</a></sup> where a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रौहिणायन">rauhiṇāyana</a></b> of that name is mentioned as a teacher.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202092">↑</a> vii. 34.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1202092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1202092">↑</a> vii. 34.</li><li id="fx_120_2b_vei_1202092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2b_vei_1202092">↑</a> xv. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3b_vei_1202092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3b_vei_1202092">↑</a> x. 3, 5, 14.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 8, 136, n.</li></ol></div>

प्रेंख|प्रेङ्ख|ప్రేంఖ|ప్రేఙ్ఖ|ಪ್ರೇಂಖ|ಪ್ರೇಙ್ಖ|preṅkha|preMkha|prenkha|_vei_120@2093|_vei2_pn052
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2092">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2094">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn051">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn052">2:052</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn053">↷</a><hr><p><b>Preṅkha,</b> ‘swing,’ is mentioned in the description of the Mahāvrata rite, given in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202093"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202093">1</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a>  Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202093"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202093">2</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202093"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202093">3</a></sup> and elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202093"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202093">4</a></sup> As far as can be judged from the notices available,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202093"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202093">5</a></sup> the swing was made just like a modern swing. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्लेंख">pleṅkha</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202093"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202093">↑</a> xxxiv. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202093"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202093">↑</a> i. 2, 3. 4; v. 1, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202093"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202093">↑</a> v. 5, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202093"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202093">↑</a> Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, ii. 17, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202093"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202093">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvii. 1 11; 7, 2, etc.</li></ol></div>

प्रेत|ప్రేత|ಪ್ರೇತ|preta|_vei_120@2094|_vei2_pn053
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2093">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2095">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn052">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn053">2:053</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn054">↷</a><hr><p><b>Preta,</b> ‘departed,’ is used to denote a ‘dead man’ in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202094"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202094">1</a></sup> but not in the sense of ‘ghost,’ which only appears later, in post-Vedic literature.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202094"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202094">↑</a> x. 5, 2, 13; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 11, 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

प्रेदि|ప్రేది|ಪ್ರೇದಿ|predi|_vei_120@2095|_vei2_pn053
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2094">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2096">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn052">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn053">2:053</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn054">↷</a><hr><p><b>Predi.</b> See <b>Proti.</b></p></div>

प्रेष्य|ప్రేష్య|ಪ್ರೇಷ್ಯ|preṣya|preShya|_vei_120@2096|_vei2_pn053
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2095">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2097">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn052">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn053">2:053</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn054">↷</a><hr><p><b>Preṣya</b> (‘to be sent’ on an errand) denotes a menial servant or slave, being applied in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202096">1</a></sup> to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>.</b> In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202096">2</a></sup> the adjective <i>praiṣya,</i> ‘menial,’ occurs.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202096">↑</a> vii. 29. See also <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xvii. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202096">↑</a> v. 22, 14.</li></ol></div>

प्रैय-मेध|प्रैयमेध|प्रैय|मेध|ప్రైయ-మేధ|ప్రైయమేధ|ప్రైయ|మేధ|ಪ್ರೈಯ-ಮೇಧ|ಪ್ರೈಯಮೇಧ|ಪ್ರೈಯ|ಮೇಧ|praiya-medha|praiyamedha|praiya|medha|_vei_120@2097|_vei2_pn053
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2096">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2098">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn052">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn053">2:053</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn054">↷</a><hr><p><b>Praiya-medha,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रियमेध">priyamedha</a>,</b>’ is a patronymic of the priests who sacrificed for the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आत्रेय">ātreya</a> Udamaya</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202097"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202097">1</a></sup> They appear in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202097"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202097">2</a></sup> as priests who ‘knew all’ (sacrificial lore). Three Praiyamedhas are referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202097"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202097">3</a></sup> In the Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202097"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202097">4</a></sup> they are called <b>Bharadvājas.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202097"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202097">↑</a> viii. 22.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202097"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202097">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 474); Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 8, 7; Lévi, <i>La doctrine du sacrifice,</i> 150.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202097"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202097">↑</a> ii. 1, 9, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202097"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202097">↑</a> i. 3, 15. The name is also written Prayyamedha and, incorrectly, Praiyyamedha.</li></ol></div>

प्रैष|ప్రైష|ಪ್ರೈಷ|praiṣa|praiSha|_vei_120@2098|_vei2_pn053
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2097">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2099">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn052">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn053">2:053</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn054">↷</a><hr><p><b>Praiṣa</b> is a liturgical term meaning ‘direction’ or ‘invitation,’ repeatedly found in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202098"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202098">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202098"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202098">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202098"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202098">↑</a> Av. v. 26, 4; xi. 7, 18; xvi. 7, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 3, 11, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 19, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202098"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202098">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 13; iii. 9; v. 9, etc.; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 3, 15; xiii. 5, 2, 23; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxviii. 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

प्रोति कौशाम्बेय कौसुरु-बिन्दि|प्रोति कौशाम्बेय कौसुरुबिन्दि|प्रोति कौशाम्बेय कौसुरु|बिन्दि|प्रोति कौशांबेय कौसुरु-बिन्दि|प्रोति कौशांबेय कौसुरुबिन्दि|प्रोति कौशांबेय कौसुरु|ప్రోతి కౌశామ్బేయ కౌసురు-బిన్ది|ప్రోతి కౌశామ్బేయ కౌసురుబిన్ది|ప్రోతి కౌశామ్బేయ కౌసురు|బిన్ది|ప్రోతి కౌశాంబేయ కౌసురు-బిన్ది|ప్రోతి కౌశాంబేయ కౌసురుబిన్ది|ప్రోతి కౌశాంబేయ కౌసురు|ಪ್ರೋತಿ ಕೌಶಾಮ್ಬೇಯ ಕೌಸುರು-ಬಿನ್ದಿ|ಪ್ರೋತಿ ಕೌಶಾಮ್ಬೇಯ ಕೌಸುರುಬಿನ್ದಿ|ಪ್ರೋತಿ ಕೌಶಾಮ್ಬೇಯ ಕೌಸುರು|ಬಿನ್ದಿ|ಪ್ರೋತಿ ಕೌಶಾಂಬೇಯ ಕೌಸುರು-ಬಿನ್ದಿ|ಪ್ರೋತಿ ಕೌಶಾಂಬೇಯ ಕೌಸುರುಬಿನ್ದಿ|ಪ್ರೋತಿ ಕೌಶಾಂಬೇಯ ಕೌಸುರು|proti kauśāmbeya kausuru-bindi|proti kauśāmbeya kausurubindi|proti kauśāmbeya kausuru|bindi|proti kaushaambeya kausuru-bindi|proti kaushaambeya kausurubindi|proti kaushaambeya kausuru|_vei_120@2099|_vei2_pn054
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2098">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2100">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn053">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn054">2:054</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn055">↷</a><hr><p><b>Proti <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशाम्बेय">kauśāmbeya</a> Kausuru-bindi</b> (‘descendant of <b>Kusurubinda</b>’) is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202099"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202099">1</a></sup> as a pupil and contemporary of <b>Uddālaka.</b> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202099"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202099">2</a></sup> on the other hand, Kusurubinda is called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="औद्दालकि">auddālaki</a>,</b> ‘descendant of Uddālaka,’ a fact which seems to indicate that little value is to be attached to these patronymics and allegations of contemporaneousness.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202099"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202099">↑</a> xii. 2, 2, 13. In the parallel passage, Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 24, <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रेदि">predi</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशाम्बेय">kauśāmbeya</a> Kausuravinda is the form of the name.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202099"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202099">↑</a> vii. 2, 2, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Atharvaveda,</i> p. 115.</li></ol></div>

प्रोष्ठ|ప్రోష్ఠ|ಪ್ರೋಷ್ಠ|proṣṭha|proShTha|_vei_120@2100|_vei2_pn054
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2099">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2101">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn053">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn054">2:054</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn055">↷</a><hr><p><b>Proṣṭha,</b> denoting perhaps a ‘bench,’ is found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202100"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202100">1</a></sup> in the adjective <i>proṣṭhaśaya,</i> ‘lying on a bench,’ used of women, and uncompounded in the Taittīriya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202100"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202100">2</a></sup> In the first passage it is distinguished from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तल्प">talpa</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वह्य">vahya</a>,</b> but what the exact difference was there is not sufficient evidence to show.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202100"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202100">↑</a> vii. 55, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202100"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202100">↑</a> ii. 7, 17, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 154.</li></ol></div>

प्रोष्ठ-पद|प्रोष्ठपद|प्रोष्ठ|पद|ప్రోష్ఠ-పద|ప్రోష్ఠపద|ప్రోష్ఠ|పద|ಪ್ರೋಷ್ಠ-ಪದ|ಪ್ರೋಷ್ಠಪದ|ಪ್ರೋಷ್ಠ|ಪದ|proṣṭha-pada|proṣṭhapada|proṣṭha|pada|proShTha-pada|proShThapada|proShTha|_vei_120@2101|_vei2_pn054
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2100">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2102">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn053">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn054">2:054</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn055">↷</a><hr><p><b>Proṣṭha-pada,</b> m., <b>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पदा">padā</a>,</b> f. (‘foot of a bench’), is the name of a double <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p></div>

प्रोष्ठ-पाद वारक्य|प्रोष्ठपाद वारक्य|प्रोष्ठ|पाद वारक्य|ప్రోష్ఠ-పాద వారక్య|ప్రోష్ఠపాద వారక్య|ప్రోష్ఠ|పాద వారక్య|ಪ್ರೋಷ್ಠ-ಪಾದ ವಾರಕ್ಯ|ಪ್ರೋಷ್ಠಪಾದ ವಾರಕ್ಯ|ಪ್ರೋಷ್ಠ|ಪಾದ ವಾರಕ್ಯ|proṣṭha-pāda vārakya|proṣṭhapāda vārakya|proṣṭha|pāda vārakya|proShTha-paada vaarakya|proShThapaada vaarakya|proShTha|paada vaarakya|_vei_120@2102|_vei2_pn054
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2101">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2103">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn053">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn054">2:054</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn055">↷</a><hr><p><b>Proṣṭha-<a class="vei_120a" href="पाद">pāda</a> Vārakya</b> is mentioned in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 41, 1) as a pupil of <a class="vei_120a" href="कंस">kaṃsa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वारकि">vāraki</a>.</p></div>

प्लक्ष|प्लक्ष|ప్లక్ష_౧|ప్లక్ష|ಪ್ಲಕ್ಷ_೧|ಪ್ಲಕ್ಷ|plakṣa_1|plakṣa|plakSha_1|plakSha|plaxa_1|plaxa|_vei_120@2103|_vei2_pn054
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2102">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2104">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn053">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn054">2:054</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn055">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Plakṣa</b> is the name of the waved leaf fig-tree (<i>Ficus infectoria</i>), a large and beautiful tree with small white fruit. It is mentioned in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202103">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202103">2</a></sup> along with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="न्यग्रोध">nyagrodha</a></b> and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्ण">parṇa</a>.</b> Its name is altered in the latter Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202103">3</a></sup> to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रक्ष">prakṣa</a></b> for the sake of an etymology. It is also mentioned in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202103">4</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202103">↑</a> v. 5, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202103">↑</a> vii. 4, 12, 1. <i>Cf.</i> iii. 4, 8, 4; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 10, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202103">↑</a> vi. 3, 10, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202103">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 32; viii. 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8, 19, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8, 3, 10, 12, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 58.</li></ol></div>

प्लक्ष दय्यांपाति|प्लक्ष दय्यांपाति|ప్లక్ష దయ్యాంపాతి_౨|ప్లక్ష దయ్యాంపాతి|ಪ್ಲಕ್ಷ ದಯ್ಯಾಂಪಾತಿ_೨|ಪ್ಲಕ್ಷ ದಯ್ಯಾಂಪಾತಿ|plakṣa dayyāṃpāti_2|plakṣa dayyāṃpāti|plakSha dayyaaMpaati_2|plakSha dayyaaMpaati|plaxa dayyaaMpaati_2|plaxa dayyaaMpaati|_vei_120@2104|_vei2_pn055
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2103">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2105">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn054">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn055">2:055</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn056">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Plakṣa Dayyāṃpāti</b> (‘descendant of Dyāṃpati’ or Dyāṃpāta) was a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अत्यंहस् आरुणि">atyaṃhas āruṇi</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 10, 9, 3. 5).</p></div>

प्लक्ष प्रा-स्रवण|प्लक्ष प्रा-स्रवण|प्लक्ष प्रास्रवण|प्लक्ष प्रा|स्रवण|ప్లక్ష ప్రా-స్రవణ_౩|ప్లక్ష ప్రా-స్రవణ|ప్లక్ష ప్రాస్రవణ|ప్లక్ష ప్రా|స్రవణ|ಪ್ಲಕ್ಷ ಪ್ರಾ-ಸ್ರವಣ_೩|ಪ್ಲಕ್ಷ ಪ್ರಾ-ಸ್ರವಣ|ಪ್ಲಕ್ಷ ಪ್ರಾಸ್ರವಣ|ಪ್ಲಕ್ಷ ಪ್ರಾ|ಸ್ರವಣ|plakṣa prā-sravaṇa_3|plakṣa prā-sravaṇa|plakṣa prāsravaṇa|plakṣa prā|sravaṇa|plakSha praa-sravaNa_3|plakSha praa-sravaNa|plakSha praasravaNa|plakSha praa|sravaNa|plaxa praa-sravaNa_3|plaxa praa-sravaNa|plaxa praasravaNa|plaxa praa|_vei_120@2105|_vei2_pn055
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2104">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2106">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn054">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn055">2:055</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn056">↷</a><hr><p>3. <b>Plakṣa <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रा">prā</a>-sravaṇa</b> is the name of a locality, forty-four days’ journey from the spot where the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a></b> disappears. It is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202105"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202105">1</a></sup> and the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202105"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202105">2</a></sup> In the latter text it is said that the middle of the earth is only a span (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रादेश">prādeśa</a></b>) to the north of it. In the Rigveda Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202105"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202105">3</a></sup> the locality is called Plākṣa <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रस्रवण">prasravaṇa</a>, and is apparently meant to designate the source of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> rather than the place of its reappearance.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202105"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202105">↑</a> xxv. 10, 16. 22; Kātyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxiv. 6, 7; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 17, 12. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202105"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202105">↑</a> iv. 26, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202105"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202105">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 6, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiii. 29, 24.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 31, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

प्लति|ప్లతి|ಪ್ಲತಿ|plati|_vei_120@2106|_vei2_pn055
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2105">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2107">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn054">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn055">2:055</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn056">↷</a><hr><p><b>Plati</b> is the name of a man, the father of the seer of two hymns of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202106">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202106">↑</a> x. 63, 17; 64, 17. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 133.</li></ol></div>

प्लव|प्लव|ప్లవ_౧|ప్లవ|ಪ್ಲವ_೧|ಪ್ಲವ|plava_1|plava|_vei_120@2107|_vei2_pn055
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2106">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2108">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn054">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn055">2:055</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn056">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Plava</b> (‘float’) denotes a ‘boat’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202107"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202107">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202107"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202107">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202107"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202107">↑</a> i. 182, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202107"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202107">↑</a> Av. xii. 2, 48; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 3, 10, 2; vii. 3, 5, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 10, 17, etc.</li></ol></div>

प्लव|प्लव|ప్లవ_౨|ప్లవ|ಪ್ಲವ_೨|ಪ್ಲವ|plava_2|plava|_vei_120@2108|_vei2_pn055
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2107">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2109">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn054">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn055">2:055</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn056">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Plava</b> is the name of an aquatic bird mentioned in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202108">1</a></sup> Perhaps the ‘pelican’ is meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202108">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 20, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 15; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 34.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 93.</li></ol></div>

प्लाक्षि|ప్లాక్షి|ಪ್ಲಾಕ್ಷಿ|plākṣi|plaakShi|plaaxi|_vei_120@2109|_vei2_pn055
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2108">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2110">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn054">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn055">2:055</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn056">↷</a><hr><p><b>Plākṣi,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्लक्ष">plakṣa</a>,</b>’ is the name of a man mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202109"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202109">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a>  Prātiśākhya.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202109"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202109">2</a></sup> In the same Prātiśākhya<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202109"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202109">3</a></sup> a Plākṣāyaṇa, or ‘descendant of Plākṣa,’ is mentioned.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202109"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202109">↑</a> i. 7, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202109"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202109">↑</a> i. 5. 9; ii. 2. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202109"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202109">↑</a> i. 9; ii. 2. 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 35.</li></ol></div>

प्लात|ప్లాత|ಪ್ಲಾತ|plāta|plaata|_vei_120@2110|_vei2_pn056
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2109">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2111">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn055">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn056">2:056</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn057">↷</a><hr><p><b>Plāta,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्लति">plati</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गय">gaya</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (v. 2).</p></div>

प्ला-योगि|प्लायोगि|प्ला|योगि|ప్లా-యోగి|ప్లాయోగి|ప్లా|యోగి|ಪ್ಲಾ-ಯೋಗಿ|ಪ್ಲಾಯೋಗಿ|ಪ್ಲಾ|ಯೋಗಿ|plā-yogi|plāyogi|plā|yogi|plaa-yogi|plaayogi|plaa|_vei_120@2111|_vei2_pn056
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2110">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2112">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn055">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn056">2:056</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn057">↷</a><hr><p><b>Plā-yogi,</b> ‘descendant of Playoga,’ is the patronymic of <b>Āsaṅga</b> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202111"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202111">1</a></sup> According to the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202111"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202111">2</a></sup> Āsaṅga was a woman, but became a man. This version, repeated by Sāyaṇa in his commentary on the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202111"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202111">3</a></sup> is a mere blunder based on the fact that an additional verse, tacked on to the hymn,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202111"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202111">3</a></sup> contains the expression <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शश्वती">śaśvatī</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="नारी">nārī</a>,</i> which has been taken to mean ‘his wife <a class="vei_120a" href="शश्वती">śaśvatī</a>,’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202111"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202111">4</a></sup> instead of merely ‘every woman.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202111"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202111">↑</a> viii. 1, 33.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202111"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202111">↑</a> xvi. 11, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202111"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202111">↑</a> viii. 1, 34. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Religions of India,</i> 150.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202111"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202111">↑</a> viii. 1, 34. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Religions of India,</i> 150.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202111"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202111">↑</a> So even Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 2, 107. But see Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 354.</li></ol></div>

प्लाशुक|ప్లాశుక|ಪ್ಲಾಶುಕ|plāśuka|plaashuka|_vei_120@2112|_vei2_pn056
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2111">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2113">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn055">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn056">2:056</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn057">↷</a><hr><p><b>Plāśuka</b> is found in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (v, 3, 3, 2) as an epithet of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रीहि">vrīhi</a>,</b> ‘rice,’ in the sense of ‘shooting up rapidly.’</p></div>

प्लीहा-कर्ण|प्लीहाकर्ण|प्लीहा|कर्ण|ప్లీహా-కర్ణ|ప్లీహాకర్ణ|ప్లీహా|కర్ణ|ಪ್ಲೀಹಾ-ಕರ್ಣ|ಪ್ಲೀಹಾಕರ್ಣ|ಪ್ಲೀಹಾ|ಕರ್ಣ|plīhā-karṇa|plīhākarṇa|plīhā|karṇa|pliihaa-karNa|pliihaakarNa|pliihaa|karNa|pleehaa-karNa|pleehaakarNa|pleehaa|_vei_120@2113|_vei2_pn056
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2112">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2114">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn055">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn056">2:056</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn057">↷</a><hr><p><b>Plīhā-karṇa</b> as an epithet of cattle in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202113">1</a></sup> probably denotes ‘having a spleen-shaped mark branded on the ear,’ not as Mahīdhara in his commentary on the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202113">2</a></sup> takes it, ‘having a disease called Plīhan in the ear.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202113">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 13, 5 (<i>cf.</i> iv. 2, 9); Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 24.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202113">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

प्लुष्|ప్లుష్|ಪ್ಲುಷ್|pluṣ|pluSh|_vei_120@2114|_vei2_pn056
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2113">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2115">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn055">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn056">2:056</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn057">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pluṣ</b> is the name of some noxious insect in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202114"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202114">1</a></sup> It is also included in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>  (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202114"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202114">2</a></sup> and is mentioned in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202114"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202114">3</a></sup> Possibly a species of ant may be meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202114"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202114">↑</a> i. 191, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202114"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202114">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 20.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202114"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202114">↑</a> i. 3, 24.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 98.</li></ol></div>

प्लेंख|प्लेङ्ख|ప్లేంఖ|ప్లేఙ్ఖ|ಪ್ಲೇಂಖ|ಪ್ಲೇಙ್ಖ|pleṅkha|pleMkha|plenkha|_vei_120@2115|_vei2_pn057
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2114">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2116">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn056">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn057">2:057</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn058">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pleṅkha</b> is a variant form of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रेंख">preṅkha</a>,</b> ‘swing,’ found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (vii. 5, 8, 5) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 2, 6, 6).</p></div>

फण|ఫణ|ಫಣ|phaṇa|phaNa|_vei_120@2116|_vei2_pn057
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2115">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2117">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn056">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn057">2:057</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn058">↷</a><hr><p><b>Phaṇa</b> occurs in some manuscripts of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202116"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202116">1</a></sup> and is explained as meaning an ‘ornament.’ But it is merely a misreading of the correct word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="फल">phala</a></i> in the compound <i>phalahastāḥ,</i> ‘bearing fruits in their hands.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202116"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202116">↑</a> i. 4. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 398; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 19, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

फर्वर|ఫర్వర|ಫರ್ವರ|pharvara|_vei_120@2117|_vei2_pn057
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2116">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2118">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn056">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn057">2:057</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn058">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pharvara,</b> a word occurring only once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202117"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202117">1</a></sup> cannot be interpreted with certainty. It may mean a ‘field in bloom.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202117"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202117">2</a></sup> Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202117"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202117">3</a></sup> explains it as ‘filler,’ and Grassmann as perhaps a ‘sower.’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202117"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202117">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202117"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202117">↑</a> x. 106, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202117"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202117">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 260.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202117"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202117">↑</a> In his commentary on Rv. x. 106, 2. He explains the intensive form <i>parpharat,</i> occurring in the same hymn (x. 106, 7), as from a verb meaning to ‘fill.’</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202117"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202117">↑</a> <i>Wo7rterbuch, s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

फल|ఫల|ಫಲ|phala|_vei_120@2118|_vei2_pn057
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2117">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2119">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn056">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn057">2:057</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn058">↷</a><hr><p><b>Phala,</b> denoting ‘fruit’ generally, especially the fruit of a tree, occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202118"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202118">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202118"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202118">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202118"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202118">↑</a> iii. 45, 4; x. 146, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202118"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202118">↑</a> Av. vi. 124, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 3, 14, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x. 13; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 4, 8; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 1, etc., and see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="फण">phaṇa</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

फलक|ఫలక|ಫಲಕ|phalaka|_vei_120@2119|_vei2_pn057
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2118">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2120">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn056">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn057">2:057</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn058">↷</a><hr><p><b>Phalaka</b> denotes ‘plank,’ as applied in the construction of a cart or chariot, or as used for pressing <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अधि">adhi</a>-ṣavaṇe phalake</i>),<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202119">2</a></sup> or for any other purpose.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202119">3</a></sup></p><p>^1Pañcaviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xvii. 1, 14 (<i>cf. Indische Studien,</i> 1, 33, 44). </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="2"><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202119">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> vii. 30.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202119">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 4, 9; xiii. 4, 3, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 2, 3 (of the swing), etc.</li></ol></div>

फलवती|फलवति|ఫలవతీ|ఫలవతి|ಫಲವತೀ|ಫಲವತಿ|phalavatī|phalavati|phalavatii|phalavatee|_vei_120@2120|_vei2_pn058
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2119">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2121">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn057">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn058">2:058</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn059">↷</a><hr><p><b>Phalavatī,</b> ‘fruitful,’ is the name of a plant in the Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202120">1</a></sup> identified by the scholiast with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रियंगु">priyaṅgu</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202120">↑</a> v. 2. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Omina und Portenta,</i> 315.</li></ol></div>

फल्गु|ఫల్గు|ಫಲ್ಗು|phalgu|_vei_120@2121|_vei2_pn058
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2120">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2122">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn057">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn058">2:058</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn059">↷</a><hr><p><b>Phalgu.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p></div>

फल्गुनी|फल्गुनि|ఫల్గునీ|ఫల్గుని|ಫಲ್ಗುನೀ|ಫಲ್ಗುನಿ|phalgunī|phalguni|phalgunii|phalgunee|_vei_120@2122|_vei2_pn058
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2121">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2123">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn057">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn058">2:058</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn059">↷</a><hr><p><b>Phalgunī.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p></div>

फाण्ट|ఫాణ్ట|ಫಾಣ್ಟ|phāṇṭa|phaaNTa|_vei_120@2123|_vei2_pn058
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2122">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2124">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn057">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn058">2:058</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn059">↷</a><hr><p><b>Phāṇṭa</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202123"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202123">1</a></sup> is said to denote the first particles of butter produced by churning, ‘creamy butter.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202123"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202123">↑</a> iii. 1, 3, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 14, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

फाल|ఫాల|ಫಾಲ|phāla|phaala|_vei_120@2124|_vei2_pn058
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2123">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2125">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn057">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn058">2:058</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn059">↷</a><hr><p><b>Phāla,</b> ‘ploughshare,’ occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202124">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202124">2</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="लांगल">lāṅgala</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202124">↑</a> iv. 57, 8; x. 117, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202124">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xix. 1. <i>Cf. suphāla,</i> Av. iii. 17, 5; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 7, 12; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="फाल">phāla</a>-kṛṣṭa,</i> ‘growing on ploughed land,’ as opposed to <i>āraṇya,</i> ‘wild,’ <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 15.</li></ol></div>

बक दाल्भ्य|బక దాల్భ్య|ಬಕ ದಾಲ್ಭ್ಯ|baka dālbhya|baka daalbhya|_vei_120@2125|_vei2_pn058
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2124">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2126">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn057">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn058">2:058</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn059">↷</a><hr><p><b>Baka Dālbhya</b> (‘descendant of Dalbha’) is the name of a person mentioned in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> as constraining <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> for the <b>Ājakeśins</b> (i. 9, 2), and as a <b>KuruPañcāla</b> (iv. 7, 2).</p></div>

बकुर|బకుర|ಬಕುರ|bakura|_vei_120@2126|_vei2_pn058
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2125">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2127">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn057">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn058">2:058</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn059">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bakura</b> is mentioned in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202126"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202126">1</a></sup> where it is said that the Aśvins made light for the Āryan by blowing their <a class="vei_120a" href="बकुर">bakura</a> against the <b>Dasyus.</b> According to the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202126"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202126">2</a></sup> the thunderbolt<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1202126"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1202126">1b</a></sup> is meant; but much more probable is Roth's<sup id="ref_120_3b_vei_1202126"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3b_vei_1202126">3b</a></sup> view, that the object blown was a musical instrument. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बाकुर">bākura</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202126"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202126">↑</a> i. 117, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202126"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202126">↑</a> vi. 25. <i>Cf.</i> Naighaṇṭuka, iv. 3.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1202126"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1202126">↑</a> i. 117, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_3b_vei_1202126"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3b_vei_1202126">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 290; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 466.</li></ol></div>

बज|బజ|ಬಜ|baja|_vei_120@2127|_vei2_pn059
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2126">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2128">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn058">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn059">2:059</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn060">↷</a><hr><p><b>Baja</b> is the name in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202127"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202127">1</a></sup> of a plant used against a demon of disease. Some sort of mustard plant may be meant.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202127"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202127">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202127"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202127">↑</a> viii. 6, 3. 6. 7. 24.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202127"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202127">↑</a> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 494.</li></ol></div>

बदर|బదర|ಬದರ|badara|_vei_120@2128|_vei2_pn059
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2127">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2129">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn058">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn059">2:059</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn060">↷</a><hr><p><b>Badara</b> denoting, like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कर्कन्धु">karkandhu</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुवल">kuvala</a>,</b> a kind of jujube, is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202128"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202128">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202128"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202128">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202128"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202128">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 10; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 11, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 22, 90; xxi. 30.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202128"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202128">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 5, 1; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 4, 10; xii. 7, 1, 3; 2, 9; 9, 1, 8, etc.; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 156, 5.</li></ol></div>

बद्वन्|బద్వన్|ಬದ್ವನ್|badvan|_vei_120@2129|_vei2_pn059
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2128">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2130">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn058">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn059">2:059</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn060">↷</a><hr><p><b>Badvan</b> seems in one passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202129"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202129">1</a></sup> to denote a ‘causeway.’ It is said to be firmer than an ordinary road.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202129"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202129">↑</a> i. 1, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Lāṭyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 1, 23.</li></ol></div>

बन्धन|బన్ధన|ಬನ್ಧನ|bandhana|_vei_120@2130|_vei2_pn059
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2129">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2131">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn058">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn059">2:059</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn060">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bandhana</b> denotes a ‘rope’ or other fastening in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202130">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202130">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202130">↑</a> Av. iii. 6, 7 (of a boat, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नौ">nau</a></b>); vi. 14, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202130">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 1, 6, 2 (of a horse); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8, 9, 4; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 8, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, xii. 38, etc.</li></ol></div>

बन्धु|బన్ధు|ಬನ್ಧು|bandhu|_vei_120@2131|_vei2_pn059
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2130">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2132">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn058">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn059">2:059</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn060">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bandhu,</b> denoting ‘relationship’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202131"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202131">1</a></sup> in the abstract and ‘relation’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202131"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202131">2</a></sup> in the concrete, occurs in the Rigveda and later.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202131"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202131">↑</a> Rv. v. 73, 4; vii. 72, 2; viii. 73, 12, etc.; Av. v. 11, 10, 11; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iv. 22; x. 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202131"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202131">↑</a> Rv. i. 164, 33; vii. 67, 9; Av. x. 10, 23; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 7, 5, 5, etc.; <i>bandhumant,</i> ‘having relations,’ Rv. viii. 21, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 5, 1, 4, etc.</li></ol></div>

बबर प्रा-वाहणि|बबर प्रावाहणि|बबर प्रा|वाहणि|బబర ప్రా-వాహణి|బబర ప్రావాహణి|బబర ప్రా|వాహణి|ಬಬರ ಪ್ರಾ-ವಾಹಣಿ|ಬಬರ ಪ್ರಾವಾಹಣಿ|ಬಬರ ಪ್ರಾ|ವಾಹಣಿ|babara prā-vāhaṇi|babara prāvāhaṇi|babara prā|vāhaṇi|babara praa-vaahaNi|babara praavaahaNi|babara praa|vaahaNi|_vei_120@2132|_vei2_pn059
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2131">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2133">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn058">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn059">2:059</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn060">↷</a><hr><p><b>Babara <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रा">prā</a>-vāhaṇi</b> (‘descendant of <b>Pravāhaṇa</b>’) is the name of a man who, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202132"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202132">1</a></sup> wished to become an orator, and obtained rhetorical power by the use of the Pañcarātra sacrifice. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202132"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202132">↑</a> vii. 1, 10, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 148.</li></ol></div>

बभ्रु|बभ्रु|బభ్రు_౧|బభ్రు|ಬಭ್ರು_೧|ಬಭ್ರು|babhru_1|babhru|_vei_120@2133|_vei2_pn060
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2132">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2134">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn059">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn060">2:060</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn061">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Babhru</b> is the name, in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202133"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202133">1</a></sup> of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> who received gifts from King <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋणंचय">ṛṇaṃcaya</a>.</b> The same <a class="vei_120a" href="बभ्रु">babhru</a> may be meant in another passage,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202133"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202133">2</a></sup> where he is mentioned as a protégé of the Aśvins; but it is doubtful whether the word is a proper name at all in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202133"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202133">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202133"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202133">↑</a> v. 30, 11. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202133"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202133">↑</a> viii. 22, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202133"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202133">↑</a> iv. 29, 2. It is here taken as a proper name by the St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 126. But Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 199, denies that a proper name is meant.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 214.</li></ol></div>

बभ्रु कौम्भ्य|बभ्रु कौम्भ्य|बभ्रु कौंभ्य_२|बभ्रु कौंभ्य|బభ్రు కౌమ్భ్య_౨|బభ్రు కౌమ్భ్య|బభ్రు కౌంభ్య_౨|బభ్రు కౌంభ్య|ಬಭ್ರು ಕೌಮ್ಭ್ಯ_೨|ಬಭ್ರು ಕೌಮ್ಭ್ಯ|ಬಭ್ರು ಕೌಂಭ್ಯ_೨|ಬಭ್ರು ಕೌಂಭ್ಯ|babhru kaumbhya_2|babhru kaumbhya|_vei_120@2134|_vei2_pn060
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2133">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2135">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn059">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn060">2:060</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn061">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Babhru Kaumbhya</b> (‘descendant of Kumbha’) is the name of the seer of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> or Chant in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xv. 3, 13).</p></div>

बभ्रु दैवा-वृध|बभ्रु दैवा-वृध|बभ्रु दैवावृध|बभ्रु दैवा|वृध|బభ్రు దైవా-వృధ_౩|బభ్రు దైవా-వృధ|బభ్రు దైవావృధ|బభ్రు దైవా|వృధ|ಬಭ್ರು ದೈವಾ-ವೃಧ_೩|ಬಭ್ರು ದೈವಾ-ವೃಧ|ಬಭ್ರು ದೈವಾವೃಧ|ಬಭ್ರು ದೈವಾ|ವೃಧ|babhru daivā-vṛdha_3|babhru daivā-vṛdha|babhru daivāvṛdha|babhru daivā|vṛdha|babhru daivaa-vRdha_3|babhru daivaa-vRdha|babhru daivaavRdha|babhru daivaa|vRdha|babhru daivaa-vRidha_3|babhru daivaa-vRidha|babhru daivaavRidha|vRidha|babhru daivaa-vRudha_3|babhru daivaa-vRudha|babhru daivaavRudha|vRudha|babhru daivaa-vRRidha_3|babhru daivaa-vRRidha|babhru daivaavRRidha|vRRidha|_vei_120@2135|_vei2_pn060
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2134">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2136">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn059">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn060">2:060</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn061">↷</a><hr><p>3. <b>Babhru Daivā-vṛdha</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vii. 34) as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्वत">parvata</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नारद">nārada</a>.</b></p></div>

बम्ब आज-द्विष|बम्ब आजद्विष|बम्ब आज|द्विष|बंब आज-द्विष|बंब आजद्विष|बंब आज|బమ్బ ఆజ-ద్విష|బమ్బ ఆజద్విష|బమ్బ ఆజ|ద్విష|బంబ ఆజ-ద్విష|బంబ ఆజద్విష|బంబ ఆజ|ಬಮ್ಬ ಆಜ-ದ್ವಿಷ|ಬಮ್ಬ ಆಜದ್ವಿಷ|ಬಮ್ಬ ಆಜ|ದ್ವಿಷ|ಬಂಬ ಆಜ-ದ್ವಿಷ|ಬಂಬ ಆಜದ್ವಿಷ|ಬಂಬ ಆಜ|bamba āja-dviṣa|bamba ājadviṣa|bamba āja|dviṣa|bamba aaja-dviSha|bamba aajadviSha|bamba aaja|dviSha|_vei_120@2136|_vei2_pn060
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2135">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2137">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn059">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn060">2:060</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn061">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bamba <a class="vei_120a" href="आज">āja</a>-dviṣa</b> (‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="अज">aja</a>-dviṣ’) is mentioned as a teacher in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (ii. 7, 2). <a class="vei_120a" href="बिम्ब">bimba</a> is a various reading.</p></div>

बम्बा-विश्ववयसौ|बम्बाविश्ववयसौ|बम्बा|विश्ववयसौ|बंबा-विश्ववयसौ|बंबाविश्ववयसौ|बंबा|బమ్బా-విశ్వవయసౌ|బమ్బావిశ్వవయసౌ|బమ్బా|విశ్వవయసౌ|బంబా-విశ్వవయసౌ|బంబావిశ్వవయసౌ|బంబా|ಬಮ್ಬಾ-ವಿಶ್ವವಯಸೌ|ಬಮ್ಬಾವಿಶ್ವವಯಸೌ|ಬಮ್ಬಾ|ವಿಶ್ವವಯಸೌ|ಬಂಬಾ-ವಿಶ್ವವಯಸೌ|ಬಂಬಾವಿಶ್ವವಯಸೌ|ಬಂಬಾ|bambā-viśvavayasau|bambāviśvavayasau|bambā|viśvavayasau|bambaa-vishvavayasau|bambaavishvavayasau|bambaa|vishvavayasau|_vei_120@2137|_vei2_pn060
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2136">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2138">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn059">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn060">2:060</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn061">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bambā-Viśvavayasau</b> are the names, in the form of a compound, of two <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषिस्">ṛṣis</a> who, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202137"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202137">1</a></sup> invented a certain rite.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202137"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202137">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 6, 8, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā xxix. 7, where the reading adopted in the text is Bambhā, though the reading of the Berlin manuscript is Bambhār. The name is taken to be <a class="vei_120a" href="बम्बा">bambā</a> by the St. Petersburg Dictionary; but Bamba is possible, the Dvandva compound accounting for the form with <i>ā.</i> The Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 7, 3, has Bamba-.</li></ol></div>

बरासी|बरासि|బరాసీ|బరాసి|ಬರಾಸೀ|ಬರಾಸಿ|barāsī|barāsi|baraasii|baraasi|baraasee|_vei_120@2138|_vei2_pn060
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2137">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2139">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn059">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn060">2:060</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn061">↷</a><hr><p><b>Barāsī</b> is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202138"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202138">1</a></sup> and the Pañcaviṃśa Brāhmana<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202138"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202138">2</a></sup> denoting a garment of some kind.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202138"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202138">↑</a> xv. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202138"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202138">↑</a> xviii. 9, 16 (where the commentator explains it as made of bark); xxi. 3, 4.</li></ol></div>

बरु|బరు|ಬರು|baru|_vei_120@2139|_vei2_pn060
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2138">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2140">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn059">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn060">2:060</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn061">↷</a><hr><p><b>Baru</b> is the name of the author of a hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202139"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202139">1</a></sup> according to the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202139"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202139">2</a></sup> of that <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202139"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202139">↑</a> x. 96.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202139"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202139">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 25; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 8.</li></ol></div>

बर्कु वार्ष्ण|బర్కు వార్ష్ణ|ಬರ್ಕು ವಾರ್ಷ್ಣ|barku vārṣṇa|barku vaarShNa|_vei_120@2140|_vei2_pn061
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2139">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2141">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn060">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn061">2:061</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn062">↷</a><hr><p><b>Barku Vārṣṇa</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वृषन्">vṛṣan</a></b>’) is the name of a teacher in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202140"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202140">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202140"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202140">↑</a> i. 1, 1, 10; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1, 8 (Mādhyaṃdina = iv. 1, 4 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

बर्हिस्|बर्हि|బర్హిస్|బర్హి|ಬರ್ಹಿಸ್|ಬರ್ಹಿ|barhis|barhi|_vei_120@2141|_vei2_pn061
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2140">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2142">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn060">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn061">2:061</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn062">↷</a><hr><p><b>Barhis</b> is found repeatedly in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202141"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202141">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202141"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202141">2</a></sup> denoting the litter of grass strewn on the sacrificial ground on which the gods are summoned to seat themselves.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202141"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202141">↑</a> i. 63, 7; 108, 4; iii. 4, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202141"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202141">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 4, 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ii. 1; xviii. 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

बलाका|బలాకా|ಬಲಾಕಾ|balākā|balaakaa|_vei_120@2142|_vei2_pn061
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2141">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2143">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn060">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn061">2:061</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn062">↷</a><hr><p><b>Balākā,</b> ‘crane,’ is mentioned in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202142"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202142">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202142"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202142">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 16, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 3, 14; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 22, 23. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 92.</li></ol></div>

बलाय|బలాయ|ಬಲಾಯ|balāya|balaaya|_vei_120@2143|_vei2_pn061
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2142">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2144">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn060">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn061">2:061</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn062">↷</a><hr><p><b>Balāya</b> is the name of an unknown animal mentioned in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202143"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202143">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202143"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202143">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 38; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 19.</li></ol></div>

बलास|బలాస|ಬಲಾಸ|balāsa|balaasa|_vei_120@2144|_vei2_pn061
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2143">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2145">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn060">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn061">2:061</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn062">↷</a><hr><p><b>Balāsa</b> is the name of a disease mentioned several times in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202144">1</a></sup> and occasionally later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202144">2</a></sup> Mahīdhara<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202144">3</a></sup> and Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202144">4</a></sup> interpret the term as ‘consumption.’ Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202144">5</a></sup> supports this view on the ground that it is mentioned<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202144">6</a></sup> as a kind of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यक्ष्म">yakṣma</a>,</b> makes the bones and joints fall apart (<i>asthi-sraṃsa, paruḥ-sraṃsa</i>),<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202144">7</a></sup> and is caused by love, aversion, and the heart,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202144">8</a></sup> characteristics which agree with the statements of the later Hindu medicine.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202144">9</a></sup> It is in keeping with a demon of the character of consumption that <a class="vei_120a" href="बलास">balāsa</a> should appear as an accompaniment of <a class="vei_120a" href="तक्मन्">takman</a>.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202144">10</a></sup> Grohmann,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202144">11</a></sup> however, thought  that a ‘sore’ or ‘swelling’ (in the case of fever caused by dropsy) was meant. Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202144">12</a></sup> considers that the question is still open. Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202144">13</a></sup> renders the word by ‘dropsy.’</p><p>As remedies against the disease the salve (<b>Āñjasa</b>) from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रिककुद्">trikakud</a></b><sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1202144">14</a></sup> and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जंगिड">jaṅgiḍa</a></b><sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1202144">15</a></sup> plant are mentioned.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202144">↑</a> iv. 9, 8; v. 22, 11; vi. 14; 1; 127, 1; ix. 8, 8; xix. 34, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202144">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xii. 97.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202144">↑</a> On Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202144">↑</a> On Av. xix. 34, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202144">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 385-387.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202144">↑</a> Av. ix. 8, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202144">↑</a> Av. vi. 14, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202144">↑</a> ix. 8, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202144">↑</a> Wise, <i>Hindu System of Medicine,</i> 321, 322.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202144">↑</a> Av. iv. 9, 8; xix. 34, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202144">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 396 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202144">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 450.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202144">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 510.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1202144">↑</a> Av. iv. 9, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1202144"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1202144">↑</a> Av. xix. 34, 10.</li></ol></div>

बलि|బలి|ಬಲಿ|bali|_vei_120@2145|_vei2_pn062
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2144">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2146">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn061">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn062">2:062</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn063">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bali</b> occurs several times in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202145">1</a></sup> and often later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202145">2</a></sup> in the sense of tribute to a king or offering to a god. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202145">3</a></sup> thinks that the offerings were in both cases voluntary. He compares the notices of the Germans in Tacitus,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202145">4</a></sup> where the kings of the tribes are said to receive gifts in kind as presents, but not a regular tribute. There seems to be no ground whatever for this view. No doubt in origin the prerogatives of monarchy were due to voluntary action on the part of the tribesmen,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202145">5</a></sup> but that the Vedic peoples, who were essentially a body of conquering invaders, were in this state is most improbable, and the attitude of the Vedic Indian to his gods was at least as compatible with tribute as with voluntary gifts. Zimmer admits that in the case of hostile tribes<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202145">6</a></sup> tribute must be meant even in the Rigveda. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202145">↑</a> To a god, Rv. i. 70, 9; v. 1, 10; viii. 100, 9; to a king, in the compound <i><a class="vei_120a" href="बलि">bali</a>-hṛt,</i> ‘paying tribute,’ vii. 6, 5; x. 173, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202145">↑</a> Metaphorically: Av. vi. 117, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 3, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxix. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 5, 3, etc.; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="बलि">bali</a>-hṛt,</i> Av. xi. 4, 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.; <a class="vei_120a" href="बलि">bali</a>-hāra,</i> Av. xi. 1, 20; literally: Av. iii. 4, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 18, 3; iii. 12, 2, 7; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 2, 15; 5, 3, 18; 6, 3, 17; xi 2, 6, 14; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xv. 7, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 29 (<i>cf.</i> vii. 34); <i><a class="vei_120a" href="बलि">bali</a>-hṛt,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxix. 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202145">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 166, 167.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202145">↑</a> <i>Germania,</i> 15.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202145">↑</a> Later, too, benevolences (<i>praṇayākriyā</i>) were known. See Fleet, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1909, 760762.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202145"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202145">↑</a> See Rv. vii. 6, 5; 18, 19.</li></ol></div>

बल्कस|బల్కస|ಬಲ್ಕಸ|balkasa|_vei_120@2146|_vei2_pn062
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2145">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2147">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn061">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn062">2:062</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn063">↷</a><hr><p><b>Balkasa</b> denotes impure matter given off in the process of fermentation in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202146"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202146">1</a></sup> The exact sense  may be either ‘scum,’ ‘sediment,’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202146"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202146">2</a></sup> or perhaps more probably vegetable matter in the form of ‘husks.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202146"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202146">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202146"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202146">↑</a> xii. 8, 1, 16; 9, 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202146"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202146">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v., Flocken</i> (‘flakes’).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202146"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202146">↑</a> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 236, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

बल्बज|బల్బజ|ಬಲ್ಬಜ|balbaja|_vei_120@2147|_vei2_pn063
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2146">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2148">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn062">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn063">2:063</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn064">↷</a><hr><p><b>Balbaja</b> is the name of the grass called <i>Eleusine indica.</i> It is mentioned in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202147"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202147">1</a></sup> and is said in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202147"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202147">2</a></sup> to be produced from the excrements of cattle. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202147"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202147">2</a></sup> it is stated to be used for the sacrificial litter (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="बर्हिस्">barhis</a></b>) and for fuel. Baskets or other products made from this grass are referred to in a Dānastuti (‘Praise of Gifts’) in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202147"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202147">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202147"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202147">↑</a> xiv. 2, 22. 23.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202147"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202147">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 8, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, x. 10; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202147"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202147">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 8, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, x. 10; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202147"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202147">↑</a> viii. 55. 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 69, 70.</li></ol></div>

बल्हिक|बल्हिक|బల్హిక_౧|బల్హిక|ಬಲ್ಹಿಕ_೧|ಬಲ್ಹಿಕ|balhika_1|balhika|_vei_120@2148|_vei2_pn063
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2147">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2149">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn062">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn063">2:063</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn064">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Balhika</b> is the name of a people in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202148"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202148">1</a></sup> where the fever (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="तक्मन्">takman</a></b>) is called upon to go to the <b>Mūjavants,</b> the <b>Mahāvṛṣas,</b> and the Balhikas. The Mūjavants are quite certainly a northern tribe, and though, as Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202148"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202148">2</a></sup> suggests, the passage may contain a pun on <a class="vei_120a" href="बल्हिक">balhika</a> as suggesting ‘outsider’ (from <i>bahis,</i> ‘without’), still no doubt the name was chosen from a northern tribe. But the view of Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202148"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202148">3</a></sup> and Weber,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202148"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202148">4</a></sup> which Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202148"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202148">5</a></sup> once accepted, that an Iranian tribe is referred to (<i>cf.</i> Balkh), is not at all probable. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202148"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202148">6</a></sup> shows that there is no need whatever to assume Iranian influence. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्शु">parśu</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202148"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202148">↑</a> v. 22, 5. 7. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202148"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202148">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 446.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202148"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202148">↑</a> <i>Zur Litteratur und Geschichte des Weda,</i> 41.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202148"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202148">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 205; <i>Proceedings of the Berlin Academy,</i> 1892, 985-995.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202148"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202148">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 130.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202148"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202148">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 431-433.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 260; Hopkins, <i>Great Epic of India,</i> 373.</li></ol></div>

बल्हिक प्रातिपीय|बल्हिक प्रातिपीय|బల్హిక ప్రాతిపీయ_౨|బల్హిక ప్రాతిపీయ|ಬಲ್ಹಿಕ ಪ್ರಾತಿಪೀಯ_೨|ಬಲ್ಹಿಕ ಪ್ರಾತಿಪೀಯ|balhika prātipīya_2|balhika prātipīya|balhika praatipiiya_2|balhika praatipiiya|balhika praatipeeya_2|balhika praatipeeya|_vei_120@2149|_vei2_pn063
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2148">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2150">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn062">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn063">2:063</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn064">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Balhika Prātipīya</b> is the name of a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a></b> king in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202149"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202149">1</a></sup> where he appears as having been opposed to the restoration of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दुष्टरीतु">duṣṭarītu</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पौंसायन">pauṃsāyana</a></b> to his hereditary sovereignty over the <b>Sṛñjayas,</b> but as having failed to prevent  the restoration being carried out by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रेवोत्तरस्">revottaras</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पाटव">pāṭava</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="चाक्र">cākra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="स्थपति">sthapati</a>.</b> The epithet <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रातिपीय">prātipīya</a> is curious: if it connects him with <b>Pratīpa</b> (whose son he is in the Epic), the form is remarkable, Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202149"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202149">2</a></sup> indeed tacitly altering it to Prātīpīya. In the Epic and the Purāṇas<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202149"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202149">3</a></sup> he is in the form of Vāhlīka made a brother of <b>Devāpi</b> and <b>Śantanu,</b> and a son of Pratīpa. To base chronological conclusions on this<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202149"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202149">4</a></sup> would be utterly misleading, for the facts are that Devāpi was son of <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋष्टिषेण">ṛṣṭiṣeṇa</a> and a priest, while Śantanu was a <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a> prince of unknown parentage, but not probably a son of Pratīpa, who seems to be a late figure in the Vedic age, later than <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परिक्षित्">parikṣit</a>,</b> being his great-grandson in the Epic. Very possibly <a class="vei_120a" href="बल्हिक">balhika</a> was a descendant of Pratīpa. Why he bore the name <a class="vei_120a" href="बल्हिक">balhika</a> must remain uncertain, for there is no evidence of any sort regarding it.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202149"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202149">↑</a> xii. 9, 3, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202149"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202149">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 432.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202149"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202149">↑</a> See Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 273 <i>et seq.;</i> Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 131-136.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202149"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202149">↑</a> Pargiter, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1910, 52.</li></ol></div>

बल्बूथ|బల్బూథ|ಬಲ್ಬೂಥ|balbūtha|balbuutha|balbootha|_vei_120@2150|_vei2_pn064
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2149">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2151">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn063">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn064">2:064</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn065">↷</a><hr><p><b>Balbūtha</b> is mentioned in one hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202150"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202150">1</a></sup> along with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तरुक्ष">tarukṣa</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पृथुश्रवस्">pṛthuśravas</a>,</b> as a giver of gifts to the singer. He is called a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>,</b> but Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202150"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202150">2</a></sup> was inclined to amend the text so as to say that the singer received a hundred Dāsas from <a class="vei_120a" href="बल्बूथ">balbūtha</a>. Zimmer's<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202150"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202150">3</a></sup> suggestion that he may have been the son of an aboriginal mother, or perhaps an aboriginal himself, seems probable.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202150"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202150">4</a></sup> If this was the case, it would be a clear piece of evidence for the establishment of friendly relations between the Āryans and the Dāsas.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202150"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202150">↑</a> viii. 46, 32.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202150"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202150">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202150"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202150">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 117.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202150"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202150">↑</a> Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 30; Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 2, 196.</li></ol></div>

बस्त|बस्त|బస్త_౧|బస్త|ಬಸ್ತ_೧|ಬಸ್ತ|basta_1|basta|_vei_120@2151|_vei2_pn064
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2150">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2152">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn063">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn064">2:064</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn065">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Basta</b> denotes the ‘goat’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202151"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202151">1</a></sup> and the later literature.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202151"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202151">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202151"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202151">↑</a> i. 161, 13. The passage is unintelligible; for a guess, see Tilak, <i>Orion,</i> 166 <i>et seq.,</i> and <i>cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 145, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202151"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202151">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 7, 4; v. 3, 1, 5; 7, 10, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiv. 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 7, 7; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 4, 9 (Mādhyaṃdina = i. 4, 4 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>), etc., and <i>cf.</i> Av. viii. 6, 12; xi. 9, 22.</li></ol></div>

बस्त रामकायन|बस्त रामकायन|బస్త రామకాయన_౨|బస్త రామకాయన|ಬಸ್ತ ರಾಮಕಾಯನ_೨|ಬಸ್ತ ರಾಮಕಾಯನ|basta rāmakāyana_2|basta rāmakāyana|basta raamakaayana_2|basta raamakaayana|_vei_120@2152|_vei2_pn065
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2151">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2153">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn064">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn065">2:065</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn066">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Basta Rāmakāyana</b> is the name of a teacher in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā (iv. 2, 10). The patronymic is variously read Samakāyana.</p></div>

बहु-वचन|बहुवचन|बहु|वचन|బహు-వచన|బహువచన|బహు|వచన|ಬಹು-ವಚನ|ಬಹುವಚನ|ಬಹು|ವಚನ|bahu-vacana|bahuvacana|bahu|vacana|bahu-vachana|bahuvachana|vachana|_vei_120@2153|_vei2_pn065
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2152">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2154">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn064">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn065">2:065</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn066">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bahu-vacana</b> denotes in grammatical terminology the ‘plural’ in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202153"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202153">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202153"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202153">2</a></sup> So <i>dvivat, bahuvat,</i> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202153"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202153">3</a></sup> means ‘in the dual and the plural.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202153"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202153">↑</a> xiii. 5, 1, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202153"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202153">↑</a> v. 23; xi. 16; xii. 7 (which recognises the plural <i>majestatis</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202153"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202153">↑</a> ii. 24. 27; xi. 16.</li></ol></div>

बह्व्-ऋच|बह्वृच|बह्व्|ऋच|బహ్వ్-ఋచ|బహ్వృచ|బహ్వ్|ఋచ|ಬಹ್ವ್-ಋಚ|ಬಹ್ವೃಚ|ಬಹ್ವ್|ಋಚ|bahv-ṛca|bahvṛca|bahv|ṛca|bahv-Rcha|bahvRcha|Rcha|bahv-Richa|bahvRicha|Richa|bahv-Rucha|bahvRucha|Rucha|bahv-RRicha|bahvRRicha|RRicha|_vei_120@2154|_vei2_pn065
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2153">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2155">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn064">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn065">2:065</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn066">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bahv-ṛca</b> denotes an adherent of the Rigveda. The term is found in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202154"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202154">1</a></sup> of the Rigveda, in the Śatapatha<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202154"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202154">2</a></sup> and the Pañcaviṃśa Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202154"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202154">3</a></sup> and in the Āraṇyakas of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202154"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202154">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202154"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202154">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 36; v. 2; vi. 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 11; xvi. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202154"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202154">↑</a> x. 5, 2, 20; xi. 5, 1, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202154"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202154">↑</a> v. 6, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202154"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202154">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 2, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, viii. 4.</li></ol></div>

बाकुर|బాకుర|ಬಾಕುರ|bākura|baakura|_vei_120@2155|_vei2_pn065
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2154">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2156">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn064">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn065">2:065</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn066">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bākura</b> in one passage of the Rigveda (ix. 1, 8) is used as an epithet of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृति">dṛti</a>,</b> the combined words denoting a wind instrument of some kind. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बकुर">bakura</a>.</b></p></div>

बाडेयी-पुत्र|बाडेयीपुत्र|बाडेयी|बाडेयि|पुत्र|బాడేయీ-పుత్ర|బాడేయీపుత్ర|బాడేయీ|బాడేయి|పుత్ర|ಬಾಡೇಯೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಬಾಡೇಯೀಪುತ್ರ|ಬಾಡೇಯೀ|ಬಾಡೇಯಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|bāḍeyī-putra|bāḍeyīputra|bāḍeyī|bāḍeyi|putra|baaDeyii-putra|baaDeyiiputra|baaDeyii|baaDeyi|baaDeyee-putra|baaDeyeeputra|baaDeyee|_vei_120@2156|_vei2_pn065
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2155">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2157">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn064">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn065">2:065</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn066">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bāḍeyī-putra</b> (‘son of Bāḍeyī’) is mentioned in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Mādhyaṃdina recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 4, 30) as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मौषिकीपुत्र">mauṣikīputra</a>.</b></p></div>

बाण|బాణ|ಬಾಣ|bāṇa|baaNa|_vei_120@2157|_vei2_pn065
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2156">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2158">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn064">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn065">2:065</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn066">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bāṇa</b> denotes ‘arrow’ in the Rigveda (vi. 75, 17) and later (Av. iii. 23, 2; vi. 105, 2, etc.).</p></div>

बाणवन्त्|బాణవన్త్|ಬಾಣವನ್ತ್|bāṇavant|baaNavant|_vei_120@2158|_vei2_pn065
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2157">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2159">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn064">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn065">2:065</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn066">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bāṇavant</b> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iii. 8, 2) denotes an ‘arrow’ like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बाण">bāṇa</a>.</b> Its more normal sense is ‘quiver’ (lit., ‘containing arrows’), which is its sense in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā (xvi. 10) and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (v. 3, 1, 11). </p></div>

बादरायण|బాదరాయణ|ಬಾದರಾಯಣ|bādarāyaṇa|baadaraayaNa|_vei_120@2159|_vei2_pn066
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2158">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2160">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn065">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn066">2:066</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn067">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bādarāyaṇa</b> (‘descendant of Badara’) is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) at the end of the Sāmavidhāna <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202159"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202159">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202159"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202159">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 377. Bādari is found in the Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 3, 18; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 34, n.</li></ol></div>

बाध्योग|బాధ్యోగ|ಬಾಧ್ಯೋಗ|bādhyoga|baadhyoga|_vei_120@2160|_vei2_pn066
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2159">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2161">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn065">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn066">2:066</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn067">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bādhyoga</b> (‘descendant of Badhyoga’) is the patronymic of <b>Jihvāvant,</b> a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="असित">asita</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वार्षगण">vārṣagaṇa</a>,</b> in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Mādhyaṃdina recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 4, 33).</p></div>

बाध्व|బాధ్వ|ಬಾಧ್ವ|bādhva|baadhva|_vei_120@2161|_vei2_pn066
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2160">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2162">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn065">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn066">2:066</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn067">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bādhva</b> is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka (iii. 2, 3). The reading in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka (viii. 3) is <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वात्स्य">vātsya</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202161"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202161">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202161"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202161">↑</a> See Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 249, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

बाभ्रव|బాభ్రవ|ಬಾಭ್ರವ|bābhrava|baabhrava|_vei_120@2162|_vei2_pn066
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2161">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2163">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn065">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn066">2:066</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn067">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bābhrava,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बभ्रु">babhru</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b>Vatsanapāt</b> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202162">1</a></sup> In the legend of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a></b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202162">2</a></sup> the <b>Kāpileyas</b> and the Bābhravas are enumerated as the descendants of <a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a> under his adoptive name of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="देवरात">devarāta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्वामित्र">vaiśvāmitra</a>.</b> A <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a>, or Chant, of <a class="vei_120a" href="बभ्रु">babhru</a> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202162">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202162">↑</a> ii. 5, 22; iv. 5, 28 (Mādhyaṃdina = ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202162">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 17. The <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> version omits the words.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202162">↑</a> xv. 3, 12.</li></ol></div>

बाभ्रव्य|బాభ్రవ్య|ಬಾಭ್ರವ್ಯ|bābhravya|baabhravya|_vei_120@2163|_vei2_pn066
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2162">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2164">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn065">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn066">2:066</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn067">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bābhravya,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बभ्रु">babhru</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b>Girija</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vii. 1), and of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शंख">śaṅkha</a></b> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 41, 1; iv. 17, 1).</p></div>

बार्हत्-सामा|बार्हत्सामा|बार्हत्|सामा|బార్హత్-సామా|బార్హత్సామా|బార్హత్|సామా|ಬಾರ್ಹತ್-ಸಾಮಾ|ಬಾರ್ಹತ್ಸಾಮಾ|ಬಾರ್ಹತ್|ಸಾಮಾ|bārhat-sāmā|bārhatsāmā|bārhat|sāmā|baarhat-saamaa|baarhatsaamaa|baarhat|saamaa|_vei_120@2164|_vei2_pn066
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2163">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2165">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn065">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn066">2:066</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn067">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bārhat-sāmā</b> is an anomalously formed word meaning ‘daughter of Bṛhatsāman’ in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202164"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202164">1</a></sup> where her name occurs in a hymn for easy conception. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202164"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202164">↑</a> v. 25, 9. <i>Cf</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 267.</li></ol></div>

बार्हस्-पत्य|बार्हस्पत्य|बार्हस्|बार्ह|पत्य|బార్హస్-పత్య|బార్హస్పత్య|బార్హస్|బార్హ|పత్య|ಬಾರ್ಹಸ್-ಪತ್ಯ|ಬಾರ್ಹಸ್ಪತ್ಯ|ಬಾರ್ಹಸ್|ಬಾರ್ಹ|ಪತ್ಯ|bārhas-patya|bārhaspatya|bārhas|bārha|patya|baarhas-patya|baarhaspatya|baarhas|baarha|_vei_120@2165|_vei2_pn067
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2164">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2166">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn066">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn067">2:067</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn068">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bārhas-patya,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="बृहस्पति">bṛhaspati</a>,’ is the patronymic of the mythical <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शंयु">śaṃyu</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202165"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202165">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202165"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202165">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 10, 1; v. 2, 6, 4; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 9, 1, 24; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iv. 21, etc.</li></ol></div>

बाल|బాల|ಬಾಲ|bāla|baala|_vei_120@2166|_vei2_pn067
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2165">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2167">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn066">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn067">2:067</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn068">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bāla</b> denotes ‘boy,’ ‘young child,’ in the Upaniṣads.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202166"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202166">1</a></sup> The later definition<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202166"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202166">2</a></sup> makes childhood extend to the sixteenth year.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202166"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202166">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 1, 11; 24, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202166"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202166">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

बालन्दन|బాలన్దన|ಬಾಲನ್ದನ|bālandana|baalandana|_vei_120@2167|_vei2_pn067
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2166">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2168">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn066">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn067">2:067</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn068">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bālandana</b> is a variant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भालन्दन">bhālandana</a>,</b> the patronymic of <b>Vatsaprī.</b></p><p>^1 See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 459, 478.</p></div>

बालाकि|बालाक्या|బాలాకి|బాలాక్యా|ಬಾಲಾಕಿ|ಬಾಲಾಕ್ಯಾ|bālāki|bālākyā|baalaaki|baalaakyaa|_vei_120@2168|_vei2_pn067
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2167">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2169">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn066">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn067">2:067</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn068">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bālāki, Bālākyā.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृप्त">dṛpta</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="बालाकि">bālāki</a></b> and <b>Kāśyapī-bālākyāmāṭharī-<a class="vei_120a" href="पुत्र">putra</a>.</b></p></div>

बालेय|బాలేయ|ಬಾಲೇಯ|bāleya|baaleya|_vei_120@2169|_vei2_pn067
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2168">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2170">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn066">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn067">2:067</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn068">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bāleya</b> is a patronymic (‘descendant of Bali’) of <b>Gandharvāyaṇa</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (xx. 25).</p></div>

बाष्कल|బాష్కల|ಬಾಷ್ಕಲ|bāṣkala|baaShkala|_vei_120@2170|_vei2_pn067
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2169">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2171">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn066">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn067">2:067</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn068">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bāṣkala.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वार्कलि">vārkali</a>.</b></p></div>

बाष्किह|బాష్కిహ|ಬಾಷ್ಕಿಹ|bāṣkiha|baaShkiha|_vei_120@2171|_vei2_pn067
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2170">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2172">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn066">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn067">2:067</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn068">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bāṣkiha,</b> ‘descendant of Baṣkiha,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनस्कर्ण">śunaskarṇa</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202171">1</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202171">2</a></sup> he is a descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शिबि">śibi</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202171">↑</a> xvii. 12, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202171">↑</a> xxi. 17. See Caland, <i>Über das rituelle <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> des <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>,</i> 28.</li></ol></div>

बाहीक|బాహీక|ಬಾಹೀಕ|bāhīka|baahiika|baaheeka|_vei_120@2172|_vei2_pn067
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2171">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2173">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn066">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn067">2:067</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn068">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bāhīka</b> is applied in the Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202172"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202172">1</a></sup> to the people of the west, of the Panjab,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202172"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202172">2</a></sup> as opposed to the <b>Prācyas</b> or easterns. They are said to have called <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> by the name of <a class="vei_120a" href="भव">bhava</a>. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202172"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202172">↑</a> i. 7, 3, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202172"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202172">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Mahābhārata, viii. 2030 <i>et seq.,</i> where the Bāhīkas are defined as the people of the Panjab and the Indus. This coincides exactly with what seems to be meant by the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, which regards as the middle the land to the east of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>.</b></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 189; 2, 37; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 201, n 2.</li></ol></div>

बाहु|బాహు|ಬಾಹು|bāhu|baahu|_vei_120@2173|_vei2_pn068
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2172">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2174">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn067">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn068">2:068</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn069">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bāhu,</b> ‘arm,’ as a measure of length, is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (vi. 2, 11, 1) and often in the Sūtras.</p></div>

बाहु-वृक्त|बाहुवृक्त|बाहु|वृक्त|బాహు-వృక్త|బాహువృక్త|బాహు|వృక్త|ಬಾಹು-ವೃಕ್ತ|ಬಾಹುವೃಕ್ತ|ಬಾಹು|ವೃಕ್ತ|bāhu-vṛkta|bāhuvṛkta|bāhu|vṛkta|baahu-vRkta|baahuvRkta|baahu|vRkta|baahu-vRikta|baahuvRikta|vRikta|baahu-vRukta|baahuvRukta|vRukta|baahu-vRRikta|baahuvRRikta|vRRikta|_vei_120@2174|_vei2_pn068
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2173">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2175">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn067">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn068">2:068</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn069">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bāhu-vṛkta</b> is the name of a man, apparently a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>, who overcame foes in battle, according to the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202174">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202174">↑</a> v. 44, 12. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 138, 139. The Anukramaṇī (Index) attributes to him two hymns of the Rigveda, v. 71 and 72.</li></ol></div>

बिदल-कारी|बिदल-कारि|बिदलकारी|बिदलकारि|बिदल|कारी|कारि|బిదల-కారీ|బిదల-కారి|బిదలకారీ|బిదలకారి|బిదల|కారీ|కారి|ಬಿದಲ-ಕಾರೀ|ಬಿದಲ-ಕಾರಿ|ಬಿದಲಕಾರೀ|ಬಿದಲಕಾರಿ|ಬಿದಲ|ಕಾರೀ|ಕಾರಿ|bidala-kārī|bidala-kāri|bidalakārī|bidalakāri|bidala|kārī|kāri|bidala-kaarii|bidala-kaari|bidalakaarii|bidalakaari|kaarii|kaari|bidala-kaaree|bidalakaaree|kaaree|_vei_120@2175|_vei2_pn068
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2174">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2176">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn067">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn068">2:068</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn069">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bidala-kārī,</b> ‘female splitter of bamboos,’ is the name of one of the victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202175"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202175">1</a></sup> Eggeling<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202175"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202175">2</a></sup> renders the word as ‘basket-maker.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202175"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202175">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 8; <i>bidalakāra,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 5, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202175"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202175">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 414.</li></ol></div>

बिम्ब|बिंब|బిమ్బ|బింబ|ಬಿಮ್ಬ|ಬಿಂಬ|bimba|_vei_120@2176|_vei2_pn068
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2175">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2177">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn067">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn068">2:068</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn069">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bimba</b> appears in one passage of the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 5, 6) to denote the plant <i>Momordica monadelpha.</i></p></div>

बिल्व|బిల్వ|ಬಿಲ್ವ|bilva|_vei_120@2177|_vei2_pn068
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2176">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2178">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn067">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn068">2:068</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn069">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bilva</b> is the name of the wood-apple tree (<i>Aigle marmelos</i>). It is mentioned in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202177"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202177">1</a></sup> and in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202177"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202177">2</a></sup> where a reference to its valuable fruit may be intended. According to the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202177"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202177">3</a></sup> the sacrificial post was made of <a class="vei_120a" href="बिल्व">bilva</a> wood in some cases. The <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202177"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202177">4</a></sup> contains a hymn in praise of the virtues of an amulet of <a class="vei_120a" href="बिल्व">bilva</a> (<i>irā-<a class="vei_120a" href="मणि">maṇi</a> bailva</i>).<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202177"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202177">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202177"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202177">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 4, 8, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 9, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202177"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202177">↑</a> xx. 136, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202177"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202177">↑</a> ii. 1, 8, 1. 2. <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 3, 20 (<i>paridhayaḥ</i>); <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202177"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202177">↑</a> xii. 20 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202177"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202177">↑</a> At the present day the tree is called Bel, and its leaves are used in the ritual of Siva worship.</li></ol></div>

बिस|బిస|ಬಿಸ|bisa|_vei_120@2178|_vei2_pn068
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2177">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2179">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn067">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn068">2:068</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn069">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bisa</b> denotes the radical fibres of the lotus, which seem to have been eaten as a delicacy as early as the times of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202178"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202178">1</a></sup> It is mentioned also in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202178"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202178">2</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202178"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202178">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202178"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202178">↑</a> iv. 34, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202178"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202178">↑</a> v. 30.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202178"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202178">↑</a> iii. 2, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xi. 4. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 70.</li></ol></div>

बीज|బీజ|ಬೀಜ|bīja|biija|beeja|_vei_120@2179|_vei2_pn069
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2178">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2180">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn068">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn069">2:069</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn070">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bīja</b> denotes ‘seed,’ the operation of sowing seed (<i>vap</i>) being several times referred to in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202179"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202179">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202179"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202179">2</a></sup> In a metaphorical sense the term is used in the Upaniṣads of the classes of beings according to origin, of which the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202179"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202179">3</a></sup> enumerates three, the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202179"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202179">4</a></sup> four. The former list includes <i>aṇḍa-ja,</i> ‘egg-born,’ <i><a class="vei_120a" href="जीव-ज">jīva-ja</a>,</i> ‘born alive,’ and <i>udbhij-ja,</i> ‘produced from sprouts,’ ‘germinating,’ while the latter adds <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्वेद-ज">sveda-ja</a>,</i> ‘sweat-born’--that is, ‘generated by hot moisture,’ an expression which is glossed to comprise flies, worms, etc. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृषि">kṛṣi</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202179"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202179">↑</a> x. 94, 13; 101, 3. <i>Cf.</i> metaphorically, x. 85, 37. In v. 53, 13, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="धान्य">dhānya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="बीज">bīja</a></i> means the ‘seed which produces corn.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202179"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202179">↑</a> Av. x. 6, 33; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 2, 2, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202179"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202179">↑</a> vi. 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202179"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202179">↑</a> iii. 3. See Keith. <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 235.</li></ol></div>

बुडिल आश्वतराश्वि|आश्वतर आश्वि|బుడిల ఆశ్వతరాశ్వి|ఆశ్వతర ఆశ్వి|ಬುಡಿಲ ಆಶ್ವತರಾಶ್ವಿ|ಆಶ್ವತರ ಆಶ್ವಿ|buḍila āśvatarāśvi|āśvatara āśvi|buDila aashvataraashvi|aashvatara aashvi|_vei_120@2180|_vei2_pn069
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2179">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2181">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn068">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn069">2:069</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn070">↷</a><hr><p><b>Buḍila Āśvatarāśvi</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आश्वतर आश्वि">āśvatara āśvi</a></b> is mentioned several times in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> literature as a teacher. According to the Chāndogya<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202180">1</a></sup> and the Bṛhadāraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202180">2</a></sup> Upaniṣads, he was a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a></b> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>,</b> and, according to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202180">3</a></sup> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वपति">aśvapati</a>,</b> the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="केकय">kekaya</a></b> king. He is also mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.^4</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202180">↑</a> v. 11, 1; 16, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202180">↑</a> v. 15, 11 (Mādhyaṃdina = v. 14, 8 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202180">↑</a> x. 6, 1, 1. <i>Cf.</i> iv. 6, 1, 9. vi. 30.</li></ol></div>

बुध सौमायन|బుధ సౌమాయన|ಬುಧ ಸೌಮಾಯನ|budha saumāyana|budha saumaayana|_vei_120@2181|_vei2_pn069
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2180">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2182">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn068">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn069">2:069</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn070">↷</a><hr><p><b>Budha Saumāyana,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>,</b>’ is the name of a teacher mentioned in a verse in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202181"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202181">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202181"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202181">↑</a> xxiv. 18, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 55, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

बुन्द|బున్ద|ಬುನ್ದ|bunda|_vei_120@2182|_vei2_pn069
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2181">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2183">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn068">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn069">2:069</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn070">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bunda</b> means ‘arrow’ in a few passages of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202182"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202182">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202182"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202182">↑</a> viii. 45, 4; 77, 6. 11. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 32.</li></ol></div>

बृबु|బృబు|ಬೃಬು|bṛbu|bRbu|bRibu|bRubu|bRRibu|_vei_120@2183|_vei2_pn069
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2182">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2184">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn068">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn069">2:069</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn070">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bṛbu</b> is mentioned in a hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202183">1</a></sup> where he is described as a most generous giver (<i>sahasra-dātama</i>), and as at the head of the <b>Paṇis.</b> According to the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202183">2</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a></b> received gifts from <a class="vei_120a" href="बृबु">bṛbu</a> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तक्षन्">takṣan</a></b> and  <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रस्तोक">prastoka</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सार्ञ्जय">sārñjaya</a>,</b> a fact alluded to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Śāstra,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202183">3</a></sup> where <i><a class="vei_120a" href="तक्षन्">takṣan</a></i> is treated as a descriptive attribute, ‘a carpenter.’ Apparently <a class="vei_120a" href="बृबु">bṛbu</a> was a <a class="vei_120a" href="पणि">paṇi</a>, though the words of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1202183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1202183">1b</a></sup> might be taken to mean that he was one who had overthrown them entirely. If so, <a class="vei_120a" href="पणि">paṇi</a> must here certainly mean a merchant in a good sense, <a class="vei_120a" href="बृबु">bṛbu</a> being then a merchant prince.<sup id="ref_120_4b_vei_1202183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4b_vei_1202183">4b</a></sup> According to Weber,<sup id="ref_120_5b_vei_1202183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5b_vei_1202183">5b</a></sup> the name suggests connexion with Babylon, but this conjecture must be regarded as quite improbable. Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_6b_vei_1202183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6b_vei_1202183">6b</a></sup> sensibly expresses no opinion as to <a class="vei_120a" href="बृबु">bṛbu</a>, while Brunnhofer's<sup id="ref_120_7b_vei_1202183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7b_vei_1202183">7b</a></sup> attempt to recognize a people named , and to connect them with the Vedic word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="तक्षन्">takṣan</a>,</i> is valueless, especially considering the fact that <a class="vei_120a" href="तक्षन्">takṣan</a> is not found as an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="बृबु">bṛbu</a> in the Rigveda.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202183">↑</a> vi. 45, 31. 33.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202183">↑</a> xvi. 11, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202183">↑</a> x. 107.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1202183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1202183">↑</a> vi. 45, 31. 33.</li><li id="fx_120_4b_vei_1202183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4b_vei_1202183">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 1, 606, n.</li><li id="fx_120_5b_vei_1202183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5b_vei_1202183">↑</a> <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 28 <i>et seq.; Proceedings of the Berlin Academy,</i> 1898, 563, n. 1; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 198. The Bāveru Jātaka, on which stress is laid in connexion with the Indian knowledge of Babylon, being of quite unknown date, has no cogency as evidence for any early period. <i>Cf.</i> Bühler, <i>Indische Palœographie,</i> 17-19; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 79 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature</i> 3; Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India,</i> 201 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6b_vei_1202183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6b_vei_1202183">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 93, 104, 107.</li><li id="fx_120_7b_vei_1202183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7b_vei_1202183">↑</a> <i>Iran und Turan,</i> 127.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 275; Bṛhaddevatā, v. 108, 109, with Macdonell's notes; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 316.</li></ol></div>

बृसय|బృసయ|ಬೃಸಯ|bṛsaya|bRsaya|bRisaya|bRusaya|bRRisaya|_vei_120@2184|_vei2_pn070
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2183">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2185">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn069">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn070">2:070</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn071">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bṛsaya</b> is mentioned twice in the Rigveda, being in the first passage<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202184"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202184">1</a></sup> connected with the <b>Paṇis,</b> and in the second<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202184"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202184">2</a></sup> with the <b>Pārāvatas</b> and the Paṇis. According to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, the word is the name of a demon,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202184"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202184">3</a></sup> but is in the second passage<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202184"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202184">2</a></sup> used as an appellative, perhaps meaning ‘sorcerer.’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202184"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202184">4</a></sup> Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202184"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202184">5</a></sup> thinks that a people is meant locating them in Arachosia or Drangiana with the Pārāvatas and the Paṇis, and comparing , satrap of Arachosia and Drangiana in the time of Darius.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202184"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202184">6</a></sup> But this theory is not probable. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202184"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202184">↑</a> i. 93, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202184"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202184">↑</a> vi. 61, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202184"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202184">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Sāyaṇa on Rv., <i>loc., <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202184"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202184">↑</a> vi. 61, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202184"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202184">↑</a> Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> following Grassmann.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202184"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202184">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 97-104.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202184"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202184">↑</a> Arrian, <i>Indica,</i> viii. 4; xxi. 1; xxv. 8.</li></ol></div>

बृसी|बृसि|బృసీ|బృసి|ಬೃಸೀ|ಬೃಸಿ|bṛsī|bṛsi|bRsii|bRsi|bRsee|bRisii|bRisi|bRusii|bRusi|bRRisii|bRRisi|_vei_120@2185|_vei2_pn071
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2184">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2186">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn070">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn071">2:071</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn072">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bṛsī,</b> denoting a ‘cushion’ of grass, is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202185"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202185">1</a></sup> and the Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202185"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202185">2</a></sup> The incorrect forms Vṛśī and Vṛṣī also occur occasionally.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202185"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202185">↑</a> i. 2, 4; v. 1, 3, with Keith's note; 3, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202185"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202185">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvii. 4, 7; 6, 6; Kātyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiii. 3, 1.</li></ol></div>

बृहच्-छन्दस्|बृहच्-छन्द|बृहच्छन्दस्|बृहच्छन्द|बृहच्|छन्दस्|छन्द|బృహచ్-ఛన్దస్|బృహచ్-ఛన్ద|బృహచ్ఛన్దస్|బృహచ్ఛన్ద|బృహచ్|ఛన్దస్|ఛన్ద|ಬೃಹಚ್-ಛನ್ದಸ್|ಬೃಹಚ್-ಛನ್ದ|ಬೃಹಚ್ಛನ್ದಸ್|ಬೃಹಚ್ಛನ್ದ|ಬೃಹಚ್|ಛನ್ದಸ್|ಛನ್ದ|bṛhac-chandas|bṛhac-chanda|bṛhacchandas|bṛhacchanda|bṛhac|chandas|chanda|bRhach-Chandas|bRhach-Chanda|bRhachChandas|bRhachChanda|bRhach|Chandas|Chanda|bRihach-Chandas|bRihach-Chanda|bRihachChandas|bRihachChanda|bRihach|bRuhach-Chandas|bRuhach-Chanda|bRuhachChandas|bRuhachChanda|bRuhach|bRRihach-Chandas|bRRihach-Chanda|bRRihachChandas|bRRihachChanda|bRRihach|_vei_120@2186|_vei2_pn071
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2185">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2187">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn070">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn071">2:071</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn072">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bṛhac-chandas</b> is found in one passage of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202186"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202186">1</a></sup> as an epithet of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाला">śālā</a>,</b> ‘house.’ It is apparently<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202186"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202186">2</a></sup> an error for <i><a class="vei_120a" href="बृहद्">bṛhad</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="छदिस्">chadis</a>,</i> ‘broad-roofed,’ which in any case is the sense.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202186"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202186">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202186"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202186">↑</a> iii. 12, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202186"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202186">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 105.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202186"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202186">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 345.</li></ol></div>

बृहत्-सामन्|बृहत्सामन्|बृहत्|सामन्|బృహత్-సామన్|బృహత్సామన్|బృహత్|సామన్|ಬೃಹತ್-ಸಾಮನ್|ಬೃಹತ್ಸಾಮನ್|ಬೃಹತ್|ಸಾಮನ್|bṛhat-sāman|bṛhatsāman|bṛhat|sāman|bRhat-saaman|bRhatsaaman|bRhat|saaman|bRihat-saaman|bRihatsaaman|bRihat|bRuhat-saaman|bRuhatsaaman|bRuhat|bRRihat-saaman|bRRihatsaaman|bRRihat|_vei_120@2187|_vei2_pn071
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2186">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2188">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn070">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn071">2:071</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn072">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bṛhat-sāman</b> is mentioned in the Atharvaveda (v. 19, 2) as an <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आंगिरस">āṅgirasa</a></b> who was oppressed by Kṣatriyas. The latter are said to have been ruined in consequence. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सृञ्जय">sṛñjaya</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बार्हत्सामा">bārhatsāmā</a>.</b></p></div>

बृहद्-उक्थ|बृहदुक्थ|बृहद्|उक्थ|బృహద్-ఉక్థ|బృహదుక్థ|బృహద్|ఉక్థ|ಬೃಹದ್-ಉಕ್ಥ|ಬೃಹದುಕ್ಥ|ಬೃಹದ್|ಉಕ್ಥ|bṛhad-uktha|bṛhaduktha|bṛhad|uktha|bRhad-uktha|bRhaduktha|bRhad|bRihad-uktha|bRihaduktha|bRihad|bRuhad-uktha|bRuhaduktha|bRuhad|bRRihad-uktha|bRRihaduktha|bRRihad|_vei_120@2188|_vei2_pn071
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2187">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2189">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn070">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn071">2:071</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn072">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bṛhad-uktha</b> is mentioned in an obscure hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202188">1</a></sup> as a priest; in two hymns of the tenth Maṇḍala<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202188">2</a></sup> he is definitely a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>. He is also mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202188">3</a></sup> as having consecrated <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दुर्मुख">durmukha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पाञ्चाल">pāñcāla</a>,</b> and is called <b>Vāmadeva's</b> son in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202188">4</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202188">5</a></sup> he appears as <b>Vāmneya,</b> ‘descendant of Vāmnī.’ Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202188">6</a></sup> suggestion that he may have been there thought of as Vāmadevya also is quite probable.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202188">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202188">↑</a> v. 19, 3, where Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> treats it as adjectival. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft.</i> 42, 214; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 126.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202188">↑</a> x. 54, 6; 56, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202188">↑</a> viii. 23.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202188">↑</a> xiii. 2, 2, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202188">↑</a> xiv. 9, 37, 38.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202188">↑</a> <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 55, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202188">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 9, 27, is parallel with xiv. 9, 38.</li></ol></div>

बृहद्-गिरि|बृहद्गिरि|बृहद्|गिरि|బృహద్-గిరి|బృహద్గిరి|బృహద్|గిరి|ಬೃಹದ್-ಗಿರಿ|ಬೃಹದ್ಗಿರಿ|ಬೃಹದ್|ಗಿರಿ|bṛhad-giri|bṛhadgiri|bṛhad|giri|bRhad-giri|bRhadgiri|bRhad|bRihad-giri|bRihadgiri|bRihad|bRuhad-giri|bRuhadgiri|bRuhad|bRRihad-giri|bRRihadgiri|bRRihad|_vei_120@2189|_vei2_pn071
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2188">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2190">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn070">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn071">2:071</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn072">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bṛhad-giri</b> is said in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 1, 4) to have been one of the three <b>Yatis</b> who survived the slaughter of them by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>. A <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a>, or Chant, of his is mentioned in the same <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 4, 15-17). </p></div>

बृहद्-दिव|बृहद्दिव|बृहद्|दिव|బృహద్-దివ|బృహద్దివ|బృహద్|దివ|ಬೃಹದ್-ದಿವ|ಬೃಹದ್ದಿವ|ಬೃಹದ್|ದಿವ|bṛhad-diva|bṛhaddiva|bṛhad|diva|bRhad-diva|bRhaddiva|bRhad|bRihad-diva|bRihaddiva|bRihad|bRuhad-diva|bRuhaddiva|bRuhad|bRRihad-diva|bRRihaddiva|bRRihad|_vei_120@2190|_vei2_pn072
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2189">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2191">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn071">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn072">2:072</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn073">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bṛhad-diva</b> appears in a hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202190"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202190">1</a></sup> as its author, calling himself an <a class="vei_120a" href="अथर्वन्">atharvan</a>. He is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202190"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202190">2</a></sup> and is named in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202190"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202190">3</a></sup> as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुम्नयु">sumnayu</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202190"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202190">↑</a> x. 120, 8. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202190"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202190">↑</a> iv. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202190"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202190">↑</a> xv. 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 133; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 141.</li></ol></div>

बृहद्-रथ|बृहद्रथ|बृहद्|रथ|బృహద్-రథ|బృహద్రథ|బృహద్|రథ|ಬೃಹದ್-ರಥ|ಬೃಹದ್ರಥ|ಬೃಹದ್|ರಥ|bṛhad-ratha|bṛhadratha|bṛhad|ratha|bRhad-ratha|bRhadratha|bRhad|bRihad-ratha|bRihadratha|bRihad|bRuhad-ratha|bRuhadratha|bRuhad|bRRihad-ratha|bRRihadratha|bRRihad|_vei_120@2191|_vei2_pn072
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2190">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2192">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn071">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn072">2:072</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn073">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bṛhad-ratha</b> is mentioned twice in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202191"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202191">1</a></sup> in both cases beside <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नववास्त्व">navavāstva</a>.</b> The name may thus be an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="नववास्त्व">navavāstva</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202191"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202191">↑</a> i. 36, 18; x. 49, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 147, 148.</li></ol></div>

बृहद्-वसु|बृहद्वसु|बृहद्|वसु|బృహద్-వసు|బృహద్వసు|బృహద్|వసు|ಬೃಹದ್-ವಸು|ಬೃಹದ್ವಸು|ಬೃಹದ್|ವಸು|bṛhad-vasu|bṛhadvasu|bṛhad|vasu|bRhad-vasu|bRhadvasu|bRhad|bRihad-vasu|bRihadvasu|bRihad|bRuhad-vasu|bRuhadvasu|bRuhad|bRRihad-vasu|bRRihadvasu|bRRihad|_vei_120@2192|_vei2_pn072
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2191">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2193">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn071">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn072">2:072</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn073">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bṛhad-vasu</b> is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202192"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202192">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202192"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202192">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 374.</li></ol></div>

बृहस्पति|బృహస్పతి|ಬೃಹಸ್ಪತಿ|bṛhaspati|bRhaspati|bRihaspati|bRuhaspati|bRRihaspati|_vei_120@2193|_vei2_pn072
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2192">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2194">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn071">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn072">2:072</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn073">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bṛhaspati,</b> ‘lord of prayer,’ is the name of a god in the Vedic texts. The view of Thibaut,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202193">1</a></sup> that the name designates the planet Jupiter, is certainly not supported by good evidence. Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202193">2</a></sup> seems clearly right in rejecting it.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202193">↑</a> <i>Astronomie, Astrologie und Mathematik.</i> 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202193">↑</a> <i>Nachrichten der ko7niglichen Gesellschaft der Wessenschaften zu Go7ttingen,</i> 1909, 568, n. 3; Whitney, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, xciv, correcting Tilak, <i>Orion,</i> 101. See also Fleet, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1911, 514-518; Keith, <i>ibid.,</i> 794-800.</li></ol></div>

बृहस्पति-गुप्त शायस्थि|बृहस्पतिगुप्त शायस्थि|बृहस्पति|गुप्त शायस्थि|బృహస్పతి-గుప్త శాయస్థి|బృహస్పతిగుప్త శాయస్థి|బృహస్పతి|గుప్త శాయస్థి|ಬೃಹಸ್ಪತಿ-ಗುಪ್ತ ಶಾಯಸ್ಥಿ|ಬೃಹಸ್ಪತಿಗುಪ್ತ ಶಾಯಸ್ಥಿ|ಬೃಹಸ್ಪತಿ|ಗುಪ್ತ ಶಾಯಸ್ಥಿ|bṛhaspati-gupta śāyasthi|bṛhaspatigupta śāyasthi|bṛhaspati|gupta śāyasthi|bRhaspati-gupta shaayasthi|bRhaspatigupta shaayasthi|bRhaspati|gupta shaayasthi|bRihaspati-gupta shaayasthi|bRihaspatigupta shaayasthi|bRihaspati|bRuhaspati-gupta shaayasthi|bRuhaspatigupta shaayasthi|bRuhaspati|bRRihaspati-gupta shaayasthi|bRRihaspatigupta shaayasthi|bRRihaspati|_vei_120@2194|_vei2_pn072
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2193">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2195">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn071">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn072">2:072</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn073">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bṛhaspati-<a class="vei_120a" href="गुप्त">gupta</a> Śāyasthi</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202194"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202194">1</a></sup> as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भवत्रात शायस्थि">bhavatrāta śāyasthi</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202194"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202194">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372.</li></ol></div>

बृहस्पति-सव|बृहस्पतिसव|बृहस्पति|सव|బృహస్పతి-సవ|బృహస్పతిసవ|బృహస్పతి|సవ|ಬೃಹಸ್ಪತಿ-ಸವ|ಬೃಹಸ್ಪತಿಸವ|ಬೃಹಸ್ಪತಿ|ಸವ|bṛhaspati-sava|bṛhaspatisava|bṛhaspati|sava|bRhaspati-sava|bRhaspatisava|bRhaspati|bRihaspati-sava|bRihaspatisava|bRihaspati|bRuhaspati-sava|bRuhaspatisava|bRuhaspati|bRRihaspati-sava|bRRihaspatisava|bRRihaspati|_vei_120@2195|_vei2_pn072
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2194">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2196">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn071">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn072">2:072</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn073">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bṛhaspati-sava</b> is the name of a sacrifice by which, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202195">1</a></sup> the priest who desired to become a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a></b> obtained that office. According to the Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202195">2</a></sup> it was the sacrifice to be performed by a priest after the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वाजपेय">vājapeya</a>,</b> while the king performed the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>.</b> In  the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202195">3</a></sup> on the other hand, the <a class="vei_120a" href="बृहस्पतिसव">bṛhaspatisava</a> is identified with the <a class="vei_120a" href="वाजपेय">vājapeya</a>; but such identity is clearly not primitive.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202195">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202195">↑</a> ii. 7, 1, 2. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvii. 11, 4; xxv. 1, 1, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202195">↑</a> ix. 9. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202195">↑</a> v. 2, 1, 19.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202195">↑</a> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, xxiv, xxv; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 107, 108.</li></ol></div>

बेकनाट|బేకనాట|ಬೇಕನಾಟ|bekanāṭa|bekanaaTa|_vei_120@2196|_vei2_pn073
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2195">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2197">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn072">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn073">2:073</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn074">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bekanāṭa</b> occurs only once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202196">1</a></sup> when <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> is said to overcome all the Bekanāṭas and the Paṇis. The natural sense, therefore, seems to be ‘usurer,’ the explanation given by <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202196">2</a></sup> The word has a foreign appearance, but its provenance can hardly be determined: it might just as well be aboriginal as Babylonian.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202196">3</a></sup> Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202196">4</a></sup> thinks Brunnhofer is right in identifying <a class="vei_120a" href="बेकनाट">bekanāṭa</a> with Bikanir.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202196">↑</a> viii. 16, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202196">↑</a> Nirūkta, vi. 26.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202196">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 44.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202196">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 268, n. 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 259.</li></ol></div>

बेकुरा|బేకురా|ಬೇಕುರಾ|bekurā|bekuraa|_vei_120@2197|_vei2_pn073
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2196">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2198">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn072">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn073">2:073</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn074">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bekurā</b> occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202197">1</a></sup> where it may mean ‘voice’ or ‘sound,’ the sense assigned to the word in the Naighaṇṭuka.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202197">2</a></sup> It is, however, possibly, like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बकुर">bakura</a>,</b> the name of a musical instrument. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202197">3</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202197">4</a></sup> Saṃhitās the words Bekuri and Vekuri occur as epithets of Apsarases, or celestial nymphs, meaning, perhaps, ‘melodious’; in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202197">5</a></sup> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202197">6</a></sup> the variations Bhakuri and <a class="vei_120a" href="भाकुरि">bhākuri</a> are found.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202197">↑</a> i. 3, 1; vi. 7, 6; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 82.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202197">↑</a> i. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202197">↑</a> iii. 4, 7, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202197">↑</a> xviii. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202197">↑</a> xviii. 42.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202197">↑</a> ix. 4, 1, 9.</li></ol></div>

बैज-वाप|बैजवाप|बैज|वाप|బైజ-వాప|బైజవాప|బైజ|వాప|ಬೈಜ-ವಾಪ|ಬೈಜವಾಪ|ಬೈಜ|ವಾಪ|baija-vāpa|baijavāpa|baija|vāpa|baija-vaapa|baijavaapa|vaapa|_vei_120@2198|_vei2_pn073
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2197">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2199">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn072">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn073">2:073</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn074">↷</a><hr><p><b>Baija-vāpa,</b> ‘descendant of Bījavāpa,’ is the name of a teacher in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) in the Mādhyaṃdina recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (ii. 5, 20; iv. 5, 26).</p></div>

बैज-वापायन|बैजवापायन|बैज|वापायन|బైజ-వాపాయన|బైజవాపాయన|బైజ|వాపాయన|ಬೈಜ-ವಾಪಾಯನ|ಬೈಜವಾಪಾಯನ|ಬೈಜ|ವಾಪಾಯನ|baija-vāpāyana|baijavāpāyana|baija|vāpāyana|baija-vaapaayana|baijavaapaayana|vaapaayana|_vei_120@2199|_vei2_pn073
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2198">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2200">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn072">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn073">2:073</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn074">↷</a><hr><p><b>Baija-vāpāyana,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बैजवाप">baijavāpa</a>,</b>’ is the name of a teacher in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) in the Mādhyaṃdina recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (ii. 5, 20; iv. 5, 26). The name is also spelt Vaijavāpāyana. </p></div>

बैज-वापि|बैजवापि|बैज|वापि|బైజ-వాపి|బైజవాపి|బైజ|వాపి|ಬೈಜ-ವಾಪಿ|ಬೈಜವಾಪಿ|ಬೈಜ|ವಾಪಿ|baija-vāpi|baijavāpi|baija|vāpi|baija-vaapi|baijavaapi|vaapi|_vei_120@2200|_vei2_pn074
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2199">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2201">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn073">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn074">2:074</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn077">↷</a><hr><p><b>Baija-vāpi,</b> ‘descendant of Bījavāpa, or Bījavāpin,’ is the name of a teacher in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā (i. 4, 7).</p></div>

बैन्द|బైన్ద|ಬೈನ್ದ|bainda|_vei_120@2201|_vei2_pn074
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2200">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2202">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn073">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn074">2:074</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn077">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bainda</b> is the name of one of the victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202201"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202201">1</a></sup> According to the commentator Mahīdhara, the word denotes a <a class="vei_120a" href="निषाद">niṣāda</a>, but according to Sāyaṇa a catcher of fish. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मृगयु">mṛgayu</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202201"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202201">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 12, 1.</li></ol></div>

बोध|బోధ|ಬೋಧ|bodha|_vei_120@2202|_vei2_pn074
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2201">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2203">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn073">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn074">2:074</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn077">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bodha</b> is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> Pāṭha.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202202">1</a></sup> He is mentioned with <b>Pratibodha</b> in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202202">2</a></sup> but Whitney<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202202">3</a></sup> thinks that in the second passage, at least, the word is an ordinary noun meaning ‘the wakeful one.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202202">↑</a> ii. 16, 14. <i>Cf.</i> Winternitz, <i>Mantrapāṭha,</i> xiv.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202202">↑</a> v. 30, 10; viii. 1, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202202">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 474.</li></ol></div>

बौधायन|బౌధాయన|ಬೌಧಾಯನ|baudhāyana|baudhaayana|_vei_120@2203|_vei2_pn074
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2202">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2204">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn073">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn074">2:074</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn077">↷</a><hr><p><b>Baudhāyana,</b> ‘descendant of <b>Budha</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बोध">bodha</a>,</b>’ is the name of a teacher who is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202203">1</a></sup> and under whose name are current a <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> described<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202203">2</a></sup> and in part edited by Caland,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202203">3</a></sup> and a <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> which has been edited<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202203">4</a></sup> and translated,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202203">5</a></sup> while the <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> is still unedited.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202203">↑</a> iv. 11, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202203">↑</a> <i>Über das rituelle <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> des <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>,</i> 1903.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202203">↑</a> <i>Bibliotheca Indica,</i> 1904, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202203">↑</a> By Hultzsch, Leipzig, 1884.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202203">↑</a> Bühler, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 14. See his Introduction, xxix <i>et seq.,</i> where, however, he tends to overestimate considerably the age of <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>.</li></ol></div>

बौधी-पुत्र|बौधीपुत्र|बौधी|बौधि|पुत्र|బౌధీ-పుత్ర|బౌధీపుత్ర|బౌధీ|బౌధి|పుత్ర|ಬೌಧೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಬೌಧೀಪುತ್ರ|ಬೌಧೀ|ಬೌಧಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|baudhī-putra|baudhīputra|baudhī|baudhi|putra|baudhii-putra|baudhiiputra|baudhii|baudhee-putra|baudheeputra|baudhee|_vei_120@2204|_vei2_pn074
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2203">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2205">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn073">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn074">2:074</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn077">↷</a><hr><p><b>Baudhī-putra,</b> ‘son of a female descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बोध">bodha</a>,</b>’ is the name of a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शालंकायनीपुत्र">śālaṅkāyanīputra</a></b> in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Mādhyaṃdina recension of the Bṛhadāranyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 4, 31).</p></div>

ब्रह्म-चर्य|ब्रह्मचर्य|ब्रह्म|चर्य|బ్రహ్మ-చర్య|బ్రహ్మచర్య|బ్రహ్మ|చర్య|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮ-ಚರ್ಯ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮಚರ್ಯ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮ|ಚರ್ಯ|brahma-carya|brahmacarya|brahma|carya|brahma-charya|brahmacharya|charya|_vei_120@2205|_vei2_pn074
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2204">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2206">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn073">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn074">2:074</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn077">↷</a><hr><p><b>Brahma-carya</b> denotes the condition of life of the <b>Brahmacārin</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202205">1</a></sup> or religious student. The technical sense is first found in  the last Maṇḍala of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202205">2</a></sup> The practice of studentship doubtless developed, and was more strictly regulated by custom as time went on, but it is regularly assumed and discussed in the later Vedic literature, being obviously a necessary part of Vedic society</p><p>The Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202205">3</a></sup> has in honour of the Brahmacārin a hymn which already gives all the characteristic features of religious studentship. The youth is initiated (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="उप">upa</a>-nī</i>) by the teacher<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202205">4</a></sup> into a new life; he wears an antelope skin, and lets his hair grow long;<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202205">5</a></sup> he collects fuel,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202205">6</a></sup> and begs,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202205">7</a></sup> learns, and practises penance. All these characteristics appear in the later literature. The student lives in the house of his teacher (<i>ācārya-<a class="vei_120a" href="कुल">kula</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वासिन्">vāsin</a>;</i><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202205">8</a></sup> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अन्ते-वासिन्">ante-vāsin</a></i>);<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202205">9</a></sup> he begs,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202205">10</a></sup> looks after the sacrificial fires,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202205">11</a></sup> and tends the house.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202205">12</a></sup> His term of studentship might be long extended: it was normally fixed at twelve years,<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202205">13</a></sup> but much longer periods, such as thirty-two years, are mentioned.<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1202205">14</a></sup> The  age at which studentship began varied.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1202205">15</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्वेतकेतु">śvetaketu</a></b> commenced at twelve and studied for twelve years.<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1202205">16</a></sup></p><p>It is assumed in the <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> Sūtras that the three Āryan castes were all required to pass through a period of studentship. But that this is much more than priestly schematism is uncertain. No doubt individuals of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a></b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a></b> caste might go through part of the period of studentship, just as Burmese boys of all classes now pass some time in a monastery as students. This is borne out by the reference in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1202205">17</a></sup> to the king guarding his country by <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मचर्य">brahmacarya</a>--though that is susceptible of a different interpretation--and more clearly by the reference in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1202205">18</a></sup> to a rite intended to benefit one who, although not a Brahmin, had studied (<i>vidyām anūcya</i>), but had not gained renown, and by references in the Upaniṣads to kings who like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a></b> studied the Vedas and the Upaniṣads.<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1202205">19</a></sup> Normally, however, the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> studied the art of war.<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1202205">20</a></sup></p><p>One of the duties of the Brahmacārin was chastity. But reference is in several places<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1202205">21</a></sup> made to the possibility of misconduct between a student and the wife of his preceptor, nor is any very severe penance imposed in early times--later it is different--for such a sin. In certain cases the ritual required a breach of chastity, no doubt as a magic spell to secure fertility.<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1202205">22</a></sup></p><p>Even an old man might on occasion become a pupil, as the story of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आरुणि">āruṇi</a></b> shows.<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1202205">23</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202205">↑</a> Rv. x. 109, 5; Av. vi. 108, 2; 133, 3; xi. 5, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 3, 3, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202205">↑</a> Rv., <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202205">↑</a> xi. 5. <i>Cf.</i> Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 1-8, which contains an independent account of the Brahmacārin (Bloomfield, <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 110); Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 3, 3, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 3, 10, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202205">↑</a> Av. xi. 5, 3. It is used in the ritual of the Upanayana, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, lv. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202205">↑</a> Av. xi. 5, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202205">↑</a> Av. xi. 5, 4, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202205">↑</a> Av. xi. 5, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202205">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 23, 2. So regularly <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्म">brahma</a>-caryeṇa vas,</i> Av. vii. 109, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 14, etc.; or <i>car,</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 3, 3, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 7, 6, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202205">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> iii. 11, 5; iv. 10, 1; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 3, 15 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 3, 7 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 3, 3; 11. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202205">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 3, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Av. vi. 133, 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 3, 3, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202205">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 10, 2 <i>et seq.;</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>. xi. 3, 3, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202205">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 6, 2, 15. A <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> in the Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 22, 1. 2, and elsewhere, sums up the duties of the Brahmacārin as ‘thou art a Brahmacārin: eat water; perform thy duty; sleep not in the daytime; obedient to thy teacher study the <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a> (<i>brahmacāry asy; apo ‘śāna; karma <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>; divā mā svāpsīr; ācāryāyādhīno vedam adhīṣva</i>).’ One duty specially referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 1, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, vii. 19, and the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 5, 5, was the guarding of the teacher's cattle when they were grazing on their pasture grounds. From these grounds, too, the pupil would, no doubt, bring dried dung for fuel, as well as any available sticks. As regards obedience to the teacher, <i>cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 3, 3, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202205">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 10; vi. 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1202205">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> viii. 7, 3 (thirty-two years); 15 (for life), etc.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1202205">↑</a> See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 21. The Sūtras allowed for a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> the ages 8-16; for a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>, 11-22; for a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>, 12-24. The difference between the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>, compared with that between the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>, shows that the two latter castes were in a different position from the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1202205">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1202205">↑</a> xv. 5, 17. <i>Cf.</i> Lanman in Whitney's Translation of the Atharvaveda, 639.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1202205">↑</a> ix. 16 (reading <i>abrāhmaṇa</i>) .</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1202205">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1202205">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 106-113.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1202205">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, x. 65; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 10, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1202205">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxiv. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 9, 4; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 125, n. 1; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 79.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1202205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1202205">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 6 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 2, 4 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 202, 203; Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 151; Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 10, 121 <i>et seq.;</i> Deussen, <i>Philosophy of the Upanishads,</i> 370, 371, and see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

ब्रह्म-ज्य|ब्रह्मज्य|ब्रह्म|ज्य|బ్రహ్మ-జ్య|బ్రహ్మజ్య|బ్రహ్మ|జ్య|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮ-ಜ್ಯ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮಜ್ಯ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮ|ಜ್ಯ|brahma-jya|brahmajya|brahma|jya|_vei_120@2206|_vei2_pn077
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2205">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2207">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn074">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn077">2:077</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn078">↷</a><hr><p><b>Brahma-jya,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202206">1</a></sup> ‘oppressor of a Brahmin,’ and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्म">brahma</a>-jyeya,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202206">2</a></sup> ‘oppression of a Brahmin,’ are terms mentioned several times in the Atharvaveda as expressing a heinous crime which involves its perpetrator in ruin. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202206">↑</a> v. 19, 7. 12; xii. 5, 15 <i>et seq.;</i> xiii. 3, 1. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 7, 9, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202206">↑</a> Av. xii. 4, 11.</li></ol></div>

ब्रह्म-दत्त चैकितानेय|ब्रह्मदत्त चैकितानेय|ब्रह्म|दत्त चैकितानेय|బ్రహ్మ-దత్త చైకితానేయ|బ్రహ్మదత్త చైకితానేయ|బ్రహ్మ|దత్త చైకితానేయ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮ-ದತ್ತ ಚೈಕಿತಾನೇಯ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮದತ್ತ ಚೈಕಿತಾನೇಯ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮ|ದತ್ತ ಚೈಕಿತಾನೇಯ|brahma-datta caikitāneya|brahmadatta caikitāneya|brahma|datta caikitāneya|brahma-datta chaikitaaneya|brahmadatta chaikitaaneya|datta chaikitaaneya|_vei_120@2207|_vei2_pn077
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2206">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2208">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn074">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn077">2:077</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn078">↷</a><hr><p><b>Brahma-<a class="vei_120a" href="दत्त">datta</a> Caikitāneya</b> (‘descendant of Cekitāna’) is the name of a teacher in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (i. 3, 26). He is mentioned also in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (i. 38, 1; 59, 1) as patronized by <b>Abhipratārin,</b> the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a> king.</p></div>

ब्रह्मन्|ब्रह्मन्|బ్రహ్మన్_౧|బ్రహ్మన్|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮನ್_೧|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮನ್|brahman_1|brahman|_vei_120@2208|_vei2_pn077
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2207">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2209">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn074">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn077">2:077</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn078">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Brahman</b> (neut.) denotes the priestly class as opposed to the warrior class and the people (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्र">kṣatra</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a></b>). The term is found in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202208"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202208">1</a></sup> and repeatedly later on.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202208"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202208">2</a></sup> For the position, etc., of this class, see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202208"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202208">↑</a> ii. 15, 4; ix. 7, 9; xii. 5, 8; xv. 10, 3, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202208"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202208">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 3, 1, 1, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, vi. 3; vii. 21, etc. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्र">kṣatra</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

ब्रह्मन्|ब्रह्मन्|బ్రహ్మన్_౨|బ్రహ్మన్|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮನ್_೨|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮನ್|brahman_2|brahman|_vei_120@2209|_vei2_pn077
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2208">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2210">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn074">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn077">2:077</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn078">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Brahman</b> is found in many passages of the Rigveda and later in the sense of ‘priest.’ In many passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202209">1</a></sup> he is referred to as praising the gods; in others<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202209">2</a></sup> the sense of ‘priest’ is adequate. In not a few cases<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202209">3</a></sup> the priesthood as a profession is clearly alluded to, nor is there any reason to doubt<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202209">4</a></sup> that in all cases the word has the technical sense of a member of the priesthood. There is, however, considerable doubt as to the number of cases in the Rigveda, where it has the technical  sense of the priest who guides the sacrifice generally. It is undoubtedly found in that sense, both Muir<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202209">5</a></sup> and Roth<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202209">6</a></sup> recognizing instances of its being used thus. Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202209">7</a></sup> however, is anxious to find that sense in a large number of passages, and insists that the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a></b> was normally a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> in the narrower sense. Oldenberg,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202209">8</a></sup> on the other hand, holds with greater probability that in most of the passages adduced <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> means simply ‘priest,’ and that the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, who was essentially not a member of the ordinary body of sacrificing priests (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋत्विज्">ṛtvij</a></b>), was, when he officiated at the sacrifice, more usually the <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a> priest, and only later became the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>. This change he regards as having taken place when the importance of the hymns declined, and most weight was laid on the functions of the priest who superintended the sacrifice as a whole, and by his magic repaired the flaws in the sacrifice.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202209">9</a></sup> In the later literature both senses of the word are quite common.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202209">10</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202209">↑</a> i. 80, 1; 164, 34; ii. 2, 6; vi. 45, 7; vii. 33, 11; viii. 16, 7; x. 71, 11; 77, 1; 85, 3, 16. 34; 107, 6; 117, 7; 125, 5; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 244-246.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202209">↑</a> i. 10, 1; 33, 9; 101, 5; 108, 7; 158, 6; ii. 39, 1; iv. 50, 8, 9; 58, 2; v. 29, 3; 31, 4; 32, 12; 40, 8; vii. 7, 5; 42, 1; viii. 7, 20; 17, 2; 31, 1; 32, 16; 33 19; 45, 39; 64, 7; 77, 5; 92, 30; 96, 5; ix. 96, 6; 112, 1; 113, 6; x. 28 11; 71, 11; 85, 29; 141, 3; Muir, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 12, 246-251.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202209">↑</a> i. 108, 7; iv. 50, 8, 9; viii. 7, 20; 45, 39; 64, 7; 92, 30; ix. 112, 1; x. 85, 29; Muir, 12, 258.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202209">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>. Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 190 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202209">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 12, 251, citing ii. 1, 2 (= ix. 91, 10); iv. 9, 4; x. 52, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202209">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 2, citing ii. 1, 2; ix. 96, 6; x. 71, 11; 107, 6. In none of the last three passages is the specific sense cogently required.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202209">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 145 <i>et seq.;</i> 3, 155. He thinks that the sense of ‘superintending priest’ is the older, and sees it in i. 158, 6; iv. 9, 4; 50, 7. 8; vii. 7, 5; 33, 11; x. 141, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202209">↑</a> <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 396, 397, who thinks that the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> priest known to the Rigveda was the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मणाच्छंसिन्">brāhmaṇācchaṃsin</a></b> and who in most passages (<i>e.g.,</i> iv. 50, 7. 8) sees only the sense of ‘priest.’ <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 376, 377.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202209">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Pischel, <i>Go7ttingische Gelehrte Anzeigen,</i> 1894, 420; Hillebrandt, <i>Rituallitteratur,</i> 13; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> lxviii; <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 32; and see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202209">↑</a> As ‘priest,’ Av. ii. 7, 2; iv. 35, 1. 2; v. 8, 5; 17, 8; 18, 7; 19, 8; vi. 122, 5; viii. 9, 3; x. 1, 3; 4, 30. 33; 7, 24; xi. 1, 25; xii. 1, 38; xix. 32, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 1, 7, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxvi. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, etc. As ‘superintending priest,’ Av. xviii. 4, 15; xx. 2, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 9, 1; ii. 3, 11, 4; iii. 5, 2, 1, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 17; and see Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 34, 35; 114; 135-138; 327; 330-337.</li></ol></div>

ब्रह्म-पुत्र|ब्रह्मपुत्र|ब्रह्म|पुत्र|బ్రహ్మ-పుత్ర|బ్రహ్మపుత్ర|బ్రహ్మ|పుత్ర|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮಪುತ್ರ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮ|ಪುತ್ರ|brahma-putra|brahmaputra|brahma|putra|_vei_120@2210|_vei2_pn078
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2209">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2211">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn077">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn078">2:078</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn079">↷</a><hr><p><b>Brahma-putra</b> in a few passages is used in the sense of a ‘priest's son.’</p><p>^1 Rv. ii. 43, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 4, 1, 2. 9. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 43, 69; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 252. </p></div>

ब्रह्म-पुरोहित|ब्रह्मपुरोहित|ब्रह्म|पुरोहित|బ్రహ్మ-పురోహిత|బ్రహ్మపురోహిత|బ్రహ్మ|పురోహిత|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮ-ಪುರೋಹಿತ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮಪುರೋಹಿತ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮ|ಪುರೋಹಿತ|brahma-purohita|brahmapurohita|brahma|purohita|_vei_120@2211|_vei2_pn079
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2210">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2212">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn078">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn079">2:079</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn080">↷</a><hr><p><b>Brahma-purohita</b> is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202211"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202211">1</a></sup> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202211"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202211">2</a></sup> where the St. Petersburg Dictionary gives the sense as ‘having the priesthood as its <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>.’ This seems rather doubtful; more probably the sense should be ‘having a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> priest as <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>,’ unless the word merely means ‘having the priesthood superior to it,’ as an epithet of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्र">kṣatra</a>,</b> the ‘warrior caste,’ which seems to be Weber's view.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202211"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202211">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202211"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202211">↑</a> xix. 10; xxvii. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202211"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202211">↑</a> xii. 8, 3, 29.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202211"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202211">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 30.</li></ol></div>

ब्रह्म-बन्धु|ब्रह्मबन्धु|ब्रह्म|बन्धु|బ్రహ్మ-బన్ధు|బ్రహ్మబన్ధు|బ్రహ్మ|బన్ధు|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮ-ಬನ್ಧು|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮಬನ್ಧು|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮ|ಬನ್ಧು|brahma-bandhu|brahmabandhu|brahma|bandhu|_vei_120@2212|_vei2_pn079
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2211">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2213">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn078">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn079">2:079</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn080">↷</a><hr><p><b>Brahma-bandhu</b> (‘priest fellow’) denotes, in a deprecatory sense, an ‘unworthy priest,’ ‘priest in name only,’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202212"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202212">1</a></sup> and the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202212"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202212">2</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्यबन्धु">rājanyabandhu</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202212"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202212">↑</a> vii. 27.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202212"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202212">↑</a> vi. 1, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 6, 28; Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxii. 4, 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 29, 9; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 99, 100.</li></ol></div>

ब्रह्मर्षि-देश|ब्रह्मर्षिदेश|ब्रह्मर्षि|देश|బ్రహ్మర్షి-దేశ|బ్రహ్మర్షిదేశ|బ్రహ్మర్షి|దేశ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮರ್ಷಿ-ದೇಶ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮರ್ಷಿದೇಶ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮರ್ಷಿ|ದೇಶ|brahmarṣi-deśa|brahmarṣideśa|brahmarṣi|deśa|brahmarShi-desha|brahmarShidesha|brahmarShi|desha|_vei_120@2213|_vei2_pn079
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2212">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2214">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn078">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn079">2:079</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn080">↷</a><hr><p><b>Brahmarṣi-deśa.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a>.</b></p></div>

ब्रह्म-वद्य|ब्रह्मवद्य|ब्रह्म|वद्य|బ్రహ్మ-వద్య|బ్రహ్మవద్య|బ్రహ్మ|వద్య|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮ-ವದ್ಯ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮವದ್ಯ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮ|ವದ್ಯ|brahma-vadya|brahmavadya|brahma|vadya|_vei_120@2214|_vei2_pn079
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2213">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2215">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn078">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn079">2:079</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn080">↷</a><hr><p><b>Brahma-vadya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मोद्य">brahmodya</a>.</b></p></div>

ब्रह्म-वादिन्|ब्रह्मवादिन्|ब्रह्म|वादिन्|బ్రహ్మ-వాదిన్|బ్రహ్మవాదిన్|బ్రహ్మ|వాదిన్|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮ-ವಾದಿನ್|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮವಾದಿನ್|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮ|ವಾದಿನ್|brahma-vādin|brahmavādin|brahma|vādin|brahma-vaadin|brahmavaadin|vaadin|_vei_120@2215|_vei2_pn079
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2214">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2216">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn078">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn079">2:079</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn080">↷</a><hr><p><b>Brahma-vādin</b> (‘expounder of the Veda’) in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202215"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202215">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202215"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202215">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘theologian.’ <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्म">brahma</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="विद्">vid</a> (‘knowing what is sacred’) has the same sense.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202215"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202215">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202215"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202215">↑</a> Av. xi. 3, 26; xv. 1, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 7, 1, 4; ii. 6, 2, 3; 3, 1; v. 2, 7, 1; 5, 3, 2; vi. 1, 4, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202215"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202215">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 10, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 3, 13; vi. 4, 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka. i. 22, 9; v. 2, 2; 4, 6; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 24, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202215"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202215">↑</a> Av. x. 7, 24. 27; 8, 43; xix. 43, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 8, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 1; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 7, 4; iv. 4, 11, 12, etc.</li></ol></div>

ब्रह्म-विद्या|ब्रह्मविद्या|ब्रह्म|विद्या|బ్రహ్మ-విద్యా|బ్రహ్మవిద్యా|బ్రహ్మ|విద్యా|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮ-ವಿದ್ಯಾ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮವಿದ್ಯಾ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮ|ವಿದ್ಯಾ|brahma-vidyā|brahmavidyā|brahma|vidyā|brahma-vidyaa|brahmavidyaa|vidyaa|_vei_120@2216|_vei2_pn079
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2215">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2217">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn078">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn079">2:079</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn080">↷</a><hr><p><b>Brahma-vidyā,</b> ‘knowledge of the Absolute,’ is the name of one of the sciences enumerated in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202216"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202216">1</a></sup> It is also mentioned elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202216"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202216">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202216"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202216">↑</a> vii. 1, 2, 4; 2, 1; 7, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202216"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202216">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 4, 20, etc.</li></ol></div>

ब्रह्म-वृद्धि|ब्रह्मवृद्धि|ब्रह्म|वृद्धि|బ్రహ్మ-వృద్ధి|బ్రహ్మవృద్ధి|బ్రహ్మ|వృద్ధి|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮ-ವೃದ್ಧಿ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮವೃದ್ಧಿ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮ|ವೃದ್ಧಿ|brahma-vṛddhi|brahmavṛddhi|brahma|vṛddhi|brahma-vRddhi|brahmavRddhi|vRddhi|brahma-vRiddhi|brahmavRiddhi|vRiddhi|brahma-vRuddhi|brahmavRuddhi|vRuddhi|brahma-vRRiddhi|brahmavRRiddhi|vRRiddhi|_vei_120@2217|_vei2_pn079
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2216">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2218">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn078">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn079">2:079</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn080">↷</a><hr><p><b>Brahma-vṛddhi</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202217"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202217">1</a></sup> as a pupil of <b>Mitravarcas.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202217"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202217">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372, 382.</li></ol></div>

ब्रह्म-हत्या|ब्रह्महत्या|ब्रह्म|हत्या|బ్రహ్మ-హత్యా|బ్రహ్మహత్యా|బ్రహ్మ|హత్యా|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮ-ಹತ್ಯಾ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮಹತ್ಯಾ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮ|ಹತ್ಯಾ|brahma-hatyā|brahmahatyā|brahma|hatyā|brahma-hatyaa|brahmahatyaa|hatyaa|_vei_120@2218|_vei2_pn080
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2217">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2219">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn079">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn080">2:080</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn092">↷</a><hr><p><b>Brahma-hatyā,</b> the ‘murder of a Brahmin,’ is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202218">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202218">2</a></sup> as a heinous crime. The murderer is called <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्म">brahma</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="हन्">han</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202218">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202218">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 1, 2; v. 3, 12, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxix. 13, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202218">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 3, 1, 1; 5, 3; 5, 4, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, x. 38; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 27, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202218">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 1, 2; vi. 5, 10, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxi. 7; Kapiṣṭhala Saṃhitā, xlvii. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 8, 12; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 3, 5, 4, etc. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

ब्रह्मावर्त|బ్రహ్మావర్త|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮಾವರ್ತ|brahmāvarta|brahmaavarta|_vei_120@2219|_vei2_pn080
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2218">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2220">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn079">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn080">2:080</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn092">↷</a><hr><p><b>Brahmāvarta.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a>.</b></p></div>

ब्रह्मोद्य|బ్రహ్మోద్య|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮೋದ್ಯ|brahmodya|_vei_120@2220|_vei2_pn080
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2219">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2221">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn079">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn080">2:080</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn092">↷</a><hr><p><b>Brahmodya</b> in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202220">1</a></sup> denotes a ‘theological riddle,’ such as formed an essential part of various ceremonies in the Vedic ritual, as at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> or the Daśarātra. <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्म-वद्य">brahma-vadya</a> is the form found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202220">2</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्म">brahma</a>-vādya in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202220">3</a></sup> probably has the same sense.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202220">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 6, 9, 20; xi. 4, 1, 2; 5, 3, 1; 6, 2, 5; xiii. 2, 6, 9; 5, 2, 11; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 8, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 25.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202220">↑</a> xxvii. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202220">↑</a> ii. 5, 8, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 172; <i>Religion of the <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 216 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 118, 119; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 390 <i>et seq.;</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 452, 453.</li></ol></div>

ब्रह्मोपनिषद्|బ్రహ్మోపనిషద్|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮೋಪನಿಷದ್|brahmopaniṣad|brahmopaniShad|_vei_120@2221|_vei2_pn080
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2220">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2222">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn079">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn080">2:080</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn092">↷</a><hr><p><b>Brahmopaniṣad,</b> a ‘secret doctrine regarding the Absolute,’ is the name of a discussion in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iii. 11, 3).</p></div>

ब्रह्मौदन|బ్రహ్మౌదన|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮೌದನ|brahmaudana|_vei_120@2222|_vei2_pn080
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2221">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2223">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn079">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn080">2:080</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn092">↷</a><hr><p><b>Brahmaudana</b> denotes in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202222"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202222">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202222"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202222">2</a></sup> the ‘rice boiled (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="ओदन">odana</a></b>) for the priests’ officiating at the sacrifice.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202222"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202222">↑</a> Av. iv. 35, 7; xi. 1, 1, 3, 20. 23 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 8, 7; v. 7, 3, 4; vi. 5, 6, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202222"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202222">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 1, 1, 1. 4; 3, 6, 6; 4, 1, 5, etc.</li></ol></div>

ब्राह्मण|ब्राह्मण|బ్రాహ్మణ_౧|బ్రాహ్మణ|ಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ_೧|ಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ|brāhmaṇa_1|brāhmaṇa|braahmaNa_1|braahmaNa|_vei_120@2223|_vei2_pn080
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2222">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2224">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn079">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn080">2:080</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn092">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Brāhmaṇa,</b> ‘descendant of a Brahman’ (<i>i.e.,</i> of a priest), is found only a few times in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202223">1</a></sup> and mostly in its latest  parts. In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202223">2</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202223">3</a></sup> it is a very common word denoting ‘priest,’ and it appears in the quadruple division of the castes in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुष">puruṣa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="सूक्त">sūkta</a> (‘hymn of man’) of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202223">4</a></sup></p><p>It seems certain that in the Rigveda this <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, or Brahmin, is already a separate caste, differing from the warrior and agricultural castes.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202223">5</a></sup> The texts regularly claim for them a superiority to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a></b> caste,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202223">6</a></sup> and the Brahmin is able by his spells or manipulation of the rite to embroil the people and the warriors<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202223">7</a></sup> or the different sections of the warriors.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202223">8</a></sup> If it is necessary to recognize, as is sometimes done, that the Brahmin does pay homage to the king at the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202223">9</a></sup> nevertheless the unusual fact is carefully explained away so as to leave the priority of the Brahmin unaffected. But it is expressly recognized that the union of the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> is essential for complete prosperity.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202223">10</a></sup> It is admitted<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202223">11</a></sup> that the king or the nobles might at times oppress the Brahmins, but it is indicated that ruin is then certain swiftly to follow. </p><p>The Brahmins are gods on earth,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202223">12</a></sup> like the gods in heaven, but this claim is hardly found in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202223">13</a></sup></p><p>In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1202223">14</a></sup> the Brahmin is said to be the ‘recipient of gifts’ (<i>ādāyī</i>) and the ‘drinker of the offering’ (<i>āpāyī</i>). The other two epithets applied, <i>āvasāyī</i> and <i>yathākāma-prayāpya,</i> are more obscure; the former denotes either ‘dwelling everywhere’<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1202223">15</a></sup> or ‘seeking food’;<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1202223">16</a></sup> the latter is usually taken as ‘moving at pleasure,’ but it must rather allude to the power of the king to assign a place of residence to the Brahmin.</p><p>In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1202223">17</a></sup> the prerogatives of the Brahmin are summed up as (1) <a class="vei_120a" href="अर्चा">arcā</a>, ‘honour’; (2) <a class="vei_120a" href="दान">dāna</a>, ‘gifts’; (3) <a class="vei_120a" href="अज्येयता">ajyeyatā</a>, ‘freedom from oppression’; and (4) <a class="vei_120a" href="अवध्यता">avadhyatā</a>, ‘freedom from being killed.’ On the other hand, his duties are summed up as (5) Brāhmaṇya, ‘purity of descent’; (6) Pratirūpa-caryā, ‘devotion of the duties of his caste’; and (7) <a class="vei_120a" href="लोक">loka</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पक्ति">pakti</a>, ‘the perfecting of people’ (by teaching).</p><p>1. <i>Respect paid to Brahmins.</i>--The texts are full<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1202223">18</a></sup> of references to the civilities to be paid to the Brahmin. He is styled <i>bhagavant,</i><sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1202223">19</a></sup> and is provided with good food<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1202223">20</a></sup> and entertainment wherever he goes. Indeed, his sanctity exempts him from any close inquiry into his real claim to Brahminhood according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1202223">21</a></sup></p><p>2. <i>Gifts to Brahmins.</i>--The Dānastuti (‘Praise of gifts’) is a recognized feature of the Rigveda, and the greed of the poets for <b>Dakṣiṇās,</b> or sacrificial fees, is notorious. Vedic texts<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1202223">22</a></sup> themselves recognize that the literature thence resulting (<b>Nārā śaṃsī</b>) was often false to please the donors. It was, however, a rule<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1202223">23</a></sup> that Brahmins should not accept what had been refused by others; this indicates a keen sense of the danger of cheapening their wares. So exclusively theirs was the right to receive gifts that the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1202223">24</a></sup> has to explain how <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तरन्त">taranta</a></b> and <b>Purumīḷha</b> became able to accept gifts by composing a Rigvedic hymn.<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1202223">25</a></sup> The exaggerations in the celebration of the gifts bestowed on the priests has the curious result of giving us a series of numerals of some interest (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="दशन्">daśan</a></b>). In some passages<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_1202223">26</a></sup> certain gifts--those of a horse or sheep--are forbidden, but this rule was not, it is clear, generally observed.</p><p>3. <i>Immunities of Brahmins.</i>--The Brahmin claimed to be exempt from the ordinary exercise of the royal power. When a king gives all his land and what is on it to the priests, the gift does not cover the property of the Brahmin according to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_1202223">27</a></sup> The king censures all, but not the Brahmin,<sup id="ref_120_28_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_1202223">28</a></sup> nor can he safely oppress any Brahmin other than an ignorant priest.<sup id="ref_120_29_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29_vei_1202223">29</a></sup> An arbitrator (or a witness) must decide (or speak) for a Brahmin against a non-Brahmin in a legal dispute.<sup id="ref_120_30_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_30_vei_1202223">30</a></sup></p><p>The Brahmin's proper food is the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_31_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_31_vei_1202223">31</a></sup> not <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुरा">surā</a></b><sup id="ref_120_32_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_32_vei_1202223">32</a></sup> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परिस्रुत्">parisrut</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_33_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_33_vei_1202223">33</a></sup> and he is forbidden to eat certain forms of flesh.<sup id="ref_120_34_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_34_vei_1202223">34</a></sup> On the other hand, he alone is allowed to eat the remains of the sacrifice,<sup id="ref_120_35_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_35_vei_1202223">35</a></sup> for no one else is sufficiently holy to consume food which the gods have eaten. Moreover, though he cannot be a physician,<sup id="ref_120_36_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_36_vei_1202223">36</a></sup> he helps the physician by being beside him  while he exercises his art.<sup id="ref_120_37_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_37_vei_1202223">37</a></sup> His wife<sup id="ref_120_38_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_38_vei_1202223">38</a></sup> and his cow<sup id="ref_120_39_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_39_vei_1202223">39</a></sup> are both sacred.</p><p>4. <i>Legal Position of Brahmins.</i>--The <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_40_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_40_vei_1202223">40</a></sup> lays down a penalty of a hundred (the unit meant is unknown) for an insult to a Brahmin, and of a thousand for a blow; but if his blood is drawn, the penalty is a spiritual one. The only real murder is the slaying of a Brahmin according to the Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_41_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_41_vei_1202223">41</a></sup> The crime of slaying a Brahmin ranks above the sin of killing any other man, but below that of killing an embryo (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="भ्रूण">bhrūṇa</a></i>) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>;<sup id="ref_120_42_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_42_vei_1202223">42</a></sup> the crime of slaying an embryo whose sex is uncertain is on a level with that of slaying a Brahmin.<sup id="ref_120_43_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_43_vei_1202223">43</a></sup> The murder of a Brahmin can be expiated only by the horse sacrifice,<sup id="ref_120_44_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_44_vei_1202223">44</a></sup> or by a lesser rite in the late <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_45_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_45_vei_1202223">45</a></sup> The ritual slaying of a Brahmin is allowed in the later ceremonial,<sup id="ref_120_46_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_46_vei_1202223">46</a></sup> and hinted at in the curious legend of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a>;</b><sup id="ref_120_47_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_47_vei_1202223">47</a></sup> and a <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> might be punished with death for treachery to his master.<sup id="ref_120_48_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_48_vei_1202223">48</a></sup></p><p>5. <i>Purity of Birth.</i>--The importance of pure descent is seen in the stress laid on being a descendant of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> (<i>ārṣeya</i>).<sup id="ref_120_49_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_49_vei_1202223">49</a></sup> But, on the other hand, there are clear traces of another doctrine, which requires learning, and not physical descent, as the true criterion of Ṛṣihood.<sup id="ref_120_50_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_50_vei_1202223">50</a></sup> In agreement with this is the fact that <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सत्यकाम">satyakāma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="जाबाल">jābāla</a></b> was received as a pupil, though his parentage was unknown, his mother being a slave girl who had been connected with several men,<sup id="ref_120_51_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_51_vei_1202223">51</a></sup> and that in the Śatapatha  <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_52_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_52_vei_1202223">52</a></sup> the ceremony on acceptance as a pupil required merely the name of the pupil. So <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कवष">kavaṣa</a></b> is taunted in the Rigveda Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_53_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_53_vei_1202223">53</a></sup> as being the son of a female slave (<b>Dāsī</b>), and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वत्स">vatsa</a></b> cleared himself of a similar imputation by a fire ordeal.<sup id="ref_120_54_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_54_vei_1202223">54</a></sup> Moreover, a very simple rite was adequate to remove doubts as to origin.<sup id="ref_120_55_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_55_vei_1202223">55</a></sup> In these circumstances it is doubtful whether much value attaches to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रवर">pravara</a></b> lists in which the ancestors of the priest were invoked at the beginning of the sacrifice by the <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="अध्वर्यु">adhvaryu</a> priests.<sup id="ref_120_56_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_56_vei_1202223">56</a></sup> Still, in many parts of the ritual the knowledge of two or more generations was needed,<sup id="ref_120_57_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_57_vei_1202223">57</a></sup> and in one ceremony<sup id="ref_120_58_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_58_vei_1202223">58</a></sup> ten ancestors who have drunk the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> are required, but a literal performance of the rite is excused. Moreover, there are clear traces of ritual variations in schools, like those of the <b>Vasiṣṭhas</b> and the <b>Viśvāmitras.</b></p><p>6. <i>The Conduct of the Brahmin.</i>--The Brahmin was required to maintain a fair standard of excellence.<sup id="ref_120_59_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_59_vei_1202223">59</a></sup> He was to be kind to all<sup id="ref_120_60_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_60_vei_1202223">60</a></sup> and gentle,<sup id="ref_120_61_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_61_vei_1202223">61</a></sup> offering sacrifice and receiving gifts.<sup id="ref_120_62_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_62_vei_1202223">62</a></sup> Especial stress was laid on purity of speech;<sup id="ref_120_63_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_63_vei_1202223">63</a></sup> thus <b>Viśvantara's</b> excuse for excluding the <b>Śyāparṇas</b> from his retinue was their impure (<i>apūtā</i>) speech.<sup id="ref_120_64_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_64_vei_1202223">64</a></sup> Theirs was the craving for knowledge<sup id="ref_120_65_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_65_vei_1202223">65</a></sup> and the life of begging.<sup id="ref_120_66_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_66_vei_1202223">66</a></sup> False Brahmins are those who do not fulfil their duties<sup id="ref_120_67_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_67_vei_1202223">67</a></sup> (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मबन्धु">brahmabandhu</a></b>) .  But the penances for breach of duty are, in the Sūtras, of a very light and unimportant character.<sup id="ref_120_68_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_68_vei_1202223">68</a></sup></p><p>7. <i>Brahminical Studies.</i>--The aim of the priest is to obtain pre-eminence in sacred knowledge (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्म">brahma</a>-varcasam</i>), as is stated in numerous passages of Vedic literature.<sup id="ref_120_69_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_69_vei_1202223">69</a></sup> Such distinction is not indeed confined to the Brahmin: the king has it also, but it is not really in a special manner appropriate to the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>.<sup id="ref_120_70_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_70_vei_1202223">70</a></sup> Many ritual acts are specified as leading to Brahmavarcasa,<sup id="ref_120_71_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_71_vei_1202223">71</a></sup> but more stress is laid on the study of the sacred texts: the importance of such study is repeatedly insisted upon.<sup id="ref_120_72_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_72_vei_1202223">72</a></sup></p><p>The technical name for study is <a class="vei_120a" href="स्वाध्याय">svādhyāya</a>: the Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> is eloquent upon its advantages,<sup id="ref_120_73_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_73_vei_1202223">73</a></sup> and it is asserted that the joy of the learned <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रोत्रिय">śrotriya</a>, or ‘student,’ is equal to the highest joy possible.<sup id="ref_120_74_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_74_vei_1202223">74</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नाक">nāka</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मौद्गल्य">maudgalya</a></b> held that study and the teaching of others were the true penance (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="तपस्">tapas</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_75_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_75_vei_1202223">75</a></sup> The object was the ‘threefold knowledge’ (<i>trayī <a class="vei_120a" href="विद्या">vidyā</a></i>), that of the Ṛc, <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुस्">yajus</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a>,<sup id="ref_120_76_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_76_vei_1202223">76</a></sup> a student of all three Vedas being called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रि">tri</a>-śukriya</i><sup id="ref_120_77_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_77_vei_1202223">77</a></sup> or <i><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रि">tri</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="शुक्र">śukra</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_78_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_78_vei_1202223">78</a></sup> ‘thrice pure.’ Other objects of study are enumerated in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_79_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_79_vei_1202223">79</a></sup> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_80_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_80_vei_1202223">80</a></sup> the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_81_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_81_vei_1202223">81</a></sup> etc. (See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a>; <a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="नाराशंसी">nārāśaṃsī</a>; <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मोद्य">brahmodya</a>; <a class="vei_120a" href="अनुशासन">anuśāsana</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अनुव्याख्यान">anuvyākhyāna</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अन्वाख्यान">anvākhyāna</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कल्प">kalpa</a>,</b> 2. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>; <a class="vei_120a" href="विद्या">vidyā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रविद्या">kṣatravidyā</a>, Devajanavidyā, <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्रविद्या">nakṣatravidyā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="भूतविद्या">bhūtavidyā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सर्पविद्या">sarpavidyā</a>; <a class="vei_120a" href="अथर्वांगिरसः">atharvāṅgirasaḥ</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="दैव">daiva</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="निधि">nidhi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पित्र्य">pitrya</a>, Rāśi; <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,</b> etc.) </p><p>Directions as to the exact place and time of study are given in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_82_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_82_vei_1202223">82</a></sup> and in the Sūtras. If study is carried on in the village, it is to be done silently (<i>manasā</i>); if outside, aloud (<i>vācā</i>).</p><p>Learning is expected even from persons not normally competent as teachers, such as the <b>Carakas,</b> who are recognized in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_83_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_83_vei_1202223">83</a></sup> as possible sources of information. Here, too, may be mentioned the cases of Brahmins learning from princes, though their absolute value is doubtful, for the priests would naturally represent their patrons as interested in their sacred science: it is thus not necessary to see in these notices any real and independent study on the part of the Kṣatriyas.<sup id="ref_120_84_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_84_vei_1202223">84</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a></b> learnt from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_85_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_85_vei_1202223">85</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उद्दालक आरुणि">uddālaka āruṇi</a></b> and two other Brahmins from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रवाहण जैवलि">pravāhaṇa jaivali</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_86_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_86_vei_1202223">86</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृप्तबालाकि गार्ग्य">dṛptabālāki gārgya</a></b> from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अजातशत्रु">ajātaśatru</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_87_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_87_vei_1202223">87</a></sup> and five Brahmins under the lead of Aruṇa from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वपति">aśvapati</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कैकेय">kaikeya</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_88_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_88_vei_1202223">88</a></sup> A few notices show the real educators of thought: wandering scholars went through the country<sup id="ref_120_89_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_89_vei_1202223">89</a></sup> and engaged in disputes and discussions in which a prize was staked by the disputants.<sup id="ref_120_90_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_90_vei_1202223">90</a></sup> Moreover, kings like <a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a> offered rewards to the most learned of the Brahmins;<sup id="ref_120_91_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_91_vei_1202223">91</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="अजातशत्रु">ajātaśatru</a> was jealous of his renown, and imitated his generosity. Again, learned women are several times mentioned in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_92_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_92_vei_1202223">92</a></sup></p><p>A special form of disputation was the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मोद्य">brahmodya</a>,</b> for which there was a regular place at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’)<sup id="ref_120_93_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_93_vei_1202223">93</a></sup> and at the Daśarātra (‘ten-day festival’).<sup id="ref_120_94_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_94_vei_1202223">94</a></sup> The reward of learning was the gaining of the title of Kavi or <a class="vei_120a" href="विप्र">vipra</a>, ‘sage.’<sup id="ref_120_95_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_95_vei_1202223">95</a></sup> </p><p>8. <i>The Functions of the Brahmin.</i>--The Brahmin was required not merely to practise individual culture, but also to give others the advantage of his skill, either as a teacher or as a sacrificial priest, or as a <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>.</p><p>As a teacher the Brahmin has, of course, the special duty of instructing his own son in both study and sacrificial ritual.<sup id="ref_120_96_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_96_vei_1202223">96</a></sup> The texts give examples of this, such as <a class="vei_120a" href="आरुणि">āruṇi</a> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्वेतकेतु">śvetaketu</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_97_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_97_vei_1202223">97</a></sup> or mythically <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="भृगु">bhṛgu</a>.<sup id="ref_120_98_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_98_vei_1202223">98</a></sup> This fact also appears from some of the names in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_99_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_99_vei_1202223">99</a></sup> of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_100_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_100_vei_1202223">100</a></sup> On the other hand, these Vaṃśas and the Vaṃśas of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> show that a father often preferred to let his son study under a famous teacher. The relation of pupil and teacher is described under <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मचर्य">brahmacarya</a>.</b> A teacher might take several pupils,<sup id="ref_120_101_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_101_vei_1202223">101</a></sup> and he was bound to teach them with all his heart and soul.<sup id="ref_120_102_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_102_vei_1202223">102</a></sup> He was bound to reveal everything to his pupil, at any rate to one who was staying with him for a year (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="संवत्सर">saṃvatsara</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वासिन्">vāsin</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_103_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_103_vei_1202223">103</a></sup> an expression which shows, as was natural, that a pupil might easily change teachers. But, nevertheless, certain cases of learning kept secret and only revealed to special persons are enumerated.<sup id="ref_120_104_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_104_vei_1202223">104</a></sup> The exact times and modes of teaching are elaborately laid down in the Sūtras,<sup id="ref_120_105_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_105_vei_1202223">105</a></sup> but not in the earlier texts.</p><p>As priest the Brahmin operated in all the greater sacrifices; the simple domestic (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a></i>) rites could normally be performed without his help, but not the more important rites (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a></i>).  The number varied: the ritual literature requires sixteen priests to be employed at the greatest sacrifices (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋत्विज्">ṛtvij</a></b>), but other rites could be accomplished with four,<sup id="ref_120_106_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_106_vei_1202223">106</a></sup> five,<sup id="ref_120_107_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_107_vei_1202223">107</a></sup> six,<sup id="ref_120_108_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_108_vei_1202223">108</a></sup> seven,<sup id="ref_120_109_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_109_vei_1202223">109</a></sup> or ten<sup id="ref_120_110_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_110_vei_1202223">110</a></sup> priests. Again, the Kauṣītakins<sup id="ref_120_111_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_111_vei_1202223">111</a></sup> had a seventeenth priest beside the usual sixteen, the <a class="vei_120a" href="सदस्य">sadasya</a>, so called because he watched the performance from the <a class="vei_120a" href="सदस्">sadas</a>, ‘seat.’ In one rite, the Sattra (‘sacrificial session’) of the serpents, the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_112_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_112_vei_1202223">112</a></sup> adds three more to the sixteen, a second Unnetṛ, an Abhigara, and an Apagara. The later ritual places the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> at the head of all the priests, but this is probably not the early view (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a></b>).</p><p>The sacrifice ensured, if properly performed, primarily the advantages of the sacrificer (<i>yajamāna</i>),<sup id="ref_120_113_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_113_vei_1202223">113</a></sup> but the priest shared in the profit, besides securing the <b>Dakṣiṇās.</b> Disputes between sacrificers and the priests were not rare, as in the case of <b>Viśvantara</b> and the <b>Śyāparṇas,</b><sup id="ref_120_114_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_114_vei_1202223">114</a></sup> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनमेजय">janamejaya</a></b> and the <b>Asitamṛgas;</b><sup id="ref_120_115_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_115_vei_1202223">115</a></sup> and the <b>Aiṣāvīras</b> are referred to as undersirable priests.<sup id="ref_120_116_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_116_vei_1202223">116</a></sup> Moreover, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a></b> once held the post of <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>,</b> but gave place to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>.</b> </p><p>The position of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a></b> differed considerably from that of the ordinary priest, for the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> not merely might officiate at the sacrifice, but was the officiator in all the private sacrifices of his king. Hence he could, and undoubtedly sometimes did, obtain great influence over his master in matters of secular importance; and the power of the priesthood in political as opposed to domestic and religious matters, no doubt rested on the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>.</p><p>There is no recognition in Vedic literature of the rule later prevailing by which, after spending part of his life as a Brahmacārin, and part as a householder, the Brahmin became an ascetic<sup id="ref_120_117_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_117_vei_1202223">117</a></sup> (later divided into the two stages of Vānaprastha, ‘forest-dweller,’ and Saṃnyāsin, ‘mystic’). Yājñavalkya's case<sup id="ref_120_118_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_118_vei_1202223">118</a></sup> shows that study of the Absolute might empty life of all its content for the sage, and drive him to abandon wife and family. In Buddhist times the same phenomenon is seen<sup id="ref_120_119_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_119_vei_1202223">119</a></sup> applying to other than Brahmins. The Buddhist texts are here confirmed in some degree by the Greek authorities.<sup id="ref_120_120_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_120_vei_1202223">120</a></sup> The practice bears a certain resemblance to the habit of kings, in the Epic tradition,<sup id="ref_120_121_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_121_vei_1202223">121</a></sup> of retiring to the forest when active life is over.</p><p>From the Greek authorities<sup id="ref_120_122_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_122_vei_1202223">122</a></sup> it also appears--what is certainly the case in the Buddhist literature<sup id="ref_120_123_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_123_vei_1202223">123</a></sup> --that Brahmins practised the most diverse occupations. It is difficult to say how far this was true for the Vedic period. The analogy of the Druids<sup id="ref_120_124_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_124_vei_1202223">124</a></sup> --in some respects very close--suggests that the Brahmins may have been mainly confined to their professional tasks, including all the learned professions such as astronomy<sup id="ref_120_125_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_125_vei_1202223">125</a></sup>  and so forth. This is not contradicted by any Vedic evidence, for instance, the poet of a hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_126_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_126_vei_1202223">126</a></sup> says he is a poet, his father a physician (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="भिषज्">bhiṣaj</a></b>), and his mother a grinder of corn (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपल-प्रक्षिणी">upala-prakṣiṇī</a></b>). This would seem to show that a Brahmin could be a doctor, while his wife would perform the ordinary household duties. So a <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> could perhaps take the field to assist the king by prayer, as <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_127_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_127_vei_1202223">127</a></sup> and later on <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a><sup id="ref_120_128_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_128_vei_1202223">128</a></sup> do, but this does not show that priests normally fought. Nor do they seem normally to have been agriculturists or merchants. On the other hand, they kept cattle: a Brahmacārin's duty was to watch his master's cattle.<sup id="ref_120_129_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_129_vei_1202223">129</a></sup> It is therefore needless to suppose that they could not, and did not, on occasion turn to agricultural or mercantile pursuits, as they certainly did later. But it must be remembered that in all probability there was more purity of blood, and less pressure of life, among the Brahmins of the Vedic age than later in Buddhist times, when the Vedic sacrificial apparatus was falling into grave disrepute.</p><p>It is clear that the Brahmins, whatever their defects, represented the intellectual side of Vedic life, and that the Kṣatriyas, if they played a part in that life, did so only in a secondary degree, and to a minor extent. It is natural to suppose that the Brahmins also composed ballads, the precursors of the epic; for though none such have survived, a few stanzas of this character, celebrating the generosity of patrons, have been preserved by being embedded in priestly compositions. A legend in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_130_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_130_vei_1202223">130</a></sup> shows clearly that the Brahmins regarded civilization as being spread by them only:  <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>,</b> no doubt settled by Āryan tribes, are only rendered civilized and habitable by the influence of pious Brahmins. We need not doubt that the non-Brahminical tribes (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात्य">vrātya</a></b>) had attained intellectual as well as material civilization, but it is reasonable to assume that their civilization was inferior to that of the Brahmins, for the history of Hinduism is the conquest by the Brahmins--not by arms, but by mind--of the tribes Āryan and non-Āryan originally beyond the pale.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202223">↑</a> i. 164, 45; vi. 75, 10; vii. 103, 1. 7. 8; x. 16, 6; 71, 8. 9; 88, 19; 90, 12; 97, 22; 109, 4. See Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 251-257; Roth, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, Erläuterungen,</i> 126; St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> where Rv. viii. 58, 1, is added; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 220-226.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202223">↑</a> ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 1; v. 17, 9; 18, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> 19, 2 <i>et seq.;</i> xi. 1, 28; xix. 34, 6; 35, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 7, 2; ii. 1, 2, 8, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, vii. 46. etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202223">↑</a> x. 90.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202223">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 235; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 146, n. 1; and see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202223">↑</a> See Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxix. 10; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxi. 21; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 4, 15; xiii. 1, 9, 1; 3, 7, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 15; viii. 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 8, 2; xi. 11; 9; xv. 6, 3; and <i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मपुरोहित">brahmapurohita</a>;</b> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 27 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202223">↑</a> See Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 7; iii. 3, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 11, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202223">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 3, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202223">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 4, 23 (Mādhyaṃdina = i. 4, 11 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>). <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxviii. 5; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 3, 2; v. 4, 2, 7. Contrast the claim that <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> alone is King of the Brahmins, Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x. 18; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 2, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202223">↑</a> See <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 1, 10, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xix. 10; xxvii. 4; xxix. 10; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 3; 7, 7; iii. 1, 9; 2, 3; iv. 3, 9; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xx. 25; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xix. 17, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 4, 6; v. 4, 4, 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 10. 17, 24, 25, etc. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202223">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 8, 7; Pañcavimśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xviii. 10, 8; Av. v. 1719; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 2, 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 1, 5, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202223">↑</a> Av. v. 3, 2; vi. 13, 1; 44, 2; xix. 62, 1 (compared with xix. 32, 8), and probably v. 11, 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 7, 3, 1; ii. 5, 9, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, viii. 13; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 4, 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 2, 2, 6; 4, 3, 14; iii. 1, 1, 11; iv. 3, 4, 4. See Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 10, 35, 36; von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 146, 147.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202223">↑</a> Neither in i. 139, 7, nor ix. 99, 6 (see Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, (<i>s.v. <a class="vei_120a" href="देव">deva</a></i>), is this sense at all probable. Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 206, quotes 1, 128, 8, but that also is uncertain.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1202223">↑</a> vii. 29, 2. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>.</b> n. 71.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1202223">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 326.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1202223">↑</a> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 439.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1202223">↑</a> xi. 5, 7, 1 <i>et seq.</i> See Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 10, 41 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1202223">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā; xxv. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 10, 6; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 1, 10; 3, 4, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1202223">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 6, 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xix. 12.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1202223">↑</a> vi. 5, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxvii. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xiv. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 2, 6, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1202223">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 5, 1, 25. <i>Cf.</i> also Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 15, 8; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 3, 14, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1202223">↑</a> xiii. 7, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1202223">↑</a> ix. 58, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 12, 1. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_1202223">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 29; 6, 2, 18; 7, 1, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_1202223">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> v. 4, 2, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_29_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29_vei_1202223">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> xiii. 4, 2, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_30_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_30_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 11, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_31_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_31_vei_1202223">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 7, 2, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 29. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ix. 40; x. 18, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_32_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_32_vei_1202223">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 8, 1,--5.</li><li id="fx_120_33_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_33_vei_1202223">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> xii. 9, 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_34_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_34_vei_1202223">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 1, 2, 3, 9; vii. 5, 2, 37; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_35_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_35_vei_1202223">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 3, 1, 39; 5, 3, 16, etc. On the food of the Brahmins, <i>cf.</i> also <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 4, 5; xvii. 1, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_36_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_36_vei_1202223">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 5, 8-14, where the Aśvins, who are famous as physicians (viii. 2, 1, 3; xii. 7, 1, 11). are treated as impure.</li><li id="fx_120_37_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_37_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 4, 9, 3. Contrast Rv. x. 97, 22, where no discredit attaches to the profession.</li><li id="fx_120_38_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_38_vei_1202223">↑</a> Av. v. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_39_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_39_vei_1202223">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> v. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_40_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_40_vei_1202223">↑</a> ii. 6, 10. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_41_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_41_vei_1202223">↑</a> xiii. 3, 5, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_42_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_42_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxi. 7; Kapiṣṭhala Saṃhitā, xlvii. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 8, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_43_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_43_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 5, 10, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxvii. 9; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 481; 10, 66.</li><li id="fx_120_44_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_44_vei_1202223">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 3, 1, 1; 5,