="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>. ii. 3, 2, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_61_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_61_vei_1202223">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> ii. 3, 4, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_62_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_62_vei_1202223">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> xiii. 1, 5, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_63_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_63_vei_1202223">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> iii. 2, 1, 24. <i>Cf.</i> iv. 1, 3, 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, xiii. 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xiv. 5; xxxvii. 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 62.</li><li id="fx_120_64_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_64_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 27; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 438.</li><li id="fx_120_65_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_65_vei_1202223">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 8, 8; v. 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_66_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_66_vei_1202223">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> iii. 4, 1; iv. 4, 26.</li><li id="fx_120_67_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_67_vei_1202223">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> vi. 4, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_68_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_68_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 18, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_69_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_69_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 1, 7, 1; vii. 5, 18, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, v. 14; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxii. 22; xxvii. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8, 13, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 11, 6-9; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 2, 6, 10; x. 3, 5, 16; xi. 4, 4, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 3, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_70_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_70_vei_1202223">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 3, 6; xiii. 1, 5, 3, 5; 2, 6, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_71_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_71_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 1, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxiii. 7, 3, etc.; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 3, 1, 31, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_72_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_72_vei_1202223">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 2, 3; xi. 3, 3, 3-6; 5, 7, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_73_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_73_vei_1202223">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 6, 3, 9; 7, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_74_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_74_vei_1202223">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 3, 35-39; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ix. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_75_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_75_vei_1202223">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> vii. 8. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_76_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_76_vei_1202223">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 4, 2. 3; ii. 6, 4, 2-7; iv. 6, 7, 1, 2; v. 5, 5, 9; vi. 3, 1, 10. 11. 20; x. 5, 2, 1. 2; xi. 5, 4, 18; xii. 3, 3, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_77_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_77_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_78_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_78_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_79_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_79_vei_1202223">↑</a> xi. 5, 7, 5-8.</li><li id="fx_120_80_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_80_vei_1202223">↑</a> ii. 9. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_81_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_81_vei_1202223">↑</a> vii. 1, 2, 4; 2, 1; 7, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_82_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_82_vei_1202223">↑</a> ii. 11. 12-15.</li><li id="fx_120_83_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_83_vei_1202223">↑</a> iv. 2, 4. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_84_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_84_vei_1202223">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> (1) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a></b> and (2) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_85_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_85_vei_1202223">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 6, 2, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_86_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_86_vei_1202223">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 11; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 3, 1, and i. 8, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5. 436, 514-516.</li><li id="fx_120_87_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_87_vei_1202223">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upanisad, ii. 1, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_88_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_88_vei_1202223">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 6, 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_89_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_89_vei_1202223">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 3, 1. <i>Cf.</i> iii. 7, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_90_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_90_vei_1202223">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 4, 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_91_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_91_vei_1202223">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> xi. 6, 3, 1; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 1-9, 20. 29.</li><li id="fx_120_92_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_92_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 29; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 9; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 3, 1; 7, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 4, 4; Sāṅkhāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_93_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_93_vei_1202223">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 2, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_94_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_94_vei_1202223">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> iv. 6, 9, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_95_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_95_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 9, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 5, 3, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 2. 7; iii. 5, 3, 12. <i>Cf.</i> also Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upanisad, vi. 4, 29.</li><li id="fx_120_96_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_96_vei_1202223">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 2, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_97_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_97_vei_1202223">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 1. (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 2, 1 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_98_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_98_vei_1202223">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 6, 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_99_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_99_vei_1202223">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 376.</li><li id="fx_120_100_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_100_vei_1202223">↑</a> xv. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_101_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_101_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, vii. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_102_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_102_vei_1202223">↑</a> See <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, vii. 4 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 2, 211).</li><li id="fx_120_103_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_103_vei_1202223">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 1, 1, 26. 27. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, v. 3, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_104_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_104_vei_1202223">↑</a> So the Vasiṣṭhas and the Stomabhāgas, <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xv. 5, 24; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 5, 2, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रवाहण जैवलि">pravāhaṇa jaivali</a> and his knowledge of <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>, Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 11; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 3, where the claim is made that the <i>praśāsana</i> belongs to the Kṣatriyas. Śaṅkara, in his commentary, takes the word to mean the ‘giving of instruction,’ but this must be regarded as improbable, ‘rule’ being more probably the sense. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 128; Bo7htlingk, Translation of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 8, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_105_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_105_vei_1202223">↑</a> Rigveda Prātiśākhya, xv. 1 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, v. 3, 3; and see Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 10, 129-135.</li><li id="fx_120_106_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_106_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 3, 6, 1-4; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 4, 2. The four are the <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अध्वर्यु">adhvaryu</a>, Agnīdh, and Upavaktṛ: Weber, 10, 139, n. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_107_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_107_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, ix. 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 4, 2, with a second <a class="vei_120a" href="अध्वर्यु">adhvaryu</a>, as well as the four enumerated in the previous note.</li><li id="fx_120_108_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_108_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, ix. 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 2, 2, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 4, 6; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 7, 2, 6, where the list has <a class="vei_120a" href="अध्वर्यु">adhvaryu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>, with the Pratiprasthātṛ, Maitrāvaruṇa, Āgnīdhra.</li><li id="fx_120_109_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_109_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, ix. 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 2, 2, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 4, 2. The number seems to be made up of the five of note 107 and the Abhigarau--<i>i.e.,</i> probably the Abhigara and the Apagara.</li><li id="fx_120_110_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_110_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhita, ix. 8, 13-16; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 2, 4, 1; 3, 6, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 25; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 4, 2. What ten are meant is uncertain; the four of note 106 are enumerated.</li><li id="fx_120_111_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_111_vei_1202223">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 4, 2, 19; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 37.</li><li id="fx_120_112_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_112_vei_1202223">↑</a> xxv. 14, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_113_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_113_vei_1202223">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 1, 20; 9, 1, 12; ii. 2, 2, 7; iii. 4, 2, 15; iv. 2, 5, 9, 10; viii. 5, 3, 8; ix. 5, 2, 16; xii. 8, 1, 17, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_114_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_114_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 27 <i>et seq.;</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 436 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_115_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_115_vei_1202223">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 27.</li><li id="fx_120_116_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_116_vei_1202223">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 2, 7, 32, where Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 153, n. 1, interprets <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐषावीर">aiṣāvīra</a>, not as a proper name, but as meaning ‘contemptible’; but Sāyaṇa thinks a proper name is meant, a view accepted by Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 45, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_117_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_117_vei_1202223">↑</a> See Deussen, <i>Philosophy of the Upanishads,</i> 372 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_118_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_118_vei_1202223">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 4, 1; iv. 5, 1. See iii. 5, 1, for his teaching, of which his action is a logical consequence.</li><li id="fx_120_119_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_119_vei_1202223">↑</a> Fick, <i>Die sociale Gliederung,</i> 40 <i>et seq.;</i> Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i>^5 72 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_120_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_120_vei_1202223">↑</a> Arrian, <i>Indica,</i> xii. 8. 9; Strabo, xv. 1, 49, 60.</li><li id="fx_120_121_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_121_vei_1202223">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 179 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_122_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_122_vei_1202223">↑</a> See Fick, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_123_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_123_vei_1202223">↑</a> Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India,</i> 57.</li><li id="fx_120_124_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_124_vei_1202223">↑</a> Cæsar, <i>Bellum Gallicum,</i> vi. 14. The Druids did not fight, did not pay tribute, studied for many years, observed secrecy as to matters of ritual and learning, did not use writing, and had a certain belief in transmigration. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 19.</li><li id="fx_120_125_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_125_vei_1202223">↑</a> Hence the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> is the 28th <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>:</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 3, 3; Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 306, 311; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 40.</li><li id="fx_120_126_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_126_vei_1202223">↑</a> ix. 112.</li><li id="fx_120_127_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_127_vei_1202223">↑</a> Rv. iii. 33. 53.</li><li id="fx_120_128_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_128_vei_1202223">↑</a> Rv. vii. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_129_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_129_vei_1202223">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 4, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 1, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_130_vei_1202223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_130_vei_1202223">↑</a> i. 4, 1, 14-17. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 257, 277, 278, and <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 44.</p><p>Almost all that can be said of the Brahmins is collected in Weber's <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 40-158. <i>Cf.</i> also Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 220-226; Fick, <i>Die sociale Gliederung</i> (for Buddhist times; the evidence is, however, of uncertain, and much of it probably of late, date); Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 82, 182, etc. (for notices of the Brahmins in the Epic): <i>The Mutual Relations of the Four Castes according to the Mānavadharmacāstram</i> (for the <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> view). Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 248 <i>et seq.,</i> discusses the priesthood in the Rigveda, and Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 197212, gives an excellent summary of the facts.</li></ol></div>

ब्राह्मण|ब्राह्मण|బ్రాహ్మణ_౨|బ్రాహ్మణ|ಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ_೨|ಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ|brāhmaṇa_2|brāhmaṇa|braahmaNa_2|braahmaNa|_vei_120@2224|_vei2_pn092
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2223">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2225">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn080">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn092">2:092</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn093">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Brāhmaṇa,</b> ‘religious explanation,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202224"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202224">1</a></sup> is the title of a class of books which as such are only mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202224"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202224">2</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202224"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202224">3</a></sup> and then in the Sūtras, where the names of the Brāhmaṇas occur, showing that literary works were in existence.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202224"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202224">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 25, 15; iii. 45, 8; vi. 25, 1, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 1, 9, 5; 5, 2, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 4, 1, etc. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, i. and ii., the use is constant.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202224"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202224">↑</a> ii. 16; xiii. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202224"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202224">↑</a> ii. 10.</li></ol></div>

ब्राह्मण|ब्राह्मण|బ్రాహ్మణ_౩|బ్రాహ్మణ|ಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ_೩|ಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ|brāhmaṇa_3|brāhmaṇa|braahmaNa_3|braahmaNa|_vei_120@2225|_vei2_pn092
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2224">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2226">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn080">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn092">2:092</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn093">↷</a><hr><p>3. <b>Brāhmaṇa</b> is taken by Roth in the St. Petersburg Dictionary<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202225"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202225">1</a></sup> to mean the ‘Soma cup of the Brahman’ in two passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202225"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202225">2</a></sup> and one of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202225"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202225">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202225"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202225">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 253, n. 26.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202225"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202225">↑</a> i. 15, 5; ii. 36, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202225"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202225">↑</a> xx. 2, 3.</li></ol></div>

ब्राह्मणाच्-छंसिन्|ब्राह्मणाच्छंसिन्|ब्राह्मणाच्|छंसिन्|బ్రాహ్మణాచ్-ఛంసిన్|బ్రాహ్మణాచ్ఛంసిన్|బ్రాహ్మణాచ్|ఛంసిన్|ಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣಾಚ್-ಛಂಸಿನ್|ಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣಾಚ್ಛಂಸಿನ್|ಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣಾಚ್|ಛಂಸಿನ್|brāhmaṇāc-chaṃsin|brāhmaṇācchaṃsin|brāhmaṇāc|chaṃsin|braahmaNaach-ChaMsin|braahmaNaachChaMsin|braahmaNaach|ChaMsin|_vei_120@2226|_vei2_pn092
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2225">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2227">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn080">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn092">2:092</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn093">↷</a><hr><p><b>Brāhmaṇāc-chaṃsin</b> (‘reciting after the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>--<i>i.e.,</i> Brahman’) is the name of a priest in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202226"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202226">1</a></sup> In the technical division of the sacrificial priests (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋत्विज्">ṛtvij</a></b>) he is classed with the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202226"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202226">2</a></sup> but it is clear that he was really a <a class="vei_120a" href="होत्रक">hotraka</a> or assistant of the <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202226"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202226">3</a></sup> According to Oldenberg,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202226"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202226">4</a></sup> he was known to the Rigveda as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>.</b> This is denied by Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202226"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202226">5</a></sup> who sees in <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> merely the ‘superintending priest’ or the ‘priest.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202226"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202226">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 4, 2; 6, 3. 4; 10, 1; 18, 5; vii. 1, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxviii. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 6, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 2, 3, 13, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202226"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202226">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 144.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202226"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202226">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> Āśvalāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, v. 10, 10; Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 9, 374-376.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202226"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202226">↑</a> <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 396.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202226"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202226">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 145 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

ब्लेष्क|బ్లేష్క|ಬ್ಲೇಷ್ಕ|bleṣka|bleShka|_vei_120@2227|_vei2_pn093
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2226">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2228">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn092">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn093">2:093</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn094">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bleṣka</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202227">1</a></sup> denotes a rope or noose for strangling. It is spelt <a class="vei_120a" href="व्लेष्क">vleṣka</a> in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202227">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202227">↑</a> xxiii. 6; xxxvii. 13. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202227">↑</a> iii. 6, 10. In Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 19, 1, <i>meṣka</i> is read.</li></ol></div>

भग|భగ|ಭಗ|bhaga|_vei_120@2228|_vei2_pn093
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2227">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2229">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn092">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn093">2:093</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn094">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhaga</b> denotes a part of the chariot in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202228">1</a></sup> according to Hillebrandt.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202228">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202228">↑</a> ii. 34, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202228">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 95.</li></ol></div>

भगिनी|भगिनि|భగినీ|భగిని|ಭಗಿನೀ|ಭಗಿನಿ|bhaginī|bhagini|bhaginii|bhaginee|_vei_120@2229|_vei2_pn093
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2228">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2230">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn092">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn093">2:093</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn094">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhaginī,</b> ‘sister,’ literally the ‘fortunate one’ in so far as she has a brother, occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> (iii. 6).</p></div>

भगी-रथ ऐक्ष्वाक|भगीरथ ऐक्ष्वाक|भगी|भगि|रथ ऐक्ष्वाक|భగీ-రథ ఐక్ష్వాక|భగీరథ ఐక్ష్వాక|భగీ|భగి|రథ ఐక్ష్వాక|ಭಗೀ-ರಥ ಐಕ್ಷ್ವಾಕ|ಭಗೀರಥ ಐಕ್ಷ್ವಾಕ|ಭಗೀ|ಭಗಿ|ರಥ ಐಕ್ಷ್ವಾಕ|bhagī-ratha aikṣvāka|bhagīratha aikṣvāka|bhagī|bhagi|ratha aikṣvāka|bhagii-ratha aikShvaaka|bhagiiratha aikShvaaka|bhagii|ratha aikShvaaka|bhagee-ratha aikShvaaka|bhageeratha aikShvaaka|bhagee|bhagii-ratha aixvaaka|bhagiiratha aixvaaka|ratha aixvaaka|_vei_120@2230|_vei2_pn093
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2229">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2231">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn092">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn093">2:093</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn094">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhagī-<a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a> Aikṣvāka</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इक्ष्वाकु">ikṣvāku</a></b>’) is the name of a king in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iv. 6, 1. 2). It is important to note that he is regarded as being on friendly terms with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas,</b> which points to the Ikṣvākus being allied to that people, and not belonging (as is the case in the Buddhist books) to the east of India.</p></div>

भंग|भङ्ग|భంగ|భఙ్గ|ಭಂಗ|ಭಙ್ಗ|bhaṅga|bhaMga|bhanga|_vei_120@2231|_vei2_pn093
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2230">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2232">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn092">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn093">2:093</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn094">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhaṅga,</b> ‘hemp,’ is mentioned in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202231"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202231">1</a></sup> In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202231"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202231">2</a></sup> it is an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>, presumably<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202231"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202231">3</a></sup> in the sense of ‘intoxicating,’ which then came to designate hemp.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202231"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202231">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202231"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202231">↑</a> xi. 6, 15; conceivably in <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xii. 14, but not probably.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202231"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202231">↑</a> ix. 61, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202231"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202231">↑</a> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 299.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202231"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202231">↑</a> Hence the modern ‘Bang’ or Bhang,’ an intoxicant made from the dried leaves and small stalks of hemp, taken either by smoking or by eating when mixed up into a sweetmeat.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 68; Grierson, <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 23, 260; Yule and Burnell, <i>Hobson-Jobson, s.v. Bang.</i></li></ol></div>

भंगाश्विन|भङ्गाश्विन|భంగాశ్విన|భఙ్గాశ్విన|ಭಂಗಾಶ್ವಿನ|ಭಙ್ಗಾಶ್ವಿನ|bhaṅgāśvina|bhaMgaashvina|bhangaashvina|_vei_120@2232|_vei2_pn093
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2231">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2233">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn092">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn093">2:093</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn094">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhaṅgāśvina</b> is the name of the father of <b>Ṛtuparna</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202232">1</a></sup> In the Mahābhārata<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202232">2</a></sup> he is called Bhāṅgāsuri. In the Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202232">3</a></sup> mention is made of <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋतुपर्ण">ṛtuparṇa</a>-Kayovadhī as the Bhaṅgyaśvinau. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202232">↑</a> xx. 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202232">↑</a> iii. 2745.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202232">↑</a> xxi. 20; Caland, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 57, 745.</li></ol></div>

भंग्य-श्रवस्|भंग्य-श्रव|भंग्यश्रवस्|भंग्यश्रव|भंग्य|श्रवस्|श्रव|भङ्ग्य-श्रवस्|भङ्ग्य-श्रव|भङ्ग्यश्रवस्|भङ्ग्यश्रव|भङ्ग्य|భంగ్య-శ్రవస్|భంగ్య-శ్రవ|భంగ్యశ్రవస్|భంగ్యశ్రవ|భంగ్య|శ్రవస్|శ్రవ|భఙ్గ్య-శ్రవస్|భఙ్గ్య-శ్రవ|భఙ్గ్యశ్రవస్|భఙ్గ్యశ్రవ|భఙ్గ్య|ಭಂಗ್ಯ-ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಭಂಗ್ಯ-ಶ್ರವ|ಭಂಗ್ಯಶ್ರವಸ್|ಭಂಗ್ಯಶ್ರವ|ಭಂಗ್ಯ|ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಶ್ರವ|ಭಙ್ಗ್ಯ-ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಭಙ್ಗ್ಯ-ಶ್ರವ|ಭಙ್ಗ್ಯಶ್ರವಸ್|ಭಙ್ಗ್ಯಶ್ರವ|ಭಙ್ಗ್ಯ|bhaṅgya-śravas|bhaṅgya-śrava|bhaṅgyaśravas|bhaṅgyaśrava|bhaṅgya|śravas|śrava|bhaMgya-shravas|bhaMgya-shrava|bhaMgyashravas|bhaMgyashrava|bhaMgya|shravas|shrava|bhangya-shravas|bhangya-shrava|bhangyashravas|bhangyashrava|bhangya|_vei_120@2233|_vei2_pn094
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2232">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2234">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn093">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn094">2:094</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn097">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhaṅgya-śravas</b> is the name of a man in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202233"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202233">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202233"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202233">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 78.</li></ol></div>

भजे-रथ|भजेरथ|भजे|रथ|భజే-రథ|భజేరథ|భజే|రథ|ಭಜೇ-ರಥ|ಭಜೇರಥ|ಭಜೇ|ರಥ|bhaje-ratha|bhajeratha|bhaje|ratha|_vei_120@2234|_vei2_pn094
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2233">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2235">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn093">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn094">2:094</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn097">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhaje-ratha</b> is mentioned in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202234"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202234">1</a></sup> where Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202234"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202234">2</a></sup> thinks a place-name is meant. Griffith<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202234"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202234">3</a></sup> is doubtful whether the word is the name of a place or a man. Roth<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202234"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202234">4</a></sup> was inclined to see a corruption of the text.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202234"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202234">5</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b>Bhagīratha.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202234"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202234">↑</a> x. 60, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202234"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202234">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 138, 165.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202234"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202234">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 2, 463.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202234"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202234">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202234"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202234">↑</a> Grassmann, <i>Wo7rterbuch, s.v.,</i> thinks that the compound should be read as two words: <i><a class="vei_120a" href="भजे">bhaje</a> rathasya (satpatim),</i> ‘to win (the lord) of the car.’</li></ol></div>

भद्र-पदा|भद्रपदा|भद्र|पदा|భద్ర-పదా|భద్రపదా|భద్ర|పదా|ಭದ್ರ-ಪದಾ|ಭದ್ರಪದಾ|ಭದ್ರ|ಪದಾ|bhadra-padā|bhadrapadā|bhadra|padā|bhadra-padaa|bhadrapadaa|padaa|_vei_120@2235|_vei2_pn094
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2234">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2236">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn093">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn094">2:094</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn097">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhadra-padā.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p></div>

भद्र-सेन आजातशत्रव|भद्रसेन आजातशत्रव|भद्र|सेन आजातशत्रव|భద్ర-సేన ఆజాతశత్రవ|భద్రసేన ఆజాతశత్రవ|భద్ర|సేన ఆజాతశత్రవ|ಭದ್ರ-ಸೇನ ಆಜಾತಶತ್ರವ|ಭದ್ರಸೇನ ಆಜಾತಶತ್ರವ|ಭದ್ರ|ಸೇನ ಆಜಾತಶತ್ರವ|bhadra-sena ājātaśatrava|bhadrasena ājātaśatrava|bhadra|sena ājātaśatrava|bhadra-sena aajaatashatrava|bhadrasena aajaatashatrava|sena aajaatashatrava|_vei_120@2236|_vei2_pn094
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2235">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2237">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn093">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn094">2:094</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn097">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhadra-<a class="vei_120a" href="सेन">sena</a> Ājātaśatrava</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अजातशत्रु">ajātaśatru</a></b>’) is the name of a man, presumably a prince, whom <b>Uddālaka</b> is said in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (v. 5, 5, 14) to have bewitched.</p></div>

भय-द आसमात्य|भयद आसमात्य|भय|द आसमात्य|భయ-ద ఆసమాత్య|భయద ఆసమాత్య|భయ|ద ఆసమాత్య|ಭಯ-ದ ಆಸಮಾತ್ಯ|ಭಯದ ಆಸಮಾತ್ಯ|ಭಯ|ದ ಆಸಮಾತ್ಯ|bhaya-da āsamātya|bhayada āsamātya|bhaya|da āsamātya|bhaya-da aasamaatya|bhayada aasamaatya|da aasamaatya|_vei_120@2237|_vei2_pn094
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2236">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2238">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn093">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn094">2:094</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn097">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhaya-da Āsamātya</b> (‘descendant of <b>Asamāti</b>’) is the name of a king in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202237"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202237">1</a></sup> Oertel,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202237"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202237">2</a></sup> however, seems to take the name as Abhayada, but this is not probable, for Bhayada is a name in the Purāṇas.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202237"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202237">↑</a> iv. 8, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202237"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202237">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, 247.</li></ol></div>

भयमान|భయమాన|ಭಯಮಾನ|bhayamāna|bhayamaana|_vei_120@2238|_vei2_pn094
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2237">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2239">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn093">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn094">2:094</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn097">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhayamāna</b> is, according to Sāyaṇa, the name of a man in one hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202238"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202238">1</a></sup> which is ascribed by the Anukramaṇī (Index) to his authorship. The interpretation is, however, uncertain.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202238"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202238">↑</a> i. 100, 17. <i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 266.</li></ol></div>

भरत|భరత|ಭರತ|bharata|_vei_120@2239|_vei2_pn094
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2238">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2240">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn093">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn094">2:094</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn097">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bharata</b> is the name of a people of great importance in the Rigveda and the later literature. In the Rigveda they appear prominently in the third and seventh Maṇḍalas in connexion  with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a></b> and the <b>Tṛtsus,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202239">1</a></sup> while in the sixth Maṇḍala they are associated with <b>Divodāsa.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202239">2</a></sup> In one passage<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202239">3</a></sup> the Bharatas are, like the Tṛtsus, enemies of the <b>Pūrus:</b> there can be little doubt that Ludwig's view of the identity of the Bharatas and and Tṛtsus is practically correct. More precisely Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202239">5</a></sup> considers that the Tṛtsus are the <b>Vasiṣṭhas,</b> the family singers of the Bharatas; while Geldner<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202239">6</a></sup> recognizes, with perhaps more probability, in the Tṛtsus the royal family of the Bharatas. That the Tṛtsus and Bharatas were enemies, as Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202239">7</a></sup> holds, is most improbable even on geographical grounds, for the Tṛtsus in Zimmer's view<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202239">8</a></sup> occupied the country to the east of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परुष्णी">paruṣṇī</a></b> (Ravi), and the Bharatas must therefore be regarded as coming against the Tṛtsus from the west, whereas the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202239">9</a></sup> recognizes two <a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a> chiefs on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="आपया">āpayā</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृषद्वती">dṛṣadvatī</a></b>--that is, in the holy land of India, the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a>.</b> Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202239">10</a></sup> sees in the connexion of the Tṛtsus and the Bharatas a fusion of two tribes; but this is not supported by any evidence beyond the fact that in his opinion some such theory is needed to explain Divodāsa's appearing in connexion with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a></b> family, while <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>, his son,</p><p>^4 Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 172 <i>et seq.</i>  or perhaps grandson (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पिजवन">pijavana</a></b>), is connected with the Vasiṣṭhas and the <b>Viśvāmitras.</b></p><p>In the later literature the Bharatas appear as especially famous. The Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202239">11</a></sup> mentions <a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दौःषन्ति">dauḥṣanti</a> as a king, sacrificer of the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शतानीक सात्राजित">śatānīka sātrājita</a>,</b> as another <a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a> who offered that sacrifice. The <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202239">12</a></sup> mentions <a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दौःषन्ति">dauḥṣanti</a> as receiving the kingly coronation from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दीर्घतमस्">dīrghatamas</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मामतेय">māmateya</a>,</b> and Śatānīka as being consecrated by <b>Somaśuṣman <a class="vei_120a" href="वाजरत्नायन">vājaratnāyana</a>,</b> a priest whose name is of quite late origin. The geographical position of the <a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a> people is clearly shown by the fact that the <a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a> kings win victories over the <b>Kāśis,</b> and make offerings on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a></b> (Jumna) and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गंगा">gaṅgā</a></b> (Ganges).<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202239">13</a></sup> Moreover, in the formula of the king's proclamation for the people, the variants recorded<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1202239">14</a></sup> include <i>Kuravaḥ, Pañcālāḥ, <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālāḥ,</i> and <i>Bharatāḥ;</i> and the Mahābhārata consistently recognizes the royal family of the <b>Kurus</b> as a <a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a> family.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1202239">15</a></sup> It is therefore extremely probable that Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1202239">16</a></sup> is right in holding that the Bharatas in the times of the Brāhmaṇas were merging in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a> people.</p><p>The ritual practices of the Bharatas are repeatedly mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1202239">17</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1202239">18</a></sup> the  Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1202239">19</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1202239">20</a></sup> Already in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1202239">21</a></sup> there is mention made of <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="भारत">bhārata</a> (‘of the Bharatas’). In the Āprī hymns<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1202239">22</a></sup> occurs a goddess Bhāratī, the personified divine protective power of the Bharatas: her association in the hymns with <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> reflects the connexion of the Bharatas with the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202239">9</a></sup> Again, in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1202239">23</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> is referred to as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="भारत">bhārata</a>,</i> ‘priest of the Bharatas,’ and is invited to dispose of the offering <i>Manuṣvat Bharatavat,</i> ‘like <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>,’ ‘like <a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a>.’<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1202239">24</a></sup></p><p>In one or two passages<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1202239">25</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a> or Divodāsa and, on the other hand, <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a> or <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a> appear in a friendly relation. Possibly this points, as Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_1202239">26</a></sup> suggests, to the union of Bharatas and Pūrus with the Kurus.</p><p>A <a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a> is referred to in the fifth Maṇḍala of the Rigveda:<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_1202239">27</a></sup> who he was is uncertain.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202239">↑</a> iii. 53, 9. 12, 24; 33, 11, 12 (<a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वा">viśvā</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मित्र">mitra</a>, who is accordingly hailed as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषभ">ṛṣabha</a>,</i> ‘bull of the Bharatas,’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 17, 7); vii. 8, 4; 33, 6, in which passage a defeat of the Bharatas, and their rescue by the aid of <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>, is clearly referred to; not, as was formerly thought (<i>e.g.,</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 354; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 127), a defeat of the Bharatas by the Tṛtsus.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202239">↑</a> vi. 16, 4. 5. <i>Cf.</i> verse 19.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202239">↑</a> vii. 8, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202239">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 207. In <i>Buddha,</i> 405 <i>et seq.,</i> he accepted the identification of Ludwig.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202239">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 136 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202239">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 127. This is also Bloomfield's view (see <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, 41, 42).</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202239">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 124.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202239">↑</a> iii. 23, 4: in verse 2 <b>Devasravas</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="देववात">devavāta</a></b> are mentioned as Bhāratas, Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 410, n., mentions that in the Mahābhārata, iii. 6065, a tributary of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> is called <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिकी">kauśikī</a>, and the Kuśikas are, of course, the family of <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>, whose connexion with the Bharatas is beyond question.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202239">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 111. His view is that <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a> and the Bharatas were later comers than the Tṛtsus, who joined them as one people, the Vasiṣṭhas becoming the priests of the Bharatas. He suggests that the Vasiṣṭhas were not originally adherents of the IndraSoma cult, but were specially devoted to the <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a> cult; but there is no decisive evidence for either suggestion. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, as cited in n. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202239">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202239">↑</a> viii. 23 and 21.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202239">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 4, 11. 21.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1202239">↑</a> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 10, 2, and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 4, 2, the phrase is <i>eṣa vo, Bharatā, rājā;</i> the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension, xi. 3, 3; 6, 3, has <i>Kuravaḥ, Pañcālāḥ</i> (evidently as a joint people); Āpastamba, xviii. 12, 7, gives <i>Bharatāḥ, Kuravaḥ, Pañcālāḥ, <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālāḥ,</i> and <i>janatāḥ,</i> as alternatives, according to the people to whom the king belongs; the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 7, and the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 7, read <i>esa te janate rājā.</i> See Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 114, n.; von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 465.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1202239">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 409.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1202239">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 408. He points out (409, n.) that in the Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 4, only the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a> king. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनमेजय">janamejaya</a>,</b> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a> kings are mentioned without specification of the peoples over whom they ruled.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1202239">↑</a> xiv. 3, 13; xv. 5, 24, and perhaps xviii. 10, 8, on which see Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 28, n. 2; below, p. 98.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1202239">↑</a> ii. 25; iii. 18. The sense ‘mercenary soldier,’ here seen by the St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 2 (no longer mentioned in the Dictionary of Bo7htlingk), cannot be accepted. See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 254; Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 407, n. On the other hand, there is no mention of the Bharatas in the geographical lists of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 14). in the <a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Śāstra, or in the Buddhist texts. This means that the Bharatas were no longer a people, but a family or sub-tribe in a larger people.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1202239">↑</a> v. 4, 4, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1202239">↑</a> i. 27, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1202239">↑</a> ii. 7, 1. 5; iv. 25, 4; vi. 16, 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 9, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 2, 2. Roth thinks this epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> perhaps means ‘warlike,’ but this is unlikely.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1202239">↑</a> Rv. i. 22, 10; 142, 9; 188, 8; ii. 1, 11; 3, 8; iii. 4, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202239">↑</a> iii. 23, 4: in verse 2 <b>Devasravas</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="देववात">devavāta</a></b> are mentioned as Bhāratas, Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 410, n., mentions that in the Mahābhārata, iii. 6065, a tributary of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> is called <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिकी">kauśikī</a>, and the Kuśikas are, of course, the family of <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>, whose connexion with the Bharatas is beyond question.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1202239">↑</a> i. 4, 2, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1202239">↑</a> i. 5, 1, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1202239">↑</a> i. 112, 14; vii. 19, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_1202239">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 410.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_1202239"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_1202239">↑</a> v. 54, 14.</p><p>For a later legend of <a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a>, <i>cf.</i> Leumann, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 80 <i>et seq.;</i> von Bradke, <i>ibid.,</i> 498-503; and see Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 13, 338, 340, etc.</li></ol></div>

भरद्-वाज|भरद्वाज|भरद्|वाज|భరద్-వాజ|భరద్వాజ|భరద్|వాజ|ಭರದ್-ವಾಜ|ಭರದ್ವಾಜ|ಭರದ್|ವಾಜ|bharad-vāja|bharadvāja|bharad|vāja|bharad-vaaja|bharadvaaja|vaaja|_vei_120@2240|_vei2_pn097
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2239">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2241">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn094">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn097">2:097</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn098">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bharad-vāja</b> is the name of the reputed<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202240">1</a></sup> author of the sixth Maṇḍala of the Rigveda. The attribution is so far correct that <a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202240">2</a></sup> and the Bharadvājas<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202240">3</a></sup> are repeatedly mentioned as singers in that Maṇḍala. Judging by the tone of the references to <a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a>, he can hardly be deemed to have been a contemporary of any of the hymns.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202240">4</a></sup> According to the Pañcaviṃśa  <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202240">5</a></sup> he was the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> of <b>Divodāsa.</b> This interpretation is to be preferred to that of Roth,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202240">6</a></sup> who suggests that he and Divodāsa were identical. His connexion with the house of Divodāsa also appears from the statement of the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202240">7</a></sup> that <a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a> gave <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतर्दन">pratardana</a></b> the kingdom. It is unnecessary to suppose that the same <a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a> was meant in both cases, and that <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतर्दन">pratardana</a> was a son of Divodāsa: the later Saṃhitās refer to <a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a>, like the other great sages, irrespective of chronology.</p><p>The Bharadvājas in their poems mention <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बृबु">bṛbu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="बृसय">bṛsaya</a>,</b> and the <b>Pārāvatas.</b><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202240">8</a></sup> Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202240">9</a></sup> has pointed out that they are also connected with the <b>Sṛñjayas.</b> In particular, the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202240">10</a></sup> mentions that <a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a> gained largesse from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रस्तोक">prastoka</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सार्ञ्जय">sārñjaya</a></b> and <a class="vei_120a" href="बृबु">bṛbu</a>. But it is very doubtful if it is correct to place all these people and Divodāsa in Arachosia and Drangiana.</p><p><a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a> as an author and a seer is frequently referred to in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202240">11</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202240">12</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202240">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 4, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 10; Bṛhaddevatā, v. 102 <i>et seq.,</i> where he is said to be a son of <a class="vei_120a" href="बृहस्पति">bṛhaspati</a>, and a grandson of <a class="vei_120a" href="अंगिरस्">aṅgiras</a> (<i>cf.</i> Rv. vi. 2, 10; 11, 3, etc.); Arnold, <i>Vedic Metre,</i> 61, 62.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202240">↑</a> Rv. vi. 15, 3; 16, 5. 33; 17, 4; 31, 4; 48, 7. 13; 63, 10; 65, 6. See also Rv. i. 112, 13; 116, 18; x. 150, 5; 181, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202240">↑</a> Rv. vi. 10, 6; 16, 33; 17, 14; 23, 10; 25, 9; 35, 4; 47, 25; 59, 15. See also Rv. i. 59, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202240">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 210 212.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202240">↑</a> xv. 3, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202240">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> See Rv. i. 116, 18; vi. 16, 5; 31, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202240">↑</a> xxi. 10 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 478).</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202240">↑</a> vi. 61, 1-3.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202240">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 104.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202240">↑</a> xvi. 11, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202240">↑</a> Av. ii. 12, 2; iv. 29, 5; xviii. 3, 16; xix. 48, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvi. 19; xx. 9; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 7, 19; iv. 8, 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiii. 55, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202240"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202240">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 18; viii. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 10, 11, 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 2, 2; 4, 2; ii. 2, 2. 4, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xv. 1; xxix. 3; xxx. 9.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 128; Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 31.</li></ol></div>

भरन्त्|భరన్త్|ಭರನ್ತ್|bharant|_vei_120@2241|_vei2_pn098
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2240">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2242">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn097">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn098">2:098</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn099">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bharant,</b> ‘bearing,’ in the plural denotes in one passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202241"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202241">1</a></sup> according to Bo7htlingk,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202241"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202241">2</a></sup> following Sāyaṇa, ‘the warrior caste,’ but the sense is not certain. Weber<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202241"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202241">3</a></sup> was inclined to see a reference to the Bharatas, though the form of the word is that of the present participle.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202241"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202241">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202241"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202241">↑</a> xviii. 10, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202241"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202241">↑</a> Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202241"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202241">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 28, n. 2. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a>,</b> n. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202241"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202241">↑</a> <i>Bharatām,</i> interpreted by Sāyaṇa as <i>bharaṇaṃ kurvatām kṣatriyāṇām.</i></li></ol></div>

भरूजी|भरूजि|భరూజీ|భరూజి|ಭರೂಜೀ|ಭರೂಜಿ|bharūjī|bharūji|bharuujii|bharuuji|bharoojee|bharooji|_vei_120@2242|_vei2_pn098
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2241">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2243">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn097">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn098">2:098</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn099">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bharūjī</b> in one passage of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202242"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202242">1</a></sup> may denote, according to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202242"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202242">2</a></sup> a noxious animal. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202242"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202242">↑</a> ii. 24. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202242"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202242">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

भर्तृ|భర్తృ|ಭರ್ತೃ|bhartṛ|bhartR|bhartRi|bhartRu|bhartRRi|_vei_120@2243|_vei2_pn099
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2242">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2244">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn098">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn099">2:099</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn100">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhartṛ,</b> besides having the literal sense of ‘bearer,’ means ‘supporter’ or ‘master’ in the older literature;<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202243"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202243">1</a></sup> but it is doubtful whether the sense of ‘husband’ is ever found there. In one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202243"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202243">2</a></sup> ‘husband’ is certainly the most natural sense, but, as Delbrück<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202243"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202243">3</a></sup> correctly remarks, even there ‘father’ may be meant, since ‘mother’ is here and there<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202243"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202243">4</a></sup> called Bhartrī.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202243"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202243">↑</a> Av. xi. 7, 15; xviii. 2, 30; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 3, 4, 7 (where ‘husband’ is possible); iv. 6, 7, 21, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202243"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202243">↑</a> v. 58, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202243"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202243">↑</a> <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 415, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202243"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202243">↑</a> Av. v. 5, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 1, 4.</li></ol></div>

भलानस्|भलान|భలానస్|భలాన|ಭಲಾನಸ್|ಭಲಾನ|bhalānas|bhalāna|bhalaanas|bhalaana|_vei_120@2244|_vei2_pn099
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2243">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2245">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn098">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn099">2:099</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn100">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhalānas,</b> plural, is the name in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202244">1</a></sup> of one of the five tribes, <b>Pakthas,</b> Bhalānases, <b>Alinas, Viṣāṇins,</b> and <b>Śivas,</b> who are mentioned as ranged on the side<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202244">2</a></sup> of the enemies of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a></b> in the battle of the ten kings (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="दाशराज्ञ">dāśarājña</a></b>), not opposed to them, as Roth,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202244">3</a></sup> and at one time Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202244">4</a></sup> thought. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202244">5</a></sup> suggests as their original home East Kabulistan, comparing the name of the Bolan pass. This seems a reasonably probable view.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202244">↑</a> vii. 18, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202244">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 260, 261, who takes the form of the name to be <a class="vei_120a" href="भलान">bhalāna</a> (but the text of the Rv. has <i>bhalāna¤saḥ</i>), and who overlooks Zimmer's later view.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202244">↑</a> <i>Zur Litteratur und Geschichte des Weda,</i> 95.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202244">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 126.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202244">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 431. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 173, 207.</li></ol></div>

भव-त्रात शायस्थि|भवत्रात शायस्थि|भव|त्रात शायस्थि|భవ-త్రాత శాయస్థి|భవత్రాత శాయస్థి|భవ|త్రాత శాయస్థి|ಭವ-ತ್ರಾತ ಶಾಯಸ್ಥಿ|ಭವತ್ರಾತ ಶಾಯಸ್ಥಿ|ಭವ|ತ್ರಾತ ಶಾಯಸ್ಥಿ|bhava-trāta śāyasthi|bhavatrāta śāyasthi|bhava|trāta śāyasthi|bhava-traata shaayasthi|bhavatraata shaayasthi|traata shaayasthi|_vei_120@2245|_vei2_pn099
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2244">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2246">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn098">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn099">2:099</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn100">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhava-trāta Śāyasthi</b> is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202245"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202245">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202245"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202245">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372; Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 443.</li></ol></div>

भस्त्रा|భస్త్రా|ಭಸ್ತ್ರಾ|bhastrā|bhastraa|_vei_120@2246|_vei2_pn099
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2245">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2247">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn098">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn099">2:099</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn100">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhastrā</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 1, 2, 7; 6, 3, 16) denotes a leathern bottle or pouch.</p></div>

भाकुरि|భాకురి|ಭಾಕುರಿ|bhākuri|bhaakuri|_vei_120@2247|_vei2_pn099
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2246">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2248">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn098">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn099">2:099</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn100">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhākuri.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बेकुरा">bekurā</a>.</b> </p></div>

भाग-दुघ|भागदुघ|भाग|दुघ|భాగ-దుఘ|భాగదుఘ|భాగ|దుఘ|ಭಾಗ-ದುಘ|ಭಾಗದುಘ|ಭಾಗ|ದುಘ|bhāga-dugha|bhāgadugha|bhāga|dugha|bhaaga-dugha|bhaagadugha|bhaaga|_vei_120@2248|_vei2_pn100
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2247">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2249">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn099">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn100">2:100</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn101">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhāga-dugha,</b> ‘dealer out of portions,’ ‘distributor,’ is the name of one of the king's ‘jewels’ (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="रत्निन्">ratnin</a></b>) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202248">1</a></sup> and Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202248">2</a></sup> What his functions exactly were is uncertain. Sāyaṇa in some places<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202248">3</a></sup> renders the word by ‘tax-collector’ but in others<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202248">4</a></sup> as ‘carver,’ thus making this functionary either a revenue officer or a mere court official.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202248">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 9 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 4; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 5; iv. 3, 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202248">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 3, 5; iii. 4, 8, 1; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 2, 17; v. 3, 1, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202248">↑</a> On <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā and <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> and on Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 1, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202248">↑</a> On Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 2, 17.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 63, n.</li></ol></div>

भाग-वित्ति|भागवित्ति|भाग|वित्ति|భాగ-విత్తి|భాగవిత్తి|భాగ|విత్తి|ಭಾಗ-ವಿತ್ತಿ|ಭಾಗವಿತ್ತಿ|ಭಾಗ|ವಿತ್ತಿ|bhāga-vitti|bhāgavitti|bhāga|vitti|bhaaga-vitti|bhaagavitti|bhaaga|_vei_120@2249|_vei2_pn100
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2248">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2250">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn099">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn100">2:100</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn101">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhāga-vitti</b> (‘descendant of Bhagavitta’) is the patronymic of a teacher called Cūḍa<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202249"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202249">1</a></sup> or Cūla<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202249"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202249">2</a></sup> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202249"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202249">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 3, 17, 18 Mādhyaṃdina.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202249"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202249">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> vi. 3, 9 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</li></ol></div>

भाडितायन|భాడితాయన|ಭಾಡಿತಾಯನ|bhāḍitāyana|bhaaDitaayana|_vei_120@2250|_vei2_pn100
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2249">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2251">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn099">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn100">2:100</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn101">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhāḍitāyana,</b> ‘descendant of Bhaḍita,’ is the patronymic of <b>Śākadāsa</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202250"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202250">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202250"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202250">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373.</li></ol></div>

भानुमन्त् औपमन्यव|భానుమన్త్ ఔపమన్యవ|ಭಾನುಮನ್ತ್ ಔಪಮನ್ಯವ|bhānumant aupamanyava|bhaanumant aupamanyava|_vei_120@2251|_vei2_pn100
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2250">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2252">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn099">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn100">2:100</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn101">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhānumant Aupamanyava</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपमन्यु">upamanyu</a></b>’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b>Ānandaja,</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202251">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202251">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372.</li></ol></div>

भाय-जात्य|भायजात्य|भाय|जात्य|భాయ-జాత్య|భాయజాత్య|భాయ|జాత్య|ಭಾಯ-ಜಾತ್ಯ|ಭಾಯಜಾತ್ಯ|ಭಾಯ|ಜಾತ್ಯ|bhāya-jātya|bhāyajātya|bhāya|jātya|bhaaya-jaatya|bhaayajaatya|bhaaya|jaatya|_vei_120@2252|_vei2_pn100
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2251">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2253">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn099">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn100">2:100</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn101">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhāya-jātya,</b> ‘descendant of Bhayajāta,’ is the patronymic of <b>Nikothaka</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202252">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202252">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373; Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 444.</li></ol></div>

भारत|భారత|ಭಾರತ|bhārata|bhaarata|_vei_120@2253|_vei2_pn100
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2252">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2254">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn099">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn100">2:100</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn101">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhārata.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a>.</b></p></div>

भारद्-वाज|भारद्वाज|भारद्|वाज|భారద్-వాజ|భారద్వాజ|భారద్|వాజ|ಭಾರದ್-ವಾಜ|ಭಾರದ್ವಾಜ|ಭಾರದ್|ವಾಜ|bhārad-vāja|bhāradvāja|bhārad|vāja|bhaarad-vaaja|bhaaradvaaja|bhaarad|vaaja|_vei_120@2254|_vei2_pn100
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2253">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2255">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn099">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn100">2:100</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn101">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhārad-vāja,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of many teachers. In the Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, Bhāradvājas are mentioned as  pupils of <a class="vei_120a" href="भारद्वाज">bhāradvāja</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202254"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202254">1</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पाराशर्य">pārāśarya</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202254"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202254">2</a></sup> <b>Balākākauśika,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202254"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202254">3</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202254"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202254">4</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आसुरायण">āsurāyaṇa</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202254"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202254">5</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बैजवापायन">baijavāpāyana</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202254"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202254">6</a></sup> A <a class="vei_120a" href="भारद्वाज">bhāradvāja</a> occurs in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202254"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202254">7</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शूष वाह्नेय">śūṣa vāhneya</a></b> is mentioned as a <a class="vei_120a" href="भारद्वाज">bhāradvāja</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202254"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202254">8</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202254"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202254">↑</a> ii. 5, 21; iv. 5, 27 (Mādhyaṃdina = ii. 6, 2 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202254"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202254">↑</a> ii. 6, 2 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202254"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202254">↑</a> iv. 5, 27 Mādhyaṃdina.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202254"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202254">↑</a> ii. 5, 21; iv. 5, 27 (Mādhyaṃdina = ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202254"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202254">↑</a> ii. 5, 21; iv. 5, 27 Mādhyaṃdina.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202254"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202254">↑</a> ii. 5, 21; iv. 5, 27 Mādhyaṃdina.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202254"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202254">↑</a> v. 61, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202254"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202254">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373.</li></ol></div>

भारद्वाजायन|భారద్వాజాయన|ಭಾರದ್ವಾಜಾಯನ|bhāradvājāyana|bhaaradvaajaayana|_vei_120@2255|_vei2_pn101
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2254">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2256">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn100">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn101">2:101</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn102">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhāradvājāyana,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202255">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202255">↑</a> x. 12, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ix. 9. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 61, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

भारद्वाजी-पुत्र|भारद्वाजीपुत्र|भारद्वाजी|भारद्वाजि|पुत्र|భారద్వాజీ-పుత్ర|భారద్వాజీపుత్ర|భారద్వాజీ|భారద్వాజి|పుత్ర|ಭಾರದ್ವಾಜೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಭಾರದ್ವಾಜೀಪುತ್ರ|ಭಾರದ್ವಾಜೀ|ಭಾರದ್ವಾಜಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|bhāradvājī-putra|bhāradvājīputra|bhāradvājī|bhāradvāji|putra|bhaaradvaajii-putra|bhaaradvaajiiputra|bhaaradvaajii|bhaaradvaaji|bhaaradvaajee-putra|bhaaradvaajeeputra|bhaaradvaajee|_vei_120@2256|_vei2_pn101
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2255">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2257">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn100">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn101">2:101</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn102">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhāradvājī-putra,</b> ‘son of a female descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a>,</b>’ is the metronymic of several teachers in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, pupils of <b>Pāraśarīputra,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202256"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202256">1</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पैंगीपुत्र">paiṅgīputra</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202256"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202256">2</a></sup> and <b>Vātsīmāṇḍavīputra</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202256"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202256">3</a></sup> respectively.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202256"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202256">↑</a> vi. 4, 31 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 5, 2 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202256"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202256">↑</a> vi. 4, 30 Mādhyaṃdina.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202256"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202256">↑</a> <i>Ibid.</i></li></ol></div>

भार्गव|భార్గవ|ಭಾರ್ಗವ|bhārgava|bhaargava|_vei_120@2257|_vei2_pn101
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2256">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2258">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn100">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn101">2:101</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn102">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhārgava,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भृगु">bhṛgu</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of several teachers, including <b><a class="vei_120a" href="च्यवन">cyavana</a></b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202257"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202257">1</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गृत्समद">gṛtsamada</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202257"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202257">2</a></sup> Other Bhārgavas are also mentioned without dication of their personal names.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202257"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202257">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202257"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202257">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 5, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>. viii. 21.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202257"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202257">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxii. 4 (with a <i>varia lectio,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="बाभ्रव">bābhrava</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202257"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202257">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 18, 1; Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, vii. 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 2, 1. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रश्न">praśna</a> Upanisad, i. 1 (<a class="vei_120a" href="वैदर्भि">vaidarbhi</a>), etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 2, 23; 9, 19, 39, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> xxxv.</li></ol></div>

भार्गायण|భార్గాయణ|ಭಾರ್ಗಾಯಣ|bhārgāyaṇa|bhaargaayaNa|_vei_120@2258|_vei2_pn101
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2257">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2259">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn100">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn101">2:101</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn102">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhārgāyaṇa,</b> ‘descendant of Bharga,’ is the patronymic of <b>Sutvan</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 28).</p></div>

भार्म्य्-अश्व|भार्म्यश्व|भार्म्य्|अश्व|భార్మ్య్-అశ్వ|భార్మ్యశ్వ|భార్మ్య్|అశ్వ|ಭಾರ್ಮ್ಯ್-ಅಶ್ವ|ಭಾರ್ಮ್ಯಶ್ವ|ಭಾರ್ಮ್ಯ್|ಅಶ್ವ|bhārmy-aśva|bhārmyaśva|bhārmy|aśva|bhaarmy-ashva|bhaarmyashva|bhaarmy|ashva|_vei_120@2259|_vei2_pn101
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2258">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2260">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn100">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn101">2:101</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn102">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhārmy-aśva,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="भृम्यश्व">bhṛmyaśva</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मुद्गल">mudgala</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> (ix. 23) and the Brhaddevatā (vi. 46; viii. 12). </p></div>

भार्या|భార్యా|ಭಾರ್ಯಾ|bhāryā|bhaaryaa|_vei_120@2260|_vei2_pn102
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2259">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2261">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn101">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn102">2:102</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn103">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhāryā,</b> later a common expression for ‘wife,’ does not occur in that sense at all in the Saṃhitās. It first appears, according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202260">1</a></sup> where, however, Delbrück<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202260">2</a></sup> suggests that merely a member of the household (‘who is to be maintained’) may be meant. In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202260">3</a></sup> however, the two wives of <a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a> are so designated.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202260">↑</a> vii. 9. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202260">↑</a> <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 415. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 29, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202260">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 4, 1; iv. 5, 1.</li></ol></div>

भालन्दन|భాలన్దన|ಭಾಲನ್ದನ|bhālandana|bhaalandana|_vei_120@2261|_vei2_pn102
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2260">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2262">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn101">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn102">2:102</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn103">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhālandana,</b> ‘descendant of Bhalandana,’ is the patronymic of <b>Vatsaprī</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,’ the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202261"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202261">2</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202261"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202261">3</a></sup></p><p>^1 v. 2, 1, 6.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="2"><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202261"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202261">↑</a> xix. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202261"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202261">↑</a> xii. 11, 25; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 59.</li></ol></div>

भालुकी-पुत्र|भालुकीपुत्र|भालुकी|भालुकि|पुत्र|భాలుకీ-పుత్ర|భాలుకీపుత్ర|భాలుకీ|భాలుకి|పుత్ర|ಭಾಲುಕೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಭಾಲುಕೀಪುತ್ರ|ಭಾಲುಕೀ|ಭಾಲುಕಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|bhālukī-putra|bhālukīputra|bhālukī|bhāluki|putra|bhaalukii-putra|bhaalukiiputra|bhaalukii|bhaaluki|bhaalukee-putra|bhaalukeeputra|bhaalukee|_vei_120@2262|_vei2_pn102
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2261">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2263">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn101">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn102">2:102</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn103">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhālukī-putra,</b> ‘son of <a class="vei_120a" href="भालुकी">bhālukī</a>,’ is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रौञ्चिकीपुत्र">krauñcikīputra</a></b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202262"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202262">1</a></sup> or of <b>Prācīnayogīputra,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202262"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202262">2</a></sup> in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202262"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202262">↑</a> vi. 5, 2 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202262"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202262">↑</a> vi. 4, 32 Mādhyaṃdina.</li></ol></div>

भाल्ल|భాల్ల|ಭಾಲ್ಲ|bhālla|bhaalla|_vei_120@2263|_vei2_pn102
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2262">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2264">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn101">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn102">2:102</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn103">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhālla</b> is the name or patronymic of a teacher who bears the patronymic <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रातृद">prātṛda</a> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 31, 4).</p></div>

भाल्लवि|భాల్లవి|ಭಾಲ್ಲವಿ|bhāllavi|bhaallavi|_vei_120@2264|_vei2_pn102
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2263">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2265">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn101">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn102">2:102</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn103">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhāllavi</b> is the name of a school mentioned as authorities in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (ii. 2, 4).</p></div>

भाल्लविन्|భాల్లవిన్|ಭಾಲ್ಲವಿನ್|bhāllavin|bhaallavin|_vei_120@2265|_vei2_pn102
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2264">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2266">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn101">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn102">2:102</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn103">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhāllavin,</b> ‘pupil of Bhallavin,’ is the name of a school of teachers mentioned in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202265">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202265">↑</a> ii. 4, 7 (spelt Bhāllabin). <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 44; 2, 100; 390; <a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, v. 1; Anupada <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 1; vii. 12; Bṛhaddevatā v. 23. 159.</li></ol></div>

भाल्लवेय|భాల్లవేయ|ಭಾಲ್ಲವೇಯ|bhāllaveya|bhaallaveya|_vei_120@2266|_vei2_pn103
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2265">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2267">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn102">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn103">2:103</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn104">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhāllaveya,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भाल्लवि">bhāllavi</a>,</b>’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202266">1</a></sup> is the patronymic of <b>Indradyumna</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1202266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1202266">1b</a></sup> and the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2b_vei_1202266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2b_vei_1202266">2b</a></sup> Probably the same person is meant by the <a class="vei_120a" href="भाल्लवेय">bhāllaveya</a>, who is cited frequently as an authority in the same <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3b_vei_1202266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3b_vei_1202266">3b</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202266">↑</a> x. 6, 1, 1.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1202266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1202266">↑</a> x. 6, 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2b_vei_1202266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2b_vei_1202266">↑</a> v. 11, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3b_vei_1202266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3b_vei_1202266">↑</a> i. 7, 3, 19; ii. 1, 4, 6; xiii. 4, 2, 3; 5, 3, 4.</li></ol></div>

भावयव्य|భావయవ్య|ಭಾವಯವ್ಯ|bhāvayavya|bhaavayavya|_vei_120@2267|_vei2_pn103
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2266">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2268">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn102">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn103">2:103</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn104">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhāvayavya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भाव्य">bhāvya</a>.</b></p></div>

भाव्य|భావ్య|ಭಾವ್ಯ|bhāvya|bhaavya|_vei_120@2268|_vei2_pn103
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2267">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2269">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn102">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn103">2:103</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn104">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhāvya</b> is the name of a patron, as it seems, in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202268">1</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202268">2</a></sup> the form given is <a class="vei_120a" href="भावयव्य">bhāvayavya</a>, being a patronymic of <b>Svanaya,</b> who is the patron of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a>.</b> This combination is borne out by the Rigveda, where <a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a> and Svanaya are mentioned in the same verse,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202268">3</a></sup> while Svanaya must be meant in the verse of the same hymn,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202268">4</a></sup> where <a class="vei_120a" href="भाव्य">bhāvya</a> is mentioned as ‘living on the Sindhu’ (Indus). Roth's<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202268">5</a></sup> view that <a class="vei_120a" href="भाव्य">bhāvya</a> here is perhaps a gerundive meaning to be ‘reverenced’ is not probable. Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202268">6</a></sup> thinks Svanaya was connected with the <b>Nahuṣas.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202268">↑</a> i. 126, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ix. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202268">↑</a> xvi. 11, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Bṛhaddevatā, iii. 140.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202268">↑</a> i. 126, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202268">↑</a> i. 126, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202268">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 1 f.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202268"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202268">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 151.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Episches im Vedischen Ritual,</i> 22; Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 128.</li></ol></div>

भाषा|భాషా|ಭಾಷಾ|bhāṣā|bhaaShaa|_vei_120@2269|_vei2_pn103
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2268">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2270">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn102">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn103">2:103</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn104">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhāṣā</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202269">1</a></sup> and Pāṇini<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202269">2</a></sup> denotes the ordinary speech of the day as opposed to Vedic language. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वाच्">vāc</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202269">↑</a> i. 4, 5. <i>Cf.</i> ii. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202269">↑</a> iii. 2, 108; vi. 1, 181. <i>Cf.</i> Franke, Bezzenberger's <i>Beiträge,</i> 17, 54 <i>et seq.,</i> who distinguishes the <a class="vei_120a" href="भाषा">bhāṣā</a> as the speech of conversational use from the language regulated by Pāṇini's rules. But see Wackernagel, <i>Altindische Grammatik,</i> 1, xliv; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 179, 180.</li></ol></div>

भास|భాస|ಭಾಸ|bhāsa|bhaasa|_vei_120@2270|_vei2_pn103
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2269">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2271">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn102">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn103">2:103</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn104">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhāsa</b> is the name of a bird of prey in the Adbhuta <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202270"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202270">1</a></sup> and often in the Epic. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202270"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202270">↑</a> vi. 8, See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 40.</li></ol></div>

भिक्षा|భిక్షా|ಭಿಕ್ಷಾ|bhikṣā|bhikShaa|bhixaa|_vei_120@2271|_vei2_pn104
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2270">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2272">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn103">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn104">2:104</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn106">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhikṣā,</b> ‘begging,’ is one of the duties of the <b>Brahmacārin</b> according to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202271"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202271">1</a></sup> The word has also the sense of ‘alms,’ as that which is obtained by begging, in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202271"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202271">2</a></sup> According to the St. Petersburg Dictionary,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202271"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202271">3</a></sup> it has this sense in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202271"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202271">4</a></sup> also, but the correct reading there is probably <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आमिक्षा">āmikṣā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202271"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202271">↑</a> xi. 3, 3, 7. <i>Cf.</i> a <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> in Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 9, etc.; and <i>bhikṣācarya,</i> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 4, 1; iv. 4, 26.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202271"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202271">↑</a> xi. 5, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202271"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202271">↑</a> <i>s.v.</i> 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202271"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202271">↑</a> viii. 8, 5, where the scholiast explains the word by ‘perfumes, garlands, food,’ etc. (<i>gandhamālyānnādi</i>).</li></ol></div>

भिक्षु|భిక్షు|ಭಿಕ್ಷು|bhikṣu|bhikShu|bhixu|_vei_120@2272|_vei2_pn104
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2271">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2273">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn103">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn104">2:104</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn106">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhikṣu,</b> ‘beggar,’ is a term not found in Vedic literature. The begging of the <b>Brahmacārin</b> is quite a different thing from the duties of the <a class="vei_120a" href="भिक्षु">bhikṣu</a> in the later system of the Āśramas (religious stages of life), when the Brahmin in the last stage of his life, after leaving his home and family, lives on alms alone. See 1. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.</b></p></div>

भित्ति|భిత్తి|ಭಿತ್ತಿ|bhitti|_vei_120@2273|_vei2_pn104
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2272">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2274">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn103">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn104">2:104</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn106">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhitti</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202273"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202273">1</a></sup> denotes a mat made of split reeds.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202273"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202273">↑</a> iii. 5, 3, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Sāṅkhāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 3, 24.</li></ol></div>

भिषज्|భిషజ్|ಭಿಷಜ್|bhiṣaj|bhiShaj|_vei_120@2274|_vei2_pn104
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2273">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2275">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn103">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn104">2:104</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn106">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhiṣaj,</b> ‘physician,’ is a word of common occurrence in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202274">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202274">2</a></sup> There is no trace whatever in the former text of the profession being held in disrepute: the Aśvins,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202274">3</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202274">4</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="रुद्र">rudra</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202274">5</a></sup> are all called physicians. On the other hand, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> literature<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202274">6</a></sup> this profession is utterly  despised. This dislike is found as early as the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202274">7</a></sup> where the Aśvins are condemned because of their having to do with the practice of medicine (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="भेषज">bheṣaja</a></i>), on the ground that it brings them too much among men, an allusion to the caste dislike of promiscuous contact.</p><p>The Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202274">8</a></sup> contains a hymn in which a physician celebrates his plants and their healing powers. Moreover, wonderful cures are referred to as performed by the Aśvins: the healing of the lame<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202274">9</a></sup> and of the blind;<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202274">10</a></sup> the rejuvenation of the aged <b><a class="vei_120a" href="च्यवन">cyavana</a></b><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202274">11</a></sup> and of <b>Puraṃdhi's</b> husband;<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202274">12</a></sup> the giving of an iron leg (<i>jaṅghā āyasī</i>) to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्पला">viśpalā</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202274">13</a></sup> a deed only more wonderful if we assume that <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्पला">viśpalā</a> was a mare, as has been suggested by Pischel.<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1202274">14</a></sup> It would in all probability be a mistake to assume<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1202274">15</a></sup> that the Vedic Indians had any surgical skill: they no doubt applied simples to wounds,<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1202274">16</a></sup> but both their medicien and their surgery must have been most primitive. All that the Atharvaveda shows in regard to medicine is the use of herbs combined with spells,<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1202274">17</a></sup> and of water (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जलाष">jalāṣa</a></b>), remedies Indo-European in character, but not of much scientific value. On the other hand, the knowledge of anatomy shown (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शरीर">śarīra</a></b>), though betraying grave inaccuracies, is not altogether insignificant; but that was due no doubt mainly to the practice of dissecting animals at the sacrifice.</p><p>There is some evidence in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1202274">18</a></sup> that the practice of medicine was already a profession; this is supported by the inclusion of a physician in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1202274">19</a></sup> According to  Bloomfield,<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1202274">20</a></sup> a hymn of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1202274">21</a></sup> contains a physician's deprecation of the use of home-made remedies instead of reliance on his professional training.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202274">↑</a> ii. 33, 4; vi. 50, 7; ix. 112, 1; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="भेषज">bheṣaja</a>,</i> adjective, ii. 33, 7; x. 137, 6; substantive, i. 23, 19. 20; ii. 33, 2. 4; vi. 74, 3; vii. 46, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202274">↑</a> Av. v. 29, 1; vi. 24, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 4, 9, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 5; xix. 12. 88; xxx. 10, etc.; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="भेषज">bheṣaja</a>,</i> adjective, Av. vi. 109, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 45, etc.; substantive, Av. v. 29, 1; vi. 21, 2; xi. 1, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202274">↑</a> Rv. i. 116, 16; 157, 6; viii. 18, 8; 86, 1; x. 39, 3. 5; Av. vii. 53, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202274">↑</a> See Rv. i. 24, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202274">↑</a> Rv. ii. 33, 4. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202274">↑</a> See Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 6, 18, 20; 19, 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvii. 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiv. 2, 19; Viṣṇu, li. 10; lxxxii. 9; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 1.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202274">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 4, 9, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 6, 2; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 5, 14; Bloomfield, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> xxxix, xl.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202274">↑</a> x. 97.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202274">↑</a> Rv. i. 112, 8; x. 39, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202274">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> the case of <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋज्राश्व">ṛjrāśva</a>, Rv. i. 116, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202274">↑</a> Rv. x. 39, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202274">↑</a> i. 116, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202274">↑</a> Rv. i. 116, 15, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1202274">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 171 <i>et seq.;</i> 305.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1202274">↑</a> As Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 398, is inclined to do.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1202274">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. ix. 112, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1202274">↑</a> So it is said in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 9, 10: <i>bheṣajaṃ vā Ātharvaṇāni,</i> ‘the <a class="vei_120a" href="अथर्वन्">atharvan</a> hymns are medicine’; xvi. 10, 10; and <i>cf. ibid.,</i> xxiii. 16, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 5 and 2. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भिषज्">bhiṣaj</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1202274">↑</a> ix. 112, where a profession must be meant. <i>Ibid.,</i> 3, refers to the fees of the physician. <i>Cf.</i> also x. 97, 4. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1202274">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 4, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1202274">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 456.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1202274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1202274">↑</a> v. 30, 5. But this sense is doubtful. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 277.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 397-399; Bloomfield, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>., passim</i> (see references on p. 697); <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 59 <i>et seq.;</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 420 <i>et seq.;</i> Jolly, <i>Medicin,</i> 16, 17; Winternitz, <i>Nature,</i> 1898, 233-235; Caland, <i>Altindisches Zauberritual, passim.</i></li></ol></div>

भिषज् आथर्वण|भिषज् आथर्वण|భిషజ్ ఆథర్వణ_౨|భిషజ్ ఆథర్వణ|ಭಿಷಜ್ ಆಥರ್ವಣ_೨|ಭಿಷಜ್ ಆಥರ್ವಣ|bhiṣaj ātharvaṇa_2|bhiṣaj ātharvaṇa|bhiShaj aatharvaNa_2|bhiShaj aatharvaNa|_vei_120@2275|_vei2_pn106
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2274">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2276">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn104">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn106">2:106</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn107">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Bhiṣaj Ātharvaṇa</b> is the name of a mythic physician mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202275"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202275">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202275"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202275">↑</a> xvi. 3 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 459). <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> xxi; <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 181.</li></ol></div>

भीम वैदर्भ|భీమ వైదర్భ|ಭೀಮ ವೈದರ್ಭ|bhīma vaidarbha|bhiima vaidarbha|bheema vaidarbha|_vei_120@2276|_vei2_pn106
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2275">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2277">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn104">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn106">2:106</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn107">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhīma Vaidarbha</b> (‘prince of Vidarbha’) is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vii. 34) as having received instruction regarding the substitute for the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> juice, through a succession of teachers, from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्वत">parvata</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नारद">nārada</a>.</b></p></div>

भीम-सेन|भीमसेन|भीम|सेन|భీమ-సేన|భీమసేన|భీమ|సేన|ಭೀಮ-ಸೇನ|ಭೀಮಸೇನ|ಭೀಮ|ಸೇನ|bhīma-sena|bhīmasena|bhīma|sena|bhiima-sena|bhiimasena|bhiima|bheema-sena|bheemasena|bheema|_vei_120@2277|_vei2_pn106
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2276">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2278">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn104">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn106">2:106</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn107">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhīma-sena</b> is the name of one of the brothers of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनमेजय">janamejaya</a>,</b> the <b>Pārikṣitīyas,</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202277"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202277">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202277"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202277">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 3. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 9, 3.</li></ol></div>

भुज्यु|भुज्यु|భుజ్యు_౧|భుజ్యు|ಭುಜ್ಯು_೧|ಭುಜ್ಯು|bhujyu_1|bhujyu|_vei_120@2278|_vei2_pn106
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2277">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2279">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn104">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn106">2:106</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn107">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Bhujyu</b> denotes, according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, an ‘adder’ in two passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202278">1</a></sup> and one of the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202278">2</a></sup> But the sense is uncertain in all these passages.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202278">↑</a> iv. 27, 4; x. 95, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202278">↑</a> xviii. 42.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 126, who takes <i><a class="vei_120a" href="भुज्यु">bhujyu</a></i> in Rv. x. 95, 8, as meaning ‘ardent,’ ‘rutting.’</li></ol></div>

भुज्यु|भुज्यु|భుజ్యు_౨|భుజ్యు|ಭುಜ್ಯು_೨|ಭುಜ್ಯು|bhujyu_2|bhujyu|_vei_120@2279|_vei2_pn106
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2278">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2280">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn104">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn106">2:106</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn107">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Bhujyu</b> is the name of a man, son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुग्र">tugra</a>,</b> who is repeatedly mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202279">1</a></sup> as saved from the deep by the Aśvins. According to Bühler,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202279">2</a></sup> the passages refer to  <a class="vei_120a" href="भुज्यु">bhujyu</a> being saved from shipwreck during a voyage in the Indian Ocean, but the evidence is inadequate to support this conclusion. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="समुद्र">samudra</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202279">↑</a> i. 112, 6, 20; 116, 3; 117, 14; 119, 4; vi. 62, 6; vii. 68, 7; 69, 7; x. 40, 7; 65, 12; 143, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202279">↑</a> <i>Indische Palœographie,</i> 17.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Baunack, Kuhn's <i>Zeitschrift,</i> 35, 485; Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 214; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 16, n. 5; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 244, 245; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 52.</li></ol></div>

भुज्यु लाह्यायनि|భుజ్యు లాహ్యాయని|ಭುಜ್ಯು ಲಾಹ್ಯಾಯನಿ|bhujyu lāhyāyani|bhujyu laahyaayani|_vei_120@2280|_vei2_pn107
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2279">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2281">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn106">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn107">2:107</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn108">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhujyu Lāhyāyani</b> (‘descendant of Lahyāyana’) is the name of a teacher, a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a>,</b> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iii. 3, 1).</p></div>

भुरिज्|భురిజ్|ಭುರಿಜ್|bhurij|_vei_120@2281|_vei2_pn107
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2280">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2282">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn106">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn107">2:107</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn108">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhurij</b> (used in the dual only) is a word of somewhat doubtful sense. Roth<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202281"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202281">1</a></sup> regarded it as meaning in some passages<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202281"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202281">2</a></sup> ‘scissors,’ and in others<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202281"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202281">3</a></sup> an apparatus consisting of two arms used by the chariot-maker for fixing the wood at which he worked, being of the nature of a carpenter's vice. See also <b>Ksura</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202281"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202281">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 466.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202281"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202281">↑</a> Rv. viii. 4, 16; Av. xx. 127, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202281"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202281">↑</a> Rv. iv. 2, 14; ix. 26, 4; 71, 5. where Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 239243, considers that the shafts of the chariot are meant (<i>cf.</i> Gobhila <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 4, 31, whence it appears that the chariot-pole, spoken of as having two arms, was forked). The same view regarding the passages cited in n. 2 gives the sense of a stropping apparatus, consisting of two pieces of wood, between which a grindstone moves.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 252, 255.</li></ol></div>

भूत-विद्या|भूतविद्या|भूत|विद्या|భూత-విద్యా|భూతవిద్యా|భూత|విద్యా|ಭೂತ-ವಿದ್ಯಾ|ಭೂತವಿದ್ಯಾ|ಭೂತ|ವಿದ್ಯಾ|bhūta-vidyā|bhūtavidyā|bhūta|vidyā|bhuuta-vidyaa|bhuutavidyaa|bhuuta|vidyaa|bhoota-vidyaa|bhootavidyaa|bhoota|_vei_120@2282|_vei2_pn107
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2281">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2283">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn106">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn107">2:107</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn108">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhūta-vidyā</b> is one of the sciences enumerated in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202282"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202282">1</a></sup> It seems to mean the ‘science of creatures’ that trouble men, and of the means of warding them off, ‘demonology.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202282"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202282">↑</a> vii. 1, 2. 4; 2, 1; 7, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Little, <i>Grammatical Index,</i> 115.</li></ol></div>

भूत-वीर|भूतवीर|भूत|वीर|భూత-వీర|భూతవీర|భూత|వీర|ಭೂತ-ವೀರ|ಭೂತವೀರ|ಭೂತ|ವೀರ|bhūta-vīra|bhūtavīra|bhūta|vīra|bhuuta-viira|bhuutaviira|bhuuta|viira|bhoota-veera|bhootaveera|bhoota|veera|_vei_120@2283|_vei2_pn107
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2282">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2284">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn106">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn107">2:107</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn108">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhūta-vīra</b> is the name of a family of priests who, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202283"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202283">1</a></sup> were employed by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनमेजय">janamejaya</a></b> to the exclusion of the <b>Kaśyapas.</b> A family of the latter, the <b>Asitamṛgas,</b> however, won back the favour of <a class="vei_120a" href="जनमेजय">janamejaya</a>, and ousted the Bhūtavīras. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202283"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202283">↑</a> vii. 27. <i>Cf.</i> Roth, <i>Zur Litteratur und Geschichte des Weda,</i> 118; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 43, 344, n. 3; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 437 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

भूतांश|భూతాంశ|ಭೂತಾಂಶ|bhūtāṃśa|bhuutaaMsha|bhootaaMsha|_vei_120@2284|_vei2_pn108
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2283">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2285">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn107">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn108">2:108</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn109">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhūtāṃśa</b> is in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202284"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202284">1</a></sup> the name of a poet, a descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="कश्यप">kaśyapa</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202284"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202284">↑</a> x. 106, 11. See <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, xii, 41; Bṛhaddevatā. viii. 18. 19; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 133.</li></ol></div>

भूति|భూతి|ಭೂತಿ|bhūti|bhuuti|bhooti|_vei_120@2285|_vei2_pn108
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2284">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2286">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn107">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn108">2:108</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn109">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhūti</b> is the term used in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202285"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202285">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202285"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202285">2</a></sup> for ‘prosperity.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202285"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202285">↑</a> viii. 59, 7. <i>Cf.</i> i. 161, 1 (both late passages).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202285"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202285">↑</a> Av. ix. 6, 45; x. 3, 17; 6, 9; xi. 7, 22; 8, 21; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 1, 1; 3, 5, etc.; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="भूति">bhūti</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="काम">kāma</a>,</i> ‘desiring prosperity,’ <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 1, 1; 2, 3, 3; v. 1, 9, 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

भूमि|भूमी|భూమి|భూమీ|ಭೂಮಿ|ಭೂಮೀ|bhūmi|bhūmī|bhuumi|bhuumii|bhoomi|bhoomee|_vei_120@2286|_vei2_pn108
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2285">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2287">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn107">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn108">2:108</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn109">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhūmi</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भूमी">bhūmī</a></b> is a common word for ‘earth’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202286"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202286">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202286"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202286">2</a></sup> being practically a synonym of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पृथिवी">pṛthivī</a>.</b> It is also used of the land given by the god to the Āryan,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202286"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202286">3</a></sup> and of grants of land.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202286"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202286">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202286"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202286">↑</a> i. 64, 5; 161, 14; ii. 14, 7, etc. So in x. 18, 10, ‘mother earth’ receives the remains of the dead.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202286"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202286">↑</a> Av. vi. 2, 1, where it is said that the <a class="vei_120a" href="भूमि">bhūmi</a> is the highest of the three earths (<a class="vei_120a" href="पृथिवी">pṛthivī</a>); xi. 7, 14, where nine earths and seas are mentioned; ii. 9, 4; vi. 8, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202286"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202286">↑</a> Rv. iv. 26, 2. <i>Cf.</i> vi. 47, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202286"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202286">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 4, 24; 6, 2, 18.</li></ol></div>

भूमि-दुन्दुभि|भूमिदुन्दुभि|भूमि|दुन्दुभि|భూమి-దున్దుభి|భూమిదున్దుభి|భూమి|దున్దుభి|ಭೂಮಿ-ದುನ್ದುಭಿ|ಭೂಮಿದುನ್ದುಭಿ|ಭೂಮಿ|ದುನ್ದುಭಿ|bhūmi-dundubhi|bhūmidundubhi|bhūmi|dundubhi|bhuumi-dundubhi|bhuumidundubhi|bhuumi|bhoomi-dundubhi|bhoomidundubhi|bhoomi|_vei_120@2287|_vei2_pn108
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2286">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2288">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn107">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn108">2:108</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn109">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhūmi-dundubhi,</b> ‘earth drum,’ denotes a <a class="vei_120a" href="पित्">pit</a> covered with a hide used at the Mahāvrata rite, and mentioned in the Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202287"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202287">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202287"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202287">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202287"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202287">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 9, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxiv. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202287"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202287">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, v. 1, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 277, n. 14.</li></ol></div>

भूमि-पाश|भूमिपाश|भूमि|पाश|భూమి-పాశ|భూమిపాశ|భూమి|పాశ|ಭೂಮಿ-ಪಾಶ|ಭೂಮಿಪಾಶ|ಭೂಮಿ|ಪಾಶ|bhūmi-pāśa|bhūmipāśa|bhūmi|pāśa|bhuumi-paasha|bhuumipaasha|bhuumi|paasha|bhoomi-paasha|bhoomipaasha|bhoomi|_vei_120@2288|_vei2_pn108
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2287">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2289">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn107">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn108">2:108</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn109">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhūmi-pāśa,</b> ‘earth net,’ is the name of a plant in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, probably some sort of creeper.</p><p>^1 xiii. 8, 1, 16. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 427, n. 1.</p></div>

भृगवाण|భృగవాణ|ಭೃಗವಾಣ|bhṛgavāṇa|bhRgavaaNa|bhRigavaaNa|bhRugavaaNa|bhRRigavaaNa|_vei_120@2289|_vei2_pn108
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2288">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2290">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn107">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn108">2:108</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn109">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhṛgavāṇa</b> is found in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202289">1</a></sup> apparently<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202289">2</a></sup> as a name of a man who is called <b>Śobha.</b> Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202289">3</a></sup> however, thinks that his name was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="घोष">ghoṣa</a>.</b> Elsewhere the word appears as an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>, doubtless in allusion to his cult by the <b>Bhṛgus.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202289">↑</a> i. 120, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202289">↑</a> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> i. 4; 2, 92.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202289"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202289">↑</a> <i>Über Methode bei Interpretation,</i> 4.</li></ol></div>

भृगु|భృగు|ಭೃಗು|bhṛgu|bhRgu|bhRigu|bhRugu|bhRRigu|_vei_120@2290|_vei2_pn109
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2289">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2291">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn108">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn109">2:109</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn110">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhṛgu</b> is a sage of almost entirely mythical character in the Rigveda and later. He counts as a son of <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202290">1</a></sup> bearing the patronymic <a class="vei_120a" href="वारुणि">vāruṇi</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202290">2</a></sup> In the plural the Bhṛgus are repeatedly<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202290">3</a></sup> alluded to as devoted to the fire cult. They are clearly<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202290">4</a></sup> no more than a group of ancient priests and ancestors with an eponymous <a class="vei_120a" href="भृगु">bhṛgu</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202290">5</a></sup> in the Rigveda, except in three passages,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202290">6</a></sup> where they are evidently regarded as an historic family. It is not clear, however, whether they were priests or warriors: in the battle of the ten kings the Bhṛgus appear with the <b>Druhyus,</b> perhaps as their priests, but this is not certain.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202290">7</a></sup></p><p>In the later literature the Bhṛgus are a real family, with subdivisions like the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतशायन">aitaśāyana</a>,</b> according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202290">8</a></sup> The Bhṛgus are mentioned as priests in connexion with various rites, such as the Agnisthāpana<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202290">9</a></sup> and the Daśapeyakratu.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202290">10</a></sup> In many passages they are conjoined with the <b>Aṅgirases.</b><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202290">11</a></sup> the close association of the two families is shown  by the fact that <a class="vei_120a" href="च्यवन">cyavana</a> is called either a <a class="vei_120a" href="भार्गव">bhārgava</a> or an <a class="vei_120a" href="आंगिरस">āṅgirasa</a> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202290">12</a></sup> In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202290">13</a></sup> the name of <a class="vei_120a" href="भृगु">bhṛgu</a> is selected to exemplify the dangers incurred by the oppressors of Brahmans: the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सृञ्जय">sṛñjaya</a> Vaitahavyas</b> perish in consequence of an attack on <a class="vei_120a" href="भृगु">bhṛgu</a>. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1202290">14</a></sup> also <a class="vei_120a" href="भृगु">bhṛgu</a> has this representative character. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भृगवाण">bhṛgavāṇa</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भार्गव">bhārgava</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202290">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 6, 1, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ix. 1. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xviii. 9, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202290">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 34, and n. 14. For a different form of the legend, <i>cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 2, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202290">↑</a> Rv. i. 58, 6; 127, 7; 143, 4; ii. 4, 2; iii. 2, 4; iv. 7, 1, etc. See Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> 51. The legend of their chariot-making (Rv. iv. 16, 20; x. 39, 14) may be due, as Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> suggests, to a confusion with the Ṛbhus. It may, however, be an allusion to the historic Bhṛgus, whom we find in the battle of the ten kings.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202290">↑</a> As shown by the legend of fire having been brought to them by <a class="vei_120a" href="मातरिश्वन्">mātariśvan</a>, Rv. iii. 5, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202290">↑</a> i. 60, 1, where, however, Roth, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> takes the singular in a collective sense, an interpretation which may be correct, but is not necessary.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202290">↑</a> Rv. vii. 18, 6; viii. 3, 9; 6, 18, to which list, given by Macdonell, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> Roth adds viii. 102, 4, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="और्व">aurva</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="भृगु">bhṛgu</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वत्">vat</a>,</i> ‘like <a class="vei_120a" href="और्व">aurva</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="भृगु">bhṛgu</a>.’ <i>Cf.</i> the fact that the <b>Aurvas,</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 33, take the place of the Bhṛgus of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxx. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202290">↑</a> In viii. 3, 9; 6, 18; 102, 4, the reference to a priestly family is the more natural; in vii. 18, 6, warriors may be meant. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 262, n., where he cites ix. 101, 13, as perhaps denoting the same thing.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202290">↑</a> xxx. 5. See n. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202290">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 6, 5, 2; v. 6, 8, 6; Av. iv. 14, 5; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 4, 1 (p. 48).</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202290">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 2, 5; Pañcavimśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xviii. 9, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202290">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 1, 7, 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 1, 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, i. 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 4, 8; iii. 2, 7, 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 1, 13, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Rv. viii. 35, 3; 43, 13; x. 14, 6, in the first and last of which passages the Atharvans also occur. See Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> xxvii. n. 2. Hence, in the Atharvanic ritual texts, the term <i>Bhṛgvaṅgirasaḥ</i> is applied to the Atharvaveda (Bloomfield, <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 9, 10, 107 <i>et seq.</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202290">↑</a> iv. 1, 5, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202290">↑</a> v. 19, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1202290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1202290">↑</a> ii. 20. In the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 42-44 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 204), <a class="vei_120a" href="भृगु">bhṛgu</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वारुणि">vāruṇi</a> appears as a student. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 169-173; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 140; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 443 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

भृंगा|भृङ्गा|భృంగా|భృఙ్గా|ಭೃಂಗಾ|ಭೃಙ್ಗಾ|bhṛṅgā|bhRMgaa|bhRngaa|bhRiMgaa|bhRuMgaa|bhRRiMgaa|bhRingaa|bhRungaa|bhRRingaa|_vei_120@2291|_vei2_pn110
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2290">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2292">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn109">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn110">2:110</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn111">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhṛṅgā</b> is the name of a species of bee, later specified as large and black, in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202291"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202291">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202291"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202291">2</a></sup> which include it in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202291"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202291">↑</a> ix. 2, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202291"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202291">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 29. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 96.</li></ol></div>

भृम्य्-अश्व|भृम्यश्व|भृम्य्|अश्व|భృమ్య్-అశ్వ|భృమ్యశ్వ|భృమ్య్|అశ్వ|ಭೃಮ್ಯ್-ಅಶ್ವ|ಭೃಮ್ಯಶ್ವ|ಭೃಮ್ಯ್|ಅಶ್ವ|bhṛmy-aśva|bhṛmyaśva|bhṛmy|aśva|bhRmy-ashva|bhRmyashva|bhRmy|ashva|bhRimy-ashva|bhRimyashva|bhRimy|bhRumy-ashva|bhRumyashva|bhRumy|bhRRimy-ashva|bhRRimyashva|bhRRimy|_vei_120@2292|_vei2_pn110
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2291">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2293">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn109">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn110">2:110</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn111">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhṛmy-aśva</b> is the name of the father of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मुद्गल">mudgala</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> (ix. 24).</p></div>

भेकुरि|భేకురి|ಭೇಕುರಿ|bhekuri|_vei_120@2293|_vei2_pn110
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2292">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2294">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn109">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn110">2:110</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn111">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhekuri.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बेकुरा">bekurā</a>.</b></p></div>

भेद|भेद|భేద_౧|భేద|ಭೇದ_೧|ಭೇದ|bheda_1|bheda|_vei_120@2294|_vei2_pn110
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2293">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2295">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn109">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn110">2:110</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn111">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Bheda,</b> one of the enemies of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a></b> and the <b>TṛtsuBharatas,</b> was defeated by the former in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a></b> (Jumna),<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202294">1</a></sup> apparently in a second conflict fought after the battle of the ten kings, in which <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a> successfully defended his western frontier against the confederate foes. The <b>Ajas, Śigrus,</b> and <b>Yakṣus,</b> who are mentioned as also defeated, may have been united under his leadership if he was a king; or the Bhedas may have been a separate people, as Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202294">2</a></sup> thinks. Hopkins'<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202294">3</a></sup> opinion that the defeat was on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परुष्णी">paruṣṇī</a>,</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a> being another  name of that stream, is most improbable; nor is the view that <a class="vei_120a" href="भेद">bheda</a> was one of the ten kings essential.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202294">4</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202294">↑</a> Rv. vii. 18, 18. 19; 33, 3; 83, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202294">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 12 (the word is always used in the singular).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202294">↑</a> <i>India, Old and New,</i> 52.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202294">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 260 <i>et seq.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 2, 20, n.; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 126; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 319, 327.</li></ol></div>

भेद|भेद|భేద_౨|భేద|ಭೇದ_೨|ಭೇದ|bheda_2|bheda|_vei_120@2295|_vei2_pn111
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2294">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2296">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn110">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn111">2:111</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn112">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Bheda</b> is mentioned in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202295"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202295">1</a></sup> as having come to a bad end because he refused a cow (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वशा">vaśā</a></i>) to <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> when asked for it. That he is different from the preceding <a class="vei_120a" href="भेद">bheda</a>, as Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202295"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202295">2</a></sup> assumes, is not certain. Indeed, it may very well be that his defeat led to his being chosen as the representative of the evil end of the wicked man. Moreover, the irreligious character of <a class="vei_120a" href="भेद">bheda</a> may be ascribed to his being a leader of non-Āryan folk, if the <b>Ajas</b> and <b>Śigrus,</b> with whom in the Rigveda he is connected or associated were, as is possible, though by no means certain, un-Āryan tribes of totemists.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202295"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202295">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202295"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202295">↑</a> xii. 4, 49. 50.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202295"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202295">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 13.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202295"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202295">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 153, who inclines to see in them totemistic tribes, but the names furnish the sole support of this conjecture. On this supposition they were probably non-Āryan. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अज">aja</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

भेषज|भेषज|భేషజ_౧|భేషజ|ಭೇಷಜ_೧|ಭೇಷಜ|bheṣaja_1|bheṣaja|bheShaja_1|bheShaja|_vei_120@2296|_vei2_pn111
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2295">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2297">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn110">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn111">2:111</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn112">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Bheṣaja,</b> denoting a ‘remedial agent,’ ‘medicine,’ is often mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202296">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202296">2</a></sup> being also used in a figurative sense.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202296">3</a></sup> Plants,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202296">4</a></sup> waters,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202296">5</a></sup> and spells<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202296">6</a></sup> are repeatedly enumerated as medicines. Most of the medical practices of the Atharvaveda are merely examples of sympathetic magic. For example, in one hymn<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202296">7</a></sup> the yellow of jaundice is entreated to pass into yellow birds. In another<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202296">8</a></sup> fever is to be banished by means of a frog; for the frog, being a potent means of cooling fire<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202296">9</a></sup> (because of its association with water), is regarded as analogously effective in banishing the fire of fever. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भिषज्">bhiṣaj</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202296">↑</a> i. 89, 4; ii. 33, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202296">↑</a> Av. v. 29, 1; vi. 21, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202296">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 3, 1, 1; 5, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 41.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202296">↑</a> Rv. x. 97, and <i>passim</i> in the Atharvaveda.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202296">↑</a> i. 23, 19. 20; 34, 6, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 4, 9, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xvi. 7, etc. Possibly there is some truth in Zimmer's view, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 399, that the reference is to the beneficial effects of bathing.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202296">↑</a> Exemplified in the medical spells of the Atharvaveda and the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202296">↑</a> i. 22; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 264, <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202296">↑</a> vii. 116; Bloomfield, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 565 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202296">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. x. 16, 14; Av. xviii. 3, 60.</li></ol></div>

भेषज|भेषज|భేషజ_౨|భేషజ|ಭೇಷಜ_೨|ಭೇಷಜ|bheṣaja_2|bheṣaja|bheShaja_2|bheShaja|_vei_120@2297|_vei2_pn112
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2296">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2298">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn111">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn112">2:112</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn113">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Bheṣaja</b> in the plural is found in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202297"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202297">1</a></sup> and in the Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202297"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202297">2</a></sup> denoting the hymns of the Atharvaveda in so far as they are regarded as having ‘healing’ powers.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202297"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202297">↑</a> xi. 6, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202297"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202297">↑</a> Āśvalāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 7, 3; Sāṅkhāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 2, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 9, 10.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> xxxi. 628.</li></ol></div>

भैम-सेन|भैमसेन|भैम|सेन|భైమ-సేన|భైమసేన|భైమ|సేన|ಭೈಮ-ಸೇನ|ಭೈಮಸೇನ|ಭೈಮ|ಸೇನ|bhaima-sena|bhaimasena|bhaima|sena|_vei_120@2298|_vei2_pn112
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2297">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2299">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn111">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn112">2:112</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn113">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhaima-sena,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भीमसेन">bhīmasena</a>,</b>’ is the name of a man in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā (iv. 6, 6).</p></div>

भैम-सेनि|भैमसेनि|भैम|सेनि|భైమ-సేని|భైమసేని|భైమ|సేని|ಭೈಮ-ಸೇನಿ|ಭೈಮಸೇನಿ|ಭೈಮ|ಸೇನಿ|bhaima-seni|bhaimaseni|bhaima|seni|_vei_120@2299|_vei2_pn112
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2298">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2300">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn111">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn112">2:112</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn113">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhaima-seni,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भीमसेन">bhīmasena</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b>Divodāsa</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202299"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202299">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202299"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202299">↑</a> vii. 8 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 460, 472).</li></ol></div>

भैषज्य|భైషజ్య|ಭೈಷಜ್ಯ|bhaiṣajya|bhaiShajya|_vei_120@2300|_vei2_pn112
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2299">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2301">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn111">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn112">2:112</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn113">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhaiṣajya</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xii, 7. 1, 12) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> (x. 7. 25) denotes ‘healing remedy’ or ‘medicine,’ like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भेषज">bheṣaja</a>.</b></p></div>

भोग|భోగ|ಭೋಗ|bhoga|_vei_120@2301|_vei2_pn112
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2300">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2302">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn111">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn112">2:112</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn113">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhoga</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202301"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202301">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202301"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202301">2</a></sup> denotes the ‘coil’ of a serpent.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202301"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202301">↑</a> v. 29, 6; vi. 75, 14 (where the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हस्तघ्न">hastaghna</a>,</b> or ‘hand-guard,’ of the archer is compared to a snake).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202301"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202301">↑</a> Av. xi. 9, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 4, 5. 6; v. 4, 5, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xiii. 4; xxi. 8, etc.</li></ol></div>

भोज|భోజ|ಭೋಜ|bhoja|_vei_120@2302|_vei2_pn112
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2301">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2303">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn111">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn112">2:112</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn113">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhoja</b> in several passages of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 12. 14. 17) seems to be used as a king's title.</p></div>

भौज्य|భౌజ్య|ಭೌಜ್ಯ|bhaujya|_vei_120@2303|_vei2_pn112
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2302">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2304">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn111">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn112">2:112</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn113">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhaujya</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202303">1</a></sup> denotes the rank of a prince bearing the title of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भोज">bhoja</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202303"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202303">↑</a> vii. 32; viii. 6. 12. 14. 16.</li></ol></div>

भौमक|భౌమక|ಭೌಮಕ|bhaumaka|_vei_120@2304|_vei2_pn112
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2303">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2305">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn111">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn112">2:112</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn113">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhaumaka</b> is the name of some animal in the late Adbhuta <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202304"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202304">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202304"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202304">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 40.</li></ol></div>

भौमी|भौमि|భౌమీ|భౌమి|ಭೌಮೀ|ಭೌಮಿ|bhaumī|bhaumi|bhaumii|bhaumee|_vei_120@2305|_vei2_pn112
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2304">«</a>pada9<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2306">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn111">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn112">2:112</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn113">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhaumī</b> is the name of an animal in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202305">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202305"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202305">↑</a> v. 5, 18, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 99.</li></ol></div>

भौवन|భౌవన|ಭೌವನ|bhauvana|_vei_120@2306|_vei2_pn113
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2305">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2307">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn112">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn113">2:113</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn114">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhauvana,</b> ‘descendant of Bhuvana,’ is the patronymic of the mythical <b>Viśvakarman</b> in the Śatapatha (xiii. 7, 1, 15) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> (viii. 21, 8. 10) Brāhmaṇas, and the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> (x. 26).</p></div>

भौवायन|భౌవాయన|ಭೌವಾಯನ|bhauvāyana|bhauvaayana|_vei_120@2307|_vei2_pn113
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2306">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2308">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn112">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn113">2:113</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn114">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhauvāyana,</b> ‘descendant of Bhuva,’ is the patronymic of <b>Kapivana</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202307"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202307">1</a></sup> It is also found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202307"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202307">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202307"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202307">↑</a> xx. 13, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202307"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202307">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxii. 2 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 473); Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 4, 5; and Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiii. 54, where Kapivana is not mentioned.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15 55, 69.</li></ol></div>

भ्रातृ|భ్రాతృ|ಭ್ರಾತೃ|bhrātṛ|bhraatR|bhraatRi|bhraatRu|bhraatRRi|_vei_120@2308|_vei2_pn113
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2307">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2309">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn112">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn113">2:113</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn114">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhrātṛ</b> is the common designation of ‘brother’ from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202308">1</a></sup> onwards. The word is also applied to a relation or close friend generally,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202308">2</a></sup> but here the persons concerned are, it should be noted, in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202308">3</a></sup> deities, who are brothers of one another or of the worshipper. Thus in the early literature the word has not really lost its precise sense. The derivation from the root <i>bhṛ,</i> ‘support,’ is probably correct, designating the brother as the support of his sister. This harmonizes with the fact that in Vedic literature the brother plays the part of protector of his sister when bereft of her father, and that maidens deprived of their brothers (<i>abhrātṛ</i>) meet an evil fate.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202308">4</a></sup> The gradation of the relations in the home is shown by the order in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202308">5</a></sup> where father, mother, brother, and sister are successively mentioned. Strife between brothers is occasionally referred to.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202308">6</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202308">↑</a> i. 164, 1; iv. 3, 13; v. 34, 4, etc.; Av. i. 14, 2; ii. 13, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 8, 4, etc.; <i>bhrātṛtva,</i> Rv. viii. 20, 22; 83, 8; x. 108, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202308">↑</a> Bo7htlingk and Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 462.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202308">↑</a> i. 161, 1; 170, 2; iii. 53, 5; iv. 1, 2; vi. 51, 5; viii. 43, 16. <i>Cf.</i> Av. iv. 4, 5; v. 22, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202308">↑</a> Rv. i. 124, 7; iv. 5, 5; Av. i. 17, 1; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 328. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अयोगू">ayogū</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202308">↑</a> vii. 15, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202308"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202308">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Av. iii. 30, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 5, 3, where it is a sign of serious confusion; <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 11, cxlv; Bloomfield <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 72.</li></ol></div>

भ्रातृव्य|భ్రాతృవ్య|ಭ್ರಾತೃವ್ಯ|bhrātṛvya|bhraatRvya|bhraatRivya|bhraatRuvya|bhraatRRivya|_vei_120@2309|_vei2_pn114
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2308">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2310">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn113">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn114">2:114</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn115">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhrātṛvya</b> is found in one passage of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202309">1</a></sup> where, being named with brother and sister, it must be an expression of relationship. The sense appears to be ‘(father's) brother's son,’ ‘cousin,’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202309">2</a></sup> this meaning alone accounting for the sense of ‘rival,’ ‘enemy,’ found elsewhere in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202309">3</a></sup> and repeatedly in the other Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202309">4</a></sup> In an undivided family the relations of cousins would easily develop into rivalry and enmity. The original meaning may, however, have been ‘nephew,’<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202309">5</a></sup> as the simple etymological sense would be ‘brother's son’; but this seems not to account for the later meaning so well. The <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202309">6</a></sup> prescribes the telling of a falsehood to a <a class="vei_120a" href="भ्रातृव्य">bhrātṛvya</a>, who, further, is often given the epithets ‘hating’ (<i>dviṣan</i>) and ‘evil’ (<i>apriya, pāpman</i>) in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202309">7</a></sup> The Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202309">8</a></sup> also contains various spells, which aim at destroying or expelling one's ‘rivals.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202309">↑</a> v. 22, 12, and perhaps x. 3, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202309">↑</a> The word is rendered ‘cousin’ by Whitney in his Translation of the Atharvaveda (x. 6, 1; xv. 1, 8).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202309">↑</a> ii. 18, 1; viii. 10, 18. 33; x. 9, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202309">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 5, 9, 2, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, x. 7; xxvii. 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, i. 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 7, etc.; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 1, 21, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 13, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Rv. viii. 21, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202309">↑</a> Whitney, in his Translation of the Atharvaveda (ii. 18, 1), while rendering the word by ‘adversary,’ explains it in a note as meaning literally ‘nephew,’ or ‘brother's son.’</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202309">↑</a> xxvii. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202309">↑</a> See several of the passages given in n. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202309">↑</a> ii. 18, 1; x. 9, 1, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Tait tirīya Saṃhitā, i. 3. 2, 1, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 501, 506, 507, who thinks it means a kind of brother, and through early family conditions was restricted to cousins; Bo7htlingk and Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 307.</li></ol></div>

भ्रूण-हन्|भ्रूणहन्|भ्रूण|हन्|భ్రూణ-హన్|భ్రూణహన్|భ్రూణ|హన్|ಭ್ರೂಣ-ಹನ್|ಭ್ರೂಣಹನ್|ಭ್ರೂಣ|ಹನ್|bhrūṇa-han|bhrūṇahan|bhrūṇa|han|bhruuNa-han|bhruuNahan|bhruuNa|bhrooNa-han|bhrooNahan|bhrooNa|_vei_120@2310|_vei2_pn114
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2309">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2311">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn113">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn114">2:114</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn115">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhrūṇa-han,</b> ‘slaying an embryo’; <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भ्रूण">bhrūṇa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="हत्या">hatyā</a>,</b> ‘the slaying of an embryo,’ are terms expressing a crime which is repeatedly and severely censured in the later Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202310">1</a></sup> where it is said to be the greatest of all crimes, and one of which the  guilt cannot be removed. In many later passages<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202310">2</a></sup> the same crime is referred to, always with reprobation: this fact alone shows the erroneousness of the theory<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202310">3</a></sup> that daughters could be allowed, once born, to die if their fathers so desired.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202310">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 1, 9; Kāthaka Sāṃhitā, xxxi. 7; Kapiṣṭhala Saṃhitā, xlvii (cited in Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 579, 580); Av. vi. 112, 3; 113, 2. The <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 5, 10, 3, and <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 8; 11, have <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्म">brahma</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="हन्">han</a></i> instead; but see <i>ibid.,</i> 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202310">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 9, 15, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 8, 2; x. 1, 15; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 3, 22. The substantive is found in <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8, 20, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 7, 3; 8, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 18, 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 27. <a class="vei_120a" href="भ्रूण">bhrūṇa</a> itself occurs in Rv. x. 155. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202310">↑</a> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>,</b> p. 487, with n. 131.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 481; 10, 66; Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 17, 430; <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 521, 522.</li></ol></div>

मकक|మకక|ಮಕಕ|makaka|_vei_120@2311|_vei2_pn115
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2310">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2312">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn114">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn115">2:115</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn116">↷</a><hr><p><b>Makaka,</b> a word occurring once in the Atharvaveda (viii. 6, 12), may be the name of some unknown animal; but it is possibly an adjective having some such sense as ‘bleating.’</p></div>

मकर|మకర|ಮಕರ|makara|_vei_120@2312|_vei2_pn115
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2311">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2313">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn114">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn115">2:115</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn116">↷</a><hr><p><b>Makara</b> is the name of an animal, probably the ‘crocodile,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202312">1</a></sup> which is included in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202312">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202312">↑</a> The <a class="vei_120a" href="मकर">makara</a>, as a Hindu sculptural ornament, originally represented a crocodile. <i>Cf.</i> Cousen's article in the <i>Annual Report of the Archaological Survey of India</i> for 1903-4, pp. 227-231 (where the <a class="vei_120a" href="मकर">makara</a> appears as the vehicle of <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a> and of <a class="vei_120a" href="गंगा">gaṅgā</a>). <i>Cf.</i> also <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1904-5, pp. 80, 83, 84.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202312">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 13, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="वाज">vāja</a> saneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 35. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 97.</li></ol></div>

मक्ष|మక్ష|ಮಕ್ಷ|makṣa|makSha|maxa|_vei_120@2313|_vei2_pn115
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2312">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2314">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn114">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn115">2:115</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn116">↷</a><hr><p><b>Makṣa,</b> ‘fly,’ is found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202313">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202313">2</a></sup> where its fondness for sweet things is alluded to. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अद्मसद्">admasad</a></b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202313">↑</a> iv. 45, 4; vii. 32, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202313">↑</a> ix. 1, 17.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 97.</li></ol></div>

मक्षा|मक्षिका|మక్షా|మక్షికా|ಮಕ್ಷಾ|ಮಕ್ಷಿಕಾ|makṣā|makṣikā|makShaa|makShikaa|maxaa|maxikaa|_vei_120@2314|_vei2_pn115
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2313">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2315">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn114">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn115">2:115</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn116">↷</a><hr><p><b>Makṣā, Makṣikā,</b> denote both ‘fly’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202314"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202314">1</a></sup> and ‘bee’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202314"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202314">2</a></sup> in the Rigveda and later. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202314"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202314">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मक्षिका">makṣikā</a>, Rv. i. 162, 9; Av. xi. 1, 2; 9, 10; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 3, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202314"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202314">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मक्षा">makṣā</a>, Rv. x. 40, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="मक्षिका">makṣikā</a>, i. 119, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रश्न">praśna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 4, where a ‘king bee’ (<i>madhukara-<a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a></i>) is referred to.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 97; Hillebrandt. <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 240, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

मख|మఖ|ಮಖ|makha|_vei_120@2315|_vei2_pn116
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2314">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2316">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn115">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn116">2:116</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn118">↷</a><hr><p><b>Makha</b> appears to designate a person in two passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202315">1</a></sup> but in neither passage does the context explain who he was. Probably a demon of some kind is meant. In the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202315">2</a></sup> mention is also made of the ‘head of <a class="vei_120a" href="मख">makha</a>,’ an expression which has become unintelligible to the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202315">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202315">↑</a> ix. 101, 13, where the <b>Bhṛgus</b> are mentioned as opposed to <a class="vei_120a" href="मख">makha</a> (<i>cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 51); x. 171, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202315">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xi. 57; xxxvii. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 1, 8, 1; iii. 2, 4, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202315"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202315">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 1, 2, 17. <i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

मगध|మగధ|ಮಗಧ|magadha|_vei_120@2316|_vei2_pn116
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2315">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2317">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn115">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn116">2:116</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn118">↷</a><hr><p><b>Magadha</b> is the name of a people who appear throughout Vedic literature as of little repute. Though the name is not actually found in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202316">1</a></sup> it occurs in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202316">2</a></sup> where fever is wished away to the <b>Gandhāṛis</b> and <b>Mūjavants,</b> northern peoples, and to the <b>Aṅgas</b> and Magadhas, peoples of the east. Again, in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202316">3</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="मागध">māgadha</a>, or man of <a class="vei_120a" href="मगध">magadha</a>, is included as dedicated to <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अति">ati</a>-kruṣṭa,</i> ‘loud noise’(?), while in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात्य">vrātya</a></b> hymn of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202316">4</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="मागध">māgadha</a> is said to be connected with the <a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात्य">vrātya</a> as his <a class="vei_120a" href="मित्र">mitra</a>, his <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a>, his laughter, and his thunder in the four quarters. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202316">5</a></sup> the equipment characteristic of the <a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात्य">vrātya</a> is said to be given, when the latter is admitted into the Āryan Brahminical community, to a bad Brahmin living in <a class="vei_120a" href="मगध">magadha</a> (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्म-बन्धु">brahma-bandhu</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मागध-देशीय">māgadha-deśīya</a></i>), but this point does not occur in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202316">6</a></sup> On the other hand, respectable Brahmins sometimes lived there, for the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202316">7</a></sup> mentions <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यम">madhyama</a>, Prātībodhī-<a class="vei_120a" href="पुत्र">putra</a>,</b> as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मगध">magadha</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वासिन्">vāsin</a>,</i> ‘living in <a class="vei_120a" href="मगध">magadha</a>.’ Oldenberg,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202316">8</a></sup> however, seems clearly right in regarding this as unusual. </p><p>The Magadhas are evidently a people in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> and other Sūtras,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202316">9</a></sup> possibly also in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202316">10</a></sup> It is therefore most improbable that Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202316">11</a></sup> can be right in thinking that in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202316">3</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202316">4</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="मागध">māgadha</a> is not a man of <a class="vei_120a" href="मगध">magadha</a>, but a member of the mixed caste produced by a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a></b> marrying a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a></b> woman.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202316">12</a></sup> But the theory of mixed castes, in any case open to some doubt, cannot be accepted when used to explain such obviously tribal names as <a class="vei_120a" href="मागध">māgadha</a>. The fact that the <a class="vei_120a" href="मागध">māgadha</a> is often in later times a minstrel is easily accounted for by the assumption that the country was the home of minstrelsy and that wandering bards from <a class="vei_120a" href="मगध">magadha</a> were apt to visit the more western lands. This class the later texts recognize as a caste, inventing an origin by intermarriage of the old-established castes.</p><p>The dislike of the Magadhas, which may be Rigvedic, since the <b>Kīkaṭas</b> were perhaps the prototype of the Magadhas, was in all probability due, as Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202316">13</a></sup> thinks, to the fact that the Magadhas were not really Brahminized. This is entirely in accord with the evidence of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1202316">14</a></sup> that neither <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a></b> nor <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a></b> were fully Brahminized at an early date, much less <a class="vei_120a" href="मगध">magadha</a>. Weber<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1202316">15</a></sup> suggests two other grounds that may have influenced the position--the persistence of aboriginal blood and the growth of Buddhism. The latter consideration is hardly applicable to the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> or the Atharvaveda; but the imperfect Brahminization of the land, if substituted for it in accordance with Oldenberg's suggestion,  would have some force. The former motive, despite Oldenberg's doubt, seems fully justified. Pargiter<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1202316">16</a></sup> has gone so far as to suggest that in <a class="vei_120a" href="मगध">magadha</a> the Āryans met and mingled with a body of invaders from the east by sea. Though there is no evidence for this view in the Vedic texts, it is reasonable to suppose that the farther east the Āryans penetrated, the less did they impress themselves upon the aborigines. Modern ethnology confirms this <i>a priori</i> supposition in so far as it shows Āryan types growing less and less marked as the eastern part of India is reached, although such evidence is not decisive in view of the great intermixture of peoples in India.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202316">↑</a> See <b>Kikaṭa.</b></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202316">↑</a> v. 22, 14, where the Paippalāda recension has <i>mayebhiḥ,</i> which is a mere blunder, but substitutes the <b>Kāśis</b> for the Aṅgas.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202316">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 5, 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202316">↑</a> xv. 2, 1-4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202316">↑</a> Lāṭyāyar <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 6, 28; Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxii. 4, 22. <i>Cf.</i> Sāyaṇa on <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvii. 1, 16. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202316">↑</a> xvii. 1, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202316">↑</a> vii. 13; this is not mentioned in the earlier <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202316">↑</a> <i>Buddha,</i> 400, n.; Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 112, n.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202316">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 2, 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xx. 13; Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxii. 6, 18; Hiraṇyakeśi <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvii. 6. See Caland, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Mergenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 56, 553.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202316">↑</a> ii. 1, 1. See Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 200; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 46, n. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202316">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 35. <i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> 20.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202316">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 5, 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202316">↑</a> xv. 2, 1-4.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202316">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>, x. 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>. iv. 17. So Sāyaṇa, on the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> explains <a class="vei_120a" href="मागध">māgadha</a>, and Mahīdhara, on the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, offers this as one version.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202316">↑</a> <i>Buddha,</i> 400, n.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1202316">↑</a> i. 4, 1, 10 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 170 <i>et seq.;</i> Oldenberg, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 398. <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a> here appears as more Brahminical than <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>; it is interesting to note that, while <a class="vei_120a" href="वैदेह">vaideha</a>, like <a class="vei_120a" href="मागध">māgadha</a>, is used in the later theory as a name of a mixed caste, <a class="vei_120a" href="कौसल्य">kausalya</a> is not so degraded (Oldenberg, 399, n.).</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1202316">↑</a> See <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 52, 53; 185; 10, 99; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 79, n. 1; 111, 112.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1202316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1202316">↑</a> <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, pp. 851-853.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India,</i> 6, 24, 260, 267.</li></ol></div>

मगुन्दी|मगुन्दि|మగున్దీ|మగున్ది|ಮಗುನ್ದೀ|ಮಗುನ್ದಿ|magundī|magundi|magundii|magundee|_vei_120@2317|_vei2_pn118
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2316">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2318">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn116">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn118">2:118</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn119">↷</a><hr><p><b>Magundī</b> is the name of some pest occurring in a verse of an Atharvaveda hymn<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202317">1</a></sup> employed to exorcise evil influences. By that verse the ‘daughters of the Magundī’ are to be expelled from the cowstall, the wagon, and the house. It is uncertain whether an animal, insect, or demoness is meant.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202317">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202317">↑</a> ii. 14, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202317">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 58.</li></ol></div>

मघ|మఘ|ಮಘ|magha|_vei_120@2318|_vei2_pn118
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2317">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2319">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn116">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn118">2:118</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn119">↷</a><hr><p><b>Magha</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202318">1</a></sup> denotes ‘bounty,’ and Maghavan<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202318">2</a></sup> is the regular Vedic name for the ‘generous giver’ of bounties to priests. It is doubtful whether the Maghavans were more than this, or had any special rank as a class in Vedic society. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202318">↑</a> i. 11, 3; 104, 5; iii. 13, 3; 19, 1; iv. 17, 8; v. 30, 12; 32, 12, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 16. Very rarely later, <i>e.g.,</i> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xx. 67.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202318">↑</a> Rv. i. 31, 12; ii. 6, 4; 27, 17; v. 39, 4; 42, 8; vi. 27, 8, etc. So <a class="vei_120a" href="मघ">magha</a>-tti, ‘giving of gifts,’ Rv. iv. 37, 8; v. 79, 5; viii. 24, 10, etc.; Maghadeya, ‘giving of gifts,’ vii. 67, 9; x. 156, 2; Maghavat-tva, ‘liberality,’ vi. 27, 3. The word Maghavan is the epithet <i>par excellence</i> of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> in the Rv. (iii. 30, 3; iv. 16, 1; 31, 7; 42, 5, etc.), and survives in post-Vedic literature as a name of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>; otherwise, even in the later Saṃhitās, it is very rare, occurring practically as a divine epithet only (of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 4, 8, 1; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 3, 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 11).</li></ol></div>

मघा|మఘా|ಮಘಾ|maghā|maghaa|_vei_120@2319|_vei2_pn118
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2318">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2320">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn116">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn118">2:118</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn119">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maghā.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अघा">aghā</a>.</b></p></div>

मंगल|मङ्गल|మంగల|మఙ్గల|ಮಂಗಲ|ಮಙ್ಗಲ|maṅgala|maMgala|mangala|_vei_120@2320|_vei2_pn118
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2319">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2321">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn116">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn118">2:118</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn119">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maṅgala</b> is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (xxvi. 2). </p></div>

मंगीर|मङ्गीर|మంగీర|మఙ్గీర|ಮಂಗೀರ|ಮಙ್ಗೀರ|maṅgīra|maMgiira|maMgeera|mangiira|mangeera|_vei_120@2321|_vei2_pn119
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2320">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2322">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn118">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn119">2:119</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn120">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maṅgīra</b> is found in an obscure verse in the Vaitāna<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202321">1</a></sup> and other<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202321">2</a></sup> Sūtras with reference to cows. It is quite uncertain whether a river or a man<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202321">3</a></sup> is meant. The <a class="vei_120a" href="गंगा">gaṅgā</a> (Ganges) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a> (Jumna) are mentioned in the same verse. The correct form of the word is doubtful.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202321">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202321">↑</a> xxxiv. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202321">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, vii. 2, 7; <i>Mandīrasya,</i> Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiii. 3, 21; <i>Maṅkīrasya,</i> Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxi. 20, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202321">↑</a> So apparently Garbe, Translation of the Vaitāna <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, 97; Caland. <i>Das Vaitānasūtra,</i> 102; Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202321">↑</a> See the variants in n. 2.</li></ol></div>

मञ्जिष्ठा|మఞ్జిష్ఠా|ಮಞ್ಜಿಷ್ಠಾ|mañjiṣṭhā|ma~njiShThaa|_vei_120@2322|_vei2_pn119
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2321">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2323">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn118">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn119">2:119</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn120">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mañjiṣṭhā,</b> ‘madder,’ is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> (iii. 2, 4) and <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> (viii. 7) Āraṇyakas.</p></div>

मटची|मटचि|మటచీ|మటచి|ಮಟಚೀ|ಮಟಚಿ|maṭacī|maṭaci|maTachii|maTachi|maTachee|_vei_120@2323|_vei2_pn119
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2322">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2324">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn118">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn119">2:119</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn120">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maṭacī</b> occurs in a passage of the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202323"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202323">1</a></sup> where reference is made to the <b>Kurus</b> being overwhelmed by Maṭacīs.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202323"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202323">2</a></sup> Śaṅkara interprets the word by ‘thunderbolts’ (<i>aśanayaḥ</i>), while Ānandatīrtha in his commentary gives, as an alternative rendering, <i>pāṣāṇa-vṛṣṭayaḥ--i.e.,</i> ‘hailstones,’ which may be the sense. The Śabdakalpadruma,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202323"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202323">3</a></sup> agreeing with Ānandatīrtha,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202323"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202323">4</a></sup> says that <a class="vei_120a" href="मटची">maṭacī</a> means, ‘a kind of small red bird’ (<i>rakta-<a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="क्षुद्र">kṣudra</a>-pakṣi-viśeṣa,</i> reading <i>-pakṣī-</i>), and Jacob<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202323"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202323">5</a></sup> suggests that the ‘locust’ is meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202323"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202323">↑</a> i. 10, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202323"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202323">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मटची">maṭacī</a>-hata.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202323"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202323">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202323"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202323">↑</a> On Brahmasūtra, iii. 4, 28.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202323"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202323">↑</a> <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1911, p. 510.</li></ol></div>

मणि|మణి|ಮಣಿ|maṇi|maNi|_vei_120@2324|_vei2_pn119
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2323">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2325">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn118">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn119">2:119</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn120">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maṇi</b> is the name in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202324">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202324">2</a></sup> of a ‘jewel’ used as an amulet against all kinds of evil. That either ‘pearl’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202324">3</a></sup> or ‘diamond’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202324">4</a></sup> is denoted is not clear.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202324">5</a></sup> It is evident that the  <a class="vei_120a" href="मणि">maṇi</a> could be strung on a thread (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a></i>), which is referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202324">6</a></sup> and elsewhere;<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202324">7</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="मणि">maṇi</a> was certainly also worn round the neck, for in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202324">8</a></sup> occurs the epithet <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मणि">maṇi</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="ग्रीव">grīva</a>,</i> ‘having a jewel on the neck.’ An amulet of <a class="vei_120a" href="बिल्व">bilva</a> is celebrated in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202324">9</a></sup> and many varieties of amulet are there enumerated.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202324">10</a></sup> The ‘jeweller’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="मणि">maṇi</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कार">kāra</a></i>) is mentioned in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202324">11</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202324">↑</a> i. 33, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202324">↑</a> Av. i. 29, 1; ii. 4, 1, 2; viii. 5, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> x. 6, 24; xii. 1, 44; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 3, 4, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxv. 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vii. 23, where <a class="vei_120a" href="दुर्ग">durga</a>, in his commentary, takes <a class="vei_120a" href="मणि">maṇi</a> as <i>āditya-<a class="vei_120a" href="मणि">maṇi</a>,</i> or ‘sun-stone,’ while the St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> suggests that a crystal used as a burning glass may be meant.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202324">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202324">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben</i> 53.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202324">↑</a> The expression <i><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्य">hiraṇya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मणि">maṇi</a></i> in Rv i. 33, 8, might possibly mean ‘gold a an ornament,’ but ‘gold (and) jewels is more probable. <i>Cf.</i> Av. xii. 1, 44, where <i>maṇiṃ hiraṇyam</i> must mean a jewel (and) gold.’</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202324">↑</a> xx. 16, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202324">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 18, 8. <i>Cf.</i> iii. 4, 13; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 248; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 3, 4, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202324">↑</a> i. 122, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202324">↑</a> xii. 18 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202324">↑</a> xii. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202324">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 3, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 337; Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 253; Weber, <i>Omina und Portenta,</i> 317, 374; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 2, 2, n. 4; 5, 386; 18, 37; <i>Proceedings of the Berlin Academy,</i> 1891, 796. Weber is inclined to detect a Babylonian origin of <a class="vei_120a" href="मणि">maṇi</a> (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मना">manā</a></b>), but the evidence is not convincing.</li></ol></div>

मणिक|మణిక|ಮಣಿಕ|maṇika|maNika|_vei_120@2325|_vei2_pn120
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2324">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2326">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn119">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn120">2:120</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn121">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maṇika</b> in the late Adbhuta <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202325">1</a></sup> and the Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202325">2</a></sup> denotes a large ‘water bottle.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202325">↑</a> Weber, <i>Omina und Portenta,</i> 316.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202325">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 9, 3; iv. 6, 4; Gobhila <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 1, 26; iii. 9, 6. 7, etc.; Sāṅkhāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 14.</li></ol></div>

मण्ड|మణ్డ|ಮಣ್ಡ|maṇḍa|maNDa|_vei_120@2326|_vei2_pn120
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2325">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2327">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn119">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn120">2:120</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn121">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maṇḍa,</b> n., is found in the compound <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नौ">nau</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="मण्ड">maṇḍa</a></i> (du.), denoting the two ‘rudders’ of a ship in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202326"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202326">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202326"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202326">↑</a> ii. 3, 3, 15. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 345, n. 3, who, following the commentary, accepts ‘sides’ as the meaning; Caland, <i>Über das rituelle <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> des <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>,</i> 60.</li></ol></div>

मण्डूक|మణ్డూక|ಮಣ್ಡೂಕ|maṇḍūka|maNDuuka|maNDooka|_vei_120@2327|_vei2_pn120
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2326">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2328">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn119">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn120">2:120</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn121">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maṇḍūka</b> is the name of ‘frog’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202327">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202327">2</a></sup> the feminine Maṇḍūkī also occurring.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202327">3</a></sup> The famous frog hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202327">4</a></sup> compares with Brahmins the frogs croaking  as they awake to activity at the beginning of the rains. It has been explained by Max Müller<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202327">5</a></sup> as a satire on the Brahmins. Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202327">6</a></sup> agreeing with this view, thinks that it is directed by its <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a></b> composer against rival Brahmins, probably the <b>Viśvāmitras.</b><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202327">7</a></sup> The view, however, which interprets the hymn as a rain charm<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202327">8</a></sup> seems on the whole more likely. The frog, from its connexion with water, was considered to have cooling properties. Thus after the burning of the dead body the frog is invited to come to the spot where the cremation has taken place in order to cool it down.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202327">9</a></sup> Similarly the frog is invoked in the Atharvaveda against the fire of fever.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202327">10</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202327">↑</a> vii. 103, 1; x. 166, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202327">↑</a> Av. vii. 112, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 4, 4, 3; 7, 11, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xiii. 1; xxi. 7; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 36; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 4, 16; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 1, 2, 20 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ix. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202327">↑</a> Rv. x. 16, 14; Av. xviii. 3, 60; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvii. 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 6, 1, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 17; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 10, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, vi. 4, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202327">↑</a> vii. 103. <i>Cf.</i> Av. iv. 15, 12, as explained by Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 223, where reference is made to frogs in the clefts of the earth (<a class="vei_120a" href="इरिण">iriṇa</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202327">↑</a> <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 494, 495.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202327">↑</a> <i>Rigveda, Kommentar,</i> 117.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202327">↑</a> Geldner, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> very plausibly points out that the last <a class="vei_120a" href="पाद">pāda</a> of this <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> hymn is borrowed from the most important <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> hymn (Rv. iii. 53, 7).</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202327">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,</i> ix. 5; Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 173-179. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 151; <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 121, 122.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202327">↑</a> Rv. x. 16, 14. See Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 11, 342350; Lanman in Whitney's Translation of the Atharvaveda, 850.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202327">↑</a> Av. vii. 116. See Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 565.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 95.</li></ol></div>

मत्स्य|मत्स्य|మత్స్య_౧|మత్స్య|ಮತ್ಸ್ಯ_೧|ಮತ್ಸ್ಯ|matsya_1|matsya|_vei_120@2328|_vei2_pn121
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2327">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2329">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn120">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn121">2:121</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn122">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Matsya,</b> ‘fish,’ is mentioned only once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202328">1</a></sup> but frequently later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202328">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202328">↑</a> x. 68, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202328">↑</a> Av. xi. 2, 25; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 9, 5; 14, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 21, 34; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 6, 1; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 1, 1 (the famous fish of the deluge legend); Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 4, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 2; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="महा-मत्स्य">mahā-matsya</a>,</i> ‘great fish,’ Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 3, 18. In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 3, 12 (<i>cf.</i> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 7, 8; Sāṅkhāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 2, 23), a <a class="vei_120a" href="मत्स्य">matsya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सांमद">sāṃmada</a> is personfied as the king of the fishes.</li></ol></div>

मत्स्य|मत्स्य|మత్స్య_౨|మత్స్య|ಮತ್ಸ್ಯ_೨|ಮತ್ಸ್ಯ|matsya_2|matsya|_vei_120@2329|_vei2_pn121
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2328">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2330">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn120">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn121">2:121</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn122">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Matsya</b> appears to be the name of a people in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202329"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202329">1</a></sup> where they are ranged with the other enemies of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>,</b> although it is possible to see merely the sense of ‘fish’ in that passage. In the list of Aśvamedhins, ‘offerers of the horse sacrifice,’ in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202329"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202329">2</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ध्वसन् द्वैतवन">dhvasan dvaitavana</a></b> is mentioned as a <a class="vei_120a" href="मत्स्य">matsya</a> king (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="मात्स्य">mātsya</a></b>). The Matsyas as a people occur also in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202329"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202329">3</a></sup> in  connexion with the <b>Vaśas,</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202329"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202329">4</a></sup> and in the Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202329"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202329">5</a></sup> in connexion with <b>Śālvas.</b> In <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202329"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202329">6</a></sup> the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>,</b> the Matsyas, the <b>Pancālas,</b> and the <b>Śūrasenakas</b> comprise the land of the Brahmin <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषिस्">ṛṣis</a> (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मर्षि-देश">brahmarṣi-deśa</a></i>). There is no reason to doubt that the Matsyas occupied much the same territory as in Epic times, say Alwar, Jaipur, Bharatpur.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202329"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202329">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202329"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202329">↑</a> vii. 18, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202329"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202329">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202329"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202329">↑</a> iv. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202329"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202329">↑</a> This is the most probable reading, which results from a comparison with Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 9, where <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शाल्व">śālva</a>-Matsyeṣu</i> is followed by <i>savaśaUśīnareṣu</i> (misprinted <i>śavaśa-</i>). See Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 367. The older view was <i>SatvanMatsyeṣu,</i> Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 1, lxxvii, following Cowell; St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. <a class="vei_120a" href="सत्वन्त्">satvant</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202329"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202329">↑</a> i. 2, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202329"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202329">↑</a> ii. 19; vii. 193.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202329"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202329">↑</a> See Vincent Smith, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 56, 675.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 166; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 211; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 127.</li></ol></div>

मदा-वती|मदा-वति|मदावती|मदावति|मदा|वती|वति|మదా-వతీ|మదా-వతి|మదావతీ|మదావతి|మదా|వతీ|వతి|ಮದಾ-ವತೀ|ಮದಾ-ವತಿ|ಮದಾವತೀ|ಮದಾವತಿ|ಮದಾ|ವತೀ|ವತಿ|madā-vatī|madā-vati|madāvatī|madāvati|madā|vatī|vati|madaa-vatii|madaa-vati|madaavatii|madaavati|madaa|vatii|madaa-vatee|madaavatee|vatee|_vei_120@2330|_vei2_pn122
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2329">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2331">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn121">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn122">2:122</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn123">↷</a><hr><p><b>Madā-vatī,</b> ‘intoxicating,’ is the name of a plant in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202330">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202330"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202330">↑</a> vi. 16, 2; <i>cf.</i> iv. 7, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 292; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 465; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 72.</li></ol></div>

मदुघ|మదుఘ|ಮದುಘ|madugha|_vei_120@2331|_vei2_pn122
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2330">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2332">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn121">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn122">2:122</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn123">↷</a><hr><p><b>Madugha,</b> ‘honey-plant,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202331"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202331">1</a></sup> is the name of a sweet herb in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202331"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202331">2</a></sup> The spelling is somewhat uncertain, since many manuscripts read Madhugha.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202331"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202331">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202331"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202331">↑</a> The literal meaning is probably ‘yielding honey,’ the word being, according to the commentator, derived from <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मधु">madhu</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="दुघ">dugha</a>,</i> a word actually occurring in the Rigveda (vi. 70, 1. 5).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202331"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202331">↑</a> i. 34, 4; vi. 102, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 386, n.; 404; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 34, 35, 355; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 275; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 69.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202331"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202331">↑</a> These two forms probably stand by haplology for <i>ma[dhu]-<a class="vei_120a" href="दुघ">dugha</a></i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मधु">madhu</a>-[du]gha. Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> 64, 1<i>a.</i></li></ol></div>

मद्गु|మద్గు|ಮದ್ಗು|madgu|_vei_120@2332|_vei2_pn122
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2331">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2333">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn121">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn122">2:122</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn123">↷</a><hr><p><b>Madgu,</b> ‘diver’ (from the root <i>majj,</i><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202332"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202332">1</a></sup> ‘dive’), is the name of some aquatic bird which is included in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202332"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202332">2</a></sup> and is occasionally mentioned elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202332"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202332">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202332"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202332">↑</a> See Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> 38<i>c;</i> 44<i>a</i> 3<i>a</i>.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202332"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202332">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 20, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 22. 34.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202332"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202332">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 8, 1. 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 93.</li></ol></div>

मद्य|మద్య|ಮದ್ಯ|madya|_vei_120@2333|_vei2_pn123
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2332">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2334">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn122">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn123">2:123</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn124">↷</a><hr><p><b>Madya,</b> ‘intoxicating liquor,’ is not mentioned until the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202333"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202333">1</a></sup> where it occurs in the compound <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मद्य">madya</a>-pā,</i> ‘drinking intoxicating liquor.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202333"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202333">↑</a> v. 11, 5. The word is found in the Epic and often in the Dharmaśāstras, as well as in medical texts.</li></ol></div>

मद्र|మద్ర|ಮದ್ರ|madra|_vei_120@2334|_vei2_pn123
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2333">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2335">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn122">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn123">2:123</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn124">↷</a><hr><p><b>Madra</b> denotes a people who are mentioned in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>;<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202334"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202334">1</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काप्य">kāpya</a> Patañcala</b> was then living among them. Their name appears elsewhere in Vedic literature, only in that of a branch, the <b>Uttara Madras,</b> the ‘northern Madras,’ who are referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202334"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202334">2</a></sup> as living beyond the Himālaya (<i>pareṇa Himavantam</i>) in the neighbourhood of the <b>Uttara Kurus,</b> probably, as Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202334"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202334">3</a></sup> conjectures, in the land of Kaśmīr. The Madras mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> were, like the <b>Kurus,</b> probably settled somewhere in <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a></b> in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a></b> or ‘Middle Land.’ <i>Cf.</i> <b>Madragāra.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202334"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202334">↑</a> iii. 3, 1; 7, 1</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202334"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202334">↑</a> viii. 14, 3</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202334"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202334">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 102.</li></ol></div>

मद्र-गार शौंगायनि|मद्रगार शौंगायनि|मद्र|गार शौंगायनि|मद्र-गार शौङ्गायनि|मद्रगार शौङ्गायनि|गार शौङ्गायनि|మద్ర-గార శౌంగాయని|మద్రగార శౌంగాయని|మద్ర|గార శౌంగాయని|మద్ర-గార శౌఙ్గాయని|మద్రగార శౌఙ్గాయని|గార శౌఙ్గాయని|ಮದ್ರ-ಗಾರ ಶೌಂಗಾಯನಿ|ಮದ್ರಗಾರ ಶೌಂಗಾಯನಿ|ಮದ್ರ|ಗಾರ ಶೌಂಗಾಯನಿ|ಮದ್ರ-ಗಾರ ಶೌಙ್ಗಾಯನಿ|ಮದ್ರಗಾರ ಶೌಙ್ಗಾಯನಿ|ಗಾರ ಶೌಙ್ಗಾಯನಿ|madra-gāra śauṅgāyani|madragāra śauṅgāyani|madra|gāra śauṅgāyani|madra-gaara shauMgaayani|madragaara shauMgaayani|gaara shauMgaayani|madra-gaara shaungaayani|madragaara shaungaayani|gaara shaungaayani|_vei_120@2335|_vei2_pn123
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2334">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2336">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn122">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn123">2:123</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn124">↷</a><hr><p><b>Madra-gāra Śauṅgāyani</b> (‘descendant of Śuṅga’) is the name of a teacher, whose pupil was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काम्बोज">kāmboja</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="औपमन्यव">aupamanyava</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202335"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202335">1</a></sup> Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202335"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202335">2</a></sup> concludes, with probability, that these names point to a connexion of the Kambojas and the Madras.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202335"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202335">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202335"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202335">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 102.</li></ol></div>

मधु|మధు|ಮಧು|madhu|_vei_120@2336|_vei2_pn123
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2335">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2337">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn122">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn123">2:123</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn124">↷</a><hr><p><b>Madhu</b> denotes anything sweet used as food, and especially drink, ‘mead,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202336"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202336">1</a></sup> a sense often found in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202336"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202336">2</a></sup> More precisely it denotes either ‘Soma’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202336"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202336">3</a></sup> or ‘milk,’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202336"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202336">4</a></sup> or less often  ‘honey,’<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202336"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202336">5</a></sup> which, however, is the most definite sense in the later literature. Taboos against the use of honey are recorded.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202336"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202336">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202336"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202336">↑</a> The word is etymologically identical with Greek , ‘intoxicating drink,’ and Anglo-Saxon <i>medu,</i> ‘mead.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202336"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202336">↑</a> Used as an adjective, ‘sweet,’ in Rv. i. 90, 6. 8; 187, 2; iii. 1, 8; iv. 34, 2; 42, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxviii. 10, etc.; as a substantive, Rv. i. 154, 4; ii. 37, 5; iii. 39, 6; iv. 38, 10, etc.; Av. vi. 69, 1; ix. 1, 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 2, 4, 13, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202336"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202336">↑</a> Rv. i. 19, 9; ii. 19, 2; 34, 5; 36, 4; iii. 43, 3; iv. 18, 13, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202336"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202336">↑</a> Rv. i. 117, 6; 169, 4; 177, 3; iii. 8, 1; vii. 24, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, vi. 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202336"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202336">↑</a> Rv. viii. 4, 8 (where the sense is made certain by the adjective <i>sāragha,</i> ‘derived from the bee’); perhaps also iv. 45, 4; vii. 32, 2; viii. 24, 20, and, according to Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 239 <i>et seq.,</i> in many other passages; Av. ix. 1, 17, 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 10, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 9, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 15; viii. 5. 20; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 2, 1. 2; xi. 5, 4, 18; Bṛhadāraṇyaka, ii. 5, 1; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 9, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202336"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202336">↑</a> In the case of women, Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 55, 2; of students, Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 4, 18.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 321; St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

मधुक पैंग्य|मधुक पैङ्ग्य|మధుక పైంగ్య|మధుక పైఙ్గ్య|ಮಧುಕ ಪೈಂಗ್ಯ|ಮಧುಕ ಪೈಙ್ಗ್ಯ|madhuka paiṅgya|madhuka paiMgya|madhuka paingya|_vei_120@2337|_vei2_pn124
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2336">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2338">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn123">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn124">2:124</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn125">↷</a><hr><p><b>Madhuka Paiṅgya</b> (‘descendant of Piṅga’) is the name of a teacher mentioned in the Śatapatha<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202337"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202337">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202337"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202337">2</a></sup> Brāhmaṇas.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202337"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202337">↑</a> xi. 7, 2, 8; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 3, 17. 18 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 3, 8 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202337"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202337">↑</a> xvi. 9.</li></ol></div>

मधु-कशा|मधुकशा|मधु|कशा|మధు-కశా|మధుకశా|మధు|కశా|ಮಧು-ಕಶಾ|ಮಧುಕಶಾ|ಮಧು|ಕಶಾ|madhu-kaśā|madhukaśā|madhu|kaśā|madhu-kashaa|madhukashaa|kashaa|_vei_120@2338|_vei2_pn124
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2337">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2339">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn123">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn124">2:124</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn125">↷</a><hr><p><b>Madhu-kaśā,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202338"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202338">1</a></sup> or <b>Madhoḥ Kasā,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202338"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202338">2</a></sup> is the name in the Rigveda of the Aśvins’ ‘honey-whip,’ by which they impart sweetness to the sacrifice. Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202338"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202338">3</a></sup> ingeniously conjectures that the idea was derived from an instrument provided with thongs for whipping milk, a ‘milk-whip.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202338"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202338">↑</a> Rv. i. 22, 3; 157, 4, Av. x. 7, 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxi. 10, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202338"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202338">↑</a> Av. ix. 1, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202338"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202338">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

मधु-कृत्|मधुकृत्|मधु|कृत्|మధు-కృత్|మధుకృత్|మధు|కృత్|ಮಧು-ಕೃತ್|ಮಧುಕೃತ್|ಮಧು|ಕೃತ್|madhu-kṛt|madhukṛt|madhu|kṛt|madhu-kRt|madhukRt|kRt|madhu-kRit|madhukRit|kRit|madhu-kRut|madhukRut|kRut|madhu-kRRit|madhukRRit|kRRit|_vei_120@2339|_vei2_pn124
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2338">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2340">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn123">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn124">2:124</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn125">↷</a><hr><p><b>Madhu-kṛt,</b> ‘honey-maker,’ denotes ‘bee’ in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202339"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202339">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202339"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202339">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202339"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202339">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i, 5, 6, 5; iv. 2, 9, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202339"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202339">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 10, 10, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 2, 1. 2; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 1, 2; vi. 9, 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

मधु-छन्दस्|मधु-छन्द|मधुछन्दस्|मधुछन्द|मधु|छन्दस्|छन्द|మధు-ఛన్దస్|మధు-ఛన్ద|మధుఛన్దస్|మధుఛన్ద|మధు|ఛన్దస్|ఛన్ద|ಮಧು-ಛನ್ದಸ್|ಮಧು-ಛನ್ದ|ಮಧುಛನ್ದಸ್|ಮಧುಛನ್ದ|ಮಧು|ಛನ್ದಸ್|ಛನ್ದ|madhu-chandas|madhu-chanda|madhuchandas|madhuchanda|madhu|chandas|chanda|madhu-Chandas|madhu-Chanda|madhuChandas|madhuChanda|Chandas|Chanda|_vei_120@2340|_vei2_pn124
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2339">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2341">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn123">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn124">2:124</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn125">↷</a><hr><p><b>Madhu-chandas,</b> the reputed author of the first ten hymns of the first Maṇḍala of the Rigveda, is mentioned as a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202340"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202340">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202340"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202340">2</a></sup> In the  <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202340"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202340">3</a></sup> he counts as the fifty-first son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>,</b> and his Praüga (hymn at the morning service) is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202340"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202340">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202340"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202340">↑</a> xxviii. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202340"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202340">↑</a> i. 1, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202340"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202340">↑</a> vii. 17, 7; 18, 1; <i>cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 26, 1 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 167.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202340"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202340">↑</a> xiii. 5, 1, 8.</li></ol></div>

मधु-ब्राह्मण|मधुब्राह्मण|मधु|ब्राह्मण|మధు-బ్రాహ్మణ|మధుబ్రాహ్మణ|మధు|బ్రాహ్మణ|ಮಧು-ಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ|ಮಧುಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ|ಮಧು|ಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ|madhu-brāhmaṇa|madhubrāhmaṇa|madhu|brāhmaṇa|madhu-braahmaNa|madhubraahmaNa|braahmaNa|_vei_120@2341|_vei2_pn125
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2340">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2342">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn124">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn125">2:125</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn127">↷</a><hr><p><b>Madhu-brāhmaṇa,</b> ‘the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> of the Honey,’ is the designation of a certain mystical doctrine in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202341"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202341">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202341"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202341">↑</a> iv. 1, 5, 18; xiv. 1, 4, 13; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 5, 16. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 290.</li></ol></div>

मध्य-देश|मध्यदेश|मध्य|देश|మధ్య-దేశ|మధ్యదేశ|మధ్య|దేశ|ಮಧ್ಯ-ದೇಶ|ಮಧ್ಯದೇಶ|ಮಧ್ಯ|ದೇಶ|madhya-deśa|madhyadeśa|madhya|deśa|madhya-desha|madhyadesha|desha|_vei_120@2342|_vei2_pn125
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2341">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2343">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn124">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn125">2:125</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn127">↷</a><hr><p><b>Madhya-deśa,</b> the ‘Middle Country,’ is, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Śāstra,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202342">1</a></sup> the land between the Himālaya in the north, the Vindhya in the south, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विनशन">vinaśana</a></b> in the west, and Prayāga (now Allahabad) in the east--that is, between the place where the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a></b> disappears in the desert, and the point of the confluence of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a></b> (Jumna) and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गंगा">gaṅgā</a></b> (Ganges). The same authority<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202342">2</a></sup> defines <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मर्षि-देश">brahmarṣi-deśa</a> as denoting the land of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>,</b> the <b>Matsyas, Pañcālas,</b> and <b>Śūrasenakas,</b> and <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मावर्त">brahmāvarta</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202342">3</a></sup> as meaning the particularly holy land between the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृषद्वती">dṛṣadvatī</a>.</b> The <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202342">4</a></sup> defines Āryāvarta as the land east of <a class="vei_120a" href="विनशन">vinaśana</a>; west of the Kālaka-<a class="vei_120a" href="वन">vana</a>, ‘Black Forest,’ or rather Kanakhala, near Hardvār; south of the Himālaya; and north of the Pāriyātra or the Pāripātra Mountains; adding that, in the opinion of others,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202342">5</a></sup> it was confined to the country between the <a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="गंगा">gaṅgā</a>, while the <b>Bhāllavins</b><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202342">6</a></sup> took it as the country between the boundary-river (or perhaps the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्">saras</a> vatī)<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202342">7</a></sup> and the region where the sun rises. The <a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Śāstra,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202342">8</a></sup> in accord with the <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202342">9</a></sup> defines Āryāvarta as the region between the Vindhya and the Himālaya, the two ranges which seem to be the boundaries of the Āryan world in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> also.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202342">10</a></sup></p><p>The term <a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a> is not Vedic, but it is represented in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202342">11</a></sup> by the expression <i>madhyamā <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतिष्ठा">pratiṣṭhā</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दिश्">diś</a>,</i> ‘the middle fixed region,’ the inhabitants of which are stated to be the Kurus, the Pañcālas, the <b>Vaśas,</b> and the <b>Uśīnaras.</b> The latter two peoples practically disappear later on, the <a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a> being the country of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas, the land where the Brāhmaṇas and the later Saṃhitās were produced, bounded on the east by the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a>-Videhas,</b> and on the west by the desert. The western tribes are mentioned with disapproval both in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202342">12</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202342">13</a></sup> while the tradition of the Brahminization of the Kosalas and the Videhas from the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a> country is preserved in the former <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1202342">14</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202342">↑</a> ii. 21.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202342">↑</a> ii. 19.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202342">↑</a> ii. 17. 19.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202342">↑</a> i. 2, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202342">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>, i. 2, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>, i. 12. See on Kanakhala, Hultzsch, <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 34, 179.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202342">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>, i. 2, 11. 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>, i. 14. 15, quoting in each case a verse of the <a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a> (what work is referred to is not certain; there is similar doubt as to the quotation in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a> of the <a class="vei_120a" href="भाल्लवि">bhāllavi</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, according to the Bṛhaddevatā, v. 23, where see Macdonell's note, and <i>cf.</i> Bühler, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 14, 3, n.).</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202342">↑</a> The readings are doubtful, varying between <i>sindhur vidhāraṇī</i> or <i>vidharaṇī</i> and <i>sindhur vicaraṇī</i> or <i>visavaṇī.</i> The latter expression must refer to the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>; the former may, but not necessarily. Conceivably the <a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धु">sindhu</a> (Indus) is meant; for it was a great boundary, with Āryan tribes to the east of it.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202342">↑</a> ii. 22.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202342">↑</a> i. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202342">↑</a> ii. 13. <i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 28, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202342">↑</a> viii. 14, 3. The Uśīnaras may be recognized as in the north, for the Buddhist texts give Usīragiri as the northern boundary of the middle country. See Hultzsch, <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 34, 179.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202342">↑</a> ix. 3, 1, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202342">↑</a> iii. 44, 3; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 245.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1202342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1202342">↑</a> i. 4, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bühler, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 14, 2, 3; 146, 147, who points out that the Pāripātra Mountains are a part of the Vindhya range in Mālvā, and who suggests that the western boundary was originally the <a class="vei_120a" href="आदर्श">ādarśa</a> Mountains; for the reading of the manuscripts, and of the scholiast Kṛṣṇapaṇḍita, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 8, is <i>prāg ādarśanāt,</i> not <i>adarśanāt</i> (corresponding with the <a class="vei_120a" href="विनशन">vinaśana</a> of <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 2, 9), and the Mahābhāṣya on Pāṇini, ii. 4, 10, has <i>prāg ādarśāt.</i> See also for the Buddhist ‘Middle Country’ an article by Rhys Davids, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1904, 83 <i>et seq.,</i> with Fleet's corrections, <i>ibid.,</i> 1907, 657; and <i>cf.</i> Keith, <i>ibid.,</i> 1908, 1143; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 58, 59; <i>Indian Empire,</i> 1, 303, 304, where the extraordinary theory is adopted that the <a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a> was peopled by a new race of immigrant Āryans, who, travelling viâ Chitral and Gilgit, and bringing no women with them, married Dravidian women, and produced the so-called Āryo-Dravidians. It is quite impossible to find any support for this theory in Vedic literature. To say, as is there said, that the ‘Vedic hymns contain no reference to the route by which the Āryans entered India or to their earlier settlements on the Indus,’ and that this is explained by the theory of the entry of the Vedic Indians viâ Chitral, is to assert absurdities. The theory is based on the later dialects and their affinities (see Grierson, <i>Indian Empire,</i> 1, 357 <i>et seq.</i>); it can probably not be regarded as at all valid for any period--at any rate, it is not cogent for the eighth century, B.C.</li></ol></div>

मध्यं-दिन|मध्यंदिन|मध्यं|दिन|मध्यन्दिन|మధ్యం-దిన|మధ్యందిన|మధ్యం|దిన|మధ్యన్దిన|ಮಧ್ಯಂ-ದಿನ|ಮಧ್ಯಂದಿನ|ಮಧ್ಯಂ|ದಿನ|ಮಧ್ಯನ್ದಿನ|madhyaṃ-dina|madhyaṃdina|madhyaṃ|dina|madhyaM-dina|madhyaMdina|madhyaM|madhyandina|_vei_120@2343|_vei2_pn127
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2342">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2344">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn125">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn127">2:127</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn128">↷</a><hr><p><b>Madhyaṃ-dina,</b> ‘mid-day,’ is a frequent designation of time in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202343"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202343">1</a></sup> the later Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202343"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202343">2</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202343"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202343">3</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अहन्">ahan</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202343"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202343">↑</a> iv. 28, 3; viii. 1, 29; 13, 13; 27, 19; x. 151, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202343"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202343">↑</a> Av. ix. 6, 46; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 5, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202343"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202343">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xv. 9, 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 3, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 2, 3, 9; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 9, 6; 14, 1, etc. The word is sometimes used as an abbreviation for the ‘midday libation’ (like <i>mittag</i> in German for ‘midday meal’) in <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 10, 2, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxix. 8.</li></ol></div>

मध्यम-वह्|मध्यमवह्|मध्यम|वह्|మధ్యమ-వహ్|మధ్యమవహ్|మధ్యమ|వహ్|ಮಧ್ಯಮ-ವಹ್|ಮಧ್ಯಮವಹ್|ಮಧ್ಯಮ|ವಹ್|madhyama-vah|madhyamavah|madhyama|vah|_vei_120@2344|_vei2_pn127
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2343">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2345">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn125">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn127">2:127</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn128">↷</a><hr><p><b>Madhyama-vah</b> occurs in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202344"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202344">1</a></sup> as an epithet of the chariot. The exact interpretation is doubtful. Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202344"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202344">2</a></sup> assigns to it the expression the sense of ‘driving with a single horse between the shafts.’ According to Sāyana's explanation, it means ‘driving with middling speed.’ It might mean ‘driving in the middle’--that is, ‘only half-way.’^3</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202344"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202344">↑</a> ii. 29, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202344"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202344">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 210. The context seems to require the sense of ‘keeping away’ from the sacrifice.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पूर्ववह्">pūrvavah</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

मध्यम-शी|मध्यम-शि|मध्यमशी|मध्यमशि|मध्यम|शी|शि|మధ్యమ-శీ|మధ్యమ-శి|మధ్యమశీ|మధ్యమశి|మధ్యమ|శీ|శి|ಮಧ್ಯಮ-ಶೀ|ಮಧ್ಯಮ-ಶಿ|ಮಧ್ಯಮಶೀ|ಮಧ್ಯಮಶಿ|ಮಧ್ಯಮ|ಶೀ|ಶಿ|madhyama-śī|madhyama-śi|madhyamaśī|madhyamaśi|madhyama|śī|śi|madhyama-shii|madhyama-shi|madhyamashii|madhyamashi|shii|shi|madhyama-shee|madhyamashee|shee|_vei_120@2345|_vei2_pn127
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2344">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2346">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn125">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn127">2:127</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn128">↷</a><hr><p><b>Madhyama-śī</b> is found in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202345">1</a></sup> where Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202345">2</a></sup> assigns to the word the meaning of <i>intercessor,</i> which Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202345">3</a></sup> accepts, in the sense of ‘mediator’ or ‘arbiter,’ as a legal term, but which Roth may, as Lanman<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202345">4</a></sup> suggests, have intended to express ‘adversary’ or ‘preventer’ of the disease referred to in the hymn. Whitney<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202345">5</a></sup> thinks that it means ‘mid most man’ or ‘chief’ as the one round whom his followers encamp.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202345">6</a></sup> Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202345">7</a></sup> however, thinks that a third king, who is ‘neutral’ between two enemies, is intended.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202345">↑</a> x. 97, 12 = Av. iv. 9, 4 = Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xii. 86.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202345">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202345">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 180. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202345">↑</a> In Whitney's Translation of the Atharvaveda, 159. But see Roth, <i>Siebenzig Lieder,</i> 174, which Lanman may have overlooked, since he does not refer to it.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202345">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202345">↑</a> <i>Madhyamaśivan,</i> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 408, is obscure.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202345">↑</a> <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 131; <i>Kommentar,</i> 196 (where he seems to decide in favour of derivation from <i>śī,</i> not <i>śṛ</i>).</li></ol></div>

मध्यम-स्थ|मध्यमस्थ|मध्यम|स्थ|మధ్యమ-స్థ|మధ్యమస్థ|మధ్యమ|స్థ|ಮಧ್ಯಮ-ಸ್ಥ|ಮಧ್ಯಮಸ್ಥ|ಮಧ್ಯಮ|ಸ್ಥ|madhyama-stha|madhyamastha|madhyama|stha|_vei_120@2346|_vei2_pn128
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2345">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2347">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn127">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn128">2:128</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn129">↷</a><hr><p><b>Madhyama-stha,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202346"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202346">1</a></sup> <b>Madhyame-<a class="vei_120a" href="ष्ठ">ṣṭha</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202346"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202346">2</a></sup> in the later Saṃhitās denotes the chief in his relation to his followers <i>(<a class="vei_120a" href="सजात">sajāta</a>). Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यमशी">madhyamaśī</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202346"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202346">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxvii. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202346"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202346">↑</a> Av. iii. 8, 2, and <i>cf. Madhyamastheya,</i> ‘position of chief,’ <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 4, 5, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 96.</li></ol></div>

मध्या-वर्ष|मध्यावर्ष|मध्या|वर्ष|మధ్యా-వర్ష|మధ్యావర్ష|మధ్యా|వర్ష|ಮಧ್ಯಾ-ವರ್ಷ|ಮಧ್ಯಾವರ್ಷ|ಮಧ್ಯಾ|ವರ್ಷ|madhyā-varṣa|madhyāvarṣa|madhyā|varṣa|madhyaa-varSha|madhyaavarSha|madhyaa|varSha|_vei_120@2347|_vei2_pn128
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2346">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2348">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn127">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn128">2:128</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn129">↷</a><hr><p><b>Madhyā-varṣa,</b> the ‘middle of the rains,’ is specially mentioned as a time of the year in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202347"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202347">1</a></sup> and in the Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202347"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202347">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202347"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202347">↑</a> i. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202347"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202347">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 5, 5. 7, etc.</li></ol></div>

मनस|మనస|ಮನಸ|manasa|_vei_120@2348|_vei2_pn128
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2347">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2349">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn127">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn128">2:128</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn129">↷</a><hr><p><b>Manasa,</b> occurring in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202348"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202348">1</a></sup> seems clearly to be the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>, in accordance with Sāyaṇa's interpretation.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202348"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202348">↑</a> v. 44, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 139.</li></ol></div>

मना|మనా|ಮನಾ|manā|manaa|_vei_120@2349|_vei2_pn128
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2348">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2350">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn127">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn128">2:128</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn129">↷</a><hr><p><b>Manā</b> is found in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202349">1</a></sup> in an enumeration of gifts, where it is described as ‘golden’ (<i>sacā <a class="vei_120a" href="मना">manā</a> hiraṇyayā</i>). It therefore seems to designate some ornament, or possibly a weight, and has accordingly been compared<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202349">2</a></sup> with the Greek  (Herodotus has ), the Latin <i>mina.</i> All three words have been considered Semitic in origin, as borrowed from the Phœnicians<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202349">3</a></sup> in the case of Greece, from Carthage by way  of Etruria or Sicily in the case of Rome, and from Babylon in the case of India. The identification as regards <a class="vei_120a" href="मना">manā</a> is very conjectural, depending merely on the probabilities of Babylonian borrowing<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202349">4</a></sup> seen--<i>e.g.,</i> in the legend of the flood, and in the system of the <b>Nakṣatras.</b> On the other hand, <a class="vei_120a" href="महा">mahā</a> may very well be identical with the word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मना">manā</a></i> which occurs several times in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202349">5</a></sup> in the sense of ‘desire’ (from the root <i>man,</i> ‘think’), and which may have in this one passage the concrete sense of ‘desirable object.’ It is to be noted that in Bo7htlingk's Dictionary a single word <a class="vei_120a" href="मना">manā</a> appears, to which the only senses assigned are ‘wish,’ ‘desire,’ ‘jealousy.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202349">↑</a> viii. 78, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202349">↑</a> As. <i>e.g.,</i> by Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 50, 51; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 386; 17, 202, 203; Wackernagel, <i>Altindische Grammatik,</i> 1, xxii; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, 278.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202349">↑</a> Or perhaps from Babylon viâ Asia Minor. The part played by the Phœnicians in Greek life is now reduced within narrow limits; in the case of the <i>mina,</i> probably their commercial activities may be considered as likely to have caused the adoption of the term.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202349">↑</a> See, <i>e.g.,</i> for borrowing, Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 276; <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 50, 43 <i>et seq.;</i> Bühler, <i>Indian Studies,</i> 3, 16 <i>et seq.; Indische Palœographie,</i> 17; Vincent Smith, <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 34, 230. On the other side, <i>cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>India,</i> 133-138; Hopkins, <i>Religions of India,</i> 160; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 139 (as regards the flood legend): Bloomfield, <i>Religions of India,</i> 133 <i>et seq.</i> (as regards the Ādityas).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202349"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202349">↑</a> i. 173, 2; iv. 33, 2, x. 6, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iv. 19; ‘jealousy, Rv. ii. 33, 5, Kanśika <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, cvii. 2. There are also the derivatives <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मना">manā</a>-ya,</i> ‘think of,’ ‘be zealous’: Rv. i. 133, 4; ii. 26, 2; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मना">manā</a>-ya,</i> ‘desirous’: Rv. i. 92, 9; iv. 24, 7; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मना">manā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वसु">vasu</a>,</i> ‘rich in devotion’: Rv. v. 74, 1.</li></ol></div>

मनावी|मनावि|మనావీ|మనావి|ಮನಾವೀ|ಮನಾವಿ|manāvī|manāvi|manaavii|manaavi|manaavee|_vei_120@2350|_vei2_pn129
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2349">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2351">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn128">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn129">2:129</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn130">↷</a><hr><p><b>Manāvī,</b> ‘wife of <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>,’ is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202350">1</a></sup> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202350">2</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202350">↑</a> xxx. 1 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 462).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202350">↑</a> i. 1, 4, 16.</li></ol></div>

मनु|మను|ಮನು|manu|_vei_120@2351|_vei2_pn129
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2350">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2352">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn128">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn129">2:129</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn130">↷</a><hr><p><b>Manu</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202351">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202351">2</a></sup> has no claim to historical reality. He is simply the first man, father of the race, and its guide in all matters, sacrificial and other. Hence the views of the texts on inheritance are foisted on <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a> and his youngest son, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नाभानेदिष्ठ">nābhānediṣṭha</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202351">3</a></sup> He also plays the part of the hero in the Vedic legend of the flood.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202351">4</a></sup> </p><p><a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a> is called Vivasvan<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202351">5</a></sup> or Vaivasvata,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202351">6</a></sup> ‘son of Vivasvant’ (the god); <a class="vei_120a" href="सावर्णि">sāvarṇi</a>,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202351">6</a></sup> ‘descendant of Savarṇā’ (the substitute of Saraṇyū in the legend of her wedding); and <a class="vei_120a" href="सांवरणि">sāṃvaraṇi</a>,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202351">7</a></sup> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="संवरण">saṃvaraṇa</a>.’ The first name is, of course, mythical. The other two have been regarded as historical, <a class="vei_120a" href="सावर्णि">sāvarṇi</a> being taken by Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202351">8</a></sup> as a king of the <b>Turvaśas,</b> but this is very doubtful.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202351">↑</a> i. 80, 16; ii. 33. 13; viii. 63. 1; x. 100, 5, etc. See Macdonell, <i>Velk Mythology,</i> 50.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202351">↑</a> Av. xiv. 2, 41; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 5, 1, 3; vii. 5. 15, 3; ii. 5, 9, 1; 6, 7, 1; iii. 3, 2, 1; v. 4, 10, 5; vi. 6. 6, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā. viii. 15; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 4. 14, etc. Jainiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 15, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202351">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 1, 9, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 14, 1. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202351">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 1, 1 <i>et seq.,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202351">↑</a> Rv. viii. 52, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202351">↑</a> Av. viii. 10, 24; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 3, 3; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, xii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202351">↑</a> Av. viii. 10, 24; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 3, 3; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, xii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202351">↑</a> Rv. viii. 51, 1; Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 180, n., conjectures <a class="vei_120a" href="सावर्णि">sāvarṇi</a> instead. <i>Cf.</i> Scheftelowitz, <i>Die Apokryphen des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 38.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202351"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202351">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 166. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 195; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 11, 240; Lévi, <i>La Doctrine du Sacrifice,</i> 114 <i>et seq.;</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit texts,</i> 1^2, 161 <i>et seq.;</i> Bühler, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 25, lvii <i>et seq.;</i> Lanman, <i>Sanskrit Reader,</i> 340 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

मनोर् अवसर्पण|మనోర్ అవసర్పణ|ಮನೋರ್ ಅವಸರ್ಪಣ|manor avasarpaṇa|manor avasarpaNa|_vei_120@2352|_vei2_pn130
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2351">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2353">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn129">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn130">2:130</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn131">↷</a><hr><p><b>Manor Avasarpaṇa</b> is the name, in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202352"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202352">1</a></sup> of the mountain on which the vessel of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a></b> rested. In the Epic the name is Naubandhana, but the view<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202352"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202352">2</a></sup> that it is alluded to as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नावप्रभ्रंशन">nāvaprabhraṃśana</a></b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202352"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202352">3</a></sup> is now abandoned.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202352"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202352">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202352"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202352">↑</a> i. 8, 1, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202352"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202352">↑</a> See Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 139; Whitney, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 162; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 30; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 676.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202352"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202352">↑</a> xix. 39, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202352"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202352">↑</a> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 961; Macdonell, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1907, 1107.</li></ol></div>

मनुष्य-राज|मनुष्यराज|मनुष्य|राज|మనుష్య-రాజ|మనుష్యరాజ|మనుష్య|రాజ|ಮನುಷ್ಯ-ರಾಜ|ಮನುಷ್ಯರಾಜ|ಮನುಷ್ಯ|ರಾಜ|manuṣya-rāja|manuṣyarāja|manuṣya|rāja|manuShya-raaja|manuShyaraaja|manuShya|raaja|_vei_120@2353|_vei2_pn130
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2352">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2354">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn129">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn130">2:130</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn131">↷</a><hr><p><b>Manuṣya-rāja</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202353"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202353">1</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मनुष्य">manuṣya</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a></b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202353"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202353">2</a></sup> denote in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas a ‘king of men.’ <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202353"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202353">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 30; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 15, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxiv. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202353"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202353">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xviii. 10, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 26, 4.</li></ol></div>

मनुष्य-विश्|मनुष्यविश्|मनुष्य|विश्|మనుష్య-విశ్|మనుష్యవిశ్|మనుష్య|విశ్|ಮನುಷ್ಯ-ವಿಶ್|ಮನುಷ್ಯವಿಶ್|ಮನುಷ್ಯ|ವಿಶ್|manuṣya-viś|manuṣyaviś|manuṣya|viś|manuShya-vish|manuShyavish|manuShya|vish|_vei_120@2354|_vei2_pn130
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2353">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2355">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn129">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn130">2:130</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn131">↷</a><hr><p><b>Manuṣya-viś,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202354">1</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मनुष्य">manuṣya</a>-viśa,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202354">2</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मनुष्य">manuṣya</a>-viśā</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202354">3</a></sup> denote ‘mankind,’ ‘the human race,’ in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202354">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 9, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202354">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 4, 7, 7; vi. 1, 5, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202354">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 6; xxiii. 8.</li></ol></div>

मन्त्र|మన్త్ర|ಮನ್ತ್ರ|mantra|_vei_120@2355|_vei2_pn131
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2354">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2356">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn130">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn131">2:131</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn132">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mantra</b> (from the root <i>man,</i> ‘think’) denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202355"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202355">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202355"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202355">2</a></sup> the ‘hymn’ as the product of the singer's creative thought. In the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202355"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202355">3</a></sup> the word is regularly used of the poetic and prose utterances of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषिस्">ṛṣis</a>, including not merely the verse parts of the Saṃhitās, but also the prose formulæ that betray by their style their special and archaic character.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202355"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202355">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202355"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202355">↑</a> i. 31, 13; 40, 5; 67, 4; 74, 1; 152, 2; ii. 35, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202355"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202355">↑</a> Av. xv. 2, 1; xix. 54, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 5, 4, 1; 5, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202355"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202355">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 14, 23; vi. 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxvi. 3. 5; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 4, 6; xi. 2, 1, 6, <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vii. 1, etc.; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vii. 1, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202355"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202355">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>Vedic Concordance,</i> viii; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 298. Macdonel's <i>Vedic Grammar</i> covers the <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> material of the Vedic Saṃhitās, prose as well as poetry.</li></ol></div>

मन्त्र-कृत्|मन्त्रकृत्|मन्त्र|कृत्|మన్త్ర-కృత్|మన్త్రకృత్|మన్త్ర|కృత్|ಮನ್ತ್ರ-ಕೃತ್|ಮನ್ತ್ರಕೃತ್|ಮನ್ತ್ರ|ಕೃತ್|mantra-kṛt|mantrakṛt|mantra|kṛt|mantra-kRt|mantrakRt|kRt|mantra-kRit|mantrakRit|kRit|mantra-kRut|mantrakRut|kRut|mantra-kRRit|mantrakRRit|kRRit|_vei_120@2356|_vei2_pn131
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2355">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2357">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn130">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn131">2:131</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn132">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mantra-kṛt</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202356"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202356">1</a></sup> and the <b>Brāhmaṇas</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202356"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202356">2</a></sup> denotes a poet as a ‘maker of Mantras.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202356"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202356">↑</a> ix. 114, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202356"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202356">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 1, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 3, 24; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, iv. 1.</li></ol></div>

मन्थ|మన్థ|ಮನ್ಥ|mantha|_vei_120@2357|_vei2_pn131
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2356">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2358">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn130">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn131">2:131</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn132">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mantha</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202357"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202357">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202357"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202357">2</a></sup> denotes a drink in which solid ingredients are mixed with a fluid by stirring, usually parched barley-meal (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="सक्तु">saktu</a></b>) with milk.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202357"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202357">3</a></sup> All sorts of mixed beverages of this type are mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202357"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202357">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202357"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202357">↑</a> x. 86, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202357"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202357">↑</a> Av. ii. 29, 6; v. 29, 7; x. 6, 2; xviii. 4, 42; xx. 127, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 5, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202357"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202357">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 2, 1, 2; Suśruta, 1, 233, 12, in St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 1<i>b ad fin.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202357"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202357">↑</a> xii. 8.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 268, 269; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology, 108</i></li></ol></div>

मन्था|మన్థా|ಮನ್ಥಾ|manthā|manthaa|_vei_120@2358|_vei2_pn131
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2357">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2359">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn130">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn131">2:131</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn132">↷</a><hr><p><b>Manthā</b> in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202358"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202358">1</a></sup> seems to mean a ‘churn.’ So the root <i>math</i> denotes to ‘churn’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202358"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202358">2</a></sup> In one passage of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202358"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202358">3</a></sup> the word is used to denote a drink like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मन्थ">mantha</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202358"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202358">↑</a> i. 28, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202358"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202358">↑</a> ii. 2, 10, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 2, 6; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 6, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 161.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202358"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202358">↑</a> xx. 127, 9. Scheftelowitz in <a class="vei_120a" href="खिल">khila</a>, v. 10, 3, reads <i>manthāṁ</i> with Pluti, following the Kaśmīr MS., but misquoting the <a class="vei_120a" href="अथर्वन्">atharvan</a> text.</li></ol></div>

मन्थावल|మన్థావల|ಮನ್ಥಾವಲ|manthāvala|manthaavala|_vei_120@2359|_vei2_pn132
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2358">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2360">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn131">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn132">2:132</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn133">↷</a><hr><p><b>Manthāvala</b> is the name of an animal in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202359"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202359">1</a></sup> a sort of snake according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary. Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202359"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202359">2</a></sup> understands it to be a kind of animal which hangs head downwards from the branches of trees, meaning, presumably, the flying fox.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202359"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202359">3</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मान्थाल">mānthāla</a>, Mānthīlava.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202359"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202359">↑</a> iii. 26, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202359"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202359">↑</a> P. 291 (ed. Aufrecht). <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 86.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202359"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202359">↑</a> This is the probable meaning of the word according to Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

मन्थिन्|మన్థిన్|ಮನ್ಥಿನ್|manthin|_vei_120@2360|_vei2_pn132
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2359">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2361">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn131">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn132">2:132</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn133">↷</a><hr><p><b>Manthin</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202360"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202360">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202360"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202360">2</a></sup> denotes <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> juice mixed with meal (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="सक्तु">saktu</a></b>) by stirring.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202360"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202360">↑</a> iii. 32, 2; ix. 46, 4. Tilak's conjecture that the planets are referred to here is absurd. See <i>Orion,</i> 162; Whitney, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, xciv.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202360"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202360">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 1, 6, 3; vi. 4, 10, 1; vii. 2, 7, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, vii. 18; viii. 57; xiii. 57; xviii. 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 6, etc.</li></ol></div>

मन्दीर|మన్దీర|ಮನ್ದೀರ|mandīra|mandiira|mandeera|_vei_120@2361|_vei2_pn132
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2360">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2362">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn131">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn132">2:132</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn133">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mandīra</b> is perhaps the name of a man whose cattle, according to a <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> in the Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (xiii. 3, 21), did not drink the water of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गंगा">gaṅgā</a></b> (Ganges). See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मंगीर">maṅgīra</a>.</b></p></div>

मन्धातृ|मन्धातृ|మన్ధాతృ_౧|మన్ధాతృ|ಮನ್ಧಾತೃ_೧|ಮನ್ಧಾತೃ|mandhātṛ_1|mandhātṛ|mandhaatR_1|mandhaatR|mandhaatRi_1|mandhaatRi|mandhaatRu_1|mandhaatRu|mandhaatRRi_1|mandhaatRRi|_vei_120@2362|_vei2_pn132
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2361">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2363">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn131">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn132">2:132</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn133">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Mandhātṛ</b> occurs in several passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202362">1</a></sup> in all of which Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202362">2</a></sup> takes the word as merely an adjective used substantively, ‘the pious man.’ In one passage<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202362">3</a></sup> the word, being applied to <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>, is thus used, but in another<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202362">4</a></sup> <i>Mandhātṛvat</i> being parallel with <i>Aṅgirasvat,</i> ‘like <a class="vei_120a" href="अंगिरस्">aṅgiras</a>,’ is naturally to be understood as a proper name, which is probably also the sense of the word in the preceding hymn.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202362">5</a></sup> A different <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्धातृ">mandhātṛ</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202362">6</a></sup> may be meant in the first Maṇḍala,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202362">6</a></sup> where he is mentioned as a protégé of the Aśvins, and evidently as a king. To equate these persons, and make a Rājarṣi out of <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्धातृ">mandhātṛ</a>, as Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202362">7</a></sup> and Griffith<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202362">8</a></sup> do, is unnecessary and improbable. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202362">↑</a> i. 112, 13; viii. 39, 8; 40, 12; x. 2, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202362">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202362">↑</a> Rv. x. 2, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202362">↑</a> Rv. viii. 40, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202362">↑</a> Rv. viii. 39, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202362">↑</a> Rv. i. 112, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202362">↑</a> Rv. i. 112, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202362">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 107. where he attributes Rv. viii. 39-42 to him as a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नाभाक">nābhāka</a>,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नभाक">nabhāka</a>.</b>’</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202362"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202362">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 1, 147.</li></ol></div>

मन्धातृ यौवनाश्व|मन्धातृ यौवनाश्व|మన్ధాతృ యౌవనాశ్వ_౨|మన్ధాతృ యౌవనాశ్వ|ಮನ್ಧಾತೃ ಯೌವನಾಶ್ವ_೨|ಮನ್ಧಾತೃ ಯೌವನಾಶ್ವ|mandhātṛ yauvanāśva_2|mandhātṛ yauvanāśva|mandhaatR yauvanaashva_2|mandhaatR yauvanaashva|mandhaatRi yauvanaashva_2|mandhaatRi yauvanaashva|mandhaatRu yauvanaashva_2|mandhaatRu yauvanaashva|mandhaatRRi yauvanaashva_2|mandhaatRRi yauvanaashva|_vei_120@2363|_vei2_pn133
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2362">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2364">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn132">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn133">2:133</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn134">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Mandhātṛ Yauvanāśva</b> (‘descendant of Yuvanāśva’) is in the Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202363">1</a></sup> the name of an emperor who was instructed by <b>Vicārin,</b> son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कबन्ध आथर्वण">kabandha ātharvaṇa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202363">↑</a> i. 2, 10 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 111.</li></ol></div>

मन्या|మన్యా|ಮನ್ಯಾ|manyā|manyaa|_vei_120@2364|_vei2_pn133
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2363">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2365">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn132">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn133">2:133</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn134">↷</a><hr><p><b>Manyā</b> (plur.), ‘nape of the neck,’ occurs in a passage of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202364"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202364">1</a></sup> directed against a disease which Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202364"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202364">2</a></sup> regards as scrofulous swellings on the neck. He compares the disease Manskunder, ‘tumours of the neck’ (which looks like a combination of the words <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मन्या">manyā</a></i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्कन्ध्या">skandhyā</a>,</i> ‘pains of the neck and shoulders,’ both occurring in verses 1 and 3 of the <a class="vei_120a" href="अथर्वन्">atharvan</a> hymn), mentioned by Wise.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202364"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202364">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202364"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202364">↑</a> vi. 25, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202364"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202364">↑</a> <i>Proceedings of the American Oriental Society,</i> October, 1887, xix; <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 11, 327 <i>et seq.; Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 472.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202364"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202364">↑</a> <i>System of Hindu Medicine,</i> 316.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 202; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 298, 299.</li></ol></div>

ममता|మమతా|ಮಮತಾ|mamatā|mamataa|_vei_120@2365|_vei2_pn133
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2364">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2366">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn132">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn133">2:133</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn134">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mamatā</b> is, according to Sāyaṇa, in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202365"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202365">1</a></sup> the wife of Ucathya and the mother of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दीर्घतमस्">dīrghatamas</a>.</b> But the word may be merely an abstract noun meaning ‘selfinterest,’ a sense which it often has in the later language. Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202365"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202365">2</a></sup> finds a mention of Mamata (masc.) in a verse of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202365"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202365">3</a></sup> as the name of a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202365"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202365">↑</a> vi. 10, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Mahābhārata, i. 4179 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202365"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202365">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenlāndischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 212.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202365"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202365">↑</a> vi. 50, 15, where the reading of the received text is <i>mama tasya.</i></li></ol></div>

मय|మయ|ಮಯ|maya|_vei_120@2366|_vei2_pn133
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2365">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2367">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn132">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn133">2:133</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn134">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maya</b> is found once in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā (xxii. 19) in the sense of ‘horse.’</p></div>

मयु|మయు|ಮಯು|mayu|_vei_120@2367|_vei2_pn133
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2366">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2368">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn132">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn133">2:133</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn134">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mayu</b> occurs in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202367"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202367">1</a></sup> The commentator on the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1202367"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1202367">1b</a></sup> explains the word as meaning either an ‘ape’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="किंपुरुष">kiṃpuruṣa</a></i>) or a ‘forest peacock’ (<i>āraṇya-<a class="vei_120a" href="मयूर">mayūra</a></i>).  The former sense is supported by another passage of the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2b_vei_1202367"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2b_vei_1202367">2b</a></sup> where the <a class="vei_120a" href="मयु">mayu</a>, being a substitute for the man, must be an ape. This sense also suits the word in the other passage<sup id="ref_120_3b_vei_1202367"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3b_vei_1202367">3b</a></sup> where it occurs.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202367"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202367">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 12, 1; Vājasanevi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 31.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1202367"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1202367">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 12, 1: Vājasanevi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 31.</li><li id="fx_120_2b_vei_1202367"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2b_vei_1202367">↑</a> viii. 47; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मयु">mayu</a> āraṇya</i> in <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 10, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3b_vei_1202367"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3b_vei_1202367">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 5, 2, 22.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 85; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 246.</li></ol></div>

मयूख|మయూఖ|ಮಯೂಖ|mayūkha|mayuukha|mayookha|_vei_120@2368|_vei2_pn134
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2367">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2369">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn133">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn134">2:134</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn135">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mayūkha</b> denotes, from the Rigveda onwards,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202368"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202368">1</a></sup> a ‘peg,’ especially as used for keeping a web stretched.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202368"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202368">2</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ओतु">otu</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202368"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202368">↑</a> Rv. vii. 99, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 1, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 15, 9. etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202368"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202368">↑</a> Rv. x. 130, 2 (in a metaphor); Av. x. 7, 42; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxvi. 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 5, 5, 3, etc.</li></ol></div>

मयूर|మయూర|ಮಯೂರ|mayūra|mayuura|mayoora|_vei_120@2369|_vei2_pn134
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2368">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2370">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn133">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn134">2:134</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn135">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mayūra,</b> ‘peacock,’ occurs in the Rigveda in the compounds describing Indra's horses, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मयूर">mayūra</a>-roman,</i><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202369">1</a></sup> ‘with hair like peacocks’ feathers,’ and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मयूर">mayūra</a>-śepya,</i><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202369">2</a></sup> ‘with tails like those of peacocks.’ The peacock also appears in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202369">3</a></sup> The pea-hen, Mayūrī, is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202369">4</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202369">5</a></sup> in both cases with reference to the bird's efficacy against poison, a curious superstition to be compared with the modern dislike of peacocks' feathers.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202369">↑</a> Rv. iii. 45, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202369">↑</a> Rv. viii. 1, 25.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202369">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 23. 27.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202369">↑</a> i. 191, 14 (a late hymn).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202369">↑</a> vii. 56, 7.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 90.</li></ol></div>

मरीचि|మరీచి|ಮರೀಚಿ|marīci|mariichi|mareechi|_vei_120@2370|_vei2_pn134
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2369">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2371">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn133">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn134">2:134</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn135">↷</a><hr><p><b>Marīci</b> in the plural denotes according to Weber,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202370">1</a></sup> the ‘particles of light’ or ‘shining motes’ that fill the air, as opposed to rays of light (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="रश्मि">raśmi</a></i>). This meaning adequatelysuits the passages in the early Vedic literature<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202370">2</a></sup> where the  word occurs; but the sense of ‘ray’ is quite clearly found in the Upaniṣads,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202370">3</a></sup> as well as the older sense.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202370">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202370">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 9, accepted by the St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202370">↑</a> Rv. x. 57, 12; 177, 1; Av. iv. 38, 5 (where <i><a class="vei_120a" href="रश्मि">raśmi</a></i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मरीचि">marīci</a></i> are opposed); v. 27, 10; vi. 113, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 4, 5, 5 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="मरीचि">marīci</a>-pa,</i> ‘drinking atoms of light,’ said of the gods); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 2, 9, 2 (where Sāyaṇa's version, <i>sarvatra-prasṛtaprabhā-dravya,</i> refers to the light as everywhere diffused), etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202370">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रश्न">praśna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 2. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 1, 2; 2, 1; <b>Maitrāyaṇī</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 31.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202370">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 2.</li></ol></div>

मरु|మరు|ಮರು|maru|_vei_120@2371|_vei2_pn135
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2370">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2372">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn134">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn135">2:135</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn136">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maru,</b> in the plural, is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202371"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202371">1</a></sup> as the <i>utkara</i> (‘mound of earth thrown up’ from the excavation of the altar<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202371"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202371">2</a></sup> ) of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>.</b> This seems to mean that the <a class="vei_120a" href="मरु">maru</a> deserts (the later <a class="vei_120a" href="मरु">maru</a>-sthala)<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202371"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202371">3</a></sup> were so called because they stood to the ‘altar,’ <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>, in the same relation as the waste earth of the <i>utkara</i> to the altar at the sacrifice.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202371"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202371">↑</a> v. 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202371"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202371">↑</a> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> xii. 25, 54.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202371"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202371">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 48, and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धन्वन्">dhanvan</a>.</b></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 78.</li></ol></div>

मरुत्त आवि-क्षित|मरुत्त आविक्षित|मरुत्त आवि|क्षित|మరుత్త ఆవి-క్షిత|మరుత్త ఆవిక్షిత|మరుత్త ఆవి|క్షిత|ಮರುತ್ತ ಆವಿ-ಕ್ಷಿತ|ಮರುತ್ತ ಆವಿಕ್ಷಿತ|ಮರುತ್ತ ಆವಿ|ಕ್ಷಿತ|marutta āvi-kṣita|marutta āvikṣita|marutta āvi|kṣita|marutta aavi-kShita|marutta aavikShita|marutta aavi|kShita|marutta aavi-xita|marutta aavixita|xita|_vei_120@2372|_vei2_pn135
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2371">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2373">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn134">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn135">2:135</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn136">↷</a><hr><p><b>Marutta Āvi-kṣita</b> (‘descendant of Avikṣit’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काम-प्रि">kāma-pri</a></b> (‘descendant of Kāmapra’) is the name of a king who was anointed by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="संवर्त">saṃvarta</a></b> according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202372"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202372">1</a></sup> In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202372"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202372">2</a></sup> account of the same king he is called <b>Āyogava.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202372"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202372">↑</a> viii. 21, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202372"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202372">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 6. <i>Cf.</i> also <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 9, 14. 16; Maitrāyaṇī <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 4.</li></ol></div>

मरुद्-वृधा|मरुद्वृधा|मरुद्|वृधा|మరుద్-వృధా|మరుద్వృధా|మరుద్|వృధా|ಮರುದ್-ವೃಧಾ|ಮರುದ್ವೃಧಾ|ಮರುದ್|ವೃಧಾ|marud-vṛdhā|marudvṛdhā|marud|vṛdhā|marud-vRdhaa|marudvRdhaa|vRdhaa|marud-vRidhaa|marudvRidhaa|vRidhaa|marud-vRudhaa|marudvRudhaa|vRudhaa|marud-vRRidhaa|marudvRRidhaa|vRRidhaa|_vei_120@2373|_vei2_pn135
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2372">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2374">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn134">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn135">2:135</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn136">↷</a><hr><p><b>Marud-vṛdhā</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202373"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202373">1</a></sup> is the name of a stream mentioned in the Nadīstuti (‘Praise of Rivers’) in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202373"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202373">2</a></sup> along with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="असिक्नी">asiknī</a></b> (Akesines) and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वितस्ता">vitastā</a></b> (Hydaspes). Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202373"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202373">3</a></sup> considers that the <a class="vei_120a" href="मरुद्वृधा">marudvṛdhā</a> denotes the stream formed by the combined waters of these two rivers down to its junction with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परुष्णी">paruṣṇī</a></b> (Ravi), a view accepted by Zimmer.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202373"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202373">4</a></sup> On the other hand, Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202373"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202373">5</a></sup> thinks that the <a class="vei_120a" href="मरुद्वृधा">marudvṛdhā</a> designates  the stream formed by the junction of the <a class="vei_120a" href="परुष्णी">paruṣṇī</a> with the combined waters of the <a class="vei_120a" href="असिक्नी">asiknī</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="वितस्ता">vitastā</a>, a view which seems less likely.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202373"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202373">↑</a> Literally, ‘rejoicing in the Maruts’ -<i>i.e.,</i> ‘swollen by the rainy winds.’ The misspelling of the name as Marudvṛddhā in Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> pp. 80, 88, is corrected in the Index and the Addenda of that work. On the accentuation of the name, see Vārttika 2 on Pāṇini, vi. 2, 106.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202373"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202373">↑</a> x. 75, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202373"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202373">↑</a> <i>Zur Litteratur und Geschichte des Weda,</i> 138 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202373"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202373">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 11, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202373"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202373">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 200.</li></ol></div>

मर्क|मर्क|మర్క_౧|మర్క|ಮರ್ಕ_೧|ಮರ್ಕ|marka_1|marka|_vei_120@2374|_vei2_pn136
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2373">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2375">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn135">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn136">2:136</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn137">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Marka</b> is found in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202374"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202374">1</a></sup> where Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202374"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202374">2</a></sup> sees in the expression <i>sūro markaḥ</i> the ‘eclipse of the sun.’ Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202374"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202374">3</a></sup> thinks the meaning is ‘purifying.’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202374"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202374">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202374"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202374">↑</a> x. 27, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202374"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202374">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> He thinks, however, that if the word means ‘eclipse,’ it cannot be derived from the root <i>mṛc,</i> ‘injure.’</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202374"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202374">↑</a> As from the root <i>mṛj,</i> derivation from which is not phonetically justified.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202374"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202374">↑</a> Ludwig cites this passage, in his essay on eclipses in the Rigveda (Proceedings of the Bohemian Academy, 1885), as a proof that the Vedic <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषिस्">ṛṣis</a> knew of the moon as eclipsing the sun; but see Whitney's reply, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, lxi <i>et seq.,</i> and <a class="vei_120a" href="सूर्य">sūrya</a>.</li></ol></div>

मर्क|मर्क|మర్క_౨|మర్క|ಮರ್ಕ_೨|ಮರ್ಕ|marka_2|marka|_vei_120@2375|_vei2_pn136
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2374">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2376">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn135">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn136">2:136</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn137">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Marka</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202375">1</a></sup> and elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202375">2</a></sup> as the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, along with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शण्ड">śaṇḍa</a>,</b> of the Asuras, while <a class="vei_120a" href="बृहस्पति">bṛhaspati</a> is, of course, the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> of the gods. <a class="vei_120a" href="मर्क">marka</a> is mentioned elsewhere also.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202375">3</a></sup> The name may quite possibly have Iranian affinities, as believed by Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202375">4</a></sup> and by Hopkins.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202375">5</a></sup> Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202375">6</a></sup> also sees in a <a class="vei_120a" href="गृध्र">gṛdhra</a> mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202375">7</a></sup> and elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202375">8</a></sup> a prototype of <a class="vei_120a" href="मर्क">marka</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202375">↑</a> vi. 4, 10, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202375">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 6, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 1, 5; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 2, 1, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202375">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, vii. 16, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202375">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 442 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202375">↑</a> <i>Cf. Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 49, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202375">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 223 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202375">↑</a> v. 77, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202375"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202375">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, iv. 29; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 9, 19.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 279 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

मर्कट|మర్కట|ಮರ್ಕಟ|markaṭa|markaTa|_vei_120@2376|_vei2_pn136
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2375">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2377">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn135">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn136">2:136</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn137">↷</a><hr><p><b>Markaṭa,</b> ‘ape,’ is enumerated in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202376">1</a></sup> It is classified in the same Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202376">2</a></sup> with man and the elephant as ‘taking hold by the hand’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="हस्तादान">hastādāna</a></i>) instead of ‘taking hold by the mouth’ (<i>mukhādāna</i>). The animal is mentioned several times elsewhere also.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202376">3</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुष हस्तिन्">puruṣa hastin</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मयु">mayu</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202376">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 11, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 11; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 30.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202376">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 4, 5, 7; Maitāyani Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202376">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 2, 4; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 184; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 11, 32, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 85.</li></ol></div>

मर्य|मर्य|మర్య_౧|మర్య|ಮರ್ಯ_೧|ಮರ್ಯ|marya_1|marya|_vei_120@2377|_vei2_pn137
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2376">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2378">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn136">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn137">2:137</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn138">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Marya</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202377">1</a></sup> denotes a ‘man’ especially regarded as young and a lover, being constantly mentioned as in company with maidens (<i>yuvatī</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202377">↑</a> iii. 31, 7; 33, 10; iv. 20, 5; ix. 96, 20, etc.; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मर्य">marya</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="श्री">śrī</a>,</i> ‘adorred as a lover,’ ii. 10, 5. <i>Cf.</i> <b>Nīrukta,</b> iii. 15; iv. 2.</li></ol></div>

मर्य|मर्य|మర్య_౨|మర్య|ಮರ್ಯ_೨|ಮರ್ಯ|marya_2|marya|_vei_120@2378|_vei2_pn137
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2377">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2379">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn136">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn137">2:137</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn138">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Marya</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202378"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202378">1</a></sup> in several passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1202378"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1202378">1b</a></sup> denotes a ‘stallion.’<sup id="ref_120_2b_vei_1202378"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2b_vei_1202378">2b</a></sup> It is once<sup id="ref_120_3b_vei_1202378"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3b_vei_1202378">3b</a></sup> described as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पस्त्यावन्त्">pastyāvant</a>,</i> ‘a stalled horse’--that is, one carefully tended, and not allowed out to graze.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202378"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202378">↑</a> vii. 56, 16; viii. 43, 25.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1202378"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1202378">↑</a> vii. 56, 16; viii. 43, 25.</li><li id="fx_120_2b_vei_1202378"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2b_vei_1202378">↑</a> This is, of course, only a specialized sense of 1. <a class="vei_120a" href="मर्य">marya</a> as meaning a ‘male’ (<i>cf.</i> Lat. <i>mas, maritus</i>). The specialized meaning is somewhat analogous to the use of ‘sire’ in English.</li><li id="fx_120_3b_vei_1202378"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3b_vei_1202378">↑</a> Rv. ix. 97, 18. Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 2, thinks Rv. i. 91, 13, may have the same sense.</li></ol></div>

मर्यक|మర్యక|ಮರ್ಯಕ|maryaka|_vei_120@2379|_vei2_pn137
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2378">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2380">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn136">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn137">2:137</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn138">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maryaka,</b> occurring only once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202379"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202379">1</a></sup> seems to denote the bull which is described as separated from the cows.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202379"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202379">↑</a> v. 2. 5. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 313.</li></ol></div>

मर्यादा|మర్యాదా|ಮರ್ಯಾದಾ|maryādā|maryaadaa|_vei_120@2380|_vei2_pn137
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2379">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2381">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn136">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn137">2:137</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn138">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maryādā,</b> ‘boundary,’ is found in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202380"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202380">1</a></sup> referring to the boundary between the <b>Kosalas</b> and the <b>Videhas.</b> Usually the word is metaphorically employed.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202380"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202380">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202380"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202380">↑</a> i. 4, 1, 17. <i>Cf.</i> xiii. 8, 4, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202380"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202380">↑</a> Rv. iv. 5, 13; x. 5, 6; Av. vi. 81, 2 (of an amulet). In the Atharvaveda passage Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 392, suggests, owing to the very curious use of the word, the emendation <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मर्य">marya</a>-dā,</i> ‘giver of a son.’</li></ol></div>

मल|మల|ಮಲ|mala|_vei_120@2381|_vei2_pn137
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2380">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2382">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn136">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn137">2:137</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn138">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mala</b> in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202381"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202381">1</a></sup> is used of the garments of the Munis. The St. Petersburg Dictionary takes it to mean a ‘leathern garment,’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202381"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202381">2</a></sup> but Ludwig and Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202381"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202381">3</a></sup> think it means only ‘soiled’ raiment, which, of course, suits the ordinary sense of the word (‘dirt’) in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202381"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202381">4</a></sup> and the character of the long-haired (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="केशिन्">keśin</a></i>) hermit (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="मुनि">muni</a></b>). <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मलग">malaga</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202381"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202381">↑</a> x. 136, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202381"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202381">↑</a> If this were correct, the word might be derived from <i>mlā</i> in the sense of ‘to tan.’ <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चर्मन्">carman</a>,</b> especially notes 6 and 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202381"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202381">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 262.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202381"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202381">↑</a> vi. 115, 3; vii. 89, 3; x. 5, 24, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 333, n.</li></ol></div>

मल-ग|मलग|मल|మల-గ|మలగ|మల|ಮಲ-ಗ|ಮಲಗ|ಮಲ|mala-ga|malaga|mala|_vei_120@2382|_vei2_pn138
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2381">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2383">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn137">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn138">2:138</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn139">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mala-ga</b> in one passage of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202382"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202382">1</a></sup> denotes a cleanser of clothes, a ‘washerman,’ but the origin of the word is somewhat uncertain.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202382"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202382">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202382"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202382">↑</a> xii. 3, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202382"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202382">↑</a> It may, perhaps, have primarily meant ‘concerned with dirt.’ See the St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. ga</i> 1, on the use of <i>ga</i> as forming compounds; and <i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मल">mala</a>.</b></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 262; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 188.</li></ol></div>

मलिम्लु|మలిమ్లు|ಮಲಿಮ್ಲು|malimlu|_vei_120@2383|_vei2_pn138
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2382">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2384">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn137">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn138">2:138</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn139">↷</a><hr><p><b>Malimlu</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202383"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202383">1</a></sup> denotes a ‘robber,’ specifically, according to the commentator Mahīdhara, a burglar or housebreaker. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तायु">tāyu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="तस्कर">taskara</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="स्तेन">stena</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="देवमलिम्लुच्">devamalimluc</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202383"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202383">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 3, 2, 6; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xi. 78. 79; Av. xix. 49, 10.</li></ol></div>

मलिम्लुच|మలిమ్లుచ|ಮಲಿಮ್ಲುಚ|malimluca|malimlucha|_vei_120@2384|_vei2_pn138
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2383">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2385">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn137">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn138">2:138</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn139">↷</a><hr><p><b>Malimluca</b> is the name of an intercalary month in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202384">1</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202384"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202384">↑</a> xxxv. 10; xxxviii. 14. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ज्योतिष">jyotiṣa</a>,</i> 100, 102; <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 350.</li></ol></div>

मशक|मशक|మశక_౧|మశక|ಮಶಕ_೧|ಮಶಕ|maśaka_1|maśaka|mashaka_1|mashaka|_vei_120@2385|_vei2_pn138
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2384">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2386">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn137">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn138">2:138</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn139">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Maśaka</b> denotes a ‘biting fly’ or ‘mosquito,’ being described in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202385"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202385">1</a></sup> as ‘quickly(?) biting’ (<i>tṛpradaṃśin</i>), and as having a poisonous sting. The elephant is mentioned<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202385"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202385">2</a></sup> as particularly subject to its stings. The insect is often referred to elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202385"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202385">3</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दंश">daṃśa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202385"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202385">↑</a> vii. 56, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202385"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202385">↑</a> Av. iv. 36, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202385"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202385">↑</a> Av. xi. 3, 5; at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’), Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 29; xxv. 3; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 3, 24 (Mādhyaṃdina = i. 3, 22 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>); Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 9, 3; 10, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 97.</li></ol></div>

मशक गार्ग्य|मशक गार्ग्य|మశక గార్గ్య_౨|మశక గార్గ్య|ಮಶಕ ಗಾರ್ಗ್ಯ_೨|ಮಶಕ ಗಾರ್ಗ್ಯ|maśaka gārgya_2|maśaka gārgya|mashaka gaargya_2|mashaka gaargya|_vei_120@2386|_vei2_pn138
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2385">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2387">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn137">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn138">2:138</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn139">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Maśaka Gārgya</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गर्ग">garga</a></b>’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्थिरक गार्ग्य">sthiraka gārgya</a>,</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202386"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202386">1</a></sup> He is also mentioned in the Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202386"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202386">2</a></sup> of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>, and is the reputed author of an extant <a class="vei_120a" href="कल्प">kalpa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202386"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202386">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373, 382.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202386"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202386">↑</a> Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, vii. 9, 14; Anupada <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ix. 9.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 75, 76; 83, 84.</li></ol></div>

मशर्शार|మశర్శార|ಮಶರ್ಶಾರ|maśarśāra|masharshaara|_vei_120@2387|_vei2_pn139
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2386">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2388">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn138">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn139">2:139</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn140">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maśarśāra</b> is the name of a king, according to Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202387"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202387">1</a></sup> of the <b>Nahuṣas,</b> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202387"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202387">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202387"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202387">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 206.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202387"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202387">↑</a> i. 122, 15.</li></ol></div>

मष्णार|మష్ణార|ಮಷ್ಣಾರ|maṣṇāra|maShNaara|_vei_120@2388|_vei2_pn139
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2387">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2389">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn138">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn139">2:139</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn140">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maṣṇāra</b> is the name of a locality, the scene of the victory of a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a></b> king, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202388"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202388">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202388"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202388">↑</a> viii. 23, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Bhāgavata <a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a>, v. 13, 26 <i>et seq.;</i> Leumann, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenlāndischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 80, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

मसूर|మసూర|ಮಸೂರ|masūra|masuura|masoora|_vei_120@2389|_vei2_pn139
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2388">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2390">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn138">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn139">2:139</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn140">↷</a><hr><p><b>Masūra</b> is the name of a kind of lentil (<i>Ervum hirsutum</i>) in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202389">1</a></sup> and the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202389">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202389">↑</a> xviii. 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202389">↑</a> vi. 3, 22 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 3, 13 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 355; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 241.</li></ol></div>

मसूस्य|మసూస్య|ಮಸೂಸ್ಯ|masūsya|masuusya|masoosya|_vei_120@2390|_vei2_pn139
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2389">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2391">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn138">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn139">2:139</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn140">↷</a><hr><p><b>Masūsya,</b> occurring in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 8, 14, 6), is, according to the commentator, the name of a grain of the north country.</p></div>

मस्तु|మస్తు|ಮಸ್ತು|mastu|_vei_120@2391|_vei2_pn139
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2390">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2392">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn138">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn139">2:139</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn140">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mastu</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202391"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202391">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202391"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202391">2</a></sup> denotes ‘sour curds.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202391"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202391">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 1, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvi. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202391"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202391">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 1, 7; iii. 3, 3, 2, etc.</li></ol></div>

मह-र्त्विज्|महर्त्विज्|मह|र्त्विज्|మహ-ర్త్విజ్|మహర్త్విజ్|మహ|ర్త్విజ్|ಮಹ-ರ್ತ್ವಿಜ್|ಮಹರ್ತ್ವಿಜ್|ಮಹ|ರ್ತ್ವಿಜ್|maha-rtvij|mahartvij|maha|rtvij|_vei_120@2392|_vei2_pn139
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2391">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2393">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn138">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn139">2:139</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn140">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maha-rtvij,</b> ‘great priest,’ is the collective name of the four chief priests--<a class="vei_120a" href="अध्वर्यु">adhvaryu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a>, and Udgātṛ--in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202392"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202392">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202392"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202392">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8, 2, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 1, 1, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 1, 7, etc.</li></ol></div>

मह-र्षभ|महर्षभ|मह|र्षभ|మహ-ర్షభ|మహర్షభ|మహ|ర్షభ|ಮಹ-ರ್ಷಭ|ಮಹರ್ಷಭ|ಮಹ|ರ್ಷಭ|maha-rṣabha|maharṣabha|maha|rṣabha|maha-rShabha|maharShabha|rShabha|_vei_120@2393|_vei2_pn139
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2392">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2394">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn138">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn139">2:139</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn140">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maha-rṣabha,</b> a ‘great bull,’ is mentioned in the Atharvaveda (iv. 15, 1).</p></div>

मह-र्षि|महर्षि|मह|र्षि|మహ-ర్షి|మహర్షి|మహ|ర్షి|ಮಹ-ರ್ಷಿ|ಮಹರ್ಷಿ|ಮಹ|ರ್ಷಿ|maha-rṣi|maharṣi|maha|rṣi|maha-rShi|maharShi|rShi|_vei_120@2394|_vei2_pn139
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2393">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2395">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn138">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn139">2:139</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn140">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maha-rṣi,</b> a ‘great <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>,’ is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka (i. 9, 6). <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="महाब्राह्मण">mahābrāhmaṇa</a>.</b> </p></div>

महा-कुल|महाकुल|महा|कुल|మహా-కుల|మహాకుల|మహా|కుల|ಮಹಾ-ಕುಲ|ಮಹಾಕುಲ|ಮಹಾ|ಕುಲ|mahā-kula|mahākula|mahā|kula|mahaa-kula|mahaakula|mahaa|_vei_120@2395|_vei2_pn140
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2394">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2396">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn139">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn140">2:140</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn141">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahā-kula,</b> ‘sprung from a great family,’ is the designation of a bowl or cup (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="चमस">camasa</a></b>) in the Rigveda (i. 161, 1). The metaphorical use of this word shows that the high position of certain families was already recognized in the times of the Rigveda.</p></div>

महा-कौषीतक|महाकौषीतक|महा|कौषीतक|మహా-కౌషీతక|మహాకౌషీతక|మహా|కౌషీతక|ಮಹಾ-ಕೌಷೀತಕ|ಮಹಾಕೌಷೀತಕ|ಮಹಾ|ಕೌಷೀತಕ|mahā-kauṣītaka|mahākauṣītaka|mahā|kauṣītaka|mahaa-kauShiitaka|mahaakauShiitaka|mahaa|kauShiitaka|mahaa-kauSheetaka|mahaakauSheetaka|kauSheetaka|_vei_120@2396|_vei2_pn140
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2395">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2397">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn139">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn140">2:140</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn141">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahā-kauṣītaka,</b> the ‘Great <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतक">kauṣītaka</a> (<a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>),’ is the name of a Vedic text in the Rigveda <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202396"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202396">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202396"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202396">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 4, 4; Mahākauṣītaki, the teacher, in <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 10; vi. 1, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 29, 3, 4.</li></ol></div>

महाज|మహాజ|ಮಹಾಜ|mahāja|mahaaja|_vei_120@2397|_vei2_pn140
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2396">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2398">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn139">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn140">2:140</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn141">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahāja,</b> a ‘great goat’ (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अज">aja</a></b>) is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 4, 1, 2).</p></div>

महा-धन|महाधन|महा|धन|మహా-ధన|మహాధన|మహా|ధన|ಮಹಾ-ಧನ|ಮಹಾಧನ|ಮಹಾ|ಧನ|mahā-dhana|mahādhana|mahā|dhana|mahaa-dhana|mahaadhana|mahaa|_vei_120@2398|_vei2_pn140
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2397">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2399">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn139">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn140">2:140</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn141">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahā-dhana</b> in the Rigveda denotes either a ‘great fight’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202398">1</a></sup> or a ‘great prize’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202398">2</a></sup> as the result of a fight. In many cases the fight may mean merely the contest of the chariot race.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202398">↑</a> Rv. i. 7, 5; 40, 8; 112, 17; vi. 59, 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202398"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202398">↑</a> ix. 86, 12.</li></ol></div>

महा-नग्नी|महा-नग्नि|महानग्नी|महानग्नि|महा|नग्नी|नग्नि|మహా-నగ్నీ|మహా-నగ్ని|మహానగ్నీ|మహానగ్ని|మహా|నగ్నీ|నగ్ని|ಮಹಾ-ನಗ್ನೀ|ಮಹಾ-ನಗ್ನಿ|ಮಹಾನಗ್ನೀ|ಮಹಾನಗ್ನಿ|ಮಹಾ|ನಗ್ನೀ|ನಗ್ನಿ|mahā-nagnī|mahā-nagni|mahānagnī|mahānagni|mahā|nagnī|nagni|mahaa-nagnii|mahaa-nagni|mahaanagnii|mahaanagni|mahaa|nagnii|mahaa-nagnee|mahaanagnee|nagnee|_vei_120@2399|_vei2_pn140
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2398">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2400">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn139">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn140">2:140</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn141">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahā-nagnī</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202399">1</a></sup> denotes a ‘courtesan.’ The masculine, <a class="vei_120a" href="महा">mahā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="नग्न">nagna</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202399">2</a></sup> ‘paramour,’ is probably secondarily. derived from the feminine <a class="vei_120a" href="महानग्नी">mahānagnī</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202399">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202399">↑</a> xiv. 1, 36; xx. 136, 5 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 27, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202399">↑</a> Av. xx. 136, 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 24, 14. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 747; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 280, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202399">↑</a> As <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स-पत्न">sa-patna</a>,</i> ‘rival,’ is unmistakably formed from <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स-पत्नी">sa-patnī</a>,</i> ‘co-wife.’</li></ol></div>

महा-नाग|महानाग|महा|नाग|మహా-నాగ|మహానాగ|మహా|నాగ|ಮಹಾ-ನಾಗ|ಮಹಾನಾಗ|ಮಹಾ|ನಾಗ|mahā-nāga|mahānāga|mahā|nāga|mahaa-naaga|mahaanaaga|mahaa|naaga|_vei_120@2400|_vei2_pn140
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2399">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2401">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn139">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn140">2:140</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn141">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahā-nāga,</b> a ‘great snake,’ is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xi. 2, 7, 12), where it is plainly mythical.</p></div>

महा-निरष्ट|महानिरष्ट|महा|निरष्ट|మహా-నిరష్ట|మహానిరష్ట|మహా|నిరష్ట|ಮಹಾ-ನಿರಷ್ಟ|ಮಹಾನಿರಷ್ಟ|ಮಹಾ|ನಿರಷ್ಟ|mahā-niraṣṭa|mahāniraṣṭa|mahā|niraṣṭa|mahaa-niraShTa|mahaaniraShTa|mahaa|niraShTa|_vei_120@2401|_vei2_pn140
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2400">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2402">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn139">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn140">2:140</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn141">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahā-niraṣṭa,</b> a ‘great castrated’ ox, is mentioned as the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दक्षिणा">dakṣiṇā</a>,</b> or ‘sacrificial fee,’ in the house of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सूत">sūta</a></b> at the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a></b> (‘royal consecration’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202401"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202401">1</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अनड्वाह्">anaḍvāh</a></b> and <b>Go.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202401"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202401">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 9, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 4, 9; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 5.</li></ol></div>

महा-पथ|महापथ|महा|पथ|మహా-పథ|మహాపథ|మహా|పథ|ಮಹಾ-ಪಥ|ಮಹಾಪಥ|ಮಹಾ|ಪಥ|mahā-patha|mahāpatha|mahā|patha|mahaa-patha|mahaapatha|mahaa|_vei_120@2402|_vei2_pn141
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2401">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2403">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn140">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn141">2:141</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn142">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahā-patha</b> in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202402">1</a></sup> denotes the ‘high road’ between two villages.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202402">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 17, 8; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, viii. 6, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 271, n.</li></ol></div>

महा-पुर|महापुर|महा|पुर|మహా-పుర|మహాపుర|మహా|పుర|ಮಹಾ-ಪುರ|ಮಹಾಪುರ|ಮಹಾ|ಪುರ|mahā-pura|mahāpura|mahā|pura|mahaa-pura|mahaapura|mahaa|_vei_120@2403|_vei2_pn141
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2402">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2404">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn140">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn141">2:141</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn142">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahā-pura</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202403"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202403">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202403"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202403">2</a></sup> denotes a great fortress.<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1202403"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1202403">1b</a></sup> Probably the only difference between the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुर्">pur</a></b> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="महापुर">mahāpura</a> was size.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202403"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202403">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 3, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxiv. 10; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 8, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202403"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202403">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 23, 2; Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 2, 7.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1202403"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1202403">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 3, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxiv. 10; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 8, 1.</li></ol></div>

महा-ब्राह्मण|महाब्राह्मण|महा|ब्राह्मण|మహా-బ్రాహ్మణ|మహాబ్రాహ్మణ|మహా|బ్రాహ్మణ|ಮಹಾ-ಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ|ಮಹಾಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ|ಮಹಾ|ಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ|mahā-brāhmaṇa|mahābrāhmaṇa|mahā|brāhmaṇa|mahaa-braahmaNa|mahaabraahmaNa|mahaa|braahmaNa|_vei_120@2404|_vei2_pn141
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2403">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2405">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn140">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn141">2:141</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn142">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahā-brāhmaṇa,</b> a ‘great Brahmin,’ is found in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (ii. 1, 19. 22) denoting a Brahmin of great consequence. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="महर्षि">maharṣi</a>.</b></p></div>

महाभिषेक|మహాభిషేక|ಮಹಾಭಿಷೇಕ|mahābhiṣeka|mahaabhiSheka|_vei_120@2405|_vei2_pn141
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2404">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2406">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn140">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn141">2:141</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn142">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahābhiṣeka,</b> ‘great consecration,’ is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202405">1</a></sup> and described as a ceremony performed for great kings, a list of whom is given. It is equivalent to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202405">↑</a> viii. 14, 4; 19, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 8. The list is <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनमेजय">janamejaya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पारिक्षित">pārikṣita</a>,</b> whose friend was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर कावषेय">tura kāvaṣeya</a>; <a class="vei_120a" href="शार्यात">śāryāta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="च्यवन">cyavana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="भार्गव">bhārgava</a>; Satānīka <a class="vei_120a" href="सात्राजित">sātrājita</a></b> and <b>Somaśuṣma <a class="vei_120a" href="भार्गव">bhārgava</a>; <a class="vei_120a" href="अम्बरीष">ambarīṣa</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्वत">parvata</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नारद">nārada</a>; Yudhāṃśrauṣṭi <a class="vei_120a" href="औग्रसैन्य">augrasainya</a></b> and the same two <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषिस्">ṛṣis</a>; <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वकर्मन् भौवन">viśvakarman bhauvana</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कश्यप">kaśyapa</a>; <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पैजवन">paijavana</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>; <a class="vei_120a" href="मरुत्त आविक्षित">marutta āvikṣita</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="संवर्त">saṃvarta</a>; <a class="vei_120a" href="अंग वैरोचन">aṅga vairocana</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उदमय आत्रेय">udamaya ātreya</a>; <a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दौःषन्ति">dauḥṣanti</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दीर्घतमस्">dīrghatamas</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मामतेय">māmateya</a>; <a class="vei_120a" href="दुर्मुख">durmukha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पाञ्चाल">pāñcāla</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बृहदुक्थ">bṛhaduktha</a>; Atyarāti <a class="vei_120a" href="जानंतपि">jānaṃtapi</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वासिष्ठ">vāsiṣṭha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सात्यहव्य">sātyahavya</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

महा-भूत|महाभूत|महा|भूत|మహా-భూత|మహాభూత|మహా|భూత|ಮಹಾ-ಭೂತ|ಮಹಾಭೂತ|ಮಹಾ|ಭೂತ|mahā-bhūta|mahābhūta|mahā|bhūta|mahaa-bhuuta|mahaabhuuta|mahaa|bhuuta|mahaa-bhoota|mahaabhoota|bhoota|_vei_120@2406|_vei2_pn141
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2405">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2407">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn140">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn141">2:141</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn142">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahā-bhūta</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> (xiv. 5, 10) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iii. 2, 3) denotes the ‘gross elements’ (earth, water, fire, air, ether).</p></div>

महा-मत्स्य|महामत्स्य|महा|मत्स्य|మహా-మత్స్య|మహామత్స్య|మహా|మత్స్య|ಮಹಾ-ಮತ್ಸ್ಯ|ಮಹಾಮತ್ಸ್ಯ|ಮಹಾ|ಮತ್ಸ್ಯ|mahā-matsya|mahāmatsya|mahā|matsya|mahaa-matsya|mahaamatsya|mahaa|_vei_120@2407|_vei2_pn141
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2406">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2408">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn140">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn141">2:141</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn142">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahā-matsya,</b> a ‘great fish,’ is mentioned in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iv. 3, 18).</p></div>

महा-मेरु|महामेरु|महा|मेरु|మహా-మేరు|మహామేరు|మహా|మేరు|ಮಹಾ-ಮೇರು|ಮಹಾಮೇರು|ಮಹಾ|ಮೇರು|mahā-meru|mahāmeru|mahā|meru|mahaa-meru|mahaameru|mahaa|_vei_120@2408|_vei2_pn141
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2407">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2409">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn140">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn141">2:141</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn142">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahā-meru,</b> ‘great <a class="vei_120a" href="मेरु">meru</a>,’ is the name of a mountain in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202408">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202408">↑</a> i. 7, 1, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 78; 3, 123.</li></ol></div>

महा-रथ|महारथ|महा|रथ|మహా-రథ|మహారథ|మహా|రథ|ಮಹಾ-ರಥ|ಮಹಾರಥ|ಮಹಾ|ರಥ|mahā-ratha|mahāratha|mahā|ratha|mahaa-ratha|mahaaratha|mahaa|_vei_120@2409|_vei2_pn142
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2408">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2410">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn141">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn142">2:142</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn143">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahā-ratha,</b> ‘having a great car’--<i>i.e.,</i> ‘a great chariot fighter,’ is an epithet of the hero who is prayed for in the ritual of the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202409"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202409">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202409"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202409">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 18, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxii. 22.</li></ol></div>

महा-राज|महाराज|महा|राज|మహా-రాజ|మహారాజ|మహా|రాజ|ಮಹಾ-ರಾಜ|ಮಹಾರಾಜ|ಮಹಾ|ರಾಜ|mahā-rāja|mahārāja|mahā|rāja|mahaa-raaja|mahaaraaja|mahaa|raaja|_vei_120@2410|_vei2_pn142
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2409">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2411">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn141">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn142">2:142</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn143">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahā-rāja,</b> a ‘great king,’ is frequently referred to in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202410"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202410">1</a></sup> It seems to mean no more than a king, or rather perhaps a reigning and powerful king, as opposed to a mere prince, who would also be called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202410"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202410">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 34, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 4, 21; ii. 5, 4, 9; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 1, 19 <i>et seq.;</i> Maitrāyaṇī <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

महा-रात्र|महारात्र|महा|रात्र|మహా-రాత్ర|మహారాత్ర|మహా|రాత్ర|ಮಹಾ-ರಾತ್ರ|ಮಹಾರಾತ್ರ|ಮಹಾ|ರಾತ್ರ|mahā-rātra|mahārātra|mahā|rātra|mahaa-raatra|mahaaraatra|mahaa|raatra|_vei_120@2411|_vei2_pn142
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2410">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2412">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn141">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn142">2:142</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn143">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahā-rātra,</b> ‘advanced night,’ is a phrase found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202411">1</a></sup> and the Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202411">2</a></sup> to denote the latter part of the night, after midnight and before dawn.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202411">↑</a> ii. 9; xi. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202411">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, vi. 2, 1; xvii. 7, 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

महार्णव|మహార్ణవ|ಮಹಾರ್ಣವ|mahārṇava|mahaarNava|_vei_120@2412|_vei2_pn142
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2411">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2413">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn141">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn142">2:142</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn143">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahārṇava,</b> a ‘great ocean,’ is a phrase not found before the late Maitrāyaṇī <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (i. 4), where the drying up of ‘great oceans’ is one of the marvels enumerated. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="समुद्र">samudra</a>.</b></p></div>

महा-वीर|महावीर|महा|वीर|మహా-వీర|మహావీర|మహా|వీర|ಮಹಾ-ವೀರ|ಮಹಾವೀರ|ಮಹಾ|ವೀರ|mahā-vīra|mahāvīra|mahā|vīra|mahaa-viira|mahaaviira|mahaa|viira|mahaa-veera|mahaaveera|veera|_vei_120@2413|_vei2_pn142
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2412">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2414">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn141">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn142">2:142</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn143">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahā-vīra</b> (‘great hero’) is the name in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202413">1</a></sup> of a large earthenware pot which could be placed on the fire, and which was especially employed at the introductory <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> ceremony called Pravargya.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202413">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 14; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 1, 2, 9, 17; 3, 1, 13; 4, 16; 2, 2, 13. 40; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 10, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 3, 7, etc.</li></ol></div>

महा-वृक्ष|महावृक्ष|महा|वृक्ष|మహా-వృక్ష|మహావృక్ష|మహా|వృక్ష|ಮಹಾ-ವೃಕ್ಷ|ಮಹಾವೃಕ್ಷ|ಮಹಾ|ವೃಕ್ಷ|mahā-vṛkṣa|mahāvṛkṣa|mahā|vṛkṣa|mahaa-vRkSha|mahaavRkSha|mahaa|vRkSha|mahaa-vRikSha|mahaavRikSha|vRikSha|mahaa-vRukSha|mahaavRukSha|vRukSha|mahaa-vRRikSha|mahaavRRikSha|vRRikSha|mahaa-vRxa|mahaavRxa|vRxa|_vei_120@2414|_vei2_pn142
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2413">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2415">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn141">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn142">2:142</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn143">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahā-vṛkṣa</b> a ‘great tree,’ is mentioned sometimes in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (vii. 6, 15; xiv. 1, 12) and in the Sūtras.</p></div>

महा-वृष|महावृष|महा|वृष|మహా-వృష|మహావృష|మహా|వృష|ಮಹಾ-ವೃಷ|ಮಹಾವೃಷ|ಮಹಾ|ವೃಷ|mahā-vṛṣa|mahāvṛṣa|mahā|vṛṣa|mahaa-vRSha|mahaavRSha|mahaa|vRSha|mahaa-vRiSha|mahaavRiSha|vRiSha|mahaa-vRuSha|mahaavRuSha|vRuSha|mahaa-vRRiSha|mahaavRRiSha|vRRiSha|_vei_120@2415|_vei2_pn142
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2414">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2416">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn141">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn142">2:142</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn143">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahā-vṛṣa</b> is the name of a tribe mentioned along with the <b>Mūjavants</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202415">1</a></sup> as a locality to which fever is to be relegated. It is reasonable to suppose that they were  northerners, though Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202415">2</a></sup> suggests that the name may be chosen more for its sound and sense (as ‘of mighty strength’ to resist the disease) than for its geographical position. In the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202415">3</a></sup> the place <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रैक्वपर्ण">raikvaparṇa</a></b> is said to be in the <a class="vei_120a" href="महावृष">mahāvṛṣa</a> country. The king of the Mahāvṛṣas in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202415">4</a></sup> is said to be <b>Hṛtsvāśaya.</b> The Mahāvṛṣas are also known from a <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202415">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202415">↑</a> v. 22, 4, 5, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202415">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 446.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202415">↑</a> iv. 2, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202415">↑</a> iii. 40, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202415">↑</a> ii. 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 70, 147; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 129; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 259, 260.</li></ol></div>

महा-शाल|महा-शाल|महाशाल|महा|शाल|మహా-శాల_౧|మహా-శాల|మహాశాల|మహా|శాల|ಮಹಾ-ಶಾಲ_೧|ಮಹಾ-ಶಾಲ|ಮಹಾಶಾಲ|ಮಹಾ|ಶಾಲ|mahā-śāla_1|mahā-śāla|mahāśāla|mahā|śāla|mahaa-shaala_1|mahaa-shaala|mahaashaala|mahaa|shaala|_vei_120@2416|_vei2_pn143
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2415">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2417">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn142">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn143">2:143</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn144">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Mahā-śāla</b> (lit., ‘having a great house’), a ‘great householder,’ is an expression applied in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (v. 11, 1) to the Brahmins who were instructed by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वपति">aśvapati</a>,</b> no doubt to emphasize their importance. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="महाब्राह्मण">mahābrāhmaṇa</a>.</b></p></div>

महा-शाल जाबाल|महा-शाल जाबाल|महाशाल जाबाल|महा|शाल जाबाल|మహా-శాల జాబాల_౨|మహా-శాల జాబాల|మహాశాల జాబాల|మహా|శాల జాబాల|ಮಹಾ-ಶಾಲ ಜಾಬಾಲ_೨|ಮಹಾ-ಶಾಲ ಜಾಬಾಲ|ಮಹಾಶಾಲ ಜಾಬಾಲ|ಮಹಾ|ಶಾಲ ಜಾಬಾಲ|mahā-śāla jābāla_2|mahā-śāla jābāla|mahāśāla jābāla|mahā|śāla jābāla|mahaa-shaala jaabaala_2|mahaa-shaala jaabaala|mahaashaala jaabaala|mahaa|shaala jaabaala|_vei_120@2417|_vei2_pn143
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2416">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2418">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn142">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn143">2:143</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn144">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Mahā-<a class="vei_120a" href="शाल">śāla</a> Jābāla</b> is the name of a teacher twice mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, once as instructing <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धीर शातपर्णेय">dhīra śātaparṇeya</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202417"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202417">1</a></sup> and once as one of the Brahmins who received instruction from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वपति">aśvapati</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202417"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202417">2</a></sup> In the parallel passage of the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202417"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202417">3</a></sup> the name is <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राचीनशाल औपमन्यव">prācīnaśāla aupamanyava</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202417"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202417">4</a></sup> The word must be considered a proper name rather than an adjective (1. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="महाशाल">mahāśāla</a></b>), as it is taken in the St. Petersburg Dictionary.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202417"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202417">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202417"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202417">↑</a> x. 3, 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202417"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202417">↑</a> x. 6, 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202417"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202417">↑</a> v. 11, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202417"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202417">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 43, 393, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202417"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202417">↑</a> In Muṇḍaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 1, 3, the word is used of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शौनक">śaunaka</a>,</b> perhaps merely as an epithet. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 161.</li></ol></div>

महा-सुपर्ण|महासुपर्ण|महा|सुपर्ण|మహా-సుపర్ణ|మహాసుపర్ణ|మహా|సుపర్ణ|ಮಹಾ-ಸುಪರ್ಣ|ಮಹಾಸುಪರ್ಣ|ಮಹಾ|ಸುಪರ್ಣ|mahā-suparṇa|mahāsuparṇa|mahā|suparṇa|mahaa-suparNa|mahaasuparNa|mahaa|suparNa|_vei_120@2418|_vei2_pn143
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2417">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2419">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn142">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn143">2:143</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn144">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahā-suparṇa</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xii. 2, 3, 7) denotes a ‘great bird’ or ‘great eagle.’</p></div>

महा-सुहय|महासुहय|महा|सुहय|మహా-సుహయ|మహాసుహయ|మహా|సుహయ|ಮಹಾ-ಸುಹಯ|ಮಹಾಸುಹಯ|ಮಹಾ|ಸುಹಯ|mahā-suhaya|mahāsuhaya|mahā|suhaya|mahaa-suhaya|mahaasuhaya|mahaa|_vei_120@2419|_vei2_pn143
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2418">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2420">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn142">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn143">2:143</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn144">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahā-suhaya,</b> a ‘great (<i>i.e.,</i> high-spirited) horse,’ is the description in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202419"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202419">1</a></sup> of the steed from  the Indus (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सैन्धव">saindhava</a></i>) which tears away the peg of its hobble (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पड्बीश">paḍbīśa</a>-śaṅkhu</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202419"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202419">↑</a> vi. 2, 13. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, ix. 7; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 1, 12; Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 234, 235; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 57, n. 3. <i>Cf.</i> <b>Paḍhīśa.</b></li></ol></div>

महा-सूक्त|महासूक्त|महा|सूक्त|మహా-సూక్త|మహాసూక్త|మహా|సూక్త|ಮಹಾ-ಸೂಕ್ತ|ಮಹಾಸೂಕ್ತ|ಮಹಾ|ಸೂಕ್ತ|mahā-sūkta|mahāsūkta|mahā|sūkta|mahaa-suukta|mahaasuukta|mahaa|suukta|mahaa-sookta|mahaasookta|sookta|_vei_120@2420|_vei2_pn144
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2419">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2421">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn143">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn144">2:144</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn145">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahā-sūkta,</b> m. plur., the ‘composers of the long hymns’ of the tenth Maṇḍala of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202420"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202420">1</a></sup> are mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202420"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202420">2</a></sup> and the Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202420"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202420">3</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षुद्र-सूक्त">kṣudra-sūkta</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202420"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202420">↑</a> x. 1-128.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202420"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202420">↑</a> ii. 2, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202420"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202420">↑</a> Aśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 4, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 10.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 115; 390; Roth, <i>Zur Litteratur und Geschichte des Weda,</i> 27.</li></ol></div>

महाह्न|మహాహ్న|ಮಹಾಹ್ನ|mahāhna|mahaahna|_vei_120@2421|_vei2_pn144
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2420">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2422">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn143">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn144">2:144</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn145">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahāhna</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (ii. 9) denotes the ‘advanced (time of the) day’--that is, ‘afternoon.’ <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="महारात्र">mahārātra</a>.</b></p></div>

महि-दास ऐतरेय|महिदास ऐतरेय|महि|दास ऐतरेय|మహి-దాస ఐతరేయ|మహిదాస ఐతరేయ|మహి|దాస ఐతరేయ|ಮಹಿ-ದಾಸ ಐತರೇಯ|ಮಹಿದಾಸ ಐತರೇಯ|ಮಹಿ|ದಾಸ ಐತರೇಯ|mahi-dāsa aitareya|mahidāsa aitareya|mahi|dāsa aitareya|mahi-daasa aitareya|mahidaasa aitareya|daasa aitareya|_vei_120@2422|_vei2_pn144
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2421">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2423">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn143">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn144">2:144</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn145">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahi-<a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a> Aitareya</b> (‘descendant of Itara or Itarā’) is the name of the sage from whom the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> and Āraṇyaka take their names. He is several times referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202422"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202422">1</a></sup> but not as its author. He is credited with a life of 116 years in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202422"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202422">2</a></sup> and the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202422"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202422">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202422"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202422">↑</a> ii. 1, 8; 3, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202422"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202422">↑</a> iii. 16, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202422"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202422">↑</a> iv. 2, 11 (<i>cf. Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 246).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 16, 17.</li></ol></div>

महिष|మహిష|ಮಹಿಷ|mahiṣa|mahiSha|_vei_120@2423|_vei2_pn144
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2422">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2424">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn143">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn144">2:144</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn145">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahiṣa,</b> the ‘strong,’ with<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202423">1</a></sup> or without<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202423">2</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मृग">mṛga</a>,</b> ‘wild beast,’ denotes the ‘buffalo’ in the Rigveda and the later texts. The feminine, <a class="vei_120a" href="महिषी">mahiṣī</a>, is found in the later Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202423">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202423">↑</a> Rv. viii. 58, 15; ix. 92, 6; 96, 6; x. 123, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202423">↑</a> Rv. v. 29, 7; vi. 67, 11; viii. 12, 8; 66, 10; ix. 87, 7; x. 28, 10; 189, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 28, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202423"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202423">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxv. 6; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 8, 5; Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 7, 11.</li></ol></div>

महिषी|महिषी|महिषि|మహిషీ_౧|మహిషీ|మహిషి|ಮಹಿಷೀ_೧|ಮಹಿಷೀ|ಮಹಿಷಿ|mahiṣī_1|mahiṣī|mahiṣi|mahiShii_1|mahiShii|mahiShi|mahiShee_1|mahiShee|_vei_120@2424|_vei2_pn144
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2423">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2425">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn143">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn144">2:144</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn145">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Mahiṣī.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="महिष">mahiṣa</a>.</b></p></div>

महिषी|महिषी|महिषि|మహిషీ_౨|మహిషీ|మహిషి|ಮಹಿಷೀ_೨|ಮಹಿಷೀ|ಮಹಿಷಿ|mahiṣī_2|mahiṣī|mahiṣi|mahiShii_2|mahiShii|mahiShi|mahiShee_2|mahiShee|_vei_120@2425|_vei2_pn144
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2424">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2426">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn143">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn144">2:144</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn145">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Mahiṣī,</b> ‘the powerful one,’ the name of the first of the four wives (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a></b>) of the king, is mentioned frequently in  the later literature.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202425"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202425">1</a></sup> Perhaps even in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202425"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202425">2</a></sup> the technical sense of ‘first wife’ is present.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202425"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202425">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 9, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 4; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xix. 1, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 5, 3, 1; vii. 5, 1, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202425"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202425">↑</a> v. 2, 2; 37, 3.</li></ol></div>

महैतरेय|మహైతరేయ|ಮಹೈತರೇಯ|mahaitareya|_vei_120@2426|_vei2_pn145
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2425">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2427">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn144">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn145">2:145</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn147">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahaitareya</b> is the title of a Vedic text according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> Sūtras of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202426">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202426">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 4, 4; of a teacher, in <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 10; vi. 1. <i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 39; Oldenberg, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 29, 3, 4.</li></ol></div>

महोक्ष|మహోక్ష|ಮಹೋಕ್ಷ|mahokṣa|mahokSha|mahoxa|_vei_120@2427|_vei2_pn145
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2426">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2428">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn144">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn145">2:145</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn147">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mahokṣa,</b> a ‘great bull,’ is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 4, 1, 2).</p></div>

मांस|మాంస|ಮಾಂಸ|māṃsa|maaMsa|_vei_120@2428|_vei2_pn145
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2427">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2429">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn144">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn145">2:145</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn147">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māṃsa,</b> ‘flesh.’ The eating of flesh appears as something quite regular in the Vedic texts, which show no trace of the doctrine of Ahiṃsā, or abstaining from injury to animals. For example, the ritual offerings of flesh contemplate that the gods will eat it, and again the Brahmins ate the offerings.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202428">1</a></sup> Again, the slaying of a ‘great ox’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="महोक्ष">mahokṣa</a></i>) or a ‘great goat’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="महाज">mahāja</a></i>) for a guest was regularly prescribed;<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202428">2</a></sup> and the name <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अतिथिग्व">atithigva</a></b> probably means ‘slaying cows for guests.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202428">3</a></sup> The great sage <b><a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a></b> was wont to eat the meat of milch cows and bullocks (<i>dhenv-anaḍuha</i>) if only it was <i>aṃsala</i> (‘firm’ or ‘tender’).<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202428">4</a></sup> The slaughter of a hundred bulls (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="उक्षन्">ukṣan</a></i>) was credited to one sacrificer, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अगस्त्य">agastya</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202428">5</a></sup> The marriage ceremony was accompanied by the slaying of oxen, clearly for food.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202428">6</a></sup> </p><p>That there was any general objection to the eating of flesh is most improbable. Sometimes it is forbidden, as when a man is performing a vow,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202428">7</a></sup> or its use is disapproved, as in a passage of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202428">8</a></sup> where meat is classed with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुरा">surā</a>,</b> or intoxicating liquor, as a bad thing. Again, in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202428">9</a></sup> the slaying of the cows is said to take place in the <b>Aghās,</b> a deliberate variation for <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मघास्">maghās</a>;</b> but this may be the outcome merely of a natural association of death with gloom, even when cows alone are the victims in question. The Brāhmaṇas also contain the doctrine of the eater in this world being eaten in the next,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202428">10</a></sup> but this is not to be regarded as a moral or religious disapproval of eating flesh, though it no doubt contains the germ of such a view, which is also in harmony with the persuasion of the unity of existence, which becomes marked in the Brāhmaṇas. But Ahiṃsā as a developed and articulate doctrine would seem to have arisen from the acceptance of the doctrine of transmigration, which in its fundamentals is later than the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> period.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202428">11</a></sup></p><p>On the other hand, it is to be noted that the cow was on the road to acquire special sanctity in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202428">12</a></sup> as is shown by the name <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अघ्न्या">aghnyā</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202428">13</a></sup> ‘not to be slain,’ applied to it in several passages. But this fact cannot be regarded as showing that  meat eating generally was condemned. Apart from mythical considerations, such as the identification of the cow with earth or Aditi (which are, of course, much more than an effort of priestly ingenuity), the value of the cow for other purposes than eating was so great as to account adequately for its sanctity, the beginnings of which can in fact be traced back to Indo-Iranian times.<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1202428">14</a></sup> Moreover, the ritual of the cremation of the dead required the slaughter of a cow as an essential part, the flesh being used to envelope the dead body.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1202428">15</a></sup></p><p>The usual food of the Vedic Indian, as far as flesh was concerned, can be gathered from the list of sacrificial victims: what man ate he presented to the gods--that is, the sheep, the goat, and the ox. The horse sacrifice was an infrequent exception: it is probably not to be regarded as a trace of the use of horseflesh as food, though the possibility of such being the case cannot be overlooked in view of the widespread use of horseflesh as food in different countries and times. It is, however, more likely that the aim of this sacrifice was to impart magic strength, the speed and vigour of the horse, to the god and his worshippers, as Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1202428">16</a></sup> argues.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202428">↑</a> So <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> is called ‘eater of ox and cow’ in Rv. viii. 43, 11 = Av. iii. 21, 6 = <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 3, 14, 7; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 280, 281; Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 355.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202428">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 1, 2. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 15, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202428">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 17, 426; <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, cxxiv. <i>Cf. atithinīr gāḥ,</i> ‘cows fit for guests,’ Rv. x. 68, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202428">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 2, 21. The sense of <i>aṃsala</i> is given as <i>sthūla,</i> firm,’ in the scholiast. <i>Cf.</i> Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, vii. 2, 23-25. Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 11, has tender.’ ‘Off the shoulder’ (<i>aṃsa</i>) is also a possible version.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202428">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 11, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxi. 14, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202428">↑</a> Q.v. x. 85, 13. <i>Cf.</i> Winternitz, <i>Das altindische Hochzeitsrituell,</i> 33.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202428">↑</a> Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 1, 8. So a <b>Brahmacārin</b> is not to eat flesh. See Oldenberg, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 468, n. 3. The blood of an animal is always a somewhat mysterious and dangerous substance; hence taboos on meat-eating, which in another form arise from fear of the spirits of the dead (<i>cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 414, n. 1). See also Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 1, 1, 29; Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1909, 588, n. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202428">↑</a> vi. 70, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 493.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202428">↑</a> x. 85, 13. In the Atharvaveda, xiv. 1, 13, the ordinary word <a class="vei_120a" href="मघास्">maghās</a> is found, and is, no doubt, really to be preferred. See Weber, Proceedings of the Berlin Academy, 1894, 807.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202428">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> the story of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भृगु">bhṛgu</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वारुणि">vāruṇi</a></b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 6, 1, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 42-44; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 1, 2, with Keith's notes (pp. 202, 203).</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202428">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Deussen, <i>Philosophy of the Upanishads,</i> 317 <i>et seq.;</i> Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1909, 565.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202428">↑</a> viii. 101, 15. 16; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iv. 19, 20; Av. x. 10; xii. 4, 5; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 151.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202428">↑</a> Found sixteen times in the Rigveda, as opposed to three instances of Aghnya (masculine); Macdonell, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i> The sense of ‘hard to overcome,’ preferred by the St. Petersburg Dictionary, to that of ‘not to be killed,’ is, however, quite possible. Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 17, 281, tries to derive the word from <i>ahanya,</i> ‘bright-coloured like dya,’ a derivation that must be regarded as illegitimate.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1202428">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 68.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1202428">↑</a> Rv. x. 16, 7. See Oldenberg, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 576.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1202428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1202428">↑</a> <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 356, n. 4. As to meat-eating in Buddhist times, <i>cf.</i> the death of the Buddha from a meal of pork, Fleet, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1906, 881, 882; Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i>^5 231, n. 2 (<i>contra</i> Neumann, <i>Die Reden des Gotamo Buddho,</i> 1, xix). As to meat-eating in the Epic, see Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 119, 120; <i>Great Epic of India,</i> 377-379; and see for modern instances Jolly, <i>Deutsche Rundschau,</i> July, 1884. 118; Bühler, <i>Report,</i> 23.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 316; Hopkins, <i>Religions of India,</i> 156, 189.</li></ol></div>

मांसौदन|మాంసౌదన|ಮಾಂಸೌದನ|māṃsaudana|maaMsaudana|_vei_120@2429|_vei2_pn147
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2428">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2430">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn145">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn147">2:147</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn148">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māṃsaudana</b> denotes in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202429"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202429">1</a></sup> a dish consisting of ‘meat cooked with rice.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202429"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202429">↑</a> xi. 5, 7, 5; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xii. 8.</li></ol></div>

माक्षव्य|మాక్షవ్య|ಮಾಕ್ಷವ್ಯ|mākṣavya|maakShavya|maaxavya|_vei_120@2430|_vei2_pn147
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2429">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2431">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn145">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn147">2:147</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn148">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mākṣavya,</b> ‘descendant of Makṣu,’ is the patronymic of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202430">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202430"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202430">↑</a> iii. 1, 1, which is discussed in the preface to the Rigveda Prātiśākhya. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 391; 2, 212.</li></ol></div>

मागध|మాగధ|ಮಾಗಧ|māgadha|maagadha|_vei_120@2431|_vei2_pn148
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2430">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2432">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn147">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn148">2:148</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn149">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māgadha.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मगध">magadha</a>.</b></p></div>

मागध-देशीय|मागधदेशीय|मागध|देशीय|మాగధ-దేశీయ|మాగధదేశీయ|మాగధ|దేశీయ|ಮಾಗಧ-ದೇಶೀಯ|ಮಾಗಧದೇಶೀಯ|ಮಾಗಧ|ದೇಶೀಯ|māgadha-deśīya|māgadhadeśīya|māgadha|deśīya|maagadha-deshiiya|maagadhadeshiiya|maagadha|deshiiya|maagadha-desheeya|maagadhadesheeya|desheeya|_vei_120@2432|_vei2_pn148
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2431">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2433">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn147">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn148">2:148</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn149">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māgadha-deśīya,</b> ‘belonging to the district of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मगध">magadha</a>,</b>’ is the description in the Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202432">1</a></sup> of a Brahmin of <a class="vei_120a" href="मगध">magadha</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202432">↑</a> Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxii. 4, 22; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 6, 28.</li></ol></div>

माचल|మాచల|ಮಾಚಲ|mācala|maachala|_vei_120@2433|_vei2_pn148
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2432">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2434">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn147">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn148">2:148</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn149">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mācala,</b> mentioned in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202433"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202433">1</a></sup> apparently denotes some sort of dog found in <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विदर्भ">vidarbha</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202433"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202433">↑</a> ii. 440. <i>Cf. Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 19, 103, n. 3.</li></ol></div>

माठरी|माठरि|మాఠరీ|మాఠరి|ಮಾಠರೀ|ಮಾಠರಿ|māṭharī|māṭhari|maaTharii|maaThari|maaTharee|_vei_120@2434|_vei2_pn148
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2433">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2435">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn147">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn148">2:148</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn149">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māṭharī,</b> ‘female descendant of Maṭhara,’ occurs in the curious name, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काश्यपी-बालाक्या-माठरी-पुत्र">kāśyapī-<a class="vei_120a" href="बालाक्या">bālākyā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="माठरी">māṭharī</a>-putra</a>,</b> of a teacher in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 4, 31 Mādhyaṃdina).</p></div>

माण्टि|మాణ్టి|ಮಾಣ್ಟಿ|māṇṭi|maaNTi|_vei_120@2435|_vei2_pn148
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2434">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2436">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn147">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn148">2:148</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn149">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māṇṭi</b> is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a>,</b> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202435">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202435">↑</a> ii. 5, 22; iv. 5, 28 (Mādhyaṃdina = ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

माण्डवी|माण्डवि|మాణ్డవీ|మాణ్డవి|ಮಾಣ್ಡವೀ|ಮಾಣ್ಡವಿ|māṇḍavī|māṇḍavi|maaNDavii|maaNDavi|maaNDavee|_vei_120@2436|_vei2_pn148
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2435">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2437">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn147">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn148">2:148</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn149">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māṇḍavī,</b> ‘female descendant of Maṇḍu,’ occurs in the name of a teacher, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वात्सी-माण्डवी-पुत्र">vātsī-<a class="vei_120a" href="माण्डवी">māṇḍavī</a>-putra</a>,</b> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 4, 30 Mādhyaṃdina).</p></div>

माण्डव्य|మాణ్డవ్య|ಮಾಣ್ಡವ್ಯ|māṇḍavya|maaNDavya|_vei_120@2437|_vei2_pn148
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2436">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2438">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn147">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn148">2:148</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn149">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māṇḍavya,</b> ‘descendant of Maṇḍu,’ is mentioned as a teacher in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202437">1</a></sup> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202437">2</a></sup> and in the Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202437">3</a></sup> He is also mentioned as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौत्स">kautsa</a></b> in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202437">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202437">↑</a> x. 6, 5, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202437">↑</a> vii. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202437">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 4, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 10; vi. 1. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien</i> 1, 482 (in the Epic a friend of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a></b> is so named).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202437">↑</a> vi. 5, 4 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</li></ol></div>

माण्डूकायनि|మాణ్డూకాయని|ಮಾಣ್ಡೂಕಾಯನಿ|māṇḍūkāyani|maaNDuukaayani|maaNDookaayani|_vei_120@2438|_vei2_pn148
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2437">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2439">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn147">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn148">2:148</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn149">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māṇḍūkāyani,</b> ‘descendant of Māṇḍūka,’ is mentioned as a teacher in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202438"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202438">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202438"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202438">↑</a> x. 6, 5, 9; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 5, 4 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</li></ol></div>

माण्डूकायनी-पुत्र|माण्डूकायनीपुत्र|माण्डूकायनी|माण्डूकायनि|पुत्र|మాణ్డూకాయనీ-పుత్ర|మాణ్డూకాయనీపుత్ర|మాణ్డూకాయనీ|మాణ్డూకాయని|పుత్ర|ಮಾಣ್ಡೂಕಾಯನೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಮಾಣ್ಡೂಕಾಯನೀಪುತ್ರ|ಮಾಣ್ಡೂಕಾಯನೀ|ಮಾಣ್ಡೂಕಾಯನಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|māṇḍūkāyanī-putra|māṇḍūkāyanīputra|māṇḍūkāyanī|māṇḍūkāyani|putra|maaNDuukaayanii-putra|maaNDuukaayaniiputra|maaNDuukaayanii|maaNDuukaayani|maaNDookaayanee-putra|maaNDookaayaneeputra|maaNDookaayanee|maaNDookaayani|_vei_120@2439|_vei2_pn149
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2438">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2440">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn148">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn149">2:149</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn150">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māṇḍūkāyanī-putra,</b> ‘son of a female descendant of Māṇḍūka,’ is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="माण्डूकीपुत्र">māṇḍūkīputra</a></b> in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Brhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202439"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202439">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202439"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202439">↑</a> vi. 4, 32 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 5, 2 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

माण्डूकी-पुत्र|माण्डूकीपुत्र|माण्डूकी|माण्डूकि|पुत्र|మాణ్డూకీ-పుత్ర|మాణ్డూకీపుత్ర|మాణ్డూకీ|మాణ్డూకి|పుత్ర|ಮಾಣ್ಡೂಕೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಮಾಣ್ಡೂಕೀಪುತ್ರ|ಮಾಣ್ಡೂಕೀ|ಮಾಣ್ಡೂಕಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|māṇḍūkī-putra|māṇḍūkīputra|māṇḍūkī|māṇḍūki|putra|maaNDuukii-putra|maaNDuukiiputra|maaNDuukii|maaNDuuki|maaNDookee-putra|maaNDookeeputra|maaNDookee|maaNDooki|_vei_120@2440|_vei2_pn149
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2439">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2441">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn148">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn149">2:149</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn150">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māṇḍūkī-putra,</b> ‘son of a female descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="मण्डूक">maṇḍūka</a>,’ is mentioned as a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्डिलीपुत्र">śāṇḍilīputra</a>,</b> in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202440"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202440">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202440"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202440">↑</a> vi. 4, 32 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 5, 2 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

माण्डूकेय|మాణ్డూకేయ|ಮಾಣ್ಡೂಕೇಯ|māṇḍūkeya|maaNDuukeya|maaNDookeya|_vei_120@2441|_vei2_pn149
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2440">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2442">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn148">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn149">2:149</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn150">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māṇḍūkeya,</b> ‘descendant of Māṇḍūka,’ is the patronymic of several teachers in the Rigveda Āraṇyakas--viz., <b>Śūravīra,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202441">1</a></sup> <b>Hrasva,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202441">2</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दीर्घ">dīrgha</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202441">3</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यम">madhyama</a> Prātībodhīputra.</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202441">4</a></sup> The Māṇḍūkeyas also occur as a school in the Āraṇyakas:<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202441">5</a></sup> a special form of the text of the Rigveda evidently appertained to them.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202441">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202441">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 1, 1; Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, vii. 2. 8. 9. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202441">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, vii. 12; viii. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202441">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> vii. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202441">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> vii. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202441">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 1, 1; Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, vii. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202441">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> the <i>Māṇḍākeyīya adhyāya</i> of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 2, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, viii. 11; Scheftelowitz, <i>Die Apokryphen des Rigveda,</i> 12; Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1907, 227; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 239; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 391.</li></ol></div>

मातरिश्वन्|మాతరిశ్వన్|ಮಾತರಿಶ್ವನ್|mātariśvan|maatarishvan|_vei_120@2442|_vei2_pn149
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2441">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2443">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn148">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn149">2:149</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn150">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mātariśvan</b> is mentioned in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वालखिल्य">vālakhilya</a> hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202442">1</a></sup> as a sacrificer along with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मेध्य">medhya</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पृषध्र">pṛṣadhra</a>.</b> He seems to be mentioned also in one other passage, possibly in two.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202442">2</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202442">3</a></sup> a patron, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पृषध्र">pṛṣadhra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मेध्य">medhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मातरिश्वन्">mātariśvan</a></b> or <b>Mātariśva</b> is created by a misunderstanding of the Rigvedic text. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202442">↑</a> Rv. viii. 52, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202442">↑</a> Rv. x. 48, 2; 105, 6. The former reference is much more probable than the latter.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202442">↑</a> xvi. 11, 26; Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 39, 40. The manuscripts vary between <a class="vei_120a" href="मातरिश्वन्">mātariśvan</a> and Mātariśva.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 163.</li></ol></div>

मातुर्-भ्रात्र|मातुर्भ्रात्र|मातुर्|भ्रात्र|మాతుర్-భ్రాత్ర|మాతుర్భ్రాత్ర|మాతుర్|భ్రాత్ర|ಮಾತುರ್-ಭ್ರಾತ್ರ|ಮಾತುರ್ಭ್ರಾತ್ರ|ಮಾತುರ್|ಭ್ರಾತ್ರ|mātur-bhrātra|māturbhrātra|mātur|bhrātra|maatur-bhraatra|maaturbhraatra|maatur|bhraatra|_vei_120@2443|_vei2_pn150
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2442">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2444">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn149">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn150">2:150</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn151">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mātur-bhrātra</b> is a curiously formed compound, occurring once in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202443"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202443">1</a></sup> as a designation of the ‘maternal uncle,’ who in the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> period bears the name of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मातुल">mātula</a>.</b> Thus little is heard of the maternal uncle in the Vedic period: it is not till the Epic<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202443"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202443">2</a></sup> that traces appear of his prominence as compared with the paternal uncle (<i>pitṛvya</i>). This fact is significant for the ‘patriarchal’ character of the early Indian family organization.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202443"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202443">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202443"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202443">↑</a> i. 6, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202443"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202443">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 141.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202443"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202443">↑</a> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 484, 586-588. <i>Cf.</i> also Rivers, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1907, 629 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

मातुल|మాతుల|ಮಾತುಲ|mātula|maatula|_vei_120@2444|_vei2_pn150
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2443">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2445">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn149">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn150">2:150</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn151">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mātula,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202444"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202444">1</a></sup> ‘maternal uncle,’ is found only in the Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202444"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202444">2</a></sup> and later.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202444"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202444">↑</a> This peculiarly formed word was presumably a dialectic form which made its way into the written speech.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202444"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202444">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 24, 4, etc.</li></ol></div>

मातृ|మాతృ|ಮಾತೃ|mātṛ|maatR|maatRi|maatRu|maatRRi|_vei_120@2445|_vei2_pn150
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2444">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2446">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn149">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn150">2:150</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn151">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mātṛ</b> is the regular word for ‘mother’ from the Rigveda onwards,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202445"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202445">1</a></sup> being a formation probably developed under the influence of an onomatopœtic word <i>mā,</i><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202445"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202445">2</a></sup> used like Ambā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202445"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202445">3</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नना">nanā</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202445"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202445">4</a></sup></p><p>The relations of wife and husband, as well as of mother and children, are treated under <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>.</b> It remains only to add that details are given in the Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202445"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202445">5</a></sup> of the respectful attention paid to a mother, and of the ceremonies in which she is concerned. The mother also appears interested in the fate of her children as in the story of the sale of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a></b> for adoption by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202445"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202445">6</a></sup> </p><p>In the household the mother ranked after the father (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पितृ">pitṛ</a></b>). Occasionally <i>mātarā</i> is used for ‘parents,’ as are also <i>pitarā</i> and <i>mātarā pitarā</i><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202445"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202445">7</a></sup> and <i>mātā-pitaraḥ.</i><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202445"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202445">8</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202445"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202445">↑</a> i. 24, 1; vii. 101, 3, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiii. 21, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202445"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202445">↑</a> Bo7htlingk and Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> note.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202445"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202445">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <i>ambe ambike ambalike,</i> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 18, with variations in <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 4, 19, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 12, 20; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 9, 6, 3; also <i>ambā ambāyavī, ambayā,</i> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202445"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202445">↑</a> Rv. ix. 112, 3 (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपलप्रक्षिणी">upalaprakṣiṇī</a></b>). See von Schroeder, <i>Mysterium und Mimus,</i> 412.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202445"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202445">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 460, 476, 477.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202445"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202445">↑</a> vii. 18 <i>seq. Cf.</i> also Leist, <i>Altarisches Jus Gentium,</i> 104; Jolly, <i>Die Adoption in Indien,</i> 16, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202445"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202445">↑</a> Rv. iii. 33, 3; vii. 2, 5, etc. For <i>mātarā pitarā,</i> see Rv. iv. 6, 7; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ix. 19.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202445"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202445">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 3, 10, vi. 3, 11, 3.</li></ol></div>

मातृ-वध|मातृवध|मातृ|वध|మాతృ-వధ|మాతృవధ|మాతృ|వధ|ಮಾತೃ-ವಧ|ಮಾತೃವಧ|ಮಾತೃ|ವಧ|mātṛ-vadha|mātṛvadha|mātṛ|vadha|maatR-vadha|maatRvadha|maatR|maatRi-vadha|maatRivadha|maatRi|maatRu-vadha|maatRuvadha|maatRu|maatRRi-vadha|maatRRivadha|maatRRi|_vei_120@2446|_vei2_pn151
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2445">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2447">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn150">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn151">2:151</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn152">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mātṛ-vadha,</b> ‘matricide,’ is mentioned as a very grave crime in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iii. 1), but as one that can be expiated by the knowledge of the truth.</p></div>

मातृ-हन्|मातृहन्|मातृ|हन्|మాతృ-హన్|మాతృహన్|మాతృ|హన్|ಮಾತೃ-ಹನ್|ಮಾತೃಹನ್|ಮಾತೃ|ಹನ್|mātṛ-han|mātṛhan|mātṛ|han|maatR-han|maatRhan|maatR|maatRi-han|maatRihan|maatRi|maatRu-han|maatRuhan|maatRu|maatRRi-han|maatRRihan|maatRRi|_vei_120@2447|_vei2_pn151
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2446">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2448">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn150">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn151">2:151</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn152">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mātṛ-han,</b> ‘mother-killer,’ ‘matricide,’ occurs in a Vedic quotation mentioned by the commentator on Pāṇini.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202447"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202447">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202447"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202447">↑</a> Kāśikā <a class="vei_120a" href="वृत्ति">vṛtti</a> on Pāṇīni, iii. 2, 88; <i>mātṛhā saptamaṃ narakaṃ praviśet.</i></li></ol></div>

मात्रा|మాత్రా|ಮಾತ್ರಾ|mātrā|maatraa|_vei_120@2448|_vei2_pn151
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2447">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2449">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn150">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn151">2:151</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn152">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mātrā</b> in the Upaniṣads<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202448">1</a></sup> denotes a mora, the length of a short vowel.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202448">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 2, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 1, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, vii. 15.</li></ol></div>

मात्स्य|मात्स्य|మాత్స్య_౧|మాత్స్య|ಮಾತ್ಸ್ಯ_೧|ಮಾತ್ಸ್ಯ|mātsya_1|mātsya|maatsya_1|maatsya|_vei_120@2449|_vei2_pn151
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2448">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2450">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn150">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn151">2:151</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn152">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Mātsya,</b> ‘prince of the <a class="vei_120a" href="मत्स्य">matsya</a> people.’ See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मत्स्य">matsya</a>.</b></p></div>

मात्स्य|मात्स्य|మాత్స్య_౨|మాత్స్య|ಮಾತ್ಸ್ಯ_೨|ಮಾತ್ಸ್ಯ|mātsya_2|mātsya|maatsya_2|maatsya|_vei_120@2450|_vei2_pn151
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2449">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2451">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn150">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn151">2:151</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn152">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Mātsya</b> occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202450"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202450">1</a></sup> as the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> skilled in sacrifice. Possibly,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202450"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202450">2</a></sup> but not probably, he may also be meant in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202450"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202450">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202450"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202450">↑</a> i. 5, 2, 1, where he serves <a class="vei_120a" href="यज्ञेषु">yajñeṣu</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="शतद्युम्न">śatadyumna</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202450"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202450">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 681.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202450"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202450">↑</a> xix. 39, 9.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 306.</li></ol></div>

माथव|మాథవ|ಮಾಥವ|māthava|maathava|_vei_120@2451|_vei2_pn151
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2450">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2452">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn150">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn151">2:151</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn152">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māthava,</b> ‘descendant of Mathu,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विदेघ">videgha</a>,</b> perhaps ‘king of <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>,’ in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202451"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202451">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202451"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202451">↑</a> i. 4, 1, 10. 17. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, xli, 104, n. 1; 26, xxix.; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 170.</li></ol></div>

माधुकि|మాధుకి|ಮಾಧುಕಿ|mādhuki|maadhuki|_vei_120@2452|_vei2_pn152
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2451">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2453">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn151">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn152">2:152</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn153">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mādhuki,</b> ‘descendant of <b>Madhuka,</b> is the patronymic of a teacher mentioned with disapproval in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202452"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202452">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202452"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202452">↑</a> ii. 1, 4, 27. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 434.</li></ol></div>

माध्यंदिनायन|माध्यन्दिनायन|మాధ్యందినాయన|మాధ్యన్దినాయన|ಮಾಧ್ಯಂದಿನಾಯನ|ಮಾಧ್ಯನ್ದಿನಾಯನ|mādhyaṃdināyana|maadhyaMdinaayana|maadhyandinaayana|_vei_120@2453|_vei2_pn152
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2452">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2454">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn151">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn152">2:152</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn153">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mādhyaṃdināyana,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यंदिन">madhyaṃdina</a>,’ is the patronymic of a teacher mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iv. 6, 2).</p></div>

माध्यम|మాధ్యమ|ಮಾಧ್ಯಮ|mādhyama|maadhyama|_vei_120@2454|_vei2_pn152
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2453">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2455">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn151">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn152">2:152</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn153">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mādhyama</b> (‘relating to the middle’) is a term applied in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202454"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202454">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202454"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202454">2</a></sup> to denote the ‘authors of the middle books’ (ii.-vii.) of the Rigveda.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202454"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202454">↑</a> xii. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202454"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202454">↑</a> ii. 2, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 115, 389; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 4, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 10, etc.</li></ol></div>

मान|मान|మాన_౧|మాన|ಮಾನ_೧|ಮಾನ|māna_1|māna|maana_1|maana|_vei_120@2455|_vei2_pn152
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2454">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2456">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn151">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn152">2:152</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn153">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Māna</b> as a measure of weight is said to be the equivalent of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्णल">kṛṣṇala</a></b> or Raktikā--that is, the berry of the Guñjā (<i>Abrus precatorius</i>). It occurs in compounds in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202455"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202455">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202455"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202455">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 2, 6, 3; vi. 4, 10, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 7, 7; 7, 6, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 3, 24; 5, 5, 16, etc.</li></ol></div>

मान|मान|మాన_౨|మాన|ಮಾನ_೨|ಮಾನ|māna_2|māna|maana_2|maana|_vei_120@2456|_vei2_pn152
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2455">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2457">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn151">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn152">2:152</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn153">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Māna</b> is the name of a man occurring in several passages of the Rigveda. In one place<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202456">1</a></sup> express mention is made of his son (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सूनु">sūnu</a></i>), by whom, despite Bergaigne's view to the contrary,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202456">2</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अगस्त्य">agastya</a></b> must be meant. In another passage,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202456">3</a></sup> apparently the same meaning applies to <a class="vei_120a" href="मान">māna</a>--that is, <a class="vei_120a" href="अगस्त्य">agastya</a> as ‘a <a class="vei_120a" href="मान">māna</a>.’ In a third passage<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202456">4</a></sup> the expression <i>sūnave Mānena</i> has been held by Sieg<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202456">5</a></sup> to be an inversion of <i>Mānasya sūnunā,</i> ‘by the son of Māna’--<i>i.e.,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="अगस्त्य">agastya</a>; but it seems more likely<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202456">6</a></sup> that either <i>sūnor <a class="vei_120a" href="मान">māna</a></i> is the fuller form of Agastya's name (‘pride  of the son,’ with reference to his high ancestry), or that the son<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202456">7</a></sup> of <a class="vei_120a" href="मान">māna</a> (= <a class="vei_120a" href="अगस्त्य">agastya</a>) is alluded to as interested in <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्पला">viśpalā</a>.</b></p><p>The Mānas--that is, the descendants of <a class="vei_120a" href="मान">māna</a>, are in several passages alluded to as singers.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202456">8</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मान्य">mānya</a>, Māndārya.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202456">↑</a> Rv. i. 189, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202456">↑</a> <i>Religion Védique,</i> 2, 394. <i>Cf.</i> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 173; Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 221, n. 5; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 110; Sieg. <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 107; Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 135.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202456">↑</a> vii. 33, 13. <i>Cf.</i> verse 10.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202456">↑</a> i. 117, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202456">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202456">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten, loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202456">↑</a> Bergaigne, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Pischel, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>. Cf.</i> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> where <i>sūnoḥ</i> is taken as dependent on <i>vājam.</i></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202456"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202456">↑</a> Rv. i. 169, 8; 171, 5; 182, 8; 184, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 116, 117, who thinks the Mānas were settled on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धु">sindhu</a></b> (Indus). See Rv. i. 186,5</li></ol></div>

मानव|మానవ|ಮಾನವ|mānava|maanava|_vei_120@2457|_vei2_pn153
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2456">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2458">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn152">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn153">2:153</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn154">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mānava,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>,</b>’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202457">1</a></sup> is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नाभानेदिष्ठ">nābhānediṣṭha</a></b> and of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शार्यात">śāryāta</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202457">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202457">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 14, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202457">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> iv. 32, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 5, 2 (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="शर्यात">śaryāta</a></b>).</li></ol></div>

मानवी|मानवि|మానవీ|మానవి|ಮಾನವೀ|ಮಾನವಿ|mānavī|mānavi|maanavii|maanavi|maanavee|_vei_120@2458|_vei2_pn153
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2457">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2459">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn152">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn153">2:153</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn154">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mānavī,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of the mythical Iḍā (‘oblation’) in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202458">1</a></sup> and of a woman named <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्शु">parśu</a></b> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202458">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202458">↑</a> i. 8, 1, 26; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 7, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202458">↑</a> x. 86, 23.</li></ol></div>

मानु-तन्तव्य|मानुतन्तव्य|मानु|तन्तव्य|మాను-తన్తవ్య|మానుతన్తవ్య|మాను|తన్తవ్య|ಮಾನು-ತನ್ತವ್ಯ|ಮಾನುತನ್ತವ್ಯ|ಮಾನು|ತನ್ತವ್ಯ|mānu-tantavya|mānutantavya|mānu|tantavya|maanu-tantavya|maanutantavya|maanu|_vei_120@2459|_vei2_pn153
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2458">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2460">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn152">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn153">2:153</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn154">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mānu-tantavya,</b> ‘descendant of Manutantu,’ is the patronymic of <b>Aikādaśākṣa</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (v. 30, 15). The Saumāpau Mānutantavyau, ‘two Saumāpas, descendants of Manutantu,’ are mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 5, 3, 2).</p></div>

मान्थाल|మాన్థాల|ಮಾನ್ಥಾಲ|mānthāla|maanthaala|_vei_120@2460|_vei2_pn153
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2459">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2461">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn152">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn153">2:153</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn154">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mānthāla</b> is the form in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (ii. 5, 8, 4) of the next name.</p></div>

मन्थालव|మన్థాలవ|ಮನ್ಥಾಲವ|manthālava|manthaalava|_vei_120@2461|_vei2_pn153
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2460">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2462">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn152">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn153">2:153</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn154">↷</a><hr><p><b>Manthālava,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202461">1</a></sup> <b>Mānthīlava</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202461">2</a></sup> are the names in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās of a victim at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’). What it was is unknown: the commentator Mahīdhara<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202461">3</a></sup> thinks it was a kind of mouse; Sāyaṇa explains it as a ‘water-cock’  (<i>jala-<a class="vei_120a" href="कुक्कुट">kukkuṭa</a></i>). Possiby, if Sāyaṇa's<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202461">4</a></sup> version of the parallel word <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मन्थावल">manthāvala</a></b> is to be trusted, the ‘flying fox’ may be meant.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202461">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202461">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 19, where there is a variant Mātālava; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 38.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202461">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 18, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202461">↑</a> On Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202461">↑</a> On <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>. Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 86.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202461"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202461">↑</a> Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> and also <i>s.v. māndhāla.</i></li></ol></div>

मान्दार्य मान्य|మాన్దార్య మాన్య|ಮಾನ್ದಾರ್ಯ ಮಾನ್ಯ|māndārya mānya|maandaarya maanya|_vei_120@2462|_vei2_pn154
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2461">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2463">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn153">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn154">2:154</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn155">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māndārya Mānya,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मान">māna</a>,</b>’ is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202462"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202462">1</a></sup> It seems most probable that <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अगस्त्य">agastya</a></b> himself is meant.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202462"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202462">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202462"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202462">↑</a> i. 165, 15 = i. 166, 15 = i. 167, 11 = i. 168, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202462"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202462">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 135; Bergaigne, <i>Religion Védique,</i> 2, 394; Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 221; Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 107; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 183 <i>et seq.,</i> 206.</li></ol></div>

मान्य|మాన్య|ಮಾನ್ಯ|mānya|maanya|_vei_120@2463|_vei2_pn154
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2462">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2464">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn153">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn154">2:154</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn155">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mānya,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मान">māna</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b>Māndārya</b> in several passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202463">1</a></sup> being also found alone in others.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202463">2</a></sup> It probably denotes <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अगस्त्य">agastya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202463">↑</a> See <b>Māndārya,</b> n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202463">↑</a> i. 165, 14; 177, 5; 184, 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a></i> 107.</li></ol></div>

मान्यमान|మాన్యమాన|ಮಾನ್ಯಮಾನ|mānyamāna|maanyamaana|_vei_120@2464|_vei2_pn154
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2463">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2465">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn153">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn154">2:154</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn155">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mānyamāna</b> occurs with the word <b>Devaka</b> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202464"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202464">1</a></sup> The word seems to be a patronymic from Manyamāna, meaning ‘son of the proud one.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202464"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202464">2</a></sup> Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202464"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202464">3</a></sup> renders the two words ‘the godling, the proudling (hast thou smitten).’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202464"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202464">↑</a> viii. 18, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202464"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202464">↑</a> Sāyaṇa takes Manyamāna as a proper name.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202464"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202464">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 264.</li></ol></div>

मामतेय|మామతేయ|ಮಾಮತೇಯ|māmateya|maamateya|_vei_120@2465|_vei2_pn154
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2464">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2466">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn153">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn154">2:154</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn155">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māmateya,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="ममता">mamatā</a>,’ is the metronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दीर्घतमस्">dīrghatamas</a></b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202465"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202465">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202465"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202465">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202465"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202465">↑</a> i. 147, 3; 152, 6; 158, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202465"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202465">↑</a> viii. 23, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 17. For <a class="vei_120a" href="ममता">mamatā</a>, <i>cf.</i> Bṛhaddevatā, iii. 56; iv. 11.</li></ol></div>

मायव|మాయవ|ಮಾಯವ|māyava|maayava|_vei_120@2466|_vei2_pn154
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2465">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2467">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn153">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn154">2:154</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn155">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māyava,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="मयु">mayu</a> or <a class="vei_120a" href="मायु">māyu</a>,’ is the patronymic of a patron in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202466"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202466">1</a></sup> perhaps of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राम">rāma</a>,</b> as Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202466"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202466">2</a></sup> thinks. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202466"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202466">↑</a> x. 93, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202466"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202466">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 106.</li></ol></div>

माय|మాయ|ಮಾಯ|māya|maaya|_vei_120@2467|_vei2_pn155
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2466">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2468">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn154">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn155">2:155</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn156">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māya</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 4, 3, 11) corresponds to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="असुरविद्या">asuravidyā</a>,</b> ‘magic.’</p></div>

मायु|మాయు|ಮಾಯು|māyu|maayu|_vei_120@2468|_vei2_pn155
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2467">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2469">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn154">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn155">2:155</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn156">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māyu</b> denotes the ‘lowing’ of a cow and the ‘bleating’ of a sheep or goat in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202468">1</a></sup> as well as the ‘chattering’ of a monkey in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202468">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202468">↑</a> i. 164, 28 (cow); vii. 103, 2 (cow); x. 95, 3 (ewe); <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202468">↑</a> vi. 38, 4; xix. 49, 4 (called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुष">puruṣa</a>; cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 85, 86; St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. <a class="vei_120a" href="मायु">māyu</a></i>).</li></ol></div>

मारुत|మారుత|ಮಾರುತ|māruta|maaruta|_vei_120@2469|_vei2_pn155
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2468">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2470">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn154">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn155">2:155</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn156">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māruta,</b> ‘descendant of Marut,’ is the patronymic of <b>Dyutāna</b> and of <b>Nitāna.</b></p></div>

मारुताश्व|మారుతాశ్వ|ಮಾರುತಾಶ್ವ|mārutāśva|maarutaashva|_vei_120@2470|_vei2_pn155
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2469">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2471">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn154">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn155">2:155</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn156">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mārutāśva,</b> ‘descendant of Marutāśva,’ is, according to Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202470"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202470">1</a></sup> the patronymic of a patron in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202470"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202470">2</a></sup> The word may, however, be merely an adjective ‘having windswift horses.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202470"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202470">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 155. It may be a patronymic of <b>Cyavatāna.</b></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202470"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202470">↑</a> v. 33, 9.</li></ol></div>

मार्गवेय|మార్గవేయ|ಮಾರ್ಗವೇಯ|mārgaveya|maargaveya|_vei_120@2471|_vei2_pn155
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2470">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2472">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn154">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn155">2:155</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn156">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mārgaveya</b> is the patronymic or metronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राम">rāma</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vii. 27, 3. 4), where he is mentioned as a <b>Śyāparṇa.</b></p></div>

मार्गार|మార్గార|ಮಾರ್ಗಾರ|mārgāra|maargaara|_vei_120@2472|_vei2_pn155
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2471">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2473">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn154">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn155">2:155</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn156">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mārgāra</b> is the name of one of the victims at the Paruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202472"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202472">1</a></sup> The sense of the word is apparently ‘hunter,’ or possibly ‘fisherman,’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202472"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202472">2</a></sup> as a patronymic from <i>mṛgāri,</i> ‘enemy of wild beasts.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202472"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202472">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 12, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202472"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202472">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Sāyaṇa on <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

माल्य|माल्य|మాల్య_౧|మాల్య|ಮಾಲ್ಯ_೧|ಮಾಲ್ಯ|mālya_1|mālya|maalya_1|maalya|_vei_120@2473|_vei2_pn155
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2472">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2474">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn154">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn155">2:155</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn156">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Mālya,</b> ‘garland,’ is found in the Upaniṣads.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202473"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202473">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202473"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202473">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, viii. 2, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 4, etc.</li></ol></div>

माल्य|माल्य|మాల్య_౨|మాల్య|ಮಾಲ್ಯ_೨|ಮಾಲ್ಯ|mālya_2|mālya|maalya_2|maalya|_vei_120@2474|_vei2_pn155
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2473">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2475">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn154">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn155">2:155</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn156">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Mālya,</b> ‘descendant of Māla,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 4, 11). </p></div>

माष|మాష|ಮಾಷ|māṣa|maaSha|_vei_120@2475|_vei2_pn156
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2474">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2476">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn155">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn156">2:156</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn163">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māṣa</b> is the name of a kind of bean (<i>Phaseolus radiatus</i>) in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202475">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202475">2</a></sup> It is still one of the most valuable of similar plants in India. The seeds<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202475">3</a></sup> were pounded (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पिष्ट">piṣṭa</a></i>) according to the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202475">4</a></sup> These beans ripened in the winter (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="हेमन्त">hemanta</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202475">5</a></sup> In the ritual the human head for the sacrifice is bought for twenty-one Māṣas<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202475">6</a></sup> : it does not appear that the word here means a weight of metal, as it often does later.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202475">7</a></sup> A taboo on beans is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202475">8</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202475">↑</a> vi. 140, 2; xii. 2, 53.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202475">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 1, 8, 1; vii. 2, 10, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 7; xxxii. 7; xxxvii. 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 12; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 1, 10; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 3, 22 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 3, 13 (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202475">↑</a> Later described as marked with black and grey spots. <i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202475">↑</a> xii. 2, 53. <i>Ibid.,</i> xii. 2, 4, an offering of crushed beans (<i>māṣājya</i>) is mentioned.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202475">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 2, 10, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202475">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> v. 1, 8, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xx. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202475">↑</a> Weber, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 18, 267. According to <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>, viii. 134, one <a class="vei_120a" href="माष">māṣa</a> is equivalent to five (erroneously stated as four above, vol. i., p. 185) <b>Kṛṣṇalas.</b> <i>Cf.</i> also St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 2.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202475">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxii. 7; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 4, 10. <i>Cf.</i> von Schroeder, <i>Vienna Oriental Journal,</i> 15, 187-212; Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1909, 587, 588. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 240.</li></ol></div>

मास्|मा|మాస్|మా|ಮಾಸ್|ಮಾ|mās|mā|maas|maa|_vei_120@2476|_vei2_pn156
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2475">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2477">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn155">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn156">2:156</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn163">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mās</b> denotes rarely ‘moon,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202476"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202476">1</a></sup> and often ‘month’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202476"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202476">2</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202476"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202476">3</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202476"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202476">↑</a> Rv. x. 12, 7. <i>Cf.</i> also the compound <i>sūryā-māsā,</i> ‘sun and moon,’ viii. 94, 2; x. 64, 3; 68, 10; 92, 12; 93, 5, which may, however, be formed from <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>. Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> p. 220, n. 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202476"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202476">↑</a> Rv. i. 25, 8; iv. 18, 4; v. 45, 7. 11; vii. 91, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202476"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202476">↑</a> Av. viii. 10, 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 2, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 4, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 9, 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

मास|మాస|ಮಾಸ|māsa|maasa|_vei_120@2477|_vei2_pn156
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2476">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2478">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn155">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn156">2:156</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn163">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māsa</b> denotes a ‘month,’ a period of time repeatedly mentioned in the Rigveda and later.</p><p>The characteristic days (or rather nights) of the month were those of the new moon, <a class="vei_120a" href="अमा">amā</a>-vasyā, ‘home-staying (night),’ and ‘of the full moon,’ <a class="vei_120a" href="पौर्ण-मासी">paurṇa-māsī</a>. Two hymns of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202477">1</a></sup> celebrate these days respectively. A personification of the phases of the moon is seen in the four names <a class="vei_120a" href="सिनीवाली">sinīvālī</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202477">2</a></sup>  the day before new moon; <a class="vei_120a" href="कुहू">kuhū</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202477">3</a></sup> also called Guṅgū,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202477">4</a></sup> the new moon day; <a class="vei_120a" href="अनुमति">anumati</a>,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202477">5</a></sup> the day before full moon; and <a class="vei_120a" href="राका">rākā</a>,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202477">6</a></sup> the day of new moon. The importance of the new and full moon days is seen in the <a class="vei_120a" href="दर्श">darśa</a>-pūrṇamāsau, or festivals of the new and full moon days respectively.</p><p>One special day in the month, the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="एकाष्टका">ekāṣṭakā</a>,</b> or eighth day after full moon, was important. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202477">7</a></sup> there are stated to be in the year twelve such, mentioned between the twelve days of full moon and the twelve days of new moon. But one <a class="vei_120a" href="एकाष्टका">ekāṣṭakā</a> is referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās and elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202477">8</a></sup> as of quite special importance. This was, in the accordant opinion of most commentators, the eighth day after the full moon of <b>Māgha.</b> It marked the end of the year, or the beginning of the new year. Though the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202477">9</a></sup> places the winter solstice in the new moon of Māgha, the latter date probably means the new moon preceding full moon in Māgha,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202477">10</a></sup> not the new moon following  full moon; but it is perhaps possible to account adequately for the importance of the <a class="vei_120a" href="एकाष्टका">ekāṣṭakā</a> as being the first <a class="vei_120a" href="अष्टका">aṣṭakā</a> after the beginning of the new year.</p><p>It is not certain exactly how the month was reckoned, whether from the day after new moon to new moon--the system known as <i>amānta,</i> or from the day after full moon to full moon--the <i>pūrṇimānta</i> system, which later, at any rate, was followed in North India, while the other system prevailed in the south. Jacobi<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202477">11</a></sup> argues that the year began in the full moon of Phālguna, and that only by the full moon's conjunction with the <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> could the month be known. Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202477">12</a></sup> points to the fact that the new moon is far more distinctively an epoch than the full moon; that the Greek, Roman, and Jewish years began with the new moon; and that the Vedic evidence is the division of the month into the former (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पूर्व">pūrva</a></i>) and latter (<i>apara</i>) halves, the first being the bright (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शुक्ल">śukla</a></i>), the second the dark (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण">kṛṣṇa</a></i>) period. Thibaut<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202477">13</a></sup> considers that to assume the existence of the <i>pūrṇimānta</i> system for the <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a> is unnecessary, though possible. Weber<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202477">10</a></sup> assumes that it occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> as held by the scholiasts. But it would probably be a mistake to press that passage, or to assume that the <i>amānta</i> system was rigidly accepted in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>: it seems at least as probable that the month was vaguely regarded as beginning with the new moon day, so that new moon preceded full moon, which was in the middle, not the end or the beginning of the month.</p><p>That a month regularly had 30 days is established by the conclusive evidence of numerous passages in which the year is given 12 months and 360 days. This month is known from the earliest records, being both referred to directly and alluded to.<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1202477">14</a></sup>  It is the regular month of the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1202477">15</a></sup> and must be regarded as the month which the Vedic Indian recognized. No other month is mentioned as such in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> literature; it is only in the Sūtras that months of different length occur. The <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a> Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1202477">16</a></sup> refer to (1) years with 324 days--<i>i.e.,</i> periodic years with 12 months of 27 days each; (2) years with 351 days--<i>i.e.,</i> periodic years with 12 months of 27 days each, plus another month of 27 days; (3) years with 354 days--<i>i.e.,</i> 6 months of 30 days, and 6 with 29 days, in other words, lunar synodic years; (4) years with 360 days, or ordinary civil (<i>sāvana</i>) years; (5) years with 378 days, which, as Thibaut<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1202477">17</a></sup> clearly shows, are third years, in which, after two years of 360 days each, 18 days were added to bring about correspondence between the civil year and the solar year of 366 days. But even the Sāmasūtras do not mention the year of 366 days, which is first known to the <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्योतिष">jyotiṣa</a><sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1202477">18</a></sup> and to <a class="vei_120a" href="गर्ग">garga</a>.<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1202477">19</a></sup></p><p>That the Vedic period was acquainted with the year of 354 days cannot be affirmed with certainty. Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1202477">20</a></sup> indeed, thinks that it is proved by the fact that pregnancy is estimated at ten months, or sometimes a year.<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1202477">21</a></sup> But Weber<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1202477">22</a></sup> may be right in holding that the month is the periodic month of 27 days, for the period is otherwise too long if a year is taken. On the other hand, the period of ten months quite well suits the period of gestation, if birth takes place in the tenth month, so that in this sense the month of 30 days may well be meant. </p><p>The year of 12 months of 30 days each being admittedly quite unscientific, Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1202477">23</a></sup> is strongly of opinion that it was only used with a recognition of the fact that intercalation took place, and that the year formed part of a greater complex, normally the five your <a class="vei_120a" href="युग">yuga</a> or cycle. This system is well known from the Jyotṣa: it consists of 62 months of 29(16/31) days each = 1, 830 days (two of these months being intercalary, one in the middle and one at the end), or 61 months of 30 days, or 60 months of 30(1/2) days, the unit being clearly a solar year of 366 days. It is not an ideal system, since the year is too long;<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1202477">24</a></sup> but it is one which cannot be claimed even for the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> period, during which no decision as to the true length of the year seems to have been arrived at. The references to it seen by Zimmer in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1202477">25</a></sup> are not even reasonably plausible, while the <i>pañcaka <a class="vei_120a" href="युग">yuga</a>,</i> cited by him from the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_1202477">26</a></sup> occurs only in a quotation in a commentary, and had no authority for the text itself.</p><p>On the other hand, there was undoubtedly some attempt to bring the year of 360 days--a synodic lunar year--roughly into connexion with reality. A Sāmasūtra<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_1202477">27</a></sup> treats it as a solar year, stating that he sun perambulates each Naxatra in 13(1/3) days, while others again evidently interpolated 18 days every third year, in order to arrive at some equality. But Vedic literature, from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_28_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_1202477">28</a></sup> downwards,<sup id="ref_120_29_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29_vei_1202477">29</a></sup> teems with the assertion of the difficulty of ascertaining the month. The length is variously given as 30 days,<sup id="ref_120_30_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_30_vei_1202477">30</a></sup> 35 days,<sup id="ref_120_31_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_31_vei_1202477">31</a></sup> or  36 days.<sup id="ref_120_32_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_32_vei_1202477">32</a></sup> The last number possibly indicates an intercalation after six years (6×6 = 36, or for ritual purposes 35), but for this we have no special evidence. There are many references<sup id="ref_120_33_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_33_vei_1202477">33</a></sup> to the year having 12 or 13 months.</p><p>The names of the months are, curiously enough, not at all ancient. The sacrificial texts of the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> give them in their clearest form where the Agnicayana, ‘building of the fire-altar,’ is described.<sup id="ref_120_34_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_34_vei_1202477">34</a></sup> These names are the following: (1) <a class="vei_120a" href="मधु">madhu</a>, (2) Mādhava (spring months, <i>vāsantikāv ṛtū</i>); (3) <a class="vei_120a" href="शुक्र">śukra</a>, (4) <a class="vei_120a" href="शुचि">śuci</a> (summer months, <i>graiṣmāv ṛtū</i>); (5) Nabha (or Nabhas),<sup id="ref_120_35_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_35_vei_1202477">35</a></sup> (6) Nabhasya (rainy months, <i>vārṣikāv ṛtū</i>); (7) Iṣa, (8) Ūrja (autumn months, <i>śāradāv ṛtū</i>); (9) <a class="vei_120a" href="सह">saha</a> (or Sahas),<sup id="ref_120_35_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_35_vei_1202477">35</a></sup> (10) Sahasya (winter months, <i>haimantikāv ṛtū</i>); (11) <a class="vei_120a" href="तप">tapa</a> (or <a class="vei_120a" href="तपस्">tapas</a>),<sup id="ref_120_35_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_35_vei_1202477">35</a></sup> (12) <a class="vei_120a" href="तपस्य">tapasya</a> (cool months, <i>śaiśirāv ṛtū</i>).</p><p>There are similar lists in the descriptions of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> sacrifice<sup id="ref_120_36_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_36_vei_1202477">36</a></sup> and of the horse sacrifice,<sup id="ref_120_37_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_37_vei_1202477">37</a></sup> all of them agreeing in essentials. There are other lists of still more fanciful names,<sup id="ref_120_38_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_38_vei_1202477">38</a></sup> but these have no claim at all to represent actual divisions in popular use. It is doubtful if the list given above is more than a matter of priestly invention. Weber points out that <a class="vei_120a" href="मधु">madhu</a> and Mādhava later appear as names of spring, and that these two are mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_39_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_39_vei_1202477">39</a></sup> as if actually  employed; but the evidence is very inadequate to show that the other names of the months given in the list were in ordinary use.<sup id="ref_120_40_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_40_vei_1202477">40</a></sup></p><p>In some of these lists the intercalary month is mentioned. The name given to it in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_41_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_41_vei_1202477">41</a></sup> is <a class="vei_120a" href="अंहसस्पति">aṃhasaspati</a>, while that given in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a><sup id="ref_120_42_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_42_vei_1202477">42</a></sup> and Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_43_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_43_vei_1202477">43</a></sup> is <a class="vei_120a" href="संसर्प">saṃsarpa</a>. The <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_44_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_44_vei_1202477">44</a></sup> gives it the name of <a class="vei_120a" href="मलिम्लुच">malimluca</a>, which also occurs elsewhere, along with <a class="vei_120a" href="संसर्प">saṃsarpa</a>, in one of the lists of fanciful names.<sup id="ref_120_45_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_45_vei_1202477">45</a></sup> The Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_46_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_46_vei_1202477">46</a></sup> describes it as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सनिस्रस">sanisrasa</a>,</i> ‘slipping,’ owing no doubt to its unstable condition.</p><p>The other method of naming the months is from the <b>Nakṣatras.</b> It is only beginning to be used in the Brāhmaṇas, but is found regularly in the Epic and later. The <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्योतिष">jyotiṣa</a><sup id="ref_120_47_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_47_vei_1202477">47</a></sup> mentions that Māgha and <a class="vei_120a" href="तप">tapa</a> were identical: this is the fair interpretation of the passage, which also involves the identification of <a class="vei_120a" href="मधु">madhu</a> with <a class="vei_120a" href="चैत्र">caitra</a>, a result corresponding with the view frequently found in the Brāhmaṇas, that the full moon in <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्रा">citrā</a>, and not that in <a class="vei_120a" href="फल्गुनी">phalgunī</a>, is the beginning of the year.<sup id="ref_120_48_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_48_vei_1202477">48</a></sup></p><p>In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_49_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_49_vei_1202477">49</a></sup> are found two curious expressions, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="यव">yava</a></i> and <i>ayava,</i> for the light and dark halves of the month, which is clearly considered to begin with the light half. Possibly the words are derived, as Eggling<sup id="ref_120_50_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_50_vei_1202477">50</a></sup> thinks, from <i>yu,</i> ‘ward off,’ with reference to evil spirits. The word <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्वन्">parvan</a></b>  (‘joint’ = division of time) probably<sup id="ref_120_51_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_51_vei_1202477">51</a></sup> denotes a half of the month, perhaps already in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_52_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_52_vei_1202477">52</a></sup> More precisely the first half, the time of the waxing light, is called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पूर्व-पक्ष">pūrva-pakṣa</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_53_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_53_vei_1202477">53</a></sup> the second, that of the waning light, <i>apara-<a class="vei_120a" href="पक्ष">pakṣa</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_54_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_54_vei_1202477">54</a></sup> Either of these might be called a half-month (<i>ardha-<a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_55_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_55_vei_1202477">55</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202477">↑</a> vii. 79 and 80. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 5, 1, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 7, 5, 13, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202477">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 8, 1;  iii. 4, 9, 1; Rv. ii. 32, 6; Av. ii. 26, 2; vi. 11, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xi. 55. 56; xxxiv. 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 8; Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202477">↑</a> Av. vii. 47; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 8, 1; iii. 4, 9, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202477">↑</a> Rv. ii. 32, 8, where Sāyaṇa identifies it with <a class="vei_120a" href="कुहू">kuhū</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202477">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 8, 1; iii. 4, 9, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxix. 60; xxxiv. 8, 9; Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202477">↑</a> Rv. ii. 32, 4; v. 42, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 8, 1; iii. 4, 9, 1. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, xi. 31; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 228 <i>et seq.;</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 189.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202477">↑</a> x. 3, 11. <i>Cf.</i> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 2, 2, 23; Av. xv. 16, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202477">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 4, 8, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 9, 1. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 3, 8, 4; iv. 3, 11, 3; v. 7, 2, 2; Av. iii. 10; viii. 9, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxix. 10; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 13, 21, etc. See Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiii. 1, 2, with the commentary; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> with Sāyaṇa's notes; Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 341, 342; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 219 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202477">↑</a> xix. 23.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202477">↑</a> So Vināyaka on <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Ānartīya on <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiii. 19, 1; Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 345, 346, 353, 354. Weber accepts the scholiasts’ view that Māgha is here regarded as beginning with the day after full moon in Taiṣa; but it is simpler to suppose the meaning to be that Māgha is regarded as commencing with, not after, the new moon and ending with the day before new moon. Several passages in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (ii. 12; iii. 1; xxvi. 18; xxx. 3; see Caland, <i>Über das vituelle <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> des <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>,</i> 36, 37) and <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 1, 1, 7, point to the full moon being the middle of the month, and the new moon being regarded as either the beginning or the end. Hopkins (n. 11) thinks <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 2, 2, 18, point to the commencement of the month with the full moon. If this could be accepted, then the <a class="vei_120a" href="अष्टका">aṣṭakā</a> would fall a week before the winter solstice in Māgha.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202477">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrfit der Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 49, 229, n. 1; 50, 81. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 24, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202477">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 48, 633, n. 1; 49, 476, 477. This is the Epic rule, Hopkins, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202477">↑</a> <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 24, 87. None of the evidence is absolutely conclusive one way or the other. It is perfectly possible that the usage of families or districts differed. <i>Cf.</i> Thibaut, <i>Astronomie, Astrologie und Mathematik,</i> 12.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202477">↑</a> So Vināyaka on <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Ānartīya on <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiii. 19, 1; Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 345, 346, 353, 354. Weber accepts the scholiasts’ view that Māgha is here regarded as beginning with the day after full moon in Taiṣa; but it is simpler to suppose the meaning to be that Māgha is regarded as commencing with, not after, the new moon and ending with the day before new moon. Several passages in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (ii. 12; iii. 1; xxvi. 18; xxx. 3; see Caland, <i>Über das rituelle <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> des <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>,</i> 36, 37) and <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 1, 1, 7, point to the full moon being the middle of the month, and the new moon being regarded as either the beginning or the end. Hopkins (n. 11) thinks <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 2, 2, 18, point to the commencement of the month with the full moon. If this could be accepted, then the <a class="vei_120a" href="अष्टका">aṣṭakā</a> would fall a week before the winter solstice in Māgha.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1202477">↑</a> Rv. i. 164, 11. 14, 48; x. 189, 3; 190, 2; Av. iv. 35, 4; x. 7, 6; 8, 23; xiii. 3, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1202477">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 10, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvi. 2, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 2, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxvi. 10; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 5, 22. See also Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 288; Thibaut, <i>Astronomie, Astrologie und Mathematik,</i> 8.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1202477">↑</a> Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 8, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, v. 11. 12; Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 281-288.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1202477">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 8, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1202477">↑</a> verse 28.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1202477">↑</a> Cited in the commentary on the <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्योतिष">jyotiṣa</a>, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1202477">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 365, 366.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1202477">↑</a> Ten months is the period according to Rv. v. 78, 7-9; x. 184, 3; Av. i. 11, 6; iii. 23, 2; v. 25, 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxviii. 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 5, 2, 4. 5 (<i>ibid.,</i> ix. 5, 1, 63, a six months' embryo is alone able to live). A year is mentioned in <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 1, 9 (ten months in vi. 1, 3); <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxiii. 8; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 1, 3, 8; xi. 5, 4, 6-11; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 22.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1202477">↑</a> <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 313, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1202477">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 369, 370.</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1202477">↑</a> The <a class="vei_120a" href="युग">yuga</a> is too long by nearly four days. The true year has 365 days, 5 hours, 48 minutes, 46 seconds. <i>Cf.</i> Thibaut, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 24, 25.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1202477">↑</a> i. 164, 14; iii. 55, 18. These passages are, of course, obscure, but to interpret them as referring to the ten half years of the <a class="vei_120a" href="युग">yuga</a> is particularly gratuitous.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_1202477">↑</a> xvii. 13, 17. See also Thibaut, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 7, 8; Weber, <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 91, and references. The most that can be said is that a tendency to accept five years as a convenient period for intercalation was arising, which ultimately appears developed in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्योतिष">jyotiṣa</a>. But we cannot say that a year of 366 days is known until then.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_1202477">↑</a> Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 8, has nothing of this, but <a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, v. 12, 2, 5, is quite clear.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_1202477">↑</a> i. 25, 8; perhaps 165, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_29_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29_vei_1202477">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 3, 1, 5; vi. 2, 2, 29; xii. 2, 1, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxiv. 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 3, 2; xxiii. 2, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, v. 4, 29; Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 336, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_30_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_30_vei_1202477">↑</a> Av. xiii. 3, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_31_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_31_vei_1202477">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 5, 4, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_32_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_32_vei_1202477">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> ix. 1, 1, 43; 3, 3, 18. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 43, 167, n. 1. Shamasastry, <i>Gavām <a class="vei_120a" href="अयन">ayana</a>,</i> 122, interprets these passages in quite an impossible manner. There is no trace of a month of 35-36 days in the Epic: Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 24, 42.</li><li id="fx_120_33_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_33_vei_1202477">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 6, 7, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxi. 5; xxxiv. 9; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 10, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 2, 3, 27; iii. 6, 4, 24; v. 4, 5, 23; vii. 2, 3, 9, etc.; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 10, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_34_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_34_vei_1202477">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 4, 11, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 10; xxxv. 9; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 8, 12; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiii. 25; xiv. 6. 15. 16 27; xv. 57.</li><li id="fx_120_35_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_35_vei_1202477">↑</a> In Maitrāyaṇī, <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a>, and Vājasaneyi Saṃhitās. See notes 34, 36.</li><li id="fx_120_35_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_35_vei_1202477">↑</a> In Maitrāyaṇī, <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a>, and Vājasaneyi Saṃhitās. See notes 34, 36.</li><li id="fx_120_35_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_35_vei_1202477">↑</a> In Maitrāyaṇī, <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a>, and Vājasaneyi Saṃhitās. See notes 34, 36.</li><li id="fx_120_36_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_36_vei_1202477">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 4, 14, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 3, 16; iv. 6, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 7; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, vii. 30 (where Iṣ and Ūrj appear as the names of the months).</li><li id="fx_120_37_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_37_vei_1202477">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 12, 13; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxii. 31.</li><li id="fx_120_38_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_38_vei_1202477">↑</a> See, <i>e.g.,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 7, 9, 1; iv. 7, 11, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā ix. 20; xviii. 28; xxii. 32; Kāthaka Saṃhitā, xxxv. 10, Weber, 2, 349, 350.</li><li id="fx_120_39_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_39_vei_1202477">↑</a> iv. 7, 2; v. 6, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_40_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_40_vei_1202477">↑</a> Cases like that of <i>nabhas,</i> used by Mallinātha on Meghadūta, i. 4, are merely scholastic.</li><li id="fx_120_41_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_41_vei_1202477">↑</a> vii. 30; xxii. 31.</li><li id="fx_120_42_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_42_vei_1202477">↑</a> i. 4, 14, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_43_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_43_vei_1202477">↑</a> iii. 12, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_44_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_44_vei_1202477">↑</a> xxxviii. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_45_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_45_vei_1202477">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> xxxv. 10; Vājasaneyi Saṃitā, xxii. 30.</li><li id="fx_120_46_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_46_vei_1202477">↑</a> v. 6, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_47_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_47_vei_1202477">↑</a> Verse 6 <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुस्">yajus</a> recension = verse 5 Ṛc recension: Weber, 2, 354 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_48_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_48_vei_1202477">↑</a> Weber's theory (359) that <a class="vei_120a" href="चैत्र">caitra</a> was as the first spring month secondary to Phālguna is, of course, an error; for, owing to the precession of the equinoxes, Phālguna became the first month of spring <i>de facto,</i> while <a class="vei_120a" href="चैत्र">caitra</a> became virtually the last month of the preceding season. The truth is that the six seasons are an arbitrary division of the year, and that either Phālguna or <a class="vei_120a" href="चैत्र">caitra</a> could be regarded as the beginning of spring without much impropriety. See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 457; 10, 231, 232; Whitney, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 8, 71, 397, 398.</li><li id="fx_120_49_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_49_vei_1202477">↑</a> viii. 4, 2, 12; 3, 18. See Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiv. 26. 31. The <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 10, 3, has the words in the form of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="याव">yāva</a></i> and <i>ayāva,</i> which are explained in v. 3, 4, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_50_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_50_vei_1202477">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 43, 69, n.</li><li id="fx_120_51_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_51_vei_1202477">↑</a> The months and the half months are the <i>parvāṇi</i> of the sacrificial horse in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 1, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 3, 35; vi. 2, 2, 24; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiii. 43; St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 4, where the sense is left vague.</li><li id="fx_120_52_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_52_vei_1202477">↑</a> i. 94, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 189.</li><li id="fx_120_53_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_53_vei_1202477">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 9, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 25, 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 7, 4, 7; viii. 4, 2, 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 11,; xi. 5. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_54_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_54_vei_1202477">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 7, 4, 7; viii. 4, 2, 11; xi. 1, 5, 3; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 1, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 11; xi. 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_55_vei_1202477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_55_vei_1202477">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 5, 21; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 1, 1; iii. 8, 9 etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 1, 15, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 12, 7; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxii. 28.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 364 <i>et seq.;</i> Thibaut, <i>Astronomie, Astrologie und Mathematik,</i> 7-9; Weber, Proceedings of the Berlin Academy, 1894, 37 <i>et seq.; Naxatra,</i> 2, <i>passim.</i></li></ol></div>

मासर|మాసర|ಮಾಸರ|māsara|maasara|_vei_120@2478|_vei2_pn163
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2477">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2479">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn156">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn163">2:163</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn164">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māsara</b> is mentioned as a beverage in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202478"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202478">1</a></sup> Its composition is described fully in the Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202478"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202478">2</a></sup> It seems to have been a mixture of rice and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्यामाक">śyāmāka</a></b> with grass, parched barley, etc.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202478"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202478">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 11, 2. 9; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 14. 82; xx. 68; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 11, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202478"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202478">↑</a> xix. 1, 20. 21; Mahīdhara on Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 1, 14.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Griffith, Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, 172, n.</li></ol></div>

माहकि|మాహకి|ಮಾಹಕಿ|māhaki|maahaki|_vei_120@2479|_vei2_pn163
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2478">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2480">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn156">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn163">2:163</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn164">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māhaki,</b> ‘descendant of Mahaka,’ is the patronymic of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202479"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202479">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202479"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202479">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 382.</li></ol></div>

माहा-चमस्य|माहाचमस्य|माहा|चमस्य|మాహా-చమస్య|మాహాచమస్య|మాహా|చమస్య|ಮಾಹಾ-ಚಮಸ್ಯ|ಮಾಹಾಚಮಸ್ಯ|ಮಾಹಾ|ಚಮಸ್ಯ|māhā-camasya|māhācamasya|māhā|camasya|maahaa-chamasya|maahaachamasya|maahaa|chamasya|_vei_120@2480|_vei2_pn163
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2479">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2481">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn156">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn163">2:163</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn164">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māhā-camasya,</b> ‘descendant of <b>Mahācamasa,</b>’ is the patronymic of a teacher to whom the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202480"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202480">1</a></sup> ascribes the addition of Mahas to the triad Bhūr <b>Bhuvas</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="स्वर्">svar</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202480"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202480">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202480"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202480">↑</a> i. 5, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202480"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202480">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 180.</li></ol></div>

माहा-रजन|माहारजन|माहा|रजन|మాహా-రజన|మాహారజన|మాహా|రజన|ಮಾಹಾ-ರಜನ|ಮಾಹಾರಜನ|ಮಾಹಾ|ರಜನ|māhā-rajana|māhārajana|māhā|rajana|maahaa-rajana|maahaarajana|maahaa|_vei_120@2481|_vei2_pn163
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2480">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2482">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn156">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn163">2:163</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn164">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māhā-rajana,</b> ‘dyed with saffron’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="महा">mahā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="रजन">rajana</a></i>), is applied to a garment (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="वासस्">vāsas</a></b>) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (ii. 3, 10). </p></div>

माहा-राज्य|माहाराज्य|माहा|राज्य|మాహా-రాజ్య|మాహారాజ్య|మాహా|రాజ్య|ಮಾಹಾ-ರಾಜ್ಯ|ಮಾಹಾರಾಜ್ಯ|ಮಾಹಾ|ರಾಜ್ಯ|māhā-rājya|māhārājya|māhā|rājya|maahaa-raajya|maahaaraajya|maahaa|raajya|_vei_120@2482|_vei2_pn164
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2481">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2483">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn163">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn164">2:164</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn165">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māhā-rājya,</b> ‘the dignity of a great king’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="महा-राज">mahā-rāja</a></i>), is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 6, 5; 12, 4; 15, 3).</p></div>

माहित्थि|మాహిత్థి|ಮಾಹಿತ್ಥಿ|māhitthi|maahitthi|_vei_120@2483|_vei2_pn164
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2482">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2484">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn163">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn164">2:164</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn165">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māhitthi,</b> ‘descendant of Mahittha,’ is the patronymic of a teacher mentioned several times in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202483"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202483">1</a></sup> He is said to be a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वामकक्षायण">vāmakakṣāyaṇa</a></b> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202483"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202483">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202483"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202483">↑</a> vi. 2, 2, 10; viii. 6, 1, 16 <i>et seq.;</i> ix. 5, 1, 57; x. 6, 5, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202483"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202483">↑</a> vi. 5, 4 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</li></ol></div>

माहीन|మాహీన|ಮಾಹೀನ|māhīna|maahiina|maaheena|_vei_120@2484|_vei2_pn164
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2483">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2485">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn163">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn164">2:164</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn165">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māhīna</b> occurs in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202484"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202484">1</a></sup> which celebrates <b>Asamāti</b> as a king. The word, used in the plural, may be a patronymic referring to the priests who praised Asamāti, or it may be an adjective of uncertain meaning.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202484"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202484">↑</a> x. 60, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 138.</li></ol></div>

मित्र|మిత్ర|ಮಿತ್ರ|mitra|_vei_120@2485|_vei2_pn164
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2484">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2486">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn163">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn164">2:164</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn165">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mitra</b> denotes ‘friend’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202485">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202485">2</a></sup> According to the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202485">3</a></sup> a wife is a man's friend, and in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202485">4</a></sup> the value of a friend is insisted upon. Treachery to a friend is reprobated.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202485">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202485">↑</a> Masculine: i. 58, 1; 67, 1; 75, 4; 156, 1; 170, 5; ii. 4, 1. 3, etc. The neuter does not with certainty occur in the sense of ‘friend’ in the Rv.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202485">↑</a> Masculine: Av. v. 19, 15; xi. 9, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxvii. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, x. 80. Neuter: <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 4, 8, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 8, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 20, 17; viii. 27, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 4, 8; v. 3, 5, 13; xi. 4, 3, 20, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202485">↑</a> vi. 2, 9, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202485">↑</a> i. 5, 3, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202485"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202485">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 1, 7.</li></ol></div>

मित्र-भू काश्यप|मित्रभू काश्यप|मित्र|भू काश्यप|మిత్ర-భూ కాశ్యప|మిత్రభూ కాశ్యప|మిత్ర|భూ కాశ్యప|ಮಿತ್ರ-ಭೂ ಕಾಶ್ಯಪ|ಮಿತ್ರಭೂ ಕಾಶ್ಯಪ|ಮಿತ್ರ|ಭೂ ಕಾಶ್ಯಪ|mitra-bhū kāśyapa|mitrabhū kāśyapa|mitra|bhū kāśyapa|mitra-bhuu kaashyapa|mitrabhuu kaashyapa|bhuu kaashyapa|mitra-bhoo kaashyapa|mitrabhoo kaashyapa|bhoo kaashyapa|_vei_120@2486|_vei2_pn164
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2485">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2487">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn163">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn164">2:164</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn165">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mitra-<a class="vei_120a" href="भू">bhū</a> Kāśyapa</b> (‘descendant of Kaśyapa’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विभण्डक काश्यप">vibhaṇḍaka kāśyapa</a>,</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202486"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202486">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202486"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202486">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 374.</li></ol></div>

मित्र-भूति लौहित्य|मित्रभूति लौहित्य|मित्र|भूति लौहित्य|మిత్ర-భూతి లౌహిత్య|మిత్రభూతి లౌహిత్య|మిత్ర|భూతి లౌహిత్య|ಮಿತ್ರ-ಭೂತಿ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|ಮಿತ್ರಭೂತಿ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|ಮಿತ್ರ|ಭೂತಿ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|mitra-bhūti lauhitya|mitrabhūti lauhitya|mitra|bhūti lauhitya|mitra-bhuuti lauhitya|mitrabhuuti lauhitya|bhuuti lauhitya|mitra-bhooti lauhitya|mitrabhooti lauhitya|bhooti lauhitya|_vei_120@2487|_vei2_pn164
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2486">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2488">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn163">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn164">2:164</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn165">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mitra-<a class="vei_120a" href="भूति">bhūti</a> Lauhitya</b> (‘descendant of Lohita’) is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a></b> (iii. 42, 1) as a pupil of <b>Kṛṣṇadatta <a class="vei_120a" href="लौहित्य">lauhitya</a>.</b> </p></div>

मित्र-वर्चस् स्थैरकायण|मित्रवर्चस् स्थैरकायण|मित्र|वर्चस् स्थैरकायण|మిత్ర-వర్చస్ స్థైరకాయణ|మిత్రవర్చస్ స్థైరకాయణ|మిత్ర|వర్చస్ స్థైరకాయణ|ಮಿತ್ರ-ವರ್ಚಸ್ ಸ್ಥೈರಕಾಯಣ|ಮಿತ್ರವರ್ಚಸ್ ಸ್ಥೈರಕಾಯಣ|ಮಿತ್ರ|ವರ್ಚಸ್ ಸ್ಥೈರಕಾಯಣ|mitra-varcas sthairakāyaṇa|mitravarcas sthairakāyaṇa|mitra|varcas sthairakāyaṇa|mitra-varchas sthairakaayaNa|mitravarchas sthairakaayaNa|varchas sthairakaayaNa|_vei_120@2488|_vei2_pn165
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2487">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2489">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn164">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn165">2:165</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn166">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mitra-varcas Sthairakāyaṇa</b> (‘descendant of Sthiraka’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुप्रतीत औलुण्ड्य">supratīta auluṇḍya</a>,</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202488"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202488">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202488"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202488">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372.</li></ol></div>

मित्र-विन्द काश्यप|मित्रविन्द काश्यप|मित्र|विन्द काश्यप|మిత్ర-విన్ద కాశ్యప|మిత్రవిన్ద కాశ్యప|మిత్ర|విన్ద కాశ్యప|ಮಿತ್ರ-ವಿನ್ದ ಕಾಶ್ಯಪ|ಮಿತ್ರವಿನ್ದ ಕಾಶ್ಯಪ|ಮಿತ್ರ|ವಿನ್ದ ಕಾಶ್ಯಪ|mitra-vinda kāśyapa|mitravinda kāśyapa|mitra|vinda kāśyapa|mitra-vinda kaashyapa|mitravinda kaashyapa|vinda kaashyapa|_vei_120@2489|_vei2_pn165
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2488">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2490">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn164">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn165">2:165</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn166">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mitra-<a class="vei_120a" href="विन्द">vinda</a> Kāśyapa</b> (‘descendant of Kaśyapa’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b>Sunītha,</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202489"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202489">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202489"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202489">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372.</li></ol></div>

मित्रातिथि|మిత్రాతిథి|ಮಿತ್ರಾತಿಥಿ|mitrātithi|mitraatithi|_vei_120@2490|_vei2_pn165
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2489">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2491">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn164">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn165">2:165</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn166">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mitrātithi</b> is mentioned in one hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202490">1</a></sup> as the father of <b>Kuruśravaṇa</b> and the grandfather of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपमश्रवस्">upamaśravas</a>,</b> all being evidently kings.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202490">↑</a> x. 33, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 165; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 150, 184; Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1910, 922, 923; Lanman, <i>Sanskrit Reader,</i> 384; Bṛhaddevatā, vii. 35, 36, with Macdonell's notes.</li></ol></div>

मुक्षीजा|ముక్షీజా|ಮುಕ್ಷೀಜಾ|mukṣījā|mukShiijaa|mukSheejaa|muxiijaa|_vei_120@2491|_vei2_pn165
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2490">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2492">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn164">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn165">2:165</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn166">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mukṣījā</b> is found in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202491"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202491">1</a></sup> where the sense seems clearly to be ‘net’ for catching animals. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पदि">padi</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202491"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202491">↑</a> i. 125, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 19. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 244.</li></ol></div>

मुञ्ज|मुञ्ज|ముఞ్జ_౧|ముఞ్జ|ಮುಞ್ಜ_೧|ಮುಞ್ಜ|muñja_1|muñja|mu~nja_1|mu~nja|_vei_120@2492|_vei2_pn165
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2491">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2493">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn164">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn165">2:165</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn166">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Muñja</b> denotes a grass, the <i>Saccharum <a class="vei_120a" href="मुञ्ज">muñja</a>,</i> which is of luxuriant growth, attaining to a height of ten feet. It is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202492">1</a></sup> along with other kinds of grasses as the lurking-place of venomous creatures. In the same text<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202492">2</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="मुञ्ज">muñja</a> grass is spoken of as purifying, apparently being used as the material of a filter for <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>. The grass is often mentioned in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202492">3</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202492">4</a></sup> It is in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202492">5</a></sup> said to be ‘hollow’ (<i>suṣira</i>) and to be used for the plaited part of the throne (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="आसन्दी">āsandī</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202492">6</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202492">↑</a> i. 191, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202492">↑</a> i. 161, 8 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="मुञ्ज">muñja</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="नेजन">nejana</a>,</i> which Sāyaṇa explains as <i>apagata-<a class="vei_120a" href="तृण">tṛṇa</a>,</i> ‘with the grass removed’).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202492">↑</a> Av. i. 2, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 1, 9, 5; 10, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202492">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xviii. 7; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 3, 3, 16; vi. 6, 1, 23; 2, 15. 16, etc. <i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. mauñja.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202492">↑</a> vi. 3, 1, 26.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202492">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 8, 3, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 72.</li></ol></div>

मुञ्ज साम-श्रवस|मुञ्ज साम-श्रवस|मुञ्ज सामश्रवस|मुञ्ज साम|श्रवस|ముఞ్జ సామ-శ్రవస_౨|ముఞ్జ సామ-శ్రవస|ముఞ్జ సామశ్రవస|ముఞ్జ సామ|శ్రవస|ಮುಞ್ಜ ಸಾಮ-ಶ್ರವಸ_೨|ಮುಞ್ಜ ಸಾಮ-ಶ್ರವಸ|ಮುಞ್ಜ ಸಾಮಶ್ರವಸ|ಮುಞ್ಜ ಸಾಮ|ಶ್ರವಸ|muñja sāma-śravasa_2|muñja sāma-śravasa|muñja sāmaśravasa|muñja sāma|śravasa|mu~nja saama-shravasa_2|mu~nja saama-shravasa|mu~nja saamashravasa|mu~nja saama|shravasa|_vei_120@2493|_vei2_pn166
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2492">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2494">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn165">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn166">2:166</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn167">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Muñja <a class="vei_120a" href="साम">sāma</a>-śravasa</b> (‘descendant of Sāmaśravas’) is the name of a man, possibly a king, mentioned in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202493"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202493">1</a></sup> and the Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202493"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202493">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202493"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202493">↑</a> iii. 5, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202493"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202493">↑</a> iv. 1 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 39).</li></ol></div>

मुण्डिभ औदन्य|ముణ్డిభ ఔదన్య|ಮುಣ್ಡಿಭ ಔದನ್ಯ|muṇḍibha audanya|muNDibha audanya|_vei_120@2494|_vei2_pn166
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2493">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2495">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn165">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn166">2:166</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn167">↷</a><hr><p><b>Muṇḍibha Audanya</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202494"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202494">1</a></sup> or <b>Audanyava</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202494"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202494">2</a></sup> is the name of a man in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1202494"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1202494">1b</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2b_vei_1202494"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2b_vei_1202494">2b</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202494"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202494">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 3, 5, 4. Apparently the word is a patronymic, ‘son of Udanya’ (so Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 341, n. 1), or ‘son of Odana’ (so St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202494"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202494">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 9, 15, 3 (‘descendant of Udanyu’).</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1202494"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1202494">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 3, 5, 4. Apparently the word is a patronymic, ‘son of Udanya’ (so Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 341, n. 1), or ‘son of Odana’ (so St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_2b_vei_1202494"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2b_vei_1202494">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 9, 15, 3 (‘descendant of Udanyu’).</li></ol></div>

मुद्ग|ముద్గ|ಮುದ್ಗ|mudga|_vei_120@2495|_vei2_pn166
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2494">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2496">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn165">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn166">2:166</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn167">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mudga,</b> denoting a kind of bean (<i>Phascolus Mungo</i>), occurs in a list of vegetables in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202495">1</a></sup> A ‘soup of rice with beans’ (<i>mudgaudana</i>) is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202495">2</a></sup> and the Sūtras. <i>Cf.</i> perhaps <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मुद्गल">mudgala</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202495">↑</a> xviii. 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202495"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202495">↑</a> xii. 8.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 240.</li></ol></div>

मुद्गल|ముద్గల|ಮುದ್ಗಲ|mudgala|_vei_120@2496|_vei2_pn166
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2495">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2497">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn165">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn166">2:166</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn167">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mudgala</b> and <b>Mudgalanī,</b> ‘Mudgala's wife,’ both figure in a hopelessly obscure hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202496">1</a></sup> variously interpreted by Pischel<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202496">2</a></sup> and Geldner<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202496">3</a></sup> and von Bradke<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202496">4</a></sup> as telling of a real chariot race in which, despite difficulties, <a class="vei_120a" href="मुद्गल">mudgala</a> won by his wife's aid. The Indian tradition is as variant as the interpretations of modern authorities. Ṣaḍguruśiṣya<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202496">5</a></sup> explains that Mudgala's oxen were stolen, that he pursued the thieves with the one old ox he had left, and that hurling his hammer (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="द्रु-घण">dru-ghaṇa</a></i>) he caught the marauders. <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202496">6</a></sup> on the other hand, says that <a class="vei_120a" href="मुद्गल">mudgala</a> won a race with a <i><a class="vei_120a" href="द्रुघण">drughaṇa</a></i> and an ox instead of with two oxen. It is pretty clear that, as Roth<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202496">7</a></sup> observed, the tradition is merely a guess, and a bad one, at the meaning of an obscure hymn, and this view is  accepted by Oldenberg.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202496">8</a></sup> Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202496">9</a></sup> has interpreted the legend as one of heavenly, not of human, events. <a class="vei_120a" href="मुद्गल">mudgala</a>, probably a variant form of Mudgara,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202496">10</a></sup> which in the later language means a hammer or a similar weapon, may be meant as a personfication of the thunderbolt of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, rather than a real man.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202496">11</a></sup> Later<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202496">12</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="मुद्गल">mudgala</a> is a mythical sage.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202496">↑</a> x. 102.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202496">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 124.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202496">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 1, 138; 2, 1-22.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202496">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 46, 445 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202496">↑</a> Macdonell's edition of the Sarvānukramaṇī, p. 158.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202496">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ix. 23, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202496">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, Erlänterungen,</i> 129.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202496">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 39, 78.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202496">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 48, 547.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202496">↑</a> According to Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 1, Indrasenā in x. 102, 2, is the name of Mudgalānī; but its sense, ‘Indra's bolt,’ rather indicates the mythical character of the passage.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202496">↑</a> If the name means a real man, it may be connected with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मुद्ग">mudga</a>,</b> ‘bean.’ See Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 240.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202496">↑</a> Av. iv. 29, 6; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 12; Bṛhaddevatā, vi. 46; viii. 12. 90.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 166, 167; Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 280; Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society</i> 1911, 1005, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

मुनि|ముని|ಮುನಿ|muni|_vei_120@2497|_vei2_pn167
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2496">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2498">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn166">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn167">2:167</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn168">↷</a><hr><p><b>Muni</b> occurs in one hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202497">1</a></sup> where it seems to denote an ascetic of magic powers with divine afflatus (<i>deveṣita</i>), the precursor of the strange ascetics of later India. This agrees with the fact that <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतश">aitaśa</a>,</b> the <a class="vei_120a" href="मुनि">muni</a>, is in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202497">2</a></sup> regarded by his son as deranged, a view not unjustified if the nonsense which passes as the Aitaśapralāpa.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202497">3</a></sup> ‘Chatter of <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतश">aitaśa</a>,’ was really his. The Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202497">4</a></sup> calls <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> the ‘friend of Munis,’ and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202497">5</a></sup> refers to a ‘divine Muni’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="देव">deva</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मुनि">muni</a></i>), by whom a similar ascetic may be meant.</p><p>In the Upaniṣads<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202497">6</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="मुनि">muni</a> is of a more restrained type: he is one who learns the nature of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>, the Absolute, by study, or sacrifice, or penance, or fasting, or faith (<i>śraddhā</i>). It must not of course be thought that there is any absolute distinction between the older <a class="vei_120a" href="मुनि">muni</a> and the later: in both cases the man is in a peculiar ecstatic condition, but the ideal of the Upaniṣads is less material than the earlier picture of the <a class="vei_120a" href="मुनि">muni</a>, who is more of a ‘medicine man’ than a sage. Nor would it be wise to conclude from the comparative rareness  of the mention of the <a class="vei_120a" href="मुनि">muni</a> in the Vedic texts that he was an infrequent figure in Vedic times: he was probably not approved by the priests who followed the ritual, and whose views were essentially different from the ideals of a <a class="vei_120a" href="मुनि">muni</a>, which were superior to earthly considerations, such as the desire for children and <b>Dakṣiṇās.</b><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202497">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202497">↑</a> x. 136, 2. 4. 5. In verse I he is described as ‘long-haired.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202497">↑</a> vi. 33, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202497">↑</a> See Bloomfield, <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 98 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202497">↑</a> viii. 17, 14. <i>Cf.</i> vii. 56, 8; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 376.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202497">↑</a> vii. 74, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 440; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 5, 2, 15, and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मुनिमरण">munimaraṇa</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202497">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 4, 1; iv. 4, 25; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 20.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202497">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> {|Bṛhadāranyaka|} <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 4, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 406; <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 49, 480; <i>Buddha,</i>^5 36.</li></ol></div>

मुनि-मरण|मुनिमरण|मुनि|मरण|ముని-మరణ|మునిమరణ|ముని|మరణ|ಮುನಿ-ಮರಣ|ಮುನಿಮರಣ|ಮುನಿ|ಮರಣ|muni-maraṇa|munimaraṇa|muni|maraṇa|muni-maraNa|munimaraNa|maraNa|_vei_120@2498|_vei2_pn168
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2497">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2499">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn167">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn168">2:168</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn169">↷</a><hr><p><b>Muni-maraṇa,</b> ‘Saints’ Death,’ is the name of the place where, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xiv. 4, 7), the <b>Vaikhanasas</b> were slain.</p></div>

मुलालिन्|ములాలిన్|ಮುಲಾಲಿನ್|mulālin|mulaalin|_vei_120@2499|_vei2_pn168
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2498">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2500">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn167">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn168">2:168</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn169">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mulālin</b> (masc.) or <b>Mulālī</b> (fem.) is the name of some part of an edible lotus (perhaps the <i>Nymphaea esculenta</i>) in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202499">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202499">↑</a> iv. 34. 5. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, lxvi. 10; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 138; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 70; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 207.</li></ol></div>

मुषीवन्|ముషీవన్|ಮುಷೀವನ್|muṣīvan|muShiivan|muSheevan|_vei_120@2500|_vei2_pn168
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2499">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2501">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn167">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn168">2:168</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn169">↷</a><hr><p><b>Muṣīvan</b> denotes ‘robber’ in one passage of the Rigveda (i. 42, 3).</p></div>

मुष्कर|ముష్కర|ಮುಷ್ಕರ|muṣkara|muShkara|_vei_120@2501|_vei2_pn168
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2500">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2502">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn167">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn168">2:168</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn169">↷</a><hr><p><b>Muṣkara</b> occurs in one passage of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202501">1</a></sup> possibly in the sense of a small animal or insect, as suggested by Roth,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202501">2</a></sup> who, however, thought the passage corrupt. Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202501">3</a></sup> suggests that the reading of the Paippalāda text <i>puṣkaram,</i> (‘blue lotus’) is the correct form.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202501">↑</a> vi. 14, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202501">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202501">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 463, 464.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 297.</li></ol></div>

मुष्टि-हन्|मुष्टिहन्|मुष्टि|हन्|ముష్టి-హన్|ముష్టిహన్|ముష్టి|హన్|ಮುಷ್ಟಿ-ಹನ್|ಮುಷ್ಟಿಹನ್|ಮುಷ್ಟಿ|ಹನ್|muṣṭi-han|muṣṭihan|muṣṭi|han|muShTi-han|muShTihan|muShTi|_vei_120@2502|_vei2_pn168
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2501">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2503">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn167">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn168">2:168</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn169">↷</a><hr><p><b>Muṣṭi-han,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202502"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202502">1</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मुष्टि">muṣṭi</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="हत्या">hatyā</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202502"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202502">2</a></sup> in the Rigveda and the Atharvaveda denote. respectively, the ‘hand to hand fighter’--that is, the ordinary warrior as opposed to the charioteer, and the  ‘fight’ itself. So also in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202502"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202502">3</a></sup> the charioteer (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="रथिन्">rathin</a></i>) is opposed to the foot-soldier (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पत्ति">patti</a></i>), and in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202502"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202502">4</a></sup> the chariots are opposed to the troops (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a></i>) of the infantry. The parallel of the Greek and other Āryan races shows that the Kṣatriyas were the fighters from chariots, while the ordinary host fought on foot.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202502"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202502">↑</a> Rv. v. 58, 4; vi. 26, 2; viii. 20, 20; Av. v. 22, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202502"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202502">↑</a> Rv. i. 8, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202502"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202502">↑</a> vii. 62, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202502"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202502">↑</a> i. 100, 10.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 297.</li></ol></div>

मुसल|ముసల|ಮುಸಲ|musala|_vei_120@2503|_vei2_pn169
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2502">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2504">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn168">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn169">2:169</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn170">↷</a><hr><p><b>Musala</b> denotes a ‘pestle’ in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202503">1</a></sup> and in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202503">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202503">↑</a> Av. x. 9, 26; xi. 3, 3; xii. 3, 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 8, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202503">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xii. 8; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 5, 2, 7; in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 42, 44 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 235, 237), <i>musalin</i> means a ‘man armed with a club.’</li></ol></div>

मुहूर्त|ముహూర్త|ಮುಹೂರ್ತ|muhūrta|muhuurta|muhoorta|_vei_120@2504|_vei2_pn169
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2503">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2505">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn168">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn169">2:169</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn170">↷</a><hr><p><b>Muhūrta</b> denotes a division of time, one-thirtieth of a day, or an hour of forty-eight minutes, in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202504"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202504">1</a></sup> In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202504"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202504">2</a></sup> the sense of ‘moment’ only is found. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अहन्">ahan</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202504"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202504">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 10, 1, 1 (for the names); 9, 7; 12, 9, 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 4, 2, 18. 25. 27; 3, 20; xii. 3, 2, 5; x. 4, 4, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202504"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202504">↑</a> iii. 33, 5; 53, 8. The sense of ‘moment’ is also common in the Brāhmaṇas.</p><p><i>Cf. Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 9, 139 <i>et seq.; Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 92 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

मूचीप|मूवीप|మూచీప|మూవీప|ಮೂಚೀಪ|ಮೂವೀಪ|mūcīpa|mūvīpa|muuchiipa|muuviipa|moocheepa|mooveepa|_vei_120@2505|_vei2_pn169
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2504">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2506">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn168">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn169">2:169</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn170">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mūcīpa</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मूवीप">mūvīpa</a></b> is the variant in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (xv. 26, 6) of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मूतिब">mūtiba</a></b> of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> as the name of a barbarian tribe.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 67, n. 1.</p></div>

मूजवन्त्|మూజవన్త్|ಮೂಜವನ್ತ್|mūjavant|muujavant|moojavant|_vei_120@2506|_vei2_pn169
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2505">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2507">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn168">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn169">2:169</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn170">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mūjavant</b> is the name of a people who, along with the <b>Mahāvṛṣas,</b> the <b>Gandhāris,</b> and the <b>Balhikas,</b> are mentioned in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202506">1</a></sup> as dwelling far away, and to whom fever is to be banished. Similarly in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202506">2</a></sup> the Mūjavants are chosen as a type of distant folk, beyond  which <a class="vei_120a" href="रुद्र">rudra</a> with his bow is entreated to depart. In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202506">3</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> is described as <a class="vei_120a" href="मौजवत">maujavata</a>, ‘coming from the Mūjavants,’ or, as <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202506">4</a></sup> takes it, ‘from Mount <a class="vei_120a" href="मूजवन्त्">mūjavant</a>.’ The Indian commentators<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202506">5</a></sup> agree with <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a> in taking <a class="vei_120a" href="मूजवन्त्">mūjavant</a> as the name of a mountain, and though Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202506">6</a></sup> is justified in saying that the identification of <a class="vei_120a" href="मूजवन्त्">mūjavant</a> by Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202506">7</a></sup> with one of the lower hills on the south-west of Kaśmīr lacks evidence, it is not reasonable to deny that <a class="vei_120a" href="मूजवन्त्">mūjavant</a> was a hill from which the people took their name. <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202506">8</a></sup> suggests that <a class="vei_120a" href="मूजवन्त्">mūjavant</a> is equivalent to Muñjavant, which actually occurs later, in the Epic,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202506">9</a></sup> as the name of a mountain in the Himālaya.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202506">↑</a> v. 22, 5, 7, 8. 14. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202506">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 6, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, ix. 7; xxxvi. 14; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 4, 10. 20; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iii. 61; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 2, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202506">↑</a> x. 34, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202506">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ix. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202506">↑</a> Mahīdhara on Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Sāyaṇa on Rv. i. 161, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रयोग">prayoga</a>, cited by Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 63.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202506">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 65.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202506">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 29.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202506">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>. Cf.</i> Siddhānta Kaumudī on Pāṇini, iv. 4, 110, where instead of <a class="vei_120a" href="मौजवत">maujavata</a> in Rv. x. 34, 1, Mauñjavata is read.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202506">↑</a> Mahābhārata, x. 785; xiv. 180.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 198.</li></ol></div>

मूत|మూత|ಮೂತ|mūta|muuta|moota|_vei_120@2507|_vei2_pn170
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2506">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2508">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn169">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn170">2:170</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn171">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mūta</b> in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202507"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202507">1</a></sup> denotes a ‘woven basket.’ Mūtaka means a ‘small basket.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202507"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202507">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202507"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202507">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvi. 14; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 10, 5; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 3, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202507"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202507">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 2, 17.</li></ol></div>

मूतिब|మూతిబ|ಮೂತಿಬ|mūtiba|muutiba|mootiba|_vei_120@2508|_vei2_pn170
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2507">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2509">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn169">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn170">2:170</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn171">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mūtiba</b> appears in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202508"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202508">1</a></sup> as the name of one of the barbarous peoples enumerated as nominally <b>Viśvāmitra's</b> outcast offspring. The <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202508"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202508">2</a></sup> gives the name as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मूचीप">mūcīpa</a></b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मूवीप">mūvīpa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202508"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202508">↑</a> vii. 18, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202508"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202508">↑</a> xv. 26, 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 358, 483.</li></ol></div>

मूल|मूलबर्हण|మూల|మూలబర్హణ|ಮೂಲ|ಮೂಲಬರ್ಹಣ|mūla|mūlabarhaṇa|muula|muulabarhaNa|moola|moolabarhaNa|_vei_120@2509|_vei2_pn170
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2508">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2510">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn169">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn170">2:170</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn171">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mūla, Mūlabarhaṇa.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p></div>

मूस्|मू|మూస్|మూ|ಮೂಸ್|ಮೂ|mūs|mū|muus|muu|moos|moo|_vei_120@2510|_vei2_pn170
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2509">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2511">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn169">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn170">2:170</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn171">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mūs,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202510">1</a></sup> <b>Mūṣikā,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202510">2</a></sup> are the names of ‘mouse’ occurring in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1202510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1202510">1b</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_2b_vei_1202510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2b_vei_1202510">2b</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202510">↑</a> Rv. i. 105, 8 = x. 33, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iv. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202510">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 17; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 36.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 85; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 248.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1202510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1202510">↑</a> Rv. i. 105, 8 = x. 33, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iv. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2b_vei_1202510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2b_vei_1202510">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 17; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 36.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 85; Schrader, <i>Prchistoric Antiquities,</i> 248.</li></ol></div>

मृग|मृग|మృగ_౧|మృగ|ಮೃಗ_೧|ಮೃಗ|mṛga_1|mṛga|mRga_1|mRga|mRiga_1|mRiga|mRuga_1|mRuga|mRRiga_1|mRRiga|_vei_120@2511|_vei2_pn171
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2510">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2512">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn170">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn171">2:171</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn172">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Mṛga</b> has the generic sense of ‘wild beast’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202511">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202511">2</a></sup> Sometimes it is qualified by the epithet ‘terrible’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="भीम">bhīma</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202511">3</a></sup> which indicates that a savage wild beast is meant. Elsewhere the buffalo is shown to be denoted by the epithet <i><a class="vei_120a" href="महिष">mahiṣa</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202511">4</a></sup> ‘powerful,’ which later becomes the name of the buffalo. More particularly the word has the sense of an animal of the gazelle type.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202511">5</a></sup> In some passages<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202511">6</a></sup> Roth<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202511">7</a></sup> sees the sense of ‘bird.’ See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मृग हस्तिन्">mṛga hastin</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुष हस्तिन्">puruṣa hastin</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202511">↑</a> i. 173, 2; 191, 4; viii. 1, 20; 5, 36; x. 146, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202511">↑</a> Av. iv. 3, 6; x. 1, 26; xii. 1, 48 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सूकर">sūkara</a>,</i> ‘boar’); xix. 38, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 7, 10; xxiv. 11, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 31, 2; viii. 23, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202511">↑</a> Rv. i. 154, 2; 190, 3; ii. 33, 11; 34, 1; x. 180, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202511">↑</a> Rv. viii. 69, 15; ix. 92, 6; x. 123, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202511">↑</a> Rv. i. 38, 5; 105, 7; vi. 75, 11; ix. 32, 4; Av. v. 21, 4 (not a certain instance); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 3, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 5 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 8, 4, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202511">↑</a> Rv. i. 182, 7; x. 136, 6, and perhaps i. 145, 5; vii. 87, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202511">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> 1<i>e.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> i. 99; 2, 122.</li></ol></div>

मृग|मृग|మృగ_౨|మృగ|ಮೃಗ_೨|ಮೃಗ|mṛga_2|mṛga|mRga_2|mRga|mRiga_2|mRiga|mRuga_2|mRuga|mRRiga_2|mRRiga|_vei_120@2512|_vei2_pn171
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2511">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2513">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn170">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn171">2:171</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn172">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Mṛga</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202512"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202512">1</a></sup> denotes, according to Sāyaṇa's commentary, the constellation <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मृगशिरस्">mṛgaśiras</a>.</b> But it seems more probable<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202512"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202512">2</a></sup> that <a class="vei_120a" href="मृग">mṛga</a> there really covers the whole of Orion, not merely the inconspicuous group of stars in the head of Orion that make up the <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मृगशिरस्">mṛgaśiras</a>, but also the star <i>a</i> in his shoulder, which is reckoned as <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्द्रा">ārdrā</a>, and  in his left shoulder. Tilak,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202512"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202512">3</a></sup> however, makes <a class="vei_120a" href="मृग">mṛga</a> or <a class="vei_120a" href="मृगशिरस्">mṛgaśiras</a> into a different group, consisting of the belt of Orion, with two stars in the knees and one in the left shoulder, which he deems to resemble a deer's head with an arrow through it, an implausible and unlikely theory. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मृगव्याध">mṛgavyādha</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202512"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202512">↑</a> iii. 33, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202512"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202512">↑</a> See Whitney, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, xcii.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202512"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202512">↑</a> <i>Orion,</i> 99 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

मृग हस्तिन्|मृग हस्तिन्|మృగ హస్తిన్_౩|మృగ హస్తిన్|ಮೃಗ ಹಸ್ತಿನ್_೩|ಮೃಗ ಹಸ್ತಿನ್|mṛga hastin_3|mṛga hastin|mRga hastin_3|mRga hastin|mRiga hastin_3|mRiga hastin|mRuga hastin_3|mRuga hastin|mRRiga hastin_3|mRRiga hastin|_vei_120@2513|_vei2_pn171
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2512">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2514">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn170">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn171">2:171</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn172">↷</a><hr><p>3. <b>Mṛga Hastin,</b> the ‘animal with a hand,’ is mentioned in two passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202513"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202513">1</a></sup> in which Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202513"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202513">2</a></sup> recognizes that the elephant is meant, but concludes that the compound name  is a proof of the newness of the elephant to the Vedic Indians.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202513"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202513">3</a></sup> Later the adjective <a class="vei_120a" href="हस्तिन्">hastin</a> alone became the regular name of the animal (like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="महिष">mahiṣa</a></b> of the ‘buffalo’). The elephant is also denoted in the Rigveda by the descriptive term <a class="vei_120a" href="मृग">mṛga</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वारण">vāraṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202513"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202513">4</a></sup> the ‘wild or dangerous animal,’ the adjective <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वारण">vāraṇa</a></i> similarly becoming one of the names for ‘elephant’ in the later language. Pischel's view<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202513"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202513">5</a></sup> that the catching of elephants by the use of tame female elephants is already alluded to in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202513"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202513">6</a></sup> seems very doubtful. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202513"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202513">7</a></sup> elephants are described as ‘black, white-toothed, adorned with gold.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202513"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202513">↑</a> i. 64, 7; iv. 16, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202513"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202513">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, Erläuterungen,</i> 79.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202513"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202513">↑</a> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 99, 100, combats the view that the eler hant was new to the Vedic Indian, because of the similar use of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मृग">mṛga</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="महिष">mahiṣa</a></i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मृग">mṛga</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूकर">sūkara</a></i> (Av. xii. 1, 48) to denote the ‘buffalo’ and the ‘boar’ respectively. But <b><a class="vei_120a" href="महिष">mahiṣa</a></b> seems rather to bear out Roth's conclusion; while <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सूकर">sūkara</a></b> appears alone in the Rigveda, and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मृग">mṛga</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूकर">sūkara</a>,</i> ‘wild hog,’ seems to be used in one passage of the Av. (xii. 1, 48) to distinguish it from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वराह">varāha</a>,</b> ‘boar,’ in the same verse.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202513"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202513">↑</a> Rv. viii. 33, 8; x. 40, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202513"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202513">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 121-123; 317319. <i>Cf.</i> Strabo, pp. 704, 705; Arrian, <i>Indica,</i> 13. 14 (from Megasthenes).</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202513"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202513">↑</a> viii. 2, 6; x. 40, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202513"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202513">↑</a> viii. 23, 3 (<i>hiraṇyena parīvṛtān kṛṣṇāñ chukladato mṛgān</i>). See Pischel, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 122.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 80.</li></ol></div>

मृगय|మృగయ|ಮೃಗಯ|mṛgaya|mRgaya|mRigaya|mRugaya|mRRigaya|_vei_120@2514|_vei2_pn172
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2513">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2515">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn171">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn172">2:172</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn174">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mṛgaya</b> occurs in several passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202514">1</a></sup> as defeated by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>. That he was a human foe, as Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202514">2</a></sup> thinks, seems unlikely: more probably he was a demon, as <a class="vei_120a" href="मृग">mṛga</a> unquestionably is.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202514">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202514">↑</a> iv. 16, 13; viii. 3, 19; x. 49, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202514">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 166.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202514">↑</a> Rv. i. 80, 7; v. 29, 4, etc.</li></ol></div>

मृगयु|మృగయు|ಮೃಗಯು|mṛgayu|mRgayu|mRigayu|mRugayu|mRRigayu|_vei_120@2515|_vei2_pn172
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2514">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2516">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn171">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn172">2:172</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn174">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mṛgayu,</b> ‘hunter,’ occurs in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202515">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202515">2</a></sup> but not very often. The Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202515">3</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202515">4</a></sup> however, in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) include a number of names which seem to be those of persons who make a livelihood by fishing or by hunting, such as the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मार्गार">mārgāra</a>,</b> ‘hunter,’  the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कैवर्त">kaivarta</a></b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="केवर्त">kevarta</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पौञ्जिष्ठ">pauñjiṣṭha</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="दश">daśa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मैनाल">maināla</a>,</b> ‘fisher man,’ and perhaps the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बैन्द">bainda</a></b> and the Ānda,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202515">5</a></sup> who seem to have been some sort of fishermen.</p><p>It is not probable that even in the earliest Vedic period hunting formed the main source of livelihood for any of the Vedic tribes: pastoral pursuits and agriculture (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृषि">kṛṣi</a></b>) were, no doubt, the mainstay of their existence. But it would be unreasonable to suppose that not much hunting was done, both for recreation and for purposes of food, as well as for protection of flocks from wild beasts. The Rigveda is naturally our chief source of information in regard to hunting. The arrow was sometimes employed,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202515">6</a></sup> but, as is usual with primitive man, the normal instruments of capture were nets and pitfalls. Birds were regularly caught in nets (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="पाश">pāśa</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202515">7</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="निधा">nidhā</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202515">8</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जाल">jāla</a></b>),<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202515">9</a></sup> The bird-catcher being called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="निधा">nidhā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202515">10</a></sup> ‘master of snares.’ The net was fastened on pegs<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202515">11</a></sup> (as is done with modern nets for catching birds). Another name of net is apparently <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मुक्षीजा">mukṣījā</a>.</b></p><p>Pits were used for catching antelopes (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋश्य">ṛśya</a></b>), and so were called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋश्य">ṛśya</a>-da,</i><sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202515">12</a></sup> ‘antelope-catching.’ Elephants were captured as in Greek times, perhaps through the instrumentality of tame females (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मृग हस्तिन्">mṛga hastin</a></b>). Apparently the boar was captured in the chase, dogs being used,<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202515">13</a></sup> but the passage from which this view is deduced is of uncertain mythological content. There is also an obscure reference<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1202515">14</a></sup> to the capture of the buffalo (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौर">gaura</a></b>), but it is not clear whether the reference is to shooting with an arrow or capturing by means of ropes, perhaps a lasso, or a net. The lion was captured in pitfalls,<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1202515">15</a></sup> or was surrounded by the hunters and slain;<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1202515">16</a></sup> one very obscure passage refers to the lion being caught by ambuscade, which perhaps merely alludes to the use of the hidden <a class="vei_120a" href="पित्">pit</a>.<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1202515">17</a></sup></p><p>The modes of catching fish are little known, for the only evidence available are the explanations of the various names  mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>. Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1202515">18</a></sup> says that <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धैवर">dhaivara</a></b> is one who takes fish by netting a tank on either side; <a class="vei_120a" href="दाश">dāśa</a> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शौष्कल">śauṣkala</a></b> do so by means of a fish-hook (<i>baḍiśa</i>); <a class="vei_120a" href="बैन्द">bainda</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कैवर्त">kaivarta</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="मैनाल">maināla</a> by means of a net (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="जाल">jāla</a></i>); <a class="vei_120a" href="मार्गार">mārgāra</a> catches fish in the water with his hands; Ānda by putting in pegs at a ford (apparently by building a sort of <a class="vei_120a" href="दम्">dam</a>); <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्णक">parṇaka</a></b> by putting a poisoned leaf on the water. But none of these explanations can claim much authority.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202515">↑</a> Av. x. 1, 26; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 27; xxx. 7, etc. <i>Cf. mṛgaṇyu,</i> Rv. x. 40, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202515">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 1, 1; iii. 4, 3, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 9, 12, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202515">↑</a> xxx.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202515">↑</a> iii. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202515">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 12, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202515">↑</a> Rv. ii. 42, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202515">↑</a> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पाशिन्">pāśin</a>,</b> ‘hunter,’ Rv. iii. 45, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202515">↑</a> Rv. ix. 83, 4; x. 73, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202515">↑</a> Av. x. 1, 30.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202515">↑</a> Rv. ix. 83, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202515">↑</a> Av. viii. 8, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202515">↑</a> Rv. x. 39, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202515">↑</a> Rv. x. 86, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1202515">↑</a> Rv. x. 51, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1202515">↑</a> Rv. x. 28, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1202515">↑</a> Rv. v. 15, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1202515">↑</a> Rv. v. 74, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 1, 542, n.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1202515"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1202515">↑</a> On <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 12, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 18, 281.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 243245.</li></ol></div>

मृग-व्याध|मृगव्याध|मृग|व्याध|మృగ-వ్యాధ|మృగవ్యాధ|మృగ|వ్యాధ|ಮೃಗ-ವ್ಯಾಧ|ಮೃಗವ್ಯಾಧ|ಮೃಗ|ವ್ಯಾಧ|mṛga-vyādha|mṛgavyādha|mṛga|vyādha|mRga-vyaadha|mRgavyaadha|mRga|vyaadha|mRiga-vyaadha|mRigavyaadha|mRiga|mRuga-vyaadha|mRugavyaadha|mRuga|mRRiga-vyaadha|mRRigavyaadha|mRRiga|_vei_120@2516|_vei2_pn174
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2515">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2517">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn172">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn174">2:174</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn175">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mṛga-vyādha,</b> ‘the hunter,’ is the name of Sirius in the legned of Prajāpati's daughter in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202516"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202516">1</a></sup> Prajāpati (Orion) pursues his daughter (<a class="vei_120a" href="रोहिणी">rohiṇī</a>), and is shot by the archer Sirius. The transference of the legend of Prajāpati to the sky is no doubt secondary, caused by the obvious similarity of the constellation in question to the idea of an archer.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202516"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202516">↑</a> iii. 33, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 205, n. 1, 208, n. 3; Tilak, <i>Orion,</i> 98 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूर्य">sūrya</a> Siddhānta, viii. 10; ix. 12, preserves the name.</li></ol></div>

मृग-शिरस्|मृग-शिर|मृगशिरस्|मृगशिर|मृग|शिरस्|शिर|मृग-शीर्ष|मृगशीर्ष|शीर्ष|మృగ-శిరస్|మృగ-శిర|మృగశిరస్|మృగశిర|మృగ|శిరస్|శిర|మృగ-శీర్ష|మృగశీర్ష|శీర్ష|ಮೃಗ-ಶಿರಸ್|ಮೃಗ-ಶಿರ|ಮೃಗಶಿರಸ್|ಮೃಗಶಿರ|ಮೃಗ|ಶಿರಸ್|ಶಿರ|ಮೃಗ-ಶೀರ್ಷ|ಮೃಗಶೀರ್ಷ|ಶೀರ್ಷ|mṛga-śiras|mṛga-śira|mṛgaśiras|mṛgaśira|mṛga|śiras|śira|mṛga-śīrṣa|mṛgaśīrṣa|śīrṣa|mRga-shiras|mRga-shira|mRgashiras|mRgashira|mRga|shiras|shira|mRga-shiirSha|mRgashiirSha|shiirSha|mRga-sheerSha|mRgasheerSha|sheerSha|mRiga-shiras|mRiga-shira|mRigashiras|mRigashira|mRiga|mRiga-shiirSha|mRigashiirSha|mRuga-shiras|mRuga-shira|mRugashiras|mRugashira|mRuga|mRuga-shiirSha|mRugashiirSha|mRRiga-shiras|mRRiga-shira|mRRigashiras|mRRigashira|mRRiga|mRRiga-shiirSha|mRRigashiirSha|_vei_120@2517|_vei2_pn174
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2516">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2518">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn172">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn174">2:174</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn175">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mṛga-<a class="vei_120a" href="शिरस्">śiras</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मृग">mṛga</a>-śīrṣa.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>,</b> 1. and 2. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मृग">mṛga</a>.</b></p></div>

मृगाखर|మృగాఖర|ಮೃಗಾಖರ|mṛgākhara|mRgaakhara|mRigaakhara|mRugaakhara|mRRigaakhara|_vei_120@2518|_vei2_pn174
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2517">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2519">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn172">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn174">2:174</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn175">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mṛgākhara</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (vii. 5, 21, 1) and <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 9, 17, 3) denotes the ‘lair of wild beasts.’</p></div>

मृड|మృడ|ಮೃಡ|mṛḍa|mRDa|mRiDa|mRuDa|mRRiDa|_vei_120@2519|_vei2_pn174
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2518">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2520">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn172">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn174">2:174</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn175">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mṛḍa</b> is found only in compounds in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202519">1</a></sup> where it seems to denote a small weight of gold. It is uncertain whether the reading should not be Pṛda, as in the grammatical tradition.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202519">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202519">↑</a> <i>Upacāya-mṛḍaṃ hiraṇyam,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 1; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अष्टा">aṣṭā</a>-mṛḍaṃ hiraṇyam, ibid.,</i> xiii. 10; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अष्टा">aṣṭā</a>-pruḍ-ḍhiraṇyam,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 1, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202519">↑</a> See Pāṇini, iii. 1, 123, with the Vārttika; von Schroeder, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 49, 164.</li></ol></div>

मृत्तिका|మృత్తికా|ಮೃತ್ತಿಕಾ|mṛttikā|mRttikaa|mRittikaa|mRuttikaa|mRRittikaa|_vei_120@2520|_vei2_pn174
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2519">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2521">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn172">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn174">2:174</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn175">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mṛttikā,</b> ‘clay,’ is mentioned in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202520"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202520">1</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मृद्">mṛd</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202520"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202520">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 34, 2; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, x. 1, 8. 9.</li></ol></div>

मृत्यु|మృత్యు|ಮೃತ್ಯು|mṛtyu|mRtyu|mRityu|mRutyu|mRRityu|_vei_120@2521|_vei2_pn175
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2520">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2522">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn174">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn175">2:175</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn176">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mṛtyu,</b> ‘death,’ is repeatedly mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202521">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202521">2</a></sup> as a thing of terror. There are a hundred and one forms of death, the natural one by old age<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202521">3</a></sup> (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="जरा">jarā</a></i>), and a hundred others, all to be avoided.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202521">4</a></sup> To die before old age (<i>purā jarasaḥ</i>)<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202521">5</a></sup> is to die before the allotted span (<i>purā āyuṣaḥ</i>),<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202521">6</a></sup> the normal length of life being throughout Vedic literature spoken of as a hundred years.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202521">7</a></sup> On the other hand, the evils of old age in the loss of physical strength were clearly realized.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202521">8</a></sup> one of the feats of the Aśvins was to restore old <b>Cyavāna</b> to his former youth and powers, and another was the rejuventation of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कलि">kali</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202521">9</a></sup> The Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202521">10</a></sup> is full of charms of all sorts to avert death and secure length of years (<i>āyuṣya</i>).</p><p>The modes of disposing of the dead were burial and cremation (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अग्निदग्ध">agnidagdha</a></b>). Both existed in the early Vedic period,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202521">11</a></sup> as in Greece;<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202521">12</a></sup> but the former method was on the whole less favoured, and tended to be regarded with disapproval. The bones of the dead, whether burned or not, were marked by the erection of a tumulus (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्मशान">śmaśāna</a></b>): the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202521">13</a></sup> preserves traces of strong differences of opinion as to the mode in which these tumuli should be constructed. There is little or no trace<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1202521">14</a></sup> of the custom common in northern lands of sending the dead man to sea in a burning ship: the reference  to a ship<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1202521">15</a></sup> seems to point to mythical perils after death, not to the mode of burial.</p><p>The life after death was to the Vedic Indian a repetition of the life in this world. He passed into the next world <i>sarvatanuḥ sāṅgaḥ,</i> ‘with whole body and all his members,’<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1202521">16</a></sup> enjoying there the same pleasures as he had enjoyed on earth. Even in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1202521">17</a></sup> there are hints of evil awaiting evil-doers, but it is not until the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1202521">18</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1202521">19</a></sup> that a hell of punishment is set out, and it is in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1202521">20</a></sup> that good and evil deeds are said to produce happiness or hell hereafter. But there is no hint of extinction<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1202521">21</a></sup> in the Rigveda as the fate of the wicked, as Roth<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1202521">22</a></sup> inclined to think. The Vedic poet not being deeply moral, his verses do not convey, as would those of a man convinced of sin, warnings of future judgment.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202521">↑</a> vii. 59, 12; x. 13, 4; 18, 1, 2; x. 48, 5; 60, 5. So <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मृत्यु">mṛtyu</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="बन्धु">bandhu</a>,</i> ‘akin to death,’ Rv. viii. 18, 22; x. 95, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202521">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 5, 9, 4, where the world is said to be ‘yoked with death’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="मृत्यु">mṛtyu</a>-saṃyuta</i>); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 9, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8, 2; 14, 1. 2. 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 6, 5, 1, etc. So often the ‘bonds of death’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="मृत्यु">mṛtyu</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पाश">pāśa</a></i>), Av. viii. 2, 2; 8, 10. 16; xvii. 1, 30; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 10, 8, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 18, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202521">↑</a> Av. ii. 13, 2; 28, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202521">↑</a> Av. i. 30, 3; ii. 28, 1; iii. 11, 5; viii. 2, 27; xi. 6, 16, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202521">↑</a> Rv. viii. 67, 20; Av. v. 30, 17; x. 2, 30; xiii. 3, 56.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202521">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 4, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202521">↑</a> Rv. i. 64, 14; 89, 9; ii. 33, 2, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Lanman, <i>Sanskrit Reader,</i> 384; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 193; <i>Festgruss an Reth,</i> 137.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202521">↑</a> Rv. i. 71, 10; 179, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202521">↑</a> x. 39, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 243.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202521">↑</a> See Bloomfield, <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 62 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202521">↑</a> See Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, clii; Winternitz, <i>Geschichte der indischen Literatur,</i> 1, 84, 85.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202521">↑</a> See Lang, <i>Homer and his Age,</i> 82 <i>et seq.; cf.</i> Burrows, <i>Discoveries in Crete,</i> 209-213.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202521">↑</a> xiii. 8, 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1202521">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 410; Weinhold, <i>Altnordisches Leben,</i> 483 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1202521">↑</a> Rv. x. 63, 10; 135, 4; Av. vii. 6, 3, and <i>cf.</i> Weber, Proceedings of the Berlin Academy, 1895, 856.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1202521">↑</a> Av. v. 6, 11; xviii. 4, 64; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 6, 1, 1; xi. 1, 8, 6; xii. 8, 3, 31, and <i>cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 3, 5, 2; 6, 3; 6, 6, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8, 20, 5; 10, 11, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1202521">↑</a> Rv. ii. 29, 6; iii. 26, 8; iv. 5, 5; 25, 6; vii. 104, 3. 11. 17; x. 152, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1202521">↑</a> ii. 14, 3; v. 19, 3; 30, 11; viii. 2, 24; xii. 4, 36; xviii. 3, 3. <i>Cf.</i> also v. 19; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1202521">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 6, 1, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 9, 240 <i>et seq.;</i> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 42-44 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 236 <i>et seq.</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1202521">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 2, 2, 27; x. 6, 3, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1202521">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 169.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1202521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1202521">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 3, 329-347; Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 238 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

मृद्|మృద్|ಮೃದ್|mṛd|mRd|mRid|mRud|mRRid|_vei_120@2522|_vei2_pn176
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2521">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2523">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn175">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn176">2:176</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn177">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mṛd</b> denotes ‘clay’ in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202522">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202522">2</a></sup> (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मृत्तिका">mṛttikā</a></b>). A ‘lump of clay’ also occurs in the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202522">3</a></sup> and a Mṛt-paca, ‘potter,’ in the Maitrāyaṇī <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202522">4</a></sup> A ‘clay vessel,’ Mṛtpātra,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202522">5</a></sup> and vessels (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पात्र">pātra</a></i>) made of clay (<i>mṛn-<a class="vei_120a" href="मय">maya</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202522">6</a></sup> are mentioned, and the grave is called the ‘house of clay.’<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202522">7</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202522">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 7, 9, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xi. 55.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202522">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 1, 1, 13; 2, 34; 3, 3; 3, 1, 22, 32; 3, 1; Maitrāyaṇī <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> vi. 27, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202522">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 4, 2, 1; 5, 2, 1; xiv. 2, 1, 8; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202522">↑</a> ii. 6; iii. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202522">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxi. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202522">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 1, 3, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202522">↑</a> Rv. vii. 89, 1 (<i>mṛnmaya <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह">gṛha</a></i>).</li></ol></div>

मृध्|మృధ్|ಮೃಧ್|mṛdh|mRdh|mRidh|mRudh|mRRidh|_vei_120@2523|_vei2_pn177
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2522">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2524">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn176">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn177">2:177</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn178">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mṛdh</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202523">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202523">2</a></sup> denotes ‘enemy.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202523">↑</a> i. 131, 6; 138, 2; 182, 4; ii. 22, 3; 23, 13; 28, 7; iii. 47, 2; v. 30, 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202523">↑</a> Av. v. 20, 12; vi. 2, 2; viii. 5, 8; xiii. 1, 5, 27; xviii. 2, 59; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 7, 4; 5, 3, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, v. 37; xi. 10 72, etc.</li></ol></div>

मृध्र-वाच्|मृध्रवाच्|मृध्र|वाच्|మృధ్ర-వాచ్|మృధ్రవాచ్|మృధ్ర|వాచ్|ಮೃಧ್ರ-ವಾಚ್|ಮೃಧ್ರವಾಚ್|ಮೃಧ್ರ|ವಾಚ್|mṛdhra-vāc|mṛdhravāc|mṛdhra|vāc|mRdhra-vaach|mRdhravaach|mRdhra|vaach|mRidhra-vaach|mRidhravaach|mRidhra|mRudhra-vaach|mRudhravaach|mRudhra|mRRidhra-vaach|mRRidhravaach|mRRidhra|_vei_120@2524|_vei2_pn177
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2523">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2525">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn176">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn177">2:177</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn178">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mṛdhra-vāc.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दस्यु">dasyu</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>.</b></p></div>

मेक्षण|మేక్షణ|ಮೇಕ್ಷಣ|mekṣaṇa|mekShaNa|mexaNa|_vei_120@2525|_vei2_pn177
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2524">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2526">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn176">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn177">2:177</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn178">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mekṣaṇa</b> is the name in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202525"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202525">1</a></sup> of a wooden ladle used for stirring up the oblation (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="चरु">caru</a></b>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202525"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202525">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 10, 4; iii. 7, 4, 9; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 2, 13, etc.</li></ol></div>

मेखला|మేఖలా|ಮೇಖಲಾ|mekhalā|mekhalaa|_vei_120@2526|_vei2_pn177
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2525">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2527">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn176">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn177">2:177</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn178">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mekhalā</b> denotes ‘girdle’ in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202526"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202526">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202526"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202526">2</a></sup> The <b>Brahmacārin</b> wore a girdle.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202526"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202526">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202526"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202526">↑</a> Av. vi. 133, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 3, 3, 5; vi. 2, 2, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxiii. 4; xxiv. 9; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 6, 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202526"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202526">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 1, 10; iv. 4, 5, 2; vi. 2, 2, 39, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202526"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202526">↑</a> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> Sūtras the girdle of the Brahmin is of <a class="vei_120a" href="मुञ्ज">muñja</a>, that of the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> of a bowstring, and that of the <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> of wool or hemp. See Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 19, 12, etc.</li></ol></div>

मेघ|మేఘ|ಮೇಘ|megha|_vei_120@2527|_vei2_pn177
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2526">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2528">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn176">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn177">2:177</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn178">↷</a><hr><p><b>Megha</b> denotes ‘cloud’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202527"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202527">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202527"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202527">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202527"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202527">↑</a> i. 181, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202527"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202527">↑</a> Av. iv. 15, 7; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 2, 5; xii. 3, 2, 6; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="महा">mahā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="मेघ">megha</a>,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 2, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, vii. 3; viii. 7. The verb <i>meghay,</i> ‘to make cloudy weather,’ is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 4, 5, 1, and <i>meghayantī</i> is the name of one of the seven <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्तिकास्">kṛttikās</a>,</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 4, 1; Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 301, 368.</li></ol></div>

मेथि|మేథి|ಮೇಥಿ|methi|_vei_120@2528|_vei2_pn177
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2527">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2529">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn176">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn177">2:177</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn178">↷</a><hr><p><b>Methi</b> is found in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202528"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202528">1</a></sup> denoting ‘post.’ The word is also found in the marriage ritual,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202528"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202528">2</a></sup> when the sense is, according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, a post to support the pole of a chariot. In one passage of the Rigveda it is perhaps used of posts forming a palisade.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202528"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202528">3</a></sup> In the Pañcaviṃśa  <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202528"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202528">4</a></sup> it appears in the form of Methī to denote the post to which the sacrificial cow is tied. The word is very variously spelt, Medhi and Meṭhī also being found.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202528"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202528">↑</a> viii. 5, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202528"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202528">↑</a> Av. xiv. 1, 40. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 9, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxv. 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 29, 22; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 5, 3, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202528"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202528">↑</a> viii. 53, 5 (<i>mita-methībhiḥ</i> for <i>medhābhiḥ,</i> conjectured by Roth, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesell schaft,</i> 48, 109).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202528"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202528">↑</a> xiii. 9, 17. <i>Cf.</i> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 19, 1 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 23, 329).</li></ol></div>

मेध|మేధ|ಮೇಧ|medha|_vei_120@2529|_vei2_pn178
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2528">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2530">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn177">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn178">2:178</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn179">↷</a><hr><p><b>Medha</b> is a word of uncertain sense occurring in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वालखिल्य">vālakhilya</a> hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202529">1</a></sup> According to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, the proper name of a sacrificer may be meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202529">↑</a> viii. 50, 10 (<i>cf.</i> viii. 49, 10), where, as a rule, the sense of ‘sacrifice’ is accepted as adequate.</li></ol></div>

मेधातिथि|మేధాతిథి|ಮೇಧಾತಿಥಿ|medhātithi|medhaatithi|_vei_120@2530|_vei2_pn178
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2529">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2531">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn177">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn178">2:178</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn179">↷</a><hr><p><b>Medhātithi,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202530">1</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मेध्यातिथि">medhyātithi</a></b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202530">2</a></sup> (‘having a guest at the sacrifice’) appear to be the names of one and the same man, a descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a></b> and a famous Vedic <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>,</b> to whom the authorship of various hymns<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202530">3</a></sup> is attributed in the Anukramaṇī (Index). To him <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> is said in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202530">4</a></sup> to have come in the form of a ram: this myth is perpetuated in the Subrahmaṇyā formula<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202530">5</a></sup> recited by the priest while the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> is being carried within the sacrificial enclosure, when <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> is hailed as ‘ram of <a class="vei_120a" href="मेधातिथि">medhātithi</a>.’ He appears also as a rival of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वत्स">vatsa</a>,</b> whom he accused of low birth, but who convinced him of his error by undergoing a fire ordeal (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दिव्य">divya</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202530">6</a></sup> In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202530">7</a></sup> he is mentioned with many other sages, and occurs elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202530">8</a></sup> also as a sage. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202530">↑</a> This is the form of the later texts and of Rv. viii. 8, 20, where he occurs with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202530">↑</a> This is the usual form in the Rv.: i. 36, 10. 11. 17; viii. 1, 30; 2, 40; 33, 4; 49, 9; 51, 1; ix. 43, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202530">↑</a> i. 12-23; viii. 1-3; 22. 23; ix. 4143. In the ascriptions <a class="vei_120a" href="मेधातिथि">medhātithi</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="मेध्यातिथि">medhyātithi</a> are confused.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202530">↑</a> viii. 2, 40. <i>Cf.</i> i. 51, 1, where, however, there is no mention of Medhāithi.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202530">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 79; Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 4, 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 12, 3. Moreover, the legend is alluded to in the Śāṭyāyanaka. See Sāyaṇa on Rv. i. 51, 1; viii. 2, 40, and Oertel, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, ccxl. On the explanation of the legend, <i>cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 38 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202530">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 6, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202530">↑</a> iv. 29, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202530">↑</a> As a Gṛhapati at the sacrifice of the <b>Vibhindukīyas,</b> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 233 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 38); <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xv. 10, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxviii. 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 102, 105; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 146.</li></ol></div>

मेध्य|మేధ్య|ಮೇಧ್ಯ|medhya|_vei_120@2531|_vei2_pn179
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2530">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2532">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn178">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn179">2:179</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn180">↷</a><hr><p><b>Medhya</b> is the name of a man, an ancient sacrificer, in a hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202531"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202531">1</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202531"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202531">2</a></sup> he is erroneously transmuted into <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पृषध्र">pṛṣadhra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मेध्य">medhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मातरिश्वन्">mātariśvan</a>,</b> the patron of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रस्कण्व">praskaṇva</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202531"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202531">↑</a> viii. 52, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202531"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202531">↑</a> xvi. 11, 26. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 39; Ludwig. Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 163.</li></ol></div>

मेध्यातिथि|మేధ్యాతిథి|ಮೇಧ್ಯಾತಿಥಿ|medhyātithi|medhyaatithi|_vei_120@2532|_vei2_pn179
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2531">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2533">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn178">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn179">2:179</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn180">↷</a><hr><p><b>Medhyātithi.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मेधातिथि">medhātithi</a>.</b></p></div>

मेनका|మేనకా|ಮೇನಕಾ|menakā|menakaa|_vei_120@2533|_vei2_pn179
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2532">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2534">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn178">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn179">2:179</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn180">↷</a><hr><p><b>Menakā.</b> See 2. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मेना">menā</a>.</b></p></div>

मेना|मेना|మేనా_౧|మేనా|ಮೇನಾ_೧|ಮೇನಾ|menā_1|menā|menaa_1|menaa|_vei_120@2534|_vei2_pn179
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2533">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2535">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn178">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn179">2:179</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn180">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Menā</b> in a few passages of the Rigveda denotes ‘woman.’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202534"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202534">1</a></sup> The word is also used in the sense of the ‘female’ of an animal, either mare<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202534"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202534">2</a></sup> or cow.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202534"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202534">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202534"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202534">↑</a> Rv. i. 62, 7; 95, 6; ii. 39, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202534"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202534">↑</a> Rv. i. 121, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202534"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202534">↑</a> x. 111, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Pischel, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 2, 316, 317.</li></ol></div>

मेना|मेना|మేనా_౨|మేనా|ಮೇನಾ_೨|ಮೇನಾ|menā_2|menā|menaa_2|menaa|_vei_120@2535|_vei2_pn179
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2534">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2536">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn178">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn179">2:179</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn180">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Menā</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202535"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202535">1</a></sup> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मेनका">menakā</a></b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202535"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202535">2</a></sup> is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202535"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202535">3</a></sup> and in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202535"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202535">4</a></sup> as the daughter, or perhaps wife, of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वृषणश्व">vṛṣaṇaśva</a>.</b> The meaning of the legend connected with her is quite unknown. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मैनाक">maināka</a></b> or <b>Mainaga.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202535"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202535">↑</a> This is the ordinary form of the name.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202535"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202535">↑</a> So Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, where the masculine form Mena is the epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="वृषणश्व">vṛṣaṇaśva</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202535"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202535">↑</a> Rv. i. 51, 13, where Sāyaṇa tells the legend from the Śāṭyāyanaka. <i>Cf.</i> Oertel, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, ccxl.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202535"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202535">↑</a> Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 4, 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 12, 3; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>. i. 3, 17.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 16, 81, n.</li></ol></div>

मेष|మేష|ಮೇಷ|meṣa|meSha|_vei_120@2536|_vei2_pn179
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2535">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2537">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn178">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn179">2:179</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn180">↷</a><hr><p><b>Meṣa</b> denotes ‘ram’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202536"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202536">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202536"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202536">2</a></sup> while Meṣī means ‘sheep.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202536"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202536">3</a></sup> Both words are also used to denote the  ‘wool’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202536"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202536">4</a></sup> of the sheep, <b>especially</b> as employed for the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> filter. A wild (<i>āraṇya</i>) <b>ram is</b> mentioned in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202536"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202536">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202536"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202536">↑</a> i. 43, 6; 116, 16; viii. 2, 40; x. 27, 17, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202536"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202536">↑</a> Av. vi. 49, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iii. 59; xix. 90; xxiv. 30; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 4, 12, 1; Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 4, 18, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202536"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202536">↑</a> Rv. i. 43, 6; Vājasaneyī Saṃhitā, iii. 59; xxiv. 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 4, 4. etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202536"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202536">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मेष">meṣa</a>,</i> Rv. viii. 86, 11; <i>Meṣī.</i> ix. 8, 5; 86, 47; 107, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202536"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202536">↑</a> xxiv. 30.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 66, 67.</li></ol></div>

मेहत्नू|మేహత్నూ|ಮೇಹತ್ನೂ|mehatnū|mehatnuu|mehatnoo|_vei_120@2537|_vei2_pn180
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2536">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2538">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn179">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn180">2:180</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn181">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mehatnū</b> is the name of a stream in the Nadīstuti (‘Praise of Rivers’) in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202537"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202537">1</a></sup> It must apparently have been a tributary of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धु">sindhu</a></b> (Indus), entering that river before the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रुमु">krumu</a></b> (Kurum) and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोमती">gomatī</a></b> (Gomal). It may conceivably have been a tributary of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रुमु">krumu</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202537"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202537">↑</a> x. 75, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 14; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 344.</li></ol></div>

मैत्रायणीय ब्राह्मण|మైత్రాయణీయ బ్రాహ్మణ|ಮೈತ್ರಾಯಣೀಯ ಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ|maitrāyaṇīya brāhmaṇa|maitraayaNiiya braahmaNa|maitraayaNeeya braahmaNa|_vei_120@2538|_vei2_pn180
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2537">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2539">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn179">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn180">2:180</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn181">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maitrāyaṇīya Brāhmaṇa</b> is the name of a text mentioned in the Śulba <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> of <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202538"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202538">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202538"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202538">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxxii. 8. <i>Cf.</i> Caland, <i>Über das rituclle <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> des <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>,</i> 41, who cannot trace the citation in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā.</li></ol></div>

मैत्रेय|మైత్రేయ|ಮೈತ್ರೇಯ|maitreya|_vei_120@2539|_vei2_pn180
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2538">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2540">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn179">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn180">2:180</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn181">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maitreya</b> is the patronymic or metronymic<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202539"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202539">1</a></sup> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौषारव">kauṣārava</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202539"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202539">2</a></sup> It is also applied to <b>Glāva</b> in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202539"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202539">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202539"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202539">↑</a> Patronymic from Mitrayu, according to Pāṇini, vi. 4, 174; vii. 3, 2; metronymic from Mitrā, according to the commentator on Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 12, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202539"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202539">↑</a> viii. 28, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202539"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202539">↑</a> i. 12, 1; Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 31 <i>et seq.;</i> Bloomfield, <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 110.</li></ol></div>

मैत्रेयी|मैत्रेयि|మైత్రేయీ|మైత్రేయి|ಮೈತ್ರೇಯೀ|ಮೈತ್ರೇಯಿ|maitreyī|maitreyi|maitreyii|maitreyee|_vei_120@2540|_vei2_pn180
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2539">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2541">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn179">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn180">2:180</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn181">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maitreyī</b> is the name of one of the wives of <b>Yājṅavalkya</b> according to the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (ii. 4, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> iv. 5, 2 <i>et seq.</i>).</p></div>

मैनाक|మైనాక|ಮೈನಾಕ|maināka|mainaaka|_vei_120@2541|_vei2_pn180
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2540">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2542">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn179">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn180">2:180</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn181">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maināka,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मेनका">menakā</a>,</b>’ is the name of a mountain among the Himālayas in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202541"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202541">1</a></sup> There is a various reading Maināga. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202541"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202541">↑</a> i. 31, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 78; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 93.</li></ol></div>

मैनाल|మైనాల|ಮೈನಾಲ|maināla|mainaala|_vei_120@2542|_vei2_pn181
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2541">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2543">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn180">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn181">2:181</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn182">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maināla</b> occurs in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202542">1</a></sup> It seems clearly to mean ‘fisherman’ from Mīna, ‘fish,’ as Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202542">2</a></sup> and Mahīdhara<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202542">3</a></sup> explain it.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202542">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 12, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202542">↑</a> On <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202542">↑</a> On Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

मौजवत|మౌజవత|ಮೌಜವತ|maujavata|_vei_120@2543|_vei2_pn181
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2542">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2544">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn180">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn181">2:181</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn182">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maujavata.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मूजवन्त्">mūjavant</a>.</b></p></div>

मौद्गल्य|మౌద్గల్య|ಮೌದ್ಗಲ್ಯ|maudgalya|_vei_120@2544|_vei2_pn181
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2543">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2545">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn180">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn181">2:181</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn182">↷</a><hr><p><b>Maudgalya,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मुद्गल">mudgala</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of several persons, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नाक">nāka</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202544">1</a></sup> <b>Śatabalākṣa,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202544">2</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="लांगलायन">lāṅgalāyana</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202544">3</a></sup> <b>A Brahmacārin</b> of the name is mentioned in the Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202544">4</a></sup> as disputing with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्लाव मैत्रेय">glāva maitreya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202544">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 5, 2, 1; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 9, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202544">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, xi. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202544">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202544">↑</a> i. 1, 31; Bloomfield, <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 110. <i>Cf.</i> also Caland, <i>Über das rituelle <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> des <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>,</i> 35.</li></ol></div>

मौन|మౌన|ಮೌನ|mauna|_vei_120@2545|_vei2_pn181
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2544">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2546">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn180">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn181">2:181</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn182">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mauna,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="मुनि">muni</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b>Aṇīcin</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xxiii. 5).</p></div>

मौषिकी-पुत्र|मौषिकीपुत्र|मौषिकी|मौषिकि|पुत्र|మౌషికీ-పుత్ర|మౌషికీపుత్ర|మౌషికీ|మౌషికి|పుత్ర|ಮೌಷಿಕೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಮೌಷಿಕೀಪುತ್ರ|ಮೌಷಿಕೀ|ಮೌಷಿಕಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|mauṣikī-putra|mauṣikīputra|mauṣikī|mauṣiki|putra|mauShikii-putra|mauShikiiputra|mauShikii|mauShiki|mauShikee-putra|mauShikeeputra|mauShikee|_vei_120@2546|_vei2_pn181
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2545">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2547">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn180">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn181">2:181</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn182">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mauṣikī-putra,</b> ‘son of a female descendant of Mūṣikā,’ is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हारिकर्णीपुत्र">hārikarṇīputra</a></b> in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Mādhyaṃdina recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 4, 30).</p></div>

म्लेच्छ|మ్లేచ్ఛ|ಮ್ಲೇಚ್ಛ|mleccha|mlechCha|_vei_120@2547|_vei2_pn181
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2546">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2548">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn180">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn181">2:181</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn182">↷</a><hr><p><b>Mleccha</b> occurs in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202547">1</a></sup> in the sense of a barbarian in speech. The Brahmin is there forbidden to use barbarian speech. The example<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202547">2</a></sup> given of such speech is <i>lie 'lavo,</i> explained by Sāyaṇa as <i>he 'rayaḥ,</i> ‘ho, foes.’ If this is correct--the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension has a different reading<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202547">3</a></sup> --the barbarians referred to were Āryan speakers, though not speakers of Sanskrit, but of a Prākṛta form of speech.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202547">4</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वाच्">vāc</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202547">↑</a> iii. 2, 1, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202547">↑</a> iii. 2, 1, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202547">↑</a> See Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 31, n. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202547">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 180; <i>cf.</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āranyaka,</i> 179, 180, 196.</li></ol></div>

यक्ष|యక్ష|ಯಕ್ಷ|yakṣa|yakSha|yaxa|_vei_120@2548|_vei2_pn182
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2547">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2549">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn181">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn182">2:182</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn183">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yakṣa</b> is found several times in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202548">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202548">2</a></sup> in passages in which Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202548">3</a></sup> sees the meaning of a feast or holy practice in accordance with the native commentators. It is, however, very doubtful whether this sense ever occurs.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202548">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202548">↑</a> i. 190, 4; iv. 3, 13, v. 70, 4; vii. 56, 16; 61, 5; x. 88, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202548">↑</a> viii. 9, 25; x. 2, 32; 7, 38; 8, 43; xi. 2, 24, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202548">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 262.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202548">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> and Geldner's full discussion, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 126-143.</li></ol></div>

यक्षु|యక్షు|ಯಕ್ಷು|yakṣu|yakShu|yaxu|_vei_120@2549|_vei2_pn182
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2548">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2550">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn181">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn182">2:182</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn183">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yakṣu</b> is mentioned, once in the singular and once in the plural, in the hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202549"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202549">1</a></sup> which celebrates <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a></b> battle with the ten kings. Who they were and what part they played in that conflict is quite uncertain. They seem, from the wording of the text, to have taken part in two conflicts, as Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202549"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202549">2</a></sup> says--one on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परुष्णी">paruṣṇī</a></b> (Ravi), and one on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a></b> (Jumna)--with the aid of the <b>Ajas</b> and <b>Śigrus,</b> under the leadership of <b>Bḥeda.</b> It is, however, at least possible that in the former passage <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a></b> should be read for <a class="vei_120a" href="यक्षु">yakṣu</a>, or, at any rate, <a class="vei_120a" href="यक्षु">yakṣu</a> be deemed a contemptuous substitute of the name of a possibly un-Āryan or unimportant tribe (as their allies, the Ajas and Śigrus, clearly were) for the name of the certainly famous Yadus, as is suggested by Hopkins.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202549"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202549">3</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202549"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202549">↑</a> vii. 18, 6. 19.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202549"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202549">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 126, 127.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202549"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202549">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 259 <i>et seq.</i> It is not clear however, whether Hopkins thinks that the Yadus are alluded to, but it seems probable.</li></ol></div>

यक्ष्म|యక్ష్మ|ಯಕ್ಷ್ಮ|yakṣma|yakShma|yaxma|_vei_120@2550|_vei2_pn182
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2549">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2551">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn181">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn182">2:182</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn183">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yakṣma</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202550"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202550">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202550"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202550">2</a></sup> frequently denotes ‘illness,’ in general, perhaps as rendering the body emaciated. A hundred kinds of <a class="vei_120a" href="यक्ष्म">yakṣma</a> are referred to in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202550"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202550">3</a></sup> and <i>a-<a class="vei_120a" href="यक्ष्म">yakṣma</a></i> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202550"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202550">4</a></sup>  denotes ‘free from disease.’ In the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202550"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202550">5</a></sup> an account is given of the origin of <a class="vei_120a" href="यक्ष्म">yakṣma</a>, which is distinguished as of three kinds--<b><a class="vei_120a" href="राज-यक्ष्म">rāja-yakṣma</a>,</b> ‘royal <a class="vei_120a" href="यक्ष्म">yakṣma</a>,’ <a class="vei_120a" href="पाप-यक्ष्म">pāpa-yakṣma</a>, ‘evil <a class="vei_120a" href="यक्ष्म">yakṣma</a>,’ and <b>Jāyenya,</b> most probably ‘syphilis.’ The second of the series is elsewhere unknown, and can hardly be defined, for it merely means ‘serious or deadly disease.’ <i>Cf.</i> also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अज्ञातयक्ष्म">ajñātayakṣma</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202550"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202550">↑</a> i. 122, 9; x. 85, 31; 97, 11. 12; 137, 4; 163, 1-6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202550"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202550">↑</a> ii. 10, 5. 6; iii. 31, 1; v. 4, 9; 30, 6; viii. 7, 2; ix. 8, 3. 7. 10; xii. 2, 1. 2; 4, 8; xix. 36, 1; 38, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202550"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202550">↑</a> xii. 97.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202550"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202550">↑</a> xvii. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202550"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202550">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 5, 2; 5, 6, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 3; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 7; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 3, 9.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 375 <i>et seq.;</i> Grohmann, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 400; Bloomfield, <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 60; Jolly, <i>Medicin,</i> 89.</li></ol></div>

यजत|యజత|ಯಜತ|yajata|_vei_120@2551|_vei2_pn183
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2550">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2552">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn182">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn183">2:183</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn184">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yajata</b> occurs in a hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202551"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202551">1</a></sup> where he is apparently a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> or a sacrificer.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202551"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202551">↑</a> v. 44, 10, 11. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 138.</li></ol></div>

यजुर्-वेद|यजुर्वेद|यजुर्|वेद|యజుర్-వేద|యజుర్వేద|యజుర్|వేద|ಯಜುರ್-ವೇದ|ಯಜುರ್ವೇದ|ಯಜುರ್|ವೇದ|yajur-veda|yajurveda|yajur|veda|_vei_120@2552|_vei2_pn183
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2551">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2553">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn182">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn183">2:183</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn184">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yajur-veda,</b> the ‘Veda of the sacrificial utterance’ (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="यजुस्">yajus</a></b>), is mentioned frequently in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202552">1</a></sup> and Upaniṣads.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202552">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202552">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 12, 9, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 32, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 8, 3; xii. 3, 4, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202552"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202552">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 2, 3, 5; Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, viii. 3, 8; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 5, 5; ii. 4. 10; iv. 1, 2; 5, 11; Chāndogya Upar <a class="vei_120a" href="षद्">ṣad</a>, i. 3, 7; iii. 2, 1. 2; 15, 7; vii. 2, 4; 2, 1; 7, 1; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="स्र">sra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 7, 2; Sāṅkhāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="स्र">sra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 2, 6, etc.</li></ol></div>

यजुस्|यजु|యజుస్|యజు|ಯಜುಸ್|ಯಜು|yajus|yaju|_vei_120@2553|_vei2_pn183
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2552">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2554">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn182">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn183">2:183</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn184">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yajus</b> is repeatedly distinguished from the Ṛc and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a></b> in Vedic literature.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202553"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202553">1</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुस्">yajus</a> is the utterance accompanying the sacrifice, and may have the form of verse or prose, the tern covering both. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202553"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202553">↑</a> Rv. x. 90, 9; Av. v. 26, 1; ix. 6, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 3, 1; 9, 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, i. 30; iv. 1; xix. 28; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 29, 21; viii. 13, 2; Śatapatha Brahmaṇa, i. 2, 1, 7; vi. 5, 1, 2; 3, 4, etc. In the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 33, there is a reference to the <i>śuklāni Yajūṃṣi,</i> ‘white or pure <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुस्">yajus</a>,’ as promulgated by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वाजसनेय">vājasaneya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a>,</b> whence the Vājasaneyi Saṃhītā is popularly known as the ‘White <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.’ The theory that this is due to the fact that in the Vājasaneyi the <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> parts of the text are not accompanied by <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> passages is, though accepted by Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 103, 104; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, xxvii, and others, now to be abandoned. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, v. 10, the expression <i>śukrayajāṃṣi</i> seems to refer to books iv. and v. of that text. <i>Cf.</i> also Winternitz. <i>Geschichte der indischen Literatur,</i> 1, 149, n.</li></ol></div>

यज्ञ-गाथा|यज्ञगाथा|यज्ञ|गाथा|యజ్ఞ-గాథా|యజ్ఞగాథా|యజ్ఞ|గాథా|ಯಜ್ಞ-ಗಾಥಾ|ಯಜ್ಞಗಾಥಾ|ಯಜ್ಞ|ಗಾಥಾ|yajña-gāthā|yajñagāthā|yajña|gāthā|yaj~na-gaathaa|yaj~nagaathaa|yaj~na|gaathaa|_vei_120@2554|_vei2_pn184
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2553">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2555">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn183">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn184">2:184</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn185">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yajña-gāthā</b> denotes a verse (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a></b>) containing a maxim as to the sacrifice of any kind or sort,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202554"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202554">1</a></sup> or, as it is expressed in the Mahābhārata,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202554"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202554">2</a></sup> a ‘verse sung regarding the sacrifice’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="यज्ञ">yajña</a>-gītā</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202554"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202554">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 43, 5; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 12, 6; Śānkhāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 8, 26; 9, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202554"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202554">↑</a> xii. 791. 2316.</li></ol></div>

यज्ञ-वचस् राजस्तम्बायन|यज्ञवचस् राजस्तम्बायन|यज्ञ|वचस् राजस्तम्बायन|यज्ञ-वचस् राजस्तंबायन|यज्ञवचस् राजस्तंबायन|वचस् राजस्तंबायन|యజ్ఞ-వచస్ రాజస్తమ్బాయన|యజ్ఞవచస్ రాజస్తమ్బాయన|యజ్ఞ|వచస్ రాజస్తమ్బాయన|యజ్ఞ-వచస్ రాజస్తంబాయన|యజ్ఞవచస్ రాజస్తంబాయన|వచస్ రాజస్తంబాయన|ಯಜ್ಞ-ವಚಸ್ ರಾಜಸ್ತಮ್ಬಾಯನ|ಯಜ್ಞವಚಸ್ ರಾಜಸ್ತಮ್ಬಾಯನ|ಯಜ್ಞ|ವಚಸ್ ರಾಜಸ್ತಮ್ಬಾಯನ|ಯಜ್ಞ-ವಚಸ್ ರಾಜಸ್ತಂಬಾಯನ|ಯಜ್ಞವಚಸ್ ರಾಜಸ್ತಂಬಾಯನ|ವಚಸ್ ರಾಜಸ್ತಂಬಾಯನ|yajña-vacas rājastambāyana|yajñavacas rājastambāyana|yajña|vacas rājastambāyana|yaj~na-vachas raajastambaayana|yaj~navachas raajastambaayana|yaj~na|vachas raajastambaayana|_vei_120@2555|_vei2_pn184
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2554">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2556">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn183">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn184">2:184</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn185">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yajña-<a class="vei_120a" href="वचस्">vacas</a> Rājastambāyana,</b> ‘descendant of Rājastamba,’ is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर कावषेय">tura kāvaṣeya</a>,</b> according to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202555"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202555">1</a></sup> He is also mentioned in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202555"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202555">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202555"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202555">↑</a> x. 4, 2, 1; 6, 5, 9; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 5, 4 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202555"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202555">↑</a> iii. 10, 3; iv. 8, 2.</li></ol></div>

यज्ञ-सेन|यज्ञसेन|यज्ञ|सेन|యజ్ఞ-సేన|యజ్ఞసేన|యజ్ఞ|సేన|ಯಜ್ಞ-ಸೇನ|ಯಜ್ಞಸೇನ|ಯಜ್ಞ|ಸೇನ|yajña-sena|yajñasena|yajña|sena|yaj~na-sena|yaj~nasena|yaj~na|_vei_120@2556|_vei2_pn184
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2555">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2557">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn183">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn184">2:184</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn185">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yajña-sena</b> is the name of a teacher with the patronymic <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चैत्र">caitra</a></b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चैत्रियायण">caitriyāyaṇa</a></b> mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202556"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202556">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202556"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202556">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 3, 8, 1 (<a class="vei_120a" href="चैत्रियायण">caitriyāyaṇa</a>); <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxi. 4 (<a class="vei_120a" href="चैत्र">caitra</a>).</li></ol></div>

यज्ञेषु|యజ్ఞేషు|ಯಜ್ಞೇಷು|yajñeṣu|yaj~neShu|_vei_120@2557|_vei2_pn184
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2556">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2558">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn183">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn184">2:184</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn185">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yajñeṣu</b> is the name of a man in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202557"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202557">1</a></sup> He was made to prosper by his priest <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मात्स्य">mātsya</a>,</b> who knew the exact moment of sacrificing.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202557"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202557">↑</a> i. 5, 2, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 306.</li></ol></div>

यज्ञोपवीत|యజ్ఞోపవీత|ಯಜ್ಞೋಪವೀತ|yajñopavīta|yaj~nopaviita|yaj~nopaveeta|_vei_120@2558|_vei2_pn184
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2557">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2559">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn183">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn184">2:184</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn185">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yajñopavīta</b> denotes the ‘wearing of the Brahminical thread over the left shoulder at the sacrifice,’ and is mentioned as early as the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202558">1</a></sup> Tilak,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202558">2</a></sup> however, urges that it was not originally a thread that was worn, but a garment of cloth (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="वासस्">vāsas</a></b>) or of deerskin (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अजिन">ajina</a></b>). This seems quite probable. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202558">↑</a> iii. 10, 9, 12. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 11, 1; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 2, 1; 6, 1, 12; and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राचीनावीत">prācīnāvīta</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202558">↑</a> <i>Orion,</i> 145 <i>et seq.,</i> quoting <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 1, and, the view of the Mīmāmsists, Jaiminīyanyāyamālāvistara, iii. 4, 1. This view is not prejudiced by the quite implausible conjectures as to Orion's belt with which it is combined. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books o the East,</i> 12, 361, 424.</li></ol></div>

यति|యతి|ಯತಿ|yati|_vei_120@2559|_vei2_pn185
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2558">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2560">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn184">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn185">2:185</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn186">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yati</b> is the name of an ancient clan which is connected with the <b>Bhṛgus</b> in two passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202559"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202559">1</a></sup> where the Yatis certainly seem to be real persons. In another hymn,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202559"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202559">2</a></sup> however, they already appear as almost mythical. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202559"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202559">3</a></sup> and elsewhere,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202559"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202559">4</a></sup> the Yatis are a race whom <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, in an evil moment, gave over to the hyænas (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="सालावृक">sālāvṛka</a></b>): exactly what is referred to is uncertain. <a class="vei_120a" href="यति">yati</a> is mentioned with <a class="vei_120a" href="भृगु">bhṛgu</a> in a verse of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202559"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202559">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202559"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202559">↑</a> viii. 3, 9; 6, 18; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 465, n.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202559"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202559">↑</a> x. 72, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202559"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202559">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 4, 9, 2; vi. 2, 7, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, viii. 5; xi. 10; xxv. 6; xxxvi. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 1, 4; xiii. 4, 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 28, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 3, etc.; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 437 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202559"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202559">↑</a> ii. 304. In the parallel passage, Av. ii. 5, 3, the reading <i>Yatīr</i> is found, possibly an error for <i>Yatīn,</i> or merely a blunder. <i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 5, 49, n. 92; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 44; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, vi. 3, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 146.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202559"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202559">↑</a> ii. 304. In the parallel passage, Av. ii. 5, 3, the reading <i>Yatīr</i> is found, possibly an error for <i>Yatīn,</i> or merely a blunder. <i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 5, 49, n. 92; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 44; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, vi. 3, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 146.</li></ol></div>

यदु|యదు|ಯದು|yadu|_vei_120@2560|_vei2_pn185
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2559">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2561">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn184">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn185">2:185</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn186">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yadu</b> is the name of a tribe and of the king of the tribe. They are mentioned repeatedly in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202560"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202560">1</a></sup> normally in conjunction with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a>.</b> They seem to have taken part in the great battle against <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>:</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202560"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202560">2</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> kings seem to have escaped with their lives, while the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अनु">anu</a></b> and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="द्रुह्यु">druhyu</a></b> kings perished. This is at least the most natural explanation of several passages,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202560"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202560">3</a></sup> though these passages possibly refer to a successful raid across the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरयु">sarayu</a>,</b> and a defeat of two princes, Arṇa and <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्ररथ">citraratha</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202560"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202560">4</a></sup> That <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> was the <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a> king, as Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202560"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202560">5</a></sup> holds, is most improbable.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202560"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202560">↑</a> i. 36, 18; 54, 6; 174, 9; iv. 30, 17; v. 31, 8; vi. 45, 1; viii. 4, 7; 7, 18; 9, 14; 10, 5; 45, 27; ix. 61, 2; x. 49, 8; plural, i. 108, 8. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a>,</b> and Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 258 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202560"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202560">↑</a> Whether <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a> should be read in Rv. vii. 18, 6, or not, the Yadus seem to be meant. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यक्षु">yakṣu</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202560"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202560">↑</a> Rv. i. 174, 9; iv. 30, 17; v. 31, 3; vi. 20, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202560"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202560">↑</a> Rv. iv. 30, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202560"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202560">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 122, 124; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 205; 5, 142; Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 37.</li></ol></div>

यन्तृ|యన్తృ|ಯನ್ತೃ|yantṛ|yantR|yantRi|yantRu|yantRRi|_vei_120@2561|_vei2_pn185
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2560">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2562">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn184">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn185">2:185</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn186">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yantṛ</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202561"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202561">1</a></sup> and in the Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202561"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202561">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘driver’ of horses or ‘charioteer.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202561"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202561">↑</a> i. 162, 19; x. 22, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202561"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202561">↑</a> Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 6, 29, etc.</li></ol></div>

यम|యమ|ಯಮ|yama|_vei_120@2562|_vei2_pn186
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2561">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2563">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn185">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn186">2:186</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn187">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yama</b> denotes ‘twins,’ the birth of which is frequently alluded to in Vedic literature.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202562"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202562">1</a></sup> Twins of different sex seem to be indicated by the expression <i>yamau mithunau.</i><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202562"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202562">2</a></sup> There are traces of the belief--widely spread among negro and other races--that twins are uncanny and of evil omen,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202562"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202562">3</a></sup> but there are also vestiges of the opposite opinion, that twins are lucky.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202562"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202562">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202562"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202562">↑</a> Rv. i. 66, 4; 164, 15; ii. 39, 2; iii. 39, 3; v. 57, 4; vi. 59, 2; x. 13, 2; 117, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvi. 4, 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202562"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202562">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xiii. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, xii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202562"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202562">↑</a> Av. iii. 28; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 9, 8; Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxv. 4, 35; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 4, 14, etc. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यमसू">yamasū</a>; <a class="vei_120a" href="युक्ताश्व">yuktāśva</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202562"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202562">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 1, 1, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxiv. 12, 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 1, 8, and <i>cf.</i> Rv. iii. 39, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 298300; <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 314, n.</li></ol></div>

यम-नक्षत्र|यमनक्षत्र|यम|नक्षत्र|యమ-నక్షత్ర|యమనక్షత్ర|యమ|నక్షత్ర|ಯಮ-ನಕ್ಷತ್ರ|ಯಮನಕ್ಷತ್ರ|ಯಮ|ನಕ್ಷತ್ರ|yama-nakṣatra|yamanakṣatra|yama|nakṣatra|yama-nakShatra|yamanakShatra|nakShatra|yama-naxatra|yamanaxatra|naxatra|_vei_120@2563|_vei2_pn186
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2562">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2564">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn185">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn186">2:186</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn187">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yama-nakṣatra.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p></div>

यम-सू|यमसू|यम|యమ-సూ|యమసూ|యమ|ಯಮ-ಸೂ|ಯಮಸೂ|ಯಮ|yama-sū|yamasū|yama|yama-suu|yamasuu|yama-soo|yamasoo|_vei_120@2564|_vei2_pn186
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2563">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2565">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn185">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn186">2:186</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn187">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yama-sū,</b> a ‘bearer of twins,’ is one of the victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202564"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202564">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202564"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202564">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 11, 1.</li></ol></div>

यमुना|యమునా|ಯಮುನಾ|yamunā|yamunaa|_vei_120@2565|_vei2_pn186
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2564">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2566">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn185">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn186">2:186</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn187">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yamunā,</b> ‘twin,’ the name of a river, so called as running parallel with the Ganges, is mentioned thrice in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202565">1</a></sup> and not rarely later. According to the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202565">2</a></sup> the <b>Tṛtsus</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a></b> won a great victory against their foes on the <a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a>; there is no reason<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202565">3</a></sup> whatever to accept Hopkins'<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202565">4</a></sup> view that the <a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a> here was another name of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परुष्णी">paruṣṇī</a></b> (Ravi). In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202565">5</a></sup> the salve (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="आञ्जन">āñjana</a></b>) of the <a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a> (<i>Yāmuna</i>) is mentioned along with that of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रिककुद्">trikakud</a></b> (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रैककुद">traikakuda</a></i>) as of value. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202565">6</a></sup> and the Śatapatha<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202565">7</a></sup> Brāhmaṇas the <b>Bharatas</b> are famed as victorious on the <a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a>. Other Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202565">8</a></sup>  also mention this river. In the Mantrapāṭha<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202565">9</a></sup> the <b>Sālvas</b> are spoken of as dwelling on its banks.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202565">↑</a> v. 52, 17; vii. 18, 19; x. 75, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202565">↑</a> vii. 18, 19. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202565">↑</a> The Tṛtsus’ territory lay between the <a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a> and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a></b> on the east and the west respectively.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202565">↑</a> <i>India, Old and New,</i> 52.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202565">↑</a> iv. 9, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202565">↑</a> viii. 23.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202565">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202565">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 4, 11 (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पारावत">pārāvata</a></b>); xxv. 10, 24; 13, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiii. 29, 25. 33; Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxiv. 6, 10. 39; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 19, 9, 10; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 6, 28, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202565">↑</a> ii. 11, 12.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 5; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 323.</li></ol></div>

ययाति|యయాతి|ಯಯಾತಿ|yayāti|yayaati|_vei_120@2566|_vei2_pn187
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2565">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2567">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn186">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn187">2:187</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn188">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yayāti</b> is mentioned twice in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202566">1</a></sup> once as an ancient sacrificer, and once as Nahuṣya, ‘descendant of <b>Nahuṣa,</b>’ apparently a king. There is no trace whatever of his connexion with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पूरु">pūru</a>,</b> as in the Epic,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202566">2</a></sup> the tradition of which must be deemed to be inaccurate.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202566">↑</a> i. 31, 17; x. 63, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202566">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 147; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts</i> 12, 232.</li></ol></div>

यव|यव|యవ_౧|యవ|ಯವ_೧|ಯವ|yava_1|yava|_vei_120@2567|_vei2_pn187
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2566">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2568">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn186">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn187">2:187</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn188">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Yava</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202567">1</a></sup> appears to be a generic term for any sort of ‘grain,’ and not merely ‘barley.’ The latter sense is probably found in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202567">2</a></sup> and is regular later. The barley harvest came after spring,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202567">3</a></sup> in the summer.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202567">4</a></sup> That barley was cultivated in the period of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202567">5</a></sup> is not certain, but on the whole very probable.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202567">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202567">↑</a> i. 23, 15; 66, 3; 117, 21; 135, 8; 176, 2; ii. 5, 6; 14, 11; v. 85, 3; vii. 3, 4; viii. 2, 3; 22, 6; 63. 9; 78, 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202567">↑</a> ii. 8, 3; vi. 30, 1; 50. 1. 2; 91, 1; 141, 2; 142, 1. 2; viii. 7, 20; ix. 1, 22; 6, 14; xii. 1, 42; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 10, 3; 4, 10, 5; vii. 2, 10, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxv. 10; xxvi. 5; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, v. 26; xviii. 12; xxiii. 30; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 4, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 4, 20: ii. 5, 2, 1; iii. 6, 1, 9. 10; iv. 2, 1, 11; xii. 7, 2, 9; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 14, 3, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 12.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202567">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202567">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 2, 10, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202567">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 86, n.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202567">↑</a> Sowing (<i>vap</i>) grain is referred to in Rv. i. 117, 21; ripening of grain in 135, 8; ploughing (<i>kṛṣ</i>) in i. 176, 2. Grain rejoicing in rain is alluded to in ii. 5, 6. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृषि">kṛṣi</a>.</b></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 282; Kuhn, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 355, 356; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 238, 239.</li></ol></div>

यव|यव|యవ_౨|యవ|ಯವ_೨|ಯವ|yava_2|yava|_vei_120@2568|_vei2_pn187
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2567">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2569">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn186">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn187">2:187</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn188">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Yava.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></p></div>

यवस|యవస|ಯವಸ|yavasa|_vei_120@2569|_vei2_pn187
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2568">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2570">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn186">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn187">2:187</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn188">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yavasa</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202569"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202569">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202569"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202569">2</a></sup> denotes the ‘grass’ on which animals feed, and which is burned by the forest fire.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202569"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202569">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202569"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202569">↑</a> i. 38, 5; 91, 13; iii. 45, 3; iv. 41, 10; 42, 5; vii. 18, 10; 87, 2; 93, 2; 102, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202569"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202569">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxi. 43, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202569"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202569">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>, <i>yavasād,</i> in Rv. i. 94, 11.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 47; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 87.</li></ol></div>

यवागू|యవాగూ|ಯವಾಗೂ|yavāgū|yavaaguu|yavaagoo|_vei_120@2570|_vei2_pn188
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2569">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2571">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn187">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn188">2:188</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn189">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yavāgū</b> means ‘barley-gruel,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202570"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202570">1</a></sup> but is also used of weak decoctions of other kinds of grain.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202570"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202570">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202570"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202570">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 5, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 8, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 13, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202570"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202570">↑</a> Of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जर्तिल">jartila</a></b> and <b>Gvīdhuka,</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 4, 3, 2.</li></ol></div>

यवाशिर्|యవాశిర్|ಯವಾಶಿರ್|yavāśir|yavaashir|_vei_120@2571|_vei2_pn188
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2570">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2572">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn187">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn188">2:188</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn189">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yavāśir</b> is used in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202571"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202571">1</a></sup> as an epithet of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>,</b> meaning ‘mixed with grain.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202571"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202571">↑</a> i. 187, 9; ii. 22, 1; iii. 42, 7; viii. 94, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 227; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 279.</li></ol></div>

यवाष|యవాష|ಯವಾಷ|yavāṣa|yavaaSha|_vei_120@2572|_vei2_pn188
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2571">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2573">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn187">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn188">2:188</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn189">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yavāṣa.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="येवाष">yevāṣa</a>.</b></p></div>

यव्य|యవ్య|ಯವ್ಯ|yavya|_vei_120@2573|_vei2_pn188
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2572">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2574">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn187">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn188">2:188</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn189">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yavya</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 7, 2, 46) denotes ‘month’ (lit., ‘containing a first half,’ see 2. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यव">yava</a></b>).</p></div>

यव्यावती|यव्यावति|యవ్యావతీ|యవ్యావతి|ಯವ್ಯಾವತೀ|ಯವ್ಯಾವತಿ|yavyāvatī|yavyāvati|yavyaavatii|yavyaavati|yavyaavatee|_vei_120@2574|_vei2_pn188
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2573">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2575">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn187">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn188">2:188</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn189">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yavyāvatī</b> is the name of a river in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202574"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202574">1</a></sup> and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202574"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202574">2</a></sup> Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202574"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202574">3</a></sup> thinks that the river is one in Iran, the Djob (Zhobe), near the Iryāb (Haliāb), but there is no reason to accept this identification.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202574"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202574">↑</a> vi. 27, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202574"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202574">↑</a> xxv. 7, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202574"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202574">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 268, n. 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 18, 19; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 204; Kaegi, <i>Rigveda,</i> n. 338; Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 168, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

यशस्विन् जयन्त लौहित्य|యశస్విన్ జయన్త లౌహిత్య|ಯಶಸ್ವಿನ್ ಜಯನ್ತ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|yaśasvin jayanta lauhitya|yashasvin jayanta lauhitya|_vei_120@2575|_vei2_pn188
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2574">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2576">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn187">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn188">2:188</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn189">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yaśasvin <a class="vei_120a" href="जयन्त">jayanta</a> Lauhitya</b> (‘descendant of Lohita’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b>Kṛṣṇarāta Triveda <a class="vei_120a" href="लौहित्य">lauhitya</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 42, 1).</p></div>

यष्टि|యష్టి|ಯಷ್ಟಿ|yaṣṭi|yaShTi|_vei_120@2576|_vei2_pn188
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2575">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2577">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn187">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn188">2:188</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn189">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yaṣṭi,</b> ‘staff,’ is mentioned in the latest parts of the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202576"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202576">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202576"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202576">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 2, 17 of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वेणु">veṇu</a>,</i> ‘bamboo’); Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 7; Kauṣitāki Upaniṣap iv. 19. etc.</li></ol></div>

यस्क|యస్క|ಯಸ್ಕ|yaska|_vei_120@2577|_vei2_pn189
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2576">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2578">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn188">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn189">2:189</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn190">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yaska</b> is the name of a man. The Yaskas, descendants of Girikṣit (<i>Gairikṣitāḥ</i>) are mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202577"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202577">1</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202577"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202577">↑</a> xiii. 12. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 475 <i>et seq.;</i> 8, 245 <i>et seq.; Indian Literature,</i> 41, n. 30.</li></ol></div>

याज्ञ-तुर|याज्ञतुर|याज्ञ|तुर|యాజ్ఞ-తుర|యాజ్ఞతుర|యాజ్ఞ|తుర|ಯಾಜ್ಞ-ತುರ|ಯಾಜ್ಞತುರ|ಯಾಜ್ಞ|ತುರ|yājña-tura|yājñatura|yājña|tura|yaaj~na-tura|yaaj~natura|yaaj~na|_vei_120@2578|_vei2_pn189
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2577">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2579">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn188">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn189">2:189</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn190">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yājña-tura,</b> ‘descendant of Yajñatura,’ is the patronymic of 2. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषभ">ṛṣabha</a></b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202578"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202578">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202578"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202578">↑</a> xii. 8, 3, 7; xiii. 5, 4, 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 9, 8, 10.</li></ol></div>

याज्ञ-वल्क्य|याज्ञवल्क्य|याज्ञ|वल्क्य|యాజ్ఞ-వల్క్య|యాజ్ఞవల్క్య|యాజ్ఞ|వల్క్య|ಯಾಜ್ಞ-ವಲ್ಕ್ಯ|ಯಾಜ್ಞವಲ್ಕ್ಯ|ಯಾಜ್ಞ|ವಲ್ಕ್ಯ|yājña-valkya|yājñavalkya|yājña|valkya|yaaj~na-valkya|yaaj~navalkya|yaaj~na|_vei_120@2579|_vei2_pn189
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2578">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2580">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn188">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn189">2:189</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn190">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yājña-valkya,</b> ‘descendant of Yajñavalkya,’ is repeatedly mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202579">1</a></sup> as an authority on questions of ritual. He is, however, also given as an authority on questions of philosophy in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202579">2</a></sup> but Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202579">3</a></sup> is, no doubt, right in thinking that no possible importance can be attached to the mention of <a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a> in the latter capacity. He is said to have been a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उद्दालक आरुणि">uddālaka āruṇi</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202579">4</a></sup> whom he opposed successfully in a dispute.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202579">5</a></sup> His two wives, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मैत्रेयी">maitreyī</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कात्यायनी">kātyāyanī</a>,</b> are mentioned in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202579">6</a></sup> which concludes<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202579">7</a></sup> with a passage ascribing to <a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वाजसनेय">vājasaneya</a> the ‘white Yajus’ (<i>śuklāni yajūṃṣi</i>). It is remarkable that <a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a> is never mentioned in any other Vedic text outside the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> except the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202579">8</a></sup> where, however, both reference are merely transcripts from the Śatapatha.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202579">9</a></sup> It has  been supposed by Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202579">10</a></sup> and others that <a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a> belonged to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>,</b> but despite the legend of Janaka's patronage of him, his association with <b>Uddālaka,</b> the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a>,</b> renders this doubtful.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202579">↑</a> i. 1, 1, 9; 3, 1, 21. 26; 9, 3, 16; ii. 3, 1, 21; 4, 3, 2; 5, 1, 2 (where he is said to be in contradiction with the Rigveda); iii. 1, 1, 4; 2, 21; 3, 10; 8, 2, 24 (cursed by a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चरक">caraka</a></b> teacher); iv. 2, 1, 7; 6, 1, 10; 8, 7, etc. There are no references to <a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a> in books v-ix, which, on the contrary, owe their doctrine to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर कावषेय">tura kāvaṣeya</a></b> and <a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्डिल्य">śāṇḍilya</a>; but the fame of <a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a> revives in books x-xiv--<i>e.g.,</i> xi. 3, 1, 2; 4, 2, 17; 3, 20; 6, 2, 1; 3, 1; xii. 4, 1, 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202579">↑</a> iii. 1, 2 <i>et seq.;</i> 2, 10 <i>et seq.;</i> 3, 1; 4, 1; 5, 1; 6, 1; 7, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202579">↑</a> <i>Buddha,</i>^5 34, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202579">↑</a> vi. 4, 33 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 5, 4 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202579">↑</a> iii. 7, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202579">↑</a> ii. 4, 1; iv. 5, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202579">↑</a> vi. 4, 33 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 5, 4 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202579">↑</a> ix. 7; xiii. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202579">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 132, n. *; Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 374.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202579">↑</a> <i>Buddha,</i>^5 34, n. 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 120 <i>et seq.; Indische Studien,</i> 1, 173; 13, 265269; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, xxx <i>et seq.;</i> von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 188.</li></ol></div>

याज्या|యాజ్యా|ಯಾಜ್ಯಾ|yājyā|yaajyaa|_vei_120@2580|_vei2_pn190
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2579">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2581">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn189">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn190">2:190</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn191">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yājyā</b> (scil. ṛc, ‘verse’) denotes the words uttered at the moment of offering the sacrifice, ‘consecrating sacrificial formula,’ in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202580"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202580">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202580"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202580">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202580"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202580">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 5, 2, 1; 6, 10, 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 20; xx. 12, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202580"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202580">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 8; 11, 10; ii. 13, 2; 26, 3. 5. 6; 40, 8; iii. 32, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 2, 19; iii. 4, 4, 2; vii. 2, 7, 11, etc.</li></ol></div>

यातु-धान|यातुधान|यातु|धान|యాతు-ధాన|యాతుధాన|యాతు|ధాన|ಯಾತು-ಧಾನ|ಯಾತುಧಾನ|ಯಾತು|ಧಾನ|yātu-dhāna|yātudhāna|yātu|dhāna|yaatu-dhaana|yaatudhaana|yaatu|dhaana|_vei_120@2581|_vei2_pn190
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2580">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2582">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn189">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn190">2:190</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn191">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yātu-dhāna</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202581"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202581">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202581"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202581">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘sorcerer,’ ‘wizard,’ or ‘magician.’ The sense of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202581"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202581">3</a></sup> is clearly unfavourable to sorcery. The feminine, Yātudhānī, is also found in the Rigveda and later.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202581"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202581">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202581"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202581">↑</a> i. 35, 10; x. 87, 2. 3. 7. 10; 120, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202581"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202581">↑</a> Av. i. 7, 1; iv. 3, 4; vi. 13, 3; 32, 2; vii. 70, 2; xix. 46, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 14; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiii. 7; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 4, 1, 29, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202581"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202581">↑</a> vii. 104, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202581"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202581">↑</a> Rv. i. 191, 8; x. 118, 8; Av. i. 28, 24; ii. 14, 3; iv. 9, 9; 18, 17; xix. 37, 8, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 26, 65 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

यातु-विद्|यातुविद्|यातु|विद्|యాతు-విద్|యాతువిద్|యాతు|విద్|ಯಾತು-ವಿದ್|ಯಾತುವಿದ್|ಯಾತು|ವಿದ್|yātu-vid|yātuvid|yātu|vid|yaatu-vid|yaatuvid|yaatu|_vei_120@2582|_vei2_pn190
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2581">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2583">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn189">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn190">2:190</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn191">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yātu-vid,</b> denoting in the plural ‘those who know sorcery,’ designates the Atharvaveda in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202582"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202582">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202582"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202582">↑</a> x. 5, 2, 20. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> xxii.; <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 1. 8, 9, 23.</li></ol></div>

याद्व|యాద్వ|ಯಾದ್ವ|yādva|yaadva|_vei_120@2583|_vei2_pn190
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2582">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2584">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn189">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn190">2:190</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn191">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yādva,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a>,</b>’ is used of the <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a> prince in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202583">1</a></sup> while the largesse of the Yādvas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202583">2</a></sup> is alluded to elsewhere. In another passage a beast (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पशु">paśu</a></i>) of the Yadus or Yādvas is mentioned.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202583">3</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202583">↑</a> vii. 19, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202583">↑</a> Rv. viii. 6, 46. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 5, 142.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202583">↑</a> Rv. viii. 1, 31.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 3; <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 37.</li></ol></div>

यान|యాన|ಯಾನ|yāna|yaana|_vei_120@2584|_vei2_pn191
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2583">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2585">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn190">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn191">2:191</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn192">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yāna</b> denotes ‘vehicle’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202584"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202584">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202584"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202584">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202584"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202584">↑</a> iv. 43, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202584"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202584">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 3, 7; Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 3, 10; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, viii. 12, 3, etc.</li></ol></div>

याम|యామ|ಯಾಮ|yāma|yaama|_vei_120@2585|_vei2_pn191
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2584">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2586">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn190">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn191">2:191</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn192">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yāma,</b> used in the plural, denotes in one passage of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202585"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202585">1</a></sup> according to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202585"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202585">2</a></sup> the planets among which the sun (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="भग">bhaga</a></i>) wanders. But both Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202585"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202585">3</a></sup> and Whitney<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202585"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202585">4</a></sup> accept the sense--the regular one in the later language--of ‘night watches.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202585"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202585">↑</a> vi. 21, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202585"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202585">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 1<i>d.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202585"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202585">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 30.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202585"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202585">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 396.</li></ol></div>

यामन्|యామన్|ಯಾಮನ್|yāman|yaaman|_vei_120@2586|_vei2_pn191
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2585">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2587">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn190">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn191">2:191</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn192">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yāman</b> denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202586"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202586">1</a></sup> a ‘march’ or ‘expedition’ in war.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202586"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202586">↑</a> iv. 24. 2; vii. 66, 5; 85, 1; ix. 64, 10; x. 78, 6; 80, 5.</li></ol></div>

यायावर|యాయావర|ಯಾಯಾವರ|yāyāvara|yaayaavara|_vei_120@2587|_vei2_pn191
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2586">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2588">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn190">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn191">2:191</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn192">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yāyāvara</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202587"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202587">1</a></sup> denotes a person of no fixed abode in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202587"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202587">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202587"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202587">↑</a> Literally, ‘wandering about,’ from the intensive of <i>yā,</i> ‘to go.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202587"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202587">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 2, 1, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xix. 12.</li></ol></div>

याव|యావ|ಯಾವ|yāva|yaava|_vei_120@2588|_vei2_pn191
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2587">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2589">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn190">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn191">2:191</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn192">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yāva.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></p></div>

यास्क|యాస్క|ಯಾಸ್ಕ|yāska|yaaska|_vei_120@2589|_vei2_pn191
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2588">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2590">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn190">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn191">2:191</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn192">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yāska</b> (‘descendant of Yaska’) is mentioned in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202589">1</a></sup> as a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आसुरायण">āsurāyaṇa</a></b> and a teacher of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भारद्वाज">bhāradvāja</a>.</b> Whether <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>, author of the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202589">2</a></sup> was the same person, it is, of course, impossible to say.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202589">↑</a> ii. 5, 21; iv. 5, 27 (Mādhyaṃdina = ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>). <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 128.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202589"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202589">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a> Prātiśākhya, xvii. 25; Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 25, 26, etc.; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 17, 103; 3, 396; 8, 243, etc.; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 41, n. 30.</li></ol></div>

यु|యు|ಯು|yu|_vei_120@2590|_vei2_pn191
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2589">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2591">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn190">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn191">2:191</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn192">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yu,</b> appearing in the dual in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 7, 4, 10), seems to mean ‘yoke animals.’ </p></div>

युक्त|యుక్త|ಯುಕ್ತ|yukta|_vei_120@2591|_vei2_pn192
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2590">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2592">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn191">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn192">2:192</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn194">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yukta</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vi. 7, 4, 8; xii. 4, 1, 2) denotes a ‘yoke’ of oxen. <i>Cf.</i> 1. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="युग">yuga</a>.</b></p></div>

युक्ताश्व|యుక్తాశ్వ|ಯುಕ್ತಾಶ್ವ|yuktāśva|yuktaashva|_vei_120@2592|_vei2_pn192
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2591">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2593">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn191">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn192">2:192</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn194">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yuktāśva</b> is the name of a man who is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202592"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202592">1</a></sup> as the seer of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a>, or chant. He is said to have exposed a pair of twins,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202592"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202592">2</a></sup> but Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202592"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202592">3</a></sup> thinks that the reference is only to an exchange of children.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202592"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202592">↑</a> xi. 8, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202592"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202592">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 314, n., whose view is that of Sāyaṇa on the passage. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यम">yama</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202592"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202592">↑</a> <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 61, 62.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie.</i> 2, 160.</li></ol></div>

युग|युग|యుగ_౧|యుగ|ಯುಗ_೧|ಯುಗ|yuga_1|yuga|_vei_120@2593|_vei2_pn192
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2592">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2594">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn191">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn192">2:192</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn194">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Yuga</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202593"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202593">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202593"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202593">2</a></sup> denotes ‘yoke.’ <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202593"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202593">↑</a> i. 115, 2; 184, 3; ii. 39, 4; iii. 53, 17; viii. 80, 7; x. 60, 8; 101, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202593"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202593">↑</a> Av. iv. 1, 40; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 5, 1, 24. 34; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 1, 3, etc.</li></ol></div>

युग|युग|యుగ_౨|యుగ|ಯುಗ_೨|ಯುಗ|yuga_2|yuga|_vei_120@2594|_vei2_pn192
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2593">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2595">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn191">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn192">2:192</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn194">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Yuga</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202594">1</a></sup> frequently denotes a ‘generation’; but the expression <i>daśame yuge</i> applied to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दीर्घतमस्">dīrghatamas</a></b> in one passage<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202594">2</a></sup> must mean ‘tenth decade’ of life.</p><p>There is no reference in the older Vedic texts to the five-year cycle (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="संवत्सर">saṃvatsara</a></b>). The quotation from the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202594">3</a></sup> given in the St. Petersburg Dictionary, and by Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202594">4</a></sup> and others, is merely a citation from a modern text in the commentary on that work.</p><p>Nor do the older Vedic texts know of any series of Yugas or ages such as are usual later. In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202594">5</a></sup> there are mentioned in order a hundred years, an <i>ayuta</i> (10,000?), and then two, three, or four Yugas: the inference from this seems to be that a <a class="vei_120a" href="युग">yuga</a> means more than an <i>ayuta,</i> but is not very  certain. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202594">6</a></sup> adduces a passage from the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202594">7</a></sup> but the reference there, whatever it may be,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202594">8</a></sup> is certainly not to the four ages (<i>cf.</i> also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रियुग">triyuga</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202594">9</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202594">10</a></sup> recognizes long periods of time--<i>e.g.,</i> one of 100,000 years.</p><p>To the four ages, <a class="vei_120a" href="कलि">kali</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="द्वापर">dvāpara</a>, Tretā, and <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत">kṛta</a>, there is no certain reference in Vedic literature, though the names occur as the designations of throws at dice (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a></b>). In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202594">11</a></sup> the names occur, but it is not clear that the ages are really meant. Haug<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202594">12</a></sup> thought that the dice were meant: this view is at least as probable as the alternative explanation, which is accepted by Weber,<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202594">13</a></sup> Roth,<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1202594">14</a></sup> Wilson,<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1202594">15</a></sup> Max Müller,<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1202594">16</a></sup> and Muir.<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1202594">17</a></sup> Roth, indeed, believes that the verse is an interpolation; but in any case it must be remembered that the passage is from a late book of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>. Four ages--<a class="vei_120a" href="पुष्य">puṣya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="द्वापर">dvāpara</a>, Khārvā, and <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत">kṛta</a>--are mentioned in the late Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1202594">18</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="द्वापर">dvāpara</a> in the Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1202594">19</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202594">↑</a> <i>Yuge-yuge,</i> ‘in every age,’ i. 139, 8; iii. 26, 3; vi. 8, 5; 15, 8; 36, 5; ix. 94, 12; <i>uttarā yugāni,</i> ‘future ages,’ iii. 33, 8; x. 10, 10; <i>pūrvāṇi yugāni,</i> vii. 70, 4; <i>uttare yuge,</i> x. 72, 1, etc. In i. 92, 11; 103, 4; 115, 2; 124, 2; 144, 4, etc., the phrase ‘generations of men’ (<i>manṣyā, manuṣaḥ, janānām</i>) are referred to. See Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 45, 46.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202594">↑</a> i. 158, 6. Wilson, Translation, 2, 104, n., suggests that <i><a class="vei_120a" href="युग">yuga</a></i> here means a lustrum of five years; but the tenth decade is far more likely, as <a class="vei_120a" href="दीर्घतमस्">dīrghatamas</a> is said to be ‘aged’ (<i>jujurvān</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202594">↑</a> xvii. 13, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202594">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 368.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202594">↑</a> viii. 2, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202594">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 371.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202594">↑</a> viii. 101, 4 = Av. x. 8, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202594">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 1, 1, with Keith's note; Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 2, 253.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202594">↑</a> In Rv. x. 72, 2, <i>devānāṃ pūrvye yuge,</i> ‘in the earlier age of the gods,’ occurs.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202594">↑</a> iii. 12, 9, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Muir, 12, 42, n. 66.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202594">↑</a> vii. 15, 4 (in the description of the merits of exertion): ‘A man while lying is the <a class="vei_120a" href="कलि">kali</a>; moving himself, he is the <a class="vei_120a" href="द्वापर">dvāpara</a>; rising, he is the Tretā; walking, he becomes the Kṛta’ (<i>Kaliḥ śayāno bhavati saṃjihānas tu Dvāparaḥ | uttiṣṭhaṃs Tretā bhavati, Kṛtaṃ saṃpadyate caran||</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202594">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, 2, 464, criticized by Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 319.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202594">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 286; 9, 315 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1202594">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 460.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1202594">↑</a> <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1851, 99.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1202594">↑</a> <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 412.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1202594">↑</a> <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 48, n. 86.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1202594">↑</a> v. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1202594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1202594">↑</a> i. 1, 28; Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 151, n. 166; Windisch, <i>Buddha und <a class="vei_120a" href="मार">māra</a>,</i> 151.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 367371; Weber, <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 91. A quite different theory of the Yugas is given by Shamasastry, <i>Gavām <a class="vei_120a" href="अयन">ayana</a>,</i> 141 <i>et seq.,</i> but his whole theory is quite impossible. Weber once (<i>Indian Literature,</i> 113, n. 127) found the mention of the quinquennial <a class="vei_120a" href="युग">yuga</a> in Rv. iii. 55, 18, but that passage refers to the five or six seasons (see Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 1, 382, n.), while i. 25, 8, merely alludes to the intercalary month. Weber also (<i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 70, 247) considers that the Yugas are derived from the phases of the moon, but this idea was long since disposed of by Roth, <i>Die Lehre von den vier Weltaltern</i> (Tübingen, 1860).</li></ol></div>

युद्ध|యుద్ధ|ಯುದ್ಧ|yuddha|_vei_120@2595|_vei2_pn194
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2594">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2596">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn192">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn194">2:194</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn195">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yuddha</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202595"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202595">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202595"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202595">2</a></sup> denotes ‘battle.’ The more usual word earlier<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202595"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202595">3</a></sup> is Yudh.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202595"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202595">↑</a> x. 54, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202595"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202595">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 9, 1; Aitareva <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 39, 1. 2; vi. 36, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 1, 5, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202595"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202595">↑</a> Rv. i. 53, 7; 59, 1; v. 25, 6; vi. 46, 11, etc.; Av. i. 24, 1; iv. 24, 7; vi. 66, 1; 103, 3, etc.; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 2, 4, 16, etc.</li></ol></div>

युधां-श्रौष्टि औग्र-सैन्य|युधां-श्रौष्टि औग्रसैन्य|युधां-श्रौष्टि औग्र|सैन्य|యుధాం-శ్రౌష్టి ఔగ్ర-సైన్య|యుధాం-శ్రౌష్టి ఔగ్రసైన్య|యుధాం-శ్రౌష్టి ఔగ్ర|సైన్య|ಯುಧಾಂ-ಶ್ರೌಷ್ಟಿ ಔಗ್ರ-ಸೈನ್ಯ|ಯುಧಾಂ-ಶ್ರೌಷ್ಟಿ ಔಗ್ರಸೈನ್ಯ|ಯುಧಾಂ-ಶ್ರೌಷ್ಟಿ ಔಗ್ರ|ಸೈನ್ಯ|yudhāṃ-śrauṣṭi augra-sainya|yudhāṃ-śrauṣṭi augrasainya|yudhāṃ-śrauṣṭi augra|sainya|yudhaaM-shrauShTi augra-sainya|yudhaaM-shrauShTi augrasainya|yudhaaM-shrauShTi augra|_vei_120@2596|_vei2_pn194
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2595">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2597">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn192">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn194">2:194</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn195">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yudhāṃ-śrauṣṭi <a class="vei_120a" href="औग्र">augra</a>-sainya</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उग्रसेन">ugrasena</a></b>’) is the name, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202596"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202596">1</a></sup> of a king who was anointed by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्वत">parvata</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नारद">nārada</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202596"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202596">↑</a> viii. 21, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 8. The Paurāṇic form is <a class="vei_120a" href="युद्ध">yuddha</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="मुष्टि">muṣṭi</a>.</li></ol></div>

युध्यामधि|యుధ్యామధి|ಯುಧ್ಯಾಮಧಿ|yudhyāmadhi|yudhyaamadhi|_vei_120@2597|_vei2_pn194
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2596">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2598">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn192">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn194">2:194</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn195">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yudhyāmadhi</b> is apparently the name of a king who was defeated by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>.</b> The mention of him occurring only in the verses added at the end of the hymn celebrating the victory of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a> over the ten kings<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202597"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202597">1</a></sup> can claim little authenticity as a notice of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202597"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202597">↑</a> Rv. vii. 18, 24. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 173.</li></ol></div>

युवति|యువతి|ಯುವತಿ|yuvati|_vei_120@2598|_vei2_pn194
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2597">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2599">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn192">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn194">2:194</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn195">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yuvati</b> is the ordinary expression for a ‘young woman’ or ‘maiden’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202598"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202598">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202598"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202598">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202598"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202598">↑</a> i. 118, 5; ii. 35, 4; iii. 54, 14; iv. 18, 8; v. 2, 1. 2; ix. 86, 16; x. 30, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202598"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202598">↑</a> Av. xiv. 2, 61; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 1, 9; 2, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 1, 9, 6; 4, 3, 8, etc.</li></ol></div>

यूथ|యూథ|ಯೂಥ|yūtha|yuutha|yootha|_vei_120@2599|_vei2_pn194
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2598">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2600">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn192">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn194">2:194</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn195">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yūtha</b> is the word for ‘herd’ of cows in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202599">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202599">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202599">↑</a> i. 10, 2; 81, 7; iii. 55, 17; iv. 2, 18; 38, 5; v. 41, 19; ix. 71, 9, etc.</p><p><i>Cf. yūthya,</i> ‘of the herd,’ viii. 56, 4; ix. 15, 4; x. 23, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202599">↑</a> Av. v. 20, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 7, 2, 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

यूप|యూప|ಯೂಪ|yūpa|yuupa|yoopa|_vei_120@2600|_vei2_pn194
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2599">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2601">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn192">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn194">2:194</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn195">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yūpa</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202600"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202600">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202600"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202600">2</a></sup> means a ‘post,’ usually that to which the sacrificial victim was tied. It also designates the post to which the door of the house was attached (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="दुर्य">durya</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202600"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202600">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202600"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202600">↑</a> v. 2, 7 (of <b>Sunaḥśepa</b>).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202600"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202600">↑</a> Av. ix. 6, 22; xii. 1, 38; xiii. 1, 47; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 3, 4, 1; vii. 2, 1, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>; ix. 10, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202600"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202600">↑</a> Rv. i. 51, 14. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 153.</li></ol></div>

यूषन्|యూషన్|ಯೂಷನ್|yūṣan|yuuShan|yooShan|_vei_120@2601|_vei2_pn195
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2600">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2602">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn194">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn195">2:195</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn196">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yūṣan,</b> occurring in the description of the horse-sacrifice in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202601"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202601">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202601"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202601">2</a></sup> denotes the ‘broth’ which was made from the flesh of the sacrificial animal, and was no doubt used as food. Vessels employed for holding it, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पात्र">pātra</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आसेचन">āsecana</a>,</b> are mentioned. Another form of the word, found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202601"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202601">3</a></sup> is Yūs, which corresponds to the Latin <i>jus.</i></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202601"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202601">↑</a> i. 162, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202601"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202601">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 3, 11, 1. 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxv. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202601"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202601">↑</a> vi. 3, 11, 1. 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 271; Schrader, <i>Prehisteric Antiquities,</i> 316.</li></ol></div>

येवाष|యేవాష|ಯೇವಾಷ|yevāṣa|yevaaSha|_vei_120@2602|_vei2_pn195
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2601">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2603">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn194">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn195">2:195</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn196">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yevāṣa</b> is the name of a destructive insect in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202602">1</a></sup> The form <a class="vei_120a" href="यवाष">yavāṣa</a> is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202602">2</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वृष">vṛṣa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202602">↑</a> v. 23, 7. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202602"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202602">↑</a> xxx. 1 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 462). The same form occurs in the Gaṇas, <i>kumudādi</i> and <i>prekṣādi</i> (Pāṇini, iv. 2, 80). <i>Cf.</i> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 8, 1, where <a class="vei_120a" href="यवाष">yavāṣa</a> should be read; Kapiṣṭhala Saṃhitā, xlvi. 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 98; St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

योक्त्र|యోక్త్ర|ಯೋಕ್ತ್ರ|yoktra|_vei_120@2603|_vei2_pn195
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2602">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2604">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn194">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn195">2:195</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn196">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yoktra</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202603"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202603">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202603"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202603">2</a></sup> denotes the ‘thongs’ used for yoking the chariot or cart.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202603"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202603">↑</a> iii. 33, 13 v. 33, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202603"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202603">↑</a> Av. iii. 30 6; vii. 78, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 4, 3, <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 3, 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 1, 13; vi. 4, 3, 7, etc.</li></ol></div>

योग|యోగ|ಯೋಗ|yoga|_vei_120@2604|_vei2_pn195
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2603">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2605">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn194">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn195">2:195</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn196">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yoga</b> denotes the yoke of oxen or horses drawing a car in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202604"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202604">1</a></sup> and the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202604"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202604">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202604"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202604">↑</a> vi. 91, 1 (yokes of six or eight); <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 2, etc. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सीर">sīra</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202604"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202604">↑</a> iv. 3, 11 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a>-yogāḥ,</i> ‘chariot teams’).</li></ol></div>

योजन|యోజన|ಯೋಜನ|yojana|_vei_120@2605|_vei2_pn195
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2604">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2606">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn194">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn195">2:195</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn196">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yojana</b> occurs frequently in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202605"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202605">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202605"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202605">2</a></sup> as a measure of distance,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202605"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202605">3</a></sup> but there is no reference defining its real  length. Later it is reckoned at four <b>Krośas,</b> or about nine miles.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202605"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202605">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202605"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202605">↑</a> i. 123, 8; ii. 16, 3; x. 78, 7; 86, 20, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202605"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202605">↑</a> Av. iv. 26, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9, 9; iii. 8, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 2, 7, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 363, who finds in Rv. i. 123, 8, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="योजन">yojana</a></i> as a division of time equivalent to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मुहूर्त">muhūrta</a>.</b> But this is most improbable.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202605"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202605">↑</a> That is, the distance driven in one ‘harnessing’ (without unyoking), a ‘stage.’</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202605"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202605">↑</a> Sometimes calculated at 8 krośas, or 18 miles. The estimate of 2(1/2) miles is also found.</li></ol></div>

योध|యోధ|ಯೋಧ|yodha|_vei_120@2606|_vei2_pn196
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2605">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2607">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn195">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn196">2:196</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn197">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yodha</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202606"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202606">1</a></sup> means ‘fighter,’ ‘warrior,’ ‘soldier.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202606"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202606">↑</a> i. 143, 5; iii. 39, 4; vi. 25, 5; x. 78, 3.</li></ol></div>

योषन्|योषणा|योषा|योषित्|యోషన్|యోషణా|యోషా|యోషిత్|ಯೋಷನ್|ಯೋಷಣಾ|ಯೋಷಾ|ಯೋಷಿತ್|yoṣan|yoṣaṇā|yoṣā|yoṣit|yoShan|yoShaNaa|yoShaa|yoShit|_vei_120@2607|_vei2_pn196
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2606">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2608">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn195">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn196">2:196</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn197">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yoṣan, <a class="vei_120a" href="योषणा">yoṣaṇā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="योषा">yoṣā</a>, Yoṣit.</b> all denote ‘young woman,’ ‘maiden,’ as an object of affection, and as meet for wedlock.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202607">1</a></sup> So these terms are often opposed in the Brāhmaṇas to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वृषन्">vṛṣan</a>,</b> ‘male,’ in the general sense of ‘female,’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202607">2</a></sup> but they also occur in the sense of ‘wife,’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202607">3</a></sup> or ‘daughter,’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202607">4</a></sup> or merely ‘girl.’<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202607">5</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्त्री">strī</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202607">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="योषन्">yoṣan</a>,</i> Rv. iv. 5, 5; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="योषणा">yoṣaṇā</a>,</i> iii. 52, 3; 56, 5; 62, 8; vii. 95, 3. etc.; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="योषा">yoṣā</a>,</i> i. 48, 5; 92, 11; iii. 33, 10; 38, 8, etc.; Av. xii. 3, 29; xiv. 1, 56, etc.; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="योषित्">yoṣit</a>,</i> Rv. ix. 28, 4; Av. vi. 101, 1, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 418.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202607">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 5, 15 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="योषा">yoṣā</a></i>), and often in the Brāhmaṇas.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202607">↑</a> Av. xii. 3, 29 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="योषा">yoṣā</a></i>).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202607">↑</a> So <i><a class="vei_120a" href="योषा">yoṣā</a></i> in Rv. i. 117, 20. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 310.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202607">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 1, 7.</li></ol></div>

यौगं-धरि|यौगंधरि|यौगं|धरि|यौगन्धरि|యౌగం-ధరి|యౌగంధరి|యౌగం|ధరి|యౌగన్ధరి|ಯೌಗಂ-ಧರಿ|ಯೌಗಂಧರಿ|ಯೌಗಂ|ಧರಿ|ಯೌಗನ್ಧರಿ|yaugaṃ-dhari|yaugaṃdhari|yaugaṃ|dhari|yaugaM-dhari|yaugaMdhari|yaugaM|yaugandhari|_vei_120@2608|_vei2_pn196
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2607">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2609">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn195">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn196">2:196</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn197">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yaugaṃ-dhari,</b> ‘descendant of Yugaṃdhara,’ is the name of a king of the <b>Sālvas</b> in the Mantrapāṭha (ii. 11, 12).</p></div>

यौवन|యౌవన|ಯೌವನ|yauvana|_vei_120@2609|_vei2_pn196
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2608">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2610">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn195">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn196">2:196</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn197">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yauvana,</b> ‘youth,’ is found in the Atharvaveda (xviii. 4, 50), where it is opposed to ‘old age.’</p></div>

रक्षितृ|రక్షితృ|ರಕ್ಷಿತೃ|rakṣitṛ|rakShitR|rakShitRi|rakShitRu|rakShitRRi|raxitR|_vei_120@2610|_vei2_pn196
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2609">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2611">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn195">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn196">2:196</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn197">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rakṣitṛ,</b> ‘protector,’ ‘guardian,’ occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202610"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202610">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202610"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202610">2</a></sup> usually in a metaphorical sense.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202610"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202610">↑</a> i. 89, 1. 5; ii. 39, 6; guardian of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>, vi. 7, 7; of the dogs of <a class="vei_120a" href="यम">yama</a>, x. 14, 11, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202610"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202610">↑</a> Av. iii. 27, 1; xii. 3, 55; xix. 15, 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 2, 5, etc.</li></ol></div>

रघट्|రఘట్|ರಘಟ್|raghaṭ|raghaT|_vei_120@2611|_vei2_pn196
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2610">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2612">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn195">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn196">2:196</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn197">↷</a><hr><p><b>Raghaṭ</b> occurs once in the plural in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202611">1</a></sup> where the Paippalāda recension reads <i>vaghaṭah.</i> Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202611">2</a></sup> once con jectured <i>raghavaḥ,</i> ‘swift,’ as the correct reading. Bloomfield,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202611">3</a></sup> who in his translation explains the word as ‘falcons,’ in his notes inclines to think Roth's conjecture likely. Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202611">4</a></sup> suggests ‘bees’ as the meaning. Possibly some kind of bird may be intended.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202611">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202611">↑</a> viii. 7, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202611">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> 1<i>a.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202611">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 580.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202611">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 504.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202611">↑</a> Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v. Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 501.</li></ol></div>

रजत|రజత|ರಜತ|rajata|_vei_120@2612|_vei2_pn197
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2611">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2613">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn196">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn197">2:197</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn198">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rajata</b> as an adjective with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्य">hiraṇya</a></b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202612">1</a></sup> designates ‘silver,’ and ornaments (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="रुक्म">rukma</a></b>),<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202612">2</a></sup> dishes (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="पात्र">pātra</a></b>),<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202612">3</a></sup> and coins (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="निष्क">niṣka</a></b>)<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202612">4</a></sup> ‘made of silver’ are mentioned. The word is also used alone as a substantive to denote ‘silver.’<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202612">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202612">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 5, 1, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, x. 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 4, 4, 7; xiii. 4, 2, 10; xiv. 1, 3, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202612">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 8, 3, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202612">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 2, 9, 7; iii. 9, 6, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202612">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvii. 1, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202612">↑</a> Av. v. 28, 1; xiii. 4, 51; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 12, 2; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 17, 7; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 17, 3; Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 180; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 56; Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 151, 152; Vincent Smith, <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 34, 230.</li></ol></div>

रजन कोणेय|कौणेय|రజన కోణేయ|కౌణేయ|ರಜನ ಕೋಣೇಯ|ಕೌಣೇಯ|rajana koṇeya|kauṇeya|rajana koNeya|kauNeya|_vei_120@2613|_vei2_pn197
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2612">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2614">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn196">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn197">2:197</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn198">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rajana Koṇeya,</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौणेय">kauṇeya</a>,</b> is the name of a teacher mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202613">1</a></sup> It is said in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202613">2</a></sup> that <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रतुजित् जानकि">kratujit jānaki</a></b> successfully sacrificed for him when he desired to obtain eyesight. He is also mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202613">3</a></sup> where the name of his son, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उग्रदेव">ugradeva</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="राजनि">rājani</a>,</b> also occurs.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202613">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202613">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 8, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxvii. 2 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 474).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202613">↑</a> xi. 1 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 474).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202613">↑</a> xiii. 4, 11. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 58, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202613">↑</a> He was a leper, and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रजनी">rajanī</a></b> is used against leprosy, Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 266.</li></ol></div>

रजनी|रजनि|రజనీ|రజని|ರಜನೀ|ರಜನಿ|rajanī|rajani|rajanii|rajanee|_vei_120@2614|_vei2_pn197
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2613">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2615">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn196">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn197">2:197</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn198">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rajanī</b> is found in one passage of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202614"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202614">1</a></sup> where it denotes some sort of plant, probably so called because of its  power of ‘colouring’ (from <i>rañj,</i> ‘to colour’). The species cannot be identified owing to the untrustworthiness of the later authorities who attempt its identification.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202614"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202614">↑</a> i. 23, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Roth in Whitney's Translation of the Atharvaveda, 24; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 267.</li></ol></div>

रजयित्री|रजयित्रि|రజయిత్రీ|రజయిత్రి|ರಜಯಿತ್ರೀ|ರಜಯಿತ್ರಿ|rajayitrī|rajayitri|rajayitrii|rajayitree|_vei_120@2615|_vei2_pn198
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2614">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2616">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn197">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn198">2:198</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn199">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rajayitrī,</b> a ‘female dyer,’ is included in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202615">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202615">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 7, 1.</li></ol></div>

रजस्|रजस्|रज|రజస్_౧|రజస్|రజ|ರಜಸ್_೧|ರಜಸ್|ರಜ|rajas_1|rajas|raja|_vei_120@2616|_vei2_pn198
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2615">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2617">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn197">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn198">2:198</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn199">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Rajas</b> denotes the region of the atmosphere between heaven and earth in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202616"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202616">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202616"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202616">2</a></sup> The atmosphere, like the sky (<b>Div</b>), is divided into three regions,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202616"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202616">3</a></sup> but more normally into two, the ‘earthly’ (<i>pārthiva</i>)<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202616"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202616">4</a></sup> and the ‘heavenly’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दिव्य">divya</a></i> or <i>divaḥ</i>).<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202616"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202616">5</a></sup> In some passages<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202616"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202616">6</a></sup> the word refers in the plural to the dusty fields on earth.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202616"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202616">↑</a> i. 56, 5; 62, 5; 84, 1; 124, 5; 168, 6; 187, 4; ii. 40, 3; vi. 62, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202616"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202616">↑</a> Av. iv. 25, 2; vii. 25, 1; 41, 1; x. 3, 9; xiii. 2, 8. 43; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 5, 4, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiii. 44, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202616"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202616">↑</a> Rv. iv. 53, 5; v. 69, 1; ix. 74, 6; x. 45, 3; 123, 8; Av. xiii. 1, 11, etc. In Rv. i. 164, 6, six ‘regions’ are mentioned.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202616"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202616">↑</a> Rv. i. 81, 5; 90, 7; 154, 1; vi. 49, 3; viii. 88, 5; ix. 72, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202616"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202616">↑</a> Rv. iv. 53, 3; i. 110, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 10; St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202616"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202616">↑</a> Rv. i. 166, 3; iii. 62, 16; x. 75. 7.</li></ol></div>

रजस्|रजस्|रज|రజస్_౨|రజస్|రజ|ರಜಸ್_೨|ರಜಸ್|ರಜ|rajas_2|rajas|raja|_vei_120@2617|_vei2_pn198
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2616">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2618">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn197">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn198">2:198</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn199">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Rajas</b> in one passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202617"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202617">1</a></sup> clearly means ‘silver,’ like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रजत">rajata</a>.</b> It is also taken in this sense in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202617"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202617">2</a></sup> by Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202617"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202617">3</a></sup> but this interpretation is doubtful.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202617"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202617">↑</a> <i>Rajaḥ-śaya,</i> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, v. 8; <i>rajāśaya,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 2, 11, 2 (Sāyaṇa on <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 23, 2); Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 2, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202617"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202617">↑</a> x. 105, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202617"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202617">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 55, 56.</li></ol></div>

रजस|రజస|ರಜಸ|rajasa|_vei_120@2618|_vei2_pn198
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2617">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2619">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn197">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn198">2:198</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn199">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rajasa</b> occurs once in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202618"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202618">1</a></sup> apparently as the name of a kind of ‘fish.’ Roth,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202618"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202618">2</a></sup> however, understood it as an adjective meaning ‘impure.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202618"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202618">↑</a> x. 2, 25.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202618"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202618">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 621; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveḍa, 624.</li></ol></div>

रजि|రజి|ರಜಿ|raji|_vei_120@2619|_vei2_pn199
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2618">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2620">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn198">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn199">2:199</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn201">↷</a><hr><p><b>Raji</b> occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202619"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202619">1</a></sup> seemingly as the name of a king, or perhaps demon, slain by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> for <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पिठीनस्">piṭhīnas</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202619"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202619">↑</a> vi. 26, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 156; St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> where Roth compares a conjecture in Av. xx. 128, 13</li></ol></div>

रज्जव्य|రజ్జవ్య|ರಜ್ಜವ್ಯ|rajjavya|_vei_120@2620|_vei2_pn199
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2619">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2621">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn198">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn199">2:199</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn201">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rajjavya</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vi. 7, 1, 28) den ‘cord’ or ‘rope.’</p></div>

रज्जु|రజ్జు|ರಜ್ಜು|rajju|_vei_120@2621|_vei2_pn199
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2620">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2622">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn198">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn199">2:199</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn201">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rajju</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202621"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202621">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202621"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202621">2</a></sup> denotes ‘rope.’ In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202621"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202621">3</a></sup> the serpent is called the ‘toothed rope’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="रज्जु">rajju</a> datvatī</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202621"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202621">↑</a> i. 162, 8 (<i>śīrṣaṇyā <a class="vei_120a" href="रशना">raśanā</a> rajjuḥ,</i> referring to the horse presumably means the head harness).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202621"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202621">↑</a> Av. iii. 11, 8; vi. 121, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 1, 7; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 1, 14; x. 2, 3, 8; xi. 3, 1, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202621"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202621">↑</a> iv. 3, 2; xix. 47, 7, 8; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 368.</li></ol></div>

रज्जु-दाल|रज्जुदाल|रज्जु|दाल|రజ్జు-దాల|రజ్జుదాల|రజ్జు|దాల|ರಜ್ಜು-ದಾಲ|ರಜ್ಜುದಾಲ|ರಜ್ಜು|ದಾಲ|rajju-dāla|rajjudāla|rajju|dāla|rajju-daala|rajjudaala|daala|_vei_120@2622|_vei2_pn199
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2621">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2623">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn198">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn199">2:199</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn201">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rajju-dāla</b> is the name of a tree (<i>Cordia myxa</i> or <i>latifolia</i>) the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202622"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202622">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202622"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202622">↑</a> xiii. 4, 4, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 373, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

रज्जु-सर्ज|रज्जुसर्ज|रज्जु|सर्ज|రజ్జు-సర్జ|రజ్జుసర్జ|రజ్జు|సర్జ|ರಜ್ಜು-ಸರ್ಜ|ರಜ್ಜುಸರ್ಜ|ರಜ್ಜು|ಸರ್ಜ|rajju-sarja|rajjusarja|rajju|sarja|_vei_120@2623|_vei2_pn199
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2622">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2624">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn198">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn199">2:199</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn201">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rajju-sarja,</b> ‘rope-maker,’ is one of the victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202623"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202623">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202623"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202623">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 3, 1.</li></ol></div>

रण|రణ|ರಣ|raṇa|raNa|_vei_120@2624|_vei2_pn199
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2623">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2625">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn198">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn199">2:199</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn201">↷</a><hr><p><b>Raṇa</b> denotes properly the ‘joy’ of battle, then ‘battle,’ ‘combat’ itself in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202624">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202624">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202624">↑</a> i. 61, 1. 9; 74, 3; 119, 3; vi. 16, 15, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202624">↑</a> Av. v. 2, 4, etc.</li></ol></div>

रत्न|రత్న|ರತ್ನ|ratna|_vei_120@2625|_vei2_pn199
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2624">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2626">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn198">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn199">2:199</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn201">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ratna</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202625"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202625">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202625"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202625">2</a></sup> denotes a precious object, not specifically a ‘jewel,’ as in post-Vedic literature.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202625"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202625">↑</a> i. 20, 7; 35, 8; 41, 6; 125, 1; 140, 11; 141, 10; ii. 38, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202625"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202625">↑</a> Av. v. 1, 7; vii. 14, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 1, 1.</li></ol></div>

रत्नि|రత్ని|ರತ್ನಿ|ratni|_vei_120@2626|_vei2_pn199
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2625">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2627">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn198">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn199">2:199</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn201">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ratni,</b> ‘ell,’ occurring in the Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iv. 4) is a corruption of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अरत्नि">aratni</a>.</b></p></div>

रत्निन्|రత్నిన్|ರತ್ನಿನ್|ratnin|_vei_120@2627|_vei2_pn199
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2626">«</a>pada9<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2628">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn198">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn199">2:199</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn201">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ratnin,</b> ‘receiving gifts,’ is the term applied to those people of the royal entourage in whose houses the <a class="vei_120a" href="रत्न">ratna</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="हविस्">havis</a>, a  special rite, was performed in the course of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a></b> or ‘royal consecration.’ The list given in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202627">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202627">2</a></sup> consists of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> (<i>i.e.,</i> the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>), the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a>, the <a class="vei_120a" href="महिषी">mahiṣī</a> (the first wife of the king), the <b>Vāvāta</b> (the favourite wife of the king), the <a class="vei_120a" href="परिवृक्ती">parivṛktī</a> (the discarded wife), the <a class="vei_120a" href="सेनानी">senānī</a>, ‘commander of the army’; the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत">sūta</a>, ‘charioteer’; the Grāmaṇī, ‘village headman’; the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्तृ">kṣattṛ</a>, ‘chamberlain’; the <a class="vei_120a" href="संग्रहीतृ">saṃgrahītṛ</a>, ‘charioteer’ or ‘treasurer’; the <a class="vei_120a" href="भागदुघ">bhāgadugha</a>, ‘collector of taxes’ or ‘divider of food’; and the Akṣāvāpa, ‘superintender of dicing’ or ‘thrower of dice.’ In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202627">3</a></sup> the order is <a class="vei_120a" href="सेनानी">senānī</a>; <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>; Mahisī; <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत">sūta</a>; Grāmaṇī; <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्तृ">kṣattṛ</a>; Saṃgrahitṛ; <a class="vei_120a" href="भागदुघ">bhāgadugha</a>; Akṣāvāpa; Go-nikartana, ‘slayer of cows’ or ‘huntsman’; and <a class="vei_120a" href="पालागल">pālāgala</a>, ‘courier’; the ‘discarded wife’ being mentioned as forbidden to stay at home<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202627">4</a></sup> on the day of the ceremony of offering a pap for Nirṛti in her house. In the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202627">5</a></sup> the list is <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> (<i>i.e.,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>); <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a>; <a class="vei_120a" href="महिषी">mahiṣī</a>; <a class="vei_120a" href="परिवृक्ती">parivṛktī</a>; <a class="vei_120a" href="सेनानी">senānī</a>; <a class="vei_120a" href="संग्रहीतृ">saṃgrahītṛ</a>; <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्तृ">kṣattṛ</a>; <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत">sūta</a>; Vaiśyagrāmaṇī; <a class="vei_120a" href="भागदुघ">bhāgadugha</a>; Takṣa-Rathakārau, ‘carpenter and chariot-maker’; Akṣāvāpa; and Go-vikarta. The <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202627">6</a></sup> substitutes Go-<a class="vei_120a" href="व्यछ">vyacha</a> for Govikarta, and omits Takṣa-Rathakārau.</p><p>It will be seen that the list is essentially that of the royal household, and of the king's servants in the administration of the country, though the exact sense of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="संग्रहीतृ">saṃgrahītṛ</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="भागदुघ">bhāgadugha</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत">sūta</a>, Grāmaṇī, <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्तृ">kṣattṛ</a>,</b> is open to reasonable doubt, mainly as to whether public officers or private servants<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202627">7</a></sup> are meant, for the names are of uncertain significance. A briefer list of eight <b>Vīras,</b> ‘heroes,’ as among the friends of the king,  is given in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>:<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202627">8</a></sup> brother, son, <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="महिषी">mahiṣī</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत">sūta</a>, Grāmaṇī, <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्तृ">kṣattṛ</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="संग्रहीतृ">saṃgrahītṛ</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202627">↑</a> i. 8, 9, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202627">↑</a> i. 7, 3, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202627">↑</a> v. 3, 1, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202627">↑</a> According to Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 3, 35, she goes to a Brahmin's house, where she shares his inviolability and exemption from jurisdiction.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202627">↑</a> ii. 6, 5; iv. 3, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202627">↑</a> xv. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202627">↑</a> Similarly Akṣāvāpa is either the man who dices for the king--<i>i.e.,</i> a professional dicer who plays with the king or watches his play--or a public officer who superintends the gambling halls of the state and collects the revenue, as was regularly done later on. Early English history shows similar evolution of household officers into ministers of state.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202627">↑</a> xix. 1, 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 200; <i>Über den <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>,</i> 4; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 128; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 58-65; Hopkins, <i>Trañsactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 30, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

रथ|రథ|ರಥ|ratha|_vei_120@2628|_vei2_pn201
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2627">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2629">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn199">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn201">2:201</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn203">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ratha</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202628">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202628">2</a></sup> denotes ‘chariot’ as opposed to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अनस्">anas</a>,</b> ‘cart,’ though the distinction is not absolute. Of differences in the structure of the two we have no information, except that the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ख">kha</a>,</b> or nave hole, in the wheel of the chariot was greater than in that of the cart.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202628">3</a></sup></p><p>The chariot has, as a rule, two wheels (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="चक्र">cakra</a></b>), to which reference is frequently made.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202628">4</a></sup> The wheel consisted of a rim (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="पवि">pavi</a></b>), a felly (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रधि">pradhi</a></b>), spokes (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अर">ara</a></b>),<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202628">5</a></sup> and a nave (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="नभ्य">nabhya</a></b>). The rim and the felly together constitute the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नेमि">nemi</a>.</b> The hole in the nave is called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ख">kha</a>:</b> into it the end of the axle was inserted; but there is some uncertainty whether <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आणि">āṇi</a></b> denotes the extremity of the axle that was inserted in the nave, or the lynch-pin used to keep that extremity in the wheel. Sometimes a solid wheel was used.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202628">6</a></sup></p><p>The axle (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a></b>) was, in some cases, made of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अरटु">araṭu</a></b> wood;<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202628">7</a></sup> round its ends the wheels revolved. To the axle was attached the body of the chariot (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="कोश">kośa</a></b>). This part is also denoted by the word <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वन्धुर">vandhura</a>,</b> which more precisely means the ‘seat’ of the chariot. The epithet <i><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रि">tri</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वन्धुर">vandhura</a></i> is used of the chariot of the Aśvins, seemingly to correspond with another of its epithets, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रि">tri</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="चक्र">cakra</a>:</i> perhaps, as Weber<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202628">8</a></sup> thinks, a chariot with three seats and three wheels was a real form of vehicle; but Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202628">9</a></sup> considers that the vehicle was purely mythical. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गर्त">garta</a></b> also denotes the seat of the warrior. </p><p>At right angles to the axle was the pole of the chariot (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="ईषा">īṣā</a>, Praüga</b>). Normally there was, it seems, one pole, on either side of which the horses were harnessed, a yoke (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="युग">yuga</a></b>) being laid across their necks; the pole was passed through the hole in the yoke (called <a class="vei_120a" href="ख">kha</a><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202628">10</a></sup> or <a class="vei_120a" href="तर्द्मन्">tardman</a>),<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202628">11</a></sup> the yoke and the pole then being tied together.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202628">12</a></sup></p><p>The horses were tied by the neck (<i>grīvā</i>), where the yoke was placed, and also at the shoulder, presumably by traces fastened to a bar of wood at right angles to the pole, or fastened to the ends of the pole, if that is to be regarded, as it probably should, as of triangular shape, wide at the foot and coming to a point at the tip.<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202628">13</a></sup> The traces seem to be denoted by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रश्मि">raśmi</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रशना">raśanā</a>.</b> These words also denote the ‘reins,’ which were fastened to the bit (perhaps <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शिप्रा">śiprā</a></i>) in the horse's mouth. The driver controlled the horses by reins, and urged them on with a whip (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कशा">kaśā</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1202628">14</a></sup> The girths of the horse were called Kakṣyā.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1202628">15</a></sup></p><p>The normal number of horses seems to have been two, but three or four<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1202628">16</a></sup> were often used. It is uncertain whether, in these cases, the extra horse was attached in front or at the side; possibly both modes were in use. Even five steeds could be employed.<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1202628">17</a></sup> Horses were normally used for chariots, but the ass (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="गर्दभ">gardabha</a></i>)<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1202628">18</a></sup> or mule (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वतरी">aśvatarī</a></i>)<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1202628">19</a></sup> are also mentioned. The ox was employed for drawing carts, and in fact derived its  name, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अनड्वाह्">anaḍvāh</a>,</b> from this use. Sometimes a poor man had to be content with a single steed, which then ran between two shafts.<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1202628">20</a></sup></p><p>In the chariot the driver stood on the right, while the warrior was on the left, as indicated by his name, <b>Savyeṣṭha</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सव्यष्ठा">savyaṣṭhā</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1202628">21</a></sup> He could also sit when he wanted, for the chariot had seats, and an archer would naturally prefer to sit while shooting his arrows.</p><p>The dimensions of the chariot are given in the Śulba <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1202628">22</a></sup> of Āpastamba at 188 Aṅgulis (finger-breadths) for the pole, 104 for the axle, and 86 for the yoke. The material used in its construction was wood, except for the rim of the wheel.<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1202628">23</a></sup></p><p>Many other parts of the chariot are mentioned, their names being often obscure in meaning: see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अंक">aṅka</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="न्यंक">nyaṅka</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="उद्धि">uddhi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पक्षस्">pakṣas</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पातल्य">pātalya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="भुरिज्">bhurij</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="रथोपस्थ">rathopastha</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="रथवाहन">rathavāhana</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202628">↑</a> i. 20, 3; iii. 15, 5; iv. 4, 10; 16, 20; 36, 2; 43, 25, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202628">↑</a> Av. v. 14, 5; x. 1, 8: <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 12, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202628">↑</a> viii. 91, 7, with Sāyaṇa's note; <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 333.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202628">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 16, 5; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 16, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202628">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 32, 15; 141, 9; v. 13, 6; 58, 5; viii. 20, 14; 77, 3; x. 78, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, x. 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202628">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <b>Pradhī.</b></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202628">↑</a> Rv. viii. 46, 27; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 247, n.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202628">↑</a> <i>Proceedings of the Berlin Academy,</i> 1898, 564; Virchow, <i>Zeitschrift für Ethnologie</i> 5, 200. <i>Cf.</i> note 21.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202628">↑</a> <i>Op.</i> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i> iii.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202628">↑</a> This seems to be the sense of Rv. viii. 91, 7; but it has also been taken as the opening in the yoke through which the ox's head passed (the Homeric ). See Cowell's note on Wilson's translation; Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 2, 237, n.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202628">↑</a> Av. xiv. 1, 40.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202628">↑</a> Rv. iii. 6, 6; v. 56, 4; x. 60, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202628">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 249, thinks that <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वाणी">vāṇī</a></i> in Rv. i. 119, 5, denotes the two bars of wood to which the traces were fastened. This is also the view of Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> Bo7htlingk's Lexicon, and Grassmann. The word may mean ‘two voices’ (Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 1, 162).</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1202628">↑</a> Rv. v. 83, 3; vi. 75, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1202628">↑</a> Rv. x. 10, 13; <i>kakṣya-<a class="vei_120a" href="प्रा">prā</a>,</i> ‘filling out the girths’ (<i>i.e.,</i> ‘well fed’), is an epithet of Indra's horses, i. 10, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1202628">↑</a> Three horses are mentioned in Rv. x. 33, 5, and <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रष्टि">praṣṭi</a> in Rv. i. 39, 6; viii. 7, 28, etc., may have the sense of ‘third horse.’ See also Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1, 4, 11; 2, 4, 9, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvi. 13, 12. For four horses, <i>cf.</i> Rv. ii. 18, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 3, 17; 1, 4, 11; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 21, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1202628">↑</a> <i>Rathaḥ pañcavāhī,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 3. In the parallel passage the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 7, 2, has <i>praṣṭivāhī.</i></li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1202628">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 9, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1202628">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 2, 1; v. 13, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 9, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1202628">↑</a> Rv. x. 101, 11; 131, 3, and vi. 15, 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvi. 13, 12; xxi. 13, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1202628">↑</a> This is the case in Av. viii. 8, 23, with <a class="vei_120a" href="सव्यष्ठा">savyaṣṭhā</a>, and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 7, 9, 1, <i>savyeṣṭha-<a class="vei_120a" href="सारथि">sārathi</a></i> occurs as a compound where the sense is certainly ‘the warrior and the charioteer.’ See also Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 1, 8, and Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 62, n. 1. The Greek notices speak of two warriors and a charioteer. <i>Cf.</i> the Aśvins' car with its three seats. See von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 435.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1202628">↑</a> vi. 5 (Bürk, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 56, 344, 345).</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1202628"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1202628">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 3, 16. The chariot used at the bridal procession was made of Śalmali wood, Rv. x. 85, 20.</p><p>For the chariot in the Epic, see Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 235-262; and <i>cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 338, 339; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 245-252; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 38, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

रथ-कार|रथकार|रथ|कार|రథ-కార|రథకార|రథ|కార|ರಥ-ಕಾರ|ರಥಕಾರ|ರಥ|ಕಾರ|ratha-kāra|rathakāra|ratha|kāra|ratha-kaara|rathakaara|kaara|_vei_120@2629|_vei2_pn203
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2628">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2630">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn201">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn203">2:203</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn204">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ratha-kāra,</b> ‘chariot-maker,’ is mentioned in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202629">1</a></sup> as one of those who are to be subject to the king, seeming to stand generally as an example of the industrial population. He is also referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202629">2</a></sup> and in the Brāhmaṇas:<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202629">3</a></sup> in all these passages, as well a probably in the Atharvaveda also, the <a class="vei_120a" href="रथकार">rathakāra</a> already forms a caste. The  later system<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202629">4</a></sup> regards the <a class="vei_120a" href="रथकार">rathakāra</a> as the offspring of a Māhiṣya (the son of a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> husband and a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> wife) and a Karaṇī (the daughter of a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> husband and a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> wife), but it is unreasonable to suppose that such an origin is historically accurate. The Rathakāras must rather be deemed to have been a functional caste. Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202629">5</a></sup> suggests that the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अनु">anu</a></b> tribe formed the basis of the <a class="vei_120a" href="रथकार">rathakāra</a> caste, referring to their worship of the Ṛbhus, who are, of course, the chariotmakers <i>par excellence.</i> But there is little ground for this view.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202629">↑</a> iii. 5, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202629">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 13; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9, 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 17; xxx. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202629">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 4, 8; iii. 4, 2, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 2, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202629">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a>, i. 95. On the special position, in the later ritual, of the <a class="vei_120a" href="रथकार">rathakāra</a> as a caste below the <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>, but superior to the <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>, <i>cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 12, 13, and <i>cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>, see also Fick, <i>Die sociale Gliederung,</i> 209, 210.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202629">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 152, 153.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 196 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

रथ-गृत्स|रथगृत्स|रथ|गृत्स|రథ-గృత్స|రథగృత్స|రథ|గృత్స|ರಥ-ಗೃತ್ಸ|ರಥಗೃತ್ಸ|ರಥ|ಗೃತ್ಸ|ratha-gṛtsa|rathagṛtsa|ratha|gṛtsa|ratha-gRtsa|rathagRtsa|gRtsa|ratha-gRitsa|rathagRitsa|gRitsa|ratha-gRutsa|rathagRutsa|gRutsa|ratha-gRRitsa|rathagRRitsa|gRRitsa|_vei_120@2630|_vei2_pn204
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2629">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2631">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn203">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn204">2:204</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn205">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ratha-gṛtsa</b> in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā (xv. 15) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 48, 9) denotes a ‘skilled charioteer.’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202630"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202630">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202630"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202630">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 4, 3, 1; kṛtsna, Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 8, 10; kṛtsa, <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 9.</li></ol></div>

रथ-चक्र|रथचक्र|रथ|चक्र|రథ-చక్ర|రథచక్ర|రథ|చక్ర|ರಥ-ಚಕ್ರ|ರಥಚಕ್ರ|ರಥ|ಚಕ್ರ|ratha-cakra|rathacakra|ratha|cakra|ratha-chakra|rathachakra|chakra|_vei_120@2631|_vei2_pn204
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2630">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2632">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn203">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn204">2:204</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn205">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ratha-cakra,</b> ‘chariot wheel,’ is often mentioned in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202631">1</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चक्र">cakra</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202631">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 43, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 6, 8; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 3, 3, 12; v. 1, 5, 2; xi. 8, 1, 11, etc.</li></ol></div>

रथ-चर्षण|रथचर्षण|रथ|चर्षण|రథ-చర్షణ|రథచర్షణ|రథ|చర్షణ|ರಥ-ಚರ್ಷಣ|ರಥಚರ್ಷಣ|ರಥ|ಚರ್ಷಣ|ratha-carṣaṇa|rathacarṣaṇa|ratha|carṣaṇa|ratha-charShaNa|rathacharShaNa|charShaNa|_vei_120@2632|_vei2_pn204
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2631">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2633">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn203">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn204">2:204</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn205">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ratha-carṣaṇa</b> occurs once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202632"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202632">1</a></sup> where the sense is doubtful. Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202632"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202632">2</a></sup> thought that some part of the chariot was meant, but the sense is perhaps only the ‘pathway of the chariot.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202632"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202632">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202632"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202632">↑</a> viii. 5, 19.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202632"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202632">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202632"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202632">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> also the citation and explanation in Durga's commentary on the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 12.</li></ol></div>

रथ-जूति|रथजूति|रथ|जूति|రథ-జూతి|రథజూతి|రథ|జూతి|ರಥ-ಜೂತಿ|ರಥಜೂತಿ|ರಥ|ಜೂತಿ|ratha-jūti|rathajūti|ratha|jūti|ratha-juuti|rathajuuti|juuti|ratha-jooti|rathajooti|jooti|_vei_120@2633|_vei2_pn204
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2632">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2634">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn203">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn204">2:204</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn205">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ratha-jūti</b> in the Atharvaveda (xix. 44, 3) is either an adjective meaning ‘driving swiftly in a chariot,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202633"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202633">1</a></sup> or a proper name, as Roth in the St. Petersburg Dictionary suggests. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202633"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202633">↑</a> ‘Of chariot-swiftness’ according to Whitney, Translation of the Athar vaveda, 967. <i>Cf.</i> his note on the passage.</li></ol></div>

रथ-नाभि|रथनाभि|रथ|नाभि|రథ-నాభి|రథనాభి|రథ|నాభి|ರಥ-ನಾಭಿ|ರಥನಾಭಿ|ರಥ|ನಾಭಿ|ratha-nābhi|rathanābhi|ratha|nābhi|ratha-naabhi|rathanaabhi|naabhi|_vei_120@2634|_vei2_pn205
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2633">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2635">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn204">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn205">2:205</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn206">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ratha-nābhi,</b> the ‘nave of the chariot-wheel,’ is mentioned in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202634"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202634">1</a></sup> and in the Upaniṣads.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202634"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202634">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202634"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202634">↑</a> xxxiv. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202634"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202634">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 5, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 2, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 8; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>. vii. 15, 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

रथ-प्रोत दार्भ्य|रथप्रोत दार्भ्य|रथ|प्रोत दार्भ्य|రథ-ప్రోత దార్భ్య|రథప్రోత దార్భ్య|రథ|ప్రోత దార్భ్య|ರಥ-ಪ್ರೋತ ದಾರ್ಭ್ಯ|ರಥಪ್ರೋತ ದಾರ್ಭ್ಯ|ರಥ|ಪ್ರೋತ ದಾರ್ಭ್ಯ|ratha-prota dārbhya|rathaprota dārbhya|ratha|prota dārbhya|ratha-prota daarbhya|rathaprota daarbhya|prota daarbhya|_vei_120@2635|_vei2_pn205
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2634">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2636">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn204">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn205">2:205</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn206">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ratha-prota Dārbhya</b> (‘descendant of Darbha’) is mentioned in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā (ii. 1, 3) perhaps as a king, but possibly as a priest.</p></div>

रथ-प्रोष्ठ|रथप्रोष्ठ|रथ|प्रोष्ठ|రథ-ప్రోష్ఠ|రథప్రోష్ఠ|రథ|ప్రోష్ఠ|ರಥ-ಪ್ರೋಷ್ಠ|ರಥಪ್ರೋಷ್ಠ|ರಥ|ಪ್ರೋಷ್ಠ|ratha-proṣṭha|rathaproṣṭha|ratha|proṣṭha|ratha-proShTha|rathaproShTha|proShTha|_vei_120@2636|_vei2_pn205
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2635">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2637">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn204">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn205">2:205</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn206">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ratha-proṣṭha</b> occurs as the name of a princely family in the Rigveda (x. 60, 5). See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुबन्धु">subandhu</a>.</b></p></div>

रथ-मुख|रथमुख|रथ|मुख|రథ-ముఖ|రథముఖ|రథ|ముఖ|ರಥ-ಮುಖ|ರಥಮುಖ|ರಥ|ಮುಖ|ratha-mukha|rathamukha|ratha|mukha|_vei_120@2637|_vei2_pn205
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2636">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2638">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn204">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn205">2:205</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn206">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ratha-mukha</b> in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202637">1</a></sup> denotes the fore-part of a chariot. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथशीर्ष">rathaśīrṣa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202637">↑</a> Av. viii. 8, 23; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 3, 2; v. 4, 9, 3, etc.</li></ol></div>

रथर्वी|रथर्वि|రథర్వీ|రథర్వి|ರಥರ್ವೀ|ರಥರ್ವಿ|ratharvī|ratharvi|ratharvii|ratharvee|_vei_120@2638|_vei2_pn205
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2637">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2639">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn204">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn205">2:205</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn206">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ratharvī</b> is the name of a snake in the Atharvaveda (x. 4, 5).</p></div>

रथ-वाहन|रथवाहन|रथ|वाहन|రథ-వాహన|రథవాహన|రథ|వాహన|ರಥ-ವಾಹನ|ರಥವಾಹನ|ರಥ|ವಾಹನ|ratha-vāhana|rathavāhana|ratha|vāhana|ratha-vaahana|rathavaahana|vaahana|_vei_120@2639|_vei2_pn205
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2638">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2640">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn204">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn205">2:205</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn206">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ratha-vāhana</b> is the name in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202639"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202639">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202639"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202639">2</a></sup> for a movable stand to hold the chariot. According to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202639"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202639">3</a></sup> it corresponds to the Greek , on which the chariot rested when out of use. The word <a class="vei_120a" href="रथवाहन">rathavāhana</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वाह">vāha</a> is employed in the sense of the two horses that draw the stand.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202639"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202639">4</a></sup> Weber<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202639"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202639">5</a></sup> thinks it was used to convey the war chariot to the scene of action. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202639"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202639">↑</a> vi. 75, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202639"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202639">↑</a> Av. iii. 17, 3 = <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 5, 5 = <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvi. 11 = Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 7, 12 = <a class="vei_120a" href="वासिष्ठ">vāsiṣṭha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 34. 35. See also <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxi. 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 9, 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 3, 23 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202639"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202639">↑</a> <i>Festgruss an Bo7htlingk,</i> 95 <i>et seq.;</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 116.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202639"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202639">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 20, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 4, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 9; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202639"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202639">↑</a> <i>Über den <a class="vei_120a" href="वाजपेय">vājapeya</a>,</i> 27, n. 2, followed by Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 275. Weber, however, admits that the <a class="vei_120a" href="रथवाहन">rathavāhana</a> may at times have served as a mere stand, like the Homeric  while Geldner expresses the opinion that it never has that sense. The use of the term Rathavāhanavāha shows that the stand was movable.</li></ol></div>

रथवीति दार्भ्य|రథవీతి దార్భ్య|ರಥವೀತಿ ದಾರ್ಭ್ಯ|rathavīti dārbhya|rathaviiti daarbhya|rathaveeti daarbhya|_vei_120@2640|_vei2_pn206
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2639">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2641">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn205">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn206">2:206</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn207">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rathavīti Dārbhya</b> (‘descendant of Darbha’) is mentioned once in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202640"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202640">1</a></sup> as residing in places abounding in kine (<i>gomatīr <a class="vei_120a" href="अनु">anu</a></i>) far away among the hills, possibly the Himālayas, and as the patron of the singer of the hymn. Later the tradition<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202640"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202640">2</a></sup> makes him the king, whose daughter <b>Śyāsvāśva</b> won for his wife by his father's and the Maruts' aid.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202640"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202640">↑</a> v. 61, 17. 19.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202640"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202640">↑</a> See Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 50 <i>et seq.,</i> 62, n. 2, and the criticism in Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Neten,</i> 1, 353, 354; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 359, 362.</li></ol></div>

रथ-शीर्ष|रथशीर्ष|रथ|शीर्ष|రథ-శీర్ష|రథశీర్ష|రథ|శీర్ష|ರಥ-ಶೀರ್ಷ|ರಥಶೀರ್ಷ|ರಥ|ಶೀರ್ಷ|ratha-śīrṣa|rathaśīrṣa|ratha|śīrṣa|ratha-shiirSha|rathashiirSha|shiirSha|ratha-sheerSha|rathasheerSha|sheerSha|_vei_120@2641|_vei2_pn206
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2640">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2642">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn205">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn206">2:206</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn207">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ratha-śīrṣa,</b> the ‘head of the chariot’--that is, its fore-part -is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (ix. 4. 1, 13).</p></div>

रथ-संग|रथसंग|रथ|संग|रथ-सङ्ग|रथसङ्ग|सङ्ग|రథ-సంగ|రథసంగ|రథ|సంగ|రథ-సఙ్గ|రథసఙ్గ|సఙ్గ|ರಥ-ಸಂಗ|ರಥಸಂಗ|ರಥ|ಸಂಗ|ರಥ-ಸಙ್ಗ|ರಥಸಙ್ಗ|ಸಙ್ಗ|ratha-saṅga|rathasaṅga|ratha|saṅga|ratha-saMga|rathasaMga|saMga|ratha-sanga|rathasanga|sanga|_vei_120@2642|_vei2_pn206
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2641">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2643">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn205">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn206">2:206</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn207">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ratha-saṅga</b> in the Rigveda (ix. 53, 2) denotes the hostile encounter of chariots.</p></div>

रथाक्ष|రథాక్ష|ರಥಾಕ್ಷ|rathākṣa|rathaakSha|rathaaxa|_vei_120@2643|_vei2_pn206
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2642">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2644">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn205">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn206">2:206</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn207">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rathākṣa</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202643">1</a></sup> denotes the ‘axle of the chariot.’ Its length is given by the scholiast on the Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202643">2</a></sup> as 104 Aṅgulas (‘finger-breadths’), which agrees with the statement in the Āpastamba Śulba <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202643">3</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202643">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 6, 4, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxix. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202643">↑</a> viii. 8, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202643">↑</a> vi. 5 (Bürk, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenläudischen Gesellschaft,</i> 56, 344, 345).</li></ol></div>

रथाह्न्य|రథాహ్న్య|ರಥಾಹ್ನ್ಯ|rathāhnya|rathaahnya|_vei_120@2644|_vei2_pn206
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2643">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2645">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn205">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn206">2:206</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn207">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rathāhnya</b> in the Śatapatha Brāhmana (xii. 2, 3, 12) denotes a ‘day's journey by chariot.’</p></div>

रथिन्|రథిన్|ರಥಿನ್|rathin|_vei_120@2645|_vei2_pn206
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2644">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2646">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn205">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn206">2:206</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn207">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rathin</b> and <b>Rathī</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202645">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202645">2</a></sup> denote ‘one who goes in a chariot,’ an expression which includes both the driver and the warrior who accompanied him. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202645">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="रथिन्">rathin</a>, i. 122, 8; v. 83, 3; vi. 47, 31; viii. 4, 9; x. 40, 5; 51, 6; Rathī, i. 25, 3; ii. 39, 2; iii. 3, 6; v. 87, 8; vii. 39, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202645">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="रथिन्">rathin</a>, Av. iv. 34, 4; vii. 62, 1; 73, 1; xi. 10, 24; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 2, 2, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 26; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 7, 3, 7, etc.; Rathī, <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 7, 15, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 296.</li></ol></div>

रथीतर|రథీతర|ರಥೀತರ|rathītara|rathiitara|ratheetara|_vei_120@2646|_vei2_pn207
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2645">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2647">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn206">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn207">2:207</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn208">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rathītara</b> (‘good charioteer’) is the name of a teacher mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202646"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202646">1</a></sup> and the Bṛhaddevatā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202646"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202646">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202646"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202646">↑</a> xxii. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202646"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202646">↑</a> i. 26; iii. 40; vii. 145 (ed. Macdonell).</li></ol></div>

रथे-ष्ठा|रथेष्ठा|रथे|ष्ठा|రథే-ష్ఠా|రథేష్ఠా|రథే|ష్ఠా|ರಥೇ-ಷ್ಠಾ|ರಥೇಷ್ಠಾ|ರಥೇ|ಷ್ಠಾ|rathe-ṣṭhā|ratheṣṭhā|rathe|ṣṭhā|rathe-ShThaa|ratheShThaa|ShThaa|_vei_120@2647|_vei2_pn207
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2646">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2648">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn206">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn207">2:207</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn208">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rathe-ṣṭhā,</b> ‘standing on the chariot,’ denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202647">1</a></sup> the warrior who fights from the chariot, ‘car-fighter.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202647">↑</a> i. 173, 4. 5; ii. 17, 3; vi. 21, 1; 22, 5; 29, 1; viii. 4, 13; 33, 14; ix. 97, 49; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxii. 32</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 296.</li></ol></div>

रथोपस्थ|రథోపస్థ|ರಥೋಪಸ್ಥ|rathopastha|_vei_120@2648|_vei2_pn207
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2647">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2649">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn206">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn207">2:207</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn208">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rathopastha,</b> ‘lap of the chariot,’ in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202648"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202648">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202648"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202648">2</a></sup> seems to denote the ‘bottom’ or lower part on which the driver and the fighter stand.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202648"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202648">↑</a> viii. 8, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202648"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202648">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 10, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 3, 3, 12, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 238, n.</li></ol></div>

रन्ध्र|రన్ధ్ర|ರನ್ಧ್ರ|randhra|_vei_120@2649|_vei2_pn207
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2648">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2650">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn206">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn207">2:207</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn208">↷</a><hr><p><b>Randhra</b> seems, in the phrase <a class="vei_120a" href="उक्ष्णो">ukṣṇo</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="रन्ध्र">randhra</a> occurring in one passage of the Rigveda (viii. 7, 26), to be the name of a place, but the sense is very doubtful. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 9, 13) <a class="vei_120a" href="उक्ष्णो">ukṣṇo</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="रन्ध्र">randhra</a> is the name of a man.</p></div>

रभि|రభి|ರಭಿ|rabhi|_vei_120@2650|_vei2_pn207
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2649">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2651">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn206">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn207">2:207</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn208">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rabhi,</b> occurring once in the Rigveda (viii. 5, 29), designates some part of the chariot. The term perhaps means ‘supporting shaft.’</p></div>

रम्भ|रंभ|రమ్భ|రంభ|ರಮ್ಭ|ರಂಭ|rambha|_vei_120@2651|_vei2_pn207
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2650">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2652">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn206">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn207">2:207</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn208">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rambha</b> seems to mean a ‘staff’ or ‘support’ in one passage of the Rigveda (viii. 45, 20). In another place (ii. 15, 9) a man is described as Rambhin, apparently as carrying a staff to support himself in old age; Sāyaṇa explains this word as ‘door-keeper’ (like one of the senses of <i>daṇḍin,</i> ‘staff-bearer,’ in later Sanskrit).</p></div>

रम्भिणी|रम्भिणि|रंभिणी|रंभिणि|రమ్భిణీ|రమ్భిణి|రంభిణీ|రంభిణి|ರಮ್ಭಿಣೀ|ರಮ್ಭಿಣಿ|ರಂಭಿಣೀ|ರಂಭಿಣಿ|rambhiṇī|rambhiṇi|rambhiNii|rambhiNi|rambhiNee|_vei_120@2652|_vei2_pn207
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2651">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2653">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn206">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn207">2:207</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn208">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rambhiṇī</b> occurs in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202652"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202652">1</a></sup> as being on the shoulders of the Maruts. A ‘spear’ seems to be meant, perhaps conceived as clinging (<i>rambh = rabk,</i> ‘clasp’) to the shoulders of a man. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202652"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202652">↑</a> i. 168, 3. <i>Cf.</i> i. 167, 3, and see Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 283.</li></ol></div>

रयि|రయి|ರಯಿ|rayi|_vei_120@2653|_vei2_pn208
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2652">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2654">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn207">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn208">2:208</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn209">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rayi</b> is the common word for ‘wealth’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202653">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202653">2</a></sup> Special mention is often made of wealth in ‘heroes’ <i>(<a class="vei_120a" href="वीर">vīra</a>)--i.e.,</i> in ‘good sons,’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202653">3</a></sup> in horses,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202653">4</a></sup> in cattle,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202653">5</a></sup> etc.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202653">↑</a> i. 73, 1; 159, 4; ii. 21, 6; iii. 1, 19; iv. 2, 7; 34, 10; 36, 9; vi. 6, 7; 31, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202653">↑</a> Av. iii. 14, 1; vi. 33, 3; vii. 80, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 1, 72; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ix. 22; xiv. 22; xxvii. 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202653">↑</a> Rv. ii. 11. 13; 30, 11; iv. 51, 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202653">↑</a> Rv. v. 41, 5; viii. 6, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202653">↑</a> Rv. v. 4, 11, etc.</li></ol></div>

रशना|రశనా|ರಶನಾ|raśanā|rashanaa|_vei_120@2654|_vei2_pn208
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2653">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2655">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn207">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn208">2:208</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn209">↷</a><hr><p><b>Raśanā</b> means generally ‘cord’ or ‘rope.’ In the Rigveda the word ofter refers to various fastenings of a horse. In one passage<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202654"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202654">1</a></sup> the expression <i>śīrṣaṇyā <a class="vei_120a" href="रशना">raśanā</a>,</i> ‘head rope,’ perhaps means not so much ‘reins’ as ‘headstall.’ In others<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202654"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202654">2</a></sup> the sense of ‘traces’ seems certain, though sometimes<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202654"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202654">3</a></sup> ‘reins’ or ‘traces’ may equally well be intended. Elsewhere the more general sense of ‘rope’ for stening is meant.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202654"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202654">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202654"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202654">↑</a> i. 162, 8. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रज्जु">rajju</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202654"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202654">↑</a> Rv. i. 163, 2. 5; x. 79, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202654"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202654">↑</a> Rv. iv. 1. 9; ix. 87, 1; x. 18, 14. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 4, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202654"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202654">↑</a> Rv. ii. 28, 5; Av. viii. 78, 1; x. 9, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxi. 46; xxii. 2; xxviii. 33; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 6. 4, 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 6, 3. 10, etc. <i>Cf.</i> the use of <a class="vei_120a" href="रशना">raśanā</a> as equivalent to ‘finger’ in Rv. x. 4, 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 249.</li></ol></div>

रश्मि|रश्मि|రశ్మి_౧|రశ్మి|ರಶ್ಮಿ_೧|ರಶ್ಮಿ|raśmi_1|raśmi|rashmi_1|rashmi|_vei_120@2655|_vei2_pn208
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2654">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2656">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn207">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn208">2:208</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn209">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Raśmi</b> is not rarely found in the sense of ‘rope’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202655"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202655">1</a></sup> generally; but more usually it denotes either the ‘reins’ or the ‘traces’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202655"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202655">2</a></sup> of a chariot, either sense being equally good in most passages.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202655"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202655">↑</a> Rv. i. 28, 4; iv. 22, 8; viii. 25, 18, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 19, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202655"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202655">↑</a> Rv. viii. 7, 8; x. 130, 7, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 4, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 14; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 4, 2, etc. In <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 37, 1, the two inner (<i>antarau</i>) reins or traces of the chariot are mentioned.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 249.</li></ol></div>

रश्मि|रश्मि|రశ్మి_౨|రశ్మి|ರಶ್ಮಿ_೨|ರಶ್ಮಿ|raśmi_2|raśmi|rashmi_2|rashmi|_vei_120@2656|_vei2_pn208
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2655">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2657">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn207">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn208">2:208</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn209">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Raśmi</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202656">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202656">2</a></sup> regularly denotes a ‘ray’ of the sun. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202656">↑</a> i. 35, 7; iv. 52, 7; vii. 36, 1; 77, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202656">↑</a> Av. ii. 32, 1; xii. 1, 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 1, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 2, 3, 14, etc.</li></ol></div>

रसा|రసా|ರಸಾ|rasā|rasaa|_vei_120@2657|_vei2_pn209
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2656">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2658">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn208">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn209">2:209</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn210">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rasā</b> is found in three passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202657"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202657">1</a></sup> clearly as the name of a real stream in the extreme north-west of the Vedic territory. Elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202657"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202657">2</a></sup> it is the name of a mythic stream at the ends of the earth, which as well as the atinosphere it encompasses. It is reasonable to assume that, as in the case of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>,</b> the literal is the older sense, and to see in the river a genuine stream, perhaps originally the Araxes or Jaxartes, because the Vendidad mentions the Raṅhā, the Avestan form of <a class="vei_120a" href="रसा">rasā</a>. But the word seems originally to allude merely to the ‘sap’ or ‘flavour’ of the waters,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202657"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202657">3</a></sup> and so could be applied to every river, like <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202657"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202657">↑</a> i. 112, 12; v. 53, 9; x. 75, 6. In v. 53, 9, the phrase <i>rasānitabhā</i> is found. Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 202, is inclined to regard <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अनितभा">anitabhā</a></i> as an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="रसा">rasā</a>, perhaps for <i>amitabhā,</i> ‘of unmeasured splendour,’ but this is hardly probable. It seems better to take <a class="vei_120a" href="अनितभा">anitabhā</a> as the name of an otherwise unknown river. <i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>India,</i> 166, 173, n.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202657"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202657">↑</a> Rv. v. 41, 15; ix. 41, 6; x. 100, 1, 2 (<i>cf.</i> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 348; <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 19, 100 <i>et seq.</i>); 121, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202657"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202657">↑</a> Rv. iv. 43, 6; viii. 72, 13.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 15, 16; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 323; Brunnhofer, <i>Iran und Turan,</i> 86; Weber, Proceedings of the Berlin Academy, 1898, 567-569.</li></ol></div>

रसाशिर्|రసాశిర్|ರಸಾಶಿರ್|rasāśir|rasaashir|_vei_120@2658|_vei2_pn209
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2657">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2659">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn208">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn209">2:209</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn210">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rasāśir</b> as an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202658">1</a></sup> means ‘mixed with juice’--<i>i.e.,</i> with milk.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202658">↑</a> iii. 48, 1, where Sāyaṇa explains <i>rasa</i> as ‘milk.’ <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 211, n. 5.</li></ol></div>

रह-सू|रहसू|रह|రహ-సూ|రహసూ|రహ|ರಹ-ಸೂ|ರಹಸೂ|ರಹ|raha-sū|rahasū|raha|raha-suu|rahasuu|raha-soo|rahasoo|_vei_120@2659|_vei2_pn209
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2658">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2660">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn208">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn209">2:209</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn210">↷</a><hr><p><b>Raha-sū,</b> ‘bearing in secret,’ is a term applied in one passage of the Rigveda (ii. 29, 1) to an unmarried mother. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a>.</b></p></div>

रहस्यु देव-मलिम्लुच्|रहस्यु देवमलिम्लुच्|रहस्यु देव|मलिम्लुच्|రహస్యు దేవ-మలిమ్లుచ్|రహస్యు దేవమలిమ్లుచ్|రహస్యు దేవ|మలిమ్లుచ్|ರಹಸ್ಯು ದೇವ-ಮಲಿಮ್ಲುಚ್|ರಹಸ್ಯು ದೇವಮಲಿಮ್ಲುಚ್|ರಹಸ್ಯು ದೇವ|ಮಲಿಮ್ಲುಚ್|rahasyu deva-malimluc|rahasyu devamalimluc|rahasyu deva|malimluc|rahasyu deva-malimluch|rahasyu devamalimluch|malimluch|_vei_120@2660|_vei2_pn209
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2659">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2661">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn208">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn209">2:209</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn210">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rahasyu <a class="vei_120a" href="देव">deva</a>-malimluc</b> is the name, in the Pañcavimśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xiv. 4, 7), of a mythical person who at <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मुनिमरण">munimaraṇa</a></b> slew the saintly <b>Vaikhānases.</b></p></div>

रहू-गण|रहूगण|रहू|गण|రహూ-గణ|రహూగణ|రహూ|గణ|ರಹೂ-ಗಣ|ರಹೂಗಣ|ರಹೂ|ಗಣ|rahū-gaṇa|rahūgaṇa|rahū|gaṇa|rahuu-gaNa|rahuugaNa|rahuu|gaNa|rahoo-gaNa|rahoogaNa|rahoo|_vei_120@2661|_vei2_pn209
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2660">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2662">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn208">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn209">2:209</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn210">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rahū-gaṇa</b> is the name of a family mentioned in the plural in one passage of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202661"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202661">1</a></sup> According to Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202661"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202661">2</a></sup> they were connected with the <b>Gotamas,</b> as is shown by the name <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोतम">gotama</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="राहूगण">rāhūgaṇa</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202661"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202661">↑</a> i. 78, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202661"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202661">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 110. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen, Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 236, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

राका|రాకా|ರಾಕಾ|rākā|raakaa|_vei_120@2662|_vei2_pn210
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2661">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2663">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn209">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn210">2:210</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn215">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rākā</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202662"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202662">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202662"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202662">2</a></sup> denotes the full moon day as a personification. <i>Cf.</i> <b>Candramās.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202662"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202662">↑</a> ii. 32, 4; v. 42, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202662"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202662">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 8, 1; iii. 4, 9, 1. 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 37, 2, 6; 47, 4, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvi. 13, 1. etc.</li></ol></div>

राज-कर्तृ|राजकर्तृ|राज|कर्तृ|రాజ-కర్తృ|రాజకర్తృ|రాజ|కర్తృ|ರಾಜ-ಕರ್ತೃ|ರಾಜಕರ್ತೃ|ರಾಜ|ಕರ್ತೃ|rāja-kartṛ|rājakartṛ|rāja|kartṛ|raaja-kartR|raajakartR|raaja|kartR|raaja-kartRi|raajakartRi|kartRi|raaja-kartRu|raajakartRu|kartRu|raaja-kartRRi|raajakartRRi|kartRRi|_vei_120@2663|_vei2_pn210
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2662">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2664">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn209">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn210">2:210</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn215">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rāja-kartṛ,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202663">1</a></sup> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राज">rāja</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्">kṛt</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202663">2</a></sup> ‘king-maker,’ is the term applied in the Atharvaveda and the Brāhmaṇas to those who, ‘not themselves kings,’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202663">3</a></sup> aid in the consecration of the king. In the Śatapatha<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202663">2</a></sup> the persons meant and specified are the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सूत">sūta</a>,</b> ‘charioteer,’ and the <b>Grāmaṇī,</b> ‘village chief,’ probably a representative chief from the village nearest to the place of consecration, as Eggeling<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202663">4</a></sup> suggests. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1202663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1202663">1b</a></sup> according to the commentator's explanation, the father, brother, etc., are meant; in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2b_vei_1202663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2b_vei_1202663">2b</a></sup> also, the meaning of the expression is not stated in the text.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202663">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 17, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202663">↑</a> Av. iii. 5, 7; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 1, 7; xiii. 2, 2, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202663">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202663">↑</a> Av. iii. 5, 7; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 1, 7; xiii. 2, 2, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202663">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 60, n.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 199 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1202663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1202663">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 17. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2b_vei_1202663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2b_vei_1202663">↑</a> Av. iii. 5, 7; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 1, 7; xiii. 2, 2, 18.</li></ol></div>

राज-कुल|राजकुल|राज|कुल|రాజ-కుల|రాజకుల|రాజ|కుల|ರಾಜ-ಕುಲ|ರಾಜಕುಲ|ರಾಜ|ಕುಲ|rāja-kula|rājakula|rāja|kula|raaja-kula|raajakula|raaja|_vei_120@2664|_vei2_pn210
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2663">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2665">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn209">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn210">2:210</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn215">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rāja-kula,</b> a ‘kingly family,’ is mentioned in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 28, 4), where, it is to be noted, such a family is ranked after, not before, a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कुल">kula</a>, a ‘Brahmin family.’</p></div>

राजन्|राजन्|రాజన్_౧|రాజన్|ರಾಜನ್_೧|ರಾಜನ್|rājan_1|rājan|raajan_1|raajan|_vei_120@2665|_vei2_pn210
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2664">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2666">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn209">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn210">2:210</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn215">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Rājan,</b> ‘king,’ is a term repeatedly occurring in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202665">1</a></sup> and the later literature.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202665">2</a></sup> It is quite clear that the normal, though not universal form of government, in early India was that by kings, as might be expected in view of the fact that the Āryan Indians were invaders in a hostile territory: a situation which, as in the case of the Āryan invaders of Greece and of the German invaders of England, resulted almost necessarily in strengthening the monarchic element of the constitution.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202665">3</a></sup> The mere patriarchal organization of society is not sufficient, as Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202665">4</a></sup> assumes, to explain the Vedic kingship. </p><p><i>Tenure of Monarchy.</i>--Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202665">5</a></sup> is of opinion that while the Vedic monarchy was sometimes hereditary, as is indeed shown by several cases where the descent can be traced,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202665">6</a></sup> yet in others the monarchy was elective, though it is not clear whether the selection by the people was between the members of the royal family only or extended to members of all the noble clans. It must, however, be admitted that the evidence for the elective monarchy is not strong. As Geldner<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202665">7</a></sup> argues, all the passages cited<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202665">8</a></sup> can be regarded not as choice by the cantons (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a></b>), but as acceptance by the subjects (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a></i>): this seems the more probable sense. Of course this is no proof that the monarchy was not sometimes elective: the practice of selecting one member of the family to the exclusion of another less well qualified is exemplified by the legend in <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202665">9</a></sup> of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a> brothers, <b>Devāpi</b> and <b>Śantanu,</b> the value of which, as evidence of contemporary views, is not seriously affected by the legend itself being of dubious character and validity.</p><p>Royal power was clearly insecure: there are several references to kings being expelled from their realms, and their efforts to recover their sovereignty,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202665">10</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda contains spells in the interest of royalty.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202665">11</a></sup></p><p><i>The King in War.</i>--Naturally the Vedic texts, after the Rigveda, contain few notices of the warlike adventures that no doubt formed a very considerable proportion of the royal functions. But the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202665">12</a></sup> contains the statement that the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a></b> kings, who, like the Brahmins of  those tribes, stand as representatives of good form, used to make their raids in the dewy season. The word <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उदाज">udāja</a>,</b> too, with its variant Nirāja, records that kings took a share of the booty of war. The Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202665">13</a></sup> has many references to Vedic wars: it is clear that the Kṣatriyas were at least as intent on fulfilling their duty of war as the Brahmins on sacrificing and their other functions. Moreover, beside offensive war, defence was a chief duty of the king: he is emphatically the ‘protector of the tribe’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="गोपा">gopā</a> janasya</i>), or, as is said in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a></b> (‘royal consecration’), ‘protector of the Brahmin.’<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1202665">14</a></sup> His <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a></b> was expected to use his spells and charms to secure the success of his king's arms. The king no doubt fought in person: so <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतर्दन">pratardana</a></b> met death in war according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>;<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1202665">15</a></sup> and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a> the king is invoked as ‘sacker of cities’ (<i>purāṃ bhettā</i>).</p><p><i>The King in Peace.</i>--In return for his warlike services the king received the obedience<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1202665">16</a></sup> --sometimes forced<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1202665">17</a></sup> --of the people, and in particular their contributions for the maintenance of royalty. The king is regularly<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1202665">18</a></sup> regarded as ‘devouring  the people,’ but this phrase must not be explained as meaning that he necessarily oppressed them. It obviously has its origin in a custom by which the king and his retinue were fed by the people's contributions, a plan with many parallels. It is also probable that the king could assign the royal right of maintenance to a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>, thus developing a nobility supported by the people. Taxation would not normally fall on <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> or Brahmin; the texts contam emphatic assertions of the exemption of the goods of the latter from the royal bounty.<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1202665">19</a></sup>In the people, however, lay the strength of the king.<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1202665">20</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बलि">bali</a>.</b></p><p>In return the king performed the duties of judge. Himself immune from punishment (<i>a-daṇḍya</i>), he wields the rod of punishment (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="दण्ड">daṇḍa</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1202665">21</a></sup> It is probable that criminal justice remained largely in his actual administration, for the Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1202665">22</a></sup> preserve clear traces of the personal exercise of royal criminal jurisdiction. Possibly the jurisdiction could be exercised by a royal officer, or even by a delegate, for a <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a> is mentioned as an overseer (<i>adhyakṣa</i>) of the punishment of a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1202665">23</a></sup> In civil justice it may be that the king played a much less prominent part, save as a court of final appeal, but evidence is lacking on this head. The <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यमशी">madhyamaśī</a></b> of the Rigveda was probably not a royal, but a private judge or arbitrator. A wide criminal jurisdiction is, however, to some extent supported<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1202665">24</a></sup> by the frequent mention of Varuṇa's spies, for <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a> is the divine counterpart of the human king.<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1202665">25</a></sup> Possibly such spies could be used in war also.<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_1202665">26</a></sup></p><p>There is no reference in early Vedic literature to the exercise of legislative activitv by the king, though later it is an essential  part of his duties.<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_1202665">27</a></sup> Nor can we say exactly what executive functions devolved on the king.</p><p>In all his acts the king was regularly advised by his <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>;</b> he also had the advantage of the advice of the royal ministers and attendants (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रत्निन्">ratnin</a></b>). The local administration was entrusted to the <b>Grāmaṇī,</b> or village chief, who may have been selected or appointed by the king. The outward signs of the king's rank were his palace<sup id="ref_120_28_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_1202665">28</a></sup> and his brilliant dress.<sup id="ref_120_29_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29_vei_1202665">29</a></sup></p><p><i>The King as Landowner.</i>--The position of the king with regard to the land is somewhat obscure. The Greek notices,<sup id="ref_120_30_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_30_vei_1202665">30</a></sup> in which, unhappily, it would be dangerous to put much trust, since they were collected by observers who were probably little used to accurate investigations of such matters, and whose statements were based on inadequate information, vary in their statements. In part they speak of rent being paid, and declare that only the king and no private person could own land, while in part they refer to the taxation of land. Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_31_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_31_vei_1202665">31</a></sup> is strongly of opinion that the payments made were paid for protection -<i>i.e.,</i> in modern terminology as a tax, but that the king was recognized as the owner of all the land, while yet the individual or the joint family also owned the land. As against BadenPowell,<sup id="ref_120_32_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_32_vei_1202665">32</a></sup> who asserted that the idea of the king as a landowner was later, he urges for the Vedic period that the king, as we have seen, is described as devouring the people, and that, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_33_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_33_vei_1202665">33</a></sup> the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a></b> can be devoured at will and maltreated (but, unlike the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>,</b> not killed); and for the period of the legal Sūtras and Śāstras he cites <a class="vei_120a" href="बृहस्पति">bṛhaspati</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="नारद">nārada</a> as clearly recognizing the king's overlordship, besides a passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Śāstra<sup id="ref_120_34_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_34_vei_1202665">34</a></sup> which describes the king as ‘lord of all,’ a phrase which  Bühler<sup id="ref_120_35_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_35_vei_1202665">35</a></sup> was inclined to interpret as a proof of landowning. The evidence is, however, inadequate to prove what is sought. It is not denied that gradually the king came to be vaguely conceived--as the English king still is--as lord of all the land in a proprietorial sense, but it is far more probable that such an idea was only a gradual development than that it was primitive. The power of devouring the people is a political power, not a right of ownership; precisely the same feature can be traced in South Africa,<sup id="ref_120_36_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_36_vei_1202665">36</a></sup> where the chief can deprive a man arbitrarily of his land, though the land is really owned by the native. The matter is ultimately to some extent one of terminology, but the parallel cases are in favour of distinguishing between the political rights of the crown, which can be transferred by way of a grant, and the rights of ownership. Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_37_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_37_vei_1202665">37</a></sup> thinks that the gifts of land to priests, which seems to be the first sign of land transactions in the Brāhmaṇas, was an actual gift of land; it may have been so in many cases, but it may easily also have been the grant of a superiority: the Epic grants are hardly decisive one way or the other.</p><p>For the relations of the king with the assembly, see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a>;</b> for his consecration, see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>.</b> A-<a class="vei_120a" href="राज">rāja</a>-tā, ‘lack of a king,’ means ‘anarchy.’<sup id="ref_120_38_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_38_vei_1202665">38</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202665">↑</a> iii. 43, 5; v. 54, 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202665">↑</a> Av. iv. 22, 3. 5; viii. 7, 16, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Stubbs, <i>Constitutional History of England,</i> 59 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202665">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 162.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202665">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 162 <i>et seq.</i> So Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 188; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 336.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202665">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वध्र्यश्व">vadhryaśva</a>, Divodāsa, <a class="vei_120a" href="पिजवन">pijavana</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>;</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मित्रातिथि">mitrātithi</a>, Kuruśravaṇa <a class="vei_120a" href="उपमश्रवस्">upamaśravas</a>,</b> etc.; Lanman, <i>Sanskrit Reader,</i> 386. So a ‘kingdom of ten generations’ (<b>Daśapuruṣaṃrājya</b>) is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 9, 3, 3; and <i>cf.</i> v. 4, 2, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 12. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202665">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 303.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202665">↑</a> Rv. x. 124, 8; 173; Av. i. 9; iii. 4; iv. 22.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202665">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202665">↑</a> The technical term is <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अप">apa</a>-ruddha. Cf.</i> Av. iii. 3, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxviii. 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 12, 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 9, 3, 3, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 30; Caland, <i>Altindisches Zauberritual,</i> 37 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202665">↑</a> Especially iii. 3. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 111 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202665">↑</a> i. 8, 4, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202665">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दाशराज्ञ">dāśarājña</a>,</b> Rv. vii. 18. 33, 83, and <i>cf.</i> Rv. iii. 33, 53.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1202665">↑</a> Rv. iii. 43, 5. References to attacks on aborigines are common in the Rigveda--<i>e.g.,</i> ii. 12, 11; iv. 26, 3; vi. 26, 5; 33, 4, etc. For later references to war, <i>cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, ix. 17; x. 3; xxviii. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 4, 8, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 4, 2 <i>et seq.;</i> and Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 187, 215. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a> the protection of the Brahmin is compensated with the ‘eating’ of the <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>, the latter interesting the king more than the older duty of protection. See <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 12. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1202665">↑</a> iii. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1202665">↑</a> See, <i>e.g.,</i> <b>Janaka's</b> offer of the <b>Videhas</b> as slaves to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a>,</b> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 4, 30, and see <i>ibid.,</i> ii. 1, 20; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 6, 10, etc.; Rv. i. 67, 1; iv. 50, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1202665">↑</a> Rv. ix. 7, 5. <i>Cf.</i> vii. 6, 5, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 18, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1202665">↑</a> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बलि">bali</a>,</b> and <i>cf.</i> Rv. i. 65, 4; Av. iv. 22, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 29; viii. 12. 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 12; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 2, 17; iv. 2, 1, 3, 17; v. 3, 3, 12; 4, 2, 3; x. 6, 2, 1; xiii. 2, 9, 6, 8, etc.; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 93, n.; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 246; Pischel and Geldner, <i>Vedische Stadien,</i> 1, xvi; Winternitz, <i>Geschichte der indischen Litteratur,</i> 1, 173, 174; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 161. It is to this form of taxation that the share of village (<i>grāme</i>), horses (<i>aśveṣu</i>), and kine (<i>goṣu</i>) of Av. iv. 22, 2, is to be referred. It is significant that the village and cattle are put on the same footing, as tending to refute the argument that the king was supreme landowner. See n. 31 below. For the rate of taxation, which later was one sixth, <i>cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 85, 86; <i>India, Old and New,</i> 238 <i>et seq.;</i> 333; Mrs. Rhys Davids, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1901, 860.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1202665">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 6, 2, 18; 7, 1, 13. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a></b> (above, 2, 83) for the claim of the Brahmins to serve only king <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>, not the temporal king.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1202665">↑</a> <i>Cf., e.g.,</i> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 8; iii. 11, 8; iv. 4, 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 4, 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1202665">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 4, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1202665">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 43 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1202665">↑</a> xxvii. 4. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>,</b> n. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1202665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 25, 13; iv. 4, 3; vi. 67, 5; vii. 61, 3; 87, 3; x. 10, 8 (= Av. xviii. 1, 9); Av. iv. 16, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1202665">↑</a> See Foy, <i>Die kōnigliche Gewalt,</i> 80 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_1202665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. viii. 47, 11; Foy, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 84. The reference is not certain.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_1202665">↑</a> See Foy, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> chap. iii.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_1202665">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Varuṇa's palace, Rv. ii. 41, 5; vii. 88, 5. The throne, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आसन्दी">āsandī</a>,</b> is used to form the name of <b>Janamejaya's</b> royal city, Asandīvant. <i>Cf.</i> also Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 4, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 12, 3-5.</li><li id="fx_120_29_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29_vei_1202665">↑</a> See, <i>e.g.,</i> Rv. i. 85, 8; viii. 5, 38; x. 78, 1, etc. So the king is the great lord of riches (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="धन">dhana</a>-patir dhanānām</i>), Av. iv. 22, 3, and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 31, he is likened to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="न्यग्रोध">nyagrodha</a></b> tree.</li><li id="fx_120_30_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_30_vei_1202665">↑</a> See Diodorus, ii. 40; Arrian, <i>Indica,</i> 11; Strabo, p. 703, and Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 87 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_31_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_31_vei_1202665">↑</a> <i>India, Old and New,</i> 221 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_32_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_32_vei_1202665">↑</a> <i>Village Communities in India,</i> 145; <i>Indian Village Community,</i> 207 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_33_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_33_vei_1202665">↑</a> vii. 29, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_34_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_34_vei_1202665">↑</a> viii. 39.</li><li id="fx_120_35_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_35_vei_1202665">↑</a> In his note on <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>., Sacred Books of the East,</i> 25, 259.</li><li id="fx_120_36_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_36_vei_1202665">↑</a> See Keith, <i>Journal of the African Society,</i> 6, 202 <i>et seq.</i> The evidence, so far as it goes, of other Āryan peoples does not support the theory of original kingly ownership. Such ownership did not exist, as far as can be seen, in Anglo-Saxon times (<i>English Historical Review,</i> viii. 1-7). nor in Homeric Greece (Lang, <i>Homer and His Age,</i> 236 <i>et seq.</i>), nor at Rome.</li><li id="fx_120_37_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_37_vei_1202665">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_38_vei_1202665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_38_vei_1202665">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 9, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 14, 6; Lévi, <i>La Doctrine du Sacrifice,</i> 74.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 162 <i>et seq.;</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 84 <i>et seq.;</i> Foy, <i>Die ko7nigliche Gewalt nach den Dharmasūtren</i> (Leipzig, 1895); Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India,</i> 46 <i>et seq.; Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1901, 860, 861.</li></ol></div>

राजन्|राजन्|రాజన్_౨|రాజన్|ರಾಜನ್_೨|ರಾಜನ್|rājan_2|rājan|raajan_2|raajan|_vei_120@2666|_vei2_pn215
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2665">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2667">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn210">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn215">2:215</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn216">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Rājan</b> in several passages-means no more than a ‘noble of the ruling house,’ or perhaps even merely a ‘noble,’ there being</p><p>1 <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 40, 8; 108, 7; x. 42, 10; 97, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 6, 8, 3; v. 7, 6, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xl. 13; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 48; xxvi. 2; Av. xix. 62, 1, and possibly ii. 6, 4, etc.; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 236, 237. Possibly <i>rājñaḥ</i> in Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 5, may be taken in this sense. The king there is said to be a non-Āryan, but the reading is corrupt, and Oertel's conjecture is not probable. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राज्य">rājya</a>,</b> n. 2.  no decisive passage. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202666">2</a></sup> sees traces in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202666">3</a></sup> that in times of peace there was no king in some states, the members of the royal family holding equal rights. He compares this with the state of affairs in early Germany.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202666">4</a></sup> But the passage merely shows that the nobles could be called <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a>, and is not decisive for the sense ascribed to it by Zimmer. Of course this state of affairs is perfectly possible, and is exemplified later in Buddhist times.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202666">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="2"><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202666">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 176, 177.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202666">↑</a> x. 97, 6. He also compares Av. i. 9; iii. 4; iv. 22, where the king is referred to as superior to the other royal personages.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202666">↑</a> The case of the Cherusci and Arminius' attempt to make himself king, which his relatives, the royal family, foiled (see Tacitus, <i>Annals,</i> ii. 88).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202666">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India,</i> 19.</li></ol></div>

राजनि|రాజని|ರಾಜನಿ|rājani|raajani|_vei_120@2667|_vei2_pn216
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2666">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2668">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn215">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn216">2:216</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn217">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rājani,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रजन">rajana</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उग्रदेव">ugradeva</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xiv. 3, 17; xxiii. 16, 11) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka (v. 4. 12).</p></div>

राजन्य|రాజన్య|ರಾಜನ್ಯ|rājanya|raajanya|_vei_120@2668|_vei2_pn216
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2667">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2669">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn215">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn216">2:216</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn217">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rājanya</b> is the regular term in Vedic literature<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202668"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202668">1</a></sup> for a man of the royal family, probably including also those who were not actually members of that family, but were nobles, though it may have been originally restricted to members of the royal family. This, however, does not appear clearly from any passage; the term may originally have applied to all the nobles irrespective of kingly power. In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202668"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202668">2</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a> is different from the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजपुत्र">rājaputra</a>, who is literally a son of the king. The functions and place of the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a> are described under <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>,</b> which expression later normally takes the place of <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a> as a designation for the ruling class. His high place is shown by the fact that in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202668"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202668">3</a></sup> he is ranked with the learned Brahmin and the  <b>Grāmaṇī</b> (who was a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a></b>) as having reached the height of prosperity (<i>gata-<a class="vei_120a" href="श्री">śrī</a></i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202668"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202668">↑</a> Only once in the Rv. in the late <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुष">puruṣa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="सूक्त">sūkta</a>, x. 90, 12; but often in the Av.: v. 17, 9; 18, 2; vi. 38, 4; x. 10, 18; xii. 4, 32 <i>et seq.;</i> xv. 8, 1; xix. 32, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 4, 13, 1; 5, 4, 4; 10, 1; v. 1, 10, 3, etc. Even in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, where, on the whole, the later use of <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> prevails, the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a> is often mentioned. See Eggeling's index, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 561.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202668"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202668">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> xiii. 4, 2, 17, with xiii. 1, 6, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202668"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202668">↑</a> ii. 5, 4, 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 258 <i>et seq.;</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 191. It is quite likely that the noble families not related to the royal family were families of minor princes whose rule was merged in that of the king on the formation of a powerful tribe, as was the case in Germany.</li></ol></div>

राजन्य-बन्धु|राजन्यबन्धु|राजन्य|बन्धु|రాజన్య-బన్ధు|రాజన్యబన్ధు|రాజన్య|బన్ధు|ರಾಜನ್ಯ-ಬನ್ಧು|ರಾಜನ್ಯಬನ್ಧು|ರಾಜನ್ಯ|ಬನ್ಧು|rājanya-bandhu|rājanyabandhu|rājanya|bandhu|raajanya-bandhu|raajanyabandhu|raajanya|_vei_120@2669|_vei2_pn217
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2668">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2670">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn216">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn217">2:217</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn218">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rājanya-bandhu</b> denotes a <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a>, but usually with a depreciating sense. Thus in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202669">1</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a></b> is called by the Brahmins, whom he defeated in disputation, ‘a fellow of a Rājanya’; the same description is applied to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रवाहण जैवलि">pravāhaṇa jaivali</a></b> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202669">2</a></sup> for a similar reason. On the other hand, in one passage<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202669">3</a></sup> where reference is made to men eating apart from women, princes are said to do so most of all: the term <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्यबन्धु">rājanyabandhu</a> cannot here be deemed to be contemptuous, unless, indeed, it is the expression of Brahmin contempt for princes, such as clearly appears in the treatment of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नग्नजित्">nagnajit</a></b> in another passage.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202669">4</a></sup> Again, in a passage<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202669">5</a></sup> in which the four castes are mentioned, the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a></b> precedes the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्यबन्धु">rājanyabandhu</a>, a curious inversion of the order of the second and third castes.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202669">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202669">↑</a> xi. 6, 2, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202669">↑</a> vi. 1, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202669">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 5, 2. 10, where <i>cf.</i> Eggeling's note, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 43, 370, n. 1. A similar case is apparently i. 2, 4, 2, where any special contempt cannot be meant.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202669">↑</a> viii. 1, 4, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 515.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202669">↑</a> i. 1, 4, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202669">↑</a> Eggeling, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 12, 28.</li></ol></div>

राजन्य-र्षि|राजन्यर्षि|राजन्य|र्षि|రాజన్య-ర్షి|రాజన్యర్షి|రాజన్య|ర్షి|ರಾಜನ್ಯ-ರ್ಷಿ|ರಾಜನ್ಯರ್ಷಿ|ರಾಜನ್ಯ|ರ್ಷಿ|rājanya-rṣi|rājanyarṣi|rājanya|rṣi|raajanya-rShi|raajanyarShi|raajanya|rShi|_vei_120@2670|_vei2_pn217
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2669">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2671">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn216">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn217">2:217</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn218">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rājanya-rṣi,</b> ‘royal sage,’ is a term applied to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धुक्षित्">sindhukṣit</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202670"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202670">1</a></sup> The story about him is, however, purely mythical.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202670"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202670">↑</a> xii. 12, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 235, n. 3, and see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a></b> (p. 261).</li></ol></div>

राज-पति|राजपति|राज|पति|రాజ-పతి|రాజపతి|రాజ|పతి|ರಾಜ-ಪತಿ|ರಾಜಪತಿ|ರಾಜ|ಪತಿ|rāja-pati|rājapati|rāja|pati|raaja-pati|raajapati|raaja|_vei_120@2671|_vei2_pn217
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2670">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2672">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn216">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn217">2:217</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn218">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rāja-pati,</b> ‘lord of kings,’ is found in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xi. 4, 3, 9) as an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>. It is not used elsewhere as a title of imperial temporal supremacy: see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राज्य">rājya</a>.</b> </p></div>

राज-पितृ|राजपितृ|राज|पितृ|రాజ-పితృ|రాజపితృ|రాజ|పితృ|ರಾಜ-ಪಿತೃ|ರಾಜಪಿತೃ|ರಾಜ|ಪಿತೃ|rāja-pitṛ|rājapitṛ|rāja|pitṛ|raaja-pitR|raajapitR|raaja|pitR|raaja-pitRi|raajapitRi|pitRi|raaja-pitRu|raajapitRu|pitRu|raaja-pitRRi|raajapitRRi|pitRRi|_vei_120@2672|_vei2_pn218
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2671">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2673">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn217">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn218">2:218</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn219">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rāja-pitṛ</b> is one of the titles given to the king in the rite of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a></b> (‘royal consecration’) according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 12, 5; 17, 5). It probably designates the king as ‘father of a king,’ and indicates the hereditary character of the monarchy. Possibly the later plan<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202672">1</a></sup> of associating the king's son in the monarchy prevailed in earlier times also.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202672">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 139. The sense of ‘having a king as father’ is also possible.</li></ol></div>

राज-पुत्र|राजपुत्र|राज|पुत्र|రాజ-పుత్ర|రాజపుత్ర|రాజ|పుత్ర|ರಾಜ-ಪುತ್ರ|ರಾಜಪುತ್ರ|ರಾಜ|ಪುತ್ರ|rāja-putra|rājaputra|rāja|putra|raaja-putra|raajaputra|raaja|_vei_120@2673|_vei2_pn218
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2672">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2674">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn217">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn218">2:218</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn219">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rāja-putra,</b> ‘king's son,’ ‘prince,’ seems to be capable of being interpreted literally in every passage of the older literature<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202673">1</a></sup> in which it is found, though it may also be capable of a wider interpretation.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202673">2</a></sup> Later the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजपुत्र">rājaputra</a> degenerates into a mere ‘landowner.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202673">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202673">↑</a> Rv. x. 40, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 17, 6 (of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>,</b> but probably in a mythical sense); <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xix. 1, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xiv. 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8, 5, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 2, 5; 5, 2, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202673">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxviii. 1, may be cited as identifying the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a></b> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजपुत्र">rājaputra</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202673">↑</a> Jolly, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 50, 514, who points out that in the Rājataraṅgiṇī, vii. 360, traces of the older position of the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजपुत्र">rājaputra</a> are seen.</li></ol></div>

राज-पुरुष|राजपुरुष|राज|पुरुष|రాజ-పురుష|రాజపురుష|రాజ|పురుష|ರಾಜ-ಪುರುಷ|ರಾಜಪುರುಷ|ರಾಜ|ಪುರುಷ|rāja-puruṣa|rājapuruṣa|rāja|puruṣa|raaja-puruSha|raajapuruSha|raaja|puruSha|_vei_120@2674|_vei2_pn218
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2673">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2675">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn217">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn218">2:218</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn219">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rāja-puruṣa</b> denotes a ‘royal servant’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> (ii. 3). <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पूरुष">pūruṣa</a>.</b></p></div>

राज-भ्रातृ|राजभ्रातृ|राज|भ्रातृ|రాజ-భ్రాతృ|రాజభ్రాతృ|రాజ|భ్రాతృ|ರಾಜ-ಭ್ರಾತೃ|ರಾಜಭ್ರಾತೃ|ರಾಜ|ಭ್ರಾತೃ|rāja-bhrātṛ|rājabhrātṛ|rāja|bhrātṛ|raaja-bhraatR|raajabhraatR|raaja|bhraatR|raaja-bhraatRi|raajabhraatRi|bhraatRi|raaja-bhraatRu|raajabhraatRu|bhraatRu|raaja-bhraatRRi|raajabhraatRRi|bhraatRRi|_vei_120@2675|_vei2_pn218
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2674">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2676">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn217">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn218">2:218</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn219">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rāja-bhrātṛ,</b> the ‘brother of the king,’ is mentioned as one of the eight <b>Vīras,</b> or supporters of the monarchy, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202675">1</a></sup> He is also alluded to elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202675">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202675">↑</a> xix. 1, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 30, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202675">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 13, 18, etc.</li></ol></div>

राज-मात्र|राजमात्र|राज|मात्र|రాజ-మాత్ర|రాజమాత్ర|రాజ|మాత్ర|ರಾಜ-ಮಾತ್ರ|ರಾಜಮಾತ್ರ|ರಾಜ|ಮಾತ್ರ|rāja-mātra|rājamātra|rāja|mātra|raaja-maatra|raajamaatra|raaja|maatra|_vei_120@2676|_vei2_pn218
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2675">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2677">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn217">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn218">2:218</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn219">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rāja-mātra</b> is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xxvii. 6) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (xvii. 5, 3. 4; 15, 3), where it seems to include ‘the whole class of persons (who could be called) Rājan’--<i>i.e.,</i> the <b>Rājaputras</b> and the <b>Rājanyas.</b> </p></div>

राज-यक्ष्म|राजयक्ष्म|राज|यक्ष्म|రాజ-యక్ష్మ|రాజయక్ష్మ|రాజ|యక్ష్మ|ರಾಜ-ಯಕ್ಷ್ಮ|ರಾಜಯಕ್ಷ್ಮ|ರಾಜ|ಯಕ್ಷ್ಮ|rāja-yakṣma|rājayakṣma|rāja|yakṣma|raaja-yakShma|raajayakShma|raaja|yakShma|raaja-yaxma|raajayaxma|yaxma|_vei_120@2677|_vei2_pn219
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2676">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2678">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn218">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn219">2:219</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn220">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rāja-yakṣma,</b> ‘royal sickness,’ is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202677">1</a></sup> and several times later on.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202677">2</a></sup> Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202677">3</a></sup> identifies it with consumption: this identification seems certain, being supported by the later view of the disease.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202677">4</a></sup> Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202677">5</a></sup> suggests ‘king's evil,’ or syphilis, as the sense, but this is not probable.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202677">↑</a> i. 161, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202677">↑</a> Av. xi. 3, 39; xii. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 5, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 3; xxvii. 3; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202677">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 375 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202677">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Wise, <i>System of Hindu Medicine,</i> 321 <i>et seq.;</i> Jolly, <i>Medicin,</i> 88, 89, n. 2, who takes <a class="vei_120a" href="राजयक्ष्म">rājayakṣma</a> as denoting the worst of diseases, not the disease cured by the king. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजाश्व">rājāśva</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202677">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 697. But contrast <i>ibid.,</i> 415.</li></ol></div>

राज-सूय|राजसूय|राज|सूय|రాజ-సూయ|రాజసూయ|రాజ|సూయ|ರಾಜ-ಸೂಯ|ರಾಜಸೂಯ|ರಾಜ|ಸೂಯ|rāja-sūya|rājasūya|rāja|sūya|raaja-suuya|raajasuuya|raaja|suuya|raaja-sooya|raajasooya|sooya|_vei_120@2678|_vei2_pn219
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2677">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2679">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn218">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn219">2:219</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn220">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rāja-sūya</b> is the name in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202678">1</a></sup> and the later literature<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202678">2</a></sup> of the ceremony of the ‘royal consecration.’ The rite is described at great length in the Sūtras,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202678">3</a></sup> but its main features are clearly outlined in the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202678">4</a></sup> while the verses used in the ceremony are preserved in the Saṃhitās of the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202678">5</a></sup> Besides much mere priestly elaboration, the ritual contains traces of popular ceremonial. For example, the king is clothed in the ceremonial garments of his rank, and provided with bow and arrow as emblems of sovereignty. He is formally anointed; he performs a mimic cow raid against a relative of his;<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202678">6</a></sup> or engages in a sham fight with a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202678">7</a></sup> A game of dice is played in which he is made to be the victim;<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202678">8</a></sup> he symbolically ascends the quarters of the sky as an indication of his universal rule; and steps on a tiger skin, thus gaining the strength and the pre-eminence of the tiger. </p><p>A list of the consecrated kings is given in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202678">9</a></sup> where the royal inauguration is called the ‘great unction’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="महाभिषेक">mahābhiṣeka</a></i>) connected with <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>. It corresponds generally with a list of Aśvamedhins, ‘performers of the horse sacrifice,’ given in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202678">10</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202678">11</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202678">↑</a> iv. 8, 1; xi. 7, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202678">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 6, 2, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 15, 8; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1, 1, 12, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202678">↑</a> See Weber, <i>Über die Ko7nigsweihe, den <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>;</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Rituallitteratur,</i> 144-147; Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 472, 491. The relation of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a></b> episode formed part of the ritual. That this points to human sacrifice having once formed part of the ritual of the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a> as supposed by Hillebrandt, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Weber, 47; and Oldenberg, 366, n. 1, seems very doubtful. <i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1907, 844, 845.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202678">↑</a> Especially Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 2, 3, 1 <i>et seq.</i> See also Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 1, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202678">↑</a> See <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 6; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202678">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 3, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202678">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 15 with commentary; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 100, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202678">↑</a> See 2. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a></b> (p. 3)</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202678">↑</a> viii. 21-23. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 8.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202678">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202678">↑</a> xvi. 9.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, xxiv, xxv.</li></ol></div>

राज-स्तम्बायन|राजस्तम्बायन|राज|स्तम्बायन|राज-स्तंबायन|राजस्तंबायन|स्तंबायन|రాజ-స్తమ్బాయన|రాజస్తమ్బాయన|రాజ|స్తమ్బాయన|రాజ-స్తంబాయన|రాజస్తంబాయన|స్తంబాయన|ರಾಜ-ಸ್ತಮ್ಬಾಯನ|ರಾಜಸ್ತಮ್ಬಾಯನ|ರಾಜ|ಸ್ತಮ್ಬಾಯನ|ರಾಜ-ಸ್ತಂಬಾಯನ|ರಾಜಸ್ತಂಬಾಯನ|ಸ್ತಂಬಾಯನ|rāja-stambāyana|rājastambāyana|rāja|stambāyana|raaja-stambaayana|raajastambaayana|raaja|stambaayana|_vei_120@2679|_vei2_pn220
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2678">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2680">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn219">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn220">2:220</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn221">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rāja-stambāyana,</b> ‘descendant of Rājastamba,’ is the patronymic of <b>Yajñavacas</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202679"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202679">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202679"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202679">↑</a> x. 4, 2, 1 (oxytone), 6, 5, 9 (proparoxytone). No stress need be laid on the accents of the Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.</li></ol></div>

राजाधिराज|రాజాధిరాజ|ರಾಜಾಧಿರಾಜ|rājādhirāja|raajaadhiraaja|_vei_120@2680|_vei2_pn220
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2679">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2681">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn219">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn220">2:220</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn221">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rājādhirāja,</b> ‘king of kings,’ later a title of paramount sovereignty, is only found in Vedic literature in the late <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka (i. 31, 6) as a divine epithet.</p></div>

राजाश्व|రాజాశ్వ|ರಾಜಾಶ್ವ|rājāśva|raajaashva|_vei_120@2681|_vei2_pn220
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2680">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2682">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn219">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn220">2:220</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn221">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rājāśva</b> (‘king's horse’) in the Atharvaveda (vi. 102, 2) seems merely to denote a powerful horse.</p></div>

राज्ञी|राज्ञि|రాజ్ఞీ|రాజ్ఞి|ರಾಜ್ಞೀ|ರಾಜ್ಞಿ|rājñī|rājñi|raaj~nii|raaj~ni|raaj~nee|_vei_120@2682|_vei2_pn220
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2681">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2683">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn219">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn220">2:220</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn221">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rājñī,</b> ‘queen,’ is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202682">1</a></sup> and in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202682">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202682">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 6, 2; 4, 2, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 8, 3, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 3, 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiv. 13; xv. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202682">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 2, 6, 2; iii. 11, 3, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 23, 2, etc.</li></ol></div>

राज्य|రాజ్య|ರಾಜ್ಯ|rājya|raajya|_vei_120@2683|_vei2_pn220
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2682">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2684">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn219">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn220">2:220</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn221">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rājya</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202683">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202683">2</a></sup> regularly denotes ‘sovereign power,’ from which, as the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202683">3</a></sup> notes, the Brahmin is excluded.</p><p>In addition to <a class="vei_120a" href="राज्य">rājya</a>, the texts give other expressions of sovereign power. Thus the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202683">4</a></sup> contends  that the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a> sacrifice is that of a king, the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वाजपेय">vājapeya</a></b> that of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सम्राज्">samrāj</a> or emperor, the status of the latter (<a class="vei_120a" href="साम्राज्य">sāmrājya</a>) being superior to that of the former (<a class="vei_120a" href="राज्य">rājya</a>). The sitting on a throne (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="आसन्दी">āsandī</a></b>) is given in the same text<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202683">5</a></sup> as one of the characteristics of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सम्राज्">samrāj</a>. Elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202683">6</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="स्वाराज्य">svārājya</a>, ‘uncontrolled dominion,’ is opposed to <a class="vei_120a" href="राज्य">rājya</a>. In the ritual of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a></b> the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202683">7</a></sup> gives a whole series of terms: <a class="vei_120a" href="राज्य">rājya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="साम्राज्य">sāmrājya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="भौज्य">bhaujya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="स्वाराज्य">svārājya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वैराज्य">vairājya</a>, Pārameṣṭhya, and <a class="vei_120a" href="माहाराज्य">māhārājya</a>, while Ādhipatya, ‘supreme power,’ is found elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202683">8</a></sup> But there is no reason to believe that these terms refer to essentially different forms of authority. A king might be called a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="महाराज">mahārāja</a></b> or a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सम्राज्">samrāj</a>,</b> without really being an overlord of kings; he would be so termed if he were an important sovereign, or by his own entourage out of compliment, as was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a></b> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202683">9</a></sup> That a really great monarchy of the Aśoka or <a class="vei_120a" href="गुप्त">gupta</a> type ever existed in the Vedic period seems highly improbable.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202683">10</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202683">↑</a> iii. 4, 2; iv. 8, 1; xi. 6, 15; xii. 3, 31; xviii. 4, 31.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202683">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 3, 4; 6, 6, 5; vii. 5, 8, 3, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 23, etc.; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 5, as emended by Roth, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, ccxliii.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202683">↑</a> v. 1, 1, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202683">↑</a> v. 1, 1, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202683">↑</a> xii. 8, 3, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202683">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xiv. 5; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 11, 5. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 2, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202683">↑</a> viii. 12, 4. 5. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvii. 16, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202683">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xv. 3, 35; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 2, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202683">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 3, 1, 2. 6; 2, 2, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202683"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202683">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 30.</li></ol></div>

रात्री|रात्रि|రాత్రీ|రాత్రి|ರಾತ್ರೀ|ರಾತ್ರಿ|rātrī|rātri|raatrii|raatri|raatree|_vei_120@2684|_vei2_pn221
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2683">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2685">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn220">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn221">2:221</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn222">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rātrī</b> is the most usual word in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202684"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202684">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202684"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202684">2</a></sup> for ‘night.’ <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202684"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202684">↑</a> i. 35, 1; 94, 7; 113, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202684"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202684">↑</a> Av. i. 16, 1; v. 5, 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

राथीतर|రాథీతర|ರಾಥೀತರ|rāthītara|raathiitara|raatheetara|_vei_120@2685|_vei2_pn221
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2684">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2686">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn220">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn221">2:221</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn222">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rāthītara,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="रथीतर">rathītara</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सत्यवचस्">satyavacas</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (i. 9, 1), and occurs several times as the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (vii. 4, etc.).</p></div>

राथीतरी-पुत्र|राथीतरीपुत्र|राथीतरी|राथीतरि|पुत्र|రాథీతరీ-పుత్ర|రాథీతరీపుత్ర|రాథీతరీ|రాథీతరి|పుత్ర|ರಾಥೀತರೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ರಾಥೀತರೀಪುತ್ರ|ರಾಥೀತರೀ|ರಾಥೀತರಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|rāthītarī-putra|rāthītarīputra|rāthītarī|rāthītari|putra|raathiitarii-putra|raathiitariiputra|raathiitarii|raathiitari|raatheetaree-putra|raatheetareeputra|raatheetaree|raatheetari|_vei_120@2686|_vei2_pn221
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2685">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2687">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn220">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn221">2:221</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn222">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rāthītarī-putra,</b> ‘son of a female descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="रथीतर">rathītara</a>,’ is the name of a teacher in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, the pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भालुकी-पुत्र">bhālukī-putra</a>,</b> according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension (vi. 5, 1), of the <b>Krauñcikīputras</b> according to the Mādhyaṃdina (vi. 4, 32). </p></div>

राध गौतम|రాధ గౌతమ|ರಾಧ ಗೌತಮ|rādha gautama|raadha gautama|_vei_120@2687|_vei2_pn222
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2686">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2688">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn221">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn222">2:222</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn223">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rādha Gautama</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोतम">gotama</a></b>’) is the name of two teachers in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202687"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202687">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202687"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202687">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373, 384.</li></ol></div>

राधेय|రాధేయ|ರಾಧೇಯ|rādheya|raadheya|_vei_120@2688|_vei2_pn222
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2687">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2689">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn221">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn222">2:222</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn223">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rādheya,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="राधा">rādhā</a>,’ is the metronymic of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka (vii. 6).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 372.</p></div>

राम|राम|రామ_౧|రామ|ರಾಮ_೧|ರಾಮ|rāma_1|rāma|raama_1|raama|_vei_120@2689|_vei2_pn222
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2688">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2690">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn221">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn222">2:222</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn223">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Rāma</b> is the name of a man in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202689"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202689">1</a></sup> Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202689"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202689">2</a></sup> thinks that he bore the patronymic <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मायव">māyava</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202689"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202689">3</a></sup> but this is doubtful.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202689"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202689">↑</a> x. 93, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202689"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202689">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 166.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202689"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202689">↑</a> Rv. x. 93, 15.</li></ol></div>

राम औप-तस्विनि|राम औप-तस्विनि|राम औपतस्विनि|राम औप|तस्विनि|రామ ఔప-తస్విని_౨|రామ ఔప-తస్విని|రామ ఔపతస్విని|రామ ఔప|తస్విని|ರಾಮ ಔಪ-ತಸ್ವಿನಿ_೨|ರಾಮ ಔಪ-ತಸ್ವಿನಿ|ರಾಮ ಔಪತಸ್ವಿನಿ|ರಾಮ ಔಪ|ತಸ್ವಿನಿ|rāma aupa-tasvini_2|rāma aupa-tasvini|rāma aupatasvini|rāma aupa|tasvini|raama aupa-tasvini_2|raama aupa-tasvini|raama aupatasvini|raama aupa|_vei_120@2690|_vei2_pn222
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2689">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2691">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn221">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn222">2:222</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn223">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Rāma <a class="vei_120a" href="औप">aupa</a>-tasvini</b> (‘descendant of Upatasvina’) is the name of a teacher in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iv. 6, 1, 7).</p></div>

राम क्रातु-जातेय|राम क्रातु-जातेय|राम क्रातुजातेय|राम क्रातु|जातेय|రామ క్రాతు-జాతేయ_౩|రామ క్రాతు-జాతేయ|రామ క్రాతుజాతేయ|రామ క్రాతు|జాతేయ|ರಾಮ ಕ್ರಾತು-ಜಾತೇಯ_೩|ರಾಮ ಕ್ರಾತು-ಜಾತೇಯ|ರಾಮ ಕ್ರಾತುಜಾತೇಯ|ರಾಮ ಕ್ರಾತು|ಜಾತೇಯ|rāma krātu-jāteya_3|rāma krātu-jāteya|rāma krātujāteya|rāma krātu|jāteya|raama kraatu-jaateya_3|raama kraatu-jaateya|raama kraatujaateya|raama kraatu|jaateya|_vei_120@2691|_vei2_pn222
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2690">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2692">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn221">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn222">2:222</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn223">↷</a><hr><p>3. <b>Rāma <a class="vei_120a" href="क्रातु">krātu</a>-jāteya</b> (‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="क्रतु">kratu</a>-jāta’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैयाघ्र-पद्य">vaiyāghra-padya</a></b> (‘descendant of Vyāghrapad’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शंग शाट्यायनि">śaṅga śāṭyāyani</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="आत्रेय">ātreya</a>,</b> who is mentioned in two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 40, 1; iv. 16, 1).</p></div>

राम मार्ग-वेय|राम मार्ग-वेय|राम मार्गवेय|राम मार्ग|वेय|రామ మార్గ-వేయ_౪|రామ మార్గ-వేయ|రామ మార్గవేయ|రామ మార్గ|వేయ|ರಾಮ ಮಾರ್ಗ-ವೇಯ_೪|ರಾಮ ಮಾರ್ಗ-ವೇಯ|ರಾಮ ಮಾರ್ಗವೇಯ|ರಾಮ ಮಾರ್ಗ|ವೇಯ|rāma mārga-veya_4|rāma mārga-veya|rāma mārgaveya|rāma mārga|veya|raama maarga-veya_4|raama maarga-veya|raama maargaveya|raama maarga|_vei_120@2692|_vei2_pn222
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2691">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2693">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn221">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn222">2:222</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn223">↷</a><hr><p>4. <b>Rāma <a class="vei_120a" href="मार्ग">mārga</a>-veya</b> is the name of a man of the priestly family of the <b>Śyāparṇas</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202692"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202692">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202692"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202692">↑</a> vii. 27, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 43, 345, n.; <b>Muir</b> <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 438.</li></ol></div>

रामकायन|రామకాయన|ರಾಮಕಾಯನ|rāmakāyana|raamakaayana|_vei_120@2693|_vei2_pn222
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2692">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2694">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn221">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn222">2:222</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn223">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rāmakāyana.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बस्त">basta</a>.</b></p></div>

रामा|రామా|ರಾಮಾ|rāmā|raamaa|_vei_120@2694|_vei2_pn222
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2693">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2695">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn221">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn222">2:222</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn223">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rāmā</b> in a few passages<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202694"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202694">1</a></sup> seems to have the sense of a ‘hetaera.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202694"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202694">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 6, 8, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, v. 8, 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxii. 7. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 74, 84.</li></ol></div>

रायो-वाज|रायोवाज|रायो|वाज|రాయో-వాజ|రాయోవాజ|రాయో|వాజ|ರಾಯೋ-ವಾಜ|ರಾಯೋವಾಜ|ರಾಯೋ|ವಾಜ|rāyo-vāja|rāyovāja|rāyo|vāja|raayo-vaaja|raayovaaja|raayo|vaaja|_vei_120@2695|_vei2_pn222
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2694">«</a>pada9<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2696">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn221">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn222">2:222</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn223">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rāyo-vāja</b> is the name of a seer of Sāmans or chants in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 1, 4; xiii. 4, 17; <i>cf.</i> xxiv. 1, 7). </p></div>

राष्ट्र|రాష్ట్ర|ರಾಷ್ಟ್ರ|rāṣṭra|raaShTra|_vei_120@2696|_vei2_pn223
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2695">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2697">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn222">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn223">2:223</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn224">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rāṣṭra</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202696"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202696">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202696"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202696">2</a></sup> denotes ‘kingdom’ or ‘royal territory.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202696"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202696">↑</a> iv. 42, 1; vii. 34, 11; 84, 2; x. 109, 3; 124, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202696"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202696">↑</a> Av. x. 3, 12; xii. 1, 8; xiii. 1, 35; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ix. 23; xx. 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 10, 3; iii. 5, 7, 3; v. 7, 4, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 1, 13, etc.; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 3, 7; 7, 4; 8, 6; iv. 6, 3.</li></ol></div>

राष्ट्र-गोप|राष्ट्रगोप|राष्ट्र|गोप|రాష్ట్ర-గోప|రాష్ట్రగోప|రాష్ట్ర|గోప|ರಾಷ್ಟ್ರ-ಗೋಪ|ರಾಷ್ಟ್ರಗೋಪ|ರಾಷ್ಟ್ರ|ಗೋಪ|rāṣṭra-gopa|rāṣṭragopa|rāṣṭra|gopa|raaShTra-gopa|raaShTragopa|raaShTra|_vei_120@2697|_vei2_pn223
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2696">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2698">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn222">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn223">2:223</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn224">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rāṣṭra-gopa,</b> ‘protector of the realm,’ is the epithet applied, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 25), to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>,</b> whose special charge it was to preserve the king and realm from harm by his spells and rites.</p></div>

रासभ|రాసభ|ರಾಸಭ|rāsabha|raasabha|_vei_120@2698|_vei2_pn223
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2697">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2699">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn222">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn223">2:223</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn224">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rāsabha</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202698"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202698">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202698"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202698">2</a></sup> denotes an ‘ass.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202698"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202698">↑</a> i. 34, 9; 116, 2; 162, 21; iii. 53, 5; viii. 85, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202698"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202698">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1, 5, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xviii. 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 1, 1 11; 3, 1, 23; 2, 3; 4, 4, 3, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 233; Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 149, who suggests ‘mule’ as a possible sense in Rv. iii. 53, 5.</li></ol></div>

रास्ना|రాస్నా|ರಾಸ್ನಾ|rāsnā|raasnaa|_vei_120@2699|_vei2_pn223
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2698">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2700">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn222">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn223">2:223</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn224">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rāsnā</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202699"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202699">1</a></sup> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> denotes ‘girdle’ or ‘band,’ like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रशना">raśanā</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रश्मि">raśmi</a>.</b></p><p>vi. 2, 2, 25; 5, 2, 11, 13. <i>Cf. rāsnāva,</i> ‘girdled,’ iv. 1, 5, 19.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202699"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202699">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, i. 30; xi. 59; xxxviii. 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 1, 2, 2; iv. 1, 5, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, i. 2; xvi. 5; xix. 6, etc.</li></ol></div>

राहु|రాహు|ರಾಹು|rāhu|raahu|_vei_120@2700|_vei2_pn223
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2699">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2701">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn222">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn223">2:223</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn224">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rāhu,</b> the demon that eclipses the sun, seems to be referred to in one passage of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202700"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202700">1</a></sup> The reading here is somewhat uncertain, but <a class="vei_120a" href="राहु">rāhu</a> is probably meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202700"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202700">↑</a> xix. 9, 10. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, 100; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 87; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 914.</li></ol></div>

राहू-गण|राहूगण|राहू|गण|రాహూ-గణ|రాహూగణ|రాహూ|గణ|ರಾಹೂ-ಗಣ|ರಾಹೂಗಣ|ರಾಹೂ|ಗಣ|rāhū-gaṇa|rāhūgaṇa|rāhū|gaṇa|raahuu-gaNa|raahuugaNa|raahuu|gaNa|raahoo-gaNa|raahoogaNa|raahoo|_vei_120@2701|_vei2_pn223
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2700">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2702">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn222">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn223">2:223</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn224">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rāhū-gaṇa,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रहू-गण">rahū-gaṇa</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोतम">gotama</a></b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202701"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202701">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202701"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202701">↑</a> 1. 4, 1, 10. 18; xi. 4, 3, 20. <i>Cf.</i> also Sāyaṇa on Rv. i. 81, 3; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 151, 152; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 2, 8.</li></ol></div>

रिक्थ|రిక్థ|ರಿಕ್ಥ|riktha|_vei_120@2702|_vei2_pn224
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2701">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2703">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn223">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn224">2:224</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn225">↷</a><hr><p><b>Riktha</b> is found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202702"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202702">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202702"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202702">2</a></sup> denoting ‘inheritance.’<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1202702"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1202702">1b</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202702"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202702">↑</a> iii. 31, 2, on which <i>cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 5; Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Kommentar,</i> 49, 50; Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 239 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202702"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202702">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 18, 9 (of <b>Śunaḥśepa's</b> double inheritance, which, according to that text, is the learning of the <b>Gāthins</b> and the sovereignty of the <b>Jahnus;</b> but see Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 16, who thinks the real succession was to the two houses, the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आंगिरस">āṅgirasa</a></b> and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुशिक">kuśika</a></b>).</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1202702"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1202702">↑</a> iii. 31, 2, on which <i>cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 5; Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Kommentar,</i> 49, 50; Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 239 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

रिपु|రిపు|ರಿಪು|ripu|_vei_120@2703|_vei2_pn224
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2702">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2704">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn223">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn224">2:224</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn225">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ripu</b> is a common word for ‘foe,’ ‘enemy,’ in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202703"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202703">1</a></sup> It occurs in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202703"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202703">2</a></sup> also.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202703"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202703">↑</a> i. 36, 16; 147, 3; 148, 5; ii. 23, 16; 27, 16; 34, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202703"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202703">↑</a> xix. 49, 9.</li></ol></div>

रुक्म|రుక్మ|ರುಕ್ಮ|rukma|_vei_120@2704|_vei2_pn224
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2703">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2705">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn223">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn224">2:224</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn225">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rukma</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202704"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202704">1</a></sup> denotes an ornament, probably of gold, usually worn on the breast. Being in several passages used of the sun, it probably had the form of a disk. In the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202704"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202704">2</a></sup> it designates a gold plate. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रजत">rajata</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202704"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202704">↑</a> i. 166, 10; iv. 10, 5; v. 53, 4; 56, 1, etc. So <i><a class="vei_120a" href="रुक्म">rukma</a>-vakṣas,</i> ‘wearing golden ornaments on the breast,’ ii. 34, 2. 8; v. 55, 1; 57, 5, etc.; <i>rukmin,</i> i. 66, 6; ix. 15, 5. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 2, 3; v. 1, 10, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiii. 40, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202704"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202704">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 5, 1, 20; v. 2, 1, 21; 4, 1, 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 2, 3; 9, 1, etc. So <i>rukmin</i> in Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 4, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 21, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 260, 263; Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 160, who suggests as a possible sense ‘gold coin’; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the; East,</i> 32, 112, 299.</li></ol></div>

रुक्म-पाश|रुक्मपाश|रुक्म|पाश|రుక్మ-పాశ|రుక్మపాశ|రుక్మ|పాశ|ರುಕ್ಮ-ಪಾಶ|ರುಕ್ಮಪಾಶ|ರುಕ್ಮ|ಪಾಶ|rukma-pāśa|rukmapāśa|rukma|pāśa|rukma-paasha|rukmapaasha|paasha|_vei_120@2705|_vei2_pn224
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2704">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2706">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn223">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn224">2:224</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn225">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rukma-pāśa</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202705"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202705">1</a></sup> denotes the ‘cord’ on which ‘the gold plate’ is hung.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202705"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202705">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 7, 1, 7. 27; 3, 8; vii. 2, 1, 15, etc.</li></ol></div>

रुद्र-ब्नुति द्राह्यायण|रुद्रब्नुति द्राह्यायण|रुद्र|ब्नुति द्राह्यायण|రుద్ర-బ్నుతి ద్రాహ్యాయణ|రుద్రబ్నుతి ద్రాహ్యాయణ|రుద్ర|బ్నుతి ద్రాహ్యాయణ|ರುದ್ರ-ಬ್ನುತಿ ದ್ರಾಹ್ಯಾಯಣ|ರುದ್ರಬ್ನುತಿ ದ್ರಾಹ್ಯಾಯಣ|ರುದ್ರ|ಬ್ನುತಿ ದ್ರಾಹ್ಯಾಯಣ|rudra-bnuti drāhyāyaṇa|rudrabnuti drāhyāyaṇa|rudra|bnuti drāhyāyaṇa|rudra-bnuti draahyaayaNa|rudrabnuti draahyaayaNa|bnuti draahyaayaNa|_vei_120@2706|_vei2_pn224
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2705">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2707">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn223">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn224">2:224</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn225">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rudra-bnuti Drāhyāyaṇa</b> is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b>Trāta</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202706"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202706">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202706"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202706">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372.</li></ol></div>

रुम|రుమ|ರುಮ|ruma|_vei_120@2707|_vei2_pn224
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2706">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2708">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn223">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn224">2:224</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn225">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ruma</b> is mentioned in one hymn of the Rigveda (viii. 4, 2) with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रुशम">ruśama</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="श्यावक">śyāvaka</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृप">kṛpa</a></b> as a favourite of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>. </p></div>

रुरु|రురు|ರುರು|ruru|_vei_120@2708|_vei2_pn225
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2707">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2709">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn224">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn225">2:225</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn226">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ruru</b> is one of the victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202708"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202708">1</a></sup> A kind of deer is meant. The Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202708"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202708">2</a></sup> mentions ‘deer-headed’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="रुरु">ruru</a>-śīrṣan</i>) arrows, meaning such as have points made of deer's horn.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202708"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202708">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 19, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 27. 39; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202708"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202708">↑</a> vi. 75, 15.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 83.</li></ol></div>

रुशम|రుశమ|ರುಶಮ|ruśama|rushama|_vei_120@2709|_vei2_pn225
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2708">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2710">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn224">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn225">2:225</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn226">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ruśama</b> is mentioned three times in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202709"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202709">1</a></sup> as a protégé of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>. The Ruśamas occur in another passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202709"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202709">2</a></sup> with their generous king <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋणंचय">ṛṇaṃcaya</a>;</b> they are also referred to, with their king <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौरम">kaurama</a>,</b> in a passage of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202709"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202709">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202709"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202709">↑</a> viii. 3, 13; 4, 2; 51, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202709"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202709">↑</a> v. 30, 12-15.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202709"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202709">↑</a> xx. 127, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 129; Ludwig. Translation of the Rigveda. 3, 154; Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 409; <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 214; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 690.</li></ol></div>

रुशमा|రుశమా|ರುಶಮಾ|ruśamā|rushamaa|_vei_120@2710|_vei2_pn225
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2709">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2711">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn224">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn225">2:225</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn226">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ruśamā</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xxv. 13, 3), where she is said to have run round <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>,</b> and so to have defeated <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, who understood her challenge to refer to the earth proper. The story indicates the connexion of the Ruśamas with the <b>Kurus.</b></p></div>

रुषती|रुषति|రుషతీ|రుషతి|ರುಷತೀ|ರುಷತಿ|ruṣatī|ruṣati|ruShatii|ruShati|ruShatee|_vei_120@2711|_vei2_pn225
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2710">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2712">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn224">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn225">2:225</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn226">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ruṣatī</b> in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202711"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202711">1</a></sup> denotes, according to Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202711"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202711">2</a></sup> a maiden who was married to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्याव">śyāva</a>.</b> On the other hand, Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202711"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202711">3</a></sup> treats the word as <i>ruśatī,</i> ‘white,’ and <i>ruśatīm</i> seems clearly to be the reading of the text. It is doubtful what the meaning is, and whether <a class="vei_120a" href="श्याव">śyāva</a> is a proper name at all.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202711"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202711">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202711"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202711">↑</a> i. 117, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202711"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202711">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 150.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202711"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202711">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. ruśant.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202711"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202711">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 110, who suggests that Kṣoṇa may be a man's name.</li></ol></div>

रेक्णस्|रेक्ण|రేక్ణస్|రేక్ణ|ರೇಕ್ಣಸ್|ರೇಕ್ಣ|rekṇas|rekṇa|rekNas|rekNa|_vei_120@2712|_vei2_pn225
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2711">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2713">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn224">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn225">2:225</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn226">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rekṇas</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202712"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202712">1</a></sup> denotes ‘inherited property,’ and then ‘property’ in general. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202712"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202712">↑</a> i. 31, 14; 121, 5; 158, 1; 162, 2; vi. 20, 7; vii. 4, 7; 40, 2, etc.</li></ol></div>

रेणु|రేణు|ರೇಣು|reṇu|reNu|_vei_120@2713|_vei2_pn226
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2712">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2714">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn225">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn226">2:226</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn227">↷</a><hr><p><b>Reṇu</b> is the name of a son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vii. 17, 7) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (xv. 26, 1).</p></div>

रेभ|रेभ|రేభ_౧|రేభ|ರೇಭ_೧|ರೇಭ|rebha_1|rebha|_vei_120@2714|_vei2_pn226
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2713">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2715">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn225">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn226">2:226</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn227">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Rebha</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202714"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202714">1</a></sup> denotes a ‘singer’ of praise, a ‘panegyrist.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202714"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202714">↑</a> i. 127, 10; vi. 3, 6; 11, 3; vii. 63, 3; viii. 97, 11; ix. 7, 6, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Av. xx. 127, 4.</li></ol></div>

रेभ|रेभ|రేభ_౨|రేభ|ರೇಭ_೨|ರೇಭ|rebha_2|rebha|_vei_120@2715|_vei2_pn226
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2714">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2716">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn225">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn226">2:226</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn227">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Rebha</b> occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202715"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202715">1</a></sup> as the name of a protégé of the Aśvins, who saved him from the waters and from imprisonment.<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1202715"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1202715">1b</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202715"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202715">↑</a> i. 112, 5; 116, 24; 117, 4; 118, 6; 119, 6; x. 39, 9.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1202715"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1202715">↑</a> i. 112, 5; 116, 24; 117, 4; 118, 6; 119, 6; x. 39, 9.</li></ol></div>

रेवा|రేవా|ರೇವಾ|revā|revaa|_vei_120@2716|_vei2_pn226
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2715">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2717">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn225">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn226">2:226</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn227">↷</a><hr><p><b>Revā,</b> a name of the Narmadā (Nerbudda) river, otherwise occurring only in post-Vedic literature, is seen by Weber<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202716">1</a></sup> in the word <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रेवोत्तरस्">revottaras</a>,</b> which is found in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202716">2</a></sup> and is certainly a man's name.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202716">↑</a> <i>Indian Literature,</i> 123 (‘a native of the country south of the Revā’). <i>Cf. Indian Antiquary,</i> 30, 273, n. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202716">↑</a> xii. 8, 1, 17; 9, 3, 1.</li></ol></div>

रेवती|रेवति|రేవతీ|రేవతి|ರೇವತೀ|ರೇವತಿ|revatī|revati|revatii|revatee|_vei_120@2717|_vei2_pn226
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2716">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2718">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn225">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn226">2:226</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn227">↷</a><hr><p><b>Revatī.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p></div>

रेवोत्तरस्|रेवोत्तर|రేవోత్తరస్|రేవోత్తర|ರೇವೋತ್ತರಸ್|ರೇವೋತ್ತರ|revottaras|revottara|_vei_120@2718|_vei2_pn226
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2717">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2719">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn225">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn226">2:226</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn227">↷</a><hr><p><b>Revottaras</b> is the name of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पाटव">pāṭava</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="चाक्र">cākra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="स्थपति">sthapati</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202718">1</a></sup> who was expelled, with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दुष्टरीतु">duṣṭarītu</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पौंसायन">pauṃsāyana</a>,</b> by the <b>Sṛnjayas,</b> and who was in part instrumental in the restoration of his master to power, despite the opposition of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बल्हिक प्रातिपीय">balhika prātipīya</a>,</b> the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a></b> king.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202718">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 9, 3, 1 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> xii. 8, 1, 17.</li></ol></div>

रेष्मन्|రేష్మన్|ರೇಷ್ಮನ್|reṣman|reShman|_vei_120@2719|_vei2_pn226
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2718">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2720">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn225">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn226">2:226</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn227">↷</a><hr><p><b>Reṣman</b> in the Atharvaveda (vi. 102, 2; xv. 2, 1), the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā (iii. 15, 2), and the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā (xxv. 2) denotes a ‘whirlwind.’</p></div>

रैक्व|రైక్వ|ರೈಕ್ವ|raikva|_vei_120@2720|_vei2_pn226
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2719">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2721">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn225">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn226">2:226</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn227">↷</a><hr><p><b>Raikva</b> is the name of a man who is mentioned several times in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iv. 1, 3. 5. 8; 2, 2. 4). </p></div>

रैक्व-पर्ण|रैक्वपर्ण|रैक्व|पर्ण|రైక్వ-పర్ణ|రైక్వపర్ణ|రైక్వ|పర్ణ|ರೈಕ್ವ-ಪರ್ಣ|ರೈಕ್ವಪರ್ಣ|ರೈಕ್ವ|ಪರ್ಣ|raikva-parṇa|raikvaparṇa|raikva|parṇa|raikva-parNa|raikvaparNa|parNa|_vei_120@2721|_vei2_pn227
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2720">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2722">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn226">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn227">2:227</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn228">↷</a><hr><p><b>Raikva-parṇa,</b> masc. plur., is the name of a locality in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="महावृष">mahāvṛṣa</a></b> country according to the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202721"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202721">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202721"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202721">↑</a> iv. 2, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 130.</li></ol></div>

रैभी|रैभि|రైభీ|రైభి|ರೈಭೀ|ರೈಭಿ|raibhī|raibhi|raibhii|raibhee|_vei_120@2722|_vei2_pn227
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2721">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2723">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn226">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn227">2:227</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn228">↷</a><hr><p><b>Raibhī,</b> fem. plur., occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202722">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202722">2</a></sup> together with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नाराशंसी">nārāśaṃsī</a>,</b> as a form of literature. Later on<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202722">3</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="रैभी">raibhī</a> verses are identified with certain verses of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202722">4</a></sup> but that this identification holds<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202722">5</a></sup> in the Rigveda and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā seems very doubtful.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202722">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202722">↑</a> x. 85, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202722">↑</a> vii. 5, 11, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, v. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202722">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 32, 1; Kanṣītaki <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxx. 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202722">↑</a> xx. 127, 4-6 = <a class="vei_120a" href="खिल">khila</a>, v. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202722">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 689.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202722">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 238.</li></ol></div>

रैभ्य|రైభ్య|ರೈಭ್ಯ|raibhya|_vei_120@2723|_vei2_pn227
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2722">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2724">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn226">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn227">2:227</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn228">↷</a><hr><p><b>Raibhya,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रेभ">rebha</a>,</b>’ is the name of a teacher in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) in the Mādhyaṃdina recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (ii. 5, 20; iv. 5, 26), where he is said to be a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पौतिमाष्यायण">pautimāṣyāyaṇa</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौण्डिन्यायन">kauṇḍinyāyana</a>.</b></p></div>

रोग|రోగ|ರೋಗ|roga|_vei_120@2724|_vei2_pn227
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2723">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2725">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn226">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn227">2:227</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn228">↷</a><hr><p><b>Roga</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202724"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202724">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202724"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202724">2</a></sup> denotes ‘disease’ generally.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202724"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202724">↑</a> i. 2, 4; ii. 3, 3; iii. 28, 5; vi. 44, 1; 120, 3; of the head (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शीर्षण्य">śīrṣaṇya</a></i>), ix. 8. 1. 21 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202724"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202724">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vii. 26, 2.</li></ol></div>

रोपणाका|రోపణాకా|ರೋಪಣಾಕಾ|ropaṇākā|ropaNaakaa|_vei_120@2725|_vei2_pn227
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2724">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2726">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn226">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn227">2:227</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn228">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ropaṇākā</b> is the name of a bird mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202725">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202725">2</a></sup> The ‘thrush’ seems to be meant;<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202725">3</a></sup> but Keśava, the commentator on the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202725">4</a></sup> is inclined to understand the word to mean a sort of wood. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202725">↑</a> i. 50, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202725">↑</a> i. 22, 4. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 7, 6, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202725">↑</a> <i>Śārikā,</i> Sāyaṇa on Rv., <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i> On Av. i. 22, 4, he explains it as <i>kāṣṭhaśuka,</i> perhaps a kind of parrot.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202725">↑</a> xxvi. 20.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 92; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 266; Caland, <i>Altindisches Zauberritual,</i> 76, n. 13; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 23.</li></ol></div>

रोमशा|రోమశా|ರೋಮಶಾ|romaśā|romashaa|_vei_120@2726|_vei2_pn228
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2725">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2727">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn227">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn228">2:228</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn229">↷</a><hr><p><b>Romaśā</b> is mentioned in the Bṛhaddevatā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202726"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202726">1</a></sup> as the wife of king <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भावयव्य">bhāvayavya</a>,</b> and is credited with the authorship of a Rigvedic verse.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202726"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202726">2</a></sup> But in reality the word <i><a class="vei_120a" href="रोमशा">romaśā</a></i> in that verse, which is the source of the legend, is merely an adjective meaning ‘hairy.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202726"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202726">↑</a> iii. 156 <i>et seq.,</i> with Macdonell's notes.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202726"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202726">↑</a> i. 126, 7.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 128.</li></ol></div>

रोहिणी|रोहिणी|रोहिणि|రోహిణీ_౧|రోహిణీ|రోహిణి|ರೋಹಿಣೀ_೧|ರೋಹಿಣೀ|ರೋಹಿಣಿ|rohiṇī_1|rohiṇī|rohiṇi|rohiNii_1|rohiNii|rohiNi|rohiNee_1|rohiNee|_vei_120@2727|_vei2_pn228
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2726">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2728">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn227">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn228">2:228</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn229">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Rohiṇī</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202727"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202727">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202727"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202727">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘red cow.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202727"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202727">↑</a> viii. 93, 13; 101, 13 (reading <i>rohiṇyāḥ</i> with Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202727"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202727">↑</a> Av. xiii. 1, 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 6, 2; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 2, 6; iv. 5, 8, 2, etc.</li></ol></div>

रोहिणी|रोहिणी|रोहिणि|రోహిణీ_౨|రోహిణీ|రోహిణి|ರೋಹಿಣೀ_೨|ರೋಹಿಣೀ|ರೋಹಿಣಿ|rohiṇī_2|rohiṇī|rohiṇi|rohiNii_2|rohiNii|rohiNi|rohiNee_2|rohiNee|_vei_120@2728|_vei2_pn228
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2727">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2729">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn227">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn228">2:228</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn229">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Rohiṇī.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p></div>

रोहित्|రోహిత్|ರೋಹಿತ್|rohit|_vei_120@2729|_vei2_pn228
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2728">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2730">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn227">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn228">2:228</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn229">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rohit</b> in some passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202729"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202729">1</a></sup> denotes, according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, a ‘red mare,’ while later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202729"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202729">2</a></sup> it denotes a ‘red doe.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202729"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202729">↑</a> i. 14, 12; 100, 16; v. 56, 5; vii. 42, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202729"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202729">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 6, 5; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 11, 18; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 30. 37; Av. iv. 4, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 33, 1 (<i>cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 178, n.).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 82.</li></ol></div>

रोहित|रोहित|రోహిత_౧|రోహిత|ರೋಹಿತ_೧|ರೋಹಿತ|rohita_1|rohita|_vei_120@2730|_vei2_pn228
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2729">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2731">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn227">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn228">2:228</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn229">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Rohita</b> denotes a ‘red horse’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202730">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202730">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202730">↑</a> i. 94, 10; 134, 9; ii. 10, 2; iii. 6, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202730">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 4, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 3, 12, etc. So <a class="vei_120a" href="रोहित">rohita</a> in Av. xiii. 1, 1 <i>et seq.,</i> represents the sun as a ‘red horse.’</li></ol></div>

रोहित|रोहित|రోహిత_౨|రోహిత|ರೋಹಿತ_೨|ರೋಹಿತ|rohita_2|rohita|_vei_120@2731|_vei2_pn228
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2730">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2732">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn227">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn228">2:228</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn229">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Rohita</b> is a son of <b>Hariścandra</b> in the famous tale of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vii. 14) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (xv. 18, 8).</p></div>

रोहितक|రోహితక|ರೋಹಿತಕ|rohitaka|_vei_120@2732|_vei2_pn228
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2731">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2733">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn227">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn228">2:228</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn229">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rohitaka</b> occurs in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā (iii. 9, 3) with a variant <a class="vei_120a" href="रोहीतक">rohītaka</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202732"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202732">1</a></sup> as the name of the tree <i>Andersonia <a class="vei_120a" href="रोहितक">rohitaka</a>.</i></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202732"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202732">↑</a> So Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 5, 8.</li></ol></div>

रोहितक-कूल|रोहितककूल|रोहितक|कूल|రోహితక-కూల|రోహితకకూల|రోహితక|కూల|ರೋಹಿತಕ-ಕೂಲ|ರೋಹಿತಕಕೂಲ|ರೋಹಿತಕ|ಕೂಲ|rohitaka-kūla|rohitakakūla|rohitaka|kūla|rohitaka-kuula|rohitakakuula|kuula|rohitaka-koola|rohitakakoola|koola|_vei_120@2733|_vei2_pn228
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2732">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2734">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn227">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn228">2:228</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn229">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rohitaka-kūla</b> is in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202733"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202733">1</a></sup> the name of a locality after which a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> or chant was called. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202733"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202733">↑</a> xiv. 3, 12. <i>Cf.</i> xv. 11, 6; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, vi. 11, 4.</li></ol></div>

रोहीतक|రోహీతక|ರೋಹೀತಕ|rohītaka|rohiitaka|roheetaka|_vei_120@2734|_vei2_pn229
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2733">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2735">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn228">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn229">2:229</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn230">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rohītaka.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रोहितक">rohitaka</a>.</b></p></div>

रौहिण|रौहिण|రౌహిణ_౧|రౌహిణ|ರೌಹಿಣ_೧|ರೌಹಿಣ|rauhiṇa_1|rauhiṇa|rauhiNa_1|rauhiNa|_vei_120@2735|_vei2_pn229
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2734">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2736">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn228">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn229">2:229</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn230">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Rauhiṇa</b> is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202735">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202735">2</a></sup> as a demon foe of Indra's. Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202735">3</a></sup> is inclined to see in the word the name of a planet (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रोहिणी">rohiṇī</a></b>), but without any clear reason.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202735">↑</a> i. 103, 2; ii. 12, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202735">↑</a> xx. 128, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202735">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 207.</li></ol></div>

रौहिण|रौहिण|రౌహిణ_౨|రౌహిణ|ರೌಹಿಣ_೨|ರೌಹಿಣ|rauhiṇa_2|rauhiṇa|rauhiNa_2|rauhiNa|_vei_120@2736|_vei2_pn229
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2735">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2737">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn228">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn229">2:229</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn230">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Rauhiṇa</b> (‘born under the <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> Rohiṇī’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वासिष्ठ">vāsiṣṭha</a></b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a></b>’), is the name of a man in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka (i. 12, 5).</p></div>

रौहिणायन|రౌహిణాయన|ರೌಹಿಣಾಯನ|rauhiṇāyana|rauhiNaayana|_vei_120@2737|_vei2_pn229
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2736">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2738">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn228">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn229">2:229</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn230">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rauhiṇāyana</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रौहिण">rauhiṇa</a></b>’) is the patronymic of <b>Priyavrata</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (x. 3, 5, 14). It is also in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) in the Mādhyaṃdina recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (ii. 5, 20; iv. 5, 26) the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शौनक">śaunaka</a></b> and others.</p></div>

लक्ष|లక్ష|ಲಕ್ಷ|lakṣa|lakSha|laxa|_vei_120@2738|_vei2_pn229
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2737">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2739">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn228">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn229">2:229</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn230">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lakṣa</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202738"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202738">1</a></sup> denotes the ‘prize’ at dicing.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202738"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202738">↑</a> ii. 12, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Lüders, <i>Das Würfelspiel im alten Indien,</i> 4, n. 1; Zimmer. <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 287.</li></ol></div>

लक्षण|లక్షణ|ಲಕ್ಷಣ|lakṣaṇa|lakShaNa|laxaNa|_vei_120@2739|_vei2_pn229
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2738">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2740">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn228">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn229">2:229</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn230">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lakṣaṇa</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202739"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202739">1</a></sup> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="लक्ष्मन्">lakṣman</a></b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202739"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202739">2</a></sup> denotes the ‘mark’ made on cattle by branding to distinguish ownership. According to the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202739"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202739">3</a></sup> it was to be made under the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="रेवती">revatī</a>,</b> clearly because of the property indicated in the name (‘wealthy’) of that <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अष्टकर्णी">aṣṭakarṇī</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202739"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202739">↑</a> Gobhila <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 6, 5. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 10; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 35; 13, 466.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202739"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202739">↑</a> Av. vi. 141, 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202739"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202739">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

लक्ष्मण्य|లక్ష్మణ్య|ಲಕ್ಷ್ಮಣ್ಯ|lakṣmaṇya|lakShmaNya|laxmaNya|_vei_120@2740|_vei2_pn230
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2739">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2741">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn229">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn230">2:230</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn231">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lakṣmaṇya</b> in one verse of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202740"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202740">1</a></sup> seems to be a patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ध्वन्य">dhvanya</a>,</b> ‘son of Lakṣmaṇa.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202740"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202740">↑</a> v. 33, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 155.</li></ol></div>

लक्ष्मन्|లక్ష్మన్|ಲಕ್ಷ್ಮನ್|lakṣman|lakShman|laxman|_vei_120@2741|_vei2_pn230
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2740">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2742">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn229">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn230">2:230</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn231">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lakṣman.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="लक्षण">lakṣaṇa</a>.</b></p></div>

लब|లబ|ಲಬ|laba|_vei_120@2742|_vei2_pn230
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2741">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2743">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn229">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn230">2:230</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn231">↷</a><hr><p><b>Laba,</b> ‘quail’ (<i>Perdix chinensis</i>) is one of the victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202742"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202742">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202742"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202742">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 24. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vii. 2, where Rv. x. 119 is called the Labasūkta; the Anukramaṇī (Index), too, gives Aindra <a class="vei_120a" href="लब">laba</a> as the author of that hymn. <i>Cf.</i> Bṛhaddevatā, viii. 40, with Macdonell's note. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 90.</li></ol></div>

लम्बन|लंबन|లమ్బన|లంబన|ಲಮ್ಬನ|ಲಂಬನ|lambana|_vei_120@2743|_vei2_pn230
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2742">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2744">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn229">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn230">2:230</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn231">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lambana</b> is the reading in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension (v. 10, 1) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> for <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आडम्बर">āḍambara</a>,</b> ‘drum,’ in the Mādhyaṃdina recension (v. 12, 1).</p></div>

लवण|లవణ|ಲವಣ|lavaṇa|lavaNa|_vei_120@2744|_vei2_pn230
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2743">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2745">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn229">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn230">2:230</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn231">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lavaṇa,</b> ‘salt,’ is never mentioned in the Rigveda, only once in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202744"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202744">1</a></sup> and not after that until the latest part of the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202744"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202744">2</a></sup> where it is regarded as of extremely high value.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202744"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202744">3</a></sup> This silence in the early period is somewhat surprising if the regions then occupied by the Indians were the Panjab and the Indus valley, where salt abounds; it would at first sight seem less curious if the home of the early Vedic Indian is taken to be <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202744"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202744">4</a></sup> It is, however, quite conceivable that a necessary commodity might happen to be passed over without literary mention in a region where it is very common, but to be referred to in a locality where it is not found, and consequently becomes highly prized. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202744"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202744">↑</a> vii. 76, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202744"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202744">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 17, 7 = Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 17, 3. <i>Cf.</i> also Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 13, 1; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 4, 12; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 2, 1, 16; and see Strabo, xv. 1, 30.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202744"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202744">↑</a> It seems to be placed above gold in value in Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 17, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202744"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202744">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Map 19 in the Atlas of the <i>Imperial Gazetteer of India,</i> vol. 26, and see Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 19, 21 <i>et seq.; India, Old and New,</i> 30 <i>et seq.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 54, 55; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 318; Geiger, <i>Ostiranische Kultur,</i> 419; Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 150.</li></ol></div>

लवन|లవన|ಲವನ|lavana|_vei_120@2745|_vei2_pn231
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2744">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2746">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn230">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn231">2:231</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn232">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lavana</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> (ii. 2) denotes the ‘mowing’ or ‘reaping’ of corn.</p></div>

लाक्षा|లాక్షా|ಲಾಕ್ಷಾ|lākṣā|laakShaa|laaxaa|_vei_120@2746|_vei2_pn231
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2745">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2747">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn230">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn231">2:231</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn232">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lākṣā</b> occurs once in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202746"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202746">1</a></sup> as the name of a plant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202746"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202746">↑</a> v. 5, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 229; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 387, 421.</li></ol></div>

लांगल|लाङ्गल|లాంగల|లాఙ్గల|ಲಾಂಗಲ|ಲಾಙ್ಗಲ|lāṅgala|laaMgala|laangala|_vei_120@2747|_vei2_pn231
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2746">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2748">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn230">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn231">2:231</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn232">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lāṅgala</b> is the regular word for ‘plough’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202747">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202747">2</a></sup> It is described in a series of passages<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202747">3</a></sup> as ‘lancepointed’ (<i>pavīravat</i> or <i>pavīravam</i>), ‘well-lying’ (<i>susīmam</i>),<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202747">4</a></sup> and ‘having a well-smoothed handle’ (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्सरु">tsaru</a></b>). See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सीर">sīra</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202747">↑</a> iv. 57, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202747">↑</a> Av. ii. 8, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 6, 7, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 26, etc.; <i>lāṅgaleṣā,</i> Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxii. 4, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202747">↑</a> Av. iii. 17, 3 = <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 5, 6 = <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvi. 11 = Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 7, 12 = Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xii. 71 = <a class="vei_120a" href="वासिष्ठ">vāsiṣṭha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 34. 35.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202747">↑</a> The texts have <i>suśevam;</i> Roth conjectures <i>susīmam.</i> See Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 116.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 236.</li></ol></div>

लांगलायन|लाङ्गलायन|లాంగలాయన|లాఙ్గలాయన|ಲಾಂಗಲಾಯನ|ಲಾಙ್ಗಲಾಯನ|lāṅgalāyana|laaMgalaayana|laangalaayana|_vei_120@2748|_vei2_pn231
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2747">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2749">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn230">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn231">2:231</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn232">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lāṅgalāyana,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="लांगल">lāṅgala</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मौद्गल्य">maudgalya</a></b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मुद्गल">mudgala</a></b>’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (v. 3, 8).</p></div>

लाज|లాజ|ಲಾಜ|lāja|laaja|_vei_120@2749|_vei2_pn231
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2748">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2750">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn230">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn231">2:231</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn232">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lāja,</b> masc. plur., in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202749"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202749">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202749"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202749">2</a></sup> denotes ‘fried or parched grain.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202749"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202749">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 11, 2, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 13. 81; xxi. 42, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202749"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202749">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 8, 2, 7. 10; 9, 1, 2; xiii. 2, 1, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 269.</li></ol></div>

लाजि|లాజి|ಲಾಜಿ|lāji|laaji|_vei_120@2750|_vei2_pn231
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2749">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2751">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn230">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn231">2:231</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn232">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lāji</b> in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā (xxiii. 8) and the Taittīrīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 9, 4, 8) is a word of uncertain meaning; according to Sāyaṇa, it is a vocative of Lājin, ‘having parched grain’; according to Mahīdhara, it denotes a ‘quantity of parched grain.’ </p></div>

लातव्य|లాతవ్య|ಲಾತವ್ಯ|lātavya|laatavya|_vei_120@2751|_vei2_pn232
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2750">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2752">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn231">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn232">2:232</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn233">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lātavya,</b> ‘descendant of Latu,’ is the patronymic of <b>Kūśāmba <a class="vei_120a" href="स्वायव">svāyava</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202751"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202751">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202751"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202751">↑</a> viii. 6, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 7; Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 25 (a <a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a> is there mentioned).</li></ol></div>

लामकायन|లామకాయన|ಲಾಮಕಾಯನ|lāmakāyana|laamakaayana|_vei_120@2752|_vei2_pn232
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2751">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2753">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn231">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn232">2:232</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn233">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lāmakāyana,</b> ‘descendant of Lamaka,’ is often mentioned as an authority in the Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202752">1</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202752">2</a></sup> and the Drāhyāyaṇa <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>;<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202752">3</a></sup> also with the name <b>Saṃvargajit</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202752">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202752">↑</a> iv. 9, 22; vi. 9, 18, etc.; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 49.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202752">↑</a> iii. 12. 13; vii. 4, 8, etc.; Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 45.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202752">↑</a> Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 4, 384.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202752">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373.</li></ol></div>

लाह्यायन|లాహ్యాయన|ಲಾಹ್ಯಾಯನ|lāhyāyana|laahyaayana|_vei_120@2753|_vei2_pn232
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2752">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2754">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn231">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn232">2:232</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn233">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lāhyāyana,</b> ‘descendant of Lahya,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भुज्यु">bhujyu</a></b> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iv. 5, 1. 2).</p></div>

लिबुजा|లిబుజా|ಲಿಬುಜಾ|libujā|libujaa|_vei_120@2754|_vei2_pn232
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2753">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2755">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn231">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn232">2:232</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn233">↷</a><hr><p><b>Libujā</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202754">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202754">2</a></sup> denotes a creeping plant that climbs trees.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202754">↑</a> x. 10, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202754">↑</a> vi. 8, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 13, 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 28; xi. 34.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 70.</li></ol></div>

लुश|లుశ|ಲುಶ|luśa|lusha|_vei_120@2755|_vei2_pn232
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2754">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2756">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn231">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn232">2:232</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn233">↷</a><hr><p><b>Luśa</b> is represented in a series of passages in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202755"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202755">1</a></sup> as a rival of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुत्स">kutsa</a></b> for the favour of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>. To <a class="vei_120a" href="लुश">luśa</a> Dhānāka the authorship of certain hymns<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202755"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202755">2</a></sup> is ascribed by the Anukramaṇī (Index) of the Rigveda.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202755"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202755">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 2, 22; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 128; Śāṭyāyanaka in Oertel, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 31 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202755"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202755">↑</a> x. 35. 36. <i>Cf.</i> Bṛhaddevatā, ii. 129; iii. 55, with Macdonell's notes.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 291, n. 3; Lévi, <i>La Doctrine du Sacrifice,</i> 37, 38.</li></ol></div>

लुशाकपि खार्गलि|లుశాకపి ఖార్గలి|ಲುಶಾಕಪಿ ಖಾರ್ಗಲಿ|luśākapi khārgali|lushaakapi khaargali|_vei_120@2756|_vei2_pn232
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2755">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2757">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn231">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn232">2:232</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn233">↷</a><hr><p><b>Luśākapi Khārgali</b> (‘descendant of Khṛgala’) is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202756"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202756">1</a></sup> as having cursed <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुषीतक">kuṣītaka</a></b> and the <b>Kauṣītakins.</b> He was a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="केशिन्">keśin</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दाल्भ्य">dālbhya</a></b> according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202756"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202756">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202756"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202756">↑</a> xvii. 4, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 145, n. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202756"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202756">↑</a> xxx. 2 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 471); Kapiṣṭhala Saṃhitā, xlvi. 5.</li></ol></div>

लोक|లోక|ಲೋಕ|loka|_vei_120@2757|_vei2_pn233
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2756">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2758">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn232">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn233">2:233</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn234">↷</a><hr><p><b>Loka</b> denotes ‘world’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202757">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202757">2</a></sup> Mention is often made of the three worlds,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202757">3</a></sup> and <i>ayaṃ lokaḥ,</i> ‘this world,’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202757">4</a></sup> is constantly opposed to <i>asau lokaḥ,</i><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202757">5</a></sup> ‘yonder world’ -<i>i.e.,</i> ‘heaven.’ <a class="vei_120a" href="लोक">loka</a> itself sometimes means ‘heaven,’<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202757">6</a></sup> while in other passages several different sorts of world are mentioned.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202757">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202757">↑</a> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 2, quotes no example of this meaning for the Rigveda, where he sees the word used only in the sense of ‘place,’ ‘room,’ ‘free or open space.’ But Rv. x. 14, 9, is a fairly certain example of the wider sense.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202757">↑</a> Av. viii. 9, 1. 15; iv. 38, 5; xi. 5, 7; 8, 10, etc.; in ix. 5, 14, the worlds of heaven (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दिव्य">divya</a></i>) and of earth (<i>pārthiva</i>) are distinguished; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxii. 11 <i>et seq.,</i> etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202757">↑</a> Av. x. 6, 31; xii. 3, 20; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 8; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 1, 7, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202757">↑</a> Av. v. 30, 17; viii. 8, 8; xii. 5, 38; xix. 54, 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 46, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202757">↑</a> Av. xii. 5, 38. 57; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 5, 9, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 28, 2; viii. 2, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202757">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 1, 7; x. 5, 4, 16; xi. 2, 7, 19; and so probably <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 13, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202757"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202757">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxvi. 4; Kauṣī taki <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xx. 1; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 6, 1; iv. 3, 36 <i>et seq.;</i> vi. 1, 18, etc.</li></ol></div>

लोध|లోధ|ಲೋಧ|lodha|_vei_120@2758|_vei2_pn233
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2757">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2759">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn232">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn233">2:233</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn234">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lodha</b> occurs in a very obscure verse of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202758"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202758">1</a></sup> where Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202758"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202758">2</a></sup> conjectures that some sort of ‘red’ animal is meant, and Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202758"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202758">3</a></sup> shows some reason for thinking that a ‘red goat’ is intended.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202758"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202758">↑</a> iii. 53, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202758"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202758">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202758"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202758">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 255.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> the obscure <i>adhī-<a class="vei_120a" href="लोध">lodha</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कर्ण">karṇa</a></i> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 6, 16, 1, perhaps meaning ‘having quite red ears.’ <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iv. 12, equates the word with <i>lubdha,</i> ‘confused,’ but this does not suit the context. So also Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 84; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 160; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>, Glossar,</i> 151, who sees in the word the designation of a noble steed.</li></ol></div>

लोपा|లోపా|ಲೋಪಾ|lopā|lopaa|_vei_120@2759|_vei2_pn233
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2758">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2760">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn232">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn233">2:233</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn234">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lopā</b> is mentioned in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202759"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202759">1</a></sup> where Sāyaṇa explains it as a kind of bird, perhaps the carrion crow (<i>śmaśānaśakuni</i>). </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202759"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202759">↑</a> v. 5, 18, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 93.</li></ol></div>

लोपा-मुद्रा|लोपामुद्रा|लोपा|मुद्रा|లోపా-ముద్రా|లోపాముద్రా|లోపా|ముద్రా|ಲೋಪಾ-ಮುದ್ರಾ|ಲೋಪಾಮುದ್ರಾ|ಲೋಪಾ|ಮುದ್ರಾ|lopā-mudrā|lopāmudrā|lopā|mudrā|lopaa-mudraa|lopaamudraa|lopaa|mudraa|_vei_120@2760|_vei2_pn234
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2759">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2761">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn233">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn234">2:234</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn235">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lopā-mudrā</b> appears in one hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202760">1</a></sup> where she is seemingly the wife of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अगस्त्य">agastya</a>,</b> whose embraces she solicits.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202760">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202760">↑</a> i. 179, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202760">↑</a> The story is differently told in the Bṛhaddevatā, iv. 57 <i>et seq.,</i> with Macdonell's notes. See also Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenldndischen Gesellschaft,</i> 39, 68; <i>Go7ttingische Gelehrte Anzeigen,</i> 1909, 76 <i>et seq.;</i> Sieg. <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 120 <i>et seq.;</i> Winternitz, <i>Vienna Oriental Journal,</i> 20, 2 <i>et seq.;</i> von Schroeder, <i>Mysterium und Mimus,</i> 156 <i>et seq.;</i> Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1909, 204; 1911, 997, n. 3.</li></ol></div>

लोपाश|లోపాశ|ಲೋಪಾಶ|lopāśa|lopaasha|_vei_120@2761|_vei2_pn234
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2760">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2762">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn233">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn234">2:234</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn235">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lopāśa</b> is the name of an animal, probably the ‘jackal’ or ‘fox,’ which is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202761"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202761">1</a></sup> and is included in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202761"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202761">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202761"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202761">↑</a> x. 28, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202761"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202761">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 21, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 17; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 36.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 84.</li></ol></div>

लोह|లోహ|ಲೋಹ|loha|_vei_120@2762|_vei2_pn234
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2761">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2763">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn233">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn234">2:234</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn235">↷</a><hr><p><b>Loha,</b> primarily an adjective meaning ‘red,’ is used as a neuter substantive to designate a metal, probably ‘copper,’ but possibly ‘bronze.’ It is mentioned in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202762"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202762">1</a></sup> and the Taittīriya Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202762"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202762">2</a></sup> as distinguished from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्याम">śyāma</a>.</b> It also occurs several times in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202762"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202762">3</a></sup> See <a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्">ayas</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202762"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202762">↑</a> xviii. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202762"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202762">↑</a> iv. 7, 5, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202762"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202762">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 2, 2, 18; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 17, 7; vi. 1, 5; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 4, where Oertel takes ‘copper’ to be meant in contrast with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्">ayas</a>,</b> which he renders ‘brass.’ The sense of ‘iron’ is nowhere needed.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Vincent Smith, <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 34, 230; and on the early history of metals; Mosso, <i>Mediterranean Civilization,</i> 57-62.</li></ol></div>

लोह-मणि|लोहमणि|लोह|मणि|లోహ-మణి|లోహమణి|లోహ|మణి|ಲೋಹ-ಮಣಿ|ಲೋಹಮಣಿ|ಲೋಹ|ಮಣಿ|loha-maṇi|lohamaṇi|loha|maṇi|loha-maNi|lohamaNi|maNi|_vei_120@2763|_vei2_pn234
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2762">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2764">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn233">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn234">2:234</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn235">↷</a><hr><p><b>Loha-maṇi</b> in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 2, 5) denotes a ‘copper amulet,’ as Bo7htlingk<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202763">1</a></sup> renders it, rather than a ‘lump of gold,’ as translated by Max Müller following the scholiast.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202763">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Little, <i>Grammatical Index,</i> 134.</li></ol></div>

लोहायस|లోహాయస|ಲೋಹಾಯಸ|lohāyasa|lohaayasa|_vei_120@2764|_vei2_pn234
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2763">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2765">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn233">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn234">2:234</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn235">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lohāyasa,</b> ‘red metal,’ is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202764"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202764">1</a></sup> where it is distinguished from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्">ayas</a></b> and gold. In the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202764"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202764">2</a></sup> the contrast is with Kārṣṇā yasa, ‘iron,’ and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202764"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202764">3</a></sup> with <a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्णायस">kṛṣṇāyasa</a>, ‘iron.’ ‘Copper’ seems to be meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202764"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202764">↑</a> v. 4, 1, 1. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202764"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202764">↑</a> iii. 17, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202764"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202764">↑</a> iii. 62, 6, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 90, n.; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 189.</li></ol></div>

लोहित|లోహిత|ಲೋಹಿತ|lohita|_vei_120@2765|_vei2_pn235
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2764">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2766">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn234">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn235">2:235</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn236">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lohita,</b> often occurring as an adjective meaning ‘red,’ is used as a neuter substantive in the Atharvaveda (xi. 3, 7) to denote a metal, presumably ‘copper.’ As a proper name it is found in Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxiv. 9, 7.</p></div>

लोहितायस|లోహితాయస|ಲೋಹಿತಾಯಸ|lohitāyasa|lohitaayasa|_vei_120@2766|_vei2_pn235
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2765">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2767">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn234">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn235">2:235</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn236">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lohitāyasa,</b> ‘red metal,’ ‘copper,’ is the variant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="लोह">loha</a></b> in the Maitrāyaṇī (ii. 11, 5; iv. 4, 4) and <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> (xviii. 10) Saṃhitās.</p></div>

लोहिताहि|లోహితాహి|ಲೋಹಿತಾಹಿ|lohitāhi|lohitaahi|_vei_120@2767|_vei2_pn235
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2766">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2768">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn234">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn235">2:235</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn236">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lohitāhi,</b> ‘red snake,’ is the name of a variety of serpent mentioned in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202767"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202767">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202767"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202767">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 14, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 12; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 31. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 95.</li></ol></div>

लौहित्य|లౌహిత్య|ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|lauhitya|_vei_120@2768|_vei2_pn235
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2767">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2769">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn234">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn235">2:235</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn236">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lauhitya,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="लोहित">lohita</a>,’ is the patronymic of a large number of teachers in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, which clearly must have been the special object of study of the <a class="vei_120a" href="लौहित्य">lauhitya</a> family. See <b>Kṛṣṇadatta, Kṛṣṇarāta, Jayaka, Triveda Kṛṣṇarāta, Dakṣa <a class="vei_120a" href="जयन्त">jayanta</a>, Palligupta, Mitrabhūti, Yaśasvin <a class="vei_120a" href="जयन्त">jayanta</a>, Vipaścit <a class="vei_120a" href="दृढजयन्त">dṛḍhajayanta</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वैपश्चित">vaipaścita</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दार्ढजयन्ति">dārḍhajayanti</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वैपश्चित">vaipaścita</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दार्ढजयन्ति">dārḍhajayanti</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दृढजयन्त">dṛḍhajayanta</a>, Śyāmajayanta, Śyāmasujayanta, <a class="vei_120a" href="सत्यश्रवस्">satyaśravas</a>.</b> A <a class="vei_120a" href="लौहित्य">lauhitya</a> or Lauhikya is also mentioned as a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202768"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202768">1</a></sup> The form of name (<a class="vei_120a" href="जयन्त">jayanta</a>) affected by the family, and the silence of the older texts, proves that they were modern. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202768"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202768">↑</a> vii. 22; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 50, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

वंश|वंश|వంశ_౧|వంశ|ವಂಶ_೧|ವಂಶ|vaṃśa_1|vaṃśa|vaMsha_1|vaMsha|_vei_120@2769|_vei2_pn236
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2768">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2770">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn235">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn236">2:236</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn237">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Vaṃśa,</b> denoting the ‘rafters’ or ‘beams’ of the house as made of bamboo cane, is found in this sense from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202769"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202769">1</a></sup> onwards.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202769"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202769">2</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तिरश्चीनवंश">tiraścīnavaṃśa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राचीनवंश">prācīnavaṃśa</a>,</b> and see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गृह">gṛha</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202769"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202769">↑</a> i. 10, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202769"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202769">↑</a> Av. iii. 12, 6; ix. 3, 4; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 8, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 3, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 1, 2, 25; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शाला">śālā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a>,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 2, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, viii. 1, where perhaps the main beam of the house is meant. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 71, 153; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 346.</li></ol></div>

वंश|वंश|వంశ_౨|వంశ|ವಂಶ_೨|ವಂಶ|vaṃśa_2|vaṃśa|vaMsha_2|vaMsha|_vei_120@2770|_vei2_pn236
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2769">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2771">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn235">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn236">2:236</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn237">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Vaṃśa</b> (lit. ‘bamboo’) in the sense of ‘spiritual genealogy,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202770"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202770">1</a></sup> ‘list of teachers,’ is found in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202770"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202770">2</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202770"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202770">3</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202770"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202770">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202770"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202770">↑</a> From the analogy of the successive joints of the bamboo. <i>Cf.</i> ‘familytree.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202770"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202770">↑</a> x. 6, 5, 9; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 3, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202770"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202770">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 374.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202770"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202770">↑</a> xv. 1.</li></ol></div>

वंश-नर्तिन्|वंशनर्तिन्|वंश|नर्तिन्|వంశ-నర్తిన్|వంశనర్తిన్|వంశ|నర్తిన్|ವಂಶ-ನರ್ತಿನ್|ವಂಶನರ್ತಿನ್|ವಂಶ|ನರ್ತಿನ್|vaṃśa-nartin|vaṃśanartin|vaṃśa|nartin|vaMsha-nartin|vaMshanartin|vaMsha|_vei_120@2771|_vei2_pn236
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2770">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2772">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn235">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn236">2:236</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn237">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaṃśa-nartin</b> is mentioned as one of the victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202771">1</a></sup> A ‘poledancer’ or ‘acrobat’ seems to be meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202771"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202771">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 21; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 17, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 290.</li></ol></div>

वंसग|వంసగ|ವಂಸಗ|vaṃsaga|vaMsaga|_vei_120@2772|_vei2_pn236
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2771">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2773">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn235">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn236">2:236</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn237">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaṃsaga</b> is in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202772"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202772">1</a></sup> a common name of the ‘bull’ that leads the herds.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202772"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202772">↑</a> i. 7, 8; 55, 1; 58, 4; v. 36, 1, etc.; Av. xviii. 3, 36.</li></ol></div>

वक दाल्भ्य|వక దాల్భ్య|ವಕ ದಾಲ್ಭ್ಯ|vaka dālbhya|vaka daalbhya|_vei_120@2773|_vei2_pn236
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2772">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2774">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn235">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn236">2:236</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn237">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaka Dālbhya</b> (‘descendant of Dalbha’) is the name of a teacher in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202773"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202773">1</a></sup> According to the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202773"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202773">2</a></sup> he was engaged in a ritual dispute with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धृतराष्ट्र">dhṛtarāṣṭra</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202773"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202773">↑</a> i. 2, 13; 12, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202773"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202773">↑</a> xxx. 2 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 471).</li></ol></div>

वकल|వకల|ವಕಲ|vakala|_vei_120@2774|_vei2_pn236
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2773">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2775">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn235">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn236">2:236</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn237">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vakala</b> denotes in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202774"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202774">1</a></sup> the ‘inner bark’ of a tree, ‘bast.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202774"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202774">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 7, 4, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 2.</li></ol></div>

वक्षणा|వక్షణా|ವಕ್ಷಣಾ|vakṣaṇā|vakShaNaa|vaxaNaa|_vei_120@2775|_vei2_pn237
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2774">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2776">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn236">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn237">2:237</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn238">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vakṣaṇā,</b> fem. plur. denotes in one passage of the <b>Rigveda</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202775"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202775">1</a></sup> the bed of a stream.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202775"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202775">↑</a> iii. 33, 12. <i>Cf.</i> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 175-181.</li></ol></div>

वघा|వఘా|ವಘಾ|vaghā|vaghaa|_vei_120@2776|_vei2_pn237
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2775">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2777">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn236">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn237">2:237</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn238">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaghā</b> is the name of a noxious animal the <b>Atharvaveda.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202776">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202776">↑</a> vi. 50, 3; ix. 2, 22. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 98.</li></ol></div>

वंग|वङ्ग|వంగ|వఙ్గ|ವಂಗ|ವಙ್ಗ|vaṅga|vaMga|vanga|_vei_120@2777|_vei2_pn237
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2776">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2778">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn236">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn237">2:237</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn238">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaṅga,</b> the designation of Bengal proper is not found in the earlier Vedic literature unless it is to be recognized in the curious word <i>Vaṅgāvagadhāḥ,</i> which occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202777"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202777">1</a></sup> and which suggests amendment to <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वंग">vaṅga</a>-Magadhāḥ,</i> ‘the Vaṅgas and the Magadhas,’ two neighbouring peoples. The name is certainly found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202777"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202777">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202777"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202777">↑</a> ii. 1, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 200; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मगध">magadha</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वंग">vaṅga</a>-Matsyāḥ</i> occurs in the Atharvaveda Pariśiṣṭas (i. 7, 7). but that is very late.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202777"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202777">↑</a> i. 1, 14. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 394, n.; Calan <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 56, 553.</li></ol></div>

वंगृद|वङ्गृद|వంగృద|వఙ్గృద|ವಂಗೃದ|ವಙ್ಗೃದ|vaṅgṛda|vaMgRda|vangRda|vaMgRida|vaMgRuda|vaMgRRida|vangRida|vangRuda|vangRRida|_vei_120@2778|_vei2_pn237
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2777">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2779">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn236">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn237">2:237</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn238">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaṅgṛda</b> is the name of a demon or a human foe in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202778"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202778">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202778"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202778">↑</a> i. 53, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 149.</li></ol></div>

वज्र|వజ్ర|ವಜ್ರ|vajra|_vei_120@2779|_vei2_pn237
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2778">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2780">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn236">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn237">2:237</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn238">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vajra</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202779"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202779">1</a></sup> denotes, according to Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202779"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202779">2</a></sup> the ‘handle,’ while <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कूट">kūṭa</a></b> means the ‘head’ of the hammer.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202779"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202779">↑</a> vi. 24, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202779"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202779">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 138.</li></ol></div>

वडव|వడవ|ವಡವ|vaḍava|vaDava|_vei_120@2780|_vei2_pn237
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2779">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2781">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn236">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn237">2:237</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn238">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaḍava</b> is a common name for a ‘mare’ in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202780">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202780">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 1, 1, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 6, 3; iii. 8, 22, 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 5, 2. 19, etc. A derivative of this word is the masculine <a class="vei_120a" href="वडव">vaḍava</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 8, 3.</li></ol></div>

वणिज्|వణిజ్|ವಣಿಜ್|vaṇij|vaNij|_vei_120@2781|_vei2_pn237
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2780">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2782">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn236">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn237">2:237</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn238">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaṇij</b> denotes ‘merchant’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202781"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202781">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202781"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202781">2</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पणि">paṇi</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रय">kraya</a>;</b> <i>cf.</i> also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वाणिज">vāṇija</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202781"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202781">↑</a> i. 112, 11; v. 45, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202781"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202781">↑</a> Av. iii. 15, 1, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 257.</li></ol></div>

वणिज्या|వణిజ్యా|ವಣಿಜ್ಯಾ|vaṇijyā|vaNijyaa|_vei_120@2782|_vei2_pn238
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2781">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2783">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn237">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn238">2:238</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn239">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaṇijyā</b> in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202782"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202782">1</a></sup> denotes the business of a merchant (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="वणिज्">vaṇij</a></b>) ‘trade.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202782"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202782">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 4, 21; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvii. 1, 2.</li></ol></div>

वत्स|वत्स|వత్స_౧|వత్స|ವತ್ಸ_೧|ವತ್ಸ|vatsa_1|vatsa|_vei_120@2783|_vei2_pn238
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2782">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2784">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn237">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn238">2:238</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn239">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Vatsa</b> is often found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202783"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202783">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202783"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202783">2</a></sup> in the sense of ‘calf.’ Reference is made to the use of a calf to induce the cow to give milk,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202783"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202783">3</a></sup> and to the separation of the cows from the calves at certain times.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202783"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202783">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202783"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202783">↑</a> iii. 33, 3; iv. 18, 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202783"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202783">↑</a> Av. iv. 18, 2; xii. 4, 7 (wolves kill them); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 4, 11, 4 (the cow caresses the calf on birth), etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202783"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202783">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 6, 2; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 13, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202783"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202783">↑</a> Rv. v. 30, 10; viii. 88, 1. See Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 11<i>c.</i></li></ol></div>

वत्स|वत्स|వత్స_౨|వత్స|ವತ್ಸ_೨|ವತ್ಸ|vatsa_2|vatsa|_vei_120@2784|_vei2_pn238
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2783">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2785">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn237">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn238">2:238</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn239">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Vatsa</b> occurs several times in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202784">1</a></sup> as the name of a singer, a son or descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a>.</b> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202784">2</a></sup> he is said to have passed successfully through a fire ordeal to which he resorted for the purpose of proving to his rival, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मेधातिथि">medhātithi</a>,</b> the purity of his descent. He is also mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202784">3</a></sup> as the recipient of bounty from <b>Tirindara <a class="vei_120a" href="पारशव्य">pāraśavya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202784">↑</a> viii. 6, 1; 8, 8; 9, 1; 11, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202784">↑</a> xiv. 6, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202784">↑</a> xvi. 11, 20. He also occurs in Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxiv. 5, 11.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 105; Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 36-38.</li></ol></div>

वत्सतर|वत्सतरि|వత్సతర|వత్సతరి|ವತ್ಸತರ|ವತ್ಸತರಿ|vatsatara|vatsatari|_vei_120@2785|_vei2_pn238
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2784">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2786">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn237">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn238">2:238</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn239">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vatsatara, Vatsatari,</b> denotes a ‘young calf’ in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202785"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202785">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202785"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202785">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 17, 1; 18, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxiv. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 27, 2, etc.</li></ol></div>

वत्स-नपात् बाभ्रव|वत्सनपात् बाभ्रव|वत्स|नपात् बाभ्रव|వత్స-నపాత్ బాభ్రవ|వత్సనపాత్ బాభ్రవ|వత్స|నపాత్ బాభ్రవ|ವತ್ಸ-ನಪಾತ್ ಬಾಭ್ರವ|ವತ್ಸನಪಾತ್ ಬಾಭ್ರವ|ವತ್ಸ|ನಪಾತ್ ಬಾಭ್ರವ|vatsa-napāt bābhrava|vatsanapāt bābhrava|vatsa|napāt bābhrava|vatsa-napaat baabhrava|vatsanapaat baabhrava|napaat baabhrava|_vei_120@2786|_vei2_pn238
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2785">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2787">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn237">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn238">2:238</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn239">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vatsa-<a class="vei_120a" href="नपात्">napāt</a> Bābhrava</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बभ्रु">babhru</a></b>’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पथिन् सौभर">pathin saubhara</a>,</b> in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202786"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202786">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202786"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202786">↑</a> ii. 5, 22; iv. 5, 28 (Mādhyaṃdina = ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

वत्स-प्री भालन्दन|वत्सप्री भालन्दन|वत्स|प्री भालन्दन|వత్స-ప్రీ భాలన్దన|వత్సప్రీ భాలన్దన|వత్స|ప్రీ భాలన్దన|ವತ್ಸ-ಪ್ರೀ ಭಾಲನ್ದನ|ವತ್ಸಪ್ರೀ ಭಾಲನ್ದನ|ವತ್ಸ|ಪ್ರೀ ಭಾಲನ್ದನ|vatsa-prī bhālandana|vatsaprī bhālandana|vatsa|prī bhālandana|vatsa-prii bhaalandana|vatsaprii bhaalandana|prii bhaalandana|vatsa-pree bhaalandana|vatsapree bhaalandana|pree bhaalandana|_vei_120@2787|_vei2_pn238
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2786">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2788">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn237">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn238">2:238</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn239">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vatsa-prī Bhālandana</b> (‘descendant of Bhalandana’) is the name of a sage who ‘saw’ the Vātsapra <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> (chant). He  is mentioned in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202787"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202787">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202787"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202787">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202787"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202787">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 2, 1, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xix. 12 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 470); Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 2, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202787"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202787">↑</a> xii. 11, 25. <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 7, 4, 1.</li></ol></div>

वधक|వధక|ವಧಕ|vadhaka|_vei_120@2788|_vei2_pn239
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2787">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2789">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn238">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn239">2:239</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn240">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vadhaka</b> is the name of some sort of ‘reed’ in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202788"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202788">1</a></sup> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202788"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202788">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202788"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202788">↑</a> viii. 8, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202788"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202788">↑</a> v. 4, 5, 14.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 72; St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

वधर्|వధర్|ವಧರ್|vadhar|_vei_120@2789|_vei2_pn239
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2788">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2790">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn238">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn239">2:239</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn240">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vadhar</b> means a ‘weapon’ generally; it is used not merely of a divine,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202789"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202789">1</a></sup> but also of a human<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202789"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202789">2</a></sup> weapon in the Rigveda.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202789"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202789">↑</a> i. 32, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202789"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202789">↑</a> Rv. iv. 22, 9; viii. 22, 8; 24, 27.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 221.</li></ol></div>

वधू|वधू|వధూ_౧|వధూ|ವಧೂ_೧|ವಧೂ|vadhū_1|vadhū|vadhuu_1|vadhuu|vadhoo_1|vadhoo|_vei_120@2790|_vei2_pn239
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2789">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2791">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn238">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn239">2:239</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn240">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Vadhū</b> is a frequent word for ‘woman’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202790">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202790">2</a></sup> It denotes, according to Delbrück,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202790">3</a></sup> the woman as either married or as seeking a husband, or as a bride in the wedding ceremony. The word appears to be derived from a form of the root <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वह्">vah</a>,</i> ‘to carry,’ as is <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वहतु">vahatu</a>,</i> ‘the bridal procession,’ thus meaning ‘she who is to be or has been conducted home.’ Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202790">4</a></sup> however, objects to this explanation, regarding <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वधू">vadhū</a></i> as a derivative from a different root meaning ‘to marry.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202790">↑</a> v. 37, 3; 47, 6; vii. 69, 3; viii. 26, 13; x. 27, 12; 85, 30; 107, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202790">↑</a> Av. i. 14, 2; iv. 20, 3; x. 1, 1; xiv. 2, 9, 41, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202790">↑</a> <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 414, 439.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202790">↑</a> <i>Altindsiches Leben,</i> 108.</li></ol></div>

वधू|वधू|వధూ_౨|వధూ|ವಧೂ_೨|ವಧೂ|vadhū_2|vadhū|vadhuu_2|vadhuu|vadhoo_2|vadhoo|_vei_120@2791|_vei2_pn239
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2790">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2792">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn238">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn239">2:239</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn240">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Vadhū</b> is in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202791">1</a></sup> taken by Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202791">2</a></sup> to denote a ‘female animal,’ while Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202791">3</a></sup> urges that it means a ‘female slave.’ As far as the use of <a class="vei_120a" href="वधू">vadhū</a> goes, either meaning is abnormal, for if <a class="vei_120a" href="वधू">vadhū</a> never elsewhere  means a female animal (from <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वह्">vah</a>,</i> to ‘draw’ a cart), neither does it denote a slave: as the passage refers to a gift of fifty Vadhūs by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a> Paurukutsya</b> to the singer, the latter must have been a polygamist of an advanced type to require fifty wives. The same doubt arises in the case of <i>vadhūmant,</i> which is used in the Rigveda and Atharvaveda as an epithet of the chariot (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a></b>),<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202791">4</a></sup> of horses (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्व">aśva</a></b>),<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202791">5</a></sup> and of buffaloes (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="उष्ट्र">uṣṭra</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202791">6</a></sup> Zimmer sees in all cases a reference to slaves in the chariots or with the horses: this interpretation has the support of the Bṛhaddevatā.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202791">7</a></sup> Roth's version of the references to horses or buffaloes as ‘suitable for draught’ is not very happy; if <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वधू">vadhū</a></i> is really a female animal <i>vadhūmant</i> means rather ‘together with mares,’ or ‘together with female buffaloes,’ which makes reasonable sense.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202791">8</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202791">↑</a> viii. 19, 36. <i>Cf.</i> also v. 47, 6, as taken by Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 319.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202791">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202791">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 108, 109.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202791">↑</a> i. 126, 3; vii. 18, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202791">↑</a> viii. 68, 17. <i>Cf.</i> vi. 27, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202791">↑</a> Av. xx. 127, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202791">↑</a> iii. 147 <i>et seq.,</i> with Macdonell's notes.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202791">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 197; Pischel, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 35, 712 <i>et seq.;</i> Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

वध्रिमती|वध्रिमति|వధ్రిమతీ|వధ్రిమతి|ವಧ್ರಿಮತೀ|ವಧ್ರಿಮತಿ|vadhrimatī|vadhrimati|vadhrimatii|vadhrimatee|_vei_120@2792|_vei2_pn240
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2791">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2793">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn239">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn240">2:240</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn241">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vadhrimatī,</b> ‘having an impotent man as a husband,’ seems in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202792">1</a></sup> to be the name of a woman who owed the restoration of her husband's virility to the Aśvins, and obtained a son, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्यहस्त">hiraṇyahasta</a>.</b> The word is, however, possibly only descriptive.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202792">↑</a> i. 116, 13; 117, 24; vi. 62, 7; x. 39, 7; 65, 12.</li></ol></div>

वध्र्य्-अश्व|वध्र्य्-अश्व|वध्र्यश्व|वध्र्य्|अश्व|వధ్ర్య్-అశ్వ_౧|వధ్ర్య్-అశ్వ|వధ్ర్యశ్వ|వధ్ర్య్|అశ్వ|ವಧ್ರ್ಯ್-ಅಶ್ವ_೧|ವಧ್ರ್ಯ್-ಅಶ್ವ|ವಧ್ರ್ಯಶ್ವ|ವಧ್ರ್ಯ್|ಅಶ್ವ|vadhry-aśva_1|vadhry-aśva|vadhryaśva|vadhry|aśva|vadhry-ashva_1|vadhry-ashva|vadhryashva|ashva|_vei_120@2793|_vei2_pn240
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2792">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2794">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn239">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn240">2:240</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn241">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Vadhry-aśva,</b> ‘having castrated horses,’ is the name in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202793"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202793">1</a></sup> of a prince, the father of <b>Divodāsa,</b> and an energetic supporter of the fire cult, as was his son after him. He is mentioned in a long list of names in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202793"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202793">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202793"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202793">↑</a> vi. 61, 1; x. 69, 1 <i>et seq.</i> <b>Sumitra,</b> in the latter hymn, can hardly be a name of his.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202793"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202793">↑</a> iv. 29, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxiv. 6, 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie.</i> 1, 97.</li></ol></div>

वध्र्य्-अश्व आनूप|वध्र्य्-अश्व आनूप|वध्र्यश्व आनूप|वध्र्य्|अश्व आनूप|వధ్ర్య్-అశ్వ ఆనూప_౨|వధ్ర్య్-అశ్వ ఆనూప|వధ్ర్యశ్వ ఆనూప|వధ్ర్య్|అశ్వ ఆనూప|ವಧ್ರ್ಯ್-ಅಶ್ವ ಆನೂಪ_೨|ವಧ್ರ್ಯ್-ಅಶ್ವ ಆನೂಪ|ವಧ್ರ್ಯಶ್ವ ಆನೂಪ|ವಧ್ರ್ಯ್|ಅಶ್ವ ಆನೂಪ|vadhry-aśva ānūpa_2|vadhry-aśva ānūpa|vadhryaśva ānūpa|vadhry|aśva ānūpa|vadhry-ashva aanuupa_2|vadhry-ashva aanuupa|vadhryashva aanuupa|ashva aanuupa|vadhry-ashva aanoopa_2|vadhry-ashva aanoopa|vadhryashva aanoopa|ashva aanoopa|_vei_120@2794|_vei2_pn240
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2793">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2795">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn239">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn240">2:240</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn241">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Vadhry-<a class="vei_120a" href="अश्व">aśva</a> Ānūpa</b> (‘descendant of Anūpa’) is the name of the seer of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a>, or chant, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 3, 17). </p></div>

वन|వన|ವನ|vana|_vei_120@2795|_vei2_pn241
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2794">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2796">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn240">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn241">2:241</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn242">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vana</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202795">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202795">2</a></sup> denotes the ‘forest,’ not necessarily of trees only, but, like <a class="vei_120a" href="अरण्य">araṇya</a>, the wild uninhabited land.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202795">3</a></sup> It also means ‘wooden cup’ used in the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> ritual,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202795">4</a></sup> and in one passage perhaps a part of the chariot.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202795">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202795">↑</a> i. 54, 1; 65, 8; iii. 51, 5; v. 41, 11, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202795">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, lxxvi. 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202795">↑</a> Rv. vii. 1, 19 (opposed to <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दम">dama</a>,</i> ‘home’).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202795">↑</a> Rv. i. 55, 4; ii. 14, 9, etc. See Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 163, 166, 193.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202795">↑</a> viii. 34, 18.</li></ol></div>

वन-प|वनप|वन|వన-ప|వనప|వన|ವನ-ಪ|ವನಪ|ವನ|vana-pa|vanapa|vana|_vei_120@2796|_vei2_pn241
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2795">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2797">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn240">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn241">2:241</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn242">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vana-pa,</b> ‘forest-guardian,’ is included in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202796">1</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b>Dāvapa.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202796"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202796">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Samhitā, xxx. 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 11, 1.</li></ol></div>

वनर्-गु|वनर्गु|वनर्|వనర్-గు|వనర్గు|వనర్|ವನರ್-ಗು|ವನರ್ಗು|ವನರ್|vanar-gu|vanargu|vanar|_vei_120@2797|_vei2_pn241
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2796">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2798">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn240">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn241">2:241</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn242">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vanar-gu,</b> ‘forest-goer,’ is used in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202797">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202797">2</a></sup> to designate robbers who haunt the forests. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202797">3</a></sup> the term is more generally opposed to civilized men (<i>kavayaḥ,</i> ‘sages’; <i>vanargavaḥ,</i> ‘savages’).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202797">↑</a> x. 4, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202797">↑</a> iv. 36, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202797">↑</a> Āraṇya Saṃhitā, iv. 9.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

वनस्-पति|वनस्पति|वनस्|वन|पति|వనస్-పతి|వనస్పతి|వనస్|వన|పతి|ವನಸ್-ಪತಿ|ವನಸ್ಪತಿ|ವನಸ್|ವನ|ಪತಿ|vanas-pati|vanaspati|vanas|vana|pati|_vei_120@2798|_vei2_pn241
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2797">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2799">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn240">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn241">2:241</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn242">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vanas-pati,</b> ‘lord of the forest,’ primarily denotes ‘tree,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202798">1</a></sup> and then ‘post’ or ‘pole.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202798">2</a></sup> In some passages it is applied either to a part of the chariot or to the chariot as a whole.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202798">3</a></sup> It also means a ‘wooden drum’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202798">4</a></sup> and a ‘wooden amulet,’<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202798">5</a></sup> while in some passages<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202798">6</a></sup> it denotes the plant <i>par excellence,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202798">↑</a> Rv. i. 166, 5; iii. 34, 10; v. 7, 4; 41, 8, etc.; Av. xi. 6, 1 (distinguished from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वीरुध्">vīrudh</a></b> and <b>Oradhi</b>); 9, 24, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202798">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 8, 4; Av. ix. 3, 11, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202798">↑</a> Rv. ii. 37, 3; iii. 53, 20; vi. 47, 26; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ix. 11. See Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 251.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202798">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ix. 12. <i>Cf.</i> Av. xii. 3, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202798">↑</a> Av. vi. 85, 1; x. 3, 8. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202798">↑</a> Rv. i. 91, 6; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x. 23, etc.</li></ol></div>

वन्दन|वन्दन|వన్దన_౧|వన్దన|ವನ್ದನ_೧|ವನ್ದನ|vandana_1|vandana|_vei_120@2799|_vei2_pn241
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2798">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2800">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn240">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn241">2:241</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn242">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Vandana</b> is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202799"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202799">1</a></sup> as the name of a disease, apparently some sort of eruption spreading over the body. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202799"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202799">↑</a> vii. 50, 2 <i>Cf.</i> 21, 5; Av. vii. 113, 2; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="तृष्ट">tṛṣṭa</a>-vandanā,</i> ‘having a rough eruption,’ vii. 113, 1; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 391; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 564, 565 Whitney. Translation of the Atharvaveda, 469.</li></ol></div>

वन्दन|वन्दन|వన్దన_౨|వన్దన|ವನ್ದನ_೨|ವನ್ದನ|vandana_2|vandana|_vei_120@2800|_vei2_pn242
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2799">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2801">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn241">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn242">2:242</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn243">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Vandana</b> is the name of a protégé of the Aśvins in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202800"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202800">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202800"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202800">↑</a> i. 112, 5; 116, 11; 117, 5; 118, 8; x. 39, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Baunack, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 50, 263 <i>et seq.;</i> Oldenberg, <i>ṚgvedaNoten,</i> 1, 109.</li></ol></div>

वन्धुर|వన్ధుర|ವನ್ಧುರ|vandhura|_vei_120@2801|_vei2_pn242
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2800">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2802">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn241">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn242">2:242</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn243">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vandhura</b> denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202801"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202801">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202801"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202801">2</a></sup> the ‘seat’ of the chariot. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202801"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202801">↑</a> i. 139, 4; iii. 14, 3; vi. 47, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202801"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202801">↑</a> Av. x. 4, 2. The Aśvins' car is <i>trivandhura,</i> ‘having three seats,’ because the Aśvins are a pair, and the charioteer makes a third. <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 47, 2; 118, 1. 2; 157, 3; 183, 1; vii. 69, 2; 71. 4; viii. 22, 5; and <i>cf.</i> ix. 62, 17. See Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> viii, 247; Weber, Proceedings of the Berlin Academy, 1898, 564; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 241, n. 371.</li></ol></div>

वप|వప|ವಪ|vapa|_vei_120@2802|_vei2_pn242
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2801">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2803">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn241">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn242">2:242</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn243">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vapa,</b> ‘sower,’ is mentioned in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202802"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202802">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202802"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202802">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 3, 1.</li></ol></div>

वपन|వపన|ವಪನ|vapana|_vei_120@2803|_vei2_pn242
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2802">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2804">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn241">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn242">2:242</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn243">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vapana</b> in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202803"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202803">1</a></sup> denotes the process of ‘shaving.’ <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षुर">kṣura</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="केश">keśa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202803"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202803">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 7, 17, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 2, 1.</li></ol></div>

वपा|వపా|ವಪಾ|vapā|vapaa|_vei_120@2804|_vei2_pn242
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2803">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2805">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn241">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn242">2:242</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn243">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vapā</b> in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202804"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202804">1</a></sup> denotes an ant-hill.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202804"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202804">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 1, 2, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 3, 4; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 3, 3, 5.</li></ol></div>

वप्तृ|వప్తృ|ವಪ್ತೃ|vaptṛ|vaptR|vaptRi|vaptRu|vaptRRi|_vei_120@2805|_vei2_pn242
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2804">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2806">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn241">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn242">2:242</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn243">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaptṛ</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202805">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202805">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘shaver,’ ‘barber.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202805">↑</a> x. 142, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202805"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202805">↑</a> Av. viii. 2, 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 6, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 266; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 235, n. 4.</li></ol></div>

वप्र|వప్ర|ವಪ್ರ|vapra|_vei_120@2806|_vei2_pn242
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2805">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2807">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn241">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn242">2:242</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn243">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vapra,</b> ‘rampart,’ is a conjectural reading in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202806">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202806">↑</a> vii. 71, 1. See Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 435, 436.</li></ol></div>

वम्र|वम्र|వమ్ర_౧|వమ్ర|ವಮ್ರ_೧|ವಮ್ರ|vamra_1|vamra|_vei_120@2807|_vei2_pn243
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2806">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2808">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn242">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn243">2:243</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn244">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Vamra,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202807">1</a></sup> <b>Vamrī,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202807">2</a></sup> are the names of the male and female ‘ant’ in the Rigveda and later. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वपा">vapā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202807">↑</a> Rv. i. 51, 9; viii. 102, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202807">↑</a> Rv. iv. 19, 9 (where the son of an unmarried maiden is exposed to be eaten by ants); Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 1, 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 1, 1, 8. 14, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 97.</li></ol></div>

वम्र|वम्र|వమ్ర_౨|వమ్ర|ವಮ್ರ_೨|ವಮ್ರ|vamra_2|vamra|_vei_120@2808|_vei2_pn243
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2807">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2809">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn242">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn243">2:243</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn244">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Vamra</b> is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> in the <b>Rigveda.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202808"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202808">1</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वम्रक">vamraka</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202808"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202808">↑</a> i. 51, 9; 112, 15; x. 99, 5.</li></ol></div>

वम्रक|వమ్రక|ವಮ್ರಕ|vamraka|_vei_120@2809|_vei2_pn243
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2808">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2810">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn242">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn243">2:243</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn244">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vamraka</b> is mentioned in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202809"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202809">1</a></sup> where Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202809"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202809">2</a></sup> thinks that an ‘ant’ is meant. But Pischel,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202809"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202809">3</a></sup> with more probability, thinks that it is a proper name, perhaps equivalent to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वम्र">vamra</a>,</b> and denoting the child of a maiden who was saved from being devoured by ants.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202809"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202809">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202809"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202809">↑</a> x. 99, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202809"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202809">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202809"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202809">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 238, 239.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202809"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202809">↑</a> Rv. iv. 19, 9; 30, 16.</li></ol></div>

वयस्|वयस्|वय|వయస్_౧|వయస్|వయ|ವಯಸ್_೧|ವಯಸ್|ವಯ|vayas_1|vayas|vaya|_vei_120@2810|_vei2_pn243
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2809">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2811">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn242">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn243">2:243</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn244">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Vayas</b> is a common name for ‘bird’ in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202810"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202810">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202810"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202810">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202810"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202810">↑</a> iii. 21, 2; vi. 59, 1; vii. 96, 1; viii. 7, 24, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202810"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202810">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 1, 1, 1; v. 2, 5, 1; 5, 3, 2. etc.</li></ol></div>

वयस्|वयस्|वय|వయస్_౨|వయస్|వయ|ವಯಸ್_೨|ವಯಸ್|ವಯ|vayas_2|vayas|vaya|_vei_120@2811|_vei2_pn243
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2810">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2812">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn242">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn243">2:243</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn244">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Vayas</b> denotes in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202811"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202811">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202811"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202811">2</a></sup> the ‘age of animals or men.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202811"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202811">↑</a> xii. 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202811"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202811">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 12, 5, 9; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 2, 21; 3, 3, 3. etc.</li></ol></div>

वया|వయా|ವಯಾ|vayā|vayaa|_vei_120@2812|_vei2_pn243
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2811">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2813">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn242">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn243">2:243</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn244">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vayā</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202812">1</a></sup> denotes the ‘branch’ of a tree.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202812">↑</a> ii. 5, 4; v. 1, 1; vi. 7, 6; 13, 1; viii. 13, 6, 17, etc.</li></ol></div>

वयित्री|वयित्रि|వయిత్రీ|వయిత్రి|ವಯಿತ್ರೀ|ವಯಿತ್ರಿ|vayitrī|vayitri|vayitrii|vayitree|_vei_120@2813|_vei2_pn243
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2812">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2814">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn242">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn243">2:243</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn244">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vayitrī</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 8, 9) denotes a ‘female weaver.’ </p></div>

वय्य|వయ్య|ವಯ್ಯ|vayya|_vei_120@2814|_vei2_pn244
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2813">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2815">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn243">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn244">2:244</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn245">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vayya</b> occurs in several passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202814"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202814">1</a></sup> in connexion with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वीति">turvīti</a>,</b> of whom the word is, according to Sāyaṇa,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202814"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202814">2</a></sup> a patronymic in one passage. Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202814"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202814">3</a></sup> is inclined to think that the sense of ‘companion’ would suit all passages.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202814"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202814">↑</a> i. 54, 6; 112, 6 (where <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वीति">turvīti</a> does not occur); ii. 13, 12; iv. 19, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202814"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202814">↑</a> On Rv. i. 54, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202814"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202814">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> quoting ix. 68, 8, as a clear case.</li></ol></div>

वर|వర|ವರ|vara|_vei_120@2815|_vei2_pn244
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2814">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2816">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn243">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn244">2:244</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn245">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vara</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202815"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202815">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202815"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202815">2</a></sup> regularly denotes a ‘wooer.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202815"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202815">↑</a> i. 83, 2; v. 60, 4; ix. 101, 14; x. 85, 8. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202815"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202815">↑</a> Av. ii. 36, 1. 5. 6; xi. 8, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 7, 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

वरण|వరణ|ವರಣ|varaṇa|varaNa|_vei_120@2816|_vei2_pn244
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2815">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2817">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn243">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn244">2:244</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn245">↷</a><hr><p><b>Varaṇa</b> is the name of a tree (<i>Crataeva Roxburghii</i>) in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202816"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202816">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202816"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202816">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202816"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202816">↑</a> vi. 85, 1; x. 3, 1, etc.; xix. 32, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202816"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202816">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 9. 10; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 8, 4, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 60, 61; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 505.</li></ol></div>

वरणावती|वरणावति|వరణావతీ|వరణావతి|ವರಣಾವತೀ|ವರಣಾವತಿ|varaṇāvatī|varaṇāvati|varaNaavatii|varaNaavati|varaNaavatee|_vei_120@2817|_vei2_pn244
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2816">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2818">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn243">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn244">2:244</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn245">↷</a><hr><p><b>Varaṇāvatī</b> is found in one passage of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202817">1</a></sup> It seems to be, as Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202817">2</a></sup> thought, the name of a stream, and is regarded by Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202817">3</a></sup> as the Ganges. Bloomfield,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202817">4</a></sup> while considering that a plant may, as Sāyaṇa thinks, be meant, yet regards a reference to a river as probable. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202817">↑</a> iv. 7, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202817">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202817">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 201.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 20.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202817">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 376.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 26, 27; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 154.</li></ol></div>

वरत्रा|వరత్రా|ವರತ್ರಾ|varatrā|varatraa|_vei_120@2818|_vei2_pn244
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2817">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2819">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn243">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn244">2:244</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn245">↷</a><hr><p><b>Varatrā</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202818">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202818">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘thong’ or ‘strap.’ It was used to fasten the oxen to the yoke,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202818">3</a></sup> or perhaps to fasten the yoke to the pole.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202818">4</a></sup> Or, again, it denotes<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202818">5</a></sup> the strap which was used in drawing up water from the well (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अवत">avata</a></b>). </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202818">↑</a> iv. 57, 4 (of the plough), etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202818">↑</a> Av. xi. 3, 10; xx. 135, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202818">↑</a> Rv. x. 60, 8; 102, 8; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202818">↑</a> This suits x. 60, 8, rather more naturally, and is so taken by Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 248, 249.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202818">↑</a> Rv. x. 106, 5; Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 156.</li></ol></div>

वरशिख|వరశిఖ|ವರಶಿಖ|varaśikha|varashikha|_vei_120@2819|_vei2_pn245
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2818">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2820">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn244">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn245">2:245</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn246">↷</a><hr><p><b>Varaśikha</b> is the name of a leader whose tribe is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202819"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202819">1</a></sup> as being defeated by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अभ्यावर्तिन् चायमान">abhyāvartin cāyamāna</a></b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202819"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202819">↑</a> vi. 27, 4. 5. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 156; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 105; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 133, who thinks that <a class="vei_120a" href="वरशिख">varaśikha</a> was the leader of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a>-Vṛcīvants,</b> but this is conjectural, and not very probable. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पार्थव">pārthava</a>.</b> In the Bṛhaddevatā, v. 124 <i>et seq.,</i> the form of the name is Vāraśikha (‘descendant of Varaśikha’), occurring in the plural only.</li></ol></div>

वराह|వరాహ|ವರಾಹ|varāha|varaaha|_vei_120@2820|_vei2_pn245
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2819">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2821">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn244">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn245">2:245</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn246">↷</a><hr><p><b>Varāha,</b> ‘boar,’ is found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202820">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202820">2</a></sup> The god <a class="vei_120a" href="रुद्र">rudra</a> is described as the ‘boar of heaven.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202820">3</a></sup> The use of dogs to hunt the boar is once alluded to.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202820">4</a></sup> The variant form of the word, Varāhu, is not used except metaphorically of divinities.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202820">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202820">↑</a> i. 61, 7; viii. 77, 10; ix. 97, 7; x. 28, 4 (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रोष्टृ">kroṣṭṛ</a></b>), etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202820">↑</a> Av. viii. 7, 23; xii. 1, 48; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, viii. 2; xxv. 2, etc.; Maitrāyaṇi Saṃhitā, iii. 14. 19, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202820">↑</a> Rv. i. 114, 5. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 4, 2; vii. 1, 5, 1. etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202820">↑</a> Rv. x. 86, 4, an obscure passage.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202820"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202820">↑</a> Rv. i. 88, 5; 121, 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 9, 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 81, 82; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 67, who points out that, even in the Rigveda, its use is predominantly metraphorical, x. 28, 4, and x. 86, 4, being the only clear instances of the real sense, and of these x. 86, 4, is doubtful. See also Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 66 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

वरु|వరు|ವರು|varu|_vei_120@2821|_vei2_pn245
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2820">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2822">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn244">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn245">2:245</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn246">↷</a><hr><p><b>Varu</b> is held by Sāyaṇa to be a proper name in several passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202821">1</a></sup> where it is accented as a vocative followed by <i>suṣāmṇe.</i> Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202821">2</a></sup> considers that the name must be Varosuṣāman, despite its doubtful formation.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202821">↑</a> viii. 23, 28; 24, 28; 26, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202821">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 39, 84, 85.</li></ol></div>

वरुण-गृहीत|वरुणगृहीत|वरुण|गृहीत|వరుణ-గృహీత|వరుణగృహీత|వరుణ|గృహీత|ವರುಣ-ಗೃಹೀತ|ವರುಣಗೃಹೀತ|ವರುಣ|ಗೃಹೀತ|varuṇa-gṛhīta|varuṇagṛhīta|varuṇa|gṛhīta|varuNa-gRhiita|varuNagRhiita|varuNa|gRhiita|varuNa-gRheeta|varuNagRheeta|gRheeta|varuNa-gRihiita|varuNagRihiita|gRihiita|varuNa-gRuhiita|varuNagRuhiita|gRuhiita|varuNa-gRRihiita|varuNagRRihiita|gRRihiita|_vei_120@2822|_vei2_pn245
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2821">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2823">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn244">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn245">2:245</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn246">↷</a><hr><p><b>Varuṇa-gṛhīta,</b> ‘seized by <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a>,’ is found in several passages<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202822"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202822">1</a></sup> as a description of a man afflicted with dropsy, which is the disease sent by <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a> as a punishment for sin.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202822"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202822">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202822"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202822">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 2, 1; vi. 4, 2, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 4, 5, 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 4, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202822"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202822">↑</a> Rv. vi. 74, 4; vii. 88. 7; Av. ii. 10, 1; iv. 16, 6. 7; xiv. 1, 57; 2, 49, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 203; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 29, n. 16.</li></ol></div>

वर्चिन्|వర్చిన్|ವರ್ಚಿನ್|varcin|varchin|_vei_120@2823|_vei2_pn246
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2822">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2824">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn245">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn246">2:246</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn247">↷</a><hr><p><b>Varcin</b> is the name of a foe of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202823"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202823">1</a></sup> Being called a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202823"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202823">2</a></sup> and coupled with <a class="vei_120a" href="शम्बर">śambara</a>, he is probably to be regarded as a terrestrial foe, though he is also spoken of as an <a class="vei_120a" href="असुर">asura</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202823"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202823">3</a></sup> He may possibly have been connected with the <b>Vṛcīvants.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202823"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202823">↑</a> ii. 14, 6; iv. 30, 14. 15; vi. 47, 21; vii. 99, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202823"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202823">↑</a> Rv. iv. 30, 15; vi. 47, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202823"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202823">↑</a> Rv. vii. 99, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 152; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 103, n. 3; 3, 273; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 162 (F).</li></ol></div>

वर्ण|वर्ण|వర్ణ_౧|వర్ణ|ವರ್ಣ_೧|ವರ್ಣ|varṇa_1|varṇa|varNa_1|varNa|_vei_120@2824|_vei2_pn246
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2823">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2825">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn245">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn246">2:246</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn247">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Varṇa,</b> ‘colour,’ is a common word in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202824">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202824">2</a></sup> A large number of colours are enumerated in Vedic literature, but it is not possible to deduce any clear information as to the accuracy with which the Vedic Indian distinguished colours, or as to the principle on which his distinctions were based. The Rigveda seems to show that red or yellow colours were the most noticed, but this may be accidental.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202824">3</a></sup> ‘Black’ or ‘dark’ is denoted by <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण">kṛṣṇa</a>,</i> ‘white’ or ‘light-coloured’ by <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शुक्ल">śukla</a></i> or <i><a class="vei_120a" href="श्वेत">śveta</a>.</i> ‘Black’ seems to be meant in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202824">4</a></sup> by <i>śyenī</i> also. ‘Dark-grey’ or ‘dusky’ is expressed by <i><a class="vei_120a" href="श्याम">śyāma</a>.</i><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202824">5</a></sup> The sense of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नील">nīla</a></i><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202824">6</a></sup> is doubtful, perhaps ‘dark-blue,’ ‘bluish-black.’ The series of words <i><a class="vei_120a" href="हरि">hari</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="हरिण">hariṇa</a>, harit, <a class="vei_120a" href="हरित">harita</a>,</i> seems, on the whole, to denote ‘yellow,’ but ‘green’ is also a possible rendering, since the epithet is used of the frog.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202824">7</a></sup> ‘Brown’ is certainly the meaning of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="बभ्रु">babhru</a>,</i> which is used of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विभीतक">vibhītaka</a></b> nut (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a></b>). ‘Reddish-brown’ seems to be the tinge implied by <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कपिल">kapila</a></i><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202824">8</a></sup> (‘monkey-coloured’), while <i>piṅgala</i> appears to denote a shade of brown in which yellow pre dominates, ‘tawny.’<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202824">9</a></sup> ‘Yellow’ is expressed by <i>pīta</i> as well as <i>pāṇḍu.</i><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202824">10</a></sup> A garment of saffron (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="माहारजन">māhārajana</a></i>) is mentioned in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202824">11</a></sup> <i>Rudhira</i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="लोहित">lohita</a></i> are red, while <i>aruṇa</i> is ‘ruddy.’ <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कल्माष">kalmāṣa</a></i> means ‘spotted,’<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202824">12</a></sup> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शिल्प">śilpa</a></i> ‘dappled,’<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202824">13</a></sup> while mingled shades like <i>aruṇa-<a class="vei_120a" href="पिशंग">piśaṅga</a>,</i> ‘reddish brown,’ also occur.<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1202824">14</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202824">↑</a> i. 73, 7; 96, 5; 113, 2; iv. 5. 13; ix. 97, 15; 104, 4; 105, 1; x. 3, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202824">↑</a> Av. i. 22, 1. 2; 23, 2; xi. 8, 16; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 26, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202824">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 11, cxxi <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202824">↑</a> i. 140, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 8; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 250, 251.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202824">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1, 3, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202824">↑</a> The <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नील">nīla</a></i> of the Chāndogya Upaniśad iii. 6, 1, is replaced by <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण">kṛṣṇa</a></i> in the Kausītaki <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> iv. to <i>Cf.</i> Rv. viii. 19, 31. In the post-Vedic language <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नील">nīla</a></i> describes the colour of dark blue objects, such as indigo, sapphire, etc. That the word already had some such sense in the Rigveda is suggested by its use in allusions to the smoke of <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202824">↑</a> Rv. vii. 103, 6, and <i>cf.</i> iii. 44, 3; Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 1, 365, n.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202824">↑</a> Rv. x. 27, 16; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202824">↑</a> Av. xi. 5, 26; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 1, 6, 2; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202824">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 3, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202824">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202824">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxix. 58.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202824">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 5; xxix. 58; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 22, 1; 6, 13, 1; 20, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1202824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1202824">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 6, 11, 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 119 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

वर्ण|वर्ण|వర్ణ_౨|వర్ణ|ವರ್ಣ_೨|ವರ್ಣ|varṇa_2|varṇa|varNa_2|varNa|_vei_120@2825|_vei2_pn247
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2824">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2826">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn246">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn247">2:247</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn271">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Varṇa</b> (lit. ‘colour’) in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202825">1</a></sup> is applied to denote classes of men, the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a></b> and the Āryan <a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a> being contrasted, as other passages<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202825">2</a></sup> show, on account of colour. But this use is confined to distinguishing two colours: in this respect the Rigveda differs fundamentally from the later Saṃhitās and Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202825">3</a></sup> where the four castes (<i>varṇāḥ</i>) are already fully recognized.</p><p><i>(a) Caste in the Rigveda.</i>--The use of the term <a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a> is not, of course, conclusive for the question whether caste existed in the Rigveda. In one sense it must be admitted to have existed: the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुष">puruṣa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="सूक्त">sūkta</a>, ‘hymn of man,’ in the tenth  Maṇḍala<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202825">4</a></sup> clearly contemplates the division of mankind into four classes--the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>. But the hymn being admittedly late,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202825">5</a></sup> its evidence is not cogent for the bulk of the Rigveda. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202825">6</a></sup> has with great force combatted the view that the Rigveda was produced in a society that knew the caste system. He points out that the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202825">7</a></sup> show us the Vedic Indians on the Indus as unbrahminized, and not under the caste system; he argues that the Rigveda was the product of tribes living in the Indus region and the Panjab; later on a part of this people, who had wandered farther east, developed the peculiar civilization of the caste system. He adopts the arguments of Muir,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202825">8</a></sup> derived from the study of the data of the Rigveda, viz.: that (<i>a</i>) the four castes appear only in the late Puruṣasūkta; (<i>b</i>) the term <a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>, as shown above, covers the three highest castes of later times, and is only contrasted with <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>; (<i>c</i>) that <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> is rare in the Rigveda, <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> occurs seldom,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202825">9</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a> only in the Puruṣasūkta, where too, alone, <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> are found; (<i>d</i>) that <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> denotes at first ‘poet,’ ‘sage,’ and then ‘officiating priest,’ or still later a special class of priest; (<i>e</i>) that in some only of the passages<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202825">10</a></sup> where it occurs does <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> denote a ‘priest by profession,’ while in others it denotes something peculiar to the individual, designating a person distinguished for genius or virtue, or specially chosen to receive divine inspiration.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202825">11</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, on the other hand, as Muir admits,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202825">12</a></sup> already denotes a hereditary professional priesthood.</p><p>Zimmer connects the change from the casteless system of the Rigveda to the elaborate system of the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> with the  advance of the Vedic Indians to the east, comparing the Germanic invasions that transformed the German tribes into monarchies closely allied with the church. The needs of a conquering people evoke the monarch; the lesser princes sink to the position of nobles; for repelling the attacks of aborigines or of other Āryan tribes, and for quelling the revolts of the subdued population, the state requires a standing army in the shape of the armed retainers of the king, and beside the nobility of the lesser princes arises that of the king's chief retainers, as the Thegns supplemented the Gesiths of the Anglo-Saxon monarchies.<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202825">13</a></sup> At the same time the people ceased to take part in military matters, and under climatic influences left the conduct of war to the nobility and their retainers, devoting themselves to agriculture, pastoral pursuits, and trade. But the advantage won by the nobles over the people was shared by them with the priesthood, the origin of whose power lies in the Purohitaship, as Roth first saw.<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1202825">14</a></sup></p><p>Originally the prince could sacrifice for himself and the people, but the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1202825">15</a></sup> itself shows cases, like those of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a></b> illustrating forcibly the power of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, though at the same time the right of the noble to act as <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> is seen in the case of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="देवापि आर्ष्टिषेण">devāpi ārṣṭiṣeṇa</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1202825">16</a></sup> The Brahmins saw their opportunity, through the Purohitaship, of gaining practical power during the confusion and difficulties of the wars of invasion, and secured it, though only after many struggles, the traces of which are seen in the Epic tradition.<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1202825">17</a></sup> The Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1202825">18</a></sup> also preserves relics of these conflicts in its narration of the ruin of the <b>Sṛñjayas</b> because of oppressing Brahmins, and besides other hymns of the Atharvaveda (viiixii), the <a class="vei_120a" href="शतरुद्रिय">śatarudriya</a> litany of the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a><sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1202825">19</a></sup> reflects the period of storm and stress when the aboriginal population was  still seething with discontent, and <a class="vei_120a" href="रुद्र">rudra</a> was worshipped as the patron god of all sorts of evil doers.<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1202825">20</a></sup></p><p>This version of the development of caste has received a good deal of acceptance in its main outlines, and it may almost be regarded as the recognized version.<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1202825">21</a></sup> It has, however, always been opposed by some scholars, such as Haug,<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1202825">22</a></sup> Kern,<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1202825">23</a></sup> Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1202825">24</a></sup> and more recently by Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1202825">25</a></sup> and by Geldner.<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_1202825">26</a></sup> The matter may be to some extent simplified by recognizing at once that the caste system is one that has progressively developed, and that it is not legitimate to see in the Rigveda the full caste system even of the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>; but at the same time it is difficult to doubt that the system was already well on its way to general acceptance. The argument from the nonbrahminical character of the <b>Vrātyas</b> of the Indus and Panjab loses its force when it is remembered that there is much evidence in favour of placing the composition of the bulk of the Rigveda, especially the books<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_1202825">27</a></sup> in which <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a></b> appears with <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>, in the east, the later <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a>,</b> a view supported by Pischel,<sup id="ref_120_28_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_1202825">28</a></sup> Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_29_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29_vei_1202825">29</a></sup> Hopkins,<sup id="ref_120_30_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_30_vei_1202825">30</a></sup> and Macdonell.<sup id="ref_120_31_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_31_vei_1202825">31</a></sup> Nor is it possible to maintain that <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> in the Rigveda merely means a ‘poet’ or ‘sage.’ It is admitted by Muir that in some passages it must mean a hereditary profession; in fact, there is not a single passage in which it occurs where the sense of ‘priest’ is not allowable, since the priest was of course the singer. Moreover, there are traces in the Rigveda of the threefold<sup id="ref_120_32_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_32_vei_1202825">32</a></sup> or fourfold<sup id="ref_120_33_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_33_vei_1202825">33</a></sup> division of the people  into <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्म">brahma</a>, kṣatram,</i> and <i>viśaḥ,</i> or into the three classes and the servile population. Nor even in respect to the later period, any more than to the Rigveda, is the view correct that regards the Vaiśyas as not taking part in war. The Rigveda evidently<sup id="ref_120_34_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_34_vei_1202825">34</a></sup> knows of no restriction of war to a nobility and its retainers, but the late Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_35_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_35_vei_1202825">35</a></sup> equally classes the folk with the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="बल">bala</a>,</i> ‘power,’ representing the <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> as associated with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="समिति">samiti</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सेना">senā</a>,</b> the assemblies of the people and the armed host. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_36_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_36_vei_1202825">36</a></sup> explains these references as due to tradition only; but this is hardly a legitimate argument, resting, as it does, on the false assumption that only a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> can fight. But it is (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a></b>) very doubtful whether <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> means anything more than a member of the nobility, though later, in the Epic, it included the retainers of the nobility, who increased in numbers with the growth of military monarchies, and though later the ordinary people did not necessarily take part in wars, an abstention that is, however, much exaggerated if it is treated as an absolute one. The Kṣatriyas were no doubt a hereditary body; monarchy was already hereditary (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a></b>), and it is admitted that the Śūdras were a separate body: thus all the elements of the caste system were already in existence. The <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, indeed, was a person of great importance, but it is clear, as Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_37_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_37_vei_1202825">37</a></sup> urges, that he was not the creator of the power of the priesthood, but owed his position, and the influence he could in consequence exert, to the fact that the sacrifice required for its proper performance the aid of a hereditary priest in whose possession was the traditional sacred knowledge.</p><p>Nor can any argument for the non-existence of the caste system be derived from cases like that of <b>Devāpi.</b> For, in the first place, the Upaniṣads show kings in the exercise of the priestly functions of learning and teaching, and the Upaniṣads are certainly contemporaneous with an elaborated caste system. In the second place the Rigvedic evidence is very weak, for Devāpi, who certainly acts as <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, is not stated in the  Rigveda to be a prince at all, though <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a><sup id="ref_120_38_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_38_vei_1202825">38</a></sup> calls him a <a class="vei_120a" href="कौरव्य">kauravya</a>; the hymns attributed to kings and others cannot be vindicated for them by certain evidence, though here, again, the Brāhmaṇas do not scruple to recognize Rājanyarṣis, or ‘royal sages’; and the famous <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> shows in the Rigveda no sign of the royal character which the Brāhmaṇas insist on fastening on him in the shape of royal descent in the line of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जह्नु">jahnu</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_39_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_39_vei_1202825">39</a></sup></p><p><i>(b) Caste in the later Saṃhitās and Brāhmaṇas.</i>--The relation between the later and the earlier periods of the Vedic history of caste must probably be regarded in the main as the hardening of a system already formed by the time of the Rigveda.</p><p>1. <i>The Names of the Castes.</i>--The most regular names are <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_40_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_40_vei_1202825">40</a></sup> or later <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_41_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_41_vei_1202825">41</a></sup> There are many other variants: <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्र">kṣatra</a>, Śūdrāryau;<sup id="ref_120_42_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_42_vei_1202825">42</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a>;<sup id="ref_120_43_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_43_vei_1202825">43</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>;<sup id="ref_120_44_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_44_vei_1202825">44</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a>, Viśya, <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>;<sup id="ref_120_45_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_45_vei_1202825">45</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="देव">deva</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a>;<sup id="ref_120_46_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_46_vei_1202825">46</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्र">kṣatra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_47_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_47_vei_1202825">47</a></sup> In other cases the fourth class is represented by a special member: <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="चाण्डाल">cāṇḍāla</a>.<sup id="ref_120_48_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_48_vei_1202825">48</a></sup> Often only the three upper classes are mentioned, as <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>;<sup id="ref_120_49_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_49_vei_1202825">49</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>, Kṣatram, <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>,<sup id="ref_120_50_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_50_vei_1202825">50</a></sup>  etc.<sup id="ref_120_51_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_51_vei_1202825">51</a></sup> Three castes--<a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>--are mentioned in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_52_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_52_vei_1202825">52</a></sup> and two castes are repeatedly mentioned together, either <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्र">kṣatra</a>, or <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्र">kṣatra</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>.<sup id="ref_120_53_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_53_vei_1202825">53</a></sup></p><p>2. <i>The Relation of the Castes.</i>--The ritual literature is full of minute differences respecting the castes. Thus, for example, the Śatapatha prescribes different sizes of funeral mounds for the four castes,<sup id="ref_120_54_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_54_vei_1202825">54</a></sup> Different modes of address are laid down for the four castes,<sup id="ref_120_55_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_55_vei_1202825">55</a></sup> as <i>ehi,</i> ‘approach’; <i>āgaccha,</i> ‘come’; <i>ādrava,</i> ‘run up’; <i>ādhāva,</i> ‘hasten up,’ which differ in degrees of politeness. The representatives of the four castes are dedicated at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) to different deities.<sup id="ref_120_56_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_56_vei_1202825">56</a></sup> The Sūtras have many similar rules.<sup id="ref_120_57_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_57_vei_1202825">57</a></sup></p><p>But the three upper castes in some respects differ markedly from the fourth, the Śūdras. The latter are in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_58_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_58_vei_1202825">58</a></sup> declared not fit to be addressed by a Dīkṣita, ‘consecrated person,’ and no <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> is to milk the cow whose milk is to be used for the Agnihotra<sup id="ref_120_59_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_59_vei_1202825">59</a></sup> (‘fire-oblation’). On the other hand, in certain passages, the <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> is given a place in the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> sacrifice,<sup id="ref_120_60_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_60_vei_1202825">60</a></sup> and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_61_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_61_vei_1202825">61</a></sup> there are given formulæ for the placing of the sacrificial fire not only for the three upper castes, but also for the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथकार">rathakāra</a>,</b>  ‘chariot-maker.’ Again, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_62_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_62_vei_1202825">62</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> is opposed as ‘eater of the oblation’ to the members of the other three castes.</p><p>The characteristics of the several castes are given under <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>:</b> they may be briefly summed up as follows: The <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> forms the basis of the state on which the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्र">kṣatra</a> rest;<sup id="ref_120_63_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_63_vei_1202825">63</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्र">kṣatra</a> are superior to the <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>;<sup id="ref_120_64_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_64_vei_1202825">64</a></sup> while all three classes are superior to the Śūdras. The real power of the state rested with the king and his nobles, with their retainers, who may be deemed the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> element. Engaged in the business of the protection of the country, its administration, the decision of legal cases, and in war, the nobles subsisted, no doubt, on the revenues in kind levied from the people, the king granting to them villages (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a></b>) for their maintenance, while some of them, no doubt, had lands of their own cultivated for them by slaves or by tenants. The states were seemingly small:<sup id="ref_120_65_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_65_vei_1202825">65</a></sup> there are no clear signs of any really large kingdoms, despite the mention of <b>Mahārājas.</b> The people, engaged in agriculture, pastoral pursuits, and trade (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="वणिज्">vaṇij</a></b>), paid tribute to the king and nobles for the protection afforded them. That, as BadenPowell suggests,<sup id="ref_120_66_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_66_vei_1202825">66</a></sup> they were not themselves agriculturists is probably erroneous; some might be landowners on a large scale, and draw their revenues from <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> tenants, or even Āryan tenants, but that the people as a whole were in this  position is extremely unlikely.<sup id="ref_120_67_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_67_vei_1202825">67</a></sup> In war the people shared the conflicts of the nobles, for there was not yet any absolute separation of the functions of the several classes. The priests may be divided into two classes--the Purohitas of the kings, who guided their employers by their counsel, and were in a position to acquire great influence in the state, as it is evident they actually did, and the ordinary priests who led quiet lives, except when they were engaged on some great festival of a king or a wealthy noble.<sup id="ref_120_68_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_68_vei_1202825">68</a></sup></p><p>The relations and functions of the castes are well summed up in a passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_69_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_69_vei_1202825">69</a></sup> which treats of them as opposed to the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>. The <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> is a receiver of gifts (<i>ā-dāyī</i>), a drinker of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> (<i>ā-pāyī</i>), a seeker of food (<i>āvasāyī</i>),<sup id="ref_120_70_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_70_vei_1202825">70</a></sup> and liable to removal at will (<i>yathākāma-prayāpyaḥ</i>).<sup id="ref_120_71_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_71_vei_1202825">71</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> is tributary to another (<i>anyasya balikṛt</i>), to be lived on by another (<i>anyasyādyaḥ</i>), and to be oppressed at will (<i>yathākāma-jyeyaḥ</i>).<sup id="ref_120_72_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_72_vei_1202825">72</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> is the servant of another (<i>anyasya preṣyaḥ</i>), to be expelled at will (<i>kāmotthāpyaḥ</i>), and to be slain  at pleasure (<i>yathākāma-vadhyaḥ</i>).<sup id="ref_120_73_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_73_vei_1202825">73</a></sup> The descriptions seem calculated to show the relation of each of the castes to the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a>. Even the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> he can control, whilst the <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> is his inferior and tributary, whom he can remove without cause from his land,<sup id="ref_120_74_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_74_vei_1202825">74</a></sup> but who is still free, and whom he cannot maim or slay without due process. The <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> has no rights of property or life against the noble, especially the king.</p><p>The passage is a late one, and the high place of the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> is to some extent accounted for by this fact. It is clear that in the course of time the <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> fell more and more in position with the hardening of the divisions of caste. Weber<sup id="ref_120_75_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_75_vei_1202825">75</a></sup> shows reason for believing that the <a class="vei_120a" href="वाजपेय">vājapeya</a> sacrifice, a festival of which a chariot race forms an integral part,<sup id="ref_120_76_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_76_vei_1202825">76</a></sup> was, as the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_77_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_77_vei_1202825">77</a></sup> says, once a sacrifice for a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>, as well as for a priest or king. But the king, too, had to suffer diminution of his influence at the hands of the priest: the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> texts<sup id="ref_120_78_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_78_vei_1202825">78</a></sup> show that the <a class="vei_120a" href="वाजपेय">vājapeya</a> was originally a lesser sacrifice which, in the case of a king, was followed by the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>, or consecration of him as an overlord of lesser kings, and in that of the Brahmin by the <a class="vei_120a" href="बृहस्पतिसव">bṛhaspatisava</a>, a festival celebrated on his appointment as a royal <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>. But the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_79_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_79_vei_1202825">79</a></sup> exalts the <a class="vei_120a" href="वाजपेय">vājapeya</a>, in which a priest could be the sacrificer, over the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>, from which he was excluded, and identifies it with the <a class="vei_120a" href="बृहस्पतिसव">bṛhaspatisava</a>, a clear piece of juggling in the interests of the priestly pretentions. But we must not overestimate the value of such passages, or the exaltation of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> in the later books of the Śatapatha  and <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Brāhmaṇas as evidence of a real growth in the priestly power: these books represent the views of the priests of what their own powers should be, and to some extent were in the <a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a>. Another side of the picture is presented in the Pāli literature,<sup id="ref_120_80_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_80_vei_1202825">80</a></sup> which, belonging to a later period than the Vedic, undoubtedly underestimates the position of the priests; while the Epic,<sup id="ref_120_81_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_81_vei_1202825">81</a></sup> more nearly contemporaneous with the later Vedic period, displays, despite all priestly redaction, the temporal superiority of the nobility in clear light.</p><p>Although clear distinctions were made between the different castes, there is little trace in Vedic literature of one of the leading characteristics of the later system, the impurity communicated by the touch or contact of the inferior castes,<sup id="ref_120_82_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_82_vei_1202825">82</a></sup> which is seen both directly in the purification rendered necessary in case of contact with a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>, and indirectly in the prohibition of eating in company with men of lower caste.<sup id="ref_120_83_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_83_vei_1202825">83</a></sup> It is true that prohibition of eating in company with others does appear,<sup id="ref_120_84_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_84_vei_1202825">84</a></sup> but not in connexion with caste: its purpose is to preserve the peculiar sanctity of those who perform a certain rite or believe in a certain doctrine; for persons who eat of the same food together, according to primitive thought, acquire the same characteristics and enter into a sacramental communion. But Vedic literature does not yet show that to take food from an inferior caste was forbidden as destroying  purity.<sup id="ref_120_85_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_85_vei_1202825">85</a></sup> Nor, of course, has the caste system developed the constitution with a head, a council, and common festivals which the modern caste has; for such an organization is not found even in the Epic or in the Pāli literature.<sup id="ref_120_86_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_86_vei_1202825">86</a></sup> The Vedic characteristics of caste are heredity, pursuit of a common occupation, and restriction on intermarriage.</p><p>3. <i>Restrictions on Intermarriage.</i>--Arrian, in his <i>Indica,</i><sup id="ref_120_87_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_87_vei_1202825">87</a></sup> probably on the authority of Megasthenes, makes the prohibition of marriage between , no doubt ‘castes,’ a characteristic of Indian life. The evidence of Pāli literature<sup id="ref_120_88_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_88_vei_1202825">88</a></sup> is in favour of this view, though it shows that a king could marry whom he wished, and could make his son by that wife the heir appearent. But it equally shows that there were others who held that not the father's but the mother's rank determined the social standing of the son. Though <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a><sup id="ref_120_89_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_89_vei_1202825">89</a></sup> recognizes the possibility of marriage with the next lower caste as producing legitimate children, still he condemns the marriage of an Āryan with a woman of lower caste. The Pāraskara <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_90_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_90_vei_1202825">90</a></sup> allows the marriage of a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> with a wife of his own caste or of the lower caste, of a Brahmin with a wife of his own caste or of the two lower classes, and of a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> with a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> wife only. But it quotes the opinion of others that all of them can marry a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> wife, while other authorities condemn the marriage with a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> wife in certain circumstances, which implies that in other cases it might be justified.<sup id="ref_120_91_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_91_vei_1202825">91</a></sup> The earlier  literature bears out this impression: much stress is laid on descent from a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>, and on purity of descent;<sup id="ref_120_92_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_92_vei_1202825">92</a></sup> but there is other evidence for the view that even a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> need not be of pure lineage. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कवष">kavaṣa</a></b> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐलूष">ailūṣa</a> is taunted with being the son of a Dāsī, ‘slave woman,’<sup id="ref_120_93_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_93_vei_1202825">93</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वत्स">vatsa</a></b> was accused of being a Śūdrā's son, but established his purity by walking unhurt through the flames of a fire ordeal.<sup id="ref_120_94_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_94_vei_1202825">94</a></sup> He who is learned (<i>śuśruvān</i>) is said to be a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, descended from a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> (<i>ārṣeya</i>), in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā;<sup id="ref_120_95_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_95_vei_1202825">95</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सत्यकाम">satyakāma</a>,</b> son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जबाला">jabālā</a>,</b> was accepted as a pupil by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हारिद्रुमत">hāridrumata</a></b> <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a>, though he could not name his father.<sup id="ref_120_96_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_96_vei_1202825">96</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_97_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_97_vei_1202825">97</a></sup> says that knowledge is all-important, not descent. But all this merely goes to show that there was a measure of laxity in the hereditary character of caste, not that it was not based on heredity. The <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_98_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_98_vei_1202825">98</a></sup> recognize the illicit union of <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्रा">śūdrā</a>, and <i>vice versa:</i> it is not unlikely that if illicit unions took place, legal marriage was quite possible. The <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_99_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_99_vei_1202825">99</a></sup> indeed, recognizes such a case in that of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दीर्घतमस्">dīrghatamas</a>,</b> son of the slave girl Uśij, if we may adopt the description of Uśij given in the Bṛhaddevatā.<sup id="ref_120_100_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_100_vei_1202825">100</a></sup></p><p>In a hymn of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_101_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_101_vei_1202825">101</a></sup> extreme claims are put  forward for the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, who alone is a true husband and the real husband, even if the woman has had others, a <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a> or a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>: a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> husband is not mentioned, probably on purpose.<sup id="ref_120_102_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_102_vei_1202825">102</a></sup> The marriage of Brāhmaṇas with <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a> women is illustrated by the cases of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुकन्या">sukanyā</a>,</b> daughter of king <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शर्यात">śaryāta</a>,</b> who married <b><a class="vei_120a" href="च्यवन">cyavana</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_103_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_103_vei_1202825">103</a></sup> and of <b>Rathavīti's</b> daughter, who married <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्यावाश्व">śyāvāśva</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_104_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_104_vei_1202825">104</a></sup></p><p>4. <i>Occupation and Caste.</i>--The Greek authorities<sup id="ref_120_105_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_105_vei_1202825">105</a></sup> and the evidence of the Jātakas<sup id="ref_120_106_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_106_vei_1202825">106</a></sup> concur in showing it to have been the general rule that each caste was confined to its own occupations, but that the Brāhmaṇas did engage in many professions beside that of simple priest, while all castes gave members to the Śramaṇas, or homeless ascetics. The Jātakas<sup id="ref_120_107_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_107_vei_1202825">107</a></sup> recognize the Brahmins as engaged in all sorts of occupations, as merchants, traders, agriculturists, and so forth. Matters are somewhat simpler in Vedic literature, where the Brāhmaṇas and Kṣatriyas appear as practically confined to their own professions of sacrifice and military or administrative functions. Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_108_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_108_vei_1202825">108</a></sup> sees in <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दीर्घश्रवस्">dīrghaśravas</a></b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_109_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_109_vei_1202825">109</a></sup> a Brahmin reduced by indigence to acting as a merchant, as allowed even later by the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> literature; but this is not certain, though it is perfectly possible. More interesting is the question how far the Kṣatriyas practised the duties of priests; the evidence here is conflicting. The best known case is, of course, that of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>.</b> In the Rigveda he appears merely as a priest who is attached to the court of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>,</b> king of the <b>Tṛtsus;</b> but in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_110_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_110_vei_1202825">110</a></sup> he is called a king, a descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जह्नु">jahnu</a>,</b> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_111_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_111_vei_1202825">111</a></sup> refers to <b>Śunaḥśepa's</b>  succeeding, through his adoption by <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>, to the divine lore (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दैव">daiva</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a></i>) of the <b>Gāthins</b> and the lordship of the Jahnus. That in fact this tradition is correct seems most improbable, but it serves at least to illustrate the existence of seers of royal origin. Such figures appear more than once in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_112_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_112_vei_1202825">112</a></sup> which knows the technical terms <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्यर्षि">rājanyarṣi</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="देवराजन्">devarājan</a></b> corresponding to the later Rājarṣi, ‘royal sage.’ The Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_113_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_113_vei_1202825">113</a></sup> says of one who knows a certain doctrine, ‘being a king he becomes a seer’ (<i>rājā sann ṛṣir bhavati</i>), and the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_114_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_114_vei_1202825">114</a></sup> applies the term <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a> to a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>. Again, it is argued that <b><a class="vei_120a" href="देवापि आर्ष्टिषेण">devāpi ārṣṭiṣeṇa</a>,</b> who acted as <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, according to the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_115_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_115_vei_1202825">115</a></sup> for <b>Śantanu,</b> was a prince, as <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a><sup id="ref_120_116_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_116_vei_1202825">116</a></sup> says or implies he was.<sup id="ref_120_117_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_117_vei_1202825">117</a></sup> But this assumption seems to be only an error of Yāska's. Since nothing in the Rigveda alludes to any relationship, it is impossible to accept Sieg's view<sup id="ref_120_118_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_118_vei_1202825">118</a></sup> that the Rigveda recognizes the two as brothers, but presents the fact of a prince acting the part of <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> as unusual and requiring explanation. The principle, however, thus accepted by Sieg as to princes in the Rigveda seems sound enough. Again, Muir<sup id="ref_120_119_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_119_vei_1202825">119</a></sup> has argued that Hindu tradition, as shown in Sāyaṇa,<sup id="ref_120_120_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_120_vei_1202825">120</a></sup> regards many hymns of the Rigveda as composed by royal personages, but he admits that in many cases the ascription is wrong; it may be added that in the case of <b>Pṛthī <a class="vei_120a" href="वैन्य">vainya</a>,</b> where the hymn<sup id="ref_120_121_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_121_vei_1202825">121</a></sup> ascribed to him seems to be his, it is not shown in the hymn itself that he is other than a seer; the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_122_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_122_vei_1202825">122</a></sup> calls him a king, but that is probably of no more value than the later tradition as to <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>. The case of <b>Viśvantara</b>  and the <b>Śyāparṇas</b> mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_123_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_123_vei_1202825">123</a></sup> has been cited<sup id="ref_120_124_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_124_vei_1202825">124</a></sup> as that of a king sacrificing without priestly aid, but the interpretation is quite uncertain, while the parallel of the <b>Kaśyapas, Asitamṛgas,</b> and <b>Bhūtavīras</b> mentioned in the course of the narrative renders it highly probable that the king had other priests to carry out the sacrifice.</p><p>Somewhat different are a series of other cases found in the Upaniṣads, where the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्म">brahma</a> doctrine is ascribed to royal persons. Thus <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a></b> is said in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_125_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_125_vei_1202825">125</a></sup> to have become a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>; <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अजातशत्रु">ajātaśatru</a></b> taught <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गार्ग्य">gārgya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="बालाकि">bālāki</a>;</b><sup id="ref_120_126_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_126_vei_1202825">126</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रवाहण जैवलि">pravāhaṇa jaivali</a></b> instructed <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्वेतकेतु">śvetaketu</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="आरुणेय">āruṇeya</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_127_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_127_vei_1202825">127</a></sup> as well as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शिलक शालावत्य">śilaka śālāvatya</a></b><sup id="ref_120_128_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_128_vei_1202825">128</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चैकितायन">caikitāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दाल्भ्य">dālbhya</a>;</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वपति">aśvapati</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कैकेय">kaikeya</a></b> taught Brahmins.<sup id="ref_120_129_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_129_vei_1202825">129</a></sup> It has been deduced<sup id="ref_120_130_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_130_vei_1202825">130</a></sup> from such passages that the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्म">brahma</a> doctrine was a product of the Kṣatriyas. This conclusion is, however, entirely doubtful,<sup id="ref_120_131_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_131_vei_1202825">131</a></sup> for kings were naturally willing to be flattered by the ascription to them of philosophic activity, and elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_132_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_132_vei_1202825">132</a></sup> the opinion of a <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a> is treated with contempt.</p><p>It is probably a fair deduction that the royal caste did not much concern itself with the sacred lore of the priests, though it is not unlikely that individual exceptions occurred. But that warriors became priests, that an actual change of caste took place, is quite unproved by a single genuine example. That it was impossible we cannot say, but it seems not to have taken place. To be distinguished from a caste change, as Fick<sup id="ref_120_133_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_133_vei_1202825">133</a></sup> points out, is the fact that a member of any caste could, in the later period at least, become a <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रमण">śramaṇa</a>, as is recorded in effect  of many kings in the Epic.<sup id="ref_120_134_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_134_vei_1202825">134</a></sup> Whether the practice is Vedic is not clear: <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a><sup id="ref_120_135_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_135_vei_1202825">135</a></sup> records it of Devāpi, but this is not evidence for times much anterior to the rise of Buddhism.</p><p>On the other hand, the Brahmins, or at least the Purohitas, accompanied the princes in battle, and probably, like the mediæval clergy, were not unprepared to fight,<sup id="ref_120_136_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_136_vei_1202825">136</a></sup> as <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> seem to have done, and as priests do even in the Epic from time to time.<sup id="ref_120_137_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_137_vei_1202825">137</a></sup> But a priest cannot be said to change caste by acting in this way.</p><p>More generally the possibility of the occurrence of change of caste may be seen in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_138_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_138_vei_1202825">138</a></sup> where <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्यापर्ण सायकायन">śyāparṇa sāyakāyana</a></b> is represented as speaking of his offspring as if they could have become the nobles, priests, and commons of the Śalvas; and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_139_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_139_vei_1202825">139</a></sup> where Viśvantara is told that if the wrong offering were made his children would be of the three other castes. A drunken <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_140_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_140_vei_1202825">140</a></sup> talks as if he could be converted into a king. On the other hand, certain kings, such as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर आट्णार">para āṭṇāra</a>,</b> are spoken of as performers of Sattras, ‘sacrificial sessions.’<sup id="ref_120_141_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_141_vei_1202825">141</a></sup> As evidence for caste exchange all this amounts to little; later a Brahmin might become a king, while the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> in the Rigveda is represented as speaking in a state of intoxication; the great kings could be called sacrificers if, for the nonce, they were consecrated (<i>dīkṣita</i>), and so temporarily became Brahmins.<sup id="ref_120_142_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_142_vei_1202825">142</a></sup> The hypothetical passages, too, do not help much. It would be unwise to deny the possibility of caste exchange, but it is not clearly indicated by any record. Even cases like that of <a class="vei_120a" href="सत्यकाम">satyakāma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="जाबाल">jābāla</a> do not go far; for <i>ex hypothesi</i> that teacher  did not know who his father was, and the latter could quite well have been a Brahmin.</p><p>It may therefore be held that the priests and the nobles practised hereditary occupations, and that either class was a closed body into which a man must be born. These two Varṇas may thus be fairly regarded as castes. The Vaiśyas offer more difficulty, for they practised a great variety of occupations (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a></b>). Fick<sup id="ref_120_143_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_143_vei_1202825">143</a></sup> concludes that there is no exact sense in which they can be called a caste, since, in the Buddhist literature, they were divided into various groups, which themselves practised endogamy such as the <i>gahapatis,</i> or smaller landowners, the <i>setthis,</i> or large merchants and members of the various guilds, while there are clear traces<sup id="ref_120_144_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_144_vei_1202825">144</a></sup> in the legal textbooks of a view that <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> stand opposed to all the other members of the community. But we need hardly accept this view for Vedic times, when the <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>, the ordinary freeman of the tribe, formed a class or caste in all probability, which was severed by its free status from the Śūdras, and which was severed by its lack of priestly or noble blood from the two higher classes in the state. It is probably legitimate to hold that any <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> could marry any member of the caste, and that the later divisions within the category of Vaiśyas are growths of divisions parallel with the original process by which priest and noble had grown into separate entities. The process can be seen to day when new tribes fall under the caste system: each class tries to elevate itself in the social scale by refusing to intermarry with inferior classes on equal terms--hypergamy is often allowed--and so those Vaiśyas who acquired wealth in trade (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्रेष्ठिन्">śreṣṭhin</a></b>) or agriculture (the Pāli Gahapatis) would become distinct, as sub-castes, from the ordinary Vaiśyas. But it is not legitimate to regard <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> as a theoretic caste; rather it is an old caste which is in process of dividing into innumerable sub-castes under influences of occupation, religion, or geographical situation.</p><p>Fick<sup id="ref_120_145_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_145_vei_1202825">145</a></sup> denies also that the Śūdras ever formed a single  caste: he regards the term as covering the numerous inferior races and tribes defeated by the Āryan invaders, but originally as denoting only one special tribe. It is reasonable to suppose that <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> was the name given by the Vedic Indians to the nations opposing them, and that these ranked as slaves beside the three castes--nobles, priests, and people--just as in the Anglo-Saxon and early German constitution beside the priests, the <i>nobiles</i> or eorls, and the <i>ingenui,</i> ordinary freemen or ceorls, there was a distinct class of slaves proper; the use of a generic expression to cover them seems natural, whatever its origin (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a></b>). In the Āryan view a marriage of Śūdras could hardly be regulated by rules; any <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> could wed another, if such a marriage could be called a marriage at all, for a slave cannot in early law be deemed to be capable of marriage proper. But what applied in the early Vedic period became no doubt less and less applicable later when many aboriginal tribes and princes must have come into the Āryan community by peaceful means, or by conquest, without loss of personal liberty, and when the term <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> would cover many sorts of people who were not really slaves, but were freemen of a humble character occupied in such functions as supplying the numerous needs of the village, like the <b>Caṇḍālas,</b> or tribes living under Āryan control, or independent, such as the <b>Niṣādas.</b></p><p>But it is also probable that the Śūdras came to include men of Āryan race, and that the Vedic period saw the degradation of Āryans to a lower social status. This seems, at any rate, to have been the case with the <b>Rathakāras.</b> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_146_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_146_vei_1202825">146</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="रथकार">rathakāra</a> is placed as a special class along with the Brāhmaṇas, Rājanyas, and Vaiśyas: this can hardly be interpreted except to mean that the Rathakāras were not included in the Āryan classes, though it is just possible that only a subdivision of the Vaiśyas is meant. There is other evidence<sup id="ref_120_147_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_147_vei_1202825">147</a></sup> that the Rathakāras were regarded as Śūdras. But in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_148_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_148_vei_1202825">148</a></sup> the Rathakāras and the <b>Karmāras</b> appear in a position of importance in connexion with the  selection of the king; these two classes are also referred to in an honourable way in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā;<sup id="ref_120_149_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_149_vei_1202825">149</a></sup> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_150_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_150_vei_1202825">150</a></sup> too, the <a class="vei_120a" href="रथकार">rathakāra</a> is mentioned as a a person of high standing. It is impossible to accept the view suggested by Fick<sup id="ref_120_151_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_151_vei_1202825">151</a></sup> that these classes were originally nonĀryan; we must recognize that the Rathakāras, in early Vedic times esteemed for their skill, later became degraded because of the growth of the feeling that manual labour was not dignified. The development of this idea was a departure from the Āryan conception; it is not unnatural, however undesirable, and has a faint parallel in the class distinctions of modern Europe. Similarly, the <a class="vei_120a" href="कर्मार">karmāra</a>, the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तक्षन्">takṣan</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_152_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_152_vei_1202825">152</a></sup> the Carmamna, or ‘tanner,’ the weaver and others, quite dignified occupations in the Rigveda, are reckoned as Śūdras in the Pāli texts.<sup id="ref_120_153_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_153_vei_1202825">153</a></sup></p><p>The later theory, which appears fully developed in the <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Sūtras,<sup id="ref_120_154_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_154_vei_1202825">154</a></sup> deduces the several castes other than the original four from the intermarriage of the several castes. This theory has no justification in the early Vedic literature. In some cases it is obviously wrong; for example, the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सूत">sūta</a></b> is said to be a caste of this kind, whereas it is perfectly clear that if the Sūtas did form a caste, it was one ultimately due to occupation. But there is no evidence at all that the Sūtas, Grāmaṇīs, and other members of occupations were real castes in the sense that they were endogamic in the early Vedic period. All that we can say is that there was a steady progress by which caste after caste was formed, occupation being an important determining feature, just as in modern times there are castes bearing names like Gopāla (‘cowherd’) <a class="vei_120a" href="कैवर्त">kaivarta</a> or Dhīvara (‘fisherman’), and <a class="vei_120a" href="वणिज्">vaṇij</a> (‘merchant’).<sup id="ref_120_155_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_155_vei_1202825">155</a></sup> </p><p>Fick<sup id="ref_120_156_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_156_vei_1202825">156</a></sup> finds in the Jātakas mention of a number of occupations whose members did not form part of any caste at all, such as the attendants on the court, the actors and dancers who went from village to village, and the wild tribes that lived in the mountains, fishermen, hunters, and so on. In Vedic times these people presumably fell under the conception of <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>, and may have included the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्णक">parṇaka</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पौल्कस">paulkasa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="बैन्द">bainda</a>,</b> who are mentioned with many others in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’). The slaves also, whom Fick<sup id="ref_120_157_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_157_vei_1202825">157</a></sup> includes in the same category, were certainly included in the term <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>.</p><p>5. <i>Origin of the Castes.</i>--The question of the origin of the castes presents some difficulty. The ultimate cause of the extreme rigidity of the caste system, as compared with the features of any other Āryan society, must probably be sought in the sharp distinction drawn from the beginning between the Āryan and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>. The contrast which the Vedic Indians felt as existing between themselves and the conquered population, and which probably rested originally on the difference of colour between the upper and the lower classes, tended to accentuate the natural distinctions of birth, occupation, and locality which normally existed among the Āryan Indians, but which among other Āryan peoples never developed into a caste system like that of India. The doctrine of hypergamy which marks the practical working of the caste system, seems clearly to point to the feeling that the Āryan could marry the <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्रा">śūdrā</a>, but not the <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> the Āryā. This distinction probably lies at the back of all other divisions: its force may be illustrated by the peculiar state of feeling as to mixed marriages, for example, in the Southern States of America and in South Africa, or even in India itself, between the new invaders from Europe and the mingled population which now peoples the country. Marriages between persons of the white and the dark race are disapproved in principle, but varying degrees of condemnation attach to (1) the marriage of a man of the white race with a woman of the dark race;  (2) an informal connexion between these two; (3) a marriage between a woman of the white race and a man of the dark race; and (4) an informal connexion between these two. Each category, on the whole, is subject to more severe reprobation than the preceding one. This race element, it would seem, is what has converted social divisions into castes. There appears, then, to be a large element of truth in the theory, best represented by Risley,<sup id="ref_120_158_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_158_vei_1202825">158</a></sup> which explains caste in the main as a matter of blood, and which holds that the higher the caste is, the greater is the proportion of Āryan blood.</p><p>The chief rival theory is undoubtedly that of Senart,<sup id="ref_120_159_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_159_vei_1202825">159</a></sup> which places the greatest stress on the Āryan constitution of the family. According to Senart the Āryan people practised in affairs of marriage both a rule of exogamy, and one of endogamy. A man must marry a woman of equal birth, but not one of the same <i>gens,</i> according to Roman law as interpreted by Senart and Kovalevsky;<sup id="ref_120_160_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_160_vei_1202825">160</a></sup> and an Athenian must marry an Athenian woman, but not one of the same . In India these rules are reproduced in the form that one must not marry within the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a>,</b> but not without the caste. The theory, though attractively developed, is not convincing; the Latin and Greek parallels are not even probably accurate;<sup id="ref_120_161_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_161_vei_1202825">161</a></sup> and in India the rule forbidding marriage within the <a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a> is one which grows; in strictness as the evidence grows later in date.<sup id="ref_120_162_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_162_vei_1202825">162</a></sup></p><p>On the other hand, it is not necessary to deny that the development of caste may have been helped by the family traditions of some <i>gentes,</i> or , or Gotras. The Patricians of Rome for a long time declined intermarriage with the plebeians; the Athenian Eupatridai seem to have kept their pure from contamination by union with lower blood; and there may well have been noble families among the Vedic Indians who intermarried only among themselves. The  Germans known to Tacitus<sup id="ref_120_163_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_163_vei_1202825">163</a></sup> were divided into <i>nobiles</i> and <i>ingenui,</i> and the Anglo-Saxons into eorls and ceorls, noble and non-noble freemen.<sup id="ref_120_164_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_164_vei_1202825">164</a></sup> The origin of nobility need not be sought in the Vedic period proper, for it may already have existed. It may have been due to the fact that the king, whom we must regard as originally elected by the people, was as king often in close relation with, or regarded as an incarnation of, the deity;<sup id="ref_120_165_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_165_vei_1202825">165</a></sup> and that hereditary kingship would tend to increase the tradition of especially sacred blood: thus the royal family and its offshoots would be anxious to maintain the purity of their blood. In India, beside the sanctity of the king, there was the sanctity of the priest. Here we have in the family exclusiveness of king and nobles, and the similar exclusiveness of a priesthood which was not celibate, influences that make for caste, especially when accompanying the deep opposition between the general folk and the servile aborigines.</p><p>Caste, once created, naturally developed in different directions. Nesfield<sup id="ref_120_166_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_166_vei_1202825">166</a></sup> was inclined to see in occupation the one ground of caste. It is hardly necessary seriously to criticize this view considered as an ultimate explanation of caste, but it is perfectly certain that gilds of workers tend to become castes. The carpenters (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="तक्षन्">takṣan</a></b>), the chariot-makers (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथकार">rathakāra</a></b>), the fishermen (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="धैवर">dhaivara</a></b>) and others are clearly of the type of caste, and the number extends itself as time goes on. But this is not to say that caste is founded on occupation pure and simple in its first origin, or that mere difference of occupation would have produced the system of caste without the interposition of the fundamental difference between Āryan and <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a> or <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> blood and colour. This difference rendered increasingly important what the history of the Āryan peoples shows us to be declining, the distinction between the noble and the non-noble  freemen, a distinction not of course ultimate, but one which seems to have been developed in the Āryan people before the separation of its various branches.</p><p>It is well known that the Iranian polity presents a division of classes comparable in some respects<sup id="ref_120_167_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_167_vei_1202825">167</a></sup> with the Indian polity. The priests (Atharvas) and warriors (Rathaesthas) are unmistakably parallel, and the two lower classes seem to correspond closely to the Pāli Gahapatis, and perhaps to the Śūdras.<sup id="ref_120_168_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_168_vei_1202825">168</a></sup> But they are certainly not castes in the Indian sense of the word. There is no probability in the view of Senart<sup id="ref_120_169_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_169_vei_1202825">169</a></sup> or of Risley<sup id="ref_120_170_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_170_vei_1202825">170</a></sup> that the names of the old classes were later superimposed artificially on a system of castes that were different from them in origin. We cannot say that the castes existed before the classes, and that the classes were borrowed by India from Iran, as Risley maintains, ignoring the early <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> evidence for the four Varṇas, and treating the transfer as late. Nor can we say with Senart that the castes and classes are of independent origin. If there had been no <a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>, caste might never have arisen; both colour and class occupation are needed for a plausible account of the rise of caste.<sup id="ref_120_171_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_171_vei_1202825">171</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>,</i> Rv. ii. 12, 4; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a></i> as against <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दस्यु">dasyu</a>,</i> iii. 34, 9; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a></i> itself opposed to <i><a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>,</i> i. 104, 2. <i>Cf.</i> ii. 3, 5. <i>Cf.</i> a verse in <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 25, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 14. Roth, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 113, reads <i>varṇaśeṣas</i> in Rv. v. 65, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202825">↑</a> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दस्यु">dasyu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>;</b> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 113, 114. There is no trace in Vedic literature of any real distinction of colour save this main one. In the Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 23, the Brahman's colour is white (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शुक्ल">śukla</a></i>); the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 6, calls the <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> ‘white’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शुक्ल">śukla</a></i>), the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a> ‘swarthy’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="धूम्र">dhūmra</a></i>); and the later view makes the four castes black, yellow (<i>pīta</i>), red (<i>rakta</i>), and white respectively. See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 10, 11; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 153, etc., 176. <i>Cf.</i> also Av. iii. 4, 6, where Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 90, with hesitation suggests the reading <i>varṇaiḥ,</i> ‘castes.’</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Catvāro varṇāḥ,</i> ‘four castes,’ Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 4, 9; vi. 4, 4, 13; <i>śaudra <a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>,</i> ‘Śūdra caste,’ <i>ibid.,</i> vi. 4, 4, 9; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 2, 25; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 4. <i>Cf.</i> also <i><a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a></i> opposed to <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxiv. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 17, and see <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 6, 7. <i>Vaṇṇa</i> appears in this sense sometimes in Pāli. See Fick, <i>Die sociale Gliederung,</i> 22, n. 4; Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India,</i> 53.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202825">↑</a> Rv. x. 90, 12 = Av. xix. 6, 6 = Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxi. 11 = <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 12, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Muir, 12, 7-15, and references.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202825">↑</a> Max Müller, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 570 <i>et seq.;</i> Muir, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 3 <i>et seq.;</i> Colebrooke, <i>Essays,</i> 1, 309; Arnold, <i>Vedic Metre,</i> p. 167.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 185-203.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202825">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvii. 1. <i>Cf.</i> Av. xv., and see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात्य">vrātya</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 239 <i>et seq.,</i> especially 258.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202825">↑</a> Rv. viii. 104, 13; x. 109, 3, and <i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202825">↑</a> Rv. i. 108, 7; iv. 50, 8 <i>et seq.;</i> viii. 7, 20; 45, 39; 53, 7; 81, 30; ix. 112, 1; x. 85, 29.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202825">↑</a> Rv. x. 107, 6; 125, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 259.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202825">↑</a> Maitland, <i>Domesday Book,</i> 164 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Zur Litteratur und Geschichte des Weda,</i> 117 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1202825">↑</a> Rv. iii. 33, 8; vii. 18; 83.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1202825">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 10, explaining Rv. x. 98.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1202825">↑</a> Lassen, <i>Indische Alterthumskunde,</i> 1^2, 705 <i>et seq.;</i> Muir, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2^2, 296479.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1202825">↑</a> v. 17-19; Muir, 22, 280-289.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1202825">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi = <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 1-11 = <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 11-16 = Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9. 1-10.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1202825">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 2, 22 <i>et seq.; Indian Literature,</i> 110, 111.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1202825">↑</a> See, <i>e.g.,</i> von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 152 <i>et seq.;</i> Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 159 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> Kaegi, <i>Rigveda,</i> n. 58.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्म">brahma</a> und die Brahmanen,</i> 1871.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Indische Theorien over de Standenverdeeling,</i> 1871. <i>Cf.</i> for this, and the preceding work, Muir, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 22, 454 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Die Nachrichten des Rig und Atharvaveda über Geographie, Geschichte und Verfassung des alten Indien,</i> 36 <i>et seq.;</i> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 237-243, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 373 <i>et seq.,</i> and <i>cf. Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 51, 267 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 146, n.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_1202825">↑</a> iii. and vii.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 218.</li><li id="fx_120_29_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 3, 152.</li><li id="fx_120_30_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_30_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 19, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_31_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_31_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 145.</li><li id="fx_120_32_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_32_vei_1202825">↑</a> Rv. viii. 35, 16-18.</li><li id="fx_120_33_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_33_vei_1202825">↑</a> Rv. i. 113, 6. More doubtful are the references seen by Ludwig to the three castes in ii. 27, 8; vi. 51, 2; vii. 66, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_34_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_34_vei_1202825">↑</a> See Ludwig, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 3, 231 <i>et seq.,</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 94, 95, and see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_35_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_35_vei_1202825">↑</a> iii. 19, 1; ix. 7, 9; xv. 9, 2. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_36_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_36_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 194.</li><li id="fx_120_37_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_37_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 382, 383.</li><li id="fx_120_38_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_38_vei_1202825">↑</a> ii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_39_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_39_vei_1202825">↑</a> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जह्नु">jahnu</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_40_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_40_vei_1202825">↑</a> Rv. x. 90; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 1, 1, 4. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 19, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 4, 12; iii. 1, 1, 10; v. 5, 4, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 1, 6-11.</li><li id="fx_120_41_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_41_vei_1202825">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 2, 27 (Mādhyaṃdina = i. 4, 15 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>); Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 4, 4, 13; xiii. 6, 2, 10; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_42_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_42_vei_1202825">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 10, 1-3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiv. 28-30.</li><li id="fx_120_43_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_43_vei_1202825">↑</a> Av. xix. 32, 8. <i>Cf.</i> 62, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 949, 1003.</li><li id="fx_120_44_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_44_vei_1202825">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_45_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_45_vei_1202825">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 7, 6, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xl. 13; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 48; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 6, 4, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_46_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_46_vei_1202825">↑</a> Av. xix. 62, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxvi. 2. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अर्य">arya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_47_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_47_vei_1202825">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 2, 13, (Mādhyaṃdina = i. 4, 15 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_48_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_48_vei_1202825">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 10, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_49_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_49_vei_1202825">↑</a> Av. v. 17, 9; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 1, 5; 2, 2; iv. 4, 9 (with <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> before <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a>); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 12, 9, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 5, 2. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 8, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_50_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_50_vei_1202825">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x. 10-12; xxxviii. 14; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 4, 11; xi. 2, 7, 15 <i>et seq.;</i> xiv. 2, 2, 30; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, iv. 10, 10-12.</li><li id="fx_120_51_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_51_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Av. v. 18, 15, where the two lower castes are addressed (<a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>) respectively as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नृ-पति">nṛ-pati</a></i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पशु">paśu</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>,</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 252; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 1; xxix. 10; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxviii. 19.</li><li id="fx_120_52_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_52_vei_1202825">↑</a> x. 1, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_53_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_53_vei_1202825">↑</a> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_54_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_54_vei_1202825">↑</a> xiii. 8, 3, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_55_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_55_vei_1202825">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 4, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_56_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_56_vei_1202825">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 1, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 6, 2, 10. For other similar differences in the Brāhmaṇas, see <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 10, 1. 2; vii. 1, 1, 4. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 4; xxxvii. 1; xxxix. 7; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x. 10; xiv. 24; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 23. 24; viii. 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_57_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_57_vei_1202825">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 24, 11. 12, and see Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 20 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_58_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_58_vei_1202825">↑</a> iii. 1, 1, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Āpastamba, cited in scholia on Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, vii. 5, 7; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 8, 7; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 12 <i>et seq.</i> Generally Śūdras are impure, and cannot be allowed at the place of sacrifice (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="देव">deva</a>-yajana</i>), Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 1, 9. <i>Cf.</i> v. 3, 3, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 1, 1, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 10 (Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 4, 8, does not contain this notice).</li><li id="fx_120_59_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_59_vei_1202825">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxi. 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 1, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_60_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_60_vei_1202825">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 4, 9. <i>Cf.</i> also <i>ibid.,</i> i. 1, 4, 12. The scholiast on Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 1, 6, refers these notices to the <a class="vei_120a" href="रथकार">rathakāra</a> alone, but this is obviously secondary.</li><li id="fx_120_61_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_61_vei_1202825">↑</a> i. 1, 4, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_62_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_62_vei_1202825">↑</a> vii. 19, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 4, 6; Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 6; Lévi, <i>La Doctrine du Sacrifice,</i> 81.</li><li id="fx_120_63_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_63_vei_1202825">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 2, 7, 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xvi. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_64_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_64_vei_1202825">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 8, 2; xi. 11, 9; xv. 6, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 33, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxix. 10;  <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 10, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 4, 4, 13, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_65_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_65_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 32, for the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>. The Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> and the later parts of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, with their traditions of Aśvamedhas, ‘horse sacrifices,’ and their recollections of the glories of the <b>Bharatas,</b> represent a more advanced stage of social relations and of city life, but even they hardly know really great kingdoms.</li><li id="fx_120_66_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_66_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Indian Village Community</i> and <i>Village Communities in India,</i> where much stress is laid on the idea of a settlement of Āryans on lands already occupied by Dravidian clans, much as Anglo-Saxon invaders on one theory occupied lands already held by Britons who became serfs, while the invaders were a landholding aristocracy, a theory supported by the fact that the normal holding of a hide is estimated at 120 acres.</li><li id="fx_120_67_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_67_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>India, Old and New,</i> 222. The point is much the same as that at issue between the different schools of opinion as to early English history. Did the Āryans in India occupy the land as a people, driving out or exterminating or enslaving the Dāsas, and themselves carrying on the occupations of a people, or did they merely form a small aristocracy of superior military force, and were the Kṣatriyas the true Āryans? The evidence of the Rigveda is really fatal to the latter alternative hypothesis.</li><li id="fx_120_68_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_68_vei_1202825">↑</a> For the superiority of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> to the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> or <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a>, see <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 11, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxi. 21; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1, 1, 12; 4, 4, 15; xiii. 1, 9, 1; 3, 7, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 15, 8; viii. 9, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 20, 12. The <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> is, in his turn, dependent on the king (Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 3, 3; v. 4, 2, 7), and at the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a> sits beside him, but is none the less superior (Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 2, 23). The <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxviii. 5, says the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्र">kṣatra</a> is over the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>, but this is not a usual view. <i>Cf.</i> xxvii. 4. A <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> can get along without a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>, but not <i>vice versa</i> (Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 4, 6), and a <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a> with a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> surpasses all other Rājanyas (<a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 1, 10, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xix. 10; xxvii. 4, etc.).</li><li id="fx_120_69_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_69_vei_1202825">↑</a> vii. 29. See Muir, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1^2, 436 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_70_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_70_vei_1202825">↑</a> Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 9, 326; 10, 14, prefers ‘moving’ or ‘dwelling’ everywhere.</li><li id="fx_120_71_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_71_vei_1202825">↑</a> Muir, Haug, and Weber take the word as active in sense, ‘moving at will.’ But both the parallelism of the passage and the formation of the word require a passive causative sense. The reference is perhaps to the general political control of the king over the priest, whom he can ‘move on’ from place to place.</li><li id="fx_120_72_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_72_vei_1202825">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 29, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_73_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_73_vei_1202825">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 29, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_74_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_74_vei_1202825">↑</a> This seems to be the most probable reference of <i>yathākāmajyeyaḥ.</i> The expulsion of the <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> is here not in allusion to quasi-ownership of land by the King or <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>; it is an act of royal authority, not an incident of tenure. See Keith, <i>Journal of the African Society,</i> 6, 202 <i>et seq.,</i> and <i>cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>India, Old and New,</i> 222, 223.</li><li id="fx_120_75_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_75_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Ueber den <a class="vei_120a" href="वाजपेय">vājapeya</a>,</i> 10 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_76_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_76_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Ibid. Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 247; <i>Festgruss an Bo7htlingk,</i> 40 <i>et seq.; Rituallitteratur,</i> 141.</li><li id="fx_120_77_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_77_vei_1202825">↑</a> xvi. 17, 4. <i>Cf.</i> xv. 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_78_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_78_vei_1202825">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 6, 2, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 6, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 11, 1; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ix. 9, 19; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41. xxiv. xxv.</li><li id="fx_120_79_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_79_vei_1202825">↑</a> v. 1, 1, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> 2, 1, 19; Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 1, 1-2. Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 8, 9, interprets the situation differently from Eggeling.</li><li id="fx_120_80_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_80_vei_1202825">↑</a> Fick, <i>Dic sociale Gliederung,</i> 107 <i>et seq.;</i> Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India,</i> 53 <i>et seq.;</i> 158.</li><li id="fx_120_81_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_81_vei_1202825">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 984 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_82_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_82_vei_1202825">↑</a> See, <i>e.g.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>, iii. 239; v. 85; Fick, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 26 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_83_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_83_vei_1202825">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वासिष्ठ">vāsiṣṭha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiv. 1 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvii. 17; Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 6, 18, 16 <i>et seq.;</i> ii. 4, 9, 7, with Bühler's note; <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>, iv. 210 <i>et seq.;</i> Viṣṇu, 41, 7 <i>et seq.;</i> Fick, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 30-33, who points out that the Jātakas cohtain little evidence on the practice. Senart, <i>Les Castes dans l'Inde,</i> 48 <i>et seq.,</i> 212 <i>et seq.,</i> attributes great importance to the question of eating together, and compares the sacrificial meals of the <i>gens</i> at Rome, where strangers were excluded (Fustel de Coulanges, <i>La Cité Antique,</i> 117). But this is not conclusive; a caste is not a <i>gens,</i> and the <i>gens</i> excluded strangers only at a solemn festival, when the whole <i>gens</i> renewed its blood kinship. If we have no evidence exactly establishing this for the <a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a> in early Vedic literature, we need not nesitate to believe that in the earliest Vedic period the <a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a> had solemn festivals of union, and of communication with the dead, but that again does not explain or amount to the caste prohibition of taking food from an inferior.</li><li id="fx_120_84_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_84_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, v. 3, 3. with Keith's note.</li><li id="fx_120_85_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_85_vei_1202825">↑</a> For a case of objection to eating food after another, see Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 10, 1. Possibly the idea there is that eating the food of a chief is dangerous, since the eater thus enters into possession of part of his substance, and consequently at once becomes an object of anger to the chief, as well as of danger to himself; for the chief may be so full of divine force that it would be unsafe for an ordinary man to be assimilated to him--a common idea in primitive societies. See also <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, v. 8, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_86_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_86_vei_1202825">↑</a> Fick, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 24. Senart, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 219, 220, compares the family councils of Greece, Rome, and Germany (Leist, <i>Altarisches Jus Civile,</i> 273 <i>et seq.;</i> Kovalevsky, <i>Famille et Propriété Primitives,</i> 119; Fustel de Coulanges, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 118, 119). But here again the system may have applied to the <a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a> without its really explaining the later appearance of the practice in the caste, and the absence of the mention of a council in the early and late literature alike is conclusive against its existence.</li><li id="fx_120_87_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_87_vei_1202825">↑</a> xii. 8. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_88_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_88_vei_1202825">↑</a> Fick, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 34-40.</li><li id="fx_120_89_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_89_vei_1202825">↑</a> x. 5; iii. 15.</li><li id="fx_120_90_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_90_vei_1202825">↑</a> i. 4. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 21, 74.</li><li id="fx_120_91_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_91_vei_1202825">↑</a> Gobhila <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 2, 42.</li><li id="fx_120_92_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_92_vei_1202825">↑</a> See <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 6, 1, 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, vii. 46; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 4, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 3, 4, 19; xii. 4, 4, 6; Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxv. 3, 17; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 1, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, 67, etc. So one of the characteristics of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> given in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 7, 1, is <i>brāhmaṇya,</i> which Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 10, 69, takes as referring to descent. <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्म-पुत्र">brahma-putra</a></i> is a title of honour, Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 4, 1, 2. 9; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 18, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 21, 1. 2; and to be born the son of a wise <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> is the highest fortune, Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 29.</li><li id="fx_120_93_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_93_vei_1202825">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 19, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 3. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 311; 9, 42, 44, 46.</li><li id="fx_120_94_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_94_vei_1202825">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 6. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_95_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_95_vei_1202825">↑</a> vi. 6, 1, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_96_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_96_vei_1202825">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 4; Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 263. <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 4, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_97_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_97_vei_1202825">↑</a> xxx. 1. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 3, 462.</li><li id="fx_120_98_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_98_vei_1202825">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 4, 19, 3. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, iv. 7; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 30. 31. The word <a class="vei_120a" href="अर्य">arya</a> here must refer in all probability to any Āryan, not merely to a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>, Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 10, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_99_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_99_vei_1202825">↑</a> xiv. 11, 17; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 56, n. But there is no mention here of Uśij being a slave.</li><li id="fx_120_100_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_100_vei_1202825">↑</a> iv. 24. 25.</li><li id="fx_120_101_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_101_vei_1202825">↑</a> v. 17, 8. 9. See Muir, 12, 282, n. 76; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 249. The exact sense is not clear, but the passage is intended to show in the strongest light the high position of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_102_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_102_vei_1202825">↑</a> The sense of v. 17, 18, is obscure; it can be interpreted to mean that the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> should be provided with a temporary wife on each occasion when he pays a visit (<i>cf.</i> Whitney, 250). But this is hardly likely. Muir takes it as referring to his own wife.</li><li id="fx_120_103_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_103_vei_1202825">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 5, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 244, 245; Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 10, 73 <i>et seq.;</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 352, 353.</li><li id="fx_120_104_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_104_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Bṛhaddevatā, v. 50 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_105_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_105_vei_1202825">↑</a> Arrian, <i>Indica,</i> xii. 8. 9; Strabo, xv. 4, 49.</li><li id="fx_120_106_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_106_vei_1202825">↑</a> Fick, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 40 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_107_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_107_vei_1202825">↑</a> Rhys Davids, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 54 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_108_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_108_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 3, 237 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_109_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_109_vei_1202825">↑</a> i. 112, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_110_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_110_vei_1202825">↑</a> xxi. 12, 2. See Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 54.</li><li id="fx_120_111_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_111_vei_1202825">↑</a> vii. 18, 9. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 21, where the reading is different, but worse. But see Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 16.</li><li id="fx_120_112_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_112_vei_1202825">↑</a> xii. 12, 6; xviii. 10, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 235, n. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_113_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_113_vei_1202825">↑</a> P. 562 of the manuscript, cited by Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 154, n.</li><li id="fx_120_114_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_114_vei_1202825">↑</a> i. 4, 2. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 17, 6, where <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> is addressed as <a class="vei_120a" href="राजपुत्र">rājaputra</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_115_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_115_vei_1202825">↑</a> x. 98. See Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 196; Senart, <i>Les Castes dans l'Inde,</i> 165; Muir, 12, 269 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_116_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_116_vei_1202825">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_117_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_117_vei_1202825">↑</a> It may be added that a family of Ārṣṭiṣeṇas appear as ritual authorities in a scholium on Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 9, 3; Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 10. 95.</li><li id="fx_120_118_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_118_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 142.</li><li id="fx_120_119_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_119_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 12, 265 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_120_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_120_vei_1202825">↑</a> On Rv. i. 100; iv. 42. 43. 44; v. 27; vi. 15; x. 9. 75. 133. 134. 148. 179, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_121_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_121_vei_1202825">↑</a> x. 148, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_122_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_122_vei_1202825">↑</a> v. 3, 5, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_123_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_123_vei_1202825">↑</a> vii. 27 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_124_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_124_vei_1202825">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 196.</li><li id="fx_120_125_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_125_vei_1202825">↑</a> xi. 6, 2, 10; Muir, 12, 426-430.</li><li id="fx_120_126_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_126_vei_1202825">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 1, 1; Kauṣitaki <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_127_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_127_vei_1202825">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 1 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 2, 1 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>); Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_128_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_128_vei_1202825">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 8, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_129_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_129_vei_1202825">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 6, 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_130_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_130_vei_1202825">↑</a> Deussen, <i>Allgemcine Geschichte der Philosophie,</i> 1, 2, 354; <i>Philosophy of the Upanishads,</i> 17 <i>et seq.;</i> Garbe, <i>Beiträge zur indischen Kulturgeschichte,</i> 1 <i>et seq.; Philosophy of Ancient India,</i> 73 <i>et seq.;</i> Grierson, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 602 <i>et seq.;</i> Winternitz. <i>Geschichte der indischen Litteratur,</i> 1, 256 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_131_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_131_vei_1202825">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>Religion of the <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 218 <i>et seq.;</i> Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 838, 868, 1142; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 50, 51, 257; Oldenberg. <i>Buddha,</i>^5 73, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_132_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_132_vei_1202825">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 1, 4, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_133_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_133_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 44, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_134_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_134_vei_1202825">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 179 <i>et seq.,</i> who treats this as a change of caste.</li><li id="fx_120_135_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_135_vei_1202825">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 10. He went to the forest and practised asceticism, which is not necessarily a change of caste.</li><li id="fx_120_136_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_136_vei_1202825">↑</a> See Rv. iii. 53, 12. 13; i. 129, 4; 152, 7; 157, 2; vii. 83, 4; x. 38; 103, etc; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 220-226; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 135, n. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_137_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_137_vei_1202825">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 13, 184.</li><li id="fx_120_138_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_138_vei_1202825">↑</a> x. 4, 1, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_139_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_139_vei_1202825">↑</a> vii. 29.</li><li id="fx_120_140_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_140_vei_1202825">↑</a> iii. 43, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_141_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_141_vei_1202825">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 16, 3. <i>Cf.</i> for their share in the piling of the sacrificial altar, <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 6, 5, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxii. 3 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 473); Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 10. 25.</li><li id="fx_120_142_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_142_vei_1202825">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 1, 13; Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 10, 17, and <i>cf.</i> the case of <a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a>, Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 6, 2, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_143_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_143_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 19 <i>et seq.;</i> 162 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_144_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_144_vei_1202825">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>The Mutual Relations of the Four Castes according to the Mānavadharmaśāstram,</i> 78, 82 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_145_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_145_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 202 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_146_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_146_vei_1202825">↑</a> i. 1, 4, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_147_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_147_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 1, 9, with the scholiast; iv. 7, 7; 9, 5; Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 10, 12, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_148_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_148_vei_1202825">↑</a> Av. iv. 5, 6. That the words <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कर्मार">karmāra</a></i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="रथकार">rathakāra</a></i> are here appellatives, as Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 17, 198. suggests, is quite impossible.</li><li id="fx_120_149_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_149_vei_1202825">↑</a> xxx. 6. 7. <i>Cf.</i> xiv. 27; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 2, 1 (<a class="vei_120a" href="रथकार">rathakāra</a>); 3, 1 (<a class="vei_120a" href="कर्मार">karmāra</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_150_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_150_vei_1202825">↑</a> xiii. 4, 2, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_151_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_151_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 209, 210.</li><li id="fx_120_152_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_152_vei_1202825">↑</a> The name is applied to <a class="vei_120a" href="बृबु">bṛbu</a> (Rv. vi. 45, 31) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 11, 11. According to Brunnhofer, <i>Iran und Turan,</i> 127, the name is a people's name, but this is very unlikely. See Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 107.</li><li id="fx_120_153_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_153_vei_1202825">↑</a> Fick, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 160, 210.</li><li id="fx_120_154_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_154_vei_1202825">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv; <a class="vei_120a" href="वासिष्ठ">vāsiṣṭha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xviii; <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 16. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_155_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_155_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Jolly, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 50, 507 <i>et seq.;</i> Bühler, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 14, xxxviii, xxxix.</li><li id="fx_120_156_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_156_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 184 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_157_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_157_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 197 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_158_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_158_vei_1202825">↑</a> Best stated and summed up in <i>The Peoples of India.</i> See also the summary in <i>The Indian Empire,</i> 1, chap. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_159_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_159_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Les Castes dans l'Inde.</i></li><li id="fx_120_160_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_160_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Famille et Propriété Primitives,</i> 19, <i>et seq. Cf.</i> L. de la Vallée Poussin, <i>Le Védisme,</i> 15 <i>et seq.,</i> with <i>Le Brahmanisme,</i> 7.</li><li id="fx_120_161_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_161_vei_1202825">↑</a> Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1909, 472.</li><li id="fx_120_162_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_162_vei_1202825">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 74 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_163_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_163_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Germania,</i> 7, 13, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_164_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_164_vei_1202825">↑</a> Medley, <i>English Constitutional History,</i>^2 21 <i>et seq.,</i> and authorities there cited. In the formation of a kingdom minor chiefs, once petty kings, would become nobles.</li><li id="fx_120_165_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_165_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> Frazer, <i>Early History of the Kingship</i> and <i>The Golden Bough</i> (ed. 3), Part 1., <i>The Magic Art and the Evolution of Kings.</i> The traces of this conception in Āryan peoples are clear--<i>e.g.,</i> the <i>rex sacrificulus</i> in Rome, the sacred functions of the Archon Basileus in Athens; <i>cf.</i> Ridgway, <i>Origin of Tragedy,</i> p. 29.</li><li id="fx_120_166_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_166_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Brief View of the Caste System of the North-Western Provinces and Oudh,</i> Allahabad, 1885.</li><li id="fx_120_167_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_167_vei_1202825">↑</a> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 243, 244.</li><li id="fx_120_168_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_168_vei_1202825">↑</a> Senart, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 141.</li><li id="fx_120_169_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_169_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Ibid.</i> 140.</li><li id="fx_120_170_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_170_vei_1202825">↑</a> <i>Indian Empire,</i> 1, 336-348.</li><li id="fx_120_171_vei_1202825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_171_vei_1202825">↑</a> The Indian theories of the origin of caste are merely religious or philosophical, and have no value. See for them, Rv. x. 90 (which is repeated in other Saṃhitās); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 1, 1, 4 <i>et seq.; ibid.,</i> iv. 3, 10, 1-3 = <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 5 = Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiv. 28-30; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 4, 3, 1 <i>et seq.</i> For the origin of the Brahmins, see Av. iv. 6, 1; xv. 9, 1; of the <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a>, Av. xv. 8, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 4, 13, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> Muir, 12, 8 <i>et seq.;</i> Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 217-220.</p><p>The most important collection of texts on caste are those of Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, and of Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, where practically all the data of the Brāhmaṇas are extracted; there have to be added only the data of the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, which are merely confirmatory of those of the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitās, The Epic materials concerning caste are given by Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, who has also analyzed the caste relations of the Mānavadharmaśāstra in <i>The Mutual Relation of the Four Castes according to the Mānavadharmaśāstram. Cf.</i> also Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 212 <i>et seq.;</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 185 <i>et seq.;</i> Senart, <i>Les Castes dans l'Inde;</i> Barth, <i>Revue de l'Histoire des Religions,</i> 1894, 75 <i>et seq.;</i> Jolly, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 50, 507 <i>et seq.;</i> Oldenberg, <i>ibid.,</i> 51, 267-290, a valuable criticism of Senart's views; von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 152 <i>et seq.;</i> 425 <i>et seq.;</i> Schlagintweit, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 33, 549; Shridhar V. Katkar, <i>History of Caste in India.</i> The Jātaka evidence is all collected by Fick, <i>Die sociale Gliederung im nordo7stlichen Indien zu Buddha's Zeit</i> (1897); its value is considerable, but its date is extremely doubtful, and it certainly cannot be regarded as really contemporary with Buddha (fifth century B.C.). The <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Sūtras also give full details, but the¤ir date likewise is uncertain.</li></ol></div>

वर्त|వర్త|ವರ್ತ|varta|_vei_120@2826|_vei2_pn271
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2825">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2827">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn247">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn271">2:271</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn272">↷</a><hr><p><b>Varta.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्त्र">vartra</a>.</b></p></div>

वर्तनि|వర్తని|ವರ್ತನಿ|vartani|_vei_120@2827|_vei2_pn271
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2826">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2828">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn247">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn271">2:271</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn272">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vartani</b> as a part of a chariot seems to denote the ‘felly’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202827"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202827">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202827"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202827">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202827"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202827">↑</a> i. 53, 8; vii. 69, 3; viii. 63, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202827"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202827">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 33, 2; as part of the sacrificial <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> vehicle. <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 4, 9, 5; Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, etc.</li></ol></div>

वर्तिका|వర్తికా|ವರ್ತಿಕಾ|vartikā|vartikaa|_vei_120@2828|_vei2_pn271
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2827">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2829">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn247">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn271">2:271</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn272">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vartikā,</b> a ‘quail,’ is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202828">1</a></sup> as having been saved by the Aśvins from a wolf's jaws. It is also included in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the Yajurvedas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202828">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202828">↑</a> i. 112, 18; 116, 4; 117, 16; 118, 8; x. 39, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202828">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 11, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 20, 30; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 1.</p><p>As to the form of the word, <i>cf.</i> Vārttika on Pāṇini, vii. 3, 45 where it is said to be ‘northern,’ as opposed to the eastern Vartakā. <i>Cf.</i> also Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 45, n.; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 90</li></ol></div>

वर्त्र|వర్త్ర|ವರ್ತ್ರ|vartra|_vei_120@2829|_vei2_pn271
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2828">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2830">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn247">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn271">2:271</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn272">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vartra</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202829"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202829">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202829"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202829">2</a></sup> denote the ‘dam’ of a tank. In the former passage the commentator and some manuscripts have <a class="vei_120a" href="वर्त">varta</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202829"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202829">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202829"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202829">↑</a> i. 3, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202829"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202829">↑</a> i. 6, 8, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202829"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202829">↑</a> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 4.</li></ol></div>

वर्ध्र|వర్ధ్ర|ವರ್ಧ್ರ|vardhra|_vei_120@2830|_vei2_pn271
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2829">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2831">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn247">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn271">2:271</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn272">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vardhra</b> denotes a ‘thong’ or ‘strap’ with which a woven couch is fastened. It is mentioned in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202830"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202830">1</a></sup> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202830"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202830">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202830"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202830">↑</a> xiv. 1, 60, where the Paippalāda recension has <i>varadhrā.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202830"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202830">↑</a> v. 4, 4, 1.</li></ol></div>

वर्मन्|వర్మన్|ವರ್ಮನ್|varman|_vei_120@2831|_vei2_pn271
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2830">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2832">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn247">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn271">2:271</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn272">↷</a><hr><p><b>Varman</b> denotes ‘body armour,’ ‘coat of mail,’ ‘corselet,’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202831">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202831">2</a></sup> Of what material it was made is  uncertain; there are references to sewing (<i>syūta</i>)<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202831">3</a></sup> which may be reckoned in favour of the use of linen corselets such as those recorded by Herodotus,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202831">4</a></sup> but there is a later reference<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202831">5</a></sup> to corselets of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्">ayas</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="लोह">loha</a>,</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रजत">rajata</a>,</b> on which it is doubtful whether much stress can be laid. They may, however, have been either of metal or of leather covered with metal.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202831">↑</a> i. 31, 15; 140, 10; vi. 75, 1. 8. 18. 19; viii. 47, 8; x. 107, 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202831">↑</a> Atharvaveda, viii. 5, 7 <i>et seq.;</i> ix. 5, 26; xvii. 1, 27, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202831">↑</a> Rv. i. 31, 15; x. 101, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202831">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Hehn, <i>Kulturpflanzen,</i>^6 167 <i>et seq.;</i> Lang, <i>Homer and his Age,</i> 150 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202831">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 298; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 222; von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 34.</li></ol></div>

वर्ष|వర్ష|ವರ್ಷ|varṣa|varSha|_vei_120@2832|_vei2_pn272
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2831">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2833">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn271">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn272">2:272</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn273">↷</a><hr><p><b>Varṣa</b> denotes primarily ‘rain,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202832">1</a></sup> then ‘rainy season’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202832">2</a></sup> and ‘year.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202832">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202832">↑</a> Neuter: Rv. v. 58, 7; 83, 10; Av. iii. 27, 6; iv. 15, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202832">↑</a> Feminine plural; Av. vi. 55, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 2, 3; ii. 6, 1, 1; v. 6, 10, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x. 12, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202832">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 17, 5; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 9, 3, 19, etc.</li></ol></div>

वलग|వలగ|ವಲಗ|valaga|_vei_120@2833|_vei2_pn272
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2832">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2834">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn271">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn272">2:272</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn273">↷</a><hr><p><b>Valaga</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202833"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202833">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202833"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202833">2</a></sup> seems to denote a ‘secret spell.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202833"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202833">↑</a> v. 31, 4; x. 1, 18; xix. 9, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202833"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202833">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 3, 2, 1 (where see Sāyaṇa's note); vi. 2, 11, 1. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 11; xxv. 9; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, v. 23; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 5, 4, 2.</li></ol></div>

वल्क|వల్క|ವಲ್ಕ|valka|_vei_120@2834|_vei2_pn272
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2833">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2835">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn271">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn272">2:272</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn273">↷</a><hr><p><b>Valka</b> in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202834"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202834">1</a></sup> denotes ‘bark’ of a tree.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202834"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202834">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 3, 5; iii. 7, 4, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 7. 6.</li></ol></div>

वल्मीक|వల్మీక|ವಲ್ಮೀಕ|valmīka|valmiika|valmeeka|_vei_120@2835|_vei2_pn272
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2834">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2836">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn271">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn272">2:272</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn273">↷</a><hr><p><b>Valmīka</b> denotes an ‘ant-hill’ in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202835">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202835">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202835">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 1, 3, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xix. 2; xxxi. 12; xxxv. 19; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxv. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202835">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 2, 17; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 4, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 3, 4.</li></ol></div>

वल्श|వల్శ|ವಲ್ಶ|valśa|valsha|_vei_120@2836|_vei2_pn272
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2835">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2837">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn271">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn272">2:272</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn273">↷</a><hr><p><b>Valśa</b> denotes ‘twig,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202836"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202836">1</a></sup> usually in the compounds <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शत">śata</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वल्श">valśa</a>,</i> ‘having a hundred twigs,’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202836"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202836">2</a></sup> or <i>sahasra-<a class="vei_120a" href="वल्श">valśa</a>,</i> ‘having a thousand twigs,’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202836"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202836">3</a></sup> which is applied metaphorically of ‘offspring.’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202836"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202836">4</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202836"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202836">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 3, 9, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202836"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202836">↑</a> Rv. iii. 8, 11; Av. vi. 30, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202836"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202836">↑</a> Rv. iii. 8, 11; vii. 33, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202836"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202836">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 3, 5, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 2, etc.</li></ol></div>

वश अश्व्य|वश अश्व्य|వశ అశ్వ్య_౧|వశ అశ్వ్య|ವಶ ಅಶ್ವ್ಯ_೧|ವಶ ಅಶ್ವ್ಯ|vaśa aśvya_1|vaśa aśvya|vasha ashvya_1|vasha ashvya|_vei_120@2837|_vei2_pn273
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2836">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2838">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn272">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn273">2:273</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn274">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Vaśa Aśvya</b> is the name in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202837"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202837">1</a></sup> of a protégé of the Aśvins. He is also mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202837"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202837">2</a></sup> as having received bounty from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पृथुश्रवस्">pṛthuśravas</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कानीत">kānīta</a>.</b> He is the reputed author of a Rigvedic hymn,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202837"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202837">3</a></sup> which is repeatedly referred to by his name <a class="vei_120a" href="वश">vaśa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202837"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202837">4</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्यश्व">vyaśva</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202837"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202837">↑</a> i. 112, 10; 116, 21; viii. 8, 20; 24, 14; 46, 21. 23; 50, 9; x. 40, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202837"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202837">↑</a> xvi. 11, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202837"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202837">↑</a> viii. 46.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202837"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202837">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 6, 2, 3; ix. 3, 3, 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 5, 1, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 10. 11.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 38, 39.</li></ol></div>

वश|वश|వశ_౨|వశ|ವಶ_೨|ವಶ|vaśa_2|vaśa|vasha_2|vasha|_vei_120@2838|_vei2_pn273
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2837">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2839">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn272">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn273">2:273</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn274">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Vaśa,</b> plur., is the name of a tribe mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202838">1</a></sup> as being in <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a></b> along with the <b>Kurus,</b> the <b>Pañcālas,</b> and the <b>Uśīnaras.</b> They are also connected with the <b>Matsyas</b> according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202838">2</a></sup> The Vaśas and Uśīnaras are spoken of as united in the Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>:<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202838">3</a></sup> the names<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202838">4</a></sup> seem to indicate that the Vaśas and Uśīnaras were connected.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202838">↑</a> viii. 14, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202838">↑</a> iv. 1 (reading <i>sa-<a class="vei_120a" href="वश">vaśa</a>-Matsyeṣu</i> for the <i>savasan-Matsyeṣu</i> of the manuscripts, which is otherwise emended to <i>SatvanMatsyeṣu,</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 36, n. 2; <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 367).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202838">↑</a> i. 2, 9, where the text has <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शवस">śavasa</a>-Uśīnareṣu,</i> which is nonsense. <i>Cf. Sa-<a class="vei_120a" href="वश">vaśa</a>-Uśīnarāṇām</i> in <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 14, 3. and n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202838">↑</a> As both derived from the root <i>vaś,</i> ‘desire.’</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 393, n.; 407, n.</li></ol></div>

वशा|వశా|ವಶಾ|vaśā|vashaa|_vei_120@2839|_vei2_pn273
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2838">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2840">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn272">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn273">2:273</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn274">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaśā</b> denotes ‘cow’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202839"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202839">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202839"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202839">2</a></sup> According to the commentators, the word means a ‘barren cow,’ but this is not a necessary sense except in a few passages.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202839"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202839">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202839"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202839">↑</a> ii. 7, 5; vi. 63, 9; x. 91, 14, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202839"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202839">↑</a> Av. iv. 24, 4; x. 10, 2; xii. 4, 1, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 4, 4. 5; iii. 4, 2, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xiii. 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202839"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202839">↑</a> Av. vii. 113, 2, where the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परिवृक्ता">parivṛktā</a>,</b> ‘rejected wife,’ is compared with a <a class="vei_120a" href="वशा">vaśā</a>. In xii. 4 (where <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वशा">vaśā</a></i> alternates with <i>go</i>) there is no indication that <a class="vei_120a" href="वशा">vaśā</a> means a barren cow, except perhaps in verse 16, on which <i>cf.</i> Bloomfield. <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 656, 658. The Brahmins there claim as their own a barren cow. A <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सूत-वशा">sūta-vaśā</a>--i.e.,</i> a cow barren after once calving--is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 5, 4. etc. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 2, 2, and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2. 5, 2, used with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अवि">avi</a>,</b> Sūtā denotes a ‘mother sheep,’ ‘ewe.’</li></ol></div>

वसति|వసతి|ವಸತಿ|vasati|_vei_120@2840|_vei2_pn274
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2839">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2841">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn273">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn274">2:274</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn277">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vasati</b> denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202840"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202840">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202840"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202840">2</a></sup> ‘abode,’ ‘house.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202840"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202840">↑</a> i. 31, 15; v. 2, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202840"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202840">↑</a> Vājasaneyī¤ Saṃhitā, xviii. 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 3, 5, 4; iii. 7, 3, 3, etc.</li></ol></div>

वसन|వసన|ವಸನ|vasana|_vei_120@2841|_vei2_pn274
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2840">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2842">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn273">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn274">2:274</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn277">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vasana</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202841"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202841">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202841"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202841">2</a></sup> denotes ‘dress.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202841"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202841">↑</a> i. 95, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202841"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202841">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, viii. 8, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, viii. 9, etc.</li></ol></div>

वसन्त|వసన్త|ವಸನ್ತ|vasanta|_vei_120@2842|_vei2_pn274
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2841">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2843">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn273">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn274">2:274</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn277">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vasanta,</b> ‘spring,’ is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202842"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202842">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202842"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202842">2</a></sup> It is regularly identified with the first of the months. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋतु">ṛtu</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202842"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202842">↑</a> x. 90, 6; 161, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202842"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202842">↑</a> Av. vi. 55, 2; viii. 2, 22; xii. 1, 36, etc.</li></ol></div>

वसावि|వసావి|ವಸಾವಿ|vasāvi|vasaavi|_vei_120@2843|_vei2_pn274
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2842">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2844">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn273">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn274">2:274</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn277">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vasāvi</b> in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202843"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202843">1</a></sup> denotes, according to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202843"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202843">2</a></sup> a ‘treasure house.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202843"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202843">↑</a> x. 73, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202843"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202843">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

वसिष्ठ|వసిష్ఠ|ವಸಿಷ್ಠ|vasiṣṭha|vasiShTha|_vei_120@2844|_vei2_pn274
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2843">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2845">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn273">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn274">2:274</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn277">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vasiṣṭha</b> is the name of one of the most prominent priestly figures of Vedic tradition. The seventh Maṇḍala of the Rigveda is ascribed to him; this ascription is borne out by the fact that the Vasiṣṭhas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202844">1</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202844">2</a></sup> are frequently mentioned in that Maṇḍala, besides being sometimes referred to elsewhere. That by the name <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> a definite individual is always meant is most improbable, as Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202844">3</a></sup> shows; <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> must normally mean simply ‘a <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>.’ But it is not necessary to deny that a real <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> existed, for one hymn<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202844">4</a></sup> seems to show clear traces of his authorship, and of his assistance to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a></b> against the ten kings.</p><p>The most important feature of Vasiṣṭha's life was apparently  his hostility to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>.</b> The latter was certainly<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202844">5</a></sup> at one time the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a></b> (‘domestic priest’) of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>, but he seems to have been deposed from that post, to have joined Sudās' enemies, and to have taken part in the onslaught of the kings against him, for the hymn of Sudās' triumph<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202844">4</a></sup> has clear references to the ruin <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> brought on his allies.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202844">6</a></sup> Oldenberg,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202844">7</a></sup> however, holds that the strife of <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> is not to be found in the Rigveda. On the other hand, Geldner<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202844">8</a></sup> is hardly right in finding in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202844">9</a></sup> a compressed account indicating the rivalry of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शक्ति">śakti</a>,</b> Vasiṣṭha's son, with <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>, the acquisition by <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> of special skill in speech, and the revenge of <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>, who secured the death of <a class="vei_120a" href="शक्ति">śakti</a> by Sudās' servants, an account which is more fully related by Ṣaḍguruśiṣya,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202844">10</a></sup> which appeared in the Śāṭyāyanaka,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202844">11</a></sup> and to which reference seems to be made in the brief notices of the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202844">12</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202844">13</a></sup> regarding Vasiṣṭha's sons having been slain, and his overcoming the Saudāsas. But it is important to note that no mention is made in these authorities of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a> himself being actually opposed to <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>, while in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1202844">14</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> appears as the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> and consecrator of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पैजवन">paijavana</a>. <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a><sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1202844">15</a></sup> recognizes <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> as the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>; this accords with what seems to have been the fact  that <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> originally held the post. Probably, however, with the disappearance of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> recovered his position, whereupon <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> in revenge for the murder of his sons secured in some way unspecified the defeat of the Saudāsas.<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1202844">16</a></sup></p><p>At any rate it is hardly necessary to suppose that the enmity of the Saudāsas and Vasiṣṭhas was permanent. There is evidence<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1202844">17</a></sup> that the Bharatas had the Vasiṣṭhas as Purohitas, while other versions<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1202844">18</a></sup> regard them as Purohitas for people (<i>prajāḥ</i>) generally. It seems that the Vasiṣṭhas were pioneers in adopting the rule that Purohitas should act as <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> priest<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1202844">19</a></sup> at the sacrifice: the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1202844">20</a></sup> states that the Vasiṣṭhas were once the only priests to act as Brahmans, but that later any priest could serve as such.<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1202844">21</a></sup> A rivalry with Jamadgni and <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> is reported in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1202844">22</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="पराशर">parāśara</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="शतयातु">śatayātu</a> are associated with <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1202844">23</a></sup> being apparently, as Geldner<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1202844">24</a></sup> thinks, the grandson and a son of <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>. According to Pischel,<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1202844">25</a></sup> in another hymn,<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_1202844">26</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> appears as attempting to steal the goods of his father <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a>; Geldner<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_1202844">27</a></sup> also shows that the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_28_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_1202844">28</a></sup> contains a clear reference to Vasiṣṭha's being a son of <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a> and the nymph Urvaśī. Perhaps this explains the fact that the Vasiṣṭhas are called the <b>Tṛtsus</b> in one passage  of the Rigveda;<sup id="ref_120_29_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29_vei_1202844">29</a></sup> for being of miraculous parentage, <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> would need adoption into a <a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a>, that of the princes whom he served, and to whom <a class="vei_120a" href="अगस्त्य">agastya</a> seems to have introduced him.</p><p>There are numerous other references to <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> as a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> in Vedic literature,<sup id="ref_120_30_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_30_vei_1202844">30</a></sup> in the Sūtras,<sup id="ref_120_31_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_31_vei_1202844">31</a></sup> and in the Epic, where he and <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> fight out their rivalry.<sup id="ref_120_32_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_32_vei_1202844">32</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202844">↑</a> Rv. vii. 7, 7; 12, 3; 23, 6; 33, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> 37, 4; 39, 7; 40, 7; 76, 6. 7; 77, 6; 80, 1; 90, 7; 91, 7; x. 15, 8; 66, 14; 122, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202844">↑</a> Rv. vii. 9, 6; 13, 4. 21; 22, 3; 23, 1; 26, 5; 33, 11 <i>et seq.;</i> 42, 6; 59, 3; 70, 6; 73, 3; 86, 5; 88, 1; 95, 6; 96, 1; x. 65, 15; 150, 5; i. 112, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202844">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 204 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> vii. 23, 1 (singular) with verse 6 (plural).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202844">↑</a> Rv. vii. 18. As to vii. 33, Oldenberg and Geldner differ. See <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 130. But it is rather doubtful whether it can possibly be said to be as early as vii. 18, or to have any claim to be really an utterance of <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202844">↑</a> See Rv. iii. 33. 53; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 328 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202844">↑</a> Rv. vii. 18. As to vii. 33, Oldenberg and Geldner differ. See <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 130. But it is rather doubtful whether it can possibly be said to be as early as vii. 18, or to have any claim to be really an utterance of <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202844">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 260 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202844">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 204, n. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202844">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 158 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202844">↑</a> iii. 53, 15. 16. 21-24, the last four verses being the famous <i>Vasiṣṭhadveṣiṇyaḥ,</i> which <a class="vei_120a" href="दुर्ग">durga</a>, the commentator on the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, declines to explain, because he was a Kāpiṣṭhala <a class="vei_120a" href="वासिष्ठ">vāsiṣṭha</a> (see Muir, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 12, 344; Bṛhaddevatā, iv. 117 <i>et seq.,</i> with Macdonell's notes). What the verses really mean is not at all certain. See Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 254 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202844">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Sāyaṇa on Rv. vii. 32, and Macdonell's edition of the Sarvānukramaṇī, 107; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 119.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202844">↑</a> See the note in the Anukramaṇī on vii. 32, where both the Tāṇḍaka and the Śāṭyāyanaka are quoted (Muir, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 12, 328).</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202844">↑</a> vii. 4, 7, 1. In iii. 1, 7, 3; v. 4, 11, 3, also <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> is a foe of <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202844">↑</a> iv. 7, 3; viii. 2, 3; xix. 3, 8; xxi. 11, 2. The story is alluded to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 8, and in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 150; iii. 26. 83. 149. 204. In ii. 390 it is definitely stated, as in the Śāṭyāyanaka (n. 10), that <a class="vei_120a" href="शक्ति">śakti</a> was cast into the fire by the Saudāsas.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1202844">↑</a> vii. 34, 9; viii. 21, 11. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 11, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1202844">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 24; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxvi. 12, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1202844">↑</a> Roth, <i>Zur Litteratur und Geschichte des Weda,</i> 121 <i>et seq.,</i> considered that the Vasiṣṭhas were finally successful in the effort to remove the Viśvāmitras from favour. Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 120; <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 34, doubted this, and Muir, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 12, 371-375, held the problem to be insoluble. Roth and Muir, however, both complicated the question by regarding the Bharatas as enemies of the Tṛtsus, which (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तृत्सु">tṛtsu</a></b>) is not at all probable, though it is still the view of Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, 41, 42.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1202844">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xv. 4, 24; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 34.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1202844">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 5, 2, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1202844">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> was <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> at the sacrifice of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a>,</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 21, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1202844">↑</a> xii. 6, 1, 41. <i>Cf.</i> iv. 6, 6, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1202844">↑</a> Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 35.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1202844">↑</a> iii. 1, 7, 3. <i>Cf.</i> n. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1202844">↑</a> vii. 18, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1202844">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 132.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1202844">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 55 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_1202844">↑</a> vii. 55. Aufrecht, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 337, took the hymn to refer to a lover's visit to a maiden. <i>Cf.</i> Lanman, <i>Sanskrit Reader,</i> 370; Bṛhaddevatā, vi. 11, with Macdonell's notes.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_1202844">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 138. So also <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 13; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 231, n. 97; Bṛhaddevatā, v. 150. 151.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_1202844">↑</a> vii. 33, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_29_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29_vei_1202844">↑</a> vii. 83, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_30_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_30_vei_1202844">↑</a> Rv. i. 112, 9; vii. 88, 4; 96, 3; x. 95, 17; 181, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvi. 19; xx. 9; xxxii. 2 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 478); Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 4, 12; ii. 7, 9; iv. 2, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 2, 10, 5; Av. iv. 29, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 18, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxvi. 14; xxix. 2. 3; xxx. 3; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 13; 15, 2; 18, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 2, 2; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 2, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_31_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_31_vei_1202844">↑</a> See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 89-92; <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 35.</li><li id="fx_120_32_vei_1202844"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_32_vei_1202844">↑</a> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 12, 375-414.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 131 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 31-34; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 31, 37, 53, 79, 123, 162; Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 204-207.</li></ol></div>

वसु|వసు|ವಸು|vasu|_vei_120@2845|_vei2_pn277
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2844">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2846">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn274">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn277">2:277</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn278">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vasu</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202845"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202845">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202845"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202845">2</a></sup> denotes ‘wealth,’ ‘property.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202845"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202845">↑</a> iv. 17, 11. 13; 20, 8; vi. 55, 3; viii. 13, 22, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202845"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202845">↑</a> Av. vii. 115, 2; ix. 4, 3; x. 8, 20; xiv. 2, 8, etc.</li></ol></div>

वसुक्र|వసుక్ర|ವಸುಕ್ರ|vasukra|_vei_120@2846|_vei2_pn277
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2845">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2847">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn274">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn277">2:277</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn278">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vasukra</b> and his wife are the reputed composers of certain hymns of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202846"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202846">1</a></sup> The ascription goes back to the Rigveda Āraṇyakas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202846"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202846">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202846"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202846">↑</a> x. 27-29.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202846"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202846">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 2, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 3.</li></ol></div>

वसु-रोचिस्|वसु-रोचि|वसुरोचिस्|वसुरोचि|वसु|रोचिस्|रोचि|వసు-రోచిస్|వసు-రోచి|వసురోచిస్|వసురోచి|వసు|రోచిస్|రోచి|ವಸು-ರೋಚಿಸ್|ವಸು-ರೋಚಿ|ವಸುರೋಚಿಸ್|ವಸುರೋಚಿ|ವಸು|ರೋಚಿಸ್|ರೋಚಿ|vasu-rocis|vasu-roci|vasurocis|vasuroci|vasu|rocis|roci|vasu-rochis|vasu-rochi|vasurochis|vasurochi|rochis|rochi|_vei_120@2847|_vei2_pn277
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2846">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2848">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn274">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn277">2:277</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn278">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vasu-rocis</b> is a name occurring only once in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202847"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202847">1</a></sup> in a form which may be interpreted as either plural or singular. In the former alternative it denotes a family of singers;<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202847"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202847">2</a></sup> in the latter a patron.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202847"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202847">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202847"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202847">↑</a> viii. 34, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202847"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202847">↑</a> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 162.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202847"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202847">↑</a> Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 2, 175, n.</li></ol></div>

वस्तु|వస్తు|ವಸ್ತು|vastu|_vei_120@2848|_vei2_pn277
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2847">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2849">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn274">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn277">2:277</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn278">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vastu</b> as a designation of time is the ‘early morning’ in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202848"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202848">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202848"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202848">↑</a> i. 79, 6; 104, 1; 179, 1, etc. So Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxviii. 12. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 361.</li></ol></div>

वस्त्र|వస్త్ర|ವಸ್ತ್ರ|vastra|_vei_120@2849|_vei2_pn278
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2848">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2850">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn277">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn278">2:278</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn279">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vastra</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202849">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202849">2</a></sup> denotes ‘dress,’ ‘clothing.’ See <b>Yāsas.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202849">↑</a> i. 26, 1; 134, 4; iii. 39, 2; v. 29, 15, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202849"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202849">↑</a> Av. v. 1, 3; ix. 5. 25; xii. 3, 21, etc.</li></ol></div>

वस्न|వస్న|ವಸ್ನ|vasna|_vei_120@2850|_vei2_pn278
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2849">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2851">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn277">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn278">2:278</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn279">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vasna</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202850"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202850">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202850"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202850">2</a></sup> denotes the ‘price’ paid for anything or its ‘value,’ or the thing itself as an object of purchase, ‘ware.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202850"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202850">↑</a> iv. 24, 9. where the phrase <i>bhūyasā vasnam acarat kanīyaḥ</i> must mean ‘with a greater price he obtained a lesser value.’ For the exact sense, <i>cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 419, 420.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202850"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202850">↑</a> Av. xii. 2, 36 (‘price’) = Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iii. 49 = <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 4, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, ix. 5; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 10, 2, where the sense seems to be ‘let us barter food and drink like wares.’ <i>Cf.</i> also <i>vasnikā,</i> ‘worth a price,’ in <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 3, 13.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 247; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 382.</li></ol></div>

वहतु|వహతు|ವಹತು|vahatu|_vei_120@2851|_vei2_pn278
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2850">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2852">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn277">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn278">2:278</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn279">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vahatu</b> is the regular name in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202851"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202851">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202851"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202851">2</a></sup> for the ceremonial conducting of the bride from the house of her parents to that of her husband.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202851"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202851">↑</a> i. 184, 3; iv. 58, 9; x. 17, 1 (= Av. iii. 31, 5); 32, 3; 85, 13 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202851"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202851">↑</a> Av. x. 1, 1; xiv. 2, 9, 12, 66, 73; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 7, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 1, 2.</li></ol></div>

वह्नि|వహ్ని|ವಹ್ನಿ|vahni|_vei_120@2852|_vei2_pn278
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2851">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2853">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn277">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn278">2:278</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn279">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vahni,</b> ‘carrier,’ denotes any draught animal--<i>e.g.,</i> a ‘horse,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202852"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202852">1</a></sup> a ‘goat,’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202852"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202852">2</a></sup> or an ‘ox.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202852"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202852">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202852"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202852">↑</a> Rv. ii. 24, 13; 37, 3; iii. 6, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202852"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202852">↑</a> Rv. vi. 57, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202852"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202852">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 2, 5, etc.</li></ol></div>

वह्य|వహ్య|ವಹ್ಯ|vahya|_vei_120@2853|_vei2_pn278
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2852">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2854">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn277">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn278">2:278</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn279">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vahya</b> denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202853"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202853">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202853"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202853">2</a></sup> a ‘couch’ or ‘bed’ of a comfortable kind used by women.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202853"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202853">↑</a> vii. 55, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202853"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202853">↑</a> iv. 5, 3; 20, 3; xiv. 2, 30.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 154.</li></ol></div>

वाको-वाक्य|वाकोवाक्य|वाको|वाक्य|వాకో-వాక్య|వాకోవాక్య|వాకో|వాక్య|ವಾಕೋ-ವಾಕ್ಯ|ವಾಕೋವಾಕ್ಯ|ವಾಕೋ|ವಾಕ್ಯ|vāko-vākya|vākovākya|vāko|vākya|vaako-vaakya|vaakovaakya|vaako|vaakya|_vei_120@2854|_vei2_pn278
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2853">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2855">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn277">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn278">2:278</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn279">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāko-vākya,</b> ‘dialogue,’ is the name given in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202854"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202854">1</a></sup> to certain portions of the Vedic texts. In one place<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202854"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202854">2</a></sup> the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मोद्य">brahmodya</a></b> is said to be a dialogue; very probably in all the passages the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मोद्य">brahmodya</a> is meant by this term. Geldner's  view<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202854"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202854">3</a></sup> is different: he sees in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वाकोवाक्य">vākovākya</a> an essential part of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पुराण">purāṇa</a>,</b> the dialogue or dramatic element as opposed to the narrative portion.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202854"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202854">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 6, 9, 20; xi. 5, 6, 8; 7, 5; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vii. 1, 2, 4; 2, 1; 7, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202854"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202854">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 6, 9, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202854"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202854">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 291.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 267; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 98, n. 3. It is certain that ‘logic’ is not meant, though Max Müller so renders it in his translation of the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.</li></ol></div>

वाच्|వాచ్|ವಾಚ್|vāc|vaach|_vei_120@2855|_vei2_pn279
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2854">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2856">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn278">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn279">2:279</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn280">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāc,</b> ‘speech,’ plays a great part in Vedic speculation, but only a few points are of other than mythological significance. Speech is in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202855">1</a></sup> divided into four kinds -that of men, of animals, of birds (<i>vayāṃsi</i>), and of small creeping things (<i>kṣudraṃ sarīsṛpam</i>). The discrimination or making articulate of speech is ascribed to <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> by the Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202855">2</a></sup> The ‘speech’ of the following musical instruments -<b><a class="vei_120a" href="तूणव">tūṇava</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वीणा">vīṇā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="दुन्दुभि">dundubhi</a></b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202855">3</a></sup> --is mentioned, and in one Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202855">4</a></sup> also that of the axle of a chariot. The speech of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas</b> was especially renowned,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202855">5</a></sup> as well as that of the northern country, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202855">6</a></sup> so that men went there to study the language. On the other hand, barbarisms in speech were known, and were to be avoided.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202855">7</a></sup> </p><p>One division of speech referred to<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202855">8</a></sup> is that of the divine (<i>daivī</i>) and the human (<i>mānuṣī</i>), of which some specimens are given, such as <i>om,</i> the divine counterpart of <i>tathā,</i> and so forth. The Brahmin is said to know both;<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202855">9</a></sup> it seems best to regard the distinction not as between Sanskrit and Apabhraṃśa, as Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202855">10</a></sup> suggests, but as between the Sanskrit of the ritual and the hymns and that of ordinary life.</p><p>Reference is also made to Āryan<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202855">11</a></sup> and to Brahmin<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202855">12</a></sup> speech, by which Sanskrit, as opposed to non-Āryan tongues, seems to be meant. The <b>Vrātyas</b> are described as speaking the language of the initiated (<i>dīkṣita-<a class="vei_120a" href="वाच्">vāc</a></i>), though not themselves initiated (<i>a-dīkṣita</i>), but as calling that which is easy to utter (<i>a-durukta</i>), difficult to utter.<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202855">13</a></sup> This may mean that the non-Brahminical Indians were advancing more rapidly than the Brahminical tribes to Prākrit speech, especially if it is legitimate to connect the Vrātyas with the barbarians in speech alluded to in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202855">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202855">↑</a> iv. 1, 3, 16. There are quite different accounts in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xiv. 5; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 11, 5. Oldenberg finds traces of the origin of the legend in Rv. viii. 100; but see v. Schroeder, <i>Mysterium und Mimus,</i> 339 <i>et seq.;</i> Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1911, 993 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202855">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 4, 7, 3; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202855">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 5, 1013; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 4, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 6, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxiii. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202855">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202855">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 3, 15. The difficult phrase has caused some doubt as to the sense, for <i>uttarāhi vāg vadati Kurupañcālatrā</i> seems to mean ‘speech in the north among the KuruPañcālas,’ this version being slightly supported by the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension of the passage quoted by Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, xlii. n. 1. That recension, however, is not merely obscure, but it seems to couple the Kurus with the northern <b>Mahāvṛṣas</b> (so we must emend <i>Mahāviṣeṣu</i>), and it cannot be relied on. Eggeling's attempt to remove the difficulty by taking <i>uttarāhi</i> as ‘higher’ in tone is not satisfactory. The most probable solution is that of Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 191, who takes <i>Kurupañcālatrā</i> to be ‘as among the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas,’ which gives a good sense, especially when it is remembered that the northerners were probably the <b>Uttara-Kurus</b> in Kaśmīr, which seems to have been a home of Sanskrit (<i>cf.</i> Franke, <i>Pāli und Sanskrit,</i> 89).</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202855">↑</a> vii. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202855">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 1, 23. 24, where the Asuras are described as saying <i>he 'lavaḥ,</i> perhaps for <i>he 'rayaḥ</i> But the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> version is different See Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East</i> 26, 31, n. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202855">↑</a> See <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xiv. 5; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 11, 5 (where the words <i>yaś ca <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a> vaś ca na</i> replace the ordinary distinction of <i>daivī</i> and <i>mānuṣī:</i> perhaps <i>vedo</i> should be read); Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 2, 1, 34; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 18, 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 3, 1; a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> in <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, xiii. 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202855">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> etc.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202855">↑</a> See Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 200, n.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202855">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 2, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, viii. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202855">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 5, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202855">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvii. 1, 9.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Lévi, <i>La Doctrine du Sacrifice,</i> 34, 35; Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 175-180; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 179, 180; 196.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202855"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202855">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 1, 23. 24, where the Asuras are described as saying <i>he 'lavaḥ,</i> perhaps for <i>he 'rayaḥ</i> But the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> version is different See Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East</i> 26, 31, n. 3.</li></ol></div>

वाचक्नवी|वाचक्नवि|వాచక్నవీ|వాచక్నవి|ವಾಚಕ್ನವೀ|ವಾಚಕ್ನವಿ|vācaknavī|vācaknavi|vaachaknavii|vaachaknavi|vaachaknavee|_vei_120@2856|_vei2_pn280
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2855">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2857">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn279">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn280">2:280</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn281">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vācaknavī,</b> ‘descendant of Vacaknu,’ is the patronymic of a woman with the further patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गार्गी">gārgī</a>,</b> who appears as a student of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a></b> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202856"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202856">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202856"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202856">↑</a> iii. 6, 1; 8, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 4, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 10; Atharvaveda Parisiṣṭa, xliii. 4, 23.</li></ol></div>

वाज|వాజ|ವಾಜ|vāja|vaaja|_vei_120@2857|_vei2_pn280
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2856">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2858">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn279">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn280">2:280</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn281">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāja</b> from the meaning of ‘strength,’ ‘speed,’ in its application to horses derives the sense of ‘race’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202857"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202857">1</a></sup> and ‘prize,’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202857"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202857">2</a></sup> or  merely ‘prosperity.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202857"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202857">3</a></sup> That it ever means ‘horse’ is most improbable, that sense being given by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वाजिन्">vājin</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202857"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202857">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202857"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202857">↑</a> Rv. ii. 23, 13; iii. 11, 9; 37, 6; 42, 6; v. 35, 1; 86, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202857"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202857">↑</a> Rv. i. 64, 13; ii. 26, 3; 31, 7; iii. 2, 3; viii. 103, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202857"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202857">↑</a> Rv. i. 27, 5; 92, 7; vi. 45, 21. 23, etc.; Av. xiii. 1, 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xviii. 7, 1. 12.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202857"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202857">↑</a> See Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 10 <i>et seq.,</i> where he explains otherwise all the passages cited for the sense by the St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 8.</li></ol></div>

वाजपेय|వాజపేయ|ವಾಜಪೇಯ|vājapeya|vaajapeya|_vei_120@2858|_vei2_pn281
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2857">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2859">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn280">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn281">2:281</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn282">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vājapeya</b> is the name of a ceremony which, according to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202858">1</a></sup> and later authorities,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202858">2</a></sup> is only performed by a Brahmin or a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>. The same <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202858">3</a></sup> insists that this sacrifice is superior to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>,</b> but the consensus of other authorities<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202858">4</a></sup> assigns to it merely the place of a preliminary to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बृहस्पतिसव">bṛhaspatisava</a></b> in the case of a priest, and to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a></b> in the case of a king, while the Śatapatha<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202858">5</a></sup> is compelled to identify the <a class="vei_120a" href="बृहस्पतिसव">bṛhaspatisava</a> with the <a class="vei_120a" href="वाजपेय">vājapeya</a>. The essential ceremony is a chariot race in which the sacrificer is victorious. There is evidence in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202858">6</a></sup> showing that once the festival was one which any Āryan could perform. Hillebrandt,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202858">7</a></sup> indeed, goes so far as to compare it with the Olympic games; but there is hardly much real ground for this: the rite seems to have been developed round a primitive habit of chariot racing, transformed into a ceremony which by sympathetic magic secures the success of the sacrificer. In fact<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202858">8</a></sup> Eggeling seems correct in holding that the <a class="vei_120a" href="वाजपेय">vājapeya</a> was a preliminary rite performed by a Brahmin prior to his formal installation as a <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, or by a king prior to his consecration. The <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वाजपेय">vājapeya</a> was specially well known.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202858">9</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202858">↑</a> v. 1, 5, 2. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202858">↑</a> See Weber, <i>Über den <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>;</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Rituallitteratur,</i> 147 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202858">↑</a> v. 1, 1, 13; Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 1, 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202858">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 6, 2, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 6, 1; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ix. 9, 19; Lāṭyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 11, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202858">↑</a> v. 2, 1, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Kātyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiv. 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202858">↑</a> xv. 1. See Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 41 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202858">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 247.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202858">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, xxiv, xxv.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202858"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202858">↑</a> Sāṅkhāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 3, 14 <i>et seq.;</i> Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xviii. 3, 7.</li></ol></div>

वाज-बन्धु|वाजबन्धु|वाज|बन्धु|వాజ-బన్ధు|వాజబన్ధు|వాజ|బన్ధు|ವಾಜ-ಬನ್ಧು|ವಾಜಬನ್ಧು|ವಾಜ|ಬನ್ಧು|vāja-bandhu|vājabandhu|vāja|bandhu|vaaja-bandhu|vaajabandhu|vaaja|_vei_120@2859|_vei2_pn281
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2858">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2860">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn280">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn281">2:281</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn282">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāja-bandhu</b> in one verse of the Rigveda (viii. 68, 19) may be a proper name. It may, however, merely be an adjective meaning ‘ally in conflict.’ </p></div>

वाज-रत्नायन|वाजरत्नायन|वाज|रत्नायन|వాజ-రత్నాయన|వాజరత్నాయన|వాజ|రత్నాయన|ವಾಜ-ರತ್ನಾಯನ|ವಾಜರತ್ನಾಯನ|ವಾಜ|ರತ್ನಾಯನ|vāja-ratnāyana|vājaratnāyana|vāja|ratnāyana|vaaja-ratnaayana|vaajaratnaayana|vaaja|ratnaayana|_vei_120@2860|_vei2_pn282
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2859">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2861">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn281">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn282">2:282</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn283">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāja-ratnāyana,</b> ‘descendant of Vājaratna,’ is the patronymic of <b>Somaśuṣman</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 21, 5).</p></div>

वाज-श्रवस्|वाज-श्रव|वाजश्रवस्|वाजश्रव|वाज|श्रवस्|श्रव|వాజ-శ్రవస్|వాజ-శ్రవ|వాజశ్రవస్|వాజశ్రవ|వాజ|శ్రవస్|శ్రవ|ವಾಜ-ಶ್ರವಸ್|ವಾಜ-ಶ್ರವ|ವಾಜಶ್ರವಸ್|ವಾಜಶ್ರವ|ವಾಜ|ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಶ್ರವ|vāja-śravas|vāja-śrava|vājaśravas|vājaśrava|vāja|śravas|śrava|vaaja-shravas|vaaja-shrava|vaajashravas|vaajashrava|vaaja|shravas|shrava|_vei_120@2861|_vei2_pn282
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2860">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2862">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn281">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn282">2:282</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn283">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāja-śravas</b> is mentioned in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202861">1</a></sup> as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जिह्वावन्त् बाध्योग">jihvāvant bādhyoga</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202861"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202861">↑</a> vi. 4, 33 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 5, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

वाज-श्रवस|वाजश्रवस|वाज|श्रवस|వాజ-శ్రవస|వాజశ్రవస|వాజ|శ్రవస|ವಾಜ-ಶ್ರವಸ|ವಾಜಶ್ರವಸ|ವಾಜ|ಶ್ರವಸ|vāja-śravasa|vājaśravasa|vāja|śravasa|vaaja-shravasa|vaajashravasa|vaaja|shravasa|_vei_120@2862|_vei2_pn282
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2861">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2863">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn281">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn282">2:282</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn283">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāja-śravasa,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वाजश्रवस्">vājaśravas</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b>Kuśri</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202862">1</a></sup> It is also the patronymic of the father of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नचिकेतस्">naciketas</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202862">2</a></sup> where the name is apparently Uśant, though it is understood by Sāyaṇa as a participle in the sense of ‘desiring.’ The Vājaśravases are in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> said to have been sages.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202862">3</a></sup> They were <b>Gotamas.</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202862">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202862">↑</a> x. 5, 5, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202862">↑</a> iii. 11, 8, 1. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 1, with different names, on which see Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 157, n.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202862">↑</a> i. 3, 10, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202862"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202862">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 11, 8.</li></ol></div>

वाजसनेय|వాజసనేయ|ವಾಜಸನೇಯ|vājasaneya|vaajasaneya|_vei_120@2863|_vei2_pn282
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2862">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2864">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn281">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn282">2:282</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn283">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vājasaneya</b> is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a></b> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202863"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202863">1</a></sup> and the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202863"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202863">2</a></sup> His school, the Vājasaneyins, are mentioned in the Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202863"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202863">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202863"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202863">↑</a> vi. 3, 15; 4, 33 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 3, 7; 5, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202863"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202863">↑</a> ii. 76 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 238).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202863"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202863">↑</a> Anupada <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, vii. 12; viii. 1. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 44, 53, 83, 283; 2, 9; 4, 140, 257, 309; 10, 37, 76, 393, etc.</li></ol></div>

वाजिन्|వాజిన్|ವಾಜಿನ್|vājin|vaajin|_vei_120@2864|_vei2_pn282
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2863">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2865">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn281">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn282">2:282</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn283">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vājin</b> in several passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202864"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202864">1</a></sup> denotes ‘steed’ with reference to its swiftness and strength. In one passage<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202864"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202864">2</a></sup> it is perhaps, as Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202864"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202864">3</a></sup> thinks, a proper name, that of a son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बृहदुक्थ">bṛhaduktha</a>,</b> but this view seems forced.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202864"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202864">↑</a> ii. 5, 1; 10, 1; 34, 7; iii. 53, 23; vi. 75, 6; x. 103, 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202864"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202864">↑</a> x. 56, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202864"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202864">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 133.</li></ol></div>

वाजिन|వాజిన|ವಾಜಿನ|vājina|vaajina|_vei_120@2865|_vei2_pn282
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2864">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2866">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn281">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn282">2:282</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn283">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vājina</b> in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202865"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202865">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202865"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202865">2</a></sup> denotes a mixture of hot fresh milk with sour milk. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202865"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202865">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 3, 10; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 21. 23.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202865"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202865">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 4, 21; iii. 3, 3, 2; ix. 5, 1, 57, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 381, n. 2; Garbe, Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, 3, 445, calls it ‘whey.’</li></ol></div>

वाज्य|వాజ్య|ವಾಜ್ಯ|vājya|vaajya|_vei_120@2866|_vei2_pn283
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2865">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2867">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn282">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn283">2:283</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn284">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vājya,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="वाज">vāja</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="केतु">ketu</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202866">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202866"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202866">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372, 383.</li></ol></div>

वाडेयी-पुत्र|वाडेयीपुत्र|वाडेयी|वाडेयि|पुत्र|వాడేయీ-పుత్ర|వాడేయీపుత్ర|వాడేయీ|వాడేయి|పుత్ర|ವಾಡೇಯೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ವಾಡೇಯೀಪುತ್ರ|ವಾಡೇಯೀ|ವಾಡೇಯಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|vāḍeyī-putra|vāḍeyīputra|vāḍeyī|vāḍeyi|putra|vaaDeyii-putra|vaaDeyiiputra|vaaDeyii|vaaDeyi|vaaDeyee-putra|vaaDeyeeputra|vaaDeyee|_vei_120@2867|_vei2_pn283
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2866">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2868">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn282">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn283">2:283</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn284">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāḍeyī-putra.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बाडेयीपुत्र">bāḍeyīputra</a>.</b></p></div>

वाण|వాణ|ವಾಣ|vāṇa|vaaNa|_vei_120@2868|_vei2_pn283
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2867">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2869">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn282">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn283">2:283</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn284">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāṇa</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202868">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202868">2</a></sup> denotes ‘instrumental music’ according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary; but in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202868">3</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202868">4</a></sup> a ‘harp’ or ‘lyre’ with a hundred strings (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शत">śata</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="तन्तु">tantu</a></i>), used at the Mahāvrata ceremony. The Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202868">5</a></sup> clearly refers to the seven ‘notes’ (<i>dhātu</i>) of the instrument, which are called elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202868">6</a></sup> the seven Vāṇīs, unless the latter expression be taken as referring to the metres.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202868">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202868">↑</a> i. 85, 10; viii. 20, 8; ix. 97, 8; x. 32, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 67.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202868">↑</a> x. 2, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202868">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 9, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxiv. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202868">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 6, 12; xiv. 7, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, v. 1, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202868">↑</a> x. 32, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202868">↑</a> i. 164, 24; iii. 1, 6; 7, 1; ix. 103, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202868"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202868">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> 64.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 289, who thinks the meaning is ‘flute’ in Rv. i. 85, 10, but not necessarily. Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 138, render it ‘voice’ in i. 85, 10; ix. 97, 8, and ‘arrow’ in viii. 20, 8; ix. 50, 1, and this sense is accepted in Bo7htlingk's Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 1 <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वाण">vāṇa</a></i> for ix. 50, 1.</li></ol></div>

वाणिज|వాణిజ|ವಾಣಿಜ|vāṇija|vaaNija|_vei_120@2869|_vei2_pn283
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2868">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2870">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn282">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn283">2:283</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn284">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāṇija</b> denotes a ‘merchant’ as a hereditary profession (‘son of a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वणिज्">vaṇij</a></b>’) in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202869"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202869">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202869"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202869">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 14, 1.</li></ol></div>

वाणी|वाणि|వాణీ|వాణి|ವಾಣೀ|ವಾಣಿ|vāṇī|vāṇi|vaaNii|vaaNi|vaaNee|_vei_120@2870|_vei2_pn283
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2869">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2871">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn282">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn283">2:283</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn284">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāṇī.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वाण">vāṇa</a>.</b></p></div>

वाणीची|वाणीचि|వాణీచీ|వాణీచి|ವಾಣೀಚೀ|ವಾಣೀಚಿ|vāṇīcī|vāṇīci|vaaNiichii|vaaNiichi|vaaNeechee|vaaNeechi|_vei_120@2871|_vei2_pn283
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2870">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2872">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn282">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn283">2:283</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn284">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāṇīcī</b> occurs in a verse of the Rigveda (v. 75, 4), where the St. Petersburg Dictionary ascribes to it the sense of ‘musical instrument.’ </p></div>

वात|వాత|ವಾತ|vāta|vaata|_vei_120@2872|_vei2_pn284
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2871">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2873">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn283">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn284">2:284</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn285">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāta</b> is the regular word for ‘wind’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202872"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202872">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202872"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202872">2</a></sup> Five winds are mentioned.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202872"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202872">3</a></sup> In one passage<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202872"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202872">4</a></sup> Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202872"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202872">5</a></sup> sees a reference to the north-east monsoon. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सलिलवात">salilavāta</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202872"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202872">↑</a> i. 28, 6; ii. 1, 6; 38, 3; iii. 14, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202872"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202872">↑</a> Av. iv. 5, 2; v. 5, 7; xii. 1, 51, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202872"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202872">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6; 1, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxii. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202872"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202872">↑</a> Rv. v. 53, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202872"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202872">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 45, who compares also Rv. x. 137, 2, which refers to two winds.</li></ol></div>

वात-पान|वातपान|वात|पान|వాత-పాన|వాతపాన|వాత|పాన|ವಾತ-ಪಾನ|ವಾತಪಾನ|ವಾತ|ಪಾನ|vāta-pāna|vātapāna|vāta|pāna|vaata-paana|vaatapaana|vaata|paana|_vei_120@2873|_vei2_pn284
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2872">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2874">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn283">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn284">2:284</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn285">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāta-pāna</b> (‘wind guard’) apparently means some sort of garment as protecting against wind in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (vi. 1, 1, 3).</p></div>

वात-रशन|वातरशन|वात|रशन|వాత-రశన|వాతరశన|వాత|రశన|ವಾತ-ರಶನ|ವಾತರಶನ|ವಾತ|ರಶನ|vāta-raśana|vātaraśana|vāta|raśana|vaata-rashana|vaatarashana|vaata|rashana|_vei_120@2874|_vei2_pn284
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2873">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2875">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn283">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn284">2:284</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn285">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāta-raśana,</b> ‘wind-girt,’ is applied to the <b>Munis</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202874"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202874">1</a></sup> and to the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषिस्">ṛṣis</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202874"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202874">2</a></sup> Naked ascetics, such as are known throughout later Indian religious history, are evidently meant</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202874"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202874">↑</a> x. 136, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202874"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202874">↑</a> i. 23, 2; 24, 4; ii. 7, 1. Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 78, was inclined, though without sufficient reason, to take the word as a proper name.</li></ol></div>

वातवन्त्|వాతవన్త్|ವಾತವನ್ತ್|vātavant|vaatavant|_vei_120@2875|_vei2_pn284
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2874">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2876">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn283">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn284">2:284</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn285">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vātavant</b> is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xxv. 3, 6). He and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृति">dṛti</a></b> performed a certain Sattra or sacrificial session, but by stopping at a particular time he came to grief, and his descendants, the Vātavatas, were less prosperous than the <b>Dārteyas.</b></p></div>

वातवत|వాతవత|ವಾತವತ|vātavata|vaatavata|_vei_120@2876|_vei2_pn284
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2875">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2877">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn283">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn284">2:284</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn285">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vātavata,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वातवन्त्">vātavant</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b>Vṛṣaśuṣma</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202876">1</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202876">2</a></sup> has the same form with a variant <a class="vei_120a" href="वाधावत">vādhāvata</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202876">↑</a> v. 29. <i>Cf. Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202876">↑</a> ii. 9.</li></ol></div>

वात्सि|వాత్సి|ವಾತ್ಸಿ|vātsi|vaatsi|_vei_120@2877|_vei2_pn284
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2876">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2878">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn283">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn284">2:284</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn285">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vātsi,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वत्स">vatsa</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सर्पि">sarpi</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vi. 24, 16).</p></div>

वात्सी-पुत्र|वात्सीपुत्र|वात्सी|वात्सि|पुत्र|వాత్సీ-పుత్ర|వాత్సీపుత్ర|వాత్సీ|వాత్సి|పుత్ర|ವಾತ್ಸೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ವಾತ್ಸೀಪುತ್ರ|ವಾತ್ಸೀ|ವಾತ್ಸಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|vātsī-putra|vātsīputra|vātsī|vātsi|putra|vaatsii-putra|vaatsiiputra|vaatsii|vaatsi|vaatsee-putra|vaatseeputra|vaatsee|_vei_120@2878|_vei2_pn284
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2877">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2879">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn283">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn284">2:284</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn285">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vātsī-putra,</b> ‘son of a female descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वत्स">vatsa</a>,</b>’ as the name of a teacher mentioned in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers)  of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पाराशरीपुत्र">pārāśarīputra</a></b> according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension (vi, 5, 2), as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भारद्वाजीपुत्र">bhāradvājīputra</a></b> according to the Mādhyaṃdina (vi. 4, 31).</p></div>

वात्सी-माण्डवी-पुत्र|वात्सी-माण्डवीपुत्र|वात्सी-माण्डवी|वात्सी-माण्डवि|पुत्र|వాత్సీ-మాణ్డవీ-పుత్ర|వాత్సీ-మాణ్డవీపుత్ర|వాత్సీ-మాణ్డవీ|వాత్సీ-మాణ్డవి|పుత్ర|ವಾತ್ಸೀ-ಮಾಣ್ಡವೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ವಾತ್ಸೀ-ಮಾಣ್ಡವೀಪುತ್ರ|ವಾತ್ಸೀ-ಮಾಣ್ಡವೀ|ವಾತ್ಸೀ-ಮಾಣ್ಡವಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|vātsī-māṇḍavī-putra|vātsī-māṇḍavīputra|vātsī-māṇḍavī|vātsī-māṇḍavi|putra|vaatsii-maaNDavii-putra|vaatsii-maaNDaviiputra|vaatsii-maaNDavii|vaatsii-maaNDavi|vaatsee-maaNDavee-putra|vaatsee-maaNDaveeputra|vaatsee-maaNDavee|vaatsee-maaNDavi|_vei_120@2879|_vei2_pn285
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2878">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2880">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn284">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn285">2:285</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn286">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vātsī-<a class="vei_120a" href="माण्डवी">māṇḍavī</a>-putra</b> is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पाराशरीपुत्र">pārāśarīputra</a>,</b> according to the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Mādhyaṃdina recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 4, 30).</p></div>

वात्स्य|వాత్స్య|ವಾತ್ಸ್ಯ|vātsya|vaatsya|_vei_120@2880|_vei2_pn285
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2879">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2881">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn284">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn285">2:285</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn286">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vātsya,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वत्स">vatsa</a>,</b>’ is the name of one or more teachers. One is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202880">1</a></sup> where the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202880">2</a></sup> in the parallel passage has <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बाध्व">bādhva</a>.</b> Others occur in the Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> as pupils of <b>Kuśri,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202880">3</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्डिल्य">śāṇḍilya</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202880">4</a></sup> or another <a class="vei_120a" href="वात्स्य">vātsya</a>,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202880">5</a></sup> while a <a class="vei_120a" href="वात्स्य">vātsya</a> is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202880">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202880">↑</a> viii. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202880">↑</a> iii. 2, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202880">↑</a> vi. 5, 4 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202880">↑</a> ii. 5, 22; iv. 5, 28 (Mādhyaṃdina = ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>); Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 6, 5, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202880">↑</a> ii. 5, 20; iv. 5, 26 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202880">↑</a> ix. 5, 1, 62.</li></ol></div>

वात्स्यायन|వాత్స్యాయన|ವಾತ್ಸ್ಯಾಯನ|vātsyāyana|vaatsyaayana|_vei_120@2881|_vei2_pn285
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2880">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2882">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn284">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn285">2:285</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn286">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vātsyāyana,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वात्स्य">vātsya</a>,</b>’ is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka (i. 7, 2).</p></div>

वादन|వాదన|ವಾದನ|vādana|vaadana|_vei_120@2882|_vei2_pn285
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2881">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2883">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn284">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn285">2:285</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn286">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vādana</b> denotes the plectrum of a harp in the Āraṇyakas of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202882"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202882">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202882"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202882">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 2, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, viii. 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvii. 3, 14, etc.</li></ol></div>

वादित|వాదిత|ವಾದಿತ|vādita|vaadita|_vei_120@2883|_vei2_pn285
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2882">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2884">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn284">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn285">2:285</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn286">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vādita</b> is found denoting ‘music’ in the compound <i>gītavādita,</i> ‘song and music,’ in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (viii. 2, 8), and uncompounded in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xxix. 5) along with <b>Nṛtya,</b> ‘dance,’ and Gīta, ‘song.’ See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शिल्प">śilpa</a>.</b></p></div>

वाधावत|వాధావత|ವಾಧಾವತ|vādhāvata|vaadhaavata|_vei_120@2884|_vei2_pn285
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2883">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2885">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn284">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn285">2:285</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn286">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vādhāvata</b> is a various reading in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202884"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202884">1</a></sup> for <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वातावत">vātāvata</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202884"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202884">↑</a> ii. 9. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 215, n.; 2, 293, n.</li></ol></div>

वाधूय|వాధూయ|ವಾಧೂಯ|vādhūya|vaadhuuya|vaadhooya|_vei_120@2885|_vei2_pn286
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2884">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2886">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn285">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn286">2:286</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn287">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vādhūya</b> denotes the garment of the bride worn at the marriage ceremony and afterwards given to a Brahmin.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202885"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202885">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202885"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202885">↑</a> Rv. x. 85, 34; Av. xiv. 2, 41. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, lxxix. 21; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 8, 12, etc.</li></ol></div>

वाध्र्यश्व|వాధ్ర్యశ్వ|ವಾಧ್ರ್ಯಶ್ವ|vādhryaśva|vaadhryashva|_vei_120@2886|_vei2_pn286
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2885">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2887">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn285">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn286">2:286</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn287">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vādhryaśva,</b> ‘connected with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वध्र्यश्व">vadhryaśva</a>,</b>’ is apparently the epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> in a hymn of the Rigveda (x. 69, 5).</p></div>

वानस्पत्य|వానస్పత్య|ವಾನಸ್ಪತ್ಯ|vānaspatya|vaanaspatya|_vei_120@2887|_vei2_pn286
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2886">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2888">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn285">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn286">2:286</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn287">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vānaspatya</b> (as a masculine) in one or two passages of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202887">1</a></sup> seems to denote a ‘small tree.’ Elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202887">2</a></sup> (as a neuter) it has the sense of the ‘fruit of a tree’ (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="वनस्पति">vanaspati</a></b>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202887">↑</a> viii. 8, 14; xi. 9, 24. <i>Cf.</i> xii. 1, 27.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202887">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 1, 7, 2; 3, 1, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 16, 1.</li></ol></div>

वाम-कक्षायण|वामकक्षायण|वाम|कक्षायण|వామ-కక్షాయణ|వామకక్షాయణ|వామ|కక్షాయణ|ವಾಮ-ಕಕ್ಷಾಯಣ|ವಾಮಕಕ್ಷಾಯಣ|ವಾಮ|ಕಕ್ಷಾಯಣ|vāma-kakṣāyaṇa|vāmakakṣāyaṇa|vāma|kakṣāyaṇa|vaama-kakShaayaNa|vaamakakShaayaNa|vaama|kakShaayaNa|vaama-kaxaayaNa|vaamakaxaayaNa|kaxaayaNa|_vei_120@2888|_vei2_pn286
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2887">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2889">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn285">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn286">2:286</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn287">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāma-kakṣāyaṇa</b> is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वात्स्य">vātsya</a></b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202888"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202888">1</a></sup> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्डिल्य">śāṇḍilya</a></b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202888"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202888">2</a></sup> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202888"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202888">↑</a> x. 6, 5, 9. <i>Cf.</i> vii. 2, 1, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202888"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202888">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 5, 4 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>. <i>Cf.</i> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 4, 1, 11.</li></ol></div>

वाम-देव|वामदेव|वाम|देव|వామ-దేవ|వామదేవ|వామ|దేవ|ವಾಮ-ದೇವ|ವಾಮದೇವ|ವಾಮ|ದೇವ|vāma-deva|vāmadeva|vāma|deva|vaama-deva|vaamadeva|vaama|_vei_120@2889|_vei2_pn286
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2888">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2890">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn285">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn286">2:286</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn287">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāma-deva</b> is credited<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202889">1</a></sup> by tradition with the authorship of the fourth Maṇḍala of the Rigveda, and he is once mentioned in that Maṇḍala.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202889">2</a></sup> He is, moreover, credited with the authorship of the fourth hymn of the Maṇḍala by the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202889">3</a></sup> He there appears as a son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोतम">gotama</a>,</b> while in one hymn of the fourth Maṇḍala of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202889">4</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="गोतम">gotama</a> is mentioned as the father of the singer, and in another<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202889">5</a></sup> the Gotamas occur as praising <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>. In the Bṛhaddevatā<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202889">6</a></sup> two absurd legends are narrated of <a class="vei_120a" href="वामदेव">vāmadeva</a>. One describes <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> as revealing himself in the form of an eagle to the seer as he cooked the entrails of a dog; the other tells of his successful conflict with <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, whom he sold among the seers. Sieg<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202889">7</a></sup> has endeavoured to trace these tales in the  Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202889">8</a></sup> but without any success. Moreover, though <a class="vei_120a" href="वामदेव">vāmadeva</a> is mentioned in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202889">9</a></sup> and often in the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202889">10</a></sup> he never figures there as a hero of these legends.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202889">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 2, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202889">↑</a> iv. 16, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202889">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, x. 5; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 11; iii. 2, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202889">↑</a> iv. 4, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202889">↑</a> iv. 32, 9. 12.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202889">↑</a> iv. 126 131 <i>et seq.,</i> with Macdonell' notes.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202889">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 76 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202889">↑</a> Rv iv. 27 and iv. 24 respectively. On the former hymn, see Oldenberg, <i>Ṛigveda-Noten,</i> 1, 291 <i>et seq.;</i> on the latter, <i>ibid.,</i> 419 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202889">↑</a> See Av. xviii. 3, 15. 16.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202889"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202889">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 30, 2; vi. 18, 1. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 5, 1 (= <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 5, where <a class="vei_120a" href="वामदेव">vāmadeva</a> is credited with knowledge before birth); Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 4, 22 (Mādhyaṃdina = i. 4, 10 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>); <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 9, 27.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 123, 124; Weber, Proceedings of the Berlin Academy, 1894, 789 <i>et seq.;</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 215.</li></ol></div>

वायत|వాయత|ವಾಯತ|vāyata|vaayata|_vei_120@2890|_vei2_pn287
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2889">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2891">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn286">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn287">2:287</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn288">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāyata,</b> ‘descendant of Vayant,’ is the patronymic of <b>Pāśadyumna</b> in the Rigveda (vii. 33, 2). <i>Cf.</i> <b>Vyant.</b></p></div>

वायस|వాయస|ವಾಯಸ|vāyasa|vaayasa|_vei_120@2891|_vei2_pn287
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2890">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2892">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn286">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn287">2:287</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn288">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāyasa</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202891">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202891">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘large bird.’ The sense of ‘crow’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202891">3</a></sup> occurs in the Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> only.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202891">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202891">↑</a> i. 164, 32.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202891">↑</a> In a Vedic citation in <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iv. 17; and in verse 1 of <a class="vei_120a" href="खिल">khila</a> after Rv. v. 51.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202891">↑</a> The only sense of the word in the post-Vedic language.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202891"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202891">↑</a> vi. 8.</li></ol></div>

वायो-विद्यिक|वायोविद्यिक|वायो|विद्यिक|వాయో-విద్యిక|వాయోవిద్యిక|వాయో|విద్యిక|ವಾಯೋ-ವಿದ್ಯಿಕ|ವಾಯೋವಿದ್ಯಿಕ|ವಾಯೋ|ವಿದ್ಯಿಕ|vāyo-vidyika|vāyovidyika|vāyo|vidyika|vaayo-vidyika|vaayovidyika|vaayo|_vei_120@2892|_vei2_pn287
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2891">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2893">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn286">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn287">2:287</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn288">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāyo-vidyika,</b> ‘bird-catcher,’ is found in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202892"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202892">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202892"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202892">↑</a> xiii. 4, 3, 13. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 369, n. 5.</li></ol></div>

वाय्य|వాయ్య|ವಾಯ್ಯ|vāyya|vaayya|_vei_120@2893|_vei2_pn287
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2892">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2894">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn286">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn287">2:287</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn288">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāyya,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वय्य">vayya</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सत्यश्रवस्">satyaśravas</a></b> in the Rigveda (v. 79, 1. 2).</p></div>

वार्|వార్|ವಾರ್|vār|vaar|_vei_120@2894|_vei2_pn287
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2893">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2895">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn286">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn287">2:287</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn288">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vār</b> is found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202894">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202894">2</a></sup> denoting ‘water.’ In some passages<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202894">3</a></sup> ‘stagnant water,’ ‘pond,’ is meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202894">↑</a> i. 116, 22; ii. 4, 6; x. 12, 3; 99, 4; 105, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202894">↑</a> Av. iii. 13, 8; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 1, 1, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202894">↑</a> Rv. iv. 19, 4; viii. 98, 8; ix. 112, 4.</li></ol></div>

वारकि|వారకి|ವಾರಕಿ|vāraki|vaaraki|_vei_120@2895|_vei2_pn287
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2894">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2896">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn286">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn287">2:287</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn288">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāraki,</b> ‘descendant of Varaka,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कंस">kaṃsa</a></b> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 41, 1). </p></div>

वारक्य|వారక్య|ವಾರಕ್ಯ|vārakya|vaarakya|_vei_120@2896|_vei2_pn288
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2895">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2897">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn287">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn288">2:288</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn289">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vārakya,</b> ‘descendant of Varaka,’ is the patronymic, in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कंस">kaṃsa</a>, Kubera, <a class="vei_120a" href="जनश्रुत">janaśruta</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="जयन्त">jayanta</a>,</b> and <b>Proṣṭhapad.</b></p></div>

वारण|వారణ|ವಾರಣ|vāraṇa|vaaraNa|_vei_120@2897|_vei2_pn288
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2896">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2898">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn287">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn288">2:288</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn289">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāraṇa</b> in two passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202897"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202897">1</a></sup> is taken by Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202897"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202897">2</a></sup> as an adjective with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मृग">mṛga</a>,</b> meaning ‘wild beast.’ But the sense intended must have been ‘elephant,’ the usual sense of <a class="vei_120a" href="वारण">vāraṇa</a> in the classical literature. Probably the feminine Vāraṇī in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202897"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202897">3</a></sup> likewise denotes a ‘female elephant.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202897"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202897">↑</a> viii. 33, 8; x. 40, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202897"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202897">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 1<i>c.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202897"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202897">↑</a> v. 14, 11.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Pischel and Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, xv. 100-102; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 296; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 467; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 80.</li></ol></div>

वारुणि|వారుణి|ವಾರುಣಿ|vāruṇi|vaaruNi|_vei_120@2898|_vei2_pn288
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2897">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2899">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn287">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn288">2:288</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn289">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāruṇi,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भृगु">bhṛgu</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202898">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202898"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202898">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 34, 1; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 6, 1, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

वार्कलि|వార్కలి|ವಾರ್ಕಲಿ|vārkali|vaarkali|_vei_120@2899|_vei2_pn288
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2898">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2900">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn287">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn288">2:288</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn289">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vārkali,</b> ‘descendant of Vṛkalā,’ is the metronymic of a teacher in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202899"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202899">1</a></sup> The name in the form of Vārkalin has been seen in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202899"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202899">2</a></sup> but wrongly.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202899"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202899">↑</a> xii. 3, 2, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202899"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202899">↑</a> iii. 2, 2, and Keith's note; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, viii. 2. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 33, 123, who thinks <a class="vei_120a" href="वार्कलि">vārkali</a> is equivalent to Vāṣkali.</li></ol></div>

वार्कारुणी-पुत्र|वार्कारुणीपुत्र|वार्कारुणी|वार्कारुणि|पुत्र|వార్కారుణీ-పుత్ర|వార్కారుణీపుత్ర|వార్కారుణీ|వార్కారుణి|పుత్ర|ವಾರ್ಕಾರುಣೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ವಾರ್ಕಾರುಣೀಪುತ್ರ|ವಾರ್ಕಾರುಣೀ|ವಾರ್ಕಾರುಣಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|vārkāruṇī-putra|vārkāruṇīputra|vārkāruṇī|vārkāruṇi|putra|vaarkaaruNii-putra|vaarkaaruNiiputra|vaarkaaruNii|vaarkaaruNi|vaarkaaruNee-putra|vaarkaaruNeeputra|vaarkaaruNee|_vei_120@2900|_vei2_pn288
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2899">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2901">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn287">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn288">2:288</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn289">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vārkāruṇī-putra</b> is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आर्तभागीपुत्र">ārtabhāgīputra</a>,</b> in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202900"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202900">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202900"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202900">↑</a> vi. 4, 31 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 5, 2 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>, where also <a class="vei_120a" href="वार्कारुणीपुत्र">vārkāruṇīputra</a> is duplicated, one being the pupil of the other).</li></ol></div>

वार्ध्रा-णस|वार्ध्राणस|वार्ध्रा|णस|వార్ధ్రా-ణస|వార్ధ్రాణస|వార్ధ్రా|ణస|ವಾರ್ಧ್ರಾ-ಣಸ|ವಾರ್ಧ್ರಾಣಸ|ವಾರ್ಧ್ರಾ|ಣಸ|vārdhrā-ṇasa|vārdhrāṇasa|vārdhrā|ṇasa|vaardhraa-Nasa|vaardhraaNasa|vaardhraa|Nasa|_vei_120@2901|_vei2_pn288
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2900">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2902">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn287">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn288">2:288</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn289">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vārdhrā-ṇasa,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202901"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202901">1</a></sup> <b>Vārdhrī-<a class="vei_120a" href="नस">nasa</a></b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202901"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202901">2</a></sup> is the name of an animal in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the  <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās. The meaning seems to be, as taken by Sāyaṇa,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202901"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202901">3</a></sup> ‘rhinoceros.’ Bo7htlingk<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202901"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202901">4</a></sup> quotes as other interpretations ‘an old white he-goat’ or ‘a kind of crane.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202901"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202901">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 20, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202901"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202901">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 39 (Prātiśākhya, iii. 89; vi. 28).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202901"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202901">↑</a> On <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202901"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202901">↑</a> Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 80.</li></ol></div>

वार्ष-गण|वार्षगण|वार्ष|गण|వార్ష-గణ|వార్షగణ|వార్ష|గణ|ವಾರ್ಷ-ಗಣ|ವಾರ್ಷಗಣ|ವಾರ್ಷ|ಗಣ|vārṣa-gaṇa|vārṣagaṇa|vārṣa|gaṇa|vaarSha-gaNa|vaarShagaNa|vaarSha|gaNa|_vei_120@2902|_vei2_pn289
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2901">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2903">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn288">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn289">2:289</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn290">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vārṣa-gaṇa,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="वृषगण">vṛṣagaṇa</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="असित">asita</a></b> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202902"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202902">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202902"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202902">↑</a> vi. 4, 33 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 5, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

वार्षगणी-पुत्र|वार्षगणीपुत्र|वार्षगणी|वार्षगणि|पुत्र|వార్షగణీ-పుత్ర|వార్షగణీపుత్ర|వార్షగణీ|వార్షగణి|పుత్ర|ವಾರ್ಷಗಣೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ವಾರ್ಷಗಣೀಪುತ್ರ|ವಾರ್ಷಗಣೀ|ವಾರ್ಷಗಣಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|vārṣagaṇī-putra|vārṣagaṇīputra|vārṣagaṇī|vārṣagaṇi|putra|vaarShagaNii-putra|vaarShagaNiiputra|vaarShagaNii|vaarShagaNi|vaarShagaNee-putra|vaarShagaNeeputra|vaarShagaNee|_vei_120@2903|_vei2_pn289
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2902">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2904">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn288">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn289">2:289</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn290">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vārṣagaṇī-putra,</b> ‘son of a female descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="वृषगण">vṛṣagaṇa</a>,’ is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौतमी-पुत्र">gautamī-putra</a></b> in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Mādhyaṃdina recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 4, 31).</p></div>

वार्ष-गण्य|वार्षगण्य|वार्ष|गण्य|వార్ష-గణ్య|వార్షగణ్య|వార్ష|గణ్య|ವಾರ್ಷ-ಗಣ್ಯ|ವಾರ್ಷಗಣ್ಯ|ವಾರ್ಷ|ಗಣ್ಯ|vārṣa-gaṇya|vārṣagaṇya|vārṣa|gaṇya|vaarSha-gaNya|vaarShagaNya|vaarSha|gaNya|_vei_120@2904|_vei2_pn289
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2903">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2905">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn288">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn289">2:289</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn290">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vārṣa-gaṇya,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="वृषगण">vṛṣagaṇa</a>,’ is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202904"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202904">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202904"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202904">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372; <a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 9; vi. 7, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Garbe. <i>Sāṃkhya Philosophie,</i> 36.</li></ol></div>

वार्षा-गिर|वार्षागिर|वार्षा|गिर|వార్షా-గిర|వార్షాగిర|వార్షా|గిర|ವಾರ್ಷಾ-ಗಿರ|ವಾರ್ಷಾಗಿರ|ವಾರ್ಷಾ|ಗಿರ|vārṣā-gira|vārṣāgira|vārṣā|gira|vaarShaa-gira|vaarShaagira|vaarShaa|_vei_120@2905|_vei2_pn289
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2904">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2906">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn288">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn289">2:289</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn290">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vārṣā-gira,</b> ‘descendant of Vṛṣāgir,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अम्बरीष">ambarīṣa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋज्राश्व">ṛjrāśva</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="भयमान">bhayamāna</a>. <a class="vei_120a" href="सहदेव">sahadeva</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुराधस्">surādhas</a>,</b> in the Rigveda (i. 100, 17).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 113.</p></div>

वार्ष्ण|వార్ష్ణ|ವಾರ್ಷ್ಣ|vārṣṇa|vaarShNa|_vei_120@2906|_vei2_pn289
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2905">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2907">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn288">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn289">2:289</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn290">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vārṣṇa,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="वृषन्">vṛṣan</a> or Vṛṣṇi or <a class="vei_120a" href="वृष्ण">vṛṣṇa</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोबल">gobala</a></b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202906"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202906">1</a></sup> and <b>Barku,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202906"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202906">2</a></sup> and of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐक्ष्वाक">aikṣvāka</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202906"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202906">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202906"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202906">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 11, 9, 3; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202906"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202906">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 1, 10; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1, 8, where the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension (iv. 1, 4) has a <i>varia lectio</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वार्ष्म">vārṣma</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202906"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202906">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 4.</li></ol></div>

वार्ष्णि-वृद्ध|वार्ष्णिवृद्ध|वार्ष्णि|वृद्ध|వార్ష్ణి-వృద్ధ|వార్ష్ణివృద్ధ|వార్ష్ణి|వృద్ధ|ವಾರ್ಷ್ಣಿ-ವೃದ್ಧ|ವಾರ್ಷ್ಣಿವೃದ್ಧ|ವಾರ್ಷ್ಣಿ|ವೃದ್ಧ|vārṣṇi-vṛddha|vārṣṇivṛddha|vārṣṇi|vṛddha|vaarShNi-vRddha|vaarShNivRddha|vaarShNi|vRddha|vaarShNi-vRiddha|vaarShNivRiddha|vRiddha|vaarShNi-vRuddha|vaarShNivRuddha|vRuddha|vaarShNi-vRRiddha|vaarShNivRRiddha|vRRiddha|_vei_120@2907|_vei2_pn289
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2906">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2908">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn288">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn289">2:289</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn290">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vārṣṇi-vṛddha,</b> ‘descendant of Vṛṣṇivṛddha,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उल">ula</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vii. 4).</p></div>

वार्ष्णेय|వార్ష్ణేయ|ವಾರ್ಷ್ಣೇಯ|vārṣṇeya|vaarShNeya|_vei_120@2908|_vei2_pn289
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2907">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2909">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn288">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn289">2:289</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn290">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vārṣṇeya,</b> ‘descendant of Vṛṣṇi,’ is the patronymic of <b>Śūṣa</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 10, 9, 15). </p></div>

वार्ष्ण्य|వార్ష్ణ్య|ವಾರ್ಷ್ಣ್ಯ|vārṣṇya|vaarShNya|_vei_120@2909|_vei2_pn290
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2908">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2910">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn289">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn290">2:290</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn291">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vārṣṇya,</b> ‘descendant of Vṛṣṇi, is the patronymic of a man in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202909"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202909">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202909"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202909">↑</a> iii. 1, 1, 4. The <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension omits the name. See Eggeling <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 2, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

वार्ष्म|వార్ష్మ|ವಾರ್ಷ್ಮ|vārṣma|vaarShma|_vei_120@2910|_vei2_pn290
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2909">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2911">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn289">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn290">2:290</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn291">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vārṣma.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वार्ष्ण">vārṣṇa</a>.</b></p></div>

वाल|వాల|ವಾಲ|vāla|vaala|_vei_120@2911|_vei2_pn290
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2910">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2912">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn289">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn290">2:290</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn291">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāla</b> denotes a ‘hair sieve’ in the later Saṃhitās and he Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202911"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202911">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202911"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202911">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 88; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 7, 3, 11; 8, 1. 14, etc.</li></ol></div>

वाल-खिल्य|वालखिल्य|वाल|खिल्य|వాల-ఖిల్య|వాలఖిల్య|వాల|ఖిల్య|ವಾಲ-ಖಿಲ್ಯ|ವಾಲಖಿಲ್ಯ|ವಾಲ|ಖಿಲ್ಯ|vāla-khilya|vālakhilya|vāla|khilya|vaala-khilya|vaalakhilya|vaala|_vei_120@2912|_vei2_pn290
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2911">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2913">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn289">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn290">2:290</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn291">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāla-khilya</b> is the term applied in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202912"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202912">1</a></sup> to the supplementary hymns inserted after Rigveda viii. 48. The <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषिस्">ṛṣis</a> of these hymns are so named in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202912"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202912">2</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> 2. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="खिल">khila</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202912"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202912">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 15, 1, 3, 4; vi. 24, 1. 4. 5. 10. 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxx. 4. 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 11, 3; xiv. 5, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, v. 2, 4, etc.; Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202912"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202912">↑</a> i. 23.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 220; <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, xlvi <i>et seq.;</i> Bṛhaddevatā, vi. 84 <i>et seq.,</i> with Macdonell's notes; Scheftelowitz, <i>Die Apokryphen des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 35 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

वाल-दामन्|वालदामन्|वाल|दामन्|వాల-దామన్|వాలదామన్|వాల|దామన్|ವಾಲ-ದಾಮನ್|ವಾಲದಾಮನ್|ವಾಲ|ದಾಮನ್|vāla-dāman|vāladāman|vāla|dāman|vaala-daaman|vaaladaaman|vaala|daaman|_vei_120@2913|_vei2_pn290
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2912">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2914">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn289">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn290">2:290</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn291">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāla-dāman</b> denotes a ‘horse-hair strap’ in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (v. 3, 1, 10).</p></div>

वालिशिखायनि|వాలిశిఖాయని|ವಾಲಿಶಿಖಾಯನಿ|vāliśikhāyani|vaalishikhaayani|_vei_120@2914|_vei2_pn290
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2913">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2915">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn289">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn290">2:290</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn291">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāliśikhāyani</b> is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202914"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202914">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202914"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202914">↑</a> vii. 21. <i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 49, n. 5.</li></ol></div>

वावाता|వావాతా|ವಾವಾತಾ|vāvātā|vaavaataa|_vei_120@2915|_vei2_pn290
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2914">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2916">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn289">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn290">2:290</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn291">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāvātā</b> is in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202915"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202915">1</a></sup> the name of the king's ‘favourite’ wife. inferior to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="महिषी">mahiṣī</a></b> only. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202915"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202915">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 22, 1. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 3, 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 2, 15 4, 1, 8; 5, 2, 6, etc.</li></ol></div>

वाशिता|వాశితా|ವಾಶಿತಾ|vāśitā|vaashitaa|_vei_120@2916|_vei2_pn291
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2915">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2917">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn290">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn291">2:291</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn292">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāśitā</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202916">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202916">2</a></sup> denotes a cow desiring the bull.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202916">↑</a> v. 20, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202916"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202916">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xiii. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 9, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 18, 10; 21, 14, etc.</li></ol></div>

वाशी|वाशि|వాశీ|వాశి|ವಾಶೀ|ವಾಶಿ|vāśī|vāśi|vaashii|vaashi|vaashee|_vei_120@2917|_vei2_pn291
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2916">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2918">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn290">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn291">2:291</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn292">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāśī</b> is mentioned in the Rigveda both as a weapon of the Maruts<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202917"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202917">1</a></sup> and as held by the god <a class="vei_120a" href="त्वष्टृ">tvaṣṭṛ</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202917"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202917">2</a></sup> as well as in other mythical surroundings.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202917"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202917">3</a></sup> It is used, however, in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202917"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202917">4</a></sup> of the carpenter's knife; here it may mean ‘awl,’ in accordance with Sāyaṇa's view.</p><p>5 Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 301.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202917"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202917">↑</a> i. 37, 2; 88, 3; v. 53, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202917"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202917">↑</a> viii. 29, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202917"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202917">↑</a> viii. 12, 12; x. 53, 10; 101, 10 (of the stones with which the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> plant is manipulated), all doubtful passages.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202917"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202917">↑</a> x. 6, 3 (where the manuscripts all have <i>vāsyā:</i> perhaps this is really a different word).</li></ol></div>

वासः-पल्पूली|वासः-पल्पूलि|वासःपल्पूली|वासःपल्पूलि|वासः|पल्पूली|पल्पूलि|వాసః-పల్పూలీ|వాసః-పల్పూలి|వాసఃపల్పూలీ|వాసఃపల్పూలి|వాసః|పల్పూలీ|పల్పూలి|ವಾಸಃ-ಪಲ್ಪೂಲೀ|ವಾಸಃ-ಪಲ್ಪೂಲಿ|ವಾಸಃಪಲ್ಪೂಲೀ|ವಾಸಃಪಲ್ಪೂಲಿ|ವಾಸಃ|ಪಲ್ಪೂಲೀ|ಪಲ್ಪೂಲಿ|vāsaḥ-palpūlī|vāsaḥ-palpūli|vāsaḥpalpūlī|vāsaḥpalpūli|vāsaḥ|palpūlī|palpūli|vaasaH-palpuulii|vaasaH-palpuuli|vaasaHpalpuulii|vaasaHpalpuuli|vaasaH|palpuulii|palpuuli|vaasaH-palpoolee|vaasaH-palpooli|vaasaHpalpoolee|vaasaHpalpooli|palpoolee|palpooli|_vei_120@2918|_vei2_pn291
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2917">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2919">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn290">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn291">2:291</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn292">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāsaḥ-palpūlī,</b> ‘washer of clothes,’ is the name of one of the victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202918"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202918">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202918"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202918">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 7, 1.</li></ol></div>

वासस्|वास|వాసస్|వాస|ವಾಸಸ್|ವಾಸ|vāsas|vāsa|vaasas|vaasa|_vei_120@2919|_vei2_pn291
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2918">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2920">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn290">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn291">2:291</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn292">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāsas</b> is the most usual word in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202919">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202919">2</a></sup> for ‘clothing.’ Clothes were often woven of sheep's wool (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऊर्णा">ūrṇā</a></b>); the god Pūṣan is called a ‘weaver of garments’ (<i>vāsevāya</i>)<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202919">3</a></sup> because of his connexion with the fashioning of forms. The garments worn were often embroidered (<i>cf.</i> <b>Peśas</b>), and the Maruts are described as wearing mantles adorned with gold.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202919">4</a></sup> When the ‘giver of garments’ (<i>vāso-dā</i>)<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202919">5</a></sup> is mentioned along with the giver of horses and gold, ornamental garments are probably meant. There are several references in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202919">6</a></sup> to the Indians' love of ornament, which is attested by Megas thenes for his day.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202919">7</a></sup> The Rigveda also presents epithets like <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सु-वसन">su-vasana</a></i><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202919">8</a></sup> and <i>su-<a class="vei_120a" href="रभि">rabhi</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202919">9</a></sup> implying that garments were becoming or well-fitting.</p><p>The Vedic Indian seems often to have worn three garments -an undergarment (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नीवि">nīvi</a></b>),<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202919">10</a></sup> a garment,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202919">11</a></sup> and an overgarment (<i>cf.</i> <b>Adhīvāsa</b>),<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202919">12</a></sup> which was presumably a mantle, and for which the names <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अत्क">atka</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="द्रापि">drāpi</a></b> also seem to be used. This accords with the description of the sacrificial garments given in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202919">13</a></sup> which comprise a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तार्प्य">tārpya</a>,</b> perhaps a ‘silken undergarment’; secondly, a garment of undyed wool, and then a mantle, while the ends of the turban, after being tied behind the neck, are brought forward and tucked away in front. The last point would hardly accord with the usual practice in ordinary life, but seems to be a special sacrificial ritual act. A similar sort of garments in the case of women appears to be alluded to in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1202919">14</a></sup> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1202919">15</a></sup> There is nothing to show exactly what differences there were between male and female costume, nor what was exactly the nature of the clothes in either case.</p><p>It is important to note that the Vedic Indian evidently assumed that all civilized persons other than inspired Munis would wear clothing of some sort.<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1202919">16</a></sup></p><p>See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वसन">vasana</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वस्त्र">vastra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="ओतु">otu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="तन्तु">tantu</a>.</b> For the use of skin garments, see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मल">mala</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202919">↑</a> i. 34, 1; 115, 4; 162, 16; viii. 3, 24; x. 26, 6; 102, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202919">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 9, 7; 11, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ii. 32; xi. 40; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, etc. A garment of <a class="vei_120a" href="कुश">kuśa</a> grass is mentioned in Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 2, 1, 8, as worn by the wife of the sacrificer at the consecration, but it is doubtful whether such dresses were normally worn. <i>Cf.</i> also <i>kausumbha-<a class="vei_120a" href="परिधान">paridhāna</a>,</i> ‘a silken garment,’ <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xi. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202919">↑</a> Rv. x. 26, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202919">↑</a> Rv. v. 55, 6 (<i>hiraṇyayān athān</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202919">↑</a> Rv. x. 107, 2. <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="वस्त्र">vastra</a>-dā,</i> v. 24, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202919">↑</a> Rv. i. 85, 1; 92, 4; ix. 96, 1, x. 1, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202919">↑</a> See Strabo, p. 709; Arrian, <i>Indica,</i> v. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202919">↑</a> Rv. ix. 97, 50.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202919">↑</a> With <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अत्क">atka</a>,</i> vi. 29, 3; x. 123, 7. this word may possibly indicate that early Vedic dress was fitted like the Minoan style of dress, and unlike the later Achæan style as seen in Homer (<i>cf.</i> Lang, <i>The World of Homer,</i> 60 <i>et seq.</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202919">↑</a> Av. viii. 2, 16; xiv. 2, 50. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 1, 3; Vājasanevi Saṃhitā, iv. 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202919">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वासस्">vāsas</a> in the narrower sense, Av. viii. 2, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202919">↑</a> Rv. i. 140, 9; 162, 16; x. 5, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202919">↑</a> v. 3, 5, 20 <i>et seq.</i> See Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 85 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1202919">↑</a> viii. 2, 16; xiv. 2, 50.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1202919">↑</a> v. 2, 1, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1202919"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1202919">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 1, 1; and iii. 1, 2, 13-17, where the fact that man alone wears clothes is accounted for by a silly legend.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 261, 262.</li></ol></div>

वासिष्ठ|వాసిష్ఠ|ವಾಸಿಷ್ಠ|vāsiṣṭha|vaasiShTha|_vei_120@2920|_vei2_pn292
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2919">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2921">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn291">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn292">2:292</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn293">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāsiṣṭha,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सात्यहव्य">sātyahavya</a>,</b> a teacher mentioned several times in the later  Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202920"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202920">1</a></sup> of <b>Rauhina</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202920"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202920">2</a></sup> and of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चैकितानेय">caikitāneya</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202920"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202920">3</a></sup> Moreover, reference is made to the claim of the Vāsiṣṭhas to be <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> priest at the sacrifice.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202920"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202920">4</a></sup> A <a class="vei_120a" href="वासिष्ठ">vāsiṣṭha</a> is mentioned as a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202920"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202920">5</a></sup> and the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202920"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202920">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202920"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202920">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 6, 2, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxiv. 17 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 474); Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 3, 9; iv. 8, 7. For his enmity to <b>Atyarāti,</b> see <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 23, 9, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202920"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202920">↑</a> i. 12, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202920"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202920">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 42, 1; Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 384. <i>Cf.</i> Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 2, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202920"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202920">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 5, 2, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 17; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 6, 1, 41. See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 34; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 212, n. (correcting the rendering of Delbrück, <i>Altindische Syntax,</i> 570).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202920"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202920">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202920"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202920">↑</a> iii. 15, 2.</li></ol></div>

वास्तु-पश्य|वास्तुपश्य|वास्तु|पश्य|వాస్తు-పశ్య|వాస్తుపశ్య|వాస్తు|పశ్య|ವಾಸ್ತು-ಪಶ್ಯ|ವಾಸ್ತುಪಶ್ಯ|ವಾಸ್ತು|ಪಶ್ಯ|vāstu-paśya|vāstupaśya|vāstu|paśya|vaastu-pashya|vaastupashya|vaastu|pashya|_vei_120@2921|_vei2_pn293
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2920">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2922">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn292">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn293">2:293</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn294">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāstu-paśya,</b> according to Bo7htlingk<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202921">1</a></sup> a name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, is a mere error for <i>Vāstupasya</i><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202921">2</a></sup> in the Jaiminiya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202921">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202921">↑</a> Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> supplement 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202921">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 26, 61.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202921"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202921">↑</a> iii. 120.</li></ol></div>

वाह|వాహ|ವಾಹ|vāha|vaaha|_vei_120@2922|_vei2_pn293
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2921">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2923">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn292">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn293">2:293</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn294">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāha</b> is found in the Rigveda (iv. 57, 4. 8) and the Atharvaveda (vi. 102, 1) apparently denoting an ox for ‘drawing’ the plough. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथवाहन">rathavāhana</a>.</b></p></div>

वाहन|వాహన|ವಾಹನ|vāhana|vaahana|_vei_120@2923|_vei2_pn293
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2922">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2924">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn292">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn293">2:293</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn294">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāhana</b> (neut.) in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202923"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202923">1</a></sup> denotes a ‘beast of burden,’ or occasionally<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202923"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202923">2</a></sup> a ‘cart.’ <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथवाहन">rathavāhana</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202923"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202923">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 9, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 2, 9; ii. 1, 4, 4; iv. 4, 4, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202923"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202923">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 4, 2, 11.</li></ol></div>

वाहस|వాహస|ವಾಹಸ|vāhasa|vaahasa|_vei_120@2924|_vei2_pn293
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2923">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2925">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn292">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn293">2:293</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn294">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāhasa,</b> ‘boa constrictor,’ is included in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202924"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202924">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202924"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202924">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 13, 1; 14, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 15; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 34. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 94.</li></ol></div>

वि|వి|ವಿ|vi|_vei_120@2925|_vei2_pn294
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2924">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2926">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn293">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn294">2:294</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn295">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi</b> in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202925"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202925">1</a></sup> and sometimes later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202925"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202925">2</a></sup> denotes ‘bird.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202925"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202925">↑</a> ii. 29, 5; 38, 7; vi. 64, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202925"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202925">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 6, 15, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 87.</li></ol></div>

वि-ककर|विककर|ककर|వి-కకర|వికకర|కకర|ವಿ-ಕಕರ|ವಿಕಕರ|ಕಕರ|vi-kakara|vikakara|kakara|_vei_120@2926|_vei2_pn294
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2925">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2927">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn293">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn294">2:294</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn295">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-kakara</b> is the name of some bird, a victim at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) according to the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202926"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202926">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202926"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202926">↑</a> xxiv. 20. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 94; in Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xx. 14, 5, <i>vikira</i> (with variants <i>vikikira, <a class="vei_120a" href="विककर">vikakara</a></i>) is read.</li></ol></div>

वि-कंकत|विकंकत|कंकत|वि-कङ्कत|विकङ्कत|कङ्कत|వి-కంకత|వికంకత|కంకత|వి-కఙ్కత|వికఙ్కత|కఙ్కత|ವಿ-ಕಂಕತ|ವಿಕಂಕತ|ಕಂಕತ|ವಿ-ಕಙ್ಕತ|ವಿಕಙ್ಕತ|ಕಙ್ಕತ|vi-kaṅkata|vikaṅkata|kaṅkata|vi-kaMkata|vikaMkata|kaMkata|vi-kankata|vikankata|kankata|_vei_120@2927|_vei2_pn294
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2926">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2928">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn293">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn294">2:294</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn295">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-kaṅkata</b> is the name of a tree (<i>Flacourtia sapida</i>), often mentioned in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202927">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202927">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202927">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 5, 7, 3; vi. 4, 10, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xix. 10; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 1, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Av. xi. 10, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202927"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202927">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 2, 4, 10; v. 2, 4, 18, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 59.</li></ol></div>

वि-क्रय|विक्रय|क्रय|వి-క్రయ|విక్రయ|క్రయ|ವಿ-ಕ್ರಯ|ವಿಕ್ರಯ|ಕ್ರಯ|vi-kraya|vikraya|kraya|_vei_120@2928|_vei2_pn294
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2927">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2929">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn293">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn294">2:294</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn295">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-kraya</b> is found in the Atharvaveda (iii. 15, 4) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> (iii. 4) denoting ‘sale.’ See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रय">kraya</a>.</b></p></div>

वि-क्लिन्दु|विक्लिन्दु|क्लिन्दु|వి-క్లిన్దు|విక్లిన్దు|క్లిన్దు|ವಿ-ಕ್ಲಿನ್ದು|ವಿಕ್ಲಿನ್ದು|ಕ್ಲಿನ್ದು|vi-klindu|viklindu|klindu|_vei_120@2929|_vei2_pn294
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2928">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2930">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn293">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn294">2:294</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn295">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-klindu</b> is the name of a disease in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202929">1</a></sup> Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202929">2</a></sup> suggests ‘catarrh.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202929">↑</a> xii. 4. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202929"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202929">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 658.</li></ol></div>

वि-घन|विघन|घन|వి-ఘన|విఘన|ఘన|ವಿ-ಘನ|ವಿಘನ|ಘನ|vi-ghana|vighana|ghana|_vei_120@2930|_vei2_pn294
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2929">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2931">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn293">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn294">2:294</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn295">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-ghana</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202930"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202930">1</a></sup> seems to denote ‘club.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202930"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202930">↑</a> iii. 2, 4, 1. The Av., vii. 28, 1, has <i><a class="vei_120a" href="द्रुघण">drughaṇa</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

वि-चक्षण ताण्ड्य|विचक्षण ताण्ड्य|चक्षण ताण्ड्य|వి-చక్షణ తాణ్డ్య|విచక్షణ తాణ్డ్య|చక్షణ తాణ్డ్య|ವಿ-ಚಕ್ಷಣ ತಾಣ್ಡ್ಯ|ವಿಚಕ್ಷಣ ತಾಣ್ಡ್ಯ|ಚಕ್ಷಣ ತಾಣ್ಡ್ಯ|vi-cakṣaṇa tāṇḍya|vicakṣaṇa tāṇḍya|cakṣaṇa tāṇḍya|vi-chakShaNa taaNDya|vichakShaNa taaNDya|chakShaNa taaNDya|vi-chaxaNa taaNDya|vichaxaNa taaNDya|chaxaNa taaNDya|_vei_120@2931|_vei2_pn294
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2930">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2932">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn293">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn294">2:294</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn295">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-cakṣaṇa Tāṇḍya</b> is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गर्दभीमुख">gardabhīmukha</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202931"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202931">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202931"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202931">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373.</li></ol></div>

वि-चारिन् काबन्धि|विचारिन् काबन्धि|चारिन् काबन्धि|వి-చారిన్ కాబన్ధి|విచారిన్ కాబన్ధి|చారిన్ కాబన్ధి|ವಿ-ಚಾರಿನ್ ಕಾಬನ್ಧಿ|ವಿಚಾರಿನ್ ಕಾಬನ್ಧಿ|ಚಾರಿನ್ ಕಾಬನ್ಧಿ|vi-cārin kābandhi|vicārin kābandhi|cārin kābandhi|vi-chaarin kaabandhi|vichaarin kaabandhi|chaarin kaabandhi|_vei_120@2932|_vei2_pn294
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2931">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2933">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn293">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn294">2:294</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn295">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-cārin Kābandhi</b> (‘descendant of <b>Kabandha</b>’) is the name of a mythical teacher in the Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202932"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202932">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202932"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202932">↑</a> i. 2, 9, 18. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 176, n. 4; Bloomfield, <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 111, 112.</li></ol></div>

वि-चृत्|विचृत्|चृत्|వి-చృత్|విచృత్|చృత్|ವಿ-ಚೃತ್|ವಿಚೃತ್|ಚೃತ್|vi-cṛt|vicṛt|cṛt|vi-chRt|vichRt|chRt|vi-chRit|vichRit|chRit|vi-chRut|vichRut|chRut|vi-chRRit|vichRRit|chRRit|_vei_120@2933|_vei2_pn295
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2932">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2934">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn294">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn295">2:295</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn296">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-cṛt</b> in the dual is found in three passages of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202933">1</a></sup> where Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202933">2</a></sup> sees in the term the name of two stars, while in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202933">3</a></sup> he thinks they mean the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a></b> called <a class="vei_120a" href="मूल">mūla</a>. There can, however, be no doubt that the asterism is intended in all the passages.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202933">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202933">↑</a> ii. 8, 1; vi. 110, 2; 121, 3. See also iii. 7, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202933">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202933">↑</a> iv. 4, 10, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202933"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202933">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 356; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 361, points out that <a class="vei_120a" href="विचृतौ">vicṛtau</a> are and  Scorpionis, while <a class="vei_120a" href="मूल">mūla</a> includes the tail as a whole.</li></ol></div>

विज्|విజ్|ವಿಜ್|vij|_vei_120@2934|_vei2_pn295
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2933">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2935">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn294">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn295">2:295</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn296">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vij.</b> See 2. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a>.</b></p></div>

वि-जामातृ|विजामातृ|जामातृ|వి-జామాతృ|విజామాతృ|జామాతృ|ವಿ-ಜಾಮಾತೃ|ವಿಜಾಮಾತೃ|ಜಾಮಾತೃ|vi-jāmātṛ|vijāmātṛ|jāmātṛ|vi-jaamaatR|vijaamaatR|jaamaatR|vi-jaamaatRi|vijaamaatRi|jaamaatRi|vi-jaamaatRu|vijaamaatRu|jaamaatRu|vi-jaamaatRRi|vijaamaatRRi|jaamaatRRi|_vei_120@2935|_vei2_pn295
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2934">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2936">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn294">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn295">2:295</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn296">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-jāmātṛ.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जामातृ">jāmātṛ</a>.</b></p></div>

वितस्ता|వితస్తా|ವಿತಸ್ತಾ|vitastā|vitastaa|_vei_120@2936|_vei2_pn295
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2935">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2937">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn294">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn295">2:295</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn296">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vitastā,</b> the most westerly of the five rivers of the Panjab, is only mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202936"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202936">1</a></sup> in the Nadīstuti (‘Praise of Rivers’).<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202936"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202936">2</a></sup> It is the Hydaspes of Alexander's historians, more correctly reproduced by Ptolemy as Bidaspes. The name appears in the Mohammedan historians corrupted to Bihat or Wihat, and survives in the modern Kashmīrī form of Veth.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202936"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202936">↑</a> x. 75, 5; <b><a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,</b> ix. 26; <i>cf.</i> Kāśikā <a class="vei_120a" href="वृत्ति">vṛtti</a> on Pāṇini, i. 4, 31. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 12; <i>Imperial Gazetteer of India,</i> 14, 160.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202936"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202936">↑</a> The rareness of the name in the Rigveda points to the Panjab not having been the seat of the activity of the greater part of the Vedic Indians.</li></ol></div>

वित्त|విత్త|ವಿತ್ತ|vitta|_vei_120@2937|_vei2_pn295
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2936">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2938">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn294">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn295">2:295</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn296">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vitta</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202937"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202937">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202937"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202937">2</a></sup> denotes ‘wealth,’ ‘possessions.’ The earth is referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202937"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202937">3</a></sup> as full of riches (<i>vittasya pūrṇā</i>). The doctrine that a man's greatness depends on his wealth is found as early as the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202937"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202937">4</a></sup> The striving after wealth (<i>vittaiṣaṇā</i>) is mentioned in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202937"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202937">5</a></sup> as one of the things abandoned by the sage. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202937"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202937">↑</a> v. 42, 9; x. 34, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202937"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202937">↑</a> Av. xii. 3, 52; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 5, 9, 2; vi. 2, 4, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 11. 14, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202937"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202937">↑</a> ii. 8. <i>Cf.</i> the name <i>vasumatī</i> found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xiii. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202937"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202937">↑</a> i. 4, 7, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202937"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202937">↑</a> iii. 4, 1; iv. 4, 26.</li></ol></div>

विदग्ध शाकल्य|విదగ్ధ శాకల్య|ವಿದಗ್ಧ ಶಾಕಲ್ಯ|vidagdha śākalya|vidagdha shaakalya|_vei_120@2938|_vei2_pn296
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2937">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2939">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn295">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn296">2:296</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn297">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vidagdha Śākalya</b> is the name of a teacher, a contemporary and rival of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a></b> at the court of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a></b> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a></b> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202938"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202938">1</a></sup> the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202938"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202938">2</a></sup> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202938"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202938">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202938"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202938">↑</a> iii. 9, 1; iv. 1, 17 (Mādhyaṃdina = 7 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202938"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202938">↑</a> ii. 76 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 239).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202938"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202938">↑</a> xi. 6, 3, 3.</li></ol></div>

विदथ|విదథ|ವಿದಥ|vidatha|_vei_120@2939|_vei2_pn296
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2938">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2940">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn295">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn296">2:296</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn297">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vidatha</b> is a word of obscure sense, confined mainly to the Rigveda. According to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202939">1</a></sup> the sense is primarily ‘order,’ then the concrete body which gives orders, then ‘assembly’ for secular<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202939">2</a></sup> or religious ends,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202939">3</a></sup> or for war.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202939">4</a></sup> Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202939">5</a></sup> once thought that the main idea is ‘ordinance’ (from <i>vi-<a class="vei_120a" href="धा">dhā</a>,</i> ‘dispose,’ ‘ordain’), and thence ‘sacrifice.’ Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202939">6</a></sup> thinks that the root idea is an ‘assembly,’ especially of the <b>Maghavans</b> and the Brahmins. Geldner<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202939">7</a></sup> considers that the word primarily means ‘knowledge,’ ‘wisdom,’ ‘priestly lore,’ then ‘sacrifice’ and ‘spiritual authority.’ Bloomfield,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202939">8</a></sup> on the other hand, insists that <a class="vei_120a" href="विदथ">vidatha</a> refers to the ‘house’<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202939">9</a></sup> in the first place (from <i><a class="vei_120a" href="विद्">vid</a>,</i> ‘acquire’), and then to the ‘sacrifice,’ as connected with the house; this interpretation, at any rate, appears to suit all the passages. The term <i>vidathya,</i> once<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202939">10</a></sup> applied to the king (<i>samrāṭ</i>), might seem to be against this view, but it may refer to his being ‘rich in homesteads’; and the connexion of the woman with the <a class="vei_120a" href="विदथ">vidatha</a>, as opposed to  the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a>,</b> tells in favour<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202939">11</a></sup> of Bloomfield's explanation. That the word ever denotes an asylum, like the house of the Brahmin,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202939">12</a></sup> as Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202939">13</a></sup> suggests, is doubtful.<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1202939">14</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202939">↑</a> Rv. i. 31, 6; 117, 25; iii. 1, 18; 27, 7; iv. 38, 4; vi. 8, 1; x. 85, 26; 92, 2; Av. iv. 25, 1; v. 20, 12; xviii. 3, 70, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202939">↑</a> ii. 1, 4; 27, 12, 17; iii. 38, 5, 6; v. 63, 2; vii. 66, 10; viii. 39, 1; x. 12, 7; Av. xvii. 1, 15. So Whitney renders the word in Av. i. 13, 4, as ‘council,’ Translation of the Atharvaveda, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202939">↑</a> Rv. i. 60, 1; ii. 4, 8; 39, 1; iii. 1, 1; 56, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202939">↑</a> Rv. i. 166, 2; 167, 6; v. 59, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202939">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 46, 26 <i>et seq.</i> But in <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenlāndischen Gesellschaft,</i> 54, 609-611, he falls back on the derivation from <i><a class="vei_120a" href="विध्">vidh</a>,</i> ‘worship.’ <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> p. 23, n. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202939">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 259 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202939">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 147; <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 52, 757; <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 161.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202939">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 19, 12 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202939">↑</a> See Rigveda, x. 85, 26. 27 (of the wife in the marriage ritual); i. 117, 25; ii. 1, 6; Av. xviii. 3, 70.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202939">↑</a> iv. 27, 2. In i. 91, 20; 167, 3; Av. xx. 128, 1, <i>vidathya,</i> ‘having an establishment,’ seems adequate.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202939">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Av. vii. 38, 4; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 7, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202939">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 1, 13, with Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 3, 35.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202939">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 3, 261.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1202939"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1202939">↑</a> Rv. i. 31, 6; v. 62, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 30, 27, 28, certainly does not show this clearly.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 177, who suggests that <a class="vei_120a" href="विदथ">vidatha</a> sometimes means (<i>e.g.,</i> in <i>vidatheṣu praśastaḥ,</i> Rv. ii. 27, 12) a smaller assembly than the <a class="vei_120a" href="समिति">samiti</a>. But we have no ground to be certain that such smaller assemblies ever existed at an early date either in India or elsewhere among Āryan peoples.</li></ol></div>

विदन्वन्त् भार्गव|విదన్వన్త్ భార్గవ|ವಿದನ್ವನ್ತ್ ಭಾರ್ಗವ|vidanvant bhārgava|vidanvant bhaargava|_vei_120@2940|_vei2_pn297
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2939">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2941">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn296">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn297">2:297</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn298">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vidanvant Bhārgava</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भृगु">bhṛgu</a></b>’) is mentioned as the seer of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> or chant in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202940"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202940">1</a></sup> and in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202940"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202940">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202940"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202940">↑</a> xiii. 11, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202940"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202940">↑</a> iii. 159 <i>et seq.</i> (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 26, 64).</li></ol></div>

विदर्भ|విదర్భ|ವಿದರ್ಭ|vidarbha|_vei_120@2941|_vei2_pn297
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2940">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2942">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn296">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn297">2:297</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn298">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vidarbha</b> occurs in the earlier Vedic literature as the name of a place only in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202941"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202941">1</a></sup> where its <b>Mācalas</b> (perhaps a species of dog) are said to kill tigers.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202941"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202941">↑</a> ii. 440 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 19, 103, n. 3).</li></ol></div>

विदर्भी-कौण्डिनेय|विदर्भीकौण्डिनेय|विदर्भी|विदर्भि|कौण्डिनेय|విదర్భీ-కౌణ్డినేయ|విదర్భీకౌణ్డినేయ|విదర్భీ|విదర్భి|కౌణ్డినేయ|ವಿದರ್ಭೀ-ಕೌಣ್ಡಿನೇಯ|ವಿದರ್ಭೀಕೌಣ್ಡಿನೇಯ|ವಿದರ್ಭೀ|ವಿದರ್ಭಿ|ಕೌಣ್ಡಿನೇಯ|vidarbhī-kauṇḍineya|vidarbhīkauṇḍineya|vidarbhī|vidarbhi|kauṇḍineya|vidarbhii-kauNDineya|vidarbhiikauNDineya|vidarbhii|kauNDineya|vidarbhee-kauNDineya|vidarbheekauNDineya|vidarbhee|_vei_120@2942|_vei2_pn297
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2941">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2943">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn296">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn297">2:297</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn298">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vidarbhī-Kauṇḍineya</b> is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b>Vatsanapāt</b> in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202942">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202942"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202942">↑</a> ii. 5, 22; iv. 5, 28 (Mādhyaṃdina = ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>)</li></ol></div>

वि-दिश्|विदिश्|दिश्|వి-దిశ్|విదిశ్|దిశ్|ವಿ-ದಿಶ್|ವಿದಿಶ್|ದಿಶ್|vi-diś|vidiś|diś|vi-dish|vidish|dish|_vei_120@2943|_vei2_pn297
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2942">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2944">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn296">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn297">2:297</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn298">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-diś</b> denotes<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202943"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202943">1</a></sup> an ‘intermediate quarter.’ See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दिश्">diś</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202943"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202943">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, vi. 19; Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 4.</li></ol></div>

विदीगय|విదీగయ|ವಿದೀಗಯ|vidīgaya|vidiigaya|videegaya|_vei_120@2944|_vei2_pn297
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2943">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2945">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn296">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn297">2:297</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn298">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vidīgaya</b> is the name of an animal in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202944"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202944">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202944"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202944">2</a></sup> The commentary on the former takes it as a kind of cock (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कुक्कुट">kukkuṭa</a>-viśeṣa</i>), that on the latter as a white heron (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="श्वेत">śveta</a>-baka</i>). </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202944"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202944">↑</a> v. 6, 22, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202944"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202944">↑</a> iii. 9, 9, 3; Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xx. 22, 13.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 94.</li></ol></div>

विदेघ|విదేఘ|ವಿದೇಘ|videgha|_vei_120@2945|_vei2_pn298
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2944">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2946">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn297">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn298">2:298</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn299">↷</a><hr><p><b>Videgha</b> is the name of a man, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="माथव">māthava</a>,</b> in the Śatapatha Brāhmana.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202945"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202945">1</a></sup> It is legitimate to assume<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202945"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202945">2</a></sup> that it is a name given to him as king of the Videghas who are the later <b>Videhas.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202945"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202945">↑</a> i. 4, 1, 10 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202945"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202945">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, xli, n. 4; 104, n.; Weber <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 170; <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 13; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 134.</li></ol></div>

विदेह|విదేహ|ವಿದೇಹ|videha|_vei_120@2946|_vei2_pn298
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2945">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2947">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn297">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn298">2:298</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn299">↷</a><hr><p><b>Videha</b> is the name of a people who are not mentioned before the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> period. In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202946">1</a></sup> the legend of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विदेघ">videgha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="माथव">māthava</a></b> preserves clearly a tradition that in <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a> culture came from the Brahmins of the West, and that <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a></b> was brahminized before <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>. The Videhas, however, derived some fame later from the culture of their king <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a>,</b> who figures in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202946">2</a></sup> as one of the leading patrons of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a> doctrine. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202946">3</a></sup> the Videhas are joined with the <b>Kāśis;</b> in the list of peoples in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202946">4</a></sup> the Videhas are passed over, probably because, with <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a>, they are included in the term <b>Prācyas,</b> ‘easterners.’ Again, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202946">5</a></sup> it is recorded that the <a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a> kingdoms had each the one <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जल जातूकर्ण्य">jala jātūkarṇya</a>;</b> and in another passage of the same text<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202946">6</a></sup> the connexion between the <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a> king, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर आट्णार">para āṭṇāra</a>,</b> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a> king, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्यनाभ">hiraṇyanābha</a>,</b> is explained, while the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202946">7</a></sup> speaks of <a class="vei_120a" href="पर आट्णार">para āṭṇāra</a> as the <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a> king, descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्यनाभ">hiraṇyanābha</a>.</p><p>Another king of <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a> was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नमी साप्य">namī sāpya</a>,</b> mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202946">8</a></sup> In the Saṃhitās of the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202946">9</a></sup> ‘cows of Videha’ seem to be alluded to, though the commentator on the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā merely takes the adjective <i>vaidehī</i> as ‘having a splendid body’ (<i>viśiṣṭa-deha-sambandhinī</i>), and the point of a place name in the expression is not very obvious. The Videhas also occur in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202946">10</a></sup> in <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>-like passages.</p><p>The boundary of <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a> was the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सदानीरा">sadānīrā</a>,</b>  probably the modern Gandak<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202946">11</a></sup> (the Kondochates of the Greek geographers), which, rising in Nepal, flows into the Ganges opposite <a class="vei_120a" href="पत्न">patna</a>. <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a> itself corresponds roughly to the modern Tirhut.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202946">↑</a> i. 4, 1, 10 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202946">↑</a> iii. 8, 2. <i>Cf.</i> iv. 2, 6; 9, 30; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 3, 1, 2; 6, 2, 1; 3, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 10, 9, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202946">↑</a> iv. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202946">↑</a> viii. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202946">↑</a> xvi. 29. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202946">↑</a> xvi. 9, 11. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202946">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202946">↑</a> xxv. 10, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202946">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 4, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xiv. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202946">↑</a> ii. 5; xxi. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202946"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202946">↑</a> <i>Cf. Imperial Gaxetteer of India,</i> 12, 125.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 170; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 10, 33, 53, 127, 129, etc.; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, xli; Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 398, 399; Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India,</i> 26, 37; Pargiter, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1910, 19 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

विद्या|విద్యా|ವಿದ್ಯಾ|vidyā|vidyaa|_vei_120@2947|_vei2_pn299
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2946">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2948">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn298">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn299">2:299</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn300">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vidyā</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202947"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202947">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202947"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202947">2</a></sup> denotes ‘knowledge,’ especially that of the three Vedas, which are called the <i>trayī <a class="vei_120a" href="विद्या">vidyā</a>,</i> ‘the threefold knowledge,’ as early as the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202947"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202947">3</a></sup> In a more special sense <a class="vei_120a" href="विद्या">vidyā</a> occurs in lists of objects of study in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202947"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202947">4</a></sup> What exactly the expression here means is uncertain: Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202947"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202947">5</a></sup> suggests the philosophic systems; Geldner<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202947"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202947">6</a></sup> the first Brāhmaṇas; and Eggeling,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202947"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202947">7</a></sup> more probably, special sciences like the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सर्पविद्या">sarpavidyā</a></b> or the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विषविद्या">viṣavidyā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202947"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202947">↑</a> vi. 116, 1; xi. 7, 10; 8, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202947"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202947">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 2, 8; v. 1, 7, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 23, 8. 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202947"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202947">↑</a> iii. 10, 11, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 5, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202947"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202947">↑</a> xi. 5, 6, 8; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 4, 10; iv. 5, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202947"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202947">↑</a> On Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 6, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202947"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202947">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 290, n. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202947"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202947">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 98, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

विद्रध|విద్రధ|ವಿದ್ರಧ|vidradha|_vei_120@2948|_vei2_pn299
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2947">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2949">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn298">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn299">2:299</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn300">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vidradha</b> denotes a disease, ‘abscesses,’ in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202948">1</a></sup> According to Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202948">2</a></sup> it was a symptom accompanying <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यक्ष्म">yakṣma</a>.</b> Later it is called Vidradhi. Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202948">3</a></sup> compares the obscure <a class="vei_120a" href="विद्रध">vidradha</a> of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202948">4</a></sup> where, however, the sense of the word is very uncertain.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202948">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202948">↑</a> vi. 127, 1; ix. 8, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202948">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 386.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202948">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 5, 93. <i>Cf.</i> Roth, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, Erläuterungen,</i> 42, 43.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202948">↑</a> iv. 32, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202948"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202948">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 295.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Wise, <i>System of Hindu Medicine,</i> 210; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 531, 602; <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 60; Grohmann, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 397; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 376.</li></ol></div>

विधवा|విధవా|ವಿಧವಾ|vidhavā|vidhavaa|_vei_120@2949|_vei2_pn299
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2948">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2950">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn298">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn299">2:299</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn300">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vidhavā</b> denotes ‘widow’ as the ‘desolate one,’ from the root <i><a class="vei_120a" href="विध्">vidh</a>,</i> ‘be bereft.’ The masculine <i>vidhava</i> is conjectured  by Roth<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202949">1</a></sup> in a difficult passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202949">2</a></sup> where the received text presents the apparent false concord <i>vidhantaṃ vidhavām,</i> in which he sees a metrical lengthening for <i>vidhavam,</i> ‘the sacrificing widower.’ Ludwig in his version takes <i>vidhantam</i> as equivalent to a feminine, while Delbrück<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202949">3</a></sup> prefers ‘the worshipper and the widow.’ Possibly ‘the widower and the widow’ may be meant; but we know nothing of the mythological allusion in question, the feat being one of those attributed to the Aśvins, and the natural reference to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="घोषा">ghoṣā</a></b> as ‘husbandless’ being rendered unlikely because their feat in regard to her has already been mentioned a few verses before in the same hymn.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202949">4</a></sup> The word <a class="vei_120a" href="विधवा">vidhavā</a> is not of common occurrence.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202949">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202949">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> so also Grassmann.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202949">↑</a> x. 40, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202949">↑</a> <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 443.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202949">↑</a> x. 40. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202949"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202949">↑</a> Rv. iv. 18, 12; x. 40, 2; Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 15.</li></ol></div>

विधु|విధు|ವಿಧು|vidhu|_vei_120@2950|_vei2_pn300
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2949">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2951">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn299">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn300">2:300</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn301">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vidhu</b> seems clearly to mean (as it does in the post-Vedic language) the ‘moon’ in a passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202950"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202950">1</a></sup> where it is alluded to as ‘wandering solitary in the midst of many’ (<i>vidhuṃ dadrāṇaṃ samane bahūnām</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202950"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202950">↑</a> x. 55, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, xiv. 18. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 465. That the ‘many’ are the <b>Nakṣatras</b> is neither certain nor even probable. The stars are an adequate explanation.</li></ol></div>

वि-नशन|विनशन|नशन|వి-నశన|వినశన|నశన|ವಿ-ನಶನ|ವಿನಶನ|ನಶನ|vi-naśana|vinaśana|naśana|vi-nashana|vinashana|nashana|_vei_120@2951|_vei2_pn300
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2950">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2952">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn299">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn300">2:300</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn301">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-naśana,</b> ‘disappearance,’ is the name of the place where the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a></b> is lost in the sands of the desert. It is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202951"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202951">1</a></sup> and the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202951"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202951">2</a></sup> The locality is the Patiala district of the Panjab.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202951"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202951">3</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्लक्ष प्रास्रवण">plakṣa prāsravaṇa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202951"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202951">↑</a> xxv. 10, 6; Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxiv. 5, 30; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 15, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 1, 2, 12. <i>Cf.</i> Bühler, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 14, 2, 147.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202951"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202951">↑</a> iv. 26.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202951"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202951">↑</a> <i>Cf. Imperial Gazetteer of India,</i> 22, 97.</li></ol></div>

विप्|విప్|ವಿಪ್|vip|_vei_120@2952|_vei2_pn300
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2951">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2953">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn299">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn300">2:300</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn301">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vip</b> in several passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202952"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202952">1</a></sup> refers, according to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202952"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202952">2</a></sup> to the rods which form the bottom of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> filter,  and on which the straining cloth is stretched. But this explanation is very doubtful.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202952"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202952">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202952"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202952">↑</a> ix. 3, 2; 65, 12; 99, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202952"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202952">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202952"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202952">↑</a> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 203; Bergaigne, <i>Religion Vedique,</i> 1, v; Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellchaft,</i> 54, 171; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 97-110.</li></ol></div>

वि-पथ|विपथ|पथ|వి-పథ|విపథ|పథ|ವಿ-ಪಥ|ವಿಪಥ|ಪಥ|vi-patha|vipatha|patha|_vei_120@2953|_vei2_pn301
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2952">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2954">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn300">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn301">2:301</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn302">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-patha,</b> in the description of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात्य">vrātya</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202953"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202953">1</a></sup> denotes a vehicle suited for rough roads. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अनस्">anas</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202953"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202953">↑</a> Av. xv. 2, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvii. 1, 14; Lāṭyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 6, 9; Anupada <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, v. 4; Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxii. 4, 11; Āpastamba Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxii. 5, 5; <i>cf.</i> vii. 3, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 44.</li></ol></div>

विपश्चित् दृढ-जयन्त लौहित्य|विपश्चित् दृढ-जयन्त लौहित्य|विपश्चित् दृढजयन्त लौहित्य|विपश्चित् दृढ|जयन्त लौहित्य|విపశ్చిత్ దృఢ-జయన్త లౌహిత్య_౧|విపశ్చిత్ దృఢ-జయన్త లౌహిత్య|విపశ్చిత్ దృఢజయన్త లౌహిత్య|విపశ్చిత్ దృఢ|జయన్త లౌహిత్య|ವಿಪಶ್ಚಿತ್ ದೃಢ-ಜಯನ್ತ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ_೧|ವಿಪಶ್ಚಿತ್ ದೃಢ-ಜಯನ್ತ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|ವಿಪಶ್ಚಿತ್ ದೃಢಜಯನ್ತ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|ವಿಪಶ್ಚಿತ್ ದೃಢ|ಜಯನ್ತ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|vipaścit dṛḍha-jayanta lauhitya_1|vipaścit dṛḍha-jayanta lauhitya|vipaścit dṛḍhajayanta lauhitya|vipaścit dṛḍha|jayanta lauhitya|vipashchit dRDha-jayanta lauhitya_1|vipashchit dRDha-jayanta lauhitya|vipashchit dRDhajayanta lauhitya|vipashchit dRDha|vipashchit dRiDha-jayanta lauhitya_1|vipashchit dRiDha-jayanta lauhitya|vipashchit dRiDhajayanta lauhitya|vipashchit dRiDha|vipashchit dRuDha-jayanta lauhitya_1|vipashchit dRuDha-jayanta lauhitya|vipashchit dRuDhajayanta lauhitya|vipashchit dRuDha|vipashchit dRRiDha-jayanta lauhitya_1|vipashchit dRRiDha-jayanta lauhitya|vipashchit dRRiDhajayanta lauhitya|vipashchit dRRiDha|_vei_120@2954|_vei2_pn301
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2953">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2955">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn300">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn301">2:301</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn302">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Vipaścit <a class="vei_120a" href="दृढ-जयन्त">dṛḍha-jayanta</a> Lauhitya</b> (‘descendant of Lohita’) is mentioned in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 42, 1) as the pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दक्ष जयन्त लौहित्य">dakṣa <a class="vei_120a" href="जयन्त">jayanta</a> lauhitya</a>.</b></p></div>

विपश्चित् शकुनि-मित्र पाराशर्य|विपश्चित् शकुनि-मित्र पाराशर्य|विपश्चित् शकुनिमित्र पाराशर्य|विपश्चित् शकुनि|मित्र पाराशर्य|విపశ్చిత్ శకుని-మిత్ర పారాశర్య_౨|విపశ్చిత్ శకుని-మిత్ర పారాశర్య|విపశ్చిత్ శకునిమిత్ర పారాశర్య|విపశ్చిత్ శకుని|మిత్ర పారాశర్య|ವಿಪಶ್ಚಿತ್ ಶಕುನಿ-ಮಿತ್ರ ಪಾರಾಶರ್ಯ_೨|ವಿಪಶ್ಚಿತ್ ಶಕುನಿ-ಮಿತ್ರ ಪಾರಾಶರ್ಯ|ವಿಪಶ್ಚಿತ್ ಶಕುನಿಮಿತ್ರ ಪಾರಾಶರ್ಯ|ವಿಪಶ್ಚಿತ್ ಶಕುನಿ|ಮಿತ್ರ ಪಾರಾಶರ್ಯ|vipaścit śakuni-mitra pārāśarya_2|vipaścit śakuni-mitra pārāśarya|vipaścit śakunimitra pārāśarya|vipaścit śakuni|mitra pārāśarya|vipashchit shakuni-mitra paaraasharya_2|vipashchit shakuni-mitra paaraasharya|vipashchit shakunimitra paaraasharya|vipashchit shakuni|mitra paaraasharya|_vei_120@2955|_vei2_pn301
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2954">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2956">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn300">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn301">2:301</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn302">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Vipaścit <a class="vei_120a" href="शकुनि-मित्र">śakuni-mitra</a> Pārāśarya</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पराशर">parāśara</a></b>’) is the name of a teacher, pupil of <a class="vei_120a" href="अषाढ उत्तर पाराशर्य">aṣāḍha uttara pārāśarya</a>, in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> Brāhmana (iii. 41, 1).</p></div>

वि-पाश्|विपाश्|पाश्|వి-పాశ్|విపాశ్|పాశ్|ವಿ-ಪಾಶ್|ವಿಪಾಶ್|ಪಾಶ್|vi-pāś|vipāś|pāś|vi-paash|vipaash|paash|_vei_120@2956|_vei2_pn301
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2955">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2957">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn300">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn301">2:301</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn302">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-pāś</b> (‘fetterless’) is the name of a river mentioned twice in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202956"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202956">1</a></sup> It is the modern Beās in the Panjab, the Hyphasis, Hypanis or Bipasis of the Greeks. Its small importance for the Vedic Indians is indicated by the fact that it is never mentioned in the earlier Vedic literature except in two hymns of the Rigveda. The <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202956"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202956">2</a></sup> preserves the notice that its earlier name was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उरुञ्जिरा">uruñjirā</a>,</b> while the Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202956"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202956">3</a></sup> places in the middle of it the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>-śilāḥ.</i> Pāṇinī<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202956"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202956">4</a></sup> mentions the name, which otherwise in post-Vedic literature appears as Vipāśā. This river has changed its course considerably since ancient times.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202956"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202956">5</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202956"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202956">↑</a> iii. 33, 1. 3; iv. 30, 11. <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, xi. 48, sees in the latter passage an adjective <i>vi-<a class="vei_120a" href="पाशिन्">pāśin</a>,</i> but this is very improbable. See Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 294.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202956"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202956">↑</a> ix. 26. The <a class="vei_120a" href="विपाश्">vipāś</a> is also mentioned in connexion with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुतुद्री">śutudrī</a></b> in ii. 24; ix. 36.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202956"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202956">↑</a> i. 2, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202956"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202956">↑</a> iv. 2, 74.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202956"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202956">↑</a> See <i>Imperiat Gazctteer of India,</i> 7, 138 (Beās).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 11.</li></ol></div>

वि-पूजन शौराकि|विपूजन शौराकि|पूजन शौराकि|వి-పూజన శౌరాకి|విపూజన శౌరాకి|పూజన శౌరాకి|ವಿ-ಪೂಜನ ಶೌರಾಕಿ|ವಿಪೂಜನ ಶೌರಾಕಿ|ಪೂಜನ ಶೌರಾಕಿ|vi-pūjana śaurāki|vipūjana śaurāki|pūjana śaurāki|vi-puujana shauraaki|vipuujana shauraaki|puujana shauraaki|vi-poojana shauraaki|vipoojana shauraaki|poojana shauraaki|_vei_120@2957|_vei2_pn302
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2956">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2958">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn301">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn302">2:302</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn303">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-pūjana Śaurāki</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202957"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202957">1</a></sup> or Saurāki<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202957"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202957">2</a></sup> is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202957"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202957">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 1, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202957"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202957">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxvii. 5.</li></ol></div>

विपृथु|విపృథు|ವಿಪೃಥು|vipṛthu|vipRthu|vipRithu|vipRuthu|vipRRithu|_vei_120@2958|_vei2_pn302
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2957">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2959">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn301">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn302">2:302</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn303">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vipṛthu</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (xiv. 72, 3) is apparently equivalent to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विपथ">vipatha</a>,</b> ‘rough cart,’ of other texts. It is probably a mere blunder.</p></div>

विप्र|విప్ర|ವಿಪ್ರ|vipra|_vei_120@2959|_vei2_pn302
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2958">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2960">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn301">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn302">2:302</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn303">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vipra</b> seems to mean ‘inspired singer’ (from <i><a class="vei_120a" href="विप्">vip</a>,</i> ‘quiver’) in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202959"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202959">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202959"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202959">2</a></sup> More especially in the later texts<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202959"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202959">3</a></sup> it denotes a ‘learned Brahmin.’ In the epic style it comes to mean no more than ‘Brahmin.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202959"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202959">↑</a> i. 129, 2. 11; 162, 7; iv. 26, 1, etc. Seven are spoken of in iii. 7, 7; 31. 5; iv. 2, 15, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202959"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202959">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 9, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ix. 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 2, 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202959"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202959">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 5, 3, 12, etc.</li></ol></div>

विप्र-चित्ति|विप्रचित्ति|विप्र|चित्ति|విప్ర-చిత్తి|విప్రచిత్తి|విప్ర|చిత్తి|ವಿಪ್ರ-ಚಿತ್ತಿ|ವಿಪ್ರಚಿತ್ತಿ|ವಿಪ್ರ|ಚಿತ್ತಿ|vipra-citti|vipracitti|vipra|citti|vipra-chitti|viprachitti|chitti|_vei_120@2960|_vei2_pn302
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2959">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2961">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn301">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn302">2:302</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn303">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vipra-citti</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202960"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202960">1</a></sup> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विप्र">vipra</a>-jitti</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202960"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202960">2</a></sup> is the name of a teacher in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202960"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202960">↑</a> ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202960"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202960">↑</a> ii. 5, 22; iv. 5, 28 Mādhyaṃdina.</li></ol></div>

विप्र-जन सौराकि|विप्रजन सौराकि|विप्र|जन सौराकि|విప్ర-జన సౌరాకి|విప్రజన సౌరాకి|విప్ర|జన సౌరాకి|ವಿಪ್ರ-ಜನ ಸೌರಾಕಿ|ವಿಪ್ರಜನ ಸೌರಾಕಿ|ವಿಪ್ರ|ಜನ ಸೌರಾಕಿ|vipra-jana saurāki|viprajana saurāki|vipra|jana saurāki|vipra-jana sauraaki|viprajana sauraaki|jana sauraaki|_vei_120@2961|_vei2_pn302
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2960">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2962">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn301">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn302">2:302</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn303">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vipra-<a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a> Saurāki</b> is the form of the name of <b>Vipūjana</b> given by the St. Petersburg Dictionaries for the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202961"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202961">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202961"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202961">↑</a> xxvii. 5; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 477, gives this form, which is due to a misreading of the ligature for <i>ū.</i></li></ol></div>

विबाली|विबालि|విబాలీ|విబాలి|ವಿಬಾಲೀ|ವಿಬಾಲಿ|vibālī|vibāli|vibaalii|vibaali|vibaalee|_vei_120@2962|_vei2_pn302
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2961">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2963">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn301">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn302">2:302</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn303">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vibālī</b> is found once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202962">1</a></sup> apparently as the name of an unknown stream.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202962"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202962">↑</a> iv. 30, 12. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 12, 18.</li></ol></div>

विभण्डक काश्यप|విభణ్డక కాశ్యప|ವಿಭಣ್ಡಕ ಕಾಶ್ಯಪ|vibhaṇḍaka kāśyapa|vibhaNDaka kaashyapa|_vei_120@2963|_vei2_pn302
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2962">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2964">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn301">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn302">2:302</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn303">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vibhaṇḍaka Kāśyapa</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कश्यप">kaśyapa</a></b>’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋष्यशृंग">ṛṣyaśṛṅga</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202963"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202963">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202963"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202963">↑</a> <i>Indische Studīen,</i> 4, 374. <i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. Vibhāṇḍaka,</i> which is the more correct spelling (Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i>).</li></ol></div>

वि-भिन्दु|विभिन्दु|भिन्दु|వి-భిన్దు|విభిన్దు|భిన్దు|ವಿ-ಭಿನ್ದು|ವಿಭಿನ್ದು|ಭಿನ್ದು|vi-bhindu|vibhindu|bhindu|_vei_120@2964|_vei2_pn303
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2963">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2965">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn302">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn303">2:303</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn304">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-bhindu</b> is the name of a sacrificer in the Rigveda (viii. 2, 91).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hopkins. <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 19, 59.</p></div>

विभिन्दुक|విభిన్దుక|ವಿಭಿನ್ದುಕ|vibhinduka|_vei_120@2965|_vei2_pn303
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2964">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2966">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn302">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn303">2:303</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn304">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vibhinduka</b> occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202965"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202965">1</a></sup> as the name of a man or a demon<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202965"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202965">2</a></sup> from whom <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मेधातिथि">medhātithi</a></b> drove away the cows. Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202965"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202965">3</a></sup> is inclined to read Vaibhinduka as a patronymic of <a class="vei_120a" href="मेधातिथि">medhātithi</a>. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विभिन्दुकीय">vibhindukīya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202965"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202965">↑</a> xv. 10, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202965"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202965">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Sāyaṇa, <i>a.l.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202965"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202965">↑</a> <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 60, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

विभिन्दुकीय|విభిన్దుకీయ|ವಿಭಿನ್ದುಕೀಯ|vibhindukīya|vibhindukiiya|vibhindukeeya|_vei_120@2966|_vei2_pn303
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2965">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2967">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn302">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn303">2:303</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn304">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vibhindukīya</b> is the name of a group of priests whose Sattra is mentioned in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202966"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202966">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202966"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202966">↑</a> iii. 233 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 38).</li></ol></div>

विभीतक|విభీతక|ವಿಭೀತಕ|vibhītaka|vibhiitaka|vibheetaka|_vei_120@2967|_vei2_pn303
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2966">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2968">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn302">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn303">2:303</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn304">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vibhītaka</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202967"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202967">1</a></sup> and <b>Vibhīdaka,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202967"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202967">2</a></sup> the latter being the old form, denote a large tree, the <i>Terminalia bellerica,</i> the nut of which was used in dicing.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202967"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202967">3</a></sup> The wood was also used for making the<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202967"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202967">4</a></sup> sacrificial fire burn.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202967"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202967">↑</a> This form is the regular one after the Rigveda.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202967"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202967">↑</a> Rv. vii. 86, 6; x. 34, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202967"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202967">↑</a> Rv., <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i> See 2. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202967"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202967">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 5, 8; 7, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 8, 1, 16, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 62; Roth, <i>Gurupūjākaumudī,</i> 1-4; Lüders, <i>Das Wūrfelspiel im alten Indien,</i> 17-19.</li></ol></div>

वि-मद|वि-मद|विमद|मद|వి-మద_౧|వి-మద|విమద|మద|ವಿ-ಮದ_೧|ವಿ-ಮದ|ವಿಮದ|ಮದ|vi-mada_1|vi-mada|vimada|mada|_vei_120@2968|_vei2_pn303
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2967">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2969">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn302">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn303">2:303</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn304">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Vi-mada</b> is credited by the Anukramaṇī (Index) with the authorship of a number of hymns of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202968"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202968">1</a></sup> This attribution is supported by the occurrence in this group of the name of the seer,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202968"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202968">2</a></sup> and once of his family, the Vimadas,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202968"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202968">3</a></sup> besides the repeated refrain<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202968"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202968">4</a></sup> <i>vi vo made,</i> ‘in your carouses.’ <a class="vei_120a" href="विमद">vimada</a> is occasionally alluded to later.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202968"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202968">5</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202968"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202968">↑</a> Rv. x. 20-26.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202968"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202968">↑</a> Rv. x. 20, 10; 23, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202968"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202968">↑</a> Rv. x. 23, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202968"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202968">↑</a> Rv. x. 21, 1-8; 24, 1-3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202968"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202968">↑</a> Av. iv. 29, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 1.</li></ol></div>

विमद|विमद|విమద_౨|విమద|ವಿಮದ_೨|ವಿಮದ|vimada_2|vimada|_vei_120@2969|_vei2_pn304
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2968">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2970">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn303">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn304">2:304</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn305">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Vimada</b> is mentioned in several passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202969">1</a></sup> as a protégé of the Aśvins, who gave him a wife, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कमद्यू">kamadyū</a>.</b> His identity with the preceding is improbable.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202969"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202969">↑</a> i. 51, 3; 112, 19; 116, 1; 117, 20; x. 39, 7; 65, 12, From viii. 9, 15, Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 105, has inferred that <a class="vei_120a" href="विमद">vimada</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="वत्स">vatsa</a> were identical.</li></ol></div>

वि-मुक्ता|विमुक्ता|मुक्ता|వి-ముక్తా|విముక్తా|ముక్తా|ವಿ-ಮುಕ್ತಾ|ವಿಮುಕ್ತಾ|ಮುಕ್ತಾ|vi-muktā|vimuktā|muktā|vi-muktaa|vimuktaa|muktaa|_vei_120@2970|_vei2_pn304
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2969">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2971">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn303">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn304">2:304</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn305">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-muktā</b> (lit., ‘secreted’), ‘pearl,’ is found in the late Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (v. 6).</p></div>

वि-मोक्तृ|विमोक्तृ|मोक्तृ|వి-మోక్తృ|విమోక్తృ|మోక్తృ|ವಿ-ಮೋಕ್ತೃ|ವಿಮೋಕ್ತೃ|ಮೋಕ್ತೃ|vi-moktṛ|vimoktṛ|moktṛ|vi-moktR|vimoktR|moktR|vi-moktRi|vimoktRi|moktRi|vi-moktRu|vimoktRu|moktRu|vi-moktRRi|vimoktRRi|moktRRi|_vei_120@2971|_vei2_pn304
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2970">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2972">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn303">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn304">2:304</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn305">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-moktṛ</b> in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202971"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202971">1</a></sup> (‘human sacrifice’) denotes one who unharnesses horses from the chariot, as opposed to Yoktṛ, ‘one who yokes.’ The corresponding verbal noun Vimocana, ‘unyoking,’ is often found.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202971"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202971">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202971"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202971">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 14; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 10, 1 (<i>cf. vimoktrī,</i>) used metaphorically, <i>ibid.,</i> iii. 7. 14, 1).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202971"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202971">↑</a> Rv. iii. 53, 5, 20; iv. 46, 7, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 1, 5, etc.</li></ol></div>

वि-राज्|विराज्|राज्|వి-రాజ్|విరాజ్|రాజ్|ವಿ-ರಾಜ್|ವಿರಾಜ್|ರಾಜ್|vi-rāj|virāj|rāj|vi-raaj|viraaj|raaj|_vei_120@2972|_vei2_pn304
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2971">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2973">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn303">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn304">2:304</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn305">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-rāj</b> as a title of royalty is mentioned several times in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202972"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202972">1</a></sup> but only in a metaphorical sense. As an actual title, it is asserted in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202972"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202972">2</a></sup> to be used by the <b>Uttara Kurus</b> and the <b>Uttara Madras.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202972"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202972">↑</a> i. 188, 5; ix. 96, 18; x. 166, 1, etc.; Av. xii. 3, 11; xiv. 2, 15, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202972"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202972">↑</a> viii. 14, 3.</li></ol></div>

वि-रूप|विरूप|रूप|వి-రూప|విరూప|రూప|ವಿ-ರೂಪ|ವಿರೂಪ|ರೂಪ|vi-rūpa|virūpa|rūpa|vi-ruupa|viruupa|ruupa|vi-roopa|viroopa|roopa|_vei_120@2973|_vei2_pn304
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2972">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2974">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn303">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn304">2:304</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn305">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-rūpa</b> is the name of an Aṅgirasa who is twice mentioned in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202973"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202973">1</a></sup> and to whom certain hymns are attributed by the Anukramaṇī (Index).</p><p>^2 viii. 43 <i>et seq.;</i> 64.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202973"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202973">↑</a> i. 45, 3; viii. 75, 6.</li></ol></div>

विलिगी|विलिगि|విలిగీ|విలిగి|ವಿಲಿಗೀ|ವಿಲಿಗಿ|viligī|viligi|viligii|viligee|_vei_120@2974|_vei2_pn304
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2973">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2975">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn303">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn304">2:304</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn305">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viligī</b> denotes a kind of snake in the Atharvaveda (v. 13, 7).</p></div>

विलिष्ट-भेषज|विलिष्टभेषज|विलिष्ट|भेषज|విలిష్ట-భేషజ|విలిష్టభేషజ|విలిష్ట|భేషజ|ವಿಲಿಷ್ಟ-ಭೇಷಜ|ವಿಲಿಷ್ಟಭೇಷಜ|ವಿಲಿಷ್ಟ|ಭೇಷಜ|viliṣṭa-bheṣaja|viliṣṭabheṣaja|viliṣṭa|bheṣaja|viliShTa-bheShaja|viliShTabheShaja|viliShTa|bheShaja|_vei_120@2975|_vei2_pn304
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2974">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2976">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn303">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn304">2:304</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn305">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viliṣṭa-bheṣaja</b> in the Atharvaveda (Paippalāda, xx. 5, 2) denotes a remedy for a dislocation or a sprain. </p></div>

वि-लोहित|विलोहित|लोहित|వి-లోహిత|విలోహిత|లోహిత|ವಿ-ಲೋಹಿತ|ವಿಲೋಹಿತ|ಲೋಹಿತ|vi-lohita|vilohita|lohita|_vei_120@2976|_vei2_pn305
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2975">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2977">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn304">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn305">2:305</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn307">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-lohita</b> is the name of a disease mentioned in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202976"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202976">1</a></sup> Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202976"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202976">2</a></sup> thinks that ‘flow of blood from the nose’ is meant; Henry<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202976"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202976">3</a></sup> renders it ‘decomposition of the blood’; and Whitney<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202976"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202976">4</a></sup> has ‘anæmia.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202976"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202976">↑</a> ix. 8, 1; xii. 4, 4</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202976"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202976">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 657.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202976"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202976">↑</a> <i>Les livres viii. et ix. de l' Atharvaveda,</i> 105, 142.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202976"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202976">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda 549.</li></ol></div>

वि-वध|विवध|वध|वी-वध|वीवध|వి-వధ|వివధ|వధ|వీ-వధ|వీవధ|ವಿ-ವಧ|ವಿವಧ|ವಧ|ವೀ-ವಧ|ವೀವಧ|vi-vadha|vivadha|vadha|vī-vadha|vīvadha|vii-vadha|viivadha|vee-vadha|veevadha|_vei_120@2977|_vei2_pn305
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2976">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2978">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn304">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn305">2:305</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn307">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-vadha</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वी-वध">vī-vadha</a></b> seems to denote a yoke borne on the shoulders to enable one to carry a weight. But it is found in the Brāhmaṇas used only metaphorically in such phrases as <i>vi-<a class="vei_120a" href="विवध">vivadha</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202977"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202977">1</a></sup> ‘with the weight unequally distributed,’ and <i>savīvadhatā,</i><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202977"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202977">2</a></sup> ‘equality of burden.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202977"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202977">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 2, 5, 2; 7, 3; <i>vivīvadha,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 5, 19; <i>ubhayato -<a class="vei_120a" href="वीवध">vīvadha</a>,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxvii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202977"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202977">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 1, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 1, 10; <i>sa-<a class="vei_120a" href="वीवध">vīvadha</a>-tva,</i> v. 1, 11: xxii. 5, 7, etc.</li></ol></div>

वि-वयन|विवयन|वयन|వి-వయన|వివయన|వయన|ವಿ-ವಯನ|ವಿವಯನ|ವಯನ|vi-vayana|vivayana|vayana|_vei_120@2978|_vei2_pn305
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2977">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2979">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn304">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn305">2:305</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn307">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-vayana</b> denotes in the Brāhmaṇas ‘plaited work,’ such as that used in a couch (<b>Asandī</b>).</p><p>^1 <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 5, 3; Śatapatha Brāhmaṇā, xii. 8, 3, 6. In the Sūtras <i>vivāna</i> has the same sense; Lāṭyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii, 12, 1, etc.</p></div>

वि-वाह|विवाह|वाह|వి-వాహ|వివాహ|వాహ|ವಿ-ವಾಹ|ವಿವಾಹ|ವಾಹ|vi-vāha|vivāha|vāha|vi-vaaha|vivaaha|vaaha|_vei_120@2979|_vei2_pn305
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2978">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2980">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn304">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn305">2:305</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn307">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-vāha,</b> ‘marriage,’ is mentioned in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202979"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202979">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202979"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202979">2</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202979"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202979">↑</a> xii. 1, 24; xiv. 2, 65. The Rigvedic term is <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वहतु">vahatu</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202979"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202979">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 2, 8, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxv. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 10, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv, 27, 5, and often in the Sūtras.</li></ol></div>

विश्|విశ్|ವಿಶ್|viś|vish|_vei_120@2980|_vei2_pn305
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2979">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2981">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn304">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn305">2:305</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn307">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viś</b> is an expression of somewhat doubtful significance. In many passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202980">1</a></sup> the sense of ‘settlement’ or ‘dwelling’ is adequate and probable, since the root <i><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a></i> means to ‘enter’ or ‘settle.’ In other passages, where the Viśaḥ  stand in relation to a prince, the term must mean ‘subject’;<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202980">2</a></sup> so, for example, when the people of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तृणस्कन्द">tṛṇaskanda</a></b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202980">3</a></sup> or of the <b>Tṛtsus</b> are mentioned.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202980">4</a></sup> Again, in some passages<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202980">5</a></sup> the general sense of ‘people’ is adequate; as when the Rigveda speaks of the ‘Āryan people,’<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202980">6</a></sup> or the ‘divine people,’<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202980">7</a></sup> or the ‘Dāsa people,’ and so on.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202980">8</a></sup></p><p>Sometimes,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202980">9</a></sup> however, the <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> appear in a more special sense as a subdivision of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a></b> or whole people. This is, however, not common, for in most passages one or other of the senses given above is quite possible. Moreover, it is very difficult to decide whether the <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> as a subdivision of the <a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a> is to be considered as being a local subdivision (canton) or a blood kinship equivalent to a clan in the large sense of the word, while the relation of the <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a></b> or to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a></b> is quite uncertain. In one passage of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202980">10</a></sup> the Viśaḥ are mentioned along with the <i>sabandhavaḥ</i> or relatives, but no definite conclusion can be drawn from that fact. Nor does the analogy of the Roman <i>curia</i> or the Greek  throw much light, as these institutions are themselves of obscure character, and the parallelism need not be cogent. It is, at any rate, possible that the <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> may in some cases have been no more than a <a class="vei_120a" href="गोत्र">gotra</a> or clan, or different clans may sometirnes  have made up a <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>, while <a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a> is more definitely, perhaps, a local designation. But the Vedic evidence is quite inconclusive.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202980">11</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्पति">viśpati</a>.</b></p><p>In the later period the sense of <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> is definitely restricted in some cases<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202980">12</a></sup> to denote the third of the classes of the Vedic polity, the people or clansmen as opposed to the nobles (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्र">kṣatra</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a></b>) and the priests (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a></b>). For the position of this class, see <b>Vaiśva.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202980">↑</a> iv. 4, 3; 37, 1; v. 3. 5; vi. 21, 4; 48, 8; vii. 56, 22; 61, 3, 70, 3; 104, 18; x. 91, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202980">↑</a> Rv. iv. 50, 8; vi. 8, 4; x. 124, 8; 173, 6; Av. iii. 4, 1; iv. 8, 4; 22, 1. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 2, 8, 6; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, viii. 46; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 2. 17; iv. 2, 1, 3; v. 3, 3, 12; 4, 2, 3; x. 6, 2, 1; xiii. 6, 2, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv, 12, etc. Many of the passages cited under note 11 may also belong here, while in Av. iii. 4, 1, etc., reference to the cantons as electing a king has been seen; but see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a></b> and <i>cf.</i> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 179; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 303; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 113.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202980">↑</a> Rv. i. 172, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202980">↑</a> Rv. vii. 33, 6; Geldner, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 136.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202980">↑</a> E.g., Rv. vi. 1, 8; 26, 1; viii. 71, 11; <i>manuṣo viśāḥ,</i> vi. 14, 2; viii. 23, 13; <i>mānuṣīḥ,</i> x. 80, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202980">↑</a> Rv. x. 11, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202980">↑</a> Rv. iii. 34, 2; Av. vi. 98, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvii. 86.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202980">↑</a> Rv. iv. 28, 4; vi. 25, 2; <i>adevīḥ,</i> viii. 96, 15; <i>asiknīḥ,</i> vii. 5, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202980">↑</a> Rv. ii. 26, 3, where it is opposed to <i><a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="जन्मन्">janman</a>,</i> and <i>putrāḥ;</i> x. 84, 4, where in battle <i>viśaṃ-viśam</i> apparently refers to divisions of the host <i>cf.</i> also iv. 24, 4. <i>viśo yudhmāḥ</i>); x. 91, 2, where it is opposed to <i><a class="vei_120a" href="गृह">gṛha</a></i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a>;</i> Av. xiv. 2, 27, where <i>gṛhebhyaḥ</i> is followed by <i>asyai sarvasyai viśe,</i> which must mean a division less than a whole people. Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 159, reckons here Rv. i. 172, 3; vii. 33, 6; ix. 7, 5; x. 124, 8; 173, 1; but these cases and many others are rather instances of ‘subjects’ than of a division of the tribe such as ‘canton.’</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202980">↑</a> xv. 8, 2, 3, <i>Cf.</i> xiv. 2, 27, and Rv. x. 91, 2, in n. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202980">↑</a> The <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> may have been oringinally a clan settled in one place: there is no passage where ‘Gotra’ would not probably make sense; Rv. ii. 26, 3, cannot be pressed unduly to distinguish <i>ianman</i> and <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>. Compare the phrase used of the Maruts <i>śardhaṃ śardham, vrātaṃ vrātam, gaṇaṃ gaṇam,</i> in Rv. v. 53. 11, where no precise sense can fairly be attributed to the words, though Zimmer sees in them a threefold division of the host corresponding to <a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a>. The rendering ‘Gau’ has therefore little foundation.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202980"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202980">↑</a> Perhaps to this sense belong the numerous passages in the Brāhmaṇas and later Saṃhitās referring to strife between the <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्र">kṣatra</a>, the clansmen and the chiefs, or the peasantry and the nobles--<i>e.g.,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 11, 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii, 1, 9; iii. 3, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhita, xix. 9 and often. See also <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvii. 10, 9; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 3, 5; viii. 7, 2, 3; xiii. 2, 2, 17. 19; 9, 6; xiv. 1, 3, 27, etc.; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, viii. 14.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Ātindisches Leben,</i> 15 <i>et seq.;</i> Schrader. <i>Prehistoric Autiquities,</i> 800 <i>et seq.;</i> Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 158; von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literalur und Cultur,</i> 32, 33; St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> For the Roman <i>curia,</i> which was apparently a collection of <i>gentes,</i> perhaps local, <i>cf.</i> Mommsen, <i>History of Rome,</i> 1, 72, <i>et seq.; Ro7mische Forschungen,</i> 1. 140-150; <i>Ro7misches Staatsrecht,</i> 3, 9; Taylor, <i>History of Rome.</i> 11, 12; Smith, <i>Dictionary of Antiquities,</i> 1, 576; Cuq, <i>Les institutions juridiques des Romains,</i> 30-36, For the Greek Phratria which was probably similar in character, consisting of a union of  see <i>Dictionary of Antiquities,</i> 2, 876 <i>et seq.;</i> Greenidge, <i>Greek Constitutional History,</i> 128 <i>et seq.;</i> Bury, <i>History of Greece,</i> 69, 70; Gilbert, <i>Greek Constitutional Antiquities,</i> 1, 104 <i>et seq.,</i> 210. For the English hundreds, and the supposed analogy of the <i>pagi</i> of Tacitus, see the references in Medley, <i>English Constitutional History,^2</i> 318 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

वि-शर|विशर|शर|వి-శర|విశర|శర|ವಿ-ಶರ|ವಿಶರ|ಶರ|vi-śara|viśara|śara|vi-shara|vishara|shara|_vei_120@2981|_vei2_pn307
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2980">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2982">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn305">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn307">2:307</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn308">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-śara</b> is found as the name of a disease, perhaps ‘tearing pains,’ in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202981"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202981">1</a></sup> Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202981"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202981">2</a></sup> thinks that the pains in the limbs attendant on fever (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="तक्मन्">takman</a></b>) are alluded to. Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202981"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202981">3</a></sup> sees in the word the name of a demon. The view of Zimmer is supported by the use of <i>viśarīka,</i> ‘rending,’ beside <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बलास">balāsa</a></b> in another passage.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202981"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202981">4</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202981"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202981">↑</a> ii. 4, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202981"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202981">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben.</i> 391.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202981"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202981">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202981"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202981">↑</a> xix. 34, 10.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymas of the Athervaveda,</i> 284.</li></ol></div>

वि-शाखे|विशाखे|शाखे|వి-శాఖే|విశాఖే|శాఖే|ವಿ-ಶಾಖೇ|ವಿಶಾಖೇ|ಶಾಖೇ|vi-śākhe|viśākhe|śākhe|vi-shaakhe|vishaakhe|shaakhe|_vei_120@2982|_vei2_pn308
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2981">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2983">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn307">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn308">2:308</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn309">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-śākhe.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a></b></p></div>

विश्-पति|विश्पति|विश्|पति|విశ్-పతి|విశ్పతి|విశ్|పతి|ವಿಶ್-ಪತಿ|ವಿಶ್ಪತಿ|ವಿಶ್|ಪತಿ|viś-pati|viśpati|viś|pati|vish-pati|vishpati|vish|_vei_120@2983|_vei2_pn308
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2982">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2984">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn307">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn308">2:308</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn309">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viś-pati</b> is a word of somewhat uncertain signification, reflecting in this respect the nature of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>.</b> Zimmer holds that in its strict sense it denotes the head of a canton, but he admits that there is no passage requiring this sense, the only one quoted by him<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202983"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202983">2</a></sup> being certainly indecisive. In the great majority of passages the word simply means the ‘lord of the dwelling,’ whether used of a man or of the god <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> as the householder <i>par excellence,</i> or possibly as the fire of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a></b> or assembly house of the people. This sense suits even the passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202983"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202983">4</a></sup> in which the <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्पति">viśpati</a>, as well as the father and the mother of a maiden,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202983"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202983">5</a></sup> are to be lulled to sleep in order to allow her lover to approach her, for the household may well be deemed to have been a joint family, in which the <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्पति">viśpati</a> could easily be different from the father of the girl--<i>e.g.,</i> a grandfather or uncle. In other passages<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202983"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202983">6</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्पति">viśpati</a> is the king as ‘lord of the subject-people’ (<i>viśām</i>), though here Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202983"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202983">7</a></sup> thinks reference is made to the election of a king.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202983"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202983">8</a></sup> Or again,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202983"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202983">7</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्पति">viśpati</a> is the chief of the <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>, probably in the sense of ‘subjects.’</p><p>1) <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 171.</p><p>9) <i>E.g.,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 1, 3, where <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> must clearly be the people or subject class, and the <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्पति">viśpati</a> their chief representative; we cannot from such a passage infer a formal office of <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्पति">viśpati</a> even as head of the <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="2"><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202983"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202983">↑</a> Rv. i. 37. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202983"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202983">↑</a> vii. 55, 5 = Av. iv. 5, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202983"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202983">↑</a> So Aufrecht, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 337 <i>et seq.;</i> Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 308. <i>Cf.</i> Lanman, <i>Sanskrit Reader,</i> 370. Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 55 <i>et seq.,</i> accepts the view of the Bṛhaddevatā, vi. 11 <i>et seq.</i> (where see Macdonell's note), that the hymn refers to Vasiṣṭha's approaching a house as a thief! The interpretation does not affect the sense of <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्पति">viśpati</a>, which here is clearly not the title of a cantonal chief. <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a> is sometimes equivalent to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सजात">sajāta</a>;</b> <i>cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 3, 2. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202983"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202983">↑</a> Av. iii. 4, 1; iv. 22, 3. Perhaps Rv. iii. 13, 5, is so to be taken; <i>cf.</i> vii. 39, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202983"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202983">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 164, 165.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202983"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202983">↑</a> But see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202983"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202983">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 164. 165.</li></ol></div>

विश्पला|విశ్పలా|ವಿಶ್ಪಲಾ|viśpalā|vishpalaa|_vei_120@2984|_vei2_pn308
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2983">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2985">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn307">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn308">2:308</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn309">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viśpalā</b> is, according to the tradition in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202984"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202984">1</a></sup> the name of a woman to whom the Aśvins gave an iron (<i>āyasī</i>)  limb to replace one lost by her in a contest. Pischel<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202984"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202984">2</a></sup> considers that a racing horse miraculously cured of a broken limb by the Aśvins is meant, but this is no more than an improbable conjecture.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202984"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202984">↑</a> i. 112, 10: 116 15; 11. 11: 118. 8; x. 39, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202984"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202984">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1. 171-173.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> 52; Muir, <i>Saniskrit Texts,</i> 5, 245; Bloomfield, <i>Religion of the <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 113; Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 110, 111.</li></ol></div>

विश्वक|విశ్వక|ವಿಶ್ವಕ|viśvaka|vishvaka|_vei_120@2985|_vei2_pn309
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2984">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2986">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn308">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn309">2:309</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn310">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viśvaka,</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202985"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202985">1</a></sup> called Kṛṣṇiya (possibly ‘son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण">kṛṣṇa</a></b>’) is a protégé of the Aśvins, who restored to him his lost son, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विष्णापु">viṣṇāpu</a>.</b> See 2. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण">kṛṣṇa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202985"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202985">↑</a> i. 116, 23; 117. 7; viii. 86, 1; x. 65, 12. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> 52.</li></ol></div>

विश्व-कर्मन् भौवन|विश्वकर्मन् भौवन|विश्व|कर्मन् भौवन|విశ్వ-కర్మన్ భౌవన|విశ్వకర్మన్ భౌవన|విశ్వ|కర్మన్ భౌవన|ವಿಶ್ವ-ಕರ್ಮನ್ ಭೌವನ|ವಿಶ್ವಕರ್ಮನ್ ಭೌವನ|ವಿಶ್ವ|ಕರ್ಮನ್ ಭೌವನ|viśva-karman bhauvana|viśvakarman bhauvana|viśva|karman bhauvana|vishva-karman bhauvana|vishvakarman bhauvana|vishva|_vei_120@2986|_vei2_pn309
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2985">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2987">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn308">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn309">2:309</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn310">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viśva-karman Bhauvana</b> (‘descendant of Bhuvana’) is the name of a quite mythical king. He is said in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> to have been consecrated by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कश्यप">kaśyapa</a>,</b> to whom he offered the earth (<i>i.e.,</i> presumably a piece of land) as a sacrificial fee; in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202986"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202986">2</a></sup> he performed the Sarvamedha (‘universal sacrifice’), and made a similar offer; in both cases the earth refused to be given. The story seems to contain a reference to the early dislike of gifts of land,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202986"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202986">3</a></sup> but it cannot be stated with certainty that this is the meaning.</p><p>^1 viii. 21, 8.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="2"><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202986"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202986">↑</a> xiii. 7, 1, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202986"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202986">↑</a> Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India,</i> 47.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 421, n, 1; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 456, 457.</li></ol></div>

विश्वन्-तर सौ-षद्मन|विश्वन्-तर सौषद्मन|विश्वन्-तर सौ|षद्मन|విశ్వన్-తర సౌ-షద్మన|విశ్వన్-తర సౌషద్మన|విశ్వన్-తర సౌ|షద్మన|ವಿಶ್ವನ್-ತರ ಸೌ-ಷದ್ಮನ|ವಿಶ್ವನ್-ತರ ಸೌಷದ್ಮನ|ವಿಶ್ವನ್-ತರ ಸೌ|ಷದ್ಮನ|viśvan-tara sau-ṣadmana|viśvan-tara sauṣadmana|viśvan-tara sau|ṣadmana|vishvan-tara sau-Shadmana|vishvan-tara sauShadmana|vishvan-tara sau|Shadmana|_vei_120@2987|_vei2_pn309
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2986">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2988">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn308">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn309">2:309</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn310">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viśvan-<a class="vei_120a" href="तर">tara</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सौ">sau</a>-ṣadmana</b> (‘descendant of Suṣadman’) is the name in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202987"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202987">1</a></sup> of a prince who set aside the <b>Śyāparṇas,</b> his priests, and performed a sacrifice without their help, presumably with the aid of others. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राम मार्गवेय">rāma mārgaveya</a>,</b> one of the Śyāparṇas, however, succeeded in inducing the king to reinstate the Śyāparṇas, and to give him a thousand cows.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202987"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202987">↑</a> vii. 27, 3, 4; 34, 7, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 431-440; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 43, 344, n.</li></ol></div>

विश्व-मनस्|विश्व-मन|विश्वमनस्|विश्वमन|विश्व|मनस्|मन|విశ్వ-మనస్|విశ్వ-మన|విశ్వమనస్|విశ్వమన|విశ్వ|మనస్|మన|ವಿಶ್ವ-ಮನಸ್|ವಿಶ್ವ-ಮನ|ವಿಶ್ವಮನಸ್|ವಿಶ್ವಮನ|ವಿಶ್ವ|ಮನಸ್|ಮನ|viśva-manas|viśva-mana|viśvamanas|viśvamana|viśva|manas|mana|vishva-manas|vishva-mana|vishvamanas|vishvamana|vishva|_vei_120@2988|_vei2_pn309
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2987">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2989">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn308">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn309">2:309</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn310">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viśva-manas</b> is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> mentioned in two passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202988"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202988">1</a></sup> and as a frined of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> in the Pañcaviṃśa  <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202988"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202988">2</a></sup> According to the Anukramaṇī (Index), he was a descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्यश्व">vyaśva</a>,</b> and the author of certain hymns.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202988"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202988">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202988"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202988">↑</a> viii. 23, 2; 24, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202988"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202988">↑</a> xv. 5, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202988"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202988">↑</a> Rv. viii. 23-26.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 106.</li></ol></div>

विश्व-मानुष|विश्वमानुष|विश्व|मानुष|విశ్వ-మానుష|విశ్వమానుష|విశ్వ|మానుష|ವಿಶ್ವ-ಮಾನುಷ|ವಿಶ್ವಮಾನುಷ|ವಿಶ್ವ|ಮಾನುಷ|viśva-mānuṣa|viśvamānuṣa|viśva|mānuṣa|vishva-maanuSha|vishvamaanuSha|vishva|maanuSha|_vei_120@2989|_vei2_pn310
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2988">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2990">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn309">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn310">2:310</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn312">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viśva-mānuṣa</b> in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202989"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202989">1</a></sup> may be a proper name, but more probably merely means ‘all mankind.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202989"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202989">↑</a> viii. 45, 22. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 187.</li></ol></div>

विश्व-वार|विश्ववार|विश्व|वार|విశ్వ-వార|విశ్వవార|విశ్వ|వార|ವಿಶ್ವ-ವಾರ|ವಿಶ್ವವಾರ|ವಿಶ್ವ|ವಾರ|viśva-vāra|viśvavāra|viśva|vāra|vishva-vaara|vishvavaara|vishva|vaara|_vei_120@2990|_vei2_pn310
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2989">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2991">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn309">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn310">2:310</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn312">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viśva-vāra</b> occurs in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202990"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202990">1</a></sup> apparently as the name of a sacrificer.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202990"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202990">↑</a> v. 44, 11. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 138.</li></ol></div>

विश्वा-सामन्|विश्वासामन्|विश्वा|सामन्|విశ్వా-సామన్|విశ్వాసామన్|విశ్వా|సామన్|ವಿಶ್ವಾ-ಸಾಮನ್|ವಿಶ್ವಾಸಾಮನ್|ವಿಶ್ವಾ|ಸಾಮನ್|viśvā-sāman|viśvāsāman|viśvā|sāman|vishvaa-saaman|vishvaasaaman|vishvaa|saaman|_vei_120@2991|_vei2_pn310
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2990">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2992">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn309">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn310">2:310</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn312">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viśvā-sāman</b> is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>, an <b>Atreya,</b> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202991"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202991">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202991"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202991">↑</a> v. 22, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg. <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 215.</li></ol></div>

विश्व-सृज्|विश्वसृज्|विश्व|सृज्|విశ్వ-సృజ్|విశ్వసృజ్|విశ్వ|సృజ్|ವಿಶ್ವ-ಸೃಜ್|ವಿಶ್ವಸೃಜ್|ವಿಶ್ವ|ಸೃಜ್|viśva-sṛj|viśvasṛj|viśva|sṛj|vishva-sRj|vishvasRj|vishva|sRj|vishva-sRij|vishvasRij|sRij|vishva-sRuj|vishvasRuj|sRuj|vishva-sRRij|vishvasRRij|sRRij|_vei_120@2992|_vei2_pn310
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2991">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2993">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn309">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn310">2:310</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn312">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viśva-sṛj</b> is the name of certain mythical beings to whom, however, a Sattra, or sacrificial session, is ascribed in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xxv. 18, 1 <i>et seq.</i>).</p></div>

विश्वा-मित्र|विश्वामित्र|विश्वा|मित्र|విశ్వా-మిత్ర|విశ్వామిత్ర|విశ్వా|మిత్ర|ವಿಶ್ವಾ-ಮಿತ್ರ|ವಿಶ್ವಾಮಿತ್ರ|ವಿಶ್ವಾ|ಮಿತ್ರ|viśvā-mitra|viśvāmitra|viśvā|mitra|vishvaa-mitra|vishvaamitra|vishvaa|_vei_120@2993|_vei2_pn310
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2992">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2994">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn309">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn310">2:310</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn312">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viśvā-mitra</b> (‘friend of all’) is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> who is mentioned in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202993">1</a></sup> and to whom the third Maṇḍala is attributed by tradition. In one hymn<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202993">2</a></sup> which appears to be his own composition, he praises the rivers <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विपाश्">vipāś</a></b> (Beas) and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुतुद्री">śutudrī</a></b> (Sutlej). There he calls himself the son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुशिक">kuśika</a>,</b> and seems unquestionably to be the helper of the <b>Bharatas,</b> whom he mentions. The tribe, engaged in a raid, apparently came to the rivers from the east.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202993">4</a></sup> Anxious to cross them, they</p><p>^3 Rv. iii. 33, 5.  found them in high flood, but <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> by prayer induced the waters to subside. The same feat appears to be referred to in another passage of the same book of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202993">5</a></sup> Curiously enough, Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202993">6</a></sup> quite misunderstands the situation: according to him, <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> having obtained wealth by the exercise of his office, went off with it to the rivers, pursued by others. Yāska's<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1202993">7</a></sup> version of the tale merely seems to mean that the king paid <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> to act as his <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>,</b> or domestic priest. For the relations of <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> to <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a> connected with their service of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>, see <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>.</b></p><p>The Viśvāmitras are mentioned in several other passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1202993">8</a></sup> and are also designated as a family by the term <b>Kuśikas.</b><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1202993">9</a></sup></p><p>In the later literature <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> becomes, like <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>, a mythical sage, usually<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1202993">10</a></sup> mentioned in connection with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जमदग्नि">jamadagni</a>;</b> he was <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a> priest at the sacrifice of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a>,</b> whom he adopted, and to whom he gave the name of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="देवरात">devarāta</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1202993">11</a></sup> He was a protégé of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, with whom he had an interview according to the Rigveda Āraṇyakas.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1202993">12</a></sup> He is also often mentioned as a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>.<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1202993">13</a></sup></p><p>In the Epic<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1202993">14</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> is represented as a king, who becomes a Brahmin. There is no trace of his kingship in the Rigveda, but the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1202993">15</a></sup> calls his father, <a class="vei_120a" href="कुशिक">kuśika</a>, a king; the  <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1202993">16</a></sup> refers to <a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a> as succeeding to the lordship of the <b>Jahnus,</b> as well as the ‘divine lore’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="दैव">daiva</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a></i>) of the <b>Gāthins;</b> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1202993">17</a></sup> mentions <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> as a king. But there is no real-trace of this kingship of <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>: it may probably be dismissed as a mere legend, with no more foundation at most than that <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> was of a family which once had been royal. But even this is doubtful.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202993">↑</a> As son of <a class="vei_120a" href="कुशिक">kuśika</a> in Rv. iii. 33, 5; as <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> in iii. 53, 7, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202993">↑</a> iii. 33. Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 121, thinks the hymn too poetical to be a real composition of the reputed author.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202993">↑</a> So Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 152. Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 127, 128, takes a different view: with Roth, <i>Zur Litteratur und Geschichte des Weda,</i> 90, he assumes that the Bharatas were different from the Tṛtsus, and that they came under <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a> from the West, but were defeated (see Rv. vii. 33, 6). But see Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 34, n. 1; Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 136. Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, 41, 42, still defends Roth's view.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202993">↑</a> iii. 53, 9-11. This hymn is probably later.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202993">↑</a> Sāyaṇa on Rv. iii. 33.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1202993">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 24.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1202993">↑</a> iii. 1, 21; 18, 4; 53, 13; x. 89, 17; Av. xviii. 3, 6; 4, 54; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 15, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1202993">↑</a> Rv. iii. 26, 1. 3; 29, 15; 30, 20; 42, 9; 53, 9. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1202993">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. iii. 53, 15. 16; Ṣaḍguruśiṣya in Macdonell's edition of the Sarvānukramaṇī, p. 107; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 117; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 343; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 158 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1202993">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 16 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 17 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1202993">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka. ii. 2, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1202993">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 2, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 18, 1; 20, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 1, 2; iii. 1, 7, 3; v. 2, 3, 4, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvi. 19; xx. 9; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 7, 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xv. 1; xxvi. 14; xxviii. 1. 2; xxix. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 3, 12; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 2, 4; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 13; 15, 1, etc. <a class="vei_120a" href="जमदग्नि">jamadagni</a> is often associated with him, Av. iv. 29, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1202993">↑</a> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 388 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1202993">↑</a> ii. 24.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1202993">↑</a> vii. 18, 9. But the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 27, has a completely different version, which Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 16, n. 3, prefers, and which omits all allusion to the ‘lordship’ of the Jahnus. This shows how little stress can-be laid on this latetradition.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1202993"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1202993">↑</a> xxi. 12, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 121; Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 209, 210; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 337 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 16 <i>et seq.;</i> <i>Indian Literature,</i> 31, 37, 38, 53, etc.</li></ol></div>

विष|విష|ವಿಷ|viṣa|viSha|_vei_120@2994|_vei2_pn312
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2993">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2995">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn310">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn312">2:312</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn313">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viṣa</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202994"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202994">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202994"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202994">2</a></sup> regularly denotes ‘poison’ as an antidote, for which the Atharvaveda supplies spells.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202994"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202994">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202994"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202994">↑</a> i. 117, 16; 191, 11; vi. 61, 3; x. 87. 18, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202994"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202994">↑</a> Av. iv. 6, 2; v. 19, 10; vi. 90, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202994"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202994">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 61.</li></ol></div>

विष-विद्या|विषविद्या|विष|विद्या|విష-విద్యా|విషవిద్యా|విష|విద్యా|ವಿಷ-ವಿದ್ಯಾ|ವಿಷವಿದ್ಯಾ|ವಿಷ|ವಿದ್ಯಾ|viṣa-vidyā|viṣavidyā|viṣa|vidyā|viSha-vidyaa|viShavidyaa|viSha|vidyaa|_vei_120@2995|_vei2_pn312
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2994">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2996">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn310">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn312">2:312</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn313">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viṣa-vidyā,</b> the ‘science of poison,’ is enumerated with other sciences in the Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (x. 75). <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विद्या">vidyā</a>.</b></p></div>

विषाणा|విషాణా|ವಿಷಾಣಾ|viṣāṇā|viShaaNaa|_vei_120@2996|_vei2_pn312
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2995">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2997">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn310">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn312">2:312</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn313">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viṣāṇā</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202996">1</a></sup> and later^2 denotes an animal's ‘horn.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202996"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202996">↑</a> iii. 7, 1. 2; vi. 121, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 11, 10; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 3, 2, 17. Primarily a deciduous horn is meant. See Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 94.</li></ol></div>

विषाणका|విషాణకా|ವಿಷಾಣಕಾ|viṣāṇakā|viShaaNakaa|_vei_120@2997|_vei2_pn312
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2996">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2998">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn310">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn312">2:312</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn313">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viṣāṇakā</b> is the name of a plant in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202997">1</a></sup> Bloomfield,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202997">2</a></sup> however, thinks that the word may merely mean ‘horn.’ It is used as a remedy against the disease Vātīkāra.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202997">3</a></sup> That  disease is of doubtful character: Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1202997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1202997">4</a></sup> thinks that it is one ‘caused by wounds,’ comparing the adjective <i>a-<a class="vei_120a" href="वात">vāta</a>,</i> ‘uninjured,’ in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1202997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1202997">5</a></sup> but Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1202997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1202997">6</a></sup> shows that ‘wind’ in the body is meant as causing the disease.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202997">↑</a> vi. 44, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Viṣāṇikā in Wise, <i>Hindu System of Medicine,</i> 146, perhaps the <i>Asclepias geminata;</i> Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 12, 426; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 68. But <i>cf.</i> Whitney. Translation of the Atharvaveda, 313.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202997">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 482.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202997">↑</a> Av. ix. 8, 20; Vātī-<a class="vei_120a" href="कृत">kṛta</a>, vi. 44, 3; 109, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1202997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1202997">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 389. 390.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1202997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1202997">↑</a> vi. 16, 20; ix. 96, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1202997"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1202997">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 481 <i>et seq.,</i> 516.</li></ol></div>

विषाणिन्|విషాణిన్|ವಿಷಾಣಿನ್|viṣāṇin|viShaaNin|_vei_120@2998|_vei2_pn313
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2997">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2999">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn312">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn313">2:313</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn314">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viṣāṇin</b> occurs once in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202998"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202998">1</a></sup> as the name of a tribe in the list of the enemies of the <b>Tṛtsus,</b> not as Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202998"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202998">2</a></sup> thought, of their allies. The word seems to mean ‘having horns,’ but in what sense is unknown; perhaps their helmets were horn-shaped or ornamented with horns. They may, like their allies, the <b>Alinas, <a class="vei_120a" href="भलानस्">bhalānas</a>, Śivas,</b> and <b>Pakthas,</b> be reckoned as belonging to the tribes of the north-west.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202998"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202998">↑</a> vii. 18, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202998"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202998">↑</a> <i>Zur Litteratur und Geschichte des Weda,</i> 95; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 126. But Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 430, 431, altered his view, and Hopkins' criticism, overlooking this retractation, in the <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 260, 261, is so far unjustified.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 173.</li></ol></div>

विषूवन्त्|विषूवन्त्|విషూవన్త్_౧|విషూవన్త్|ವಿಷೂವನ್ತ್_೧|ವಿಷೂವನ್ತ್|viṣūvant_1|viṣūvant|viShuuvant_1|viShuuvant|viShoovant_1|viShoovant|_vei_120@2999|_vei2_pn313
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2998">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3000">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn312">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn313">2:313</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn314">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Viṣūvant</b> denotes in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1202999"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1202999">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1202999"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1202999">2</a></sup> the middle day in the Sattra or sacrificial session of a year's duration. Tilak<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1202999"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1202999">3</a></sup> argues that the <a class="vei_120a" href="विषूवन्त्">viṣūvant</a> literally means the day when night and daylight are equal--<i>i.e.,</i> the equinoctial day--and that this is the true sense of the word. But the theory is without probability.</p><p>^4 <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, ixxxiii. <i>et seq.</i></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1202999"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1202999">↑</a> xi. 7, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1202999"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1202999">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 5, 2; 7, 1; v. 9, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 41, 4; iv. 18, 1; 22, 1. 2; vi. 18, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 1; xxvi. 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 3, 2; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 1, 2, 2; 3, 14. 23; 4, 2; 2, 1, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1202999"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1202999">↑</a> <i>Orion,</i> 21, 22.</li></ol></div>

विषूवन्त्|विषूवन्त्|విషూవన్త్_౨|విషూవన్త్|ವಿಷೂವನ್ತ್_೨|ವಿಷೂವನ್ತ್|viṣūvant_2|viṣūvant|viShuuvant_2|viShuuvant|viShoovant_2|viShoovant|_vei_120@3000|_vei2_pn313
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@2999">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3001">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn312">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn313">2:313</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn314">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Viṣūvant</b> occurs in the description of the house in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203000"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203000">1</a></sup> The meaning seems to be tha ‘ridge of the roof.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203000"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203000">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203000"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203000">↑</a> ix. 3, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203000"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203000">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer. <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 151 (who thinks it is a metaphor from the parting of the hair); Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 598; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 526.</li></ol></div>

विषूचिका|విషూచికా|ವಿಷೂಚಿಕಾ|viṣūcikā|viShuuchikaa|viShoochikaa|_vei_120@3001|_vei2_pn314
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3000">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3002">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn313">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn314">2:314</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn315">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viṣūcikā</b> is the name of a disease mentioned in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203001"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203001">1</a></sup> as a result of over-indulgence in <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a></b> drinking. It seems clearly to be ‘dyser ery,’ or, as Wise calls it, ‘sporadic cholera.’ The term apparently means ‘causing evacuations in both directions.’</p><p>2) <i>Hindu System of Medicine,</i> 330.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 275, 392.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203001"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203001">↑</a> xix. 10 = Maitrāyaḥī Saṃhitā, iii. 11, 7 = <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 18 = <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 1, 5 = Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 7, 3, 2.</li></ol></div>

वि-ष्कन्ध|विष्कन्ध|ष्कन्ध|వి-ష్కన్ధ|విష్కన్ధ|ష్కన్ధ|ವಿ-ಷ್ಕನ್ಧ|ವಿಷ್ಕನ್ಧ|ಷ್ಕನ್ಧ|vi-ṣkandha|viṣkandha|ṣkandha|vi-Shkandha|viShkandha|Shkandha|_vei_120@3002|_vei2_pn314
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3001">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3003">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn313">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn314">2:314</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn315">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-ṣkandha</b> occurs severai times in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203002">1</a></sup> as the name of a disease. As rem dies against it a lead amulet,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203002">2</a></sup> or hemp,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203002">3</a></sup> or a salve,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203002">4</a></sup> or the <b>Ja giḍa</b> plant are recommended for use.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203002">5</a></sup> Weber<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203002">6</a></sup> suggests that the disease meant is ‘rheumatism,’ because it draws the shoulders apart (<i>vi-<a class="vei_120a" href="स्कन्ध">skandha</a></i>), but Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203002">7</a></sup> thinks that it is rather the name of a demon, like the Rigvedic Vyaṃśa<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203002">8</a></sup> and Vigrīva,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203002">9</a></sup> both of which are similarly formed and are names of demons. Possibly Karśapha and Viśapha mentioned in one hymn<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203002">10</a></sup> are plants used to cure the disease.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203002">↑</a> i. 16, 3; ii. 4, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> iii. 9, 2. 6; iv. 9, 5; xix. 34, 5. It is also found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 3, 11, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203002">↑</a> Av. i. 16, 3. <i>Cf.</i> ii. 4; iii. 9, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203002">↑</a> Av. ii. 4, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203002">↑</a> Av. iv. 9, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203002">↑</a> Av. ii. 4, 1. 5; xix. 34. 5; 35, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203002">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 410; 13, 141; 17, 215. See Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 390, 391; Grill, <i>Hundert Lieder,^2</i> 75.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203002">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 282, 283.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203002">↑</a> Rv. i. 32, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203002">↑</a> Rv. viii. 4, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203002"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203002">↑</a> Av. iii. 9, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 340. Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> thinks demons are meant: this seems the more probable view.</li></ol></div>

वि-ष्टारिन्|विष्टारिन्|ष्टारिन्|వి-ష్టారిన్|విష్టారిన్|ష్టారిన్|ವಿ-ಷ್ಟಾರಿನ್|ವಿಷ್ಟಾರಿನ್|ಷ್ಟಾರಿನ್|vi-ṣṭārin|viṣṭārin|ṣṭārin|vi-ShTaarin|viShTaarin|ShTaarin|_vei_120@3003|_vei2_pn314
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3002">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3004">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn313">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn314">2:314</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn315">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-ṣṭārin</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203003"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203003">1</a></sup> denotes a special sort of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ओदन">odana</a></b> or porridge.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203003"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203003">↑</a> iv. 34, 1 <i>et seq.</i> According to Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 206, the designation ‘outspread’ is due to the fact that the rice mess was kneaded into furrows and juices (<i>rasa</i>) were poured into them. See <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, lxvi. 6.</li></ol></div>

विष्ठा-व्राजिन्|विष्ठाव्राजिन्|विष्ठा|व्राजिन्|విష్ఠా-వ్రాజిన్|విష్ఠావ్రాజిన్|విష్ఠా|వ్రాజిన్|ವಿಷ್ಠಾ-ವ್ರಾಜಿನ್|ವಿಷ್ಠಾವ್ರಾಜಿನ್|ವಿಷ್ಠಾ|ವ್ರಾಜಿನ್|viṣṭhā-vrājin|viṣṭhāvrājin|viṣṭhā|vrājin|viShThaa-vraajin|viShThaavraajin|viShThaa|vraajin|_vei_120@3004|_vei2_pn314
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3003">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3005">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn313">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn314">2:314</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn315">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viṣṭhā-vrājin</b> is a word of doubtful significance in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203004">1</a></sup> According to Sāyaṇa, it means ‘remaining in one and the same place’; if this is right, the rendering of the  St. Petersburg Dictionary and of Bo7htlingk's Dictionary, ‘one whose herd is stationary,’ seems legitimate. But, as Eggeling<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203004">2</a></sup> points out, the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> in another passage<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203004">3</a></sup> seems to treat the word as denoting a disease: thus <a class="vei_120a" href="विष्ठाव्राजिन्">viṣṭhāvrājin</a> may mean ‘one afflicted by dysentery.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203004">↑</a> v. 1, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203004">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 123, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203004"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203004">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 50, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

विष्णापु|విష్ణాపు|ವಿಷ್ಣಾಪು|viṣṇāpu|viShNaapu|_vei_120@3005|_vei2_pn315
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3004">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3006">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn314">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn315">2:315</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn316">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viṣṇāpu</b> is the son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वक">viśvaka</a></b> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203005"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203005">1</a></sup> When lost he was restored to his father by the Aśvins.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203005"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203005">↑</a> i. 116, 23; 117, 7; viii. 86, 3; x. 65, 12.</li></ol></div>

विष्फुलिंग|विष्फुलिङ्ग|విష్ఫులింగ|విష్ఫులిఙ్గ|ವಿಷ್ಫುಲಿಂಗ|ವಿಷ್ಫುಲಿಙ್ಗ|viṣphuliṅga|viShphuliMga|viShphulinga|_vei_120@3006|_vei2_pn315
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3005">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3007">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn314">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn315">2:315</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn316">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viṣphuliṅga</b> denotes a ‘spark’ of fire in the Upaniṣads.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203006">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203006"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203006">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii, 1, 23; vi. 1, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 3; iv. 20, etc. <i>Cf. viṣpuliṅgaka,</i> ‘scattering sparks of fire,’ in Rv. i. 191, 12 (Sāyaṇa, ‘a tongue of fire,’ or ‘sparrow’).</li></ol></div>

विष्वक्-सेन|विष्वक्सेन|विष्वक्|सेन|విష్వక్-సేన|విష్వక్సేన|విష్వక్|సేన|ವಿಷ್ವಕ್-ಸೇನ|ವಿಷ್ವಕ್ಸೇನ|ವಿಷ್ವಕ್|ಸೇನ|viṣvak-sena|viṣvaksena|viṣvak|sena|viShvak-sena|viShvaksena|viShvak|_vei_120@3007|_vei2_pn315
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3006">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3008">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn314">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn315">2:315</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn316">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viṣvak-sena</b> is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नारद">nārada</a>,</b> mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) at the end of the Sāmavidhāna <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.</p></div>

विसल्य|విసల్య|ವಿಸಲ್ಯ|visalya|_vei_120@3008|_vei2_pn315
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3007">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3009">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn314">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn315">2:315</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn316">↷</a><hr><p><b>Visalya</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203008">1</a></sup> and <b>Visalyaka</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203008">2</a></sup> are names of a disease in the Atharvaveda. Since Shaṅkar Paṇḍit's reciters<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203008">3</a></sup> pronounced the word as Visalpaka in all the passages, that should probably be adopted as the right reading.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203008">4</a></sup> Some sort of pain is meant, perhaps ‘neuralgia,’ in connexion with fever.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203008">↑</a> ix. 8, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203008">↑</a> vi. 127, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> ix. 8, 2. 5; xix. 44, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203008">↑</a> See Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 601; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 376. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 378, 384.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203008"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203008">↑</a> The commentator Sāyaṇa on vi. 127 reads <i>visalpakaḥ,</i> and on xix. 44, 2, <i>visarpakaḥ.</i></li></ol></div>

वि-स्रस्|वि-स्र|विस्रस्|विस्र|स्रस्|स्र|వి-స్రస్|వి-స్ర|విస్రస్|విస్ర|స్రస్|స్ర|ವಿ-ಸ್ರಸ್|ವಿ-ಸ್ರ|ವಿಸ್ರಸ್|ವಿಸ್ರ|ಸ್ರಸ್|ಸ್ರ|vi-sras|vi-sra|visras|visra|sras|sra|sra|_vei_120@3009|_vei2_pn315
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3008">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3010">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn314">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn315">2:315</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn316">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vi-sras</b> denotes the ‘decay’ of old age, ‘decrepitude,’ ‘senility.’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203009"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203009">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203009"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203009">↑</a> Av. xix. 34, 3, where Bohtlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> suggests for <i>visrasas</i> the emendation <i>visruhas</i> (<i>cf.</i> Rv. vi. 7, 6); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8, 20, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 3, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 20, 7; Kāthaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4.</li></ol></div>

विहल्ह|విహల్హ|ವಿಹಲ್ಹ|vihalha|_vei_120@3010|_vei2_pn316
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3009">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3011">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn315">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn316">2:316</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn317">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vihalha</b> is found in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203010"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203010">1</a></sup> apparently as the name of a plant. The forms Vihaṃla and Vihahla occur as variants.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203010"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203010">↑</a> vi. 16, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 72.</li></ol></div>

वीणा|వీణా|ವೀಣಾ|vīṇā|viiNaa|veeNaa|_vei_120@3011|_vei2_pn316
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3010">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3012">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn315">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn316">2:316</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn317">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vīṇā</b> in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203011">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203011">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘lute.’ A <a class="vei_120a" href="वीणा-वाद">vīṇā-vāda</a>, ‘lute-player,’ is included in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203011">3</a></sup> and is also mentioned elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203011">4</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203011">5</a></sup> which states that the instrument was once covered with a hairy skin, enumerates its parts as <a class="vei_120a" href="शिरस्">śiras</a>, ‘head’ (<i>i.e.,</i> neck); Udara, ‘cavity’; Ambhaṇa, ‘sounding board’; <a class="vei_120a" href="तन्त्र">tantra</a>, ‘string’; and <a class="vei_120a" href="वादन">vādana</a>, ‘plectrum.’ In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203011">6</a></sup> the Uttaramandrā is either a tune or a kind of lute. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वाण">vāṇa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203011">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 4, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxiv. 5; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 6, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203011">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 4, 6; xiii. 1, 5, 1; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शत">śata</a>-tantrī,</i> ‘hundredstringed’ (like the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वाण">vāṇa</a></b>), at the Mahāvrata rite, <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvii. 3, 1, etc.; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 42 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 235).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203011">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 20; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 15, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203011">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 4, 8; iv. 5, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203011">↑</a> iii. 2, 5; <i>cf.</i> Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, viii. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203011"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203011">↑</a> xiii. 4, 2, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 356, n. 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 289; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 328; von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 755.</li></ol></div>

वीणा-गाथिन्|वीणागाथिन्|वीणा|गाथिन्|వీణా-గాథిన్|వీణాగాథిన్|వీణా|గాథిన్|ವೀಣಾ-ಗಾಥಿನ್|ವೀಣಾಗಾಥಿನ್|ವೀಣಾ|ಗಾಥಿನ್|vīṇā-gāthin|vīṇāgāthin|vīṇā|gāthin|viiNaa-gaathin|viiNaagaathin|viiNaa|gaathin|veeNaa-gaathin|veeNaagaathin|veeNaa|_vei_120@3012|_vei2_pn316
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3011">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3013">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn315">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn316">2:316</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn317">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vīṇā-gāthin</b> denotes ‘lute-player’ in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203012"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203012">1</a></sup> In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> Vīṇāgaṇagin denotes the ‘leader of a band.’</p><p>2) xiii. 4, 3, 3; 4, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 1, 29.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203012"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203012">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 9, 14, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 1, 5, 1; 4, 2, 8. 11. 14; 3, 5.</li></ol></div>

वीणा-वाद|वीणावाद|वीणा|वाद|వీణా-వాద|వీణావాద|వీణా|వాద|ವೀಣಾ-ವಾದ|ವೀಣಾವಾದ|ವೀಣಾ|ವಾದ|vīṇā-vāda|vīṇāvāda|vīṇā|vāda|viiNaa-vaada|viiNaavaada|viiNaa|vaada|veeNaa-vaada|veeNaavaada|veeNaa|_vei_120@3013|_vei2_pn316
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3012">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3014">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn315">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn316">2:316</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn317">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vīṇā-vāda.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वीणा">vīṇā</a>.</b></p></div>

वीत-हव्य|वीतहव्य|वीत|हव्य|వీత-హవ్య|వీతహవ్య|వీత|హవ్య|ವೀತ-ಹವ್ಯ|ವೀತಹವ್ಯ|ವೀತ|ಹವ್ಯ|vīta-havya|vītahavya|vīta|havya|viita-havya|viitahavya|viita|veeta-havya|veetahavya|veeta|_vei_120@3014|_vei2_pn316
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3013">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3015">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn315">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn316">2:316</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn317">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vīta-havya</b> is the name of a prince who is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203014"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203014">1</a></sup> along with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a>,</b> and as a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203014"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203014">2</a></sup> though in both passages it is possible to understand the  word as a mere adjective. In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203014"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203014">3</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="वीतहव्य">vītahavya</a> appears as connected with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जमदग्नि">jamadagni</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="असित">asita</a>,</b> but it is clear that the legend there has no value. It is possible, though not certain, that he was a king of the <b>Sṛṇjayas.</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203014"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203014">4</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203014"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203014">5</a></sup> a <a class="vei_120a" href="वीतहव्य">vītahavya</a> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्रायस">śrāyasa</a></b> appears as a king: he may be identical with the <a class="vei_120a" href="वीतहव्य">vītahavya</a> of the Rigveda, or belong to the same line. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैतहव्य">vaitahavya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203014"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203014">↑</a> vi. 15, 2. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203014"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203014">↑</a> vii. 19, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203014"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203014">↑</a> vi. 137, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203014"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203014">↑</a> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 105.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203014"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203014">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 6, 5, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxii. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 16, 3. <i>Ibid.,</i> ix. 1, 9, he is represented as being <i>niruddha,</i> apparently in ‘banishment’; but the scholiast explains him as not a king, but a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>, which is quite possible.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 212; <i>Buddha,</i> 405.</li></ol></div>

वीर|వీర|ವೀರ|vīra|viira|veera|_vei_120@3015|_vei2_pn317
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3014">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3016">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn316">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn317">2:317</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn318">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vīra</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203015"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203015">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203015"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203015">2</a></sup> denotes ‘man’ as the strong and heroic. Collectively in the singular<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203015"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203015">3</a></sup> the word denotes ‘male offspring,’ an object of great desire (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुत्र">putra</a></b>) to the Vedic Indian. The <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203015"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203015">4</a></sup> gives a list of eight Vīras of the king, constituting his supporters and entourage.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203015"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203015">↑</a> i. 18, 4; 114, 8; iv. 29, 2; v. 20, 4; 61, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203015"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203015">↑</a> Av. ii. 26, 4; iii. 5, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203015"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203015">↑</a> Rv. ii. 32, 4; iii. 4, 9; 36, 10; vii. 34, 20, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 1, 8, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203015"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203015">↑</a> xix. 1, 4. Viz., the king's brother, his son, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="महिषी">mahiṣī</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत">sūta</a>, Grāmaṇī, <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्तृ">kṣattṛ</a>,</b> and <a class="vei_120a" href="संग्रहीतृ">saṃgrahītṛ</a>. See <a class="vei_120a" href="रत्निन्">ratnin</a>.</li></ol></div>

वीरण|వీరణ|ವೀರಣ|vīraṇa|viiraNa|veeraNa|_vei_120@3016|_vei2_pn317
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3015">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3017">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn316">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn317">2:317</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn318">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vīraṇa</b> is the form in the late Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (v. 2) of the name of the plant <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वीरिण">vīriṇa</a>.</b></p></div>

वीर-हत्या|वीरहत्या|वीर|हत्या|వీర-హత్యా|వీరహత్యా|వీర|హత్యా|ವೀರ-ಹತ್ಯಾ|ವೀರಹತ್ಯಾ|ವೀರ|ಹತ್ಯಾ|vīra-hatyā|vīrahatyā|vīra|hatyā|viira-hatyaa|viirahatyaa|viira|hatyaa|veera-hatyaa|veerahatyaa|veera|_vei_120@3017|_vei2_pn317
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3016">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3018">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn316">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn317">2:317</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn318">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vīra-hatyā,</b> ‘murder of a man,’ is one of the crimes referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203017"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203017">1</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="वीर">vīra</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="हन्">han</a>, ‘man-slayer,’ is often mentioned in the older texts.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203017"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203017">2</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैर">vaira</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203017"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203017">↑</a> x. 40.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203017"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203017">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 5, 2, 1; ii. 2, 5, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxi. 7; Kapiṣṭhala Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 7; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 1, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 8, 12; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 6, 8; xvi. 1, 12, etc.</li></ol></div>

वीरिण|వీరిణ|ವೀರಿಣ|vīriṇa|viiriNa|veeriNa|_vei_120@3018|_vei2_pn318
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3017">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3019">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn317">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn318">2:318</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn319">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vīriṇa</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203018"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203018">1</a></sup> denotes a kind of grass (<i>Andropogon muricatus</i>). See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैरिण">vairiṇa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203018"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203018">↑</a> xiii. 8, 1, 15. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 70.</li></ol></div>

वीरुध्|వీరుధ్|ವೀರುಧ್|vīrudh|viirudh|veerudh|_vei_120@3019|_vei2_pn318
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3018">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3020">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn317">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn318">2:318</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn319">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vīrudh</b> means ‘plant’ in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203019">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203019">2</a></sup> As contrasted with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ओषधि">oṣadhi</a>,</b> it denotes the inferior order of plants, but it often has practically the same sense as <a class="vei_120a" href="ओषधि">oṣadhi</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203019">↑</a> i. 67, 9; 141, 4; ii. 1, 14; 35, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203019"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203019">↑</a> Av. i. 32, 3; 34, 1; ii. 7, 1; v. 4, 1; xix. 35, 4, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 57.</li></ol></div>

वृक|वृक|వృక_౧|వృక|ವೃಕ_೧|ವೃಕ|vṛka_1|vṛka|vRka_1|vRka|vRika_1|vRika|vRuka_1|vRuka|vRRika_1|vRRika|_vei_120@3020|_vei2_pn318
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3019">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3021">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn317">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn318">2:318</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn319">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Vṛka,</b> ‘wolf,’ is mentioned frequently in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203020">1</a></sup> and also later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203020">2</a></sup> It was an enemy of sheep<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203020">3</a></sup> and of calves,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203020">4</a></sup> being dangerous even to men.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203020">5</a></sup> Its colour is stated to be reddish (<i>aruṇa</i>).<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203020">6</a></sup> The ‘she-wolf,’ <a class="vei_120a" href="वृकी">vṛkī</a>, is also mentioned several times in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203020">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203020">↑</a> i. 42, 2; 105, 7; 116, 14; ii. 29, 6; vi. 51, 14; vii. 38, 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203020">↑</a> Av. vii. 95, 2; xii. 1, 49; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 10; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iv. 34; xix. 10. 92, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203020">↑</a> Rv. viii. 34, 3; <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उरा">urā</a>-mathi,</b> ‘worrying sheep,’ x. 66, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203020">↑</a> Av. xii. 4. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203020">↑</a> Rv. i. 105, 11, 18; ii. 29, 6. In <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 21, Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 16, sees the sense of ‘dog,’ which seems needless. <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, Erlāuterungen,</i> 67.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203020">↑</a> Rv. i. 105, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203020"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203020">↑</a> i. 116, 16; 117, 17; 183, 4; vi. 51, 6; x. 127, 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 81; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 14.</li></ol></div>

वृक|वृक|వృక_౨|వృక|ವೃಕ_೨|ವೃಕ|vṛka_2|vṛka|vRka_2|vRka|vRika_2|vRika|vRuka_2|vRuka|vRRika_2|vRRika|_vei_120@3021|_vei2_pn318
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3020">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3022">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn317">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn318">2:318</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn319">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Vṛka</b> in two passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203021"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203021">1</a></sup> denotes ‘plough.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203021"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203021">↑</a> i. 117, 21; viii. 22, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 26.</li></ol></div>

वृक-द्वरस्|वृक-द्वर|वृकद्वरस्|वृकद्वर|वृक|द्वरस्|द्वर|వృక-ద్వరస్|వృక-ద్వర|వృకద్వరస్|వృకద్వర|వృక|ద్వరస్|ద్వర|ವೃಕ-ದ್ವರಸ್|ವೃಕ-ದ್ವರ|ವೃಕದ್ವರಸ್|ವೃಕದ್ವರ|ವೃಕ|ದ್ವರಸ್|ದ್ವರ|vṛka-dvaras|vṛka-dvara|vṛkadvaras|vṛkadvara|vṛka|dvaras|dvara|vRka-dvaras|vRka-dvara|vRkadvaras|vRkadvara|vRka|vRika-dvaras|vRika-dvara|vRikadvaras|vRikadvara|vRika|vRuka-dvaras|vRuka-dvara|vRukadvaras|vRukadvara|vRuka|vRRika-dvaras|vRRika-dvara|vRRikadvaras|vRRikadvara|vRRika|_vei_120@3022|_vei2_pn318
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3021">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3023">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn317">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn318">2:318</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn319">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛka-dvaras</b> is found in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203022"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203022">1</a></sup> which Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203022"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203022">2</a></sup> interprets as referring to a battle against <a class="vei_120a" href="वृकद्वरस्">vṛkadvaras</a>, king of the <b>Śaṇḍikas.</b> But this is quite uncertain. Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203022"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203022">3</a></sup> and Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203022"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203022">4</a></sup> incline to read <i>vṛkadhvaras.</i> Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203022"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203022">5</a></sup> suggests Iranian connections, but without any clear reason. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203022"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203022">↑</a> ii. 30, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203022"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203022">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 153; Griffith; <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 1, 297, n.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203022"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203022">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.; Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenlāndischen Gescllschaft,</i> 48, 110.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203022"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203022">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 211.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203022"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203022">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 442.</li></ol></div>

वृक्ष|వృక్ష|ವೃಕ್ಷ|vṛkṣa|vRkSha|vRikSha|vRukSha|vRRikSha|vRxa|_vei_120@3023|_vei2_pn319
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3022">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3024">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn318">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn319">2:319</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn320">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛkṣa</b> is the ordinary term for tree' in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203023">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203023">2</a></sup> In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203023">3</a></sup> it denotes the coffin made from a tree, no doubt by hollowing it out. The Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203023">4</a></sup> refers to the portent of a tree secreting blood.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203023">↑</a> i. 164, 20. 22; ii. 14, 2; 39, 1; iv. 20, 5; v. 78, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203023">↑</a> Av. i. 14, 1; ii. 12, 3; vi. 45, 1; xii. 1, 27, 51, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203023">↑</a> Av. xviii. 2, 25. <i>Cf.</i> Bṛhaddevatā, v. 83, with Macdonell's note (<i>d</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203023"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203023">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 40, and <i>cf. Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 214.</li></ol></div>

वृक्ष-सर्पी|वृक्ष-सर्पि|वृक्षसर्पी|वृक्षसर्पि|वृक्ष|सर्पी|सर्पि|వృక్ష-సర్పీ|వృక్ష-సర్పి|వృక్షసర్పీ|వృక్షసర్పి|వృక్ష|సర్పీ|సర్పి|ವೃಕ್ಷ-ಸರ್ಪೀ|ವೃಕ್ಷ-ಸರ್ಪಿ|ವೃಕ್ಷಸರ್ಪೀ|ವೃಕ್ಷಸರ್ಪಿ|ವೃಕ್ಷ|ಸರ್ಪೀ|ಸರ್ಪಿ|vṛkṣa-sarpī|vṛkṣa-sarpi|vṛkṣasarpī|vṛkṣasarpi|vṛkṣa|sarpī|sarpi|vRkSha-sarpii|vRkSha-sarpi|vRkShasarpii|vRkShasarpi|vRkSha|sarpii|vRkSha-sarpee|vRkShasarpee|sarpee|vRikSha-sarpii|vRikSha-sarpi|vRikShasarpii|vRikShasarpi|vRikSha|vRukSha-sarpii|vRukSha-sarpi|vRukShasarpii|vRukShasarpi|vRukSha|vRRikSha-sarpii|vRRikSha-sarpi|vRRikShasarpii|vRRikShasarpi|vRRikSha|vRxa-sarpii|vRxa-sarpi|vRxasarpii|vRxasarpi|vRxa|_vei_120@3024|_vei2_pn319
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3023">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3025">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn318">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn319">2:319</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn320">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛkṣa-sarpī,</b> ‘tree-creeper,’ is the name of a species of worm or female serpent in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203024"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203024">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203024"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203024">↑</a> ix. 2, 22. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 98.</li></ol></div>

वृक्ष्य|వృక్ష్య|ವೃಕ್ಷ್ಯ|vṛkṣya|vRkShya|vRikShya|vRukShya|vRRikShya|vRxya|_vei_120@3025|_vei2_pn319
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3024">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3026">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn318">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn319">2:319</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn320">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛkṣya</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 1, 1, 10) denotes the ‘fruit of a tree.’</p></div>

वृचया|వృచయా|ವೃಚಯಾ|vṛcayā|vRchayaa|vRichayaa|vRuchayaa|vRRichayaa|_vei_120@3026|_vei2_pn319
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3025">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3027">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn318">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn319">2:319</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn320">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛcayā</b> is referred to once in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203026"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203026">1</a></sup> as the spouse given by the Aśvins to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203026"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203026">↑</a> i. 51, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 3, 203, who distinguishes two Kakṣīvants, but without sufficient reason, since i. 116, 17, must clearly refer to <a class="vei_120a" href="वृचया">vṛcayā</a>.</li></ol></div>

वृचीवन्त्|వృచీవన్త్|ವೃಚೀವನ್ತ್|vṛcīvant|vRchiivant|vRcheevant|vRichiivant|vRuchiivant|vRRichiivant|_vei_120@3027|_vei2_pn319
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3026">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3028">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn318">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn319">2:319</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn320">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛcīvant</b> is the name of a tribe referred to once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203027">1</a></sup> where it is clearly stated that the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सृञ्जय">sṛñjaya</a></b> king, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दैववात">daivavāta</a>,</b> conquered the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a></b> king and the Vṛcīvants. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203027">2</a></sup> thinks that the Vṛcīvants and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> people should be identified, but this is both unnecessary and improbable; it is adequate to assume that they were allied against<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203027">3</a></sup> the Sṛñjayas. The Vṛcīvants appear again only in the strange legend in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203027">4</a></sup> according to which the <b>Jahnus</b> and the Vṛcīvants contended for sovereignty, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>,</b> the <a class="vei_120a" href="जह्नु">jahnu</a> king, winning it by his knowledge of a certain rite. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हरियूपीया">hariyūpīyā</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203027">↑</a> vi. 27, 5 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203027">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 124.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203027">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 404; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 153: Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 105.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203027"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203027">↑</a> xxi. 12, 2.</li></ol></div>

वृजन|వృజన|ವೃಜನ|vṛjana|vRjana|vRijana|vRujana|vRRijana|_vei_120@3028|_vei2_pn320
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3027">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3029">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn319">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn320">2:320</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn321">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛjana,</b> according to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203028">1</a></sup> denotes in several passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203028">2</a></sup> the ‘settlement’ or ‘village,’ the German ‘Mark’ and its inhabitants. Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203028">3</a></sup> accepting this view, sees in <a class="vei_120a" href="वृजन">vṛjana</a> the ‘secure abode’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षिति">kṣiti</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ध्रुवा">dhruvā</a></i>) where the clan lives,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203028">4</a></sup> the clan itself as a village community (like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a></b>), and the clan in war.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203028">5</a></sup> Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203028">6</a></sup> on the other hand, takes the literal sense of <a class="vei_120a" href="वृजन">vṛjana</a> ṭo be ‘net,’ developing all the other senses from that idea, but the traditional view seems more natural.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203028">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203028">↑</a> i. 51, 15, 73, 2; 91, 21; 105, 19; 128, 7; 165, 15; 166, 14, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203028">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 142, 159, 161.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203028">↑</a> Rv. i. 51, 15; 73, 2 (<i>cf.</i> i. 73, 4).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203028">↑</a> Rv. vii. 32, 27; x. 42, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203028"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203028">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 139 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

वृत्र-घ्न|वृत्रघ्न|वृत्र|घ्न|వృత్ర-ఘ్న|వృత్రఘ్న|వృత్ర|ఘ్న|ವೃತ್ರ-ಘ್ನ|ವೃತ್ರಘ್ನ|ವೃತ್ರ|ಘ್ನ|vṛtra-ghna|vṛtraghna|vṛtra|ghna|vRtra-ghna|vRtraghna|vRtra|vRitra-ghna|vRitraghna|vRitra|vRutra-ghna|vRutraghna|vRutra|vRRitra-ghna|vRRitraghna|vRRitra|_vei_120@3029|_vei2_pn320
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3028">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3030">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn319">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn320">2:320</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn321">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛtra-ghna</b> occurs in a passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203029"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203029">1</a></sup> where in a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a></b> reciting the prowess of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a></b> it is said that he bound horses on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a></b> (Jumna) and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गंगा">gaṅgā</a></b> (Ganges) <i>Vṛtraghne,</i> which Sāyaṇa renders ‘at <a class="vei_120a" href="वृत्रघ्न">vṛtraghna</a>,’ as the name of a place. Roth,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203029"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203029">2</a></sup> however, seems right in interpreting the form as a dative, ‘for the slayer of Vṛtra’--<i>i.e.,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203029"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203029">↑</a> viii. 23, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203029"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203029">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. Cf.</i> Aufrecht, <i>Aitarecya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,</i> 425.</li></ol></div>

वृत्र-शंकु|वृत्रशंकु|वृत्र|शंकु|वृत्र-शङ्कु|वृत्रशङ्कु|शङ्कु|వృత్ర-శంకు|వృత్రశంకు|వృత్ర|శంకు|వృత్ర-శఙ్కు|వృత్రశఙ్కు|శఙ్కు|ವೃತ್ರ-ಶಂಕು|ವೃತ್ರಶಂಕು|ವೃತ್ರ|ಶಂಕು|ವೃತ್ರ-ಶಙ್ಕು|ವೃತ್ರಶಙ್ಕು|ಶಙ್ಕು|vṛtra-śaṅku|vṛtraśaṅku|vṛtra|śaṅku|vRtra-shaMku|vRtrashaMku|vRtra|shaMku|vRtra-shanku|vRtrashanku|shanku|vRitra-shaMku|vRitrashaMku|vRitra|vRutra-shaMku|vRutrashaMku|vRutra|vRRitra-shaMku|vRRitrashaMku|vRRitra|vRitra-shanku|vRitrashanku|vRutra-shanku|vRutrashanku|vRRitra-shanku|vRRitrashanku|_vei_120@3030|_vei2_pn320
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3029">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3031">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn319">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn320">2:320</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn321">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛtra-śaṅku,</b> literally ‘Vṛtra-peg,’ found in one passage of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203030"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203030">1</a></sup> is said by the scholiast on the Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203030"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203030">2</a></sup> to denote a stone pillar. This improbable interpretation is based on another passage in the same <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203030"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203030">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203030"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203030">↑</a> xiii. 8, 4, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203030"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203030">↑</a> xxi. 3, 31.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203030"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203030">↑</a> iv. 2, 5, 15. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling. <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 437, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

वृद्ध-द्युम्न आभिप्रतारिण|वृद्धद्युम्न आभिप्रतारिण|वृद्ध|द्युम्न आभिप्रतारिण|వృద్ధ-ద్యుమ్న ఆభిప్రతారిణ|వృద్ధద్యుమ్న ఆభిప్రతారిణ|వృద్ధ|ద్యుమ్న ఆభిప్రతారిణ|ವೃದ್ಧ-ದ್ಯುಮ್ನ ಆಭಿಪ್ರತಾರಿಣ|ವೃದ್ಧದ್ಯುಮ್ನ ಆಭಿಪ್ರತಾರಿಣ|ವೃದ್ಧ|ದ್ಯುಮ್ನ ಆಭಿಪ್ರತಾರಿಣ|vṛddha-dyumna ābhipratāriṇa|vṛddhadyumna ābhipratāriṇa|vṛddha|dyumna ābhipratāriṇa|vRddha-dyumna aabhiprataariNa|vRddhadyumna aabhiprataariNa|vRddha|dyumna aabhiprataariNa|vRiddha-dyumna aabhiprataariNa|vRiddhadyumna aabhiprataariNa|vRiddha|vRuddha-dyumna aabhiprataariNa|vRuddhadyumna aabhiprataariNa|vRuddha|vRRiddha-dyumna aabhiprataariNa|vRRiddhadyumna aabhiprataariNa|vRRiddha|_vei_120@3031|_vei2_pn320
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3030">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3032">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn319">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn320">2:320</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn321">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛddha-<a class="vei_120a" href="द्युम्न">dyumna</a> Ābhipratāriṇa</b> (‘descendant of Abhipratārin’) is the name of a prince (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्य">rājanya</a></i>) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 48, 9), where his priest, <b>Śucivṛkṣa Gaupalāyana,</b> is praised. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (xv, 16, 10-13), on the contrary, he is said to have erred in the <b>sacrifice,</b> when a Brahmin prophesied that the result would be the expulsion of the <b>Kurus</b> from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>, an event which</b> actually came to pass. </p></div>

वृद्ध-वाशिनी|वृद्ध-वाशिनि|वृद्धवाशिनी|वृद्धवाशिनि|वृद्ध|वाशिनी|वाशिनि|వృద్ధ-వాశినీ|వృద్ధ-వాశిని|వృద్ధవాశినీ|వృద్ధవాశిని|వృద్ధ|వాశినీ|వాశిని|ವೃದ್ಧ-ವಾಶಿನೀ|ವೃದ್ಧ-ವಾಶಿನಿ|ವೃದ್ಧವಾಶಿನೀ|ವೃದ್ಧವಾಶಿನಿ|ವೃದ್ಧ|ವಾಶಿನೀ|ವಾಶಿನಿ|vṛddha-vāśinī|vṛddha-vāśini|vṛddhavāśinī|vṛddhavāśini|vṛddha|vāśinī|vāśini|vRddha-vaashinii|vRddha-vaashini|vRddhavaashinii|vRddhavaashini|vRddha|vaashinii|vaashini|vRddha-vaashinee|vRddhavaashinee|vaashinee|vRiddha-vaashinii|vRiddha-vaashini|vRiddhavaashinii|vRiddhavaashini|vRiddha|vRuddha-vaashinii|vRuddha-vaashini|vRuddhavaashinii|vRuddhavaashini|vRuddha|vRRiddha-vaashinii|vRRiddha-vaashini|vRRiddhavaashinii|vRRiddhavaashini|vRRiddha|_vei_120@3032|_vei2_pn321
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3031">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3033">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn320">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn321">2:321</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn322">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛddha-vāśinī</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> (v. 21) denotes the ‘female jackal.’</p><p>^1. <b>Vṛśa.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वृष">vṛṣa</a>.</b></p></div>

वृश जान|वृश जान|వృశ జాన_౨|వృశ జాన|ವೃಶ ಜಾನ_೨|ವೃಶ ಜಾನ|vṛśa jāna_2|vṛśa jāna|vRsha jaana_2|vRsha jaana|vRisha jaana_2|vRisha jaana|vRusha jaana_2|vRusha jaana|vRRisha jaana_2|vRRisha jaana|_vei_120@3033|_vei2_pn321
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3032">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3034">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn320">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn321">2:321</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn322">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Vṛśa Jāna</b> (‘descendant of Jana’) is the name of a famous <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, who was unfortunate enough, while with his royal master, <b>Tryaruṇa,</b> to see a boy killed by the chariot which the king drove too fast. He thereupon recalled the boy to life. The story is told briefly in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203033">1</a></sup> the Śāṭyāyanaka,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203033">2</a></sup> the Tāṇḍaka,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203033">3</a></sup> was also narrated in the <a class="vei_120a" href="भाल्लवि">bhāllavi</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203033">4</a></sup> and is preserved in the Bṛhaddevatā,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203033">5</a></sup> Sieg<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203033">6</a></sup> has endeavoured to trace the story in part in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203033">7</a></sup> but there is a consensus of opinion<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203033">8</a></sup> against the correctness of such a view.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203033">↑</a> xiii. 3, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203033">↑</a> See Sāyaṇa on Rv. v. 2, and the Jaiminīya version in the <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203033">↑</a> See Sāyaṇa, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203033">↑</a> Referred to in the Bṛhaddevatā, v. 23, apparently as cited in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a>. The passage is not in the extant text of the <a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>. See Sieg. <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a></i> 65, n. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203033">↑</a> v. 14 <i>et seq.,</i> where see Macdonell's notes.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203033">↑</a> <i>Op, <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 64-76.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203033">↑</a> v. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203033"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203033">↑</a> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 4, 324; Hillebrandt, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 33, 248 <i>et seq.;</i> Oldenberg, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 46, 366 <i>et seq.; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 312. On the other hand, Geldner, <i>Festgruss an Roth,</i> 192, supports the tradition. <i>Cf.</i> Weber. <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 32.</li></ol></div>

वृश्चिक|వృశ్చిక|ವೃಶ್ಚಿಕ|vṛścika|vRshchika|vRishchika|vRushchika|vRRishchika|_vei_120@3034|_vei2_pn321
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3033">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3035">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn320">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn321">2:321</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn322">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛścika</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203034">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203034">2</a></sup> denote ‘scorpion.’ Its poison was feared<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203034">3</a></sup> like that of serpents. It is described as lying torpid in the earth during winter.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203034">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203034">↑</a> i. 191, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203034">↑</a> x. 4, 9, 15; xii. 1, 46; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xii. 27.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203034">↑</a> Rv., <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Av. x. 4, 9. 15.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203034"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203034">↑</a> Av. xii. 1, 46.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 98.</li></ol></div>

वृष|వృష|ವೃಷ|vṛṣa|vRSha|vRiSha|vRuSha|vRRiSha|_vei_120@3035|_vei2_pn321
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3034">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3036">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn320">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn321">2:321</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn322">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛṣa</b> is the name of a plant of some kind in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203035"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203035">1</a></sup> Later the <i>Gendarussa vulgaris</i> is so styled. Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203035"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203035">2</a></sup> has Vṛśa, which Bo7htlingk<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203035"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203035">3</a></sup> takes to mean a small animal, a quite possible sense. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="येवाष">yevāṣa</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203035"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203035">↑</a> xxx. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203035"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203035">↑</a> iv. 8, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203035"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203035">↑</a> Dictionary, General Inden to Supplements, 376.</li></ol></div>

वृष-खादि|वृषखादि|वृष|खादि|వృష-ఖాది|వృషఖాది|వృష|ఖాది|ವೃಷ-ಖಾದಿ|ವೃಷಖಾದಿ|ವೃಷ|ಖಾದಿ|vṛṣa-khādi|vṛṣakhādi|vṛṣa|khādi|vRSha-khaadi|vRShakhaadi|vRSha|khaadi|vRiSha-khaadi|vRiShakhaadi|vRiSha|vRuSha-khaadi|vRuShakhaadi|vRuSha|vRRiSha-khaadi|vRRiShakhaadi|vRRiSha|_vei_120@3036|_vei2_pn322
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3035">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3037">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn321">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn322">2:322</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn323">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛṣa-khādi</b> is used as an epithet of the Maruts in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203036"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203036">1</a></sup> The sense is doubtful: Bollensen<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203036"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203036">2</a></sup> thought the expression referred to the wearing of rings in the ears; Max Müller<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203036"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203036">3</a></sup> renders it ‘strong rings,’ comparing the later <a class="vei_120a" href="चक्र">cakra</a> or discus.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203036"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203036">↑</a> i. 64, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203036"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203036">↑</a> <i>Orient und Occident,</i> 2, 461, n.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203036"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203036">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 107, 120, <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 263.</li></ol></div>

वृष-गण|वृषगण|वृष|गण|వృష-గణ|వృషగణ|వృష|గణ|ವೃಷ-ಗಣ|ವೃಷಗಣ|ವೃಷ|ಗಣ|vṛṣa-gaṇa|vṛṣagaṇa|vṛṣa|gaṇa|vRSha-gaNa|vRShagaNa|vRSha|gaNa|vRiSha-gaNa|vRiShagaNa|vRiSha|vRuSha-gaNa|vRuShagaNa|vRuSha|vRRiSha-gaNa|vRRiShagaNa|vRRiSha|_vei_120@3037|_vei2_pn322
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3036">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3038">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn321">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn322">2:322</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn323">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛṣa-gaṇa</b> is the name of a family of singers mentioned in one passage of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203037"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203037">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203037"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203037">↑</a> ix. 97, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 132.</li></ol></div>

वृषण्-अश्व|वृषणश्व|वृषण्|अश्व|వృషణ్-అశ్వ|వృషణశ్వ|వృషణ్|అశ్వ|ವೃಷಣ್-ಅಶ್ವ|ವೃಷಣಶ್ವ|ವೃಷಣ್|ಅಶ್ವ|vṛṣaṇ-aśva|vṛṣaṇaśva|vṛṣaṇ|aśva|vRShaN-ashva|vRShaNashva|vRShaN|ashva|vRiShaN-ashva|vRiShaNashva|vRiShaN|vRuShaN-ashva|vRuShaNashva|vRuShaN|vRRiShaN-ashva|vRRiShaNashva|vRRiShaN|_vei_120@3038|_vei2_pn322
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3037">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3039">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn321">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn322">2:322</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn323">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛṣaṇ-aśva</b> is the name of a man referred to in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203038">1</a></sup> where <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> is called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मेना">menā</a>,</b> perhaps his ‘wife’ or ‘daughter.’ The same legend is alluded to in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203038">2</a></sup> the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203038">3</a></sup> the Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203038">4</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203038">5</a></sup> but it is clear that all of these texts had no real tradition of what was referred to.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203038">↑</a> i. 51, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203038">↑</a> ii. 79 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 37).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203038">↑</a> iii. 3, 4, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203038">↑</a> i. 1, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203038"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203038">↑</a> i. 12, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 81, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

वृष-दंश|वृषदंश|वृष|दंश|వృష-దంశ|వృషదంశ|వృష|దంశ|ವೃಷ-ದಂಶ|ವೃಷದಂಶ|ವೃಷ|ದಂಶ|vṛṣa-daṃśa|vṛṣadaṃśa|vṛṣa|daṃśa|vRSha-daMsha|vRShadaMsha|vRSha|daMsha|vRiSha-daMsha|vRiShadaMsha|vRiSha|vRuSha-daMsha|vRuShadaMsha|vRuSha|vRRiSha-daMsha|vRRiShadaMsha|vRRiSha|_vei_120@3039|_vei2_pn322
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3038">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3040">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn321">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn322">2:322</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn323">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛṣa-daṃśa,</b> ‘strong-toothed,’ is the name of the cat in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203039">1</a></sup> where it figures as a victim at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’). It also appears in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>;<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203039">2</a></sup> the fact that the sneeze of the cat is here referred to renders it likely that the animal was already tamed. Geldner<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203039">3</a></sup> sees a house cat in the animal alluded to in a hymn of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203039">4</a></sup> by a set of curious epithets, including <i>vṛṣadatī,</i> ‘strong-toothed,’ but Whitney<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203039">5</a></sup> decisively rejects the idea that the hymn refers to the domestic cat. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203039">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 21, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 12; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 31.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203039">↑</a> viii. 2, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203039">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 313-315.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203039">↑</a> i. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203039"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203039">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 19, 20; Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 153, n.; <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 261.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 86.</li></ol></div>

वृषन्|వృషన్|ವೃಷನ್|vṛṣan|vRShan|vRiShan|vRuShan|vRRiShan|_vei_120@3040|_vei2_pn323
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3039">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3041">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn322">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn323">2:323</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn324">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛṣan</b> in two passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203040">1</a></sup> seems to denote a man, with the patronymic <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पाथ्य">pāthya</a></b> in one of them.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203040"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203040">↑</a> i. 36, 10; vi. 16, 14. 15. <i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 152, 153; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 104.</li></ol></div>

वृषभ|వృషభ|ವೃಷಭ|vṛṣabha|vRShabha|vRiShabha|vRuShabha|vRRiShabha|_vei_120@3041|_vei2_pn323
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3040">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3042">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn322">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn323">2:323</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn324">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛṣabha</b> regularly denotes a ‘bull’ in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203041">1</a></sup> but usually in a metaphorical sense.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203041"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203041">↑</a> i. 94, 10; 160, 3; vi. 46, 4; of Parjanya, vii. 101, 1, 6, etc. Roth renders <i>vrsabhānna,</i> ii. 16, 5, ‘eating strong food’; but the literal sense, ‘whose food is bulls,’ will answer. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मांस">māṃsa</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

वृषल|వృషల|ವೃಷಲ|vṛṣala|vRShala|vRiShala|vRuShala|vRRiShala|_vei_120@3042|_vei2_pn323
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3041">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3043">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn322">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn323">2:323</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn324">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛṣala</b> in the dicing hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203042"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203042">1</a></sup> denotes an ‘outcast’; the same sense appears in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203042"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203042">2</a></sup> where the touch of either a <a class="vei_120a" href="वृषल">vṛṣala</a> or a Vṛṣalī is to be avoided.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203042"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203042">↑</a> x. 34, 11. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 16.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203042"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203042">↑</a> vi. 4, 12 Mādhyaṃdina.</li></ol></div>

वृष-शुष्म वातावत|वृषशुष्म वातावत|वृष|शुष्म वातावत|వృష-శుష్మ వాతావత|వృషశుష్మ వాతావత|వృష|శుష్మ వాతావత|ವೃಷ-ಶುಷ್ಮ ವಾತಾವತ|ವೃಷಶುಷ್ಮ ವಾತಾವತ|ವೃಷ|ಶುಷ್ಮ ವಾತಾವತ|vṛṣa-śuṣma vātāvata|vṛṣaśuṣma vātāvata|vṛṣa|śuṣma vātāvata|vRSha-shuShma vaataavata|vRShashuShma vaataavata|vRSha|shuShma vaataavata|vRiSha-shuShma vaataavata|vRiShashuShma vaataavata|vRiSha|vRuSha-shuShma vaataavata|vRuShashuShma vaataavata|vRuSha|vRRiSha-shuShma vaataavata|vRRiShashuShma vaataavata|vRRiSha|_vei_120@3043|_vei2_pn323
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3042">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3044">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn322">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn323">2:323</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn324">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛṣa-śuṣma Vātāvata</b> (‘descendant of Vātāvant’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जातूकर्ण्य">jātūkarṇya</a></b> is the name of a priest in the Brāhmaṇas of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203043"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203043">1</a></sup> Vṛṣaśuṣma in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203043"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203043">2</a></sup> is probably intended for the same name.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203043"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203043">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 29, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 9 (with a <i>varia lectio</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="वाधावत">vādhāvata</a>: <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 215, n. 1).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203043"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203043">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373.</li></ol></div>

वृषा-रव|वृषारव|वृषा|रव|వృషా-రవ|వృషారవ|వృషా|రవ|ವೃಷಾ-ರವ|ವೃಷಾರವ|ವೃಷಾ|ರವ|vṛṣā-rava|vṛṣārava|vṛṣā|rava|vRShaa-rava|vRShaarava|vRShaa|vRiShaa-rava|vRiShaarava|vRiShaa|vRuShaa-rava|vRuShaarava|vRuShaa|vRRiShaa-rava|vRRiShaarava|vRRiShaa|_vei_120@3044|_vei2_pn323
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3043">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3045">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn322">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn323">2:323</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn324">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛṣā-rava,</b> ‘roaring like a bull,’ is the name of some animal in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203044"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203044">1</a></sup> In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203044"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203044">2</a></sup> the word occurs in the dual, meaning perhaps ‘mallet’ or ‘drumstick.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203044"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203044">↑</a> x. 146, 2 =  <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 5, 5, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203044"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203044">↑</a> xii. 5, 2, 7.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 426: Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 90.</li></ol></div>

वृष्टि|వృష్టి|ವೃಷ್ಟಿ|vṛṣṭi|vRShTi|vRiShTi|vRuShTi|vRRiShTi|_vei_120@3045|_vei2_pn323
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3044">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3046">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn322">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn323">2:323</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn324">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛṣṭi</b> is the regular word for ‘rain’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203045"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203045">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203045"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203045">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203045"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203045">↑</a> i. 116, 12; ii. 5, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203045"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203045">↑</a> Av. iii. 31, 11; vi. 22, 3, etc.</li></ol></div>

वृष्टि-हव्य|वृष्टिहव्य|वृष्टि|हव्य|వృష్టి-హవ్య|వృష్టిహవ్య|వృష్టి|హవ్య|ವೃಷ್ಟಿ-ಹವ್ಯ|ವೃಷ್ಟಿಹವ್ಯ|ವೃಷ್ಟಿ|ಹವ್ಯ|vṛṣṭi-havya|vṛṣṭihavya|vṛṣṭi|havya|vRShTi-havya|vRShTihavya|vRShTi|vRiShTi-havya|vRiShTihavya|vRiShTi|vRuShTi-havya|vRuShTihavya|vRuShTi|vRRiShTi-havya|vRRiShTihavya|vRRiShTi|_vei_120@3046|_vei2_pn324
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3045">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3047">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn323">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn324">2:324</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn325">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vṛṣṭi-havya</b> is in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203046"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203046">1</a></sup> the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>, whose sons were the <b>Upastutas.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203046"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203046">↑</a> x. 115, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Max Mūller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 152, 153; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 108, 109.</li></ol></div>

वेणु|వేణు|ವೇಣು|veṇu|veNu|_vei_120@3047|_vei2_pn324
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3046">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3048">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn323">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn324">2:324</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn325">↷</a><hr><p><b>Veṇu</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203047">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203047">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘reed’ of bamboo, It is described in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203047">3</a></sup> as ‘hollow’ (<i>su-ṣira</i>). In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203047">4</a></sup> it occurs only in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वालखिल्य">vālakhilya</a> hymn in a Dānastuti (‘praise of gifts’), where Roth<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203047">5</a></sup> thinks that ‘flutes of reed’ are meant, a sense which <a class="vei_120a" href="वेणु">veṇu</a> has in the later texts. The <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203047">6</a></sup> couples <a class="vei_120a" href="वेणु">veṇu</a> with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सस्य">sasya</a>,</b> stating that they ripen in <a class="vei_120a" href="वसन्त">vasanta</a>, ‘spring.’ Apparently bamboo reeds are meant.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203047">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203047">↑</a> i. 27, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203047">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 2, 5, 2; vii. 4, 19, 2; Kāthaka Saṃhitā, xiii. 12; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 4, 19; ii. 6, 2, 17, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203047">↑</a> v. 1, 1, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203047">↑</a> viii. 55, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203047">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203047">↑</a> iv. 12.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203047"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203047">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 6, 17, with the scholiast; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 343.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 71.</li></ol></div>

वेतस|వేతస|ವೇತಸ|vetasa|_vei_120@3048|_vei2_pn324
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3047">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3049">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn323">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn324">2:324</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn325">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vetasa</b> is the name of the water plant <i>Calamus Rotang,</i> or a similar reed, in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203048">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203048">2</a></sup> It is called ‘golden’ (<i>hiraṇyaya</i>) and ‘water-born’ (<i>apsuja</i>).<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203048">4</a></sup></p><p>^3 Rv. <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Av. x. 7, 41.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203048">↑</a> iv. 58, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203048">↑</a> Av. x. 7, 41; xviii. 3, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 3, 12, 2; 4, 4, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvii. 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8, 4, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203048"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203048">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 3, 12, 2, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 71.</li></ol></div>

वेतसु|వేతసు|ವೇತಸು|vetasu|_vei_120@3049|_vei2_pn324
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3048">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3050">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn323">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn324">2:324</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn325">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vetasu</b> is a name occurring in the singular in two passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203049"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203049">1</a></sup> and once in the plural.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203049"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203049">2</a></sup> It seems that he was defeated by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, but there is no reason to assume that he was a demon. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203049"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203049">3</a></sup> thinks that the Vetasus were probably the tribe of which <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दशद्यु">daśadyu</a></b> was a member, and that they defeated the <b>Tugras.</b> The passages are too obscure to render any version probable. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203049"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203049">↑</a> vi. 20, 8; 26, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203049"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203049">↑</a> x. 49, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203049"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203049">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 128. <i>Cf.</i> Kaegi, <i>Die Rigveda,</i> n. 337.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 55, 328.</li></ol></div>

वेतस्वन्त्|వేతస్వన్త్|ವೇತಸ್ವನ್ತ್|vetasvant|_vei_120@3050|_vei2_pn325
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3049">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3051">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn324">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn325">2:325</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn326">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vetasvant,</b> ‘abounding in reeds,’ is the name of a place in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203050">1</a></sup> not, as Weber<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203050">2</a></sup> once took it, a part of the name of <b>Ekayāvan <a class="vei_120a" href="गांदम">gāṃdama</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203050">↑</a> xxi. 14, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203050"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203050">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 32. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 69.</li></ol></div>

वेद|వేద|ವೇದ|veda|_vei_120@3051|_vei2_pn325
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3050">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3052">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn324">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn325">2:325</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn326">↷</a><hr><p><b>Veda</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203051"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203051">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203051"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203051">2</a></sup> denotes ‘sacred lore.’ In the plural<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203051"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203051">3</a></sup> it more definitely refers to the Vedas of the Ṛc, <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुस्">yajus</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a>. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विद्या">vidyā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203051"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203051">↑</a> Av. vii. 54, 2; x. 8, 17; xv. 3, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203051"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203051">↑</a> <i>Traya,</i> ‘threefold,’ Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 5, 10; xiii. 4, 3, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, i. 2. 18. 20, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203051"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203051">↑</a> Av. iv. 35, 6; xix. 2, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 11, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 32, 1; vi. 15, 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 10, 11, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 3, 3, 7; xii. 3, 4, 11, etc. In the Brāhmaṇas the word, no doubt, has normally the sense of the extant collections, which appear under their accepted titles, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>,</b> in the Āraṇyakas.</li></ol></div>

वेदांग|वेदाङ्ग|వేదాంగ|వేదాఙ్గ|ವೇದಾಂಗ|ವೇದಾಙ್ಗ|vedāṅga|vedaaMga|vedaanga|_vei_120@3052|_vei2_pn325
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3051">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3053">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn324">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn325">2:325</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn326">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vedāṅga,</b> as the name of a text subsidiary to the study of the Rigveda, is first found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203052">1</a></sup> and the Rigveda Prātiśākhya.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203052">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203052">↑</a> i. 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203052"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203052">↑</a> xii. 40.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Roth, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,</i> xv. <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 42.</li></ol></div>

वेन|वेन|వేన_౧|వేన|ವೇನ_೧|ವೇನ|vena_1|vena|_vei_120@3053|_vei2_pn325
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3052">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3054">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn324">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn325">2:325</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn326">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Vena</b> occurs in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203053"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203053">1</a></sup> as a generous patron. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पृथवान">pṛthavāna</a>,</b> found in the same passage, may or may not be another name of his, and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पार्थ्य">pārthya</a></b> in the following stanza of the hymn is perhaps his patronymic.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203053"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203053">↑</a> x. 93, 14. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 166.</li></ol></div>

वेन|वेन|వేన_౨|వేన|ವೇನ_೨|ವೇನ|vena_2|vena|_vei_120@3054|_vei2_pn325
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3053">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3055">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn324">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn325">2:325</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn326">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Vena</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203054"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203054">1</a></sup> is thought by Tilak<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203054"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203054">2</a></sup> to be the planet Venus. But this is certainly impossible. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203054"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203054">↑</a> x. 123.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203054"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203054">↑</a> <i>Oriea,</i> 163 <i>et seq</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Whitney, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, xciv.</li></ol></div>

वेश|वेश|వేశ_౧|వేశ|ವೇಶ_೧|ವೇಶ|veśa_1|veśa|vesha_1|vesha|_vei_120@3055|_vei2_pn326
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3054">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3056">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn325">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn326">2:326</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn327">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Veśa</b> is a term of somewhat doubtful sense, apparently denoting ‘vassal,’ ‘tenant,’ in a few passages,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203055"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203055">1</a></sup> and, according to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203055"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203055">2</a></sup> ‘dependent neighbour.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203055"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203055">↑</a> Rv. iv. 3, 13; v. 85, 7; possibly x. 49, 5; but <i>cf.</i> 2. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वेश">veśa</a>;</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 5 (<i>veśatva.</i>); xxxi. 12; xxxii. 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>, ii. 5, 7; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 4, 8; ii. 3, 7; iv. 1, 13. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 204, who takes <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वेशस्">veśas</a></i> in Av. ii. 32, 5, where <i><a class="vei_120a" href="परि">pari</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वेशस्">veśas</a></i> also occurs in the same sense, and compares <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a></i> in <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 7, 1, as meaning ‘servitude’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203055"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203055">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 1, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वेश">veśa</a>,</i> and <i>veśatva. Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 75, who seems inclined to read <i>veṣas</i> in Av. ii. 32, 5; but Weber's explanation of the origin of the sense of ‘servant’ is adequate. Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 135, n. 4, sees in <a class="vei_120a" href="वेश">veśa</a> either a neighbour or a member of the same village community. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सजात">sajāta</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

वेश|वेश|వేశ_౨|వేశ|ವೇಶ_೨|ವೇಶ|veśa_2|veśa|vesha_2|vesha|_vei_120@3056|_vei2_pn326
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3055">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3057">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn325">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn326">2:326</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn327">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Veśa</b> may be a proper name in two passages of the Rigveda;<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203056">1</a></sup> if so, it is quite uncertain whether a demon is meant or not.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203056"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203056">↑</a> ii. 13, 8; x. 49, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 152, 164.</li></ol></div>

वेशन्ता|వేశన్తా|ವೇಶನ್ತಾ|veśantā|veshantaa|_vei_120@3057|_vei2_pn326
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3056">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3058">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn325">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn326">2:326</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn327">↷</a><hr><p><b>Veśantā,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203057">1</a></sup> <b>Veśantī,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203057">2</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वेशान्ता">veśāntā</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203057">3</a></sup> all denote a ‘pond’ or ‘tank.’ <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैशन्त">vaiśanta</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203057">↑</a> Av. xi. 6, 10; xx. 128, 8, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 12, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203057">↑</a> Av. i. 3, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203057"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203057">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 3, 11.</li></ol></div>

वेशस्|वेश|వేశస్|వేశ|ವೇಶಸ್|ವೇಶ|veśas|veśa|veshas|vesha|_vei_120@3058|_vei2_pn326
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3057">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3059">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn325">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn326">2:326</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn327">↷</a><hr><p><b>Veśas.</b> See 1. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वेश">veśa</a>.</b></p></div>

वेशान्ता|వేశాన్తా|ವೇಶಾನ್ತಾ|veśāntā|veshaantaa|_vei_120@3059|_vei2_pn326
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3058">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3060">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn325">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn326">2:326</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn327">↷</a><hr><p><b>Veśāntā.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वेशन्ता">veśantā</a>.</b></p></div>

वेशी|वेशि|వేశీ|వేశి|ವೇಶೀ|ವೇಶಿ|veśī|veśi|veshii|veshi|veshee|_vei_120@3060|_vei2_pn326
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3059">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3061">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn325">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn326">2:326</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn327">↷</a><hr><p><b>Veśī</b> in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203060"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203060">1</a></sup> seems to denote a ‘needle.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203060"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203060">↑</a> vii. 18, 17. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 264, n.</li></ol></div>

वेश्मन्|వేశ్మన్|ವೇಶ್ಮನ್|veśman|veshman|_vei_120@3061|_vei2_pn326
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3060">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3062">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn325">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn326">2:326</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn327">↷</a><hr><p><b>Veśman,</b> ‘house,’ occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203061">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203061">2</a></sup> It denotes the house as the place where a man is ‘settled’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a></i>). </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203061">↑</a> x. 107, 10; 146, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203061"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203061">↑</a> Av. v. 17, 13; ix. 6, 30; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 24, 6, etc. In Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 2, 14, the single house (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="एक">eka</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वेश्मन्">veśman</a></i>) of the king is contrasted with the numerous dwellings of the people.</li></ol></div>

वेश्य|వేశ్య|ವೇಶ್ಯ|veśya|veshya|_vei_120@3062|_vei2_pn327
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3061">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3063">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn326">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn327">2:327</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn328">↷</a><hr><p><b>Veśya</b> in two passages of the Rigveda (iv. 26, 3; vi. 61, 14) seems to denote the relation of ‘dependence’ rather than ‘neighbourhood.’ <i>Cf.</i> 1. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वेश">veśa</a>.</b></p></div>

वेष्क|వేష్క|ವೇಷ್ಕ|veṣka|veShka|_vei_120@3063|_vei2_pn327
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3062">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3064">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn326">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn327">2:327</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn328">↷</a><hr><p><b>Veṣka</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 8, 1, 15) denotes the ‘noose’ for strangling the sacrificial animal. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्लेष्क">bleṣka</a>.</b></p></div>

वेहत्|వేహత్|ವೇಹತ್|vehat|_vei_120@3064|_vei2_pn327
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3063">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3065">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn326">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn327">2:327</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn328">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vehat</b> seems to mean a ‘cow that miscarries.’ It is mentioned in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203064">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203064">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203064">↑</a> xii. 4, 37 <i>et seq.</i> In iii. 23, 1, a woman is called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वेहत्">vehat</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203064"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203064">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 27; xxiv. 1, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 5, 3, etc. In Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 4, 4, 6, Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 195, adopts the sense ‘a cow desiring the bull.’ But <i>cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 127.</li></ol></div>

वैकर्ण|వైకర్ణ|ವೈಕರ್ಣ|vaikarṇa|vaikarNa|_vei_120@3065|_vei2_pn327
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3064">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3066">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn326">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn327">2:327</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn328">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaikarṇa</b> occurs but once in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203065">1</a></sup> in the description of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दाशराज्ञ">dāśarājña</a>,</b> where <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a></b> is stated to have overthrown the twenty-one tribes (<i>janān</i>) of the kings or folk of the two Vaikarṇas. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203065">2</a></sup> conjectures that they were a joint people, the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Krivis:</b> this is quite possible, and even probable. Vikarṇa as the name of a people is found in the Mahābhārata,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203065">3</a></sup> and a lexicographer<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203065">4</a></sup> places the Vikarṇas in Kaśmīr, a reminiscence probably of a real settlement of the Kurus in that country. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उत्तर कुरु">uttara kuru</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203065">↑</a> vii. 18, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203065">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 103.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203065">↑</a> vi. 2105.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203065"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203065">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 261 <i>et seq.,</i> who sees in Vaikarṇau the two <a class="vei_120a" href="वैकर्ण">vaikarṇa</a> kings.</li></ol></div>

वैखानस|వైఖానస|ವೈಖಾನಸ|vaikhānasa|vaikhaanasa|_vei_120@3066|_vei2_pn327
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3065">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3067">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn326">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn327">2:327</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn328">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaikhānasa</b> is the name of a mythical group of <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषिस्">ṛṣis</a> who are said in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203066"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203066">1</a></sup> to have been slain at <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मुनिमरण">munimaraṇa</a></b> by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रहस्यु देवमलिम्लुच्">rahasyu devamalimluc</a>,</b> and who are mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka also.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203066"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203066">2</a></sup> An individual <a class="vei_120a" href="वैखानस">vaikhānasa</a> is <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुहन्मन्">puruhanman</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203066"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203066">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203066"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203066">↑</a> xiv. 4, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203066"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203066">↑</a> i. 23, 3 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 78).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203066"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203066">↑</a> xiv. 9, 29.</li></ol></div>

वैजान|వైజాన|ವೈಜಾನ|vaijāna|vaijaana|_vei_120@3067|_vei2_pn328
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3066">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3068">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn327">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn328">2:328</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn329">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaijāna,</b> ‘descendant of Vijāna,’ is Sāyaṇa's version of the patronymic of <b>Vṛśa</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203067"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203067">1</a></sup> The real reading is <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वै">vai</a> Jānaḥ,</i> as pointed out by Weber.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203067"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203067">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203067"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203067">↑</a> xiii. 3, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203067"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203067">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 32.</li></ol></div>

वैट्टभटी-पुत्र|वैट्टभटीपुत्र|वैट्टभटी|वैट्टभटि|पुत्र|వైట్టభటీ-పుత్ర|వైట్టభటీపుత్ర|వైట్టభటీ|వైట్టభటి|పుత్ర|ವೈಟ್ಟಭಟೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ವೈಟ್ಟಭಟೀಪುತ್ರ|ವೈಟ್ಟಭಟೀ|ವೈಟ್ಟಭಟಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|vaiṭṭabhaṭī-putra|vaiṭṭabhaṭīputra|vaiṭṭabhaṭī|vaiṭṭabhaṭi|putra|vaiTTabhaTii-putra|vaiTTabhaTiiputra|vaiTTabhaTii|vaiTTabhaTi|vaiTTabhaTee-putra|vaiTTabhaTeeputra|vaiTTabhaTee|_vei_120@3068|_vei2_pn328
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3067">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3069">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn327">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn328">2:328</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn329">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaiṭṭabhaṭī-putra</b> is the name in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 5, 2) of a teacher, a pupil of <b>Kāraśakeyīputra,</b> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैदभृतीपुत्र">vaidabhṛtīputra</a>.</b></p></div>

वैडव|వైడవ|ವೈಡವ|vaiḍava|vaiDava|_vei_120@3069|_vei2_pn328
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3068">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3070">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn327">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn328">2:328</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn329">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaiḍava,</b> ‘descendant of <b>Vīḍu,</b>’ is the patronymic of a <b>Vaisiṣṭha</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xi. 8, 14), where he is said to have been the seer of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> or Chant.</p></div>

वैदूर्य|వైదూర్య|ವೈದೂರ್ಯ|vaidūrya|vaiduurya|vaidoorya|_vei_120@3070|_vei2_pn328
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3069">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3071">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn327">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn328">2:328</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn329">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaidūrya,</b> ‘beryl,’ is first <b>found in</b> the late Adbhuta <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203070"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203070">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203070"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203070">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 40; <i>Omina und Portenta,</i> 325 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

वैतरण|వైతరణ|ವೈತರಣ|vaitaraṇa|vaitaraNa|_vei_120@3071|_vei2_pn328
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3070">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3072">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn327">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn328">2:328</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn329">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaitaraṇa</b> occurs once in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203071"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203071">1</a></sup> Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203071"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203071">2</a></sup> thinks the word is a patronymic, but it seems rather<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203071"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203071">3</a></sup> to be an adjective in the sense of ‘belonging to Vitaraṇa’ used of <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>, like <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a></b> or of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वध्र्यश्व">vadhryaśva</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203071"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203071">↑</a> x. 61, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203071"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203071">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203071"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203071">↑</a> Ludwig, Translation of the Rig. <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>, 3, 165; Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 2, 457, n.</li></ol></div>

वैतहव्य|వైతహవ్య|ವೈತಹವ್ಯ|vaitahavya|_vei_120@3072|_vei2_pn328
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3071">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3073">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn327">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn328">2:328</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn329">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaitahavya,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वीतहव्य">vītahavya</a>,</b>’ is the name of a family who are said in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203072"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203072">1</a></sup> to have come to ruin because they devoured a Brahmin's cow. They are said to be <b>Sṛñjayas,</b> but as the exact form of the legend here referred to does not occur elsewhere, its authenticity is open to some doubt.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203072"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203072">2</a></sup> According to Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203072"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203072">3</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="वैतहव्य">vaitahavya</a> is a mere epithet of the Sṛñjayas, but this is not probable<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203072"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203072">4</a></sup> in view of the existence of a <a class="vei_120a" href="वीतहव्य">vītahavya</a>. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203072"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203072">↑</a> v. 18, 10. 11; 19, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203072"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203072">↑</a> Moomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 434.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203072"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203072">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 132.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203072"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203072">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 405; Weber <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 233.</li></ol></div>

वैद|వైద|ವೈದ|vaida|_vei_120@3073|_vei2_pn329
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3072">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3074">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn328">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn329">2:329</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn330">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaida,</b> ‘descendant of Vida,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्यदन्त्">hiraṇyadant</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203073"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203073">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203073"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203073">2</a></sup> The word is also written Baida.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203073"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203073">↑</a> iii. 6, 4; Āśvalāyana Śranta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 10, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203073"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203073">↑</a> ii. 1, 5.</li></ol></div>

वैदथिन|వైదథిన|ವೈದಥಿನ|vaidathina|_vei_120@3074|_vei2_pn329
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3073">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3075">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn328">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn329">2:329</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn330">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaidathina,</b> ‘descendant of Vidathin,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋजिश्वन्">ṛjiśvan</a></b> in the Rigveda (iv. 16, 11; v. 29, 13).</p></div>

वैदद्-अश्वि|वैददश्वि|वैदद्|अश्वि|వైదద్-అశ్వి|వైదదశ్వి|వైదద్|అశ్వి|ವೈದದ್-ಅಶ್ವಿ|ವೈದದಶ್ವಿ|ವೈದದ್|ಅಶ್ವಿ|vaidad-aśvi|vaidadaśvi|vaidad|aśvi|vaidad-ashvi|vaidadashvi|ashvi|_vei_120@3075|_vei2_pn329
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3074">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3076">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn328">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn329">2:329</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn330">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaidad-aśvi,</b> ‘descendant of Vidadaśva,’ is the patronymic of <a class="vei_120a" href="तरन्त">taranta</a> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203075"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203075">1</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203075"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203075">2</a></sup> and the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203075"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203075">3</a></sup> the Vaidadaśvis are <a class="vei_120a" href="तरन्त">taranta</a> and <b>Purumīlha.</b> The latter is not a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैददश्वि">vaidadaśvi</a> in the Rigveda, a clear sign of the worthlessness of the legends relative to these two men in the Brāhmaṇas.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203075"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203075">↑</a> v. 61, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203075"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203075">↑</a> xiii. 7, 12. <i>Cf.</i> Śāṭyāyanaka in Sāyaṇa on Rv. ix. 58, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203075"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203075">↑</a> i. 151; iii. 139, where Vaitadaśvi is the form. <i>Cf.</i> Ārṣeya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, p. 54 (ed. Burnell).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 360; Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesell schaft,</i> 42, 232, n.; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 354; Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 62 <i>et seq.</i>.</li></ol></div>

वैदभृती-पुत्र|वैदभृतीपुत्र|वैदभृती|वैदभृति|पुत्र|వైదభృతీ-పుత్ర|వైదభృతీపుత్ర|వైదభృతీ|వైదభృతి|పుత్ర|ವೈದಭೃತೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ವೈದಭೃತೀಪುತ್ರ|ವೈದಭೃತೀ|ವೈದಭೃತಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|vaidabhṛtī-putra|vaidabhṛtīputra|vaidabhṛtī|vaidabhṛti|putra|vaidabhRtii-putra|vaidabhRtiiputra|vaidabhRtii|vaidabhRti|vaidabhRtee-putra|vaidabhRteeputra|vaidabhRtee|vaidabhRitii-putra|vaidabhRitiiputra|vaidabhRitii|vaidabhRiti|vaidabhRutii-putra|vaidabhRutiiputra|vaidabhRutii|vaidabhRuti|vaidabhRRitii-putra|vaidabhRRitiiputra|vaidabhRRitii|vaidabhRRiti|_vei_120@3076|_vei2_pn329
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3075">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3077">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn328">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn329">2:329</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn330">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaidabhṛtī-putra,</b> ‘son of a female descendant of Vedabhṛt,’ is the name of a teacher in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Mādhyaṃdina recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 4, 32). <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैट्टभटीपुत्र">vaiṭṭabhaṭīputra</a>.</b></p></div>

वैदर्भ|వైదర్భ|ವೈದರ್ಭ|vaidarbha|_vei_120@3077|_vei2_pn329
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3076">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3078">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn328">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn329">2:329</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn330">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaidarbha,</b> ‘prince of <a class="vei_120a" href="विदर्भ">vidarbha</a>,’ is applied to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भीम">bhīma</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vii. 34, 9).</p></div>

वैदर्भि|వైదర్భి|ವೈದರ್ಭಿ|vaidarbhi|_vei_120@3078|_vei2_pn329
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3077">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3079">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn328">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn329">2:329</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn330">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaidarbhi,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विदर्भ">vidarbha</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भार्गव">bhārgava</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रश्न">praśna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (i. 1; ii. 1).</p></div>

वैदेह|వైదేహ|ವೈದೇಹ|vaideha|_vei_120@3079|_vei2_pn329
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3078">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3080">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn328">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn329">2:329</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn330">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaideha,</b> ‘prince of <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>,’ is the title of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a></b> and of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नमी साप्य">namī sāpya</a>.</b></p></div>

वैधस|వైధస|ವೈಧಸ|vaidhasa|_vei_120@3080|_vei2_pn329
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3079">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3081">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn328">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn329">2:329</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn330">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaidhasa,</b> ‘descendant of Vedhas,’ is the patronymic of <b>Hariścandra</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Brāhmana (vii. 13, 1) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (xv. 17. 1). </p></div>

वैन्य|వైన్య|ವೈನ್ಯ|vainya|_vei_120@3081|_vei2_pn330
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3080">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3082">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn329">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn330">2:330</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn331">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vainya,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वेन">vena</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of the mythic <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पृथि">pṛthi</a>, Pṛthī,</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पृथु">pṛthu</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203081">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203081"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203081">↑</a> Rv. viii. 9, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 20; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>. v. 3, 5, 4, etc.</li></ol></div>

वैपश्चित|వైపశ్చిత|ವೈಪಶ್ಚಿತ|vaipaścita|vaipashchita|_vei_120@3082|_vei2_pn330
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3081">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3083">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn329">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn330">2:330</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn331">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaipaścita</b> (‘descendant of <b>Vipaścit</b>’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दार्ढ-जयन्ति">dārḍha-jayanti</a></b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृढजयन्त">dṛḍhajayanta</a></b>’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गुप्त लौहित्य">gupta lauhitya</a></b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="लोहित">lohita</a></b>’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैपश्चित">vaipaścita</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दार्ढजयन्ति">dārḍhajayanti</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दृढजयन्त">dṛḍhajayanta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="लौहित्य">lauhitya</a>,</b> in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 42, 1).</p></div>

वैपश्चित|వైపశ్చిత|ವೈಪಶ್ಚಿತ|vaipaścita|vaipashchita|_vei_120@3083|_vei2_pn330
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3082">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3084">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn329">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn330">2:330</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn331">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaipaścita</b> (‘descendant of <b>Vipaścit</b>’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दार्ढजयन्ति">dārḍhajayanti</a></b> (‘descendant of <b>Dṛdhajayanta</b>’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृढजयन्त">dṛḍhajayanta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="लौहित्य">lauhitya</a></b> (‘descendant of Lohita’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विपश्चित् दृढजयन्त लौहित्य">vipaścit <a class="vei_120a" href="दृढजयन्त">dṛḍhajayanta</a> lauhitya</a>,</b> in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 42, 1).</p></div>

वैयश्व|వైయశ్వ|ವೈಯಶ್ವ|vaiyaśva|vaiyashva|_vei_120@3084|_vei2_pn330
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3083">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3085">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn329">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn330">2:330</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn331">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaiyaśva,</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्यश्व">vyaśva</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वमनस्">viśvamanas</a></b> in the Rigveda (viii. 23, 24, 24, 23; 26, 11).</p></div>

वैयाघ्रपदी-पुत्र|वैयाघ्रपदीपुत्र|वैयाघ्रपदी|वैयाघ्रपदि|पुत्र|వైయాఘ్రపదీ-పుత్ర|వైయాఘ్రపదీపుత్ర|వైయాఘ్రపదీ|వైయాఘ్రపది|పుత్ర|ವೈಯಾಘ್ರಪದೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ವೈಯಾಘ್ರಪದೀಪುತ್ರ|ವೈಯಾಘ್ರಪದೀ|ವೈಯಾಘ್ರಪದಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|vaiyāghrapadī-putra|vaiyāghrapadīputra|vaiyāghrapadī|vaiyāghrapadi|putra|vaiyaaghrapadii-putra|vaiyaaghrapadiiputra|vaiyaaghrapadii|vaiyaaghrapadi|vaiyaaghrapadee-putra|vaiyaaghrapadeeputra|vaiyaaghrapadee|_vei_120@3085|_vei2_pn330
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3084">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3086">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn329">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn330">2:330</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn331">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaiyāghrapadī-putra,</b> ‘son of a female descendant of Vyāghrapad,’ is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काण्वी-पुत्र">kāṇvī-putra</a>,</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension of the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 5, 1).</p></div>

वैयाघ्र-पद्य|वैयाघ्रपद्य|वैयाघ्र|पद्य|వైయాఘ్ర-పద్య|వైయాఘ్రపద్య|వైయాఘ్ర|పద్య|ವೈಯಾಘ್ರ-ಪದ್ಯ|ವೈಯಾಘ್ರಪದ್ಯ|ವೈಯಾಘ್ರ|ಪದ್ಯ|vaiyāghra-padya|vaiyāghrapadya|vaiyāghra|padya|vaiyaaghra-padya|vaiyaaghrapadya|vaiyaaghra|_vei_120@3086|_vei2_pn330
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3085">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3087">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn329">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn330">2:330</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn331">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaiyāghra-padya,</b> ‘descendant of Vyāghrapad,’ is the patronymic of <b>Indradyumna <a class="vei_120a" href="भाल्लवेय">bhāllaveya</a></b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203086">1</a></sup> and the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203086">2</a></sup> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बुडिल आश्वतराश्वि">buḍila āśvatarāśvi</a></b> in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203086">3</a></sup> and of <b>Gośruti</b> in that <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203086">4</a></sup> and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203086">5</a></sup> In the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203086">6</a></sup> the patronymic is applied to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राम क्रातुजातेय">rāma krātujāteya</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203086">↑</a> x. 6, 1, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203086">↑</a> v. 14, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203086">↑</a> v. 16, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203086">↑</a> v. 2, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203086">↑</a> ix. 7 (Gośruta-<a class="vei_120a" href="वैयाघ्रपद्य">vaiyāghrapadya</a> as a compound).</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203086"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203086">↑</a> iii. 40, 1; iv. 16. 1.</li></ol></div>

वैयास्क|వైయాస్క|ವೈಯಾಸ್ಕ|vaiyāska|vaiyaaska|_vei_120@3087|_vei2_pn331
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3086">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3088">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn330">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn331">2:331</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn332">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaiyāska</b> is read in one passage of the Rigveda Prātiśākhya,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203087">1</a></sup> as the name of an authority on the metres of the Rigveda. Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203087">2</a></sup> is clearly right in thinking that <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a></b> is meant.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203087">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203087">↑</a> xvii. 25.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203087">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203087"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203087">↑</a> The name not being a patronymic from Viyāska, but standing for <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वै">vai</a> Yāskaḥ, Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैजान">vaijāna</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

वैर|వైర|ವೈರ|vaira|_vei_120@3088|_vei2_pn331
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3087">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3089">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn330">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn331">2:331</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn332">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaira</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203088">1</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैर">vaira</a>-deya</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203088">2</a></sup> seem to have in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas the definite and technical sense of ‘wergeld,’ the money to be paid for killing a man as a compensation to his relatives. This view is borne out by the Sūtras of Āpastamba<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203088">3</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203088">4</a></sup> Both prescribe the scale of 1,000 cows for a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203088">5</a></sup> 100 for a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>,</b> 10 for a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>,</b> and a bull over and above in each case. Āpastamba leaves the destination of the payment vague, but <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> assigns it to the king. It is reasonable to suppose that the cows were intended for the relations, and the bull was a present to the king for his intervention to induce the injured relatives to abandon the demand for the life of the offender. The Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203088">6</a></sup> allows the same scale of wergeld for women, but the <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203088">7</a></sup> puts them on a level with men of the <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> caste only, except in one special case. The payment is made for the purpose of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वैर">vaira</a>-yātana</i> or <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वैर">vaira</a>-niryātana,</i> ‘requital of enmity,’ ‘expiation.’</p><p>The Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203088">8</a></sup> preserves, also, the important notice that a man's wergeld was a hundred (cows), for it contains the epithet <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शत">śata</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="दाय">dāya</a>,</i> ‘one whose wergeld is a hundred.’ No doubt the values varied, but in the case of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a></b> the amount is a hundred (cows) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203088">9</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203088">10</a></sup> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शत">śata</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="दाय">dāya</a></i> again appears. </p><p>The fixing of the price shows that already public opinion, and perhaps the royal authority, was in Rigvedic times diminishing the sphere of private revenge; on the other hand, the existence of the system shows how weak was the criminal authority of the king (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a></b>).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Roth, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 41, 672676; Bühler and von Schroeder, <i>Festgruss an Roth,</i> 44-52; Bühler, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 2, 78, 79; 14, 201 Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 402 Jolly, <i>Recht und Sitte,</i> 131, 132; Delbrück in Leist, <i>Altarisches Jus Gentium,</i> 297.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203088">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvi. 1, 12. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 5, 2, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, ix. 2; Kapiṣṭhala Saṃhitā, viii. 5; Maitrāyaṇī, Saṃhitā, i. 7, 5, all of which have <i>vīram</i> for <i>vairam,</i> perhaps wrongly.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203088">↑</a> Rv. v. 61, 8 (on the exact sense of which, <i>cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 361; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 92; Oldenberg, <i>RgvedaNoten,</i> 1, 354); <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā xxiii. 8; xxviii. 2. 3. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203088">↑</a> i. 9, 24, 1-4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203088">↑</a> i. 10, 19, 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203088">↑</a> The crime of slaying a Brahmin is too heinous for a wergeld. See Āpastamba, i. 9, 24, 7 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>, i. 10, 18, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203088">↑</a> i. 9, 24, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203088">↑</a> i. 10, 19, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203088">↑</a> ii. 32, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203088">↑</a> vii. 15, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203088"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203088">↑</a> See n. 1. The word is not found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a>.</li></ol></div>

वैर-हत्य|वैरहत्य|वैर|हत्य|వైర-హత్య|వైరహత్య|వైర|హత్య|ವೈರ-ಹತ್ಯ|ವೈರಹತ್ಯ|ವೈರ|ಹತ್ಯ|vaira-hatya|vairahatya|vaira|hatya|_vei_120@3089|_vei2_pn332
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3088">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3090">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn331">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn332">2:332</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn333">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaira-hatya,</b> ‘manslaughter,’ is mentioned in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā (xxx. 13) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 5, 9, 5). <i>Cf.</i> <b>Vīrahan.</b></p></div>

वै-राज्य|वैराज्य|वै|राज्य|వై-రాజ్య|వైరాజ్య|వై|రాజ్య|ವೈ-ರಾಜ್ಯ|ವೈರಾಜ್ಯ|ವೈ|ರಾಜ್ಯ|vai-rājya|vairājya|vai|rājya|vai-raajya|vairaajya|raajya|_vei_120@3090|_vei2_pn332
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3089">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3091">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn331">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn332">2:332</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn333">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vai-rājya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राज्य">rājya</a>.</b></p></div>

वैरूप|వైరూప|ವೈರೂಪ|vairūpa|vairuupa|vairoopa|_vei_120@3091|_vei2_pn332
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3090">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3092">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn331">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn332">2:332</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn333">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vairūpa,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="विरूप">virūpa</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b>Aṣṭādaṃṣṭra</b> in the Pañcavimśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 9, 21).</p></div>

वैशन्त|వైశన్త|ವೈಶನ್ತ|vaiśanta|vaishanta|_vei_120@3092|_vei2_pn332
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3091">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3093">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn331">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn332">2:332</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn333">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaiśanta</b> is the name in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203092">1</a></sup> of a prince whose offering <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> is said to have deserted for that of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a></b> through the aid of the <b>Vasiṣṭhas.</b> Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203092">2</a></sup> thinks that the name is Veśanta, and that he was a priest of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पृथु">pṛthu</a>-Parśus;</b> Griffith<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203092">3</a></sup> says that probably a river is meant, but neither of these views is plausible.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203092">↑</a> vii. 33, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203092">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 173.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203092"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203092">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 2, 24, n. <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 130.</li></ol></div>

वैशंपायन|వైశంపాయన|ವೈಶಂಪಾಯನ|vaiśaṃpāyana|vaishaMpaayana|_vei_120@3093|_vei2_pn332
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3092">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3094">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn331">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn332">2:332</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn333">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaiśaṃpāyana,</b> ‘descendant of Viśaṃpa,’ is the name of a teacher, famous later, but in the earlier Vedic literature known only to the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka (i. 7, 5) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> Sūtras.</p></div>

वै-शालेय|वैशालेय|वै|शालेय|వై-శాలేయ|వైశాలేయ|వై|శాలేయ|ವೈ-ಶಾಲೇಯ|ವೈಶಾಲೇಯ|ವೈ|ಶಾಲೇಯ|vai-śāleya|vaiśāleya|vai|śāleya|vai-shaaleya|vaishaaleya|shaaleya|_vei_120@3094|_vei2_pn332
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3093">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3095">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn331">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn332">2:332</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn333">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vai-śāleya,</b> ‘descendant of Viśāla,’ is the patronymic of the mythic <b>Takṣaka</b> in the Atharvaveda (viii. 10, 29).</p></div>

वैशी-पुत्र|वैशीपुत्र|वैशी|वैशि|पुत्र|వైశీ-పుత్ర|వైశీపుత్ర|వైశీ|వైశి|పుత్ర|ವೈಶೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ವೈಶೀಪುತ್ರ|ವೈಶೀ|ವೈಶಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|vaiśī-putra|vaiśīputra|vaiśī|vaiśi|putra|vaishii-putra|vaishiiputra|vaishii|vaishi|vaishee-putra|vaisheeputra|vaishee|_vei_120@3095|_vei2_pn332
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3094">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3096">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn331">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn332">2:332</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn333">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaiśī-putra,</b> ‘the son of a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> wife,’ is mentioned in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203095">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203095"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203095">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 9, 7, 3; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 2.</li></ol></div>

वैश्य|వైశ్య|ವೈಶ್ಯ|vaiśya|vaishya|_vei_120@3096|_vei2_pn333
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3095">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3097">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn332">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn333">2:333</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn335">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaiśya</b> denotes a man, not so much of the people, as of the subject class, distinct from the ruling noble (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a></b>) and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,</b> the higher strata of the Āryan community on the one side, and from the aboriginal <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a></b> on the other. The name is first found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुष">puruṣa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="सूक्त">sūkta</a> (‘hymn of man’) in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203096">1</a></sup> and then frequently from the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203096">2</a></sup> onwards,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203096">3</a></sup> sometimes in the form of Viśya.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203096">4</a></sup></p><p>The <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> plays singularly little part in Vedic literature, which has much to say of <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> and Brahmin. His characteristics are admirably summed up in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203096">5</a></sup> in the adjectives <i>anyasya <a class="vei_120a" href="बलि">bali</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्">kṛt</a>,</i> ‘tributary to another’; <i>anyasyādya,</i> ‘to be lived upon by another’; and <i>yathākāmajyeyaḥ,</i> ‘to be oppressed at will.’ He was unquestionably taxed by the king (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a></b>), who no doubt assigned to his retinue the right of support by the people, so that the Kṣatriyas grew more and more to depend on the services rendered to them by the Vaiśyas. But the <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> was not a slave: he could not be killed by the king or anyone else without the slayer incurring risk and the payment of a wergeld (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैर">vaira</a></b>), which even in the Brahmin books extends to 100 cows for a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>. Moreover, though the <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> could be expelled by the king at pleasure, he cannot be said to have been without property in his land. Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203096">6</a></sup> thinks it is absurd to suppose that he could really be a landowner when he was subject to removal at will, but this is to ignore the fact that normally the king could not remove the landowner, and that kings were ultimately dependent on the people, as the tales of exiled kings show.</p><p>On the other hand, Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203096">7</a></sup> is clearly right in holding that the <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> was really an agriculturist, and that Vedic society was not merely a landholding aristocracy, superimposed upon an agricultural aboriginal stock, as Baden Powell<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203096">8</a></sup> urged. Without ignoring the possibility that the Dravidians were agriculturists, there is no reason to deny that the Āryans were  so likewise, and the goad of the plougher was the mark of a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> in life<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203096">9</a></sup> and in death.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203096">10</a></sup> It would be absurd to suppose that the Āryan Vaiśyas did not engage in industry and commerce (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पणि">paṇi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वणिज्">vaṇij</a></b>), but pastoral pursuits and agriculture must have been their normal occupations.</p><p>In war the Vaiśyas must have formed the bulk of the force under the <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a> leaders (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a></b>). But like the Homeric commoners, the Vaiśyas may well have done little of the serious fighting, being probably ill-provided with either body armour or offensive weapons.</p><p>That the Vaiśyas were engaged in the intellectual life of the day is unlikely; nor is there any tradition, corresponding to that regarding the Kṣatriyas, of their having taken part in the evolution of the doctrine of <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a>, the great philosophic achievement of the age. The aim of the Vaiśya's ambition was, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203096">11</a></sup> to become a <b>Grāmaṇī,</b> or village headman, a post probably conferred by the king on wealthy Vaiśyas, of whom no doubt there were many. It is impossible to say if in Vedic times a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> could attain to nobility or become a Brahmin. No instance can safely be quoted in support of such a view,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1203096">12</a></sup> though such changes of status may have taken place (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a></b>).</p><p>It is denied by Fick<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1203096">13</a></sup> that the Vaiśyas were ever a caste, and the denial is certainly based on good grounds if it is held that a caste means a body within which marriage is essential, and which follows a hereditary occupation (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a></b>). But it would be wrong<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1203096">14</a></sup> to suppose that the term <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> was merely applied by theorists to the people who were not nobles or priests. It must have been an early appellation of a definite class which was separate from the other classes, and properly to be compared with them. Moreover, though there were differences among Vaiśyas, there were equally differences among Kṣatriyas and Brāhmanas, and it is impossible to deny  the Vaiśyas' claim to be reckoned a class or caste it the other two are such, though at the present day things are different.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 213 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 7 <i>et seq.;</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 242, 243; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 76 <i>et seq.</i> (for the Epic <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203096">↑</a> x. 90, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203096">↑</a> v. 17. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203096">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 5. etc. See <a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203096">↑</a> Av. vi. 13, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 48, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203096">↑</a> vii. 29. <i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 439.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203096">↑</a> <i>India, Old and New,</i> 222 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203096">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 210 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203096">↑</a> <i>Indian Village Community,</i> 190 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203096">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203096">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, lxxx.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203096">↑</a> ii. 5, 4, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1203096">↑</a> Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India,</i> 55 <i>et seq.,</i> argues to the contrarya from Buddhist evidence; but this has no cogency for the Vedic period, and much, if not all, of it is hardly in point as concerns this issue.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1203096">↑</a> <i>Die sociale Gliederung,</i> 163, <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1203096"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1203096">↑</a> <i>Cf. Indian Empire,</i> 1, 347.</li></ol></div>

वैश्वा-मित्र|वैश्वामित्र|वैश्वा|मित्र|వైశ్వా-మిత్ర|వైశ్వామిత్ర|వైశ్వా|మిత్ర|ವೈಶ್ವಾ-ಮಿತ್ರ|ವೈಶ್ವಾಮಿತ್ರ|ವೈಶ್ವಾ|ಮಿತ್ರ|vaiśvā-mitra|vaiśvāmitra|vaiśvā|mitra|vaishvaa-mitra|vaishvaamitra|vaishvaa|_vei_120@3097|_vei2_pn335
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3096">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3098">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn333">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn335">2:335</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn336">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaiśvā-mitra,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>,</b>’ is the term by which that famous priest's line is referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vii. 17 <i>et seq.</i>).</p></div>

वैष्ठ-पुरेय|वैष्ठपुरेय|वैष्ठ|पुरेय|వైష్ఠ-పురేయ|వైష్ఠపురేయ|వైష్ఠ|పురేయ|ವೈಷ್ಠ-ಪುರೇಯ|ವೈಷ್ಠಪುರೇಯ|ವೈಷ್ಠ|ಪುರೇಯ|vaiṣṭha-pureya|vaiṣṭhapureya|vaiṣṭha|pureya|vaiShTha-pureya|vaiShThapureya|vaiShTha|_vei_120@3098|_vei2_pn335
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3097">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3099">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn333">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn335">2:335</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn336">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaiṣṭha-pureya,</b> ‘descendant of Viṣṭhapura,’ is the name of a teacher in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> in the Mādhyaṃdina recension (ii. 5, 20; iv. 5, 25). He was a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्डिल्य">śāṇḍilya</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रौहिणायन">rauhiṇāyana</a>.</b></p></div>

व्यछ|వ్యఛ|ವ್ಯಛ|vyacha|vyaCha|_vei_120@3099|_vei2_pn335
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3098">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3100">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn333">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn335">2:335</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn336">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vyacha</b> in <i>go-<a class="vei_120a" href="व्यछ">vyacha</a>,</i> the name of one of the victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203099"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203099">1</a></sup> is of uncertain signification. According to Sāyaṇa,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203099"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203099">2</a></sup> the compound denotes a ‘driver out of cows.’ Perhaps it means a ‘tormentor of cows,’ as the St. Petersburg Dictionary takes it. Weber<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203099"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203099">3</a></sup> renders it as ‘knacker of cows,’ Eggeling as ‘one who approaches cows.’</p><p>^4 <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 416.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203099"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203099">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 16, 1. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203099"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203099">↑</a> On <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203099"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203099">↑</a> <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 82, n. 11. This interpretation is supported by the use of the word in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a>, where it replaces the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोविकर्तन">govikartana</a></b> of other texts, See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रत्निन्">ratnin</a></b> (p. 200).</li></ol></div>

व्य्-अद्वर|व्यद्वर|व्य्|अद्वर|వ్య్-అద్వర|వ్యద్వర|వ్య్|అద్వర|ವ್ಯ್-ಅದ್ವರ|ವ್ಯದ್ವರ|ವ್ಯ್|ಅದ್ವರ|vy-advara|vyadvara|vy|advara|_vei_120@3100|_vei2_pn335
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3099">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3101">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn333">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn335">2:335</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn336">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vy-advara,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203100"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203100">1</a></sup> <b>Vy-advarī,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203100"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203100">2</a></sup> are the names of a ‘gnawing’ (<i>ad,</i> ‘eat’) animal in the Atharvaveda and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>. <i>Cf.</i> also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्यध्वर">vyadhvara</a>,</b> which the St. Petersburg Dictionary would read throughout. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203100"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203100">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 4, 1, 27. <i>Cf.</i> Av. vi. 50, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203100"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203100">↑</a> Av. iii. 28, 2, where a worm is certainly not meant.</li></ol></div>

व्यध्वर|వ్యధ్వర|ವ್ಯಧ್ವರ|vyadhvara|_vei_120@3101|_vei2_pn336
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3100">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3102">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn335">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn336">2:336</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn337">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vyadhvara,</b> ‘perforating,’ designates a worm in one passage of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203101">1</a></sup> where there seems to be no good reason to alter the reading to <a class="vei_120a" href="व्यद्वर">vyadvara</a>, though Whitney<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203101">2</a></sup> thinks that it may rather be connected with <i>vi-adhvan</i><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203101">3</a></sup> than with the root <i>vyadh,</i> ‘pierce.’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203101">4</a></sup> The term occurs with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मशक">maśaka</a>,</b> ‘fly,’ in the Hiraṇyakeśi <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203101">5</a></sup> and perhaps also in another passage of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203101">6</a></sup> where, however, both Whitney<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203101">7</a></sup> and Shaṅkar Paṇḍit read <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्यद्वर">vyadvara</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203101">↑</a> ii. 31, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203101">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 74.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203101">↑</a> This would mean ‘diverging from the road,’ ‘devious.’</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203101">↑</a> The Padapāṭha analyzes the word as <i>vi-adhvara.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203101">↑</a> ii. 16, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203101">↑</a> vi. 50, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203101"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203101">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 318, <i>Cf.</i> 135.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> also Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 316, 361, 487; Lanman in Whitney, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 318.</li></ol></div>

व्यल्कशा|వ్యల్కశా|ವ್ಯಲ್ಕಶಾ|vyalkaśā|vyalkashaa|_vei_120@3102|_vei2_pn336
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3101">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3103">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn335">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn336">2:336</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn337">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vyalkaśā</b> is the name of a plant in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203102"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203102">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203102"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203102">↑</a> x. 16, 13. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 70.</li></ol></div>

व्य्-अश्व|व्यश्व|व्य्|अश्व|వ్య్-అశ్వ|వ్యశ్వ|వ్య్|అశ్వ|ವ್ಯ್-ಅಶ್ವ|ವ್ಯಶ್ವ|ವ್ಯ್|ಅಶ್ವ|vy-aśva|vyaśva|vy|aśva|vy-ashva|vyashva|ashva|_vei_120@3103|_vei2_pn336
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3102">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3104">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn335">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn336">2:336</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn337">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vy-aśva</b> is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>, a protégé of the Aśvins,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203103">1</a></sup> mentioned in several hymns of the eighth Maṇḍala,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203103">2</a></sup> which may have been the composition of a descendant of his, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वमनस्">viśvamanas</a>.</b> In two other passages<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203103">3</a></sup> he is referred to only as a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> of the past, and Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203103">4</a></sup> points out that none of his own work appears in the Saṃhitā. The Rigveda also mentions<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203103">5</a></sup> the Vyaśvas, with whom Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203103">6</a></sup> is inclined to connect <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वश अश्व्य">vaśa aśvya</a>.</b> An <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आंगिरस">āṅgirasa</a></b> <a class="vei_120a" href="व्यश्व">vyaśva</a> occurs as a seer of Sāmans or Chants in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203103">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203103">↑</a> Rv. i. 112, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203103">↑</a> viii. 23, 16. 23; 24, 22; 26, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203103">↑</a> Rv. viii. 9, 10; ix. 65, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203103">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenlāndischen Gesellschaft</i> 42, 217.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203103">↑</a> Rv. viii. 24, 28.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203103">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 106.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203103"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203103">↑</a> xiv. 10. 9.</li></ol></div>

व्य्-अष्टि|व्यष्टि|व्य्|अष्टि|వ్య్-అష్టి|వ్యష్టి|వ్య్|అష్టి|ವ್ಯ್-ಅಷ್ಟಿ|ವ್ಯಷ್ಟಿ|ವ್ಯ್|ಅಷ್ಟಿ|vy-aṣṭi|vyaṣṭi|vy|aṣṭi|vy-aShTi|vyaShTi|aShTi|_vei_120@3104|_vei2_pn336
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3103">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3105">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn335">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn336">2:336</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn337">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vy-aṣṭi</b> is the name of a mythical teacher in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203104">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203104"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203104">↑</a> iv. 5, 22; iv. 5, 28 Mādhyaṃdina.</li></ol></div>

व्या-ख्यान|व्याख्यान|व्या|ख्यान|వ్యా-ఖ్యాన|వ్యాఖ్యాన|వ్యా|ఖ్యాన|ವ್ಯಾ-ಖ್ಯಾನ|ವ್ಯಾಖ್ಯಾನ|ವ್ಯಾ|ಖ್ಯಾನ|vyā-khyāna|vyākhyāna|vyā|khyāna|vyaa-khyaana|vyaakhyaana|vyaa|khyaana|_vei_120@3105|_vei2_pn337
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3104">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3106">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn336">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn337">2:337</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn338">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vyā-khyāna</b> in one passage of the Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203105"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203105">1</a></sup> clearly denotes a ‘narrative’ merely--viz., that of the dispute of <a class="vei_120a" href="कद्रू">kadrū</a> and Suparṇī. In other passages<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203105"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203105">2</a></sup> the word means simply ‘commentary.’ In the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203105"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203105">3</a></sup> used in the plural, it signifies a species of writing, apparently ‘commentaries,’ though its exact relation to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अनुव्याख्यान">anuvyākhyāna</a></b> must remain obscure. Sieg<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203105"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203105">4</a></sup> thinks that the Vyākhyānas were forms of narrative like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अन्वाख्यान">anvākhyāna</a></b> and <a class="vei_120a" href="अनुव्याख्यान">anuvyākhyāna</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203105"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203105">↑</a> iii. 6, 2, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203105"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203105">↑</a> vi. 1, 27. 33; vii. 2, 4, 28.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203105"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203105">↑</a> ii. 4, 10; iv. 1, 6 (Mādhyaṃdina = 2 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>); 5, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203105"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203105">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 21, 34.</li></ol></div>

व्याघ्र|వ్యాఘ్ర|ವ್ಯಾಘ್ರ|vyāghra|vyaaghra|_vei_120@3106|_vei2_pn337
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3105">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3107">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn336">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn337">2:337</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn338">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vyāghra,</b> ‘tiger,’ is never found in the Rigveda, but frequently occurs in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203106">1</a></sup> as well as the lion This fact is legitimately regarded as an indication that the Atharvaveda belongs to a period when the Vedic Indian had approached and entered the territory of Bengal. Later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203106">2</a></sup> also, mention of the tiger is quite common. The <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203106">3</a></sup> preserves a reference to the danger of waking a sleeping tiger. The destructive character of the animal is often alluded to,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203106">4</a></sup> the man-eater (<i>puruṣād</i>)<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203106">5</a></sup> being also mentioned. Like the lion, the tiger passes as a symbol of strength.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203106">6</a></sup> This idea is illustrated by the fact that the king at the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a></b> (‘royal consecration’) steps<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203106">7</a></sup> on a tiger's skin to win himself the strength of the animal. <i>Cf.</i> also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शार्दूल">śārdūla</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पेत्व">petva</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203106">↑</a> iv. 3, 1; 36, 6; vi. 38, 1; 103, 3; 140, 1; xii. 1, 49; 2, 43; xix. 46, 5; 49, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203106">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 5, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 9; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiv. 9; xix. 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 5, 3 Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 7, 1, 8; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 9, 3; 10, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203106">↑</a> v. 4, 10, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203106">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Av. iv. 36, 6; viii. 5, 11, and see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शशयु">śaśayu</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203106">↑</a> Av. xii. 1, 49.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203106">↑</a> Av. iv. 8, 4, 7. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203106"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203106">↑</a> Av. iv. 8, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 92. He does not wear the skin, as Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 79, says.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities</i> 249, 250.</li></ol></div>

व्याघ्र-पद्य|व्याघ्रपद्य|व्याघ्र|पद्य|వ్యాఘ్ర-పద్య|వ్యాఘ్రపద్య|వ్యాఘ్ర|పద్య|ವ್ಯಾಘ್ರ-ಪದ್ಯ|ವ್ಯಾಘ್ರಪದ್ಯ|ವ್ಯಾಘ್ರ|ಪದ್ಯ|vyāghra-padya|vyāghrapadya|vyāghra|padya|vyaaghra-padya|vyaaghrapadya|vyaaghra|_vei_120@3107|_vei2_pn337
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3106">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3108">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn336">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn337">2:337</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn338">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vyāghra-padya</b> is a false reading in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (v. 16, 1) for <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैयाघ्रपद्य">vaiyāghrapadya</a>.</b> </p></div>

व्याधि|వ్యాధి|ವ್ಯಾಧಿ|vyādhi|vyaadhi|_vei_120@3108|_vei2_pn338
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3107">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3109">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn337">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn338">2:338</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn339">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vyādhi,</b> ‘disease,’ occurs several times in Vedic literature.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203108">1</a></sup> The specific diseases are dealt with under the separate names, but the Vedic texts also mention innumerable bodily defects. The list of victims<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203108">2</a></sup> at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) includes a ‘dwarf’ (<i>vāmana, kubja</i>), a ‘bald’ person (<i>khalati</i>),<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203108">3</a></sup> a ‘blind’ man (<i>andha</i>),<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203108">4</a></sup> a ‘deaf’ man (<i>badhira</i>),<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203108">5</a></sup> a ‘dumb’ man (<i>mūka</i>),<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203108">6</a></sup> a ‘fat’ man (<i>pīvan</i>), a ‘leper’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सिध्मल">sidhmala</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="किलास">kilāsa</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203108">7</a></sup> a ‘yellow-eyed’ man (<i>hary-<a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a></i>), a ‘tawny-eyed’ man (<i>piṅgākṣa</i>), a ‘cripple’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पीठ">pīṭha</a>-sarpin</i>), a ‘lame’ man (<i>srāma</i>), a ‘sleepless’ man (<i>jāgaraṇa</i>), a ‘sleepy’ man (<i>svapana</i>), one<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203108">8</a></sup> ‘too tall’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अति">ati</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="दीर्घ">dīrgha</a></i>), one ‘too short’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अति">ati</a>-hrasva</i>), one ‘too stout’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अति">ati</a>-sthāla</i> or <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अत्य्">aty</a>-aṃsala</i>), one ‘too thin’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अति">ati</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कृश">kṛśa</a></i>), one ‘too white’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अति">ati</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="शुक्ल">śukla</a></i>), one ‘too dark’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अति">ati</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण">kṛṣṇa</a></i>), one ‘too bald’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अति">ati</a>-kulva</i>), and one ‘too hairy’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अति">ati</a>-lomaśa</i>).</p><p>In the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203108">9</a></sup> the man with bad nails and the man with brown teeth are mentioned along with sinners like the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दिधिषूपति">didhiṣūpati</a>.</b> The Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203108">10</a></sup> mentions a ‘white-spotted (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शुक्ल">śukla</a></i>), bald-headed man, with projecting teeth (<i>viklidha</i>) and reddish-brown eyes.’ Interesting is Zimmer's<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203108">11</a></sup> suggestion that <i>kirmira</i> found in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1203108">12</a></sup> means ‘spotty’ as an intermixture of races, but it is only a conjecture, apparently based on a supposed connexion of the word with <i>kṛ,</i> ‘mix.’ In the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1203108">13</a></sup> and the  <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1203108">14</a></sup> various epithets are applied to women, some of which seem to denote disease, and in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1203108">15</a></sup> the feminine adjectives, ‘antelope-footed’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋश्य">ṛśya</a>-padī</i>) and ‘bulltoothed’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वृष">vṛṣa</a>-datī</i>), probably refer to bodily defects.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203108">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 10, 3; Saḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 4, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203108">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 10. 17. 21; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 6, 1; 14, 1; 17, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203108">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 3, 6, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203108">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 2, 9; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 1, 9; 13, 2; viii. 4, 2; 9, 1; 10, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203108">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 2, 10; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 1, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203108">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203108">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="किलास">kilāsa</a></i> also in <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 3, 17; xxiii. 16, 11. etc.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203108">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 19, 1, where are added the man who winks too much (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अति">ati</a>-mirmira</i>), has too prominent teeth (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अति">ati</a>-dantura</i>) or too small teeth (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अति">ati</a>-kiriṭa</i>). and who stares excessively (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अति">ati</a>-memiṣa</i>), <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 84, n. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203108">↑</a> iv. 1, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 8, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Av. vii. 65, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203108">↑</a> xiii. 3, 6, 5. See Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 323, n.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203108">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 428.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1203108">↑</a> xxx. 21.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1203108">↑</a> xxx. 15, especially <i>avijātā</i> and <i>vijarjarā,</i> beside <i>avatokā</i> and <i>paryāyiṇī; atītvarī</i> and <i>atiṣkadvarī</i> are also possibly so to be understood. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 80.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1203108">↑</a> iii. 4, 11, 1, where <i>apaskadvarī</i> and <i>paryāriṇī</i> are read.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1203108"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1203108">↑</a> i. 18, 4. Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 314, understands the hymn as referring to the domestic cat, but this lacks plausibility. The sense of the other epithets there occurring is quite obscure.</li></ol></div>

व्य्-आन|व्यान|व्य्|आन|వ్య్-ఆన|వ్యాన|వ్య్|ఆన|ವ್ಯ್-ಆನ|ವ್ಯಾನ|ವ್ಯ್|ಆನ|vy-āna|vyāna|vy|āna|vy-aana|vyaana|aana|_vei_120@3109|_vei2_pn339
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3108">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3110">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn338">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn339">2:339</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn340">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vy-āna</b> is the name of one of the vital airs. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a>.</b></p></div>

व्याम|వ్యామ|ವ್ಯಾಮ|vyāma|vyaama|_vei_120@3110|_vei2_pn339
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3109">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3111">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn338">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn339">2:339</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn340">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vyāma</b> in the Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203110"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203110">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203110"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203110">2</a></sup> denotes the ‘span’ of the outstretched arms as a measure of length. It may be estimated at six feet or equivalent to a fathom.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203110"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203110">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203110"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203110">↑</a> Av. vi. 137, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 1, 1, 4; 2, 5, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203110"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203110">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 2, 3, 1. 2; i. 2, 5, 14; vii. 1, 1, 37, where the scholiast equates it to 4 Aratnis or cubits (while the scholiast on Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ix. 1, 9, regards it as equal to 5 Aratnis). According to the Śulva <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> of <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>, the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अरत्नि">aratni</a></b> equals 24 <i>aṅgula</i> (= (3/4) inch). See Fleet, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatie Society,</i> 1912, 231, 233, 234.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203110"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203110">↑</a> See Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 309, n. 5.</li></ol></div>

व्यास पाराशर्य|వ్యాస పారాశర్య|ವ್ಯಾಸ ಪಾರಾಶರ್ಯ|vyāsa pārāśarya|vyaasa paaraasharya|_vei_120@3111|_vei2_pn339
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3110">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3112">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn338">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn339">2:339</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn340">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vyāsa Pārāśarya</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पराशर">parāśara</a></b>’) is the name of a mythical sage who in the Vedic period is found only as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विष्वक्सेन">viṣvaksena</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teacheres) at the end of the Sāmavidhāna <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> and in the late <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203111"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203111">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203111"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203111">↑</a> i. 9, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 156; 4, 377; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 184, n. 199.</li></ol></div>

व्र|వ్ర|ವ್ರ|vra|_vei_120@3112|_vei2_pn339
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3111">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3113">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn338">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn339">2:339</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn340">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vra,</b> according to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203112">1</a></sup> means ‘troop’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203112">2</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203112">3</a></sup> Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203112">4</a></sup> sees in the word (in the feminine form of <i>vrā</i>) a designation in one passage of the village host which formed part of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a>,</b> and was composed of relations (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सु-बन्धु">su-bandhu</a></i>). On the other hand, Pischel<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203112">5</a></sup> thinks that in all the passages Vrā means ‘female,’ used either of animals<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203112">6</a></sup> or of  women who go to the feast (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="समन">samana</a></b>),<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203112">7</a></sup> or courtezans (<i>viśyā,</i> ‘of the people’),<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203112">8</a></sup> or, metaphorically,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203112">9</a></sup> the hymns compared with courtezans; these senses are perhaps adequate.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203112">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. Cf.</i> Bechtel, <i>Nachrichten der ko7niglichen Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften zu Go7ttingen,</i> 1894, 393.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203112">↑</a> i. 124, 8; 126, 5; iv. 1, 16; viii. 2, 6; x. 123, 2. He omits i. 121, 2, where Bo7litlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> treats the word as a feminine (<i>vrā</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203112">↑</a> ii. 1, 1, a confused passage, on which see Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 37, 38.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203112">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 162.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203112">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 121, 313 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203112">↑</a> Rv. i. 121, 2; viii. 2, 6 (female elephants).</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203112">↑</a> Rv. i. 124, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203112">↑</a> Rv. i. 126. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203112"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203112">↑</a> Rv. iv. 1, 16; x. 123, 2; Av., <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

व्रज|వ్రజ|ವ್ರಜ|vraja|_vei_120@3113|_vei2_pn340
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3112">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3114">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn339">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn340">2:340</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn341">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vraja</b> denotes in the first instance, in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203113">1</a></sup> the place to which the cattle resort (from <i>vraj,</i> ‘go’), the ‘feeding ground’ to which the milk-giving animals go out<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203113">2</a></sup> in the morning from the village (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a></b>), while the others stay in it all day and night.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203113">3</a></sup> Secondarily it denotes the ‘herd’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203113">4</a></sup> itself. This is Geldner's view,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203113">5</a></sup> which seems clearly better than that of Roth<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203113">6</a></sup> who regards <a class="vei_120a" href="व्रज">vraja</a> as primarily the ‘enclosure’ (from <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वृज्">vṛj</a></i>), and only thence the ‘herd’; for the <a class="vei_120a" href="व्रज">vraja</a> does not normally mean an ‘enclosure’ at all: the Vedic cattle were not stall-fed as a general rule. In some passages, however, ‘pen,’<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203113">7</a></sup> in others ‘stall,’<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203113">8</a></sup> is certainly meant. The word is often used in the myth of the robbing of the kine.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203113">9</a></sup> It occasionally denotes a ‘cistern.’<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203113">10</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203113">↑</a> Rv. ii. 38, 8; x. 26, 3, and perhaps 97, 10; 101, 8. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="मेधातिथि">medhātithi</a> on <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>, iv. 45, and Mahābhārata, i. 41, 15, where <i>go-<a class="vei_120a" href="व्रज">vraja</a></i> is equal to <i>gavāṃ pracārāḥ,</i> ‘the pastures of the kine,’ in i. 40, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203113">↑</a> Rv. ii. 38, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203113">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Sāyaṇa on <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 18, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203113">↑</a> Rv. v. 35, 4; vii. 27, 1; 32, 10; viii. 46, 9; 51, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203113">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 282 <i>et seq.; Rigreda, Glossar,</i> 174. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 77.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203113">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> But <i>cf.</i> Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203113">↑</a> Av. iii. 11, 5; iv. 38, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 16. Metaohorically, in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 22, Mādhyaṃdina, it is a pen with a bolt (<i>sārgala</i>) and with a palisade (<i>sa-pariśraya</i>). The sense of ‘pen’ is also possible in Rv. x. 97, 10; 101, 8, and is not radically opposed to it, for <a class="vei_120a" href="व्रज">vraja</a> denotes the place where the cattle are fed, and can therefore be applied to the stall where they are during the night. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोष्ठ">goṣṭha</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203113">↑</a> Rv. x. 4, 2, where the ‘warm Vraja’ to which the cows resort is alluded to, and iv. 51, 2, where the Dawns open wide the doors of the <a class="vei_120a" href="व्रज">vraja</a> of darkness; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a>. <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8, 12, 2, where the <a class="vei_120a" href="व्रज">vraja</a> is said to be made of <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वत्थ">aśvattha</a> wood. The sense of ‘stall’ is probable in Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, i. 25.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203113">↑</a> See Geldner, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 283 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203113"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203113">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x. 4 = <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 11, 1 = Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 7.</li></ol></div>

व्रत|వ్రత|ವ್ರತ|vrata|_vei_120@3114|_vei2_pn341
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3113">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3115">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn340">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn341">2:341</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn342">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vrata</b> (‘vow’) in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203114"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203114">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203114"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203114">2</a></sup> has the peculiar sense of the ‘milk’ used by one who is living on that beverage alone as a vow or penance.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203114"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203114">↑</a> Av. vi. 133, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 5, 3. 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iv. 11. etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203114"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203114">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 2, 10. 14, 17; 4, 2, 15; ix. 2, 1, 18. <i>Cf. ghṛtavrata,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xviii. 2, 5. 6, and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रत">vrata</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="दुघा">dughā</a>,</i> the ‘cow that gives the <a class="vei_120a" href="व्रत">vrata</a> milk,’ Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 2, 14; xiv. 3, 1, 34. etc.</li></ol></div>

व्रतति|వ్రతతి|ವ್ರತತಿ|vratati|_vei_120@3115|_vei2_pn341
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3114">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3116">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn340">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn341">2:341</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn342">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vratati</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203115"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203115">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203115"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203115">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘creeping plant.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203115"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203115">↑</a> viii. 40, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, i. 14; vi. 28.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203115"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203115">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 1, 3, etc.</li></ol></div>

व्राज-पति|व्राजपति|व्राज|पति|వ్రాజ-పతి|వ్రాజపతి|వ్రాజ|పతి|ವ್ರಾಜ-ಪತಿ|ವ್ರಾಜಪತಿ|ವ್ರಾಜ|ಪತಿ|vrāja-pati|vrājapati|vrāja|pati|vraaja-pati|vraajapati|vraaja|_vei_120@3116|_vei2_pn341
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3115">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3117">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn340">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn341">2:341</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn342">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vrāja-pati</b> is found in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203116"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203116">1</a></sup> where it is said that comrades attend <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, as the <b>Kulapas</b> the <a class="vei_120a" href="व्राजपति">vrājapati</a>, when he goes about. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203116"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203116">2</a></sup> thinks that this refers to the heads of families being subordinate in war to the village headman (<b>Grāmaṇī</b>), but Whitney<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203116"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203116">3</a></sup> seems to be right in seeing merely the chieftain surrounded by the leading men, the family heads, not necessarily merely a village headman. <a class="vei_120a" href="व्राज">vrāja</a> alone occurs in one passage of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203116"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203116">4</a></sup> adverbially in the sense of ‘in troops.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203116"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203116">↑</a> x. 179, 2 = Av. vii. 72, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203116"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203116">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 171.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203116"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203116">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 436.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203116"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203116">↑</a> i. 16, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 17.</li></ol></div>

व्राज-बाहु|व्राजबाहु|व्राज|बाहु|వ్రాజ-బాహు|వ్రాజబాహు|వ్రాజ|బాహు|ವ್ರಾಜ-ಬಾಹು|ವ್ರಾಜಬಾಹು|ವ್ರಾಜ|ಬಾಹು|vrāja-bāhu|vrājabāhu|vrāja|bāhu|vraaja-baahu|vraajabaahu|vraaja|baahu|_vei_120@3117|_vei2_pn341
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3116">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3118">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn340">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn341">2:341</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn342">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vrāja-bāhu</b> is used in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (ii. 9) of the ‘encompassing arms’ of death, <a class="vei_120a" href="व्राज">vrāja</a> here apparently meaning a ‘pen,’ like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रज">vraja</a>.</b> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विष्ठा-व्राजिन्">viṣṭhā-vrājin</a>.</b></p></div>

व्रात|వ్రాత|ವ್ರಾತ|vrāta|vraata|_vei_120@3118|_vei2_pn341
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3117">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3119">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn340">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn341">2:341</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn342">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vrāta</b> is found in several passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203118"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203118">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203118"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203118">2</a></sup> in the sense of ‘troop.’ In one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203118"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203118">3</a></sup> the troops of the Maruts are referred to by three different terms--<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शर्ध">śardha</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात">vrāta</a>,</i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="गण">gaṇa</a>.</i> From this fact Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203118"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203118">4</a></sup> has  deduced that a Vedic host fought according to clan (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्">viś</a></b>), village (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम">grāma</a></b>), and family, but this conclusion is hardly warranted, there being nothing to show that there is any intention to present a distinct series of divisions. It is not probable that the word ever has the technical sense of ‘guild,’ as Roth<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203118"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203118">5</a></sup> thinks. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रातपति">vrātapati</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203118"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203118">↑</a> i. 163, 8; iii. 26, 2; v. 53, 11; ix. 14, 2 (perhaps an allusion to the five tribes); x. 34, 8. 12 (of dice). In x. 57, 5, the host of the living (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="जीव">jīva</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात">vrāta</a></i>) is referred to.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203118"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203118">↑</a> Av. ii. 9, 2 (‘host of the living’); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 10, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 25; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 9. 24; xvii. 1, 5. 12, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203118"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203118">↑</a> v. 53, 11. <i>Cf.</i> iii. 26, 2, where <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शर्ध">śardha</a></i> is not mentioned.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203118"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203118">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 162.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203118"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203118">↑</a> In the St. Petersburg Dictionary, where this is taken to be the sense; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 9, 25; xvii. 1, 5, 12; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 25; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 10, 2.</li></ol></div>

व्रात-पती|व्रात-पति|व्रातपती|व्रातपति|व्रात|पती|पति|వ్రాత-పతీ|వ్రాత-పతి|వ్రాతపతీ|వ్రాతపతి|వ్రాత|పతీ|పతి|ವ್ರಾತ-ಪತೀ|ವ್ರಾತ-ಪತಿ|ವ್ರಾತಪತೀ|ವ್ರಾತಪತಿ|ವ್ರಾತ|ಪತೀ|ಪತಿ|vrāta-patī|vrāta-pati|vrātapatī|vrātapati|vrāta|patī|pati|vraata-patii|vraata-pati|vraatapatii|vraatapati|vraata|patii|vraata-patee|vraatapatee|patee|_vei_120@3119|_vei2_pn342
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3118">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3120">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn341">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn342">2:342</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn345">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vrāta-patī,</b> ‘lord of troops,’ is an epithet included in the names of <a class="vei_120a" href="रुद्र">rudra</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203119">1</a></sup> along with the epithet <a class="vei_120a" href="गण">gaṇa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>, ‘lord of groups.’ The exact sense is quite uncertain, but the term may allude to the chief of a band of robbers, as Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203119">2</a></sup> thinks.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203119">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 4, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 13; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9, 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 25.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203119"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203119">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 179.</li></ol></div>

व्रात्य|వ్రాత్య|ವ್ರಾತ್ಯ|vrātya|vraatya|_vei_120@3120|_vei2_pn342
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3119">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3121">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn341">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn342">2:342</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn345">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vrātya</b> is included in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203120">1</a></sup> where, however, no further explanation of the name is given. Fuller information is furnished by the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203120">2</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203120">3</a></sup> and the Sūtras.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203120">4</a></sup> which describe at length a certain rite intended for the use of Vrātyas. According to the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, there are four different kinds of ‘outcasts’--viz., the <i>hīna,</i> who are merely described as ‘depressed’; those who have become outcasts for some sin (<i>nindita</i>); those who become outcasts at an early age, apparently by living among outcasts; and those old men who, being impotent (<i>śama-nīcameḍhra</i>), have gone to live with outcasts. The last three categories are by no means of the same importance as the first. The motive of the fourth is hard to understand: according to Rājārām Rām krishṇa Bhāgavat,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203120">5</a></sup> they were men who had enfeebled their constitutions by undue intercourse with women in the lands of the outcasts, and returned home in a debilitated state. But this is not stated in the text.</p><p>It seems probable that the really important Vrātyas were those referred to as <i>hīna,</i> and that the other classes were only subsidiary. According to Rājārām,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203120">6</a></sup> there were two categories of the first class: (<i>a</i>) The depressed (<i>hīna</i>), who were nonĀryan; and (<i>b</i>) degraded Āryans (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="गर">gara</a>-gir</i>). This, however, is a mere guess, and devoid of probability. There seems to have been but one class of Vrātyas. That they were non-Āryan is not probable, for it is expressly said<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203120">7</a></sup> that, though unconsecrated, they spoke the tongue of the consecrated: they were thus apparently Āryans. This view is confirmed by the statement that ‘they call what is easy of utterance, difficult to utter’: probable they and already a somewhat Prakritic form of speech (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वाच्">vāc</a></b>). The Sūtras mention their Arhants (‘saints’) and Yaudhas (‘warrioes’), corresponding to the Brahminical <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>.</p><p>Other particulars accord with the view that they were Āryans outside the sphere of Brahmin culture. Thus they are said<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203120">8</a></sup> not to practise agriculture or commerce (an allusion to a nomadic life), not to observe the rules of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मचर्य">brahmacarya</a></b>--<i>i.e.,</i> the principle regulating the Brahminic order of life. They were also allowed to become members of the Brahminical community by performance of the ritual prescribed, which would hardly be so natural in the case of non-Āryans.</p><p>Some details are given of the life and dress of the Vrātyas. Their principles were opposed to those of the Brahmins: they beat those unworthy of correction.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203120">9</a></sup> Their leder (Gṛhapati) or householder wore a turbrn (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="उष्णीष">uṣṇīṣa</a></b>), carried a whip (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतोद">pratoda</a></b>), a kind of bow (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="ज्याह्रोड">jyāhroḍa</a></b>), was clothed in a black (<i>kṛṣṇaśa</i>) garment and two skins (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="अजिन">ajina</a></b>), black and white (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्ण">kṛṣṇa</a>-valakṣa</i>), and owned a rough vagon (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="विपथ">vipatha</a></b>) covered with planks  (<i>phalakāstīrṇa</i>). The others,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203120">10</a></sup> subordinate to the leader, had garments with fringes of red (<i>valūkāntāni dāmatūṣāṇi</i>), two fringes on each, skins folded double (<i>dviṣaṃhitāny ajināni</i>), and sandals (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपानह्">upānah</a></b>). The leader wore also an ornament (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="निष्क">niṣka</a></b>) of silver, which Rājārām<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203120">11</a></sup> converts into a silver coinage. The Vrātyas, on becoming consecrated, were expected to hand over their goods to the priest. Many other details are given in the Sūtras (<i>e.g.,</i> that the shoes or sandals were of variegated black hue and pointed), but these are not authenticated by the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.</p><p>The locality in which the Vrātyas lived cannot be stated with certainty, but their nomad life<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1203120">12</a></sup> suggests the western tribes beyond the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>. But they may equally well have been in the east: this possibility is so far supported by the fact that the Sūtras make the Brahmin receiving the gift of the Vrātya's outfit an inhabitant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मगध">magadha</a>.</b> The Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1203120">13</a></sup> does not help, for it treats the <a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात्य">vrātya</a> in so mystical a way that he is represented as being in all the quacters. Indeed, Roth<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1203120">14</a></sup> believed that it was here not a case of the <a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात्य">vrātya</a> of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> at all, but of a glorification of the <a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात्य">vrātya</a> as the type of the pious vagrant or wandering religious mendicant (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="परिव्राजक">parivrājaka</a></b>). This view is clearly wrong, as the occurrence of the words <i>uṣṇīsa, <a class="vei_120a" href="विपथ">vipatha</a>,</i> and <i>pratoaa</i> shows. It is probable that the 15th Book of the Atharvaveda, which deals with the <a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात्य">vrātya</a>, and is of a mystical character, exalts the converted <a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात्य">vrātya</a> as a type of the perfect <b>Brahmicārin,</b> and, in so far, of the divinity.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1203120">15</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203120">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 5, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203120">↑</a> xv. 1, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203120">↑</a> xvii. 1-4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203120">↑</a> Kātyāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 1; xxii. 4; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 6; Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxii. 5, 4-14. See Hillebrandt, <i>Ritualliteratur,</i> 139, 140.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203120">↑</a> <i>Journal of the Bombay Branch of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 19, 360.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203120">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 359.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203120">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvii. 1</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203120">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> xvii. 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203120">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> xvii. 1, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203120">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> xvii. 1, 15. The exact sense of the passages is obscure; and was, as Lāṭyāyana shows, already obscure in his time and earlier; the translations given are all vague. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 32 <i>et seq.; Indian Literature,</i> 67, 68; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 31, 32; Rājārām, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203120">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 361.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1203120">↑</a> Which is indicated by their name, ‘belonging to a roving band’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात">vrāta</a></i>), ‘vagrant.’</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1203120">↑</a> See Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 770 <i>et seq.,</i> with <b>Lanman's</b> additions.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1203120">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1203120"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1203120">↑</a> Boomfield, <i>Atharvaveda,</i> 94.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> i. 33, 52, 445, n.; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 67, 78, 110112, 14, 146; Aufrecht, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 130 <i>et seq.;</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, xxvi <i>et seq.;</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 216.</li></ol></div>

व्रीहि|వ్రీహి|ವ್ರೀಹಿ|vrīhi|vriihi|vreehi|_vei_120@3121|_vei2_pn345
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3120">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3122">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn342">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn345">2:345</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn346">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vrīhi,</b> ‘rice,’ is never mentioned in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203121"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203121">1</a></sup> but is frequently alluded to in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203121"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203121">2</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203121"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203121">3</a></sup> Rice seems to be indigenous in the south-east of India:<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203121"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203121">4</a></sup> this fact accounts well for the absence of any mention of it in the Rigveda. Black and white rice is contrasted in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203121"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203121">5</a></sup> where also<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203121"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203121">6</a></sup> the distinctions of dark, swift-growing (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="आशु">āśu</a></i>), and large rice (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="महा">mahā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="व्रीहि">vrīhi</a></i>) are found. Probably the swift-growing variety is that later known as <i>ṣaṣṭika,</i> ‘ripening in sixty days.’ <a class="vei_120a" href="व्रीहि">vrīhi</a> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यव">yava</a>,</b> ‘barley,’ are normally conjoined in the texts.^7 <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्लाशुक">plāśuka</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203121"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203121">↑</a> To take <i><a class="vei_120a" href="धान्य">dhānya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="बीज">bīja</a></i> in Rv. v. 53, 13, as ‘rice seeds’ is unnecessary and very improbable, nor is there better reason to see in <i><a class="vei_120a" href="धान्य">dhānya</a> rasa</i> in Av. ii. 26, 5. a ‘rice drink.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203121"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203121">↑</a> vi. 140, 2; viii. 7, 20; ix. 6, 14, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203121"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203121">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 2, 10, 3, where it is said to ripen in autumn; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, x. 6; xi. 5; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 10, 2; iv. 3, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 12; AitareyaBrāhmaṇa, ii. 8, 7; 11, 12; viii. 16, 3.4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 5, 9; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 3, 22 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 3, 13 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>); Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 14, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203121"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203121">↑</a> ii. 3, 1, 3. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, 1, 7, 3, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 4. 5, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203121"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203121">↑</a> i. 8, 10, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203121"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203121">↑</a> Av. xi. 4, 13; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 43; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 10, 6, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 239.</li></ol></div>

व्लेष्क|వ్లేష్క|ವ್ಲೇಷ್ಕ|vleṣka|vleShka|_vei_120@3122|_vei2_pn345
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3121">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3123">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn342">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn345">2:345</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn346">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vleṣka.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्लेष्क">bleṣka</a>.</b></p></div>

शंयु|శంయు|ಶಂಯು|śaṃyu|shaMyu|_vei_120@3123|_vei2_pn345
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3122">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3124">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn342">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn345">2:345</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn346">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaṃyu</b> is the name of a mythical son of <a class="vei_120a" href="बृहस्पति">bṛhaspati</a>. He is quoted as a teacher in the texts of the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203123"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203123">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203123"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203123">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 10, 1; v. 2, 6, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 8, 11; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 9, 1, 24; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, i. 5, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Lévi, <i>La Doctrine du Sacrifice,</i> 113.</li></ol></div>

शकट|శకట|ಶಕಟ|śakaṭa|shakaTa|_vei_120@3124|_vei2_pn345
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3123">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3125">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn342">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn345">2:345</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn346">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śakaṭa,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203124">1</a></sup> <b>Śakaṭī,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203124">2</a></sup> are rare words in the older literature for a ‘cart.’ The creaking of a cart is referred to in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203124">2</a></sup> as like the sound heard by night in the forest. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203124">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 22; xi. 47; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203124">↑</a> Rv. x. 146, 3; Saḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203124"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203124">↑</a> Rv. x. 146, 3; Saḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 7.</li></ol></div>

शक-धूम|शकधूम|शक|धूम|శక-ధూమ|శకధూమ|శక|ధూమ|ಶಕ-ಧೂಮ|ಶಕಧೂಮ|ಶಕ|ಧೂಮ|śaka-dhūma|śakadhūma|śaka|dhūma|shaka-dhuuma|shakadhuuma|shaka|dhuuma|shaka-dhooma|shakadhooma|dhooma|_vei_120@3125|_vei2_pn346
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3124">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3126">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn345">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn346">2:346</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn347">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaka-dhūma</b> is found in one hymn of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203125">1</a></sup> where it is celebrated as the king of the asterisms. The word seems to mean the ‘smoke of (burning) cow-dung,’ or else the ‘smoke (rising) from (fresh) cow-dung’: it may well be, as Weber<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203125">2</a></sup> thinks, that this was deemed to be significant of the weather. Bloomfield,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203125">3</a></sup> however, considers that the word is to be rendered as ‘weather prophet,’ that is, one who foretells the weather by means of the smoke of a fire. Whitney<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203125">4</a></sup> objects to this view with reason. It is not at all improbable that, as Roth<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203125">5</a></sup> believed, an asterism of some sort is meant, probably the ‘milky way.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203125">↑</a> vi. 128, 1, 3. 4, and in the <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कल्प">kalpa</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203125">↑</a> <i>Omina and Portenta,</i> 363; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 257; 10, 65; <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 272, n.; 293.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203125">↑</a> <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 7, 484 <i>et seq.; Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, cxxxiii; <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 532, 533.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203125">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda. 377, 378.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203125"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203125">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 353; Caland, <i>Altindisches Zauberritnal,</i> 175, n. 3.</li></ol></div>

शकन्|శకన్|ಶಕನ್|śakan|shakan|_vei_120@3126|_vei2_pn346
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3125">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3127">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn345">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn346">2:346</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn347">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śakan.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शकृत्">śakṛt</a>.</b></p></div>

शक-पूत|शकपूत|शक|पूत|శక-పూత|శకపూత|శక|పూత|ಶಕ-ಪೂತ|ಶಕಪೂತ|ಶಕ|ಪೂತ|śaka-pūta|śakapūta|śaka|pūta|shaka-puuta|shakapuuta|shaka|puuta|shaka-poota|shakapoota|poota|_vei_120@3127|_vei2_pn346
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3126">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3128">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn345">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn346">2:346</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn347">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaka-pūta</b> (‘purified by cow-dung’) is the name, apparently, of a prince, in one hymn of the Rigveda (x. 132, 5).</p></div>

शकं-भर|शकंभर|शकं|भर|శకం-భర|శకంభర|శకం|భర|ಶಕಂ-ಭರ|ಶಕಂಭರ|ಶಕಂ|ಭರ|śakaṃ-bhara|śakaṃbhara|śakaṃ|bhara|shakaM-bhara|shakaMbhara|shakaM|_vei_120@3128|_vei2_pn346
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3127">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3129">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn345">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn346">2:346</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn347">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śakaṃ-bhara,</b> ‘bearer of dung,’ is found in one passage of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203128"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203128">1</a></sup> where the sense is doubtful. Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203128"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203128">2</a></sup> and Grill<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203128"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203128">3</a></sup> see in it a tribal name, Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203128"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203128">4</a></sup> the personification of excessive evacuation (diarrhœa), while Whitney<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203128"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203128">5</a></sup> considers that it may refer to the <b>Mahāvṛṣas,</b> despised as having to collect dung for fuel in the absence of wood in their country.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203128"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203128">↑</a> v. 22. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203128"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203128">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 510.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203128"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203128">↑</a> <i>Hundert Lieder^2,</i> 154.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203128"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203128">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 445, 446.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203128"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203128">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 259.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 253.</li></ol></div>

शका|శకా|ಶಕಾ|śakā|shakaa|_vei_120@3129|_vei2_pn346
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3128">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3130">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn345">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn346">2:346</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn347">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śakā</b> is the name of one of the victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203129"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203129">1</a></sup> It is uncer tain whether a kind of bird<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203129"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203129">2</a></sup> or fly,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203129"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203129">3</a></sup> or long-eared beast<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203129"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203129">4</a></sup> is intended.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203129"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203129">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 12, 1; 18, 1; Maitrāyaṇi Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 13; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 32.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203129"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203129">↑</a> Mahīdhara on Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203129"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203129">↑</a> Sāyaṇa on <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 12, 1; 18, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203129"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203129">↑</a> Sāyaṇa on <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 12, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 99.</li></ol></div>

शकुन|శకున|ಶಕುನ|śakuna|shakuna|_vei_120@3130|_vei2_pn347
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3129">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3131">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn346">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn347">2:347</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn348">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śakuna,</b> ‘bird,’ is mentioned frequently in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203130">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203130">2</a></sup> It usually denotes a large bird,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203130">3</a></sup> or a bird which gives omens.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203130">4</a></sup> Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203130">5</a></sup> compares  which also is a bird of omen.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203130">↑</a> iv. 26, 6; ix. 85, 11; 86, 13; 107, 20; 112, 2; x. 68, 7; 106, 3; 123, 6; 165. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203130">↑</a> Av. xii. 1, 51; 3, 13; xx. 127, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 2, 6, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvii. 53, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203130">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Av. xi. 2, 24, as compared with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वयस्">vayas</a>;</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203130">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 4; Maitrāyaṇī <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 34, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203130"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203130">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 430.</li></ol></div>

शकुनि|శకుని|ಶಕುನಿ|śakuni|shakuni|_vei_120@3131|_vei2_pn347
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3130">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3132">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn346">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn347">2:347</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn348">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śakuni,</b> ‘bird,’ is used practically like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शकुन">śakuna</a>,</b> but with a much clearer reference to divination. It was smaller than the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्येन">śyena</a></b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुपर्ण">suparṇa</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203131"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203131">1</a></sup> gave signs,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203131"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203131">2</a></sup> and foretold ill-luck.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203131"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203131">3</a></sup> When it is mentioned<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203131"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203131">4</a></sup> in the list of sacrificial victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’), a special species must be meant: later the falcon is so called, but the ‘raven’ may be intended; the commentator on the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā thinks that it is the ‘crow.’ It is mentioned several times elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203131"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203131">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203131"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203131">↑</a> Rv. ii. 42, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203131"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203131">↑</a> Rv. ii. 42, 1; 43, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203131"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203131">↑</a> Av. x. 3, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203131"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203131">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 19, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 40; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203131"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203131">↑</a> Av. ii. 25, 2; vii. 64, 1; xi. 9, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxv. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 15, 12; iv. 7, 3; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 1, 1, 31; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 8, 2, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 88, 430.</li></ol></div>

शकुनि-मित्र|शकुनिमित्र|शकुनि|मित्र|శకుని-మిత్ర|శకునిమిత్ర|శకుని|మిత్ర|ಶಕುನಿ-ಮಿತ್ರ|ಶಕುನಿಮಿತ್ರ|ಶಕುನಿ|ಮಿತ್ರ|śakuni-mitra|śakunimitra|śakuni|mitra|shakuni-mitra|shakunimitra|shakuni|_vei_120@3132|_vei2_pn347
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3131">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3133">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn346">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn347">2:347</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn348">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śakuni-mitra</b> is one of the names of <b>Vipaścit <a class="vei_120a" href="पाराशर्य">pārāśarya</a></b> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 41, 1).</p></div>

शकुन्त|శకున్త|ಶಕುನ್ತ|śakunta|shakunta|_vei_120@3133|_vei2_pn347
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3132">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3134">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn346">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn347">2:347</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn348">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śakunta</b> is a name for ‘bird’ in the Atharvaveda (xi. 6, 8). </p></div>

शकुन्तक|శకున్తక|ಶಕುನ್ತಕ|śakuntaka|shakuntaka|_vei_120@3134|_vei2_pn348
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3133">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3135">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn347">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn348">2:348</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn349">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śakuntaka,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203134"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203134">1</a></sup> <b>Śakuntikā,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203134"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203134">2</a></sup> are diminutives, meaning ‘little bird’ in the Saṃhitās.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203134"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203134">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="खिल">khila</a> after Rv. ii. 43; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 23.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203134"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203134">↑</a> Rv. i. 191, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 22.</li></ol></div>

शकुन्तला|శకున్తలా|ಶಕುನ್ತಲಾ|śakuntalā|shakuntalaa|_vei_120@3135|_vei2_pn348
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3134">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3136">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn347">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn348">2:348</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn349">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śakuntalā</b> is the name of an Apsaras who bore <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a>,</b> according to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203135"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203135">1</a></sup> at <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नाडपित्">nāḍapit</a>.</b> Weber<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203135"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203135">2</a></sup> doubtfully reads the latter word as Nāḍapitī, an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="शकुन्तला">śakuntalā</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203135"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203135">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203135"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203135">↑</a> <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 6.</li></ol></div>

शकुन्ति|శకున్తి|ಶಕುನ್ತಿ|śakunti|shakunti|_vei_120@3136|_vei2_pn348
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3135">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3137">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn347">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn348">2:348</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn349">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śakunti</b> is found in the Rigveda (ii. 42, 3; 43, 1) denoting a ‘bird’ of omen.</p></div>

शकुल|శకుల|ಶಕುಲ|śakula|shakula|_vei_120@3137|_vei2_pn348
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3136">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3138">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn347">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn348">2:348</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn349">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śakula</b> in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203137"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203137">1</a></sup> denotes an unknown species of fish.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203137"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203137">↑</a> Av. xx. 136, 1; Vājasanevi Saṃhitā, xxii. 28. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 97.</li></ol></div>

शकृत्|శకృత్|ಶಕೃತ್|śakṛt|shakRt|shakRit|shakRut|shakRRit|_vei_120@3138|_vei2_pn348
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3137">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3139">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn347">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn348">2:348</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn349">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śakṛt,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203138"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203138">1</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शकन्">śakan</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203138"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203138">2</a></sup> denotes ‘dung’ in the Rigveda and later. It is clear that the value of manure was early appreciated (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="करीष">karīṣa</a></b>). For the use of the smoke of dung or of a dung fire for prognosticating the weather, see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शकधूम">śakadhūma</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203138"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203138">↑</a> Used only in nominative and accusative: Rv. i. 161, 10; Av. xii. 4, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 1, 19, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203138"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203138">↑</a> In the oblique cases <a class="vei_120a" href="शकन्">śakan</a> is the base, Av. xii. 4, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 7, 23, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 9.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 236.</li></ol></div>

शक्ति|शक्ति|శక్తి_౧|శక్తి|ಶಕ್ತಿ_೧|ಶಕ್ತಿ|śakti_1|śakti|shakti_1|shakti|_vei_120@3139|_vei2_pn348
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3138">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3140">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn347">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn348">2:348</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn349">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Śakti</b> is said in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203139"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203139">1</a></sup> to have been the son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>,</b> and to have been cast into the fire by the <b>Viśvāmitras.</b> According to Ṣaḍguruśiṣya,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203139"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203139">2</a></sup> who appears to follow the Śāṭyāyanaka,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203139"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203139">3</a></sup> the story of <a class="vei_120a" href="शक्ति">śakti</a> is as follows: <a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>, being defeated in a contest by <a class="vei_120a" href="शक्ति">śakti</a>, had recourse  to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जमदग्नि">jamadagni</a>,</b> who taught him the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ससर्परी">sasarparī</a>;</b> later he revenged himself on <a class="vei_120a" href="शक्ति">śakti</a> by having him burnt in the forest. The Bṛhaddevatā<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203139"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203139">4</a></sup> relates the first part of the tale only. Geldner<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203139"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203139">5</a></sup> sees in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203139"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203139">6</a></sup> a description of the death struggle of <a class="vei_120a" href="शक्ति">śakti</a>, but this interpretation is more than doubtful.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203139"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203139">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203139"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203139">↑</a> ii. 390 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 47).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203139"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203139">↑</a> Sarvānukramaṇī, ed. Macdonell, p. 107, and on Rv. vii. 32.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203139"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203139">↑</a> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 159, n. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203139"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203139">↑</a> iv. 112 <i>et seq.,</i> with Macdonell's notes.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203139"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203139">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 159 <i>et seq.;</i> more doubtfully, <i>Rigveda, Kommentar,</i> 89.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203139"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203139">↑</a> iii. 53, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203139"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203139">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 254.</li></ol></div>

शक्ती आंगिरस|शक्ती आंगिरस|शक्ती आङ्गिरस_२|शक्ती आङ्गिरस|శక్తీ ఆంగిరస_౨|శక్తీ ఆంగిరస|శక్తీ ఆఙ్గిరస_౨|శక్తీ ఆఙ్గిరస|ಶಕ್ತೀ ಆಂಗಿರಸ_೨|ಶಕ್ತೀ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಶಕ್ತೀ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ_೨|ಶಕ್ತೀ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|śaktī āṅgirasa_2|śaktī āṅgirasa|shaktii aaMgirasa_2|shaktii aaMgirasa|shaktee aaMgirasa_2|shaktee aaMgirasa|shaktii aangirasa_2|shaktii aangirasa|shaktee aangirasa_2|shaktee aangirasa|_vei_120@3140|_vei2_pn349
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3139">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3141">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn348">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn349">2:349</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn350">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Śaktī Āṅgirasa</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अंगिरस्">aṅgiras</a></b>’) is the name of a seer of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> or Chant in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203140"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203140">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203140"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203140">↑</a> xii. 5, 16, <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 160.</li></ol></div>

शक्वरी|शक्वरि|శక్వరీ|శక్వరి|ಶಕ್ವರೀ|ಶಕ್ವರಿ|śakvarī|śakvari|shakvarii|shakvari|shakvaree|_vei_120@3141|_vei2_pn349
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3140">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3142">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn348">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn349">2:349</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn350">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śakvarī,</b> fem. plur., denotes the <a class="vei_120a" href="शक्वरी">śakvarī</a> verses, known also as the Mahānāmnī verses, to which the <a class="vei_120a" href="शाक्वर">śākvara</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> (chant) is sung. This sense seems to occur in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203141"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203141">1</a></sup> and is certain later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203141"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203141">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203141"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203141">↑</a> vii. 33, 4; x. 71, 14; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, i. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203141"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203141">↑</a> Av. xiii. 1, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 8, 5; 6, 2, 3; iii. 4, 4, 1; v. 4, 12, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxvi. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 6, 5; xii. 13, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 5, 11; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 1, 1; 9, 2, 17, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 258 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

शंकु|शङ्कु|శంకు|శఙ్కు|ಶಂಕು|ಶಙ್ಕು|śaṅku|shaMku|shanku|_vei_120@3142|_vei2_pn349
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3141">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3143">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn348">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn349">2:349</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn350">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaṅku</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203142"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203142">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203142"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203142">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘wooden peg.’ Thus the <b>term</b> is used of the pags by which a skin is stretched out in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203142"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203142">3</a></sup> and of the pin of hobbles (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="पड्बीश">paḍbīśa</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203142"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203142">4</a></sup> <b>In</b> the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203142"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203142">5</a></sup> it may mean ‘stalk’<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203142"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203142">6</a></sup> or ‘fibre of a <b>leaf.</b>’<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203142"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203142">7</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203142"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203142">↑</a> 1. 164. 48.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203142"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203142">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 5, 1, 1; 2, 2; 6, 1, 3; xiii. 8, 4, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 18, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203142"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203142">↑</a> ii. 1, 1, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203142"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203142">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka, vi. 2, 13 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 1, 13 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>), etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203142"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203142">↑</a> ii. 23, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203142"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203142">↑</a> Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 1, 35.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203142"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203142">↑</a> Little, <i>Grammatical Index,</i> 149, But <i>cf.</i> Oertel, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, 228, who compares <i>śūcī</i> in Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 10; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 10, 3.</li></ol></div>

शंख|शंख|शङ्ख_१|शङ्ख|శంఖ_౧|శంఖ|శఙ్ఖ_౧|శఙ్ఖ|ಶಂಖ_೧|ಶಂಖ|ಶಙ್ಖ_೧|ಶಙ್ಖ|śaṅkha_1|śaṅkha|shaMkha_1|shaMkha|shankha_1|shankha|_vei_120@3143|_vei2_pn350
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3142">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3144">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn349">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn350">2:350</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn351">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Śaṅkha</b> in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203143"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203143">1</a></sup> with the epithet <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृशन">kṛśana</a>,</b> denotes a pearl-shell used as an amulet. In the later literature<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203143"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203143">2</a></sup> it denotes a ‘shell’ or ‘conch’ used for blowing as a wind instrument.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203143"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203143">↑</a> iv. 10, 1. See Lanman in Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 161.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203143"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203143">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 4, 9; iv. 5, 10.</li></ol></div>

शंख कौष्य|शंख कौष्य|शङ्ख कौष्य_२|शङ्ख कौष्य|శంఖ కౌష్య_౨|శంఖ కౌష్య|శఙ్ఖ కౌష్య_౨|శఙ్ఖ కౌష్య|ಶಂಖ ಕೌಷ್ಯ_೨|ಶಂಖ ಕೌಷ್ಯ|ಶಙ್ಖ ಕೌಷ್ಯ_೨|ಶಙ್ಖ ಕೌಷ್ಯ|śaṅkha kauṣya_2|śaṅkha kauṣya|shaMkha kauShya_2|shaMkha kauShya|shankha kauShya_2|shankha kauShya|_vei_120@3144|_vei2_pn350
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3143">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3145">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn349">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn350">2:350</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn351">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Śaṅkha Kauṣya</b> is mentioned as a teacher whom <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जात शाकायन्य">jāta śākāyanya</a></b> criticized in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā (xxii. 7; <i>cf.</i> 6).</p></div>

शंख बाभ्रव्य|शंख बाभ्रव्य|शङ्ख बाभ्रव्य_३|शङ्ख बाभ्रव्य|శంఖ బాభ్రవ్య_౩|శంఖ బాభ్రవ్య|శఙ్ఖ బాభ్రవ్య_౩|శఙ్ఖ బాభ్రవ్య|ಶಂಖ ಬಾಭ್ರವ್ಯ_೩|ಶಂಖ ಬಾಭ್ರವ್ಯ|ಶಙ್ಖ ಬಾಭ್ರವ್ಯ_೩|ಶಙ್ಖ ಬಾಭ್ರವ್ಯ|śaṅkha bābhravya_3|śaṅkha bābhravya|shaMkha baabhravya_3|shaMkha baabhravya|shankha baabhravya_3|shankha baabhravya|_vei_120@3145|_vei2_pn350
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3144">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3146">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn349">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn350">2:350</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn351">↷</a><hr><p>3. <b>Śaṅkha Bābhravya</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बभ्रु">babhru</a></b>’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राम">rāma</a>,</b> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 41, 1; iv. 17, 1).</p></div>

शंख-धम|शंखधम|शंख|धम|शङ्ख-धम|शङ्खधम|शङ्ख|శంఖ-ధమ|శంఖధమ|శంఖ|ధమ|శఙ్ఖ-ధమ|శఙ్ఖధమ|శఙ్ఖ|ಶಂಖ-ಧಮ|ಶಂಖಧಮ|ಶಂಖ|ಧಮ|ಶಙ್ಖ-ಧಮ|ಶಙ್ಖಧಮ|ಶಙ್ಖ|śaṅkha-dhama|śaṅkhadhama|śaṅkha|dhama|shaMkha-dhama|shaMkhadhama|shaMkha|shankha-dhama|shankhadhama|shankha|_vei_120@3146|_vei2_pn350
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3145">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3147">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn349">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn350">2:350</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn351">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaṅkha-dhama,</b> a ‘conch-blower,’ is enumerated among the victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203146"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203146">1</a></sup> and is mentioned in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203146"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203146">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203146"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203146">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Brāhaṃa, iii. 4, 13, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203146"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203146">↑</a> ii. 4, 9; iv. 5, 10.</li></ol></div>

शंग शाट्यायनि|शङ्ग शाट्यायनि|శంగ శాట్యాయని|శఙ్గ శాట్యాయని|ಶಂಗ ಶಾಟ್ಯಾಯನಿ|ಶಙ್ಗ ಶಾಟ್ಯಾಯನಿ|śaṅga śāṭyāyani|shaMga shaaTyaayani|shanga shaaTyaayani|_vei_120@3147|_vei2_pn350
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3146">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3148">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn349">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn350">2:350</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn351">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaṅga Śāṭyāyani</b> (‘descendant of Śāṭyāyana’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आत्रेय">ātreya</a></b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अत्रि">atri</a></b>’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b>Nagarin,</b> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 40, 1).</p></div>

शचीवन्त्|శచీవన్త్|ಶಚೀವನ್ತ್|śacīvant|shachiivant|shacheevant|_vei_120@3148|_vei2_pn350
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3147">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3149">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn349">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn350">2:350</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn351">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śacīvant</b> is apparently the name of a man in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203148"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203148">1</a></sup> where the vocative <i>Śacīvaḥ</i> occurs. But Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203148"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203148">2</a></sup> perfers to read <i>Śacī ca</i> instead.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203148"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203148">↑</a> x. 74. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203148"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203148">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 108; Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 2, 489, n.</li></ol></div>

शण|శణ|ಶಣ|śaṇa|shaNa|_vei_120@3149|_vei2_pn350
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3148">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3150">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn349">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn350">2:350</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn351">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaṇa</b> denotes a kind of ‘hemp’ (<i>Cannabis sativa</i> or <i>Crotolaria juncea</i>). It is mentioned in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203149"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203149">1</a></sup> as growing in the forest, and as used like the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जंगिड">jaṅgiḍa</a></b> as a remedy against <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विष्कन्ध">viṣkandha</a>.</b> It also occurs in the Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203149"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203149">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203149"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203149">↑</a> ii. 4, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203149"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203149">↑</a> iii. 2, 1, 11 i. 6, 1, 24; 2, 15.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 68.</li></ol></div>

शण्ड|శణ్డ|ಶಣ್ಡ|śaṇḍa|shaNDa|_vei_120@3150|_vei2_pn351
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3149">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3151">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn350">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn351">2:351</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn352">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaṇḍa</b> is joined with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मर्क">marka</a></b> as a <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> of the Asuras in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203150"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203150">1</a></sup> and Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203150"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203150">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203150"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203150">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 4, 10, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 6, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, vii. 12. 13 (<a class="vei_120a" href="मर्क">marka</a> in 16. 17).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203150"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203150">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 2, 1, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, i, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 223.</li></ol></div>

शण्डिक|శణ్డిక|ಶಣ್ಡಿಕ|śaṇḍika|shaNDika|_vei_120@3151|_vei2_pn351
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3150">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3152">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn350">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn351">2:351</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn352">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaṇḍika</b> is found in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203151"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203151">1</a></sup> in the plural. According to Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203151"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203151">2</a></sup> the hymn is a prayer for victory over the Śaṇḍikas and their king.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203151"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203151">↑</a> iii. 30, 8,</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203151"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203151">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 153.</li></ol></div>

शत-द्युम्न|शतद्युम्न|शत|द्युम्न|శత-ద్యుమ్న|శతద్యుమ్న|శత|ద్యుమ్న|ಶತ-ದ್ಯುಮ್ನ|ಶತದ್ಯುಮ್ನ|ಶತ|ದ್ಯುಮ್ನ|śata-dyumna|śatadyumna|śata|dyumna|shata-dyumna|shatadyumna|shata|_vei_120@3152|_vei2_pn351
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3151">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3153">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn350">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn351">2:351</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn352">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śata-dyumna</b> (‘possessing a hundred glories’) is the name of a man who, along with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यज्ञेषु">yajñeṣu</a>,</b> was made prosperous by the priest <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मात्स्य">mātsya</a></b> through his knowledge of the exact moment for sacrifice, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 5, 2, 1).</p></div>

शत-पति|शतपति|शत|पति|శత-పతి|శతపతి|శత|పతి|ಶತ-ಪತಿ|ಶತಪತಿ|ಶತ|ಪತಿ|śata-pati|śatapati|śata|pati|shata-pati|shatapati|shata|_vei_120@3153|_vei2_pn351
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3152">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3154">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn350">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn351">2:351</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn352">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śata-pati</b> occurs in a verse of the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203153"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203153">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203153"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203153">2</a></sup> as an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, who is described as alone the ‘lord of a hundred’ among men. To interpret the expression as ‘lord of a hundred gods,’ as does the commentary on the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, is obviously impossible. It seems clear that there is a reference to an analogous human functionary--viz., the lord of a hundred villages, known in the later law<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203153"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203153">3</a></sup> --who was probably at once a judicial deputy of the sovereign and a revenue collector, an ancient magistrate and collector.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203153"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203153">↑</a> iv. 14, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203153"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203153">↑</a> ii. 8, 4, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203153"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203153">↑</a> See Foy, <i>Die ko7nigliche Gewalt,</i> 74.</li></ol></div>

शत-बलाक्ष मौद्गल्य|शतबलाक्ष मौद्गल्य|शत|बलाक्ष मौद्गल्य|శత-బలాక్ష మౌద్గల్య|శతబలాక్ష మౌద్గల్య|శత|బలాక్ష మౌద్గల్య|ಶತ-ಬಲಾಕ್ಷ ಮೌದ್ಗಲ್ಯ|ಶತಬಲಾಕ್ಷ ಮೌದ್ಗಲ್ಯ|ಶತ|ಬಲಾಕ್ಷ ಮೌದ್ಗಲ್ಯ|śata-balākṣa maudgalya|śatabalākṣa maudgalya|śata|balākṣa maudgalya|shata-balaakSha maudgalya|shatabalaakSha maudgalya|shata|balaakSha maudgalya|shata-balaaxa maudgalya|shatabalaaxa maudgalya|balaaxa maudgalya|_vei_120@3154|_vei2_pn351
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3153">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3155">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn350">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn351">2:351</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn352">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śata-balākṣa Maudgalya</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मुद्गल">mudgala</a></b>’) is the name of a grammarian in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> (xi. 6).</p></div>

शत-मान|शतमान|शत|मान|శత-మాన|శతమాన|శత|మాన|ಶತ-ಮಾನ|ಶತಮಾನ|ಶತ|ಮಾನ|śata-māna|śatamāna|śata|māna|shata-maana|shatamaana|shata|maana|_vei_120@3155|_vei2_pn351
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3154">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3156">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn350">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn351">2:351</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn352">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śata-māna.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मान">māna</a></b> and <b>Kṛṣṇaia.</b> </p></div>

शत-यातु|शतयातु|शत|यातु|శత-యాతు|శతయాతు|శత|యాతు|ಶತ-ಯಾತು|ಶತಯಾತು|ಶತ|ಯಾತು|śata-yātu|śatayātu|śata|yātu|shata-yaatu|shatayaatu|shata|yaatu|_vei_120@3156|_vei2_pn352
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3155">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3157">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn351">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn352">2:352</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn353">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śata-yātu</b> (‘having a hundred magic powers’) is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203156"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203156">1</a></sup> He is enumerated after <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पराशर">parāśara</a></b> and before <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>.</b> Geldner<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203156"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203156">2</a></sup> thinks he may have been a son of <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203156"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203156">↑</a> vii. 18, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203156"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203156">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 132.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 3, 139.</li></ol></div>

शत-रुद्रिय|शतरुद्रिय|शत|रुद्रिय|శత-రుద్రియ|శతరుద్రియ|శత|రుద్రియ|ಶತ-ರುದ್ರಿಯ|ಶತರುದ್ರಿಯ|ಶತ|ರುದ್ರಿಯ|śata-rudriya|śatarudriya|śata|rudriya|shata-rudriya|shatarudriya|shata|_vei_120@3157|_vei2_pn352
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3156">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3158">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn351">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn352">2:352</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn353">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śata-rudriya,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203157"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203157">1</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शत">śata</a>-rudrīya</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203157"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203157">2</a></sup> (hymn ‘relating to the hundred Rudras’), is the name of a section of the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203157"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203157">3</a></sup> which celebrates the god <a class="vei_120a" href="रुद्र">rudra</a> in his hundred aspects, enumerating his many epithets.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203157"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203157">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxi. 6; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 1, 1, 1; 2, 1; x. 1, 5, 3. 15.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203157"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203157">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 4, 3, 1; 5, 9, 4; 7, 3, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 11, 9, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203157"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203157">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 1-11; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 11-16; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 1 <i>et seq.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 202; Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 108, 111, 159, 169, 170; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 43, 150 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

शत-शारद|शतशारद|शत|शारद|శత-శారద|శతశారద|శత|శారద|ಶತ-ಶಾರದ|ಶತಶಾರದ|ಶತ|ಶಾರದ|śata-śārada|śataśārada|śata|śārada|shata-shaarada|shatashaarada|shata|shaarada|_vei_120@3158|_vei2_pn352
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3157">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3159">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn351">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn352">2:352</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn353">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śata-śārada</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203158">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203158">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘period of a hundred autumns’ or years.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203158">↑</a> vii. 101, 6; x. 161, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203158"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203158">↑</a> i. 35, 1; viii. 2, 2; 5, 21.</li></ol></div>

शतानीक सात्राजित|శతానీక సాత్రాజిత|ಶತಾನೀಕ ಸಾತ್ರಾಜಿತ|śatānīka sātrājita|shataaniika saatraajita|shataaneeka saatraajita|_vei_120@3159|_vei2_pn352
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3158">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3160">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn351">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn352">2:352</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn353">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śatānīka Sātrājita</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Brāhmana<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203159"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203159">1</a></sup> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203159"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203159">2</a></sup> as a great king who defeated <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धृतराष्ट्र">dhṛtarāṣṭra</a>,</b> the prince of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a>,</b> and took away his sacrificial horse. He was clearly a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a>.</b> He is also alluded to in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203159"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203159">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203159"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203159">↑</a> viii. 21, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203159"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203159">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 9-13.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203159"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203159">↑</a> i. 35. 1 = Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxiv. 52, in connexion with the <b>Dākṣāyaṇas.</b></li></ol></div>

शत्रि आग्नि-वेशि|शत्रि आग्निवेशि|शत्रि आग्नि|वेशि|శత్రి ఆగ్ని-వేశి|శత్రి ఆగ్నివేశి|శత్రి ఆగ్ని|వేశి|ಶತ್ರಿ ಆಗ್ನಿ-ವೇಶಿ|ಶತ್ರಿ ಆಗ್ನಿವೇಶಿ|ಶತ್ರಿ ಆಗ್ನಿ|ವೇಶಿ|śatri āgni-veśi|śatri āgniveśi|śatri āgni|veśi|shatri aagni-veshi|shatri aagniveshi|shatri aagni|veshi|_vei_120@3160|_vei2_pn352
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3159">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3161">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn351">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn352">2:352</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn353">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śatri <a class="vei_120a" href="आग्नि">āgni</a>-veśi</b> (‘descendant of Agniveśa’) is the name of a generous patron in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203160"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203160">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203160"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203160">↑</a> v. 34. 9. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig. Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 155.</li></ol></div>

शत्रु|శత్రు|ಶತ್ರು|śatru|shatru|_vei_120@3161|_vei2_pn352
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3160">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3162">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn351">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn352">2:352</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn353">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śatru</b> denotes ‘enemy’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203161"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203161">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203161"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203161">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203161"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203161">↑</a> i. 33, 13; 61, 13; ii. 23, 11; 30, 3 <i>et seq.;</i> iii. 16, 2; iv. 28, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203161"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203161">↑</a> Av. iv. 3, 1; vi. 4, 2; x. 3, 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

शं-तनु|शंतनु|शं|तनु|शन्तनु|శం-తను|శంతను|శం|తను|శన్తను|ಶಂ-ತನು|ಶಂತನು|ಶಂ|ತನು|ಶನ್ತನು|śaṃ-tanu|śaṃtanu|śaṃ|tanu|shaM-tanu|shaMtanu|shaM|shantanu|_vei_120@3162|_vei2_pn353
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3161">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3163">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn352">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn353">2:353</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn354">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaṃ-tanu</b> is the hero of a tale told by <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203162">1</a></sup> and often found later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203162">2</a></sup> He supersedes his elder brother <b>Devāpi</b> as king of the <b>Kurus.</b> When his improper deed brings on a prolonged drought in his realm, he is compelled to ask his brother to assume the kingship; Devāpi, however, refuses, but instead performs a sacrifice which produces rain. Sieg<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203162">3</a></sup> endeavours to trace this story in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203162">4</a></sup> but all that is there stated is that Devāpi <b>Ārṣṭiṣeṇa</b> obtained (no doubt as priest) rain for <a class="vei_120a" href="शंतनु">śaṃtanu</a> (no doubt a king). There is no hint of relationship at all.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203162">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203162">↑</a> Bṛhaddevatā, vii. 155 <i>et seq.,</i> with Macdonell's notes; Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 129 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203162">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203162"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203162">↑</a> x. 98.</li></ol></div>

शपथ|శపథ|ಶಪಥ|śapatha|shapatha|_vei_120@3163|_vei2_pn353
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3162">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3164">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn352">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn353">2:353</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn354">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śapatha</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203163">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203163">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘curse,’ not an ‘oath,’ as a judicial process. But that an oath of such a kind was possible as it was later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203163">2</a></sup> is shown by at least one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203163">3</a></sup> where the speaker, possibly <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>,</b> imprecates death on himself if he is a wizard, and death on his foes if he is not.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203163">↑</a> x. 87, 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vii. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203163">↑</a> Av. iii. 9, 5; iv. 9, 5; 18, 7; 19, 7. etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203163">↑</a> Av. iii. 9, 5; iv. 9, 5; 18, 7; 19, 7. etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203163"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203163">↑</a> vii. 104, 15.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 326, 327.</li></ol></div>

शफ|शफ|శఫ_౧|శఫ|ಶಫ_೧|ಶಫ|śapha_1|śapha|shapha_1|shapha|_vei_120@3164|_vei2_pn353
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3163">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3165">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn352">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn353">2:353</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn354">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Śapha,</b> ‘hoof,’ comes to be used to denote the fraction ‘one-eighth,’ because of the divided hoofs of the cow, just as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पाद">pāda</a>,</b> the ‘foot’ of a quadruped, also means a ‘quarter.’ This sense in found as early as the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203164"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203164">1</a></sup> and is not rare later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203164"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203164">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203164"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203164">↑</a> viii. 47, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203164"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203164">↑</a> Av. vi. 46, 3; xix. 57, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 10, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 3, 3, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, 278; 17, 47; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 259.</li></ol></div>

शफ|शफ|శఫ_౨|శఫ|ಶಫ_೨|ಶಫ|śapha_2|śapha|shapha_2|shapha|_vei_120@3165|_vei2_pn353
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3164">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3166">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn352">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn353">2:353</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn354">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Śapha</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203165"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203165">1</a></sup> is the name (used in the dual) of a wooden implement, acting like a pair of tongs, for lifting an iron pot from the fire. It is probably so called because it resembled a hoof in being divided. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203165"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203165">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 22, 14; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 2, 1, 16. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 458, n. 4; 476.</li></ol></div>

शफक|శఫక|ಶಫಕ|śaphaka|shaphaka|_vei_120@3166|_vei2_pn354
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3165">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3167">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn353">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn354">2:354</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn355">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaphaka</b> is the name of some plant in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203166"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203166">1</a></sup> It is also mentioned in the Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203166"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203166">2</a></sup> where it seems to denote an edible water plant or fruit, perhaps a water nut. It may be so called from its leaves being shaped like hoofs (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="शफ">śapha</a></b>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203166"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203166">↑</a> iv. 34, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203166"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203166">↑</a> ix. 14, 14.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 138; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 70; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda 207.</li></ol></div>

शफाल|శఫాల|ಶಫಾಲ|śaphāla|shaphaala|_vei_120@3167|_vei2_pn354
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3166">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3168">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn353">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn354">2:354</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn355">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaphāla</b> is the name of <b>Ṛtuparṇa's</b> kingdom in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> Sātra.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203167"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203167">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203167"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203167">↑</a> xx. 12. <i>Cf.</i> Caland, <i>Über das rituelle <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> des <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>,</i> 21, 36.</li></ol></div>

सबर|సబర|ಸಬರ|sabara|_vei_120@3168|_vei2_pn354
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3167">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3169">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn353">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn354">2:354</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn355">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sabara</b> is the name of a wild tribe who in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203168">1</a></sup> are classed as <b>Dasyus,</b> with the <b>Andhras, Pulindas, Mūtibas,</b> and <b>Puṇḍras.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203168"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203168">↑</a> vii. 18, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 26, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 483.</li></ol></div>

शमितृ|శమితృ|ಶಮಿತೃ|śamitṛ|shamitR|shamitRi|shamitRu|shamitRRi|_vei_120@3169|_vei2_pn354
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3168">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3170">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn353">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn354">2:354</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn355">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śamitṛ</b> denotes the ‘man who cuts up’ the slaughtered animal in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203169"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203169">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203169"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203169">2</a></sup> sometimes having merely the sense of ‘cook.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203169"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203169">↑</a> i. 162, 9 <i>et seq.;</i> ii. 3, 10; iii. 4, 10; v. 43, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203169"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203169">↑</a> Av. x. 9, 7 (‘cook’ of the Śataudanā, or offering of a cow and a hunḍred rice-dishes); Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvii. 57; xxi. 21; xxiii. 39; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 2; 7, 10-12; vii. 1, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 18, 4, etc.</li></ol></div>

शमी|शमि|శమీ|శమి|ಶಮೀ|ಶಮಿ|śamī|śami|shamii|shami|shamee|_vei_120@3170|_vei2_pn354
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3169">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3171">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn353">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn354">2:354</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn355">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śamī</b> is the name of a tree in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203170"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203170">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203170"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203170">2</a></sup> It is described in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203170"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203170">3</a></sup> as destructive to the hair,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203170"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203170">4</a></sup> as producing intoxication, and as broad-leaved. These characteristics are totally wanting in the two trees, <i>Prosopis spicigera</i> or <i>Mimosa suma,</i> with which the <a class="vei_120a" href="शमी">śamī</a> is usually identified.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203170"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203170">5</a></sup>  From the soft wood of the <a class="vei_120a" href="शमी">śamī</a> was formed the lower of the two sticks (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अरणी">araṇī</a></i>) used for kindling the sacred fire,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203170"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203170">6</a></sup> the upper one (the drill) being of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वत्थ">aśvattha</a>.</b> The fruit of the tree is called Śamīdhānya.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203170"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203170">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203170"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203170">↑</a> Av. vi. 11, 1; 30, 2, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203170"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203170">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 1, 9, 6; 4, 7, 4 (for the lower <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अरणी">araṇī</a></i>); <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvi. 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 3, 11 <i>et seq.;</i> 6, 4, 5; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 5, 2, 12; ix. 2, 3, 37, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203170"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203170">↑</a> Av. vi. 30, 2. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203170"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203170">↑</a> In the Dhanvantarīya Nighaṇṭu, p. 188 (ed. Poona), the <a class="vei_120a" href="शमी">śamī</a> and its fruit are said to destroy the hair.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203170"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203170">↑</a> See Roth in Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 302.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203170"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203170">↑</a> Av. vi. 11, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 1, 15; <i>Cf.</i> 13; iii. 4, 1, 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 1, 9, 6; 4, 7, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203170"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203170">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 1, 10.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 59, 60.</li></ol></div>

शम्बर|शंबर|శమ్బర|శంబర|ಶಮ್ಬರ|ಶಂಬರ|śambara|shambara|_vei_120@3171|_vei2_pn355
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3170">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3172">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn354">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn355">2:355</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn356">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śambara</b> is the name of an enemy of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203171">1</a></sup> He is mentioned along with <b>Śuṣṇa, <a class="vei_120a" href="पिप्रु">pipru</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्चिन्">varcin</a>,</b> being in one passage called a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>,</b> son of Kulitara.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203171">2</a></sup> In another passage<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203171">3</a></sup> he is said to have deemed himself a godling (<i>devaka</i>). His forts, ninety,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203171">4</a></sup> ninety-nine,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203171">5</a></sup> or a hundred<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203171">6</a></sup> in number, are alluded to, the word itself in the neuter plural once<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203171">7</a></sup> meaning the ‘forts of <a class="vei_120a" href="शम्बर">śambara</a>.’ His great foe was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दिवोदास अतिथिग्व">divodāsa atithigva</a>,</b> who won victories over him by Indra's aid.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203171">8</a></sup></p><p>It is impossible to say with certainty whether <a class="vei_120a" href="शम्बर">śambara</a> was a real person or not. Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203171">9</a></sup> is strongly in favour of the theory that he was a real chief as enemy of Divodāsa: he relies on the statistics<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203171">10</a></sup> of the mention of the name to show that, where as he was conceived as a real foe in the hymns of the time of Divodāsa, later texts, like those of the seventh Maṇḍala, make him into a demon, as a result of the change of scene from Arachosia to India. As a matter of fact, apart from this theory, <a class="vei_120a" href="शम्बर">śambara</a> was quite possibly an aboriginal enemy in India, living in the mountains.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203171">11</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203171">↑</a> i. 51, 6; 54, 4; 59, 6; 101, 2; 103, 8; 112, 14; 130, 7; ii. 12, 11; 14, 6; 19, 6; iv. 26, 3; 30, 14; vi. 18, 8; 26, 5; 31, 4; 43, 1; 47, 2. 21; vii. 18, 20; 99, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203171">↑</a> Rv. vi. 26, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203171">↑</a> Rv. vii. 18, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203171">↑</a> Rv. i. 130, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203171">↑</a> Rv. ii. 19, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203171">↑</a> Rv. ii. 14, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203171">↑</a> Rv. ii. 24, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203171">↑</a> Rv. i. 51, 6; 130, 7; ii. 19, 6; iv. 26, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203171">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 103, 108; 3, 273.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203171">↑</a> Seven times in Maṇḍala i; four in ii; two in iv; six in vi; two in vii. These references show <i>primâ facis</i> greater reality in Maṇḍala vi than elsewhere. The references in ii. are certainly all of the mythical kind, and those in vii are of much the same sort.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203171"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203171">↑</a> Rv. i. 130, 7; iv. 30, 14; vi. 26, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 177; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 161; Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 210; Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 178.</li></ol></div>

शम्बिन्|शंबिन्|శమ్బిన్|శంబిన్|ಶಮ್ಬಿನ್|ಶಂಬಿನ್|śambin|shambin|_vei_120@3172|_vei2_pn356
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3171">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3173">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn355">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn356">2:356</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn357">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śambin,</b> a word occurring only once, appears to mean ‘ferryman’ in the Atharvaveda (ix. 2, 6). The literal sense is probably ‘pole-man’ (from <i>śamba,</i> a word of doubtful signification found in the Rigveda, x. 42, 7).</p></div>

शंमद् आंगिरस|शंमद् आङ्गिरस|శంమద్ ఆంగిరస|శంమద్ ఆఙ్గిరస|ಶಂಮದ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಶಂಮದ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|śaṃmad āṅgirasa|shaMmad aaMgirasa|shaMmad aangirasa|_vei_120@3173|_vei2_pn356
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3172">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3174">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn355">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn356">2:356</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn357">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaṃmad Āṅgirasa</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अंगिरस्">aṅgiras</a></b>’) is the name of the seer of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> or Chant in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xv. 5, 11).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologic.</i> 2, 160.</p></div>

शम्या|శమ్యా|ಶಮ್ಯಾ|śamyā|shamyaa|_vei_120@3174|_vei2_pn356
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3173">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3175">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn355">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn356">2:356</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn357">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śamyā</b> denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203174">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203174">2</a></sup> a ‘peg,’ more particularly one on the millstone;<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203174">3</a></sup> and on the yoke,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203174">4</a></sup> where it seems to mean the pin of wood attached to either end so as to keep the yoke in place on the ox's neck.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203174">5</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="शम्या">śamyā</a> was also used as a measure of length.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203174">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203174">↑</a> x. 31, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203174">↑</a> Av. vi. 138, 4; xx. 136, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 7, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 10, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 5, 2, 7. etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203174">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 1, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 1, 22; 2, 1, 16 <i>et seq.;</i> v. 2, 3, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203174">↑</a> Rv. iii. 33, 13; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 4, 25; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, 6, 8, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203174">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 5, 20. <i>Cf.</i> xi. 1, 6; xv. 7, 6; Grierson, <i>Bihar Pcasant Life,</i> 194, and illustration, p. 33; Cuningham, <i>The <a class="vei_120a" href="स्तूप">stūpa</a> of Bharhut,</i> Plate xxviii.; Caland and Henry, <i>L'Agniṣṭoma,</i> 49.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203174"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203174">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 6, 2. According to the commentary on Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, v. 3, 20, the length was 32 Aṅgulas, or fingerbreadths This would be equivalent to 2 feet: <i>cf.</i> Fleet, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1912, 232.</li></ol></div>

शयण्डक|శయణ్డక|ಶಯಣ್ಡಕ|śayaṇḍaka|shayaNDaka|_vei_120@3175|_vei2_pn356
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3174">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3176">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn355">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn356">2:356</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn357">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śayaṇḍaka.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शयाण्डक">śayāṇḍaka</a>.</b></p></div>

शयन|శయన|ಶಯನ|śayana|shayana|_vei_120@3176|_vei2_pn356
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3175">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3177">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn355">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn356">2:356</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn357">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śayana</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203176"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203176">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203176"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203176">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘couch.’ <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तल्प">talpa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वह्य">vahya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203176"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203176">↑</a> iii. 25. 1; v. 29. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203176"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203176">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 1, 2; 7, 4.</li></ol></div>

शयाण्डक|శయాణ్డక|ಶಯಾಣ್ಡಕ|śayāṇḍaka|shayaaNDaka|_vei_120@3177|_vei2_pn356
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3176">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3178">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn355">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn356">2:356</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn357">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śayāṇḍaka</b> is the form in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203177"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203177">1</a></sup> of the name of an animal which in the Maitrāyaṇī<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203177"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203177">2</a></sup> and Vājasaneyi Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203177"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203177">3</a></sup> is written as <a class="vei_120a" href="शयण्डक">śayaṇḍaka</a>. Some sort of bird is meant according to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203177"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203177">4</a></sup> but the commentator on the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā equates the word with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृकलास">kṛkalāsa</a>,</b> ‘chameleon.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203177"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203177">↑</a> v. 5, 14, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203177"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203177">↑</a> iii. 14. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203177"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203177">↑</a> xxiv. 33.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203177"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203177">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 95.</li></ol></div>

शयु|శయు|ಶಯು|śayu|shayu|_vei_120@3178|_vei2_pn357
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3177">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3179">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn356">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn357">2:357</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn358">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śayu</b> is the name in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203178"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203178">1</a></sup> of a protégé of the Aśvins, who made his cow to give milk.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203178"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203178">↑</a> i. 112, 16; 116, 22; 117, 20; 118, 8; 119, 6; vi. 62, 7; vii. 68, 8; x. 39, 13; 40, 8.</li></ol></div>

शर|शर|శర_౧|శర|ಶರ_೧|ಶರ|śara_1|śara|shara_1|shara|_vei_120@3179|_vei2_pn357
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3178">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3180">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn356">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn357">2:357</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn358">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Śara</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203179"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203179">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203179"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203179">2</a></sup> denotes a kind of ‘reed’ (<i>Saccharum <a class="vei_120a" href="सर">sara</a></i>). Its use for arrow shafts,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203179"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203179">3</a></sup> and its brittleness,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203179"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203179">4</a></sup> are expressly referred to in the Atharvaveda. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शर्य">śarya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203179"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203179">↑</a> i. 191, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203179"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203179">↑</a> Av. iv. 7, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 2, 6, 2; vi. 1, 3, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 5; xxiii. 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 4, 1; iii. 1, 3, 13; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 11, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203179"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203179">↑</a> Av. i. 2, 1; 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203179"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203179">↑</a> Av. viii. 8, 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 71.</li></ol></div>

शर अर्चत्क|शर अर्चत्क|శర అర్చత్క_౨|శర అర్చత్క|ಶರ ಅರ್ಚತ್ಕ_೨|ಶರ ಅರ್ಚತ್ಕ|śara arcatka_2|śara arcatka|shara archatka_2|shara archatka|_vei_120@3180|_vei2_pn357
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3179">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3181">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn356">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn357">2:357</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn358">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Śara Arcatka</b> (‘descendant of Ṛcatka’) is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203180">1</a></sup> It is very doubtful, however, whether Ārcatka is really a patronymic.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203180"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203180">↑</a> i. 116, 22; <i>cf.</i> perhaps. i. 112, 16; Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 103, <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 150.</li></ol></div>

शर शौर-देव्य|शर शौर-देव्य|शर शौरदेव्य|शर शौर|देव्य|శర శౌర-దేవ్య_౩|శర శౌర-దేవ్య|శర శౌరదేవ్య|శర శౌర|దేవ్య|ಶರ ಶೌರ-ದೇವ್ಯ_೩|ಶರ ಶೌರ-ದೇವ್ಯ|ಶರ ಶೌರದೇವ್ಯ|ಶರ ಶೌರ|ದೇವ್ಯ|śara śaura-devya_3|śara śaura-devya|śara śauradevya|śara śaura|devya|shara shaura-devya_3|shara shaura-devya|shara shauradevya|shara shaura|_vei_120@3181|_vei2_pn357
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3180">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3182">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn356">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn357">2:357</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn358">↷</a><hr><p>3. <b>Śara Śaura-devya</b> (‘descendant of Śūradeva’) is the name of a generous prince in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203181"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203181">1</a></sup> who gave one calf to three singers. That this Dānastuti (‘praise of gifts’) is ironical seems certain.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203181"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203181">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203181"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203181">↑</a> viii. 70, 13-15.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203181"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203181">↑</a> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 5-7; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 163; 5. 175.</li></ol></div>

शरद्|శరద్|ಶರದ್|śarad|sharad|_vei_120@3182|_vei2_pn357
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3181">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3183">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn356">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn357">2:357</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn358">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śarad.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋतु">ṛtu</a>.</b></p></div>

शरभ|शरभ|శరభ_౧|శరభ|ಶರಭ_೧|ಶರಭ|śarabha_1|śarabha|sharabha_1|sharabha|_vei_120@3183|_vei2_pn357
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3182">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3184">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn356">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn357">2:357</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn358">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Śarabha</b> is the name of some wild animal in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203183">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203183">2</a></sup> In the <b>classical</b> literature it is a fabulous, eight-legged beast, dwelling in the snowy mountains, a foe of lions and elephants: the commentator Mahīdhara sees this sense, but without reason, in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā. The  animal is spoken of as akin to the goat;<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203183">3</a></sup> it was probably a kind of deer.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203183">↑</a> ix. 5, 9. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शलभ">śalabha</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203183">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 10, 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiii. 51; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 8, 5; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 3, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203183"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203183">↑</a> Av., <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 52, n. 1, accepts the traditional rendering.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 89.</li></ol></div>

शरभ|शरभ|శరభ_౨|శరభ|ಶರಭ_೨|ಶರಭ|śarabha_2|śarabha|sharabha_2|sharabha|_vei_120@3184|_vei2_pn358
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3183">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3185">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn357">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn358">2:358</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn363">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Śarabha</b> is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203184"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203184">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203184"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203184">↑</a> viii. 100, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 163.</li></ol></div>

शरव्या|శరవ్యా|ಶರವ್ಯಾ|śaravyā|sharavyaa|_vei_120@3185|_vei2_pn358
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3184">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3186">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn357">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn358">2:358</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn363">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaravyā,</b> ‘arrow-shot,’ is an expression found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203185"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203185">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203185"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203185">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203185"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203185">↑</a> vi. 75, 16; x. 87, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203185"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203185">↑</a> Av. i. 19, 1. 3; v. 18, 9; xi. 10, 6; xii. 5, 25, 29; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 5. 1, 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

शराव|శరావ|ಶರಾವ|śarāva|sharaava|_vei_120@3186|_vei2_pn358
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3185">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3187">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn357">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn358">2:358</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn363">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śarāva</b> is a measure of corn in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203186"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203186">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203186"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203186">↑</a> <i>Saptadaśa-<a class="vei_120a" href="शराव">śarāva</a>,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 4, 5; 6, 8; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1, 4, 12.</li></ol></div>

शरीर|శరీర|ಶರೀರ|śarīra|shariira|shareera|_vei_120@3187|_vei2_pn358
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3186">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3188">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn357">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn358">2:358</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn363">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śarīra,</b> ‘body,’ is a word of frequent occurrence in Vedic literature.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203187">1</a></sup> The interest of the Vedic Indians seems early to have been attracted to the consideration of questions connected with the anatomy of the body. Thus a hymn of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203187">2</a></sup> enumerates many parts of the body with some approach to accuracy and orderly arrangement.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203187">3</a></sup> It mentions the heels (<i>pārṣṇī</i>), the flesh (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="मांस">māṃsa</a></i>), the ankle-bones (<i>gulphau</i>), the fingers (<i>aṅgulīḥ</i>), the apertures (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="ख">kha</a></i>), the two metatarsi (<i>uchlakau</i>), the tarsus (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतिष्ठा">pratiṣṭhā</a></i>), the two knee-caps (<i>aṣṭhīvantau</i>), the two legs (<i>jaṅghe</i>), the two knee-joints (<i>jānunoḥ sandhī</i>). Then comes above the two kness (<i>jānū</i>) the foursided (<i>catuṣṭaya</i>), pliant (<i>śithira</i>) trunk (<i>kabandha</i>). The two hips (<i>śroṇī</i>) and the two thighs (<i>ārū</i>) are the props of the frame  (<i>kusindha</i>). Next come the breast-bone (<i>uras</i>), the cervical cartilages (<i>grīvāḥ</i>), the two breast pieces (<i>stanau</i>), the two shoulder-blades (<i>kaphoḍau</i>), the neck-bones (<i>skandhau</i>), and the backbones (<i>pṛṣṭīḥ</i>), the collar-bones (<i>aṃsau</i>), the arms (<i>bāhū</i>), the seven apertures in the head (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सप्त">sapta</a> khāni śīrṣaṇi</i>), the ears (<i>karṇau</i>), the nostrils (<i>nāsike</i>), the eyes (<i>cakṣaṇī</i>), the mouth (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="मुख">mukha</a></i>), the jaws (<i>hanū</i>), the tongue (<i>jihvā</i>), the brain (<i>mastiṣka</i>), the forehead (<i>lalāṭa</i>), the facial bone (<i>kakāṭikā</i>), the cranium (<i>kapāla</i>), and the structure of the jaws (<i>cityā hanvoḥ</i>).</p><p>This system presents marked similarities with the later system of <a class="vei_120a" href="चरक">caraka</a> and Suśruta,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203187">4</a></sup> which render certain the names ascribed to the several terms by Hoernle. <i>Kaphoḍau,</i> which is variously read in the manuscripts,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203187">5</a></sup> is rendered ‘collar-bone’ by Whitney, but ‘elbow’ in the St. Petersburg Dictionary. <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्कन्ध">skandha</a></i> in the plural regularly<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203187">6</a></sup> denotes ‘neck-bones,’ or, more precisely, ‘cervical vertebræ,’ a part denoted also by <i>uṣṇihā</i> in the plural.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203187">7</a></sup> <i>Pṛṣṭī</i><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203187">8</a></sup> denotes not ‘rib,’ which is <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्शु">parśu</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203187">9</a></sup> but a transverse process of a vertebra, and so the vertebra itself, there being in the truncal portion of the spinal column seventeen vertebræ and thirty-four transverse processes. The vertebræ are also denoted by <i>kīkasā</i> in the plural,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203187">10</a></sup> which sometimes<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203187">11</a></sup> is limited to the upper portion of the vertebral column, sometimes<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1203187">12</a></sup> to the thoracic portion of the spine. <i>Anūka</i> also denotes the vertebral column,<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1203187">13</a></sup> or more specially the lumbar<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1203187">14</a></sup> or thoracic<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1203187">15</a></sup> portion of the spine; it is said in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1203187">15</a></sup>  that there are twenty transverse processes in the lumbar spine (<i>udara</i>) and thirty-two in the thoracic, which gives twenty-six vertebræ, the true number (but the modern division is seven cervical, twelve thoracic, five lumbar, and two false--the sacrum and the coccyx). The vertebral column is also denoted by <i>karūkara,</i><sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1203187">16</a></sup> which, however, is usually found in the plural<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1203187">17</a></sup> denoting the transverse processes of the vertebræ, a sense expressed also by <i>kuntāpa.</i><sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1203187">18</a></sup></p><p><i>Grīvā,</i> in the plural, denotes cervical vertebræ, the number seven being given by the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1203187">19</a></sup> but usually<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1203187">20</a></sup> the word simply means windpipe, or, more accurately, the cartilaginous rings under the skin. <b>Jatru,</b> also in the plural, denotes the cervical cartilages,<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1203187">21</a></sup> or possibly the costal cartilages, which are certainly so called in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1203187">22</a></sup> where their number is given as eight.</p><p><i>Bhaṃsas,</i> which occurs thrice in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1203187">23</a></sup> seems to denote the pubic bone or arch rather than the ‘buttocks’ or ‘fundament,’ as Whitney<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1203187">24</a></sup> takes it.</p><p>In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1203187">25</a></sup> the number of bones in the the human body is given as 360. The number of the bones of the head and trunk are given in another passage<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_1203187">26</a></sup> as follows: The head is threefold, consisting of skin (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="त्वच्">tvac</a></i>), bone (<i>asthi</i>), brain (<i>matiṣka</i>); the neck has 15 bones: 14 transverse processes (<i>karūkara</i>) and the strength (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वीर्य">vīrya</a></i>)--<i>i.e.,</i> the bone of the centre regarded as one--as the 15th; the breast has 17: 16 cervical cartilages (<i>jatru</i>), and the sternum (<i>uras</i>) as the 17th; the abdominal portion of the spine has 21: 20 trans verse processes (<i>kuntāpa</i>), and the abdominal portion (<i>udara</i>) as the 21st; the two sides have 27: 26 ribs (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्शु">parśu</a></i>), and the two sides as the 27th; the thoracic portion of the spine (<i>anūka</i>) has 33: 32 transverse processes, and the thoracic portion as 33rd.</p><p>There are several enumerations of the parts of the body, not merely of the skeleton, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_1203187">27</a></sup> They include the hair (<i>lomāni</i>), skin (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="त्वच्">tvac</a></i>), flesh (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="मांस">māṃsa</a></i>), bone (<i>asthi</i>), marrow (<i>majjan</i>), liver (<i>yakṛt</i>), lungs (<i>kloman</i>), kidneys (<i>matasne</i>), gall (<i>pitta</i>), entrails (<i>āntrāṇi</i>), bowels (<i>gudāḥ</i>), spleen (<i>plīhan</i>), navel (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="नाभि">nābhi</a></i>), belly (<i>udara</i>), rectum (<i>vaniṣṭhu</i>), womb (<i>yoni</i>), penis (<i>plāśi</i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शेप">śepa</a></i>), face (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="मुख">mukha</a></i>), head (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शिरस्">śiras</a></i>), tongue (<i>jihvā</i>), mouth (<i>āsan</i>), rump (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पायु">pāyu</a></i>), leech (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वाल">vāla</a></i>), eye (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="चक्षुस्">cakṣus</a></i>), eyelashes (<i>pakṣmāṇi</i>), eyebrows (<i>utāni</i>), nose (<i>nas</i>), breath (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="व्यान">vyāna</a></i>), nose-hairs (<i>nasyāni</i>), ears (<i>karṇau</i>), brows (<i>bhrū</i>), body or trunk (<i>ātman</i>), waist (<i>upastha</i>), hair on the face (<i>śmaśrūṇi</i>), and on the head (<i>keśāḥ</i>). Another enumeration<sup id="ref_120_28_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_1203187">28</a></sup> gives <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शिरस्">śiras</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मुख">mukha</a>, keśāḥ, śmaśrūṇi, <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a></i> (breath), <i><a class="vei_120a" href="चक्षुस्">cakṣus</a>, śrotra</i> (ear), <i>jihvā, <a class="vei_120a" href="वाच्">vāc</a></i> (speech), <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मनस्">manas</a></i> (mind), <i>aṅgulīḥ, aṅgāni</i> (limbs), <i>bāhū, hastau</i> (hands), <i>karṇau, ātmā, uras</i> (sternum), <i>pṛṣṭīḥ</i> (vertebræ), <i>udara, aṃsau, grīvāḥ, śroṇī, ūrū, aratnī</i> (elbows), <i>jānūni, <a class="vei_120a" href="नाभि">nābhi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पायु">pāyu</a>, bhasat</i> (fundament), <i>āṇḍau</i> (testicles), <i>pasas</i> (membrum virile), <i>jaṅghā, <a class="vei_120a" href="पद्">pad</a></i> (foot), <i>lomāni, <a class="vei_120a" href="त्वच्">tvac</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मांस">māṃsa</a>, asthi, majjan.</i> Another set of names<sup id="ref_120_29_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29_vei_1203187">29</a></sup> includes <i>vaniṣṭhu, purītat</i> (pericardium), <i>lomāni, <a class="vei_120a" href="त्वच्">tvac</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="लोहित">lohita</a></i> (blood), <i>medas</i> (fat), <i>māṃsāni, snāvāni</i> (sinews), <i>asthīni, majjānaḥ, <a class="vei_120a" href="रेतस्">retas</a></i> (semen), <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पायु">pāyu</a>, kośya</i> (flesh near the heart), <i>pārśvya</i> (intercostal flesh), etc.</p><p>The bones of the skeleton of the horse are enumerated in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_30_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_30_vei_1203187">30</a></sup></p><p>In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_31_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_31_vei_1203187">31</a></sup> the human body is regarded as made up of one hundred and one items; there are four parts,  each of twenty-five members, with the trunk as one hundred and first. In the two upper parts there are five four-jointed<sup id="ref_120_32_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_32_vei_1203187">32</a></sup> fingers, two <i>kakṣasī</i> (of uncertain meaning),<sup id="ref_120_33_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_33_vei_1203187">33</a></sup> the arm (<i>dos</i>), the collar-bone (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a></i>), and the shoulder-blade (<i>aṃsa-<a class="vei_120a" href="फलक">phalaka</a></i>). In the two lower portions there are five four-jointed toes, the thigh, the leg, and three articulations, according to Sāyaṇa's commentary.</p><p>The <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_34_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_34_vei_1203187">34</a></sup> enumerates three bones in the head,<sup id="ref_120_35_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_35_vei_1203187">35</a></sup> three joints (<i>parvāṇi</i>) in the neck,<sup id="ref_120_36_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_36_vei_1203187">36</a></sup> the collar-bone (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_37_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_37_vei_1203187">37</a></sup> three joints in the fingers,<sup id="ref_120_38_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_38_vei_1203187">38</a></sup> and twenty-one transverse processes in the spine (<i>anūka</i>).<sup id="ref_120_39_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_39_vei_1203187">39</a></sup> The Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_40_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_40_vei_1203187">40</a></sup> enumerates four constituents in the head (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="चक्षुस्">cakṣus</a>, śrotra, <a class="vei_120a" href="वाच्">vāc</a></i>), but there are many variations, the number going up to twelve on one calculation.<sup id="ref_120_41_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_41_vei_1203187">41</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_42_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_42_vei_1203187">42</a></sup> an enumeration is given consisting of <i>carma</i> (skin), <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मांस">māṃsa</a>, snāvan, asthi,</i> and <i>majjan;</i> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_43_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_43_vei_1203187">43</a></sup> has <i>lomāni, <a class="vei_120a" href="मांस">māṃsa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="त्वच्">tvac</a>, asthi, majjan,</i> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_44_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_44_vei_1203187">44</a></sup> couples <i>majjānaḥ, snāvāni,</i> and <i>asthīni.</i> Other terms relating to the body are <i>kaṅkūṣa,</i><sup id="ref_120_45_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_45_vei_1203187">45</a></sup> perhaps a part of the ear,<sup id="ref_120_46_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_46_vei_1203187">46</a></sup> <i>yoni</i> (female organ), <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कक्ष">kakṣa</a></i><sup id="ref_120_47_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_47_vei_1203187">47</a></sup> (armpit), <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दन्त">danta</a></b> (tooth), <i><a class="vei_120a" href="नख">nakha</a></i> (nail), <i>prapada</i><sup id="ref_120_48_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_48_vei_1203187">48</a></sup> (forepart of the foot), <i>halīkṣṇa</i><sup id="ref_120_49_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_49_vei_1203187">49</a></sup> (gall). </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203187">↑</a> Rv. i. 32, 10; x. 16, 1, etc.; Av. v. 9, 7; xviii. 3, 9, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxiv. 55; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 7, 2, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 13; 14, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 1, 4, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 1, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203187">↑</a> x. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203187">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Hoernle, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1907, 10-12; <i>Osteology,</i> 109-111, 242.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203187">↑</a> <i>Osteology,</i> 112.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203187">↑</a> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 568.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203187">↑</a> Av. x. 7. 3 (where the Skandhas are compared with the <a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्तिकास्">kṛttikās</a>, probably because both were seven in number, but this is not certain); 9, 20; vi. 135, 1; xii. 5, 67; Hoernle, <i>Journal,</i> 1906, 918; 1907, 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203187">↑</a> Av. vi. 134, 1; Rv. vi. 163, 2= Av. ii. 33, 2; Av. ix. 8, 21; x. 10, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203187">↑</a> Rv. x. 87. 10 = Av. viii. 3, 10; Av. ix. 7, 5, 6; x. 9, 20; xii. 1, 34; xviii. 4, 10; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 6, 2, 7. See Hoernle, <i>Journal,</i> 1907. 2 <i>et seq.;</i> Whitney, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 548; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44. 164. n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203187">↑</a> Av. ix. 7, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203187">↑</a> Av. ix. 7, 5; 8, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203187">↑</a> Av. xi. 8, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1203187">↑</a> Av. ii. 33, 2; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 6, 2, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1203187">↑</a> Av. iv. 14, 8. <i>Cf.</i> ix. 8, 21 (the spine of the trunk).</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1203187">↑</a> Av. ii. 33, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1203187">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 2, 4. 12. 14. <i>Cf.</i> the phrase <i>īṣc anūkye,</i> Av. xi. 3, 9, where the two shafts of a cart are compared with the transverse processes of a vertebra.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1203187">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 2, 4, 12. 14. <i>Cf.</i> the phrase <i>īṣc anūkye,</i> Av. xi. 3, 9, where the two shafts of a cart are compared with the transverse processes of a vertebra.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1203187">↑</a> Av. xi. 9, 8; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 124.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1203187">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 2, 4, 10, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1203187">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> xii. 2, 4, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1203187">↑</a> <i>Ibid.</i> xii. 2, 4, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1203187">↑</a> Rv. vi. 163, 2 = Av. ii. 33, 2; Av. vi. 134, 1; ix. 7, 3; x. 9, 20; xi. 8, 15; Hoernle, <i>Journal,</i> 1906, 916 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1203187">↑</a> Rv. vii. 1, 12 = Av. xiv. 2, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1203187">↑</a> xii. 2, 4, 11. <i>Cf.</i> vii. 6, 2, 10; Hoernle, <i>Journal,</i> 1906, 922 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1203187">↑</a> Av. ii. 33, 5; ix. 8, 21, with a fuller version in the Paippalāda recension (Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 77, 551). In viii. 6, 5, it denotes vulva: Hoernle, 16-18.</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1203187">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1203187">↑</a> x. 5, 4, 12; xii. 3, 2, 3, 4; <b>Hoernle,</b> <i>Osteology,</i> 238, 239, and the criticism in 106-109, which shows how far removed the Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> is from a scientific system. <i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 62, 135 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_1203187">↑</a> xii. 2, 4, 9-14; Hoernle, <i>Osteology,</i> 240.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_1203187">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 81-93; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 11, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxviii. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_1203187">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xx. 5-13; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 11, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxviii. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_29_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29_vei_1203187">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxix. 8. 9. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_30_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_30_vei_1203187">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxv. 1-9; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 15. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_31_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_31_vei_1203187">↑</a> i. 2, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_32_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_32_vei_1203187">↑</a> This is contrary to fact: Hoernle, <i>Osteology,</i> 122, 123.</li><li id="fx_120_33_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_33_vei_1203187">↑</a> Perhaps the armpit regarded as in some way double; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 175.</li><li id="fx_120_34_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_34_vei_1203187">↑</a> ii. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_35_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_35_vei_1203187">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Hoernle, <i>Osteology,</i> 172 <i>et seq.;</i> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 2, 4, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_36_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_36_vei_1203187">↑</a> ii. 3, See Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 9, n. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_37_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_37_vei_1203187">↑</a> ii. 4; Hoernle, <i>Osteology,</i> 202 <i>et seq.;</i> Keith, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 9, n. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_38_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_38_vei_1203187">↑</a> ii. 5. <i>Cf.</i> n. 32. The later <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> here improves on the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> osteology.</li><li id="fx_120_39_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_39_vei_1203187">↑</a> ii. 6. See Keith, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 10, n. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_40_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_40_vei_1203187">↑</a> iii. 2, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_41_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_41_vei_1203187">↑</a> See references in Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 185, 192, 195. The numbers vary and are fanciful, being of no scientific importance.</li><li id="fx_120_42_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_42_vei_1203187">↑</a> i. 7, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_43_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_43_vei_1203187">↑</a> vi. 29, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_44_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_44_vei_1203187">↑</a> iii. 2, 1. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, viii. 1. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_45_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_45_vei_1203187">↑</a> Av. ix. 8, 2, where the Paippalāda recension has <i>kaṅkukha.</i></li><li id="fx_120_46_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_46_vei_1203187">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 378.</li><li id="fx_120_47_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_47_vei_1203187">↑</a> Av. vi. 127, 2. <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षी">kakṣī</a>,</i> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_48_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_48_vei_1203187">↑</a> Av. ii. 33, 5, with Lanman's note in Whitney's Translation, p. 77; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 204. In that Āraṇyaka the passage ii. 1, 4 makes the sense ‘toe’ improbable.</li><li id="fx_120_49_vei_1203187"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_49_vei_1203187">↑</a> Av. ii. 33, 3; Whitney, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 76.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hoernle, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1906, 916 <i>et seq.;</i> 1907, 1, <i>et seq.; Osteology, passim.</i></li></ol></div>

शरु|శరు|ಶರು|śaru|sharu|_vei_120@3188|_vei2_pn363
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3187">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3189">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn358">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn363">2:363</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn364">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaru</b> denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203188">1</a></sup> and Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203188">2</a></sup> a missile weapon, often certainly an ‘arrow,’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203188">3</a></sup> but perhaps sometimes a ‘dart’ or ‘spear.’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203188">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203188">↑</a> i. 100, 18; 172, 2; 186, 9; ii. 12, 10; iv. 3, 7; 28, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203188">↑</a> i. 2, 3; 19, 2; vi. 65, 2; xii. 2, 47.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203188">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> Rv. x. 125, 6; and x. 87, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203188"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203188">↑</a> Perhaps Rv. iv. 3, 7, where <i>bṛhatī</i> is applied to it, and where ‘lance’ seems the best sense, the use being metaphorical.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 223; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 301.</li></ol></div>

शर्करा|శర్కరా|ಶರ್ಕರಾ|śarkarā|sharkaraa|_vei_120@3189|_vei2_pn363
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3188">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3190">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn358">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn363">2:363</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn364">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śarkarā,</b> fem. plur., denotes in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203189">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203189">2</a></sup> ‘grit’ or ‘gravel.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203189">↑</a> Av. xi. 7, 21; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 1, 6, 2; 2, 6, 2; 6, 4, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203189"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203189">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 3, 7; 2, 1, 4; iii. 12, 6, atapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 1, 8, etc.</li></ol></div>

शर्कराख्य|శర్కరాఖ్య|ಶರ್ಕರಾಖ್ಯ|śarkarākhya|sharkaraakhya|_vei_120@3190|_vei2_pn363
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3189">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3191">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn358">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn363">2:363</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn364">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śarkarākhya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शार्कराक्ष">śārkarākṣa</a>.</b></p></div>

शर्कोट|శర్కోట|ಶರ್ಕೋಟ|śarkoṭa|sharkoTa|_vei_120@3191|_vei2_pn363
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3190">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3192">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn358">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn363">2:363</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn364">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śarkoṭa</b> is the name of an animal in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203191"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203191">1</a></sup> either a ‘serpent,’ as Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203191"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203191">2</a></sup> and Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203191"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203191">3</a></sup> held, or a ‘scorpion,’ as Grill,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203191"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203191">4</a></sup> Henry,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203191"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203191">5</a></sup> and Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203191"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203191">6</a></sup> think.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203191"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203191">↑</a> vii. 56, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203191"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203191">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> comparing the later Karkoṭaka.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203191"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203191">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 95.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203191"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203191">↑</a> <i>Hundert Lieder,^2</i> 183.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203191"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203191">↑</a> <i>Le livre vii de l' Atharvavéda,</i> 82.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203191"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203191">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 554, 555.</li></ol></div>

शर्ध|శర్ధ|ಶರ್ಧ|śardha|shardha|_vei_120@3192|_vei2_pn363
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3191">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3193">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn358">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn363">2:363</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn364">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śardha.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात">vrāta</a>.</b></p></div>

शर्ध्य|శర్ధ్య|ಶರ್ಧ್ಯ|śardhya|shardhya|_vei_120@3193|_vei2_pn363
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3192">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3194">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn358">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn363">2:363</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn364">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śardhya</b> in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203193">1</a></sup> is taken by Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203193">2</a></sup> as perhaps denoting a part of the chariot. The sense is, however, quite uncertain.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203193">↑</a> i. 119, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203193"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203193">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

शर्य|శర్య|ಶರ್ಯ|śarya|sharya|_vei_120@3194|_vei2_pn363
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3193">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3195">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn358">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn363">2:363</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn364">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śarya,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203194"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203194">1</a></sup> <b>Śaryā,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203194"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203194">2</a></sup> seem in the Rigveda to denote an ‘arrow.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203194"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203194">3</a></sup> Perhaps, also, Śaryā<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203194"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203194">4</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="शर्य">śarya</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203194"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203194">5</a></sup> (neut.) mean the ‘wicker work’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> sieve, but the exact sense of the passages is doubtful.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203194"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203194">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203194"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203194">↑</a> i. 119, 10, where the sense is not certain.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203194"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203194">↑</a> i. 148, 4; x. 178, 3. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> v. 4; x. 29.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203194"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203194">↑</a> As derived from 1. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शर">śara</a>,</b> and meaning literally ‘made from a reed.’</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203194"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203194">↑</a> Rv. ix. 110, 5; x. 61, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203194"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203194">↑</a> Rv. ix. 14, 4; 68, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203194"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203194">↑</a> Hillebrandt; <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 52, takes <i>śaryāṇi</i> in Rv. ix. 68, 2, as denoting the outer husk of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> plant. See also Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 255, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

शर्यणावन्त्|శర్యణావన్త్|ಶರ್ಯಣಾವನ್ತ್|śaryaṇāvant|sharyaNaavant|_vei_120@3195|_vei2_pn364
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3194">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3196">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn363">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn364">2:364</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn365">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaryaṇāvant</b> occurs in several passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203195">1</a></sup> in all of which Sāyaṇa sees a local name. According to his account, Śaryaṇāḥ (masc. plur.) is a district in <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>,</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="शर्यणावन्त्">śaryaṇāvant</a> being a lake not far from it in the back part (<i>jaghanārdhe</i>) of <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>. The unusual consistency of his statements on this point is in favour of the word being a place name; it is also to be noted that <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a> contained the lake <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अन्यतःप्लक्षा">anyataḥplakṣā</a>.</b> Roth,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203195">2</a></sup> however, thought that in two passages<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203195">3</a></sup> the word denoted merely a ‘lake,’ literally ‘(water) covered with a thicket of reeds’ (<i>śaryaṇa</i>), and in the others a <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> vessel. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203195">4</a></sup> inclines to this rendering. On the other hand, Pischel<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203195">5</a></sup> accepts Sāyaṇa's view. Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203195">6</a></sup> also sees in the word a place name, but he is inclined to locate it among the ‘five tribes,’<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203195">7</a></sup> which is not quite inconsistent with its being in <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>, for the connexion of the <b>Pūrus</b> with the later <b>Kurus</b> is known;<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203195">8</a></sup> or perhaps, he suggests, <a class="vei_120a" href="शर्यणावन्त्">śaryaṇāvant</a> is an old name for the Wular sea of Kaśmīr, which was only a reminiscence in Vedic times. This is not probable; still less so is Ludwig's hypothesis<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203195">9</a></sup> that the <a class="vei_120a" href="शर्यणावन्त्">śaryaṇāvant</a> is the later eastern <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>.</b> Bergaigne<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203195">10</a></sup> regards the name as that of a celestial preparer of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203195">↑</a> i. 84, 14; viii. 6, 39; 7, 29; 64, 11; ix. 65, 22; 113, 1; x. 35, 2. See Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 64 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 17); Śāṭyāyanaka in Sāyaṇa on Rv. i. 84, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203195">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203195">↑</a> i. 84, 14; x. 35, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203195">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 19, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203195">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 217. So Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 398, 399.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203195">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 126, <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203195">↑</a> This is deduced, not with any certainty, from Rv. ix. 65, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203195">↑</a> Hillebrandt, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 142, n. 4, Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 205.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203195">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 3, 201.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203195"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203195">↑</a> <i>Religion Védique,</i> 1, 206.</li></ol></div>

शर्यात|శర్యాత|ಶರ್ಯಾತ|śaryāta|sharyaata|_vei_120@3196|_vei2_pn364
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3195">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3197">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn363">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn364">2:364</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn365">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaryāta</b> is mentioned once in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203196">1</a></sup> as a protégé of the Aśvins. Of him in the Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa^2 and the  Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203196">3</a></sup> is told a story how <b><a class="vei_120a" href="च्यवन">cyavana</a></b> was annoyed by the <b>Śāryātas,</b> and appeased by the gift of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुकन्या">sukanyā</a>,</b> Śaryāta's daughter, as a wife, and how <a class="vei_120a" href="च्यवन">cyavana</a> was then restored to youth by the Aśvins. He is there called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a></b> (‘descendant of Manu’). He appears also as <a class="vei_120a" href="शर्यात">śaryāta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a>, a sacrificer, in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203196">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203196">↑</a> i. 112, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203196">↑</a> iii. 120-122 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 11, cxlv).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203196"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203196">↑</a> iv. 7, 1; 8, 3, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 250 <i>et seq.;</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, 272 <i>et seq.;</i> Oertel, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, 236, 237.</li></ol></div>

शर्व-दत्त|शर्वदत्त|शर्व|दत्त|శర్వ-దత్త|శర్వదత్త|శర్వ|దత్త|ಶರ್ವ-ದತ್ತ|ಶರ್ವದತ್ತ|ಶರ್ವ|ದತ್ತ|śarva-datta|śarvadatta|śarva|datta|sharva-datta|sharvadatta|sharva|_vei_120@3197|_vei2_pn365
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3196">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3198">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn364">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn365">2:365</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn366">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śarva-datta</b> (‘given by the god Śarva’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गार्ग्य">gārgya</a></b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गर्ग">garga</a></b>’) is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203197">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203197"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203197">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372.</li></ol></div>

शल|శల|ಶಲ|śala|shala|_vei_120@3198|_vei2_pn365
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3197">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3199">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn364">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn365">2:365</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn366">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śala</b> is explained by the St. Petersburg Dictionary as a measure of length in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203198"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203198">1</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203198"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203198">2</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203198"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203198">3</a></sup> Whitney<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203198"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203198">4</a></sup> objects that the sense in all<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203198"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203198">5</a></sup> these passages is not suited by this meaning.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203198"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203198">↑</a> viii. 7, 28.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203198"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203198">↑</a> xii. 10 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 464).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203198"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203198">↑</a> i. 5, 10, 1 (where this is the interpretation of the commentator).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203198"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203198">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 501.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203198"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203198">↑</a> He does not notice the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a>. Against his criticism it must be noted that in every one of the passages a numeral is compounded with <a class="vei_120a" href="शल">śala</a>, as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रि">tri</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="शल">śala</a>,</i> etc.</li></ol></div>

शलभ|శలభ|ಶಲಭ|śalabha|shalabha|_vei_120@3199|_vei2_pn365
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3198">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3200">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn364">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn365">2:365</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn366">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śalabha,</b> ‘locust,’ appears in the Paippalāda recension of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203199"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203199">1</a></sup> for <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शरभ">śarabha</a>,</b> the reading of the received text, and is regarded by Whitney<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203199"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203199">2</a></sup> as making better sense.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203199"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203199">↑</a> ix. 5, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203199"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203199">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 534. But the mention of the goat in the passage strongly supports Sarabha. See Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> i. 2, 3, 9.</li></ol></div>

शलली|शललि|శలలీ|శలలి|ಶಲಲೀ|ಶಲಲಿ|śalalī|śalali|shalalii|shalali|shalalee|_vei_120@3200|_vei2_pn365
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3199">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3201">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn364">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn365">2:365</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn366">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śalalī</b> denotes the ‘quill’ of the porcupine, used for parting the hair and anointing the eyes.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203200">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203200"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203200">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxiii. 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 6, 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 4, 5.</li></ol></div>

शलुन|శలున|ಶಲುನ|śaluna|shaluna|_vei_120@3201|_vei2_pn366
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3200">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3202">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn365">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn366">2:366</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn367">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaluna</b> is found in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203201"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203201">1</a></sup> denoting a ‘worm.’ The Paippalāda recension reads Śalūla, and Sāyaṇa Śalga.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203201"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203201">↑</a> ii. 31, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 73; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 315; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 98 (Śalunna).</li></ol></div>

शल्क|శల్క|ಶಲ್ಕ|śalka|shalka|_vei_120@3202|_vei2_pn366
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3201">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3203">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn365">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn366">2:366</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn367">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śalka</b> denotes in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203202">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203202">2</a></sup> chip' or ‘shaving’ used for kindling a fire, etc.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203202">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 2, 9, 3; 4, 2, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xx. 8; xxvii. 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203202"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203202">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 14, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 9, 9; 2, 1, 15.</li></ol></div>

शल्मल्ल्|శల్మల్ల్|ಶಲ್ಮಲ್ಲ್|śalmall|shalmall|_vei_120@3203|_vei2_pn366
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3202">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3204">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn365">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn366">2:366</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn367">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śalmall</b> is the name of the ‘silk cotton tree’ (<i>Salmalia Malabarica</i>). Its fruit is regarded as poisonous in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203203">1</a></sup> but the car of the bridal procession is made of its wood.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203203">2</a></sup> It is described as the tallest of trees.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203203">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203203">↑</a> vii. 50, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203203">↑</a> x. 85, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203203"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203203">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 4, 12, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 13; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 2, 7, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 4, 11, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 317, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

शल्य|శల్య|ಶಲ್ಯ|śalya|shalya|_vei_120@3204|_vei2_pn366
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3203">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3205">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn365">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn366">2:366</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn367">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śalya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इषु">iṣu</a>.</b></p></div>

शल्यक|శల్యక|ಶಲ್ಯಕ|śalyaka|shalyaka|_vei_120@3205|_vei2_pn366
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3204">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3206">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn365">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn366">2:366</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn367">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śalyaka</b> denotes in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203205">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203205">2</a></sup> the ‘porcupine.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203205">↑</a> xxiv. 35.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203205"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203205">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 26, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 82.</li></ol></div>

शवर्त|శవర్త|ಶವರ್ತ|śavarta|shavarta|_vei_120@3206|_vei2_pn366
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3205">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3207">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn365">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn366">2:366</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn367">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śavarta</b> is the name of a species of ‘worm’ in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203206">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203206">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203206">↑</a> ix. 4, 16, with the various reading Svavarta, Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 531.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203206"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203206">↑</a> v. 7, 23, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 98. Possibly Roth is right in holding that the word = <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शव">śava</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वर्त">varta</a>,</i> a worm ‘living on carrion.’</li></ol></div>

शवस्|शव|శవస్|శవ|ಶವಸ್|ಶವ|śavas|śava|shavas|shava|_vei_120@3207|_vei2_pn366
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3206">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3208">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn365">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn366">2:366</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn367">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śavas</b> is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अग्निभू काश्यप">agnibhū kāśyapa</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203207">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203207"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203207">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373.</li></ol></div>

शवस|శవస|ಶವಸ|śavasa|shavasa|_vei_120@3208|_vei2_pn367
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3207">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3209">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn366">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn367">2:367</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn368">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śavasa</b> occurs only in the false reading <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शवस">śavasa</a>-Uśinareṣu</i> in the Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i, 2, 9) for <i>sa-Vaśośīnareṣu.</i> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वश">vaśa</a>.</b></p></div>

शविष्ठ|శవిష్ఠ|ಶವಿಷ್ಠ|śaviṣṭha|shaviShTha|_vei_120@3209|_vei2_pn367
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3208">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3210">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn366">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn367">2:367</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn368">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaviṣṭha</b> is, according to Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203209">1</a></sup> the name of a generous patron in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203209">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203209">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 163.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203209"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203209">↑</a> viii. 74, 14, 15.</li></ol></div>

शश|శశ|ಶಶ|śaśa|shasha|_vei_120@3210|_vei2_pn367
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3209">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3211">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn366">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn367">2:367</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn368">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaśa,</b> ‘hare,’ is found once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203210"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203210">1</a></sup> where it is said to have swallowed a razor. The animal is occasionally mentioned later also.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203210"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203210">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203210"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203210">↑</a> x. 28, 2. Later, a goat supplants the hare in this curious story; see Bo7thling, Proceedings of the Saxon Academy, 1894, <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203210"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203210">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 56; xxiv. 38; Maitrāyaṇī Samhitā, iii. 14, 15; the hare in the moon, Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 1, 5, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 84.</li></ol></div>

शशयु|శశయు|ಶಶಯು|śaśayu|shashayu|_vei_120@3211|_vei2_pn367
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3210">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3212">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn366">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn367">2:367</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn368">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaśayu,</b> ‘pursuing the hare,’ is the epithet of some animal (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="मृग">mṛga</a></b>) in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203211"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203211">1</a></sup> Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203211"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203211">2</a></sup> thinks the tiger is meant, but this is not likely. Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203211"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203211">3</a></sup> considers that a bird of prey is intended, while Whitney,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203211"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203211">4</a></sup> following the commentator, renders the word by ‘lurking.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203211"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203211">↑</a> iv. 3, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203211"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203211">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 79, 84.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203211"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203211">↑</a> In Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 149.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203211"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203211">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 368.</li></ol></div>

शश्वती|शश्वति|శశ్వతీ|శశ్వతి|ಶಶ್ವತೀ|ಶಶ್ವತಿ|śaśvatī|śaśvati|shashvatii|shashvati|shashvatee|_vei_120@3212|_vei2_pn367
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3211">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3213">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn366">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn367">2:367</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn368">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaśvatī.</b> See <b>Āsaṅga.</b></p></div>

शष्प|శష్ప|ಶಷ್ಪ|śaṣpa|shaShpa|_vei_120@3213|_vei2_pn367
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3212">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3214">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn366">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn367">2:367</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn368">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaṣpa</b> in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203213"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203213">1</a></sup> denotes ‘young or sprouting grass.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203213"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203213">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 13, 81; xxi. 29; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 5, 3; 8, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 7, 2, 8; 9, 1, 2, etc.</li></ol></div>

शस्तृ|శస్తృ|ಶಸ್ತೃ|śastṛ|shastR|shastRi|shastRu|shastRRi|_vei_120@3214|_vei2_pn367
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3213">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3215">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn366">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn367">2:367</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn368">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śastṛ</b> in the Rigveda (i. 162, 5) and the Atharvaveda (ix. 3, 3) denotes the slaughterer of an animal. </p></div>

शस्त्र|శస్త్ర|ಶಸ್ತ್ರ|śastra|shastra|_vei_120@3215|_vei2_pn368
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3214">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3216">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn367">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn368">2:368</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn369">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śastra</b> is the technical term<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203215"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203215">1</a></sup> for the ‘recitation’ of the <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a> priest, as opposed to the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्तोत्र">stotra</a></b> of the Udgātṛ. The recitations at the morning offering of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> are called the <a class="vei_120a" href="आज्य">ājya</a> and Praüga; at the midday offering, the Marutvatīya and the Niṣkevalya; at the evening offering, the Vaiśvadeva and the Āgnimāruta.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203215"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203215">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 2, 7, 2, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxix. 2, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 25. 28, etc.; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 2, 4, 20, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 353, and Caland and Henry, <i>L'Agniṣṭoma, passim,</i> where the Śastras are set out at length.</li></ol></div>

शाकटायन|శాకటాయన|ಶಾಕಟಾಯನ|śākaṭāyana|shaakaTaayana|_vei_120@3216|_vei2_pn368
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3215">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3217">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn367">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn368">2:368</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn369">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śākaṭāyana,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="शकट">śakaṭa</a>,’ is the patronymic of a grammarian referred to by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a></b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203216"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203216">1</a></sup> and in the Prātiśākhyas,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203216"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203216">2</a></sup> as well as often later.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203216"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203216">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, i. 3, 12 <i>et. seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203216"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203216">↑</a> Rigveda Prātiśakhya, i. 3; xiii. 16; Vājasaneyi Prātiśakhya, iii. 8, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 143, 151, 152, 217.</li></ol></div>

शाक-दास भाडितायन|शाकदास भाडितायन|शाक|दास भाडितायन|శాక-దాస భాడితాయన|శాకదాస భాడితాయన|శాక|దాస భాడితాయన|ಶಾಕ-ದಾಸ ಭಾಡಿತಾಯನ|ಶಾಕದಾಸ ಭಾಡಿತಾಯನ|ಶಾಕ|ದಾಸ ಭಾಡಿತಾಯನ|śāka-dāsa bhāḍitāyana|śākadāsa bhāḍitāyana|śāka|dāsa bhāḍitāyana|shaaka-daasa bhaaDitaayana|shaakadaasa bhaaDitaayana|shaaka|daasa bhaaDitaayana|_vei_120@3217|_vei2_pn368
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3216">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3218">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn367">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn368">2:368</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn369">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śāka-<a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a> Bhāḍitāyana</b> (‘descendant of Bhaḍita’) is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203217"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203217">1</a></sup> as the pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विचक्षण ताण्ड्य">vicakṣaṇa tāṇḍya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203217"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203217">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373</li></ol></div>

शाक-पूणि|शाकपूणि|शाक|पूणि|శాక-పూణి|శాకపూణి|శాక|పూణి|ಶಾಕ-ಪೂಣಿ|ಶಾಕಪೂಣಿ|ಶಾಕ|ಪೂಣಿ|śāka-pūṇi|śākapūṇi|śāka|pūṇi|shaaka-puuNi|shaakapuuNi|shaaka|puuNi|shaaka-pooNi|shaakapooNi|pooNi|_vei_120@3218|_vei2_pn368
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3217">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3219">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn367">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn368">2:368</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn369">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śāka-pūṇi,</b> ‘descendant of Śakapūṇa,’ is the name of a grammarian often mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203218">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203218"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203218">↑</a> iii. 11; viii. 5, 6, 14; xii. 19; xiii. 10, 11. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 85.</li></ol></div>

शाकल|శాకల|ಶಾಕಲ|śākala|shaakala|_vei_120@3219|_vei2_pn368
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3218">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3220">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn367">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn368">2:368</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn369">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śākala</b> in the Aitaṛeya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203219"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203219">1</a></sup> denotes the ‘teaching of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाकल्य">śākalya</a></b>’ according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary. But Bo7htlingk<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203219"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203219">2</a></sup> seems right in taking it as a kind of snake in that passage.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203219"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203219">↑</a> iii. 43, 5 (Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 277). <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 33, n.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203219"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203219">↑</a> Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

शाकल्य|శాకల్య|ಶಾಕಲ್ಯ|śākalya|shaakalya|_vei_120@3220|_vei2_pn368
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3219">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3221">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn367">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn368">2:368</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn369">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śākalya,</b> ‘descendant of Śakala,’ is the patronymic of <b>Vidagdha</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203220">1</a></sup> and of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्थविर">sthavira</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203220">2</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyakas.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203220">3</a></sup> An undefined <a class="vei_120a" href="शाकल्य">śākalya</a>  is mentioned in the same Āraṇyakas,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203220">4</a></sup> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203220">5</a></sup> and often later, as a teacher dealing with the text of the Rigveda. Weber<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203220">6</a></sup> is inclined to identify Vidagdha with the <a class="vei_120a" href="शाकल्य">śākalya</a> who is known as the maker of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पद">pada</a> Pāṭha of the Rigveda, but Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203220">7</a></sup> thinks that the latter was later than the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> period. Geldner<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203220">8</a></sup> identifies the two; this view, however, is not very probable.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203220">9</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203220">↑</a> xi. 6, 3, 3; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 9, 1; iv. 1, 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203220">↑</a> iii. 2, 1. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203220">↑</a> vii. 16; viii. 1. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203220">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a>, iii. 1, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a>, vii. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203220">↑</a> vi. 28.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203220">↑</a> <i>Indian Literature,</i> 32, 33.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203220">↑</a> <i>Prolegomena,</i> 380, n.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203220">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 144-146.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203220"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203220">↑</a> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 239, 240.</li></ol></div>

शाकायनिन्|శాకాయనిన్|ಶಾಕಾಯನಿನ್|śākāyanin|shaakaayanin|_vei_120@3221|_vei2_pn369
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3220">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3222">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn368">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn369">2:369</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn370">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śākāyanin,</b> in the plural, denotes the followers of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाकायन्य">śākāyanya</a></b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (x. 4, 5, 1).</p></div>

शाकायन्य|శాకాయన్య|ಶಾಕಾಯನ್ಯ|śākāyanya|shaakaayanya|_vei_120@3222|_vei2_pn369
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3221">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3223">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn368">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn369">2:369</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn370">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śākāyanya,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="शाक">śāka</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जात">jāta</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203222"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203222">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203222"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203222">↑</a> xxii. 7 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 472). <i>Cf.</i> Maitrāyaṇī <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 2; vi. 29.</li></ol></div>

शाकिन्|శాకిన్|ಶಾಕಿನ್|śākin|shaakin|_vei_120@3223|_vei2_pn369
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3222">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3224">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn368">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn369">2:369</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn370">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śākin,</b> plur., is believed by Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203223">1</a></sup> to designate a group of generous donors in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203223">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203223">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 155; Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 1, 521, n.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203223"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203223">↑</a> v. 52, 17.</li></ol></div>

शाक्त्य|శాక్త్య|ಶಾಕ್ತ್ಯ|śāktya|shaaktya|_vei_120@3224|_vei2_pn369
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3223">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3225">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn368">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn369">2:369</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn370">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śāktya,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शक्ति">śakti</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b>Gaurivīti.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203224"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203224">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203224"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203224">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 19, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 8, 3, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 14; xii. 13, 10; xxv. 7, 2; Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxiii. 11, 14; xxiv. 10, 6. 8.</li></ol></div>

शाक्वर|శాక్వర|ಶಾಕ್ವರ|śākvara|shaakvara|_vei_120@3225|_vei2_pn369
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3224">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3226">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn368">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn369">2:369</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn370">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śākvara.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शक्वरी">śakvarī</a>.</b></p></div>

शाखा|శాఖా|ಶಾಖಾ|śākhā|shaakhaa|_vei_120@3226|_vei2_pn369
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3225">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3227">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn368">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn369">2:369</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn370">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śākhā</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203226"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203226">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203226"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203226">2</a></sup> denotes the ‘branch’ of a tree. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वया">vayā</a></b> is more often used in this sense in the Rigveda. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203226"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203226">↑</a> i. 8, 8; vii. 43, 1; x. 94, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203226"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203226">↑</a> Av. iii. 6, 8; x. 7, 21; xi. 2, 19, etc.</li></ol></div>

शांखायन|शाङ्खायन|శాంఖాయన|శాఙ్ఖాయన|ಶಾಂಖಾಯನ|ಶಾಙ್ಖಾಯನ|śāṅkhāyana|shaaMkhaayana|shaankhaayana|_vei_120@3227|_vei2_pn370
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3226">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3228">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn369">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn370">2:370</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn371">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śāṅkhāyana</b> as the name of a teacher is not mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, but it occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) at the end of the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203227">1</a></sup> where Guṇākhya is given as the authority for that work. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203227">2</a></sup> the name of <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> never occurs, but the <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203227">3</a></sup> seem to recognize as teacher <b>Suyajña <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a>.</b> In later times<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203227">4</a></sup> the school flourished in Northern Gujarat. <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> appears in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Prātiśākhya<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203227">5</a></sup> along with Kāṇḍamāyana.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203227">↑</a> xv. 1. Oldenberg's suggestion (<i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> xxix. 4, 5) that Guṇākhya is intended as the author of the Sūtras is quite unnecessary; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 328.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203227">↑</a> Hillebrandt, <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, 1, viii <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203227">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 10; vi. 10; Śāmbavya Gṛhyā <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> in <i>Indische Studien,</i> 15, 154; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 4, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Kārikā in Nārāyaṇa on <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 1, 10; Ānartīya on <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 2, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203227">↑</a> Bühler, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 2, xxxi.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203227"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203227">↑</a> xv. 7.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 32, 44, 50 <i>et seq.;</i> 80, 313, 314; Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 45, 191, 205, 245, 249.</li></ol></div>

शाट्यायन|శాట్యాయన|ಶಾಟ್ಯಾಯನ|śāṭyāyana|shaaTyaayana|_vei_120@3228|_vei2_pn370
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3227">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3229">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn369">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn370">2:370</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn371">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śāṭyāyana,</b> ‘descendant of Śāṭya,’ is the patronymic of a teacher mentioned twice in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203228">1</a></sup> and often in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203228">2</a></sup> In a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the latter work<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203228">3</a></sup> he is called a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ज्वालायन">jvālāyana</a>,</b> while in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> at the end of the Sāmavidhāna <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> he appears as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बादरायण">bādarāyaṇa</a>.</b> The Śāṭyāyanins, his followers, are frequently mentioned in the Sūtras,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203228">4</a></sup> the Śāṭyāyani <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203228">5</a></sup> and the Śāṭyāyanaka<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203228">6</a></sup> being also referred to in them. It has been shown by Oertel<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203228">7</a></sup> that this <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> bore a close resemblance to, and probably belonged to the same period as, the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203228">↑</a> viii. 1, 4, 9; x. 4, 5, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203228">↑</a> i. 6, 2; 30, 1; ii. 2, 8; 4, 3; 9, 10; iii. 13, 6; 28, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203228">↑</a> iv. 16, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203228">↑</a> Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 5, 18; Anupada <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 8; ii. 9; iii. 2, 11; iv. 8, etc.; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 44.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203228">↑</a> Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, v. 23, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203228">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> x. 12, 13. 14; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 2, 24; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 4, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203228"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203228">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, ccxli; 18, 20 <i>et seq.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 203; Aufrecht, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 151, 152.</li></ol></div>

शाण्ड|శాణ్డ|ಶಾಣ್ಡ|śāṇḍa|shaaNDa|_vei_120@3229|_vei2_pn371
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3228">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3230">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn370">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn371">2:371</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn372">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śāṇḍa,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शण्ड">śaṇḍa</a>,</b>’ is the name of a man in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203229"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203229">1</a></sup> who is praised for his generosity. It is not likely that he is identical with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुपन्था">purupanthā</a></b> mentioned in the next verse.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203229"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203229">↑</a> vi. 63, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 158.</li></ol></div>

शाण्डिल|శాణ్డిల|ಶಾಣ್ಡಿಲ|śāṇḍila|shaaNDila|_vei_120@3230|_vei2_pn371
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3229">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3231">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn370">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn371">2:371</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn372">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śāṇḍila,</b> masc. plur., is the term applied to the ‘descendants of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्डिल्य">śāṇḍilya</a></b>’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka (i. 22, 10).</p></div>

शाण्डिली-पुत्र|शाण्डिलीपुत्र|शाण्डिली|शाण्डिलि|पुत्र|శాణ్డిలీ-పుత్ర|శాణ్డిలీపుత్ర|శాణ్డిలీ|శాణ్డిలి|పుత్ర|ಶಾಣ್ಡಿಲೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಶಾಣ್ಡಿಲೀಪುತ್ರ|ಶಾಣ್ಡಿಲೀ|ಶಾಣ್ಡಿಲಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|śāṇḍilī-putra|śāṇḍilīputra|śāṇḍilī|śāṇḍili|putra|shaaNDilii-putra|shaaNDiliiputra|shaaNDilii|shaaNDili|shaaNDilee-putra|shaaNDileeputra|shaaNDilee|_vei_120@3231|_vei2_pn371
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3230">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3232">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn370">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn371">2:371</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn372">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śāṇḍilī-putra,</b> ‘pupil of a female descendant of Śaṇḍila,’ is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राथीतरीपुत्र">rāthītarīputra</a>,</b> in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203231"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203231">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203231"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203231">↑</a> vi. 4, 32 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 5, 2 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

शाण्डिल्य|శాణ్డిల్య|ಶಾಣ್ಡಿಲ್ಯ|śāṇḍilya|shaaNDilya|_vei_120@3232|_vei2_pn371
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3231">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3233">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn370">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn371">2:371</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn372">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śāṇḍilya,</b> ‘descendant of Śaṇḍila,’ is the patronymic of several teachers (see <b>Udara</b> and <b>Suyajña</b>). The most important <a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्डिल्य">śāṇḍilya</a> is the one cited several times as an authority in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203232">1</a></sup> where his <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>, or ‘sacrificial fire,’ is called <a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्डिल">śāṇḍila</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203232">2</a></sup> From this it appears clearly that he was one of the great teachers of the fire ritual which occupies the fifth and following books of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) at the end of the tenth book<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203232">3</a></sup> he is given as a pupil of <b>Kuśri</b> and a teacher of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वात्स्य">vātsya</a>;</b> another list at the end of the last book in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203232">4</a></sup> gives him as a pupil of <a class="vei_120a" href="वात्स्य">vātsya</a>, and the latter as a pupil of Kuśri. In the confused and worthless<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203232">5</a></sup> lists of teachers at the end of the second and fourth books of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> he is said to be the pupil of various persons--<b><a class="vei_120a" href="कैशोर्य">kaiśorya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काप्य">kāpya</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203232">6</a></sup> <b>Vaiṣṭapureya,</b><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203232">7</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203232">8</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203232">9</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बैजवाप">baijavāpa</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203232">10</a></sup> and <b>Āṇa bhimlāta.</b><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203232">11</a></sup> No doubt different Śāṇḍilyas may be meant, but the lists are too confused to claim serious consideration.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203232">↑</a> ix. 4, 4, 17; 5, 2, 15; x. 1, 4, 10; 4, 1, 11; 6, 3, 5; 5, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 11, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203232">↑</a> ix. 1, 1, 43; 3, 3, 18; 5, 1, 61, 68, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203232">↑</a> x. 6, 5, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203232">↑</a> vi. 5, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203232">↑</a> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, xxxiv. n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203232">↑</a> ii. 5, 22; iv. 5, 28 (Mādhyaṃdina = ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203232">↑</a> ii. 5, 20; iv. 5, 26 Mādhyaṃdina.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203232">↑</a> ii. 6, 1; iv. 6, 1 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203232">↑</a> ii. 5, 20; iv. 5, 26 (Mādhyaṃdina = ii. 6, 1; iv. 6, 1 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203232">↑</a> ii. 5, 20; iv. 5, 26 Mādhyaṃdina.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203232"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203232">↑</a> ii. 6, 2 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, xxxi <i>et seq.;</i> 43, xviii <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 71, 76 <i>et seq.;</i> 120, 131, 132; Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 213.</li></ol></div>

शाण्डिल्यायन|శాణ్డిల్యాయన|ಶಾಣ್ಡಿಲ್ಯಾಯನ|śāṇḍilyāyana|shaaNDilyaayana|_vei_120@3233|_vei2_pn372
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3232">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3234">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn371">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn372">2:372</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn373">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śāṇḍilyāyana,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्डिल्य">śāṇḍilya</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of a teacher in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203233"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203233">1</a></sup> Apparently he is identical with <b>Celaka,</b> also mentioned in that text;<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203233"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203233">2</a></sup> it is thus reasonable to suppose that <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चैलकि">cailaki</a> Jīvala</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203233"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203233">3</a></sup> was his son. It is much more doubtful whether he was<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203233"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203233">4</a></sup> the grandfather of <b>Pravāhaṇa <a class="vei_120a" href="जैवल">jaivala</a>,</b> who was a prince rather than a Brahmin.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203233"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203233">↑</a> ix. 5, 1, 64.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203233"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203233">↑</a> x. 4, 5, 3. The name <a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्डिल्यायन">śāṇḍilyāyana</a>, like that of Sāṇḍilya, is common in the Sūtras. See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 45, <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203233"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203233">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 3, 1, 34.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203233"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203233">↑</a> Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 259.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 53, 76, 120.</li></ol></div>

शात-पर्णेय|शातपर्णेय|शात|पर्णेय|శాత-పర్ణేయ|శాతపర్ణేయ|శాత|పర్ణేయ|ಶಾತ-ಪರ್ಣೇಯ|ಶಾತಪರ್ಣೇಯ|ಶಾತ|ಪರ್ಣೇಯ|śāta-parṇeya|śātaparṇeya|śāta|parṇeya|shaata-parNeya|shaataparNeya|shaata|parNeya|_vei_120@3234|_vei2_pn372
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3233">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3235">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn371">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn372">2:372</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn373">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śāta-parṇeya</b> ‘descendant of Śataparṇa,’ is the patronymic of <b>Dhīra</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (x. 3, 3, 1).</p></div>

शाद|శాద|ಶಾದ|śāda|shaada|_vei_120@3235|_vei2_pn372
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3234">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3236">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn371">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn372">2:372</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn373">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śāda</b> denotes ‘grass’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203235"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203235">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203235"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203235">2</a></sup> and later.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203235"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203235">↑</a> ix. 15, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203235"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203235">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxv. 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

शाप|శాప|ಶಾಪ|śāpa|shaapa|_vei_120@3236|_vei2_pn372
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3235">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3237">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn371">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn372">2:372</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn373">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śāpa</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203236"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203236">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203236"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203236">2</a></sup> denotes the ‘drift’ brought down by streams, possibly conceived as the ‘curse’ of the waters.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203236"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203236">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203236"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203236">↑</a> vii. 18, 5; x. 28, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203236"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203236">↑</a> Av. iii. 24, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xii. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203236"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203236">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 178; <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 184, 185.</li></ol></div>

शामुल्य|శాముల్య|ಶಾಮುಲ್ಯ|śāmulya|shaamulya|_vei_120@3237|_vei2_pn372
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3236">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3238">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn371">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn372">2:372</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn373">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śāmulya</b> in the marriage hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203237"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203237">1</a></sup> denotes a collen garment’ worn at night. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203237"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203237">↑</a> x. 85, 29. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 262.</li></ol></div>

शमूल|శమూల|ಶಮೂಲ|śamūla|shamuula|shamoola|_vei_120@3238|_vei2_pn373
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3237">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3239">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn372">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn373">2:373</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn374">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śamūla</b> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a></b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203238"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203238">1</a></sup> seems to have much the same sense as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शामुल्य">śāmulya</a>,</b> ‘a <b>woollen</b> shirt,’ generally. Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203238"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203238">2</a></sup> emends to <i>śamīla,</i> ‘pieces of <a class="vei_120a" href="शमी">śamī</a> wood.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203238"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203238">↑</a> i. 38, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Oertel, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, 116, 233; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ix. 4, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, lxix. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203238"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203238">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, ccxliii.</li></ol></div>

शाम्ब|शांब|శామ్బ|శాంబ|ಶಾಮ್ಬ|ಶಾಂಬ|śāmba|shaamba|_vei_120@3239|_vei2_pn373
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3238">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3240">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn372">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn373">2:373</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn374">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śāmba.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शार्कराक्ष">śārkarākṣa</a>.</b></p></div>

शाम्बर|शांबर|శామ్బర|శాంబర|ಶಾಮ್ಬರ|ಶಾಂಬರ|śāmbara|shaambara|_vei_120@3240|_vei2_pn373
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3239">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3241">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn372">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn373">2:373</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn374">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śāmbara,</b> properly an adjective in the sense of ‘relating to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शम्बर">śambara</a>,</b>’ appears in one passage of the Rigveda (iii. 47, 4) to be used as a substantive denoting ‘the contest with <a class="vei_120a" href="शम्बर">śambara</a>.’</p></div>

शाम्बु|शांबु|శామ్బు|శాంబు|ಶಾಮ್ಬು|ಶಾಂಬು|śāmbu|shaambu|_vei_120@3241|_vei2_pn373
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3240">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3242">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn372">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn373">2:373</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn374">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śāmbu</b> occurs in the plural with the <b>Aṅgirases</b> in a passage of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203241"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203241">1</a></sup> no doubt as the name of a family of ancient teachers. There is extant in manuscript a <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> of the Śāmbavyas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203241"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203241">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203241"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203241">↑</a> xix. 39, 5, where Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 960, retracts the emendation <i>Bhṛgubhyaḥ</i> for <i>Śāmbubhyaḥ</i> in the text.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203241"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203241">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 15, 4, 154.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 678.</li></ol></div>

शायस्थि|శాయస్థి|ಶಾಯಸ್ಥಿ|śāyasthi|shaayasthi|_vei_120@3242|_vei2_pn373
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3241">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3243">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn372">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn373">2:373</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn374">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śāyasthi</b> is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203242"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203242">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203242"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203242">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien.</i> 4, 372.</li></ol></div>

शारद|శారద|ಶಾರದ|śārada|shaarada|_vei_120@3243|_vei2_pn373
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3242">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3244">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn372">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn373">2:373</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn374">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śārada.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुर्">pur</a>.</b></p></div>

शारि|शारि|శారి_౧|శారి|ಶಾರಿ_೧|ಶಾರಿ|śāri_1|śāri|shaari_1|shaari|_vei_120@3244|_vei2_pn373
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3243">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3245">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn372">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn373">2:373</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn374">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Śāri</b> occurs in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203244">1</a></sup> It seems clear, since it is described as ‘of human speech’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुष">puruṣa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वाच्">vāc</a></i>), that it was some kind of bird, possibly the later Sārikā (‘starling’), as Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203244">2</a></sup> suggests. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शारिशाका">śāriśākā</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203244">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 12, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 14; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 33.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203244"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203244">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 90, 91.</li></ol></div>

शारि|शारि|శారి_౨|శారి|ಶಾರಿ_೨|ಶಾರಿ|śāri_2|śāri|shaari_2|shaari|_vei_120@3245|_vei2_pn374
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3244">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3246">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn373">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn374">2:374</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn375">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Śāri,</b> occurring in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203245"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203245">1</a></sup> is said by Sāyaṇa to mean ‘arrow.’ This is uncertain, but connexion with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शर">śara</a></b> or 1. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शारि">śāri</a></b> is quite possible.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203245"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203245">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203245"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203245">↑</a> i. 112, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203245"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203245">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 103.</li></ol></div>

शारिशाका|శారిశాకా|ಶಾರಿಶಾಕಾ|śāriśākā|shaarishaakaa|_vei_120@3246|_vei2_pn374
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3245">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3247">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn373">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn374">2:374</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn375">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śāriśākā</b> is an utterly obscure expression found in one passage of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203246"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203246">1</a></sup> Weber<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203246"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203246">2</a></sup> thinks it means ‘dung (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शकन्">śakan</a></i>) of the <a class="vei_120a" href="शारि">śāri</a> bird’; Grill<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203246"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203246">3</a></sup> sees in the word the <i>śārikā,</i> ‘the hooded crow’; Roth<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203246"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203246">4</a></sup> suggests the emendation (<i>śāriḥ (= śāliḥ) <a class="vei_120a" href="शक">śaka</a> iva,</i> ‘like rice in manure’; and Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203246"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203246">5</a></sup> emends <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शारि">śāri</a>-śukeva,</i> ‘like starlings and parrots.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203246"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203246">↑</a> iii. 14, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203246"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203246">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 246.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203246"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203246">↑</a> <i>Hundert Lieder,^2</i> 112.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203246"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203246">↑</a> In Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 110.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203246"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203246">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 351. But see Lanman's note in Whitney, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

शार्कराक्ष|శార్కరాక్ష|ಶಾರ್ಕರಾಕ್ಷ|śārkarākṣa|shaarkaraakSha|shaarkaraaxa|_vei_120@3247|_vei2_pn374
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3246">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3248">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn373">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn374">2:374</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn375">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śārkarākṣa</b> is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203247"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203247">1</a></sup> as the patronymic of a teacher, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाम्ब">śāmba</a>,</b> perhaps by a blunder for <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शार्कराक्ष्य">śārkarākṣya</a>,</b> ‘descendant of Śarkarākṣa.’ In the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203247"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203247">2</a></sup> a teacher, <a class="vei_120a" href="शर्कराख्य">śarkarākhya</a>, occurs, again probably a blunder, in this case for Śarkarākṣa himself. The patronymic Śārkarākṣi is found in the Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203247"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203247">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203247"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203247">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203247"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203247">↑</a> xxii. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203247"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203247">↑</a> xii. 10. 10.</li></ol></div>

शार्कराक्ष्य|శార్కరాక్ష్య|ಶಾರ್ಕರಾಕ್ಷ್ಯ|śārkarākṣya|shaarkaraakShya|shaarkaraaxya|_vei_120@3248|_vei2_pn374
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3247">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3249">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn373">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn374">2:374</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn375">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śārkarākṣya,</b> ‘descendant of Śarkarākṣa,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a></b> in the Śatapatha Brāhmana<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203248">1</a></sup> and the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203248">2</a></sup> In the plural they occur in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203248">3</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203248">4</a></sup> It is not necessary to assume that the form is incorrect for <a class="vei_120a" href="शार्कराक्ष">śārkarākṣa</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203248">↑</a> x. 6, 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203248">↑</a> v. 11, 1; 15, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203248">↑</a> ii. 1, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203248"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203248">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 382.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 204; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 388; 3, 259.</li></ol></div>

शार्ग|శార్గ|ಶಾರ್ಗ|śārga|shaarga|_vei_120@3249|_vei2_pn374
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3248">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3250">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn373">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn374">2:374</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn375">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śārga</b> is the name of a bird in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203249"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203249">1</a></sup> Sāyana on the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā calls it the ‘wild Caṭaka.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203249"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203249">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 19, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 14; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 33. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 93.</li></ol></div>

शार्ंग|शार्ङ्ग|శార్ంగ|శార్ఙ్గ|ಶಾರ್ಂಗ|ಶಾರ್ಙ್ಗ|śārṅga|shaarMga|shaarnga|_vei_120@3250|_vei2_pn375
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3249">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3251">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn374">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn375">2:375</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn376">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śārṅga.</b>--The Anukramaṇī<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203250"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203250">1</a></sup> (Index) of the Rigveda ascribes a hymn of that text<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203250"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203250">2</a></sup> to the Śārṅgas, <a class="vei_120a" href="जरितृ">jaritṛ</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="द्रोण">droṇa</a>, Sārisṛkva, and Stambamitra. The Mahābhārata<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203250"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203250">3</a></sup> contains a tale describing how the four Śārṅgas, sons of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> Mandapāla, were saved from the great fire in the <a class="vei_120a" href="खाण्डव">khāṇḍava</a> forest by means of prayers. Sieg<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203250"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203250">4</a></sup> has attempted to use this tale for the elucidation of the hymn in question, but without substantial success. As Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203250"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203250">5</a></sup> says, the tale is based on the hymn rather than <i>vice versa.</i></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203250"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203250">↑</a> See also Sāyaṇa on Rv. x. 142; Ṣaḍguruśiṣya on the Sarvānukramaṇī (ed. Macdonell), p. 163.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203250"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203250">↑</a> x. 142.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203250"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203250">↑</a> i. 8334 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203250"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203250">↑</a> <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 44-50.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203250"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203250">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 39, 79.</li></ol></div>

शार्दूल|శార్దూల|ಶಾರ್ದೂಲ|śārdūla|shaarduula|shaardoola|_vei_120@3251|_vei2_pn375
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3250">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3252">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn374">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn375">2:375</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn376">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śārdūla,</b> ‘tiger,’ is mentioned in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203251">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203251">2</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्याघ्र">vyāghra</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203251">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 11, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 10; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 11; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 30.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203251"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203251">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 5, 3; iv. 1, 9, 11; 5, 4, 10; xi. 8, 4, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 8, 1; 8, 5, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 2, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 79.</li></ol></div>

शार्यात|శార్యాత|ಶಾರ್ಯಾತ|śāryāta|shaaryaata|_vei_120@3252|_vei2_pn375
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3251">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3253">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn374">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn375">2:375</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn376">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śāryāta,</b> perhaps ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शर्यात">śaryāta</a>,</b>’ is the name of a singer in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203252">1</a></sup> A <a class="vei_120a" href="शार्यात">śāryāta</a> appears also in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203252">2</a></sup> with the patronymic <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a></b> as the seer of a Rigvedic hymn,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203252">3</a></sup> and as having been anointed by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="च्यवन">cyavana</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203252">4</a></sup> The same man is evidently meant by <a class="vei_120a" href="शर्यात">śaryāta</a> in the story of <a class="vei_120a" href="च्यवन">cyavana</a> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203252">5</a></sup> and the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203252">6</a></sup> In both these passages the Śāryātas are mentioned as his descendants, and his daughter is called Śāryātī.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203252">↑</a> i. 51, 12; iii. 51, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203252">↑</a> iv. 32, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203252">↑</a> x. 92.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203252">↑</a> viii. 21, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203252">↑</a> iv. 1, 5, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203252"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203252">↑</a> iii. 121 <i>et seq.</i> (Whitney, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society</i>) 11, cxlv.; Hopkins, <i>ibid.,</i> 26, 58.</li></ol></div>

शालंकायन|शालङ्कायन|శాలంకాయన|శాలఙ్కాయన|ಶಾಲಂಕಾಯನ|ಶಾಲಙ್ಕಾಯನ|śālaṅkāyana|shaalaMkaayana|shaalankaayana|_vei_120@3253|_vei2_pn375
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3252">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3254">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn374">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn375">2:375</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn376">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śālaṅkāyana,</b> ‘descendant of Śalaṅku,’ is the patronymic of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203253"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203253">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203253"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203253">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 383; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 10, 10; Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxiv. 9, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 75; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 49.</li></ol></div>

शालंकायनी-पुत्र|शालंकायनीपुत्र|शालंकायनी|शालंकायनि|पुत्र|शालङ्कायनी-पुत्र|शालङ्कायनीपुत्र|शालङ्कायनी|शालङ्कायनि|శాలంకాయనీ-పుత్ర|శాలంకాయనీపుత్ర|శాలంకాయనీ|శాలంకాయని|పుత్ర|శాలఙ్కాయనీ-పుత్ర|శాలఙ్కాయనీపుత్ర|శాలఙ్కాయనీ|శాలఙ్కాయని|ಶಾಲಂಕಾಯನೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಶಾಲಂಕಾಯನೀಪುತ್ರ|ಶಾಲಂಕಾಯನೀ|ಶಾಲಂಕಾಯನಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|ಶಾಲಙ್ಕಾಯನೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಶಾಲಙ್ಕಾಯನೀಪುತ್ರ|ಶಾಲಙ್ಕಾಯನೀ|ಶಾಲಙ್ಕಾಯನಿ|śālaṅkāyanī-putra|śālaṅkāyanīputra|śālaṅkāyanī|śālaṅkāyani|putra|shaalaMkaayanii-putra|shaalaMkaayaniiputra|shaalaMkaayanii|shaalaMkaayani|shaalaMkaayanee-putra|shaalaMkaayaneeputra|shaalaMkaayanee|shaalankaayanii-putra|shaalankaayaniiputra|shaalankaayanii|shaalankaayani|shaalankaayanee-putra|shaalankaayaneeputra|shaalankaayanee|_vei_120@3254|_vei2_pn376
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3253">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3255">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn375">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn376">2:376</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn377">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śālaṅkāyanī-putra,</b> ‘son of a female descendant of Śalaṅku,’ is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वार्षगणीपुत्र">vārṣagaṇīputra</a></b> in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Mādhyaṃdina recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 4, 31).</p></div>

शाला|శాలా|ಶಾಲಾ|śālā|shaalaa|_vei_120@3255|_vei2_pn376
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3254">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3256">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn375">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn376">2:376</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn377">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śālā</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203255">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203255">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘house’ in the wide sense of the word, including such meanings as ‘stall’ for cattle, ‘shed’ for corn, etc.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203255">3</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गृह">gṛha</a>.</b> The householder is called <a class="vei_120a" href="शाला">śālā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>, ‘lord of the house,’ in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203255">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203255">↑</a> v. 31, 5; vi. 106, 3; viii. 6, 10; ix. 3, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> xiv. 1, 63.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203255">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 3, 1; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 1, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203255">↑</a> Av. iii. 12, 1 <i>et seq.,</i> and <i>cf.</i> ix. 3, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203255"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203255">↑</a> ix. 3, 12.</li></ol></div>

शालावत्य|శాలావత్య|ಶಾಲಾವತ್ಯ|śālāvatya|shaalaavatya|_vei_120@3256|_vei2_pn376
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3255">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3257">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn375">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn376">2:376</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn377">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śālāvatya,</b> ‘descendant of Śalāvant,’ is the patronymic of <b>Śilaka</b> in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (i. 8, 1), and of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गलूनस आर्क्षाकायण">galūnasa ārkṣākāyaṇa</a></b> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 38, 4).</p></div>

सालि|సాలి|ಸಾಲಿ|sāli|saali|_vei_120@3257|_vei2_pn376
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3256">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3258">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn375">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn376">2:376</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn377">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sāli,</b> a later word for ‘rice,’ is conjectured by Roth to be the equivalent of <a class="vei_120a" href="शारि">śāri</a> in the word <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शारिशाका">śāriśākā</a></b> in the Atharvaveda.</p></div>

शालूक|శాలూక|ಶಾಲೂಕ|śālūka|shaaluuka|shaalooka|_vei_120@3258|_vei2_pn376
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3257">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3259">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn375">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn376">2:376</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn377">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śālūka</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203258"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203258">1</a></sup> denotes the edible roots of the lotus.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203258"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203258">↑</a> iv. 34, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 70; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 207.</li></ol></div>

शाल्व|శాల్వ|ಶಾಲ್ವ|śālva|shaalva|_vei_120@3259|_vei2_pn376
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3258">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3260">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn375">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn376">2:376</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn377">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śālva</b> as the name of a people is found in the Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203259"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203259">1</a></sup> coupled with that of the <b>Matsyas.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203259"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203259">↑</a> i. 2, 9. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सल्व">salva</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

सावसायन|సావసాయన|ಸಾವಸಾಯನ|sāvasāyana|saavasaayana|_vei_120@3260|_vei2_pn376
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3259">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3261">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn375">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn376">2:376</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn377">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sāvasāyana,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="शवस्">śavas</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b>Devataras</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203260">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203260"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203260">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373.</li></ol></div>

शास|శాస|ಶಾಸ|śāsa|shaasa|_vei_120@3261|_vei2_pn376
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3260">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3262">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn375">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn376">2:376</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn377">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śāsa</b> denotes in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203261"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203261">1</a></sup> a ‘sword’ or ‘knife.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203261"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203261">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 17, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 25, 1 (of the knife to be used in slaying <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a></b>); Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8, 1, 4, 5; xiii. 2, 3, 16.</li></ol></div>

शिंशपा|శింశపా|ಶಿಂಶಪಾ|śiṃśapā|shiMshapaa|_vei_120@3262|_vei2_pn377
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3261">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3263">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn376">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn377">2:377</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn378">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śiṃśapā</b> is the name of a tree (<i>Dalbergia Sisu</i>) in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203262"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203262">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203262"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203262">2</a></sup> It is a sately and beautiful tree.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203262"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203262">↑</a> iii. 53, 19 (with the <a class="vei_120a" href="खदिर">khadira</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203262"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203262">↑</a> Av. xx. 129, 7. <i>Cf. śāṃśapa</i> in vi. 129, 1; Whiteny, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 378.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 61.</li></ol></div>

शिंशु-मार|शिंशुमार|शिंशु|मार|శింశు-మార|శింశుమార|శింశు|మార|ಶಿಂಶು-ಮಾರ|ಶಿಂಶುಮಾರ|ಶಿಂಶು|ಮಾರ|śiṃśu-māra|śiṃśumāra|śiṃśu|māra|shiMshu-maara|shiMshumaara|shiMshu|maara|_vei_120@3263|_vei2_pn377
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3262">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3264">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn376">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn377">2:377</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn378">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śiṃśu-māra,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203263">1</a></sup> or <b>Śiśu-<a class="vei_120a" href="मार">māra</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203263">2</a></sup> is the name of an aquatic creature in the Rigveda and the later Saṃhitās. It is either the ‘crocodile,’ the ‘alligator,’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203263">3</a></sup> or the ‘porpoise’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203263">4</a></sup> (<i>Delphinus Gangeticus</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203263">↑</a> Rv. i. 116, 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 11; Av. xi. 2, 25. In <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xii. 28, the reading is doubtful.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203263">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 30, and the Paippalāda recension of the Av., <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 19.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203263">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 325, and Sāyaṇa on Rv., <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Av., <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203263"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203263">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. <a class="vei_120a" href="शिशुमार">śiśumāra</a>;</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 157; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 624.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 96; Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 179.</li></ol></div>

शिक्य|శిక్య|ಶಿಕ್ಯ|śikya|shikya|_vei_120@3264|_vei2_pn377
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3263">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3265">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn376">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn377">2:377</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn378">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śikya</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203264"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203264">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203264"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203264">2</a></sup> seems to mean a carrying ‘sling’ of rope.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203264"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203264">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203264"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203264">↑</a> ix. 3, 6, where Whitney suggests that it may be an ornamental hanging appendage. See Lanman in Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 526. Whitney's alternative rendering ‘slings’ is better. See Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 597. <i>Cf.</i> perhaps Av. xiii. 4, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203264"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203264">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 2, 4, 2. 3; 6, 9, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203264"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203264">↑</a> This is pretty clearly the meaning in Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 4, 28; vi. 7, 1, 16. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 268, n. 3.</li></ol></div>

शिख|శిఖ|ಶಿಖ|śikha|shikha|_vei_120@3265|_vei2_pn377
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3264">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3266">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn376">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn377">2:377</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn378">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śikha</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अनुशिख">anuśikha</a></b> are the names of two priests who served as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नेष्टृ">neṣṭṛ</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पोतृ">potṛ</a></b> at the snake festival in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्च">pañca</a>-</b> viṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203265">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203265"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203265">↑</a> xxv. 15, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 35.</li></ol></div>

शिखण्ड|శిఖణ్డ|ಶಿಖಣ್ಡ|śikhaṇḍa|shikhaNDa|_vei_120@3266|_vei2_pn377
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3265">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3267">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn376">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn377">2:377</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn378">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śikhaṇḍa</b> denotes a ‘tuft’ or ‘lock,’ as a mode of wearing the hair, in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203266">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203266"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203266">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 3, 16, 2 (in the plural); <i>catuḥ-<a class="vei_120a" href="शिखण्ड">śikhaṇḍa</a>,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 1, 27; iii. 7, 6, 4 (corresponding to <i>catuḥ-<a class="vei_120a" href="कपर्द">kaparda</a>,</i> Rv. x. 114, 3). So <i>śikhaṇḍin</i> means ‘wearing a tuft of hair,’ and is found in Av. iv. 37, 7; xi. 2, 12, etc.</li></ol></div>

शिखण्डिन् याज्ञसेन|శిఖణ్డిన్ యాజ్ఞసేన|ಶಿಖಣ್ಡಿನ್ ಯಾಜ್ಞಸೇನ|śikhaṇḍin yājñasena|shikhaNDin yaaj~nasena|_vei_120@3267|_vei2_pn378
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3266">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3268">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn377">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn378">2:378</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn379">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śikhaṇḍin Yājñasena</b> (‘descendant of Yajñasena’) is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vii. 4) as a priest of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="केशिन्">keśin</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दाल्भ्य">dālbhya</a>.</b></p></div>

शिखर|శిఖర|ಶಿಖರ|śikhara|shikhara|_vei_120@3268|_vei2_pn378
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3267">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3269">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn377">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn378">2:378</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn379">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śikhara</b> as a ‘peak’ of a mountain is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xxvi. 1), and often in the Epic.</p></div>

शिखा|శిఖా|ಶಿಖಾ|śikhā|shikhaa|_vei_120@3269|_vei2_pn378
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3268">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3270">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn377">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn378">2:378</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn379">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śikhā</b> denotes in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203269">1</a></sup> the ‘knot of hair’ worn on the top of the head. Wearing the top-knot unloosened was the sign of mourning in the case of women and men alike.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203269">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203269">↑</a> i. 3, 3, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203269"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203269">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 2, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 11, 340; <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 634, on Av. ix. 9, 7.</li></ol></div>

शिग्रु|శిగ్రు|ಶಿಗ್ರು|śigru|shigru|_vei_120@3270|_vei2_pn378
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3269">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3271">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn377">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn378">2:378</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn379">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śigru</b> is the name of a tribe occurring in the passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203270"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203270">1</a></sup> in which they are mentioned with the <b>Ajas</b> and the <b>Yakṣus</b> as having been defeated by the <b>Tṛtsus</b> and King <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>.</b> It is impossible to say whether they were or were not under the leadership of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भेद">bheda</a>,</b> as Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203270"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203270">2</a></sup> plausibly suggests. If Sigru is connected with the later <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शिग्रु">śigru</a>,</i> ‘horse-radish’ (<i>Moringa pterygosperma</i>), which is quite probable, it is possible that the tribe was totemistic and non-Āryan, but this is a mere matter of conjecture.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203270"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203270">3</a></sup> The <b>Matsyas</b> (‘Fishes’) were probably Āryan.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203270"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203270">↑</a> vii. 18, 19.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203270"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203270">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 173.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203270"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203270">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 85; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> 153; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, cliv; Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1907, 929 <i>et seq.; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 200, n.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 127.</li></ol></div>

शिञ्जार|శిఞ్జార|ಶಿಞ್ಜಾರ|śiñjāra|shi~njaara|_vei_120@3271|_vei2_pn378
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3270">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3272">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn377">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn378">2:378</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn379">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śiñjāra</b> is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> twice mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203271"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203271">1</a></sup> along with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रियमेध">priyamedha</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="उपस्तुत">upastuta</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अत्रि">atri</a>.</b> Geldner<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203271"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203271">2</a></sup> takes the word either as a name of <a class="vei_120a" href="अत्रि">atri</a> or an adjective. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203271"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203271">↑</a> viii. 5, 25; x. 40, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 3, 139.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203271"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203271">↑</a> <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 179.</li></ol></div>

शिति-कक्षी|शिति-कक्षि|शितिकक्षी|शितिकक्षि|शिति|कक्षी|कक्षि|శితి-కక్షీ|శితి-కక్షి|శితికక్షీ|శితికక్షి|శితి|కక్షీ|కక్షి|ಶಿತಿ-ಕಕ್ಷೀ|ಶಿತಿ-ಕಕ್ಷಿ|ಶಿತಿಕಕ್ಷೀ|ಶಿತಿಕಕ್ಷಿ|ಶಿತಿ|ಕಕ್ಷೀ|ಕಕ್ಷಿ|śiti-kakṣī|śiti-kakṣi|śitikakṣī|śitikakṣi|śiti|kakṣī|kakṣi|shiti-kakShii|shiti-kakShi|shitikakShii|shitikakShi|shiti|kakShii|kakShi|shiti-kakShee|shitikakShee|kakShee|shiti-kaxii|shiti-kaxi|shitikaxii|shitikaxi|kaxii|kaxi|_vei_120@3272|_vei2_pn379
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3271">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3273">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn378">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn379">2:379</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn380">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śiti-kakṣī</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203272"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203272">1</a></sup> is explained by Sāyaṇa as a ‘white-breasted’ (<i>pāṇḍarodara</i>) vulture. The word may, however, well be only an adjective.^2</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203272"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203272">↑</a> v. 5, 20, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 4; Av. v. 23, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 93.</li></ol></div>

शिति-पृष्ठ|शितिपृष्ठ|शिति|पृष्ठ|శితి-పృష్ఠ|శితిపృష్ఠ|శితి|పృష్ఠ|ಶಿತಿ-ಪೃಷ್ಠ|ಶಿತಿಪೃಷ್ಠ|ಶಿತಿ|ಪೃಷ್ಠ|śiti-pṛṣṭha|śitipṛṣṭha|śiti|pṛṣṭha|shiti-pRShTha|shitipRShTha|shiti|pRShTha|shiti-pRiShTha|shitipRiShTha|pRiShTha|shiti-pRuShTha|shitipRuShTha|pRuShTha|shiti-pRRiShTha|shitipRRiShTha|pRRiShTha|_vei_120@3273|_vei2_pn379
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3272">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3274">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn378">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn379">2:379</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn380">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śiti-pṛṣṭha</b> (‘white-backed’) is the name of the Maitrāvaruṇa priest at the snake festival in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203273"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203273">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203273"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203273">↑</a> xxv. 15, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 35.</li></ol></div>

शिति-बाहु ऐषकृत नैमिशि|शितिबाहु ऐषकृत नैमिशि|शिति|बाहु ऐषकृत नैमिशि|శితి-బాహు ఐషకృత నైమిశి|శితిబాహు ఐషకృత నైమిశి|శితి|బాహు ఐషకృత నైమిశి|ಶಿತಿ-ಬಾಹು ಐಷಕೃತ ನೈಮಿಶಿ|ಶಿತಿಬಾಹು ಐಷಕೃತ ನೈಮಿಶಿ|ಶಿತಿ|ಬಾಹು ಐಷಕೃತ ನೈಮಿಶಿ|śiti-bāhu aiṣakṛta naimiśi|śitibāhu aiṣakṛta naimiśi|śiti|bāhu aiṣakṛta naimiśi|shiti-baahu aiShakRta naimishi|shitibaahu aiShakRta naimishi|shiti|baahu aiShakRta naimishi|shiti-baahu aiShakRita naimishi|shitibaahu aiShakRita naimishi|baahu aiShakRita naimishi|shiti-baahu aiShakRuta naimishi|shitibaahu aiShakRuta naimishi|baahu aiShakRuta naimishi|shiti-baahu aiShakRRita naimishi|shitibaahu aiShakRRita naimishi|baahu aiShakRRita naimishi|_vei_120@3274|_vei2_pn379
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3273">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3275">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn378">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn379">2:379</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn380">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śiti-<a class="vei_120a" href="बाहु">bāhu</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐषकृत">aiṣakṛta</a> Naimiśi</b> is mentioned as a sacrificer in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203274">1</a></sup> where it is recorded that a monkey an off with his sacrificial cake.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203274"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203274">↑</a> i. 363 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 26, 192).</li></ol></div>

शित्पुट|శిత్పుట|ಶಿತ್ಪುಟ|śitpuṭa|shitpuTa|_vei_120@3275|_vei2_pn379
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3274">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3276">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn378">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn379">2:379</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn380">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śitpuṭa</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203275"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203275">1</a></sup> denotes, according to the commentator, a kind of cat.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203275"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203275">↑</a> v. 5, 17, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 86.</li></ol></div>

शिपद|శిపద|ಶಿಪದ|śipada|shipada|_vei_120@3276|_vei2_pn379
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3275">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3277">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn378">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn379">2:379</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn380">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śipada</b> occurs only in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203276"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203276">1</a></sup> in the negative form <i>a-<a class="vei_120a" href="शिपद">śipada</a>,</i> together with <i>a-<a class="vei_120a" href="शिमिद">śimida</a>.</i> Both <a class="vei_120a" href="शिपद">śipada</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="शिमिद">śimida</a> are perhaps names of unknown diseases.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203276"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203276">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203276"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203276">↑</a> vii. 50, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203276"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203276">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 394.</li></ol></div>

शिपवित्नुक|శిపవిత్నుక|ಶಿಪವಿತ್ನುಕ|śipavitnuka|shipavitnuka|_vei_120@3277|_vei2_pn379
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3276">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3278">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn378">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn379">2:379</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn380">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śipavitnuka</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203277"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203277">1</a></sup> seems to denote a species of worm.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203277"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203277">↑</a> v. 20, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 98; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 262.</li></ol></div>

शिप्रा|శిప్రా|ಶಿಪ್ರಾ|śiprā|shipraa|_vei_120@3278|_vei2_pn379
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3277">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3279">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn378">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn379">2:379</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn380">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śiprā</b> is a word of somewhat uncertain sense: it seems to mean ‘cheeks’ in several passages;<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203278">1</a></sup> in others<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203278">2</a></sup> it appears to  designate the ‘cheek-pieces’ of a helmet, or of the ‘bit’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203278">3</a></sup> of a horse. In <i>ayaḥ-<a class="vei_120a" href="शिप्र">śipra</a>,</i> used of the Aśvins,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203278">4</a></sup> and the other compounds, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्य">hiraṇya</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="शिप्र">śipra</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203278">5</a></sup> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="हरि">hari</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="शिप्र">śipra</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203278">6</a></sup> and <i>hiri-<a class="vei_120a" href="शिप्र">śipra</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203278">7</a></sup> the word probably has the extended sense of ‘helmet,’ described as ‘of iron,’ ‘of gold,’ or ‘yellow,’ Similarly <i>śiprin</i><sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203278">8</a></sup> would mean ‘wearing a helmet.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203278">↑</a> Rv. iii. 32, 1; v. 36, 2; viii. 76, 10; x. 96, 9; 105, 5, all according to Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> Geldner. <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 179, who treats the word as a neuter (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शिप्र">śipra</a></i>), takes it in i. 101, 10, as ‘lip’ (<i>cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 249, n.), and sees in iii. 32, 1; viii. 76, 10; x. 96, 9, the sense of ‘moustache.’ <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 17, gives the alternative senses of ‘jaw’ and ‘nose.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203278">↑</a> Rv. v. 54, 11; viii. 7, 25. Geldner, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> here accepts <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शिप्रा">śiprā</a></i> as ‘helmet.’</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203278">↑</a> Rv. i. 101, 10; Zimmer, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203278">↑</a> Rv. iv. 37, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203278">↑</a> Rv. ii. 34, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203278">↑</a> Rv. x. 96, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203278">↑</a> Rv. ii. 2, 3; vi. 25, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203278"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203278">↑</a> Rv. i. 29, 2; 81, 4; vi, 44, 14, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 301; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 39, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

शिफा|శిఫా|ಶಿಫಾ|śiphā|shiphaa|_vei_120@3279|_vei2_pn380
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3278">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3280">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn379">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn380">2:380</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn381">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śiphā</b> is found in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203279">1</a></sup> where Sāyaṇa explains the word as the name of a river, quite a possible interpretation.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203279"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203279">↑</a> i. 104, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 18; Perry, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 11, 201.</li></ol></div>

शिबि|శిబి|ಶಿಬಿ|śibi|shibi|_vei_120@3280|_vei2_pn380
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3279">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3281">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn379">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn380">2:380</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn381">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śibi,</b> son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उशीनर">uśīnara</a>,</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203280">1</a></sup> as a protégé of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, who sacrificed for him on the Varṣiṣṭhīya plain, and saved him from fear of foreign invasion.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203280"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203280">↑</a> xxi. 18. <i>Cf.</i> Caland, <i>Über das rituelle <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> des <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>,</i> 28.</li></ol></div>

शिमिद|శిమిద|ಶಿಮಿದ|śimida|shimida|_vei_120@3281|_vei2_pn380
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3280">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3282">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn379">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn380">2:380</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn381">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śimida,</b> occurring in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203281"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203281">1</a></sup> in the compound <i>a-śinida,</i> perhaps denotes a disease. The feminine form, Śimidā, is found as the name of a demoness in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203281"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203281">2</a></sup> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203281"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203281">3</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शिपद">śipada</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203281"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203281">↑</a> vii. 50, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 394.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203281"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203281">↑</a> iv. 25, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203281"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203281">↑</a> vii. 4, 1, 27.</li></ol></div>

शिम्बल|शिंबल|శిమ్బల|శింబల|ಶಿಮ್ಬಲ|ಶಿಂಬಲ|śimbala|shimbala|_vei_120@3282|_vei2_pn380
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3281">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3283">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn379">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn380">2:380</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn381">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śimbala</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203282"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203282">1</a></sup> denotes, according to Sāyaṇa, the flower of the Śālmali (= <b>Śalmali</b>), ‘silk-cotton tree.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203282"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203282">↑</a> iii. 53, 22. <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 179; Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 254.</li></ol></div>

शिम्यु|శిమ్యు|ಶಿಮ್ಯು|śimyu|shimyu|_vei_120@3283|_vei2_pn381
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3282">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3284">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn380">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn381">2:381</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn382">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śimyu</b> occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203283"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203283">1</a></sup> as the name of one of the peoples or kings who were defeated by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a></b> in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दाशराज्ञ">dāśarājña</a></b> (‘battle of the ten kings’). Since in another passage<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203283"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203283">2</a></sup> the Śimyus are coupled with the <b>Dasyus,</b> Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203283"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203283">3</a></sup> plausibly concludes that they were non-Āryans.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203283"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203283">↑</a> vii. 18, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203283"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203283">↑</a> i. 100, 18, where Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> thinks that the word simply means ‘enemy.’</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203283"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203283">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 118, 119.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 261.</li></ol></div>

शिरिम्बिठ|शिरिंबिठ|శిరిమ్బిఠ|శిరింబిఠ|ಶಿರಿಮ್ಬಿಠ|ಶಿರಿಂಬಿಠ|śirimbiṭha|shirimbiTha|_vei_120@3284|_vei2_pn381
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3283">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3285">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn380">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn381">2:381</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn382">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śirimbiṭha</b> occurs in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203284"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203284">1</a></sup> where the name of a man may possibly be meant, the Anukramaṇī (Index) ascribing the hymn in which the word occurs to his authorship. <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203284"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203284">2</a></sup> however, renders the term by ‘cloud.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203284"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203284">↑</a> x. 155, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203284"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203284">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 30. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 167.</li></ol></div>

शिलक शालावत्य|శిలక శాలావత్య|ಶಿಲಕ ಶಾಲಾವತ್ಯ|śilaka śālāvatya|shilaka shaalaavatya|_vei_120@3285|_vei2_pn381
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3284">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3286">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn380">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn381">2:381</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn382">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śilaka Śālāvatya</b> (‘descendant of Śalāvant’) is the name of a teacher, a contemporary of <b>Calkitāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="दाल्भ्य">dālbhya</a></b> and <b>Pravāhaṇa <a class="vei_120a" href="जैवल">jaivala</a>,</b> in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (i. 8, 1).</p></div>

शिल्प|शिल्प|శిల్ప_౧|శిల్ప|ಶಿಲ್ಪ_೧|ಶಿಲ್ಪ|śilpa_1|śilpa|shilpa_1|shilpa|_vei_120@3286|_vei2_pn381
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3285">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3287">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn380">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn381">2:381</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn382">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Śilpa</b> means ‘art,’ of which three kinds--<i>nṛtya,</i> ‘dance’; <i>gīta,</i> ‘song’; and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वादित">vādita</a>,</i> ‘instrumental music,’ are enumerated in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xxix. 5).</p></div>

शिल्प कश्यप|शिल्प कश्यप|శిల్ప కశ్యప_౨|శిల్ప కశ్యప|ಶಿಲ್ಪ ಕಶ್ಯಪ_೨|ಶಿಲ್ಪ ಕಶ್ಯಪ|śilpa kaśyapa_2|śilpa kaśyapa|shilpa kashyapa_2|shilpa kashyapa|_vei_120@3287|_vei2_pn381
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3286">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3288">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn380">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn381">2:381</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn382">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Śilpa Kaśyapa</b> is named in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203287"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203287">1</a></sup> as a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कश्यप नैध्रुवि">kaśyapa naidhruvi</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203287"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203287">↑</a> vi. 4, 33 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 5, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

शिव|శివ|ಶಿವ|śiva|shiva|_vei_120@3288|_vei2_pn381
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3287">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3289">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn380">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn381">2:381</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn382">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śiva</b> as the name of a people occurs once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203288">1</a></sup> where they share with the <b>Alinas, Pakthas, Bhalānases,</b> and <b>Viṣāṇins</b> the honour of being defeated by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>,</b> not of being, as Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203288">2</a></sup> thought, his allies. There can hardly be any doubt  of their identity with the <sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203288">3</a></sup> or <sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203288">4</a></sup> of the Greeks, who dwelt between the Indus and the Akesines (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="असिक्नी">asiknī</a></b>) in Alexander's time. The village of <a class="vei_120a" href="शिव">śiva</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पुर">pura</a>, mentioned by the scholiast on Pāṇini<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203288">5</a></sup> as situated in the northern country, may also perserve the name. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शिबि">śibi</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203288">↑</a> vii. 18, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203288">↑</a> <i>Zur Litteratur und Geschichte des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 95 <i>et seq.;</i> once accepted by Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 126.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203288">↑</a> Arrian, <i>Indica,</i> v. 12.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203288">↑</a> Diodorus, xvii. 96.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203288"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203288">↑</a> iv. 2, 109. Connected with <a class="vei_120a" href="शिव">śiva</a> by Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 376. <i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 431; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 173; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 260 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

शिशिर|శిశిర|ಶಿಶಿರ|śiśira|shishira|_vei_120@3289|_vei2_pn382
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3288">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3290">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn381">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn382">2:382</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn383">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śiśira.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋतु">ṛtu</a>.</b></p></div>

शिशु आंगिरस|शिशु आङ्गिरस|శిశు ఆంగిరస|శిశు ఆఙ్గిరస|ಶಿಶು ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಶಿಶು ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|śiśu āṅgirasa|shishu aaMgirasa|shishu aangirasa|_vei_120@3290|_vei2_pn382
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3289">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3291">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn381">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn382">2:382</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn383">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śiśu Āṅgirasa</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अंगिरस्">aṅgiras</a></b>’) is the name of the seer of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> or Chant in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203290">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203290"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203290">↑</a> xiii. 3, 24. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 160.</li></ol></div>

शिशुक|శిశుక|ಶಿಶುಕ|śiśuka|shishuka|_vei_120@3291|_vei2_pn382
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3290">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3292">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn381">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn382">2:382</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn383">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śiśuka</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203291"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203291">1</a></sup> seems to be an adjective meaning ‘young,’ but according to Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203291"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203291">2</a></sup> it has the sense of ‘foal.’ The commentator, Sāyaṇa, reads <a class="vei_120a" href="शुशुक">śuśuka</a>, which he explains as a ‘wild animal so called.’ <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आशुंग">āśuṃga</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203291"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203291">↑</a> vi. 14, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 291.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203291"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203291">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 464.</li></ol></div>

शिशुमार|शिशुमार|శిశుమార_౧|శిశుమార|ಶಿಶುಮಾರ_೧|ಶಿಶುಮಾರ|śiśumāra_1|śiśumāra|shishumaara_1|shishumaara|_vei_120@3292|_vei2_pn382
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3291">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3293">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn381">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn382">2:382</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn383">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Śiśumāra.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शिंशुमार">śiṃśumāra</a>.</b></p></div>

शिशुमार|शिशुमार|శిశుమార_౨|శిశుమార|ಶಿಶುಮಾರ_೨|ಶಿಶುಮಾರ|śiśumāra_2|śiśumāra|shishumaara_2|shishumaara|_vei_120@3293|_vei2_pn382
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3292">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3294">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn381">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn382">2:382</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn383">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Śiśumāra</b> is a term applied to <b>Śarkara</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xiv. 5, 15), where he is called a Śiśumārarṣi, explained by the commentator to mean a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> in the form of a Siśumāra.</p></div>

सिश्न-देव|सिश्नदेव|सिश्न|देव|సిశ్న-దేవ|సిశ్నదేవ|సిశ్న|దేవ|ಸಿಶ್ನ-ದೇವ|ಸಿಶ್ನದೇವ|ಸಿಶ್ನ|ದೇವ|siśna-deva|siśnadeva|siśna|deva|sishna-deva|sishnadeva|sishna|_vei_120@3294|_vei2_pn382
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3293">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3295">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn381">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn382">2:382</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn383">↷</a><hr><p><b>Siśna-deva,</b> occurring twice in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203294">1</a></sup> in the plural, means ‘those who have the phallus for a deity.’ The term most probably refers to the phallus worship of the aborigines. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203294"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203294">↑</a> vii. 21, 5; x. 99, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 118; Hopkins, <i>Religions of India,</i> 150; von Schroeder, <i>Vienna Oriental Journal,</i> 9, 237; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> 155; Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1911, 1002, n. 5.</li></ol></div>

शीपाल|శీపాల|ಶೀಪಾಲ|śīpāla|shiipaala|sheepaala|_vei_120@3295|_vei2_pn383
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3294">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3296">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn382">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn383">2:383</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn384">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śīpāla</b> is the name of a water plant (<i>Blyxa Octandra</i>) mentioned in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203295"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203295">1</a></sup> Its later name is Śaivala.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203295"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203295">↑</a> x. 68, 5. <i>Cf.</i> the derivative adjective <i>śīpalya,</i> ‘overgrown with <a class="vei_120a" href="शीपाल">śīpāla</a> plants,’ Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 71.</li></ol></div>

शीपाला|శీపాలా|ಶೀಪಾಲಾ|śīpālā|shiipaalaa|sheepaalaa|_vei_120@3296|_vei2_pn383
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3295">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3297">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn382">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn383">2:383</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn384">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śīpālā</b> is found once in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203296">1</a></sup> where it may mean either a ‘pool abounding in <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शीपाल">śīpāla</a></b> plants’ or the proper name of a river or lake.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203296"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203296">↑</a> vi. 12, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 71; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 289, 290; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 462.</li></ol></div>

शीपुद्रु|శీపుద్రు|ಶೀಪುದ್ರು|śīpudru|shiipudru|sheepudru|_vei_120@3297|_vei2_pn383
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3296">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3298">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn382">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn383">2:383</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn384">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śīpudru</b> is merely an incorrect reading in the text of the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203297"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203297">1</a></sup> for <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चीपुद्रु">cīpudru</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203297"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203297">↑</a> vi. 127, 1. See Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 376.</li></ol></div>

शीर्षक्ति|శీర్షక్తి|ಶೀರ್ಷಕ್ತಿ|śīrṣakti|shiirShakti|sheerShakti|_vei_120@3298|_vei2_pn383
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3297">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3299">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn382">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn383">2:383</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn384">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śīrṣakti</b> is a common word for ‘headache’ in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203298"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203298">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203298"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203298">↑</a> i. 12, 3; ix. 8, 1; xii. 2, 19; 5, 23. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, xxxv; <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 252; <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 17, 416, who sees in it <i>śīrṣasakti</i> (<i>cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> 64, 2). Bo7htlingk, Proceedings of the Saxon Academy, 1897, 50, thinks the word means ‘a stiff neck, with head awry.’ See Lanman in Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 14. In Av. xix. 39, 10, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शीर्ष">śīrṣa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="शोक">śoka</a></i> is used for ‘headache.’</li></ol></div>

शीर्षण्य|శీర్షణ్య|ಶೀರ್ಷಣ್ಯ|śīrṣaṇya|shiirShaNya|sheerShaNya|_vei_120@3299|_vei2_pn383
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3298">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3300">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn382">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn383">2:383</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn384">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śīrṣaṇya</b> in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203299"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203299">1</a></sup> denotes the ‘head’ of a couch (<b>Asandī</b>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203299"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203299">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 5, 3; 12, 3; 17, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 5 Sāṅkhāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>. xvii. 2, 8.</li></ol></div>

शीर्षामय|శీర్షామయ|ಶೀರ್ಷಾಮಯ|śīrṣāmaya|shiirShaamaya|sheerShaamaya|_vei_120@3300|_vei2_pn383
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3299">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3301">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn382">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn383">2:383</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn384">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śīrṣāmaya,</b> ‘disease of the head,’ is mentioned in the Atharvaveda (v. 4, 10; ix. 8, 1).</p></div>

शीष्ट|శీష్ట|ಶೀಷ್ಟ|śīṣṭa|shiiShTa|sheeShTa|_vei_120@3301|_vei2_pn383
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3300">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3302">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn382">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn383">2:383</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn384">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śīṣṭa</b> occurs only in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वालखिल्य">vālakhilya</a> hymn of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203301"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203301">1</a></sup> where the word seems to be the name of an unimportant clan. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203301"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203301">↑</a> viii. 53, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 163.</li></ol></div>

शुक|శుక|ಶುಕ|śuka|shuka|_vei_120@3302|_vei2_pn384
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3301">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3303">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn383">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn384">2:384</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn385">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śuka,</b> ‘parrot,’ is mentioned in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203302"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203302">1</a></sup> where a desire is expressed to transfer to the <a class="vei_120a" href="शुक">śuka</a> and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रोपणाका">ropaṇākā</a></b> the yellowness of jaundice. The bird is included in the list of sacrificial victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203302"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203302">2</a></sup> It is described as yellow and as ‘of human speech’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुष">puruṣa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वाच्">vāc</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203302"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203302">3</a></sup> According to Bloomfield,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203302"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203302">4</a></sup> this word is the correct reading for the second half of the obscure <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शारिशाका">śāriśākā</a></b> of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203302"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203302">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203302"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203302">↑</a> i. 50, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203302"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203302">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 12, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 14; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 33; and <i>cf. śukababhru,</i> ‘reddish, like a parrot,’ <i>ibid.,</i> xxiv. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203302"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203302">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> and Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitās, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203302"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203302">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 352.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203302"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203302">↑</a> iii. 14, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 90.</li></ol></div>

शुक्ति आंगिरस|शुक्ति आङ्गिरस|శుక్తి ఆంగిరస|శుక్తి ఆఙ్గిరస|ಶುಕ್ತಿ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಶುಕ್ತಿ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|śukti āṅgirasa|shukti aaMgirasa|shukti aangirasa|_vei_120@3303|_vei2_pn384
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3302">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3304">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn383">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn384">2:384</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn385">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śukti Āṅgirasa</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अंगिरस्">aṅgiras</a></b>’) is the name of the seer of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> or Chant in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xii. 5, 16).</p></div>

शुक्र|शुक्र|శుక్ర_౧|శుక్ర|ಶುಕ್ರ_೧|ಶುಕ್ರ|śukra_1|śukra|shukra_1|shukra|_vei_120@3304|_vei2_pn384
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3303">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3305">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn383">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn384">2:384</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn385">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Śukra,</b> according to Tilak,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203304"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203304">1</a></sup> has in two passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203304"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203304">2</a></sup> the sense of a planet. This is most improbable. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मन्थिन्">manthin</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203304"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203304">↑</a> <i>Orion,</i> 162.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203304"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203304">↑</a> iii. 32, 2; ix. 46, 4.</li></ol></div>

शुक्र जाबाल|शुक्र जाबाल|శుక్ర జాబాల_౨|శుక్ర జాబాల|ಶುಕ್ರ ಜಾಬಾಲ_೨|ಶುಕ್ರ ಜಾಬಾಲ|śukra jābāla_2|śukra jābāla|shukra jaabaala_2|shukra jaabaala|_vei_120@3305|_vei2_pn384
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3304">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3306">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn383">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn384">2:384</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn385">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Śukra Jābāla</b> (‘descendant of Jabālā’) is the name of a teacher in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 7, 7).</p></div>

शुक्ल|శుక్ల|ಶುಕ್ಲ|śukla|shukla|_vei_120@3306|_vei2_pn384
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3305">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3307">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn383">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn384">2:384</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn385">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śukla.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यजुस्">yajus</a>.</b></p></div>

शुक्ल-दन्त्|शुक्लदन्त्|शुक्ल|दन्त्|శుక్ల-దన్త్|శుక్లదన్త్|శుక్ల|దన్త్|ಶುಕ್ಲ-ದನ್ತ್|ಶುಕ್ಲದನ್ತ್|ಶುಕ್ಲ|ದನ್ತ್|śukla-dant|śukladant|śukla|dant|shukla-dant|shukladant|shukla|_vei_120@3307|_vei2_pn384
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3306">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3308">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn383">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn384">2:384</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn385">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śukla-dant,</b> ‘white-tusked,’ is applied as an epithet to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मृग">mṛga</a>,</b> ‘wild beast,’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 23, 3). ‘Elephants’ must be meant.</p></div>

शुच|శుచ|ಶುಚ|śuca|shucha|_vei_120@3308|_vei2_pn384
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3307">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3309">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn383">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn384">2:384</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn385">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śuca</b> and <b>Śucā</b> occur in an obscure verse of the Rigveda (x. 26, 6), where a man and a woman may be meant. </p></div>

शुचन्ति|శుచన్తి|ಶುಚನ್ತಿ|śucanti|shuchanti|_vei_120@3309|_vei2_pn385
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3308">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3310">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn384">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn385">2:385</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn386">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śucanti</b> is the name of a protégé of the Aśvins in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203309">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203309"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203309">↑</a> i. 112, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 165.</li></ol></div>

शुचि-वृक्ष गौपालायन|शुचिवृक्ष गौपालायन|शुचि|वृक्ष गौपालायन|శుచి-వృక్ష గౌపాలాయన|శుచివృక్ష గౌపాలాయన|శుచి|వృక్ష గౌపాలాయన|ಶುಚಿ-ವೃಕ್ಷ ಗೌಪಾಲಾಯನ|ಶುಚಿವೃಕ್ಷ ಗೌಪಾಲಾಯನ|ಶುಚಿ|ವೃಕ್ಷ ಗೌಪಾಲಾಯನ|śuci-vṛkṣa gaupālāyana|śucivṛkṣa gaupālāyana|śuci|vṛkṣa gaupālāyana|shuchi-vRkSha gaupaalaayana|shuchivRkSha gaupaalaayana|shuchi|vRkSha gaupaalaayana|shuchi-vRikSha gaupaalaayana|shuchivRikSha gaupaalaayana|vRikSha gaupaalaayana|shuchi-vRukSha gaupaalaayana|shuchivRukSha gaupaalaayana|vRukSha gaupaalaayana|shuchi-vRRikSha gaupaalaayana|shuchivRRikSha gaupaalaayana|vRRikSha gaupaalaayana|shuchi-vRxa gaupaalaayana|shuchivRxa gaupaalaayana|vRxa gaupaalaayana|_vei_120@3310|_vei2_pn385
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3309">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3311">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn384">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn385">2:385</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn386">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śuci-<a class="vei_120a" href="वृक्ष">vṛkṣa</a> Gaupālāyana</b> (‘descendant of Gopāla’) is the name of the priest of <b>Vṛddhadyumna Abhipratāriṇa</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203310">1</a></sup> He is also mentioned in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203310">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203310">↑</a> iii. 48, 9 (Ganpalāyana in Aufrecht's edition).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203310"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203310">↑</a> iii. 10, 4.</li></ol></div>

शुतुद्री|शुतुद्रि|శుతుద్రీ|శుతుద్రి|ಶುತುದ್ರೀ|ಶುತುದ್ರಿ|śutudrī|śutudri|shutudrii|shutudri|shutudree|_vei_120@3311|_vei2_pn385
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3310">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3312">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn384">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn385">2:385</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn386">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śutudrī,</b> twice mentioned in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203311">1</a></sup> is the name of the most easterly river of the Panjab, the modern Sutlej, the Zaradros of Ptolemy and Arrian.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203311">2</a></sup> In the post-Vedic period the name of this river appears transformed to Śatadru (‘flowing in a hundred channels’). The Sutlej has changed its course very considerably within historical times.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203311">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203311">↑</a> iii. 33, 1; x. 75, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ix. 26.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203311">↑</a> In Arrian's time the Sutlej flowed independently into the Rann of Cutch: <i>Imperial Gazetteer of India,</i> 23, 179.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203311"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203311">↑</a> <i>Ibid.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 10, 11.</li></ol></div>

शुनः-पुच्छ|शुनःपुच्छ|शुनः|पुच्छ|శునః-పుచ్ఛ|శునఃపుచ్ఛ|శునః|పుచ్ఛ|ಶುನಃ-ಪುಚ್ಛ|ಶುನಃಪುಚ್ಛ|ಶುನಃ|ಪುಚ್ಛ|śunaḥ-puccha|śunaḥpuccha|śunaḥ|puccha|shunaH-puchCha|shunaHpuchCha|shunaH|puchCha|_vei_120@3312|_vei2_pn385
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3311">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3313">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn384">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn385">2:385</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn386">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śunaḥ-puccha,</b> ‘Dog’s tail,’ is the name of a brother of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a></b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203312">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203312"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203312">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 15, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, v. 20, 1.</li></ol></div>

शुनः-शेप|शुनःशेप|शुनः|शेप|శునః-శేప|శునఃశేప|శునః|శేప|ಶುನಃ-ಶೇಪ|ಶುನಃಶೇಪ|ಶುನಃ|ಶೇಪ|śunaḥ-śepa|śunaḥśepa|śunaḥ|śepa|shunaH-shepa|shunaHshepa|shunaH|shepa|_vei_120@3313|_vei2_pn385
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3312">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3314">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn384">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn385">2:385</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn386">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śunaḥ-śepa,</b> ‘Dog’s tail,’ is the name of a man with the patronymic <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आजीगर्ति">ājīgarti</a>.</b> According to a tale told in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203313">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203313">2</a></sup> he was purchased as a victim by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रोहित">rohita</a>,</b> King <b>Hariścandra's</b> son, who had been promised by his father to <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a> as a sacrifice. He was actually bound to the stake, but was released in time through his supplications, supposed to be preserved in certain hymns of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203313">3</a></sup> He was adopted by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>,</b> to whose advice he owed the inspiration to ask the gods to release him, and became his son as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="देवरात">devarāta</a>,</b> much to the annoyance  of some of Viśvāmitra's sons, who in consequence were cursed by their father. The Rigveda, however, contains merely the statement of Śunaḥśepa's deliverance from peril of death by the divine help, and the Yajurvedas<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203313">4</a></sup> simply say that he was seized by <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a> (perhaps with dropsy),<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203313">5</a></sup> but saved himself from Varuṇa's bonds.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203313">↑</a> vii. 13-18.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203313">↑</a> xv. 20, 1 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> xvi. 11, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203313">↑</a> i. 24 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> v. 2, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203313">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 2, 1, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xix. 11. The story is not found in Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203313"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203313">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Varuna <a class="vei_120a" href="गृहीत">gṛhīta</a>.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 408 <i>et seq.;</i> 573 <i>et seq.;</i> Roth, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 457; ii. 112 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 47, 48; <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 10-16; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 355 <i>et seq.;</i> Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 207; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 146; Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1911, 988, 989.</li></ol></div>

शुनस्-कर्ण|शुनस्कर्ण|शुनस्|शुन|कर्ण|శునస్-కర్ణ|శునస్కర్ణ|శునస్|శున|కర్ణ|ಶುನಸ್-ಕರ್ಣ|ಶುನಸ್ಕರ್ಣ|ಶುನಸ್|ಶುನ|ಕರ್ಣ|śunas-karṇa|śunaskarṇa|śunas|śuna|karṇa|shunas-karNa|shunaskarNa|shunas|shuna|karNa|_vei_120@3314|_vei2_pn386
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3313">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3315">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn385">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn386">2:386</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn387">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śunas-karṇa,</b> ‘Dog-ear,’ is the name of a king,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203314"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203314">1</a></sup> son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शिबि">śibi</a></b> or of Baṣkiha,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203314"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203314">2</a></sup> who performed a certain rite, the Sarvasvāra, and so died without disease.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203314"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203314">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxi. 17; Caland, <i>Über das rituelle <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> des <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>,</i> 28.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203314"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203314">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvii. 12, 6.</li></ol></div>

सुन-होत्र|सुनहोत्र|सुन|होत्र|సున-హోత్ర|సునహోత్ర|సున|హోత్ర|ಸುನ-ಹೋತ್ರ|ಸುನಹೋತ್ರ|ಸುನ|ಹೋತ್ರ|suna-hotra|sunahotra|suna|hotra|_vei_120@3315|_vei2_pn386
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3314">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3316">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn385">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn386">2:386</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn387">↷</a><hr><p><b>Suna-hotra,</b> in the plural, denotes a family of seers in the Rigveda (ii. 18, 6; 41, 14. 17).</p></div>

शुना-सीर|शुनासीर|शुना|सीर|శునా-సీర|శునాసీర|శునా|సీర|ಶುನಾ-ಸೀರ|ಶುನಾಸೀರ|ಶುನಾ|ಸೀರ|śunā-sīra|śunāsīra|śunā|sīra|shunaa-siira|shunaasiira|shunaa|siira|shunaa-seera|shunaaseera|seera|_vei_120@3316|_vei2_pn386
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3315">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3317">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn385">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn386">2:386</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn387">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śunā-sīra,</b> in the dual, occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203316">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203316">2</a></sup> as the names of two agricultural deities, the personifications, probably, of ‘the share and the plough,’ as Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203316">3</a></sup> thinks.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203316">↑</a> iv. 57, 5. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203316">↑</a> Av. iii. 17 5; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 7, 12; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xii. 69, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203316"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203316">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> For the native explanations, see Bṛhaddevatā, v. 8 <i>et seq.,</i> with Macdonell's notes. Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 116, 117, renders <i>śunam</i> adverbially as ‘successfully.’</li></ol></div>

शुनो-लांगूल|शुनोलांगूल|शुनो|लांगूल|शुनो-लाङ्गूल|शुनोलाङ्गूल|लाङ्गूल|శునో-లాంగూల|శునోలాంగూల|శునో|లాంగూల|శునో-లాఙ్గూల|శునోలాఙ్గూల|లాఙ్గూల|ಶುನೋ-ಲಾಂಗೂಲ|ಶುನೋಲಾಂಗೂಲ|ಶುನೋ|ಲಾಂಗೂಲ|ಶುನೋ-ಲಾಙ್ಗೂಲ|ಶುನೋಲಾಙ್ಗೂಲ|ಲಾಙ್ಗೂಲ|śuno-lāṅgūla|śunolāṅgūla|śuno|lāṅgūla|shuno-laaMguula|shunolaaMguula|shuno|laaMguula|shuno-laaMgoola|shunolaaMgoola|laaMgoola|shuno-laanguula|shunolaanguula|laanguula|shuno-laangoola|shunolaangoola|laangoola|_vei_120@3317|_vei2_pn386
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3316">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3318">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn385">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn386">2:386</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn387">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śuno-lāṅgūla,</b> ‘Dog’s tail,’ is the name of a brother of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनः-शेप">śunaḥ-śepa</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203317">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203317"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203317">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 15, 7; Sāṅkhāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 20, 1.</li></ol></div>

सुम्बल|सुंबल|సుమ్బల|సుంబల|ಸುಮ್ಬಲ|ಸುಂಬಲ|sumbala|_vei_120@3318|_vei2_pn387
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3317">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3319">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn386">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn387">2:387</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn388">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sumbala</b> is found in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203318">1</a></sup> The meaning of the word is uncertain: Harisvāmi in his commentary takes it to be ‘straw’; Eggeling<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203318">2</a></sup> suggests that dried cotton fibre or pods may be meant. In any case, some substance that easily catches fire is intended.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203318">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203318">↑</a> xii. 5, 2. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203318">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 202, n. 3; comparing Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxv. 7, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203318"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203318">↑</a> Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

शुल्क|శుల్క|ಶುಲ್ಕ|śulka|shulka|_vei_120@3319|_vei2_pn387
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3318">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3320">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn386">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn387">2:387</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn388">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śulka</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203319">1</a></sup> clearly means ‘price.’ In the <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203319">2</a></sup> it denotes a ‘tax,’ a sense which is found by Muir<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203319">3</a></sup> in a passage of the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203319">4</a></sup> where <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शुक्ल">śukla</a></i> is read in the edition with great detriment to the sense. This correction is accepted by Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203319">5</a></sup> and by Whitney.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203319">6</a></sup> In another passage the same change made by Weber<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203319">7</a></sup> is not accepted by Whitney,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203319">8</a></sup> and doubtfully by Bloomfield.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203319">9</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203319">↑</a> vii. 82, 6; viii. 1. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203319">↑</a> See Foy, <i>Die Ro7nigliche Gewalt,</i> 39 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203319">↑</a> <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 310.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203319">↑</a> iii. 29, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203319">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvida,</i> 434.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203319">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 136.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203319">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 304.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203319">↑</a> <i>Op, <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i> 253.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203319"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203319">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i> <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 413.</li></ol></div>

शुशुक|శుశుక|ಶುಶುಕ|śuśuka|shushuka|_vei_120@3320|_vei2_pn387
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3319">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3321">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn386">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn387">2:387</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn388">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śuśuka.</b> See <b>Aśuṃga</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शिशुक">śiśuka</a>.</b></p></div>

शुशुलूक|శుశులూక|ಶುಶುಲೂಕ|śuśulūka|shushuluuka|shushulooka|_vei_120@3321|_vei2_pn387
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3320">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3322">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn386">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn387">2:387</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn388">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śuśulūka</b> is found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203321">1</a></sup> in the compound <i>śuśulūkayātu,</i> the name of a demon. According to Sāyaṇa, the word means a ‘small owl.’ It appears in the feminine form, Śuśulūkā, in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203321">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203321">↑</a> vii. 104, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203321"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203321">↑</a> iii. 14, 17. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 93.</li></ol></div>

शुष्क-भृंगार|शुष्कभृंगार|शुष्क|भृंगार|शुष्क-भृङ्गार|शुष्कभृङ्गार|भृङ्गार|శుష్క-భృంగార|శుష్కభృంగార|శుష్క|భృంగార|శుష్క-భృఙ్గార|శుష్కభృఙ్గార|భృఙ్గార|ಶುಷ್ಕ-ಭೃಂಗಾರ|ಶುಷ್ಕಭೃಂಗಾರ|ಶುಷ್ಕ|ಭೃಂಗಾರ|ಶುಷ್ಕ-ಭೃಙ್ಗಾರ|ಶುಷ್ಕಭೃಙ್ಗಾರ|ಭೃಙ್ಗಾರ|śuṣka-bhṛṅgāra|śuṣkabhṛṅgāra|śuṣka|bhṛṅgāra|shuShka-bhRMgaara|shuShkabhRMgaara|shuShka|bhRMgaara|shuShka-bhRngaara|shuShkabhRngaara|bhRngaara|shuShka-bhRiMgaara|shuShkabhRiMgaara|bhRiMgaara|shuShka-bhRuMgaara|shuShkabhRuMgaara|bhRuMgaara|shuShka-bhRRiMgaara|shuShkabhRRiMgaara|bhRRiMgaara|shuShka-bhRingaara|shuShkabhRingaara|bhRingaara|shuShka-bhRungaara|shuShkabhRungaara|bhRungaara|shuShka-bhRRingaara|shuShkabhRRingaara|bhRRingaara|_vei_120@3322|_vei2_pn387
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3321">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3323">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn386">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn387">2:387</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn388">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śuṣka-bhṛṅgāra</b> is the name of a teacher in the Kauṣitaki <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203322">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203322"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203322">↑</a> ii. 6. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,</b> xvii. 7, 13.</li></ol></div>

सुश्मिण|సుశ్మిణ|ಸುಶ್ಮಿಣ|suśmiṇa|sushmiNa|_vei_120@3323|_vei2_pn387
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3322">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3324">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn386">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn387">2:387</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn388">↷</a><hr><p><b>Suśmiṇa</b> is a name of <b>Amitratapana,</b> a king of the <b>Śibis,</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 23, 10). </p></div>

शूद्र|శూద్ర|ಶೂದ್ರ|śūdra|shuudra|shoodra|_vei_120@3324|_vei2_pn388
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3323">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3325">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn387">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn388">2:388</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn392">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śūdra</b> is the designation of the fourth caste in the Vedic state (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a></b>). It is quite unknown in the Rigveda except in the Puruṣasūkta<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203324">1</a></sup> (‘hymn of man’) in the tenth Maṇḍla, where is the earliest version of the origin of the castes the Śudra for the first time appears. The Rigveda, on the other hand, knows <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दस्यु">dasyu</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a>,</b> both as aborigines independent of Āryan control and as subjugated slaves: it is reasonable to reckon the <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> of the later texts as belonging to the aborigines who had been reduced to subjection by the Āryans. Strictly speaking, the defeated aborigines must have been regarded as slaves, but it is obvious that, except on occasions when most of the men were slain, which may have occurred quite often, there must have remained too many of them to be used as slaves of individual owners. The villages of the aborigines must have continued to subsist, but under Āryan lordship and control: there may be this amount of truth in Baden Powell's theory, which practically traced all the early cultivating villages in India to Dravidian origin. On the other hand, the term <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> would also cover the wild hill tribes which lived by hunting and fishing, and many of which would acknowledge the superiority of their Āryan neighbours: it could, in fact, be applied to all beyond the pale of the Āryan state.</p><p>This view of the <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> suits adequately the Vedic references to his condition, which would not apply adequately to domestic slaves only. The <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> is continually opposed to the Āryan,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203324">2</a></sup> and the colour of the <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> is compared with that of the Āryan,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203324">3</a></sup> just as his ways are so contrasted.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203324">4</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203324">5</a></sup> in its account of the castes, declares that the  <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> is <i>anyasya <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रेष्य">preṣya</a>,</i> ‘the servant of another’; <i>kāmotthāpya,</i> ‘to be expelled at will’; and <i>yathākāmavadhya,</i> ‘to be slain at will.’ All these terms well enough describe the position of the serf as the result of a conquest: the epithets might have been applied to the English serf after the Norman Conquest with but slight inaccuracy, especially if his master had received a grant of jurisdiction from the Crown. The <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203324">6</a></sup> explains that even if prosperous (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="बहु">bahu</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पशु">paśu</a>,</i> ‘having many cows’) a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> could not be other than a servant: his business was <i>pādāvanejya,</i> ‘the washing of the feet’ of his superiors. The Mahābhārata<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203324">7</a></sup> says out and out that a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> has no property (<i>na hi svam asti śūdrasya,</i> ‘the <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> has nothing he can call his own’). On the other hand, just as in England the royal justice would protect the serf in life and limb,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203324">8</a></sup> so it appears that the slaying of a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> involved a wergeld of ten cows according to both <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203324">9</a></sup> and Āpastamba.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203324">10</a></sup> It may, indeed, be held that this wergeld was only due in case of murder by another than the master, but such limitation is nowhere stated.</p><p>In sacred matters the distinction between Āryan and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a></b> was, of course, specially marked. The texts<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203324">11</a></sup> do not hesitate to declare that the upper castes were ‘all,’ ignoring the Śūdras; the <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> is prohibited<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1203324">12</a></sup> from milking the cow for the milk required at the Agnihotra (‘oblation to Agni’); and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1203324">13</a></sup> forbids a man who has been consecrated (<i>dīkṣita</i>) for a sacrifice to speak to a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> at all for the time, though the Śāṭyāyanaka<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1203324">14</a></sup> seems to have relaxed this rule by confining it to cases in which the <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> was guilty of some sin.  At the sacrifice itself the <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> could not be present in the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शाला">śālā</a>,</i> ‘hall’; he is definitely classed in the Śatapatha Brāhmáṇa<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1203324">15</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1203324">16</a></sup> as unfit for ‘sacrifice’ (<i>ayajñiya</i>); and declared in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1203324">17</a></sup> not to be admitted to drink <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>. At the Pravargya (introductory <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>) rite the performer is not allowed to come in contact with a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1203324">18</a></sup> who here, as in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1203324">17</a></sup> is reckoned as excluded from a share in the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>-draught. On the other hand, the <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> is one of the victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>,<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1203324">19</a></sup> and a fight between an Āryan and a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>, in which, of course, the former wins, forms a part of the Mahāvrata rite, being perhaps a precursor of the Indian drama.<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1203324">20</a></sup></p><p>Other indications, however, exist, showing that it would be undesirable to ignore the real importance of the <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>, which again reminds us of the condition of the serf, who, though legally restrained, still gradually won his way to the rank of a free man. Rich Śūdras are mentioned in the early texts,<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1203324">21</a></sup> just as <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> <i>gahapatis,</i> ‘householders,’ occur in the Buddhist texts, and <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> kings in the legal literature.<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1203324">22</a></sup> Sin against <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> and Āryan is mentioned;<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1203324">23</a></sup> prayers for glory on behalf of Śūdras, as well as of the other castes<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1203324">24</a></sup> occur; and the desire to be dear to <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> as well as to Āryan is expressed.<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1203324">25</a></sup> </p><p>The Sūtras also, while they emphasize as general rules points earlier not insisted on, such as their inferiority in sitting, etc.,<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_1203324">26</a></sup> their exclusion from the study of the Vedas,<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_1203324">27</a></sup> the danger of contact with them<sup id="ref_120_28_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_1203324">28</a></sup> or their food,<sup id="ref_120_29_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29_vei_1203324">29</a></sup> still recognize that Śūdras can be merchants,<sup id="ref_120_30_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_30_vei_1203324">30</a></sup> or even exercise any trade.<sup id="ref_120_31_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_31_vei_1203324">31</a></sup></p><p>Moreover, the Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_32_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_32_vei_1203324">32</a></sup> permit the marriage of a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्रा">śūdrā</a> woman with members of all castes. Though it was a reproach to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वत्स">vatsa</a></b><sup id="ref_120_33_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_33_vei_1203324">33</a></sup> and to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कवष">kavaṣa</a></b><sup id="ref_120_34_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_34_vei_1203324">34</a></sup> that they were the sons of a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्रा">śūdrā</a> and a Dāsī respectively, still the possibility of such a reproach shows that marriages of this kind did take place. Moreover, illicit unions of <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्रा">śūdrā</a>, or <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> and Āryā, are referred to in the Saṃhitās of the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_35_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_35_vei_1203324">35</a></sup></p><p>The origin of the term <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> is quite obscure, but Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_36_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_36_vei_1203324">36</a></sup> points out that Ptolemy<sup id="ref_120_37_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_37_vei_1203324">37</a></sup> mentions  as a people, and he thinks that the Brāhui may be meant. Without laying any stress on this identification,<sup id="ref_120_38_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_38_vei_1203324">38</a></sup> it is reasonable to accept the  view<sup id="ref_120_39_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_39_vei_1203324">39</a></sup> that the term was originally the name of a large tribe opposed to the Āryan invasion. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="निषाद">niṣāda</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203324">↑</a> x. 90, 12. See Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 8 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203324">↑</a> Av. iv. 20, 4; xix. 32, 8; 62, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiv. 30; xxiii. 30. 31; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 10, 2; vii. 4, 19, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, iv. 7; xvii. 5; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 8, 6; iii. 13, 1, etc. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अर्य">arya</a>.</b> In <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 3, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xx. 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxviii. 5, <b>Sūdra</b> is opposed to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अर्य">arya</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203324">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxiv. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 17. <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 4, 4, 9; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 4, 25; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 4, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 6, 7; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 4; Muir, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1^2, 140; Mahābhārata, xii. 188, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203324">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 17, 3. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 24.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203324">↑</a> vii. 29, 4; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 439.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203324">↑</a> vi. 1. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203324">↑</a> xii. 30, 7 (Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 73). The same text, xii. 294, 21 (<i>ibid.,</i> 74, n.). insists on his duty of service.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203324">↑</a> Pollock and Maitland, <i>History of English Law,</i> 1, 350, 355, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203324">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 10, 19, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203324">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 9, 24, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203324">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 4, 2; iv. 2, 2, 14, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, xvi. <i>et seq.;</i> 26, 292. <i>Cf.</i> <b>Hopkins,</b> <i>op, <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 13, 73, 75, n.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1203324">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxi. 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 1, 3. So the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्थाली">sthālī</a>,</i> ‘cooking vessel,’ is to be prepared by an Āryan, Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 8, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1203324">↑</a> iii. 1, 1. 10. <i>Cf.</i> v. 3, 2, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1203324">↑</a> Quoted by Āpastamba, cited in the scholiast on Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, vii. 5, 7. The sense is not quite certain, but that given in the text seems reasonable. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 10, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1203324">↑</a> iii. 1, 1, 10. See also Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, vii. 1, 1, 6; Lévi <i>La Doctrine du Sacrifice,</i> 82.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1203324">↑</a> vi. 1, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1203324">↑</a> xi. 10, where he therefore does not receive <b>Karīras.</b></li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1203324">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 1, 1, 34.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1203324">↑</a> xi. 10, where he therefore does not receive <b>Karīras.</b></li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1203324">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 1, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 6, 2, 10. He is also present at the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>,</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1203324">↑</a> Keith, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 64, 534.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1203324">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 7, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 1, 11. Some of the kings' ministers were Śūdras; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 2, 2, with Sāyaṇa's note.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1203324">↑</a> Foy, <i>Die ko7nigliche Gewalt,</i> 8; Fick, <i>Die sociale Gliederung,</i> 83, 84. See <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>, iv. 61; Viṣṇu, lxxi. 64; perhaps Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 5. But see Roth's emendation, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, ccxliii.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1203324">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhiṭā, xxxviii. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 3, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xx. 17.</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1203324">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 7, 6, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xl. 13; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 48, On the other hand, the <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> uses magic just as an <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a> does, Av. x. 1, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1203324">↑</a> Av. xix. 32, 8; 62, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxvi. 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_1203324">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 7; Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ii. 10, 27, 15, So he can be insulted with impunity, <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a>, xii. 13, and is punished for insult by mutilation, <i>ibid.,</i> xii. 1; Āpastamba, ii. 10, 27, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_1203324">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a>, xii. 4-6.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_1203324">↑</a> Āpastamba, i. 5, 17, 1; ii. 2, 3, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_29_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29_vei_1203324">↑</a> Āpastamba, i. 5, 16, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_30_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_30_vei_1203324">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a>, x. 60. <i>Cf.</i> x. 50-67 for an exhaustive account of the Śūdra's duties in theory. His relations to his master are those of mutual support.</li><li id="fx_120_31_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_31_vei_1203324">↑</a> Viṣṇu, ii. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_32_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_32_vei_1203324">↑</a> Pāraskara <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 4, 11. Rules to the contrary (<i>e.g.,</i> Gobhila <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 2, 52) are for special occasions. See Weber, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 10, 74. On the other hand, criminal intercourse of a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> and an Āryan woman is severely punished in the Sūtras. See Āpastamba, i. 10, 26, 20; 27, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a>, xii. 2. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_33_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_33_vei_1203324">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 6, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_34_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_34_vei_1203324">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 19, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_35_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_35_vei_1203324">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्य">ārya</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्रा">śūdrā</a>: Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 30; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 4, 19, 3; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 13, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, iv. 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> and Āryā: Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxii. 31. This verse the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> no doubt deliberately ignores.</li><li id="fx_120_36_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_36_vei_1203324">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 216, 435.</li><li id="fx_120_37_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_37_vei_1203324">↑</a> vi. 20.</li><li id="fx_120_38_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_38_vei_1203324">↑</a> The Brāhui are now held not to be Dravidian ethnologically, but TurcoIranian (<i>Indian Empire,</i> 1, 292, 310). It is suggested (<i>ibid.,</i> 1, 382) that they represent the original Dravidian type, which in India has been merged in the Muṇḍā type; but this suggestion is invalidated by the fact that the Rigveda speaks of the Dasyus as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अनास्">anās</a>,</i> ‘noseless’ (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दस्यु">dasyu</a>,</b> 1, 347, n. 7). a term admirably applicable to Dravidians, but ludicrous as applied to the TurcoIranian type. It is much more plausible to assume that the Brāhuis are a mixed race, which in course of time has lost most of its Dravidian features. On the relation of Dravidians and speakers of Muṇḍā tongues, the Vedic texts throw no light.</li><li id="fx_120_39_vei_1203324"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_39_vei_1203324">↑</a> See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 85, 255; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda. 3, 212; Fick, <i>Die sociale Gliederung,</i> 201, 202.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 154, 155; Jolly, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 50, 515; Fick, <i>Die sociale Gliederung,</i> 201 <i>et seq.,</i> Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India,</i> 54; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 73 <i>et seq.</i> (for the <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> in the Epic); Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 191, <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 18, 77, 111, 112, 276; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 4 <i>et seq.;</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 8 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

शूद्रा|శూద్రా|ಶೂದ್ರಾ|śūdrā|shuudraa|shoodraa|_vei_120@3325|_vei2_pn392
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3324">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3326">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn388">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn392">2:392</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn393">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śūdrā</b> denoted a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> woman in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203325">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203325">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203325">↑</a> v. 22. 7 (= Dāsī, v. 22, 6).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203325"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203325">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 4, 19, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, iv. 8; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 13, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 30, etc.; <i>śūdrāputra,</i> ‘son of a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> woman,’ <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 6, 6.</li></ol></div>

शूर|శూర|ಶೂರ|śūra|shuura|shoora|_vei_120@3326|_vei2_pn392
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3325">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3327">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn388">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn392">2:392</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn393">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śūra</b> is the regular word in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203326"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203326">1</a></sup> and occasionally later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203326"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203326">2</a></sup> to denote a ‘hero’ or ‘brave warrior.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203326"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203326">↑</a> i. 70, 11; 101, 6; 141, 8; 158, 3; ii. 17, 2; 30, 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203326"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203326">↑</a> Av. viii. 8, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 34; xx. 37, etc. (of gods, <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>); <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शूर">śūra</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वीर">vīra</a>,</i> Av. viii. 5, 1.</li></ol></div>

शूर-वीर माण्डूक्य|शूरवीर माण्डूक्य|शूर|वीर माण्डूक्य|శూర-వీర మాణ్డూక్య|శూరవీర మాణ్డూక్య|శూర|వీర మాణ్డూక్య|ಶೂರ-ವೀರ ಮಾಣ್ಡೂಕ್ಯ|ಶೂರವೀರ ಮಾಣ್ಡೂಕ್ಯ|ಶೂರ|ವೀರ ಮಾಣ್ಡೂಕ್ಯ|śūra-vīra māṇḍūkya|śūravīra māṇḍūkya|śūra|vīra māṇḍūkya|shuura-viira maaNDuukya|shuuraviira maaNDuukya|shuura|viira maaNDuukya|shoora-veera maaNDookya|shooraveera maaNDookya|shoora|veera maaNDookya|_vei_120@3327|_vei2_pn392
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3326">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3328">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn388">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn392">2:392</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn393">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śūra-<a class="vei_120a" href="वीर">vīra</a> Māṇḍūkya</b> (‘descendant of Maṇḍūka’) is the name of a teacher in the Āraṇyakas of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203327">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203327"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203327">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 1, 1, 3, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, vii. 2. 8. 9. 10 (where the name is read Śauravīra).</li></ol></div>

शूर्प|శూర్ప|ಶೂರ್ಪ|śūrpa|shuurpa|shoorpa|_vei_120@3328|_vei2_pn392
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3327">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3329">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn388">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn392">2:392</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn393">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śūrpa</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203328">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203328">2</a></sup> denotes a wickerwork basket for winnowing grain. It is called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ष">varṣa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वृद्ध">vṛddha</a>,</i> ‘swollen by rain,’ in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203328">3</a></sup> which shows, as Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203328">4</a></sup> says, that it was sometimes made of reeds, not of dry wood. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203328">↑</a> ix. 6, 16; x. 9, 26; xi. 3, 4; xii. 3, 19 <i>et seq.;</i> xx. 136, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203328">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 5, 4; iii. 2, 5, 11. etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203328">↑</a> xii. 3, 19.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203328"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203328">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 238.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Lanman in Whitney's Translation of the Atharvaveda, 686; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharavaveda,</i> 649.</li></ol></div>

शूल|శూల|ಶೂಲ|śūla|shuula|shoola|_vei_120@3329|_vei2_pn393
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3328">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3330">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn392">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn393">2:393</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn394">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śūla,</b> denoting the ‘spit,’ used for roasting flesh on, is found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203329"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203329">1</a></sup> and the later Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203329"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203329">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203329"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203329">↑</a> i. 162, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203329"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203329">↑</a> Śatapatha Brāhmaṇ, xi. 4, 2, 4; 7, 3, 2; 4, 3; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vii. 15, 3 (used at cremation and sugrestive of roasting). The <a class="vei_120a" href="शूल">śūla</a>, as the weapon of <a class="vei_120a" href="रुद्र">rudra</a>, is not mentioned till the late Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 11. In the post-Vedic language the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रि">tri</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="शूल">śūla</a>,</i> or ‘trident,’ is the regular emblem of <a class="vei_120a" href="शिव">śiva</a>.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 271.</li></ol></div>

सुष वार्ष्ण|सुष वार्ष्ण|సుష వార్ష్ణ_౧|సుష వార్ష్ణ|ಸುಷ ವಾರ್ಷ್ಣ_೧|ಸುಷ ವಾರ್ಷ್ಣ|suṣa vārṣṇa_1|suṣa vārṣṇa|suSha vaarShNa_1|suSha vaarShNa|_vei_120@3330|_vei2_pn393
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3329">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3331">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn392">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn393">2:393</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn394">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Suṣa Vārṣṇa</b> (‘descendant of Vṛṣṇi’) is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Brāhmaṇ (iii. 10, 9, 15) as having been honoured by a consecration with Āditya.</p></div>

शूष वाह्नेय|शूष वाह्नेय|శూష వాహ్నేయ_౨|శూష వాహ్నేయ|ಶೂಷ ವಾಹ್ನೇಯ_೨|ಶೂಷ ವಾಹ್ನೇಯ|śūṣa vāhneya_2|śūṣa vāhneya|shuuSha vaahneya_2|shuuSha vaahneya|shooSha vaahneya_2|shooSha vaahneya|_vei_120@3331|_vei2_pn393
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3330">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3332">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn392">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn393">2:393</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn394">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Śūṣa Vāhneya</b> (‘descendant of Vahni’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भारद्वाज">bhāradvāja</a></b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a></b>’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of Arāḍa <a class="vei_120a" href="दात्रेय">dātreya</a> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शौनक">śaunaka</a>,</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203331"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203331">1</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b>Śruṣa.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203331"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203331">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373.</li></ol></div>

शृंग|शृङ्ग|శృంగ|శృఙ్గ|ಶೃಂಗ|ಶೃಙ್ಗ|śṛṅga|shRMga|shRnga|shRiMga|shRuMga|shRRiMga|shRinga|shRunga|shRRinga|_vei_120@3332|_vei2_pn393
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3331">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3333">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn392">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn393">2:393</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn394">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śṛṅga</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203332"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203332">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203332"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203332">2</a></sup> denotes the ‘horn’ of any sort of animal. Hence the ‘barb’ of the arrow is called its horn in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203332"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203332">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203332"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203332">↑</a> i. 140, 6; 163, 11; ii. 39, 3; iii. 8. 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203332"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203332">↑</a> Av. ii. 32, 6; viii. 6, 14; ix. 4, 17, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203332"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203332">↑</a> iv. 6, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 154.</li></ol></div>

शृंग-वृष्|शृंगवृष्|शृंग|वृष्|शृङ्ग-वृष्|शृङ्गवृष्|शृङ्ग|శృంగ-వృష్|శృంగవృష్|శృంగ|వృష్|శృఙ్గ-వృష్|శృఙ్గవృష్|శృఙ్గ|ಶೃಂಗ-ವೃಷ್|ಶೃಂಗವೃಷ್|ಶೃಂಗ|ವೃಷ್|ಶೃಙ್ಗ-ವೃಷ್|ಶೃಙ್ಗವೃಷ್|ಶೃಙ್ಗ|śṛṅga-vṛṣ|śṛṅgavṛṣ|śṛṅga|vṛṣ|shRMga-vRSh|shRMgavRSh|shRMga|vRSh|shRnga-vRSh|shRngavRSh|shRnga|shRiMga-vRiSh|shRiMgavRiSh|shRiMga|vRiSh|shRuMga-vRuSh|shRuMgavRuSh|shRuMga|vRuSh|shRRiMga-vRRiSh|shRRiMgavRRiSh|shRRiMga|vRRiSh|shRinga-vRiSh|shRingavRiSh|shRinga|shRunga-vRuSh|shRungavRuSh|shRunga|shRRinga-vRRiSh|shRRingavRRiSh|shRRinga|_vei_120@3333|_vei2_pn393
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3332">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3334">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn392">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn393">2:393</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn394">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śṛṅga-vṛṣ</b> is the name of a man in one hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203333"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203333">1</a></sup> According to Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203333"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203333">2</a></sup> he is father of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पृदाकुसानु">pṛdākusānu</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203333"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203333">↑</a> viii. 17, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203333"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203333">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 161.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 2, 142, n.</li></ol></div>

शेरभ|శేరభ|ಶೇರಭ|śerabha|sherabha|_vei_120@3334|_vei2_pn393
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3333">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3335">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn392">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn393">2:393</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn394">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śerabha</b> and <b>Śerabhaka</b> are names of snakes or demons in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203334"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203334">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203334"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203334">↑</a> ii. 24, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 64.</li></ol></div>

शेव-धि|शेवधि|शेव|धि|శేవ-ధి|శేవధి|శేవ|ధి|ಶೇವ-ಧಿ|ಶೇವಧಿ|ಶೇವ|ಧಿ|śeva-dhi|śevadhi|śeva|dhi|sheva-dhi|shevadhi|sheva|_vei_120@3335|_vei2_pn393
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3334">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3336">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn392">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn393">2:393</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn394">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śeva-dhi</b> denotes ‘treasure’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203335"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203335">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203335"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203335">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203335"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203335">↑</a> ii. 13, 6; vii. 53, 5; ix. 3, 15 (metaphorically). <i>Cf.</i> viii. 52, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203335"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203335">↑</a> Av. v. 22, 14; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 59, etc.</li></ol></div>

शेवृध|శేవృధ|ಶೇವೃಧ|śevṛdha|shevRdha|shevRidha|shevRudha|shevRRidha|_vei_120@3336|_vei2_pn394
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3335">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3337">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn393">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn394">2:394</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn395">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śevṛdha</b> and <b>Śevṛdhaka</b> are the names of snakes or demons in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203336"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203336">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203336"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203336">↑</a> ii. 24, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney Translation of the Atharvaveda, 64.</li></ol></div>

शेषण|శేషణ|ಶೇಷಣ|śeṣaṇa|sheShaNa|_vei_120@3337|_vei2_pn394
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3336">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3338">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn393">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn394">2:394</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn395">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śeṣaṇa</b> in the Atharvaveda (vii. 109, 5), means the ‘leaving’ of the dice as opposed to Glahana (<i>grahaṇa</i>), the ‘taking up’ of them for the throw. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्लह">glaha</a>.</b></p></div>

शेषस्|शेष|శేషస్|శేష|ಶೇಷಸ್|ಶೇಷ|śeṣas|śeṣa|sheShas|sheSha|_vei_120@3338|_vei2_pn394
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3337">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3339">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn393">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn394">2:394</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn395">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śeṣas</b> denotes ‘offspring’ in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203338"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203338">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203338"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203338">↑</a> i. 93, 4; v. 12, 6; 70, 4; vi. 27, 4. 5; vii. 1, 12; 4, 7; x. 16, 5.</li></ol></div>

शैब्य|శైబ్య|ಶೈಬ್ಯ|śaibya|shaibya|_vei_120@3339|_vei2_pn394
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3338">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3340">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn393">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn394">2:394</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn395">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaibya,</b> ‘belonging to the <b>Śibis,</b>’ is a designation of king Amitratapana Śuṣmiṇa in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 23, 10). In the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रश्न">praśna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (i. 1; v. 1) <a class="vei_120a" href="शैब्य">śaibya</a> is the patronymic of a teacher, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सत्यकाम">satyakāma</a>.</b></p></div>

शैलन|శైలన|ಶೈಲನ|śailana|shailana|_vei_120@3340|_vei2_pn394
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3339">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3341">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn393">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn394">2:394</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn395">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śailana,</b> in the plural, is the name of a school of teachers in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 2, 3; ii. 4, 6).</p></div>

सैलालि|సైలాలి|ಸೈಲಾಲಿ|sailāli|sailaali|_vei_120@3341|_vei2_pn394
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3340">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3342">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn393">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn394">2:394</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn395">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sailāli,</b> ‘descendant of Śilālin,’ is the name of a ritual teacher in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203341"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203341">1</a></sup> A Śailāli <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> is mentioned in the Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203341"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203341">2</a></sup> and the school of the Śailālins often occurs in the Śūtras.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203341"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203341">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203341"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203341">↑</a> xiii. 5, 3, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203341"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203341">↑</a> vi. 4, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203341"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203341">↑</a> Anupada <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 5, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 156; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 197. who compares the Naṭa <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> attributed to Śilālin by Pāṇni, iv. 2, 110, 111.</li></ol></div>

शैलिन|शैलिनि|శైలిన|శైలిని|ಶೈಲಿನ|ಶೈಲಿನಿ|śailina|śailini|shailina|shailini|_vei_120@3342|_vei2_pn394
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3341">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3343">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn393">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn394">2:394</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn395">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śailina</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शैलिनि">śailini</a>,</b> ‘descendant of Śilina,’ is the patronymic of <b>Jitvan</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203342">1</a></sup> Perhaps <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शैलन">śailana</a></b> should be compared. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203342"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203342">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शैलिन">śailina</a> in Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1, 5 Mādhyaṃdina: <a class="vei_120a" href="शैलिनि">śailini</a>. iv. 1, 2 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>. <i>Cf.</i> Max Mūller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 15, 152, n. 2.</li></ol></div>

शैलूष|శైలూష|ಶೈಲೂಷ|śailūṣa|shailuuSha|shailooSha|_vei_120@3343|_vei2_pn395
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3342">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3344">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn394">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn395">2:395</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn396">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śailūṣa</b> is included in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203343"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203343">1</a></sup> An ‘actor’ or ‘dancer’ may be meant. Sāyaṇa says it is a man who lives on the prostitution of his wife.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203343"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203343">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 2, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 290; Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 111, 196, 197. The exact sense of <a class="vei_120a" href="शैलूष">śailūṣa</a> depends on the question of how old the drama is in India. As to this, <i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a>;</b> Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1911, 995 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

शोण सात्रासाह|శోణ సాత్రాసాహ|ಶೋಣ ಸಾತ್ರಾಸಾಹ|śoṇa sātrāsāha|shoNa saatraasaaha|_vei_120@3344|_vei2_pn395
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3343">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3345">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn394">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn395">2:395</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn396">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śoṇa Sātrāsāha,</b> king of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a></b> and father of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कोक">koka</a>,</b> is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203344"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203344">1</a></sup> as having offered the horse sacrifice, which was attended by the <b>Turvaśas</b> also.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203344"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203344">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 16-18. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 400.</li></ol></div>

शौंगायनि|शौङ्गायनि|శౌంగాయని|శౌఙ్గాయని|ಶೌಂಗಾಯನಿ|ಶೌಙ್ಗಾಯನಿ|śauṅgāyani|shauMgaayani|shaungaayani|_vei_120@3345|_vei2_pn395
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3344">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3346">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn394">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn395">2:395</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn396">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śauṅgāyani,</b> ‘descendant of Śauṅga,’ is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203345">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203345"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203345">↑</a> Indische Studien, 4, 372, 383. The Śuṅgas are known as teachers in the Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 13, 5. etc.</li></ol></div>

शौंगी-पुत्र|शौंगीपुत्र|शौंगी|शौंगि|पुत्र|शौङ्गी-पुत्र|शौङ्गीपुत्र|शौङ्गी|शौङ्गि|శౌంగీ-పుత్ర|శౌంగీపుత్ర|శౌంగీ|శౌంగి|పుత్ర|శౌఙ్గీ-పుత్ర|శౌఙ్గీపుత్ర|శౌఙ్గీ|శౌఙ్గి|ಶೌಂಗೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಶೌಂಗೀಪುತ್ರ|ಶೌಂಗೀ|ಶೌಂಗಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|ಶೌಙ್ಗೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಶೌಙ್ಗೀಪುತ್ರ|ಶೌಙ್ಗೀ|ಶೌಙ್ಗಿ|śauṅgī-putra|śauṅgīputra|śauṅgī|śauṅgi|putra|shauMgii-putra|shauMgiiputra|shauMgii|shauMgi|shauMgee-putra|shauMgeeputra|shauMgee|shaungii-putra|shaungiiputra|shaungii|shaungi|shaungee-putra|shaungeeputra|shaungee|_vei_120@3346|_vei2_pn395
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3345">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3347">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn394">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn395">2:395</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn396">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śauṅgī-putra,</b> ‘son of a female descendant of Śuṅga,’ is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सांकृती-पुत्र">sāṃkṛtī-putra</a></b> in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203346"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203346">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203346"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203346">↑</a> vi. 4, 31 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 5, 2 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

शौच|శౌచ|ಶೌಚ|śauca|shaucha|_vei_120@3347|_vei2_pn395
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3346">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3348">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn394">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn395">2:395</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn396">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śauca</b> (‘descendant of Śuci’) is the patronymic of a man, called also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आह्नेय">āhneya</a>,</b> who is mentioned as a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka (ii. 12).</p></div>

शौचद्-रथ|शौचद्रथ|शौचद्|रथ|శౌచద్-రథ|శౌచద్రథ|శౌచద్|రథ|ಶೌಚದ್-ರಥ|ಶೌಚದ್ರಥ|ಶೌಚದ್|ರಥ|śaucad-ratha|śaucadratha|śaucad|ratha|shauchad-ratha|shauchadratha|shauchad|_vei_120@3348|_vei2_pn395
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3347">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3349">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn394">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn395">2:395</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn396">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaucad-ratha</b> (‘descendant of Śucad-ratha’) is the patronymic of <b>Sunītha</b> in the Rigveda (v. 79, 2).</p></div>

शौचेय|శౌచేయ|ಶೌಚೇಯ|śauceya|shaucheya|_vei_120@3349|_vei2_pn395
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3348">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3350">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn394">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn395">2:395</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn396">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śauceya</b> (‘descendant of Śuci’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राचीनयोग्य">prācīnayogya</a></b> (‘descendant of Prācīnayoga’) is the name of a teacher in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xi. 5, 3, 1. 8). <a class="vei_120a" href="शौचेय">śauceya</a> is also the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सार्वसेनि">sārvaseni</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (vii. 1, 10, 2). </p></div>

शौनक|శౌనక|ಶೌನಕ|śaunaka|shaunaka|_vei_120@3350|_vei2_pn396
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3349">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3351">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn395">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn396">2:396</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn397">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaunaka,</b> ‘descendant of Śunaka,’ is a common patronymic. It is applied to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्रोत">indrota</a></b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203350">1</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्वैदायन">svaidāyana</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203350">2</a></sup> A <a class="vei_120a" href="शौनक">śaunaka</a> appears as a teacher of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रौहिणायन">rauhiṇāyana</a></b> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203350">3</a></sup> A <a class="vei_120a" href="शौनक">śaunaka</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="यज्ञ">yajña</a>, or <a class="vei_120a" href="शौनक">śaunaka</a> sacrifice, occurs in the Kauṣitaki <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203350">4</a></sup> In the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203350">5</a></sup> <b>Atidhanvan</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="शौनक">śaunaka</a> appears as a teacher. That <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203350">6</a></sup> and the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203350">7</a></sup> mention a <a class="vei_120a" href="शौनक">śaunaka</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कापेय">kāpeya</a> who was a contemporary of <b>Abhipratārin <a class="vei_120a" href="काक्षसेनि">kākṣaseni</a>,</b> whose <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शौनक">śaunaka</a> was according to another passage<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203350">8</a></sup> of the latter <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>. In the Sūtras, the Bṛhaddevatā, etc., a <a class="vei_120a" href="शौनक">śaunaka</a> appears as a great authority on grammatical, ritual, and other matters.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203350">9</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203350">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 3, 5; 4, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203350">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> xi. 4, 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203350">↑</a> ii. 5, 20; iv. 5, 26 Mādhyaṃdina.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203350">↑</a> iv. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203350">↑</a> i. 9, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203350">↑</a> iv. 3, 5. 7.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203350">↑</a> iii. 1, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203350">↑</a> i. 59, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203350"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203350">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 24, 32-34, 49, 54, 56, 59, 62, 85, 143; Macdonell, <i>Bṛhaddevatā,</i> 1, xxiii; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 18, 19, 297.</li></ol></div>

शौनकी-पुत्र|शौनकीपुत्र|शौनकी|शौनकि|पुत्र|శౌనకీ-పుత్ర|శౌనకీపుత్ర|శౌనకీ|శౌనకి|పుత్ర|ಶೌನಕೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಶೌನಕೀಪುತ್ರ|ಶೌನಕೀ|ಶೌನಕಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|śaunakī-putra|śaunakīputra|śaunakī|śaunaki|putra|shaunakii-putra|shaunakiiputra|shaunakii|shaunaki|shaunakee-putra|shaunakeeputra|shaunakee|_vei_120@3351|_vei2_pn396
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3350">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3352">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn395">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn396">2:396</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn397">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaunakī-putra,</b> ‘son of a female descendant of Śunaka,’ is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b>Kāśyapībālākyāmāṭharīputra</b> in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Mādhyaṃdina recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 4, 30. 31).</p></div>

शौर्प-णाय्य|शौर्पणाय्य|शौर्प|णाय्य|శౌర్ప-ణాయ్య|శౌర్పణాయ్య|శౌర్ప|ణాయ్య|ಶೌರ್ಪ-ಣಾಯ್ಯ|ಶೌರ್ಪಣಾಯ್ಯ|ಶೌರ್ಪ|ಣಾಯ್ಯ|śaurpa-ṇāyya|śaurpaṇāyya|śaurpa|ṇāyya|shaurpa-Naayya|shaurpaNaayya|shaurpa|Naayya|_vei_120@3352|_vei2_pn396
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3351">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3353">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn395">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn396">2:396</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn397">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaurpa-ṇāyya,</b> ‘descendant of Śūrpaṇāya,’ is the patronymic of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a>,</b> in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) in the Mādhyaṃdina recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (ii. 5, 20; iv. 5, 26).</p></div>

शौल्बायन|शौल्वायन|శౌల్బాయన|శౌల్వాయన|ಶೌಲ್ಬಾಯನ|ಶೌಲ್ವಾಯನ|śaulbāyana|śaulvāyana|shaulbaayana|shaulvaayana|_vei_120@3353|_vei2_pn396
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3352">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3354">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn395">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn396">2:396</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn397">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śaulbāyana</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शौल्वायन">śaulvāyana</a>,</b> ‘descendant of Śulba,’ is the patronymic of a teacher, <b>Udaṅka.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203353"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203353">1</a></sup> According to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203353"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203353">2</a></sup> a <a class="vei_120a" href="शौल्बायन">śaulbāyana</a> was the <a class="vei_120a" href="अध्वर्यु">adhvaryu</a>, or sacrificing priest, of those who had <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्थूण">ayasthūṇa</a></b> as Gṛhapati (‘householder,’ the title of the sacrificer who has precedence at a <i>sattra,</i> or sacrificial session). </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203353"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203353">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 4, 5, 4; 5, 4, 2; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1, 2 Mādhyaṃdina.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203353"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203353">↑</a> xi. 4, 2, 17 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

शौष्कल|శౌష్కల|ಶೌಷ್ಕಲ|śauṣkala|shauShkala|_vei_120@3354|_vei2_pn397
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3353">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3355">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn396">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn397">2:397</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn398">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śauṣkala</b> is the name of one of the sacrificial victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203354">1</a></sup> It means, according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, ‘living on dried fish or flesh,’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203354">2</a></sup> or, according to the native lexicographers, ‘selling dried fish,’ while, Sāyaṇa's commentary on the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> explains the meaning to be one who catches fish with a hook, ‘angler.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203354">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 12, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 81, n. 7; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 415.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203354"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203354">↑</a> The literal meaning is, ‘relating to what is dried’ (<i>śuṣkala</i>).</li></ol></div>

श्रुष्टि आंगिरस|श्रुष्टि आङ्गिरस|శ్రుష్టి ఆంగిరస|శ్రుష్టి ఆఙ్గిరస|ಶ್ರುಷ್ಟಿ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಶ್ರುಷ್ಟಿ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|śruṣṭi āṅgirasa|shruShTi aaMgirasa|shruShTi aangirasa|_vei_120@3355|_vei2_pn397
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3354">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3356">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn396">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn397">2:397</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn398">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śruṣṭi Āṅgirasa</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अंगिरस्">aṅgiras</a></b>’) is the name of the seer of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> or Chant in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203355"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203355">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203355"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203355">↑</a> xiii. 11, 21. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 160; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 68.</li></ol></div>

श्मशान|శ్మశాన|ಶ್ಮಶಾನ|śmaśāna|shmashaana|_vei_120@3356|_vei2_pn397
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3355">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3357">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn396">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn397">2:397</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn398">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śmaśāna</b> is the name of the ‘burial mound’ in which the bones of the dead man were laid to rest (<i>cf.</i> <b>Anagnidagdha</b>). It is mentioned in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203356"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203356">1</a></sup> and often later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203356"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203356">2</a></sup> The Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203356"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203356">3</a></sup> prescribes a four-cornered mound facing the south-east, on ground inclined to the north, out of sight of the village, in a peaceful spot amid beautiful surroundings, or on barren ground. For an <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a> (‘builder of a fire-latar’) a funeral mound like a fire-altar is prescribed. The Easterners (<i>Prācyāḥ</i>) made their mounds round.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203356"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203356">↑</a> v. 31, 8; x. 1. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203356"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203356">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 2, 8, 5; 4, 11, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxi. 4; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 7; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 5, 2, 15, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203356"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203356">↑</a> xiii. 8, 1, 1 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> Eggeling. <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 424 <i>et seq.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 407; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, cliii.</li></ol></div>

श्मश्रु|శ్మశ్రు|ಶ್ಮಶ್ರು|śmaśru|shmashru|_vei_120@3357|_vei2_pn397
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3356">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3358">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn396">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn397">2:397</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn398">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śmaśru</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203357"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203357">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203357"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203357">2</a></sup> means ‘beard’ and ‘moustache,’ being sometimes contrasted with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="केश">keśa</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203357"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203357">3</a></sup> ‘hair of the head.’ Shaving was known (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वप्तृ">vaptṛ</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्षुर">kṣura</a></b>). The  wearing of a beard was a sign of manhood according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203357"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203357">4</a></sup> with which agrees the notice of Megasthenes<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203357"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203357">5</a></sup> that the Indians carefully tended their beards up to the day of their death.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203357"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203357">↑</a> ii. 11, 17; viii. 33, 6; x. 23, 1, 4; 26, 7; 142, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203357"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203357">↑</a> Av. v. 19, 14; vi. 68, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 92; xx. 5, etc. Applied to animals, <i>ibid.,</i> xxv. 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 9, 1, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203357"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203357">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 5, 2, 48, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203357"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203357">↑</a> v. 5, 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203357"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203357">↑</a> In Diodorus, iii. 63.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 265267.</li></ol></div>

श्यापर्ण सायकायन|శ్యాపర్ణ సాయకాయన|ಶ್ಯಾಪರ್ಣ ಸಾಯಕಾಯನ|śyāparṇa sāyakāyana|shyaaparNa saayakaayana|_vei_120@3358|_vei2_pn398
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3357">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3359">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn397">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn398">2:398</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn399">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śyāparṇa Sāyakāyana</b> is the name of a man, the last for whom five victims were slain at the building of the sacrificial altar according to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203358"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203358">1</a></sup> The same text<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203358"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203358">2</a></sup> again mentions him as a builder of the fire-altar. He must have been connected in some way with the <b>Salvas.</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203358"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203358">3</a></sup> His family, the Śyāparṇas, appear in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203358"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203358">4</a></sup> as a self-assertive family of priests whom king <b>Viśvantara</b> excluded from his sacrifice, but whose leader, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राम मार्गवेय">rāma mārgaveya</a>,</b> induced him to take them back. In some way Śyāparṇa was connected with the defeat of the <b>Pañcālas</b> by the <b>Kuntis.</b><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203358"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203358">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203358"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203358">↑</a> vi. 2, 1, 39.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203358"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203358">↑</a> ix. 5, 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203358"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203358">↑</a> x. 4, 1, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203358"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203358">↑</a> vii. 27. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 43, 344, 345; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 437 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Indisches Studien,</i> 1, 215, 216.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203358"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203358">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 471.</li></ol></div>

श्याम|శ్యామ|ಶ್ಯಾಮ|śyāma|shyaama|_vei_120@3359|_vei2_pn398
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3358">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3360">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn397">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn398">2:398</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn399">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śyāma</b> (‘swarthy’) with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अयस्">ayas</a></b> (‘metal’) in all probability denotes ‘iron’ in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203359"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203359">1</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्याम">śyāma</a> alone has the same sense in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203359"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203359">2</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203359"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203359">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203359"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203359">↑</a> xi. 3, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203359"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203359">↑</a> ix. 5, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203359"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203359">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 7. 5, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xviii. 10; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 11, 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 13.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 52, 54; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 189.</li></ol></div>

श्याम-जयन्त लौहित्य|श्यामजयन्त लौहित्य|श्याम|जयन्त लौहित्य|శ్యామ-జయన్త లౌహిత్య|శ్యామజయన్త లౌహిత్య|శ్యామ|జయన్త లౌహిత్య|ಶ್ಯಾಮ-ಜಯನ್ತ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|ಶ್ಯಾಮಜಯನ್ತ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|ಶ್ಯಾಮ|ಜಯನ್ತ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|śyāma-jayanta lauhitya|śyāmajayanta lauhitya|śyāma|jayanta lauhitya|shyaama-jayanta lauhitya|shyaamajayanta lauhitya|shyaama|_vei_120@3360|_vei2_pn398
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3359">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3361">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn397">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn398">2:398</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn399">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śyāma-<a class="vei_120a" href="जयन्त">jayanta</a> Lauhitya</b> (‘descendant of Lohita’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जयन्त">jayanta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पाराशर्य">pārāśarya</a>,</b> in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Jiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 42, 1). Another man of the same name occurs in the same place as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मित्रभूति लौहित्य">mitrabhūti lauhitya</a>.</b></p></div>

श्याम-पर्ण|श्यामपर्ण|श्याम|पर्ण|శ్యామ-పర్ణ|శ్యామపర్ణ|శ్యామ|పర్ణ|ಶ್ಯಾಮ-ಪರ್ಣ|ಶ್ಯಾಮಪರ್ಣ|ಶ್ಯಾಮ|ಪರ್ಣ|śyāma-parṇa|śyāmaparṇa|śyāma|parṇa|shyaama-parNa|shyaamaparNa|shyaama|parNa|_vei_120@3361|_vei2_pn398
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3360">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3362">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn397">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn398">2:398</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn399">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śyāma-parṇa</b> is, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203361"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203361">1</a></sup> and Maitrāyaṇī<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203361"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203361">2</a></sup> Saṃhitās, the name of a man who was instructed by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सोमदक्ष कौश्रेय">somadakṣa kauśreya</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203361"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203361">↑</a> xx. 8 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 472).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203361"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203361">↑</a> iii. 2, 7.</li></ol></div>

श्याम-सुजयन्त लौहित्य|श्यामसुजयन्त लौहित्य|श्याम|सुजयन्त लौहित्य|శ్యామ-సుజయన్త లౌహిత్య|శ్యామసుజయన్త లౌహిత్య|శ్యామ|సుజయన్త లౌహిత్య|ಶ್ಯಾಮ-ಸುಜಯನ್ತ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|ಶ್ಯಾಮಸುಜಯನ್ತ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|ಶ್ಯಾಮ|ಸುಜಯನ್ತ ಲೌಹಿತ್ಯ|śyāma-sujayanta lauhitya|śyāmasujayanta lauhitya|śyāma|sujayanta lauhitya|shyaama-sujayanta lauhitya|shyaamasujayanta lauhitya|shyaama|_vei_120@3362|_vei2_pn399
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3361">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3363">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn398">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn399">2:399</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn400">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śyāma-sujayanta Lauhitya</b> (‘descendant of Lohita’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृष्णधृति सात्यकि">kṛṣṇadhṛti sātyaki</a>,</b> in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 42, 1).</p></div>

श्यामाक|శ్యామాక|ಶ್ಯಾಮಾಕ|śyāmāka|shyaamaaka|_vei_120@3363|_vei2_pn399
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3362">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3364">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn398">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn399">2:399</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn400">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śyāmāka</b> is the name of a cultivated millet (<i>Panicum frumentaceum</i>) in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203363">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203363">2</a></sup> The lightness of its seed is alluded to in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203363">3</a></sup> where it is spoken of as blown away by the wind. There it is also mentioned as the food of pigeons.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203363">4</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="श्यामाक">śyāmāka</a> and its seed (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="तण्डुल">taṇḍula</a></b>) are referred to as very small in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203363">5</a></sup> where Max Müller<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203363">6</a></sup> renders it as ‘canary seed.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203363">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 1, 2; ii. 3, 2, 6; iv. 7, 4, 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 11, 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, x. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203363">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 6, 3, 2; xii. 7, 1, 9, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 12.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203363">↑</a> xix. 50, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203363">↑</a> xx. 135, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203363">↑</a> iii. 14, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203363"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203363">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 1, 48.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 241, 275.</li></ol></div>

श्याव|श्याव|శ్యావ_౧|శ్యావ|ಶ್ಯಾವ_೧|ಶ್ಯಾವ|śyāva_1|śyāva|shyaava_1|shyaava|_vei_120@3364|_vei2_pn399
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3363">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3365">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn398">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn399">2:399</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn400">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Śyāva</b> is the name of a protégé of the Aśvins in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203364"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203364">1</a></sup> He may be identical with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्यहस्त">hiraṇyahasta</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203364"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203364">↑</a> i. 117, 24; x. 65, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 150; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 32.</li></ol></div>

श्याव|श्याव|శ్యావ_౨|శ్యావ|ಶ್ಯಾವ_೨|ಶ್ಯಾವ|śyāva_2|śyāva|shyaava_2|shyaava|_vei_120@3365|_vei2_pn399
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3364">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3366">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn398">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn399">2:399</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn400">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Śyāva</b> is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203365"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203365">1</a></sup> as a generous donor on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुवास्तु">suvāstu</a></b> river.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203365"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203365">↑</a> viii. 19, 37. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 161.</li></ol></div>

श्याव|श्याव|శ్యావ_౩|శ్యావ|ಶ್ಯಾವ_೩|ಶ್ಯಾವ|śyāva_3|śyāva|shyaava_3|shyaava|_vei_120@3366|_vei2_pn399
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3365">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3367">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn398">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn399">2:399</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn400">↷</a><hr><p>3. <b>Śyāva</b> in one passage of the Rigveda (v. 61, 9) seems clearly, as Sāyaṇa thinks, to denote <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्यावाश्व">śyāvāśva</a>.</b></p></div>

श्यावक|శ్యావక|ಶ್ಯಾವಕ|śyāvaka|shyaavaka|_vei_120@3367|_vei2_pn399
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3366">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3368">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn398">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn399">2:399</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn400">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śyāvaka</b> is mentioned as a sacrificer and friend of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> in the Rigveda (viii. 3, 12; 4, 2). He may be identical with 2. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्याव">śyāva</a>.</b></p></div>

श्यावसायन|శ్యావసాయన|ಶ್ಯಾವಸಾಯನ|śyāvasāyana|shyaavasaayana|_vei_120@3368|_vei2_pn399
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3367">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3369">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn398">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn399">2:399</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn400">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śyāvasāyana</b> is the patronymic of <b>Devataras</b> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 40, 2). The form is perhaps an error for <b>Śāvasāyana.</b> </p></div>

श्यावाश्व|శ్యావాశ్వ|ಶ್ಯಾವಾಶ್ವ|śyāvāśva|shyaavaashva|_vei_120@3369|_vei2_pn400
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3368">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3370">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn399">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn400">2:400</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn401">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śyāvāśva</b> is the name of a man mentioned several times in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203369">1</a></sup> The Anukramaṇī (Index) assigns to him a series of hymns in the fifth, eight, and ninth books.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203369">2</a></sup> In one of the hymns<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203369">3</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्यावाश्व">śyāvāśva</a> mentions, apparently as his patrons, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तरन्त">taranta</a></b> (a son of <b>Vidadaśva</b>) and <b>Purumīlha,</b> as well as <b>Rathavīti.</b> On this hymn is based a legend found in the Bṛhaddevatā,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203369">4</a></sup> that he was the son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अर्चनानस्">arcanānas</a>,</b> who was sacrificing for Rathavīti <a class="vei_120a" href="दाल्भ्य">dālbhya</a>. The father was anxious to obtain the king's daughter for his son in marriage; but though the father was willing, his wife insisted on her son-in-law being a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>. The father and son, repulsed, were returning home, when they met on the way <a class="vei_120a" href="तरन्त">taranta</a> and Purumīḍha, former patrons of the father. These showed him respect, while Taranta's wife, Śaśīyasī, presented <a class="vei_120a" href="श्यावाश्व">śyāvāśva</a> with much wealth. The son was then fortunate enough to meet the Maruts in the forest, and praised them, thus becoming a seer. As a result the king himself ultimately offered his daughter to <a class="vei_120a" href="श्यावाश्व">śyāvāśva</a>. Sieg<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203369">5</a></sup> seeks to show that this legend is presupposed in the Rigveda; but it is difficult to accept this view, since the references in the Rigveda are very obscure, and Śaśīyasī is probably no more than an epithet.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203369">6</a></sup> That there is some <a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a> at the back of the hymn is clear: what it is can hardly now be determined.</p><p>Śyāvāśva's obtaining gifts from <a class="vei_120a" href="वैददश्वि">vaidadaśvi</a> is referred to also in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203369">7</a></sup> His name occurs in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203369">8</a></sup> in two lists of persons, of which the former includes Purumīḍha, the latter also <a class="vei_120a" href="अर्चनानस्">arcanānas</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="अत्रि">atri</a>. A <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> is ascribed to him in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203369">9</a></sup> and he is perhaps referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203369">10</a></sup> In the  <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203369">11</a></sup> he is styled Ārcanānasa, ‘son of <a class="vei_120a" href="अर्चनानस्">arcanānas</a>,’ and later<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1203369">12</a></sup> he is called <a class="vei_120a" href="आत्रेय">ātreya</a>, ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="अत्रि">atri</a>.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203369">↑</a> v. 52, 1; 61, 5. 9 (Syāva, a short form of the name, is here used); 81, 5; viii. 35, 19; 36, 7; 37, 7; 38, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203369">↑</a> v. 52-61; 81; 82; viii. 35-38; ix. 32.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203369">↑</a> v. 61.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203369">↑</a> v. 49 <i>et seq.</i> See also Ṣaḍguruśiṣya on Anukramaṇī to Rv. v. 61 (ed. Macdonell, p. 117 <i>et seq.</i>); Sāyaṇa on Rv. v. 61, 17-19; Nītimañjarī in Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 50 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203369">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> 50-60. <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 148.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203369">↑</a> v. 61, 6. The word is taken as an epithet by Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> and by Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 27.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203369">↑</a> xvi. 11, 7-9.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203369">↑</a> iv. 29. 4; xviii. 3, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203369">↑</a> viii. 5. 9. Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 27, n. 4, bases on this an improbable conjeciure that he was a <a class="vei_120a" href="क्षत्रिय">kṣatriya</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203369">↑</a> i. 11, 2. But <i>cf.</i> Sieg, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 61, n. 4, who takes the word adjectivally, as in Av. xi. 2, 18, <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xix. 33, 26.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203369">↑</a> viii. 5, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1203369"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1203369">↑</a> The Anukramaṇī calls him and his father <a class="vei_120a" href="आत्रेय">ātreya</a>. In the passages from book viii. of the Rv., cited in n. 1, <a class="vei_120a" href="अत्रि">atri</a> is mentioned with him.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 126, 127; Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 214; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 354; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 359 <i>et seq.;</i> Lévi, <i>Ea Doctrine du Sacrifice,</i> 122.</li></ol></div>

श्येन|శ్యేన|ಶ್ಯೇನ|śyena|shyena|_vei_120@3370|_vei2_pn401
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3369">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3371">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn400">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn401">2:401</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn402">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śyena</b> is the name in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203370">1</a></sup> of a strong bird of prey, most probably the ‘eagle’; later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203370">2</a></sup> (as in post-Vedic Sanskrit) it seems to mean the ‘falcon’ or ‘hawk.’ It is the swiftest of birds,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203370">3</a></sup> and a source of terror to smaller birds.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203370">4</a></sup> It is the strongest of birds,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203370">5</a></sup> and even attacks herds.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203370">6</a></sup> It watches over men (<i>nṛ-cakṣas</i>),<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203370">7</a></sup> a reference, no doubt, to its lofty flight in air. It brings the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> from heaven.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203370">8</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203370">↑</a> i. 32, 14; 33, 2; 118, 11; 163, 1; 165, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203370">↑</a> Av. iii. 3, 4; vii. 41, 2; xi. 9, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203370">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 4, 7, 1; v. 4, 11, 1; Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203370">↑</a> Rv. ii. 42, 2; Av. v. 21, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203370">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203370">↑</a> Rv. iv. 38, 5. This corresponds well enough with the eagle's known habit of carrying off young lambs.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203370">↑</a> Av. vii. 41, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203370"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203370">↑</a> See Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, 1-24, who cites all relevant passages.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 87, 88, who points out that the epithet <i>ṛjipya,</i> ‘flying upwards,’ applied to the eagle, appears as an actual name of the eagle in Iranian.</li></ol></div>

श्रपयितृ|శ్రపయితృ|ಶ್ರಪಯಿತೃ|śrapayitṛ|shrapayitR|shrapayitRi|shrapayitRu|shrapayitRRi|_vei_120@3371|_vei2_pn401
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3370">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3372">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn400">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn401">2:401</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn402">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śrapayitṛ,</b> ‘cook,’ is a term mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i, 2, 2, 14).</p></div>

श्रमण|శ్రమణ|ಶ್ರಮಣ|śramaṇa|shramaNa|_vei_120@3372|_vei2_pn401
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3371">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3373">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn400">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn401">2:401</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn402">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śramaṇa</b> ‘mendicant monk,’ is first found in the Upaniṣads.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203372"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203372">1</a></sup> According to Fick,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203372"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203372">2</a></sup> anyone could become a <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रमण">śramaṇa</a>. For the time of Megasthenes this seems indicated by his evidence, which, however, refers only to the east of India, beyond the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a></b> proper.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203372"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203372">3</a></sup> The Vedic evidence is merely the name and the fact that <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तापस">tāpasa</a>,</b> ‘ascetic,’ follows it in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203372"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203372">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 3, 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 7, in <i>IndischeStudien,</i> 1, 78.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203372"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203372">↑</a> <i>Die sociale Gliederung,</i> 39 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203372"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203372">↑</a> Strabo, xv. 1, 49, 60; Arrian <i>Indica,</i> xii. 8, 9.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 27, 28, 129, 138.</li></ol></div>

श्रवण|శ్రవణ|ಶ್ರವಣ|śravaṇa|shravaNa|_vei_120@3373|_vei2_pn402
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3372">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3374">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn401">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn402">2:402</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn403">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śravaṇa.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p></div>

श्रवण-दत्त|श्रवणदत्त|श्रवण|दत्त|శ్రవణ-దత్త|శ్రవణదత్త|శ్రవణ|దత్త|ಶ್ರವಣ-ದತ್ತ|ಶ್ರವಣದತ್ತ|ಶ್ರವಣ|ದತ್ತ|śravaṇa-datta|śravaṇadatta|śravaṇa|datta|shravaNa-datta|shravaNadatta|shravaNa|_vei_120@3374|_vei2_pn402
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3373">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3375">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn401">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn402">2:402</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn403">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śravaṇa-datta</b> (‘given by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्रवण">śravaṇa</a></b>’) <b>Kauhala</b> (‘descendant of Kohala’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुशारद शालंकायन">suśārada śālaṅkāyana</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203374"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203374">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203374"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203374">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372.</li></ol></div>

श्रविष्ठ|శ్రవిష్ఠ|ಶ್ರವಿಷ್ಠ|śraviṣṭha|shraviShTha|_vei_120@3375|_vei2_pn402
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3374">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3376">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn401">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn402">2:402</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn403">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śraviṣṭha.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p></div>

श्रायस|శ్రాయస|ಶ್ರಾಯಸ|śrāyasa|shraayasa|_vei_120@3376|_vei2_pn402
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3375">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3377">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn401">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn402">2:402</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn403">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śrāyasa</b> is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203376">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203376">2</a></sup> where he appears as a teacher, and of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वीतहव्य">vītahavya</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203376">3</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203376">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203376">↑</a> v. 4, 7, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203376">↑</a> xxi. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203376">↑</a> v. 6, 5, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203376"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203376">↑</a> ix. 1, 9; xxv. 16, 3.</li></ol></div>

श्री|श्रि|శ్రీ|శ్రి|ಶ್ರೀ|ಶ್ರಿ|śrī|śri|shrii|shri|shree|_vei_120@3377|_vei2_pn402
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3376">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3378">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn401">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn402">2:402</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn403">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śrī</b> is the regular word for ‘prosperity,’ found once in the <b>Rigveda</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203377">1</a></sup> and often later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203377">2</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्रेष्ठिन्">śreṣṭhin</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203377">↑</a> viii. 2, 19, seems to have this sense.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203377"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203377">↑</a> Av. vi. 54, 1; 73, 1; ix. 5, 31; x. 6, 26; xi. 1, 12. 21; xii. 1, 63; 5, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 8, 6; v. 1, 8, 6; vi. 1, 10, 3; vii. 2, 7, 3, etc. Already in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xi. 4, 3) she is regarded as a goddess. See Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India,</i> 217 <i>et seq.</i> She already appears in the earliest Buddhist sculptures seated on a lotus between two elephants that pour water over her. This type of the goddess has survived down to the present day in India.</li></ol></div>

श्रुत-कक्ष|श्रुतकक्ष|श्रुत|कक्ष|శ్రుత-కక్ష|శ్రుతకక్ష|శ్రుత|కక్ష|ಶ್ರುತ-ಕಕ್ಷ|ಶ್ರುತಕಕ್ಷ|ಶ್ರುತ|ಕಕ್ಷ|śruta-kakṣa|śrutakakṣa|śruta|kakṣa|shruta-kakSha|shrutakakSha|shruta|kakSha|shruta-kaxa|shrutakaxa|kaxa|_vei_120@3378|_vei2_pn402
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3377">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3379">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn401">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn402">2:402</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn403">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śruta-kakṣa</b> is mentioned once in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203378"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203378">1</a></sup> as the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> of a hymn, the authorship of which the Anukramaṇī (Index) ascribes to him. A <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> or chant of his is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203378"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203378">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203378"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203378">↑</a> viii. 92, 25. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 108.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203378"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203378">↑</a> ix. 2, 7 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कक्ष">kakṣa</a></i>).</li></ol></div>

श्रुत-रथ|श्रुतरथ|श्रुत|रथ|శ్రుత-రథ|శ్రుతరథ|శ్రుత|రథ|ಶ್ರುತ-ರಥ|ಶ್ರುತರಥ|ಶ್ರುತ|ರಥ|śruta-ratha|śrutaratha|śruta|ratha|shruta-ratha|shrutaratha|shruta|_vei_120@3379|_vei2_pn402
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3378">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3380">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn401">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn402">2:402</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn403">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śruta-ratha</b> is the name of a young king in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203379"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203379">1</a></sup> He is also the patron of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पज्र">pajra</a></b> family, including <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203379"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203379">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203379"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203379">↑</a> i. 122, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203379"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203379">↑</a> Rv. v. 36, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 155; Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 97.</li></ol></div>

श्रुतर्य|శ్రుతర్య|ಶ್ರುತರ್ಯ|śrutarya|shrutarya|_vei_120@3380|_vei2_pn403
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3379">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3381">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn402">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn403">2:403</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn404">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śrutarya</b> occurs once in the Rigveda (i. 112, 9) as the name of a protégé of the Aśvins.</p></div>

श्रुतर्वन् आर्क्ष|శ్రుతర్వన్ ఆర్క్ష|ಶ್ರುತರ್ವನ್ ಆರ್ಕ್ಷ|śrutarvan ārkṣa|shrutarvan aarkSha|shrutarvan aarxa|_vei_120@3381|_vei2_pn403
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3380">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3382">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn402">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn403">2:403</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn404">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śrutarvan Ārkṣa</b> (‘descendant of Ṛkṣa’) is the name of a prince whose liberality is celebrated in one hymn of the Rigveda (viii. 74, 4. 13), and whose victory over <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मृगय">mṛgaya</a></b> is mentioned in another (x. 49, 5).</p></div>

श्रुतर्-विद्|श्रुतर्विद्|श्रुतर्|विद्|శ్రుతర్-విద్|శ్రుతర్విద్|శ్రుతర్|విద్|ಶ್ರುತರ್-ವಿದ್|ಶ್ರುತರ್ವಿದ್|ಶ್ರುತರ್|ವಿದ್|śrutar-vid|śrutarvid|śrutar|vid|shrutar-vid|shrutarvid|shrutar|_vei_120@3382|_vei2_pn403
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3381">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3383">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn402">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn403">2:403</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn404">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śrutar-vid</b> is the name of a man in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203382"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203382">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203382"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203382">↑</a> v. 44, 12. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 138, 139.</li></ol></div>

श्रुत-सेन|श्रुतसेन|श्रुत|सेन|శ్రుత-సేన|శ్రుతసేన|శ్రుత|సేన|ಶ್ರುತ-ಸೇನ|ಶ್ರುತಸೇನ|ಶ್ರುತ|ಸೇನ|śruta-sena|śrutasena|śruta|sena|shruta-sena|shrutasena|shruta|_vei_120@3383|_vei2_pn403
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3382">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3384">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn402">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn403">2:403</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn404">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śruta-sena</b> is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 5, 4, 3) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (xvi. 9, 4) as one of the brothers of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनमेजय">janamejaya</a>.</b></p></div>

श्रुष वाह्नेय|శ్రుష వాహ్నేయ|ಶ್ರುಷ ವಾಹ್ನೇಯ|śruṣa vāhneya|shruSha vaahneya|_vei_120@3384|_vei2_pn403
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3383">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3385">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn402">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn403">2:403</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn404">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śruṣa Vāhneya</b> (‘descendant of Vahni’) <b>Kāśyapa</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कश्यप">kaśyapa</a></b>’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b>Devataras,</b> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 40, 1). It is much more likely that Śruṣa is a mere misreading for <b>Śūṣa.</b></p></div>

श्रुष्टि-गु|श्रुष्टिगु|श्रुष्टि|శ్రుష్టి-గు|శ్రుష్టిగు|శ్రుష్టి|ಶ್ರುಷ್ಟಿ-ಗು|ಶ್ರುಷ್ಟಿಗು|ಶ್ರುಷ್ಟಿ|śruṣṭi-gu|śruṣṭigu|śruṣṭi|shruShTi-gu|shruShTigu|shruShTi|_vei_120@3385|_vei2_pn403
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3384">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3386">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn402">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn403">2:403</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn404">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śruṣṭi-gu</b> (‘possessing obedient oxen’) is the name of a man in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वालखिल्य">vālakhilya</a> hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203385"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203385">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203385"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203385">↑</a> viii. 51, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 140, 141; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 90.</li></ol></div>

श्रेणि|శ్రేణి|ಶ್ರೇಣಿ|śreṇi|shreNi|_vei_120@3386|_vei2_pn403
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3385">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3387">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn402">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn403">2:403</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn404">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śreṇi</b> means a ‘row’ or ‘line’ of birds,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203386"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203386">1</a></sup> or horses,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203386"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203386">2</a></sup> or chariots,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203386"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203386">3</a></sup> and so forth.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203386"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203386">↑</a> Rv. v. 59, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203386"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203386">↑</a> Rv. i. 126, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203386"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203386">↑</a> Rv. iv. 38, 6; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 14, 1.</li></ol></div>

श्रेष्ठिन्|శ్రేష్ఠిన్|ಶ್ರೇಷ್ಠಿನ್|śreṣṭhin|shreShThin|_vei_120@3387|_vei2_pn403
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3386">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3388">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn402">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn403">2:403</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn404">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śreṣṭhin</b> occurs in several passages of the Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203387"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203387">1</a></sup> where the St. Petersburg Dictionary assigns to the word the sense of ‘a man of consequence.’ It is, however, possible that  the word may already have the sense of the ‘headman of a guild,’ the modern Seth.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203387"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203387">2</a></sup> There is a similar doubt in the use of <i>śraiṣṭhya,</i><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203387"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203387">3</a></sup> which is perhaps not merely ‘the foremost place,’ as usually assumed, but definitely ‘the presidency of a guild.’</p><p>Guilds are referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Sūtras,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203387"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203387">4</a></sup> and they play a considerable part in the Buddhist texts<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203387"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203387">5</a></sup> and the Epic.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203387"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203387">6</a></sup> But the Vedic evidence is inadequate to afford ground for positive assertion or denial of their existence or organization in Vedic times.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203387"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203387">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 30, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxviii. 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 20. <a class="vei_120a" href="भग">bhaga</a> is the <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रेष्ठिन्">śreṣṭhin</a> of the gods, <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 4, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203387"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203387">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>India, Old and New,</i> 168 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203387"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203387">↑</a> Av. i. 9, 3 = <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 5, 4, 2 = <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, v. 6 = Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 4, 3. See also for the word, Av. x. 6, 31; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 25, 8; vii. 18, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8, 9, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 7, 1, 1; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 2, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 6; iv. 15, 20, etc. The use of <i>śraiṣṭhya</i> is, on the whole, not in favour of the theory that it is a technical term.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203387"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203387">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xi. 20, 21, etc.; Foy, <i>Die ko7nigliche Gewalt,</i> 14, n. 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203387"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203387">↑</a> Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India,</i> 88 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203387"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203387">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 81 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

श्रोत्रिय|శ్రోత్రియ|ಶ್ರೋತ್ರಿಯ|śrotriya|shrotriya|_vei_120@3388|_vei2_pn404
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3387">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3389">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn403">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn404">2:404</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn405">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śrotriya</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203388"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203388">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203388"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203388">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘Brahmin learned in holy lore,’ ‘theologian.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203388"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203388">↑</a> ix. 6, 37; x. 2, 20 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203388"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203388">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxiii. 4; xxviii. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 25, 15; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 4, 5; xiii. 4, 3. 14; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 8, etc. <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="महा">mahā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="श्रोत्रिय">śrotriya</a>,</i> ‘a great theologian,’ in Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 11, 1.</li></ol></div>

श्रौत-ऋषि|श्रौतऋषि|श्रौत|ऋषि|శ్రౌత-ఋషి|శ్రౌతఋషి|శ్రౌత|ఋషి|ಶ್ರೌತ-ಋಷಿ|ಶ್ರೌತಋಷಿ|ಶ್ರೌತ|ಋಷಿ|śrauta-ṛṣi|śrautaṛṣi|śrauta|ṛṣi|shrauta-RShi|shrautaRShi|shrauta|RShi|shrauta-RiShi|shrautaRiShi|RiShi|shrauta-RuShi|shrautaRuShi|RuShi|shrauta-RRiShi|shrautaRRiShi|RRiShi|_vei_120@3389|_vei2_pn404
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3388">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3390">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn403">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn404">2:404</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn405">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śrauta-ṛṣi</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203389">1</a></sup> or <b>Śrautarṣi,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203389">2</a></sup> ‘descendant of Srutaṛṣi or Śrutarṣi,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="देवभाग">devabhāga</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203389">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 1, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203389"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203389">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 4, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 10, 9, 11.</li></ol></div>

श्रौमत्य|శ్రౌమత్య|ಶ್ರೌಮತ್ಯ|śraumatya|shraumatya|_vei_120@3390|_vei2_pn404
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3389">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3391">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn403">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn404">2:404</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn405">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śraumatya,</b> ‘descendant of Śrumant,’ is the name of a teacher in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (x. 4, 5, 1).</p></div>

श्लेष्मन्|శ్లేష్మన్|ಶ್ಲೇಷ್ಮನ್|śleṣman|shleShman|_vei_120@3391|_vei2_pn404
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3390">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3392">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn403">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn404">2:404</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn405">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śleṣman</b> means generally that with which parts of a thing are joined together (from <i>śliṣ,</i> ‘join’): with reference to a hide,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203391"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203391">1</a></sup>  ‘laces’ of some sort may be intended; to a chariot,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203391"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203391">2</a></sup> ‘bonds’ or ‘cords’ are probably meant; and to wood,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203391"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203391">3</a></sup> ‘glue’ is perhaps the sense.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203391"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203391">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 32, 6; Jaiminīya Upanisad <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 17, 3; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 17, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203391"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203391">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxiv. 9. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvi. 1, 13, where a chariot (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a></b>) is called <i>śleṣmavant,</i> ‘tied with ropes.’</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203391"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203391">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 12. <i>Cf.</i> the Upaniṣads, cited in n. 1, and Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, ii. 1, which looks like a bad secondary version of the passage in the Jaiminīya.</li></ol></div>

श्लोक|శ్లోక|ಶ್ಲೋಕ|śloka|shloka|_vei_120@3392|_vei2_pn405
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3391">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3393">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn404">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn405">2:405</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn406">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śloka,</b> in the plural, is found enumerated after the <b>Upaniṣads,</b> and before the <b>Sūtras,</b> in the list of literary types given in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203392"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203392">1</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203392"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203392">2</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="श्लोक">śloka</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्">kṛt</a> appears: he is rather the ‘poet,’ as Max Müller<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203392"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203392">3</a></sup> renders it, than merely one who ‘calls aloud,’ as the St. Petersburg Dictionary explains the term.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203392"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203392">4</a></sup> Exactly what is meant cannot be said: ‘verses’ generally may be intended, several kinds being preserved in the Brāhmaṇas and called Ślokas.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203392"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203392">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203392"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203392">↑</a> ii. 4, 10; iv. 1, 6 (Mādhyaṃdina = iv. 1, 2 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>); 5, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203392"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203392">↑</a> iii. 10, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203392"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203392">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 15, 69</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203392"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203392">↑</a> In Av. v. 20, 7, the word has the sense assigned to it by the Dictionary.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203392"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203392">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 3, 1, 5; 5, 4, 12; xiii. 7, 1, 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 22, 3: <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, ii. 3, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxiv. 18, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, viii. 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 6, etc.</li></ol></div>

श्लोण्य|శ్లోణ్య|ಶ್ಲೋಣ್ಯ|śloṇya|shloNya|_vei_120@3393|_vei2_pn405
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3392">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3394">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn404">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn405">2:405</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn406">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śloṇya</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203393"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203393">1</a></sup> denotes ‘lameness,’ not ‘skin disease’ (<i>tvag-doṣa</i>), as explained by the commentator.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203393"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203393">↑</a> iii. 9, 17, 2. <i>Cf. śloṇa,</i> ‘lame,’ Av. xii. 4, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 6, 7, etc.</li></ol></div>

श्व-घ्निन्|श्वघ्निन्|श्व|घ्निन्|శ్వ-ఘ్నిన్|శ్వఘ్నిన్|శ్వ|ఘ్నిన్|ಶ್ವ-ಘ್ನಿನ್|ಶ್ವಘ್ನಿನ್|ಶ್ವ|ಘ್ನಿನ್|śva-ghnin|śvaghnin|śva|ghnin|shva-ghnin|shvaghnin|shva|_vei_120@3394|_vei2_pn405
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3393">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3395">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn404">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn405">2:405</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn406">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śva-ghnin</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203394"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203394">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203394"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203394">2</a></sup> clearly means a ‘gamester’ or ‘professional gambler.’ It may originally have denoted a ‘hunter.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203394"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203394">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203394"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203394">↑</a> i. 92, 10; ii. 12, 4; iv. 20, 3; viii. 45, 38.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203394"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203394">↑</a> iv. 16, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203394"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203394">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 71.</li></ol></div>

श्वन्|శ్వన్|ಶ್ವನ್|śvan|shvan|_vei_120@3395|_vei2_pn405
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3394">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3396">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn404">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn405">2:405</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn406">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śvan</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203395">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203395">2</a></sup> is the word for ‘dog,’ the feminine being Śunī.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203395">3</a></sup> The dog was a tame animal,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203395">4</a></sup> and used  to guard the house from thieves or other intruders.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203395">5</a></sup> He was also employed in hunting the boar (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="वराह">varāha</a>-yu</i>),<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203395">6</a></sup> but was no match for the lion.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203395">7</a></sup> A hundred dogs are mentioned as a gift in a Dānastuti (‘Praise of Gifts’) in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वालखिल्य">vālakhilya</a> hymn.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203395">8</a></sup> Elsewhere the dog is regarded as unfit for sacrifice, as being unclean,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203395">9</a></sup> and is driven away from the sacrifice.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203395">10</a></sup> To eat dog's flesh was a last resort of despair and hunger.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203395">11</a></sup> The bones of the feast were given to the dog.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1203395">12</a></sup> Saramā figures in legend as Indra's faithful dog<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1203395">13</a></sup> searching for the cows. <a class="vei_120a" href="रुद्र">rudra</a> is lord of dogs (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="श्व">śva</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a></i>) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>;<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1203395">14</a></sup> the ‘dog-keeper’ (<i>śvanin</i>) is mentioned in the list of sacrificial victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the same Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1203395">15</a></sup> The four-eyed (<i>catur-<a class="vei_120a" href="अक्ष">akṣa</a></i>) dogs of certain texts<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1203395">16</a></sup> are, of course, mythological.<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1203395">17</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुर्कुर">kurkura</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203395">↑</a> i. 161, 13 (where the sense is quite obscure); 182, 4; ii. 39, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203395">↑</a> Av. vi. 37, 3; xi. 2, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 8, 22, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203395">↑</a> Av. iv. 20, 7 (<i>catur-akṣī</i>); Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 5, 2, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203395">↑</a> Rv. ii. 39, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203395">↑</a> Rv. vii. 55, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203395">↑</a> Rv. x. 86, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203395">↑</a> Av. iv. 36, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203395">↑</a> Rv. viii. 55, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203395">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 51, 4; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 4, 1, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203395">↑</a> Rv. ix. 101, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203395">↑</a> Rv. iv. 18, 3. Later, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="श्व">śva</a>-paca</i> (‘dog-cooking’) denotes a degraded caste.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1203395">↑</a> Av. vi. 37, 3. <i>Cf.</i> ix. 4, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1203395">↑</a> i. 62, 3; 72, 8, etc. See Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 151.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1203395">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 28; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 13; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1203395">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 27; xxx. 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 3, 1, etc. <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="श्व">śva</a>-nī</i> (‘dog-leader’), Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1203395">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. x. 14, 10. 11; Av. xviii. 2, 11. 12: <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, vi. 3, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 8, 4, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 1, 2, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1203395"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1203395">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 165 <i>et seq.; Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 500, thinks that Yama's two dogs are the sun and the moon (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दिव्य श्वन्">divya śvan</a></b>).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 233: Hopkins, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 15, 154-163.</li></ol></div>

स्व-पद्|स्वपद्|स्व|पद्|స్వ-పద్|స్వపద్|స్వ|పద్|ಸ್ವ-ಪದ್|ಸ್ವಪದ್|ಸ್ವ|ಪದ್|sva-pad|svapad|sva|pad|_vei_120@3396|_vei2_pn406
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3395">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3397">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn405">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn406">2:406</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn407">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sva-pad</b> denotes a ‘savage animal,’ ‘beast of prey,’ in the Atharvaveda (viii. 5, 11; xix. 39, 4).</p></div>

श्वयथ|శ్వయథ|ಶ್ವಯಥ|śvayatha|shvayatha|_vei_120@3397|_vei2_pn406
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3396">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3398">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn405">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn406">2:406</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn407">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śvayatha</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203397"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203397">1</a></sup> means ‘swelling.’ Possibly <i>śīyathu,</i> the disease prevalent in <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a></b> according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> Sutra,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203397"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203397">2</a></sup> was a kind of ‘swelling’ (? goitre). </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203397"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203397">↑</a> iv. 2, 1, 11 (of the eye, in a myth).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203397"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203397">↑</a> ii. 5: Caland, <i>Über das rituelle <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> des <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>,</i> 35, 36.</li></ol></div>

श्व-वर्त|श्ववर्त|श्व|वर्त|శ్వ-వర్త|శ్వవర్త|శ్వ|వర్త|ಶ್ವ-ವರ್ತ|ಶ್ವವರ್ತ|ಶ್ವ|ವರ್ತ|śva-varta|śvavarta|śva|varta|shva-varta|shvavarta|shva|_vei_120@3398|_vei2_pn407
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3397">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3399">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn406">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn407">2:407</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn408">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śva-varta,</b> ‘found in dogs,’ is, according to some manuscripts, the name of a species of worm in the Atharvaveda (ix. 4, 16). See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शवर्त">śavarta</a>.</b></p></div>

श्वशुर|శ్వశుర|ಶ್ವಶುರ|śvaśura|shvashura|_vei_120@3399|_vei2_pn407
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3398">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3400">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn406">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn407">2:407</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn408">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śvaśura</b> from the Rigveda onwards<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203399">1</a></sup> denotes the ‘father-inlaw’ of the wife; not till the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> period does it include the ‘father-in-law’ of the husband.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203399">2</a></sup> The daughter-in-law (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्नुषा">snuṣā</a></b>), in the normal case when the father-in-law was the head of the family to which her husband belonged in fact as well as in age, was bound to pay him all respect.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203399">3</a></sup> When the old man had ceased to exercise control, she became mistress (<i>samrājñī</i>) over him and his wife.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203399">4</a></sup> In the plural<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203399">5</a></sup> the word denotes the ‘parents-in-law.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203399">↑</a> x. 28, 1; 85, 46; 95, 4; Av. viii. 6, 24; xiv. 2, 26, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203399">↑</a> Pāraskara <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 10, 46.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203399">↑</a> See Rv. x. 95, 4; Av. viii. 6, 24; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 4, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 12 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 260); <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 22, 7. So in Av. xiv. 2, 26, the daughter-in-law is to be ‘helpful’ to the father-in-law.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203399">↑</a> Rv. x. 85, 46. See <a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203399"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203399">↑</a> Rv. x. 95, 12; Av. xiv. 2, 27; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i> Or it may be a plural <i>majestatis,</i> but not a sign of polyandry.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 515, 516.</li></ol></div>

श्वश्रू|శ్వశ్రూ|ಶ್ವಶ್ರೂ|śvaśrū|shvashruu|shvashroo|_vei_120@3400|_vei2_pn407
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3399">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3401">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn406">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn407">2:407</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn408">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śvaśrū</b> denotes ‘mother-in-law’ of the husband<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203400">1</a></sup> as well as of the wife.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203400">2</a></sup> She, together with her husband, if he became unable to manage the family,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203400">3</a></sup> fell under the daughter-in-law's sway, but otherwise was entitled to regard.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203400">4</a></sup> The gambler in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203400">5</a></sup> complains of his having lost the favour of his wife's mother as one of the misfortunes brought upon him by dicing.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203400">↑</a> Rv. x. 85, 46; Av. xiv. 2, 26.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203400">↑</a> Rv. x. 34, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203400">↑</a> Rv. x. 85, 46.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203400">↑</a> Av. xiv. 2, 26.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203400"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203400">↑</a> Rv. x. 34, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Vermandtschaftsnamen,</i> 516.</li></ol></div>

श्वाजनि|శ్వాజని|ಶ್ವಾಜನಿ|śvājani|shvaajani|_vei_120@3401|_vei2_pn407
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3400">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3402">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn406">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn407">2:407</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn408">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śvājani</b> is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 5, 2). </p></div>

श्वापद|శ్వాపద|ಶ್ವಾಪದ|śvāpada|shvaapada|_vei_120@3402|_vei2_pn408
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3401">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3403">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn407">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn408">2:408</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn409">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śvāpada,</b> like <b>Śvapad,</b> denotes a ‘savage animal’ or ‘beast of prey.’ It is mentioned in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203402">1</a></sup> in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203402">2</a></sup> and occasionally later.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203402">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203402">↑</a> x. 16, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203402">↑</a> xi. 10, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203402"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203402">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 4, 10 (where the tiger, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शार्दूल">śārdūla</a>,</b> is mentioned as the chief of them); xii. 2, 4, 16; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 4, 29; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xii. 16, etc.</li></ol></div>

श्वा-विध्|श्वाविध्|श्वा|विध्|శ్వా-విధ్|శ్వావిధ్|శ్వా|విధ్|ಶ್ವಾ-ವಿಧ್|ಶ್ವಾವಿಧ್|ಶ್ವಾ|ವಿಧ್|śvā-vidh|śvāvidh|śvā|vidh|shvaa-vidh|shvaavidh|shvaa|_vei_120@3403|_vei2_pn408
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3402">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3404">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn407">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn408">2:408</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn409">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śvā-vidh</b> (‘dog-piercing’) is the name of the ‘porcupine’ in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203403"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203403">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203403"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203403">2</a></sup> It is called ‘long-eared’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कर्ण">karṇa</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203403"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203403">3</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शल्यक">śalyaka</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203403"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203403">↑</a> v. 13, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203403"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203403">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 20, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 14; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 56; xxiv. 33, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203403"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203403">↑</a> Av., <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 82.</li></ol></div>

श्विक्न|శ్విక్న|ಶ್ವಿಕ್ನ|śvikna|shvikna|_vei_120@3404|_vei2_pn408
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3403">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3405">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn407">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn408">2:408</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn409">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śvikna</b> is the name of a people twice mentioned in the Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203404"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203404">1</a></sup> in connexion with their king, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषभ">ṛṣabha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञतुर">yājñatura</a>.</b> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्वैक्न">śvaikna</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203404"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203404">↑</a> xii. 8, 3, 7; xiii. 5, 4, 15. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 209, 210.</li></ol></div>

श्वित्र|श्वित्र|శ్విత్ర_౧|శ్విత్ర|ಶ್ವಿತ್ರ_೧|ಶ್ವಿತ್ರ|śvitra_1|śvitra|shvitra_1|shvitra|_vei_120@3405|_vei2_pn408
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3404">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3406">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn407">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn408">2:408</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn409">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Śvitra</b> (‘white’) is the name of a species of serpent in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203405">1</a></sup> and the later Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203405">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203405">↑</a> iii. 27, 6 (where there is a variant <i><a class="vei_120a" href="चित्र">citra</a></i>); x. 4, 5, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203405"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203405">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 10, 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 13, 21, has in the parallel passage <i><a class="vei_120a" href="चित्र">citra</a>,</i> probably by error.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 95; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 134. Perhaps <a class="vei_120a" href="श्वित्र">śvitra</a>, in the lis of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 39, has this sense; but the St. Petersburg Dictionary explains it as ‘a certain domestic animal,’ or, generally ‘a white animal.’</li></ol></div>

श्वित्र|श्वित्र|శ్విత్ర_౨|శ్విత్ర|ಶ್ವಿತ್ರ_೨|ಶ್ವಿತ್ರ|śvitra_2|śvitra|shvitra_2|shvitra|_vei_120@3406|_vei2_pn408
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3405">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3407">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn407">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn408">2:408</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn409">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Śvitra</b> is found as an adjective in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xii. 11, 11) in the sense of ‘afflicted with white leprosy.’</p></div>

श्वित्र्य|శ్విత్ర్య|ಶ್ವಿತ್ರ್ಯ|śvitrya|shvitrya|_vei_120@3407|_vei2_pn408
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3406">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3408">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn407">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn408">2:408</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn409">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śvitrya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्वैत्रेय">śvaitreya</a>.</b> </p></div>

श्वेत-केतु|श्वेतकेतु|श्वेत|केतु|శ్వేత-కేతు|శ్వేతకేతు|శ్వేత|కేతు|ಶ್ವೇತ-ಕೇತು|ಶ್ವೇತಕೇತು|ಶ್ವೇತ|ಕೇತು|śveta-ketu|śvetaketu|śveta|ketu|shveta-ketu|shvetaketu|shveta|_vei_120@3408|_vei2_pn409
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3407">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3409">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn408">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn409">2:409</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn410">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śveta-ketu</b> Aruṇeya<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203408">1</a></sup> (‘descendant of <b>Aruṇa</b>’) or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="औद्दालकि">auddālaki</a></b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203408">2</a></sup> (‘son of <b>Uddālaka</b>’) is mentioned repeatedly in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> and the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203408">3</a></sup> he appears as <a class="vei_120a" href="श्वेतकेतु">śvetaketu</a>, son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आरुणि">āruṇi</a>,</b> and as a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a>.</b> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203408">4</a></sup> he is quoted as an authority on the vexed question of the duty of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सदस्य">sadasya</a>,</b> or the seventeenth priest, at the ritual of the Kauṣītakins, to notify errors in the sacrifice; <a class="vei_120a" href="आरुणि">āruṇi</a>, his father, is also cited. He was a person of some originality, for he insisted on eating honey despite the general prohibition of the use of that delicacy by Brahmacārins or religious students.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203408">5</a></sup> He was a contemporary of, and was instructed by the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a></b> king <b>Pravāhaṇa <a class="vei_120a" href="जैवल">jaivala</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203408">6</a></sup> He was also a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a>,</b> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>,</b> and figured among the Brahmin disputants at his court.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203408">7</a></sup> A story is told of him in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>:<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203408">8</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जल जातूकर्ण्य">jala jātūkarṇya</a></b> was lucky enough to become the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> of three peoples or kings, of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काशि">kāśi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>.</b> Seeing this, <a class="vei_120a" href="श्वेतकेतु">śvetaketu</a> felt annoyed and reproached his father with his excessive devotion to sacrifice, which merely enriched and glorified others, not himself. His father replied, forbidding him to speak thus: he had learned the true method of sacrificing, and his ambition in life had been to discuss it with every Brahmin.</p><p>All the references to <a class="vei_120a" href="श्वेतकेतु">śvetaketu</a> belong to the latest period of Vedic literature. It is, therefore, not surprising that the Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203408">9</a></sup> should refer to him as an Avara, or  person of later days, who still became a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> by special merit. His date, however, must not be fixed too low, because the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> in which he plays so marked a part is certainly earlier than Pāṇini, and was apparently even in that grammarian's time believed to be an ancient work: hence 500 B.C. is probably rather too late than too early a period for <a class="vei_120a" href="श्वेतकेतु">śvetaketu</a> as a rough approximation to a date.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203408">10</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203408">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 2, 7, 12; 5, 4, 18; 6, 2, 1; xii. 2, 1, 9; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 7, 1; vi. 1, 1 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 2, 1 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>); Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 3, 1; vi. 1, 1; 8, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203408">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 3, 13; iv. 2, 5, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203408">↑</a> i. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203408">↑</a> xxvi. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203408">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 4, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203408">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 1 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 2, 1 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>); Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203408">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 6, 2, 1 (it is to be noted that he came upon <a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a> when travelling about with some other Brahmins: he was never settled in the <a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a> country, but was clearly a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a>,</b> like his father); Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 7, 1, where he shares the usual fate of defeat in argument by <a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203408">↑</a> xvi. 27, 6 <i>et seq.</i> The exact sense of <i>kṛtsnake brahmabandhau vyajijñāsiṣi</i> is not quite certain. But <a class="vei_120a" href="आरुणि">āruṇi</a> seems to assert the love of knowledge, not of material advantages accruing to the <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>, to have been his concern in life.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203408">↑</a> i. 2, 5, 4-6.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203408"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203408">↑</a> See on this, Bühler, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 2, xxxvii <i>et seq.;</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, xxxv <i>et seq.;</i> Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 360 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 65; 13, 443; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 22 <i>et seq.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 433; Max Müller, <i>Ancient Sanskrit Literature,</i> 421 <i>et seq.;</i> Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 397, n.</li></ol></div>

श्वेत्या|శ్వేత్యా|ಶ್ವೇತ್ಯಾ|śvetyā|shvetyaa|_vei_120@3409|_vei2_pn410
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3408">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3410">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn409">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn410">2:410</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn411">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śvetyā</b> appears in the <a class="vei_120a" href="नदी">nadī</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="स्तुति">stuti</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203409"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203409">1</a></sup> (‘praise of rivers’) to be a stream, probably a tributary of the Indus.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203409"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203409">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203409"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203409">↑</a> x. 75, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203409"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203409">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 14, 15; udwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 200, gives the form as Śvetī; Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 184, gives both forms.</li></ol></div>

श्वैक्न|శ్వైక్న|ಶ್ವೈಕ್ನ|śvaikna|shvaikna|_vei_120@3410|_vei2_pn410
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3409">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3411">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn409">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn410">2:410</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn411">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śvaikna,</b> ‘king of the <b>Śviknas,</b>’ is the title of <b>Pratīdarśa,</b> who was, according to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203410"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203410">1</a></sup> one of those who offered the <a class="vei_120a" href="दाक्षायण">dākṣāyaṇa</a> sacrifice. He also taught <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुप्लन् सार्ञ्जय">suplan sārñjaya</a></b> the sacrifice: hence Weber<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203410"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203410">2</a></sup> has inferred a connexion of the Śviknas and the <b>Sṛñjayas.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203410"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203410">↑</a> ii. 4, 4, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203410"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203410">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 209, 210.</li></ol></div>

श्वैत्रेय|శ్వైత్రేయ|ಶ್ವೈತ್ರೇಯ|śvaitreya|shvaitreya|_vei_120@3411|_vei2_pn410
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3410">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3412">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn409">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn410">2:410</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn411">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śvaitreya</b> occurs in two passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203411">1</a></sup> where Sāyaṇa sees in the word the name of a man, a ‘descendant of Śvitrā.’ The first passage is almost identical with one in the sixth Maṇḍala of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203411">2</a></sup> where, however, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दशद्यु">daśadyu</a></b> appears alone without <a class="vei_120a" href="श्वैत्रेय">śvaitreya</a>. Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203411">3</a></sup> identifies <a class="vei_120a" href="दशद्यु">daśadyu</a> with <a class="vei_120a" href="श्वैत्रेय">śvaitreya</a> (‘son of Śvitrī’), and considers him a son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुत्स">kutsa</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203411">4</a></sup> Bergaigne<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203411">5</a></sup> and Baunack<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203411">6</a></sup> think he is really <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भुज्यु">bhujyu</a>.</b> Geldner<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203411">7</a></sup> considers that he was a bull used for fighting, the son  of a Śvitrā cow,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203411">8</a></sup> but this is very doubtful, though the term <i><a class="vei_120a" href="श्वैत्रेय">śvaitreya</a></i> is elsewhere applied to a bull.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203411">9</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्वित्र्य">śvitrya</a><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203411">10</a></sup> seems to have the same sense as <a class="vei_120a" href="श्वैत्रेय">śvaitreya</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203411">↑</a> i. 33, 14; v. 19, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203411">↑</a> vi. 26, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203411">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 147.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203411">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 51, 6; vi. 26, 3, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203411">↑</a> <i>Religion Védique,</i> 3, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203411">↑</a> Kuhn's <i>Zeitschrift,</i> 35, 527.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203411">↑</a> <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 7, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203411">↑</a> <i>Cf. śvaitarī,</i> Rv. iv. 33, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203411">↑</a> Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1910, 935.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203411"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203411">↑</a> Rv. i. 33, 15, where Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> takes <i>śvitryam</i> as the accusative of <i>śvitrī.</i></li></ol></div>

षण्ड|షణ్డ|ಷಣ್ಡ|ṣaṇḍa|ShaNDa|_vei_120@3412|_vei2_pn411
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3411">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3413">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn410">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn411">2:411</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn413">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ṣaṇḍa</b> is the name of a priest at the snake festival described in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203412"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203412">1</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुषण्ड">kuṣaṇḍa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203412"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203412">↑</a> xxv. 15, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 35.</li></ol></div>

षण्डिक|షణ్డిక|ಷಣ್ಡಿಕ|ṣaṇḍika|ShaNDika|_vei_120@3413|_vei2_pn411
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3412">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3414">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn410">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn411">2:411</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn413">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ṣaṇḍika</b> is mentioned in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203413">1</a></sup> as a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="केशिन्">keśin</a>.</b> Probably <b>Khaṇḍika</b> should be read as usual elsewhere.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203413"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203413">↑</a> i. 4, 12, where von Schroeder gives no variant. But <i>ṣ</i> and <i>kh</i> are constantly interchanged in manuscripts.</li></ol></div>

सं-रुध्|संरुध्|सं|रुध्|సం-రుధ్|సంరుధ్|సం|రుధ్|ಸಂ-ರುಧ್|ಸಂರುಧ್|ಸಂ|ರುಧ್|saṃ-rudh|saṃrudh|saṃ|rudh|saM-rudh|saMrudh|saM|_vei_120@3414|_vei2_pn411
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3413">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3415">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn410">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn411">2:411</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn413">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃ-rudh</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सं">saṃ</a>-likhita</b> occur in the Atharvaveda (vii. 50, 5) as two technical terms, of unknown sense, used in dicing.</p></div>

सं-वत्सर|संवत्सर|सं|वत्सर|సం-వత్సర|సంవత్సర|సం|వత్సర|ಸಂ-ವತ್ಸರ|ಸಂವತ್ಸರ|ಸಂ|ವತ್ಸರ|saṃ-vatsara|saṃvatsara|saṃ|vatsara|saM-vatsara|saMvatsara|saM|_vei_120@3415|_vei2_pn411
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3414">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3416">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn410">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn411">2:411</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn413">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃ-vatsara,</b> ‘year,’ is repeatedly mentioned from the Rigveda onwards.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203415">1</a></sup></p><p>It duration was, according to the concurrent evidence of the Saṃhitās and Brāhmaṇas, 360 days, divided into 12 months, being, no doubt, roughly a lunar synodic year, which, however, it exceeded in length by 6 days.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203415">2</a></sup> As a solar year it appears only in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निदान">nidāna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203415">3</a></sup> of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>, where the sun is stated to spend 13(1/3) days in each of the 27 Nakṣatras. </p><p>The year being obviously out of harmony with the solar year (whether sidereal or tropical), efforts were certainly made to effect an assimilation of the natural and the accepted year. As has been seen (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a></b>), the evidence goes strongly to show that the intercalation was not an easy matter in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> period, though there are traces of what may be regarded as a five-yearly or six-yearly intercalation. But there is no conclusive evidence that these periods were really observed.</p><p>Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203415">4</a></sup> indeed, considers that the evidence required is afforded by the lists of the years, which are sometimes enumerated as five: <a class="vei_120a" href="संवत्सर">saṃvatsara</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="परिवत्सर">parivatsara</a>, Idāvatsara, Idvatsara, and <a class="vei_120a" href="वत्सर">vatsara</a>;<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203415">5</a></sup> or <a class="vei_120a" href="संवत्सर">saṃvatsara</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="परिवत्सर">parivatsara</a>, Idāvatsara, <a class="vei_120a" href="इदुवत्सर">iduvatsara</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वत्सर">vatsara</a>;<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203415">6</a></sup> or <a class="vei_120a" href="संवत्सर">saṃvatsara</a>, Idāvatsara, <a class="vei_120a" href="इदुवत्सर">iduvatsara</a>, Idvatsara, <a class="vei_120a" href="वत्सर">vatsara</a>;<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203415">7</a></sup> or <a class="vei_120a" href="संवत्सर">saṃvatsara</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="परिवत्सर">parivatsara</a>, Idāvatsara, Anuvatsara, Udvatsara;<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203415">8</a></sup> or <a class="vei_120a" href="संवत्सर">saṃvatsara</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="परिवत्सर">parivatsara</a>, Idāvatsara, Anuvatsara, Idvatsara.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203415">9</a></sup> But it must be noted not merely that the names vary considerably, but that four only are mentioned in some places,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203415">10</a></sup> in others<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203415">11</a></sup> three, in others<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1203415">12</a></sup> two, and in yet others<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1203415">13</a></sup> six. Moreover, in none of these enumerations is there any reference to the names being connected with a system of intercalation. It is most probable that here we have no more that a mere series of priestly variations of <a class="vei_120a" href="वत्सर">vatsara</a>, based on the older and more genuine <a class="vei_120a" href="संवत्सर">saṃvatsara</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="परिवत्सर">parivatsara</a> as variants of the simple <a class="vei_120a" href="वत्सर">vatsara</a>, ‘year.’ The key to the invention of the series is probably to be found in passages like that of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1203415">14</a></sup> where the several <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चातुर्मास्य">cāturmāsya</a></b> (‘four-monthly’) sacrifices are equated  with the different years.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1203415">15</a></sup> Particularly unjustifiable is the attempt of Zimmer to see in the two-year series a series of two years of 354 days each, with an intercalary month in the second; for the year of 354 days, as such, is not known to have existed before the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> period.</p><p>Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1203415">16</a></sup> also finds an attempt at intercalation in the famous 12 days in which the Ṛbhus are said to have slept in the house of Agohya.<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1203415">17</a></sup> He thinks that they represent twelve days added at the winter solstice to equate the lunar year of 354 days and the solar year of 366 days; and from the reverence paid in German antiquity to the ‘12 nights,’ he infers that this mode of intercalation is Indo-Germanic.<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1203415">18</a></sup> There can be little doubt that this view is wrong, and that the 12 days are merely the ‘reflexion of the year’ (<i>saṃvatsarasya pratimā</i>)<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1203415">19</a></sup> in the sense that they represent the twelve months, and have no relation to chronology at all.</p><p>A reference to the use of <a class="vei_120a" href="संवत्सर">saṃvatsara</a> alone as the fifth year of the cycle is seen by Shamasastry<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1203415">20</a></sup> in the peculiar dating of certain notices in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1203415">21</a></sup> but this view is improbable.<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1203415">22</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203415">↑</a> Rv. i. 110, 4; 140, 2; 161, 13; vii. 103, 1, 7, etc.; Av. i. 35, 4; ii. 6, 1; iii. 10, 2; iv. 35, 4; vi. 53, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203415">↑</a> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203415">↑</a> v. 12, 2. 5. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 284.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203415">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 369, 370, and <i>cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. <a class="vei_120a" href="संवत्सर">saṃvatsara</a>,</i> 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203415">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxvii. 45.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203415">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 7, 3, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203415">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 10, 4, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203415">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xiii. 15; xxxix. 6; xl. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203415">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="गर्ग">garga</a>, quoted in the commentary on <a class="vei_120a" href="ज्योतिष">jyotiṣa</a>, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203415">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सं">saṃ</a>-, <a class="vei_120a" href="परि">pari</a>-, <a class="vei_120a" href="इदा-">idā-</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अनु">anu</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वत्सर">vatsara</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvii. 13, 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 10, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203415">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="इदा-">idā-</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="परि">pari</a>-, <a class="vei_120a" href="सं-वत्सर">saṃ-vatsara</a>, Av. vi. 55, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="इदु">idu</a>-, <a class="vei_120a" href="परि">pari</a>-, <a class="vei_120a" href="सं-वत्सर">saṃ-vatsara</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 7, 2, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1203415">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सं">saṃ</a>-, <a class="vei_120a" href="परि-वत्सर">pari-vatsara</a>, Av. viii. 8, 23; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, x. 80.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1203415">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सं">saṃ</a>-, <a class="vei_120a" href="परि">pari</a>-, <a class="vei_120a" href="इदा-">idā-</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अनु">anu</a>-, <a class="vei_120a" href="वत्सर">vatsara</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सं-वत्सर">saṃ-vatsara</a>, Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="सं">saṃ</a>-, <a class="vei_120a" href="परि">pari</a>-, <a class="vei_120a" href="इदा-">idā-</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="इदु">idu</a>-, <a class="vei_120a" href="इद्-">id-</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वत्सर">vatsara</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, iv. 19, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 298, n. 1; Max Müller, <i>Rigveda,</i> 4^3, xxv, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1203415">↑</a> xvii. 13, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1203415">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 91; Thibaut, <i>Astronomie, Astrologie und Mathematik,</i> 12; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 24, 42.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1203415">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 366, 367; Tilak, <i>Orion,</i> 16 <i>et seq.;</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 145.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1203415">↑</a> Rv. iv. 33, 7. <i>Cf.</i> i. 110, 2; 161, 13. See on this legend, Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 133; Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 236.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1203415">↑</a> See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 242 <i>et seq.;</i> 17, 223, 224; 18, 45, 46; <i>Proceedings of the Berlin Academy,</i> 1894, 809; Thibaut, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 10; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 308, 310; Whitney, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, xciv.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1203415">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 9, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 15. See also Atharvaveda, iv. 11, 11; Weber, <i>Omina und Portenta,</i> 388.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1203415">↑</a> <i>Gavām <a class="vei_120a" href="अयन">ayana</a>,</i> 137, 138.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1203415">↑</a> ii. 12; iii. 1; xxvi. 18; xxx. 3. See also <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 1, 1, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1203415"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1203415">↑</a> Caland, <i>Über das rituelle <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> des Bandhāyana,</i> 36, 37, gives a much more reasonable explanation of the anomaly.</li></ol></div>

संवरण|సంవరణ|ಸಂವರಣ|saṃvaraṇa|saMvaraNa|_vei_120@3416|_vei2_pn413
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3415">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3417">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn411">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn413">2:413</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn414">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃvaraṇa</b> is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> mentioned in one passage of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203416"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203416">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203416"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203416">↑</a> v. 33, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 215.</li></ol></div>

संवर्ग-जित् लामकायन|संवर्गजित् लामकायन|संवर्ग|जित् लामकायन|సంవర్గ-జిత్ లామకాయన|సంవర్గజిత్ లామకాయన|సంవర్గ|జిత్ లామకాయన|ಸಂವರ್ಗ-ಜಿತ್ ಲಾಮಕಾಯನ|ಸಂವರ್ಗಜಿತ್ ಲಾಮಕಾಯನ|ಸಂವರ್ಗ|ಜಿತ್ ಲಾಮಕಾಯನ|saṃvarga-jit lāmakāyana|saṃvargajit lāmakāyana|saṃvarga|jit lāmakāyana|saMvarga-jit laamakaayana|saMvargajit laamakaayana|saMvarga|jit laamakaayana|_vei_120@3417|_vei2_pn414
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3416">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3418">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn413">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn414">2:414</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn415">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃvarga-<a class="vei_120a" href="जित्">jit</a> Lāmakāyana</b> is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b>Śākadāsa</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203417"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203417">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203417"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203417">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373.</li></ol></div>

सं-वर्त|सं-वर्त|संवर्त|सं|वर्त|సం-వర్త_౧|సం-వర్త|సంవర్త|సం|వర్త|ಸಂ-ವರ್ತ_೧|ಸಂ-ವರ್ತ|ಸಂವರ್ತ|ಸಂ|ವರ್ತ|saṃ-varta_1|saṃ-varta|saṃvarta|saṃ|varta|saM-varta_1|saM-varta|saMvarta|saM|_vei_120@3418|_vei2_pn414
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3417">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3419">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn413">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn414">2:414</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn415">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Saṃ-varta</b> occurs once in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203418"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203418">1</a></sup> with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृश">kṛśa</a></b> as an ancient sacrificer. He may be identical with the next.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203418"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203418">↑</a> viii. 54, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 141, 164.</li></ol></div>

सं-वर्त आंगिरस|सं-वर्त आंगिरस|संवर्त आंगिरस|सं|वर्त आंगिरस|सं-वर्त आङ्गिरस_२|सं-वर्त आङ्गिरस|संवर्त आङ्गिरस|वर्त आङ्गिरस|సం-వర్త ఆంగిరస_౨|సం-వర్త ఆంగిరస|సంవర్త ఆంగిరస|సం|వర్త ఆంగిరస|సం-వర్త ఆఙ్గిరస_౨|సం-వర్త ఆఙ్గిరస|సంవర్త ఆఙ్గిరస|వర్త ఆఙ్గిరస|ಸಂ-ವರ್ತ ಆಂಗಿರಸ_೨|ಸಂ-ವರ್ತ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಸಂವರ್ತ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಸಂ|ವರ್ತ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಸಂ-ವರ್ತ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ_೨|ಸಂ-ವರ್ತ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|ಸಂವರ್ತ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|ವರ್ತ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|saṃ-varta āṅgirasa_2|saṃ-varta āṅgirasa|saṃvarta āṅgirasa|saṃ|varta āṅgirasa|saM-varta aaMgirasa_2|saM-varta aaMgirasa|saMvarta aaMgirasa|saM|varta aaMgirasa|saM-varta aangirasa_2|saM-varta aangirasa|saMvarta aangirasa|varta aangirasa|_vei_120@3419|_vei2_pn414
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3418">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3420">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn413">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn414">2:414</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn415">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Saṃ-<a class="vei_120a" href="वर्त">varta</a> Āṅgirasa</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अंगिरस्">aṅgiras</a></b>’) is said in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203419"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203419">1</a></sup> to have consecrated <b>Marutta.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203419"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203419">↑</a> viii. 21, 12. <i>Cf.</i> Leumann, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 67 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

सं-श्रवस् सौवर्चनस|संश्रवस् सौवर्चनस|सं|श्रवस् सौवर्चनस|సం-శ్రవస్ సౌవర్చనస|సంశ్రవస్ సౌవర్చనస|సం|శ్రవస్ సౌవర్చనస|ಸಂ-ಶ್ರವಸ್ ಸೌವರ್ಚನಸ|ಸಂಶ್ರವಸ್ ಸೌವರ್ಚನಸ|ಸಂ|ಶ್ರವಸ್ ಸೌವರ್ಚನಸ|saṃ-śravas sauvarcanasa|saṃśravas sauvarcanasa|saṃ|śravas sauvarcanasa|saM-shravas sauvarchanasa|saMshravas sauvarchanasa|saM|shravas sauvarchanasa|_vei_120@3420|_vei2_pn414
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3419">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3421">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn413">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn414">2:414</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn415">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃ-<a class="vei_120a" href="श्रवस्">śravas</a> Sauvarcanasa</b> is the name of a teacher who, according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (i. 7, 2, 1), discussed a point of ritual with <b>Tumiñja.</b></p></div>

सं-श्रावयितृ|संश्रावयितृ|सं|श्रावयितृ|సం-శ్రావయితృ|సంశ్రావయితృ|సం|శ్రావయితృ|ಸಂ-ಶ್ರಾವಯಿತೃ|ಸಂಶ್ರಾವಯಿತೃ|ಸಂ|ಶ್ರಾವಯಿತೃ|saṃ-śrāvayitṛ|saṃśrāvayitṛ|saṃ|śrāvayitṛ|saM-shraavayitR|saMshraavayitR|saM|shraavayitR|saM-shraavayitRi|saMshraavayitRi|shraavayitRi|saM-shraavayitRu|saMshraavayitRu|shraavayitRu|saM-shraavayitRRi|saMshraavayitRRi|shraavayitRRi|_vei_120@3421|_vei2_pn414
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3420">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3422">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn413">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn414">2:414</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn415">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃ-śrāvayitṛ</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (ii. 1) denotes the attendant who announces visitors, the ‘doorkeeper.’</p></div>

सं-श्लिष्टका|संश्लिष्टका|सं|श्लिष्टका|సం-శ్లిష్టకా|సంశ్లిష్టకా|సం|శ్లిష్టకా|ಸಂ-ಶ್ಲಿಷ್ಟಕಾ|ಸಂಶ್ಲಿಷ್ಟಕಾ|ಸಂ|ಶ್ಲಿಷ್ಟಕಾ|saṃ-śliṣṭakā|saṃśliṣṭakā|saṃ|śliṣṭakā|saM-shliShTakaa|saMshliShTakaa|saM|shliShTakaa|_vei_120@3422|_vei2_pn414
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3421">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3423">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn413">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn414">2:414</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn415">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃ-śliṣṭakā</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203422"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203422">1</a></sup> or <b>Saṃśviṣṭikā</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203422"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203422">2</a></sup> is the name of an animal mentioned in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> and the Śāṭyāyanaka along with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोधा">godhā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203422"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203422">↑</a> Śāṭyāyanaka in Sāyaṇa on Rv. viii. 91.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203422"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203422">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 221 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 29).</li></ol></div>

सं-सर्प|संसर्प|सं|सर्प|సం-సర్ప|సంసర్ప|సం|సర్ప|ಸಂ-ಸರ್ಪ|ಸಂಸರ್ಪ|ಸಂ|ಸರ್ಪ|saṃ-sarpa|saṃsarpa|saṃ|sarpa|saM-sarpa|saMsarpa|saM|_vei_120@3423|_vei2_pn414
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3422">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3424">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn413">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn414">2:414</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn415">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃ-sarpa.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></p></div>

सं-स्कन्ध|संस्कन्ध|सं|स्कन्ध|సం-స్కన్ధ|సంస్కన్ధ|సం|స్కన్ధ|ಸಂ-ಸ್ಕನ್ಧ|ಸಂಸ್ಕನ್ಧ|ಸಂ|ಸ್ಕನ್ಧ|saṃ-skandha|saṃskandha|saṃ|skandha|saM-skandha|saMskandha|saM|_vei_120@3424|_vei2_pn414
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3423">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3425">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn413">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn414">2:414</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn415">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃ-skandha</b> (‘having the shoulders together’) is the name of a disease mentioned with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विष्कन्ध">viṣkandha</a></b> in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203424"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203424">1</a></sup> Whitney,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203424"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203424">2</a></sup> however, thinks it is intended as an adjective implying the sense of ‘counteracting the disease <a class="vei_120a" href="विष्कन्ध">viṣkandha</a>.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203424"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203424">↑</a> xix. 34, 5, with Sāyaṇa's note.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203424"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203424">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 952.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 65, 391; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 283.</li></ol></div>

सं-होत्र|संहोत्र|सं|होत्र|సం-హోత్ర|సంహోత్ర|సం|హోత్ర|ಸಂ-ಹೋತ್ರ|ಸಂಹೋತ್ರ|ಸಂ|ಹೋತ್ರ|saṃ-hotra|saṃhotra|saṃ|hotra|saM-hotra|saMhotra|saM|_vei_120@3425|_vei2_pn415
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3424">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3426">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn414">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn415">2:415</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn416">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃ-hotra</b> occurs once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203425"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203425">1</a></sup> where Geldner<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203425"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203425">2</a></sup> thinks the sense of ‘school,’ referring to a school of pupils of the ritual, is most appropriate.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203425"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203425">↑</a> x. 86, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203425"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203425">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 38.</li></ol></div>

सक्तु|సక్తు|ಸಕ್ತು|saktu|_vei_120@3426|_vei2_pn415
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3425">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3427">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn414">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn415">2:415</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn416">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saktu</b> in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203426">1</a></sup> denotes ‘coarsely ground meal,’ ‘groats,’ especially ‘barley meal.’ In the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203426">2</a></sup> where the word occurs only once, it seems rather to mean grain before it is winnowed by the <b>Titaü.</b> If the latter word, however, designates a ‘sieve,’ <a class="vei_120a" href="सक्तु">saktu</a> might still mean ‘groats,’ as opposed to fine meal.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203426">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 4, 10, 6; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 21 <i>et seq.;</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 3, 16; ix. 1, 1, 8 (<i>cf.</i> <b>Gavedhukā</b>), etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 2 (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अपामार्ग">apāmārga</a></b>). <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुवल">kuvala</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="कर्कन्धु">karkandhu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="बदर">badara</a>:</b> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 4, 22, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203426"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203426">↑</a> x. 71, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 238.</li></ol></div>

सखि|సఖి|ಸಖಿ|sakhi|_vei_120@3427|_vei2_pn415
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3426">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3428">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn414">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn415">2:415</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn416">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sakhi,</b> ‘friend,’ is common from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203427">1</a></sup> onwards,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203427">2</a></sup> both literally and metaphorically.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203427">↑</a> i. 164, 20 (of birds); iii. 43, 4 (of steeds); ii. 1, 9; v. 12, 5; vi. 75, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203427"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203427">↑</a> Av. v. 4, 7; 11, 9; 13, 5, etc. So <i>sakhitva</i> and <i>sakhya,</i> ‘friendship,’ are also common--<i>e.g.,</i> Rv. i. 10, 6; iii. 1, 15; iv. 25, 2, etc., and Rv. 1. 178, 2; ii. 18, 8; vii. 22, 9, etc.</li></ol></div>

सघन्|సఘన్|ಸಘನ್|saghan|_vei_120@3428|_vei2_pn415
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3427">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3429">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn414">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn415">2:415</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn416">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saghan</b> is the name of a bird, perhaps ‘eagle’ or ‘vulture,’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203428">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203428">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203428">↑</a> iii. 2, 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203428"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203428">↑</a> ii. 8, 6, 1; Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> (‘valture’), <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 88.</li></ol></div>

संग प्रयोगि|सङ्ग प्रयोगि|సంగ ప్రయోగి|సఙ్గ ప్రయోగి|ಸಂಗ ಪ್ರಯೋಗಿ|ಸಙ್ಗ ಪ್ರಯೋಗಿ|saṅga prayogi|saMga prayogi|sanga prayogi|_vei_120@3429|_vei2_pn415
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3428">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3430">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn414">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn415">2:415</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn416">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṅga Prayogi</b> seems to be mentioned as a teacher in the Maitrāyaṇi Saṃhitā (iii. 1, 9).</p></div>

सं-गति|संगति|सं|गति|सङ्गति|సం-గతి|సంగతి|సం|గతి|సఙ్గతి|ಸಂ-ಗತಿ|ಸಂಗತಿ|ಸಂ|ಗತಿ|ಸಙ್ಗತಿ|saṃ-gati|saṃgati|saṃ|gati|saM-gati|saMgati|saM|sangati|_vei_120@3430|_vei2_pn415
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3429">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3431">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn414">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn415">2:415</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn416">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃ-gati</b> in one passage of the Rigveda (x. 141, 4) seems to have the sense of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="समिति">samiti</a>,</b> ‘assembly of the people.’ </p></div>

सं-गव|संगव|सं|गव|सङ्गव|సం-గవ|సంగవ|సం|గవ|సఙ్గవ|ಸಂ-ಗವ|ಸಂಗವ|ಸಂ|ಗವ|ಸಙ್ಗವ|saṃ-gava|saṃgava|saṃ|gava|saM-gava|saMgava|saM|sangava|_vei_120@3431|_vei2_pn416
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3430">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3432">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn415">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn416">2:416</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn418">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃ-gava</b> denotes the time when the grazing cows are driven together for milking. In the division of the day the word denotes the period before midday, ‘forenoon.’ It is found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203431"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203431">1</a></sup> and often later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203431"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203431">2</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b>Go</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अहन्">ahan</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203431"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203431">↑</a> v. 76, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203431"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203431">↑</a> Av. ix. 6, 46; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 9, 2; 5, 3, 1; ii. 1, 1, 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 2, 3, 9; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 9, 4; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 12, 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 112 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

सं-गविनी|सं-गविनि|संगविनी|संगविनि|सं|गविनी|गविनि|सङ्गविनी|सङ्गविनि|సం-గవినీ|సం-గవిని|సంగవినీ|సంగవిని|సం|గవినీ|గవిని|సఙ్గవినీ|సఙ్గవిని|ಸಂ-ಗವಿನೀ|ಸಂ-ಗವಿನಿ|ಸಂಗವಿನೀ|ಸಂಗವಿನಿ|ಸಂ|ಗವಿನೀ|ಗವಿನಿ|ಸಙ್ಗವಿನೀ|ಸಙ್ಗವಿನಿ|saṃ-gavinī|saṃ-gavini|saṃgavinī|saṃgavini|saṃ|gavinī|gavini|saM-gavinii|saM-gavini|saMgavinii|saMgavini|saM|gavinii|saM-gavinee|saMgavinee|gavinee|sangavinii|sangavini|sangavinee|_vei_120@3432|_vei2_pn416
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3431">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3433">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn415">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn416">2:416</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn418">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃ-gavinī</b> is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203432">1</a></sup> where it is said that the animals of the <b>Bharatas</b> in the evening were at the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोष्ठ">goṣṭha</a>,</b> ‘pasture,’ but at midday came to the <a class="vei_120a" href="संगविनी">saṃgavinī</a>, apparently a shed or an enclosure in which during the heat of the day they were milked.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203432"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203432">↑</a> iii. 18, 14. <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 3, 112, 113; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 362.</li></ol></div>

सं-ग्रहीतृ|संग्रहीतृ|सं|ग्रहीतृ|सङ्ग्रहीतृ|సం-గ్రహీతృ|సంగ్రహీతృ|సం|గ్రహీతృ|సఙ్గ్రహీతృ|ಸಂ-ಗ್ರಹೀತೃ|ಸಂಗ್ರಹೀತೃ|ಸಂ|ಗ್ರಹೀತೃ|ಸಙ್ಗ್ರಹೀತೃ|saṃ-grahītṛ|saṃgrahītṛ|saṃ|grahītṛ|saM-grahiitR|saMgrahiitR|saM|grahiitR|saM-graheetR|saMgraheetR|graheetR|sangrahiitR|sangraheetR|saM-grahiitRi|saMgrahiitRi|grahiitRi|saM-grahiitRu|saMgrahiitRu|grahiitRu|saM-grahiitRRi|saMgrahiitRRi|grahiitRRi|sangrahiitRi|sangrahiitRu|sangrahiitRRi|_vei_120@3433|_vei2_pn416
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3432">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3434">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn415">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn416">2:416</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn418">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃ-grahītṛ</b> is found in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203433"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203433">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203433"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203433">2</a></sup> He is an official who figures among the <b>Ratnins</b> of the king. The sense of ‘charioteer’ seems adequate for every passage, but Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203433"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203433">3</a></sup> in some passages inclines to think that the meaning is ‘treasurer’ of the king.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203433"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203433">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 9, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 4; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 5; iv. 3, 8 (as a <a class="vei_120a" href="रत्निन्">ratnin</a>) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शतरुद्रिय">śatarudriya</a> in the plural <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 4, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 13; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9, 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 26.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203433"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203433">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 3, 5; 9, 6; iii. 8, 5, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii, 25, 6. Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 1, 8; 4, 3, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203433"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203433">↑</a> on <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 9, 2, and optionally on i. 8, 16; but as charioteer on i. 8, 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 10, 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 63, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

सं-ग्राम|संग्राम|सं|ग्राम|सङ्ग्राम|సం-గ్రామ|సంగ్రామ|సం|గ్రామ|సఙ్గ్రామ|ಸಂ-ಗ್ರಾಮ|ಸಂಗ್ರಾಮ|ಸಂ|ಗ್ರಾಮ|ಸಙ್ಗ್ರಾಮ|saṃ-grāma|saṃgrāma|saṃ|grāma|saM-graama|saMgraama|saM|graama|sangraama|_vei_120@3434|_vei2_pn416
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3433">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3435">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn415">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn416">2:416</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn418">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃ-grāma</b> denotes primarily, it seems, ‘assembly’ either in peace<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203434">1</a></sup> or in war,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203434">2</a></sup> when it means an ‘armed band.’ Its normal sense in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203434">3</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203434">4</a></sup> is ‘war,’ ‘battle.’ </p><p>Little is known of Vedic warfare, but it seems to have been simple. A body of foot soldiers with charioteers composed every army, the two going together,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203434">5</a></sup> and the foot soldiers being often overthrown by the charioteers,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203434">6</a></sup> who were doubtless the Kṣatriyas and their foremost retainers. Probably the foot soldiers bore little armour, and used only the bow for offence, as is suggested by the account that Herodotus gives of the Indian contingent of the army with which Xerxes invaded Greece.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203434">7</a></sup> The nobles, on the other hand, may have had cuirass (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्मन्">varman</a></b>), helmet (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="शिप्रा">śiprā</a></b>), and hand-guard (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="हस्तघ्न">hastaghna</a></b>) as a protection from the friction of the bowstring. On the car was the charioteer, and on his left the warrior (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="सारथि">sārathi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सव्यष्ठा">savyaṣṭhā</a></b>). Riding is never mentioned in war,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203434">8</a></sup> and would hardly have been suited to Vedic ideas, for the warrior mainly depended on his bow, which he could not have used effectively from horseback. The offensive weapon (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="आयुध">āyudha</a></b>) was practically the bow; spear and sword and axe were very seldom used.</p><p>Whether there was a strict tribal organization of the host, such as is once alluded to in the Homeric poems,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203434">9</a></sup> and is also recognized in Germany by Tacitus,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203434">10</a></sup> is uncertain (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="व्रात">vrāta</a></b>), but in the Epic relations (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="ज्ञाति">jñāti</a></b>) fight together,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203434">11</a></sup> and this rule, no doubt, applied more or less in Vedic times also.</p><p>Cities were besieged and invested (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="उप">upa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="सद्">sad</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="प्र">pra</a>-bhid</i>),<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1203434">12</a></sup> probably as a rule by blockade, since the ineffective means of assault of the time would have rendered storming difficult and expensive. Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1203434">13</a></sup> thinks that the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पुर्">pur</a> cariṣṇū</i> of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1203434">14</a></sup> was a kind of chariot; it may--like the Trojan horse--have been an Indian anticipation of the Roman means of assaulting a town.</p><p>Besides ordinary wars of defence and conquest, raids into  neighbouring territory seem to have been frequent and normal,<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1203434">15</a></sup> no doubt because of the booty (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="उदाज">udāja</a>, Nirāja</b>) which was to be won, and which the king had to share with the people.</p><p>Banners (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="ध्वज">dhvaja</a></b>) were borne in war, and musical instruments (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="दुन्दुभि">dundubhi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="बकुर">bakura</a></b>)<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1203434">16</a></sup> were used by the combatants.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203434">↑</a> Av. xii. 1, 56, where it is joined with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="समिति">samiti</a>.</b> We might see in this passage, and that cited in n. 2, the technical name of the village assembly as opposed to the larger assemblies of the people, but there is no good warrant for so doing.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203434">↑</a> Av. iv. 24, 7, where <i>saṃgrāmān</i> is read; but the parallel passages (<a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 7, 15, 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 16, 5) have <i>saṃgrāmani.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203434">↑</a> v. 21, 7; xi. 9, 26.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203434">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Samhitā, ii. 1, 3, 1; 8, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203434">↑</a> Rv. ii. 12, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203434">↑</a> Av. vii. 62, 1. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मुष्टिहन्">muṣṭihan</a></b></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203434">↑</a> Herodotus, vii. 65.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203434">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 296, where he admits riding to be mentioned elsewhere; Whitney, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 3, 312.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203434">↑</a> <i>Ibid,</i> ii. 362.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203434">↑</a> <i>Germania,</i> 7.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203434">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 193.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1203434">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 3, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 4, 3-5; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 23, 2, etc.; Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 2, 7; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 300, n.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1203434">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i> 3, 289, n.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1203434">↑</a> viii. 1, 2-8, where it is attributed to the demon Śuṣṇa.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1203434">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. x. 142, 4, as interpreted by Sāyaṇa and by Hillebrandt, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 2, 64, n. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 4, 1 (of the model <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a> kings).</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1203434"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1203434">↑</a> So, later, Arrian, <i>Indica,</i> vii. 9. The shouts of either side are shown in the word <i>krandas</i> (Rv. ii. 12, 8; <i>cf.</i> vi. 25, 6; x. 121, 6), which came to mean the ‘shouting host.’ <i>Cf.</i> also Tacitus, <i>Germania,</i> 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 469472; Weber, Proceedings of the Berlin Academy, 1898, 564; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 293-301. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इषु">iṣu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="धन्वन्">dhanvan</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a>.</b> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 281 <i>et seq.,</i> gives a full account of the later Epic armour and warfare. See also his note, <i>ibid.,</i> 15, 265, 266. For sacrifice in battle, <i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>.</b></li></ol></div>

सं-घात|संघात|सं|घात|सङ्घात|సం-ఘాత|సంఘాత|సం|ఘాత|సఙ్ఘాత|ಸಂ-ಘಾತ|ಸಂಘಾತ|ಸಂ|ಘಾತ|ಸಙ್ಘಾತ|saṃ-ghāta|saṃghāta|saṃ|ghāta|saM-ghaata|saMghaata|saM|ghaata|sanghaata|_vei_120@3435|_vei2_pn418
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3434">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3436">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn416">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn418">2:418</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn419">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃ-ghāta</b> seems in a few passages<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203435">1</a></sup> to have the sense of ‘battle.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203435"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203435">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxix. 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, i. 16; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 4, 18.</li></ol></div>

सचिव|సచివ|ಸಚಿವ|saciva|sachiva|_vei_120@3436|_vei2_pn418
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3435">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3437">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn416">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn418">2:418</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn419">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saciva</b> ‘companion,’ ‘attendant’ (from <i>sac,</i> ‘follow’), later a common word for the comrade of a king, his minister, is found in Vedic literature in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 20, 1), where it is used by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> of the Maruts. It seems to correspond in sense to the German <i>comes</i> or the English <i>gesith.</i><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203436"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203436">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203436"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203436">↑</a> Stubbs, <i>Select Charters,</i> 57.</li></ol></div>

स-जात|सजात|जात|స-జాత|సజాత|జాత|ಸ-ಜಾತ|ಸಜಾತ|ಜಾತ|sa-jāta|sajāta|jāta|sa-jaata|sajaata|jaata|_vei_120@3437|_vei2_pn418
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3436">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3438">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn416">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn418">2:418</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn419">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sa-jāta</b> (‘born together’) is found once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203437">1</a></sup> and very often later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203437">2</a></sup> The word must clearly mean a ‘relative,’ and then more widely a man of the same position or rank, but the senses cannot be distinguished, so much do they merge into each other. The Sajātas of a king are of course princes;<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203437">3</a></sup> of an  ordinary man, Vaiśyas;<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203437">4</a></sup> of a military man, Kṣatriyas. But there is no clear reference to caste as in the later Sajāti<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203437">5</a></sup> (‘man of the same caste’). The disputes of Sajātas were notorious.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203437">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203437">↑</a> i. 109, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203437">↑</a> Av. i. 9, 3; 19, 3; ii. 6, 4; iii. 3, 6; vi. 5, 2; 73, 1; xi. 1, 6, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 3, 2; 2, 1, 2; 6, 9, 7; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 12, 13; xii. 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, v. 23; x. 29; xxvii. 5, and often in the Brāhmaṇas.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203437">↑</a> Av. iii. 3, 4. 6; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 188.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203437">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 4, 19 (the Sajātas of a <b>Grāmaṇī</b>).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203437">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>, ix. 87; x. 41, etc. The abstract <i>sajātya</i> (‘kinship’) is found in Rv. ii. 1, 5; iii. 54, 16; viii. 18, 19; 20, 21; 27, 10; x. 64, 13; but even it has no definite caste reference.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203437"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203437">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 7, 12, 2.</li></ol></div>

सं-चर|संचर|सं|चर|सञ्चर|సం-చర|సంచర|సం|చర|సఞ్చర|ಸಂ-ಚರ|ಸಂಚರ|ಸಂ|ಚರ|ಸಞ್ಚರ|saṃ-cara|saṃcara|saṃ|cara|saM-chara|saMchara|saM|chara|sanchara|_vei_120@3438|_vei2_pn419
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3437">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3439">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn418">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn419">2:419</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn420">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃ-cara</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203438"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203438">1</a></sup> has the sense of the ‘path’ of animals. Normally it is the term designating the ‘passage’ or ‘space’ on the sacrificial ground used or occupied by the several persons taking part in the rite.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203438"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203438">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203438"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203438">↑</a> v. 4, 3, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203438"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203438">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 9, 2, 4; iii. 1, 3, 28; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 7, 11; Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 3, 42, etc.</li></ol></div>

सं-ज्ञान|संज्ञान|सं|ज्ञान|सञ्ज्ञान|సం-జ్ఞాన|సంజ్ఞాన|సం|జ్ఞాన|సఞ్జ్ఞాన|ಸಂ-ಜ್ಞಾನ|ಸಂಜ್ಞಾನ|ಸಂ|ಜ್ಞಾನ|ಸಞ್ಜ್ಞಾನ|saṃ-jñāna|saṃjñāna|saṃ|jñāna|saM-j~naana|saMj~naana|saM|j~naana|sanj~naana|_vei_120@3439|_vei2_pn419
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3438">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3440">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn418">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn419">2:419</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn420">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃ-jñāna,</b> ‘concord,’ ‘harmony,’ is mentioned from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203439"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203439">1</a></sup> onwards<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203439"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203439">2</a></sup> as a matter of great consequence; the Atharvaveda contains many spells to bring it about. The lack of peace in the Vedic village was almost inevitable in view of its small size and the economic interdependence of its inhabitants. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भ्रातृव्य">bhrātṛvya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203439"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203439">↑</a> x. 19, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203439"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203439">↑</a> Av. iii. 30, 4; vii. 52, 1; xi. 1, 26, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 2, 3, 2; 3, 1, 14; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxvi. 1; xxx. 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iv. 21, etc.</li></ol></div>

सत|సత|ಸತ|sata|_vei_120@3440|_vei2_pn419
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3439">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3441">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn418">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn419">2:419</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn420">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sata</b> is the name of a vessel of some kind mentioned in the ritual.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203440"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203440">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203440"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203440">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 27, 88; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 7, 2, 13; 8, 3, 14.</li></ol></div>

सतीन-कंकत|सतीनकंकत|सतीन|कंकत|सतीन-कङ्कत|सतीनकङ्कत|कङ्कत|సతీన-కంకత|సతీనకంకత|సతీన|కంకత|సతీన-కఙ్కత|సతీనకఙ్కత|కఙ్కత|ಸತೀನ-ಕಂಕತ|ಸತೀನಕಂಕತ|ಸತೀನ|ಕಂಕತ|ಸತೀನ-ಕಙ್ಕತ|ಸತೀನಕಙ್ಕತ|ಕಙ್ಕತ|satīna-kaṅkata|satīnakaṅkata|satīna|kaṅkata|satiina-kaMkata|satiinakaMkata|satiina|kaMkata|sateena-kaMkata|sateenakaMkata|sateena|satiina-kankata|satiinakankata|kankata|sateena-kankata|sateenakankata|_vei_120@3441|_vei2_pn419
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3440">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3442">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn418">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn419">2:419</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn420">↷</a><hr><p><b>Satīna-kaṅkata</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203441">1</a></sup> is, in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203441">2</a></sup> the name of some animal, according to Sāyaṇa an ‘aquatic snake.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203441">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203441">↑</a> The literal meaning seems to be ‘having a real comb.’</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203441">↑</a> i. 191, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203441"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203441">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 98.</li></ol></div>

सत्य-काम|सत्यकाम|सत्य|काम|సత్య-కామ|సత్యకామ|సత్య|కామ|ಸತ್ಯ-ಕಾಮ|ಸತ್ಯಕಾಮ|ಸತ್ಯ|ಕಾಮ|satya-kāma|satyakāma|satya|kāma|satya-kaama|satyakaama|kaama|_vei_120@3442|_vei2_pn420
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3441">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3443">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn419">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn420">2:420</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn421">↷</a><hr><p><b>Satya-kāma</b> (‘lover of truth’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जाबाल">jābāla</a></b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जबाला">jabālā</a></b>) is the name of a teacher, the son of a slave girl by an unknown father. He was initiated as a <b>Brahmacārin,</b> or religious student, by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="हारिद्रुमत">hāridrumata</a></b> according to the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203442">1</a></sup> He is often cited as an authority in that <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203442">2</a></sup> and in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203442">3</a></sup> where he learns a certain doctrine from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जानकि">jānaki</a> Āyasthūṇa.</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203442">4</a></sup> He is also mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203442">5</a></sup> and the Śatapatha Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203442">6</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203442">↑</a> iv. 4, 1, <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203442">↑</a> iv. 5, 1; 6, 2; 7, 2; 8, 2; 9, 10; 10, 1; v. 2, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203442">↑</a> iv. 1, 14 (Mādhyaṃdina = iv. 1, 6 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203442">↑</a> vi. 3, 19 (= vi. 3, 12).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203442">↑</a> viii. 7, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203442"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203442">↑</a> xiii. 5, 3, 1.</li></ol></div>

सत्य-यज्ञ|सत्ययज्ञ|सत्य|यज्ञ|సత్య-యజ్ఞ|సత్యయజ్ఞ|సత్య|యజ్ఞ|ಸತ್ಯ-ಯಜ್ಞ|ಸತ್ಯಯಜ್ಞ|ಸತ್ಯ|ಯಜ್ಞ|satya-yajña|satyayajña|satya|yajña|satya-yaj~na|satyayaj~na|yaj~na|_vei_120@3443|_vei2_pn420
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3442">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3444">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn419">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn420">2:420</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn421">↷</a><hr><p><b>Satya-yajña</b> (‘true sacrificer’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पौलुषि">pauluṣi</a></b> (‘descendant of <b>Puluṣa</b>’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राचीनयोग्य">prācīnayogya</a></b> (‘descendant of Prācīnayoga’) is the name of a teacher in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203443"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203443">1</a></sup> the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203443"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203443">2</a></sup> and the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203443"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203443">3</a></sup> In the latter text he is said to have been the pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुलुष प्राचीनयोग्य">puluṣa prācīnayogya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203443"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203443">↑</a> x. 6, 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203443"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203443">↑</a> v. 11, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203443"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203443">↑</a> iii. 40, 2 (in a <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a>, ‘list of teachers’).</li></ol></div>

सत्य-वचस्|सत्य-वच|सत्यवचस्|सत्यवच|सत्य|वचस्|वच|సత్య-వచస్|సత్య-వచ|సత్యవచస్|సత్యవచ|సత్య|వచస్|వచ|ಸತ್ಯ-ವಚಸ್|ಸತ್ಯ-ವಚ|ಸತ್ಯವಚಸ್|ಸತ್ಯವಚ|ಸತ್ಯ|ವಚಸ್|ವಚ|satya-vacas|satya-vaca|satyavacas|satyavaca|satya|vacas|vaca|satya-vachas|satya-vacha|satyavachas|satyavacha|vachas|vacha|_vei_120@3444|_vei2_pn420
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3443">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3445">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn419">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn420">2:420</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn421">↷</a><hr><p><b>Satya-vacas</b> (‘true-speaking’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राथीतर">rāthītara</a></b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथीतर">rathītara</a></b>’) is, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (i. 9, 1), the name of a teacher who insisted on the importance of truth.</p></div>

सत्य-श्रवस्|सत्य-श्रव|सत्यश्रवस्|सत्यश्रव|सत्य|श्रवस्|श्रव|సత్య-శ్రవస్|సత్య-శ్రవ|సత్యశ్రవస్|సత్యశ్రవ|సత్య|శ్రవస్|శ్రవ|ಸತ್ಯ-ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಸತ್ಯ-ಶ್ರವ|ಸತ್ಯಶ್ರವಸ್|ಸತ್ಯಶ್ರವ|ಸತ್ಯ|ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಶ್ರವ|satya-śravas|satya-śrava|satyaśravas|satyaśrava|satya|śravas|śrava|satya-shravas|satya-shrava|satyashravas|satyashrava|shravas|shrava|_vei_120@3445|_vei2_pn420
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3444">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3446">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn419">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn420">2:420</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn421">↷</a><hr><p><b>Satya-śravas</b> (‘of true renown’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वाय्य">vāyya</a></b> (‘descendant of Vayya’) is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203445"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203445">1</a></sup> Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203445"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203445">2</a></sup> thinks that he was the son of <b>Sunītha <a class="vei_120a" href="शौचद्रथ">śaucadratha</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203445"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203445">↑</a> v. 79, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203445"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203445">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 156.</li></ol></div>

सत्य-हविस्|सत्य-हवि|सत्यहविस्|सत्यहवि|सत्य|हविस्|हवि|సత్య-హవిస్|సత్య-హవి|సత్యహవిస్|సత్యహవి|సత్య|హవిస్|హవి|ಸತ್ಯ-ಹವಿಸ್|ಸತ್ಯ-ಹವಿ|ಸತ್ಯಹವಿಸ್|ಸತ್ಯಹವಿ|ಸತ್ಯ|ಹವಿಸ್|ಹವಿ|satya-havis|satya-havi|satyahavis|satyahavi|satya|havis|havi|havis|havi|_vei_120@3446|_vei2_pn420
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3445">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3447">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn419">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn420">2:420</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn421">↷</a><hr><p><b>Satya-havis</b> is the name of a mythical <a class="vei_120a" href="अध्वर्यु">adhvaryu</a>, or sacrificial priest, in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā (i. 9, 1, 5).</p></div>

सत्याधिवाक चैत्ररथि|సత్యాధివాక చైత్రరథి|ಸತ್ಯಾಧಿವಾಕ ಚೈತ್ರರಥಿ|satyādhivāka caitrarathi|satyaadhivaaka chaitrarathi|_vei_120@3447|_vei2_pn420
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3446">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3448">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn419">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn420">2:420</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn421">↷</a><hr><p><b>Satyādhivāka Caitrarathi</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चित्ररथ">citraratha</a></b>’) is the name of a man in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 39, 1). </p></div>

सत्वन्|సత్వన్|ಸತ್ವನ್|satvan|_vei_120@3448|_vei2_pn421
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3447">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3449">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn420">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn421">2:421</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">↷</a><hr><p><b>Satvan</b> in the <b>Rigveda,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203448">1</a></sup> and occasionally later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203448">2</a></sup> has the sense of ‘warrior.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203448">↑</a> i. 133, 6; 173, 5; ii. 25, 4; 30, 10; iii. 49, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203448"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203448">↑</a> v. 20, 8; vi. 65, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 8. 20, etc.</li></ol></div>

सत्वन्त्|సత్వన్త్|ಸತ್ವನ್ತ್|satvant|_vei_120@3449|_vei2_pn421
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3448">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3450">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn420">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn421">2:421</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">↷</a><hr><p><b>Satvant</b> is the name of a people who are stated in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203449">1</a></sup> to belong to the south. In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203449">2</a></sup> the defeat by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a></b> of the Satvants, and his taking away the horse which they had prepared for an <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’), are referred to: this reference clearly shows that in another passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203449">3</a></sup> the text must be altered from <i>satvanām</i> to <i>Satvatām,</i> ‘of the Satvants,’ against whom it seems the Bharatas made regular raids. The name has also been found by the St. Petersburg Dictionary, Cowell, and Max Müller in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203449">4</a></sup> but it is certain<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203449">5</a></sup> that the reading there is not <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सत्वन्">satvan</a>-Matsyeṣu,</i> but <i>sa-<a class="vei_120a" href="वश">vaśa</a>-Matsyeṣu.</i></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203449">↑</a> viii. 14, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203449">↑</a> xiii. 5, 4, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203449">↑</a> ii. 25, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203449">↑</a> iv. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203449"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203449">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 393, n., correcting Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 1, lxxvii.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 211, 212, 419; 9, 254; Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1908, 367.</li></ol></div>

सदन|సదన|ಸದನ|sadana|_vei_120@3450|_vei2_pn421
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3449">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3451">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn420">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn421">2:421</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sadana.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गृह">gṛha</a>.</b></p></div>

सदंदि|सदन्दि|సదంది|సదన్ది|ಸದಂದಿ|ಸದನ್ದಿ|sadaṃdi|sadaMdi|sadandi|_vei_120@3451|_vei2_pn421
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3450">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3452">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn420">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn421">2:421</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sadaṃdi.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तक्मन्">takman</a>.</b></p></div>

सदस्|सद|సదస్|సద|ಸದಸ್|ಸದ|sadas|sada|_vei_120@3452|_vei2_pn421
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3451">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3453">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn420">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn421">2:421</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sadas.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गृह">gṛha</a>.</b></p></div>

सदस्य|సదస్య|ಸದಸ್ಯ|sadasya|_vei_120@3453|_vei2_pn421
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3452">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3454">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn420">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn421">2:421</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sadasya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋत्विज्">ṛtvij</a>.</b></p></div>

सदा-नीरा|सदानीरा|सदा|नीरा|సదా-నీరా|సదానీరా|సదా|నీరా|ಸದಾ-ನೀರಾ|ಸದಾನೀರಾ|ಸದಾ|ನೀರಾ|sadā-nīrā|sadānīrā|sadā|nīrā|sadaa-niiraa|sadaaniiraa|sadaa|niiraa|sadaa-neeraa|sadaaneeraa|neeraa|_vei_120@3454|_vei2_pn421
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3453">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3455">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn420">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn421">2:421</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sadā-nīrā,</b> ‘having water always’ (‘perennial’), is the name of a stream which, according to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203454"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203454">1</a></sup> was the boundary between the <b>Kosalas</b> and the <b>Videhas.</b> The river is identified by the native lexicographers with the Karatoyā,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203454"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203454">2</a></sup> but this seems to be too far east. Weber's<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203454"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203454">3</a></sup> identification  of it with the Gaṇḍakī<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203454"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203454">4</a></sup> is probably correct; for though the Mahābhārata<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203454"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203454">5</a></sup> distinguishes the two rivers, there is nothing to show that this is due to any good tradition.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203454"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203454">↑</a> i. 4, 1, 14 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203454"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203454">↑</a> See <i>Imperial Gazetteer of India,</i> 15, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203454"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203454">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 172, 181.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203454"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203454">↑</a> See <i>s.v.</i> Great Gandak, <i>Imperial Gazetteer of India,</i> 12, 125.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203454"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203454">↑</a> ii. 794.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 398, n.</li></ol></div>

सदा-पृण|सदापृण|सदा|पृण|సదా-పృణ|సదాపృణ|సదా|పృణ|ಸದಾ-ಪೃಣ|ಸದಾಪೃಣ|ಸದಾ|ಪೃಣ|sadā-pṛṇa|sadāpṛṇa|sadā|pṛṇa|sadaa-pRNa|sadaapRNa|sadaa|pRNa|sadaa-pRiNa|sadaapRiNa|pRiNa|sadaa-pRuNa|sadaapRuNa|pRuNa|sadaa-pRRiNa|sadaapRRiNa|pRRiNa|_vei_120@3455|_vei2_pn422
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3454">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3456">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn421">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">2:422</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn423">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sadā-pṛṇa</b> is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203455"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203455">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203455"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203455">↑</a> v. 44, 12. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 139.</li></ol></div>

सद्यन्|సద్యన్|ಸದ್ಯನ್|sadyan|_vei_120@3456|_vei2_pn422
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3455">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3457">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn421">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">2:422</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn423">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sadyan</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (ii. 8, 6, 1) is a misreading of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सघन्">saghan</a>.</b></p></div>

सध्रि|సధ్రి|ಸಧ್ರಿ|sadhri|_vei_120@3457|_vei2_pn422
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3456">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3458">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn421">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">2:422</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn423">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sadhri</b> is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203457">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203457"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203457">↑</a> v. 44, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 139.</li></ol></div>

सनक|సనక|ಸನಕ|sanaka|_vei_120@3458|_vei2_pn422
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3457">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3459">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn421">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">2:422</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn423">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sanaka</b> occurs as the name of one of the two <b>Kāpyas</b> (the other being <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नवक">navaka</a></b>) who took part in the sacrifice of the <b>Vibhindukīyas,</b> which is mentioned in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203458">1</a></sup> Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203458">2</a></sup> thinks that the Sanakas are referred to as non-sacrificers in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203458">3</a></sup> but this is very doubtful.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203458">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203458">↑</a> iii. 233 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 38).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203458">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 147.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203458">↑</a> i. 33, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203458"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203458">↑</a> Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 189.</li></ol></div>

सनग|సనగ|ಸನಗ|sanaga|_vei_120@3459|_vei2_pn422
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3458">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3460">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn421">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">2:422</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn423">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sanaga.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सनातन">sanātana</a>.</b></p></div>

सनत्-कुमार|सनत्कुमार|सनत्|कुमार|సనత్-కుమార|సనత్కుమార|సనత్|కుమార|ಸನತ್-ಕುಮಾರ|ಸನತ್ಕುಮಾರ|ಸನತ್|ಕುಮಾರ|sanat-kumāra|sanatkumāra|sanat|kumāra|sanat-kumaara|sanatkumaara|kumaara|_vei_120@3460|_vei2_pn422
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3459">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3461">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn421">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">2:422</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn423">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sanat-kumāra</b> is the name of a mythical sage in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vii. 1, 1; 26, 2).</p></div>

सन-श्रुत|सनश्रुत|सन|श्रुत|సన-శ్రుత|సనశ్రుత|సన|శ్రుత|ಸನ-ಶ್ರುತ|ಸನಶ್ರುತ|ಸನ|ಶ್ರುತ|sana-śruta|sanaśruta|sana|śruta|sana-shruta|sanashruta|shruta|_vei_120@3461|_vei2_pn422
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3460">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3462">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn421">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">2:422</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn423">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sana-śruta</b> (‘famed of old’) <b>Ariṃdama</b> (‘tamer of foes’) is mentioned as a <a class="vei_120a" href="महाराज">mahārāja</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vii. 34, 9).</p></div>

सनाच्-छव|सनाच्छव|सनाच्|छव|సనాచ్-ఛవ|సనాచ్ఛవ|సనాచ్|ఛవ|ಸನಾಚ್-ಛವ|ಸನಾಚ್ಛವ|ಸನಾಚ್|ಛವ|sanāc-chava|sanācchava|sanāc|chava|sanaach-Chava|sanaachChava|sanaach|Chava|_vei_120@3462|_vei2_pn422
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3461">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3463">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn421">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">2:422</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn423">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sanāc-chava</b> is perhaps the proper name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203462"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203462">1</a></sup> The Kapiṣṭhala Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203462"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203462">2</a></sup> has <i>Śahanāśchiva.</i> Very probably the reading of both texts is bad. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203462"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203462">↑</a> xx. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203462"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203462">↑</a> xxxi. 3 (von Schroeder, <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, 2, 18, n. 5).</li></ol></div>

सनातन|సనాతన|ಸನಾತನ|sanātana|sanaatana|_vei_120@3463|_vei2_pn423
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3462">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3464">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn423">2:423</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn424">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sanātana</b> is the name of a mythical <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā. In the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203463">2</a></sup> he appears in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) as the pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सनग">sanaga</a></b> and the teacher of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सनारु">sanāru</a>,</b> both equally mythical persons.</p><p>^1 iv. 3, 3, 1.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="2"><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203463"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203463">↑</a> ii. 5, 22; iv. 5, 28 (Mādhyaṃdina = ii. 6, 3; iv. 6, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

सनारु|సనారు|ಸನಾರು|sanāru|sanaaru|_vei_120@3464|_vei2_pn423
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3463">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3465">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn423">2:423</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn424">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sanāru.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सनातन">sanātana</a>.</b></p></div>

सनिस्रस|సనిస్రస|ಸನಿಸ್ರಸ|sanisrasa|_vei_120@3465|_vei2_pn423
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3464">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3466">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn423">2:423</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn424">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sanisrasa.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></p></div>

सं-दंश|संदंश|सं|दंश|सन्दंश|సం-దంశ|సందంశ|సం|దంశ|సన్దంశ|ಸಂ-ದಂಶ|ಸಂದಂಶ|ಸಂ|ದಂಶ|ಸನ್ದಂಶ|saṃ-daṃśa|saṃdaṃśa|saṃ|daṃśa|saM-daMsha|saMdaMsha|saM|daMsha|sandaMsha|_vei_120@3466|_vei2_pn423
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3465">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3467">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn423">2:423</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn424">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃ-daṃśa.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गृह">gṛha</a>.</b></p></div>

सं-दान|संदान|सं|दान|सन्दान|సం-దాన|సందాన|సం|దాన|సన్దాన|ಸಂ-ದಾನ|ಸಂದಾನ|ಸಂ|ದಾನ|ಸನ್ದಾನ|saṃ-dāna|saṃdāna|saṃ|dāna|saM-daana|saMdaana|saM|daana|sandaana|_vei_120@3467|_vei2_pn423
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3466">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3468">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn423">2:423</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn424">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃ-dāna</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203467">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203467">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘bond,’ ‘halter,’ or ‘fetter.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203467">↑</a> i. 162, 8. 16.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203467"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203467">↑</a> Av. vi. 103, 1; 104, 1; xi. 9, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 4, 7, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="सत">sata</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पथ">patha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 3, 1, 22, etc.</li></ol></div>

सं-धा|संधा|सं|धा|सन्धा|సం-ధా|సంధా|సం|ధా|సన్ధా|ಸಂ-ಧಾ|ಸಂಧಾ|ಸಂ|ಧಾ|ಸನ್ಧಾ|saṃ-dhā|saṃdhā|saṃ|dhā|saM-dhaa|saMdhaa|saM|dhaa|sandhaa|_vei_120@3468|_vei2_pn423
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3467">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3469">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn423">2:423</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn424">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃ-dhā</b> denotes in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203468">1</a></sup> an ‘agreement’ or ‘compact.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203468"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203468">↑</a> Av. xi. 10, 9, 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 7, 8, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 1, 6; ii. 1, 1, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 1.</li></ol></div>

सं-धि|संधि|सं|धि|सन्धि|సం-ధి|సంధి|సం|ధి|సన్ధి|ಸಂ-ಧಿ|ಸಂಧಿ|ಸಂ|ಧಿ|ಸನ್ಧಿ|saṃ-dhi|saṃdhi|saṃ|dhi|saM-dhi|saMdhi|saM|sandhi|_vei_120@3469|_vei2_pn423
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3468">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3470">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn423">2:423</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn424">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃ-dhi</b> denotes the ‘juncture’ of heaven and earth, the ‘horizon,’ in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203469">1</a></sup> It also has the sense of ‘twilight’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203469">2</a></sup> as the juncture of light and dark.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203469">↑</a> iii. 2, 1, 5; x. 5, 4, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203469"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203469">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 25; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 5, 1; ii. 2, 9, 8; dual; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 3, 55; ix. 4, 4, 13, etc. The later term is Saṃdhyā.</li></ol></div>

सं-नहन|संनहन|सं|नहन|సం-నహన|సంనహన|సం|నహన|ಸಂ-ನಹನ|ಸಂನಹನ|ಸಂ|ನಹನ|saṃ-nahana|saṃnahana|saṃ|nahana|saM-nahana|saMnahana|saM|_vei_120@3470|_vei2_pn423
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3469">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3471">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn422">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn423">2:423</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn424">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃ-nahana</b> in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203470"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203470">1</a></sup> denotes a ‘band’ or ‘rope.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203470"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203470">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 1, 2, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 3, 6; ii. 6, 1, 15, etc.</li></ol></div>

स-पत्न|सपत्न|पत्न|స-పత్న|సపత్న|పత్న|ಸ-ಪತ್ನ|ಸಪತ್ನ|ಪತ್ನ|sa-patna|sapatna|patna|_vei_120@3471|_vei2_pn424
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3470">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3472">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn423">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn424">2:424</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn425">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sa-patna,</b> ‘rival,’ is a common word in the later Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203471"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203471">1</a></sup> being also found in the tenth Maṇḍala of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203471"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203471">2</a></sup> It is a curious masculine formed by analogy from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स-पत्नी">sa-patnī</a>,</b> ‘co-wife,’ and so ‘female rival.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203471"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203471">↑</a> Av. i. 19, 4; x. 6, 30; xii. 2, 46; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 2, 2; iii. 2, 8, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203471"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203471">↑</a> x. 166, 1, etc.; also in the compound <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सपत्न">sapatna</a>-kan,</i> ‘slaying rivals,’ x. 159, 5, etc.; Av. i. 29, 5, etc.</li></ol></div>

स-पत्नी|स-पत्नि|सपत्नी|सपत्नि|पत्नी|पत्नि|స-పత్నీ|స-పత్ని|సపత్నీ|సపత్ని|పత్నీ|పత్ని|ಸ-ಪತ್ನೀ|ಸ-ಪತ್ನಿ|ಸಪತ್ನೀ|ಸಪತ್ನಿ|ಪತ್ನೀ|ಪತ್ನಿ|sa-patnī|sa-patni|sapatnī|sapatni|patnī|patni|sa-patnii|sapatnii|patnii|sa-patnee|sapatnee|patnee|_vei_120@3472|_vei2_pn424
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3471">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3473">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn423">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn424">2:424</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn425">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sa-patnī</b> occurs in the Rigveda in the sense of ‘co-wife’;<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203472"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203472">1</a></sup> in the first and the last Maṇḍalas it means co-wife as a ‘rival.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203472"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203472">2</a></sup> In post-Vedic Sanskrit the word becomes a synonym for ‘rival.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203472"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203472">↑</a> iii. 1, 10; 6, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203472"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203472">↑</a> i. 105, 8; x. 145, 1-5 (<i>cf.</i> in verse 2, <i>patim me kevalam <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>,</i> ‘make my husband exclusively mine’).</li></ol></div>

सप्त-गु|सप्तगु|सप्त|సప్త-గు|సప్తగు|సప్త|ಸಪ್ತ-ಗು|ಸಪ್ತಗು|ಸಪ್ತ|sapta-gu|saptagu|sapta|_vei_120@3473|_vei2_pn424
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3472">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3474">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn423">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn424">2:424</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn425">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sapta-gu</b> is the reputed author of a Rigvedic hymn in a verse of which he is mentioned.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203473"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203473">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203473"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203473">↑</a> x. 47, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 17, 423.</li></ol></div>

सप्त सिन्धवः|సప్త సిన్ధవః|ಸಪ್ತ ಸಿನ್ಧವಃ|sapta sindhavaḥ|sapta sindhavaH|_vei_120@3474|_vei2_pn424
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3473">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3475">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn423">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn424">2:424</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn425">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sapta Sindhavaḥ,</b> ‘the seven rivers,’ occur only once in the Rigveda as the designation of a definite country,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203474">1</a></sup> while elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203474">2</a></sup> the seven rivers themselves are meant. Max Müller<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203474">3</a></sup> thinks that the five streams of the Panjab, with the Indus and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>,</b> are intended; others<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203474">4</a></sup> hold that the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुभा">kubhā</a></b> should be substituted for the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>, or that perhaps the Oxus<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203474">5</a></sup> must originally have been one of the seven. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203474">6</a></sup> is probably right in laying no stress at all on any identifications; ‘seven’ being one of the favourite numbers in the Rigveda and later. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203474">↑</a> viii. 24, 27.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203474">↑</a> Rv. i. 32, 12; 34, 8; 35, 8; 71, 7; 102, 2; iv. 28, 1; viii. 96, 1, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxviii. 26; Atharvaveda, iv. 6, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 6, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203474">↑</a> <i>Chips,</i> 1, 63. <i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 490, n.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203474">↑</a> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 200; Lassen, <i>Indische Alterthumskunde,</i> 1^2, 3; Whitney, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 3, 311.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203474">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Thomas, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1883, 371 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203474"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203474">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 21.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, 278; <i>India, Old and New,</i> 33.</li></ol></div>

सप्त सूर्याः|సప్త సూర్యాః|ಸಪ್ತ ಸೂರ್ಯಾಃ|sapta sūryāḥ|sapta suuryaaH|sapta sooryaaH|_vei_120@3475|_vei2_pn425
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3474">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3476">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn424">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn425">2:425</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn426">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sapta Sūryāḥ,</b> the ‘seven suns’ referred to in the Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203475">1</a></sup> are named in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203475">2</a></sup> as Āroga, Bhrāja, Paṭara, <a class="vei_120a" href="पतंग">pataṅga</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="स्वर्णर">svarṇara</a>, Jyotiṣīmant, and Vibhāsa, but these occur very rarely even later.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203475">3</a></sup> Weber at one time<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203475">4</a></sup> thought that the seven planets (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्रह">graha</a></b>) were meant by the phrase, but later he abandoned the idea.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203475">5</a></sup> Probably the ‘seven rays’ of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203475">6</a></sup> are meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203475">↑</a> Av. xiii. 3, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203475">↑</a> i. 7. <i>Cf.</i> the ‘seven tongues’ of <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> which are mentioned in the Rigveda, and each of which later receives an individual name: Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 89.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203475">↑</a> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 266; Hopkins, <i>Great Epic of India,</i> 475.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203475">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 170; 2, 238.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203475">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 10, 271, n., where he compares the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सप्त">sapta</a> diśo nānā-sūryāḥ,</i> ‘seven regions with various suns,’ of Rv. ix. 114, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203475"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203475">↑</a> Rv. i. 105, 9; viii. 72, 16; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, 277.</li></ol></div>

सप्त-मानुष|सप्तमानुष|सप्त|मानुष|సప్త-మానుష|సప్తమానుష|సప్త|మానుష|ಸಪ್ತ-ಮಾನುಷ|ಸಪ್ತಮಾನುಷ|ಸಪ್ತ|ಮಾನುಷ|sapta-mānuṣa|saptamānuṣa|sapta|mānuṣa|sapta-maanuSha|saptamaanuSha|maanuSha|_vei_120@3476|_vei2_pn425
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3475">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3477">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn424">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn425">2:425</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn426">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sapta-mānuṣa</b> is found in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203476"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203476">1</a></sup> as an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>, ‘belonging to the seven tribes.’ Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203476"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203476">2</a></sup> thinks that this is a reference to the seven ‘family’ books of the Rigveda (ii.-viii.), but this seems less likely than the view of Roth,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203476"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203476">3</a></sup> that <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सप्तमानुष">saptamānuṣa</a></i> is equivalent to <i>vaiśvānara.</i></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203476"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203476">↑</a> viii. 39, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203476"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203476">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, 278.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203476"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203476">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

सप्त-वध्रि|सप्तवध्रि|सप्त|वध्रि|సప్త-వధ్రి|సప్తవధ్రి|సప్త|వధ్రి|ಸಪ್ತ-ವಧ್ರಿ|ಸಪ್ತವಧ್ರಿ|ಸಪ್ತ|ವಧ್ರಿ|sapta-vadhri|saptavadhri|sapta|vadhri|_vei_120@3477|_vei2_pn425
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3476">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3478">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn424">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn425">2:425</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn426">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sapta-vadhri</b> is the name of a protégé of the Aśvins, who appear from several passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203477">1</a></sup> to have rescued him from a tree in which he had got fastened. He is mentioned in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203477">2</a></sup> According to Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203477">3</a></sup> he is identical with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अत्रि">atri</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203477">↑</a> v. 78, 5; viii. 73, 9; x. 39, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203477">↑</a> iv. 29, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203477"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203477">↑</a> <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 190.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 156; Baunack, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 50, 268.</li></ol></div>

सप्ति|సప్తి|ಸಪ್ತಿ|sapti|_vei_120@3478|_vei2_pn425
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3477">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3479">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn424">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn425">2:425</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn426">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sapti</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203478"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203478">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203478"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203478">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘swift steed.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203478"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203478">↑</a> i. 85, 1, 6; 162, 1; ii. 34, 7; iii. 22, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203478"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203478">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxii. 19, 22.</li></ol></div>

सप्त्य|సప్త్య|ಸಪ್ತ್ಯ|saptya|_vei_120@3479|_vei2_pn426
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3478">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3480">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn425">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn426">2:426</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn427">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saptya</b> in one passage of the Rigveda (viii. 41, 4) seems to denote a ‘racecourse.’</p></div>

स-बन्धु|सबन्धु|बन्धु|స-బన్ధు|సబన్ధు|బన్ధు|ಸ-ಬನ್ಧು|ಸಬನ್ಧು|ಬನ್ಧು|sa-bandhu|sabandhu|bandhu|_vei_120@3480|_vei2_pn426
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3479">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3481">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn425">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn426">2:426</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn427">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sa-bandhu</b> (‘of the same kin’) in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203480"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203480">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203480"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203480">2</a></sup> denotes ‘related.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203480"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203480">↑</a> iii. 1, 10; v. 47, 5; viii. 20, 21, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203480"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203480">↑</a> Av. vi. 15, 2; viii. 2, 26; xv. 8, 2. 3, etc.</li></ol></div>

सभा|సభా|ಸಭಾ|sabhā|sabhaa|_vei_120@3481|_vei2_pn426
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3480">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3482">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn425">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn426">2:426</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn427">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sabhā</b> is the name of an ‘assembly’ of the Vedic Indians as well as of the ‘hall’ where they met in assembly. It is often mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203481">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203481">2</a></sup> but its exact character is not certain. The hall was clearly used for dicing,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203481">3</a></sup> presurnably when the assembly was not transacting public business: a dicer is called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सभा-स्थाणु">sabhā-sthāṇu</a>,</i> ‘pillar of the assembly hall,’ doubtless because of his constant presence there.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203481">4</a></sup> The hall also served, like the Homeric , as a meeting-place for social intercourse and general conversation about cows and so forth,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203481">5</a></sup> possibly for debates and verbal contests.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203481">6</a></sup></p><p>According to Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203481">7</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a> was an assembly not of all the people, but of the Brahmins and <b>Maghavans</b> (‘rich patrons’). This view can be supported by the expressions  <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सभेय">sabheya</a>,</i> ‘worthy of the assembly,’ applied to a Brahmin,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203481">8</a></sup> <i>rayiḥ sabhāvān,</i> ‘wealth fitting for the assembly,’<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203481">9</a></sup> and so on. But Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203481">10</a></sup> plausibly sees in these passages a domestic use of <a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a>, which is recognized by the St. Petersburg Dictionary in several passages<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203481">11</a></sup> as relating to a house, not to the assembly at all. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1203481">12</a></sup> is satisfied that the <a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a> was the meetingplace of the village council, presided over by the <b>Grāmaṇī.</b> But of this there is no trace whatever. Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1203481">13</a></sup> seems right in maintaining that the <a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a> and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="समिति">samiti</a></b> cannot be distinguished, and that the reference to well-born (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सु-जात">su-jāta</a></i>)<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1203481">14</a></sup> men being there in session is to the Āryan as opposed to the <a class="vei_120a" href="दास">dāsa</a> or <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>, not to one class of Āryan as opposed to the other. Hillebrandt also sees in <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> ‘of the hall’ (<i>sabhya</i>) a trace of the fire used in sacrifice on behalf of the assembly when it met.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1203481">15</a></sup></p><p>Women did not go to the <a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a>,<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1203481">16</a></sup> for they were, of course, excluded from political activity. For the <a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a> as a courthouse, <i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ग्राम्यवादिन्">grāmyavādin</a>.</b> There is not a single notice of the work done by the <a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203481">↑</a> vi. 28, 6; viii. 4, 9; x. 34, 6. <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="सह">saha</a>,</i> ‘eminent in the assembly,’ x. 71, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203481">↑</a> Av. v. 31, 6; vii. 12, 1, 2; viii. 10, 5; xii. 1, 56; xix. 55, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 7, 6, 7; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 7, 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iii. 45; xvi. 24; xx. 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 10, 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 3, 2, 3; v. 3, 1, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203481">↑</a> Rv. x. 34, 6; Av. v. 31, 6; xii. 3, 46 (here <i><a class="vei_120a" href="द्यूत">dyūta</a></i> is used in place of <a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203481">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 16, 1, with Sāyaṇa's note. Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 172, inclines to see in the formula (Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iii. 45; xx. 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 3, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, ix. 4; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 10, 2) ‘what sin we have committed in the village, the jungle, the Sabhā’ a reference to attacks on the great (Mahīdhara on Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iii. 45), or partiality in deciding disputes (Mahīdhara, <i>ibid.,</i> xx. 17). But it may refer to gambling or other nonpolitical activity, as Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 398, takes it, though he renders it differently, <i>ibid.,</i> 44, 265.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203481">↑</a> Rv. vi. 28, 6. <i>Cf.</i> viii. 4, 9. So in Av. vii. 12, 2, the assembly is hailed as <i>nariṣṭā,</i> ‘merriment.’ But the same hymn (vii. 12, 3) contains a clear reference to serious speech in the <a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a>. For the blending of serious political work and amusement, <i>cf.</i> Tacitus, <i>Germania,</i> 22.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203481">↑</a> So Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 174, takes <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सभेय">sabheya</a></i> in Rv. ii. 24, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203481">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 253256. He quotes for this view Rv. viii. 4, 9; x. 71, 10 (passages which are quite vague). <i>Cf.</i> also Rv. vii. 1, 4; Av. xix. 57, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203481">↑</a> Rv. ii. 24, 13. <i>Cf.</i> i. 91, 20; Av. xx. 128, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxii. 22, etc. Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 276, sees in <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सभेय">sabheya</a></i> the implication of ‘courtly manners,’ but this is rather doubtful; manner is not conspicuous in Vedic society as in Homeric.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203481">↑</a> Rv. iv. 2, 5; in i. 167, 3, <i>sabhāvatī</i> is applied to ‘speech,’ or perhaps to <i><a class="vei_120a" href="योषा">yoṣā</a>,</i> ‘woman.’</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203481">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 19, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203481">↑</a> Av. viii. 10, 5 (where the sense is, however, clearly ‘assembly’; see viii. 10, 6); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 8, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 10, 3; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, viii. 14 (but here the sense is certainly ‘assembly hall,’; see v. 3, 6, where the king is described as going to the assembly hall: <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a>-ga</i>) The exact sense given by the St. Petersburg Dictionary is the ‘society room’ in a dwelling-house.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1203481">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 174. But he ignores Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 4, 14; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 3, 6, which show that the king went to the <a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a> just as much as to the <a class="vei_120a" href="समिति">samiti</a>, and he cannot adduce any passage to show that the Grāmaṇī presided.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1203481">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 123-125.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1203481">↑</a> Rv. vii. 1, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1203481">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a> is <i>sabhya,</i> Av. viii. 10, 5; xix. 55, 6. For the Rv., see iii. 23, 4; v. 3, 11; vii. 7, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1203481"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1203481">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 7, 4.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 172174.</li></ol></div>

सभा-चर|सभाचर|सभा|चर|సభా-చర|సభాచర|సభా|చర|ಸಭಾ-ಚರ|ಸಭಾಚರ|ಸಭಾ|ಚರ|sabhā-cara|sabhācara|sabhā|cara|sabhaa-chara|sabhaachara|sabhaa|chara|_vei_120@3482|_vei2_pn427
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3481">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3483">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn426">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn427">2:427</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn428">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sabhā-cara</b> is one of the victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203482"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203482">1</a></sup> The St. Petersburg  Dictionary thinks it is an adjective equivalent in sense to <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a>-ga,</i>’ ‘going to the assembly.’ As he is dedicated to <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a>, ‘Justice,’ it is difficult not to see in him a member of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a> as a law court, perhaps as one of those who sit to decide cases: there is nothing to show whether the whole assembly did so, or only a chosen body. The special use of <a class="vei_120a" href="सभाचर">sabhācara</a> suggests the latter alternative. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सभासद्">sabhāsad</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203482"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203482">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 2, 1 with Sāyaṇa's note. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 77, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

सभा-पति|सभापति|सभा|पति|సభా-పతి|సభాపతి|సభా|పతి|ಸಭಾ-ಪತಿ|ಸಭಾಪತಿ|ಸಭಾ|ಪತಿ|sabhā-pati|sabhāpati|sabhā|pati|sabhaa-pati|sabhaapati|sabhaa|_vei_120@3483|_vei2_pn428
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3482">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3484">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn427">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn428">2:428</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn429">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sabhā-pati,</b> ‘lord of the assembly,’ is an epithet in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शतरुद्रिय">śatarudriya</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203483"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203483">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203483"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203483">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 24; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 3, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 13, etc.</li></ol></div>

सभा-पाल|सभापाल|सभा|पाल|సభా-పాల|సభాపాల|సభా|పాల|ಸಭಾ-ಪಾಲ|ಸಭಾಪಾಲ|ಸಭಾ|ಪಾಲ|sabhā-pāla|sabhāpāla|sabhā|pāla|sabhaa-paala|sabhaapaala|sabhaa|paala|_vei_120@3484|_vei2_pn428
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3483">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3485">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn427">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn428">2:428</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn429">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sabhā-pāla</b> is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 7, 4, 6), where the sense may be ‘guardian of an assembly hall.’</p></div>

सभाविन्|సభావిన్|ಸಭಾವಿನ್|sabhāvin|sabhaavin|_vei_120@3485|_vei2_pn428
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3484">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3486">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn427">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn428">2:428</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn429">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sabhāvin</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 4, 16, 1) denotes, according to the commentator Sāyaṇa, the ‘keeper of a gambling hall.’</p></div>

सभा-सद्|सभासद्|सभा|सद्|సభా-సద్|సభాసద్|సభా|సద్|ಸಭಾ-ಸದ್|ಸಭಾಸದ್|ಸಭಾ|ಸದ್|sabhā-sad|sabhāsad|sabhā|sad|sabhaa-sad|sabhaasad|sabhaa|_vei_120@3486|_vei2_pn428
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3485">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3487">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn427">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn428">2:428</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn429">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sabhā-sad,</b> ‘sitter in the assembly,’ is probably a technical description of the assessors who decided legal cases in the assembly (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सभाचर">sabhācara</a></b>). The term, which is found in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203486"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203486">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203486"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203486">2</a></sup> cannot well merely denote any member of the assembly. It is also possible that the Sabhāsads, perhaps the heads of families, were expected to be present at the <a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a> oftener than the ordinary man: the meetings of the assembly for justice may have been more frequent than for general discussion and decision.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203486"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203486">↑</a> iii. 29, 1 (of <a class="vei_120a" href="यम">yama</a>); vii. 12, 2; xix. 55, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203486"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203486">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, viii. 7; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 6, 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 1, 26; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 21, 14.</li></ol></div>

सभा-स्थाणु|सभास्थाणु|सभा|स्थाणु|సభా-స్థాణు|సభాస్థాణు|సభా|స్థాణు|ಸಭಾ-ಸ್ಥಾಣು|ಸಭಾಸ್ಥಾಣು|ಸಭಾ|ಸ್ಥಾಣು|sabhā-sthāṇu|sabhāsthāṇu|sabhā|sthāṇu|sabhaa-sthaaNu|sabhaasthaaNu|sabhaa|sthaaNu|_vei_120@3487|_vei2_pn428
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3486">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3488">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn427">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn428">2:428</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn429">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sabhā-sthāṇu.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a>.</b></p></div>

सभेय|సభేయ|ಸಭೇಯ|sabheya|_vei_120@3488|_vei2_pn428
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3487">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3489">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn427">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn428">2:428</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn429">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sabheya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a>.</b> </p></div>

सम्-अंक|समंक|सम्|अंक|सम्-अङ्क|समङ्क|अङ्क|सं|సమ్-అంక|సమంక|సమ్|అంక|సమ్-అఙ్క|సమఙ్క|అఙ్క|సం|ಸಮ್-ಅಂಕ|ಸಮಂಕ|ಸಮ್|ಅಂಕ|ಸಮ್-ಅಙ್ಕ|ಸಮಙ್ಕ|ಅಙ್ಕ|ಸಂ|sam-aṅka|samaṅka|sam|aṅka|sam-aMka|samaMka|aMka|sam-anka|samanka|anka|_vei_120@3489|_vei2_pn429
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3488">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3490">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn428">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn429">2:429</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn430">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sam-aṅka</b> is a word of obscure sense occurring in two passages of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203489"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203489">1</a></sup> Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203489"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203489">2</a></sup> renders it ‘hook’ in the first, and takes it to mean an insect destructive of grain in the other.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203489"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203489">↑</a> i. 12, 2; vi. 50, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203489"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203489">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 7, 142. <i>Cf.</i> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

समन|సమన|ಸಮನ|samana|_vei_120@3490|_vei2_pn429
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3489">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3491">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn428">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn429">2:429</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn430">↷</a><hr><p><b>Samana</b> is a word of somewhat doubtful sense in the Rigveda. Roth<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203490">1</a></sup> renders it either ‘battle’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203490">2</a></sup> or ‘festival.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203490">3</a></sup> Pischel<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203490">4</a></sup> thinks that it was a general popular festivity to which women went to enjoy themselves,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203490">5</a></sup> poets to win fame,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203490">6</a></sup> bowmen to gain prizes at archery,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203490">7</a></sup> horses to run races;<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203490">8</a></sup> and which lasted until morning<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203490">9</a></sup> or until a conflagration, caused by the fires kept burning all night, scattered the celebrators.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203490">10</a></sup> Young women,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203490">11</a></sup> elderly women,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1203490">12</a></sup> sought there to find a husband, and courtezans to make profit of the occasion.<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1203490">13</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203490">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203490">↑</a> Rv. vi. 75, 3. 5; ix. 96, 9; x. 143, 4; Av. vi. 92, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ix. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203490">↑</a> Rv. ii. 16, 7; vi. 60, 2; vii. 2, 5; viii. 12, 9; ix. 97, 47; x. 55, 5; 86, 10; Av. ii. 36, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203490">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 314.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203490">↑</a> Rv. i. 124, 8 (<i>cf.</i> <b>Vrā</b>); iv. 58, 8; vi. 75, 4; vii. 2, 5; x. 86, 10; 168, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203490">↑</a> Rv. ii. 16, 7; ix. 97, 47. <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 38.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203490">↑</a> Rv. vi. 75, 3. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203490">↑</a> Rv. ix. 96, 9; Av. vi. 92, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203490">↑</a> Rv. i. 48, 6, which Roth takes as referring to men going to business.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203490">↑</a> Rv. x. 69, 11. <i>Cf.</i> vii. 9, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203490">↑</a> Av. ii. 36, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1203490">↑</a> Rv. vii. 2, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1203490"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1203490">↑</a> Rv. iv. 58, 8, where, as in vi. 75, 4; x. 168, 2, Roth sees the sense of ‘embrace.’ The parallel with the festivals of Greece, where only young girls were able freely to mix with strangers, and which afforded the basis of so many of the comedies of the later school, is striking (<i>cf.</i> Mahaffy, <i>Greek Literature,</i> 1, 2, 259 <i>et seq.</i>).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 190.</li></ol></div>

समर|సమర|ಸಮರ|samara|_vei_120@3491|_vei2_pn429
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3490">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3492">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn428">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn429">2:429</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn430">↷</a><hr><p><b>Samara</b> in the sense of ‘battle’ is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203491"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203491">1</a></sup> and, according to Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203491"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203491">2</a></sup> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203491"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203491">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203491"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203491">↑</a> vii. 9; Sāṅkhāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 15, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203491"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203491">↑</a> <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 190.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203491"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203491">↑</a> vi. 9, 2 (at the sacrifice; <i>cf. samarya,</i> iv. 24, 8, etc.).</li></ol></div>

समा|సమా|ಸಮಾ|samā|samaa|_vei_120@3492|_vei2_pn429
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3491">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3493">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn428">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn429">2:429</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn430">↷</a><hr><p><b>Samā</b> appears originally to have denoted ‘summer,’ a sense which may be seen in a few passages of the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203492">1</a></sup>  Hence it also denotes more generally ‘season,’ a rare use.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203492">2</a></sup> More commonly it is simply ‘year’;<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203492">3</a></sup> but in one place the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203492">4</a></sup> interprets it in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203492">5</a></sup> as meaning ‘month,’ a doubtful sense.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203492">↑</a> i. 35, 4; ii. 6, 1; iii. 10, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 36.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203492">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 25, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ix. 41.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203492">↑</a> Rv. iv. 57, 7; x. 85, 5; 124, 4; Av. v. 8, 8; vi. 75, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203492">↑</a> vi. 2, 1, 25.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203492"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203492">↑</a> xxvii. 1, with Mahīdhara's note. See Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 168, n. 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 372; Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 301.</li></ol></div>

समान|సమాన|ಸಮಾನ|samāna|samaana|_vei_120@3493|_vei2_pn430
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3492">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3494">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn429">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn430">2:430</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn431">↷</a><hr><p><b>Samāna.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राण">prāṇa</a>.</b></p></div>

समान-गोत्र|समानगोत्र|समान|गोत्र|సమాన-గోత్ర|సమానగోత్ర|సమాన|గోత్ర|ಸಮಾನ-ಗೋತ್ರ|ಸಮಾನಗೋತ್ರ|ಸಮಾನ|ಗೋತ್ರ|samāna-gotra|samānagotra|samāna|gotra|samaana-gotra|samaanagotra|samaana|_vei_120@3494|_vei2_pn430
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3493">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3495">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn429">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn430">2:430</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn431">↷</a><hr><p><b>Samāna-gotra</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203494"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203494">1</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="समान">samāna</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="जन">jana</a></b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203494"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203494">2</a></sup> mean ‘belonging to the same family’ and ‘class’ respectively in the Brāhmaṇas. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="समान">samāna</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="बन्धु">bandhu</a>,</b> ‘having the same kin,’ is found in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203494"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203494">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203494"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203494">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 15.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203494"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203494">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvi. 6, 9; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 2, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203494"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203494">↑</a> i. 113, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 5, 1, 25.</li></ol></div>

समान्त|సమాన్త|ಸಮಾನ್ತ|samānta|samaanta|_vei_120@3495|_vei2_pn430
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3494">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3496">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn429">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn430">2:430</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn431">↷</a><hr><p><b>Samānta</b> (‘having the same boundary’), ‘neighbour,’ and therefore ‘foe,’ occurs in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā (ii. 1, 24).</p></div>

सम्-इति|समिति|सम्|इति|सं|సమ్-ఇతి|సమితి|సమ్|ఇతి|సం|ಸಮ್-ಇತಿ|ಸಮಿತಿ|ಸಮ್|ಇತಿ|ಸಂ|sam-iti|samiti|sam|iti|_vei_120@3496|_vei2_pn430
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3495">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3497">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn429">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn430">2:430</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn431">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sam-iti</b> denotes an ‘assembly’ of the Vedic tribe. It is already mentioned in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203496">1</a></sup> and often later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203496">2</a></sup> sometimes in connexion with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203496">3</a></sup> Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203496">4</a></sup> considers that the <a class="vei_120a" href="समिति">samiti</a> included all the people, primarily the <i>viśaḥ,</i> ‘subjects,’ but also the <b>Maghavans</b> and Brahmins if they desired, though the <a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a> was their special assembly. This view is not probable, nor is that of Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203496">5</a></sup> that the <a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a> was the village assembly. Hillebrandt appears to be right in holding that <a class="vei_120a" href="समिति">samiti</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a> are much the same, the one being the assembly, the other primarily the place of assembly.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203496">6</a></sup> </p><p>The king went to the assembly<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203496">7</a></sup> just as he went to the <a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a>. That he was elected there, as Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203496">8</a></sup> thinks, is as uncertain as whether he was elected at all (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a></b>). But there are clear signs that concord between king and assembly were essential for his prosperity.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203496">9</a></sup></p><p>It is reasonable to assume that the business of the assembly was general deliberation on policy of all kinds, legislation so far as the Vedic Indian cared to legislate, and judicial work (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सभासद्">sabhāsad</a></b>). But of all these occupations there is, perhaps as a result of the nature of the texts, little or no evidence directly available.</p><p>The gods had a <a class="vei_120a" href="समिति">samiti</a>, hence called <i>daivī,</i> ‘divine,’<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203496">10</a></sup> just as they had a <a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a>.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203496">11</a></sup></p><p>The assembly disappears as an effective part of government in the Buddhist texts,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1203496">12</a></sup> the Epic,<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1203496">13</a></sup> and the law-books.<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1203496">14</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203496">↑</a> i. 95, 8; ix. 92, 6; x. 97, 6; 166, 4; 191, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203496">↑</a> Av. v. 19, 15; vi. 88, 3; vii. 12, 1; xii. 1, 56, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203496">↑</a> Av. vii. 12, 1; xii. 1, 56; xv. 9, 2. 3; viii. 10, 5. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203496">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 253 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203496">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 172 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203496">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 124, n. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203496">↑</a> Rv. ix. 92, 6; x. 97, 6 (where the reference is hardly to an oligarchy, as Zimmer, 176, 177, holds, but merely to the princes of the blood going to the assembly with the rest).</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203496">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 175, quoting Av. vi. 87, 88, with Rv. x. 173, and Av. v. 19, 15, with Av. iii. 4, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203496">↑</a> Av. vi. 88, 3. Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 2, takes <a class="vei_120a" href="समिति">samiti</a> here and in v. 19, 15; Rv. x. 166, 4; 191, 3, to mean ‘union,’ but this is neither necessary nor probable.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203496">↑</a> Rv. x. 11, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203496">↑</a> Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 11, 13, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1203496">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Bühler, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 55, on the Parīsa.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1203496">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 148-152, who traces the decay of the old assembly through the aristocratic war council and the secret priestly conclave. It is, of course, very probable that at no time was the <a class="vei_120a" href="समिति">samiti</a> a place where any or much attention was paid to the views of the common man. Princes and great men spoke; the rest approved or disapproved, as in Homeric times and in Germany (<i>cf.</i> Lang, <i>Anthropology and the Classics,</i> 51 <i>et seq.;</i> Tacitus, <i>Germania,</i> 11, 12, where their general duty of discussion and their criminal jurisdiction are mentioned).</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1203496"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1203496">↑</a> Foy, <i>Die ko7nigliche Gewalt,</i> 6, 7, 10.</li></ol></div>

सम्-इध्|समिध्|सम्|इध्|सं|సమ్-ఇధ్|సమిధ్|సమ్|ఇధ్|సం|ಸಮ್-ಇಧ್|ಸಮಿಧ್|ಸಮ್|ಇಧ್|ಸಂ|sam-idh|samidh|sam|idh|_vei_120@3497|_vei2_pn431
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3496">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3498">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn430">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn431">2:431</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn433">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sam-idh</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203497">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203497">2</a></sup> denotes the ‘fuel’ for kindling fire. Geldner<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203497">3</a></sup> inclines to see in one passage<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203497">4</a></sup> the name of a priest, the later Agnidh.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203497">↑</a> iv. 4, 15; vi. 15, 7; 16, 11; vii. 14, 1; x. 12, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203497">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, iii. 4; xx. 25, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203497">↑</a> <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 191.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203497"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203497">↑</a> Rv. x. 52, 2.</li></ol></div>

सम्-उद्र|समुद्र|सम्|उद्र|सं|సమ్-ఉద్ర|సముద్ర|సమ్|ఉద్ర|సం|ಸಮ್-ಉದ್ರ|ಸಮುದ್ರ|ಸಮ್|ಉದ್ರ|ಸಂ|sam-udra|samudra|sam|udra|_vei_120@3498|_vei2_pn431
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3497">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3499">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn430">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn431">2:431</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn433">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sam-udra</b> (literally ‘gathering of waters’), ‘ocean,’ is a frequent word in the Rigveda and later. It is of importance in  so far as it indicates that the Vedic Indians knew the sea. This is, indeed, denied by Vivien de Saint Martin,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203498">1</a></sup> but not only do Max Müller<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203498">2</a></sup> and Lassen<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203498">3</a></sup> assert it, but even Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203498">4</a></sup> who is inclined to restrict their knowledge of the sea as far as possible, admits it in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203498">5</a></sup> and of course later.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203498">6</a></sup> He points out that the ebb and flow of the sea are unknown, that the mouths of the Indus are never mentioned, that fish is not a known diet in the Rigveda (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मत्स्य">matsya</a></b>), and that in many places <a class="vei_120a" href="समुद्र">samudra</a> is metaphorically used, as of the two oceans,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203498">7</a></sup> the lower and the upper oceans,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203498">8</a></sup> etc. In other passages he thinks that <a class="vei_120a" href="समुद्र">samudra</a> denotes the river Indus when it receives all its Panjab tributaries.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203498">9</a></sup> It is probable that this is to circumscribe too narrowly the Vedic knowledge of the ocean, which was almost inevitable to people who knew the Indus. There are references to the treasures of the ocean,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203498">10</a></sup> perhaps pearls or the gains of trade,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203498">11</a></sup> and the story of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भुज्यु">bhujyu</a></b> seems to allude to marine navigation.</p><p>That there was any sea trade with Babylon in Vedic times cannot be proved: the stress Iaid<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1203498">12</a></sup> on the occurrence in the Hebrew Book of Kings<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1203498">13</a></sup> of <i>qof</i> and <i>tukhiīm,</i> ‘monkey’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="कपि">kapi</a></i>) and ‘peacock,’ is invalidated by the doubtful date of the Book of Kings. There is, besides, little reason to assume an early date for the trade that no doubt developed later, perhaps about 700 B.C.<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1203498">14</a></sup> </p><p>In the later texts <a class="vei_120a" href="समुद्र">samudra</a> repeatedly means the sea.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1203498">15</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203498">↑</a> <i>Étude sur la géographie du Véda,</i> 62 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> Wilson, <i>Rigveda,</i> 1, xii.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203498">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 61 <i>et seq.,</i> quoting Rv. i. 71, 7; 190, 7; v. 78, 8; vii. 49, 2; 95, 2; x. 58.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203498">↑</a> <i>Indische Alterthumskunde,</i> 1^2, 883.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203498">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 22 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 143, 144.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203498">↑</a> vii. 95, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203498">↑</a> Av. iv. 10, 4 (pearl shell); vi. 105, 3 (the outflow, <i>vi-kṣara,</i> of the ocean); xix. 38, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 4, 13, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203498">↑</a> Rv. x. 136, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Av. xi. 5, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203498">↑</a> Rv. vii. 6, 7; x. 98, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203498">↑</a> See, <i>e.g.,</i> Rv. i. 71, 7; iii. 36, 7; 46, 4; v. 85, 6; vi. 36, 3; vii. 95, 2; viii. 16, 2; 44, 25; ix. 88, 6; 107, 9; 108, 16 (where reference is made to streams); or Rv. i. 163, 1; iv. 21, 3; v. 55, 5; viii. 6, 29, where land and <a class="vei_120a" href="समुद्र">samudra</a> are contrasted.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203498">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 47, 6; vii. 6, 7; ix. 97, 44.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203498">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 48, 3; 56, 2; iv. 55, 6; and the general parallelism of the Dioscuri and the Aśvins.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1203498">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> by Weber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 3.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1203498">↑</a> 1. Kings x. 22.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1203498">↑</a> See Kennedy, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1898, 241-288; Bühler, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 79 <i>et seq.; Indisehe Palœographie,</i> 17-19, who much exaggerates the antiquity of the traffic; Vincent Smith, <i>Early History of India,</i> 25, n.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1203498"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1203498">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 4, 8, 2; vii. 5, 1, 2. It is described as unfailing in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 16, 7 (<i>cf.</i> iii. 39, 7); it encircles the earth, <i>ibid.,</i> viii. 25, 1. The eastern and western oceans in Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 3, 11 (<i>cf.</i> x. 6, 4, 1), though metaphorical, probably indicate an acquaintance with both seas, the Indian Ocean and the Arabian Sea.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 14-19; Pischel and Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, xxiii.</li></ol></div>

सम्राज्|సమ్రాజ్|ಸಮ್ರಾಜ್|samrāj|samraaj|_vei_120@3499|_vei2_pn433
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3498">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3500">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn431">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn433">2:433</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn434">↷</a><hr><p><b>Samrāj</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203499">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203499">2</a></sup> means ‘superior ruler, ‘sovereign,’ as expressing a greater degree of power than ‘king’ (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a></b>). In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203499">3</a></sup> in accordance with its curious theory of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वाजपेय">vājapeya</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>,</b> the <a class="vei_120a" href="सम्राज्">samrāj</a> is asserted to be a higher authority than a king, and to have become one by the sacrifice of the <a class="vei_120a" href="वाजपेय">vājapeya</a>. There is, however, no trace of the use of the word as ‘emperor’ in the sense of an ‘overlord of kings,’ probably because political conditions furnished no example of such a status, as for instance was attained in the third century B.C. by Aśoka. At the same time <a class="vei_120a" href="सम्राज्">samrāj</a> denotes an important king like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a></b> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203499">4</a></sup> It is applied in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203499">5</a></sup> as the title of the eastern kings. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राज्य">rājya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203499">↑</a> iii. 55, 7; 56, 5; iv. 21, 1; vi. 27, 8; viii. 19, 32.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203499">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, v. 32; xiii. 35; xx. 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203499">↑</a> v. 1, 1, 13. <i>Cf.</i> xii. 8, 3, 4; xiv. 1, 3, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203499">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 3, 2, 1. 6; 2, 2, 3; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 1, 1; 3, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Über den <a class="vei_120a" href="वाजपेय">vājapeya</a>,</i> 8.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203499"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203499">↑</a> viii. 14, 2. 3. The other names are given as follows: For the northerners it is <a class="vei_120a" href="विराज्">virāj</a>: for the southerners, <a class="vei_120a" href="स्वराज्">svarāj</a>; for the Satvants, <a class="vei_120a" href="भोज">bhoja</a>: for the middle people (<a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चाल">pañcāla</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वश">vaśa</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="उशीनर">uśīnara</a>), <a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a> simply. This is probably a sound tradition.</li></ol></div>

सरघ्|సరఘ్|ಸರಘ್|saragh|_vei_120@3500|_vei2_pn433
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3499">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3501">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn431">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn433">2:433</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn434">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saragh,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203500"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203500">1</a></sup> <b>Saraghā,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203500"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203500">2</a></sup> both denote ‘bee’ in the Brāhmaṇas. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरह्">sarah</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203500"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203500">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 3, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203500"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203500">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxi. 4, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 10, 10, 1.</li></ol></div>

सरयु|సరయు|ಸರಯು|sarayu|_vei_120@3501|_vei2_pn433
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3500">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3502">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn431">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn433">2:433</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn434">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sarayu</b> is mentioned thrice in the Rigveda as the name of river. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चित्ररथ">citraratha</a></b> and Arṇa are said to have been defeated apparently by the <b>Turvaśas</b> and <b>Yadus</b> who crossed the  <a class="vei_120a" href="सरयु">sarayu</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203501">1</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="सरयु">sarayu</a> appears in one passage with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धु">sindhu</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203501">2</a></sup> and in another with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रसा">rasā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="अनितभा">anitabhā</a>,</b> and <b>Kubha.</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203501">3</a></sup> Later, in the post-Vedic period, Sarayū, rarely <a class="vei_120a" href="सरयु">sarayu</a>, is the name of a river in Oudh, the modern Sarjū.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203501">4</a></sup> Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203501">5</a></sup> regards this as the river meant in all the Vedic passages, seeing in the last,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203501">3</a></sup> which may be used as an argument for locating the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरयु">sarayu</a> in the Panjab, a reference to the north-east monsoon as well as to the usual monsoon from the west. Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203501">6</a></sup> thinks that the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरयु">sarayu</a> is to be found in the west, and Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203501">7</a></sup> identifies it with the Kurum (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रुमु">krumu</a></b>). Vivien de St. Martin considered it to be probably identical with the united course of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुतुद्री">śutudrī</a></b> (Sutlej) and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विपाश्">vipāś</a></b> (Beas).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203501">↑</a> iv. 30, 18. This passage gives no help, because the possibility is open either to suppose that the <a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a> are not mentioned as defeating the Āryans <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्ररथ">citraratha</a> and Arṇa; or, if they are, to suppose that they may have come east against the two.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203501">↑</a> x. 64, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203501">↑</a> v. 53, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203501">↑</a> This is a tributary joining the Gogrā, the great river of Oudh, on the left of its upper course. The name Sarjū is also applied to the Gogrā itself below <b>Bahrāmghāt.</b> A branch of the Lower Gogrā, given off on the right, flowing in an old bed of the Gogrā, and falling into the Ganges after passing Balliā, is called the Chhotī (Lesser) Sarjū. <i>Cf. Imperial Gazetteer of India,</i> 22, 109; 12, 302 (Gogrā); 23, 418 (Eastern Tons); 26, Plate 31.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203501">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 17, 45. <i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 2^2, xxv; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 323.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203501">↑</a> v. 53, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203501">↑</a> <i>Religions of India,</i> 34.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203501"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203501">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 280.</li></ol></div>

सरस्|सर|సరస్|సర|ಸರಸ್|ಸರ|saras|sara|_vei_120@3502|_vei2_pn434
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3501">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3503">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn433">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn434">2:434</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn437">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saras</b> denotes ‘lake’ in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203502"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203502">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203502"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203502">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203502"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203502">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 47, 48; xxx. 16.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203502"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203502">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 33, 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 4, 9; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, viii. 5, 3.</li></ol></div>

सरस्वती|सरस्वति|సరస్వతీ|సరస్వతి|ಸರಸ್ವತೀ|ಸರಸ್ವತಿ|sarasvatī|sarasvati|sarasvatii|sarasvatee|_vei_120@3503|_vei2_pn434
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3502">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3504">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn433">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn434">2:434</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn437">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sarasvatī</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203503">1</a></sup> is the name of a river frequently mentioned in the Rigveda and later. In many passages<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203503">2</a></sup> of the later texts it is certain the river meant is the modern <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>, which loses  itself in the sands of Patiala (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विनशन">vinaśana</a></b>). Even Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203503">3</a></sup> admits that this river is intended in some passages of the Rigveda. With the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृषद्वती">dṛṣadvatī</a></b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203503">4</a></sup> it formed the western boundary of <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मावर्त">brahmāvarta</a> (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a></b>). It is the holy stream of early Vedic India. The Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203503">5</a></sup> mention sacrifices held on its banks as of great importance and sanctity.</p><p>In many other passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203503">6</a></sup> and even later,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203503">7</a></sup> Roth held that another river, the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धु">sindhu</a></b> (Indus), was really meant: only thus could it be explained why the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> is called the ‘foremost of rivers’ (<i>nadītamā</i>),<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203503">8</a></sup> is said to go to the ocean,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203503">9</a></sup> and is referred to as a large river, on the banks of which many kings,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203503">10</a></sup> and, indeed, the five tribes, were located.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203503">11</a></sup> This view is accepted by Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1203503">12</a></sup> and others.<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1203503">13</a></sup></p><p>On the other hand, Lassen<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1203503">14</a></sup> and Max Müller<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1203503">15</a></sup> maintain the identity of the Vedic <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> with the later <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>.<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1203503">16</a></sup> The latter is of opinion that in Vedic times the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> was as large a stream as the Sutlej, and that it actually reached the  sea either after union with the Indus or not, being the ‘iron citadel,’ as the last boundary on the west, a frontier of the Panjab against the rest of India. There is no conclusive evidence of there having been any great change in the size or course of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>, though it would be impossible to deny that the river may easily have diminished in size. But there are strong reasons to accept the identification of the later and the earlier <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> throughout. The insistence on the divine character of the river is been in the very hymn<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1203503">17</a></sup> which refers to it as the support of the five tribes, and corresponds well with its later sacredness. Moreover, that hymn alludes to the <b>Pārāvatas,</b> a people shown by the later evidence of the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1203503">18</a></sup> to have been in the east, a very long way from their original home, if <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> means the Indus. Again, the <b>Pūrus,</b> who were settled on the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>,<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1203503">19</a></sup> could with great difficulty be located in the far west. Moreover, the five tribes might easily be held to be on the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>, when they were, as they seem to have been, the western neighbours of the <b>Bharatas</b> in <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरुक्षेत्र">kurukṣetra</a>,</b> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> could easily be regarded as the boundary of the Panjab in that sense. Again, the ‘seven rivers’ in one passage<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1203503">20</a></sup> clearly designate a district: it is most probable that they are not the five rivers with the Indus and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुभा">kubhā</a></b> (Cabul river), but the five rivers, the Indus and the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>. Nor is it difficult to see why the river is said to flow to the sea: either the Vedic poet had never followed the course of the river to its end, or the river did actually penetrate the desert either completely or for a long distance, and only in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> period was its disappear ance in the desert found out. It is said, indeed, in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1203503">21</a></sup> that the five rivers go to the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>, but this passage is not only late (as the use of the word <b><a class="vei_120a" href="देश">deśa</a></b> shows), but it does not say that the five rivers meant are those of the Panjab. Moreover, the passage has neither a parallel in the other Saṃhitās, nor can it possibly be regarded as an early production; if it is late it must refer to the later <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>.</p><p>Hillebrandt,<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1203503">22</a></sup> on the whole, adopts this view of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>,<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1203503">23</a></sup> but he also sees in it, besides the designation of a mythical stream, the later Vaitaraṇī,<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1203503">24</a></sup> as well as the name of the Arghandab in Arachosia.<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1203503">25</a></sup> This opinion depends essentially on his theory that the sixth Maṇḍala of the Rigveda places the scene of its action in Iranian lands, as opposed to the seventh Maṇḍala: it is as untenable as that theory itself.<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_1203503">26</a></sup> Brunnhofer<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_1203503">27</a></sup> at one time accepted the Iranian identification, but later<sup id="ref_120_28_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_1203503">28</a></sup> decided for the Oxus, which is quite out of the question. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्लक्ष प्रास्रवण">plakṣa prāsravaṇa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203503">↑</a> Literally, ‘abounding in pools,’ perhaps with reference to its condition when the water was low. The name corresponds phonetically to the Iranian Haraqaiti (the modern Helmand).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203503">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 2, 1, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 10, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 2, 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 1, 14; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 19, 1. 2; probably Av. vi. 30, 1. This list is according to Roth's view, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 3<i>c.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203503">↑</a> Rv. iii. 23, 4 (where the <a class="vei_120a" href="दृषद्वती">dṛṣadvatī</a> appears); x. 64, 9: 75, 5 (where the Śindhu also is mentioned).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203503">↑</a> Probably the modern Chautang, which flows to the east of Thanesar. <i>Cf.</i> Oldham, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 25, 58; <i>Imperial Gasetteer of India,</i> 26, Plate 32.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203503">↑</a> Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 3, 20; xxiv. 6, 22; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 15, 1; 18, 13; 19, 4; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xii. 6, 2. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiii. 29.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203503">↑</a> i. 89, 3; 164, 19; ii. 41, 16 <i>et seq.;</i> 30, 8; 32, 8; iii. 54, 13; v. 42, 12; 43, 11; 46, 2; vi. 49, 7; 50, 12; 52, 6; vii. 9, 5; 36, 6; 39, 5; 40, 3; viii. 21, 17; 54, 4; x. 17, 7; 30, 12; 131, 5; 184, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203503">↑</a> Av. iv. 4. 6; v. 23, 1; vi. 3, 2; 89, 3; vii. 68, 1; xiv. 2, 15. 20; xvi. 4, 4; xix. 32, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, 1, 8, 13, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 93; xxxiv. 11; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 2, 4; xi. 4, 3, 3; xii. 7, 1, 12; 2, 5; Bṛnadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 3, 8. These passages should all be classed in n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203503">↑</a> Rv. ii. 41, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203503">↑</a> Rv. vi. 61, 2. 8; vii. 96, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203503">↑</a> Rv. viii. 21, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203503">↑</a> Rv. vi. 61, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1203503">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 5-10.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1203503">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 1, 60; 2, 90, etc.; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 201, 202.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1203503">↑</a> <i>Indische Alterthumshunde,</i> 1^2, 118.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1203503">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 60.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1203503">↑</a> In the enumeration of rivers (evidently from east to west) in Rv. x. 75, 5, <a class="vei_120a" href="गंगा">gaṅgā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शुतुद्री">śutudrī</a>, the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> comes between the Jumna and the Sutlej, the position of the modern Sarsūti (Saraswatī), which, flowing to the west of Thanesar, is joined in Patiala territory by a more westerly stream, the Ghaggar, and, passing Sirsa, is lost in the desert at Bhatnair; but a dry river bed (Hakra or Ghaggar) can be traced from that point to the Indus. See <i>Imperial Gasetteer of India,</i> 26, Plate 32. <i>Cf.</i> also Oldham, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 25, 49-76.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1203503">↑</a> Rv. ii. 41, 16 (<i>devitame</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1203503">↑</a> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पारावत">pārāvata</a>,</b> and <i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बृसय">bṛsaya</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1203503">↑</a> Rv. vii. 95, 96. Ludwig, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 3, 175, admits that the Indus cannot be meant here. See Hillebrandt. <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 115.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1203503">↑</a> Rv. viii. 24, 27. The connexion of <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> and the seven rivers is rather vague. In Rv. viii. 54, 4, <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> and the seven rivers are separately invoked, and in vi. 61, 10, 12, she is referred to as ‘seven-sistered’ (<i>saptasvasā</i>). In vii. 36, 6, she is called the ‘seventh,’ which makes the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> one of the rivers. If the former passages are to be treated as precise, then <i>saptasvasā</i> may be considered to show that the <a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a> was outside the river system (which would then be Indus, <a class="vei_120a" href="कुभा">kubhā</a>, and the five rivers of the Panjab; see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सप्त सिन्धवः">sapta sindhavaḥ</a></b>); but the expression may be loosely meant for one of seven sisters.</li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1203503">↑</a> xxxiv. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1203503">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 99 <i>et seq.;</i> 3, 372-378.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1203503">↑</a> He sees this sense in the Rigveda everywhere, except in the passages indicated in notes 24 and 25.</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1203503">↑</a> vii. 95, 6; x. 17, 7; Av. vii. 68, 2; xiv. 2, 20; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxxv. 10, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1203503">↑</a> Rv. vi. 49, 7; 61; possibly Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxiv. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_1203503">↑</a> See <b>Divodāsa.</b></li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_1203503">↑</a> Bezzenberger's <i>Beiträge,</i> 10, 261, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_1203503"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_1203503">↑</a> <i>Iran und Turan,</i> 127.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 337 <i>et seq.,</i> Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 141, 142; <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> pp. 86-88; von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 84, 164.</li></ol></div>

सरह्|సరహ్|ಸರಹ್|sarah|_vei_120@3504|_vei2_pn437
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3503">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3505">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn434">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn437">2:437</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn438">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sarah</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203504"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203504">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203504"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203504">2</a></sup> denotes ‘bee.’ <i>Cf.</i> <b>Saragha.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203504"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203504">↑</a> i. 112, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203504"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203504">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 3, 12, 12; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 3, 1, 4. The stem is given as Saraṭ in the Uṇādisūtra, 1, 133; but <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरघ्">saragh</a></b> shows that <a class="vei_120a" href="सरह्">sarah</a> must be meant (<i>cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> p. 238, n. 2).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 97.</li></ol></div>

सरित्|సరిత్|ಸರಿತ್|sarit|_vei_120@3505|_vei2_pn437
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3504">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3506">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn434">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn437">2:437</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn438">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sarit</b> denotes ‘stream’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203505"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203505">1</a></sup> and later.^2 </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203505"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203505">↑</a> iv. 58, 6; vii. 70, 2; Av. xii. 2, 41; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxiv. 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 2, 1, 11, etc.</li></ol></div>

सरीसृप|సరీసృప|ಸರೀಸೃಪ|sarīsṛpa|sariisRpa|sareesRpa|sariisRipa|sariisRupa|sariisRRipa|_vei_120@3506|_vei2_pn438
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3505">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3507">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn437">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn438">2:438</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn439">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sarīsṛpa</b> denotes in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203506">1</a></sup> and often later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203506">2</a></sup> any ‘creeping animal’ or ‘reptile.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203506">↑</a> x. 162, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203506"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203506">↑</a> Av. iii. 10, 6: xix. 7, 1; 48, 3, etc.</li></ol></div>

सर्प|సర్ప|ಸರ್ಪ|sarpa|_vei_120@3507|_vei2_pn438
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3506">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3508">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn437">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn438">2:438</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn439">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sarpa,</b> ‘serpent,’ occurs once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203507"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203507">1</a></sup> where <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अहि">ahi</a></b> is the usual word, but often later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203507"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203507">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203507"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203507">↑</a> x. 16, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203507"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203507">↑</a> Av. x. 4, 23; xi. 3, 47; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 5, 4, 1; iii. 1, 1, 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

सर्प-राज्ञी|सर्प-राज्ञि|सर्पराज्ञी|सर्पराज्ञि|सर्प|राज्ञी|राज्ञि|సర్ప-రాజ్ఞీ|సర్ప-రాజ్ఞి|సర్పరాజ్ఞీ|సర్పరాజ్ఞి|సర్ప|రాజ్ఞీ|రాజ్ఞి|ಸರ್ಪ-ರಾಜ್ಞೀ|ಸರ್ಪ-ರಾಜ್ಞಿ|ಸರ್ಪರಾಜ್ಞೀ|ಸರ್ಪರಾಜ್ಞಿ|ಸರ್ಪ|ರಾಜ್ಞೀ|ರಾಜ್ಞಿ|sarpa-rājñī|sarpa-rājñi|sarparājñī|sarparājñi|sarpa|rājñī|rājñi|sarpa-raaj~nii|sarpa-raaj~ni|sarparaaj~nii|sarparaaj~ni|raaj~nii|raaj~ni|sarpa-raaj~nee|sarparaaj~nee|raaj~nee|_vei_120@3508|_vei2_pn438
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3507">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3509">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn437">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn438">2:438</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn439">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sarpa-rājñī,</b> ‘serpent-queen,’ is the alleged authoress of a hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203508"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203508">1</a></sup> according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203508"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203508">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203508"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203508">↑</a> x. 189.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203508"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203508">↑</a> i. 5, 4, 1; vii. 3, 1, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 6, 6; ii. 2, 6, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 23, 1, 2.</li></ol></div>

सर्प-विद्या|सर्पविद्या|सर्प|विद्या|సర్ప-విద్యా|సర్పవిద్యా|సర్ప|విద్యా|ಸರ್ಪ-ವಿದ್ಯಾ|ಸರ್ಪವಿದ್ಯಾ|ಸರ್ಪ|ವಿದ್ಯಾ|sarpa-vidyā|sarpavidyā|sarpa|vidyā|sarpa-vidyaa|sarpavidyaa|vidyaa|_vei_120@3509|_vei2_pn438
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3508">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3510">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn437">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn438">2:438</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn439">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sarpa-vidyā,</b> the ‘science of snakes,’ is enumerated in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> among branches of learning. It must have been reduced to fixed rules, since a section (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्वन्">parvan</a></i>) of it is referred to as studied. The Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203509"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203509">2</a></sup> has the form <a class="vei_120a" href="सर्प">sarpa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>.</p><p>^1 xiii. 4, 3, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Śānkhāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvi. 2, 25. The Āśvalāyana Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 7, 5, has <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विष-विद्या">viṣa-vidyā</a>,</b> and the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vii. 1. 2. 4; 2, 1; 4, 1; 7, 1) has <i>sarpadevajana-<a class="vei_120a" href="विद्या">vidyā</a>.</i></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="2"><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203509"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203509">↑</a> i. 1, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 367, n. 3.</li></ol></div>

सर्पि वात्सि|సర్పి వాత్సి|ಸರ್ಪಿ ವಾತ್ಸಿ|sarpi vātsi|sarpi vaatsi|_vei_120@3510|_vei2_pn438
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3509">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3511">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn437">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn438">2:438</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn439">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sarpi Vātsi</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वत्स">vatsa</a></b>’) is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203510">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203510"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203510">↑</a> vi. 24, 15. Aufrecht, <i>Altareya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,</i> 424, takes the name to be Sarpir. The point is, of course, doubtful, since the word occurs in the nominative only.</li></ol></div>

सर्पिस्|सर्पि|సర్పిస్|సర్పి|ಸರ್ಪಿಸ್|ಸರ್ಪಿ|sarpis|sarpi|_vei_120@3511|_vei2_pn438
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3510">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3512">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn437">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn438">2:438</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn439">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sarpis</b> denotes ‘melted butter,’ whether in a liquid or solidified condition, and not differing from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="घृत">ghṛta</a></b> according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary. Roth there rejects the definition cited by Sāyaṇa in his commentary on the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203511">1</a></sup> which discriminates <a class="vei_120a" href="सर्पिस्">sarpis</a> as the liquid and <a class="vei_120a" href="घृत">ghṛta</a> as the solid condition of the butter. The word is repeatedly mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203511">2</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203511">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203511">↑</a> i. 3, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203511">↑</a> i. 127, 1; v. 6, 9; x. 18, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203511"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203511">↑</a> Av. i. 15, 4; ix. 6, 41; x. 9, 12; xii. 3, 45; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 10, 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

सर्व-चरु|सर्वचरु|सर्व|चरु|సర్వ-చరు|సర్వచరు|సర్వ|చరు|ಸರ್ವ-ಚರು|ಸರ್ವಚರು|ಸರ್ವ|ಚರು|sarva-caru|sarvacaru|sarva|caru|sarva-charu|sarvacharu|charu|_vei_120@3512|_vei2_pn439
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3511">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3513">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn438">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn439">2:439</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn440">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sarva-caru</b> is found in a passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203512"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203512">1</a></sup> and of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203512"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203512">2</a></sup> where the gods are referred to as holding a sacrifice <i>sarvacarau.</i> The word is the name of a man according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary; the name of a place<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203512"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203512">3</a></sup> seems possible, or even a mere adjective may be meant.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203512"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203512">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203512"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203512">↑</a> vi. 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203512"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203512">↑</a> xxix. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203512"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203512">↑</a> Sāyaṇa on <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203512"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203512">↑</a> Aufrecht, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,</i> 425, n. 1, who suggests that <i>yajñe</i> is to be supplied.</li></ol></div>

सर्व-वेदस|सर्ववेदस|सर्व|वेदस|సర్వ-వేదస|సర్వవేదస|సర్వ|వేదస|ಸರ್ವ-ವೇದಸ|ಸರ್ವವೇದಸ|ಸರ್ವ|ವೇದಸ|sarva-vedasa|sarvavedasa|sarva|vedasa|_vei_120@3513|_vei2_pn439
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3512">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3514">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn438">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn439">2:439</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn440">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sarva-vedasa</b> denotes in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas either a sacrifice in which the sacrificer gives his all to the priests,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203513"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203513">1</a></sup> or the whole property of a man.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203513"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203513">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203513"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203513">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 7, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 14; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 3, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203513"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203513">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 1, 1, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 8, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 7, 15; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 6, 1, 15, etc.</li></ol></div>

सर्षप|సర్షప|ಸರ್ಷಪ|sarṣapa|sarShapa|_vei_120@3514|_vei2_pn439
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3513">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3515">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn438">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn439">2:439</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn440">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sarṣapa,</b> denoting ‘mustard’ or ‘mustard seed,’ occurs only a few times in later Vedic texts.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203514">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203514"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203514">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 14, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Srauta <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 15, 8, etc. It is common in the later language.</li></ol></div>

सला-वृकी|सला-वृकि|सलावृकी|सलावृकि|सला|वृकी|वृकि|సలా-వృకీ|సలా-వృకి|సలావృకీ|సలావృకి|సలా|వృకీ|వృకి|ಸಲಾ-ವೃಕೀ|ಸಲಾ-ವೃಕಿ|ಸಲಾವೃಕೀ|ಸಲಾವೃಕಿ|ಸಲಾ|ವೃಕೀ|ವೃಕಿ|salā-vṛkī|salā-vṛki|salāvṛkī|salāvṛki|salā|vṛkī|vṛki|salaa-vRkii|salaa-vRki|salaavRkii|salaavRki|salaa|vRkii|vRki|salaa-vRkee|salaavRkee|vRkee|salaa-vRikii|salaa-vRiki|salaavRikii|salaavRiki|vRikii|vRiki|salaa-vRukii|salaa-vRuki|salaavRukii|salaavRuki|vRukii|vRuki|salaa-vRRikii|salaa-vRRiki|salaavRRikii|salaavRRiki|vRRikii|vRRiki|_vei_120@3515|_vei2_pn439
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3514">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3516">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn438">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn439">2:439</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn440">↷</a><hr><p><b>Salā-vṛkī.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सालावृक">sālāvṛka</a>.</b></p></div>

सलिल-वात|सलिलवात|सलिल|वात|సలిల-వాత|సలిలవాత|సలిల|వాత|ಸಲಿಲ-ವಾತ|ಸಲಿಲವಾತ|ಸಲಿಲ|ವಾತ|salila-vāta|salilavāta|salila|vāta|salila-vaata|salilavaata|vaata|_vei_120@3516|_vei2_pn439
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3515">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3517">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn438">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn439">2:439</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn440">↷</a><hr><p><b>Salila-vāta</b> occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203516"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203516">1</a></sup> as an adjective meaning ‘favoured with a wind from the water.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203516"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203516">2</a></sup> It probably refers to the wind from the ocean, the south-west monsoon.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203516"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203516">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203516"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203516">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 4, 12, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxiv. 4; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 16, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203516"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203516">↑</a> Or, according to the commentator, <i>salilākhyena <a class="vei_120a" href="वात">vāta</a>-viśeṣeṇa anugṛhītaḥ,</i> ‘favoured by a kind of wind called <a class="vei_120a" href="सलिल">salila</a>.’</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203516"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203516">↑</a> <i>Indian Empire,</i> 1, 110. The monsoon is little noted in the Vedic texts, except in so far as the Marut hymns may be deemed to be a description of the monsoon. See Rv. i. 19, 7; 37, 6 <i>et seq.;</i> 38, 8; 64, 8; 88, 5; v. 83, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> 85, 4; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 42-44.</li></ol></div>

सल्व|సల్వ|ಸಲ್ವ|salva|_vei_120@3517|_vei2_pn440
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3516">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3518">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn439">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn440">2:440</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn441">↷</a><hr><p><b>Salva</b> is the name of a people mentioned in a passage of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203517"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203517">1</a></sup> which records a boast by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्यापर्ण सायकायन">śyāparṇa sāyakāyana</a></b> that if a certain rite of his had been completed, his race would have been the nobles, Brahmins, and peasants of the Salvas, and even as it was his race would surpass the Salvas. This people appears also to be alluded to as <i>Sālvīḥ (prajāḥ)</i> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> Pāṭha,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203517"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203517">2</a></sup> where they are said to have declared that their king was <b>Yaugandhari</b> when they stayed their chariots<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203517"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203517">3</a></sup> on the banks of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a>.</b> There is later evidence<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203517"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203517">4</a></sup> indicating that the Sālvas or Śālvas were closely connected with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a>-Pañcālas,</b> and that apparently some of them, at least, were victorious near the banks of the <a class="vei_120a" href="यमुना">yamunā</a>. There is no good evidence to place them in the north-west in Vedic times.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203517"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203517">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203517"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203517">↑</a> x. 4, 1, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203517"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203517">↑</a> ii. 11, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203517"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203517">↑</a> Winternitz, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a>-pāṭha,</i> xlv-xlvii, sees in the verse an allusion to the Sālva women turning round the wheel (? spinning-wheel). But a reference to a warlike raid seems more plauaible.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203517"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203517">↑</a> Mahābhārata, iv. 1, 12; viii. 44 (45), 14. The Yugandharas are also referred to in a Kārikā quoted in the Kāśikā <a class="vei_120a" href="वृत्ति">vṛtti</a> on Pāṇini, iv. 1, 173.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203517"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203517">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 215. Later, they may have been found in Rājasthān, Lassen, <i>Indische Alterthumskunde,</i> 1^2, 760.</li></ol></div>

सव्य-ष्ठा|सव्यष्ठा|सव्य|ष्ठा|సవ్య-ష్ఠా|సవ్యష్ఠా|సవ్య|ష్ఠా|ಸವ್ಯ-ಷ್ಠಾ|ಸವ್ಯಷ್ಠಾ|ಸವ್ಯ|ಷ್ಠಾ|savya-ṣṭhā|savyaṣṭhā|savya|ṣṭhā|savya-ShThaa|savyaShThaa|ShThaa|_vei_120@3518|_vei2_pn440
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3517">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3519">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn439">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn440">2:440</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn441">↷</a><hr><p><b>Savya-ṣṭhā,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203518"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203518">1</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सव्य">savya</a>-ṣṭhṛ,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203518"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203518">2</a></sup> <b>Savye-<a class="vei_120a" href="ष्ठ">ṣṭha</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203518"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203518">3</a></sup> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सव्य">savya</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="स्थ">stha</a></b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203518"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203518">4</a></sup> are all various forms of the word for ‘car-fighter,’ as opposed to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सारथि">sārathi</a>,</b> ‘charioteer,’ showing that, as was natural, the fighter stood on the left of the driver. The commentators<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203518"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203518">5</a></sup> are inclined to see in the <a class="vei_120a" href="सव्यष्ठा">savyaṣṭhā</a> merely another ‘charioteer,’ but this is quite unjustifiable,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203518"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203518">6</a></sup> and is perhaps due to later caste prejudice against a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a> charioteer.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203518"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203518">↑</a> Av. viii. 8, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203518"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203518">↑</a> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 2, 4, 9; 3, 1, 8; 4, 3, 17, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203518"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203518">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>. i. 7, 9, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203518"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203518">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 62, n. 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203518"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203518">↑</a> On Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 1, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203518"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203518">↑</a> Eggeling, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 235.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 296.</li></ol></div>

सस|సస|ಸಸ|sasa|_vei_120@3519|_vei2_pn440
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3518">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3520">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn439">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn440">2:440</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn441">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sasa</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203519">1</a></sup> denotes ‘herb’ or ‘grass.’<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1203519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1203519">1b</a></sup> The word is also applied to the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> plant<sup id="ref_120_2b_vei_1203519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2b_vei_1203519">2b</a></sup> and the sacrificial straw.<sup id="ref_120_3b_vei_1203519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3b_vei_1203519">3b</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203519">↑</a> i. 51, 3; x. 79, 3.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1203519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1203519">↑</a> i. 51, 3; x. 79, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2b_vei_1203519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2b_vei_1203519">↑</a> iii. 5, 6; iv. 5, 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3b_vei_1203519"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3b_vei_1203519">↑</a> v. 21, 4.</li></ol></div>

ससर्परी|ससर्परि|ససర్పరీ|ససర్పరి|ಸಸರ್ಪರೀ|ಸಸರ್ಪರಿ|sasarparī|sasarpari|sasarparii|sasarparee|_vei_120@3520|_vei2_pn441
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3519">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3521">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn440">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn441">2:441</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn442">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sasarparī</b> is a word occurring in two curious verses of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203520"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203520">1</a></sup> According to a later interpretation,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203520"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203520">2</a></sup> it designates a particular kind of skill in speech which <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a></b> obtained from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जमदग्नि">jamadagni</a>.</b> What it was is quite uncertain.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203520"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203520">↑</a> iii. 53, 15, 16.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203520"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203520">↑</a> Bṛhaddevatā, iii. 113, with Macdonell's notes. <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 159.</li></ol></div>

सस्य|సస్య|ಸಸ್ಯ|sasya|_vei_120@3521|_vei2_pn441
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3520">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3522">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn440">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn441">2:441</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn442">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sasya</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203521">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203521">2</a></sup> regularly denotes ‘corn’ generally. It corresponds to the Avestan <i>hahya.</i> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कृषि">kṛṣi</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203521">↑</a> vii. 11, 1; viii. 10, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203521"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203521">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 3, 3; v. 1, 7, 3; vii. 5, 20, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 2, 2, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 284.</li></ol></div>

सह|సహ|ಸಹ|saha|_vei_120@3522|_vei2_pn441
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3521">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3523">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn440">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn441">2:441</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn442">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saha</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203522">1</a></sup> is, according to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203522">2</a></sup> the name of a plant, but Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203522">3</a></sup> thinks the word is only an adjective meaning ‘mighty.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203522">↑</a> xi. 6, 15. <i>Cf.</i> Sāmavidhāna <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203522">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 2<i>b.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203522"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203522">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 648.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 642; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 72.</li></ol></div>

सह-देव|सहदेव|सह|देव|సహ-దేవ|సహదేవ|సహ|దేవ|ಸಹ-ದೇವ|ಸಹದೇವ|ಸಹ|ದೇವ|saha-deva|sahadeva|saha|deva|_vei_120@3523|_vei2_pn441
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3522">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3524">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn440">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn441">2:441</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn442">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saha-deva</b> is the name of a prince in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203523">1</a></sup> where he is victorious over the <b>Śimyus</b> and <b>Dasyus.</b> It is quite probable that he is identical with King <a class="vei_120a" href="सहदेव">sahadeva</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सार्ञ्जय">sārñjaya</a>, who is mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203523">2</a></sup> as having once been called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुप्लन् सार्ञ्जय">suplan sārñjaya</a>,</b> and as having changed his name because of his success in performing the <a class="vei_120a" href="दाक्षायण">dākṣāyaṇa</a> sacrifice. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203523">3</a></sup> he is mentioned with <b>Somaka Sahadevya,</b> who also appears in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203523">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203523">↑</a> i. 100, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203523">↑</a> ii. 4, 4, 3. 4. <i>Cf.</i> xii. 8, 2, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203523">↑</a> vii. 34, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203523"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203523">↑</a> iv. 15, 7 <i>et seq.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 132; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 105, 106.</li></ol></div>

सह-देवी|सह-देवि|सहदेवी|सहदेवि|सह|देवी|देवि|సహ-దేవీ|సహ-దేవి|సహదేవీ|సహదేవి|సహ|దేవీ|దేవి|ಸಹ-ದೇವೀ|ಸಹ-ದೇವಿ|ಸಹದೇವೀ|ಸಹದೇವಿ|ಸಹ|ದೇವೀ|ದೇವಿ|saha-devī|saha-devi|sahadevī|sahadevi|saha|devī|devi|saha-devii|sahadevii|devii|saha-devee|sahadevee|devee|_vei_120@3524|_vei2_pn441
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3523">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3525">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn440">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn441">2:441</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn442">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saha-devī</b> is the name of a plant in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203524"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203524">1</a></sup> according to the reading of the commentary. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203524"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203524">↑</a> vi. 59, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Grill, <i>Hundert Lieder,</i>^2 163; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 325; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 490, who does not accept this reading. A plant called <a class="vei_120a" href="सहदेव">sahadeva</a> occurs in the Sāmavidhāna <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 10.</li></ol></div>

सहमान|సహమాన|ಸಹಮಾನ|sahamāna|sahamaana|_vei_120@3525|_vei2_pn442
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3524">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3526">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn441">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn442">2:442</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn443">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sahamāna</b> is the name of a plant in the Atharvaveda (ii. 25, 2; iv. 17, 2; viii. 2, 6; 7, 5).</p></div>

सहो-जित्|सहोजित्|सहो|जित्|సహో-జిత్|సహోజిత్|సహో|జిత్|ಸಹೋ-ಜಿತ್|ಸಹೋಜಿತ್|ಸಹೋ|ಜಿತ್|saho-jit|sahojit|saho|jit|_vei_120@3526|_vei2_pn442
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3525">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3527">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn441">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn442">2:442</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn443">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saho-jit.</b> See <b>Jaitrāyaṇa.</b></p></div>

सांवरणि|సాంవరణి|ಸಾಂವರಣಿ|sāṃvaraṇi|saaMvaraNi|_vei_120@3527|_vei2_pn442
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3526">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3528">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn441">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn442">2:442</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn443">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sāṃvaraṇi</b> is found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203527"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203527">1</a></sup> in one passage, where it naturally seems to be a patronymic (‘descendant of Saṃvaraṇa’) of <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>. According to Bloomfield,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203527"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203527">2</a></sup> it is a corruption for <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सावर्णि">sāvarṇi</a>,</b> a reference to Manu's birth from the <i>savarṇā,</i> ‘similar’ female who was substituted for Saraṇyū according to the legend (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a></b>). This is possible, but not certain. Scheftelowitz<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203527"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203527">3</a></sup> thinks that the reading of the Kaśmir manuscript of the Rigveda, which has <i>sāṃvaraṇam,</i> ‘found on the sacrificial ground,’ as an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>, is to be preferred. But this seems quite improbable.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203527"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203527">4</a></sup> We must either recognize a real man called <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सांवरणि">sāṃvaraṇi</a>; or take <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a> as one name, <a class="vei_120a" href="सांवरणि">sāṃvaraṇi</a> as another; or admit that <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सांवरणि">sāṃvaraṇi</a> is simply <a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a> with a patronymic derived from an unknown legend.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203527"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203527">↑</a> viii. 51, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203527"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203527">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 180, n.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203527"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203527">↑</a> <i>Die Apokryphen des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 38.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203527"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203527">↑</a> See Oldenberg, <i>Go7ttingische Gelehrte Anzeigen,</i> 1907, 237.</li></ol></div>

साकम्-अश्व देवरात|साकमश्व देवरात|साकम्|अश्व देवरात|साकं|సాకమ్-అశ్వ దేవరాత|సాకమశ్వ దేవరాత|సాకమ్|అశ్వ దేవరాత|సాకం|ಸಾಕಮ್-ಅಶ್ವ ದೇವರಾತ|ಸಾಕಮಶ್ವ ದೇವರಾತ|ಸಾಕಮ್|ಅಶ್ವ ದೇವರಾತ|ಸಾಕಂ|sākam-aśva devarāta|sākamaśva devarāta|sākam|aśva devarāta|saakam-ashva devaraata|saakamashva devaraata|saakam|ashva devaraata|_vei_120@3528|_vei2_pn442
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3527">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3529">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn441">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn442">2:442</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn443">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sākam-<a class="vei_120a" href="अश्व">aśva</a> Devarāta</b> is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>,</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) which concludes the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka (xv. 1).</p></div>

सांकृती-पुत्र|सांकृतीपुत्र|सांकृती|सांकृति|पुत्र|साङ्कृती-पुत्र|साङ्कृतीपुत्र|साङ्कृती|साङ्कृति|సాంకృతీ-పుత్ర|సాంకృతీపుత్ర|సాంకృతీ|సాంకృతి|పుత్ర|సాఙ్కృతీ-పుత్ర|సాఙ్కృతీపుత్ర|సాఙ్కృతీ|సాఙ్కృతి|ಸಾಂಕೃತೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಸಾಂಕೃತೀಪುತ್ರ|ಸಾಂಕೃತೀ|ಸಾಂಕೃತಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|ಸಾಙ್ಕೃತೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಸಾಙ್ಕೃತೀಪುತ್ರ|ಸಾಙ್ಕೃತೀ|ಸಾಙ್ಕೃತಿ|sāṃkṛtī-putra|sāṃkṛtīputra|sāṃkṛtī|sāṃkṛti|putra|saaMkRtii-putra|saaMkRtiiputra|saaMkRtii|saaMkRti|saaMkRtee-putra|saaMkRteeputra|saaMkRtee|saankRtii-putra|saankRtiiputra|saankRtii|saankRti|saankRtee-putra|saankRteeputra|saankRtee|saaMkRitii-putra|saaMkRitiiputra|saaMkRitii|saaMkRiti|saaMkRutii-putra|saaMkRutiiputra|saaMkRutii|saaMkRuti|saaMkRRitii-putra|saaMkRRitiiputra|saaMkRRitii|saaMkRRiti|saankRitii-putra|saankRitiiputra|saankRitii|saankRiti|saankRutii-putra|saankRutiiputra|saankRutii|saankRuti|saankRRitii-putra|saankRRitiiputra|saankRRitii|saankRRiti|_vei_120@3529|_vei2_pn442
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3528">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3530">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn441">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn442">2:442</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn443">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sāṃkṛtī-putra</b> (‘son of a female descendant of Saṃkṛta’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आलम्बायनीपुत्र">ālambāyanīputra</a></b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203529">1</a></sup> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आलम्बीपुत्र">ālambīputra</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203529">2</a></sup> in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203529">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 5, 2 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203529"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203529">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> vi. 4, 32 Mādhyaṃdina.</li></ol></div>

सांकृत्य|साङ्कृत्य|సాంకృత్య|సాఙ్కృత్య|ಸಾಂಕೃತ್ಯ|ಸಾಙ್ಕೃತ್ಯ|sāṃkṛtya|saaMkRtya|saankRtya|saaMkRitya|saaMkRutya|saaMkRRitya|saankRitya|saankRutya|saankRRitya|_vei_120@3530|_vei2_pn442
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3529">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3531">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn441">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn442">2:442</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn443">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sāṃkṛtya,</b> ‘descendant of Saṃkṛti,’ is the name of a teacher whose pupil was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पाराशर्य">pārāśarya</a></b> in the first two Vaṃśas (lists of teachers) in the Mādhyaṃdina recension of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203530">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203530"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203530">↑</a> ii. 5, 20; iv. 5, 26. A <a class="vei_120a" href="सांकृत्य">sāṃkṛtya</a> occurs also in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Prātiśākhya, viii. 21; x. 21; xvi. 16.</li></ol></div>

साची-गुण|साचीगुण|साची|साचि|गुण|సాచీ-గుణ|సాచీగుణ|సాచీ|సాచి|గుణ|ಸಾಚೀ-ಗುಣ|ಸಾಚೀಗುಣ|ಸಾಚೀ|ಸಾಚಿ|ಗುಣ|sācī-guṇa|sācīguṇa|sācī|sāci|guṇa|saachii-guNa|saachiiguNa|saachii|saachi|guNa|saachee-guNa|saacheeguNa|saachee|_vei_120@3531|_vei2_pn443
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3530">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3532">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn442">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn443">2:443</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn444">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sācī-guṇa</b> is mentioned, apparently as a place in the territory of the <b>Bharatas,</b> in a verse occurring in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203531"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203531">1</a></sup> Leumann,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203531"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203531">2</a></sup> however, thinks an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, Śācīgu, may be meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203531"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203531">↑</a> viii. 23, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203531"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203531">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 80, n. 5. This conjecture seems improbable.</li></ol></div>

सांजीवी-पुत्र|सांजीवीपुत्र|सांजीवी|सांजीवि|पुत्र|साञ्जीवी-पुत्र|साञ्जीवीपुत्र|साञ्जीवी|साञ्जीवि|సాంజీవీ-పుత్ర|సాంజీవీపుత్ర|సాంజీవీ|సాంజీవి|పుత్ర|సాఞ్జీవీ-పుత్ర|సాఞ్జీవీపుత్ర|సాఞ్జీవీ|సాఞ్జీవి|ಸಾಂಜೀವೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಸಾಂಜೀವೀಪುತ್ರ|ಸಾಂಜೀವೀ|ಸಾಂಜೀವಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|ಸಾಞ್ಜೀವೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಸಾಞ್ಜೀವೀಪುತ್ರ|ಸಾಞ್ಜೀವೀ|ಸಾಞ್ಜೀವಿ|sāṃjīvī-putra|sāṃjīvīputra|sāṃjīvī|sāṃjīvi|putra|saaMjiivii-putra|saaMjiiviiputra|saaMjiivii|saaMjiivi|saaMjeevee-putra|saaMjeeveeputra|saaMjeevee|saaMjeevi|saanjiivii-putra|saanjiiviiputra|saanjiivii|saanjiivi|saanjeevee-putra|saanjeeveeputra|saanjeevee|saanjeevi|_vei_120@3532|_vei2_pn443
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3531">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3533">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn442">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn443">2:443</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn444">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sāṃjīvī-putra,</b> ‘son of <a class="vei_120a" href="सांजीवी">sāṃjīvī</a>,’ is the name of a teacher who appears in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) at the end of the tenth <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्ड">kāṇḍa</a> of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203532"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203532">1</a></sup> and at the end of the fourteenth <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्ड">kāṇḍa</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> recension,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203532"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203532">2</a></sup> as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="माण्डूकायनि">māṇḍūkāyani</a>.</b> In the Vaṃśas at the end of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> in both recensions<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203532"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203532">3</a></sup> he is given as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राश्नीपुत्र">prāśnīputra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="आसुरिवासिन्">āsurivāsin</a>.</b> It seems clear that he united in himself two lines of teachers--that of the tradition of the firecult from <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्डिल्य">śāṇḍilya</a>,</b> and that of the tradition of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203532"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203532">↑</a> x. 6, 5, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203532"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203532">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 5. 4 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203532"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203532">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> vi. 4, 32 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 5, 2 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, xxxiv <i>et seq.;</i> Wéber, <i>Indian Literature,</i> 131.</li></ol></div>

साति औष्ट्राक्षि|సాతి ఔష్ట్రాక్షి|ಸಾತಿ ಔಷ್ಟ್ರಾಕ್ಷಿ|sāti auṣṭrākṣi|saati auShTraakShi|saati auShTraaxi|_vei_120@3533|_vei2_pn443
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3532">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3534">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn442">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn443">2:443</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn444">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sāti Auṣṭrākṣi</b> (‘descendant of Uṣṭrākṣa’) is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203533">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203533"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203533">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372.</li></ol></div>

सात्य-कामि|सात्यकामि|सात्य|कामि|సాత్య-కామి|సాత్యకామి|సాత్య|కామి|ಸಾತ್ಯ-ಕಾಮಿ|ಸಾತ್ಯಕಾಮಿ|ಸಾತ್ಯ|ಕಾಮಿ|sātya-kāmi|sātyakāmi|sātya|kāmi|saatya-kaami|saatyakaami|saatya|kaami|_vei_120@3534|_vei2_pn443
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3533">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3535">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn442">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn443">2:443</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn444">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sātya-kāmi</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सत्यकाम">satyakāma</a></b>’) is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="केशिन्">keśin</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (ii. 6, 2, 3).</p></div>

सात्य-कीर्त|सात्यकीर्त|सात्य|कीर्त|సాత్య-కీర్త|సాత్యకీర్త|సాత్య|కీర్త|ಸಾತ್ಯ-ಕೀರ್ತ|ಸಾತ್ಯಕೀರ್ತ|ಸಾತ್ಯ|ಕೀರ್ತ|sātya-kīrta|sātyakīrta|sātya|kīrta|saatya-kiirta|saatyakiirta|saatya|kiirta|saatya-keerta|saatyakeerta|keerta|_vei_120@3535|_vei2_pn443
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3534">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3536">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn442">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn443">2:443</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn444">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sātya-kīrta</b> is the name of a school of teachers mentioned in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 32, 1).</p></div>

सात्य-यज्ञ|सात्ययज्ञ|सात्य|यज्ञ|సాత్య-యజ్ఞ|సాత్యయజ్ఞ|సాత్య|యజ్ఞ|ಸಾತ್ಯ-ಯಜ್ಞ|ಸಾತ್ಯಯಜ್ಞ|ಸಾತ್ಯ|ಯಜ್ಞ|sātya-yajña|sātyayajña|sātya|yajña|saatya-yaj~na|saatyayaj~na|saatya|yaj~na|_vei_120@3536|_vei2_pn443
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3535">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3537">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn442">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn443">2:443</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn444">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sātya-yajña</b> (‘descendant of Saryayajña’) is the name of a teacher in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 1, 1, 4).</p></div>

सात्य-यज्ञि|सात्य-यज्ञि|सात्ययज्ञि|सात्य|यज्ञि|సాత్య-యజ్ఞి_౧|సాత్య-యజ్ఞి|సాత్యయజ్ఞి|సాత్య|యజ్ఞి|ಸಾತ್ಯ-ಯಜ್ಞಿ_೧|ಸಾತ್ಯ-ಯಜ್ಞಿ|ಸಾತ್ಯಯಜ್ಞಿ|ಸಾತ್ಯ|ಯಜ್ಞಿ|sātya-yajñi_1|sātya-yajñi|sātyayajñi|sātya|yajñi|saatya-yaj~ni_1|saatya-yaj~ni|saatyayaj~ni|saatya|yaj~ni|_vei_120@3537|_vei2_pn443
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3536">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3538">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn442">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn443">2:443</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn444">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Sātya-yajñi</b> (‘descendant of Satyayajña’) is the patronymic of <b>Somaśuṣma</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xi. 6, 2, 1. 3; xiii. 4, 2, 4; 5, 3, 9). </p></div>

सात्य-यज्ञि|सात्य-यज्ञि|सात्ययज्ञि|सात्य|यज्ञि|సాత్య-యజ్ఞి_౨|సాత్య-యజ్ఞి|సాత్యయజ్ఞి|సాత్య|యజ్ఞి|ಸಾತ್ಯ-ಯಜ್ಞಿ_೨|ಸಾತ್ಯ-ಯಜ್ಞಿ|ಸಾತ್ಯಯಜ್ಞಿ|ಸಾತ್ಯ|ಯಜ್ಞಿ|sātya-yajñi_2|sātya-yajñi|sātyayajñi|sātya|yajñi|saatya-yaj~ni_2|saatya-yaj~ni|saatyayaj~ni|saatya|yaj~ni|_vei_120@3538|_vei2_pn444
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3537">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3539">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn443">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn444">2:444</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn445">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Sātya-yajñi</b> is the name of a school of teachers mentioned in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (ii. 4, 5) with the <b>Śailanas</b> and the <b>Kārīradis.</b></p></div>

सात्य-हव्य|सात्यहव्य|सात्य|हव्य|సాత్య-హవ్య|సాత్యహవ్య|సాత్య|హవ్య|ಸಾತ್ಯ-ಹವ್ಯ|ಸಾತ್ಯಹವ್ಯ|ಸಾತ್ಯ|ಹವ್ಯ|sātya-havya|sātyahavya|sātya|havya|saatya-havya|saatyahavya|saatya|_vei_120@3539|_vei2_pn444
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3538">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3540">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn443">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn444">2:444</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn445">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sātya-havya</b> (‘descendant of Satyahavya’) is the patronymic of a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वासिष्ठ">vāsiṣṭha</a></b> who is mentioned as a contemporary of <b>Atyarāti <a class="vei_120a" href="जानंतपि">jānaṃtapi</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 23, 9), and of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="देवभाग">devabhāga</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (vi. 6, 2, 2).</p></div>

सात्राजित|సాత్రాజిత|ಸಾತ್ರಾಜಿತ|sātrājita|saatraajita|_vei_120@3540|_vei2_pn444
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3539">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3541">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn443">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn444">2:444</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn445">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sātrājita</b> (‘descendant of Satrājit’) is the patronymic of <b>Śatānīka.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203540"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203540">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203540"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203540">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 21, 5; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 4, 19. 21.</li></ol></div>

सात्रा-साह|सात्रासाह|सात्रा|साह|సాత్రా-సాహ|సాత్రాసాహ|సాత్రా|సాహ|ಸಾತ್ರಾ-ಸಾಹ|ಸಾತ್ರಾಸಾಹ|ಸಾತ್ರಾ|ಸಾಹ|sātrā-sāha|sātrāsāha|sātrā|sāha|saatraa-saaha|saatraasaaha|saatraa|saaha|_vei_120@3541|_vei2_pn444
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3540">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3542">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn443">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn444">2:444</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn445">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sātrā-sāha</b> (‘descendant of Satrāsāha’) is the patronymic of <b>Śoṇa.</b></p><p>^1 Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 4, 16, 18.</p></div>

सादिन्|సాదిన్|ಸಾದಿನ್|sādin|saadin|_vei_120@3542|_vei2_pn444
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3541">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3543">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn443">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn444">2:444</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn445">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sādin</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203542">1</a></sup> denotes the ‘rider’ of a horse as opposed to <i>a-<a class="vei_120a" href="साद">sāda</a>,</i> ‘pedestrian.’ An <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्व">aśva</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="सादिन्">sādin</a>,</i> ‘horse-rider,’ is known to the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203542">2</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203542">3</a></sup> and the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203542">4</a></sup> itself contain clear references to horse-riding, while the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203542">5</a></sup> refers to mounting a horse sideways. Āśvalāyana<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203542">6</a></sup> knows <i>sādya</i> as a ‘riding horse’ opposed to <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वह्य">vahya</a>,</i> a ‘draught animal.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203542">↑</a> xi. 10, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203542">↑</a> xxx. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203542">↑</a> iii. 4, 7, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203542">↑</a> i. 162, 17; v. 61, 3. <i>Cf.</i> i. 163, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203542">↑</a> i. 2, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 3, 2, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203542"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203542">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ix. 9, 14.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 230, 295, 296; Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 358; Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 177; Weber, Proceedings of the Berlin Academy, 1898, 564.</li></ol></div>

साधारणी|साधारणि|సాధారణీ|సాధారణి|ಸಾಧಾರಣೀ|ಸಾಧಾರಣಿ|sādhāraṇī|sādhāraṇi|saadhaaraNii|saadhaaraNi|saadhaaraNee|_vei_120@3543|_vei2_pn444
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3542">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3544">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn443">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn444">2:444</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn445">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sādhāraṇī</b> in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203543"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203543">1</a></sup> seems to refer not so much to an <i>uxor communis,</i> like Draupadī in the Epic, as Max Müller<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203543"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203543">2</a></sup> suggests, but to a courtezan. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203543"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203543">↑</a> i. 167, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203543"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203543">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 277.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 332; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 461; Pischel and Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, xxv.</li></ol></div>

साप्त|సాప్త|ಸಾಪ್ತ|sāpta|saapta|_vei_120@3544|_vei2_pn445
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3543">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3545">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn444">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn445">2:445</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn446">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sāpta</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203544">1</a></sup> may be a proper name, but the sense is quite uncertain.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203544"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203544">↑</a> viii. 55, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 5, 552; Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 2, 266.</li></ol></div>

साप्तरथ-वाहनि|साप्तरथवाहनि|साप्तरथ|वाहनि|సాప్తరథ-వాహని|సాప్తరథవాహని|సాప్తరథ|వాహని|ಸಾಪ್ತರಥ-ವಾಹನಿ|ಸಾಪ್ತರಥವಾಹನಿ|ಸಾಪ್ತರಥ|ವಾಹನಿ|sāptaratha-vāhani|sāptarathavāhani|sāptaratha|vāhani|saaptaratha-vaahani|saaptarathavaahani|saaptaratha|vaahani|_vei_120@3545|_vei2_pn445
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3544">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3546">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn444">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn445">2:445</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn446">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sāptaratha-vāhani</b> (‘descendant of Saptarathavāhana’) is the patronymic of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्डिल्य">śāṇḍilya</a>,</b> in the <b>Śatapatha</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203545"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203545">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203545"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203545">↑</a> x. 1, 4, 10. 11. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 259, n.</li></ol></div>

साप्य|साय्य|సాప్య|సాయ్య|ಸಾಪ್ಯ|ಸಾಯ್ಯ|sāpya|sāyya|saapya|saayya|_vei_120@3546|_vei2_pn445
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3545">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3547">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn444">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn445">2:445</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn446">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sāpya</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="साय्य">sāyya</a></b> is the patronymic of <b>Namī</b> in the Rigveda (vi. 20, 6).</p></div>

साम-वेद|सामवेद|साम|वेद|సామ-వేద|సామవేద|సామ|వేద|ಸಾಮ-ವೇದ|ಸಾಮವೇದ|ಸಾಮ|ವೇದ|sāma-veda|sāmaveda|sāma|veda|saama-veda|saamaveda|saama|_vei_120@3547|_vei2_pn445
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3546">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3548">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn444">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn445">2:445</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn446">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sāma-veda,</b> ‘the <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a> of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> chants,’ is the name of a collection of verses for chanting, often mentioned in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203547">1</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> itself is repeatedly referred to in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203547">2</a></sup> and the triad Ṛc, <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुस्">yajus</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> is common from the Atharvaveda onwards.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203547">3</a></sup> These texts know also the <a class="vei_120a" href="साम">sāma</a>-ga, the ‘Sāman-chanter,’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203547">4</a></sup> who occurs later.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203547">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203547">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 12, 9, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 32, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 8, 3; xii. 3, 4, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 2, 3; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 5, 13 (Mādhyaṃdina = i. 5, 5 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>); ii. 4, 10; iv. 1, 6 (= iv. 1, 2); 5, 11; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 3, 7; iii. 3, 1. 2; 15, 7; vii. 1, 2. 4; 2, 1; 7, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203547">↑</a> i. 62, 2; 107, 2; 164, 24, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 38, 439 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203547">↑</a> x. 7, 14; xi. 7, 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxxiv. 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203547">↑</a> Rv. ii. 43, 1; x. 107, 6; Av. ii. 12, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203547"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203547">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 22, 3; 37, 4; iii. 4, 1.</li></ol></div>

साम-श्रवस्|साम-श्रव|सामश्रवस्|सामश्रव|साम|श्रवस्|श्रव|సామ-శ్రవస్|సామ-శ్రవ|సామశ్రవస్|సామశ్రవ|సామ|శ్రవస్|శ్రవ|ಸಾಮ-ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಸಾಮ-ಶ್ರವ|ಸಾಮಶ್ರವಸ್|ಸಾಮಶ್ರವ|ಸಾಮ|ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಶ್ರವ|sāma-śravas|sāma-śrava|sāmaśravas|sāmaśrava|sāma|śravas|śrava|saama-shravas|saama-shrava|saamashravas|saamashrava|saama|shravas|shrava|_vei_120@3548|_vei2_pn445
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3547">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3549">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn444">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn445">2:445</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn446">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sāma-śravas</b> (‘famed for chants’) occurs in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203548">1</a></sup> According to Max Müller,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203548">2</a></sup> the word is an epithet of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a>,</b> but Bo7htlingk<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203548">3</a></sup> takes it as the name of a pupil of that teacher.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203548">↑</a> iii. 1, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203548">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 15, 121.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203548"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203548">↑</a> Translation, 36.</li></ol></div>

साम-श्रवस|सामश्रवस|साम|श्रवस|సామ-శ్రవస|సామశ్రవస|సామ|శ్రవస|ಸಾಮ-ಶ್ರವಸ|ಸಾಮಶ್ರವಸ|ಸಾಮ|ಶ್ರವಸ|sāma-śravasa|sāmaśravasa|sāma|śravasa|saama-shravasa|saamashravasa|saama|shravasa|_vei_120@3549|_vei2_pn445
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3548">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3550">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn444">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn445">2:445</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn446">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sāma-śravasa</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सामश्रवस्">sāmaśravas</a></b>’) is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुषीतक">kuṣītaka</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xvii. 4, 3). </p></div>

सामुद्रि|సాముద్రి|ಸಾಮುದ್ರಿ|sāmudri|saamudri|_vei_120@3550|_vei2_pn446
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3549">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3551">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn445">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn446">2:446</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn447">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sāmudri</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="समुद्र">samudra</a></b>’) is the name of a mythical sage, <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्व">aśva</a>, in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 2, 2, 14).</p></div>

सांमद|సాంమద|ಸಾಂಮದ|sāṃmada|saaMmada|_vei_120@3551|_vei2_pn446
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3550">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3552">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn445">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn446">2:446</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn447">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sāṃmada</b> (‘descendant of Saṃmada’) is the patronymic of the mythical <a class="vei_120a" href="मत्स्य">matsya</a> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 4, 3, 12).</p></div>

साम्राज्य|సామ్రాజ్య|ಸಾಮ್ರಾಜ್ಯ|sāmrājya|saamraajya|_vei_120@3552|_vei2_pn446
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3551">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3553">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn445">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn446">2:446</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn447">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sāmrājya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सम्राज्">samrāj</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राज्य">rājya</a>.</b></p></div>

साय|సాయ|ಸಾಯ|sāya|saaya|_vei_120@3553|_vei2_pn446
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3552">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3554">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn445">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn446">2:446</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn447">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sāya</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203553"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203553">1</a></sup> denotes ‘evening’ in the Rigveda and later, usually appearing in the adverbial form Sāyam,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203553"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203553">2</a></sup> ‘in the evening.’ <i>Cf.</i> <b>Ahar.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203553"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203553">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 3, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 8; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 3, 2, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203553"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203553">↑</a> Rv. v. 77, 2; x. 146, 4; Av. iii. 12, 3; iv. 11, 12; viii. 6, 10, etc. <i>Cf. Sāyaṃprātar,</i> ‘morning and evening,’ Av. iii. 30, 7; xix. 39, 2, etc.</li></ol></div>

सायक|सायक|సాయక_౧|సాయక|ಸಾಯಕ_೧|ಸಾಯಕ|sāyaka_1|sāyaka|saayaka_1|saayaka|_vei_120@3554|_vei2_pn446
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3553">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3555">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn445">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn446">2:446</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn447">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Sāyaka</b> denotes ‘arrow’ in the <b>Rigveda</b> (ii. 33, 10; iii. 53, 23; x. 48, 4).</p></div>

सायक जान-श्रुतेय|सायक जान-श्रुतेय|सायक जानश्रुतेय|सायक जान|श्रुतेय|సాయక జాన-శ్రుతేయ_౨|సాయక జాన-శ్రుతేయ|సాయక జానశ్రుతేయ|సాయక జాన|శ్రుతేయ|ಸಾಯಕ ಜಾನ-ಶ್ರುತೇಯ_೨|ಸಾಯಕ ಜಾನ-ಶ್ರುತೇಯ|ಸಾಯಕ ಜಾನಶ್ರುತೇಯ|ಸಾಯಕ ಜಾನ|ಶ್ರುತೇಯ|sāyaka jāna-śruteya_2|sāyaka jāna-śruteya|sāyaka jānaśruteya|sāyaka jāna|śruteya|saayaka jaana-shruteya_2|saayaka jaana-shruteya|saayaka jaanashruteya|saayaka jaana|shruteya|_vei_120@3555|_vei2_pn446
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3554">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3556">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn445">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn446">2:446</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn447">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Sāyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="जान">jāna</a>-śruteya</b> (‘descendant of Janaśruta’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काण्ड्विय">kāṇḍviya</a></b> is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनश्रुत">janaśruta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्ड्विय">kāṇḍviya</a>,</b> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 40, 2).</p></div>

सायकायन|సాయకాయన|ಸಾಯಕಾಯನ|sāyakāyana|saayakaayana|_vei_120@3556|_vei2_pn446
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3555">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3557">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn445">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn446">2:446</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn447">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sāyakāyana</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सायक">sāyaka</a></b>’) is the patronymic of <b>Śyāparṇa</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203556"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203556">1</a></sup> and also of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिकायनि">kauśikāyani</a></b> in the second <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203556"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203556">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203556"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203556">↑</a> x. 3, 6, 10; 5, 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203556"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203556">↑</a> iv. 5, 27 (Mādhyaṃdina = iv. 6, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

साय्य|సాయ్య|ಸಾಯ್ಯ|sāyya|saayya|_vei_120@3557|_vei2_pn446
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3556">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3558">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn445">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn446">2:446</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn447">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sāyya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="साप्य">sāpya</a>.</b></p></div>

सारथि|సారథి|ಸಾರಥಿ|sārathi|saarathi|_vei_120@3558|_vei2_pn446
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3557">«</a>pada9<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3559">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn445">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn446">2:446</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn447">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sārathi</b> denotes the ‘charioteer’ as opposed to the warrior (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="सव्यष्ठा">savyaṣṭhā</a></b>) in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203558">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203558">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203558">↑</a> i. 55, 7; 144, 3; ii. 19, 6; vi. 20, 5; 57, 6; x. 102, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203558"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203558">↑</a> Av. xv. 2, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 9, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 8, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 296.</li></ol></div>

सारमेय|సారమేయ|ಸಾರಮೇಯ|sārameya|saarameya|_vei_120@3559|_vei2_pn447
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3558">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3560">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn446">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn447">2:447</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn448">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sārameya,</b> ‘descendant of Saramā,’ Indra's mythical dog, is applied to a dog on earth in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203559"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203559">1</a></sup> as also to the dogs of <a class="vei_120a" href="यम">yama</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203559"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203559">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203559"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203559">↑</a> vii. 55, 2 (unless that passage be deemed to refer to the souls of the departed).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203559"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203559">↑</a> x. 14, 10.</li></ol></div>

सार्ञ्जय|సార్ఞ్జయ|ಸಾರ್ಞ್ಜಯ|sārñjaya|saar~njaya|_vei_120@3560|_vei2_pn447
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3559">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3561">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn446">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn447">2:447</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn448">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sārñjaya</b> is found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203560"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203560">1</a></sup> in a Dānastuti (‘praise of gifts’) where the word probably denotes the ‘<b><a class="vei_120a" href="सृञ्जय">sṛñjaya</a></b> king’ rather than a ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="सृञ्जय">sṛñjaya</a>.’ According to the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203560"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203560">2</a></sup> he was <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रस्तोक">prastoka</a>,</b> mentioned, in the same hymn, but this conclusion is not very cogent. He was clearly a patron of the <b>Bharadvājas.</b> The same epithet belongs to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सहदेव">sahadeva</a>,</b> <i>alias</i> <b>Suplan.</b></p><p>^3 Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 4, 4; xii. 8, 2, 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 104, 105.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203560"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203560">↑</a> vi. 47, 25.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203560"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203560">↑</a> xvi. 11, 11.</li></ol></div>

सार्प-राज्ञी|सार्प-राज्ञि|सार्पराज्ञी|सार्पराज्ञि|सार्प|राज्ञी|राज्ञि|సార్ప-రాజ్ఞీ|సార్ప-రాజ్ఞి|సార్పరాజ్ఞీ|సార్పరాజ్ఞి|సార్ప|రాజ్ఞీ|రాజ్ఞి|ಸಾರ್ಪ-ರಾಜ್ಞೀ|ಸಾರ್ಪ-ರಾಜ್ಞಿ|ಸಾರ್ಪರಾಜ್ಞೀ|ಸಾರ್ಪರಾಜ್ಞಿ|ಸಾರ್ಪ|ರಾಜ್ಞೀ|ರಾಜ್ಞಿ|sārpa-rājñī|sārpa-rājñi|sārparājñī|sārparājñi|sārpa|rājñī|rājñi|saarpa-raaj~nii|saarpa-raaj~ni|saarparaaj~nii|saarparaaj~ni|saarpa|raaj~nii|raaj~ni|saarpa-raaj~nee|saarparaaj~nee|raaj~nee|_vei_120@3561|_vei2_pn447
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3560">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3562">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn446">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn447">2:447</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn448">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sārpa-rājñī</b> in the Pañcaviṃśa (iv. 9, 4) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> (xxvii. 4) Brāhmaṇas is identical with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सर्पराज्ञी">sarparājñī</a>.</b></p></div>

सार्व-सेनि|सार्वसेनि|सार्व|सेनि|సార్వ-సేని|సార్వసేని|సార్వ|సేని|ಸಾರ್ವ-ಸೇನಿ|ಸಾರ್ವಸೇನಿ|ಸಾರ್ವ|ಸೇನಿ|sārva-seni|sārvaseni|sārva|seni|saarva-seni|saarvaseni|saarva|_vei_120@3562|_vei2_pn447
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3561">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3563">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn446">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn447">2:447</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn448">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sārva-seni</b> (‘descendant of Sarvasena’) is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शौचेय">śauceya</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (vii. 1, 10, 3).</p></div>

साला-वृक|सालावृक|साला|वृक|సాలా-వృక|సాలావృక|సాలా|వృక|ಸಾಲಾ-ವೃಕ|ಸಾಲಾವೃಕ|ಸಾಲಾ|ವೃಕ|sālā-vṛka|sālāvṛka|sālā|vṛka|saalaa-vRka|saalaavRka|saalaa|vRka|saalaa-vRika|saalaavRika|vRika|saalaa-vRuka|saalaavRuka|vRuka|saalaa-vRRika|saalaavRRika|vRRika|_vei_120@3563|_vei2_pn447
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3562">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3564">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn446">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn447">2:447</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn448">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sālā-vṛka</b> is found twice in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203563"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203563">1</a></sup> apparently denoting the ‘hyæna’ or ‘wild dog.’ This sense also seems appropriate in the later narrative of the destruction of the <b>Yatis</b> by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203563"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203563">2</a></sup> who is said to have handed them over to the Sālāvṛkas. Sālāvṛkeya<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203563"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203563">3</a></sup> is a variant form of the same word, meaning literally ‘descendant of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सालावृक">sālāvṛka</a>.’ The feminine is Sālāvṛkī,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203563"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203563">4</a></sup> but in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203563"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203563">5</a></sup> it appears as <a class="vei_120a" href="सलावृकी">salāvṛkī</a>. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तरक्षु">tarakṣu</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203563"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203563">↑</a> x. 73, 2; 95, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203563"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203563">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 7, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 28, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 1 (<i>varia lectio</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203563"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203563">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 1, 4; xiii. 4, 16; xiv. 11, 28; xviii. 1, 9; xix. 4, 7; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 185 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 19, 123); <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, viii. 5; xi. 10; xxv. 6; xxxvi. 7 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 465, 466); <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 1 (according to Śaṅkarānanda's recension). In Av. ii. 27, 5, <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> is alluded to as an enemy of the Sālāvṛkas.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203563"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203563">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxviii. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203563"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203563">↑</a> vi. 2, 7, 5; also in Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 8, 3; Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 10, 17; 11, 33.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 81; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 192; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 68; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 306, who decides in favour of ‘jackal.’</li></ol></div>

सावयस|సావయస|ಸಾವಯಸ|sāvayasa|saavayasa|_vei_120@3564|_vei2_pn448
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3563">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3565">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn447">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn448">2:448</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn449">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sāvayasa</b> (‘descendant of Savayasa’) is the patronymic of Aṣāḍha, or Āṣāḍha, in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 1, 1, 7).</p></div>

सा-वर्णि|सावर्णि|वर्णि|సా-వర్ణి|సావర్ణి|వర్ణి|ಸಾ-ವರ್ಣಿ|ಸಾವರ್ಣಿ|ವರ್ಣಿ|sā-varṇi|sāvarṇi|varṇi|saa-varNi|saavarNi|varNi|_vei_120@3565|_vei2_pn448
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3564">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3566">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn447">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn448">2:448</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn449">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sā-varṇi</b> is found as a patronymic in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203565">1</a></sup> together with Sāvarṇya.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203565">2</a></sup> It is clear that no man called Savarṇa ever existed, though Roth<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203565">3</a></sup> accepted that view, and that the reference is to the mythical <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a></b> <a class="vei_120a" href="सावर्णि">sāvarṇi</a>, the descendant of the <i>sa-varṇā</i> female, who, according to the legend,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203565">4</a></sup> took the place of Saraṇyū.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203565">↑</a> x. 62, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203565">↑</a> x. 62, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203565">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203565"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203565">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society</i> 15, 179 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

सिंह|సింహ|ಸಿಂಹ|siṃha|siMha|_vei_120@3566|_vei2_pn448
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3565">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3567">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn447">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn448">2:448</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn449">↷</a><hr><p><b>Siṃha</b> denotes the ‘lion’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203566">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203566">2</a></sup> The roaring (<i>nad</i>) of the lion is often alluded to,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203566">2</a></sup> and is called thundering (<i>stanatha</i>).<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203566">4</a></sup> He wanders about (<i>ku-<a class="vei_120a" href="चर">cara</a></i>) and lives in the hills (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="गिरि">giri</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="ष्ठ">ṣṭha</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203566">5</a></sup> and is clearly the ‘dread wild beast that slays’ (<i>mṛgo <a class="vei_120a" href="भीम">bhīma</a> upahatnuḥ</i>)<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203566">6</a></sup> to which <a class="vei_120a" href="रुद्र">rudra</a> is compared. When <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>, who has entered the waters, is compared to a lion,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203566">7</a></sup> the reference may be to the lion's habit of springing on animals at drinking places. That a jackal should defeat the lion is spoken of as a marvel.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203566">8</a></sup> The lion, being dangerous to men,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203566">9</a></sup> was trapped,<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203566">10</a></sup> lain in wait for in ambush,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203566">11</a></sup> or chased by hunting bands.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1203566">12</a></sup> But dogs were terrified of lions.<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1203566">13</a></sup> The liones (<i>siṃhī</i>) was also famous for her courage: the aid given by <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a></b> against the vast host of his enemies is compared to the defeat of a lioness by a ram (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="पेत्व">petva</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1203566">14</a></sup> The gaping jaws of the lioness when attacking men are alluded to in  the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1203566">15</a></sup> The lioness is also mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1203566">16</a></sup> See also <b>Halīkṣṇa.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203566">↑</a> i. 64, 8; 95, 5; iii. 2, 11; 9, 4; 26, 5; iv. 16, 14, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203566">↑</a> Av. iv. 36, 6; v. 20, 1, 2; 21, 6; viii. 7, 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 21, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 10, etc.; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203566">↑</a> Av. iv. 36, 6; v. 20, 1, 2; 21, 6; viii. 7, 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 21, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 10, etc.; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203566">↑</a> Rv. v. 83, 3; Av. v. 21, 6; viii. 7, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203566">↑</a> Rv. i. 154, 2; x. 160, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203566">↑</a> Rv. ii. 33, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203566">↑</a> Rv. iii. 9, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203566">↑</a> Rv. x. 28, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203566">↑</a> Rv. i. 174, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203566">↑</a> Rv. x. 28, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203566">↑</a> Rv. v. 74, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1203566">↑</a> Rv. v. 15, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Strabo, xv, 1, 31.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1203566">↑</a> Av. v. 36, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1203566">↑</a> Rv. vii. 18, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1203566">↑</a> vi. 35, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1203566"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1203566">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 2, 12, 2; vi. 2, 7, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, v. 10; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 5, 1, 21; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 8, 5.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 78, 79.</li></ol></div>

सिच्|सिच्|సిచ్_౧|సిచ్|ಸಿಚ್_೧|ಸಿಚ್|sic_1|sic|sich_1|sich|_vei_120@3567|_vei2_pn449
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3566">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3568">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn448">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn449">2:449</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn450">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Sic</b> denotes the ‘border’ of a garment. The Rigveda refers to a son clutching the hem of his father's robe to attract his attention,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203567">1</a></sup> and to a mother's covering her son with the edge of her garment.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203567">2</a></sup> The word also occurs later.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203567">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203567">↑</a> iii. 53, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203567">↑</a> x. 18, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203567"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203567">↑</a> Av. xiv. 2, 51; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 1, 18.</li></ol></div>

सिच्|सिच्|సిచ్_౨|సిచ్|ಸಿಚ್_೨|ಸಿಚ್|sic_2|sic|sich_2|sich|_vei_120@3568|_vei2_pn449
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3567">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3569">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn448">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn449">2:449</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn450">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Sic</b> denotes, in the dual, the ‘wings’ of an army,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203568"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203568">1</a></sup> or, in the plural, the ‘lines.’</p><p>2) Av. xi. 9, 18; 10, 20.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 65; Geldner, <i>ibid.,</i> 3, 31.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203568"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203568">↑</a> Rv. x. 75, 4.</li></ol></div>

सिच्|सिच्|సిచ్_౩|సిచ్|ಸಿಚ್_೩|ಸಿಚ್|sic_3|sic|sich_3|sich|_vei_120@3569|_vei2_pn449
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3568">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3570">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn448">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn449">2:449</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn450">↷</a><hr><p>3. <b>Sic</b> seems in one passage of the Rigveda (i. 95, 7), where it is used in the dual, to denote the ‘horizon’ (meaning literally the ‘two borders’; <i>i.e.,</i> of heaven and of earth).</p></div>

सिध्मल|సిధ్మల|ಸಿಧ್ಮಲ|sidhmala|_vei_120@3570|_vei2_pn449
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3569">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3571">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn448">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn449">2:449</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn450">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sidhmala,</b> ‘leprous,’ is found in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā (xxx. 17) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 4, 14, 1) as a designation of one of the victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’). <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="किलास">kilāsa</a>.</b></p></div>

सिनीवाली|सिनीवालि|సినీవాలీ|సినీవాలి|ಸಿನೀವಾಲೀ|ಸಿನೀವಾಲಿ|sinīvālī|sinīvāli|siniivaalii|siniivaali|sineevaalee|sineevaali|_vei_120@3571|_vei2_pn449
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3570">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3572">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn448">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn449">2:449</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn450">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sinīvālī</b> denotes the day of new moon and its presiding spirit, which, in accordance with widespread ideas concerning the connexion of the moon and vegetation, is one of fertility and growth. It occurs very frequently from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203571"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203571">1</a></sup> onwards.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203571"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203571">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203571"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203571">↑</a> ii. 32, 7, 8; x. 184, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203571"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203571">↑</a> Av. ii. 26, 2; vi. 11, 3; ix. 4, 14; xiv. 2, 15; xix. 31, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 4, 6, 2; iii. 4, 9, 1. 6; v. 5, 17, 1; 6, 18, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxv. 2, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 352; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 125.</li></ol></div>

सिन्धु|సిన్ధు|ಸಿನ್ಧು|sindhu|_vei_120@3572|_vei2_pn450
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3571">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3573">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn449">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn450">2:450</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn451">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sindhu</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203572"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203572">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203572"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203572">2</a></sup> often means ‘stream’ merely (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सप्त सिन्धवः">sapta sindhavaḥ</a></b>), but it has also<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203572"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203572">3</a></sup> the more exact sense of ‘the stream’ <i>par excellence,</i> ‘the Indus.’ The name is, however, rarely mentioned after the period of the Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203572"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203572">4</a></sup> always then occurring in such a way as to suggest distance. The horses from the Indus (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सैन्धव">saindhava</a></i>) were famous.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203572"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203572">5</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सैन्धव">saindhava</a>.</b> <i>Cf.</i> also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203572"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203572">↑</a> i. 97, 8; 125, 5; ii. 11, 9; 25, 3, 5; iii. 53, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203572"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203572">↑</a> iii. 13, 1; iv. 24, 2; x. 4, 15; xiii. 3, 50, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203572"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203572">↑</a> Rv. i. 122, 6; 126, 1; iv. 54, 6; 55, 3; v. 53, 9; vii. 95, 1; viii. 12, 3; 25, 14; 20, 25; 26, 18; x. 64, 9; Av. xii. 1, 3; xiv. 1, 43; perhaps also vi. 24, 1; vii. 45, 1; xix. 38, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, viii. 59.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203572"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203572">↑</a> The <a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धु">sindhu</a>-Sauvīras occur in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 2, 14. <i>Cf.</i> Bühler, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 14, 148; Oldenberg, <i>Buddha,</i> 394, n.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203572"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203572">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 2, 15 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 1, 13 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 16, 17, 27.</li></ol></div>

सिन्धु-क्षित्|सिन्धुक्षित्|सिन्धु|क्षित्|సిన్ధు-క్షిత్|సిన్ధుక్షిత్|సిన్ధు|క్షిత్|ಸಿನ್ಧು-ಕ್ಷಿತ್|ಸಿನ್ಧುಕ್ಷಿತ್|ಸಿನ್ಧು|ಕ್ಷಿತ್|sindhu-kṣit|sindhukṣit|sindhu|kṣit|sindhu-kShit|sindhukShit|kShit|sindhu-xit|sindhuxit|xit|_vei_120@3573|_vei2_pn450
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3572">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3574">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn449">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn450">2:450</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn451">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sindhu-kṣit</b> is the name of a long-banished but finally restored <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्यर्षि">rājanyarṣi</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203573"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203573">1</a></sup> probably quite a mythical personage.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203573"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203573">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203573"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203573">↑</a> xii. 12, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203573"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203573">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 235, n. 3.</li></ol></div>

सिरी|सिरि|సిరీ|సిరి|ಸಿರೀ|ಸಿರಿ|sirī|siri|sirii|siree|_vei_120@3574|_vei2_pn450
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3573">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3575">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn449">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn450">2:450</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn451">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sirī</b> in the Rigveda (x. 71, 9) seems to denote a ‘female weaver.’</p></div>

सिलाची|सिलाचि|సిలాచీ|సిలాచి|ಸಿಲಾಚೀ|ಸಿಲಾಚಿ|silācī|silāci|silaachii|silaachi|silaachee|_vei_120@3575|_vei2_pn450
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3574">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3576">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn449">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn450">2:450</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn451">↷</a><hr><p><b>Silācī</b> is, in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203575"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203575">1</a></sup> the name of a healing plant, also called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="लाक्षा">lākṣā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203575"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203575">↑</a> v. 5, 1, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 419; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 228.</li></ol></div>

सिलाञ्जाला|సిలాఞ్జాలా|ಸಿಲಾಞ್ಜಾಲಾ|silāñjālā|silaa~njaalaa|_vei_120@3576|_vei2_pn450
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3575">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3577">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn449">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn450">2:450</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn451">↷</a><hr><p><b>Silāñjālā,</b> which the commentator reads as Śalāñjālā, is the name of a plant, perhaps a ‘grain creeper,’ in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203576"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203576">1</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203576"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203576">2</a></sup> reads the word as Śilāñjālā. <i>Cf.</i> <b>Silāeī.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203576"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203576">↑</a> vi. 16, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203576"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203576">↑</a> ii. 16. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 466; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 292, 293.</li></ol></div>

सीचापू|సీచాపూ|ಸೀಚಾಪೂ|sīcāpū|siichaapuu|seechaapoo|_vei_120@3577|_vei2_pn451
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3576">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3578">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn450">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn451">2:451</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn452">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sīcāpū</b> in the list of victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203577"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203577">1</a></sup> seems to denote a kind of bird.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203577"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203577">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 19, 6; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 25. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 94.</li></ol></div>

सीता|సీతా|ಸೀತಾ|sītā|siitaa|seetaa|_vei_120@3578|_vei2_pn451
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3577">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3579">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn450">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn451">2:451</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn452">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sītā,</b> ‘furrow,’ occurs in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203578"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203578">1</a></sup> and often later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203578"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203578">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203578"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203578">↑</a> iv. 57, 6. 7 (the most agricultural of Rigvedic hymns, and probably late).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203578"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203578">↑</a> Av. xi. 3, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 2, 5, 4. 5; 6, 2, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xx. 3, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 86, n.</li></ol></div>

सीमन्|సీమన్|ಸೀಮನ್|sīman|siiman|seeman|_vei_120@3579|_vei2_pn451
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3578">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3580">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn450">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn451">2:451</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn452">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sīman</b> denotes the ‘parting’ of the hair in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203579">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203579">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203579">↑</a> ix. 8, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203579"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203579">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 7, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 1; xv. 5, 20; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 4, 1, 14. <i>Cf. sīmanta</i> in Av. vi. 134, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 17, 3.</li></ol></div>

सीर|సీర|ಸೀರ|sīra|siira|seera|_vei_120@3580|_vei2_pn451
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3579">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3581">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn450">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn451">2:451</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn452">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sīra,</b> ‘plough,’ is mentioned in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203580"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203580">1</a></sup> and often later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203580"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203580">2</a></sup> It was large and heavy, as is shown by the fact that six oxen,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203580"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203580">3</a></sup> or eight,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203580"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203580">4</a></sup> or twelve,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203580"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203580">5</a></sup> or even twenty-four,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203580"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203580">6</a></sup> were used to drag it. The animals which drew the plough were oxen, which were, no doubt, yoked and harnessed with traces.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203580"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203580">7</a></sup> The ox was guided by the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अष्ट्रा">aṣṭrā</a>,</b> or ‘goad,’ of the ploughman (<i>cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैश्य">vaiśya</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203580"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203580">8</a></sup> Little is known of the parts of the plough. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="लांगल">lāṅgala</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="फाल">phāla</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203580"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203580">↑</a> iv. 57, 8; x. 101, 3, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203580"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203580">↑</a> Av. vi. 30, 1; 91, 1; viii. 9, 16, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 1, 2; ii. 5, 8, 12; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 7; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 11, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203580"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203580">↑</a> Av. vi. 91, 1; viii. 9, 16; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 2, 5, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 2; xx. 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 2, 2, 6; xiii. 8, 2, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203580"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203580">↑</a> Av. vi. 91, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203580"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203580">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 7, 1; v. 2, 5, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203580"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203580">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 2. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 244. n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203580"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203580">↑</a> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वरत्रा">varatrā</a></b> is found in Rv. iv. 57, 4, and (of the ox in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मुद्गल">mudgala</a></b> story) in x. 102, 8. It may denote the fastening of the ox to the yoke rather than to the plough by traces.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203580"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203580">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. iv. 57, 4; x. 102, 8.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 236, 237.</li></ol></div>

सील|సీల|ಸೀಲ|sīla|siila|seela|_vei_120@3581|_vei2_pn451
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3580">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3582">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn450">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn451">2:451</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn452">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sīla,</b> ‘plough,’ is found in the Kapiṣṭhala Saṃhitā (xxviii. 8). </p></div>

सीलमावती|सीलमावति|సీలమావతీ|సీలమావతి|ಸೀಲಮಾವತೀ|ಸೀಲಮಾವತಿ|sīlamāvatī|sīlamāvati|siilamaavatii|siilamaavati|seelamaavatee|seelamaavati|_vei_120@3582|_vei2_pn452
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3581">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3583">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn451">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn452">2:452</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn453">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sīlamāvatī</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203582"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203582">1</a></sup> is, according to Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203582"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203582">2</a></sup> the name of a river; but this is most improbable.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203582"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203582">3</a></sup> Sāyaṇa thinks the word means ‘rich in hemp.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203582"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203582">↑</a> x. 75, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203582"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203582">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 200.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203582"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203582">↑</a> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 429; Bo7htlingk, Dictionary, <i>s.v.;</i> Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 195.</li></ol></div>

सीस|సీస|ಸೀಸ|sīsa|siisa|seesa|_vei_120@3583|_vei2_pn452
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3582">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3584">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn451">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn452">2:452</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn453">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sīsa,</b> ‘lead,’ occurs first in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203583">1</a></sup> where it is mentioned as used for amulets.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203583">2</a></sup> The word is then quite common.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203583">3</a></sup> The use of lead by the weaver as a weight is perhaps also referred to.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203583">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203583">↑</a> xii. 2, 1. 19 <i>et seq.,</i> 53.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203583">↑</a> i. 16, 2, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203583">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 4, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xviii. 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 12, 6, 5; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1, 2, 14; 4, 1, 9; xii. 7, 1, 7; 2, 10; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 17, 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203583"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203583">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 80; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 11, 9; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 4. This is the view of Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> and of Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 53. But Griffith, Translation of the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, 183, n., thinks that in xix. 80 lead is referred to not as a weight, but as a charm against demons and sorcery.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 157, 158.</li></ol></div>

सु-कन्या|सुकन्या|कन्या|సు-కన్యా|సుకన్యా|కన్యా|ಸು-ಕನ್ಯಾ|ಸುಕನ್ಯಾ|ಕನ್ಯಾ|su-kanyā|sukanyā|kanyā|su-kanyaa|sukanyaa|kanyaa|_vei_120@3584|_vei2_pn452
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3583">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3585">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn451">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn452">2:452</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn453">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-kanyā</b> is the name of <b>Śaryāta's</b> daughter, who married <b><a class="vei_120a" href="च्यवन">cyavana</a></b> according to the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203584"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203584">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203584"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203584">↑</a> iv. 1, 5, 6; 10, 13; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 121 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

सु-कपर्द|सुकपर्द|कपर्द|సు-కపర్ద|సుకపర్ద|కపర్ద|ಸು-ಕಪರ್ದ|ಸುಕಪರ್ದ|ಕಪರ್ದ|su-kaparda|sukaparda|kaparda|_vei_120@3585|_vei2_pn452
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3584">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3586">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn451">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn452">2:452</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn453">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-kaparda.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कपर्द">kaparda</a>.</b></p></div>

सु-करीर|सुकरीर|करीर|సు-కరీర|సుకరీర|కరీర|ಸು-ಕರೀರ|ಸುಕರೀರ|ಕರೀರ|su-karīra|sukarīra|karīra|su-kariira|sukariira|kariira|su-kareera|sukareera|kareera|_vei_120@3586|_vei2_pn452
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3585">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3587">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn451">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn452">2:452</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn453">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-karīra</b> in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā (ii. 7, 5) is a misreading of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सु-कुरीर">su-kurīra</a>.</i> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरीर">kurīra</a>.</b></p></div>

सु-कीर्ति काक्षीवत|सुकीर्ति काक्षीवत|कीर्ति काक्षीवत|సు-కీర్తి కాక్షీవత|సుకీర్తి కాక్షీవత|కీర్తి కాక్షీవత|ಸು-ಕೀರ್ತಿ ಕಾಕ್ಷೀವತ|ಸುಕೀರ್ತಿ ಕಾಕ್ಷೀವತ|ಕೀರ್ತಿ ಕಾಕ್ಷೀವತ|su-kīrti kākṣīvata|sukīrti kākṣīvata|kīrti kākṣīvata|su-kiirti kaakShiivata|sukiirti kaakShiivata|kiirti kaakShiivata|su-keerti kaakSheevata|sukeerti kaakSheevata|keerti kaakSheevata|su-kiirti kaaxiivata|sukiirti kaaxiivata|kiirti kaaxiivata|_vei_120@3587|_vei2_pn452
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3586">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3588">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn451">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn452">2:452</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn453">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-kīrti Kākṣīvata</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a></b>’) is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> to whom the Brāhmaṇas of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203587"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203587">1</a></sup> ascribe the authorship of a Vedic hymn.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203587"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203587">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203587"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203587">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 15, 4; vi. 29, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxx. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203587"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203587">↑</a> x. 131.</li></ol></div>

सु-केशिन् भारद्वाज|सुकेशिन् भारद्वाज|केशिन् भारद्वाज|సు-కేశిన్ భారద్వాజ|సుకేశిన్ భారద్వాజ|కేశిన్ భారద్వాజ|ಸು-ಕೇಶಿನ್ ಭಾರದ್ವಾಜ|ಸುಕೇಶಿನ್ ಭಾರದ್ವಾಜ|ಕೇಶಿನ್ ಭಾರದ್ವಾಜ|su-keśin bhāradvāja|sukeśin bhāradvāja|keśin bhāradvāja|su-keshin bhaaradvaaja|sukeshin bhaaradvaaja|keshin bhaaradvaaja|_vei_120@3588|_vei2_pn452
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3587">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3589">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn451">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn452">2:452</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn453">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-<a class="vei_120a" href="केशिन्">keśin</a> Bhāradvāja</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a></b>’) is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रश्न">praśna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (i. 1). </p></div>

सु-कुरीर|सुकुरीर|कुरीर|సు-కురీర|సుకురీర|కురీర|ಸು-ಕುರೀರ|ಸುಕುರೀರ|ಕುರೀರ|su-kurīra|sukurīra|kurīra|su-kuriira|sukuriira|kuriira|su-kureera|sukureera|kureera|_vei_120@3589|_vei2_pn453
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3588">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3590">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn452">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn453">2:453</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn454">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-kurīra.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुरीर">kurīra</a>.</b></p></div>

सु-ख|सुख|ख|సు-ఖ|సుఖ|ఖ|ಸು-ಖ|ಸುಖ|ಖ|su-kha|sukha|kha|_vei_120@3590|_vei2_pn453
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3589">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3591">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn452">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn453">2:453</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn454">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-kha.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ख">kha</a>.</b></p></div>

सुगन्धि-तेजन|सुगन्धितेजन|सुगन्धि|तेजन|సుగన్ధి-తేజన|సుగన్ధితేజన|సుగన్ధి|తేజన|ಸುಗನ್ಧಿ-ತೇಜನ|ಸುಗನ್ಧಿತೇಜನ|ಸುಗನ್ಧಿ|ತೇಜನ|sugandhi-tejana|sugandhitejana|sugandhi|tejana|_vei_120@3591|_vei2_pn453
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3590">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3592">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn452">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn453">2:453</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn454">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sugandhi-tejana</b> in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203591"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203591">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203591"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203591">2</a></sup> denotes a kind of fragrant grass.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203591"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203591">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 8, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxv. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203591"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203591">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 28, 28; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 5, 2, 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxiv. 13, 5.</li></ol></div>

सु-चित्त शैलन|सुचित्त शैलन|चित्त शैलन|సు-చిత్త శైలన|సుచిత్త శైలన|చిత్త శైలన|ಸು-ಚಿತ್ತ ಶೈಲನ|ಸುಚಿತ್ತ ಶೈಲನ|ಚಿತ್ತ ಶೈಲನ|su-citta śailana|sucitta śailana|citta śailana|su-chitta shailana|suchitta shailana|chitta shailana|_vei_120@3592|_vei2_pn453
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3591">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3593">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn452">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn453">2:453</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn454">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-<a class="vei_120a" href="चित्त">citta</a> Śailana</b> is the name of a teacher in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 14, 4).</p></div>

सु-जात|सुजात|जात|సు-జాత|సుజాత|జాత|ಸು-ಜಾತ|ಸುಜಾತ|ಜಾತ|su-jāta|sujāta|jāta|su-jaata|sujaata|jaata|_vei_120@3593|_vei2_pn453
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3592">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3594">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn452">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn453">2:453</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn454">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-jāta,</b> ‘well-born,’ is an epithet found applied to men in a few passages of the Rigveda. It would probably be a mistake to press the sense so as to denote ‘nobles’ as compared with the people. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a>.</b></p><p>^1 ii. 2, 11; v. 6, 2; vii. 1, 4. 15; viii. 20, 8.</p></div>

सुतं-भर|सुतंभर|सुतं|भर|సుతం-భర|సుతంభర|సుతం|భర|ಸುತಂ-ಭರ|ಸುತಂಭರ|ಸುತಂ|ಭರ|sutaṃ-bhara|sutaṃbhara|sutaṃ|bhara|sutaM-bhara|sutaMbhara|sutaM|_vei_120@3594|_vei2_pn453
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3593">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3595">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn452">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn453">2:453</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn454">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sutaṃ-bhara</b> is credited by the <b>Anukramaṇī</b> (Index) with the authorship of certain hymns of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203594">1</a></sup> The word does not occur in those hymns, but it appears as an adjective (‘carrying away Soma’) elsewhere,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203594">2</a></sup> and may, in a second passage,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203594">3</a></sup> by a conjecture<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203594">4</a></sup> be taken as a man's name.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203594">↑</a> v. 11-14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203594">↑</a> v. 44, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203594">↑</a> ix. 6, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203594"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203594">↑</a> If <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सुतं">sutaṃ</a>-bharāya</i> be read for <i>sutdṃ bhárāya,</i> as Roth suggests in the St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

सुत्वन् कैरिशि भार्गायण|సుత్వన్ కైరిశి భార్గాయణ|ಸುತ್ವನ್ ಕೈರಿಶಿ ಭಾರ್ಗಾಯಣ|sutvan kairiśi bhārgāyaṇa|sutvan kairishi bhaargaayaNa|_vei_120@3595|_vei2_pn453
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3594">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3596">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn452">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn453">2:453</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn454">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sutvan <a class="vei_120a" href="कैरिशि">kairiśi</a> Bhārgāyaṇa</b> is, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 28, 18), the name of a king who, being taught a spell by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मैत्रेय">maitreya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषारव">kauṣārava</a>,</b> slew five kings and became great.</p></div>

सु-दक्षिण क्षैमि|सुदक्षिण क्षैमि|दक्षिण क्षैमि|సు-దక్షిణ క్షైమి|సుదక్షిణ క్షైమి|దక్షిణ క్షైమి|ಸು-ದಕ್ಷಿಣ ಕ್ಷೈಮಿ|ಸುದಕ್ಷಿಣ ಕ್ಷೈಮಿ|ದಕ್ಷಿಣ ಕ್ಷೈಮಿ|su-dakṣiṇa kṣaimi|sudakṣiṇa kṣaimi|dakṣiṇa kṣaimi|su-dakShiNa kShaimi|sudakShiNa kShaimi|dakShiNa kShaimi|su-daxiNa xaimi|sudaxiNa xaimi|daxiNa xaimi|_vei_120@3596|_vei2_pn453
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3595">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3597">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn452">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn453">2:453</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn454">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-dakṣiṇa Kṣaimi</b> (‘descendant of Kṣema’) is the name of a teacher in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 6, 3; 7, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> 8, 6). </p></div>

सु-दत्त पाराशर्य|सुदत्त पाराशर्य|दत्त पाराशर्य|సు-దత్త పారాశర్య|సుదత్త పారాశర్య|దత్త పారాశర్య|ಸು-ದತ್ತ ಪಾರಾಶರ್ಯ|ಸುದತ್ತ ಪಾರಾಶರ್ಯ|ದತ್ತ ಪಾರಾಶರ್ಯ|su-datta pārāśarya|sudatta pārāśarya|datta pārāśarya|su-datta paaraasharya|sudatta paaraasharya|datta paaraasharya|_vei_120@3597|_vei2_pn454
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3596">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3598">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn453">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn454">2:454</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn455">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-<a class="vei_120a" href="दत्त">datta</a> Pārāśarya</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पराशर">parāśara</a></b>’) is in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 41, 1; iv. 17, 1) the name of a teacher who was a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनश्रुत">janaśruta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वारक्य">vārakya</a>.</b></p></div>

सु-दामन्|सुदामन्|दामन्|సు-దామన్|సుదామన్|దామన్|ಸು-ದಾಮನ್|ಸುದಾಮನ್|ದಾಮನ್|su-dāman|sudāman|dāman|su-daaman|sudaaman|daaman|_vei_120@3598|_vei2_pn454
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3597">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3599">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn453">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn454">2:454</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn455">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-dāman</b> is the name of a river in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xxii. 18, 1).</p></div>

सु-दास्|सु-दा|सुदास्|सुदा|दास्|సు-దాస్|సు-దా|సుదాస్|సుదా|దాస్|ಸು-ದಾಸ್|ಸು-ದಾ|ಸುದಾಸ್|ಸುದಾ|ದಾಸ್|su-dās|su-dā|sudās|sudā|dās|su-daas|su-daa|sudaas|sudaa|daas|_vei_120@3599|_vei2_pn454
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3598">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3600">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn453">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn454">2:454</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn455">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-dās</b> is the name of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तृत्सु">tṛtsu</a></b> king who won a famous victory over the ten kings, as described in a hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203599">1</a></sup> At one time <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a></b> was his <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a>,</b> and accompanied him in his victorious raids over the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विपाश्">vipāś</a></b> (Beās) and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुतुद्री">śutudrī</a></b> (Sutlej).<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203599">2</a></sup> The Aśvins gave him a queen, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदेवी">sudevī</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203599">3</a></sup> and also helped him on another occasion.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203599">4</a></sup> He appears with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a></b> in a late hymn without hint of rivalry,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203599">5</a></sup> but elsewhere he seems to be referred to as defeated by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरुकुत्स">purukutsa</a>,</b> Trasadasyu's father.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203599">6</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203599">7</a></sup> he is recognized as a great king, with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a></b> as his <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरो">puro</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="हित">hita</a>, and similarly in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>,<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203599">8</a></sup> where his generosity to his priest is related.</p><p>His exact ancestry is a little uncertain, because he is called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पैजवन">paijavana</a>,</b> ‘son of <a class="vei_120a" href="पिजवन">pijavana</a>,’ as <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a> explains the patronymic. If this explanation is correct, <b>Divodāsa</b> must have been his grandfather. If he was the son of Divodāsa, <a class="vei_120a" href="पिजवन">pijavana</a> must be understood as a more remote ancestor. The former alternative seems the more probable. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="दाशराज्ञ">dāśarājña</a>. <a class="vei_120a" href="पैजवन">paijavana</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सौदास">saudāsa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203599">↑</a> vii. 18. See also Rv. vii. 20, 2; 25, 3; 32, 10; 33, 3; 64, 3; 83, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203599">↑</a> Rv. iii. 53, 9, 11. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a></b> and <b>Vaṣiṣṭha.</b></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203599">↑</a> Rv. i. 112, 19.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203599">↑</a> Rv. i. 47, 9, where, however, Roth, St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 1, takes <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सु-दास्">su-dās</a></i> as an adjective (‘worshipping well’).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203599">↑</a> Rv. vii. 19, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203599">↑</a> Rv. i. 63, 7, reading <i>Sudāsam</i> for <i>Sudāse</i> with Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 174. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 112, n. 1; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 153; Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 63.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203599">↑</a> vii. 34, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203599"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203599">↑</a> xvi. 11, 14.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 107 <i>et seq.;</i> Weber, <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 31 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

सु-देव|सु-देव|सुदेव|देव|సు-దేవ_౧|సు-దేవ|సుదేవ|దేవ|ಸು-ದೇವ_೧|ಸು-ದೇವ|ಸುದೇವ|ದೇವ|su-deva_1|su-deva|sudeva|deva|_vei_120@3600|_vei2_pn454
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3599">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3601">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn453">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn454">2:454</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn455">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Su-deva</b> is, according to Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203600"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203600">1</a></sup> the proper name of a sacrificer in one hymn of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203600"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203600">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203600"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203600">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 160.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203600"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203600">↑</a> viii. 5, 6.</li></ol></div>

सु-देव काश्यप|सु-देव काश्यप|सुदेव काश्यप|देव काश्यप|సు-దేవ కాశ్యప_౨|సు-దేవ కాశ్యప|సుదేవ కాశ్యప|దేవ కాశ్యప|ಸು-ದೇವ ಕಾಶ್ಯಪ_೨|ಸು-ದೇವ ಕಾಶ್ಯಪ|ಸುದೇವ ಕಾಶ್ಯಪ|ದೇವ ಕಾಶ್ಯಪ|su-deva kāśyapa_2|su-deva kāśyapa|sudeva kāśyapa|deva kāśyapa|su-deva kaashyapa_2|su-deva kaashyapa|sudeva kaashyapa|deva kaashyapa|_vei_120@3601|_vei2_pn455
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3600">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3602">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn454">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn455">2:455</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn456">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Su-<a class="vei_120a" href="देव">deva</a> Kāśyapa</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कश्यप">kaśyapa</a></b>’) is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203601"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203601">1</a></sup> who set forth the expiation for lack of chastity.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203601"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203601">↑</a> ii. 18. <i>Cf.</i> x. 1, 8; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 188, n.; 10, 103.</li></ol></div>

सु-देवला|सुदेवला|देवला|సు-దేవలా|సుదేవలా|దేవలా|ಸು-ದೇವಲಾ|ಸುದೇವಲಾ|ದೇವಲಾ|su-devalā|sudevalā|devalā|su-devalaa|sudevalaa|devalaa|_vei_120@3602|_vei2_pn455
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3601">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3603">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn454">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn455">2:455</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn456">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-devalā</b> was the name of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋतुपर्ण">ṛtuparṇa</a></b> as a woman according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (xx. 12).</p></div>

सु-देवी|सु-देवि|सुदेवी|सुदेवि|देवी|देवि|సు-దేవీ|సు-దేవి|సుదేవీ|సుదేవి|దేవీ|దేవి|ಸು-ದೇವೀ|ಸು-ದೇವಿ|ಸುದೇವೀ|ಸುದೇವಿ|ದೇವೀ|ದೇವಿ|su-devī|su-devi|sudevī|sudevi|devī|devi|su-devii|sudevii|devii|su-devee|sudevee|devee|_vei_120@3603|_vei2_pn455
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3602">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3604">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn454">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn455">2:455</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn456">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-devī.</b> See <b>Sudas.</b></p></div>

सु-धन्वन् आंगिरस|सुधन्वन् आंगिरस|धन्वन् आंगिरस|सु-धन्वन् आङ्गिरस|सुधन्वन् आङ्गिरस|धन्वन् आङ्गिरस|సు-ధన్వన్ ఆంగిరస|సుధన్వన్ ఆంగిరస|ధన్వన్ ఆంగిరస|సు-ధన్వన్ ఆఙ్గిరస|సుధన్వన్ ఆఙ్గిరస|ధన్వన్ ఆఙ్గిరస|ಸು-ಧನ್ವನ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಸುಧನ್ವನ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಧನ್ವನ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಸು-ಧನ್ವನ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|ಸುಧನ್ವನ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|ಧನ್ವನ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|su-dhanvan āṅgirasa|sudhanvan āṅgirasa|dhanvan āṅgirasa|su-dhanvan aaMgirasa|sudhanvan aaMgirasa|dhanvan aaMgirasa|su-dhanvan aangirasa|sudhanvan aangirasa|dhanvan aangirasa|_vei_120@3604|_vei2_pn455
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3603">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3605">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn454">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn455">2:455</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn456">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-<a class="vei_120a" href="धन्वन्">dhanvan</a> Āṅgirasa</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अंगिरस्">aṅgiras</a></b>’) is the name of a teacher in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iii. 3, 1).</p></div>

सु-नीथ शौचद्-रथ|सु-नीथ शौचद्-रथ|सु-नीथ शौचद्रथ|सु-नीथ शौचद्|रथ|సు-నీథ శౌచద్-రథ_౧|సు-నీథ శౌచద్-రథ|సు-నీథ శౌచద్రథ|సు-నీథ శౌచద్|రథ|ಸು-ನೀಥ ಶೌಚದ್-ರಥ_೧|ಸು-ನೀಥ ಶೌಚದ್-ರಥ|ಸು-ನೀಥ ಶೌಚದ್ರಥ|ಸು-ನೀಥ ಶೌಚದ್|ರಥ|su-nītha śaucad-ratha_1|su-nītha śaucad-ratha|su-nītha śaucadratha|su-nītha śaucad|ratha|su-niitha shauchad-ratha_1|su-niitha shauchad-ratha|su-niitha shauchadratha|su-niitha shauchad|su-neetha shauchad-ratha_1|su-neetha shauchad-ratha|su-neetha shauchadratha|su-neetha shauchad|_vei_120@3605|_vei2_pn455
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3604">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3606">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn454">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn455">2:455</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn456">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Su-<a class="vei_120a" href="नीथ">nītha</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शौचद्">śaucad</a>-ratha</b> (‘descendant of Śucadratha’) is the name of a man in the Rigveda (v. 79, 2). <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सत्यश्रवस्">satyaśravas</a>.</b></p></div>

सु-नीथ कापटव|सु-नीथ कापटव|सुनीथ कापटव|नीथ कापटव|సు-నీథ కాపటవ_౨|సు-నీథ కాపటవ|సునీథ కాపటవ|నీథ కాపటవ|ಸು-ನೀಥ ಕಾಪಟವ_೨|ಸು-ನೀಥ ಕಾಪಟವ|ಸುನೀಥ ಕಾಪಟವ|ನೀಥ ಕಾಪಟವ|su-nītha kāpaṭava_2|su-nītha kāpaṭava|sunītha kāpaṭava|nītha kāpaṭava|su-niitha kaapaTava_2|su-niitha kaapaTava|suniitha kaapaTava|niitha kaapaTava|su-neetha kaapaTava_2|su-neetha kaapaTava|suneetha kaapaTava|neetha kaapaTava|_vei_120@3606|_vei2_pn455
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3605">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3607">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn454">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn455">2:455</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn456">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Su-<a class="vei_120a" href="नीथ">nītha</a> Kāpaṭava</b> is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203606"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203606">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203606"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203606">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372.</li></ol></div>

सु-पर्ण|सु-पर्ण|सुपर्ण|पर्ण|సు-పర్ణ_౧|సు-పర్ణ|సుపర్ణ|పర్ణ|ಸು-ಪರ್ಣ_೧|ಸು-ಪರ್ಣ|ಸುಪರ್ಣ|ಪರ್ಣ|su-parṇa_1|su-parṇa|suparṇa|parṇa|su-parNa_1|su-parNa|suparNa|parNa|_vei_120@3607|_vei2_pn455
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3606">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3608">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn454">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn455">2:455</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn456">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Su-parṇa,</b> ‘well-winged,’ designates a large bird of prey, the ‘eagle’ or the ‘vulture,’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203607">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203607">2</a></sup> In the passages in which it appears as an eater of carrion<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203607">3</a></sup> it must be the vulture. The Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203607">4</a></sup> mentions an eagle which separates milk from water like the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रुञ्च्">kruñc</a>.</b> In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203607">5</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="सुपर्ण">suparṇa</a> is said to be the child of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्येन">śyena</a>,</b> and is distinguished from the latter in another passage:<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203607">6</a></sup> this led Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203607">7</a></sup> to think that the falcon is probably meant.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203607">8</a></sup> The Atharvaveda alludes to its cry,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203607">9</a></sup> and describes it as living in the hills.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203607">10</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203607">↑</a> i. 164, 20; ii. 42, 2; iv. 26, 4; viii. 100, 8; ix. 48, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203607">↑</a> Av. i. 24, 1; ii. 27. 2; 30, 3; iv. 6, 3, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 8, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203607">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 9, 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka, iv. 29.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203607">↑</a> ii. 438 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 19, 101).</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203607">↑</a> x. 144, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203607">↑</a> ii. 42, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203607">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 88.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203607">↑</a> In the post-Vedic period <a class="vei_120a" href="सुपर्ण">suparṇa</a> became a mythical bird, identified with Viṣnu's vehicle, Garuḍa, who, however, is also regarded as king of the Suparṇas.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203607">↑</a> ii. 30, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203607"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203607">↑</a> v. 4, 2.</li></ol></div>

सुपर्ण|सुपर्ण|సుపర్ణ_౨|సుపర్ణ|ಸುಪರ್ಣ_೨|ಸುಪರ್ಣ|suparṇa_2|suparṇa|suparNa_2|suparNa|_vei_120@3608|_vei2_pn456
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3607">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3609">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn455">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn456">2:456</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn457">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Suparṇa</b> is personified in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203608"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203608">1</a></sup> as a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203608"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203608">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 3, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxix. 7.</li></ol></div>

सु-पित्र्य|सुपित्र्य|पित्र्य|సు-పిత్ర్య|సుపిత్ర్య|పిత్ర్య|ಸು-ಪಿತ್ರ್ಯ|ಸುಪಿತ್ರ್ಯ|ಪಿತ್ರ್ಯ|su-pitrya|supitrya|pitrya|_vei_120@3609|_vei2_pn456
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3608">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3610">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn455">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn456">2:456</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn457">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-pitrya,</b> a word occurring once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203609"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203609">1</a></sup> is probably an adjective (‘maintaining his paternal character well’). Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203609"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203609">2</a></sup> however, regards it, but without any great probability, as a proper name.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203609"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203609">↑</a> x. 115, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203609"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203609">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 169.</li></ol></div>

सु-प्रतीत औलुण्ड्य|सुप्रतीत औलुण्ड्य|प्रतीत औलुण्ड्य|సు-ప్రతీత ఔలుణ్డ్య|సుప్రతీత ఔలుణ్డ్య|ప్రతీత ఔలుణ్డ్య|ಸು-ಪ್ರತೀತ ಔಲುಣ್ಡ್ಯ|ಸುಪ್ರತೀತ ಔಲುಣ್ಡ್ಯ|ಪ್ರತೀತ ಔಲುಣ್ಡ್ಯ|su-pratīta auluṇḍya|supratīta auluṇḍya|pratīta auluṇḍya|su-pratiita auluNDya|supratiita auluNDya|pratiita auluNDya|su-prateeta auluNDya|suprateeta auluNDya|prateeta auluNDya|_vei_120@3610|_vei2_pn456
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3609">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3611">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn455">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn456">2:456</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn457">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-pratīta Auluṇḍya</b> is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b>Bṛhaspatigupta,</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203610"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203610">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203610"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203610">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372.</li></ol></div>

सुप्लन् सार्ञ्जय|సుప్లన్ సార్ఞ్జయ|ಸುಪ್ಲನ್ ಸಾರ್ಞ್ಜಯ|suplan sārñjaya|suplan saar~njaya|_vei_120@3611|_vei2_pn456
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3610">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3612">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn455">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn456">2:456</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn457">↷</a><hr><p><b>Suplan Sārñjaya</b> is the name of a prince of the <b>Sṛñjayas</b> who was taught the <a class="vei_120a" href="दाक्षायण">dākṣāyaṇa</a> sacrifice by <b>Pratīdarśa,</b> and took the name of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सहदेव">sahadeva</a></b> as a token of his success.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203611">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203611"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203611">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 4, 4; xii. 8, 2, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Lévi, <i>La Doctrine du Sacrifice,</i> 139; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 105, 106.</li></ol></div>

सु-बन्धु|सुबन्धु|बन्धु|సు-బన్ధు|సుబన్ధు|బన్ధు|ಸು-ಬನ್ಧು|ಸುಬನ್ಧು|ಬನ್ಧು|su-bandhu|subandhu|bandhu|_vei_120@3612|_vei2_pn456
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3611">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3613">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn455">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn456">2:456</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn457">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-bandhu</b> in the hymns of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203612">1</a></sup> is taken by Sāyaṇa to be a proper name; but this is not certain, Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203612">2</a></sup> seeing in the passages only an ordinary noun meaning ‘a good friend.’ The later tradition<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203612">3</a></sup> explains that <a class="vei_120a" href="सुबन्धु">subandhu</a> and his brothers, called <b>Gaupāyanas,</b> were priests of <b>Asamāti,</b> who cast them off and took two others, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="किरात">kirāta</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आकुलि">ākuli</a>.</b> By these two in pigeon form <a class="vei_120a" href="सुबन्धु">subandhu</a> was caused to swoon, but was revived by his three brothers, who recited certain hymns.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203612">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203612">↑</a> x. 59, 8; 60, 7, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203612">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203612">↑</a> Bṛhaddevatā, vii. 83 <i>et seq.,</i> with Macdonell's notes. See also <b>Asamāti,</b> n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203612"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203612">↑</a> Rv. x. 57-60.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 2, 420-455; Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 39, 90.</li></ol></div>

सु-ब्रह्मण्य|सुब्रह्मण्य|ब्रह्मण्य|సు-బ్రహ్మణ్య|సుబ్రహ్మణ్య|బ్రహ్మణ్య|ಸು-ಬ್ರಹ್ಮಣ್ಯ|ಸುಬ್ರಹ್ಮಣ್ಯ|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮಣ್ಯ|su-brahmaṇya|subrahmaṇya|brahmaṇya|su-brahmaNya|subrahmaNya|brahmaNya|_vei_120@3613|_vei2_pn456
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3612">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3614">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn455">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn456">2:456</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn457">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-brahmaṇya</b> in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203613">1</a></sup> denotes a priest who officiates as one of the three assistants of the Udgātṛ (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋत्विज्">ṛtvij</a></b>). His office is Subrahmaṇyā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203613">2</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203613">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 4, 6; 18, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 4, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 362, 374.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203613"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203613">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 3, 1-7. 11, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxvii. 6, etc. The priest himself is so styled, <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 1, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xviii. 9, 19, etc.</li></ol></div>

सु-भगा|सुभगा|भगा|సు-భగా|సుభగా|భగా|ಸು-ಭಗಾ|ಸುಭಗಾ|ಭಗಾ|su-bhagā|subhagā|bhagā|su-bhagaa|subhagaa|bhagaa|_vei_120@3614|_vei2_pn457
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3613">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3615">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn456">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn457">2:457</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn458">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-bhagā,</b> in the vocative <i>subhage,</i> is a frequent form of courteous address to women from the Rigveda onwards.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203614"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203614">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203614"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203614">↑</a> Rv. x. 10, 10. 12; 108, 5; Av. v. 5, 6; vi. 30, 3, etc.</li></ol></div>

सु-भद्रिका|सुभद्रिका|भद्रिका|సు-భద్రికా|సుభద్రికా|భద్రికా|ಸು-ಭದ್ರಿಕಾ|ಸುಭದ್ರಿಕಾ|ಭದ್ರಿಕಾ|su-bhadrikā|subhadrikā|bhadrikā|su-bhadrikaa|subhadrikaa|bhadrikaa|_vei_120@3615|_vei2_pn457
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3614">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3616">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn456">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn457">2:457</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn458">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-bhadrikā</b> occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) section of the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203615">1</a></sup> as in some way connected with the rite. Weber<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203615">2</a></sup> thinks that a proper name, that of the wife of the king of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काम्पील">kāmpīla</a>,</b> is intended, but Mahīdhara<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203615">3</a></sup> explains the word merely as a lady with many lovers or a courtezan, a view followed by Roth.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203615">4</a></sup> Since the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203615">5</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203615">6</a></sup> Saṃhitās have no <a class="vei_120a" href="सुभद्रिका">subhadrikā</a>, but a vocative <i>subhage</i> (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुभगा">subhagā</a></b>), the sense remains very doubtful.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203615">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 18 (<i>cf.</i> Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 2, 8, 3); Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 12, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203615">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 183, 184; <i>Indian Literature,</i> 114, 115. <i>Cf.</i> Griffith, Translation of the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, 212, n.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203615">↑</a> On Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203615">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v. subhadraka,</i> 2<i>b;</i> Bo7htlingk's Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 2<i>a.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203615">↑</a> vii. 4, 19, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 9, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203615"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203615">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>,</i> iv. 8.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 36, 37; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 321, 322.</li></ol></div>

सुमति-त्सरु|सुमतित्सरु|सुमति|त्सरु|సుమతి-త్సరు|సుమతిత్సరు|సుమతి|త్సరు|ಸುಮತಿ-ತ್ಸರು|ಸುಮತಿತ್ಸರು|ಸುಮತಿ|ತ್ಸರು|sumati-tsaru|sumatitsaru|sumati|tsaru|_vei_120@3616|_vei2_pn457
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3615">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3617">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn456">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn457">2:457</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn458">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sumati-tsaru.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्सरु">tsaru</a>.</b></p></div>

सु-मन्त्र बाभ्रव|सुमन्त्र बाभ्रव|मन्त्र बाभ्रव|సు-మన్త్ర బాభ్రవ|సుమన్త్ర బాభ్రవ|మన్త్ర బాభ్రవ|ಸು-ಮನ್ತ್ರ ಬಾಭ್ರವ|ಸುಮನ್ತ್ರ ಬಾಭ್ರವ|ಮನ್ತ್ರ ಬಾಭ್ರವ|su-mantra bābhrava|sumantra bābhrava|mantra bābhrava|su-mantra baabhrava|sumantra baabhrava|mantra baabhrava|_vei_120@3617|_vei2_pn457
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3616">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3618">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn456">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn457">2:457</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn458">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-<a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> Bābhrava</b> (‘descendant of Babhru’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a></b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गोतम">gotama</a></b>’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शूष वाह्नेय">śūṣa vāhneya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="भारद्वाज">bhāradvāja</a>,</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203617"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203617">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203617"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203617">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373.</li></ol></div>

सु-मित्र वाध्र्यस्व|सुमित्र वाध्र्यस्व|मित्र वाध्र्यस्व|సు-మిత్ర వాధ్ర్యస్వ|సుమిత్ర వాధ్ర్యస్వ|మిత్ర వాధ్ర్యస్వ|ಸು-ಮಿತ್ರ ವಾಧ್ರ್ಯಸ್ವ|ಸುಮಿತ್ರ ವಾಧ್ರ್ಯಸ್ವ|ಮಿತ್ರ ವಾಧ್ರ್ಯಸ್ವ|su-mitra vādhryasva|sumitra vādhryasva|mitra vādhryasva|su-mitra vaadhryasva|sumitra vaadhryasva|mitra vaadhryasva|_vei_120@3618|_vei2_pn457
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3617">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3619">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn456">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn457">2:457</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn458">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-<a class="vei_120a" href="मित्र">mitra</a> Vādhryasva</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वध्र्यश्व">vadhryaśva</a></b>’) is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203618"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203618">1</a></sup> where also<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203618"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203618">2</a></sup> the <b>Sumitras,</b> his family, are mentioned.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203618"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203618">↑</a> x. 69, 3. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203618"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203618">↑</a> x. 69, 1, 7, 8. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 133.</li></ol></div>

su-mīḷha|sumīḷha|mīḷha|su-miiḷha|sumiiḷha|miiḷha|su-meeḷha|sumeeḷha|meeḷha|_vei_120@3619|_vei2_pn457
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3618">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3620">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn456">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn457">2:457</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn458">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-mīḷha</b> is the name of a patron in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203619"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203619">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203619"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203619">↑</a> vi. 63, 9. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 158.</li></ol></div>

सु-मेध|सुमेध|मेध|సు-మేధ|సుమేధ|మేధ|ಸು-ಮೇಧ|ಸುಮೇಧ|ಮೇಧ|su-medha|sumedha|medha|_vei_120@3620|_vei2_pn458
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3619">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3621">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn457">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn458">2:458</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn459">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-medha</b> occurs in an obscure hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203620"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203620">1</a></sup> either as an adjective (‘of good understanding’) or a proper name, perhaps identical with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नृमेध">nṛmedha</a></b> or his brother.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203620"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203620">↑</a> x. 132, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 133; Griffith, <i>Hymns of the Rigveda,</i> 2, 579, n.</li></ol></div>

सुम्न-यु|सुम्नयु|सुम्न|సుమ్న-యు|సుమ్నయు|సుమ్న|ಸುಮ್ನ-ಯು|ಸುಮ್ನಯು|ಸುಮ್ನ|sumna-yu|sumnayu|sumna|_vei_120@3621|_vei2_pn458
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3620">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3622">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn457">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn458">2:458</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn459">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sumna-yu</b> is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) at the end of the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka (xv. 1) as a pupil of <b>Uddālaka.</b></p></div>

सु-यज्ञ शाण्डिल्य|सुयज्ञ शाण्डिल्य|यज्ञ शाण्डिल्य|సు-యజ్ఞ శాణ్డిల్య|సుయజ్ఞ శాణ్డిల్య|యజ్ఞ శాణ్డిల్య|ಸು-ಯಜ್ಞ ಶಾಣ್ಡಿಲ್ಯ|ಸುಯಜ್ಞ ಶಾಣ್ಡಿಲ್ಯ|ಯಜ್ಞ ಶಾಣ್ಡಿಲ್ಯ|su-yajña śāṇḍilya|suyajña śāṇḍilya|yajña śāṇḍilya|su-yaj~na shaaNDilya|suyaj~na shaaNDilya|yaj~na shaaNDilya|_vei_120@3622|_vei2_pn458
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3621">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3623">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn457">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn458">2:458</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn459">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-<a class="vei_120a" href="यज्ञ">yajña</a> Śāṇḍilya</b> is the name of a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कंस">kaṃsa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वारक्य">vārakya</a></b> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iv. 17, 1). Another Suyajña is a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a>,</b> author of the <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.</p></div>

सु-यवस|सुयवस|यवस|సు-యవస|సుయవస|యవస|ಸು-ಯವಸ|ಸುಯವಸ|ಯವಸ|su-yavasa|suyavasa|yavasa|_vei_120@3623|_vei2_pn458
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3622">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3624">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn457">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn458">2:458</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn459">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-yavasa</b> denotes a ‘good pasture’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203623"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203623">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203623"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203623">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203623"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203623">↑</a> i. 42, 8; vi. 28, 7; vii. 18, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203623"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203623">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 7, 5, 2, etc.</li></ol></div>

सुरा|సురా|ಸುರಾ|surā|suraa|_vei_120@3624|_vei2_pn458
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3623">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3625">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn457">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn458">2:458</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn459">↷</a><hr><p><b>Surā</b> is the name of an intoxicating ‘spirituous liquor,’ often mentioned in Vedic literature. In some passages<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203624">1</a></sup> it is referred to favourably, in others with decided disapproval.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203624">2</a></sup> It is classed with the use of meat and with dicing as an evil in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203624">3</a></sup> and often with dicing.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203624">4</a></sup> It was, as opposed to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>,</b> essentially a drink of ordinary life.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203624">5</a></sup> It was the drink of men in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सभा">sabhā</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203624">6</a></sup> and gave rise to broils.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203624">7</a></sup></p><p>Its exact nature is not certain. It may have been a strong spirit prepared from fermented grains and plants, as Eggeling<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203624">8</a></sup>  holds, or, as Whitney<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203624">9</a></sup> thought, a kind of beer or ale. Geldner<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203624">10</a></sup> renders it ‘brandy.’ It is sometimes mentioned in connexion with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मधु">madhu</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203624">11</a></sup> It was kept in skins.<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1203624">12</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203624">↑</a> Rv. i. 116, 7; x. 131, 4, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Av. iv. 34, 6; x. 6, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 3, 3, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 7, 3, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203624">↑</a> Rv. vii. 86, 6; viii. 2, 12; 21, 14; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, i. 11, 6; ii. 4, 2; iv. 2, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203624">↑</a> vi. 70, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 493.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203624">↑</a> Rv. vii. 86, 6; Av. xiv. 1, 35. 36; xv. 9, 1. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203624">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 3, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203624">↑</a> See n. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203624">↑</a> Rv. viii. 2, 12; 21, 14. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xiv. 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 3, 4; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 4, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203624">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 223, n. 2; Caland, <i>Altindisches Zauberritual,</i> 21, n. 1; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 280, 281. <i>Cf.</i> Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xix. 1, 20-27; Mahīdhara on Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203624">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 207. <i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 326.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203624">↑</a> <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 198.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203624">↑</a> Av. vi. 69, 1; ix. 1, 18, 19; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 95. See Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 251, who attempts to show that <a class="vei_120a" href="सुरा">surā</a> and <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> were rival priestly drinks at one time, belonging to different sections of the people.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1203624"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1203624">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 11, 26. <i>Cf.</i> Rv. i. 191, 10.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 121.</li></ol></div>

सुरा-कार|सुराकार|सुरा|कार|సురా-కార|సురాకార|సురా|కార|ಸುರಾ-ಕಾರ|ಸುರಾಕಾರ|ಸುರಾ|ಕಾರ|surā-kāra|surākāra|surā|kāra|suraa-kaara|suraakaara|suraa|kaara|_vei_120@3625|_vei2_pn459
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3624">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3626">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn458">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn459">2:459</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn460">↷</a><hr><p><b>Surā-kāra,</b> ‘maker of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुरा">surā</a>,</b>’ is included in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203625"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203625">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203625"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203625">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 7, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 281, who compares Rv. i. 191, 10, which may refer to such a person.</li></ol></div>

सु-राधस्|सु-राध|सुराधस्|सुराध|राधस्|राध|సు-రాధస్|సు-రాధ|సురాధస్|సురాధ|రాధస్|రాధ|ಸು-ರಾಧಸ್|ಸು-ರಾಧ|ಸುರಾಧಸ್|ಸುರಾಧ|ರಾಧಸ್|ರಾಧ|su-rādhas|su-rādha|surādhas|surādha|rādhas|rādha|su-raadhas|su-raadha|suraadhas|suraadha|raadhas|raadha|_vei_120@3626|_vei2_pn459
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3625">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3627">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn458">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn459">2:459</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn460">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-rādhas</b> is the name of a man in the Rigveda (i. 100, 17). where he is mentioned with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अम्बरीष">ambarīṣa</a></b> and others.</p></div>

सुराम|సురామ|ಸುರಾಮ|surāma|suraama|_vei_120@3627|_vei2_pn459
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3626">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3628">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn458">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn459">2:459</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn460">↷</a><hr><p><b>Surāma</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203627">1</a></sup> refers to the illness caused by drinking <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुरा">surā</a></b> to excess. <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> is described as suffering from it in the Namuci legend.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203627">2</a></sup> Later <a class="vei_120a" href="सुराम">surāma</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203627">3</a></sup> was treated as an epithet of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>,</b> meaning ‘delightful.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203627">↑</a> x. 131, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203627">↑</a> Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 148 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203627"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203627">↑</a> Or Surāman. <i>Cf.</i> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxi. 42; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 11, 4; iv. 12, 5. Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 245 <i>et seq.,</i> renders it ‘Surā mixed,’ which is doubtful.</li></ol></div>

सु-वर्ण|सुवर्ण|वर्ण|సు-వర్ణ|సువర్ణ|వర్ణ|ಸು-ವರ್ಣ|ಸುವರ್ಣ|ವರ್ಣ|su-varṇa|suvarṇa|varṇa|su-varNa|suvarNa|varNa|_vei_120@3628|_vei2_pn459
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3627">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3629">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn458">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn459">2:459</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn460">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-varṇa,</b> ‘beautiful coloured,’ is an epithet of gold (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्य">hiraṇya</a></b>), and then comes to be used as a substantive denoting ‘gold.’</p><p>^1) <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 7, 4; 8, 9, 1, etc.</p><p>^2) Av. xv. 1, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 12, 6, 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 4, 1, 8, etc.; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 6, 6; iii. 19, 1; iv. 17, 7, etc.</p></div>

सु-वसन|सुवसन|वसन|సు-వసన|సువసన|వసన|ಸು-ವಸನ|ಸುವಸನ|ವಸನ|su-vasana|suvasana|vasana|_vei_120@3629|_vei2_pn459
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3628">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3630">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn458">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn459">2:459</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn460">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-vasana</b> in the Rigveda denotes a ‘splendid garment,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203629">1</a></sup> and is also used adjectivally, ‘clothing well.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203629">2</a></sup> Su-<a class="vei_120a" href="वासस्">vāsas</a>, ‘welldressed,’ is a common adjective.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203629">3</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वासस्">vāsas</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203629">↑</a> vi. 51, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203629">↑</a> ix. 97, 50.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203629"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203629">↑</a> Rv. i. 124, 7; iii. 8, 4; x. 71, 4, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 262.</li></ol></div>

सु-वास्तु|सुवास्तु|वास्तु|సు-వాస్తు|సువాస్తు|వాస్తు|ಸು-ವಾಸ್ತು|ಸುವಾಸ್ತು|ವಾಸ್ತು|su-vāstu|suvāstu|vāstu|su-vaastu|suvaastu|vaastu|_vei_120@3630|_vei2_pn460
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3629">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3631">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn459">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn460">2:460</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn461">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-vāstu</b> (‘having fair dwellings’) is the name of a river in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203630"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203630">1</a></sup> It is clearly the Soastos of Arrian<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203630"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203630">2</a></sup> and the modern Swāt, a tributary of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुभा">kubhā</a></b> (Kabul river) which is itself an affluent of the Indus.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203630"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203630">↑</a> viii. 19, 37; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iv. 15.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203630"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203630">↑</a> <i>Indica,</i> iv. 11.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Roth, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, Erlänterungen,</i> 43; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 18; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3. 200; <i>Imperial Gazetteer of India,</i> 23, 187.</li></ol></div>

सु-शारद शालंकायन|सुशारद शालंकायन|शारद शालंकायन|सु-शारद शालङ्कायन|सुशारद शालङ्कायन|शारद शालङ्कायन|సు-శారద శాలంకాయన|సుశారద శాలంకాయన|శారద శాలంకాయన|సు-శారద శాలఙ్కాయన|సుశారద శాలఙ్కాయన|శారద శాలఙ్కాయన|ಸು-ಶಾರದ ಶಾಲಂಕಾಯನ|ಸುಶಾರದ ಶಾಲಂಕಾಯನ|ಶಾರದ ಶಾಲಂಕಾಯನ|ಸು-ಶಾರದ ಶಾಲಙ್ಕಾಯನ|ಸುಶಾರದ ಶಾಲಙ್ಕಾಯನ|ಶಾರದ ಶಾಲಙ್ಕಾಯನ|su-śārada śālaṅkāyana|suśārada śālaṅkāyana|śārada śālaṅkāyana|su-shaarada shaalaMkaayana|sushaarada shaalaMkaayana|shaarada shaalaMkaayana|su-shaarada shaalankaayana|sushaarada shaalankaayana|shaarada shaalankaayana|_vei_120@3631|_vei2_pn460
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3630">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3632">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn459">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn460">2:460</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn461">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-<a class="vei_120a" href="शारद">śārada</a> Śālaṅkāyana</b> is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऊर्जयन्त् औपमन्यव">ūrjayant aupamanyava</a>,</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203631">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203631"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203631">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372.</li></ol></div>

सु-श्रवस्|सु-श्रवस्|सु-श्रव|सुश्रवस्|सुश्रव|श्रवस्|श्रव|సు-శ్రవస్_౧|సు-శ్రవస్|సు-శ్రవ|సుశ్రవస్|సుశ్రవ|శ్రవస్|శ్రవ|ಸು-ಶ್ರವಸ್_೧|ಸು-ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಸು-ಶ್ರವ|ಸುಶ್ರವಸ್|ಸುಶ್ರವ|ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಶ್ರವ|su-śravas_1|su-śravas|su-śrava|suśravas|suśrava|śravas|śrava|su-shravas_1|su-shravas|su-shrava|sushravas|sushrava|shravas|shrava|_vei_120@3632|_vei2_pn460
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3631">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3633">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn459">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn460">2:460</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn461">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Su-śravas</b> is the name of a man in the Rigveda (i. 53, 9) according to Sāyaṇa.</p></div>

सु-श्रवस्|सु-श्रवस्|सु-श्रव|सुश्रवस्|सुश्रव|श्रवस्|श्रव|సు-శ్రవస్_౨|సు-శ్రవస్|సు-శ్రవ|సుశ్రవస్|సుశ్రవ|శ్రవస్|శ్రవ|ಸು-ಶ್ರವಸ್_೨|ಸು-ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಸು-ಶ್ರವ|ಸುಶ್ರವಸ್|ಸುಶ್ರವ|ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಶ್ರವ|su-śravas_2|su-śravas|su-śrava|suśravas|suśrava|śravas|śrava|su-shravas_2|su-shravas|su-shrava|sushravas|sushrava|shravas|shrava|_vei_120@3633|_vei2_pn460
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3632">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3634">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn459">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn460">2:460</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn461">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Su-śravas</b> is the name of the father of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपगु सौश्रवस">upagu sauśravasa</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xiv. 6, 8).</p></div>

सु-श्रवस् कौष्य|सु-श्रवस् कौष्य|सुश्रवस् कौष्य|श्रवस् कौष्य|సు-శ్రవస్ కౌష్య_౩|సు-శ్రవస్ కౌష్య|సుశ్రవస్ కౌష్య|శ్రవస్ కౌష్య|ಸು-ಶ್ರವಸ್ ಕೌಷ್ಯ_೩|ಸು-ಶ್ರವಸ್ ಕೌಷ್ಯ|ಸುಶ್ರವಸ್ ಕೌಷ್ಯ|ಶ್ರವಸ್ ಕೌಷ್ಯ|su-śravas kauṣya_3|su-śravas kauṣya|suśravas kauṣya|śravas kauṣya|su-shravas kauShya_3|su-shravas kauShya|sushravas kauShya|shravas kauShya|_vei_120@3634|_vei2_pn460
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3633">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3635">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn459">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn460">2:460</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn461">↷</a><hr><p>3. <b>Su-<a class="vei_120a" href="श्रवस्">śravas</a> Kauṣya</b> is the name of a teacher, a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुश्रि वाजश्रवस">kuśri vājaśravasa</a>,</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (x. 5, 5, 1 <i>et seq.</i>).</p></div>

सु-श्रवस् वार्ष-गण्य|सु-श्रवस् वार्ष-गण्य|सु-श्रवस् वार्षगण्य|सु-श्रवस् वार्ष|गण्य|సు-శ్రవస్ వార్ష-గణ్య_౪|సు-శ్రవస్ వార్ష-గణ్య|సు-శ్రవస్ వార్షగణ్య|సు-శ్రవస్ వార్ష|గణ్య|ಸು-ಶ್ರವಸ್ ವಾರ್ಷ-ಗಣ್ಯ_೪|ಸು-ಶ್ರವಸ್ ವಾರ್ಷ-ಗಣ್ಯ|ಸು-ಶ್ರವಸ್ ವಾರ್ಷಗಣ್ಯ|ಸು-ಶ್ರವಸ್ ವಾರ್ಷ|ಗಣ್ಯ|su-śravas vārṣa-gaṇya_4|su-śravas vārṣa-gaṇya|su-śravas vārṣagaṇya|su-śravas vārṣa|gaṇya|su-shravas vaarSha-gaNya_4|su-shravas vaarSha-gaNya|su-shravas vaarShagaNya|su-shravas vaarSha|gaNya|_vei_120@3635|_vei2_pn460
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3634">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3636">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn459">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn460">2:460</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn461">↷</a><hr><p>4. <b>Su-<a class="vei_120a" href="श्रवस्">śravas</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="वार्ष">vārṣa</a>-gaṇya</b> (‘descendant of Vṛṣagaṇa’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रातरह्न कौहल">prātarahna kauhala</a>,</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203635"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203635">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203635"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203635">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372.</li></ol></div>

सु-षामन्|सुषामन्|षामन्|సు-షామన్|సుషామన్|షామన్|ಸು-ಷಾಮನ್|ಸುಷಾಮನ್|ಷಾಮನ್|su-ṣāman|suṣāman|ṣāman|su-Shaaman|suShaaman|Shaaman|_vei_120@3636|_vei2_pn460
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3635">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3637">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn459">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn460">2:460</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn461">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-ṣāman</b> is the name of a man in one verse of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203636"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203636">1</a></sup> and probably forms part of the strange name, Varo <a class="vei_120a" href="सुषामन्">suṣāman</a>, in other passages.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203636"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203636">2</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वरु">varu</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203636"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203636">↑</a> viii. 25, 22; possibly 60, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203636"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203636">↑</a> viii. 23, 28; 24, 28; 26, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 162.</li></ol></div>

सु-षोमा|सुषोमा|षोमा|సు-షోమా|సుషోమా|షోమా|ಸು-ಷೋಮಾ|ಸುಷೋಮಾ|ಷೋಮಾ|su-ṣomā|suṣomā|ṣomā|su-Shomaa|suShomaa|Shomaa|_vei_120@3637|_vei2_pn460
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3636">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3638">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn459">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn460">2:460</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn461">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-ṣomā</b> occurs certainly as the name of a river in the Nadīstuti (‘praise of rivers’) in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203637">1</a></sup> In two other passages it would seem to be a proper name, once masculine,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203637">2</a></sup> perhaps  the people, and once feminine,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203637">3</a></sup> though Roth<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203637">4</a></sup> sees in the word the designation of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> vessel. Its identification is quite uncertain, though it has been thought to be the  of Megasthenes,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203637">5</a></sup> the modern Suwan.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203637">↑</a> x. 75, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ix. 26, where it is absurdly identified with the <b>Sindu</b> (Indus).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203637">↑</a> viii. 7, 29.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203637">↑</a> viii. 64, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203637">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203637"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203637">↑</a> See Arrian, <i>Indica,</i> iv. 12; Schwanbeck, <i>Megasthenes,</i> 31, where there is a various reading .</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 126 <i>et seq.;</i> Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 32, 398, 399; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 12-14.</li></ol></div>

सु-सर्तु|सुसर्तु|सर्तु|సు-సర్తు|సుసర్తు|సర్తు|ಸು-ಸರ್ತು|ಸುಸರ್ತು|ಸರ್ತು|su-sartu|susartu|sartu|_vei_120@3638|_vei2_pn461
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3637">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3639">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn460">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn461">2:461</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn462">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-sartu</b> is the name of a river in the <a class="vei_120a" href="नदी">nadī</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="स्तुति">stuti</a> (‘praise of rivers’) in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203638"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203638">1</a></sup> That it was a tributary of the Indus is certain, but which one is unknown.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203638"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203638">↑</a> x. 75, 6. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 14; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 200.</li></ol></div>

सु-हविस् आंगिरस|सुहविस् आंगिरस|हविस् आंगिरस|सु-हविस् आङ्गिरस|सुहविस् आङ्गिरस|हविस् आङ्गिरस|సు-హవిస్ ఆంగిరస|సుహవిస్ ఆంగిరస|హవిస్ ఆంగిరస|సు-హవిస్ ఆఙ్గిరస|సుహవిస్ ఆఙ్గిరస|హవిస్ ఆఙ్గిరస|ಸು-ಹವಿಸ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಸುಹವಿಸ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಹವಿಸ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಸು-ಹವಿಸ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|ಸುಹವಿಸ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|ಹವಿಸ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|su-havis āṅgirasa|suhavis āṅgirasa|havis āṅgirasa|su-havis aaMgirasa|suhavis aaMgirasa|havis aaMgirasa|su-havis aangirasa|suhavis aangirasa|havis aangirasa|_vei_120@3639|_vei2_pn461
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3638">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3640">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn460">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn461">2:461</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn462">↷</a><hr><p><b>Su-<a class="vei_120a" href="हविस्">havis</a> Āṅgirasa</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अंगिरस्">aṅgiras</a></b>’) is the name of the seer of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> or chant in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xiv. 3, 25).</p></div>

सू-कर|सूकर|कर|సూ-కర|సూకర|కర|ಸೂ-ಕರ|ಸೂಕರ|ಕರ|sū-kara|sūkara|kara|suu-kara|suukara|soo-kara|sookara|_vei_120@3640|_vei2_pn461
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3639">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3641">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn460">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn461">2:461</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn462">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sū-kara</b> ‘wild-boar,’ has the appearance of being an onomatopoetic word (‘making the sound <i>sū</i>’); it is more probably a very old word going back to the Indo-European period, and cognate with the Latin <i>su-culus</i> (‘little pig’), being transformed in sense by popular etymology.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203640"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203640">1</a></sup> It occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203640"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203640">2</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203640"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203640">3</a></sup> It appears once in the Atharvaveda accompanied by <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मृग">mṛga</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203640"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203640">4</a></sup> the combined words apparently meaning ‘wild hog,’ as opposed to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वराह">varāha</a>,</b> ‘boar.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203640"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203640">↑</a> The <i>sū-</i> corresponding to Lat. <i>sū-s,</i> Gk. -<i>s,</i> Old High German, <i>sū. Cf.</i> Brugmann, <i>Grundriss,</i> 2^2, 483.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203640"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203640">↑</a> vii. 55, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203640"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203640">↑</a> Av. ii. 27, 2; v. 14, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 21; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 40; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, v. 10, 2, etc. None of the passages refer to the flesh as eaten: Buddha's death was due to a meal of <i>sūkaramaddava,</i> which may well mean ‘tender parts of pork’ (see Fleet, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1906, 881, n.), though the Rājanighaṇṭu, vii. 85, gives <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सूकर">sūkara</a></i> as meaning the <i>Batatas edulis.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203640"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203640">↑</a> xii. 1, 48. The use of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मृग">mṛga</a></i> here does not indicate that <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सूकर">sūkara</a></i> is a new name, because the latter word elsewhere always occurs alone both in the Rigveda and later (n. 3). <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मृग">mṛga</a></b> above, 2, 172, n. 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 82; Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 100.</li></ol></div>

सूक्त|సూక్త|ಸೂಕ್ತ|sūkta|suukta|sookta|_vei_120@3641|_vei2_pn462
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3640">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3642">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn461">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn462">2:462</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn463">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sūkta,</b> ‘well uttered,’ is the regular term for a ‘hymn’ as part of the <a class="vei_120a" href="शस्त्र">śastra</a> in the later Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203641"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203641">1</a></sup> and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203641"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203641">2</a></sup> The sense of ‘hymn’ must also be recognized in several passages of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203641"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203641">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203641"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203641">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 4, 5, 5; vii. 1, 5, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203641"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203641">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 33; iii. 11, 9. 12-15; iv. 21, 5; vi. 8, 10; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 1; xv. 3; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 1, 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iv. 6; xi. 16.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203641"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203641">↑</a> i. 42, 20; 171, 1; ii. 6, 2; vii. 29, 3, etc.</li></ol></div>

सूची|सूचि|సూచీ|సూచి|ಸೂಚೀ|ಸೂಚಿ|sūcī|sūci|suuchii|suuchi|soochee|soochi|_vei_120@3642|_vei2_pn462
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3641">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3643">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn461">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn462">2:462</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn463">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sūcī,</b> ‘needle,’ is found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203642"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203642">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203642"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203642">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203642"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203642">↑</a> ii. 32, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203642"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203642">↑</a> Av. xi. 10, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 33; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 9, 6, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 18, 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 2, 10, 2. 3; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 10; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 10, 3 (Oertel, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, 228).</li></ol></div>

सूचीक|సూచీక|ಸೂಚೀಕ|sūcīka|suuchiika|soocheeka|_vei_120@3643|_vei2_pn462
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3642">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3644">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn461">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn462">2:462</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn463">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sūcīka</b> is the name of a stinging insect in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203643">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203643"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203643">↑</a> i. 191, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 98.</li></ol></div>

सूत|సూత|ಸೂತ|sūta|suuta|soota|_vei_120@3644|_vei2_pn462
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3643">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3645">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn461">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn462">2:462</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn463">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sūta</b> is the name of a court official who is often mentioned with the <b>Grāmaṇī.</b> He is one of the eight <b>Vīras</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203644">1</a></sup> and of the eleven <b>Ratnins</b> in other texts.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203644">2</a></sup> He also appears in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203644">3</a></sup> among the kingmakers (<b>Rājakṛt</b>) and in the Śatrarudriya<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203644">4</a></sup> (‘section dealing with the hundred Rudras’) of the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>. The commentators are agreed in seeing in him the ‘charioteer’ (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="सारथि">sārathi</a></b>) or ‘master of the horse,’ of the king; this sense is accepted by Roth,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203644">5</a></sup> by Whitney,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203644">6</a></sup> and by Bloomfield.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203644">7</a></sup> But the fact that the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सं">saṃ</a> grahītṛ,</b> who occurs in several passages beside the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत">sūta</a>, is the ‘charioteer,’ renders this version improbable. Eggeling<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203644">8</a></sup> thinks that he was, in the Brāhmaṇas at least, a minstrel and court poet, while Weber<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203644">9</a></sup> considers that his name denotes him as ‘consecrated’--that is, one who has constant access to the king. In the Epic the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत">sūta</a> serves as a royal herald and bard:<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203644">10</a></sup> it may be that the curious words <i>ahanti,</i><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203644">11</a></sup> <i>ahantya,</i><sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1203644">12</a></sup> or <i>ahantva</i><sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1203644">13</a></sup> applied to him<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1203644">14</a></sup> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शतरुद्रिय">śatarudriya</a> denote his sacred character at once as minstrel and as herald--a combination of functions not unknown elsewhere.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203644">↑</a> ix. 1, 4, where he follows the chief queen (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="महिषी">mahiṣī</a></b>), and precedes the Grāmaṇī in the list.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203644">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 4; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 5; iv. 3, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 3, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 9, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 1, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203644">↑</a> iii. 5, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203644">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 5, 2, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 18. So also in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’), Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 2, 1. For other references to the <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत">sūta</a>, see <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 18, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 4, 7; xiii. 4, 2, 5; 7, 1, 43; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxviii. 3; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 3, 37, 38.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203644">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203644">↑</a> Translation of the Atharvaveda, 62.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203644">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 114.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203644">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 62, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203644">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 200.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203644">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 254, 255.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203644">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 18. This word means ‘non-fighter’ according to Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 17, 200.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1203644">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1203644">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1203644"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1203644">↑</a> The last two forms appear to be equivalent in sense to <i>ahanya,</i> ‘not to be slain,’ ‘inviolable.’</li></ol></div>

सूत-वशा|सूतवशा|सूत|वशा|సూత-వశా|సూతవశా|సూత|వశా|ಸೂತ-ವಶಾ|ಸೂತವಶಾ|ಸೂತ|ವಶಾ|sūta-vaśā|sūtavaśā|sūta|vaśā|suuta-vashaa|suutavashaa|suuta|vashaa|soota-vashaa|sootavashaa|soota|_vei_120@3645|_vei2_pn463
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3644">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3646">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn462">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn463">2:463</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn464">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sūta-vaśā</b> denotes in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203645">1</a></sup> a cow barren after having one calf.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203645"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203645">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 5, 4; vi. 1, 3, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvii. 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 4, 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

सूत्र|సూత్ర|ಸೂತ್ರ|sūtra|suutra|sootra|_vei_120@3646|_vei2_pn463
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3645">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3647">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn462">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn463">2:463</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn464">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sūtra</b> has the sense of ‘thread’ in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203646"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203646">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203646"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203646">2</a></sup> In the sense of a ‘book of rules’ for the guidance of sacrificers and so forth, the word occurs in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203646"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203646">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203646"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203646">↑</a> iii. 9, 3; xviii. 8, 37.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203646"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203646">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 4, 14; vii. 3, 2, 13; xii. 3, 4, 2; 7, 2, 10; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 8, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iv. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203646"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203646">↑</a> ii. 4, 10; iv. 1, 6 (Mādhyaṃdina = iv. 1, 2 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>); 5, 11.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 24, 25; Sieg, <i>Die Sagenstoffe des <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>,</i> 21.</li></ol></div>

सूद|సూద|ಸೂದ|sūda|suuda|sooda|_vei_120@3647|_vei2_pn463
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3646">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3648">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn462">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn463">2:463</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn464">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sūda,</b> according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, means ‘well’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203647">1</a></sup> and the ‘mud of a dried-up pool.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203647">2</a></sup> Pischel,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203647">3</a></sup> however, shows clearly that <a class="vei_120a" href="सूद">sūda</a> denotes what is added to <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> to  make it fit for use, especially the warm milk, and this sense suits all the passages. Eggeling<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203647">4</a></sup> renders it ‘well,’ and Grassmann ‘sweet drink.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203647">↑</a> Rv. vii. 36, 3; ix. 97, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203647">↑</a> Rv. x. 61, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvi. 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 3, 5; 2, 1, 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 7, 3, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203647">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 72, 73.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203647"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203647">↑</a> <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 43, 144.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 211.</li></ol></div>

सूद-दोहस्|सूद-दोह|सूददोहस्|सूददोह|सूद|दोहस्|दोह|సూద-దోహస్|సూద-దోహ|సూదదోహస్|సూదదోహ|సూద|దోహస్|దోహ|ಸೂದ-ದೋಹಸ್|ಸೂದ-ದೋಹ|ಸೂದದೋಹಸ್|ಸೂದದೋಹ|ಸೂದ|ದೋಹಸ್|ದೋಹ|sūda-dohas|sūda-doha|sūdadohas|sūdadoha|sūda|dohas|doha|suuda-dohas|suuda-doha|suudadohas|suudadoha|suuda|sooda-dohas|sooda-doha|soodadohas|soodadoha|sooda|_vei_120@3648|_vei2_pn464
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3647">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3649">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn463">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn464">2:464</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn465">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sūda-dohas</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203648"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203648">1</a></sup> denotes ‘milking <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सूद">sūda</a></b>’--<i>i.e.,</i> what is requisite to mix with <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>, according to Pischel.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203648"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203648">2</a></sup> According to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203648"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203648">3</a></sup> it means ‘yielding milk like a well.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203648"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203648">↑</a> viii. 69, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203648"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203648">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 72. <i>Sūdayitnu</i> in Rv. x. 64, 9, may be taken in the same way, and <i>sūdin</i> in <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxvii. 2; <i>sūdya</i> in <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 4, 13, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxii. 25, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203648"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203648">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

सूना|సూనా|ಸೂನಾ|sūnā|suunaa|soonaa|_vei_120@3649|_vei2_pn464
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3648">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3650">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn463">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn464">2:464</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn465">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sūnā</b> means, in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203649"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203649">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203649"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203649">2</a></sup> apparently a ‘woven (from <i>sīv,</i> ‘sew’) wickerwork basket’ for holding flesh.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203649"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203649">↑</a> i. 161, 10; 162, 13; x. 86, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203649"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203649">↑</a> Av. v. 17, 14. Crates of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पलाश">palāśa</a></b> wood are mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xvii. 3, 2. 3.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 271.</li></ol></div>

सूनु|సూను|ಸೂನು|sūnu|suunu|soonu|_vei_120@3650|_vei2_pn464
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3649">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3651">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn463">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn464">2:464</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn465">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sūnu</b> is a common word for ‘son’ from the Rigveda onwards.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203650"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203650">1</a></sup> The etymological sense seems to be ‘he who is borne,’ and then ‘the begotten.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203650"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203650">2</a></sup> But the use of <a class="vei_120a" href="सूनु">sūnu</a> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203650"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203650">3</a></sup> is predominantly in relation to the father, and only rarely in its connexion with words for mother.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203650"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203650">4</a></sup> Thus a father is ‘easy of access’ (<i>sāpāyana</i>) to his son (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सूनु">sūnu</a></i>);<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203650"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203650">5</a></sup> but in another passage,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203650"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203650">6</a></sup> where the same term is applied to earth as a mother, the word used for son is <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुत्र">putra</a>.</b> No conclusion as to matriarchy can of course be drawn from the etymology. On the relation of son and father, see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पितृ">pitṛ</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203650"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203650">↑</a> Rv. i. 26, 3; ii. 38, 5; vi. 52, 9, etc.; Av. vi. 1, 2; vii. 2, 2; xii. 3, 23, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203650"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203650">↑</a> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandischaftsnamen,</i> 453.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203650"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203650">↑</a> Usually in a figurative sense--<i>e.g sahasaḥ, adreḥ Sūruḥ.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203650"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203650">↑</a> v. 42, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203650"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203650">↑</a> Rv. i. 1, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203650"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203650">↑</a> Rv. x. 18, 11.</li></ol></div>

सूरि|సూరి|ಸೂರಿ|sūri|suuri|soori|_vei_120@3651|_vei2_pn465
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3650">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3652">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn464">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn465">2:465</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn468">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sūri</b> is the regular word in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203651"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203651">1</a></sup> for the sacrificer, the later Yajamāna--that is, the man who pays the priests for performing the rite, and reaps the benefit of that service. The Sūris are often coupled with the <b>Maghavans,</b><sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1203651"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1203651">1b</a></sup> described as heroes or warriors,<sup id="ref_120_2b_vei_1203651"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2b_vei_1203651">2b</a></sup> and as related to the priests by their patronage<sup id="ref_120_3b_vei_1203651"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3b_vei_1203651">3b</a></sup> or as companions.<sup id="ref_120_4b_vei_1203651"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4b_vei_1203651">4b</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203651"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203651">↑</a> i. 31, 7. 12; 48, 24; 54, 11; 73, 5. 8, 9; iii. 31, 14; v. 42, 4; 79, 6; vi. 4, 8; 23, 10; vii. 32, 15; viii. 70, 15; x. 61, 22; 115, 5. 7. 8.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1203651"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1203651">↑</a> i. 31, 7. 12; 48, 24; 54, 11; 73, 5. 8, 9; iii. 31, 14; v. 42, 4; 79, 6; vi. 4, 8; 23, 10; vii. 32, 15; viii. 70, 15; x. 61, 22; 115, 5. 7. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2b_vei_1203651"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2b_vei_1203651">↑</a> i. 69, 3; 73, 9; 119, 3; 122, 12; 180, 9; vii. 32, 15.</li><li id="fx_120_3b_vei_1203651"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3b_vei_1203651">↑</a> i. 97, 3, 4; v. 10, 6; vi. 8, 7; 25, 7; vii. 3, 8; 44, 18; viii. 60, 6; x. 66, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4b_vei_1203651"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4b_vei_1203651">↑</a> v. 64, 5; vii. 32, 25; viii. 45, 36; ix. 96, 4; x. 115, 7.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 236.</li></ol></div>

सूर्मि|సూర్మి|ಸೂರ್ಮಿ|sūrmi|suurmi|soormi|_vei_120@3652|_vei2_pn465
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3651">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3653">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn464">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn465">2:465</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn468">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sūrmi</b> denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203652"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203652">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203652"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203652">2</a></sup> according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, a kind of ‘tube’ serving as a lamp. In one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203652"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203652">3</a></sup> it means a ‘pipe’ for conveying water. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अवत">avata</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203652"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203652">↑</a> vii. 1, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203652"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203652">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 5, 7, 6; v. 4, 7, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxi. 9, where it is described as <i>karṇakāvatī,</i> rendered by Roth as ‘provided with a handle.’</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203652"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203652">↑</a> viii. 69, 12. <b>Sūrmya</b> in <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 9, 2, may mean ‘being in pipes or channels.’</li></ol></div>

सूर्य|సూర్య|ಸೂರ್ಯ|sūrya|suurya|soorya|_vei_120@3653|_vei2_pn465
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3652">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3654">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn464">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn465">2:465</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn468">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sūrya,</b> the ‘sun,’ plays a great part in Vedic mythology and religion,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203653">1</a></sup> corresponding with the importance of the sun as a factor in the physical life of the peninsula. In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203653">2</a></sup> the sun is normally regarded as a beneficent power, a not unnatural view in a people which must apparently have issued from the cold regions of the Himālaya mountains. Its heat is, however, alluded to in some passages of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203653">3</a></sup> as well as referred to in the Atharvaveda and the literature of the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203653">4</a></sup></p><p>In one myth <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> is said to have vanquished <a class="vei_120a" href="सूर्य">sūrya</a> and to have stolen his wheel:<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203653">5</a></sup> this is possibly a reference to the  obscuration of the sun by a thunderstorm.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203653">6</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203653">7</a></sup> presents a naive conception of the course of the sun, which it regards as bright on one side only, and as returning from west to east by the same road, but with the reverse side turned towards the earth, thus at night illumining the stars in heaven.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203653">8</a></sup> In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203653">9</a></sup> wonder is expressed that the sun does not fall.</p><p>There are several references to eclipses in the Rigveda. In one passage<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203653">10</a></sup> Svarbhānu, a demon, is said to have eclipsed the sun with darkness, while <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अत्रि">atri</a></b> restores the light of the sun, a similar feat being elsewhere attributed to his family, the Atris.<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203653">11</a></sup> In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1203653">12</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राहु">rāhu</a></b> appears for the first time in connexion with the sun. Indra's defeat of <a class="vei_120a" href="सूर्य">sūrya</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203653">6</a></sup> may also be explained as alluding to an eclipse; in two other passages<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1203653">13</a></sup> such an interpretation seems at least probable. Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1203653">14</a></sup> not only argues that the Rigveda knows the theory of eclipses caused by an occultation of the sun by the moon, and regards the sun as going round the earth,<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1203653">15</a></sup> but even endeavours to identify an eclipse referred to in the Rigveda with one that occurred in 1029 B.C. These views are completely refuted by Whitney.<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1203653">16</a></sup></p><p>The sun as a maker of time<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1203653">17</a></sup> determines the year of 360 days, which is the civil year and the usual year (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="संवत्सर">saṃvatsara</a></b>) of  Vedic literature. This solar year is divided into two halves-the Uttarāyaṇa,<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1203653">18</a></sup> when the sun goes north, and the <a class="vei_120a" href="दक्षिणायन">dakṣiṇāyana</a>,<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1203653">19</a></sup> when it goes south. There can be no doubt that these periods denote the time when the sun turns north from the winter solstice, and when it turns south from the summer solstice, for the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1203653">20</a></sup> says so in perfectly clear language. The alternative theory is to regard the periods as those when the sun is in the north--<i>i.e.,</i> when it is north of the equator, and when it is in the south, taking as points of departure the equinoxes, not the solstices; but this view has no support in Vedic literature, and is opposed to the fact that the equinoxes play no part in Vedic astronomical theory.<sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1203653">21</a></sup> There are only doubtful references to the solstices in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1203653">22</a></sup></p><p>The Brāhmaṇas,<sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1203653">23</a></sup> and perhaps the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1203653">24</a></sup> regard the moon as entering the sun at new moon. According to Hillebrandt,<sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1203653">25</a></sup> the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_26_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_1203653">26</a></sup> recognizes that the moon shines by the borrowed light of the sun, but this seems very doubt ful. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अर्यम्णः पन्था">aryamṇaḥ panthā</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_27_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_1203653">27</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>,</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सप्त सूर्याः">sapta sūryāḥ</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203653">↑</a> See Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 30 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203653">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> i. 50, 6; 115, 1. 3; 164, 11. 13; 191, 8. 9; vii. 63, 1; x. 37, 4; 85, 9; 88, 11; 139, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203653">↑</a> Rv. vii. 34, 19; ix. 107, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203653">↑</a> Ehni, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="यम">yama</a>,</i> 134; Macdonell, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> p. 31.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203653">↑</a> i. 175, 4; iv. 30, 4; x. 43, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203653">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203653">↑</a> iii. 44, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203653">↑</a> Macdonell, p. 10, who compares Rv. i. 115. 5; x. 37, 3. See also Speyer, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1906, 723; Thibaut, <i>Astronomie, Astrologie und Mathematik,</i> 6.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203653">↑</a> Rv. iv. 13, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203653">↑</a> Rv. v. 40, 5-9. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, p. 160; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 5, 2; 6, 14; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxiv. 3; Tilak, <i>Orion,</i> 159.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203653">↑</a> Av. xiii. 2, 4, 12, 36; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 3, 4, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1203653">↑</a> Av. xix. 9, 10; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 351.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203653">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1203653">↑</a> Rv. iv. 28, 2, 3; v. 33, 4. In x. 27 20, <i>sūro markaḥ</i> means, according to Roth. St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> and Zimmer, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> a demon of eclipse; but it may also mean the ‘cleansing’ sun. Av. ii. 10, 8, clearly refers to an eclipse. See Lanman, <i>Festgruss an Roth,</i> 187-190.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1203653">↑</a> Proceedings of the Bohemian Academy of Sciences, May, 1885; Translation of the Rigveda, 6, x.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1203653">↑</a> See Rv. iv. 28, 23; v. 33, 4; x. 37, 3 138, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1203653">↑</a> <i>Proceedings of the American Oriental Society,</i> October, 1885, xvii (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, lxilxvi); <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, lxxxii, lxxxiii; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 65, 66; Thibaut, <i>Astronomie, Astrologie und Mathematik,</i> 6.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1203653">↑</a> Rv. v. 81, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1203653">↑</a> The form Uttarāyaṇa is later (<a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>, vi. 10, etc.). <a class="vei_120a" href="उदगयन">udagayana</a> occurs in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 3, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, lxvii. 4; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 1, 1; Gobhila <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 1, 3; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 4, etc.; Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 201, 212; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ज्योतिष">jyotiṣa</a>,</i> 107 <i>et seq.;</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,</i> xiv. 10.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1203653">↑</a> The form is late (<a class="vei_120a" href="मनु">manu</a>, i. 67, etc.). In the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 3, the two Ayanas are equated each with three seasons--the northern course with spring, summer, and rains; the southern with autumn, winter, and cool season. But this is merely an inevitable inaccuracy, since no real season begins with the winter solstice.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1203653">↑</a> xix. 3. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 5, 3; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 1, 18 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 2, 18 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>); Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 345 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1203653">↑</a> Thibaut, <i>Indian Antiquary,</i> 24, 96; <i>Astronomie, Astrologie und Mathematik,</i>  10; Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 631 <i>et seq.;</i> 49, 473 <i>et seq.; Nachrichten der ko7niglichen Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften zu Go7ttingen,</i> 1909, 564, n. 1; Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1909, 1103. On the other side. see Tilak, <i>Orion,</i> 22-31.</li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1203653">↑</a> See Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 279-283, who cites Rv. i. 61, 15; v. 29, 5; x. 171, 4; 179, 2. But none of these passages are conclusive. <i>Cf.</i> Thibaut, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 6.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1203653">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 4, 18; iv. 6, 7, 12; x. 6, 2, 3; xi. 1, 6, 19; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 2, 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 28, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1203653">↑</a> v. 47, 3; ix. 25, 6; 71, 2; x. 55, 5; 138, 4. <i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 463-466.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1203653">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 3, 467, 468.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_1203653">↑</a> ix. 71, 9; 76, 4; 86, 32; perhaps i. 190, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>, ii. 9, 2, 12, 1. Thibaut, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 6, considers that the meaning of the passages is merely that the moon is filled up during the bright half of the month by light emanating from the sun.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_1203653"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_1203653">↑</a> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 188, finds in the Rigveda, i. 110, 2, a mention of the inclination of the ecliptic to the equator, and in x. 86, 4, a reference to the axis of the earth. <i>Cf.</i> Tilak, <i>Orion,</i> 158 <i>et seq.;</i> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 102, 105. These views are clearly quite wrong. The notions of the sun given in the Brāhmaṇas are all very naive and simple; the distance of heaven and the sun from the earth is the height of a thousand cows one on the top of the other (<a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvi. 8, 6), or forty-four days' journey for a horse (<i>ibid.,</i> xxv. 10, 16), or a thousand days' journey for a horse (<a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 17, 8), or a hundred leagues (Kausītaki <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 3). They record also such facts as that the sun rises from and sets in the waters (<a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 20, 13; <i>of.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxiv. 4, 5; xxvi. 1), and that it sets in the west (<i>ibid.,</i> xviii. 9). The Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> calls the sun circular (vii. 4, 1, 17), and also fourcornered (<i>catuḥ-śrakti</i>) in xiv. 3, 1, 17, and so on. See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 358 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

सूर्य-नक्षत्र|सूर्यनक्षत्र|सूर्य|नक्षत्र|సూర్య-నక్షత్ర|సూర్యనక్షత్ర|సూర్య|నక్షత్ర|ಸೂರ್ಯ-ನಕ್ಷತ್ರ|ಸೂರ್ಯನಕ್ಷತ್ರ|ಸೂರ್ಯ|ನಕ್ಷತ್ರ|sūrya-nakṣatra|sūryanakṣatra|sūrya|nakṣatra|suurya-nakShatra|suuryanakShatra|suurya|nakShatra|soorya-nakShatra|sooryanakShatra|soorya|suurya-naxatra|suuryanaxatra|naxatra|_vei_120@3654|_vei2_pn468
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3653">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3655">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn465">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn468">2:468</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn469">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sūrya-nakṣatra</b> is found in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203654"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203654">1</a></sup> in a passage where Sāyaṇa takes it as denoting a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>,</b> which gives out rays of light like the sun. But the real sense (as the <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a> text helps to show) is that the sacrificer may take the sun for his <a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>--<i>i.e.,</i> he may neglect the Nakṣatras altogether and rely on the sun.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203654"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203654">↑</a> ii. 1, 2, 19. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 288.</li></ol></div>

सूर्य-चन्द्रमसा|सूर्यचन्द्रमसा|सूर्य|चन्द्रमसा|सूर्य-चन्द्रमसौ|सूर्यचन्द्रमसौ|चन्द्रमसौ|సూర్య-చన్ద్రమసా|సూర్యచన్ద్రమసా|సూర్య|చన్ద్రమసా|సూర్య-చన్ద్రమసౌ|సూర్యచన్ద్రమసౌ|చన్ద్రమసౌ|ಸೂರ್ಯ-ಚನ್ದ್ರಮಸಾ|ಸೂರ್ಯಚನ್ದ್ರಮಸಾ|ಸೂರ್ಯ|ಚನ್ದ್ರಮಸಾ|ಸೂರ್ಯ-ಚನ್ದ್ರಮಸೌ|ಸೂರ್ಯಚನ್ದ್ರಮಸೌ|ಚನ್ದ್ರಮಸೌ|sūrya-candramasā|sūryacandramasā|sūrya|candramasā|sūrya-candramasau|sūryacandramasau|candramasau|suurya-chandramasaa|suuryachandramasaa|suurya|chandramasaa|suurya-chandramasau|suuryachandramasau|chandramasau|soorya-chandramasaa|sooryachandramasaa|soorya|soorya-chandramasau|sooryachandramasau|_vei_120@3655|_vei2_pn468
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3654">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3656">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn465">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn468">2:468</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn469">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sūrya-candramasā</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सूर्य-चन्द्रमसौ">sūrya-candramasau</a></b> denotes ‘sun and moon’ as a pair of luminaries in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203655"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203655">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203655"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203655">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203655"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203655">↑</a> i. 102, 2; v. 51, 15; x. 190, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203655"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203655">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 8, 9; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vii. 12, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Naxatra,</i> 2, 293; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ज्योतिष">jyotiṣa</a></i> 28, 50; <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 112.</li></ol></div>

सृक|సృక|ಸೃಕ|sṛka|sRka|sRika|sRuka|sRRika|_vei_120@3656|_vei2_pn468
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3655">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3657">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn465">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn468">2:468</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn469">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sṛka</b> in a couple of passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203656">1</a></sup> denotes a weapon of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, perhaps a ‘lance.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203656"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203656">↑</a> i. 32, 12; x. 180, 2. <i>Cf. sṛkāyin, sṛkā-<a class="vei_120a" href="हस्त">hasta</a>,</i> ‘bearing a lance in his hand,’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शतरुद्रिय">śatarudriya</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="वाजसनेय">vājasaneya</a> Saṃhitā, xvi. 21, 61, etc.</li></ol></div>

सृगाल|సృగాల|ಸೃಗಾಲ|sṛgāla|sRgaala|sRigaala|sRugaala|sRRigaala|_vei_120@3657|_vei2_pn468
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3656">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3658">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn465">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn468">2:468</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn469">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sṛgāla</b>, ‘jackal,’ is not found until the Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> xii. 5, 2, 5), but is common in the Epic. </p></div>

सृजय|సృజయ|ಸೃಜಯ|sṛjaya|sRjaya|sRijaya|sRujaya|sRRijaya|_vei_120@3658|_vei2_pn469
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3657">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3659">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn468">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn469">2:469</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn471">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sṛjaya</b> is the name of one of the victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203658">1</a></sup> What it was is unknown: Mahīdhara on the Vājasaneyi passage calls it a kind of bird; Sāyaṇa on the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā gives the alternatives ‘black fly’ (when <i>sṛjayā</i> must be read), ‘white serpent,’ and ‘black buffalo.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203658"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203658">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 14, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 14; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 23. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 99.</li></ol></div>

सृञ्जय|సృఞ్జయ|ಸೃಞ್ಜಯ|sṛñjaya|sR~njaya|sRi~njaya|sRu~njaya|sRRi~njaya|_vei_120@3659|_vei2_pn469
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3658">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3660">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn468">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn469">2:469</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn471">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sṛñjaya</b> is the name of a people mentioned as early as the Rigveda. <a class="vei_120a" href="सृञ्जय">sṛñjaya</a> (that is, the king of this people) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दैववात">daivavāta</a></b> is celebrated as victorious over the <b>Turvaśas</b> and the <b>Vṛcīvants,</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203659">1</a></sup> and his sacrificial fire is referred to.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203659">2</a></sup> In connexion with <a class="vei_120a" href="दैववात">daivavāta</a> is also mentioned <b>Sāhadevya Somaka,</b><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203659">3</a></sup> no doubt another prince; for in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203659">4</a></sup> we find <a class="vei_120a" href="सोमक साहदेव्य">somaka sāhadevya</a> and his father, <a class="vei_120a" href="सहदेव">sahadeva</a> (originally <b>Suplan</b>) <a class="vei_120a" href="सार्ञ्जय">sārñjaya</a>, as kings who were anointed by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्वत">parvata</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नारद">nārada</a>.</b> The Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203659">5</a></sup> has also a Dānastuti (‘praise of gifts’) of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रस्तोक">prastoka</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203659">6</a></sup> a <a class="vei_120a" href="सृञ्जय">sṛñjaya</a>, who is lauded along with <b>Divodāsa.</b> Moreover, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वीतहव्य">vītahavya</a></b><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203659">7</a></sup> seems to have been a <a class="vei_120a" href="सृञ्जय">sṛñjaya</a>, though Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203659">8</a></sup> prefers to take the derivative word, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैतहव्य">vaitahavya</a>,</b> not as a patronymic, but as an epithet.</p><p>It seems probable that the Sṛñjayas and the <b>Tṛtsus</b> were closely allied, for Divodāsa and a <a class="vei_120a" href="सृञ्जय">sṛñjaya</a> prince are celebrated together,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203659">9</a></sup> and the Turvaśas were enemies of both.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203659">10</a></sup> This view is borne out by the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203659">11</a></sup> which recognizes <b><a class="vei_120a" href="देवभाग श्रौतर्ष">devabhāga śrautarṣa</a></b> as <a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a> of the <b>Kurus</b> and the Sṛñjayas. </p><p>On the other hand, some disaster certainly befel the Sṛñjayas, at least the Vaitahavyas, for they are said in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1203659">12</a></sup> to have offended the <b>Bhṛgus</b> and to have ended miserably. There is, it is true, no precise confirmation of this notice, but both the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1203659">13</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1203659">14</a></sup> in independent passages, refer to the Sṛñjayas having sustained some serious loss, though the notice is in each case coupled with a ritual error, much as in the Old Testament the fate of kings depends on their devotion to Jahve or their disobedience. It is justifiable to recognize some disaster in this allusion.</p><p>The geographical position of the Sṛñjayas is uncertain. Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1203659">15</a></sup> suggests that in early times they must be looked for west of the Indus with Divodāsa; he also mentions, though he does not definitely adopt, the suggestion of Brunnhofer that the Sṛñjayas are to be compared with the <sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1203659">16</a></sup> of the Greeks, and to be located in Drangiana. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1203659">17</a></sup> is inclined to locate them on the upper Indus; but it is difficult to decide definitely in favour of any particular location. They may well have been a good deal farther east than the Indus, since their allies, the Tṛtsus, were in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a>,</b> and were certainly absorbed in the Kurus.</p><p>Of the history of this clan we have one notice.<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1203659">18</a></sup> They expelled <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दुष्टरीतु">duṣṭarītu</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पौंसायन">pauṃsāyana</a>,</b> one of their kings, from the hereditary monarchy--of ten generations--and also drove out <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रेवोत्तरस्">revottaras</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पाटव">pāṭava</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="चाक्र">cākra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="स्थपति">sthapati</a>,</b> probably his minister, who, however, succeeded in effecting the restoration of the king, despite the opposition of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a> prince, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बल्हिक">balhika</a> Prātīpya.</b> Very probably this <a class="vei_120a" href="कुरु">kuru</a> prince may have been at the bottom of the movement which led to the expulsion of the king and his minister. But the restoration of the king can  hardly be regarded, in accordance with Bloomfield's view,<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1203659">19</a></sup> as a defeat of the Sṛñjayas.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203659">↑</a> Rv. vi. 27, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203659">↑</a> Rv. iv. 15, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203659">↑</a> Rv. iv. 15, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203659">↑</a> vii. 34, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203659">↑</a> Rv. vi. 47, 22. 25.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203659">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> Sutra, xvi. 11, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203659">↑</a> Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 212; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 105.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203659">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 132 (on <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वैतहव्य">vaitahavya</a></i>).</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203659">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> also the connexion of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्">bharad</a> vājas</b> and Divodāsa (Rv. vi. 16, 5; 31, 4; Hillebrandt, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 104), and their connexion with the Sṛñjayas (<a class="vei_120a" href="वीतहव्य">vītahavya</a> in Rv. vi. 15, 2. 3, and see vi. 27, 7, both passages that can safely be accepted as derived from the <a class="vei_120a" href="भरद्वाज">bharadvāja</a> family).</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203659">↑</a> See, on the one hand, Rv. vii. 18 (Turvaśas and Tṛtsus), and, on the other, vi. 27, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203659">↑</a> ii. 4, 4, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1203659">↑</a> v. 19, 1. <i>Cf.</i> v. 18, 10. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1203659">↑</a> xii. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1203659">↑</a> vi. 6, 2, 2, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1203659">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 106.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1203659">↑</a> Herodotus has the form  and , Strabo and Arrian. The Avestic is <i>zrayaṅk,</i> Old Persian <i>daraya.</i> The Indian <i>s</i> is curious if the words are parallel (see, however, Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 25, 11; Oldenberg, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1909, 1098).</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1203659">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 132, 133; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 232. This is a guess from Rv. i. 100, where <a class="vei_120a" href="सहदेव">sahadeva</a> occurs and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धु">sindhu</a></b> is mentioned.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1203659">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 9, 3, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1203659"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1203659">↑</a> <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 433. So also Zimmer, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 132.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 208; 3, 472; 18, 237; <i>Episches im vedischen Ritual,</i> 31; Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 153; Oldenberg, <i>Buddha</i> 405.</li></ol></div>

सृणी¤|సృణీ¤|ಸೃಣೀ¤|sṛṇī¤|sRNii¤|sRNee¤|sRiNii¤|sRuNii¤|sRRiNii¤|_vei_120@3660|_vei2_pn471
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3659">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3661">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn469">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn471">2:471</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn472">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sṛṇī¤</b> is found certainly in one,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203660">1</a></sup> and probably also in two other<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203660">2</a></sup> passages of the Rigveda. The sense appears to be ‘sickle.’ In one other passage <a class="vei_120a" href="सृण्य">sṛṇya</a> is coupled with <i>jetā:</i><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203660">3</a></sup> the sense is doubtful, Roth<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203660">4</a></sup> conjecturing <i>cetā,</i> and Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203660">5</a></sup> pointing out that <i>chettā</i> is also possible. Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203660">6</a></sup> thinks that a ‘hook’ is here meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203660">↑</a> i. 58, 4, where <i>sṛṇyā,</i> according to Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 116, n. 1, and to Pischel, <i>ibid.,</i> 2, 111, stands for <i>sṛṇyābhiḥ,</i> and, as an adjective agreeing with <i>juhūbhiḥ,</i> means ‘sickle-shaped sacrificial ladles.’ But this is very doubtful.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203660">↑</a> x. 101, 3 (<a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 28); 106, 6 (<i>ibid.,</i> xiii. 5). It is certain in Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 2, 2, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203660">↑</a> iv. 20, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203660">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 48, 111.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203660">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 284.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203660"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203660">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 86, n.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 238: Oldenberg, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 1, 58.</li></ol></div>

सृण्य|సృణ్య|ಸೃಣ್ಯ|sṛṇya|sRNya|sRiNya|sRuNya|sRRiNya|_vei_120@3661|_vei2_pn471
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3660">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3662">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn469">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn471">2:471</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn472">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sṛṇya.</b> See <b>Sṛṇī.</b></p></div>

सृबिन्द|సృబిన్ద|ಸೃಬಿನ್ದ|sṛbinda|sRbinda|sRibinda|sRubinda|sRRibinda|_vei_120@3662|_vei2_pn471
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3661">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3663">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn469">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn471">2:471</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn472">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sṛbinda</b> is the name of a foe of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203662"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203662">1</a></sup> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203662"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203662">2</a></sup> The word may denote a real foe, since it has no obvious <b>Āryan</b> derivation.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203662"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203662">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 162.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203662"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203662">↑</a> viii. 32, 2.</li></ol></div>

सृमर|సృమర|ಸೃಮರ|sṛmara|sRmara|sRimara|sRumara|sRRimara|_vei_120@3663|_vei2_pn471
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3662">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3664">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn469">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn471">2:471</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn472">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sṛmara</b> is the name of an unknown animal at the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a></b> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203663">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203663"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203663">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhita, v. 5, 16, 1 (according to Sāyaṇa = <i>camara</i>); Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 20; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 39 (where <b>Mahīdhara</b> identifies it with the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गवय">gavaya</a></b>).</li></ol></div>

सेतु|సేతు|ಸೇತು|setu|_vei_120@3664|_vei2_pn472
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3663">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3665">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn471">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn472">2:472</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn473">↷</a><hr><p><b>Setu</b> appears in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203664"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203664">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203664"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203664">2</a></sup> to denote merely a raised bank for crossing inundated land,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203664"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203664">3</a></sup> a ‘causeway,’ such as are common all the world over. This sense explains best the later meaning of ‘boundary.’ The word in Vedic literature is probably always metaphorical.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203664"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203664">↑</a> ix. 41, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203664"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203664">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 2, 2, 1; vi. 1, 4, 9; 5, 3, 3; vii. 5, 8, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxviii. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 35; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 2, 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 2, 10, 1; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 4, 24; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, viii. 4, 1. 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203664"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203664">↑</a> Max Müller, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 1, 130, n. 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 257.</li></ol></div>

सेना|సేనా|ಸೇನಾ|senā|senaa|_vei_120@3665|_vei2_pn472
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3664">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3666">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn471">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn472">2:472</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn473">↷</a><hr><p><b>Senā</b> denotes primarily a ‘missile,’ a sense found in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203665">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203665">2</a></sup> and then a ‘host’ or ‘army,’ which is its normal meaning.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203665">3</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="संग्राम">saṃgrāma</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203665">↑</a> Rv. i. 66, 7; 116, 1 (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="सेना">senā</a>-jū,</i> ‘swift as an arrow’); 143, 5; 186, 9; ii. 33, 11; v. 30, 9; vii. 3, 4; viii. 75, 7; x. 23, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203665">↑</a> viii. 8, 7; xi. 10, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203665"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203665">↑</a> Rv. i. 33, 6; vii. 25, 1; ix. 96, 1; x. 103, 1. 4. 7; 142, 4; 156, 2; Av. iii. 1, 1; 19, 6; iv. 19, 2; v. 21, 9, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> von Bradke, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 46, 456; Bloomfield, <i>ibid.,</i> 48, 549, 550; Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 231, n. 2, denies that <a class="vei_120a" href="सेना">senā</a> ever means ‘missile,’ and compares <i>exercitus effusus, agmen effusum.</i></li></ol></div>

सेना-नी|सेना-नि|सेनानी|सेनानि|सेना|సేనా-నీ|సేనా-ని|సేనానీ|సేనాని|సేనా|ಸೇನಾ-ನೀ|ಸೇನಾ-ನಿ|ಸೇನಾನೀ|ಸೇನಾನಿ|ಸೇನಾ|senā-nī|senā-ni|senānī|senāni|senā|senaa-nii|senaa-ni|senaanii|senaani|senaa|senaa-nee|senaanee|_vei_120@3666|_vei2_pn472
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3665">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3667">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn471">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn472">2:472</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn473">↷</a><hr><p><b>Senā-nī,</b> ‘leader of an army,’ is the title of the royal ‘general.’ He is mentioned in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203666">1</a></sup> where also<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203666">2</a></sup> the word is used metaphorically. He is also referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शतरुद्रिय">śatarudriya</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203666">3</a></sup> as well as elsewhere in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās and Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203666">4</a></sup> He is one of the <b>Ratnins</b> of the king.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203666">5</a></sup> Presumably he was appointed by the king, not by the people, to command in war when the king became too important to lead every little fray in person. In the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203666">6</a></sup> this official is called <a class="vei_120a" href="सेना">senā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203666">↑</a> vii. 20, 5; ix. 96, 1; x. 84, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203666">↑</a> x. 34, 12 (the gambling hymn).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203666">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 11; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203666">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xv. 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 9; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 8, 10; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 6, 1, 21.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203666">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 9, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 7, 3, 4; Maitrayaṇ Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 5; iv. 3, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xv. 4; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 1, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203666"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203666">↑</a> viii. 23, 10.</li></ol></div>

सेलग|సేలగ|ಸೇಲಗ|selaga|_vei_120@3667|_vei2_pn473
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3666">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3668">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn472">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn473">2:473</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn474">↷</a><hr><p><b>Selaga</b> in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203667"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203667">1</a></sup> appears to denote ‘robber.’ See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सैलग">sailaga</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203667"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203667">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 1, 5; viii. 11, 8; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 3, 10.</li></ol></div>

सेहु|సేహు|ಸೇಹು|sehu|_vei_120@3668|_vei2_pn473
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3667">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3669">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn472">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn473">2:473</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn474">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sehu</b> occurs in a comparison in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203668"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203668">1</a></sup> where it must denote a very sapless (<i>arasa</i>) substance.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203668"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203668">↑</a> vii. 76, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 54; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 441.</li></ol></div>

सैतव|సైతవ|ಸೈತವ|saitava|_vei_120@3669|_vei2_pn473
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3668">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3670">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn472">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn473">2:473</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn474">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saitava,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="सेतु">setu</a>,’ is the name of a teacher in the first two Vaṃśas (list of teachers) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203669">1</a></sup> He is described as a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पाराशर्य">pārāśarya</a></b> or of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पाराशर्यायण">pārāśaryāyaṇa</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203669">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203669">↑</a> ii. 5, 21; iv. 5, 27 Mādhyaṃdina; ii. 6, 2 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203669"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203669">↑</a> iv. 6, 2 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>.</li></ol></div>

सैन्धव|సైన్ధవ|ಸೈನ್ಧವ|saindhava|_vei_120@3670|_vei2_pn473
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3669">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3671">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn472">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn473">2:473</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn474">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saindhava,</b> ‘coming from the Indus,’ is a term applied to water in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203670"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203670">1</a></sup> to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गुग्गुलु">guggulu</a></b> in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203670"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203670">2</a></sup> to a horse in the Śatapatha, <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203670"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203670">3</a></sup> and to salt in the same text.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203670"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203670">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203670"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203670">↑</a> vii. 4, 13, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203670"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203670">↑</a> xix. 38, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203670"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203670">↑</a> xi. 5, 5, 12; Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 2, 13 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 1, 13 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203670"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203670">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 4, 12 (<i>ºkhilya</i>); iv. 5, 13 (<i>ºghana</i>).</li></ol></div>

सैर्य|సైర్య|ಸೈರ್ಯ|sairya|_vei_120@3671|_vei2_pn473
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3670">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3672">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn472">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn473">2:473</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn474">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sairya</b> is the name in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203671"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203671">1</a></sup> of some species of grass infested by insects.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203671"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203671">↑</a> i. 191, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 72.</li></ol></div>

सैलग|సైలగ|ಸೈಲಗ|sailaga|_vei_120@3672|_vei2_pn473
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3671">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3673">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn472">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn473">2:473</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn474">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sailaga</b> is found in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203672">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203672">2</a></sup> as the name of one of the victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’). ‘Robber’ seems to be the sense of this word, as of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सेलग">selaga</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203672">↑</a> xxx. 18.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203672"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203672">↑</a> iii. 4, 16, 1; Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, xii. 23 (also spelt Śailaga)</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 367, n. 4.</li></ol></div>

सोभरि|సోభరి|ಸೋಭರಿ|sobhari|_vei_120@3673|_vei2_pn474
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3672">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3674">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn473">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn474">2:474</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn476">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sobhari</b> is the name of a <a class="vei_120a" href="ऋषि">ṛṣi</a> frequently mentioned in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203673">1</a></sup> The family is also referred to,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203673">2</a></sup> and a father, Sobharī.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203673">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203673">↑</a> viii. 5, 26; 19, 2; 20, 19; 22, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203673">↑</a> Rv. viii. 19, 32; 20, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203673"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203673">↑</a> Rv. viii. 22, 15. <i>Cf.</i> viii. 103, 14; Av. xviii. 3, 15.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 105; Oldenberg, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 42, 217.</li></ol></div>

सोम|సోమ|ಸೋಮ|soma|_vei_120@3674|_vei2_pn474
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3673">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3675">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn473">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn474">2:474</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn476">↷</a><hr><p><b>Soma</b> was the famous plant which was used for the preparation of the libation of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> made at the Vedic sacrifice. Its importance is sufficiently shown by the fact that the whole of the ninth Maṇḍala of the Rigveda, and six hymns in other Maṇḍalas, are devoted to its praise.</p><p>Nevertheless, little is actually known of the plant. Its twigs or shoots are described as brown (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="बभ्रु">babhru</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203674">1</a></sup> ruddy (<i>aruṇa</i>),<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203674">2</a></sup> of tawny (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="हरि">hari</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203674">3</a></sup> Possibly its twigs hang down if the epithet <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नैचाशाख">naicāśākha</a></b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203674">4</a></sup> refers to the plant as Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203674">5</a></sup> thinks. The shoot is called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अंशु">aṃśu</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203674">6</a></sup> while the plant as a whole is called <i>andhas,</i><sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203674">7</a></sup> which also denotes the juice.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203674">8</a></sup> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्वन्">parvan</a></i><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203674">9</a></sup> is the stem. <i>Kṣip,</i><sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203674">10</a></sup> ‘finger,’ is used as a designation of the shoots, which may therefore have resembled fingers in shape; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वक्षणा">vakṣaṇā</a></i><sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203674">11</a></sup> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वाण">vāṇa</a></i><sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1203674">12</a></sup> also seem to have the sense of the shoot. There is some slight. evidence to suggest that the stem was not round, but angular.<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1203674">13</a></sup>  The plant grew on the mountains,<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1203674">14</a></sup> that of <b>Müjavant</b> being specially renowned.</p><p>These notices are inadequate to identify the plant. It has been held<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1203674">15</a></sup> to be the <i>Sarcostemma viminale</i> or the <i>Asclepias acida (= Sarcostemma brevistigma).</i> Roth<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1203674">16</a></sup> held that the <i>Sarcostemma acidum</i> more nearly met the requirements of the case. Watt<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1203674">17</a></sup> suggested the Afghan grape as the real <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>, and Rice<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1203674">18</a></sup> thought a sugar-cane might be meant, while Max Müller and Rājendralāla <a class="vei_120a" href="मित्र">mitra</a> suggested that the juice was used as an ingredient in a kind of beer--<i>i.e.,</i> that the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> plant was a species of hop. Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1203674">19</a></sup> considers that neither hops nor the grape can explain the references to <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>. It is very probable that the plant cannot now be identified.<sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1203674">20</a></sup></p><p>In the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a><sup id="ref_120_21_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_21_vei_1203674">21</a></sup> the plant is purchased ere it is pressed. Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_22_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_22_vei_1203674">22</a></sup> considers that the sale must be assumed for the Rigveda. It grew on a mountain, and could not be obtained by ordinary people: perhaps some special tribe or prince owned it, like the <b>Kīkaṭas.</b><sup id="ref_120_23_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_23_vei_1203674">23</a></sup> As it stands, the ritual performance is clearly an acquisition of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> from the Gandharvas (represented by a <a class="vei_120a" href="शूद्र">śūdra</a>), a ritual imitation of the action which may have been one of the sources of the drama. Owing to the difficulty of obtaining the real plant from a great  distance, several substitutes were allowed in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> period.<sup id="ref_120_24_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_24_vei_1203674">24</a></sup></p><p>The plant was prepared for use by being pounded with stones or in a mortar. The former was the normal method of procedure, appearing in the Rigveda as the usual one. The stones are called <i>grāvan</i><sup id="ref_120_25_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_25_vei_1203674">25</a></sup> or <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अद्रि">adri</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_26_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_26_vei_1203674">26</a></sup> and were, of course, held in the hands.<sup id="ref_120_27_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_27_vei_1203674">27</a></sup> The plant was laid on boards one beside the other (<b>Adhiṣavana</b>), and, according at least to the later ritual,<sup id="ref_120_28_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_28_vei_1203674">28</a></sup> a hole was dug below, so that the pounding of the plant by means of the stones resulted in a loud noise, doubtless a prophylactic against demoniac influences.</p><p>The plant was placed on a skin and on the Vedi<sup id="ref_120_29_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_29_vei_1203674">29</a></sup> --which was no longer done in the later ritual--<b><a class="vei_120a" href="धिषणा">dhiṣaṇā</a></b> in some passages denoting the Vedi.<sup id="ref_120_30_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_30_vei_1203674">30</a></sup></p><p>Sometimes the mortar and pestle were used in place of the stones.<sup id="ref_120_31_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_31_vei_1203674">31</a></sup> This use, though Iranian, was apparently not common in Vedic times.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203674">↑</a> The word is not actually found applied to the plant itself; but the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 5, 3, prescribes <i>arjunāni,</i> plants of this indefinite colour, as a substitute for <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>, if <b>Pūtīkas</b> cannot be obtained.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203674">↑</a> Rv. vii. 98, 1; x. 94, 3; 144, 5. <i>Cf.</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 5, 10, 1 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203674">↑</a> Rv. ix. 92, 1. The <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> cow, with which <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> was purchased, is called <i><a class="vei_120a" href="बभ्रु">babhru</a></i> or <i>aruṇā,</i> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 1, 15; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 7, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203674">↑</a> Rv. iii. 53, 14. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नैचाशाख">naicāśākha</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203674">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 14-18; 2, 241-245.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203674">↑</a> Rv. i. 168, 3 iii. 48, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203674">↑</a> Rv. i. 28, 7; iii. 48, 1; iv. 16, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203674">↑</a> ii. 14, 1; 19, 1; 35, 1. etc.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203674">↑</a> Rv. i. 9, 1. <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="परुस्">parus</a>,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 7, 13; Vaitāna <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, 24.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203674">↑</a> Rv. ix. 79, 4. <i>Cf.,</i> however, Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 70.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203674">↑</a> Rv. viii. 1, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1203674">↑</a> Rv. iv. 24, 9; ix. 50, 1. But these passages are both very doubtful. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वाण">vāṇa</a>.</b> The husk left after the pressing is called <i>andhas</i> (ix. 86, 44; <i>vavri</i> (ix. 69, 9), <i><a class="vei_120a" href="त्वच्">tvac</a></i> (ix. 86, 44; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 7, 13, 1), <i>śarīva</i> (<i>ibid.,</i> 2), <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शर्य">śarya</a></i> (ix. 68, 2), <i><a class="vei_120a" href="तान्व">tānva</a></i> (ix. 78, 1).</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1203674">↑</a> <i>Cf. pṛṣṭhya</i> in Rv. iv. 20, 4; Hille brandt, 1, 54, 55.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1203674">↑</a> Rv. i. 93, 6; iii. 48, 2; v. 36, 2; 43, 4; 85, 2; ix. 18, 1; 46, 1; 71, 4; 82, 3; Av. iii. 21, 10. So in the Avesta, Yasna, x. 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1203674">↑</a> Lassen, <i>Indische Alterthumskunde,</i> 1^2, 931; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 261 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> Haug, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Brahmaṇa,</i> 2, 489; Max Müller, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 9, liv.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1203674">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 35, 680 <i>et seq. Cf.</i> also 38, 134 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1203674">↑</a> See Hillebrandt, 1, 7 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1203674">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 10.</li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1203674">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 12. The dispute between Max Müller, Roth, J. G. Baker, W. T. Thiselton Dyer, Charles G. Leland, and A. Houtum-Schindler, as to the identity of the plant, is reprinted in Max Müller's <i>Biographies of Words,</i> 222 <i>et seq.,</i> and reviewed by Hillebrandt. See also Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, xxiv. <i>et seq.,</i> who thinks that the traditional identification is not far wrong. Caland, <i>Altindisches Zauberritual,</i> 188, gives it as <i>Sarcostemma acidum.</i></li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1203674">↑</a> The original <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> plant was, doubtless, identical with the Haoma of the Avesta. On the plant from which the present Parsis of Kermān and Yezd obtain their Hūm juice, and which they regard as identical with the Avestic Haoma, see Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, xxiv <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_21_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_21_vei_1203674">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 3, 1, 1 <i>et seq.;</i> Hillebrandt, 1, 89 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_22_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_22_vei_1203674">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 70.</li><li id="fx_120_23_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_23_vei_1203674">↑</a> Rv. iii. 53, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_24_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_24_vei_1203674">↑</a> See Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 5, 10, 1-6, and <i>cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, xxvii; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, ix. 5, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_25_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_25_vei_1203674">↑</a> i. 83, 6; 135, 7, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_26_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_26_vei_1203674">↑</a> i. 130, 2; 135, 5; 137, 1, etc. <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अद्रि">adri</a></i> is used oftener with the verb <i>su,</i> ‘press,’ than <i>grāvan,</i> which is more personally conceived, and so appears with <i>vad,</i> ‘speak,’ and so forth; Hillebrandt, 1, 153, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_27_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_27_vei_1203674">↑</a> Rv. v. 45, 7; ix. 11, 5; x. 76, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_28_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_28_vei_1203674">↑</a> Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 4, 28; that <i>ākhara,</i> Rv. x. 94, 5, denotes this is uncertain.</li><li id="fx_120_29_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_29_vei_1203674">↑</a> Rv. v. 31, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_30_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_30_vei_1203674">↑</a> Rv. i. 109, 3; iii. 2, 1; vi. 11, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_31_vei_1203674"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_31_vei_1203674">↑</a> Rv. i. 28. The pestle is <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मन्था">manthā</a>,</i> the mortar <i><a class="vei_120a" href="उलूखल">ulūkhala</a>;</i> in x. 101, 11, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वनस्पति">vanaspati</a></i> and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वन">vana</a></i> may have the same senses respectively.</li></ol></div>

चमू|చమూ|ಚಮೂ|camū|chamuu|chamoo|_vei_120@3675|_vei2_pn476
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3674">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3676">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn474">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn476">2:476</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn479">↷</a><hr><p><b>Camū</b> denotes the vessel used for the offering to the god,<sup id="ref_120_32_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_32_vei_1203675">32</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कलश">kalaśa</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चमस">camasa</a></b> those used for the priests to drink from. Sometimes<sup id="ref_120_33_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_33_vei_1203675">33</a></sup> the <a class="vei_120a" href="चमू">camū</a> denotes the mortar and pestle. Perhaps the vessel was so called because of its mortar-like shape.</p><p>The skin on which the shoots were placed was called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्वच्">tvac</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_34_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_34_vei_1203675">34</a></sup> or twice <i>go</i> (‘cow-hide’).<sup id="ref_120_35_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_35_vei_1203675">35</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कोश">kośa</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_36_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_36_vei_1203675">36</a></sup> Sadhastha,<sup id="ref_120_37_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_37_vei_1203675">37</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="द्रु">dru</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_38_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_38_vei_1203675">38</a></sup>  <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वन">vana</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_39_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_39_vei_1203675">39</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="द्रोण">droṇa</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_40_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_40_vei_1203675">40</a></sup> are all terms used for <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> vessels, while <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्रुव">sruva</a></b><sup id="ref_120_41_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_41_vei_1203675">41</a></sup> denotes the ‘ladle.’</p><p>Apparently the plant was sometimes steeped in water to increase its yield of juice.<sup id="ref_120_42_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_42_vei_1203675">42</a></sup></p><p>It is not possible to describe exactly the details of the process of pressing the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> as practised in the Rigveda. It was certainly purified by being pressed through a sieve<sup id="ref_120_43_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_43_vei_1203675">43</a></sup> (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="पवित्र">pavitra</a></b>). The <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> was then used unmixed (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="शुक्र">śukra</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_44_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_44_vei_1203675">44</a></sup> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शुचि">śuci</a></i>)<sup id="ref_120_45_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_45_vei_1203675">45</a></sup> for <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> and <b>Vāyu,</b> but the <b>Kaṇvas</b> seem to have dropped this usage.<sup id="ref_120_46_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_46_vei_1203675">46</a></sup> The juice is described as brown (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="बभ्रु">babhru</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_47_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_47_vei_1203675">47</a></sup> tawny (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="हरि">hari</a></i>),<sup id="ref_120_48_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_48_vei_1203675">48</a></sup> or ruddy (<i>aruṇa</i>),<sup id="ref_120_49_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_49_vei_1203675">49</a></sup> and as having a fragrant smell,<sup id="ref_120_50_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_50_vei_1203675">50</a></sup> at least as a rule.<sup id="ref_120_51_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_51_vei_1203675">51</a></sup></p><p><a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> was mixed with milk (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="गवाशिर्">gavāśir</a></b>),<sup id="ref_120_52_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_52_vei_1203675">52</a></sup> curd or sour milk (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="दध्याशिर्">dadhyāśir</a></b>),<sup id="ref_120_53_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_53_vei_1203675">53</a></sup> or grain (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="यवाशिर्">yavāśir</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_54_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_54_vei_1203675">54</a></sup> The admixtures are  alluded to with various figurative expressions, as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अत्क">atka</a>,</b> ‘armour’;<sup id="ref_120_55_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_55_vei_1203675">55</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वस्त्र">vastra</a></b><sup id="ref_120_56_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_56_vei_1203675">56</a></sup> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वासस्">vāsas</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_57_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_57_vei_1203675">57</a></sup> ‘garment’; <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अभिश्री">abhiśrī</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_58_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_58_vei_1203675">58</a></sup> ‘admixture’; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="रूप">rūpa</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_59_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_59_vei_1203675">59</a></sup> ‘beauty’; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="श्री">śrī</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_60_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_60_vei_1203675">60</a></sup> ‘splendour’; <i>rasa,</i><sup id="ref_120_61_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_61_vei_1203675">61</a></sup> ‘flavour’; <i>prayas,</i><sup id="ref_120_62_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_62_vei_1203675">62</a></sup> ‘dainty’; and perhaps <i>nabhas,</i><sup id="ref_120_63_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_63_vei_1203675">63</a></sup> ‘fragrance.’ The adjective <i>tīvra</i><sup id="ref_120_64_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_64_vei_1203675">64</a></sup> denotes the ‘pungent’ flavour of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> when so mixed. The <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> shoots, after the juice has been pressed out, are denoted by <i>ṛjīṣa,</i> ‘residue.’<sup id="ref_120_65_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_65_vei_1203675">65</a></sup></p><p>It seems probable that in some cases honey was mixed with <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>: perhaps the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कोश">kośa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="मधु">madhu</a>-ścut,</i> ‘the pail distilling sweetness,’ was used for the mixing.<sup id="ref_120_66_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_66_vei_1203675">66</a></sup> It seems doubtful if <a class="vei_120a" href="सुरा">surā</a> was ever so mixed.<sup id="ref_120_67_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_67_vei_1203675">67</a></sup></p><p>There were three pressings a day of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>, as opposed to the two of the Avesta.<sup id="ref_120_68_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_68_vei_1203675">68</a></sup> The evening pressing was specially connected with the Ṛbhus, the midday with <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>, the morning with <a class="vei_120a" href="अग्नि">agni</a>, but the ritual shows that many other gods also had their share.<sup id="ref_120_69_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_69_vei_1203675">69</a></sup> The drinker of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> and the nondrinker are sharply discriminated in the texts.<sup id="ref_120_70_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_70_vei_1203675">70</a></sup> Localities where <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> was consumed were <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आर्जीक">ārjīka</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पस्त्यावन्त्">pastyāvant</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="शर्यणावन्त्">śaryaṇāvant</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सुषोमा">suṣomā</a>,</b> the territory of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चजनाः">pañcajanāḥ</a></b> or ‘five  peoples,’ and so on.<sup id="ref_120_71_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_71_vei_1203675">71</a></sup> The effects of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> in exhilarating and exciting the drinkers are often alluded to.<sup id="ref_120_72_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_72_vei_1203675">72</a></sup></p><p>It is difficult to decide if <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> was ever a popular, as opposed to a hieratic drink. The evidence for its actual popularity is very slight,<sup id="ref_120_73_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_73_vei_1203675">73</a></sup> and not decisive.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="32"><li id="fx_120_32_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_32_vei_1203675">↑</a> Not the pressing boards, which are unknown in the Rigveda. <i>Cf.</i> Rv. ix. 99, 8; x. 91, 15, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_33_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_33_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. i. 28, 9; iv. 18, 3; vi. 57, 2, etc.; Hillebrandt, 1, 170, 173.</li><li id="fx_120_34_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_34_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. ix. 65, 25; 66, 29; 70, 7; 79, 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_35_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_35_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. x. 94, 9; 116, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_36_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_36_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. vii. 101, 4; viii. 20, 8, etc. It denotes the larger vessel from which the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> is poured into the Kalaśas, or cups.</li><li id="fx_120_37_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_37_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. iii. 62, 15; ix. 1, 2; 17, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_38_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_38_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. ix. 1, 2; 65, 6; 98, 2; in x. 101, 10, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="द्रु">dru</a></i> = mortar.</li><li id="fx_120_39_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_39_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. ii. 14, 9; ix. 66, 9, etc. The word can mean both the vessel into which the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> was poured after preparation, and the vessel from which it was offered to the gods.</li><li id="fx_120_40_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_40_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. ix. 15, 7; 33, 2, etc. The word, having no definite sense, can denote any of the vessels. The <i><a class="vei_120a" href="चमू">camū</a>,</i> on the other hand, was the cup for the gods, the <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कलश">kalaśa</a></i> that for the priests (later it was also used as = <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कोश">kośa</a>,</i> when <i><a class="vei_120a" href="चमस">camasa</a></i> had replaced <i><a class="vei_120a" href="कलश">kalaśa</a></i> as cup for the priests; Hillebrandt, 1, 187).</li><li id="fx_120_41_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_41_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. i. 116, 24. <i>Cf.</i> also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अमत्र">amatra</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="खारी">khārī</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_42_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_42_vei_1203675">↑</a> This process is technically called <i>āpyāyana,</i> ‘causing to swell.’ <i>Cf.</i> Rv. ix. 74, 9; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 5. The exact nature and extent of this process is quite uncertain; Hillebrandt,</p><p>193-195; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 26, xxvi.</li><li id="fx_120_43_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_43_vei_1203675">↑</a> Whether the later practice of purification by means of shoots held in the hands was known to the Rigveda is uncertain, since ii. 14, 8; ix. 71, 3, are quite indecisive. For the various terms used to designate the sieve, see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पवित्र">pavitra</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_44_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_44_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. i. 137, 1; iii. 32, 2; viii. 2, 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_45_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_45_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. i. 5, 5; 30, 2; viii. 2, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_46_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_46_vei_1203675">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. viii. 2, 5. 9. 10, 28, etc. The Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 7, 4, disapproves of the unmixed <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>. Possibly Hillebrandt, 1, 207, 208, may be right in thinking that the Kāṇvas had to lay special stress on the use of the mixtures, because they used a plant which was deficient in the true <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> character.</li><li id="fx_120_47_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_47_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. ix. 33, 2; 63, 4. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_48_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_48_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. ix. 3, 9; 7, 6; 65, 8. 12. 25, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_49_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_49_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. ix. 40, 2; 45, 3; <i>aruṣa,</i> ix. 61, 21; <i>śoṇa,</i> ix. 97, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_50_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_50_vei_1203675">↑</a> ix. 97, 19; 107, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_51_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_51_vei_1203675">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 3, 6. This passage has been relied on by Eggeling, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 26, xxv, as a confirmation of the later description, in a medical work, of the plant as illsmelling. But this plant may have been a different one from that used in Vedic times. The smell may have been due to a substitute being used, or to the genuine plant, brought from a distance, being old and withered.</li><li id="fx_120_52_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_52_vei_1203675">↑</a> Hillebrandt, 1, 219-222.</li><li id="fx_120_53_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_53_vei_1203675">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 221.</li><li id="fx_120_54_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_54_vei_1203675">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 222 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_55_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_55_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. ix. 69, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_56_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_56_vei_1203675">↑</a> ix. 8, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_57_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_57_vei_1203675">↑</a> ix. 69, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_58_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_58_vei_1203675">↑</a> ix. 79, 5; 86, 27.</li><li id="fx_120_59_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_59_vei_1203675">↑</a> Av. ix. 25, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_60_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_60_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. iv. 41, 8; ix. 16, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_61_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_61_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. iii. 48, 1; vi. 47, 1; ix. 97, 14. See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सूद">sūda</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_62_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_62_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. iii. 30, 1; ix. 46, 3; 66, 23.</li><li id="fx_120_63_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_63_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. ix. 83, 5; 97, 21, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_64_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_64_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. i. 23, 1; ii. 41, 14; v. 37, 4; vi. 47, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_65_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_65_vei_1203675">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 8, 5; Av. ix. 6, 16, etc.; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 72; citation in <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 12, etc. <i>Ṛjīṣa</i> as an adjective occurs in Rv. i. 32, 6, and <i>ṛjīṣin</i> in the Rigveda means, according to Hillebrandt, 1, 236, 237, generally ‘one to whom the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> shoots belong.’ <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> tiroahnya</i> is ‘Soma pressed the day before yesterday.’</li><li id="fx_120_66_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_66_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. ix. 103, 3. <i>Cf.</i> ix. 17, 8; ix. 86, 48; 97, 11; 109, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_67_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_67_vei_1203675">↑</a> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुराम">surāma</a>.</b> <i>Cf.</i> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 12, 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxi. 42, and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सुरा">surā</a>-somā, ibid.,</i> xxi. 60.</li><li id="fx_120_68_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_68_vei_1203675">↑</a> Yasna, x. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_69_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_69_vei_1203675">↑</a> Hillebrandt, 1, 257 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_70_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_70_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. i. 110, 7; ii. 30, 7; v. 34, 3. 5; iv. 17, 17; 25, 6. 7; v. 37, 3; vi. 41, 4; vii. 26, 1, etc. There were also rivalries with other <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> sacrifices, Rv. ii. 18, 3; viii. 33, 14; 66, 12, and especially vii. 33, 2, where the Vasiṣṛhas take away <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> from <b>Pāśadyumna Vāyata's</b> <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> sacrifice to <b>Sudās'.</b> Many famous <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> offerers are mentioned: <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अत्रि">atri</a>,</b> v. 51, 8; 72, 1; viii. 42, 5; <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शार्यात">śāryāta</a>,</b> i. 51, 12; iii. 51, 7; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, vii. 35; <b>Śīṣṭas,</b> viii. 53, 4, etc.; <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तुर्वश">turvaśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="यदु">yadu</a>,</b> viii. 45, 27; <b><a class="vei_120a" href="संवर्त">saṃvarta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="कृश">kṛśa</a>,</b> viii. 54, 2; <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नीपातिथि">nīpātithi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="मेध्यातिथि">medhyātithi</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पुष्टिगु">puṣṭigu</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रुष्टिगु">śruṣṭigu</a></b>, viii. 51, 1, etc. The ritual lays stress on the need of continuity in <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>-drinking in a family: <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 5, 5 <i>et seq.;</i> Maitrāvaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_71_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_71_vei_1203675">↑</a> See <i>s.v.;</i> Hillebrandt, 1, 125-143. It is possible that <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> may have grown on the mountains to the north of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a>,</b> whatever may have been its original home, on which <i>cf.</i> Roth, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 38, 134 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_72_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_72_vei_1203675">↑</a> See Rv. viii. 48. It was equally prized in Avestic times. It is, however, seldom spoken of as giving the priests pleasure: Rv. i. 91, 13; viii. 2, 12; x. 167, 3. There are many references to sickness caused by it (Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 13 etc.). The <a class="vei_120a" href="सौत्रामणी">sautrāmaṇī</a> was a rite designed to expiate the ill caused by vomiting <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> as <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a> had done: <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 2, 5, 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 4, 9; xii. 7, 1, 11. The name of the rite is already found in Av. vii. 3, 2, and the rite itself is no doubt older (see also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विषूचिका">viṣūcikā</a></b>). This fact tells in favour of the traditional identification of the plant, for the medical passage quoted by Max Müller refers to its producing vomiting. See also Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 275; Rv. i. 91, 13; 118, 3; viii. 2, 12; 17, 6; 48, 12. Perhaps <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वम्र">vamra</a></b> in i. 112, 15, got his name thence.</li><li id="fx_120_73_vei_1203675"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_73_vei_1203675">↑</a> Rv. viii. 69, 8-10. <i>Cf.</i> viii. 31, 5; i. 28, 5; Hillebrandt, 1, 143-147. The evidence is not decisive; the ordinary <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> sacrifice was clearly a sacrifice of rich patrons.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 272280; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 1-266; 2, 209 <i>et seq.;</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 104 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

सोम प्राति-वेश्य|सोम प्राति-वेश्य|सोम प्रातिवेश्य|सोम प्राति|वेश्य|సోమ ప్రాతి-వేశ్య_౨|సోమ ప్రాతి-వేశ్య|సోమ ప్రాతివేశ్య|సోమ ప్రాతి|వేశ్య|ಸೋಮ ಪ್ರಾತಿ-ವೇಶ್ಯ_೨|ಸೋಮ ಪ್ರಾತಿ-ವೇಶ್ಯ|ಸೋಮ ಪ್ರಾತಿವೇಶ್ಯ|ಸೋಮ ಪ್ರಾತಿ|ವೇಶ್ಯ|soma prāti-veśya_2|soma prāti-veśya|soma prātiveśya|soma prāti|veśya|soma praati-veshya_2|soma praati-veshya|soma praativeshya|soma praati|veshya|_vei_120@3676|_vei2_pn479
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3675">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3677">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn476">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn479">2:479</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn480">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Soma <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राति">prāti</a>-veśya</b> (‘descendant of Prativeśya’) is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रतिवेश्य">prativeśya</a>, in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) at the end of the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka (xv. 1).</p></div>

सोमक साह-देव्य|सोमक साहदेव्य|सोमक साह|देव्य|సోమక సాహ-దేవ్య|సోమక సాహదేవ్య|సోమక సాహ|దేవ్య|ಸೋಮಕ ಸಾಹ-ದೇವ್ಯ|ಸೋಮಕ ಸಾಹದೇವ್ಯ|ಸೋಮಕ ಸಾಹ|ದೇವ್ಯ|somaka sāha-devya|somaka sāhadevya|somaka sāha|devya|somaka saaha-devya|somaka saahadevya|somaka saaha|_vei_120@3677|_vei2_pn479
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3676">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3678">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn476">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn479">2:479</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn480">↷</a><hr><p><b>Somaka <a class="vei_120a" href="साह">sāha</a>-devya</b> (‘descendant of Sahadeva’) is the name of a king of the <b>Sṛnjayas</b> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203677">1</a></sup> He is mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203677">2</a></sup> as having <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्वत">parvata</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नारद">nārada</a></b> as his priests.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203677">↑</a> iv. 15, 7-10.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203677"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203677">↑</a> vii. 34, 9.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 154; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 105.</li></ol></div>

सोम-दक्ष कौश्रेय|सोमदक्ष कौश्रेय|सोम|दक्ष कौश्रेय|సోమ-దక్ష కౌశ్రేయ|సోమదక్ష కౌశ్రేయ|సోమ|దక్ష కౌశ్రేయ|ಸೋಮ-ದಕ್ಷ ಕೌಶ್ರೇಯ|ಸೋಮದಕ್ಷ ಕೌಶ್ರೇಯ|ಸೋಮ|ದಕ್ಷ ಕೌಶ್ರೇಯ|soma-dakṣa kauśreya|somadakṣa kauśreya|soma|dakṣa kauśreya|soma-dakSha kaushreya|somadakSha kaushreya|dakSha kaushreya|soma-daxa kaushreya|somadaxa kaushreya|daxa kaushreya|_vei_120@3678|_vei2_pn479
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3677">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3679">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn476">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn479">2:479</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn480">↷</a><hr><p><b>Soma-dakṣa Kauśreya</b> (‘descendant of <b>Kuśri</b>’) is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203678">1</a></sup> and the Maitrāyaṇī<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203678">2</a></sup> Saṃhitās. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203678">↑</a> xx. 8; xxi. 9, where Somarakṣa Kośreya is read by the Chambers MS. in xx. 8, and Kośreya in xxi. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203678"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203678">↑</a> iii. 2, 7.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 472, 473.</li></ol></div>

सोमपि-त्सरु|सोमपित्सरु|सोमपि|त्सरु|సోమపి-త్సరు|సోమపిత్సరు|సోమపి|త్సరు|ಸೋಮಪಿ-ತ್ಸರು|ಸೋಮಪಿತ್ಸರು|ಸೋಮಪಿ|ತ್ಸರು|somapi-tsaru|somapitsaru|somapi|tsaru|_vei_120@3679|_vei2_pn480
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3678">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3680">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn479">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn480">2:480</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">↷</a><hr><p><b>Somapi-tsaru.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्सरु">tsaru</a>.</b></p></div>

सोम-शुष्म सात्य-यज्ञि|सोम-शुष्म सात्ययज्ञि|सोम-शुष्म सात्य|यज्ञि|సోమ-శుష్మ సాత్య-యజ్ఞి|సోమ-శుష్మ సాత్యయజ్ఞి|సోమ-శుష్మ సాత్య|యజ్ఞి|ಸೋಮ-ಶುಷ್ಮ ಸಾತ್ಯ-ಯಜ್ಞಿ|ಸೋಮ-ಶುಷ್ಮ ಸಾತ್ಯಯಜ್ಞಿ|ಸೋಮ-ಶುಷ್ಮ ಸಾತ್ಯ|ಯಜ್ಞಿ|soma-śuṣma sātya-yajñi|soma-śuṣma sātyayajñi|soma-śuṣma sātya|yajñi|soma-shuShma saatya-yaj~ni|soma-shuShma saatyayaj~ni|soma-shuShma saatya|yaj~ni|_vei_120@3680|_vei2_pn480
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3679">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3681">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn479">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn480">2:480</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">↷</a><hr><p><b>Soma-śuṣma <a class="vei_120a" href="सात्य">sātya</a>-yajñi</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सत्ययज्ञ">satyayajña</a></b>’) is the name in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xi. 6, 2, 1. 3) of a travelling Brahmin who met <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जनक">janaka</a></b> of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विदेह">videha</a>.</b> He may be identical with the man of the same name with the additional patronymic <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्राचीनयोग्य">prācīnayogya</a></b> (‘descendant of Prācīnayoga’), who is mentioned as a pupil of <a class="vei_120a" href="सत्ययज्ञ">satyayajña</a> in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 40, 2).</p></div>

सोम-शुष्मन् वाज-रत्नायन|सोम-शुष्मन् वाजरत्नायन|सोम-शुष्मन् वाज|रत्नायन|సోమ-శుష్మన్ వాజ-రత్నాయన|సోమ-శుష్మన్ వాజరత్నాయన|సోమ-శుష్మన్ వాజ|రత్నాయన|ಸೋಮ-ಶುಷ್ಮನ್ ವಾಜ-ರತ್ನಾಯನ|ಸೋಮ-ಶುಷ್ಮನ್ ವಾಜರತ್ನಾಯನ|ಸೋಮ-ಶುಷ್ಮನ್ ವಾಜ|ರತ್ನಾಯನ|soma-śuṣman vāja-ratnāyana|soma-śuṣman vājaratnāyana|soma-śuṣman vāja|ratnāyana|soma-shuShman vaaja-ratnaayana|soma-shuShman vaajaratnaayana|soma-shuShman vaaja|ratnaayana|_vei_120@3681|_vei2_pn480
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3680">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3682">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn479">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn480">2:480</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">↷</a><hr><p><b>Soma-śuṣman <a class="vei_120a" href="वाज">vāja</a>-ratnāyana</b> (‘descendant of Vājaratna’) is the name in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 21, 5) of the priest who consecrated <b>Śatānīka.</b></p></div>

सौकरायण|సౌకరాయణ|ಸೌಕರಾಯಣ|saukarāyaṇa|saukaraayaNa|_vei_120@3682|_vei2_pn480
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3681">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3683">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn479">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn480">2:480</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saukarāyaṇa</b> is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काषायण">kāṣāyaṇa</a></b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203682">1</a></sup> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रैवणि">traivaṇi</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203682">2</a></sup> in the second <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203682">↑</a> iv. 6, 2. (<a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203682"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203682">↑</a> iv. 5, 27 (Mādhyaṃdina)</li></ol></div>

सौ-जात आराढि|सौजात आराढि|सौ|जात आराढि|సౌ-జాత ఆరాఢి|సౌజాత ఆరాఢి|సౌ|జాత ఆరాఢి|ಸೌ-ಜಾತ ಆರಾಢಿ|ಸೌಜಾತ ಆರಾಢಿ|ಸೌ|ಜಾತ ಆರಾಢಿ|sau-jāta ārāḍhi|saujāta ārāḍhi|sau|jāta ārāḍhi|sau-jaata aaraaDhi|saujaata aaraaDhi|jaata aaraaDhi|_vei_120@3683|_vei2_pn480
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3682">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3684">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn479">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn480">2:480</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sau-<a class="vei_120a" href="जात">jāta</a> Ārāḍhi</b> is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vii. 22, 1).</p></div>

सौत्रामणी|सौत्रामणि|సౌత్రామణీ|సౌత్రామణి|ಸೌತ್ರಾಮಣೀ|ಸೌತ್ರಾಮಣಿ|sautrāmaṇī|sautrāmaṇi|sautraamaNii|sautraamaNi|sautraamaNee|_vei_120@3684|_vei2_pn480
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3683">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3685">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn479">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn480">2:480</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sautrāmaṇī.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>.</b></p></div>

सौ-दन्ति|सौदन्ति|सौ|दन्ति|సౌ-దన్తి|సౌదన్తి|సౌ|దన్తి|ಸೌ-ದನ್ತಿ|ಸೌದನ್ತಿ|ಸೌ|ದನ್ತಿ|sau-danti|saudanti|sau|danti|_vei_120@3685|_vei2_pn480
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3684">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3686">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn479">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn480">2:480</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sau-danti</b> (‘descendant of Sudanta’) occurs in the plural in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xiv. 3, 13), apparently as the name of priests who were contemporaries of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विश्वामित्र">viśvāmitra</a>.</b></p></div>

सौदास|సౌదాస|ಸೌದಾಸ|saudāsa|saudaasa|_vei_120@3686|_vei2_pn480
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3685">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3687">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn479">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn480">2:480</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saudāsa</b> in the plural designates the ‘descendants of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुदास्">sudās</a>,</b>’ who are referred to in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203686"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203686">1</a></sup> as having cast <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शक्ति">śakti</a>,</b> the son of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>,</b> into the fire. Other texts<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203686"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203686">2</a></sup> relate  that <a class="vei_120a" href="वसिष्ठ">vasiṣṭha</a>, his son being slain, desired to avenge himself on the Saudāsas, and eventually succeeded. Geldner<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203686"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203686">3</a></sup> sees a reference to the story in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203686"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203686">4</a></sup> but without cause.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203686"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203686">↑</a> ii. 390 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 18, 47). The story must have appeared in the Śāṭyāyanaka also. <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 159, n. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203686"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203686">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 4, 7, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 7, 3. See also Caland, <i>Über das rituelle <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> des <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>,</i> 20.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203686"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203686">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203686"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203686">↑</a> iii. 53, 22.</li></ol></div>

सौ-द्युम्नि|सौद्युम्नि|सौ|द्युम्नि|సౌ-ద్యుమ్ని|సౌద్యుమ్ని|సౌ|ద్యుమ్ని|ಸೌ-ದ್ಯುಮ್ನಿ|ಸೌದ್ಯುಮ್ನಿ|ಸೌ|ದ್ಯುಮ್ನಿ|sau-dyumni|saudyumni|sau|dyumni|_vei_120@3687|_vei2_pn481
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3686">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3688">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn480">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">2:481</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sau-dyumni</b> (‘descendant of Sudyumna’) is the patronymic of king <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भरत">bharata</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="दौःषन्ति">dauḥṣanti</a></b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 5, 4, 12).</p></div>

सौ-बल|सौबल|सौ|बल|సౌ-బల|సౌబల|సౌ|బల|ಸೌ-ಬಲ|ಸೌಬಲ|ಸೌ|ಬಲ|sau-bala|saubala|sau|bala|_vei_120@3688|_vei2_pn481
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3687">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3689">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn480">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">2:481</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sau-bala,</b> ‘descendant of Subala,’ is the name of a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सर्पि वात्सि">sarpi vātsi</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vi. 24, 16).</p></div>

सौभर|సౌభర|ಸೌಭರ|saubhara|_vei_120@3689|_vei2_pn481
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3688">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3690">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn480">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">2:481</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saubhara,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोभरि">sobhari</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पथिन्">pathin</a></b> in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203689"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203689">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203689"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203689">↑</a> ii. 5, 22 (Mādhyaṃdina = ii. 6, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>); iv. 5, 28 (Mādhyaṃdina = iv. 6, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

सौमाप|సౌమాప|ಸೌಮಾಪ|saumāpa|saumaapa|_vei_120@3690|_vei2_pn481
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3689">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3691">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn480">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">2:481</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saumāpa</b> ‘descendant of Somāpa,’ is the patronymic of the two <b>Mānutantavyas,</b> teachers in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203690"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203690">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203690"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203690">↑</a> xiii. 5, 3, 2, where Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 44, 392, has Saumapa.</li></ol></div>

सौमापि|సౌమాపి|ಸೌಮಾಪಿ|saumāpi|saumaapi|_vei_120@3691|_vei2_pn481
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3690">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3692">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn480">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">2:481</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saumāpi,</b> ‘descendant of Somāpa,’ is the patronymic of a teacher called <b>Priyavrata</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka (xv. 1).</p></div>

सौमायन|సౌమాయన|ಸೌಮಾಯನ|saumāyana|saumaayana|_vei_120@3692|_vei2_pn481
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3691">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3693">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn480">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">2:481</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saumāyana,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b>Budha</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xxiv. 18, 6).</p></div>

सौम्य|సౌమ్య|ಸೌಮ್ಯ|saumya|_vei_120@3693|_vei2_pn481
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3692">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3694">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn480">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">2:481</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saumya</b> is a term of affectionate address (‘my dear’) in the Upaniṣads.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203693"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203693">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203693"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203693">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 1, 3; 2, 13 (<i>varia lectia, somya</i>); Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 4, 4 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

सौ-यवसि|सौयवसि|सौ|यवसि|సౌ-యవసి|సౌయవసి|సౌ|యవసి|ಸೌ-ಯವಸಿ|ಸೌಯವಸಿ|ಸೌ|ಯವಸಿ|sau-yavasi|sauyavasi|sau|yavasi|_vei_120@3694|_vei2_pn481
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3693">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3695">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn480">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">2:481</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sau-yavasi,</b> ‘descendant of <a class="vei_120a" href="सुयवस">suyavasa</a>,’ is the patronymic of <b>Ajīgarta.</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203694"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203694">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203694"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203694">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Bṛāhmaṇa, vii. 15, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xv. 19, 29.</li></ol></div>

सौरी|सौरि|సౌరీ|సౌరి|ಸೌರೀ|ಸೌರಿ|saurī|sauri|saurii|sauree|_vei_120@3695|_vei2_pn482
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3694">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3696">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">2:482</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn483">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saurī</b> is given by Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203695">1</a></sup> as the name of an unknown animal at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203695">2</a></sup> But this is an error: <i><a class="vei_120a" href="सौरी">saurī</a></i> means ‘dedicated to the sun.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203695">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 99.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203695"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203695">↑</a> v. 5, 16, 1 = Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 33 = Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 14.</li></ol></div>

सौ-वर्चनस|सौवर्चनस|सौ|वर्चनस|సౌ-వర్చనస|సౌవర్చనస|సౌ|వర్చనస|ಸೌ-ವರ್ಚನಸ|ಸೌವರ್ಚನಸ|ಸೌ|ವರ್ಚನಸ|sau-varcanasa|sauvarcanasa|sau|varcanasa|sau-varchanasa|sauvarchanasa|varchanasa|_vei_120@3696|_vei2_pn482
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3695">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3697">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">2:482</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn483">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sau-varcanasa</b> is the patronymic of <b>Saṃśravas</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (i. 7, 2, 1).</p></div>

सौ-श्रवस|सौश्रवस|सौ|श्रवस|సౌ-శ్రవస|సౌశ్రవస|సౌ|శ్రవస|ಸೌ-ಶ್ರವಸ|ಸೌಶ್ರವಸ|ಸೌ|ಶ್ರವಸ|sau-śravasa|sauśravasa|sau|śravasa|sau-shravasa|saushravasa|shravasa|_vei_120@3697|_vei2_pn482
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3696">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3698">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">2:482</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn483">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sau-śravasa,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुश्रवस्">suśravas</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b>Upagu</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203697">1</a></sup> and the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कण्व">kaṇva</a></b> Sauśravasas are mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203697">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203697">↑</a> xiv. 6, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203697"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203697">↑</a> xiii. 12 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 3, 474).</li></ol></div>

सौ-श्रोमतेय|सौश्रोमतेय|सौ|श्रोमतेय|సౌ-శ్రోమతేయ|సౌశ్రోమతేయ|సౌ|శ్రోమతేయ|ಸೌ-ಶ್ರೋಮತೇಯ|ಸೌಶ್ರೋಮತೇಯ|ಸೌ|ಶ್ರೋಮತೇಯ|sau-śromateya|sauśromateya|sau|śromateya|sau-shromateya|saushromateya|shromateya|_vei_120@3698|_vei2_pn482
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3697">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3699">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">2:482</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn483">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sau-śromateya,</b> ‘descendant of Suśromatā,’ is the metronymic of <b>Aṣāḍhi</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203698"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203698">1</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b>Āṣādhi.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203698"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203698">↑</a> vi. 2, 1, 37. <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 171, n. 1.</li></ol></div>

सौ-षद्मन|सौषद्मन|सौ|षद्मन|సౌ-షద్మన|సౌషద్మన|సౌ|షద్మన|ಸೌ-ಷದ್ಮನ|ಸೌಷದ್ಮನ|ಸೌ|ಷದ್ಮನ|sau-ṣadmana|sauṣadmana|sau|ṣadmana|sau-Shadmana|sauShadmana|Shadmana|_vei_120@3699|_vei2_pn482
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3698">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3700">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">2:482</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn483">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sau-ṣadmana,</b> ‘descendant of Suṣadman,’ is the patronymic of <b>Viśvantara</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (vii. 27, 1; 34, 7).</p></div>

स्कन्ध्या|స్కన్ధ్యా|ಸ್ಕನ್ಧ್ಯಾ|skandhyā|skandhyaa|_vei_120@3700|_vei2_pn482
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3699">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3701">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">2:482</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn483">↷</a><hr><p><b>Skandhyā,</b> fem. plur., is used in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203700"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203700">1</a></sup> of a disease ‘of the shoulders,’ probably tumours of some kind.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203700"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203700">↑</a> vi. 25, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 472, 473.</li></ol></div>

स्तनयित्नु|స్తనయిత్ను|ಸ್ತನಯಿತ್ನು|stanayitnu|_vei_120@3701|_vei2_pn482
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3700">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3702">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">2:482</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn483">↷</a><hr><p><b>Stanayitnu,</b> sing. and plur., denotes ‘thunder’ from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203701"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203701">1</a></sup> onwards.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203701"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203701">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203701"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203701">↑</a> v. 83, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203701"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203701">↑</a> Av. i. 13, 1; iv. 15, 11; vii. 11, 1, etc.</li></ol></div>

स्तम्ब|स्तंब|స్తమ్బ|స్తంబ|ಸ್ತಮ್ಬ|ಸ್ತಂಬ|stamba|_vei_120@3702|_vei2_pn482
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3701">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3703">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn481">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">2:482</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn483">↷</a><hr><p><b>Stamba</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203702"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203702">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203702"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203702">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘tuft of grass,’ or more generally a ‘bunch’ or ‘cluster.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203702"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203702">↑</a> viii. 6, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203702"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203702">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 6, 4, 1 (of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दर्भ">darbha</a></b>); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 7, 17, 3; iii. 2, 2, 4; 3, 3, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 23, 9, etc.</li></ol></div>

स्तम्भ|स्तंभ|స్తమ్భ|స్తంభ|ಸ್ತಮ್ಭ|ಸ್ತಂಭ|stambha|_vei_120@3703|_vei2_pn483
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3702">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3704">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn483">2:483</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn484">↷</a><hr><p><b>Stambha,</b> ‘pillar,’ is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203703"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203703">1</a></sup> and often in the Sūtras. Earlier <a class="vei_120a" href="स्कम्भ">skambha</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203703"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203703">2</a></sup> is used, but only metaphorically.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203703"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203703">↑</a> xxx. 9; xxxi. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203703"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203703">↑</a> Rv. i. 34, 2; iv. 13, 5, etc.</li></ol></div>

स्तरी|स्तरि|స్తరీ|స్తరి|ಸ್ತರೀ|ಸ್ತರಿ|starī|stari|starii|staree|_vei_120@3704|_vei2_pn483
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3703">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3705">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn483">2:483</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn484">↷</a><hr><p><b>Starī</b> denotes a ‘barren cow’ in the Rigveda (i. 101, 3; 116, 22; 117, 20, etc.).</p></div>

स्ति|స్తి|ಸ್ತಿ|sti|_vei_120@3705|_vei2_pn483
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3704">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3706">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn483">2:483</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn484">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sti.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपस्ति">upasti</a>.</b></p></div>

स्ति-पा|स्तिपा|स्ति|స్తి-పా|స్తిపా|స్తి|ಸ್ತಿ-ಪಾ|ಸ್ತಿಪಾ|ಸ್ತಿ|sti-pā|stipā|sti|sti-paa|stipaa|_vei_120@3706|_vei2_pn483
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3705">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3707">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn483">2:483</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn484">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sti-pā.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपस्ति">upasti</a>.</b></p></div>

स्तुका|స్తుకా|ಸ್ತುಕಾ|stukā|stukaa|_vei_120@3707|_vei2_pn483
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3706">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3708">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn483">2:483</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn484">↷</a><hr><p><b>Stukā</b> denotes a ‘tuft’ of hair or wool in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203707"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203707">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203707"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203707">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203707"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203707">↑</a> ix. 97, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203707"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203707">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxv. 6; Av. vii. 74, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 1, 13, etc.</li></ol></div>

स्तुति|స్తుతి|ಸ್ತುತಿ|stuti|_vei_120@3708|_vei2_pn483
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3707">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3709">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn483">2:483</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn484">↷</a><hr><p><b>Stuti</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203708"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203708">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203708"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203708">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘song of praise.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203708"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203708">↑</a> i. 84, 2; vi. 34, 1; x. 31, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203708"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203708">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 5, 2, 39</li></ol></div>

स्तुप|స్తుప|ಸ್ತುಪ|stupa|_vei_120@3709|_vei2_pn483
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3708">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3710">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn483">2:483</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn484">↷</a><hr><p><b>Stupa</b> means ‘tuft of hair’ in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā (ii. 2; xxv. 2) and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (i. 3, 3, 5; iii. 5, 3, 4). See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्तुका">stukā</a>.</b></p></div>

स्तूप|స్తూప|ಸ್ತೂಪ|stūpa|stuupa|stoopa|_vei_120@3710|_vei2_pn483
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3709">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3711">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn483">2:483</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn484">↷</a><hr><p><b>Stūpa</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203710"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203710">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203710"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203710">2</a></sup> denotes the ‘top-knot’ of hair as designating the upper part of the head.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203710"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203710">↑</a> vii. 2, 1. <i>Cf.</i> i. 24, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203710"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203710">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 3, 6, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 4, 4.</li></ol></div>

स्तृ|స్తృ|ಸ್ತೃ|stṛ|stR|stRi|stRu|stRRi|_vei_120@3711|_vei2_pn483
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3710">«</a>pada9<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3712">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn482">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn483">2:483</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn484">↷</a><hr><p><b>Stṛ</b> (used in the instrumental plural only) denotes the ‘stars’ of heaven.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203711"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203711">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203711"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203711">↑</a> i. 68, 5; 166, 11; ii. 2, 5; 34, 2; iv. 7, 3; vi. 49, 3. 12. In i. 87, 1, it seems to denote a ‘star-like spot’ on the forehead of a cow or bull, but this is uncertain. <i>Cf.</i> Grassmann, <i>Wo7rterbuch, s.v.;</i> above, 1, 233.</li></ol></div>

स्तेग|స్తేగ|ಸ್ತೇಗ|stega|_vei_120@3712|_vei2_pn484
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3711">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3713">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn483">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn484">2:484</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn485">↷</a><hr><p><b>Stega</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203712"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203712">1</a></sup> seems to denote a species of ‘worm.’ The word occurs in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203712"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203712">2</a></sup> also, where its sense is unknown, but may possibly be ‘ploughshare.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203712"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203712">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203712"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203712">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 7, 11, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxv. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203712"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203712">↑</a> x. 31, 9 = Av. xviii. 1, 39. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 98.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203712"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203712">↑</a> Weber, Proceedings of the Berlin Academy, 1895, 833. <i>Cf.</i> Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 824.</li></ol></div>

स्तेन|స్తేన|ಸ್ತೇನ|stena|_vei_120@3713|_vei2_pn484
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3712">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3714">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn483">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn484">2:484</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn485">↷</a><hr><p><b>Stena</b> is a common word for ‘thief’ from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203713">1</a></sup> onwards.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203713">2</a></sup> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तस्कर">taskara</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203713">↑</a> ii. 23, 16; 28, 10; 42, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203713"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203713">↑</a> Av. iv. 3, 4, 5; 36, 7; xix. 47, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 30, 11, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 178 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

स्तेय|స్తేయ|ಸ್ತೇಯ|steya|_vei_120@3714|_vei2_pn484
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3713">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3715">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn483">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn484">2:484</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn485">↷</a><hr><p><b>Steya</b> denotes ‘theft’ in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203714"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203714">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203714"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203714">2</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203714"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203714">↑</a> xi. 8, 20; xiv. 1, 57.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203714"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203714">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vi. 27; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iii. 1. <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="स्तेय">steya</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्">kṛt</a>,</i> ‘thief,’ in Rv. vii. 104, 10.</li></ol></div>

स्तोतृ|స్తోతృ|ಸ್ತೋತೃ|stotṛ|stotR|stotRi|stotRu|stotRRi|_vei_120@3715|_vei2_pn484
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3714">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3716">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn483">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn484">2:484</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn485">↷</a><hr><p><b>Stotṛ</b> denotes ‘praiser’ or ‘panegyrist’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203715"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203715">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203715"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203715">2</a></sup> The word often<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203715"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203715">3</a></sup> occurs in connexion with patrons, the <b>Maghavan</b> or <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सूरि">sūri</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203715"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203715">↑</a> i. 11, 3; 38, 4; iii. 18, 5; vi. 34, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203715"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203715">↑</a> Av. vi. 2, 1; xix. 48, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203715"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203715">↑</a> Rv. i. 124, 10; ii. 1, 16; v. 64, 1; vii. 7, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, vii. 2.</li></ol></div>

स्तोत्र|స్తోత్ర|ಸ್ತೋತ್ರ|stotra|_vei_120@3716|_vei2_pn484
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3715">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3717">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn483">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn484">2:484</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn485">↷</a><hr><p><b>Stotra</b> denotes the ‘song’ of the Udgātṛ and his assistant priests (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋत्विज्">ṛtvij</a></b>), just as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शस्त्र">śastra</a></b> denotes the ‘recitation’ of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a></b> and his assistants. The word has this technical sense quite frequently in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203716">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203716"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203716">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 1, 2, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxix. 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 37, 4; iii. 46, 8; iv. 12, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xvii. 7; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 1, 7; viii. 1, 3, 4, etc. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 353, and Caland and Henry, <i>L'Agniṣṭoma,</i> where the Stotras for that sacrifice are given at length.</li></ol></div>

स्तोम|స్తోమ|ಸ್ತೋಮ|stoma|_vei_120@3717|_vei2_pn485
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3716">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3718">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn484">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn485">2:485</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn486">↷</a><hr><p><b>Stoma</b> denotes ‘song of praise’ in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203717"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203717">1</a></sup> Later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203717"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203717">2</a></sup> the term has the technical sense of the typical forms in which the <b>Stotras</b> are chanted.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203717"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203717">↑</a> i. 114, 9; iii. 5, 2; 58, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203717"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203717">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 1, 2, 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ix. 33; x. 10, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 9, 229, 276; 10, 355; Hillebrandt, Rituallitteratur, 101.</li></ol></div>

स्त्री|स्त्रि|స్త్రీ|స్త్రి|ಸ್ತ್ರೀ|ಸ್ತ್ರಿ|strī|stri|strii|stree|_vei_120@3718|_vei2_pn485
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3717">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3719">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn484">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn485">2:485</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn486">↷</a><hr><p><b>Strī</b> is the ordinary word in poetry and prose for ‘woman,’ without special reference to her as a wife or as a maiden. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नारी">nārī</a></b> has the same sense, but disappears in later prose, while <b>Gnā</b> refers only to the wives of the gods, and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="योषित्">yoṣit</a>,</b> with its cognate words, denotes the young woman as ripe for marriage.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203718">1</a></sup> In the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203718">2</a></sup> <a class="vei_120a" href="स्त्री">strī</a> stands opposed to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुमांस्">pumāṃs</a>,</b> ‘man,’ and once to <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वृषन्">vṛṣan</a>,</i> ‘male person’; not until the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203718">3</a></sup> does it mean ‘wife’ as opposed to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>,</b> ‘husband,’ and even in the Sūtras it is sharply opposed to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जाया">jāyā</a>.</b></p><p>In Vedic India by far the greater part of a woman's life was taken up in her marriage and marital relations (see <a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मातृ">mātṛ</a></b>). There is no trace in the Rigveda of the seclusion of women, which was practically complete in all but the earliest Epic:<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203718">4</a></sup> the maiden may be assumed to have grown up in her father's house, enjoying free intercourse with the youth of the village, and sharing in the work of the house. Education<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203718">5</a></sup> was not denied to them, at any rate in certain cases, for we hear in the Upaniṣads of women who could take no unimportant part in disputations on philosophical topics. Moreover, women were taught to dance and sing, which were unmanly accomplishments.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203718">6</a></sup></p><p>Of the exact legal position of daughters the notices are few and meagre. The Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203718">7</a></sup> however, shows that in the place  of a father the brother was looked to for aid, and that brotherless maidens were apt to be ruined, though religious terrors were believed to await the man who took advantage of their defencelessness.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203718">8</a></sup> Moreover, women could not take an inheritance,<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203718">9</a></sup> and were not independent persons in the eyes of the law, whether married or not. Presumably before marriage they lived on their parents or brothers, and after that on their husbands, while in the event of their husbands predeceasing them, their relatives took the property, burdened with the necessity of maintaining the wife.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203718">10</a></sup> Their earnings would be appropriated by their nearest relative--usually father or brother -in the few cases in which unmarried women could earn anything, as in the case of courtezans.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203718">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtsehaftsnamen,</i> 417.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203718">↑</a> Rv. i. 164, 16; v. 61, 8, etc. So also often later--<i>e.g.,</i> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 7, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 5, 8, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203718">↑</a> xii. 2, 39. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 22, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203718">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 349, 350.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203718">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 351, 352. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गार्गी वाचक्नवी">gārgī vācaknavī</a></b> and others enumerated in the Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 4, 4; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 118, 119.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203718">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 6, 5, Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 7, 3; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 4, 3-6.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203718">↑</a> i. 124, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Av. i. 14, 2; 17, 1; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 328; Hopkins, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 341, and see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्याल">syāla</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="पुत्रिका">putrikā</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203718">↑</a> Rv. iv. 5, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203718">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 5, 8, 2; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 6, 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 4, 2, 13; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 4.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203718"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203718">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> the Attic , Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1912, 427.</li></ol></div>

स्थ-पति|स्थपति|स्थ|पति|స్థ-పతి|స్థపతి|స్థ|పతి|ಸ್ಥ-ಪತಿ|ಸ್ಥಪತಿ|ಸ್ಥ|ಪತಿ|stha-pati|sthapati|stha|pati|_vei_120@3719|_vei2_pn486
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3718">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3720">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn485">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn486">2:486</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn487">↷</a><hr><p><b>Stha-pati</b> is the name of a royal official mentioned in the Atharvaveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203719"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203719">1</a></sup> and often later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203719"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203719">2</a></sup> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रेवोत्तरस्">revottaras</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="चाक्र">cākra</a></b> was the <a class="vei_120a" href="स्थपति">sthapati</a> of the exiled <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दुष्टरीतु">duṣṭarītu</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पौंसायन">pauṃsāyana</a>,</b> a king of the <b>Sṛñjayas,</b> and succeeded in restoring him to his royal dignity.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203719"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203719">3</a></sup> The exact sense of the term is not certain: ‘governor’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203719"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203719">4</a></sup> is possible, but perhaps ‘chief judge’<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203719"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203719">5</a></sup> is more likely; as in the case of the early English judges, his functions may have been both executive and judicial. He is inferior in position to the king's brother.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203719"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203719">6</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203719"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203719">↑</a> ii. 32, 4; v. 23, 11 (of the chief of the worms in both cases).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203719"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203719">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 5, 2, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xvii. 12; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 9, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xvi. 19; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvii. 11, 6. 7; xxiv. 18, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 4, 17, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203719"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203719">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 8, 1, 17; 9, 3, 1 <i>et seq.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="स्थपति">sthapati</a> here is part, as it were, of the name of the man.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203719"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203719">↑</a> In the Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 1, 12; Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, ix. 14, 12, is found <a class="vei_120a" href="निषाद">niṣāda</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="स्थपति">sthapati</a>; perhaps ‘governor of <b>Niṣādas.</b>’ But it may also mean (<i>cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 207, n.) a <a class="vei_120a" href="निषाद">niṣāda</a> as a <a class="vei_120a" href="स्थपति">sthapati</a>; thus, little can be deduced from this passage in particular. Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 41, 111, renders it ‘governor.’ <i>Cf.</i> also St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="औपोदिति">aupoditi</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203719"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203719">↑</a> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 10, 13, n. 3; 13, 203; 17, 200; 18, 260; <i>Über den <a class="vei_120a" href="राजसूय">rājasūya</a>,</i> 15, n. 6; <i>Über den <a class="vei_120a" href="वाजपेय">vājapeya</a>,</i> 9, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxii. 5, 28; 11, 11; Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 7, 11; Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xxii. 7, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203719"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203719">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 4, 4, 17.</li></ol></div>

स्थविर|స్థవిర|ಸ್ಥವಿರ|sthavira|_vei_120@3720|_vei2_pn487
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3719">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3721">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn486">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn487">2:487</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn488">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sthavira,</b> literally ‘elder,’ is used as a sort of epithet of several men; <a class="vei_120a" href="स्थविर">sthavira</a> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाकल्य">śākalya</a></b> occurs in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203720"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203720">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203720"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203720">2</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="स्थविर">sthavira</a> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जातूकर्ण्य">jātūkarṇya</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203720"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203720">3</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> the names <b>Hrasva</b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दीर्घ">dīrgha</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203720"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203720">↑</a> iii. 2, 1. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203720"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203720">↑</a> vii. 16; viii. 1. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203720"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203720">↑</a> xxvi. 5.</li></ol></div>

स्थागर|స్థాగర|ಸ್ಥಾಗರ|sthāgara|sthaagara|_vei_120@3721|_vei2_pn487
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3720">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3722">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn486">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn487">2:487</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn488">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sthāgara</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203721"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203721">1</a></sup> is applied to an ornament (<i>alaṃkāra</i>) meaning ‘made of the fragrant substance Sthagara,’ which elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203721"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203721">2</a></sup> appears as Sthakara.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203721"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203721">↑</a> ii. 3, 10, 2; Apastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiv. 15, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203721"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203721">↑</a> See Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 198; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 311, n. 2; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 5, 265.</li></ol></div>

स्थाणु|స్థాణు|ಸ್ಥಾಣು|sthāṇu|sthaaNu|_vei_120@3722|_vei2_pn487
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3721">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3723">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn486">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn487">2:487</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn488">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sthāṇu</b> in the <b>Rigveda</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203722">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203722">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘stump’ or ‘post’ of wood.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203722">↑</a> x. 40, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203722"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203722">↑</a> Av. x. 4, 1; xiv. 2, 48; xix. 49, 10, etc.</li></ol></div>

स्थातृ|స్థాతృ|ಸ್ಥಾತೃ|sthātṛ|sthaatR|sthaatRi|sthaatRu|sthaatRRi|_vei_120@3723|_vei2_pn487
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3722">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3724">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn486">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn487">2:487</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn488">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sthātṛ</b> (‘he who stands’) in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203723"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203723">1</a></sup> denotes the ‘driver’ of horses or a car.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203723"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203723">↑</a> i. 33, 5; 181, 3; iii. 45, 2, etc.</li></ol></div>

स्था-पत्य|स्थापत्य|स्था|पत्य|స్థా-పత్య|స్థాపత్య|స్థా|పత్య|ಸ್ಥಾ-ಪತ್ಯ|ಸ್ಥಾಪತ್ಯ|ಸ್ಥಾ|ಪತ್ಯ|sthā-patya|sthāpatya|sthā|patya|sthaa-patya|sthaapatya|sthaa|_vei_120@3724|_vei2_pn487
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3723">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3725">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn486">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn487">2:487</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn488">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sthā-patya</b> denotes in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (xvii. 11, 6. 7) the ‘rank or status of a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्थपति">sthapati</a>.</b>’</p></div>

स्थाली|स्थालि|స్థాలీ|స్థాలి|ಸ್ಥಾಲೀ|ಸ್ಥಾಲಿ|sthālī|sthāli|sthaalii|sthaali|sthaalee|_vei_120@3725|_vei2_pn487
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3724">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3726">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn486">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn487">2:487</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn488">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sthālī</b> denotes a ‘cooking pot,’ usually of earthenware, in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203725">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203725">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203725">↑</a> viii. 6, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203725"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203725">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 5, 10, 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 27. 86; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 11, 8, etc. <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्थाली">sthālī</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पाक">pāka</a>,</i> a dish of rice or barley boiled in milk, is mentioned in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vi. 4, 18; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 2, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xi. 6, etc.</li></ol></div>

स्थिरक गार्ग्य|స్థిరక గార్గ్య|ಸ್ಥಿರಕ ಗಾರ್ಗ್ಯ|sthiraka gārgya|sthiraka gaargya|_vei_120@3726|_vei2_pn487
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3725">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3727">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn486">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn487">2:487</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn488">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sthiraka Gārgya</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गर्ग">garga</a></b>’) is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203726"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203726">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203726"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203726">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 373.</li></ol></div>

स्थिवि|స్థివి|ಸ್ಥಿವಿ|sthivi|_vei_120@3727|_vei2_pn487
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3726">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3728">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn486">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn487">2:487</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn488">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sthivi</b> occurs once (used in the plural) in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203727"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203727">1</a></sup> probably meaning ‘bushel.’ The word is also found once in the adjective <i>sthivimant,</i><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203727"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203727">2</a></sup> ‘provided with bushels.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203727"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203727">↑</a> x. 68, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203727"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203727">↑</a> Rv. x. 27, 15. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 238.</li></ol></div>

स्थूणा|స్థూణా|ಸ್ಥೂಣಾ|sthūṇā|sthuuNaa|sthooNaa|_vei_120@3728|_vei2_pn488
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3727">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3729">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn487">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn488">2:488</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn489">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sthūṇā</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203728"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203728">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203728"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203728">2</a></sup> denotes the ‘pillar’ or ‘post’ of a house.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203728"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203728">↑</a> i. 59, 1; v. 45, 2; 62, 7; viii. 17, 14; x. 18, 13 (of the grave).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203728"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203728">↑</a> Av. iii. 12, 6 (of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a>,</b> ‘beam,’ being placed on the pillar); xiv. 1, 63; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiv. 1, 3, 7; 3, 1, 22, etc.; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्थूणा">sthūṇā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="राज">rāja</a>,</i> ‘main pillar,’ iii. 1, 1, 11; 5, 1, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 153.</li></ol></div>

स्थूरि|స్థూరి|ಸ್ಥೂರಿ|sthūri|sthuuri|sthoori|_vei_120@3729|_vei2_pn488
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3728">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3730">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn487">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn488">2:488</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn489">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sthūri</b> has in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203729"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203729">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203729"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203729">2</a></sup> the sense of ‘drawn by one animal’ instead of the usual two (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रथ">ratha</a></b>), and always with an implication of inferiority.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203729"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203729">↑</a> x. 131, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203729"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203729">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 2, 4; iii. 8, 21, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvi. 13, 12; xviii. 9, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 30, 6; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 3, 3, 9, etc.</li></ol></div>

स्थैरकायण|స్థైరకాయణ|ಸ್ಥೈರಕಾಯಣ|sthairakāyaṇa|sthairakaayaNa|_vei_120@3730|_vei2_pn488
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3729">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3731">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn487">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn488">2:488</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn489">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sthairakāyaṇa,</b> ‘descendant of <b>Sthiraka,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b>Mitravarcas</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203730">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203730"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203730">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 372 (where the word is spelt with <i>n</i>).</li></ol></div>

स्थौलाष्ठीवि|స్థౌలాష్ఠీవి|ಸ್ಥೌಲಾಷ್ಠೀವಿ|sthaulāṣṭhīvi|sthaulaaShThiivi|sthaulaaShTheevi|_vei_120@3731|_vei2_pn488
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3730">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3732">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn487">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn488">2:488</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn489">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sthaulāṣṭhīvi,</b> ‘descendant of Sthūlāṣṭhīva,’ is the patronymic of a grammarian in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a> (vii. 14; x. 1).</p></div>

स्नातक|స్నాతక|ಸ್ನಾತಕ|snātaka|snaataka|_vei_120@3732|_vei2_pn488
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3731">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3733">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn487">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn488">2:488</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn489">↷</a><hr><p><b>Snātaka,</b> the designation of the student ‘who has taken the bath,’ marking the termination of his studentship under a religious teacher, occurs in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xii. 1, 1, 10), and repeatedly in the Sūtras. <i>Cf.</i> <b>Brahmacārin.</b></p></div>

स्नावन्य|स्नावन्य|స్నావన్య_౧|స్నావన్య|ಸ್ನಾವನ್ಯ_೧|ಸ್ನಾವನ್ಯ|snāvanya_1|snāvanya|snaavanya_1|snaavanya|_vei_120@3733|_vei2_pn488
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3732">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3734">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn487">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn488">2:488</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn489">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Snāvanya,</b> used in the plural, denotes particular parts of the body of a horse in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (v. 7, 23, 1).</p></div>

स्नावन्य|स्नावन्य|స్నావన్య_౨|స్నావన్య|ಸ್ನಾವನ್ಯ_೨|ಸ್ನಾವನ್ಯ|snāvanya_2|snāvanya|snaavanya_2|snaavanya|_vei_120@3734|_vei2_pn488
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3733">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3735">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn487">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn488">2:488</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn489">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Snāvanya</b> appears to be the name of a people in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203734"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203734">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203734"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203734">↑</a> ii. 5 (in a <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a>). <i>Cf.</i> Caland, <i>Über das rituelle <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> des <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>,</i> 35.</li></ol></div>

स्नुषा|స్నుషా|ಸ್ನುಷಾ|snuṣā|snuShaa|_vei_120@3735|_vei2_pn488
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3734">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3736">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn487">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn488">2:488</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn489">↷</a><hr><p><b>Snuṣā</b> denotes the ‘daughter-in-law’ in relation primarily to her father-in-law, but also to her mother-in-law. In the latter sense the word appears in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203735">1</a></sup> in the epithet <i>su-<a class="vei_120a" href="स्नुषा">snuṣā</a>,</i>  ‘having a good daughter-in-law,’ used of Vṛṣākapāyī, while in the former it occurs in several passages, where the daughterin-law's respect for her father-in-law is mentioned,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203735">2</a></sup> a respect which spirituous liquor alone causes to be violated.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203735">3</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्वशुर">śvaśura</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पति">pati</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203735">↑</a> x. 86, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203735">↑</a> Av. viii. 6, 24; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 22, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 4, 6, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203735"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203735">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 4, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xii. 12 (<i>Indische Studien,</i> 5, 260).</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 414, 415.</li></ol></div>

स्पन्दन|స్పన్దన|ಸ್ಪನ್ದನ|spandana|_vei_120@3736|_vei2_pn489
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3735">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3737">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn488">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn489">2:489</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn490">↷</a><hr><p><b>Spandana</b> in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203736"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203736">1</a></sup> denotes a certain tree. Roth,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203736"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203736">2</a></sup> however, reads <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्यन्दन">syandana</a>,</i> ‘chariot.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203736"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203736">↑</a> iii. 53, 19.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203736"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203736">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Aufrecht, <i>Rigveda,</i> 2, vi; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 63.</li></ol></div>

स्पर्शु|స్పర్శు|ಸ್ಪರ್ಶು|sparśu|sparshu|_vei_120@3737|_vei2_pn489
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3736">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3738">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn488">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn489">2:489</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn490">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sparśu</b> is apparently the name of a western people in the <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (xxi. 13).</p></div>

स्पश्|స్పశ్|ಸ್ಪಶ್|spaś|spash|_vei_120@3738|_vei2_pn489
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3737">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3739">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn488">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn489">2:489</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn490">↷</a><hr><p><b>Spaś.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राजन्">rājan</a>.</b></p></div>

स्फूर्जक|స్ఫూర్జక|ಸ್ಫೂರ್ಜಕ|sphūrjaka|sphuurjaka|sphoorjaka|_vei_120@3739|_vei2_pn489
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3738">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3740">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn488">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn489">2:489</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn490">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sphūrjaka</b> denotes a tree (<i>Diospyros embryopteris</i>) mentioned in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 8, 1, 16).</p></div>

स्मद्-इभ|स्मदिभ|स्मद्|इभ|స్మద్-ఇభ|స్మదిభ|స్మద్|ఇభ|ಸ್ಮದ್-ಇಭ|ಸ್ಮದಿಭ|ಸ್ಮದ್|ಇಭ|smad-ibha|smadibha|smad|ibha|_vei_120@3740|_vei2_pn489
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3739">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3741">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn488">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn489">2:489</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn490">↷</a><hr><p><b>Smad-ibha</b> is found once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203740"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203740">1</a></sup> where Roth<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203740"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203740">2</a></sup> takes the word as perhaps the name of an enemy of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुत्स">kutsa</a>.</b> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इभ">ibha</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203740"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203740">↑</a> x. 49, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203740"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203740">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 380; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 291, n. 5.</li></ol></div>

स्यन्दन|స్యన్దన|ಸ್ಯನ್ದನ|syandana|_vei_120@3741|_vei2_pn489
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3740">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3742">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn488">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn489">2:489</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn490">↷</a><hr><p><b>Syandana,</b> ‘chariot,’ is found in the earlier literature only, if at all, in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203741"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203741">1</a></sup> where <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्पन्दन">spandana</a></b> is the received reading. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203741"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203741">↑</a> iii. 53, 19, according to Roth. A wood called <a class="vei_120a" href="स्यन्दन">syandana</a> seems to be meant in the <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, viii. 15.</li></ol></div>

स्याल|స్యాల|ಸ್ಯಾಲ|syāla|syaala|_vei_120@3742|_vei2_pn490
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3741">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3743">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn489">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn490">2:490</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn491">↷</a><hr><p><b>Syāla,</b> a word occurring in only one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203742"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203742">1</a></sup> appears to denote the brother of a man's wife, who is regarded as willing to protect her, and so secure a marriage for her.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203742"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203742">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203742"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203742">↑</a> i. 109, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203742"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203742">↑</a> So Sāyaṇa on Rv., <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></p><p><i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 517; Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 79.</li></ol></div>

स्यूम-गभस्ति|स्यूमगभस्ति|स्यूम|गभस्ति|స్యూమ-గభస్తి|స్యూమగభస్తి|స్యూమ|గభస్తి|ಸ್ಯೂಮ-ಗಭಸ್ತಿ|ಸ್ಯೂಮಗಭಸ್ತಿ|ಸ್ಯೂಮ|ಗಭಸ್ತಿ|syūma-gabhasti|syūmagabhasti|syūma|gabhasti|syuuma-gabhasti|syuumagabhasti|syuuma|syooma-gabhasti|syoomagabhasti|syooma|_vei_120@3743|_vei2_pn490
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3742">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3744">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn489">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn490">2:490</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn491">↷</a><hr><p><b>Syūma-gabhasti.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गभस्ति">gabhasti</a>.</b></p></div>

स्यूम-गृभ्|स्यूमगृभ्|स्यूम|गृभ्|స్యూమ-గృభ్|స్యూమగృభ్|స్యూమ|గృభ్|ಸ್ಯೂಮ-ಗೃಭ್|ಸ್ಯೂಮಗೃಭ್|ಸ್ಯೂಮ|ಗೃಭ್|syūma-gṛbh|syūmagṛbh|syūma|gṛbh|syuuma-gRbh|syuumagRbh|syuuma|gRbh|syooma-gRbh|syoomagRbh|syooma|syuuma-gRibh|syuumagRibh|gRibh|syuuma-gRubh|syuumagRubh|gRubh|syuuma-gRRibh|syuumagRRibh|gRRibh|_vei_120@3744|_vei2_pn490
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3743">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3745">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn489">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn490">2:490</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn491">↷</a><hr><p><b>Syūma-gṛbh,</b> used of a horse in the Rigveda (vi. 36, 2) seems to mean ‘grasping the bit between his teeth,’ as a horse does when anxious to break away from control.</p></div>

स्यूमन्|స్యూమన్|ಸ್ಯೂಮನ್|syūman|syuuman|syooman|_vei_120@3745|_vei2_pn490
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3744">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3746">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn489">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn490">2:490</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn491">↷</a><hr><p><b>Syūman</b> in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203745"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203745">1</a></sup> denotes, according to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203745"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203745">2</a></sup> the strap fastening the door of a house, the Homeric.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203745"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203745">↑</a> iii. 61, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203745"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203745">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i></li></ol></div>

स्यूम-रश्मि|स्यूमरश्मि|स्यूम|रश्मि|స్యూమ-రశ్మి|స్యూమరశ్మి|స్యూమ|రశ్మి|ಸ್ಯೂಮ-ರಶ್ಮಿ|ಸ್ಯೂಮರಶ್ಮಿ|ಸ್ಯೂಮ|ರಶ್ಮಿ|syūma-raśmi|syūmaraśmi|syūma|raśmi|syuuma-rashmi|syuumarashmi|syuuma|rashmi|syooma-rashmi|syoomarashmi|syooma|_vei_120@3746|_vei2_pn490
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3745">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3747">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn489">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn490">2:490</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn491">↷</a><hr><p><b>Syūma-raśmi</b> is the name of a protégé of the Aśvins in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203746"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203746">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203746"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203746">↑</a> i. 112 16; viii. 52, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 150, 163.</li></ol></div>

स्रक्ति|స్రక్తి|ಸ್ರಕ್ತಿ|srakti|_vei_120@3747|_vei2_pn490
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3746">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3748">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn489">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn490">2:490</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn491">↷</a><hr><p><b>Srakti</b> is found in the description of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दाशराज्ञ">dāśarājña</a></b> in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203747">1</a></sup> where Hopkins<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203747">2</a></sup> thinks the sense of ‘spears’ essential.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203747">↑</a> vii. 18, 17.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203747"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203747">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 15, 264, n.</li></ol></div>

स्रज्|స్రజ్|ಸ್ರಜ್|sraj|_vei_120@3748|_vei2_pn490
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3747">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3749">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn489">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn490">2:490</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn491">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sraj,</b> ‘garland,’ is mentioned in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203748"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203748">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203748"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203748">2</a></sup> as often worn by men when anxious to appear handsome, as at a wedding and so on. The Aśvins are described as ‘lotuswreathed’ (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="पुष्कर">puṣkara</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="स्रज्">sraj</a></i>).<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203748"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203748">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203748"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203748">↑</a> iv. 38, 6; v. 53, 4; viii. 47, 15; 56, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203748"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203748">↑</a> Av. i. 14, 1 (where it means a ‘cluster of flowers’ from a tree); <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvi. 4, 1; xviii. 3, 2; 7, 6; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xiii. 5, 4, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203748"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203748">↑</a> Rv. x. 184, 3. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 265.</li></ol></div>

स्राक्त्य|స్రాక్త్య|ಸ್ರಾಕ್ತ್ಯ|srāktya|sraaktya|_vei_120@3749|_vei2_pn491
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3748">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3750">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn490">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn491">2:491</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn492">↷</a><hr><p><b>Srāktya</b> is an adjective describing an amulet (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="मणि">maṇi</a></b>) in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203749"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203749">1</a></sup> According to Weber,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203749"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203749">2</a></sup> it designates a crystal (literally ‘many-cornered’). The commentators,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203749"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203749">3</a></sup> however, agree in explaining the word to mean ‘derived from the Sraktya’--<i>i.e.,</i> from the Tilaka tree (<i>Clerodendrum phlomoides</i>).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203749"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203749">↑</a> viii. 5, 4. 7. 8. <i>Cf.</i> ii. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203749"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203749">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 164.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203749"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203749">↑</a> See Bloomfield, <i>American Journal of Philology,</i> 7, 477; <i>Hymns of the Athar vaveda,</i> 577.</li></ol></div>

स्रुच्|స్రుచ్|ಸ್ರುಚ್|sruc|sruch|_vei_120@3750|_vei2_pn491
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3749">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3751">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn490">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn491">2:491</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn492">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sruc</b> denotes a ‘large wooden sacrificial ladle’ (used for pouring clarified butter on the fire) in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203750"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203750">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203750"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203750">2</a></sup> It is of the length of an arm, with a bowl of the size of a hand and a beaklike spout.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203750"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203750">↑</a> i. 84, 18; 110, 6; 144, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203750"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203750">↑</a> Av. v. 27, 5; vi. 114, 3; ix. 6, 17, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> for its shape, etc., Max Müller, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 9, xli, lxxx; Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 67; 26, 20, 23.</li></ol></div>

स्रुव|స్రువ|ಸ್ರುವ|sruva|_vei_120@3751|_vei2_pn491
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3750">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3752">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn490">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn491">2:491</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn492">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sruva,</b> as opposed to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्रुच्">sruc</a>,</b> denotes in the ritual literature<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203751"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203751">1</a></sup> a small ladle used to convey the offering (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="आज्य">ājya</a></b>) from the cookingpot (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्थाली">sthālī</a></b>) to the large ladle (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="जुहू">juhū</a></b>). In the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203751"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203751">2</a></sup> however, it was clearly used for the actual <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> libation.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203751"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203751">↑</a> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 11, 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203751"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203751">↑</a> i. 116, 24; 121, 6, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Max Müller, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 9, viii; Caland and Henry, <i>L'Agniṣṭoma</i> xliv; Plate I., No. 9; Plate II., No. 11 Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 12, 68; 26, 20.</li></ol></div>

स्रेक-पर्ण|स्रेकपर्ण|स्रेक|पर्ण|స్రేక-పర్ణ|స్రేకపర్ణ|స్రేక|పర్ణ|ಸ್ರೇಕ-ಪರ್ಣ|ಸ್ರೇಕಪರ್ಣ|ಸ್ರೇಕ|ಪರ್ಣ|sreka-parṇa|srekaparṇa|sreka|parṇa|sreka-parNa|srekaparNa|parNa|_vei_120@3752|_vei2_pn491
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3751">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3753">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn490">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn491">2:491</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn492">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sreka-parṇa</b> in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203752">1</a></sup> seems to mean ‘like the oleander leaf.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203752"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203752">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 6, 6, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 15.</li></ol></div>

स्वज|స్వజ|ಸ್ವಜ|svaja|_vei_120@3753|_vei2_pn491
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3752">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3754">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn490">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn491">2:491</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn492">↷</a><hr><p><b>Svaja</b> in the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203753"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203753">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203753"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203753">2</a></sup> denotes the ‘viper.’ The word is explained by the commentators as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्व">sva</a>-ja,</i> ‘self born,’ but Roth,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203753"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203753">3</a></sup> Weber,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203753"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203753">4</a></sup> and Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203753"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203753">5</a></sup> prefer to derive it from the root <i>svaj,</i> ‘clasp,’ ‘encircle.’ In the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203753"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203753">6</a></sup> the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हरिण">hariṇa</a></b> is said to kill the viper.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203753"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203753">↑</a> iii. 27, 4; v. 14, 10; vi. 56, 2; x. 4, 10, 15, 17; xii. 3, 58.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203753"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203753">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 10, 2; 14, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 26, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203753"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203753">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.,</i> gives the alternative <i>vivipara.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203753"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203753">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, 2, 89, n.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203753"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203753">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 95.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203753"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203753">↑</a> iii. 9, 3.</li></ol></div>

स्वधिति|स्वधिति|స్వధితి_౧|స్వధితి|ಸ್ವಧಿತಿ_೧|ಸ್ವಧಿತಿ|svadhiti_1|svadhiti|_vei_120@3754|_vei2_pn492
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3753">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3755">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn491">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn492">2:492</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn493">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Svadhiti</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203754">1</a></sup> denotes the ‘axe’ or ‘knife’ used for dissecting the sacrificial horse. In all the other passages in that Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203754">2</a></sup> the sense of ‘axe’ for cutting wood is adequate; reference is made in one place<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203754">3</a></sup> to sharpening the axe on the whetstone (<i>kṣṇotra</i>). In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203754">4</a></sup> the term seems once to denote the copper (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="लोहित">lohita</a></i>)<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203754">5</a></sup> knife used to mark the ears of cattle; the carpenter's knife or axe is also twice referred to there.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203754">6</a></sup> Later the word means ‘axe’ generally.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203754">7</a></sup> As a weapon it does not appear at all.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203754">8</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203754">↑</a> i. 162, 9. 18. 20.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203754">↑</a> ii. 39, 7; iii. 2, 10; 8, 6, 11; v. 7, 8; vii. 3, 9; viii. 102, 19; x. 89, 7. <i>Cf.</i> n. 8.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203754">↑</a> ii. 39, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203754">↑</a> vi. 141, 2. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="मन्त्र">mantra</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 8, 7; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 386, 387; Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 159, who is inclined to compare <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परशु">paraśu</a></b> in Rv. iii. 53, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203754">↑</a> Geldner, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> understands this word to mean ‘red-hot.’</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203754">↑</a> ix. 4, 6 (probably so to be taken); xii. 3, 33. In xviii. 2, 35, the sense is quite different. See Whitney, <i>op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 845.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203754">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 3, 3, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ii. 15 (the slaughteringknife); v. 43 (the axe to fell the tree) etc.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203754"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203754">↑</a> In Rv. x. 92, 15, <a class="vei_120a" href="स्वधिति">svadhiti</a> may refer to the thunderbolt of <a class="vei_120a" href="इन्द्र">indra</a>.</li></ol></div>

स्वधिति|स्वधिति|స్వధితి_౨|స్వధితి|ಸ್ವಧಿತಿ_೨|ಸ್ವಧಿತಿ|svadhiti_2|svadhiti|_vei_120@3755|_vei2_pn492
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3754">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3756">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn491">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn492">2:492</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn493">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Svadhiti</b> in certain passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203755"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203755">1</a></sup> denotes, according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, a great tree with hard wood. This interpretation seems probable.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203755"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203755">↑</a> v. 32, 10; ix. 96, 6. <i>Cf.</i> i. 88, 2.</li></ol></div>

स्वनद्-रथ|स्वनद्रथ|स्वनद्|रथ|స్వనద్-రథ|స్వనద్రథ|స్వనద్|రథ|ಸ್ವನದ್-ರಥ|ಸ್ವನದ್ರಥ|ಸ್ವನದ್|ರಥ|svanad-ratha|svanadratha|svanad|ratha|_vei_120@3756|_vei2_pn492
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3755">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3757">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn491">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn492">2:492</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn493">↷</a><hr><p><b>Svanad-ratha</b> (‘having a rattling car’) is taken by Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203756"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203756">1</a></sup> as a proper name of <b>Āsaṅga</b> in the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203756"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203756">2</a></sup> But the word is most probably only an epithet. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203756"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203756">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 159.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203756"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203756">↑</a> viii. 1, 32.</li></ol></div>

स्वनय भाव्य|స్వనయ భావ్య|ಸ್ವನಯ ಭಾವ್ಯ|svanaya bhāvya|svanaya bhaavya|_vei_120@3757|_vei2_pn493
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3756">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3758">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn492">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn493">2:493</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn494">↷</a><hr><p><b>Svanaya Bhāvya</b> is the name of a prince on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सिन्धु">sindhu</a></b> (Indus) who bestowed gifts on <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कक्षीवन्त्">kakṣīvant</a>,</b> according to the Rigveda (i. 126, 1. 3). He is called Svanaya <a class="vei_120a" href="भावयव्य">bhāvayavya</a> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (xvi. 11, 5).</p></div>

स्वप्न|స్వప్న|ಸ್ವಪ್ನ|svapna|_vei_120@3758|_vei2_pn493
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3757">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3759">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn492">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn493">2:493</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn494">↷</a><hr><p><b>Svapna,</b> ‘dream,’ is referred to in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203758"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203758">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203758"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203758">2</a></sup> Evil dreams<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203758"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203758">3</a></sup> are often mentioned. The Āraṇyakas of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203758"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203758">4</a></sup> contain a list of dreams with their signification, as well as of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रत्यक्ष">pratyakṣa</a>-darśanāni,</i> ‘sights seen with one's own eyes.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203758"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203758">↑</a> ii. 28, 10; x. 162, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203758"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203758">↑</a> Av. vii. 101, 1; x. 3, 6; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xx. 16; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 2, 2, 23, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203758"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203758">↑</a> Rv. ii. 28, 10; Av. x. 3, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203758"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203758">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 2, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, xi. 3. <i>Cf.</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="कौशिक">kauśika</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xlvi. 9 <i>et seq.;</i> Atharvaveda Pariśiṣṭa, lxviii.</li></ol></div>

स्वर्|స్వర్|ಸ್ವರ್|svar|_vei_120@3759|_vei2_pn493
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3758">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3760">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn492">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn493">2:493</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn494">↷</a><hr><p><b>Svar</b> denotes the ‘sun’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203759"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203759">1</a></sup> and the ‘heaven of light’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1b_vei_1203759"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1b_vei_1203759">1b</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2b_vei_1203759"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2b_vei_1203759">2b</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203759"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203759">↑</a> i. 71, 2; 105, 3; 148, 1, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 14.</li></ol><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1b_vei_1203759"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1b_vei_1203759">↑</a> i. 71, 2; 105, 3; 148, 1, etc.; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ii. 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2b_vei_1203759"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2b_vei_1203759">↑</a> Rv. iii. 2, 7; v. 83, 4; x. 66, 4. 9, etc.; Av. iv. 11, 6; 14, 2, etc.</li></ol></div>

स्वर|స్వర|ಸ್ವರ|svara|_vei_120@3760|_vei2_pn493
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3759">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3761">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn492">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn493">2:493</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn494">↷</a><hr><p><b>Svara</b> denotes in the Upaniṣads<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203760">1</a></sup> the sound of a vowel: these are described<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203760">2</a></sup> as being <i><a class="vei_120a" href="घोषवन्त्">ghoṣavant</a>,</i> ‘sonant,’ and also as <i>balavant,</i> ‘uttered with force.’ The precise word for a mute is <i>sparśa,</i><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203760">3</a></sup> ‘contact,’ while <i>ūṣman</i> denotes a ‘sibilant,’ and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="स्वर">svara</a></i> a ‘vowel,’ in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203760">3</a></sup> and <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyakas.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203760">4</a></sup> The semivowels are there denoted by <i>anta-<a class="vei_120a" href="स्था">sthā</a></i> (‘intermediate’)<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203760">5</a></sup> or <i>akṣara.</i><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203760">6</a></sup> Another division in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203760">7</a></sup> is into <i><a class="vei_120a" href="घोष">ghoṣa</a>, ūṣman,</i> and <i>vyañjana,</i> apparently ‘vowels,’ ‘sibilants,’ and ‘consonants’ respectively. <i><a class="vei_120a" href="घोष">ghoṣa</a></i> elsewhere in that Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203760">8</a></sup> seems to have the general sense of ‘sounds.’ The <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a><sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203760">9</a></sup> refers to <i><a class="vei_120a" href="मात्रा">mātrā</a>,</i> a ‘mora’;<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203760">10</a></sup> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="बल">bala</a>,</i> ‘force’ of utterance, and <i><a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a>,</i> ‘letter,’ an expression found elsewhere<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203760">11</a></sup> in the explanation of <i>om,</i> as compacted of <i>a + u + m.</i> </p><p>The <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1203760">12</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1203760">13</a></sup> recognize the three forms of the Rigveda text as <i>pratṛṇṇa, nirbhuja,</i> and <i>ubhayam-antareṇa,</i> denoting respectively the Saṃhitā, <a class="vei_120a" href="पद">pada</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="क्रम">krama</a> Pāṭhas of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1203760">14</a></sup> The same authorities<sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1203760">15</a></sup> recognize the importance of the distinction of the cerebral and dental <i>n</i> and <i>s,</i> and refer<sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1203760">16</a></sup> to the <b>Māṇḍūkeyas'</b> mode of recitation. They also discuss<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1203760">17</a></sup> Sandhi, the euphonic ‘combination’ of letters.</p><p>The Prātiśākhyas of the several Saṃhitās develop in detail the grammatical terminology, and Yāska's <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a><sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1203760">18</a></sup> contains a good deal of grammatical material. The Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_19_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_19_vei_1203760">19</a></sup> distinguishes the genders, and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_20_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_20_vei_1203760">20</a></sup> the division of words in the <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> recitation.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203760">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 22, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203760">↑</a> Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203760">↑</a> iii. 2, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203760">↑</a> iii. 2, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203760">↑</a> viii. 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203760">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203760">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> Āraṇyaka, viii. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203760">↑</a> ii. 2, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203760">↑</a> ii. 2, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Keith's edition, p. 213.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203760">↑</a> <i>Loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203760">↑</a> Also <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka, iii. 1, 5; Sāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, vii. 13.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203760">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 32, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxvi. 5; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, x. 4; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> v. 32.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1203760">↑</a> iii. 1, 3, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1203760">↑</a> vii. 10, 12.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1203760">↑</a> Max Müller, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a> Prātiśākhya,</i> ii. <i>et seq.; Nachträge,</i> ii; Oldenberg, <i>Prolegomena,</i> 380 <i>et seq.; Sacred Books of the East,</i> 30, 146 <i>et seq.;</i> Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 51.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1203760">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a>, iii. 2, 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a>, viii. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1203760">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a>, iii. 1, 1; 2, 6; Sāṅkhāyana, vii. 2; viii. 11.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1203760">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a>, iii. 1, 2. 3. 5; 2, 2 <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a>, vii. 13; viii. 1, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1203760">↑</a> See Roth's edition (1852), p. 222, for a list of teachers cited by <a class="vei_120a" href="यास्क">yāska</a>, especially <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौत्स">kautsa</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाकटायन">śākaṭāyana</a>.</b></li><li id="fx_120_19_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_19_vei_1203760">↑</a> x. 5, 1, 2. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_20_vei_1203760"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_20_vei_1203760">↑</a> x. 9, 1. 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cultur,</i> 701 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

स्व-राज्|स्वराज्|स्व|राज्|స్వ-రాజ్|స్వరాజ్|స్వ|రాజ్|ಸ್ವ-ರಾಜ್|ಸ್ವರಾಜ್|ಸ್ವ|ರಾಜ್|sva-rāj|svarāj|sva|rāj|sva-raaj|svaraaj|raaj|_vei_120@3761|_vei2_pn494
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3760">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3762">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn493">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn494">2:494</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn495">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sva-rāj,</b> ‘self-ruler,’ ‘king,’ is found frequently in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203761"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203761">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203761"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203761">2</a></sup> It is the technical term for the kings of the west according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203761"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203761">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203761"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203761">↑</a> i. 36, 7; 51, 15; 61, 9, etc. (of gods).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203761"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203761">↑</a> Av. xvii. 1, 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 6, 2; iv. 4, 8, 1; v. 5, 4, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203761"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203761">↑</a> viii. 14. <i>Cf.</i> perhaps the republican form of government of which traces are seen in the Buddhist literature by Rhys Davids, <i>Buddhist India,</i> 19.</li></ol></div>

स्वरु|స్వరు|ಸ್ವರು|svaru|_vei_120@3762|_vei2_pn494
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3761">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3763">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn493">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn494">2:494</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn495">↷</a><hr><p><b>Svaru</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203762"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203762">1</a></sup> or later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203762"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203762">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘post,’ or more precisely in the ritual a splinter of the <a class="vei_120a" href="यूप">yūpa</a>, or sacrificial post.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203762"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203762">3</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203762"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203762">↑</a> i. 92, 5; 162, 9; iii. 8, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203762"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203762">↑</a> Av. iv. 24, 4; xii. 1, 13, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203762"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203762">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 3, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 7, 1; vi. 3, 4 9, etc.; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 7, 1, 22; 8, 1, 5, etc.</li></ol></div>

स्वर्-जित् नाग्न-जित|स्वर्-जित् नाग्नजित|स्वर्-जित् नाग्न|जित|స్వర్-జిత్ నాగ్న-జిత|స్వర్-జిత్ నాగ్నజిత|స్వర్-జిత్ నాగ్న|జిత|ಸ್ವರ್-ಜಿತ್ ನಾಗ್ನ-ಜಿತ|ಸ್ವರ್-ಜಿತ್ ನಾಗ್ನಜಿತ|ಸ್ವರ್-ಜಿತ್ ನಾಗ್ನ|ಜಿತ|svar-jit nāgna-jita|svar-jit nāgnajita|svar-jit nāgna|jita|svar-jit naagna-jita|svar-jit naagnajita|svar-jit naagna|_vei_120@3763|_vei2_pn495
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3762">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3764">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn494">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn495">2:495</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn496">↷</a><hr><p><b>Svar-<a class="vei_120a" href="जित्">jit</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="नाग्न">nāgna</a>-jita</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नग्न-जित्">nagna-jit</a></b>’) is the name of a royal personage in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203763">1</a></sup> a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गन्धार">gandhāra</a></b> prince, whose views on the ritual are referred to with contempt.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203763"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203763">↑</a> viii. 1, 4, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 1^2, 515.</li></ol></div>

स्वर्-णर|स्वर्णर|स्वर्|णर|స్వర్-ణర|స్వర్ణర|స్వర్|ణర|ಸ್ವರ್-ಣರ|ಸ್ವರ್ಣರ|ಸ್ವರ್|ಣರ|svar-ṇara|svarṇara|svar|ṇara|svar-Nara|svarNara|Nara|_vei_120@3764|_vei2_pn495
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3763">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3765">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn494">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn495">2:495</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn496">↷</a><hr><p><b>Svar-ṇara</b> appears to be the proper name of a sacrificer in two verses of the Rigveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203764"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203764">1</a></sup> According to Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203764"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203764">2</a></sup> it everywhere<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203764"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203764">3</a></sup> means a specially sacred lake and the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>-producing district around it.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203764"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203764">↑</a> viii. 3, 12; 12, 2. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 160; Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 17, 89.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203764"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203764">↑</a> <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 209.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203764"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203764">↑</a> Rv. iv. 21, 3; v. 18, 4; 14, 1; viii. 6, 39; 65, 2; 103, 14; ix. 70, 6; x. 65, 4. Perhaps in viii. 12, 2, it means ‘coming from <a class="vei_120a" href="स्वर्णर">svarṇara</a>.’</li></ol></div>

स्वर्-भानु असुर|स्वर्भानु असुर|स्वर्|भानु असुर|స్వర్-భాను అసుర|స్వర్భాను అసుర|స్వర్|భాను అసుర|ಸ್ವರ್-ಭಾನು ಅಸುರ|ಸ್ವರ್ಭಾನು ಅಸುರ|ಸ್ವರ್|ಭಾನು ಅಸುರ|svar-bhānu asura|svarbhānu asura|svar|bhānu asura|svar-bhaanu asura|svarbhaanu asura|bhaanu asura|_vei_120@3765|_vei2_pn495
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3764">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3766">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn494">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn495">2:495</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn496">↷</a><hr><p><b>Svar-bhānu Asura</b> is the name, in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203765"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203765">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203765"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203765">2</a></sup> of a demon supposed to have eclipsed the sun. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सूर्य">sūrya</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203765"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203765">↑</a> v. 40, 5. 6. 8. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203765"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203765">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 2, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 5, 2; 6, 13; vi. 6, 8; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 2, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xxiv. 3.</li></ol></div>

स्व-सर|स्वसर|स्व|सर|స్వ-సర|స్వసర|స్వ|సర|ಸ್ವ-ಸರ|ಸ್ವಸರ|ಸ್ವ|ಸರ|sva-sara|svasara|sva|sara|_vei_120@3766|_vei2_pn495
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3765">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3767">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn494">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn495">2:495</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn496">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sva-sara,</b> according to the St. Petersburg Dictionary, denotes ‘cattle stall,’<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203766"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203766">1</a></sup> and more generally ‘dwelling-place,’ ‘house,’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203766"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203766">2</a></sup> and then ‘nest of birds.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203766"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203766">3</a></sup> Geldner,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203766"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203766">4</a></sup> however, shows that the real sense is the ‘wandering at will’ of cattle, more precisely their ‘grazing in the morning,’<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203766"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203766">5</a></sup> and in the case of birds their ‘early flight’ from the nest,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203766"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203766">6</a></sup> while metaphorically it is applied first to the morning pressing of <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> and then to all three pressings.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203766"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203766">7</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203766"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203766">↑</a> Rv. i. 3, 8; ii. 2, 2; 34, 8; v. 62, 2; viii. 88, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="सामवेद">sāmaveda</a>, i. 5, 2, 3, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203766"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203766">↑</a> Rv. i. 34, 7; iii. 60, 6; 61, 4; vi. 68, 10; viii. 99, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 3, 5, 20.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203766"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203766">↑</a> Rv. ii. 19, 2; 34, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203766"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203766">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 110-115.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203766"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203766">↑</a> Rv. i. 3, 8; ii. 2, 2; 34, 8; v. 62, 2; viii. 88, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203766"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203766">↑</a> Rv. ii. 19, 2; 34, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203766"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203766">↑</a> Rv. i. 34, 7; iii. 60, 6; vi. 68, 10; viii. 99, 1. In Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> it is equivalent to ‘libation.’</li></ol></div>

स्वसृ|స్వసృ|ಸ್ವಸೃ|svasṛ|svasR|svasRi|svasRu|svasRRi|_vei_120@3767|_vei2_pn495
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3766">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3768">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn494">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn495">2:495</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn496">↷</a><hr><p><b>Svasṛ</b> is the regular word from the Rigveda onwards<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203767"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203767">1</a></sup> for ‘sister.’ Like the word <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भ्रातृ">bhrātṛ</a>,</b> the term sister can be applied  to things not precisely so related. For example, in the Rigveda the fingers and the seasons are ‘sisters,’ and night is the sister of dawn, for whom, as the elder, she makes way.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203767"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203767">2</a></sup> The <b>Paṇis</b> offer to adopt Saramā as their sister;<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203767"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203767">3</a></sup> but this use is not applied--any more than in the case of <a class="vei_120a" href="भ्रातृ">bhrātṛ</a>--to ordinary human beings.</p><p>The sister stood in a close relation to her brother. If the father was dead or feeble, the sister was dependent on her brother and on his wife, as appears from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203767"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203767">4</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203767"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203767">5</a></sup> Moreover, maidens without brothers were apt to find marriage difficult, and to degenerate into Hetairai;<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203767"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203767">6</a></sup> but it is not certain whether this was due, as Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203767"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203767">7</a></sup> thinks, to brothers being required to arrange marriages for orphan girls, or because sonless fathers were anxious to make their daughters <b>Putrikās,</b> in order that they themselves, instead of the husbands, should count the daughters' sons as their own.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203767"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203767">8</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जामि">jāmi</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203767"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203767">↑</a> Rv. ii. 32, 6; vi. 55, 4. 5; viii. 101, 15; x. 108, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203767"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203767">↑</a> Rv. i. 124, 8. See Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 463; Rv. i. 62, 10; 64, 7, 71, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203767"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203767">↑</a> Rv. x. 108, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203767"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203767">↑</a> x. 85, 46. <i>Cf.</i> ix. 96, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203767"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203767">↑</a> iii. 37, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203767"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203767">↑</a> Av. i. 17, 1; Rv. i. 124, 7; iv. 5, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, iii. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203767"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203767">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 328.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203767"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203767">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Geldner, <i>Rigveda, Kommentar,</i> 22, 48, 49 (on Rv. iii. 31, 1 <i>et seq.</i>).</li></ol></div>

स्वस्रीय|స్వస్రీయ|ಸ್ವಸ್ರೀಯ|svasrīya|svasriiya|svasreeya|_vei_120@3768|_vei2_pn496
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3767">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3769">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn495">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn496">2:496</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn497">↷</a><hr><p><b>Svasrīya</b> occurs in the sense of ‘sister's son’ in the description of Viśvarūpa's ancestry in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203768"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203768">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203768"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203768">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 5, 1, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 4, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Delbrück, <i>Die indogermanischen Verwandtschaftsnamen,</i> 485.</li></ol></div>

स्वाती|स्वाति|స్వాతీ|స్వాతి|ಸ್ವಾತೀ|ಸ್ವಾತಿ|svātī|svāti|svaatii|svaati|svaatee|_vei_120@3769|_vei2_pn496
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3768">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3770">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn495">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn496">2:496</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn497">↷</a><hr><p><b>Svātī.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p></div>

स्वाध्याय|స్వాధ్యాయ|ಸ್ವಾಧ್ಯಾಯ|svādhyāya|svaadhyaaya|_vei_120@3770|_vei2_pn496
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3769">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3771">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn495">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn496">2:496</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn497">↷</a><hr><p><b>Svādhyāya</b> (‘reciting to oneself’) in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203770"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203770">1</a></sup> denotes the study or repetition of the Vedic texts. The Sūtras give rules for it in great detail. <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203770"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203770">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 3, 6; iv. 6, 9, 6; xi. 5, 6, 3; 7, 1, 4, 7; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 12, 1; 8, 15; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 1.</li></ol></div>

स्वायव|స్వాయవ|ಸ್ವಾಯವ|svāyava|svaayava|_vei_120@3771|_vei2_pn497
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3770">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3772">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn496">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn497">2:497</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn498">↷</a><hr><p><b>Svāyava,</b> ‘descendant of Svāyu,’ is the patronymic of <b>Kūśāmba <a class="vei_120a" href="लातव्य">lātavya</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a> (viii. 6, 8).</p></div>

स्वा-राज्य|स्वाराज्य|स्वा|राज्य|స్వా-రాజ్య|స్వారాజ్య|స్వా|రాజ్య|ಸ್ವಾ-ರಾಜ್ಯ|ಸ್ವಾರಾಜ್ಯ|ಸ್ವಾ|ರಾಜ್ಯ|svā-rājya|svārājya|svā|rājya|svaa-raajya|svaaraajya|svaa|raajya|_vei_120@3772|_vei2_pn497
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3771">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3773">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn496">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn497">2:497</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn498">↷</a><hr><p><b>Svā-rājya.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="राज्य">rājya</a>.</b></p></div>

स्वेद-ज|स्वेदज|स्वेद|స్వేద-జ|స్వేదజ|స్వేద|ಸ್ವೇದ-ಜ|ಸ್ವೇದಜ|ಸ್ವೇದ|sveda-ja|svedaja|sveda|_vei_120@3773|_vei2_pn497
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3772">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3774">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn496">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn497">2:497</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn498">↷</a><hr><p><b>Sveda-ja,</b> ‘born of sweat’--that is, ‘engendered by hot moisture’--is used in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iii. 3, 3) as a term designating a class of creatures comprising vermin of all sorts. The <a class="vei_120a" href="मानव">mānava</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="धर्म">dharma</a> Śāstra (i. 45) explains it as ‘flies, mosquitoes, lice, bugs, and so forth.’</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Keith, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka,</i> 235.</p></div>

स्वैदायन|స్వైదాయన|ಸ್ವೈದಾಯನ|svaidāyana|svaidaayana|_vei_120@3774|_vei2_pn497
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3773">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3775">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn496">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn497">2:497</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn498">↷</a><hr><p><b>Svaidāyana,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्वेद">sveda</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शौनक">śaunaka</a></b> in the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203774"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203774">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203774"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203774">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 4, 1, 2. 3; Gopatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 6.</li></ol></div>

स्वौपश|స్వౌపశ|ಸ್ವೌಪಶ|svaupaśa|svaupasha|_vei_120@3775|_vei2_pn497
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3774">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3776">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn496">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn497">2:497</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn498">↷</a><hr><p><b>Svaupaśa.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ओपश">opaśa</a>.</b></p></div>

हंस|హంస|ಹಂಸ|haṃsa|haMsa|_vei_120@3776|_vei2_pn497
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3775">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3777">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn496">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn497">2:497</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn498">↷</a><hr><p><b>Haṃsa</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203776">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203776">2</a></sup> denotes the ‘gander.’ These birds are described as dark in colour on the back (<i>nīlapṛṣṭha</i>);<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203776">3</a></sup> they fly in troops,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203776">4</a></sup> swim in the water (<i>uda-prut</i>),<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203776">5</a></sup> make loud noises,<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203776">6</a></sup> and are wakeful at night.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203776">7</a></sup> The <a class="vei_120a" href="हंस">haṃsa</a> is credited with the power of separating <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> from water (as later milk from water) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203776">8</a></sup> It is also mentioned as one of the victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’).<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203776">9</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203776">↑</a> i. 65, 5; 163, 10; ii. 34, 5; iii. 8, 9, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203776">↑</a> Av. vi. 12, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203776">↑</a> Rv. vii. 59, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203776">↑</a> Rv. iii. 8, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203776">↑</a> Rv. i. 65, 5; iii. 45, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203776">↑</a> Rv. iii. 53, 10.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203776">↑</a> Av. vi. 12, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203776">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxviii. 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 11, 6; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 74; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 6, 2, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203776"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203776">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 21, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 22. 35.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 89, 90; Lanman, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 19, 151; Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 150.</li></ol></div>

हंस-साचि|हंससाचि|हंस|साचि|హంస-సాచి|హంససాచి|హంస|సాచి|ಹಂಸ-ಸಾಚಿ|ಹಂಸಸಾಚಿ|ಹಂಸ|ಸಾಚಿ|haṃsa-sāci|haṃsasāci|haṃsa|sāci|haMsa-saachi|haMsasaachi|haMsa|saachi|_vei_120@3777|_vei2_pn498
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3776">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3778">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn497">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn498">2:498</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn499">↷</a><hr><p><b>Haṃsa-sāci</b> is the name of an unknown bird mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203777"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203777">1</a></sup> as one of the victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203777"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203777">↑</a> v. 5, 20, 1. <i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 93.</li></ol></div>

हय|హయ|ಹಯ|haya|_vei_120@3778|_vei2_pn498
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3777">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3779">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn497">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn498">2:498</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn499">↷</a><hr><p><b>Haya</b> denotes ‘horse’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203778"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203778">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203778"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203778">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203778"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203778">↑</a> v. 46, 1; vii. 74, 4; ix. 107, 25.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203778"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203778">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, vii. 47; xxii. 19, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 231.</li></ol></div>

हर-याण|हरयाण|हर|याण|హర-యాణ|హరయాణ|హర|యాణ|ಹರ-ಯಾಣ|ಹರಯಾಣ|ಹರ|ಯಾಣ|hara-yāṇa|harayāṇa|hara|yāṇa|hara-yaaNa|harayaaNa|yaaNa|_vei_120@3779|_vei2_pn498
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3778">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3780">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn497">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn498">2:498</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn499">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hara-yāṇa</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203779"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203779">1</a></sup> is clearly the name of a man mentioned along with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उक्षण्यायन">ukṣaṇyāyana</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुषामन्">suṣāman</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203779"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203779">↑</a> viii. 28, 22; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, v. 15. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 162.</li></ol></div>

हरिण|హరిణ|ಹರಿಣ|hariṇa|hariNa|_vei_120@3780|_vei2_pn498
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3779">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3781">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn497">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn498">2:498</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn499">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hariṇa</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203780">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203780">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘gazelle.’ It is at once a type of speed<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203780">3</a></sup> and terror.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203780">4</a></sup> Its horns are used as amulets.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203780">5</a></sup> It is fond of eating barley (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="यव">yava</a></b>).<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203780">6</a></sup> In the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203780">7</a></sup> it is said to kill vipers (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्वज">svaja</a></b>). <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुलुंग">kuluṅga</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="न्यंकु">nyaṅku</a>.</b> The feminine is Hariṇī.<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203780">8</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203780">↑</a> i. 163, 1; v. 78, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203780">↑</a> Av. vi. 67, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203780">↑</a> Av. iii. 7, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203780">↑</a> Av. vi. 67, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203780">↑</a> Av. iii. 7, 1. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203780">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 4, 19, 2 (<i>hariṇī</i>); Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiii. 30; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 13, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a>, iv. 8 (also <i>hariṇī</i>); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 9, 7, 2 (<i>hariṇī</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203780">↑</a> iii. 9, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203780"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203780">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 4, 19, 2, and see n. 6.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 83; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 336, 337.</li></ol></div>

हरित|हरित|హరిత_౧|హరిత|ಹರಿತ_೧|ಹರಿತ|harita_1|harita|_vei_120@3781|_vei2_pn498
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3780">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3782">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn497">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn498">2:498</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn499">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Harita</b> seems to mean ‘gold’ in a few passages of the Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203781"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203781">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203781"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203781">↑</a> Av. v. 28, 5. 9; xi. 3, 8; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, viii. 5.</li></ol></div>

हरित कश्यप|हरित कश्यप|హరిత కశ్యప_౨|హరిత కశ్యప|ಹರಿತ ಕಶ್ಯಪ_೨|ಹರಿತ ಕಶ್ಯಪ|harita kaśyapa_2|harita kaśyapa|harita kashyapa_2|harita kashyapa|_vei_120@3782|_vei2_pn498
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3781">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3783">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn497">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn498">2:498</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn499">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Harita Kaśyapa</b> is mentioned as a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शिल्प कश्यप">śilpa kaśyapa</a>,</b> in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203782"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203782">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203782"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203782">↑</a> vi. 4, 33 (Mādhyaṃdina = vi. 5, 3 <a class="vei_120a" href="काण्व">kāṇva</a>).</li></ol></div>

हरि-द्रु|हरिद्रु|हरि|द्रु|హరి-ద్రు|హరిద్రు|హరి|ద్రు|ಹರಿ-ದ್ರು|ಹರಿದ್ರು|ಹರಿ|ದ್ರು|hari-dru|haridru|hari|dru|_vei_120@3783|_vei2_pn499
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3782">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3784">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn498">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn499">2:499</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn500">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hari-dru</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 8, 1, 16) is the name of a tree (<i>Pinus deodora</i>).</p></div>

हरिमन्|హరిమన్|ಹರಿಮನ್|hariman|_vei_120@3784|_vei2_pn499
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3783">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3785">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn498">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn499">2:499</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn500">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hariman</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203784">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203784">2</a></sup> denotes ‘yellowness’ as a disease, ‘jaundice.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203784">↑</a> i. 50, 11 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203784"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203784">↑</a> i. 22, 1; ix. 8, 9; xix. 44, 2.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 378, 388.</li></ol></div>

हरि-यूपीया|हरियूपीया|हरि|यूपीया|హరి-యూపీయా|హరియూపీయా|హరి|యూపీయా|ಹರಿ-ಯೂಪೀಯಾ|ಹರಿಯೂಪೀಯಾ|ಹರಿ|ಯೂಪೀಯಾ|hari-yūpīyā|hariyūpīyā|hari|yūpīyā|hari-yuupiiyaa|hariyuupiiyaa|yuupiiyaa|hari-yoopeeyaa|hariyoopeeyaa|yoopeeyaa|_vei_120@3785|_vei2_pn499
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3784">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3786">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn498">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn499">2:499</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn500">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hari-yūpīyā</b> is mentioned in a hymn of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203785"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203785">1</a></sup> as the scene of the defeat of the <b>Vṛcīvants</b> by <b>Abhyāvartin Cāyamana.</b> It may denote either a place or a river, since many battles seem to have been fought on the banks of rivers. Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203785"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203785">2</a></sup> took it as the name of a town on the river <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यव्यावती">yavyāvatī</a>,</b> which is identified with it in Sāyaṇa's commentary on the passage. Hillebrandt<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203785"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203785">3</a></sup> thinks that it is the river Iryāb (Haliāb), a tributary of the Kurum (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="क्रुमु">krumu</a></b>), but this is not at all probable.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203785"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203785">↑</a> vi. 27, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203785"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203785">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 158.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203785"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203785">↑</a> <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 268, n. 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 18, 19; Kaegi, <i>Rigveda,</i> n. 328.</li></ol></div>

हरि-वर्ण अंगिरस|हरिवर्ण अंगिरस|हरि|वर्ण अंगिरस|हरि-वर्ण अङ्गिरस|हरिवर्ण अङ्गिरस|वर्ण अङ्गिरस|హరి-వర్ణ అంగిరస|హరివర్ణ అంగిరస|హరి|వర్ణ అంగిరస|హరి-వర్ణ అఙ్గిరస|హరివర్ణ అఙ్గిరస|వర్ణ అఙ్గిరస|ಹರಿ-ವರ್ಣ ಅಂಗಿರಸ|ಹರಿವರ್ಣ ಅಂಗಿರಸ|ಹರಿ|ವರ್ಣ ಅಂಗಿರಸ|ಹರಿ-ವರ್ಣ ಅಙ್ಗಿರಸ|ಹರಿವರ್ಣ ಅಙ್ಗಿರಸ|ವರ್ಣ ಅಙ್ಗಿರಸ|hari-varṇa aṅgirasa|harivarṇa aṅgirasa|hari|varṇa aṅgirasa|hari-varNa aMgirasa|harivarNa aMgirasa|varNa aMgirasa|hari-varNa angirasa|harivarNa angirasa|varNa angirasa|_vei_120@3786|_vei2_pn499
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3785">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3787">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn498">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn499">2:499</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn500">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hari-<a class="vei_120a" href="वर्ण">varṇa</a> Aṅgirasa</b> (‘descendant of Aṅgiras’) is the name of the seer of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> or chant in the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203786"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203786">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203786"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203786">↑</a> viii. 9, 4. 5. <i>Cf.</i> Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 63.</li></ol></div>

हरि-श्चन्द्र वैधस|हरिश्चन्द्र वैधस|हरि|श्चन्द्र वैधस|హరి-శ్చన్ద్ర వైధస|హరిశ్చన్ద్ర వైధస|హరి|శ్చన్ద్ర వైధస|ಹರಿ-ಶ್ಚನ್ದ್ರ ವೈಧಸ|ಹರಿಶ್ಚನ್ದ್ರ ವೈಧಸ|ಹರಿ|ಶ್ಚನ್ದ್ರ ವೈಧಸ|hari-ścandra vaidhasa|hariścandra vaidhasa|hari|ścandra vaidhasa|hari-shchandra vaidhasa|harishchandra vaidhasa|shchandra vaidhasa|_vei_120@3787|_vei2_pn499
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3786">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3788">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn498">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn499">2:499</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn500">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hari-ścandra Vaidhasa</b> (‘descendant of Vedhas’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऐक्ष्वाक">aikṣvāka</a></b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="इक्ष्वाकु">ikṣvāku</a></b>’) is the name of a probably mythical king whose rash vow to offer up his son <b><a class="vei_120a" href="रोहित">rohita</a></b> to <a class="vei_120a" href="वरुण">varuṇa</a> is the source of the tale of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शुनःशेप">śunaḥśepa</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Brāhmana (vii. 14, 2) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (xv. 17).</p></div>

हर्म्य|హర్మ్య|ಹರ್ಮ್ಯ|harmya|_vei_120@3788|_vei2_pn499
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3787">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3789">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn498">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn499">2:499</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn500">↷</a><hr><p><b>Harmya</b> denotes the Vedic ‘house’ as a unity including the stabling<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203788"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203788">1</a></sup> and so forth, and surrounded by a fence or wall of  some sort.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203788"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203788">2</a></sup> It is several times referred to in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203788"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203788">3</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203788"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203788">4</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गृह">gṛha</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203788"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203788">↑</a> Rv. vii. 56, 16. <i>Cf.</i> x. 106, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203788"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203788">↑</a> Rv. vii. 55, 6. Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 278, n. 2, takes <i>harmyeṣṭhāḥ,</i> ‘standing on a house’ (Rv. vii. 56, 16), to refer to princes on the roof of a palace.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203788"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203788">↑</a> i. 121, 1 (the people, <i>viśaḥ,</i> of the house); 166, 4; ix. 71, 4; 78, 3; x. 43, 3; 73, 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203788"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203788">↑</a> Av. xviii. 4, 55 (a palace of <a class="vei_120a" href="यम">yama</a>); <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 7, 6, 3, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 149.</li></ol></div>

हलिक्ष्ण|హలిక్ష్ణ|ಹಲಿಕ್ಷ್ಣ|halikṣṇa|halikShNa|halixNa|_vei_120@3789|_vei2_pn500
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3788">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3790">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn499">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn500">2:500</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn501">↷</a><hr><p><b>Halikṣṇa</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203789"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203789">1</a></sup> or <b>Halīkṣṇa</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203789"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203789">2</a></sup> is mentioned as one of the victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās. The commentator Mahīdhara<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203789"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203789">3</a></sup> thinks that it is a kind of lion, Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203789"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203789">4</a></sup> that a green Caṭaka bird or a lion (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="तृण">tṛṇa</a>-hiṃsa</i>)<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203789"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203789">5</a></sup> is meant. In the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203789"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203789">6</a></sup> Halīkṣṇa seems to be some particular intestine, but Weber<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203789"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203789">7</a></sup> thinks it may mean ‘gall.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203789"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203789">↑</a> Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 12; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 31.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203789"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203789">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 12, 1; 7, 23, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203789"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203789">↑</a> On Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203789"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203789">↑</a> On <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203789"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203789">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="तृण">tṛṇa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="सिंह">siṃha</a></i> is not much more intelligible.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203789"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203789">↑</a> ii. 33, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203789"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203789">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 13, 206.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 79.</li></ol></div>

हविर्-धान|हविर्धान|हविर्|धान|హవిర్-ధాన|హవిర్ధాన|హవిర్|ధాన|ಹವಿರ್-ಧಾನ|ಹವಿರ್ಧಾನ|ಹವಿರ್|ಧಾನ|havir-dhāna|havirdhāna|havir|dhāna|havir-dhaana|havirdhaana|dhaana|_vei_120@3790|_vei2_pn500
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3789">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3791">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn499">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn500">2:500</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn501">↷</a><hr><p><b>Havir-dhāna</b> (‘oblation receptacle’) denotes primarily the cart on which the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> plants are conveyed to be pressed,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203790">1</a></sup> then the shed in which these <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> vehicles were kept.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203790">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203790">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 1, 3, 1; vi. 2, 9, 1. 4, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203790"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203790">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 11, 1. 4, etc.</p><p>See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गृह">gṛha</a>;</b> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 154.</li></ol></div>

हविष्-कृत् आंगिरस|हविष्कृत् आंगिरस|हविष्|कृत् आंगिरस|हविष्-कृत् आङ्गिरस|हविष्कृत् आङ्गिरस|कृत् आङ्गिरस|హవిష్-కృత్ ఆంగిరస|హవిష్కృత్ ఆంగిరస|హవిష్|కృత్ ఆంగిరస|హవిష్-కృత్ ఆఙ్గిరస|హవిష్కృత్ ఆఙ్గిరస|కృత్ ఆఙ్గిరస|ಹವಿಷ್-ಕೃತ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಹವಿಷ್ಕೃತ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಹವಿಷ್|ಕೃತ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಹವಿಷ್-ಕೃತ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|ಹವಿಷ್ಕೃತ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|ಕೃತ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|haviṣ-kṛt āṅgirasa|haviṣkṛt āṅgirasa|haviṣ|kṛt āṅgirasa|haviSh-kRt aaMgirasa|haviShkRt aaMgirasa|haviSh|kRt aaMgirasa|haviSh-kRt aangirasa|haviShkRt aangirasa|kRt aangirasa|haviSh-kRit aaMgirasa|haviShkRit aaMgirasa|kRit aaMgirasa|haviSh-kRut aaMgirasa|haviShkRut aaMgirasa|kRut aaMgirasa|haviSh-kRRit aaMgirasa|haviShkRRit aaMgirasa|kRRit aaMgirasa|haviSh-kRit aangirasa|haviShkRit aangirasa|kRit aangirasa|haviSh-kRut aangirasa|haviShkRut aangirasa|kRut aangirasa|haviSh-kRRit aangirasa|haviShkRRit aangirasa|kRRit aangirasa|_vei_120@3791|_vei2_pn500
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3790">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3792">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn499">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn500">2:500</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn501">↷</a><hr><p><b>Haviṣ-<a class="vei_120a" href="कृत्">kṛt</a> Āṅgirasa</b> (‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अंगिरस्">aṅgiras</a></b>’) is the name of the seer of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> or chant according to the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203791">1</a></sup> and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203791">2</a></sup> See the following.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203791">↑</a> xi. 10, 9. 10; xx. 11, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203791"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203791">↑</a> vii. 1, 4, 1.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 2, 160; Hopkins, <i>Transactions of the Connecticut Academy of Arts and Sciences,</i> 15, 62.</li></ol></div>

हविष्मन्त् आंगिरस|हविष्मन्त् आङ्गिरस|హవిష్మన్త్ ఆంగిరస|హవిష్మన్త్ ఆఙ్గిరస|ಹವಿಷ್ಮನ್ತ್ ಆಂಗಿರಸ|ಹವಿಷ್ಮನ್ತ್ ಆಙ್ಗಿರಸ|haviṣmant āṅgirasa|haviShmant aaMgirasa|haviShmant aangirasa|_vei_120@3792|_vei2_pn500
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3791">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3793">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn499">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn500">2:500</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn501">↷</a><hr><p><b>Haviṣmant Āṅgirasa</b> is mentioned along with <b>Haviṣkṛt,</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā and the <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203792">2</a></sup> as the seer of a <a class="vei_120a" href="सामन्">sāman</a> or chant.</p><p>^1) vii. 1, 4, 1. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="2"><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203792"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203792">↑</a> xi. 10, 9. 10; xx. 11, 3.</li></ol></div>

हविस्|हवि|హవిస్|హవి|ಹವಿಸ್|ಹವಿ|havis|havi|_vei_120@3793|_vei2_pn501
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3792">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3794">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn500">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn501">2:501</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn502">↷</a><hr><p><b>Havis</b> is the general term for an offering to the gods, ‘oblation,’ whether of grain, or <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>, or milk, or clarified butter, etc. It is common from the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203793"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203793">1</a></sup> onwards.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203793"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203793">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203793"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203793">↑</a> i. 24, 11; 26, 6; 170, 5, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203793"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203793">↑</a> Av. iii. 10, 5; vi. 5, 3, etc.</li></ol></div>

हस्त|హస్త|ಹಸ್ತ|hasta|_vei_120@3794|_vei2_pn501
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3793">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3795">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn500">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn501">2:501</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn502">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hasta.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="नक्षत्र">nakṣatra</a>.</b></p></div>

हस्त-घ्न|हस्तघ्न|हस्त|घ्न|హస్త-ఘ్న|హస్తఘ్న|హస్త|ఘ్న|ಹಸ್ತ-ಘ್ನ|ಹಸ್ತಘ್ನ|ಹಸ್ತ|ಘ್ನ|hasta-ghna|hastaghna|hasta|ghna|_vei_120@3795|_vei2_pn501
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3794">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3796">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn500">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn501">2:501</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn502">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hasta-ghna</b> denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203795">1</a></sup> a ‘hand-guard,’ a covering used as a protection of the hand and arm from the impact of the bowstring. The word is of remarkable and still unexplained formation.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203795">2</a></sup> Lāṭyāyana<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203795">3</a></sup> has <i><a class="vei_120a" href="हस्त">hasta</a>-tra</i> and the Epic <i>hastāvāpa</i><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203795">4</a></sup> as its equivalent in sense.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203795">↑</a> vi. 75, 14; <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>, ix. 14. The reading is assured by the parallels in the Saṃhitās: <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 6, 6, 5; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 16, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxix. 51.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203795">↑</a> Pischel, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 296; Oldenberg, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>-Noten,</i> 1, 416.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203795">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 10, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203795"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203795">↑</a> Hopkins, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 13, 308.</li></ol></div>

हस्तादान|హస్తాదాన|ಹಸ್ತಾದಾನ|hastādāna|hastaadaana|_vei_120@3796|_vei2_pn501
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3795">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3797">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn500">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn501">2:501</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn502">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hastādāna.</b> See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पशु">paśu</a>.</b></p></div>

हस्तिन्|హస్తిన్|ಹಸ್ತಿನ್|hastin|_vei_120@3797|_vei2_pn501
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3796">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3798">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn500">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn501">2:501</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn502">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hastin,</b> ‘having a hand,’ with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मृग">mṛga</a>,</b> ‘beast,’ denotes in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203797">1</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203797">2</a></sup> the ‘elephant.’ Later the adjective alone comes to mean ‘elephant.’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203797">3</a></sup> The animal was famed for its strength<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203797">4</a></sup> as well as its virility.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203797">5</a></sup> It is mentioned with man and monkey as one of the beasts that take hold by the hand (<i><a class="vei_120a" href="हस्तादान">hastādāna</a></i>), as opposed to those that take hold by the mouth (<i>mukhādāna</i>).<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203797">6</a></sup> It was tamed, as the expression <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हस्तिप">hastipa</a>,</b> ‘elephant-keeper,’ shows, and tame elephants were used to catch others (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वारण">vāraṇa</a></b>). But there is no trace of its use in war, though Ktesias and Megasthenes both record such use for  their times.<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203797">7</a></sup> The Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203797">8</a></sup> alludes to its being pestered by mosquitoes.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203797">↑</a> i. 64, 7; iv. 16, 14.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203797">↑</a> xii. 1, 25. Elsewhere <a class="vei_120a" href="हस्तिन्">hastin</a> is used alone: iii. 22, 3; iv. 36, 9; vi. 38, 2; 70, 2; xix. 1, 32.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203797">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 11, 1; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 14, 8; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 29; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 8, 8; xxiii. 13, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 14; v. 31, 2; vi. 27, 2; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 3, 4, etc.; Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, vii. 24, 2 (coupled with gold), etc.; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 22, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203797">↑</a> Rv. <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.;</i> Av ii. 22, 1, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203797">↑</a> Av. iii. 22, 6; vi. 70, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203797">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 4, 5, 7; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv, 5, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203797">↑</a> Von Schroeder, <i>Indiens Literatur und Cidtur,</i> 434.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203797"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203797">↑</a> Av. iv. 36, 9.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 80.</li></ol></div>

हस्ति-प|हस्तिप|हस्ति|హస్తి-ప|హస్తిప|హస్తి|ಹಸ್ತಿ-ಪ|ಹಸ್ತಿಪ|ಹಸ್ತಿ|hasti-pa|hastipa|hasti|_vei_120@3798|_vei2_pn502
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3797">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3799">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn501">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn502">2:502</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn503">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hasti-pa,</b> ‘elephant-keeper,’ is mentioned as one of the victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203798">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203798"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203798">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 11; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 9, 1.</li></ol></div>

हस्रा|హస్రా|ಹಸ್ರಾ|hasrā|hasraa|_vei_120@3799|_vei2_pn502
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3798">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3800">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn501">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn502">2:502</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn503">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hasrā</b> (‘laughing woman’) in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203799"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203799">1</a></sup> denotes a courtesan according to Pischel.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203799"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203799">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203799"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203799">↑</a> i. 124, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203799"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203799">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 196, 308.</li></ol></div>

हायन|హాయన|ಹಾಯನ|hāyana|haayana|_vei_120@3800|_vei2_pn502
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3799">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3801">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn501">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn502">2:502</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn503">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hāyana</b> denotes a ‘year,’ usually in compounds.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203800"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203800">1</a></sup> In the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203800"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203800">2</a></sup> and the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203800"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203800">3</a></sup> the term appears as a designation of a species of red rice. As an adjective in the sense of ‘lasting a year’ or ‘recurring every year,’ it is applied to fever in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203800"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203800">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203800"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203800">↑</a> Av. viii. 2, 21; <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शत">śata</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="हायन">hāyana</a>,</i> ‘a hundred years old,’ viii. 2, 8; 7, 22; <i>hāyanī,</i> xii. 1, 36 (probably corrupt).</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203800"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203800">↑</a> xv. 5.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203800"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203800">↑</a> v. 3, 3, 6 (the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 8, 10, 1, has instead <i><a class="vei_120a" href="महा">mahā</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="व्रीहि">vrīhi</a>,</i> ‘great rice’).</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203800"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203800">↑</a> xix. 39, 10.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Schrader, <i>Prehistoric Antiquities,</i> 301.</li></ol></div>

हारिकर्णी-पुत्र|हारिकर्णीपुत्र|हारिकर्णी|हारिकर्णि|पुत्र|హారికర్ణీ-పుత్ర|హారికర్ణీపుత్ర|హారికర్ణీ|హారికర్ణి|పుత్ర|ಹಾರಿಕರ್ಣೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಹಾರಿಕರ್ಣೀಪುತ್ರ|ಹಾರಿಕರ್ಣೀ|ಹಾರಿಕರ್ಣಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|hārikarṇī-putra|hārikarṇīputra|hārikarṇī|hārikarṇi|putra|haarikarNii-putra|haarikarNiiputra|haarikarNii|haarikarNi|haarikarNee-putra|haarikarNeeputra|haarikarNee|_vei_120@3801|_vei2_pn502
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3800">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3802">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn501">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn502">2:502</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn503">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hārikarṇī-putra,</b> ‘son of a female descendant of Harikarṇa,’ is the name of a teacher, a pupil of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भारद्वाजी-पुत्र">bhāradvājī-putra</a>,</b> in the last <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) in the Mādhyaṃdina recension of the Brhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 4, 30).</p></div>

हारिद्रव|హారిద్రవ|ಹಾರಿದ್ರವ|hāridrava|haaridrava|_vei_120@3802|_vei2_pn502
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3801">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3803">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn501">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn502">2:502</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn503">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hāridrava</b> is the name of a yellow bird in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203802"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203802">1</a></sup> perhaps the ‘yellow water-wagtail.’<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203802"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203802">2</a></sup> Geldner<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203802"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203802">3</a></sup> compares the Greek  </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203802"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203802">↑</a> i. 50, 12; viii. 35, 7.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203802"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203802">↑</a> Sāyaṇa on Rv. i. 50, 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 7, 6, 2, treats it as the name of a plant (<i>cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 62). But on Av. i. 22, 4, he takes it as <i>gopītanaka,</i> ‘wagtail.’</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203802"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203802">↑</a> <i>Rigveda, Glossar,</i> 213.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 264, n. 1; 266; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 23.</li></ol></div>

हारिद्रविक|హారిద్రవిక|ಹಾರಿದ್ರವಿಕ|hāridravika|haaridravika|_vei_120@3803|_vei2_pn503
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3802">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3804">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn502">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn503">2:503</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn504">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hāridravika</b> is the title of a work by Hāridravin mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203803"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203803">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203803"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203803">↑</a> ix. 5. See Roth, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>,</i> xxiii; von Schroeder, Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, 1, xiii.</li></ol></div>

हारि-द्रुमत|हारिद्रुमत|हारि|द्रुमत|హారి-ద్రుమత|హారిద్రుమత|హారి|ద్రుమత|ಹಾರಿ-ದ್ರುಮತ|ಹಾರಿದ್ರುಮತ|ಹಾರಿ|ದ್ರುಮತ|hāri-drumata|hāridrumata|hāri|drumata|haari-drumata|haaridrumata|haari|_vei_120@3804|_vei2_pn503
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3803">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3805">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn502">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn503">2:503</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn504">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hāri-drumata,</b> ‘descendant of Haridrumant,’ is the patronymic of a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गौतम">gautama</a></b> in the Chāndogya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (iv. 4, 3).</p></div>

हालिंगव|हालिङ्गव|హాలింగవ|హాలిఙ్గవ|ಹಾಲಿಂಗವ|ಹಾಲಿಙ್ಗವ|hāliṅgava|haaliMgava|haalingava|_vei_120@3805|_vei2_pn503
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3804">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3806">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn502">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn503">2:503</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn504">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hāliṅgava,</b> ‘descendant of Haliṅgu,’ is the name of a teacher in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (x. 4, 5, 1).</p></div>

हिता|హితా|ಹಿತಾ|hitā|hitaa|_vei_120@3806|_vei2_pn503
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3805">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3807">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn502">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn503">2:503</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn504">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hitā</b> in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203806">1</a></sup> is the name of certain ‘veins.’ <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरा">hirā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203806"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203806">↑</a> Bṛhadāraṇyaka <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, ii. 1, 21; iv. 2, 4; 3, 20; <a class="vei_120a" href="कौषीतकि">kauṣītaki</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 19.</li></ol></div>

हिम|హిమ|ಹಿಮ|hima|_vei_120@3807|_vei2_pn503
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3806">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3808">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn502">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn503">2:503</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn504">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hima,</b> denoting ‘cold,’ ‘cold weather,’ is quite common in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203807">1</a></sup> but less frequent later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203807">2</a></sup> As ‘snow’ the word appears as a masculine in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203807">3</a></sup> and often later as a neuter.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203807">4</a></sup> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हेमन्त">hemanta</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203807">↑</a> i. 116, 8; 119, 6; viii. 32, 26, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203807">↑</a> Av. vii. 18, 2; xiii. 1, 46; xix. 49, 5 (night as mother of coolness), etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203807">↑</a> iii. 12, 7, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203807"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203807">↑</a> Saḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 9, etc.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 3, 192-195.</li></ol></div>

हिमवन्त्|హిమవన్త్|ಹಿಮವನ್ತ್|himavant|_vei_120@3808|_vei2_pn503
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3807">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3809">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn502">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn503">2:503</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn504">↷</a><hr><p><b>Himavant,</b> ‘snowy,’ appears as an epithet of mountains in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203808"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203808">1</a></sup> It is also used both there<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203808"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203808">2</a></sup> and in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203808"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203808">3</a></sup> as well as later,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203808"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203808">4</a></sup> as a noun. There seems no reasen to deny that in all the passages the word refers vaguely to the mountains now called Himālaya, though it is possible that the name may include mountains not strictly in that system, like the Suleiman hills.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203808"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203808">5</a></sup> See also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मूजवन्त्">mūjavant</a></b> and <b>Trikakubh.</b> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203808"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203808">↑</a> xii. 1, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203808"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203808">↑</a> vi. 95, 3. See also iv. 9, 9; v. 4, 2. 8; 25. 7; vi. 24, 1 (where reference is made to the rivers of the Himālayas); xix. 39, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203808"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203808">↑</a> x. 121, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203808"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203808">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 5, 11, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 30; xxv. 12; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, viii. 14, 3 (the <b>Uttara Kurus</b> and <b>Uttara Madras</b> live beyond it; perhaps in Kaśmīr), etc.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203808"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203808">↑</a> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 198.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leven,</i> 29; Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 18, 12, who inclines to see a reminiscence of the Caucasus.</li></ol></div>

हिमा|హిమా|ಹಿಮಾ|himā|himaa|_vei_120@3809|_vei2_pn504
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3808">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3810">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn503">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn504">2:504</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn505">↷</a><hr><p><b>Himā</b> denotes ‘winter’ in the combination a ‘hundred winters’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203809"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203809">1</a></sup> and elsewhere.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203809"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203809">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203809"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203809">↑</a> i. 64, 14; ii. 33, 2; v. 54, 15; vi. 48, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203809"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203809">↑</a> Av. ii. 28, 4; xii. 2, 28; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 6, 6, 3; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, ii. 27.</li></ol></div>

हिरणिन्|హిరణిన్|ಹಿರಣಿನ್|hiraṇin|hiraNin|_vei_120@3810|_vei2_pn504
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3809">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3811">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn503">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn504">2:504</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn505">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hiraṇin,</b> ‘rich in gold,’ is apparently an epithet of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="त्रसदस्यु">trasadasyu</a></b> in one verse of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203810"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203810">1</a></sup> referring to the golden raiment or possessions of the king. Ludwig,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203810"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203810">2</a></sup> however, thinks the word is a proper name, possibly of Trasadasyu's son.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203810"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203810">↑</a> v. 53, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203810"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203810">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 155.</li></ol></div>

हिरणिन|హిరణిన|ಹಿರಣಿನ|hiraṇina|hiraNina|_vei_120@3811|_vei2_pn504
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3810">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3812">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn503">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn504">2:504</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn505">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hiraṇina</b> is found in one passage of the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203811"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203811">1</a></sup> where Ludwig<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203811"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203811">2</a></sup> takes the word as a name of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्ड">śāṇḍa</a>.</b> But it seems to be an adjectival form of <i><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरणिन्">hiraṇin</a>,</i> ‘golden.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203811"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203811">↑</a> vi. 63, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203811"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203811">↑</a> Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 158.</li></ol></div>

हिरण्य|హిరణ్య|ಹಿರಣ್ಯ|hiraṇya|hiraNya|_vei_120@3812|_vei2_pn504
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3811">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3813">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn503">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn504">2:504</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn505">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hiraṇya</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203812">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203812">2</a></sup> denotes ‘gold.’ It is hardly possible to exaggerate the value attached to gold by the Vedic Indians. The metal was, it is clear, won from the bed of rivers. Hence the Indus is called ‘golden’<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203812">3</a></sup> and ‘of golden stream.’<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203812">4</a></sup> Apparently the extraction of gold from the earth was known,<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203812">5</a></sup> and washing for gold is also recorded.<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203812">6</a></sup></p><p>Gold is the object of the wishes of the Vedic singer,<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203812">7</a></sup> and golden treasures (<i>hiraṇyāni</i>) are mentioned as given by patrons<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203812">8</a></sup> along with cows and horses. Gold was used for ornaments for neck and breast (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="निष्क">niṣka</a></b>), for ear-rings (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="कर्ण-शोभन">karṇa-śobhana</a></b>), and even for cups.<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203812">9</a></sup> Gold is always associated with the gods.<sup id="ref_120_10_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_10_vei_1203812">10</a></sup> </p><p>In the plural <a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्य">hiraṇya</a> denotes ‘ornaments of gold.’<sup id="ref_120_11_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_11_vei_1203812">11</a></sup></p><p>A gold currency was evidently beginning to be known in so far as definite weights of gold are mentioned: thus a weight, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अष्टा">aṣṭā</a>-prūḍ,</i> occurs in the Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_12_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_12_vei_1203812">12</a></sup> and the golden <i><a class="vei_120a" href="शतमान">śatamāna</a>,</i> ‘weight of a hundred (<b>Kṛṣṇalas</b>)’ is found in the same texts.<sup id="ref_120_13_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_13_vei_1203812">13</a></sup> In several passages,<sup id="ref_120_14_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_14_vei_1203812">14</a></sup> moreover, <i><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्य">hiraṇya</a></i> or <i>hiraṇyāni</i> may mean ‘pieces of gold.’</p><p>Gold is described sometimes as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="हरित">harita</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_15_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_15_vei_1203812">15</a></sup> ‘yellowish,’ sometimes as <i><a class="vei_120a" href="रजत">rajata</a>,</i><sup id="ref_120_16_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_16_vei_1203812">16</a></sup> ‘whitish,’ when probably. ‘silver’ is alluded to. It was obtained from the ore by smelting.<sup id="ref_120_17_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_17_vei_1203812">17</a></sup> Megasthenes<sup id="ref_120_18_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_18_vei_1203812">18</a></sup> bears testimony to the richness in gold of India in his time.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203812">↑</a> i. 43, 5; iii. 34, 9; iv. 10, 6; 17, 11, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203812">↑</a> Av. i. 9, 2; ii. 36, 7; v. 28, 6; vi. 38, 2, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203812">↑</a> Rv. x. 75, 8.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203812">↑</a> Rv. vi. 61, 7; viii. 26, 18.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203812">↑</a> Rv. i. 117, 5; Av. xii. 1, 6. 26. 44.</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203812">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 1, 7, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 1, 1, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203812">↑</a> Rv. vi. 47, 23; viii. 78, 9; Pischel and Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, xxiv.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203812">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> also <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्यस्तूप">hiraṇyastūpa</a></b> as a proper name.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203812">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 7, 1, 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 3, 3, 7; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 1, 2, 19; 5, 28.</li><li id="fx_120_10_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_10_vei_1203812">↑</a> All that is connected with them is of gold; the horses of the sun are <i><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्य">hiraṇya</a>-tvacas,</i> ‘gold-skinned’ (Av. xiii. 2, 8), and so on.</li><li id="fx_120_11_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_11_vei_1203812">↑</a> Rv. i. 122, 2; 162, 16; ii. 33, 9; v. 60, 4; Av. iv. 10, 6; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xv. 50; xx. 37; also in the singular, Av. i. 35, 1; xviii. 4, 56.</li><li id="fx_120_12_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_12_vei_1203812">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iii. 4, 1, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xi. 1; xiii. 10; von Schroeder, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 49, 164.</li><li id="fx_120_13_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_13_vei_1203812">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 5, 5, 16; xii. 7, 2, 13; 9, 1, 4. <i>Cf.</i> xiii. 1, 1, 4; 2, 3, 2; 4, 1, 13; 2, 7. 13; xiv. 3, 1, 32; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 3, 11, 5; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, viii. 5; xxii. 8; Weber, <i>Indische Streifen,</i> 1, 101. Geldner, <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 1, 268, is inclined to think that a gold unit is alluded to in the vague phrases ‘thousands,’ etc., of the Rigveda. See viii. 1, 13; 65, 12; x. 95, 3, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_14_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_14_vei_1203812">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 7, 4; iii. 8, 2, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 7, 1, 7; xiii. 4, 1, 6, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_15_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_15_vei_1203812">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, x. 4; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 4, 4, 6; Ṣaḍviṃśa <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 9.</li><li id="fx_120_16_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_16_vei_1203812">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, i. 5, 1, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xii. 4, 4, 7; xiii. 4, 2, 10, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_17_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_17_vei_1203812">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vi. 1, 3, 5. <i>Cf.</i> ii. 2, 3, 28; xii. 4, 3, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xvii. 6, 4 (<i>niṣ-ṭap,</i> ‘heat’); Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 10 (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 16, 234, ccxliii); Lāṭyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iii. 1, 9, etc.; Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 34, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_18_vei_1203812"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_18_vei_1203812">↑</a> See Diodorus Siculus, ii. 36; Strabo, pp. 703, 711.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 49-51; Macdonell, <i>Sanskrit Literature,</i> 151.</li></ol></div>

हिरण्य-कशिपु|हिरण्यकशिपु|हिरण्य|कशिपु|హిరణ్య-కశిపు|హిరణ్యకశిపు|హిరణ్య|కశిపు|ಹಿರಣ್ಯ-ಕಶಿಪು|ಹಿರಣ್ಯಕಶಿಪು|ಹಿರಣ್ಯ|ಕಶಿಪು|hiraṇya-kaśipu|hiraṇyakaśipu|hiraṇya|kaśipu|hiraNya-kashipu|hiraNyakashipu|hiraNya|kashipu|_vei_120@3813|_vei2_pn505
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3812">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3814">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn504">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn505">2:505</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn506">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hiraṇya-kaśipu</b> in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203813"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203813">1</a></sup> denotes a ‘golden seat,’ probably one covered with cloth of gold.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203813"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203813">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 9, 20, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, vii. 18, 12. <i>Cf.</i> the word as an adjective with the sense of ‘having a golden cloth,’ Av. v. 7, 10.</li></ol></div>

हिरण्य-कार|हिरण्यकार|हिरण्य|कार|హిరణ్య-కార|హిరణ్యకార|హిరణ్య|కార|ಹಿರಣ್ಯ-ಕಾರ|ಹಿರಣ್ಯಕಾರ|ಹಿರಣ್ಯ|ಕಾರ|hiraṇya-kāra|hiraṇyakāra|hiraṇya|kāra|hiraNya-kaara|hiraNyakaara|hiraNya|kaara|_vei_120@3814|_vei2_pn505
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3813">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3815">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn504">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn505">2:505</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn506">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hiraṇya-kāra</b> denotes a ‘worker in gold’ mentioned in the list of victims at the Puruṣamedha (‘human sacrifice’) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203814"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203814">1</a></sup> </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203814"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203814">↑</a> Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxx. 17; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 14, 1.</li></ol></div>

हिरण्य-दन्त्|हिरण्यदन्त्|हिरण्य|दन्त्|హిరణ్య-దన్త్|హిరణ్యదన్త్|హిరణ్య|దన్త్|ಹಿರಣ್ಯ-ದನ್ತ್|ಹಿರಣ್ಯದನ್ತ್|ಹಿರಣ್ಯ|ದನ್ತ್|hiraṇya-dant|hiraṇyadant|hiraṇya|dant|hiraNya-dant|hiraNyadant|hiraNya|_vei_120@3815|_vei2_pn506
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3814">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3816">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn505">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn506">2:506</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn507">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hiraṇya-dant</b> (‘gold-toothed’) <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वैद">vaida</a></b> (‘descendant of Veda’) is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 6, 3) and the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka (ii. 1, 5). The name presumably refers to the use of gold to stop the teeth; see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दन्त्">dant</a>.</b></p></div>

हिरण्य-नाभ|हिरण्यनाभ|हिरण्य|नाभ|హిరణ్య-నాభ|హిరణ్యనాభ|హిరణ్య|నాభ|ಹಿರಣ್ಯ-ನಾಭ|ಹಿರಣ್ಯನಾಭ|ಹಿರಣ್ಯ|ನಾಭ|hiraṇya-nābha|hiraṇyanābha|hiraṇya|nābha|hiraNya-naabha|hiraNyanaabha|hiraNya|naabha|_vei_120@3816|_vei2_pn506
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3815">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3817">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn505">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn506">2:506</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn507">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hiraṇya-nābha</b> is the name of a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कौसल्य">kausalya</a></b> or <a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a> prince, whose horse sacrifice appears to be alluded to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (xvi. 9, 13). He is also referred to in the <a class="vei_120a" href="प्रश्न">praśna</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> (vi. 1), and may have been connected with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर आट्णार">para āṭṇāra</a>.</b> <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हैरण्य-नाभ">hairaṇya-nābha</a>.</b></p></div>

हिरण्य-स्तूप|हिरण्यस्तूप|हिरण्य|स्तूप|హిరణ్య-స్తూప|హిరణ్యస్తూప|హిరణ్య|స్తూప|ಹಿರಣ್ಯ-ಸ್ತೂಪ|ಹಿರಣ್ಯಸ್ತೂಪ|ಹಿರಣ್ಯ|ಸ್ತೂಪ|hiraṇya-stūpa|hiraṇyastūpa|hiraṇya|stūpa|hiraNya-stuupa|hiraNyastuupa|hiraNya|stuupa|hiraNya-stoopa|hiraNyastoopa|stoopa|_vei_120@3817|_vei2_pn506
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3816">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3818">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn505">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn506">2:506</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn507">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hiraṇya-stūpa</b> is the name of a man in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203817">1</a></sup> and in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203817">2</a></sup> He is called an <b><a class="vei_120a" href="आंगिरस">āṅgirasa</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203817">3</a></sup> which credits him with the authorship of a Rigvedic hymn.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203817">4</a></sup> The Anukramaṇī (Index) ascribes to him several other hymns.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203817">5</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203817">↑</a> x. 149, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203817">↑</a> i. 6, 4, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203817">↑</a> iii. 24, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203817">↑</a> i. 32.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203817"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203817">↑</a> i. 31-35; ix. 4, 69. <i>Cf.</i> Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 104, 141.</li></ol></div>

हिरण्य-हस्त|हिरण्यहस्त|हिरण्य|हस्त|హిరణ్య-హస్త|హిరణ్యహస్త|హిరణ్య|హస్త|ಹಿರಣ್ಯ-ಹಸ್ತ|ಹಿರಣ್ಯಹಸ್ತ|ಹಿರಣ್ಯ|ಹಸ್ತ|hiraṇya-hasta|hiraṇyahasta|hiraṇya|hasta|hiraNya-hasta|hiraNyahasta|hiraNya|_vei_120@3818|_vei2_pn506
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3817">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3819">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn505">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn506">2:506</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn507">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hiraṇya-hasta</b> is in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203818">1</a></sup> the name of a son given by the Aśvins to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वध्रिमती">vadhrimatī</a></b> (who, as her name denotes, was the wife of a eunuch).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203818"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203818">↑</a> i. 116, 13; 117, 24; vi. 62, 7; x. 39, 7. He appears to be called <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्याव">śyāva</a></b> in x. 65, 12. <i>Cf.</i> Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 52.</li></ol></div>

हिरा|హిరా|ಹಿರಾ|hirā|hiraa|_vei_120@3819|_vei2_pn506
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3818">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3820">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn505">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn506">2:506</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn507">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hirā</b> in the <b>Atharvaveda</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203819"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203819">1</a></sup> denotes ‘vein,’ like <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हिता">hitā</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203819"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203819">↑</a> i. 17, 1; vii. 35, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxv. 8. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Omina und Portenta,</i> 346.</li></ol></div>

हृत्स्व्-आशय आल्लकेय|हृत्स्वाशय आल्लकेय|हृत्स्व्|आशय आल्लकेय|హృత్స్వ్-ఆశయ ఆల్లకేయ|హృత్స్వాశయ ఆల్లకేయ|హృత్స్వ్|ఆశయ ఆల్లకేయ|ಹೃತ್ಸ್ವ್-ಆಶಯ ಆಲ್ಲಕೇಯ|ಹೃತ್ಸ್ವಾಶಯ ಆಲ್ಲಕೇಯ|ಹೃತ್ಸ್ವ್|ಆಶಯ ಆಲ್ಲಕೇಯ|hṛtsv-āśaya āllakeya|hṛtsvāśaya āllakeya|hṛtsv|āśaya āllakeya|hRtsv-aashaya aallakeya|hRtsvaashaya aallakeya|hRtsv|aashaya aallakeya|hRitsv-aashaya aallakeya|hRitsvaashaya aallakeya|hRitsv|hRutsv-aashaya aallakeya|hRutsvaashaya aallakeya|hRutsv|hRRitsv-aashaya aallakeya|hRRitsvaashaya aallakeya|hRRitsv|_vei_120@3820|_vei2_pn506
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3819">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3821">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn505">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn506">2:506</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn507">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hṛtsv-āśaya Āllakeya</b> is mentioned in the Jaiminīya <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 40, 2) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="वंश">vaṃśa</a> (list of teachers) as a pupil of <b>Somaśuṣma <a class="vei_120a" href="सात्ययज्ञि">sātyayajñi</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="प्राचीनयोग्य">prācīnayogya</a>.</b></p></div>

हृदयामय|హృదయామయ|ಹೃದಯಾಮಯ|hṛdayāmaya|hRdayaamaya|hRidayaamaya|hRudayaamaya|hRRidayaamaya|_vei_120@3821|_vei2_pn506
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3820">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3822">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn505">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn506">2:506</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn507">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hṛdayāmaya,</b> ‘disease of the heart,’ is mentioned in the Atharvaveda in connexion with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="यक्ष्म">yakṣma</a></b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203821">1</a></sup> and with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बलास">balāsa</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203821">2</a></sup>  Zimmer,<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203821">3</a></sup> who thinks that <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बलास">balāsa</a></b> is consumption, connects the name with the later view of the medical Saṃhitās,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203821">4</a></sup> that love is one of the causes of the disease. But it would be more natural to see in it a disease affecting the heart.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203821">↑</a> v. 30, 9.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203821">↑</a> vi. 14, 1; 127, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203821">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 387.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203821"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203821">↑</a> Wise, <i>Hindu System of Medicine,</i> 321, 322.</li></ol></div>

हृद्-योत|हृद्योत|हृद्|योत|హృద్-యోత|హృద్యోత|హృద్|యోత|ಹೃದ್-ಯೋತ|ಹೃದ್ಯೋತ|ಹೃದ್|ಯೋತ|hṛd-yota|hṛdyota|hṛd|yota|hRd-yota|hRdyota|hRd|hRid-yota|hRidyota|hRid|hRud-yota|hRudyota|hRud|hRRid-yota|hRRidyota|hRRid|_vei_120@3822|_vei2_pn507
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3821">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3823">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn506">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn507">2:507</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn508">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hṛd-yota</b><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203822"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203822">1</a></sup> (for <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हृद्">hṛd</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="द्योत">dyota</a></b>) and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हृद्">hṛd</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="रोग">roga</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203822"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203822">2</a></sup> ‘heart disease,’ are mentioned in the Atharvaveda and the Rigveda respectively. Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203822"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203822">3</a></sup> identifies the disease in the Atharvaveda with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हृदयामय">hṛdayāmaya</a>,</b> ‘consumption caused by love.’ In the Rigveda this is not at all likely: later in the medical Saṃhitās the word probably denotes <i>angina pectoris.</i><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203822"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203822">4</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203822"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203822">↑</a> Av. i. 22, 1. <i>Cf.</i> vi. 24, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203822"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203822">↑</a> Rv. i. 50, 11.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203822"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203822">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 388.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203822"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203822">↑</a> Wise, <i>Hindu System of Medicine,</i> 321.</li></ol></div>

हेमन्|హేమన్|ಹೇಮನ್|heman|_vei_120@3823|_vei2_pn507
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3822">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3824">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn506">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn507">2:507</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn508">↷</a><hr><p><b>Heman</b> (used only in the locative singular) denotes ‘winter’ in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203823"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203823">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203823"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203823">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 1, 1; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxxvi. 6; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 10, 10; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 5, 4, 5; xi. 2, 7, 32.</li></ol></div>

हेमन्त|హేమన్త|ಹೇಮನ್ತ|hemanta|_vei_120@3824|_vei2_pn507
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3823">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3825">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn506">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn507">2:507</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn508">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hemanta,</b> ‘winter,’ occurs only once in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203824">1</a></sup> but often in the later texts.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203824">2</a></sup> Zimmer<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203824">3</a></sup> is inclined to trace differences of climate in the Rigveda: he thinks that certain hymns,<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203824">4</a></sup> which ignore winter and insist on the rains, indicate a different place and time of origin from those which refer to the snowy mountains.<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203824">5</a></sup> It is, however, quite impossible to separate parts of the Rigveda on this basis. It is probable that that text owes its composition in the main to residents in the later <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मध्यदेश">madhyadeśa</a>;</b> hence the references to cold and snow are rather a sign of local than of temporal differences. It is otherwise with the later expansion of the three into four seasons,  which represents clearly the earlier advance of the Indians (see <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋतु">ṛtu</a></b>).</p><p>The Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203824">6</a></sup> describes winter as the time when the plants wither, the leaves fall from the trees, the birds fly low and retire more and more.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203824">↑</a> x. 161, 4.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203824">↑</a> Av. vi. 55, 2; viii. 2, 22; xii. 1, 36; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, v. 7, 2, 4; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xiii. 58; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxi. 15, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 4, 10, 10; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, x. 4, 5, 2 etc.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203824">↑</a> <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 40.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203824">↑</a> Rv. vii. 103; x. 90.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203824">↑</a> Rv. x. 68, 10; 121, 4 (both these are not early hymns).</li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203824"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203824">↑</a> i. 5, 4, 5.</li></ol></div>

हैत-नामन|हैतनामन|हैत|नामन|హైత-నామన|హైతనామన|హైత|నామన|ಹೈತ-ನಾಮನ|ಹೈತನಾಮನ|ಹೈತ|ನಾಮನ|haita-nāmana|haitanāmana|haita|nāmana|haita-naamana|haitanaamana|naamana|_vei_120@3825|_vei2_pn508
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3824">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3826">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn507">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn508">2:508</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn509">↷</a><hr><p><b>Haita-nāmana,</b> ‘descendant of Hitanāman,’ is the patronymic of a teacher apparently called Āhṛta in the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203825">1</a></sup> though the verse is a strange one.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203825"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203825">↑</a> iii. 4, 6. See Pāṇini, vi. 4, 170, Vārttika, and von Schroeder, Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, 2, ix.</li></ol></div>

हैरण्य-नाभ|हैरण्यनाभ|हैरण्य|नाभ|హైరణ్య-నాభ|హైరణ్యనాభ|హైరణ్య|నాభ|ಹೈರಣ್ಯ-ನಾಭ|ಹೈರಣ್ಯನಾಭ|ಹೈರಣ್ಯ|ನಾಭ|hairaṇya-nābha|hairaṇyanābha|hairaṇya|nābha|hairaNya-naabha|hairaNyanaabha|hairaNya|naabha|_vei_120@3826|_vei2_pn508
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3825">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3827">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn507">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn508">2:508</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn509">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hairaṇya-nābha,</b> ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="हिरण्यनाभ">hiraṇyanābha</a>,</b>’ is the patronymic of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पर आट्णार">para āṭṇāra</a>,</b> the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कोसल">kosala</a></b> king, in a <b><a class="vei_120a" href="गाथा">gāthā</a></b> occurring in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (xiii. 5, 4, 4).</p></div>

होतृ|హోతృ|ಹೋತೃ|hotṛ|hotR|hotRi|hotRu|hotRRi|_vei_120@3827|_vei2_pn508
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3826">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3828">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn507">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn508">2:508</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn509">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hotṛ</b> is the name of one of the oldest and most important priests of the Vedic ritual, the counterpart of the Zaotar of the Avestan priesthood.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203827"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203827">1</a></sup> The word must be derived from <i>hu,</i> ‘sacrifice,’ as was held by <b><a class="vei_120a" href="और्णवाभ">aurṇavābha</a>;</b><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203827"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203827">2</a></sup> this indicates a time when the <a class="vei_120a" href="होतृ">hotṛ</a> was at once sacrificer (the later <a class="vei_120a" href="अध्वर्यु">adhvaryu</a>) and singer. But the functions were already clearly divided in the Rigveda, where the Hotṛ's chief duty was the recitation of the <b>Śastras.</b> He was also in the older period often the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="पुरोहित">purohita</a></b> of the king, an office later filled by the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ब्रह्मन्">brahman</a></b> priest.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203827"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203827">↑</a> Rv. i. 1, 1; 14, 11; 139, 10, etc.; <b><a class="vei_120a" href="होत्र">hotra</a>,</b> the ‘Hotṛ's office,’ Rv. ii. 1, 2; 36, 1; 37, 1, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203827"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203827">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="निरुक्त">nirukta</a>. iv. 26. <i>Cf.</i> Oldenberg, <i>Religion des <a class="vei_120a" href="वेद">veda</a>,</i> 380 <i>et seq.</i></li></ol></div>

होत्रक|హోత్రక|ಹೋತ್ರಕ|hotraka|_vei_120@3828|_vei2_pn508
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3827">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3829">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn507">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn508">2:508</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn509">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hotraka</b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203828">1</a></sup> denotes ‘assistant of the Hotṛ’ priest. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203828"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203828">↑</a> ii. 36, 5; vi. 6, 2. In the Sūtras it is variously used--sometimes in this sense, sometimes more widely--so as to include all the priests except the four chief priests (<i>cf.</i> Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, v. 6, 17).</li></ol></div>

ह्यस्|ह्य|హ్యస్|హ్య|ಹ್ಯಸ್|ಹ್ಯ|hyas|hya|_vei_120@3829|_vei2_pn509
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3828">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3830">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn508">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn509">2:509</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn510">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hyas</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203829"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203829">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203829"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203829">2</a></sup> denotes ‘yesterday.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203829"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203829">↑</a> viii. 66, 7; 99, 1; x. 55, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203829"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203829">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 9, 3.</li></ol></div>

ह्रद|హ్రద|ಹ್ರದ|hrada|_vei_120@3830|_vei2_pn509
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3829">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3831">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn508">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn509">2:509</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn510">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hrada</b> in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203830"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203830">1</a></sup> and later<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203830"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203830">2</a></sup> denotes a ‘lake’ or ‘pond.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203830"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203830">↑</a> i. 52, 7; iii. 36, 8; 45, 3; x. 43, 7; 71, 7; 102, 4; 142, 8, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203830"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203830">↑</a> Av. iv. 15, 4; vi. 37, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, xxv. 10, 18; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iv. 1, 5, 12; 4, 5, 10; xi. 5, 5, 8, etc.</li></ol></div>

ह्रदे-चक्षुस्|ह्रदे-चक्षु|ह्रदेचक्षुस्|ह्रदेचक्षु|ह्रदे|चक्षुस्|चक्षु|హ్రదే-చక్షుస్|హ్రదే-చక్షు|హ్రదేచక్షుస్|హ్రదేచక్షు|హ్రదే|చక్షుస్|చక్షు|ಹ್ರದೇ-ಚಕ್ಷುಸ್|ಹ್ರದೇ-ಚಕ್ಷು|ಹ್ರದೇಚಕ್ಷುಸ್|ಹ್ರದೇಚಕ್ಷು|ಹ್ರದೇ|ಚಕ್ಷುಸ್|ಚಕ್ಷು|hrade-cakṣus|hrade-cakṣu|hradecakṣus|hradecakṣu|hrade|cakṣus|cakṣu|hrade-chakShus|hrade-chakShu|hradechakShus|hradechakShu|chakShus|chakShu|hrade-chaxus|hrade-chaxu|hradechaxus|hradechaxu|chaxus|chaxu|_vei_120@3831|_vei2_pn509
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3830">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3832">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn508">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn509">2:509</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn510">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hrade-cakṣus</b> in one passage of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203831">1</a></sup> is thought by Jackson<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203831">2</a></sup> to mean ‘will of the wisp.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203831">↑</a> x. 95, 6.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203831"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203831">↑</a> <i>Proceedings of the American Oriental Society,</i> May, 1890, iv.</li></ol></div>

ह्रस्व माण्डूकेय|హ్రస్వ మాణ్డూకేయ|ಹ್ರಸ್ವ ಮಾಣ್ಡೂಕೇಯ|hrasva māṇḍūkeya|hrasva maaNDuukeya|hrasva maaNDookeya|_vei_120@3832|_vei2_pn509
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3831">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3833">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn508">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn509">2:509</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn510">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hrasva Māṇḍūkeya</b> (‘descendant of Maṇḍūka’) is the name of a teacher in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> Āraṇyaka.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203832">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203832"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203832">↑</a> iii. 1, 5; 2, 1. 6. <i>Cf.</i> Weber, <i>Indische Studien,</i> 1, 391. The word must be regarded as a proper name, given from a personal characteristic, much as <b><a class="vei_120a" href="स्थविर">sthavira</a></b> is used.</li></ol></div>

ह्रादुनि|హ్రాదుని|ಹ್ರಾದುನಿ|hrāduni|hraaduni|_vei_120@3833|_vei2_pn509
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3832">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3834">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn508">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn509">2:509</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn510">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hrāduni</b> denotes ‘hail’ in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203833"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203833">1</a></sup> and later.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203833"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203833">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203833"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203833">↑</a> i. 32, 13; v. 54, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203833"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203833">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vii. 4, 13, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxii. 26; xxvi. 9, etc.</li></ol></div>

ह्रूडु|హ్రూడు|ಹ್ರೂಡು|hrūḍu|hruuDu|hrooDu|_vei_120@3834|_vei2_pn509
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3833">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3835">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn508">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn509">2:509</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn510">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hrūḍu</b> is a word of unknown meaning applied to <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तक्मन्">takman</a></b> in the Atharvaveda.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203834"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203834">1</a></sup> It is variously spelled in the manuscript as <i>hruḍa, hūḍu, rūḍu,</i> and so forth; the Paippalāda recension reads <i>huḍu,</i> ‘ram.’ Henry<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203834"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203834">2</a></sup> has conjectured that the word is the equivalent of a proto-Semitic <i>ḥarāḍu,</i> ‘gold’ (Assyrian <i>huraçu</i> and Hebrew <i>ḥarāç</i>), while Halévy<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203834"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203834">3</a></sup> suggests that it may be the Greek  ‘greenish-yellow’; but both conjectures are highly improbable.<sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203834"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203834">4</a></sup> Weber<sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203834"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203834">5</a></sup> thinks ‘cramp’ is meant. </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203834"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203834">↑</a> i. 25, 2. 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203834"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203834">↑</a> <i>Journal Asiatique,</i> 9th series, 10, 513.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203834"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203834">↑</a> <i>Ibid.,</i> 11, 320 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203834"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203834">↑</a> Macdonell, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1907, 1106.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203834"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203834">↑</a> <i>Indische Studien,</i> 4, 420.</p><p><i>Cf.</i> Lanman on Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 26; Bloomfield, <i>Hymns of the Atharvaveda,</i> 273.</li></ol></div>

ह्वरस्|ह्वर|హ్వరస్|హ్వర|ಹ್ವರಸ್|ಹ್ವರ|hvaras|hvara|_vei_120@3835|_vei2_pn510
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3834">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3836">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn509">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn510">2:510</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn511">↷</a><hr><p><b>Hvaras</b> in three passages of the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203835">1</a></sup> denotes, according to Roth,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203835">2</a></sup> a part of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> sieve, perhaps the part through which the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> juice flowed. But Geldner<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203835">3</a></sup> thinks that in all these cases the sense is merely ‘hindrance.’ </p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203835">↑</a> ix. 3, 2; 63, 4; 106, 13.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203835">↑</a> St. Petersburg Dictionary, <i>s.v.</i> 1; Zimmer, <i>Altindisches Leben,</i> 278, n.; Hillebrandt, <i>Vedische Mythologie,</i> 1, 203.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203835"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203835">↑</a> <i>Vedische Studien,</i> 2, 20</li></ol></div>

अंगुलि|अङ्गुलि|అంగులి|అఙ్గులి|ಅಂಗುಲಿ|ಅಙ್ಗುಲಿ|aṅguli|aMguli|anguli|_vei_120@3836|_vei2_pn511
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3835">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3837">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn510">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn511">2:511</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn512">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aṅguli,</b> ‘finger-breadth,’ is mentioned as the ‘lowest measure’ in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203836"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203836">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203836"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203836">↑</a> x. 2, 1, 2. See Fleet, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1912, 231.</li></ol></div>

अनस्|अन|అనస్|అన|ಅನಸ್|ಅನ|anas|ana|_vei_120@3837|_vei2_pn511
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3836">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3838">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn510">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn511">2:511</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn512">↷</a><hr><p><b>Anas.</b>--In the Sūtras<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203837"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203837">1</a></sup> mention is made of a part of the cart called Gadhā, which in Garbe's<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203837"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203837">2</a></sup> opinion means ‘roof.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203837"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203837">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiii. 38; Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xix. 26, 4. <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="त्रि">tri</a>-gadha, ibid.,</i> xix. 26, 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203837"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203837">↑</a> Edition of Āpastamba, 3, 356.</li></ol></div>

अरणी|अरणि|అరణీ|అరణి|ಅರಣೀ|ಅರಣಿ|araṇī|araṇi|araNii|araNi|araNee|_vei_120@3838|_vei2_pn511
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3837">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3839">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn510">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn511">2:511</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn512">↷</a><hr><p><b>Araṇī</b> is the designation, in the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203838">1</a></sup> and later,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203838">2</a></sup> of the two pieces of wood used in producing the sacrificial fire by friction. The upper (<i>uttarā</i>) and the lower (<i>adharā</i>) are distinguished.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203838">3</a></sup> The upper, in the form of a drill, is made of the hard wood of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वत्थ">aśvattha</a>,</b><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203838">4</a></sup> the lower, in the form of a slab, of the soft wood of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शमी">śamī</a>.</b><sup id="ref_120_5_vei_1203838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_5_vei_1203838">5</a></sup> The drill is twirled forcibly (<i>sahasā</i>)<sup id="ref_120_6_vei_1203838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_6_vei_1203838">6</a></sup> backwards and forwards with the arms (<i>bāhubhyām</i>)<sup id="ref_120_7_vei_1203838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_7_vei_1203838">7</a></sup> by means of cords (<i>raśanābhiḥ</i>).<sup id="ref_120_8_vei_1203838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_8_vei_1203838">8</a></sup> The action doubtless resembled that by which butter is separated from milk in India  at the present day, the same verb (<i>math,</i> ‘twirl,’ ‘churn’)<sup id="ref_120_9_vei_1203838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_9_vei_1203838">9</a></sup> being used for both processes. This method of producing the sacrificial fire still survives in India. Specimens of the modern apparatus may be seen in the Indian Institute and in the PittRivers Museum at Oxford.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203838">↑</a> i. 127, 4; 129, 5; iii. 29, 2; v. 9, 3; vii. 1, 1; x. 184, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203838">↑</a> Av. x. 8, 20; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 1, 1, 11; iv. 6, 8, 3; xii. 4, 3, 3. 10; Kaṭha <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, iv. 7; Śvetāśvatara <a class="vei_120a" href="उपनिषद्">upaniṣad</a>, i. 14. 15; Āśvalāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="गृह्य">gṛhya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 6.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203838">↑</a> Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 1, 22; xi. 5, 1, 15; Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, v. 1, 30, etc.</li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203838">↑</a> Av. vi. 11, 1; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, xi. 5, 1, 13; Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, iv. 7, 22.</li><li id="fx_120_5_vei_1203838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_5_vei_1203838">↑</a> Av. vi. 11, 1; 30, 2. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 3, 11 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_6_vei_1203838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_6_vei_1203838">↑</a> Rv. vi. 48, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_7_vei_1203838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_7_vei_1203838">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, iii. 4, 7. <i>Cf.</i> Rv. x. 7, 5.</li><li id="fx_120_8_vei_1203838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_8_vei_1203838">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Rv. x. 4, 6. See Macdonell, <i>Vedic Mythology,</i> p. 91.</li><li id="fx_120_9_vei_1203838"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_9_vei_1203838">↑</a> Fire: Rv. vi. 15, 17; 48, 5, etc. Butter: <i>dugdhaṃ mathitam ājyaṃ bhavati,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 10, 2; Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, v. 3, 2, 6; Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, v. 8, 18.</li></ol></div>

अरत्नि|అరత్ని|ಅರತ್ನಿ|aratni|_vei_120@3839|_vei2_pn512
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3838">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3840">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn511">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn512">2:512</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aratni,</b> ‘cubit.’ According to the Śulvasūtra of <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a>,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203839"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203839">1</a></sup> this measure is equal to 24 Aṅgulas or ‘finger-breadths.’ The Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203839"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203839">2</a></sup> also mentions 24 Aṅgulis or ‘fingerbreadths’ as a measure, but without reference to the <a class="vei_120a" href="अरत्नि">aratni</a>.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203839"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203839">3</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203839"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203839">↑</a> Fleet, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1912, 231, n. 2.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203839"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203839">↑</a> x. 2, 1, 3.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203839"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203839">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Eggeling, <i>Sacred Books of the East,</i> 43, 300, n. 3.</li></ol></div>

अर्क|అర్క|ಅರ್ಕ|arka|_vei_120@3840|_vei2_pn512
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3839">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3841">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn511">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn512">2:512</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">↷</a><hr><p><b>Arka</b> (<i>Calotropis gigantea</i>) is often referred to in the Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (ix. 1, 1, 4. 9; its leaf: <i><a class="vei_120a" href="अर्क">arka</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="पर्ण">parṇa</a>,</i> 42; <i>arkapalāśa,</i> i. 2, 3, 12. 13).</p></div>

आधान|ఆధాన|ಆಧಾನ|ādhāna|aadhaana|_vei_120@3841|_vei2_pn512
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3840">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3842">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn511">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn512">2:512</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">↷</a><hr><p><b>Ādhāna</b> denotes ‘bridle,’ and especially the ‘bit’ of the bridle in the <a class="vei_120a" href="यजुर्वेद">yajurveda</a> Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203841"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203841">1</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203841"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203841">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 5, 9, 2. 3; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxviii. 9; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iv. 7, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 3, 10.</li></ol></div>

इतिहास|ఇతిహాస|ಇತಿಹಾಸ|itihāsa|itihaasa|_vei_120@3842|_vei2_pn512
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3841">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3843">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn511">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn512">2:512</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">↷</a><hr><p><b>Itihāsa.</b>--The question of the nature of the Vedic <a class="vei_120a" href="इतिहास">itihāsa</a> has been further considered by Keith, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1911, 979-995; 1912, 429-438; and by Oldenberg, <i>Nachrichten der ko7niglichen Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften zu Go7ttingen,</i> 1911, 441-468.</p></div>

ऐक्ष्वाक|ఐక్ష్వాక|ಐಕ್ಷ್ವಾಕ|aikṣvāka|aikShvaaka|aixvaaka|_vei_120@3843|_vei2_pn512
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3842">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3844">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn511">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn512">2:512</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">↷</a><hr><p><b>Aikṣvāka.</b> For ‘Vārṣṇi’ read ‘Vārṣṇa.’</p></div>

कक्ष|కక్ష|ಕಕ್ಷ|kakṣa|kakSha|kaxa|_vei_120@3844|_vei2_pn512
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3843">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3845">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn511">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn512">2:512</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kakṣa,</b> 1, 131, should be 2. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कंस">kaṃsa</a>,</b> coming before <b><a class="vei_120a" href="ककर">kakara</a>,</b> 1, 130, and after <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कंस">kaṃsa</a>,</b> ‘pot or vessel of metal,’ which should be 1. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कंस">kaṃsa</a>.</b></p></div>

कम्बोज|कंबोज|కమ్బోజ|కంబోజ|ಕಮ್ಬೋಜ|ಕಂಬೋಜ|kamboja|_vei_120@3845|_vei2_pn512
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3844">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3846">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn511">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn512">2:512</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kamboja.</b>--For these Iranian connexions, see Kuhn, <i>Avesta, Pehlvi and Ancient Persian Studies in Honour of the late Shamsul-ulema Dastur Peshotanji Behramji Sanjana</i> (Strassburg und Leipzig, 1904), 213 <i>et seq.;</i> Grierson, <i>Journal of the Royal  Asiatic Society,</i> 1911, 801, 802; 1912, 255; G. K. Nariman, <i>ibid,</i> 255-257; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 2^2, 355, 356.</p></div>

काण्डा-विष|काण्डाविष|काण्डा|विष|కాణ్డా-విష|కాణ్డావిష|కాణ్డా|విష|ಕಾಣ್ಡಾ-ವಿಷ|ಕಾಣ್ಡಾವಿಷ|ಕಾಣ್ಡಾ|ವಿಷ|kāṇḍā-viṣa|kāṇḍāviṣa|kāṇḍā|viṣa|kaaNDaa-viSha|kaaNDaaviSha|kaaNDaa|viSha|_vei_120@3846|_vei2_pn513
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3845">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3847">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn512">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">2:513</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāṇḍā-viṣa,</b> 1, 148, should be <b>Kāndā-<a class="vei_120a" href="विष">viṣa</a>.</b></p></div>

काण्वी-पुत्र|काण्वीपुत्र|काण्वी|काण्वि|पुत्र|కాణ్వీ-పుత్ర|కాణ్వీపుత్ర|కాణ్వీ|కాణ్వి|పుత్ర|ಕಾಣ್ವೀ-ಪುತ್ರ|ಕಾಣ್ವೀಪುತ್ರ|ಕಾಣ್ವೀ|ಕಾಣ್ವಿ|ಪುತ್ರ|kāṇvī-putra|kāṇvīputra|kāṇvī|kāṇvi|putra|kaaNvii-putra|kaaNviiputra|kaaNvii|kaaNvi|kaaNvee-putra|kaaNveeputra|kaaNvee|_vei_120@3847|_vei2_pn513
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3846">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3848">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn512">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">2:513</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kāṇvī-putra,</b> 1, 147, should come after <b><a class="vei_120a" href="काण्वायन">kāṇvāyana</a>.</b></p></div>

कुमार-हारित|कुमारहारित|कुमार|हारित|కుమార-హారిత|కుమారహారిత|కుమార|హారిత|ಕುಮಾರ-ಹಾರಿತ|ಕುಮಾರಹಾರಿತ|ಕುಮಾರ|ಹಾರಿತ|kumāra-hārita|kumārahārita|kumāra|hārita|kumaara-haarita|kumaarahaarita|kumaara|haarita|_vei_120@3848|_vei2_pn513
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3847">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3849">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn512">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">2:513</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kumāra-hārita,</b> 1, 172, should come after <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुभ्र">kubhra</a>,</b> 1, 162.</p></div>

कुमल बर्हिस्|कुमल बर्हि|కుమల బర్హిస్|కుమల బర్హి|ಕುಮಲ ಬರ್ಹಿಸ್|ಕುಮಲ ಬರ್ಹಿ|kumala barhis|kumala barhi|_vei_120@3849|_vei2_pn513
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3848">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3850">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn512">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">2:513</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kumala barhis,</b> 1, 172, should be <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कुल्मल">kulmala</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="बर्हिस्">barhis</a>.</b></p></div>

कुषुम्भक|कुषुंभक|కుషుమ్భక|కుషుంభక|ಕುಷುಮ್ಭಕ|ಕುಷುಂಭಕ|kuṣumbhaka|kuShumbhaka|_vei_120@3850|_vei2_pn513
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3849">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3851">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn512">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">2:513</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kuṣumbhaka,</b> according to Egerton (<i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 31, 134), denotes ‘poison-bag’ in both passages of the Rigveda.</p></div>

कृष्णल|కృష్ణల|ಕೃಷ್ಣಲ|kṛṣṇala|kRShNala|kRiShNala|kRuShNala|kRRiShNala|_vei_120@3851|_vei2_pn513
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3850">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3852">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn512">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">2:513</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kṛṣṇala,</b> line 1 and note^4, read ‘seed’ for ‘berry.’</p></div>

क्रैव्य|క్రైవ్య|ಕ್ರೈವ್ಯ|kraivya|_vei_120@3852|_vei2_pn513
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3851">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3853">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn512">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">2:513</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">↷</a><hr><p><b>Kraivya.</b>--For ‘on the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परिवक्रा">parivakrā</a></b>’ read ‘at <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परिवक्रा">parivakrā</a>.</b>’</p></div>

क्रोश|క్రోశ|ಕ್ರೋಶ|krośa|krosha|_vei_120@3853|_vei2_pn513
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3852">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3854">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn512">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">2:513</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">↷</a><hr><p><b>Krośa.</b>--In note<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203853"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203853">1</a></sup> for ‘about two miles’ read ‘1(1/8) miles.’ See Fleet, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 1912, 237.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203853"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203853">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 62, 459-470.</li></ol></div>

ग्राम|గ్రామ|ಗ್ರಾಮ|grāma|graama|_vei_120@3854|_vei2_pn513
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3853">«</a>pada9<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3855">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn512">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">2:513</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">↷</a><hr><p><b>Grāma.</b>--The phrase Grāmin, ‘possessing a village,’ occurs often in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (ii. 1, 3, 2; 6, 7; 2, 8, 1; 11, 1; 3, 3, 5; 9, 2), usually in connexion with various rites for acquiring a village. Since in these cases repeated mention is made of obtaining pre-eminence over <b>Sajātas</b> and Samānas, ‘equals,’ it is probable that allowance must also be made for the control over his fellow-villagers which an ambitious man could obtain (<i>e.g.,</i> by loans), and which might end by giving him the position of a great landlord, even without the intervention of the king.</p></div>

चण्डातक|చణ్డాతక|ಚಣ್ಡಾತಕ|caṇḍātaka|chaNDaataka|_vei_120@3855|_vei2_pn513
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3854">«</a>pada10<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3856">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn512">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">2:513</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">↷</a><hr><p><b>Caṇḍātaka</b> in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (v. 2, 1, 8) and the Kātyāyana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a> (xiv. 5, 3) denotes an undergarment worn by women.</p></div>

चमू|చమూ|ಚಮೂ|camū|chamuu|chamoo|_vei_120@3856|_vei2_pn513
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3855">«</a>pada11<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3857">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn512">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">2:513</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">↷</a><hr><p><b>Camū.</b>--Oldenberg<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203856"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203856">1</a></sup> considers that even in the dual the word denotes two vessels into which the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a>, often mixed with water in the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कोश">kośa</a></b> and purified with the sieve, was poured, and that, in the plural, reference is made to these and other vessels into which the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> was put at the various stages of the  process. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="कलश">kalaśa</a></b> similarly denoted either one (sing.) or several (plur.) of the vessels, the dual not being used, since the dual of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चमू">camū</a></b> was reserved for the two vessels <i>par excellence.</i> In the later ritual the Camūs are replaced by the <a class="vei_120a" href="द्रोण">droṇa</a>-<a class="vei_120a" href="कलश">kalaśa</a> and the Pūtabhṛt, which was, however, assimilated in form and material (being made of clay, not of wood) to the Ādhāvanīya, the later name of the <a class="vei_120a" href="कोश">kośa</a>. The main difficulty of this theory is that it is hard to explain why <a class="vei_120a" href="कलश">kalaśa</a> never occurs in the dual. Geldner<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203856"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203856">2</a></sup> falls back on the older view that in <a class="vei_120a" href="चमू">camū</a> (dual) the two boards of the <a class="vei_120a" href="सोम">soma</a> press are meant.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203856"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203856">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 62, 459-470.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203856"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203856">↑</a> <i><a class="vei_120a" href="ऋग्वेद">ṛgveda</a>, Glossar,</i> 60.</li></ol></div>

चाण्डाल|చాణ్డాల|ಚಾಣ್ಡಾಲ|cāṇḍāla|chaaNDaala|_vei_120@3857|_vei2_pn514
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3856">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3858">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">2:514</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">↷</a><hr><p><b>Cāṇḍāla,</b> 1, 258, should come after <b><a class="vei_120a" href="चाक्षुष">cākṣuṣa</a>.</b></p></div>

जाबाल|జాబాల|ಜಾಬಾಲ|jābāla|jaabaala|_vei_120@3858|_vei2_pn514
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3857">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3859">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">2:514</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">↷</a><hr><p><b>Jābāla.</b>--For ‘descendant of <b>Jabāla</b>’ read ‘descendant of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="जबाला">jabālā</a>.</b>’</p></div>

तलव|తలవ|ತಲವ|talava|_vei_120@3859|_vei2_pn514
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3858">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3860">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">2:514</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">↷</a><hr><p><b>Talava,</b> 1, 302, should come after <b><a class="vei_120a" href="तर्य">tarya</a>,</b> 1, 301.</p></div>

दृषद्वती|दृषद्वति|దృషద్వతీ|దృషద్వతి|ದೃಷದ್ವತೀ|ದೃಷದ್ವತಿ|dṛṣadvatī|dṛṣadvati|dRShadvatii|dRShadvati|dRShadvatee|dRiShadvatii|dRiShadvati|dRuShadvatii|dRuShadvati|dRRiShadvatii|dRRiShadvati|_vei_120@3860|_vei2_pn514
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3859">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3861">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">2:514</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">↷</a><hr><p><b>Dṛṣadvatī.</b>--The identification of this river with the Ghaggar (Macdonell, <i>History of Sanskrit Literature,</i> 142) seems to be wrong. It is almost certainly the modern Chitang (which is the correct spelling according to Raverty, <i>Journal of the Asiatic Society of Bengal,</i> 61, 422), or Chitrung (Oldham, <i>Journal of the Royal Asiatic Society,</i> 25, 58; <i>cf.</i> the sketch map opposite p. 49). See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>,</b> 2, 435, note^4.</p></div>

देवभाग|దేవభాగ|ದೇವಭಾಗ|devabhāga|devabhaaga|_vei_120@3861|_vei2_pn514
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3860">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3862">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">2:514</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">↷</a><hr><p><b>Devabhāga,</b> is also mentioned in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (vi. 6, 2, 2) as having ruined the <b>Sṛñjayas</b> by an error in the sacrifice, and as a contemporary of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वासिष्ठ">vāsiṣṭha</a> Sātahavya.</b></p></div>

नक्षत्र|నక్షత్ర|ನಕ್ಷತ್ರ|nakṣatra|nakShatra|naxatra|_vei_120@3862|_vei2_pn514
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3861">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3863">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">2:514</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nakṣatra.</b>--In note ^137, 1, 424, supply ‘of’ before ‘the criticisms made,’ and in the following lines read ‘<i>Journal</i>’ and ‘466.’</p></div>

निषाद|నిషాద|ನಿಷಾದ|niṣāda|niShaada|_vei_120@3863|_vei2_pn514
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3862">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3864">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">2:514</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">↷</a><hr><p><b>Niṣāda.</b>--The Niṣādas, according to the Mahābhārata (iii. 10 538), are situated beyond <b><a class="vei_120a" href="विनशन">vinaśana</a>,</b> the ‘place of disappear ance’ of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सरस्वती">sarasvatī</a>.</b></p></div>

नृमेध|నృమేధ|ನೃಮೇಧ|nṛmedha|nRmedha|nRimedha|nRumedha|nRRimedha|_vei_120@3864|_vei2_pn514
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3863">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3865">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">2:514</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">↷</a><hr><p><b>Nṛmedha.</b>--For <b>Sumedhas</b> read <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सुमेध">sumedha</a>.</b></p></div>

पति|పతి|ಪತಿ|pati|_vei_120@3865|_vei2_pn514
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3864">«</a>pada9<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3866">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">2:514</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pati,</b> 1, 489, note 145, line 7, after ‘ritual’ delete ‘of.’</p></div>

परावृज्|పరావృజ్|ಪರಾವೃಜ್|parāvṛj|paraavRj|paraavRij|paraavRuj|paraavRRij|_vei_120@3866|_vei2_pn514
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3865">«</a>pada10<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3867">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn513">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">2:514</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">↷</a><hr><p><b>Parāvṛj,</b> 1, 493, headline, for ‘49,’ read ‘493.’ </p></div>

पष्ठवाह्|పష్ఠవాహ్|ಪಷ್ಠವಾಹ್|paṣṭhavāh|paShThavaah|_vei_120@3867|_vei2_pn515
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3866">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3868">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">2:515</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">↷</a><hr><p><b>Paṣṭhavāh</b> in the later literature appears sometimes as Praṣṭhavāḥ: if Bloomfield's view<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203867"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203867">1</a></sup> that <b><a class="vei_120a" href="प्रष्टि">praṣṭi</a></b> is from <i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्र">pra</a></i> and <i>as,</i> ‘be,’ is correct, this may be the older form. Against this, however, is to be set the constant earlier tradition.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203867"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203867">2</a></sup> Macdonell<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203867"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203867">3</a></sup> connects the word with <i>pṛṣṭhavāh,</i> ‘carrying on the back.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203867"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203867">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 29, 78 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203867"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203867">↑</a> Wackernagel, <i>Altindische Grammatik,</i> 1, 235.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203867"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203867">↑</a> <i>Vedic Grammar,</i> p. 48.</li></ol></div>

प्रतिष्ठा|ప్రతిష్ఠా|ಪ್ರತಿಷ್ಠಾ|pratiṣṭhā|pratiShThaa|_vei_120@3868|_vei2_pn515
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3867">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3869">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">2:515</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pratiṣṭhā.</b>--For the succour of a fugitive offender may be compared the phrase in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (vi. 5, 6, 3; 8, 4. 5), ‘men do not deliver up even one deserving death (<i>vadhya</i>) who has come to them for protection (<i>prapanna</i>).’ <i>Cf.</i> <b><a class="vei_120a" href="परिदा">paridā</a>.</b></p></div>

प्रवर्त|ప్రవర్త|ಪ್ರವರ್ತ|pravarta|_vei_120@3869|_vei2_pn515
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3868">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3870">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">2:515</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">↷</a><hr><p><b>Pravarta.</b>--The sense of ‘ear-ornament’ is justified by Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xix. 23, 11; 24, 10. <i>Cf.</i> Baudhāvana <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xiii. 31.</p></div>

प्रष्टि|ప్రష్టి|ಪ್ರಷ್ಟಿ|praṣṭi|praShTi|_vei_120@3870|_vei2_pn515
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3869">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3871">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">2:515</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">↷</a><hr><p><b>Praṣṭi</b> is considered by Bloomfield<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203870"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203870">1</a></sup> to refer to a horse yoked in front to guide the team, a sense clearly occurring in some places, and to be derived from <i><a class="vei_120a" href="प्र">pra</a></i> and <i>as,</i> ‘be,’ as in <b><a class="vei_120a" href="उपस्ति">upasti</a>.</b> The word is usually<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203870"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203870">2</a></sup> considered to be connected with <i><a class="vei_120a" href="पर्शु">parśu</a>,</i> ‘rib.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203870"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203870">↑</a> <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 29, 78 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203870"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203870">↑</a> Wackernagel, <i>Altindische Grammatik,</i> 1, 230, 235; Macdonell, <i>Vedic Grammer,</i> p. 43.</li></ol></div>

बल्बूथ|బల్బూథ|ಬಲ್ಬೂಥ|balbūtha|balbuutha|balbootha|_vei_120@3871|_vei2_pn515
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3870">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3872">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">2:515</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">↷</a><hr><p><b>Balbūtha,</b> 2, 64, should come after <b><a class="vei_120a" href="बल्बज">balbaja</a>,</b> 2, 63.</p></div>

बाहीक|బాహీక|ಬಾಹೀಕ|bāhīka|baahiika|baaheeka|_vei_120@3872|_vei2_pn515
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3871">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3873">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">2:515</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bāhīka.</b>--For the later traditions, see Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 2^2, 482 <i>et seq.;</i> Grierson, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 66, 68, 73.</p></div>

ब्रह्मचर्य|బ్రహ్మచర్య|ಬ್ರಹ್ಮಚರ್ಯ|brahmacarya|brahmacharya|_vei_120@3873|_vei2_pn515
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3872">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3874">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">2:515</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">↷</a><hr><p><b>Brahmacarya.</b>--The later rules are exhaustively given by O{??}aser, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 66, 1 <i>et seq.</i></p></div>

भंग-श्रवस्|भंग-श्रव|भंगश्रवस्|भंगश्रव|भंग|श्रवस्|श्रव|भङ्ग-श्रवस्|भङ्ग-श्रव|भङ्गश्रवस्|भङ्गश्रव|भङ्ग|భంగ-శ్రవస్|భంగ-శ్రవ|భంగశ్రవస్|భంగశ్రవ|భంగ|శ్రవస్|శ్రవ|భఙ్గ-శ్రవస్|భఙ్గ-శ్రవ|భఙ్గశ్రవస్|భఙ్గశ్రవ|భఙ్గ|ಭಂಗ-ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಭಂಗ-ಶ್ರವ|ಭಂಗಶ್ರವಸ್|ಭಂಗಶ್ರವ|ಭಂಗ|ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಶ್ರವ|ಭಙ್ಗ-ಶ್ರವಸ್|ಭಙ್ಗ-ಶ್ರವ|ಭಙ್ಗಶ್ರವಸ್|ಭಙ್ಗಶ್ರವ|ಭಙ್ಗ|bhaṅga-śravas|bhaṅga-śrava|bhaṅgaśravas|bhaṅgaśrava|bhaṅga|śravas|śrava|bhaMga-shravas|bhaMga-shrava|bhaMgashravas|bhaMgashrava|bhaMga|shravas|shrava|bhanga-shravas|bhanga-shrava|bhangashravas|bhangashrava|bhanga|_vei_120@3874|_vei2_pn515
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3873">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3875">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">2:515</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhaṅga-śravas</b> is the form of a man's name found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā (xxxviii. 12) in the parallel to the passage of the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Āraṇyaka (vi. 5, 2), which has <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भंग्यश्रवस्">bhaṅgyaśravas</a>.</b></p></div>

भिषज्|భిషజ్|ಭಿಷಜ್|bhiṣaj|bhiShaj|_vei_120@3875|_vei2_pn515
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3874">«</a>pada9<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3876">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn514">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">2:515</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">↷</a><hr><p><b>Bhiṣaj,</b> 2, 104, should be 1. <b><a class="vei_120a" href="भिषज्">bhiṣaj</a>.</b> </p></div>

मत्य|మత్య|ಮತ್ಯ|matya|_vei_120@3876|_vei2_pn516
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3875">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3877">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">2:516</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn517">↷</a><hr><p><b>Matya,</b> ‘harrow’ or ‘roller,’ is found in the <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> portions of the Saṃhitās.<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203876">1</a></sup> Sāyaṇa<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203876">2</a></sup> takes it as ‘manure.’</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203876">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, vi. 6, 7, 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, xxix. 4; <a class="vei_120a" href="पञ्चविंश ब्राह्मण">pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa</a>, ii. 9, 2. <i>Cf. <a class="vei_120a" href="सुमतित्सरु">sumatitsaru</a></i> above, i. 334.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203876"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203876">↑</a> On <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, <i>loc. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.</i></li></ol></div>

माय|మాయ|ಮಾಯ|māya|maaya|_vei_120@3877|_vei2_pn516
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3876">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3878">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">2:516</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn517">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māya,</b> 2, 155, should be <b>Māyā.</b></p></div>

मास|మాస|ಮಾಸ|māsa|maasa|_vei_120@3878|_vei2_pn516
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3877">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3879">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">2:516</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn517">↷</a><hr><p><b>Māsa,</b> 2, 157, note ^10, add: according to <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Samhitā, iii. 5, 1, 3, the new moon begins the month.</p></div>

युग|యుగ|ಯುಗ|yuga|_vei_120@3879|_vei2_pn516
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3878">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3880">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">2:516</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn517">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yuga.</b>--Tilak<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203879"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203879">1</a></sup> has pressed this word into his theory of the reminiscence in the Vedas of an arctic home. He finds in it the sense of ‘month,’ interpreting the legend of <b><a class="vei_120a" href="दीर्घतमस्">dīrghatamas</a></b> (= the sun) as an allusion to the arctic summer of ten months, followed by a night of two, traces of which he thinks exist in the notices of the seasons. This theory is, however, most improbable, as is his explanation<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203879"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203879">2</a></sup> of the <a class="vei_120a" href="ऐतरेय">aitareya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203879"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203879">3</a></sup> reference by the theory that it portrays the various stages of the life of the Āryans.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203879"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203879">↑</a> <i>The Arctic Home in the Vedas,</i> 172187. <i>Cf.</i> Bloomfield, <i>Journal of the American Oriental Society,</i> 30, 60.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203879"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203879">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 455.</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203879"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203879">↑</a> vii. 15.</li></ol></div>

योजन|యోజన|ಯೋಜನ|yojana|_vei_120@3880|_vei2_pn516
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3879">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3881">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">2:516</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn517">↷</a><hr><p><b>Yojana.</b>--From the attribution of thirty Yojanas to the dawn in the Rigveda,<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203880">1</a></sup> Tilak<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203880">2</a></sup> has argued that the dawns of the arctic regions in the interglacial period must be meant. But the reference is apparently to the thirty dawns of the thirty days which constitute the Vedic month. See <b><a class="vei_120a" href="मास">māsa</a>.</b></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203880">↑</a> i. 123, 8. <i>Cf.</i> vi. 59, 6, and the thirty dawns of <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, iv. 3, 11, 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203880"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203880">↑</a> <i>The Arctic Home in the Vedas,</i> 103107.</li></ol></div>

रक्षस्|रक्ष|రక్షస్|రక్ష|ರಕ್ಷಸ್|ರಕ್ಷ|rakṣas|rakṣa|rakShas|rakSha|raxas|raxa|_vei_120@3881|_vei2_pn516
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3880">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3882">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">2:516</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn517">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rakṣas</b> in the early Vedic literature normally refers to demons, and is only metaphorically<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203881"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203881">1</a></sup> applied to human foes. No definite tribe is meant.<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203881"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203881">2</a></sup></p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203881"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203881">↑</a> Rv. iii. 30, 15-17; vii. 104, 1. 2; Muir, <i>Sanskrit Texts,</i> 2^2, 389 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203881"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203881">↑</a> <i>Cf.</i> Grierson, <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 66, 68. Similarly <b>Piśācas</b> are not a tribe in Vedic literature, whatever they may be later.</li></ol></div>

राजसूय|రాజసూయ|ರಾಜಸೂಯ|rājasūya|raajasuuya|raajasooya|_vei_120@3882|_vei2_pn516
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3881">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3883">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">2:516</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn517">↷</a><hr><p><b>Rājasūya.</b>--Read ‘victor’ for ‘victim’ in line 12.</p></div>

लह्यायन|లహ్యాయన|ಲಹ್ಯಾಯನ|lahyāyana|lahyaayana|_vei_120@3883|_vei2_pn516
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3882">«</a>pada8<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3884">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn515">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">2:516</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn517">↷</a><hr><p><b>Lahyāyana,</b> 2, 232, should be <b>Lāhyāyani,</b> and the reference, iii. 3, 1, 2. </p></div>

वर्षा-हू|वर्षा-हू|वर्षाहू|वर्षा|వర్షా-హూ_౧|వర్షా-హూ|వర్షాహూ|వర్షా|ವರ್ಷಾ-ಹೂ_೧|ವರ್ಷಾ-ಹೂ|ವರ್ಷಾಹೂ|ವರ್ಷಾ|varṣā-hū_1|varṣā-hū|varṣāhū|varṣā|varShaa-huu_1|varShaa-huu|varShaahuu|varShaa|varShaa-hoo_1|varShaa-hoo|varShaahoo|_vei_120@3884|_vei2_pn517
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3883">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3885">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn517">2:517</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn518">↷</a><hr><p>1. <b>Varṣā-hū,</b> ‘frog’ (‘calling in the rains’), is one of the victims at the <a class="vei_120a" href="अश्वमेध">aśvamedha</a> (‘horse sacrifice’) in the Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā (xxiv. 38).</p></div>

वर्षा-हू|वर्षा-हू|वर्षाहू|वर्षा|వర్షా-హూ_౨|వర్షా-హూ|వర్షాహూ|వర్షా|ವರ್ಷಾ-ಹೂ_೨|ವರ್ಷಾ-ಹೂ|ವರ್ಷಾಹೂ|ವರ್ಷಾ|varṣā-hū_2|varṣā-hū|varṣāhū|varṣā|varShaa-huu_2|varShaa-huu|varShaahuu|varShaa|varShaa-hoo_2|varShaa-hoo|varShaahoo|_vei_120@3885|_vei2_pn517
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3884">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3886">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn517">2:517</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn518">↷</a><hr><p>2. <b>Varṣā-hū</b> (‘produced in the rains’) is the name<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203885"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203885">1</a></sup> of a plant (<i>Boerhavia procumbens</i>) in the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (iii. 4, 10, 3).</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203885"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203885">↑</a> The form seems to show <i>h</i> for <i>bh,</i> as in other cases given by Wackernagel, <i>Altindische Grammatik,</i> 1, 217^b; <i>varṣābhū</i> actually occurs in the same sense in post-Vedic Sanskrit.</li></ol></div>

वशा|వశా|ವಶಾ|vaśā|vashaa|_vei_120@3886|_vei2_pn517
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3885">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3887">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn517">2:517</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn518">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vaśā</b> is frequently qualified by <i>anūbandhyā</i><sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203886"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203886">1</a></sup> (‘to be bound for slaughtering’); the economy of killing a barren cow probably tended to produce the sense of ‘barren’ in the word.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203886"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203886">↑</a> <i>E.g.,</i> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā, ii. 2, 9, 7; <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā, x. 1.</li></ol></div>

वाच्|వాచ్|ವಾಚ್|vāc|vaach|_vei_120@3887|_vei2_pn517
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3886">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3888">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn517">2:517</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn518">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāc.</b>--Grierson, in his discussion<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203887">1</a></sup> of the Paiśācī speech, holds that the passage cited as the speech of the Asuras in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, <i>he 'lavo (= he 'rayaḥ),</i><sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203887">2</a></sup> can be regarded as in Paiśācī just as much as in Māgadhī, since the change of <i>r</i> to <i>l,</i> and of <i>y</i> to <i>v,</i> is found in Paiśācī also. Sten Konow,<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203887">2</a></sup> however, considers that Paiśācī was the speech used in the Vindhya region. It would be unwise, as a matter of fact, to lay stress on the phrase <i>he 'lavo,</i> because both the reading and the sense are by no means certain.<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203887">3</a></sup> But it should be noted that the easterners and the Asuras are elsewhere in the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a><sup id="ref_120_4_vei_1203887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_4_vei_1203887">4</a></sup> connected: this tells against Dr. Grierson's view.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203887">↑</a> <i>Zeitschrift der Deutschen Morgenländischen Gesellschaft,</i> 66, 66, n. 1.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203887">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 64, 104 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203887">↑</a> <i>Op. <a class="vei_120a" href="चित्">cit</a>.,</i> 64, 104 <i>et seq.</i></li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203887">↑</a> It should be noted that the phrase cannot be genuine Prākrit as it stands, for that would not give us <i>he 'lavo he 'lavaḥ.</i></li><li id="fx_120_4_vei_1203887"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_4_vei_1203887">↑</a> xiii. 8, 1, 5. Probably the view of the earlier part of the Śatapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (iii. 2, 1, 23) would be the same, since its reputed author, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="याज्ञवल्क्य">yājñavalkya</a></b> is connected in tradition with the East. Dr. Grierson's argument would have been stronger had the reference occurred in one of the <b><a class="vei_120a" href="शाण्डिल्य">śāṇḍilya</a></b> books</li></ol></div>

वातावत|వాతావత|ವಾತಾವತ|vātāvata|vaataavata|_vei_120@3888|_vei2_pn517
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3887">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3889">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn517">2:517</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn518">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vātāvata</b> and <b>Vātāvant,</b> 2, 284, should be read for <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वातवत">vātavata</a></b> and <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वातवन्त्">vātavant</a>.</b></p></div>

वासस्|वास|వాసస్|వాస|ವಾಸಸ್|ವಾಸ|vāsas|vāsa|vaasas|vaasa|_vei_120@3889|_vei2_pn517
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3888">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3890">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn517">2:517</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn518">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vāsas.</b>--In the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> Saṃhitā (ii. 2, 11, 4) <i>upādhāyyapūrvaya,</i> as an epithet of <a class="vei_120a" href="वासस्">vāsas</a>, appears to denote ‘fringed (<i>citrānta</i>) according to Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xix. 20, 2.</p></div>

विषाणका|విషాణకా|ವಿಷಾಣಕಾ|viṣāṇakā|viShaaNakaa|_vei_120@3890|_vei2_pn517
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3889">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3891">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn516">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn517">2:517</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn518">↷</a><hr><p><b>Viṣāṇakā,</b> 2, 313, headline, for ‘31,’ read ‘313.’ </p></div>

वेहत्|వేహత్|ವೇಹತ್|vehat|_vei_120@3891|_vei2_pn518
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3890">«</a>pada1<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3892">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn517">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn518">2:518</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120pn">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vehat</b> occurs in conjunction with <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वशा">vaśā</a></b> in the <a class="vei_120a" href="काठक">kāṭhaka</a> Saṃhitā (xxxviii. 10), the Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā (iii. 11, 11), the Vājasaneyī Saṃhitā (xxi. 21), and the <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a> (ii. 6, 18, 4).</p></div>

वैरिण|వైరిణ|ವೈರಿಣ|vairiṇa|vairiNa|_vei_120@3892|_vei2_pn518
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3891">«</a>pada2<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3893">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn517">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn518">2:518</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120pn">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vairiṇa,</b> 2, 318, should be <b><a class="vei_120a" href="वीरण">vīraṇa</a>.</b></p></div>

व्यत्|వ్యత్|ವ್ಯತ್|vyat|_vei_120@3893|_vei2_pn518
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3892">«</a>pada3<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3894">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn517">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn518">2:518</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120pn">↷</a><hr><p><b>Vyat,</b> 1, 523, should be Vyant.</p></div>

शम्या|శమ్యా|ಶಮ್ಯಾ|śamyā|shamyaa|_vei_120@3894|_vei2_pn518
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3893">«</a>pada4<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3895">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn517">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn518">2:518</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120pn">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śamyā</b> in the Brāhmaṇas<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203894">1</a></sup> frequently denotes the wooden support on which the lower of the two millstones (<b><a class="vei_120a" href="दृषद्">dṛṣad</a></b>) is placed.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203894"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203894">↑</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="तैत्तिरीय">taittirīya</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 6, 1, 1; Satapatha <a class="vei_120a" href="ब्राह्मण">brāhmaṇa</a>, i. 1, 1, 22; 2, 1, 16; v. 2, 3, 2; <a class="vei_120a" href="बौधायन">baudhāyana</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, i. 7; Āpastamba <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a> <a class="vei_120a" href="सूत्र">sūtra</a>, xviii. 8, 12, etc.</li></ol></div>

स्यामैअजयन्त|స్యామైఅజయన్త|ಸ್ಯಾಮೈಅಜಯನ್ತ|syāmaiajayanta|syaamaiajayanta|_vei_120@3895|_vei2_pn518
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3894">«</a>pada5<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3896">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn517">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn518">2:518</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120pn">↷</a><hr><p><b>Syāmaiajayanta,</b> 1, 185, should be <b>Śyāmasujayanta.</b></p></div>

श्रुष्टि|శ్రుష్టి|ಶ್ರುಷ್ಟಿ|śruṣṭi|shruShTi|_vei_120@3896|_vei2_pn518
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3895">«</a>pada6<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3897">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn517">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn518">2:518</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120pn">↷</a><hr><p><b>Śruṣṭi</b> (more correctly <b>Śnuṣṭi</b>) <b>Aṅgirasa,</b> 2, 397, should come on p. 403, before <b><a class="vei_120a" href="श्रुष्टिगु">śruṣṭigu</a>; and <a class="vei_120a" href="सुकुरीर">sukurīra</a></b> before <b>Sukeśin,</b> 2, 453.</p></div>

संवत्सर|సంవత్సర|ಸಂವತ್ಸರ|saṃvatsara|saMvatsara|_vei_120@3897|_vei2_pn518
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3896">«</a>pada7<a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120@3898">»</a> :::: <a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn517">↶</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei2_pn518">2:518</a><a class="vei_120a" href="_vei_120pn">↷</a><hr><p><b>Saṃvatsara.</b>--Tilak<sup id="ref_120_1_vei_1203897"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_1_vei_1203897">1</a></sup> argues that the Rigveda<sup id="ref_120_2_vei_1203897"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_2_vei_1203897">2</a></sup> and the Atharvaveda<sup id="ref_120_3_vei_1203897"><a class="vei_120a" href="#fx_120_3_vei_1203897">3</a></sup> contain signs of a dating by season and day, but neither of the passages adduced by him is at all probably so taken.</p><i>Foot notes</i><hr noshade size=1 color=black><ol start="1"><li id="fx_120_1_vei_1203897"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_1_vei_1203897">↑</a> <i>The Arctic Home in the Vedas,</i> 280288.</li><li id="fx_120_2_vei_1203897"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_2_vei_1203897">↑</a> ii. 12, 11 (<i>catvāriṃśyāṃ śaradi</i>).</li><li id="fx_120_3_vei_1203897"><a class="vei_120a" href="#ref_120_3_vei_1203897">↑</a> xii. 3, 34 (<i>ṣaṣṭyāṃ śaratsu;</i> the plural, Tilak thinks, denotes, every year’).</li></ol></div>

सायकायन|సాయకాయన|ಸಾಯಕಾಯನ|sāyakāyana|saayakaayana|_vei_120@3898|_vei2_pn518
<style>a.vei_120a{text-decoration:none;}:target{background: #ccc;border:solid 1px #aaa;}</style><style>div.vei_120patra{background: #FDD5B1;margin:0 auto 0 auto;padding:1em;max-width:55.8em;font-size: 1em;line-height:1.5em;}div.vei_120shiram{font-style:bold;padding-bottom:0.3em;max-width:20em;text-align:left;float:left;height:3em;} div.vei_120shiraputa{ max-width:15em; text-align:right; float:right; } p.vei_120para{text-indent: 2em;}  a.vei_120a_pglink,a.vei_120ft,a.vei_120a_pada{text-decoration:none; color:#664427;}a.vei_120ft{color: #097168;}a.pe_a_pada:hover{ text-decoration: underline;}div.vei_120footnts{font-size: 0.9em;}:target {background: #ccc; border: solid 1px #aaa; }</style><div><div><h2> सायकायन</h2></div><div><p><b>Sāyakāyana,</b> 1, 155, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सोभरि">sobhari</a>,</b> 1, 261, <b><a class="vei_120a" href="सौकरायण">saukarāyaṇa</a>,</b> 1, 155, should be read for <a class="vei_120a" href="सायकायन">sāyakāyana</a>, <a class="vei_120a" href="सोभरि">sobhari</a>, and <a class="vei_120a" href="सौकरायण">saukarāyaṇa</a> respectively.</p><p>Several misprints are due to the loss of diacritical marks: thus for Ajya read <a class="vei_120a" href="आज्य">ājya</a>, 2, 20; for Arcatka, Ārcatka, 2, 357; for Artava, <a class="vei_120a" href="आर्तव">ārtava</a>, 1, 63; for Apayā, <a class="vei_120a" href="आपया">āpayā</a>, 1, 218; for Amikṣā, <a class="vei_120a" href="आमिक्षा">āmikṣā</a>, 1, 250; for Aśuṃga, <a class="vei_120a" href="आशुंग">āśuṃga</a>, 2, 387; for Asandī, <a class="vei_120a" href="आसन्दी">āsandī</a>, 2, 383; for Dirghatamas, <a class="vei_120a" href="दीर्घतमस्">dīrghatamas</a>, 1, 366; for Satapatha, Śatapatha, 1, 18, 34, 55, 67, 111, 119, 157, 242, 291, 371, 463, 516, 523; 2, 24, 80, 220, 221, 358, 362, 433; for Srauta, <a class="vei_120a" href="श्रौत">śrauta</a>, 1, 18, 55, 281, 282, 373; 2, 34, 71, 281, 301; for Sāṅkhāyana, <a class="vei_120a" href="शांखायन">śāṅkhāyana</a>, 1, 257, 281, 400, 469; 2, 34, 87, 281, 383; for Sāṭyāyanaka, Śātyāyanaka, i. 407; for <a class="vei_120a" href="सावसायन">sāvasāyana</a>, Śāvasāyana, 2, 376. </p></div>

